d Stine later , but then the master and all his apprentices and journeymen were up every morning at three o'clock and hammered away and made", "context_len": 128}}}, "apprentices": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 108, "context": "tricked into moving into another house when anybody would tell them of a very quiet place , and as she long had been wishing for an opportunity to play a trick upon this brownie , she took courage and spoke to him , -- her voice was a little shaky at the time , -- that he ought to remove to the tinman 's over the way , where it was so very quiet and pleasant , because they always went to bed at nine o'clock every evening ; which was true enough , as the cook told Stine later , but then the master and all his apprentices and journeymen were up every morning at three o'clock and hammered away and made a terrible noise all day", "context_len": 128}}}, "journeymen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 110, "context": "tricked into moving into another house when anybody would tell them of a very quiet place , and as she long had been wishing for an opportunity to play a trick upon this brownie , she took courage and spoke to him , -- her voice was a little shaky at the time , -- that he ought to remove to the tinman 's over the way , where it was so very quiet and pleasant , because they always went to bed at nine o'clock every evening ; which was true enough , as the cook told Stine later , but then the master and all his apprentices and journeymen were up every morning at three o'clock and hammered away and made a terrible noise all day", "context_len": 128}}}, "hammering": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 15, "context": "He seemed to feel quite at home at the tinman 's , although they were hammering and tapping away there all day ; but people said that the gude-wife put a dish of porridge up in the garret for him every Thursday evening , and it 's no wonder that they got on well and became rich when they had a brownie in the house", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "There was the sound of active hammering as Bart entered the office , which Darry suspended long enough to remark :", "context_len": 21}}}, "tapping": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 91, "context": "'s evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and sent such cold shivers running down their backs that they grew almost too frightened to go to bed", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was a grand march and the tapping of the feet seemed like an encore to the music", "context_len": 18}}}, "garret": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 37, "context": "He seemed to feel quite at home at the tinman 's , although they were hammering and tapping away there all day ; but people said that the gude-wife put a dish of porridge up in the garret for him every Thursday evening , and it 's no wonder that they got on well and became rich when they had a brownie in the house", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It went up and down stairs , from cellar to garret , and in and out of all the rooms , like anybody , with a faint , cynical indifference in the glance of its cold gray eyes that gave no hint of its purposes or performances", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was the talk of the town how he had shut himself in his garret day after day in a most suspicious manner and had often boasted that some day he would \u2019 make a lot of money", "context_len": 38}}}, "coughing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Whether it was the brownie or not who really helped them , I can not say , '' said Mother Skau , in conclusion , and got a fit of coughing and choking after the exertion of telling this , for her , unusually long story", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "He preached very nicely indeed , and there was not the usual noisy coughing and hawking which you always hear at the morning services on a Christmas Day ; it was so quiet , you could have heard a needle drop on the floor , -- in fact , it was so quiet she began to feel quite uneasy and uncomfortable", "context_len": 61}}}, "pinch": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 42, "context": "But Blue -- Elizabeth , you know that this is your ranch , and that your father wanted you brought up to know all about it , so as to be able to manage things for yourself a bit -- at a pinch", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` We never know beforehand where the shoe is going to pinch , '' remarked Jane in a sententious voice", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "At times he would reach out and pinch Bomba \u2019 s legs until the lad winced", "context_len": 16}}}, "snuff": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Look here , young 'un ; a lad as ca n't swim ai n't -- 'cordin ' to my ideas -- hardly worth the snuff of a candle", "context_len": 28}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "There is little recorded of Simeon 's early life to indicate the character of the future leader of men ; for , to `` jump over half a dozen chairs in succession , and snuff a candle with his feet , '' is an ordinary schoolboy accomplishment", "context_len": 47}}}, "truthful": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 9, "context": "For the most part , also , they were truthful , and they only told lies when they felt obliged to do so , as when they had been in swimming and said they had not , or as when they wanted to get away from some of the boys , or did not wish the whole crowd to know what they were doing", "context_len": 64}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "It was the same gas that dentists now give when they draw teeth ; but it was then used to make people merry and truthful , to make them laugh and say just what they thought", "context_len": 36}}}, "untrue": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "Why , when Henry Bolingbroke was made king he had pardoned this Huntington , though he had been as untrue to Henry as he could be , because he was his sister 's husband", "context_len": 34}}}, "coughed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 4, "context": "The stranger shivered , coughed , grumbled out a few more oaths in bad Spanish , and hitching his chair nearer to the fire he lifted the tankard the woman of the house brought to him , and nodded over at Richard", "context_len": 42}}}, "proceeded": {"freq": 155, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 155, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}}}, "teens": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "But now , as they had heard that Philip , who had been so terrible a warrior , was no more , and that his son , scarcely out of his teens , had succeeded to the throne , they thought a suitable occasion had arrived to try their strength", "context_len": 50}}}, "widow": {"freq": 75, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 42, "context": "When I see five or six boys now lying under a tree on the grass , and they fall silent as I pass them , I have no right to say that they are not arranging to go and carry some poor widow 's winter wood into her shed and pile it neatly up for her , and wish to keep it a secret from everybody ; but forty years ago I should have had good reason for thinking that they were debating how to tie a piece of her clothes-line along the ground so that when her orphan boy came out for an armload of wood after dark , he would trip on it and send his wood flying all over the yard", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "At the last moment , smitten by regret because fancied necessity seemed to compel him to leave without bidding the kind widow good-by , he seized a piece of brown paper and the stub of a pencil and sat down to write a few words of farewell Jerry urging him to hasten even while he was scribbling", "context_len": 57}}}, "coffee": {"freq": 86, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 86, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Retrench ! how ? Do you suppose the boarders will do without their comfortable hot coffee , and the other luxuries on the board at breakfast ? Do you suppose they will do without their lunch , their afternoon tea with plenty of cakes and plenty of cream , their late dinner , at which appears all the luxuries of the season ? -- why , the house would be empty in a week", "context_len": 75}}}, "streaming": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 65, "context": "I 've seen it too , continued Donald , wondering whether an artist-proof and a waterproof had anything in common ; but the girls are n't often so handsome as that ; but I 'll tell you when they do look pretty as a picture : that 's on a clear Sunday morning , just about midway in the service , when the sun comes streaming through one of the choir windows in a great white shaft of light , I think they call it", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "After that he wore it several times with streaming tears ; and then he was allowed to lay it aside , and compromise on an unstylish cap of velvet , which he had despised before", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "The little low whitewashed cottage was a humble enough place , but it looked cheerful this Sunday afternoon , with the sunlight streaming in through the tiny window , and a few early white roses shedding their sweet perfume in the small garden in front", "context_len": 45}}}, "overslept": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "She must have overslept herself ! She called the girl and told her to get the coffee ready , while she dressed herself", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "She had , in fact , overslept herself a little , and as Becky had done the same , neither had had time to see the other , and each had come downstairs in haste", "context_len": 35}}}, "customary": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 5, "context": "We knew that it was customary for the slavers coming out of the Gulf of Guinea to endeavour to sight Cape Palmas , in order that they might obtain a good `` departure '' for the run across the Atlantic , also because they might usually reckon upon picking up the Trades somewhere in that neighbourhood", "context_len": 56}}}, "pews": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "When she went inside , she sat down in her customary seat in one of the pews , but when she looked around her she thought that the people were so pale and so strange , -- exactly as if they were all dead", "context_len": 44}}}, "minister": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 98, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}}}, "pulpit": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 52, "context": "Although General Harrison 's strong personal fascination and unselfish ardour still commanded his nephew 's affection and even admiration , the young man 's irreverent common sense could not help viewing these new Fifth Monarchy opinions held by his uncle and his uncle 's friends as fitter for Bedlam than for the pulpit or the parliament house", "context_len": 57}}}, "ministers": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 97, "context": "dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of the sunset", "context_len": 128}}}, "preached": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 2, "context": "So I preached upon the Saint of the day , who was pre-eminently a man of peace , and I took occasion to tell my people that there were many hurtful men about , who , like their master , Satan , were seeking whom they might devour , and that , like that master , they chose the night for their misdeeds , seeing they loved darkness rather than light", "context_len": 71}}}, "hawking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "He preached very nicely indeed , and there was not the usual noisy coughing and hawking which you always hear at the morning services on a Christmas Day ; it was so quiet , you could have heard a needle drop on the floor , -- in fact , it was so quiet she began to feel quite uneasy and uncomfortable", "context_len": 61}}}, "female": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "When the singing commenced again , a female who sat next to her leant towards her and whispered in her ear , 'Throw the cloak loosely around you and go , because if you wait here till the service is over they will make short work of you", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 21, "context": "Sp\u00e1ne or Spanish gens ; m\u00edngos or chiefs could be chosen from this gens only , and were hereditary in the female line ; sh\u0103-\u00e9 or racoon gens ; second chiefs or headmen were selected from it ; kuishto or tiger gens ; ko-intch\u016bsh or catamount gens ; n\u00e1ni or fish gens ; \u00edssi or deer gens ; hal\u00f3ba or ? gens ; fosh\u00e9 or bird gens ; hunshkon\u00e9 or skunk gens , the least respected of them all", "context_len": 79}}}, "cloak": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then a tall and enormously stout man , clad in a leopard-skin moucha , and with a handsome leopard- skin cloak on his shoulders , came forth from the palace , leaning upon the shoulders of two other men , and advanced toward the chair which had first been placed in position , into which he subsided heavily , casting a strongly disapproving glance at the second chair as he did so", "context_len": 72}}}, "loosely": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 25, "context": "When the singing commenced again , a female who sat next to her leant towards her and whispered in her ear , 'Throw the cloak loosely around you and go , because if you wait here till the service is over they will make short work of you", "context_len": 48}}}, "whimpered": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 1, "context": "They whimpered and cowered and glanced at the water , as though they were almost willing to take their chances with the alligators rather than approach the island they deemed accursed", "context_len": 31}}}, "hush": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 43, "context": "Then she knelt by the broad , low window-seat , and said her prayers , looking away to the stars , which glowed red , and green , and yellow , in the soft summer sky , and then , in a great hush of delight , she lay down between the delicately-perfumed sheets , and gave herself up to the enjoyment of the present which God had given her", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "Bessie woke many times , and as often as she did so , she found that nurse had not come to bed , and when she looked through the open door which led into her mother 's room , she saw either her father or mother walking up and down with the baby , trying to hush her pitiful cries and moans", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Oh , Pansy dear , do hush ! You miss Marilla , do n't you ? The best little nurse in all the world", "context_len": 24}}}, "recognized": {"freq": 105, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 105, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` She got away from them safe enough ; only listen ! When the widow heard the voice of the person next to her , she turned round to look at her , -- but what a start she got ! She recognized her ; it was her neighbour who died many years ago ; and when she looked around the church , she remembered well that she had seen both the minister and several of the congregation before , and that they had died long ago", "context_len": 87}}}, "remembered": {"freq": 206, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 203, "word_index": 86, "context": "that he should suppose her to be capable of such an evasion , which to her sense of uprightness would have been as bad as speaking a falsehood by word of mouth ; `` no , of course I did not , that would have been telling her , would it not ? One can do a falsehood as well as tell it , '' and although she had intended no reflection upon her brother , no thrust at him , Percy was ashamed as he remembered how often , during the last few months , he had done this very thing ; how he had shuffled and evaded , and thought it no great harm as long as he did not put his falsehood into actual words", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "Dimly remembered stories her mother used to tell her of the big white house standing amidst its tall trees came back to her , and the vague hopes and dreams that had been filling her thoughts for weeks past began to take definite form", "context_len": 44}}}, "congregation": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 77, "context": "`` She got away from them safe enough ; only listen ! When the widow heard the voice of the person next to her , she turned round to look at her , -- but what a start she got ! She recognized her ; it was her neighbour who died many years ago ; and when she looked around the church , she remembered well that she had seen both the minister and several of the congregation before , and that they had died long ago", "context_len": 87}}}, "pew": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 64, "context": "side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all service time , and if she did not behave", "context_len": 128}}}, "clutches": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` No , indeed ! Nurse , with Maggie on one arm and Bessie on the other , fairly dancing about the room in her efforts to save the former from Aunt Patty 's clutches , both terrified babies screaming at the top of their voices , both old women scolding at the top of theirs ; while Fred , the monkey , young as he was , stood by , clapping his hands and setting them at each other as if they had been two cats", "context_len": 87}}}, "torn": {"freq": 100, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 18, "context": "He had no difficulty in getting half-a-dozen damaged mackerel down in the harbour -- fish that had been torn by the nets ; but he was only just in time , for in the soft grey light he could see the gulls already busy floating down on their ghostly-looking wings in the gloom , uttering a mournful , peevish wail , and carrying off fragments of fish for their morning meal", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 63, "context": "The tiny fences were pulled up , and scattered far and wide ; lady-slippers , mignonette , verbenas , and all the other simple flowers which had flourished so well , and given such pride and delight to the little gardeners , were rooted up and trampled into the earth ; and , worse than all , the beloved heliotrope and geranium were torn leaf from leaf and sprig from sprig , while their main stems had been twisted and bent , till no hope remained that even these could be revived", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "Cleaning her cellar and playing with him brought no money to give food to either ; she must go out to earn something , she must leave the child behind her , and if he cried in any way the neighbours overhead would discover his existence , and then her secret would be out , and her treasure torn from her arms", "context_len": 62}}}, "thousand": {"freq": 335, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 110, "word_index": 44, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 200, "word_index": 64, "context": "All the beasts of the prairie had he slain in his terrible anger , and all the people had fled to the mountains for food , thither he thought he would follow them , and he sat down upon a ridge of sand , to strengthen his bow , and sharpen his arrows , when , lo ! quite unmindful of him , a thousand little creatures he had fancied too insignificant to notice , sprang forth from their holes , and gathered in groups for their daily gossip", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 12, "context": "Two sets of mail bags were approaching each other from points two thousand miles apart , and there were times when this approach was at the astounding speed of forty and even fifty miles an hour ! The average daily rate was two hundred and fifty miles a day , but where everything was favorable , or when an express rider was fleeing from the vengeful red men , his pony struck a gait of twenty-five miles and maintained it , when an untrained horse would have dropped in his tracks", "context_len": 91}}}, "lining": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "I have a nice little pot buried in a safe place on a certain key ; I 've got a map here '' -- and he thumped his broad chest -- '' here , sewed in the lining of my coat , and the place marked with a cross ; and I tell you , sir , there 's enough gold in that pot to fit out the snuggest little pinnace any man need want to see", "context_len": 77}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "The question of decorations was much discussed , for while Father suggested hanging the walls with sacking , and Lilian voted for garlanding them with wild flowers , both ideas were rejected , the one as too prosaic and the other as not sufficiently durable , and it was not until Peggy conceived the brilliant thought of lining their dwelling with moss that a satisfactory solution was arrived at", "context_len": 69}}}, "borders": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 30, "context": "Joseph 's tree at Glastonbury , and that he was there on pilgrimage when Alfgar saw him -- saw him , mark you -- at the Danish camp on the borders of Sussex ; and I saw men , I wo n't mention names , who had more than once taken reward to slay the innocent , look as if they would go down on their knees to this holy thorn , which was n't a holy thorn at all , but plucked from some hedge hard at hand", "context_len": 89}}}, "childhood": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 64, "context": "Her friendships were so keen and her affections so warm , that I think she found the world a very pleasant place to be in , and made it better and pleasanter for other people too ( which is , after all , the grand secret of happiness ) , and the energy and enterprising spirits which had caused the tomboy pranks of her childhood were qualities which , turned to good use , proved of the utmost service to her in after-years", "context_len": 83}}}, "rarely": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 57, "context": "He was smuggled into the town and sold to Ben Ibyn the Berber , who , to conceal the matter , dyed him black ; but it got to the ears of the sultan , and he had him taken from the Berber , and brought here ; I have no doubt the merchant has been squeezed rarely", "context_len": 58}}}, "cloaks": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 56, "context": "The light of day was rapidly fading ; a light breeze brought down the remaining leaves from the trees , or whirled them about in all directions ; winter was plainly about to assume the mastery of the scene , as was evident from the clothing the people wore , the thick fur and warm woollen cloaks which covered their light tunics", "context_len": 62}}}, "expressed": {"freq": 106, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 106, "word_index": 102, "context": "it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached L'Esperance , and the extraordinary story I had heard while aboard her", "context_len": 128}}}, "considerable": {"freq": 150, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 147, "word_index": 73, "context": "The little old gentleman turned out to be a very learned don from Oxford , with a terrific list of letters at the end of his name , and in the course of a few days a representative arrived from the Bodleian Library with a request to be allowed to inspect the books ( which by this time had been removed to a corner of the big drawing-room ) , and after a considerable amount of talking and writing it was arranged that they should be sent to London at once to be sold by auction", "context_len": 96}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I 've seen a good bit of towns and been to considerable many ports , but I ai n't seen nawthin ' I 'd swop this place fur", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "And so they were not to see Lewis Carroll , which was of course considerable of a disappointment to Marie-Celeste and Harold , and to Dorothy as well ; but all the same the recollection of that Sunday afternoon in Oxford will doubtless long hold its place among the most delightful memories of their lives", "context_len": 55}}}, "uneasiness": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 76, "context": "The strong sentiment of love and devotion with which he inspired all around him at the commencement of his career , was now gone , and his generals and officers were continually planning schemes to depose him from the power which he seemed no longer to have the energy to wield ; or , at least , Alexander was continually suspecting that such plans were formed , and he was kept in a continual state of uneasiness and anxiety in discovering and punishing them", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 57, "context": "I wish he were more like Lane \u2019 s boy I don \u2019 t like him , for he is obstinate and headstrong , and utterly unreasonable in his demands upon me , but he is steady and correct in his habits , and if he were in Victor \u2019 s place would never give me any uneasiness", "context_len": 58}}}, "plucking": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "They had crept up into the sofa and on the chairs , but still they thought they felt somebody plucking at them from underneath the table", "context_len": 26}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "She could not take the rig on the train , anyway , and plucking up courage , and shivering in the cold that had seized upon him during his deliberations , he meditatively and angrily began to plod over the route that he had recommended to her", "context_len": 47}}}, "discovered": {"freq": 211, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 209, "word_index": 69, "context": "They said that if caught they would be either killed or made slaves of , and I could not contradict them , but said that , in the first place , as I was ready to run the risk , there was no reason why they should n't do so , and in the second , there was no chance whatever of their being taken , as , if discovered , we should get so long a start that we could either escape them altogether or run the boat ashore at some point where the trees came down to the water 's edge , carry the boat up and hide it , and then move up into the hills until the corsairs had gone", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "DISCOVERED 151", "context_len": 2}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "DISCOVERED", "context_len": 1}}}, "jellies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The lights , the Christmas cake , the jellies , the tarts and the wine soon chased away the horrible ghost story and all fear from their minds , revived everybody 's spirits , and brought the conversation on to their neighbours and the topics of the day", "context_len": 48}}}, "tarts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The lights , the Christmas cake , the jellies , the tarts and the wine soon chased away the horrible ghost story and all fear from their minds , revived everybody 's spirits , and brought the conversation on to their neighbours and the topics of the day", "context_len": 48}}}, "wine": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 9, "context": "When the shout had died away , and the wine cups were emptied , Gervaise , who was sitting on Sir John Kendall 's right hand , would gladly have retained his seat , but the bailiff told him that he must say a few words , and after standing in embarrassed silence for a minute he said , Sir John Kendall , and brother knights , I can only say that I am very sensible of the kindness with which you have been pleased to regard what seems to me after all to have been a very ordinary affair", "context_len": 100}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Moreover , do thou seek of him wine , and that red , [ 597 ] and make him a show of all joy and gladness and drink to his health", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "Oh , how hollow the mirth and revelry that night ! How he loathed the singing , the drunken shouting , the fierce imprecation over the wine cup -- the sensuality , which now distinguished his bloodthirsty companions", "context_len": 38}}}, "horrible": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 89, "word_index": 70, "context": "As we drew near to the exceedingly narrow gate in the palisade , which had been thrown open to admit us -- and which , I presently saw , was strongly guarded by a number of warriors armed with heavy , broad-bladed spears , murderous-looking swords , and small round shields , or targets , of wood covered with what looked like crocodile hide -- I became sensible of a horrible charnel-house smell ; but it was not until we had passed through the gate , and were inside the palisaded enclosure , that I discovered from whence it emanated", "context_len": 100}}}, "ghost": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 79, "context": "The idea that the Creeks knew anything of the devil of the Christian religion is a pure invention of the missionaries ; being christianized , they call him now : \u00edsti f\u00fatchig\u014d `` the man acting perversely , '' taso l\u00e4 ya , or : \u00edsti nikl\u00e9-idsha ats\u016b li `` the old person-burner '' ( \u00e1ni nikl\u00e9-idsh\u00e4s I burn somebody , something ) ; the Yuchi call him `` the swinging man , '' just as they call a ghost `` a hunting man", "context_len": 84}}}, "minds": {"freq": 108, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 105, "word_index": 94, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "'This way for the giant and dwarf ! Come in , and open yer minds ! '", "context_len": 17}}}, "revived": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 91, "context": "The tiny fences were pulled up , and scattered far and wide ; lady-slippers , mignonette , verbenas , and all the other simple flowers which had flourished so well , and given such pride and delight to the little gardeners , were rooted up and trampled into the earth ; and , worse than all , the beloved heliotrope and geranium were torn leaf from leaf and sprig from sprig , while their main stems had been twisted and bent , till no hope remained that even these could be revived", "context_len": 92}}}, "everybody": {"freq": 150, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 147, "word_index": 102, "context": "to learn just what the issue had been , and at a time when he would be able to stop and tell about it ? Would any one else in the world have thought of suggesting that he should give three short little whistles when he reached the Browns ' , in Remsen Street , so that she should know just how near he was ? Surely no one ; and it was just this surpassing interest in every living body , to the utter forgetting of all that concerned herself , that made Marie-Celeste different from other children , that made everybody love her , and that makes it worth while for me to try to tell this story of one summer in her blessed little life", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` You forget , '' he said , `` that Sweyn will shortly be here ; your friend , the Etheling , may have told you that , if you did not know it before ; he is telling it to everybody , but no one believes him", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` I guess everybody heard it , '' said Jane", "context_len": 10}}}, "spirits": {"freq": 156, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 155, "word_index": 12, "context": "But the dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "But strange ghosts arise from it , spirits that harm", "context_len": 10}}}, "topics": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "They were full of their own interests , and , on the few occasions when she happened to meet them , had talked so much of new teachers , new pupils , and class topics of which she knew nothing , that she had felt a little 'out of it , ' and had an uneasy sensation that , if she spoke of the quiet little episodes of her daily round they would vote her housekeeping experiences as decidedly 'slow", "context_len": 80}}}, "substantial": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 22, "context": "Thus the time passed on until the first day of the Customs dawned , when , having received a more than usually substantial meal , I was stripped of the few rags that still covered my nakedness , and , with my hands tightly bound behind me by a thin but strong raw-hide rope , was led forth to the great square wherein the Customs were celebrated , and firmly bound to one of the posts , the erection of which I had witnessed a week earlier", "context_len": 87}}}, "roast": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 69, "context": "We are all just now in a state of indignation about your pork ! Don \u2019 t suppose that it is any fault in the pork ; on the contrary , it is acknowledged to be the most refined pork ever known ; and Mother says that if she shut her eyes , she would not know that she was not eating chicken ! ! We had a beautiful roast of it one day at luncheon ; and Mother cut off a choice bit , to be reserved for our table , cold , while the servants were indulged with the rest of that joint", "context_len": 105}, "VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 80, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Jones found how fond the children were of roast clams , she often had them for their breakfast or supper ; but they never tasted so good as they did when they were cooked in the sand and eaten on the shore", "context_len": 42}}}, "ribs": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 32, "context": "But before I could compose myself to rest two other women entered , one of whom bore , upon a thick biscuit-like cake the size of an ordinary dinner-plate , two roast ribs of goat and a generous portion of boiled yam , while the other carried a calabash full of what I took to be some kind of native beer", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Whereupon the Vizier 's son arose forthright and coming down from the bed , fell to donning his clothes , with ribs cracking for cold ; for that , when the Sultan entered , it was no great while since the genie had brought them back", "context_len": 46}}}, "wishes": {"freq": 108, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 67, "word_index": 109, "context": "'s service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut her ear to the cry of the needy , because to answer it would cost a sacrifice of her own wishes ? Should she bear the burdens of others only when they did not weigh heavily on herself ?", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 50, "context": "If there is to be any order , if the whole scheme which mother has thought out so carefully , and father has approved of , means to establish a girl like Rosamund Cunliffe here as our leader , so that we are forced to do every single thing she wishes , I shall beg and implore of father and mother to let me go and live with Aunt Susan in the old Rectory at Dartford", "context_len": 76}}}, "supper": {"freq": 273, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 216, "word_index": 54, "context": "It was nearly noon when I was conducted back to my hut after my futile attempt to cure the king ; and it was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` The twilight gathered about the two and in the dim light Uncle Justus did not appear in the least a person to stand in awe of , for when Ellen came to call them to supper she was surprised to see the little girl still sitting on the old man 's knee , his arm around her and her head on his shoulder", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` 'Oh , dear , ' said little Aleck , 'there 's Aunt Henrietta ! Now she 'll make us go in the house , and she 'll give me my supper early and send me to bed , though Aunt Mary said I might sit up and have tea with the rest , even if they came home late", "context_len": 60}}}, "hither": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 22, "context": "When you went to Scotland first , you and he were of one mind , one heart , but when you came hither again , your head was stuffed full of worldly wisdom and time-serving devices , talking of a Lord Protector instead of the glory of God , and hand and glove with that cruel Cromwell who did throw my saint into prison ! Your heart was turned from those that reared you , and given to their enemies ! And now you can stand by unmoved and see him you once loved haled to prison and death ! ''", "context_len": 101}, "NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 81, "context": "you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been seized while travelling with a safe conduct from the governor of Syria", "context_len": 128}}}, "thither": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 28, "context": "My uncle busied himself till all traces of his worry lost themselves in his activity ; my aunt bustled about the house , driving the servants hither and thither , bringing linen from the huge oak chest , furbishing the massive furniture , and causing a huge supply of viands to be prepared for the entertainment of our guests -- for the invited company numbered close on a score , so that our house would be filled to overflowing , and rooms had to be engaged at `` The Bell Inn '' and `` The Blue Posts ''", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 120, "context": "replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both far and near have heard ? How is it thou hast heard nought of this nor of the name of Alaeddin , whom Our Lord increase in glory and prosper ? '' Quoth the Maugrabin , `` Marry , it is the utmost of my wish to look upon the palace ; so , an thou wouldst do me a kindness , direct me thither , for that I am a stranger", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "And thither the party set out , the cadets freely discussing the probable fate of the unhappy plebe", "context_len": 18}}}, "soldiers": {"freq": 210, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 209, "word_index": 93, "context": "had hay in his right shoe , and straw in his left , because a Dutchman was too dumb , as the boys said for stupid , to know his feet apart any other way ; and that the Dutch officers had to call out to the men when they were marching , `` Up mit de hay-foot , down mit de straw-foot -- links , links , links ! '' ( Left , left , left ! ) But the boys honored even these imperfect intelligences so much in their quality of soldiers that they would any of them have been proud to be marker in the Dutch company ; and they followed the Dutchmen round in their march as fondly as any other body of troops", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Why , the very soldiers look like figures in plaster of Paris", "context_len": 12}}}, "uniform": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 28, "context": "His shoes , though well worn and patched , had been blacked and polished till they looked quite respectable ; the suit made from his father 's old uniform was still neat and whole , for Willie 's present quiet life was a great saving to his clothes , if that were any comfort ; his white collar was turned back and neatly tied with a black ribbon , and Mrs", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 24, "context": "It bore the inscription `` 1812 , '' and according to the colonel , portrayed a military man life-size , epaulettes , sword , uniform and all -- his maternal grandfather as he had appeared in the battle scene where he had lost a limb", "context_len": 45}}}, "lasses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "There were nobody there but peasants in their red caps , soldiers in full uniform , country lasses with their white head-dresses and red cheeks", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "There , Faith , child , why do n't you go to Sunday-school , same as the other little lads and lasses about ? They teach everythink about coming to Jesus in Sunday-school", "context_len": 33}}}, "grandfather": {"freq": 104, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 103, "word_index": 98, "context": "the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner , and the oak dresser , with its rows of blue willow-pattern plates ; and back again at last to where Mrs", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` Oh , but we are glad to see you , and are you all right , and are you coming home to supper with us ? I do n't believe I 've slept a wink this week , have I , grandfather ? ''", "context_len": 45}}}, "sermon": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 116, "context": "tell you one thing , Uncle Everett , it was a talk I had with that dear child under an apple-tree , down at Nuneham , that made me feel that some people whom I care a great deal for still had faith in me , and it was she who gave me courage by what she told me to go home as fast as ever I could get there and then , Marie-Celeste , what do you suppose I said to him ? Well , I just , told him that that same dear child had preached me two blessed sermons -- one on the deck of the Majestic and the other exactly where a sermon should be preached , beneath the roof of dear old St", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "And so to all outward appearances there was no change whatever in this particular old bachelor , and the little sermon Marie-Celeste had unconsciously preached on the steamer and the reading of the Story of a Short Life had only given him a glimpse of what a noble thing life might be , without awakening any real determination to make his own life noble", "context_len": 64}}}, "somersault": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 29, "context": "Who but she could climb to the very highest branch of the tallest tree ? Who but she could swing so high that she seemed almost to turn a somersault in the air before she came down again ? Who but she could invent the most daring games ? And then , when all other things failed , who but she could tell such weird stories ? Her eyes shone ; her lips were wreathed in smiles", "context_len": 77}}}, "smartest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "Her mother sprang up and kissed her rapturously , crying -- 'Is n't she the dearest and sweetest thing and the smartest ! Think of her learning that and acting it off so completely , and not three years old ! She is smarter than Violet ' -- and then Violet set up such a howl ! Her mother pacified her by saying Marilla should tell her a piece , and after several efforts Cinderella did induce her to say by a great deal of prompting 'Milkman , Milkman , where have you been ? ' Think of the wear on the child 's nerves , and she looked so tired", "context_len": 111}}}, "surplice": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "But just as he was in the middle of the sermon , he made a somersault -- he was known as one of the smartest men in the parish -- right into the middle of the church ; the surplice flew one way and the collar another", "context_len": 47}}}, "parson": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` There lies the parson , and here am I , '' he said , with one of his well-known airs , `` and now let us have a spring dance ! '' In an instant the whole of the congregation was in the midst of a wild dance", "context_len": 49}}}, "airs": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 14, "context": "So they plied their finger-tips and touching their strings , struck up with plaintive airs , till they clove in sunder the hearts of the listeners , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour redoubled in wonderment and said in herself , `` Never in my life heard I the like of these songs ; '' so that she forgot to eat and fell to listening", "context_len": 63}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Where did she get that splendid silk from ? But what airs she does put on ; how vulgar she is ! ''", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "'Well , I hope they 're not comin ' trailin ' down here with their city airs , ' said Mrs Harding shortly", "context_len": 23}}}, "peasant": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Then he rose and going forth the [ privy ] door of his palace , walked on till he encountered a peasant by the way and said to him , `` Harkye , sirrah , take my clothes and give me thine", "context_len": 43}}}, "bible": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "He then read a short chapter from the bible and all knelt down while he offered up a prayer for guidance and protection", "context_len": 23}}}, "dream": {"freq": 160, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 140, "word_index": 77, "context": "grow , when they were not there to watch it all , for most of us have a kind of feeling that we are the important centre around which Nature turns , instead of only mere spectators of her varying moods , and sometimes it felt so impossible that such an utter upheaval in their lives could really come to pass that she would have to shake herself to believe that it was not all a bad dream ; but as she noticed the quiet preparations that went on , and the added worry on Father 's face , she realized that it was only too true , and that every day was bringing them nearer to that terrible twenty-fifth of July when the mortgages would fall due", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 103, "context": "of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very strongly influence him", "context_len": 128}}}, "fixedly": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 16, "context": "When it was full , his mother looked at the dish , but could not gaze fixedly thereon , for the radiance of the jewels and their lustre and the excess of their flashing ; so she shut her eyes and her wit was confounded at them ; yet was she not certified that their value was in very deed so great as her son had said , but bethought her that his speech might be true in that their like was not found with kings", "context_len": 86}}}, "drops": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 64, "context": "all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to find the poor creature ; so that , his hair being wet , he was a sight to behold when Harry pulled him out , covered with mud from head to", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 59, "context": "No fear of that , but he such a big , hulking chap and eats so much that he oughter be made to do something ; I \u2019 m going to put him on tother side of this wagon and make him b \u2019 leve it \u2019 s the most important post of all , and that if he drops asleep , the whole shebang will be wiped out by the redskins", "context_len": 72}}}, "lair": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 77, "context": "As this seemed the most humane suggestion , Master Dormouse was tucked up in bed once more , and , still protesting , was carried to a sunny bank under an apple-tree , and stowed away under a protecting clump of leaves , where his plaintive voice gradually subsided , and he settled down for five months of oblivion , to ignore the winter frosts and storms until the April sunshine should tempt him out of his lair", "context_len": 78}}}, "heavens": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Good heavens ! '' exclaimed the skipper , stopping short and staring at me as though he had seen a ghost -- '' is it possible ? It ca n't be -- and yet , by Jove , it is -- Mr Fortescue ! Welcome back to the Eros , Mr Fortescue ; I am delighted to see you again", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was a dull day , dull as the gloom which was upon our spirits ; the very heavens seemed to have put on funeral attire , and the chilly wind which swept over the scene seemed quite at home", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "A crash of thunder that seemed to rip the very heavens asunder , a flash of lightning like a jagged finger of fire searing the sky , and again Bomba saw that blot , but this time more distinctly", "context_len": 39}}}, "specimen": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 21, "context": "It happened to be a local holiday in Cumberland , and as a result here and there they encountered a solitary specimen of humanity prone upon his back or his face , just as it chanced , by the roadside , or , not quite so badly off as that , reeling along to wherever home might be in that apparently houseless region", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Brooke , we hear a good deal about American scenery , but if this is a specimen I can only say that it is a good deal overrated", "context_len": 28}}}, "daylight": {"freq": 99, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Yes , I see what you mean , Mr Fortescue , '' interrupted the skipper ; `` and doubtless there are many cases where the plan would be very commendable ; but in this case I think it would be better to close with her while it is still daylight and we can see exactly what we -- and they -- are doing", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 77, "word_index": 67, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}, "VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 65, "context": "Ye can steal back and forth on the inside of the circle of wagons , fur that will help keep yer peepers peeled , but ba \u2019 rs and beavers ! no man \u2019 s wuth shucks if he can \u2019 t stay awake till midnight , and them as comes on duty then will have had \u2019 nough sleep to last \u2019 em till daylight", "context_len": 66}}}, "undo": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 68, "context": "Then they went to the Sultan and told him that Alaeddin had commanded them of this ; whereupon he asked them , `` What said he to you and why would he not have the lattice-work finished and why undid he that which you had done ? '' And they said to him , `` O my lord , we know nothing , save that he bade us undo all that we had done", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "When it was morning , Alaeddin went up to view the jewelers ' work and saw that they had not completed half the lacking lattice-work ; whereupon he bade them incontinent undo all that they had wrought and restore the jewels to their owners", "context_len": 44}}}, "boisterous": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was a merry , not to say boisterous , party , for Archie was in one of his funniest moods , and told 'tall ' Yankee stories till the children nearly rolled off their seats with laughter , and Lilian went on pouring into her overflowing cup till the tray was swimming with tea", "context_len": 55}}}, "chains": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 28, "context": "Friends ! out upon them ! They mock the name ; it were not thus , if thou Hadst drawn thy gallant sword with those who wear No chains but those of Virtue , those who own No earthly Monarch , and uphold no power But that of Liberty ; whose friendship lasts Not only when the red wine sparkles high , And revelry and song profane the night ; If such had been thy comrades and thy friends , Thou hadst not been forsaken thus", "context_len": 86}}}, "denoted": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 13, "context": "The magistrate closed a book in front of him in a manner that denoted a feeling of disappointment , then , signing to our guards , he motioned us to be removed", "context_len": 32}}}, "obvious": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 32, "context": "Yet I scarcely knew precisely what it was that I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether", "context_len": 128}}}, "typographical": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "Obvious typographical errors and punctuation errors have been corrected after careful comparison with other occurrences within the text and consultation of external sources", "context_len": 23}}}, "errors": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 9, "context": "Minor changes have been made to correct typesetters ' errors , and to ensure consistent spelling and punctuation in this etext ; otherwise , every effort has been made to remain true to the original book", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "X stands for SCORE-MARK , which errors point out", "context_len": 9}}}, "corrected": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 43, "context": "The poor child had no one to tell her how foolish and sinful this was , or to warn her that she was laying up trouble for herself and her friends , for her father would never suffer her to be contradicted or corrected", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "was the corrected position , and to the unbounded satisfaction of us all it was found that it marked a small island almost in the centre of the Rosario Channel , agreeing with the description which Pedro Alvarez had given", "context_len": 40}}}, "comparison": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 16, "context": "True they had passed over a wonderful country , but the alkali plains seemed small in comparison to the desert , over which they had passed some weeks before , and all the grandeur of the Sierra Nevada Mountains could not awaken in them one iota of the enthusiasm with which they had greeted the first glimpse of the snow-capped summits of the Rocky Mountains", "context_len": 65}}}, "consultation": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 47, "context": "When they reached the dining-room , Miss Belmore was already seated at the table , ready to receive them ; but as places were set for only three , two little hearts were again doomed to disappointment , for two little minds , without any sort of consultation , had separately arrived at the conclusion that all that elaborate preparation could certainly mean nothing less than luncheon with Her Majesty in person", "context_len": 72}}}, "external": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 3, "context": "But the dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of", "context_len": 128}}}, "sources": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 3, "context": "From more recent sources it appears that these settlements consisted of smaller groups of cabins clustered together in tribes , perhaps also after gentes , as we see it done among the Mississippi tribes and in a few instances among the Creeks", "context_len": 42}}}, "detail": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 14, "context": "And very attentively everybody listened to the story , as I told it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached", "context_len": 128}}}, "marshall": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 0, "context": "Marshall , '' called Ed Kerr , as the college physician was seen hurrying across the campus , with the Jersey twins trailing along behind", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Marshall or one of his assistants", "context_len": 6}}}, "illustrated": {"freq": 97, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 89, "word_index": 47, "context": "But he could not make anything either of Byron or Cowper ; and he did not even try to read the little tree-calf volumes of Homer and Virgil which his father had in the versions of Pope and Dryden ; the small copperplates with which they were illustrated conveyed no suggestion to him", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "ILLUSTRATED", "context_len": 1}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "ILLUSTRATED BY HAROLD PIFFARD", "context_len": 4}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Howell had suggested 'The Vicar of Wakefield ' as being less solid for a tired mind than 'The French Revolution , ' lending her his own charmingly illustrated copy ; and Father had forgotten the farm and all his other worries in a hearty laugh over Moses and the spectacles , or Mrs", "context_len": 53}}}, "thou": {"freq": 391, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 202, "word_index": 40, "context": "However , yesterday , I saw the Lady Bedrulbudour , the Sultan 's daughter , as she went to the bath ; '' and he told her all that had happened to him , adding , `` And most like thou heardest the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 111, "context": "advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we will set over you guards to guard you ; but do not thou let slip this great honour , for that it hath fallen to none other than thyself", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 119, "word_index": 58, "context": "Accordingly , when she came up , he saw her and viewed her well , beyond his wish ; and from that time till now , O King of the Age , life hath not been pleasant to him [ 366 ] and he will e'en have me seek her of Thy Grace , [ 367 ] so thou mayst marry her with him , and I can not do away this conceit from his wit , for that the love of her hath gotten possession of his vitals , so that he saith to me , 'Know , O mother mine , that , except I attain my desire , assuredly I am a dead man", "context_len": 117}}}, "mirror": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 67, "context": "For the same reason he did his best , or his worst , to fall into a trance , in which he should know everything that was going on about him , all the preparations for his funeral , all the sorrow and lamentation , but should be unable to move or speak , and only be saved at the last moment by some one putting a mirror to his lips and finding a little blur of mist on it", "context_len": 80}}}, "publisher": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` It 's wonderful , my love , the interest he takes in it , '' she said ; `` I never saw anything like it in the whole course of my life , and for a publisher too ! But his idea is no less than this : When the lease of the next house falls in , we take it too , and break open doors , and have the two houses instead of one", "context_len": 77}}}, "colophon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ Illustration : ( publisher 's colophon ) ]", "context_len": 9}}}, "exigencies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "serial": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "You see , in those days , stories of the quality of those now published in the Brave and Bold Weekly were bound in cloth covers or else published little by little in boys \u2019 serial papers , under which circumstances each story was paid for at the rate of one dollar or more", "context_len": 54}}}, "publication": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 121, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}}}, "condensed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Although the recent war with Spain has aroused general interest and caused a great demand for literature relating to the subject , there has not as yet been published a condensed history of Spain for young people", "context_len": 37}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Beside these stones , which were kept constantly hot , they made Mahdrusa sit down , and while she still wept , her tears fell upon the stones , and a great vapor arose , which the sorcerers condensed upon clay vessels into drops of water as pure as crystal , and with them and the herbs that hung around , made a decoction so powerful that when they had forced Mahdrusa to drink it , she lost all power and reason , and her spirit lay passive in the hands of her tormentors", "context_len": 95}}}, "omitted": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 74, "context": "`` The Sultan `` goes round in the kiosk '' and seeing `` the casement ( shubbak ) , which Alaeddin had purposely left defective , without completion , '' said to the Vizier , `` Knowest thou the reason ( or cause ) of the lack of completion of this casement and its lattices ? '' ( shearihi , or quaere , `` [ this ] lattice , '' the copyist having probably omitted by mistake the diacritical points over the final ha )", "context_len": 85}}}, "portions": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 43, "context": "All being now ready , the gate in the palisade was thrown open , a conch- shell was blown , and the waiting inhabitants began to pour into the enclosure with all the eagerness and excitement of an audience crowding into the unreserved portions of a theatre , and in a very short time the great square was full , the front ranks pressing close up to a cordon of armed guards that had been drawn round the circle of posts", "context_len": 81}}}, "restored": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 63, "context": "and as Donald looked as though he thought he might , and as that was exactly the way he was expected to look , Marie-Celeste complacently continued : Well , first you go up a flight of steps , and you find yourself in a sort of vestibule ; and there 's a splendid portrait of the architect there -- the man who restored the old parts of the castle and added new parts to it and made it all beautiful as it is now ; and from this vestibule you go on and on from one grand room to another", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now , he said , as soon as silence was a little restored , the knights of the langues on board the prizes will send at once to the coaster on the left of the other two", "context_len": 37}}}, "chapter": {"freq": 933, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 933, "word_index": 20, "context": "But all this , as you can plainly see , has nothing whatever to do with the title of this chapter , so it behooves us , as the preachers used to say , to turn our back on Harold and the charms of the renowned old college , and our faces toward the ocean and a far-off land -- far off , that is , as far as Windsor and the English are concerned , but very near and dear to the hearts of some of the rest of us", "context_len": 91}}}, "xi": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 18, "context": "Amidst the shouts of the populace , who loved to look on the display , the Bishop Ednoth xi and the chief magistrates of the city received the monarch and his councillor in front of the church of Sts", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "XI FEROCIOUS FOES 86", "context_len": 4}}}, "list": {"freq": 128, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 126, "word_index": 18, "context": "The little old gentleman turned out to be a very learned don from Oxford , with a terrific list of letters at the end of his name , and in the course of a few days a representative arrived from the Bodleian Library with a request to be allowed to inspect the books ( which by this time had been removed to a corner of the big drawing-room ) , and after a considerable amount of talking and writing it was arranged that they should be sent to London at once to be sold by auction", "context_len": 96}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` No , no ; I am sure you are no evil doer , and , I am sure you can if you list , keep silence from such wild words", "context_len": 31}}}, "crows": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 65, "context": "Here in Maine you might take the bounty off bob-cats , an ' let 'em have their few sheep , an ' you might stand between the mink and the spawning trout , and if you want to put a check on the robins who make war on the cherries an ' strawberries , I guess it would be more sensible than chasing up the crows", "context_len": 66}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "If I could ' a ' caught one o ' them crows this mornin ' I 'd ' a ' hugged it an ' kissed it", "context_len": 26}}}, "south": {"freq": 102, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 68, "context": "As it wound through the streets in splendid array , the grand master and officials in all their robes of state , the knights in full armour and the mantles of the Order , while the inhabitants in gala costume lined the streets , windows , and housetops , the ladies waving scarves and scattering flowers down on the knights , the roar of great cannon on the south side of the city showed that the Turks had commenced the attack in another quarter", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 83, "context": "As Ama , taking her bow and arrows , tripped lightly away toward the forest , I proceeded to shin up the tree , and presently , after some labour , found myself among its topmost branches , which towered high above those of all the other trees in the neighbourhood , and -- it being by this time broad daylight -- obtained a magnificent view of the surrounding country , extending for , as I estimated , some forty miles toward the south and east , while toward the north and west the view was shut off by high hills , through which the river wound its way", "context_len": 109}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "De Soto arrived in Mavila on October 18th , and the whole distance from Co\u00e7a to Tuscalusa is computed by the Knight of Elvas at sixty leagues , the direction of the route being from north to south", "context_len": 38}}}, "advance": {"freq": 120, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 88, "word_index": 29, "context": "When [ 413 ] the Vizier heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 28, "context": "When the winter came on , Alexander and his army were about three or four hundred miles from home ; and , as he did not intend to advance much farther until the spring should open , he announced to the army that all those persons , both officers and soldiers who had been married within the year , might go home if they chose , and spend the winter with their brides , and return to the army in the spring", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "The principal motive for Indian wars being the conquest of scalps , slaves , plunder and hunting grounds , the Creeks , conscious of their great power , were not very particular in finding causes for warfare , and did not even advance specious reasons for declaring war", "context_len": 48}}}, "guard": {"freq": 185, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 153, "word_index": 59, "context": "Thanks be to Allah , the All Powerful , one of the men began , my lips are unsealed , and I can tell you the great news that our hour for escape from bondage is at hand ! We need not fear the warder there , he went on , as several eyes were turned apprehensively towards the guard , who , with his spear beside him , was leaning carelessly against the wall at the farther end , looking through the window into the courtyard ; he is with us", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 105, "context": "advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we will set over you guards to guard you ; but do not thou let slip this great honour , for that it hath fallen to none other than thyself", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "There was never an instant that she was off guard with regard to herself ; there was no time left in her busy life for reckless speeches and reckless deeds", "context_len": 30}}}, "solemn": {"freq": 98, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 91, "context": "I 'm goin ' to be a good girl now , an ' never tell no more lies nor steal , nor do anythin ' bad , ' but he jus ' said ever so grumpy , 'This is a cast down , no credit system store , ' but I was n't cas ' down , an ' I said , 'S'pose you was a lame man , an ' a bad little girl burnt up your crutches , how would you feel ? 'Then he looked kind o ' solemn , an ' said , 'Whose crutches was burnt up ? ' An ' I said , 'Mr", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 113, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "How solemn the moon was , staring down at her with that section of a face on which she fancied she saw an ear , the corner of a mouth , and one terrible , glistening eye", "context_len": 37}}}, "army": {"freq": 308, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 307, "word_index": 25, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "He heard the words , `` a tac ! a tac ! '' and knew the fight had been discovered by an army officer", "context_len": 24}}}, "soared": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 92, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}}}, "cawing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "pine": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 41, "context": "He strolled about the open space , pausing now and then to survey through his glasses the snowy peaks which towered far into the sky to the westward , or at the lower hills to the north , where the gnarled pine , the dwarfed cedar , the rushing torrents and the gorges made up one of the many wild regions which abound over thousands of square miles of area", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 110, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "You know how great is the mortality among the slaves -- how they pine away and die from no material malady that can be detected , but simply from hopelessness and weariness of life , aided , undoubtedly , in the case of the galley slaves , by sleeping in the damp night air after an exposure all day to the full heat of the sun", "context_len": 66}}}, "woods": {"freq": 312, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 312, "word_index": 73, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "spruces": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 34, "context": "Joe said the way to tell it was , right off the mouth of the cove there was a great , high rock , that came up out of the water , with three spruces on the top of it , and a little turf , but the sides were all bare ; and he said there were reefs and breakers all round it ; but I \u2019 ll bet , if we could find it , we could see the reefs break , and keep clear of them", "context_len": 89}}}, "exposed": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "They were cawing over the green pine woods of North Marsden , they were cawing over the black spruces of South Marsden , and in Middle Marsden , where the sun had melted the snow on a few exposed knolls , they were having a serious and chattering jubilation over their return to their summer haunts", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 59, "context": "The hostess who brought Dick 's bowl of onion broth at this moment destroyed his chance of feigning sleep , and he had to resign himself to endure his companion 's conversation which flowed on , garnished with oaths and cant phrases in three or four different languages , without any interruption , till by an unguarded movement Richard exposed his face to the light of the fire , and the stranger stared a moment , and then sprang up exclaiming , `` Body o ' me if it be not Measter Dick himself ! ''", "context_len": 96}}}, "knolls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "They were cawing over the green pine woods of North Marsden , they were cawing over the black spruces of South Marsden , and in Middle Marsden , where the sun had melted the snow on a few exposed knolls , they were having a serious and chattering jubilation over their return to their summer haunts", "context_len": 56}}}, "serious": {"freq": 181, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 181, "word_index": 7, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "jubilation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 48, "context": "They were cawing over the green pine woods of North Marsden , they were cawing over the black spruces of South Marsden , and in Middle Marsden , where the sun had melted the snow on a few exposed knolls , they were having a serious and chattering jubilation over their return to their summer haunts", "context_len": 56}}}, "haunts": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 12, "context": "Here and there Lilian ran , hunting in all Peggy 's favourite haunts -- now peeping into a hollow yew-tree , now peering at the top of a ladder , now rummaging in the tool-shed , then back through the sand-quarry into the stack-yard , where there was a very good chance that the young lady might be hidden away in some snug little hole among the hay ; but though Lilian got a tolerable amount of hay-seed into her hair , her efforts were fruitless , and she was just turning away , hot and out of breath , to give up the useless search , when the sound of a low , chuckling laugh attracted her to the barn", "context_len": 121}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "The Indians have a legend about its being bewitched , and about a pre-historic monster much larger than a moose who haunts its forests", "context_len": 24}}}, "ai": {"freq": 245, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 244, "word_index": 10, "context": "Grubs just fly down his red throat , and grasshoppers ai n't nowhere , but because he now and then lifts a hill o ' petetters , and pulls a mite o ' corn when it gets toothsome , and makes way once in so often with a fat chicken that 's a heap better out o ' the world than in it , the farmers is down on him , the Legislature wo n't protect him , and the crow -- man 's good friend -- gets shot by everybody and everything ! ''", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Look here , young 'un ; a lad as ca n't swim ai n't -- 'cordin ' to my ideas -- hardly worth the snuff of a candle", "context_len": 28}}}, "elfish": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Land ! ai n't they sweet ! '' muttered a little girl , who was herself almost as elfish and impish as a crow", "context_len": 25}}}, "impish": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Land ! ai n't they sweet ! '' muttered a little girl , who was herself almost as elfish and impish as a crow", "context_len": 25}}}, "crow": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 80, "context": "Grubs just fly down his red throat , and grasshoppers ai n't nowhere , but because he now and then lifts a hill o ' petetters , and pulls a mite o ' corn when it gets toothsome , and makes way once in so often with a fat chicken that 's a heap better out o ' the world than in it , the farmers is down on him , the Legislature wo n't protect him , and the crow -- man 's good friend -- gets shot by everybody and everything ! ''", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "What is most pleasing is that our boys did not crow as they might have done , -- their opponents were too utterly smashed", "context_len": 24}}}, "clasped": {"freq": 80, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 40, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Oh , this was to have been my wedding day , my wedding day ! '' and he clasped his hands in agony ; then the thought of his master -- his slain lord -- returned , and he cried , `` O Edmund ! my master , my dear master , so good , so gentle , yet so brave ; who else could slay him ? what fiend else than Edric , the murderer Edric ? That they should think I , or any one else than Edric , could have done such a deed , such an evil deed ! ''", "context_len": 105}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "That night Hannah Searles slept again with a child clasped to her bosom", "context_len": 13}}}, "spruce": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 9, "context": "Charlie had made a bridge of poles from the spruce to the pine , and used to sit there , when the wind blew , till the tree shook so much that it frightened him , or the eagles came to their nest ; but , after a while , they became so accustomed to him as to take fish from the limbs where he placed it", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 108, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Breakfast passed without incident , and the plebes were marched back to barracks , there breaking ranks , and scattering to quarters to `` spruce up '' for inspection", "context_len": 29}}}, "thicket": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 31, "context": "It was perfectly good form , also , to get a boy , if you could , to shut his eyes , and then lead him into a mud-puddle or a thicket of briers or nettles , or to fool him in any heartless way , such as promising to pump easy when he put his mouth to the pump-spout , and then coming down on the pump-handle with a rush that flooded him with water and sent him off blowing the tide from his nostrils like a whale", "context_len": 89}}}, "chilled": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 36, "context": "I remember seeing the lieutenant , the bos \u2019 n , Greville Drake , and about a score of the men , but , huddled on the lee side of the bulwarks , I remained , chilled to the bone and drenched by the drifting spray , hardly conscious of my peril or the presence of my shipmates in distress", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "Glad was I , although the distance was not great , to see the lights of the priory , and to dry my chilled limbs and wet garments before the fire in the common room while I told my brethren the tidings of the night , and the suspicions which we entertained", "context_len": 52}}}, "peered": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 103, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "priest": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 8, "context": "They knew the fellows who were helping the priest ; one of them was the boy who stood on his head till he had to have it shaved ; they would have liked to mock him then and there for wearing a petticoat , and most of them had the bitterest scorn and hate for Catholics in their hearts ; but they were afraid of the sacristan , and they behaved very well as long as they were in the church ; but as soon as they got out they whooped and yelled , and stoned the sacristan when he ran after them", "context_len": 103}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "We gather from his statements , that at Odshi-ap\u00f3fa or `` Hickory Ground , '' which is a white town also , the `` priest , or fire-maker of the town '' had the privilege of determining the days of the busk , and that in doing so he was led by the ripening of the maize-crop and by the growth of the cassine-shrub", "context_len": 64}}}, "gossiping": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 65, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}}}, "village": {"freq": 348, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 345, "word_index": 64, "context": "of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The peculiar and very interesting feature of the affair is that in this Rajput village a little flock has been gathered just by Native agency", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "to a little better education , perhaps his little village school may prove not a bad idea , for the scholars would learn what is good from him , though they could only have elementary teaching", "context_len": 36}}}, "parishioner": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "Time after time she returned to her hiding-place with a muttered , `` No good ! '' She allowed a priest to go by , two gossiping women on their way from the village to spend a day in the country , a minister hurrying to the sick-bed of a parishioner , and several loaded wood-sleds , but finally a hilarious jingle drew her hopefully from her retreat", "context_len": 68}}}, "loaded": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 41, "context": "Two young theows , whose fathers had gone to the war , but who had been left behind as being too young to share its dangers , although in the flush of early youth , accompanied them , and were soon loaded with the lighter game their masters had killed , while a deer they had slain was hung in the trees , where a wolf could not reach it , and where wayfarers were not likely to pass until the sportsmen should return for their own", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "One of the apprentices in the printing-office kept a shot-gun loaded beside the press while he was rolling , and whenever he caught the soft twitter that the doves make with their wings , he rushed out with his gun and knocked over two or three of them", "context_len": 48}}}, "hilarious": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "Time after time she returned to her hiding-place with a muttered , `` No good ! '' She allowed a priest to go by , two gossiping women on their way from the village to spend a day in the country , a minister hurrying to the sick-bed of a parishioner , and several loaded wood-sleds , but finally a hilarious jingle drew her hopefully from her retreat", "context_len": 68}}}, "jingle": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 61, "context": "Time after time she returned to her hiding-place with a muttered , `` No good ! '' She allowed a priest to go by , two gossiping women on their way from the village to spend a day in the country , a minister hurrying to the sick-bed of a parishioner , and several loaded wood-sleds , but finally a hilarious jingle drew her hopefully from her retreat", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "But he knows very well I have few pennies to jingle in my pocket , for he has fewer yet", "context_len": 20}}}, "hopefully": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "Time after time she returned to her hiding-place with a muttered , `` No good ! '' She allowed a priest to go by , two gossiping women on their way from the village to spend a day in the country , a minister hurrying to the sick-bed of a parishioner , and several loaded wood-sleds , but finally a hilarious jingle drew her hopefully from her retreat", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 42, "context": "She did not speak about it , or show it to anyone , but after that not a further grumble escaped her , and she managed to find such a bright side to the question , and talked so often and so hopefully of the future , that Father said she was as good as a tonic , and began to find his little daughter such a comfort to him , and so different to the old thoughtless Peggy of former days , that I scarcely know how he would have got through that trying time without her", "context_len": 98}}}, "retreat": {"freq": 73, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 55, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}, "VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 105, "context": "that if he wished to marry his daughter he could do it without his consent ; as to the ransom , he was not in want of money ; in respect to Darius 's offering to give him up all west of the Euphrates , it was absurd for a man to speak of giving what was no longer his own ; that he had crossed too many seas in his military expeditions , since he left Macedon , to feel any concern about the rivers that he might find in his way ; and that he should continue to pursue Darius wherever he might retreat in search of safety and protection , and he had no fear but that he should find and conquer him at last", "context_len": 128}}}, "screwed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "glittering": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 3, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "It seemed above and below -- to redden the skies , the frozen treetops with their glittering snow wreaths , and the smooth surface beneath alike", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "He had purloined David 's scarf from the saddle-room , and dropped it into the horse-trough , had filled Bobby 's hat with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread", "context_len": 128}}}, "newcomer": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 1, "context": "The newcomer was a burly , good-natured farmer , who , after giving the three men a cheery salutation , which they returned surlily enough , sat down opposite to me", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The newcomer sank its teeth deep into Polulu \u2019 s flank while at the same time a claw of its hindleg tore a strip of the puma \u2019 s hide", "context_len": 30}}}, "murdered": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Because I know the secrets of the prison house -- knew them before the Danes had murdered the once happy dwellers in this garden of England , which they have made a howling wilderness ; hence I escaped the wrath of the furious parricide , whom the saints destroy , with ease , and laughed in security at their vain efforts to take me ; but we must waste no time ; it yet wants five hours to daybreak ; within those five hours we must reach the opposite shore", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "His headpiece and armour received the blows as lightly as if they were from a straw , and with an oath he smote me heavily on the chest with the butt of his pistol , so that I reeled , fell backward across the body of my murdered sire , and struck my head on the frosty road", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Go , wretched man ! bring back the murdered Camoens ! Go , make thy peace", "context_len": 16}}}, "addressed": {"freq": 105, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 105, "word_index": 38, "context": "The boy was playing before his door , when a constable came by with his rifle on his shoulder , and asked him if he had seen any unmuzzled dogs about ; and partly from pride at being addressed by a constable , partly from a nervous fear of refusing to answer , and partly from a childish curiosity to see what would happen , he said , `` Yes ; one over there by the pork-house", "context_len": 77}}}, "stepped": {"freq": 197, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 195, "word_index": 2, "context": "As he stepped through the gateway one of the pirates sprang past him , but as he did so , a figure leapt out from beyond the wall , and plunged a dagger into his back , while at the same moment , by cutting down another pirate , Gervaise rid himself of one of his assailants in front ; but as he did so , he himself received a severe wound on the left shoulder from Hassan , who , before he could again raise his weapon , sprang upon him , and tried to hurl him to the ground", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "For a moment he felt inclined to turn back , but he remembered that his mother had told him always to finish anything he undertook to accomplish , and so stepped briskly forward quite determined to find Mr", "context_len": 38}}}, "fence": {"freq": 82, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 82, "word_index": 51, "context": "Then the boys saw him going about the streets , pale and tremulous , in a second-hand suit of his benefactor 's clothes , and fighting hard against the tempter that beset him on every side in that town ; and then some day they saw him dead drunk in a fence corner ; and they did not understand how seven devils worse than the first had entered in the place which had been swept and garnished for them", "context_len": 79}}}, "mister": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I 'll tell you , mister , '' broke forth Lem , after another moment or two of silence , `` I was awful sorry when I heard what Doll did to them gardens after the little gals begged me out ; but you see she did n't know it , and she thought I was took to jail", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` We did fix it up fustrate ; did n't we mister ? ''", "context_len": 14}}}, "bobbing": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "He stepped out to the veranda and there was Cassowary dancing up and down and looking more like a bird than ever in her tight fitting suit with its bobbing tail", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "Away went the light oar over the surface , bobbing down at one end , and raising the blade in the air , while , putting the other over the stern , Harry stood up , full of excitement , and began sculling after the novel travelling float , when a wild cry for help , that seemed to send a shudder through his frame , came from behind him over the surface of the sea", "context_len": 76}}}, "snowy": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 1, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}, "UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 35, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "Do you wonder that the sight drove her frantic ? That those who stood beside her could scarcely prevent her from throwing herself into those waters which covered all she loved best ? Then came a long and terrible illness , during which that dark hair changed to snowy white", "context_len": 50}}}, "observed": {"freq": 232, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 226, "word_index": 64, "context": "\u2019 importe ! the man , the man remains the same ! These locks have proved the keys to a Lady \u2019 s heart e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon express for", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "somewhat drily observed afterwards , she very cleverly avoided hurting herself by her blows , and none of her hair seemed to come out with all the apparent tearing", "context_len": 29}}}, "peeping": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Coronation ! Where did that noise come from ? Hello , wood-lark , '' as he observed the little girl peeping at him through the fence , `` is there a hawk in your nest ? ''", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 15, "context": "Here and there Lilian ran , hunting in all Peggy 's favourite haunts -- now peeping into a hollow yew-tree , now peering at the top of a ladder , now rummaging in the tool-shed , then back through the sand-quarry into the stack-yard , where there was a very good chance that the young lady might be hidden away in some snug little hole among the hay ; but though Lilian got a tolerable amount of hay-seed into her hair , her efforts were fruitless , and she was just turning away , hot and out of breath , to give up the useless search , when the sound of a low , chuckling laugh attracted her to the barn", "context_len": 121}}}, "hawk": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "My wife was distressed about the children , especially the girl , to think she should be growing up in such wickedness and misery ; and last winter she fixed up a suit of warm clothes for her , and coaxing her into the house with a deal of trouble , for she is as wild as a hawk , she dressed her in them , and promised to give her and her brother a good meal every day if they would come quietly to the house and get it", "context_len": 90}}}, "flicking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` I 've got an awful pretty name , '' he replied , flicking his whip over the snow-bank beside him , `` too pretty to tell", "context_len": 27}}}, "pertinaciously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Who be you ? '' she asked , pertinaciously", "context_len": 10}}}, "creamery": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then the ghastliness vanished , and she approvingly took in the picture , -- the fat young creamery shark standing over the white cat and rubbing her with his toe , the firelight on the wall and snowy table , and the big lamp on the mantel", "context_len": 47}}}, "shark": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 69, "context": "`` To school ! '' said the little girl , `` to learn more -- to know how to speak proper ! Oh , oh , I 'm mos ' too happy to live ! Hank Dillson , I think you 're the mos ' beautiful man that was ever made ! '' and , dropping her sugar-bowl on the shelf , she seized a hand of the ex-creamery shark , and warmly pressed it between her little lean palms", "context_len": 80}}}, "sharks": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 1, "context": "The sharks still kept off ; our gunners ceased to fire , and the running bowline was dropped from our catheads for the man to be hauled on board , when , within fifty yards from us , he suddenly disappeared , and over the spot darted yet another huge shark that , unobserved , had lurked under our bows", "context_len": 60}}}, "soberly": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 44, "context": "To go with Father upon one of these mountain excursions had been the dream of Peggy and Bobby 's lives , so it was with very gay faces that , their fishing-baskets full of provisions slung over their shoulders , and old Rover trotting soberly behind , they started off on their eight-mile tramp up the hillside", "context_len": 57}}}, "dote": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` They dote on it", "context_len": 5}}}, "chasing": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 63, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}}}, "oily": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "I ca n't bear to have the Romney picture go , either , because it 's just like Peggy ; it might have been painted from her ' -- the vision of the family portraits and the armour of her ancestors being turned over and valued by oily gentlemen of the Hebrew persuasion adding a double sting to the trial", "context_len": 60}}}, "befitting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 122, "context": "on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me , died out , and I began to prepare myself , as best I could , to meet whatever fate might have in store for me with the fortitude befitting a Christian and an Englishman", "context_len": 128}}}, "purpose": {"freq": 206, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 185, "word_index": 54, "context": "It seems that in the old days , when Lewis Carroll loved to play host to the children , they would often come to take afternoon tea in his lodgings , and then likely as not , if the light were good , he would spirit them into a 'room fitted up for the purpose and take their pictures ; and then , if they promised to be good and not to bother , they might follow him into the queer-smelling little room where he made the pictures come out , and they would be permitted to have a look at the dripping glass plate , from which they could seldom make head nor tail , held up against the dark-room 's lantern for inspection", "context_len": 125}, "VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 109, "context": "the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to demand her of the Sultan her father in the way of law and righteousness", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "She had given me fair warning , but she should have known that it was my purpose to carry out the orders of General Forrest ; and if I was to be warned at all she should have told me the precise nature of the danger", "context_len": 46}}}, "cunningly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "There were statues of marble , tables cunningly carved and inlaid with gold and precious stones , curious medallions and intaglios , suits of armor and swords of the finest steel , a vast number of objects of art of all kinds scattered about in profusion", "context_len": 46}}}, "runaway": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 63, "context": "It stipulated a cession of the lands between the Savannah and Altamaha rivers , and of some islands on the Atlantic coast , to the British ; it further stipulated promises to enter into a commercial treaty at a later date , to place themselves under the general government of Great Britain , to live in peace with the colonies , to capture runaway slaves and deliver them at Charleston , Savannah or Palachukla garrison for a consideration", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "That young one has got the face of a government mule , but I 'm used to mules , and when she asked me I said , `` Pears to me , little girl , you favour a runaway , and I ai n't got no room for runaways in this narrow rig , 'specially as I 'm taking a bundle of clothing to my dear old father ' -- likewise a young pig , '' he added , as there was a decided squeal from between his feet", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The boys knew vaguely that his family helped runaway slaves on their way North , and in a community that was for the most part bitterly pro-slavery these negroes were held in a sort of respect for their courageous fidelity to their race", "context_len": 43}}}, "orphans": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 93, "context": "`` The soul that sinneth it shall die '' -- '' For whosoever shall keep the whole law , and yet offend in one point , he is guilty of all '' -- '' Keep thyself pure '' -- '' For without are dogs , and sorcerers , and murderers , and idolaters , and whosoever loveth and maketh a lie '' -- that meant without the city , the beautiful city of gold where her mother probably was , and many of her unknown relatives , and where all good matrons , orphans , and `` lady-boards '' went", "context_len": 100}}}, "pays": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 35, "context": "That invention fell into the hands of Asher Rand , who , on learning its value , was sorely tempted and kept its existence a secret , finally disposing of it to a concern that pays a royalty upon it of three thousand dollars yearly", "context_len": 45}}}, "fend": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "With his teeth set , he hugged the ball beneath one arm , the other thrown out stiffly to fend off a dark figure he saw coming at him ; and he left the would-be tackler jarred , dazed and knocked to his knees", "context_len": 44}}}, "sum": {"freq": 145, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 138, "word_index": 101, "context": "Again , in 1889 , when a dear friend of mine , Pastor and Teacher in the United States of North America , with whom I had come out to India in 1869 , had decided to return to India as a Missionary , in order to join and to help me in the multifarious work at Clarkabad , and he found that the money in hand was insufficient to pay for his and his family \u2019 s voyage from Germany , and Miss Tucker heard of it , she immediately sent me 100 , with the direction to forward that sum to him , on condition that he had not left Germany again for America", "context_len": 116}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "I have received from you a letter , asking me to send you twenty thousand dollars , alleging that some years since I promised to give you that sum upon the death of my uncle", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "The grand master had advanced Gervaise a sum equal to half a year 's income of his commandery , and with this he had purchased a stock of the best wines , and various other luxuries , to supplement the rations supplied from the funds of the Order to knights when at sea", "context_len": 53}}}, "newspaper": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 32, "context": "He never let on that he suspected for a moment that he was not entirely alone , but , walking over to a tree stump , where , spread out on a newspaper , was the remains of a lunch , he acted delighted at the discovery , picked up a hunk of bread in one hand , a piece of cheese in the other , and , throwing himself on the green sward at full length , proceeded to munch the eatables , with every semblance of satisfaction", "context_len": 89}}}, "skip": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` And if , '' continued the `` bunting-tosser '' , carried away by his brilliant brain-wave , `` Panjie was to fall in with the physical-exercise party , an ' skip round the ship 'arf a dozen times afore breakfast , I 'll allow he 'd scoff his 'ard tack without a murmur", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 62, "context": "No one can deny that some of the said volumes do contain a large amount of direct preaching \u2019 ; not merely of life-lessons , interwoven with the story in such wise that the one could not be read and the other missed , but rather of little sermons so alternating with the story that a child might read the latter and skip the former", "context_len": 65}}}, "passenger": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 91, "context": "Yes , you said something like that to the captain ; and as though she would have given all the world if he had n't , but I did n't mean to be bold really , only I felt so sorry for Chris ; and then she proceeded to tell , as coherently as her emotions would allow , of her unexpected encounter with her old friend , and how dreadful it would have been if they could not have seen anything of each other just because Chris was a second-cabin passenger , and of how she had mustered all her courage and gone straight to the captain to see what could be done about it", "context_len": 116}}}, "granddaddy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` The very first Christmas what I makes my fortunes I vill put a four-masted Yankee ship in your stockings , boy , mit stores and crew ready for sea , and this granddaddy of yours walkin ' up and down the poop , so ? ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "siree": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` No , siree , but I 've got somethin ' mos ' as good as a granddaddy , an ' I 'd thank you to keep a straight tongue when you speak of him", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I don \u2019 t b \u2019 lieve he put them things there , Abel Hubbard no , siree ! I dunno how they come to be there , but that boy never stole \u2019 em", "context_len": 35}}}, "offending": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 29, "context": "Meantime , what had not that audacious Albert ventured ! The rapid and indignant pace at which he had sought to put as much space as possible between the offending Marie-Celeste and himself had brought him in a trice to the foot of the double flight of steps that ascend from the garden to the terrace", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Once his men failed in their attack on an offending tribe ; and , believing they had been misled by the Sheik , wanted to punish him ; but Gordon saw the other side of the man 's character -- '' He was a brave patriotic man , '' he said ; `` and I shall let him go ''", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Where did that come from ? '' demanded Sid , getting up from the couch with an effort and striding over to the offending emblem", "context_len": 26}}}, "enjoyably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The young man put the offending tongue in his cheek , and chuckled enjoyably as the small , elfish figure disappeared in the wood", "context_len": 24}}}, "wiggling": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "She loved birds ; she told me afterward ; it was perfectly delicious to feel a little feathery ball wiggling between your claws , but she said , `` I wanted to live , so I gave them up and as I just had to hunt something , I got after the things that even my masters hunt , namely fishes", "context_len": 61}}}, "weasel": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 5, "context": "We try to get a weasel , but have not succeeded yet", "context_len": 12}}}, "deposited": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 67, "context": "Wilfrid at Aescendune , it seems in some sort my duty , following the example of many worthy brethren , to write some account of the origin and history of the priory over which it has pleased God to make me overseer , and to note , as occasion serves from time to time , such passing events as seem worthy of remembrance ; which record , deposited in the archives of the house , may preserve our memory when our bodies are but dust , and other brethren fill our places in the choir", "context_len": 95}}}, "war": {"freq": 319, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 319, "word_index": 20, "context": "She laid down her work to wipe her eyes , and , observing two of her sons grappling in fraternal war at the house corner , she arose to cuff each one impartially , exclaiming , `` Ea , muchachos ! You fight before my very eyes , eh ? Take that ! and that ! '' Waddling reluctantly back to her sewing , she saw Lola standing in the white-pillared porch of the big adobe house beyond , and a gleam of inspiration crossed the se\u00f1ora 's dark , fat face", "context_len": 92}}}, "carted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Perhaps to impress the citizens of Philadelphia with his industry , he carted his supplies from the wharf in a wheelbarrow , wearing his leather apron", "context_len": 26}}}, "clicked": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "The wind howled and whistled around the house , the sharp snow crystals clicked against the panes , but as Edna crept under the covers she could feel only thankful that she had this shelter and was soon asleep with Nettie beside her already in the land of Dreams", "context_len": 49}}}, "rig": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 52, "context": "That young one has got the face of a government mule , but I 'm used to mules , and when she asked me I said , `` Pears to me , little girl , you favour a runaway , and I ai n't got no room for runaways in this narrow rig , 'specially as I 'm taking a bundle of clothing to my dear old father ' -- likewise a young pig , '' he added , as there was a decided squeal from between his feet", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "Every boy who knows the lure of exploring and who loves to rig up huts and caves and tree-houses to fortify himself against imaginary enemies will enjoy these books , for they give a vivid chronicle of the doings and inventions of a group of boys who are shipwrecked and have to make themselves snug and safe in tropical islands where the dangers are too real for play", "context_len": 68}}}, "youngster": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 41, "context": "The scent of the ursus species is by no means as fine as that of many other animals , but this one unquestionably was on the track of something and it looked very much as if that something was an American youngster crouching behind a boulder a few rods off and scared almost out of his wits", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Well , now , let Harold , who is pretty well out of the notion of Ted already , come to hear of this last scrape , and , youngster as he is , I believe he 'd throw him over ; and Ted , you know , would n't stand any nonsense of that sort and would tell Harold 'to go his own way and welcome , ' and who knows what the upshot of that would be ! If Ted does not feel he must make an effort to lead a different sort of life for Harold 's sake , he may come to the conclusion that the thing 's not worth trying", "context_len": 115}}}, "freeze": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Striped -- she had the impudence to steal it from the matron , and leave a note saying she did it because her jacket was locked up , and she was afraid her dog would freeze -- I 'm under a great obligation to you , sir", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "It can \u2019 t continner , he added , so we must make the best of it while we \u2019 ve got it ; we \u2019 re getting into the mountains , and though it \u2019 s about summer , we \u2019 ll catch some squalls that \u2019 ll freeze the nose onto yer faces", "context_len": 55}}}, "toward": {"freq": 650, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 650, "word_index": 34, "context": "The party who had brought me to this remote spot were of an entirely different race from those among whom I now found myself , and the fact that we were making our way toward what was obviously the aristocratic part of the town , coupled with the expressive conversation carried on by the leader of my custodians with three or four individuals who had joined us , led me to surmise -- although of course I did not understand a word of what was said -- that I had been brought up the river as a peace- offering or something of that sort , which conclusion was again the reverse of reassuring", "context_len": 113}}}, "overspread": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "A shadow overspread 'Tilda Jane 's face , and seeing it , the boy said , impulsively , `` Stop here a minute -- I want to speak to papa , '' and he rushed away", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "The next instant he reappeared ; but though he kept his head above the water , his legs were held by the weeds , and a look of horror overspread his face when he realized the danger of his position", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "At first they looked pleased as well as interested , but when it was made plain to them that some stranger had done the very thing on which they had set their hearts , a look of blank dismay and disappointment overspread their faces", "context_len": 44}}}, "passion": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 75, "context": "the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to demand her of the Sultan her father in the way of law and righteousness", "context_len": 128}}}, "ye": {"freq": 392, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 177, "word_index": 38, "context": "A red varmint never takes chances when he has a show of getting what he wants without it ; there ain \u2019 t \u2019 nough of \u2019 em to ride up and open fire ; don \u2019 t ye see that if they hold off till darkness , believing as how we haven \u2019 t an idee they \u2019 re within a thousand miles , and we ain \u2019 t keeping a lookout , they believe they can play thunder with us ?", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 139, "word_index": 57, "context": "But O Sir , how Miss Sophy trembled and turned as white as a lily , and Miss Ratty stamped and sent me to the village -- at that hour , your honour , company in the house -- the ground covered with frost -- I subject to the rheumatics -- and what for , d \u2019 ye think ? to get her twopenceworth of shoe-ribbon , your honour ; and when I brought it , would you believe it ? -- she roared out that it was too narrow and sent me back again", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 4, "context": "The bottomless pit awaits ye , '' threatened Sandy , and then , one at a time , the four were carefully lowered over the side of the wagon , down into some depths , as they supposed , but in reality only a short distance , so strangely are distances rendered when one is blindfolded", "context_len": 56}}}, "baddie": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "' An ' it seemed I 'd come to the bear 's den , an ' there was a soft bed , an ' I just lay down , an ' was goin ' to sleep when I thought , 'Mebbe if I sleep , some little bird 'll tell him I 'm a baddie , an ' he 'll eat me up , ' an ' I felt just awful ; then I forgot everythin ' till I woke up here -- I guess I 'm obliged to you", "context_len": 90}}}, "obliged": {"freq": 172, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 170, "word_index": 48, "context": "The wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Oh , I should be so much obliged if you would post a letter for me , '' said Florence", "context_len": 21}}}, "dryly": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 11, "context": "Albert looked at Marie-Celeste a little whimsically , and then said dryly : Well , I don ' know much about what 's happened here , and I s'ould n't sink jus ' an American little girl would know so very much eider", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": ", which the teacher gave him , perhaps because Lexington may have been his native town ; but the history must have been very dryly told , for not a fact of it remained in the boy 's mind", "context_len": 39}}}, "inexorably": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Fall to , '' he said , inexorably , `` eat your bacon and beans , an ' be thankful you 've got 'em", "context_len": 25}}}, "tole": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` You 've stole pies , an ' tole lies , an ' fed dogs an ' cats on the sly", "context_len": 21}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "Shagbark tole me how dem critters chaw and stomp and bite a feller ; I knowed dat if I brunged down dis one , it would make de oder two so mad dey \u2019 d come at me afore I could load up ag \u2019 in , and you wouldn \u2019 t hab any Jeth any more", "context_len": 57}}}, "dogs": {"freq": 164, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 163, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` So it was agreed that her spirit should dwell as a prairie dog , and before long out sprang one from a reeking cauldron of herbs , and they took it to the holes of the prairie dogs and left it there , placing beside it a terrible serpent , that all others might be afraid to approach it , and an owl at the door , as a sentinel that would stand looking patiently for an enemy both night and day , and never breathe to the gossips around her the tale of the princess that was prisoned within", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "So heavy , indeed , lay the burden of sin upon him that he envied the very dogs , wishing that he could change places with them", "context_len": 27}}}, "cats": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 42, "context": "Then there was Pixie , the pony , who would come whinnying up from the further side of a field to poke her soft nose into the children 's pockets for pieces of bread or lumps of sugar ; there were numerous cats who lived in the barns and stables , and Tabbyskins , the stately gray Persian , who usually sat sunning herself on the pigsty wall , keeping a strict eye on naughty Jack , who was wont to harry her if he got the chance", "context_len": 88}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "reverie": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Come out of it ! '' hailed a jolly voice , and Bart was disturbed in his pleasant reverie by the appearance of Darry and Bob Haven", "context_len": 28}}}, "fields": {"freq": 95, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 95, "word_index": 21, "context": "The wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his", "context_len": 128}}}, "ketch": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 52, "context": "Then I tried to stay awake , so I could carry him out early in the mornin ' , but did n't I fall to sleep , an ' the first thing I knowed there was the matron a-spearin ' me with her eyes , an she put out her hand to ketch the dog , an ' he up an ' bit her , an ' then there was trouble", "context_len": 71}}}, "gleefully": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` The wind is certainly chilly , '' she replied , and then , moved by embarrassment , or stirred by the motherly instinct that constitutes more than half the charm of womanhood , she leaned over and tucked the lap-robe about my knees , and then fell back in her place , laughing gleefully , as a child might have laughed", "context_len": 62}}}, "diminutive": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 84, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Pails clattered , water sluiced along the diminutive after deck", "context_len": 10}}}, "witch": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 41, "context": "How came that picture in the dwelling of Japazy , the half-breed ? Why had a similar picture been in the hut of Sobrinini ? What mysterious link was there between the lovely original of that picture and Sobrinini , the witch woman , Japazy , the master of Jaguar Island , Jojasta , the medicine man of the Moving Mountain , and Casson , his friend and former protector ? Somewhere , some time , those residents of the Amazonian jungle had known the mysterious Bartow , his wife , Laura , and perhaps the little child named Bonny", "context_len": 100}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` The Happys had a very happy thing happen to them witch delited them very much", "context_len": 16}}}, "clasping": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 59, "context": "The smile relieved matters somewhat , and Marie-Celeste , setting the little tray on the ground , picked up the cup-custard , which had suffered nothing by its fall , and putting it back in its place on the tray , took a seat in the corner of the rug , to which Ted motioned her , and then clasping her two hands round her knees , asked in a tone of most earnest inquiry , Now tell me , Cousin Theodore , why do you do things like this ?", "context_len": 91}}}, "lap": {"freq": 113, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 110, "word_index": 49, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 73, "context": "They went up the hill and up and up until they came to a place where they suddenly found themselves on the edge of a green hollow , and the minute they saw it the Good Wolf cried out , `` There's something we want , '' and trotted down as fast as he could to a big , clear pool which lay at the bottom of the hollow , and began to lap quickly", "context_len": 75}}}, "countenance": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 51, "word_index": 106, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "'What mad folly is this ? Really , Lilian , I am astonished that you countenance such wild proceedings ! Peggy I knew was a sad tomboy , but I thought you , at least , were the sensible member of the family , and would try to train the younger ones into more civilized habits", "context_len": 56}}}, "shrewder": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Her countenance was so much older and shrewder in some phases than in others that the young man was puzzled to guess her age", "context_len": 24}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "If he had been shrewder , it would have occurred to him that a prosperous business-man , such as Richmond claimed to be , was unusually anxious for a small accommodation", "context_len": 31}}}, "phases": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "There are few more fascinating phases of colonial history than that which tells the wanderings and adventures of the two judges who , because they sat in judgment over that royal criminal , Charles the First of England , were hunted out of England into hiding in New England , and there remained , a mystery and fugitives , in their celebrated cave in New Haven Colony", "context_len": 67}}}, "puzzled": {"freq": 114, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 3, "context": "He had been puzzled when , four days before , he had received an order from the grand master that Ahmet , a servitor in the auberge of the English langue , should be permitted to pass the night in his house , with authority to move freely and without question , at any hour , in the courtyard of the gaol , and to depart at any hour , secretly and without observation , by the private gate", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 74, "context": "But as it happened , the warmth of the wrapping , the influence of the spirit that had been poured liberally down his throat , and the chafing , combined with his naturally strong animal power to revive him from the state of insensibility into which he had fallen , and long before they reached the granite pier of the little harbour his eyes had opened , and he was staring in a peculiarly puzzled way at Mark Penelly , who still knelt beside him in the double character of medical man and nurse", "context_len": 94}}}, "shawl": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 13, "context": "You were bending forward , doing something for your mother , putting a shawl about her or something , and you just gave the tiniest bit of a smile , and I saw a gleam of your teeth , and I looked at your grey eyes ; and , upon my word , it was all over with me", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "When she saw that he was asleep , and not before , the woman paused to wrap her own dirty shawl a little over him", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "She wrapped her old shawl round little Roy , and forgetting even to close her cellar door , went out", "context_len": 20}}}, "resigned": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` I have been with many people suffering as your mother suffers , but I never before came across any one quite so gentle , so resigned , so happy , so peaceful , -- it is the peace of God", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 71, "context": "Meanwhile I had practically abandoned my attempts to effect my escape , for the time being at least ; for the conviction had at length been forced upon me that neither Banda nor his people would ever willingly let me go , and that , therefore , before engaging in any further attempts , I must contrive to disarm suspicion completely , and create the impression that I had at length resigned myself to live out my life in this remote African town , and with savages only for my companions", "context_len": 91}}}, "lem": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now , said Ben , handing his weapon to Noel Brooke , lem me see what you can do !", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Lem grabbed up a handful , and forcibly stuffed it into Bart 's mouth", "context_len": 14}}}, "sneak": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` I say , '' said George , slapping Guy on the shoulder , the moment after his father bade them `` good-night '' and went to his wagon , telling them to go to theirs , `` I say , I have got the best thing to tell you , and we 'll have the greatest fun , if you do n't turn sneak and try to get out of it", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 21, "context": "No , he won \u2019 t ; he ain \u2019 t such a fool ; he \u2019 ll make a big sneak to the left and get past \u2019 em ; if it was among the mountains , he wouldn \u2019 t have half the chance , but he knows what to do and he \u2019 ll do it , as sure as ye are knee high to a grasshopper", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 12, "context": "I can \u2019 t say he showed any sign of wishing to sneak out , and when I remark that his uncle didn \u2019 t have any trouble in lugging him off , I suppose I exaggerate , but I want to make sure the scamp doesn \u2019 t find an excuse for dodging a square , stand up fight", "context_len": 60}}}, "delightedly": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 40, "context": "Ah , now , I have had a fine meal , a luxurious meal , fit for a king no , fit for a bear ! And the bear rubbed his front paw over his well-filled tummy and again growled delightedly to himself some more , saying over and over again : Delicious ; such a meal !", "context_len": 58}}}, "skimming": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 31, "context": "At first they had all floundered hopelessly about , and it had taken some practice even to slide a few steps ; but long before they had reached the stage of skimming over the frozen surface at the rate of eight or ten miles an hour , which had been their fond ambition , the snow had melted into moist and dirty slush , which was particularly trying , as they had just decided to make a sledge out of an old packing-case , and drag Lilian round the pasture", "context_len": 90}}}, "grand": {"freq": 309, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 309, "word_index": 64, "context": "Bobby 's hat with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread and half-picked bones which the thief had concealed in some cunning corner inside a manger or under the roof", "context_len": 128}}}, "coachman": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 45, "context": "David could not be spared to go with them , but all the children had been taught to drive , and even Bobby had a firm hand on the reins , and knew the rules of the road as well as many a more experienced coachman ; and I think , too , that Pixie had a sense of her responsibilities , and could be trusted not to get the wheel locked with a passing waggon , or to race too furiously down a steep hill , whatever feats her drivers might urge her to perform", "context_len": 96}}}, "roan": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "There was a very grand sleigh approaching him , with a befurred coachman on the seat driving a pair of roan horses , and behind him a gray-haired lady smothered in handsome robes", "context_len": 33}}}, "smothered": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 49, "context": "It had originally been a grove of nut trees , and though some of these still flourished and bore nuts that had not their equal for size and flavour in all the country-side , they had for the most part been strangled by blackberry bushes and briers , and smothered by masses of wild clematis", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` But you were n't at all likely to be in his place or any other where arrows were flying , '' interrupted Gus , with a laugh , which quickly subsided into a smothered titter as George looked at him , with the remark : `` You had better mind your bones", "context_len": 53}}}, "robes": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 88, "context": "But still she went on , but only slowly , because of the great press of people around her ; and suddenly an icy wind passed over them , and all fell to the earth shivering and terror-stricken , for they had never felt cold before , and they looked up to the mountain , and lo ! upon the very summit , at the door of the deserted wigwam , stood a terrible figure , clothed in white , and having a face as white as his robes , and his hair was like the long crystals that hang from the roofs of caves that the water goes through , and his eyes were like two great diamonds , white , yet blazing like the sun", "context_len": 127}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "A strange minstrel , a young gleeman , had been admitted that night -- one whose chain and robes bespoke him of the privileged class -- and he sang in a voice which thrilled all the revellers into awed silence", "context_len": 40}}}, "pathetically": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Really , said Ted at last , able to stand it no longer , and looking pathetically toward Chris , I do n't mean to be inquisitive , but do I understand you that the father of your friend , Marie-Celeste , is coming here to your cottage to recruit from some illness , and that you plan to entertain him by putting him to work on the farm ?", "context_len": 70}}}, "blinked": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}}}, "excitedly": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 35, "context": "And Gippie was after him , for , snorting with rage and mortification at the resuscitation of his rival , he had bounded from under the stove , and , with his maimed tail wagging excitedly in the air , was biting , snapping , growling at Poacher 's heels , nipping him fiercely , if by chance he paused a second to rest", "context_len": 64}}}, "limping": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Oh , have you seen a little girl ? '' she said excitedly ; `` a poor little girl , very thin and miserable , and with a lame , brown dog limping after her ? She 's wandering somewhere -- the unfortunate , misguided child", "context_len": 47}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 28, "context": "They were certainly ingenious , though hardly elegant , and it did not comfort her much to be told that she would be taken for a wounded soldier limping back from the wars ; indeed , Father made such fun of her that she grew quite indignant , and began to think she would really rather have been scolded a little than so very much laughed at", "context_len": 67}}}, "unfortunate": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 88, "word_index": 64, "context": "understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Her head was turned far round , she was looking over her shoulder in the direction away from the front of the house , and yet she went right to the spot where the unfortunate Poacher had fallen", "context_len": 38}}}, "misguided": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` Oh , have you seen a little girl ? '' she said excitedly ; `` a poor little girl , very thin and miserable , and with a lame , brown dog limping after her ? She 's wandering somewhere -- the unfortunate , misguided child", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "Hold , '' as the other raised his hand in an ecstasy of thankfulness , `` I go not to join you , but to speak a word of common sense to your misguided followers , if they will hear it , and to remove Mrs", "context_len": 46}}}, "clothed": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 43, "context": "They fascinated her with their crude drawing and colours still vivid and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined", "context_len": 128}}}, "preternaturally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The fat young man became preternaturally solemn , the more so as he heard a low growl somewhere in the region of his feet", "context_len": 24}}}, "growl": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 46, "context": "As I rounded the rock and came in sight of him he rose to his feet , with his two front paws on Ama 's body , and bared his great fangs at me in a hideous grin , as he gave utterance to a snarling growl that might well have struck terror to the boldest", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "Joe never saw Toby without trying to provoke him to a quarrel by making faces at him , and throwing sticks and stones ; but though the dog would growl and show his teeth , he had never yet tried to bite him", "context_len": 43}}}, "region": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 14, "context": "518 , in describing the mounds of the country , states `` that the region lying between Savanna river and Oakmulge , east and west , and from the sea coast ( of the Atlantic ) to the Cherokee or Apalachean mountains ( filled with these mounds ) was possessed by the Cherokees since the arrival of the Europeans ; but they were afterwards dispossessed by the Muscogulges , and all that country was probably , many ages preceding the Cherokee invasion , inhabited by one nation or confederacy ( unknown to the Cherokees , Creeks )", "context_len": 97}}}, "lanky": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "He was a tall man , considerably over six feet in height , very lanky and thin , with a somewhat German cast of face , pale-blue eyes , a bald forehead , hair slightly inclined to be sandy , an ugly mouth with broken teeth , and a long moustache which , with all his efforts , did not conceal this defect", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He had dashed wildly from the woods , a tall , lanky , long-haired figure", "context_len": 15}}}, "sickly": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Trouble enough ; and she 's awful bad herself with the rheumatics , and a sickly baby , and a blind boy , and debts to pay , and that scandal of a doctor , and no way of laying up much ; for the children must be fed and warmed , bless their hearts ! and a police-sergeant 's pay ai n't no great ; yes , yes , honey , lots of trouble and no help for it as I see", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Once more Ben found himself vis \u00e0 vis with Barney Carney , in whose faded smile there was now a slight sickly tinge", "context_len": 23}}}, "complexion": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 73, "context": "have got some good points , and if I were your daughter , Aunt Susan -- and I am heartily glad I 'm not your daughter ; I would much , much rather be Mummy 's daughter , poor as she is -- but if I were your daughter you would dress me in such a fashion that my good points would come out , for I have good points ; a nice complexion , fine hair and plenty of it , and fairly good eyes , and my figure would not look clumsy if I wore proper stays and properly-made dresses ; and my feet would not be like clodhoppers , if I had fine well-made boots and silk stockings ; and my hands -- -- ''", "context_len": 128}}}, "steely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` And a sort of steely air as if she 'd dare the world ? ''", "context_len": 16}}}, "sighted": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 65, "context": "But the steamer did not mind how any of her passengers were feeling -- she must make the best possible record , no matter who was glad or sorry ; and on she steamed , past lonely and beautiful Holyhead , and then through the wide Irish Sea ( that seems indeed a veritable ocean in its wideness ) , until land once more was sighted and the harbor reached , and the anchor dropped off the wonderful docks at Liverpool", "context_len": 81}}}, "considering": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 28, "context": "My first impulse was to carry the body down to where the soil was softer , and there dig a grave for it ; but while I was considering this plan , it occurred to me that , with no more efficient tool than a spear to serve as a shovel , it would be practically impossible for me to bury the body deep enough to protect it from the jackals and hyaenas ; and I therefore determined that , instead of burying it , I would burn it", "context_len": 89}}}, "pinching": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Then I 've sighted your game , '' he said , gravely , very gravely , considering that the `` game '' was pinching one of his legs", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "This , by great pinching and saving , the policeman thought he would be able to do", "context_len": 17}}}, "scent": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 64, "context": "With his body bent , his head thrust forward , and his nostrils working , he slowly passed along the inner face of the crowd , his shifty eyes darting hither and thither , until his gaze happened to fall upon one of the individuals for whom he was looking , when he would come to a halt , appear to be following a scent , and finally stretch forth his spear and lightly smite some man or woman on the head with it", "context_len": 84}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 60, "context": "For a time poor Guy sat upon the ground helpless , and hopeless , listening intently to the rustling movements of the numerous small animals , that wandered about seeking food ; fearing to move , lest he should encounter a prairie wolf , or some other ferocious beast , and equally afraid to remain still , lest they should scent him there", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "At length she drew up about fifty yards from our starboard quarter , and even at that short distance they did not scent danger , their eagerness blinding them to the fact that twenty-five closed ports separated them from a death-dealing hail of iron", "context_len": 44}}}, "sixty": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` What a reply from a youth of twenty-two to a venerable general of sixty , who had been so tried and faithful a friend , and so efficient a coadjutor both to his father and to himself , for so many years", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 72, "context": "Many will , we may calculate , jump overboard and swim ashore rather than face us , and we shall be able , at any rate , to capture three or four of their craft , for , as they come out , one by one , we can all close round them ; and with nearly fifty knights , ninety released captives , burning for vengeance , and some fifty or sixty fishermen , for those from the fire ships will , of course , join us -- we shall make short work of them , and may even hope to entirely destroy their fleet", "context_len": 106}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "The fleet now seen approaching numbered a hundred and sixty large ships , besides a great number of small craft , conveying a force variously estimated at from seventy to a hundred thousand men", "context_len": 34}}}, "wig": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 53, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}}}, "advisory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Dabley -- he 's one of our advisory committee", "context_len": 9}}}, "committee": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 46, "context": "There were two reasons : In the first place , Mark Mallory , together with the rest of the plebes , was being bullied and tormented just then in the course of a squad drill ; and , in the second place , one of the committee , Cadet Spencer , was engaged in doing the bullying , having been appointed `` on duty over plebes", "context_len": 66}}}, "deceived": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 64, "context": "past ! another weary weary day , And I am so much nearer to my grave ! Oh that I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I am the deceived , not the deceiver", "context_len": 8}}}, "overtake": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 21, "context": "Alden \u2019 s belief was that the monstrous animal would keep on with his ponderous gait in the grotesque attempt to overtake the fleet footed horse which was safe beyond his reach ; but to the consternation of the watcher the brute halted at the very spot where the fugitive had landed when he dropped from the saddle", "context_len": 58}}}, "ma'am": {"freq": 92, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` I did n't want to be settin ' myself up , but I was n't goin ' to have her take no false notions about me , so I says , 'Yes , ma'am , but when a body 's sick , and ai n't no appetite to eat only what one forces one 's self to , I do n't think it no sin to spend a bit for a nice nourishin ' mouthful", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 44, "context": "As the stranger 's eyes were fixed upon Hafed as she spoke , the little girl supposed the `` pretty thing '' meant the dress of the young Persian , which the children thought very elegant ; and she answered , `` Yes , ma'am , but he is not to wear it much longer , 'cause the boys yun after him in the street , so Uncle Yuthven is having some English clothes made for him", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` The saints bless and protect you ma'am for not lettin ' her be sent to a hospital ! But do you think she can get well ? And if that other maid suits , could n't you keep her here all summer and let her get good and strong ? I 'm going out to my cousin at Fairfield to stay until next Monday", "context_len": 65}}}, "regular": {"freq": 180, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 180, "word_index": 108, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}}}, "escape": {"freq": 321, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 147, "word_index": 50, "context": "It looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 173, "word_index": 82, "context": "it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached L'Esperance , and the extraordinary story I had heard while aboard her", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Ah ! young springal , thou wilt not escape again", "context_len": 10}}}, "robe": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 101, "context": "In the grate there was a glowing , blazing fire ; on the hob was a little brass kettle hissing and boiling ; spread upon the floor was a thick , warm crimson rug ; before the fire a folding-chair , unfolded , and with cushions on it ; by the chair a small folding-table , unfolded , covered with a white cloth , and upon it spread small covered dishes , a cup , a saucer , a teapot ; on the bed were new warm coverings and a satin-covered down quilt ; at the foot a curious wadded silk robe , a pair of quilted slippers , and some books", "context_len": 112}}}, "breathe": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 57, "context": "Playing in the garden , they saw Mary sitting outside the gate , counting over the money she had made by the sale of her radishes : and as they were talking to her , it came about that she told them of the sick brother lying on his bed , never able to go out and breathe the fresh air , or see the beautiful blue sky and green trees , in this lovely Summer weather ; and how she and her mother were working and saving , that they might have enough to buy the easy chair", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 86, "context": "`` So it was agreed that her spirit should dwell as a prairie dog , and before long out sprang one from a reeking cauldron of herbs , and they took it to the holes of the prairie dogs and left it there , placing beside it a terrible serpent , that all others might be afraid to approach it , and an owl at the door , as a sentinel that would stand looking patiently for an enemy both night and day , and never breathe to the gossips around her the tale of the princess that was prisoned within", "context_len": 101}}}, "freely": {"freq": 76, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 73, "context": "queer it is , he said to himself , that I should feel constrained in this way to run out to Windsor ! Land knows ! I have no desire to come to be on intimate terms of acquaintance with Evelyn 's boys ; and what would be the satisfaction of prowling around just to see where they live ? Their father gave me up after that time he spoke his mind so freely about my aimless life -- as he was pleased to call it -- and there is no reason whatever why I should bother myself about my sister 's children , since she , poor thing ! is dead and gone , and they have enough of this world 's goods to make them comfortable", "context_len": 128}}}, "striped": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Say , ma'am , was that a striped or a plain shawl she had her dog wrapped in ? ''", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 54, "context": "As my boy was skilful at marbles , he was able to start out in the morning with his toy , or the marble he shot with , and a commy , or a brown marble of the Lowest value , and come home at night with a pocketful of white-alleys and blood-alleys , striped plasters find bull's-eyes , and crystals , clear and clouded", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "The train had come and gone , and his eager eyes went to the pony tied safe and sound under the shed , with not only the lap-robe over his back , but also the striped shawl -- the first and last time that he would have the pleasure of wearing it", "context_len": 52}}}, "impudence": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 18, "context": "But though he was hail-fellow with every boy in town , and although every boy rejoiced in his impudence , he was so panoplied in the awfulness of his relation to the constabulary functions that , however remote it was , no boy would have thought of trifling with him when he was on duty", "context_len": 55}}}, "jacket": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 9, "context": "So I put away the new hat and pretty jacket and went down to mother , and I amused mother by showing her the lace I had bought , and I told her all about the Duchess , and mother was much pleased at the thought of seeing her old friend on the following morning , and she and I sat that afternoon in the drawing-room making up the pretty lace fichu , and I resolved that mother should wear it the next day when the Duchess came", "context_len": 88}}}, "locked": {"freq": 97, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 95, "word_index": 89, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "But then , it was Polly calling , and such a thing as a locked door between them would never do", "context_len": 21}}}, "obligation": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 87, "context": "Alfred , her eldest son -- for Edmund was the offspring of an early amour of the king -- was on the other side of the bed , a well-made youth , combining in his features the haughty bearing of his Norman maternal ancestors with the English traits of his father ; but now his expression was one of distress and anxiety , which was yet more deeply shared by his younger brother , Edward , who even at this period manifested that strong sense of religious obligation and that early devotion which in later years caused him to be numbered amongst canonised saints", "context_len": 104}}}, "lifting": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 77, "context": "Her Spanish crew we soon found snugly hidden away in spacious quarters beneath the lazaret ; and , as to the name on her stern , we found that the piece of wood on which it was carved and painted was reversible , having Virginia , New Orleans , carved on one side of it and Preciosa , Havana , on the other , and that it could be unbolted and reversed in a few minutes by lifting a couple of movable planks in the after cabin", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "In the mean time , back at the farm the cattle had begun to stir about in the barnyard with the lifting of the night shadows", "context_len": 26}}}, "chase": {"freq": 84, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 85, "context": "So he told them how , when he sat alone , ill and dull and irritable , Ram Dass had tried to distract him by describing the passers by , and there was one child who passed oftener than any one else ; he had begun to be interested in her -- partly perhaps because he was thinking a great deal of a little girl , and partly because Ram Dass had been able to relate the incident of his visit to the attic in chase of the monkey", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 40, "context": "The incident was much more gratifying to me than all the honour paid me by the Republic , and during the long months of my captivity it has recurred to me so frequently that I have in vain endeavoured to chase it from my thoughts , as sinful thus to allow myself constantly to think of any woman", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Ahem ! If you had lived in Boston , and devoted yourself to the cultivation of the intellectualities -- yea , by Zeus ! -- instead of learning to lose your temper and chase yearlings like a wild Texan -- -- However , I 'll explain it", "context_len": 48}}}, "nerve": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 94, "context": ", for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed , with feelings of envy , that many of my fellow-sufferers had already succumbed and become unconscious , if indeed they were not dead", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "He knew not then that the example before him would nerve him in moments of severest trial , then fast approaching , that the one accusation urged against the Christians , which he had felt most keenly , that of cowardice , was answered in the weak yet valiant boy , who found strength in the name of Christ to endure all for His sake ; neither did his fierce countrymen know that they were preparing a disappointment for the pagan Anlaf , and for all those of his house and lineage", "context_len": 92}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Ginger ! but you 're nerve and action , '' commented the admiring Bob", "context_len": 15}}}, "wonderingly": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 13, "context": "I I haven \u2019 t much time , faltered Stone ; but he wonderingly took his place at Roger \u2019 s side and was whirled away , regretfully watched by Jimmy , who hung on the sagging gate and stared after the carriage until it turned the corner under the street-light opposite the post office", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "She stood still , and her green-gray eyes met his wonderingly", "context_len": 11}}}, "commotion": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 11, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}}}, "bark": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "If Miss Pussy had been civil , Flossy would probably have taken no notice of her ; but when she drew attention upon herself by this very rude behavior , he began to bark and jump about her , more with a love of teasing than with any idea of hurting her", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 56, "context": "Even Toby seemed to forget himself for a moment or two ; and once , when the chickens were all flying over the grass , screaming and laughing , he sprang up from his post on the porch , where he had been quietly watching them , and came bounding down among them with a joyous bark , and seized hold of the fox by the coat tails , just as he pounced on Harry and Walter , as if he thought they had need of his help", "context_len": 88}}}, "refugee": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` If you bark I 'll break your neck , '' he heard the refugee say in a menacing whisper , and , to cover a series of protesting growls , he shouted , lustily , `` Yes , ma'am , but first I passed her on foot", "context_len": 48}}}, "menacing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` If you bark I 'll break your neck , '' he heard the refugee say in a menacing whisper , and , to cover a series of protesting growls , he shouted , lustily , `` Yes , ma'am , but first I passed her on foot", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Hartley should wonder why I do not come hack and Ted obeyed orders with an alacrity rather menacing to his digestive powers", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "All this is making very free with State belongings , and one of the guides , in charge of a small party of visitors , starts toward him in a decidedly menacing manner ; but Harold intercepts him and explains , and the guide , himself much amused , decides to leave unmolested this gallant little tar of Her Majesty 's", "context_len": 61}}}, "series": {"freq": 88, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 88, "word_index": 92, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "growls": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` If you bark I 'll break your neck , '' he heard the refugee say in a menacing whisper , and , to cover a series of protesting growls , he shouted , lustily , `` Yes , ma'am , but first I passed her on foot", "context_len": 48}}}, "government": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 20, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "mule": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 10, "context": "That young one has got the face of a government mule , but I 'm used to mules , and when she asked me I said , `` Pears to me , little girl , you favour a runaway , and I ai n't got no room for runaways in this narrow rig , 'specially as I 'm taking a bundle of clothing to my dear old father ' -- likewise a young pig , '' he added , as there was a decided squeal from between his feet", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "How dare that fellow come here and talk to me in such a fashion ! he snarled , pacing the floor of his room ; how dare he ! So he \u2019 s going to stand by Stone at any cost ! Judging by what I \u2019 ve heard about him , he \u2019 s just mule enough to do it , too", "context_len": 63}}}, "mules": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 17, "context": "That young one has got the face of a government mule , but I 'm used to mules , and when she asked me I said , `` Pears to me , little girl , you favour a runaway , and I ai n't got no room for runaways in this narrow rig , 'specially as I 'm taking a bundle of clothing to my dear old father ' -- likewise a young pig , '' he added , as there was a decided squeal from between his feet", "context_len": 89}}}, "runaways": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "That young one has got the face of a government mule , but I 'm used to mules , and when she asked me I said , `` Pears to me , little girl , you favour a runaway , and I ai n't got no room for runaways in this narrow rig , 'specially as I 'm taking a bundle of clothing to my dear old father ' -- likewise a young pig , '' he added , as there was a decided squeal from between his feet", "context_len": 89}}}, "likewise": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 70, "context": "That young one has got the face of a government mule , but I 'm used to mules , and when she asked me I said , `` Pears to me , little girl , you favour a runaway , and I ai n't got no room for runaways in this narrow rig , 'specially as I 'm taking a bundle of clothing to my dear old father ' -- likewise a young pig , '' he added , as there was a decided squeal from between his feet", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 43, "context": "Certainly it is true that the thief might have retained the missing sum , but does it not seem remarkable that he should have done so and left behind him in his flight the larger amount hidden in that room ? It is likewise true that the beforesaid Davis might have learned from the before-mentioned Hayden just what sum of money was recovered , but , being nonchalantly questioned regarding this , he denied it", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 65, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}}}, "decided": {"freq": 350, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 30, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 331, "word_index": 104, "context": "She did not talk nearly so much as in former times of her son ; perhaps he had told her that I was not to be worried , but she was very good and very nice , and I got sincerely attached to her : and I never saw the Duchess nor Lady Thesiger nor my old friends , although I heard that the Duchess was fairly well again , and was out and going into society ; and every one now seemed certain that Jim Randolph had gone to the bottom in the Star of Hope , but by universal consent the boarders decided that the news should be kept from mother , and mother grew much better", "context_len": 119}}}, "squeal": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 84, "context": "That young one has got the face of a government mule , but I 'm used to mules , and when she asked me I said , `` Pears to me , little girl , you favour a runaway , and I ai n't got no room for runaways in this narrow rig , 'specially as I 'm taking a bundle of clothing to my dear old father ' -- likewise a young pig , '' he added , as there was a decided squeal from between his feet", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "Day before yesterday she laughed out loud when I was playing with her , and put her dear little hands in my curls and pulled them , and I did n't mind it so very much if she did pull so hard I had to squeal a little ; and oh ! I 'd let her do it again , if she would only get well", "context_len": 66}}}, "spanking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Thank you , thank you , '' came faintly after him as he started off at a spanking gait , and , `` You 're badder than I thought you was , '' came reproachfully from the tumbled head peeping above the lap-robe", "context_len": 44}}}, "gait": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 73, "context": "Two sets of mail bags were approaching each other from points two thousand miles apart , and there were times when this approach was at the astounding speed of forty and even fifty miles an hour ! The average daily rate was two hundred and fifty miles a day , but where everything was favorable , or when an express rider was fleeing from the vengeful red men , his pony struck a gait of twenty-five miles and maintained it , when an untrained horse would have dropped in his tracks", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The road itself formed the main street of the place , and while the boys were jogging at a very leisurely gait toward the huddle of store buildings , a man in a flannel shirt and with his trousers tucked in his boot tops , jumped across the road , dragging a rattling chain behind him", "context_len": 56}}}, "badder": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Thank you , thank you , '' came faintly after him as he started off at a spanking gait , and , `` You 're badder than I thought you was , '' came reproachfully from the tumbled head peeping above the lap-robe", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Where are de children , anyway ? asked Albert , wonderfully reassured by Donald 's courteous reception ; wo n't you fin ' dem for me , please , Donald , and tell dem I wo n't be a badder , nor ask queshuns , and I 'll jus ' eat my own lunch and --", "context_len": 56}}}, "reproachfully": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Thank you , thank you , '' came faintly after him as he started off at a spanking gait , and , `` You 're badder than I thought you was , '' came reproachfully from the tumbled head peeping above the lap-robe", "context_len": 44}}}, "tumbled": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "The best place for them was in some woods-pasture where the trees stood free of one another , and around them , in among the tall , frosty grass , the tumbled nuts lay scattered in groups of twos and threes , or fives , some still yellowish-green in their hulls , and some black , but all sending up to the nostrils of the delighted boy the incense of their clean , keen , wild-woody smell , to be a memory forever", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 62, "context": "Jennie laughed merrily ; but as she was about to answer , the click of the gate was heard , and with shouts of `` He 's coming ! '' from Charlie , `` Poppy , poppy ! '' from the younger boy , and a confused jargon from Tommy , which no one but Jennie could understand , the whole three tumbled down from the window and rushed to the door", "context_len": 72}}}, "ironically": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Air you also cravin ' for an argyment ? '' asked Lucas , ironically , of Joe", "context_len": 18}}}, "forgive": {"freq": 123, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 104, "word_index": 28, "context": "The affair made a great impression but somehow did not increase Miss Newman 's popularity , for the idea of the drawings was hers and Clara could not forgive her for the position into which she had forced her , therefore she lost no opportunity of making it as unpleasant for her teacher as she could in the thousand and one ways a sly and unprincipled girl can , and her little pin-pricks were so annoying , that finally Dorothy and Edna , who had not particularly cared for the new teacher , began to stand up for her and to do as many kind things as they could", "context_len": 109}, "UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 0, "context": "Forgive me , Lady Jane , but I want us all to have a very good tea ; and as it is such a lovely , lovely day , may we go for a little picnic afterwards , and take our supper with us into the woods ? Will you come too , and Miss Frost ? It would be so delightful ! ''", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "That was well planned , Charlie , said Joe ; and I \u2019 ll forgive you for singeing me so", "context_len": 20}}}, "opinions": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 13, "context": "Sir Knights , he said , I have heard from my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "companion": {"freq": 260, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 260, "word_index": 113, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}}}, "rat": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 30, "context": "In one corner of the room a crowd of Afrikanders swayed in a compact mass , as if eager to wreak their vengeance on someone , while held like a rat in the jaws of a terrier was the man from Muizenberg , his captor being Jan van Eindhovengen", "context_len": 49}}}, "flask": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 52, "context": "He was speaking to his mother of this and hearing very thankfully her assurance that she felt better , if she did not look so , when Gus and George came up to him , and rapidly told him that their father had gone to the hunt and had left his powder flask behind and that their mother said he was to take it to them", "context_len": 66}}}, "ulster": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` No , but I 've got something better , '' and he drew a flask from the pocket of his big ulster and put it to her mouth", "context_len": 29}}}, "nostrils": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 85, "context": "It was perfectly good form , also , to get a boy , if you could , to shut his eyes , and then lead him into a mud-puddle or a thicket of briers or nettles , or to fool him in any heartless way , such as promising to pump easy when he put his mouth to the pump-spout , and then coming down on the pump-handle with a rush that flooded him with water and sent him off blowing the tide from his nostrils like a whale", "context_len": 89}}}, "dilated": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 57, "context": "At this juncture the Vizier presented himself and as he entered , he espied the new palace and the carpet and marvelled also ; so , when he came in to the Sultan , the twain fell to talking of this strange matter and marvelling , for that they saw a thing which amazed the beholder and dilated the heart ; and they said , `` Verily , methinketh kings may not avail unto the building of the like of this palace", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` The deed ? '' whispered the boy , with dilated eyes , `` the judgment ? ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "bottles": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 70, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "beware": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "But , when he saw that she heartened herself for that which he set forth to her of its virtues , he feared lest she should talk of this to the folk , so he said to her , `` O my mother , beware lest thou bespeak any of the lamp and its uses , for that this is our fortune ; be careful [ 345 ] and exceed not in speech thereof to any one , lest we lose it and lose this our present prosperity , for that it is from it", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Of trees and of ponds too beware ; Mind likewise to treat well your ass ; And then , with attention and care , Your time will in happiness pass", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "So beware , Boshot", "context_len": 4}}}, "evil": {"freq": 145, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "I know you began it all with a view to charitable ends ; but he who does evil that good may come sets his foot in a crooked path , of which none can see the close", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 87, "word_index": 101, "context": "`` Oh , this was to have been my wedding day , my wedding day ! '' and he clasped his hands in agony ; then the thought of his master -- his slain lord -- returned , and he cried , `` O Edmund ! my master , my dear master , so good , so gentle , yet so brave ; who else could slay him ? what fiend else than Edric , the murderer Edric ? That they should think I , or any one else than Edric , could have done such a deed , such an evil deed ! ''", "context_len": 105}, "NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Quoth the Sultan , `` I marvel at thee how thou still deemest evil of Alaeddin ; but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed", "context_len": 128}}}, "sups": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Beware of bottles , beware of cups , Evil to him who evil sups", "context_len": 15}}}, "temperance": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 16, "context": "The pity for the boys was that they saw the drunkards every day , and the temperance men only now and then ; and out of the group of boys who were my boy 's friends , many kindly fellows came to know how strong drink could rage , how it could bite like the serpent , and sting like an adder", "context_len": 62}}}, "crank": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 79, "context": "Now my poor old dad 's gone , and my mother 's married again , and the boys all trading off on their own account , I do n't want to go back to Colorado ; but I like colonial life , and farming would be a lot jollier than school , any way , for the fellows in my house are awful Johnnies , they can talk of nothing but games , and laugh at one for a crank if one tries to make things", "context_len": 86}}}, "hunk": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "He never let on that he suspected for a moment that he was not entirely alone , but , walking over to a tree stump , where , spread out on a newspaper , was the remains of a lunch , he acted delighted at the discovery , picked up a hunk of bread in one hand , a piece of cheese in the other , and , throwing himself on the green sward at full length , proceeded to munch the eatables , with every semblance of satisfaction", "context_len": 89}}}, "avidity": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "'Tilda Jane took the two-pronged fork he put in her hand , and began to eat with slow avidity , not disregarding the requests for titbits from her dog , who occasionally paused for that purpose in his endeavours to lick himself dry", "context_len": 43}}}, "section": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 91, "context": "In my collection I have them varying from almost straight to a shape like that of the letter V , nearly straight , curved , plain , ornamented , some with strange carvings , and all varying according to different sections of the country and individual tribes , each having its own make or style , showing respectively rough crudeness or considerable finish , and being especially characteristic in the ends or points -- all of which a boomerang connoisseur will distinguish at once , and locate as to tribe and section", "context_len": 92}}}, "morsel": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 40, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "muzzle": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 87, "context": "Certain tones in the man 's hearty voice reminded me of the boy 's sweet notes , and was n't there a queer suggestion of each other when I stood near them ? We Shetland ponies as I have said before are very close to human beings for our ancestors were literally brought up with the children in the crofters ' huts , and my mother has often told me that her mother had many a lick at the family platter and many a time her soft muzzle was buried in the children 's necks", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The only thing to do was to muzzle him , and this was done by my boy 's brother with a piece of heavy twine , in such a manner as to interfere with Tip 's happiness as little as possible", "context_len": 41}}}, "hunger": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 76, "context": "or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing his victims to the tree with spike nails driven through the hands and feet into the tough timber", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He preferred to fight Indians and cold and hunger for the sake of a few peltries , whose sale brought enough to support him in idleness between trapping seasons", "context_len": 29}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "They said she starved herself to death ; but it was , probably , grief and despair at being thus left , in her declining years , so hopelessly friendless and alone , and not hunger , that destroyed her", "context_len": 40}}}, "stops": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Let 's wait a bit , and see if it stops snowing , '' proposed Frank , and they did , standing in the shelter of some trees", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "Every Sunday mornin ' there 's a big boy that goes to the same school stops for Willie and Jennie , and totes them with him ; and if their father or me ca n't go to church , he just totes them back after service", "context_len": 46}}}, "greed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "And , even as he spoke , he realized what a very fool he had been to let this fellow gull him with his talk of a pot of gold ! The gleam of disappointed greed that shone in Astbury 's eyes told what he might have guessed already , that it was no old affection or fidelity that had drawn the man to him , but merely the hope of making money", "context_len": 73}}}, "begins": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "The big boys have known all along that he was not a real country-jake ; but when the trained mule begins , and shakes everybody off , just like the horse , and another country-jake gets up , and offers to bet that he can ride that mule , nobody can tell whether he is a real country-jake or not", "context_len": 60}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 45, "context": "A grand place , too , to work , you think ; and then you recall with a sigh that the light of that glorious mind has well-nigh gone out , 'neath the weight of physical weariness and infirmity , and then the solitary home begins to look a little like a prison in your eyes , as you realize how glad its inmate would be to exchange it for the Palace of that King whose divine intent for the world he has so marvellously interpreted for us all in the days when soul was still master of hand and brain", "context_len": 101}}}, "poetry": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 52, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "' Some of the lines have been ringing in my head all day , and I always like the sort of cadence in your voice when you read poetry aloud", "context_len": 30}}}, "gabble": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "I 'll gabble on a bit if you 'll give me a starter", "context_len": 13}}}, "shrewdly": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 3, "context": "For I very shrewdly suspected that Gouroo and Mafuta were equally interested in my downfall -- might , indeed , have conspired in some mysterious manner to bring it about -- and would probably take care that it should be as complete and disastrous as savage vindictiveness could make it", "context_len": 50}}}, "vehemently": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 42, "context": "They stood in silence watching the strange interview between the royal prisoner and his republican guardian ; but no word of the conversation reached their ears , till , in answer to some word of the king 's , Harrison said very vehemently --", "context_len": 44}}}, "worm": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 46, "context": "Say , Lieutenant , came Fred Marmon \u2019 s reply , did you ever try to bandage a man \u2019 s seat with a roll of one-inch gauze ? I might do it if Soapy would hold still , but he \u2019 s wiggling like a worm on a fishhook", "context_len": 50}}}, "hugged": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 5, "context": "As soon as Kitty had hugged her father , one close , passionate , voiceless hug , she released him , stepped back a pace , looked him in the face , and then said eagerly , `` Come away quickly , father ; there is a meadow at the back of the cherry orchard which we can have quite to ourselves", "context_len": 62}}}, "judicial": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Well , I don ' know about lovely , '' said the young man , in a judicial manner , `` but the crow , as I take him , is a kind of long-suffering orphan among birds", "context_len": 39}}}, "furrows": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Ben would have ploughed two or three furrows around his buildings , which would have been the most effectual preventive ; but , after the vessel was built , he had put his oxen away to pasture", "context_len": 37}}}, "grasshoppers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Grubs just fly down his red throat , and grasshoppers ai n't nowhere , but because he now and then lifts a hill o ' petetters , and pulls a mite o ' corn when it gets toothsome , and makes way once in so often with a fat chicken that 's a heap better out o ' the world than in it , the farmers is down on him , the Legislature wo n't protect him , and the crow -- man 's good friend -- gets shot by everybody and everything ! ''", "context_len": 95}}}, "nowhere": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 54, "context": "She was askin ' some more questions about my sick friend , when somebody pulls the door-bell as furious , and when it was opened , there was a servant-gal lookin ' as scared as anything , and she tells the old lady her little granddaughter was lost , and could n't be found nowhere , and was she here , and did they know anything about her ? Well , they did n't know nothin ' , and the old lady said she 'd be round right away , and she herself looked scared ready to drop , and I see she had n't no more thought for me nor my belongin 's , nor could n't be expected to , so I just takes my leave", "context_len": 128}}}, "lifts": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Grubs just fly down his red throat , and grasshoppers ai n't nowhere , but because he now and then lifts a hill o ' petetters , and pulls a mite o ' corn when it gets toothsome , and makes way once in so often with a fat chicken that 's a heap better out o ' the world than in it , the farmers is down on him , the Legislature wo n't protect him , and the crow -- man 's good friend -- gets shot by everybody and everything ! ''", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "If they wanted to risk getting off course and piling up on a reef , they could sneak up within striking distance of this coast , and land their troops when the fog lifts", "context_len": 34}}}, "pulls": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "She was askin ' some more questions about my sick friend , when somebody pulls the door-bell as furious , and when it was opened , there was a servant-gal lookin ' as scared as anything , and she tells the old lady her little granddaughter was lost , and could n't be found nowhere , and was she here , and did they know anything about her ? Well , they did n't know nothin ' , and the old lady said she 'd be round right away , and she herself looked scared ready to drop , and I see she had n't no more thought for me nor my belongin 's , nor could n't be expected to , so I just takes my leave", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then he sticks a short pipe into his mouth , and pulls on an old wool hat , and flourishes a stick that the supe throws to him , and you see that he is an Irishman just come across the sea ; and then off goes another coat , and he comes out a British soldier in white duck trousers and red coat", "context_len": 64}}}, "mite": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Grubs just fly down his red throat , and grasshoppers ai n't nowhere , but because he now and then lifts a hill o ' petetters , and pulls a mite o ' corn when it gets toothsome , and makes way once in so often with a fat chicken that 's a heap better out o ' the world than in it , the farmers is down on him , the Legislature wo n't protect him , and the crow -- man 's good friend -- gets shot by everybody and everything ! ''", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` What could such a mite as Bessie do with a garden of her own ? She might dig and plant in it to be sure , but then she would not know how to take care of her flowers and things , and she would only be disappointed if she failed", "context_len": 52}}}, "corn": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` For the sake of him who hath even now rendered up himself as a martyr for the Lord 's cause , I may not be angry with any word of yours , '' he answered sadly ; `` but I do entreat of you to take heed ! Would you lay down your arms and live in peace among your cattle and your corn , coached and complimented into effeminacy and foolishness ? Oh , for shame ! Rub your eyes and look about you ! What was the fate of the men of Sodom when they thought Lot was one that mocked when he warned them ! ''", "context_len": 110}}}, "toothsome": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Grubs just fly down his red throat , and grasshoppers ai n't nowhere , but because he now and then lifts a hill o ' petetters , and pulls a mite o ' corn when it gets toothsome , and makes way once in so often with a fat chicken that 's a heap better out o ' the world than in it , the farmers is down on him , the Legislature wo n't protect him , and the crow -- man 's good friend -- gets shot by everybody and everything ! ''", "context_len": 95}}}, "chicken": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 24, "context": "If so be you laid hands if it were but on a hen , if you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "There is going to be chicken pie , besides the turkeys and the oysters", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Bingi brought bacon , cold potatoes , an egg salad , jelly , chicken sandwiches , a chocolate cake , ginger snaps , two cream pies and lots of spread bread and butter", "context_len": 33}}}, "queen": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Then she told her mother the queen all that had betided her that night and how they had taken her bridegroom , leaving her alone , and how after a little there came another youth and lay down in the place of her bridegroom , putting a sword between himself and her ; `` and in the morning '' [ quoth she ] `` he who had brought us thither returned and taking us up , carried us back to our place here : and hardly had he brought us hither and left us when my father the Sultan entered and I had neither heart nor tongue to answer him for stress of fright and trembling which possessed me", "context_len": 120}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "But before the evening was over he presented to the fairy queen a copy of his book , which said on the cover , A BOOK , by Wise Mr", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 4, "context": "Indeed , the little queen , whose realm was the hearts of her nearest and dearest , would have been obliged to abdicate , for a while at least , I fancy , had she not chosen before nightfall of that same day to bury her head in the lap of her very most loyal subject , and with tears and sohs confess to her extreme unreasonableness and avow her determination not soon again to be overtaken by such a sorry state of mind and temper", "context_len": 86}}}, "passionately": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 16, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "She dashed up to Irene , flung her arms round her neck , and kissed her passionately", "context_len": 17}}}, "sissy": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 28, "context": "Papa , you may strap me if you want to , but if I had n't fit the boys would have made fun of me and called me sissy , and we went at it like fury", "context_len": 37}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` No , I ai n't , but sissy there says 'Liance gave it to her to carry and she ai n't no notion of what she done with it , thought mebbe she might ha ' drapped it in here", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "I 've had a great talk with that friend of yours -- and sissy , I 'm obliged to you", "context_len": 20}}}, "unmake": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Well , sissy , if you ever get to be one , just unmake a few laws that are passed to please the men who have a pull", "context_len": 29}}}, "laws": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 102, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}}}, "bounty": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 24, "context": "He passed no folk but they were descanting upon the palace and its magnificence and talking of Alaeddin 's grace and comeliness and his bounty and munificence and the goodliness of his manners and disposition ; so [ 555 ] he went up to one of those who were extolling Alaeddin on this wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "mink": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Here in Maine you might take the bounty off bob-cats , an ' let 'em have their few sheep , an ' you might stand between the mink and the spawning trout , and if you want to put a check on the robins who make war on the cherries an ' strawberries , I guess it would be more sensible than chasing up the crows", "context_len": 66}}}, "spawning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Here in Maine you might take the bounty off bob-cats , an ' let 'em have their few sheep , an ' you might stand between the mink and the spawning trout , and if you want to put a check on the robins who make war on the cherries an ' strawberries , I guess it would be more sensible than chasing up the crows", "context_len": 66}}}, "trout": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}}}, "check": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 40, "context": "Here in Maine you might take the bounty off bob-cats , an ' let 'em have their few sheep , an ' you might stand between the mink and the spawning trout , and if you want to put a check on the robins who make war on the cherries an ' strawberries , I guess it would be more sensible than chasing up the crows", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 77, "context": "So rapt was he in the grandeur of the scene that he had forgotten all about the object of his journey , but he was brought back to the matter-of-fact present by a tremendous snatch which jerked his arm hanging over the side , and made the cord cut so violently into his hand that he was glad to give the line a twist and set it free to run for some distance before he began to check it a little", "context_len": 81}}}, "robins": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 43, "context": "Here in Maine you might take the bounty off bob-cats , an ' let 'em have their few sheep , an ' you might stand between the mink and the spawning trout , and if you want to put a check on the robins who make war on the cherries an ' strawberries , I guess it would be more sensible than chasing up the crows", "context_len": 66}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "After a little while the baby robins came , poor little timid creatures , with scarcely any feathers at all", "context_len": 20}}}, "cherries": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 39, "context": "Clavering then read out certain rules which Sir John had made with regard to the Scholarship , and soon afterwards the proceedings of the evening broke up ; the guests departed to their homes , carrying their baskets of cherries with them ; and Kitty , Florence and Mary were surrounded by their companions , who wished them joy and cheered them three times three , and took them up to their dormitory in triumph", "context_len": 75}}}, "smarter": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Come in , '' bawled Lucas , not inhospitably , and two men , much smarter , cleaner , and more dapper-looking than the red-haired man and his sons , entered the cabin", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "Her mother sprang up and kissed her rapturously , crying -- 'Is n't she the dearest and sweetest thing and the smartest ! Think of her learning that and acting it off so completely , and not three years old ! She is smarter than Violet ' -- and then Violet set up such a howl ! Her mother pacified her by saying Marilla should tell her a piece , and after several efforts Cinderella did induce her to say by a great deal of prompting 'Milkman , Milkman , where have you been ? ' Think of the wear on the child 's nerves , and she looked so tired", "context_len": 111}}}, "emphatically": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 71, "context": "wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he could reach , in most uncourtly fashion", "context_len": 128}}}, "creeping": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 123, "context": ", love , should we fear the act of departing ? How many , many , pass Jordan , as it were , dry-shod ? Remember how peacefully sweet Fanny sank to rest , -- dearest Mother , -- how my Letitia \u2019 s face was lighted up with a smile , -- how our Bible-woman at Batala sang aloud a happy hymn within a few hours of her going ! To me it seems such a simple thing for the -- I had almost said imprisoned soul , to leave its cottage of clay , -- for the bird , as soon as fledged , to spread its wings ! We are winged creatures , and it seems a humiliation to be creeping on earth so long", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "They ran as fast as they had run in the storm , and when they got to the creeping in place they were inside in two minutes", "context_len": 27}}}, "vines": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 12, "context": "The old Moss Glen Inn , elm-shaded and half covered by creeping vines , is a favourite resort for travellers in the eastern part of Maine , for there a good dinner can be obtained in a shorter space of time than in any other country hotel in the length and breadth of the State", "context_len": 55}}}, "resort": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 37, "context": "In striking contrast to this mournful scene of sorrow in the palace of Sysigambis , there was an exhibition of the most wild and tumultuous joy in the streets , and in all the public places of resort in the city of Athens , when the tidings of the death of the great Macedonian king arrived there", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 34, "context": "Knowing well that the grand master was the heart and soul of the defence , and that the failure of the assault was mainly due to his energy and ability , he determined to resort to the weapon so frequently in use in Eastern warfare -- that of assassination", "context_len": 49}}}, "travellers": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 75, "context": "`` There I think you are mistaken , Maggie , for , so far as we can judge , there is reason to think Aunt Patty is walking in the safe and narrow road which leads to the Father 's house ; and , since she has not been brought to it by paths quite so easy and pleasant as some of us have known , there is all the more reason that we happier travellers should give her a helping hand", "context_len": 82}}}, "eastern": {"freq": 105, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 105, "word_index": 75, "context": "It was at this inauspicious moment that the Danes charged the palisades again with deadly fury , while the attention of all was drawn to the flames ; so fierce was the attack , that it was necessary once more to concentrate all the strength of the besieged to repel them ; and the fire gained in strength , roared and hissed in its fury , seizing for its prey the whole roof of the eastern wing of the building", "context_len": 80}}}, "breadth": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 84, "context": "I suppose you want to tell me that Westenra here , my pretty little girl , who is going to give herself to me on the 1st of June next year , does not care for me , but she will care for me by-and-by , for my feeling is that love like mine must be returned in the long run , and if after a year she do n't tell your Grace that she is the happiest little wife in the length and breadth of England , I shall be greatly surprised", "context_len": 93}}}, "waif": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` And all because there 's a smart woman at the head of it , '' explained the young man to the little waif beside him", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "But , though strength and health came to her with tender nursing and the lovely air and sunny days , they did not bring them to the poor little waif who had been cast upon the care and pity of these kind friends", "context_len": 43}}}, "crowning": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "A round , good-natured face , crowning a rotund , generous figure , smiled at them from the kitchen window , but while the eyes smiled , the thick , full lips uttered a somewhat different message to a tall , thin woman , bending over the stove", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But the pie was the crowning triumph", "context_len": 7}}}, "rotund": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "A round , good-natured face , crowning a rotund , generous figure , smiled at them from the kitchen window , but while the eyes smiled , the thick , full lips uttered a somewhat different message to a tall , thin woman , bending over the stove", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "One was a rotund pleasant-faced man of about fifty who was puffing sedately at a long-stemmed , huge-bowled pipe", "context_len": 19}}}, "soapy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Ruth Ann , here 's that soapy Hank Dillson round again , -- takin ' in the farmers , as usual , engagin ' them to pay for machinery and buildings more than are needed , considerin ' the number of their cows , an ' he 's got a washed-out lookin ' young one with him", "context_len": 58}}}, "takin": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Ruth Ann , here 's that soapy Hank Dillson round again , -- takin ' in the farmers , as usual , engagin ' them to pay for machinery and buildings more than are needed , considerin ' the number of their cows , an ' he 's got a washed-out lookin ' young one with him", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I was n't scared , neither , '' said Lem , sullenly ; `` takin ' a few apples aint no great ; but I knowed for sure they was after me for some harm", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` No , I do n't ; and if I did I should want to know whose money I was takin '", "context_len": 22}}}, "machinery": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 38, "context": "And now you have at least an idea of how Marie-Celeste passed her time on the steamer , and you can understand how there might have been some people rather less glad than sorry when they felt the machinery stop at two o'clock one morning , and knew that the Queenstown passengers were being transferred to the tender , and that before sunset all the people aboard the great steamer would be separated to the four winds", "context_len": 77}}}, "buildings": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 40, "context": "None could look back , not even to behold If those beloved were nigh ; one thrilling thought Rapt all the multitude , -- Can HE be near ? Then cries of terror rose -- I scarcely heard ; And buildings shook and rocked , and crashing fell , -- I scarcely marked their fall ; the trembling ground Rose like the billowy sea , -- I scarcely felt The motion ; such intensity of hope -- Joy -- expectation -- flooded all my soul ; A tide of living light , o \u2019 erwhelming all The hopes and fears , the cares and woes of earth", "context_len": 107}}}, "cows": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 93, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}}}, "breach": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 77, "context": "At the end of that time he roused himself from his stupor of grief and disappointment , and , abandoning the idea of any further attack upon the point that had cost him so dearly , he ordered the troops to move round and renew the attack upon the wall in front of the Jews ' quarter , and commence the construction of a battery on the edge of the great ditch facing the retrenchment behind the breach before effected", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "'Tilda Jane hesitated , stammered , looked into the dog 's anxious face , and the boys ' protesting ones , and said at last , `` But the ole man where I 'm goin ' , mebbe he 'll breach at my havin ' two dogs", "context_len": 47}}}, "victuals": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}}}, "affairs": {"freq": 112, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 112, "word_index": 16, "context": "And certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most", "context_len": 128}}}, "de": {"freq": 297, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 278, "word_index": 6, "context": "She used to study with Madame de Coucy in the mornings and go for walks among the flowers out in the garden there in the afternoon , and way beyond it too sometimes , and Richard would often come down from London for a visit , and he taught her English courtly ways and to play the mandolin ( Albert looked significantly toward the quaint mandolin , with a faded blue ribbon attached to it , that was lying among the other treasures on the table ) ; and when the King could not come for a regular visit , he would just ride down for a word and kiss", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 13, "context": "The following account of the geomantic process , as described by Arabic writers de re magicf , is mainly derived from the Mukeddimat or Prolegomena of Abdurrehman ibn Aboubekr Mohammed ( better known as Ibn Khaldoun ) to his great work of universal history", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "It \u2019 ll be jes \u2019 like him to kick when he sees me standing with my back toward de Injins , and dere ain \u2019 t any use ob habing any quarrels at a time like dis", "context_len": 38}}}, "waft": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "'Tilda Jane walked gravely into the kitchen , and although her head was heavy , and her feet as light as if they were about to waft her to regions above , she took time to scrutinise the broad face that would have been generous but for the deceitful lips , and also to cast a glance at the hard , composed woman at the window , who looked as if her head , including the knob of tightly curled hair at the back , had been carved from flint", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Into the midst of my meditations there came a brisk voice , the door was opened suddenly , there was a waft of fresh air , and Lady Thesiger stood near me", "context_len": 32}}}, "regions": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 16, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Glad were all the chiefs of the state and the folk rejoiced in all the realm , whilst there came from far regions the notables of the provinces and the governors of the cities , so they might see Alaeddin 's wedding and his bride-feast", "context_len": 45}}}, "deceitful": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 48, "context": "'Tilda Jane walked gravely into the kitchen , and although her head was heavy , and her feet as light as if they were about to waft her to regions above , she took time to scrutinise the broad face that would have been generous but for the deceitful lips , and also to cast a glance at the hard , composed woman at the window , who looked as if her head , including the knob of tightly curled hair at the back , had been carved from flint", "context_len": 90}}}, "composed": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 61, "context": "'Tilda Jane walked gravely into the kitchen , and although her head was heavy , and her feet as light as if they were about to waft her to regions above , she took time to scrutinise the broad face that would have been generous but for the deceitful lips , and also to cast a glance at the hard , composed woman at the window , who looked as if her head , including the knob of tightly curled hair at the back , had been carved from flint", "context_len": 90}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 50, "context": "As we sat down to partake , of it together , Ama informed me that one of her father 's largest canoes , manned by forty paddlers , and commanded by a chief whom she had recognised , had passed slowly down the river about an hour after I had composed myself to rest , the chief in charge intently scrutinising both banks , as they went , evidently in search of some indication of our presence , and had finally passed out of sight to the southward ; after which Ama had descended and taken a few hours ' rest", "context_len": 101}}}, "knob": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 76, "context": "'Tilda Jane walked gravely into the kitchen , and although her head was heavy , and her feet as light as if they were about to waft her to regions above , she took time to scrutinise the broad face that would have been generous but for the deceitful lips , and also to cast a glance at the hard , composed woman at the window , who looked as if her head , including the knob of tightly curled hair at the back , had been carved from flint", "context_len": 90}}}, "curled": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 79, "context": "'Tilda Jane walked gravely into the kitchen , and although her head was heavy , and her feet as light as if they were about to waft her to regions above , she took time to scrutinise the broad face that would have been generous but for the deceitful lips , and also to cast a glance at the hard , composed woman at the window , who looked as if her head , including the knob of tightly curled hair at the back , had been carved from flint", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "carved": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 26, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 64, "context": "the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all service time , and if she did not behave properly it might", "context_len": 128}}}, "flint": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 89, "context": "'Tilda Jane walked gravely into the kitchen , and although her head was heavy , and her feet as light as if they were about to waft her to regions above , she took time to scrutinise the broad face that would have been generous but for the deceitful lips , and also to cast a glance at the hard , composed woman at the window , who looked as if her head , including the knob of tightly curled hair at the back , had been carved from flint", "context_len": 90}}}, "bustling": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 57, "context": "The whole family was gathered together on the grass round the wood fire , the smoke of which rose up faintly blue against the russet of the beech-woods behind -- handsome , fierce-eyed men , lying slouching idly on the ground smoking short black pipes ; slatternly women with gay handkerchiefs tied round their black hair , bustling about with something savoury inside tin cans ; ragged brown-skinned little children , gnawing at bones with savage haste ; while a few disreputable dogs waited eagerly for the scraps that were thrown to them from time to time", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Long , who was a small man , with a bustling , nervous manner , came briskly to the edge of the platform", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "It almost shocks my sense of propriety , young girls travelling at night , -- it is funny even to an old lady , hurrying up and down a bustling platform amongst Natives", "context_len": 33}}}, "wished": {"freq": 264, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 264, "word_index": 86, "context": "wrote to me several times , and in each letter said that he knew perfectly well that I meant to be free until the year was up , and that he was not going to worry me with overmuch love-making , or any nonsense of that sort ; but he thought I would like Switzerland , and the change would do me good , and although he would not say much , and would not even ask me to go out walking with him unless I wished it , yet I was to be certain of one thing , that he was ready to lay down his life for me , and that I was the one thought of his heart , the one treasure of his soul", "context_len": 128}}}, "insecurity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Even in a crisis , people get more or less accustomed to the thundercloud overhead , and the feeling of insecurity below", "context_len": 22}}}, "overcame": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 65, "context": "A fellow would be very good at home , really and truly good , and as soon as he got out with the other fellows he would yield to almost any temptation to mischief that offered , and if none offered he would go and hunt one up , and would never stop till he had found one , and kept at it till it overcame him", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "This over , they returned to the deck till sleep overcame them and they were put in charge of the stewardess while Mr", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 79, "context": "I was doing a most imprudent thing to linger by the side of the pyre , for the smoke , in the first place , and the light of the flames when it fell dark , could scarcely have failed to attract to the spot any savages who might have been in the neighbourhood , when my plight would probably have been as bad as ever ; but at that moment my sorrow at the loss of my companion overcame every other feeling , and , for the moment at least , I was quite indifferent as to what befell me", "context_len": 101}}}, "painted": {"freq": 75, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 4, "context": "Good Parson Perrient had painted Providence plantation as a sort of paradise , where the liberty and toleration dreamt of by a few in England were the law for all ; but was that refuge open to him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought ,", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "He liked to go to the Catholic church , though he was afraid of the painted figure that hung full length on the wooden crucifix , with the blood-drops under the thorns on its forehead , and the red wound in its side", "context_len": 43}}}, "wooden": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 14, "context": "' I suppose not , ' said Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 79, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "My young master was intensely interested as his uncle talked to him about the width of stalls , the size of windows , the proper kind of concrete flooring which should have wooden stall racks , the ventilating flues and rolling doors", "context_len": 42}}}, "relation": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 32, "context": "Could it be that she had made a mistake , after all , and that the neglected child had some powerful though eccentric friend in the background -- perhaps some previously unknown relation , who had suddenly traced her whereabouts , and chose to provide for her in this mysterious and fantastic way ? Relations were sometimes very odd -- particularly rich old bachelor uncles , who did not care for having children near them", "context_len": 75}}}, "stooping": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 59, "context": "It was Faith 's duty to make this room ready for breakfast ; and , as a rule , Warden would have been angry with her for its present state of neglect ; but this morning he said nothing , only when his eyes rested on the torn pieces of the essay he uttered a groan , and , stooping down , he picked them all up and put them in the grate", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "And that reminds me , he continued with a sudden thought , I waked up during the night , that is I partially awakened , and thought I saw a figure near your bed in a stooping position", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "I became conscious of a grey-haired lady stooping over me , offering some eau de cologne , and with a winning smile asking if she could do anything for me", "context_len": 30}}}, "unloose": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "However , by leaving scraps of her clothing on every sharp thorn , and getting her hands and legs terribly scratched , she forced her way out at last ; and keeping a wary outlook on the fort , she tried to unloose the knots that bound Jim", "context_len": 48}}}, "newspapers": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 24, "context": "There were not so very many persons in the car to interest them and for a time the children gave their attention to the newspapers and the box of chocolates , but after a while they wearied of these , and began to look at their fellow travellers", "context_len": 48}}}, "landlady": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 7, "context": "When Keimer took him to his new landlady , Ben was dressed in his best , a handsome , husky well-mannered young man , about five feet ten inches , with a wide mouth and a humorous light in his brown eyes", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The landlady drew away from the bundle , and after she had seen the tired head laid on the pillow , she softly closed the door of the room", "context_len": 29}}}, "package": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 66, "context": "The letter gave instructions for me to be sent to my uncle \u2019 s at Portsmouth , to be provided for until I could choose for myself what I should be , at the same time exhorting me to serve faithfully His Majesty King Charles II or his lawful successor , and to abstain from vain or idle longings to break the seals of the enclosed package till the stipulated time limit had expired", "context_len": 74}}}, "allow": {"freq": 192, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 191, "word_index": 84, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He wouldn \u2019 t allow it ; we should be too much in his way , and we couldn \u2019 t keep up with him for more than a few miles", "context_len": 31}}}, "sakes": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 41, "context": "They want to make use of the woods and fields and rivers ; and when they become men they find these aspects of nature endeared to them by association , and so they think that they were dear for their own sakes ; but the taste for nature is as purely acquired as the taste for poetry or the taste for tomatoes", "context_len": 62}}}, "harm": {"freq": 172, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 147, "word_index": 115, "context": "that he should suppose her to be capable of such an evasion , which to her sense of uprightness would have been as bad as speaking a falsehood by word of mouth ; `` no , of course I did not , that would have been telling her , would it not ? One can do a falsehood as well as tell it , '' and although she had intended no reflection upon her brother , no thrust at him , Percy was ashamed as he remembered how often , during the last few months , he had done this very thing ; how he had shuffled and evaded , and thought it no great harm as long as he did not put his falsehood into actual words", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 69, "context": "The melancholy faces of the two little girls grieved their older friends ; and , besides , it was sad to know that children like Lem and Dolly should take delight in such wicked , wanton mischief , and to know that there seemed to be no way to do them good ; since they only came near those who were weaker and younger than themselves to do them harm , and ran from those who were older and wiser , in fear of the punishment and reproof their wickedness deserved", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "But the commons , hearing of this his commandment , shut the gates of the palace and sent to say to the Sultan , `` This very moment we will overthrow the palace upon thee and all who are therein , an the least harm happen to Alaeddin", "context_len": 48}}}, "rampage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` No ma'am , he 'll not rampage", "context_len": 8}}}, "snap": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 47, "context": "In less than two minutes after the resumption of play the spectators perceived that a great change had taken place in the home team , for the Clearporters had returned to the field firmly resolved to redeem themselves , and they went into the struggle with a snap and dash that temporarily swept the visitors off their feet", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "For instance , if you had been beguiled , as a little boy , into being the last in the game of snap-the-whip , and the snap sent you rolling head over heels on the hard ground , and skinned your nose and tore your trousers , you could cry from the pain without disgrace , and some of the fellows would come up and try to comfort you ; but you were bound in honor not to appeal to the teacher , and you were expected to use every device to get the blood off you before you went in , and to hide the tear in your trousers", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was a very curious kind of gun ; you had to snap a good many caps on it , sometimes , before the load would go off ; and sometimes it would hang fire , and then seem to recollect itself , and go off , maybe , just when you were going to take it down from your shoulder", "context_len": 61}}}, "protection": {"freq": 67, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 67, "word_index": 47, "context": "And suppose I gain admission to the presence , what shall I take by way of offering to the Sultan 's highness ? It [ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm", "context_len": 128}}}, "playfully": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 2, "context": "I write playfully , but the real undermost feeling in my heart is that of humble gratitude to Him from Whom all blessings flow , -- the love of true and God-fearing hearts being one of the most precious of those blessings", "context_len": 42}}}, "jokes": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 127, "context": "and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say afterwards upon the danger of such heedless practical jokes", "context_len": 128}}}, "cajoling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "He smiled , well pleased at her cajoling tone , then , stretching himself out in an armchair , he announced that dinner must be postponed for an hour to let the child have her sleep out", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "But once so near shore as the second board brought him , Jetty was not afraid to swim the remaining distance , having gathered up a little added strength , and after coaxing , ordering and cajoling , the girls were rewarded by seeing the little creature creep to the edge of the board , take to the water again and paddle ashore , crouching at their feet in an ecstasy of joy", "context_len": 73}}}, "postponed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 62, "context": "It was such a beautiful , stanch , great ship , with its large masts and spars and network of interlacing halyards , that its wideness meant more to her just then than even the wideness of the sea ; and she felt so safe and at home on it withal , that all the expected uncanny sensations had need to be postponed to some more favorable occasion", "context_len": 68}}}, "vent": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 69, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "We who take the forts are provided with hammers and long nails , to drive down the vent holes of the cannon ; when we have done this , we are to wait until one of the galleys fetches us off", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 66, "context": "She lived for others ; whether in the great work of her life , the use of her pen , the proceeds of which went to fill her charity purse , or in the simple act of leaving her quiet room , on a dull , rainy afternoon , to play a bright country dance or Scotch reel , and set the little ones dancing to vent their superfluous spirits", "context_len": 70}}}, "grumbling": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "Meanwhile the night had fallen so intensely dark that the completion of the task of feeding us unfortunates had to be accomplished by torchlight ; and we had not been very long left to ourselves before the faint flickering of distant lightning and the low muttering and grumbling of thunder warned us to expect a storm of more than ordinary violence", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 30, "context": "The air seemed to have grown more close and sultry while the children were within the cave , and , just as they reached the mouth , a low , grumbling sound , which they had heard for some time , but not taken much notice of , broke into a crash of thunder that seemed to make the ground shake beneath them , while at the same instant a brilliant streak of lightning flashed zigzag across the sky , lighting up the gloom behind them to its furthest recess", "context_len": 90}}}, "fortitude": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 121, "context": "on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me , died out , and I began to prepare myself , as best I could , to meet whatever fate might have in store for me with the fortitude befitting a Christian and an Englishman", "context_len": 128}}}, "losing": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 14, "context": "And do you think that people are to be all massed in heaven , losing their various identities , their differing tastes , their separate natures ? Going from this lower world so full of its adaptations , where colour and form take on a thousand changes , and life and pursuit can be varied almost at will , to a mere dead level of perfect felicity ? To leave earth where no two things are alike , and go to heaven to find no two different ! The Lord 's preparations mean more than that", "context_len": 96}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The losing was not indeed from any fault of my own , and had the pirate thrown it into the sea I should have held myself free from disgrace ; but as it was still in existence , and I knew its possessor , I was bound in honour to recover it", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Despite the fact that he was on the losing side , my father was not backward in declaring his attachment to His Gracious Majesty King Charles II ; and although our neighbours , even the Roundheads , were favourably disposed to him , making allowance for his fiery temper , yet with strangers who passed along the great highway betwixt London Town and Portsmouth , honest Owen \u2019 s outspoken declarations oft led to wordy strife , and on occasions ended in blows", "context_len": 83}}}, "shaken": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 56, "context": "If you had the courage you could go up the ladder into the curtained tower on the elephant 's back , and ride round the ring with some of the other fellows ; but my boy at least never had the courage ; and he never was of those who mounted the trick pony and were shaken off as soon as they got on", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Scull , being much shaken , is actually admitted within the walls of the old Hall , to the great disquiet of Mr", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "I never was more shaken with the excitement of temptation than at that moment", "context_len": 14}}}, "police": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 32, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "scared": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 24, "context": "Up till then the fishermen had been ready enough to go where I wanted , but the sight of that clump of galleys regularly scared them , and they wanted to row straight away ; but of course I pointed out to them that they had taken pay to do this thing , and that they had to do it", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 68, "context": "I know a dozen rich Moors in Tripoli or Tunis who would be glad to have him ; and we agreed that we would run down to the African coast for awhile , for that galley has been altogether too busy of late for our comfort , and will be all the more active after this little affair ; besides , people in these islands have got so scared that one ca n't get within ten miles of any of them now without seeing their signal smokes rising on the hills , and finding , when they land , the villages deserted and stripped of everything worth carrying away", "context_len": 109}}}, "bite": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 38, "context": "Even old Jumbo caught the infection , and though he very seldom condescended to take any notice of the other rabbits , now he gave Toby a playful poke with his nose , following it up by a bite on his ear that was not quite so playful", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 75, "context": "Bring that to bear over the western pint of the Junk of Pork , at high-water mark ; then bring the north-west side of Smutty Nose , and the south-east side of Oak Island , just touching on to each other , and you \u2019 ll be on a kelp shoal , where there \u2019 s plenty of rock cod , and where it is so shallow that at low water you can see them bite", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The father , however , felt eager to have the presumptuous Briton bite the dust under the triumphant blows of his eldest son", "context_len": 23}}}, "staggered": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 50, "context": "Guy had witnessed three or four such falls , and in his excitement scarcely knowing what he did , went up to the foaming animal intending to put it out of its misery by a shot from his gun , when , suddenly , it rose to its feet , staggered forward , and ere Guy could wheel his frightened horse , plunged his horns into his breast , and buffalo , horse and rider rolled upon the plain together", "context_len": 80}}}, "orphanage": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "At the orphan asylum there had never been fare such as this , and , after she had finished her chocolate pudding , and put in her pocket a juicy orange that she could not possibly eat , she bowed her head , and internally and thankfully repeated the orphanage grace after meat", "context_len": 53}}}, "giants": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 24, "context": "All round , before the circus doors were open , the doorkeepers of the side-shows were inviting people to come in and see the giants and fat woman and boa-constrictors , and there were stands for peanuts and candy and lemonade ; the vendors cried , `` Ice-cold lemonade , from fifteen hundred miles under ground ! Walk up , roll up , tumble up , any way to get up ! '' The boys thought this brilliant drolling , but they had no time to listen after the doors were open , and they had no money to spend on side-shows or dainties , anyway", "context_len": 106}}}, "bodies": {"freq": 99, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 99, "word_index": 50, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "crosswise": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Sceptre , suggested Marie-Celeste , coming to the rescue ; and then she wears -- for Marie-Celeste had studied the statue too -- a beautiful broad ribbon coming from one shoulder , crosswise this way to her belt , does n't she ?", "context_len": 43}}}, "flames": {"freq": 86, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 85, "word_index": 30, "context": "I was doing a most imprudent thing to linger by the side of the pyre , for the smoke , in the first place , and the light of the flames when it fell dark , could scarcely have failed to attract to the spot any savages who might have been in the neighbourhood , when my plight would probably have been as bad as ever ; but at that moment my sorrow at the loss of my companion overcame every other feeling , and , for the moment at least , I was quite indifferent as to what befell me", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 89, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "blood": {"freq": 282, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 280, "word_index": 64, "context": "had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed , with feelings of", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` When we can not get blood we can drink the red wine , The Sea King sang in his might ; For it maddens the brain , it gives strength to the arm , And kindles the soul in the fight", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Bomba thought at first that it might be blood from his head , for he remembered a terrific blow as he fell", "context_len": 22}}}, "licked": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 73, "context": "He ran after the children with little short , sharp barks , worrying at their heels till Aunt Helen declared there would soon not be a whole stocking left in the establishment ; he had a fray at once with Jack , but was much worsted by that worthy , returning to Peggy for protection with his scrap of a tail between his legs ; he tore Bobby 's cricket-ball to pieces , licked the polish off Father 's boots , devoured Aunt Helen 's goloshes , and nestled cosily down to sleep on the top of Nancy 's best Sunday hat , which that luckless damsel had imprudently left in an open band-box on the kitchen settle", "context_len": 118}}}, "sooty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The red flames were from their blood that was being licked up against the sooty stones", "context_len": 16}}}, "ghastliness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "My boy was too young to know whether it did either ; but he was exactly the right age , when on another night there was a large picture of Death on a Pale Horse shown , to be harrowed to the bottom of his soul by its ghastliness", "context_len": 49}}}, "cat": {"freq": 127, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 125, "word_index": 39, "context": "These sand-birds feed on the shore till they are chock brimful , and the tide comes and drives them off ; then they fly on to these ledges ; but they are as afraid of getting wet as a cat ; and when the tide comes up around the rock , they huddle together to keep out of the water , till they are all in a bunch , and the rock looks blue with them ; it \u2019 s the greatest chance for a shot ; but , continued he , after a pause , perhaps Mrs", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` So , you cat with nine lives , we 've to thank you for this beautiful fix ! '' remarked Captain Nicholson after he had greeted his companions in misfortune", "context_len": 31}}}, "rubbing": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 117, "context": "of his love and affection for his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour , was wont , every day , when he awoke from his sleep , to open the window and look at her therefrom ; so he arose on the morrow , according to his wont , and opened his chamber-window , so he might see his daughter ; but [ 571 ] when he put out his head and looked for Alaeddin 's palace , he beheld nothing but a place swept [ and level ] , like as it was aforetime , and saw neither palace nor inhabitants ; [ 572 ] whereat amazement clad him and his wit was bewildered and he fell to rubbing his eyes , so haply they were bleared or dimmed", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Then slings were fastened round the crate , the tackle creaked , and I was on my way to the open air , the box rubbing and grinding against the sides of the shaft in its ascent", "context_len": 37}}}, "firelight": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "Then the ghastliness vanished , and she approvingly took in the picture , -- the fat young creamery shark standing over the white cat and rubbing her with his toe , the firelight on the wall and snowy table , and the big lamp on the mantel", "context_len": 47}}}, "lamp": {"freq": 111, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 108, "word_index": 61, "context": "Then he took leave of the Sultan and going forth , mounted and rode , with his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like ,", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "But look at the light at my Lord Spangle Down 's door , it is the most glorious of them all , and held by poor little Jetty Back ! Jetty Back ! Jetty Back , where did you light your lamp to-night ? ''", "context_len": 45}}}, "mantel": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 77, "context": "The next thing , and sometimes a better thing than knowing your favorite author , is to know how and where he lives ; and it was a matter of supreme delight to Marie-Celeste that henceforth when she should open Lewis Carroll 's books she should be able to picture him working away here in his study , and just as he really looked , too , for by chance or accidents full-length photograph stood on the mantel , which Dorothy , from her visita few years before , was able to pronounce an excellent likeness , and very characteristic", "context_len": 100}}}, "pup": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` Why , that Patsy 's a reg'lar bum ! He 's called me names -- he plays hookey too , and he tried to trip me up and I give him a left-hander , and he called me a stinking pup and ever so many nasty names and then we went at it", "context_len": 54}}}, "vacated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Jack got up , the conductor took the vacated seat , and waited one , two , three minutes , and then Jack reappeared from between the curtains of the drawing-room at the rear of the car", "context_len": 37}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The donkey was then led down the steps of the platform , and Lord Shaftesbury remarked , `` I hope the reporters of the press will state that , the donkey having vacated the chair , the place was taken by Lord Shaftesbury ''", "context_len": 44}}}, "outstretched": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 45, "context": "Nothing was farther from Timothy 's thoughts than to make any disturbance whatever -- why should he ? Were they not the best of friends , he and that blessed little Albert ? so he never raised his head from where it rested upon his outstretched paws , only looked up with that gaze of implicit confidence peculiar to the kind eyes of the Laverick setter , and which made Albert lose not a second in spreading his little coat out beneath him , throwing his two arms around Timothy 's neck , and pillowing his head on his beautiful silky coat", "context_len": 102}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 50, "context": "In her longing for the human love and sympathy she had once cast from her , and which she could not now bring herself to ask , the poor old lady almost forgot that there was one Eye to see the struggles made for Jesus ' sake , one Hand outstretched to save and to help , one Voice to whisper , `` Be of good courage", "context_len": 67}}}, "nips": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` He nips worse'n a lobster", "context_len": 6}}}, "lobster": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "You don \u2019 t want any ; rock fish will bite at clams ; and it is most low water ; then you can get some ; and if you could get a lobster it would be first rate", "context_len": 39}}}, "rampaging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I guess that rat 's rampaging again", "context_len": 7}}}, "indicated": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 79, "context": "She further mentioned that , as she felt sure her father would have despatched a party in pursuit of us , which , she expected , would by this time be , not far behind us , it would be very desirable to keep a watch for them , since it was important that we should know as much as possible of their movements ; and she accordingly suggested that I should climb a particularly lofty tree which she indicated , and keep a look-out for them , while she went off into the forest to seek the wherewithal to furnish a breakfast", "context_len": 103}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "That would have taken too long , but , after quickly scratching his initials on the back of the turtle 's shell , together with the date , so he would know the reptile again , Fenn replaced it on the ground , and started off through the woods in the indicated direction", "context_len": 53}}}, "fro": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 17, "context": "At their foot the foam fretted and beat , and the forests of seaweed washed to and fro , presenting an almost impenetrable barrier to any one wishing to land ; though here it was impossible , for the racing current formed another barrier , which a boat propelled by stout rowers would hardly have passed", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Walkin ' to an ' fro las ' night , a verse o ' Scripter kep ' comin ' to me , 'Children , obey your parents in the Lord -- ' Now , I ai n't got any parents , but I had lady-boards", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Jephson 's face gradually changed in colour fro light bronze to a deep purple", "context_len": 14}}}, "lank": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Minley waited on them herself , and , as she passed to and fro between the dining-room and kitchen , she bestowed many glances on the lean , lank , little girl with the brown face", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Early next morning the work of pumping was resumed , and as the water sank slowly down the mouth of the cavity , a dark object showed amidst the lank weeds", "context_len": 31}}}, "nudged": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "The recognition seemed to be mutual , for the elder child nudged the younger , and Peggy could see that they were looking her up and down , and evidently taking in all the details of her costume", "context_len": 38}}}, "elbow": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 16, "context": "Mamma used to dress me in white dresses with short sleeves that came just to my elbow , tied round with pink or blue ribbons , and a sash to match , tied on one side in front , and I knew it was pretty and stylish , and used to walk around with my head in the air , and people would laugh and say I was awfully cunning", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "A tall burly fellow swaggered into the room , and flung into the elbow chair with a weight that made it creak", "context_len": 22}}}, "downcast": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 21, "context": "Though he was unaware , of course , that they had overheard his conversation with Dodds , he guessed from their downcast faces that they knew that he intended to desert them on his and Drusie 's birthday , and was not going near the fort", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "Maggie and Bessie obeyed , but slowly and unwillingly , as the old lady grieved to see , the former with drooping head and downcast eyes , while Bessie peeped shyly up at her aunt from under her eyelashes", "context_len": 39}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Every time I git sorter billious and downcast an \u2019 dejec \u2019 ed I look \u2019 round till I find somebody that \u2019 s wuss off than I be , an \u2019 then I take holt an \u2019 try to give them a lift , an \u2019 that cheers me up an \u2019 makes me feel thankful an \u2019 content with my lot", "context_len": 63}}}, "knife": {"freq": 161, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 159, "word_index": 38, "context": "First , the hearth , on which no spot or speck was to be seen , must be brushed up anew ; next , the corner of the table-cloth was to be twitched into place , and a knife laid more exactly into straight line ; then a ball , belonging to one of the younger children , was picked up and put in the toy-basket , with the reminder to little Tommy that father was coming , and the room must be kept in good order", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Take the point of your knife away , Lodo", "context_len": 9}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Doto break branches so that Bomba can get knife that is at his belt , directed the jungle boy", "context_len": 19}}}, "fork": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 59, "context": "Peggy and Bobby as 'the children , ' with crumpled pinafores and smudged faces , were an equal success , for in watching Archie Peggy had forgotten her sudden shyness , and she now threw herself thoroughly into her part , howling most realistically when her nose was scrubbed in the wash-tub , or her hair combed with a fork ; while Bobby stole jam and resisted medicine with lifelike zeal", "context_len": 71}}}, "ham": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` You 've got ham and eggs ; fried petetters and toast , and two kinds of preserve , and hot rolls and coffee , and cake and doughnuts , which is more 'n you ever got at the asylum , I 'll warrant , and yet you 're crying , -- and after all the trouble you 've been to me", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Oh , that ai n't ham to me , '' she said , eloquently", "context_len": 15}}}, "kinds": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 79, "word_index": 9, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}}}, "rolls": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 34, "context": "Men who had prospected for precious metals all summer in the mountains now bundled their pots and pans and blankets back to shelter for the winter ; the long-eared burros , lost in great rolls of bedding , stood about the tipple awaiting the result of their masters ' interviews with the mine boss , concerning work and the occupancy of any `` shack '' that might still be empty", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 63, "context": "He loved equality and fraternity , and he came out to America towards the close of the last century to prospect for these as well as for a good location to manufacture Welsh flannels ; but after being presented to Washington , then President , at Philadelphia , and buying a tract of land somewhere near the District of Columbia , his phantom rolls a shadowy barrel of dollars on board ship at Baltimore , and sails back in the Flying Dutchman to South Wales", "context_len": 85}}}, "doughnuts": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` You 've got ham and eggs ; fried petetters and toast , and two kinds of preserve , and hot rolls and coffee , and cake and doughnuts , which is more 'n you ever got at the asylum , I 'll warrant , and yet you 're crying , -- and after all the trouble you 've been to me", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "I \u2019 d took along some bread and doughnuts out of the pantry , and , when it come morning and I could feel the sun shining , we had breakfast side of a little brook , after which we crept into the bushes and hid all day long", "context_len": 49}}}, "warrant": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 76, "context": "If our vows had not forbidden our fighting I should have called him to account long ago ; but the only thing else to do was for me to lodge a formal complaint before the bailiff , of his continually offensive bearing and manner , which I could not bring myself to do , and indeed there was no special matter that would have seemed to justify me , no single speech that in itself would warrant such grave action on my part", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 35, "context": "No wonder that the boy saw with his eyes and heard with his ears and modelled himself as nearly as he could on the ideals of his hero ; and when Colonel Harrison signed the warrant for the king 's execution , the boy was as convinced a regicide as any of the judges whose names were written beside that of Harrison on the fatal parchment", "context_len": 66}}}, "satisfying": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 77, "context": "canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have heard of , they had never actually seen a white man before , a conclusion which I afterwards found to be correct", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "He gathered some jaboty eggs which he ate raw , and which , with some cured meat he had brought with him from the cabin of Pipina , made a simple but satisfying meal", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The odor of the broiling meat whetted every appetite and the meal was one of the most satisfying of which they had partaken since crossing the Missouri", "context_len": 27}}}, "wiped": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 67, "context": "No fear of that , but he such a big , hulking chap and eats so much that he oughter be made to do something ; I \u2019 m going to put him on tother side of this wagon and make him b \u2019 leve it \u2019 s the most important post of all , and that if he drops asleep , the whole shebang will be wiped out by the redskins", "context_len": 72}}}, "stolidly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "Not to be outdone in politeness , we hoisted our colours also ; and for the next quarter of an hour the two craft continued to close , the chase stolidly maintaining her course , while we , under the skipper 's skilful conning , continued to edge very gradually away , as the other vessel sped to leeward , checking our weather braces by a few inches at a time until our yards were all but square", "context_len": 78}}}, "picking": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 72, "context": "I could , of course , leap through a port into the sea , taking my chance of being picked up by the pursuing craft , which I fondly hoped would be the avenging Gannet ; but I did not know what distance separated us , and even then , in the eagerness of the chase , there was little likelihood of their noticing me , still less of heaving to and picking me up", "context_len": 75}}}, "toothpick": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "He was in excellent humour himself , and , sitting tilted back in his chair by the fireplace , played a tune on his big white teeth with a toothpick", "context_len": 30}}}, "plumb": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "I won \u2019 t keep y \u2019 u up no longer , for I know y \u2019 u must be tired an \u2019 want to go to bed anyhow , I \u2019 m dead sartain your brother is plumb pegged out", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "So strong was the construction of the masonry that the work of destruction was only partially done , though the keep was riven from base to summit , and several of the smaller towers were thrown bodily out of plumb", "context_len": 40}}}, "drapery": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "A white bed in the alcove , white window drapery , a carpet with considerable light blue in it , a dressing case , a writing desk , some books and pictures , mostly Madonnas", "context_len": 35}}}, "cur": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 27, "context": "'The way you rolled him flat was the funniest sight I 've seen for many a year ! Get up , Jones , you sneaking , drivelling cur ! ' -- kicking the prostrate form of that fallen hero", "context_len": 39}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Shut up , you cur", "context_len": 5}}}, "viciously": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 21, "context": "But he could not get near enough to slash , as the boa \u2019 s head , reared aloft , struck viciously at him whenever he came almost within reaching distance", "context_len": 31}}}, "unsteadiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "A little , thin , old , brown cur staggered out , with lips viciously rolled back , and a curious unsteadiness of gait", "context_len": 24}}}, "wobbles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` It wobbles from having tin cans tied to it", "context_len": 10}}}, "cans": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 64, "context": "The whole family was gathered together on the grass round the wood fire , the smoke of which rose up faintly blue against the russet of the beech-woods behind -- handsome , fierce-eyed men , lying slouching idly on the ground smoking short black pipes ; slatternly women with gay handkerchiefs tied round their black hair , bustling about with something savoury inside tin cans ; ragged brown-skinned little children , gnawing at bones with savage haste ; while a few disreputable dogs waited eagerly for the scraps that were thrown to them from time to time", "context_len": 97}}}, "poured": {"freq": 93, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 93, "word_index": 17, "context": "Appalled by the disaster that had befallen them , by the terrible effect of the broadsides , poured in at a few yards ' distance , and by the sight of so many of the dreaded warriors of the Cross , some of the corsairs threw down their arms and flung themselves on the deck or into the hold , crying for quarter ; those who resisted fell either under the swords of the knights , the vengeful axes of the late captives , or the pikes of the Sards ; but the great bulk , leaping from the bow or stern , swam ashore", "context_len": 105}}}, "saucer": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 76, "context": "In the grate there was a glowing , blazing fire ; on the hob was a little brass kettle hissing and boiling ; spread upon the floor was a thick , warm crimson rug ; before the fire a folding-chair , unfolded , and with cushions on it ; by the chair a small folding-table , unfolded , covered with a white cloth , and upon it spread small covered dishes , a cup , a saucer , a teapot ; on the bed were new warm coverings and a satin-covered down quilt ; at the foot a curious wadded silk robe , a pair of quilted slippers , and some books", "context_len": 112}}}, "partially": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` From the moment when I first became aware of my father 's illness I was not entirely free from suspicion ; and when at length I saw that your efforts to cure him were only partially successful , and that his symptoms persistently recurred , I was convinced that there was foul play somewhere , though why , I could not at first imagine", "context_len": 65}}}, "blind": {"freq": 159, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` Oh , mamma ! '' exclaimed Bessie , as her mother just then entered the room , `` what do you think ? This very nice woman lives with my station policeman , who was so kind to me , and his name is Yichards , and he has a lame baby and a sick wife and a blind boy , and no doctor to pay , and the children must be fed , and a great deal of trouble , and she do n't get well because of it , and he does have trust in the Lord , but he has n't helped him yet -- ''", "context_len": 110}, "NOUN": {"freq": 98, "word_index": 16, "context": "Why will you not openly share with us the pleasure we must all feel at the blind boy 's restoration to sight ? Did you not see dear Bessie 's wistful look at you as she bade you good-night ? These little ones can not understand why there should be any reason to hide such kindness as you have shown to these people , or why you should refuse to show an interest you really feel", "context_len": 76}, "VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 19, "context": "Whatever price the rich Colonel Harrington was paying Lem Wacker for his co\u00f6peration , it was not enough to blind that individual to a realization of the fact that accident had placed in Wacker 's grasp the great haul of his life , and he was making off with this fortune , leaving the colonel in the lurch", "context_len": 58}}}, "smelled": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 1, "context": "He smelled about it for a moment , and then , with a snort of disgust , turned about , and dashing his heels upon it , sent it flying over the cliff , then walked quietly to the sledge , and placed himself ready to be harnessed , with an air which said , 'That can do no more harm", "context_len": 61}}}, "condescended": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 60, "context": "When the Vizier heard the Sultan 's words , his tongue was tied for despite and he was overcome with exceeding chagrin , forasmuch as the King had promised him that he would marry his daughter to his son ; so , after a little , he said to him , `` O King of the age , Thy Grace condescended to promise me [ 371 ] that the Lady Bedrulbudour should be my son 's ; wherefore it behoveth thine exalted highness appoint a delay of three months , [ 372 ] and God willing , my son 's present shall be greater than this", "context_len": 106}}}, "lick": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 8, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Yes , I will ! Them ole cadets git after me , now , by jingo , I 'll go out there an ' yank some of 'em out that parade an ' lick them all t'once", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 76, "context": "Certain tones in the man 's hearty voice reminded me of the boy 's sweet notes , and was n't there a queer suggestion of each other when I stood near them ? We Shetland ponies as I have said before are very close to human beings for our ancestors were literally brought up with the children in the crofters ' huts , and my mother has often told me that her mother had many a lick at the family platter and many a time her soft muzzle was buried in the children 's necks", "context_len": 95}}}, "stroking": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` He 's got to go , '' said Lucas , stroking Poacher 's head , `` an ' mind me , dog , '' and he put his hand under the dog 's jaws and lifted them so that he could look in his eyes , `` no runnin ' away from Ciscasset", "context_len": 54}}}, "afore": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 32, "context": "Pshaw ! dat ain \u2019 t nuffin , he said when the wife of Abner Fleming complimented him ; I \u2019 spects to do de same thing a good many more times afore we gits across de plains ; de fac \u2019 is , I \u2019 m on to dem Injins and dey \u2019 ll find it out ; when dey wokes up Jethro Mix dey wokes up de wrong passenger and dere \u2019 s gwine to be trouble in de land", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 21, "context": "I left Roy asleep , and I never guessed as he 'ud wake ; I thought I 'd be back long afore , and I never guessed as you 'd come home ; I never , never guessed it", "context_len": 39}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Shagbark tole me how dem critters chaw and stomp and bite a feller ; I knowed dat if I brunged down dis one , it would make de oder two so mad dey \u2019 d come at me afore I could load up ag \u2019 in , and you wouldn \u2019 t hab any Jeth any more", "context_len": 57}}}, "hovering": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 64, "context": "eerie sitting by the peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to laugh at the supernatural , she did not feel quite so brave as she pretended , and found little cold shivers stealing down her back when the wind rose suddenly , and began to wail round the cottage like a hungry creature waiting", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "The words could not be distinguished , but when the soft tones , like those of an angel hovering near reached the couple , the trapper abruptly stopped and listened", "context_len": 30}}}, "reel": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Even as the thought formed in his mind , there came a crash , a jar that shook the automobile in every part , and made it reel drunkenly , and a clash of broken glass", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 57, "context": "She lived for others ; whether in the great work of her life , the use of her pen , the proceeds of which went to fill her charity purse , or in the simple act of leaving her quiet room , on a dull , rainy afternoon , to play a bright country dance or Scotch reel , and set the little ones dancing to vent their superfluous spirits", "context_len": 70}}}, "doggedly": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 22, "context": "Keeping close together , and yawing from time to time in order to deliver a broadside at our pursuers , we held doggedly on our course , till at length a flat calm set in , and both fleets lay inactive at a mile apart , in which situation darkness again overtook us", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Well , it 's the truth , '' he went on doggedly", "context_len": 13}}}, "poorhouse": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 78, "context": "She waited for her hearer to ask why the mother was forbidden to come where the daughter could enter , but he did not do so , and she continued , `` She was a poor woman from Boston , an ' her mother was a poor woman from Canada , an ' they said if she come in 'twould be two poor women together , an ' first thing they knowed they 'd be both in the poorhouse", "context_len": 79}}}, "curly": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 15, "context": "Little feet would come pattering , or skipping along the hall , a small , curly head peep within the door , and then vanish at sight of her , while a whisper of `` She 's there ; let 's run , '' told the cause of its sudden disappearance", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "But alas ! I for one know to a Certainty that in the little castle there was rebellion almost amounting to mutiny , and that one curly , uncrowned head , that need not have had a care in all the world , was tossing uneasily on its pillow", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Do n't you think all babies ought to be pretty ? And have curly hair ? ''", "context_len": 17}}}, "servant": {"freq": 104, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 92, "word_index": 122, "context": "she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little creature whom every servant and every governess alike dreaded", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 77, "context": "It is very hard on a girl to be dressed as shabbily as I am , and to be twitted by her companions for what she can not help ; and although you kept me at Cherry Court School , there have been times over and over when I hated you , Aunt Susan , and but for my dear little Mummy I would have left the school and earned my bread as a dressmaker or a servant", "context_len": 78}}}, "squealed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "When the men come to kill the pigs I 'd shut myself in my room , an ' put my fingers in my ears , an ' I could n't hear , but I 'd always squeal when the pigs squealed", "context_len": 41}}}, "eloquently": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "But Donald afterward exonerated Ted from any lack of courage , and was of course delighted when he found that she had pleaded his cause so eloquently as to convince even the old keeper that Ted was fully justified in the course he had thought best to pursue", "context_len": 48}}}, "teeny": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "He lets me wash , an ' scrub , an ' cook , an ' never says a word excep ' not to work too hard , an ' if he wants to be jus ' a little bit cranky , jus ' a teeny little bit , he goes in his room an ' shuts the door till the bad spirit gets out of him", "context_len": 66}}}, "kite": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 3, "context": "But after the kite was pasted , and dried in the sun , or behind the kitchen stove , if you were in very much of a hurry ( and you nearly always were ) , it had to be hung , with belly-bands and tail-bands ; that is , with strings carried from stick to stick over the face and at the bottom , to attach the cord for flying it and to fasten on the tail by", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "interjected": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` You 're a queer little kite , '' interjected the young man , and he exchanged an amused glance with Mrs", "context_len": 22}}}, "forth": {"freq": 295, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 167, "word_index": 60, "context": "He ceased not to go daily to the market of the jewellers and to clap up acquaintance with the folk , making friends with them and questioning them of buying and selling and giving and taking and dear and cheap , till , one day of the days , he arose in the morning and donning his clothes , went forth , intending , as of wont , for the jewellers ' market ; but , as he went , he heard the crier proclaiming aloud on this wise , `` By commandment of the Lord of Beneficence , the king of the age and monarch of the time and the tide , let all the folk shut their shops and stores and enter their houses , for", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 119, "word_index": 99, "context": "the safe keeping of the lamp under the earth ; and he looked well into [ 547 ] the figures , both mothers and daughters , [ 548 ] but saw not the lamp , whereupon rage overrode him and he smote the sand a second time , that he might certify himself of Alaeddin 's death , but saw him not in the treasure ; whereat he redoubled in wrath , and yet more when it was certified to him that the lad was alive upon the surface of the earth and he knew that he had come forth from under the ground and had gotten the lamp , on account whereof he himself had suffered toil and torment such as passeth man 's power to endure", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "But working with a will sometimes means nothing more than determination to do one 's duty ; and from what we happen to know , Chris would much have preferred setting cheerily forth on his round in Uncle Sam 's far-away city , and Donald was probably dreaming of the blue boundless sea and the steamer ploughing its way in the teeth of a driving nor'easter", "context_len": 66}}}, "washerwoman": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The washerwoman 's little girl had heard footsteps behind her on the drive and a distinct clanking as of chains , while old Betty Carson swore on her Bible oath that she had seen something white moving about among the ruins , which groaned as in the expression of the keenest mental anguish ; and when Mr", "context_len": 57}}}, "pantry": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 49, "context": "He grew very tame , and butted children over , right and left , in the village streets ; and he behaved like one of the family whenever he got into a house ; he ate the sugar out of the bowl on the table , and plundered the pantry of its sweet cakes", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "repeated": {"freq": 232, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 226, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` No , '' repeated Lena , still more vexed that he should suppose her to be capable of such an evasion , which to her sense of uprightness would have been as bad as speaking a falsehood by word of mouth ; `` no , of course I did not , that would have been telling her , would it not ? One can do a falsehood as well as tell it , '' and although she had intended no reflection upon her brother , no thrust at him , Percy was ashamed as he remembered how often , during the last few months , he had done this very thing ; how he had shuffled and evaded , and thought it no great harm as long", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "Their repeated disasters weakened every man 's heart , and gave rise to a well-founded belief that there was treachery in their midst , and that plans decided even in their secret councils were made known to the Danes", "context_len": 39}}}, "interlocutor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "'Tilda Jane did not understand a word of what he was saying , but his gestures were expressive , and she anxiously watched his interlocutor", "context_len": 25}}}, "slapped": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` -- And he slapped me in the face , and I kicked his shins good , and then we fit and I give him a punch in the belly and a good bunk in the eye -- ''", "context_len": 39}}}, "whipped": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 64, "context": "with what a retinue did he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions , but she can", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "Captain Poynings was already on board , directing his officers , while gun after gun was dragged over greased planks to the ship \u2019 s side , there to be `` whipped '' aboard by heavy tackle", "context_len": 37}}}, "coldest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "96 ) : `` Creeks make the boys swim in the coldest weather ; make them frequently undergo scratching from head to foot , through the skin , with broken glass or gar-fish teeth [ 120 ] , so as to make them all in a gore of blood , and then wash them with cold water ; this is with them the arcanum against all diseases ; but when they design it as a punishment to the boys , they dry-scratch them , i", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Duke told her he liked the cold weather and that he always lived in the coldest part of the city", "context_len": 21}}}, "icicles": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "He wore a most beautiful crown of snow and he showed the Icicle family the compliment of wearing icicles from his beard and his crown and his locks of snow and from his hanging snowy sleeves", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "You should see my children and animals disporting themselves in the snow and on the ice -- often icicles hanging to them , their breath like steam", "context_len": 27}}}, "crazy": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 55, "context": "Well , this little queen , began Marie-Celeste , was the child of a French king , and she was born in the Louvre , the King 's palace in Paris , and she was born in a very troubled time -- such a troubled time , that her father , the King , went crazy ; and then the little Isabel spent most of her time in the Hotel de St", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 69, "context": "They 'll come ravagin ' out -- 'Ruth Ann , that young one 's run off ! ' An ' I 'll be busy with my pots an ' pans , an ' all I 'll have to say is : 'Do tell ! ' or , 'Why , how you talk ! ' An ' sister 'll rave an ' tear , an ' run round like a crazy thing , an ' look at Mis ' Grannis out o ' the corner of her eye", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Well , '' he said , `` I s'pose there 's no hope for it , but I wish I 'd gone in with some fellows who were n't crazy on the girl question", "context_len": 35}}}, "lugged": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "I says , ' I 'll take him in bed with me an ' warm him for a spell , an ' no one 'll know ; ' so I lugged him in the house , an ' he cuddled down on my arm just so cunnin '", "context_len": 48}}}, "awake": {"freq": 94, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then I tried to stay awake , so I could carry him out early in the mornin ' , but did n't I fall to sleep , an ' the first thing I knowed there was the matron a-spearin ' me with her eyes , an she put out her hand to ketch the dog , an ' he up an ' bit her , an ' then there was trouble", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 36, "context": "At another time nurse would have ordered them to shut their eyes and not speak another word ; but to-night she seemed to think it quite right and natural that they should be so very wide awake , and not only gave them an extra amount of petting and kissing , but told them stories of Uncle Ruthven 's pranks when he was a boy , and of his wonderful sayings and doings , till mamma , coming up and finding this going on , was half inclined to find fault with the old woman herself", "context_len": 96}, "VERB": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 28, "context": "He felt better after this and went in ; but that night the excitement of the adventure and the effects of his immersion were sufficient to keep him awake hour after hour , while when he dropped off into an uneasy slumber it was for his mind to be haunted by dreams in which he was being dragged down into the depths of the sea by a strange monster that clung to his limbs and writhed about him , making him shudder as he felt the chilling embrace", "context_len": 88}}}, "managers": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 61, "context": "He did not tell her that he had heard it was one of the few badly managed institutions for orphans in the State , that the children were kept strictly , fed poorly , and were rapidly `` institutionalised '' while under the care of uneducated , ignorant women , who were only partially supervised by a vacillating board of lady managers", "context_len": 62}}}, "demonstrative": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "She was very resolute ; very persevering ; very affectionate ; reserved , yet demonstrative ; untidy , yet methodical ; exceedingly anxious for the happiness of all around ; apt often to think people better than they really were ; generous to a fault ; unselfishly ready at all times to put her own wishes aside ; vehement and impulsive , yet never in a hurry or flurry ; unyielding , yet tender ; severe , yet frisky", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "At the exact hour agreed upon as the best time for dinner , the little maid , turned cook and waitress , announced the meal as ready , and her reward came in the children 's demonstrative approval", "context_len": 38}}}, "feverishness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was not in her nature to be very demonstrative , yet she could not altogether hide a certain feverishness and anxiety", "context_len": 22}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "I tried in vain to shake off the feeling , and at last I pretended to trace it to feverishness resulting from the wound in the scalp ; but I knew this was not so -- I knew that one of the great things of life was behind it all ; I knew that I had come to the hour that young men hope for and older men dread ; I knew that for good or evil my future was wrapped in the mystery and tangle of which Jane Ryder was the centre", "context_len": 93}}}, "subdued": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 3, "context": "Their voices were subdued lest they should reach the king 's ears ; but , respectful as was the bearing of all the members of the group by the fire , they clearly split into two halves : on the one hand , the officers of the escort who were teasing the boy , and on the other , a group of gentlemen , some wearing the conventional ribbons and laces of a cavalier , others in the rough cloth of country wear , stained with the mud of country lanes , while the master and mistress of the house moved from one guest to another , evidently nervous at the doubtful honour that such a royal visit had brought to their roof", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "I had an excellent view of our poor dear Queen ; and the sight of her mournful subdued countenance , as she bowed graciously to her people , but without the shadow of a smile , quite touched my heart", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "All the remainder of that day Grandpapa went around very much subdued", "context_len": 12}}}, "suppress": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "The Scholarship money would have done more good to a poorer girl '' -- and here Sir John had quickly to suppress a sigh , for was he not thinking of Kitty -- Kitty , who had never looked sweeter than during this evening of defeat , who had never , never been nearer to his heart ?", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Indeed , Chris himself could not quite suppress a smile as he saw the relief expressed in every line of Martha 's face at the click of the closing door", "context_len": 30}}}, "gravity": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 16, "context": "And that reminds me , Dick , '' she added , with a sudden access of gravity , `` if you had not been clever enough to save my life when the snake bit me , you would most certainly have been one of the victims ; indeed it was with a view to sacrificing you at the Customs that my father accepted you from the Igbo", "context_len": 67}}}, "entertain": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 37, "context": "Such proceedings as these could not of course long exist without the fame of them reaching the ears of the king , and I had only given some three or four performances when I was summoned to entertain his Majesty and his household , which I did in the great square before the palace , my audience numbering quite two thousand ; Banda and his numerous family being seated in a huge semicircle -- of which I was the centre -- in front of the palace , while the rest of the audience filled the remaining portion of the square", "context_len": 100}}}, "rapped": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 44, "context": "Then , towards the end of the week they heard that he had the flux ; and on Friday , just before school let out , the teacher -- it was the one that whipped so , and that the fellows all liked -- rapped on his desk , and began to speak very solemnly to the scholars", "context_len": 58}}}, "hammer": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 14, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "Stanton reminded him of his work , he would hammer or cut away for one moment ; the next his hands would be clasping his knees in an ecstasy of delight and wonder at the strange but true tale he was listening to", "context_len": 43}}}, "dissection": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "One lady was at the top , an ' she rapped on the table with a little hammer , an ' said , `` Tention , ladies ! ' Then she says , 'Here is the 'fortinate object of dissection", "context_len": 40}}}, "stake": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 30, "context": "Of course I was but one of many who were to gasp out their lives in this dreadful Aceldama ; and in a very short time each post , or stake , was decorated with its own separate victim , some of whom , it seemed , were to perish by the torture of fire , for after the victims had been secured to the stakes , huge bundles of faggots , composed of dry twigs and branches , were piled around some of them", "context_len": 85}}}, "president": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 9, "context": "One perfectly good chair was brought up for the president , the rest were content to be seated on whatever came handy , two chairs very much gone as to backs , one with the bottom entirely through , and a rickety camp stool made up the remainder of the furniture , but Agnes had taken care that there were flowers on the table and that pens , pencils and paper were supplied", "context_len": 73}}}, "feverishly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 16, "context": "Irene herself poured out the tea and brought it to little Agnes , who drank it feverishly , and then lay down ; but she was too tired and too ill from her journey to care to eat any cakes", "context_len": 40}}}, "mam": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "One lady said she 's mend the mendment , an ' then the mam pressiding got kind o ' mixy-maxy , an ' said they 'd better start all over agin , 'cause she 'd lose her way 'mong so many mendments", "context_len": 42}}}, "staked": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "He measured the distance still to be traversed , staked his all on one swift run , sped across the treacherous log , and with one last leap reached the farther shore in safety", "context_len": 34}}}, "lily": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "Says she , 'Here is a little girl -- she come to us like a lily o ' the field ; no dress on , no bunnit , no nothin '", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Guy thought he had never witnessed so sad a scene as when they laid the beautiful baby , that looked as pure and sweet as a white lily , in the rough coffin that some of the young men had hastily made , and carried him to a lonely spot , that perhaps no feet had ever trod before , and , breathing a prayer over him , left him to his long sleep , far from the place of his birth , or that for which his kindred were bound , and where never a tear would be dropped above him , or a sigh breathed", "context_len": 107}}}, "field": {"freq": 291, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 291, "word_index": 52, "context": "Among other things were two or three peacock feathers , a turkey wing , some pebbles from the brook where papa used to sail his boats when he was a boy , a piece of rusty tin pipe , which , because it looked black and smoky , and came from the field where the burnt barn had stood , they persuaded themselves must be a part of the very leader down which papa slid when he ran for the ladder to save his little brother , -- all these , and other treasures of like value , were carefully collected and stowed in the wagons , to be carried to the Lake House , and thence to the city", "context_len": 120}}}, "guv": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "We 've fed an ' clothed the lily , an ' guv her good advice , an ' she 's lifted up her heel agin us", "context_len": 26}}}, "deifies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "She deifies us , she introjuces toads an ' snakes into the sacred presings of our sinningcherry for orphans", "context_len": 19}}}, "sacred": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 32, "context": "But in carrying a city by storm , which takes place usually at an unexpected time , and often in the night , the maddened and victorious assaulter suddenly burst into the sacred scenes of domestic peace , and seclusion , and love -- the very worst of men , filled with the worst of passions , stimulated by the resistance they have encountered , and licensed by their victory to give all these passions the fullest and most unrestricted gratification", "context_len": 81}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 70, "context": "I have often wondered why maidens should so carelessly bestow their favours upon every knight who begged for them , and have said to myself that when my time came I would grant it but once , and only then to one whom I deemed worthy of it in all ways -- one in whose loyalty and honour I could trust implicitly , and who would regard it as something sacred , deeming it an honour to wear it , as being the pledge of my trust and esteem", "context_len": 89}}}, "packs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "She packs a dirty dog in bed", "context_len": 7}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Backs began to ache under the drag of the packs ; entrenching tools began to make their presence aggressively known as they chafed the men 's legs ; rifles were being constantly shifted from shoulder to shoulder or carried at the trail , as the weapons seemingly increased in weight at each step", "context_len": 53}}}, "lowering": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 29, "context": "Whiles she goes away , and no one sees her for long enough , and whiles she comes back ; and they do say , ' said David , lowering his voice , 'that if there 's a death in the family , or any evil hangin ' over the Vaughans , that she be there shriekin ' and wringin ' her hands to give warnin ' of what be to come", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "You notice now and then , and you 'll see that Aunt Lou is forever changing the position of something , if it 's only a chair as she passes or the lowering of a window-shade by the fraction of an inch", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The glowing golden October weather had given place to several lowering days", "context_len": 12}}}, "key": {"freq": 110, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 92, "word_index": 14, "context": "I have a nice little pot buried in a safe place on a certain key ; I 've got a map here '' -- and he thumped his broad chest -- '' here , sewed in the lining of my coat , and the place marked with a cross ; and I tell you , sir , there 's enough gold in that pot to fit out the snuggest little pinnace any man need want to see", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 15, "context": "Panting , Stone heard Sage calling the signal , and at the sound of the key number every nerve in his body went taut as a bow-string ; for it was the play by which the most effective gains had been made in the first half Hayden was to go through Clearport \u2019 s right wing with the ball", "context_len": 59}}}, "reptiles": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` Of course it was a foolish , and , at the same time , a risky trick to play , and , while I believe you had no intention of keeping the bracelet , I can not but believe that in some way you removed it from the cabinet , either in catching up one of the reptiles hurriedly , or otherwise", "context_len": 63}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Then it turned partly around , and , to Fenn 's delight , started to crawl in the same direction as that taken by the other two reptiles the previous evening", "context_len": 31}}}, "quadruples": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "She knows not the place of reptiles an ' quadruples", "context_len": 10}}}, "thy": {"freq": 244, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 215, "word_index": 107, "context": "and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm , that thou shouldst merit of him this boon ? Again , this that thou cravest is beyond thy condition ; [ 336 ] so it can not be that the king will grant thee that which thou seekest", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 10, "context": "What could be easier than to take the money of thy father ? I tell you , Lolita , that you can not go to school because Mees Combs has had to use your money to pay them ! Eh , but your father will be mad ! He is not working himself to a bone that strangers should build themselves fine houses ! My Pablo said a little time ago that people said your father 's riches were going astray", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 61, "context": "`` The King , for all he knew that this was a thing whereto the Vizier might not avail , no , nor the greatest King , [ 373 ] nevertheless exercised his clemency [ 374 ] and granted him the delay he sought ; then , turning to the old woman , he said to her , `` Go to thy son and tell him I give him [ my ] word that my daughter shall be in his name ; [ 375 ] but needs must I take order for her equipment ; [ 376 ] wherefore it behoveth him grant us a delay of three months", "context_len": 109}}}, "straggle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "If thy foot straggle , treat it likewise", "context_len": 8}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "At the same moment of his arrival the innkeeper came back , and a little later the grooms began to straggle in", "context_len": 22}}}, "dropping": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` To school ! '' said the little girl , `` to learn more -- to know how to speak proper ! Oh , oh , I 'm mos ' too happy to live ! Hank Dillson , I think you 're the mos ' beautiful man that was ever made ! '' and , dropping her sugar-bowl on the shelf , she seized a hand of the ex-creamery shark , and warmly pressed it between her little lean palms", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Rush tried to have the children all settle down quietly again ; but the little ones were uneasy and disturbed , starting at every sound , -- the twitter of a bird , the splash of a fish , or the dropping of a leaf , -- and the Colonel , seeing this , hastened to bring his story to a close , and take them back to the house", "context_len": 70}}}, "juice": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 34, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}}}, "nip": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "'When did it happen ? ' asked Peggy , hastily , hoping to nip in the bud one of the old fellow 's , rambling divergences from the point", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Any of you fellows got some more ? No ; well , I 'll nip round to see if I can find any", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "But Uncle John did not mean to let him come out until he had shown Bessie what a nip he could give with those pincers of his", "context_len": 27}}}, "pets": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Very well ; it shall be as you say , '' replied the colonel , who , provided his pets were satisfied , was so himself , and after the children had gone , he said to his wife , `` Certainly there are few things in which our sweet little Maggie does not act up to the Golden Rule , of which she is so fond", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "PETS", "context_len": 1}}}, "squirrels": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Well , this lady went on an ' says she , 'Let 's send away the cats an ' dogs an ' all the children 's pets -- squirrels an ' pigeons an ' rabbits , 'cause this little girl 's disruptin ' every child on the place", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 64, "context": "A grassy glade under a tall beech seemed intended by Nature for a summer-house , for there was a large flat stone in the middle , which served for a table , and a circle of little stones round , just high enough for seats , so that you might imagine Queen Mab and her fairies dined here on moonlight nights , with the squirrels for guests and the bats and owls for waiters", "context_len": 74}}}, "pigeons": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 88, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}}}, "rabbits": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then the king shouted something to his women-folk which caused them to scuttle back into the palace like so many rabbits ; and the next moment the two guards who had me in charge marched me off to an empty hut behind the palace -- which was , luckily , to windward of the crucifixion tree , the odour from which therefore did not reach as far as my lodging -- and , having signed to me to enter , mounted guard , one on each side of the door", "context_len": 90}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "After they grew up into bigger rabbits some of them were given away and became the pets of other children and Clover and Pinky were together again once more without the young ones", "context_len": 33}}}, "stoned": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 95, "context": "They knew the fellows who were helping the priest ; one of them was the boy who stood on his head till he had to have it shaved ; they would have liked to mock him then and there for wearing a petticoat , and most of them had the bitterest scorn and hate for Catholics in their hearts ; but they were afraid of the sacristan , and they behaved very well as long as they were in the church ; but as soon as they got out they whooped and yelled , and stoned the sacristan when he ran after them", "context_len": 103}}}, "feller": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 66, "context": "I wouldn \u2019 t ; he \u2019 s a particular old toad , and would rather have it just as it grew ; but if you touch it , he \u2019 ll think you \u2019 ve taken off some where you ought not to , and spilte it ; he \u2019 ll no more thank you for saving him labor on a float-piece , than a feller would thank you for courting a girl for him ; he \u2019 d rather do it himself", "context_len": 84}}}, "lesson": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 88, "word_index": 31, "context": "None of our friends or kindred were on board the fleet ; and we traders have rather reason to rejoice , for , in the first place , so severe a lesson will keep the corsairs in their ports for a long time ; and in the second , had the fleet succeeded according to general expectation , so great a store of European goods would have been brought home that the market would have been glutted , and the goods in our storehouses would have lost all their value", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Now I see a man from the sawmill out there and you have had lesson enough for one morning", "context_len": 19}}}, "beetle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "I come across one little feller jus ' now , an ' instead o ' learnin ' his lesson he was playin ' with a beetle", "context_len": 26}}}, "sends": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Her accent was sharp with anxiety as she added , `` But of course he sends the -- board-money for me -- he would remember that ? '' Evidently she recalled the Se\u00f1ora Vigil 's questions and doubts on this subject , for there was such intensity of apprehension in her look that Jane felt herself full of pain", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` I have not brought any of my things with me , except just what I am wearing , so you will have to provide me until mother sends me a boxful from London", "context_len": 34}}}, "blamed": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 22, "context": "I felt sure , Lady Claudia , that had it been absolutely beyond my power to regain it you would not have blamed me ; but it was not beyond my power , and that being so had I been obliged to wait for ten years , instead of two , I would not have come back to you without it", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "I never use them things , but my eyes tell me a blamed sight more than that can tell ye ; them horsemen ye obsarved are Injins", "context_len": 27}}}, "faithful": {"freq": 123, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 101, "word_index": 43, "context": "Oh ! if only every one might have his turn in this world of ours ; but since that is out of the question , let there at least be as much Coney Island for housemaids as is consistent with good morals and faithful discharge of their duties ; at least so thought one dear little mistress , with more heart , perhaps , than discretion , but a heart , all the same , that won every one to her and made life in her household move with infinite smoothness", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 10, "context": "Yes , she was quite dead , this gentle , faithful , savage girl who , in return for a comparatively slight service , had unhesitatingly abandoned home , kindred , everything , to save me from a cruel and lingering death ; and now the only thing that I could do to show my gratitude was to make sure that no further violence should be offered to her remains", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was expected , of course , that faithful old Margaret , who was master in chief of affairs in the Little Castle , would , as usual , in the absence of the family , answer the bell , and the message intended for her was half way over Albert 's lips before he took in the fact that the individual who had opened the door bore about as close resemblance to Margaret as the tower of the Little Castle to its door-mat", "context_len": 84}}}, "rats": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 99, "context": "`` Did n't I do it well ? Oh , what a hateful , hateful girl she is ! Now , Rosamund -- Rose -- whatever you call yourself -- you had better just get right out of this window with me as fast as ever you can , or you 'll have Lucy bringing her precious governesses , and her mother , and that sick girl , Jane Denton -- how dare she call herself Jane , my dear mother 's name ? -- as well as the Professor himself , on the scene to hunt for the rats", "context_len": 100}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He could only guess that they had fallen a prey to rats , or to some owl that had got into their coop ; but , as they had not been torn or carried away , he guessed in vain", "context_len": 40}}}, "mice": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` And call it 'The Happy Family , ' '' said Fred , mischievously , `` after those celebrated bears and dogs and cats and mice who live together in the most peaceable manner so long as they have no teeth and claws , but who immediately fall to and eat one another up as soon as these are allowed to grow", "context_len": 62}}}, "traps": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 12, "context": "Every nook and corner was visited , and all kinds of odd traps were dragged to light , and presented by the young cousins to be kept in memory of the old place , `` family relics , '' Maggie called them ; and very curious `` family relics '' some of them were", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Goodness gracious ! ain \u2019 t y \u2019 u fussed \u2019 round amusin \u2019 Jimmy , a-fixin \u2019 squirrel traps an \u2019 swings an \u2019 things for him ? That \u2019 s more \u2019 n squared any little thing I could do for y \u2019 u to make y \u2019 u comf \u2019 table", "context_len": 54}}}, "record": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 30, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I must now pass over a space of a year , there being but little of interest to record", "context_len": 19}}}, "dissolution": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I 'll mend my dissolution", "context_len": 5}}}, "sot": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "After a long time , they got their ideas sot , an ' they said that I was to stay , but all the animiles was to go", "context_len": 28}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Your mother 'll be sot up , an ' you mind what she says , an ' I 'll soon come home", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "You must be awful hongry , an \u2019 I know cold beans won \u2019 t hurt your deejeshun , though they alwus sot monstrous hard on Joel \u2019 s stummick", "context_len": 30}}}, "snuffle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I did n't snuffle nor nothin ' , but I just said , 'Are you plannin ' to kill that there dog ? '", "context_len": 24}}}, "tails": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 40, "context": "But now she took the babies out to the big park where she could sit and watch the merry children at play and the beds of flowers coming out , and there were the funny pussy willows and the long tails of yellow forsythia and some squirrels running around , and birds calling to each other", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 67, "context": "Even Toby seemed to forget himself for a moment or two ; and once , when the chickens were all flying over the grass , screaming and laughing , he sprang up from his post on the porch , where he had been quietly watching them , and came bounding down among them with a joyous bark , and seized hold of the fox by the coat tails , just as he pounced on Harry and Walter , as if he thought they had need of his help", "context_len": 88}}}, "mad": {"freq": 173, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 125, "word_index": 87, "context": "`` For hundreds upon hundreds of years the winds and tides have combined to rid themselves of this obstacle to their progress , the winds urging the waves that come rolling in from the vast Atlantic , gathering force as they increase in speed , like one rushing at a leap ; and at last leap they do , upon the great black mass of shale , tons upon tons in weight , seeming as if they would sweep it clear away , and rush on in mad ruin to tumble the fishing luggers together and shatter them like eggs as they lie softly rubbing together in the harbour", "context_len": 109}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 81, "context": "When Alaeddin 's mother heard her son 's speech , she thought little of his wit and said to him , `` O my son , the name of God encompass thee ! Meseemeth thou hast lost thy wit ; return to thy senses , [ 325 ] O my son , and be not like the madmen ! '' `` Nay , O my mother , '' replied he , `` I have not lost my wits nor am I mad ; and this thy speech shall not change that which is in my mind , nor is rest possible to me except I get the darling of my heart , the lovely Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 116}, "NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 76, "context": "the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the mind by the capricious action of memory , while far greater events drop out of it", "context_len": 128}}}, "closing": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 30, "context": "To-morrow morning , then , my dear , if your Father will be at liberty , I shall give myself the pleasure of calling upon him ; ' and regretfully closing the lid , he tore himself away from the old chest , like a reluctant lover from his mistress , and took his departure , leaving Peggy , nearly wild with excitement , to rush tumultuously into the house with her wonderful piece of news", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 46, "context": "The girl had a sort of barbaric love of brightness and softness ; and one day , as she looked over some fabrics for which Jane , spurred by the approach of the vacation and the fact that Lola was to have a part in the closing exercises of school , had sent to Denver , the girl said suddenly , `` How good my father is to me , tia ! ''", "context_len": 73}}}, "dogged": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "The dull listlessness from which his encounter with Astbury aroused him for a moment , closed on him again as soon as he was once more alone ; the glimpse of his old ideals that had revisited him had faded , and only left him with a dogged determination to do nothing unworthy of them , but with no pride or pleasure in his resolve", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "The two men in the other room there are known around town as jolly good fellows ; neither of them are college men , but they have dogged Harris 's footsteps ever since they came to know him , a year or so ago , and have done all in their power to drag him down", "context_len": 56}}}, "resolution": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 66, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}}}, "direct": {"freq": 73, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 118, "context": "replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both far and near have heard ? How is it thou hast heard nought of this nor of the name of Alaeddin , whom Our Lord increase in glory and prosper ? '' Quoth the Maugrabin , `` Marry , it is the utmost of my wish to look upon the palace ; so , an thou wouldst do me a kindness , direct me thither , for that I am a stranger", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 73, "context": "It was weary , anxious work ; for of course our movements were being regulated by a theory that , for aught we knew to the contrary , might be all wrong ; and as day succeeded day without bringing the expected sail within our ken there were not wanting among us those who denounced the skipper 's plan as foolish , and argued that the proper thing would have been to go direct to the Gaboon , and look there for the Virginia", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "A minute later he was running down the hill to the town , accompanied by three or four other knights ; they made direct for the outer port , where two galleys were lying in readiness , leapt on board one of them , which already contained its quota of knights , and at once rowed out of the port", "context_len": 60}}}, "checking": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then , instead of attempting to restrain him , and worrying and checking him by useless efforts to hold him in , he gave him the rein freely , and animated and encouraged him with his voice , so that the horse flew across the plains at the top of his speed , the king and the courtiers looking on , at first with fear and trembling , but soon afterward with feelings of the greatest admiration and pleasure", "context_len": 79}}}, "raring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Little girl , you 've started for Australia , and as I do n't believe in checking a raring , tearing ambition , I wo n't try to block you , exactly , but only to sidetrack", "context_len": 38}}}, "ambition": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 52, "context": "At first they had all floundered hopelessly about , and it had taken some practice even to slide a few steps ; but long before they had reached the stage of skimming over the frozen surface at the rate of eight or ten miles an hour , which had been their fond ambition , the snow had melted into moist and dirty slush , which was particularly trying , as they had just decided to make a sledge out of an old packing-case , and drag Lilian round the pasture", "context_len": 90}}}, "block": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 6, "context": "He could use a saw to block the wheels out of a pine board , and he could use a hatchet to rough off the corners of the blocks , but he had to use his knife to give them any sort of roundness , and they were not very round then ; they were apt to be oval in shape , and they always wabbled", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 28, "context": "My ! what a lot of bundles ! And now the street is quite empty again , not a person on either side of the one , short block ; but the whistle that has been ringing out more and more clearly at quite regular , three-minute intervals sounds very near indeed , and in another second a gray-suited individual , with soldier-like cap to match and a glitter of shining brass buttons , swings round the opposite corner , and makes a bee-line across the street", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "There won \u2019 t be much of this left in you at the end of fifteen miles , \u2019 Cephy , and I have no objection so long as it doesn \u2019 t block the game", "context_len": 36}}}, "sidetrack": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Little girl , you 've started for Australia , and as I do n't believe in checking a raring , tearing ambition , I wo n't try to block you , exactly , but only to sidetrack", "context_len": 38}}}, "funds": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 23, "context": "But see what greater pleasure it would give to my life were you my son , for whom I could lay by such funds as I could well spare , instead of spending all my appointments on myself , and having neither kith nor kin to give a sigh of regret when the news comes that I have fallen in some engagement with the infidels", "context_len": 65}}}, "proposition": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 7, "context": "Merrily laughed the bonny blue-bell at my proposition that , in addition to the fine of 100 imposed on Mission Miss Sahibas who marry within three years of coming out , it should be part of the contract that they should have all their hair shaved off on the day before the wedding", "context_len": 53}}}, "cranky": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I 've got an old father 'most as cranky as that there dog", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "He lets me wash , an ' scrub , an ' cook , an ' never says a word excep ' not to work too hard , an ' if he wants to be jus ' a little bit cranky , jus ' a teeny little bit , he goes in his room an ' shuts the door till the bad spirit gets out of him", "context_len": 66}}}, "needs": {"freq": 96, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 86, "context": "maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions , but she can not look on me as all a stranger , even if she hold no memory of those old days at Highgate", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 28, "context": "Also his energy in teaching was reflected by her own burning desire , in old age , to speak on all occasions to the Natives of their deepest needs , and never to miss an opportunity of trying to lead some poor Hindu or Muhammadan to Christ , always with the vivid sense upon her , when she met man or woman , that the call to herself might come before they could meet again , and so a second opportunity might never recur", "context_len": 84}}}, "everlasting": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "Before him the alternatives were sharply defined : Apostasy , and his ancestral honours , with all that the sword of the conqueror could give ; and on the other hand , the martyr 's lingering agony , but the hope of everlasting life after death", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "I ask for no earthly remuneration ; my position in life renders me independent of any exertions of my own ; I pray but for God \u2019 s blessing upon my attempts to instruct His lambs in the things which concern their everlasting welfare ; and deeply gratified should I feel , were my little work to be classed among the numerous valuable publications which you have already given to the world", "context_len": 72}}}, "temper": {"freq": 118, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 112, "word_index": 17, "context": "Have a brother about four years older than yourself , I should say ; and if your temper is hot , and your disposition revengeful , and you are a vain and ridiculous dreamer at the same time that you are eager to excel in feats of strength and games of skill , and to do everything that the other fellows do , and are ashamed to be better than the worst boy in the crowd , your brother can be of the greatest use to you , with his larger experience and wisdom", "context_len": 94}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "As she grew older , she became more reasonable , and as every one around her lived only for her pleasure , and she had all she desired , it was not difficult for her to keep her temper under control", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas must never complain ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must temper restrain -- When sust [ 91 ] pankah-wala won \u2019 t pull at the cane ; Must never be fanciful , foolish , or vain", "context_len": 39}}}, "hanker": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "And you 'd have a home which you seem to hanker for", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I allus do hanker fur light", "context_len": 7}}}, "flesh": {"freq": 75, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 48, "context": "And in the train of Him who sitteth upon the white horse , we do confidently expect to behold General Harrison and those other saints who have died , either as at this time , or formerly , for the Good Old Cause , raised again in the flesh , that we and they may all triumph as one man", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 63, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}}}, "convenient": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 43, "context": "Next he started to carry outside and pile neatly at the blind end of the building all the boards , boxes and other debris littering up the room , swept it , and selected two packing cases and nailed them up into a convenient impromptu desk , manufactured a bench seat out of some loose boards , set his pen , ink and paper in order , and felt quite ready for business", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 55, "context": "Having tried in vain to edge a way through the press , Peggy and Bobby tacked round by the fields , and scrambling over a garden wall , found a temporary refuge in the churchyard , which stood some height above the level of the road , and where , from the vantage-ground of a convenient tombstone , they found they had an excellent view of the fair below", "context_len": 69}}}, "route": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 7, "context": "Had any other child friend along his route , no matter how friendly , questioned him day after day as to how he was getting on with his training for the race ? Had any other among them promised to be on hand at the latest delivery on the afternoon succeeding it , so as to learn just what the issue had been , and at a time when he would be able to stop and tell about it ? Would any one else in the world have thought of suggesting that he should give three short little whistles when he reached the Browns ' , in Remsen Street , so that she should know just how near he was ? Surely no one ; and it was", "context_len": 128}}}, "aboard": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 74, "word_index": 126, "context": "it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached L'Esperance , and the extraordinary story I had heard while aboard her", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 68, "context": "Others would make for one of the cinnamon-sailed luggers lying at anchor , to go round and back , or would get into one of the boats ; while some , more venturesome , or really more confident in their powers over the water , would go boldly out , perhaps a mile , to meet some lugger coming in from the fishing-ground , sure of being taken aboard and riding back abreast of the boulders where they had left their clothes", "context_len": 82}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I have n't had a chance to do anything for you aboard ship", "context_len": 14}}}, "statue": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 106, "context": "For there was something very stirring in the act , and a stranger to the place would hold his breath in dread as he saw Mark Penelly , who was the finest swimmer at the port of Carn Du , climb up the side of the great black rock upon some fine summer evening , then go round along the narrow shelf of shaley stone , till he stood alone there forty feet above the sea , his white figure as he rested against the black rock , every muscle standing out from his well-knit frame , and his arms crossed , looking like some antique statue in its niche", "context_len": 110}}}, "grave": {"freq": 185, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 5, "context": "But who could long be grave at the outset of so promising an expedition ! The idea of a leisurely driving trip through the lovely Lake Country , stopping here and there , as the spirit moved them , at the comfortable little inns and hotels that abound in the region , had been such a supremely delightful idea , even in mere anticipation , that now that they were actually off enthusiasm knew no bounds , and mirth was literally unconfined", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 110, "word_index": 18, "context": "Another day Has past ! another weary weary day , And I am so much nearer to my grave ! Oh that I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 78, "context": "If our vows had not forbidden our fighting I should have called him to account long ago ; but the only thing else to do was for me to lodge a formal complaint before the bailiff , of his continually offensive bearing and manner , which I could not bring myself to do , and indeed there was no special matter that would have seemed to justify me , no single speech that in itself would warrant such grave action on my part", "context_len": 83}}}, "profile": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "She is so fair , so graceful , so pure-looking , with her chiselled profile and her sweet expression ; I could not make out , however , anything that would do for the lion", "context_len": 35}}}, "unchanged": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "I would keep everything unchanged that is not wrong or foolish -- and there is such a fearful amount of things that are wrong and foolish , that one might think that to get rid of them would give all occupation sufficient", "context_len": 42}}}, "deliberately": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 2, "context": "You have deliberately set about to entrap and disgrace two men vastly your superiors , but so far as in me lies I am going to do all in my power to free them from your clutches and save them from the scandal of this thing , and if I hear of its becoming known through you I 'll --", "context_len": 60}}}, "betrayed": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 19, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The ray of sunshine that had aroused Ben crept on across the mow , leaving them in shadow , and presently Jerry once more betrayed tokens of drowsiness , slumber again claiming him at last", "context_len": 35}}}, "address": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 53, "context": "At Pensford the rabble made a bull savage , and then tried to make it attack his congregation ; at Whitechapel they drove cows among the listeners and threw stones , one of which hit Wesley between the eyes ; but after he had wiped away the blood he went on with his address , telling the people that `` God hath not given us the spirit of fear ''", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 38, "context": "'They tell me , dear Marie-Celeste ( and they means , of course , your Cousin Theodore and Donald ) , that you are taking a driving tour through the English lakes , and that if I should address a letter to you , care of Miss Dorothy Allyn , no one would be any the wiser ; and that 's just what I 've done , you see , as , for reasons of his own , your Cousin Theodore seems to prefer it", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He did not , however , address this to Jake Amsden , Esq", "context_len": 13}}}, "card": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 59, "context": "Whether the powers that rule the entrance to the same came to the conclusion that to a little girl of twelve and a little boy of four the term of general public could not honestly be applied , or whether all rules of procedure and precedence were magnanimously waived in their favor , certain it is that the little card in question bore the incredible inscription : Admit Master Albert Allyn and his little friend , Miss Marie-Celeste Harris , to the East Terrace between the hours of twelve and three on Thursday", "context_len": 93}}}, "train": {"freq": 312, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 297, "word_index": 86, "context": "-- Mine own Laura , how could you write regarding the little meeting , at which you and sweet Margaret were , Would you not like to be in my shoes at the time , and hold your darling friend in your arms ? I would much rather have been in Margaret \u2019 s shoes , and have held some one else in my arms , -- only for the wrench that would have followed ! But O love , we are travelling in the same train , only in different carriages ; and I am thankful that though we can not see each other , we can as it were talk to each other out of the windows", "context_len": 119}, "VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 56, "context": "Very few of us are heroes when it really comes to the point , and I grieve to say that she looked so glum , and was generally so grumpy and discontented over the arrangement , that unselfish Lilian , divining the cause , instantly proposed to give up her own part to Peggy , and train a little village girl for the second 'child ' instead", "context_len": 67}}}, "conductor": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Is your rifle loaded ? Then , if it is , for mercy sakes fire and see if you can sever that main feed wire , '' and the lineman pointed to a thick conductor , which was shooting out blue sparks , and which had charged all the other wires with the deadly current", "context_len": 56}}}, "holler": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Minley here will put you on a train that comes by here in the morning , and all you 've got to do is to sit still in it , till you hear the conductor holler Ciscasset", "context_len": 37}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "I 'm goin ' ter stay up fer hours an ' hours , an ' if yer do n't keep right on tellin ' stories quick , I 'll holler , an ' that 'll make mar mad , an ' then she 'll send par up with a stick ter beat me", "context_len": 53}}}, "hustle": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "We 've only ten minutes 'fore drill , an ' if there 's anybody got to be licked , why , we got to hustle", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` You little huzzy ! What are you doing up this time of night , instead of asleep in bed ? Rouse up ! rouse up ! Lucky you did n't let my fire go out this cold night ! Come , hustle ! ''", "context_len": 45}}}, "throws": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 25, "context": "Then he sticks a short pipe into his mouth , and pulls on an old wool hat , and flourishes a stick that the supe throws to him , and you see that he is an Irishman just come across the sea ; and then off goes another coat , and he comes out a British soldier in white duck trousers and red coat", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Fancy a tree waiting till May or June before it will put off its old dress ! It waits till all its new leaves are well out ; and in midsummer throws off the withered ones", "context_len": 36}}}, "crutch": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 26, "context": "With a deftness and lightness that would have been admirable if it had not been cruel , the old man bore all his weight on one crutch , swung the other around in the air , and with the heavy end struck a swift , sure blow on Poacher 's glossy black forehead", "context_len": 53}}}, "suffocating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "'Tilda Jane choked back the suffocating lump in her throat , and gravely rose to her feet", "context_len": 17}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The air was heavy with suffocating smoke ; fragments of shell were flying with complete impartiality ; shouts , oaths , and curses punctuated the crash of steel and the rattle of musketry , as men in their blind ferocity clutched at each other 's throats and rolled in mortal combat upon the ground", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Here he is , '' sounded a suffocating voice", "context_len": 10}}}, "lump": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 38, "context": "Randolph to have come as he had done , to have forced himself upon us , to have invited us to go out with him , to have -- -- and then I stopped , and a great lump rose in my throat , and I burst into tears , for in my heart of hearts I knew well that I did not think what he did intolerable at all , that I respected him , and -- but I did not dare to allow my thoughts to go any further", "context_len": 91}}}, "mention": {"freq": 134, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}, "NOUN": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 46, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}}}, "acquaintance": {"freq": 80, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 80, "word_index": 39, "context": "How queer it is , he said to himself , that I should feel constrained in this way to run out to Windsor ! Land knows ! I have no desire to come to be on intimate terms of acquaintance with Evelyn 's boys ; and what would be the satisfaction of prowling around just to see where they live ? Their father gave me up after that time he spoke his mind so freely about my aimless life -- as he was pleased to call it -- and there is no reason whatever why I should bother myself about my sister 's children , since she , poor thing ! is dead and gone , and they have enough of this world 's goods to make them", "context_len": 128}}}, "vague": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 24, "context": "Who can tell all that goes on in the mind of a little child , or what it understands of death ? In a vague way Peggy knew that her playfellow had 'gone to heaven , where mother is , ' but she did not think of that as any cause for sorrow , nor did she connect him for an instant with the place where she stood , but , with her nurse 's words still troubling her , she knelt down and searched among the white flowers that hid the bare earth beneath", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 34, "context": "There was not a boy in the Boy 's Town who would not gladly have turned from the town and lived in the woods if his mother had let him ; and in every vague plan of running off the forest had its place as a city of refuge from pursuit and recapture", "context_len": 53}}}, "arranged": {"freq": 131, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 129, "word_index": 81, "context": "The little old gentleman turned out to be a very learned don from Oxford , with a terrific list of letters at the end of his name , and in the course of a few days a representative arrived from the Bodleian Library with a request to be allowed to inspect the books ( which by this time had been removed to a corner of the big drawing-room ) , and after a considerable amount of talking and writing it was arranged that they should be sent to London at once to be sold by auction", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "After our friend Dewey has seen his young lady and arranged matters we 'll dust back to our friends , the miners who came near giving us a ticket to the next world , and see whether fortune wo n't favor us a little more", "context_len": 45}}}, "beseeching": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "Baby ceased her loud cries , and stretched out her dimpled arms to her uncle , with a beseeching whimper ; Hafed paused in his antics , and stood like a statue at sight of his master ; and the three other children all turned to him with exclamations of `` Oh , Uncle Ruthven ; we 're so glad ! '' and `` Please do n't leave us , '' from Maggie and Bessie ; and `` Make dat Patty be off wiz herself , '' from Franky", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "The puma whined , pressed against him , and looked up into his face as though beseeching that he might go with him", "context_len": 23}}}, "graciously": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 58, "context": "Moreover , he fell to going everywhile [ 539 ] to the chase and the horse course and to practicing horsemanship and archery [ 540 ] before the Sultan , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour redoubled in love of him , whenassoever she saw him disporting himself a horseback , and thought in herself that God had wrought exceeding graciously by her in that there had befallen her what befell with the Vizier 's son , so He might keep her for her true bridegroom Alaeddin", "context_len": 85}}}, "sunk": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 70, "context": "His kingdom lost ; his family in captivity ; his beloved wife in the grave , where the sorrows and sufferings of separation from her husband had borne her ; his cities sacked ; his palaces and treasures plundered ; and now he himself , in the last hour of his extremity , abandoned and betrayed by all in whom he had placed his confidence and trust , his heart sunk within him in despair", "context_len": 75}, "VERB": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 23, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "Mark Penelly 's eyes seemed starting out of his head as , with a convulsive gasp , he seized hold of the net , along with the master and another , and they began to haul in fathom after fathom , which came up slowly , and as if a great deal of it were sunk", "context_len": 56}}}, "unknown": {"freq": 86, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 85, "word_index": 41, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Just as I am , -- thy love unknown Has broken every barrier down ; Now to be thine , yea , thine alone O Lamb of God ! I come", "context_len": 32}}}, "clinging": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 64, "context": "He judged Harry Paul , in fact , by the same measure as he would have meted out to an enemy himself ; and so terrible were his thoughts , so horrifying to him was the thought of the death from which he had escaped , that he was robbed of all energy ; he had not strength to do more than hang there clinging to the weeds with desperate clutch , and , with only his head out of water , gaze up in Harry 's stern eyes", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 65, "context": "They were creaking all around the circular opening -- those spying trees -- staring through the curtainless windows at her , and instead of throwing on more wood , and making a blaze that would enable her to be plainly seen , she opened the stove door , and , cowering over the embers , changed her wet foot-gear , and tried to dry her clinging skirts", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Fanning 's had a wonderfully clinging effect ; it seemed to encircle my fingers something like an octopus , and she pulled me gently towards the corner she had in view , and presently had pinned me there , seating herself well in front of me , so that there was no possible escape", "context_len": 54}}}, "settlements": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` They had spread out into two branch settlements : Hitchit\u00fadshi or Little Hitchiti , on both sides of Flint river , below the junction of Kitchofuni creek , which passes through a county named after it ; and Tutal\u00f3si on Tutalosi creek , a branch of Kitchofuni creek , twenty miles west of Hitchit\u00fadshi ( Hawkins , p", "context_len": 59}}}, "composure": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` Ca n't a fellow take a run around the house without anything being the matter with him ? '' asked Lewis , sharply , but with a little nervous trepidation in his tone and manner ; but the subject was now dropped , and he had more than recovered his composure and was taking an apparently interested part in the renewed discussion over Percy 's loss , when the enraged Seabrooke entered the room", "context_len": 75}}}, "dignity": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 17, "context": "Nothing could have been prettier than the wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he", "context_len": 128}}}, "sad": {"freq": 191, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 177, "word_index": 81, "context": "maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions , but she can not look on me as all a stranger , even if she hold no memory of those old days at Highgate", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 59, "context": "Yes , said the other pieces of coal , we can now hold up our coal heads and say to all the world , Well , now what do you think of the coal-bin ? You think a lot of it if we \u2019 re within it , and if the coal-bin is empty ah , you \u2019 re sad ! \u2019", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "It made Peggy quite sad to think that the little creatures were mostly bought for the mines , and that , once broken and trained , they would never see daylight again , but spend their lives drawing trucks up and down the low galleries underground , having said good-bye to their native moorlands for ever", "context_len": 56}}}, "yawn": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "At last Florence stopped her work abruptly , leant back in her chair , stretched out her arms behind her head , uttered a profound yawn which ended in a sigh , and then , turning round , she spoke", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then his merriment over , he began to yawn , and 'Tilda Jane , as keen of observation as ever , immediately espied this sign of fatigue", "context_len": 27}}}, "obediently": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "The horses chose the best position , according to the instinct which had been given them to escape from danger , and they maintained it until the fury of the storm was spent , and then obediently carried their riders to the camp , where they found two or three of the lighter wagons blown over , and a number of articles scattered hither and thither", "context_len": 66}}}, "seating": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 42, "context": "Not in the least alarmed by the eager delight of the children , or the whistling and shouts of the boys , he sat still till all the wagons had passed , when he darted ahead of the foremost one , and seating himself this time on an old rail fence , began his pretty call again , and took a second close look at our friends", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Now the headsman had spread the carpet of blood and seating Alaeddin thereon , had bound his eyes and gone round him three times , [ 576 ] awaiting the King 's final commandment", "context_len": 35}}}, "curiosity": {"freq": 106, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 106, "word_index": 96, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "undress": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Meanwhile , half a dozen children in various stages of undress swarmed from under the bed and stood staring at us", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "But there , I must be quick ; I have to return home in time to undress the little ones", "context_len": 20}}}, "feeble": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 46, "context": "How very great and wonderful He must be ! It seems surprising that He should care for every one on this little ball , -- how much more astonishing that He should have condescended to come and live upon it , to have appeared as a feeble Child in one of the worlds that He had made , and then actually to die , like one of the creatures that He had formed ! Is not God \u2019 s power wonderful , and His love more wonderful still ?", "context_len": 89}}}, "illuminated": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 23, "context": "The sun was now sinking behind the huge rounded forms of some chalk hills in the west , when the camp became gradually illuminated by the light of numberless fires , whereat oxen were roasted whole , and partridges and hares by the dozen , for the Danes were voracious in their appetites", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 97, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "listless": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 55, "context": "No ; turn round ; if it 's all the same to you we 'll go there now ; and this last a little gruffly ; for one has to be a good deal of a philosopher to continue on the friendliest of terms with a man that has just informed you that you look listless and lazy", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was looking pale and listless , and there were heavy , dark lines about his eyes", "context_len": 17}}}, "uninterested": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Peggy grew up without many of the luxuries which fall to the lot of richer girls , but she was none the worse for that , and I think , when she saw how bored and uninterested Maud Middleton was in the midst of her round of London society and foreign travel , she felt that it is sometimes better to have the capacity for enjoyment than to have the means without the happy talent of being able to appreciate your good things", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Dillson looked entirely uninterested in the case of the would-be immigrant , so , after a farewell announcement that sometimes as many as two hundred `` furriners '' went through Vanceboro in a single day , 'Tilda Jane passed on to another branch of her subject", "context_len": 46}}}, "petition": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "At the same time , the boys were to be dressed as Roman conspirators , and one of them was to give the teacher a petition to read , while another plunged a dagger into his vitals , and still another shouted , `` Strike , Stephanos , strike ! '' It seemed to my boy that he had invented a situation which he had lifted almost bodily out of Goldsmith 's history ; and he did not feel that his lines ,", "context_len": 83}}}, "praying": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 85, "context": "The Sultan still marvelled in himself at Alaeddin 's mother , how she had come to him in poor clothes , whilst her son had command of this exceeding wealth ; and as for the folk , who came to the Sultan 's palace , to gaze upon the wedding-festivities , when they saw Alaeddin 's palace and the goodliness of its building , there took them great wonderment how so magnificent a building had been upreared in one night and they fell all to praying for Alaeddin and saying , `` God prosper him ! By Allah , he is deserving", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` What did the Major gain by all his fighting and praying ? Nothing but the gallows ! Now , for me ! I 've been near the gallows a good few times , but I bea n't hanged yet , and I 've had a merry life of it ; and I 've got that pot of gold I told you of", "context_len": 63}}}, "earnestly": {"freq": 127, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 127, "word_index": 21, "context": "And now we were to welcome the well-known and gifted authoress into our house ! This was a privilege ; and earnestly did we look forward to the pleasure of receiving her ; though at the same time we were perhaps conscious of a slight shadow of doubt crossing our minds , as to how far one of Miss Tucker \u2019 s age would be able to accommodate herself to the new surroundings , and bear the trials incident to life and work in a tropical climate", "context_len": 87}}}, "didst": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Trotton , say you ? Then why didst take this road , seeing that the turning at Milland is the right and proper one ? '' demanded Sir George sternly", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` When [ 409 ] the Sultan heard his daughter 's story , he was sore concerned and his eyes brimmed with tears ; then , sheathing the sword and coming up to her , he kissed her and said to her , `` O my daughter , why didst thou not tell me yesterday , so I might have warded off from thee the torment and affright which have befallen thee this night ? But no matter ; arise and put away from thee this thought , and to-night I will set over thee those who shall guard thee , so there shall not again befall thee that which befell yesternight", "context_len": 112}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Alfgar , son of Anlaf , answer ; whom didst thou espy talking with Sweyn ? ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "maddest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Bless the matron an ' make her forgive me , an ' bless all the lady-boards -- Mis ' Grannis 'specially , 'cause she 'll be maddest with me", "context_len": 29}}}, "amen": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I 'll be waiting for you , forever and ever , amen", "context_len": 13}}}, "sits": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 31, "context": "and I returned from the village , being rather tired of going about twelve miles in a canvas box , -- of course there is no seat in it ; one sits half-Oriental style on a kind of coarse carpet , -- I got out to walk the last mile home", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` She 's the little nurse girl who brings those babies , twins I suppose they are , and sits on the stoop over opposite", "context_len": 25}}}, "liberties": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 57, "context": "And , in the mean time , what a pleasure to talk familiarly with these same bright little visitors , who more likely than not would have retired into speechless embarrassment had anyone ventured the announcement that the great Queen of England was none other than the friendly old lady with whom they were taking all the liberties of commonplace , every-day acquaintance ! And so , happily , no doubt , for their ease of mind , no one felt called upon to make the announcement", "context_len": 87}}}, "cowered": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 7, "context": "At the same time , while he cowered along in the shadow of unreal dangers , he had a boy 's boldness with most of the real ones , and he knew how to resent an indignity even at the hands of the teacher who could send him to the Chemical Room at pleasure", "context_len": 54}}}, "sheets": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 53, "context": "Then she knelt by the broad , low window-seat , and said her prayers , looking away to the stars , which glowed red , and green , and yellow , in the soft summer sky , and then , in a great hush of delight , she lay down between the delicately-perfumed sheets , and gave herself up to the enjoyment of the present which God had given her", "context_len": 70}}}, "possessed": {"freq": 99, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 96, "word_index": 46, "context": "Thou sayst , O my mother , that I have no gift to present to the Sultan , according to the wont of the folk , whilst in fact I have by me a gift and an offering , the like whereof methinketh no king ever possessed , no , nor aught to match therewith ; for [ 338 ] thou must know , O my mother , that the fruits , which I brought in the purses [ 339 ] from the treasure and which I deemed glass or crystal , are very jewels , methinketh all the kings of the world may not compass the least of them , and I , of my companying with the jewellers , know that they are precious stones", "context_len": 127}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Now and then men have managed to become possessed of a file , and have , by long and patient work , sawn through a chain , and have , when a galley has been lying near our own shore , sprung overboard and escaped ; but for every attempt that succeeds there must be twenty failures , for the chains are frequently examined , and woe be to the man who is found to have been tampering with his", "context_len": 80}}}, "roof": {"freq": 160, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 157, "word_index": 123, "context": "tell you one thing , Uncle Everett , it was a talk I had with that dear child under an apple-tree , down at Nuneham , that made me feel that some people whom I care a great deal for still had faith in me , and it was she who gave me courage by what she told me to go home as fast as ever I could get there and then , Marie-Celeste , what do you suppose I said to him ? Well , I just , told him that that same dear child had preached me two blessed sermons -- one on the deck of the Majestic and the other exactly where a sermon should be preached , beneath the roof of dear old St", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "speeding": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "Ah , how happy would she be , could she know that the young creamery man was sleeping under the same roof ! But he was speeding somewhere far away over the snowy roads", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The `` Diggers '' accepted the `` speeding up '' with alacrity", "context_len": 12}}}, "hopefulness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "Peggy does not feel the least little bit older than when she climbed the water-wheel , or thrashed Jones minor behind the paling of the cricket-field -- if growing old means losing your ideals , and your keen enthusiasm , and your hopefulness , and a certain appreciation of other people 's worth which is our birthright when we are young", "context_len": 61}}}, "shutting": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "She lay very still wishing the noises below would cease , the running back and forth , the shutting of doors , the calling of the boys to one another and the crying of the baby", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 7, "context": "Warden laughed again disagreeably , and , shutting the door on Davis , returned to his work ; but the little incident and the burning , angry words of the despairing man shook him unpleasantly , and his temper , never one of the best , was in such a ruffled condition , that it only wanted the faintest provocation to kindle it into a blaze", "context_len": 66}}}, "strangely": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 98, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "confused": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 48, "context": "Her stern gallery was upheld by a row of gilt figures representing that hero of mythology , Master Atlas , of whom my father used to speak ; while over the gallery towered three enormous lanterns , and above everything was a maze of spars and rigging that confused me not a little , though before long I was well acquainted with the names of all of them", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 12, "context": "[ Footnote 436 : The whole of the foregoing passage is so confused that I think it well to add here ( l ) a literal translation , as I read it : `` So the Vizier , yea , indeed , he marvelled at the greatness of that wealth more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more when he saw the Sultan that he was satisfied with ( or accepted of ) the bride-gift and the dowry ; however , it was not possible to him that he should gainsay the truth and should say to the Sultan , 'He is not worthy ; ' only , he practised with a device upon the Sultan so he should", "context_len": 128}}}, "dreamy": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 7, "context": "Her dark face cleared , and in dreamy content she listened to the string of hunting stories reeled off by the two boys , who , without addressing her directly , were evidently stimulated by the knowledge that here was an interested , appreciative , and `` brand new '' listener", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "A small and sullen cloud had hidden the dreamy , thoughtful sun , and out of the cloud came wheeling , choking gusts , bearing bewildered snowflakes up and down , hither and thither , before allowing them to alight turbulently upon the quiet earth", "context_len": 45}}}, "forerunner": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "They were strangely confused in her mind with the ringing of the rising-bell at the orphan asylum , and she was just sinking into a dreamy condition , a forerunner of sleep , when she heard a hard voice in her ear", "context_len": 42}}}, "quietly": {"freq": 214, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 214, "word_index": 82, "context": "As long as he stayed she never left him , and her mother sometimes feared that the colonel might grow tired of having such a little child so much with him , but he told her it was a great pleasure to him ; and indeed it seemed to be so , for though there were a great many people at Quam Beach who knew him and liked to talk to him , he never forgot the little friend who sat so quietly at his side , and had every now and then a word , or smile , or a touch of his hand for her", "context_len": 107}}}, "gaunt": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 26, "context": "The cold east wind , for it was but the beginning of March , in the year of grace 1660 , whistled through the clump of gaunt pine trees that marked the summit of the hill , and , despite the fact that each of us wore a thick doublet , the chilly blast cut us like a knife", "context_len": 59}}}, "perturbed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "He was caught in a twisted skein of events , and was the more perturbed because he could not think of any possible object the girl might have in trying to deceive him", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I am offered twenty dollars , '' said Bart , outwardly cool as a cucumber , inwardly greatly perturbed over the incident in hand , and hastening to close it in favor of a friend", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Sure you 'll forget It ! '' he persisted , very much perturbed", "context_len": 14}}}, "graven": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "To her perturbed mind , there rose a vision of a graven image from the Bible , as she stared at the woman 's stony countenance", "context_len": 26}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "I was told that the horseman was supposed to be the arch-traitor , Old Noll , but a clever hewer of wood had caused all likeness of the great rebel to disappear , and had graven instead the features of honest George , now created Duke of Albemarle", "context_len": 48}}}, "image": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 33, "context": "One flash ends all ! O Clara , when my soul Hath ceased to suffer , can it cease to love ? Methinks , when quitting Earth , \u2019 twill still retain Her image , who was more than Earth to me ! It is a portion of my being , twined With every thought and feeling ; thou wilt weep , My Clara ; thou canst not believe him false To faith and friends , who is so true to thee", "context_len": 82}}}, "stony": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 57, "context": "A very happy , grateful thing , the dear mamma thought it too ; and very happy , grateful tears were those which dimmed her eyes as she read her little daughter 's simple story , and then thanked God that the lessons of love and forgiveness which were given to her little ones fell not upon stony ground , but took root and bore precious fruit in those tender young hearts", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "And her bosom throbbed at the sight of the little , stony face now lifted upon her from the dusk of the floor -- a face with a fierce gleam in its dark eyes , and clouded with a wild array of black hair in which was knotted and twisted a fantastic faja of green wool , narrowly woven", "context_len": 59}}}, "candle": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 55, "context": "The wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "I really hope that it may do good for it to be known through Batala that , in a manly game , the Hindus and Muhammadans can not hold a candle to the Christian boys , who go preaching and singing hymns on Sunday ! Piety is all the more attractive from union with manliness", "context_len": 55}}}, "unmistakable": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 88, "context": "Naturally this called forth inquiry , and it soon became known that Percy had entrusted to Seabrooke 's keeping a large sum of money , lest he should himself be tempted to spend any portion of it , as it was to be reserved for a special purpose ; that Seabrooke before going to the dinner on the previous evening had put it , as he supposed , in a secure place , and that this morning the money was gone , while he had discovered slight but unmistakable evidence that his quarters had been ransacked in search of it", "context_len": 100}}}, "compassion": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 25, "context": "Some of the loiterers about the station gathered around him , and seeing that his bodily infirmities were a subject of mirth rather than of compassion , 'Tilda Jane , in spite of warm protests on his part , once more swathed him in his shawl , and carried him with dignity into the waiting-room", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "[ 407 ] Do but hearken what hath betided me and I am well assured that , whenas thou hearest my story of that which hath happened to me these two nights past , thou wilt excuse me and Thy Grace will be moved to compassion upon me , as I know from thy love for me", "context_len": 57}}}, "peck": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "And he loves to tease her too , for if there are any flowers in the room he will fly over to them , peck at them and begin munching at them", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Presently the blue and white bird flew down from the twig she was sitting on and began to peck very hard at some green leaves growing among the grass", "context_len": 29}}}, "command": {"freq": 253, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 200, "word_index": 25, "context": "The Sultan still marvelled in himself at Alaeddin 's mother , how she had come to him in poor clothes , whilst her son had command of this exceeding wealth ; and as for the folk , who came to the Sultan 's palace , to gaze upon the wedding-festivities , when they saw Alaeddin 's palace and the goodliness of its building , there took them great wonderment how so magnificent a building had been upreared in one night and they fell all to praying for Alaeddin and saying , `` God prosper him ! By Allah , he is deserving", "context_len": 102}, "VERB": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 81, "context": "`` Ask your pardon , sir ; my tongue runs away with me sometimes , and thinking of Solomon made me say it , and 't is all in the Bible , sir , now is n't it ? But to go back to what I was a saying , you know well , sir , as no one would follow a chap like me as captain , but if we could get a real gentleman , and one used to command to lead us -- why , hang me , sir , if we would n't be masters of St", "context_len": 101}}}, "inspired": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 3, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Rogers had occasional flashes of what can only be termed inspired common sense ; and if he were judiciously approached , it was even possible that such an incalculable person might use his influence to restrain the old soldiers of his congregation from rushing on immediate destruction", "context_len": 47}}}, "confidence": {"freq": 149, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 149, "word_index": 95, "context": "not to let our left hand , know that which is done by our right hand ; but are we not also commanded so to let our light shine before men that they may see our good works and glorify our Father in heaven ? And can we do so , or truly show our love to him , if we hide the services rendered for his sake behind a mask of coldness and reserve ? My dear aunt , for his sake , for your own , for the sake of the affection and confidence which I wish my children to feel for you , and which I believe you wish to gain , let me satisfy them that it was really you who did this thing", "context_len": 128}}}, "intense": {"freq": 88, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 87, "word_index": 11, "context": "For the space of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Is your sister going to play this morning ? asked Marie-Celeste , with an eagerness on her face that gave place to intense satisfaction as Albert answered , Yes ; she 's comin ' in a little while ; since to have Miss Allyn at the organ during these visits of hers to the chapel was just the most delightful thing that could possibly happen for Marie-Celeste", "context_len": 67}}}, "desperation": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 11, "context": "And while he holds that slippery throat with the clutch of desperation , as the snake twists and writhes and tries to bite , it may be well , for the benefit of those who have not read the preceding volumes of this series , to tell who Bomba was and what had been his adventures up to the time at which this story opens", "context_len": 65}}}, "trace": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 40, "context": "It was the great political year of `` Tippecanoe and Tyler too , '' when the grandfather of our President Harrison was elected President ; but the wild hard-cider campaign roared by my boy 's little life without leaving a trace in it , except the recollection of his father wearing a linsey-woolsey hunting-shirt , belted at the waist and fringed at the skirt , as a Whig who loved his cause and honored the good old pioneer times was bound to do", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 55, "context": "But Percy was popular , Seabrooke was not ; and even the masters were inclined to believe that the latter must have been careless and forgetful and mislaid the money , while believing he had put it in the place he indicated , and presently -- no one knew exactly how it started or could trace the rumor to its source -- presently it began to be bruited about among the boys that Seabrooke was keeping it for his own use and had never intended to return it to Percy , and was now making him his scape-goat", "context_len": 98}}}, "kinship": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was the realization of this kinship of experience and yet of the widely different effect upon soul and character that had impelled him to take his sudden leave of Marie-Celeste , and then , pausing a moment at the smoking-room door , he went on and down to his state-room , for he had much to think over , and a long , long time he sat there , his elbows resting on his knees and his face buried in his hands", "context_len": 83}}}, "flashed": {"freq": 101, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 101, "word_index": 74, "context": "The air seemed to have grown more close and sultry while the children were within the cave , and , just as they reached the mouth , a low , grumbling sound , which they had heard for some time , but not taken much notice of , broke into a crash of thunder that seemed to make the ground shake beneath them , while at the same instant a brilliant streak of lightning flashed zigzag across the sky , lighting up the gloom behind them to its furthest recess", "context_len": 90}}}, "buttoning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Beyond Barville they paused to rest , and Ben , hearing Jerry \u2019 s teeth chatter , persisted in pulling off his coat and buttoning it about the blind lad \u2019 s shoulders", "context_len": 33}}}, "grimace": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The young skipper freed himself with a grimace of disgust", "context_len": 10}}}, "reminds": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 2, "context": "And that reminds me , Dick , '' she added , with a sudden access of gravity , `` if you had not been clever enough to save my life when the snake bit me , you would most certainly have been one of the victims ; indeed it was with a view to sacrificing you at the Customs that my father accepted you from the Igbo", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Reminds me of a story I once heard , b'gee -- pshaw , what 's the use of trying to tell a good story with everybody trying to shout at once", "context_len": 31}}}, "shalt": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 100, "context": "Put away from thee this extravagance : [ 326 ] who shall undertake an affair like this and demand it of the Sultan ? Meknoweth not how thou wilt do to make this request of the Sultan , and if thou speak sooth , [ 327 ] by whom wilt thou make it ? '' `` O my mother , '' rejoined Alaeddin , `` by whom [ should I make ] a request like this , when thou art at hand , and whom have I trustier [ 328 ] than thyself ? Wherefore my intent is that thou shalt make this request for me", "context_len": 106}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "' A poor man served by thee shall make thee rich ; A sick man helped by thee shall make thee strong ; Thou shalt be served thyself by every sense Of service which thou renderest", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "excel": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "Have a brother about four years older than yourself , I should say ; and if your temper is hot , and your disposition revengeful , and you are a vain and ridiculous dreamer at the same time that you are eager to excel in feats of strength and games of skill , and to do everything that the other fellows do , and are ashamed to be better than the worst boy in the crowd , your brother can be of the greatest use to you , with his larger experience and wisdom", "context_len": 94}}}, "choke": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "Can \u2019 t you choke off , pard , begged the cowboy , and tell me how they played tag with you ? Sufferin \u2019 tenterhooks , but this business has got me all at sea", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "Armed with mattocks and picks , Malcolm , M'Turk , and M'Kane attacked the side of the entrance-tunnel at a spot a few yards beneath the choke", "context_len": 27}}}, "scintillated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "'Tilda Jane made no remark , but as she threw her dress over her head her two black eyes scintillated wonderingly in the woman 's direction", "context_len": 26}}}, "bitterly": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 45, "context": "Furlong had been more than kind , and more than good ; and the Duchess had arrived one morning and gone into the room where mother lay ( that is , what was left of mother ) , and had sobbed , oh , so bitterly , holding mother 's cold hand , and kissing her cheek ; and then she had turned to me , and said --", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "This man 's orders are from no competent authority , and I give you fair warning that you will bitterly regret your part in this night 's work if you live through it", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` By the powers they have , '' agreed the Skipper bitterly , and straightway he left the bridge and went below", "context_len": 22}}}, "foul": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "Had you first fallen foul of the chief 's galley , it would have gone hard with you , for his crew were so strong that you could scarce have overcome them before the other two vessels came up to his assistance", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` From the moment when I first became aware of my father 's illness I was not entirely free from suspicion ; and when at length I saw that your efforts to cure him were only partially successful , and that his symptoms persistently recurred , I was convinced that there was foul play somewhere , though why , I could not at first imagine", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Thus , at the earliest , it must be from six to seven days before you can bring us the news there ; if you meet with calms or foul winds you may be well nigh double that time", "context_len": 39}}}, "slaved": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "I 've slaved for her an ' waited on her , an ' heard her praised for work I 've done , and seen young men oggle her , an ' she oggle back , an ' I 've never had an offer an ' never will , an ' sometimes I think I hate her", "context_len": 56}}}, "praised": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 50, "context": "Alaeddin , seeing her with the dish in her hand , knew that most like some mischance had betided her , but cared not to question her till she entered and setting down the dish , told him what had passed and finally said to him , `` God be praised , O my son , I mustered courage to find myself a place in the Divan , albeit I could not win to speak with the Sultan to day ; but to-morrow , an it please God the Most High , I will bespeak him", "context_len": 96}}}, "offer": {"freq": 178, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 82, "word_index": 38, "context": "How came it that this girl had guessed his secret at once when most people passed him , taking him but for a sturdy beggar ? What made her suspect him of being a fugitive ? Was her offer of shelter but careless good nature , or a heroic endeavour to save a hunted man ? At any rate he had not fallen so low as to draw suspicion on a woman , and a young woman to boot , although she was plainly no nervous , fanciful , fine lady , but a bright , resolute , country girl , with good health and high spirits gleaming from every flash of her bright eyes , and every turn of her auburn head", "context_len": 123}, "VERB": {"freq": 96, "word_index": 68, "context": "Some hours later the captain returned , and upon my repeating to him the yarn which I had spun to the mate he not only very readily consented to my working my passage , but also offered me two excellent suits of clothes , two shirts , two pairs of stockings , a pair of shoes , and a worsted cap in exchange for my leopard-skin , which offer I gladly accepted ; and that night found me domiciled in the forecastle of L'Esperance as one of her crew", "context_len": 89}}}, "hate": {"freq": 136, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 119, "word_index": 76, "context": "She is not the least like my dear mother , and I never loved her , and I know that the best in me will not be brought to the fore if I am with her ; and I sha n't learn those things which would delight dear father ; I shall not know modern languages , nor be a good musical scholar , nor be able to sing nicely , and I -- I shall hate that life , and my nature may be warped , and I -- but , oh ! I will win the Scholarship", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 53, "context": "They knew the fellows who were helping the priest ; one of them was the boy who stood on his head till he had to have it shaved ; they would have liked to mock him then and there for wearing a petticoat , and most of them had the bitterest scorn and hate for Catholics in their hearts ; but they were afraid of the sacristan , and they behaved very well as long as they were in the church ; but as soon as they got out they whooped and yelled , and stoned the sacristan when he ran after them", "context_len": 103}}}, "dressing": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 37, "context": "Peggy and Bobby liked the fun of the preparations , though they crept out of the way of the visitors as much as possible , for to be shown up in the drawing-room involved an amount of dressing and tidying which did not fit in at all with their ideas of holiday enjoyment , and they much preferred a picnic tea in the orchard , with Rollo and the rabbits for company , to the more select charms of the best china and the stately patronage of the neighbouring dowagers", "context_len": 90}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}}}, "sisterly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "Speaking to a friend , long after in old age , she said that in her young days at home , \u2019 when a certain nameless gentleman was supposed to be paying his addresses to Fanny , the other sisters were very indignant \u2019 at the idea of any man wishing to break into their sisterly circle", "context_len": 57}}}, "repulsion": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "With a shudder of repulsion he examined his foe , after the thrashing had subsided and the great snake lay quiet", "context_len": 21}}}, "feared": {"freq": 132, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Then when she gets sick from stuffin ' herself , I 'm feared , an ' think she 's goin ' to die , but she 'll 'tend my funeral , an ' cry an ' look so handsome that some ole Jack will pop the question on the way home", "context_len": 52}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 126, "word_index": 10, "context": "Yet I scarcely knew precisely what it was that I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether", "context_len": 128}}}, "funeral": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 37, "context": "For the same reason he did his best , or his worst , to fall into a trance , in which he should know everything that was going on about him , all the preparations for his funeral , all the sorrow and lamentation , but should be unable to move or speak , and only be saved at the last moment by some one putting a mirror to his lips and finding a little blur of mist on it", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "The Duchess and the Duke both went to the funeral , which made a great impression on all the guests in the boarding-house ; and Lady Thesiger went ; I saw her at a little distance , as I stood close to mother 's grave ; but all these things were over , and father and mother were together again", "context_len": 60}}}, "pop": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 12, "context": "Edna thought it was , and now that she was ready to pop into bed , Reliance left her with a happy `` Good-night ! '' It was like sunshine in the house to have such a dear little girl as Edna , she thought as she went downstairs , and though Amanda reprimanded her sharply for not being in bed , she did not answer back , for , in fact , she scarcely heard her , so busy was she with pleasant thoughts , and so excited over the idea of the club", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "He forgot 'Tilda Jane for an instant , `` Sons -- ye know yer pop loves ye , do n't ye ? ''", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` I saw 'em in a window , grampa , -- a great big druggist 's window , -- an ' I went in an ' said to the man , 'Can you trust me for 'em ? I 'll pay you , sure pop , if you 'll gim me time", "context_len": 52}}}, "dislike": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 17, "context": "Mark Penelly watched him as he swam ashore easily and well , and the bitter feelings of dislike which had for the time being lain in abeyance before the scene of peril of which he had been witness , began once more to grow stronger , completely changing the appearance of his face as now , to get rid of the thoughts that troubled him , he took hold of one of the sweeps and began to row", "context_len": 78}, "VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 23, "context": "Fanning , I also will take the present opportunity of telling you that you and I have nothing in common , that I dislike your singling me out for special conversations of any kind , and that I hope in the future you will clearly understand that I do not wish you to do so", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now Mamie saw how she had made the other children dislike her by her ill-humor and unkindness , and she did not find it at all pleasant to stand there and have them all saying they would not sit by her", "context_len": 41}}}, "mortgage": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` I know , but I wish she could : Papa said I need n't worry about it , as the mortgage was not so very big , and the money they had to pay on it every year did not amount to such a great deal , but I know from what Miss Eloise said that she would like it to be paid ; she said she would feel ever so much more comfortable", "context_len": 75}}}, "lends": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "It is this home that lends its own special interest to the little lake , for it is the home of that grand old idealist , Ruskin", "context_len": 27}}}, "god": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Mis ' Grannis lends her money , Mis ' Grannis is the god my sister bows down to", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 62, "context": "Heaven knows who wrote it or published it ; his father bought it with a number of other books at an auction , and the boy , who had about that time discovered the chapter on prosody in the back part of his grammar , made poems from it for years , and appeared in many transfigurations , as this and that god and demigod and hero upon imagined occasions in the Boy 's Town , to the fancied admiration of all the other fellows", "context_len": 85}}}, "git": {"freq": 88, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 30, "context": "Do you think she 'd let you stand between her and Mis ' Grannis ? No -- the minute she heard you say Mis ' Grannis would be pleased to git you back , that minute she made up her mind to fool you and Hank Dillson that she ca n't abide 'cause he ai n't never asked her to stop bein ' a widow", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 29, "context": "I don \u2019 t s \u2019 pose y \u2019 u have et anything since y \u2019 u was locked up ; but I cal \u2019 lated you \u2019 d git off , an \u2019 I \u2019 ve got Sadie tendin \u2019 a big roast , an \u2019 we \u2019 ll have a feed that \u2019 ll give y \u2019 u injunjesshun , which I guess y \u2019 u can stand once if Joel , my late departed , could endure it all his born days", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "We \u2019 ll git our man sure , for the minute he blazes away , I \u2019 ll shoot a little to his right and ye do the same to his left : that \u2019 ll settle him", "context_len": 38}}}, "abide": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` [ 428 ] So he said to Alaeddin 's mother , `` Go and tell thy son that I abide by the promise which I made him , but an if he avail unto my daughter 's dowry ; to wit , I require of him forty dishes of pure gold , which must all be full of jewels [ such as ] thou broughtest me [ erst ] , together with forty slave-girls to carry them and forty male slaves to escort and attend them", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "When [ 438 ] the Sultan heard the Vizier 's words , he knew that this his speech arose from the excess of his envy ; so he turned to Alaeddin 's mother and said to her , `` O woman , go to thy son and tell him that I accept of him the marriage-gift and abide by my promise to him and that my daughter is his bride and he my son-in-law ; so bid him come hither , that I may make acquaintance with him", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Send one of thy servants to fill us from the jar and abide thou sitting with me , that I may take comfort in thee", "context_len": 25}}}, "hitch": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "So she made me help her hitch up , an ' she 's off on the wings of the wind to tell her sweet Mis ' Grannis to come an ' git you ; an ' just to fool her who is so cute at foolin ' other folks , I made up my mind to git you off", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Well , I guess we kin hitch up for no thieves , '' and she got up and moved deliberately toward the door as if she would recall her son", "context_len": 31}}}, "cute": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "So she made me help her hitch up , an ' she 's off on the wings of the wind to tell her sweet Mis ' Grannis to come an ' git you ; an ' just to fool her who is so cute at foolin ' other folks , I made up my mind to git you off", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Ai n't she cute ? What kind of folks must she have to let such a poor little innocent travel alone ? I do n't believe she 's obstinate", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Franky , '' said mamma , as the little girls , seeing Aunt Patty was not displeased , began to chuckle over their brother 's cute speech , `` you must not ask Aunt Patty to run about for you", "context_len": 41}}}, "folks": {"freq": 184, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 183, "word_index": 57, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Ai n't it queer how folks kin live 'slong as that and not see the ocean , '' he said , turning to Jennie", "context_len": 25}}}, "alarming": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "There was nothing of an alarming character in sight , and therefore , as soon as I had descended to the ground , we both set out for the spot where we had hidden our canoe , launched her , and made our way out through the labyrinth of mangrove roots to the margin of the river , where we lay perdu until the darkness had completely fallen , when we boldly pushed off into the strength of the current , and steadily pursued our way", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 87, "context": "But no sooner did he lower the threatening spear than I once more glued my lips to the wound , sucking hard at it with the object of extracting the poison before it had contaminated the blood ; and in this effort I was happily successful , for although there was a slight swelling of the limb , and some pain for an hour or two , that was all that happened ; and before morning my patient had quite recovered from all the effects of her alarming adventure", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "At that moment Barty heard the sound of many feet running on the deck and the shouting of many voices , as if something new and alarming was happening", "context_len": 29}}}, "aghast": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "When Becky wakened , and she sat upright staring aghast , her face still smudged with traces of tears , beside her stood a little figure in a luxurious wadded robe of crimson silk", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` The two ministers were aghast , and when Wolfe 's carriage was announced and he left the room , Pitt threw up his hands and said , 'To think that the fate of my country is in such hands ! ' ''", "context_len": 43}}}, "lacing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` MAMMA , '' said Maggie Bradford , as she sat upon the floor in her mother 's room , lacing her walking boots , -- '' mamma , I wish I had another terrible fault", "context_len": 36}}}, "gritty": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "There ain \u2019 t many men that \u2019 s as tough and gritty as Sal Peters", "context_len": 16}}}, "admiringly": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 32, "context": "Nothing could have been prettier than the wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Reliance carefully attended to her duties , Edna watching her admiringly", "context_len": 11}}}, "skedaddle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "When we git mos ' there , I 'll skedaddle home an ' to bed , 'cause I do n't want sister to find me out", "context_len": 26}}}, "tippet": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 19, "context": "Throwing the charred and smoking tops to him , she shut up the stove , took her hat and tippet from a peg in the wall , and clasping Gippie to her , left the house without one glance at the old man as he sat in the smoky atmosphere mumbling to himself , and fumbling over the burnt pieces of wood as tenderly as if they had been babies", "context_len": 70}}}, "fumbled": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 1, "context": "He fumbled desperately in the snow , for he wanted fifty cents above all things in the world just then , but he was destined not to find it ; and at last , cold , weary , and yet with all his faults not inclined to wreak his wrath on the pony who stood patiently watching him , he threw himself into the sleigh and sped gloomily homeward", "context_len": 69}}}, "plodded": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 30, "context": "Maggie had found that `` trudging up the hills '' took the breath out of a little girl , and papa 's words soon proved themselves true ; but she plodded along perseveringly , flushed and panting , holding to papa 's hand , and happy in her belief that she was sparing the horses by her own exertions", "context_len": 59}}}, "sprung": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 57, "context": "France and Auvergne , led by Ralph Harcourt , boarded at the bow , the other five langues at the poop ; and so impetuous was their onset that the pirates , who had still scarce recovered from their surprise at being hastily aroused from sleep to repel the attack of the foe who had so suddenly sprung out from the darkness upon them , offered but a feeble resistance", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "He was soon released , and explained that as he was setting the noose trap , his feet had unexpectedly gotten under the box trap , which was sprung , and then the noose slipped , holding him fast at both ends", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Now and then men have managed to become possessed of a file , and have , by long and patient work , sawn through a chain , and have , when a galley has been lying near our own shore , sprung overboard and escaped ; but for every attempt that succeeds there must be twenty failures , for the chains are frequently examined , and woe be to the man who is found to have been tampering with his", "context_len": 80}}}, "flakes": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 47, "context": "Abraham Lincoln and the spring waggon ! Had the whole beautiful summer been one delicious dream ? Could it be only a week since she had stood entranced in that forest of flame ? Here the leaves hung brown and shrivelled on the denuded branches , stray flakes of snow were in the air , and the early twilight fell chill and dreary", "context_len": 63}}}, "flake": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "Now , John , said Uncle Isaac , these fish can stay in the pickle till you get back from the island ; I \u2019 ve salted them slack , so they will not be hard and dry ; then you can take them out , put them on the flake , and dry them", "context_len": 55}}}, "struggled": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 61, "context": "`` And it means no , and thank you , and if you please , and I love you , and everything else he wants to say , '' said Maggie , catching up her frisky pet in her arms and giving him a hug , which he returned by putting his cold nose in her face , after which he struggled to be put down again , for so glad was he to be free this pleasant morning that he wished to show it by frolicking about on his own four feet", "context_len": 93}}}, "complaint": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` Now , sons , '' he said , straightening himself up and drawing on his woollen mittens , `` I 'm goin ' one way an ' you another , but if ye act contrairy an ' pouty to that leetle gal , I 'll know it , for she 's goin ' to write me , an ' if there 's any complaint , there 'll be such a wallopin ' as these ones this mornin ' would be a shadder an ' a dream to", "context_len": 88}}}, "whirl": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was all a whirl of delight to Peggy and Bobby , from the accomplished dogs who danced skirt-dances to the little pig who dragged the clown in a mail-cart and turned somersaults with amazing dexterity", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "Now and then the tail of a kite would part midway , and then the kite would begin to dart violently from side to side , and then to whirl round and round in swifter and narrower circles till it dashed itself to the ground", "context_len": 45}}}, "fatigue": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 7, "context": "Covered with dust , gasping with the fatigue of a three hours ' run beside the procession , but fresh at heart as in the beginning , they arrived with it on the Commons , where the tent-wagons were already drawn up , and the ring was made , and mighty men were driving the iron-headed tent-stakes , and stretching the ropes of the great skeleton of the pavilion which they were just going to clothe with canvas", "context_len": 78}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Ever and anon we looked forth from doors and windows into the black darkness without ; but although it was near midnight , neither sight nor sound told of aught amiss , and we were beginning to yield to fatigue , when I ascended the tower in company with Father Adhelm , to survey the scene for the last time", "context_len": 60}}}, "actions": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was clear to me that this unsophisticated young savage had been dominated in her actions by one idea alone , that of saving me from a death of unspeakable horror ; she knew that , in so doing , she was cutting herself off for ever from her own people , to whom it would be impossible for her to return ; and , in her absolute simplicity , she had evidently thought that it would be the easiest thing in the world for her to throw in her lot with mine", "context_len": 93}}}, "opposition": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 20, "context": "Mark declined , however , saying that he was quite well ; and soon after , in spite of the opposition he met with from the master , who said it was foolishness , Harry Paul plunged overboard , and swam to the bathing-place , where he dressed ; and , saving that he was suffering from a peculiar sensation of stiffness , he was not much the worse", "context_len": 69}}}, "precept": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "What was she doing ? Had she -- a little girl -- any right to give so much trouble to grown people ? Her actions were exactly in opposition to every precept that had been instilled into her mind", "context_len": 39}}}, "instilled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "What was she doing ? Had she -- a little girl -- any right to give so much trouble to grown people ? Her actions were exactly in opposition to every precept that had been instilled into her mind", "context_len": 39}}}, "obedient": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 59, "context": "They did not want to go , and begged her to let them stay at home ; but she said she could not keep Franky in the house all the afternoon , and she thought their mamma would wish them to go out as usual ; so they said no more , and went with her , like the obedient children they were", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Because he was strong , quick , and obedient , the navigating officers took a genuine interest in his welfare", "context_len": 20}}}, "uncomplaining": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Day after day he lay upon his bed , sometimes suffering very much , but always gentle , patient , and uncomplaining", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "All the while , however , he was utterly uncomplaining , seeming to be bent upon making up for all the fretful moodiness of the selfish old bachelor days up in London", "context_len": 32}}}, "punishment": {"freq": 83, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 80, "word_index": 116, "context": "and dear and cheap , till , one day of the days , he arose in the morning and donning his clothes , went forth , intending , as of wont , for the jewellers ' market ; but , as he went , he heard the crier proclaiming aloud on this wise , `` By commandment of the Lord of Beneficence , the king of the age and monarch of the time and the tide , let all the folk shut their shops and stores and enter their houses , for that the Lady Bedrulbudour , daughter of the Sultan , purposeth to go to the bath , and whoso transgresseth the commandment , his punishment shall be death and his blood be on his own head", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Some men are foolish and obstinate , but , so far from doing any good , this only brings trouble on themselves ; they come in for punishment daily , they are closely watched , and their lives made hells for them", "context_len": 42}}}, "mischievous": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 3, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}}}, "frame": {"freq": 84, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 74, "word_index": 54, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 33, "context": "He hesitated for a moment , not from any intention of refusing to give his uncle his confidence , or denying that he had been in trouble , but from a desire to frame his confession in the best manner possible ; but nothing came to his aid other than the plain , unvarnished truth ; nothing else , he felt , would serve his turn here with that steady , searching eye upon him ; and in a moment he had taken his resolve , and the whole shameful tale was poured into Colonel Rush 's ears", "context_len": 98}}}, "subjected": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 16, "context": "Children in factories and mines required to be protected from the cruelties to which they were subjected ; chimney sweeps needed to be guarded from the dangers to which they were exposed ; the hours of labour in factories were excessive ; thieves required to be shown a way of escape from their wretched life ; ragged schools and other institutions needed support", "context_len": 63}}}, "steeling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Could she endure the thought of that little feeble frame subjected to torture ? She could not , and steeling her heart against the asylum , the matron , and the lady managers , she walked on more quickly than ever", "context_len": 41}}}, "ghostly": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 26, "context": "Beyond the fact that a lady in white was supposed to haunt both the ruins and the oak-wood , weeping and wringing her hands in orthodox ghostly fashion , the children had not been able to learn much of the story , for there were so many and divergent accounts of it , all told with uncertainty as to names and dates , and in that very oracular -- not to say muddled -- style sometimes indulged in by rustic historians", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "They may or may not have belonged to Dowd , and Dowd may have been an Irishman in the neighborhood , or he may not ; he may have died , or he may not ; but there was something in the mere gruesome mention of his spectacles which related itself to all the boy had conceived of the ghostly and ghastly , and all that was alarming in the supernatural ; he could never say in the least how or why", "context_len": 82}}}, "sentinels": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 8, "context": "Fortunate is that company which has two such sentinels as you , commented the man , with something like a chuckle ; if we run into danger from Indians , shall we be able to borrow you two , or if your friends can not spare both , can we have one ?", "context_len": 53}}}, "slanting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 63, "context": "Yes , it had come , and it was a long letter closely written on foreign paper , and Kitty took a very long time reading it , so long that little Dolly slipped off her lap and wandered restlessly to the window and stood there gazing out into the court , and then back again into the softly-shaded room , with the slanting rays of the afternoon sun making bars of light across the oak", "context_len": 76}}}, "mystery": {"freq": 106, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 105, "word_index": 83, "context": "I tried in vain to shake off the feeling , and at last I pretended to trace it to feverishness resulting from the wound in the scalp ; but I knew this was not so -- I knew that one of the great things of life was behind it all ; I knew that I had come to the hour that young men hope for and older men dread ; I knew that for good or evil my future was wrapped in the mystery and tangle of which Jane Ryder was the centre", "context_len": 93}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "What they wanted was mystery , a place which was out of the way , and one specially set aside for their meetings", "context_len": 23}}}, "solemnity": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "And when , at night , impressed with the sense of solemnity which night always imparts to strange and novel scenes , they looked up to the bright round moon , pleased with the expression of cheerfulness and companionship which beams always in her light , to find her suddenly waning , changing her form , withdrawing her bright beams , and looking down upon them with a lurid and murky light , it was not surprising that they felt an emotion of terror", "context_len": 84}}}, "pure": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 109, "context": "him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought , of God , why did He not own us ? What were General Harrison 's dreams of a pure republic , but vanity ? Who can say if his dreams of heaven were any truer ? ''", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 71, "context": "When [ 312 ] the goldsmith heard this , `` Out on this accursed one , '' cried he , `` who fleeceth the servants of God the Most High ! '' Then he looked at Alaeddin and said to him , `` O my son , this Jew is a cheat , who hath cheated thee and laughed at thee , for that the silver of this thy platter is pure and fine ; and I have weighed it and find its worth threescore diners and ten ; so , an it please thee take its price , take [ it ]", "context_len": 103}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Women and children , civilians and soldiers , gave themselves up to pure gladness of heart , and in that meeting all thought of past woes and dangers faded away", "context_len": 30}}}, "trailing": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 10, "context": "She had got her guilty feet off that angel 's trailing robe , and the little letters from heaven were not dashing in her face , nor was there any danger now that one of the groaning trees bending to lament over her would fall and crush her shrinking form", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 72, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}}}, "angel": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 67, "word_index": 18, "context": "Yet there was a wondrous beauty still lingering over them ; they seemed etherialised -- as if an angel 's smile had last stirred their lines , when the spirit went forth , and left its imprint of wonder , joy , and awe thereon ; and Alfgar instinctively turned from them to the blue depths of heaven above , where a few stars were visible , although dimmed by the moonlight ; and he seemed to trace his beloved Bertric 's passage to the realms of bliss", "context_len": 88}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "She soon became known amongst the members of the Indian Church as the Buzurg , or Honourable Miss Sahib ; and the title of Firishta or angel was not unseldom heard in connection with her name", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "And when you die does n't an angel come and take you in his arms and just carry you up and up to the other side of the sky where everything is peace and loveliness , and no one will torment you -- -- ''", "context_len": 45}}}, "snowflakes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "Jack Frost threw photographs of fairyland upon the windows , and hung the roofs with fringes of crystal pendants , while the snowflakes piled themselves over the fences and made a shroud for the trees , and every day Pauline , with this strange peace in her heart , did her housework to the glory of God", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "But look , there are really snowflakes falling now", "context_len": 9}}}, "showered": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 52, "context": "Goaded by his words , the men made a general rush upon Gervaise , and , in spite of his desperate efforts , threw him on to the deck and bound him ; then the captain , seizing a heavy stick in his left hand , his right being still powerless , showered blows upon him until Gervaise almost lost consciousness", "context_len": 61}}}, "erring": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "So Rollo bore his punishment as best he could , and was fed behind the pigsty by the sympathetic Peggy , who , while mourning for the departed Ruffles , forgave her erring pet from the bottom of her heart when she saw the depths of his unutterable woe", "context_len": 49}}}, "missives": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` We 're ready for it , and the folks know we 're all right , '' for they had walked to a cross-roads rural free delivery box that day , and deposited some letters to go to Darewell , as they knew the mail carrier would collect the missives", "context_len": 50}}}, "skies": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 85, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The dawn was already breaking in the east , and even as he gazed upon the purpling skies the birds began their matin songs of praise , and the valley awoke", "context_len": 31}}}, "stubbornly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "He was threatened on one occasion that he should not go on a pleasure excursion because of some offence he had committed ; and when afterwards he was given permission he stubbornly refused the treat -- circus though it was , dear to the heart of a lad", "context_len": 48}}}, "repent": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 68, "context": "`` Suppose you had a nice little girl an ' some one wanted to take her away , an ' frighten her , an ' tie jinglin ' things to her an ' make her run , an ' you 'd ketch her up an ' run off to the woods , would that be awful wicked , do you s'pose , an ' would you have to repent ? ''", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Forswear Square Toes ; repent thee of Republicanism , and I 'll stand godfather to thy new life ! So go and get thee wisdom ! '' And the young fellow turned , laughing , back to his work , while Richard sadly retraced his steps to Mrs", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "Art thou willing to accept such penance as the Church , in consideration of thy perjuries , thy murders , which man may not avenge , since treaties protect thee -- but which God will surely remember , if thou repent not -- to accept such penance , I say , as the Church shall impose ? ''", "context_len": 58}}}, "stomach": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 84, "context": "In one of his school-books was a story that began , `` Charles was an honest boy , but Robert was the name of a thief , '' and it went on to show how Charles grew up in the respect and affection of all who knew him by forbearing to steal some oranges which their owner had set for safe-keeping at the heels of his horse , while Robert was kicked at once ( there was a picture that showed him holding his stomach with both hands ) , and afterwards came to a bad end , through attempting to take one", "context_len": 103}}}, "brisk": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "The sick feeling continued , and she gave Ruth Ann only a feeble `` yes , '' when she suddenly turned and threw the light of the lantern on her with a brisk , `` Do n't you want to know what lie I 'm goin ' to tell 'bout your leavin ' ?", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 11, "context": "Although the gale had entirely abated , there was still a brisk wind blowing , and it was evident to the captain of the corsair that under such circumstances he could outsail the galley that had long been searching for him ; when , therefore , the Santa Barbara came in sight , just as he and his crew had finished stripping the wreck of its contents , the idea had occurred to him to attempt to entice some of the knights to land", "context_len": 84}}}, "pots": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 26, "context": "They 'll come ravagin ' out -- 'Ruth Ann , that young one 's run off ! ' An ' I 'll be busy with my pots an ' pans , an ' all I 'll have to say is : 'Do tell ! ' or , 'Why , how you talk ! ' An ' sister 'll rave an ' tear , an ' run round like a crazy thing , an ' look at Mis ' Grannis out o ' the corner of her eye", "context_len": 87}}}, "pans": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 79, "context": "Beyond the hedge lies a pasture meadow , where a flock of sheep are grazing , and encircling the meadow another hedge , less closely clipped , and so making bold to riot here and there in a snowy wealth of hawthorn blossom , A fine Alderney cow , with coat as well cared for as the gray mare 's in the stable , is also enjoying the sweet grass of the meadow , and the shining milk , pans ranged beneath the kitchen window bear witness to the generous service she renders", "context_len": 93}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It 'll be richer than any claim we 've worked yet , if it pans out as well as I expect -- eh , Tom ? ''", "context_len": 27}}}, "rave": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "They 'll come ravagin ' out -- 'Ruth Ann , that young one 's run off ! ' An ' I 'll be busy with my pots an ' pans , an ' all I 'll have to say is : 'Do tell ! ' or , 'Why , how you talk ! ' An ' sister 'll rave an ' tear , an ' run round like a crazy thing , an ' look at Mis ' Grannis out o ' the corner of her eye", "context_len": 87}}}, "tear": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 106, "context": "For instance , if you had been beguiled , as a little boy , into being the last in the game of snap-the-whip , and the snap sent you rolling head over heels on the hard ground , and skinned your nose and tore your trousers , you could cry from the pain without disgrace , and some of the fellows would come up and try to comfort you ; but you were bound in honor not to appeal to the teacher , and you were expected to use every device to get the blood off you before you went in , and to hide the tear in your trousers", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "Minnie finds that there are holes in the ground , hidden under the grass , which make her trip , and there are disagreeable plants growing among the daisies , which sting her legs , and even some that tear little slits in her frock but when one goes a-hunting one must not be particular ; another time she will ask for a pair of gaiters , like Bertie \u2019 s , and a very thick frock", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 29, "context": "Poor , poor little Roy , how innocently he had committed this great crime ! how often had she kept him quiet by giving him an old newspaper to tear ! Yes , she , and she alone , was the only one to blame for the mischief done that night ; but whoever was the guilty party , Roy must have his supper and go to bed ; it was far too late already for a little child only two years old to be up", "context_len": 86}}}, "flitting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Ye \u2019 re right , younker , remarked Shagbark , who appeared at the side of the two with no more noise than that of the flitting shadows on the plain", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "How long I sat there I know not ; it may have been one hour or it may have been many ; but some time during the night there came a rap at the door and the pictures of Jane Ryder were blotted out of the fire and went flitting up the chimney", "context_len": 53}}}, "peremptorily": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "A grey-coated policeman drew his sabre and attempted to bar the way , shouting peremptorily in a manner that clearly indicated `` Stop ! ''", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Nicht mehr ! '' exclaimed a corporal peremptorily", "context_len": 9}}}, "walking": {"freq": 183, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 159, "word_index": 81, "context": "wrote to me several times , and in each letter said that he knew perfectly well that I meant to be free until the year was up , and that he was not going to worry me with overmuch love-making , or any nonsense of that sort ; but he thought I would like Switzerland , and the change would do me good , and although he would not say much , and would not even ask me to go out walking with him unless I wished it , yet I was to be certain of one thing , that he was ready to lay down his life for me , and that I was the one thought of his heart , the one treasure of his soul", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 13, "context": "She had still as of old a peculiarly elastic and springy mode of walking ; and while possessing no pretensions to actual good looks , there was much charm of manner , together with great animation", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Marshalled in military formation , the `` walking cases '' boarded the carriages , which were similar to the fourth-class compartments of the German State Railways -- hard wooden seats not excepted", "context_len": 32}}}, "circular": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 43, "context": "Besides the bed , there was in the room for furniture a broken-backed rocking-chair ; a small table with a split top , on which stood a common kerosene hand-lamp ; a dingy white earthen water pitcher and bowl the former with a circular piece broken out of its nose sitting on a washstand , made of a long box stood on one end , with a muslin curtain hanging in front of it", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It was made distinct from the rest of the square by one or two low embankments or terraces ; in its centre stood , on a low circular mound , a four-sided pole or pillar , sometimes forty feet high", "context_len": 40}}}, "agent": {"freq": 90, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 90, "word_index": 68, "context": "It had been an idle bachelor 's fancy to build after this unique fashion some ten years before ; but when Harold 's mother had come seeking a home in Windsor , he was already tired of it , and she found the house was To be let , provided desirable tenants could be found ; and desirable the little widow proved in the eyes of the discriminating agent", "context_len": 69}}}, "snug": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 62, "context": "Here and there Lilian ran , hunting in all Peggy 's favourite haunts -- now peeping into a hollow yew-tree , now peering at the top of a ladder , now rummaging in the tool-shed , then back through the sand-quarry into the stack-yard , where there was a very good chance that the young lady might be hidden away in some snug little hole among the hay ; but though Lilian got a tolerable amount of hay-seed into her hair , her efforts were fruitless , and she was just turning away , hot and out of breath , to give up the useless search , when the sound of a low , chuckling laugh attracted her to the barn", "context_len": 121}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Prickles generally slept most of the day in a snug box lined with hay , but in the evenings he woke up , and Peggy would carry him into the kitchen , where he devoured black-beetles , much to his own delight and Nancy 's satisfaction", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "The lumber merchant and his son were down in a black valley of soft , smothering furs , Poacher was running agreeably behind , and Gippie was snug and warm in her lap", "context_len": 33}}}, "bug": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 21, "context": "I think it \u2019 s real nice to see them play in the water ; and , when one gets a bug , the others swim after , and try to get it away from him , and all going one right after the other to the pond in the morning", "context_len": 51}}}, "asks": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 9, "context": "I sez he ai n't in , but I asks Miss Lilian , and she tells 'em `` Yes , '' and there they all is , pokin ' about amongst the nettles , and grubbin ' up stones , and stickin ' bits of mortar in their pockets , and dodgin ' about with yard measures , for all the world like a set of lunatics", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "What 's the matter ? asks Harold , who is on his knees on the floor , trying to make some very stiff wrapping-paper accommodate itself to the edges and corners of a generous box of luncheon , and is : quite too preoccupied to look up", "context_len": 47}}}, "ticket": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` I enclose herewith a five-pound note , and please ask Florence to exchange her third-class ticket for a first-class one , and telegraph to the station-master at Hilchester to have a carriage waiting for her , in order to take her back to the school as my niece ought to arrive", "context_len": 52}}}, "fret": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 49, "context": "If there were more girls like you in the world I should n't be surprised if there were a lot of good men too ; and the world could be oiled on all its hinges , so to speak , so that it would n't creak and jump and fret one at every turn as it seems to have an unpleasant habit of doing at the present moment", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Indeed , this not knowing is responsible for a great deal of this world 's fret and worry , and yet too much knowing would be just as sorry a thing sometimes ; so perhaps it would be as well for us to leave matters as they are for the present", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "we grow sullen , silent , and reserved , and fret at each other upon every little occasion", "context_len": 18}}}, "anyway": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 88, "word_index": 105, "context": "All round , before the circus doors were open , the doorkeepers of the side-shows were inviting people to come in and see the giants and fat woman and boa-constrictors , and there were stands for peanuts and candy and lemonade ; the vendors cried , `` Ice-cold lemonade , from fifteen hundred miles under ground ! Walk up , roll up , tumble up , any way to get up ! '' The boys thought this brilliant drolling , but they had no time to listen after the doors were open , and they had no money to spend on side-shows or dainties , anyway", "context_len": 106}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "A girl that spent half her time at school and the other half skylarking would n't amount to much anyway was her opinion", "context_len": 23}}}, "winder": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "He smelled to 'em and tasted 'em , and he said as there wor poison in 'em ; and he threw 'em , bottle and h'all , out of winder", "context_len": 30}}}, "shuddered": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 20, "context": "Guy was greatly saddened by the sufferings of the child , and by the grief of its parents , and shuddered when he saw the bones of animals which lay by thousands bleaching upon the desert , and once was filled with horror on coming across a human skull , which the prairie wolves had dragged from some shallow grave , and separated far from its kindred bones", "context_len": 68}}}, "guilty": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` The soul that sinneth it shall die '' -- '' For whosoever shall keep the whole law , and yet offend in one point , he is guilty of all '' -- '' Keep thyself pure '' -- '' For without are dogs , and sorcerers , and murderers , and idolaters , and whosoever loveth and maketh a lie '' -- that meant without the city , the beautiful city of gold where her mother probably was , and many of her unknown relatives , and where all good matrons , orphans , and `` lady-boards '' went", "context_len": 100}}}, "groaning": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "She had got her guilty feet off that angel 's trailing robe , and the little letters from heaven were not dashing in her face , nor was there any danger now that one of the groaning trees bending to lament over her would fall and crush her shrinking form", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Stumbling over the sleepers , the ration-party kept their groaning vehicles rumbling along the hastily-laid track", "context_len": 16}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "So she watched with delight the growth of the hydraulic pump , groaning a little over the dirty boots and muddied clothes that ensued , but assuring her friends that the 'dear boy ' was a perfect genius , and would make his mark in the world , and relating the story of his achievements with most unbounded pride and satisfaction", "context_len": 61}}}, "lament": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "She had got her guilty feet off that angel 's trailing robe , and the little letters from heaven were not dashing in her face , nor was there any danger now that one of the groaning trees bending to lament over her would fall and crush her shrinking form", "context_len": 50}}}, "crush": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 42, "context": "Looking back , the manacled lad saw Jerry standing there , his face hidden in his hands , the yellow dog gazing up sympathetically at him , a spectacle never to be forgotten ; and the frightful injustice of fate seemed to crush and smother the last spark of hope and strength in Ben \u2019 s soul", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Just you wait till we 've got shot of this crush , '' said the man , indicating a number of South Africans who had just completed their two-hours ' voluntary task", "context_len": 33}}}, "shrinking": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 50, "context": "In her little sketch Charlotte says of him , -- Careless of his own comfort , restricting his personal expenses to a very narrow compass , he gave to the Missionary cause at the rate of forty pounds monthly , and one year even more \u2019 ; adding that with shrinking from ostentation \u2019 he had never given his name on these occasions", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "'What on earth has that stupid Nancy given me a hot bottle for on such a warm evening ? ' he exclaimed ; and flinging down the clothes he disclosed to view , not the expected earthenware bottle , but the shrinking and apologetic form of Master Rollo , who , if ever a dog could be said to own a conscience , surely showed he knew he had offended , and repented heartily of his sins", "context_len": 77}}}, "creaking": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "They were creaking all around the circular opening -- those spying trees -- staring through the curtainless windows at her , and instead of throwing on more wood , and making a blaze that would enable her to be plainly seen , she opened the stove door , and , cowering over the embers , changed her wet foot-gear , and tried to dry her clinging skirts", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then he lifted the head of the sofa , and warned by an ominous creaking , he lowered it gently to the floor of the college room which he and his chum , Sid Henderson , were about to leave , with the assistance of Phil Clinton to help them move", "context_len": 51}}}, "spying": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "They were creaking all around the circular opening -- those spying trees -- staring through the curtainless windows at her , and instead of throwing on more wood , and making a blaze that would enable her to be plainly seen , she opened the stove door , and , cowering over the embers , changed her wet foot-gear , and tried to dry her clinging skirts", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "At the gate they paused , for it was shut , and he of the gray whiskers , after fumbling at the latch with his whip , was evidently preparing with much reluctance to dismount , when , spying Peggy , he called to her , and asked her to open it", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "blaze": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 50, "context": "But one wet afternoon , finding David alone in the harness-room , where the old man had lighted a fire to make some wonderful decoction of foxgloves wherewith to doctor a cow with a strained leg , they seated themselves on a sack of potatoes in front of the cheerful blaze , and with the aid of a little judicious flattery and coaxing managed to cajole him into a true and circumstantial account of the family ghost", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "There was a porch , too , with snow-white pillars , and an open fireplace , all tiled with adobe , in which might blaze fires of pi\u00f1on wood , full of resin and burning as nothing else can burn save driftwood , sodden with salt and oil and the mystery of old ocean", "context_len": 54}}}, "enable": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 56, "context": "I must cure the king , or it would be so much the worse for me ! And how was I to cure him ? My knowledge of disease was of the slightest and most amateurish kind , and , for aught that I could tell to the contrary , might not even be sufficient to enable me to diagnose the case correctly , much less to treat it successfully ! However , there was no use in meeting trouble half-way ; the only possible course was to obey the summons forthwith , and do my best , leaving the result in the hands of Providence", "context_len": 106}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "The Maugrabin fell to examining it and knew that this all of it was the work of the Lamp ; so he said , `` Alack ! Alack ! Needs must I dig a pit for this accursed one , this tailor 's son , who could not come by a night 's supper ; but , an destiny enable me , I will send his mother back to spin at her wheel , like as she did erst , and as for him , it shall cost him [ 556 ] his life", "context_len": 94}}}, "cowering": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "They were creaking all around the circular opening -- those spying trees -- staring through the curtainless windows at her , and instead of throwing on more wood , and making a blaze that would enable her to be plainly seen , she opened the stove door , and , cowering over the embers , changed her wet foot-gear , and tried to dry her clinging skirts", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "She did so , at the same time cowering and raising the other arm to shield her head and face as if expecting a blow", "context_len": 25}}}, "embers": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 95, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}}}, "skirts": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 90, "context": "Neither Peggy nor Bobby had ever seen a wild-beast show before , for those visits to the Zoo , which are the joy of little Londoners , do not fall to the share of country children , and Archie had quite a lively time keeping them out of harm 's way , for Peggy declared the leopard looked so exactly like Tabbyskins at home that she must positively try to stroke it , and was under the rope and up to the bars before Archie could seize her by her skirts and drag her back , while Bobby 's curiosity on the subject of jackals and hyenas nearly led to the ejection of the whole party from the tent", "context_len": 119}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Just see those grass skirts scatter ! You ought to be ashamed of scaring the ladies this way , Barry !", "context_len": 21}}}, "entirely": {"freq": 168, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 168, "word_index": 13, "context": "The party who had brought me to this remote spot were of an entirely different race from those among whom I now found myself , and the fact that we were making our way toward what was obviously the aristocratic part of the town , coupled with the expressive conversation carried on by the leader of my custodians with three or four individuals who had joined us , led me to surmise -- although of course I did not understand a word of what was said -- that I had been brought up the river as a peace- offering or something of that sort , which conclusion was again the reverse of reassuring", "context_len": 113}}}, "weaker": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 61, "context": "The melancholy faces of the two little girls grieved their older friends ; and , besides , it was sad to know that children like Lem and Dolly should take delight in such wicked , wanton mischief , and to know that there seemed to be no way to do them good ; since they only came near those who were weaker and younger than themselves to do them harm , and ran from those who were older and wiser , in fear of the punishment and reproof their wickedness deserved", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}}}, "hugging": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 49, "context": "It did not take very long for the children to be hauled up by Joe at the top , and they found themselves standing among the Abbey ruins in the early gray of the dawn , with Aunt Helen rushing to catch them in her arms , and Lilian hugging them and sobbing over them by turns , while Nancy , her face all blotched and swollen with crying , kept hovering round to put in a kiss whenever the others gave her a chance , and even old David cleared his throat hard , and 'blessed the Lord they were safe", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` Grampa , I guess it 'll have to be you , '' she said , gaily , and , running to the old man , she threw her arms around his wrinkled neck , kissed his bald head , and fulfilled her promise of a hugging so vigorously that at last he called for mercy", "context_len": 56}}}, "quietest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The quietest and most undemonstrative passenger on the night train from Boston was the shabby little girl in the corner , with the bundle beside her on the seat", "context_len": 29}}}, "undemonstrative": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "To say that Ben was touched , would be to fail utterly in expressing the smallest degree of his feelings , yet he was a silent , undemonstrative fellow , and now he groped in vain for satisfactory words with which to thank the widow", "context_len": 45}}}, "shabby": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 51, "context": "Florence 's change in circumstances were much commented upon by the other girls , and there is no doubt that in her neatly-fitting dress with her abundant pocket-money she did appear a more gracious and a more agreeable girl than she had done in the old days when her frock was shabby , her pinafore ugly , her pocket-money almost nil", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 32, "context": "And Florence was naturally not at all a contented girl , and she had fought against her position , and disliked having to stint and screw , and she had hated her shabby dress and unwieldy boots and ugly hats and coarse fare", "context_len": 43}}}, "brakemen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The conductor , after one sharp glance , paid no attention to her , the brakemen paid no attention to her , the boy with the gum-drops and novels ignored her", "context_len": 31}}}, "novels": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 55, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}}}, "ignored": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 14, "context": "It seems to me that it is partly because death is hushed up and ignored between them that it rests such a burden on the soul ; but if children were told as soon as they are old enough that death is a part of nature , and not a calamitous accident , they would be somewhat strengthened to meet it", "context_len": 61}}}, "uninteresting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "She had the air of knowing where she was going , and also of being utterly uninteresting , and greatly to her relief she was left entirely to her own devices", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I am afraid you found it lonely and uninteresting", "context_len": 9}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "A most uninteresting city , this city of Brooklyn , some people affirm ; even those of us who love it best can not claim that it is great in anything but bigness but there are homes there we will match against homes the world over , not for show or for luxury , but for pure and transcendent comfort", "context_len": 60}}}, "devices": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 35, "context": "When you went to Scotland first , you and he were of one mind , one heart , but when you came hither again , your head was stuffed full of worldly wisdom and time-serving devices , talking of a Lord Protector instead of the glory of God , and hand and glove with that cruel Cromwell who did throw my saint into prison ! Your heart was turned from those that reared you , and given to their enemies ! And now you can stand by unmoved and see him you once loved haled to prison and death ! ''", "context_len": 101}}}, "precincts": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 45, "context": "When you moved away from a neighborhood you did not lose all your rights in it ; you did not have to fight when you went back to see the boys , or anything ; but if one of them met you in your new precincts you might have to try conclusions with him ; and perhaps , if he was a boy who had been in the habit of whipping you , you were quite ready to do so", "context_len": 80}}}, "vicinity": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 36, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "rate": {"freq": 207, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 207, "word_index": 58, "context": "How came it that this girl had guessed his secret at once when most people passed him , taking him but for a sturdy beggar ? What made her suspect him of being a fugitive ? Was her offer of shelter but careless good nature , or a heroic endeavour to save a hunted man ? At any rate he had not fallen so low as to draw suspicion on a woman , and a young woman to boot , although she was plainly no nervous , fanciful , fine lady , but a bright , resolute , country girl , with good health and high spirits gleaming from every flash of her bright eyes , and every turn of her auburn head", "context_len": 123}}}, "flaming": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 41, "context": "Such an inducement sent even the attractions of the baby 'possum to the winds , and feeling that Archie was indeed a friend worth having , they trotted ecstatically under his wing to the great , flapping tent , where the flaming posters set forth the attractions of 'The Brothers Amalfi , ' 'Jingo , the Performing Pig , ' and the 'Marvellous Flying Girl", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "The sky , flaming in bands of crimson , violet and orange , looked like the very gate of heaven , a golden city which you had only to cross the hills to reach -- surely another page in that book of which Father had spoken", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "'How unfair ! How shamefully mean ! ' cried Peggy , with flaming cheeks", "context_len": 14}}}, "roaring": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 100, "context": "in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war , and praising Little Van , whom he had hitherto despised as the `` Fox of Kinderhook", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 37, "context": "Up the steep bank they trudged , through fields of bracken higher than Bobby 's head , getting out at last upon a white road that wound along the top of a cliff , with a stream roaring below , till round a sudden turn they came upon Pengarth , a prim little village , nestling in a hollow , and consisting of a short street and a big chapel and school-house", "context_len": 72}}}, "monster": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 7, "context": "Some he said were telling how a monster rattlesnake had come to visit them without any invitation , and that the only food he would eat , was the youngest and fattest of their families ; and that their constant intruders , the owls , had the same carnivorous tastes , besides which they rendered themselves particularly disagreeable , by standing in the doors and staring at every dog that went by , and even preventing the entrance of visitors , to the great distress of all the belles and beaux in town", "context_len": 93}}}, "devour": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 126, "context": "legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all service time , and if she did not behave properly it might come down in great wrath and devour her", "context_len": 128}}}, "devoured": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 26, "context": "He had purloined David 's scarf from the saddle-room , and dropped it into the horse-trough , had filled Bobby 's hat with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread", "context_len": 128}}}, "guided": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 69, "context": "Dear little pilgrims ! there were jewels in their way such as they did not dream of ; but it was only earnest seeking such as theirs which could find them ; for they lay hidden beneath many a thorn and bramble and unsightly weed ; and they were to be found only by the help of this very jewel of prayer which shone so brightly that its light guided the little feet to the dark places where the hidden gems lay", "context_len": 82}}}, "staggering": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "No more stately and beautiful sea picture could be imagined than the Sea Witch , when Captain Bracewell had put her under this staggering press of sail", "context_len": 27}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 55, "context": "Noel Brooke skilfully evaded the close embrace which would have settled the fight then and there in favor of Ben , and skipping , first to one side , then to the other , rained in a shower of blows upon Ben , one of which took effect in his jaw , and drove him staggering back discomfited", "context_len": 58}}}, "bewilderment": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 13, "context": "When Morn began to pick himself weakly up , with eyes of pathetic bewilderment , they helped him find his cap , and tried to engage him in conversation , for the pleasure of seeing him twist his mouth when he said , of a famous town drunkard whom he admired , `` He 's a strong man ; he eats liquor", "context_len": 62}}}, "prey": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 3, "context": "Baulked of their prey , the mob still surged round us , and with a shout of : `` Let \u2019 em have the cat ! '' a great black object was sent flying towards us , and , striking my horse on the crupper , resolved itself into an enormous black cat , that spat and howled , digging its claws into the horse \u2019 s hide , and arching its back like the demon cat that is the reputed companion of every witch", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "As soon as it was understood that the galley of the knights had not only captured the thirteen corsairs , but had destroyed eleven others , and had thus annihilated a fleet that was intended to prey upon the commerce of Italy , and ravage the western coast , the alarm was succeeded by the wildest enthusiasm", "context_len": 57}}}, "pitiless": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "When it had passed , not a sign was to be seen of the brave and ill-fated captain , who , with a score of his men , had been swept against the pitiless rocks", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Alone and unaided she kept the boat in a favourable position in the teeth of that pitiless gale ; and as soon as her father signalled to her she waited for an opportune moment and rowed in", "context_len": 37}}}, "acquaint": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "For two hours she sat with swimming brain , then it occurred to her that she must in some way acquaint this wonderful and frightful means of locomotion , with her desire to alight at her destination", "context_len": 37}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 105, "context": "you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been seized while travelling with a safe conduct from the governor of Syria", "context_len": 128}}}, "frightful": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 44, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "At that moment above the jangling , clumping activity of the yards there arose on the night air one frightful , piercing shriek", "context_len": 23}}}, "locomotion": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "For two hours she sat with swimming brain , then it occurred to her that she must in some way acquaint this wonderful and frightful means of locomotion , with her desire to alight at her destination", "context_len": 37}}}, "alight": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "A small and sullen cloud had hidden the dreamy , thoughtful sun , and out of the cloud came wheeling , choking gusts , bearing bewildered snowflakes up and down , hither and thither , before allowing them to alight turbulently upon the quiet earth", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 70, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` The night is dark , yet must we watch till the day dawn ! -- watch -- ay , and not alone shall our lamps be burning , but our matches are alight and our muskets loaded", "context_len": 38}}}, "car": {"freq": 225, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 225, "word_index": 51, "context": "Matt recounted the manner in which he had been beguiled into the open country by the supposed messenger ; and he told about the accident to the taxicab , the revelation that the supposed youth was a girl , the finding of the driver , the passing of the red touring car with McGlory in the tonneau , the work of Dimmock and Sanders , a mile west of Rye , and the journey through Connecticut and into Massachusetts , finishing with his meeting with McGlory", "context_len": 86}}}, "bawled": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "The instant the canoe touched the beach Charlie leaped from it , and , rushing into the house , bawled out , Mother , put on the pot ! They \u2019 re coming with the birds ! O , lashings of them ! I \u2019 ll make a fire ! and ran for the wood-pile", "context_len": 55}}}, "unintelligible": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 70, "context": "It was exceedingly interesting to the American contingent of the party to gain a little insight into the life of the English navvies ; and they passed the little tap-room , reeking with smoke and smelling of pipes and beer mugs , rather more often than circumstances would warrant , for the sake of looking in on the jolly fellows , and catching a sentence or so of their almost unintelligible dialect", "context_len": 72}}}, "exclamation": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 74, "word_index": 7, "context": "In spite of myself I gave an exclamation of surprise , for he was none other than the man with the scarred face who had tried to rob me on the Portsmouth road over three years ago , and who had escaped from Colonel Middleton \u2019 s troopers in the Forest of Bere", "context_len": 53}}}, "elapsed": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 23, "context": "The degree of dialectic difference points approximately to the date of the separation of the respective communities , and untold centuries must have elapsed since the two main branches of the family were torn asunder , for Cha'hta differs about as much from Creek as the literary German does from Icelandic", "context_len": 51}}}, "brushing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "Already the snake \u2019 s tail was brushing the trunk of a tree and , feeling the contact , it was pulling its victim in the required direction", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I counted four boys and two girls , brushing , washing , and taking off their overalls in six different rooms", "context_len": 21}}}, "moustaches": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` ( Now this the accursed did , of his fear lest his chin veil should be raised , when he ate , and so his case be exposed and they know him for a man by his beard and moustaches", "context_len": 41}}}, "toothpicks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Oh , so you have toothpicks , have you ? Well , why did you say you did n't ? Take 'em out of your pockets and row yourself along that mantelpiece with 'em", "context_len": 34}}}, "brakeman": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` The train 's wrecked ! Come on , let 's get out , '' and he scrambled to his feet and started for the door as the conductor and a brakeman ran through the car toward the engine", "context_len": 39}}}, "ship": {"freq": 510, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 26, "context": "Why do n't you shout Westward Ho ! and come along o ' me ? I know many a roaring blade that would be proud to ship under such a captain as you 'd make ! '' Then leaning forward , he continued in a solemn whisper , `` What though I seem no better than a beggar -- cavado , cleaned out , as the Spaniards say -- if I could but get a loan of as much as would carry me across sea , I 'd be a rich man again", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 492, "word_index": 44, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "\u2019 I have on board , \u2019 quoth Red Peter , \u2019 twenty-five officers and men of his Britannic Majesty \u2019 s ship Gannet , not including this youngster ( meaning me ) and another ; you have Captain Lewis and four other of our men", "context_len": 46}}}, "distinguished": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 33, "context": "Marching up to what I conjectured to be the king 's house -- from the fact that it was not only by far the largest dwelling in the enclosure , but was also distinguished by an exclusive embellishment in the form of a row of a dozen poles , each surmounted by a human skull , planted upright in the ground before it -- we halted at a distance of some twenty paces from the entrance , with our backs turned toward the crucifixion tree , the leafless branches of which overshadowed us , and waited", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 53, "context": "You see , a gage of this kind does not necessarily mean that there is any deep feeling between the knight who bears it and the lady who bestows it ; it shows only that she , on her part , feels it an honour that her gage should be worn by a distinguished knight , and , on his part , that he considers it as somewhat more than a compliment , and wears it as a proof of regard on the part of one whose good opinion at least he values", "context_len": 93}}}, "issuing": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was not long before fresh light was thrown upon the scene , as the fire ships , issuing out from the inner harbour , burst into columns of flame , and , towed by boats , came into action", "context_len": 40}}}, "someone": {"freq": 94, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 94, "word_index": 59, "context": "Osborne arrived o ' Monday , with me in the midst of my washing , and never a bit of cake in the house ; and there was Squire Henley and his lady o ' churning day , and I had to leave the butter half made to bring in tea ; and ten to one there 'll be someone more o ' Friday , when I 'm cleaning my kitchens", "context_len": 71}}}, "platform": {"freq": 106, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 105, "word_index": 43, "context": "But here the Rector again stepped in , and limited the performance to half an hour 's duration , begging them to choose something appropriate for a Sunday-school audience , and if possible with a moral , and further reminding them that a platform consisting of tables hastily pushed together after the tea was concluded would scarcely give facilities for either footlights , drop-scenes , or side-shifts , a curtain being the utmost he could undertake to manage in that direction", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "With these thoughts in mind and forgetful of the fact that he had had nothing to eat since the afternoon of the day before , Bomba strode into the passage and found his way back to the strange room with the chairs and platform where Sobrinini had sung to him", "context_len": 50}}}, "motioned": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 54, "context": "The smile relieved matters somewhat , and Marie-Celeste , setting the little tray on the ground , picked up the cup-custard , which had suffered nothing by its fall , and putting it back in its place on the tray , took a seat in the corner of the rug , to which Ted motioned her , and then clasping her two hands round her knees , asked in a tone of most earnest inquiry , Now tell me , Cousin Theodore , why do you do things like this ?", "context_len": 91}}}, "beefsteak": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Come have some hot coffee , '' and he passed through a doorway into an eating-room , where 'Tilda Jane presently found herself seated before a steaming cup of coffee , and a plate of beefsteak and potatoes", "context_len": 39}}}, "bluntly": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I 'm lookin ' for Hobart Dillson 's , '' she said , bluntly , `` but I guess there ai n't no such person , for the nearer I get the more he seems to run off", "context_len": 39}}}, "viands": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "My uncle busied himself till all traces of his worry lost themselves in his activity ; my aunt bustled about the house , driving the servants hither and thither , bringing linen from the huge oak chest , furbishing the massive furniture , and causing a huge supply of viands to be prepared for the entertainment of our guests -- for the invited company numbered close on a score , so that our house would be filled to overflowing , and rooms had to be engaged at `` The Bell Inn '' and `` The Blue Posts ''", "context_len": 98}}}, "immensely": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 12, "context": "Hartley , in which the old keeper had taken to the boy immensely ; had made friends with Martha , as she showed him to the little room under the eaves and helped him to stow away the contents of his sailor chest , and had won his way straight to Mrs", "context_len": 52}}}, "partook": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "The chamber occupied by the king was a long roomy place , containing a single bedstead of carved wood , surmounted by the usual distinctive canopy , from which tapestried hangings depended , and upon which scriptural subjects were woven ; the furniture of the room partook of the usual meagreness of the times", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The cheese , apples , and cider furnished the dessert , of which the boys freely partook , as cider was not mentioned in Uncle Isaac \u2019 s pledge , or even thought of", "context_len": 34}}}, "emptied": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 12, "context": "When the shout had died away , and the wine cups were emptied , Gervaise , who was sitting on Sir John Kendall 's right hand , would gladly have retained his seat , but the bailiff told him that he must say a few words , and after standing in embarrassed silence for a minute he said , Sir John Kendall , and brother knights , I can only say that I am very sensible of the kindness with which you have been pleased to regard what seems to me after all to have been a very ordinary affair", "context_len": 100}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "There is a fine name for a Missionary Miss Sahiba ! Fancy my discovering one day that , in her crowded little dwelling , she had so emptied herself of needful comforts , that she had not so much as a basin to wash in", "context_len": 45}}}, "scraps": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 85, "context": "with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread and half-picked bones which the thief had concealed in some cunning corner inside a manger or under the roof of the loft", "context_len": 128}}}, "counter": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Well , I 'll call you Rosamund , because all the rest of the people here do ; but by-and-by perhaps I shall be behind a counter , and you will come in and ask for stationery -- I want particularly to go into a stationer 's shop -- or any other article you fancy , and I 'll have to say , 'Yes , miss", "context_len": 67}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Every life now is precious , and no amount of spoil that can be brought in will counter balance the loss of those who fall", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "outdoors": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "They had indeed `` waked the natives , '' for gazing downhill against the lights of the street and stores at its base they could see people rushing outdoors in palpable agitation", "context_len": 32}}}, "unrolled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Oh , the beauty eggs ! '' and she carefully unrolled a napkin , `` an ' the white rolls , an ' Washington cake , an ' a meat pie , an ' a tart -- I say , grampa , we 'll have a good dinner ! ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "freeing": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 47, "context": "In the first place , I am well treated here , and my position is not an unpleasant one ; secondly , the sum required for ransom is altogether preposterous ; thirdly , I am not without hopes that I may some day find other means of freeing myself without so great a sacrifice ; and lastly , that I have a reason which I can not mention , why , at present , I would not quit Tripoli , even were I free tomorrow", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "The painful freeing of his right hand , the fear that in the fall he might have sustained an injury that would cripple him and leave him a prey to the first beast of the jungle that might roam that way , the dread that he might never be able to free himself from those ruthless , imprisoning branches had blinded him to another and more imminent peril that threatened", "context_len": 70}}}, "disinclination": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "He ate with avidity , then , showing a disinclination to return to his bandages , hopped on his three legs along the platform beside her , his crooked tail meanwhile describing successive circles in the air", "context_len": 37}}}, "successive": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 32, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}}}, "circles": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 32, "context": "Well , the first place we went into was the Albert Chapel ; and oh , Donald , but it 's beautiful ! There 's a marble floor shaped in diamonds and circles , and there are such beautiful stained-glass windows , and under each window a picture of something from the Bible , and these pictures are made of different sorts of marble , somehow , and there 's a great deal of gold in them , that makes them more beautiful still", "context_len": 84}}}, "loiterers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The shouts and excitement in the meeting-house had warned the passers-by that something was in the wind , and a good many loiterers were hanging about the doors , who welcomed them with cries of `` Whoop , Roundhead ! whoop , crop ears ! '' and ribald parodies on the war-like psalms , whose sound could be clearly heard through the open windows of the room they had just left", "context_len": 71}}}, "infirmities": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Some of the loiterers about the station gathered around him , and seeing that his bodily infirmities were a subject of mirth rather than of compassion , 'Tilda Jane , in spite of warm protests on his part , once more swathed him in his shawl , and carried him with dignity into the waiting-room", "context_len": 55}}}, "protests": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "Some of the loiterers about the station gathered around him , and seeing that his bodily infirmities were a subject of mirth rather than of compassion , 'Tilda Jane , in spite of warm protests on his part , once more swathed him in his shawl , and carried him with dignity into the waiting-room", "context_len": 55}}}, "agreeable": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 53, "context": "Stanton understood it no better than she did , perhaps not so well ; but he was very much amused ; and as he thought these young nieces and nephews of his were very wonderful little beings , he told Aunt Patty many of their droll sayings and doings , making himself so agreeable and entertaining , that by the time his sister came in , the old lady had almost forgotten that she had cause to be offended , and was not only quite ready to meet Mrs", "context_len": 89}}}, "implore": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 56, "context": "If there is to be any order , if the whole scheme which mother has thought out so carefully , and father has approved of , means to establish a girl like Rosamund Cunliffe here as our leader , so that we are forced to do every single thing she wishes , I shall beg and implore of father and mother to let me go and live with Aunt Susan in the old Rectory at Dartford", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Then you would like me to leave you , Professor ? You would like me to write to my mother and explain all the circumstances to her , and beg and implore of her to take me away ? ''", "context_len": 41}}}, "paralysis": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "Something warned her that she ought to implore him to tell some one to have a care of her -- to see that she did not again get carried beyond her destination , but a kind of paralysis seized upon her tongue , and she could only open her mouth and gape stupidly at him", "context_len": 55}}}, "gape": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "Something warned her that she ought to implore him to tell some one to have a care of her -- to see that she did not again get carried beyond her destination , but a kind of paralysis seized upon her tongue , and she could only open her mouth and gape stupidly at him", "context_len": 55}}}, "nod": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 23, "context": "People knew the time of their passing , and looked out for them daily ; kindly women would come to cottage doorways to nod and smile ; the inn-keeper at the Halfway Arms set his clock by their arrival ; they had struck up quite a friendship with the postman , two milkmen , and the driver of a fish-cart , while the old man who broke stones by the roadside always nodded and gave them 'Good-morning , ' and fired a facetious remark or two after them , which the children imagined he must relieve the monotony of his work by composing during the intervals of their coming and going", "context_len": 111}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Armstrong was looking somewhat mysterious , and when she saw me she gave me a kindly nod , but I perceived the curiosity in her eyes and turned my face away", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "Baby seemed to think this was a good place for a nap , for she began to shut her eyes and nod her little head about , till mamma laid her down in her lap , where she went fast asleep", "context_len": 41}}}, "addressing": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 26, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}}}, "mildly": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Bless the child ! '' ejaculated an old lady in the seat before her , `` I guess this is her first journey , '' and turning around , she stared mildly", "context_len": 33}}}, "official": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 57, "context": "At the council this morning it was urged that some signal mark of honour should be granted ; but I interposed , saying that you had already received exceptional promotion , and that it would not be for your good , or that of the Order , for so young a knight to be raised to an official position of a character usually held by seniors , and that I was perfectly sure you would prefer remaining in command of your galley to any promotion whatever that would retain you on the Island", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 24, "context": "This young Greek , a stranger to Rhodes , was in communication not only with some of the slaves , but with a prison official , and the matter appeared so grave to Gervaise that , after some deliberation , he thought it was too important for him to endeavour to follow out alone , and that it was necessary to lay it before the bailiff", "context_len": 66}}}, "smartly": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 59, "context": "They were within a mile of Warford , and were going along at a quick trot , when , without any warning , a carriage and pair came suddenly dashing up behind from a side road , and passed them , giving such scant room that if Peggy had not been a clever little driver , and turned Pixie smartly into the hedge , an accident could scarcely have been avoided", "context_len": 71}}}, "informed": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 46, "context": "It was nearly noon when I was conducted back to my hut after my futile attempt to cure the king ; and it was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` As to his pugilistic abilities , I am by no means so accurately informed , but if my conjecture be of any value whatsoever , I should be inclined to infer , from the fact that our enemies , the representatives of tyranny and oppression , who are endeavoring to reduce us to submission , have selected him as their champion and representative in arms , that -- -- ''", "context_len": 71}}}, "mishap": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 13, "context": "The rope served as a guideline to the rest , and without further mishap the twenty-two survivors of the Gannet made the perilous passage , though after three years \u2019 knocking about on the high seas it was a sorry homecoming", "context_len": 41}}}, "dreamed": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 48, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "' '' It 's little wonder if I do love her , after thirty years of such nursing as no one even dreamed of", "context_len": 24}}}, "untied": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Thank God ! the very thing ! '' exclaimed the Colonel ; and , in an instant , the broad , stout ribbons were untied from the children 's waists , and strongly knotted together", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "He carefully lifted Gippie to a resting-place by the kitchen stove , untied 'Tilda Jane 's snow-shoes and strapped them on his back , stroked Poacher repeatedly , and finally with a hearty `` So long , little gal , let 's hear from you , '' he made her an awkward bob of his head and ran after his brother , who had reached the road", "context_len": 67}}}, "cents": {"freq": 130, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 130, "word_index": 56, "context": "There 's glass dishes on it , with oranges , an ' bananas , an ' cakes an ' pies , an ' sangwiches , an ' a funny machine where you drop a nickel in a crack , an ' if the hand points to five , or ten or fifteen , you get twenty-five cents ' worth of candy , an ' if you do n't get candy you get good advice like as , 'You 've been keepin ' bad comp'ny , quit it or you will never prosper , ' or 'You 've run away from home , an ' the perlice is on your track , ' or 'Smokin is a bad thing for your health", "context_len": 121}}}, "briskly": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 65, "context": "It was not very easy reading , for the print had faded till it was almost the colour of the yellow leaves , and the 's 's ' were all long , so that Peggy found herself continually reading 'fins ' instead of 'sins ' ; but she did her best , conscientiously , and the old man nodded in his chair , sitting up briskly , however , when he felt her reproachful eyes upon him", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Now then , my friends , '' said Bart briskly , as he saw to it that everything was in order for the sale , `` the motto for the hour is quick action and cash on delivery ! ''", "context_len": 41}}}, "preserved": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 51, "context": "I am sure , quite sure , that it has been your dear little Bessie 's unquestioning faith , her love and trust in the power and goodness of the Almighty and , more than all , her firm belief that one for whom he had done so much , and preserved through so many dangers , must of necessity have a double share of faith and love , which has touched his heart", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Was not the very fact that he had been so preserved , Bomba asked himself , a proof that the gods of the Indians , in whom he half believed , were on his side ? Surely he could not have come so far only to be mocked at last at the very moment when the end of his mission was in sight", "context_len": 63}}}, "discreet": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "Recovering his head-gear , Rifleman Carr joined the group by a discreet and circuitous route , to find Grouser Joliffe and half a dozen men of his platoon engaged upon the task -- up to now unsuccessful -- of clearing out a dug-out", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "To my delight , no one had said , `` Go to your stable , '' so I made a discreet circle of the house , which was pretty well spread out over the ground instead of being built away up in the air", "context_len": 44}}}, "waving": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 100, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 56, "context": "I say it from my heart that I consider it is to Sir Gervaise Tresham that we owe our success , and that , had it not been for his happy thought , the sun would have gone down on our dead bodies lying on the summit of the breach , and on the Turkish flag waving over the fort of St", "context_len": 62}}}, "aimless": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "'Just imagine , Paul , ' the letter went on , ' I , of all possible people , a missionary 's wife ! But the fact of the matter is , my precious saint , your splendid , consecrated life made me tingle with shame to my finger tips when I thought of my aimless existence , and when I remembered how you took up your cross and followed your Master to Sleepy Hollow , there seemed to be no reason why I should not follow Him to Africa", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 76, "context": "queer it is , he said to himself , that I should feel constrained in this way to run out to Windsor ! Land knows ! I have no desire to come to be on intimate terms of acquaintance with Evelyn 's boys ; and what would be the satisfaction of prowling around just to see where they live ? Their father gave me up after that time he spoke his mind so freely about my aimless life -- as he was pleased to call it -- and there is no reason whatever why I should bother myself about my sister 's children , since she , poor thing ! is dead and gone , and they have enough of this world 's goods to make them comfortable", "context_len": 128}}}, "scrutiny": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 35, "context": "The conductor , meanwhile , had gone to a Pullman car in the rear , where a man in plain clothes was lying back on a seat , apparently engaged in an aimless , leisurely scrutiny of the occupants of the car", "context_len": 42}}}, "emigrant": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 44, "context": "There seemed to her little prospect of freedom or pleasure in becoming a servant ; yet she said but little about it to Guy as she did not wish to dampen the ardor of his feelings , fearing that the stern reality of an emigrant 's life would soon throw a cloud over his blissful hopes", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 1, "context": "The emigrant trains were disposed to keep to certain paths , where the face of the country compelled a closing in , but in other sections the respective courses were separated by miles , and , as has been shown the parties plodding across the plains , even though their routes were parallel , were often so far apart that for days they saw nothing of one another", "context_len": 68}}}, "laconically": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "No home , said Donald , laconically , and with an unconscious little sigh that went straight to Marie-Celeste 's heart ; I was in the Foundling Hospital all my life till I came on the Majestic", "context_len": 37}}}, "imperceptible": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "Gervaise slept for some time ; when he awoke the same stillness reigned , but there was a change in the appearance of the sky ; its brightness was dulled by a faint mist , while , although the sea was of a glassy smoothness , there was an imperceptible swell that caused the felucca to sway uneasily", "context_len": 58}}}, "trio": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 51, "context": "They made no secret of the fact that my inquisitive glances were unwelcome , and so much did they scowl that I turned hastily away and looked out of the window , where the brilliant sunshine , beating down on the crowded courtyard , made a pleasing contrast to the dismal trio within", "context_len": 53}}}, "nab": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Why didn \u2019 t you nab him in New York , instead o \u2019 bringin \u2019 him \u2019 way out here ?", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "They \u2019 ll nab you in Leeville if you don \u2019 t", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then we 'll leave the letter in the tree , hide , and nab him when he comes for it", "context_len": 20}}}, "jeweller": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Want to see me nab that New York jeweller 's clerk ? ''", "context_len": 14}}}, "clerk": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 18, "context": "Then came a hurried discussion as to the destination of the booty , some , including the parish clerk , Fallowfield , who had gained the upper regions by means of the tackle , urging that it had best be taken and placed in the tower of Worth Church , the others insisting that it would be best to make one journey do , and convey it as close to Wareham as possible , where their accomplices could make arrangements for its distribution", "context_len": 83}}}, "curtains": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 56, "context": "There was a large oak staircase , with wide , shallow steps , leading to a panelled gallery , where hung swords , and rusty armour , and moth-eaten tapestry , and many an ancient relic of the past ; while in the best bedroom was a great carved four-post bed , hung with faded yellow curtains , where Queen Elizabeth herself was said to have slept in much state for two nights on her journey from Shrewsbury to Wrexham", "context_len": 80}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "The king , exhausted by his own violent emotions , lay back upon the bed , which occupied the centre of the room , surmounted by a wooden canopy , richly carved , from which curtains depended on either side", "context_len": 40}}}, "cards": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 11, "context": "If we stop now we will have the whole house of cards about our ears ; the tradespeople will sue for their money , the bailiffs will be in and will take possession of the furniture , even the very bed your mother sleeps on will be taken from under her", "context_len": 51}}}, "involuntarily": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 16, "context": "Passing the corner of the high board fence that surrounded the football field , his eyes involuntarily sought the open gate , through which he saw for a moment , as he hastened along , a bunch of boys bent over and packed together , saw a sudden movement as the football was passed , and then beheld them rush forward a short distance", "context_len": 64}}}, "assumed": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 59, "context": "What did it matter to me whether Albert Fanning paid for his household goods or not ? and how could it concern me what shop he chose to buy them at ? But I felt myself more or less in a trap , and knew the best way to prevent any crisis taking place was to put on an assumed air of absolute indifference , and to take the first possible opportunity of returning home", "context_len": 75}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 54, "context": "Could it be , Tom thought , that she was jealous of him , and took this method of showing it ? He did not think Madge would do such a thing , yet he felt that part of her gaiety and good spirits , when in company with the handsome quarter-back , were assumed for some purpose", "context_len": 58}}}, "players": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 4, "context": "One thing only the players felt to be missing in their entertainments , and that was the very important feature of an audience ; for what is the use of learning up parts , and constructing scenery , if there is nobody to come and watch you act ? Peggy sounded Father on the subject of a children 's party , but he did not rise at all to the occasion", "context_len": 71}}}, "surveyed": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 20, "context": "Peggy , hard at work behind the scenes , put her eye to a hole in the curtain , and surveyed the prospect below , where children big and little were engaged in jumping over the forms , chasing each other round the stove , and generally acting more like monkeys at the Zoo than civilized human beings , while the Rector strove in vain to collect them at the empty end of the room to play games , and the curate , a shy young man fresh from Oxford , looked as if he would have preferred to wrestle with a consignment of heathens from the Cannibal Islands", "context_len": 109}}}, "detected": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "He would have denied it could he have found words in which to do it , had he had time to frame a denial , but he was so entirely off his guard , so confounded by Seabrooke 's sudden accusation and this evidence of the dastardly deed he had performed that he was utterly overwhelmed , and stood speechless , and the picture of detected guilt", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 23, "context": "But a minute or two later , as we drew nearer the tree , the stench meanwhile becoming almost overpoweringly strong , I detected fastened to the trunk of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may", "context_len": 128}}}, "smuggler": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "Master Fallowfield , who , as I afterwards learned from the conversation , was the parish clerk of Worth Matravers church on Sabbaths and holydays , and an arrant smuggler at other times , took the paper and read in a sonorous voice a message from a neighbouring justice to the ill-fated James Harker , telling him that the reward due to the informer Crocker would be paid at any time after Martinmas", "context_len": 73}}}, "innocently": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 7, "context": "Poor , poor little Roy , how innocently he had committed this great crime ! how often had she kept him quiet by giving him an old newspaper to tear ! Yes , she , and she alone , was the only one to blame for the mischief done that night ; but whoever was the guilty party , Roy must have his supper and go to bed ; it was far too late already for a little child only two years old to be up", "context_len": 86}}}, "ineffectual": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "They therefore remained firm in their original simplicity and integrity , and after some respectful but ineffectual remonstrances , they stood aloof , alienated from their commander in heart , and condemning very strongly , among themselves , his wickedness and folly", "context_len": 42}}}, "attempts": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 74, "context": "Now that the danger was past , Drake and I could not help laughing at our cavalier companion and his fair burden , for the hag had clasped him tightly round the waist with her skinny arms , while the cat , perched on her shoulders , was rubbing its head against the back of Felgate \u2019 s plumed hat , so that it was being continually thrust over his eyes despite his frequent attempts to place it firmly on his head", "context_len": 82}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 86, "context": "`` First , Gervassen fell , almost at the side of Alstarnah : then , one by one , all the rest of the people sank down and were buried by the soft , white snow , until at last not one remained to tell of the verdure that once crowned the mountains where the North King still reigns , or of the people he slew with terrible arrows of snow , like those he still loves to throw in derision upon any daring traveler that attempts to invade his dominions", "context_len": 91}}}, "candy": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 38, "context": "All round , before the circus doors were open , the doorkeepers of the side-shows were inviting people to come in and see the giants and fat woman and boa-constrictors , and there were stands for peanuts and candy and lemonade ; the vendors cried , `` Ice-cold lemonade , from fifteen hundred miles under ground ! Walk up , roll up , tumble up , any way to get up ! '' The boys thought this brilliant drolling , but they had no time to listen after the doors were open , and they had no money to spend on side-shows or dainties , anyway", "context_len": 106}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "bag": {"freq": 109, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 100, "word_index": 17, "context": "The `` Little Japanese Cousin , '' with her toys in her wide sleeve and her tiny bag of paper handkerchiefs ; the `` Little Brown Cousin , '' in whose home the leaves of the breadfruit-tree serve for plates and the halves of the cocoanut shells for cups ; the `` Little Indian Cousin , '' who lives the free life of the forest , and the `` Little Russian Cousin , '' who dwells by the wintry Neva , are truly fascinating characters to the little cousins who will read about them", "context_len": 94}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` I should say -- not , '' answered Tom Hadley in a dissatisfied tone , bringing out the last word after a slight pause ; `` but I do n't see why I should n't carry the bag part of the time", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I wonder if I can shoot straight enough to bag them ? '' murmured the lad , as he quickly raised his gun , and banged away , first with the left , and then with the right barrel", "context_len": 40}}}, "agreeably": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` 'T was n't us , '' said the man , agreeably , `` 't was Poacher there , '' and he indicated the dog under the table , who , at the mention of his name , rose and walked politely toward the little girl", "context_len": 46}}}, "noting": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 0, "context": "Noting a small crab of the same yellowish color as the weed , he deduced erroneously but with logic that the crab came from the vegetable animals as a butterfly comes from a cocoon", "context_len": 34}}}, "stirring": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 5, "context": "For there was something very stirring in the act , and a stranger to the place would hold his breath in dread as he saw Mark Penelly , who was the finest swimmer at the port of Carn Du , climb up the side of the great black rock upon some fine summer evening , then go round along the narrow shelf of shaley stone , till he stood alone there forty feet above the sea , his white figure as he rested against the black rock , every muscle standing out from his well-knit frame , and his arms crossed , looking like some antique statue in its niche", "context_len": 110}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "William Lang told of his visit to Oak Swamp , and how he had fled at the sight of the boys , fearing they wanted to cause his arrest , and he also mentioned his trips to the mud volcano , and how he had run away at the sound of some one stirring in the campers ' tent , likewise how he had led the boys a chase about the town , just prior to the accident on the pole", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Sir George made a stirring speech , the drums beat to quarters , and then came that irksome interval before opening fire that tells so acutely on the nerves of even the most hardened veteran", "context_len": 35}}}, "border": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 84, "context": "But Lilian had kept her surprise in the background , and it was only when all was ready that she opened a basket , and proudly drew out her masterpiece , a substantial-looking cake , with a cut-paper frill , and white icing on the top , on which in pink sugary letters were inscribed the words : 'Success to Sky Cottage ! ' Certainly some of the capitals were a little staggery , and the ' y ' had strayed into the pink border round the edge , but it was felt to be a triumph of culinary art all the same , and gave quite a grace to the table", "context_len": 112}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "interposed": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 25, "context": "Wentworth 's upraised hands ; she set the boys to swing honey-pots , and the girls to play 'Drop the Handkerchief ' ; she boldly interposed her small person between the fists of two fighting hobbledehoys , and seized a little boy by the boot who had ventured to climb up the stove-pipe ; she welcomed some of the parents who had begun to arrive for the entertainment , and found them comfortable places on the benches , even nursing one of the babies while its mother went to the rescue of an older child , who was being forcibly held down and sat upon by several of its companions ; and by the time the bell was rung , and the audience requested to take their places", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` But , tia ! '' interposed Lola , `` the doctor was just going to tell me something very important ! ''", "context_len": 23}}}, "suspicious": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 23, "context": "For since Flossy was stolen , he had been very careful as to the company he kept , and looked with a very suspicious eye upon any one who wore a ragged coat , which was not very just of Flossy , since a ragged coat may cover as true and honest a heart as ever beat ; but as the poor puppy knew no better , and had received some hard treatment at the hands of those whose miserable garments covered hard and cruel hearts , he must be excused for thinking that the one was a sign of the other", "context_len": 102}}}, "apprehensively": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 56, "context": "Thanks be to Allah , the All Powerful , one of the men began , my lips are unsealed , and I can tell you the great news that our hour for escape from bondage is at hand ! We need not fear the warder there , he went on , as several eyes were turned apprehensively towards the guard , who , with his spear beside him , was leaning carelessly against the wall at the farther end , looking through the window into the courtyard ; he is with us", "context_len": 92}}}, "displayed": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 89, "context": "Moreover , she had with her eight and-forty slave-girls , whom Alaeddin had presented to her , in each one 's hand a great candle of camphor and ambergris , set in a candlestick of gold , studded with jewels ; and all the men and women in the palace went out with her and fared on before her , till they brought her to her bridegroom 's palace and carrying her up to her pavilion , [ 495 ] attired her in various robes [ 496 ] and displayed her", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` She went to where a flag-pole displayed a blue and white pennant", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Bridges , too , caused delays , for , in spite of vigorous blasts of the tug 's fog-horn , the persons in charge displayed no great activity in manning the winches by which the obstructions were swung", "context_len": 38}}}, "badge": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 59, "context": "The prize itself was to consist of a free education at Cherry Court School for the space of three years ; accompanying it was a certificate in parchment , which in itself was to be considered a very high honor ; and thirdly , a locket set with a beautiful ruby to represent a cherry , which was the badge of the school", "context_len": 63}}}, "inspector": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 16, "context": "The whole erection was so steady and well made that , though Father , as chief inspector , stamped vigorously about , he could not make it shake , and was able to pass it as perfectly safe , and give his congratulations to the young architect , while even Joe grudgingly admitted that 'Master Forster had n't made half a bad job of it neither", "context_len": 66}}}, "eyeing": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 43, "context": "He came down feet foremost , however , and they sauntered off along the road to the old water-mill , where the miller 's man was slinging a sack of flour on to a patient donkey who stood , with drooping ears , eyeing the burden which he must carry up far into the mountains , while his mistress , a little black-eyed Welshwoman , poured forth a torrent of gossip in high-pitched tones", "context_len": 74}}}, "glasses": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 15, "context": "He strolled about the open space , pausing now and then to survey through his glasses the snowy peaks which towered far into the sky to the westward , or at the lower hills to the north , where the gnarled pine , the dwarfed cedar , the rushing torrents and the gorges made up one of the many wild regions which abound over thousands of square miles of area", "context_len": 70}}}, "madam": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Dearest madam , '' he urged , `` when the Great Day of the Lord doth arrive , it will surely be of no moment whether it find you in London or in Newcastle ; it will be as the lightning that shineth from the east even unto the west", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "Harris said , Thank you , madam , with an affectation of profound gratitude ; but for all that he was none the less truly grateful", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 56, "context": "But what I wanted to say to you now was this : May Miss Wickham come for a drive with me in my son 's own brougham ? I am anxious to have an outing with her , and I see by her face she is desirous to come ; may she ? Say yes , madam ; if you are wise , you will", "context_len": 65}}}, "explanation": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 47, "context": "Harold 's principal achievement lay in The Walrus and the Carpenter , and Marie-Celeste 's in the recitation of Jabberwocky from Through the Looking-Glass ; for not only was she able to slip its almost unpronounceable words quite easily from her tongue , but she remembered the explanation of them given by Humpty Dumpty , when Alice appeals to him a little later on in the story , and he modestly informs her that he can explain all the poems that ever were invented , and a good many beside that have n't been invented just yet", "context_len": 97}}}, "namely": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 10, "context": "He knew well enough what he ought to do , namely , seize the drowning man by the hair , and then turn upon his own back and float , drawing the other after him ; but on trying this a difficulty met him at the offset : the man 's hair was very short ; but he got over it by grasping his ears , and then , throwing himself back , he struck out with his legs so as to keep afloat and go with the racing current", "context_len": 90}}}, "detain": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Well , madam , there ai n't much time for explanation , but I can tell you this much , namely , that we have to detain and examine all persons without means of livelihood who attempt to enter the United States from foreign countries", "context_len": 46}}}, "examine": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then ( says our author ) , the geomant proceeds to examine the sixteen figures thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it", "context_len": 128}}}, "livelihood": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 21, "context": "Moreover , O my mother , thou knowest of the lamp which is with me and which presently provideth for our livelihood ; [ 344 ] nay , all that I seek of it it will bring me , and I trust by its means I shall know how to answer the Sultan , an he ask me of this", "context_len": 60}}}, "foreign": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 95, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "suspiciously": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 10, "context": "Miss Ashton came over to her with her eyes very suspiciously shining , and stooping down kissed Bessie , saying , `` You blessed child ! '' while Maggie , always readily moved to tears or smiles , as befitted the occasion , put her arms about Bessie 's neck , and grasping her teacher 's skirts with the other hand and laying her head against her , began to cry softly", "context_len": 72}}}, "pauper": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "The boarding-house , on its present terms , can not go on , but we will close up gradually , and poor old Miss Mullins need not be a pauper for the rest of her days", "context_len": 36}}}, "foreigners": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 24, "context": "The Yamacraw chief Tomochichi , then banished from one of the Lower Creek towns , spoke in favor of making a treaty with the foreigners , and Yah\u00f3la ` l\u00e1ko , m\u00edko of Kaw\u00edta , allowed Tomochichi and his relatives `` to call the kindred , that love them , out of each of the Creek towns , that they may come together and make one town", "context_len": 67}}}, "guardedly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "The stranger did not retreat , when Alden quickly but guardedly approached , and after a couple of feints landed a blow fair and square on his cheek that staggered him", "context_len": 31}}}, "determined": {"freq": 260, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 231, "word_index": 57, "context": "And all this while the wilful little queen was trying to convince herself that it was ready for Donald 's sake , when the truth was that the long walks with Donald , when Harold -- who was making up some necessary back work at college -- was not at her service , were what she was determined not to give up , and the reading aloud in the evenings , when Donald was such a delightful listener ; and , in fact , the hundred and one little amusing things that Donald was continually doing , and that made the days go by in such happy , merry fashion", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 28, "context": "He of the scarred face was quickly captured , being well-nigh winded with the effects of the stone I had thrown ; but the third was a more determined quarry , though , in his stupidity , instead of climbing upwards ( being far lighter than the soldiers , encumbered as they were with breast-pieces and riding-boots ) , he must needs make for the road , where he raced off at breakneck speed", "context_len": 74}}}, "hailed": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 59, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "indignantly": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 58, "context": "To do Peggy justice , I think she was right , for though she delighted in outdoor life , she was in no sense a rough or ill-mannered child , and loved pretty things and dainty ways as well as quieter Lilian ; but it was a case of a dog with a bad name , for however indignantly she might remonstrate , people had got into the habit of dubbing her 'tomboy , ' and at that valuation she seemed likely to remain", "context_len": 84}}}, "passage": {"freq": 144, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 143, "word_index": 9, "context": "[ Footnote 436 : The whole of the foregoing passage is so confused that I think it well to add here ( l ) a literal translation , as I read it : `` So the Vizier , yea , indeed , he marvelled at the greatness of that wealth more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more when he saw the Sultan that he was satisfied with ( or accepted of ) the bride-gift and the dowry ; however , it was not possible to him that he should gainsay the truth and should say to the Sultan , 'He is not worthy ; ' only , he practised with a device upon the Sultan so he should", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Collins persuaded the captain of a New York sloop to give him passage , telling a fantastic yarn about Benjamin being pursued by a young woman who wanted to marry him", "context_len": 31}}}, "innocent": {"freq": 92, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 127, "context": "he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward , [ 660 ] I excuse you , for that ye are innocent", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 55, "context": "Joseph 's tree at Glastonbury , and that he was there on pilgrimage when Alfgar saw him -- saw him , mark you -- at the Danish camp on the borders of Sussex ; and I saw men , I wo n't mention names , who had more than once taken reward to slay the innocent , look as if they would go down on their knees to this holy thorn , which was n't a holy thorn at all , but plucked from some hedge hard at hand", "context_len": 89}}}, "doings": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 72, "context": "At another time nurse would have ordered them to shut their eyes and not speak another word ; but to-night she seemed to think it quite right and natural that they should be so very wide awake , and not only gave them an extra amount of petting and kissing , but told them stories of Uncle Ruthven 's pranks when he was a boy , and of his wonderful sayings and doings , till mamma , coming up and finding this going on , was half inclined to find fault with the old woman herself", "context_len": 96}}}, "papers": {"freq": 152, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 152, "word_index": 86, "context": "`` Uncle John had a picture paper the other day , and in it was a picture of a woman coming in at the door , and she had her hands up so , and she looked as frightened , as frightened , and a man was standing behind the curtain doing so , and under the picture was 'Beware , woman ! ' I asked Uncle John what it meant , and he said that was a wicked woman who was going to steal some papers so she could get some money , and when she came in , she heard somebody say , 'Beware , woman , ' and she was so frightened she ran away and was never seen again", "context_len": 123}}}, "detained": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 5, "context": "Hartley 's , Ted has detained her for a moment , to ask some trifling question , and just as she is about to make a reply , Chris , returning from his daily ride into Nuneham for the mail , swings into the room with his breezy , postman-like air , and empties the contents of the little Hartley mail-bag upon the table", "context_len": 64}}}, "expense": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 52, "context": "You think I have done something unworthy of your god-daughter , but do n't you really , really believe that you would have a much poorer , more contemptible , more worthless sort of god-daughter if she were now to break her bond to the man who saved her mother at considerable expense -- the man who was so good , so kind , so faithful ? Would you really counsel me to break my bond ? ''", "context_len": 78}}}, "chambermaid": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I knocked at your door , but you was n't there , and the chambermaid said you might be in here", "context_len": 22}}}, "villains": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 45, "context": "The whole day long he dwelt in a dream of it that blotted out , or rather consumed with more effulgent brightness , all the other day-dreams he had dreamed before , and his heart almost burst with longing to be a villain like those villains on the stage , to have a moustache -- a black moustache -- such as they wore at a time when every one off the stage was clean shaven , and somehow to end bloodily , murderously , as became a villain", "context_len": 88}}}, "law": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 87, "word_index": 27, "context": "Good Parson Perrient had painted Providence plantation as a sort of paradise , where the liberty and toleration dreamt of by a few in England were the law for all ; but was that refuge open to him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "groaned": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 40, "context": "When I told her we had a three months ' vacation , she never said , `` How delightful ! '' as most girls would , but calmly inquired what I took up in the holidays , and when I groaned at the very thought of taking up anything , she said so seriously , `` But you do n't let your mind lie fallow for three whole months ? '' And then she sighed a little , and added , half to herself , `` Some girls would give all the world for such a chance to read", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "' What a nuisance ! ' groaned Lilian , who was not generally inhospitably disposed", "context_len": 15}}}, "fusses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "He waits and fusses and wastes his time , and then he wonders how he can reach somewhere on time reaching there almost before he starts if he wants to be on time", "context_len": 33}}}, "overtaken": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Come , little girl , '' said Jack , kindly , and 'Tilda Jane , seeing that the inevitable had once more overtaken her , rose resignedly , but the too kind and officious old lady clung to her so wildly that the two men were forced to draw her away from her", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 76, "context": "Indeed , the little queen , whose realm was the hearts of her nearest and dearest , would have been obliged to abdicate , for a while at least , I fancy , had she not chosen before nightfall of that same day to bury her head in the lap of her very most loyal subject , and with tears and sohs confess to her extreme unreasonableness and avow her determination not soon again to be overtaken by such a sorry state of mind and temper", "context_len": 86}}}, "resignedly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Come , little girl , '' said Jack , kindly , and 'Tilda Jane , seeing that the inevitable had once more overtaken her , rose resignedly , but the too kind and officious old lady clung to her so wildly that the two men were forced to draw her away from her", "context_len": 54}}}, "officious": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Come , little girl , '' said Jack , kindly , and 'Tilda Jane , seeing that the inevitable had once more overtaken her , rose resignedly , but the too kind and officious old lady clung to her so wildly that the two men were forced to draw her away from her", "context_len": 54}}}, "complete": {"freq": 123, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 99, "word_index": 110, "context": "the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change of air , ordering him off immediately to the high meadows of the Alps", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 94, "context": "When the Sultan examined it and saw that it was unfinished , he said , `` Woe is me for thee , O casement , that thou art not perfect ! '' Then , turning to the Vizier , he said to him , `` Knowest thou the reason of the lack of completion of this casement and its lattices ? '' `` O [ 512 ] my lord , '' answered the Vizier , `` methinketh it is because Thy Grace hastened upon Alaeddin with the wedding and he had no time to complete it", "context_len": 96}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The tale now presented to the indulgence of the public is the second of a series of tales , each complete in itself , which , as stated in the preface to the first of the series , have been told to the senior boys of a large school , in order to secure their interest in historical characters , and to illustrate great epochs in human affairs by the aid of fiction", "context_len": 73}}}, "totally": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 60, "context": "Yet I scarcely knew precisely what it was that I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether", "context_len": 128}}}, "unmixed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "I very soon found , however , that this reputation was by no means an unmixed blessing ; for the people jumped to the conclusion that if I could cure one disease I could of course cure all ; and I speedily found myself consulted by patients suffering from ailments of which I did not even know the names , and expecting to be cured of them", "context_len": 67}}}, "liking": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 25, "context": "They all stopped and looked at it for a moment , each secretly wishing that somebody else would offer to go first , but nobody liking to make the plunge ; even Rollo stood whimpering and whining on the bank , as if he did not half like the adventure", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 8, "context": "' Being well provided with pocket-money , and liking to do things in style , Archie took reserved seats , and they sat in great state on a front bench covered with faded scarlet cloth , and felt proudly that the man with the drum kept his eye upon them , and the clown turned in their direction when he let off his best jokes", "context_len": 65}}}, "shadow": {"freq": 122, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 117, "word_index": 47, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "A shadow overspread 'Tilda Jane 's face , and seeing it , the boy said , impulsively , `` Stop here a minute -- I want to speak to papa , '' and he rushed away", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "It has n't been found , and what would be more natural than for Professor Long to hire a private detective , and have him shadow us", "context_len": 27}}}, "waitresses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Two trains were standing at the station , and in a large dining-room on her right she saw thronged tables and hurrying waitresses", "context_len": 23}}}, "tearful": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "She was ushered into a room where there was a handsomely dressed woman with a flushed face and tearful eyes , a dejected looking boy and girl sitting very close to each other , a diminutive and poorly dressed German Jew , and a composed looking man sitting behind a small table", "context_len": 52}}}, "dejected": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "But the remains of the revel , crammed generously into her arms , seemed the last straw to poor Peggy , and Father took home such a very limp and dejected young lady that he might well remark it was a good thing fair-day only came once a year , an opinion in which a great many people in Gorswen might feel disposed to agree with him", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 35, "context": "In the original it runs thus , `` Alaeddin 's mother , whenas she took a wont and became every Divan-day going and standing in the Divan before the Sultan , withal that she was dejected , wearying exceedingly , but for Alaeddin 's sake , her son , she used to make light of all weariness", "context_len": 57}}}, "poorly": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 44, "context": "One of his river-faring uncles was visiting with his family at the boy 's home when he laid out the scheme of his great fiction of `` Hamet el Zegri , '' and the kindly young aunt took an interest in it which he poorly rewarded a few months later , when she asked how the story was getting on , and he tried to ignore the whole matter , and showed such mortification at the mention of it that the poor lady was quite bewildered", "context_len": 86}}}, "reassuring": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 112, "context": "The party who had brought me to this remote spot were of an entirely different race from those among whom I now found myself , and the fact that we were making our way toward what was obviously the aristocratic part of the town , coupled with the expressive conversation carried on by the leader of my custodians with three or four individuals who had joined us , led me to surmise -- although of course I did not understand a word of what was said -- that I had been brought up the river as a peace- offering or something of that sort , which conclusion was again the reverse of reassuring", "context_len": 113}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "She said that her mother intended staying all night at the side of her suffering husband and had tried to send some reassuring word to her son", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "Having been sworn , Sleuth cast a reassuring glance in the direction of Ben Stone , who was watching him intently , following which his eyes wandered to Spotty \u2019 s face , who once more suddenly fell to shivering , touched by the chill hand of apprehension and dread", "context_len": 50}}}, "bench": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 59, "context": "The twinkling lights in the harbor were still shining when the little girls went to bed , but before morning a wilder light was blazing from the point where old Cap'n Si 's little house stood , and , the next morning when the children looked across to where yesterday they had seen the old man sitting on the bench outside his door , the smoke curling from the chimney and the flowers in his little garden making a brave showing , they beheld but a heap of blackened ruins", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Next he started to carry outside and pile neatly at the blind end of the building all the boards , boxes and other debris littering up the room , swept it , and selected two packing cases and nailed them up into a convenient impromptu desk , manufactured a bench seat out of some loose boards , set his pen , ink and paper in order , and felt quite ready for business", "context_len": 73}}}, "fashionably": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The fashionably dressed woman was speaking and gesticulating earnestly in front of the man whose face was only a trifle less calm and stony than that of Ruth Ann", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was a soft shimmering silk of light texture , fashionably made and very girlish and simple", "context_len": 17}}}, "gesticulating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The fashionably dressed woman was speaking and gesticulating earnestly in front of the man whose face was only a trifle less calm and stony than that of Ruth Ann", "context_len": 29}}}, "trifle": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 28, "context": "And what hard work it was to struggle with the evil tempers and passions to which she had so long given way ; how difficult , when some trifle vexed her , to keep back the sharp and angry word , to put down the wish to bend everything to her own will , to learn of Him who was meek and lowly in heart !", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 61, "context": "At the same time , my heart , in a way , is still with the little people who count their years by a single numeral ; and so , if you please , I want to take them aside for a moment , and just whisper in their ears that , although A Little Oueen of Hearts may seem a trifle too old for them at first , I have an idea they will not find that fault later on", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "Orton Campbell should have known that it was dangerous to trifle with a man so thoroughly roused as Jones was , but his love of money and dislike to part with it overcame every other consideration , and he said , `` You 've refused my offer , and I have done with you", "context_len": 54}}}, "sealskin": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "Then the young girl rushed upstairs to her dormitory , unlocked her little private drawer , took out her sealskin purse , extracted one of the new half-crowns , and was down again by the little governess cart , whispering eagerly to Mademoiselle Le Brun , within the prescribed time", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Fanning was attired in a huge sealskin cape , which went down below her knees , and a bonnet with a large bird of paradise perched on one side of the brim", "context_len": 32}}}, "dollars": {"freq": 297, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 297, "word_index": 34, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}}}, "orders": {"freq": 232, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 232, "word_index": 72, "context": "This , doubtless , may be so , Sir Gervaise , the doge said , with a slight smile , but it is to the head that plans , rather than to the hand that strikes , that such success as you have achieved is due ; and the credit of this night attack is , as the cavalier Caretto tells me , wholly yours , for until you issued your final orders it seemed to him , and to the two good knights his companions , that there was naught to do but to remain in port and watch this corsair fleet sail away to carry out its work of destruction", "context_len": 112}}}, "jackets": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 20, "context": "The Eton boys in their tall hats ( atoning , as it were , for the extreme briefness of their jackets ) and wide-rolling linen collars were skurrying through the streets as though they had the right of way , as indeed they have in dear old royal Windsor ; and here and there the flowing gown of a colleger spread itself to the April wind and floated out behind , to all appearances as glad as any peacock to show what it could do in that direction", "context_len": 88}}}, "garment": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 54, "context": "Meanwhile , since I was almost naked , and was hoping soon to find myself once more among civilised people , it occurred to me that the skin of the leopard which had wrought this dire tragedy might be of use to me as material out of which to fashion some sort of a garment ; and , therefore , while the flames of the pyre were still blazing brilliantly I utilised their light to enable me to strip the pelt off the great carcase", "context_len": 85}}}, "discussion": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then came a hurried discussion as to the destination of the booty , some , including the parish clerk , Fallowfield , who had gained the upper regions by means of the tackle , urging that it had best be taken and placed in the tower of Worth Church , the others insisting that it would be best to make one journey do , and convey it as close to Wareham as possible , where their accomplices could make arrangements for its distribution", "context_len": 83}}}, "deprive": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "This question reminded him again of his determination to leave Oakdale directly ; and , knowing the good woman had regarded the room as engaged by him for the time of the fall term of school , and also feeling that to leave her thus and so deprive her of the rent money she expected to receive for weeks to come would be a poor return for her kindness , he hesitated in confusion and reluctance to tell her the truth", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I do n't think she means to be , but when she wants to deprive you of good times with other girls , or is jealous of your friendship for them , she is encouraging conceit and selfishness", "context_len": 39}}}, "requires": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 31, "context": "Very little alteration will make them go very well to the air , for I have tried them ; and the repetition of the last words , which your sweet music requires , suits lines the whole emphasis of which falls on the closing words ; at least I fancy so", "context_len": 51}}}, "officials": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 61, "context": "The enormous crowd who had followed us up from the beach were not permitted to enter the palisaded enclosure , which was strictly taboo to the common herd ; our party therefore now consisted solely of those who had brought me up the river , four individuals who had joined us outside the gate -- and whom I took to be officials of some sort -- and my unworthy self ; and , for my own part , I would very willingly have waived the distinction of forming one of the party", "context_len": 92}}}, "enforce": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "Disappointed though they were , the men maintained their cheerfulness , and before the long column was clear of the dock gates they were cheering , laughing , and shouting frantically , despite all attempts on the part of their officers to enforce silence", "context_len": 44}}}, "importation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "It merely requires its custom officials , of whom I am one , to enforce the law which has been made to prevent the importation of sealskin coats free of duty", "context_len": 31}}}, "duty": {"freq": 309, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 309, "word_index": 45, "context": "When she was much younger , extreme carelessness had been her besetting fault , and , as is the manner of this `` little fox , '' had created much trouble for herself and for others ; but having become convinced that it was her duty to cure herself of this , she had set to work to do it in such earnest that that which had been a burden and a care to her was fast becoming a settled habit , and it was but seldom now that any act or word of heedlessness could be laid to her charge , while her ever obliging disposition and loving heart prompted many a deed of kindness which she never failed to carry out if it were in her", "context_len": 128}}}, "sneered": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 23, "context": "Now , sink me ! \u2019 tis a fair exchange : give us the five and take your enterprising \u2019 ( how he sneered when he said this ) \u2019 king \u2019 s men unhurt , or else , for every man of ours who dances at the end of a rope , five of yours shall dangle from our yardarm", "context_len": 61}}}, "frigid": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "He was wet to the skin by the frigid water which made his teeth chatter , and the night had grown so cold that he must do something quickly to save himself from perishing", "context_len": 34}}}, "trousseau": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` I was going to buy my daughter 's trousseau in New York , but I 'll spend every cent at home", "context_len": 22}}}, "cent": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 45, "context": "Schwitz had promised that his charges should be small , but how he never would give in his bill , the policeman and his wife thinking all the while that it was kindness which kept him from doing so ; how it had taken every cent of his salary to pay the other expenses of illness , and keep the family barely warmed and fed ; of the disappointment of their hopes for Willie for , at least , some time to come ; and finally of the terrible bill which Dr", "context_len": 92}}}, "hateful": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 13, "context": "'Oh , no , no ! We ca n't go back over that hateful plank again and sit watching the water come up ! I would rather be drowned here than there ! Oh , Father , Father ! do come and find us ! ' And Bobby sat down upon the ground with such a wail of despair that Peggy at last lost her self-control and found herself joining in his sobs", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "flounced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "That 's the way we will make New York suffer on account of your government being so hateful ! '' and she flounced from the room", "context_len": 26}}}, "remaining": {"freq": 73, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 95, "context": "Such proceedings as these could not of course long exist without the fame of them reaching the ears of the king , and I had only given some three or four performances when I was summoned to entertain his Majesty and his household , which I did in the great square before the palace , my audience numbering quite two thousand ; Banda and his numerous family being seated in a huge semicircle -- of which I was the centre -- in front of the palace , while the rest of the audience filled the remaining portion of the square", "context_len": 100}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "When I gained the gunroom I told the news with unrestrained enthusiasm , and my remaining companions , now reduced to two in number , Greville Drake and Alan Wood , though not slow in offering their congratulations , did not conceal the fact that my good fortune was their disappointment", "context_len": 51}}}, "mournfully": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` But , '' said Bessie , mournfully , as she wiped her eyes , `` we wanted to use it for Willie , and we thought so much about it , and we were so glad when we thought how pleased he would be ! Oh ! we are very much trialed ; are we not , Maggie ? ''", "context_len": 61}}}, "responded": {"freq": 133, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 132, "word_index": 25, "context": "Billy Potter with round eyes stared straight ahead , taking interest only in the passing of an occasional vessel ; Mallie , with drooping head responded yes or no to the questions put to her , and both visitors refused to leave their chairs to go anywhere or see anything", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Had they not so responded , had they insisted on having her with them still wherever they went , Charlotte would have given way", "context_len": 24}}}, "lugubriously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Man and wife , '' responded the boy , lugubriously", "context_len": 11}}}, "assistant": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 82, "context": "the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change of air , ordering him off immediately to the high meadows of the Alps", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "You do n't mean it ! for Bradford was assistant postman on the route that included the Terrace , and Marie-Celeste was naturally quite overwhelmed at the thought that both their men should have won", "context_len": 35}}}, "elevated": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "The only chance they had of bringing me down was by training a piece of ordnance on the top ; but either they did not possess a cannon capable of being elevated to that height , or else they feared that the damage done would be greater than the success of getting rid of me", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 30, "context": "One day , indeed , they were greatly surprised by the descent of a terrific rain storm upon them , for they had never dreamed of encountering rain in that elevated region , where not even a drop of dew was found in the early morning", "context_len": 46}}}, "eyebrows": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 55, "context": "He also brought Ben a cocoanut to keep liquor in ; the end of it , where the eyes are , was made in the shape of a negro \u2019 s face ; the two little round places , where we bore to let the milk out , serving the purpose of eyes , with eyebrows cut over them , and filled with some red matter ; in the mouth was a lead pipe to drink from ; large ears were also made , and a nose ; the figure looked somewhere between a monkey and a negro , funny enough , and was full of rum", "context_len": 107}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "I always said to myself , 'They are all exactly alike -- a pair of eyes , a nose , a mouth , a chin of sorts , eyebrows indifferent or not , hair dark or fair", "context_len": 37}}}, "seventeen": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 12, "context": "She was the older daughter , a strong , healthy girl of seventeen , who never helped her mother about the work , who dressed in such cheap finery as she could obtain by hook or crook , who took music lessons on a rented melodeon paid for out of her mother \u2019 s hard earnings , who felt herself to be a lady unfortunately born out of her sphere , and who was unquestionably ashamed of her surviving parent and her brother and sister", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 15, "context": "Their names were Mabel and Alice Cunningham , two handsome dark-eyed girls , aged respectively seventeen and fifteen ; Florence Aylmer , who was also fifteen and the romp of the school ; Mary Bateman , a stolid-looking girl of fourteen ; Bertha Kennedy , who had only lately been raised to the rank of the Upper school ; Edith King , a handsome , graceful girl , who competed with Mabel for the honors of the head of her class ; and Kitty Sharston , who had only lately come , and who had some Irish blood in her , and was very daring and very much inclined to break the rules", "context_len": 113}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "This reduced the strength on board the galley to seventeen ; but as they would have the assistance of a strong band of Sards they considered this to be ample , under the circumstances", "context_len": 34}}}, "seventy": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 26, "context": "Bradley had paid Mosely in his own coin , and boasted of his prowess even more extravagantly than that braggadocio , claiming to have killed from seventy to eighty men in the course of his experience", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 40, "context": "I think I could be like Peter , and forgive Lem and Dolly seven times ; but every time I do it , it grows harder and harder , and I never could do it by the time it was seventy times seven", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "They dwelt in the middle and southern parts of what is now Mississippi State , where , according to early authors , they had from fifty to seventy villages ; they then extended from the Mississippi to Tombigbee river , and east of it", "context_len": 44}}}, "distracted": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 7, "context": "Her attention , however , was soon distracted , for the little Jew , the instant he was indicated , sprang from his seat , extended both hands , and nimbly skipping over the floor between his numerous bundles , overwhelmed the inspector with a flood of German", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Lady Jane was in the midst of her meditations , and a more confused , distracted poor woman it would be difficult to find in the length and breadth of the land , when suddenly she heard a step in the hall , a frank young voice -- not Irene 's , but bright and young and full of courage -- and the next instant Rosamund Cunliffe entered the room", "context_len": 70}}}, "skipping": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "Wondering what could be coming now , but sure from papa 's face that it was something very pleasant , the little girls went skipping and dancing before him to the library , where , sitting down , papa lifted Bessie to his knee , and Maggie upon the arm of the chair , holding her there with his arm about her waist", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Noel Brooke skilfully evaded the close embrace which would have settled the fight then and there in favor of Ben , and skipping , first to one side , then to the other , rained in a shower of blows upon Ben , one of which took effect in his jaw , and drove him staggering back discomfited", "context_len": 58}}}, "overwhelmed": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 45, "context": "And very attentively everybody listened to the story , as I told it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached", "context_len": 128}}}, "flood": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 58, "context": "The great waves of the ocean rolled up on the beach with a loud , deafening roar , the house rocked with the terrible wind , and the rain poured in such torrents that Maggie asked her mother if she did not think `` the windows of heaven were opened , '' and there was to be another flood", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It \u2019 s flood tide , said John ; we can do both", "context_len": 13}}}, "commanding": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "One of the seamen on the quay hailed her through a speaking-trumpet , and presently a longboat came off for us , manned by ten lusty rowers , while a boy of about my own age sat in the stern-sheets steering the boat and giving orders to the men as if the commanding of the boat had been his life-long business", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 27, "context": "In this publication I call the Maskoki proper by the name of Creeks only , and have used their name on account of the central location and commanding position of the Maskoki proper , to whom this appellation properly belongs , to designate the whole Cha'hta-Maskoki family of Indians", "context_len": 49}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 90, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}}}, "relatives": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The old man paused open-mouthed , and the inspector went on in German : `` You left your home , you crossed the sea , you wish to go to Portland to relatives -- so far , so good , but where are your papers ? ''", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 85, "context": "`` The soul that sinneth it shall die '' -- '' For whosoever shall keep the whole law , and yet offend in one point , he is guilty of all '' -- '' Keep thyself pure '' -- '' For without are dogs , and sorcerers , and murderers , and idolaters , and whosoever loveth and maketh a lie '' -- that meant without the city , the beautiful city of gold where her mother probably was , and many of her unknown relatives , and where all good matrons , orphans , and `` lady-boards '' went", "context_len": 100}}}, "eloquence": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 35, "context": "Mais n \u2019 importe ! the man , the man remains the same ! These locks have proved the keys to a Lady \u2019 s heart e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon", "context_len": 128}}}, "certificate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "The prize itself was to consist of a free education at Cherry Court School for the space of three years ; accompanying it was a certificate in parchment , which in itself was to be considered a very high honor ; and thirdly , a locket set with a beautiful ruby to represent a cherry , which was the badge of the school", "context_len": 63}}}, "reiterated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "But Ki Sing 's broad face seemed utterly void of expression as , neglecting to answer the question , he reiterated his statement , `` Housee long way", "context_len": 28}}}, "writing": {"freq": 182, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 35, "context": "It may be also that , now he was gone for whom and with whom she had written her plays , all zest in that direction was gone with him , and the gift of writing , like a river dammed up in one direction and forced to turn elsewhere , sought naturally a fresh outlet , -- an outlet with which there should be no overpoweringly sad associations", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 117, "word_index": 38, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}}}, "expressive": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 48, "context": "The party who had brought me to this remote spot were of an entirely different race from those among whom I now found myself , and the fact that we were making our way toward what was obviously the aristocratic part of the town , coupled with the expressive conversation carried on by the leader of my custodians with three or four individuals who had joined us , led me to surmise -- although of course I did not understand a word of what was said -- that I had been brought up the river as a peace- offering or something of that sort , which conclusion was again the reverse of reassuring", "context_len": 113}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 57, "context": "Living on grass , and in the woods , with the milk from the house , he had not that protuberance of belly which swine reared in sties possess , and really merited Charlie \u2019 s encomium of being handsome ; he jumped up on his master and rubbed against his legs , with low grunts , expressive of satisfaction", "context_len": 60}}}, "expecting": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 3, "context": "The idea of expecting that a stranger , to whom his only introduction was that of a cabin-boy of the White Star Line , would be likely to take an interest in him to the extent of making him a loan of a large sum of money at rather a low rate of interest ; and then Ted realized what some of us have realized before , that all he had practically to build upon was Marie-Celeste 's remark to Donald , that she felt very sure that the best thing that could happen to this same rich Mr", "context_len": 99}}}, "groans": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 12, "context": "He was prepared to make the most of this , and his groans grew louder as he drew near home in the arms of the boys who took turns , two and two , in carrying him the whole long way from Dayton Lane , with a terrified procession of alternates behind them", "context_len": 53}}}, "lamentations": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "The Christmas holidays being over , and Archie in the rudest of health , Miss Forster had no more excuse for keeping that young gentleman at home , and after much packing and preparation , he departed to school , amid the fond regrets of his aunt and the lamentations of his friends at the Abbey , to whom he promised to write as often as time and the regulations of his house would allow him", "context_len": 76}}}, "waved": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 1, "context": "Gervaise waved his hand , as he neared the end of the harbour , to the officer on the walls , and while the six knights who were with him ran off to tell the master of the galleys to prepare two of them to leave the port instantly , Gervaise explained to the officer in charge of the wall at that point the plan that he was charged to carry out , and asked for twenty knights to assist him", "context_len": 81}}}, "dumbness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "When the door closed , and 'Tilda Jane felt that the cold and scrutinising eyes of the inspector were fixed on her , she was stricken with sudden dumbness", "context_len": 29}}}, "loquacity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The result of their loquacity had been a seeming paralysis of her organs of speech", "context_len": 15}}}, "organs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "The distance from my hut to the palace was but a few yards ; and we quickly arrived at our destination , being at once conducted into the presence of the king , who , stretched upon a couch , and evidently suffering severe pain in his internal organs , was surrounded by the somewhat numerous members of his family", "context_len": 60}}}, "geniality": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Why , sir , '' returned Richard , making an effort to respond to the geniality of the gentleman in waiting , `` I thought you were on the sunny side of the hedge nowadays ? ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "meaning": {"freq": 125, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 102, "word_index": 108, "context": "asked Alaeddin , `` What is the cause that the lattice of yonder kiosk ( kushk ) is not complete ? '' The defective part is soon after referred to , no less than four times , as `` the lattice of the kiosk '' ( sheriyyetu ' l kushk ) , thus showing that , in the writer 's mind , kushk , liwan and shubbak were synonymous terms for the common Arab projecting square-sided window , made of latticework , and I have therefore rendered the three words , when they occur in this sense , by our English `` oriel , '' to whose modern meaning ( a window that juts out , so as to form a small apartment ) , they exactly correspond", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 37, "context": "The following is a literal rendering of the above passage : `` An upper chamber ( keszr ) and ( or ? ) a kiosk ( kushk , a word explained by a modern Syrian dictionary as meaning ' [ a building ] like a balcony projecting from the level of the rest of the house , ' but by others as an isolated building or pavilion erected on the top of a house , i", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "I do but come here by day to attend to the dairy , so '' -- she concluded with a somewhat meaning tone -- '' you can shelter here , to-night , without any one asking whence you come , or whither you go", "context_len": 44}}}, "signify": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "They are the `` masters of ceremonies , '' and the name h\u00edniha , \u00edniha , which is no longer understood by the present generation , is said to signify `` self-adorner , '' in the sense of `` warrior embellished with body paint", "context_len": 44}}}, "negatived": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Her head again negatived this question , and he inquired in Spanish if she knew that tongue", "context_len": 17}}}, "languages": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 55, "context": "She is not the least like my dear mother , and I never loved her , and I know that the best in me will not be brought to the fore if I am with her ; and I sha n't learn those things which would delight dear father ; I shall not know modern languages , nor be a good musical scholar , nor be able to sing nicely , and I -- I shall hate that life , and my nature may be warped , and I -- but , oh ! I will win the Scholarship", "context_len": 99}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "It will be well to remember that in Indian and all illiterate languages the sounds of the same organ-class are interchangeable ; thus , a word may be correctly pronounced and written in six , ten , or twelve different ways", "context_len": 41}}}, "successively": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "These shepherds observed that nearly all the stars were fixed in relation to each other , that is , although they rose successively in the east , and , passing over , set in the west , they did not change in relation to each other", "context_len": 46}}}, "seconds": {"freq": 82, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 79, "word_index": 75, "context": "She did not call to me , or announce her return in any way , but set to work to mow a circle of about ten feet in diameter in the long grass ; and then , having produced fire by rubbing two pieces of wood together , she proceeded very carefully to burn off the short grass left inside the circle , setting fire to it , allowing it to burn for a few seconds , and then beating it out again with a branch , in order that the fire might not spread and burn us out , to say nothing of betraying our presence by the smoke that it would raise", "context_len": 114}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "Ten seconds later he was in the grip of three hulking Saxons , who promptly bound his wrists behind his back and propped him up against the fire-step of the trench", "context_len": 31}}}, "meditative": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "With his usual meditative manner he turned and took his seat again , carefully pulling up his trousers and moving his coat tails as he did so", "context_len": 27}}}, "uncurled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "'Tilda Jane joyfully uncurled her hands from the bundle on her lap", "context_len": 12}}}, "tempted": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 30, "context": "Naturally this called forth inquiry , and it soon became known that Percy had entrusted to Seabrooke 's keeping a large sum of money , lest he should himself be tempted to spend any portion of it , as it was to be reserved for a special purpose ; that Seabrooke before going to the dinner on the previous evening had put it , as he supposed , in a secure place , and that this morning the money was gone , while he had discovered slight but unmistakable evidence that his quarters had been ransacked in search of it", "context_len": 100}}}, "betray": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 64, "context": "It is well said that no man is utterly bad , and perhaps he possessed domestic virtues which were thought unworthy of the attention of the chroniclers ; but as they picture him -- now prompting Ethelred to deeds of treachery against the Danes , now joining those Danes themselves , and surpassing them in cruelty -- now seeking pretended reconciliation , only to betray his foe more surely , and in all this aided and supported by the weak , unprincipled king -- as thus pictured there is scarcely a blacker character in history", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Oh , because , '' said Charlie , thinking how he could best warn Seabrooke and yet not betray Flagg , `` because -- there 's something in your trunk", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "He had sent Doto off in another direction , as the monkey could be of no help to him if it came to a fight , and might by its chattering betray his hiding place to his enemies", "context_len": 38}}}, "accordingly": {"freq": 108, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 108, "word_index": 89, "context": "these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about to work upon him by practice , that he might hinder him from giving his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour to Alaeddin , and accordingly said to him , [ 436 ] `` O my lord , all the treasures of the world were not worth a paring of thy daughter Bedrulbudour 's nails ; indeed , Thy Highness overrateth this upon her", "context_len": 128}}}, "spelled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "Oh , doctor , when I wrote you that letter last summer was n't some words wrongly spelled ? You see I had forgotten some things , and I am learning so much", "context_len": 33}}}, "tips": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 49, "context": "'Just imagine , Paul , ' the letter went on , ' I , of all possible people , a missionary 's wife ! But the fact of the matter is , my precious saint , your splendid , consecrated life made me tingle with shame to my finger tips when I thought of my aimless existence , and when I remembered how you took up your cross and followed your Master to Sleepy Hollow , there seemed to be no reason why I should not follow Him to Africa", "context_len": 90}}}, "peremptory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The object of this peremptory challenge turned , as also did his companion , the terrified Indian -- once more about to be hazed", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The captain and the wireless operator were the last to leave , the latter striving in vain to get the damaged transmitter into working order until peremptory orders from his superior obliged him to desist", "context_len": 35}}}, "taps": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "He heard ghostly footfalls among the ruins and mysterious taps on the stable-window when he was suppering the horses , which , in spite of Lilian 's reassurances , he persisted in regarding as a warning , though for which of his numerous relatives it was intended kept him in a state of perpetual doubt and uneasiness", "context_len": 57}}}, "spirited": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 24, "context": "It was Heaven directed me ; and , oh , your face in church -- so like your mother 's , and withal so spirited and so sweet ! And then I felt that if only my little darling were in any way like you ! I have had sorrow in my life ; but no sorrow has been so great and terrible as that which I have endured with regard to that poor child", "context_len": 75}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "It is tied firmly in a packet within a great leather bag , and , having been just in time to catch the mail-train , is being spirited down to Queenstown , where one of the great White Star steamers has been waiting full four long hours , so important are these reams upon reams of letters we and our English cousins keep sending one to the other across the water", "context_len": 71}}}, "deception": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 8, "context": "Rebellion against lawful authority , rank disobedience and deception were to be laid at Percy 's door , not to speak of the pitiable weakness which had suffered him to be led into this wrong , and the enormity of his at least passive acquiescence when Flagg had stolen Seabrooke 's letter ; still worse his own destruction of it , almost involuntary though it was", "context_len": 66}}}, "practised": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 78, "context": "was being taken for granted , will you please tell me what is a tilt-yard ? and what it was dat de knignts would not tome to ? and what was dat little white ting of the Queen 's dat de King carried ? and impatiently as Marie-Celeste brooked the interruption , there was nothing for it but to wait while Her Majesty explained that the tilt-yard was a sort of riding-school for the knights , where they practised for the tournaments , and that the tournaments were occasions when the knights , spear in hand , came together to ride against each other , with a great many people looking on , and when the one who unseated all those who rode against him won the prize", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "A love for music was , beside her affection for Lem , the one soft spot in poor Dolly 's sinful , hardened heart ; but the practised voices of the two ladies had not half the charm for her of the simple , childish tones which had first sung to her the hymn which had taken such hold upon her fancy , or rather on her heart", "context_len": 68}}}, "callous": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "She had also ceased to worry about the board of lady managers , and in her present comfortable , callous state of mind she reflected that she might stay here a year , and they would never think of looking for her in a railway station", "context_len": 46}}}, "hummed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 69, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}}}, "strolled": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 35, "context": "But Dolly turned sulky in her turn , and refused to say a word more ; and Lem , knowing it was useless to try to make her speak when she did not choose , strolled into the woods to see if he could find any berries for his breakfast ; while she , still shivering from her night 's exposure in the Ice Glen , tried to kindle a fire from the wet sticks which lay around ; and finding this in vain , crept to her wretched bed , and tried to warm herself there", "context_len": 97}}}, "shunting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "She was lost to them , and she gaily hummed a tune as she strolled to and fro on the big wooden platform , watching the shunting engines , the busy custom-house officers , and the station yard employees , who were cleaning , rubbing , scouring , and preparing cars for further journeys", "context_len": 54}}}, "employees": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "She was lost to them , and she gaily hummed a tune as she strolled to and fro on the big wooden platform , watching the shunting engines , the busy custom-house officers , and the station yard employees , who were cleaning , rubbing , scouring , and preparing cars for further journeys", "context_len": 54}}}, "scouring": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "She was lost to them , and she gaily hummed a tune as she strolled to and fro on the big wooden platform , watching the shunting engines , the busy custom-house officers , and the station yard employees , who were cleaning , rubbing , scouring , and preparing cars for further journeys", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "There was a stout , rather vacant looking German girl , a good worker who delighted in scrubbing and scouring and who would make an excellent kitchen maid", "context_len": 28}}}, "journeys": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 17, "context": "In another month I shall have been two years in India , -- only two months , journeys included , spent in the Hills ; all the remaining twenty-two in the Plains , with one peculiarly unhealthy season , and another of unusually prolonged heat ; -- and yet I am just as strong and well as if I had been just sauntering about an English garden all the time", "context_len": 70}}}, "stifle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "At twelve o'clock , just as she was beginning to stifle yawns , and gaze wistfully at the windows of the dining-room , a young girl in a white apron came and stood in the doorway , and , shading her eyes from the sun shining in such dazzling brightness on the snow , beckoned vigorously to 'Tilda Jane", "context_len": 59}}}, "yawns": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "At twelve o'clock , just as she was beginning to stifle yawns , and gaze wistfully at the windows of the dining-room , a young girl in a white apron came and stood in the doorway , and , shading her eyes from the sun shining in such dazzling brightness on the snow , beckoned vigorously to 'Tilda Jane", "context_len": 59}}}, "apron": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 32, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "apron curly lights bunch about smelled could matches cooking", "context_len": 9}}}, "dazzling": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 48, "context": "At twelve o'clock , just as she was beginning to stifle yawns , and gaze wistfully at the windows of the dining-room , a young girl in a white apron came and stood in the doorway , and , shading her eyes from the sun shining in such dazzling brightness on the snow , beckoned vigorously to 'Tilda Jane", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "On and on the riders travelled , through the exhilarating autumn air , until they stopped for lunch on the borders of a forest which Jack Frost had set ablaze , and which glowed in the sunshine with a dazzling splendour of crimson and bronze and gold", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "The only trouble was that the tall , sedate-looking officer of the day , in his spotless uniform of gray and white and gold with a dazzling red sash thrown in , strode out of the guardhouse just then ; a moment later came the cry , `` New cadets turn out ! '' and Indian drew a breath of relief at being delivered from his uncomfortable situation", "context_len": 68}}}, "brightness": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 61, "context": "There had been quite a spell of sickness in Gorswen at the end of the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Another was spent at New Windsor where the suffering from the cold had been intense , but there was brightness that day because of the arrival of a great wagon filled with shirts and warm clothing for Washington and his men", "context_len": 41}}}, "beckoned": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 42, "context": "On the third evening , after the allowance of broth and bread had been consumed , and the door was closed and locked upon them for the night , three or four of the galley slaves , after talking eagerly together , beckoned to the others to gather round them at the end of the room farthest from the door", "context_len": 60}}}, "compassionately": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Poor little soul -- she 's deef and dumb , '' said the dining-room girl , compassionately , as she passed a group of men in the hall", "context_len": 29}}}, "hall": {"freq": 290, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 290, "word_index": 39, "context": "in the face of the carelessness with which the text treats kushk as synonymous now with keszr or teyyareh and now with liwan or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this", "context_len": 128}}}, "conscience": {"freq": 76, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 49, "context": "From Norfolk Island , in 1870 , he wrote to his sister when he was holding an ordination : `` At such times as these , when one is specially engaged in solemn work , there is much heart searching ; and I can not tell you how my conscience accuses me of such systematic selfishness during many long years -- I mean I see how I was all along making self the centre , and neglecting all kinds of duties -- social and others -- in consequence ''", "context_len": 89}}}, "troublesomely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "'Tilda Jane did not speak or smile , nor did her conscience , often so troublesomely sensitive , now give one reproving twinge", "context_len": 23}}}, "sensitive": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 74, "context": "You won \u2019 t be too harsh with him , Father ? You won \u2019 t allow him to suffer too much ? If he don \u2019 t come back soon , you \u2019 ll go and find him , won \u2019 t you , -- if he don \u2019 t come back by the end of next week ? He isn \u2019 t strong , you know , and he \u2019 s so sensitive", "context_len": 75}}}, "reproving": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "'Tilda Jane did not speak or smile , nor did her conscience , often so troublesomely sensitive , now give one reproving twinge", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "Even Donald stared at Marie-Celeste in grieved and reproving wonder , and yet to all appearances it was all for Donald 's sake , this defiant , protesting attitude of hers , and Donald knew it", "context_len": 36}}}, "twinge": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 14, "context": "With an effort he regained his feet , clenched his teeth as a sharp twinge shot through his frame , and again pushed onwards", "context_len": 24}}}, "officially": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "She had been officially proclaimed so , and in meek patience she seated herself at the table , calmly pointed to what she wished , and , being most tenderly and assiduously waited upon by the pitying girl , ate a large and excellent dinner", "context_len": 45}}}, "proclaimed": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 26, "context": "Then , while the air vibrated with the hum and murmur of many excited tongues , shouts and a disturbance in the direction of the palace proclaimed the approach of the king and his household , and presently the entire party , numbering some three hundred , passed in and made their way to a kind of grand- stand , from which an admirable view of all the proceedings was to be obtained", "context_len": 73}}}, "tenderly": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` Alas , alas ! and did I not kneel and entreat my dearest husband to heed the words of those good men if he would not mine ? How happy we might have been , even in a hut among the savages ! And you , too , Dick , '' she said tenderly , `` you would have liked well to follow Master Perrient 's leading ; and my dear husband was ready to have you go , seeing all he and Sir Gyles Perrient had set their minds upon for your happiness", "context_len": 95}}}, "assiduously": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 51, "context": "I am sure , comrades , that there are few , if any , among us who would not infinitely prefer fighting the Moslems to spending our whole time in learning their language ; and I for one consider the fact that he has for nine months laboured so incessantly and assiduously that he has come , as Boswell says , to speak it like a native , is even more to his credit than the deed for which he was knighted", "context_len": 82}}}, "pitying": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "She had been officially proclaimed so , and in meek patience she seated herself at the table , calmly pointed to what she wished , and , being most tenderly and assiduously waited upon by the pitying girl , ate a large and excellent dinner", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Delay youself yet a leetle , '' urged the woman , and her pitying eyes ran over the girl 's drooping figure", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "Dolly had heard of God before , but not as the kind , loving Father , -- the merciful , gracious Saviour , -- who stands ready to receive all who will turn to Him , who comes after us when we go from Him , and who had now put out His pitying hand to draw to Himself this poor little stricken lamb who had wandered so far from his fold", "context_len": 72}}}, "chocolate": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 20, "context": "At the orphan asylum there had never been fare such as this , and , after she had finished her chocolate pudding , and put in her pocket a juicy orange that she could not possibly eat , she bowed her head , and internally and thankfully repeated the orphanage grace after meat", "context_len": 53}}}, "juicy": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 57, "context": "We simply broke open the lumps of baked clay with our spears and took out the birds -- minus the skin and feathers , which adhered to the clay -- and , splitting them open , removed the interior organs and devoured the flesh , which I found done to a turn , and particularly rich and juicy in flavour", "context_len": 60}}}, "orange": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 12, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Cousin Black Maple is almost exactly like us , but its branches are orange colored and its keys \u2019 spread more widely than ours do", "context_len": 25}}}, "bowed": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 4, "context": "The slender , slightly bowed figure in the velvet coat and blue ribbon , with soft curls that flowed from beneath a plumed hat , the sad eyes , the regular features only marred by a look of weakness and almost peevishness about the mouth ; the boy had seen them all often enough in pictures , but to-day he stood for the first time in the presence of a king , of King Charles the First of England", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Like a dart Panjie dived betwixt the bowed legs of the accountant officer , and , in blind desperation , butted the senior officer of the engineering branch full in the chest", "context_len": 32}}}, "internally": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "At the orphan asylum there had never been fare such as this , and , after she had finished her chocolate pudding , and put in her pocket a juicy orange that she could not possibly eat , she bowed her head , and internally and thankfully repeated the orphanage grace after meat", "context_len": 53}}}, "admiring": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "The plucky animal let himself out immediately and the admiring Alden still allowed the reins to lie on his neck", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 54, "context": "To be sure , the perspective of her cottages was apt to be rather peculiar at times , and the Welsh mountains turned out a more vivid shade of purple , and the fields a far more brilliant green than Nature ever painted them ; but it was all good practice , and the admiring Peggy thought that no Royal Academician could have produced such charming masterpieces", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Well , you are a hero ! was the admiring comment", "context_len": 11}}}, "travel": {"freq": 101, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 51, "context": "Peggy grew up without many of the luxuries which fall to the lot of richer girls , but she was none the worse for that , and I think , when she saw how bored and uninterested Maud Middleton was in the midst of her round of London society and foreign travel , she felt that it is sometimes better to have the capacity for enjoyment than to have the means without the happy talent of being able to appreciate your good things", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 92, "context": "Had Ben set the fire at first along the whole line of the brush , there would have been burnt ground between him and the mass of fire , which would have cut off the communication , and he would have been safe ; but he set it on one corner , and when the wind shifted , the flame driven by it dried the moisture from the grass , and made rapid progress towards the house , while a large strip of dry grass made a bridge for the fire to travel on", "context_len": 94}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` That will doubtless be much to the advantage of Keith 's manners , '' laughed the other , `` but scarce to the furtherance of your safety ! Now , I ride to Harrow to-night -- why will you not bear me company and lie at my house , and so travel into the country for a while", "context_len": 59}}}, "obstinate": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 65, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 6, "context": "The defence , though even more obstinate and desperate than the first , was attended with less success , for after inflicting enormous losses upon the great army , commanded by the Sultan Solyman himself , the town was forced to yield ; for although the Grand Master L'Isle Adam , and most of his knights , would have preferred to bury themselves beneath the ruins rather than yield , they were deterred from doing so , by the knowledge that it would have entailed the massacre of the whole of the inhabitants , who had throughout the siege fought valiantly in the defence of the town", "context_len": 107}}}, "satisfy": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 117, "context": "not to let our left hand , know that which is done by our right hand ; but are we not also commanded so to let our light shine before men that they may see our good works and glorify our Father in heaven ? And can we do so , or truly show our love to him , if we hide the services rendered for his sake behind a mask of coldness and reserve ? My dear aunt , for his sake , for your own , for the sake of the affection and confidence which I wish my children to feel for you , and which I believe you wish to gain , let me satisfy them that it was really you who did this thing", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Harwood used both freely , being anxious to gain the camp and satisfy himself of the safety of his family", "context_len": 20}}}, "demands": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "I wish he were more like Lane \u2019 s boy I don \u2019 t like him , for he is obstinate and headstrong , and utterly unreasonable in his demands upon me , but he is steady and correct in his habits , and if he were in Victor \u2019 s place would never give me any uneasiness", "context_len": 58}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` I owe a duty to my King ; and that duty imperatively demands that I shall return at once to the ship in which I am serving him -- and where , Ama , I may mention , no place could possibly be found for you", "context_len": 47}}}, "appetite": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` I did n't want to be settin ' myself up , but I was n't goin ' to have her take no false notions about me , so I says , 'Yes , ma'am , but when a body 's sick , and ai n't no appetite to eat only what one forces one 's self to , I do n't think it no sin to spend a bit for a nice nourishin ' mouthful", "context_len": 76}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "I observed that ye eat as much as me and young Payne together ; don \u2019 t seem to affect yer appetite any", "context_len": 23}}}, "apartment": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 122, "context": "asked Alaeddin , `` What is the cause that the lattice of yonder kiosk ( kushk ) is not complete ? '' The defective part is soon after referred to , no less than four times , as `` the lattice of the kiosk '' ( sheriyyetu ' l kushk ) , thus showing that , in the writer 's mind , kushk , liwan and shubbak were synonymous terms for the common Arab projecting square-sided window , made of latticework , and I have therefore rendered the three words , when they occur in this sense , by our English `` oriel , '' to whose modern meaning ( a window that juts out , so as to form a small apartment ) , they exactly correspond", "context_len": 128}}}, "slumber": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 41, "context": "He felt better after this and went in ; but that night the excitement of the adventure and the effects of his immersion were sufficient to keep him awake hour after hour , while when he dropped off into an uneasy slumber it was for his mind to be haunted by dreams in which he was being dragged down into the depths of the sea by a strange monster that clung to his limbs and writhed about him , making him shudder as he felt the chilling embrace", "context_len": 88}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "Sleep refused to come , and she lay awake hour after hour , imagining every sound to be a burglar breaking into the premises , and wondering how Peggy could slumber so peacefully in the other little white bed", "context_len": 39}}}, "guests": {"freq": 123, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 122, "word_index": 74, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was speedily noised about in the mining-camp that a party of horse-thieves had had the audacity to visit the settlement , and were even now guests of the Golden Gulch Hotel", "context_len": 32}}}, "tipped": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 19, "context": "That evening , when some of the custom-house officials and some of the guests of the hotel were sitting tipped back in chairs in the smoking-room , the assistant inspector said to the inspector , who had just come in , `` I could n't make anything of your deaf and dumb kid , Jack", "context_len": 55}}}, "cigar": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Worse ! I wonder what sort of dope they put in those cigarettes ? I like a good pipe or a cigar , but I 'm blessed if I can go those coffin nails ! Ah , that air smells good , '' and he breathed in deep of the September air at the window", "context_len": 56}}}, "limber": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "They went up to the Basin bank and got lots of salt out of the holes in the barrels lying there , and then they ate all the apples they could hold , and after that they cut limber sticks off the trees , and sharpened the points , and stuck apples on them and threw them", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Then you gasped and limped round , with your feet like fire , till you could get out and limber your boots up in some water somewhere", "context_len": 27}}}, "swan": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Well , I swan ! '' said the assistant inspector , blankly , and , as he spoke , he brought his chair down on its four legs , and gazed about the room with an expression of such utter helplessness that the other men broke into a roar of laughter", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "The angry , surprised swan dived his head down in search of him ; but the current , which swept round here with some force , carried Jumbo away , and finally flung him , a bedraggled and most unhappy-looking rabbit , on to a corner of the island", "context_len": 49}}}, "blankly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Well , I swan ! '' said the assistant inspector , blankly , and , as he spoke , he brought his chair down on its four legs , and gazed about the room with an expression of such utter helplessness that the other men broke into a roar of laughter", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "It is not remarkable that he hesitated over this , or that as the shadows once more thickened in that room he sat for a long time on his trunk , his elbows on his knees and his chin on his hands , gazing blankly at the one leaden window", "context_len": 50}}}, "helplessness": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 47, "context": "The cross old man would have given the world to have turned her out of his house at that very minute , but his night of sleeplessness and raging temper had given him a fierce headache , a bad taste in his mouth , and such a helplessness of limbs that he could not turn in bed", "context_len": 57}}}, "coon": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 68, "context": "The boys hunted rats with him at night , when they came out of the stables that backed down to the Hydraulic , for water ; and a dog who liked above all things to lie asleep on the back-step , by day , and would no more think of chasing a pig out of the garden than he would think of sitting up all night with a coon , would get frantic about rats , and would perfectly wear himself out hunting them on land and in the water , and keep on after the boys themselves were tired", "context_len": 100}}}, "fooled": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 33, "context": "If I had the chance I \u2019 d shoot that confounded \u2019 Ceph ! he exclaimed ; if a horse knew how to grin , how he is grinning over the youngster he fooled ! Well , I shan \u2019 t bother with you , that \u2019 s certain", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Here them ole cadets went an ' fooled Mark Mallory again , an ' you -- bah ! ''", "context_len": 20}}}, "tipping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Do you suppose the slyboots has gone to bed ? '' asked Blakeman , again tipping back his chair , and returning to his professional manner", "context_len": 27}}}, "professional": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 51, "context": "All this while poor wayward Ted , whose name you must have guessed almost from the first , was lying wholly oblivious to everything about him , muttering now and then a few delirious , incoherent words , and yet by degrees subsiding into a gentle , regular breathing that the professional ear was quick to detect , and that was full of good omen for the waking in the morning", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "And so the tour of the chapel was at last made ; and although his little guide had omitted much historical detail that the professional would have furnished , she had put in with telling force many little points of her own", "context_len": 42}}}, "cash": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` Are you going to have the bed on which your mother sleeps sold under her , and she dying , or are you not ? I can help you , I have plenty of money , I have a lot of loose cash in the bank which may as well go in your direction as any other", "context_len": 58}}}, "consented": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 27, "context": "Some hours later the captain returned , and upon my repeating to him the yarn which I had spun to the mate he not only very readily consented to my working my passage , but also offered me two excellent suits of clothes , two shirts , two pairs of stockings , a pair of shoes , and a worsted cap in exchange for my leopard-skin , which offer I gladly accepted ; and that night found me domiciled in the forecastle of L'Esperance as one of her crew", "context_len": 89}}}, "questioned": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 14, "context": "Had any other child friend along his route , no matter how friendly , questioned him day after day as to how he was getting on with his training for the race ? Had any other among them promised to be on hand at the latest delivery on the afternoon succeeding it , so as to learn just what the issue had been , and at a time when he would be able to stop and tell about it ? Would any one else in the world have thought of suggesting that he should give three short little whistles when he reached the Browns ' , in Remsen Street , so that she should know just how near he was ? Surely no one ; and it was", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Because I am compelled to , explained the questioned lad , still resolutely keeping his emotion in check", "context_len": 18}}}, "tribunal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "She had no doubt that this was some special tribunal called together to deliberate upon her case", "context_len": 17}}}, "deliberate": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 15, "context": "All day she loitered about the station platform watching the trains come in , -- deliberate freight-trains , with their loads of merchandise , all to be examined by the busy customs officials , and rushing express trains , with their hundreds of hungry passengers who swept in crowds into the spacious dining-room", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "With a crestfallen air John Richmond had to confess himself defeated in his mean attempt at swindling , for he had obtained Uncle Job 's indorsement with the deliberate intention of leaving him to pay the note , supposing that the old man would be too timid to do anything about it", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "Leacraft and every one else who heard the tale , gave him credit only for the deliberate theft of Percy 's money and then of the effort to throw it upon Seabrooke , either as an act of revenge or else because he feared that it would be found in his possession", "context_len": 52}}}, "articulated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "She opened her mouth once or twice , tried to speak , failed , and at last articulated with difficulty , and with an air of genuine surprise , `` Why -- ai n't I deef an ' dumb ? I ai n't spoke ever since he made me think so till now , '' and she nodded toward the assistant inspector", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Then in an instant the whole scene of the tragedy in the pine-shrouded lane burst upon me in all its horror , and I raised myself on one elbow and feebly articulated : `` Father , say it is but a dream ! ''", "context_len": 44}}}, "genuine": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 9, "context": "Joe , whose sympathy could not have been more genuine if Peggy had lost a parent , hovered about all day , trying to console the bereaved pair , with small success , till towards evening a sudden flash of genius inspired him to suggest a funeral , perhaps his village experience teaching him that the bustle and preparation necessary for such a ceremony was the best safety-valve to work off sorrow", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Milfort 's `` History of the Moskoquis , '' as given above in an extract , is a singular mixture of recent fabrications and distortions of real historic events , with some points traceable to genuine aboriginal folklore", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` But can you trust that man ? Is the letter genuine ? '' asked Mrs", "context_len": 16}}}, "irritably": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Shut up ! '' said his father , irritably , and in profound silence the three proceeded through the wood in single file , following the dog who , without excitement , but with his dark face beaming with pleasure at being understood , rapidly led them over his own tracks of a few minutes previous", "context_len": 57}}}, "lingering": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 64, "context": "the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "With a last lingering look at the beautiful cascade and a mental resolve to return to it soon , he struck off at a tangent into the jungle , gliding along silently and swiftly , eager to make up for the time he had spent in dreaming", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Before him the alternatives were sharply defined : Apostasy , and his ancestral honours , with all that the sword of the conqueror could give ; and on the other hand , the martyr 's lingering agony , but the hope of everlasting life after death", "context_len": 46}}}, "syllables": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 4, "context": "He pounded out the syllables under Bart 's very nose with resounding thumps , bringing down his fist on the impromptu office desk so forcibly that the concussion disturbed the papers on it , and several sheets fell fluttering to the floor", "context_len": 42}}}, "chock": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "These sand-birds feed on the shore till they are chock brimful , and the tide comes and drives them off ; then they fly on to these ledges ; but they are as afraid of getting wet as a cat ; and when the tide comes up around the rock , they huddle together to keep out of the water , till they are all in a bunch , and the rock looks blue with them ; it \u2019 s the greatest chance for a shot ; but , continued he , after a pause , perhaps Mrs", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 61, "context": "If so be you laid hands if it were but on a hen , if you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old", "context_len": 128}}}, "quit": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 85, "context": "There 's glass dishes on it , with oranges , an ' bananas , an ' cakes an ' pies , an ' sangwiches , an ' a funny machine where you drop a nickel in a crack , an ' if the hand points to five , or ten or fifteen , you get twenty-five cents ' worth of candy , an ' if you do n't get candy you get good advice like as , 'You 've been keepin ' bad comp'ny , quit it or you will never prosper , ' or 'You 've run away from home , an ' the perlice is on your track , ' or 'Smokin is a bad thing for your health", "context_len": 121}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "mystified": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "The pony would have gone faster had his master permitted , and why he did not was altogether beyond the understanding of the mystified lad", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 15, "context": "That was very kind of the Queen , said Marie-Celeste gratefully , while Albert looked mystified , for he was not at all aware of the Queen 's having had any part in the transaction ; but he thought it was a good time to gain a little useful information", "context_len": 50}}}, "prided": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 36, "context": "Bradford might oppose it , and fearing the strength of her own resolution , she did not care to have it shaken by any persuasions to the contrary , and partly because she had always rather prided herself on carrying out her own plans without help or advice from others", "context_len": 50}}}, "distinguishing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "While it did not seem to them that there was any chance of his distinguishing himself , they perceived , as they thought it over , the considerable danger there was of capture by pirates , and Ralph and some of his companions came down to the mole to see him off , with feelings in which envy bore no part whatever", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "Caretto , who had been appointed grand prior of Italy , had brought the knights from all the commanderies in the northern republics to do honour to the occasion , and the whole , in their rich armour and the mantles of the Order , made a distinguishing feature in the scene", "context_len": 52}}}, "nationalities": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "Among the various nationalities of the Gulf territories the Maskoki family of tribes occupied a central and commanding position", "context_len": 19}}}, "accent": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 13, "context": "Whether intentionally or not , the man had slipped back into his Staffordshire accent and dropped the strongest of his oaths , and Dick could not prevent a feeling of bitter amusement at seeing that this drunken ne'er-do-well , whom his uncle had persuaded to enlist in the hopes of drilling him into a decent life , had yielded to the influence of General Harrison 's character just as he himself had done", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "I remarked that he spoke with an accent totally different from the Southern dialect of our part of Hampshire and Sussex , but my doubts were soon set at rest", "context_len": 30}}}, "thinks": {"freq": 179, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 164, "word_index": 75, "context": "Well , this letter is just to tell you what I told you once before , you remember , and that is , that you are my good little angel , no matter how bad you may have been for three whole days together , and to ask you not to forget that there is rather a lonely fellow here at Windsor , who hopes you are having a good time , but who honestly thinks that the sooner you come home the better", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 0, "context": "Thinks I , the Lord forgive me , and grant me such a share of trust and patience as is given to this his little one ; and then I took heart , and I do n't think I 've lost it again , if I have had a hard blow I did not look for", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Ah , so thinks some one else", "context_len": 7}}}, "sentiment": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 34, "context": "I do not for a moment deceive myself with the thought that , beyond the fact that her gift has made her feel an interest in me and my fate , she has any sentiment in the matter : probably , indeed , she looks back upon the gift as a foolish act of girlish enthusiasm that led her into making a promise that she now can not but find unpleasantly binding ; for it is but natural that among the young nobles of her own rank and country there must be some whom she would see with pleasure wearing her colours", "context_len": 102}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Now , continued Uncle Isaac ( who , though one of the kindest-hearted men alive , was but little given to sentiment , and entirely practical in all his views ) , we can do no more good here ; let us bring the furniture into the house for Ben , and then I want to finish that burn ; we \u2019 ll set it on fire at the other end ; it will be fun to see it come down before the wind", "context_len": 84}}}, "murmur": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 29, "context": "There was no sound of murmuring waves on the beach , no Jennie in the next room , and no Dorothy as bed-fellow , but instead there was the murmur of leaves making a pleasant song , there was Celia playing softly on the piano , and best of all there was mother very near ; so Edna turned over with a sigh of content , glad that she was in her own home", "context_len": 74}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "I have come here to ask you one or two questions , and to tell you one or two things , and my first question is this -- Why were you sighing when I came in , and why did you murmur to yourself , 'What if it all turns out a mistake ? ' Will you explain those words , Jane", "context_len": 62}}}, "retract": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The sentiment of the meeting was against him , and a low murmur warned him to retract what he had said", "context_len": 21}}}, "sulkily": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "The tea-things were hastily collected from the stream , and the amateur anglers set to work with much enthusiasm but no success , for the fish absolutely declined to bite , in spite of the tempting bait , and lay sulkily under the stones at the bottom of the pool", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Never had no schoolin ' , '' said Lem , a little sulkily", "context_len": 14}}}, "guardians": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 60, "context": "Never had a night seemed so long ; the only one who thoroughly enjoyed himself was the puppy , who , delighted to receive so much attention , pursued his diversions until the sun was well in at the window , when at length he snuggled into Peggy 's pillow , and composed himself to sleep , leaving his weary guardians only time for a brief rest before Nancy 's unwelcome tap was heard at the door", "context_len": 77}}}, "assent": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 14, "context": "Belden to do aught but look and listen , and drop a word of assent now and then , when Marie-Celeste saw fit to impart her information in a somewhat interrogative form ; and in this way they went on from monument to monument , giving of course but a passing glance to many and stopping longest , by tacit agreement , at those which had some special charm or attraction for Marie-Celeste", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` here an idiomatic form of assent expressing condescension on the part of a superior", "context_len": 15}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` It gives me pleasure to assent cordially to the description you give of yourselves", "context_len": 15}}}, "flush": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 30, "context": "Two young theows , whose fathers had gone to the war , but who had been left behind as being too young to share its dangers , although in the flush of early youth , accompanied them , and were soon loaded with the lighter game their masters had killed , while a deer they had slain was hung in the trees , where a wolf could not reach it , and where wayfarers were not likely to pass until the sportsmen should return for their own", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "What he saw caused a fierce look to come to his face and his freckled cheeks to flush ; for in the midst of the group was Hunk Rollins , a look of vicious pleasure on his face , holding little Jimmy Jones by the ear , which he was twisting with brutal pleasure , showing his ugly teeth as he laughed at the tortured lad \u2019 s cries and pleadings", "context_len": 71}}}, "profound": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 23, "context": "Se looks jus ' as Marie-Celeste said , remarked Albert proudly , his sceptical spirit of the morning wholly transformed into one of profound admiration ; and Marie-Celeste , asking that she might hold the case in her own hand , and gazing entranced upon the dear little face looking out at her , said joyfully , Yes , she does look as I said , does n't she ? Then she reverently laid the miniature back upon the Queen 's lap , as though counting it quite too precious to be long out of royal keeping", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 35, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}}}, "convulsion": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Oh ! what hopes he had raised , and in an instant dashed to the ground , and checking the convulsion in her throat , she stealthily wiped away the two tears of distress coursing down her thin cheeks", "context_len": 39}}}, "stealthily": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 2, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Consequently Alden pushed on stealthily and slowly", "context_len": 7}}}, "coursing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Then an immense fellow who seemed the leader of the herd , began to bellow , and tear up the earth with his hoofs , and in a moment , the whole herd were coursing over the prairie at a pace , which Guy , when looking at their heavy bodies , had never imagined them capable of", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Suddenly he caught sight of a buck coursing in front", "context_len": 10}}}, "humbly": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 41, "context": "I would also submit that it were well to pick men with some strength for this labour , for many are so weak that they are well nigh useless in the moving of heavy weights ; and lastly , I would humbly submit to you that if men are to do good work they must be fed", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Yet the church is the fold of the Good Shepherd , and there is salvation therein for all who love and serve their Lord , and strive humbly to follow His example , and those of His blessed Saints", "context_len": 39}}}, "drug": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 13, "context": "He had been exasperated to a great degree by Flagg 's endeavor to drug him on the night of the expedition to Rice 's , and that with good reason ; and now his suspicions , nay , more than suspicions aroused that he was trying to make it appear that he , Seabrooke , had wrongfully kept Percy 's money and then pretended that the latter had taken it from him by stealth , enraged him beyond bounds", "context_len": 79}}}, "growled": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 39, "context": "Ah , now , I have had a fine meal , a luxurious meal , fit for a king no , fit for a bear ! And the bear rubbed his front paw over his well-filled tummy and again growled delightedly to himself some more , saying over and over again : Delicious ; such a meal !", "context_len": 58}}}, "express": {"freq": 291, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 201, "word_index": 59, "context": "Two sets of mail bags were approaching each other from points two thousand miles apart , and there were times when this approach was at the astounding speed of forty and even fifty miles an hour ! The average daily rate was two hundred and fifty miles a day , but where everything was favorable , or when an express rider was fleeing from the vengeful red men , his pony struck a gait of twenty-five miles and maintained it , when an untrained horse would have dropped in his tracks", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 63, "context": "The farmer with the plug hat he-hawed at the top of his voice , the miserable owner of the eggs got mad at him , some words ensued , the farmer started after him , the egg owner ran , once outside fired an egg which struck the smooth , shiny tile with a splatter , and the farmer came back into the express office holding his nose , bareheaded , and looking for his rejected straw head-covering", "context_len": 78}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 54, "context": "It is not to be supposed that I was allowed to spin my yarn without interruption ; on the contrary , I was bombarded with a continuous fire of questions for the elucidation of points that I had failed to make quite clear ; and when I had finished the captain was pleased to express himself as perfectly satisfied with all that I had done , and that the loss of the Dolphin was due to causes entirely beyond my control", "context_len": 81}}}, "obstacle": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` For hundreds upon hundreds of years the winds and tides have combined to rid themselves of this obstacle to their progress , the winds urging the waves that come rolling in from the vast Atlantic , gathering force as they increase in speed , like one rushing at a leap ; and at last leap they do , upon the great black mass of shale , tons upon tons in weight , seeming as if they would sweep it clear away , and rush on in mad ruin to tumble the fishing luggers together and shatter them like eggs as they lie softly rubbing together in the harbour", "context_len": 109}}}, "worrying": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 12, "context": "He ran after the children with little short , sharp barks , worrying at their heels till Aunt Helen declared there would soon not be a whole stocking left in the establishment ; he had a fray at once with Jack , but was much worsted by that worthy , returning to Peggy for protection with his scrap of a tail between his legs ; he tore Bobby 's cricket-ball to pieces , licked the polish off Father 's boots , devoured Aunt Helen 's goloshes , and nestled cosily down to sleep on the top of Nancy 's best Sunday hat , which that luckless damsel had imprudently left in an open band-box on the kitchen settle", "context_len": 118}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "She counted and recounted the linen and the silver , and sat worrying over her account-books at night till Father threatened to burn them", "context_len": 24}}}, "submissively": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` In a minute , '' said 'Tilda Jane , submissively , while she made a queer bob of a curtsey to all present", "context_len": 24}}}, "bob": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "He carefully lifted Gippie to a resting-place by the kitchen stove , untied 'Tilda Jane 's snow-shoes and strapped them on his back , stroked Poacher repeatedly , and finally with a hearty `` So long , little gal , let 's hear from you , '' he made her an awkward bob of his head and ran after his brother , who had reached the road", "context_len": 67}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Get a good education and when you 're fit for your life work it will bob up serenely in front of you", "context_len": 22}}}, "curtsey": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "A mighty noise above stairs of moving furniture and the patter of light footsteps came to Harrison as he basked by the great fire ; and it was not till the evening was growing late that Audrey reappeared , and , dropping a curtsey with a charming air of demureness , prayed leave to marshal his worship to his bedchamber", "context_len": 60}}}, "wickedness": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 39, "context": "Then he embraced Alaeddin and fell to kissing him and said to him , `` Excuse me , O my son , for that I was going [ 623 ] to bereave thee of thy life , through the wickedness of yonder accursed sorcerer who cast thee into this pit ; and indeed , O my son , I was excusable in that which I did with thee , inasmuch as I saw myself bereft of my daughter and mine only one , who is dearer to me than my kingdom , and thou knowest how fathers ' hearts yearn upon their children , more by token that I have but the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 114}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He was thinking only of his inexcusable folly and wickedness in leaving so good a home", "context_len": 16}}}, "brimstone": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 73, "context": "Dinner at length being over ( though later than usual , on account of the time occupied in baking the pie , and later , still , by reason of the goodness of it ) , they prepared to start , taking with them an axe to build a camp , tinder in a horn , flint , steel , and matches , which were made by dipping splinters of wood into melted brimstone , and which would burn when touched to the spark in the tinder", "context_len": 87}}}, "sweat": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 21, "context": "The capstan clanked slowly round as the main cable tautened and came in foot by foot ; the negroes , the sweat glistening on their ebony arms and backs , bent to their task , encouraged or goaded by the shouts of their overseers", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But , by an agonized effort that made the sweat start from his brow , he managed at last to move it , ever so slowly and painfully , drawing it by degrees from under his prostrate body , until that much of him at least was free", "context_len": 48}}}, "devil": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 4, "context": "Did I think the devil had come after me ? Yes , sirs -- gen ' l'men , I 've been awful bad , I do n't s'pose any of you knows what such badness is", "context_len": 36}}}, "sirs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` When Alaeddin heard their speech , wonderment took him and his tongue was tied , for that he knew not the cause ; then he turned to the eunuchs and officers and said , `` Prithee , sirs , [ 573 ] have you no knowledge of the cause of this commandment of the Sultan ? I know myself guiltless , forasmuch as I have done no sin against the Sultan nor against his realm", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "God bless you all , sirs -- and his voice broke again -- for the deed you have done , and for bringing us out of this living hell !", "context_len": 30}}}, "badness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Did I think the devil had come after me ? Yes , sirs -- gen ' l'men , I 've been awful bad , I do n't s'pose any of you knows what such badness is", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Sometimes badness comes into your mind just sudden like", "context_len": 10}}}, "happen": {"freq": 211, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 207, "word_index": 87, "context": "At last , when he felt that he must not delay another moment , he took leave of her , saying as he went , Well , as usual you have set me thinking , my little friend , but as though he were grateful for the same ; and Marie-Celeste , turning back to finish the copying of the Prince Imperial 's prayer , wondered in her practical little way if anything would come of the thinking , and if so , if she would ever happen to hear what it was ; and yet at the same time not a little sceptical as to any tangible result whatsoever", "context_len": 110}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "Should the worst happen , and you are captured by pirates , you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been", "context_len": 128}}}, "paths": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 107, "context": "And what more natural , when you find yourself at the foot of a flight of steps , than to walk up them , no matter if the place does chance to be Windsor Castle ; and then if at the top you find an open door confronting you , what more natural than to walk in , particularly if there happens to be no one to say you nay , and you have half a mind , besides , to seek an audience of the Queen , and report the ungracious conduct of an ungracious little American , who has been unworthily permitted to tread the paths of the royal garden", "context_len": 112}}}, "righteousness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 127, "context": "the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to demand her of the Sultan her father in the way of law and righteousness", "context_len": 128}}}, "intently": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 59, "context": "As we sat down to partake , of it together , Ama informed me that one of her father 's largest canoes , manned by forty paddlers , and commanded by a chief whom she had recognised , had passed slowly down the river about an hour after I had composed myself to rest , the chief in charge intently scrutinising both banks , as they went , evidently in search of some indication of our presence , and had finally passed out of sight to the southward ; after which Ama had descended and taken a few hours ' rest", "context_len": 101}}}, "supposedly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Supposedly the string of barges was `` manned '' by women , the diminishing group halting at each barge to prolong the conversation before the crews boarded their respective boats , until , by the clatter almost overhead , the fugitives knew that the last barge had received its complement -- two , perhaps three , buxom and stolid German women", "context_len": 61}}}, "surprises": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 23, "context": "By the way , did either of you hear or see anything of our old messmate , Glenn Crayle ? After all the surprises of the past twenty-four hours , I wouldn \u2019 t be surprised to see him waiting for us at the new airport", "context_len": 46}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Harris the surprise was in store of finding that Marie-Celeste 's steamer friend and Uncle Everett were one and the same person ; but surprises being the order of the day just then , everybody was coming to take them quite as a matter of course", "context_len": 46}}}, "brusquely": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I 'm in a little trouble , '' she said , brusquely , `` I ai n't got money to buy a ticket", "context_len": 24}}}, "remarkably": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 12, "context": "Those that had been over the route before said they got along remarkably well , while those to whom the experience was new , declared that with the breaking down of some wagons , the unloading of others , and letting them and goods they contained down the precipices by ropes , and the accidents attendant upon such work , they found the journey anything but delightful", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "Children can not too soon begin to know the wild flowers , and here they are told in a charming way where and when to look for each of fifty widely distributed common flowering plants ; also how they get their names , and how to know them from the remarkably accurate drawings of Mr", "context_len": 55}}}, "blond": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 80, "context": "There was Charley Tuttle , called Chub for short , a roly-poly , round-faced , laughing chap , who was munching peanuts ; Tim Davis , nicknamed Spotty , even more freckled than Ben , thin-legged , sly-faced , and minus the two front teeth of his upper jaw ; Sam Rollins , a big , hulking , low-browed fellow , who lost no opportunity to bully smaller boys , generally known as Hunk ; Berlin Barker , a cold blond , rather good-looking , but proud and distant in his bearing ; and others who did not impress the new boy at all with their personalities", "context_len": 107}}}, "artless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "The assistant inspector was a remarkably fine blond specimen of a man , and , as he was popular among the young women of the neighbourhood , 'Tilda Jane 's artless question produced a burst of laughter from his companions , and a furious flaming of colour in his own face", "context_len": 51}}}, "companions": {"freq": 200, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 200, "word_index": 28, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}}}, "resentful": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "Now that we have ended all this War of Words , And fall to drawing corks instead of swords , Now the Pretender may his Captors mock , And view with glee a match without the lock , Let each resentful thought and feeling cease , And General Harmony conclude the Piece !", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "In spite of himself , he could not refrain from giving them a resentful and defiant glance", "context_len": 17}}}, "pet": {"freq": 102, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` And it means no , and thank you , and if you please , and I love you , and everything else he wants to say , '' said Maggie , catching up her frisky pet in her arms and giving him a hug , which he returned by putting his cold nose in her face , after which he struggled to be put down again , for so glad was he to be free this pleasant morning that he wished to show it by frolicking about on his own four feet", "context_len": 93}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 5, "context": "She did not soothe or pet her overmuch ; she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 20, "context": "It returned her care by an affection which was quite embarrassing to the worthy girl , for however attractive a pet lamb may be , it becomes distinctly in the way when it insists upon following you into the dining-room with the dinner , or presses its attentions on you when you are engaged in cleaning the grate or scrubbing the floors", "context_len": 62}}}, "sauntered": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 10, "context": "He came down feet foremost , however , and they sauntered off along the road to the old water-mill , where the miller 's man was slinging a sack of flour on to a patient donkey who stood , with drooping ears , eyeing the burden which he must carry up far into the mountains , while his mistress , a little black-eyed Welshwoman , poured forth a torrent of gossip in high-pitched tones", "context_len": 74}}}, "immigrant": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Dillson looked entirely uninterested in the case of the would-be immigrant , so , after a farewell announcement that sometimes as many as two hundred `` furriners '' went through Vanceboro in a single day , 'Tilda Jane passed on to another branch of her subject", "context_len": 46}}}, "refuses": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Refuses to give name", "context_len": 4}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "That 's what I 'll do ; and I 'll wear my little red dress , and I 'll get Frosty into the middle of the lake , and I 'll make her promise to go away , and if she refuses to go -- I know she wo n't , for even those children wo n't keep her when such a thing as that is thought of '' -- --", "context_len": 71}}}, "stopping": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 75, "context": "What a pleasant ride that was : out of the field where the bars had been let down ; past other fields ready , or nearly ready , for the harvesting ; pale green oats , and golden wheat , the white , sweet-scented buckwheat , and the tall Indian corn ; then through the orchard where a flock of sheep were feeding , past the locust grove , and then into the farmyard ; stopping at last between the open doors of the great barn !", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "If the coaches had started , Smith 's carts would pursue for many miles , till they caught up the coaches at one of their stopping places", "context_len": 27}}}, "breathlessly": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 26, "context": "His remarks were cut short by the thunder of a horse \u2019 s hoofs , and a man suddenly burst in through the door and exclaimed breathlessly : `` Oh , Sir George ! Sir George ! ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "flinch": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Even could we say we repented , there yet were no mercy to hope for ; but we do not repent ! Were it to do again , we should not flinch", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Still Bomba did not flinch , giving the sullen Indian look for look without a sign of fear", "context_len": 18}}}, "chagrin": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 21, "context": "When the Vizier heard the Sultan 's words , his tongue was tied for despite and he was overcome with exceeding chagrin , forasmuch as the King had promised him that he would marry his daughter to his son ; so , after a little , he said to him , `` O King of the age , Thy Grace condescended to promise me [ 371 ] that the Lady Bedrulbudour should be my son 's ; wherefore it behoveth thine exalted highness appoint a delay of three months , [ 372 ] and God willing , my son 's present shall be greater than this", "context_len": 106}}}, "austerely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Have you made up your mind to talk yet ? '' he asked , austerely", "context_len": 16}}}, "amusing": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 30, "context": "Marie-Celeste straightened up a little , as though to show she did not quite approve , and then she replied , with an air of childish dignity that was vastly amusing , Because it was his family motto that helped Leonard ( he 's the boy in the story I spoke about ) ever so much , and that taught him to be cheerful and contented , and it seems to me -- this last very slowly and thoughtfully -- that you are very much like Leonard , only grown up", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 89, "context": "And all this while the wilful little queen was trying to convince herself that it was ready for Donald 's sake , when the truth was that the long walks with Donald , when Harold -- who was making up some necessary back work at college -- was not at her service , were what she was determined not to give up , and the reading aloud in the evenings , when Donald was such a delightful listener ; and , in fact , the hundred and one little amusing things that Donald was continually doing , and that made the days go by in such happy , merry fashion", "context_len": 110}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "An amusing and sympathetic study of a New England woman of humble station , but noble character", "context_len": 17}}}, "telegraphing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Young Fischer , who 's a splendid man himself , I 'll tell you , informed me yesterday that the plebe had earned his admission fee by bringing help to a wrecked train and telegraphing the account to a New York paper", "context_len": 42}}}, "stations": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 30, "context": "Already -- going for a moment last night into the court , a woman had confided to her that a little child was being advertised for at all the police stations , and that she wished she could get hold of him , for the reward offered for his recovery was ten pounds", "context_len": 53}}}, "quail": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Let me up and I will tell you , he said , refusing to quail before the fierce eyes directed upon him", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Now and then there were quail in the wheat-stubble , and there were meadow-larks in the pastures , but they were very wild", "context_len": 23}}}, "confronted": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 14, "context": "One morning in June I was called into our living-room , and found myself confronted by a gold-laced individual , who , drawing a paper from his pocket , read in a sonorous voice a summons for me to attend at the courthouse as a witness against Dick Swyre and Caleb Keeping , presented for committing a murderous attack upon divers of the king \u2019 s subjects on the highway", "context_len": 70}}}, "cherished": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "He loved the Church of England as he loved the Queen and the Parliament ; and Audrey had wondered and admired as she realized how he had endured to see the downfall of one cherished institution after another , still full of hope in the future of England , and of faith that the Divine Providence would bring good out of evil", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 59, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "darling": {"freq": 224, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 118, "word_index": 107, "context": "When Alaeddin 's mother heard her son 's speech , she thought little of his wit and said to him , `` O my son , the name of God encompass thee ! Meseemeth thou hast lost thy wit ; return to thy senses , [ 325 ] O my son , and be not like the madmen ! '' `` Nay , O my mother , '' replied he , `` I have not lost my wits nor am I mad ; and this thy speech shall not change that which is in my mind , nor is rest possible to me except I get the darling of my heart , the lovely Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 116}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 103, "word_index": 38, "context": "-- Mine own Laura , how could you write regarding the little meeting , at which you and sweet Margaret were , Would you not like to be in my shoes at the time , and hold your darling friend in your arms ? I would much rather have been in Margaret \u2019 s shoes , and have held some one else in my arms , -- only for the wrench that would have followed ! But O love , we are travelling in the same train , only in different carriages ; and I am thankful that though we can not see each other , we can as it were talk to each other out of the windows", "context_len": 119}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "And then how he lifted the little Queen in his arms , for to him she was just a darling little sister , and kissed her over and over again , while she sobbed and sobbed , and begged him not to leave her all alone", "context_len": 46}}}, "mute": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "To speak was to be sent back to the asylum , to remain silent was to let her Gippie become the cherished darling of some other girl , and in mute agony she caressed the smooth brown head , and put her hand before the almost sightless eyes as if she would hide from them even a suspicion of coming danger", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "Whenever he had thought his work too hard , or his hours too long , or the weather too inclement for further labor , he would stop in his tracks and turn his head around to his driver , and stand gazing in mute appeal , until he was urged forward", "context_len": 51}}}, "caressed": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 33, "context": "To speak was to be sent back to the asylum , to remain silent was to let her Gippie become the cherished darling of some other girl , and in mute agony she caressed the smooth brown head , and put her hand before the almost sightless eyes as if she would hide from them even a suspicion of coming danger", "context_len": 61}}}, "sightless": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 46, "context": "To speak was to be sent back to the asylum , to remain silent was to let her Gippie become the cherished darling of some other girl , and in mute agony she caressed the smooth brown head , and put her hand before the almost sightless eyes as if she would hide from them even a suspicion of coming danger", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "As the chill and dripping air changed from pearly gray to starless gloom , the hoarse and frequent whistle seemed to be appealing for guidance on this sightless sea", "context_len": 29}}}, "suspicion": {"freq": 92, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 68, "context": "How came it that this girl had guessed his secret at once when most people passed him , taking him but for a sturdy beggar ? What made her suspect him of being a fugitive ? Was her offer of shelter but careless good nature , or a heroic endeavour to save a hunted man ? At any rate he had not fallen so low as to draw suspicion on a woman , and a young woman to boot , although she was plainly no nervous , fanciful , fine lady , but a bright , resolute , country girl , with good health and high spirits gleaming from every flash of her bright eyes , and every turn of her auburn head", "context_len": 123}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "For one thing , I suspicion he was afraid of being stranded , and that I 'd offer to lend him money or something like that", "context_len": 26}}}, "slighted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Still her boy Alfred seemed slighted by the choice , and she left England at once , with Alfred and Edward , for Normandy , while Elgitha departed secretly from London to join her husband Edric , and tell him all that had been done", "context_len": 45}}}, "loitered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "All day she loitered about the station platform watching the trains come in , -- deliberate freight-trains , with their loads of merchandise , all to be examined by the busy customs officials , and rushing express trains , with their hundreds of hungry passengers who swept in crowds into the spacious dining-room", "context_len": 53}}}, "merchandise": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 61, "context": "As for Alaeddin , he left idleness and the commerce of striplings and took to consorting with grown men ; [ 316 ] nay , he would go every day to the market of the merchants and sit with the great and the small of them and question of the ways and fashions of commerce and the prices of articles of merchandise [ 317 ] and otherwhat", "context_len": 67}}}, "spacious": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 11, "context": "My prison -- if such it was -- was a tolerably spacious affair , measuring about twenty feet long by fifteen feet wide , and it was absolutely empty ; also , there being no windows to the building , and the light entering only by the open door , the obscurity , on entering , seemed profound , although a few minutes sufficed to enable one 's eyes to grow accustomed to it , when , at least during daylight , it was possible to see clearly enough for all practical purposes", "context_len": 93}}}, "captivity": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 39, "context": "Knowing that I was bound hither , a certain very beautiful young lady of noble family , to whom I had the honour of being introduced , prayed me that if you should by any chance have escaped from captivity -- and she said that she was convinced that you would , when you heard that Rhodes was threatened , assuredly endeavour to escape and to come hither to take a share in the defence -- I was to tell you that she trusted you still bore her gage , and that she , on her part , had held fast to the promise she made you", "context_len": 107}}}, "fashionable": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 29, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "But I love my own beautiful home in the summer weather best of all places on earth , and I am afraid of taking Irene to fashionable places", "context_len": 28}}}, "impress": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 98, "context": "There was Charley Tuttle , called Chub for short , a roly-poly , round-faced , laughing chap , who was munching peanuts ; Tim Davis , nicknamed Spotty , even more freckled than Ben , thin-legged , sly-faced , and minus the two front teeth of his upper jaw ; Sam Rollins , a big , hulking , low-browed fellow , who lost no opportunity to bully smaller boys , generally known as Hunk ; Berlin Barker , a cold blond , rather good-looking , but proud and distant in his bearing ; and others who did not impress the new boy at all with their personalities", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was strange how she had left her impress everywhere yet she was a very modest little girl , demanding nothing , taking all favors with a gratitude that was fascinating", "context_len": 31}}}, "beholders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The little German Jew , who had been roaming about the hotel like a restless ghost , always with his hat on and a bundle in his hand as if he wished to impress all beholders with the fact that he was only tarrying for a short time , had , on the receipt of a telegram informing the inspectors that he had merely forgotten his papers , become a happy maniac", "context_len": 72}}}, "tarrying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "The little German Jew , who had been roaming about the hotel like a restless ghost , always with his hat on and a bundle in his hand as if he wished to impress all beholders with the fact that he was only tarrying for a short time , had , on the receipt of a telegram informing the inspectors that he had merely forgotten his papers , become a happy maniac", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Right glad was he that his host had insisted upon his tarrying for that purpose", "context_len": 15}}}, "receipt": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 53, "context": "The little German Jew , who had been roaming about the hotel like a restless ghost , always with his hat on and a bundle in his hand as if he wished to impress all beholders with the fact that he was only tarrying for a short time , had , on the receipt of a telegram informing the inspectors that he had merely forgotten his papers , become a happy maniac", "context_len": 72}}}, "informing": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 75, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "inspectors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "The little German Jew , who had been roaming about the hotel like a restless ghost , always with his hat on and a bundle in his hand as if he wished to impress all beholders with the fact that he was only tarrying for a short time , had , on the receipt of a telegram informing the inspectors that he had merely forgotten his papers , become a happy maniac", "context_len": 72}}}, "maniac": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 71, "context": "The little German Jew , who had been roaming about the hotel like a restless ghost , always with his hat on and a bundle in his hand as if he wished to impress all beholders with the fact that he was only tarrying for a short time , had , on the receipt of a telegram informing the inspectors that he had merely forgotten his papers , become a happy maniac", "context_len": 72}}}, "collected": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 100, "context": "Among other things were two or three peacock feathers , a turkey wing , some pebbles from the brook where papa used to sail his boats when he was a boy , a piece of rusty tin pipe , which , because it looked black and smoky , and came from the field where the burnt barn had stood , they persuaded themselves must be a part of the very leader down which papa slid when he ran for the ladder to save his little brother , -- all these , and other treasures of like value , were carefully collected and stowed in the wagons , to be carried to the Lake House , and thence to the city", "context_len": 120}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "On Sundays he was in the habit of regularly addressing a collected crowd of Natives ; literally the poor , the maimed , the halt , the blind \u2019 ; and he did not teach them only , but also ministered liberally to their bodily needs", "context_len": 46}}}, "boarding": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 44, "context": "There was commotion in the Harris household , notwithstanding the very early hour -- the sort of commotion which means that somebody is off for Europe , somebody who has preferred remaining at home , and rising as early as need he , to boarding the steamer the night before and spending it tied to a noisy dock", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The Sharp Corner was a second-class groggery and boarding house , patronized almost entirely by the poorest and most shiftless class of trackmen", "context_len": 23}}}, "comprehension": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 42, "context": "What are you saying , Marie-Celeste ? said Albert impetuously ; I do n't understan ' you at all ; for not for one single moment was this conversation in the Queen 's own sitting-room to rise above the level of his comprehension , if it lay in his power to prevent it", "context_len": 53}}}, "system": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 45, "context": "I 'm goin ' to be a good girl now , an ' never tell no more lies nor steal , nor do anythin ' bad , ' but he jus ' said ever so grumpy , 'This is a cast down , no credit system store , ' but I was n't cas ' down , an ' I said , 'S'pose you was a lame man , an ' a bad little girl burnt up your crutches , how would you feel ? 'Then he looked kind o ' solemn , an ' said , 'Whose crutches was burnt up ? ' An ' I said , 'Mr", "context_len": 110}}}, "espionage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The paragraph was to the effect that Konrad von Feldoffer , a German convicted of espionage by a general court martial , had made a daring and successful attempt to escape", "context_len": 31}}}, "unsuspecting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "In this case , the high spirit who was challenged felt nothing base in running up behind his unsuspecting friend and popping him into the water , and I have no doubt the victim considered the affair in the right light when he found that it was a dare", "context_len": 49}}}, "wraps": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "You who are so kindly anxious about my comfort will be pleased to know that I have a very fair amount of wraps , and am more likely to suffer from heat than cold , seeing that my cabin port-hole is never opened , and that the only way of ventilating it is by leaving the door open , -- a thing not to be thought of at night , as ladies \u2019 and gentlemen \u2019 s cabins are not at all in separate parts of the vessel", "context_len": 88}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "One or two were covered with buffalo robes , of which every warrior carries one , in which he wraps himself when cold", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "This all seemed most convenient , and what with watching Emma pack the basket and with hunting up wraps the time went very rapidly and they were surprised to hear Mrs", "context_len": 31}}}, "penniless": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "Bradshaw , when , as a matter of fact , she 's a penniless cousin of mine , who would have gone to the poorhouse but for my father 's charity", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "\u2019 All this , of one who , twenty-five years before , had landed on Indian shores an almost penniless adventurer , without so much as a definite plan of what to do with himself and his energies !", "context_len": 39}}}, "nap": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Well , '' I thought , `` I 'd better take a little nap while I 'm waiting for this tangle to straighten itself out , '' and I was just turning my head to the wall of my log-house stable and drooping my head when there was a slight noise from the direction of the doorway", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 54, "context": "I wonder if I ought not to tell just Chris , anyway ; but somehow or other I do not seem to have strength enough even to make up my mind , and I 'll give up trying for the present ; and so , ceasing to make any effort whatever , the little nap that would not come for the asking stole quietly in and laid its blessed touch of oblivion upon poor , troubled Ted", "context_len": 77}}}, "spot": {"freq": 342, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 330, "word_index": 39, "context": "It looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 27, "context": "I am a black Shetland pony brought up mostly in cities and lovely open country places , and here was I shut up in a wild hilly spot miles from any human being except a few settlers", "context_len": 37}}}, "apprehensive": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 14, "context": "Bradley and Dewey , knowing the fickleness of a mining-community , were a little apprehensive that their original suspicions might again be aroused , and that some among them might be led to think they had make a mistake , after all , and hung the wrong men", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Hush ! '' said Orton looking around , apprehensive of listeners", "context_len": 12}}}, "completely": {"freq": 107, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 107, "word_index": 59, "context": "It was nearly noon when I was conducted back to my hut after my futile attempt to cure the king ; and it was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant", "context_len": 128}}}, "ardent": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 10, "context": "How sagacious a line of conduct was this ! Most ardent and enthusiastic young men , in the circumstances in which he was placed , would have been elated and vain at their elevation , and would have replaced the old and well-tried servants of the father with personal favorites of their own age , inexperienced and incompetent , and as conceited as themselves", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "Suppose she only fancied that she saw the Pillar , \u2019 -- in other words , that she was called or led or ordered to Batala ? A mistake of this description is not impossible , especially in the case of an ardent and impulsive nature", "context_len": 46}}}, "lover": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 41, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}}}, "clanging": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The clanging supper-bell disturbed her , and , with a sigh and a look of longing farewell at the sky , she closed the window and made her way to the dining-room", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Others , armed with long-handled shovels , fed the capacious furnaces so frequently that the place reverberated to the clanging of the red-hot metal doors at the ends of the multi-tube boilers", "context_len": 32}}}, "fascination": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 6, "context": "Although General Harrison 's strong personal fascination and unselfish ardour still commanded his nephew 's affection and even admiration , the young man 's irreverent common sense could not help viewing these new Fifth Monarchy opinions held by his uncle and his uncle 's friends as fitter for Bedlam than for the pulpit or the parliament house", "context_len": 57}}}, "differences": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` I am not like to mistake the colours of Master Harrison -- or Captain Harrison , is it not ? '' answered the girl ; `` and whatever differences did latterly divide us in mind , though not in love , from General Harrison , you must needs know we were all for the Parliament here -- my grandfather , my father , and I ; that is how I came to guess you for one in hiding from the king 's men ; but for your own sake I would have you careful , lest even walls should have ears", "context_len": 102}}}, "shrieked": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Beware , woman ! '' shrieked Maggie , and Bessie , thinking it time for her to come to her sister 's aid , joined in the cry , `` Beware , woman ! '' while Franky , always ready to take part in any disturbance , struck at Aunt Patty with his whip , and shouted , `` 'Ware , woman ! '' and Hafed , knowing nothing but that this old lady had alarmed and distressed his young charge , and that it was his duty to protect them , raised his voice in a whoop of defiance , and snatching up the hearth-brush , brandished it in a threatening manner as he danced wildly about her", "context_len": 120}}}, "contentment": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}}}, "dismay": {"freq": 75, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 121, "context": "would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart of your enemy", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was so unexpected and so bitter a disappointment that an expression of blank dismay overspread her face , and she sank into the nearest chair without venturing on a single word of greeting", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Words of mine can not paint the terror and dismay the tidings caused ; the scene of distress and fear is yet before my eyes as I write", "context_len": 28}}}, "starlit": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` Would you like a heaven so small , so human , that mortal words could line it out , and mortal wishes be its boundary ? The things we look for are prepared by One whose thoughts are as far above our thoughts as the broad starlit heaven is above this little gaslit earth", "context_len": 55}}}, "solitudes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "It gave her but little dismay to look out into the starlit night and fancy herself alone in snowy solitudes , but it gave her considerable dismay to look down below , and find that the hotel was neither getting dark nor perfectly quiet , as she fancied all well-regulated houses did at night", "context_len": 54}}}, "fancied": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 23, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 78, "context": "Heaven knows who wrote it or published it ; his father bought it with a number of other books at an auction , and the boy , who had about that time discovered the chapter on prosody in the back part of his grammar , made poems from it for years , and appeared in many transfigurations , as this and that god and demigod and hero upon imagined occasions in the Boy 's Town , to the fancied admiration of all the other fellows", "context_len": 85}}}, "supervision": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 62, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "evade": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "If I am captured I trust that an opportunity of escape will soon present itself , for I should , if taken , conceal from my captors the fact that I understand their language , and should thus , if I could evade my guard , have every chance of escaping , as in a native dress I could meet and converse with those hunting for me , without their having a suspicion of my being the white slave for whom they were in search", "context_len": 85}}}, "detection": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 66, "context": "Leacraft as he had with the boys , insisting that the whole affair , the abstraction of the money and the placing it in Seabrooke 's trunk , was `` only a joke ; '' but the doctor altogether refused to look upon it in any other light than that of an unmitigated theft and an atrocious attempt to fasten it upon another when he feared detection for himself", "context_len": 69}}}, "north": {"freq": 136, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 39, "context": "It was this , rather than the capture of these three vessels , that induced us to disobey the general instructions we had been given to cruise along the northern coast of Africa , and determined us to push north to give warning along the coast from Naples to Genoa of the danger that threatened , and , if possible , to enable Genoa to fit out her galleys to encounter the corsairs", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 90, "context": "As Ama , taking her bow and arrows , tripped lightly away toward the forest , I proceeded to shin up the tree , and presently , after some labour , found myself among its topmost branches , which towered high above those of all the other trees in the neighbourhood , and -- it being by this time broad daylight -- obtained a magnificent view of the surrounding country , extending for , as I estimated , some forty miles toward the south and east , while toward the north and west the view was shut off by high hills , through which the river wound its way", "context_len": 109}}}, "wink": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 53, "context": "Belden told me on the steamer , said Marie-Celeste , with the air of one who settles down for a good talk with a familiar friend , of some musician who heard some one strike two or three chords and then suddenly stop , and after that he ; could not get a wink of sleep till he jumped out of bed and rushed to his piano and struck the chord that belonged at the end of the others", "context_len": 79}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "You may believe me , sir , I got afeared of keeping along o ' him ; he 'd have turned me into a saint before I could wink", "context_len": 29}}}, "freights": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Guess I 'll take a wink of sleep , '' a tired voice responded , `` there 'll be nothing but freights for a spell , '' and then followed comparative silence", "context_len": 33}}}, "comparative": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 42, "context": "An approach to its reconstruction could be attempted by carefully comparing the lexicon and grammatic forms of the dialects presently spoken , and an individual acquainted with them all , or at least with their four representatives , might also compose a comparative grammar of these dialects as spoken at the present epoch of their development , which would reveal many points concerning the ancient or historic shape of the language once common to all these tribes", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "From a thorough comparative study of the Yuchi language , the Maskoki dialects and the local nomenclature of the country , we can alone expect any reliable information upon the extent and the area of territory anciently held by the Yuchi ; but at present it is safest to locate their `` priscan home '' upon both sides of Lower Savannah river", "context_len": 62}}}, "fewer": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 33, "context": "M\u00fcller stated that the total amount he had received by prayer and faith for the various objects of his institutions , since 5th March , 1834 , had been 1,309,627 ; that no fewer than 8727 children had been under his care ; and that he had room at his Homes for 2050 orphans", "context_len": 54}}}, "lull": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "Father Cuthbert and Edmund , amongst others , turned round to see what caused the lull , and started from their seats as they beheld at the end of the room Alfgar , his face pale as one risen from the dead , his black locks hanging dishevelled around his neck , his garments torn , his whole person disordered", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Hondura knew , though , that Nascanora \u2019 s heart was black and that the invaders were only trying to lull him into security until they were ready to fall on his people and wipe them out", "context_len": 37}}}, "noises": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 105, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}}}, "action": {"freq": 168, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 168, "word_index": 116, "context": "the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the mind by the capricious action of memory , while far greater events drop out of it", "context_len": 128}}}, "wary": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 33, "context": "However , by leaving scraps of her clothing on every sharp thorn , and getting her hands and legs terribly scratched , she forced her way out at last ; and keeping a wary outlook on the fort , she tried to unloose the knots that bound Jim", "context_len": 48}}}, "stealthy": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 9, "context": "While 'Tilda Jane wrote , Poacher suddenly made a stealthy movement , and Gippie , deaf as he was , had enough of the dog spirit left in him to know that some one was coming , and to elevate the tiny V-shaped flaps over his ears", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "So faint and stealthy was the sound that it would have been inaudible to most ears other than those of Bomba", "context_len": 21}}}, "elf": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "She rose stealthily , and took her dog in her arms -- a pathetic child figure no longer , but a wary , stealthy little elf endeavouring to escape from danger threatened by these larger and more powerful human beings", "context_len": 40}}}, "threatened": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 82, "word_index": 58, "context": "Knowing that I was bound hither , a certain very beautiful young lady of noble family , to whom I had the honour of being introduced , prayed me that if you should by any chance have escaped from captivity -- and she said that she was convinced that you would , when you heard that Rhodes was threatened , assuredly endeavour to escape and to come hither to take a share in the defence -- I was to tell you that she trusted you still bore her gage , and that she , on her part , had held fast to the promise she made you", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "Since the threatened counter-attack had not yet materialized , most of the New Zealanders were resting in the dug-outs obligingly constructed by Fritz , who little thought that he would have to abandon his painstaking work except upon the conclusion of a victorious German peace", "context_len": 45}}}, "chamber": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 57, "context": "Then he took leave of the Sultan and going forth , mounted and rode , with his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like ,", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "[ Footnote 621 : Or chamber ( keszr )", "context_len": 9}}}, "undressed": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 7, "context": "She had heard them say as they undressed that they had to get up at half-past four to iron table-cloths and napkins , and there was not an instant 's interruption of their heavy , dreamless slumber as she stole noiselessly by them", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "'If you 'll get undressed right away , Lemuel , I 'll tell you about a little boy who lived with an ' old , old man , and one night he could n't sleep , but -- -- '", "context_len": 40}}}, "napkins": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "The last spark had died out of the paper in the grate and left only black tinder ; the table was left bare , the golden plates and richly embroidered napkins , and the garlands were transformed again into old handkerchiefs , scraps of red and white paper , and discarded artificial flowers all scattered on the floor ; the minstrels in the minstrel gallery had stolen away , and the viols and bassoons were still", "context_len": 76}}}, "interruption": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 64, "context": "Albert , appealing to the Queen , for he felt that quite too much was being taken for granted , will you please tell me what is a tilt-yard ? and what it was dat de knignts would not tome to ? and what was dat little white ting of the Queen 's dat de King carried ? and impatiently as Marie-Celeste brooked the interruption , there was nothing for it but to wait while Her Majesty explained that the tilt-yard was a sort of riding-school for the knights , where they practised for the tournaments , and that the tournaments were occasions when the knights , spear in hand , came together to ride against each other , with a great many people looking on , and", "context_len": 128}}}, "dreamless": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "She had heard them say as they undressed that they had to get up at half-past four to iron table-cloths and napkins , and there was not an instant 's interruption of their heavy , dreamless slumber as she stole noiselessly by them", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "The night was now far advanced , and once at my quarters I flung myself on the rude bed that had been provided for me , and all the troubles and tangles in this world dissolved and disappeared in dreamless slumber", "context_len": 41}}}, "noiselessly": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 75, "context": "Go down to the big apple-tree in the corner of the meadow , directed Donald , half under his breath , and , look here ! you had better take this with you , dragging a steamer rug from the couch , and flinging it out after him , and I 'll come down just as soon as ever I can and let you know how things are going and then Donald drew the shutters noiselessly to and sped back to the lane at as tight a run as he had left it", "context_len": 93}}}, "staircase": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 5, "context": "There was a large oak staircase , with wide , shallow steps , leading to a panelled gallery , where hung swords , and rusty armour , and moth-eaten tapestry , and many an ancient relic of the past ; while in the best bedroom was a great carved four-post bed , hung with faded yellow curtains , where Queen Elizabeth herself was said to have slept in much state for two nights on her journey from Shrewsbury to Wrexham", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "[ Footnote 245 : Burton says here , `` The text creates some confusion by applying sullem to staircase and ladder ; hence probably the latter is not mentioned by Galland and Co", "context_len": 33}}}, "tiptoeing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "There was no one in sight , and holding her breath , and tiptoeing cautiously , she stole down step by step", "context_len": 22}}}, "cautiously": {"freq": 98, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 97, "word_index": 2, "context": "We emerged cautiously from our cover , and soon stood where , a few days before , the priory had risen , beautiful before God ; it was but a huge pile of blackened timber and stone ; and even more conspicuous above all other ruins , by the black smoke it still sent forth , was that which had been the hall", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "As John Porter and the two gentlemen stood at its foot and gazed upward , they could see nothing of Lem ; and they went on cautiously , looking from side to side lest he should be hiding among some one of the many nooks and crannies of the rocks", "context_len": 50}}}, "flights": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 68, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}}}, "halted": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 79, "word_index": 65, "context": "Marching up to what I conjectured to be the king 's house -- from the fact that it was not only by far the largest dwelling in the enclosure , but was also distinguished by an exclusive embellishment in the form of a row of a dozen poles , each surmounted by a human skull , planted upright in the ground before it -- we halted at a distance of some twenty paces from the entrance , with our backs turned toward the crucifixion tree , the leafless branches of which overshadowed us , and waited", "context_len": 96}}}, "office": {"freq": 254, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 254, "word_index": 126, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}}}, "napping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "She could see the office at the end of the hall , and the sleepy clerk napping at his desk", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "But even her favorite fairy tales failed to fix her attention , so she returned to the house to find everyone given up to napping and the place so still that finally in the coolest corner of the library where a little breeze found its way through the open windows , she herself fell asleep", "context_len": 55}}}, "retreated": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 38, "context": "She would go round with Nancy and a candle , carefully locking all the doors and securing the shutters , peering fearfully into cupboards and starting at her own shadow on the wall ; and , having finally retreated to her bedroom , would barricade the door with a tin box , and place the poker handy on a chair by her bedside", "context_len": 63}}}, "discover": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 69, "context": "Somebody has put it onto paper , however , and very successfully , too ; so that I should advise you , in case a driving trip through the English Lake Country does not soon happen to come your way , to look between the covers of The Strange Adventures of a Phaeton , as soon as you grow a bit older , and see if you do not discover the charm of it for yourself", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "It was the custom in those days , as it is now when armies are marching , to send forward small bodies of men in every direction to explore the roads , remove obstacles , and discover sources of danger", "context_len": 40}}}, "awaited": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 77, "context": "son get these dishes of jewels ? Supposing , for the jewels and the dishes , that he return to the treasure and gather the whole from the trees , -- and withal methinketh not it is possible to him ; but say that he fetch them , -- whence [ shall he get ] the slaves and slave-girls ? '' And she gave not over talking to herself till she reached the house , where Alaeddin awaited her , and when she came in to him , she said to him , `` O my son , said I not to thee , 'Think not to attain to the Lady Bedrulbudour ' ? Indeed , this is a thing that is not possible unto folk like ourselves", "context_len": 128}}}, "uncertainty": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 56, "context": "Beyond the fact that a lady in white was supposed to haunt both the ruins and the oak-wood , weeping and wringing her hands in orthodox ghostly fashion , the children had not been able to learn much of the story , for there were so many and divergent accounts of it , all told with uncertainty as to names and dates , and in that very oracular -- not to say muddled -- style sometimes indulged in by rustic historians", "context_len": 81}}}, "stifled": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "But on second thought the desire to confide in him passed out of sight , more particularly as there was a noise and confusion -- a sort of stifled confusion -- in the house : people hurrying backwards and forwards , and voices sunk to whispers , which came sometimes to Rosamund 's ears , and sometimes receded in the distance", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "The two young men were of the same age , and , though Alexander had the good sense to retain in power all the old and experienced officers which his father had employed , both in the court and army , he showed that , after all , ambition had not overwhelmed and stifled all the kindlier feelings of the heart , by his strong attachment to this young companion", "context_len": 70}}}, "sped": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 96, "context": "I was not permitted to show myself in the doorway of my dwelling , or even to approach it nearly enough to see what was going on ; and in this dreadful solitude , waiting and hoping for I knew not what impossible happening to occur and effect my deliverance , each day seemed to drag itself out to the length of a month -- until the darkness came ; and then , with the realisation of the fact that I was so much nearer to a hideous fate , the hours seemed suddenly to have sped with lightning swiftness", "context_len": 100}}}, "comparatively": {"freq": 73, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 2, "context": "During his comparatively brief service in the trenches he had been called upon to repel isolated raids , both by day and night ; he had taken part in several successful excursions over the top to harry the German trenches ; he had participated in one of the greatest actions on the Western Front ; but , for the first time , he was helping to man a captured position against a massive hostile counter-attack", "context_len": 75}}}, "shooting": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 47, "context": "Now , Uncle Isaac , said Charlie , I want you to tell me just one thing ; how did you learn to shoot so ? My grandfather killed men in battle , and used to shoot at the butts on holidays , and gained prizes for shooting , but he couldn \u2019 t shoot like that ; and I don \u2019 t believe he ever heard of anything like it", "context_len": 71}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 40, "context": "A great many points were always coming up : whether a boy took-up or edged beyond the very place where his toy lay when he shot ; whether he knuckled down , or kept his hand on the ground in shooting ; whether , when another boy 's toy drove one marble against another and knocked both out of the ring , he holloed `` Fen doubs ! '' before the other fellow holloed `` Doubs ! '' whether a marble was in or out of the ring , and whether the umpire 's decision was just or not", "context_len": 99}}}, "pressing": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 76, "context": "Cassowary , who was almost as much at home under the water as on it , was plunging about like a seal , and just as I arrived had brought Dallas to the surface , and holding him by the collar with one hand and hanging on to the crib with the other she gravely watched Champ and Big Chief giving first aid to the drowning by putting my poor little master on his face and pressing the water out of his lungs", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 34, "context": "Assuredly , Hannah , devoted as she was to the interests of her nurslings , especially Percy , would never have thought of making this sacrifice had she not felt that there was some pressing necessity ; but how in the world had the old nurse acquired this knowledge", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Many , whose favourite occupations are , to say the least , no whit more pressing than were her books , are exceedingly tenacious of their time , and exceedingly impatient of interruptions ; and with too many the home-calls come second to all personal interests", "context_len": 46}}}, "native": {"freq": 173, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 137, "word_index": 64, "context": "to the trunk of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 8, "context": "In response to this imperative demand , the native jabbered out directions , telling Bomba the course he must take to reach Snake Island if he did not wish to be swept to destruction by the rapids of the River of Death", "context_len": 42}}}, "press": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 86, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 14, "context": "But still she went on , but only slowly , because of the great press of people around her ; and suddenly an icy wind passed over them , and all fell to the earth shivering and terror-stricken , for they had never felt cold before , and they looked up to the mountain , and lo ! upon the very summit , at the door of the deserted wigwam , stood a terrible figure , clothed in white , and having a face as white as his robes , and his hair was like the long crystals that hang from the roofs of caves that the water goes through , and his eyes were like two great diamonds , white , yet blazing like the sun", "context_len": 127}}}, "sloping": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 9, "context": "Thrace was one great fertile basin or valley , sloping toward the center in every direction , so that all the streams from the mountains , increased by the rains which fell over the whole surface of the ground , flowed together into one river , which meandered through the center of the valley , and flowed out at last into the \u00c6gean Sea", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "And such a garden as it proved ! with its fountains and statues and vases , and the orangery on one side , and on the other three sides a beautiful sloping lawn , ascending from the level of the garden to the gray stonewall at the outer edge of the terrace ; and to think that here they were actually walking about in this beautiful garden , instead of merely peering through the fretwork of the iron gate , as some other little children with envious eyes were doing that very moment", "context_len": 93}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Most of the attics were quite excellent , but this room had a decidedly sloping roof , and rather small windows , and the paper on the walls was ugly , and the accommodation scanty , and what those huge boxes were going to do there was more than I could tell", "context_len": 52}}}, "habitations": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "Bartram , who saw it in 1775 , depicts it as the largest , most compact , and best situated Indian town he ever saw ; the habitations were large and neatly built , the walls of the houses consisted of a wooden frame , lathed and plastered inside and outside with a reddish clay , and roofed with cypress bark or shingles", "context_len": 63}}}, "evenings": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 49, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Clavering 's drawing-room on the company evenings , and to show it with pride to her companions as Florence 's gift", "context_len": 21}}}, "hark": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "He was absent on the third of these little excursions of his , and was surreptitiously amusing his audacious little self by seeing how it seemed to sit in the Oueen 's own stall , when hark ! -- yes , that was going to be The Roseate Hues , and with a bound that came near bringing the royal draperies with him he was out of the stall in a trice and fairly scrambling up the organ stairs", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "But , hark 'ee , Dick , dost know the king hath sent for thine uncle to make him a duke ? ''", "context_len": 23}}}, "crashing": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 46, "context": "None could look back , not even to behold If those beloved were nigh ; one thrilling thought Rapt all the multitude , -- Can HE be near ? Then cries of terror rose -- I scarcely heard ; And buildings shook and rocked , and crashing fell , -- I scarcely marked their fall ; the trembling ground Rose like the billowy sea , -- I scarcely felt The motion ; such intensity of hope -- Joy -- expectation -- flooded all my soul ; A tide of living light , o \u2019 erwhelming all The hopes and fears , the cares and woes of earth", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 90, "context": "Very slowly and gradually the storm worked its way toward the zenith , gathering intensity as it rose , and at length -- probably about ten o'clock -- the first drops of rain , hot and heavy , like gouts of blood , began to fall , quickly increasing to a drenching downpour , accompanied by lightning , green , rose-tinted , violet , sun-bright , that lighted up the town until every object , however minute , was as clearly visible as in broad daylight , while the ceaseless crashing of the thunder was unspeakably appalling", "context_len": 97}}}, "alder": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "But the boys had built for their sisters what they called a `` Fairy Bower , '' made by drawing together the top branches of some young alder trees to form a roof , and weaving in branches at the back", "context_len": 41}}}, "bushes": {"freq": 164, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 161, "word_index": 26, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I seed two er th'ee men prowlin ' roun ' in de bushes ez I come 'long", "context_len": 17}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Away they went , laughing and singing , by the lake road , and then down the side of the mountain to a spot where John said the blackberry bushes grew very thick", "context_len": 33}}}, "seize": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 86, "context": "Neither Peggy nor Bobby had ever seen a wild-beast show before , for those visits to the Zoo , which are the joy of little Londoners , do not fall to the share of country children , and Archie had quite a lively time keeping them out of harm 's way , for Peggy declared the leopard looked so exactly like Tabbyskins at home that she must positively try to stroke it , and was under the rope and up to the bars before Archie could seize her by her skirts and drag her back , while Bobby 's curiosity on the subject of jackals and hyenas nearly led to the ejection of the whole party from the tent", "context_len": 119}}}, "deerhound": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "This was a large deerhound running along with his nose to the snow , and he paid no more attention to her than if she had been one of the stumps by the side of the road", "context_len": 37}}}, "doggie": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 2, "context": "Bring her doggie , ' '' said Jennie ; then turning to her mother , she asked , `` Mother , do you b'lieve you can understand Tommy till I come back ? ''", "context_len": 34}}}, "startled": {"freq": 122, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 93, "word_index": 88, "context": "He knew what it was to be without peace ; for having got into debt when he was first in the army , and knowing the distress it caused his family at home , his mind was so troubled that he wrote to his mother : `` Oh , what agony I have endured ! What sleepless nights I have passed since the perusal of that letter ! The review of my past life , especially the retrospect of the last two years , has at last quite startled me , and at the same time disgusted me", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 54, "context": "They had gone but a few yards farther , Bomba \u2019 s eyes straining to detect the faintly marked trail , when there was a thud , and on the ground before them , directly in their path , appeared a figure so grotesque in form and ugly in face that Bomba took a startled step backward and the two slaves fell to the ground in a fit of shuddering terror", "context_len": 71}}}, "stillness": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 11, "context": "Gervaise slept for some time ; when he awoke the same stillness reigned , but there was a change in the appearance of the sky ; its brightness was dulled by a faint mist , while , although the sea was of a glassy smoothness , there was an imperceptible swell that caused the felucca to sway uneasily", "context_len": 58}}}, "hastily": {"freq": 132, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 132, "word_index": 40, "context": "Guy thought he had never witnessed so sad a scene as when they laid the beautiful baby , that looked as pure and sweet as a white lily , in the rough coffin that some of the young men had hastily made , and carried him to a lonely spot , that perhaps no feet had ever trod before , and , breathing a prayer over him , left him to his long sleep , far from the place of his birth , or that for which his kindred were bound , and where never a tear would be dropped above him , or a sigh breathed", "context_len": 107}}}, "resumed": {"freq": 78, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 78, "word_index": 35, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "waitress": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "At the exact hour agreed upon as the best time for dinner , the little maid , turned cook and waitress , announced the meal as ready , and her reward came in the children 's demonstrative approval", "context_len": 38}}}, "inquiring": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 10, "context": "When he asked if he might take the liberty of inquiring what interest she had in my family , she asked if it was necessary to Willie 's cure that he should know that ; and when he said , 'No , of course not , ' she said it was a great liberty , and as good as told him to mind his own affairs", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` She 's down there , dog , '' he said , and the boldness with which he told the story so impressed Poacher , that after one inquiring glance which convinced him that his enemy 's attitude had changed from that of a murderous to a semi-friendly one , he dashed down the steps into the cold cellar", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "In the circumstances , with the protection of the grass , it ought to be easy to steal within gunshot of the game , provided their inquiring nature was turned to good account", "context_len": 33}}}, "grumbled": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 6, "context": "The stranger shivered , coughed , grumbled out a few more oaths in bad Spanish , and hitching his chair nearer to the fire he lifted the tankard the woman of the house brought to him , and nodded over at Richard", "context_len": 42}}}, "innocence": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 86, "context": "Can he speak thus who hath so long espoused The Royal cause , and served that cause so well ? Who , girt with honours , well deserved , hath stood One in a noble Brotherhood of Fame ! Where are the Cavaliers who fought with thee In battle , side by side , who with thee shared The feast , and drained the wine-cup to your King ? Where are they now ? what , gone ? not one remains , T \u2019 assert thy innocence , or shield thee from An ignominious death", "context_len": 95}}}, "abroad": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 7, "context": "As soon as my news was bruited abroad , and they learned that the fleet consisted solely of prizes captured from the Moors by a galley of the Order , alarm quickly changed into delight , the sharp , angry clang of the bells was succeeded by peals of gladness , and the joy of the citizens at being relieved from the cloud of anxiety that had hung over the city since my last visit , was unbounded", "context_len": 78}}}, "toiling": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 38, "context": "Many of these were , however , killed by the dreadful confusion and violence of the retreat as vast bodies of horsemen , pressing through the defiles , rode over and trampled down the foot soldiers who were toiling in awful confusion along the way , having fled before the horsemen left the field", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "appreciative": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 70, "context": "Miss Forster was not sorry to find so plausible an excuse for keeping her pet nephew at the Willows , and the young man himself had no objections , being fuller than ever of ingenious schemes , only he had transferred the seat of his operations to the Abbey , as offering a wider scope in the way of material , and having the further advantage of a number of appreciative assistants", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "He had not noticed them much the night before , but that morning he became quite well acquainted with them ; discovered that the elder was called Amy , and the younger Carrie , and that they were both very pleasant , and appreciative of all little acts of kindness", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "No one attempted to do any work ; sweets and apples appeared mysteriously from within desks , and surreptitious bites were offered to appreciative neighbours", "context_len": 25}}}, "adjective": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` The adjective ( nakiszeh ) is put in the feminine , to agree with `` lattice '' ( sheriyyeh ) , which is femminine , kiosk ( kushk ) being masculine", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "The case inflection of the substantive , adjective , of some pronouns and of the nominal forms of the verb is effected by the suffixes : -i for the absolute , -ut for the subjective , -un for the objective case : y\u00e1ti person , y\u00e1tut , y\u00e1tun ; n\u00e1ki what , which , n\u00e1kut , n\u00e1kun", "context_len": 57}}}, "handful": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 35, "context": "So the kind nurse opened the paper containing the biscuits , and spread it on the flat stone on which the children sat ; next she pulled two broad mullein leaves , and put a handful of berries on each , and then having produced the drinking cup she always carried when the children went on an expedition , she asked John where she should find a stream , and one being near at hand as usual , the cup was soon filled and placed beside the other things", "context_len": 89}}}, "slightest": {"freq": 103, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 102, "word_index": 91, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The idea of unloading did not appeal to them in the slightest", "context_len": 12}}}, "indication": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 73, "context": "As we sat down to partake , of it together , Ama informed me that one of her father 's largest canoes , manned by forty paddlers , and commanded by a chief whom she had recognised , had passed slowly down the river about an hour after I had composed myself to rest , the chief in charge intently scrutinising both banks , as they went , evidently in search of some indication of our presence , and had finally passed out of sight to the southward ; after which Ama had descended and taken a few hours ' rest", "context_len": 101}}}, "faltering": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "She had again picked up the wailing dog , and had slung him on her back in the shawl , yet there was not the slightest indication of faltering in her aspect", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` My word of honor ! '' she said then in a faltering tone", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 79, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}}}, "aspect": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 96, "context": "each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the operation was undertaken", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The room had lost its former barren aspect", "context_len": 8}}}, "stealing": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 102, "context": "peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to laugh at the supernatural , she did not feel quite so brave as she pretended , and found little cold shivers stealing down her back when the wind rose suddenly , and began to wail round the cottage like a hungry creature waiting to be let in", "context_len": 128}}}, "bravery": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 108, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "rearing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 66, "context": "Harold , who had been all through the stables of the Duke of Westminster at Eton Hall , had described something much finer than this -- imposing buildings surrounding a courtyard paved with bevel-edged squares of stone , with not so much as a whisp of hay or straw to be seen anywhere , and in the centre a noble statue of a high-spirited horse , rearing and pulling hard at the bridle , held in the hand of a stalwart groom , who seems fully equal to the occasion", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "She could never afterwards tell exactly what happened at that moment , except that the gig-lamps flashed suddenly in her eyes , 'Whoa , my lad ! ' shouted Father 's voice , and Prince 's rearing , kicking form loomed large before her as he backed persistently towards the bank", "context_len": 51}}}, "aloft": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 93, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "An instant later there came tumbling from aloft a long wire , that writhed about like some snake , spitting blue flames and sparks", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Bart was about to shout to his father in front , or to his roustabout friend , whom he expected must be somewhere near by this time , when his captive gave a jerk , tore one arm free , and whirled the other aloft", "context_len": 45}}}, "unregistered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "The white glory of the snow , the stealing pink and gold glances of the sun , the bravery of the trees proudly rearing their heads aloft and stretching out their heavily laden arms , -- all made a picture that filled with awe even the heart of rough Bob Lucas , unregistered guide and nominal lumberman , noted for his skill as hunter and poacher and his queer mingling of honesty , law-breaking , piety , and profanity", "context_len": 79}}}, "nominal": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 55, "context": "The white glory of the snow , the stealing pink and gold glances of the sun , the bravery of the trees proudly rearing their heads aloft and stretching out their heavily laden arms , -- all made a picture that filled with awe even the heart of rough Bob Lucas , unregistered guide and nominal lumberman , noted for his skill as hunter and poacher and his queer mingling of honesty , law-breaking , piety , and profanity", "context_len": 79}}}, "lumberman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "The white glory of the snow , the stealing pink and gold glances of the sun , the bravery of the trees proudly rearing their heads aloft and stretching out their heavily laden arms , -- all made a picture that filled with awe even the heart of rough Bob Lucas , unregistered guide and nominal lumberman , noted for his skill as hunter and poacher and his queer mingling of honesty , law-breaking , piety , and profanity", "context_len": 79}}}, "noted": {"freq": 82, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 78, "word_index": 64, "context": ", adding , `` And most like thou heardest the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to demand her of the Sultan", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "In Portsmouth Miss Weston 's Sailors ' Rest is one of the most noted buildings in the town ; whilst the principle that Jack , who fights our battles at sea , and keeps our country prosperous by his labours aboard ship , needs to be made happy when he is ashore is far more fully acknowledged than it used to be", "context_len": 62}}}, "poacher": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 65, "context": "The white glory of the snow , the stealing pink and gold glances of the sun , the bravery of the trees proudly rearing their heads aloft and stretching out their heavily laden arms , -- all made a picture that filled with awe even the heart of rough Bob Lucas , unregistered guide and nominal lumberman , noted for his skill as hunter and poacher and his queer mingling of honesty , law-breaking , piety , and profanity", "context_len": 79}}}, "mingling": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "It is very strikingly characteristic of the mingling of poetic sentiment and enthusiasm with calm and calculating business efficiency , which shone conspicuously so often in Alexander 's career , that when he arrived at Sestos , and found that the ships were there , and the army safe , and that there was no enemy to oppose his landing on the Asiatic shore , he left Parmenio to conduct the transportation of the troops across the water , while he himself went away in a single galley on an excursion of sentiment and romantic adventure", "context_len": 96}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 70, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}}}, "honesty": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 71, "context": "The white glory of the snow , the stealing pink and gold glances of the sun , the bravery of the trees proudly rearing their heads aloft and stretching out their heavily laden arms , -- all made a picture that filled with awe even the heart of rough Bob Lucas , unregistered guide and nominal lumberman , noted for his skill as hunter and poacher and his queer mingling of honesty , law-breaking , piety , and profanity", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It was a pity that there was not one among them with insight enough to gauge the complacent fellow aright , and at the same time with honesty enough to take him to task for the profitless life he was leading", "context_len": 41}}}, "piety": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` The man accordingly told him of the divine gifts of the holy woman Fatimeh and her piety and the excellence of her devotion ; then , taking him by the hand , he carried him without the city and showed him the way to her abiding-place , which was in a cavern on the top of a little hill ; whereupon the Maugrabin thanked him amain for his kindness [ 636 ] and returned to his place in the Khan", "context_len": 81}}}, "profanity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 78, "context": "The white glory of the snow , the stealing pink and gold glances of the sun , the bravery of the trees proudly rearing their heads aloft and stretching out their heavily laden arms , -- all made a picture that filled with awe even the heart of rough Bob Lucas , unregistered guide and nominal lumberman , noted for his skill as hunter and poacher and his queer mingling of honesty , law-breaking , piety , and profanity", "context_len": 79}}}, "belonged": {"freq": 130, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 130, "word_index": 1, "context": "He belonged to a military company of big boys that had real wooden guns , such as the little boys never could get , and silk oil-cloth caps , and nankeen roundabouts , and white pantaloons with black stripes down the legs ; and once they marched out to a boy 's that had a father that had a farm , and he gave them all a free dinner in an arbor before the house ; bread and butter , and apple-butter , and molasses and pound cake , and peaches and apples ; it was splendid", "context_len": 97}}}, "candlesticks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "When the Sultan saw the jewels ( and indeed , whenas she raised the handkerchief from them , the Divan became as it were all illumined with lamp-clusters and candlesticks ) , he was amazed and confounded at their radiance and fell a-marvelling at their lustre and bigness and beauty ; and [ 370 ] he said , `` Never saw I the like of these jewels for beauty and bigness and perfection , nor methinketh is one of them found in my treasuries", "context_len": 84}}}, "altar": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 33, "context": "When they had burst their pistols or fired off their crackers , the boys sometimes huddled into the back part of the Catholic church and watched the service , awed by the dim altar lights , the rising smoke of incense , and the grimness of the sacristan , an old German , who stood near to keep order among them", "context_len": 61}}}, "vessel": {"freq": 192, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 191, "word_index": 87, "context": "You who are so kindly anxious about my comfort will be pleased to know that I have a very fair amount of wraps , and am more likely to suffer from heat than cold , seeing that my cabin port-hole is never opened , and that the only way of ventilating it is by leaving the door open , -- a thing not to be thought of at night , as ladies \u2019 and gentlemen \u2019 s cabins are not at all in separate parts of the vessel", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The captain made every effort to run the ship in between the Islands and the mainland , but in vain ; and about three o'clock on the morning of the 6th September the vessel struck on the rock with a sickening crash", "context_len": 42}}}, "lounging": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 14, "context": "Troopers were dismounting and leading their horses away to their stables , or were lounging at the doors of the houses where they were quartered , and a crowd of curious country folk and villagers gathered to stare at them , and even to put questions to the more affable-looking of the steel-coated soldiers", "context_len": 54}}}, "stalwart": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "He stood lounging in the cabin door -- red-haired , long-nosed , unkempt , and stalwart", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 80, "context": "Harold , who had been all through the stables of the Duke of Westminster at Eton Hall , had described something much finer than this -- imposing buildings surrounding a courtyard paved with bevel-edged squares of stone , with not so much as a whisp of hay or straw to be seen anywhere , and in the centre a noble statue of a high-spirited horse , rearing and pulling hard at the bridle , held in the hand of a stalwart groom , who seems fully equal to the occasion", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 15, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "sons": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 95, "context": "son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not give them his daughter", "context_len": 128}}}, "build": {"freq": 82, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "From her build and rig my craft would lay closer to the wind than the Gannet , so I ordered her to be kept on the starboard tack for two hours , then on the larboard tack for another two hours , and so on , hoping by these means to keep within sight of our escort when the gale moderated", "context_len": 61}, "VERB": {"freq": 75, "word_index": 85, "context": "his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like , and be it complete with all its requisites of kingly and magnificent furniture and so forth", "context_len": 128}}}, "wagged": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 49, "context": "It was not easy to keep the board from drifting out , but along the shallows it was quiet water and it did not go so very far , and before long , the little dog was able to reach it , crawling upon it and shivering while he wagged his tail feebly as Edna continued to cheer him", "context_len": 59}}}, "intelligence": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 54, "context": "Miss Wauton \u2019 s first impressions of her , jotted down as follows , do not give the impression of a very old lady , dearly as Charlotte Tucker loved to describe herself in those terms : Tall , slight , with lofty brow , sparkling eye , face constantly beaming with love and intelligence ; genius in every look ; figure frail and fairy-like , agile and graceful ; very brisk movements and light tread", "context_len": 76}}}, "creation": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 88, "context": "I am afraid this time her stone was bigger , for it bounced with such effect on his shoulder , that he turned round with a flow of language far from elegant , shaking his fist at the tree with so much fury that Peggy was in terror lest he should climb up to find the author of the assault ; but he evidently thought the day too hot for such exercises , and with a final abusive epithet walked away muttering curses on all the children in creation", "context_len": 89}}}, "togs": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Hello , boys ! '' said the man , stepping inside the cabin ; `` what in the name o ' creation 's this ? I call it a morsel of woman 's togs", "context_len": 35}}}, "aprons": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` I suppose not quite all , '' said Isabel , `` though the boys are to give an Indian dance in costume , and the Dutch dance is in clogs , and oh , you ca n't imagine how funny and clumpy it sounds , but it is real pretty with the aprons and the caps , but the Spanish is beautiful with castanets", "context_len": 65}}}, "strapping": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Why , you young rascal ! '' I exclaimed ; `` what do you know of me that you should speak so ? For less than nothing I 'll give you a strapping and send you to your daddy", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Hallo , here are three Diggers ! '' exclaimed a strapping Queenslander", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He was a strapping middle-aged man in corduroys and cowboy hat with holes punched in it", "context_len": 16}}}, "lads": {"freq": 160, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 146, "word_index": 17, "context": "Dotted about the swelling surface there would be the heads of plenty of swimmers -- men and lads -- some going smoothly along , mounting the rollers as they came in , and descending softly into the hollows ; others again swimming to meet each wave , then rising a little , and with a plunge like a duck or one of the great bronze-black shags , or cormorants , that sat upon the rock-shelves , diving right through the mass of water , to come out fairly on the other side", "context_len": 92}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 31, "context": "Then more of that crowd followed him ; Fischer , too , sprang up , exhausted though he was , and in the end there were at least a dozen sturdy lads swimming with all their might toward the spot where Mallory had been seen to leap", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It took the lads several trips to transfer all their possessions , but at last it was accomplished , and they sat in the new room in the midst of `` confusion worse confounded , '' as Holly Cross remarked when he looked in on them", "context_len": 46}}}, "shirts": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 45, "context": "Some hours later the captain returned , and upon my repeating to him the yarn which I had spun to the mate he not only very readily consented to my working my passage , but also offered me two excellent suits of clothes , two shirts , two pairs of stockings , a pair of shoes , and a worsted cap in exchange for my leopard-skin , which offer I gladly accepted ; and that night found me domiciled in the forecastle of L'Esperance as one of her crew", "context_len": 89}}}, "taller": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 18, "context": "Me got an onion patch , massa , -- my ole woman raise him ; got some bayberry taller , -- Spaniards buy him quick to put in de candle ; make him hard so he no melt", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "ma tch\u012b panat ma h\u00f3ktudshi ( i- ) simm\u00e1his this boy is taller than that girl", "context_len": 16}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "fingering": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "One particularly villainous-looking Boche was apparently advising that he should be shot outright , fingering the trigger of his rifle as if in joyous anticipation of playing the joint r\u00f4le of judge and executioner", "context_len": 34}}}, "cotton": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 21, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "jingo": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` If that fellow , '' continued Corporal Jasper , `` had any reason on earth for getting up at midnight , dodging sentry and running out of barracks , to stay till reveille , except to avoid fighting you that morning , now , by jingo ! I want to know what it is ! The class sent me to ask him , and he simply said he would n't tell , that 's all", "context_len": 76}}}, "gull": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Gingham , you gull , '' interposed his brother , with a guffaw of laughter", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 20, "context": "And , even as he spoke , he realized what a very fool he had been to let this fellow gull him with his talk of a pot of gold ! The gleam of disappointed greed that shone in Astbury 's eyes told what he might have guessed already , that it was no old affection or fidelity that had drawn the man to him , but merely the hope of making money", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The gull brayed like a donkey , `` Ha , ha ha ! ''", "context_len": 14}}}, "guffaw": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Here he gave a loud guffaw , which reached my ears at the further end of the table", "context_len": 19}}}, "fetched": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 22, "context": "Accordingly , [ 387 ] after he had supped , he went into his chamber and locking the door on himself , fetched the lamp and rubbed it ; whereupon the genie at once appeared to him and said , `` Seek what thou wilt , for I am thy slave and the slave of whoso hath in his hand the lamp , I and all the slaves of the lamp", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Well , you better had ' a ' fetched Leroy along , '' commented the man , and I judged that he had small stomach for the work before him", "context_len": 31}}}, "bunk": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` -- And he slapped me in the face , and I kicked his shins good , and then we fit and I give him a punch in the belly and a good bunk in the eye -- ''", "context_len": 39}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Cochran asked you to bunk in his house , to be mess-mates with him and his only boy ? Pro-dig-io-ou-s ! I 'd let any gang of roustabouts knock my head off , close behind the ears , for a gorgeous chance like that", "context_len": 44}}}, "sniff": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "Peggy 's well-meant efforts at entertainment were suddenly interrupted by a loud sniff from the other side of the wall , and , looking up , she saw the round , reproachful face of Polly Smith , a girl of about her own age , who sometimes came up to the Abbey to help Nancy at busy times", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "He had not gone far before he began to sniff", "context_len": 10}}}, "deer": {"freq": 108, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 104, "word_index": 75, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "Abundance of game was there always with the added spice of danger from the dusky hunters who preferred to bag the white invaders in preference to deer , antelope , buffalo , or mountain sheep", "context_len": 35}}}, "beaut": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "She \u2019 s a beaut , Ben , said Roger , admiring the polished , glittering automobile ; but father is queer and won \u2019 t let me drive it", "context_len": 30}}}, "nosed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "It probably would not have been , if the monster had not stopped for an instant and nosed about the log", "context_len": 21}}}, "smote": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 85, "context": "She was above the clock , which was so far up in the air , against the summer sky which was always blue , that it made your neck ache to look up at it ; and the bell was so large that once when my boy was a very little fellow , and was in the belfry with his brother , to see if they could get some of the pigeons that nested there , and the clock began to strike , it almost smote him dead with the terror of its sound , and he felt his heart quiver with the vibration of the air between the strokes", "context_len": 110}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "At last the sweet bells ringing out the time for evening service smote upon the summer air , and the two girls , in their white dresses , started off to walk to the pretty church , which was in reality not far away", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Then he proceeded to look closely till at last he was certified that there was neither trace nor sign left of the palace and knew not what was come of it ; whereupon he redoubled in perplexity and smote hand upon hand and his tears ran down upon his beard , for that he knew not what had befallen his daughter", "context_len": 61}}}, "seizing": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 0, "context": "Seizing the first opportunity of slipping away from his schoolmates , he rushed upstairs to the dormitory with the design of throwing the note under Seabrooke 's bed or bureau , where it might be supposed to have fallen ; but seeing the trunk standing there ready packed , the impulse had taken him to put it in that , and without reflecting -- perhaps hardly caring -- that this would place Seabrooke in a still more embarrassing position , he thrust the note within , as Charlie Henderson saw , and fled from the room", "context_len": 96}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The amount was enormous , both in quantity and value , and the seizing of it was a very magnificent act of plunder", "context_len": 23}}}, "hushed": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "She did not at all want to go , and she trembled as much as ever when the choir sang , and when the place became hushed and people called themselves `` miserable sinners , '' and looked so unconcerned and so well-dressed", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 35, "context": "Did Edric remember the place , and the bloody event which only he and one other present connected with the spot ? -- for Edmund had been far away , and the matter had been hushed up , as far as was possible , by all the power and influence Ethelred could exert in his favourite 's cause , or rather his own , for he , the royal villain , shared the ill-gotten spoil", "context_len": 75}}}, "beaming": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 50, "context": "Miss Wauton \u2019 s first impressions of her , jotted down as follows , do not give the impression of a very old lady , dearly as Charlotte Tucker loved to describe herself in those terms : Tall , slight , with lofty brow , sparkling eye , face constantly beaming with love and intelligence ; genius in every look ; figure frail and fairy-like , agile and graceful ; very brisk movements and light tread", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "She was in a heavy black serge dress , and a voluminous cape which came down nearly to her knees , and she had a black bonnet on , and her face was all beaming and twinkling with affection and sympathy and suppressed happiness", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was a very beaming face that greeted Lucy and the rest of the party when , accompanied by Mr", "context_len": 20}}}, "previous": {"freq": 127, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 127, "word_index": 58, "context": "Naturally this called forth inquiry , and it soon became known that Percy had entrusted to Seabrooke 's keeping a large sum of money , lest he should himself be tempted to spend any portion of it , as it was to be reserved for a special purpose ; that Seabrooke before going to the dinner on the previous evening had put it , as he supposed , in a secure place , and that this morning the money was gone , while he had discovered slight but unmistakable evidence that his quarters had been ransacked in search of it", "context_len": 100}}}, "trunk": {"freq": 174, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 174, "word_index": 27, "context": "But a minute or two later , as we drew nearer the tree , the stench meanwhile becoming almost overpoweringly strong , I detected fastened to the trunk of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may", "context_len": 128}}}, "scratching": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 76, "context": "coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to find the poor creature ; so that , his hair being wet , he was a sight to behold when Harry pulled him out , covered with mud from head to foot , and had to be sent behind in disgrace", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 69, "context": "And now , whether Miss Kitty saw through the crack of the drawer that her young master was fondling a new pet , or whether she only guessed at it , or whether she thought it hard that fun should be going on in which she had no share , can not be told ; but just then there came from her prison-place such a hissing and sputtering and scratching that every one of the children set up a shout of laughter", "context_len": 82}}}, "beating": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 79, "context": "She did not call to me , or announce her return in any way , but set to work to mow a circle of about ten feet in diameter in the long grass ; and then , having produced fire by rubbing two pieces of wood together , she proceeded very carefully to burn off the short grass left inside the circle , setting fire to it , allowing it to burn for a few seconds , and then beating it out again with a branch , in order that the fire might not spread and burn us out , to say nothing of betraying our presence by the smoke that it would raise", "context_len": 114}, "NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 26, "context": "Florence 's cheeks were deeply flushed , her eyes so bright that they looked dark as night ; but Kitty , equally excited , her heart beating , every nerve highly strung , only showed her excitement by a dewy look in the great big grey eyes , and a wild-rose bloom on the delicate cheeks", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` Well , and so it do ! Why , Faith , I 'm no use down yere ; no one wants me , and I h'a n't never a chance as far as this world goes , besides , besides , '' and here Meg pressed her hand upon her beating heart , `` besides , I ha ' a real hankering to see Him", "context_len": 66}}}, "gal": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` Now , sons , '' he said , straightening himself up and drawing on his woollen mittens , `` I 'm goin ' one way an ' you another , but if ye act contrairy an ' pouty to that leetle gal , I 'll know it , for she 's goin ' to write me , an ' if there 's any complaint , there 'll be such a wallopin ' as these ones this mornin ' would be a shadder an ' a dream to", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Do ye know why I 'm givin ' that dog to the little gal stid o ' you ? ''", "context_len": 20}}}, "thunder": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 93, "context": "Very slowly and gradually the storm worked its way toward the zenith , gathering intensity as it rose , and at length -- probably about ten o'clock -- the first drops of rain , hot and heavy , like gouts of blood , began to fall , quickly increasing to a drenching downpour , accompanied by lightning , green , rose-tinted , violet , sun-bright , that lighted up the town until every object , however minute , was as clearly visible as in broad daylight , while the ceaseless crashing of the thunder was unspeakably appalling", "context_len": 97}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "At length Felgate stood up , and , seizing a hammer that was used apparently to drive the wedges into the boot , he strode across to the door and began to thunder a rain of blows upon it", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Was it thunder ? The sky was azure and the sun was shining with dazzling brilliance", "context_len": 16}}}, "merciful": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 18, "context": "Dolly had heard of God before , but not as the kind , loving Father , -- the merciful , gracious Saviour , -- who stands ready to receive all who will turn to Him , who comes after us when we go from Him , and who had now put out His pitying hand to draw to Himself this poor little stricken lamb who had wandered so far from his fold", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Will my Bessie pray that her friend may receive all the help he needs from that great merciful Father ? ''", "context_len": 21}}}, "grindstones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Merciful grindstones , look at him ! ''", "context_len": 8}}}, "wrappings": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 48, "context": "This he opened and pulled out a human finger , the stump being mounted with silver ! For a moment he held it before my eyes ; then , as if too precious to be exposed to the light of day , he carefully replaced it in its wrappings", "context_len": 49}}}, "consisting": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 82, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "But here the Rector again stepped in , and limited the performance to half an hour 's duration , begging them to choose something appropriate for a Sunday-school audience , and if possible with a moral , and further reminding them that a platform consisting of tables hastily pushed together after the tea was concluded would scarcely give facilities for either footlights , drop-scenes , or side-shifts , a curtain being the utmost he could undertake to manage in that direction", "context_len": 81}}}, "contrast": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 23, "context": "But I hope that if , on our return , we bring back all our slaves in good condition and health , the contrast between their appearance and that of the slaves in most other galleys will be so marked that the admiral may consider it would be well to order awnings to be fixed to all the vessels of the Order , and even to grant to all slaves , when away on voyages , the little indulgences I have given them here", "context_len": 84}}}, "imagined": {"freq": 98, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 98, "word_index": 62, "context": "It is true that Alexander 's soldiers , about to leave their homes to go to another quarter of the globe , and into scenes of danger and death from which it was very improbable that many of them would ever return , had no other celestial protection to look up to than the spirits of ancient heroes , who , they imagined , had , somehow or other , found their final home in a sort of heaven among the summits of the mountains , where they reigned , in some sense , over human affairs ; but this , small as it seems to us , was a great deal to them", "context_len": 114}}}, "hound": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 52, "context": "On the third or fourth day of the feast he took him to hunt in a wood where he had prepared an ambuscade , and while all the rest were engaged in the chase , the common hangman of Shrewsbury , one Godwin `` port hund , '' or the town 's hound , bribed by Edric to commit the crime , sprang from behind a bush , and foully assassinated the innocent ealdorman", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Get up , you lazy hound ! It will be worse for you if I have to speak again", "context_len": 19}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` You hound ! '' cried Phil , his voice shrill with rage , and an instant later he had fairly leaped beside the bully", "context_len": 25}}}, "denizen": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "The hound stared inquiringly and politely at Gippie , and , being a denizen of the woods , made the first overtures to friendship by politely touching him with the end of his muzzle", "context_len": 34}}}, "overtures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The hound stared inquiringly and politely at Gippie , and , being a denizen of the woods , made the first overtures to friendship by politely touching him with the end of his muzzle", "context_len": 34}}}, "friendship": {"freq": 95, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "The hound stared inquiringly and politely at Gippie , and , being a denizen of the woods , made the first overtures to friendship by politely touching him with the end of his muzzle", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 46, "context": "People knew the time of their passing , and looked out for them daily ; kindly women would come to cottage doorways to nod and smile ; the inn-keeper at the Halfway Arms set his clock by their arrival ; they had struck up quite a friendship with the postman , two milkmen , and the driver of a fish-cart , while the old man who broke stones by the roadside always nodded and gave them 'Good-morning , ' and fired a facetious remark or two after them , which the children imagined he must relieve the monotony of his work by composing during the intervals of their coming and going", "context_len": 111}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Although Ben had not found Spotty \u2019 s friendship wholly unwelcome , he was now far from pleased by the chap \u2019 s appearance", "context_len": 24}}}, "dignified": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 44, "context": "Belden 's faultless attire and distinguished bearing , had rated him at once as one of those high and mighty Londoners , and had expected that he would of course entrench himself on the back seat of the little turnout and , preserving a dignified silence , condescendingly allow himself to be driven about and to be very much bored into the bargain -- all of which , it must be confessed , would have been more in keeping with Mr", "context_len": 81}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 43, "context": "Charlotte kept up her habit of early rising ; and from eight o \u2019 clock till half-past eight each morning she would take her devotional \u2019 walk in the garden , -- hands folded on chest , head up , step firm and dignified", "context_len": 44}}}, "owners": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 59, "context": "The only pieces of furniture which looked as if they had ever cost much were a large mahogany table with carved feet , which stood between the windows , and a bookcase of the same wood at the side of the fireplace ; but both of these were old-fashioned , and although they might be worth much to their owners , would have brought little if offered for sale", "context_len": 69}}}, "vigorous": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 27, "context": "Again , one of her eager and impulsive temperament could not have been naturally free from a clinging to her own way , and from a certain vigorous self-seeking ; and many a bitter conflict must have been gone through , before friends could , with an all but unanimous voice , speak of hers as a peculiarly unselfish character", "context_len": 60}}}, "restore": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 56, "context": "He ran home with it , and in his simple-hearted rapture he told his mother that as soon as he picked it up there came into his mind the words , `` He who picketh up this cap picketh up a fortune , '' and he could hardly wait for Monday to come and let him restore the cap to its owner and receive an enduring prosperity in reward of his virtue", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He could not , however , restore Clitus to life , nor remove from his own character the indelible stains which such deeds necessarily fixed upon it", "context_len": 27}}}, "benumbed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The smaller dog snapped at him , whereupon the hound withdrew in dignified silence , and watched his owners , who were making vigorous efforts to restore the benumbed girl", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "But , oh , what exquisite torment was mine when , my bonds being released , the blood once more began to circulate through my benumbed members ! I could have screamed aloud with the excruciating agony , had not my pride prevented me ; and it was a full hour before I had sufficiently recovered the use of my hands to enable me to convey food and drink to my lips", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "She was a plain , flaxen-haired girl , with a Teutonic cast of feature and a thin voice ; but every one , benumbed with speechless admiration of her blue silk dress , derived from her performance an impression of surpassing beauty and unbounded talent", "context_len": 45}}}, "chafing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Three minutes later the two came in sight of poor Ki Sing , chafing in his forced captivity and making ineffectual attempts to release himself from his confinement", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "But as it happened , the warmth of the wrapping , the influence of the spirit that had been poured liberally down his throat , and the chafing , combined with his naturally strong animal power to revive him from the state of insensibility into which he had fallen , and long before they reached the granite pier of the little harbour his eyes had opened , and he was staring in a peculiarly puzzled way at Mark Penelly , who still knelt beside him in the double character of medical man and nurse", "context_len": 94}}}, "wrists": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 61, "context": "The handsome house in its brilliant setting of lawns and trees , the wide verandah with its crimson Mount Washington rockers , luxurious hammocks , and low table covered with freshly-cut magazines , the pleasant-faced man who was her nearest of kin , and his graceful wife in a tea-gown of soft summer silk with rich lace about her throat and wrists , her cousins in their dainty muslins , and Russell in his fresh summer suit", "context_len": 77}}}, "stone": {"freq": 262, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 252, "word_index": 64, "context": "For there was something very stirring in the act , and a stranger to the place would hold his breath in dread as he saw Mark Penelly , who was the finest swimmer at the port of Carn Du , climb up the side of the great black rock upon some fine summer evening , then go round along the narrow shelf of shaley stone , till he stood alone there forty feet above the sea , his white figure as he rested against the black rock , every muscle standing out from his well-knit frame , and his arms crossed , looking like some antique statue in its niche", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 15, "context": "At least he could fight his brother , and , when he ran , could stone him ; and he could throw quads and quoins , and pieces of riglet at the jour printers when the story spread to them , and one of them would begin , `` He who picketh -- ''", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Stone went down into the village to purchase a pair of shoes for Jerry , whose footwear was almost wholly gone to the uppers", "context_len": 24}}}, "resuscitation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "And Gippie was after him , for , snorting with rage and mortification at the resuscitation of his rival , he had bounded from under the stove , and , with his maimed tail wagging excitedly in the air , was biting , snapping , growling at Poacher 's heels , nipping him fiercely , if by chance he paused a second to rest", "context_len": 64}}}, "marble": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 24, "context": "He was not so deeply interested in the paper as he pretended to be , for he got up suddenly , stepped to a marble column near Matt \u2019 s chair , and leaned there , still with the cigar between his lips , and the paper in front of his eyes", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 27, "context": "When there were other boys with him he would stop at the door of the shed , where a large , fair German was sawing slabs of marble with a long saw that had no teeth , and that he eased every now and then with water from a sponge he kept by him ; but if the boy was alone , and it was getting at all late in the afternoon , he always ran by the place as fast as he could", "context_len": 84}}}, "bin": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` 'You 'd better take care ; she 'll hear you , ' said Henry , as he heard Aleck 's stifled laughter ; and the next moment , through a crack in the bin , he saw his aunt 's head appearing above the stairs", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` I guess I 'd bin without , with no comfort but the dogs , '' she thought bitterly , and pushing away her plate , she said aloud , `` I thank ye kindly , but I ca n't swaller another morsel", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "You \u2019 ve bin good to my Jimmy , an \u2019 I don \u2019 t forgit nobody who \u2019 s good to him", "context_len": 23}}}, "tramped": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 40, "context": "They tried to induce Fenn to accompany them , but he was firm in his determination to solve the `` turtle mystery , '' as he called it , and , in the end , Bart , Ned and Frank tramped off through the storm , for it was still snowing , while the stout lad remained behind , watching the turtle , which he had placed on a cleared place on the ground in front of the tent", "context_len": 79}}}, "kin": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 40, "context": "One or two college bills , forwarded by Harry Allyn from Oxford , were all there was to it , for , alas ! there were no home letters for Ted in these days of self-imposed exile from kith and kin", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "But see what greater pleasure it would give to my life were you my son , for whom I could lay by such funds as I could well spare , instead of spending all my appointments on myself , and having neither kith nor kin to give a sigh of regret when the news comes that I have fallen in some engagement with the infidels", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "The handsome house in its brilliant setting of lawns and trees , the wide verandah with its crimson Mount Washington rockers , luxurious hammocks , and low table covered with freshly-cut magazines , the pleasant-faced man who was her nearest of kin , and his graceful wife in a tea-gown of soft summer silk with rich lace about her throat and wrists , her cousins in their dainty muslins , and Russell in his fresh summer suit", "context_len": 77}}}, "procession": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 87, "context": "`` `` Nay , '' answered she , `` but I am of this city ; '' and he said to her , `` Thou art of this city and knowest not that this is the night of the going in of the Grand Vizier 's son to the Lady Bedrulbudour , the Sultan 's daughter ? Nay , he is presently in the bath and yonder Amirs and troops are drawn up awaiting him , against he come forth , so they may carry him in procession to the palace of the Sultan 's daughter", "context_len": 96}}}, "squealing": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 43, "context": "A cartful of little pigs drawn up beneath the wall rent the air with their cries , and not the least funny incident was to watch a stout farmer 's wife , despairing of driving her bargain through the crowd , wrap her squealing purchase in her mantle , and carry him off in her arms , like 'Alice ' with the Duchess 's baby", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 83, "context": "Though I was then happily unaware of the fact , the period of the great annual festival , or Customs , was approaching , and the joy of the populace began to find vent in nocturnal concerts inordinately prolonged , the musical instruments consisting of tom-toms , each beaten by two , three , or four performers -- according to the size of the tom- tom -- with a monotony of cadence that soon became positively maddening , further aggravated by the discordant squealing of a number of flageolet- like instruments made of stout reeds", "context_len": 95}}}, "complaining": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "She never visited the Stirling house but she left some sting or sneer of affected superiority behind her , and when the work was done took it home , and the next day sent a note complaining that the handkerchiefs were spoiled , inclosing about one-fifth the usual compensation for such labor", "context_len": 52}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 46, "context": "Should the worst happen , and you are captured by pirates , you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "He did not like them and cried a little for his own `` pitty fock , '' and spoke again in a complaining voice of Faith", "context_len": 26}}}, "despairing": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 26, "context": "So soon as the news of the death of Ethelred travelled abroad , the bishops , abbots , ealdormen , and thanes of southern England , despairing of the cause of the house of Cerdic , met together at Southampton , and renouncing Ethelred and his descendants , elected Canute to be their king , while he swore that both in things spiritual and temporal he would maintain their liberties", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 44, "context": "I did not cry as I said the words ; I was quite past tears that evening , and Jasmine continued to sit near me and to talk in soft tones , and after a time she relapsed into silence , a sort of despairing silence , and I lay with my eyes closed , for I could not look at her , and presently I dropped asleep", "context_len": 68}}}, "lucas": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Lucas and his sons crowded to the window to see their callers depart , and when they were fairly out of sight , they burst into relieved laughter , and noisily drew their boxes up to the fire", "context_len": 38}}}, "contemptuously": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` He carefully cut the apple , which Edna had given him , pared and quartered it , stuck a piece on the end of his knife and offered it to the old man , who pushed it away contemptuously", "context_len": 40}}}, "bullet": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 56, "context": "She looked at the solitary lamp with its grotesquely hideous ornament of red flannel , at Susan 's expressionless , freckled face , at the boys in their copper-toed boots and overalls , at the good-natured , but hopelessly common-place Martha Spriggs , with her thin hair drawn tight into a knob the size of a bullet , and her bare arms akimbo", "context_len": 63}}}, "thar": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Law me , what a question ! Las ' year , leetle gal , thar was awful heavy snow , eight foot deep in Franklin County , seven foot in Somerset , Piscataquis , Penobscot , and Aroostook", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 5, "context": "More \u2019 n that , thar would \u2019 ve been such times in camp that the varmints would have sailed in , and if we \u2019 d managed to stand \u2019 em off , a good many of ye wouldn \u2019 t be left to talk about it this morning", "context_len": 50}}}, "string": {"freq": 93, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 82, "word_index": 53, "context": "Never in my life had I felt more deadly cool and self-possessed than I did then as I aimed steadily at the animal 's right eye ; I felt that I could not miss ; nor did I ; for while we thus stood motionlessly staring at each other , I released the string , and the next instant the great lithe beast sprang convulsively into the air , with the butt of my arrow protruding from his eye and the point buried deep in his brain", "context_len": 87}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 71, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "languor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "A feeling of delicious languor overpowered her , and slowly opening and shutting her eyes , she little by little allowed her surroundings to impress themselves upon her", "context_len": 28}}}, "overpowered": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 83, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Edna was quite overpowered", "context_len": 5}}}, "surroundings": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 26, "context": "Then , going into the ruins , she climbed up on to the old wall , from which the best view of the house and its surroundings could be obtained , and gazed with new eyes at the panorama of her home ; and as her glance travelled slowly from orchard to stackyard , from meadow to garden , back to the ivy-clad tower and the red gabled roof , ' I think , ' she said slowly , 'that if we had to leave the Abbey , it would break my heart ! '", "context_len": 95}}}, "overturned": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Upon an overturned sheaf , sat mammy , her baby on her knee , the little one crowing and laughing , and shaking her dimpled hands , each of which grasped half a dozen ears of wheat , a new and wonderful plaything to baby 's eyes , as they bobbed their heads up and down with the motion", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "A lamp burning in one of the two little upper rooms had been overturned by one of the children very early in the morning , and before the full danger was realized the house was in flames", "context_len": 37}}}, "sup": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 78, "context": "When the damsel saw him , she opened him the privy door and he went in to the Lady Bedrulbudour [ 596 ] and said to her , `` Harkye , I will have thee dress and tire thyself and put away melancholy from thee ; and when the accursed Maugrabin cometh to thee , do thou receive him with 'Welcome and fair welcome ' and go to meet him with a smiling face and bid him come sup with thee and profess to him that thou hast forgotten thy beloved Alaeddin and thy father and that thou lovest him with an exceeding love", "context_len": 104}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "Wherefore it is my hope that thou wilt come to-night , so we may sup together and drink somewhat of wine with each other , and I will have thee let me taste of the wine of thy country Africa , for that belike it is better [ than ours ]", "context_len": 51}}}, "languidly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Here , sup this , '' some one said , and languidly lifting her eyelids , she saw a big red-haired man bending over her", "context_len": 26}}}, "eyelids": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 48, "context": "I suppose that they were the three middle-aged or elderly native women who sat on a bed ; the other five women present , old or young , may have been servants ; but one of them , a handsome girl , with very dashing nose-ring , and eyelids blackened on the edges , native-fashion , shook hands with us as well as served us", "context_len": 65}}}, "sweetened": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "He has relieved my anxieties about you , and his friendship has sweetened the end of a pilgrimage full -- oh , full to overflowing -- of many blessings", "context_len": 29}}}, "molasses": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 84, "context": "He belonged to a military company of big boys that had real wooden guns , such as the little boys never could get , and silk oil-cloth caps , and nankeen roundabouts , and white pantaloons with black stripes down the legs ; and once they marched out to a boy 's that had a father that had a farm , and he gave them all a free dinner in an arbor before the house ; bread and butter , and apple-butter , and molasses and pound cake , and peaches and apples ; it was splendid", "context_len": 97}}}, "grub": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 29, "context": "Of course , admitted the grubs , it is not so absurd if we put ourselves in their places , but who in the world ever imagined that a grub would put itself in the place of a person ? No one would , so why should we do it ? And we don \u2019 t", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It is pleasanter to drive a team of eight horses over cleared land than to grub out dockweed and thistles all alone in one corner", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then it will be grub time", "context_len": 6}}}, "veins": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Once it was a scorpion that fell on the lad , and he was forced to act quickly to kill the creature , before it could inject into his veins the deadly poison of its bite", "context_len": 36}}}, "tether": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Fall to now -- you most got to the end of your tether", "context_len": 14}}}, "disregarding": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 57, "context": "One day I came down to breakfast , exclaiming , How beautiful the snow is ! -- when she told me how pleased she was that I could say so , instead of saying , How cold it is ! When I was ill in the year 1872 , she often came to see me , quite disregarding the infection of my throat ; she would play her guitar to me , or , as I grew better , would patiently guide my little fingers to the right places on the strings", "context_len": 92}}}, "requests": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "'Tilda Jane took the two-pronged fork he put in her hand , and began to eat with slow avidity , not disregarding the requests for titbits from her dog , who occasionally paused for that purpose in his endeavours to lick himself dry", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "She requests , however , that he will continue to direct any letters to S -- -- , to the care of Miss Lanzun", "context_len": 24}}}, "titbits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "'Tilda Jane took the two-pronged fork he put in her hand , and began to eat with slow avidity , not disregarding the requests for titbits from her dog , who occasionally paused for that purpose in his endeavours to lick himself dry", "context_len": 43}}}, "rescuers": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 12, "context": "As the women and children frantic with joy rushed to welcome their rescuers the stern-set faces of the Highlanders changed to joy and gladness ; hunger , thirst , wounds , weariness -- all were forgotten as they clasped hands with those for whom they had fought and bled", "context_len": 49}}}, "death": {"freq": 613, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 613, "word_index": 64, "context": "birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town", "context_len": 128}}}, "lumbermen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "When the cold weather came they had to put on shoes and stockings , or rather long-legged boots , such as are seen now only among lumbermen and teamsters in the country", "context_len": 32}}}, "subtlety": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "With exquisite subtlety he comprehended all that she wished to say in relation to himself , and all that she felt in relation to the dog race in general", "context_len": 29}}}, "comprehended": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` The narrow formalism of Madam Isham 's creed drove Audrey more and more to dwell on the lessons she had loved , but hardly comprehended , and in her solitude she rediscovered for herself the reasonings which had led Sir Gyles Perrient to stand with Eliot and Pym against the encroachments of the Crown", "context_len": 55}}}, "velvet": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 31, "context": "In the very centre of the garden is a grass sward , the greenest grass you ever saw , father , and , oh , so smooth -- as smooth as velvet , and on this grass , lit with fairy lamps , the girls dance all kinds of stately , wonderful , old-fashioned dances , and the neighbors sit round and watch , and then at the end we all go into the house , into the great oak hall in the middle , and Mrs", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Tia , tia , do you hear ? You are a lady of fortune and must have a velvet gown ! And , oh , tia , a tall , silver comb in your hair ! '' She dropped a sudden kiss down upon the smooth , brown bands , and added in a deeper tone , `` But nothing , nothing , can make you better or dearer ! ''", "context_len": 72}}}, "courteous": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 78, "context": "\u2019 [ 63 ] What gross , fearful ignorance ! I heard on good authority that in one place in India , not the Panjab , offerings are actually made to a dead European , who was a special object of dread to the Natives , and whom they therefore wish to propitiate as a kind of demon ! Do not the poor , deluded creatures want teachers ? I find the women in general very gentle and courteous , and quite willing to listen when they are spoken to on the subject of religion", "context_len": 95}}}, "embarrassed": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "The dog stood in courteous silence , until , feeling embarrassed under her attention , he looked somewhat foolishly at his master , and appealingly licked 'Tilda Jane 's cheek", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 51, "context": "When the shout had died away , and the wine cups were emptied , Gervaise , who was sitting on Sir John Kendall 's right hand , would gladly have retained his seat , but the bailiff told him that he must say a few words , and after standing in embarrassed silence for a minute he said , Sir John Kendall , and brother knights , I can only say that I am very sensible of the kindness with which you have been pleased to regard what seems to me after all to have been a very ordinary affair", "context_len": 100}}}, "foolishly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 33, "context": "That Lola should be engaged with mud casas was , indeed , hardly in accord with Jane 's experience of the girl 's dignity ; but that she should be playing ever so foolishly in a slush of clay delighted Jane as being a healthful symptom", "context_len": 46}}}, "appealingly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "The dog stood in courteous silence , until , feeling embarrassed under her attention , he looked somewhat foolishly at his master , and appealingly licked 'Tilda Jane 's cheek", "context_len": 30}}}, "released": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 72, "context": "you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been seized while travelling with a safe conduct from the governor of Syria", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Let our langue look after the wounded , while the released captives clear the decks of the bodies of the fallen pirates", "context_len": 22}}}, "sniffed": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 24, "context": "Bart 's opinion was unexpectedly confirmed the next moment , for the animal turned and uttered a loud `` woof ! '' as it sniffed at the snow at the foot of the bush , evidently in search of something to eat", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "The nose sniffed about in a very suspicious manner , and the eyes wandered from Bob to the group beyond , and then back again to Bob , as if they would ask , `` Who are all these strange people ? Are they friends or foes ? and why have you brought them here ? ''", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "'Oh , of course ! ' sniffed Mrs Harding in high dudgeon ; 'some folks must always have what they cry for", "context_len": 22}}}, "discovering": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 80, "context": "The strong sentiment of love and devotion with which he inspired all around him at the commencement of his career , was now gone , and his generals and officers were continually planning schemes to depose him from the power which he seemed no longer to have the energy to wield ; or , at least , Alexander was continually suspecting that such plans were formed , and he was kept in a continual state of uneasiness and anxiety in discovering and punishing them", "context_len": 84}}}, "rival": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 89, "context": "The only person who was not won over by Archie 's friendly ways and frank American manners was Joe , who gloomily prophesied broken necks and kindred evils as the result of the children 's association with 'the young master from furrin parts ' ; but I fear there was a good deal of jealousy in this , for poor Joe had been a hero to the children in his modest way , and it was hard to find himself suddenly supplanted in their affections , especially by a rival with whom it was quite impossible to compete", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 31, "context": "Mark Penelly had gone over to the other side of the lugger while the conversation was going on , and he did not face the man he looked upon as his rival ; while Harry , unnoticed by the busy fishers as he swam round , went on , touching the sides of the lugger as he lightly swam , but only the next moment to find himself entangled in a quantity of the thin mackerel net , which seemed somehow to descend upon him like a cloud , and before he could realise the fact he was under water , hopelessly fettered by the net , and feeling that if he could not extricate himself directly he should be a dead man", "context_len": 123}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Burgess 's could rival", "context_len": 4}}}, "snapping": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 43, "context": "And Gippie was after him , for , snorting with rage and mortification at the resuscitation of his rival , he had bounded from under the stove , and , with his maimed tail wagging excitedly in the air , was biting , snapping , growling at Poacher 's heels , nipping him fiercely , if by chance he paused a second to rest", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Not that they did not enjoy it when it was cold and snapping , or even snowing , but they could do much more when the weather was milder", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Price 's snapping eyes had already taken her in", "context_len": 9}}}, "averted": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 6, "context": "They probably could not now have averted destruction , but they might , perhaps , have prevented its coming upon them in so terrible a shape as the irruption of thirty thousand frantic and infuriated soldiers through the breaches in their walls to take their city by storm", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "She knew well with what force the blow so long averted had fallen at last", "context_len": 15}}}, "dislodged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "The large dog generously averted his head , and Gippie , seeing that he was not to be dislodged , meanly curled himself up on Poacher 's glossy back", "context_len": 29}}}, "meanly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "He was in an ecstasy as soon as the curtain rose that night , and he lived somewhere out of his body as long as the playing lasted , which was well on to midnight ; for in those days the theatre did not meanly put the public off with one play , but gave it a heartful and its money 's worth with three", "context_len": 65}}}, "glossy": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The large dog generously averted his head , and Gippie , seeing that he was not to be dislodged , meanly curled himself up on Poacher 's glossy back", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 37, "context": "Most of the cows had been sold first thing , and were being driven away with much forcible language on the part of their drovers , and it was now the turn of the cart-horses , beautiful glossy creatures with tails tied up with straw and manes plaited with ribbons as if for May-day", "context_len": 54}}}, "cuter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Search the State from Fort Kent to Kittery Depot , and you 'll not find a cuter", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "However , I have heard hunters say that the wolf is the cutest animal that roams the woods -- cuter even than the fox", "context_len": 24}}}, "pointers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He 's given me pointers many a time -- where you hail from , leetle gal ? ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "hail": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "The first part , We hail thee , has a transition , which we fear that the rules of thorough-bass might not permit ; and the Thistle is hardly equal to either the Shamrock or the Rose , -- of which , you see , I would make a separate song and hymn", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 25, "context": "Our appearance did not at first attract very much attention , or create any very great amount of excitement ; but when we arrived within hail of the beach in front of the town -- upon which were hauled up some three to four hundred canoes of various sizes -- our skipper suddenly sprang to his feet and , placing his hands trumpet-wise to his mouth , began , in a curious , high-pitched voice , to shout a somewhat lengthy communication", "context_len": 82}}}, "unaware": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 5, "context": "Though I was then happily unaware of the fact , the period of the great annual festival , or Customs , was approaching , and the joy of the populace began to find vent in nocturnal concerts inordinately prolonged , the musical instruments consisting of tom-toms , each beaten by two , three , or four performers -- according to the size of the tom- tom -- with a monotony of cadence that soon became positively maddening , further aggravated by the discordant squealing of a number of flageolet- like instruments made of stout reeds", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 38, "context": "Letters were written by the grand master to the Pasha of Syria , to the Emperor of Egypt , and to the Bey of Tunis , offering to ransom the knights , but all replied that they were unaware of any such captives having been landed", "context_len": 46}}}, "poised": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "They readjusted things in the comfortable little phaeton and tucked the lap-robe about them in trimmer fashion , and then the ponies , feeling a tightening grasp on the lines , and intuitively conscious of a whip poised at an easily descending angle , wisely saw fit to make up for lost time", "context_len": 53}}}, "proximity": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 21, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "pictured": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 86, "context": "It is well said that no man is utterly bad , and perhaps he possessed domestic virtues which were thought unworthy of the attention of the chroniclers ; but as they picture him -- now prompting Ethelred to deeds of treachery against the Danes , now joining those Danes themselves , and surpassing them in cruelty -- now seeking pretended reconciliation , only to betray his foe more surely , and in all this aided and supported by the weak , unprincipled king -- as thus pictured there is scarcely a blacker character in history", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "What chords of memory did that face stir in Bomba \u2019 s heart ? What recollections , faint and dim as some far off strains of music , were tugging at his consciousness ? What vague memory told that desolate lad that he was looking at the pictured face of his mother ?", "context_len": 53}}}, "disobedience": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 66, "context": "If at the end of the week you have found out that you can not really obey me -- or , rather , that you will not -- I shall have , reluctantly , to write to your mother and ask her to remove you , for the other girls can not be contaminated with that most fatal of all sins , the sin of direct disobedience", "context_len": 67}}}, "whosoever": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "Then , in batches of ten at a time , forty more victims were triced up to the boughs of this accursed tree by raw-hide ropes fastened to one wrist or one ankle , in such positions that their bodies showed clearly against the bright background of sky ; and , while thus suspended , whosoever would was at liberty to shoot at them with bows and arrows , the great object being , apparently , to pierce the body with as many arrows as possible without inflicting a mortal injury", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "If he had a commandery it would make a vacancy , and give the grand prior , or the grand master , or the council , in whosoever 's gift it might be , an opportunity of rewarding services or of gratifying some powerful family", "context_len": 45}}}, "offend": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 64, "context": "saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting", "context_len": 128}}}, "thyself": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 127, "context": "advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we will set over you guards to guard you ; but do not thou let slip this great honour , for that it hath fallen to none other than thyself", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "When the Lady Bedrulbudour saw this , she cried out and said to him , `` What hath this holy anchoress done , that thou burthenest thyself with the sore burden of her blood ? Hast thou no fear of God , that thou dost this and hast slain Fatimeh , who was a holy woman and whose divine gifts were renowned ? '' Quoth he to her , `` I have not slain Fatimeh ; nay , I have slain him who slew her ; for that this is the brother of the accursed Maugrabin enchanter , who took thee and by his sorcery transported the palace with thee to the land of Africa", "context_len": 115}}}, "sorcerers": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` The soul that sinneth it shall die '' -- '' For whosoever shall keep the whole law , and yet offend in one point , he is guilty of all '' -- '' Keep thyself pure '' -- '' For without are dogs , and sorcerers , and murderers , and idolaters , and whosoever loveth and maketh a lie '' -- that meant without the city , the beautiful city of gold where her mother probably was , and many of her unknown relatives , and where all good matrons , orphans , and `` lady-boards '' went", "context_len": 100}}}, "murderers": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` The soul that sinneth it shall die '' -- '' For whosoever shall keep the whole law , and yet offend in one point , he is guilty of all '' -- '' Keep thyself pure '' -- '' For without are dogs , and sorcerers , and murderers , and idolaters , and whosoever loveth and maketh a lie '' -- that meant without the city , the beautiful city of gold where her mother probably was , and many of her unknown relatives , and where all good matrons , orphans , and `` lady-boards '' went", "context_len": 100}}}, "idolaters": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` The soul that sinneth it shall die '' -- '' For whosoever shall keep the whole law , and yet offend in one point , he is guilty of all '' -- '' Keep thyself pure '' -- '' For without are dogs , and sorcerers , and murderers , and idolaters , and whosoever loveth and maketh a lie '' -- that meant without the city , the beautiful city of gold where her mother probably was , and many of her unknown relatives , and where all good matrons , orphans , and `` lady-boards '' went", "context_len": 100}}}, "matrons": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 91, "context": "`` The soul that sinneth it shall die '' -- '' For whosoever shall keep the whole law , and yet offend in one point , he is guilty of all '' -- '' Keep thyself pure '' -- '' For without are dogs , and sorcerers , and murderers , and idolaters , and whosoever loveth and maketh a lie '' -- that meant without the city , the beautiful city of gold where her mother probably was , and many of her unknown relatives , and where all good matrons , orphans , and `` lady-boards '' went", "context_len": 100}}}, "saluted": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 68, "context": "So she looked out and seeing her lord Alaeddin sitting under the windows of the pavilion , said to the princess , `` O my lady , my lady , here is my lord Alaeddin sitting under the pavilion ! '' Whereupon the Lady Bedrulbudour arose in haste and looking from the window , saw Alaeddin , and he raised his head and saw her ; so she saluted him and he her and they were both like to fly for joy", "context_len": 82}}}, "scented": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 5, "context": "Gippie 's inquiring muzzle had scented out the bacon and had seized it , whereupon Poacher , knowing that it was not intended for him , had gently but firmly taken it from him , and was walking about the cabin , holding it aloft , while Gippie snarled at his heels", "context_len": 52}}}, "snarled": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 16, "context": "How dare that fellow come here and talk to me in such a fashion ! he snarled , pacing the floor of his room ; how dare he ! So he \u2019 s going to stand by Stone at any cost ! Judging by what I \u2019 ve heard about him , he \u2019 s just mule enough to do it , too", "context_len": 63}}}, "destiny": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "The problem seemed absolutely insoluble ; and at length I came to the conclusion that the only thing to be done was to leave the issue to destiny", "context_len": 28}}}, "consideration": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 94, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Where does consideration fall in your case ? ''", "context_len": 9}}}, "avoided": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 53, "context": "The folk and the people of the city marvelled at this strange thing , especially when they saw the Vizier and his son go forth the palace in a pitiable plight for stress of chagrin and despite , and they fell to asking , `` What hath happened and why is the marriage avoided and the rejoicings broken off ? '' But none knew what was to do save Alaeddin , the suitor , [ 419 ] who laughed in his sleeve", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "There is some fine , old furniture there and it is a wonder everything in the house has not been stolen before now , but as the place has the reputation of being haunted it has been more or less avoided", "context_len": 41}}}, "blab": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Some one that wo n't blab , an ' that I kin tell a story to", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Look you , Ned Crocker , have ye at any time been unfairly done by ? No ? Then why did ye blab on the run we made nigh Dancing Ledge , when Thompson , John Light , and Long Will of Corfe were taken ? ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "sofas": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "She was not cross with me , for she was smiling kindly , so I followed her along the veranda to the front of the house where she went lightly into the living room and began to tidy chairs , tables and sofas", "context_len": 43}}}, "pianos": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "She recalled certain rumors long afloat in town as to Jane 's extravagance , and the inability of her means to such luxuries as pianos", "context_len": 25}}}, "apologetically": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "I didn \u2019 t hear what had happened to you until intermission time , Stone , said the football captain apologetically ; if I had , you \u2019 d seen me before this", "context_len": 33}}}, "den": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 12, "context": "' An ' it seemed I 'd come to the bear 's den , an ' there was a soft bed , an ' I just lay down , an ' was goin ' to sleep when I thought , 'Mebbe if I sleep , some little bird 'll tell him I 'm a baddie , an ' he 'll eat me up , ' an ' I felt just awful ; then I forgot everythin ' till I woke up here -- I guess I 'm obliged to you", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "Ef you say I said it , den I reckon I must ' a ' said it , but I wa n't at myse ' f , kaze ef dey ever wuz a grown man on top er de groun ' , dat chap is one", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Say , what do you want to make a rough house for ? Is n't this den bad enough as it is , without you doing that ? ''", "context_len": 30}}}, "invite": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "We 'll be happy to have you an ' your little dog , an ' I 'll not let no one eat him , an ' I 'll give a big party an ' invite all the foxes , an ' deer , an ' bears an ' squirrels 'cause you 're fond o ' wild beasts , little girl", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 80, "context": "Of course they were all exceedingly anxious to hear the story of what had befallen me since the Dolphin and the Eros had parted company ; but I steadfastly refused to tell them anything beyond the bare fact that the Dolphin had gone down with all hands , explaining that the skipper had invited me to dine with him that day , and that they would learn all particulars then , as I gathered that it was his intention to invite them all to meet me", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "As some slight amends for his disappointment , Lilian proposed that they should have a grand housewarming at Sky Cottage on Saturday afternoon , and invite Father to tea in the sanctum", "context_len": 32}}}, "reply": {"freq": 224, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 40, "context": "He was furious with himself for having fallen into the trap ; if he had , as he said to himself , lain off the beach in the boat , and questioned the supposed shipwrecked sailors , their inability to reply to him would have at once put him on his guard ; as it was , he had walked into the snare as carelessly and confidently as a child might have done", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 188, "word_index": 71, "context": "[ 358 ] Hast thou any knowledge of her , O Vizier , and knowest thou what is her want ? '' `` O our lord the Sultan , '' replied the Vizier , `` verily women are little of wit ; and most like this woman cometh to complain to thee of her husband or one of her folk , '' The Sultan was not content with the Vizier 's reply , but bade him , an she came again to the Divan , bring her before him forthright ; [ 359 ] whereupon the Vizier laid his hand on his head and answered , `` Hearkening and obedience , O our lord the Sultan", "context_len": 116}}}, "warden": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Game warden Perch , '' he said , dryly , `` and registered guide Hersey", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Get your own find out , '' said the warden , severely ; `` it will keep you out of mischief , and look here -- if I find that dog of yours up to tricks , you know what I 'll do", "context_len": 44}}}, "registered": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "Each face lit up at his appeal , and I do not think there was one but silently registered a vow to do all in his power to prove himself worthy of the confidence placed in him and his companions by the grand master and admiral", "context_len": 46}}}, "inhospitably": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Come in , '' bawled Lucas , not inhospitably , and two men , much smarter , cleaner , and more dapper-looking than the red-haired man and his sons , entered the cabin", "context_len": 34}}}, "guns": {"freq": 226, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 224, "word_index": 97, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Not a shot was fired from the Anzac trenches -- although dozens of Maxims , Lewis guns , and rifles were ready to receive Fritz in the strictly conventional way -- until the foremost of the serried grey-clad masses drew within seven hundred yards", "context_len": 44}}}, "boxes": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 52, "context": "They had a travelling uncle who came home to them at last ; but he staid away ten years and did not come home even to see his mother , and I think he ort to do n't you ? But now he is come and has brought so many trunks and boxes with such lots and lots of things and kurositys in them that he is 'most like a Norz ' Ark only better , and his gret coat and cap are made of the bears ' skins he shot and he tells us about the tigers and lions and I do n't like it and Fred and Harry do and Bessie do n't too", "context_len": 116}}}, "unmitigated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "Leacraft as he had with the boys , insisting that the whole affair , the abstraction of the money and the placing it in Seabrooke 's trunk , was `` only a joke ; '' but the doctor altogether refused to look upon it in any other light than that of an unmitigated theft and an atrocious attempt to fasten it upon another when he feared detection for himself", "context_len": 69}}}, "fixing": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 5, "context": "Suddenly Sobrinini paused , and fixing Bomba with a strange intent gaze , sang in a voice that had magically lost most of its raucous quality and for the moment had become the faint , sad echo of something that had been supremely beautiful -- sang a tender , haunting melody that touched some almost forgotten memory in Bomba \u2019 s heart and filled him with an exquisite pain", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "He took the screws out , one by one , that held the fixing frail , Till all that stood 'twixt him and Sin was but a single nail", "context_len": 29}}}, "piercing": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 17, "context": "Yet , although in appearance a very Methuselah in age , this individual had a pair of piercing black eyes that glowed and sparkled with all the fire and passion of early manhood , and , bowed as he was , and decrepit as he appeared to be , he tottered across the intervening space with extraordinary agility , and seated himself in the second chair", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "The piercing cry , `` Help ! Edmund , the king , is slain ! '' roused the household -- Elfwyn , Herstan , Hermann , the ladies , agitated beyond measure ; the household guard ; and , last of all , Edric", "context_len": 44}}}, "keeled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` When that ole man keeled over my dog , '' she said , brokenly , `` that dog as had saved my life , I wanted murder", "context_len": 28}}}, "surveying": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 24, "context": "Meantime , a party of tourists , with Marshall 's familiar pink guide-hook open in the hands of one of them , had been surveying the chapel at a distance , and now , after a word or two with the children on the doorstep , made their way within", "context_len": 50}}}, "newcomers": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 33, "context": "It was certainly delightful to be there in the cool spacious house with the noise of the sea ever in their ears and the cool breeze coming in the windows , and the newcomers felt that all this accounted for the better color in Miss Eloise 's cheeks and for the brighter look in her eyes", "context_len": 56}}}, "sneer": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 12, "context": "She never visited the Stirling house but she left some sting or sneer of affected superiority behind her , and when the work was done took it home , and the next day sent a note complaining that the handkerchiefs were spoiled , inclosing about one-fifth the usual compensation for such labor", "context_len": 52}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "I have refused to press it , for which Hayden has been pleased to sneer at me", "context_len": 17}}}, "tip": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 60, "context": "Perhaps Maggie and Fred might not have kept their plots in such good order , or worked so perseveringly at weeding , raking , and watering , had it not been for Harry and Bessie , who never forgot to go each day and look at their plants , and never suffered a weed to do more than show the tip of its head above the soil", "context_len": 67}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 41, "context": "He could make such a noise , however , that he effectually banished slumber ; he twisted and turned , he bit at the wickerwork , and scratched with his claws , and after ten minutes of much commotion managed to tip over the basket and crawl out in triumph , to renew his attacks upon the shoes and other property of the despairing girls", "context_len": 65}}}, "imperturbably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Hev some breakfus ? '' said Lucas , who was imperturbably going on with his own", "context_len": 17}}}, "inspected": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 29, "context": "He felt pleased and encouraged , for the superintendent 's cast-iron visage had softened considerably , and he manifested unmistakable interest as he reached out and took up and inspected the neatly formulated memoranda on the packing-box desk", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The Arab , without replying , again inspected Gervaise", "context_len": 9}}}, "retort": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 9, "context": "If she could only have thought of a suitable retort , she would have said something stinging ; but usually our smartest remarks occur to us when the occasion has long passed by , and perhaps it is all the better , for we are saved from bitter words , which , once spoken , are not easily forgotten by the hearers , however keenly we may regret them ourselves", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Before Helen could retort , Tommy jumped up with a shout of defiance , and snatching up two balls that lay ready to his hand , discharged them right into the centre of a bush a few yards off", "context_len": 39}}}, "commissioner": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "Instead , a mob of women and children followed Sir Thomas at a distance , reviling and cursing the king , the commissioner , and the navy in general by reason of the non-payment of their husbands \u2019 and fathers \u2019 wages", "context_len": 42}}}, "moose": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Wal , I 'll tell you , '' said Lucas , taking two moose ear skins from his pocket , and fitting them together to make a tobacco-pouch , `` them two is fancy game men", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "He was so dead beat that he jus ' stood an ' trembled , an ' one o ' Winthrop 's men put a halter on him , an ' led him to the barnyard an ' give him fodder an ' drink , an ' that livin ' young moose is in Winthrop 's park to-day , an ' he weighs four hundred pound", "context_len": 65}}}, "poachers": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Yes , we 're poachers , leetle gal , an ' when I ask ye to say nothin ' about what ye 've seen an ' heard here , I know ye 'll keep as mum as we do", "context_len": 40}}}, "fellows": {"freq": 443, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 316, "word_index": 60, "context": "For instance , if you had been beguiled , as a little boy , into being the last in the game of snap-the-whip , and the snap sent you rolling head over heels on the hard ground , and skinned your nose and tore your trousers , you could cry from the pain without disgrace , and some of the fellows would come up and try to comfort you ; but you were bound in honor not to appeal to the teacher , and you were expected to use every device to get the blood off you before you went in , and to hide the tear in your trousers", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "My boy could dimly recall the day when the water was first let into the Hydraulic , and the little fellows ran along its sides to keep abreast of the current , as they easily could ; and he could see more vividly the tumult which a break in the embankment of the Little Reservoir caused", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 121, "word_index": 55, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "layers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "Davenport 's remarks , which had been wafted along in gusts , died away in the distance as she departed down the pasture to inspect the hen-coops , and hint broadly for the gift of a couple of young pullets , 'for yours are such an excellent breed , dear Miss Vaughan , and such capital winter layers", "context_len": 58}}}, "bagging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then they put them up so sly in three layers of bagging with nothing but bears ' feet sticking out , but I caught on to those bears ' legs , and said the feet were n't big enough", "context_len": 39}}}, "forty": {"freq": 126, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 51, "word_index": 68, "context": "When I see five or six boys now lying under a tree on the grass , and they fall silent as I pass them , I have no right to say that they are not arranging to go and carry some poor widow 's winter wood into her shed and pile it neatly up for her , and wish to keep it a secret from everybody ; but forty years ago I should have had good reason for thinking that they were debating how to tie a piece of her clothes-line along the ground so that when her orphan boy came out for an armload of wood after dark , he would trip on it and send his wood flying all over the yard", "context_len": 124}, "VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 67, "context": "But , anyhow , when a boy had a coon , he had to have a store-box turned open side down to keep it in , behind the house ; and he had to have a little door in the box to pull the coon out through when he wanted to show it to other boys , or to look at it himself , which he did forty or fifty times a day , when he first got it", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 79, "context": "As Ama , taking her bow and arrows , tripped lightly away toward the forest , I proceeded to shin up the tree , and presently , after some labour , found myself among its topmost branches , which towered high above those of all the other trees in the neighbourhood , and -- it being by this time broad daylight -- obtained a magnificent view of the surrounding country , extending for , as I estimated , some forty miles toward the south and east , while toward the north and west the view was shut off by high hills , through which the river wound its way", "context_len": 109}}}, "apiece": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 27, "context": "There were two bakeries , and at the American bakery there were small sponge-cakes , which were the nicest cakes in the world , for a cent apiece ; at the Dutch bakery there were pretzels , with salt and ashes sticking on them , that the Dutch boys liked ; but the American boys made fun of them , and the bread at the Dutch bakery was always sour", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 24, "context": "Jennie wormed her way through the crowd with surprising agility and reached her two friends who had just bought five cents worth of panuchee apiece", "context_len": 25}}}, "suspected": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 6, "context": "He never let on that he suspected for a moment that he was not entirely alone , but , walking over to a tree stump , where , spread out on a newspaper , was the remains of a lunch , he acted delighted at the discovery , picked up a hunk of bread in one hand , a piece of cheese in the other , and , throwing himself on the green sward at full length , proceeded to munch the eatables , with every semblance of satisfaction", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Jones and obtained admission to the room of the suspected lad , only to discover that the room was empty , and , from indications , that Ben and his brother had made hasty flight", "context_len": 35}}}, "preservers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Ever mindful of the inestimable value of the mail treasure , he adjusted them with much care about his neck , somewhat as if they were life preservers , and holding his light rifle in his hand , he stepped cautiously into the current like an elephant venturing upon a rickety bridge", "context_len": 52}}}, "callers": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 31, "context": "Perhaps it was the natural reaction arising from this very unwonted state of affairs , or merely the desire to keep as much as possible out of the way of afternoon callers , that put it into Peggy 's head one close , sultry afternoon that they should go and explore a cave which lay on the river at no great distance from the Abbey meadows", "context_len": 66}}}, "branching": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "There were saw-logs and whole trees with their branching tops , lengths of fence and hen-coops and pig-pens ; once there was a stable ; and if the flood continued , there began to come swollen bodies of horses and cattle", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "And older people than Donald have thought the selfsame thing , as they have looked down the same matchless avenue , with its wonderful far-reaching vista of branching elms , and its perfect driveway diminishing to a thread in the distance , with here and there a flock of grazing sheep roaming its ample grass-grown borders , and finding rich and abundant pasture", "context_len": 63}}}, "horns": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 64, "context": "Guy had witnessed three or four such falls , and in his excitement scarcely knowing what he did , went up to the foaming animal intending to put it out of its misery by a shot from his gun , when , suddenly , it rose to its feet , staggered forward , and ere Guy could wheel his frightened horse , plunged his horns into his breast , and buffalo , horse and rider rolled upon the plain together", "context_len": 80}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "With one sweep of his sharp horns the beast swept the lad aside , knocking him down", "context_len": 17}}}, "confederate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "He had promised his confederate , Jones , a thousand dollars as the price of his information and co-operation , but intended all the while to avoid paying it if it were a possible thing", "context_len": 35}}}, "nabbed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` Yes , sir , '' she said , calmly , `` I 'm a-travellin ' , but I ai n't got no money to pay for me nor for this dog , an ' his head an ' tail shows this time , an ' he 'll be nabbed", "context_len": 50}}}, "slide": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 17, "context": "At first they had all floundered hopelessly about , and it had taken some practice even to slide a few steps ; but long before they had reached the stage of skimming over the frozen surface at the rate of eight or ten miles an hour , which had been their fond ambition , the snow had melted into moist and dirty slush , which was particularly trying , as they had just decided to make a sledge out of an old packing-case , and drag Lilian round the pasture", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "Of course , it was not very nice for the backs of boots and knickerbockers , and frocks and pinafores were apt to get sadly torn if they caught on a projecting angle ; but what child ever thought of clothes when a twenty-foot slide might be enjoyed ? Certainly not Peggy or Bobby , whose well-worn garments were generally made of the stoutest and most serviceable materials", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I thought rapidly , and an idea occurred to me which caused me to close my glass , re-sling it , and slide down to the cross-trees", "context_len": 27}}}, "hounds": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "And I do swear by the bones of my ancestors , by the hounds of Diana , forsooth even by Jupiter lapis and the Gemini , that never while I inspire the atmosphere of existence will I submit myself to so outrageous an imposition -- -- ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "dogging": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I wondered why you stopped dogging or perhaps I should say ponying my footsteps -- -- Now we 'll play the rising tune , '' and going to the hall she took a bugle from a hook and began a lovely tune , first soft and low , then high and piercing", "context_len": 53}}}, "horrified": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "Both uttered an exclamation of horrified surprise , not knowing at that stage if they were hurtling into a deep abyss to be dashed to pieces at the bottom", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 64, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}}}, "mysteries": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 26, "context": "Sometimes Lilian would join them , but more often she was busy indoors , helping her aunt and Nancy , the maid , and learning the mysteries of housekeeping and dairy-minding ; for she was growing quite a nice little companion to Aunt Helen , and becoming so useful that Nancy declared they should scarcely know what to do without her when the term began again", "context_len": 66}}}, "inquire": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 93, "context": "It was all very well for her to tell me that I must cure him ; but suppose that I could not , suppose that the sickness -- whatever it might be -- should run its course and fail to yield to my treatment , what then ? The reason , so urgently expressed by her , why I must effect a cure -- '' for your own sake , Dick , '' -- was significant enough of the direction in which her apprehensions pointed ; there was no necessity for me to inquire what she meant", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "When you land tomorrow , inquire for the house of Isaac Ben Ibyn", "context_len": 13}}}, "timidly": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 41, "context": "So Aunt Patty sat , and sadly thought , unconscious of the wistful pair of eyes which watched her from the other room , until by and by a gentle footstep came stealing round her chair , a soft little hand timidly slipped itself into her own , and she turned to see Bessie 's sweet face looking at her , half in pity , half in wonder", "context_len": 68}}}, "eyed": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 1, "context": "Wentworth eyed Gerald , doubting whether he should not seize him then and there and wrest from him the paper if he proved to have it , but there was something in the resolute look of the boy that daunted him , man though he was , and he decided that it would be better to have recourse to a little strategy", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "She always staid out two hours , more when it was pleasant , and brought back the babies , rosy and bright eyed", "context_len": 23}}}, "peculiarly": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 73, "context": "But as it happened , the warmth of the wrapping , the influence of the spirit that had been poured liberally down his throat , and the chafing , combined with his naturally strong animal power to revive him from the state of insensibility into which he had fallen , and long before they reached the granite pier of the little harbour his eyes had opened , and he was staring in a peculiarly puzzled way at Mark Penelly , who still knelt beside him in the double character of medical man and nurse", "context_len": 94}}}, "offensive": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 18, "context": "Then Edmund retired into Wessex to raise more troops , and during his absence the Danes took the offensive again , once more besieging London in vain , while they harried all the neighbouring districts until Edmund returned with a large army , drove them into Kent , and gave them such a fearful defeat at Otford that they fled in despair to the Isle of Sheppey , and all men said Edmund would have destroyed them utterly , had not Edric persuaded him to stop the pursuit at Aylesford", "context_len": 90}}}, "regard": {"freq": 186, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 126, "word_index": 7, "context": "Withal , O my son , of regard for thy wish , needs must I take courage and go ; but , O my son , if the King receive me and honour me for the gift 's sake and I seek of him that which thou wilt in [ 343 ] the matter of marrying his daughter and he ask me , after the wont of the folk , what are thy possessions and thy revenues , what shall I say to him ? And most like , O my son , he will ask me of this ere he ask me of thyself", "context_len": 105}, "VERB": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 86, "context": "When the shout had died away , and the wine cups were emptied , Gervaise , who was sitting on Sir John Kendall 's right hand , would gladly have retained his seat , but the bailiff told him that he must say a few words , and after standing in embarrassed silence for a minute he said , Sir John Kendall , and brother knights , I can only say that I am very sensible of the kindness with which you have been pleased to regard what seems to me after all to have been a very ordinary affair", "context_len": 100}}}, "calculate": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 5, "context": "Many will , we may calculate , jump overboard and swim ashore rather than face us , and we shall be able , at any rate , to capture three or four of their craft , for , as they come out , one by one , we can all close round them ; and with nearly fifty knights , ninety released captives , burning for vengeance , and some fifty or sixty fishermen , for those from the fire ships will , of course , join us -- we shall make short work of them , and may even hope to entirely destroy their fleet", "context_len": 106}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "'We calculate to work right down the row in due course , and we 'll take you all in turn", "context_len": 20}}}, "coolly": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 7, "context": "'Ca n't , ' said the boy coolly , ' I 'm goin ' ter tie it to Poll 's balloon , an ' let go of the string , an ' then it 'll go straight to heaven , ' and , with the letter reposing in his cheek , he began to sing vociferously : --", "context_len": 58}}}, "lumber": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 70, "context": "Dad put an axe into my hand , and taught me to chop kindling before I was out of petticoats , and when we went up the Rockies shooting grizzlies we built log-cabins , and I can tell you there was no carpenter to fetch there nearer than a hundred miles , so I guess I ought to know how to handle a saw and fix up a bit of lumber", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` No , '' replied William Perry , `` I 'm working for a lumber company , and I 'm on my way from one camp to another", "context_len": 28}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "If the fire reached the house it would not only burn that , but would run to the beach , where was lumber worth hundreds of dollars , which Ben had been nearly two years in preparing for market , -- the greater part of which was dry , and would take fire in a moment ; there , too , were the sails and rigging", "context_len": 66}}}, "camped": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Was late in startin ' last night , an ' camped here", "context_len": 12}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 6, "context": "It fretted him sorely when they camped at night , to see how hard she must have worked while he lay ill ; yet he could but perceive that she looked better and stronger than she had done since his father 's death , and joyfully felt that the excitement and toil of a journey across the plains would restore his mother to health , whatever might be the effect upon him", "context_len": 72}}}, "undertake": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 12, "context": "Put away from thee this extravagance : [ 326 ] who shall undertake an affair like this and demand it of the Sultan ? Meknoweth not how thou wilt do to make this request of the Sultan , and if thou speak sooth , [ 327 ] by whom wilt thou make it ? '' `` O my mother , '' rejoined Alaeddin , `` by whom [ should I make ] a request like this , when thou art at hand , and whom have I trustier [ 328 ] than thyself ? Wherefore my intent is that thou shalt make this request for me", "context_len": 106}}}, "mischief": {"freq": 110, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 102, "word_index": 104, "context": "Should she give Dolly her own or not ? She wanted it very much herself ; but she had asked her Father in heaven to let her find some way to be kind to Lem and Dolly , and now was He not answering her prayer ? It had been very pleasant to think of sharing the delicious fruit with her own dear friends whom she loved so much , or even of giving a piece to Starr , who was always so kind and good to her ; but to give it all to this bad girl who had done so much cruel mischief to her and hers , was another thing", "context_len": 113}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "A fellow would be very good at home , really and truly good , and as soon as he got out with the other fellows he would yield to almost any temptation to mischief that offered , and if none offered he would go and hunt one up , and would never stop till he had found one , and kept at it till it overcame him", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "It \u2019 s dem dat will flop over like a buckwheat cake de minute dey fires , reflected Jethro , but how de mischief ken I know which way de rapscallions will turn ? Mos \u2019 folks am right-handed , and I guess dat \u2019 s de way this sarpint will flop", "context_len": 52}}}, "patting": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 45, "context": "I want to stay in the game and at practice as long as I can , for when I drop out , and a new quarter-back comes in , it 's bound to throw the playing off the least bit , and I 'm not patting myself on the back when I say that , I hope", "context_len": 57}}}, "hey": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Look here , sir , who told you to be a Mormon ? You 're not a Mormon ? Got only one wife , hey ? None ? Then what sort of a Mormon are you ? Why have you got a Mormon 's name ? Did you steal it ? Do n't you know who Joseph Smith was ? No ? Not you , the great Joseph Smith ! Suppose you think you 're the great Joseph Smith", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I 'spose you 'll let me go and arrange it , hey ? ''", "context_len": 15}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "But we 'll just parcel 'em one at a time just enough to keep him worried , hey ? ''", "context_len": 20}}}, "scowl": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 17, "context": "Here Seabrooke 's hand , which lay upon his desk , clenched itself , and a dark scowl passed over his face , while Percy glanced over at him with suspicion and resentment written on every feature , and a battery of eyes turned in his direction , not one among them with friendly look for himself", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "They made no secret of the fact that my inquisitive glances were unwelcome , and so much did they scowl that I turned hastily away and looked out of the window , where the brilliant sunshine , beating down on the crowded courtyard , made a pleasing contrast to the dismal trio within", "context_len": 53}}}, "slamming": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "Florence swept up her papers , thrust them into her desk , and abruptly left the room , slamming the door after her", "context_len": 23}}}, "noisily": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 29, "context": "And when it was too late he found that the desertion of the fort was only a cleverly-planned trick on the part of its defender , who had crashed noisily into the bushes , in the hope of deceiving the attacking party into the belief that the fort was empty", "context_len": 50}}}, "fasten": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 20, "context": "They had just reached the side of a little incline when Peggy 's bootlace , which she had neglected to fasten all the morning , tripped her up , and over she went , rolling into a prickly gorse-bush , while Bobby , who was so close behind that he could not stop himself , fell over her , and collapsed into a boggy hollow , where he lay panting for breath until Peggy picked herself up and hauled him out", "context_len": 81}}}, "protectors": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Why should not other middle-aged ladies go out , as she went out ? -- not necessarily always to attempt full Zenana work ; but to be protectors , housekeepers , nurses , to younger and more active ladies ? Whether it would be right to use any portion of Mission-funds for such a purpose may be doubted ; and in many a case Mission rooms could not be spared ; but there are exceptions as to the latter", "context_len": 79}}}, "skins": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 88, "context": "They had a travelling uncle who came home to them at last ; but he staid away ten years and did not come home even to see his mother , and I think he ort to do n't you ? But now he is come and has brought so many trunks and boxes with such lots and lots of things and kurositys in them that he is 'most like a Norz ' Ark only better , and his gret coat and cap are made of the bears ' skins he shot and he tells us about the tigers and lions and I do n't like it and Fred and Harry do and Bessie do n't too", "context_len": 116}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "But I do want to say this -- watch out that nobody tries any foul play on him , skins him on demerits or reports him unfairly", "context_len": 27}}}, "lounge": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "The actors have come out of the dressing-room door , some in their everyday clothes , and some with just overcoats on over their circus-dresses , and they lounge about near the band-stand watching the performance in the ring", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 47, "context": "The horses pranced , swung a little to one side and stopped short , and I heard some one say , 'So , Barney , so Pet ! ' I did n't know what happened next but the first thing I knew I was lying on the lounge in the sitting-room , my mother bending over me , and holding a bottle of salts to my nose , 'Oh , dear , oh , dear , ' my mother was crying , 'another minute and the child might have been killed", "context_len": 91}}}, "rule": {"freq": 118, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 105, "word_index": 6, "context": "I am much better as a rule ; but it came over me in church how proper people were , and they all of them talk about being miserable sinners , and every one looks so good and righteous , and knowing down deep in their hearts that every single one of them is a miserable sinner , except your darling , precious self ; and they all repeat the words , not feeling them a bit", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 4, "context": "Whether the powers that rule the entrance to the same came to the conclusion that to a little girl of twelve and a little boy of four the term of general public could not honestly be applied , or whether all rules of procedure and precedence were magnanimously waived in their favor , certain it is that the little card in question bore the incredible inscription : Admit Master Albert Allyn and his little friend , Miss Marie-Celeste Harris , to the East Terrace between the hours of twelve and three on Thursday", "context_len": 93}}}, "killing": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 64, "context": "said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? He that can in so short a time avail unto riches like these , is he not worthy to be the Sultan 's son-in-law and to have his daughter to bride ? '' Now the Vizier marvelled at the greatness of these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about to work upon him by practice , that he", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "Sergeant David Stanlaw , stationed at Spirit River , is puzzled by a local killing , the disappearance of the body , the finding of a code message , and by the mystery of the Listening Forest , which casts a shadow of dread over the little town of Wiggin \u2019 s Creek", "context_len": 53}}}, "falteringly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Are there many men killing deer ? '' asked the little girl , falteringly", "context_len": 15}}}, "camps": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 54, "context": "Frank was inclined to blame himself , and , after that , Fenn , because the latter had put the soap powder into the cocoanut box , but the amateur cook 's chums were good-natured over his failure , and comforted him with the proverb `` accidents will happen in the best of regulated camps", "context_len": 55}}}, "smacking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "If the girl lived , and was going about saying that he had killed her dog , here was proof positive that he had not ; and smacking his lips , and making a clicking sound with his tongue , he tried to attract the resuscitated Poacher 's attention", "context_len": 49}}}, "dissatisfaction": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` 'Their children , nevertheless , were eaten by the Earth , so that , full of dissatisfaction , they journeyed toward the sunrise", "context_len": 24}}}, "reminiscences": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 90, "context": "Then , too , it was an open secret that she was Sir John 's favorite ; it had been whispered by more than one visitor to another that it was on account of Kitty Sharston that this great fuss had been made , that the Scholarship had been opened to the competition of the school , that the girls were here , that they themselves were here -- it was all on account of this slim little girl with the big eyes and the sweet pathetic face ; and reminiscences of Sir John and Kitty 's father together side by side , shoulder to shoulder , in the trenches before Sebastopol arose in the memory of one or two visitors present", "context_len": 122}}}, "starved": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 51, "context": "How well I remember going to see her shortly after poor father died , and how she used to make you cry , and how cold she always was , and what miserable tea she gave us ! We had better ask her to a meal unless we want to be starved , Mummy , dear", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "But what did it all matter while she was living in this wonderful mysterious story ? It was more romantic and delightful than anything she had ever invented to comfort her starved young soul and save herself from despair", "context_len": 39}}}, "flurry": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 68, "context": "She was very resolute ; very persevering ; very affectionate ; reserved , yet demonstrative ; untidy , yet methodical ; exceedingly anxious for the happiness of all around ; apt often to think people better than they really were ; generous to a fault ; unselfishly ready at all times to put her own wishes aside ; vehement and impulsive , yet never in a hurry or flurry ; unyielding , yet tender ; severe , yet frisky", "context_len": 79}}}, "swamp": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 11, "context": "Lacombe 's Cree or Knisteno Dictionary gives : maskek marsh , swamp , trembling ground unsafe to walk upon ; Maskekowiyiniw the Maskegons or Bogmen , a tribe of Crees , also called Maskekowok , who were formerly Odshibw\u0113 Indians , but left Lake Superior to join the Crees ; their name forms a striking parallel to our southern Maskoki", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "In places it was eight feet deep , with sand-bags on either side in addition , In others , in marshy spots , where the high-explosive shells had spitefully disturbed the tranquillity of meandering streams and carried the sluggish water to overflow and swamp the surrounding ground , the `` trench '' was above normal ground-level , with a lofty and broad wall of sand-bags to right and left", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The instructor saw before him a young man , mud covered , carrying a square , black box , and the countenance , spotted with specimens of swamp muck , was unfamiliar to him", "context_len": 34}}}, "winked": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 77, "context": "He had calculated that he would be sold at one of the Levant ports , and had thought that with his knowledge of Turkish he should have no great difficulty in escaping from any master into whose hands he might fall , and taking his chance of either seizing a fishing boat , or of making his way in a trading ship to some district where the population was a mixed one , and where trade was winked at between the merchants there , and those at some of the Greek towns", "context_len": 92}}}, "concluded": {"freq": 128, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 128, "word_index": 1, "context": "Having concluded my reading of old Russell , how can I do better than employ the interval before the arrival of the Indian letters in sitting down and writing to my fair absent sister ? Colonel Sykes let me know last night that Robin would not come by this mail , which was , he says , only from Bombay , so that letters being all we must expect before Saturday fortnight , you need not hurry home on account of Robin \u2019 s return", "context_len": 85}}}, "tannery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "My ole woman do n't set much store by dogs , an ' when I 'm workin ' in the tannery he 's off doggin ' deer by himself", "context_len": 29}}}, "piles": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 43, "context": "The fire had not consumed the bodies of many of the large trees : some of these they used to make the fence of ; the rest they cut up and hauled together with the oxen , and piled them up in great piles , and set them on fire , till they consumed the whole", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Still the beacon piles remain or did remain", "context_len": 9}}}, "crew": {"freq": 240, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 232, "word_index": 11, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 30, "context": "Fortunately I was spared the ordeal of attending the trial , but I heard that the gallows or transportation to the West Indies accounted for the whole of the rascally crew , against whom the barbaric crime of wrecking , as well as smuggling , was proved right up to the hilt", "context_len": 52}}}, "bursting": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 38, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "When Malcolm was thrown by the concussion of the bursting shell , he alighted in the trench he had left , unconscious , his uniform partly torn off , and his face and body dyed with the yellow fumes", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "For by the time Bomba \u2019 s almost bursting lungs compelled him to come to the surface for air , he found himself within a few strokes of the shore", "context_len": 30}}}, "stirred": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 23, "context": "Yet there was a wondrous beauty still lingering over them ; they seemed etherialised -- as if an angel 's smile had last stirred their lines , when the spirit went forth , and left its imprint of wonder , joy , and awe thereon ; and Alfgar instinctively turned from them to the blue depths of heaven above , where a few stars were visible , although dimmed by the moonlight ; and he seemed to trace his beloved Bertric 's passage to the realms of bliss", "context_len": 88}}}, "unkindly": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 67, "context": "Windsor surrendered to a blast of trumpets , and a few days later the little Queen was yielded up a captive into Henry 's hands , and was carried with faithful Simonette , her Saracen maid , to the Castle of Ledes ; but Ledes , fortunately , proved to be a beautiful castle , with a large garden , and she was not treated harshly or unkindly", "context_len": 68}}}, "missy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Oh , I wo n't keep them , missy ; only hold them in my hand for a minute to influence the stars ! You trust the poor gipsy , and she 'll tell you what the future has in store for you", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "What 's got you up so early , missy ? I guess you 're like the rest of us , could n't sleep for thinking of all that 's to do for Thanksgiving", "context_len": 33}}}, "park": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 69, "context": "There was now a short breathing time for the besieged ; but the depression which the failure of their efforts excited among the Turks , was shortly dispelled by the arrival of a ship , with a despatch from Constantinople , in which the pasha was informed that the sultan himself was about to proceed to Rhodes with a reinforcement of a hundred thousand men , and a fresh park of artillery", "context_len": 72}}}, "scarce": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 26, "context": "Who has said a word until now about any ifs in connection with this trip of ours ? But no sooner are we actually off , scarce ten minutes from home , in fact , than the great , uncomfortable , intimidating creatures come trooping in from every quarter , and the particular one that comes to me is this , If you find you do n't like it when you get there , do n't forget where the blame lies", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` `` Hearkening and obedience , '' answered the genie and was gone ; whereupon Alaeddin arose , -- and indeed he had scarce believed that the thing should succeed with him , -- and when he saw the Lady Bedrulbudour in his house , he entreated her with respect , albeit he had long burned for love of her , and said to her , `` O princess of the fair , think not that I have brought thee hither to soil shine honour", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 35, "context": "France and Auvergne , led by Ralph Harcourt , boarded at the bow , the other five langues at the poop ; and so impetuous was their onset that the pirates , who had still scarce recovered from their surprise at being hastily aroused from sleep to repel the attack of the foe who had so suddenly sprung out from the darkness upon them , offered but a feeble resistance", "context_len": 70}}}, "trembled": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 7, "context": "But O Sir , how Miss Sophy trembled and turned as white as a lily , and Miss Ratty stamped and sent me to the village -- at that hour , your honour , company in the house -- the ground covered with frost -- I subject to the rheumatics -- and what for , d \u2019 ye think ? to get her twopenceworth of shoe-ribbon , your honour ; and when I brought it , would you believe it ? -- she roared out that it was too narrow and sent me back again", "context_len": 95}}}, "halter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 63, "context": "How thoughtless it was to walk a pony , who had just come in from a long drive , up and down a lane for half an hour , just for the sake of giving a groom a little exercise ! They did protest with their heels now and then , but that only meant a closer , more uncomfortable grip on the halter , and made matters rather worse than better", "context_len": 72}}}, "barnyard": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 34, "context": "He was so dead beat that he jus ' stood an ' trembled , an ' one o ' Winthrop 's men put a halter on him , an ' led him to the barnyard an ' give him fodder an ' drink , an ' that livin ' young moose is in Winthrop 's park to-day , an ' he weighs four hundred pound", "context_len": 65}}}, "fodder": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "He was so dead beat that he jus ' stood an ' trembled , an ' one o ' Winthrop 's men put a halter on him , an ' led him to the barnyard an ' give him fodder an ' drink , an ' that livin ' young moose is in Winthrop 's park to-day , an ' he weighs four hundred pound", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "So she talked on until Guy had led her past the fires , the groups were busy and cheerful people , the lowing cattle and the tired horses and mules which were quietly munching their fodder and corn , until they reached the baggage wagons", "context_len": 45}}}, "sobbing": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 52, "context": "It did not take very long for the children to be hauled up by Joe at the top , and they found themselves standing among the Abbey ruins in the early gray of the dawn , with Aunt Helen rushing to catch them in her arms , and Lilian hugging them and sobbing over them by turns , while Nancy , her face all blotched and swollen with crying , kept hovering round to put in a kiss whenever the others gave her a chance , and even old David cleared his throat hard , and 'blessed the Lord they were safe", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "Aunt Bessie 's own eyes glistened as she lifted the sobbing child upon her lap , while Maggie stood beside her , holding Bessie 's hand in one of her own , and with her pocket-handkerchief wiping the tears that streamed from her little sister 's eyes", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Roy 's little sobbing voice at the other side of the door , for the bedroom was inside the sitting-room , saying `` Open , open , '' made it almost impossible for Faith to sit quiet , and she was much relieved when her father rose from the table and went out", "context_len": 53}}}, "drunk": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 84, "context": "I , said Charlie , used to drink at home , when father gave it to me ; but , after he was pressed , I promised my mother not to drink any , and I never have , of my own will ; but when I was in the Albatross they used to make me drink , and poured it down my throat if I refused , in order that I might sing songs , and make sport for them when I was drunk", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 57, "context": "He was a man of great strength , and a thorough seaman , but he often shipped as cook , because he had higher wages ; and a most excellent cook he was : he was also perfectly honest , and , like most very powerful men , of an excellent disposition ; but he would get drunk whenever the opportunity offered , insomuch that they often put him in jail , and locked him up till the vessel was ready for sea", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "Alfgar drank moderately , for sooth to say it was invigorating and welcome that cold day , but Higbald finished the bowl then and there , and then staggering down , drew the outer bolt in such a way that it missed the staple , which fact he was too drunk to perceive", "context_len": 53}}}, "jug": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 17, "context": "Ana , with her hands on her broad hips , short and thick as a squat brown jug with its handles akimbo , -- Ana , with her great clay-colored face and tiny , glittering eyes , with her thick , pale lips and coarse , black hair , -- surely none but a mother could view in Ana such charms as bedewed Se\u00f1ora Vigil 's eyes only to think of !", "context_len": 72}}}, "sober": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` Well , I seriously think I am , for if you had seen father when he was alive you 'd have said if there was a dear -- I was very fond of dad -- if there was a dear , sober , conscientious old man -- he was a good bit older than mother -- you 'd have pronounced that he was he", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 53, "context": "It looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` `` Far be it '' ( as the Arabs say ) that we should do so cruel a wrong to so well and justly beloved a memory as that of Jacques Cazotte as to attempt to perpetuate the remembrance of a literary crime which one can hardly believe him to have committed in sober earnest ! Rather let us seek to bury in oblivion this his one offence and suffer kind Lethe with its beneficent waters to wash this `` adulterous blot '' from his else unsullied name", "context_len": 89}}}, "gratitude": {"freq": 88, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 85, "word_index": 64, "context": "said to herself , if she had but a short time upon earth , was there not more reason that she should spend it in doing all she could for her Master 's service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut her ear to the cry of the", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Smith made a list of subjects for daily prayer , embracing repentance , faith , love , grace to help , gratitude , power to pray , constant direction in all things , a right understanding of the Bible , deliverance from besetting sin , constancy in God 's service , relatives and friends , missionaries , pardon for all ignorance and sin in prayer , etc", "context_len": 67}}}, "circus": {"freq": 75, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 75, "word_index": 5, "context": "All round , before the circus doors were open , the doorkeepers of the side-shows were inviting people to come in and see the giants and fat woman and boa-constrictors , and there were stands for peanuts and candy and lemonade ; the vendors cried , `` Ice-cold lemonade , from fifteen hundred miles under ground ! Walk up , roll up , tumble up , any way to get up ! '' The boys thought this brilliant drolling , but they had no time to listen after the doors were open , and they had no money to spend on side-shows or dainties , anyway", "context_len": 106}}}, "thief": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 111, "context": "with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread and half-picked bones which the thief had concealed in some cunning corner inside a manger or under the roof of the loft", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` You little thief ! You ought to go to the Station House", "context_len": 13}}}, "powers": {"freq": 88, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 88, "word_index": 82, "context": "He remembered the fearful joy of the adventure , and the pride , too ; for he had somehow the notion that this young fellow was handsome and fine , and did him an honor by his notice -- so soon does some dim notion of worldly splendor turn us into snobs ! The next thing was his own attempt at skating , when he was set down from the bank by his brother , full of a vainglorious confidence in his powers , and appeared instantly to strike on the top of his head", "context_len": 95}}}, "tipsy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Nobody stirs , till at last a tipsy country-jake is seen making his way down from one of the top-seats towards the ring", "context_len": 23}}}, "tinker": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Think I can tinker it up so as to take you and that other young chap on to Rye ?", "context_len": 20}}}, "swaggered": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "He did n't know where he was at , or where he wanted to be at , an ' he was jolly , an ' friendly , an ' see-sawed roun ' me , an ' rolled an ' swaggered till I tho't I 'd die laughin '", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Then , farewell , but I trust we shall meet and be merry many a year after Pat Keith is hanged , '' and shaking Harrison warmly by the hand , the guardsman turned on his heel and swaggered down the street", "context_len": 43}}}, "drunken": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` Upwards it leads to the banqueting hall , and you can comprehend my escape this evening , '' said he ; `` but our path is now downwards , unless you would like to go up and see the drunken beasts of murderers snoring off their debauch upon the floor as they fell ; oh , that it were lawful for a Christian man to cut their throats as they lie ; many innocent lives would be saved thereby , which those brutes will live to destroy", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 78, "context": "Yes , he was outwardly a model character : he worked early and late ; he saved money ; he was never in debt ; he defrauded no man ; his evenings were spent either in attending lectures of working men like himself or studying the subjects he loved at home ; he never drank ; he never swore ; he was looked up to , and brought forward as an example to follow for many a poor drunken wretch", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "Then he bade lay breakfast ; [ 503 ] so they laid [ it ] and they all broke their fast ; and after they had eaten and drunken their sufficiency and had finished and the servants had removed the tables from before them , Alaeddin turned to the Sultan and said to him , `` O my lord , [ belike ] Thy Grace will vouchsafe to honour me this day at the morning-meal [ 503 ] with the Lady Bedrulbudour , thy precious daughter , and be Thy Grace 's company all thy viziers and the chief officers of thy state", "context_len": 103}}}, "foolery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Just like a drunken man he was , makin ' for home , an ' in the midst of all his foolery havin ' an idea of where he 'd oughter go", "context_len": 32}}}, "reverted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` We were not the only ones in the school between Friday night and this morning ! '' exclaimed Bart , and instantly his thoughts reverted to the stranger who had acted so mysteriously", "context_len": 34}}}, "teeter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Did you ever try to teeter along on snow-shoes ? ''", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "There was a town pump there for you , so that you would not have to go into the house for a drink when you got thirsty , and perhaps be set to doing something ; and there were plenty of boards for teeter and see-saw ; and somehow that neighborhood seemed to understand boys , and did not molest them in any way", "context_len": 64}}}, "thaw": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "I shan \u2019 t kick if it doesn \u2019 t force me to swim , for I shall be getting forward all the time , but when I do get across , the first thing I shall do is to build a fire and thaw out", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The warmth of the comfortable fire had already begun to thaw his frozen wits , and he made shift to stammer a word of thanks as he fumbled with the spoon", "context_len": 31}}}, "heft": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The las ' thaw took the heft of it off the ground -- you 'd ' a ' never got in this fur if it had n't -- an ' we 're bound to hev another big fall", "context_len": 38}}}, "tottered": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` Lord ha \u2019 mercy on me if \u2019 tis not young Master Wentworth ! '' he exclaimed , showing that , though grown in stature , I had not outgrown the appearance of my boyhood ; and when Drake told him of my newly found title , the old man tottered away to let the frequenters of the inn know the news ; whereat we , unwilling to tarry longer , pushed on towards the towering heights of Hindhead", "context_len": 80}}}, "trot": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 69, "context": "Harris were out for a drive behind Harold 's chestnut ponies , and , as usual , when something important had need to be talked over , the ponies did pretty much as they liked , and that meant , I am ashamed to say ( for they were quite too young to so much as think of being lazy ) , keeping up the merest pretence of a trot for a while , and then subsiding into a walk altogether", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 4, "context": "She often used to trot over in this way , in an ekka , to Amritsar , on a road which caused many bumps and aches to most people \u2019 s heads and arms and bodies ; but she would never allow that the shaking of twenty-four miles of such travelling as this ever did her any harm", "context_len": 58}}}, "rapidity": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 17, "context": "The days now dragged themselves away upon leaden feet , yet -- apparent paradox -- with frightful rapidity ; for I now no longer had a household to attend to my wants ; my meals were brought to me with unfailing regularity by my guards , but they had apparently been forbidden to communicate with me , for not a word could I get out of them , good , bad , or indifferent", "context_len": 74}}}, "sullen": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "Thus we knew not of the glorious victory of Albemarle over de Ruyter on the 25th of July following our capture ; but by the sullen demeanour of the soldiers we guessed that once more the States of Holland were suffering for their audacity in questioning our supremacy on the sea", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "In what strange world had he been living ? Had that sullen , desperate wretch been indeed Dick Harrison ? He realized that he was alive ; he could still enjoy the common comforts of food and fire ; he could think ; he could plan ! His feet were once more treading solid earth ; his brain began to spin anew the projects that had delighted him of yore ; his heart began to stir with the hopes of old", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 63, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}}}, "wheeling": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 23, "context": "For a moment the horses stood still as if stunned , and their riders bent low over the saddles , then , suddenly wheeling , the animals turned their heads away from the furious blast , and in that position waited for it to expend its fury", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "A poor old paralyzed man sat in a wheeling chair on the porch", "context_len": 13}}}, "gusts": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 10, "context": "Davenport 's remarks , which had been wafted along in gusts , died away in the distance as she departed down the pasture to inspect the hen-coops , and hint broadly for the gift of a couple of young pullets , 'for yours are such an excellent breed , dear Miss Vaughan , and such capital winter layers", "context_len": 58}}}, "allowing": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 68, "context": "She did not call to me , or announce her return in any way , but set to work to mow a circle of about ten feet in diameter in the long grass ; and then , having produced fire by rubbing two pieces of wood together , she proceeded very carefully to burn off the short grass left inside the circle , setting fire to it , allowing it to burn for a few seconds , and then beating it out again with a branch , in order that the fire might not spread and burn us out , to say nothing of betraying our presence by the smoke that it would raise", "context_len": 114}}}, "turbulently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "A small and sullen cloud had hidden the dreamy , thoughtful sun , and out of the cloud came wheeling , choking gusts , bearing bewildered snowflakes up and down , hither and thither , before allowing them to alight turbulently upon the quiet earth", "context_len": 45}}}, "philosophically": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "Malcolm took the matter philosophically , knowing that in war-time a soldier can not pick and choose his mates ; but to his astonishment and delight he found that Fortescue and Selwyn were in the same lines", "context_len": 37}}}, "seeking": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 58, "context": "It is well said that no man is utterly bad , and perhaps he possessed domestic virtues which were thought unworthy of the attention of the chroniclers ; but as they picture him -- now prompting Ethelred to deeds of treachery against the Danes , now joining those Danes themselves , and surpassing them in cruelty -- now seeking pretended reconciliation , only to betray his foe more surely , and in all this aided and supported by the weak , unprincipled king -- as thus pictured there is scarcely a blacker character in history", "context_len": 95}}}, "firs": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "Outside at least he could have his will , and his vengeance fell upon the sturdy young firs and spruces , who at his fierce word of command threw off their burdens of snow , and bent and swayed before his wrath as wildly as the most graceful hardwood saplings", "context_len": 50}}}, "burdens": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 115, "context": "'s service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut her ear to the cry of the needy , because to answer it would cost a sacrifice of her own wishes ? Should she bear the burdens of others only when they did not weigh heavily on herself ?", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "It burdens me so heavily that , but for your sister , I would retire into a monastery , and there end my days", "context_len": 24}}}, "graceful": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 65, "context": "Their names were Mabel and Alice Cunningham , two handsome dark-eyed girls , aged respectively seventeen and fifteen ; Florence Aylmer , who was also fifteen and the romp of the school ; Mary Bateman , a stolid-looking girl of fourteen ; Bertha Kennedy , who had only lately been raised to the rank of the Upper school ; Edith King , a handsome , graceful girl , who competed with Mabel for the honors of the head of her class ; and Kitty Sharston , who had only lately come , and who had some Irish blood in her , and was very daring and very much inclined to break the rules", "context_len": 113}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 68, "context": "Miss Wauton \u2019 s first impressions of her , jotted down as follows , do not give the impression of a very old lady , dearly as Charlotte Tucker loved to describe herself in those terms : Tall , slight , with lofty brow , sparkling eye , face constantly beaming with love and intelligence ; genius in every look ; figure frail and fairy-like , agile and graceful ; very brisk movements and light tread", "context_len": 76}}}, "hardwood": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "Eliot , as he led the boy into a handsome room , where there were long shelves of books , and great comfortable leather-covered chairs , and costly Turkish rugs on the hardwood floor , with a wood fire burning cheerfully in an open fireplace , and a frail , sweet-faced woman sitting amid piled-up cushions in an invalid \u2019 s chair near a table , on which stood a shaded lamp and lay many books and magazines", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Knowing he might be set upon by the dogs , he looked about for a weapon of defense , finally discovering a thick , heavy , hardwood cudgel , about three feet in length", "context_len": 34}}}, "saplings": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "Outside at least he could have his will , and his vengeance fell upon the sturdy young firs and spruces , who at his fierce word of command threw off their burdens of snow , and bent and swayed before his wrath as wildly as the most graceful hardwood saplings", "context_len": 50}}}, "winters": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "I remember that evening well ; its stirring incidents are graven on my memory as if they had happened but yesterday , though nigh on twoscore and ten winters and summers have passed over my head since the eventful year of which I write", "context_len": 44}}}, "groan": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 55, "context": "It was Faith 's duty to make this room ready for breakfast ; and , as a rule , Warden would have been angry with her for its present state of neglect ; but this morning he said nothing , only when his eyes rested on the torn pieces of the essay he uttered a groan , and , stooping down , he picked them all up and put them in the grate", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Billy went , but he could not reach Mark , and occasionally his ardor would be checked by an unexpected blow which made his classmates groan", "context_len": 27}}}, "raging": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 17, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Every timid hare became a wolf , each gentle fawn a raging buffalo , but the brave went on undaunted , brandishing his war-club , and keeping his formidable foes at bay", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Before the rain began to fall on Lola 's bare head , as it did shortly in sheets , the stream-bed had become a raging torrent , down which froth and spume and uprooted saplings were spinning", "context_len": 37}}}, "decaying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "John told him that he bored the hole for the mouth , and then turned the milk out , filled it with salt water , and set it in the sun , when , the meat decaying , he washed it all out ; then scraping the outside with a knife and piece of glass , oiled it , and made the face with an old file , which he ground to a sharp point", "context_len": 75}}}, "giant": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 55, "context": "The soft spring air floated in through the open window , and she heard the birds twitter and the frogs peep : she heard Abraham Lincoln , the old horse that she used to ride to water before she grew big enough to work , whinney over his hay ; and Goliath , the young giant that had come to take his place in the farm work , answer him sonorously : the dog barked lazily as a nighthawk swept by , and in the distant hen-yard she heard a rooster crow", "context_len": 92}, "UNK": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was the giant mora tree , two hundred feet high , aglow with clusters of scarlet blossoms , feathery palms , the bright yellow trumpet flower with blooms so large that they were worn as hats by the Indian women and children , huge fuchsias with their purplish tubular bells , heliotrope , verbenas , orchids , glowing with all the colors of the rainbow", "context_len": 66}}}, "hurled": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 9, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "personified": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Gippie was on her lap , Poacher beside her , and this man with his two boys , who at present personified her best friends in the world , were safe and warm in their shelter", "context_len": 36}}}, "stimulated": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 57, "context": "But in carrying a city by storm , which takes place usually at an unexpected time , and often in the night , the maddened and victorious assaulter suddenly burst into the sacred scenes of domestic peace , and seclusion , and love -- the very worst of men , filled with the worst of passions , stimulated by the resistance they have encountered , and licensed by their victory to give all these passions the fullest and most unrestricted gratification", "context_len": 81}}}, "brand": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 19, "context": "He ran to the house and got some corn in a dish , and to the fire for a brand ; he called the pig , rattled the corn in the dish with one hand , and with the other lighted the brush in different places , as he walked around the heap", "context_len": 53}}}, "listener": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 77, "context": "And all this while the wilful little queen was trying to convince herself that it was ready for Donald 's sake , when the truth was that the long walks with Donald , when Harold -- who was making up some necessary back work at college -- was not at her service , were what she was determined not to give up , and the reading aloud in the evenings , when Donald was such a delightful listener ; and , in fact , the hundred and one little amusing things that Donald was continually doing , and that made the days go by in such happy , merry fashion", "context_len": 110}}}, "abate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "But accidents , he had come to learn , were almost daily occurrences in the jungle , and he did not abate a jot of his vigilance , his keen eyes keeping on the lookout everywhere -- at the water for snags or alligators , at either shore for animal or human enemies , on the trees that overhung the stream for lurking anacondas", "context_len": 64}}}, "prisoners": {"freq": 107, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 105, "word_index": 56, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "PRISONERS ON THE PIRATE SHIP", "context_len": 5}}}, "apologetic": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "'What on earth has that stupid Nancy given me a hot bottle for on such a warm evening ? ' he exclaimed ; and flinging down the clothes he disclosed to view , not the expected earthenware bottle , but the shrinking and apologetic form of Master Rollo , who , if ever a dog could be said to own a conscience , surely showed he knew he had offended , and repented heartily of his sins", "context_len": 77}}}, "gals": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` I 'll tell you , mister , '' broke forth Lem , after another moment or two of silence , `` I was awful sorry when I heard what Doll did to them gardens after the little gals begged me out ; but you see she did n't know it , and she thought I was took to jail", "context_len": 60}}}, "afeard": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Ye see we ai n't used to havin ' leetle gals , an ' I 'm afeard we ca n't make you very comfy , as my ole woman says , but we 'll do the best we kin", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Dat \u2019 s what I oughter do , but I hain \u2019 t got de courage , Al ; it \u2019 s been on my mind two , free times , and I started in to let you know , but I \u2019 se afeard", "context_len": 45}}}, "comfy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "And here I am sitting by my blazing wood fire , long ere dawn , with that same quilt like a shawl round my shoulders , -- so comfy ! Luxurious Char ! But , after all , I have not begun my thanks , and where am I to end them ?", "context_len": 53}}}, "bunks": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "See here , '' and rising , he went to one of the rough bunks built against the wall opposite the fire ; `` I 'm a-goin ' to drape ye off a place for yourself and dog , '' and , hanging a blanket on a hook by the fireplace , he called loudly for a nail to drive in the logs across the corner", "context_len": 66}}}, "drape": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "See here , '' and rising , he went to one of the rough bunks built against the wall opposite the fire ; `` I 'm a-goin ' to drape ye off a place for yourself and dog , '' and , hanging a blanket on a hook by the fireplace , he called loudly for a nail to drive in the logs across the corner", "context_len": 66}}}, "hook": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 34, "context": "She was the older daughter , a strong , healthy girl of seventeen , who never helped her mother about the work , who dressed in such cheap finery as she could obtain by hook or crook , who took music lessons on a rented melodeon paid for out of her mother \u2019 s hard earnings , who felt herself to be a lady unfortunately born out of her sphere , and who was unquestionably ashamed of her surviving parent and her brother and sister", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 37, "context": "In the same way , if one of the boys had seen a circus-man drop a quarter , he would have hurried to give it back to him , but he would only have been proud to hook into the circus-man 's show , and the other fellows would have been proud of his exploit , too , as something that did honor to them all", "context_len": 66}}}, "nail": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 13, "context": "Peggy and Bobby would have been quite content to go on adding a nail here or a shelf there , and further making improvements , but Archie , now that the chief work was over , found his interest cooling , and having got hold of a book on 'Balloons and Air-Ships , ' proposed no less daring a scheme than that he should construct a flying machine , and start it from the platform", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They don \u2019 t have sandpaper and nail scissors in the jungles , do they ?", "context_len": 16}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` But if you do n't get down here and help hold this end , while I nail the other , I know what 's going to happen", "context_len": 28}}}, "fir": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 113, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}}}, "beds": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 83, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}}}, "rustling": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 21, "context": "Charlie began to slap his hands on his sides , and cry , Rover , Rover , \u2019 when a great rustling was heard in the woods , and the pig came on the gallop , his black and white sides glistening in the sun as he ran", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "For a time poor Guy sat upon the ground helpless , and hopeless , listening intently to the rustling movements of the numerous small animals , that wandered about seeking food ; fearing to move , lest he should encounter a prairie wolf , or some other ferocious beast , and equally afraid to remain still , lest they should scent him there", "context_len": 63}}}, "evergreen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The rustling of the evergreen inside reminded her of her narrow straw bed at the orphanage , and drawing the blanket over her , she nestled down and patiently waited for her friends to seek their equally fragrant couches", "context_len": 39}}}, "seek": {"freq": 124, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 112, "word_index": 48, "context": "Accordingly , when she came up , he saw her and viewed her well , beyond his wish ; and from that time till now , O King of the Age , life hath not been pleasant to him [ 366 ] and he will e'en have me seek her of Thy Grace , [ 367 ] so thou mayst marry her with him , and I can not do away this conceit from his wit , for that the love of her hath gotten possession of his vitals , so that he saith to me , 'Know , O mother mine , that , except I attain my desire , assuredly I am a dead man", "context_len": 117}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "All the children were playing games follow the leader , prisoner \u2019 s base , blind man \u2019 s buff , hide and go seek , still-pond-no-more-moving , and many other games", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` O my son , '' replied she , `` true it is that the present is exceedingly costly and precious and as thou sayest , none hath the like thereof ; but who shall dare to come forward and seek of the Sultan his daughter Bedrulbudour ? Nay , I dare not adventure myself and say to him , ' I want thy daughter , ' whenas he asketh me , 'What wouldst thou ? ' Marry , O my son , , my tongue will be tied", "context_len": 89}}}, "couches": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was now late , but for several hours we tossed uneasily on our straw couches , till , worn out with the excitement and fatigue , we fell asleep , to be awakened only too soon by the entry of our jailers", "context_len": 43}}}, "repeat": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 19, "context": "The majority of them are laid down in phonetically defective missionary alphabets , about which we are prompted to repeat what the citizens of the young colony of Mexico wrote to the government of Spain , in Cortez 's time : `` Send to us pious and Christian men , as preachers , bishops and missionaries , but do not send us scholars , who , with their pettifogging distinctions and love of contention , create nothing but disorder and strife", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "And , Dream Fairies , continued the Dream King , I \u2019 m going to tell each girl and boy how every mean speech she or he thinks of and doesn \u2019 t make , or doesn \u2019 t repeat , will come straight to Dreamland and every week we will have a great big bonfire of them", "context_len": 58}}}, "screen": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 39, "context": "I could now see how near I had been to regaining my freedom , for just beyond the place where my tour of exploration had abruptly terminated was the entrance to the cave , skilfully hidden by a heavy screen of painted canvas that , even at a short distance , would deceive all who were not acquainted with the secret", "context_len": 61}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "Edric 's eloquence , and the strong circumstantial evidence against the prisoner , carried the day , and the ealdorman even proposed that execution should be speedy , `` lest , '' he whispered , `` Canute should interfere to screen his instrument", "context_len": 43}}}, "sleepers": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 20, "context": "Charlie knew there was a great deal of small timber in the barn frame -- braces , purlins , and sleepers -- that he could hew as well as anybody ; and , now that he had a little money , was very anxious to have a broad-axe of his own , that he might help hew the barn frame", "context_len": 60}}}, "crackling": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 14, "context": "When but half the distance was covered , I heard the sound of the crackling of the dry brushwood in a coppice on our left , followed by the cry of a bird and the fluttering of its wings as it flew over our heads", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "The sulphurous flames nearly choked him , the heat from the crackling wooden partition singed his hair , but he could only grope about blindly", "context_len": 25}}}, "heaped": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 67, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "hadst": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 121, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "wilt": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 64, "context": ", with his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like , and be it complete with all its requisites of kingly and magnificent furniture and", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 74, "context": "son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not give them his daughter", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 84, "context": "Alaeddin [ 329 ] waited till his mother had made an end of her speech and said to her , `` O my mother , all that thou thinkest I know ; marry , I know full well that I am the son of poor folk , nor may all this thy talk anywise avail to move me from my purpose ; but I beseech thee , an I be thy very son and thou love me , do me this kindness ; else wilt thou lose me , for death hasteneth upon me , an I attain not my wish of the beloved of my heart", "context_len": 107}}}, "owns": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 28, "context": "She still loves the Abbey dearly , but I do not fancy she will spend all her life there , after all , for Archie , who now owns a prosperous sheep-farm in Australia , is still as anxious as ever for her to try colonial life , and I think that the next time he comes home on a visit to Gorswen he will be able to persuade her", "context_len": 70}}}, "kills": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "That great hulking Taylor half kills young Ford now and then", "context_len": 11}}}, "coal": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 36, "context": "There were swarms of mischievous boys on the banks , whose sole amusement seemed to be throwing stones at the irate bargees , until one of the women grew so furious that she leapt upon the coal that screened the New Zealanders ' retreat , and picking up fragments hurled them at her tormentors", "context_len": 54}}}, "evermore": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "How the Mummy would enjoy it , how she would revel in the good food and the lovely house ! What a red-letter day it would be to her all her life , for all the rest of her years ! How Sukey and Ann Pratt and the neighbors down at Dawlish would respect her for evermore ! And doubtless the Mummy 's dress might be managed , and -- but what about Aunt Susan ? Would Aunt Susan ever forgive her ? She dared not run the risk of her displeasure ; too much depended on keeping her in a good humor", "context_len": 103}}}, "confess": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 85, "context": "Bless him , dear Father in heaven , an ' his two boys , an ' bless me an ' Gippie an ' Poacher an ' keep us safe for evermore , -- an ' bless the lady-boards , an ' the matron , an ' all the little orphans , an ' let them find good homes an ' get out o ' the 'sylum , -- Lord , I will write them a letter as soon as I get settled , an ' confess what is wickedness , an ' what ai n't", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` She came up to my room -- I will confess to you that I was hiding from her , absolutely hiding , and shaking from head to foot , scarcely knowing what to do -- and she came in as bold as brass , and yet with a new sort of humility about her , and she said to me , 'Will you forgive me ? And if you forgive me , will you come downstairs and let me put my hand inside your arm ? ' And somehow , although it was the very last thing on earth that I wanted to do , I did it ; and now here I am , and I do n't feel nearly so much afraid of her as", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "In the same letter , speaking of a young Indian , who had eagerly said to her that the Bible is the light of our eyes , and the root of our faith , \u2019 she sadly remarked that it was almost sickening \u2019 to think what the young Muhammadan would have to endure , did he openly confess Christ , \u2019 -- even while earnestly hoping that he would be constrained by the cords of love \u2019 to leave all and come forward", "context_len": 84}}}, "awakened": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 13, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}}}, "waked": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 84, "context": "`` I ca n't wait , '' said Maggie ; `` I want it now ; nurse is holding baby because mamma has gone to sleep too , and Jane has taken Franky to Harry 's room to dress him , because she was afraid he would make a noise ; and she said if I put on my shoes and socks , and all the rest of my under-clo 's before she came back , I might put on yours , if you waked up", "context_len": 86}}}, "religious": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 71, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He is a Mullah \u2019 s ( Muhammadan religious teacher \u2019 s ) son , and has been brought up in a fine school for bigotry", "context_len": 26}}}, "prayer": {"freq": 100, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 93, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` You may carry them to the loftiest peaks of the Cordilleras ; they may float in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war ,", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "It was very still and peaceful in there , and she liked to sit and think how the Abbey must have looked in those strange , bygone days when the little steps had led to a dormitory , and the broken pillars had held up the roof of a church , whose tinkling bell had rung out at sunset , calling to prayer those old monks who slept so quietly in their forgotten graves", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "He spent a busy time in the Crimea , doing plenty of hard work in the trenches ; and when off duty engaged in hospital visiting , tract and book distributing , attending prayer meetings and mission services , constant in his Bible reading , and always endeavouring to do good to others", "context_len": 53}}}, "sighing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 31, "context": "I have come here to ask you one or two questions , and to tell you one or two things , and my first question is this -- Why were you sighing when I came in , and why did you murmur to yourself , 'What if it all turns out a mistake ? ' Will you explain those words , Jane", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "'Here brief is the sighing , And brief is the crying , For brief is the life ! The life there is endless , The joy there is endless , And ended the strife", "context_len": 34}}}, "attend": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 88, "word_index": 85, "context": "`` [ 428 ] So he said to Alaeddin 's mother , `` Go and tell thy son that I abide by the promise which I made him , but an if he avail unto my daughter 's dowry ; to wit , I require of him forty dishes of pure gold , which must all be full of jewels [ such as ] thou broughtest me [ erst ] , together with forty slave-girls to carry them and forty male slaves to escort and attend them", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "'What did they want ? ' said Peggy , for David seemed disposed to rest from his labours and attend to his cookery", "context_len": 23}}}, "churches": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 101, "context": "as it might be here '' -- arranging a tankard at the corner of the table -- '' with a good high road leading up to it from the sea , as it might be my tobacco pipe '' -- laying it down with care ; `` and if you 'll believe me , sir , we took and run races , as it might be along my tobacco pipe , and as soon as them Spaniards was 'ware of our coming , they took and ran out by 'tother gate , and left the town empty ! There was seven churches all chock full of gold and silver idols and candle-sticks , and such like : 't was just who 'd fill his pockets fastest ! ''", "context_len": 128}}}, "pines": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 30, "context": "He met with no squalls this autumn , for when the weather looked at all unsettled he could work with Ben in the woods , and fall down the large pines for him to hew , which he dearly loved to do ; and , as it took a long time to hew out a large stick of timber , he had ample time to cut them down and trim them out", "context_len": 72}}}, "weird": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 64, "context": "Who but she could climb to the very highest branch of the tallest tree ? Who but she could swing so high that she seemed almost to turn a somersault in the air before she came down again ? Who but she could invent the most daring games ? And then , when all other things failed , who but she could tell such weird stories ? Her eyes shone ; her lips were wreathed in smiles", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 34, "context": "Everything looked bright and cheerful in the morning sun , and after sculling along for some time he was beginning to think that the cry must have been uttered by some sea-bird , seeming weird and strange in the early morning , when he suddenly recalled the fact that sound travels far over a smooth , calm sea", "context_len": 58}}}, "impatient": {"freq": 67, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 64, "context": "Notwithstanding the scorching heat of an Indian summer , -- in spite , too , of the fact that a number of the men were obliged to march in heavy garments utterly unsuited to the climate ; though death , disease , and a thousand perils lay in front of them , -- not a man of Havelock 's `` Ironsides '' but was impatient to push onward to death or victory", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 7, "context": "Richard 's youthful wisdom had often grown impatient of his uncle 's wild fancies , or smiled with affectionate mockery on his Utopian dreams ; but unconsciously the young man had always measured his own thoughts and actions by the unworldly standard of General Harrison 's ideal", "context_len": 47}}}, "impenitent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Some were clamouring for vengeance on the spy who had signalled the mob to gather round their door , others urging Richard to save himself from the fate awaiting impenitent sinners by immediately drawing his sword in the Fifth Monarchy cause , while others , of whom Mr", "context_len": 48}}}, "memories": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 76, "context": "Yet he was a boy among boys , too ; he loved to swim , to skate , to fish , to forage , and passionately , above all , he loved to hunt ; but in everything he held himself in check , that he might hold the younger boys in check ; and my boy often repaid his conscientious vigilance with hard words and hard names , such as embitter even the most self-forgiving memories", "context_len": 77}}}, "snarl": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` I do n't know whether I can claim all that ; now and then I get some poor fellow 's affairs out of a snarl and make him pay for it , and one end of something has drifted here to Newton and I 'm after that , but I thought I 'd hunt you up first", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The old man continued to splutter and snarl and flourish his cane , upon which Tuttle and Cooper made a pretense of skurrying around in great haste for rocks to pelt him with , and he beat a hasty retreat toward his wretched hovel", "context_len": 44}}}, "resolved": {"freq": 88, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 87, "word_index": 2, "context": "Finally he resolved to take matters into his own hands by suddenly slipping away from Marie-Celeste 's grasp ; and then drawing off a little , and folding both hands behind his back , as though neither of them were to be longer at anybody 's disposal , he said aggressively : And -- and now what are you afraid of , Marie-Celeste ? Do you sink somebody 's goin ' to soot you from de top of one of de towers if you speak out loud ?", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The lad cast a rapid and amused glance over her small resolved figure , then taking his handkerchief from his pocket , turned his face to the window , and coughed vigorously", "context_len": 32}}}, "shelf": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 61, "context": "For there was something very stirring in the act , and a stranger to the place would hold his breath in dread as he saw Mark Penelly , who was the finest swimmer at the port of Carn Du , climb up the side of the great black rock upon some fine summer evening , then go round along the narrow shelf of shaley stone , till he stood alone there forty feet above the sea , his white figure as he rested against the black rock , every muscle standing out from his well-knit frame , and his arms crossed , looking like some antique statue in its niche", "context_len": 110}}}, "venture": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 57, "context": "If ever I attempted such a thing -- and I did , very frequently -- a party of the king 's guards was certain to turn up , in the most exasperatingly casual and unexpected manner , and join me , under the pretence , as they made me understand , that it was extremely dangerous to venture alone beyond the confines of the town , if I pretended that I was engaged in hunting for animals , or plants to be used in my medical practice", "context_len": 87}}}, "springing": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 101, "context": "They did nothing with their boat except loll in it and tell stories , and as there was no current in the Reservoir , they must have remained pretty much in the same place ; but they had a sense of the wildest adventure , which mounted to frenzy , when some men rose out of the earth on the shore , and shouted at them , `` Hello , there ! What are you doing with that boat ? '' They must have had an oar ; at any rate , they got to the opposite bank , and , springing to land , fled somewhere into the vaguest past", "context_len": 111}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And she came in with a springing step , color in her cheeks , and a smile hovering about the corners of her mouth", "context_len": 24}}}, "locks": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 15, "context": "Mais n \u2019 importe ! the man , the man remains the same ! These locks have proved the keys to a Lady \u2019 s heart e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "I 've a great mind to tell Seabrooke to look in his trunk before he locks it", "context_len": 17}}}, "comb": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Tia , tia , do you hear ? You are a lady of fortune and must have a velvet gown ! And , oh , tia , a tall , silver comb in your hair ! '' She dropped a sudden kiss down upon the smooth , brown bands , and added in a deeper tone , `` But nothing , nothing , can make you better or dearer ! ''", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` He wo n't bite you , '' replied Helen , `` and you do n't have to get close enough to him to comb his eyebrows", "context_len": 27}}}, "reposed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 46, "context": "We shall not this voyage have the encouragement and confidence inspired by the presence of those who have long and valiantly borne the standard of the Order ; but , on the other hand , we have to show that we are worthy of the confidence reposed in us , and that the young knights of the Order can be trusted to emulate the deeds of those who have rendered the name of the Hospitallers a terror to the infidel", "context_len": 80}}}, "piped": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 30, "context": "But , when the captain heard Miss Weston was there to keep an appointment , he put out the accommodation ladder , took her on board , had the notice piped that she had come to give an address ; and soon a crowd of sailors was swarming round her in the upper deck battery , standing , sitting , lying , kneeling -- all earnestly listening", "context_len": 67}}}, "sniffing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` We were putting him on the sledge when I saw Sol , who had trotted back to the place where we found Owen , pawing away once more at the snow , snorting and sniffing as if he were displeased", "context_len": 41}}}, "carcass": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 63, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "undisguised": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 40, "context": "Grief does not for long hold its sway over the buoyant spirit of youth , and , in spite of the heavy blow that I had sustained , my boyish disposition speedily reasserted itself , and I looked forward with undisguised eagerness to my journey to my new home in Portsmouth town", "context_len": 52}}}, "readier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Joe , who was readier of speech than his brother , found his tongue first", "context_len": 15}}}, "loony": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "You 're loony ! Off your trolley ! You 've got bats in your belfry , as the poet says , '' and , when Frank and Ned thought it over , they were inclined to agree with their chum", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "He goes to town on his crutches every day , an ' Gippie limps after him -- oh , Hank Dillson , Hank Dillson , I 'm mos ' loony about this business of your goin ' away", "context_len": 38}}}, "lug": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` You must be fond of this sort of thing , to lug that heavy camera around with you , '' commented Phil", "context_len": 23}}}, "resignation": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 41, "context": "Many facts therein given have been rejected by the Author as too harrowing in their nature ; and he has preferred to render the contemplation of woe and suffering less painful , by a display of those virtues of patience , resignation , and brave submission to the Divine will , which affliction never fails to bring out in the fold of Christ , whose promise stands ever fast , that the strength of His people shall be equal to their needs", "context_len": 82}}}, "silently": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 97, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Richard assented , and they trudged on silently for some time", "context_len": 11}}}, "conclude": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 49, "context": "Now that we have ended all this War of Words , And fall to drawing corks instead of swords , Now the Pretender may his Captors mock , And view with glee a match without the lock , Let each resentful thought and feeling cease , And General Harmony conclude the Piece !", "context_len": 53}}}, "march": {"freq": 136, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 27, "context": "Notwithstanding the scorching heat of an Indian summer , -- in spite , too , of the fact that a number of the men were obliged to march in heavy garments utterly unsuited to the climate ; though death , disease , and a thousand perils lay in front of them , -- not a man of Havelock 's `` Ironsides '' but was impatient to push onward to death or victory", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 37, "context": "The auriferous ledges of the Cheroki country were said to be within `` the extreme confines of the Apalachi province '' ( Fontanedo , 1559 ) , and the Apalachi found by Narvaez was fifteen days ' march north of Aute , [ 49 ] a roadstead or harbor on the Gulf of Mexico , though the Indians had stated to him that it lay at a distance of nine days ' travel only", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 78, "word_index": 119, "context": "had hay in his right shoe , and straw in his left , because a Dutchman was too dumb , as the boys said for stupid , to know his feet apart any other way ; and that the Dutch officers had to call out to the men when they were marching , `` Up mit de hay-foot , down mit de straw-foot -- links , links , links ! '' ( Left , left , left ! ) But the boys honored even these imperfect intelligences so much in their quality of soldiers that they would any of them have been proud to be marker in the Dutch company ; and they followed the Dutchmen round in their march as fondly as any other body of troops", "context_len": 128}}}, "sternly": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 58, "context": "Peggy and Bobby tried , as Archie expressed it , to 'do a slope , ' but in vain , for , saying she had not seen them for a long time , and should like to talk to them , their unwelcome visitor took the dismayed pair into custody like a female policeman , and whirled them sternly along before her", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Frideswide 's ; there wax tapers shed a hallowed light on the sternly composed features of him who had been the bulwark of England ; and there choking sobs and bitter sighs every hour rent the air , and bore witness to a nation 's grief", "context_len": 46}}}, "settle": {"freq": 95, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 80, "word_index": 107, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 90, "context": "the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner , and the oak dresser , with its rows of blue willow-pattern plates ; and back again at last to where Mrs", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "It looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and", "context_len": 128}}}, "king": {"freq": 387, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 49, "context": "Yet I scarcely knew precisely what it was that I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 344, "word_index": 89, "context": "on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me , died out , and I began to prepare myself , as best I could , to meet whatever fate might have in store for me with the fortitude befitting a Christian and an Englishman", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Ethelred has returned , and is again king ; he has promised to amend his evil ways , and to be ruled by faithful and wise counsellors", "context_len": 27}}}, "repents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It 's when ye 're young an ' squshy that you repents", "context_len": 12}}}, "cubs": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Sharp the adder 's tooth , but sharper Spake the sea king to his foes , Spake while savage brows grew darker , As he told the countless woes Which the bear 's fierce cubs should bring To those who slew their father and their king", "context_len": 47}}}, "rebellious": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "Granby pretended to be very angry at the rebellious hair , -- '' I do n't care if it wo n't be smoothed ; it is just like father 's , mother says so ; and anything like him is good enough for me", "context_len": 44}}}, "perceiving": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 29, "context": "Most children would have thought the life of constant toil which Guy led very wretched indeed ; but he did not , for he had daily the gratification of perceiving that the great object of their journey across the plains was being gradually accomplished ; his mother 's health was slowly becoming strengthened , by every step they took toward the snowy mountains , beyond which lay the fruitful valleys in which they hoped to find a home", "context_len": 78}}}, "splinters": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 65, "context": "I never was away from home more than a day in my life till I was married , and it 's been nothing but work ever since , and now to be laid here like a useless log , with everything going hotfoot to destruction ! It 's a good thing you 've come at last , for the children are makin ' sawdust and splinters of every bit of crockery in the house , and that Martha Spriggs has no more management than a settin ' hen", "context_len": 88}}}, "ketches": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "An ' you need n't kick , 'cause I 'll lick ye all to splinters if I ketches one o ' you in the woods this spring", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` S'pose the warden ketches him ? ''", "context_len": 8}}}, "grim": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 72, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "Hardly able to repress a smile that flitted across his grim features , the sergeant deliberately walked up to Felgate , relieved him of the knife and two spikes that he had concealed , and replaced them", "context_len": 37}}}, "sulky": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 3, "context": "But Dolly turned sulky in her turn , and refused to say a word more ; and Lem , knowing it was useless to try to make her speak when she did not choose , strolled into the woods to see if he could find any berries for his breakfast ; while she , still shivering from her night 's exposure in the Ice Glen , tried to kindle a fire from the wet sticks which lay around ; and finding this in vain , crept to her wretched bed , and tried to warm herself there", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` You can put things right , of course , according to your idea of right , '' said Bertha , in a sulky voice ; `` you can go to Sir John and tell him what has happened ; you can do that if you please", "context_len": 47}}}, "conquered": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` It has been a hard fight , old friend , '' said the familiar king , `` but we have conquered ; for my part , I would far sooner have stood out against him , battle-axe in hand , than have met this struggle , could I have foreseen it beforehand ; but now I have given him the kiss of peace , peace it must be ; he has no more to dread from me", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Clara and her mother against all the girls in the club , that was the way it seemed to be , but finally the former conquered and she went back to where her mother still sat", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "Evidently Baboo Bajorum had given his gunners orders to stop firing , because they had left their cannons and with the rest of the crew had run to the side and were leaning over watching their conquered enemies just as Barty wanted to do", "context_len": 44}}}, "raving": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I 'm cravin ' to lick you , '' said the boy , bursting out into a wild raving and swearing at him", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Oh , if you could have seen her under full sail -- but what is the use of my raving about the Sea Witch ? Good-night , and God bless you all", "context_len": 32}}}, "swearing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` I 'm cravin ' to lick you , '' said the boy , bursting out into a wild raving and swearing at him", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "The two rolled over together , and Peggy clung trembling to the gatepost as she watched the confused heap at her feet , Rollo scratching , snarling , and biting like a wild beast , and the tramp kicking , fighting , and swearing in a way which made her blood go cold to hear", "context_len": 55}}}, "additional": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` As long as I live , Mabel , your fifty pounds a year is secured to you , and I shall allow you , after Florence leaves that expensive school , which has cost me from one hundred and twenty to a hundred and forty pounds a year , to give you an additional fifteen pounds , thus raising your income to the very creditable one of sixty-five pounds per annum", "context_len": 72}}}, "misfortune": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 70, "context": "Oh , the Brotherhood of Knights ! That is what an Order is , you know , and this one was founded way back in the fourteenth century , in the time of Edward the Third ; and they say the way it came to be called the Order of the Garter was this : That King Edward was dancing with the Countess of Salisbury , when she had the misfortune to lose her garter ; and then as he stooped to pick it up , and saw every one smiling , he gallantly announced , 'that they should shortly see that garter advanced to so high an honor and renown as to account themselves happy to wear it", "context_len": 119}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The people about here know she comes of good stock and that the poverty of the family was due more to misfortune than misbehavior", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "By one of those freaks of misfortune Rifleman Carr had completely lost his bearings , and in his wanderings had made his way towards the German trenches instead of towards the village of Messines", "context_len": 34}}}, "escorts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "She felt dimly that she had made trouble in this family , and brought additional misfortune upon herself , for what kind of escorts would these whipped boys be ?", "context_len": 30}}}, "tramp": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 7, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "As she swung the gate open , and passed from the field into the lane , she saw a sight which for a moment made her hang back doubtfully , for a tramp lay stretched out full length asleep in the sunshine , his tattered clothes and broken boots a strange contrast to the bed of white daisies and celandine upon which he lay", "context_len": 64}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` It was cursed ill-luck , but how could we know that that dare-devil was a friend of Dewey 's ? If we 'd let well enough alone , we should n't have lost our horses and been compelled to tramp on foot over these mountains", "context_len": 46}}}, "potato": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 5, "context": "Suppose I were a bad potato , which I \u2019 m not , I \u2019 m thankful to say , would it make any difference because my great-great-grandfather had come from some interesting land across the sea ? Not in the least", "context_len": 42}}}, "parcels": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 14, "context": "Oh , with what a heavy heavy heart must you have put up those parcels , and written those inscriptions ! It will perhaps be a long time before you can realise with calm thankfulness that it is indeed so well with the child that you can rejoice in his safety , his happiness", "context_len": 54}}}, "provisions": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 20, "context": "Our defences are all complete ; our recent peace with Egypt has enabled us to fill up our magazines with provisions of all kinds ; the inhabitants of the Island have had ample warning to move into the town , carrying with them everything of value ; so the Turks will obtain but little plunder , and will be able to gather no means of subsistence on the island , as every animal has been driven within the walls , and even the unripe corn has been reaped and brought in", "context_len": 91}}}, "moccasins": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 6, "context": "Putting on a new pair of moccasins ( stillipa\u00ed a ) , he leaves the cabin , and during twelve moons abstains from eating the meat of young bucks , of turkey-cocks , fowls , peas and salt , and is also forbidden to pick his ears and to scratch his head with his fingers , but must use a small splinter to perform these operations", "context_len": 66}}}, "raves": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "When the children raves an ' tears , they ought to be licked , an ' when the lickin 's over , the reasons come", "context_len": 25}}}, "prison": {"freq": 119, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 117, "word_index": 52, "context": "A grand place , too , to work , you think ; and then you recall with a sigh that the light of that glorious mind has well-nigh gone out , 'neath the weight of physical weariness and infirmity , and then the solitary home begins to look a little like a prison in your eyes , as you realize how glad its inmate would be to exchange it for the Palace of that King whose divine intent for the world he has so marvellously interpreted for us all in the days when soul was still master of hand and brain", "context_len": 101}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Two months after being sent to prison my father attempted to escape and was shot and killed", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The man is John Howard , who travelled tens of thousands of miles , and spent many years in visiting gaols all over England and the Continent , and in endeavouring to render prison life less degrading and brutalising", "context_len": 39}}}, "energetically": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "When this could not be done , they were to fall down upon the ground when they saw this strange military engine coming , and locking their shields together over their heads , allow the wagon to roll on over them , bracing up energetically against its weight", "context_len": 48}}}, "vacation": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 10, "context": "When I told her we had a three months ' vacation , she never said , `` How delightful ! '' as most girls would , but calmly inquired what I took up in the holidays , and when I groaned at the very thought of taking up anything , she said so seriously , `` But you do n't let your mind lie fallow for three whole months ? '' And then she sighed a little , and added , half to herself , `` Some girls would give all the world for such a chance to read", "context_len": 99}}}, "pardon": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Ask your pardon , sir ; my tongue runs away with me sometimes , and thinking of Solomon made me say it , and 't is all in the Bible , sir , now is n't it ? But to go back to what I was a saying , you know well , sir , as no one would follow a chap like me as captain , but if we could get a real gentleman , and one used to command to lead us -- why , hang me , sir , if we would n't be masters of St", "context_len": 101}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Why , my dear brother , '' said Simeon , `` I am sure you will pardon me ; you know it is all love , my brother -- but , indeed , it was just as if you were knocking on a warming-pan -- tin , tin , tin , tin , without any intermission ! ''", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "' Wherefore I crave Thy Grace 's clemency and hope that thou wilt pardon me and my son this effrontery neither be wroth with us therefor", "context_len": 26}}}, "resentfulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Zebedee listened in quiet resentfulness , but Joe , who possessed a more volatile disposition , and who having satisfied his hunger was comparatively good-natured , remarked , `` What 'll ye do about Poacher , pop ? ''", "context_len": 39}}}, "volatile": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "And she was in danger of making so much noise with her hysterical chokes and gurgles that her sister was obliged to go to her and apply salts and sal volatile to quiet her , instead of pouring forth her indignation at her audacity", "context_len": 44}}}, "disposition": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 123, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}}}, "darkened": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 66, "context": "Why did he never see that look upon the face of Casson ? Why did the faces of the natives always wear to Bomba the same dull and stupid look , as dull and stupid , Bomba thought , and often more so , than the faces of his jungle friends ? Why were the faces of the Indians always the same , except when they darkened and grew fierce and stern ? Why did beauty not appeal to them as it did to him ?", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "The polishing of the darkened gold of her Will she had long known in the small unavoidable frictions of everyday life ; and the plunging of that Will into furnace-heat , and the straightening of its crookedness by means of heavy successive blows , she had begun to know in the death of her dear Father , and would soon know more fully through other sorrows coming after", "context_len": 68}}}, "transgression": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "My boy long remembered with horror and remorse his part in giving up to justice an unconscious offender , and seeing him pay for his transgression with his life", "context_len": 29}}}, "fold": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Ye 'll never git back in the fold agin unless some good sheep leads ye", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 60, "context": "Many facts therein given have been rejected by the Author as too harrowing in their nature ; and he has preferred to render the contemplation of woe and suffering less painful , by a display of those virtues of patience , resignation , and brave submission to the Divine will , which affliction never fails to bring out in the fold of Christ , whose promise stands ever fast , that the strength of His people shall be equal to their needs", "context_len": 82}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "Many and many a night , as he lay under some unwelcoming roof , or still oftener with the open sky above him , he had dreamed of that gentle mother who used always to fold the soft covering over him , and give him the good-night kiss", "context_len": 48}}}, "leads": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "Half an hour 's sculling took him out to a great buoy close by some sunken rocks ; and having made fast his boat to the rusty , barnacle-encrusted ring , he proceeded to bait his lines , and lowered down the leads into the deep water below", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Upwards it leads to the banqueting hall , and you can comprehend my escape this evening , '' said he ; `` but our path is now downwards , unless you would like to go up and see the drunken beasts of murderers snoring off their debauch upon the floor as they fell ; oh , that it were lawful for a Christian man to cut their throats as they lie ; many innocent lives would be saved thereby , which those brutes will live to destroy", "context_len": 88}}}, "wag": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` He 'll chase deer as long as he kin wag a leg", "context_len": 13}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "He was so fond of hunting , anyway , that the sight of a gun would drive him about crazy ; he would lick the barrel all over , and wag his tail so hard that it would lift his hind-legs off the ground", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "Jane , with her rugged , middle-aged face , baggy blouse , hob-nailed shoes and man 's hat , was so unfeminine a figure as she plowed and planted her little vega , that some village wag had once referred to her as `` Annie Laurie", "context_len": 46}}}, "confusedly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Confusedly , he tried to think of the things she had said to him , but beneath the wild spell of that performance he could reason nothing out , and could only stare dazedly at this wreck of what had once been a great genius", "context_len": 45}}}, "kinder": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "His heart 's been left in the grave with the poor boy , it 's true , but , mark my words , if there 's a soul in trouble in all the parish it 's no kinder friend they 'll find than Mr", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 85, "context": "Aylmer the less on this occasion had many wild and exciting thoughts with regard to Miss Pratt and the other neighbors at Dawlish , also with regard to Sukey ; but still , her thought above all other thoughts was the consciousness that soon her beloved child would be done honor to , and her eyes , silly enough in expression , were now so full of love that many people thought her a good-natured and pleasant-looking woman , and in reality gave her far kinder thoughts than they did to Mrs", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 1, "context": "I kinder felt it to be my duty , you see , and then if you liked to say to me , ` Here , Zekle Wynn , here 's five or ten pounds for you for what you did , ' why you could , you know ; but if you did n't , why it would n't matter a bit , for I always feel as if it was a man 's duty not to take no money 'less he 's earned it", "context_len": 85}}}, "cherish": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 52, "context": "And though Alexander must have been aware of all these advantages of the act , still no one could have thought of or adopted such a plan unless he was accustomed to consider and regard , in his dealings with others , the feelings and affections of the heart , and to cherish a warm sympathy for them", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "I trust , he added , with a regret that was undoubtedly feigned , that you lads won \u2019 t cherish any hard feelings ?", "context_len": 25}}}, "grazed": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 20, "context": "The haste with which he shot and the motion of the boat disturbed his aim , so that it just grazed the animal \u2019 s head , inflicting a slight flesh wound , but no mortal injury", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Gradually he made the discovery that beyond a grazed instep , for one of the barbs had penetrated his boot , he was unwounded", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The young skipper made his way forward to where Crayle sat binding a handkerchief around his grazed shin", "context_len": 18}}}, "stammered": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 19, "context": "Astonished and delighted at the reward , which represented a very large sum in those days , the sailor stammered his thanks , and added , I hope tonight that if I again have charge of a fire ship , I may be able to do more to prove to your Highness how grateful I am for the gift", "context_len": 59}}}, "footstool": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "Them as do n't like 'em do n't want to give 'em standin ' room on this airth , but you walk right up to 'em an ' say , 'This dog has as good a right to a place on God 's footstool as you hev , an ' I 'm goin ' to see he gits it", "context_len": 59}}}, "gits": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "Them as do n't like 'em do n't want to give 'em standin ' room on this airth , but you walk right up to 'em an ' say , 'This dog has as good a right to a place on God 's footstool as you hev , an ' I 'm goin ' to see he gits it", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Pshaw ! dat ain \u2019 t nuffin , he said when the wife of Abner Fleming complimented him ; I \u2019 spects to do de same thing a good many more times afore we gits across de plains ; de fac \u2019 is , I \u2019 m on to dem Injins and dey \u2019 ll find it out ; when dey wokes up Jethro Mix dey wokes up de wrong passenger and dere \u2019 s gwine to be trouble in de land", "context_len": 82}}}, "negative": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 56, "context": "Of the sun-worship , which underlies the religions of all the tribes in the Gulf territories , only slight intimations are contained in the Kasi'hta legend , and the important problem , whether the Creeks ever crossed the Mississippi river from west to east in their migrations , seems to be settled by it in the negative , although other legends may be adduced as speaking in its favor", "context_len": 69}}}, "smartness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "Compared with the somewhat boisterous conditions obtaining on board Transport 99 , the Gosport was a floating model of smartness and efficiency", "context_len": 22}}}, "verily": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 35, "context": "[ 358 ] Hast thou any knowledge of her , O Vizier , and knowest thou what is her want ? '' `` O our lord the Sultan , '' replied the Vizier , `` verily women are little of wit ; and most like this woman cometh to complain to thee of her husband or one of her folk , '' The Sultan was not content with the Vizier 's reply , but bade him , an she came again to the Divan , bring her before him forthright ; [ 359 ] whereupon the Vizier laid his hand on his head and answered , `` Hearkening and obedience , O our lord the Sultan", "context_len": 116}}}, "overawed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "This being his first visit to any castle whatever , he was far too much astonished and overawed by everything he saw to be able to think of applying his mind to mere historical detail", "context_len": 35}}}, "reproachful": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 2, "context": "Father 's reproachful face seemed to rise up out of the darkness , asking 'Where is Bobby ? What have you done with my little boy ? ' Good resolutions made , and alas ! too often forgotten , crowded in now upon her remembrance , and as she listened to the roar of the river , she thought how strange it would seem that they two , so full of life , might in a few hours be floating very still and silent upon that flowing stream , with the world only a memory behind them", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The animal eluded him , gave him one reproachful look , turned its nose east , and shot off , headed for home like an arrow", "context_len": 26}}}, "drooping": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 40, "context": "He came down feet foremost , however , and they sauntered off along the road to the old water-mill , where the miller 's man was slinging a sack of flour on to a patient donkey who stood , with drooping ears , eyeing the burden which he must carry up far into the mountains , while his mistress , a little black-eyed Welshwoman , poured forth a torrent of gossip in high-pitched tones", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "The little dog , who had at first seemed to fear some harm threatened its master , frisked back and forth before them , barking frantically , finally sitting up on its haunches with its forward paws drooping , its mouth open and its protruding tongue quivering ; for at last it appeared to comprehend that there was really no danger , and this affair was one over which even a small yellow dog should laugh and be happy", "context_len": 79}}}, "suffering": {"freq": 121, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was his brother , I suppose , who taught him to abhor it ; and perhaps it was his own suffering from it in part ; for he , too , sometimes shed bitter tears over such a knot , as I have seen hapless little wretches do , tearing at it with their nails and gnawing at it with their teeth , knowing that the time was passing when they could hope to hide the fact that they had been in swimming , and foreseeing no remedy but to cut off the sleeve above the knot , or else put on their clothes without the shirt , and trust to untying the knot when it got dry", "context_len": 119}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 26, "context": "But although I had passed through the day unscathed , so far as actual bodily injury was concerned , it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 127, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}}}, "straightening": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Now , sons , '' he said , straightening himself up and drawing on his woollen mittens , `` I 'm goin ' one way an ' you another , but if ye act contrairy an ' pouty to that leetle gal , I 'll know it , for she 's goin ' to write me , an ' if there 's any complaint , there 'll be such a wallopin ' as these ones this mornin ' would be a shadder an ' a dream to", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The polishing of the darkened gold of her Will she had long known in the small unavoidable frictions of everyday life ; and the plunging of that Will into furnace-heat , and the straightening of its crookedness by means of heavy successive blows , she had begun to know in the death of her dear Father , and would soon know more fully through other sorrows coming after", "context_len": 68}}}, "mittens": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 84, "context": "The big drawing-room had been shut up for many years ; the Queen-Anne chairs and china-cabinets were swathed in wrappers , and the ornaments put away in boxes ; but sometimes the children would steal in and open the shutters to look at the portraits which hung upon the oak-panelled walls -- stately gentlemen with wigs and lace frills , whose eyes seemed to follow you about the room ; haughty dames with powdered hair and patches ; stiff little girls in hoops and mittens , and pretty young ladies attired as shepherdesses or classic goddesses , with cupids and nymphs in the background", "context_len": 104}}}, "pouty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` Now , sons , '' he said , straightening himself up and drawing on his woollen mittens , `` I 'm goin ' one way an ' you another , but if ye act contrairy an ' pouty to that leetle gal , I 'll know it , for she 's goin ' to write me , an ' if there 's any complaint , there 'll be such a wallopin ' as these ones this mornin ' would be a shadder an ' a dream to", "context_len": 88}}}, "lecture": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 7, "context": "Edna felt that she had brought this lecture upon Reliance and felt rather badly to have done so , but the prospect of buckwheat cakes soon drove her self-reproach away and she went in to say good morning to her grandparents , well satisfied with the world in general and content to look ahead rather than at what was now past and gone , and which could not be altered", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 98, "context": "Aunt Helen fondly imagined herself to be a great disciplinarian , but her own lively youth was still such a recent remembrance that her eyes were wont to twinkle and the corners of her mouth to twitch in the middle of her severest scoldings , and the children always had a feeling that so long as they did not do anything rude or wrong , or run into any very imminent danger , their escapades were secretly condoned by their aunt , who admired pluck and spirit , however much she might feel it incumbent upon her to lecture them", "context_len": 100}}}, "narrowly": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 35, "context": "One evening there were signs of a change of weather , and by morning it was blowing a furious gale from the north ; in spite of the efforts of the rowers , the galley narrowly escaped being driven ashore ; but she at last gained the shelter of an island , and anchored under its lee , the slaves being utterly worn out by continuous exertion", "context_len": 67}}}, "expressionless": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "She looked at the solitary lamp with its grotesquely hideous ornament of red flannel , at Susan 's expressionless , freckled face , at the boys in their copper-toed boots and overalls , at the good-natured , but hopelessly common-place Martha Spriggs , with her thin hair drawn tight into a knob the size of a bullet , and her bare arms akimbo", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "We plied him with eager questions , but his only reply was an expressionless shake of his massive head , and for the time being vague surmises had to suffice", "context_len": 30}}}, "jerks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Pop , ca n't we come back after we take her out ? '' they exclaimed , with backward jerks of their heads toward 'Tilda Jane", "context_len": 27}}}, "fondling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "And now , whether Miss Kitty saw through the crack of the drawer that her young master was fondling a new pet , or whether she only guessed at it , or whether she thought it hard that fun should be going on in which she had no share , can not be told ; but just then there came from her prison-place such a hissing and sputtering and scratching that every one of the children set up a shout of laughter", "context_len": 82}}}, "unmannerly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 99, "context": "maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions , but she can not look on me as all a stranger , even if she hold no memory of those old days at Highgate", "context_len": 128}}}, "weeds": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 56, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Our work is more like clearing in backwoods , -- there are huge trees and boulders cumbering the ground ; not just weeds overspreading a garden that once was a little cultivated", "context_len": 32}}}, "sowed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` For our Maggie and Bessie , the dear little workers in the garden of the Lord , who tended the Christian plants of patience , kindness , and forbearance , till their lovely blossoms overran the evil weeds of malice and ill-will , and sowed the seeds of that which brought forth fruit for the glory of God", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "SOWED BY THE WIND or the Poor Boy \u2019 s Fortune By Rev", "context_len": 13}}}, "friendliness": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 40, "context": "In reply to this he gave a profound bow , and told her , with a certain awkward jerk of his body , that he never gave orders in advance , that he never gave orders on the score of friendliness , that when it came to the relations between publisher and artist he was brutal", "context_len": 56}}}, "openly": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 45, "context": "But more incomprehensible than the existence of so bad a man in such a dark age is the renewed confidence ever accorded him , when , after more than once betraying the armies of his country into the hands of their foes , and fighting openly in the hostile cause , he is again forgiven , nay , received into favour , and sent once more to command the men he has already deceived , until he repeats the experiment , and when it fails is again admitted into confidence", "context_len": 90}}}, "unkind": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 13, "context": "But that this innocent little child , to whom she had been so unkind , should see for herself that she had acted in an improper way , and one that was only fit for the kitchen or stable , and should tell her so , and show such surprise at hearing her call herself a lady , was very mortifying , and she could not forget it", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "She had a very strong objection to unkind discussion of people behind their backs", "context_len": 14}}}, "neglectful": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The neglectful guard found some comfort in another fact : he was gifted with an unusually keen sense of hearing , and believed his ears would tell him as much as his eyes", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "`` neglectful of the love of thee", "context_len": 7}}}, "noon": {"freq": 84, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Toward noon the snow ceased falling , as Lucas had predicted , the sun came out brilliantly , and they began making preparations for departure", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 44, "context": "As with everything that boys do , these mills were mostly failures ; the pins which supported the wheels were always giving way ; and though there were instances of boys who started their wheels at recess and found them still fluttering away at noon when they came out of school , none ever carried his enterprise so far as to spin the cotton blowing from the balls of the cottonwood-tree by the shore , as they all meant to do", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was nearly noon when I was conducted back to my hut after my futile attempt to cure the king ; and it was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant", "context_len": 128}}}, "predicted": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was exactly as Edna had predicted ; Miss Eloise entered into the plan with the greatest eagerness , and when Ben had opened up his plot to her and had showed her how he had planned the scenes she said she would take a few minutes to think it over and then she thought she could give him some of the needed dialogue , and before they left Ben had taken down as much as was necessary for this first time , promising to come back for the rest", "context_len": 90}}}, "brilliantly": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 69, "context": "Meanwhile , since I was almost naked , and was hoping soon to find myself once more among civilised people , it occurred to me that the skin of the leopard which had wrought this dire tragedy might be of use to me as material out of which to fashion some sort of a garment ; and , therefore , while the flames of the pyre were still blazing brilliantly I utilised their light to enable me to strip the pelt off the great carcase", "context_len": 85}}}, "departure": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 55, "context": "Sir Knights , he said , I have heard from my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "humility": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` She came up to my room -- I will confess to you that I was hiding from her , absolutely hiding , and shaking from head to foot , scarcely knowing what to do -- and she came in as bold as brass , and yet with a new sort of humility about her , and she said to me , 'Will you forgive me ? And if you forgive me , will you come downstairs and let me put my hand inside your arm ? ' And somehow , although it was the very last thing on earth that I wanted to do , I did it ; and now here I am , and I do n't feel nearly so much afraid of her as", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Are we not bound to humility ? And here you are all longing for the plaudits of the nobles and ladies of Genoa !", "context_len": 24}}}, "unselfishness": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "Could it be that her father had come at last ? That , after all her waiting , she was to see him and talk with him and sob out on his breast her appreciation of his long labors in her behalf , his kindness , unselfishness and goodness ?", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "He had comrades enough and went with everybody , but till he formed that friendship with the queer fellow whom I have told of , he had no friend among the boys ; and I very much doubt whether small boys understand friendship , or can feel it as they do afterwards , in its tenderness and unselfishness", "context_len": 58}}}, "thawed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "His mother plunged them in a bowl of ice-cold water , and then rubbed them with flannel , and so thawed them out ; but that could not save him from the pain of their coming to : it was intense , and there must have been a time afterwards when he did not use his feet", "context_len": 57}}}, "reserve": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 106, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The Episcopal Purple , my grand new dress , I reserve for grand occasions", "context_len": 14}}}, "ridiculous": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 32, "context": "Have a brother about four years older than yourself , I should say ; and if your temper is hot , and your disposition revengeful , and you are a vain and ridiculous dreamer at the same time that you are eager to excel in feats of strength and games of skill , and to do everything that the other fellows do , and are ashamed to be better than the worst boy in the crowd , your brother can be of the greatest use to you , with his larger experience and wisdom", "context_len": 94}}}, "fits": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 29, "context": "Grandpapa Duncan promised that for each day , during the next three weeks , in which Bessie did not lose her temper and give way to one of her fits of passion , or in which Maggie did not fall into any great carelessness or disobedience , he would give twenty cents to each little girl", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "If a plebe 's swelled head shrinks at the rate of three inches a day , how many months will it be before it fits his brains ? ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "infirm": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Zebedee carried the infirm Gippie , who otherwise would have sunk to his neck in the snow , Poacher soberly plunged his way along , while Joe assisted 'Tilda Jane in keeping her equilibrium", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Ours is such a dear little Church , -- I am not aware that there is one really black sheep in it , though there are some infirm ones", "context_len": 29}}}, "equilibrium": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "For two or three strides it seemed that he must plunge headlong with it , and then he regained his equilibrium and brought a gasping chorus of cries from the southern side of the field as he ran on toward Clearport \u2019 s goal", "context_len": 44}}}, "expert": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "With some show of hesitation the man surrendered the thing , and upon inspection I found it to be a reed of about a foot in length , with a mouthpiece shaped something like that of a whistle , and with four small holes drilled in the length of the tube , whereby an expert performer might produce seven distinct tones ; but the tones were not consecutive , and the instrument was altogether a very poor and inefficient affair", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 22, "context": "The particular red man in whom we are now interested had a formidable bow at command , and no doubt was an expert in its use , but before discharging an arrow , he must snatch it from behind his shoulder , fit it to the string and aim", "context_len": 49}}}, "span": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "At last the little toilettes were completed , even to the reformation of Albert 's curls around an ivory curling stick ; and with embroidered dress and well-starched kilt none the worse for the decorous experiences of the morning , they emerged from the little boudoir as spick and span as from the depths of the traditional bandbox", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Come on , my lady , let 's see if I can span your slender waist", "context_len": 17}}}, "serene": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 42, "context": "Having thus done all that a commander could do to ensure the success of the day , he rode back to the high ground from which he could command a full view of the battle , and with a face calm and serene waited for the French attack", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "Sweet Grandmamma continues much the same , -- serene , -- without pain , not exactly ill , but so delicate that she is still carried up and down stairs , and sees none of the family but Aunt Clara and myself , and only a little of me", "context_len": 49}}}, "inhabited": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 83, "context": "518 , in describing the mounds of the country , states `` that the region lying between Savanna river and Oakmulge , east and west , and from the sea coast ( of the Atlantic ) to the Cherokee or Apalachean mountains ( filled with these mounds ) was possessed by the Cherokees since the arrival of the Europeans ; but they were afterwards dispossessed by the Muscogulges , and all that country was probably , many ages preceding the Cherokee invasion , inhabited by one nation or confederacy ( unknown to the Cherokees , Creeks )", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "In this alphabetic list of ancient Creek towns and villages I have included all the names of inhabited places which I have found recorded before the emigration of the people to the Indian Territory", "context_len": 34}}}, "wilderness": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Because I know the secrets of the prison house -- knew them before the Danes had murdered the once happy dwellers in this garden of England , which they have made a howling wilderness ; hence I escaped the wrath of the furious parricide , whom the saints destroy , with ease , and laughed in security at their vain efforts to take me ; but we must waste no time ; it yet wants five hours to daybreak ; within those five hours we must reach the opposite shore", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "He only knew that Solomon Whistler lived at the poor-house beyond the eastern border of the town , and that he ranged between this sojourn and the illimitable wilderness north of the town on the western shore of the river", "context_len": 40}}}, "voiceless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "As soon as Kitty had hugged her father , one close , passionate , voiceless hug , she released him , stepped back a pace , looked him in the face , and then said eagerly , `` Come away quickly , father ; there is a meadow at the back of the cherry orchard which we can have quite to ourselves", "context_len": 62}}}, "clad": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 106, "context": "of his love and affection for his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour , was wont , every day , when he awoke from his sleep , to open the window and look at her therefrom ; so he arose on the morrow , according to his wont , and opened his chamber-window , so he might see his daughter ; but [ 571 ] when he put out his head and looked for Alaeddin 's palace , he beheld nothing but a place swept [ and level ] , like as it was aforetime , and saw neither palace nor inhabitants ; [ 572 ] whereat amazement clad him and his wit was bewildered and he fell to rubbing his eyes , so haply they were bleared or dimmed", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 3, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 32, "context": "Emma 's work was taken by one of the other servants and the morning long hands and tongues were busy , so that by lunch time the three little dolls appeared nicely clad , and one of Edna 's and one of Jennie 's were nearly ready", "context_len": 47}}}, "evidences": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 47, "context": "The chums felt a little lonesome after his departure , but it soon wore off , for there was much to do , to get in wood and water , straighten up the camp , and prepare for a storm , which , according to all the evidences , was soon to break", "context_len": 53}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "As we grew older , we not only more fully understood the exceptional boundlessness of old-fashioned hospitality and kindness which that house and household exemplified thoroughly , but we came to understand somewhat of the heart-source whence issued that truest manifestation , of everyday religion , which evidences itself in an absolutely unselfish consecration , -- consistent , unreserved , and essentially practical , -- for everyday wear , and not only under stimulating environments", "context_len": 75}}}, "teams": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 12, "context": "The home crowd was still making a terrific uproar as the two teams once more spread out upon the field , and there was every reason why that portion of the spectators should rejoice ; for Clearport had won the lead by a single point , and the course of the game in the second half seemed to promise beyond doubt that this lead could be held", "context_len": 67}}}, "runners": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "They were both capital runners , having had plenty of practice at cricket and rounders , but I do not think they ever ran so fast in their lives as when they were chased by the bees", "context_len": 37}}}, "sparsely": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "Then , taking a bow and quiver of arrows , together with a brace of spears , out of the canoe , and signing to me to do the same , Ama led the way through the dense growth bordering the river bank , until we reached an open grassy space of about twenty acres , sparsely dotted here and there with magnificent trees ; and here Ama signified that we were to camp for the day", "context_len": 77}}}, "distant": {"freq": 151, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 117, "word_index": 69, "context": "it grew darker and darker , came forth another and another star , and looked down on the world which God had loved so much , till the whole sky was brilliant with them ; the soft , cool sea-breeze came gently in at the windows , bringing with it the gentle plash of the waves upon the shore , mingled with the chirp of the crickets and the distant hum of voices from the far end of the piazza ; but no one came near or disturbed them ; and still the colonel sat with his face turned towards the sea , without either speaking or moving , till his wife , as she sat with her hand in his , wondered if he could be asleep", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 45, "context": "Florence Douglas was the ward of the Albany merchant , John Campbell , who by the terms of her father 's will was entrusted with the care of her large property till she had attained the age of twenty-five , a period nearly a year distant", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 67, "context": "As the day went on , he saw that the coastline was now east and west , and felt that he must be off the most northerly point of the promontory ; he accordingly laid his course to the northeast , which would take him close to Cape Saloman , the most easterly point of Crete , and from two hundred and fifty to three hundred miles distant", "context_len": 68}}}, "farmhouses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "The trees also grew more sparsely , and soon gave place to clearings , then the distant roof of a barn appeared , and finally a long , thin string of small farmhouses winding down a bleak road before them", "context_len": 40}}}, "bleak": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "News travels apace , and in less than an hour it was all over our village that Joyce had by some means obtained a file , cut through his fetters , and , after a murderous attack on his jailer , had broken out of Midhurst Jail , and was last seen making his way towards the bleak Sussex Downs", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Although I had oft journeyed across this bleak hill , never before had I seen it under such depressing circumstances", "context_len": 20}}}, "unspeakably": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 95, "context": "Very slowly and gradually the storm worked its way toward the zenith , gathering intensity as it rose , and at length -- probably about ten o'clock -- the first drops of rain , hot and heavy , like gouts of blood , began to fall , quickly increasing to a drenching downpour , accompanied by lightning , green , rose-tinted , violet , sun-bright , that lighted up the town until every object , however minute , was as clearly visible as in broad daylight , while the ceaseless crashing of the thunder was unspeakably appalling", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I dread her unspeakably , '' said Mrs", "context_len": 9}}}, "chilling": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "Then he and all the people were filled with still greater terror and turned to flee down the mountains , but the snow flakes -- the deadly arrows of the North King , came faster and faster , falling before as well as behind them , clogging the feet and chilling the life-blood of the people Alstarnah had betrayed", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 86, "context": "He felt better after this and went in ; but that night the excitement of the adventure and the effects of his immersion were sufficient to keep him awake hour after hour , while when he dropped off into an uneasy slumber it was for his mind to be haunted by dreams in which he was being dragged down into the depths of the sea by a strange monster that clung to his limbs and writhed about him , making him shudder as he felt the chilling embrace", "context_len": 88}}}, "deathly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "He drew up before the first in the string of houses , -- a poor enough place , and unspeakably chilling in its deathly whiteness", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "He was deathly sick , as he reeled and staggered on , and when he reached home , and showed himself white and haggard to his frightened mother , he had scarcely strength to gasp out a confession of his attempt to retrieve the family honor by learning to chew tobacco", "context_len": 51}}}, "whiteness": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "The whiteness of the snow made it possible for her to see a little of the way when she first started , but as she went on and it grew darker she began to wonder if she were in the road", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "As he leaned against the rail , staring sadly out to sea , the sunlight flashed into snowy whiteness the distant royals and top-gallant sails of a square-rigger beating to the westward under a foreign flag", "context_len": 36}}}, "objected": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 12, "context": "They went in search of Tiddlywinks ' mother , but Madam Pittypat objected to being made a baby of , for , though she was gentle enough , she squirmed and twisted herself out of every garment they tried upon her , and , at the first opportunity , walked off in a most dignified manner , as though she would say : `` Such a way to treat the mother of a family ! ''", "context_len": 76}}}, "blot": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 82, "context": "`` `` Far be it '' ( as the Arabs say ) that we should do so cruel a wrong to so well and justly beloved a memory as that of Jacques Cazotte as to attempt to perpetuate the remembrance of a literary crime which one can hardly believe him to have committed in sober earnest ! Rather let us seek to bury in oblivion this his one offence and suffer kind Lethe with its beneficent waters to wash this `` adulterous blot '' from his else unsullied name", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "landscape": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 67, "context": "`` Yes , they would if they were hungry , but Lammie-noo has probably not been here more than a couple of nights , and if the bears have been roaming in some other direction they would not get wind of the fact that there was a nice plump lamb on the old Lonesome Hill farm -- -- Come up higher , and we 'll spy the landscape o'er", "context_len": 69}}}, "wavering": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 71, "context": "At the moment I took but scant notice of the circumstance , for the flickering flames of the fire which was always kindled upon such occasions played strange pranks with the lights and shadows , and often imparted a weird effect of movement to stationery and even inanimate objects ; but presently , happening to again glance in that direction , my eye was once more caught by the same queer wavering movement", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "If the captain thought so , however , he succeeded marvelously well in hiding his feelings , trying his best all the time to brace his teammates up , encouraging the equally staunch , chiding a few who showed symptoms of wavering , and entreating one or two who apparently had lost heart", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` What for ? '' propounded a gloomy , wavering voice", "context_len": 11}}}, "slatternly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "The whole family was gathered together on the grass round the wood fire , the smoke of which rose up faintly blue against the russet of the beech-woods behind -- handsome , fierce-eyed men , lying slouching idly on the ground smoking short black pipes ; slatternly women with gay handkerchiefs tied round their black hair , bustling about with something savoury inside tin cans ; ragged brown-skinned little children , gnawing at bones with savage haste ; while a few disreputable dogs waited eagerly for the scraps that were thrown to them from time to time", "context_len": 97}}}, "reluctant": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 40, "context": "We have had a beautiful time , said Marie-Celeste , with a sigh , as though unable to give full expression to her appreciation ; but I hope we have n't stayed too long ; and then , as though reluctant to take final leave of the little Isabel , she added : Do n't you think it is more comfortable just to be one of the people , and be a regular little girl , and grow up always near your mother , like other children ?", "context_len": 88}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` The fact of the matter was that he saw that Mark was in a position to force a fight if he chose , and the yearling was by no means reluctant , anyhow", "context_len": 34}}}, "stroked": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 76, "context": "Now , by the decree of destiny , Fatimeh came down on the morrow to the city and the enchanter , going forth the Khan in the morning , saw the folk crowding together ; so he went up , to see what was toward , and found Fatimeh standing , whilst every one who had a pain or an ache came to her , seeking her blessing and soliciting her prayers , and whenas she stroked him , he was made whole of his ailment", "context_len": 86}}}, "repeatedly": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 91, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}}}, "hearty": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 74, "context": "The Spaniards did not relish this , and were going to board the tiny English craft , but again they were forestalled ; for Cochrane with all his men took the Gamo by storm , killed some , and frightened others ; and ere long a marvellous sight was witnessed at Minorca , the great Gamo was brought by the Speedy into the harbour , with over 263 men on board , hale and hearty , whilst Cochrane never had a fifth of that number !", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 6, "context": "Certain tones in the man 's hearty voice reminded me of the boy 's sweet notes , and was n't there a queer suggestion of each other when I stood near them ? We Shetland ponies as I have said before are very close to human beings for our ancestors were literally brought up with the children in the crofters ' huts , and my mother has often told me that her mother had many a lick at the family platter and many a time her soft muzzle was buried in the children 's necks", "context_len": 95}}}, "compared": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then what is that field compared to all England , or England to Europe , or Europe to the whole world ? Neither your little head , nor the wisest man \u2019 s , can imagine how many blossoms and how many insects there are on this great globe , -- it makes one almost giddy to think of it , -- and then to consider that all the world itself is only like a speck in God \u2019 s Creation , that there are said to be eighty millions of fixed stars , each of which has very likely worlds moving round it", "context_len": 104}}}, "littered": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "Up-stairs were four rooms , two unfurnished , one having something the appearance of a spare room left long unoccupied , the other smelling of tobacco , exceedingly untidy , littered with old clothes , fishing rods , bats , cartridge shells , and other boyish and manly belongings", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "I guess I can take the message myself , though , '' and he sat down at the littered table , pushing the things aside , to write the dispatch", "context_len": 30}}}, "soiled": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 108, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 30, "context": "The Sergeant , having seen that a start was about to be made , backed out of the dug-out , dropped the tapestried curtain -- it was a ragged and soiled ground-sheet -- over the entrance , and disappeared along the narrow trench", "context_len": 43}}}, "frowsy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Pots and pans were heaped in a corner of the room , the table was littered with soiled dishes , the woman herself was unkempt , frowsy , and dispirited in appearance", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Did the Queen 's mother come out and ask you to come in ? whispered Marie-Celeste , detaining Albert by main force , while she straightened his necktie and gave his hopelessly frowsy curls a rearranging touch", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "He had the same squat , sturdy figure , the coarse black hair dangling about his bare shoulders , and growing low upon his forehead , the naked chest , the frowsy hunting shirt of deerskin , with leggings and dilapidated moccasins", "context_len": 42}}}, "dispirited": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "He passed through all the streets inflaming the curiosity of the watchmen ; the darkness ( for there were very few lamps or lights of any kind , in those days , for public use ) was intense , a drizzling rain was falling , and at length , weary , wet , and dispirited , he returned to the palace , and found that the council , tired of waiting , had at length broken up", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "When the party of Anlaf arrived at the northern gate , crossing the summit of the ascent on that side , they found it open and almost unguarded , so slight was the danger from the dispirited English -- now too accustomed to the idea of a foe in the heart of the land", "context_len": 54}}}, "accumulation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "The frequent removals of towns to new sites , lying at short distances only , may be easily explained by the unhealthiness of the old site , produced by the constant accumulation of refuse and filth around the towns , which never had anything like sewers or efficient regulations of sanitary police", "context_len": 52}}}, "wearily": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 38, "context": "But although I had passed through the day unscathed , so far as actual bodily injury was concerned , it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The lesson dragged on wearily for awhile , till they were disturbed by a tap at the door , and a small girl from one of the lower classes entered , full of importance at her errand", "context_len": 37}}}, "rustic": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 79, "context": "Beyond the fact that a lady in white was supposed to haunt both the ruins and the oak-wood , weeping and wringing her hands in orthodox ghostly fashion , the children had not been able to learn much of the story , for there were so many and divergent accounts of it , all told with uncertainty as to names and dates , and in that very oracular -- not to say muddled -- style sometimes indulged in by rustic historians", "context_len": 81}}}, "deliberation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "This young Greek , a stranger to Rhodes , was in communication not only with some of the slaves , but with a prison official , and the matter appeared so grave to Gervaise that , after some deliberation , he thought it was too important for him to endeavour to follow out alone , and that it was necessary to lay it before the bailiff", "context_len": 66}}}, "weighed": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 78, "context": "When [ 312 ] the goldsmith heard this , `` Out on this accursed one , '' cried he , `` who fleeceth the servants of God the Most High ! '' Then he looked at Alaeddin and said to him , `` O my son , this Jew is a cheat , who hath cheated thee and laughed at thee , for that the silver of this thy platter is pure and fine ; and I have weighed it and find its worth threescore diners and ten ; so , an it please thee take its price , take [ it ]", "context_len": 103}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Bishop , appealed to these simple savages ; a sentimental ditty , such as `` The Anchor 's weighed '' or `` Tom Bowling , '' would hold them breathless and entranced ; `` Rule , Britannia ! '' or `` Should He upbraid '' set them quivering with excitement ; and they seemed to know by intuition that `` The Sailor 's Hornpipe '' was written to be danced to , and they danced to it accordingly a wild , furious , mad fandango in which the extraordinary nature of the gambols of the performers was only equalled by the ecstasy of their enjoyment", "context_len": 105}}}, "statement": {"freq": 73, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` This same morning -- and my friend Indian will substantiate my statement , for he was there -- a low , ignorant cadet corporal did enter into my room , for inspection , by Zeus , and after generally displaying his ill-manners , he turned to me and conveyed the extraordinary information that , according to rules , forsooth , I must be deprived of the dearest object of my affections , solace of my weary hours , my friend in time of need , my companion in sickness , which through all the trials of adversity has stuck to me closer than a brother , my only joy , my -- -- ''", "context_len": 115}}}, "puzzle": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 75, "context": "`` Why you see , '' Edna began , `` Nettie has been taken into the club , and when her time comes to have the club meeting she wo n't have any way of getting the refreshments , so we thought and thought of what we could do to get some money , and last night I saw in the Children 's Corner of the Times that they would give prizes for guessing a puzzle , you know those puzzles , Cousin Ben", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Besides this attraction , there was a labyrinth , or puzzle , as the boys called it , of paths that wound in and out among bushes , so that when you got inside you were lucky if you could find your way out", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was certainly one thing to housekeep with Aunt Helen at her elbow to advise , and quite another to puzzle it out with only her memory and the cookery-book for assistance", "context_len": 32}}}, "chilly": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 52, "context": "But alas ! the doctor was not at home , he was out at church , and so was the missis ; he would be back in about an hour ; would the woman call again in an hour ? Hannah 's heart sank within her ; the night had turned very chilly , and little Roy , sleeping heavily in her arms , seemed to grow colder and colder ; dare she keep him in the winter streets for a whole hour ?", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "It was a dull day , dull as the gloom which was upon our spirits ; the very heavens seemed to have put on funeral attire , and the chilly wind which swept over the scene seemed quite at home", "context_len": 40}}}, "stairway": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 55, "context": "But you want some breakfast , dear , do n't you ? Marie-Celeste shook her head , but as she seemed to know perfectly well what she did want , he suffered her to lead him over the high sill that keeps the water from rushing indoors in rough weather , and past the main stairway , and into a far corner of the library", "context_len": 65}}}, "cher": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Bet cher life , b'gee , '' responded Mark , whereupon his new acquaintance broke into one of his merry laughs", "context_len": 22}}}, "prepossessed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "'Tilda Jane was not prepossessed in his favour as he came lumbering down the staircase , and she was still less so when he stood before her", "context_len": 27}}}, "lumbering": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "The left arm hung to his hip while the other lay on the top of the rock , and was extended full length , the hand closed around the butt of a revolver which was leveled at the mountainous brute , lumbering heavily forward with his head swinging from side to side", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "The six lumbering wagons were drawn up in a circle , the space inside being about a hundred feet across", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "He wandered back to the station where he sat down on one of the stools that had been brought outside and gloomily watched the lumbering wagons as they swung slowly westward under the strenuous pull of the oxen", "context_len": 38}}}, "youthful": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 29, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "They were just like youthful undeveloped ponies , loving , teasing , rebelling , watching each other , and over them they knew was the wise whip hand of the parents", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Alexander considered it somewhat uncertain whether the people of all these states and cities would be disposed to transfer readily , to so youthful a prince as he , the high commission which his father , a very powerful monarch and soldier , had extorted from them with so much difficulty", "context_len": 51}}}, "snatched": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 42, "context": "He had purloined David 's scarf from the saddle-room , and dropped it into the horse-trough , had filled Bobby 's hat with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread", "context_len": 128}}}, "pocketed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "I don \u2019 t understand all your high words , stranger , said the old lady , as with a look of satisfaction she pocketed the money , but I \u2019 ll be glad to see you again any time", "context_len": 40}}}, "mature": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` She 's right , '' said the more mature fox", "context_len": 11}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was Washington 's aim to mature and perfect organizations which would move on prosperously of themselves , without him ; and he was continually withdrawing his hand from action and control in public affairs , taking a higher pleasure in the independent working of the institutions which he had formed and protected , than in exercising , himself , a high personal power", "context_len": 64}}}, "coin": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 122, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}}}, "sidled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Louis Gerald was walking in one of the poorer districts of the city , when a boy of ten , with a thin , pallid face and shabby clothes , sidled up to him", "context_len": 34}}}, "covetous": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Generous prince , '' said he , `` hitherto I have had a covetous desire of your kingdom , but now I do yet more earnestly covet your friendship ; your father and my father have each reigned over the land , let us divide the inheritance like brothers", "context_len": 50}}}, "apparel": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 40, "context": "His mother forewent them and the people of the quarter , when they saw that rare and magnificent sight , stood looking and marvelling and gazing upon the faces of the slave-girls and their grace and goodliness [ and their apparel ] , for that they were clad in clothes all inwoven with gold and studded with jewels ; nay , the least one 's clothes of them were worth thousands", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He had some wearing apparel thrown over his arm , and dropped it the moment he was inside the room", "context_len": 20}}}, "stripes": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 38, "context": "He belonged to a military company of big boys that had real wooden guns , such as the little boys never could get , and silk oil-cloth caps , and nankeen roundabouts , and white pantaloons with black stripes down the legs ; and once they marched out to a boy 's that had a father that had a farm , and he gave them all a free dinner in an arbor before the house ; bread and butter , and apple-butter , and molasses and pound cake , and peaches and apples ; it was splendid", "context_len": 97}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "But if you does your duty and sticks by your friends you will wear the blue coat mit the brass stripes on the sleeve , and you will be glad you stayed by steam", "context_len": 34}}}, "premonition": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` So do I , '' replied 'Tilda Jane , and , with a premonition of what was coming , she turned her head and gazed out the window", "context_len": 29}}}, "caller": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 26, "context": "I rushed off at once to tell Miss Martin I was sure it was all a horrible mistake , but she was engaged with a tiresome caller who had just been shown into the library , and I 've been dodging about the corridor all this time waiting to see her", "context_len": 51}}}, "persuasive": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` I guess you might as well square up with us , '' said the slatternly woman , seating herself near her caller and speaking in ' persuasive accents , `` and then you 'll not hev to be beholden to Bob Lucas", "context_len": 43}}}, "accents": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "This was a part called Prosody , and it told how to make verses ; explained the feet , the accents , the stanzas -- everything that had puzzled him in his attempts to imitate the poems he had heard his father read aloud", "context_len": 44}}}, "beholden": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Indeed , O my father , there possessed me grief and sore chagrin , not only for my severance from thee , but also for the loss of my husband , to whom I shall be beholden all the days of my life , seeing he delivered me from that accursed enchanter", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The board will not come amiss , and then , straightening herself up a little , though we have no need of being beholden to anybody", "context_len": 26}}}, "challenging": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "This statement did not seem worth challenging by the woman , for she went on in the same wheedling voice , `` You 'll not hev no call for it on the cars", "context_len": 33}}}, "wheedling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "This statement did not seem worth challenging by the woman , for she went on in the same wheedling voice , `` You 'll not hev no call for it on the cars", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "'The pretty thing at the young lady 's neck would do as well , ' continued the woman in a wheedling voice , eyeing Peggy 's brooch , 'or the little gentleman 's silver chain", "context_len": 35}}}, "winking": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Me -- nowhere ! '' chuckled Bart , winking deliberately and then , walking over to the horse , he fondled his long ears , with the remark : `` If I had a dandy rig like you 've got here , I bet I 'd go somewheres , though ! ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "amiably": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Faint clucks and hen whispers reached my ears , but when she turned on some bright lights several of the hens spoke to her quite amiably and distinctly , while a finely feathered Plymouth Rock rooster got off his perch and shaking his big wings came to put his beak in her hand", "context_len": 53}}}, "sloven": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "rogue": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 26, "context": "We made our way to a window where we could overlook the front of the inn , and the horseman proved without doubt to be the rogue Joyce , though he was arrayed more gaily than of yore , and a close-trimmed beard hid the lower part of his face", "context_len": 50}}}, "recall": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 82, "context": "Naturally , I was very thankful that the awful day had passed over , and that its end found me still in the land of the living ; for `` while there is life there is hope , '' and in the course of my somewhat adventurous career I have seen so many extraordinary escapes from apparently inevitable disaster that the one piece of advice above all others which I would give to everybody is `` Never despair ! '' I can recall more than one occasion when , if I had abandoned hope and the effort which goes with it , I should not now have been alive to pen these words", "context_len": 113}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "At the time of his recall he was at the island of \u00c6gina , which is about forty miles southwest of Athens , in one of the gulfs of the \u00c6gean Sea", "context_len": 32}}}, "nimble": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "A narrow pathway led through it , on one side of which was a wall of rock , so steep that not even nimble Fred could have climbed it ; on the other was a shelving bank covered with tall pines and firs", "context_len": 43}}}, "possibilities": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 57, "context": "But there was a something , very difficult to define , in the expression of these people that I did not at all like , a hardness about the mouth , and a cruel glint in the eyes -- especially of the men -- which looked at me in a manner that suggested all sorts of unpleasant possibilities , and excited within me a distinct longing to be almost anywhere rather than where I was", "context_len": 75}}}, "sharpers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "She had fallen among sharpers , she must be as sharp as they", "context_len": 13}}}, "gray": {"freq": 127, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 103, "word_index": 89, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 31, "context": "It did not take very long for the children to be hauled up by Joe at the top , and they found themselves standing among the Abbey ruins in the early gray of the dawn , with Aunt Helen rushing to catch them in her arms , and Lilian hugging them and sobbing over them by turns , while Nancy , her face all blotched and swollen with crying , kept hovering round to put in a kiss whenever the others gave her a chance , and even old David cleared his throat hard , and 'blessed the Lord they were safe", "context_len": 102}}}, "illumined": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 25, "context": "When the Sultan saw the jewels ( and indeed , whenas she raised the handkerchief from them , the Divan became as it were all illumined with lamp-clusters and candlesticks ) , he was amazed and confounded at their radiance and fell a-marvelling at their lustre and bigness and beauty ; and [ 370 ] he said , `` Never saw I the like of these jewels for beauty and bigness and perfection , nor methinketh is one of them found in my treasuries", "context_len": 84}}}, "admonished": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "There was not a shawl like that in the settlement , and bustling to her feet , she stroked it and felt it with admiring fingers , until admonished by 'Tilda Jane that time was passing , and if she was going to get her anything to eat she had better be quick about it", "context_len": 55}}}, "sloppy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "Here in the attic -- with the cold night outside -- with the afternoon in the sloppy streets barely passed -- with the memory of the awful unfed look in the beggar child 's eyes not yet faded -- this simple , cheerful thing had happened like a thing of magic", "context_len": 51}}}, "venerable": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 11, "context": "The little girl almost choked over the sloppy tea from the venerable teapot , the shady bread and butter , and the composite dish of preserves set before her , yet resolutely shutting her eyes she ate and drank , and forced Gippie to do the same", "context_len": 47}}}, "shady": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 106, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "In this exquisite island , and other smaller ones , you wander amongst silent shady woods , or stand so close to the rushing waters , that one or two steps would send you over the brink into the cloudy chasm below", "context_len": 42}}}, "composite": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "A car of ancient and composite design -- partly Daimler , partly Darracq , and with a suspicion of half a dozen makers ' parts in the tout ensemble -- the wondrous , once-discarded vehicle had been given to Peter and Malcolm Carr by a cousin of theirs", "context_len": 48}}}, "preserves": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 117, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "The old pile had been badly preserved by the Saxon conquerors , but it had been built of that solid architecture which seems almost to defy the assaults of time , and which in some cases , after fifteen centuries , preserves all its characteristics , and promises yet to preserve them , when our frailer erections lie crumbled in the dust", "context_len": 62}}}, "eats": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 60, "context": "When Morn began to pick himself weakly up , with eyes of pathetic bewilderment , they helped him find his cap , and tried to engage him in conversation , for the pleasure of seeing him twist his mouth when he said , of a famous town drunkard whom he admired , `` He 's a strong man ; he eats liquor", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He is always hungry and eats people if they go near him", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "No fear of that , but he such a big , hulking chap and eats so much that he oughter be made to do something ; I \u2019 m going to put him on tother side of this wagon and make him b \u2019 leve it \u2019 s the most important post of all , and that if he drops asleep , the whole shebang will be wiped out by the redskins", "context_len": 72}}}, "charioteer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` How fur are we from Nicatoos ? '' inquired 'Tilda Jane of her charioteer one hour later", "context_len": 18}}}, "disengaged": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "And , waving his disengaged hand , Corporal Billy Preston resumed his long trek of pain that was to end somewhere in England under the kindly care of nurses from far-off New Zealand", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Ralph waited until Gervaise was disengaged , and then brought him across , and , after introducing him , moved away at once , leaving Gervaise to be interrogated by the ladies", "context_len": 32}}}, "nag": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "He was sulky and cold , and 'Tilda Jane averted her glance from him to his small brown nag , who was trotting along as cheerfully as if there were a reward at the end of the drive for him", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` If Master Henderson 's been breaking any rules , you 'll please not nag him about it now , Mr", "context_len": 21}}}, "gladness": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 56, "context": "The Sultan proceeded to make familiar with Alaeddin and to talk with him , and Alaeddin answered him with all courtliness and fluency , as he had been bred in kings ' palaces or as he were their constant associate ; [ 471 ] and the more the talk was prolonged between them , the more gladness and joy redoubled on the Sultan for that which he heard of the goodliness of Alaeddin 's answers and the sweetness of his speech", "context_len": 81}}}, "pursuing": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 57, "context": "Regret was expressed for the loss of Tasker and Keene , both of whom were highly esteemed by all their shipmates ; and then the conversation diverged to the topic of the audacious Virginia-Preciosa , which , protected by the very ingenious fraud of the double sets of papers and the double crews , was still merrily pursuing her way and bidding defiance to everybody", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "When again closely pressed by the pursuing Moskoquis , who had defeated them more than once , the Alibamu crossed over to the left side of the river ; but this did not save them from pursuit , for the Moskoquis followed them to the opposite side , defeated them in a sharp encounter , and drove them in the direction of Mississippi river , in which many found a watery grave in their hasty flight", "context_len": 76}}}, "respects": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 50, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 81, "context": "I do not say that every one was not before disposed to obey you in all things , willingly and cheerfully ; but he put it so strongly to them that they had volunteered specially for service in this galley , knowing well who was to be its commander , and the circumstance that the crew was to consist solely of young knights , and had therefore specially pledged their honour so to act that the enterprise should be in all respects a successful one", "context_len": 85}}}, "noble": {"freq": 133, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 132, "word_index": 64, "context": "like thou heardest the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to demand her of the Sultan her father in the way of", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Most of the young knights , belonging as they did to noble families , and accustomed from childhood to courtly ceremonies and festivities , were quite at home here", "context_len": 29}}}, "acute": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 33, "context": "But although I had passed through the day unscathed , so far as actual bodily injury was concerned , it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "With the coming of winter acute neuralgic pains took possession of him ; and though some little improvement was seen with the advent of spring , it was not permanent", "context_len": 30}}}, "appreciation": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 23, "context": "We have had a beautiful time , said Marie-Celeste , with a sigh , as though unable to give full expression to her appreciation ; but I hope we have n't stayed too long ; and then , as though reluctant to take final leave of the little Isabel , she added : Do n't you think it is more comfortable just to be one of the people , and be a regular little girl , and grow up always near your mother , like other children ?", "context_len": 88}}}, "sunset": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 64, "context": "I am so much nearer to my grave ! Oh that I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! --", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 62, "context": "And now you have at least an idea of how Marie-Celeste passed her time on the steamer , and you can understand how there might have been some people rather less glad than sorry when they felt the machinery stop at two o'clock one morning , and knew that the Queenstown passengers were being transferred to the tender , and that before sunset all the people aboard the great steamer would be separated to the four winds", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Stores of ammunition were collected in readiness at all the batteries that commanded the mouth of the outer port , and by sunset D'Aubusson felt that everything that was possible had been done to meet the impending storm", "context_len": 38}}}, "steel": {"freq": 86, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 86, "word_index": 27, "context": "Their uncle has sent with the book two butterfly-nets ; a pretty case filled with crooked scissors , tweezers , pincers , and all sorts of sharp steel instruments ; a pretty box , at the bottom of which are little round pieces of cork , glued in rows , with long large-headed pins to run through the butterflies ; and another little box , with a lot of small squares of glass , which are to be put over their wings to keep them open , and prevent them from fluttering and beating about", "context_len": 95}}}, "disdainfully": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "The house was large , because it was meant to shelter other people beside the Tracys and their children , but there was not a stick of `` genteel '' furniture in it , the new housemaid from Portland was just disdainfully observing to the cook", "context_len": 46}}}, "classed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 75, "context": "The walls were adorned with school drawings and prize maps , fastened up with tacks ; a bookcase of much-read volumes filled the space between the fireplace and the window ; and in the corner might be found a miscellaneous collection of cricket-bats , fishing-rods , tennis-racquets , bows and arrows , croquet-mallets , sticks , balls and ninepins , and other articles very dear to the children 's hearts , but which Nancy generally classed impartially under the head of 'lumber", "context_len": 82}}}, "uninviting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "A white cloth covered his formerly bare and uninviting table ; he was going to have pie , and coffee , and toast and cake for supper , -- surely the coming of this orphan had been a fortunate thing for him , and he slowly chafed his hands as he gazed at the glowing bed of coals", "context_len": 58}}}, "yards": {"freq": 229, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 229, "word_index": 20, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}}}, "huddling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Price , raising his head , and letting his snapping eyes run beyond the farmer to the flock of young men huddling together like gray sheep", "context_len": 26}}}, "fires": {"freq": 88, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 85, "word_index": 41, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "The long wished-for moon at length arose , hours after Guy had expected her , but too soon he thought when she made her appearance , for the camp fires grew dim beneath her rays , and he had to strain his aching eyes to see them at all", "context_len": 49}}}, "travels": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 5, "context": "In the course of his travels the ship in which he was a passenger was attacked by pirates , and John Howard showed himself as brave in actual battle as he was in fighting abuses ; for he loaded the big gun with which the ship was armed nearly up to the muzzle with nails and spikes , and fired it into the pirate crew just in time to save himself and his companions from destruction", "context_len": 76}}}, "cracked": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` Ye have one more chance , candidates , '' went on the president , as he touched the foreheads of the four with something cold and clammy -- a hand , from the feel of it , but it was only a rubber glove , filled with cracked ice", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 68, "context": "They usually had no such easy time getting them as they always would in a story ; they did not find them under the trees , or ready to drop off , but they had to knock them off with about six or seven clubs or rocks to every walnut , and they had to pound the hulls so hard to get the nuts out that sometimes they cracked the nuts", "context_len": 71}}}, "standstill": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "He succeeded in facing them sideways , ran their heads into some brush , vaulted over them , and , landing safely on his feet in front of them , grabbed them near the bits and held them snorting and trembling at a standstill", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Belden , and the carriage came to a standstill with paralyzing abruptness Now , turn your wheel and let me out", "context_len": 21}}}, "bullying": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "I 'm growing all the time , and perhaps one day I 'll be big enough to go for him , and after all , a fellow ought to be able to stand a bit of bullying without going whining home to his sister about it", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Peggy 's onslaught really seemed to have done some good in the school , for the attention of the older and better boys being called to the subject of bullying , a stand was made , and public opinion ran high against it , so that for a time , at any rate , the little boys had peace , and Bobby was able to return daily by the ordinary high-road , instead of seeking the shelter of side-paths and back alleys", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "There was sternness as he expressed his sentiments regarding the person with a bullying inclination who took pleasure in abusing those physically weaker than himself ; and , although Sam Rollins \u2019 name was not mentioned , every one knew at whom those open remarks were directed", "context_len": 47}}}, "plump": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` Yes , they would if they were hungry , but Lammie-noo has probably not been here more than a couple of nights , and if the bears have been roaming in some other direction they would not get wind of the fact that there was a nice plump lamb on the old Lonesome Hill farm -- -- Come up higher , and we 'll spy the landscape o'er", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "The boys pretended that the old frogs said , whenever this frog-catching boy came in sight , `` Here comes Hawkins ! -- here comes Hawkins ! Look out ! -- look out ! '' and a row of boys , perched on a log in the water , would sound this warning in mockery of the frogs or their foe , and plump one after another in the depths , as frogs follow their leader in swift succession", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` My dear , '' he continued , laying his hand on his wife 's very plump shoulder , `` you must speak to Lucy from yourself , not from me , dear ; for I am too tired", "context_len": 39}}}, "plea": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 29, "context": "He asked him just how and where he got in , and then he went to the door , and got back his money from the doorkeeper upon the plea that he did not feel well ; and in five or ten minutes he was back among the boys , a hero of such moral grandeur as would be hard to describe", "context_len": 62}}}, "patched": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 36, "context": "You boys , he continued , have drawn a thirty-day furlough , and we \u2019 re all going going home to the States in Sweet Rosy O \u2019 Grady , as soon as she \u2019 s patched up enough to make the trip", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "His shoes , though well worn and patched , had been blacked and polished till they looked quite respectable ; the suit made from his father 's old uniform was still neat and whole , for Willie 's present quiet life was a great saving to his clothes , if that were any comfort ; his white collar was turned back and neatly tied with a black ribbon , and Mrs", "context_len": 71}}}, "mended": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was nearly eight o'clock when Bart got through with his supper , did his house chores , mended a broken toy pistol for one junior brother , made up a list of purchases of torpedoes , baby-crackers and punk for the other , and helped his sisters in various ways", "context_len": 51}}}, "overpaid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "She had overpaid them for this drive , and she was not prepared to flounder on through the snow and perhaps miss her train", "context_len": 24}}}, "flounder": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The water is so black they can \u2019 t see anything and they flounder off into the clear water , while the inkfishes keep out of their way", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Beyond an eerie gurgle ( `` the last gasp of a dying flounder '' , according to the above-quoted honorary reporter ) not a sound came from the cornet", "context_len": 29}}}, "fertile": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "The scientists who now claim that they descend from the mound builders , do so only on the belief that they must have dwelt for uncounted centuries in the fertile tracts where Hernando de Soto found them , and where they have remained up to a recent epoch", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 16, "context": "Their roving disposition impelled them to leave this settlement also , and to migrate to the fertile Alachua plains , where they built Cuscowilla on the banks of a lake , and had to defend it against the attacks of the Tomocos , Utinas , Calloosas ( ? ) , Yamases and other remnant tribes of Florida , and the more northern refugees from Carolina , all of whom were helped by the Spaniards", "context_len": 74}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Ca n't you give me a note to her ? '' asked Tom , who was fertile in expedients where young ladies were concerned", "context_len": 25}}}, "homespun": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "I look as though I belonged right here , do n't I ? said Chris , glancing down at his English suit of homespun", "context_len": 24}}}, "match": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 53, "context": "Thou sayst , O my mother , that I have no gift to present to the Sultan , according to the wont of the folk , whilst in fact I have by me a gift and an offering , the like whereof methinketh no king ever possessed , no , nor aught to match therewith ; for [ 338 ] thou must know , O my mother , that the fruits , which I brought in the purses [ 339 ] from the treasure and which I deemed glass or crystal , are very jewels , methinketh all the kings of the world may not compass the least of them , and I , of my companying with the jewellers , know that they are precious stones", "context_len": 127}, "NOUN": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 101, "context": "Now his mother was with the Lady Bedrulbudour , and when he came up and did off her veil , she fell to gazing upon the bride 's beauty and grace and looked at the pavilion , the which was all wroughten [ 497 ] of gold and jewels and therein were golden lustres , all embossed with emeralds and rubies ; and she said in herself , `` Methought the Sultan 's palace was magnificent ; but , for this pavilion [ 498 ] alone , I doubt me the greatest of the Chosroes and the kings never owned its match ; nor , methinketh , might all mankind avail to make the like thereof", "context_len": 116}}}, "tight": {"freq": 83, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 85, "context": "Go down to the big apple-tree in the corner of the meadow , directed Donald , half under his breath , and , look here ! you had better take this with you , dragging a steamer rug from the couch , and flinging it out after him , and I 'll come down just as soon as ever I can and let you know how things are going and then Donald drew the shutters noiselessly to and sped back to the lane at as tight a run as he had left it", "context_len": 93}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 48, "context": "She looked at the solitary lamp with its grotesquely hideous ornament of red flannel , at Susan 's expressionless , freckled face , at the boys in their copper-toed boots and overalls , at the good-natured , but hopelessly common-place Martha Spriggs , with her thin hair drawn tight into a knob the size of a bullet , and her bare arms akimbo", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 26, "context": "Marie-Celeste was so impressed with the greatness of the privilege accorded them , that for the first five minutes or so she kept Albert 's hand tight in her own , and spoke never a word save a whispered yes or no to Albert 's questions", "context_len": 46}}}, "resist": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 93, "context": "To feel conscious that she was not one scrap afraid of this queer girl , to feel conscious also that the girl herself , notwithstanding her extreme naughtiness , could in the end be managed by her , brought such a rush of interest into her life that she forgot everything else for the moment ; and , besides , Irene was exceedingly strong , and although she was much slimmer and not so tall as Rosamund , she dragged her down the path with a power that it was almost impossible to resist", "context_len": 94}}}, "retaliation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The boy was furious , but the cold wind smote his head , and , postponing retaliation , he sprang first for his cap , shouting warningly , however , as he swung his leg over the fence , `` I 'll make you pay up for this , you -- ''", "context_len": 52}}}, "warningly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "The boy was furious , but the cold wind smote his head , and , postponing retaliation , he sprang first for his cap , shouting warningly , however , as he swung his leg over the fence , `` I 'll make you pay up for this , you -- ''", "context_len": 52}}}, "cared": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 87, "context": "The affair made a great impression but somehow did not increase Miss Newman 's popularity , for the idea of the drawings was hers and Clara could not forgive her for the position into which she had forced her , therefore she lost no opportunity of making it as unpleasant for her teacher as she could in the thousand and one ways a sly and unprincipled girl can , and her little pin-pricks were so annoying , that finally Dorothy and Edna , who had not particularly cared for the new teacher , began to stand up for her and to do as many kind things as they could", "context_len": 109}}}, "epithet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 94, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "braced": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 57, "context": "Now it was all up-hill , and the wind blowing such a gale that she had need to bend way over , holding firmly to her sailor hat the while , to make any headway whatever ; and now in a trice it was very much down-hill indeed , and the little knees had to be stiffly braced to prevent her ladyship from bowling along at a dangerously rapid pace", "context_len": 70}}}, "grasping": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 62, "context": "He knew well enough what he ought to do , namely , seize the drowning man by the hair , and then turn upon his own back and float , drawing the other after him ; but on trying this a difficulty met him at the offset : the man 's hair was very short ; but he got over it by grasping his ears , and then , throwing himself back , he struck out with his legs so as to keep afloat and go with the racing current", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "There was a chorus of ejaculations , a hurried grasping of weapons , and then shouts of pleasure as they recognized the newcomer", "context_len": 23}}}, "driven": {"freq": 122, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 115, "word_index": 118, "context": "or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing his victims to the tree with spike nails driven through the hands and feet into the tough timber", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "It would be too dangerous ; and even were a spike driven in , the Turks would have no great difficulty in extracting it , for the tubes are so big that a man might crawl in and drive the spike up from the inside", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "This is usually effected by driving what are called piles , which are long beams of wood , pointed at the end , and driven into the earth by means of powerful engines", "context_len": 33}}}, "adored": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 12, "context": "Was this the gentle and exceedingly pretty girl whom little Agnes so adored ? He was alone with her , and they were , so to speak , flying through the water , although she scarcely touched the oars , allowing them to lie almost idle by her side", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Brain of adamant ! could not instinct direct you to the feet of your adored Prince ?", "context_len": 17}}}, "experiences": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 76, "context": "They were full of their own interests , and , on the few occasions when she happened to meet them , had talked so much of new teachers , new pupils , and class topics of which she knew nothing , that she had felt a little 'out of it , ' and had an uneasy sensation that , if she spoke of the quiet little episodes of her daily round they would vote her housekeeping experiences as decidedly 'slow", "context_len": 80}}}, "lout": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "She had never driven a horse before in her life , but she adored new experiences , and she had carefully watched every motion of the young lout beside her", "context_len": 30}}}, "covering": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 46, "context": "As daylight dawned upon the third day of the fight we continued our retreat , and as a faint southerly wind sprang up the enemy drew near with the intention of renewing the fight , concentrating their efforts on Albemarle \u2019 s ship , which , covering the retreat , presented an undaunted spectacle to our relentless foes", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 4, "context": "Richards pulled down the covering of the cradle , turned over the pillow , looked under the table , peeped into the sugar-bowl , pepper-pot , and stove , and at last pretended to be much astonished to discover the baby upon its mother 's lap , after which the hunt was carried on in search of a place big enough to kiss", "context_len": 63}}}, "obliviously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Having got what she wanted , she was going obliviously on her way", "context_len": 13}}}, "slower": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "But after Matthew left him he began to think ; and the more deeply he thought , the slower became his progress", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 13, "context": "This bow is rather small for me , and the arrow will go slower than I have been accustomed to have them , which makes it difficult judging how much it will fall", "context_len": 33}}}, "stolen": {"freq": 101, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 90, "word_index": 64, "context": "'s scarf from the saddle-room , and dropped it into the horse-trough , had filled Bobby 's hat with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread and half-picked bones which", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "'How disgusting ! Who cares for old books ? ' exclaimed Bobby , whose heart had been set on stolen jewels , smuggled valuables , or daggers and firearms at the least", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 89, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}}}, "deliberations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}}}, "meditatively": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "She could not take the rig on the train , anyway , and plucking up courage , and shivering in the cold that had seized upon him during his deliberations , he meditatively and angrily began to plod over the route that he had recommended to her", "context_len": 47}}}, "plod": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "She could not take the rig on the train , anyway , and plucking up courage , and shivering in the cold that had seized upon him during his deliberations , he meditatively and angrily began to plod over the route that he had recommended to her", "context_len": 47}}}, "recommended": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 76, "context": "Zein-ud-din ( Adornment of the Faith ) [ he ] of the Images , Zein ( adornment ) not being a name used by the Arabic-speaking races , unless with some such addition as ud-Din ( `` of the Faith '' ) , and the affix ul Asnam ( `` [ He ] of the Images '' ) being a sobriquet arising from the circumstances of the hero 's after-life , unless its addition , as recommended by the astrologers , is meant as an indication of the latter 's fore-knowledge of what was to befall him thereafter", "context_len": 98}}}, "bells": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 43, "context": "As soon as my news was bruited abroad , and they learned that the fleet consisted solely of prizes captured from the Moors by a galley of the Order , alarm quickly changed into delight , the sharp , angry clang of the bells was succeeded by peals of gladness , and the joy of the citizens at being relieved from the cloud of anxiety that had hung over the city since my last visit , was unbounded", "context_len": 78}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "As soon as they really grasped the fact , they sent off messengers to the churches to order the joy bells to be rung , and to the dockyard to arrest all work upon the galleys", "context_len": 36}}}, "operator": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 5, "context": "The captain and the wireless operator were the last to leave , the latter striving in vain to get the damaged transmitter into working order until peremptory orders from his superior obliged him to desist", "context_len": 35}}}, "teasingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I guess you do n't like that black-eyed girl much , '' said the young man , teasingly", "context_len": 19}}}, "darkening": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 56, "context": "Gervaise thought the plan an excellent one ; and he was soon transformed , Muley shaving that part of the hair that would have shown below the turban , and then staining him a deep brownish black , from the waist upwards , together with his feet and his legs up to his knee , and darkening his eyebrows , eyelashes , and moustache", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "They had gone on for perhaps a mile , seeing no sign of their quarry , but loath to give up , when there was a sudden darkening of the atmosphere , the wind increased in violence , and then the air was again filled with flying flakes , so thick that the lads could not see ten feet ahead", "context_len": 60}}}, "volley": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 9, "context": "Dommett had already done likewise , and before the volley could be repeated we were well out of range , though throughout the whole time we were under fire the man still kept his pipe firmly between his teeth", "context_len": 39}}}, "witheringly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` You young hound , '' said the man , witheringly , `` if she spilt you out , I 'll bet you deserved it", "context_len": 25}}}, "deserved": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 51, "word_index": 27, "context": "Can he speak thus who hath so long espoused The Royal cause , and served that cause so well ? Who , girt with honours , well deserved , hath stood One in a noble Brotherhood of Fame ! Where are the Cavaliers who fought with thee In battle , side by side , who with thee shared The feast , and drained the wine-cup to your King ? Where are they now ? what , gone ? not one remains , T \u2019 assert thy innocence , or shield thee from An ignominious death", "context_len": 95}}}, "slammed": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 28, "context": "If you want your money , go find it , '' and throwing the fifty cents in a snow-drift , he went back into the warm station and slammed the door behind him", "context_len": 33}}}, "transgressor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "There 's no doubt about it , 'the way of the transgressor is hard ' -- very hard indeed and with a grim sort of smile on his face , Ted gathered his dressing-gown about him , and with rather shaky steps sought the seclusion of his own little room", "context_len": 50}}}, "destined": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 24, "context": "He fumbled desperately in the snow , for he wanted fifty cents above all things in the world just then , but he was destined not to find it ; and at last , cold , weary , and yet with all his faults not inclined to wreak his wrath on the pony who stood patiently watching him , he threw himself into the sleigh and sped gloomily homeward", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Before the marriage came off , the destined bridegroom and his parents became Christians", "context_len": 14}}}, "faults": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` But why should you wish for a fault which would grieve your friends and trouble yourself only that you may be at the pains of curing it , Maggie ? You have faults enough , dear ; and if they are not what may be called very terrible , they are quite serious enough to need all your attention , and you should be thankful that it does not require a harder struggle to overcome them", "context_len": 77}}}, "wreak": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "He fumbled desperately in the snow , for he wanted fifty cents above all things in the world just then , but he was destined not to find it ; and at last , cold , weary , and yet with all his faults not inclined to wreak his wrath on the pony who stood patiently watching him , he threw himself into the sleigh and sped gloomily homeward", "context_len": 69}}}, "homeward": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 68, "context": "He fumbled desperately in the snow , for he wanted fifty cents above all things in the world just then , but he was destined not to find it ; and at last , cold , weary , and yet with all his faults not inclined to wreak his wrath on the pony who stood patiently watching him , he threw himself into the sleigh and sped gloomily homeward", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "So there was a grand collecting of hats and gloves , and pumping of tyres , and many 'good-byes ' and 'thank you 's , ' and the merry party at last started off on their homeward way , ringing their bells as a parting salute , and declaring they would not soon forget their afternoon at the Abbey", "context_len": 59}}}, "maxim": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "It appears long to have been a maxim of their policy to give equal liberty and protection to tribes conquered by themselves , as well as to those vanquished by others , although many individuals taken in war are slaves among them , and their children are called of the slave race , and can not arrive to much honorary distinction in the country , on that account", "context_len": 68}}}, "inspires": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "His mother had the shawl , but he had nothing for his trouble , for he counted as nothing and worse than nothing his experience of the maxim that one sly trick inspires another", "context_len": 34}}}, "quandary": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` May I ask you to move , madam ? '' She did so , and then the brakeman was in a quandary , for it was necessary to break the glass in order to get at the tools", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Alden Payne was in the quandary of his life", "context_len": 9}}}, "boarded": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 9, "context": "France and Auvergne , led by Ralph Harcourt , boarded at the bow , the other five langues at the poop ; and so impetuous was their onset that the pirates , who had still scarce recovered from their surprise at being hastily aroused from sleep to repel the attack of the foe who had so suddenly sprung out from the darkness upon them , offered but a feeble resistance", "context_len": 70}}}, "turmoil": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "Slowly the Gannet moved towards the open water and freedom , her keel ploughing through the liquid mud and causing a regular turmoil of yellow foam within the little dock", "context_len": 30}}}, "familiarity": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 72, "context": "Then , at five every clear day , afternoon tea with the captain , for which they had a standing invitation , and by means of which both she and Chris came to be on terms of wonderful intimacy with that august officer , so that they joked over the rare souchong and delicious little toasted cakes ( the secret of whose making was kept close-guarded by the steward ) with a familiarity that , to themselves at least , never ceased to be a wonder", "context_len": 86}}}, "workings": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Homer described with great beauty and force the workings of ambition , of resentment , of pride , of rivalry , and all those other impulses of the human heart which would excite and control the action of impetuous men in the circumstances in which his heroes were placed", "context_len": 49}}}, "voluminous": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The voluminous pages of Father Cuthbert 's diary for the years of bitter woe and misery which followed can not be fully transcribed ; they would fill a volume themselves , and we must content ourselves with a few extracts , which will probably interest our readers , and carry on the thread of the history to the place where our narrative will again flow free and uninterrupted", "context_len": 68}}}, "haughtily": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "The other boys would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "You needn \u2019 t ask me if you don \u2019 t want to , said the Nail haughtily", "context_len": 18}}}, "atmosphere": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 54, "context": "It looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 49, "context": "Throwing the charred and smoking tops to him , she shut up the stove , took her hat and tippet from a peg in the wall , and clasping Gippie to her , left the house without one glance at the old man as he sat in the smoky atmosphere mumbling to himself , and fumbling over the burnt pieces of wood as tenderly as if they had been babies", "context_len": 70}}}, "hazy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 76, "context": "Below the house the meadow sloped down to a river , where a stone bridge led to the village , with its pretty thatched cottages and Norman church , whose square tower stood up like a beacon for the surrounding country ; and away in the distance , tier upon tier , rose the Welsh mountains , fading from green to purple or from purple to misty mauve , till the last were lost in the hazy blue of the sky", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Certainly not on washing day , when every energy was absorbed in the elimination of impurity from her household linen , and life looked grotesque and hazy through clouds of soapy steam", "context_len": 32}}}, "discomposure": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "The swift flash of her eye , the sudden quiver of her lip , afforded continual gratification to such as had it in mind to effect her discomposure", "context_len": 28}}}, "pecuniary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "He had been known to go to the largest dealers in Christ-church and purchase the swiftest car procurable , drive it at breakneck speed until he collided with something , and then sell the remains and retire to his pah until he found an opportunity for another exuberance of pecuniary extravagance", "context_len": 51}}}, "beggars": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 19, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "squarely": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 5, "context": "She had placed her finger squarely on my perplexity , for this was indeed the great problem that I had to solve -- what should I do with her ? Not to-morrow , nor the day after , but now -- to-night", "context_len": 42}}}, "flame": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 59, "context": "Had Ben set the fire at first along the whole line of the brush , there would have been burnt ground between him and the mass of fire , which would have cut off the communication , and he would have been safe ; but he set it on one corner , and when the wind shifted , the flame driven by it dried the moisture from the grass , and made rapid progress towards the house , while a large strip of dry grass made a bridge for the fire to travel on", "context_len": 94}}}, "precipitately": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 52, "context": "I wanted you to lend me a dollar or so to be sent back in a letter , but I would n't take it now -- no , not if you crawled after me on your hands an ' knees like a dog holdin ' it in your mouth , '' and precipitately leaving him , she sauntered down the aisle", "context_len": 61}}}, "aisle": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 60, "context": "I wanted you to lend me a dollar or so to be sent back in a letter , but I would n't take it now -- no , not if you crawled after me on your hands an ' knees like a dog holdin ' it in your mouth , '' and precipitately leaving him , she sauntered down the aisle", "context_len": 61}}}, "warn": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 45, "context": "However , when all search for him in the town and in the vessels in the port proved fruitless , doubtless mounted men would be despatched in all directions ; some would take the coast roads , while others would ride into the interior to warn the head men of the villages to be on the lookout for an escaped slave", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Who said anyting \u2019 bout fists ? I was talking \u2019 bout wrastlin \u2019 ; if your head warn \u2019 t so thick you \u2019 d understood me", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Oh , because , '' said Charlie , thinking how he could best warn Seabrooke and yet not betray Flagg , `` because -- there 's something in your trunk", "context_len": 31}}}, "adventuress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Should he warn him against the youthful adventuress ? No , he was old enough to take care of himself , and he settled back in his corner and devoted himself to his paper", "context_len": 34}}}, "devoted": {"freq": 88, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 6, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 25, "context": "He doubtless also had an instinctive feeling that the moral effect itself of so dreadful a retribution as he was about to inflict upon the devoted city would be very much increased by forbearance and generosity , and by extreme regard for the security and protection of those who had shown themselves his friends", "context_len": 54}}}, "eighteen": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 82, "context": "Sometimes I ask myself , -- Now , in my present position , if I had no pleasure in religion , if everything connected with that were cut off , what would be left me ? -- what would life be to me ? O Leila , what a tasteless , what a bitter thing ! We want delights that will not grow old , that will never pall , that will be just as fresh and lovely at eighty as at eighteen", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` Ben has a lovely little plot and all the ideas he has given us about the scenes and the dressing of the characters and the funny situations are mighty good , '' returned Agnes , `` it does seem as if between us all we ought to be able to do the rest when we have eighteen regular members in the club and two honorary ones", "context_len": 67}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "On one of these occasions , the man who conquered drank , it is said , eighteen or twenty pints of wine , after which he lingered in misery for three days , and then died ; and more than forty others , present at the same entertainment , died in consequence of their excesses", "context_len": 55}}}, "innumerable": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 22, "context": "They had been grievously led away by Mahomet , whom Gervaise regarded as a false teacher ; but as he had seen innumerable instances of the fidelity of the Moslems to their creed , and the punctuality and devotion with which the slaves said their daily prayers , exposed though they were to the scorn and even the anger of their taskmasters , he had quite lost , during his nine months of constant association with Suleiman Ali , the bigoted hatred of Mohammedanism so universal at the time", "context_len": 89}}}, "rebuff": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "If he met with a rebuff from any quarter or had to push his way in the face of any difficulties , he has never owned up to them", "context_len": 29}}}, "apply": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 39, "context": "Language is not only the most general and important help to ethnology , but outside of race , it is also the most ancient of all ; ethnologists are well aware of this fact , but do not generally apply it to their studies , because they find it too tedious to acquire the language of unlettered tribes by staying long enough among them", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Likewise did a part of the Chicasa apply for help ; they were assigned seats on Yazoo river , `` at the head of Loup river , '' [ 147 ] and soon extended their habitations up to the Cheroki boundaries", "context_len": 41}}}, "resting": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 94, "context": "It happened that one night , when , as now was frequently the case , I had been summoned to entertain the king and his household by `` obliging them with a little music , '' I was playing some soft , plaintive air -- I forget what -- when , chancing to glance toward Ama , who , seated on the ground on the extreme left of the semicircle , was well within my range of vision , I fancied I saw some moving object close to her left hand , which was resting lightly on the ground", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "Vaughan was in such haste to get home before post-time that he hurried them on , quite forgetting how much shorter their legs were than his own , and he refused to listen to any excuses for sitting down and resting , which , considering their wet condition , was perhaps just as well", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then he covered it and rolled great stones over the top that the last resting place of the stranger might be undisturbed", "context_len": 22}}}, "mischievously": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` And call it 'The Happy Family , ' '' said Fred , mischievously , `` after those celebrated bears and dogs and cats and mice who live together in the most peaceable manner so long as they have no teeth and claws , but who immediately fall to and eat one another up as soon as these are allowed to grow", "context_len": 62}}}, "passionate": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 56, "context": "Ah , the old besetting sin , strengthened by long habit and indulgence , what a tyrant it had become , and how hard she had to struggle with it , how often was she overcome ! Yes , well might little Bessie be thankful that wise and tender teachers had taught her to control that passionate temper , which later might have proved such a misery to herself and her friends", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 48, "context": "Accordingly these wicked sorcerers found constant pretexts for involving the tribes in war , especially if they supposed that the only daughter of a chieftain loved a brave of another tribe ; but for many years all their arts were in vain , for the Indians were so passionate and revengeful that immediately an affront was given or received , violent hatred vanquished love , and the chiefs and their families were saved", "context_len": 73}}}, "tones": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 87, "word_index": 1, "context": "Certain tones in the man 's hearty voice reminded me of the boy 's sweet notes , and was n't there a queer suggestion of each other when I stood near them ? We Shetland ponies as I have said before are very close to human beings for our ancestors were literally brought up with the children in the crofters ' huts , and my mother has often told me that her mother had many a lick at the family platter and many a time her soft muzzle was buried in the children 's necks", "context_len": 95}}}, "courteously": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 41, "context": "I did n't like the 'sylum , I wanted to run away , even when there was no talk o ' the dog , an ' I 'll tell you what happened , '' and while the minister and his wife courteously listened , she gave a full and entire account of her wanderings during the time that she had been absent from the asylum", "context_len": 65}}}, "flattered": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 15, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 26, "context": "He had never dreamed this would be so ; he had never meant it should be ; but that did not alter the fact that , flattered and made much of ever since he went up to Oxford , he had somehow had little time to think of his mother , and , sorrier than that , little inclination", "context_len": 59}}}, "melt": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 9, "context": "The crust was so flaky that it seemed to melt in the boy \u2019 s mouth , and the apple filling had a taste and flavor that had been imparted to it in some magical manner by the genius of the woman who seemed to bestow something sweet and wholesome upon the very atmosphere about her", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Me got an onion patch , massa , -- my ole woman raise him ; got some bayberry taller , -- Spaniards buy him quick to put in de candle ; make him hard so he no melt", "context_len": 38}}}, "compliment": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 71, "context": "You see , a gage of this kind does not necessarily mean that there is any deep feeling between the knight who bears it and the lady who bestows it ; it shows only that she , on her part , feels it an honour that her gage should be worn by a distinguished knight , and , on his part , that he considers it as somewhat more than a compliment , and wears it as a proof of regard on the part of one whose good opinion at least he values", "context_len": 93}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` You have your mother , of course , '' she continued slowly , `` but you yourself are very young , and -- now I do n't want to compliment you -- but you are much nicer looking than many girls ; you have quite a different air and appearance from any other girl in this house", "context_len": 58}}}, "calculated": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 19, "context": "That was all there was in it , and yet can you not surmise how even that letter was calculated to work great consternation in the mind of some one in the little thatched cottage -- some one who never saw the letter itself , and who did not so much as know of its existence until it had been read and re-read and thought over and answered , but who when one day he was made acquainted with its contents felt as weak as a kitten for hours afterward ? He happened to be lying on the lounge in the living-room at the time , the same lounge to which he had been carried more dead than alive apparently , just four weeks before", "context_len": 125}}}, "civil": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 6, "context": "Besides , Ted had taken quite civil leave of him before going back to Oxford , and had said he fancied would be down again next Sunday , and that he would be on hand , like as not , if Uncle Fritz decided to come over -- all of which , for any one who knew Ted as Harold knew him , was graciousness itself , and made Harold wish he had not waited so long before taking matters into his own hands", "context_len": 84}}}, "inquiry": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 4, "context": "Naturally this called forth inquiry , and it soon became known that Percy had entrusted to Seabrooke 's keeping a large sum of money , lest he should himself be tempted to spend any portion of it , as it was to be reserved for a special purpose ; that Seabrooke before going to the dinner on the previous evening had put it , as he supposed , in a secure place , and that this morning the money was gone , while he had discovered slight but unmistakable evidence that his quarters had been ransacked in search of it", "context_len": 100}}}, "shows": {"freq": 84, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 48, "context": "His influence and ascendency seemed firmly established now in the country of the Greeks , and in due time he returned to Macedon , and celebrated at \u00c6g\u00e6 , which was at this time his capital , the establishment and confirmation of his power , by games , shows , spectacles , illuminations , and sacrifices to the gods , offered on a scale of the greatest pomp and magnificence", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 32, "context": "You see , a gage of this kind does not necessarily mean that there is any deep feeling between the knight who bears it and the lady who bestows it ; it shows only that she , on her part , feels it an honour that her gage should be worn by a distinguished knight , and , on his part , that he considers it as somewhat more than a compliment , and wears it as a proof of regard on the part of one whose good opinion at least he values", "context_len": 93}}}, "assure": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 43, "context": "She sent me -- what do you think ? Instead of the beautiful ribbons which I asked for , three yards of which are absolutely necessary to make even a show of a decent appearance , six stamps ! Six stamps , I assure you , to buy what I could for myself ! Did you ever hear of anything so miserably mean ? Oh , I hate her , I do hate her ! ''", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 86, "context": "But if our Church is to go on growing , as we hope and pray that it may , what would be the advantage of having a tiny chapel , which would not comfortably accommodate ourselves in a fiery climate , and in which there would be no room at all for heathen spectators ? We should be wanting a second ; and how could we procure a second clergyman ? Please thank dear Fred very , very much for his kindness in collecting , and assure him that we wish to make the money go as far as possible", "context_len": 100}}}, "flash": {"freq": 113, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 102, "word_index": 37, "context": "It seemed to him that he had to wait an hour , and just when he was going to hollo , and tell his brother where the ducks were , the old smoothbore sent out a red flash and a white puff before he heard the report ; Tip tore loose from his grasp ; and he heard the splashing rise of the ducks , and the hurtling rush of their wings ; and he ran forward , yelling , `` How many did you hit ? Where are they ? Where are you ? Are they coming back ? It 's my turn now ! '' and making an outcry that would have frightened away a fleet of ironclads , but much less a flock of ducks", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Suddenly away flash wig and beard and false-face , the pantaloons are stripped off with the same movement , the actor stoops for the reins lying on the horse 's neck , and James Rivers , the greatest three-horse rider in the world nimbly capers on the broad pad , and kisses his hand to the shouting and cheering spectators as he dashes from the ring past the braying and bellowing brass-band into the dressing-room !", "context_len": 76}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 64, "context": "Her face was pasty in complexion without a scrap of color in it , and her eyes were of too light a blue to redeem the general insipidity of her appearance ; but when she spoke that insipidity vanished , for her lips were very firm , and were apt to utter incisive words , and at such moments her pale blue eyes would flash with a light fire which was full of sarcasm , and might even rise to positive cruelty", "context_len": 82}}}, "bookkeeper": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Years ago , when he resigned his position as bookkeeper at Waysmith and Son 's big mill , a gold-headed cane had been presented to him , on which was engraved a flattering inscription", "context_len": 34}}}, "pension": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "We pension him now , '' he added , grandly , and with a wish to impress", "context_len": 17}}}, "grandly": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 8, "context": "We know all dere is , said Albert grandly ; we had a Knight-of-the-Garter day las ' week ; and then recalling the matter of the foolish little garter , his face grew crimson , and he begged Marie-Celeste not to tell", "context_len": 42}}}, "impressed": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 92, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 69, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}}}, "tiger": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 40, "context": "She had lost her hat , and the wind had tossed up her curls , her cheeks were pale and streaked with tears , and her big brown eyes had a pitiful look in them that would have softened a tiger , let alone a man that had half a dozen little ones of his own at home ; while every now and then the great heavy sighs came struggling up , as if she had almost cried her heart out", "context_len": 81}}}, "stays": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` I am dreaming -- I am getting out of bed , '' she heard her own voice say ; and then , as she stood up in the midst of it all , turning slowly from side to side -- '' I am dreaming it stays -- real ! I 'm dreaming it FEELS real", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 98, "context": "have got some good points , and if I were your daughter , Aunt Susan -- and I am heartily glad I 'm not your daughter ; I would much , much rather be Mummy 's daughter , poor as she is -- but if I were your daughter you would dress me in such a fashion that my good points would come out , for I have good points ; a nice complexion , fine hair and plenty of it , and fairly good eyes , and my figure would not look clumsy if I wore proper stays and properly-made dresses ; and my feet would not be like clodhoppers , if I had fine well-made boots and silk stockings ; and my hands -- -- ''", "context_len": 128}}}, "capitalist": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Well , young lady , I am not a capitalist , but I think I can raise you a loan", "context_len": 21}}}, "loan": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 40, "context": "The idea of expecting that a stranger , to whom his only introduction was that of a cabin-boy of the White Star Line , would be likely to take an interest in him to the extent of making him a loan of a large sum of money at rather a low rate of interest ; and then Ted realized what some of us have realized before , that all he had practically to build upon was Marie-Celeste 's remark to Donald , that she felt very sure that the best thing that could happen to this same rich Mr", "context_len": 99}}}, "sententiously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I like some animiles better'n some people , '' observed 'Tilda Jane , sententiously", "context_len": 15}}}, "civilly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 61, "context": "But I make no doubt that the sight of these your loyal neighbours whom you have called in , and the very blessings of the poor folk in the street , are somewhat of a balm to his heart , also I can not deny that those gentlemen '' -- looking over at the officers -- '' have used us very civilly during the day 's ride ; methinks his Majesty finds himself more at ease with them than with those crop-eared parliament men and their preachers", "context_len": 87}}}, "racket": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 6, "context": "How did you stand all the racket this morning ? If a noisy noise annoys an oyster , how much of a noisy noise does it take to annoy Pinky Blooms ? That sounds like a problem in mental arithmetic , but it is n't", "context_len": 45}}}, "streak": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 71, "context": "The air seemed to have grown more close and sultry while the children were within the cave , and , just as they reached the mouth , a low , grumbling sound , which they had heard for some time , but not taken much notice of , broke into a crash of thunder that seemed to make the ground shake beneath them , while at the same instant a brilliant streak of lightning flashed zigzag across the sky , lighting up the gloom behind them to its furthest recess", "context_len": 90}}}, "lightning": {"freq": 83, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 98, "context": "I was not permitted to show myself in the doorway of my dwelling , or even to approach it nearly enough to see what was going on ; and in this dreadful solitude , waiting and hoping for I knew not what impossible happening to occur and effect my deliverance , each day seemed to drag itself out to the length of a month -- until the darkness came ; and then , with the realisation of the fact that I was so much nearer to a hideous fate , the hours seemed suddenly to have sped with lightning swiftness", "context_len": 100}, "NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 46, "context": "Just as we reached the summit , where the road makes a vast horseshoe curve round the dark , forbidding cavity known as the Devil \u2019 s Punch Bowl , a heavy rainstorm came on , blotting out the horizon , while a vivid flash of lightning , followed at a short interval by a tremendous clap of thunder , startled our horses , and , be it confessed , ourselves as well", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Two of our men went down before him , one having his skull cloven by a lightning sweep of the corsair \u2019 s razorlike blade , the other having his sword arm cut completely through at the wrist", "context_len": 38}}}, "begrudge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Old Hobart will begrudge them a bone , I assure you", "context_len": 11}}}, "rattling": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 52, "context": "The road itself formed the main street of the place , and while the boys were jogging at a very leisurely gait toward the huddle of store buildings , a man in a flannel shirt and with his trousers tucked in his boot tops , jumped across the road , dragging a rattling chain behind him", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 80, "context": "'s evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and sent such cold shivers running down their backs that they grew almost too frightened to go to bed", "context_len": 128}}}, "principle": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 25, "context": "As the father had once taught English grammar in six lessons , from a broadside of his own authorship , he may have expected the principle of heredity to help the boy ; and certainly he did dig the English grammar out of that blessed book , and the Spanish language with it , but after many long years , and much despair over the difference between a preposition and a substantive", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The fighting must have come from the drinking , which began as soon as the polls were opened , and went on all day and night with a devotion to principle which is now rarely seen", "context_len": 36}}}, "selfish": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 96, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Do you think Dorothy is conceited and selfish ? ''", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "A few years before , he would have rewarded him for his fidelity in his master 's service ; but now , grown selfish , hard hearted , and revengeful , he looked upon him with a countenance full of vindictive exultation , and said ,", "context_len": 46}}}, "hinted": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 4, "context": "Aunt Helen had broadly hinted that an hour 's practice of much-neglected pieces upon the piano in the Rose Parlour would be a profitable means of employing the time , and the fear lest this threat should become an actual command caused Peggy to shun the neighbourhood of the house with elaborate care , and betake herself , with a selection of pets , to the barn", "context_len": 67}}}, "wickeder": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "merchant": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 13, "context": "I might tell him that my employer -- and I would mention some merchant whose name would carry weight with him -- has received from the interior a large consignment of slaves , among whom are three or four girls , who would fetch high prices in Egypt , and as he believes they have been captured from a tribe within the limits of the sultan 's territory , he is anxious to get rid of them , and will either dispose of them all cheaply in a lot , or will hand them over to him to take to Egypt to sell , giving him a large commission for carrying them there and disposing of them", "context_len": 117}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Its harbor was filled with sailing vessels ; merchant ships from the Barbados or faraway England unloaded their goods at the Long Wharf", "context_len": 23}}}, "boxed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` An ' you have a nice room with a fire that ai n't boxed up , an ' you sit round , an ' no other folks come in , an ' no bells ring for you to get up and do somethin ' ? ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "boastfully": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` We have loads of rooms in our house , '' said the lad , boastfully", "context_len": 16}}}, "flattened": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 42, "context": ") A legend , which evidently originated from the name already existing , relates that wayfarers passing there had left a large provision-basket ( s\u00e1ka ) at this locality , which was upset and left rotting , so that finally it became flattened out : from pat\u00e4\u00eddsh\u00e4s I spread out something ; pat\u00e1yi , partic", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The tin roof had been blown off , and now lay upside down on a group of flattened tents", "context_len": 19}}}, "prosperous": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 30, "context": "She still loves the Abbey dearly , but I do not fancy she will spend all her life there , after all , for Archie , who now owns a prosperous sheep-farm in Australia , is still as anxious as ever for her to try colonial life , and I think that the next time he comes home on a visit to Gorswen he will be able to persuade her", "context_len": 70}}}, "mills": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 8, "context": "As with everything that boys do , these mills were mostly failures ; the pins which supported the wheels were always giving way ; and though there were instances of boys who started their wheels at recess and found them still fluttering away at noon when they came out of school , none ever carried his enterprise so far as to spin the cotton blowing from the balls of the cottonwood-tree by the shore , as they all meant to do", "context_len": 81}}}, "theatre": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 46, "context": "All being now ready , the gate in the palisade was thrown open , a conch- shell was blown , and the waiting inhabitants began to pour into the enclosure with all the eagerness and excitement of an audience crowding into the unreserved portions of a theatre , and in a very short time the great square was full , the front ranks pressing close up to a cordon of armed guards that had been drawn round the circle of posts", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "You may be giving tickets yourself for the theatre some day -- that is , if you develop your talents to the utmost", "context_len": 23}}}, "discreetly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "For so long indeed as her footsteps echoed in the passage her pupils obeyed her order , but the moment she might reasonably be believed to be out of earshot a low murmur began among the little heads bent so discreetly over the arithmetic books", "context_len": 45}}}, "mamma": {"freq": 453, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 11, "context": "He wanted to keep it even after it was dead but mamma told him it would become disagreeable ; and for some days after this , the family were much amused to see him constantly running to the spot where it was buried , and putting his nose down to the ground to see if his `` pollywod smelt bad yet", "context_len": 61}, "VERB": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 51, "context": "On the day before this , his dinner had been sent to him , but as it did not exactly suit him , he called Flossy , and writing on a piece of paper what he desired , gave it to the dog , and told him to take it to mamma", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 347, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Oh , mamma ! '' exclaimed Bessie , as her mother just then entered the room , `` what do you think ? This very nice woman lives with my station policeman , who was so kind to me , and his name is Yichards , and he has a lame baby and a sick wife and a blind boy , and no doctor to pay , and the children must be fed , and a great deal of trouble , and she do n't get well because of it , and he does have trust in the Lord , but he has n't helped him yet -- ''", "context_len": 110}}}, "impulsively": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` And one of the best , '' I said impulsively , and as I thought of all that was going to happen to that most precious mother , and how soon that presence would be withdrawn from our mortal gaze , and how soon that spirit would go to the God who gave it , tears sprang to my eyes , and even Mrs", "context_len": 65}}}, "papa": {"freq": 286, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` I 'd have to be as fat as Auntie Belle to do that , '' she laughed , `` and now papa I want Marilla to tell you about a queer Home she was put in where they would n't let the little girls have dolls nor playthings , and they made dollies out of clothes pins and had to hide them", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 236, "word_index": 41, "context": "She stood for a few minutes , as if thinking over what he had told her , and then , her whole face lighting up , she said , `` Oh , yes , I know now ! I believe what papa tells me when he says he 'll take care of me , 'cause he always tells me true , and I know he can do it ; and that 's faith ; and I believe what you tell me , 'cause you tell me true ; and that 's faith ; and we believe what God tells us , even if we ca n't understand how it can be , 'cause he tells us what is true ; and that 's faith", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 42, "context": "He wrote to the man , and had a lot of fuss and bother with him ; but it was all fixed at last , and the papers drawn up , when what does she do a week ago , but tell papa she had changed her mind , and should not buy the grove at present", "context_len": 57}}}, "wrap": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "At first the night-air was delightful ; but as the hours went by poor Rosamund , who had not brought any extra wrap with her in her hasty flight , felt chilled and tired", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "'Of course he will ! I wonder you can ask such a question ! Heaven would n't be heaven unless we found Rollo there ! We 'll wrap his body in the Union Jack , and pick all our best flowers to strew round him ; and you might fire off your old pistol over his grave , if Father will let you have any gunpowder -- a parting salute , you know , like they do for officers , ' said Peggy , cheering up a little at the thought of arranging the obsequies", "context_len": 95}}}, "animated": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 24, "context": "One who knew them both well describes the contrast between aunt and niece at this period , -- Charlotte Tucker , so upright and animated , very thin , fair , with auburn hair , not very abundant , but which curled slightly , naturally , \u2019 -- and Letitia , grave , with beautiful dark eyes and hair , and rather dark complexion", "context_len": 64}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 30, "context": "Then , instead of attempting to restrain him , and worrying and checking him by useless efforts to hold him in , he gave him the rein freely , and animated and encouraged him with his voice , so that the horse flew across the plains at the top of his speed , the king and the courtiers looking on , at first with fear and trembling , but soon afterward with feelings of the greatest admiration and pleasure", "context_len": 79}}}, "questions": {"freq": 219, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 219, "word_index": 102, "context": "maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions , but she can not look on me as all a stranger , even if she hold no memory of those old days at Highgate", "context_len": 128}}}, "trailed": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 32, "context": "I was very well pleased with the manner in which the baseball nine acquitted itself , and I trust that at the more strenuous sport the colors of Randall will not be trailed in the dust", "context_len": 36}}}, "sleighs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Here were drawn up a number of sleighs", "context_len": 8}}}, "mysterious": {"freq": 152, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 152, "word_index": 55, "context": "Then , by grace of his own idea of fitness , Harold had added to the other furnishings a Dresden china toilet-set , and in this he was perhaps far wiser than he knew , for is there anything so well calculated to captivate at sight the heart of a dainty little maiden as the mysterious round-topped boxes that compose the dainty outfit of the ideal dressing-table ? Then , to crown it all , a pair of ponies and a basket-phaeton had been purchased for the exclusive use of the guests that were to be", "context_len": 96}}}, "bearskin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` I guess so , '' replied 'Tilda Jane , with an equally mysterious whisper , and she slipped down under the soft bearskin robe", "context_len": 25}}}, "parent": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 79, "context": "She was the older daughter , a strong , healthy girl of seventeen , who never helped her mother about the work , who dressed in such cheap finery as she could obtain by hook or crook , who took music lessons on a rented melodeon paid for out of her mother \u2019 s hard earnings , who felt herself to be a lady unfortunately born out of her sphere , and who was unquestionably ashamed of her surviving parent and her brother and sister", "context_len": 85}}}, "smothering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "The lumber merchant and his son were down in a black valley of soft , smothering furs , Poacher was running agreeably behind , and Gippie was snug and warm in her lap", "context_len": 33}}}, "furs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "But it was no Santa Claus , and in spite of the muffling furs and the heavy beard , in spite of all the changes which ten long years of absence had made , the mother 's heart , and the mother 's eye knew her son , and rising from her seat with a low cry of joy , Mrs", "context_len": 61}}}, "frosty": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 27, "context": "The best place for them was in some woods-pasture where the trees stood free of one another , and around them , in among the tall , frosty grass , the tumbled nuts lay scattered in groups of twos and threes , or fives , some still yellowish-green in their hulls , and some black , but all sending up to the nostrils of the delighted boy the incense of their clean , keen , wild-woody smell , to be a memory forever", "context_len": 83}}}, "illuminations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "His influence and ascendency seemed firmly established now in the country of the Greeks , and in due time he returned to Macedon , and celebrated at \u00c6g\u00e6 , which was at this time his capital , the establishment and confirmation of his power , by games , shows , spectacles , illuminations , and sacrifices to the gods , offered on a scale of the greatest pomp and magnificence", "context_len": 70}}}, "brink": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 81, "context": "If we had it we could go on , we could go on at least till the end of the season , and there would be an excuse to take your mother to the country , and she might never know , never ; but it wants two months to the end of the season , and the house is full , and every one is in the height of good humour , and yet they are all walking on the brink of a precipice ; the earth is eaten away beneath us , and any moment the whole thing may topple through", "context_len": 103}}}, "ice": {"freq": 118, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 112, "word_index": 37, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` But once more she heard the voice as it wailed 'Too late ! too late ! ' and the icy wind came and arrested her returning footsteps , for it chilled her to ice by the side of Gervassen , for whom she had dared so much", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "At the foot of the wall of rock lay great stones piled one over another ; and looking through the spaces between these , the little girls saw masses of ice hard as the rock above , which lay there all the year round", "context_len": 44}}}, "reference": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 6, "context": ", doubtless uses the word in reference to her peculiar mode of entering into Indian ways , Indian customs , Indian thoughts , -- as , for instance , sitting on the floor among them , instead of on a chair , travelling in an ekka like them , and so far as she was able living their life , -- as well as to the rigid simplicity and self-denial which she cultivated", "context_len": 73}}}, "breaking": {"freq": 102, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 88, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` Yes , yes , '' she murmured abstractedly ; `` who could fail to love him ? so valiant and so goodly to look upon , so tender unto his friends , and to me his poor wife , and ever was the inward joy in his bosom breaking forth in praises to God -- and yet '' -- turning wildly on Dick -- '' yet you will let him die ! You are as hard as the nether millstone ! Dick , do not shake your head , you must go ! You must force Prince Rupert to hear you", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 26, "context": "The bos \u2019 n \u2019 s voice was heard faintly above the roar of the elements , and looking up I saw that , by the breaking of the ship , the forepart of the Gannet was raised in consequence of the \u2019 midship portion subsiding , and that her bows were nearly level with a flat , rocky ledge but twenty feet away", "context_len": 64}}}, "avenue": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 77, "context": "It would be useless to try the highways , she knew , so accordingly her search must be in the by-ways , and she made a little tour of investigation round all the back streets between the inn and the Grammar School , but without success , and she was just thinking she must have missed him , and had better return to the inn-yard , when a fortunate chance prompted her to turn up a retired avenue which lay between the two main roads", "context_len": 85}}}, "leafless": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "He thought it was another rabbit , and he was about to pass on when he looked up , and saw , through the leafless trees , a big buck gazing full at him", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 87, "context": "Marching up to what I conjectured to be the king 's house -- from the fact that it was not only by far the largest dwelling in the enclosure , but was also distinguished by an exclusive embellishment in the form of a row of a dozen poles , each surmounted by a human skull , planted upright in the ground before it -- we halted at a distance of some twenty paces from the entrance , with our backs turned toward the crucifixion tree , the leafless branches of which overshadowed us , and waited", "context_len": 96}}}, "thrown": {"freq": 222, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 191, "word_index": 75, "context": "While things were in this state -- the Thebans having understood that Alexander had been killed at the north , and supposing that , at all events , if this report should not be true , he was , without doubt , still far away , involved in contentions with the barbarian nations , from which it was not to be expected that he could be very speedily extricated -- the whole city was suddenly thrown into consternation by the report that a large Macedonian army was approaching from the north , with Alexander at its head , and that it was , in fact , close upon them", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 13, "context": "The boys tried a great many of them , in an old orchard thrown open for building-lots behind my boy 's yard ; but they could not find any that were not sour ; or that they could eat till they thought of putting salt on them ; if you put salt on it , you could eat any kind of green apple , whether it was going to be a sweet kind or not", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 58, "context": "Did these belong to her ? asked Marie-Celeste , standing in open-eyed wonder before a mosaic table , which had been cleared to make room for a quaint collection of foreign-looking , childish possessions -- a mandolin , a well-worn little missal , a remarkable doll , a necklace or two , numerous little childish trinkets , and thrown over a chair , standing close to the table , a little gown of white silk and exquisite embroidery , yellow and limp with age , but none the less dainty and lovely", "context_len": 92}}}, "paradise": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "Good Parson Perrient had painted Providence plantation as a sort of paradise , where the liberty and toleration dreamt of by a few in England were the law for all ; but was that refuge open to him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "homeless": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 40, "context": "But the distress brought with it a longing to be a comfort to others ; and he set out for Lisbon , which had just been visited by the great earthquake of 1755 , with the hope of assisting the homeless and suffering", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Such dogs as those , in kennels , which are for sale , will get homes , for they will be in good condition , but this is a homeless little dog and I \u2019 d rather have him", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Herstan and all his family , who had returned in peace to their dwelling , have come to us homeless and destitute", "context_len": 22}}}, "angelically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Softly tinted lights in the background illuminated and made angelically beautiful the white dresses and glowing faces of a lady and three little girls who stood on the threshold with outstretched arms", "context_len": 32}}}, "threshold": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 48, "context": "Dol was , alas ! in some new mischief , -- mischief such as John did not dream of ; or , although the gust swept through the forest and over the lake , and the rain poured heavily down just as he set his foot upon the threshold , he had not gone so quietly to his mother 's sitting-room , and read the paper aloud to her , as she knitted away on his next winter 's stockings", "context_len": 80}}}, "embraces": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The father and son welcomed to these embraces had forgotten 'Tilda Jane , and as the sleigh slowly turned and went down the cold avenue , tears streamed silently down her cheeks", "context_len": 32}}}, "immediate": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 58, "context": "But when he returned home on flying visits , he found to his dismay that his uncle 's visionary hopes were growing instead of fading ; and from desiring a merely republican England , General Harrison had begun to dream of a theocracy as complete as that of the early Jews , and to look forward to the immediate inauguration of an earthly Reign of the Saints , under the sceptre of Christ Himself , as the Fifth and last of the great monarchies of the world", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "But this consideration was soon swept aside by the realization of his own immediate peril", "context_len": 15}}}, "pacing": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 50, "context": "Martha , who had no notion of waiting on such sorry customers , was careful to place everything within arm 's reach on the table and then to disappear , and the meal was eaten in silence , with no one in the room save the doctor , who kept pacing up and down in a manner that was intended to expedite their departure", "context_len": 64}}}, "growling": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 45, "context": "And Gippie was after him , for , snorting with rage and mortification at the resuscitation of his rival , he had bounded from under the stove , and , with his maimed tail wagging excitedly in the air , was biting , snapping , growling at Poacher 's heels , nipping him fiercely , if by chance he paused a second to rest", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "But alas for his hopes ! The blow was well delivered , and sent the cat back snarling and growling , but the force of it broke the branch off close to the lad 's hand , and the best part of his weapon fell to the ground", "context_len": 48}}}, "monotonous": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 33, "context": "She had once been taken to a circus , and the picture now before her mind was that of a tiger pacing back and forth in his cage , growling in a low monotonous tone , always growling , growling at a miserable child shrinking outside", "context_len": 46}}}, "roused": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 7, "context": "At the end of that time he roused himself from his stupor of grief and disappointment , and , abandoning the idea of any further attack upon the point that had cost him so dearly , he ordered the troops to move round and renew the attack upon the wall in front of the Jews ' quarter , and commence the construction of a battery on the edge of the great ditch facing the retrenchment behind the breach before effected", "context_len": 80}}}, "blurred": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "But when they reached the spot , which , because of the warmth of the water , contained no trace of snow , though elsewhere the ground was white , there was , of course , no evidences of the man , save for blurred footprints", "context_len": 46}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The footprints were plainly visible in the snow , being blurred occasionally by little drifts that had blown over them", "context_len": 20}}}, "wavered": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 30, "context": "This suspicion was strengthened as Winton finished speaking and turned away ; for , in spite of himself , Bern could not help glancing toward Stone , and his eyes wavered beneath the boy \u2019 s steady , questioning gaze", "context_len": 40}}}, "entranced": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "And then for three days together , during the hour for the daily chat , Marie-Celeste sat an entranced listener , while the wonderful story was told of beautiful little Isabel of Valois , the child-queen whom Richard of Bordeaux brought to England at the age of nine , and whose childish reign was so soon concluded", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "Bishop , appealed to these simple savages ; a sentimental ditty , such as `` The Anchor 's weighed '' or `` Tom Bowling , '' would hold them breathless and entranced ; `` Rule , Britannia ! '' or `` Should He upbraid '' set them quivering with excitement ; and they seemed to know by intuition that `` The Sailor 's Hornpipe '' was written to be danced to , and they danced to it accordingly a wild , furious , mad fandango in which the extraordinary nature of the gambols of the performers was only equalled by the ecstasy of their enjoyment", "context_len": 105}}}, "sights": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 20, "context": "I am a working-woman myself and can not afford to do much on my own account , and there 's sights of trouble on every side ; but , if you 'll excuse me , I 'm bound to say I 've given away many a bit of bread since that wet afternoon , just along o ' thinking of you -- an ' how wet an ' cold you was , an ' how hungry you looked ; an ' yet you gave away your hot buns as if you was a princess", "context_len": 94}}}, "knitting": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 44, "context": "There were an old grandfather in the corner smoking a pipe , an old woman beside him with a white cap on her head , a middle-aged man cleaning a gun by the light of a lamp on the table , a middle-aged woman knitting a stocking , and a cluster of children of all ages about the grandfather , grandmother , father and mother", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Hartley simply took her knitting , and sat the entire morning right in the midst of the little party just outside the cottage-door", "context_len": 23}}}, "cluster": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 58, "context": "The other was a heliotrope which had been given to Bessie by her papa , and which also had been little more than a sprig when she first had it ; but it was now a flourishing little plant , not running riotous in the way Maggie 's geranium did , but fresh and fair , with a cluster or two of delicate , perfumed blossoms , and the promise of more", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The pirates had now abandoned their guns , and had begun to cluster for \u2019 ard , under the shelter of the bulwarks , each man armed with pistol and cutlass", "context_len": 31}}}, "grandmother": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 28, "context": "In the morning they were dry , but just as hard as stone , and you had to soap the heel of your woollen sock ( which your grandmother had knitted for you , or maybe some of your aunts ) before you could get your foot in , and sometimes the ears of the boot that you pulled it on by would give way , and you would have to stamp your foot in and kick the toe against the mop-board", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` No , '' replied her grandmother very kindly", "context_len": 9}}}, "clatter": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was a great clatter and noise and shouting of `` I beg your pardon , '' or `` pray excuse me , '' or `` may I ask you to be so kind , '' when the pirates fell over each other , or got in each other 's way , or wanted to be helped to lift or drag something", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "In spite of every precaution , masses of black , shiny fuel would clatter down from the steadily-diminishing heap", "context_len": 19}}}, "afterward": {"freq": 99, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 89, "context": "That was all there was in it , and yet can you not surmise how even that letter was calculated to work great consternation in the mind of some one in the little thatched cottage -- some one who never saw the letter itself , and who did not so much as know of its existence until it had been read and re-read and thought over and answered , but who when one day he was made acquainted with its contents felt as weak as a kitten for hours afterward ? He happened to be lying on the lounge in the living-room at the time , the same lounge to which he had been carried more dead than alive apparently , just four weeks before", "context_len": 125}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 32, "context": "If one of you girls will go with me to present me to the lady , I can take down what she says in shorthand and knock it off on the type-writer afterward", "context_len": 33}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "It was through such seizure of chances that the wonderful system of the Pony Express Riders amazed the country throughout the months the service lasted , until the telegraph and afterward the railway put it out of business", "context_len": 38}}}, "en": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "She don \u2019 t da \u2019 s \u2019 t talk about ye to hum , ye know , but w \u2019 en she \u2019 s away she kind o \u2019 finds opportetunities to discuss the subjec \u2019", "context_len": 37}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "You see I was jest about to enter the stable door that mornin \u2019 w \u2019 en that young feller appeared a-comin \u2019 down the path , and as \u2019 e appeared I disappeared be \u2019 ind the corner o \u2019 the barn", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas must topis [ 92 ] retain , Must guard against sunstroke , to health such a bane ; And midst frogs and mosquitoes must patient remain , Yes , e \u2019 en when tormented , must smile through their pain ; And , with courage like that of the knights of Charlemagne , By Mission Miss Sahibas snakes should be slain", "context_len": 65}}}, "rapture": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 117, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}}}, "regretfully": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 29, "context": "To-morrow morning , then , my dear , if your Father will be at liberty , I shall give myself the pleasure of calling upon him ; ' and regretfully closing the lid , he tore himself away from the old chest , like a reluctant lover from his mistress , and took his departure , leaving Peggy , nearly wild with excitement , to rush tumultuously into the house with her wonderful piece of news", "context_len": 76}}}, "rap": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "How long I sat there I know not ; it may have been one hour or it may have been many ; but some time during the night there came a rap at the door and the pictures of Jane Ryder were blotted out of the fire and went flitting up the chimney", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "As he proceeded , the somewhat extravagant language of Piper was overlooked by all , and now , with this climax , the judge was compelled to rap repeatedly to restore quiet and order in the room", "context_len": 37}}}, "expected": {"freq": 302, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 291, "word_index": 119, "context": "She was askin ' some more questions about my sick friend , when somebody pulls the door-bell as furious , and when it was opened , there was a servant-gal lookin ' as scared as anything , and she tells the old lady her little granddaughter was lost , and could n't be found nowhere , and was she here , and did they know anything about her ? Well , they did n't know nothin ' , and the old lady said she 'd be round right away , and she herself looked scared ready to drop , and I see she had n't no more thought for me nor my belongin 's , nor could n't be expected to , so I just takes my leave", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 41, "context": "It was weary , anxious work ; for of course our movements were being regulated by a theory that , for aught we knew to the contrary , might be all wrong ; and as day succeeded day without bringing the expected sail within our ken there were not wanting among us those who denounced the skipper 's plan as foolish , and argued that the proper thing would have been to go direct to the Gaboon , and look there for the Virginia", "context_len": 84}}}, "bashfully": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "Later in life , he sometimes went to the saloon where it was sold in the town , and bashfully gasped out a demand for a glass , and ate it in some sort of chilly back-parlor", "context_len": 37}}}, "alluring": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "At some points it was nearer and at some farther ; but , nearer or farther , the forest encompassed the town , and it called the boys born within its circuit , as the sea calls the boys born by its shore , with mysterious , alluring voices , kindling the blood , taking the soul with love for its strangeness", "context_len": 62}}}, "circumference": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "It was indeed a kingly tree , thirteen feet and a half in circumference at the roots , bearing its enormous coronal of leaves in that symmetry of proportions which this tree ( seen nowhere in its perfection but in the North American forests ) sometimes exhibits", "context_len": 47}}}, "hesitating": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 15, "context": "He was always in demand , notwithstanding this failing , -- the economical captains never hesitating to go one hand short when they had Flour ( alias James Peterson ) for cook , as he was always ready to lend a hand , and was worth three common men in bad weather", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` In that case it does matter not at all , '' replied the owner of the house in the slow hesitating manner of foreign-spoken English", "context_len": 26}}}, "curs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He came in , and was greeted silently and politely by two respectable curs that rose from the hearth-stone for the purpose , then he lay down beside them , and gratefully extended his limbs to the fire", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "A man who keeps such ugly curs around him deserves to be soaked", "context_len": 13}}}, "admiration": {"freq": 100, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 100, "word_index": 56, "context": "Nicholas , you saved the life of the grand master ; and in the order of the day he issued on the morning we left , granting you three months ' leave for the recovery of your wounds , he took the opportunity of recording , in the name of the council and himself , their admiration for the services rendered by you during the siege , and his own gratitude for saving his life when he lay helpless and surrounded by the Moslems -- a testimony of which any knight of Christendom might well feel proud", "context_len": 97}}}, "meditation": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 5, "context": "Harold was indulging in this meditation as he stood waiting by the break for the rest of the party , for thinking comes very easy when one has nothing to do ; but wise are the folk , big or little , who , like Harold , resolve to banish uncomfortable thoughts from the mind when convinced that thinking is not in the least likely to better them", "context_len": 68}}}, "emigrants": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 7, "context": "Boltzius , the minister of the Salzburger emigrants , settled in the vicinity , depicts their character in dark colors ; he states `` they are much inclined to Robbing and Stealing , '' but was evidently influenced by the Y\u00e1massi and Yamacraw in their vicinity , who hated them as a race foreign to themselves", "context_len": 56}}}, "liquid": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "To each great house belonged a black drink cook , and from the young warriors two or three men were appointed to attend to those who took this liquid every morning ; they called the townspeople to this ceremony by beating drums ( C", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 27, "context": "Carried away by his words , we dashed down the slope of the dyke , only to find , too late , that a broad belt of liquid mud , thickly interspersed with sedge , lay betwixt us and the water", "context_len": 41}}}, "barrel": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 103, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}}}, "um": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Ef I did n't know de diffunce I 'd turn myse ' f out ter graze wid de dry cattle , an ' stay wid um all thoo de season", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` Marse Cally Shannon , '' said Whistling Jim , the negro , addressing me , `` what you reckon make dem white folks bang aloose at we-all , when we ai n't done a blessed thing ? When it come ter dat , we ai n't ez much ez speaken ter um , an ' here dey come , bangin ' aloose at us", "context_len": 65}}}, "sepulchral": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Are you accoutered for the combat ? '' he whispered , in a sepulchral tone , sleepily brushing his long black hair from his eyes", "context_len": 26}}}, "phrase": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 58, "context": "With the fact of this garden is mixed a sense of drowsy heat and summer light , and that is all , except the blackboard at the end of the room and a big girl doing sums at it ; and the wonder why the teacher smiled when he read in one of the girls ' compositions a phrase about forging puddings and pies ; my boy did not know what forging meant , so he must have been very young", "context_len": 81}}}, "urged": {"freq": 94, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 94, "word_index": 70, "context": "is coming ; we don \u2019 t know whether she is sticking half-way on the road , waiting for the horse which we offered to send twelve miles , if she required it ! Like the famous little pig , we have eaten all the bread and butter ; and if the grand lady arrives -- without that faithless kahar -- what shall we give her to eat ? I urged Florrie at least to send to the city for meat ; but she fears that in the absence of the cook the guest may arrive", "context_len": 96}}}, "trickled": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 62, "context": "Though called boiling springs in some parts of the country they do not boil or bubble on the surface , as a rule , though there is a constant supply of warm water from some subterranean source , so , that , as in the case with the spring Fenn was viewing , the water ran over from the pool , and trickled off through the woods", "context_len": 67}}}, "delighting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Edna swung the big key on her finger by its string and trotted along by the side of Reliance , asking many questions , and delighting to hear Reliance enlarge upon the all-important subject of the Thanksgiving festivities", "context_len": 38}}}, "popper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "A little maid danced from the fireplace to one of the tiny pigeon-hole rooms opening from the large one , and presently came back with a bag of corn and a popper", "context_len": 32}}}, "sipping": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "He did not say much , but it seemed to me that at times , when he appeared to be sitting carelessly sipping his sherbet , he was really trying to listen to what Vrados was saying to me", "context_len": 39}}}, "linger": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "So while a bitter ache shot through Bomba at the loss of Tatuc , his best and oldest friend of the monkey tribe , he dared not linger in the spot", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "I was doing a most imprudent thing to linger by the side of the pyre , for the smoke , in the first place , and the light of the flames when it fell dark , could scarcely have failed to attract to the spot any savages who might have been in the neighbourhood , when my plight would probably have been as bad as ever ; but at that moment my sorrow at the loss of my companion overcame every other feeling , and , for the moment at least , I was quite indifferent as to what befell me", "context_len": 101}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The plains blazed with yellow flowers which seemed to run in streams of molten gold from every ca\u00f1on , and linger in great pools on the flats and line all the ditches", "context_len": 32}}}, "rousing": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 33, "context": "He thought to himself `` I am the first up , '' but on going forward a few steps , found himself mistaken , several of the men were moving briskly about , rousing the lazy horses and oxen , or building fires", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Now I will give him a real rousing fright , '' she said to herself", "context_len": 16}}}, "yeahs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "As fur dis chile , chattered Jethro , with his thick blanket wrapped about him to his ears ; I shan \u2019 t get warm fur sebenteen yeahs", "context_len": 28}}}, "smoothed": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 66, "context": "she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little creature whom every servant and every governess alike dreaded", "context_len": 128}}}, "tweed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The woman hesitated , laid her knitting on her lap , and thoughtfully smoothed her tweed dress", "context_len": 17}}}, "walks": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "Oh , but it is a wonderful place ! and there are probably other rooms just as wonderful ; so a little closer look at the brass shields and the helmets , and the portraits of the sovereigns ranged along one side , and then , wholly unsuspicious of any disapproval , he walks over to the children and remarks that now he would like to see the other rooms , please", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 63, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "One can walk through ghosts as one walks through the mists of the early morning", "context_len": 15}}}, "clutching": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was so steep that in places they were obliged to sit down and slide , clutching at the trees as they passed , to save themselves from falling , and bringing down such showers of small loose stones and shale that it seemed as if the whole hillside were sliding too , and Lilian was very relieved when they all got safely to the bottom", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "By the deft use of his machete Bomba cleared away the clutching branches and released her", "context_len": 16}}}, "guttural": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 6, "context": "While the Indians talked in their guttural language , sometimes sitting for many minutes of silence between their laconic sentences , Bomba fretted and fumed , eager to be once more on the trail of the headhunters", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "The Cheroki Indians call a Nache , Natche or N\u00e1ktche person A no tse , A nno tse , the people or tribe An\u00edno tse , shortened into Ani ` htse , which proves that a guttural has been elided from the present form of the name", "context_len": 47}}}, "mes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Allons mes enfants , est-ce que le -- '' and then followed more smooth syllables that 'Tilda Jane did not understand", "context_len": 21}}}, "que": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Allons mes enfants , est-ce que le -- '' and then followed more smooth syllables that 'Tilda Jane did not understand", "context_len": 21}}}, "sweetly": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 43, "context": "But he had been in the kitchen that evening , when Maggie and Bessie had gone to the porch to speak to his father for Lem , and he had heard all that had passed ; and now , as he remembered how sweetly and generously the two dear little girls had pleaded for the boy who had treated them so badly , he could not resolve to give him even a part of the punishment he so richly deserved", "context_len": 80}}}, "kernels": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "The woods were pretty full of the kind of hickory-trees called pignuts , and the boys gathered the nuts , and even ate their small , bitter kernels ; and around the Poor-House woods there were some shag-barks , but the boys did not go for them because of the bull and the crazy people", "context_len": 55}}}, "swelled": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 2, "context": "The shouts swelled into a deep roar of execration , as the sledge on which the prisoner lay bound neared the place of execution , and Richard Harrison , struggling to keep his place as close to the victim as he might , thought with grim bitterness of the day when this same mob , silent and cowed , had seen General Harrison ride back from the scaffold at Whitehall", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "If a plebe 's swelled head shrinks at the rate of three inches a day , how many months will it be before it fits his brains ? ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "gentleness": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Here all was gentleness and peace", "context_len": 6}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "As an instance of Fanny \u2019 s peculiar gentleness , it is told that one Sunday , when she saw a man trying to sell things , she went up and remonstrated with him , speaking very seriously , but in so mild and courteous a manner , so entirely as she would have spoken to one who was socially on her own level , that he was utterly unable to take offence", "context_len": 73}}}, "amiable": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 116, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}}}, "quarrel": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 28, "context": "It was somewhat neglected , it is true , but perhaps it looked none the less picturesque for that , and certainly no one would be disposed to quarrel with the beautiful ripe strawberries and the sweet little yellow gooseberries with the hairy skins , or the big red plums that hung upon the old brick walls", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 16, "context": "He simply lay and glared at her , and by no means disposed to seek a quarrel with him , she made her way back to the kitchen , opened the stove door , and , lighting a piece of paper , searched the room until she found the closet where the candles were kept", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` And do n't you and Maggie ever quarrel ? ''", "context_len": 11}}}, "slap": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 3, "context": "Charlie began to slap his hands on his sides , and cry , Rover , Rover , \u2019 when a great rustling was heard in the woods , and the pig came on the gallop , his black and white sides glistening in the sun as he ran", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` I did n't say anything , but I felt very passionate , and I thought naughty things , -- how I 'd like to give him a good slap when he teased me , and , Maggie , for a moment I 'most thought I wished he did not come home", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "And I can not go to the wonderful world where Frank and other boys and many people live and laugh and slap each other on the back", "context_len": 27}}}, "motioning": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "I accordingly rose to my feet and , motioning the guards to lead the way , followed them to the palace , with Ama walking by my side and holding my hand in a protective sort of way", "context_len": 38}}}, "yield": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 40, "context": "It was all very well for her to tell me that I must cure him ; but suppose that I could not , suppose that the sickness -- whatever it might be -- should run its course and fail to yield to my treatment , what then ? The reason , so urgently expressed by her , why I must effect a cure -- '' for your own sake , Dick , '' -- was significant enough of the direction in which her apprehensions pointed ; there was no necessity for me to inquire what she meant", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then she makes everything in the house yield to him ; none of the others are allowed to tease him or cross him in the slightest thing", "context_len": 27}}}, "spells": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 22, "context": "There was an old lady living up on the third floor , an aunt or something and she was afraid of bad spells , she did have some , and she 'd ring her bell for Marilla an ' it would be upstairs an ' down , sometimes way down to my kitchen , and lugging those two fat babies up and down the street -- -- ''", "context_len": 68}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "If you have ever studied political economy , your Grace will know for yourself that when you spend more than you receive it spells RUIN", "context_len": 25}}}, "floors": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 60, "context": "And fearful places he found them to be ! Boys who were taken to gaol for the first time were put with old and hardened criminals ; the prisons were dirty and ill-smelling ; the dungeons were dark and unhealthy ; and , unless prisoners could afford to pay for comforts , they were obliged to sleep on cold bare floors , even delicate women not being exempted from such cruel treatment", "context_len": 72}}}, "darkness": {"freq": 196, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 190, "word_index": 6, "context": "O misery ! to wake in darkness , wake From vision of unutterable joy ; Instead of trumpet-sound and song of Heaven , To hear the dull clock measuring out time , When I had seemed to touch Eternity ! In the first pang of disappointed hope , I wept that I could wake from such a dream ; Until Faith gently whispered , Wherefore weep To lose the faint dim shadow of a joy Of which the substance shall one day be thine ? Live in the hope , -- that hope shall brighten life , And sanctify it to its highest end", "context_len": 104}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "A blinding sheet of driving rain , that darkened the air and drove against the river , and rose again in clouds of spray ; a gale that lashed the water into fury ; and darkness that shut out the river , and the boat , and the swimmer , and left nothing but a humbled group of shivering cadets", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "The good and wise God who has made our earth to move in such a way as to give us both light and darkness as we need them , has also given to it a power to draw towards itself , all things that live or grow upon its surface", "context_len": 50}}}, "rage": {"freq": 134, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 127, "word_index": 48, "context": "The vast throng then rolled along upon the top of the wall till they came to stairways and slopes by which they could descend into the city , and , pouring down through all these avenues , they spread over the streets , and satiated the hatred and rage , which had been gathering strength for seven long months , in bursting into houses , and killing and destroying all that came in their way", "context_len": 75}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "after the most approved fashion [ 545 ] and adjusted [ 546 ] them , so he might see and certify himself of the death of Alaeddin and the safe keeping of the lamp under the earth ; and he looked well into [ 547 ] the figures , both mothers and daughters , [ 548 ] but saw not the lamp , whereupon rage overrode him and he smote the sand a second time , that he might certify himself of Alaeddin 's death , but saw him not in the treasure ; whereat he redoubled in wrath , and yet more when it was certified to him that the lad was alive upon the surface of the earth and he knew that he had come forth", "context_len": 128}}}, "pretentious": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` The Golden Gulch Hotel , '' answered Hadley , shading his eyes and reading from a distance of fifty rods the pretentious sign of the little inn", "context_len": 28}}}, "theirs": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` No , indeed ! Nurse , with Maggie on one arm and Bessie on the other , fairly dancing about the room in her efforts to save the former from Aunt Patty 's clutches , both terrified babies screaming at the top of their voices , both old women scolding at the top of theirs ; while Fred , the monkey , young as he was , stood by , clapping his hands and setting them at each other as if they had been two cats", "context_len": 87}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` I say , '' said George , slapping Guy on the shoulder , the moment after his father bade them `` good-night '' and went to his wagon , telling them to go to theirs , `` I say , I have got the best thing to tell you , and we 'll have the greatest fun , if you do n't turn sneak and try to get out of it", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 45, "context": "But partly because the ground was really too poor to be of much good , and partly because the children begged him to leave it alone , it had never yet been disturbed , and the Wilderness , as they had named it , remained theirs to all intents and purposes", "context_len": 51}}}, "surmounted": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 78, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "tower": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 104, "context": "Never in all this world was there a happier little host than Harold Harris when he found how kindly his guests from across the water were taking to the life at Windsor ; but who would not have taken kindly to it , I should like to know ? The Queen herself , in her great castle on the hill , could not have planned more for the comfort of her guests than did Harold in his little castle beneath it ; and , indeed , this name of Little Castle had somehow attached itself to the pretty stone house , with its round tower and moat-shaped terrace", "context_len": 108}}}, "highly": {"freq": 76, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 77, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "satisfactory": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 30, "context": "I gathered from their remarks that they were seriously concerned at my condition , for it seemed that I was reserved for some very especial refinement of torture , the satisfactory application of which demanded that I must come to it in the possession of my full strength , which they feared had been seriously sapped by the suffering which I had already endured , and they freely expressed their concern lest , under existing circumstances , I should not furnish quite so much sport as was being expected of me", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Borden on her return and found everything satisfactory and the child content", "context_len": 12}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Was ever any will satisfactory where property was divided up into small gifts ? Five hundred dollars to this one and to that one , three hundred apiece to some others", "context_len": 31}}}, "stoical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Callipers sat there in the wagon , under the eye of the deputy sheriff , with stoical indifference on his face", "context_len": 21}}}, "abode": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 7, "context": "[ 354 ] On this wise she abode a week 's space [ 355 ] and the Sultan saw her at each Divan ; so , when she went on the last day [ of the week ] and stood , according to her wont , before the Divan , till it was ended , but could not muster courage to enter [ 356 ] or say aught , the Sultan arose and entering the harem , turned to his chief Vizier , who was with him , and said to him , `` O Vizier , these six or seven days [ 357 ] past I have seen yonder old woman come hither at every Divan and I note that she still carrieth somewhat under her veil", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 60, "context": "A part of the Cheroki anciently dwelt in caves ; and concerning the caverns from which the Creeks claim to have issued , James Adair gives the following interesting disclosure : `` It is worthy of notice , that the Muskohgeh cave , out of which one of their politicians persuaded them their ancestors formerly ascended to their present terrestrial abode , lies in the Nanne Hamgeh old town , inhabited by the Mississippi-Nachee Indians , [ 150 ] which is one of the most western parts of their old-inhabited country", "context_len": 91}}}, "glimmer": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 38, "context": "The amidship portion of this space is termed the cockpit , and , though nearly empty , it did not require much imagination on my part to see the forms of mangled seamen dimly outlined in the feeble glimmer of the lantern , young Drake telling me of some of the ghastly sights of the cockpit during action in a highly-worded and realistic style", "context_len": 64}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Dillson 's eyelids were swollen with the cold , but there was still room for a disagreeable twinkle to glimmer through", "context_len": 21}}}, "perchance": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 20, "context": "And Casson ! Dear old Casson ! Was he still in the land of the living ? Had the Hondura perchance found him , and was he now safe and sound in the friendly maloca ? Bomba scarcely dared hope , and yet he forced himself to hope , for he could not bear the thought that he might not see the old man again", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "There is a saying that where there is smoke there must be flame , and , acting on this rather dubious statement , the gallant fire brigade hunted high and low , searching in every nook and corner of the building , and even searching the desk drawers to see if perchance the cunning fire had run away and hidden there", "context_len": 61}}}, "unfastened": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "For an hour or two , when we are not on a long passage , the padlocks are unfastened , in order that we may jump over and bathe , and exercise our limbs ; but at this time the knights are always on guard , and as we are without arms we are altogether powerless", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "So she dressed herself in her everyday clothes , and , taking the little parcel , she softly unfastened the door , and then she slipped down through the silent house and entered Sir John Wallis 's study , and laid the packet which contained all the symbols of her success and her letter of confession on his desk", "context_len": 59}}}, "latch": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "At the gate they paused , for it was shut , and he of the gray whiskers , after fumbling at the latch with his whip , was evidently preparing with much reluctance to dismount , when , spying Peggy , he called to her , and asked her to open it", "context_len": 52}}}, "porch": {"freq": 104, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 104, "word_index": 71, "context": "She laid down her work to wipe her eyes , and , observing two of her sons grappling in fraternal war at the house corner , she arose to cuff each one impartially , exclaiming , `` Ea , muchachos ! You fight before my very eyes , eh ? Take that ! and that ! '' Waddling reluctantly back to her sewing , she saw Lola standing in the white-pillared porch of the big adobe house beyond , and a gleam of inspiration crossed the se\u00f1ora 's dark , fat face", "context_len": 92}}}, "stumbled": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 48, "context": "She meant to lay the hands together , as she remembered her mother 's had been laid long ago in the stricken gloom of the Kansas farmhouse which had been her home ; but suddenly there was a movement at her feet , and she stopped , having stumbled over some living thing in the shadows of the couch , something that stirred and struggled and gasped passionately , `` Vamos ! Vamos ! ''", "context_len": 75}}}, "dresser": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 110, "context": "the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner , and the oak dresser , with its rows of blue willow-pattern plates ; and back again at last to where Mrs", "context_len": 128}}}, "beams": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 29, "context": "Father and Bobby were asleep in two minutes , but Peggy lay awake for a long time , watching the shadows of the peat fire flicker upon the rough beams of the roof , till at length fire and heather merged into a dreamland kingdom , where she was walking with Rollo upon the clouds , and fishing teacups out of a flowing sea below", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "afoot": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "I got to the top of the hill , and my courage gave out , and I didn \u2019 t dare come down , and I thought I would ride back on the road a piece farther , and then turn the horse loose and let him come home , while I went on afoot ; but Old Charlie would come , whether or no , and --", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "Early in the morning , before the high mass , whereat the neighbourhood is generally present , I received a missive from the sheriff , bidding me , in the name of the King , to exhort my people to remain at home tonight , since danger is afoot , and there is likely , he says , to be a rising on the part of the pagans who dwell amongst us", "context_len": 72}}}, "crutches": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 45, "context": "Then he turned his face round to the bottles , an ' when he looked out it was red , an ' he was shakin ' all over like as if he 's been cryin ' , an ' he jus ' pointed to the crutches an ' said , 'Take 'em , an ' welcome", "context_len": 56}}}, "reaching": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 15, "context": "Such proceedings as these could not of course long exist without the fame of them reaching the ears of the king , and I had only given some three or four performances when I was summoned to entertain his Majesty and his household , which I did in the great square before the palace , my audience numbering quite two thousand ; Banda and his numerous family being seated in a huge semicircle -- of which I was the centre -- in front of the palace , while the rest of the audience filled the remaining portion of the square", "context_len": 100}}}, "dangerous": {"freq": 127, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 127, "word_index": 57, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}}}, "vexed": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` No , '' repeated Lena , still more vexed that he should suppose her to be capable of such an evasion , which to her sense of uprightness would have been as bad as speaking a falsehood by word of mouth ; `` no , of course I did not , that would have been telling her , would it not ? One can do a falsehood as well as tell it , '' and although she had intended no reflection upon her brother , no thrust at him , Percy was ashamed as he remembered how often , during the last few months , he had done this very thing ; how he had shuffled and evaded , and thought it no great harm as long", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 18, "context": "The loss of the first of his much-prized Osborn apples , while they were yet half-ripe , had vexed him sorely , and he would have liked that Lem should have been severely punished for that theft , even had he not , in common with the rest of the household , believed that he had stolen the silver cup", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Guy , on his part , was very much vexed with George , for having brought him on such a profitless adventure ; but though he was suffering very much from his rashness , the whole thing appeared to him so ridiculous , that he laughed long and heartily", "context_len": 49}}}, "clutch": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 70, "context": "He judged Harry Paul , in fact , by the same measure as he would have meted out to an enemy himself ; and so terrible were his thoughts , so horrifying to him was the thought of the death from which he had escaped , that he was robbed of all energy ; he had not strength to do more than hang there clinging to the weeds with desperate clutch , and , with only his head out of water , gaze up in Harry 's stern eyes", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "It required no little effort to hold on and support the drowning man as well , but even a few moments ' rest gave him some return of power , and he was helped now by his companion , who in a feeble struggle to get at and clutch something , caught at the seaweed , into which his fingers convulsively wound themselves , and thus gave Harry Paul a hand at liberty for his own use", "context_len": 77}}}, "poise": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "He looked as if life was regaining its poise with him , and his voice seemed quite cheerful as he said , `` Well , it 's about my little girl ! I 'm bound for a mountain-camp , and it 's no place for a motherless child", "context_len": 48}}}, "disposed": {"freq": 82, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 75, "context": "What would be thought of an English soldier who , on being ordered to some lonely and difficult post , were , instead of going at once , to begin to calculate whether it were worth while , -- whether the cost and trouble of his going would be sufficiently repaid by results ? Yet such is the spirit in which certain soldiers of the Cross -- somewhat faithless soldiers , surely ! -- are disposed to regard this great Marching Order of our Captain and King", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 10, "context": "I do not say that every one was not before disposed to obey you in all things , willingly and cheerfully ; but he put it so strongly to them that they had volunteered specially for service in this galley , knowing well who was to be its commander , and the circumstance that the crew was to consist solely of young knights , and had therefore specially pledged their honour so to act that the enterprise should be in all respects a successful one", "context_len": 85}}}, "lighting": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 23, "context": "She stood for a few minutes , as if thinking over what he had told her , and then , her whole face lighting up , she said , `` Oh , yes , I know now ! I believe what papa tells me when he says he 'll take care of me , 'cause he always tells me true , and I know he can do it ; and that 's faith ; and I believe what you tell me , 'cause you tell me true ; and that 's faith ; and we believe what God tells us , even if we ca n't understand how it can be , 'cause he tells us what is true ; and that 's faith", "context_len": 124}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "They turned somersaults from the decks of the canal-boats ; some of the boys could turn double somersaults , and one boy got so far as to turn a somersault and a half ; it was long before the time of electric lighting , but when he struck the water there came a flash that seemed to illumine the universe", "context_len": 60}}}, "searched": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 84, "context": "Who can tell all that goes on in the mind of a little child , or what it understands of death ? In a vague way Peggy knew that her playfellow had 'gone to heaven , where mother is , ' but she did not think of that as any cause for sorrow , nor did she connect him for an instant with the place where she stood , but , with her nurse 's words still troubling her , she knelt down and searched among the white flowers that hid the bare earth beneath", "context_len": 95}}}, "closet": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` [ 662 ] Therewith he disappeared , and as for Alaeddin , when he heard this , his wit fled from his head and his joints trembled at the cry wherewith the Marid cried out at him ; but he took heart and leaving his closet , went in straightway to his wife and feigned to her that his head irked him , of his knowledge that Fatimeh was renowned for the secret of healing [ 663 ] all aches and pains", "context_len": 83}}}, "pattering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Little feet would come pattering , or skipping along the hall , a small , curly head peep within the door , and then vanish at sight of her , while a whisper of `` She 's there ; let 's run , '' told the cause of its sudden disappearance", "context_len": 51}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "It was at one of these times , when Maggie , Bessie , and Frankie were all enjoying themselves as much as three happy children could do ; with shoes and stockings off , pattering about with bare feet in the cool , sparkling water , while mamma , grandmamma and Aunt Patty sat with their work upon the piazza , that the little fellow secured his prize", "context_len": 68}}}, "fluency": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "The Sultan proceeded to make familiar with Alaeddin and to talk with him , and Alaeddin answered him with all courtliness and fluency , as he had been bred in kings ' palaces or as he were their constant associate ; [ 471 ] and the more the talk was prolonged between them , the more gladness and joy redoubled on the Sultan for that which he heard of the goodliness of Alaeddin 's answers and the sweetness of his speech", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "There is Sir Ralph Harcourt , father ! she exclaimed in Italian , which was the language most used on the Island , and spoken with more or less fluency by all the knights , among whom it served as a general medium of communication", "context_len": 45}}}, "brat": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Shut up , you little brat ! cried Joe , flinging a pitch knot at him with a good will , that , if he had not dodged , would have broken his head", "context_len": 34}}}, "permission": {"freq": 93, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 92, "word_index": 37, "context": "Sir Knights , he said , I have heard from my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction ,", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I can get permission easily enough , for I 've been doing good work in class lately", "context_len": 18}}}, "idiot": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 29, "context": "Toby is a good , wise , faithful dog , and knows a great deal , but the weakest , the most ignorant boy or girl -- that poor idiot you saw the other day -- is far better , of far more value in the sight of God , for he has a soul ; and to save that precious soul , our Lord left his heavenly home , and died upon the cross", "context_len": 75}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Withdraw the safety-pin , and -- -- ' Here , you idiot , what are you doing ? ''", "context_len": 19}}}, "hire": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 25, "context": "He was a slave , while slaves were held in New England , and had been many voyages with Captain Rhines , who used to hire him of Peterson , his master , to whom he was so much attached that he would never leave him , although he had every opportunity to run away when at sea ; and not even the love of liquor could prevent him from bringing home a present for his master", "context_len": 77}}}, "cluttering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "He hated to have women cluttering round , and he lay , and perspired , and inwardly raged , and obtained not one wink of sleep , while 'Tilda Jane , having obtained what she wished , peacefully composed herself to rest", "context_len": 42}}}, "perspired": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "Everything portended it ; the atmosphere was absolutely still , not a twig or even a leaf stirred , all nature seemed to be waiting in breathless suspense for the coming outbreak ; even the insects had ceased to attack us , and had retired to their leafy retreat , and the air was so heavy and close that , naked as I was , I perspired at every pore", "context_len": 70}}}, "inwardly": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 0, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "bade": {"freq": 99, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 74, "context": "[ 358 ] Hast thou any knowledge of her , O Vizier , and knowest thou what is her want ? '' `` O our lord the Sultan , '' replied the Vizier , `` verily women are little of wit ; and most like this woman cometh to complain to thee of her husband or one of her folk , '' The Sultan was not content with the Vizier 's reply , but bade him , an she came again to the Divan , bring her before him forthright ; [ 359 ] whereupon the Vizier laid his hand on his head and answered , `` Hearkening and obedience , O our lord the Sultan", "context_len": 116}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Then the Sultan bade decorate the city and hold festival and rejoicings and commanded the crier to cry in the city that that day was a great festival , wherefore rejoicings should be holden in all the realm during the space of a month , [ to wit , ] thirty days ' time , for the return of the Lady Bedrulbudour his daughter and her husband Alaeddin", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "At half past ten Sir John Kendall went round among the knights and bade fifty of them arm themselves quietly , and proceed , one by one , down to the gate , and there await orders", "context_len": 37}}}, "dusty": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "High up in the peak of the barn the morning light streamed in through a broken , dusty , cobwebby window ; with the passing of the night the storm had passed also , and the new day was bright and fair", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 31, "context": "He could no more remember learning to set type than he could remember learning to read ; and in after-life he could not come within smell of the ink , the dusty types , the humid paper , of a printing-office without that tender swelling of the heart which so fondly responds to any memory-bearing perfume : his youth , his boyhood , almost his infancy came back to him in it", "context_len": 72}}}, "comfortless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Beyond the kitchen was a front hall , -- cold , dusty , and comfortless", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "In the winter months they were very cold and comfortless apartments", "context_len": 11}}}, "unfurnished": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Up-stairs were four rooms , two unfurnished , one having something the appearance of a spare room left long unoccupied , the other smelling of tobacco , exceedingly untidy , littered with old clothes , fishing rods , bats , cartridge shells , and other boyish and manly belongings", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "After alluding to the manner of her earlier English life , and contrasting it with the manner of her existence at Batala , where two chairs were placed on two sides of a table in a large and almost unfurnished room , \u2019 Mr", "context_len": 44}}}, "unoccupied": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "Up-stairs were four rooms , two unfurnished , one having something the appearance of a spare room left long unoccupied , the other smelling of tobacco , exceedingly untidy , littered with old clothes , fishing rods , bats , cartridge shells , and other boyish and manly belongings", "context_len": 49}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Is your little room unoccupied to-night ?", "context_len": 7}}}, "smelling": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was exceedingly interesting to the American contingent of the party to gain a little insight into the life of the English navvies ; and they passed the little tap-room , reeking with smoke and smelling of pipes and beer mugs , rather more often than circumstances would warrant , for the sake of looking in on the jolly fellows , and catching a sentence or so of their almost unintelligible dialect", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 68, "context": "With a favourable wind the fleet arrived off the Forelands and thence beat up for the Downs , where we were in a position to meet either the Dutch or the French squadrons ; but off Dover we learned from a fishing boat that Beaufort was seen heading back towards Brest in order to refit some of his ships , so that for the time our chances of smelling powder were very remote", "context_len": 73}}}, "rods": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 86, "context": "or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length , as well because it is an important one as to show the almost insuperable difficulties that beset the", "context_len": 128}}}, "bats": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 69, "context": "A grassy glade under a tall beech seemed intended by Nature for a summer-house , for there was a large flat stone in the middle , which served for a table , and a circle of little stones round , just high enough for seats , so that you might imagine Queen Mab and her fairies dined here on moonlight nights , with the squirrels for guests and the bats and owls for waiters", "context_len": 74}}}, "cartridge": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}}}, "shells": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 41, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "The grandmother , who was visiting in the house at the time , declared to the mother that it would serve the father and the boys just right if she did fill these very shells up and give them to the father and the boys to eat", "context_len": 47}}}, "boyish": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Up-stairs were four rooms , two unfurnished , one having something the appearance of a spare room left long unoccupied , the other smelling of tobacco , exceedingly untidy , littered with old clothes , fishing rods , bats , cartridge shells , and other boyish and manly belongings", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 19, "context": "High or low , the drivers had a great deal of leisure , which commended their calling to the boyish fancy ; and my boy saw them , with a longing to speak to them , even to approach them , never satisfied , while they amused the long summer afternoon in the shade of the tavern by a game of skill peculiar to them", "context_len": 65}}}, "manly": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 28, "context": "It was too much for the boys , but the performance of the big dive was looked upon as the passing of a lad from boyhood into the manly stage , upon which he entered through the Shangles Gate , and then swam back , coming , as it were , of age amidst the shouts of his companions to swim ashore and land upon the big boulders , where the boys bathed and learned to swim in the calm weather , gazing the while in admiration at their older companions", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "His manly and athletic form , his frank and open manners , his spirit , his generosity , and a certain air of confidence , independence , and conscious superiority , which were combined , as they always are in the case of true greatness , with an unaffected and unassuming modesty -- these and other traits , which were obvious to all who saw him , in the person and character of Alexander , made every one his friend", "context_len": 80}}}, "belongings": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 31, "context": "It was by no means a cheery announcement to the household in the little thatched cottage when Ted told them that evening , that two days later he must gather his belongings together and turn his back on the home and the friends that had formed his little world during all the long weeks of convalescence ; and then when he asked if Donald might perhaps be permitted to go up to London with him , Mrs", "context_len": 77}}}, "damp": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 35, "context": "There had been quite a spell of sickness in Gorswen at the end of the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 60, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Luckily Christmas Day was fine ( for a wet Christmas is enough to damp the stoutest spirits ) , but after that the weather indulged in such a variety of changes , alternately freezing and thawing from day to day , that there was no time for a safe crust of ice to form even on the smallest and shallowest of ponds", "context_len": 62}}}, "coverlets": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` Good land ! '' she murmured , `` I wonder how long sence those blankets has been washed ? '' and she turned them back again , and , going to the other room , obtained two coverlets that she spread over herself , after she lay down on the outside of the bed", "context_len": 55}}}, "patter": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "It did Aunt Patty no harm , but a great deal of good , that long afternoon 's musing in the silent house , where no patter of children 's feet , nor any sound of young voices was heard ; for baby had gone to her grandmamma , so that even her soft coo and joyous crow were missing for some hours", "context_len": 63}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Through the hall and up the stairs , as fast as the little feet could patter , never pausing till she reached mamma 's room , where she buried her face in one of the sofa cushions ; and there her mother found her some moments later", "context_len": 47}}}, "milder": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "Faith ! I dare be sworn you have seen service ; but , my good enemy , '' continued Rupert , in a perceptibly milder tone , `` 't is not now war-time , and we soldiers have no say in matters of civil justice", "context_len": 45}}}, "snoring": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` Upwards it leads to the banqueting hall , and you can comprehend my escape this evening , '' said he ; `` but our path is now downwards , unless you would like to go up and see the drunken beasts of murderers snoring off their debauch upon the floor as they fell ; oh , that it were lawful for a Christian man to cut their throats as they lie ; many innocent lives would be saved thereby , which those brutes will live to destroy", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "She was very tired but she read quite awhile before the gentle snoring began , and she was glad enough to go to bed", "context_len": 24}}}, "eloquent": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "Facts are often more eloquent than words ! The Batala people have seen B -- n suffering keen anguish for Christ \u2019 s sake ; they see that the property which was -- -- s is his no more , for Christ \u2019 s sake", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "We were still too far off to hear much , and there was too much of Hindi mixed with his Urdu to make his language clear ; but we could see the man \u2019 s eloquent , animated gestures , and hear the rich tones of his voice", "context_len": 48}}}, "shine": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 43, "context": "Another day Has past ! another weary weary day , And I am so much nearer to my grave ! Oh that I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "It is true that we are told not to let our left hand , know that which is done by our right hand ; but are we not also commanded so to let our light shine before men that they may see our good works and glorify our Father in heaven ? And can we do so , or truly show our love to him , if we hide the services rendered for his sake behind a mask of coldness and reserve ? My dear aunt , for his sake , for your own , for the sake of the affection and confidence which I wish my children to feel for you , and which I believe you wish to gain , let me satisfy them that it", "context_len": 128}}}, "ideal": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 65, "context": "Then , by grace of his own idea of fitness , Harold had added to the other furnishings a Dresden china toilet-set , and in this he was perhaps far wiser than he knew , for is there anything so well calculated to captivate at sight the heart of a dainty little maiden as the mysterious round-topped boxes that compose the dainty outfit of the ideal dressing-table ? Then , to crown it all , a pair of ponies and a basket-phaeton had been purchased for the exclusive use of the guests that were to be", "context_len": 96}, "NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 43, "context": "The pioneer days were still so close to those times that the love of solitary adventure which took the boys ' fathers into the sylvan wastes of the great West might well have burned in the boys ' hearts ; and if their ideal of life was the free life of the woods , no doubt it was because their near ancestors had lived it", "context_len": 65}}}, "sleeplessness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The cross old man would have given the world to have turned her out of his house at that very minute , but his night of sleeplessness and raging temper had given him a fierce headache , a bad taste in his mouth , and such a helplessness of limbs that he could not turn in bed", "context_len": 57}}}, "commanded": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 77, "word_index": 29, "context": "It is true that we are told not to let our left hand , know that which is done by our right hand ; but are we not also commanded so to let our light shine before men that they may see our good works and glorify our Father in heaven ? And can we do so , or truly show our love to him , if we hide the services rendered for his sake behind a mask of coldness and reserve ? My dear aunt , for his sake , for your own , for the sake of the affection and confidence which I wish my children to feel for you , and which I believe you wish to gain , let me satisfy them that it", "context_len": 128}}}, "ordered": {"freq": 188, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 187, "word_index": 6, "context": "At another time nurse would have ordered them to shut their eyes and not speak another word ; but to-night she seemed to think it quite right and natural that they should be so very wide awake , and not only gave them an extra amount of petting and kissing , but told them stories of Uncle Ruthven 's pranks when he was a boy , and of his wonderful sayings and doings , till mamma , coming up and finding this going on , was half inclined to find fault with the old woman herself", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` She brought me my pills as usual this morning -- you know I am ordered pills for indigestion -- and after I had swallowed them she announced that she had changed them for wood-lice , which curl up as you touch them", "context_len": 43}}}, "pityingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "'Tilda Jane fortunately did not know that if he could have commanded his tongue he would have ordered her into the street , but she saw that there was something wrong with him , and as she stood in his doorway , she said , pityingly , `` I guess you 're sick ; I 'll make you some breakfast , '' and she vanished in the direction of the wood-shed", "context_len": 71}}}, "chopping": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "Penelly did not speak , and Zekle remained silent , but he was evidently moved to indignation at what had been said , for he kept lifting his big oar and chopping it down in the water as if he were trying to take off the master 's head", "context_len": 49}}}, "teakettle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "She built up the fire , set the teakettle on the back of the stove -- he wondered why she did not put it on the front , and why she gradually piled on sticks of wood until there was a roaring blaze that caused him some slight uneasiness", "context_len": 49}}}, "omelet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "She made an omelet , she toasted bread , she steeped him a cup of tea -- this slip of a girl", "context_len": 22}}}, "toasted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "She made an omelet , she toasted bread , she steeped him a cup of tea -- this slip of a girl", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "Then , at five every clear day , afternoon tea with the captain , for which they had a standing invitation , and by means of which both she and Chris came to be on terms of wonderful intimacy with that august officer , so that they joked over the rare souchong and delicious little toasted cakes ( the secret of whose making was kept close-guarded by the steward ) with a familiarity that , to themselves at least , never ceased to be a wonder", "context_len": 86}}}, "steeped": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "petted": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Beside , he had funny tricks and ways of his own which they thought much better than those , and was as full of play and mischief as a petted doggie could be", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 49, "context": "But the dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of", "context_len": 128}}}, "keenness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Piper , who was addressed as Sleuth by his companions , was a washed-out , colorless fellow , having an affected manner of keenness and sagacity , which were qualities he did not seem to possess to any great degree", "context_len": 40}}}, "despair": {"freq": 101, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 98, "word_index": 77, "context": "Naturally , I was very thankful that the awful day had passed over , and that its end found me still in the land of the living ; for `` while there is life there is hope , '' and in the course of my somewhat adventurous career I have seen so many extraordinary escapes from apparently inevitable disaster that the one piece of advice above all others which I would give to everybody is `` Never despair ! '' I can recall more than one occasion when , if I had abandoned hope and the effort which goes with it , I should not now have been alive to pen these words", "context_len": 113}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "And now , as she mused , it came to her swiftly that she need not despair so long as she had over her head a substantial dwelling", "context_len": 28}}}, "defensive": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "During the second round the Maori kept strictly on the defensive , while van Eindhovengen tired himself considerably in making blind and ineffectual rushes at his nimble opponent", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "In war , the Cha'hta east of the Mississippi river were less aggressive than those who resided west of it , for the policy of keeping in the defensive agreed best with their dull and slow disposition of mind", "context_len": 39}}}, "fault": {"freq": 121, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 121, "word_index": 12, "context": "When she was much younger , extreme carelessness had been her besetting fault , and , as is the manner of this `` little fox , '' had created much trouble for herself and for others ; but having become convinced that it was her duty to cure herself of this , she had set to work to do it in such earnest that that which had been a burden and a care to her was fast becoming a settled habit , and it was but seldom now that any act or word of heedlessness could be laid to her charge , while her ever obliging disposition and loving heart prompted many a deed of kindness which she never failed to carry out if it were in her", "context_len": 128}}}, "punish": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 57, "context": "She had heard His holy name taken in vain every day of her miserable little life ; she had never until now heard it spoken in love and reverence ; and the only idea she had had of Him , had been as some great but terrible being who some day might find her out , and punish her for the naughty things she had done", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Haven \u2019 t I promised that no matter what it is , I shan \u2019 t punish you , provided you make a clean breast of it", "context_len": 27}}}, "anger": {"freq": 126, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 119, "word_index": 72, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` I do n't want Jonas to own all the property in Aguilar ! '' Generosity and anger swayed him confusedly ; but as he watched Jane trudging down under the Dauntless 's tipple he became clear enough to register with himself a vow", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "Quiet and self-contained and little given to impulse as he was , Seabrooke , when roused to anger or resentment , was a very lion in his wrath , and there was one thing which he could never tolerate or overlook , and that was any attempt to take an unfair advantage of him", "context_len": 54}}}, "increased": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 85, "word_index": 105, "context": "but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed not to do aught against him , so he was dumb and could make the Sultan no answer", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` With terrific exertion , '' he writes , `` in two or three years ' time I may with God 's administration make a good province -- with a good army and a fair revenue and peace , and an increased trade , -- also have suppressed slave raids", "context_len": 50}}}, "obstinacy": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 17, "context": "She was not perfect , -- who is ? The strength of determination would occasionally run into obstinacy ; the resolute manner could be a trifle dictatorial ; the very wish to help and please others might be carried out in a way which did not gratify", "context_len": 47}}}, "unconventional": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Peggy liked her new friend at once ; his twinkling blue eyes , his bronzed face , his jovial voice , and rather unconventional dress , seemed to mark him as distinct and different to other people", "context_len": 37}}}, "ignorant": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 98, "context": "he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward , [ 660 ] I excuse you , for that ye are innocent", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 34, "context": "His face had in it that indescribable quality -- a touch of suffering or of sorrow -- that always draws me , and I thought how strange it was that he should sit there ignorant of the fact that a word or two would make me his friend for life", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "There was no longer any doubt , even to so untrained and ignorant a woman as she was , that little Roy was very ill", "context_len": 25}}}, "reflect": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 57, "context": "This was not altogether because he was needed there , I dare say , but because it was part of his father 's Swedenborgian philosophy that every one should fulfil a use ; I do not know that when the boy wanted to go swimming , or hunting , or skating , it consoled him much to reflect that the angels in the highest heaven delighted in uses ; nevertheless , it was good for him to be of use , though maybe not so much use", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "It is one thing to have Irene here ; it is another thing to look at that terrible lake and reflect that the boat is at hand", "context_len": 27}}}, "protective": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "I accordingly rose to my feet and , motioning the guards to lead the way , followed them to the palace , with Ama walking by my side and holding my hand in a protective sort of way", "context_len": 38}}}, "suppliant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "She was too unconventional , and too ignorant , to reflect that her protective attitude would have been better changed for a suppliant one in entering the old man 's domain", "context_len": 31}}}, "domain": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 3, "context": "In this superb domain there were two summer-houses and a shed where bee-hives stood ; at the end of the garden was a bath-house , and you could have a shower-bath , if you were of a mind to bring the water for it from the pump in the barn-yard", "context_len": 50}}}, "privilege": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 18, "context": "And now we were to welcome the well-known and gifted authoress into our house ! This was a privilege ; and earnestly did we look forward to the pleasure of receiving her ; though at the same time we were perhaps conscious of a slight shadow of doubt crossing our minds , as to how far one of Miss Tucker \u2019 s age would be able to accommodate herself to the new surroundings , and bear the trials incident to life and work in a tropical climate", "context_len": 87}}}, "introspection": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "However , if she had meekly begged the privilege of taking care of him , he would have sent her away , and as she was given neither to hair-splitting nor introspection , but rather to the practical concerns of life , she calmly proceeded with her task of tidying the house without reference to future possibilities", "context_len": 57}}}, "concerns": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 38, "context": "However , if she had meekly begged the privilege of taking care of him , he would have sent her away , and as she was given neither to hair-splitting nor introspection , but rather to the practical concerns of life , she calmly proceeded with her task of tidying the house without reference to future possibilities", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` It 's nothing so unimportant , '' Edna continued ; `` for it concerns you boys , too , but if you do n't want to know I 'll go up to Dorothy 's ; she 'll be interested even if she is n't going", "context_len": 46}}}, "smoked": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 39, "context": "He lived in a belated log-cabin that stood in the edge of a corn-field on the river-bank , and he seemed , one day when my boy went to find him there , to have a mother , who smoked a cob-pipe , and two or three large sisters who hulked about in the one dim , low room", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "They had sand for sugar , blades of timothy grass for corn , sea-weed for smoked beef and ham , and small pebbles for eggs , with larger ones for potatoes", "context_len": 31}}}, "girding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It needed cleaning , and girding on some old aprons she found in the porch , she let the fire go out , and then brushed , and rubbed , and poked at the stove until it was almost as clean outside as it was inside", "context_len": 46}}}, "poked": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 1, "context": "He poked his nose into all the rabbit-burrows , he chased the birds , and dug holes for rats , and generally behaved as if he were a puppy again , instead of a sedate , grown-up dog , snapping at the flies , and standing over Peggy wagging his tail in approval , while she gathered violets and wood-anemones", "context_len": 60}}}, "wipe": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 6, "context": "She laid down her work to wipe her eyes , and , observing two of her sons grappling in fraternal war at the house corner , she arose to cuff each one impartially , exclaiming , `` Ea , muchachos ! You fight before my very eyes , eh ? Take that ! and that ! '' Waddling reluctantly back to her sewing , she saw Lola standing in the white-pillared porch of the big adobe house beyond , and a gleam of inspiration crossed the se\u00f1ora 's dark , fat face", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "But the babies went to bed early an ' then she 'd come down an ' wipe the dishes for me an ' they made no call on her", "context_len": 29}}}, "reopen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then , and not until then , did she reopen the door into the old man 's room", "context_len": 18}}}, "hash": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "Dillson had not touched his breakfast , so she ate it herself , made him fresh toast , a cup of tea , and a tiny meat hash , then went up-stairs to tidy her bedroom", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "They have got potatoes in the ground , and that will give them hash all winter ; and beans growing , and a little piece of corn , that won \u2019 t come to much , but it will fat their pig , that \u2019 s now running in the woods", "context_len": 51}}}, "fuel": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 43, "context": "As only a slight sketch of Guy 's wonderful escape had been given by Harwood upon his return to camp , the children were anxious to hear a full account of it , and as soon as the dishes were washed , the fuel for the night brought in and Guy was at liberty to take his usual seat by the fire , they called upon him to tell them all about it", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "They had put upon the focus too little fuel one day when they brought it in", "context_len": 16}}}, "economy": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 16, "context": "The rigid simplicity which she cultivated was , no doubt , partly a matter of pure economy , that she might have the more to give away , -- partly a matter of her innate generosity ; but partly also it arose from a deep-rooted desire to remove the reproach , which has of late been often levelled at the ease and luxury , real or supposed , of many Missionaries in India or elsewhere", "context_len": 75}}}, "impotent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Will you not obey ? '' shouted the weak Ethelred , and stamped in impotent anger on the floor", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The youthful Prodigal appeared again in the doorway , his ready tears had made miniature deltas over his molasses-begrimed countenance , his lower lip hung down in an impotent despair", "context_len": 30}}}, "dealings": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 52, "context": "Often in my younger days had old Henry Martin and Master Collings told me tales of a buccaneering Captain Kidd and his bloodthirsty henchman , a renegade Scotsman called Angus Priddys , whose career was ended at Execution Dock ; so I formed a conclusion that these smugglers were men whose illicit dealings were not the worst of their accomplishments", "context_len": 60}}}, "happening": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 64, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 57, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}}}, "fuming": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The back of his rocking-chair was padded , and before he realised what was happening , his state of fuming passed into one of sleepiness , -- he was off , soundly and unmistakably announcing in plain terms , through throat and nose , to the world of the kitchen , that he was making up for time lost last night", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "One fuming , perspiring man , looking too fat to ever get cool , found the prize he had drawn was a moth-eaten fur overcoat", "context_len": 25}}}, "sleepiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The back of his rocking-chair was padded , and before he realised what was happening , his state of fuming passed into one of sleepiness , -- he was off , soundly and unmistakably announcing in plain terms , through throat and nose , to the world of the kitchen , that he was making up for time lost last night", "context_len": 61}}}, "soundly": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 64, "context": "retinue did he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions , but she can not look on", "context_len": 128}}}, "unmistakably": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "It testified unmistakably to the boy 's advance in years beyond the shameful period of skirts ; and no doubt it commended him to the shadowy little girl who lived so far away as to be even beyond the street-corner , and who used to look for him , as he passed , through the palings of a garden among hollyhocks and four-o'clocks", "context_len": 63}}}, "announcing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "The back of his rocking-chair was padded , and before he realised what was happening , his state of fuming passed into one of sleepiness , -- he was off , soundly and unmistakably announcing in plain terms , through throat and nose , to the world of the kitchen , that he was making up for time lost last night", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "He closed the office door , tacked to it a card announcing he would return inside of half an hour , and loaded into the wagon the entire morning 's freight except the two freezers intended for the picnic grounds", "context_len": 40}}}, "unfinished": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "When the Sultan examined it and saw that it was unfinished , he said , `` Woe is me for thee , O casement , that thou art not perfect ! '' Then , turning to the Vizier , he said to him , `` Knowest thou the reason of the lack of completion of this casement and its lattices ? '' `` O [ 512 ] my lord , '' answered the Vizier , `` methinketh it is because Thy Grace hastened upon Alaeddin with the wedding and he had no time to complete it", "context_len": 96}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Oh ! That 's because you are nothing but a girl -- but boys -- ! '' George left his sentence unfinished , for of late he had become very careful of boasting before Guy , whom he knew was too well acquainted with him to be deceived by empty words", "context_len": 52}}}, "sock": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "In the morning they were dry , but just as hard as stone , and you had to soap the heel of your woollen sock ( which your grandmother had knitted for you , or maybe some of your aunts ) before you could get your foot in , and sometimes the ears of the boot that you pulled it on by would give way , and you would have to stamp your foot in and kick the toe against the mop-board", "context_len": 82}}}, "shrewd": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "Half idly , Richard asked more questions and grew interested in the man 's tales , for the fellow 's varied experiences had given him a sort of shrewd cunning , which in a higher walk of life might have been almost worthy the name of diplomacy , and he knew how to fit his tale to his audience", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 3, "context": "Having a very shrewd suspicion that Father and Aunt Helen might not approve of such an escapade , Peggy took care not to mention her plans , and the children started off , feeling like a pair of conspirators , with the stable-lantern , a few extra pieces of candle , and a box of matches", "context_len": 56}}}, "recumbent": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 39, "context": "This is one of my greatest favorites , she exclaimed enthusiastically , as they came to the late Dean Wellesley 's monument , in the north aisle ; and she stood in rapt admiration looking down at the beautiful recumbent figure", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "There were dead , too , in ghastly heaps , some with their fingers still clutching the throats of their opponents , others in a naturally recumbent position that gave the appearance of having fallen easily to sleep", "context_len": 38}}}, "uncommunicative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "When it grew quite dark 'Tilda Jane got up , lighted a lamp , put on the teakettle , and with the slender materials at hand prepared a meal that she set before the uncommunicative old man", "context_len": 37}}}, "morsels": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "She then approached the table and ate a few morsels herself , muttering as she did so , `` I ai n't hungry , but I mus ' eat enough to help me square up to that poor ole crossy", "context_len": 40}}}, "muttering": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 81, "context": "I am afraid this time her stone was bigger , for it bounced with such effect on his shoulder , that he turned round with a flow of language far from elegant , shaking his fist at the tree with so much fury that Peggy was in terror lest he should climb up to find the author of the assault ; but he evidently thought the day too hot for such exercises , and with a final abusive epithet walked away muttering curses on all the children in creation", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 45, "context": "Meanwhile the night had fallen so intensely dark that the completion of the task of feeding us unfortunates had to be accomplished by torchlight ; and we had not been very long left to ourselves before the faint flickering of distant lightning and the low muttering and grumbling of thunder warned us to expect a storm of more than ordinary violence", "context_len": 61}}}, "absurd": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 37, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "Alexander replied , that if he wished to marry his daughter he could do it without his consent ; as to the ransom , he was not in want of money ; in respect to Darius 's offering to give him up all west of the Euphrates , it was absurd for a man to speak of giving what was no longer his own ; that he had crossed too many seas in his military expeditions , since he left Macedon , to feel any concern about the rivers that he might find in his way ; and that he should continue to pursue Darius wherever he might retreat in search of safety and protection , and he had no fear but that he should find and conquer", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "In those days the custom of drinking healths \u2019 had gained sway to an absurd and objectionable extent ; gentlemen being expected to respond to every toast , and not only to sip their wine , but very often to empty their glasses , under pain of giving serious offence", "context_len": 50}}}, "invasion": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 81, "context": "518 , in describing the mounds of the country , states `` that the region lying between Savanna river and Oakmulge , east and west , and from the sea coast ( of the Atlantic ) to the Cherokee or Apalachean mountains ( filled with these mounds ) was possessed by the Cherokees since the arrival of the Europeans ; but they were afterwards dispossessed by the Muscogulges , and all that country was probably , many ages preceding the Cherokee invasion , inhabited by one nation or confederacy ( unknown to the Cherokees , Creeks )", "context_len": 97}}}, "premises": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 45, "context": "In the first place they removed everything from the house to the beach ; then they hauled Charlie \u2019 s canoe up to the house , and filled it with water ; they also filled all the barrels , troughs , and tubs about the premises , and drove the cattle to the beach , lest the fire should run into the woods", "context_len": 63}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Though Mwanga threatened to burn alive any who frequented the mission premises , or adopted the Christian faith , they continued to come ; and the lads at the Court kept their teachers constantly informed of everything that was going on", "context_len": 41}}}, "energetic": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 28, "context": "You do n't think Father would really be likely to get us that governess , do you ? ' -- the horrible possibility of the lady , still energetic , though no longer young , and evidently capable of so much in the way of discipline , quite casting a gloom over his youthful spirits", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "He organised meetings , got up debates , and did , in short , those thousand and one things which an energetic , clever man can do to put fire and life into such proceedings", "context_len": 35}}}, "insist": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 34, "context": "I told her quite frankly that I had come without leave , and she said she was sorry for that ; and although she did wish to see me so much , she would insist on not allowing me to stay to supper , and brought me home , as you see , comparatively early", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "She was not one who would restlessly fight for and insist upon her own way at all costs , under the plea of doing what was right", "context_len": 27}}}, "storekeeper": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 59, "context": "Then the boys found another toy store ; but they considered the dealer mean ; he asked very high prices , and he said , when a boy hung back from buying a thing that it was `` a very superior article , '' and the boys had that for a by-word , and they holloed it at the storekeeper 's boy when they wanted to plague him", "context_len": 68}}}, "readily": {"freq": 73, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 26, "context": "Some hours later the captain returned , and upon my repeating to him the yarn which I had spun to the mate he not only very readily consented to my working my passage , but also offered me two excellent suits of clothes , two shirts , two pairs of stockings , a pair of shoes , and a worsted cap in exchange for my leopard-skin , which offer I gladly accepted ; and that night found me domiciled in the forecastle of L'Esperance as one of her crew", "context_len": 89}}}, "exercised": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` The King , for all he knew that this was a thing whereto the Vizier might not avail , no , nor the greatest King , [ 373 ] nevertheless exercised his clemency [ 374 ] and granted him the delay he sought ; then , turning to the old woman , he said to her , `` Go to thy son and tell him I give him [ my ] word that my daughter shall be in his name ; [ 375 ] but needs must I take order for her equipment ; [ 376 ] wherefore it behoveth him grant us a delay of three months", "context_len": 109}}}, "gleaned": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The hut had to be swept and dusted , late flowers to be gleaned from the garden to decorate the tea-table , cups and saucers packed up and conveyed in baskets , together with the little tin kettle and the methylated spirit lamp , as they could scarcely light a fire on the platform like they did for picnic teas in the woods", "context_len": 63}}}, "items": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 54, "context": "Such very Spartan fare was the result of his lecture that he wisely fixed her a weekly allowance , and left her to manage as best she could upon it , and this plan answered far better , for she had a natural aptitude for arithmetic , and soon learned to make the various items fall into their proper places , balancing her little account-book in quite a professional manner", "context_len": 70}}}, "eccentric": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 22, "context": "Could it be that she had made a mistake , after all , and that the neglected child had some powerful though eccentric friend in the background -- perhaps some previously unknown relation , who had suddenly traced her whereabouts , and chose to provide for her in this mysterious and fantastic way ? Relations were sometimes very odd -- particularly rich old bachelor uncles , who did not care for having children near them", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Oh ! I can see that you are as queer and eccentric as ever", "context_len": 15}}}, "secret": {"freq": 226, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 143, "word_index": 63, "context": "When I see five or six boys now lying under a tree on the grass , and they fall silent as I pass them , I have no right to say that they are not arranging to go and carry some poor widow 's winter wood into her shed and pile it neatly up for her , and wish to keep it a secret from everybody ; but forty years ago I should have had good reason for thinking that they were debating how to tie a piece of her clothes-line along the ground so that when her orphan boy came out for an armload of wood after dark , he would trip on it and send his wood flying all over the yard", "context_len": 124}, "NOUN": {"freq": 82, "word_index": 31, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "They really had to be secret at times , for some of the boys were not allowed to go in at all ; others were forbidden to go in more than once or twice a day ; and as they all had to go in at least three or four times a day , some sort of sign had to be used that was understood among themselves alone", "context_len": 68}}}, "ordinarily": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 10, "context": "She found out from them that the old man was ordinarily in better health than at present , -- that he was usually able to hobble about the house and wait on himself , for his temper had of late become so violent that no woman in Ciscasset would enter his house to work for him", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "His aunt had painted it and it was much handsomer than one ordinarily saw", "context_len": 14}}}, "usually": {"freq": 151, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 151, "word_index": 62, "context": "At the council this morning it was urged that some signal mark of honour should be granted ; but I interposed , saying that you had already received exceptional promotion , and that it would not be for your good , or that of the Order , for so young a knight to be raised to an official position of a character usually held by seniors , and that I was perfectly sure you would prefer remaining in command of your galley to any promotion whatever that would retain you on the Island", "context_len": 93}}}, "hobble": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "She found out from them that the old man was ordinarily in better health than at present , -- that he was usually able to hobble about the house and wait on himself , for his temper had of late become so violent that no woman in Ciscasset would enter his house to work for him", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` God bless him , and bring him safe out of their wicked hands , '' echoed the old woman , as she turned to hobble home", "context_len": 27}}}, "opportune": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "This was a singularly fortunate and opportune discovery , for I had already observed that fever and ague were very prevalent among the inhabitants , and I hoped that if by means of a decoction of cinchona bark I could effect a cure , I might be able very materially to improve and strengthen my position in the town", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The desire , then , on his part to learn Turkish is in all ways opportune", "context_len": 16}}}, "starving": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` I 'm glad there is more rice , '' said Nettie , `` for now that I know what a little it takes to make a big dish I sha n't be afraid of our starving while it lasts", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Don \u2019 t fancy we \u2019 re starving ! Oh , nothing like it ! We had a famous breakfast , chapatties , [ 61 ] eggs , etc", "context_len": 29}}}, "persuaded": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 61, "context": "Among other things were two or three peacock feathers , a turkey wing , some pebbles from the brook where papa used to sail his boats when he was a boy , a piece of rusty tin pipe , which , because it looked black and smoky , and came from the field where the burnt barn had stood , they persuaded themselves must be a part of the very leader down which papa slid when he ran for the ladder to save his little brother , -- all these , and other treasures of like value , were carefully collected and stowed in the wagons , to be carried to the Lake House , and thence to the city", "context_len": 120}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "But after some little coaxing from Bessie , who said she would not go without her , she was at last persuaded , and they set out with Jane , taking Flossy with them", "context_len": 34}}}, "benefactor": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then the boys saw him going about the streets , pale and tremulous , in a second-hand suit of his benefactor 's clothes , and fighting hard against the tempter that beset him on every side in that town ; and then some day they saw him dead drunk in a fence corner ; and they did not understand how seven devils worse than the first had entered in the place which had been swept and garnished for them", "context_len": 79}}}, "philosophical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Feeling persuaded of this , and greatly pleased to think that she had been and was of service to the father of her benefactor Hank , her attitude toward the old man continued to be one of philosophical and good-natured obstinacy", "context_len": 41}}}, "sullenness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "He still gave way occasionally to terrible fits of temper and sullenness , but 'Tilda Jane understood him better now , and was quick to soothe and pacify him , or , if he was unmanageable , to keep out of his presence until he recovered", "context_len": 46}}}, "behalf": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` How ! you must be beside yourself to come to me -- me ! -- Rupert ! on such an errand ! Because , forsooth , I exchanged civilities with one I held an honourable enemy , you dare to expect my interest on behalf of a regicide ! I vow , sir , every man who even witnessed that most abominable and unnatural murder should swing , did it depend on me", "context_len": 74}}}, "doggish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Flossy was not usually very willing to go to strangers ; but now , perhaps , his doggish instinct told him that this poor boy had need of pity and kindness", "context_len": 31}}}, "lock": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 63, "context": "So the note was written and left conspicuously upon the writing-table ; and then with one long farewell glance about them , and a flower stolen from a vase by Marie-Celeste and laid between the leaves of her prayer-book , they turned their backs on all they would ever be permitted to know of Lewis Carroll , and the door with the spring lock swung to behind them", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 54, "context": "Rosamund got up softly and locked the door , not a minute too soon , for she had scarcely done so before the handle was turned and the voice of Irene was heard outside crying through the keyhole , `` What changeling is in this room ? Which of you housemaids has dared to lock herself in ? Come out ! I 've got a big spider ready , and '' -- --", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "accession": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "The moment before Harry Paul seemed to have been exerting his full strength to force the boat through the water , but an accession of strength came to him , and with a few fierce thrusts he drove her bows into the edge of the current , which gave it so quick a snatch that it was whirled round , and its occupant nearly lost his footing ; but he was too practised a boatman for that", "context_len": 77}}}, "barrier": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` O Aescendune , once happy Aescendune ! '' was the thought , the bitter thought , as each hour placed a larger barrier of space between Alfgar and his late home ; all its happy memories came freshly back upon him , and particularly the thought of Ethelgiva , his betrothed , from whom he was so ruthlessly torn , torn as if he left part of himself behind", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Once more the sea moved on to attack the wreck , and coming to the third floating barrier the combers toppled over in harmless surf , such as that which washes the beach on a summer day when the wind is off shore", "context_len": 43}}}, "persuade": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 81, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` What 's the matter with Captain John Bracewell as master of the Sea Witch ? Would n't as fine a ship as this persuade you to go to sea again ? ''", "context_len": 33}}}, "unnatural": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` How ! you must be beside yourself to come to me -- me ! -- Rupert ! on such an errand ! Because , forsooth , I exchanged civilities with one I held an honourable enemy , you dare to expect my interest on behalf of a regicide ! I vow , sir , every man who even witnessed that most abominable and unnatural murder should swing , did it depend on me", "context_len": 74}}}, "acquainted": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "Among the twenty millions of people in the United States , there are perhaps two millions , between the ages of fifteen and twenty-five , who wish to become acquainted , in general , with the leading events in the history of the Old World , and of ancient times , but who , coming upon the stage in this land and at this period , have ideas and conceptions so widely different from those of other nations and of other times , that a mere republication of existing accounts is not what they require", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 41, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}}}, "benevolently": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 0, "context": "benevolently to shoot a few ; just to show the rest that really they must not expect to be allowed to build , and gossip , and make themselves disagreeable in every possible way in the room of a Buzurg Miss Sahiba", "context_len": 42}}}, "industriously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "He was industriously stirring more in the bread-pan , and there was a puzzled look on his face", "context_len": 18}}}, "eaves": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 37, "context": "But at length some of the deadly missiles fell where they could not be immediately reached , and one of these eluded the observation of the besieged until they saw a sheet of flame curl over the eaves beneath the roof , and play upon the surface of the huge beams above , until they suddenly started into flame", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` And she is white enough , '' he added , regarding Lola as she sat smiling and talking , with the boughy eaves making little shadows across the rim of her broad straw hat", "context_len": 35}}}, "melting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "In a little while they were ready to start , each one with a few sandwiches , while Bart , in addition , carried a small coffee pot , and a supply of the ground material for making the beverage in the woods ; water could be had by melting snow over a fire they would build", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "The course most of the time was in sight of the Platte River , which , swollen by the melting snows near the headwaters and the recent rains , was a broad , majestic stream", "context_len": 35}}}, "loosening": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Poor wretch ! '' said Harry to himself , as he gazed down at the helpless man , and , loosening and taking off his woollen jersey , he wrung it tightly , getting out as much water as he could , and then drew it on the stony cold figure lying in the washed-up dry brown weed", "context_len": 59}}}, "floating": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 79, "context": "Father 's reproachful face seemed to rise up out of the darkness , asking 'Where is Bobby ? What have you done with my little boy ? ' Good resolutions made , and alas ! too often forgotten , crowded in now upon her remembrance , and as she listened to the roar of the river , she thought how strange it would seem that they two , so full of life , might in a few hours be floating very still and silent upon that flowing stream , with the world only a memory behind them", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 11, "context": "The majority of the captives had been some time in their floating prison , and their first poignant grief had settled down into a dull and despairing acceptance of their fate ; the sailors , newly captured , had for hours raved and cursed , but , worn out by their struggle with the elements , and their rage and grief , they had now fallen asleep", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Compared with the somewhat boisterous conditions obtaining on board Transport 99 , the Gosport was a floating model of smartness and efficiency", "context_len": 22}}}, "despite": {"freq": 73, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 14, "context": "When the Vizier heard the Sultan 's words , his tongue was tied for despite and he was overcome with exceeding chagrin , forasmuch as the King had promised him that he would marry his daughter to his son ; so , after a little , he said to him , `` O King of the age , Thy Grace condescended to promise me [ 371 ] that the Lady Bedrulbudour should be my son 's ; wherefore it behoveth thine exalted highness appoint a delay of three months , [ 372 ] and God willing , my son 's present shall be greater than this", "context_len": 106}}}, "incorrigible": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "sulkiness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Anything like sulkiness or ill-temper from Jennie was so unusual that the other children listened in surprise ; but her mother saw very plainly what was the matter , and hoping it would wear off , thought it best to take no notice of it at present", "context_len": 47}}}, "sassing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` We 'll have lots o ' sport by an ' by runnin ' in the fields , Poacher , '' she whispered , lovingly , in his ear , `` you ole comfort -- always so sweet , an ' good , an ' never sassing back", "context_len": 48}}}, "creep": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 26, "context": "In spite of the best resolutions for early rising , nobody woke with the sun after all , and that luminary had plenty of time to creep round and peep in through the little window before Father sprang up from his bed of heather , and exclaiming that they were late , set the children to blow the peats into life again while he took his morning bath in the lake", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 92, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}}}, "sample": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "In a few hours , the black square , which the sun had warmed most , had sunk low into the snow ; the dark blue was almost as low ; the other colors had sunk less deeply ; while the white sample remained on the surface of the snow", "context_len": 50}}}, "abandoned": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 92, "context": "Naturally , I was very thankful that the awful day had passed over , and that its end found me still in the land of the living ; for `` while there is life there is hope , '' and in the course of my somewhat adventurous career I have seen so many extraordinary escapes from apparently inevitable disaster that the one piece of advice above all others which I would give to everybody is `` Never despair ! '' I can recall more than one occasion when , if I had abandoned hope and the effort which goes with it , I should not now have been alive to pen these words", "context_len": 113}}}, "fillip": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The dog abandoned his stately attitude , and gave his tongue a quick fillip in the direction of her forehead", "context_len": 20}}}, "mournful": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Well , I hope we may see them all back another summer , '' she said to Dolly and Fanny , who stood beside her , feeling almost as mournful ; `` if I 'd known I 'd feel so bad to part with them , I do n't know as I could have made up my mind to take them : but those dear little ones have just taken the heart right out of me", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "He had no difficulty in getting half-a-dozen damaged mackerel down in the harbour -- fish that had been torn by the nets ; but he was only just in time , for in the soft grey light he could see the gulls already busy floating down on their ghostly-looking wings in the gloom , uttering a mournful , peevish wail , and carrying off fragments of fish for their morning meal", "context_len": 71}}}, "reflections": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 30, "context": "While the latter was growing moment by moment more refulgent , the former caught the wondrous dyes , till the water seemed everywhere like molten gold with ruddy and empurpled reflections where the sea gave a gentle heave", "context_len": 38}}}, "squeezed": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 56, "context": "He was smuggled into the town and sold to Ben Ibyn the Berber , who , to conceal the matter , dyed him black ; but it got to the ears of the sultan , and he had him taken from the Berber , and brought here ; I have no doubt the merchant has been squeezed rarely", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` For , after all , they 're only the corners , '' said Maggie , `` and maybe when she tried to bear the policeman 's burden , and felt bad about the grove , that made her burden heavier , and so squeezed out her corners a little more , and they scratched her neighbors , who ought not to mind if that was the reason", "context_len": 68}}}, "reciprocating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He was boldly reciprocating , by licking her little , straight , determined nose , when there was a clicking sound around the corner of the house", "context_len": 27}}}, "licking": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 46, "context": "Tip , the dog , was already waiting for them there , for he seemed to know they were going that morning , and he began whimpering for joy , and twisting himself sideways up against them , and nearly wagging his tail off ; and licking their hands and faces , and kissing their guns all over ; he was about crazy", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Gerald was tempted to answer a licking , but he reflected that it would not be prudent", "context_len": 17}}}, "clicking": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "If the girl lived , and was going about saying that he had killed her dog , here was proof positive that he had not ; and smacking his lips , and making a clicking sound with his tongue , he tried to attract the resuscitated Poacher 's attention", "context_len": 49}}}, "penetrated": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 8, "context": "Taking my life in my hands , I penetrated their fortalice , and administered the last sacrament to her ; but they threatened my life for entering their domains , and , perhaps , had I been but a simple priest , and not also , small boast as it is , the son of a powerful English thane , whom they feared to offend , I had died in doing my duty", "context_len": 73}}}, "unlocked": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 10, "context": "Then the young girl rushed upstairs to her dormitory , unlocked her little private drawer , took out her sealskin purse , extracted one of the new half-crowns , and was down again by the little governess cart , whispering eagerly to Mademoiselle Le Brun , within the prescribed time", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "I had thought to surprise Whistling Jim , the negro , with my garb , but , as it turned out , the surprise was mine , for that night , when I went to see whether the horses had been properly groomed and fed , I found the door of the stable unlocked", "context_len": 54}}}, "dodging": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` If that fellow , '' continued Corporal Jasper , `` had any reason on earth for getting up at midnight , dodging sentry and running out of barracks , to stay till reveille , except to avoid fighting you that morning , now , by jingo ! I want to know what it is ! The class sent me to ask him , and he simply said he would n't tell , that 's all", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Barry saw three of them sink before Chick emptied the bomb racks with near misses on a dodging destroyer", "context_len": 19}}}, "yarn": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 79, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "That yarn about the convoy getting under way on Thursday night may be a blind", "context_len": 15}}}, "enemy": {"freq": 343, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 335, "word_index": 127, "context": "would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart of your enemy", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` And she said to him , `` O my son , the Sultan received me with all courtesy , according to his wont , and meseemeth he meant fairly by us , but [ for ] thine accursed enemy the Vizier ; for that , after I had bespoken the Sultan in thy name , even as thou badest me , reminding him that the term for which he had appointed us was past and saying to him , 'If Thy Grace would vouchsafe to give commandment for the marriage of thy daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour with my son Alaeddin , ' -- he turned to the Vizier and spoke to him", "context_len": 113}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Cassowary , '' he said , `` there 's some truth in that statement of yours , only the guns are family and not enemy", "context_len": 26}}}, "acuteness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "As it is , we have only been saved from the loss of our galleys and slaves by the acuteness of one of the youngest of our knights , who , in the first place , noted a suspicious occurrence which would have been passed by without attracting a moment 's thought by ninety-nine out of a hundred men", "context_len": 59}}}, "measure": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "The worth of any work can never be truly gauged by the amount of success which may appear to follow within a given time ; and to measure the extent or the effects of her loving influence , alike among younger Missionaries and among Indian Christians , especially among the boys in the Baring High School , is utterly impossible", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 73, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}}}, "depth": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 60, "context": "As soon as the battery was completed , gangs of men , accustomed to mining operations , set to work in its rear to drive sloping passages downwards , opening into the face of the great cutting , and through these vast quantities of earth and stones were poured , so as to afford a passage across it , the depth being largely diminished by the great pile of rubbish that had already fallen from the breached wall", "context_len": 78}}}, "animosity": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 13, "context": "The settlement of the French on the heights of Natchez , the growing animosity of the natives against the intruders , the three successive wars , the massacre of the colonists in November , 1729 , and the final dispersion of the tribe in February 1730 , are well-known historic facts and need not be repeated in this volume", "context_len": 59}}}, "agility": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 33, "context": "But , whatever the order may have been , Mafuta evidently objected to it , for no sooner had it been spoken than he sprang to his feet , and with quite marvellous agility , hurried to me and seized me by the left arm , saying in an angry voice something to the guards that I interpreted as an order to release their hold upon me", "context_len": 67}}}, "inspire": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "I CAN NOT quite understand why the theatre , which my boy was so full of , and so fond of , did not inspire him to write plays , to pour them out , tragedy upon tragedy , till the world was filled with tears and blood", "context_len": 48}}}, "lightness": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 74, "context": "Still , hope was in the wind , where it had not been for a long time , and , what was more , the first suggestion of spring was in it too , and every one knows what a tonic that is ; so the sigh , on the whole , did not have much of a show , and Harold set off for school with a heart that he hardly knew for lightness", "context_len": 75}}}, "twinkling": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Hearkening and obedience , '' replied the genie and disappearing , brought him in the twinkling of an eye all that he had commanded him withal , whilst in his hand he held a stallion , whose like is not among the horses of the Arabs of the Arabs , [ 456 ] with housings of the richest stuffs brocaded with gold ; whereupon Alaeddin called his mother forthright and delivered her the twelve slave-girls and gave her the [ twelve ] suits , [ 457 ] so she might dress herself [ 458 ] and go with them to the Sultan 's palace", "context_len": 105}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 1, "context": "The twinkling lights in the harbor were still shining when the little girls went to bed , but before morning a wilder light was blazing from the point where old Cap'n Si 's little house stood , and , the next morning when the children looked across to where yesterday they had seen the old man sitting on the bench outside his door , the smoke curling from the chimney and the flowers in his little garden making a brave showing , they beheld but a heap of blackened ruins", "context_len": 90}}}, "stooped": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 97, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "alternately": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 56, "context": "He cast his eyes critically toward the sun , hesitated for another minute , and then , shouldering his hoe , started down the hill toward the lake ; but before he had gone half-way to the water \u2019 s edge he stopped and stood still , nervously chewing a spear of June-grass , and glancing alternately back at the cornfield and forward to the tempting waters of the lake", "context_len": 70}}}, "unreal": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "At the same time , while he cowered along in the shadow of unreal dangers , he had a boy 's boldness with most of the real ones , and he knew how to resent an indignity even at the hands of the teacher who could send him to the Chemical Room at pleasure", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "At first the events of that fearful night seemed all like a dream , and mingled themselves with the strange spectres which haunted me in delirium ; but afterwards the real separated itself from the unreal , and I knew that my father and all his friends , my Danish uncles amongst them , had perished with the whole household assembled there that fatal day", "context_len": 65}}}, "ruffling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was the slightest ruffling of the smooth skin where the crutch had struck him", "context_len": 15}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "'Oh , I say , look here , you know , ' cried the boy , ruffling up his chestnut locks with both hands , which was a way he had when upset , 'if you go out to Australia , I shall come too ! I could persuade aunt to go in a jiffey -- the doctor said she 'd be a million times better in a drier climate -- and we 'd take the farm next to yours", "context_len": 80}}}, "redder": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "Just at that moment there came a whoop and a spring , and Hughie , his red face redder than ever , his freckles more marked , his carroty hair sticking up all over his head , and his light-blue eyes wearing a most mischievous expression , entered the little arbor and sat down at one side of Irene", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "And so nine moons passed by , and Mahdrusa was still in her horrible captivity , and the brave that sought her was bowed down as if by years , with the weight of his sorrow , and his body was so steeped in the blood of the animals he had slain that he was redder than clay , and his descendants continue so to this very time", "context_len": 68}}}, "avenging": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was an awful look to see on the face of a child , -- an avenging , unforgiving , hateful look , -- the look of a grown person in cold , profound wrath", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "I could , of course , leap through a port into the sea , taking my chance of being picked up by the pursuing craft , which I fondly hoped would be the avenging Gannet ; but I did not know what distance separated us , and even then , in the eagerness of the chase , there was little likelihood of their noticing me , still less of heaving to and picking me up", "context_len": 75}}}, "unforgiving": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was an awful look to see on the face of a child , -- an avenging , unforgiving , hateful look , -- the look of a grown person in cold , profound wrath", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was not that she was unforgiving , but this meekness from Aunt Patty was something so new , and so contrary to all the ideas she had formed of her , that she did not know how to believe in it , or to understand it", "context_len": 47}}}, "regret": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 50, "context": "The prisoner strode up and down the limited area , wrestling with self , bending the will by prayer to submit to ignominy and pain , for he knew now that his father had abandoned him , and that he had to apprehend the worst ; still he did not regret the choice he had made , and he felt , as he prayed , peace and confidence descend like heavenly dew upon his soul", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 105, "context": "it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached L'Esperance , and the extraordinary story I had heard while aboard her", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "To get a bite was so much clear gain , and when they had wheedled one from the owner of the bread , they took as large a bite as their mouths could stretch to , and they had neither shame nor regret for their behavior , but mocked his just resentment", "context_len": 52}}}, "dispose": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 82, "context": "upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant , of course , that I was to die by some exquisite refinement of torture , the nature of which would probably be too dreadful for description", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "If , on the other hand , they should succeed in increasing their own forces so as to make head against Alexander , and finally to drive him away , then Bessus was to usurp the throne , and dispose of Darius by assassinating him , or imprisoning him for life in some remote and solitary castle", "context_len": 57}}}, "murderous": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 57, "context": "One morning in June I was called into our living-room , and found myself confronted by a gold-laced individual , who , drawing a paper from his pocket , read in a sonorous voice a summons for me to attend at the courthouse as a witness against Dick Swyre and Caleb Keeping , presented for committing a murderous attack upon divers of the king \u2019 s subjects on the highway", "context_len": 70}}}, "witches": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` By virtue of the act passed in the reign of His Majesty King James the First , of blessed memory , concerning the punishment of sorcerers , witches , warlocks , and the like", "context_len": 35}}}, "slain": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 8, "context": "All the beasts of the prairie had he slain in his terrible anger , and all the people had fled to the mountains for food , thither he thought he would follow them , and he sat down upon a ridge of sand , to strengthen his bow , and sharpen his arrows , when , lo ! quite unmindful of him , a thousand little creatures he had fancied too insignificant to notice , sprang forth from their holes , and gathered in groups for their daily gossip", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Oh , this was to have been my wedding day , my wedding day ! '' and he clasped his hands in agony ; then the thought of his master -- his slain lord -- returned , and he cried , `` O Edmund ! my master , my dear master , so good , so gentle , yet so brave ; who else could slay him ? what fiend else than Edric , the murderer Edric ? That they should think I , or any one else than Edric , could have done such a deed , such an evil deed ! ''", "context_len": 105}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The piercing cry , `` Help ! Edmund , the king , is slain ! '' roused the household -- Elfwyn , Herstan , Hermann , the ladies , agitated beyond measure ; the household guard ; and , last of all , Edric", "context_len": 44}}}, "speculated": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "His eyes might stare dully at the page , and his lips might keep repeating words printed there , but his thoughts persistently dwelt on the desperate strait into which he had fallen , and he speculated on the probable course that would be pursued by Lemuel Hayden", "context_len": 48}}}, "piled": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 31, "context": "These have ground and polished off every scrap of seaweed , every barnacle , limpet , and sea-anemone , leaving the rock all smooth and bare , while the boulders lie piled to the east in a heap , where the waves that try to take the rock in flank leap amongst them , and roll them over higher and higher , to come rumbling down as if they were tiny pebbles instead of rounded masses of granite and spar-veined stone a quarter , half , and a hundredweight each", "context_len": 90}}}, "coals": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 34, "context": "At last the weather became very cold , and being without a fire , his sufferings were great , until his ferocious gaoler , finding him quite stiffened , brought up a brazier of coals , which saved his prisoner 's life , while it filled the room with smoke , which could only escape by the crevices in walls and roof , for to open a window would have been as bad as to dispense with the fire , such was the state of the outer air", "context_len": 88}}}, "covers": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 47, "context": "The children , of whom there were more than a score of different ages , delighted to play hide and seek , chasing one another over the prairie and then tumbling into the rear of the vehicles , where their merry shouts were smothered by the canvas covers which hid them from sight", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 45, "context": "Somebody has put it onto paper , however , and very successfully , too ; so that I should advise you , in case a driving trip through the English Lake Country does not soon happen to come your way , to look between the covers of The Strange Adventures of a Phaeton , as soon as you grow a bit older , and see if you do not discover the charm of it for yourself", "context_len": 76}}}, "inscription": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 65, "context": "Whether the powers that rule the entrance to the same came to the conclusion that to a little girl of twelve and a little boy of four the term of general public could not honestly be applied , or whether all rules of procedure and precedence were magnanimously waived in their favor , certain it is that the little card in question bore the incredible inscription : Admit Master Albert Allyn and his little friend , Miss Marie-Celeste Harris , to the East Terrace between the hours of twelve and three on Thursday", "context_len": 93}}}, "mill": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 46, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}}}, "cane": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 53, "context": "It 's the truth , sir , and you 've only a minute , unless you want to see him but it was so very plain that Ted did n't want to see him , that Donald , who more fully took in the need for haste , pressed Ted 's hat and cane into his hand , and then throwing open one of the shutters of the back windows of his room , helped him to make the best possible time getting through it", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` I caned Keith in the High Street of Edinburgh for that he kicked a woman who by chance stood in his way , '' answered Dick , hotly ; `` and if I meet him in Fleet Street , I will cane him once more there", "context_len": 47}}}, "engraved": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "There was a wedding among them , and one of them , irritated at not having been invited , had a golden apple made , on which were engraved the words , `` TO BE GIVEN TO THE MOST BEAUTIFUL", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Was this God 's 'best ' for her ? The letter dropped from her hand , and she sat for hours motionless , her eyes taking in every detail of the pretty moonlit room , until it was indelibly engraved upon her memory", "context_len": 43}}}, "flattering": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "He was vaguely disappointed in the book , but he valued it for the teacher 's sake whom he was secretly very fond of , and who had no doubt won the child 's heart by some flattering notice", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "And the Keeper was delighted and said many very kind and flattering things to the Walrus , which pleased him more than I can tell you", "context_len": 26}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "Phyllis , who had thought herself unobserved , happened at that moment to look up , and met Peggy 's glance , which was certainly not a flattering one", "context_len": 29}}}, "tickled": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 11, "context": "He marched off , puffing up as his vain senses were tickled with the fancy that he was a born orator , and had just given utterance to some profoundly apt and clever sentiments", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Becket , and I 'm tickled to death to see you", "context_len": 11}}}, "vanity": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 113, "context": "him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought , of God , why did He not own us ? What were General Harrison 's dreams of a pure republic , but vanity ? Who can say if his dreams of heaven were any truer ? ''", "context_len": 128}}}, "transferred": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 39, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}}}, "furnace": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 64, "context": "The mansion was seated on a considerable elevation ; below them rolled the Isis ; across the river a couple of miles of flat meadow land lay between them and the Synodune hills , and beyond the lessening range of those hills , on the southeast , they looked , and behold the smoke of the country went up as the smoke of a furnace", "context_len": 65}}}, "stuffing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "'Come along , Sedgwick ; I believe we may be on the verge of a valuable discovery ! ' -- and stuffing his papers into his capacious pockets , the old gentleman started off with impatient strides , bearing his stout friend in his wake like a little tug towing a steamer", "context_len": 52}}}, "bellow": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 70, "context": "e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon express for one \u2019 s thoughts before one can express them at all ? Better have wit to cover ignorance , than silence to conceal sense", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "It was a splendid gun , but it would not stand cocked long , and he was resting it on the wall of the fort , ready to fire when the storming-party came on , throwing sods and yelling and holloing ; and all at once his gun went off , and a cow that was grazing broadside to the fort gave a frightened bellow , and put up her tail , and started for home", "context_len": 76}}}, "handles": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "Ana , with her hands on her broad hips , short and thick as a squat brown jug with its handles akimbo , -- Ana , with her great clay-colored face and tiny , glittering eyes , with her thick , pale lips and coarse , black hair , -- surely none but a mother could view in Ana such charms as bedewed Se\u00f1ora Vigil 's eyes only to think of !", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "With voice no louder than if he were talking across a dinner table , the master of the liner waved away the tugs that swarmed out to help him , and with flawless judgment turned the six hundred feet of vibrant steel hull almost in its own length and laid her alongside her pier as delicately as a fisherman handles a dory", "context_len": 62}}}, "eulogistic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "She was burning up to the cross-bar handles , she would soon reach that gold-plate inscription , and now for the first time he knew what those eulogistic words were to him -- he , a man who had had the temper of a maniac that had cut him off from the sympathy of every human being he knew", "context_len": 59}}}, "sympathy": {"freq": 113, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 104, "word_index": 80, "context": "Since then Christ feels for all the pains and struggles through which we go for his sake , since he can make allowance for all our weakness and failures ; and as he is so ready to give us help in our temptations , so much the more ought we who are not only tempted , but too apt , in spite of our best efforts , to fall into sin , to show to others all the kindness and sympathy we may at any time need for ourselves", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "Everyone had become so accustomed to claim his help and sympathy upon every occasion as a matter of course that it was only when he was gone they realized how much they valued him , for many of our blessings are hardly appreciated until we have lost them", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "He was always warding off death , or doing all that man could do to ward it off , and in many many cases death was afraid of him , and retired from his prey , vanquished by that knowledge , that genius , that sympathy , that love for humanity , which overflowed the little doctor 's personality", "context_len": 59}}}, "incoherent": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Tears ran down his cheeks -- in incoherent words he stammered an apology for killing her dog , and then she relented", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "All this while poor wayward Ted , whose name you must have guessed almost from the first , was lying wholly oblivious to everything about him , muttering now and then a few delirious , incoherent words , and yet by degrees subsiding into a gentle , regular breathing that the professional ear was quick to detect , and that was full of good omen for the waking in the morning", "context_len": 71}}}, "apology": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 27, "context": "Every corner of the Abbey had to be revisited to feel again the joy of possession ; all the animals must be petted as a kind of apology for having even thought of parting with them ; and Peggy felt as though she would like to tell the birds and the flowers that they were not going away after all , and give them a chance of joining in the general rejoicings", "context_len": 72}}}, "charred": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Throwing the charred and smoking tops to him , she shut up the stove , took her hat and tippet from a peg in the wall , and clasping Gippie to her , left the house without one glance at the old man as he sat in the smoky atmosphere mumbling to himself , and fumbling over the burnt pieces of wood as tenderly as if they had been babies", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "Without , upon the rocks , were the charred embers of a fire , and over them two crooked sticks , and they , with a battered tin pan , and numberless bones and feathers which lay scattered about , told that there the ill-gotten food was cooked and eaten", "context_len": 50}}}, "tops": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 63, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Padre has , or had , the upper bunk , and he tops the scale at sixteen stone", "context_len": 18}}}, "smoky": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "Throwing the charred and smoking tops to him , she shut up the stove , took her hat and tippet from a peg in the wall , and clasping Gippie to her , left the house without one glance at the old man as he sat in the smoky atmosphere mumbling to himself , and fumbling over the burnt pieces of wood as tenderly as if they had been babies", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "Among other things were two or three peacock feathers , a turkey wing , some pebbles from the brook where papa used to sail his boats when he was a boy , a piece of rusty tin pipe , which , because it looked black and smoky , and came from the field where the burnt barn had stood , they persuaded themselves must be a part of the very leader down which papa slid when he ran for the ladder to save his little brother , -- all these , and other treasures of like value , were carefully collected and stowed in the wagons , to be carried to the Lake House , and thence to the city", "context_len": 120}}}, "mumbling": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "Throwing the charred and smoking tops to him , she shut up the stove , took her hat and tippet from a peg in the wall , and clasping Gippie to her , left the house without one glance at the old man as he sat in the smoky atmosphere mumbling to himself , and fumbling over the burnt pieces of wood as tenderly as if they had been babies", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "I tried to thank her , but there is a gratitude that can not be expressed in words , and I could but stand before her mumbling with my head bent", "context_len": 31}}}, "fumbling": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 55, "context": "Throwing the charred and smoking tops to him , she shut up the stove , took her hat and tippet from a peg in the wall , and clasping Gippie to her , left the house without one glance at the old man as he sat in the smoky atmosphere mumbling to himself , and fumbling over the burnt pieces of wood as tenderly as if they had been babies", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` This is such a heavy string , '' said Edna fumbling at the knot", "context_len": 15}}}, "burnt": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 25, "context": "The corsairs , packed closely together , were unable to avoid them , and , as I told you , eleven of their ships were burnt , four were run ashore to avoid the flames , while six , trying to make their way out , were captured by the galley , aided by the three prizes that were taken and which the knights had caused to be manned by Sards", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 19, "context": "Had Ben set the fire at first along the whole line of the brush , there would have been burnt ground between him and the mass of fire , which would have cut off the communication , and he would have been safe ; but he set it on one corner , and when the wind shifted , the flame driven by it dried the moisture from the grass , and made rapid progress towards the house , while a large strip of dry grass made a bridge for the fire to travel on", "context_len": 94}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 55, "context": "Among other things were two or three peacock feathers , a turkey wing , some pebbles from the brook where papa used to sail his boats when he was a boy , a piece of rusty tin pipe , which , because it looked black and smoky , and came from the field where the burnt barn had stood , they persuaded themselves must be a part of the very leader down which papa slid when he ran for the ladder to save his little brother , -- all these , and other treasures of like value , were carefully collected and stowed in the wagons , to be carried to the Lake House , and thence to the city", "context_len": 120}}}, "caring": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 66, "context": "Seizing the first opportunity of slipping away from his schoolmates , he rushed upstairs to the dormitory with the design of throwing the note under Seabrooke 's bed or bureau , where it might be supposed to have fallen ; but seeing the trunk standing there ready packed , the impulse had taken him to put it in that , and without reflecting -- perhaps hardly caring -- that this would place Seabrooke in a still more embarrassing position , he thrust the note within , as Charlie Henderson saw , and fled from the room", "context_len": 96}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Bradford ; `` and when Dolly has gone you will be glad to think that you have pleased her by even such a small thing as caring for her plants", "context_len": 30}}}, "conquest": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "For some years the city was without bishops , owing to its insecure position during the strife between Wessex and Mercia , but later it appears as the seat of the great Mercian bishopric , retaining its jurisdiction until after the Norman conquest , when the see was transferred to Lincoln", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 16, "context": "As Philip had been made commander-in-chief of the Grecian armies which were about to undertake the conquest of Asia , and as , of course , his influence and power in all that related to that vast enterprise would be paramount and supreme ; and as all were ambitious to have a large share in the glory of that expedition , and to participate , as much as possible , in the power and in the renown which seemed to be at Philip 's disposal , all were , of course , very anxious to secure his favor", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "In a word , he had a noble character , though he devoted its energies unfortunately to conquest and war", "context_len": 20}}}, "fervently": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 42, "context": "There are few cruelties which public opinion in the boys ' world condemns , but I am glad to remember , to their honor , that there were not many in that Boy 's Town who would tie shirts ; and I fervently hope that there is no boy now living who would do it", "context_len": 55}}}, "haze": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 38, "context": "He ceased to be that eager comrade he had once been ; sometimes he left his book with a sigh ; and he saw much of the outer world through a veil of fancies quivering like an autumn haze between him and its realities , softening their harsh outlines , and giving them a fairy coloring", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 60, "context": "`` Right at the very start ! 'Baby ' Edwards was telling me the other day how way last year this fellow met with an accident -- fell off the express or something -- and while he was staying down at the Falls Baby and a couple of other fellows thought he was a candidate , and started in to haze him", "context_len": 62}}}, "blown": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 18, "context": "All being now ready , the gate in the palisade was thrown open , a conch- shell was blown , and the waiting inhabitants began to pour into the enclosure with all the eagerness and excitement of an audience crowding into the unreserved portions of a theatre , and in a very short time the great square was full , the front ranks pressing close up to a cordon of armed guards that had been drawn round the circle of posts", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Lately I have seen in a great museum in one of the large cities a copy of one of these colonies made out of blown glass", "context_len": 26}}}, "departing": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "But why , love , should we fear the act of departing ? How many , many , pass Jordan , as it were , dry-shod ? Remember how peacefully sweet Fanny sank to rest , -- dearest Mother , -- how my Letitia \u2019 s face was lighted up with a smile , -- how our Bible-woman at Batala sang aloud a happy hymn within a few hours of her going ! To me it seems such a simple thing for the -- I had almost said imprisoned soul , to leave its cottage of clay , -- for the bird , as soon as fledged , to spread its wings ! We are winged creatures , and it seems a humiliation to be creeping on earth", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "The girls , laughing and squealing , crawled out from the cushions to run after the departing figures , but these had already gone too far to be overtaken and they returned to watch them row off", "context_len": 37}}}, "astir": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "When Bomba and his companion reached the outskirts of the native village they found the inhabitants already astir", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Everybody was early astir in the cottage , and quite ready to forget the anxiety and excitement of the night in the doctor 's glad assurance that the young gentleman certainly was not done for", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "'What an inveterate bookworm you are , Paul , ' and Belle looked at the pile of volumes Pauline had brought from the library to study in the long morning hours which the force of a lifelong habit gave her , before the rest of the family were astir", "context_len": 49}}}, "score": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 68, "context": "My uncle busied himself till all traces of his worry lost themselves in his activity ; my aunt bustled about the house , driving the servants hither and thither , bringing linen from the huge oak chest , furbishing the massive furniture , and causing a huge supply of viands to be prepared for the entertainment of our guests -- for the invited company numbered close on a score , so that our house would be filled to overflowing , and rooms had to be engaged at `` The Bell Inn '' and `` The Blue Posts ''", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "When the men were hewing , he , with his narrow axe , could score in and beat off for them ( that is , cut notches in the timber , close together , and then split out the wood between ) , which very much facilitated the labor of hewing", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "For a time it seemed that Boxer would score , as , after getting possession of the ball by means of a forward pass , they ripped off twenty yards , and followed that up by gathering in ten more by a smashing play through center", "context_len": 46}}}, "abounded": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 32, "context": "Only in a few spots was there any appearance of cultivation , for who would sow when they knew not who should reap ? Not one lonely country house , such as abounded in the days of Edgar the Pacific , did they see standing , although they passed the blackened ruins of many an abode , showing where once the joys of home held sway", "context_len": 66}}}, "clerks": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 14, "context": "Every one knows the familiar look of Smith 's bookstalls , with their energetic clerks , and their armies of pushing newsboys , and perchance think they were born with the railways and have grown up with them", "context_len": 38}}}, "desks": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 35, "context": "As she did so she suddenly perceived with a quickening of her heart 's pulses that Kitty through an oversight had left the key in her desk ; all the other girls had locked their desks ; but Kitty , who was generally careful enough in this matter , had left the key in hers", "context_len": 55}}}, "counters": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "She also invented a geography game for us , providing us with skeleton maps , and small round counters , on which the names of towns were printed", "context_len": 28}}}, "offices": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 60, "context": "If his mother did her own work , with help only now and then from a hired girl , that was the custom of the time and country ; and her memory was always the more reverend to him , because whenever he looked back at her in those dim years , he saw her about some of those household offices which are so beautiful to a child", "context_len": 68}}}, "swarmed": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 54, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "She had scarcely unburdened herself of all her happenings , before in swarmed Uncle Bert and his family", "context_len": 18}}}, "villages": {"freq": 117, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 115, "word_index": 114, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "An interesting reference to this event is found in a letter of Charlotte Tucker \u2019 s , written February 15 , 1890 : As I went in my duli to villages this morning , I thought , One hundred and nineteen years ago a precious Baby was born in a distant island ; and I thanked God for our beloved and honoured Father", "context_len": 63}}}, "titles": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 14, "context": "In earlier days many warriors had several , even four or five of these titles ( tassik\u00e1ya inhotch\u00edfka ) , and when participants of a war party were present in numbers at the taking of a scalp , each of them obtained a war-title according to the report of the fight made by the pak\u0101 dsha on his return home", "context_len": 60}}}, "names": {"freq": 220, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 212, "word_index": 48, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "Never was a birdie coaxed with more pretty names than was this one during the next ten minutes ; and he seemed to like them well , for , after answering with one or two more half-timid `` cheeps , '' he broke into a soft trill , which soon swelled into a clear , sweet song of joy", "context_len": 59}}}, "exception": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 48, "context": "Mabel and Alice Cunningham were in soft pink dresses , a little paler in shade than the cherry-colored ribbons which as a matter of course they would wear , and one and all of the girls of the Upper school were becomingly and suitably dressed , with the exception of poor Florence ; but Florence 's muslin dress was coarse in texture and badly made , and notwithstanding the soft cherry-colored ribbons , she did not look her best", "context_len": 79}}}, "prevailed": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 9, "context": "For the space of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very", "context_len": 128}}}, "nomenclature": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "From a thorough comparative study of the Yuchi language , the Maskoki dialects and the local nomenclature of the country , we can alone expect any reliable information upon the extent and the area of territory anciently held by the Yuchi ; but at present it is safest to locate their `` priscan home '' upon both sides of Lower Savannah river", "context_len": 62}}}, "grocers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They had sold their farm produce to grocers and stable-keepers , and were now going to the post-office for their mail", "context_len": 21}}}, "assembled": {"freq": 80, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 87, "context": "As we entered the doorway of the `` Flying Bull '' -- an old hostelry that has sheltered all sorts and conditions of men , from kings and queens even to the arch-traitor Old Noll himself , and the sign of which , painted by a limner who had learned his art in the time of the last crusade , had swung in the breeze for nigh on four hundred years -- we were greeted with a chorus of welcome from the score or so of persons assembled in the large stone-flagged common room", "context_len": 94}, "UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 69, "context": "The party consisted solely of themselves and Archie , for Father 's suggestion of the five Miss Davenports was received with howls of indignation ; and Lilian and Nancy 's combined efforts had produced a cake with twelve tiny coloured wax candles stuck into icing-sugar on the top , one for every year of Peggy 's life , and which had to be blown out in turns by the assembled company for good luck", "context_len": 74}}}, "diligently": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "Assembled a few moments later in a corner of the gray stone building , and diligently reading letters and papers , they did not see a small figure approaching , and only looked up when a grave voice inquired , `` Air you too busy to speak to me a minute ? ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "spots": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 21, "context": "In places it was eight feet deep , with sand-bags on either side in addition , In others , in marshy spots , where the high-explosive shells had spitefully disturbed the tranquillity of meandering streams and carried the sluggish water to overflow and swamp the surrounding ground , the `` trench '' was above normal ground-level , with a lofty and broad wall of sand-bags to right and left", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Precious little of the sun did we see , and it was necessary to keep one 's eyes on the path which wound up and down into dark green hollows where beds of maidenhair fern flourished , or up to hilly spots where rock ferns grew in patches on enormous boulders", "context_len": 51}}}, "oldest": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 5, "context": "It was by far the oldest part of the house ; the great beams of the roof , roughly hewn out with an axe and black with age , had been a portion of the ancient castle , and so had the mullioned windows , with their deep , old-fashioned seats and diamond panes , filled with green , uneven glass", "context_len": 61}}}, "precocious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "They always brought with them to the town an uneasy consciousness that they might in some way be fooled , and 'Tilda Jane 's air was very precocious , very citified , compared with their air of rustic coltishness", "context_len": 39}}}, "citified": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "They always brought with them to the town an uneasy consciousness that they might in some way be fooled , and 'Tilda Jane 's air was very precocious , very citified , compared with their air of rustic coltishness", "context_len": 39}}}, "coltishness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "They always brought with them to the town an uneasy consciousness that they might in some way be fooled , and 'Tilda Jane 's air was very precocious , very citified , compared with their air of rustic coltishness", "context_len": 39}}}, "grieving": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 10, "context": "Enough of mourning ; for that , an I sat grieving a thousand years , what were the profit ? Alaeddin can not return from the tomb and I have considered and believe [ 601 ] that which thou saidst to me yesterday , to wit , that most like my father the Sultan hath slain him , in the excess of his grief for my loss", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Well , what does she do , the pretty creature , but just catch herself up in the midst of her grieving and say that bit of a prayer ? and then she rested quiet and waited", "context_len": 37}}}, "preacher": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 13, "context": "On a rickety platform at the end of the of the room a preacher in a Geneva gown was holding forth in the most violent language of the sect , and all around the grim listeners hung on his words with immovable attention , leaning on their pikes or holding their drawn swords across their knees", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Moffat in those days was not only teacher and preacher , but carpenter , smith , cooper , tailor , shoemaker , miller , baker and gardener !", "context_len": 28}}}, "wags": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Can you give us the name of the preacher that wags his tongue in the church on Molunkus Street ? ''", "context_len": 22}}}, "flock": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 125, "context": "It seemed to him that he had to wait an hour , and just when he was going to hollo , and tell his brother where the ducks were , the old smoothbore sent out a red flash and a white puff before he heard the report ; Tip tore loose from his grasp ; and he heard the splashing rise of the ducks , and the hurtling rush of their wings ; and he ran forward , yelling , `` How many did you hit ? Where are they ? Where are you ? Are they coming back ? It 's my turn now ! '' and making an outcry that would have frightened away a fleet of ironclads , but much less a flock of ducks", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Another cousin , the choke-cherry , is a tree filled with very bitter fruit but the birds like the choke-cherry trees almost as well as any other and flock about any they may find", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It is all very well to say that the knights from all parts of Europe would flock hither to defend it ; but the journey would be a long one , and would occupy much time , and they would probably not receive news that the Turks had sailed until the place was already invested", "context_len": 55}}}, "pursued": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 39, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}}}, "quicksilver": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Then I 'll tell her , '' said his quicksilver friend , and he flashed to 'Tilda Jane 's side", "context_len": 21}}}, "inveighing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Merriman and one or two other girls were inveighing against Rosamund 's extreme naughtiness in going to see the great lady without permission , Jane was envying her in her heart", "context_len": 31}}}, "selfishness": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 55, "context": "From Norfolk Island , in 1870 , he wrote to his sister when he was holding an ordination : `` At such times as these , when one is specially engaged in solemn work , there is much heart searching ; and I can not tell you how my conscience accuses me of such systematic selfishness during many long years -- I mean I see how I was all along making self the centre , and neglecting all kinds of duties -- social and others -- in consequence ''", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "If only at the outset some good little fairy might have held a magic mirror close to her defiant little mind , and she could have seen selfishness written large , right straight across all her motives , there perhaps need never have been this dark chapter in her reign", "context_len": 50}}}, "vices": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He 's always inveighing against luxury and selfishness and the other crowd of vices", "context_len": 14}}}, "stacks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Such beautiful dishes and jewelry , such stacks of books ; and , oh , such dolls holding out their hands with a pleading look in their eyes", "context_len": 28}}}, "peacock": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 7, "context": "Among other things were two or three peacock feathers , a turkey wing , some pebbles from the brook where papa used to sail his boats when he was a boy , a piece of rusty tin pipe , which , because it looked black and smoky , and came from the field where the burnt barn had stood , they persuaded themselves must be a part of the very leader down which papa slid when he ran for the ladder to save his little brother , -- all these , and other treasures of like value , were carefully collected and stowed in the wagons , to be carried to the Lake House , and thence to the city", "context_len": 120}}}, "hired": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 16, "context": "If his mother did her own work , with help only now and then from a hired girl , that was the custom of the time and country ; and her memory was always the more reverend to him , because whenever he looked back at her in those dim years , he saw her about some of those household offices which are so beautiful to a child", "context_len": 68}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 55, "context": "So he rose at once and equipping himself for travel , set out and traversed plains and deserts and mountains months and months , till he came to the land of China [ and entering ] the city of the sultanate , wherein was Alaeddin , repaired to the Strangers ' Khan , where he hired him a lodging and rested there a little", "context_len": 64}}}, "ruin": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 68, "context": "But when Gouroo whispered to my father , hinting at your incapacity , and suggesting that Mafuta should be sent for , my suspicions began to take definite shape , and , although I was not able to verify those suspicions , I finally made up my mind that the whole occurrence was the outcome of a plot between Gouroo and Mafuta -- your only enemies -- to ruin you", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 88, "context": "`` For hundreds upon hundreds of years the winds and tides have combined to rid themselves of this obstacle to their progress , the winds urging the waves that come rolling in from the vast Atlantic , gathering force as they increase in speed , like one rushing at a leap ; and at last leap they do , upon the great black mass of shale , tons upon tons in weight , seeming as if they would sweep it clear away , and rush on in mad ruin to tumble the fishing luggers together and shatter them like eggs as they lie softly rubbing together in the harbour", "context_len": 109}}}, "earthly": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 86, "context": "The Outram of the book is just the Outram who was the admiration of our girlhood , -- generous , chivalrous , noble ! One feels how much pain that fine spirit would have been saved , had he realised how little it really matters whether good service be appreciated or not by man , if the great Leader accept it , -- if all be done as to Him Who never overlooks or misunderstands ! To our own Master we stand or fall ; let earthly superiors say what they will", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It also means too often leaving all things earthly that have most been loved", "context_len": 14}}}, "prospects": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 90, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "heavenly": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 70, "context": "The prisoner strode up and down the limited area , wrestling with self , bending the will by prayer to submit to ignominy and pain , for he knew now that his father had abandoned him , and that he had to apprehend the worst ; still he did not regret the choice he had made , and he felt , as he prayed , peace and confidence descend like heavenly dew upon his soul", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "A very slight and fragile link it might seem , but it was doubtless very precious in the eyes of the heavenly Father , whose hands could make it strong and lasting , and fit to shine before him in the `` day when he shall make up his jewels", "context_len": 50}}}, "sidewalk": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 52, "context": "They would follow a boy to his door , and wait for him to come out ; and they would sometimes get him to go in and ask his mother for crullers or sugar-cakes ; when they came to see him they never went indoors for him , but stood on the sidewalk and called him with a peculiar cry , something like `` E-oo-we , e-oo-we ! '' and threw stones at trees , or anything , till he came out", "context_len": 82}}}, "vehicles": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 36, "context": "The children , of whom there were more than a score of different ages , delighted to play hide and seek , chasing one another over the prairie and then tumbling into the rear of the vehicles , where their merry shouts were smothered by the canvas covers which hid them from sight", "context_len": 53}}}, "correspondence": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 26, "context": "Some years before the end , when in severe illness she thought herself to be passing away , she spoke of the possibility that her long correspondence about Batala might be so employed , and earnestly hoped that , if it were so , no one-sided account should be given , but that shadow as well as sunshine , the dark as well as the bright aspect , should be frankly presented", "context_len": 72}}}, "tracy": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 0, "context": "Tracy , '' she said , at last , `` he 's most likely , '' and whirling on her heel , she flew down the path , out the gate , and into the street", "context_len": 36}}}, "terraces": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "Marie-Celeste looked away over the tops of the fine old trees that just reach to the terraces from the steep decline of the slopes below , way to the lovely meadows , and then turned to look up at the castle , leaning comfortably against the wall at her back", "context_len": 50}}}, "genteel": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "The house was large , because it was meant to shelter other people beside the Tracys and their children , but there was not a stick of `` genteel '' furniture in it , the new housemaid from Portland was just disdainfully observing to the cook", "context_len": 46}}}, "furniture": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 124, "context": "his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like , and be it complete with all its requisites of kingly and magnificent furniture and so forth", "context_len": 128}}}, "toss": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "At this the hard hearts relented altogether , and Harold rushed out and gave Albert a toss in the air that was very threatening to the eggs in the luncheon basket ; and as soon as he was on terra firma again Marie-Celeste gave him a good hard hug , and both begged his pardon half a dozen times over for ever assuming for a moment that he was too little , and intimated that they felt very small indeed themselves to think they had been so unfeeling as to plan not to include him in the expedition", "context_len": 98}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "When they had got them all heaped up like a pile of grape-shot at the foot of the tree , they began to hull them , with blows of a stick , or with stones , and to pick the nuts from the hulls , where the grubs were battening on their assured ripeness , and to toss them into a little heap , a very little heap indeed compared with the bulk of that they came from", "context_len": 78}}}, "unconquerable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Tracy was late with his breakfast this morning , because he had been out half the night before with a drunken young man who had showed an unconquerable aversion to returning home", "context_len": 32}}}, "assuring": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 55, "context": "Davenport that country lanes were unpleasant to drive along in the dark , so drawing on her gloves she routed her groom , a small , depressed-looking boy , out of the harness-room , where he was retailing his grievances to the awe-stricken Joe , and tucked her black skirts safely into her pony-carriage , assuring the children that it should not be long before she looked them up again , as she had promised their aunt to keep an eye on them after they were left alone", "context_len": 88}}}, "clergyman": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 69, "context": "But if our Church is to go on growing , as we hope and pray that it may , what would be the advantage of having a tiny chapel , which would not comfortably accommodate ourselves in a fiery climate , and in which there would be no room at all for heathen spectators ? We should be wanting a second ; and how could we procure a second clergyman ? Please thank dear Fred very , very much for his kindness in collecting , and assure him that we wish to make the money go as far as possible", "context_len": 100}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The cream of the story is the clergyman \u2019 s reply", "context_len": 11}}}, "sorrowful": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 54, "context": "His father used to say that the taste for poetry was an acquired taste , like the taste for tomatoes , and that he would come to it yet ; but he never came to it , or so much of it as some people seemed to do , and he always had his sorrowful misgivings as to whether they liked it as much as they pretended", "context_len": 67}}}, "ache": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 29, "context": "She was above the clock , which was so far up in the air , against the summer sky which was always blue , that it made your neck ache to look up at it ; and the bell was so large that once when my boy was a very little fellow , and was in the belfry with his brother , to see if they could get some of the pigeons that nested there , and the clock began to strike , it almost smote him dead with the terror of its sound , and he felt his heart quiver with the vibration of the air between the strokes", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 83, "context": "The sandals were anything but comfortable , and her feet hurt dreadfully on the stones , while every gorse-bush she passed seemed to be stretching out spiky fingers to scratch her bare legs ; she was tired after her morning 's adventures on the moors , and the eight miles seemed to lengthen out to an interminable vista , in spite of the way being downhill ; sundry bumps and bruises , which she had never noticed at the time , began to ache now , and the bee-stings on her neck smarted , until she hardly knew how to bear the pain", "context_len": 103}}}, "canaries": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 65, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "cages": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 52, "context": "He was no nearer beauty than she was , but he had a good face , and -- let her rejoice for this -- he was fond of animals , for on the hearth lay a cat and a dog asleep side by side , in the long windows hung canaries in cages , and on a luxuriant and beautiful rose-bush , growing in a big pot drawn up to the table , sat a green and very self-possessed parrot", "context_len": 80}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Oh , look at this queer Animal ! See what he does ! \u2019 But then they would pass on and in front of the Monkey cages they stood", "context_len": 29}}}, "luxuriant": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "He was no nearer beauty than she was , but he had a good face , and -- let her rejoice for this -- he was fond of animals , for on the hearth lay a cat and a dog asleep side by side , in the long windows hung canaries in cages , and on a luxuriant and beautiful rose-bush , growing in a big pot drawn up to the table , sat a green and very self-possessed parrot", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` On the very summit of yonder peak , which seems to rise at least a thousand feet above its neighbors , and where the sun shone the warmest , the grains and fruits were most luxuriant , and the deer larger and tamer than in any other place , lived an old man , the chief of all the tribes that lived between the mountains and the great ocean in the far west", "context_len": 74}}}, "pot": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 66, "context": "He was no nearer beauty than she was , but he had a good face , and -- let her rejoice for this -- he was fond of animals , for on the hearth lay a cat and a dog asleep side by side , in the long windows hung canaries in cages , and on a luxuriant and beautiful rose-bush , growing in a big pot drawn up to the table , sat a green and very self-possessed parrot", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Cracker , be ready to strafe any antiaircraft before they can pot us", "context_len": 13}}}, "screeching": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "She was not screeching , she was not tearing at the leaves , she sat meekly and thankfully receiving from time to time such morsels as her master chose to hand her", "context_len": 32}}}, "chose": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "As I don \u2019 t think you will be able to guess right away and as I want to tell you what really happened , I will tell you the place Blackie the cat chose for his nap one Sunday", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 64, "context": "When the winter came on , Alexander and his army were about three or four hundred miles from home ; and , as he did not intend to advance much farther until the spring should open , he announced to the army that all those persons , both officers and soldiers who had been married within the year , might go home if they chose , and spend the winter with their brides , and return to the army in the spring", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "The boys ' gardens had suffered some , but not so much as those of the little girls ; whether it was that Dolly fancied Maggie and Bessie had been the most to blame for Lem 's imprisonment , and so chose first to revenge herself on them ; whether it was that their gardens lay nearer to her hand and she had been interrupted in her wicked work before she had quite destroyed the boys ' , -- could not be known", "context_len": 83}}}, "barely": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 61, "context": "Schwitz had promised that his charges should be small , but how he never would give in his bill , the policeman and his wife thinking all the while that it was kindness which kept him from doing so ; how it had taken every cent of his salary to pay the other expenses of illness , and keep the family barely warmed and fed ; of the disappointment of their hopes for Willie for , at least , some time to come ; and finally of the terrible bill which Dr", "context_len": 92}}}, "disturbing": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 7, "context": "Climbing out with some difficulty , and disturbing in the process hundreds of bat-mice and not a few rats , they found themselves in the midst of some old ruins at the foot of the acclivity whereon the fortress was built , and below them the brook ran rapidly to join the river", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Altogether it was very disturbing , especially to a man who did not understand what he had done to bring about so unpleasant a turn", "context_len": 25}}}, "mechanically": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 24, "context": "The solemn pealing of the bell for the mass at the hour of daybreak fell upon Alfgar 's ears , and he turned almost mechanically to the cathedral , yet with vague desire to communicate all his griefs and troubles to a higher power than that of man , and to seek aid from a diviner source", "context_len": 57}}}, "glide": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "When it was beginning he appeared to glide down the stairs at school without touching the steps with his feet , and afterwards his chief trouble was in not knowing , when he slept , whether he had really been asleep or not", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 85, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}}}, "fainting": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Father ; Prince ; I live to warn you -- the Danes , the Danes ! '' and he sank fainting into the arms of Herstan", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "For a few dreadful moments he was unheard , and unseen , then a shout of joy , answered his screams , and the horseman galloped rapidly toward him , and in a few minutes the poor boy lay fainting , but saved , in the arms of James Graham !", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 78, "context": ", for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed , with feelings of envy , that many of my fellow-sufferers had already succumbed and become unconscious , if indeed they were not dead", "context_len": 128}}}, "uplifted": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` You may carry them to the loftiest peaks of the Cordilleras ; they may float in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war ,", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 14, "context": "His sword had already dealt with two of the foe , and was again uplifted , when a musket shot , fired at close quarters , severed an artery ; and the work on earth of this gallant man was over", "context_len": 41}}}, "grating": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "They liked to hear the sound of the wheels grating through the sand , and to watch how they took it up and threw it off again as they moved round and round", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Then he leaped up , stumbled against his chair , seized it , felt his way in the darkness to the door , placed the chair and mounted it , till , grasping the iron bars above , he could peer out through the grating", "context_len": 45}}}, "amiability": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Cleverness was used in the Virginia sense for amiability ; a person who was clever in the English sense was smart", "context_len": 21}}}, "unmusical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` It 's a pretty world , darlin ' , '' responded the parrot , bursting into hoarse , unmusical laughter at her own addition", "context_len": 25}}}, "cynical": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 54, "context": "But the face , overshadowed by the heavy curls of a fashionable periwig , was worn and roughened by exposure and hardship , and the weary gloom that darkened the noble forehead and drooped the haughty lips marked the years of disappointment that had changed the fiery paladin of 1642 into the sad and cynical Rupert of the Restoration", "context_len": 59}}}, "afflicting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "To the gentleman and his wife there was something cynical and afflicting in the bird 's comment on mundane affairs , and they surreptitiously examined their visitor", "context_len": 27}}}, "comment": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 18, "context": "Just what he said to the boys , Edna never knew , but it is a matter of comment that from that day on there were no more tricks played on old Nathan Keener , and though the big stick was not so much in evidence , it was a long time before any of the Elderflowers made any headway in winning even so much as a grunt from him", "context_len": 70}}}, "mundane": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "To the gentleman and his wife there was something cynical and afflicting in the bird 's comment on mundane affairs , and they surreptitiously examined their visitor", "context_len": 27}}}, "surreptitiously": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "He was absent on the third of these little excursions of his , and was surreptitiously amusing his audacious little self by seeing how it seemed to sit in the Oueen 's own stall , when hark ! -- yes , that was going to be The Roseate Hues , and with a bound that came near bringing the royal draperies with him he was out of the stall in a trice and fairly scrambling up the organ stairs", "context_len": 79}}}, "haunting": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "The thoughts of his father 's plight , a haunting dread that Colonel Harrington might make him some trouble , and the uncertainty of continued work in the express service , all combined to depress his mind with anxiety and suspense , and he tried to dismiss the themes by whistling a quiet , soothing tune as he started to get the hammer to put the padlock in place", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "The little black cow with the long horns looked strange to eyes accustomed to the large red and white cows at the Abbey , but her milk seemed sweet and good , and the woman sang a song in Welsh while she milked it , to a strange , haunting kind of melody that , like most of the Celtic music , had a touch of sadness and pathos about it", "context_len": 71}}}, "lip": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 39, "context": "They had seen him off at the station -- Harold and she -- and Donald , never allowing himself for a moment to regard this whole affair in any light but the true one , kept a stiff upper lip to the last , and smiled the cheeriest good-by as the guard banged the carriage-door and the train glided out from the depot", "context_len": 63}}}, "pleasures": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 93, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}}}, "palaces": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 72, "context": "They had stories to tell him about the famous hanging gardens , which were artificially constructed in the most magnificent manner , on arches raised high in the air ; and about a vine made of gold , with all sorts of precious stones upon it instead of fruit , which was wrought as an ornament over the throne on which the King of Persia often gave audience ; of the splendid palaces and vast cities of the Persians ; and the banquets , and f\u00eates , and magnificent entertainments and celebrations which they used to have there", "context_len": 98}}}, "roam": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 38, "context": "The painful freeing of his right hand , the fear that in the fall he might have sustained an injury that would cripple him and leave him a prey to the first beast of the jungle that might roam that way , the dread that he might never be able to free himself from those ruthless , imprisoning branches had blinded him to another and more imminent peril that threatened", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Victor has all the advantages that money can command , and a brilliant prospect for the future if he will only act in accordance with my wishes , and yet he is ready to start off at a tangent and roam round the world with some scapegrace companion", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Tom Parsons stretched his legs out a little farther , and let his gaze once more roam about the room", "context_len": 20}}}, "humble": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 61, "context": "It was necessary to keep the house very still , on account of Aunt Bessie , who was so very nervous that the least sound disturbed her ; and roguish , noisy Frankie was , by Aunt Patty 's earnest request , allowed to go to her house , where , for a few days , he lorded it over that humble servant of his to his heart 's content", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I want to humble his pride , '' answered Sam , with a gleam of something like hatred in his eyes", "context_len": 22}}}, "claw": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "So the next time the crab tried to come out of the pool , Uncle John put the stick by his claw , and when he took hold of it , lifted him out of the water and laid him on the sand", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Seven times did we \u2019 bout ship to try and claw off the shore , but at daybreak we struck close to Anvil Point", "context_len": 24}}}, "rueful": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "The bird 's rueful expression as she fell , her ridiculous one as she gathered herself up , and with a surprised `` Oh , dear ! '' climbed back to her perch , were so overcoming that the minister and his wife burst into hearty laughter", "context_len": 47}}}, "overcoming": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "They were in the very midst of one of Archie 's most comical adventures , when a shout was heard underneath the tree , and going out on to the veranda , they beheld Nancy struggling timorously up the staircase , her evident anxiety to make some communication overcoming her natural abhorrence of such an airy structure", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He was used to meeting and overcoming danger", "context_len": 8}}}, "crease": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "She looked interested , and a very faint crease of amusement came in a little fold about her lips , but at once faded away", "context_len": 25}}}, "expectantly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "There was a bend in the road about a quarter of a mile ahead of us , and we waited expectantly , while Whistling Jim , with a cunning for which I did not give him credit , pretended to be fixing his saddle-girth", "context_len": 44}}}, "fatherly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Ever since the adventure with the pirates , the knight had exhibited an almost fatherly interest in him ; had encouraged him in his studies , ridden with him on such occasions as he had permitted himself a short holiday , and had , whenever they were together , related to him stories of war , sieges , battles , and escapes , from which he thought the young knight might gain lessons for his future guidance", "context_len": 77}}}, "curtain": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Uncle John had a picture paper the other day , and in it was a picture of a woman coming in at the door , and she had her hands up so , and she looked as frightened , as frightened , and a man was standing behind the curtain doing so , and under the picture was 'Beware , woman ! ' I asked Uncle John what it meant , and he said that was a wicked woman who was going to steal some papers so she could get some money , and when she came in , she heard somebody say , 'Beware , woman , ' and she was so frightened she ran away and was never seen again", "context_len": 123}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "A ray of sunshine sped into the wagon as some hand outside withdrew the rear curtain a little", "context_len": 18}}}, "confounded": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 64, "context": "When she raised the veil from her face and Alaeddin saw her , he said , `` Verily , her fashion glorifieth the Great Creator and extolled be the perfection of Him who made her and graced her with this beauty and goodliness ! '' And his back was cloven in sunder , [ 321 ] when he saw her ; his thought was confounded and his understanding [ 322 ] dazed and the love of her gat hold upon his whole heart ; so he turned back and returning home , went in to his mother , like one distraught", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Just think of it ! After I had soaked the confounded plebe for fifty and more , ho ! ho ! they got suspicious up at headquarters and transferred me , and ho ! ho ! put M-m-merry Vance on instead , and he , ho ! ho ! soaked him all the harder ! ''", "context_len": 57}}}, "capture": {"freq": 98, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 49, "context": "Should the worst happen , and you are captured by pirates , you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 28, "context": "Many will , we may calculate , jump overboard and swim ashore rather than face us , and we shall be able , at any rate , to capture three or four of their craft , for , as they come out , one by one , we can all close round them ; and with nearly fifty knights , ninety released captives , burning for vengeance , and some fifty or sixty fishermen , for those from the fire ships will , of course , join us -- we shall make short work of them , and may even hope to entirely destroy their fleet", "context_len": 106}}}, "trapped": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 22, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "But he must die here alone like any trapped beast of the jungle", "context_len": 13}}}, "exhaustion": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 29, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "croak": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Go on , '' said the parrot , hoarsely , and between intervals of climbing by means of bill and claw to the top of a chair , `` go on , and do n't croak", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` She is nothing but an old croak", "context_len": 8}}}, "sluggish": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 52, "context": "It had a river , the great Miami River , which was as blue as the sky when it was not as yellow as gold ; and it had another river , called the Old River , which was the Miami 's former channel , and which held an island in its sluggish loop ; the boys called it The Island ; and it must have been about the size of Australia ; perhaps it was not so large", "context_len": 79}}}, "unhappiness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "How weary , how unspeakably weary she was ! If she could only lie down again -- and what was the matter with her ? Why had she waked with that terrible feeling of unhappiness ?", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "And the prince had much discourse with Major Harrison , for so your uncle was then , and when he bade him farewell he gave him a gallant compliment , saying he never received such satisfaction in such unhappiness , and that if ever it were in his power he would repay it", "context_len": 53}}}, "indulged": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 98, "context": "We are all just now in a state of indignation about your pork ! Don \u2019 t suppose that it is any fault in the pork ; on the contrary , it is acknowledged to be the most refined pork ever known ; and Mother says that if she shut her eyes , she would not know that she was not eating chicken ! ! We had a beautiful roast of it one day at luncheon ; and Mother cut off a choice bit , to be reserved for our table , cold , while the servants were indulged with the rest of that joint", "context_len": 105}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I had heard from Miss Forster that that very indulged and unmanageable nephew of hers had been making some sort of place in a tree at the Abbey , but I never imagined so much as this", "context_len": 37}}}, "lamentation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "For the same reason he did his best , or his worst , to fall into a trance , in which he should know everything that was going on about him , all the preparations for his funeral , all the sorrow and lamentation , but should be unable to move or speak , and only be saved at the last moment by some one putting a mirror to his lips and finding a little blur of mist on it", "context_len": 80}}}, "untimely": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "He was asked to be one of the boys who held the banner-tassels , and he felt this a great compliment somehow , though he was so young that he had afterwards only the vaguest remembrance of marching in the procession , and going to a raw and chilly grove somewhere , and having untimely lemonade and cake", "context_len": 58}}}, "recalled": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 53, "context": "Altogether the vehicle had a lordly air , easily matching that of the individual sitting alone on the purple cushions -- a man whose features were not very clear at the distance , although the yellowness of his beard , the glitter of his studded shirt-front , and whole consequential , expansive effect recalled to the doctor 's mind an image of the past , less ornate , indeed , and affluent , but of similar aspect", "context_len": 77}}}, "consult": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was not his habit to consult any of his children , not even Maud , whom he relied on almost as he had relied on his wife ; but he went straight over that very evening to the Merrimans ' house ; and although he could not go inside for fear of infection , he had a conversation with the Professor in the garden", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "But no ; on the whole , he thought he would wait and once more consult Harry , and , besides , he was really too tired to enter upon any explanations just then", "context_len": 34}}}, "encouraged": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 32, "context": "Then , instead of attempting to restrain him , and worrying and checking him by useless efforts to hold him in , he gave him the rein freely , and animated and encouraged him with his voice , so that the horse flew across the plains at the top of his speed , the king and the courtiers looking on , at first with fear and trembling , but soon afterward with feelings of the greatest admiration and pleasure", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Noel Brooke had taken stock of his long-limbed adversary , and the result was that he felt encouraged", "context_len": 18}}}, "gratified": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 47, "context": "I ask for no earthly remuneration ; my position in life renders me independent of any exertions of my own ; I pray but for God \u2019 s blessing upon my attempts to instruct His lambs in the things which concern their everlasting welfare ; and deeply gratified should I feel , were my little work to be classed among the numerous valuable publications which you have already given to the world", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 62, "context": "So now in a few words he praised them for their earnest and faithful efforts ; said that he had been treated to a perusal of many of the compositions written during the last term in order that he might himself have an opportunity of judging whether Miss Ashton 's verdict were just , and that he had been both surprised and gratified to observe the improvement made by almost every member of the class", "context_len": 75}}}, "institutions": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then as soon as it is ready all the institutions for children in London are to be told about it , and whenever a little girl comes along who seems to be too fine , in the best sense of the word , for the life of the ordinary institution , down she is to go to Cranford , to be cared for in the Home ; and it is to be a home , Harold , prettily furnished , with rooms for ten children , and everything as dainty as can be", "context_len": 93}}}, "strictly": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 24, "context": "Instructions were therefore given to the two knights that , while Georges was to be treated with all courtesy , he was to be strictly watched , though in such a manner that he should be in ignorance of it , and that , whenever he turned his steps in the direction of those parts of the defences where fresh works had been recently added and preparations made of which it was desirable the Turks should be kept in ignorance , he was to be met , as if by accident , by one of the knights told off for the purpose , and his steps diverted in another direction", "context_len": 110}}}, "uneducated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "He did not tell her that he had heard it was one of the few badly managed institutions for orphans in the State , that the children were kept strictly , fed poorly , and were rapidly `` institutionalised '' while under the care of uneducated , ignorant women , who were only partially supervised by a vacillating board of lady managers", "context_len": 62}}}, "supervised": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "He did not tell her that he had heard it was one of the few badly managed institutions for orphans in the State , that the children were kept strictly , fed poorly , and were rapidly `` institutionalised '' while under the care of uneducated , ignorant women , who were only partially supervised by a vacillating board of lady managers", "context_len": 62}}}, "vacillating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "He did not tell her that he had heard it was one of the few badly managed institutions for orphans in the State , that the children were kept strictly , fed poorly , and were rapidly `` institutionalised '' while under the care of uneducated , ignorant women , who were only partially supervised by a vacillating board of lady managers", "context_len": 62}}}, "encouragingly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 47, "context": "Far from destroying the man , the discharge of her ordnance had proved his salvation ; so our captain resolved to act likewise and plant shot after shot close to him , so as to frighten off these tigers of the deep , while our men waved encouragingly to the swimmer", "context_len": 51}}}, "entire": {"freq": 75, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 75, "word_index": 35, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}}}, "wanderings": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 104, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}}}, "absent": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 79, "context": "`` O yes , my father is keeping high festival ; a number of his countrymen are visiting him and holding revel ; this morning they drove in a number of oxen , I know not whence , and slaughtered two on the spot , and they have broached several barrels of mead ; they will keep the feast all day , and before night my father will not be in a state to miss me ; I always absent myself if I can on such occasions", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` I grieve to say , '' he continued , `` that there is but one upon whom our suspicions can now fall with any shadow of probability -- one who is now absent , for I thought it well not to summon him to this council ; and before naming him , I must recall to you , Elfwyn , and to you , Herstan , the solemn oath we have all three taken to disregard all appeals of natural affection , and to ascertain the truth , God being our helper", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 2, "context": "He was absent on the third of these little excursions of his , and was surreptitiously amusing his audacious little self by seeing how it seemed to sit in the Oueen 's own stall , when hark ! -- yes , that was going to be The Roseate Hues , and with a bound that came near bringing the royal draperies with him he was out of the stall in a trice and fairly scrambling up the organ stairs", "context_len": 79}}}, "sojourn": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}}}, "brokenly": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` The next of whom they inquired the way spoke brokenly , but said he would put them on the right track , and under his guidance they managed to reach the church , and here they met a man in clerical dress who looked down at them with a smile", "context_len": 51}}}, "murder": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 4, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 25, "context": "As part of the plan , they were going to surprise Windsor Castle ; and Huntington , if you will believe it , hoped to murder the four sons of Henry with his own hand ; and they did march on Windsor Castle , but not before Henry and his sons had heard of the dreadful plan and ridden safely away", "context_len": 61}}}, "chip": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 97, "context": "would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart of your enemy", "context_len": 128}}}, "gazes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "I took them crutches as he ca n't move without , an ' I burnt 'em most up -- all but a little bit at the top with the gold writin ' , 'cause he sits an ' gazes at it , an ' I guess sets store by it", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "And much the same thought is reproduced in Charlotte Tucker \u2019 s own clever and amusing little book , My Neighbour \u2019 s Shoes , -- when , as Archie gazes into the mirror , he says of himself , One thing is evident ; as I can \u2019 t be admired for my beauty , I must make myself liked in some other way", "context_len": 65}}}, "sets": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 1, "context": "Two sets of mail bags were approaching each other from points two thousand miles apart , and there were times when this approach was at the astounding speed of forty and even fifty miles an hour ! The average daily rate was two hundred and fifty miles a day , but where everything was favorable , or when an express rider was fleeing from the vengeful red men , his pony struck a gait of twenty-five miles and maintained it , when an untrained horse would have dropped in his tracks", "context_len": 91}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` I do n't cheat in my lessons , and he knows I do n't , whatever Clara may say , and I 'm not the one who sets the girls up to mischief , you know I 'm not", "context_len": 40}}}, "hoarsely": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 34, "context": "Afterwards , the boy remembered nothing of the story , but a scrap of the dialogue meaninglessly remained with him ; and when the pirate captain appeared with his bloody crew and said , hoarsely , `` Let us go below and get some brandy ! '' the boy would have bartered all his hopes of bliss to have been that abandoned ruffian", "context_len": 63}}}, "obey": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 88, "context": "I must cure the king , or it would be so much the worse for me ! And how was I to cure him ? My knowledge of disease was of the slightest and most amateurish kind , and , for aught that I could tell to the contrary , might not even be sufficient to enable me to diagnose the case correctly , much less to treat it successfully ! However , there was no use in meeting trouble half-way ; the only possible course was to obey the summons forthwith , and do my best , leaving the result in the hands of Providence", "context_len": 106}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "He would make her obey him , but he would make her love him too", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "In all that touches not my faith and duty as a Christian , I am bound to love , honour , and obey you", "context_len": 24}}}, "astray": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 80, "context": "What could be easier than to take the money of thy father ? I tell you , Lolita , that you can not go to school because Mees Combs has had to use your money to pay them ! Eh , but your father will be mad ! He is not working himself to a bone that strangers should build themselves fine houses ! My Pablo said a little time ago that people said your father 's riches were going astray", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "But there were a few bunglers among the crowd , for some of the shots went so far astray that instead of hitting the mark at which they were supposed to be aimed , they hit and slew some half-a-dozen of the crucified ones", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "When it is so dark that I can hardly see his ears , I let the lines lie loose , and he never goes astray ; I wouldn \u2019 t trade my job for that of President of the United States", "context_len": 41}}}, "intensely": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 63, "context": "`` Oh , as far as any use that there is in it , I have long ago got it out of my head , '' replied Jane ; `` but the thing to be considered is this , that he has not got it out of his head -- nor has his mother -- and that between them they can make things intensely disagreeable", "context_len": 65}}}, "absently": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Her black eyes were bent absently on the parrot who had fallen prey to an immense curiosity with regard to Gippie , and having surveyed him from the back of the chair and the mantel , and finding him harmless , was now walking cautiously around him as he lay on the hearth-rug", "context_len": 53}}}, "immense": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 50, "context": "She referred him , with her own full consent , to her father ; and Elfwyn says , not unwisely , that he can not consent until the land is at peace ; that it is currently reported that Thurkill , a Danish earl , is at hand with an immense fleet , and that to marry might both hamper a warrior 's hands and be the means of bringing up children for the sword", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "For a hundred yards or more the trail led over uneven rocky ground toward an immense rock , upon rounding which I found myself face to face with , and within half-a-dozen yards of , a splendid full-grown male leopard who was crouching over poor Ama 's motionless body , snarling savagely as he strove with his claws to remove a broken spear , the head of which was buried deep in his neck", "context_len": 74}}}, "harmless": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 95, "context": "But she did not wish to vex their kind uncle , who was a very learned man , and was always thinking of collections and museums , and science and experiments ; and she did not like to tell him that she would rather her little ones should learn about butterflies from the book with its beautiful pictures , and from watching them flying about , and settling on the flowers in the fields ; and that she did not think it could be a nice play for children to catch and kill the pretty harmless creatures", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 79, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}}}, "emboldened": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Once more she glanced at the fort Hal seemed to have gone to sleep , and emboldened by that thought she rose to her feet for a swift , silent rush to the willows", "context_len": 34}}}, "beak": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "When the fire had entirely died down , and I had satisfied myself that there was nothing left of poor Ama to be desecrated by fang of beast or beak of bird , I sorrowfully retired from the fatal spot , carrying the leopard 's skin with me , and making my way with some difficulty to the place where the canoe lay concealed , sprang in and shoved off", "context_len": 70}}}, "pinched": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 107, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}}}, "discomfited": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "There was a squeal , a rush , and a discomfited parrot minus three tail feathers flying to her master 's shoulder", "context_len": 22}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "Noel Brooke skilfully evaded the close embrace which would have settled the fight then and there in favor of Ben , and skipping , first to one side , then to the other , rained in a shower of blows upon Ben , one of which took effect in his jaw , and drove him staggering back discomfited", "context_len": 58}}}, "minus": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "There was a squeal , a rush , and a discomfited parrot minus three tail feathers flying to her master 's shoulder", "context_len": 22}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "There was Charley Tuttle , called Chub for short , a roly-poly , round-faced , laughing chap , who was munching peanuts ; Tim Davis , nicknamed Spotty , even more freckled than Ben , thin-legged , sly-faced , and minus the two front teeth of his upper jaw ; Sam Rollins , a big , hulking , low-browed fellow , who lost no opportunity to bully smaller boys , generally known as Hunk ; Berlin Barker , a cold blond , rather good-looking , but proud and distant in his bearing ; and others who did not impress the new boy at all with their personalities", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "We simply broke open the lumps of baked clay with our spears and took out the birds -- minus the skin and feathers , which adhered to the clay -- and , splitting them open , removed the interior organs and devoured the flesh , which I found done to a turn , and particularly rich and juicy in flavour", "context_len": 60}}}, "mistress": {"freq": 83, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 82, "word_index": 96, "context": "Their voices were subdued lest they should reach the king 's ears ; but , respectful as was the bearing of all the members of the group by the fire , they clearly split into two halves : on the one hand , the officers of the escort who were teasing the boy , and on the other , a group of gentlemen , some wearing the conventional ribbons and laces of a cavalier , others in the rough cloth of country wear , stained with the mud of country lanes , while the master and mistress of the house moved from one guest to another , evidently nervous at the doubtful honour that such a royal visit had brought to their roof", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was the last match in the house too , and it made me laugh till I ached to hear how mistress and maid groaned over my fun", "context_len": 28}}}, "propose": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` Bradford , '' he continued , in the most mournful tones , `` since we are not to go over to the policeman 's this afternoon , I had thought we might have some other little frolic ; but of course , none of us are in spirits for the visit to the menagerie I had intended to propose", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "I don \u2019 t propose to take sides at all unless compelled to do so", "context_len": 15}}}, "oyster": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "How did you stand all the racket this morning ? If a noisy noise annoys an oyster , how much of a noisy noise does it take to annoy Pinky Blooms ? That sounds like a problem in mental arithmetic , but it is n't", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "As soon as he was sure that the street was unfamiliar he slowed his pace , looked around him , and not wishing to enter a saloon , went over to a gaudily placarded `` oyster house", "context_len": 37}}}, "animation": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 17, "context": "His armies had been broken up , his courage and hope had gradually failed , while the animation and hope of the pursuer had been gathering fresh and increasing strength from his successes , and were excited to wild enthusiasm now , as the hour for the final consummation of all his desires seemed to be drawing nigh", "context_len": 58}}}, "tense": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "I guess I 'll find some nice home for him with somebody as likes animiles , '' and a shrewd and melancholy smile hovered about her tense lips as she gazed at her host and hostess", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 56, "context": "From all this it becomes evident , that the Creek verb surpasses in its large power of polysynthesis the Algonkin , Dakota and Kalapuya verb , and in the richness of its forms approaches closely to the Iroquois verb , which is poorer in tenses , but has an impersonal conjugation and fourteen persons to each tense of the finite verb", "context_len": 61}}}, "caressing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "This was a compliment for me , so I licked a bit of this nice man 's shoulder in a caressing way , and he turned and gazed deep into what he called my `` soft and soulful '' eyes", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "He must rather have soothed against his soft , caressing ignorance the ache of his fantastic spirit , and reposed his intensity of purpose in that lax and easy aimlessness", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Yes , Jesus knows , '' said Bessie , putting up her small fingers with a little caressing touch to Aunt Patty 's cheek ; `` and is it not sweet and precious , Aunt Patty , to think he had temptations too , and so can know just how hard we have to try not to grieve him ? Mrs", "context_len": 62}}}, "embracing": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Please do n't do that , ma'am , '' said the little girl , vehemently , and slipping her shoulder from under the embracing hand", "context_len": 26}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 44, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}}}, "homey": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Please do n't do anything homey to me", "context_len": 9}}}, "clenching": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Oh , I want a home , '' cried the little girl , clenching her hands , and raising her face to the ceiling", "context_len": 25}}}, "ceiling": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 40, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}}}, "curiosities": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 72, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "nervously": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 47, "context": "He cast his eyes critically toward the sun , hesitated for another minute , and then , shouldering his hoe , started down the hill toward the lake ; but before he had gone half-way to the water \u2019 s edge he stopped and stood still , nervously chewing a spear of June-grass , and glancing alternately back at the cornfield and forward to the tempting waters of the lake", "context_len": 70}}}, "exaltation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "She was in a strange state of nervous exaltation that was the natural reaction from her terrible dejection of the evening before", "context_len": 22}}}, "reaction": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was a strange reaction as he hung there -- he , the brave and daring swimmer , famed for his dives off Carn Du , held up by the man he had always denounced as a terrible coward ; whom he had hated from boyhood almost , without cause , and whom really , under the impulse of a horrible temptation , he had on the previous night tried to hamper in his swimming , though not really to drown", "context_len": 81}}}, "dejection": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 70, "context": "So she rejoiced , albeit she had been sad and sorry for her [ whilom ] bridegroom the Vizier 's son , -- she rejoiced , [ I say ] , with an exceeding joy , when she saw the jewels and the beauty of the damsels , and was cheered ; whilst her father rejoiced exceedingly in her joy , in that he saw her put off chagrin and dejection", "context_len": 71}}}, "martyr": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` For the sake of him who hath even now rendered up himself as a martyr for the Lord 's cause , I may not be angry with any word of yours , '' he answered sadly ; `` but I do entreat of you to take heed ! Would you lay down your arms and live in peace among your cattle and your corn , coached and complimented into effeminacy and foolishness ? Oh , for shame ! Rub your eyes and look about you ! What was the fate of the men of Sodom when they thought Lot was one that mocked when he warned them ! ''", "context_len": 110}}}, "mapped": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "With all Ted 's faults he was as open as the day , and the part which Harry and discretion and the Doctor had mapped out for him to play had been harder than you can imagine", "context_len": 37}}}, "hoping": {"freq": 82, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 78, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "With one consent they stood still and looked around as if hoping to see a familiar face , but here was a denser crowd of foreigners and only the dark eyes of Russians and Poles met theirs", "context_len": 37}}}, "errand": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 25, "context": "Albert , it must be confessed , had made more than one mysterious sortie down the gallery stairs , as though bent on an important errand which had just occurred to him ; but in each case he brought up in rather aimless fashion in some remote corner of the chapel ; so it was easy to comprehend that the only real purpose in view was to give his restless little four-year-old self the benefit of a change", "context_len": 78}}}, "longed": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Indeed , he had never felt more kindly disposed toward her , and he certainly had never so longed for a sight of her little thin , ungraceful figure", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 8, "context": "In fact , he always liked , and longed to be , the villain , rather than any other person in the play , and he so glutted himself with crime of every sort in his tender years at the theatre that he afterwards came to be very tired of it , and avoided the plays and novels that had very marked villains in them", "context_len": 65}}}, "ungraceful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Indeed , he had never felt more kindly disposed toward her , and he certainly had never so longed for a sight of her little thin , ungraceful figure", "context_len": 29}}}, "felled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "But do you know , very confidentially , dat I felled asleep in de church beside Timothy , so dat de garter showed , and den de children teased me awfully 'bout it , and Marie-Celeste calls me her little knight now almost always", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Went down like a felled ox , and lay there until my puttees -- which I started to unroll but did n't finish -- began smouldering", "context_len": 26}}}, "resentment": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 27, "context": "Filelfo , who was greatly attached to D'Aubusson , saw by their manner that they wished to engage him in some intrigue , and , feigning great resentment and anger at his disgrace , led them on until they divulged the entire plot for D'Aubusson 's assassination , and made brilliant offers to him if he would afford them facilities for carrying it out , producing , in proof of their power to do so , a letter of the pasha , authorising them to make such promises in his name", "context_len": 91}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Quiet and self-contained and little given to impulse as he was , Seabrooke , when roused to anger or resentment , was a very lion in his wrath , and there was one thing which he could never tolerate or overlook , and that was any attempt to take an unfair advantage of him", "context_len": 54}}}, "locomotive": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 26, "context": "Suppose she should make way with herself ? Suppose she should jump into a hole in the ice , or throw herself in front of a locomotive , or do any other of the foolish things that desperate and maddened people were in the habit of doing ? What would then be his position ? Not an enviable one , by any means", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Matt relapsed into silence , while the car continued to speed along the gloomy , tree-bordered road , following the long shafts of light like a phantom locomotive on gleaming rails", "context_len": 31}}}, "maddened": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "Suppose she should make way with herself ? Suppose she should jump into a hole in the ice , or throw herself in front of a locomotive , or do any other of the foolish things that desperate and maddened people were in the habit of doing ? What would then be his position ? Not an enviable one , by any means", "context_len": 63}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "But in carrying a city by storm , which takes place usually at an unexpected time , and often in the night , the maddened and victorious assaulter suddenly burst into the sacred scenes of domestic peace , and seclusion , and love -- the very worst of men , filled with the worst of passions , stimulated by the resistance they have encountered , and licensed by their victory to give all these passions the fullest and most unrestricted gratification", "context_len": 81}}}, "enviable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "Suppose she should make way with herself ? Suppose she should jump into a hole in the ice , or throw herself in front of a locomotive , or do any other of the foolish things that desperate and maddened people were in the habit of doing ? What would then be his position ? Not an enviable one , by any means", "context_len": 63}}}, "partly": {"freq": 161, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 161, "word_index": 24, "context": "Gervaise , who had half risen to his knees , was unable to avoid the blow , but threw himself forward , his weight partly pinning the corsair 's shoulders to the ground , and the blow passed behind him , inflicting but a slight wound in the back ; then , with his right hand , which was now free , he grasped Hassan by the throat with a grip of iron", "context_len": 73}}}, "shreds": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "The Arab took a torch from one of the sailors , walked across to Gervaise , who was naked from the waist upwards , his upper garments having been torn into shreds in the struggle , and examined him closely", "context_len": 40}}}, "aware": {"freq": 108, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 100, "word_index": 41, "context": "He used also to go to the market of the goldsmiths and the market of the jewellers , and there he would sit and look upon the different kinds of jewels and see them bought and sold ; whereby he became aware that the fruits of the trees , wherewith he had filled the purses , [ 318 ] whenas he was in the treasure , were neither glass nor crystal , but jewels , and knew that he had happened upon great wealth , such as kings might nowise compass", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 22, "context": "They have worn the rock smooth , so that it glistens like glass in the morning sun , for , as if aware of the folly of urging on its regiments of well-mounted cavalry to come dashing in upon the wild white-maned sea-horses , or the more sober lines of heavy infantry in uniforms of green and blue , the sea has for countless ages bombarded Carn Du with stone-shot in the shape of great boulders", "context_len": 76}}}, "opinion": {"freq": 155, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 155, "word_index": 73, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}}}, "respecting": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 40, "context": "As the 23rd day of April , 1668 , drew near , that being my twenty-first birthday , I found that I was becoming more and more excited over the vague rumours that I had heard from time to time respecting the remarkable disclosures that were to be made under the terms of my father \u2019 s will", "context_len": 58}}}, "connect": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 57, "context": "Who can tell all that goes on in the mind of a little child , or what it understands of death ? In a vague way Peggy knew that her playfellow had 'gone to heaven , where mother is , ' but she did not think of that as any cause for sorrow , nor did she connect him for an instant with the place where she stood , but , with her nurse 's words still troubling her , she knelt down and searched among the white flowers that hid the bare earth beneath", "context_len": 95}}}, "glowered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "There was an ugly word they might connect with his name -- and he glowered over the fire , and felt sufficiently uncomfortable until a strange and marvellous thing happened", "context_len": 30}}}, "ell": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Farther along the street stood a square , old-fashioned , story-and-a-half house , with a more modern ell and shed adjoining , and a wretched sagging barn , that lurched on its foundations , and was only kept from toppling farther , and possibly falling , by long , crude timber props , set against its side", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "His office was in a little ell part in a rather inviting looking house , and he took his meals with the tenant", "context_len": 23}}}, "hitching": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "The kitchen was in an ell of the house , and , by hitching his chair around , he could command a view from the side window of a slice of the garden in front , and also of a narrow strip of the road before the house", "context_len": 48}}}, "slice": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 45, "context": "[ 31 ] I have now lived in four bungalows , and all have elegant rooms , and there is such an air of refinement that I have great doubts whether it would be the correct thing to put out my hand and take a slice of bread off a plate", "context_len": 51}}}, "beg": {"freq": 85, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Then you would like me to leave you , Professor ? You would like me to write to my mother and explain all the circumstances to her , and beg and implore of her to take me away ? ''", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 77, "word_index": 52, "context": "I can tell you , directly Sir John Kendall came back and told us that you had been appointed commander of the new galley , and that it was to be manned wholly by young knights , there was not one of those serving their profession in the auberge who did not beg Sir John to put down his name for it ; and ten or twelve others , myself among them , who have obtained full knighthood also", "context_len": 79}}}, "gathering": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 29, "context": "Dick hurried her into the coach with all speed , and signed to his servant to lead his horse down a retired alley , but the aspect of the gathering crowd was so threatening , and that of his attendant saints so grim , that he began to suspect that his only escape from being stoned by the unbelieving mob , or run through by a Fifth Monarchy corporal , would be to be laid by the heels in a city jail !", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` For hundreds upon hundreds of years the winds and tides have combined to rid themselves of this obstacle to their progress , the winds urging the waves that come rolling in from the vast Atlantic , gathering force as they increase in speed , like one rushing at a leap ; and at last leap they do , upon the great black mass of shale , tons upon tons in weight , seeming as if they would sweep it clear away , and rush on in mad ruin to tumble the fishing luggers together and shatter them like eggs as they lie softly rubbing together in the harbour", "context_len": 109}}}, "highway": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 58, "context": "Despite the fact that he was on the losing side , my father was not backward in declaring his attachment to His Gracious Majesty King Charles II ; and although our neighbours , even the Roundheads , were favourably disposed to him , making allowance for his fiery temper , yet with strangers who passed along the great highway betwixt London Town and Portsmouth , honest Owen \u2019 s outspoken declarations oft led to wordy strife , and on occasions ended in blows", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Some of the fellows wished to be highway robbers and outlaws ; one who intended to be a pirate afterwards got so far in a maritime career as to invent a steam-engine governor now in use on the seagoing steamers ; my boy was content to be simply a god , the god of poetry and sunshine", "context_len": 57}}}, "deceased": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Francis Le Baron transmitted to the Smithsonian Institution a few ethnologic notices and a vocabulary obtained from the Seminole Indians of Chipko 's ( since deceased ) band , which he had visited in March 1881 in their village near Lake Pierce", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Marriage between individuals of the same gens was prohibited ; the office of the m\u00edko and the succession to property of deceased persons was and is still hereditary in the gens", "context_len": 31}}}, "fore": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "The cadet shook his fist at the captain 's door and moved on to join his companions in the fore part of the ship", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 30, "context": "She is not the least like my dear mother , and I never loved her , and I know that the best in me will not be brought to the fore if I am with her ; and I sha n't learn those things which would delight dear father ; I shall not know modern languages , nor be a good musical scholar , nor be able to sing nicely , and I -- I shall hate that life , and my nature may be warped , and I -- but , oh ! I will win the Scholarship", "context_len": 99}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "I \u2019 m goin \u2019 to -- say , Jennie ! don \u2019 t you go and tell now , \u2019 fore I get started", "context_len": 25}}}, "imperfect": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 85, "context": "had hay in his right shoe , and straw in his left , because a Dutchman was too dumb , as the boys said for stupid , to know his feet apart any other way ; and that the Dutch officers had to call out to the men when they were marching , `` Up mit de hay-foot , down mit de straw-foot -- links , links , links ! '' ( Left , left , left ! ) But the boys honored even these imperfect intelligences so much in their quality of soldiers that they would any of them have been proud to be marker in the Dutch company ; and they followed the Dutchmen round in their march as fondly as any other body of troops", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "This also shows the process , how historic legends and traditions are forming among uncultured nations , which are possessed of imperfect means only for the transmission of ideas to posterity", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "the lattice ) is lacking or imperfect", "context_len": 7}}}, "moistened": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "For more than a week before Hal 's return from school the others had been busy getting the ammunition ready ; they had dug up a quantity of sand from the bed of the stream , which , when mixed with a little clay and moistened with water , represented cannon-balls", "context_len": 51}}}, "correctly": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 62, "context": "I must cure the king , or it would be so much the worse for me ! And how was I to cure him ? My knowledge of disease was of the slightest and most amateurish kind , and , for aught that I could tell to the contrary , might not even be sufficient to enable me to diagnose the case correctly , much less to treat it successfully ! However , there was no use in meeting trouble half-way ; the only possible course was to obey the summons forthwith , and do my best , leaving the result in the hands of Providence", "context_len": 106}}}, "positive": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 96, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}}}, "attract": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 41, "context": "I was doing a most imprudent thing to linger by the side of the pyre , for the smoke , in the first place , and the light of the flames when it fell dark , could scarcely have failed to attract to the spot any savages who might have been in the neighbourhood , when my plight would probably have been as bad as ever ; but at that moment my sorrow at the loss of my companion overcame every other feeling , and , for the moment at least , I was quite indifferent as to what befell me", "context_len": 101}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "My teacher used to tell me trees keep the soil moist and attract rainfalls", "context_len": 14}}}, "resuscitated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "If the girl lived , and was going about saying that he had killed her dog , here was proof positive that he had not ; and smacking his lips , and making a clicking sound with his tongue , he tried to attract the resuscitated Poacher 's attention", "context_len": 49}}}, "marshes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "We crossed the fine timber bridge over the Isis , then kept the causeway over the marshes , till , crossing an arm of the main stream , we ascended a hill and passed through the open country", "context_len": 38}}}, "impatiently": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 63, "context": "And now , said Albert , appealing to the Queen , for he felt that quite too much was being taken for granted , will you please tell me what is a tilt-yard ? and what it was dat de knignts would not tome to ? and what was dat little white ting of the Queen 's dat de King carried ? and impatiently as Marie-Celeste brooked the interruption , there was nothing for it but to wait while Her Majesty explained that the tilt-yard was a sort of riding-school for the knights , where they practised for the tournaments , and that the tournaments were occasions when the knights , spear in hand , came together to ride against each other , with a great many people", "context_len": 128}}}, "hobbled": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 27, "context": "His heart sank , for hardly half a dozen steps were taken when \u2019 Ceph limped again : the halt grew more pronounced , and suddenly he hobbled one or two steps on three legs , holding the remaining hoof clear of the ground as he did so", "context_len": 48}}}, "sash": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 28, "context": "Mamma used to dress me in white dresses with short sleeves that came just to my elbow , tied round with pink or blue ribbons , and a sash to match , tied on one side in front , and I knew it was pretty and stylish , and used to walk around with my head in the air , and people would laugh and say I was awfully cunning", "context_len": 70}}}, "cellar": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 8, "context": "It went up and down stairs , from cellar to garret , and in and out of all the rooms , like anybody , with a faint , cynical indifference in the glance of its cold gray eyes that gave no hint of its purposes or performances", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 2, "context": "Cleaning her cellar and playing with him brought no money to give food to either ; she must go out to earn something , she must leave the child behind her , and if he cried in any way the neighbours overhead would discover his existence , and then her secret would be out , and her treasure torn from her arms", "context_len": 62}}}, "boldness": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 90, "context": "True , after their first dread of her wore off , and they found she was by no means so terrible as they had imagined , they no longer scampered off at the least sound of her voice or glimpse of her skirts , as they had done at first ; and Bessie even found courage to speak to her now and then , always looking however , as if she thought she was running a great risk , and could not tell what would be the consequence of such boldness", "context_len": 91}}}, "convinced": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 47, "context": "Knowing that I was bound hither , a certain very beautiful young lady of noble family , to whom I had the honour of being introduced , prayed me that if you should by any chance have escaped from captivity -- and she said that she was convinced that you would , when you heard that Rhodes was threatened , assuredly endeavour to escape and to come hither to take a share in the defence -- I was to tell you that she trusted you still bore her gage , and that she , on her part , had held fast to the promise she made you", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 40, "context": "When she was much younger , extreme carelessness had been her besetting fault , and , as is the manner of this `` little fox , '' had created much trouble for herself and for others ; but having become convinced that it was her duty to cure herself of this , she had set to work to do it in such earnest that that which had been a burden and a care to her was fast becoming a settled habit , and it was but seldom now that any act or word of heedlessness could be laid to her charge , while her ever obliging disposition and loving heart prompted many a deed of kindness which she never failed to carry out if it were in her", "context_len": 128}}}, "stiffened": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 27, "context": "At last the weather became very cold , and being without a fire , his sufferings were great , until his ferocious gaoler , finding him quite stiffened , brought up a brazier of coals , which saved his prisoner 's life , while it filled the room with smoke , which could only escape by the crevices in walls and roof , for to open a window would have been as bad as to dispense with the fire , such was the state of the outer air", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "That 's the worst thing I ever had happen to me , '' and Sid looked at his stiffened fingers", "context_len": 20}}}, "doubted": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 57, "context": "Why should not other middle-aged ladies go out , as she went out ? -- not necessarily always to attempt full Zenana work ; but to be protectors , housekeepers , nurses , to younger and more active ladies ? Whether it would be right to use any portion of Mission-funds for such a purpose may be doubted ; and in many a case Mission rooms could not be spared ; but there are exceptions as to the latter", "context_len": 79}}}, "dawned": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 12, "context": "Thus the time passed on until the first day of the Customs dawned , when , having received a more than usually substantial meal , I was stripped of the few rags that still covered my nakedness , and , with my hands tightly bound behind me by a thin but strong raw-hide rope , was led forth to the great square wherein the Customs were celebrated , and firmly bound to one of the posts , the erection of which I had witnessed a week earlier", "context_len": 87}}}, "frost": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 43, "context": "But O Sir , how Miss Sophy trembled and turned as white as a lily , and Miss Ratty stamped and sent me to the village -- at that hour , your honour , company in the house -- the ground covered with frost -- I subject to the rheumatics -- and what for , d \u2019 ye think ? to get her twopenceworth of shoe-ribbon , your honour ; and when I brought it , would you believe it ? -- she roared out that it was too narrow and sent me back again", "context_len": 95}}}, "transform": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "They had bathed , and combed their matted hair , which was alone sufficient to transform them , but the feeling that they were once more free men , and knights of an honoured Order , had done even more to effect the change ; and although they looked thin and worn , the martial bearing had come back naturally as they donned their knightly robes and buckled on swords", "context_len": 70}}}, "execrable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Gippie was tired out , and in an execrable temper", "context_len": 10}}}, "happenings": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 27, "context": "Here they all are , Ben , Polly , Joel , Phronsie , and David , in the loved `` Little Brown House , '' with such happenings crowding one upon the other as all children delightedly follow , and their elders find no less interesting", "context_len": 46}}}, "jealous": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 69, "context": "Mother says it is very conceited of a person to think she can satisfy every need of a friend , and that it shows only love of self , and not love of your friend , when you want to exclude others from her friendship , and I am sure I do n't want to be either selfish or conceited , and I should hate to be called a jealous person", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "He had no doubt that his betrayal was due to one of the gardeners , who had several times shown him signs of ill will , being doubtless jealous of the immunity he enjoyed from hard labour , and who must , he thought , have crept up and overheard some conversation ; but in that case it was singular that the fact of his knowledge of Turkish had not been mentioned", "context_len": 72}}}, "claim": {"freq": 86, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 70, "context": "But Marie-Celeste succeeded in getting through the first few hours without telling : the two hours with Harold on the train , a very trying half hour when she was all alone with her mother , and another trying half hour the next morning , when she was sitting in the breakfast-room with Dorothy ; and after that the worst was over , so many delightful things came along to claim everyone 's thought and attention", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Of course she does n't know all I suffered , what with taking up one worthless claim after another month in and out -- if you mean that ! Why , I actually thought one time of giving up prospecting and settling down to day 's work ! Yes 'm ! It was sure enough that grub-stake you gave me last Fourth of July that brought me my first luck ! I put it right into Pony Gulch and my pick struck free-milling ore the first blow ! Some of the stuff runs ninety dollars to the ton and some higher", "context_len": 102}}}, "recognition": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 6, "context": "He had no fear of a recognition , for the darkly stained skin and the black hair had so completely altered him that when he had looked at himself in a mirror , after the application of the dye , he was surprised to find that he would not have known it to be his own face", "context_len": 57}}}, "moaned": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 41, "context": "A moment later there was a sound of harsh voices , the noise of stones striking against steel , more pistol-shots , and then quietness , broken at length by the return of the troopers bearing between them a man who moaned and cursed lustily as he was carried by none too tender hands", "context_len": 54}}}, "depression": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 13, "context": "There was now a short breathing time for the besieged ; but the depression which the failure of their efforts excited among the Turks , was shortly dispelled by the arrival of a ship , with a despatch from Constantinople , in which the pasha was informed that the sultan himself was about to proceed to Rhodes with a reinforcement of a hundred thousand men , and a fresh park of artillery", "context_len": 72}}}, "tickle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "Her face was so pale and unhappy , that even Gippie 's selfish heart was touched , and standing on his hind legs to reach her shoulder , he tenderly licked her right ear inside and out , until she brushed him aside with a half laugh , half sob , and a murmured , `` You tickle my ear , Gippie", "context_len": 62}}}, "vault": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 52, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}}}, "mottled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "It was black and cold , and the old man , sitting in the draughtiest corner of the room , right by the cellar door , was a dull , mottled purple", "context_len": 32}}}, "morose": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "He has grown so morose and snappish that no one takes the trouble to do more than nod to him nowadays", "context_len": 21}}}, "hater": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Gippie was an excellent hater , and it did him good to see the old man suffer", "context_len": 17}}}, "dismally": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 33, "context": "Here and there a dead sheep or pig drifted by , or a portion of a haystack ; a hurdle floating like a raft bore on it a few disconsolate fowls , clucking dismally ; while an occasional wash-tub or upturned table showed that the river must have already flooded some of the low-lying cottages higher up on its course", "context_len": 60}}}, "delinquent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "However , he did not care to suffer with him , and squealing dismally , he planted himself near the delinquent stove", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "But this took time ; perhaps a month ; for the gunsmiths would only work at it in their leisure ; they were delinquent subscribers , and they did it in part pay for their papers", "context_len": 36}}}, "listlessness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "The dull listlessness from which his encounter with Astbury aroused him for a moment , closed on him again as soon as he was once more alone ; the glimpse of his old ideals that had revisited him had faded , and only left him with a dogged determination to do nothing unworthy of them , but with no pride or pleasure in his resolve", "context_len": 65}}}, "kindling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Devering picked him up by the back as if he had been a puppy dog and said , `` Tear up to the house -- touch a match to the kindling in the box stove in your room and dress like sixty", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "Dad put an axe into my hand , and taught me to chop kindling before I was out of petticoats , and when we went up the Rockies shooting grizzlies we built log-cabins , and I can tell you there was no carpenter to fetch there nearer than a hundred miles , so I guess I ought to know how to handle a saw and fix up a bit of lumber", "context_len": 71}}}, "dint": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 70, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "astonishingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Under the zealous and indefatigable tuition of this young damsel I made astonishingly rapid progress as a student of the language spoken by those around me , and was soon able to converse in it with a very fair amount of freedom", "context_len": 42}}}, "nigher": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Mebbe \u2019 tain \u2019 t the same dorg , but he answers the deescription the Widder Jones give ; and it \u2019 s mighty queer a dorg should be hookin \u2019 it round here , where there ain \u2019 t no houses nigher than a quarter of a mile", "context_len": 49}}}, "supplicating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "He would say nothing , however , and 'Tilda Jane , approaching the long , peculiar looking bundle , opened it , took out a pair of crutches , and handed them to him with a humble , supplicating air", "context_len": 40}}}, "enwrapping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The warm atmosphere was now enwrapping the old man in a most agreeable manner , and when 'Tilda Jane handed him the big cup , he grunted something between an expression of thanks and a desire that she should hold it to his lips", "context_len": 44}}}, "greedily": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "So it chose a heavier one just below that on which the boy was perched , and began to creep out upon it , tail switching and jaws dripping greedily", "context_len": 30}}}, "refilled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` \u2019 Tis one of the men who has fallen over his piece , perchance , '' remarked the sailor as he refilled his pipe , ramming the weed down with his little finger", "context_len": 34}}}, "supple": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "Guy thought that the men as they sat proudly upon their beautiful horses , holding in their hands long bows made of the tough wood of the osage orange , which is as supple as elastic , looked very noble and fine", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Although only seventeen years of age , he was a full inch over six feet in height , and , although broad across the shoulders , was sparely built yet supple of frame", "context_len": 33}}}, "manipulate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "She refilled it , then , as his hands began to get supple and he could manipulate it himself , she uncovered the basket Mrs", "context_len": 25}}}, "uncovered": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "I went to see what he was about , thinking here was some other fellow buried in the snow ; but as I came up to him , he uncovered the whiskey jug , the cause of all this mischief", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Why , yes I do , Theodore for although it was years since he had seen him , the momentarily uncovered head had at once established his identity ; but how do you and Donald happen to be in each other 's company ? Marie-Celeste told me Donald was on a farm down in Oxfordshire , and that you -- well , that nobody knew where you were exactly", "context_len": 69}}}, "napkin": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Oh , the beauty eggs ! '' and she carefully unrolled a napkin , `` an ' the white rolls , an ' Washington cake , an ' a meat pie , an ' a tart -- I say , grampa , we 'll have a good dinner ! ''", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Selden ( circumstances permitting , it is better to call people by their real names ) had discovered Donald from the dining-room window just as he was descending the steps , and recognizing him instantly flung his napkin onto the table , and hurrying from the room seized his hat from the rack as he passed", "context_len": 56}}}, "tart": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "The sight of the roast mutton , and the currant tart with Devonshire cream , which formed the nursery dinner that day , made him shudder ; and going to his own room , he flung himself on the bed , and after having taken some of the soup which was brought to him , he fell asleep", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "One of the gardeners came out and began to remonstrate with Irene on picking so many roses with buds attached to them ; but Irene told him in a very tart voice to mind his own business , and in some fear the man withdrew", "context_len": 45}}}, "expectant": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "Dorothy Allyn was standing in the doorway ready and expectant , and looking as pretty as a picture in a gray costume and a hat with a wide-rolling brim , that in her case was vastly becoming", "context_len": 37}}}, "coaxingly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Oh , but you see Will Abercrombie every day , '' said Celia coaxingly , `` and we do so want to have your help , Ben", "context_len": 28}}}, "supplied": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 72, "context": "One perfectly good chair was brought up for the president , the rest were content to be seated on whatever came handy , two chairs very much gone as to backs , one with the bottom entirely through , and a rickety camp stool made up the remainder of the furniture , but Agnes had taken care that there were flowers on the table and that pens , pencils and paper were supplied", "context_len": 73}}}, "extent": {"freq": 104, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 103, "word_index": 21, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Their country had an old waste field of seven miles extent , and several others of smaller dimensions , which shows that they were formerly a numerous people , to cultivate so much land with their dull stone axes , etc", "context_len": 41}}}, "druggist": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I saw 'em in a window , grampa , -- a great big druggist 's window , -- an ' I went in an ' said to the man , 'Can you trust me for 'em ? I 'll pay you , sure pop , if you 'll gim me time", "context_len": 52}}}, "grumpy": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "I 'm goin ' to be a good girl now , an ' never tell no more lies nor steal , nor do anythin ' bad , ' but he jus ' said ever so grumpy , 'This is a cast down , no credit system store , ' but I was n't cas ' down , an ' I said , 'S'pose you was a lame man , an ' a bad little girl burnt up your crutches , how would you feel ? 'Then he looked kind o ' solemn , an ' said , 'Whose crutches was burnt up ? ' An ' I said , 'Mr", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Get out , you grumpy old misogynist ! '' exclaimed Phil , shying a sofa cushion at his chum", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "During the whole period , Shagbark was in one of his grumpy moods , and rarely passed a word with any one", "context_len": 22}}}, "cussed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "' Then he asked what you said when I burnt your crutches , an ' I said you did n't say much , you jus ' cussed", "context_len": 27}}}, "owed": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 71, "context": "The spot was on the northern borders of the ancient kingdom of Sussex -- the land of the Saxon Ella -- a spot marvelously favoured by nature , occupying the summit of a low hill , which commanded a wide prospect on all sides , while itself almost impregnable when fortified , as it was , by ditches and mounds , dug in the usual Danish fashion , for the Danes owed much of their success to their skill in fortification", "context_len": 81}}}, "grudge": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 91, "context": "But , singularly enough , no one seemed desirous to avail himself of his neighbour 's generosity ; and the reason soon became apparent ; for presently , in the midst of their wild bounding round the inner edge of the tightly packed mass of spectators , they came to a sudden halt , and Mafuta , advancing alone , proceeded to `` smell out '' those who were supposed to be inimical to the king 's or his own authority , or against whom either of them had a secret grudge", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "Darling -- my own darling Letitia ! Oh , when she looked so happy , did she not see the angels -- or her beloved Father -- or the Bedwells and old Rodman whom she had so tended , -- perhaps all coming to welcome her , -- or the loving Saviour Himself ? I do not grudge her to Him ; but oh , what a wealth of love I have ( apparently ) lost in that one young heart ! Her last parcel of letters to me contained sweet commissions for her poor", "context_len": 95}}}, "denounced": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 54, "context": "It was weary , anxious work ; for of course our movements were being regulated by a theory that , for aught we knew to the contrary , might be all wrong ; and as day succeeded day without bringing the expected sail within our ken there were not wanting among us those who denounced the skipper 's plan as foolish , and argued that the proper thing would have been to go direct to the Gaboon , and look there for the Virginia", "context_len": 84}}}, "customers": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 68, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}}}, "overcharging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "This girl had evidently gone to Peter Jerret 's store , -- Peter Jerret who had owed him a grudge ever since the day he went in and denounced him before a store full of customers for overcharging him for prescriptions", "context_len": 41}}}, "prescriptions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "This girl had evidently gone to Peter Jerret 's store , -- Peter Jerret who had owed him a grudge ever since the day he went in and denounced him before a store full of customers for overcharging him for prescriptions", "context_len": 41}}}, "affected": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 2, "context": "Horrid , affected creatures ! And then to see Carter 's frightened eyes looking at me ! Have n't I seen them in my dreams until I am sick of the sight of them ? And Rosamund wants me to go and see them again ! Why , Carter , poor thing ! would nearly die of fright , and every one of the Leaves would get into their native trees and disappear from view ! Oh , Rosamund is all very well , but she is n't worth that ! I wish I had n't given those leeches back to the chemist", "context_len": 103}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 14, "context": "She never visited the Stirling house but she left some sting or sneer of affected superiority behind her , and when the work was done took it home , and the next day sent a note complaining that the handkerchiefs were spoiled , inclosing about one-fifth the usual compensation for such labor", "context_len": 52}}}, "whine": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "A low whine and a terrific wump as a high-explosive shell arrived and burst fifty yards in the rear of the trench showed that Fritz was still strafing", "context_len": 28}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "Leaving the bathing place while there was yet time to reach home before dark , they came at last to a ford across the stream , the only spot where it could be safely forded , and as such known to the natives of the vicinity ; when their dogs began to whine , and to run with their noses to the ground , as if they had found something unusual to attract their attention", "context_len": 75}}}, "discovery": {"freq": 90, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 90, "word_index": 46, "context": "He never let on that he suspected for a moment that he was not entirely alone , but , walking over to a tree stump , where , spread out on a newspaper , was the remains of a lunch , he acted delighted at the discovery , picked up a hunk of bread in one hand , a piece of cheese in the other , and , throwing himself on the green sward at full length , proceeded to munch the eatables , with every semblance of satisfaction", "context_len": 89}}}, "biddable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "If she had come home sulky he would have allowed her to find the dog for herself , but she was meek and biddable , and she had also secretly pleased him by addressing him as `` grampa , '' in tones of such respect and affection", "context_len": 47}}}, "secretly": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 58, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "icy": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 23, "context": "But still she went on , but only slowly , because of the great press of people around her ; and suddenly an icy wind passed over them , and all fell to the earth shivering and terror-stricken , for they had never felt cold before , and they looked up to the mountain , and lo ! upon the very summit , at the door of the deserted wigwam , stood a terrible figure , clothed in white , and having a face as white as his robes , and his hair was like the long crystals that hang from the roofs of caves that the water goes through , and his eyes were like two great diamonds , white , yet blazing like the sun", "context_len": 127}}}, "scream": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 35, "context": "Without wasting a moment , I applied my lips to the double wound , intending to suck the poison from it , even as I had done in my own case ; but another startling scream from the girl caused me to look up , and , following the direction of her terrified glance , I looked behind me and beheld the king himself , his eyes ablaze with demoniac fury , in the very act of raising a spear that he had snatched from the hand of one of his guards , to drive it through my body", "context_len": 99}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "So intense was the pain he suffered that he asked to be left alone that he might scream , as that seemed to bring some relief", "context_len": 26}}}, "famished": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "There , standing on the top step , cold and shivering , half famished , but alive and well , was her beloved Poacher", "context_len": 24}}}, "beloved": {"freq": 101, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 43, "context": "But the dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 11, "context": "None could look back , not even to behold If those beloved were nigh ; one thrilling thought Rapt all the multitude , -- Can HE be near ? Then cries of terror rose -- I scarcely heard ; And buildings shook and rocked , and crashing fell , -- I scarcely marked their fall ; the trembling ground Rose like the billowy sea , -- I scarcely felt The motion ; such intensity of hope -- Joy -- expectation -- flooded all my soul ; A tide of living light , o \u2019 erwhelming all The hopes and fears , the cares and woes of earth", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 103, "context": "Alaeddin [ 329 ] waited till his mother had made an end of her speech and said to her , `` O my mother , all that thou thinkest I know ; marry , I know full well that I am the son of poor folk , nor may all this thy talk anywise avail to move me from my purpose ; but I beseech thee , an I be thy very son and thou love me , do me this kindness ; else wilt thou lose me , for death hasteneth upon me , an I attain not my wish of the beloved of my heart", "context_len": 107}}}, "gyrations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The old man was so near , that Poacher , in his wild gyrations to and fro , swept one of his crutches from him", "context_len": 25}}}, "recover": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 21, "context": "'Tilda Jane , even in the midst of her astonished and ecstatic glee , perceived this , and stooped down to recover the lost article , but she could not lay her hand on it , for the excited dog , with his head in the air and his tongue hanging out , made repeated dashes at her , beside her , behind her , -- he was everywhere that she was", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "But you know he can \u2019 t recover", "context_len": 8}}}, "article": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 38, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "dashes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "'Tilda Jane , even in the midst of her astonished and ecstatic glee , perceived this , and stooped down to recover the lost article , but she could not lay her hand on it , for the excited dog , with his head in the air and his tongue hanging out , made repeated dashes at her , beside her , behind her , -- he was everywhere that she was", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "Suddenly away flash wig and beard and false-face , the pantaloons are stripped off with the same movement , the actor stoops for the reins lying on the horse 's neck , and James Rivers , the greatest three-horse rider in the world nimbly capers on the broad pad , and kisses his hand to the shouting and cheering spectators as he dashes from the ring past the braying and bellowing brass-band into the dressing-room !", "context_len": 76}}}, "everywhere": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 62, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "' I have been looking for you everywhere", "context_len": 8}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Jane rehearsed so many pretty sayings and missed her everywhere", "context_len": 10}}}, "snorting": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 86, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}}}, "mortification": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 62, "context": "Thank goodness you 've got your senses left , said Chris , turning to the young fellow , who really seemed anxious to render every possible service ; and if we get them into the room there you can put them to bed , ca n't you ? while I go for the doctor ; and in a voice scarcely audible from mortification the young fellow replied that he thought he could ; so after some difficulty in making them understand the move impending , the two men were successfully landed in the best spare-room", "context_len": 95}}}, "maimed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "And Gippie was after him , for , snorting with rage and mortification at the resuscitation of his rival , he had bounded from under the stove , and , with his maimed tail wagging excitedly in the air , was biting , snapping , growling at Poacher 's heels , nipping him fiercely , if by chance he paused a second to rest", "context_len": 64}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "A shot announced that the maimed animal \u2019 s sufferings were ended , and the troopers , with their two prisoners now safely in the centre , broke into a trot , the coach swaying to and fro as it rumbled over the rough road", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "On Sundays he was in the habit of regularly addressing a collected crowd of Natives ; literally the poor , the maimed , the halt , the blind \u2019 ; and he did not teach them only , but also ministered liberally to their bodily needs", "context_len": 46}}}, "wagging": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 34, "context": "And Gippie was after him , for , snorting with rage and mortification at the resuscitation of his rival , he had bounded from under the stove , and , with his maimed tail wagging excitedly in the air , was biting , snapping , growling at Poacher 's heels , nipping him fiercely , if by chance he paused a second to rest", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "She found some bones and scraps , which she brought forth on a plate , and Jimmy , begging the privilege , was permitted to feed Pilot , who expressed his appreciation by a sharp bark and such frantic wagging of his tail that his whole body was shaken from side to side all the way to his forward shoulders", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Joe drew aside his jacket , and disclosed to the children 's delighted eyes the sweetest little round fuzzy ball of black fluff , just like a tiny woolly bear , with tan chest and paws , and a wagging morsel of a tail like a black tassel", "context_len": 48}}}, "biting": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 41, "context": "And Gippie was after him , for , snorting with rage and mortification at the resuscitation of his rival , he had bounded from under the stove , and , with his maimed tail wagging excitedly in the air , was biting , snapping , growling at Poacher 's heels , nipping him fiercely , if by chance he paused a second to rest", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "Barking , biting , and snarling , the combatants had continually to be seized by the tails , forcibly separated , and kicked yelping back to their duties , where they stood with bristling ears , growling at each other through the hurdles , and showing their teeth like a pack of wolves", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "At this time , when Tip was in danger of going mad and biting people , he showed a most unseasonable activity , and could hardly be kept in bounds", "context_len": 30}}}, "nipping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "And Gippie was after him , for , snorting with rage and mortification at the resuscitation of his rival , he had bounded from under the stove , and , with his maimed tail wagging excitedly in the air , was biting , snapping , growling at Poacher 's heels , nipping him fiercely , if by chance he paused a second to rest", "context_len": 64}}}, "fiercely": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 13, "context": "My first intention was to refuse to budge from the carriage , to fiercely demand that the coachman should turn round and drive me straight back again to mother , but on second thoughts , I reflected that I should lose a good deal of dignity by this proceeding , and the best possible plan was to appear as if nothing at all extraordinary had occurred , and to follow Mrs", "context_len": 71}}}, "confusion": {"freq": 95, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 95, "word_index": 73, "context": "This question reminded him again of his determination to leave Oakdale directly ; and , knowing the good woman had regarded the room as engaged by him for the time of the fall term of school , and also feeling that to leave her thus and so deprive her of the rent money she expected to receive for weeks to come would be a poor return for her kindness , he hesitated in confusion and reluctance to tell her the truth", "context_len": 81}}}, "secured": {"freq": 75, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 75, "word_index": 95, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}}}, "pantingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Finally 'Tilda Jane secured the crutch , and , pantingly brushing back her dishevelled hair , she passed it to him across the dogs ' backs", "context_len": 26}}}, "dishevelled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "Davenport , instead of coming upon a Wednesday or Thursday , when all would have been neatness and order , and Nancy in her best black dress and muslin apron , should have chosen this particular Saturday afternoon , when there was no fire in the drawing-room , a pile of mending on the dining-room table , and all the family in somewhat dishevelled array", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Father Cuthbert and Edmund , amongst others , turned round to see what caused the lull , and started from their seats as they beheld at the end of the room Alfgar , his face pale as one risen from the dead , his black locks hanging dishevelled around his neck , his garments torn , his whole person disordered", "context_len": 60}}}, "exerting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "The moment before Harry Paul seemed to have been exerting his full strength to force the boat through the water , but an accession of strength came to him , and with a few fierce thrusts he drove her bows into the edge of the current , which gave it so quick a snatch that it was whirled round , and its occupant nearly lost his footing ; but he was too practised a boatman for that", "context_len": 77}}}, "beseechingly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "Oh ! greatly relieved that the matter could be so easily explained ; and then he added , turning beseechingly to Her Majesty , Wo n't you please make her tell it ? Se always says se knows a great deal about her , but se never tells what se knows", "context_len": 51}}}, "quarrelling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "When I come back jus ' now I thought my pet was buried in the cold ground -- oh , I jus ' love you ! '' and , climbing over the quarrelling dogs , she seized the bunch of knuckles nearest her , and kissed them fervently", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "'Taint nothing much of a bill that 's owed to Pattens , and even if half of it was to be paid , I have not the slightest doubt that Pattens would take me out and give you a bit more time ; but there 's no use in quarrelling with me , nor telling me to go , for go I wo n't , and ca n't", "context_len": 68}}}, "knuckles": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 15, "context": "Some of those boys had hands as hard as horn , cracked open at the knuckles and in the palms , and the crevices blackened with earth or grime ; and they taught my boy to believe that he was an inferior and unmanly person , almost of the nature of a cry-baby , because his hands were not horn-like , and cracked open , and filled with dirt", "context_len": 69}}}, "fist": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 35, "context": "I am afraid this time her stone was bigger , for it bounced with such effect on his shoulder , that he turned round with a flow of language far from elegant , shaking his fist at the tree with so much fury that Peggy was in terror lest he should climb up to find the author of the assault ; but he evidently thought the day too hot for such exercises , and with a final abusive epithet walked away muttering curses on all the children in creation", "context_len": 89}}}, "atrocity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "The whole Macedonian force were soon in full possession of the now defenseless houses , and for many hours screams , and wailings , and cries of horror and despair testified to the awful atrocity of the crimes attendant on the sacking of a city", "context_len": 45}}}, "annoy": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 10, "context": "She drew a little closer to him on purpose to annoy Lucy ; and then , when she found that he was short-sighted and could not find his places , she found them for him , thus adding to poor Lucy 's torment ; for this had once been Lucy 's own seat , and she herself had seen to her father 's comforts", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Burton , `` Bringer of joy not of annoy", "context_len": 9}}}, "blossomed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Yet , full as London was of hopes and fears , of mad gaiety and black despair , the October day was as sweet and still as any day of any autumn ; the late roses blossomed as of old in the gardens of the Strand , and vine-leaves wreathed the citizens ' with their wonted coronals of ruby and gold", "context_len": 61}}}, "formed": {"freq": 215, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 213, "word_index": 29, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Soon after , the regiment to which he belonged formed part of the army that retreated to Corunna , when our troops suffered such terrible hardships", "context_len": 26}}}, "changing": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 76, "context": "my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction , you shall meet with the fate you so justly deserve", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "It was unfortunate for Peggy that the third form classroom was furnished with double desks , for as Miss Crossland would permit no changing of places , she was obliged to sit by her enemy for the rest of the term , to their equal discomfort and annoyance", "context_len": 48}}}, "happier": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 8, "context": "Never in all this world was there a happier little host than Harold Harris when he found how kindly his guests from across the water were taking to the life at Windsor ; but who would not have taken kindly to it , I should like to know ? The Queen herself , in her great castle on the hill , could not have planned more for the comfort of her guests than did Harold in his little castle beneath it ; and , indeed , this name of Little Castle had somehow attached itself to the pretty stone house , with its round tower and moat-shaped terrace", "context_len": 108}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "After Bessie was undressed , and had said her prayers , she sat on her mother 's lap , and told her of all her troubles , and then she felt happier", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "She was aware of making a resolute effort as she said , `` Would n't Lola be happier with the Vigils ? ''", "context_len": 23}}}, "understanding": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 12, "context": "By degrees , though , the cloud that had been over his understanding seemed to float away , and as they were nearing the harbour he began to recall the urgings he had received to leap from Carn Du , which now stood up black and forbidding on his left ; the swim out to the lugger and round ; and then -- '' Well , how do you feel now , lad ? '' said the master", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 67, "context": "When she raised the veil from her face and Alaeddin saw her , he said , `` Verily , her fashion glorifieth the Great Creator and extolled be the perfection of Him who made her and graced her with this beauty and goodliness ! '' And his back was cloven in sunder , [ 321 ] when he saw her ; his thought was confounded and his understanding [ 322 ] dazed and the love of her gat hold upon his whole heart ; so he turned back and returning home , went in to his mother , like one distraught", "context_len": 101}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 60, "context": "In addition , a piece of music will be played by each girl and a song sung by each ; but the final and most searching test of all will be the essay , which in itself will contain , I doubt not , the innermost heart of the competitor , for she can not truly write on Heroism without understanding something of what a hero or heroine should be", "context_len": 70}}}, "arisen": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 8, "context": "It is by some lexicologists supposed to have arisen from the circumstance of a man answering another , who begged of him a wine-jar ( hubb ) , with the words , `` Ay , I will give thee a jar and a cover ( kerameh ) also , '' and to have thus become a tropical expression of ready compliance with a petition , as who should say , `` I will give thee what thou askest and more", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "An idea had , however , arisen of a home , at least for a time , with their brother , Mr", "context_len": 22}}}, "soothe": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 3, "context": "She did not soothe or pet her overmuch ; she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little", "context_len": 128}}}, "pacify": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "He still gave way occasionally to terrible fits of temper and sullenness , but 'Tilda Jane understood him better now , and was quick to soothe and pacify him , or , if he was unmanageable , to keep out of his presence until he recovered", "context_len": 46}}}, "unmanageable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "He still gave way occasionally to terrible fits of temper and sullenness , but 'Tilda Jane understood him better now , and was quick to soothe and pacify him , or , if he was unmanageable , to keep out of his presence until he recovered", "context_len": 46}}}, "accommodating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Just now he was in an unusually amiable frame of mind , -- a frame of mind so accommodating that it boded storms in the near future", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Nonsense , Darry , I happen to be handy and accommodating , and they do n't want to turn the fellow down on account of his 'pull", "context_len": 28}}}, "boded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 61, "context": "Your Honor , smiled Marsh , we are seeking to establish the motive for the sudden flight of Ben Stone from this town , and we hope to show beyond doubt that he did not run away because he had committed theft , but because he knew this pursuer of his brother had arrived and feared unreasonably , doubtless that it boded harm to the blind lad", "context_len": 67}}}, "flowing": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 120, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Now that they were really close to the water the stepping-stones looked much farther apart than they had imagined , and very slippery and slimy , too , with a swift current flowing between", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "Giving her my hand to steady her in entering the crank little craft , I waited until she had seated herself aft and taken the steering paddle in her hand , then , with a powerful push that sent the canoe , stern-first , far out into the rapidly flowing stream , I sprang in over the bows , seized a paddle , and proceeded to force the craft off-shore into the strength of the current", "context_len": 76}}}, "betokened": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "The man was short , thick-set , with a heavy beard that concealed most of his features , but the look in his eyes betokened that he was no infant in the art of war , and could , if occasion served , prove a harsh taskmaster", "context_len": 47}}}, "emotions": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 55, "context": "Yes , you said something like that to the captain ; and as though she would have given all the world if he had n't , but I did n't mean to be bold really , only I felt so sorry for Chris ; and then she proceeded to tell , as coherently as her emotions would allow , of her unexpected encounter with her old friend , and how dreadful it would have been if they could not have seen anything of each other just because Chris was a second-cabin passenger , and of how she had mustered all her courage and gone straight to the captain to see what could be done about it", "context_len": 116}}}, "diplomatically": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Dillson made no reply , and seeing that he was in no mood for a sympathetic comparison of emotions , she diplomatically started another topic of conversation", "context_len": 27}}}, "topic": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 25, "context": "Striding in among the group of boys who were still discussing the very question of the disappearance of the money which had been the main topic of interest ever since the loss was discovered , the bank-note in his hand , he advanced directly to Flagg , who was taking an active part in the conversation -- that is , he had been doing so within the last few moments , since he had returned after a short absence from the school-room , looking , as more than one of the boys observed , `` flushed and rather flurried", "context_len": 99}}}, "bide": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "If she should happen to turn in at some house , he decided he would try to intercept her before she rang the bell , so that they might have at least a few moments ' chat , but otherwise he would bide his time a little while and see what came of it", "context_len": 54}}}, "toothless": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The chief himself was an old man , wizened and toothless , with an inscrutable expression in the small eyes he turned upon Bomba", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Cap'n Si , a grizzly , toothless old man with a pleasant smile and twinkling blue eyes had already drawn his boat up on the sands when they reached him", "context_len": 30}}}, "whimsical": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 10, "context": "She found beautiful new flowers growing in her room , whimsical little gifts tucked under pillows , and once , as they sat together in the evening , they heard the scratch of a heavy paw on the door , and when Sara went to find out what it was , there stood a great dog -- a splendid Russian boarhound -- with a grand silver and gold collar bearing an inscription", "context_len": 72}}}, "display": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "And , despite all her kindness , all her readiness to see the best in everybody , she could not , with her keen sense of humour , have failed to be a good deal amused with the various foibles and absurdities which certain people are wont to display , even in the best society , and when upon their most circumspect behaviour", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 55, "context": "But although I had passed through the day unscathed , so far as actual bodily injury was concerned , it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything", "context_len": 128}}}, "examination": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Further particulars with regard to the competition I will let you know in a few days , but I may as well say now that most of the examination will be viv\u00e2 voce , and will consist of eight questions relating to the study of the French language , eight questions on the study of the German tongue , eight mathematical questions , eight arithmetical questions , eight questions on English History , and eight on English Literature", "context_len": 79}}}, "features": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 35, "context": "Alfred , her eldest son -- for Edmund was the offspring of an early amour of the king -- was on the other side of the bed , a well-made youth , combining in his features the haughty bearing of his Norman maternal ancestors with the English traits of his father ; but now his expression was one of distress and anxiety , which was yet more deeply shared by his younger brother , Edward , who even at this period manifested that strong sense of religious obligation and that early devotion which in later years caused him to be numbered amongst canonised saints", "context_len": 104}}}, "sometime": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 36, "context": "Gorswen Abbey , as Peggy 's home was called , had been an important place in its time , and an air of sleepy grandeur seemed still to hang about the old walls , as if sometime it might rouse itself from its lethargy and take its part in the world again", "context_len": 52}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "So on our way back tonight , \u2019 said Peter , a lot of us will lead you to your little rooms and we \u2019 ll drop the contents of the packages of dreams which will sometime come true", "context_len": 39}}}, "nation": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 86, "context": "518 , in describing the mounds of the country , states `` that the region lying between Savanna river and Oakmulge , east and west , and from the sea coast ( of the Atlantic ) to the Cherokee or Apalachean mountains ( filled with these mounds ) was possessed by the Cherokees since the arrival of the Europeans ; but they were afterwards dispossessed by the Muscogulges , and all that country was probably , many ages preceding the Cherokee invasion , inhabited by one nation or confederacy ( unknown to the Cherokees , Creeks )", "context_len": 97}}}, "hearer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "She waited for her hearer to ask why the mother was forbidden to come where the daughter could enter , but he did not do so , and she continued , `` She was a poor woman from Boston , an ' her mother was a poor woman from Canada , an ' they said if she come in 'twould be two poor women together , an ' first thing they knowed they 'd be both in the poorhouse", "context_len": 79}}}, "announcement": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 52, "context": "Her gruesome voice as she recounted what the goblins did to naughty boys made several small sinners in the back benches shake in their shoes -- many innocent infants felt they should never dare to go to bed in the dark again ; and the wild shriek with which she ended her announcement that 'the goblins are about ' quite brought down the house , and the children shouted and yelled and cheered as the Rector strove to get order once more , and wind up the proceedings with the National Anthem", "context_len": 92}}}, "jubilee": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` It 's a reg'lar jubilee , grampa , when the trains come in -- a boy runnin ' to a big bell an ' ringin ' it , an ' people pourin ' into the lunch-room , an ' jus ' chasin ' the food into their mouths an ' lookin ' hunted-like , as if there was somethin ' after them , an ' some do n't take time to go to the tables", "context_len": 76}}}, "mouths": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 30, "context": "Then the boys and girls shrank away , for had they not heard of her pranks , and might they not expect toads and frogs to come out of their mouths , and other horrors to happen if Miss Irene had anything to do with them ? They therefore began to decline Irene 's cake , and to say , `` No , thank you , miss , '' in a very timid voice when delicious-looking pieces of bread-and-butter and jam were offered to them", "context_len": 85}}}, "shaped": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 28, "context": "Well , the first place we went into was the Albert Chapel ; and oh , Donald , but it 's beautiful ! There 's a marble floor shaped in diamonds and circles , and there are such beautiful stained-glass windows , and under each window a picture of something from the Bible , and these pictures are made of different sorts of marble , somehow , and there 's a great deal of gold in them , that makes them more beautiful still", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I have also sometimes been called the Wild Lady \u2019 s Slipper because I am shaped something like that", "context_len": 19}}}, "oranges": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "There 's glass dishes on it , with oranges , an ' bananas , an ' cakes an ' pies , an ' sangwiches , an ' a funny machine where you drop a nickel in a crack , an ' if the hand points to five , or ten or fifteen , you get twenty-five cents ' worth of candy , an ' if you do n't get candy you get good advice like as , 'You 've been keepin ' bad comp'ny , quit it or you will never prosper , ' or 'You 've run away from home , an ' the perlice is on your track , ' or 'Smokin is a bad thing for your health", "context_len": 121}}}, "bananas": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 12, "context": "There 's glass dishes on it , with oranges , an ' bananas , an ' cakes an ' pies , an ' sangwiches , an ' a funny machine where you drop a nickel in a crack , an ' if the hand points to five , or ten or fifteen , you get twenty-five cents ' worth of candy , an ' if you do n't get candy you get good advice like as , 'You 've been keepin ' bad comp'ny , quit it or you will never prosper , ' or 'You 've run away from home , an ' the perlice is on your track , ' or 'Smokin is a bad thing for your health", "context_len": 121}}}, "machine": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 29, "context": "There 's glass dishes on it , with oranges , an ' bananas , an ' cakes an ' pies , an ' sangwiches , an ' a funny machine where you drop a nickel in a crack , an ' if the hand points to five , or ten or fifteen , you get twenty-five cents ' worth of candy , an ' if you do n't get candy you get good advice like as , 'You 've been keepin ' bad comp'ny , quit it or you will never prosper , ' or 'You 've run away from home , an ' the perlice is on your track , ' or 'Smokin is a bad thing for your health", "context_len": 121}}}, "nickel": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "There 's glass dishes on it , with oranges , an ' bananas , an ' cakes an ' pies , an ' sangwiches , an ' a funny machine where you drop a nickel in a crack , an ' if the hand points to five , or ten or fifteen , you get twenty-five cents ' worth of candy , an ' if you do n't get candy you get good advice like as , 'You 've been keepin ' bad comp'ny , quit it or you will never prosper , ' or 'You 've run away from home , an ' the perlice is on your track , ' or 'Smokin is a bad thing for your health", "context_len": 121}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "prosper": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 91, "context": "There 's glass dishes on it , with oranges , an ' bananas , an ' cakes an ' pies , an ' sangwiches , an ' a funny machine where you drop a nickel in a crack , an ' if the hand points to five , or ten or fifteen , you get twenty-five cents ' worth of candy , an ' if you do n't get candy you get good advice like as , 'You 've been keepin ' bad comp'ny , quit it or you will never prosper , ' or 'You 've run away from home , an ' the perlice is on your track , ' or 'Smokin is a bad thing for your health", "context_len": 121}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "May God prosper your humble efforts , my sweet Laura", "context_len": 10}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 85, "context": "replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both far and near have heard ? How is it thou hast heard nought of this nor of the name of Alaeddin , whom Our Lord increase in glory and prosper ? '' Quoth the Maugrabin , `` Marry , it is the utmost of my wish to look upon the palace ; so , an thou wouldst do me a kindness , direct me thither , for that I am a stranger", "context_len": 128}}}, "startling": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 34, "context": "Without wasting a moment , I applied my lips to the double wound , intending to suck the poison from it , even as I had done in my own case ; but another startling scream from the girl caused me to look up , and , following the direction of her terrified glance , I looked behind me and beheld the king himself , his eyes ablaze with demoniac fury , in the very act of raising a spear that he had snatched from the hand of one of his guards , to drive it through my body", "context_len": 99}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 41, "context": "Eating and drinking were over , and the old gleeman , striking his harp , was beginning a song of harvest home , when in rushed the two young theows who had gone out with Alfgar and Bertric , with the startling intelligence that there was a band of Northmen lurking in the woods , who had seized their young lords , and were , they thought , bent on attacking the place", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then the reporter , who for a time turns detective , gets a clue revolving about a startling , ancient method of combat", "context_len": 23}}}, "railroad": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 74, "context": "Fenn and Frank were for going to the one where they had previously had such good luck hunting wild turkeys , but Bart and Ned wanted to go to a less frequented part of the State , where larger game , such as an occasional bear or deer , could be had , and , in the end they carried their point , though it meant a longer trip , and necessitated going by railroad", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "That is almost railroad time , replied Alden admiringly", "context_len": 9}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "There were several village storekeepers , some traveling men from the hotel , and railroad men off duty", "context_len": 18}}}, "accidents": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 71, "context": "The next thing , and sometimes a better thing than knowing your favorite author , is to know how and where he lives ; and it was a matter of supreme delight to Marie-Celeste that henceforth when she should open Lewis Carroll 's books she should be able to picture him working away here in his study , and just as he really looked , too , for by chance or accidents full-length photograph stood on the mantel , which Dorothy , from her visita few years before , was able to pronounce an excellent likeness , and very characteristic", "context_len": 100}}}, "chances": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 67, "context": "I have tried both , and find there is more real pleasure to be had from the intellectual than from the social side of life , for while the latter is apt to fail us just when we most require it , the former is `` warranted to wear well and improve with keeping , '' and , so far from being affected by the changes and chances of this world , sticks by us when health and wealth and even friends can fall away", "context_len": 85}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "How he does lord it over the rest of us ! And if he chances to bring a new chum along with him , whom he rather wants to impress , then heigh-ho ! for a hard time for everybody", "context_len": 40}}}, "turnover": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "If you 're inside one o ' them cars , grampa , an ' there 's goin ' to be a turnover , jus ' grip hard on somethin ' steady , 'cause then you 're not so apt to get killed", "context_len": 42}}}, "grip": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "If you 're inside one o ' them cars , grampa , an ' there 's goin ' to be a turnover , jus ' grip hard on somethin ' steady , 'cause then you 're not so apt to get killed", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 47, "context": "Acting upon the impulse of the moment , and scarcely knowing what I was about , with a single bound I flung myself upon the terrified girl and , guided more by instinct than reason , seized the reptile immediately behind the head in so vice-like a grip that its jaws at once opened wide , when I tore its hideous coils from the girl 's arm and flung it far from me into the very heart of the blazing fire", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "The other was cold , too , but I felt it grip my wrist , and then , sort of husky and choky , Uncle Asher said , The will , it \u2019 s in \u2019 and that was all", "context_len": 40}}}, "active": {"freq": 86, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 86, "word_index": 53, "context": "I know a dozen rich Moors in Tripoli or Tunis who would be glad to have him ; and we agreed that we would run down to the African coast for awhile , for that galley has been altogether too busy of late for our comfort , and will be all the more active after this little affair ; besides , people in these islands have got so scared that one ca n't get within ten miles of any of them now without seeing their signal smokes rising on the hills , and finding , when they land , the villages deserted and stripped of everything worth carrying away", "context_len": 109}}}, "plague": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 66, "context": "My birthday eve they kept in high style , the men smoking long clay pipes , till our dining-room was enveloped in a cloud of tobacco smoke , so that my aunt declared that her best hangings would reek for days like a London coffee tavern , whereat Sir George pacified her by saying that the weed of Virginia is ever a sovereign safeguard against the plague !", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 66, "context": "Then the boys found another toy store ; but they considered the dealer mean ; he asked very high prices , and he said , when a boy hung back from buying a thing that it was `` a very superior article , '' and the boys had that for a by-word , and they holloed it at the storekeeper 's boy when they wanted to plague him", "context_len": 68}}}, "puckered": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Captain Poynings lost no time in preparing to float the Gannet out of her mud dock , though it was evident from his puckered brows that he had doubts as to whether the increased weight on board would prevent the ship from coming off", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Ah , goot-morning , Mynheer ! '' was the greeting of the governor , as he made his way across the sloping deck of the galliot , his usually grave visage puckered with a thousand wrinkles , while his eyes twinkled with grim humour", "context_len": 45}}}, "pups": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "He had claimed it to be a gift from a friend just returned from Europe , who had brought over the famous litter of pups of which it was one", "context_len": 30}}}, "creepy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The word of such high authority as the minister could not be gainsaid , though the suggestion gave the girls rather a creepy feeling", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Out of sight of land had always stood with Marie-Celeste for such an awe-inspiring condition of affairs that she expected to feel all sorts of chilly and creepy feelings when she fairly faced the thought ; and yet here she stood , alone to all intents and purposes , and no land anywhere , and yet not so much as the suggestion of a chill or a creep", "context_len": 68}}}, "folded": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 25, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 27, "context": "They fascinated her with their crude drawing and colours still vivid and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined", "context_len": 128}}}, "hemming": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "'Tilda Jane folded together the apron she was hemming , and getting up , opened a door of retreat behind her into the house", "context_len": 24}}}, "agitation": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 63, "context": "Miss Jones , the village dressmaker , was installed in the Rose Parlour , and appeared to be stitching morning , noon , and night ; the postman 's bag was quite heavy with patterns and catalogues of Indian outfits ; while distant relations and old acquaintances , who had neglected the Abbey for years , kept Nancy in a state of perpetual agitation by turning up suddenly to pay congratulatory calls", "context_len": 72}}}, "spy": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 84, "context": "We drop in upon a man who is one of our most respected merchants , whose family has been established here many years , whose interests must be the same as those of the Order ; and because a guest of his does not care to take any active part in my joking with the girls , and because you imagine that there is a cunning expression on his face , you must straightway take it into your head that he must be a spy", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "To the surprise of the British officer and crew , the hitherto unsuspected spy swam across to the hostile craft", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 65, "context": "`` Yes , they would if they were hungry , but Lammie-noo has probably not been here more than a couple of nights , and if the bears have been roaming in some other direction they would not get wind of the fact that there was a nice plump lamb on the old Lonesome Hill farm -- -- Come up higher , and we 'll spy the landscape o'er", "context_len": 69}}}, "tap": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 71, "context": "Never had a night seemed so long ; the only one who thoroughly enjoyed himself was the puppy , who , delighted to receive so much attention , pursued his diversions until the sun was well in at the window , when at length he snuggled into Peggy 's pillow , and composed himself to sleep , leaving his weary guardians only time for a brief rest before Nancy 's unwelcome tap was heard at the door", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "Don \u2019 t you know that you must get to work ? This is the season for us to tap the sirup from the maple trees , and you are spending your time swinging in a hammock", "context_len": 37}}}, "bowl": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 6, "context": "The hostess who brought Dick 's bowl of onion broth at this moment destroyed his chance of feigning sleep , and he had to resign himself to endure his companion 's conversation which flowed on , garnished with oaths and cant phrases in three or four different languages , without any interruption , till by an unguarded movement Richard exposed his face to the light of the fire , and the stranger stared a moment , and then sprang up exclaiming , `` Body o ' me if it be not Measter Dick himself ! ''", "context_len": 96}, "VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 15, "context": "But whether we would or no , we have not the time just now to bowl quietly along in leisurely fashion through that lovely region of hills and lakes", "context_len": 29}}}, "pitcher": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 36, "context": "Besides the bed , there was in the room for furniture a broken-backed rocking-chair ; a small table with a split top , on which stood a common kerosene hand-lamp ; a dingy white earthen water pitcher and bowl the former with a circular piece broken out of its nose sitting on a washstand , made of a long box stood on one end , with a muslin curtain hanging in front of it", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Oh , you little pitcher ! '' cried Harry ; and Tom said , `` Maggie dear , things may be quite right for your father to say , that would not be proper for us ; because Mrs", "context_len": 40}}}, "dispiritedly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` I 'll try to come home soon again , father , '' said Hank , dispiritedly , as he looked over his shoulder before closing the bedroom door , and he was just shrugging his shoulders at the promise , when something dark and panting caught at him in the unlighted kitchen , and made him jump", "context_len": 58}}}, "dew": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 3, "context": "Whence comes the dew that stands on the outside of a tankard that has cold water in it in the summertime ? The latter was one of many scientific queries he suggested to the Junto , in line with his own curiosity about the mysteries of life", "context_len": 47}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 71, "context": "The prisoner strode up and down the limited area , wrestling with self , bending the will by prayer to submit to ignominy and pain , for he knew now that his father had abandoned him , and that he had to apprehend the worst ; still he did not regret the choice he had made , and he felt , as he prayed , peace and confidence descend like heavenly dew upon his soul", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "He entered , knelt in a mental attitude easier to imagine than describe , but felt some heavenly dew fall upon his bleeding wounds ; he left without waiting to speak to any one at the conclusion of the service , and was crossing the quadrangle to the palace which occupied a portion of the site of modern Christ Church , when a heavy hand was laid upon his shoulder", "context_len": 70}}}, "occurrence": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 44, "context": "All the statues had entered except that of Philip , which was just at the door , and Philip himself was advancing in the midst of the space left for him , up the avenue by which the theater was approached , when an occurrence took place by which the whole character of the scene , the destiny of Alexander , and the fate of fifty nations , was suddenly and totally changed", "context_len": 73}}}, "details": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 31, "context": "Yet the thousands were heavy on our hearts , and I meditated some means of carrying tidings of their danger to our pagan neighbours ; but I knew nothing of the details of the plot , only that there was a plot , and I knew that if I sent a brother , the Danes , in their hatred to monks , would probably set their huge dogs at him before he could speak , and perhaps worry him to death", "context_len": 81}}}, "elevate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "While 'Tilda Jane wrote , Poacher suddenly made a stealthy movement , and Gippie , deaf as he was , had enough of the dog spirit left in him to know that some one was coming , and to elevate the tiny V-shaped flaps over his ears", "context_len": 47}}}, "flaps": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "While 'Tilda Jane wrote , Poacher suddenly made a stealthy movement , and Gippie , deaf as he was , had enough of the dog spirit left in him to know that some one was coming , and to elevate the tiny V-shaped flaps over his ears", "context_len": 47}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Oh ! he flaps round me in the water -- strikes me with his wings", "context_len": 16}}}, "bordering": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 39, "context": "Then , taking a bow and quiver of arrows , together with a brace of spears , out of the canoe , and signing to me to do the same , Ama led the way through the dense growth bordering the river bank , until we reached an open grassy space of about twenty acres , sparsely dotted here and there with magnificent trees ; and here Ama signified that we were to camp for the day", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 98, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "awkwardly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 4, "context": "The country-jake comes stumbling awkwardly into the ring , but he is perfectly sober , and he boldly leaps astride the mule , which tries all its arts to shake him off , plunging , kicking , rearing", "context_len": 38}}}, "peaceful": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 43, "context": "The rosy light of the August sunset died out of the west , the evening star which little Bessie had once said looked `` like God 's eye taking care of her when she went to sleep , '' shone out bright and peaceful ; then , as it grew darker and darker , came forth another and another star , and looked down on the world which God had loved so much , till the whole sky was brilliant with them ; the soft , cool sea-breeze came gently in at the windows , bringing with it the gentle plash of the waves upon the shore , mingled with the chirp of the crickets and the distant hum of voices from the far end of the piazza", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "They do buck sometimes , but this animal is as mild and peaceful as a lamb", "context_len": 16}}}, "softest": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Miss Irene , who would make such a show of herself , was absolutely pretty in her shady hat of softest white , and her white dress to match , and that face , which , notwithstanding all her naughtiness , was and must ever be beautiful above ordinary faces", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}}}, "treats": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "in the face of the carelessness with which the text treats kushk as synonymous now with keszr or teyyareh and now with liwan or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Indeed , not one of the children could resist the cheery , coaxing voice and tender manner ; and in five minutes they were all crowding about her , as she told of all the treats she had in store for them ; and even shy Maggie had summoned up courage to ask a question which had long been troubling her", "context_len": 61}}}, "lets": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 1, "context": "He lets me wash , an ' scrub , an ' cook , an ' never says a word excep ' not to work too hard , an ' if he wants to be jus ' a little bit cranky , jus ' a teeny little bit , he goes in his room an ' shuts the door till the bad spirit gets out of him", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Not that father ever minded , for he is the sort of man who just lets you do what you like , and I think that is why we have grown up nicer than most girls", "context_len": 36}}}, "shuts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "He lets me wash , an ' scrub , an ' cook , an ' never says a word excep ' not to work too hard , an ' if he wants to be jus ' a little bit cranky , jus ' a teeny little bit , he goes in his room an ' shuts the door till the bad spirit gets out of him", "context_len": 66}}}, "adventure": {"freq": 106, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 101, "word_index": 43, "context": "They did nothing with their boat except loll in it and tell stories , and as there was no current in the Reservoir , they must have remained pretty much in the same place ; but they had a sense of the wildest adventure , which mounted to frenzy , when some men rose out of the earth on the shore , and shouted at them , `` Hello , there ! What are you doing with that boat ? '' They must have had an oar ; at any rate , they got to the opposite bank , and , springing to land , fled somewhere into the vaguest past", "context_len": 111}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` O my son , '' replied she , `` true it is that the present is exceedingly costly and precious and as thou sayest , none hath the like thereof ; but who shall dare to come forward and seek of the Sultan his daughter Bedrulbudour ? Nay , I dare not adventure myself and say to him , ' I want thy daughter , ' whenas he asketh me , 'What wouldst thou ? ' Marry , O my son , , my tongue will be tied", "context_len": 89}}}, "slouch": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hayden wasn \u2019 t such a slouch of a fighter , but he couldn \u2019 t hold his own with me , for I was bursting with rage", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "But the latter wore a slouch hat , which was pulled well down over his eyes , shading his features", "context_len": 20}}}, "despond": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He 's in a regular slouch of despond sometimes , Mr", "context_len": 11}}}, "hank": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Hank", "context_len": 1}}}, "listlessly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "They looked up listlessly as he entered , and then an Italian said , Cospetto , comrade ; but I know not whether your place is with us , or with the Moslems across there", "context_len": 35}}}, "dreadful": {"freq": 180, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 172, "word_index": 64, "context": "the Smith house , he could remember much more : the little upper room where the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the mind", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I guess something dreadful has happened , but I ca n't imagine what it is , '' she said", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Oh , was n't it dreadful ? How did you happen to come along ? ''", "context_len": 16}}}, "aroused": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 6, "context": "In the morning Alaeddin arose and aroused his wife , whereupon her women came to her and dressed her and busked her and adorned her ; whilst he , on his part , donned the richest of raiment , [ 620 ] and both were like to fly for joy at their reunion with each other , after their separation , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour was especially glad , for that she looked to see her father that day", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Because you blackguarded and scolded him out of his boots when he stuck to these friends of his , last year , '' bravely returned the aroused Mr", "context_len": 29}}}, "stent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` You must not stent yourself , sissy", "context_len": 8}}}, "intending": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 62, "context": "He ceased not to go daily to the market of the jewellers and to clap up acquaintance with the folk , making friends with them and questioning them of buying and selling and giving and taking and dear and cheap , till , one day of the days , he arose in the morning and donning his clothes , went forth , intending , as of wont , for the jewellers ' market ; but , as he went , he heard the crier proclaiming aloud on this wise , `` By commandment of the Lord of Beneficence , the king of the age and monarch of the time and the tide , let all the folk shut their shops and stores and enter their houses , for", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Guy had witnessed three or four such falls , and in his excitement scarcely knowing what he did , went up to the foaming animal intending to put it out of its misery by a shot from his gun , when , suddenly , it rose to its feet , staggered forward , and ere Guy could wheel his frightened horse , plunged his horns into his breast , and buffalo , horse and rider rolled upon the plain together", "context_len": 80}}}, "boils": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I feel jus ' like the teakettle afore it boils , an ' that 'minds me -- I mus ' go set it on", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "'Now , while the water boils , ' said Mr", "context_len": 10}}}, "jail": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` I 'll tell you , mister , '' broke forth Lem , after another moment or two of silence , `` I was awful sorry when I heard what Doll did to them gardens after the little gals begged me out ; but you see she did n't know it , and she thought I was took to jail", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 63, "context": "`` No more pain -- no more trouble , '' murmured Dolly , her mind wandering again ; `` want to be an angel -- I 'll give her the cup , '' she cried ; `` they say it kills folks to be too long in the Ice Glen , but I ca n't get out ; they 'll send Lem to jail , will they ? I 'll fix 'em with their fine gardens -- want to -- rest for the weary", "context_len": 84}}}, "resentfully": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Well , that 's one way to take it , '' said the younger boy , resentfully , his regrets taking flight at once as they met with this apparently ungracious reception", "context_len": 33}}}, "ungrateful": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 54, "context": "Harwood 's reproachful glance , when he had said he had been deceived in him ; and he wondered if he would carry into execution the threat he had made before they left home , and greatly feared that he would , for he felt that he had been quite disobedient , and seemingly ungrateful enough , to be left alone on the prairie", "context_len": 64}}}, "peevishly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` What in the world has happened to you now ? '' peevishly quoth the patient", "context_len": 16}}}, "cleaned": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 61, "context": "Why do n't you shout Westward Ho ! and come along o ' me ? I know many a roaring blade that would be proud to ship under such a captain as you 'd make ! '' Then leaning forward , he continued in a solemn whisper , `` What though I seem no better than a beggar -- cavado , cleaned out , as the Spaniards say -- if I could but get a loan of as much as would carry me across sea , I 'd be a rich man again", "context_len": 93}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` The Indians at Fort Orange or Albany ( probably the Mohawks ) bound up the cleaned bones in small bundles and buried them : De Vries , Voyages ( 1642 ) p", "context_len": 33}}}, "ruined": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 63, "context": "But Huntington could not believe that they had gone , and they searched everywhere in the castle here for them , and he was so angry at not finding them , that he let his soldiers in and they stove in doors and tore down curtains and cut up furniture and carried off silver , so that in five hours the castle was ruined", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 9, "context": "But how comes she to be alone in this ruined house , like an enchanted damosel of a fairy tale ? 'T is a strange plight for such a tenderly-nurtured maid , for old Sir Gyles guarded her as the very apple of his eye ! And what state did not he keep , and Hunstanton Hall ! And with what a retinue did he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "RUINED", "context_len": 1}}}, "urgency": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "He leaned slightly forward in the saddle , the front of his hat standing straight up as if plastered against his forehead , the ends of the handkerchief looped about his neck fluttering in the gale caused by his own swiftness , while he occasionally pricked the ribs of his horse with his spurs , though such urgency was hardly needed", "context_len": 61}}}, "ta": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` And each man , we constrain him to carry his actions ( ta ' r , lit", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "maybe": {"freq": 200, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 194, "word_index": 82, "context": "This was not altogether because he was needed there , I dare say , but because it was part of his father 's Swedenborgian philosophy that every one should fulfil a use ; I do not know that when the boy wanted to go swimming , or hunting , or skating , it consoled him much to reflect that the angels in the highest heaven delighted in uses ; nevertheless , it was good for him to be of use , though maybe not so much use", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "And the plebes feared it would be that way , too , and yet there were a few at the tables discussing the matter in whispers , venturesome enough to say that perhaps maybe their classmate might win and to wonder what on earth would happen to him if he did", "context_len": 51}}}, "consolingly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "The lad was speaking more to relieve his own sore heart than anything else , but Doto seemed to understand , and put his hairy paw consolingly on the boy \u2019 s arm", "context_len": 33}}}, "sleeve": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 94, "context": "It was his brother , I suppose , who taught him to abhor it ; and perhaps it was his own suffering from it in part ; for he , too , sometimes shed bitter tears over such a knot , as I have seen hapless little wretches do , tearing at it with their nails and gnawing at it with their teeth , knowing that the time was passing when they could hope to hide the fact that they had been in swimming , and foreseeing no remedy but to cut off the sleeve above the knot , or else put on their clothes without the shirt , and trust to untying the knot when it got dry", "context_len": 119}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "of jeib ) , and his sleeves '' ( ibab ) , and note ) that ubb is here used in the common sense of `` sleeve", "context_len": 27}}}, "limps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He goes to town on his crutches every day , an ' Gippie limps after him -- oh , Hank Dillson , Hank Dillson , I 'm mos ' loony about this business of your goin ' away", "context_len": 38}}}, "pleadingly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "Some of the girls returned his gaze pleadingly , some dropped their heads , Clara Adams , with a little smile of indifference , began to play with her pencil", "context_len": 30}}}, "whirling": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 51, "context": "He had a notion that if he could get up a chariot by sawing down the sides of a store-box for the body , and borrowing the hind-wheels of the baby 's willow wagon , and then , drawn by the family dog Tip at a mad gallop , come suddenly whirling round the corner of the school-house , wearing spangled circus-tights and bearing Apollo 's bow and shaft , while a silken scarf which he had seen in a bureau-drawer at home blew gallantly out behind him , it would have a fine effect with the boys", "context_len": 98}}}, "altered": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 57, "context": "Harris , to whom the idea had first occurred , suggested it , Marie-Celeste had put her two arms round her father 's neck , declaring he was just a darling and yet , when you come to think of it , he was the very same old curmudgeon of a papa , and not one whit altered either , who had been so soundly berated for insisting that it would be better for Donald to have some easy work to do than to idle away the whole summer", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "The coach , who on the previous night had felt greatly disappointed in the material from which he had hoped to build a clever high school eleven , betrayed his relief and satisfaction by the altered expression of his face and the change in his manner", "context_len": 46}}}, "stiffest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He had been , at the same time , the stiffest and the most demonstrative of parents , the young man reflected", "context_len": 22}}}, "footing": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 66, "context": "The moment before Harry Paul seemed to have been exerting his full strength to force the boat through the water , but an accession of strength came to him , and with a few fierce thrusts he drove her bows into the edge of the current , which gave it so quick a snatch that it was whirled round , and its occupant nearly lost his footing ; but he was too practised a boatman for that", "context_len": 77}}}, "parting": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 20, "context": "And very attentively everybody listened to the story , as I told it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 9, "context": "The Native Christians not unfrequently subscribe to give a parting gift to a Missionary whom they love , when starting for England ; but I suppose they thought that , in my case , if they waited for that they would never give me anything , and that it was no harm to present me with something for not going away ! Mr", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "'Of course he will ! I wonder you can ask such a question ! Heaven would n't be heaven unless we found Rollo there ! We 'll wrap his body in the Union Jack , and pick all our best flowers to strew round him ; and you might fire off your old pistol over his grave , if Father will let you have any gunpowder -- a parting salute , you know , like they do for officers , ' said Peggy , cheering up a little at the thought of arranging the obsequies", "context_len": 95}}}, "mutual": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 53, "context": "They were nearly always absent from each other , they had got out of the way of taking an active interest in each other 's concerns -- there was not yet sufficiently firm footing and enough of it to bridge to the shaky background of the past , and parting would be a mutual relief", "context_len": 55}}}, "twinkled": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 32, "context": "Jones was laughing so ! He was not laughing out loud now , but he was shaking all over , and when Maggie peeped at him from under her eyelashes , he twinkled his eyes at her , as much as to say , `` Now , what do you think of her ? '' Right glad was she when Harry called her to take her turn at the swing , and she could run away out of sight of Mr", "context_len": 81}}}, "acutely": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "Hank had told him nothing of his troubles , yet his father saw that he had lost flesh , that he had not a prosperous air , and he acutely guessed that all was not going well with him", "context_len": 39}}}, "guessed": {"freq": 98, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 97, "word_index": 7, "context": "How came it that this girl had guessed his secret at once when most people passed him , taking him but for a sturdy beggar ? What made her suspect him of being a fugitive ? Was her offer of shelter but careless good nature , or a heroic endeavour to save a hunted man ? At any rate he had not fallen so low as to draw suspicion on a woman , and a young woman to boot , although she was plainly no nervous , fanciful , fine lady , but a bright , resolute , country girl , with good health and high spirits gleaming from every flash of her bright eyes , and every turn of her auburn head", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Yes , that was it , Marie-Celeste ; but the child little guessed how the high-strung fellow winced under the confession , and how his self-disgust never reached quite such high-water mark as at that moment", "context_len": 36}}}, "shrugging": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` I 'll try to come home soon again , father , '' said Hank , dispiritedly , as he looked over his shoulder before closing the bedroom door , and he was just shrugging his shoulders at the promise , when something dark and panting caught at him in the unlighted kitchen , and made him jump", "context_len": 58}}}, "panting": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 29, "context": "It would have been as if , when once the boys were playing trip at school , and a big boy tripped him , and he lay quivering and panting on the ground , he had got up as soon as he could catch his breath and gone in and told the teacher ; or as if , when the fellows were playing soak-about , and he got hit in the pit of the stomach with a hard ball , he had complained of the fellow who threw it", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 69, "context": "'And if ever I catch you at any of your tricks with Vaughan again I 'll settle you myself , I promise you , though I do n't know whether I could have done it any better than this , after all ! ' glancing with an eye of admiration at the victorious Peggy , who , with split gloves , scarlet cheeks , and wild-flying curls , stood panting after the contest", "context_len": 73}}}, "unlighted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` I 'll try to come home soon again , father , '' said Hank , dispiritedly , as he looked over his shoulder before closing the bedroom door , and he was just shrugging his shoulders at the promise , when something dark and panting caught at him in the unlighted kitchen , and made him jump", "context_len": 58}}}, "ensued": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 14, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They stopped paddling , and excited jabbering ensued", "context_len": 8}}}, "visited": {"freq": 116, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 115, "word_index": 44, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The following year a famine so severe visited England , that even the Danes forebore to ravage so poor a land ; but in 1006 , the next year , they overspread Wessex like locusts", "context_len": 35}}}, "creameries": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` You 've visited creameries , sir ? ''", "context_len": 9}}}, "owing": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 51, "context": "The Danes who had attacked the house of Herstan were so far in advance of their countrymen that they were forced to retreat instantly before the superior force which came to the rescue of the besieged ; and they fell back , at first in some order , but shortly , owing to the darkness and the pressure of their foes , in utter confusion", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "In 1743 he set out from Epworth to Grimsby ; but was told at the ferry he could not cross the Trent owing to the storm", "context_len": 26}}}, "spotted": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 38, "context": "A few years ago , when I was casting about in my mind for something to do , I got in with a Chicago firm known as the White Elephant firm -- owing to so many States being spotted with their buildings , loaded on the farmers , and costing too much to keep up", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Nor was Nancy the only one who indulged in a little innocent vanity , for Joe , too , had been so fastidious in his choice of a red-and-blue spotted necktie and a walking-stick with an ivory handle that the children began to suspect the blacksmith 's rosy-cheeked daughter must be at the bottom of it ; and even David had taken a long-tailed coat and a beaver hat out of the retirement of some mysterious bandboxes and had been quite snappy and particular with Mrs", "context_len": 86}}}, "costing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "A few years ago , when I was casting about in my mind for something to do , I got in with a Chicago firm known as the White Elephant firm -- owing to so many States being spotted with their buildings , loaded on the farmers , and costing too much to keep up", "context_len": 55}}}, "fired": {"freq": 131, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 131, "word_index": 80, "context": "People knew the time of their passing , and looked out for them daily ; kindly women would come to cottage doorways to nod and smile ; the inn-keeper at the Halfway Arms set his clock by their arrival ; they had struck up quite a friendship with the postman , two milkmen , and the driver of a fish-cart , while the old man who broke stones by the roadside always nodded and gave them 'Good-morning , ' and fired a facetious remark or two after them , which the children imagined he must relieve the monotony of his work by composing during the intervals of their coming and going", "context_len": 111}}}, "navigate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "You navigate round among the farmers , and make a smother of big talk about hauling in buckets full of money", "context_len": 21}}}, "smother": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "There was another rush to the ship \u2019 s side , and I saw a dozen dark forms struggling in a smother of foam-lashed water under our quarter , while the triangular fins of several sharks showed that the culminating tragedy had occurred", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "Looking back , the manacled lad saw Jerry standing there , his face hidden in his hands , the yellow dog gazing up sympathetically at him , a spectacle never to be forgotten ; and the frightful injustice of fate seemed to crush and smother the last spark of hope and strength in Ben \u2019 s soul", "context_len": 57}}}, "buckets": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 75, "context": "Among the missiles hurled into the town were balls filled with Greek fire , but the houses being entirely built of stone , no conflagrations of importance were caused by them , as a band of knights was organised specially to watch for these bombs , and whenever one of them was seen to fall , they hurried from their lookout to the spot , with a gang of slaves carrying baskets of earth and buckets of water , and quenched the flames before they had made any great headway", "context_len": 90}}}, "salted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "Now , John , said Uncle Isaac , these fish can stay in the pickle till you get back from the island ; I \u2019 ve salted them slack , so they will not be hard and dry ; then you can take them out , put them on the flake , and dry them", "context_len": 55}}}, "expenses": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 53, "context": "Schwitz had promised that his charges should be small , but how he never would give in his bill , the policeman and his wife thinking all the while that it was kindness which kept him from doing so ; how it had taken every cent of his salary to pay the other expenses of illness , and keep the family barely warmed and fed ; of the disappointment of their hopes for Willie for , at least , some time to come ; and finally of the terrible bill which Dr", "context_len": 92}}}, "promoting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "When the natives gathered in this structure for sweating , either for promoting their health or as a religious ceremony , they developed steam by throwing water on heated stones , then danced around the fire , and went to plunge into the chilling waves of the river flowing past their town", "context_len": 52}}}, "stockholders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "When the whole thing 's up , it 'll usually dawn on the minds of your stockholders that a creamery ai n't much without cows , and their cows ai n't got enough milk to pay for the fuel they burn", "context_len": 41}}}, "burn": {"freq": 82, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 67, "word_index": 64, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 99, "context": "Jerusalem , what is this ! bellowed a voice , and Joe Griffin leaped out from the midst of the burning pile ; the brands rolled off the back of his woollen shirt , which was thoroughly singed , while a fox-skin cap he wore was scorched to a crisp , as was his hair ; he ran round and round , as though he was mad , blowing his fingers ( which where slightly burned ) , and slapping them on his thighs , while on his face was a mingled expression of pain , arising from the burn , and anger at being outwitted", "context_len": 106}}}, "simpleton": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "After that his father tried to teach him the need of using his reason as well as his conscience concerning himself , and not to be a little simpleton", "context_len": 29}}}, "separator": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He ruined the separator , it had to be sent back to the shop , an ' I got mad with him", "context_len": 22}}}, "blabbed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Then he blabbed , told everything he knew , an ' a lot he did n't , an ' the farmers stopped counting their cows long enough to listen", "context_len": 30}}}, "counting": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 13, "context": "Playing in the garden , they saw Mary sitting outside the gate , counting over the money she had made by the sale of her radishes : and as they were talking to her , it came about that she told them of the sick brother lying on his bed , never able to go out and breathe the fresh air , or see the beautiful blue sky and green trees , in this lovely Summer weather ; and how she and her mother were working and saving , that they might have enough to buy the easy chair", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "I \u2019 m counting on her and you men to give the Japs a very special pasting today", "context_len": 18}}}, "fraudulent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hasty words flew round , about fraudulent subscriptions , vitiated transactions , no contracts , ruined farms , going to law -- an ' I thought it was time to skip", "context_len": 31}}}, "subscriptions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Hasty words flew round , about fraudulent subscriptions , vitiated transactions , no contracts , ruined farms , going to law -- an ' I thought it was time to skip", "context_len": 31}}}, "vitiated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Hasty words flew round , about fraudulent subscriptions , vitiated transactions , no contracts , ruined farms , going to law -- an ' I thought it was time to skip", "context_len": 31}}}, "transactions": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "'Ay , he be that , and scant honest , ' cried the stout miller , with a lively remembrance of sundry hard transactions with grain for the distilleries , in which , to his chagrin , he had had distinctly the worst side of the bargain", "context_len": 47}}}, "contracts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` I put on all the official dignity I could assume , but was very polite all the time , informed her that mislaid , delayed and irregular express matter were common occurrences , that the company was responsible for its contracts , counted you one of its most reliable agents , and assured her that very possibly within twenty-four hours she would find her trunk delivered safe and sound at its destination", "context_len": 73}}}, "farms": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 30, "context": "It was long since he had ventured into a town where he might be questioned and recognized -- the hunted man had only dared ask food or lodging at solitary farms or lonely hamlets ; and as he pushed forward , the gables and twisted chimneys of a mansion house , with garden walls and dove-cote , gave him hopes of help", "context_len": 62}}}, "bounced": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 12, "context": "I am afraid this time her stone was bigger , for it bounced with such effect on his shoulder , that he turned round with a flow of language far from elegant , shaking his fist at the tree with so much fury that Peggy was in terror lest he should climb up to find the author of the assault ; but he evidently thought the day too hot for such exercises , and with a final abusive epithet walked away muttering curses on all the children in creation", "context_len": 89}}}, "bids": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` My lord bids thee cast aside thy woman 's dress , '' said he , `` and ride in thine own character", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "And he showed us how saints did not live idle lives on this earth , but always went about , like their Lord and Master , doing good , and that through much tribulation they entered the eternal kingdom , which also bids fair to be our lot nowadays , although we be all miserable sinners , and not saints", "context_len": 60}}}, "hopeful": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Come , confess , Aunt Patty , '' he said ; `` acknowledge that it is to you this good fellow who has just left us owes his freedom from debt , his child 's eyesight , his release from cares which were almost too much even for his hopeful spirit ; acknowledge that you have generously sacrificed a long-cherished desire , given up the fruits of much saving and self-denial , to make those happy in whom you could have had no interest save as creatures and children of one common Father", "context_len": 94}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "The one hopeful truth was that the living Lightfoot had come over the route unharmed within the last hour", "context_len": 19}}}, "mush": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` O Lord ! Lord ! Lord ! '' she cried , suddenly stopping and raising her clasped hands to the ceiling , `` do make his heart soft -- soft as mush , an ' do n't let him be sassy", "context_len": 42}}}, "sassy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` O Lord ! Lord ! Lord ! '' she cried , suddenly stopping and raising her clasped hands to the ceiling , `` do make his heart soft -- soft as mush , an ' do n't let him be sassy", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Yas , I is ; doan \u2019 you hyar me ? Is you deef ? You \u2019 s getting too sassy , Tone Burke ; you need taking down a peg or two , and I \u2019 m de gemman dat am gwine to doot", "context_len": 45}}}, "sass": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The minister is smooth an ' nice , an ' he would stand sass , but it 's awful bad for Hank", "context_len": 22}}}, "owl": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 63, "context": "`` So it was agreed that her spirit should dwell as a prairie dog , and before long out sprang one from a reeking cauldron of herbs , and they took it to the holes of the prairie dogs and left it there , placing beside it a terrible serpent , that all others might be afraid to approach it , and an owl at the door , as a sentinel that would stand looking patiently for an enemy both night and day , and never breathe to the gossips around her the tale of the princess that was prisoned within", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Owl wanted to write a book and he asked the fairies how to set about doing it , commenced daddy", "context_len": 20}}}, "occupation": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 81, "context": "`` Well , it is going to be a splendid day , '' said Alice , `` and , you know , there are no lessons of any sort ; all the examinations are over and the results will be known to-night ; the day is to be a long and happy one -- no lessons , nothing to do except to wander about and please ourselves ; pack our trunks , of course , which will be truly a delightful occupation", "context_len": 82}}}, "straighter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Save that your lips lack the thickness , and your nose is straighter than those of Nubians , no one would doubt but that you were one of that race ; and this is of little consequence , as many of them are of mixed blood , and , though retaining their dark colour , have features that in their outline resemble those of the Arabs", "context_len": 66}}}, "thumped": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 26, "context": "I have a nice little pot buried in a safe place on a certain key ; I 've got a map here '' -- and he thumped his broad chest -- '' here , sewed in the lining of my coat , and the place marked with a cross ; and I tell you , sir , there 's enough gold in that pot to fit out the snuggest little pinnace any man need want to see", "context_len": 77}}}, "chest": {"freq": 98, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 90, "word_index": 36, "context": "My uncle busied himself till all traces of his worry lost themselves in his activity ; my aunt bustled about the house , driving the servants hither and thither , bringing linen from the huge oak chest , furbishing the massive furniture , and causing a huge supply of viands to be prepared for the entertainment of our guests -- for the invited company numbered close on a score , so that our house would be filled to overflowing , and rooms had to be engaged at `` The Bell Inn '' and `` The Blue Posts ''", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` Heels together , turn out your toes , hands by your sides , palms to the front , fingers closed , little fingers on the seams of the trousers , head up , chin in , shoulders thrown back , chest out", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "In accordance with orders each sprang up , stood at attention -- heels together , head up , eyes to the front , chest out , etc", "context_len": 27}}}, "jam": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 80, "context": "Then the boys and girls shrank away , for had they not heard of her pranks , and might they not expect toads and frogs to come out of their mouths , and other horrors to happen if Miss Irene had anything to do with them ? They therefore began to decline Irene 's cake , and to say , `` No , thank you , miss , '' in a very timid voice when delicious-looking pieces of bread-and-butter and jam were offered to them", "context_len": 85}}}, "speechlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "She stood gaping speechlessly at him", "context_len": 6}}}, "beamed": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 1, "context": "Peggy beamed with delight , for she wanted to see more of Archie 's wonderful work , and also the cakes and jam at the Willows had a reputation for excellence quite unsurpassed in the neighbourhood", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Jones stood outside and beamed upon him", "context_len": 7}}}, "reverently": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 72, "context": "Se looks jus ' as Marie-Celeste said , remarked Albert proudly , his sceptical spirit of the morning wholly transformed into one of profound admiration ; and Marie-Celeste , asking that she might hold the case in her own hand , and gazing entranced upon the dear little face looking out at her , said joyfully , Yes , she does look as I said , does n't she ? Then she reverently laid the miniature back upon the Queen 's lap , as though counting it quite too precious to be long out of royal keeping", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Becky handed them to her reverently", "context_len": 6}}}, "shamefaced": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I am very sorry , '' said Helen in a shamefaced manner , `` but I thought it would be such a splendid thing if I could get right up to the fort before the attack began", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The young man looked around at the group of boys , now rather shamefaced , at the group of girls now gathered around Esther Ann", "context_len": 25}}}, "admire": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 50, "context": "I was bought for a great deal of money , and I make a lot ! But what does that mean to me ? Nothing ! All I want to say is that if only I knew Grown-Up talk I \u2019 d say to all these people that they could admire me if they wished but to please remember my sisters and brothers when the summer comes again", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Florence , '' she said , `` you need much correction ; you are a very bombastic , disagreeable , silly , ignorant girl , but I will own it -- I do admire spirit , you have a look of your father , and I was very fond of poor John ; not as fond of him as I was of my own dear Tom , but still I respected him", "context_len": 73}}}, "berth": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 67, "context": "Harris , said Donald , who had been listening to Ted and doing his own thinking at one and the same time , there was an Englishman came over on the steamer with us , who grew to be great friends with Marie-Celeste , and Marie-Celeste told me all about him one of those afternoons when I was too weak to do anything but lie in my berth , and she tried to entertain me", "context_len": 75}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "But you will berth with the cadets , understand ? Do n't thank me", "context_len": 14}}}, "lumps": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 35, "context": "Then there was Pixie , the pony , who would come whinnying up from the further side of a field to poke her soft nose into the children 's pockets for pieces of bread or lumps of sugar ; there were numerous cats who lived in the barns and stables , and Tabbyskins , the stately gray Persian , who usually sat sunning herself on the pigsty wall , keeping a strict eye on naughty Jack , who was wont to harry her if he got the chance", "context_len": 88}}}, "sugar": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 32, "context": "Granby made the most extraordinary flowers of red flannel and gilt paper , -- flowers whose likeness never grew in gardens or greenhouses of any known land ; had she not baked sugar cakes which were intended to represent men and women , pigs , horses , and cows ? Were not the branches looped with gay ribbons ? Did they not bear rosy-cheeked apples , an orange for each child , some cheap but much prized toys , and , better than all , several useful and greatly needed articles , which had been the gift of Mrs", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "She picked out for each child a big round chocolate egg which rattled , for inside it there were little sugar candies ; also a chocolate chicken and a chocolate bunny", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "You never did hev much of a head , an ' now you 've been up among the clouds so long , you 'll be more like to sugar the butter and salt the pies than before", "context_len": 37}}}, "socks": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 61, "context": "`` I ca n't wait , '' said Maggie ; `` I want it now ; nurse is holding baby because mamma has gone to sleep too , and Jane has taken Franky to Harry 's room to dress him , because she was afraid he would make a noise ; and she said if I put on my shoes and socks , and all the rest of my under-clo 's before she came back , I might put on yours , if you waked up", "context_len": 86}}}, "handkerchiefs": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 50, "context": "The whole family was gathered together on the grass round the wood fire , the smoke of which rose up faintly blue against the russet of the beech-woods behind -- handsome , fierce-eyed men , lying slouching idly on the ground smoking short black pipes ; slatternly women with gay handkerchiefs tied round their black hair , bustling about with something savoury inside tin cans ; ragged brown-skinned little children , gnawing at bones with savage haste ; while a few disreputable dogs waited eagerly for the scraps that were thrown to them from time to time", "context_len": 97}}}, "kit": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 20, "context": "Big Kennedy 'was as good as his word , and before nightfall each of the New Zealanders had a full kit , although they wisely refrained from asking questions as to the origin of the source of supplies", "context_len": 38}}}, "whimpering": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I 'm sure , miss , '' said James , almost whimpering , `` the trouble I 've had already , and the anxiety and worry , not to speak of the pain , miss", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "Peggy liked to see Rollo working with the sheep , the tips of his ears twitching and his faithful brown eyes bright with intelligence , as he cleverly sorted the lambs which Father had pointed out from the rest of the flock , and drove them neatly into the enclosure , coming up whimpering with pride for the praise which he knew awaited his efforts", "context_len": 65}}}, "dried": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 63, "context": "Had Ben set the fire at first along the whole line of the brush , there would have been burnt ground between him and the mass of fire , which would have cut off the communication , and he would have been safe ; but he set it on one corner , and when the wind shifted , the flame driven by it dried the moisture from the grass , and made rapid progress towards the house , while a large strip of dry grass made a bridge for the fire to travel on", "context_len": 94}, "UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 44, "context": "The noise grew louder and louder , so that it resembled the squealing of a kitten , and all four began to turn over the moss with eager fingers , till , with a cry , Archie drew out a small round ball of dried grass , about the size of Bobby 's fist , from which issued such crescendo squeaks that there could be no mistake as to the locality of the sound", "context_len": 74}}}, "undressing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Your friend Madge sends word that she hopes you 'll take her to the opening game of the football season , '' said Phil to Tom , as he was undressing", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "They left Sid undressing and went out , it not being a proscribed hour", "context_len": 14}}}, "sidling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Oh , ai n't it lovely , ai n't it lovely ? '' and catching hold of her skirts she began a sidling and peculiar dance about the room", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Is all that you 're telling me true ? '' propounded the bewildered station agent , sidling up to Bart 's side", "context_len": 23}}}, "merriment": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 46, "context": "In every room Donald brings his own peculiar methods of investigation to bear , not in the least minding a good deal of mirthful laughter at his expense on the part of Harold and Marie-Celeste ; and Albert , feeling privileged to join in the general merriment , though evidently half the time without in anywise appreciating the situation , only helps on the jollity of things", "context_len": 67}}}, "observation": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 73, "context": "He had been puzzled when , four days before , he had received an order from the grand master that Ahmet , a servitor in the auberge of the English langue , should be permitted to pass the night in his house , with authority to move freely and without question , at any hour , in the courtyard of the gaol , and to depart at any hour , secretly and without observation , by the private gate", "context_len": 79}}}, "espied": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 13, "context": "At this juncture the Vizier presented himself and as he entered , he espied the new palace and the carpet and marvelled also ; so , when he came in to the Sultan , the twain fell to talking of this strange matter and marvelling , for that they saw a thing which amazed the beholder and dilated the heart ; and they said , `` Verily , methinketh kings may not avail unto the building of the like of this palace", "context_len": 82}}}, "inmate": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 63, "context": "A grand place , too , to work , you think ; and then you recall with a sigh that the light of that glorious mind has well-nigh gone out , 'neath the weight of physical weariness and infirmity , and then the solitary home begins to look a little like a prison in your eyes , as you realize how glad its inmate would be to exchange it for the Palace of that King whose divine intent for the world he has so marvellously interpreted for us all in the days when soul was still master of hand and brain", "context_len": 101}}}, "limp": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Besides , he did n't limp , as Riggs does , from a leg that was once broken", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 38, "context": "One of the men had his left arm in a sling , the empty sleeve being pinned to his coat ; the other , in addition to wearing a bandage round his forehead , walked with a pronounced limp and leant heavily upon a rubber-shod walking-stick", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 81, "context": "Did these belong to her ? asked Marie-Celeste , standing in open-eyed wonder before a mosaic table , which had been cleared to make room for a quaint collection of foreign-looking , childish possessions -- a mandolin , a well-worn little missal , a remarkable doll , a necklace or two , numerous little childish trinkets , and thrown over a chair , standing close to the table , a little gown of white silk and exquisite embroidery , yellow and limp with age , but none the less dainty and lovely", "context_len": 92}}}, "cheerless": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "Children stood ankle deep in water , pumping hour after hour , and their work was sometimes prolonged for thirty-six hours continuously ; so that it was no wonder the children died early , that they suffered much from disease , and led cheerless , wretched lives", "context_len": 47}}}, "unpleasantly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 70, "context": "I do not for a moment deceive myself with the thought that , beyond the fact that her gift has made her feel an interest in me and my fate , she has any sentiment in the matter : probably , indeed , she looks back upon the gift as a foolish act of girlish enthusiasm that led her into making a promise that she now can not but find unpleasantly binding ; for it is but natural that among the young nobles of her own rank and country there must be some whom she would see with pleasure wearing her colours", "context_len": 102}}}, "formerly": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 13, "context": "Dodgson no longer cares to see as much of the little people as formerly ; in fact , he rather runs away from them when they seek him out ; and when he received Dorothy 's letter , what did he do but write her that he was very sorry to say that he would not be at home on the afternoon in question , but that if it would be any pleasure to her little friends to see his rooms , she might bring them there and welcome , and that he would leave some old photographs that he thought would interest them ready to her hand in a portfolio on the writing-table", "context_len": 114}}}, "fortunate": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 36, "context": "My dear Caretto , the grand master exclaimed , it is almost a resurrection , for we have all long mourned you as dead ; and your return to us at the present time is indeed fortunate ; for upon whose judgment and aid could I better rely than those of my old comrade in arms ? Then , turning to Gervaise , he went on : It was a daring and brilliant exploit indeed , Sir Gervaise , and in due time honour shall be paid to you and your brave companions , to whom and to you I now tender the thanks of the Order", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 68, "context": "It would be useless to try the highways , she knew , so accordingly her search must be in the by-ways , and she made a little tour of investigation round all the back streets between the inn and the Grammar School , but without success , and she was just thinking she must have missed him , and had better return to the inn-yard , when a fortunate chance prompted her to turn up a retired avenue which lay between the two main roads", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "We were fortunate in taking them so much by surprise that the first of their ships fell into our hands before her consorts could come to her assistance ; and her guns did us good service in our struggle with the others", "context_len": 42}}}, "chafed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 46, "context": "A white cloth covered his formerly bare and uninviting table ; he was going to have pie , and coffee , and toast and cake for supper , -- surely the coming of this orphan had been a fortunate thing for him , and he slowly chafed his hands as he gazed at the glowing bed of coals", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Bomba chafed at the necessity , though he himself was almost exhausted in mind and body by the stirring events of the day that had taxed both to the utmost", "context_len": 30}}}, "schooling": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 17, "context": "If I do get the Scholarship father means to save the money that the three years ' schooling would cost , and he means to send me when I return home at the age of eighteen to a wonderful new College for Women which has been established at a place called Girton", "context_len": 52}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "She had simply given everything she had to a forsaken , ungrateful child -- home , food , raiment , schooling", "context_len": 21}}}, "educated": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 46, "context": "To those friends who thought he lost caste thereby he had always the same argument -- that he saw no reason why the cultivation of fields should counteract the habits of refinement and good breeding to which he had been reared ; that in the colonies educated gentlemen set to work to labour with their hands , and are thought none the worse of : so why not in England , where land is good and markets are plentiful , especially when it involved the keeping of a fine old property which had been in the family for so many hundreds of years ?", "context_len": 104}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "As a scholar he shone ; as a business man he was a failure , owing to the fatal and all too common trait amongst Maoris of the educated class of pleasure in the spending of money , and , oddly enough , to an inherent tendency to relapse , if only temporarily , to an aboriginal existence", "context_len": 58}}}, "teaching": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 19, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 4, "context": "Also his energy in teaching was reflected by her own burning desire , in old age , to speak on all occasions to the Natives of their deepest needs , and never to miss an opportunity of trying to lead some poor Hindu or Muhammadan to Christ , always with the vivid sense upon her , when she met man or woman , that the call to herself might come before they could meet again , and so a second opportunity might never recur", "context_len": 84}}}, "teacher": {"freq": 144, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 137, "word_index": 81, "context": "For instance , if you had been beguiled , as a little boy , into being the last in the game of snap-the-whip , and the snap sent you rolling head over heels on the hard ground , and skinned your nose and tore your trousers , you could cry from the pain without disgrace , and some of the fellows would come up and try to comfort you ; but you were bound in honor not to appeal to the teacher , and you were expected to use every device to get the blood off you before you went in , and to hide the tear in your trousers", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "He would really rather have borne some severe punishment at the hands of his parents or teacher than he would one contemptuous word or look from him who was regarded by all his young relations and friends as a chevalier sans peur et sans reproche", "context_len": 45}}}, "talks": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 30, "context": "Whatever one gives to be made or mended is made or mended in the verandah ; and the darzi , as he cuts out , clips , and sews , talks -- perhaps with the pankah-wala , perhaps a stranger , perhaps the munshi ( tutor ) whose pupil is not quite ready to take her lesson", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "He used to talk to himself when he was little , but one day his mother said to him jokingly , `` Do n't you know that he who talks to himself has the devil for a listener ? '' and after that he never dared whisper above his breath when he was alone , though his father and mother had both taught him that there was no devil but his own evil will", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` I do n't know the reason for it , Pony-Boy , for my father never talks much to me , but he never lets me go out alone , and I 'm not allowed to speak to strangers", "context_len": 39}}}, "scholar": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 62, "context": "She is not the least like my dear mother , and I never loved her , and I know that the best in me will not be brought to the fore if I am with her ; and I sha n't learn those things which would delight dear father ; I shall not know modern languages , nor be a good musical scholar , nor be able to sing nicely , and I -- I shall hate that life , and my nature may be warped , and I -- but , oh ! I will win the Scholarship", "context_len": 99}}}, "compass": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": "greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we will set over you guards to guard you ; but do not thou let slip this great honour , for that it hath fallen to", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 90, "context": "He used also to go to the market of the goldsmiths and the market of the jewellers , and there he would sit and look upon the different kinds of jewels and see them bought and sold ; whereby he became aware that the fruits of the trees , wherewith he had filled the purses , [ 318 ] whenas he was in the treasure , were neither glass nor crystal , but jewels , and knew that he had happened upon great wealth , such as kings might nowise compass", "context_len": 91}}}, "palms": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 79, "context": "`` To school ! '' said the little girl , `` to learn more -- to know how to speak proper ! Oh , oh , I 'm mos ' too happy to live ! Hank Dillson , I think you 're the mos ' beautiful man that was ever made ! '' and , dropping her sugar-bowl on the shelf , she seized a hand of the ex-creamery shark , and warmly pressed it between her little lean palms", "context_len": 80}}}, "fudge": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Hank , in some embarrassment , murmured , `` Oh , fudge , I 'm not as good as the next one", "context_len": 22}}}, "million": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 63, "context": "'Oh , I say , look here , you know , ' cried the boy , ruffling up his chestnut locks with both hands , which was a way he had when upset , 'if you go out to Australia , I shall come too ! I could persuade aunt to go in a jiffey -- the doctor said she 'd be a million times better in a drier climate -- and we 'd take the farm next to yours", "context_len": 80}}}, "luxuriously": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 43, "context": "It must have been about four o'clock in the afternoon , judging from the position of the sun , when I awoke to find Ama crouching over the fire , busily preparing another meal which -- even as I rose and stretched myself luxuriously , feeling immeasurably refreshed and invigorated by my long sleep -- she pronounced ready", "context_len": 58}}}, "mat": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 68, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "vista": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "The sandals were anything but comfortable , and her feet hurt dreadfully on the stones , while every gorse-bush she passed seemed to be stretching out spiky fingers to scratch her bare legs ; she was tired after her morning 's adventures on the moors , and the eight miles seemed to lengthen out to an interminable vista , in spite of the way being downhill ; sundry bumps and bruises , which she had never noticed at the time , began to ache now , and the bee-stings on her neck smarted , until she hardly knew how to bear the pain", "context_len": 103}}}, "blessings": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 28, "context": "Tell me an thou have a want , that I may accomplish it unto thee ; '' whereupon she kissed the earth a second time and called down blessings upon him , then answered , `` Ay , O King of the Age , as thy head liveth , I have indeed a want ; but before all things do thou give me thine assurance , [ 362 ] so I may make bold to prefer my suit to the hearing of our lord the Sultan , for that belike Thy Grace will find it a strange one", "context_len": 98}}}, "ugliest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "When you make a little money will you let me write to the lady-boards for another orphan , -- the ugliest little orphan they 've got , -- worse than me , if it 's not unpossible", "context_len": 37}}}, "wrinkled": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 56, "context": "They were of all ages , from old , withered men to children of six or seven , but they were all alike grave and unsmiling ; the old men were not a whit more dignified than the children , and the children did not enter into their sport with more zeal and ardor than the wrinkled sages who shared it", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 45, "context": "It rolled over on its side and lay there , and there was a scuffling and a couple of black legs were to be seen kicking themselves loose , and a pair of black arms twisting themselves from under it , and a little black wrinkled face and head with cunning , bright eyes pushed themselves out , and the minute Barty saw them he shouted aloud with glee :", "context_len": 70}}}, "bald": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "He was a tall man , considerably over six feet in height , very lanky and thin , with a somewhat German cast of face , pale-blue eyes , a bald forehead , hair slightly inclined to be sandy , an ugly mouth with broken teeth , and a long moustache which , with all his efforts , did not conceal this defect", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "His history ran somewhat as follows , though it sounds bald enough without the expletives with which he garnished it , growing somewhat less shy of his Major 's nephew as he went on", "context_len": 34}}}, "fulfilled": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 67, "context": "`` Then will you trust me because your mother does ? will you believe that when I come back I shall be in a position to set all her fears and yours also absolutely at rest ? I am certain of this , I go away with a hope which I dare not express more fully ; I shall come back trusting that that hope may be fulfilled in all its magnificence for myself", "context_len": 74}}}, "caress": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Now , I 'll go take something , '' she said , demurely , and , with a last caress , `` you darlin ' ole grampa -- I could eat you -- Lord , give me a thankful heart for all these mercies , '' then , reverently bending her head over her plate , she took up her knife and fork with a long and happy sigh", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "A bright , eager boy , whose brown hands one would think fitter to hold a top than to caress the hilt of his new sword ; a boy young enough to be proud of his position , proud of his soldier 's dress ; to whom life was a very interesting but a very simple matter", "context_len": 57}}}, "mercies": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` Now , I 'll go take something , '' she said , demurely , and , with a last caress , `` you darlin ' ole grampa -- I could eat you -- Lord , give me a thankful heart for all these mercies , '' then , reverently bending her head over her plate , she took up her knife and fork with a long and happy sigh", "context_len": 70}}}, "author": {"freq": 98, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 98, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then ( says our author ) , the geomant proceeds to examine the sixteen figures thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it", "context_len": 128}}}, "neighbors": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 48, "context": "How the Mummy would enjoy it , how she would revel in the good food and the lovely house ! What a red-letter day it would be to her all her life , for all the rest of her years ! How Sukey and Ann Pratt and the neighbors down at Dawlish would respect her for evermore ! And doubtless the Mummy 's dress might be managed , and -- but what about Aunt Susan ? Would Aunt Susan ever forgive her ? She dared not run the risk of her displeasure ; too much depended on keeping her in a good humor", "context_len": 103}}}, "sequel": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 2, "context": "As a sequel to his wonderful story of the pursuit of the Alibamu by the Creeks and the final peaceable settling down of both , Milfort adds some points on the early doings and warrings of the Creeks , which had occurred but a limited number of years before his stay in the tribe , and were recounted to him by one of the m\u00edkos from their memorial beads , like the legendary migration :", "context_len": 75}}}, "teaches": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 4, "context": "It is instinct which teaches Toby to know a man or a boy who is to be trusted from one who is not ; which makes him keep Susie from creeping into danger when he is told to take care of her", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Miss Patrice teaches Orphy to sing the chants and anthems in the service of the little church where he was baptized , and with her voice new airs for his violin", "context_len": 31}}}, "literature": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 26, "context": "I think , too , that it should be a flavor , a spice , a sweet , a delicate relish in the high banquet of literature , and never a chief dish ; and I should not know how to defend my boy for trying to make long poems of his own at the very time when he found it so hard to read other people 's long poems", "context_len": 70}}}, "magic": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 20, "context": "Before he could fit the arrow to the string a hideous chorus of shouts rent the air , and like magic the jungle was filled with men , men with the ferocious faces of demons , who rushed upon him , shouting and brandishing their sharp , murderous knives above their heads", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 127, "context": "the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? Hast thou in all thy days seen aught like this ? Are there found with the greatest of the kings of the world riches and gold and jewels such as these we see in this palace ? '' `` O my lord the King , '' answered the Vizier , `` this is a thing beyond the competence of a king of the sons of Adam , nor might all the people of the earth together avail to build a palace like this ; nay , there are no craftsmen living able to do work like this , except it be , as I said to Thy Grace , by might of magic", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Dear , dear , is it a dream ? '' said Florence ; `` am I the same girl ? What magic that Scholarship has worked ! ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "torments": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The story of a household elf who torments the cook and gardener , but is a constant joy and delight to the children", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "Their bodies wasted and their strength sapped by years of deprivation and torments at the hands of Jojasta could not throw off the fatigue as readily as the healthy jungle lad , whose veins were pulsing with vitality", "context_len": 38}}}, "gardener": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 51, "context": "The Middletons had a fine collection of orchids and rare plants , which were much admired by the young visitors , though simple Peggy could not help thinking they were not half so pretty as the roses and lilies in the old garden at home , and certainly the grand Scotch gardener was not nearly so nice as David or Joe , for he seemed quite to resent their presence and followed them about grimly , for fear they should disturb anything , or pluck any of the fruit or flowers", "context_len": 91}}}, "source": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 60, "context": "But Percy was popular , Seabrooke was not ; and even the masters were inclined to believe that the latter must have been careless and forgetful and mislaid the money , while believing he had put it in the place he indicated , and presently -- no one knew exactly how it started or could trace the rumor to its source -- presently it began to be bruited about among the boys that Seabrooke was keeping it for his own use and had never intended to return it to Percy , and was now making him his scape-goat", "context_len": 98}}}, "justly": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 126, "context": "my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction , you shall meet with the fate you so justly deserve", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Might not many a one justly ask , was not her life a failure ? And the answer , based on the experience and results of what her life and death accomplished , is No -- emphatically No !", "context_len": 39}}}, "theme": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 67, "context": "`` Before you begin your duties this afternoon , young ladies , '' she said , `` I have received a communication from Sir John , and as you are all interested in the Scholarship , which may be offered another year to some further girls of Cherry Court School , I may as well say that I have just received a letter from him suggesting the theme for the essay", "context_len": 71}}}, "description": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 127, "context": "upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant , of course , that I was to die by some exquisite refinement of torture , the nature of which would probably be too dreadful for description", "context_len": 128}}}, "relating": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` Further particulars with regard to the competition I will let you know in a few days , but I may as well say now that most of the examination will be viv\u00e2 voce , and will consist of eight questions relating to the study of the French language , eight questions on the study of the German tongue , eight mathematical questions , eight arithmetical questions , eight questions on English History , and eight on English Literature", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Naturally enough , having heard that I had been a captive among the savages , they insisted upon my relating to them my adventures ; and this inaugurated an evening of yarn-spinning in the forecastle , the incidents related having reference for the most part to the slave-trade", "context_len": 48}}}, "hero": {"freq": 135, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 135, "word_index": 107, "context": "in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war , and praising Little Van , whom he had hitherto despised as the `` Fox of Kinderhook", "context_len": 128}}}, "underground": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "At one time the lift had been used for bringing up machine-guns that were stored deep underground in anticipation of a heavy bombardment of the British guns", "context_len": 27}}}, "reliant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "A fascinating story of the underground adventures of a sturdy , reliant American boy among the elves and gnomes", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "It was easy to see that they wanted to take care of him , but , for his own part , he felt a kind of guardian care for the welfare of the two `` Pilgrims , '' and he was very glad of the chance to be with them at a time when Captain Bracewell was so pitifully unlike his reliant self", "context_len": 63}}}, "patriotic": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}}}, "historical": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 57, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}}}, "governor": {"freq": 75, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 74, "word_index": 16, "context": "When I went to him again today , he said that he had learnt from the governor of the prison and from the head mason that you were almost beyond price , that you had been raised to the position of superintendent of the slaves employed in the building of his palace , and that you were a man of such skill that he would not part with you at any price until the work was finished", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Each general and governor seized the power which Alexander 's death left in his hands , and endeavored to defend himself in the possession of it against the others", "context_len": 29}}}, "heroine": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 16, "context": "Do I make too much of a little girl who , after all , was no heroine , but who tried her honest best , like many another , to fear God , tell the truth , and love her neighbour as herself ? If so , my love for her bright ways and warm heart must be my excuse , for I have told Peggy 's story just as it happened", "context_len": 72}}}, "renders": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "It does not decrease my chances , for , as I know , there are no chances ; but it renders it more easy for me to know that it is so", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 92, "context": "Beyond the hedge lies a pasture meadow , where a flock of sheep are grazing , and encircling the meadow another hedge , less closely clipped , and so making bold to riot here and there in a snowy wealth of hawthorn blossom , A fine Alderney cow , with coat as well cared for as the gray mare 's in the stable , is also enjoying the sweet grass of the meadow , and the shining milk , pans ranged beneath the kitchen window bear witness to the generous service she renders", "context_len": 93}}}, "gifted": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 9, "context": "And now we were to welcome the well-known and gifted authoress into our house ! This was a privilege ; and earnestly did we look forward to the pleasure of receiving her ; though at the same time we were perhaps conscious of a slight shadow of doubt crossing our minds , as to how far one of Miss Tucker \u2019 s age would be able to accommodate herself to the new surroundings , and bear the trials incident to life and work in a tropical climate", "context_len": 87}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 107, "context": "`` Accordingly he arose and mounting , [ set forth ] , whilst the mamelukes rode before him and after him , ( extolled be the perfection of the Lord who created them with [ 459 ] that which clothed them of beauty and grace ! ) , strewing gold upon the folk before their lord Alaeddin , who overpassed them all of his grace and goodliness , and ask thou not of kings ' sons , [ 460 ] extolled be the perfection of the Giver , the Eternal ! Now all this was of the virtue of the wonderful lamp , [ 461 ] which gifted whoso possessed it with goodliness and grace and wealth and wisdom", "context_len": 119}}}, "significance": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 38, "context": "When I persuaded Miss Jane to take you , I could n't foresee what hard luck I was going to strike , could I ? '' As he paused he caught Jane 's eye upon him in a significance which he did not understand", "context_len": 44}}}, "ewing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "EWING", "context_len": 1}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ewing 's beautiful story , graphically describing little Leonard 's fortunes and trials , and his heroic self-mastery at the last", "context_len": 21}}}, "realms": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 85, "context": "Yet there was a wondrous beauty still lingering over them ; they seemed etherialised -- as if an angel 's smile had last stirred their lines , when the spirit went forth , and left its imprint of wonder , joy , and awe thereon ; and Alfgar instinctively turned from them to the blue depths of heaven above , where a few stars were visible , although dimmed by the moonlight ; and he seemed to trace his beloved Bertric 's passage to the realms of bliss", "context_len": 88}}}, "unreality": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "Missionary life is no more one of unbroken success , even at its best , than any other kind of hard-working life , with a high aim before it ; and to present it as such , by omitting to describe failure side by side with success , would -- and often does -- produce only a sense of unreality", "context_len": 60}}}, "stevenson": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "STEVENSON", "context_len": 1}}}, "classic": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 94, "context": "The big drawing-room had been shut up for many years ; the Queen-Anne chairs and china-cabinets were swathed in wrappers , and the ornaments put away in boxes ; but sometimes the children would steal in and open the shutters to look at the portraits which hung upon the oak-panelled walls -- stately gentlemen with wigs and lace frills , whose eyes seemed to follow you about the room ; haughty dames with powdered hair and patches ; stiff little girls in hoops and mittens , and pretty young ladies attired as shepherdesses or classic goddesses , with cupids and nymphs in the background", "context_len": 104}}}, "undoubtedly": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 2, "context": "Yes , undoubtedly , whether we were the cause or not , things seemed to be flourishing , and mother enjoyed her life ; but one evening towards the end of June she began to talk of old times , of the Duchess , and the friends she knew in Mayfair , and then quite quietly her conversation turned to a subject ever I believe near her heart , James Randolph and his friendship for her", "context_len": 76}}}, "volumes": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 54, "context": "She was teeming with life and energy ; -- whether it were over her favourite chess , when she would wait patiently but eagerly , thinking out each move ; or enjoying the small-talk of society , watching faces and reading characters , to treasure them up for painting in one of her forthcoming volumes ; or teaching a niece the beauties of sound and thought in the Italian of Dante ; or playing at some game of thought with young people ; or reading aloud one of her two favourite dearly-loved and untiringly-studied authors , Shakespeare and Boswell \u2019 s Life of Johnson", "context_len": 104}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Several apprentices to booksellers with whom he made friends obligingly loaned him volumes from their masters \u2019 shelves", "context_len": 18}}}, "copies": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 0, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "dedication": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Next came , in the year 1840 , The Fatal Vow ; a Tragedy in Three Acts ; on the title-page of which is found a dedication -- To Jane Tucker ; the Mother who in the bloom of youth and beauty devoted herself to her children , and whose tender care can never by them be repaid", "context_len": 58}}}, "furnishes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "The geological formation of the land is such that it furnishes few springs of water , and no streams , and in that climate it seldom or never rains", "context_len": 29}}}, "dedicate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` I dedicate this book , made for the amusement of the boys who may read it , to the memory of one boy , who would have enjoyed as much as Peterkin the plays of the Poetry Party", "context_len": 39}}}, "deals": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Since Benjamin liked to read , what would he think of being a printer a trade that deals with pamphlets , books , everything made with words ? The idea appealed to Benjamin , though he balked when he learned he would be apprenticed to his brother until he was twenty", "context_len": 51}}}, "fisher": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The story deals with a sturdy American fisher lad during the war of 1812", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Fisher as well as Ben to comfort her in the least for her dreadful disappointment that Ben was not going to accept a long and thorough education at Mr", "context_len": 29}}}, "natures": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "And do you think that people are to be all massed in heaven , losing their various identities , their differing tastes , their separate natures ? Going from this lower world so full of its adaptations , where colour and form take on a thousand changes , and life and pursuit can be varied almost at will , to a mere dead level of perfect felicity ? To leave earth where no two things are alike , and go to heaven to find no two different ! The Lord 's preparations mean more than that", "context_len": 96}}}, "admirers": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 46, "context": "Nancy 's hat was a conspicuous object among the smart throng , for Lilian , after one peep at the fair , had charitably kept house herself , and allowed her hand-maiden to take a holiday , and the damsel seemed to have collected so many admirers that the luckless Sally was entirely eclipsed", "context_len": 54}}}, "dunn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "DUNN", "context_len": 1}}}, "intellectually": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "It seemed useless to delay longer , as he was fully prepared both intellectually and spiritually , nay , has been so for some time , only the tragic event which deprived him of his Danish kinsfolk had distracted him for a time from spiritual things", "context_len": 46}}}, "meets": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 39, "context": "I have told you all this about Hayti , because I thought that , like myself , you would be pleased to know what really became of the characters in Miss Martineau \u2019 s Romance , and one seldom meets with a book which throws any light upon such an out-of-the-way subject", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "But what can we do ? It is better for him to be idle here with us , it seems to me , than in some ordinary lodging-house , where things , to be sure , are not by any means luxurious , but where a boy who is not at work meets with so many temptations", "context_len": 57}}}, "voyage": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 78, "context": "Again , in 1889 , when a dear friend of mine , Pastor and Teacher in the United States of North America , with whom I had come out to India in 1869 , had decided to return to India as a Missionary , in order to join and to help me in the multifarious work at Clarkabad , and he found that the money in hand was insufficient to pay for his and his family \u2019 s voyage from Germany , and Miss Tucker heard of it , she immediately sent me 100 , with the direction to forward that sum to him , on condition that he had not left Germany again for America", "context_len": 116}}}, "bolton": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "BOLTON", "context_len": 1}}}, "reproductions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Fully illustrated with many reproductions from original photographs", "context_len": 8}}}, "quarto": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": ", oblong quarto , cloth decorative $ 2", "context_len": 8}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": ", large quarto $ 2", "context_len": 5}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Like the Koran , it is very long , -- I think more than 600 quarto pages , -- and with an immense deal of repetition in it", "context_len": 28}}}, "canine": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "This book of the dog and his friends does for the canine member of the household what Helen M", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Back and forth the huge front swayed until in a minute or two it remained pointed directly at the boulder ! It suggested an immense canine that had flushed its game", "context_len": 31}}}, "feline": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Winslow 's book , `` Concerning Cats , '' did for the feline", "context_len": 13}}}, "cares": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 3, "context": "Dodgson no longer cares to see as much of the little people as formerly ; in fact , he rather runs away from them when they seek him out ; and when he received Dorothy 's letter , what did he do but write her that he was very sorry to say that he would not be at home on the afternoon in question , but that if it would be any pleasure to her little friends to see his rooms , she might bring them there and welcome , and that he would leave some old photographs that he thought would interest them ready to her hand in a portfolio on the writing-table", "context_len": 114}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 102, "context": "None could look back , not even to behold If those beloved were nigh ; one thrilling thought Rapt all the multitude , -- Can HE be near ? Then cries of terror rose -- I scarcely heard ; And buildings shook and rocked , and crashing fell , -- I scarcely marked their fall ; the trembling ground Rose like the billowy sea , -- I scarcely felt The motion ; such intensity of hope -- Joy -- expectation -- flooded all my soul ; A tide of living light , o \u2019 erwhelming all The hopes and fears , the cares and woes of earth", "context_len": 107}}}, "includes": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 3, "context": "This table however includes , with a few exceptions , only places situated near Apalachicola river ( east and west of it ) , in Alabama , Georgia and Florida ; the list was probably made at a time when Florida was still under Spanish domination , which accounts for the fact that the county names are not added to the localities", "context_len": 62}}}, "felicitous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Bolton 's book is a less felicitous choice than that of its predecessor ; while the author 's well-known ability as a writer and lecturer , as well as her sympathy with her subject , are a sufficient guarantee of a happy treatment", "context_len": 43}}}, "choice": {"freq": 86, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 86, "word_index": 82, "context": "We are all just now in a state of indignation about your pork ! Don \u2019 t suppose that it is any fault in the pork ; on the contrary , it is acknowledged to be the most refined pork ever known ; and Mother says that if she shut her eyes , she would not know that she was not eating chicken ! ! We had a beautiful roast of it one day at luncheon ; and Mother cut off a choice bit , to be reserved for our table , cold , while the servants were indulged with the rest of that joint", "context_len": 105}}}, "predecessor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Bolton 's book is a less felicitous choice than that of its predecessor ; while the author 's well-known ability as a writer and lecturer , as well as her sympathy with her subject , are a sufficient guarantee of a happy treatment", "context_len": 43}}}, "lecturer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "The members seemed to take copious notes in pocket-books , and asked occasional questions , one literary-looking lady , in spectacles and a large black hat with nodding feathers , being particularly insistent , and volunteering so much information that she threatened to usurp the place of the lecturer , and had to be gently suppressed , while an old gentleman distributed pamphlets broadcast , declaring he had had them specially printed for the occasion", "context_len": 75}}}, "guarantee": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "Bolton 's book is a less felicitous choice than that of its predecessor ; while the author 's well-known ability as a writer and lecturer , as well as her sympathy with her subject , are a sufficient guarantee of a happy treatment", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "I 'll guarantee to introduce you to him , if he 's at home , '' went on Mr", "context_len": 19}}}, "treatment": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 66, "context": "it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached L'Esperance , and the extraordinary story I had heard while aboard her", "context_len": 128}}}, "rosamond": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Rosamond and Rosalind are the hero and heroine of many happy adventures in town and on their grandfather 's farm ; and the happy listeners to their story will unconsciously absorb a vast amount of interesting knowledge of birds , animals , and flowers , just the things about which the curiosity of children from four to twelve years old is most insatiable", "context_len": 63}}}, "frontispiece": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "A steel-plate frontispiece , of course , in which will be introduced a number of winged mice in a variety of positions , -- a very clever thing , I can assure you ; and then wood-cuts , -- I have the honour of being intrusted with the designs for them", "context_len": 51}}}, "listeners": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 26, "context": "At Pensford the rabble made a bull savage , and then tried to make it attack his congregation ; at Whitechapel they drove cows among the listeners and threw stones , one of which hit Wesley between the eyes ; but after he had wiped away the blood he went on with his address , telling the people that `` God hath not given us the spirit of fear ''", "context_len": 70}}}, "unconsciously": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 72, "context": "It 's just mamma 's own self , that 's what it is , interrupted Marie-Celeste , since her father seemed to be at a loss for a word , and she put her two arms around her mother 's neck , as much as to say , Is n't a mother like mine the darlingest thing ? and then a little fellow , who did n't have any mother , quite unconsciously to himself , drew a great deep sigh , and Mrs", "context_len": 84}}}, "absorb": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 47, "context": "Franklin thought in terms of the practical value of this discovery : white clothes would be more suitable than black ones in a hot climate ; summer hats should be white to repel the heat and prevent sunstroke ; fruit walls , if painted black , could absorb enough of the sun \u2019 s heat to stay warm at night , thereby helping to preserve the fruit from frost", "context_len": 69}}}, "vast": {"freq": 126, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 123, "word_index": 60, "context": "It was growing quite dark before Father returned , and I think , though neither of them would have confessed it , the children were both rather relieved to hear his cheery whistle outside the door , for it was just a little eerie sitting by the peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "It was reported here that the Genoese galleys were all laid up , and it was thought that they would be able to sweep the seas without opposition , and to bring home vast spoil and many captives , both from the ships they took and from many of the villages and small towns of the coast", "context_len": 57}}}, "amount": {"freq": 141, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 126, "word_index": 75, "context": "Here and there Lilian ran , hunting in all Peggy 's favourite haunts -- now peeping into a hollow yew-tree , now peering at the top of a ladder , now rummaging in the tool-shed , then back through the sand-quarry into the stack-yard , where there was a very good chance that the young lady might be hidden away in some snug little hole among the hay ; but though Lilian got a tolerable amount of hay-seed into her hair , her efforts were fruitless , and she was just turning away , hot and out of breath , to give up the useless search , when the sound of a low , chuckling laugh attracted her to the barn", "context_len": 121}, "VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` I know , but I wish she could : Papa said I need n't worry about it , as the mortgage was not so very big , and the money they had to pay on it every year did not amount to such a great deal , but I know from what Miss Eloise said that she would like it to be paid ; she said she would feel ever so much more comfortable", "context_len": 75}}}, "insatiable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "Rosamond and Rosalind are the hero and heroine of many happy adventures in town and on their grandfather 's farm ; and the happy listeners to their story will unconsciously absorb a vast amount of interesting knowledge of birds , animals , and flowers , just the things about which the curiosity of children from four to twelve years old is most insatiable", "context_len": 63}}}, "boon": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 21, "context": "Indeed , O King , my tongue faileth to thank thee [ as were behoving ] for the greatness of this boon , overpassing its competence , [ 465 ] wherewith thou hast favoured me , and I beseech Thy Grace to vouchsafe me ground , such as is meet , so I may build thereon a palace that shall be fit for the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 66}}}, "wade": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "across untied wade Jason brave party rained creek bridge shoe-strings invited", "context_len": 11}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 61, "context": "Look there , boys ! said John ; see the moon shining on that surf , when it rolls up , and then on the black rock when it goes back ; isn \u2019 t that handsome ? I \u2019 ve left my gun and powder-horn in the canoe , and now the tide has floated her off ; would you wade in ?", "context_len": 64}}}, "sayings": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 70, "context": "At another time nurse would have ordered them to shut their eyes and not speak another word ; but to-night she seemed to think it quite right and natural that they should be so very wide awake , and not only gave them an extra amount of petting and kissing , but told them stories of Uncle Ruthven 's pranks when he was a boy , and of his wonderful sayings and doings , till mamma , coming up and finding this going on , was half inclined to find fault with the old woman herself", "context_len": 96}}}, "toys": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 77, "context": "Granby made the most extraordinary flowers of red flannel and gilt paper , -- flowers whose likeness never grew in gardens or greenhouses of any known land ; had she not baked sugar cakes which were intended to represent men and women , pigs , horses , and cows ? Were not the branches looped with gay ribbons ? Did they not bear rosy-cheeked apples , an orange for each child , some cheap but much prized toys , and , better than all , several useful and greatly needed articles , which had been the gift of Mrs", "context_len": 99}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Oh , you darling , precious mamma , '' said Maggie , pausing in her capers to examine the pretty toys , `` they are just what we wanted", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Then , suddenly , as the tidal wave tops all other waves , came a gigantic burst of wind that bore great trees before it as though they had been toys , bending them , breaking them , uprooting them , whirling them about as in a fantastic dance", "context_len": 49}}}, "serve": {"freq": 104, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "The `` Little Japanese Cousin , '' with her toys in her wide sleeve and her tiny bag of paper handkerchiefs ; the `` Little Brown Cousin , '' in whose home the leaves of the breadfruit-tree serve for plates and the halves of the cocoanut shells for cups ; the `` Little Indian Cousin , '' who lives the free life of the forest , and the `` Little Russian Cousin , '' who dwells by the wintry Neva , are truly fascinating characters to the little cousins who will read about them", "context_len": 94}, "VERB": {"freq": 102, "word_index": 64, "context": "fairy tale ? 'T is a strange plight for such a tenderly-nurtured maid , for old Sir Gyles guarded her as the very apple of his eye ! And what state did not he keep , and Hunstanton Hall ! And with what a retinue did he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she", "context_len": 128}}}, "halves": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 36, "context": "Their voices were subdued lest they should reach the king 's ears ; but , respectful as was the bearing of all the members of the group by the fire , they clearly split into two halves : on the one hand , the officers of the escort who were teasing the boy , and on the other , a group of gentlemen , some wearing the conventional ribbons and laces of a cavalier , others in the rough cloth of country wear , stained with the mud of country lanes , while the master and mistress of the house moved from one guest to another , evidently nervous at the doubtful honour that such a royal visit had brought to their roof", "context_len": 123}}}, "cocoanut": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 5, "context": "He also brought Ben a cocoanut to keep liquor in ; the end of it , where the eyes are , was made in the shape of a negro \u2019 s face ; the two little round places , where we bore to let the milk out , serving the purpose of eyes , with eyebrows cut over them , and filled with some red matter ; in the mouth was a lead pipe to drink from ; large ears were also made , and a nose ; the figure looked somewhere between a monkey and a negro , funny enough , and was full of rum", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` I took my spy-glass and looked and saw that there really were some big creatures moving about among the cocoanut palm trees", "context_len": 23}}}, "dwells": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 75, "context": "The `` Little Japanese Cousin , '' with her toys in her wide sleeve and her tiny bag of paper handkerchiefs ; the `` Little Brown Cousin , '' in whose home the leaves of the breadfruit-tree serve for plates and the halves of the cocoanut shells for cups ; the `` Little Indian Cousin , '' who lives the free life of the forest , and the `` Little Russian Cousin , '' who dwells by the wintry Neva , are truly fascinating characters to the little cousins who will read about them", "context_len": 94}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "He thought this fine , too , but still , it seemed to him , in the narrow little world where a child dwells , that his father and his grandfather were about the only people there were who did not wish the Mexicans whipped , and he felt secretly guilty for them before the other boys", "context_len": 57}}}, "wintry": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 78, "context": "The `` Little Japanese Cousin , '' with her toys in her wide sleeve and her tiny bag of paper handkerchiefs ; the `` Little Brown Cousin , '' in whose home the leaves of the breadfruit-tree serve for plates and the halves of the cocoanut shells for cups ; the `` Little Indian Cousin , '' who lives the free life of the forest , and the `` Little Russian Cousin , '' who dwells by the wintry Neva , are truly fascinating characters to the little cousins who will read about them", "context_len": 94}}}, "classics": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Peggy , too , had begged to sit up a little later to listen , and I think she learned almost as much from the readings as from her lessons ; for our English classics are an education in themselves , and those who love them young rarely care to read much trash afterwards", "context_len": 54}}}, "announce": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "We shall issue ten new volumes in this well-known series of child classics , and announce three as follows :", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "She did not call to me , or announce her return in any way , but set to work to mow a circle of about ten feet in diameter in the long grass ; and then , having produced fire by rubbing two pieces of wood together , she proceeded very carefully to burn off the short grass left inside the circle , setting fire to it , allowing it to burn for a few seconds , and then beating it out again with a branch , in order that the fire might not spread and burn us out , to say nothing of betraying our presence by the smoke that it would raise", "context_len": 114}}}, "wheelwright": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "WHEELWRIGHT", "context_len": 1}}}, "la": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Shortly after , the Spanish captain Juan Pardo led an expedition ( 1566-67 ) through the countries along Savannah river , and the local names found in the report made of it by Juan de la Vandera ( 1569 ) also point to the presence of a people speaking Creek established on both sides of that river : [ 41 ] Ahoya `` two going '' ; Issa Cr", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 85, "context": "of the Cabinet des Fees ( printed in 1793 , though antedated 1788 and 1789 ) do not form the first edition of Chavis and Cazotte 's so-called Sequel , which was in 1793 added , by way of supplement , to the Cabinet des Fees , having been first published in 1788 ( two years after the completion-in thirty-seven volumes-of that great storehouse of supernatural fiction ) under the title of `` Les Veillees Persanes '' or `` Les Veillees du Sultan Schahriar avec la Sultane Scheherazade , histoires incroyables , amusantes et morales , traduites par M", "context_len": 99}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "262 : `` dans la direction du nord", "context_len": 8}}}, "previously": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 45, "context": "In due course she was adjudicated upon and condemned by the Mixed Commission ; but I did not remain at Sierra Leone for that to take place ; for upon our arrival we found that a packet had come in from England a few days previously bringing letters for me , acquainting me with the sad news of my father 's death and urging me to proceed home immediately to supervise the winding up of his affairs , and to assume the management of the very important property that he had left behind him", "context_len": 94}}}, "selections": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Edna followed her up into the attic and they found the book , took it down into the front room and began to make their selections and cut out paper dolls till it suddenly dawned upon Nettie that it was time for another meal", "context_len": 44}}}, "hatched": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "But our chickens are n't hatched , and you 'd better turn in , and thank God for all the blessings we have", "context_len": 23}}}, "oblong": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "The `` town-house '' was an oblong square cabin , roof `` eight feet pitch , '' sides and roof covered with pine-bark", "context_len": 23}}}, "decorative": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 13, "context": "I never knew a boy who loved flowers , or cared for their decorative qualities ; if any boy had gathered flowers the other boys would have laughed at him ; though boys gather every kind of thing that they think will be of the slightest use or profit", "context_len": 49}}}, "striking": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 58, "context": "These tales are narrations of the exploits and adventures of certain great warriors at the siege of Troy -- a siege which lasted ten years -- and they are written with so much beauty and force , they contain such admirable delineations of character , and such graphic and vivid descriptions of romantic adventures , and picturesque and striking scenes , that they have been admired in every age by all who have learned to understand the language in which they are written", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 39, "context": "Baulked of their prey , the mob still surged round us , and with a shout of : `` Let \u2019 em have the cat ! '' a great black object was sent flying towards us , and , striking my horse on the crupper , resolved itself into an enormous black cat , that spat and howled , digging its claws into the horse \u2019 s hide , and arching its back like the demon cat that is the reputed companion of every witch", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "There was a moment of vicious striking , and then the cadets gasped to see Williams give way", "context_len": 18}}}, "humor": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 102, "context": "How the Mummy would enjoy it , how she would revel in the good food and the lovely house ! What a red-letter day it would be to her all her life , for all the rest of her years ! How Sukey and Ann Pratt and the neighbors down at Dawlish would respect her for evermore ! And doubtless the Mummy 's dress might be managed , and -- but what about Aunt Susan ? Would Aunt Susan ever forgive her ? She dared not run the risk of her displeasure ; too much depended on keeping her in a good humor", "context_len": 103}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Nor will I waste words with a boy I picked off the streets ; no , not even to humor my own son 's fancies", "context_len": 26}}}, "color": {"freq": 112, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 111, "word_index": 10, "context": "Her face was pasty in complexion without a scrap of color in it , and her eyes were of too light a blue to redeem the general insipidity of her appearance ; but when she spoke that insipidity vanished , for her lips were very firm , and were apt to utter incisive words , and at such moments her pale blue eyes would flash with a light fire which was full of sarcasm , and might even rise to positive cruelty", "context_len": 82}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was Marie-Celeste 's turn to color up now , and she looked at Albert , considering for a moment in what way she should proceed to annihilate him , when Her Majesty happily put to rout all such revengeful intentions", "context_len": 41}}}, "distinct": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 59, "context": "With some show of hesitation the man surrendered the thing , and upon inspection I found it to be a reed of about a foot in length , with a mouthpiece shaped something like that of a whistle , and with four small holes drilled in the length of the tube , whereby an expert performer might produce seven distinct tones ; but the tones were not consecutive , and the instrument was altogether a very poor and inefficient affair", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Then she repeated -- 'Where are you going , my pretty maid ? ' and she had a little milk pail on her arm , and she managed to keep the two parts wonderfully distinct -- it was remarkable in a child not three years old , and when she said -- 'Then I wo n't marry you , my pretty maid ' and answered so pertly -- 'Nobody asked you , sir , she said , ' it would have done credit to an exhibition", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was made distinct from the rest of the square by one or two low embankments or terraces ; in its centre stood , on a low circular mound , a four-sided pole or pillar , sometimes forty feet high", "context_len": 40}}}, "successful": {"freq": 98, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 98, "word_index": 5, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}}}, "lithographic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The color illustrations are a distinct and successful departure from the old-fashioned lithographic work hitherto invariably used for children 's books", "context_len": 21}}}, "hitherto": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 120, "context": "in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war , and praising Little Van , whom he had hitherto despised as the `` Fox of Kinderhook", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Bill Mosely betrayed a little too much interest when he asked this question , and the Chinaman , hitherto unsuspicious , became on his guard", "context_len": 25}, "VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 6, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}}}, "invariably": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 75, "context": "For the first few days of my captivity my brain was literally seething with schemes for effecting my escape , most of them wildly impossible , I admit ; but some there were that seemed to promise just a ghost of a chance of success -- until I attempted to put them into effect , when the vigilance of my guards -- with the fear of crucifixion , head downward , before their eyes -- invariably baffled me", "context_len": 78}}}, "baring": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "BARING GOULD", "context_len": 2}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 0, "context": "Baring succeed in buying Anarkalli , he will allow our Mission to rent from him , on easy terms , that part of the house which we now occupy ( by we I mean myself ) , with the addition of the drawing-room and part at least of the grand dining-room", "context_len": 51}}}, "gilt": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 10, "context": "Granby made the most extraordinary flowers of red flannel and gilt paper , -- flowers whose likeness never grew in gardens or greenhouses of any known land ; had she not baked sugar cakes which were intended to represent men and women , pigs , horses , and cows ? Were not the branches looped with gay ribbons ? Did they not bear rosy-cheeked apples , an orange for each child , some cheap but much prized toys , and , better than all , several useful and greatly needed articles , which had been the gift of Mrs", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "It was an old-fashioned affair with a simple combination attachment , and so far as Bart could make out had suffered no damage beyond having its coat of lacquer and gilt lettering burned off", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "25 HYMNS OF THE NATIVITY , 18mo , gilt $ 1", "context_len": 11}}}, "continents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "This volume will prove a source of delight to the children of two continents , answering their always increasing demand for `` more fairy stories", "context_len": 25}}}, "answering": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 43, "context": "Should she give Dolly her own or not ? She wanted it very much herself ; but she had asked her Father in heaven to let her find some way to be kind to Lem and Dolly , and now was He not answering her prayer ? It had been very pleasant to think of sharing the delicious fruit with her own dear friends whom she loved so much , or even of giving a piece to Starr , who was always so kind and good to her ; but to give it all to this bad girl who had done so much cruel mischief to her and hers , was another thing", "context_len": 113}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "As he came now in full sight of it , there was something in the power and wild beauty of the rushing waters that struck an answering chord in his soul , causing his blood to run more swiftly and making his eyes kindle with delight", "context_len": 46}}}, "similar": {"freq": 83, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 33, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "tend": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 43, "context": "He knew , too , that if this were done it would create a strong sensation throughout the whole country , impressing every one with a sense of the energy and power of the army which he was conducting , and would thus tend to intimidate the enemy , and facilitate all future operations", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Archie insisted upon trying both the swing-boats and the merry-go-round , and supported the drooping Peggy ( who found the motion tend to sea-sickness ) with a firm arm , otherwise I fear she would have slipped off her prancing steed altogether", "context_len": 42}}}, "stimulate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "Averse as my Aunt ever was to any fuss being made about her , nothing would have reconciled her to the publication of a Biography , save the hope that its story might be used of God to stimulate others to consecrate their lives to the Service of Christ , whether in the Foreign or Home Mission Field", "context_len": 58}}}, "encourage": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 10, "context": "But alas ! there were no traditions of victory to encourage them ; only gloomy remembrances of defeat ; and , but for the stern call of duty which bade them , as men and Christians , go to the succour of their brethren , the majority would have preferred to remain at home and abide the worst , although they knew full well that submission utterly failed to mitigate the ferocious cruelty of their oppressors , who slew alike the innocent babe and the grey-haired grandsire", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahiba must know every lane , Climb ladder-like stairs , without fearing a sprain ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must speak very plain , Must rebuke and encourage , must teach and explain ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must grasp well the rein ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must not look for gain , Though doctoring sick folk , like Jenner or Quain", "context_len": 66}}}, "gould": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "GOULD", "context_len": 1}}}, "adjutant": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "The tales are supposed to be told by an old adjutant stork in the Zoological Gardens to the assembled birds located there , and they deal with legendary and folk-lore stories of the origins of various creatures , mostly birds , and their characteristics", "context_len": 44}}}, "stork": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The tales are supposed to be told by an old adjutant stork in the Zoological Gardens to the assembled birds located there , and they deal with legendary and folk-lore stories of the origins of various creatures , mostly birds , and their characteristics", "context_len": 44}}}, "located": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 18, "context": "Through the blinding , hurrying flakes she could see twinkling lights here and there , and presently she located the piece of woods just beyond her own home , but it was far to the left , and she realized that she had turned into a by-road instead of keeping to the main one", "context_len": 54}}}, "legendary": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 72, "context": "As a sequel to his wonderful story of the pursuit of the Alibamu by the Creeks and the final peaceable settling down of both , Milfort adds some points on the early doings and warrings of the Creeks , which had occurred but a limited number of years before his stay in the tribe , and were recounted to him by one of the m\u00edkos from their memorial beads , like the legendary migration :", "context_len": 75}}}, "origins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The tales are supposed to be told by an old adjutant stork in the Zoological Gardens to the assembled birds located there , and they deal with legendary and folk-lore stories of the origins of various creatures , mostly birds , and their characteristics", "context_len": 44}}}, "characteristics": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 63, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}}}, "singular": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 63, "context": "It is not the mob of Genoa only who regard the services that you have rendered as extraordinary , but it is the opinion of the councillors and authorities of the Republic , and of those who , like myself , have borne our share in warfare , that not only is the service great , but that it is due to the singular ability with which you , in command of only a single galley , have wholly destroyed or captured the fleet that threatened our commerce", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "3d person , \u00eds\u00e4s I take , when the object stands in the singular ) ; \u00edsi , \u00edssi taker , holder", "context_len": 22}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` It certainly has been intended for you , however singular the way in which it has been conveyed to you , or however disagreeable the manner of the messenger", "context_len": 30}}}, "awaken": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 83, "context": "He acted , however , himself , in this high position , with great energy and with complete success ; and , at the same time , with all that modesty of deportment , and that delicate consideration for the officers under him -- who , though inferior in rank , were yet his superiors in age and experience -- which his position rendered proper , but which few persons so young as he would have manifested in circumstances so well calculated to awaken the feelings of vanity and elation", "context_len": 90}}}, "familiar": {"freq": 104, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 104, "word_index": 58, "context": "The author dedicates this tale to his brother , engaged , like himself , in that most responsible task , the education of youth , in memory of those happy days when they pored together in rapturous delight over old legend or romantic lore in their father 's home at that very Clifton ( now Clifton Hampden ) familiar to hearers or readers of the tale as the home of Herstan , and the scene of the heroic defence of the English dwelling against the Danes", "context_len": 86}}}, "humblest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Thus the humblest coasters and the largest merchant craft go alike in fear of them , and I would that the sultan and Egypt and your Order would for two or three years put aside their differences , and confine their efforts to sweeping the seas of these pests , to storming their strongholds , and to inflicting such punishment upon them as that , for a very long time to come , peaceful merchants might carry on their trade without fear", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "No doubt she had enough without it , but there were circumstances of indignity or patronage attending the gift which were recognized in my boy 's home , and which helped afterwards to make him doubtful of all giving , except the humblest , and restive with a world in which there need be any giving at all", "context_len": 58}}}, "confidently": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 66, "context": "He was furious with himself for having fallen into the trap ; if he had , as he said to himself , lain off the beach in the boat , and questioned the supposed shipwrecked sailors , their inability to reply to him would have at once put him on his guard ; as it was , he had walked into the snare as carelessly and confidently as a child might have done", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "Irene -- who never before in all her life had any creature to look up to her , who was looked down upon with terror and shunned by her fellow-creatures , with the exception of Rosamund , who ruled her , although with the weapons of love -- felt an altogether new sensation now as the little creature , not so old as herself , clung to her confidently", "context_len": 69}}}, "standard": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "This year we still further increase this list , which has become standard throughout the country , by adding the ever-popular Green Mountain Boys and four volumes of Oliver Optic , All Over the World Library , especially timely books in view of the present interest in Asiatic matters", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 23, "context": "We shall not this voyage have the encouragement and confidence inspired by the presence of those who have long and valiantly borne the standard of the Order ; but , on the other hand , we have to show that we are worthy of the confidence reposed in us , and that the young knights of the Order can be trusted to emulate the deeds of those who have rendered the name of the Hospitallers a terror to the infidel", "context_len": 80}}}, "desired": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 33, "context": "To be a Christ Church Senior , with plenty of money and plenty of friends and a head that easily mastered enough learning to make a good showing , left little to be desired , especially when already endowed with a handsome face and a physique that every man envied -- at least , so thought Theodore Harris , and so thought and affirmed the half score of intimate friends who enjoyed many of the good things of this life through his bounty", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "The most desired kind of cap was a flat one of blue broadcloth , with a patent-leather peak , and a removable cover of oil-cloth , silk if you were rich , cotton if you were poor ; when you had pulled the top of such a cap over on one side , you were dressed for conquest , especially if you wore your hair long", "context_len": 66}}}, "john": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "JOHN", "context_len": 1}}}, "pages": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 31, "context": "What patient fingers , she wondered , had toiled over these in days gone by , working with paint-pots and palette of gold to put the glory of paradise on his pages ? Had the world altered much in all these years ? And how little did the old artist think that his work would be found and marvelled at when he and his order were alike forgotten , and the very Abbey where he had lived and laboured had long since crumbled away !", "context_len": 85}}}, "period": {"freq": 92, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 92, "word_index": 80, "context": "Alfred , her eldest son -- for Edmund was the offspring of an early amour of the king -- was on the other side of the bed , a well-made youth , combining in his features the haughty bearing of his Norman maternal ancestors with the English traits of his father ; but now his expression was one of distress and anxiety , which was yet more deeply shared by his younger brother , Edward , who even at this period manifested that strong sense of religious obligation and that early devotion which in later years caused him to be numbered amongst canonised saints", "context_len": 104}}}, "colonial": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "Now my poor old dad 's gone , and my mother 's married again , and the boys all trading off on their own account , I do n't want to go back to Colorado ; but I like colonial life , and farming would be a lot jollier than school , any way , for the fellows in my house are awful Johnnies , they can talk of nothing but games , and laugh at one for a crank if one tries to make things", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 7, "context": "There are few more fascinating phases of colonial history than that which tells the wanderings and adventures of the two judges who , because they sat in judgment over that royal criminal , Charles the First of England , were hunted out of England into hiding in New England , and there remained , a mystery and fugitives , in their celebrated cave in New Haven Colony", "context_len": 67}}}, "extension": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 7, "context": "a place of passage , but by extension a porch , a gallery , an opening , here ( and here only ) used by synecdoche for the oriel itself ) , and the famous roe 's egg is proposed to be suspended from `` the dome ( cubbeh ) of the upper chamber '' ( el keszr el faucaniyy ) , thus showing that the latter was crowned with a dome or cupola", "context_len": 74}}}, "heroes": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 57, "context": "It is true that Alexander 's soldiers , about to leave their homes to go to another quarter of the globe , and into scenes of danger and death from which it was very improbable that many of them would ever return , had no other celestial protection to look up to than the spirits of ancient heroes , who , they imagined , had , somehow or other , found their final home in a sort of heaven among the summits of the mountains , where they reigned , in some sense , over human affairs ; but this , small as it seems to us , was a great deal to them", "context_len": 114}}}, "ships": {"freq": 258, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 249, "word_index": 80, "context": "Many will , we may calculate , jump overboard and swim ashore rather than face us , and we shall be able , at any rate , to capture three or four of their craft , for , as they come out , one by one , we can all close round them ; and with nearly fifty knights , ninety released captives , burning for vengeance , and some fifty or sixty fishermen , for those from the fire ships will , of course , join us -- we shall make short work of them , and may even hope to entirely destroy their fleet", "context_len": 106}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Well , sir , if 't was a matter of working my passage , I 'd be bound to go where there would be ships trading the right way ; but if I was with a gentleman as would oblige me with a loan , 't is easy to take ship from Harwich , or find one lying in Yarmouth Roads that would carry us part way , and then we could take passage from some French or Spanish port", "context_len": 81}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "The officers held a consultation -- Sir George Ascue was not one of them ; whether he was killed or wounded I did not at that time know -- and the opinion of the council was that if we were grappled by the fire ships our fate would no doubt be a glorious one , but of little use to His Majesty the King", "context_len": 64}}}, "st": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 21, "context": "Hiram , and the constable turned to his comrade , I \u2019 ll git in with \u2019 em , so \u2019 st to make sure they don \u2019 t run , then you take down the chain , an \u2019 git in , too", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Not ye ? Ah , now harken ! Know \u2019 st Jim Harker , the court-leet man and king \u2019 s officer at Wareham ? ''", "context_len": 27}}}, "desirable": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 72, "context": "Instructions were therefore given to the two knights that , while Georges was to be treated with all courtesy , he was to be strictly watched , though in such a manner that he should be in ignorance of it , and that , whenever he turned his steps in the direction of those parts of the defences where fresh works had been recently added and preparations made of which it was desirable the Turks should be kept in ignorance , he was to be met , as if by accident , by one of the knights told off for the purpose , and his steps diverted in another direction", "context_len": 110}}}, "library": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 81, "context": "`` How are we to begin ? Shall we do penance like the old monks ? Do you know , Rosamund '' -- here Irene linked her thin , almost steel-like little hand inside Rosamund 's arm -- '' that I am a most voracious reader ? Father was a great collector of books , and when I am tired of frightening the servants , and terrifying Frosty , and annoying mother , I spend days at a time in his library swallowing down the contents of his books", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 24, "context": "Mark and Texas , it chanced , had gone out for a walk ; `` Parson '' Stanard had , wandered over to the library building to `` ascertain the extent of their geological literature , '' and to get some information , if possible , about a most interesting question which was just then troubling him", "context_len": 57}}}, "woolf": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Woolf", "context_len": 1}}}, "attraction": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 70, "context": "Belden to do aught but look and listen , and drop a word of assent now and then , when Marie-Celeste saw fit to impart her information in a somewhat interrogative form ; and in this way they went on from monument to monument , giving of course but a passing glance to many and stopping longest , by tacit agreement , at those which had some special charm or attraction for Marie-Celeste", "context_len": 73}}}, "colored": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 38, "context": "Then his master , who was a poet , would come and glance in the stable door and say , `` Lift up your eyes , boys -- there 's gold in the sky , '' and the colored boys would look and wonder and wish the gold would roll down and then their master would laugh in his pleasant Southern way , and say , `` Now it 's on the water -- now it 's on the land", "context_len": 80}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 54, "context": "None of these three even glanced at Rosamund Cunliffe and her friend ; but when Laura Everett and one or two other girls appeared , they did see the pair seated in a pew all alone at the end of the church , and Laura nodded with a bright glance at Rosamund , who colored with pleasure in reply", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Linemen were high up on poles arranging extra wires , and others , below , were passing up the colored bulbs , or pliers , and other tools needed by their mates on the high poles", "context_len": 36}}}, "binding": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 71, "context": "I do not for a moment deceive myself with the thought that , beyond the fact that her gift has made her feel an interest in me and my fate , she has any sentiment in the matter : probably , indeed , she looks back upon the gift as a foolish act of girlish enthusiasm that led her into making a promise that she now can not but find unpleasantly binding ; for it is but natural that among the young nobles of her own rank and country there must be some whom she would see with pleasure wearing her colours", "context_len": 102}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 53, "context": "Everywhere and always the world of boys is outside of the laws that govern grown-up communities , and it has its unwritten usages , which are handed down from old to young , and perpetuated on the same level of years , and are lived into and lived out of , but are binding , through all personal vicissitudes , upon the great body of boys between six and twelve years old", "context_len": 72}}}, "scope": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "Miss Forster was not sorry to find so plausible an excuse for keeping her pet nephew at the Willows , and the young man himself had no objections , being fuller than ever of ingenious schemes , only he had transferred the seat of his operations to the Abbey , as offering a wider scope in the way of material , and having the further advantage of a number of appreciative assistants", "context_len": 72}}}, "trend": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "There is the reality of history behind these stories , the successful series of `` Woodranger Tales , '' the scope and trend of which are accurately set forth in the title", "context_len": 32}}}, "accurately": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` As to his pugilistic abilities , I am by no means so accurately informed , but if my conjecture be of any value whatsoever , I should be inclined to infer , from the fact that our enemies , the representatives of tyranny and oppression , who are endeavoring to reduce us to submission , have selected him as their champion and representative in arms , that -- -- ''", "context_len": 71}}}, "title": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 45, "context": "What a sleepless night I have passed , what anxiety , what excitement ! and yet how unlike is he to what I had imagined ! so timid , so petulant ! and that perpetual punning ! It matters not , however , -- his title to our services remains the same ! A strange misgiving is on my soul ; is it the shadow of approaching danger , or only the fear of it ? The Colonel gave me a strange meaning look as he passed me this morning , and said , You are early up , Miss Ratty ; I fear that your rest was broken last night", "context_len": 111}}}, "sensational": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "Having their courage restored and being spurred on by Merwin , they lined up and lunged into the scrimmage before the visitors were wholly prepared , and a gain of nine yards through center might have developed into another sensational run had not Eliot himself nailed the man with the pigskin", "context_len": 51}}}, "liveliness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "On the surface she was free , frank , chatty , quick in response , ready to converse , full of liveliness , fun , and repartee", "context_len": 27}}}, "fail": {"freq": 95, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 86, "word_index": 75, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 106, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}}}, "prince": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 25, "context": "The door was open , an ' I jus ' walked in , an ' I dess dey sought I was jus ' a little prince or somethin ' , cause nobody said anythin ' to me till I tame to the room where de Queen 's mother was ; an ' I asked her would n't she tome out in de garden an ' see you ; an ' she said no , she did not feel able to walk very much , but for me to go an ' bring my little friend in", "context_len": 96}}}, "ninety": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 60, "context": "Many will , we may calculate , jump overboard and swim ashore rather than face us , and we shall be able , at any rate , to capture three or four of their craft , for , as they come out , one by one , we can all close round them ; and with nearly fifty knights , ninety released captives , burning for vengeance , and some fifty or sixty fishermen , for those from the fire ships will , of course , join us -- we shall make short work of them , and may even hope to entirely destroy their fleet", "context_len": 106}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 94, "context": "`` Of course she does n't know all I suffered , what with taking up one worthless claim after another month in and out -- if you mean that ! Why , I actually thought one time of giving up prospecting and settling down to day 's work ! Yes 'm ! It was sure enough that grub-stake you gave me last Fourth of July that brought me my first luck ! I put it right into Pony Gulch and my pick struck free-milling ore the first blow ! Some of the stuff runs ninety dollars to the ton and some higher", "context_len": 102}}}, "discovers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "A delightful fairy tale for children , dealing with the life of a charming young Prince , who , aided by the Moon Spirit , discovers , after many adventures , a beautiful girl whom he makes his Princess", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "But she is steering the course that we have decided the Virginia ought to be steering ; and it has just occurred to me that , should she indeed be that craft , she may give us a great deal of trouble if she discovers us prematurely , seeing that she is to windward", "context_len": 54}}}, "enamored": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He is so enamored that he dwells with his bride in complete seclusion for a while , entrusting the conduct of his kingdom meantime to his monkey servant , Longtail", "context_len": 30}}}, "bride": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 26, "context": "Now his mother was with the Lady Bedrulbudour , and when he came up and did off her veil , she fell to gazing upon the bride 's beauty and grace and looked at the pavilion , the which was all wroughten [ 497 ] of gold and jewels and therein were golden lustres , all embossed with emeralds and rubies ; and she said in herself , `` Methought the Sultan 's palace was magnificent ; but , for this pavilion [ 498 ] alone , I doubt me the greatest of the Chosroes and the kings never owned its match ; nor , methinketh , might all mankind avail to make the like thereof", "context_len": 116}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` So the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? He that can in so short a time avail unto riches like these , is he not worthy to be the Sultan 's son-in-law and to have his daughter to bride ? '' Now the Vizier marvelled at the greatness of these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Therewith Alaeddin arose and would have taken leave ; but the Sultan laid hold on him and said to him , `` Whither away , O my son ? The bride-feast is toward and the bride present ; the knot is knotted and the writ written", "context_len": 46}}}, "seclusion": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 39, "context": "But in carrying a city by storm , which takes place usually at an unexpected time , and often in the night , the maddened and victorious assaulter suddenly burst into the sacred scenes of domestic peace , and seclusion , and love -- the very worst of men , filled with the worst of passions , stimulated by the resistance they have encountered , and licensed by their victory to give all these passions the fullest and most unrestricted gratification", "context_len": 81}}}, "entrusting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 82, "context": "I have this afternoon had a meeting of your knights , and have urged upon them very strongly that , having volunteered to serve under you , they must obey your orders as promptly and willingly as if you were the senior knight of the Order , and that it behooves them specially upon the present occasion , when the crew is composed entirely of young knights , to show themselves worthy of the honour that has been done to them by entrusting a galley of the Order to their charge", "context_len": 91}}}, "kingdom": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 47, "context": "Nothing could have been prettier than the wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "meantime": {"freq": 73, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 2, "context": "In the meantime he did what he could with the family pony , and he had long rides in the woods with the other boy , who used to get his father 's horse when he was not using it on Sunday , and race with him through the dangling wild grape-vines and pawpaw thickets , and over the reedy levels of the river , their hearts both bounding with the same high hopes of a world that could never come true", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 0, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "marries": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "The latter marries a monkey princess from Amfalulu , and their joint reign is described with the drollest humor", "context_len": 19}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Folks had said that the Rector , like many a man who marries late in life , had made an idol of his motherless boy , and they had said , again , that the father 's heart was broken and the print of death was on his face as he stood by his child 's open grave", "context_len": 58}}}, "princess": {"freq": 85, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 84, "word_index": 24, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` You may do anything you like to do , princess", "context_len": 11}}}, "joint": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 104, "context": "We are all just now in a state of indignation about your pork ! Don \u2019 t suppose that it is any fault in the pork ; on the contrary , it is acknowledged to be the most refined pork ever known ; and Mother says that if she shut her eyes , she would not know that she was not eating chicken ! ! We had a beautiful roast of it one day at luncheon ; and Mother cut off a choice bit , to be reserved for our table , cold , while the servants were indulged with the rest of that joint", "context_len": 105}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "His left arm was missing , the empty sleeve being fastened to his coat ; and , as if these deficiencies were not enough , his left leg had been cut off at the knee joint , and was replaced by a wooden stump", "context_len": 44}}}, "reign": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 52, "context": "And then for three days together , during the hour for the daily chat , Marie-Celeste sat an entranced listener , while the wonderful story was told of beautiful little Isabel of Valois , the child-queen whom Richard of Bordeaux brought to England at the age of nine , and whose childish reign was so soon concluded", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "They were far too deeply impressed with a conviction of the decision and energy of Alexander 's character , as manifested in the whole course of his proceedings since he began to reign , and especially by his sudden reappearance among them so soon after this outbreak against his authority , to imagine that there was now any hope for them except in determined and successful resistance", "context_len": 67}}}, "drollest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "The children were all placed round the room , some of them on the drollest kind of seats , which Mr", "context_len": 21}}}, "rulers": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 14, "context": "It is certainly true , said Her Majesty a little gravely , that the rulers of a great country like England have often to set aside their own preferences ; but these are better times than those in which the little Isabel lived , and the idea of a king marrying a little girl of eight , no matter for what reason , would hardly be tolerated now , you know", "context_len": 71}}}, "pretenders": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}}}, "endeared": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "They want to make use of the woods and fields and rivers ; and when they become men they find these aspects of nature endeared to them by association , and so they think that they were dear for their own sakes ; but the taste for nature is as purely acquired as the taste for poetry or the taste for tomatoes", "context_len": 62}}}, "relates": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 54, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Such is the story which Josephus relates", "context_len": 7}}}, "grant": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 1, "context": "And grant that Allah make [ the thing ] possible and I take courage and say to him , ' I desire to ally myself to thee by [ marrying ] thy daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour with my son Alaeddin , ' they will straightway deem me mad and will put me out with ignominy and reproach ; nay , I need not tell thee that by this I shall fall into danger of death , and not I only , but thou also", "context_len": 84}, "VERB": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 122, "context": "and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm , that thou shouldst merit of him this boon ? Again , this that thou cravest is beyond thy condition ; [ 336 ] so it can not be that the king will grant thee that which thou seekest", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}}}, "delightfully": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 15, "context": "After sleeping all night in their clothes the cool plunge in the clear water was delightfully refreshing , and they sat about like mermaids on the rocks , basking in the sunshine , and watching a ring-ousel teaching her three big babies to fly , till Father called out that if they did not hurry up and come in at once he should eat all the breakfast before they arrived", "context_len": 70}}}, "pupils": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 51, "word_index": 30, "context": "They were full of their own interests , and , on the few occasions when she happened to meet them , had talked so much of new teachers , new pupils , and class topics of which she knew nothing , that she had felt a little 'out of it , ' and had an uneasy sensation that , if she spoke of the quiet little episodes of her daily round they would vote her housekeeping experiences as decidedly 'slow", "context_len": 80}}}, "frank": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 7, "context": "His manly and athletic form , his frank and open manners , his spirit , his generosity , and a certain air of confidence , independence , and conscious superiority , which were combined , as they always are in the case of true greatness , with an unaffected and unassuming modesty -- these and other traits , which were obvious to all who saw him , in the person and character of Alexander , made every one his friend", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 14, "context": "The only person who was not won over by Archie 's friendly ways and frank American manners was Joe , who gloomily prophesied broken necks and kindred evils as the result of the children 's association with 'the young master from furrin parts ' ; but I fear there was a good deal of jealousy in this , for poor Joe had been a hero to the children in his modest way , and it was hard to find himself suddenly supplanted in their affections , especially by a rival with whom it was quite impossible to compete", "context_len": 98}}}, "disarm": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The hero is a lovable little fellow , whose frank and winning ways disarm even the crustiest of grandmothers , and win for him the affection of all manner of unlikely people", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "Meanwhile I had practically abandoned my attempts to effect my escape , for the time being at least ; for the conviction had at length been forced upon me that neither Banda nor his people would ever willingly let me go , and that , therefore , before engaging in any further attempts , I must contrive to disarm suspicion completely , and create the impression that I had at length resigned myself to live out my life in this remote African town , and with savages only for my companions", "context_len": 91}}}, "crustiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The hero is a lovable little fellow , whose frank and winning ways disarm even the crustiest of grandmothers , and win for him the affection of all manner of unlikely people", "context_len": 32}}}, "unlikely": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 13, "context": "She had a sort of portfolio under her arm ; it was not unlikely she was going to some lesson or other , and if so , alas ! where would the chat come in ? But , as you and I happen to know , nothing was farther from Marie-Celeste 's thought that happy summer , withal she was learning so much , than any idea of lessons , and on she went till she vanished from sight through one of the castle gates", "context_len": 85}}}, "deserves": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 20, "context": "This story is so much about Peggy and her satellite Bobby that we have rather neglected Lilian , and she deserves a chapter all to herself ; for she was one of the sweetest , gentlest , most unselfish of girls , who filled a quiet niche in the little world of her home which would have been sadly empty without her", "context_len": 62}}}, "juvenile": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "I paint pictures , too , sometimes , when Isabella do n't want me , ' confided the juvenile artist ; and to judge from the smears of paint upon her pinafore , she had evidently been pursuing that art with more vigour than discretion", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "Every Washington 's Birthday and Fourth of July since , this site had been the center of all public patriotic festivities , and the headquarters for celebrating for juvenile Pleasantville", "context_len": 30}}}, "instructive": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 21, "context": "It is wholly legendary , in its first portion even mythical ; it is of a comparatively remote age , exceedingly instructive for ethnography and for the development of religious ideas ; it is full of that sort of na\u00efvet\u00e9 which we like so much to meet in the mental productions of our aborigines , and affords striking instances of the debasing and brutalizing influence of the unrestricted belief in the supernatural and miraculous", "context_len": 74}}}, "introduction": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 12, "context": "The idea of expecting that a stranger , to whom his only introduction was that of a cabin-boy of the White Star Line , would be likely to take an interest in him to the extent of making him a loan of a large sum of money at rather a low rate of interest ; and then Ted realized what some of us have realized before , that all he had practically to build upon was Marie-Celeste 's remark to Donald , that she felt very sure that the best thing that could happen to this same rich Mr", "context_len": 99}}}, "whitney": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Whitney", "context_len": 1}}}, "roses": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 64, "context": "safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner , and the oak dresser , with its rows of blue willow-pattern plates ; and back again at last", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "There had been quite a spell of sickness in Gorswen at the end of the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change", "context_len": 128}}}, "assurance": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 64, "context": "Tell me an thou have a want , that I may accomplish it unto thee ; '' whereupon she kissed the earth a second time and called down blessings upon him , then answered , `` Ay , O King of the Age , as thy head liveth , I have indeed a want ; but before all things do thou give me thine assurance , [ 362 ] so I may make bold to prefer my suit to the hearing of our lord the Sultan , for that belike Thy Grace will find it a strange one", "context_len": 98}}}, "announces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` One might almost as well offer June roses with the assurance of their sweetness , as to present this lovely little gathering of verse , which announces itself , like them , by its own deliciousness", "context_len": 37}}}, "deliciousness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 76, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}}}, "declare": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 108, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "'Why , it 's you , miss , I do declare ! ' exclaimed Polly", "context_len": 15}}}, "grandchildren": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "But these thoughts did not last long , for Jennie called her to come and see the pile of clothes her mother had laid aside for Cap'n Si 's grandchildren , and began to tell of the many things which they could make for the bazar , so she was soon interested in all this", "context_len": 55}}}, "bewitching": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "She went quickly across the room and never paused until she found herself in the woman 's arms , and showed her love by so many quaint and delicate little caresses , and had such a dainty and bewitching way about her , that no human could have held out against her", "context_len": 52}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Oh , the dancing was just bewitching ? Marilla drew long breaths and wished she was among them ; every pulse in her body kept time", "context_len": 26}}}, "personally": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 1, "context": "Hartley personally , he sought him out , where he was at work in a corner of the meadow , and the result , as he had anticipated , was a very plain talk -- so unsparingly and pointedly plain that Harry winced a good deal in the process , and once or twice came near resenting a mode of procedure that seemed very much akin to knocking a fellow when he 's down", "context_len": 74}}}, "entitled": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 100, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "based": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "The tribe itself is based upon smaller units of individuals which are joined together by a common tie ; this tie is either the archaic maternal descent , or the more modern tie of paternal descent , or a combination of both", "context_len": 42}}}, "facts": {"freq": 67, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 104, "context": "the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the mind by the capricious action of memory , while far greater events drop out of it", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Books on Natural History always proved an attraction to her ; and many little Natural History facts come incidentally into her correspondence , sometimes given from her own observation", "context_len": 29}}}, "unwelcome": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Although based strictly on historical facts the book is an interesting and exciting tale of adventure , which will delight all boys , and be by no means unwelcome to their elders", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 70, "context": "Never had a night seemed so long ; the only one who thoroughly enjoyed himself was the puppy , who , delighted to receive so much attention , pursued his diversions until the sun was well in at the window , when at length he snuggled into Peggy 's pillow , and composed himself to sleep , leaving his weary guardians only time for a brief rest before Nancy 's unwelcome tap was heard at the door", "context_len": 77}}}, "favor": {"freq": 114, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 104, "word_index": 77, "context": "Well , she was all right then , and he carried her off ; but first she walked round and made her manners to every man there as polite as you please , looking the daintiest little lady that ever walked on two feet ; and when I put her into the carriage , did n't she thank me for letting her into the station , and being kind to her , as if it was a favor I 'd been doing , and not my duty ; and as if a man could help it that once looked at her", "context_len": 101}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 15, "context": "Wondering if they would make their return trip in safety , Chris himself happened to favor them with his last waking thought , ere he fell asleep in his little room under the eaves -- a cosey little room that still was bright even at ten o'clock with the glow of the long English twilight", "context_len": 55}}}, "scrupulous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "For reasons of his own the Rector kept Peggy late that evening , and they made a little tour of the garden , selecting what they called their prize plants , putting indiarubber bands round carnation-buds to keep them from bursting , and tying up the most promising stocks and asters with a scrupulous care , working until the light had almost failed and the sky stood out yellow against the outline of the cypress-trees", "context_len": 75}}}, "accuracy": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 42, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}}}, "tropics": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "In the sea , said daddy , and far away in the tropics where the plants and birds and animals are very different from here , there are floating plant creatures known as Portuguese Men-of-War", "context_len": 35}}}, "resulting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 89, "context": "wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his absence", "context_len": 128}}}, "conflict": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 87, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}}}, "elements": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 78, "context": "But I soon discovered that even this small measure of mercy was to be denied me ; food and drink were indeed to be served out to us -- in order , as I surmised , that we might meet the ordeal in store for us with unabated strength -- but the night was to be passed , as the day had been , secured to the sacrificial post , exposed , naked and helpless , to the elements and to the myriad insect plagues that attacked us unceasingly", "context_len": 89}}}, "inherited": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 35, "context": "If I would consent to take his guilt upon myself , he agreed to pay over to my wife five hundred dollars annually out of his salary of fifteen hundred dollars , and when he inherited his uncle \u2019 s estate , he furthermore agreed to pay over to me twenty thousand dollars", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "On October 11th , 1832 , the Apalachicola tribe renewed a prior agreement to remove to the west of Mississippi river , and to surrender their inherited lands at the mouth of the Apalachicola river", "context_len": 35}}}, "ancestry": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "The sturdy independence he inherited from an ancestry of English tradesfolk and yeomen made him cherish the ideal of an English republic with religious fervour , while , whether leading a prayer meeting or heading a cavalry charge , his inspiring enthusiasm carried away all who were near him", "context_len": 49}}}, "creating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The resulting conflict between the two elements in her nature , the one inherited from her New England ancestry , and the other developed by her West Indian surroundings , gave Miss Pool unusual opportunity for creating an original and fascinating heroine", "context_len": 42}}}, "illustrating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "To offer a history of the Creek tribe from its discovery down to our epoch to the readers does not lie within the scope of this volume , and for want of sufficient documents illustrating the earlier periods it could be presented in a fragmentary manner only", "context_len": 47}}}, "failures": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 28, "context": "Since then Christ feels for all the pains and struggles through which we go for his sake , since he can make allowance for all our weakness and failures ; and as he is so ready to give us help in our temptations , so much the more ought we who are not only tempted , but too apt , in spite of our best efforts , to fall into sin , to show to others all the kindness and sympathy we may at any time need for ourselves", "context_len": 89}}}, "successes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "His armies had been broken up , his courage and hope had gradually failed , while the animation and hope of the pursuer had been gathering fresh and increasing strength from his successes , and were excited to wild enthusiasm now , as the hour for the final consummation of all his desires seemed to be drawing nigh", "context_len": 58}}}, "unruly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "No doubt they were an unruly set of boys , and I do not suppose the teacher was a hard man , though he led the life of an executioner , and seldom passed a day without inflicting pain that a fiend might shrink from giving", "context_len": 46}}}, "ultimately": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "It was undoubtedly by diligent practice in this direction that , after strict confinement to the interior of my hut for some five or six days , I was permitted , first of all , to wander at will about that portion of the town which was enclosed by the palisade , and ultimately to pass outside and go practically whither I would , always accompanied , however , by two armed guards", "context_len": 73}}}, "softened": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "Unattractive and uncomely he was , beyond question , but now his unspeakable gratitude to this kind woman so softened and transformed his face that , could they have seen him , those who fancied they knew him well would have been astonished at the change", "context_len": 46}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 38, "context": "She had lost her hat , and the wind had tossed up her curls , her cheeks were pale and streaked with tears , and her big brown eyes had a pitiful look in them that would have softened a tiger , let alone a man that had half a dozen little ones of his own at home ; while every now and then the great heavy sighs came struggling up , as if she had almost cried her heart out", "context_len": 81}}}, "civilized": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 55, "context": "Peggy , hard at work behind the scenes , put her eye to a hole in the curtain , and surveyed the prospect below , where children big and little were engaged in jumping over the forms , chasing each other round the stove , and generally acting more like monkeys at the Zoo than civilized human beings , while the Rector strove in vain to collect them at the empty end of the room to play games , and the curate , a shy young man fresh from Oxford , looked as if he would have preferred to wrestle with a consignment of heathens from the Cannibal Islands", "context_len": 109}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 39, "context": "Since the memorable time when Captain Cook sailed into Botany Bay in 1769 and saw the naked native Australian poising erect to hurl his peculiar weapon , the boomerang has continued to excite the curiosity and amazement of the civilized world ; and truly the finding of such a scientific weapon in the hands of this so-called lowest order of mankind is an astonishing fact , to be simply accepted as another oddity of this odd , topsy-turvy corner of the world", "context_len": 82}}}, "proves": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 12, "context": "On account of the recent discovery of their consonantic language , which proves to be disconnected from any other aboriginal tongue spoken in North America , a peculiar interest attaches itself to the tribe of the Taensa Indians , whose cabins stood in Tensas county , Louisiana , bordering east on Mississippi river", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Language is not an absolute indicator of race , but it is more so in America than elsewhere , for the large number of linguistic families in the western hemisphere proves that the populations speaking their dialects have suffered less than in the eastern by encroachment , foreign admixture , forcible alteration or entire destruction", "context_len": 55}}}, "loses": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Now , I want her to be thoroughly ashamed of it ; and when that takes place , and she loses all sense of satisfaction in terrifying people to death and in getting rid of servants and governesses , she will become an ordinary girl -- or rather , I should say , never an ordinary girl , but a girl very much above the ordinary", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Ai n't it a hard , hard thing when a little child loses of his mother ? '' she said to herself", "context_len": 23}}}, "finds": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Yes , '' said Maggie ; `` one little brook runs on until it finds another little brook , and then they join , and run on together , and then they meet another and another till they all make a small river , and that joins other little rivers and brooks , till there is a very large one like that we sailed on this morning , and that runs into the great , great sea that we used to see at Quam Beach last summer", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "But such lucky finds were not numerous", "context_len": 7}}}, "misspellings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Except for those changes noted below , all misspellings in the text , and inconsistent or archaic usage , have been retained", "context_len": 22}}}, "inconsistent": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Except for those changes noted below , all misspellings in the text , and inconsistent or archaic usage , have been retained", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "The Jews , as Josephus says , refused to send any such supplies , saying that it would be inconsistent with fidelity to Darius , under whose government they were", "context_len": 30}}}, "archaic": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 40, "context": "The following ancient hunting song may serve as a specimen of the female dialect of Hitchiti ; the ending -i of the verbs , standing instead of -is of the male dialect , proves it to be worded in that archaic form of speech", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "usage": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 52, "context": "It was the custom of the invaders to burn all their resting places when they left them , and to slay all captives , save such as could be held to ransom , or a few whom they detained in slavery , till they died a worse death from want and ill usage", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Through long usage the upholstering on the arms of the chair had become worn , and in places the tufts of moss or horse-hair were showing", "context_len": 26}}}, "retained": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 30, "context": "When the shout had died away , and the wine cups were emptied , Gervaise , who was sitting on Sir John Kendall 's right hand , would gladly have retained his seat , but the bailiff told him that he must say a few words , and after standing in embarrassed silence for a minute he said , Sir John Kendall , and brother knights , I can only say that I am very sensible of the kindness with which you have been pleased to regard what seems to me after all to have been a very ordinary affair", "context_len": 100}}}, "stokes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Stokes Company MCMXXIII", "context_len": 3}}}, "dedicated": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 21, "context": "In the year 1835 he published a volume of plays and enigmas , called The Tragedies of Harold and Camoens , dedicated to the Duke of Wellington , for whom he and his family had the deepest esteem and admiration", "context_len": 40}}}, "january": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 0, "context": "JANUARY 1 : A NEW YEAR \u2019 S PARTY 1", "context_len": 10}}}, "february": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 0, "context": "FEBRUARY 14 : Why There is a Saint Valentine \u2019 s Day", "context_len": 12}}}, "april": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 0, "context": "APRIL 23 : The Butterfly Who Loved the Sun", "context_len": 9}}}, "walrus": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Walrus came the warm spring days", "context_len": 6}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Walrus", "context_len": 1}}}, "june": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "JUNE 2 : The Robins Come to the Rescue", "context_len": 9}}}, "july": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "JULY 1 : A BROTHER \u2019 S PLAN 156", "context_len": 9}}}, "hippopotamus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "HIPPOPOTAMUS 162", "context_len": 2}}}, "august": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "Then , at five every clear day , afternoon tea with the captain , for which they had a standing invitation , and by means of which both she and Chris came to be on terms of wonderful intimacy with that august officer , so that they joked over the rare souchong and delicious little toasted cakes ( the secret of whose making was kept close-guarded by the steward ) with a familiarity that , to themselves at least , never ceased to be a wonder", "context_len": 86}}}, "september": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "SEPTEMBER 1 : THE WHIPPOORWILLS 210", "context_len": 6}}}, "october": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "OCTOBER 1 : GYPSY , THE CAT 232", "context_len": 8}}}, "november": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "NOVEMBER 1 : THE BROWNIES HELP 257", "context_len": 7}}}, "facing": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "FACING PAGE", "context_len": 2}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 24, "context": "But , though all is well that ends well , Peggy was still so tear-stained and upset that she did not feel equal to facing her school-fellows , however sympathetic they might be , so she escaped with Lilian through the side-door into the street , feeling she would never be really happy or at ease again till she was back in that haven of home where she was always appreciated and understood , and pouring out her troubles to Aunt Helen in the sanctuary of the Rose Parlour", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "FACING THE WORLD", "context_len": 3}}}, "afternoons": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 54, "context": "Harris , said Donald , who had been listening to Ted and doing his own thinking at one and the same time , there was an Englishman came over on the steamer with us , who grew to be great friends with Marie-Celeste , and Marie-Celeste told me all about him one of those afternoons when I was too weak to do anything but lie in my berth , and she tried to entertain me", "context_len": 75}}}, "fourth": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 55, "context": "The Captain took off his big hat with a great flourish and made a bow right down to the ground , then the second pirate took off his big hat with a great flourish and made a bow right down to the ground , then the third pirate took off his hat , and the fourth and the fifth , until all six had taken off their hats with a flourish and made the most magnificently polite bow any one had ever seen", "context_len": 83}}}, "eagle": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 53, "context": "Each of the lofty ships bore the ensign of its commander ; some carried at the prow the figures of lions , some of bulls , dolphins , dragons , or armed warriors , gaudily painted or even gilded ; while others bore from their mast the ensign of voracious birds -- the eagle , the raven -- which appeared to stretch their wings as the flag expanded in the wind", "context_len": 71}}}, "bunny": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "A lovely gray felt bunny and a brown felt monkey always were with Lucy 50", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 8, "context": "There they all were ! And the chocolate bunny did have the funniest expression ! He would have made any one laugh , and his purple ribbon bow was sticking way up on one side with such a saucy expression", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Besides the toy bunny a rag doll came to see Lucy", "context_len": 11}}}, "sirup": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Don \u2019 t you know that you must get to work ? This is the season for us to tap the sirup from the maple trees , and you are spending your time swinging in a hammock", "context_len": 37}}}, "hammock": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 41, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}}}, "brings": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 4, "context": "In every room Donald brings his own peculiar methods of investigation to bear , not in the least minding a good deal of mirthful laughter at his expense on the part of Harold and Marie-Celeste ; and Albert , feeling privileged to join in the general merriment , though evidently half the time without in anywise appreciating the situation , only helps on the jollity of things", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was then too late , however , for the father to have got back his old newspaper , even if he had wished , and the children heard , with the elation that novelty brings to all children , old or young , that they were going away from the Boy 's Town , to live in another place", "context_len": 60}}}, "caldron": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "They were real gipsies , too , not the fair-haired hybrid pretenders who go about in neat caravans with muslin curtains to the windows , and wicker baskets slung on the top , but a dark-eyed , Spanish-looking crew , who put up a tent with a pole and a ragged blanket , and stewed their supper in a black caldron hung from three sticks set in the ground", "context_len": 69}}}, "daddy": {"freq": 150, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 137, "word_index": 2, "context": "Oh , daddy ! \u2019 said Isa , instead of giving just me a valentine party couldn \u2019 t we have one for all the children in the hospital ? I \u2019 m well enough to be moved into the big ward , and all the children who are able to be there can have a party with me , and we can have a huge valentine party", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now the children who owned these rabbits didn \u2019 t know that daddy rabbits were all right when their children were big but that when their children were small they weren \u2019 t so nice because they were apt to kill them", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "It is said , daddy told the children , that the shamrock keeps away the snakes which St", "context_len": 18}}}, "named": {"freq": 214, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 213, "word_index": 98, "context": "How came that picture in the dwelling of Japazy , the half-breed ? Why had a similar picture been in the hut of Sobrinini ? What mysterious link was there between the lovely original of that picture and Sobrinini , the witch woman , Japazy , the master of Jaguar Island , Jojasta , the medicine man of the Moving Mountain , and Casson , his friend and former protector ? Somewhere , some time , those residents of the Amazonian jungle had known the mysterious Bartow , his wife , Laura , and perhaps the little child named Bonny", "context_len": 100}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Pinky was so named because of his very bright pink eyes", "context_len": 11}}}, "games": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 74, "word_index": 58, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}}}, "saucers": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "The hut had to be swept and dusted , late flowers to be gleaned from the garden to decorate the tea-table , cups and saucers packed up and conveyed in baskets , together with the little tin kettle and the methylated spirit lamp , as they could scarcely light a fire on the platform like they did for picnic teas in the woods", "context_len": 63}}}, "sugared": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "Porter came too , full of pity for the forlorn , sick child , and carrying a kettle of tea , ready milked and sugared", "context_len": 25}}}, "trimmings": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "She looked very nice in that silk , almost pretty , and as all its accompaniments were perfect , the lace ruffles round the neck , the lace hanging over her hands , the trimmings of every sort just as they ought to be , the hat which she was to wear with the dress , specially chosen by the London dressmaker for the purpose , no one could look more elegant than Florence did as she stood in the hall of Cherry Court School just before she started for Cherry Court Park", "context_len": 93}}}, "frosting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was pink and white on the top with pink sugared trimmings on the white frosting", "context_len": 16}}}, "center": {"freq": 86, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 84, "word_index": 10, "context": "There was a large , round lamp suspended from the center of the wagon , and as Guy looked at his mother 's cheerful surroundings he could not but wonder that she sighed when he spoke of the dark , lonesome lodgings they had left , until he suddenly remembered that she had been nursing the heavy , fretful baby , and trying to amuse Aggie all the day", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "No indeed ! There was the Bee who tried to get me to give him a sweet drink , but I keep it deep down in my very center dining room where even the Bee with his long , thirsty tongue can \u2019 t get at it", "context_len": 47}}}, "dolls": {"freq": 93, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 92, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Oh ! we are Harry 's soldiers , I mean your soldiers , and Franky is , too ; and we have the nursery chairs for horses , and our dolls for sick soldiers , and we have the pillows for Sepoys , and we poke them ; and nurse do n't like it , 'cause she says we make a yumpus and a muss in the nursery", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Why they have dolls by right and no one should prevent them", "context_len": 12}}}, "shawls": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Costly robes , delicate embroidery , superb carpets , shawls , goldsmiths ' work , and no small amount of jewels , were among the spoil collected , and the bulk of the merchandise captured was , two days later , despatched in galleys to Genoa and Marseilles , to be sold for the benefit of the Order", "context_len": 58}}}, "flutter": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 32, "context": "Yes , I am the last person to encourage vanity in the young , but I must repeat that if you knew all that Albert has said , you would feel that flutter of the heart which only joy brings forth", "context_len": 41}}}, "pane": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "The wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his", "context_len": 128}}}, "sill": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "But you want some breakfast , dear , do n't you ? Marie-Celeste shook her head , but as she seemed to know perfectly well what she did want , he suffered her to lead him over the high sill that keeps the water from rushing indoors in rough weather , and past the main stairway , and into a far corner of the library", "context_len": 65}}}, "lettuce": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "Biddy was gorging herself behind a tree trunk , the juncos and thrushes were eating seeds and cake crumbs scattered over the lawn , while the rabbit with a bland air surveyed the pleasant scenery and regaled himself on juicy lettuce leaves", "context_len": 42}}}, "seeds": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 53, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}}}, "chirped": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "Birdie did not like his ride up the mountain at all , but chirped in a very miserable , beseeching manner all the way ; and , when he was safely at the Lake House and hung up out of the reach of Mrs", "context_len": 44}}}, "suet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "So the little bird was given a regular New Year \u2019 s feast , and Ella and her friends were among other children who decided to look after the feathered people through the cold months of the winter and to give them bread-crumbs and suet which they love so well", "context_len": 50}}}, "feast": {"freq": 97, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 59, "context": "Can he speak thus who hath so long espoused The Royal cause , and served that cause so well ? Who , girt with honours , well deserved , hath stood One in a noble Brotherhood of Fame ! Where are the Cavaliers who fought with thee In battle , side by side , who with thee shared The feast , and drained the wine-cup to your King ? Where are they now ? what , gone ? not one remains , T \u2019 assert thy innocence , or shield thee from An ignominious death", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` O yes , my father is keeping high festival ; a number of his countrymen are visiting him and holding revel ; this morning they drove in a number of oxen , I know not whence , and slaughtered two on the spot , and they have broached several barrels of mead ; they will keep the feast all day , and before night my father will not be in a state to miss me ; I always absent myself if I can on such occasions", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 77, "context": "So thither flocked the young and the old : the wood rangers and hunters from the forests of Newenham , where Herstan had right of wood cutting ; the men who wove baskets and hurdles of osier work from the river banks ; the theows who cultivated the home farm ; the ceorls who rented a hide of land here and a hide there -- all , the grandfather and the grandson , accepted the invitation to feast", "context_len": 78}}}, "feathered": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "So the little bird was given a regular New Year \u2019 s feast , and Ella and her friends were among other children who decided to look after the feathered people through the cold months of the winter and to give them bread-crumbs and suet which they love so well", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "Faint clucks and hen whispers reached my ears , but when she turned on some bright lights several of the hens spoke to her quite amiably and distinctly , while a finely feathered Plymouth Rock rooster got off his perch and shaking his big wings came to put his beak in her hand", "context_len": 53}}}, "poem": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 26, "context": "There ought to have been more verses , but at this point Father had unfortunately got hold of the paper , and persisted in treating the poem in such a comic light that the indignant authoress had never found the heart to finish it , though the fragment was considered very talented by Aunt Helen , and carefully put away in an old work-box , with the first specimens of Peggy 's handwriting and one of Bobby 's little baby-curls", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "He praised the child 's work , and no doubt smiled at it with the mother ; but he said that the poem spoke of heaven as a place in the sky , and he wished him always to realize that heaven was a state and not a place , and that we could have it in this world as well as the next", "context_len": 64}}}, "poetical": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "The writing of the play as a whole is unequal , -- what girl of eighteen is not unequal ? -- but in these lines , as well as elsewhere , there are tokens of genuine power , alike poetical and dramatic", "context_len": 42}}}, "unable": {"freq": 122, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 122, "word_index": 60, "context": "The Sultan wondered with an exceeding wonderment and was confounded at the beauty of the girls and their loveliness , which overpassed description ; his wit was bewildered , when he saw the golden dishes , full of jewels that dazzled the sight , and he was amazed at this marvel , so that he became as one dumb , unable to speak aught , of the excess of his wonderment ; nay , his wit was the more perplexed , forasmuch as this had all been accomplished in an hour 's time", "context_len": 93}}}, "wrinkles": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Ah , goot-morning , Mynheer ! '' was the greeting of the governor , as he made his way across the sloping deck of the galliot , his usually grave visage puckered with a thousand wrinkles , while his eyes twinkled with grim humour", "context_len": 45}}}, "cheer": {"freq": 103, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 47, "context": "Then , after a while , the Sultan died and Alaeddin sat down on the throne of the kingdom and ruled and did justice among the people ; and all the folk loved him and he lived with his wife , the Lady Bedrulbudour , in all cheer and solace and contentment till there came to them the Destroyer of Delights and the Sunderer of Societies", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 38, "context": "But he gave me to understand that if for any reason his return was delayed I might act on my own discretion , and tell you what comforts me beyond all possible words , and what may also cheer you , for I can scarcely think , my darling , that the love of a man like that would be unreturned by a girl like you , when once you knew , Westenra , when once you surely knew", "context_len": 79}}}, "recited": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "And certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most", "context_len": 128}}}, "vim": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "Of course , you see I was out for fun , My life has only just begun , They said He is young and full of vim , No one can help but welcome him", "context_len": 35}}}, "conceited": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 61, "context": "How sagacious a line of conduct was this ! Most ardent and enthusiastic young men , in the circumstances in which he was placed , would have been elated and vain at their elevation , and would have replaced the old and well-tried servants of the father with personal favorites of their own age , inexperienced and incompetent , and as conceited as themselves", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "The canal-boatmen were all an heroic race , and the boys humbly hoped that some day , if they proved worthy , they might grow up to be drivers ; not indeed packet-drivers ; they were not so conceited as that ; but freight-boat drivers , of two horses , perhaps , but gladly of one", "context_len": 56}}}, "harp": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 13, "context": "Eating and drinking were over , and the old gleeman , striking his harp , was beginning a song of harvest home , when in rushed the two young theows who had gone out with Alfgar and Bertric , with the startling intelligence that there was a band of Northmen lurking in the woods , who had seized their young lords , and were , they thought , bent on attacking the place", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` It interested me , '' said Florence , `` but why do you harp so about it ? I have so much to think of , it is rather bothering for you to go back again and again to the same subject", "context_len": 43}}}, "wept": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 49, "context": "O misery ! to wake in darkness , wake From vision of unutterable joy ; Instead of trumpet-sound and song of Heaven , To hear the dull clock measuring out time , When I had seemed to touch Eternity ! In the first pang of disappointed hope , I wept that I could wake from such a dream ; Until Faith gently whispered , Wherefore weep To lose the faint dim shadow of a joy Of which the substance shall one day be thine ? Live in the hope , -- that hope shall brighten life , And sanctify it to its highest end", "context_len": 104}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Soft-hearted Joe wept at the good news , but it was for joy this time ; and so many people sent kind messages of congratulation , and expressed such pleasure at hearing they were to remain at the Abbey , that the Vaughans were quite surprised to find how popular they were in the neighbourhood", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "' It was rather a grown-up book , perhaps , for a little girl , but it was all about pirates , and sailors , and hairbreadth escapes , of so wildly exciting a nature , that she read on till she almost wept with disappointment to think she was not a boy to go to sea and meet with such thrilling adventures", "context_len": 63}}}, "ere": {"freq": 110, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 102, "word_index": 21, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 48, "context": "The company on board The Revenge was gradually getting less and less ; the Spanish ships , meanwhile , as they received a sufficient evidence of The Revenge's powers of destruction , dropped off , and their places were taken by others ; and thus it happened that ere the morning fifteen ships had been engaged , and all were so little pleased with the entertainment provided that they were far more willing to listen to proposals for an honourable arrangement than to make any more assaults", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 99, "context": "Withal , O my son , of regard for thy wish , needs must I take courage and go ; but , O my son , if the King receive me and honour me for the gift 's sake and I seek of him that which thou wilt in [ 343 ] the matter of marrying his daughter and he ask me , after the wont of the folk , what are thy possessions and thy revenues , what shall I say to him ? And most like , O my son , he will ask me of this ere he ask me of thyself", "context_len": 105}}}, "hardest": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "Take my advice : always look cheerful if you can ; always put your hand on the oar when the order is given , and row as if you were glad to be at work again ; and always make a show , as if you were working your hardest", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 37, "context": "As the crime is probably extinct , I will say that in those wicked days , if you were such a miscreant , and there was some boy you hated , you stole up and tied the hardest kind of a knot in one arm or both arms of his shirt", "context_len": 51}}}, "crab": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Oh , '' said Bessie , laughing , `` what a funny Uncle John you are ! But I should think it would hurt the crab 's feelings a great deal more to be poked with a stick , and not to be let to go home when he wants to", "context_len": 52}}}, "hovers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "First of all , the oyster-crab hovers around the oysters and then picks out a nice , fat , juicy looking oyster , saying to himself : You look as if you could feed me well without any effort", "context_len": 39}}}, "oysters": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "First of all , the oyster-crab hovers around the oysters and then picks out a nice , fat , juicy looking oyster , saying to himself : You look as if you could feed me well without any effort", "context_len": 39}}}, "picks": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Now , that poor woman , who has counted her cheese by scores , and her butter by tubs , has not a drop of milk except what the neighbors give her , nor a stick of wood but what she picks up", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` That big fellow on the bough there tells me he is delighted ; that he has at last found one that can understand his language , for he has heard so many ridiculous theories advanced by men with picks on their shoulders and books in their hands , as to what gold is , and how it came on the ground , that he has nearly burst his throat in trying to make them understand the truth , and has then been accused of making a 'senseless chatter", "context_len": 89}}}, "crawls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "\u2019 So then he crawls right inside the oyster \u2019 s shell and proceeds to enjoy himself", "context_len": 17}}}, "proceeds": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then ( says our author ) , the geomant proceeds to examine the sixteen figures thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it", "context_len": 128}}}, "dentist": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "For a dentist , he gave an upward twist of the hand from his jaw , and uttered a howl which left no doubt that he meant tooth-pulling ; and for what would happen to a boy if he kept on misbehaving , he crossed his fingers before his face and looked through them in a way that brought the jail-window clearly before the eyes of the offender", "context_len": 68}}}, "toothache": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "There were some people who believed that tobacco would keep off the fever-and-ague , which was so common then in that country , or at any rate that it was good for the toothache", "context_len": 34}}}, "dreaded": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 127, "context": "she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little creature whom every servant and every governess alike dreaded", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 35, "context": "Appalled by the disaster that had befallen them , by the terrible effect of the broadsides , poured in at a few yards ' distance , and by the sight of so many of the dreaded warriors of the Cross , some of the corsairs threw down their arms and flung themselves on the deck or into the hold , crying for quarter ; those who resisted fell either under the swords of the knights , the vengeful axes of the late captives , or the pikes of the Sards ; but the great bulk , leaping from the bow or stern , swam ashore", "context_len": 105}}}, "pullers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He got a pair of pullers , and after a moment or two Dr", "context_len": 14}}}, "tooth": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 56, "context": "Searching farther , they found a most singular-shaped stone , with an edge like an axe , and near the top a groove nearly half an inch in depth all around it ; also , a pipe , a piece of bone pointed at one end , and in the other a hole , and a tooth pointed , exceedingly hard and white", "context_len": 63}}}, "birthday": {"freq": 110, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 100, "word_index": 15, "context": "Why , Uncle Ruthven , I 'll be six years old when I have a birthday in May , and the other day we saw a little girl , -- she was blind Willie 's sister , -- and she could n't say th , though she is 'most seven ; and I thought it sounded pretty foolish ; and then I thought maybe it sounded just as foolish for me not to say r , so I tried and tried , and Maggie helped me", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 14, "context": "One more apparently early effort remains ; not this time versification , but a birthday letter to Laura , inscribed , To my dear Lady Emma , from her affectionate Tosti", "context_len": 31}}}, "cockatoo": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "\u2019 The cockatoo would raise up his pink crest on the top of his head at that just as some people raise up their foreheads only his crest went way , way higher", "context_len": 33}}}, "spoiled": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 78, "context": "George 's -- and oh ! does n't she play beautifully ! -- we talked a little while on the Castle terrace , and we talked about you , and I asked her if you were ever so nice as Harold , because we could n't help being a little disappointed in you , Cousin Ted , and she said yes , that you used to be every bit as nice , and if you had not been spoiled up at Oxford you would have turned out all right", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` You see , '' said Walter , as the cross , selfish child went off screaming to her mother , `` Mamie is the only girl , and the youngest , and she has been so spoiled there is no living with her", "context_len": 44}}}, "fussing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` Your old tongue is as good as this antiseptic ; however , one must oblige a friend , if only a dog , '' and she washed the shoulder of the pig who took on great airs at having two dogs and a girl fussing over him while a pony and a boy looked on", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Price will come fussing out of the post-office to ask if there has been an accident ; she always wants to poke her nose into everything ! '", "context_len": 28}}}, "tamer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` On the very summit of yonder peak , which seems to rise at least a thousand feet above its neighbors , and where the sun shone the warmest , the grains and fruits were most luxuriant , and the deer larger and tamer than in any other place , lived an old man , the chief of all the tribes that lived between the mountains and the great ocean in the far west", "context_len": 74}}}, "perform": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 95, "context": "David could not be spared to go with them , but all the children had been taught to drive , and even Bobby had a firm hand on the reins , and knew the rules of the road as well as many a more experienced coachman ; and I think , too , that Pixie had a sense of her responsibilities , and could be trusted not to get the wheel locked with a passing waggon , or to race too furiously down a steep hill , whatever feats her drivers might urge her to perform", "context_len": 96}}}, "inches": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 67, "context": "Not to be outdone in politeness , we hoisted our colours also ; and for the next quarter of an hour the two craft continued to close , the chase stolidly maintaining her course , while we , under the skipper 's skilful conning , continued to edge very gradually away , as the other vessel sped to leeward , checking our weather braces by a few inches at a time until our yards were all but square", "context_len": 78}}}, "expensive": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 75, "context": "Lilian 's experience of concerts was confined to an occasional afternoon performance in the Warford Assembly Rooms , or the military band in the Spa gardens ; but she bravely hid her longings for better things , for she knew that a musical education would be an utter impossibility in the family circumstances , and that Father had strained a point already to allow her to learn from Herr Frankenburg , who was the most expensive teacher in the school", "context_len": 80}}}, "fruits": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 70, "context": "Thou sayst , O my mother , that I have no gift to present to the Sultan , according to the wont of the folk , whilst in fact I have by me a gift and an offering , the like whereof methinketh no king ever possessed , no , nor aught to match therewith ; for [ 338 ] thou must know , O my mother , that the fruits , which I brought in the purses [ 339 ] from the treasure and which I deemed glass or crystal , are very jewels , methinketh all the kings of the world may not compass the least of them , and I , of my companying with the jewellers , know that they are precious stones", "context_len": 127}}}, "rice": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 45, "context": "Aunt Helen was married in September , with the Rector to read the service , and Father to give her away , and Lilian and Peggy for bridesmaids , while the Sunday-school children strewed the path from the church-door with flowers , and Bobby flung rice and old shoes after the carriage , and all the village people came to watch , and say how sweet and pretty she looked , and to wish health and happiness to 'Mrs", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I hope there will be rice enough , but it is very hard to tell when you are n't acquainted with such a thing as the boiling and swelling of it", "context_len": 31}}}, "fascinated": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 1, "context": "They fascinated her with their crude drawing and colours still vivid and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He looked at her with fascinated eyes", "context_len": 7}}}, "jewels": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 77, "word_index": 64, "context": "of Alaeddin ; but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed not to do aught against him , so he was dumb and could make the", "context_len": 128}}}, "niles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Niles had the most glorious time playing with its contents", "context_len": 10}}}, "decorated": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 30, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The Hollanders were evidently making preparations to celebrate their victory , for garlands and decorated masts were being displayed", "context_len": 19}}}, "beads": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 38, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}}}, "bangles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He decorated himself with all the beads and chains and bangles and looked at himself in the mirror", "context_len": 18}}}, "glitter": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 68, "context": "My ! what a lot of bundles ! And now the street is quite empty again , not a person on either side of the one , short block ; but the whistle that has been ringing out more and more clearly at quite regular , three-minute intervals sounds very near indeed , and in another second a gray-suited individual , with soldier-like cap to match and a glitter of shining brass buttons , swings round the opposite corner , and makes a bee-line across the street", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "They like coloured things that glitter", "context_len": 6}}}, "skating": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "The driver kept on to the stable with unslackened speed , and the line followed him , swishing and skating over the water , while the steersman put his helm hard aport , and the packet rounded to , and swam softly and slowly up to her moorings", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 61, "context": "He remembered the fearful joy of the adventure , and the pride , too ; for he had somehow the notion that this young fellow was handsome and fine , and did him an honor by his notice -- so soon does some dim notion of worldly splendor turn us into snobs ! The next thing was his own attempt at skating , when he was set down from the bank by his brother , full of a vainglorious confidence in his powers , and appeared instantly to strike on the top of his head", "context_len": 95}}}, "pond": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 102, "context": "They said there were great hills of strange-looking rocks , with a flat between them , and a brook running through it ; that the Indians used to live there ; and you could see the stones where they made their fires , and find arrow-heads , and Indian things that were buried there ; and Uncle Isaac knew where ; that somewhere along the side of the brook there was red paint , as good as ever was , and that Uncle Isaac had a room painted with it ; that there were partridges there , and way back was a pond , that the brook ran out of , with pickerel in it", "context_len": 115}}}, "blustery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a blustery , snowy afternoon , and they were the only ones on the ice", "context_len": 17}}}, "spied": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 45, "context": "And indeed it would appear that this small maiden -- small even for her six years -- did think so ; for as she talked she was trotting about the room , busying herself with arranging half a dozen trifles , which her quick eye spied out , and which , according to her way of thinking , were not just in proper order", "context_len": 64}}}, "terrific": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 48, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 27, "context": "So , once more , by the matter of a few hours , Konrad von Feldoffer escaped a well-merited death ; while , through ignorance of the terrific preparations made for the blowing up of Messines Ridge , he had unwittingly done the Allies a good turn ; for instead of withdrawing the troops the Hun commander had concentrated a thousand on the mined ridge in order to repel an infantry attack that threatened only in the minds of the German staff", "context_len": 82}}}, "barked": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 74, "context": "The soft spring air floated in through the open window , and she heard the birds twitter and the frogs peep : she heard Abraham Lincoln , the old horse that she used to ride to water before she grew big enough to work , whinney over his hay ; and Goliath , the young giant that had come to take his place in the farm work , answer him sonorously : the dog barked lazily as a nighthawk swept by , and in the distant hen-yard she heard a rooster crow", "context_len": 92}}}, "medal": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Henry Martin hath told me tales of his gallant deeds , and besides , he hath shown me his medal of bronze , inscribed : \u2019 For eminent service in saving ye Triumph , fired in fight with ye Dutch \u2019", "context_len": 42}}}, "suggestions": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 34, "context": "But this Peggy would not allow , and rushed away to the barn to weep off her ill-humour amongst the hay , returning in a much better frame of mind , with several valuable suggestions for Lilian 's make-up ; for she was a generous child at heart , though she could not give up her own way without a struggle", "context_len": 61}}}, "carpentry": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "At the first fall of deep snow Archie had mounted both himself and the children on Canadian snow-shoes , for as soon as his illness had allowed , he had persuaded his aunt to bring his tool-chest into his bedroom , and had beguiled his convalescence with a little carpentry", "context_len": 50}}}, "squirrel": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 48, "context": "Once as the party , who were all growing somewhat tired , were rather quiet , they suddenly heard a long , loud chirrup ; and looking round to the side whence the noise came , there , upon a heap of stones , sat a large gray squirrel , with his tail curled gracefully over his back like a plume , and seeming to call attention to himself by his song", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "This , in squirrel language , was what he said :", "context_len": 11}}}, "stands": {"freq": 110, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 34, "context": "All round , before the circus doors were open , the doorkeepers of the side-shows were inviting people to come in and see the giants and fat woman and boa-constrictors , and there were stands for peanuts and candy and lemonade ; the vendors cried , `` Ice-cold lemonade , from fifteen hundred miles under ground ! Walk up , roll up , tumble up , any way to get up ! '' The boys thought this brilliant drolling , but they had no time to listen after the doors were open , and they had no money to spend on side-shows or dainties , anyway", "context_len": 106}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 95, "word_index": 66, "context": "Many facts therein given have been rejected by the Author as too harrowing in their nature ; and he has preferred to render the contemplation of woe and suffering less painful , by a display of those virtues of patience , resignation , and brave submission to the Divine will , which affliction never fails to bring out in the fold of Christ , whose promise stands ever fast , that the strength of His people shall be equal to their needs", "context_len": 82}}}, "countless": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 40, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 63, "context": "They have worn the rock smooth , so that it glistens like glass in the morning sun , for , as if aware of the folly of urging on its regiments of well-mounted cavalry to come dashing in upon the wild white-maned sea-horses , or the more sober lines of heavy infantry in uniforms of green and blue , the sea has for countless ages bombarded Carn Du with stone-shot in the shape of great boulders", "context_len": 76}}}, "scold": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 107, "context": "and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say afterwards upon the danger of such heedless practical jokes", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "But for what reason she was undergoing this punishment we could not discover , though I doubt not that she was a scold , such as we in England place in the ducking stool", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "grabbed": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 21, "context": "But she got no further , for with a snarl the old man reached out one long , bony arm and grabbed her by the shoulder , raising his stick threateningly , `` I 'll larn ye , ye little varmint , '' he began", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Fenn had again grabbed hold of the horns of the infuriated animal , which , at that instant swung around , presenting a good shot to Bart", "context_len": 27}}}, "dozing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "For as the snakes , most of which had been dozing in the slimy ooze at the bottom of the pit , recovered from their surprise and coiled to attack , Sobrinini pushed through the crowd at the edge of the hole and began to sing", "context_len": 46}}}, "rock": {"freq": 215, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 206, "word_index": 89, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "It is not usual to have a picnic on top of a huge stone upon which we have to climb by a ladder if we want to reach the top , and yet which will rock when we touch it , just as though it were a rocking chair", "context_len": 49}}}, "berries": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 46, "context": "But Dolly turned sulky in her turn , and refused to say a word more ; and Lem , knowing it was useless to try to make her speak when she did not choose , strolled into the woods to see if he could find any berries for his breakfast ; while she , still shivering from her night 's exposure in the Ice Glen , tried to kindle a fire from the wet sticks which lay around ; and finding this in vain , crept to her wretched bed , and tried to warm herself there", "context_len": 97}}}, "tummies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "She also told them of the bad things they must not touch the things which would make their little tummies feel very miserable indeed and which would make them quite , quite sick", "context_len": 33}}}, "cave": {"freq": 171, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 164, "word_index": 42, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "Do you remember the well-known engraving of Una with her lion entering a witch \u2019 s cave ? Now , as I jogged along in my duli , [ 40 ] while Margaret rode on her white pony , she made me think of that picture of Una", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "He did it very carefully so that only the least bit of his curly head was above the cave window-ledge and it only stayed there for a mite of a minute", "context_len": 31}}}, "wondrous": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 4, "context": "Yet there was a wondrous beauty still lingering over them ; they seemed etherialised -- as if an angel 's smile had last stirred their lines , when the spirit went forth , and left its imprint of wonder , joy , and awe thereon ; and Alfgar instinctively turned from them to the blue depths of heaven above , where a few stars were visible , although dimmed by the moonlight ; and he seemed to trace his beloved Bertric 's passage to the realms of bliss", "context_len": 88}}}, "colder": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 12, "context": "Jones had genially assured him that on evenings when the weather became colder he would be welcome to sit and study by the open fire in the sitting-room , a concession for which he had been duly grateful", "context_len": 38}}}, "fort": {"freq": 152, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 152, "word_index": 80, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "appreciate": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 79, "context": "Peggy grew up without many of the luxuries which fall to the lot of richer girls , but she was none the worse for that , and I think , when she saw how bored and uninterested Maud Middleton was in the midst of her round of London society and foreign travel , she felt that it is sometimes better to have the capacity for enjoyment than to have the means without the happy talent of being able to appreciate your good things", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Yet it must not be supposed that Charlotte did not thoroughly enjoy London society , and did not fully appreciate intercourse with polished and intellectual minds", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "I hope you \u2019 ll appreciate it , and not try to make any trouble for us", "context_len": 17}}}, "gorgeous": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 56, "context": "In the trunk were lots and lots of lovely dresses a pink dress , a yellow satin dress , a green velvet coat , a hat with soft , big plumes on it , and , oh , so many lovely things ! Kay was breathless for a moment , she was so excited all those gorgeous things for a little girl who had seen little else beside fishing-nets and such things", "context_len": 72}}}, "princes": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 39, "context": "They were ever afterwards fully persuaded that but for his timely arrival they would have been robbed and murdered , and their bodies hidden away from their sorrowing relatives , like Eugene Aram 's victim , or the little princes in the Tower , and they felt quite an affection for Barlow in consequence , so much so that Bobby determined to wear out his boots as fast as possible , that he might put some business in the old man 's way", "context_len": 83}}}, "princesses": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 73, "context": "maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions , but she can not look on me as all a stranger , even if she hold no memory of those old days at Highgate", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Alexander himself went to pay a visit to the captive princesses the next day", "context_len": 14}}}, "royal": {"freq": 94, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Then the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How now ? Deemest thou Alaeddin worthy to be bridegroom to my daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour ? Hast thou seen and considered this royal building and all these riches which man 's wit can not comprehend ? '' The Vizier , of his envy of Alaeddin , answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , indeed this palace and its building and all these riches may not be but by means of enchantment , for that no man among men , no , not the mightiest of them in dominion or the greatest in wealth , might avail to upraise and stablish [ the like of ] this building in", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 122, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "There were present Ethelred himself , his irrepressible son , the traitor Edric , the bishop , the sheriff of the shire , and the reeve of the borough , with the captain of the hus-carles , or royal guard", "context_len": 40}}}, "majesty": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 88, "context": "would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart of your enemy", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 76, "context": "`` `` Ay , my lord , '' replied the Maugrabin , `` I am indeed a stranger and arrived but yesternight in this your town ; wherefore I beseech thee tell me of the divine gifts of this holy woman and where her place is , for that I have fallen into a calamity and would fain go to her and crave her of prayer , so haply God ( to whom belong might and majesty ) may deliver me from my stress , by means of her intercession", "context_len": 90}}}, "honored": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 82, "context": "had hay in his right shoe , and straw in his left , because a Dutchman was too dumb , as the boys said for stupid , to know his feet apart any other way ; and that the Dutch officers had to call out to the men when they were marching , `` Up mit de hay-foot , down mit de straw-foot -- links , links , links ! '' ( Left , left , left ! ) But the boys honored even these imperfect intelligences so much in their quality of soldiers that they would any of them have been proud to be marker in the Dutch company ; and they followed the Dutchmen round in their march as fondly as any other body of troops", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "The rest of the school were to arrive in the evening , but the Scholarship girls were to have the run of the Hall , and were to be entertained as the honored guests during the whole of the important day", "context_len": 41}}}, "beard": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` So saying , he did off the Maugrabin 's chin veil and the Lady Bedrulbudour looked and saw a man whose beard covered his face ; whereupon she at once knew the truth and said to Alaeddin , `` O my beloved , twice have I cast thee into danger of death ; '' and he said to her , `` O Lady Bedrulbudour , thanks to thine eyes , [ 670 ] no harm [ hath betided me thereof ; nay , ] I accept with all joy everything that cometh to me through thee", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "There was no talk of calling Cap'n Si that morning , for he would not be looking for the flag to be run up , instead he was lying helpless on a cot , his hands swathed in cotton , and his stubbly beard singed by the fire he had vainly tried to put out", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Was I mad to go to him -- to beard the lion in his den ? I did not know ; I only knew that sane or mad , I must do what I had made up my mind to do", "context_len": 41}}}, "brook": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 64, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "However , I could not brook that Thy Grace should honour me [ with thy presence ] in a palace [ 532 ] wherein there was somewhat lacking ; wherefore , so thou mayst know that it was not for lack of ableness that I left it uncomplete , [ 533 ] let Thy Grace go up and see the lattice-work of the kiosk , [ 534 ] an there be aught lacking thereto", "context_len": 74}}}, "jeweled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "They stopped me as I went by the brook , \u2019 said King Snow , and begged me to have some of their jeweled icicles", "context_len": 25}}}, "trickling": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "It had so much to tell of its travels , how it ran down a long and winding hill and how it couldn \u2019 t help trickling and laughing all the time with the jokes and merry tales it kept hearing", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` There , '' she said , pointing to the heavy beam which stretched from shore to shore and below which the water was slowly trickling , `` that 's the bridge we children always use", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "There is something so touching in his looking at such a moment to the Saviour , whose Blood , shed for his salvation , was at that moment trickling down in his view ; and there is something so sublime in our Lord \u2019 s conferring Eternal Life , -- such a gift , -- at the time when He was Himself undergoing the terrible sentence of death ! We may envy your dear suffering child , my Laura , when we think how soon , in human expectation , his eyes will behold the King in His beauty", "context_len": 99}}}, "goblin": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "brothers": {"freq": 120, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 120, "word_index": 55, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}}}, "explain": {"freq": 127, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 119, "word_index": 76, "context": "Harold 's principal achievement lay in The Walrus and the Carpenter , and Marie-Celeste 's in the recitation of Jabberwocky from Through the Looking-Glass ; for not only was she able to slip its almost unpronounceable words quite easily from her tongue , but she remembered the explanation of them given by Humpty Dumpty , when Alice appeals to him a little later on in the story , and he modestly informs her that he can explain all the poems that ever were invented , and a good many beside that have n't been invented just yet", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 7, "context": "But the boy once heard his father explain to one of them that the New Church people believed in a hell , which each cast himself into if he loved the evil rather than the good , and that no mercy could keep him out of without destroying him , for a man 's love was his very self", "context_len": 59}}}, "bedposts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Explain as much and as often as you can that you never carry them far away from home , but that you simply sit on their bedposts and talk to them", "context_len": 31}}}, "bedpost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Good evening , said Peter , as he perched himself on the edge of the right bedpost of her bed", "context_len": 20}}}, "disown": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Think of Sigeferth , of Morcar , of Elfhelm , nay , of a hundred others ; then think not how he has plotted against my life , but how he made my own father hate and disown me ; while he , the pampered favourite , swayed all the councils and betrayed the land", "context_len": 55}}}, "jumping": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 61, "context": "Then Maggie never cared to stay very long , nor indeed did the colonel care much to have her ; for though she tried her best to be gentle and quiet , those restless little hands and feet seemed as if they must be moving ; and she was almost sure to shake his sofa , or to go running and jumping across the room , in a way that distressed him very much , though her merry ways amused him when he was able to bear them", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 33, "context": "Peggy , hard at work behind the scenes , put her eye to a hole in the curtain , and surveyed the prospect below , where children big and little were engaged in jumping over the forms , chasing each other round the stove , and generally acting more like monkeys at the Zoo than civilized human beings , while the Rector strove in vain to collect them at the empty end of the room to play games , and the curate , a shy young man fresh from Oxford , looked as if he would have preferred to wrestle with a consignment of heathens from the Cannibal Islands", "context_len": 109}}}, "quake": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "I saw the people quake and shake when they were told they had to take some bitter medicine", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "But there was no roar or rumbling , such as usually accompanied a quake", "context_len": 14}}}, "swallow": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 49, "context": "No one had ever seen it happen , but every one knew that if you put long horsehairs into a puddle of water and let them stay , they would turn into hair-snakes ; and when you drank out of a spring you had to be careful not to swallow a hair-snake , or it would remain in your stomach and grow there", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Zounds ! Captain Harrison , '' cried the gentleman , merrily , `` are you the first swallow that heralds a summer ? I swear you are the first visitor that has crossed the threshold since we landed yesterday , and I thought you were anchored in Edinburgh", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Miss Frost has just gone to see the doctor because the naughty child made her swallow some repulsive insects instead of her pills", "context_len": 23}}}, "dose": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` She did indeed , and when she was a little girl it was even worse , for instead of lemonade to drink , she was made to take a very bitter dose of herb tea , or a dreadful mess called composition which had every sort of nauseous thing in it you can think of", "context_len": 56}}}, "mantelpiece": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 77, "context": "Do n't know 'em yet ? Bah , what will you do when you come to trigonometry with a hundred and fourteen formulas to learn every night ? Have you learned to stand on your head yet ? What ! Did n't I tell you to do it ? Who taught you to stand on your feet , anyhow ? Why do n't you answer me , eh ? Let 's see you get up on that mantelpiece", "context_len": 78}}}, "complaints": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 96, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "shopping": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "A little girl named Lucy was shopping with her grandmother", "context_len": 10}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 6, "context": "I hope you 'll enjoy your shopping , my dear , ' and Mr Harding started for the barn , serene in the consciousness that he had made his daughter happy in the ability to purchase an unlimited supply of the unnecessary things which girls delight in", "context_len": 47}}}, "loaves": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now the loaf of bread would have smiled , only loaves of bread can \u2019 t smile and if they should laugh they would crumble , so the loaf of bread kept a perfectly straight face", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "Every Whig family contributed loaves of bread and boiled hams ; the Whig farmers brought in barrels of cider and wagon-loads of apples ; there were heaps of pies and cakes ; sheep were roasted whole , and young roast pigs , with oranges in their mouths , stood in the act of chasing one another over the long tables which were spread in one of the largest pork-houses , where every comer was freely welcome", "context_len": 76}}}, "crumble": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was not long before the wall in the front of the Jews ' quarter began to crumble , and it was soon evident that it must , ere many days , succumb to the storm of missiles hurled against it", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "How hard some of these rocks are ! and some are soft , and crumble in your hand", "context_len": 18}}}, "relative": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 65, "context": "Young Dewey shook hands all around , and then sat down on the bed , looking at Mark with a puzzled expression on his face , as much as to say , `` what on earth have I struck -- b'gee ? '' Mark saw his expression and undertook to inform him , making haste to start before the Parson could begin again on the relative merits of Boston and the rest of the civilized universe", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 25, "context": "Uncle Isaac told him there was a vessel going to Salem with timber , and he would send by the captain , who was a relative of his , and get one for him , and then grind it for him , and put in a good white-oak handle , and bend it just right", "context_len": 55}}}, "envious": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 56, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "differently": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 25, "context": "Tell him to do a certain thing in a certain way and he would obey , unless some one else came along and told him differently ; when he would do as the last speaker said , and forget all his former orders", "context_len": 43}}}, "shape": {"freq": 127, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 117, "word_index": 25, "context": "He also brought Ben a cocoanut to keep liquor in ; the end of it , where the eyes are , was made in the shape of a negro \u2019 s face ; the two little round places , where we bore to let the milk out , serving the purpose of eyes , with eyebrows cut over them , and filled with some red matter ; in the mouth was a lead pipe to drink from ; large ears were also made , and a nose ; the figure looked somewhere between a monkey and a negro , funny enough , and was full of rum", "context_len": 107}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 38, "context": "Then presently a stretch of red stuff , zigzagged with arrow-heads of white and orange and green , grew distinct , and under the thick sweep of the Navajo blanket , the impression of a long , still shape", "context_len": 39}}}, "crust": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "Luckily Christmas Day was fine ( for a wet Christmas is enough to damp the stoutest spirits ) , but after that the weather indulged in such a variety of changes , alternately freezing and thawing from day to day , that there was no time for a safe crust of ice to form even on the smallest and shallowest of ponds", "context_len": 62}}}, "canary": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 6, "context": "People ought n't to leave a canary hanging full in the sunshine on a warm day like this ; and then she meditates awhile on the advantages of living on the side of the street that is shady in the afternoon", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "' Oh , yes , you darling birdie ! do you want to say you know all about it ? Bessie , let 's think the canary is a kind of keepsake from Dolly , 'cause you know it seems as if it came by her , and mamma says it is of no use to take the marigolds to town , for they will be quite dead in a few weeks", "context_len": 72}}}, "calls": {"freq": 93, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 25, "context": "He was took sudden , you understand -- in some sort of fit like ; and young master -- that 's Passon Perrient as they calls him , our young missis ' father -- and missis , they was away at Ipswich , and come back all of a scuffle and finds him dead ; and by all I hear , not the value of a penny-piece in the house in money -- plenty of silver and pewter you understand , but no money whatsumever", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 42, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}}}, "replies": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "She has named him Bubsie , and he knows his name too , for whenever she calls Bubsie ! \u2019 he replies with a little Peep , peep ! \u2019", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 25, "context": "Nothing in all the world would be easier than for Florence to open Kitty 's desk , to take out the envelope which contained her replies to the English History questions , and to glance at the momentous question which related to the Earl of Leicester", "context_len": 46}}}, "peep": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 57, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}, "VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 15, "context": "The large old chimney was so wide at the top that he found he could peep right down it , as if he were looking into a well , and could see a good piece of the hearth underneath , with a small fire of sticks burning under a large , three-legged iron pot , and the old woman sitting close by on a low stool , smoking a short clay pipe", "context_len": 72}}}, "sings": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then she \u2019 s an out and out Jacobite , and thumps the piano when she sings Charlie is my darling , \u2019 as though she took it for a Whig", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` Do n't love me , '' said Dolly , who , with her eyes fixed on the lady 's face , had grown quiet , and really seemed to understand what she was saying ; `` loves little gals , maybe , what sings : they has nice frocks , and I aint fit for His beautiful place", "context_len": 59}}}, "apple": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 41, "context": "But how comes she to be alone in this ruined house , like an enchanted damosel of a fairy tale ? 'T is a strange plight for such a tenderly-nurtured maid , for old Sir Gyles guarded her as the very apple of his eye ! And what state did not he keep , and Hunstanton Hall ! And with what a retinue did he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for", "context_len": 128}}}, "hops": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "She puts it on her finger between two wires of the cage , and he hops right over on his little bar and takes it from her finger", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "The blackberries were `` thick as hops '' when they came upon them , -- some still green , some red or half ripe , others as black as ink ; and these the children knew were what they must pick", "context_len": 41}}}, "spatters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He spatters the water about and has just the best time in the world", "context_len": 14}}}, "acts": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "She arrives at decisions so quickly and acts so promptly and is so outspoken , that she 'll get herself and all of us into serious trouble some day , I imagine", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 19, "context": "They had their preferences , of course , as I have hinted ; and one of the most popular acts was that where a horse has been trained to misbehave , so that nobody can mount him ; and after the actors have tried him , the ring-master turns to the audience , and asks if some gentleman among them wants to try it", "context_len": 64}}}, "smoothes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "After his bath he has a treat of delicious lettuce to eat , and then he sits in the sun and smoothes down his feathers", "context_len": 25}}}, "swing": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 17, "context": "Young Hermann 's sword broke against a Danish axe ; he rushed in and got within the swing of the weapon ; both wrestled for the deadly steel , they fell , rolled over and over on the grass ; at length Hermann grasped his opponent 's throat like a vice with his mailed hand , and held till the arms of his foe hung nerveless by the side and the face grew black , when , disengaging his right hand , he found his dagger , and drove it to the victim 's heart", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 10, "context": "Wentworth 's upraised hands ; she set the boys to swing honey-pots , and the girls to play 'Drop the Handkerchief ' ; she boldly interposed her small person between the fists of two fighting hobbledehoys , and seized a little boy by the boot who had ventured to climb up the stove-pipe ; she welcomed some of the parents who had begun to arrive for the entertainment , and found them comfortable places on the benches , even nursing one of the babies while its mother went to the rescue of an older child , who was being forcibly held down and sat upon by several of its companions ; and by the time the bell was rung , and the audience requested to take their places", "context_len": 128}}}, "romp": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "Their names were Mabel and Alice Cunningham , two handsome dark-eyed girls , aged respectively seventeen and fifteen ; Florence Aylmer , who was also fifteen and the romp of the school ; Mary Bateman , a stolid-looking girl of fourteen ; Bertha Kennedy , who had only lately been raised to the rank of the Upper school ; Edith King , a handsome , graceful girl , who competed with Mabel for the honors of the head of her class ; and Kitty Sharston , who had only lately come , and who had some Irish blood in her , and was very daring and very much inclined to break the rules", "context_len": 113}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "Some of the little ones wanted to play , and began to throw hay at each other ; but Tom put a stop to this ; he had not brought them there to romp , he said , and those who wanted to be noisy must go away", "context_len": 48}}}, "ajar": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "He led the girl across the open space in front of his desk , pushed ajar a door , and waved the girl into the private room ; then , returning to his chair , he waited", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "Howell had set the gate of the next world ajar , and allowed some of the glory to steal out and comfort her , the child closed her tired eyes , and slept as quietly as if she had been safe in her bed at home , and the storm and the rushing water nothing but a vision of the night", "context_len": 61}}}, "opens": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 84, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "'It opens so many wounds to re-read the tender words of those who are estranged or gone away from us , and all the hopes and expectations that have come to nothing", "context_len": 32}}}, "tour": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 27, "context": "'They tell me , dear Marie-Celeste ( and they means , of course , your Cousin Theodore and Donald ) , that you are taking a driving tour through the English lakes , and that if I should address a letter to you , care of Miss Dorothy Allyn , no one would be any the wiser ; and that 's just what I 've done , you see , as , for reasons of his own , your Cousin Theodore seems to prefer it", "context_len": 85}}}, "perching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "How he does enjoy flying around and perching back of the different pictures and on the window-sill", "context_len": 17}}}, "perches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He perches on her shoulder and walks around on her hand", "context_len": 11}}}, "tease": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` Did any of the boys and girls ever try the experiment of speaking to him pleasantly ? I am quite sure the boys do their best to annoy him in any way they can contrive , and even some of the girls tease him slyly and call him names , I am told", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "She put her arm through Dallas ' and said , `` Do n't mind him -- he 's an awful tease", "context_len": 21}}}, "munching": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 20, "context": "There was Charley Tuttle , called Chub for short , a roly-poly , round-faced , laughing chap , who was munching peanuts ; Tim Davis , nicknamed Spotty , even more freckled than Ben , thin-legged , sly-faced , and minus the two front teeth of his upper jaw ; Sam Rollins , a big , hulking , low-browed fellow , who lost no opportunity to bully smaller boys , generally known as Hunk ; Berlin Barker , a cold blond , rather good-looking , but proud and distant in his bearing ; and others who did not impress the new boy at all with their personalities", "context_len": 107}}}, "favorite": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 12, "context": "The next thing , and sometimes a better thing than knowing your favorite author , is to know how and where he lives ; and it was a matter of supreme delight to Marie-Celeste that henceforth when she should open Lewis Carroll 's books she should be able to picture him working away here in his study , and just as he really looked , too , for by chance or accidents full-length photograph stood on the mantel , which Dorothy , from her visita few years before , was able to pronounce an excellent likeness , and very characteristic", "context_len": 100}, "NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 15, "context": "Then , too , it was an open secret that she was Sir John 's favorite ; it had been whispered by more than one visitor to another that it was on account of Kitty Sharston that this great fuss had been made , that the Scholarship had been opened to the competition of the school , that the girls were here , that they themselves were here -- it was all on account of this slim little girl with the big eyes and the sweet pathetic face ; and reminiscences of Sir John and Kitty 's father together side by side , shoulder to shoulder , in the trenches before Sebastopol arose in the memory of one or two visitors present", "context_len": 122}}}, "celebration": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 15, "context": "The square occupied the central part of the town , and was reserved for the celebration of festivals , especially the annual busk or fast ( p\u00faskita ) , for the meetings of chiefs , headmen and `` beloved men , '' and for the performance of daily dances", "context_len": 49}}}, "joined": {"freq": 183, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 183, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Beware , woman ! '' shrieked Maggie , and Bessie , thinking it time for her to come to her sister 's aid , joined in the cry , `` Beware , woman ! '' while Franky , always ready to take part in any disturbance , struck at Aunt Patty with his whip , and shouted , `` 'Ware , woman ! '' and Hafed , knowing nothing but that this old lady had alarmed and distressed his young charge , and that it was his duty to protect them , raised his voice in a whoop of defiance , and snatching up the hearth-brush , brandished it in a threatening manner as he danced wildly about her", "context_len": 120}}}, "marvelously": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "The spot was on the northern borders of the ancient kingdom of Sussex -- the land of the Saxon Ella -- a spot marvelously favoured by nature , occupying the summit of a low hill , which commanded a wide prospect on all sides , while itself almost impregnable when fortified , as it was , by ditches and mounds , dug in the usual Danish fashion , for the Danes owed much of their success to their skill in fortification", "context_len": 81}}}, "costumes": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 51, "context": "There were gentlemen , young , old and middle-aged , some in tourist tweeds , some in boating flannels , and some in sober black , most of them with books or road-maps in their hands , while a sprinkling of ladies , both grave and frivolous , in light summer costumes and with gay parasols , completed the party", "context_len": 60}}}, "celebrations": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 91, "context": "They had stories to tell him about the famous hanging gardens , which were artificially constructed in the most magnificent manner , on arches raised high in the air ; and about a vine made of gold , with all sorts of precious stones upon it instead of fruit , which was wrought as an ornament over the throne on which the King of Persia often gave audience ; of the splendid palaces and vast cities of the Persians ; and the banquets , and f\u00eates , and magnificent entertainments and celebrations which they used to have there", "context_len": 98}}}, "returns": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 41, "context": "The total want of all society , except that which the walls of Grimhaggard Hall have the honour constantly to enclose , may perhaps have an effect upon the lady \u2019 s spirits not altogether exhilarating ; but when your brother returns from College , perhaps he may be accompanied by some of his fellow-students", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "My idea is that if Caretto returns with news that the Genoese have no galleys ready for sea , we must do what we can to injure these corsairs", "context_len": 29}}}, "doll": {"freq": 85, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 45, "context": "Did these belong to her ? asked Marie-Celeste , standing in open-eyed wonder before a mosaic table , which had been cleared to make room for a quaint collection of foreign-looking , childish possessions -- a mandolin , a well-worn little missal , a remarkable doll , a necklace or two , numerous little childish trinkets , and thrown over a chair , standing close to the table , a little gown of white silk and exquisite embroidery , yellow and limp with age , but none the less dainty and lovely", "context_len": 92}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Suppose you do n't do any more doll clothes to-day , '' said Mrs", "context_len": 15}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "But Bessie held fast to the Horace ; it was her soldier 's name , and she was quite determined to give her doll the same", "context_len": 26}}}, "workbag": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "She had lovely pink knitted bed-room slippers from her mother , a beautiful doll from her daddy , a workbag from her granny , a paint box from her auntie and a big box of candy from her brother , which he \u2019 d bought with his very own saved-up money , and which to Carry was the best present of all !", "context_len": 63}}}, "granny": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "She had lovely pink knitted bed-room slippers from her mother , a beautiful doll from her daddy , a workbag from her granny , a paint box from her auntie and a big box of candy from her brother , which he \u2019 d bought with his very own saved-up money , and which to Carry was the best present of all !", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "Hartley would say every time Chris chanced to be within hearing distance , when she brought the golden butter to the surface from the depths of the uncanny churn ; and Chris as invariably remarking , There is no fear of that , granny dear , would look as pleased and surprised as though she had not known she could count upon every word of his answer", "context_len": 67}}}, "hospitable": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 21, "context": "We have the cruel and lurking Chicasa , the powerful and ingenious but treacherous and corruptible Cha'hta , the magnanimous and hospitable , proud and revengeful Creek , the aggressive Alibamu , the quarrelsome Y\u00e1massi , and the self-willed , independent Seminole , jealous of the enjoyment of his savage freedom in the swamps and everglades of the semi-tropical peninsula", "context_len": 60}}}, "marvelous": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 25, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "spoon": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Madam , '' he said , when at length he laid down the spoon and straightened himself , `` I do truly hold you have saved my life this night ; and , indeed , not only have you delivered this poor body from danger , but the new spirit your kindness hath infused into me will go far to carry me to my journey 's end", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Barty stopped his cocoanut shell spoon halfway to his mouth", "context_len": 11}}}, "scoop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "I rather like yer looks , younker , added the grim old trapper ; I hope ye \u2019 ll git through right side up and scoop more gold than yer hoss can carry", "context_len": 33}}}, "mouthful": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 75, "context": "`` I did n't want to be settin ' myself up , but I was n't goin ' to have her take no false notions about me , so I says , 'Yes , ma'am , but when a body 's sick , and ai n't no appetite to eat only what one forces one 's self to , I do n't think it no sin to spend a bit for a nice nourishin ' mouthful", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Could n't you give us something to eat ? '' asked Mosely ; `` I have n't eaten a mouthful since yesterday noon , and I feel faint", "context_len": 29}}}, "scolding": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` No , indeed ! Nurse , with Maggie on one arm and Bessie on the other , fairly dancing about the room in her efforts to save the former from Aunt Patty 's clutches , both terrified babies screaming at the top of their voices , both old women scolding at the top of theirs ; while Fred , the monkey , young as he was , stood by , clapping his hands and setting them at each other as if they had been two cats", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 69, "context": "The old lady nodded and smiled , as if she were glad to see them so happy , but Nonesuch seemed not only surprised , but displeased , at finding himself in such jolly company ; and , after some shaking of his head and putting back of his ears , stood stock still in the middle of the road ; nor could all Aunt Patty 's coaxing or scolding , or even some gentle touches of the whip , persuade him to go on , till the whole party were out of sight", "context_len": 94}}}, "eh": {"freq": 90, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 48, "context": "She laid down her work to wipe her eyes , and , observing two of her sons grappling in fraternal war at the house corner , she arose to cuff each one impartially , exclaiming , `` Ea , muchachos ! You fight before my very eyes , eh ? Take that ! and that ! '' Waddling reluctantly back to her sewing , she saw Lola standing in the white-pillared porch of the big adobe house beyond , and a gleam of inspiration crossed the se\u00f1ora 's dark , fat face", "context_len": 92}, "UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 67, "context": "Do n't know 'em yet ? Bah , what will you do when you come to trigonometry with a hundred and fourteen formulas to learn every night ? Have you learned to stand on your head yet ? What ! Did n't I tell you to do it ? Who taught you to stand on your feet , anyhow ? Why do n't you answer me , eh ? Let 's see you get up on that mantelpiece", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Not bad for a city man 's office , eh ? '' he continued , `` all the books are amusing ; you might like to dip into 'em by-and-by , nothing deep or dull , or stodgy here , all light , frothy , and merry", "context_len": 48}}}, "greedy": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "It seems more sordid and greedy than it really was ; the pleasure was to see who could say it first ; and the boys did not care for what they got if they beat , any more than they cared for what they won in fighting eggs at Easter", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 5, "context": "Once a pirate who was greedy stole a piece of cake before he handed the plate to Barty , and Baboo Bajorum stretched out his enormously long hairy arm and seized him by the seat of his trousers and threw him over the rail into the sea", "context_len": 47}}}, "scheme": {"freq": 101, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 100, "word_index": 52, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "If you will stay here for about half an hour while I am attending to something else , I will come back and we will see what scheme we can draw up", "context_len": 32}}}, "nestle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "There we have to fill up the corners , but with you we can nestle down", "context_len": 16}}}, "stewed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "They were real gipsies , too , not the fair-haired hybrid pretenders who go about in neat caravans with muslin curtains to the windows , and wicker baskets slung on the top , but a dark-eyed , Spanish-looking crew , who put up a tent with a pole and a ragged blanket , and stewed their supper in a black caldron hung from three sticks set in the ground", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": "It was not of the least use in the world , and could not be , but the children were allowed to keep it till , one fatal day , when the mother had a number of other ladies to tea , as the fashion used to be in small towns , when they sat down to a comfortable gossip over dainty dishes of stewed chicken , hot biscuit , peach-preserves , sweet tomato-pickles , and pound-cake", "context_len": 77}}}, "prune": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "You haven \u2019 t a heart , prune , dear ; you only have a stone as your heart heart of stone that means hard-hearted , and so forth , said the orange", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "And following that great event are a number of others , and we plan to erect a monument made of prune stones and made only of those stones left by people who \u2019 ve enjoyed the prunes !", "context_len": 38}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Especially if it 's a poor , frail little soul that aint got no mother ! I did what I thought best , though I ca n't afford it no way in the world ! To prune and dress a lie aint going to make it grow into a truth ! '' She rose", "context_len": 55}}}, "happens": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 61, "context": "And what more natural , when you find yourself at the foot of a flight of steps , than to walk up them , no matter if the place does chance to be Windsor Castle ; and then if at the top you find an open door confronting you , what more natural than to walk in , particularly if there happens to be no one to say you nay , and you have half a mind , besides , to seek an audience of the Queen , and report the ungracious conduct of an ungracious little American , who has been unworthily permitted to tread the paths of the royal garden", "context_len": 112}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 27, "context": "He read this book perpetually , when he was not reading his `` Greek and Roman Mythology ; '' and then suddenly , one day , as happens in childhood with so many things , it vanished out of his possession as if by magic", "context_len": 45}}}, "prunes": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 36, "context": "And following that great event are a number of others , and we plan to erect a monument made of prune stones and made only of those stones left by people who \u2019 ve enjoyed the prunes !", "context_len": 38}}}, "bricks": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 9, "context": "The roof was semicircular , and composed of minute bricks , seeming to form one solid mass ; the floor of tiling , arranged in patterns , which could still be obscurely traced by the light of the lamp left by the charity of Sidroc to the prisoner ; for the dungeon was of bad reputation ; lights had been seen there at unearthly hours , when the outer door was fast and no inmate existed", "context_len": 76}}}, "erect": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 45, "context": "The langue of Provence was in charge of this part of the wall , and , leaving them for the present to bear the brunt of the storm , the grand master sent the knights who could be spared , to assist the inhabitants to erect shelters against the storm of missiles falling in the town", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "Since the memorable time when Captain Cook sailed into Botany Bay in 1769 and saw the naked native Australian poising erect to hurl his peculiar weapon , the boomerang has continued to excite the curiosity and amazement of the civilized world ; and truly the finding of such a scientific weapon in the hands of this so-called lowest order of mankind is an astonishing fact , to be simply accepted as another oddity of this odd , topsy-turvy corner of the world", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "Sir John paused for a moment , glanced down the page , then adjusted his glasses , drew himself up very erect and began to read", "context_len": 26}}}, "receive": {"freq": 163, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 158, "word_index": 105, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "Her punishment was that she `` should return to the Gaol from whence she came , and there remain until Saturday next between the hours of Eleven and Twelve of the Clock in the forenoon , at which time she was to be brought to the public Whipping-post , and there receive Twenty Lashes with a Cat-of-Nine-Tails from the hands of the Common Beadle on her naked back till the same shall be bloody , and then return to the said Gaol and remain until her fees be paid ! ''", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Just as I am thou wilt receive , Wilt welcome , pardon , cleanse , relieve , Because thy promise I believe , O Lamb of God ! I come", "context_len": 31}}}, "ditty": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "Bishop , appealed to these simple savages ; a sentimental ditty , such as `` The Anchor 's weighed '' or `` Tom Bowling , '' would hold them breathless and entranced ; `` Rule , Britannia ! '' or `` Should He upbraid '' set them quivering with excitement ; and they seemed to know by intuition that `` The Sailor 's Hornpipe '' was written to be danced to , and they danced to it accordingly a wild , furious , mad fandango in which the extraordinary nature of the gambols of the performers was only equalled by the ecstasy of their enjoyment", "context_len": 105}}}, "alas": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 33, "context": "Father 's reproachful face seemed to rise up out of the darkness , asking 'Where is Bobby ? What have you done with my little boy ? ' Good resolutions made , and alas ! too often forgotten , crowded in now upon her remembrance , and as she listened to the roar of the river , she thought how strange it would seem that they two , so full of life , might in a few hours be floating very still and silent upon that flowing stream , with the world only a memory behind them", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 4, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` As I have said , she was troubled by no human feeling ; but alas ! she inspired what she did not feel , for all the young braves worshipped her , not only as a priestess , but as a peerless maiden , and all their awe could not destroy their love", "context_len": 54}}}, "musical": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 61, "context": "She is not the least like my dear mother , and I never loved her , and I know that the best in me will not be brought to the fore if I am with her ; and I sha n't learn those things which would delight dear father ; I shall not know modern languages , nor be a good musical scholar , nor be able to sing nicely , and I -- I shall hate that life , and my nature may be warped , and I -- but , oh ! I will win the Scholarship", "context_len": 99}}}, "piano": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 66, "context": "Belden told me on the steamer , said Marie-Celeste , with the air of one who settles down for a good talk with a familiar friend , of some musician who heard some one strike two or three chords and then suddenly stop , and after that he ; could not get a wink of sleep till he jumped out of bed and rushed to his piano and struck the chord that belonged at the end of the others", "context_len": 79}}}, "stool": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 120, "context": "seen service under Prince Rupert ; Arthur Conolly , an Irish veteran who had served in the Low Countries , and who had come over from Chichester for the occasion ; Arthur Lewis , a gentleman of Bramshott ; Percy Young , an officer of the navy , who in his earlier days had lost a leg in the action of La Rochelle ; Herbert Collings , a master mariner of Gosport , who used to be a frequent visitor at our house , and who greatly interested me with the account of his adventures off the coast of Barbary ; and Giles Perrin , the landlord of the `` Flying Bull '' , who modestly seated himself on a stool in a remote corner of the room", "context_len": 128}}}, "strove": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 71, "context": "He snatched his dagger from its sheath , and struck down one assailant ; but ere he could raise it to strike again , another leapt on to his back , and clung there until the rest rushed up , when he shouted , Take him alive ! take him alive ! and , throwing down their weapons , half a dozen of the pirates flung themselves upon Gervaise , and strove to pull him to the ground , until at last , in spite of his desperate resistance , they succeeded in doing so", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}}}, "concert": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 33, "context": "I never in my life had heard such a sweet even flow of song from the mouth of any living being , and I had once belonged to the son of a famous concert singer and had in her home heard many beautiful voices -- why ! it was like the lovely upward gush of a fountain -- clear , pure and exquisite", "context_len": 63}}}, "molly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Molly practiced and practiced the pieces she was to play", "context_len": 10}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "MOLLY", "context_len": 1}}}, "practiced": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 3, "context": "Bern Hayden likewise practiced well , putting all his usual snap and dash into everything he did , his accomplishments plainly demonstrating why he had been generally singled out as the fellow who would certainly be chosen as captain of next year \u2019 s team", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "I can write on noble things ; I am well educated ; I am to a certain extent a practiced writer", "context_len": 21}}}, "finest": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 31, "context": "For there was something very stirring in the act , and a stranger to the place would hold his breath in dread as he saw Mark Penelly , who was the finest swimmer at the port of Carn Du , climb up the side of the great black rock upon some fine summer evening , then go round along the narrow shelf of shaley stone , till he stood alone there forty feet above the sea , his white figure as he rested against the black rock , every muscle standing out from his well-knit frame , and his arms crossed , looking like some antique statue in its niche", "context_len": 110}}}, "bedsides": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "From house to house he went , and in every house he went to the bedsides of the little boys and girls and invited them to go on the greatest coasting party of the year", "context_len": 35}}}, "coasting": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "From house to house he went , and in every house he went to the bedsides of the little boys and girls and invited them to go on the greatest coasting party of the year", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "There were places to swim , to fish , to hunt , to skate ; if there were no hills for coasting , that was not so much loss , for there was very little snow , and it melted in a day or two after it fell", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "However , your rescue more than makes up for our failure ; and thankful indeed am I that I made the suggestion that we should put out to save that little craft , though I thought it contained but a few fishermen or some coasting sailors , who had , in ignorance that the Turks were at hand , tried to enter Rhodes", "context_len": 63}}}, "coast": {"freq": 153, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "The question is , ought we to pursue them at once , or ought we to coast along until we find Visconti 's galley ? Three of these Tripoli pirates , crowded as they always are with men , would prove serious opponents , yet we might engage them with a fair hope of victory", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 150, "word_index": 64, "context": "by pirates , you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been seized while travelling with a safe conduct from the", "context_len": 128}}}, "sleds": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "They all put their sleds together and down the hill of Dreams they went until they reached the valley below", "context_len": 20}}}, "packages": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 15, "context": "On the night of the frustrated banquet Ram Dass had kept watch , all his packages being in readiness in the attic which was his own ; and the person who was to help him had waited with him , as interested as himself in the odd adventure", "context_len": 48}}}, "bedrooms": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "But with Marie-Celeste it was very different , She stood in worshipful admiration before all the royal belongings , and when permitted to gaze into one or two of the bedrooms where royalty actually put itself to bed , behind beautiful embroidered draperies , her sense of the privilege accorded her fairly made her hold her breath", "context_len": 57}}}, "tonight": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 6, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "The stars shine as brightly tonight as if they were the lights about His throne ; the very earth has decked herself in her clear and spotless robe of snow in His honour", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 28, "context": "Astonished and delighted at the reward , which represented a very large sum in those days , the sailor stammered his thanks , and added , I hope tonight that if I again have charge of a fire ship , I may be able to do more to prove to your Highness how grateful I am for the gift", "context_len": 59}}}, "hurrah": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 51, "context": "Papa says it is over three hundred miles from the Rocky Mountains to the Sierra Nevadas , yet although we have n't been out of sight of the first for more than a week , we shall see the tops of the others in a few days , and then , hurrah ! we 've only to cross them and we shall be in California ! Wo n't that be glorious ? ''", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Oh , hurrah ! You are nicer than ever", "context_len": 10}}}, "climates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Men began to cover themselves by the long moss ( abundant in southern climates ) , which they tied around their waists ; then were invented bow and arrows , and the skins of the game used for clothing", "context_len": 39}}}, "snails": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` I cruel ? Well , I like that ! I often and often put slugs and snails and worms , and that sort of thing , out of the path for fear any one should tread on them", "context_len": 39}}}, "minnows": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "They fished with minnows , which they kept in battered tin buckets that they did not allow you even to touch , or hardly to look at ; my boy scarcely breathed in their presence ; when one of them got up to cast his line in a new place , the boys all ran , and then came slowly back", "context_len": 61}}}, "necktie": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Nor was Nancy the only one who indulged in a little innocent vanity , for Joe , too , had been so fastidious in his choice of a red-and-blue spotted necktie and a walking-stick with an ivory handle that the children began to suspect the blacksmith 's rosy-cheeked daughter must be at the bottom of it ; and even David had taken a long-tailed coat and a beaver hat out of the retirement of some mysterious bandboxes and had been quite snappy and particular with Mrs", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "From there he half slid , half tumbled to the ground , arose , arranged his necktie carefully , gazed about him solemnly to hear if any one had seen him , and finally set out at a brisk pace for barracks , taking great , long strides , swinging his great , long arms , and talking sagely to himself in the meanwhile", "context_len": 64}}}, "ribbon": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 22, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` By the way , mademoiselle , '' said Florence , `` I have not thanked you yet for getting me that lovely ribbon", "context_len": 24}}}, "neckties": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "So they picked up their suitcases with plenty of Sadie Sneeze \u2019 s best frocks , and plenty of Charlie Cough \u2019 s extra neckties , and lots of Hatty Headache \u2019 s hair ribbons and Susy Sore Throat \u2019 s collars and off they went", "context_len": 46}}}, "candies": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "After that he led her into the deanery -- those are rooms set aside for different uses in connection with the chapel -- and there he gave her a royal box of candies , and sat down and ate some with her , and tried to joke with her , and sipped a little wine , and then another long farewell , and he was gone , never to see the little Queen again", "context_len": 74}}}, "highness": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "And suppose I gain admission to the presence , what shall I take by way of offering to the Sultan 's highness ? It [ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 82, "context": "When the Vizier heard the Sultan 's words , his tongue was tied for despite and he was overcome with exceeding chagrin , forasmuch as the King had promised him that he would marry his daughter to his son ; so , after a little , he said to him , `` O King of the age , Thy Grace condescended to promise me [ 371 ] that the Lady Bedrulbudour should be my son 's ; wherefore it behoveth thine exalted highness appoint a delay of three months , [ 372 ] and God willing , my son 's present shall be greater than this", "context_len": 106}}}, "honor": {"freq": 148, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 133, "word_index": 108, "context": "Oh , the Brotherhood of Knights ! That is what an Order is , you know , and this one was founded way back in the fourteenth century , in the time of Edward the Third ; and they say the way it came to be called the Order of the Garter was this : That King Edward was dancing with the Countess of Salisbury , when she had the misfortune to lose her garter ; and then as he stooped to pick it up , and saw every one smiling , he gallantly announced , 'that they should shortly see that garter advanced to so high an honor and renown as to account themselves happy to wear it", "context_len": 119}, "VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 51, "context": "My boy was by when his mother asked his father , and stood with his heart in his mouth , while the question was argued ; it was decided against him , both because his father hated the tomfoolery of the thing , and because he would not have the child honor any semblance of soldiering , even such a feeble image of it as a boys ' company could present", "context_len": 71}}}, "neighed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "And every day while he was ill they led her under his window and she neighed happily after he had called out with as much strength as he could :", "context_len": 30}}}, "smiles": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 54, "context": "Benito was a very young child , scarcely able to walk on the narrow road without the help of some older and wiser hand , and his weak shoulders could not carry any part of the old dame 's load ; but he put his baby hands beneath it , and gave her loving smiles and gentle words , and these brought her help and comfort , so that she went on her way , strengthened for the rest of the journey", "context_len": 82}}}, "beater": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "But you see , \u2019 said the egg beater , the wonderful part is that I am not cross", "context_len": 19}}}, "fluffy": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "'Is she gone ? ' said a cautious voice , and a fluffy red head appeared in the opening , only followed by the rest of Archie 's body on the full assurance of the entire retreat of the enemy", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 83, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}}}, "forever": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 63, "context": "Bomba wondered whether he would ever see that strange and wonderful city or others like it , wondered if he would always have to spend his life in the jungle , with none save Casson and a few natives for his friends , to none of whom he could speak of the longings that obsessed him , of the aspirations that seemed doomed forever to be thwarted", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 82, "context": "The best place for them was in some woods-pasture where the trees stood free of one another , and around them , in among the tall , frosty grass , the tumbled nuts lay scattered in groups of twos and threes , or fives , some still yellowish-green in their hulls , and some black , but all sending up to the nostrils of the delighted boy the incense of their clean , keen , wild-woody smell , to be a memory forever", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 59, "context": "Aylmer 's heart melted , and she flung her arms round Florence 's neck and whispered through her sobs , `` Oh , my child ! oh , my child ! I had a dreadful feeling last night when your Aunt Susan said that you were my daughter no longer ; but this -- this gives you to me forever", "context_len": 60}}}, "whipping": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 70, "context": "When you moved away from a neighborhood you did not lose all your rights in it ; you did not have to fight when you went back to see the boys , or anything ; but if one of them met you in your new precincts you might have to try conclusions with him ; and perhaps , if he was a boy who had been in the habit of whipping you , you were quite ready to do so", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "She did not look for any thing worse , as the consequence of this wickedness , than a whipping , or perhaps that he should be shut up for a few hours ; and , although she scowled angrily at her brother 's captors , she said nothing to them or to him , but turned and followed at a little distance", "context_len": 62}}}, "cheese": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now , that poor woman , who has counted her cheese by scores , and her butter by tubs , has not a drop of milk except what the neighbors give her , nor a stick of wood but what she picks up", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 61, "context": "He never let on that he suspected for a moment that he was not entirely alone , but , walking over to a tree stump , where , spread out on a newspaper , was the remains of a lunch , he acted delighted at the discovery , picked up a hunk of bread in one hand , a piece of cheese in the other , and , throwing himself on the green sward at full length , proceeded to munch the eatables , with every semblance of satisfaction", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "So he changed his trade : weaving gave place to cheese mongering ; and , after some very hard work and persevering efforts , he placed himself beyond the reach of poverty", "context_len": 32}}}, "grater": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "And what difference does that make ? \u2019 asked the cheese grater , who was feeling sad", "context_len": 17}}}, "pan": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 51, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "However , shortly afterwards , events so pan out that the tables are turned , and it is seen that our hero is not the coward , while his enemy is", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Jones with a pan full of currants in her lap which she was stringing", "context_len": 14}}}, "gravy": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Oh , \u2019 chuckled the gravy spoon , a big , good-natured , easy-going spoon , you don \u2019 t understand at all", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "It was Saturday , so the children were at home , but even their healthy appetites were not equal to the very untempting dish which was set before them , for the unfortunate veal had boiled away to ribbons , and all the goodness had gone into the lost gravy , while the pulpy remains tasted so hopelessly burnt as to be perfectly uneatable", "context_len": 64}}}, "grownups": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "You see I help the gravy at the table and I hear the grownups and children talk", "context_len": 17}}}, "finer": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 59, "context": "Why , of course , Marie-Celeste ; that is , when a man is a great man to start with , and then does something to make himself greater , the Queen may reward him by permitting him to become a member of the Order , if there happens to be a vacancy ; and there 's nothing much finer can happen to a man than that", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "Harold , who had been all through the stables of the Duke of Westminster at Eton Hall , had described something much finer than this -- imposing buildings surrounding a courtyard paved with bevel-edged squares of stone , with not so much as a whisp of hay or straw to be seen anywhere , and in the centre a noble statue of a high-spirited horse , rearing and pulling hard at the bridle , held in the hand of a stalwart groom , who seems fully equal to the occasion", "context_len": 90}}}, "stronger": {"freq": 85, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 41, "context": "For this current Irene made , and when they got there she suddenly rested on her oars , and looking at Rosamund , said , `` Are you afraid , or are you not ? If the current gets a little stronger we will be drifted to the edge of the lake , and at the edge of the lake there is a waterfall , and over it we will go , and , splash ! splash ! splash ! I took a girl there once ; she was my governess , but I was quite tired of her , and knew the fright she would get in when I took her out in the boat", "context_len": 116}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Sergeant Richards needed something good and hot when he came in from duty , and he never took nothin ' stronger , so he ought to have it", "context_len": 29}}}, "complain": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "When the Lady Bedrulbudour saw him put his hand to his head and complain of its aching , [ 664 ] she asked him what was the cause and he said , `` I know not , except that my head irketh me sore", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 49, "context": "[ 358 ] Hast thou any knowledge of her , O Vizier , and knowest thou what is her want ? '' `` O our lord the Sultan , '' replied the Vizier , `` verily women are little of wit ; and most like this woman cometh to complain to thee of her husband or one of her folk , '' The Sultan was not content with the Vizier 's reply , but bade him , an she came again to the Divan , bring her before him forthright ; [ 359 ] whereupon the Vizier laid his hand on his head and answered , `` Hearkening and obedience , O our lord the Sultan", "context_len": 116}}}, "meanings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "It was thought by all the children to be something very fine , and now Maggie sat with it upon her lap while she turned over the leaves , explaining such pictures as she knew , and inventing meanings and stories for those which were new to her", "context_len": 48}}}, "brighter": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 51, "context": "It was certainly delightful to be there in the cool spacious house with the noise of the sea ever in their ears and the cool breeze coming in the windows , and the newcomers felt that all this accounted for the better color in Miss Eloise 's cheeks and for the brighter look in her eyes", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` Yet you are scarcely seen , '' replied his companion , `` and 't is right your flame should be dull , for the cruelty you showed toward the poor widow , I caught my light from a rich man 's fire and injured no one , and that is how my lamp burns brighter than yours", "context_len": 58}}}, "sewer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I came over from Italy with a very poor girl who was a sewer", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "But what a plight he was in ! Plastered with swamp-ooze to his waist , he looked like some sewer laborer", "context_len": 21}}}, "lucifer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Lucifer looked at them as if he sometimes longed to be a real dog and to play as other dogs did", "context_len": 21}}}, "carriage": {"freq": 194, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 194, "word_index": 78, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}}}, "disgrace": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "You have deliberately set about to entrap and disgrace two men vastly your superiors , but so far as in me lies I am going to do all in my power to free them from your clutches and save them from the scandal of this thing , and if I hear of its becoming known through you I 'll --", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 127, "context": "coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to find the poor creature ; so that , his hair being wet , he was a sight to behold when Harry pulled him out , covered with mud from head to foot , and had to be sent behind in disgrace", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` It is worse than folly , '' he muttered ; `` it is disgrace to drag General Harrison 's name in the dust with fruitless entreaties", "context_len": 27}}}, "breed": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 46, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Well , do n't pull a man 's nets in like that , Mas ' r Mark ! '' said the other , now growing interested and hauling steadily in ; `` nets cost money to breed", "context_len": 38}}}, "envy": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 108, "context": ", for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed , with feelings of envy , that many of my fellow-sufferers had already succumbed and become unconscious , if indeed they were not dead", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 71, "context": "There is something so touching in his looking at such a moment to the Saviour , whose Blood , shed for his salvation , was at that moment trickling down in his view ; and there is something so sublime in our Lord \u2019 s conferring Eternal Life , -- such a gift , -- at the time when He was Himself undergoing the terrible sentence of death ! We may envy your dear suffering child , my Laura , when we think how soon , in human expectation , his eyes will behold the King in His beauty", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The authorities of the army , who now had the proud and domineering spirit which had so long excited their hatred and envy completely in their power , listened for a time to what Philotas had to say in his own justification", "context_len": 42}}}, "victoria": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "I praised the victoria to his heart 's content , and then I told him that I thought his taste was much improved", "context_len": 23}}}, "judging": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 45, "context": "So now in a few words he praised them for their earnest and faithful efforts ; said that he had been treated to a perusal of many of the compositions written during the last term in order that he might himself have an opportunity of judging whether Miss Ashton 's verdict were just , and that he had been both surprised and gratified to observe the improvement made by almost every member of the class", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "The bearers lifted the stretcher and continued on their way , while Rifleman Carr , slinging his rifle over his shoulder , hurried towards the German second-line trenches , where , judging by the deep detonations of exploding bombs and the sharp crack of rifle-shots , there was still work to be done", "context_len": 53}}}, "amounted": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 44, "context": "He then identified one of the watches and both of the rings as belonging to him , further stating that the money left by him in his locker was a five dollar bill , a two dollar bill , and change which must have amounted to nearly a dollar and a half", "context_len": 52}}}, "aristocratic": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "The party who had brought me to this remote spot were of an entirely different race from those among whom I now found myself , and the fact that we were making our way toward what was obviously the aristocratic part of the town , coupled with the expressive conversation carried on by the leader of my custodians with three or four individuals who had joined us , led me to surmise -- although of course I did not understand a word of what was said -- that I had been brought up the river as a peace- offering or something of that sort , which conclusion was again the reverse of reassuring", "context_len": 113}}}, "breeding": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 34, "context": "To those friends who thought he lost caste thereby he had always the same argument -- that he saw no reason why the cultivation of fields should counteract the habits of refinement and good breeding to which he had been reared ; that in the colonies educated gentlemen set to work to labour with their hands , and are thought none the worse of : so why not in England , where land is good and markets are plentiful , especially when it involved the keeping of a fine old property which had been in the family for so many hundreds of years ?", "context_len": 104}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I suppose it is breeding", "context_len": 5}}}, "snobbish": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` And last , but not least , '' said Cassowary , `` is dear baby John Graves Simcoe , who already has shown himself rather snobbish and is called Little Big-Wig", "context_len": 32}}}, "terrier": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "In one corner of the room a crowd of Afrikanders swayed in a compact mass , as if eager to wreak their vengeance on someone , while held like a rat in the jaws of a terrier was the man from Muizenberg , his captor being Jan van Eindhovengen", "context_len": 49}}}, "drowning": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 14, "context": "He knew well enough what he ought to do , namely , seize the drowning man by the hair , and then turn upon his own back and float , drawing the other after him ; but on trying this a difficulty met him at the offset : the man 's hair was very short ; but he got over it by grasping his ears , and then , throwing himself back , he struck out with his legs so as to keep afloat and go with the racing current", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 65, "context": "Cassowary , who was almost as much at home under the water as on it , was plunging about like a seal , and just as I arrived had brought Dallas to the surface , and holding him by the collar with one hand and hanging on to the crib with the other she gravely watched Champ and Big Chief giving first aid to the drowning by putting my poor little master on his face and pressing the water out of his lungs", "context_len": 83}}}, "mountain": {"freq": 148, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 148, "word_index": 53, "context": "But still she went on , but only slowly , because of the great press of people around her ; and suddenly an icy wind passed over them , and all fell to the earth shivering and terror-stricken , for they had never felt cold before , and they looked up to the mountain , and lo ! upon the very summit , at the door of the deserted wigwam , stood a terrible figure , clothed in white , and having a face as white as his robes , and his hair was like the long crystals that hang from the roofs of caves that the water goes through , and his eyes were like two great diamonds , white , yet blazing like the sun", "context_len": 127}}}, "rumbling": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "These have ground and polished off every scrap of seaweed , every barnacle , limpet , and sea-anemone , leaving the rock all smooth and bare , while the boulders lie piled to the east in a heap , where the waves that try to take the rock in flank leap amongst them , and roll them over higher and higher , to come rumbling down as if they were tiny pebbles instead of rounded masses of granite and spar-veined stone a quarter , half , and a hundredweight each", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 13, "context": "He had not been rescued more than a minute before there came a rumbling sound , and a spray of mud and water shot up into the air", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Younker , said the hunter in his rumbling voice ; being this the most dangerous p \u2019 int , I \u2019 m going to put two of ye here", "context_len": 29}}}, "moss": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "They had moss soup , a salad of pine needles chopped up very fine , big berry pies and nuts , for they all wanted to eat mountain food at once", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 56, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}}}, "salad": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "Then to Huyler 's for ices and macaroons , then up past St Paul 's and the Common , and then home to a lunch of chicken salad and strawberries and frothed chocolate , in the cool dining-room , with its massive leather-covered chairs and potted plants and roses", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "He called me a little thing , and he took me over the house and over the grounds , and told me , on pain of his direst displeasure , that I was never to give him salad without onions , and then he asked me to marry him", "context_len": 49}}}, "needles": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 9, "context": "They had moss soup , a salad of pine needles chopped up very fine , big berry pies and nuts , for they all wanted to eat mountain food at once", "context_len": 31}}}, "chopped": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 66, "context": "But the 'Ladies ' Column ' should have been a little more explicit as to the quantity of flavouring , for , when it came to a matter of eating , the herbs so entirely predominated that the mutton was utterly lost , and , as she had unfortunately cut up the stalks as well as the leaves , the mixture bore a horrible resemblance to chopped hay", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "But they found out the plot just in time , and Guy was hung up ; or had his head chopped of , I forget which", "context_len": 26}}}, "berry": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "There were indeed lots of blackberries in the spot to which Fred had called them ; and , screened from the rays of the sun , they picked them with comfort ; besides which , many a large berry which they did not pick themselves found its way into their pails ; so that , by the time Hafed came with his offering to Maggie , her own berries made quite a show , and she steadily refused to take his", "context_len": 81}}}, "nuts": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 32, "context": "The best place for them was in some woods-pasture where the trees stood free of one another , and around them , in among the tall , frosty grass , the tumbled nuts lay scattered in groups of twos and threes , or fives , some still yellowish-green in their hulls , and some black , but all sending up to the nostrils of the delighted boy the incense of their clean , keen , wild-woody smell , to be a memory forever", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Now , the apple tree was near Gwen \u2019 s bedroom , and the squirrels could jump from the apple tree to her window-sill , where they were very likely to find nuts waiting for them", "context_len": 36}}}, "leader": {"freq": 121, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 121, "word_index": 70, "context": "Among other things were two or three peacock feathers , a turkey wing , some pebbles from the brook where papa used to sail his boats when he was a boy , a piece of rusty tin pipe , which , because it looked black and smoky , and came from the field where the burnt barn had stood , they persuaded themselves must be a part of the very leader down which papa slid when he ran for the ladder to save his little brother , -- all these , and other treasures of like value , were carefully collected and stowed in the wagons , to be carried to the Lake House , and thence to the city", "context_len": 120}}}, "bellowed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "Jerusalem , what is this ! bellowed a voice , and Joe Griffin leaped out from the midst of the burning pile ; the brands rolled off the back of his woollen shirt , which was thoroughly singed , while a fox-skin cap he wore was scorched to a crisp , as was his hair ; he ran round and round , as though he was mad , blowing his fingers ( which where slightly burned ) , and slapping them on his thighs , while on his face was a mingled expression of pain , arising from the burn , and anger at being outwitted", "context_len": 106}}}, "lungs": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 82, "context": "Cassowary , who was almost as much at home under the water as on it , was plunging about like a seal , and just as I arrived had brought Dallas to the surface , and holding him by the collar with one hand and hanging on to the crib with the other she gravely watched Champ and Big Chief giving first aid to the drowning by putting my poor little master on his face and pressing the water out of his lungs", "context_len": 83}}}, "whistled": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 20, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}}}, "linen": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 31, "context": "My uncle busied himself till all traces of his worry lost themselves in his activity ; my aunt bustled about the house , driving the servants hither and thither , bringing linen from the huge oak chest , furbishing the massive furniture , and causing a huge supply of viands to be prepared for the entertainment of our guests -- for the invited company numbered close on a score , so that our house would be filled to overflowing , and rooms had to be engaged at `` The Bell Inn '' and `` The Blue Posts ''", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "He wore a short tunic , girt close around him , and over it a linen breast-plate , strongly quilted", "context_len": 20}}}, "purred": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "A word from the old woman caused the animal to jump towards her , and , climbing on her shoulders , it mewed and purred with a fearsome delight", "context_len": 29}}}, "queerest": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 48, "context": "He told me that he hated salad without a good dash of onion in it , and as he spoke he looked at me as much as to say , `` Do n't you ever give me salad without onion , '' and I began to feel the queerest sensation , as if I was being mastered , creeping over me", "context_len": 61}}}, "cushions": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 45, "context": "In the grate there was a glowing , blazing fire ; on the hob was a little brass kettle hissing and boiling ; spread upon the floor was a thick , warm crimson rug ; before the fire a folding-chair , unfolded , and with cushions on it ; by the chair a small folding-table , unfolded , covered with a white cloth , and upon it spread small covered dishes , a cup , a saucer , a teapot ; on the bed were new warm coverings and a satin-covered down quilt ; at the foot a curious wadded silk robe , a pair of quilted slippers , and some books", "context_len": 112}}}, "comforts": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 33, "context": "In what strange world had he been living ? Had that sullen , desperate wretch been indeed Dick Harrison ? He realized that he was alive ; he could still enjoy the common comforts of food and fire ; he could think ; he could plan ! His feet were once more treading solid earth ; his brain began to spin anew the projects that had delighted him of yore ; his heart began to stir with the hopes of old", "context_len": 81}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "But he gave me to understand that if for any reason his return was delayed I might act on my own discretion , and tell you what comforts me beyond all possible words , and what may also cheer you , for I can scarcely think , my darling , that the love of a man like that would be unreturned by a girl like you , when once you knew , Westenra , when once you surely knew", "context_len": 79}}}, "upstairs": {"freq": 108, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Oh , that is all very fine , '' he said , `` but come now ; what have I done to make myself obnoxious ? There is the old lady upstairs , she has taken no end of a fancy to you , she says you are the most charming and the prettiest girl she has ever seen , and what have you to say against my mother ? Let me tell you that she has come to this house on purpose to make your acquaintance", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 13, "context": "Seizing the first opportunity of slipping away from his schoolmates , he rushed upstairs to the dormitory with the design of throwing the note under Seabrooke 's bed or bureau , where it might be supposed to have fallen ; but seeing the trunk standing there ready packed , the impulse had taken him to put it in that , and without reflecting -- perhaps hardly caring -- that this would place Seabrooke in a still more embarrassing position , he thrust the note within , as Charlie Henderson saw , and fled from the room", "context_len": 96}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "My man and I carried him upstairs to bed", "context_len": 9}}}, "mouse": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 30, "context": "The wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his", "context_len": 128}}}, "frisk": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "They would frisk about the pantry , behind the shelves and through the drawers which were often left half-open", "context_len": 19}}}, "shelves": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 47, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "'We shall be quite dry there , for the ground shelves up", "context_len": 12}}}, "drawers": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 31, "context": "She took down the dividing curtain between herself and Jane Denton without asking any one 's permission ; and she slept in the bed intended for Jane , and rearranged the drawers , putting them into another part of the room ; and complained about the wardrobe , saying that she would like it put opposite the door instead of in its present position", "context_len": 64}}}, "inconsiderate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "I certainly don \u2019 t consider myself responsible for any rash and inconsiderate words , and I am surprised that an honorable man should seek to hold me to them", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "If others were inconsiderate to her , I never saw her resent it", "context_len": 13}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "We had chased a merchant vessel loaded with a rich cargo of gold and splendid stuff and ivory , and when we had caught it we had behaved in our usual rude and inconsiderate way", "context_len": 35}}}, "bitten": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 49, "context": "The first thing his owner would know when he pulled the chain out was that there was no coon at the end of it , and then he would have to poke round the inside of the box pretty carefully with a stick , so as not to get bitten ; after that he would have to see which tree the coon had gone up", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "But still what do we care about words except a few choice ones such as bite and bitten and will bite ? So the mosquitoes hurried , and some of them went on one piazza where people were sitting and some on another , and were happy over their mean banquet", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "-- As I was engaged yesterday with a party of our boys , I was interrupted by hearing that my poor dear Ayah had been stung -- bitten , as the people incorrectly say -- by a scorpion", "context_len": 38}}}, "cardboard": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I like little cardboard boxes best that we can nibble through", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "It was in vain that Lilian held him in her arms , and tried to soothe him to sleep ; he grunted and whined , and wriggled down on to the floor , where , with a shrill bark , he unearthed a cardboard box full of old gloves , which had been stored away under the bed", "context_len": 58}}}, "nibble": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "The old horse jogged on , trotting or walking as he liked , stopping at the roadside now and then to nibble at a tempting bunch of grass or a bit of fresh foliage , or to plunge his nose into the cooling waters of a wayside stream", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "They did not do very much except nibble at bran and lettuce-leaves , it is true , but they were pretty , soft creatures , with long , silky ears , and it was fun sometimes to let them out for a scamper on the granary floor", "context_len": 47}}}, "coasted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Two hours later the prizes got up sail , and , accompanied by the galley , coasted quietly along the shore , arriving , late in the afternoon , at Muravera", "context_len": 31}}}, "biscuits": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 9, "context": "So the kind nurse opened the paper containing the biscuits , and spread it on the flat stone on which the children sat ; next she pulled two broad mullein leaves , and put a handful of berries on each , and then having produced the drinking cup she always carried when the children went on an expedition , she asked John where she should find a stream , and one being near at hand as usual , the cup was soon filled and placed beside the other things", "context_len": 89}}}, "scampered": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 29, "context": "True , after their first dread of her wore off , and they found she was by no means so terrible as they had imagined , they no longer scampered off at the least sound of her voice or glimpse of her skirts , as they had done at first ; and Bessie even found courage to speak to her now and then , always looking however , as if she thought she was running a great risk , and could not tell what would be the consequence of such boldness", "context_len": 91}}}, "plants": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 10, "context": "The Middletons had a fine collection of orchids and rare plants , which were much admired by the young visitors , though simple Peggy could not help thinking they were not half so pretty as the roses and lilies in the old garden at home , and certainly the grand Scotch gardener was not nearly so nice as David or Joe , for he seemed quite to resent their presence and followed them about grimly , for fear they should disturb anything , or pluck any of the fruit or flowers", "context_len": 91}}}, "battleships": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The reason they \u2019 ve been given this name is because some one who was about to name them decided they looked like old battleships", "context_len": 25}}}, "gas": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 51, "word_index": 26, "context": "A newspaper cost sevenpence ; there were no national schools or Sunday schools , no penny publications , no penny postage , no railways , no gas , and no free libraries , and no free education ! Yet so resolute was he in his desire for education that , though he was not even allowed a candle after the elders went to bed , he would sit up till late at night reading by the glow of the embers", "context_len": 80}}}, "enters": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "The Portuguese Man-of-War is quite large , and when it is like this it is filled by a kind of gas which enters into it and which makes it look even larger than it is", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "And as the butterfly sips from the little flowers and enters right into the hearts of many of them , it whispers :", "context_len": 23}}}, "floats": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The thistledown that floats upon the breeze , The thorny weed which from my path I spurn , The insect which I crush beneath my tread , Are not to me more insignificant , More worthless -- than the Slave whom thou hast named", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "He accordingly made floats and rafts , which were supported by skins made buoyant by inflation , or by being stuffed with straw and hay", "context_len": 25}}}, "colony": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 11, "context": "They had been the staunchest Indian supporters of the new British colony , and had sent 28 men of auxiliary troops to Colonel Barnwell , to defeat the Tuscarora insurrection on the coast of North Carolina ( 1712-13 ) , when they suddenly revolted on April 15th , 1715 , committed the most atrocious deeds against helpless colonists , and showed themselves to be quite the reverse of what their name indicates ( y\u00e1masi , y\u00e1massi , the Creek term for mild , gentle , peaceable [ 44 ] )", "context_len": 90}}}, "feelers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Then there are members of the colony who also hang from under the many members on top , and they are the fingers or the feelers for the community", "context_len": 29}}}, "grapes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "be you laid hands if it were but on a hen , if you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old Noll and", "context_len": 128}}}, "ribbons": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 82, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "cells": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}}}, "sting": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "Minnie finds that there are holes in the ground , hidden under the grass , which make her trip , and there are disagreeable plants growing among the daisies , which sting her legs , and even some that tear little slits in her frock but when one goes a-hunting one must not be particular ; another time she will ask for a pair of gaiters , like Bertie \u2019 s , and a very thick frock", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 56, "context": "I ca n't bear to have the Romney picture go , either , because it 's just like Peggy ; it might have been painted from her ' -- the vision of the family portraits and the armour of her ancestors being turned over and valued by oily gentlemen of the Hebrew persuasion adding a double sting to the trial", "context_len": 60}}}, "slay": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 66, "context": "`` Oh , this was to have been my wedding day , my wedding day ! '' and he clasped his hands in agony ; then the thought of his master -- his slain lord -- returned , and he cried , `` O Edmund ! my master , my dear master , so good , so gentle , yet so brave ; who else could slay him ? what fiend else than Edric , the murderer Edric ? That they should think I , or any one else than Edric , could have done such a deed , such an evil deed ! ''", "context_len": 105}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Sailors are a turbulent race , and were you recognised here they might make a tumult , and slay you before a word of what was going on reached me", "context_len": 30}}}, "fishes": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 60, "context": "She loved birds ; she told me afterward ; it was perfectly delicious to feel a little feathery ball wiggling between your claws , but she said , `` I wanted to live , so I gave them up and as I just had to hunt something , I got after the things that even my masters hunt , namely fishes", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` He is an old fisherman who used to have a fishing vessel of his own , but now he is too old to go to the Banks , so he just fishes around a little , and takes people out rowing or sailing when they do n't want to go too far", "context_len": 53}}}, "colors": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 22, "context": "Among the tribes of the Maskoki family , we notice the following ethnographic practices : the use of the red and white colors as symbols of war and peace , an extensive system of totemic gentes , the use of the Ilex cassine for the manufacture of the black drink , the erection of artificial mounds , the belief in a deity called `` Master of Life , '' and original sun-worship", "context_len": 72}}}, "add": {"freq": 85, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 84, "word_index": 19, "context": "[ Footnote 436 : The whole of the foregoing passage is so confused that I think it well to add here ( l ) a literal translation , as I read it : `` So the Vizier , yea , indeed , he marvelled at the greatness of that wealth more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more when he saw the Sultan that he was satisfied with ( or accepted of ) the bride-gift and the dowry ; however , it was not possible to him that he should gainsay the truth and should say to the Sultan , 'He is not worthy ; ' only , he practised with a device upon the Sultan so he should", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "To these plants the modern Creeks add , as a fifteenth one , the p\u0101 ssa ; cf", "context_len": 18}}}, "museum": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "cities": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 100, "context": "Three times have you earned special credit ; upon the first occasion , the grand master -- no mean judge of conduct and character -- deemed you worthy of secular knighthood , an honour which has not , in my memory , been bestowed at Rhodes upon any young knight ; on the second , you were promoted to the command of a galley , though never before has such a command been given to any , save knights of long experience ; and now , for the third time , the councillors of one of the greatest of Italian cities are about to do you honour", "context_len": 107}}}, "copy": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 26, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}, "VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 71, "context": "`` Ah , dear , an ' it 's glad enough I am to get ye back , but you 've not grown very fat an ' rugged looking , but them babies do beat all ! They 're quite ginteel one may say , an how they do run and talk ! You 'll have your hands full , I 'm thinkin ' , if they 're goin ' to copy Jack ! ''", "context_len": 75}}}, "colonies": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 45, "context": "To those friends who thought he lost caste thereby he had always the same argument -- that he saw no reason why the cultivation of fields should counteract the habits of refinement and good breeding to which he had been reared ; that in the colonies educated gentlemen set to work to labour with their hands , and are thought none the worse of : so why not in England , where land is good and markets are plentiful , especially when it involved the keeping of a fine old property which had been in the family for so many hundreds of years ?", "context_len": 104}}}, "lavenders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The little creatures are lovely lavenders , and green , and purples , and browns , and pinks all like a lovely mass of soft and delicate colors", "context_len": 28}}}, "purples": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The little creatures are lovely lavenders , and green , and purples , and browns , and pinks all like a lovely mass of soft and delicate colors", "context_len": 28}}}, "browns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The little creatures are lovely lavenders , and green , and purples , and browns , and pinks all like a lovely mass of soft and delicate colors", "context_len": 28}}}, "mass": {"freq": 173, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 172, "word_index": 47, "context": "' I suppose not , ' said Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I am going to mass with the king ; shall I say a Pater and an Ave for you , since you are prevented from being there", "context_len": 27}}}, "inhabitants": {"freq": 97, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 97, "word_index": 64, "context": "he said , I have heard from my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction , you shall meet", "context_len": 128}}}, "whistle": {"freq": 78, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was growing quite dark before Father returned , and I think , though neither of them would have confessed it , the children were both rather relieved to hear his cheery whistle outside the door , for it was just a little eerie sitting by the peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 24, "context": "Pipe the men aft , '' he shouted , and in obedience to the shrill cry of the bosn \u2019 s mate \u2019 s whistle the whole ship \u2019 s company , including the officers , assembled at the waist , save the men who manned the capstan bars", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "No time was lost in booting the pigskin out of danger , and before another formation could be made the whistle blew , and the first half was over", "context_len": 29}}}, "clowns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He loved the circus band , the clowns , the animals", "context_len": 11}}}, "peanuts": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 21, "context": "There was Charley Tuttle , called Chub for short , a roly-poly , round-faced , laughing chap , who was munching peanuts ; Tim Davis , nicknamed Spotty , even more freckled than Ben , thin-legged , sly-faced , and minus the two front teeth of his upper jaw ; Sam Rollins , a big , hulking , low-browed fellow , who lost no opportunity to bully smaller boys , generally known as Hunk ; Berlin Barker , a cold blond , rather good-looking , but proud and distant in his bearing ; and others who did not impress the new boy at all with their personalities", "context_len": 107}}}, "lions": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 72, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}}}, "performers": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 95, "context": "Bishop , appealed to these simple savages ; a sentimental ditty , such as `` The Anchor 's weighed '' or `` Tom Bowling , '' would hold them breathless and entranced ; `` Rule , Britannia ! '' or `` Should He upbraid '' set them quivering with excitement ; and they seemed to know by intuition that `` The Sailor 's Hornpipe '' was written to be danced to , and they danced to it accordingly a wild , furious , mad fandango in which the extraordinary nature of the gambols of the performers was only equalled by the ecstasy of their enjoyment", "context_len": 105}}}, "parade": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` And the king proposes to enrol you amongst his chosen warriors on that day ; he has marked the skill you have displayed in the mimic contests with spear or sword , your skill as a horseman , and he wishes to see whether in actual battle you will fulfil the promise of the parade ground", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Yes , I will ! Them ole cadets git after me , now , by jingo , I 'll go out there an ' yank some of 'em out that parade an ' lick them all t'once", "context_len": 38}}}, "grounds": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "He used to follow the circus parade as it came through the town and he didn \u2019 t mind if it was always late , for he could go to the circus grounds with some of the other boys and see them unpack , and maybe he could sometimes help a little , too !", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 106, "context": "`` So he broke away from the sorcerers and plunged the enchanted one into the boiling cauldron , unheeding the agony he suffered or the cries of the little animal he held , and in a moment the moon plunged beneath the horizon ; Mahdrusa arose from her long sleep ; the sorcerers fell into the boiling cauldron and were consumed ; and all the people shouted for joy , and with one accord cried that the Ohoolees should from henceforth be their brothers , and the young brave who had rescued Mahdrusa , their chieftain , when her father was called to the happy hunting grounds", "context_len": 107}}}, "afford": {"freq": 93, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 12, "context": "But I have learned by experience that any fellow can \u2019 t afford to try squaring up scores with an enemy by fighting or any sort of personal violence ; I \u2019 ve found out he only injures himself the most , and I believe there must be other and better ways of getting even", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 78, "word_index": 8, "context": "I am a working-woman myself and can not afford to do much on my own account , and there 's sights of trouble on every side ; but , if you 'll excuse me , I 'm bound to say I 've given away many a bit of bread since that wet afternoon , just along o ' thinking of you -- an ' how wet an ' cold you was , an ' how hungry you looked ; an ' yet you gave away your hot buns as if you was a princess", "context_len": 94}}}, "chores": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was nearly eight o'clock when Bart got through with his supper , did his house chores , mended a broken toy pistol for one junior brother , made up a list of purchases of torpedoes , baby-crackers and punk for the other , and helped his sisters in various ways", "context_len": 51}}}, "daddies": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "And , \u2019 continued the Goblin , I tell mothers and daddies not to forget the good , old-fashioned way of putting a ring , a thimble and a button in the cake", "context_len": 33}}}, "teary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Marie-Celeste declared she could almost see the lump in the poor little fellow 's throat , and the recollection of the utter hopelessness of the teary brown eyes lingered rather sadly for a while in the memory of all of the party", "context_len": 42}}}, "hospitals": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "However , she got out of this Slough of Despond , and having become convinced of God 's love she told the good story to the sick in hospitals , to soldiers and sailors without number , and has done more for the good of Jack Tar afloat and ashore than perhaps any other man or woman", "context_len": 57}}}, "visits": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 109, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Later on , when the Sultan visits the enchanted palace for the first time , Alaeddin `` brought him to the high kiosk and he looked at the belvedere ( teyyareh , a square or round erection on the top of a house , either open at the sides or pierced with windows , our architectural term 'lantern ' ) and its casements ( shebabik , pl", "context_len": 68}}}, "lists": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Do you all agree that since Lena Neville has been providentially prevented from continuing her efforts , and since she made so much improvement while she was able to enter the lists , that Bessie shall be permitted to resign this reward to her , and that she shall be the one to name the candidate for my trust ? ''", "context_len": 62}}}, "thousands": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 2, "context": "Yet the thousands were heavy on our hearts , and I meditated some means of carrying tidings of their danger to our pagan neighbours ; but I knew nothing of the details of the plot , only that there was a plot , and I knew that if I sent a brother , the Danes , in their hatred to monks , would probably set their huge dogs at him before he could speak , and perhaps worry him to death", "context_len": 81}}}, "shrieking": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "And Jay Rial \u2019 s face was full of smiles as he looked at the thousands of children who were shrieking with joy over the circus , and he said , My dream has come true", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The west and the east winds make music through it , and the shrieking storm the sweetest music of all", "context_len": 20}}}, "muff": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "The children stood and looked at each other for a few minutes ; and then Sara took her hand out of her muff and held it out across the counter , and Anne took it , and they looked straight into each other 's eyes", "context_len": 45}}}, "telephone": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 16, "context": "Miss Forster opened her eyes wide enough , as it was , to see a neat telephone , made of two empty cocoa-tins and a piece of waxed string , fixed up between the house and the stables , while a small windmill on the scullery roof turned the coffee-grinder in the kitchen , to the huge amazement and delight of the cook", "context_len": 63}}}, "mildred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Mildred stood by listening", "context_len": 4}}}, "receiver": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "The grandfather was a fervent Methodist , but the father , after many years of scepticism , had become a receiver of the doctrines of Emanuel Swedenborg ; and in this faith the children were brought up", "context_len": 37}}}, "department": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 58, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}}}, "spelt": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "Ispah\u00eddshi , name of a headman , and usually spelt Spiechee : `` whooping , brawling '' while taking off the scalp", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I do , Mummy ; you know perfectly well that you have never yet spelt 'arrange ' right , nor 'agreeable", "context_len": 22}}}, "palace": {"freq": 246, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 246, "word_index": 91, "context": "his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like , and be it complete with all its requisites of kingly and magnificent furniture and so forth", "context_len": 128}}}, "grandfathers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "The Creeks called the Delaware and Sh\u00e1wano Indians grandfathers , because they regard their customs and practices as older and more venerable than their own ; others state , because they occupied their countries further back in time than the Creeks did theirs", "context_len": 43}}}, "kings": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 93, "context": "son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not give them his daughter", "context_len": 128}}}, "sizzling": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "High up in the air there was a flash of bluish fire , a sizzling , as of red-hot iron plunged into water , and then a shower of sparks", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Yes , but it would n't be so nice to be in a frying pan sizzling over a fire", "context_len": 20}}}, "whereas": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 54, "context": "[ Footnote 153 : I prefer this old English form of the Arabic word Meghrebiy ( a native of El Meghreb or North-Western Africa ) to `` Moor , '' as the latter conveys a false impression to the modern reader , who would naturally suppose him to be a native of Morocco , whereas the enchanter came , as will presently appear , from biladu ' l gherbi ' l jewwaniy , otherwise Ifrikiyeh , i", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "Later on he would spend four or five hours daily in Bible reading , meditation and prayer , so that whereas he had written a few months earlier : `` Oh ! dear mother , I wish I felt more what I write ! '' he was now daily becoming more earnest , patient and watchful , and was gradually putting on the whole armour of God", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "There need be no difficulty on this account about their writing , because letters could be forwarded promptly from Oxford , whereas if they were able to say where Harry was , then Ted would have to be accounted for , too , and there was no telling where that would end", "context_len": 52}}}, "appreciated": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 8, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "They would like to have more of us , we , the fine pieces of coal , the coal which is at last appreciated , the coal which at last gets thanks for the warmth it gives , and the coal which is missed so sadly when it isn \u2019 t around !", "context_len": 53}}}, "warmth": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 6, "context": "But as it happened , the warmth of the wrapping , the influence of the spirit that had been poured liberally down his throat , and the chafing , combined with his naturally strong animal power to revive him from the state of insensibility into which he had fallen , and long before they reached the granite pier of the little harbour his eyes had opened , and he was staring in a peculiarly puzzled way at Mark Penelly , who still knelt beside him in the double character of medical man and nurse", "context_len": 94}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "The principal continued by commending with some warmth the individual whose impulses led him , regardless of personal danger or the chance of being misunderstood , to stand up in defense of one who was being mistreated and abused", "context_len": 39}}}, "bonfire": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 55, "context": "And , Dream Fairies , continued the Dream King , I \u2019 m going to tell each girl and boy how every mean speech she or he thinks of and doesn \u2019 t make , or doesn \u2019 t repeat , will come straight to Dreamland and every week we will have a great big bonfire of them", "context_len": 58}}}, "jellyfish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "In fact , they look very much like the ordinary jellyfish", "context_len": 11}}}, "ink": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "I could chat much longer with you , dear one , but I have other notes to write ; and my pen , or ink , or paper , or something or other , will go wrong to-night , so as to make the act of writing irksome , as well as the note untidy", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 61, "context": "Next he started to carry outside and pile neatly at the blind end of the building all the boards , boxes and other debris littering up the room , swept it , and selected two packing cases and nailed them up into a convenient impromptu desk , manufactured a bench seat out of some loose boards , set his pen , ink and paper in order , and felt quite ready for business", "context_len": 73}}}, "jellyfishes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "At last the big fish had left , and all the jellyfishes congratulated the inkfishes on their wonderful means of protection , and they said they felt pretty sure that Nelly Jellyfish would be well looked after on her visit as well as have a wonderful time", "context_len": 47}}}, "entertainments": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 53, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}}}, "nelly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Nelly Jellyfish arrived at exactly the hour she was invited to commence her visit", "context_len": 14}}}, "commence": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 59, "context": "At the end of that time he roused himself from his stupor of grief and disappointment , and , abandoning the idea of any further attack upon the point that had cost him so dearly , he ordered the troops to move round and renew the attack upon the wall in front of the Jews ' quarter , and commence the construction of a battery on the edge of the great ditch facing the retrenchment behind the breach before effected", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "On one occasion , after he had spent a whole night in drinking and carousing , the guests , when the usual time arrived for separating , proposed that , instead of this , they should begin anew , and commence a second banquet at the end of the first", "context_len": 50}}}, "dove": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "\u2019 So he dove through the water toward little Nelly Jellyfish", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` I -- I never sh-should have thought it , '' said Barty , moving back a little , because the pirate Captain began to swing his sword that very minute , and it looked rather alarming even if he were as gentle as a dove", "context_len": 46}}}, "sneezed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "One little girl who had sneezed so hard and so much lost her voice for several days , for the sneezing powder got down in her throat , and her throat has never been quite so strong since", "context_len": 38}}}, "arranges": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "'We make our plans for this world , ' he said softly , speaking as much to himself as to Peggy , 'and say we will do this or that , but sometimes God takes it out of our hands and arranges it for us ; but His ways are better than our ways , and His thoughts than our thoughts , and , after all , death is but the gate to life immortal", "context_len": 75}}}, "pepper": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "After this Lilian determined to provide more generously , and astonished the grocer by ordering three pounds of cayenne pepper , and a like quantity of mace ( embarrassing possessions which lingered in the spice-cupboard for years before they were eventually finished ) , and generally running the household on so liberal a scale that Father had to interfere and preach economy", "context_len": 62}}}, "grieve": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 78, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Bradford , `` would my Bessie wish Uncle Ruthven sent away again , and so grieve dear grandmamma , who is so glad to have him at home once more , to say nothing of his other friends ? I hope my dear little daughter is not giving way to that ugly , hateful feeling , jealousy", "context_len": 57}}}, "mash": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "But as he was talking , along came the cook to mash Potato and his family , for dinner was almost ready", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Her missile flew far above the man \u2019 s head , and , running backwards in a vain endeavour to catch it , he fell heavily into a large trough of pigs \u2019 mash , where , half-stunned by the force of his fall , he lay wallowing in the sticky mess , till he was helped out by his comrades , to the accompaniment of their jeers and laughter", "context_len": 70}}}, "require": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 94, "context": "Among the twenty millions of people in the United States , there are perhaps two millions , between the ages of fifteen and twenty-five , who wish to become acquainted , in general , with the leading events in the history of the Old World , and of ancient times , but who , coming upon the stage in this land and at this period , have ideas and conceptions so widely different from those of other nations and of other times , that a mere republication of existing accounts is not what they require", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Quoth the Sultan , `` On what wise shall we rid ourselves of this man , seeing I have given him my word and a King 's word is his bond ? '' `` O my lord , '' answered the Vizier , `` my counsel is that thou require of him forty dishes of pure virgin gold , full of jewels , such as she [ 426 ] brought thee the other day , [ 427 ] and forty slave-girls to bear the dishes and forty black slaves", "context_len": 90}}}, "blinding": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 27, "context": "At length she drew up about fifty yards from our starboard quarter , and even at that short distance they did not scent danger , their eagerness blinding them to the fact that twenty-five closed ports separated them from a death-dealing hail of iron", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A blinding sheet of driving rain , that darkened the air and drove against the river , and rose again in clouds of spray ; a gale that lashed the water into fury ; and darkness that shut out the river , and the boat , and the swimmer , and left nothing but a humbled group of shivering cadets", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "Through the blinding , hurrying flakes she could see twinkling lights here and there , and presently she located the piece of woods just beyond her own home , but it was far to the left , and she realized that she had turned into a by-road instead of keeping to the main one", "context_len": 54}}}, "tugging": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 57, "context": "It was pretty to see her as she stood by the colonel , looking up at him with her eyes so full of love and pleasure , and a bright color in her cheeks ; while Maggie , almost as much delighted , ran to the heavy arm-chair in which Grandpapa Duncan usually sat , and began tugging and shoving at it with all her might", "context_len": 66}}}, "dazed": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "But fortunately they went far better than even the doctor dared to hope , and with the morning came consciousness , and all the dazed bewilderment as well , of one who finds himself in wholly new surroundings , with no idea whatever of how he came there", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 71, "context": "When she raised the veil from her face and Alaeddin saw her , he said , `` Verily , her fashion glorifieth the Great Creator and extolled be the perfection of Him who made her and graced her with this beauty and goodliness ! '' And his back was cloven in sunder , [ 321 ] when he saw her ; his thought was confounded and his understanding [ 322 ] dazed and the love of her gat hold upon his whole heart ; so he turned back and returning home , went in to his mother , like one distraught", "context_len": 101}}}, "sled": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "I , said John , mean to paint my steers \u2019 yoke , my gunning float , sled , and the boat father made me , if we can get enough ; and I \u2019 ll paint my bedroom , then put some into whitewash and paint the walls", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The old man , with the help of the dogs , put him on the big sled and dragged him back to their camp", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "A little later the train pulled in , and , getting aboard , the four chums waved a farewell to Jed , who could be observed standing up in his sled , making some queer signs , evidently with the idea of bringing more good luck", "context_len": 46}}}, "blizzard": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Where 's your camp ? '' asked William , who , as my readers will remember , was the son of the widow in whose house the chums found shelter during a blizzard that overtook them when they were on a hunting trip , as told in the second volume of this series entitled `` The Darewell Chums in the City", "context_len": 62}}}, "frozen": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 60, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 23, "context": "For a long distance Fenn tramped on , plowing through the woods , making turns now and then to avoid streams , partly frozen over , leaping them when he could , fording them at other times , for he had on high , water-proof hunting boots , but keeping as nearly as he could in the proper course", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "I shall never forget about Sebastopol now and the trenches since you told me that wonderful story about your father and Sir John Wallis , and the night they were both nearly frozen , '' said Dolly Fairfax", "context_len": 38}}}, "blinded": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 60, "context": "The painful freeing of his right hand , the fear that in the fall he might have sustained an injury that would cripple him and leave him a prey to the first beast of the jungle that might roam that way , the dread that he might never be able to free himself from those ruthless , imprisoning branches had blinded him to another and more imminent peril that threatened", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "That death seems but a covered way , Which opens into light , Wherein no blinded child can stray Beyond the Father 's sight ;", "context_len": 25}}}, "rescuer": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "The plebe was about a hundred yards from the shore , and swimming weakly ; the girl , still unconscious , was floating upon her back -- and her rescuer , holding her by the arms -- was slowly towing her toward the shore", "context_len": 44}}}, "birthdays": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "On previous birthdays they had always been satisfied with lemonade as a drink , but Drusie , feeling that this was a special occasion , had considered that lemonade was , perhaps , hardly a suitable form of refreshment ; and so , from a recipe which she was proud to think was entirely out of her own head , she had concocted a bottle of red wine", "context_len": 68}}}, "thimble": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "At these words , uttered so innocently by the little child , down would go Hannah 's work , needle , and thimble , and he would find himself clasped tightly to her bosom ; while down the red cheeks , which he had praised , would flow large salt tears which had lain locked up and frozen since Davie died", "context_len": 61}}}, "tomorrow": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 84, "context": "In the first place , I am well treated here , and my position is not an unpleasant one ; secondly , the sum required for ransom is altogether preposterous ; thirdly , I am not without hopes that I may some day find other means of freeing myself without so great a sacrifice ; and lastly , that I have a reason which I can not mention , why , at present , I would not quit Tripoli , even were I free tomorrow", "context_len": 85}}}, "coax": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 61, "context": "Then he fell sick of the cholera himself ; and , though it was a mild attack , he lay in the Valley of the Shadow of Death while it lasted , and waited the worst with such terror that when he kept asking her if he should get well , his mother tried to reason with him , and to coax him out of his fear", "context_len": 67}}}, "whispering": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` Oh no , let her stay ; I do love so much to see her , '' said my mother , so I could not have the heart to say any more , and I went away to a distant part of the room , and they began whispering again just like the dearest friends which they really were , and at last the Duchess came up to me and said --", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "Such a flutter and flurry and whispering and giggling there was on that afternoon when everything was in readiness for the little flower play", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "WHISPERING PINE or the Graduates of Radcliffe By Rev", "context_len": 9}}}, "pretended": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 95, "context": "peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to laugh at the supernatural , she did not feel quite so brave as she pretended , and found little cold shivers stealing down her back when the wind rose suddenly , and began to wail round the cottage like a hungry creature waiting to be let in", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 63, "context": "Their great trial was having to take a younger brother with them whenever they wanted to go off with other boys ; and it had been their habit to get away from him by many little deceits which they could not practise now : to tell him that their mother wanted him ; or to send him home upon some errand to his pretended advantage that had really no object but his absence", "context_len": 73}}}, "straightest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The straightest and very best-looking one he named General Strong , and another he named Captain Brave", "context_len": 17}}}, "admired": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 83, "context": "Aunt Helen fondly imagined herself to be a great disciplinarian , but her own lively youth was still such a recent remembrance that her eyes were wont to twinkle and the corners of her mouth to twitch in the middle of her severest scoldings , and the children always had a feeling that so long as they did not do anything rude or wrong , or run into any very imminent danger , their escapades were secretly condoned by their aunt , who admired pluck and spirit , however much she might feel it incumbent upon her to lecture them", "context_len": 100}}}, "exceptionally": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "They were certainly an exceptionally fine race of people , the men being lithe , clean-limbed , muscular fellows , every one of them apparently in the pink of condition , while the faces and figures of the women , especially the younger ones , would have excited the envy of many an English belle", "context_len": 55}}}, "governess": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 125, "context": "she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little creature whom every servant and every governess alike dreaded", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Then the young girl rushed upstairs to her dormitory , unlocked her little private drawer , took out her sealskin purse , extracted one of the new half-crowns , and was down again by the little governess cart , whispering eagerly to Mademoiselle Le Brun , within the prescribed time", "context_len": 50}}}, "slaves": {"freq": 240, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 236, "word_index": 64, "context": "and saying , `` Whence shall my poor son get these dishes of jewels ? Supposing , for the jewels and the dishes , that he return to the treasure and gather the whole from the trees , -- and withal methinketh not it is possible to him ; but say that he fetch them , -- whence [ shall he get ] the slaves and slave-girls ? '' And she gave not over talking to herself till she reached the house , where Alaeddin awaited her , and when she came in to him , she said to him , `` O my son , said I not to thee , 'Think not to attain to the Lady Bedrulbudour ' ? Indeed , this is a thing", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "In the third French war against the Naktche a large body of Cha'hta warriors served as allies under the French commander , and on January 27 , 1730 , before daylight , made a furious onslaught on their principal village , killing sixty enemies and rescuing fifty-nine French women and children and one hundred and fifty negro slaves previously captured by the tribe ( Claiborne , Mississippi , I , 45", "context_len": 71}}}, "tragedy": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 85, "context": "I am an Historian , for I have been discussing old dates ; a Merchant , for I add plum to plum ; a Lawyer , for I have opened many a case ; a Lord Mayor , for the mace is before me ; and a Navigator , for I am led to seize and gulf ! What if I were to stay here altogether , or set up a new company with my fair hostesses ? Miss Ratty is cut out for a tragedy Queen", "context_len": 87}}}, "wives": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 57, "context": "I wager my head that they \u2019 ll both of them come over here and make it : what a good time they will have puttering over it , and passing their compliments upon each other ! It \u2019 s my opinion , that when them old men die they won \u2019 t be buried with their wives , but alongside of each other", "context_len": 64}}}, "assistance": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 56, "context": "I do not , of course , wish you to interfere in any way in the conduct of the defence , in which you will take such share as you can ; but you are specially to observe how matters go , and if you see that the knights are pressed and in sore need of assistance to enable them to hold the post , you will at once bring the news to me , and I will hurry there with reinforcements", "context_len": 82}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "Worn by their previous exertions , the men were unable to give any permanent help to Bangura , and were in their turn dragged under several times in their efforts to afford him assistance", "context_len": 34}}}, "magnificent": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 123, "context": "his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like , and be it complete with all its requisites of kingly and magnificent furniture and so forth", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "When with Chris it was forward clear to the bow to lean over the rail and see the magnificent prow cut the water ; or way to the stern , to watch the far-shining train , the screws churned into white foam behind them ; or an hour 'midships , where the ever-varying amusements with which the steerage passengers beguile the weary hours can be looked down upon from the saloon deck of either first or second cabin", "context_len": 78}}}, "eve": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "Donald and Harold were on the eve of laughing outright , but Marie-Celeste , detecting a suspicious blinking in the long curling lashes of the eyelids , kept them still by an imperative gesture", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "My birthday eve they kept in high style , the men smoking long clay pipes , till our dining-room was enveloped in a cloud of tobacco smoke , so that my aunt declared that her best hangings would reek for days like a London coffee tavern , whereat Sir George pacified her by saying that the weed of Virginia is ever a sovereign safeguard against the plague !", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "See , all ragged and forlorn , He is resting by a tree ; And to him the light of morn And shades of eve alike must be", "context_len": 28}}}, "valentine": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Valentine", "context_len": 1}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "\u2019 It is a valentine written to her sister ; and it shows that at the early age of eleven she had at least begun a little versifying ; usually the line first adopted by incipient authors", "context_len": 37}}}, "ward": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 41, "context": "Oh , daddy ! \u2019 said Isa , instead of giving just me a valentine party couldn \u2019 t we have one for all the children in the hospital ? I \u2019 m well enough to be moved into the big ward , and all the children who are able to be there can have a party with me , and we can have a huge valentine party", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 15, "context": "He was always warding off death , or doing all that man could do to ward it off , and in many many cases death was afraid of him , and retired from his prey , vanquished by that knowledge , that genius , that sympathy , that love for humanity , which overflowed the little doctor 's personality", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Richards thought his little ward compared very favorably with the others", "context_len": 11}}}, "strung": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 79, "context": "or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length , as well because it is an important one as to show the almost insuperable difficulties that beset the", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 46, "context": "Well , he said , after a long pause , I can only hope that it will turn out all right , and promise that if you are strangled in prison , I will see that every slave who had a hand in it shall be strung up", "context_len": 48}}}, "shades": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 51, "context": "There \u2019 s rest beneath the yew ; I know There \u2019 s deeper Rest in realms above ; The Saviour \u2019 s Arm the valley through Will me uphold with strengthening love ; My hope His Righteousness ; my buckler , faith ; Why should I fear to tread the shades of death ?", "context_len": 55}}}, "attached": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 41, "context": "She did not talk nearly so much as in former times of her son ; perhaps he had told her that I was not to be worried , but she was very good and very nice , and I got sincerely attached to her : and I never saw the Duchess nor Lady Thesiger nor my old friends , although I heard that the Duchess was fairly well again , and was out and going into society ; and every one now seemed certain that Jim Randolph had gone to the bottom in the Star of Hope , but by universal consent the boarders decided that the news should be kept from mother , and mother grew much better", "context_len": 119}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "The day passes so fast ; and perhaps at the end one feels -- What has been done ? -- how little ! But as for sitting down to amuse oneself with an English story-book , -- how can that be done by your attached old Missionary Auntie ? \u2019", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Hawkins eulogizes the people by stating that they are more civil , orderly and industrious than their neighbors ( the Lower Creeks ) , the men more attached to their wives , and these more chaste", "context_len": 36}}}, "bishop": {"freq": 75, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 75, "word_index": 48, "context": "The door opened , and Edward left the room after a conference with the physician , who sat in a corner of the room compounding drugs at a small table ; a few minutes passed in silence , when he returned and held the door open for the bishop of London , who entered , bearing the viaticum , as the last communion of the sick was then called , and attended by an acolyte , who bore a lighted taper before him and carried a bell", "context_len": 87}}}, "beheaded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Valentine , and although he was so cruelly beheaded , still his deeds of charity and kindness will always be known", "context_len": 21}}}, "deeds": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 38, "context": "It is well said that no man is utterly bad , and perhaps he possessed domestic virtues which were thought unworthy of the attention of the chroniclers ; but as they picture him -- now prompting Ethelred to deeds of treachery against the Danes , now joining those Danes themselves , and surpassing them in cruelty -- now seeking pretended reconciliation , only to betray his foe more surely , and in all this aided and supported by the weak , unprincipled king -- as thus pictured there is scarcely a blacker character in history", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "First , in the name of the Order , I have to thank you all most heartily for the brave deeds that you have performed , and for the fresh honour you have won for it", "context_len": 36}}}, "kindnesses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "His friends felt dreadfully that such a good man should be so cruelly murdered , and for days they could talk of nothing else but of the good Bishop Valentine , and they \u2019 d tell one another of all his good deeds and of his love for all people and of his kindnesses", "context_len": 54}}}, "charity": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 42, "context": "The roof was semicircular , and composed of minute bricks , seeming to form one solid mass ; the floor of tiling , arranged in patterns , which could still be obscurely traced by the light of the lamp left by the charity of Sidroc to the prisoner ; for the dungeon was of bad reputation ; lights had been seen there at unearthly hours , when the outer door was fast and no inmate existed", "context_len": 76}}}, "valentines": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "misbehave": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "They had their preferences , of course , as I have hinted ; and one of the most popular acts was that where a horse has been trained to misbehave , so that nobody can mount him ; and after the actors have tried him , the ring-master turns to the audience , and asks if some gentleman among them wants to try it", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "He had noticed the quiet behavior and regular pace of the two mustangs , and concluded that they were of a different kind from those he had seen misbehave on former occasions", "context_len": 32}}}, "frightfully": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 28, "context": "At that epoch the Moskoquis formed a powerful separate republic in the northwest of Mexico ; they succored him with a numerous body of warriors , but were frightfully decimated by the Spaniards , who dismembered Montezuma 's domain , and almost completely depopulated it", "context_len": 45}}}, "fluid": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "If , for instance , a little girl or a boy is pouring from the great big grandfather Ink-Bottle and is giving you something more in the way of a nice Inky fluid or drink , and if the little girl looks the other way , you spill", "context_len": 48}}}, "spill": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "The audience 'hoorayed ' and thumped with their thick boots on the floor , and shouted 'encore ' ; but as it was not possible to break the china , spill the medicine , and upset the kettle twice over without a considerable amount of preparation , they had to be content with the graceful bows which the artistes bestowed upon them", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Besides it would be quite utterly useless any oftener for you \u2019 d only spill over me and I \u2019 d get quite horrid looking", "context_len": 25}}}, "trickle": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "Bessie had been taken from the wagon that she might rest herself by running about a little after her long ride , and now she and Maggie , as well as Hafed , forgot pricks and scratches in the pleasure of watching the brook , and feeling its cool , clear waters trickle through their fingers", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "The Roanoke heaved up her black side until he saw smears of a yellow liquid trickle from several pipes , and spread out over the frothing billows in shimmering sheets", "context_len": 30}}}, "ha": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 83, "context": "`` The Sultan `` goes round in the kiosk '' and seeing `` the casement ( shubbak ) , which Alaeddin had purposely left defective , without completion , '' said to the Vizier , `` Knowest thou the reason ( or cause ) of the lack of completion of this casement and its lattices ? '' ( shearihi , or quaere , `` [ this ] lattice , '' the copyist having probably omitted by mistake the diacritical points over the final ha )", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 2, "context": "You should ha ' said what kind o ' looking child he wor -- what wor the colour of his eyes , and his hair , and how big he wor , and what clothes ' e 'ad h'on -- all that 'ud be printed and pasted up for folks to read ; not that the talk about the clothes 'ud do much good , fur in course they 'd be made away wid first thing", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He ha ' most wonderful powers of hearing tho ' , and ef we speak in a whisper a'most down on earth He can tell wot we are a saying", "context_len": 30}}}, "varnished": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "That \u2019 s so , agreed the Desk , but even if I am made of wood I like to be varnished and made over nice and fresh every little while", "context_len": 31}}}, "oftener": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Besides it would be quite utterly useless any oftener for you \u2019 d only spill over me and I \u2019 d get quite horrid looking", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "So he told them how , when he sat alone , ill and dull and irritable , Ram Dass had tried to distract him by describing the passers by , and there was one child who passed oftener than any one else ; he had begun to be interested in her -- partly perhaps because he was thinking a great deal of a little girl , and partly because Ram Dass had been able to relate the incident of his visit to the attic in chase of the monkey", "context_len": 89}}}, "adds": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 26, "context": "As a sequel to his wonderful story of the pursuit of the Alibamu by the Creeks and the final peaceable settling down of both , Milfort adds some points on the early doings and warrings of the Creeks , which had occurred but a limited number of years before his stay in the tribe , and were recounted to him by one of the m\u00edkos from their memorial beads , like the legendary migration :", "context_len": 75}}}, "scampering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}}}, "choose": {"freq": 125, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 122, "word_index": 85, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "I should ask twice as much , the captain replied , if it were not for the regulation that one slave from each cargo brought in belongs to the sultan , and his officers would as a matter of course choose this fellow , for the others are merely such as are sold in the market every day", "context_len": 58}}}, "satchel": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "This story does not mention any crossing by the Cha'hta of the turbid waters of the mighty Mississippi , but accounts quite satisfactorily for the mysterious inclination of the pole , for the prophet must have been careful to suspend the satchel with the war-physic always on the eastern side , so as to have the pole brought down in that direction by the weight of the pouch", "context_len": 68}}}, "fights": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Why , you little freshie , '' exclaimed Phil , `` fair fights are the only kind we have at Randall ! We do n't have 'em very often , but every time we do Tom puts the kibosh all over your friend Langridge", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "In Portsmouth Miss Weston 's Sailors ' Rest is one of the most noted buildings in the town ; whilst the principle that Jack , who fights our battles at sea , and keeps our country prosperous by his labours aboard ship , needs to be made happy when he is ashore is far more fully acknowledged than it used to be", "context_len": 62}}}, "germs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` What ! Do you mean to say that you fellows are moving that fuzzy-wuzzy piece of architecture into this dormitory ? I 'll not stand for it ! I 'll complain to the proctor ! Why , it 's full of disease germs ! ''", "context_len": 46}}}, "medicines": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "The herb medicines are administered to him by the \u00edsti pak\u0101 dsha ` l\u00e1ko or `` great leader , '' who , when speaking of him , says : pusidshedsh\u0113 yi sanatchumitch\u00e4 tch\u00e4-is , [ 123 ] `` I am passing him through the physicking process repeatedly , '' or : n\u00e1ki om\u00e1lga imaki ` l\u00e4 dsh\u00e4yi s\u00e1 ` lit \u00f3m\u00e4s , tch\u00ed , `` I am teaching him all the matters proper for him to think of", "context_len": 78}}}, "cure": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 28, "context": "When he asked if he might take the liberty of inquiring what interest she had in my family , she asked if it was necessary to Willie 's cure that he should know that ; and when he said , 'No , of course not , ' she said it was a great liberty , and as good as told him to mind his own affairs", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was nearly noon when I was conducted back to my hut after my futile attempt to cure the king ; and it was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "helps": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 70, "context": "`` You see , '' said the woman , `` I told her to come when she was hungry , and when she 'd come I 'd give her odd jobs to do ; an ' I found she was willing , and somehow I got to like her ; and the end of it was , I 've given her a place an ' a home , and she helps me , an ' behaves well , an ' is as thankful as a girl can be", "context_len": 88}}}, "colds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Ah , now he helps me drive away the colds , the many horrid sicknesses that come when little boys have to stay in the house", "context_len": 26}}}, "sicknesses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Ah , now he helps me drive away the colds , the many horrid sicknesses that come when little boys have to stay in the house", "context_len": 26}}}, "nurses": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 31, "context": "Why should not other middle-aged ladies go out , as she went out ? -- not necessarily always to attempt full Zenana work ; but to be protectors , housekeepers , nurses , to younger and more active ladies ? Whether it would be right to use any portion of Mission-funds for such a purpose may be doubted ; and in many a case Mission rooms could not be spared ; but there are exceptions as to the latter", "context_len": 79}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Prolonged sounds of cheering greeted the two Red Cross nurses as they stepped upon the platform with marked timidity", "context_len": 19}}}, "total": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 37, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "It is remarkable how closely nature follows a rule in her most perfect work ; here were two figures , built by her a thousand miles apart , racing there , and each striving with might and main , yet the sum total of the energy that each was able to expend so nearly alike that yard by yard they struggled on , without an inch of difference between them", "context_len": 70}}}, "eclipse": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 8, "context": "When his florid countenance emerged from its total eclipse he groaned twice , heaved a sigh that fairly shook him , and glared at David with speechless reproach", "context_len": 28}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "As to their knowledge of the English language , it bade fair to eclipse many of the High School girls of the present day , for they did understand in the first place its literature , and in the next its grammar , and were well acquainted with the works of Shakespeare and those other lions of literature whose names we are so proud of and whose works we love", "context_len": 70}}}, "thirsty": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 26, "context": "There was a town pump there for you , so that you would not have to go into the house for a drink when you got thirsty , and perhaps be set to doing something ; and there were plenty of boards for teeter and see-saw ; and somehow that neighborhood seemed to understand boys , and did not molest them in any way", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` the thirsty one ( es szadi ) and the goer-forth by day or in the morning '' ( el ghadi ) ; but this is most probably a mistranscription for the common phrase es sari ( the goer by night ) wa ' l ghadi , often used in the sense of `` comers and goers '' simply", "context_len": 59}}}, "pat": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 15, "context": "Without this bow , murmured Bomba to himself , as he gave it an affectionate pat and slung it over his shoulder , what chance would Bomba have to find meat to eat ? How could he defend himself against the wild beasts that seek his life ? He would be helpless and could never hope to reach the island of the big cats", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Indeed , your visit came pat to the minute , for I was just shutting up for the night when you must needs get in the way of the gate , '' and she laughed saucily", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "He would never allow any grown person but his master to pet him ; and if any one tried to pat him or make him play , he would walk away and seat himself at a distance , with an offended air which seemed to say , `` What a very silly person you are ; do you not know that I am too grave and wise a dog to be pleased with such nonsense ! ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "misty": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "A cold , wet , and misty evening , and above all to one whose pockets are not lined ! My foolish fancy for the Stage has brought me to a declining stage , if not a stage of decline", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 39, "context": "It was an awful place in a storm , where the spray , broken up into feathery froth by the battle on the rocks , came flying over the town , and then away landward , like a fine misty rain ; but it was a grand place in a calm", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "Below the house the meadow sloped down to a river , where a stone bridge led to the village , with its pretty thatched cottages and Norman church , whose square tower stood up like a beacon for the surrounding country ; and away in the distance , tier upon tier , rose the Welsh mountains , fading from green to purple or from purple to misty mauve , till the last were lost in the hazy blue of the sky", "context_len": 81}}}, "fog": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 44, "context": "I was inside the cave , you know , and I was pretending to be asleep , and when I knew it was too much of a long time I thought I would go out and find Louis , and then I saw the fog and the tide was coming in just as fast", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "Do you see that fog layer hanging close to the water ? It reaches almost to the tip of Nusanive Point", "context_len": 21}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "She looked up at the sky and behind all its London gloom and fog she seemed to see the smile of Jesus shining through directly upon her", "context_len": 27}}}, "willingly": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 81, "context": "The enormous crowd who had followed us up from the beach were not permitted to enter the palisaded enclosure , which was strictly taboo to the common herd ; our party therefore now consisted solely of those who had brought me up the river , four individuals who had joined us outside the gate -- and whom I took to be officials of some sort -- and my unworthy self ; and , for my own part , I would very willingly have waived the distinction of forming one of the party", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Ay , madam , 't is easier to keep silence than to testify ; and I would not willingly vex you , but I desire that you should know me in my true colours", "context_len": 35}}}, "loyal": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 54, "context": "Indeed , the little queen , whose realm was the hearts of her nearest and dearest , would have been obliged to abdicate , for a while at least , I fancy , had she not chosen before nightfall of that same day to bury her head in the lap of her very most loyal subject , and with tears and sohs confess to her extreme unreasonableness and avow her determination not soon again to be overtaken by such a sorry state of mind and temper", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "the wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he could reach , in most uncourtly", "context_len": 128}}}, "delivering": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 27, "context": "But Cochrane , instead of waiting to be attacked , made for the Spaniard , and , after receiving the fire of all her guns , without delivering a shot , got right under the side of the Gamo ( so the vessel was called ) , and battered into her with might and main", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "A little handcart was among the office equipment , and very often Bart did light delivering", "context_len": 16}}}, "wagon": {"freq": 230, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 230, "word_index": 33, "context": "He had a notion that if he could get up a chariot by sawing down the sides of a store-box for the body , and borrowing the hind-wheels of the baby 's willow wagon , and then , drawn by the family dog Tip at a mad gallop , come suddenly whirling round the corner of the school-house , wearing spangled circus-tights and bearing Apollo 's bow and shaft , while a silken scarf which he had seen in a bureau-drawer at home blew gallantly out behind him , it would have a fine effect with the boys", "context_len": 98}}}, "owe": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 17, "context": "No , father , no ! And now that you have let me find out what I owe her , do you think I sha'n't remember it always with every beat of my heart ? Oh , yes -- although I can never repay her for all she has suffered in keeping me from knowing things which would have hurt me too much when I was little and -- and could not make allowances -- as I can now", "context_len": 79}}}, "thoughtless": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 50, "context": "Harris had said to her more than once , that there 's nothing but sin itself has so many heavy hearts to answer for as thoughtlessness ; and thoughtfulness , next to love , has lightened and brightened more hearts than anything else in the world and Marie-Celeste knew how thoughtless she had been to press home upon Harold in any way a keener sense of his own great loss", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 77, "context": "She did not speak about it , or show it to anyone , but after that not a further grumble escaped her , and she managed to find such a bright side to the question , and talked so often and so hopefully of the future , that Father said she was as good as a tonic , and began to find his little daughter such a comfort to him , and so different to the old thoughtless Peggy of former days , that I scarcely know how he would have got through that trying time without her", "context_len": 98}}}, "lavender": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "And cats of all colors ! They were even lavender and so many other queer colors for cats to be !", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "She became better skilled in the making of pickles and preserves than ever she could have been in the masculine household at Hunstanton , where the old servants would have broken their hearts if their little mistress had ever set her dainty finger to anything rougher than gathering rose-leaves and lavender to scent the best parlour", "context_len": 56}}}, "silky": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "They did not do very much except nibble at bran and lettuce-leaves , it is true , but they were pretty , soft creatures , with long , silky ears , and it was fun sometimes to let them out for a scamper on the granary floor", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to find the poor creature ; so that , his hair being wet , he was a sight to behold when", "context_len": 128}}}, "combed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 56, "context": "Peggy and Bobby as 'the children , ' with crumpled pinafores and smudged faces , were an equal success , for in watching Archie Peggy had forgotten her sudden shyness , and she now threw herself thoroughly into her part , howling most realistically when her nose was scrubbed in the wash-tub , or her hair combed with a fork ; while Bobby stole jam and resisted medicine with lifelike zeal", "context_len": 71}}}, "matched": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 32, "context": "The corsairs are bound to be very strongly manned , and ten knights , even aided by such assistance as they may get from the Christians , might find themselves altogether over matched against a crowd of desperate men", "context_len": 39}}}, "bred": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 28, "context": "The Sultan proceeded to make familiar with Alaeddin and to talk with him , and Alaeddin answered him with all courtliness and fluency , as he had been bred in kings ' palaces or as he were their constant associate ; [ 471 ] and the more the talk was prolonged between them , the more gladness and joy redoubled on the Sultan for that which he heard of the goodliness of Alaeddin 's answers and the sweetness of his speech", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "These sheep running away from us were of standard stock , and the evening before I had seen a fine herd of Holstein cows and some of the best bred pigs I had ever come across", "context_len": 36}}}, "kitchens": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 70, "context": "Osborne arrived o ' Monday , with me in the midst of my washing , and never a bit of cake in the house ; and there was Squire Henley and his lady o ' churning day , and I had to leave the butter half made to bring in tea ; and ten to one there 'll be someone more o ' Friday , when I 'm cleaning my kitchens", "context_len": 71}}}, "alleys": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 81, "context": "Peggy 's onslaught really seemed to have done some good in the school , for the attention of the older and better boys being called to the subject of bullying , a stand was made , and public opinion ran high against it , so that for a time , at any rate , the little boys had peace , and Bobby was able to return daily by the ordinary high-road , instead of seeking the shelter of side-paths and back alleys", "context_len": 82}}}, "ambitious": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 48, "context": "As Philip had been made commander-in-chief of the Grecian armies which were about to undertake the conquest of Asia , and as , of course , his influence and power in all that related to that vast enterprise would be paramount and supreme ; and as all were ambitious to have a large share in the glory of that expedition , and to participate , as much as possible , in the power and in the renown which seemed to be at Philip 's disposal , all were , of course , very anxious to secure his favor", "context_len": 98}}}, "bicycle": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "The crowd was dispersing , Darry was counting up the heap of bank notes and coin in the cash box , Bob was gloating and wild with delight as uncovering his purchase he brought to light a new bicycle", "context_len": 39}}}, "prize": {"freq": 166, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 150, "word_index": 127, "context": "was being taken for granted , will you please tell me what is a tilt-yard ? and what it was dat de knignts would not tome to ? and what was dat little white ting of the Queen 's dat de King carried ? and impatiently as Marie-Celeste brooked the interruption , there was nothing for it but to wait while Her Majesty explained that the tilt-yard was a sort of riding-school for the knights , where they practised for the tournaments , and that the tournaments were occasions when the knights , spear in hand , came together to ride against each other , with a great many people looking on , and when the one who unseated all those who rode against him won the prize", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 8, "context": "The walls were adorned with school drawings and prize maps , fastened up with tacks ; a bookcase of much-read volumes filled the space between the fireplace and the window ; and in the corner might be found a miscellaneous collection of cricket-bats , fishing-rods , tennis-racquets , bows and arrows , croquet-mallets , sticks , balls and ninepins , and other articles very dear to the children 's hearts , but which Nancy generally classed impartially under the head of 'lumber", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "It may well be that one man brought out of the darkness of heathendom is a prize worth fifty times -- or five thousand times -- the money expended in bringing him", "context_len": 32}}}, "chief": {"freq": 294, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 200, "word_index": 41, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 94, "word_index": 83, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}}}, "geese": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 105, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 54, "context": "Conway started them by the way she talked to Agnes , and I have a modest claim to some brains of my own , so I thought out the rest and talked it over with father who put things very clearly before me , and showed me that school-girls are half the time silly geese who seem to think their teachers are created for the mere purpose of making their lives miserable", "context_len": 72}}}, "parlor": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 24, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}}}, "whichever": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 13, "context": "I had resolved to make myself either a flute or a flageolet , whichever might prove easiest , and I accordingly selected with great care half-a-dozen of the most suitable reeds that I could find , and , borrowing his spear from one of my guards , cut them , taking care that they should be of ample length for my purpose", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I will give orders when I go down that he shall be always sent up with whichever gang comes here", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 77, "context": "`` You forget that when the last invasion of our pagan foes was over , it was agreed in the Witan that a set of beacons should be prepared , in readiness to fire , on the tops of the hills , and that if the Danes appeared again , they should be fired everywhere , in which case Mercia was to hold herself in readiness to come to the aid of Wessex or East Anglia , whichever the foe might be harrying", "context_len": 83}}}, "funniest": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was a merry , not to say boisterous , party , for Archie was in one of his funniest moods , and told 'tall ' Yankee stories till the children nearly rolled off their seats with laughter , and Lilian went on pouring into her overflowing cup till the tray was swimming with tea", "context_len": 55}}}, "neighborhood": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 77, "context": "It stretched eastward from the river to the Canal-Basin , with the market-house , the county buildings , and the stores and hotels on one street , and a few other stores and taverns scattering off on streets that branched from it to the southward ; but all this was a vast metropolis to my boy 's fancy , where he might get lost -- the sum of all disaster -- if he ventured away from the neighborhood of the house where he first lived , on its southwestern border", "context_len": 90}}}, "compositions": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 56, "context": "With the fact of this garden is mixed a sense of drowsy heat and summer light , and that is all , except the blackboard at the end of the room and a big girl doing sums at it ; and the wonder why the teacher smiled when he read in one of the girls ' compositions a phrase about forging puddings and pies ; my boy did not know what forging meant , so he must have been very young", "context_len": 81}}}, "recite": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 56, "context": "There 'll be no time for whims and fancies , or even for lessons ; for there is to be an enormous tea-fight , as I call it , for the young folk of the parish in the schoolhouse this afternoon , and games afterwards , and recitations ; and if you , Rosamund , can recite as well as Lucy has described , why , you will be invaluable", "context_len": 70}}}, "rehearsal": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "The day before the entertainment ( at which , of course , all the mothers and daddies of the children were to be present ) a rehearsal of everything was to take place", "context_len": 33}}}, "composition": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 46, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "faltered": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 67, "context": "Stanton , who , lying over the edge , watched him , God only knows , with what sickening hope ; the loose soil crumbled and slid beneath him ; but , light and sinewy as he was , his bare feet , trained to all kinds of mountain climbing , took hold where those of a heavier person , with shoes upon them , must have faltered and slipped past all recovery", "context_len": 73}}}, "sobbed": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 97, "context": "Fortunately , the sun 's rays came down hot and bright , and the rock grew warmer , so that by degrees the terribly void look began to leave Mark Penelly 's face , and at last , when Harry held out his hand , saying , `` Do you feel better ? '' Mark Penelly caught it in both of his , clung to it , and , turning half over on his face , laid his forehead against it , and , forgetting his years of manhood , lay there in his weakness , and sobbed and cried like a child", "context_len": 103}}}, "sobs": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 69, "context": "and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say afterwards upon the danger of such heedless practical jokes", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "As Claire \u2019 s sobs quieted , Mickey \u2019 s voice reached the others from outside the shelter of mats", "context_len": 20}}}, "cheated": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 55, "context": "When [ 312 ] the goldsmith heard this , `` Out on this accursed one , '' cried he , `` who fleeceth the servants of God the Most High ! '' Then he looked at Alaeddin and said to him , `` O my son , this Jew is a cheat , who hath cheated thee and laughed at thee , for that the silver of this thy platter is pure and fine ; and I have weighed it and find its worth threescore diners and ten ; so , an it please thee take its price , take [ it ]", "context_len": 103}}}, "copied": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 34, "context": "Silence fell upon the little group for a moment till Aunt Emmeline herself broke it by saying , `` Do you remember , Cecelia , how angry you were with Polly Parker because she copied your dress , and how you were going to have yours trimmed with daisies , and changed all that at the last moment ? I can see you now , ripping off those inoffensive daisies and flinging them on the floor", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Another paper of copied extracts has a particular interest , because it seems to show , even then , a dawning sense in the mind of Charlotte Tucker of the needs of heathen and semi-heathen lands", "context_len": 36}}}, "fists": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 31, "context": "Wentworth 's upraised hands ; she set the boys to swing honey-pots , and the girls to play 'Drop the Handkerchief ' ; she boldly interposed her small person between the fists of two fighting hobbledehoys , and seized a little boy by the boot who had ventured to climb up the stove-pipe ; she welcomed some of the parents who had begun to arrive for the entertainment , and found them comfortable places on the benches , even nursing one of the babies while its mother went to the rescue of an older child , who was being forcibly held down and sat upon by several of its companions ; and by the time the bell was rung , and the audience requested to take their places", "context_len": 128}}}, "countrymen": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 23, "context": "Then Alfgar saw what was the marvellous power of Christianity , and beheld a heroism utterly beyond the fierce excitement which nerved his countrymen for their scenes of carnage and blood ; not one of his pagan friends could have suffered as calmly , as patiently -- it seemed easier for the sufferer to bear than for Alfgar to look on ; once or twice the latter gave audible vent to his emotions , but the look which Bertric turned upon him spoke volumes , and he restrained himself lest he should add to the pain of the victim", "context_len": 99}}}, "connected": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Every expense connected with the school was to be met by this wonderful purse of money ; she was to be educated and called the Cherry Court Scholarship girl , and it was to be a wonderfully proud distinction , I can tell you , and at the end of the year Sir John Wallis was to give another purse of money , and at the end of that year another purse of money , so that the lucky girl who won the Scholarship was to be educated free of expense for three whole years", "context_len": 96}}}, "headquarters": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 8, "context": "As the heaviest part of his work at headquarters was over , and as pretty much everything had depended on the reply to General Forrest 's requisition on his superior officer -- who , unfortunately , chanced to be General Bragg -- for arms and ammunition , Harry had no difficulty in securing leave of absence for the day ; and so , when all the arrangements had been made , we set out the next evening for the house where Jack Bledsoe lay", "context_len": 84}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Ah , '' he said , `` I thought as much ; I thought I 'd get to headquarters if I came here", "context_len": 24}}}, "faced": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 7, "context": "On came the attacking party ; he faced them , and with a coolness that did him credit at such a critical moment he picked out the one that he could most easily capture , and was in the act of hurling the lasso , when , up from the very midst of the hawthorn bushes at the back of the fort , Hal 's voice was heard again", "context_len": 69}}}, "flinched": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "Stupendous though the task was , not a man flinched , although several groused at the lack of consideration on the part of the Staff to send them against a prepared position in a practically-unbroken state ; which showed that the troops were generally ignorant of the measures taken to safeguard them", "context_len": 52}}}, "deserting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "'Well , I like that , when it was your idea altogether ! No , thank you , I do n't care to be in it ! ' -- and meanly deserting at the last moment , Bobby fled into the sanctuary of the garden , leaving Peggy to carry her refreshments to the ruins alone", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He would not betray and desert his friends in anticipation of their deserting and betraying him", "context_len": 16}}}, "groups": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 84, "context": "All the beasts of the prairie had he slain in his terrible anger , and all the people had fled to the mountains for food , thither he thought he would follow them , and he sat down upon a ridge of sand , to strengthen his bow , and sharpen his arrows , when , lo ! quite unmindful of him , a thousand little creatures he had fancied too insignificant to notice , sprang forth from their holes , and gathered in groups for their daily gossip", "context_len": 89}}}, "favorites": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "But Washington went straight on , doing as he should and not stooping to play favorites or to be a favorite", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 49, "context": "How sagacious a line of conduct was this ! Most ardent and enthusiastic young men , in the circumstances in which he was placed , would have been elated and vain at their elevation , and would have replaced the old and well-tried servants of the father with personal favorites of their own age , inexperienced and incompetent , and as conceited as themselves", "context_len": 64}}}, "ingratitude": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "The general feeling was strong against the prisoner , the more so because he had been loved and trusted by Edmund , so that ingratitude added to the magnitude of his crime in their eyes", "context_len": 35}}}, "praises": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` Yes , yes , '' she murmured abstractedly ; `` who could fail to love him ? so valiant and so goodly to look upon , so tender unto his friends , and to me his poor wife , and ever was the inward joy in his bosom breaking forth in praises to God -- and yet '' -- turning wildly on Dick -- '' yet you will let him die ! You are as hard as the nether millstone ! Dick , do not shake your head , you must go ! You must force Prince Rupert to hear you", "context_len": 102}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Yet not one to be encouraged , save when it enables one to perform necessary deeds of daring for some worthy object , such as holy Scripture praises in the heroes of old", "context_len": 34}}}, "exclaiming": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 8, "context": "One day I came down to breakfast , exclaiming , How beautiful the snow is ! -- when she told me how pleased she was that I could say so , instead of saying , How cold it is ! When I was ill in the year 1872 , she often came to see me , quite disregarding the infection of my throat ; she would play her guitar to me , or , as I grew better , would patiently guide my little fingers to the right places on the strings", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 80, "context": "The hostess who brought Dick 's bowl of onion broth at this moment destroyed his chance of feigning sleep , and he had to resign himself to endure his companion 's conversation which flowed on , garnished with oaths and cant phrases in three or four different languages , without any interruption , till by an unguarded movement Richard exposed his face to the light of the fire , and the stranger stared a moment , and then sprang up exclaiming , `` Body o ' me if it be not Measter Dick himself ! ''", "context_len": 96}}}, "dislikes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He never let his own little inner feelings of likes and dislikes keep him from being fair", "context_len": 17}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Eliot is mulish , and , having taken a stand , he dislikes to turn back ; but I know and others know that he would rejoice to be rid of you", "context_len": 32}}}, "wars": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 64, "context": "It was secret sorrow and sometimes open shame to my boy that his grandfather should be an Englishman , and that even his father should have been a year old when he came to this country ; but on his mother 's side he could boast a grandfather and a great-grandfather who had taken part , however briefly or obscurely , in both the wars against Great Britain", "context_len": 68}}}, "disorder": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 78, "context": "The majority of them are laid down in phonetically defective missionary alphabets , about which we are prompted to repeat what the citizens of the young colony of Mexico wrote to the government of Spain , in Cortez 's time : `` Send to us pious and Christian men , as preachers , bishops and missionaries , but do not send us scholars , who , with their pettifogging distinctions and love of contention , create nothing but disorder and strife", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "After that all was disorder amongst the English ; but they continued fighting bravely until the moon arose , and they were becoming surrounded on all sides , when , in sheer desperation , they at last gave way", "context_len": 39}}}, "traveled": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 1, "context": "Bartram traveled through the Seminole country , about 1773 , he was informed that Cuscowilla , a town on a lake of the same name and a sort of Seminole capital , had been built by Indians from Ok\u00f3ni old town , settled upon the Alachua plains : `` They abdicated the ancient Alachua town on the borders of the savanna , about fifty miles west from the river San Juan , and built here , calling the new town Cuscowilla", "context_len": 81}}}, "rumors": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "Pleasantville for a day or two had been full of rumors as to the express robbery , but Bart decided to say very little about it , and only his intimate friends knew the actual circumstances", "context_len": 36}}}, "prejudices": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` Me eatee aftelward , '' said Ki Sing when he perceived that his presence at the table was the subject of controversy , but he was overruled by Richard Dewey , who possessed a large share of independence , and would not allow himself to be controlled or influenced by the prejudices of others", "context_len": 55}}}, "tangled": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 58, "context": "Phil took Madge , and very charming she looked in a new gown of -- oh , well , what difference does it make what her dress was like , anyhow ? Besides , I do n't know whether it was bombazine or chiffon , and the more I try to describe it the worse I will get tangled , so if you 'll take my word for it , as well as Phil 's , who ought to know , she looked very pretty indeed", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 69, "context": "she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little creature whom every servant and every governess alike dreaded", "context_len": 128}}}, "threads": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "In the evenings , long after the others were in bed , she paced up and down the kitchen , spinning Deacon Croaker 's wool into smooth , even threads , but her heart ached as she prayed for her boy , and often , when in the still watches of the night Polly kept her vigils with pain , she heard her cry softly : --", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Do you suppose she threads us too ?", "context_len": 8}}}, "unraveled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "But all of these tangled threads and oh , so tangled were they , Washington unraveled !", "context_len": 17}}}, "historians": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Washington whom historians all call very great", "context_len": 7}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 80, "context": "Beyond the fact that a lady in white was supposed to haunt both the ruins and the oak-wood , weeping and wringing her hands in orthodox ghostly fashion , the children had not been able to learn much of the story , for there were so many and divergent accounts of it , all told with uncertainty as to names and dates , and in that very oracular -- not to say muddled -- style sometimes indulged in by rustic historians", "context_len": 81}}}, "stamps": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 96, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "stamp": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 75, "context": "Pigs , poultry , and young animals had to be fed , and the eggs gathered in daily ; the dairy claimed constant attention , for the pans must be scalded and the pails polished bright , and though Joe did the milking and churning , it was Lilian 's business to superintend the making up of the butter , and to weigh each pound with her own hands and print it with the Abbey stamp", "context_len": 76}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 71, "context": "In the morning they were dry , but just as hard as stone , and you had to soap the heel of your woollen sock ( which your grandmother had knitted for you , or maybe some of your aunts ) before you could get your foot in , and sometimes the ears of the boot that you pulled it on by would give way , and you would have to stamp your foot in and kick the toe against the mop-board", "context_len": 82}}}, "disagree": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` I say over and over , ' I 'll grin and bear it ; ' and when the child comes to me and asks me so pretty for the most unwholesome food -- though nothing , for that matter , seems to disagree with her -- why , I have n't the 'eart to refuse", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The terms for maize disagree but apparently , and seem to be reducible to one radix , atch or ash ; the terms for dog agree in all dialects -- hence , the Maskoki tribes planted maize and kept dogs before , probably many centuries before they separated ; and the term \u00edfa went over from them to the Timucua", "context_len": 60}}}, "envelope": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 1, "context": "This envelope , he said , contains two important documents the written confession of Bradley Wentworth , that it was he , not I , who forged the check upon his uncle , and the last letter in which he repudiates my claim upon him for the sum he agreed to pay me", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "As he spoke , he pulled a long , ugly , foolscap envelope out of his pocket , and taking a paper from it , opened it , and showed it to me", "context_len": 33}}}, "smudgy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Sometimes I make a nice smudgy spot , and then the Creature who has been writing with me does not know what to do", "context_len": 24}}}, "argue": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` But surely , '' -- I protested , and was about to argue that , she being her father 's favourite daughter , he would never be so inhuman as to sacrifice her to his anger , when a sound of distant shouting came faintly to our ears", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I can \u2019 t argue with you about it", "context_len": 9}}}, "continues": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 12, "context": "The passage first alludes to the Seminoles as allies , and then continues : `` They are a mixture of the remains of the Cawittas , Talepoosas , Coosas , Apalachias , Conshacs or Coosades , Oakmulgis , Oconis , Okchoys , Alibamons , Natchez , Weetumkus , Pakanas , Ta\u00ebnsas , Chacsihoomas , Ab\u00e9kas and some other tribes whose names I do not recollect", "context_len": 65}}}, "blames": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "And so the Creature blames it on the rain , and I go off quite free", "context_len": 16}}}, "bulldog": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "He ran great risks , and if he escaped the vigilance of the massive circus-man who patrolled the outside of the tent with a cowhide and a bulldog , perhaps he merited the fame he was sure to win", "context_len": 39}}}, "inseparable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 46, "context": "In the big castle , let us hope , since we may not know , that even crowned heads were resting easily , and that the level rays were powerless at that early hour to waken them to that sense of great uneasiness supposed to be inseparable from the lot of the nobly born", "context_len": 54}}}, "sports": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 29, "context": "The cause of the warring , or the pretense for it , against `` some other Indians from the west '' is curiously similar to the rivalry in athletic sports , which took place between the western Iroquois and their subdivisions , and finally led to the destruction of the Erie or K\u00e1'hkwa Indians ( Cusick , Johnson )", "context_len": 59}}}, "phonograph": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Ramsey said that her neighbors need not think they could send over and borrow this new institution , though she was sure the institution was much more entertaining than any phonograph", "context_len": 31}}}, "tremendously": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` That was hottish work , '' he said , glancing round at his little army to see that none were missing , `` and we had some tremendously narrow escapes", "context_len": 31}}}, "friendless": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "Davenport a rich relation nor was she herself in the friendless condition necessary for the full requirements of fiction , so she was afraid the round of amusements and social triumphs generally enjoyed by the heroine would not fall to her share", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "You must keep in your mind all the time that he is a poor , neglected , friendless , unhappy old man and that if you can succeed in bringing even a little sunshine into his life , you will be doing a great deal", "context_len": 45}}}, "eagles": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 38, "context": "Charlie had made a bridge of poles from the spruce to the pine , and used to sit there , when the wind blew , till the tree shook so much that it frightened him , or the eagles came to their nest ; but , after a while , they became so accustomed to him as to take fish from the limbs where he placed it", "context_len": 67}}}, "terribly": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 23, "context": "Fortunately , the sun 's rays came down hot and bright , and the rock grew warmer , so that by degrees the terribly void look began to leave Mark Penelly 's face , and at last , when Harry held out his hand , saying , `` Do you feel better ? '' Mark Penelly caught it in both of his , clung to it , and , turning half over on his face , laid his forehead against it , and , forgetting his years of manhood , lay there in his weakness , and sobbed and cried like a child", "context_len": 103}}}, "glued": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 46, "context": "Their uncle has sent with the book two butterfly-nets ; a pretty case filled with crooked scissors , tweezers , pincers , and all sorts of sharp steel instruments ; a pretty box , at the bottom of which are little round pieces of cork , glued in rows , with long large-headed pins to run through the butterflies ; and another little box , with a lot of small squares of glass , which are to be put over their wings to keep them open , and prevent them from fluttering and beating about", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "But no sooner did he lower the threatening spear than I once more glued my lips to the wound , sucking hard at it with the object of extracting the poison before it had contaminated the blood ; and in this effort I was happily successful , for although there was a slight swelling of the limb , and some pain for an hour or two , that was all that happened ; and before morning my patient had quite recovered from all the effects of her alarming adventure", "context_len": 89}}}, "bushy": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "They were principally gray , with great , long bushy tails , and they were very tame", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "This he did five or six times in succession , to the great amusement and satisfaction of the little ones , who were beginning to hope he would go with them all the way to the house , when with a pert , defiant whisk of his bushy tail , he leaped down the bank , and was lost to sight in the thick trees of the ravine", "context_len": 68}}}, "peanut": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "Permit me , grinned Chub Tuttle , as a token of my high appreciation and gratitude , to present you with a genuine fresh roasted , double-jointed California peanut", "context_len": 29}}}, "public": {"freq": 145, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 126, "word_index": 12, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 31, "context": "Whether the powers that rule the entrance to the same came to the conclusion that to a little girl of twelve and a little boy of four the term of general public could not honestly be applied , or whether all rules of procedure and precedence were magnanimously waived in their favor , certain it is that the little card in question bore the incredible inscription : Admit Master Albert Allyn and his little friend , Miss Marie-Celeste Harris , to the East Terrace between the hours of twelve and three on Thursday", "context_len": 93}}}, "connection": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 59, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}}}, "gymnasium": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 61, "context": "The man who built it and attempted to run it had failed to find business profitable , and in time it was purchased at a low price by Urian Eliot , Roger \u2019 s father , who moved it to its present location and pledged it to the academy as long as the scholars should continue to use it as a gymnasium", "context_len": 62}}}, "recess": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 89, "context": "The air seemed to have grown more close and sultry while the children were within the cave , and , just as they reached the mouth , a low , grumbling sound , which they had heard for some time , but not taken much notice of , broke into a crash of thunder that seemed to make the ground shake beneath them , while at the same instant a brilliant streak of lightning flashed zigzag across the sky , lighting up the gloom behind them to its furthest recess", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Yes , said the letter M , that is true , and we can think about these things now , for it is recess time", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` You had it when we went down to recess", "context_len": 10}}}, "dramatics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then they act out these stories in their classes where they have dramatics and different ones take the parts of the bad child or the unmannerly child or the unmannerly grown-up , and of all the people whom these met with in their adventures", "context_len": 44}}}, "vacant": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 59, "context": "But the strain was too much for Peggy , and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "In a vacant lot behind one of the houses there was a whirligig , that you could ride on and get sick in about a minute ; it was splendid", "context_len": 30}}}, "disappointment": {"freq": 103, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 98, "word_index": 77, "context": "He knew not then that the example before him would nerve him in moments of severest trial , then fast approaching , that the one accusation urged against the Christians , which he had felt most keenly , that of cowardice , was answered in the weak yet valiant boy , who found strength in the name of Christ to endure all for His sake ; neither did his fierce countrymen know that they were preparing a disappointment for the pagan Anlaf , and for all those of his house and lineage", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "When they reached the dining-room , Miss Belmore was already seated at the table , ready to receive them ; but as places were set for only three , two little hearts were again doomed to disappointment , for two little minds , without any sort of consultation , had separately arrived at the conclusion that all that elaborate preparation could certainly mean nothing less than luncheon with Her Majesty in person", "context_len": 72}}}, "consisted": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 33, "context": "The enormous crowd who had followed us up from the beach were not permitted to enter the palisaded enclosure , which was strictly taboo to the common herd ; our party therefore now consisted solely of those who had brought me up the river , four individuals who had joined us outside the gate -- and whom I took to be officials of some sort -- and my unworthy self ; and , for my own part , I would very willingly have waived the distinction of forming one of the party", "context_len": 92}}}, "tenderfoot": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "At last he saw him , so he shrieked from the window , Hello , tenderfoot ! \u2019", "context_len": 18}}}, "downstairs": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 74, "context": "came up to my room -- I will confess to you that I was hiding from her , absolutely hiding , and shaking from head to foot , scarcely knowing what to do -- and she came in as bold as brass , and yet with a new sort of humility about her , and she said to me , 'Will you forgive me ? And if you forgive me , will you come downstairs and let me put my hand inside your arm ? ' And somehow , although it was the very last thing on earth that I wanted to do , I did it ; and now here I am , and I do n't feel nearly so much afraid of her as I used", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Take my thanks , Harrison , '' he chuckled , as he led him downstairs ; `` the black dog is off his Highness 's back , and when he waits on his Majesty to-night , he will be worthy himself", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "Her ear for music was so correct , that on one occasion she came downstairs from her room , to tell me I had played a wrong note in a chord of Beethoven , and the exact note I should have played", "context_len": 42}}}, "nick": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 39, "context": "Julius did not see it , but Hugh did , and as his little voice was too weak to rise above Julius \u2019 laughter he hobbled on his crutches and pulled Julius out of the way just in the nick of time", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` Can anything be more cheerful than to know that you are altogether in the hands of a higher Power -- to know that you will be taken care of ; or , if you perish , to know that it will be in the very nick of time ? ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "injury": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 15, "context": "But although I had passed through the day unscathed , so far as actual bodily injury was concerned , it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything", "context_len": 128}}}, "football": {"freq": 145, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 145, "word_index": 8, "context": "What did it matter whether he himself played football or not ? True , he would have enjoyed doing so , but , to a certain extent , he had triumphed over the fellow who had tried to drive him out of school , and might it not be best if that satisfied him ? Discord on the team was a serious misfortune , and only for Eliot \u2019 s persistence he would have taken himself away already", "context_len": 78}}}, "forgave": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Hugh forgave Julius , and they became fast friends from that time , and Julius never forgot that Hugh had saved his life", "context_len": 23}, "VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 28, "context": "It would be very hard , and I 'm afraid I would n't quite like to do it ; but I would try to think how often Jesus forgave me , and I would say , 'forgive me my trespasses ' as I forgive Lem and Dolly , and maybe that would make it easier", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "If you forgot for a moment , and your temper overcame you , I think he knew how you had struggled with it this morning , and so pitied and forgave , sending the grace and strength you needed as soon as you saw your own want of it", "context_len": 49}}}, "prisoner": {"freq": 174, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 171, "word_index": 19, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "When she saw him , she supposed that John had taken him prisoner for stealing his apples ; and Lem had too often before been in such trouble for her to think it a very serious matter", "context_len": 37}}}, "base": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 61, "context": "The favorite kite , the kite that balanced best , took the wind best , and flew best , and that would stand all day when you got it up , was the house kite , which was made of three sticks , and shaped nearly in the form of the gable of a gambrel-roofed house , only smaller at the base than at the point where the roof would begin", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "There were some things so base that a boy could not do them ; and what happened out of doors , and strictly within the boy 's world , had to be kept sacredly secret among the boys", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "When the wretched prisoner was brought into Alexander 's presence , Alexander demanded of him how he could have been so base as to have seized , bound , and at last murdered his kinsman and benefactor", "context_len": 37}}}, "buff": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "His buff coat and crimson sash were like those worn by the boy , and the velvet cap he carried in his hand left uncovered curls as brown ; but instead of the childish calm of the boy 's hazel eyes , the older man 's glance now flashed with the fire of an eagle , now glowed with the exalted enthusiasm of a poet", "context_len": 65}}}, "pockets": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 77, "word_index": 124, "context": "as it might be here '' -- arranging a tankard at the corner of the table -- '' with a good high road leading up to it from the sea , as it might be my tobacco pipe '' -- laying it down with care ; `` and if you 'll believe me , sir , we took and run races , as it might be along my tobacco pipe , and as soon as them Spaniards was 'ware of our coming , they took and ran out by 'tother gate , and left the town empty ! There was seven churches all chock full of gold and silver idols and candle-sticks , and such like : 't was just who 'd fill his pockets fastest ! ''", "context_len": 128}}}, "creamed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then they had supper and they asked him if he liked creamed chicken and he whistled", "context_len": 16}}}, "cocoa": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 34, "context": "He watched him as a cat would watch a mouse , and ate everything he ate and drank whatever he drank -- -- No one had coffee or tea , they had milk , cocoa , buttermilk , and cold water , which latter the kiddies drank as if it had been something that would make them super-children", "context_len": 58}}}, "giggle": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The children began to giggle , for they thought he was such a funny boy , and a funny boy he was", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "Ben kept the little girls in such a state of giggle that they could scarcely do the dishes , but what with the labors of the day and the later excitement they were ready for bed early , and went up leaving Cousin Ben with a book before him", "context_len": 49}}}, "tonsils": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Now , there were two tonsils and they had made their home in Lucy \u2019 s throat", "context_len": 17}}}, "throats": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 68, "context": "`` Upwards it leads to the banqueting hall , and you can comprehend my escape this evening , '' said he ; `` but our path is now downwards , unless you would like to go up and see the drunken beasts of murderers snoring off their debauch upon the floor as they fell ; oh , that it were lawful for a Christian man to cut their throats as they lie ; many innocent lives would be saved thereby , which those brutes will live to destroy", "context_len": 88}}}, "seaside": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "In a short time they will take her away , probably to some seaside place , the house will be disinfected , and the girls will come back to their work", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 56, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "twin": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 53, "context": "There was also a sort of ball play in use among the Creeks and many other Indian tribes , by which a ball ( p\u00faku ) was aimed at an object suspended on the top of a high pole , or , as it is played now , at the top of two twin poles ( puk-\u00e1bi ) , called sometimes `` maypoles", "context_len": 63}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "The heavens had opened , and a deluge of rain had descended on the jungle , filling the dry beds of the ygapos as though by magic , overflowing the banks of the streams so that along their shores twin torrents raged", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He left twin sons , both famous warriors , and it was doubtful which would be chosen by the tribe to be their chief or king in the father 's place", "context_len": 31}}}, "causing": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I \u2019 m causing trouble", "context_len": 5}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 44, "context": "My uncle busied himself till all traces of his worry lost themselves in his activity ; my aunt bustled about the house , driving the servants hither and thither , bringing linen from the huge oak chest , furbishing the massive furniture , and causing a huge supply of viands to be prepared for the entertainment of our guests -- for the invited company numbered close on a score , so that our house would be filled to overflowing , and rooms had to be engaged at `` The Bell Inn '' and `` The Blue Posts ''", "context_len": 98}}}, "pranks": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 59, "context": "At another time nurse would have ordered them to shut their eyes and not speak another word ; but to-night she seemed to think it quite right and natural that they should be so very wide awake , and not only gave them an extra amount of petting and kissing , but told them stories of Uncle Ruthven 's pranks when he was a boy , and of his wonderful sayings and doings , till mamma , coming up and finding this going on , was half inclined to find fault with the old woman herself", "context_len": 96}}}, "playmate": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 54, "context": "He hunted in the brook for sedges , captured a Red Admiral to grace Bobby 's collection , filled his pockets with sweet gale and asphodel for Lilian 's dried vases , and made himself such a delightful companion that the children agreed that Father on a holiday was out and out the best playmate they knew", "context_len": 57}}}, "surprising": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 11, "context": "How very great and wonderful He must be ! It seems surprising that He should care for every one on this little ball , -- how much more astonishing that He should have condescended to come and live upon it , to have appeared as a feeble Child in one of the worlds that He had made , and then actually to die , like one of the creatures that He had formed ! Is not God \u2019 s power wonderful , and His love more wonderful still ?", "context_len": 89}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "There ought to be no pleasure at all in conveying bad news to people , though here and there , it must be confessed , one sometimes meets individuals who seem to rejoice in any news whatsoever , and the more startling and surprising the better", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Jumbo smelt it , and lifting up his black nose gave one or two sniffs , and then darting past them at a rate surprising in a rabbit of his age made straight for the gap in the hedge ; and , of course , after that there was no more time for conversation , for where Jumbo went the other rabbits followed", "context_len": 63}}}, "puffed": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` I went across seas first time along o ' Lord General Cromwell to Ireland , and he gave us our bellyful of fighting , and no mistake ; but it ai n't fighting that I complain of , having been always held a valiant man of my inches ; '' and he puffed out his broad chest and looked a very crusader", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It \u2019 s all up with us , puffed McGlory , hanging over the edge of the car", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Uncle Joe puffed at his pipe thoughtfully", "context_len": 7}}}, "wretches": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 93, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}}}, "behaving": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 46, "context": "Merriman started the idea of a small school for the special education of special girls , she had spared no expense to have everything done in as thoroughly nice a manner as possible ; and the girls themselves were delighted , and showed their appreciation by behaving in a hoydenish and school-girl fashion", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` If you do n't want to have utter ruin you must go on behaving as nicely as ever you can , bearing with every one , being gentle and considerate , and trying to make every one happy", "context_len": 39}}}, "toy": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Some fellows had little toy guns left over from Christmas , but they were considered rather babyish , and any kind of stick was better ; the right kind of a gun for a boy 's company was a wooden gun , such as some of the big boys had , with the barrel painted different from the stock", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then the boys found another toy store ; but they considered the dealer mean ; he asked very high prices , and he said , when a boy hung back from buying a thing that it was `` a very superior article , '' and the boys had that for a by-word , and they holloed it at the storekeeper 's boy when they wanted to plague him", "context_len": 68}}}, "roguish": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "She seized Frankie in her arms and covered him with kisses ; but that roguish young gentleman after exclaiming , `` Hallo , Patty ! '' would have nothing more to say to her , and struggled to be set free that he might run and see `` dat nanny-doat and dat pony wis long ears", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "It was necessary to keep the house very still , on account of Aunt Bessie , who was so very nervous that the least sound disturbed her ; and roguish , noisy Frankie was , by Aunt Patty 's earnest request , allowed to go to her house , where , for a few days , he lorded it over that humble servant of his to his heart 's content", "context_len": 70}}}, "broth": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "There was broth and hot milk and some stewed fruit , and Jane brushed her hair that fell in a bed of rings and asked if she did n't want to sit up", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 9, "context": "The hostess who brought Dick 's bowl of onion broth at this moment destroyed his chance of feigning sleep , and he had to resign himself to endure his companion 's conversation which flowed on , garnished with oaths and cant phrases in three or four different languages , without any interruption , till by an unguarded movement Richard exposed his face to the light of the fire , and the stranger stared a moment , and then sprang up exclaiming , `` Body o ' me if it be not Measter Dick himself ! ''", "context_len": 96}}}, "hood": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 9, "context": "Jennie in a fuzzy grayish brown frock with a hood , made a dear little Pussy Willow , Edna in pink with her rosy cheeks was the very picture of Pinky Blooms , Dorothy 's golden head made a lovely centre for Daisy White , while as for Ben , the big giant , he was the roughest , toughest old Pine Knot one could imagine", "context_len": 66}}}, "sweater": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "She wore her outside jacket , too , over her sweater , and she took along her little apron , so she could wear that when she played , so as not to hurt her good dress", "context_len": 37}}}, "scarf": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "He had purloined David 's scarf from the saddle-room , and dropped it into the horse-trough , had filled Bobby 's hat with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread", "context_len": 128}}}, "congratulate": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 49, "context": "Madge and Phil seemed very good friends , and , as for Tom , though he wondered at the sudden companionship that had sprung up between the quarter-back and the pretty girl he had once been so anxious to get away from Langridge , he could not help but congratulate himself on knowing Ruth", "context_len": 54}}}, "homesick": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Bessie , '' whispered Maggie , as they went in , `` does it make you feel a little as if you was homesick for our geranium and heliotrope ? ''", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` It 's too bad ! '' exclaimed Maggie ; `` it 's an awful burden to bear , it makes me feel homesick , and I want to cry about it , and I just will -- there now ! '' and Maggie burst into tears", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "It is a pretty sight , a mass of gray in a setting of deep green -- the trees that encircle the spot , and it made the poor homesick `` beasts '' take a little interest in life once more", "context_len": 41}}}, "puppy": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 62, "context": "For since Flossy was stolen , he had been very careful as to the company he kept , and looked with a very suspicious eye upon any one who wore a ragged coat , which was not very just of Flossy , since a ragged coat may cover as true and honest a heart as ever beat ; but as the poor puppy knew no better , and had received some hard treatment at the hands of those whose miserable garments covered hard and cruel hearts , he must be excused for thinking that the one was a sign of the other", "context_len": 102}}}, "bull": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 87, "context": "the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the mind by the capricious action of memory , while far greater events drop out of it", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "At Pensford the rabble made a bull savage , and then tried to make it attack his congregation ; at Whitechapel they drove cows among the listeners and threw stones , one of which hit Wesley between the eyes ; but after he had wiped away the blood he went on with his address , telling the people that `` God hath not given us the spirit of fear ''", "context_len": 70}}}, "biscuit": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 68, "context": "It was not of the least use in the world , and could not be , but the children were allowed to keep it till , one fatal day , when the mother had a number of other ladies to tea , as the fashion used to be in small towns , when they sat down to a comfortable gossip over dainty dishes of stewed chicken , hot biscuit , peach-preserves , sweet tomato-pickles , and pound-cake", "context_len": 77}}}, "refused": {"freq": 147, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 147, "word_index": 9, "context": "But Dolly turned sulky in her turn , and refused to say a word more ; and Lem , knowing it was useless to try to make her speak when she did not choose , strolled into the woods to see if he could find any berries for his breakfast ; while she , still shivering from her night 's exposure in the Ice Glen , tried to kindle a fire from the wet sticks which lay around ; and finding this in vain , crept to her wretched bed , and tried to warm herself there", "context_len": 97}}}, "frisked": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "The little dog , who had at first seemed to fear some harm threatened its master , frisked back and forth before them , barking frantically , finally sitting up on its haunches with its forward paws drooping , its mouth open and its protruding tongue quivering ; for at last it appeared to comprehend that there was really no danger , and this affair was one over which even a small yellow dog should laugh and be happy", "context_len": 79}}}, "dizzy": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 27, "context": "They crossed the stream by a bridge roughly made of two pine-trees flung from bank to bank , with a rope for a hand-rail , a somewhat dizzy foothold for anyone who minded the rushing water beneath , but infinitely to be preferred to stones and mortar in the children 's opinion", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "A sudden horror of great rolling eyes and rending horns and crazy hoofs hurtled through the girl 's dizzy brain", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I was still dizzy from the effects of the blow I had received , and this probably accounted for the complete indifference that I felt with regard to my fate", "context_len": 30}}}, "tomato": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "Conway contained several things which made the dinner much more of a feast than it would otherwise have been , for there was a jar of tomato soup , a small chicken pie with scalloped leaves and little balls of crust on top , some delicious pickles , a glass of currant jelly and another of cranberry sauce", "context_len": 58}}}, "chummy": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "I think we really are quite nice girls -- I mean we never quarrel , and we are always chummy and affectionate , and we try to do our best", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` They 're quite chummy for a freshman and a sophomore", "context_len": 11}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Or did you just want to be chummy ? If you came to bum gas , you \u2019 re out of luck , Blake", "context_len": 24}}}, "mix": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 25, "context": "I can tell you what to do , said Uncle Isaac ; go and get some of that blue clay by the brook , and mix it up with water that has the chill taken off , and plaster it right on three inches thick , and you \u2019 ll see what it will do ; all you want is to keep the air out", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "He call me by my name , an ' den it come over me dat we done got mix ' up in de shuffle an ' dat I wuz shootin ' at you", "context_len": 33}}}, "graces": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "It is true , they must feel confident of his superiority ; but when this superiority stands out so clearly and distinctly marked , combined , too , with all the graces and attractions of youth and manly beauty , as it was in the case of Alexander , the minds of men are brought very easily and rapidly under its sway", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "But American children have a different idea of the fowl which graces the table at Christmas time", "context_len": 17}}}, "oiled": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 56, "context": "John told him that he bored the hole for the mouth , and then turned the milk out , filled it with salt water , and set it in the sun , when , the meat decaying , he washed it all out ; then scraping the outside with a knife and piece of glass , oiled it , and made the face with an old file , which he ground to a sharp point", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "Underneath were a few pieces of silver-plate , such as had not been melted down for the use of His Late Majesty King Charles , the martyr , while at the bottom of the chest was a package carefully protected by a covering of oiled silk", "context_len": 46}}}, "elephant": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 24, "context": "Tip was of no particular breed , and he had no personal beauty ; he was of the color of a mouse of an elephant , and his tail was without the smallest grace ; it was smooth and round , but it was so strong that he could pull a boy all over the town by it , and usually did ; and he had the best , and kindest , and truest ugly old face in the world", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "Ever mindful of the inestimable value of the mail treasure , he adjusted them with much care about his neck , somewhat as if they were life preservers , and holding his light rifle in his hand , he stepped cautiously into the current like an elephant venturing upon a rickety bridge", "context_len": 52}}}, "answers": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 74, "context": "The Sultan proceeded to make familiar with Alaeddin and to talk with him , and Alaeddin answered him with all courtliness and fluency , as he had been bred in kings ' palaces or as he were their constant associate ; [ 471 ] and the more the talk was prolonged between them , the more gladness and joy redoubled on the Sultan for that which he heard of the goodliness of Alaeddin 's answers and the sweetness of his speech", "context_len": 81}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Mebbe \u2019 tain \u2019 t the same dorg , but he answers the deescription the Widder Jones give ; and it \u2019 s mighty queer a dorg should be hookin \u2019 it round here , where there ain \u2019 t no houses nigher than a quarter of a mile", "context_len": 49}}}, "jumbled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I can \u2019 t answer all three at once , for then the answers would be all jumbled up", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "She picked up the books , swept the rest of the feast back into the hamper in a jumbled heap , thrust it into Ermengarde 's arms , and pushed her before her toward the door", "context_len": 36}}}, "keeper": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 11, "context": "Hartley 's last remark , Donald felt sure that the old keeper , of whom he , as well as Ted and Harry Allyn , stood in not a little awe , was not going to take offence at the new turn affairs had taken ; while Harold , to whom it sounded as though they had been having a somewhat prosy sermon , rather congratulated himself that Donald had carried him off to see a neighbor 's kennels down the river", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "You will make me well , keeper , \u2019 he seemed to say", "context_len": 13}}}, "sandpaper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "They don \u2019 t have sandpaper and nail scissors in the jungles , do they ?", "context_len": 16}}}, "scissors": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 16, "context": "Their uncle has sent with the book two butterfly-nets ; a pretty case filled with crooked scissors , tweezers , pincers , and all sorts of sharp steel instruments ; a pretty box , at the bottom of which are little round pieces of cork , glued in rows , with long large-headed pins to run through the butterflies ; and another little box , with a lot of small squares of glass , which are to be put over their wings to keep them open , and prevent them from fluttering and beating about", "context_len": 95}}}, "jungles": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "Bomba lived far back in the jungles of the Amazon with a half-demented naturalist who told the lad nothing of his past", "context_len": 22}}}, "wilds": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 35, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}}}, "rocks": {"freq": 189, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 187, "word_index": 8, "context": "They said there were great hills of strange-looking rocks , with a flat between them , and a brook running through it ; that the Indians used to live there ; and you could see the stones where they made their fires , and find arrow-heads , and Indian things that were buried there ; and Uncle Isaac knew where ; that somewhere along the side of the brook there was red paint , as good as ever was , and that Uncle Isaac had a room painted with it ; that there were partridges there , and way back was a pond , that the brook ran out of , with pickerel in it", "context_len": 115}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "O the sparkling rush of diamonds , -- the white misty foam breaking on the picturesque rocks beneath , -- the accessories so beautiful , -- the cloud-like veil so transparently lovely !", "context_len": 33}}}, "swamps": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "We have the cruel and lurking Chicasa , the powerful and ingenious but treacherous and corruptible Cha'hta , the magnanimous and hospitable , proud and revengeful Creek , the aggressive Alibamu , the quarrelsome Y\u00e1massi , and the self-willed , independent Seminole , jealous of the enjoyment of his savage freedom in the swamps and everglades of the semi-tropical peninsula", "context_len": 60}}}, "marbles": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 7, "context": "There were three kinds of games at marbles which the boys played : one with a long ring marked out on the ground , and a base some distance off , which you began to shoot from ; another with a round ring , whose line formed the base ; and another with holes , three or five , hollowed in the earth at equal distances from each other , which was called knucks", "context_len": 74}}}, "joggle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "And I joggle along and roll much better than you do", "context_len": 11}}}, "crookedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "But the big Marble was so mad that it rolled away crookedly and no one thought so much of it", "context_len": 20}}}, "advantages": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 10, "context": "And though Alexander must have been aware of all these advantages of the act , still no one could have thought of or adopted such a plan unless he was accustomed to consider and regard , in his dealings with others , the feelings and affections of the heart , and to cherish a warm sympathy for them", "context_len": 58}}}, "forlorn": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 13, "context": "Maggie and Bessie were rather uncomfortable , for they did not think the forlorn marigolds pretty , and they did not wish to hurt Dolly 's feelings by saying so ; but mamma came to their relief , by saying , as she could with truth , --", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "During many years her home was still to be in the quaint old palace , described by others as draughty , weird , forlorn , desolate ; though she herself so resolutely looked upon the discomforts of the old building through rose-tinted glasses", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Very precious it was , too , in the eyes of the earthly father , who watched the scene , and looking from his own tenderly cared for , daintily dressed darlings , to the forlorn , ragged outcast , thanked God that for all three alike had the blessed words been spoken , `` Suffer little children to come unto me", "context_len": 62}}}, "daffodils": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Time steals on very quickly when we are fully occupied , and winter seemed to slide into spring , and daffodils to replace the snowdrops in the garden before they had realized the welcome change , and Easter drew near once more , with all its delights of violets , young lambs , and early blossom", "context_len": 56}}}, "twins": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 2, "context": "After the twins were born they tried a grown nursemaid who bored them by sitting around when she was upstairs and making many excuses to get down to the kitchen , where she disputed with Bridget who declared one or the other of them must go , and they simply could not give up Bridget", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Hurrah for the Jersey twins ! '' exclaimed Tom", "context_len": 10}}}, "ticks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "And they don \u2019 t stop at night , but as it would be very hard to understand their story without leaving out all the ticks and the tocks we will hear it with those left out", "context_len": 37}}}, "forgetting": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 42, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The Master said that he had kept forgetting things all day long", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 85, "context": "to learn just what the issue had been , and at a time when he would be able to stop and tell about it ? Would any one else in the world have thought of suggesting that he should give three short little whistles when he reached the Browns ' , in Remsen Street , so that she should know just how near he was ? Surely no one ; and it was just this surpassing interest in every living body , to the utter forgetting of all that concerned herself , that made Marie-Celeste different from other children , that made everybody love her , and that makes it worth while for me to try to tell this story of one summer in her blessed little life", "context_len": 128}}}, "tick": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "Why , there ain \u2019 t no doubt in the world but he \u2019 s the thief , for wasn \u2019 t the watches and the rings and some of the money found hid under the straw tick of his bed right in your own house ? That \u2019 s proof enough , Mis \u2019 Jones , and there ain \u2019 t no gittin \u2019 round it", "context_len": 67}}}, "forsake": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "I wish the lad would leave his home , if but for a short time , until the signs are less threatening ; but he would not forsake his father in danger , and I ought hardly to wish it", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The `` Biglow Papers '' and their humor might not have moved him from his life-long allegiance , but the eloquence of Corwin 's famous speech against the Mexican war had grounded him in principles which he could not afterwards forsake", "context_len": 41}}}, "marry": {"freq": 124, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 117, "word_index": 91, "context": "son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not give them his daughter", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "Alaeddin [ 329 ] waited till his mother had made an end of her speech and said to her , `` O my mother , all that thou thinkest I know ; marry , I know full well that I am the son of poor folk , nor may all this thy talk anywise avail to move me from my purpose ; but I beseech thee , an I be thy very son and thou love me , do me this kindness ; else wilt thou lose me , for death hasteneth upon me , an I attain not my wish of the beloved of my heart", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` You think that it is very hard on you that a man of my sort should want you to be his wife , but men of my sort , when they make fortunes , often do marry girls like you", "context_len": 41}}}, "separated": {"freq": 84, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 72, "context": "And now you have at least an idea of how Marie-Celeste passed her time on the steamer , and you can understand how there might have been some people rather less glad than sorry when they felt the machinery stop at two o'clock one morning , and knew that the Queenstown passengers were being transferred to the tender , and that before sunset all the people aboard the great steamer would be separated to the four winds", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "You have your family , from whom you have been so long separated , awaiting your return with anxiety , and I pray you to make no stay on my account", "context_len": 31}}}, "behaved": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 70, "context": "They knew the fellows who were helping the priest ; one of them was the boy who stood on his head till he had to have it shaved ; they would have liked to mock him then and there for wearing a petticoat , and most of them had the bitterest scorn and hate for Catholics in their hearts ; but they were afraid of the sacristan , and they behaved very well as long as they were in the church ; but as soon as they got out they whooped and yelled , and stoned the sacristan when he ran after them", "context_len": 103}}}, "fig": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "When any one says that a person isn \u2019 t worth thinking about and he wouldn \u2019 t give a fig for that person , it doesn \u2019 t mean an insult to the family of figs , but is simply an expression people have used for a long , long time", "context_len": 52}}}, "unfair": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 31, "context": "No doubt he had a happy thought , which turned out well ; but I can not see that there was anything wonderful in it , and it seemed to me unfair that one who is a mere boy should receive higher praise than yourself , who , as I heard Sir John and Sir Adam Tedbond say last night at the refectory , bore yourself right gallantly", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "You do not know what bitter feelings I had in my heart , for they really were undeniably some of the most attractive children you could possibly find ; and Florence was so witty , and Janet so delicate and refined and sweet in all her ways ! I could not be angry with them , but I did think it fearfully unfair that so many blessings should be poured on their heads and so few given to me , for I was not even specially clever", "context_len": 87}}}, "figs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "When any one says that a person isn \u2019 t worth thinking about and he wouldn \u2019 t give a fig for that person , it doesn \u2019 t mean an insult to the family of figs , but is simply an expression people have used for a long , long time", "context_len": 52}}}, "thoroughbred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I \u2019 m not what is known as a thoroughbred , but they say I \u2019 m brighter than a great many thoroughbreds", "context_len": 23}}}, "thoroughbreds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I \u2019 m not what is known as a thoroughbred , but they say I \u2019 m brighter than a great many thoroughbreds", "context_len": 23}}}, "kennels": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 78, "context": "Hartley 's last remark , Donald felt sure that the old keeper , of whom he , as well as Ted and Harry Allyn , stood in not a little awe , was not going to take offence at the new turn affairs had taken ; while Harold , to whom it sounded as though they had been having a somewhat prosy sermon , rather congratulated himself that Donald had carried him off to see a neighbor 's kennels down the river", "context_len": 82}}}, "dreamland": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "Father and Bobby were asleep in two minutes , but Peggy lay awake for a long time , watching the shadows of the peat fire flicker upon the rough beams of the roof , till at length fire and heather merged into a dreamland kingdom , where she was walking with Rollo upon the clouds , and fishing teacups out of a flowing sea below", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "Mamma laid the little head down again , and held up a warning finger to Uncle Ruthven , who stole softly to a corner , where he was out of Miss Baby 's sight and hearing , to wait till she should be fairly off to dreamland", "context_len": 47}}}, "saddest": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "Somehow it seems to me the very saddest monument in all the chapel ; but I think it was beautiful in Queen Victoria to have it placed here out of sympathy for the poor French Empress , who had lost everything -- husband and kingdom , and , last of all , this brave son ; for I think he must have been brave , do n't you , Mr", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` The Vizier arose and looked from the window towards Alaeddin 's palace , but could see nothing , neither palace nor aught else ; so his wit was bewildered and he was amazed and returned to the Sultan , who said to him , `` Now knowest thou the cause of my distress and seest Alaeddin his palace , whereof thou saddest that it was shut", "context_len": 67}}}, "boast": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 45, "context": "It was secret sorrow and sometimes open shame to my boy that his grandfather should be an Englishman , and that even his father should have been a year old when he came to this country ; but on his mother 's side he could boast a grandfather and a great-grandfather who had taken part , however briefly or obscurely , in both the wars against Great Britain", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 70, "context": "on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me , died out , and I began to prepare myself , as best I could , to meet whatever fate might have in store for me with the fortitude befitting a Christian and an Englishman", "context_len": 128}}}, "unimportant": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 68, "context": "Yet I do not suppose that D'Aubusson selected him for this duty as a reward for so much self denial and study , but because by that self denial and study he is more fitted for it than any of us here , save some three or four knights in the other langues , all of whom are in too high a position to be employed in so unimportant a duty", "context_len": 71}}}, "fussed": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 9, "context": "Goodness gracious ! ain \u2019 t y \u2019 u fussed \u2019 round amusin \u2019 Jimmy , a-fixin \u2019 squirrel traps an \u2019 swings an \u2019 things for him ? That \u2019 s more \u2019 n squared any little thing I could do for y \u2019 u to make y \u2019 u comf \u2019 table", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "rules": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 64, "context": "nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "RULES AND REGULATIONS", "context_len": 3}}}, "cocker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then came Ouji , a cocker spaniel , and last came Susy , a little fox terrier", "context_len": 17}}}, "spaniel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "He was a water spaniel , and was a great favorite with all the boys , who often coaxed him to the shore , where they could play with him", "context_len": 30}}}, "gotten": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 106, "context": "the safe keeping of the lamp under the earth ; and he looked well into [ 547 ] the figures , both mothers and daughters , [ 548 ] but saw not the lamp , whereupon rage overrode him and he smote the sand a second time , that he might certify himself of Alaeddin 's death , but saw him not in the treasure ; whereat he redoubled in wrath , and yet more when it was certified to him that the lad was alive upon the surface of the earth and he knew that he had come forth from under the ground and had gotten the lamp , on account whereof he himself had suffered toil and torment such as passeth man 's power to endure", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 84, "context": "Accordingly , when she came up , he saw her and viewed her well , beyond his wish ; and from that time till now , O King of the Age , life hath not been pleasant to him [ 366 ] and he will e'en have me seek her of Thy Grace , [ 367 ] so thou mayst marry her with him , and I can not do away this conceit from his wit , for that the love of her hath gotten possession of his vitals , so that he saith to me , 'Know , O mother mine , that , except I attain my desire , assuredly I am a dead man", "context_len": 117}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` acquired , gotten , come by thee '' ( khetsitu bika )", "context_len": 13}}}, "baseball": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 7, "context": "Here we present three crackerjack stories of baseball , football , and basketball , written in the vernacular of the boy of to-day , full of action , suspense and thrills , in language every boy will understand , and which we know will be enthusiastically endorsed by all boys", "context_len": 50}}}, "bat": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "But he was too annoyed to be fair , and as a punishment for what he chose to call Drusie 's bragging , he tucked his bat under his arm , and told them that he was not going to play with them any more", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "And he also liked to tease Grace by making fun of her doll Gracie and by tossing her up in the air , and playing with her as though she were a ball , and he could bat her in a game of his own mean making", "context_len": 47}}}, "sweetest": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 17, "context": "Her mother sprang up and kissed her rapturously , crying -- 'Is n't she the dearest and sweetest thing and the smartest ! Think of her learning that and acting it off so completely , and not three years old ! She is smarter than Violet ' -- and then Violet set up such a howl ! Her mother pacified her by saying Marilla should tell her a piece , and after several efforts Cinderella did induce her to say by a great deal of prompting 'Milkman , Milkman , where have you been ? ' Think of the wear on the child 's nerves , and she looked so tired", "context_len": 111}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "My boy tried to sleep , but the more he reflected upon his chances of getting through the night alive the smaller they seemed ; and so he woke up his potential murderer from the sweetest and soundest slumber , and said he was going home , but he was afraid ; and the boy had to go and wake his father", "context_len": 62}}}, "paws": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 46, "context": "Nothing was farther from Timothy 's thoughts than to make any disturbance whatever -- why should he ? Were they not the best of friends , he and that blessed little Albert ? so he never raised his head from where it rested upon his outstretched paws , only looked up with that gaze of implicit confidence peculiar to the kind eyes of the Laverick setter , and which made Albert lose not a second in spreading his little coat out beneath him , throwing his two arms around Timothy 's neck , and pillowing his head on his beautiful silky coat", "context_len": 102}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He placed the monkey \u2019 s paws beneath his shoulders and signified that it was to pull with all its strength", "context_len": 21}}}, "paw": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 35, "context": "She found beautiful new flowers growing in her room , whimsical little gifts tucked under pillows , and once , as they sat together in the evening , they heard the scratch of a heavy paw on the door , and when Sara went to find out what it was , there stood a great dog -- a splendid Russian boarhound -- with a grand silver and gold collar bearing an inscription", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "There , please move that paw a little -- that 's it ; now , Timothy , keep very still ! Please , please do n't snap for that fly , or they 'll hear you ; still ! still , Timothy , while I stroke your head like this , till , till -- and the subject was dropped indefinitely", "context_len": 61}}}, "electric": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 41, "context": "They turned somersaults from the decks of the canal-boats ; some of the boys could turn double somersaults , and one boy got so far as to turn a somersault and a half ; it was long before the time of electric lighting , but when he struck the water there came a flash that seemed to illumine the universe", "context_len": 60}}}, "agree": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 31, "context": "When Claudia shortly afterwards left the room , he said to the countess , Excuse me for breaking in , Agatha , but I felt that it was much better to agree with her , and not to make overmuch of the matter ; she is just of an age to make some one a hero , and she could hardly have chosen a better subject for her worship", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "You \u2019 re the first person I \u2019 ve ever told the whole story to , and I suppose , now that you know just the sort of fellow I am , you \u2019 ll agree with Hayden that I \u2019 m no fit associate for other boys at the academy", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "I have been amused at -- -- s little criticisms , and shall like to know how far yours agree with hers , if you read my Tragedy", "context_len": 28}}}, "relations": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 43, "context": "Miss Jones , the village dressmaker , was installed in the Rose Parlour , and appeared to be stitching morning , noon , and night ; the postman 's bag was quite heavy with patterns and catalogues of Indian outfits ; while distant relations and old acquaintances , who had neglected the Abbey for years , kept Nancy in a state of perpetual agitation by turning up suddenly to pay congratulatory calls", "context_len": 72}}}, "overjoyed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "He had never before , in all his life , been in possession of so much money , and , overjoyed , ran to tell his mother", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The visitors had won , and they were being congratulated by their overjoyed admirers", "context_len": 14}}}, "suitcases": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "So they picked up their suitcases with plenty of Sadie Sneeze \u2019 s best frocks , and plenty of Charlie Cough \u2019 s extra neckties , and lots of Hatty Headache \u2019 s hair ribbons and Susy Sore Throat \u2019 s collars and off they went", "context_len": 46}}}, "rudeness": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "Horner announced , `` Clara Adams wishes to make a public acknowledgment of her part in the rudeness directed against Miss Newman by the drawing you all saw on the board , and she will also make a public apology both to her teacher and to my niece", "context_len": 48}}}, "unkindness": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 52, "context": "Indeed , so delighted was he at his escape from death , that he was inclined to regard every one with favor ! Even George Harwood , who a few days after his return to the camp , came to him , according to his father 's instructions , to confess his unkindness and to ask pardon for the pain he had caused him", "context_len": 64}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "If you love them , they love you ! They are very sensitive , both to kindness and to unkindness", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "But we must ask Him from our hearts ; and can we do so if those hearts are full of unkindness and hard feeling towards those who have injured us ? I know how hard it is for you both , my darlings ; I know by my own feelings how hard it is to forgive Lem and Dolly ; but I can not hope to be forgiven myself for what I have done wrong this day , unless I forgive them the harm they have done to me", "context_len": 89}}}, "assistants": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 71, "context": "Miss Forster was not sorry to find so plausible an excuse for keeping her pet nephew at the Willows , and the young man himself had no objections , being fuller than ever of ingenious schemes , only he had transferred the seat of his operations to the Abbey , as offering a wider scope in the way of material , and having the further advantage of a number of appreciative assistants", "context_len": 72}}}, "wands": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "From the roofs were dangling on the inside heterogeneous emblems of peace and trophies of war , as eagles ' feathers , swans ' wings , wooden scalping knives , war clubs , red-painted wands , bunches of hoops on which to dry their scalps , bundles of a war-physic called snake-root ( s\u00ednika in Cheroki ) , baskets , etc", "context_len": 61}}}, "shamrock": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "It is said , daddy told the children , that the shamrock keeps away the snakes which St", "context_len": 18}}}, "thrive": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "Sweet Mother says that it suits her very well , and I thrive on it like anything , but not every one might be the better for water , water everywhere", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` See how the Chinese thrive on it", "context_len": 8}}}, "legend": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 40, "context": "The author dedicates this tale to his brother , engaged , like himself , in that most responsible task , the education of youth , in memory of those happy days when they pored together in rapturous delight over old legend or romantic lore in their father 's home at that very Clifton ( now Clifton Hampden ) familiar to hearers or readers of the tale as the home of Herstan , and the scene of the heroic defence of the English dwelling against the Danes", "context_len": 86}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` The Hellenic , especially Doric tradition of an immigration from Thrace and Macedonia through Epirus and Thessalia into Greece is confirmed by linguistic and historic facts , but the Roman legend concerning the descent of the founders of the `` Eternal City '' from Troy was acknowledged to be a pious fraud by the ancients themselves", "context_len": 57}}}, "footprints": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 4, "context": "Fenn was looking for footprints in the soft ground , but he and his chums had made so many on their own account , on their previous visits to the place , and , as they were still visible ( for the ground had not frozen ) , the amateur detective was at a loss", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "FOOTPRINTS IN THE SNOW", "context_len": 4}}}, "harbor": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 15, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Tonica and the recently-discovered Taensa furnish the proof that the Gulf States may have harbored , or still harbor , allophylic tribes speaking languages unknown to us", "context_len": 27}}}, "chieftain": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 95, "context": "`` So he broke away from the sorcerers and plunged the enchanted one into the boiling cauldron , unheeding the agony he suffered or the cries of the little animal he held , and in a moment the moon plunged beneath the horizon ; Mahdrusa arose from her long sleep ; the sorcerers fell into the boiling cauldron and were consumed ; and all the people shouted for joy , and with one accord cried that the Ohoolees should from henceforth be their brothers , and the young brave who had rescued Mahdrusa , their chieftain , when her father was called to the happy hunting grounds", "context_len": 107}}}, "sword": {"freq": 106, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 106, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` Then she told her mother the queen all that had betided her that night and how they had taken her bridegroom , leaving her alone , and how after a little there came another youth and lay down in the place of her bridegroom , putting a sword between himself and her ; `` and in the morning '' [ quoth she ] `` he who had brought us thither returned and taking us up , carried us back to our place here : and hardly had he brought us hither and left us when my father the Sultan entered and I had neither heart nor tongue to answer him for stress of fright and trembling which possessed me", "context_len": 120}}}, "deed": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` What evil , '' asked Wesley , `` have I done ? Which of you all have I wronged by word or deed ? '' Then he began to pray ; and one of the ringleaders said to him : --", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 74, "word_index": 109, "context": "younger , extreme carelessness had been her besetting fault , and , as is the manner of this `` little fox , '' had created much trouble for herself and for others ; but having become convinced that it was her duty to cure herself of this , she had set to work to do it in such earnest that that which had been a burden and a care to her was fast becoming a settled habit , and it was but seldom now that any act or word of heedlessness could be laid to her charge , while her ever obliging disposition and loving heart prompted many a deed of kindness which she never failed to carry out if it were in her power to do so", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "When it was full , his mother looked at the dish , but could not gaze fixedly thereon , for the radiance of the jewels and their lustre and the excess of their flashing ; so she shut her eyes and her wit was confounded at them ; yet was she not certified that their value was in very deed so great as her son had said , but bethought her that his speech might be true in that their like was not found with kings", "context_len": 86}}}, "idol": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 20, "context": "Folks had said that the Rector , like many a man who marries late in life , had made an idol of his motherless boy , and they had said , again , that the father 's heart was broken and the print of death was on his face as he stood by his child 's open grave", "context_len": 58}}}, "worshipers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "His temple was in this Oasis , and it was surrounded by a considerable population , which was supported , in a great degree , by the expenditures of the worshipers who came as pilgrims , or otherwise , to sacrifice at his shrine", "context_len": 44}}}, "worshiping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Patrick went into the place where these people were and found them worshiping the idols", "context_len": 15}}}, "idols": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 109, "context": "as it might be here '' -- arranging a tankard at the corner of the table -- '' with a good high road leading up to it from the sea , as it might be my tobacco pipe '' -- laying it down with care ; `` and if you 'll believe me , sir , we took and run races , as it might be along my tobacco pipe , and as soon as them Spaniards was 'ware of our coming , they took and ran out by 'tother gate , and left the town empty ! There was seven churches all chock full of gold and silver idols and candle-sticks , and such like : 't was just who 'd fill his pockets fastest ! ''", "context_len": 128}}}, "pillar": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I mean Lot 's wife after she 'came a pillar of salt , and then maybe she 'd be all soaked away in this pouring rain , and no more left of her to come back again and bother us", "context_len": 41}}}, "slabs": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 25, "context": "When there were other boys with him he would stop at the door of the shed , where a large , fair German was sawing slabs of marble with a long saw that had no teeth , and that he eased every now and then with water from a sponge he kept by him ; but if the boy was alone , and it was getting at all late in the afternoon , he always ran by the place as fast as he could", "context_len": 84}}}, "crozier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Patrick smote the chief idol with his crozier and the idol crumbled to dust at once", "context_len": 16}}}, "crumbled": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 82, "context": "What patient fingers , she wondered , had toiled over these in days gone by , working with paint-pots and palette of gold to put the glory of paradise on his pages ? Had the world altered much in all these years ? And how little did the old artist think that his work would be found and marvelled at when he and his order were alike forgotten , and the very Abbey where he had lived and laboured had long since crumbled away !", "context_len": 85}}}, "sixteen": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then ( says our author ) , the geomant proceeds to examine the sixteen figures thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 37, "context": "Numbers of the workmen were killed , but the erection of the battery was pushed on night and day , and ere long three of the immense cannon that had been brought from Constantinople , -- where sixteen of them had been cast under the direction of Maitre Georges -- were placed in position", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Is there one among us under the age of twenty -- and I might go farther -- who has already won such credit for himself ? One who when but sixteen can make his mark in an Order like ours is certain to rise to high office , and you and I may , before many years are over , be proud to serve under him", "context_len": 66}}}, "pirates": {"freq": 169, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 167, "word_index": 10, "context": "Should the worst happen , and you are captured by pirates , you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "A desert island is a most interesting place and adventures are most exciting , but pirates chasing up a green slope to your cave , waving swords and shouting and evidently intending to search for you , seems almost too dangerous", "context_len": 41}}}, "slavery": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 84, "context": "He declared that he was absolutely unable to give any names whatever of those concerned in the plot , save those of the two overseers , as these had undertaken the work of suborning the warders and guards , though he admitted that he had on several occasions spoken to slaves as the gangs were on their way back to the prison , and had told them to be prepared to take part in a plan that was on foot for their rescue from slavery", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He cut the garrison to pieces , and sold the inhabitants to slavery", "context_len": 13}}}, "ordained": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "At first the offer of his services was declined , but later on it was accepted ; and on 30th September , 1816 , he was ordained at Surrey Chapel", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` And , Dick , if it be so ordained , and the people of England do justice on their king , thou shalt stand by my side , and share in my service", "context_len": 34}}}, "deacon": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "How the old deacon would stare if he knew how I had been prospering at the mines ! I wish there was any way of sending part of this money home", "context_len": 31}}}, "pope": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Pope ! '' said Bart , `` you do n't look as if you had an enemy in the world", "context_len": 20}}}, "preach": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 60, "context": "After this Lilian determined to provide more generously , and astonished the grocer by ordering three pounds of cayenne pepper , and a like quantity of mace ( embarrassing possessions which lingered in the spice-cupboard for years before they were eventually finished ) , and generally running the household on so liberal a scale that Father had to interfere and preach economy", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "After we had arrayed the Etheling and Alfgar this morning , I decided to accompany them on their road to Dorchester , for it happened that I had arranged to say mass and preach tomorrow at the little church of St", "context_len": 41}}}, "gospel": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "This was quite a new gospel to Peggy , and though she could not altogether reconcile it with her clinging love for the home of so many generations of Vaughans , it did her good in that it gave her a fresh aspect of life , for it is always wise to look at things from another person 's point of view , as well as your own , and she had a great respect for Archie 's opinions", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He also was aware of the fact , but in time he became firmly convinced that his statement was gospel truth", "context_len": 21}}}, "followers": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 26, "context": "But , notwithstanding that peace had been restored , they were all glad when the chief took up his presents and went back to his motley followers , and even more so , when they put their horses to their utmost speed , and returned to their lodges ; where no doubt they gave to their tribe an astounding account of the adventure of their chief in the camp of the white man", "context_len": 73}}}, "drum": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 44, "context": "' Being well provided with pocket-money , and liking to do things in style , Archie took reserved seats , and they sat in great state on a front bench covered with faded scarlet cloth , and felt proudly that the man with the drum kept his eye upon them , and the clown turned in their direction when he let off his best jokes", "context_len": 65}}}, "knocked": {"freq": 122, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 122, "word_index": 55, "context": "A great many points were always coming up : whether a boy took-up or edged beyond the very place where his toy lay when he shot ; whether he knuckled down , or kept his hand on the ground in shooting ; whether , when another boy 's toy drove one marble against another and knocked both out of the ring , he holloed `` Fen doubs ! '' before the other fellow holloed `` Doubs ! '' whether a marble was in or out of the ring , and whether the umpire 's decision was just or not", "context_len": 99}}}, "relic": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 35, "context": "There was a large oak staircase , with wide , shallow steps , leading to a panelled gallery , where hung swords , and rusty armour , and moth-eaten tapestry , and many an ancient relic of the past ; while in the best bedroom was a great carved four-post bed , hung with faded yellow curtains , where Queen Elizabeth herself was said to have slept in much state for two nights on her journey from Shrewsbury to Wrexham", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "This secretary was an object of great interest to all the children , not because it held papa 's private papers , -- those were trifles of very little account in their eyes , -- but because it contained many a relic and treasure , remembrances of bygone days , or which were in themselves odd and curious", "context_len": 58}}}, "chained": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 51, "context": "They were good places for the fever-and-ague which people had in those days without knowing it was malaria , or suffering it to interfere much with the pleasure and business of life ; but they seemed to my boy bowers of delight , especially one where there was a bear , chained to a weeping-willow , and another where there was a fishpond with gold-fish in it", "context_len": 67}}}, "serpent": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 30, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}}}, "dialect": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 71, "context": "It was exceedingly interesting to the American contingent of the party to gain a little insight into the life of the English navvies ; and they passed the little tap-room , reeking with smoke and smelling of pipes and beer mugs , rather more often than circumstances would warrant , for the sake of looking in on the jolly fellows , and catching a sentence or so of their almost unintelligible dialect", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "In the broad Dorset dialect their leader recounted the various successful runs they had made , as if vainglorious of their deeds , and finished by demanding : `` Be there any of ye as be not content with his share ? ''", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Like the town Apalachi , the inhabitants of this town spoke a dialect resembling Hitchiti very closely", "context_len": 17}}}, "tombstone": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 46, "context": "There 's a fine place round by the old abbot 's house , where the ground is soft , and we could dig easy ; and I 've a cousin in the slate-quarries at Bethogwen as has promised many a time to cut me a little tombstone as a present , if ever I was wantin ' one , so I 'll ask him to bring it next time he comes , and put Rollo 's name on it , and the day , and as how he died defendin ' you ; and we 'll fix it up nice , and plant flowers round , just as if it was the churchyard", "context_len": 113}}}, "mender": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "I think I shall tell you the story of the talking that went on one day in a clock shop when the old clock mender had gone to his lunch , said daddy", "context_len": 33}}}, "grabs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "And then he grabs a newspaper and sits down and reads", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "Number one proved to be the 'Wild Man from Borneo , ' a half-human looking creature with hairy arms ( 'Just monkey-skin stitched on to him , ' Archie assured the children ) , who sat jabbering in a corner of his cage , making occasional ape-like grabs at the clothing of the passers-by", "context_len": 54}}}, "reads": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 25, "context": "knows it is foolish to think much about dreams , because they are not a bit of consequence , and she hopes any one who reads this will not think she was so foolish as to believe any thing about it ; but it did make her feel a little glad about it , and B", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "Delightedly one reads of Otto and the white mice ; Lena and the parrot , the wild man of the circus , and Sonny Boy 's ambition to command the Poppleton Guards , but Miss Swett tells the story , and when that is said , nothing remains but to enjoy the book", "context_len": 53}}}, "mainspring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "You may be a fine Clock , \u2019 said the little Watch with the broken mainspring , but I don \u2019 t see as it does you any good", "context_len": 29}}}, "weep": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 36, "context": "So much for Alaeddin and the Lady Bedrulbudour ; and as for the Sultan , after he had released Alaeddin , he ceased not to mourn for the loss of his daughter and to sit and weep for her , like a woman , at every time and tide ; for that she was his only one and he had none other than her", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 65, "context": "O misery ! to wake in darkness , wake From vision of unutterable joy ; Instead of trumpet-sound and song of Heaven , To hear the dull clock measuring out time , When I had seemed to touch Eternity ! In the first pang of disappointed hope , I wept that I could wake from such a dream ; Until Faith gently whispered , Wherefore weep To lose the faint dim shadow of a joy Of which the substance shall one day be thine ? Live in the hope , -- that hope shall brighten life , And sanctify it to its highest end", "context_len": 104}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "And that is just the sacrifice which some of our brethren have to make ! No wonder that they hesitate , weep , shrink from the flood of sorrow before them ; but the true-hearted ones make the plunge at last", "context_len": 41}}}, "upsets": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "And then when we feel badly it upsets our works and we have to be brought to the clock mender \u2019 s shop", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Every time he turns around or takes a long breath , he breaks a dish or upsets something", "context_len": 18}}}, "masters": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 98, "context": "`` Ask your pardon , sir ; my tongue runs away with me sometimes , and thinking of Solomon made me say it , and 't is all in the Bible , sir , now is n't it ? But to go back to what I was a saying , you know well , sir , as no one would follow a chap like me as captain , but if we could get a real gentleman , and one used to command to lead us -- why , hang me , sir , if we would n't be masters of St", "context_len": 101}}}, "cracker": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 8, "context": "They gave the elephants apples and bits of cracker and cake , and some tried to put tobacco into their trunks ; though they knew very well that it was nearly certain death to do so ; for any elephant that was deceived that way would recognize the boy that did it , and kill him the next time he came , if it was twenty years afterwards", "context_len": 68}}}, "seed": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 45, "context": "Do you go over to our house , and ask John to give you some turnip-seed , and sow it on that ground you burned over when you was roasting Joe Griffin , and see what turnips will grow there ; you can hack the seed in with the hoe ; turnips will sell first rate in the West Indies ; I \u2019 ll tell them they are Yankee yams", "context_len": 70}}}, "ceilings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "So even the new part of the house had square rooms destitute of ornament , and the papers were small in pattern and without any artistic designs , and the windows were square and straight , and the ceilings were somewhat low", "context_len": 42}}}, "seasoning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "And as the Sugar Bowl can \u2019 t go walking around looking for nice things to do at least it can admire the Salt Cellar and the Pepper Shaker for the work they do in seasoning and making things have a good taste", "context_len": 43}}}, "sugary": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "But Lilian had kept her surprise in the background , and it was only when all was ready that she opened a basket , and proudly drew out her masterpiece , a substantial-looking cake , with a cut-paper frill , and white icing on the top , on which in pink sugary letters were inscribed the words : 'Success to Sky Cottage ! ' Certainly some of the capitals were a little staggery , and the ' y ' had strayed into the pink border round the edge , but it was felt to be a triumph of culinary art all the same , and gave quite a grace to the table", "context_len": 112}}}, "sneeze": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "The Pepper Shaker would tell you , too , how much he thinks of you , but if he comes about too much he is apt to make people sneeze", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "How different from the irritating city dust that made me sneeze and cough", "context_len": 13}}}, "sneezing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "From the faded cushions a cloud of dust arose , and set Tom to sneezing so hard that the old chair creaked and rattled , as if it would fall apart", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "One little girl who had sneezed so hard and so much lost her voice for several days , for the sneezing powder got down in her throat , and her throat has never been quite so strong since", "context_len": 38}}}, "powder": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 8, "context": "The captain will then light the train of powder in the hold , throw the torch among the straw , and see that , if possible , the men fasten her to the Turk ; but if this can not be done , it is not essential , for in the confusion the enemy will not be able to get out of the way of the fire ship as it drives down against her", "context_len": 74}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Just at first they almost gibbered because they did not know what to say , but when they were taken below and allowed to wash the smoke and powder off their faces and hands , and then were given cups of tea and muffins and raspberry jam , and then were shown all over the beautiful ship , they could not help but begin to be calm", "context_len": 67}}}, "sickish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "So I think we should all be used in our places and not too much of any of us , for if too much sugar is used things will be sickish and if too much pepper or salt is used the poor pepper and salt creatures are taken a mean advantage of !", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I felt sort of sickish , right away", "context_len": 8}}}, "inclosed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "The tough little pony was wrapped about by his blanket , the master inclosed his iron body in another , or partly in the same one , lay down and slept , with never a dream to disturb his rest", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "He chained many of the ships together , two and two , at a little distance apart , covering the inclosed space with a platform , on which the soldiers could stand to fight", "context_len": 34}}}, "goodies": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` Mother said I might , and oh , Uncle Justus , she knew Aunt Elizabeth would be away and she thought maybe you and I would like some of our Thanksgiving , so she has sent some of her goodies , and we 're going to have a lovely time", "context_len": 51}}}, "tunnels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "In the Sun Parlor were tunnels which led down into the cellar , and the birds used to love going through these dark passages into the Goody-shop \u2019 as it was called in Bird language and it sounded the same to them as it would to you in Boy-and-Girl language", "context_len": 50}}}, "automobile": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 15, "context": "For five days the gasoline motor had whirled the boys in every direction , an automobile rushing them around the city , with side trips to Coney Island , north as far as Tarrytown , and across the river as far as Fort Lee , while a power boat had given them a view of the bay and the sound", "context_len": 60}}}, "rudest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I know I seem like the rudest sort of an old thing", "context_len": 12}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` And what if I had , you wicked boy -- for you are about the wickedest and rudest boy I have ever come across ? Much do I care ! I wanted her to go , and I thought that would be a good way to get rid of her", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The Christmas holidays being over , and Archie in the rudest of health , Miss Forster had no more excuse for keeping that young gentleman at home , and after much packing and preparation , he departed to school , amid the fond regrets of his aunt and the lamentations of his friends at the Abbey , to whom he promised to write as often as time and the regulations of his house would allow him", "context_len": 76}}}, "scare": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 24, "context": "He had hung the great dinner-bell in the cherry-tree , and fastened a string from it to his bed-post , so that he might scare the birds from the fruit in the early morning ; but unfortunately he had flung out his arms in his sleep and set the bell ringing soon after midnight , bringing the neighbours hurrying up to the Willows , thinking it was an alarm of fire", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "From various sophomores who came into their room to exchange notes , Tom , Phil and Sid learned that the freshmen had executed a well-organized fire scare by the simple process of burning in each corridor some of the powder extensively used on Fourth of July , or in political parades", "context_len": 51}}}, "shame": {"freq": 94, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 87, "word_index": 77, "context": "`` For the sake of him who hath even now rendered up himself as a martyr for the Lord 's cause , I may not be angry with any word of yours , '' he answered sadly ; `` but I do entreat of you to take heed ! Would you lay down your arms and live in peace among your cattle and your corn , coached and complimented into effeminacy and foolishness ? Oh , for shame ! Rub your eyes and look about you ! What was the fate of the men of Sodom when they thought Lot was one that mocked when he warned them ! ''", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was an awful moment , and in his hot and bitter heart the boy , who was put to shame before all his fellows , did not know whether to order them to attack his grandfather in a body , or to engage him in single combat with his own lath-sword", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "To get a bite was so much clear gain , and when they had wheedled one from the owner of the bread , they took as large a bite as their mouths could stretch to , and they had neither shame nor regret for their behavior , but mocked his just resentment", "context_len": 52}}}, "automobiles": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then the carriages and automobiles began coming up and the children were whirled away , though in several cases the mothers who had come for them remained to speak to Miss Eloise , and one or two remained in earnest conversation with Mrs", "context_len": 43}}}, "hurts": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Oh , no ! '' said Bessie , `` I did not mean that , only you have such a bad cold , and it hurts you so to move , and these rocks are so hard , I should think you 'd be more comfortable in your bed at home", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "But little notice was taken of them at first , for the members of the train were too busy attending to the wounded , and examining their own hurts , to think of Indians , unless it was to look occasionally to satisfy themselves that they were really gone , and that there was no farther trouble to be apprehended from them", "context_len": 62}}}, "horn": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 54, "context": "Dinner at length being over ( though later than usual , on account of the time occupied in baking the pie , and later , still , by reason of the goodness of it ) , they prepared to start , taking with them an axe to build a camp , tinder in a horn , flint , steel , and matches , which were made by dipping splinters of wood into melted brimstone , and which would burn when touched to the spark in the tinder", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 55, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}}}, "tooted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "They don \u2019 t know what it means to have a horn tooted at them when they feel they can not run", "context_len": 22}}}, "star": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 31, "context": "And when she stepped over each one , it went right back to its place in the sky ; but it left a long light behind it , like the shooting star we saw the other night ; and at the top of the stair of stars was a soft , white cloud ; and when Dolly came to it , a hand came out of the cloud , and took her in , and we knew she was quite safe , and would never come back again", "context_len": 88}}}, "modern": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 107, "context": "asked Alaeddin , `` What is the cause that the lattice of yonder kiosk ( kushk ) is not complete ? '' The defective part is soon after referred to , no less than four times , as `` the lattice of the kiosk '' ( sheriyyetu ' l kushk ) , thus showing that , in the writer 's mind , kushk , liwan and shubbak were synonymous terms for the common Arab projecting square-sided window , made of latticework , and I have therefore rendered the three words , when they occur in this sense , by our English `` oriel , '' to whose modern meaning ( a window that juts out , so as to form a small apartment ) , they exactly correspond", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "This word is not sufficiently rendered by `` education , '' which modern use has practically restricted to scholastic teaching , though the good old English phrase `` to bring up '' is of course a literal translation of the Latin educare", "context_len": 42}}}, "arbutus": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "We mustn \u2019 t waste any more time , whispered one of the trailing arbutus family to another", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "Of course you are , dear little violets , said the arbutus and hepatica families together", "context_len": 16}}}, "disappoint": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 40, "context": "She only knew that her heart was beating wildly , and she was thinking all the time of that cablegram which would comfort her father when he reached Malta , and resolving as surely girl never resolved before not to disappoint him , to give him if she could , if it were any way within her power , that supreme pleasure", "context_len": 62}}}, "collie": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "He was growing clever with the sheep , too , and Father hoped to be able to train him into a really valuable collie , even hinting that he might in time gain a prize at the annual sheep-dog contests which were held for the district of Gorswen and the Welsh border", "context_len": 52}}}, "rang": {"freq": 107, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 17, "context": "In the Boy 's Town the crier carried a good-sized bell ; when he started out he rang it till he reached the street corner , and then he stopped , and began some such proclamation as , `` O , yes ! O , yes ! O , yes ! There will be an auction this evening at early candle-light , at Brown & Robinson 's store ! Dry goods , boots and shoes , hats and caps , hardware , queen 's ware , and so forth , and so forth", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 53, "context": "Sometimes the schoolboys ventured inside of the flour-mill and the iron-foundry , but I do not think this was often permitted ; and , after all , the great thing was to rush over to the river-bank , all the boys and girls together , and play with the flutter-mills till the bell rang", "context_len": 54}}}, "misses": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "Why I even know when one of you misses a lesson , and if you will let me tell you a secret , I actually cried the day Clara Adams did the caricature", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "They are generally left to the `` young misses '' and `` gents '' who live in mortal fear of being found out to be what they feel themselves to be", "context_len": 31}}}, "hears": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 19, "context": "I shall tell his aunt so the next time I see her , and I do n't care who hears me ! ' she added , catching sight of a grinning face on the veranda , for Archie had stolen out to see the fun , and overflowed in such gurgles of delight at this sally that Lilian trembled for the result", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Oh , he comes of a fine family ! And he has the gall to come here among decent fellows to try to attend the academy here ! Wait till my father hears of this ! He \u2019 ll have something to say about it", "context_len": 45}}}, "barks": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "She works in her garden a great deal but she never misses a telephone call because Rogue hears it , barks for her , and leads her in before the telephone", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "He ran after the children with little short , sharp barks , worrying at their heels till Aunt Helen declared there would soon not be a whole stocking left in the establishment ; he had a fray at once with Jack , but was much worsted by that worthy , returning to Peggy for protection with his scrap of a tail between his legs ; he tore Bobby 's cricket-ball to pieces , licked the polish off Father 's boots , devoured Aunt Helen 's goloshes , and nestled cosily down to sleep on the top of Nancy 's best Sunday hat , which that luckless damsel had imprudently left in an open band-box on the kitchen settle", "context_len": 118}}}, "porcelain": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Phil placed the mudturtle in the wash basin , where it vainly tried to climb up the slippery porcelain sides", "context_len": 20}}}, "spilled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 52, "context": "I have heard people in New England blame all the Western rivers for being yellow and turbid ; but I know that after the spring floods , when the Miami had settled down to its summer business with the boys , it was as clear and as blue as if it were spilled out of the summer sky", "context_len": 58}}}, "interrupt": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Don \u2019 t interrupt , said the kettle", "context_len": 8}, "VERB": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 65, "context": "Well , to be quite honest , Marie-Celeste , for the half-truths of conventional acquaintance did not enter into this friendship , I think I might ; I 'm nothing of a hand at calling , you know , but I 'm awfully glad , I can tell you , to have met you just in this way , only you must n't let me interrupt you", "context_len": 67}}}, "burns": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` Yet you are scarcely seen , '' replied his companion , `` and 't is right your flame should be dull , for the cruelty you showed toward the poor widow , I caught my light from a rich man 's fire and injured no one , and that is how my lamp burns brighter than yours", "context_len": 58}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` But , Aunt Susan , '' interrupted Lucy , `` do you really approve of a girl who burns the candle at both ends ? It so happens that I was obliged to invade Rosamund 's room last night , and I heard her reciting poetry in two voices , and then I heard her throw her voice into a distant part of the room , so that you might almost imagine that she was a ventriloquist", "context_len": 78}}}, "comforted": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Then [ 658 ] after he had comforted the princess and promised her all she sought , he went straight to his closet and taking the lamp rubbed it ; whereupon the Marid at once appeared and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt ; '' and Alaeddin , `` I will have thee bring me a Roc 's egg and hang it in the dome of the [ upper ] pavilion", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Two comforted children sat in the midst of delights", "context_len": 9}}}, "measles": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 27, "context": "First she had gone to the office to report to him and let him see what the seaside had done for her , then the episode of measles had kept her indoors as well as the babies", "context_len": 37}}}, "goblins": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and sent such cold shivers running down their backs that they grew almost too frightened", "context_len": 128}}}, "mumps": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "He would get many of his goblins right to work to keep an eye on them , for goblins can not get measles , mumps , chicken-pox or croup", "context_len": 29}}}, "crossly": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 12, "context": "Thus two very tiresome hours passed away , and Gus was very crossly protesting against staying any longer , when Guy motioned him very eagerly to be still , and with great triumph pointed to a number of animals that , one by one , very slowly and cautiously , were going down to the water to drink", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "You \u2019 re no better than I am , \u2019 said the Alligator crossly", "context_len": 14}}}, "croup": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "He would get many of his goblins right to work to keep an eye on them , for goblins can not get measles , mumps , chicken-pox or croup", "context_len": 29}}}, "disheartened": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "He fell dead , and his followers , disheartened by his fall and by the destruction of the bridge , at once abandoned their efforts , and rushed down to the foot of the breach", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "But though disheartened , Bomba did not wholly despair", "context_len": 9}}}, "exit": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "And the spring sent out the early Breezes and said : Whisper to the birds , the buds , the children that I \u2019 ve really , really come , and that winter has made his exit ! \u2019", "context_len": 39}}}, "absurdly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "Gad , but they 're lucky ! said Harold : and then he sent for his pony and started off for a long gallop , hoping thereby to get the better of certain absurdly jealous feelings that would not down at his bidding", "context_len": 43}}}, "blizzards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "And you won \u2019 t really send any more blizzards ? \u2019 asked Mr", "context_len": 14}}}, "morrow": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 43, "context": "The Sultan , of his love and affection for his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour , was wont , every day , when he awoke from his sleep , to open the window and look at her therefrom ; so he arose on the morrow , according to his wont , and opened his chamber-window , so he might see his daughter ; but [ 571 ] when he put out his head and looked for Alaeddin 's palace , he beheld nothing but a place swept [ and level ] , like as it was aforetime , and saw neither palace nor inhabitants ; [ 572 ] whereat amazement clad him and his wit was bewildered and he fell to rubbing his eyes , so haply they were", "context_len": 128}}}, "buds": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 17, "context": "How the boy loved this tree ! It was beautiful in the spring , with its red buds ; beautiful in summer , with its masses of dark-green foliage , and its refreshing shade ; but most beautiful of all in the autumn , with its crimson tints , relieved by the lighter colors of the surrounding trees", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "emptying": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 45, "context": "In a letter addressed by Commander Lemoyne d'Iberville to the Minister of the French Navy , dated from Bayogoulas , February 26th , 1700 , he states that an English fur-trader and Indian slave-jobber had just visited the Tonica , who are on a river emptying into the Mississippi , twenty leagues above the Taensa Indians , at some distance from the Chicasa , and 170 leagues from the Gulf of Mexico", "context_len": 72}}}, "undone": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "He had undone the clasp of his bernouse , and allowed it to fall to the ground as he addressed Hassan , and his long sword flashed in the moonlight as the corsair sprang forward", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "With characteristic caution and forethought the Huns left nothing undone to safeguard their proceedings ; hence , in the case of barges using the subterranean waterway , a corporal 's guard was placed upon each during the journey through the tunnel", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "All he had thought to do was undone by him ; and yet here he was , contented , happy and healthy in a realm of little duties", "context_len": 28}}}, "purring": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "On Willie 's knee was a Maltese kitten purring away contentedly ; but the moment she caught sight of Flossy , she sprang from her resting-place , and , scampering into a corner , put up her back , and began spitting and hissing in a very impolite manner", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "A welcome diversion came outside , however , in the shape of a beautiful white Persian kitten , who submitted to pettings with gracious condescension , arching her back and purring loudly", "context_len": 32}}}, "drinking": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 6, "context": "In those days the custom of drinking healths \u2019 had gained sway to an absurd and objectionable extent ; gentlemen being expected to respond to every toast , and not only to sip their wine , but very often to empty their glasses , under pain of giving serious offence", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Then she kissed [ 613 ] his cup and drinking it off , set it down and came up to him and kissed him on the cheek ; [ 614 ] whereat he was like to fly for joy and purposing to do even as she had done , raised the cup to his mouth and drank it all off , without looking if there were aught therein or not ; but no sooner had he done this than he turned over on his back , like a dead man , and the cup fell from his hand", "context_len": 99}}}, "pear": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "How do you feel ? asked the first apple blossom of a pear blossom on one of the other trees in the orchard", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then she offered her a piece of cake and a lovely pear , and afterward took her down to see the flower garden that was fairly rioting in beauty , and a flock of snowy white chickens , as well as some fine pigeons that circled around like swallows", "context_len": 49}}}, "warmer": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 8, "context": "When the days began to get a little warmer Gwen hung just outside the window-sill a little hammock and waited to see if the squirrels would dare to swing in it", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 16, "context": "Fortunately , the sun 's rays came down hot and bright , and the rock grew warmer , so that by degrees the terribly void look began to leave Mark Penelly 's face , and at last , when Harry held out his hand , saying , `` Do you feel better ? '' Mark Penelly caught it in both of his , clung to it , and , turning half over on his face , laid his forehead against it , and , forgetting his years of manhood , lay there in his weakness , and sobbed and cried like a child", "context_len": 103}}}, "annoyed": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Oh , he was very much annoyed , and all the squirrels stopped swinging in the hammock", "context_len": 17}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 11, "context": "Stanton , who saw that the poor lady had been greatly annoyed , and who wondered much at all the commotion he had seen in the nursery , though , like nurse , he thought it wisest to ask no questions , was doing his best to make her forget it ; and so well did he succeed , that presently Mrs", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "I used to keep a little collection of them in my handkerchief , and generally popped one on my plate ; and he used always to say 'Oh ! ' and he would generally drop and break the plate , which was a valuable china one , and mother was quite annoyed", "context_len": 52}}}, "managing": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "This he had no objection to do , and he spent nearly his whole time in practising the use of battle-axe , of bow , of spear , of sword , and shield , or in managing the war horse , for the Danes had acquired cavalry tactics on stolen horses", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "He produced a pouch , and Bill Mosely , much against his will , was compelled to divide the contents of the stolen bag , managing , however , to retain the larger share himself", "context_len": 35}}}, "idling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "And I haven \u2019 t been idling my morning , I \u2019 ve been making a friend ; and I don \u2019 t want to learn to keep house ; -- anyway , Benita wouldn \u2019 t let me keep house if I could", "context_len": 44}}}, "laziest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Now , don \u2019 t you know you can \u2019 t spend all your time idling ! You are the laziest lot of squirrels I have ever known", "context_len": 28}}}, "sap": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 25, "context": "He would choose one of the roots near the foot of the tree , and chop a clean , square hole in it ; the sap flew at each stroke of his axe , and it rose so fast in the well he made that the thirstiest boy could not keep it down , and three or four boys , with their heads jammed tight together and their straws plunged into its depths , lay stretched upon their stomachs and drank their fill at once", "context_len": 85}}}, "scamper": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "With these long , awkward legs , he can scamper out of the way , and with those sharp claws , he fights , for he is a quarrelsome little fellow", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` They generally behave beautifully , '' Drusie said , who , because the other three were rather shy , was obliged to do all the talking herself ; `` but something must have startled Jumbo when we were at the top of the hill , for he set off at a tremendous scamper , and tumbled in headforemost before we knew what was happening to him", "context_len": 67}}}, "smacked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "They got all the sap they wanted and smacked their lips over it", "context_len": 13}}}, "keys": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 19, "context": "Mais n \u2019 importe ! the man , the man remains the same ! These locks have proved the keys to a Lady \u2019 s heart e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon", "context_len": 128}}}, "secrets": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` She is a queer dick , '' said Fred , `` pulling your hair , and tormenting you out of your life one time , and telling you secrets another", "context_len": 31}}}, "gentlemen": {"freq": 124, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 123, "word_index": 62, "context": "Their voices were subdued lest they should reach the king 's ears ; but , respectful as was the bearing of all the members of the group by the fire , they clearly split into two halves : on the one hand , the officers of the escort who were teasing the boy , and on the other , a group of gentlemen , some wearing the conventional ribbons and laces of a cavalier , others in the rough cloth of country wear , stained with the mud of country lanes , while the master and mistress of the house moved from one guest to another , evidently nervous at the doubtful honour that such a royal visit had brought to their roof", "context_len": 123}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I must say , '' remarked the pious-looking man , `` that you gentlemen were never more mistaken in your lives when you hint that there is no such person as Frank Leroy", "context_len": 34}}}, "guarding": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 58, "context": "The ferryman , whose hut is situate at the bend of the river below the Synodune hills , where people cross for Wittenham , says that late on the night in question a boat with four people passed down the river , and that it struck him that one only rowed , while two of the rest seemed guarding the fourth passenger", "context_len": 62}}}, "worms": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 18, "context": "The children had made a nest of grass for him inside a basket , and fed him on worms and scraps of raw meat until he was old enough to fly , when he would follow them everywhere about the farmyard and outbuildings , calling 'Jack , Jack ! ' which , with the mewing of a cat , the gobble of the old turkey-cock , and a close imitation of David 's winter cough , made up the extent of his accomplishments", "context_len": 83}}}, "markets": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 76, "context": "To those friends who thought he lost caste thereby he had always the same argument -- that he saw no reason why the cultivation of fields should counteract the habits of refinement and good breeding to which he had been reared ; that in the colonies educated gentlemen set to work to labour with their hands , and are thought none the worse of : so why not in England , where land is good and markets are plentiful , especially when it involved the keeping of a fine old property which had been in the family for so many hundreds of years ?", "context_len": 104}}}, "lawns": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 73, "context": "Perhaps it was because he was one of those rare characters in whose presence we can feel certain of perfect sympathy , or perhaps it grew from a more subtle and silent bond , felt keenly by the child , though never spoken of , for Peggy could remember a time when the Rector 's hair was raven black , and there had been a little Raymond Howell playing about on the smooth lawns of the old garden", "context_len": 78}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "grocery": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "But they don \u2019 t do that any more than we go to the grocery stores and tell the grocer to please do us up a package of potatoes and another of onions , and one more of meat which we \u2019 d order at the meat shop", "context_len": 48}}}, "grocer": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` I expected Pattens to send a man in , for I had met him once or twice , and he told me that his bill was not paid , and that he did not mean to supply any more meat , and what Pattens said the baker and greengrocer said too , and so did Allthorp the grocer , and so did the fishmonger , Merriman , and so did all the other tradespeople , and if one spoke to me , so did they all", "context_len": 87}}}, "divided": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 3, "context": "The tables are divided down the center by an imaginary line , each part being a `` table '' ; first class men sit near the head , and so on down to the plebes , who find themselves at the center ( that is , after they have moved into camp , and been `` sized '' and assigned to companies ; before that they are `` beasts , '' herded apart , as has been said )", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "As for Edna , she was so divided between her desire to be with her favorite cousin Ben and with Uncle Justus , of whom she was very fond , that she swung between her two desires like a pendulum till Ben caught her and pretended he was going to throw her overboard because she would not walk with him up to the house", "context_len": 64}}}, "bud": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 44, "context": "Meantime , Donald 's train made good time to Nuneham ; and there was Chris at the station waiting with open arms to receive him , and , what was more , he took Donald into them in a way that nipped in the bud those queer little misgivings that spring up unbidden when one chances to be leaving old scenes for new", "context_len": 63}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It seemed hard to Peggy to think that the trees would bud and the flowers blossom , and the crops grow , when they were not there to watch it all , for most of us have a kind of feeling that we are the important centre around which Nature turns , instead of only mere spectators of her varying moods , and sometimes it felt so impossible that such an utter upheaval in their lives could really come to pass that she would have to shake herself to believe that it was not all a bad dream ; but as she noticed the quiet preparations that went on , and the added worry on Father 's face , she realized that it was only too true ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "whispers": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was very quiet in the living-room , or did she hear whispers , and subdued titters ? Was Reliance or maybe Ben going to play a trick on her ? She heard a sudden `` Hush ! Hush ! '' as she reached the door of the living-room , but she made up her mind that she would appear perfectly unconcerned , and entered the room in a very don't-care sort of manner", "context_len": 74}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "And though the flower does not altogether understand , it partly does , for it whispers : Eat all of my honey and sip my sweetness , for you are my beautiful butterfly visitor ! \u2019", "context_len": 36}}}, "showers": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 53, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Sometimes when it showers I take an extra bath", "context_len": 9}}}, "bloom": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 15, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` 'Here the flow'rets bloom forever ; Here the sun of God doth shine ; Here doth flow the crystal river , Giving all a life divine ! ' Then the peerless vision faded , And the fairies stole away ; All the dell with gloom was shaded , Darkness 'round sweet Allie lay", "context_len": 54}}}, "waits": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Take my thanks , Harrison , '' he chuckled , as he led him downstairs ; `` the black dog is off his Highness 's back , and when he waits on his Majesty to-night , he will be worthy himself", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Alfgar sees the brute swimming after the boat ; he ceases to use the pole , but takes his sword , kneels on the stern of the boat , and waits for the mastiff", "context_len": 34}}}, "wastes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "The pioneer days were still so close to those times that the love of solitary adventure which took the boys ' fathers into the sylvan wastes of the great West might well have burned in the boys ' hearts ; and if their ideal of life was the free life of the woods , no doubt it was because their near ancestors had lived it", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "If we knew with accuracy the old Indian paths of the West , we would have little difficulty in rediscovering the routes traveled by Coronado 's and Pe\u00f1alossa 's troops in New Mexico and in the great wastes of the Mississippi plains", "context_len": 42}}}, "wasted": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 37, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 64, "context": "reason that she should spend it in doing all she could for her Master 's service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut her ear to the cry of the needy , because to answer it would cost a sacrifice of her own wishes ? Should she bear", "context_len": 128}}}, "runs": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 20, "context": "Dodgson no longer cares to see as much of the little people as formerly ; in fact , he rather runs away from them when they seek him out ; and when he received Dorothy 's letter , what did he do but write her that he was very sorry to say that he would not be at home on the afternoon in question , but that if it would be any pleasure to her little friends to see his rooms , she might bring them there and welcome , and that he would leave some old photographs that he thought would interest them ready to her hand in a portfolio on the writing-table", "context_len": 114}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 11, "context": "In the broad Dorset dialect their leader recounted the various successful runs they had made , as if vainglorious of their deeds , and finished by demanding : `` Be there any of ye as be not content with his share ? ''", "context_len": 43}}}, "senseless": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` 'And all the time , ' says he , 'their chatter was far more senseless than mine , and so they would think if they had heard all of us laugh over their conjectures about a matter we knew all about , for birds have legends as well as men , and there 's none better remembered than that of the `` Enchanted Yellow Men", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 40, "context": "At this the knight laid down a paper which I recognized as the one that had been read by him under such strange circumstances at Rake , some eight years before , and the light flashed across me -- the senseless jargon that he had read was part of a secret code whereby I should inherit my fortune", "context_len": 58}}}, "disagreed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "But Captain Perry , having carefully thought the whole thing out , stuck to his guns , refusing to budge an inch from his original arrangement , in response to the hints and insinuations of those who disagreed with him", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Joel , he used to eat about four heapin \u2019 plates of beans , an \u2019 then he \u2019 d complain because every little morsel he put into his stummick disagreed with him", "context_len": 33}}}, "watches": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 49, "context": "In the evenings , long after the others were in bed , she paced up and down the kitchen , spinning Deacon Croaker 's wool into smooth , even threads , but her heart ached as she prayed for her boy , and often , when in the still watches of the night Polly kept her vigils with pain , she heard her cry softly : --", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "He follows this up , watches a masked duelist and , with the help of a girl , catches the murderer who justifies his deed on the plea of Slow Vengeance", "context_len": 31}}}, "daytime": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "She couldn \u2019 t have my real name Billie , but she had every one call me that , so it \u2019 s my daytime every-day name , and my best , dress-up name is Mary Ann , or Marion or some such fine name after my mother", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 10, "context": "I don \u2019 t like to tell you in the daytime , because it makes me cry , and I don \u2019 t like to cry before folks ; but in the night , when we are in bed , I \u2019 ll tell you", "context_len": 45}}}, "springtime": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 62, "context": "It always seemed a pity , Peggy thought , to make the nice apples into such horrid stuff as cider , and she was glad David stored so many away in the loft for winter use , and in the meantime she and Bobby consumed such quantities that Father declared they would both be breaking out into leaves and blossom in the springtime", "context_len": 63}}}, "admires": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "I feel the soft , warm wind blow over my little pink cheeks which every one admires so much and I smile and I croon and I make soft little singing sounds as the trees do", "context_len": 36}}}, "croon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "I feel the soft , warm wind blow over my little pink cheeks which every one admires so much and I smile and I croon and I make soft little singing sounds as the trees do", "context_len": 36}}}, "forsythia": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "But now she took the babies out to the big park where she could sit and watch the merry children at play and the beds of flowers coming out , and there were the funny pussy willows and the long tails of yellow forsythia and some squirrels running around , and birds calling to each other", "context_len": 56}}}, "lilacs": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 56, "context": "Good-morning , Albert , answered Marie-Celeste , smiling at the expected , and now , with which , by way of getting the best of a tendency to stutter , Albert was accustomed to preface many of his remarks ; 1 thought I should find you here , she added ; and have you seen the lilacs , Albert ?", "context_len": 60}}}, "blossoms": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 38, "context": "Then what is that field compared to all England , or England to Europe , or Europe to the whole world ? Neither your little head , nor the wisest man \u2019 s , can imagine how many blossoms and how many insects there are on this great globe , -- it makes one almost giddy to think of it , -- and then to consider that all the world itself is only like a speck in God \u2019 s Creation , that there are said to be eighty millions of fixed stars , each of which has very likely worlds moving round it", "context_len": 104}}}, "waterfalls": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}}}, "gurgle": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "His eyes fairly popping from his head , David could only gurgle , while he tried to free himself from this attack", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Beyond an eerie gurgle ( `` the last gasp of a dying flounder '' , according to the above-quoted honorary reporter ) not a sound came from the cornet", "context_len": 29}}}, "dash": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 22, "context": "The galley may meet with four or five of our ships , and be captured , or a storm may arise and dash her upon the shore ; and though many would lose their lives , some might escape , and each man , in thinking of it , believes that he will be one of the fortunate ones", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 96, "context": "In her plan to give Joe a fright , she had never thought of Father , who returned by the same road ; and now that horrible white object was stationed just in the very danger-spot where a plunging horse might mean a matter of almost certain death , for the wall of the five-acre field abutted the road on one side , and on the other there was only a narrow patch of grass between the steep bank which shelved down sheer into the river , while the closed gate stopped any chance of a dash forward", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Even the damper of his idol 's displeasure could not change Master Edwards ' mind , however ; he and nearly the whole crowd with him made a dash down the shore for a vantage point to see the finish", "context_len": 40}}}, "kicking": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 95, "context": "Dey 're trained to behave jus ' of dere own selves without any driver , and when dey get out on de road dey always do behave ; and then in the moment 's pause that followed , Marie-Celeste , remembering certain recent performances of her own , wondered if her father wished that a certain little girl , of whom he had some knowledge , more closely resembled these royal ponies , who , once trained to behave , according to Albert , never dreamed of taking the bit in their teeth or of kicking over the traces", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 38, "context": "The two rolled over together , and Peggy clung trembling to the gatepost as she watched the confused heap at her feet , Rollo scratching , snarling , and biting like a wild beast , and the tramp kicking , fighting , and swearing in a way which made her blood go cold to hear", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` Where you fellows made a mistake , '' Sid was saying , as he moved about on the creaking old sofa to get into a more comfortable position , `` where you fellows made a mistake was in not doing more kicking early in the game", "context_len": 47}}}, "bathtub": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The wash-basin , Fairy Wondrous Secrets , is my bathtub , and I splash and kick and laugh and have such a good time ! I don \u2019 t care if the water spills over the floor any more than I imagine the waterfalls care that they spill water over the rocks", "context_len": 52}}}, "spills": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The wash-basin , Fairy Wondrous Secrets , is my bathtub , and I splash and kick and laugh and have such a good time ! I don \u2019 t care if the water spills over the floor any more than I imagine the waterfalls care that they spill water over the rocks", "context_len": 52}}}, "quack": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Betsy Owen was a withered , cross-grained old dame , who by dint of the knowledge of the uses of some simple herbs and a good deal of cunning , had contrived to establish a reputation something between a witch and a quack doctor", "context_len": 44}}}, "roosters": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "The chickens and the hens and the roosters walk about and chatter , and one day a lady passed and said Hello , \u2019 to a chicken and the chicken got up from the ground most politely as a mannerly person would do , I \u2019 m told", "context_len": 48}}}, "chatter": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 88, "context": "`` That big fellow on the bough there tells me he is delighted ; that he has at last found one that can understand his language , for he has heard so many ridiculous theories advanced by men with picks on their shoulders and books in their hands , as to what gold is , and how it came on the ground , that he has nearly burst his throat in trying to make them understand the truth , and has then been accused of making a 'senseless chatter", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "When , soon afterwards , nurse called Helen and Tommy to come to bed , Hal invited Drusie and Jim to come and sit with him while he had his tea , in order that he might chatter to them of his doings that afternoon , and about what he intended to do in future", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Its harsh , deadly chatter held the hopes of fourteen souls", "context_len": 11}}}, "mannerly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "The chickens and the hens and the roosters walk about and chatter , and one day a lady passed and said Hello , \u2019 to a chicken and the chicken got up from the ground most politely as a mannerly person would do , I \u2019 m told", "context_len": 48}}}, "turtle": {"freq": 85, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 80, "word_index": 46, "context": "There are terms which occur in all dialects in the same or nearly the same form , as h\u00e1si sun , \u00edtchu , \u00edssi deer , \u00f3fi , \u00edfa dog , the terms for chief , black , yellow , bird , snake , buffalo , turtle , fox ( also in Cheroki : tsu'hl\u00e1 ) , the numerals and the personal pronouns ; they must , therefore , have been once the common property of the still undivided , primordial tribe", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Of course not , '' agreed the lineman , he and Bart having talked in whispers while the turtle collector was exhibiting some odd specimens to Fenn", "context_len": 28}}}, "sunning": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "Then there was Pixie , the pony , who would come whinnying up from the further side of a field to poke her soft nose into the children 's pockets for pieces of bread or lumps of sugar ; there were numerous cats who lived in the barns and stables , and Tabbyskins , the stately gray Persian , who usually sat sunning herself on the pigsty wall , keeping a strict eye on naughty Jack , who was wont to harry her if he got the chance", "context_len": 88}}}, "crickets": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 66, "context": "it grew darker and darker , came forth another and another star , and looked down on the world which God had loved so much , till the whole sky was brilliant with them ; the soft , cool sea-breeze came gently in at the windows , bringing with it the gentle plash of the waves upon the shore , mingled with the chirp of the crickets and the distant hum of voices from the far end of the piazza ; but no one came near or disturbed them ; and still the colonel sat with his face turned towards the sea , without either speaking or moving , till his wife , as she sat with her hand in his , wondered if he could be asleep", "context_len": 128}}}, "rattled": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 21, "context": "He ascended the narrow , uncarpeted stairs to his small , uncarpeted room over the kitchen , where a loose board rattled beneath his feet , and the dull light from a single window showed him the old-fashioned , low-posted , corded bedstead with its straw tick , coarse sheets and patchwork quilt pushed back beneath the sloping rafters of the roof", "context_len": 62}}}, "wounds": {"freq": 73, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 37, "context": "Nicholas , you saved the life of the grand master ; and in the order of the day he issued on the morning we left , granting you three months ' leave for the recovery of your wounds , he took the opportunity of recording , in the name of the council and himself , their admiration for the services rendered by you during the siege , and his own gratitude for saving his life when he lay helpless and surrounded by the Moslems -- a testimony of which any knight of Christendom might well feel proud", "context_len": 97}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The stranger speaks smooth words , but who knows what black thoughts are in his heart ? And if he wounds but does not kill Japazy , what will Japazy do to us because we did not kill the stranger when he came among us ?", "context_len": 46}}}, "cots": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 0, "context": "Cots were provided , with plenty of blankets , so they would not suffer from cold , and as an additional precaution a small fire was kept going in the small , sheet-iron , wood-burning stove , which they had brought along to warm the sleeping tent", "context_len": 47}}}, "cot": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 32, "context": "There was no talk of calling Cap'n Si that morning , for he would not be looking for the flag to be run up , instead he was lying helpless on a cot , his hands swathed in cotton , and his stubbly beard singed by the fire he had vainly tried to put out", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "But the small bed was drawn up close to the window , and was more cot than bed , having iron bars all round it", "context_len": 25}}}, "saucy": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "They were particularly amused by the antics of one saucy rogue , who , not satisfied with the share which had fallen to him , crept under Bob 's arm , and actually began thrusting his nose into his pocket in search of more almonds", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 8, "context": "As for the squirrels , they were as saucy as possible , waiting and watching with their sharp , bright eyes till the travellers were close upon them , then gliding ahead to a short distance and looking back , or perhaps leaping from one to another of the old fallen trunks which lay by the roadside almost within arm 's length", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Then Bessie told how Miss Adams ' presence of mind had saved Franky from falling into the stream , `` And then we talked a little , '' she said , `` and I told her I was sorry I had been saucy , and kissed her , and so we are all made up", "context_len": 55}}}, "lunches": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "There 's pertaters to be fried , an ' the children 's lunches to put up , an ' John Alexander 's lost his jography -- I believe that boy 'd lose his head if it twar n't glued to his shoulders", "context_len": 42}}}, "scurry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He stooped , made a diving scurry under one of them , and was lost to view", "context_len": 17}}}, "tummy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Ah , now , I have had a fine meal , a luxurious meal , fit for a king no , fit for a bear ! And the bear rubbed his front paw over his well-filled tummy and again growled delightedly to himself some more , saying over and over again : Delicious ; such a meal !", "context_len": 58}}}, "traveling": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 56, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "The going at first was heavy , for it was through the woods , over a trail hard to discern , but when they struck the lumber road , leading into Cannistota , the traveling was easier", "context_len": 37}}}, "lasting": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "Report says that when he came to Rake to settle down , some twelve or thirteen years back -- I being but an infant in arms , -- he did gain a lasting reputation by outmatching one Caleb James , a notorious bully , at his own game , breaking his pate with his own staff on the roadside hard by Milland Church", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Winter drew on , and with it the cold was intense , a sharp frost lasting for over six weeks", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "The failure of power which had followed upon the death of Letitia , lasting more or less during some years , had now pretty well passed off ; and there seemed to be good promise of a healthy old age", "context_len": 40}}}, "replanted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "We don \u2019 t have to be replanted from seeds every spring", "context_len": 12}}}, "ah": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Where has the money come from ? From the coal ? Some , perhaps , yes ; but for all of the great house , ah , it can not be ! Every one has been saying there was not enough coal in her tract to pay for what she has done ; and new debts press , doubtless", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ah ! how different it all was now ! They were all gone , -- the youth , the love , the happiness ; gone , also , were the wasted years which she might have spent in the service of the Master whom she had sought so late ; gone all the opportunities which he had given her of gaining the love and friendship of her fellow-creatures", "context_len": 68}}}, "vine": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "They had stories to tell him about the famous hanging gardens , which were artificially constructed in the most magnificent manner , on arches raised high in the air ; and about a vine made of gold , with all sorts of precious stones upon it instead of fruit , which was wrought as an ornament over the throne on which the King of Persia often gave audience ; of the splendid palaces and vast cities of the Persians ; and the banquets , and f\u00eates , and magnificent entertainments and celebrations which they used to have there", "context_len": 98}}}, "fragrance": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 31, "context": "Nothing made the dreadful things she had often to encounter in town seem more endurable than the sweet-peas , the roses , the green trees , the green grass , the fragrance and perfume of the country ; and when she saw her little niece -- for she was very fond of Lucy -- looking discontented and unhappy , Mrs", "context_len": 60}}}, "indoors": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 44, "context": "They would follow a boy to his door , and wait for him to come out ; and they would sometimes get him to go in and ask his mother for crullers or sugar-cakes ; when they came to see him they never went indoors for him , but stood on the sidewalk and called him with a peculiar cry , something like `` E-oo-we , e-oo-we ! '' and threw stones at trees , or anything , till he came out", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Saturday was an indoors day spent with Serena , Virginia and the big , red book", "context_len": 16}}}, "flower": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 43, "context": "There had for a long time existed a tradition among them , that when a daughter of a chief -- an only child , -- should love a brave of an unfriendly tribe , they would have power to change her into a flower or animal , and unless the brave should find the means within ten moons , or months , to break the enchantment , she would die , and with her every chieftain and his family", "context_len": 79}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "I \u2019 ve been a long time coming to you , flower , but the hard crawling journey had a wonderful ending", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}}}, "bumblebees": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "They love me every bit as much as the bumblebees do", "context_len": 11}}}, "dears": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "And I love them too ! The little dears ! But I must tell you the reason why I come up each year , even though sometimes it seems so hard", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 39, "context": "Clavering , just as they were approaching the house , `` I see you are all a little nervous , thinking that a somewhat strange test will be applied to you to-day , but I assure you , my dears , that nothing of the kind is intended , and I beg of you , as you wish to impress your kind host favorably , to be at any cost natural and true to yourselves", "context_len": 75}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "But somehow it did n't appear so funny to her mother as she expected it would , for instead of laughing she gathered the three children to her and kissed them all three , murmuring , `` You dears", "context_len": 39}}}, "drinks": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "A youth of the proper age gathers two handfuls of the sow\u00e1tchko plant , which intoxicates and maddens , and eats this very bitter root for a whole day , after which he steeps the leaves in water and drinks from this", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "There \u2019 s no telling , continued he , what a man , who drinks ever so little , may come to , and how it may grow upon him ; but if he don \u2019 t drink at all he is safe", "context_len": 43}}}, "scowled": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "She did not look for any thing worse , as the consequence of this wickedness , than a whipping , or perhaps that he should be shut up for a few hours ; and , although she scowled angrily at her brother 's captors , she said nothing to them or to him , but turned and followed at a little distance", "context_len": 62}}}, "honeysuckle": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 83, "context": "It was a fine summer 's evening towards the end of the month of July , and the sun was slowly setting behind the wood-crowned range of hills in the west , where the forest terminated the pastures of Aescendune ; the cattle were returning to their stalls ; the last load of hay was being transferred from the wain to the rick , and all things spoke of the calm and rest of a sweet night , fragrant with the breath of honeysuckle and wild brier , when nature herself seems to court luxurious repose", "context_len": 96}}}, "wafted": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 14, "context": "Traces of poison gas lurked in the traverses , pungent fumes from bursting shells wafted over parapet and parados , while the report , passed on from various successive occupants of this section of the line , that a dozen dead Huns had been buried under the floor of the support trench -- the old first-line trench of a Prussian regiment -- seemed to find definite confirmation", "context_len": 67}}}, "lilac": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 17, "context": "I dashed at it , tried to explain why I wore a black dress when I had lilac and blue ones at Amritsar , told her that I had never been married , answered questions regarding my family , etc", "context_len": 40}}}, "crocus": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "When the snow leaves the ground the crocus flowers appear and take the lead in the parade", "context_len": 17}}}, "tossing": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 44, "context": "But alas ! I for one know to a Certainty that in the little castle there was rebellion almost amounting to mutiny , and that one curly , uncrowned head , that need not have had a care in all the world , was tossing uneasily on its pillow", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "Maggie and all the boys were half wild about him , and as for baby , if she could have spoken , she would have said that never was there such an uncle for jumping and tossing", "context_len": 37}}}, "hyacinths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Next follow the hyacinths , the tulips , daffodils , narcissus flowers , garden violets , pansies , and little daisies", "context_len": 21}}}, "tulips": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Very likely they were not so vast or so splendid as they looked to him then ; but one of them at least had beds of tulips and nasturtiums , and borders of flags and pinks , with clumps of tiger-lilies and hollyhocks ; and in the grassy yard beside it there were high bushes full of snow-balls , and rose-trees with moss-roses on them", "context_len": 65}}}, "narcissus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Next follow the hyacinths , the tulips , daffodils , narcissus flowers , garden violets , pansies , and little daisies", "context_len": 21}}}, "violets": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 57, "context": "He poked his nose into all the rabbit-burrows , he chased the birds , and dug holes for rats , and generally behaved as if he were a puppy again , instead of a sedate , grown-up dog , snapping at the flies , and standing over Peggy wagging his tail in approval , while she gathered violets and wood-anemones", "context_len": 60}}}, "pansies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": "' said Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner , and the oak dresser", "context_len": 128}}}, "daisies": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 60, "context": "' I suppose not , ' said Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner", "context_len": 128}}}, "flowering": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "They had the most delightful summer play-room ; one side of the verandah enclosed with a lattice work , covered with flowering vines , where they kept their bats and balls , graces , hoops , rocking horse and other toys", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "Children can not too soon begin to know the wild flowers , and here they are told in a charming way where and when to look for each of fifty widely distributed common flowering plants ; also how they get their names , and how to know them from the remarkably accurate drawings of Mr", "context_len": 55}}}, "almond": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "Bunny , either more hungry or less cautious than her mate and children , came whisking down the trunk of the walnut-tree , and in another moment was seated upon Bob 's shoulder , holding in her fore-paws the almond he had given her , and opening it with her sharp , pointed teeth", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He uttered a howl of anguish , while his almond eyes seemed starting out of their sockets as his steed dashed along the road", "context_len": 24}}}, "shrubs": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 47, "context": "It represented a considerable amount of skill and artistic taste on the part of himself and his handy-man , for the rare plants and exquisite groupings of contrasting blossoms would have done credit to a more imposing establishment , and he had as choice a collection of shrubs as could be grown anywhere in the county", "context_len": 56}}}, "parading": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` She is parading as a man , '' I said , `` and I think I shall treat her as one", "context_len": 22}}}, "marching": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 64, "context": "the boys always called them ; and the boys believed that they each had hay in his right shoe , and straw in his left , because a Dutchman was too dumb , as the boys said for stupid , to know his feet apart any other way ; and that the Dutch officers had to call out to the men when they were marching , `` Up mit de hay-foot , down mit de straw-foot -- links , links , links ! '' ( Left , left , left ! ) But the boys honored even these imperfect intelligences so much in their quality of soldiers that they would any of them have been proud to be marker in the Dutch company ; and they followed the", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 26, "context": "Many a time had they seen troops fully equipped proceeding towards the docks ; never since the outbreak of hostilities had they seen soldiers in heavy marching order tramping in column of fours away from the place of embarkation", "context_len": 39}}}, "rockets": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "To be sure , the catherine-wheel stuck , and utterly refused to turn , in spite of all pokings and proddings , and the rockets only fizzled off near the ground , instead of shooting up as they ought to have done ; but the squibs and crackers were quite effective , and a train of gunpowder , laid down to represent a fiery serpent , blazed away in fine style", "context_len": 71}}}, "peonies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "As she did so she passed Florence , whose cheeks were flushed like peonies , and who was bending in some despair over her paper , for Florence was well known in the school to be ignorant as regarded all matters connected with history , although she was smart enough in her own line", "context_len": 54}}}, "honeysuckles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The rockets , peonies , honeysuckles and roses all will follow along , making a very handsome part of the parade", "context_len": 21}}}, "phlox": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "And later on the phlox , larkspur so blue , and foxglove will follow", "context_len": 14}}}, "larkspur": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And later on the phlox , larkspur so blue , and foxglove will follow", "context_len": 14}}}, "foxglove": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "And later on the phlox , larkspur so blue , and foxglove will follow", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "'There was Vaughans at the Abbey then , just as there is Vaughans at the Abbey now , ' continued the old man , staring meditatively into his foxglove brew , as though he could see a mental picture in the pot", "context_len": 42}}}, "continue": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 51, "word_index": 110, "context": "my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction , you shall meet with the fate you so justly deserve", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Campbell darkly intimated that should her opposition continue he would procure from two pliant physicians a certificate of her insanity and have her confined in that most terrible of prisons , a mad-house", "context_len": 33}}}, "bands": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 83, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The ground shook under the relentless pressure of the tractor bands , the air reeked with petrol fumes", "context_len": 18}}}, "sailors": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 73, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "homing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "In this cage were homing pigeons", "context_len": 6}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "And when that wagon passed along carrying the homing pigeons every one clapped , for here were little dumb creatures who had shown a wonderful understanding and had done all they could for the country , and the little pigeons were so modest that they actually wondered what all this excitement was about !", "context_len": 54}}}, "dangers": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 85, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He has known well enough to what dangers a convert may be exposed ; for he has experienced them", "context_len": 19}}}, "shown": {"freq": 149, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 149, "word_index": 64, "context": "a short time upon earth , was there not more reason that she should spend it in doing all she could for her Master 's service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut her ear to the cry of the needy , because to answer it would cost", "context_len": 128}}}, "alphabet": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "Creek possesses all sounds of the general Maskoki alphabet ; but here and in Hitchiti the gutturals g , k , are often pronounced with the tongue resting upon the fore or alveolar part of the palate", "context_len": 37}}}, "wiggled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "And the rabbits sniffed and their little noses wiggled and trembled as they told each other what a nice world it was with children and clover both in it !", "context_len": 30}}}, "writers": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 25, "context": "It is the custom among all nations to give kings the glory of all that is effected by their generals and officers ; and the writers of those days would , of course , in narrating the exploits of the Macedonian army , exaggerate the share which Alexander had in their performances , and underrate those of Parmenio", "context_len": 58}}}, "students": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 22, "context": "Always brightness and fun and laughter , for Aunt Rutha 's hospitable house was a favourite resort with many of the Harvard students , and it was the glorious summer time , when all the world -- their little world -- was free to be gay", "context_len": 46}}}, "wriggling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "Her light flaxen hair and pale , fair complexion gave her a smooth , shining appearance , and somehow Peggy always thought her manners were smooth and shining too , for she had a way of wriggling out of any little difficulty and unpleasantness , so that the blame rested upon other people , and was always ready to take a mean advantage , or play some of those little underhand tricks which schoolgirls know only too well", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "In an instant he had pounced upon it , and firmly grasping the wriggling creature , he rushed out of the brook , and running over the grass as fast as his little wet , bare feet could carry him , went up to Aunt Patty , and laying the slimy , muddy reptile on her lap , exclaimed , with an air of great triumph and generosity , --", "context_len": 70}}}, "doubtless": {"freq": 111, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 59, "context": "How the Mummy would enjoy it , how she would revel in the good food and the lovely house ! What a red-letter day it would be to her all her life , for all the rest of her years ! How Sukey and Ann Pratt and the neighbors down at Dawlish would respect her for evermore ! And doubtless the Mummy 's dress might be managed , and -- but what about Aunt Susan ? Would Aunt Susan ever forgive her ? She dared not run the risk of her displeasure ; too much depended on keeping her in a good humor", "context_len": 103}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 2, "context": "This , doubtless , may be so , Sir Gervaise , the doge said , with a slight smile , but it is to the head that plans , rather than to the hand that strikes , that such success as you have achieved is due ; and the credit of this night attack is , as the cavalier Caretto tells me , wholly yours , for until you issued your final orders it seemed to him , and to the two good knights his companions , that there was naught to do but to remain in port and watch this corsair fleet sail away to carry out its work of destruction", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 27, "context": "He had no doubt that his betrayal was due to one of the gardeners , who had several times shown him signs of ill will , being doubtless jealous of the immunity he enjoyed from hard labour , and who must , he thought , have crept up and overheard some conversation ; but in that case it was singular that the fact of his knowledge of Turkish had not been mentioned", "context_len": 72}}}, "tire": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 37, "context": "When the damsel saw him , she opened him the privy door and he went in to the Lady Bedrulbudour [ 596 ] and said to her , `` Harkye , I will have thee dress and tire thyself and put away melancholy from thee ; and when the accursed Maugrabin cometh to thee , do thou receive him with 'Welcome and fair welcome ' and go to meet him with a smiling face and bid him come sup with thee and profess to him that thou hast forgotten thy beloved Alaeddin and thy father and that thou lovest him with an exceeding love", "context_len": 104}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "There is something in a life of adventure which warms the blood and makes time pass swiftly ; my goodwife and I sometimes tire of each other 's company , as I expect Ethelgiva and you will in time", "context_len": 39}}}, "closes": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "He opens and closes his scissor-like tail when he does his marketing just as ladies would open and close their purses after they had bought something", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "The meeting of the syndicate is called for to-night , at the office of Random & Griggs , said McGlory , and I don \u2019 t want those two bars until the last thing before the bank closes at three o \u2019 clock", "context_len": 43}}}, "purses": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 77, "context": "Thou sayst , O my mother , that I have no gift to present to the Sultan , according to the wont of the folk , whilst in fact I have by me a gift and an offering , the like whereof methinketh no king ever possessed , no , nor aught to match therewith ; for [ 338 ] thou must know , O my mother , that the fruits , which I brought in the purses [ 339 ] from the treasure and which I deemed glass or crystal , are very jewels , methinketh all the kings of the world may not compass the least of them , and I , of my companying with the jewellers , know that they are precious stones", "context_len": 127}}}, "strings": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 35, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}}}, "twigs": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 75, "context": "Of course I was but one of many who were to gasp out their lives in this dreadful Aceldama ; and in a very short time each post , or stake , was decorated with its own separate victim , some of whom , it seemed , were to perish by the torture of fire , for after the victims had been secured to the stakes , huge bundles of faggots , composed of dry twigs and branches , were piled around some of them", "context_len": 85}}}, "natured": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "Next to these grown up `` boys , '' came Fanny and Dolly , two lively , good natured young women ; and last of all , Bob , a boy about fourteen , quite ready to make friends with the children , and to show them all the wonders of the place", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "page 11 - changed `` goodnatured '' to `` good-natured '' and page 22 - changed `` good natured '' to `` good-natured '' - other books in this series use `` good-natured '' consistently", "context_len": 35}}}, "hatching": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "What they can be going to do , or what plot they are hatching , I can not discover , only I fear that it is some design for vengeance upon the Danes -- some dark treachery plotted against those in our midst ; and , if such is the case , I can but feel uneasy for poor Alfgar", "context_len": 60}}}, "flycatchers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "When she is sitting on four whitish-brown eggs which will soon be little scissor-tailed flycatchers she is as happy as a bird can be , and that is pretty happy , as you can imagine", "context_len": 35}}}, "insect": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 83, "context": "But I soon discovered that even this small measure of mercy was to be denied me ; food and drink were indeed to be served out to us -- in order , as I surmised , that we might meet the ordeal in store for us with unabated strength -- but the night was to be passed , as the day had been , secured to the sacrificial post , exposed , naked and helpless , to the elements and to the myriad insect plagues that attacked us unceasingly", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The New Englander had stripped , and had anointed himself from head to foot with a dark , offensive-smelling grease , which , he assured us , would keep him immune from insect bites , and at the same time render him nearly invisible", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I wonder which insect stings worst ? I ought to have a few handy for the worst of the servants", "context_len": 20}}}, "flycatcher": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I am called a flycatcher and I catch flies", "context_len": 9}}}, "insects": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 67, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "I listened intently , but the silence of noontide had fallen , and everything was deathly still ; there was not the faintest zephyr to stir the foliage ; and even the very insects that so persistently attack one in the African jungle seemed to be indulging in a mid-day siesta", "context_len": 51}}}, "woodpeckers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "They distinguished , again , in its case ; and decided that it was not a woodpecker , and might be killed ; sometimes they thought that woodpeckers were so nearly yellowhammers that they might be killed , but they had never heard of any one 's eating a woodpecker , and so they could not quite bring themselves to it", "context_len": 61}}}, "drumming": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The first thing that brought him to himself was Polly 's voice , and her fingers drumming on the door", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Lying there , the water was over his ears , filling them with a drumming sound", "context_len": 16}}}, "drums": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 43, "context": "So they fared on with him in great state , with four of the Viziers for his sword-bearers , whilst all the troops and people of the city , both townsfolk and strangers , walked in procession before him , carrying flambeaux and drums and flutes and instruments of mirth and music , till they brought him to his palace , when he alighted and entering , sat down , as did also the Viziers and Amirs who were in his company , whilst the mamelukes brought sherbets and sweetmeats [ 493 ] and gave all who were with him in the procession to drink , albeit they were a multitude of folk whose number might not be told", "context_len": 119}}}, "burrowing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "In warfare , an army , in investing a fortified place , spends ordinarily a considerable time in burrowing their way along in trenches , half under ground , until they get near enough to plant their cannon where the balls can take effect upon some part of the wall", "context_len": 50}}}, "destroy": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 103, "context": "Many will , we may calculate , jump overboard and swim ashore rather than face us , and we shall be able , at any rate , to capture three or four of their craft , for , as they come out , one by one , we can all close round them ; and with nearly fifty knights , ninety released captives , burning for vengeance , and some fifty or sixty fishermen , for those from the fire ships will , of course , join us -- we shall make short work of them , and may even hope to entirely destroy their fleet", "context_len": 106}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` Because I know the secrets of the prison house -- knew them before the Danes had murdered the once happy dwellers in this garden of England , which they have made a howling wilderness ; hence I escaped the wrath of the furious parricide , whom the saints destroy , with ease , and laughed in security at their vain efforts to take me ; but we must waste no time ; it yet wants five hours to daybreak ; within those five hours we must reach the opposite shore", "context_len": 91}}}, "crested": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Crested Woodpecker and Mr", "context_len": 4}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The spar and sail acted not only as a floating anchor , but as a breakwater , and the white crested waves , which came on as if they would break upon the boat , seemed robbed of half their violence by the obstruction to their course , and passed under the felucca without breaking", "context_len": 55}}}, "instruments": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 47, "context": "So they fared on with him in great state , with four of the Viziers for his sword-bearers , whilst all the troops and people of the city , both townsfolk and strangers , walked in procession before him , carrying flambeaux and drums and flutes and instruments of mirth and music , till they brought him to his palace , when he alighted and entering , sat down , as did also the Viziers and Amirs who were in his company , whilst the mamelukes brought sherbets and sweetmeats [ 493 ] and gave all who were with him in the procession to drink , albeit they were a multitude of folk whose number might not be told", "context_len": 119}}}, "warblers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Those who sang and helped along the chorus and played little mouth-organs and various instruments made out of twigs were the warblers , the vireos , goldfinches , mocking-birds , bobolinks , chickadees , pewees , phoebes , orioles , thrushes , song-sparrows and whippoorwills", "context_len": 45}}}, "vireos": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Those who sang and helped along the chorus and played little mouth-organs and various instruments made out of twigs were the warblers , the vireos , goldfinches , mocking-birds , bobolinks , chickadees , pewees , phoebes , orioles , thrushes , song-sparrows and whippoorwills", "context_len": 45}}}, "goldfinches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Those who sang and helped along the chorus and played little mouth-organs and various instruments made out of twigs were the warblers , the vireos , goldfinches , mocking-birds , bobolinks , chickadees , pewees , phoebes , orioles , thrushes , song-sparrows and whippoorwills", "context_len": 45}}}, "bobolinks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Those who sang and helped along the chorus and played little mouth-organs and various instruments made out of twigs were the warblers , the vireos , goldfinches , mocking-birds , bobolinks , chickadees , pewees , phoebes , orioles , thrushes , song-sparrows and whippoorwills", "context_len": 45}}}, "chickadees": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Sparrows and chickadees , and other birds which make their home with us during the winter , were hopping merrily over the leafless branches , and twittering ceaselessly to one another , as if they were telling of the happy time near at hand , when the warm south winds would blow , and the trees and bushes be covered with their beautiful green summer dress", "context_len": 66}}}, "pewees": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Those who sang and helped along the chorus and played little mouth-organs and various instruments made out of twigs were the warblers , the vireos , goldfinches , mocking-birds , bobolinks , chickadees , pewees , phoebes , orioles , thrushes , song-sparrows and whippoorwills", "context_len": 45}}}, "phoebes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Those who sang and helped along the chorus and played little mouth-organs and various instruments made out of twigs were the warblers , the vireos , goldfinches , mocking-birds , bobolinks , chickadees , pewees , phoebes , orioles , thrushes , song-sparrows and whippoorwills", "context_len": 45}}}, "orioles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Those who sang and helped along the chorus and played little mouth-organs and various instruments made out of twigs were the warblers , the vireos , goldfinches , mocking-birds , bobolinks , chickadees , pewees , phoebes , orioles , thrushes , song-sparrows and whippoorwills", "context_len": 45}}}, "thrushes": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "He found three thrushes ' nests low down in the elder-bushes , all filled with gaping yellow mouths , the pretty little chaffinch 's up in the ivy-tree , with only two speckly eggs as yet , and Jenny Wren 's household , hidden away in a bank , full of so many children that she surely resembled the old woman who lived in a shoe , and it was a marvel how she could remember which little chirping atom she had fed last", "context_len": 84}}}, "whippoorwills": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Those who sang and helped along the chorus and played little mouth-organs and various instruments made out of twigs were the warblers , the vireos , goldfinches , mocking-birds , bobolinks , chickadees , pewees , phoebes , orioles , thrushes , song-sparrows and whippoorwills", "context_len": 45}}}, "woodpecker": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "They could hear the blue-jays snarling at one another , and the yellowhammer chuckling ; on some dead tree a redheaded woodpecker hammered noisily , and if the boys had only had a gun with them they could have killed lots of things", "context_len": 43}}}, "songsters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "What would a band be like in birdland without singing ? They would miss all the beautiful songsters who would add so much music , and that would be such a pity", "context_len": 32}}}, "notices": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "Francis Le Baron transmitted to the Smithsonian Institution a few ethnologic notices and a vocabulary obtained from the Seminole Indians of Chipko 's ( since deceased ) band , which he had visited in March 1881 in their village near Lake Pierce", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` I beg your pardon , sir , but there 's not many young people that notices a hungry face in that way ; and I 've thought of it many a time", "context_len": 33}}}, "sunrise": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 57, "context": "And before they went to bed that night and before they even thought of putting their heads under their wings , they sent around notices to all the birds to be on hand , and this is how the notices read : First concert of the season to be given by the Woodpeckers \u2019 Band to-morrow at sunrise", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Shortly after sunrise the remaining barges of the flotilla , which had been tied up for the night at some distance along the canal , came up and passed the solitary craft", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I \u2019 ve waked up at sunrise many times , and seen Captain Rhines and Ben ploughing for mother ; they would plough till nine o \u2019 clock , then go home , eat their breakfast , and then do their own work , while mother and I , with Sam to drop the seed , would plant it , and the next day they would get more ready", "context_len": 69}}}, "rags": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 88, "context": "with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread and half-picked bones which the thief had concealed in some cunning corner inside a manger or under the roof of the loft", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Dear brown bar\u00e9ge , what service it was doing me ! I secretly determined that it should be my dinner dress every evening until it wore itself to rags", "context_len": 29}}}, "teasing": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 50, "context": "Their voices were subdued lest they should reach the king 's ears ; but , respectful as was the bearing of all the members of the group by the fire , they clearly split into two halves : on the one hand , the officers of the escort who were teasing the boy , and on the other , a group of gentlemen , some wearing the conventional ribbons and laces of a cavalier , others in the rough cloth of country wear , stained with the mud of country lanes , while the master and mistress of the house moved from one guest to another , evidently nervous at the doubtful honour that such a royal visit had brought to their roof", "context_len": 123}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The children were taught when they teased one another that there was nothing the fiends so much delighted in as teasing", "context_len": 21}}}, "lo": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 56, "context": "But still she went on , but only slowly , because of the great press of people around her ; and suddenly an icy wind passed over them , and all fell to the earth shivering and terror-stricken , for they had never felt cold before , and they looked up to the mountain , and lo ! upon the very summit , at the door of the deserted wigwam , stood a terrible figure , clothed in white , and having a face as white as his robes , and his hair was like the long crystals that hang from the roofs of caves that the water goes through , and his eyes were like two great diamonds , white , yet blazing like the sun", "context_len": 127}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "All the beasts of the prairie had he slain in his terrible anger , and all the people had fled to the mountains for food , thither he thought he would follow them , and he sat down upon a ridge of sand , to strengthen his bow , and sharpen his arrows , when , lo ! quite unmindful of him , a thousand little creatures he had fancied too insignificant to notice , sprang forth from their holes , and gathered in groups for their daily gossip", "context_len": 89}}}, "cowardly": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 10, "context": "Her mother had told her times enough that it was cowardly to blame inanimate objects for things which we were to blame for ourselves , and Aunt Elizabeth went further and said no one but a person without any wits would abuse a senseless thing for what was his own thoughtlessness or carelessness", "context_len": 53}}}, "boasting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Oh ! That 's because you are nothing but a girl -- but boys -- ! '' George left his sentence unfinished , for of late he had become very careful of boasting before Guy , whom he knew was too well acquainted with him to be deceived by empty words", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Don \u2019 t think I \u2019 m boasting of this , Eliot , for I \u2019 m not ; I am heartily ashamed of it", "context_len": 25}}}, "vegetable": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "But still one can forgive a vegetable for boasting very often , for if we lived so near the ground or in the ground as they do we \u2019 d probably like to pretend we were higher up ! The onion was now talking", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 32, "context": "and I have just come from a loiter at the Botanical Gardens , which showed us that we need be under no great concern , were hemp and flax exterminated from the vegetable world , and silkworms to leave off being spinsters , as we could dress cheaply and well on plantain fibre , have capital paper and excellent ropes , etc", "context_len": 62}}}, "onion": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 8, "context": "The hostess who brought Dick 's bowl of onion broth at this moment destroyed his chance of feigning sleep , and he had to resign himself to endure his companion 's conversation which flowed on , garnished with oaths and cant phrases in three or four different languages , without any interruption , till by an unguarded movement Richard exposed his face to the light of the fire , and the stranger stared a moment , and then sprang up exclaiming , `` Body o ' me if it be not Measter Dick himself ! ''", "context_len": 96}}}, "ancestors": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 43, "context": "Alfred , her eldest son -- for Edmund was the offspring of an early amour of the king -- was on the other side of the bed , a well-made youth , combining in his features the haughty bearing of his Norman maternal ancestors with the English traits of his father ; but now his expression was one of distress and anxiety , which was yet more deeply shared by his younger brother , Edward , who even at this period manifested that strong sense of religious obligation and that early devotion which in later years caused him to be numbered amongst canonised saints", "context_len": 104}}}, "aunts": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 39, "context": "In the morning they were dry , but just as hard as stone , and you had to soap the heel of your woollen sock ( which your grandmother had knitted for you , or maybe some of your aunts ) before you could get your foot in , and sometimes the ears of the boot that you pulled it on by would give way , and you would have to stamp your foot in and kick the toe against the mop-board", "context_len": 82}}}, "uncles": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 4, "context": "One of his river-faring uncles was visiting with his family at the boy 's home when he laid out the scheme of his great fiction of `` Hamet el Zegri , '' and the kindly young aunt took an interest in it which he poorly rewarded a few months later , when she asked how the story was getting on , and he tried to ignore the whole matter , and showed such mortification at the mention of it that the poor lady was quite bewildered", "context_len": 86}}}, "radishes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Playing in the garden , they saw Mary sitting outside the gate , counting over the money she had made by the sale of her radishes : and as they were talking to her , it came about that she told them of the sick brother lying on his bed , never able to go out and breathe the fresh air , or see the beautiful blue sky and green trees , in this lovely Summer weather ; and how she and her mother were working and saving , that they might have enough to buy the easy chair", "context_len": 99}}}, "sarcastic": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "The man went his way without saying anything more , but his behavior was somehow so sarcastic that the boy had no pleasure in his sport that morning ; partly , perhaps , because he found no kildees to shoot at on the Common", "context_len": 44}}}, "pumpkins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "And neither did the oats , corn , pumpkins and other plain creatures who don \u2019 t know what aristocracy and grandeur mean", "context_len": 23}}}, "aristocracy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "A famous king he seems to be , or have been , with a good palace , standing army of 25,000 men kept in strict discipline , a hereditary aristocracy -- all of the colour of coal -- and ecclesiastical establishment", "context_len": 41}}}, "grandeur": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 15, "context": "Nothing could have been prettier than the wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he", "context_len": 128}}}, "grandparents": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 40, "context": "Edna felt that she had brought this lecture upon Reliance and felt rather badly to have done so , but the prospect of buckwheat cakes soon drove her self-reproach away and she went in to say good morning to her grandparents , well satisfied with the world in general and content to look ahead rather than at what was now past and gone , and which could not be altered", "context_len": 70}}}, "counts": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "I believe nothing more memorable ever happened in my Boy 's Town , as the grown-up world counts events ; but for the boys there , every day was full of wonderful occurrence and thrilling excitement", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "He had been brought up in a small country town , where a dollar counts for a good deal more than it does in the city , and where a man possessing ten thousand dollars is thought to be independently rich", "context_len": 41}}}, "nourish": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "If there is water , the most barren sands will clothe themselves with some species of vegetation , which , in its decay , will form a soil that will nourish more and more fully each succeeding generation of plants", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "But our religion forbids me to nourish revenge", "context_len": 8}}}, "cork": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 53, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "bookworm": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 3, "context": "'What an inveterate bookworm you are , Paul , ' and Belle looked at the pile of volumes Pauline had brought from the library to study in the long morning hours which the force of a lifelong habit gave her , before the rest of the family were astir", "context_len": 49}}}, "cherry": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 44, "context": "It is brown holland all during the summer , the coarsest brown holland , and it is the coarsest blue serge during the winter ; never , never anything else -- no style , no fashion , no pretty ribbons , not even a cherry ribbon for my hair , and so little pocket-money , oh ! so little -- only a penny a week", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "At last the Major and Florence reached the postern-gate which opened into the cherry orchard , and then Florence stood still and raised her voice and called , `` Kitty ! Kitty Sharston ! '' and there came an answering call , clear and high as a bird 's , and the next instant Kitty , in her white summer dress , was seen emerging from under the cherry-trees", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I did something very mean , and I did it to the girl who gave me those lovely cherry ribbons", "context_len": 21}}}, "foliage": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 107, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "willow": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "It was only before dinner I said to Marion , 'You would n't like to be in Miss Wickham 's shoes to-night , would you , Marion ? You would n't like to be wearing the willow , would you , my girl ? ' And she said no , she would n't , but then she added , 'With my soul full of Art , mother , I always can have my resources , ' and that is where Marion believes , that if she were so unlucky as to be crossed in love , she would have the advantage of you , Miss Wickham , for you have plainly said that you have no soul for h'Art", "context_len": 119}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "He had a notion that if he could get up a chariot by sawing down the sides of a store-box for the body , and borrowing the hind-wheels of the baby 's willow wagon , and then , drawn by the family dog Tip at a mad gallop , come suddenly whirling round the corner of the school-house , wearing spangled circus-tights and bearing Apollo 's bow and shaft , while a silken scarf which he had seen in a bureau-drawer at home blew gallantly out behind him , it would have a fine effect with the boys", "context_len": 98}}}, "tonics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Then medicines and tonics have been made from the fruit and bark and roots of the wild black cherry", "context_len": 19}}}, "rosewood": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Cherry-wood is considered by many people to be almost as handsome as rosewood and mahogany , and the old wild black cherry tree is the tree which is used to make beautiful cherry-wood furniture", "context_len": 34}}}, "mahogany": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "The only pieces of furniture which looked as if they had ever cost much were a large mahogany table with carved feet , which stood between the windows , and a bookcase of the same wood at the side of the fireplace ; but both of these were old-fashioned , and although they might be worth much to their owners , would have brought little if offered for sale", "context_len": 69}}}, "cultivated": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 46, "context": "So thither flocked the young and the old : the wood rangers and hunters from the forests of Newenham , where Herstan had right of wood cutting ; the men who wove baskets and hurdles of osier work from the river banks ; the theows who cultivated the home farm ; the ceorls who rented a hide of land here and a hide there -- all , the grandfather and the grandson , accepted the invitation to feast", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "And , while we \u2019 re asking questions , will somebody tell me when the natives of this country became market gardeners ? There \u2019 s enough cultivated land around those dozen thatched huts to supply food for ten villages", "context_len": 40}}}, "chatting": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 41, "context": "Our wants for the next few hours were consequently supplied , we had therefore no need to do anything further than just to wait for nightfall and then resume our voyage ; which we did , passing the intervening time in chatting together and discussing the various precautions which we must take in order to elude our pursuers , who , by the way , were now several miles ahead of us", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "By resolving to watch the others and follow their example he got along very well , and by the time the second course had disappeared their pleasant chatting and perfect freedom had loosened the strain so that he was once more somewhat at ease", "context_len": 44}}}, "farmhouse": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 26, "context": "She meant to lay the hands together , as she remembered her mother 's had been laid long ago in the stricken gloom of the Kansas farmhouse which had been her home ; but suddenly there was a movement at her feet , and she stopped , having stumbled over some living thing in the shadows of the couch , something that stirred and struggled and gasped passionately , `` Vamos ! Vamos ! ''", "context_len": 75}}}, "bugs": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 8, "context": "That \u2019 s so , said the potato bugs , and why should we be friendly with the farmers ? They don \u2019 t like us", "context_len": 26}}}, "dingy": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "No wonder the boys love the old place , and even the dingy recitation rooms , whose quaint , high desks and slippery benches are notched with the penknives of many a boy , whose name , as a man , has come to be known through the length and breadth of England", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "Besides the bed , there was in the room for furniture a broken-backed rocking-chair ; a small table with a split top , on which stood a common kerosene hand-lamp ; a dingy white earthen water pitcher and bowl the former with a circular piece broken out of its nose sitting on a washstand , made of a long box stood on one end , with a muslin curtain hanging in front of it", "context_len": 74}}}, "fairyland": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "Jack Frost threw photographs of fairyland upon the windows , and hung the roofs with fringes of crystal pendants , while the snowflakes piled themselves over the fences and made a shroud for the trees , and every day Pauline , with this strange peace in her heart , did her housework to the glory of God", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "FAIRYLAND 30", "context_len": 2}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "'How short the way to fairyland Across the purple hill", "context_len": 10}}}, "cambric": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The button bag was made of blue and white cambric , I believe you call it , and it had a blue silk ribbon around it at the top I suppose a sort of neck-tie , one might say", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "I think there is still a little milk , for it did n't take much for the cambric tea , and we can have more of that", "context_len": 27}}}, "lacked": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 56, "context": "So he said to her , `` What aileth thee , O my beloved ? Tell me , hath there befallen thee aught to trouble thee ? '' And she answered him , saying , `` There aileth me nothing ; but , O my beloved , I had thought that our palace [ 655 ] lacked of nought ; however , O my eyes [ 656 ] Alaeddin , were there hung in the dome of the upper pavilion [ 657 ] an egg of the bird Roc , there were not its like in the world", "context_len": 98}}}, "knight": {"freq": 183, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 174, "word_index": 49, "context": "Three times have you earned special credit ; upon the first occasion , the grand master -- no mean judge of conduct and character -- deemed you worthy of secular knighthood , an honour which has not , in my memory , been bestowed at Rhodes upon any young knight ; on the second , you were promoted to the command of a galley , though never before has such a command been given to any , save knights of long experience ; and now , for the third time , the councillors of one of the greatest of Italian cities are about to do you honour", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "We have borne toil , and hardship , and blows , the heat of the sun by day , and the damp by night , but we would humbly represent to you that since we were placed in the galley commanded by this knight our lot has been made bearable by his humanity and kindness", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "I am Fabricius Caretto , one said ; this is Giacomo Da Vinci ; this Pietro Forzi : all knight commanders of the Order , and now for six years prisoners in the hands of these corsairs", "context_len": 37}}}, "lord": {"freq": 142, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 136, "word_index": 100, "context": "these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about to work upon him by practice , that he might hinder him from giving his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour to Alaeddin , and accordingly said to him , [ 436 ] `` O my lord , all the treasures of the world were not worth a paring of thy daughter Bedrulbudour 's nails ; indeed , Thy Highness overrateth this upon her", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 59, "context": "A horse is quick to learn what kind of man holds the reins , and woe to him whom the equine despises ! Bucephalus would not have needed any enlightenment had Harper or any one of the regular riders been in the saddle , but he wasn \u2019 t sure about the lusty young fellow who was trying to lord it over him", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` And have you any proof , sir , that you are lord of Holwick ? '' he continued", "context_len": 19}}}, "bracing": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 42, "context": "When this could not be done , they were to fall down upon the ground when they saw this strange military engine coming , and locking their shields together over their heads , allow the wagon to roll on over them , bracing up energetically against its weight", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "This was the last shut-in day , for the weather was clear and bracing , and , well wrapped up , Edna was able to enjoy it", "context_len": 27}}}, "jingled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I \u2019 ve been for sleigh-rides where bells jingled and where my button heart leaped for joy ! \u2019", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Orders were shouted , a bell clanged , another jingled , and the tug was racing down the North River toward the Bay", "context_len": 23}}}, "bathing": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 5, "context": "I belonged to a lovely bathing suit which belonged to a little girl , and I used to see the sand made into castles and bridges and rivers , and all sorts of marvelous things , and the lovely ocean would go over me oh , it was so cool and refreshing !", "context_len": 53}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 2, "context": "Leaving the bathing place while there was yet time to reach home before dark , they came at last to a ford across the stream , the only spot where it could be safely forded , and as such known to the natives of the vicinity ; when their dogs began to whine , and to run with their noses to the ground , as if they had found something unusual to attract their attention", "context_len": 75}}}, "castles": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 23, "context": "I belonged to a lovely bathing suit which belonged to a little girl , and I used to see the sand made into castles and bridges and rivers , and all sorts of marvelous things , and the lovely ocean would go over me oh , it was so cool and refreshing !", "context_len": 53}}}, "bridges": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 25, "context": "I belonged to a lovely bathing suit which belonged to a little girl , and I used to see the sand made into castles and bridges and rivers , and all sorts of marvelous things , and the lovely ocean would go over me oh , it was so cool and refreshing !", "context_len": 53}}}, "rivers": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 79, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Yes , '' said Maggie ; `` one little brook runs on until it finds another little brook , and then they join , and run on together , and then they meet another and another till they all make a small river , and that joins other little rivers and brooks , till there is a very large one like that we sailed on this morning , and that runs into the great , great sea that we used to see at Quam Beach last summer", "context_len": 88}}}, "shriek": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "If I had known how to shriek I would have called at the top of my voice , if I had a voice :", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 46, "context": "Her gruesome voice as she recounted what the goblins did to naughty boys made several small sinners in the back benches shake in their shoes -- many innocent infants felt they should never dare to go to bed in the dark again ; and the wild shriek with which she ended her announcement that 'the goblins are about ' quite brought down the house , and the children shouted and yelled and cheered as the Rector strove to get order once more , and wind up the proceedings with the National Anthem", "context_len": 92}}}, "tan": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Well , \u2019 said the tan button , we must get ready for the party , as we must frolic all night and then be back in our places by morning , so it won \u2019 t confuse or mix any one up who may sew in the morning", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 34, "context": "Franky put his head on one side , and looking quizzically at the old lady , said , `` Oo went down-'tairs for oo bastet wis a bone in oo foot , so oo tan do for my tart wis a bone in oo foot", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I \u2019 m goin \u2019 to tan your hide , and don \u2019 t you forget it !", "context_len": 18}}}, "frolic": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Well , \u2019 said the tan button , we must get ready for the party , as we must frolic all night and then be back in our places by morning , so it won \u2019 t confuse or mix any one up who may sew in the morning", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then came a grand frolic in the barn with father and Uncle John and the boys , Tom and Walter being of the party , until Mrs", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "And they say , too , that she was never twice alike ; that sometimes she was grave and sedate as could be , and sometimes she was full of fun and frolic , but always so sweet and good and innocent that she was like a bright little star in those dark times , for there was war between England and France , and they say only the children can be light-hearted in war time", "context_len": 76}}}, "confuse": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Well , \u2019 said the tan button , we must get ready for the party , as we must frolic all night and then be back in our places by morning , so it won \u2019 t confuse or mix any one up who may sew in the morning", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "And as he proceeded the unfavorable impression that had prevailed concerning him was gradually dispelled ; for surely he did not speak like a desperate character or a thief ; nor was Lawyer Frances , by his sternest and most accusing cross-questioning , able to confuse the lad or shake him in his statements", "context_len": 54}}}, "thread": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 52, "context": "The voluminous pages of Father Cuthbert 's diary for the years of bitter woe and misery which followed can not be fully transcribed ; they would fill a volume themselves , and we must content ourselves with a few extracts , which will probably interest our readers , and carry on the thread of the history to the place where our narrative will again flow free and uninterrupted", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "Each silver thread that glitters in the hair , Is like a wayside landmark , -- planted there To show Earth \u2019 s pilgrims , as they onward wend , How nearly they approach their journey \u2019 s end ! \u2019", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Even if you had a needle and thread to stitch them together they would break to pieces because they are so dry and brittle", "context_len": 24}}}, "gleeful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Not since his blindness came upon him had his mother heard Willie 's voice sound so gleeful , and now in her heart she blessed the dear little girl who she felt had done him good", "context_len": 36}}}, "chimpanzee": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "Charlie was the name of a chimpanzee in the Zoo , said daddy , and he was ill", "context_len": 18}}}, "monkeys": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 64, "context": "If so be you laid hands if it were but on a hen , if you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old", "context_len": 128}}}, "chimpanzees": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "\u2019 For monkeys and chimpanzees are the most patient creatures when they are sick", "context_len": 14}}}, "toad": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 11, "context": "I wouldn \u2019 t ; he \u2019 s a particular old toad , and would rather have it just as it grew ; but if you touch it , he \u2019 ll think you \u2019 ve taken off some where you ought not to , and spilte it ; he \u2019 ll no more thank you for saving him labor on a float-piece , than a feller would thank you for courting a girl for him ; he \u2019 d rather do it himself", "context_len": 84}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "continent": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 16, "context": "Once , also , when the boys had gone into the vast woods of that measureless continent which they called the Island , for pawpaws or for hickory-nuts , or maybe buckeyes , they got lost ; and while they ran about in terror , they heard the distant lowing and bellowing of cattle", "context_len": 54}}}, "horned": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He was a horned toad , and he was the kind of a toad that needs little else beside air to live on", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "punched": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "Shagbark crumpled up some dry fragments from a plug of tobacco , in the palm of his hand , punched them into the bowl of his pipe , switched a match along the side of his buckskins , applied the tiny flame , and rode to the head of the company without another word", "context_len": 54}}}, "speaks": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 41, "context": "If you are very good indeed , Mary , and if you are very good , Dolly , you shall both hear some of my letter -- not the private part , of course -- but the public part , which speaks about father 's wonderful interesting travels , and his sort of public life , the life he gives to his country", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 2, "context": "137 , speaks of the `` sepulchres or tumuli of the Yamasees who were here slain in the last decisive battle , the Creeks having driven them to this point , between the doubling of the river ( St", "context_len": 39}}}, "threading": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "She was not long in discovering the landmarks of which she was in search , and a few minutes later the canoe was threading its tortuous way among a tangled mass of mangrove roots toward the solid bank of the river , landing upon which , we drew our light craft bodily up out of the water , concealing her beneath a broad overhanging mass of foliage which hid her so effectually that I would defy anybody but ourselves to find her", "context_len": 82}}}, "knit": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Yes ; Aunt Helen sent some yarn to his mother to knit socks ; and Jemmy wanted to learn so that he could do something for his country , if he was a lame boy , he said", "context_len": 39}}}, "sway": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 56, "context": "Gervaise slept for some time ; when he awoke the same stillness reigned , but there was a change in the appearance of the sky ; its brightness was dulled by a faint mist , while , although the sea was of a glassy smoothness , there was an imperceptible swell that caused the felucca to sway uneasily", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was certainly Peggy who suggested sitting on the top of the haystack , and making it sway backwards and forwards like a swing , a delightful sensation while it lasted , but which ended suddenly in the collapse of the whole top of the stack , much to Father 's wrath , for it took David a couple of hours to repair the damage , and certainly did not sweeten the old man 's already crusty temper", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 61, "context": "It is true , they must feel confident of his superiority ; but when this superiority stands out so clearly and distinctly marked , combined , too , with all the graces and attractions of youth and manly beauty , as it was in the case of Alexander , the minds of men are brought very easily and rapidly under its sway", "context_len": 62}}}, "organ": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 77, "context": "He was absent on the third of these little excursions of his , and was surreptitiously amusing his audacious little self by seeing how it seemed to sit in the Oueen 's own stall , when hark ! -- yes , that was going to be The Roseate Hues , and with a bound that came near bringing the royal draperies with him he was out of the stall in a trice and fairly scrambling up the organ stairs", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Now he was standing in admiration before the great oak chair of State beneath the organ gallery , and now nothing loath he mounts the steps that lead to it and runs a finger along the curves of its elaborate carving , and then , with a most reverent air , touches the embroidered cross and garter with which it is decorated", "context_len": 62}}}, "organist": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "He moved to one side and the organ gave a curious rumble and a very funny sound and the organist looked as if he didn \u2019 t know what the trouble could be , and the people stopped singing and then tried to go on singing without any music", "context_len": 49}}}, "nightmare": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 40, "context": "His father was so clever that he went home with my boy one night about midnight when the boy had come to pass the night with his boys , and the youngest of them had said he always had the nightmare and walked in his sleep , and as likely as not he might kill you before he knew it", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Making no sound , they went swiftly through the jungle and did not pause or stop to rest until they had put a great distance between them and the scene of the fantastic nightmare adventure of the evening before", "context_len": 39}}}, "rumble": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 118, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "At the same time there came a ripping , tearing sound , a rumble and a roar that vied with the crash of the thunder", "context_len": 25}}}, "burr": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The organist tried to play again and this awfully queer rumble and burr and buzz was heard once more", "context_len": 19}}}, "buzz": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 2, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "pedals": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "'It must be stunning to have a bicycle , ' said Bobby , regarding with envious eye the row of bright machines neatly stacked against the wall , and spinning the pedals with a not too gentle hand", "context_len": 38}}}, "coaxing": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 67, "context": "The old lady nodded and smiled , as if she were glad to see them so happy , but Nonesuch seemed not only surprised , but displeased , at finding himself in such jolly company ; and , after some shaking of his head and putting back of his ears , stood stock still in the middle of the road ; nor could all Aunt Patty 's coaxing or scolding , or even some gentle touches of the whip , persuade him to go on , till the whole party were out of sight", "context_len": 94}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 41, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Once or twice , as Bessie was passing through the hall of the hotel , Miss Adams had opened her door and called to her in a coaxing voice ; but Bessie always ran off as fast as possible , without waiting to answer", "context_len": 44}}}, "begging": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 19, "context": "But here the Rector again stepped in , and limited the performance to half an hour 's duration , begging them to choose something appropriate for a Sunday-school audience , and if possible with a moral , and further reminding them that a platform consisting of tables hastily pushed together after the tea was concluded would scarcely give facilities for either footlights , drop-scenes , or side-shifts , a curtain being the utmost he could undertake to manage in that direction", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "The poor fellow had been thinking how he could earn his livelihood , -- he has a wife and four children , and of course religious begging would be for a convert both improper and unprofitable", "context_len": 36}}}, "collection": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 74, "word_index": 81, "context": "with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread and half-picked bones which the thief had concealed in some cunning corner inside a manger or under the roof of the loft", "context_len": 128}}}, "cocoons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I saw a little boy to-day , said daddy , named Robert who has a collection of cocoons", "context_len": 18}}}, "butterflies": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "Oh , Bertie , Bertie ! \u2019 she cried , bursting into tears ; no , no ! don \u2019 t let us kill him ! Only think , Bertie , it said in uncle \u2019 s book that butterflies live for several days with pins run through them", "context_len": 49}}}, "cocoon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Noting a small crab of the same yellowish color as the weed , he deduced erroneously but with logic that the crab came from the vegetable animals as a butterfly comes from a cocoon", "context_len": 34}}}, "acted": {"freq": 78, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 75, "word_index": 1, "context": "He acted , however , himself , in this high position , with great energy and with complete success ; and , at the same time , with all that modesty of deportment , and that delicate consideration for the officers under him -- who , though inferior in rank , were yet his superiors in age and experience -- which his position rendered proper , but which few persons so young as he would have manifested in circumstances so well calculated to awaken the feelings of vanity and elation", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 63, "context": "I do not mean that there was no law for the boys , for it was manifest to their terror in two officers whom they knew as constables , and who may have reigned one after another , or together , with full power of life and death over them , as they felt ; but who in a community mainly so peaceful acted upon Dogberry 's advice , and made and meddled with rogues as little as they could", "context_len": 80}}}, "leafy": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "Everything portended it ; the atmosphere was absolutely still , not a twig or even a leaf stirred , all nature seemed to be waiting in breathless suspense for the coming outbreak ; even the insects had ceased to attack us , and had retired to their leafy retreat , and the air was so heavy and close that , naked as I was , I perspired at every pore", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` If only Miss Eloise could be here to see us , '' said Edna as she peeped from behind the leafy screen which hid the flower fairies from view", "context_len": 30}}}, "marks": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 5, "context": "Clavering having reckoned up the marks , Mademoiselle Le Brun having given her testimony , Fraulein having given hers , and the English teachers having further testified to the industry of the pupils , the girls of the Upper school were to pass muster before Sir John , who was to decide without prejudice in favor of the lucky three who alone were to compete for the great Scholarship in October", "context_len": 71}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "Hitherto he had been steering to the south of east , following the line of coast , but he now saw before him the projecting promontory of Cape Mezurata , which marks the western entrance of the great Gulf of Sydra ; and he now directed his course two points north of east , so as to strike the opposite promontory , known as Grenna , more than a hundred miles away", "context_len": 72}}}, "horseshoes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "In the first place of all I am called a Horse-Chestnut tree because I have marks between my leaves and twigs which look like horseshoes with nails in them", "context_len": 29}}}, "dandelions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "One of them stopped to pick some dandelions", "context_len": 8}}}, "scolds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "No one scolds when you pick them", "context_len": 7}}}, "posies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "And , oh , how I love them ! Dear , soft , yellow posies , \u2019 she said", "context_len": 19}}}, "dandelion": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "There were all sorts of pistols ; but the commonest was one that the boys called a Christmas-crack ; it was of brass , and when it burst the barrel curled up like a dandelion stem when you split it and put it in water", "context_len": 45}}}, "lays": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then they shout to the overseer , and he lays his whip on our backs without mercy", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "And what would our Jennie do without the little sister that she has such a pride in and lays so many plans for ? Why , it would break her heart to lose her", "context_len": 34}}}, "leaf": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 13, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "becomes": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 36, "context": "Next only to the bailiff , I am the senior of our auberge , and -- but this is between ourselves , lad -- am like to succeed to the grand priory of England when it becomes vacant , and if not I shall , as the grand master has told me , have the offer of the next high office vacant in the palace", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` 'About that time a dispute arose , as to which was the oldest and which should rule ; and they agreed , as they were four Nations , they would set up four poles , and make them red with clay , which is yellow at first , but becomes red by burning", "context_len": 54}}}, "caterpillar": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "What a bright account you give of your dear busy young party ! Tell dear Otho that I shall be charmed if he makes the discovery of a magenta-coloured caterpillar , or a mauve earwig ; and that as it will be ten times as curious as the Spongmenta Padella , it ought to have a Latin name ten times as long", "context_len": 62}}}, "chrysalis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then the caterpillar becomes a chrysalis and soon a butterfly", "context_len": 10}}}, "sips": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` This '' was a cup of hot milk of which Edna tried to take a few sips and then lay back on her pillow", "context_len": 25}}}, "crawling": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "I \u2019 ve been a long time coming to you , flower , but the hard crawling journey had a wonderful ending", "context_len": 22}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 42, "context": "It was not easy to keep the board from drifting out , but along the shallows it was quiet water and it did not go so very far , and before long , the little dog was able to reach it , crawling upon it and shivering while he wagged his tail feebly as Edna continued to cheer him", "context_len": 59}}}, "sip": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "In those days the custom of drinking healths \u2019 had gained sway to an absurd and objectionable extent ; gentlemen being expected to respond to every toast , and not only to sip their wine , but very often to empty their glasses , under pain of giving serious offence", "context_len": 50}}}, "tacks": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "The walls were adorned with school drawings and prize maps , fastened up with tacks ; a bookcase of much-read volumes filled the space between the fireplace and the window ; and in the corner might be found a miscellaneous collection of cricket-bats , fishing-rods , tennis-racquets , bows and arrows , croquet-mallets , sticks , balls and ninepins , and other articles very dear to the children 's hearts , but which Nancy generally classed impartially under the head of 'lumber", "context_len": 82}}}, "metallic": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 15, "context": "The term im\u00fakfa is Hitchiti , for ( 1 ) shell ; ( 2 ) metallic ornament of concave shape ; Hawkins interprets the name by `` gorget made of a conch", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Another tale is , that they did not originate on this continent ; that when they arrived from their country they landed at the `` Jagged Rock , '' tch\u00e1to tcha \u00e0 a ` l\u00e1ko , and brought the metallic plates with them , which they preserve to the present day with anxious care", "context_len": 54}}}, "metal": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 91, "context": "or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length , as well because it is an important one as to show the almost insuperable difficulties that beset the", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Indians use the bare hand and arm -- utensils in common stable use among us whites are the metal curry-combs , bristle body-brushes , corn-brushes , rub rags , sponges , whisks and hoof picks", "context_len": 35}}}, "tack": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 42, "context": "The outline of all these kites was given , and the sticks stayed in place by a string carried taut from stick to stick , which was notched at the ends to hold it ; sometimes the sticks were held with a tack at the point of crossing , and sometimes they were mortised into one another ; but this was apt to weaken them", "context_len": 65}}}, "rightly": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 12, "context": "Aunt Helen was very long-suffering with respect to hobbies , for she rightly thought that the more a child 's life is filled with interests , the more chance it has of growing up an intelligent and broad-minded individual , and of escaping from that lethargy of boredom which swallows up the lives of many young people who ought to know better how to amuse themselves in God 's beautiful world", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 80, "context": "I often says there ai n't a better husband and father to be found than Sergeant Richards , look the city through ; and you do deserve the best , that 's a fact , if it was gold and diamonds ; not that you would n't have a better use for them than to eat 'em ; diamonds fetches a heap , they tell me , but never havin ' had none of my own , I ca n't rightly tell of my own showin '", "context_len": 87}}}, "bah": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Bah ! such glory , such honor , such a proud moment , and to say you are miserable ! May I ask what you are miserable about ? ''", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Here them ole cadets went an ' fooled Mark Mallory again , an ' you -- bah ! ''", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Dye know , I ca n't see why we plebes suffer so , bah Jove ! I was thinking aw , do n't ye know , if some of us would band together we could -- aw -- chastise the deuced cadets and -- -- ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "banquet": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 35, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Washing-day was a perfect feast for it , for then it would banquet on the shirt-sleeves and stockings that dangled from the clothes-line , and simply glut itself with the family linen and cotton", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It is a banquet hall ! ''", "context_len": 7}}}, "misunderstanding": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Moon , said the Fairy Queen , I am so glad you told me what the trouble was , for if you hadn \u2019 t told me we might have had a very sad misunderstanding", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Although the troops on board were going to fight a common foe -- a foe that victorious would speedily prove more than a menace to Australia , to United South Africa , and to civilization in general -- there was a certain amount of misunderstanding between the Afrikanders and their brothers-in-arms", "context_len": 51}}}, "trowel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "It took Joe a considerable time to clear the stone , though Bobby went to his aid with a trowel ; but he got it free at last , and Peggy stooped down curiously to examine it", "context_len": 37}}}, "ferns": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 60, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Precious little of the sun did we see , and it was necessary to keep one 's eyes on the path which wound up and down into dark green hollows where beds of maidenhair fern flourished , or up to hilly spots where rock ferns grew in patches on enormous boulders", "context_len": 51}}}, "fernery": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Every spring she would add to her fernery , and the ferns that she had planted the year before came up more beautifully than ever", "context_len": 25}}}, "watering": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "Perhaps Maggie and Fred might not have kept their plots in such good order , or worked so perseveringly at weeding , raking , and watering , had it not been for Harry and Bessie , who never forgot to go each day and look at their plants , and never suffered a weed to do more than show the tip of its head above the soil", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Oh ! no , and I can have satis -- fac -- tion in my garden any way , mamma , '' said Bessie , `` in 'tending to it and watering it ; and then I can give my flowers to you and Aunt May and every one else I love , and that will be enough of pleasure for me", "context_len": 63}}}, "moisture": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 65, "context": "Had Ben set the fire at first along the whole line of the brush , there would have been burnt ground between him and the mass of fire , which would have cut off the communication , and he would have been safe ; but he set it on one corner , and when the wind shifted , the flame driven by it dried the moisture from the grass , and made rapid progress towards the house , while a large strip of dry grass made a bridge for the fire to travel on", "context_len": 94}}}, "watered": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "After she had planted her ferns she watered them some more , for she was very careful that her ferns should be planted just right , and that is why she had such good luck with them", "context_len": 37}}}, "transplant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "She was very particular , you may be sure , to get nice young ones , for they are the best to transplant", "context_len": 23}}}, "bluish": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "There was one great hill that almost rose to the dignity of a mountain , and from this a bluish vapor or smoke kept constantly rising and spreading out in the shape of a fan", "context_len": 35}}}, "chirping": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "They sing or make a little chirping sound as their other swallow cousins do", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 78, "context": "He found three thrushes ' nests low down in the elder-bushes , all filled with gaping yellow mouths , the pretty little chaffinch 's up in the ivy-tree , with only two speckly eggs as yet , and Jenny Wren 's household , hidden away in a bank , full of so many children that she surely resembled the old woman who lived in a shoe , and it was a marvel how she could remember which little chirping atom she had fed last", "context_len": 84}}}, "barns": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 74, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}}}, "swallows": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 54, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Aunt Helen was very long-suffering with respect to hobbies , for she rightly thought that the more a child 's life is filled with interests , the more chance it has of growing up an intelligent and broad-minded individual , and of escaping from that lethargy of boredom which swallows up the lives of many young people who ought to know better how to amuse themselves in God 's beautiful world", "context_len": 71}}}, "telephones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "They say that they wish to be modern and up-to-date , and that as they can \u2019 t answer the telephones or call their friends up , they can sit on the telephone wires and feel they are part of a nice world !", "context_len": 44}}}, "overheard": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 49, "context": "He had no doubt that his betrayal was due to one of the gardeners , who had several times shown him signs of ill will , being doubtless jealous of the immunity he enjoyed from hard labour , and who must , he thought , have crept up and overheard some conversation ; but in that case it was singular that the fact of his knowledge of Turkish had not been mentioned", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "Though he was unaware , of course , that they had overheard his conversation with Dodds , he guessed from their downcast faces that they knew that he intended to desert them on his and Drusie 's birthday , and was not going near the fort", "context_len": 46}}}, "fashioned": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was made of the softest moss and lichens , fashioned together in the shape of a bottle with the neck downwards ; for the tit must have some place in which to bestow her long tail , and she builds her home to suit her person", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "The two loosely gripped hands in front , for be it remembered the method described was the old fashioned way of wrestling , and is still popular in many parts of the country", "context_len": 33}}}, "bicycles": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 58, "context": "They sat laughing over the rhymes and chattering as only schoolgirls know how until Aunt Helen came in to announce that a light repast of cake and lemonade awaited them in the dining-room , and to gently hint that , if Warford were to be reached before darkness had fallen , it was getting time for the six bicycles to be set in motion", "context_len": 64}}}, "vain": {"freq": 180, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 157, "word_index": 106, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 17, "context": "Her missile flew far above the man \u2019 s head , and , running backwards in a vain endeavour to catch it , he fell heavily into a large trough of pigs \u2019 mash , where , half-stunned by the force of his fall , he lay wallowing in the sticky mess , till he was helped out by his comrades , to the accompaniment of their jeers and laughter", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "How sagacious a line of conduct was this ! Most ardent and enthusiastic young men , in the circumstances in which he was placed , would have been elated and vain at their elevation , and would have replaced the old and well-tried servants of the father with personal favorites of their own age , inexperienced and incompetent , and as conceited as themselves", "context_len": 64}}}, "fright": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 114, "context": "`` Then she told her mother the queen all that had betided her that night and how they had taken her bridegroom , leaving her alone , and how after a little there came another youth and lay down in the place of her bridegroom , putting a sword between himself and her ; `` and in the morning '' [ quoth she ] `` he who had brought us thither returned and taking us up , carried us back to our place here : and hardly had he brought us hither and left us when my father the Sultan entered and I had neither heart nor tongue to answer him for stress of fright and trembling which possessed me", "context_len": 120}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Oh , dear ! '' exclaimed Aggie , `` I should die with fright if I were to meet even one", "context_len": 22}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "He was hastening in a direction opposite to them , but not at such a headlong pace , as though he had a certain dignity to maintain which forbade too much yielding to fright", "context_len": 34}}}, "fashions": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 52, "context": "As for Alaeddin , he left idleness and the commerce of striplings and took to consorting with grown men ; [ 316 ] nay , he would go every day to the market of the merchants and sit with the great and the small of them and question of the ways and fashions of commerce and the prices of articles of merchandise [ 317 ] and otherwhat", "context_len": 67}}}, "schemes": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 33, "context": "The strong sentiment of love and devotion with which he inspired all around him at the commencement of his career , was now gone , and his generals and officers were continually planning schemes to depose him from the power which he seemed no longer to have the energy to wield ; or , at least , Alexander was continually suspecting that such plans were formed , and he was kept in a continual state of uneasiness and anxiety in discovering and punishing them", "context_len": 84}}}, "cured": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 60, "context": "It was nearly noon when I was conducted back to my hut after my futile attempt to cure the king ; and it was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "He gathered some jaboty eggs which he ate raw , and which , with some cured meat he had brought with him from the cabin of Pipina , made a simple but satisfying meal", "context_len": 34}}}, "kangaroo": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 25, "context": "But one day when the animals were all to be given a special treat , little Fairy Silver Wings , who had heard of the kangaroo \u2019 s sickness , whispered to the other animals to leave him behind to twist his tail and moan while they had their game of ball", "context_len": 52}}}, "pretending": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 50, "context": "Bradford , coming in unexpectedly one day , actually found the old lady on her hands and knees , in a corner , playing the part of a horse eating hay from a manger ; while Franky , clothes-brush in hand , was , much to his own satisfaction , pretending to rub her down , making the hissing noise used by coachmen when they curry a horse , and positively refusing to allow his patient playfellow to rise", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "I believe they still sometimes burn corn in the West , when they are too far from a market to sell it at a paying price ; but it always seems a sin and a shame that in a state pretending to be civilized food should ever be destroyed when so many are hungry", "context_len": 54}}}, "moaning": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "This negro was of a gigantic stature , with powerful limbs , yet of a timorous disposition , so that directly the gale came on he could with difficulty be made to do any work at all , but lay in a heap in the weather scuppers , moaning and muttering in broken English , Spanish , and his native tongue", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "In a short time he heard a dull moaning sound , a puff of wind struck the boat , but in a few seconds died out ; it was sufficient to give the light craft stern way , and she drifted backwards , the rope tightening , until the spar lay across her bows , and some twenty yards away", "context_len": 60}}}, "strongest": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 77, "context": "As it was , during her husband 's lifetime she thought of no one but him , and since his death her best thoughts were devoted to his memory : to keep flowers always on his grave ; to see that his portrait was dusted day after day , and that flowers were put under it ; to kneel there and utter prayers that he and she might be reunited in a better world , absorbed her strongest thoughts", "context_len": 79}}}, "twist": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 36, "context": "When Morn began to pick himself weakly up , with eyes of pathetic bewilderment , they helped him find his cap , and tried to engage him in conversation , for the pleasure of seeing him twist his mouth when he said , of a famous town drunkard whom he admired , `` He 's a strong man ; he eats liquor", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 112, "context": "would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart of your enemy", "context_len": 128}}}, "moan": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "The limbs of the great tree swayed gently in the westerly breeze , and the moonbeams came slanting through them most delightfully , as the boys lay listening to the moan of the night wind , the sound of the surf along the shore , and watched the clouds as they coursed by the moon , all heightened by the novelty of their situation", "context_len": 64}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The wind began to moan , as if a storm were at hand", "context_len": 13}}}, "howling": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then the kangaroo set up a howling such as never had been heard in animal land , for he didn \u2019 t want to miss a party just as Fairy Silver Wings had said ; so when they all thought he had learned a good lesson they went back for him", "context_len": 51}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 71, "context": "'s evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and sent such cold shivers running down their backs that they grew almost too frightened to go to bed", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Because I know the secrets of the prison house -- knew them before the Danes had murdered the once happy dwellers in this garden of England , which they have made a howling wilderness ; hence I escaped the wrath of the furious parricide , whom the saints destroy , with ease , and laughed in security at their vain efforts to take me ; but we must waste no time ; it yet wants five hours to daybreak ; within those five hours we must reach the opposite shore", "context_len": 91}}}, "snubbed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "But he went to Rugby , and at Christmas he brought some of his school-fellows back with him , and they paid the most absurd attentions to Florence and Janet , and they snubbed me ; and I suppose Roger , poor dear ! was weak enough to be influenced by them , for he took no notice of me either , so you can just imagine what a bad time I had", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "At school Dodds was an immensely popular fellow , and a new and comparatively small boy , as Hal was , would have been very much snubbed if he had ventured to say a word against him", "context_len": 37}}}, "foolishness": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 72, "context": "`` For the sake of him who hath even now rendered up himself as a martyr for the Lord 's cause , I may not be angry with any word of yours , '' he answered sadly ; `` but I do entreat of you to take heed ! Would you lay down your arms and live in peace among your cattle and your corn , coached and complimented into effeminacy and foolishness ? Oh , for shame ! Rub your eyes and look about you ! What was the fate of the men of Sodom when they thought Lot was one that mocked when he warned them ! ''", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Mark declined , however , saying that he was quite well ; and soon after , in spite of the opposition he met with from the master , who said it was foolishness , Harry Paul plunged overboard , and swam to the bathing-place , where he dressed ; and , saving that he was suffering from a peculiar sensation of stiffness , he was not much the worse", "context_len": 69}}}, "clover": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Clover loved to hear her name", "context_len": 6}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 48, "context": "She was full of plans for making the fight a better and more exciting one than any they had yet had , and she was suggesting a scheme by which Tommy could act both as scout and advanced outpost , when a strong , delicious scent from the clover field was wafted towards them on the soft summer , breeze", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "And the rabbits sniffed and their little noses wiggled and trembled as they told each other what a nice world it was with children and clover both in it !", "context_len": 30}}}, "paler": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 12, "context": "Mabel and Alice Cunningham were in soft pink dresses , a little paler in shade than the cherry-colored ribbons which as a matter of course they would wear , and one and all of the girls of the Upper school were becomingly and suitably dressed , with the exception of poor Florence ; but Florence 's muslin dress was coarse in texture and badly made , and notwithstanding the soft cherry-colored ribbons , she did not look her best", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "His face was paler than usual , and there was a grimness about his lips that Tom seldom saw", "context_len": 19}}}, "fluff": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Joe drew aside his jacket , and disclosed to the children 's delighted eyes the sweetest little round fuzzy ball of black fluff , just like a tiny woolly bear , with tan chest and paws , and a wagging morsel of a tail like a black tassel", "context_len": 48}}}, "hides": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "' '' Judge not the Lord by feeble sense , But trust Him for His grace : Behind a frowning providence He hides a smiling face", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 26, "context": "There they agreed to go to war for four years against their first enemy ; they made shields [ 143 ] , tup\u0115l\u00fakso , of buffalo hides and it was agreed , that the warriors of each town should dry and bring forward the \u00edka h\u00e1lbi or scalps of the enemy and pile them ; the \u00c1bika had a small pile , the Chicasa were above them , the Kawita above them , and the Kasi ta above all", "context_len": 79}}}, "naming": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` I grieve to say , '' he continued , `` that there is but one upon whom our suspicions can now fall with any shadow of probability -- one who is now absent , for I thought it well not to summon him to this council ; and before naming him , I must recall to you , Elfwyn , and to you , Herstan , the solemn oath we have all three taken to disregard all appeals of natural affection , and to ascertain the truth , God being our helper", "context_len": 93}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I do n't see the use o ' naming 'em", "context_len": 10}}}, "aigrette": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Heron , that children will remember when they grow up themselves not to wear aigrette feathers which destroy the lives of birds who want to live and care for their little ones", "context_len": 32}}}, "mates": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 55, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Groans and yelps answered him , as the tent mates moved their sore bodies and found them more painful than the night before", "context_len": 23}}}, "starvation": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 38, "context": "Next summer , Franklin descended the river to its mouth , and embarking in canoes he and his followers made towards Behring Strait , from which they were ere long driven back by their old dread enemy -- starvation", "context_len": 39}}}, "club": {"freq": 138, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 136, "word_index": 94, "context": "Edna thought it was , and now that she was ready to pop into bed , Reliance left her with a happy `` Good-night ! '' It was like sunshine in the house to have such a dear little girl as Edna , she thought as she went downstairs , and though Amanda reprimanded her sharply for not being in bed , she did not answer back , for , in fact , she scarcely heard her , so busy was she with pleasant thoughts , and so excited over the idea of the club", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Your little club meets on Friday afternoon , does n't it ? I will meet you and Celia at the station in time for the four-thirty train , which is the one you usually take , is n't it ? ''", "context_len": 41}}}, "herons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}}}, "plumes": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "In the trunk were lots and lots of lovely dresses a pink dress , a yellow satin dress , a green velvet coat , a hat with soft , big plumes on it , and , oh , so many lovely things ! Kay was breathless for a moment , she was so excited all those gorgeous things for a little girl who had seen little else beside fishing-nets and such things", "context_len": 72}}}, "meetings": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 34, "context": "He spent a busy time in the Crimea , doing plenty of hard work in the trenches ; and when off duty engaged in hospital visiting , tract and book distributing , attending prayer meetings and mission services , constant in his Bible reading , and always endeavouring to do good to others", "context_len": 53}}}, "refreshments": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Why you see , '' Edna began , `` Nettie has been taken into the club , and when her time comes to have the club meeting she wo n't have any way of getting the refreshments , so we thought and thought of what we could do to get some money , and last night I saw in the Children 's Corner of the Times that they would give prizes for guessing a puzzle , you know those puzzles , Cousin Ben", "context_len": 84}}}, "milked": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 42, "context": "The little black cow with the long horns looked strange to eyes accustomed to the large red and white cows at the Abbey , but her milk seemed sweet and good , and the woman sang a song in Welsh while she milked it , to a strange , haunting kind of melody that , like most of the Celtic music , had a touch of sadness and pathos about it", "context_len": 71}}}, "bossy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Betty always milked her own bossy herself and would carry into the house twice a day a bucket of milk", "context_len": 20}}}, "bucket": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 17, "context": "It would try to butt them into the well when they leaned down to pull up the bucket from the curb ; and if it came out of the house , and saw a boy cracking nuts at the low flat stone the children had in the back-yard to crack nuts on , it would pretend that the boy was making motions to insult it , and before he knew what he was about it would fly at him and send him spinning head over heels", "context_len": 86}}}, "pasture": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 5, "context": "Beyond the hedge lies a pasture meadow , where a flock of sheep are grazing , and encircling the meadow another hedge , less closely clipped , and so making bold to riot here and there in a snowy wealth of hawthorn blossom , A fine Alderney cow , with coat as well cared for as the gray mare 's in the stable , is also enjoying the sweet grass of the meadow , and the shining milk , pans ranged beneath the kitchen window bear witness to the generous service she renders", "context_len": 93}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "Just now , too , as Alejandro Vigil , a ragged lad with a scarlet cap on his black head , went by , driving his goats to pasture , he had said `` rogue ! '' under his breath", "context_len": 40}}}, "stall": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 49, "context": "Yes , I 'm tired , too , Marie-Celeste admitted ; but we 're ever so much obliged , it 's been very interesting ; but look here , Donald , before we go , I want to show you something , and she led the way to a stall of one of the knights", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Champ , take him up to pony stall number 8 and dry and blanket him , and above all tie him", "context_len": 22}}}, "racer": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "You see , with the cow following the little boy \u2019 s crooked route , the snake could not keep up , but got way behind , for the black racer snakes lose so much time in going from side to side that that is the way to escape them", "context_len": 50}}}, "wire": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 17, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "The thing I \u2019 ve got to do , Matt , is to wire the colonel that I \u2019 m on deck and ready to look after the bullion", "context_len": 29}}}, "adore": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Miss Carter is quite well and happy at the Singletons ' , and they just adore her , and Irene ought to apologize to her", "context_len": 26}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` I adore roosters , '' said Dallas obligingly ; then he began to laugh so violently that old Daddy started , gave him a reproachful glance and flew back to his place on the roost between two of the fattest hens", "context_len": 42}}}, "wits": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` `` O my son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not", "context_len": 128}}}, "bucketful": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "He walked off to a distance to get it in perspective , and the boys ventured so close to the paints which he had standing about by the bucketful that it seemed as if he must surely hollo at them", "context_len": 40}}}, "doubly": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 57, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "neighboring": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 50, "context": "They could follow the game over any ground , and spear the fish in any stream they chose , so that hunger never entered their wigwams ; and in course of time they became so puffed up with their good fortune that they called themselves 'gods , ' and the neighboring tribes bowed and worshipped them", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "The lawyer wrote that there happened to be in court a suit concerning the boundaries of an old Spanish land grant , which , it was claimed , extended north of the Purgatory River , and touched upon her own and other neighboring property", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Nay even mine is brighter than yours , '' cried a fly from a neighboring rose", "context_len": 17}}}, "charging": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 50, "context": "He tells me that he has heard from Ben Ibyn , the Berber , that you are a slave , and has asked me to inquire of the sultan the price that he will take for your ransom , expressing his willingness to pay whatever may be demanded , and charging me to defray the sum and to make arrangements by which you may return to Europe", "context_len": 67}}}, "rocky": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 20, "context": "What a noisy , merry , frolicksome stream this was , gurgling and splashing , rushing and tumbling in its rocky bed ; now leaping gracefully in a miniature waterfall over some narrow ledge , now rippling and singing about the roots of the trees and over the pebbles that lay in its course , now flashing in the sunlight , and now hiding in a crevice of the rocks as if it were playing at Bopeep", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "While they were looking about for a place to get up the rocky , steep shore , they stumbled upon this cave , and determined to explore it", "context_len": 28}}}, "suggestion": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 79, "word_index": 23, "context": "Certain tones in the man 's hearty voice reminded me of the boy 's sweet notes , and was n't there a queer suggestion of each other when I stood near them ? We Shetland ponies as I have said before are very close to human beings for our ancestors were literally brought up with the children in the crofters ' huts , and my mother has often told me that her mother had many a lick at the family platter and many a time her soft muzzle was buried in the children 's necks", "context_len": 95}}}, "piping": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "And such good things as they had ! They had cocoa which was piping hot , because it had been heated in a kettle on a bonfire which they had made as soon as they had arrived", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "' Little Jimmy Carson recited 'The Charge of the Light Brigade ' in a high , thin , piping voice , standing on tiptoe in his eagerness to give due effect to the famous lines , flinging out his arms wildly to indicate where", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "We had baked beans for supper , an \u2019 I \u2019 ve left the pot in the oven , so they \u2019 re piping hot", "context_len": 25}}}, "sandwiches": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 87, "context": "`` The fire was THERE '' , pointing with her finger , `` an ' the table was before it ; an ' the lamp was there , an ' the light looked rosy red ; an ' there was a satin cover on your bed , an ' a warm rug on the floor , an ' everythin ' looked beautiful ; an ' '' -- she paused a second , and laid her hand on her stomach tenderly -- '' there WAS soup an ' sandwiches an ' muffins -- there WAS", "context_len": 94}}}, "snowing": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 50, "context": "They tried to induce Fenn to accompany them , but he was firm in his determination to solve the `` turtle mystery , '' as he called it , and , in the end , Bart , Ned and Frank tramped off through the storm , for it was still snowing , while the stout lad remained behind , watching the turtle , which he had placed on a cleared place on the ground in front of the tent", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Not that they did not enjoy it when it was cold and snapping , or even snowing , but they could do much more when the weather was milder", "context_len": 29}}}, "substance": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 78, "context": "O misery ! to wake in darkness , wake From vision of unutterable joy ; Instead of trumpet-sound and song of Heaven , To hear the dull clock measuring out time , When I had seemed to touch Eternity ! In the first pang of disappointed hope , I wept that I could wake from such a dream ; Until Faith gently whispered , Wherefore weep To lose the faint dim shadow of a joy Of which the substance shall one day be thine ? Live in the hope , -- that hope shall brighten life , And sanctify it to its highest end", "context_len": 104}}}, "satin": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 16, "context": "In the trunk were lots and lots of lovely dresses a pink dress , a yellow satin dress , a green velvet coat , a hat with soft , big plumes on it , and , oh , so many lovely things ! Kay was breathless for a moment , she was so excited all those gorgeous things for a little girl who had seen little else beside fishing-nets and such things", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` The fire was THERE '' , pointing with her finger , `` an ' the table was before it ; an ' the lamp was there , an ' the light looked rosy red ; an ' there was a satin cover on your bed , an ' a warm rug on the floor , an ' everythin ' looked beautiful ; an ' '' -- she paused a second , and laid her hand on her stomach tenderly -- '' there WAS soup an ' sandwiches an ' muffins -- there WAS", "context_len": 94}}}, "subjects": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 66, "context": "wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he could reach , in most uncourtly fashion", "context_len": 128}}}, "sunsets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "There was a little boy , said daddy , whose name was Harry , and he loved sunsets and everything that had bright colors", "context_len": 24}}}, "disgraceful": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 49, "context": "This change of mind was symbolized by the custom of the women of breaking to pieces all the household utensils of the past year , and replacing them by new ones ; the men refitted all their property so as to look new , and it was considered extremely disgraceful , even for the most indigent , to eat any of the new maize before the annual busk ( Sketch , pp", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Hartley , who , enveloped in an old-fashioned , large-patterned dressing-gown , had arrived rather tardily upon the scene , and had stood for several seconds glaring down at the two disgraceful specimens", "context_len": 33}}}, "owls": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 43, "context": "Some he said were telling how a monster rattlesnake had come to visit them without any invitation , and that the only food he would eat , was the youngest and fattest of their families ; and that their constant intruders , the owls , had the same carnivorous tastes , besides which they rendered themselves particularly disagreeable , by standing in the doors and staring at every dog that went by , and even preventing the entrance of visitors , to the great distress of all the belles and beaux in town", "context_len": 93}}}, "arrangement": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 80, "context": "The company on board The Revenge was gradually getting less and less ; the Spanish ships , meanwhile , as they received a sufficient evidence of The Revenge's powers of destruction , dropped off , and their places were taken by others ; and thus it happened that ere the morning fifteen ships had been engaged , and all were so little pleased with the entertainment provided that they were far more willing to listen to proposals for an honourable arrangement than to make any more assaults", "context_len": 87}}}, "fireflies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "You see he had made a special arrangement to have his own lights , and when he said that he hadn \u2019 t enough , from all over came countless little fireflies", "context_len": 32}}}, "sparkled": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 23, "context": "Yet , although in appearance a very Methuselah in age , this individual had a pair of piercing black eyes that glowed and sparkled with all the fire and passion of early manhood , and , bowed as he was , and decrepit as he appeared to be , he tottered across the intervening space with extraordinary agility , and seated himself in the second chair", "context_len": 66}}}, "spectacles": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 56, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}}}, "picnics": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 52, "context": "Easter will soon be here , and Archie will come home , and then we shall all have glorious times again ! ' And Peggy 's momentary discontent vanished like snow in sunshine at the enthralling prospect of several new projects which her ingenious friend intended carrying out , and of the picnics , woodland scrambles , and other delights which the holidays would bring in their train", "context_len": 68}}}, "sparkling": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 45, "context": "Miss Wauton \u2019 s first impressions of her , jotted down as follows , do not give the impression of a very old lady , dearly as Charlotte Tucker loved to describe herself in those terms : Tall , slight , with lofty brow , sparkling eye , face constantly beaming with love and intelligence ; genius in every look ; figure frail and fairy-like , agile and graceful ; very brisk movements and light tread", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 43, "context": "It was at one of these times , when Maggie , Bessie , and Frankie were all enjoying themselves as much as three happy children could do ; with shoes and stockings off , pattering about with bare feet in the cool , sparkling water , while mamma , grandmamma and Aunt Patty sat with their work upon the piazza , that the little fellow secured his prize", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}}}, "wing": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 12, "context": "Among other things were two or three peacock feathers , a turkey wing , some pebbles from the brook where papa used to sail his boats when he was a boy , a piece of rusty tin pipe , which , because it looked black and smoky , and came from the field where the burnt barn had stood , they persuaded themselves must be a part of the very leader down which papa slid when he ran for the ladder to save his little brother , -- all these , and other treasures of like value , were carefully collected and stowed in the wagons , to be carried to the Lake House , and thence to the city", "context_len": 120}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Why , you are looking as fresh as possible ; and Miss Irene -- the wonderful Miss Irene '' -- here he gave a mocking bow to Irene -- '' has taken you under her wing", "context_len": 37}}}, "toasting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "You have done things uncommonly well , and now you 'll just have the goodness to sit with your feet on the fender in the drawing-room , and give yourself a right good toasting while Miss Wickham and I are examining the house", "context_len": 43}}}, "stage": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 57, "context": "Among the twenty millions of people in the United States , there are perhaps two millions , between the ages of fifteen and twenty-five , who wish to become acquainted , in general , with the leading events in the history of the Old World , and of ancient times , but who , coming upon the stage in this land and at this period , have ideas and conceptions so widely different from those of other nations and of other times , that a mere republication of existing accounts is not what they require", "context_len": 95}}}, "coach": {"freq": 128, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 125, "word_index": 5, "context": "Dick hurried her into the coach with all speed , and signed to his servant to lead his horse down a retired alley , but the aspect of the gathering crowd was so threatening , and that of his attendant saints so grim , that he began to suspect that his only escape from being stoned by the unbelieving mob , or run through by a Fifth Monarchy corporal , would be to be laid by the heels in a city jail !", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "By the way , a special master is coming twice a week now to coach all three of you", "context_len": 19}}}, "harnesses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "There were eight white horses with big red plumes standing up from their harnesses , which made them look very fine indeed", "context_len": 22}}}, "footmen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "They left the coach on the side of the cliff , and then the horses , coachman , footmen , Fairies , Gnomes , Billy and all , found nice little parts of the Tipping Bird \u2019 s wings to rest in", "context_len": 42}}}, "gallop": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 47, "context": "He had a notion that if he could get up a chariot by sawing down the sides of a store-box for the body , and borrowing the hind-wheels of the baby 's willow wagon , and then , drawn by the family dog Tip at a mad gallop , come suddenly whirling round the corner of the school-house , wearing spangled circus-tights and bearing Apollo 's bow and shaft , while a silken scarf which he had seen in a bureau-drawer at home blew gallantly out behind him , it would have a fine effect with the boys", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 46, "context": "After a plunge down the road , the rider would succeed in turning the animal \u2019 s head up again ; but he would no sooner have got a fair start in that direction , than the horse , swinging suddenly around , would begin to gallop furiously down the road once more toward the Gaston farm", "context_len": 57}}}, "flourish": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "The old man continued to splutter and snarl and flourish his cane , upon which Tuttle and Cooper made a pretense of skurrying around in great haste for rocks to pelt him with , and he beat a hasty retreat toward his wretched hovel", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` That 's my name , '' said the boy with a polite flourish of his helmet ; `` and I hear , '' glancing round at them all with an amused twinkle in his eyes , `` that none of you like me", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was only when her mother asked her one day , `` Has anyone invited Clara Adams to the great meeting of the club when you are to wind up the year with such a flourish ? '' that her conscience began to prick her", "context_len": 45}}}, "gruff": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "Thus they came upon us in the very nick of time , for I judge that the Federal officer would have held us prisoners , in spite of the information we had for him , for he was very gruff and surly", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "He was an ugly old fellow , and rather gruff and unsociable ; but , like some people , he was in reality better than he appeared", "context_len": 27}}}, "trips": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 35, "context": "William Lang told of his visit to Oak Swamp , and how he had fled at the sight of the boys , fearing they wanted to cause his arrest , and he also mentioned his trips to the mud volcano , and how he had run away at the sound of some one stirring in the campers ' tent , likewise how he had led the boys a chase about the town , just prior to the accident on the pole", "context_len": 81}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "He can hardly walk , he is so drunk , and the clown has to help him across the ring-board , and even then he trips and rolls over on the sawdust , and has to be pulled to his feet", "context_len": 41}}}, "daring": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 125, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 104, "context": "Their names were Mabel and Alice Cunningham , two handsome dark-eyed girls , aged respectively seventeen and fifteen ; Florence Aylmer , who was also fifteen and the romp of the school ; Mary Bateman , a stolid-looking girl of fourteen ; Bertha Kennedy , who had only lately been raised to the rank of the Upper school ; Edith King , a handsome , graceful girl , who competed with Mabel for the honors of the head of her class ; and Kitty Sharston , who had only lately come , and who had some Irish blood in her , and was very daring and very much inclined to break the rules", "context_len": 113}, "NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 70, "context": "My dear Caretto , the grand master exclaimed , it is almost a resurrection , for we have all long mourned you as dead ; and your return to us at the present time is indeed fortunate ; for upon whose judgment and aid could I better rely than those of my old comrade in arms ? Then , turning to Gervaise , he went on : It was a daring and brilliant exploit indeed , Sir Gervaise , and in due time honour shall be paid to you and your brave companions , to whom and to you I now tender the thanks of the Order", "context_len": 107}}}, "cliffs": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 21, "context": "At the bottom of the meadows which bounded the Abbey land the river took a sharp turn past a few bold cliffs which rose almost sheer out of the water , and by scrambling along the rocks at the base it was possible to get round this headland and reach the low entrance of the cave , which was raised only a few feet above the level of the river", "context_len": 70}}}, "narrowest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "I wish that the cross old gentleman who bequeathed my father Grimhaggard Hall , had lived to repent it , that I do ! I would rather live in the narrowest lane in the City than be cooped up here like a toad in a block", "context_len": 46}}}, "steepest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Porter said they had passed the steepest part of the ascent , the gentlemen and boys all did the same", "context_len": 20}}}, "cliff": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 32, "context": "Up the steep bank they trudged , through fields of bracken higher than Bobby 's head , getting out at last upon a white road that wound along the top of a cliff , with a stream roaring below , till round a sudden turn they came upon Pengarth , a prim little village , nestling in a hollow , and consisting of a short street and a big chapel and school-house", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "But there was no time for mournful reveries on the untimely end of our noble craft and her gallant captain , for already the exploring party had returned with the news that the cliff path had been found , and that a village was not far distant", "context_len": 47}}}, "wand": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 28, "context": "At last the trying-on was completed , the old dresses discarded and put away , and Florence came downstairs in her travelling serge , wondering if a fairy wand had been passed over her , and if she were indeed the same girl who had arrived at Dawlish a week ago", "context_len": 51}}}, "avenues": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "The vast throng then rolled along upon the top of the wall till they came to stairways and slopes by which they could descend into the city , and , pouring down through all these avenues , they spread over the streets , and satiated the hatred and rage , which had been gathering strength for seven long months , in bursting into houses , and killing and destroying all that came in their way", "context_len": 75}}}, "driveways": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "We make shady avenues and streets and driveways", "context_len": 8}}}, "rustled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "The cottonwoods rustled crisply , Alejandro Vigil was calling to his dog , and the tinkle of his herd stole softly upon her ear", "context_len": 24}}}, "soot": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "A fine strong tree Cousin Norway is , and a tree that doesn \u2019 t mind soot and dust and smoke or insects", "context_len": 23}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 72, "context": "Finally he resolved to take matters into his own hands by suddenly slipping away from Marie-Celeste 's grasp ; and then drawing off a little , and folding both hands behind his back , as though neither of them were to be longer at anybody 's disposal , he said aggressively : And -- and now what are you afraid of , Marie-Celeste ? Do you sink somebody 's goin ' to soot you from de top of one of de towers if you speak out loud ?", "context_len": 88}}}, "clusters": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 25, "context": "The leaves had dropped from the trees overhead , and the branches outlined themselves against the blue sky , and dangled from their outer stems clusters of the unfallen fruit , as large as oranges , and only wanting a touch to send them plumping down into the grass where sometimes their fat hulls burst , and the nuts almost leaped into the boys ' hands", "context_len": 66}}}, "streaked": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "They began with the little acorns that they called chinquepins , and that were such a pretty black , streaked upward from the cup with yellow , that they gathered them half for the unconscious pleasure of their beauty", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "She had lost her hat , and the wind had tossed up her curls , her cheeks were pale and streaked with tears , and her big brown eyes had a pitiful look in them that would have softened a tiger , let alone a man that had half a dozen little ones of his own at home ; while every now and then the great heavy sighs came struggling up , as if she had almost cried her heart out", "context_len": 81}}}, "oftentimes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "In the winter oftentimes its bark is used by boys for whistles and in the autumn Cousin Striped Maple is yellow and very handsome", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "He not only gave his time , but his money too ; and oftentimes , though the eldest son of an earl , and later an earl himself , he hardly knew where to turn for the means to keep his schemes going", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I was obliged to go to church , but I was determined not to listen , and oftentimes when the preacher gave out the text I have stopped my ears and shut my eyes that I might neither see nor hear", "context_len": 42}}}, "whistles": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 64, "context": "any other among them promised to be on hand at the latest delivery on the afternoon succeeding it , so as to learn just what the issue had been , and at a time when he would be able to stop and tell about it ? Would any one else in the world have thought of suggesting that he should give three short little whistles when he reached the Browns ' , in Remsen Street , so that she should know just how near he was ? Surely no one ; and it was just this surpassing interest in every living body , to the utter forgetting of all that concerned herself , that made Marie-Celeste different from other children , that made everybody love her , and", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Lem whistles fustrate", "context_len": 4}}}, "parks": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "And you , too , confess , when you weary have been , At the end of your journey , wherever you roam ; That , tho ' houses , and parks , and fine rivers you 've seen , There 's no place so happy , so sweet as your home", "context_len": 52}}}, "greens": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Their tall Christmas-tree and beautiful greens were not more splendid in the eyes of Maggie and Bessie than were the scanty wreath and two foot high cedar branch , which a good-natured market-woman had given Mrs", "context_len": 36}}}, "seashore": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "Many put the cause of the disease , rightly enough , to the filthy habits of the poorer people , and hearing that salt water was a preventive , scores of the inhabitants repaired daily to the seashore , plunging into the bitterly cold waves in the hope of staving off the malady", "context_len": 53}}}, "rubber": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 10, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Rudolph will like to go , for he has his sou'wester and rubber boots , while as for me I am used to going out in all sorts of weather", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Well , '' remarked the proctor , trying hard not to laugh as he glanced at the student 's mud-encased trousers , `` I would advise you to wear rubber boots when you go on your next nature excursion", "context_len": 40}}}, "pumped": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "By day , when they are generally receiving more gas than they are giving out , they rise ; and again at night , when less is being pumped into them than is going out for consumption in the streets and houses , they fall", "context_len": 45}}}, "tires": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "They were made of rubber , I was told , and pumped up like automobile tires , and then they were covered with canvas", "context_len": 24}}}, "fins": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 55, "context": "His captive was , however , soon safe in the large basket , and he had hardly closed the lid and placed a boulder used as ballast upon it before a tug at his other line made the thole-pin rattle , and after a little hauling he dragged in a gloriously-coloured gurnard , whose outspread fins looked like the wings of some lovely butterfly", "context_len": 64}}}, "falls": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 78, "context": "The old , familiar words seemed to have new meaning in them , said in the midst of the darkness and the danger , and the children felt that , though their earthly Father might be seeking far and wide for them in vain , they were known and cared for by 'our Father which art in heaven , ' to whom the darkness is the same as daylight , and in whose sight not even a sparrow falls to the ground unnoticed", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 7, "context": "Guy had witnessed three or four such falls , and in his excitement scarcely knowing what he did , went up to the foaming animal intending to put it out of its misery by a shot from his gun , when , suddenly , it rose to its feet , staggered forward , and ere Guy could wheel his frightened horse , plunged his horns into his breast , and buffalo , horse and rider rolled upon the plain together", "context_len": 80}}}, "ducking": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 25, "context": "The boys are standing on the shore while the freshet rushes by , and then they are in the water , splashing , diving , ducking ; it is like that ; so that I do not know just how to get in that period of fishing which must always have come between", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 34, "context": "As we approached , the crowd , too intent to notice our coming , had seized the beam and were swinging it over the pond with the object of immersing the occupant of the ducking stool", "context_len": 36}}}, "imitations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "Maud 's supercilious manner slipped away from her like magic , and she became once more the pleasant Maud of the dancing-class , and her friends , feeling the change , soon left off trying to be poor little imitations of grown-up people , and began at last to enjoy themselves ; for true enjoyment does not consist in showing off , but in being our simple , natural selves , if people would only believe it", "context_len": 77}}}, "timid": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 27, "context": "What a sleepless night I have passed , what anxiety , what excitement ! and yet how unlike is he to what I had imagined ! so timid , so petulant ! and that perpetual punning ! It matters not , however , -- his title to our services remains the same ! A strange misgiving is on my soul ; is it the shadow of approaching danger , or only the fear of it ? The Colonel gave me a strange meaning look as he passed me this morning , and said , You are early up , Miss Ratty ; I fear that your rest was broken last night", "context_len": 111}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 52, "context": "The Almighty , to whom my brother attributes the glory , has hitherto watched over Benares in so marked a manner , that it remained quiet in the midst of disturbances ; and my young niece has bravely ridden through it by her father \u2019 s side , giving confidence to the timid by her fearlessness", "context_len": 56}}}, "bleated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "And Laura Lamb bleated and said : Ba-aaa-baa-aaa , Mother Sheep ! I will do as you say", "context_len": 18}}}, "donkey": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "I have strange habits , and what is more my voice is like a donkey \u2019 s", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 56, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}}}, "bray": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "I bray just the same way , \u2019 said the Black-Footed Penguin", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "You can \u2019 t bray as well as I can , \u2019 said the Donkey", "context_len": 15}}}, "commonplace": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 59, "context": "And , in the mean time , what a pleasure to talk familiarly with these same bright little visitors , who more likely than not would have retired into speechless embarrassment had anyone ventured the announcement that the great Queen of England was none other than the friendly old lady with whom they were taking all the liberties of commonplace , every-day acquaintance ! And so , happily , no doubt , for their ease of mind , no one felt called upon to make the announcement", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was rather heroic treatment for a convalescent , who was barely equal as yet to even commonplace modes of proceeding , but there was nothing else to be done if the secret was still to be kept", "context_len": 38}}}, "fussy": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "She was generally sound , with no especial delicacy ; she did not suffer from any tendency to headache ; she was not fussy , or self-indulgent , or dainty as to her eating , or particular as to personal comforts , or squeamish as to her surroundings , or shy in making new friends , or afraid of toil and trouble", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Colonel Harrington was red of face and fussy of manner", "context_len": 10}}}, "argument": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 14, "context": "To those friends who thought he lost caste thereby he had always the same argument -- that he saw no reason why the cultivation of fields should counteract the habits of refinement and good breeding to which he had been reared ; that in the colonies educated gentlemen set to work to labour with their hands , and are thought none the worse of : so why not in England , where land is good and markets are plentiful , especially when it involved the keeping of a fine old property which had been in the family for so many hundreds of years ?", "context_len": 104}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He was a stout fighting man , but unused to argument", "context_len": 11}}}, "decide": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 118, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}}}, "alack": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "The Monkeys performed tricks , they made faces and they ate peanuts which were given to them , and at the end of the day , alas and alack , every Animal had to admit that the one who received the most attention from the Children was the old Monkey and his family", "context_len": 53}}}, "mosquito": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 7, "context": "Well , friends , said the little mosquito , how about a banquet to-night ? It \u2019 s the first warm evening of the season , and without a doubt the people will sit out on their porches and enjoy the beautiful air", "context_len": 43}}}, "porches": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Well , friends , said the little mosquito , how about a banquet to-night ? It \u2019 s the first warm evening of the season , and without a doubt the people will sit out on their porches and enjoy the beautiful air", "context_len": 43}}}, "suppers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Of all the fires he built ; the oxen he fed ; the water he carried , and even the breakfasts and suppers he helped to cook", "context_len": 27}}}, "mosquitoes": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 26, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas must topis [ 92 ] retain , Must guard against sunstroke , to health such a bane ; And midst frogs and mosquitoes must patient remain , Yes , e \u2019 en when tormented , must smile through their pain ; And , with courage like that of the knights of Charlemagne , By Mission Miss Sahibas snakes should be slain", "context_len": 65}}}, "hornets": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "He was a friend to all the boys in town , and Bart joined him without ceremony as he found him out on the lawn in his skull cap and dressing gown , studying a hornets ' nest with a magnifying glass", "context_len": 42}}}, "piazza": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 79, "context": "it grew darker and darker , came forth another and another star , and looked down on the world which God had loved so much , till the whole sky was brilliant with them ; the soft , cool sea-breeze came gently in at the windows , bringing with it the gentle plash of the waves upon the shore , mingled with the chirp of the crickets and the distant hum of voices from the far end of the piazza ; but no one came near or disturbed them ; and still the colonel sat with his face turned towards the sea , without either speaking or moving , till his wife , as she sat with her hand in his , wondered if he could be asleep", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "Porter stood upon the piazza steps , wiping her eyes with her apron , as she watched the wagons going slowly past the lake , and carrying our friends down the mountain for the last time", "context_len": 36}}}, "spit": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Just taste of that , said Fred , giving Charlie a piece of shelly , yellowish rock , who , putting it to his mouth , instantly spit it out , saying that it tasted like copperas", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "The commotion being over , with the exception of an occasional spit from the drawer , as if kitty were still conscious of the presence of her foe , Bessie walked up to Mrs", "context_len": 34}}}, "dots": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "The marking of the linguistic areas by dots , pointing to the tribal settlements , answers much better the purpose than the coloring of large areas , which conveys the erroneous impression that the population was scattered all over a certain country", "context_len": 42}}}, "headway": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 89, "context": "Among the missiles hurled into the town were balls filled with Greek fire , but the houses being entirely built of stone , no conflagrations of importance were caused by them , as a band of knights was organised specially to watch for these bombs , and whenever one of them was seen to fall , they hurried from their lookout to the spot , with a gang of slaves carrying baskets of earth and buckets of water , and quenched the flames before they had made any great headway", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Frantic with terror , as he saw what headway the fire was making , he rushed from place to place in search of the missing ladder ; but all in vain ; it could not be found", "context_len": 37}}}, "grubs": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 47, "context": "When they had got them all heaped up like a pile of grape-shot at the foot of the tree , they began to hull them , with blows of a stick , or with stones , and to pick the nuts from the hulls , where the grubs were battening on their assured ripeness , and to toss them into a little heap , a very little heap indeed compared with the bulk of that they came from", "context_len": 78}}}, "compliments": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 32, "context": "I wager my head that they \u2019 ll both of them come over here and make it : what a good time they will have puttering over it , and passing their compliments upon each other ! It \u2019 s my opinion , that when them old men die they won \u2019 t be buried with their wives , but alongside of each other", "context_len": 64}}}, "staying": {"freq": 86, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 74, "context": "Now , to know all this was naturally a great comfort to Harry 's mother and sister , for they had of course been not a little anxious on Harry 's own account at the way things seemed to be going , but there was one thing they were content not to know for a while -- for the reason that Harry strongly urged it -- and that was where he and Ted were staying", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "So my return to Abingdon is indefinitely delayed , for Herstan and my sister both insist on my staying till he is out of danger , if God will ; and indeed I know no one else to whose care I could willingly commit him", "context_len": 45}}}, "mole": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 52, "context": "Gangs of men , as usual , worked in the breach ; but , as it was deemed advisable that there should be no outward show of activity that would lead the Turks to suspect that their design had been discovered , neither reinforcements of men nor munitions were sent along the mole ; everything being taken out by boats , which , rowing closely along under the wall , were hidden from the view of the Turks", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "This happened to be Gibraltar , which we reached after thirty-six hours of anxiety and arduous labour , and when the Gannet dropped anchor off the mole our feelings were those of relief and thanksgiving", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "passes": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 14, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 99, "context": "my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction , you shall meet with the fate you so justly deserve", "context_len": 128}}}, "grasses": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "But the boys gathered them every spring , in the pleasant open woods where they grew , just beyond the densest shade of the trees , among the tall , straggling grasses ; and they had that joyous sense of the bounty of nature in hoarding them up which is one of the sweetest and dearest experiences of childhood", "context_len": 59}}}, "meadow": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 55, "context": "Then , going into the ruins , she climbed up on to the old wall , from which the best view of the house and its surroundings could be obtained , and gazed with new eyes at the panorama of her home ; and as her glance travelled slowly from orchard to stackyard , from meadow to garden , back to the ivy-clad tower and the red gabled roof , ' I think , ' she said slowly , 'that if we had to leave the Abbey , it would break my heart ! '", "context_len": 95}}}, "burrow": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Down in this humpy corner , underneath the pile of bark and skins , he will burrow like a rabbit when he goes to sleep , and from the same place he will provide himself with a weapon when starting off for a hunt", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "But Master Archie evidently thought discretion the better part of valour , for he dived through the doorway like a rabbit into a burrow , his overwrought feelings so far overcoming him that he exploded into a tremendous cock-crow as he sought the friendly shelter of Sky Cottage", "context_len": 48}}}, "gloves": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 39, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}}}, "gingham": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 18, "context": "Edna tip-toed in quietly and felt on the nail where the key should hang , thrusting aside a gingham apron belonging to Reliance which hung just above its place , but the nail was empty and she was forced to believe she had dropped the key somewhere between the spring-house and the kitchen", "context_len": 53}}}, "laces": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 70, "context": "Their voices were subdued lest they should reach the king 's ears ; but , respectful as was the bearing of all the members of the group by the fire , they clearly split into two halves : on the one hand , the officers of the escort who were teasing the boy , and on the other , a group of gentlemen , some wearing the conventional ribbons and laces of a cavalier , others in the rough cloth of country wear , stained with the mud of country lanes , while the master and mistress of the house moved from one guest to another , evidently nervous at the doubtful honour that such a royal visit had brought to their roof", "context_len": 123}}}, "jumpers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Sometimes I think I 'd like to be the greatest home-run king in the world , and then when I see those high jumpers galloping over the roofs of houses in the movies I think I 'd like to be an actor", "context_len": 43}}}, "roadways": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "The timbers are of a hugeness to strike fear into the heart of the boldest little boy ; and there is something awful even about the dust in the roadways ; soft and thrillingly cool to the boy 's bare feet , it lies thick in a perpetual twilight , streaked at intervals by the sun that slants in at the high , narrow windows under the roof ; it has a certain potent , musty smell", "context_len": 77}}}, "appearing": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 48, "context": "About the first of June they were released , came back to Millville , found their old shack burned down , and since then , the postmaster understood , had camped out in the woods , giving the town a wide berth -- in fact , only occasionally appearing , to buy a little flour , sugar or coffee , or , mostly , tobacco", "context_len": 65}}}, "granted": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 22, "context": "And now , said Albert , appealing to the Queen , for he felt that quite too much was being taken for granted , will you please tell me what is a tilt-yard ? and what it was dat de knignts would not tome to ? and what was dat little white ting of the Queen 's dat de King carried ? and impatiently as Marie-Celeste brooked the interruption , there was nothing for it but to wait while Her Majesty explained that the tilt-yard was a sort of riding-school for the knights , where they practised for the tournaments , and that the tournaments were occasions when the knights , spear in hand , came together to ride against each other , with a great many people", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "He was no longer obliged to walk with the gang to and from the palace , and was at last granted permission to go into the town for an hour or two after his work was over , instead of returning direct to the prison", "context_len": 45}}}, "mud": {"freq": 169, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 160, "word_index": 114, "context": "coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to find the poor creature ; so that , his hair being wet , he was a sight to behold when Harry pulled him out , covered with mud from head to foot , and had to be sent behind in disgrace", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 6, "context": "That Lola should be engaged with mud casas was , indeed , hardly in accord with Jane 's experience of the girl 's dignity ; but that she should be playing ever so foolishly in a slush of clay delighted Jane as being a healthful symptom", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "That mud volcano water is partly salty", "context_len": 7}}}, "tittering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "My grandmother , the other party dress said very proudly , as she walked off tittering and giggling , was a Lady Lace and she went to court affairs where my grandfather , Lord Velvet , met her", "context_len": 38}}}, "giggling": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "There was a good deal of giggling and whispered conversation behind the scenes as the actors hastily concluded their preparations , but at length , in response to the enthusiastic clapping and stamping of the audience , the curtain was drawn aside by two Sunday-school teachers , and the play began", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "However , the trunks did arrive before they had need to borrow , and the bathing-suits were easily found and hastily put on amidst much giggling and many exclamations , for it was such a new excitement it was impossible to do anything soberly", "context_len": 44}}}, "court": {"freq": 84, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 84, "word_index": 93, "context": "It was a fine summer 's evening towards the end of the month of July , and the sun was slowly setting behind the wood-crowned range of hills in the west , where the forest terminated the pastures of Aescendune ; the cattle were returning to their stalls ; the last load of hay was being transferred from the wain to the rick , and all things spoke of the calm and rest of a sweet night , fragrant with the breath of honeysuckle and wild brier , when nature herself seems to court luxurious repose", "context_len": 96}}}, "outdoor": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Tall for my age , broad-shouldered and supple-limbed , I possessed an unusual amount of strength , and could bear fatigue in a manner that could only be accounted for by the fact that I had led an active outdoor life", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 16, "context": "To do Peggy justice , I think she was right , for though she delighted in outdoor life , she was in no sense a rough or ill-mannered child , and loved pretty things and dainty ways as well as quieter Lilian ; but it was a case of a dog with a bad name , for however indignantly she might remonstrate , people had got into the habit of dubbing her 'tomboy , ' and at that valuation she seemed likely to remain", "context_len": 84}}}, "sparrow": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 77, "context": "The old , familiar words seemed to have new meaning in them , said in the midst of the darkness and the danger , and the children felt that , though their earthly Father might be seeking far and wide for them in vain , they were known and cared for by 'our Father which art in heaven , ' to whom the darkness is the same as daylight , and in whose sight not even a sparrow falls to the ground unnoticed", "context_len": 83}}}, "correct": {"freq": 67, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 82, "context": "Dear old mammy , who nursed every one of us , from Ruthven down to myself , and whom mother gave to Margaret as a treasure past all price when Harry was born , -- poor mammy , who considers herself quite as much one of the family as any Stanton , Duncan , or Bradford among us all , -- to talk of dismissing her ! But nothing less would satisfy Aunt Patty ; and Margaret gently claiming the right to correct her own children and govern her own household as she saw fit , and Henry firmly upholding his wife , Aunt Patty departed that very afternoon in a tremendous passion , and has never entered the house since", "context_len": 121}, "UNK": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 127, "context": "canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have heard of , they had never actually seen a white man before , a conclusion which I afterwards found to be correct", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "A correct and detailed knowledge of the Indian trails leading through their country , and called by them warpaths , horse trails , and by the white traders `` trading roads , '' forms an important part of Indian topography and history", "context_len": 42}}}, "chirp": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": "as it grew darker and darker , came forth another and another star , and looked down on the world which God had loved so much , till the whole sky was brilliant with them ; the soft , cool sea-breeze came gently in at the windows , bringing with it the gentle plash of the waves upon the shore , mingled with the chirp of the crickets and the distant hum of voices from the far end of the piazza ; but no one came near or disturbed them ; and still the colonel sat with his face turned towards the sea , without either speaking or moving , till his wife , as she sat with her hand in his , wondered if he could be", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Yes , it was lame Jemmy , and he was as chirp as a grasshopper", "context_len": 16}}}, "whiz": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "At the whiz of the bullets , though they passed well above us , I threw myself on the bottom of the boat ; but the firing had the opposite effect on the bos \u2019 n \u2019 s mate , for , cursing horribly , he raised himself and seized an oar", "context_len": 52}}}, "pretends": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "My goodness ! mutters Ted under his breath , staring at Chris a moment in blank astonishment , and then straightway pretends to be all absorbed in his own mail", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "But what a mistake ! Is there anything so precious in all this world as the openly expressed admiration of the people we really love ? No matter how one pretends to receive it , it makes one feel very happy at heart all the same , and humble and grateful as well", "context_len": 53}}}, "evelyn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Evelyn rushed up to Jack as soon as he came out of school one day", "context_len": 15}}}, "behaves": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Borden is a nice kind of a man and when he 's around that Jack behaves like a little gentleman , and the ladies do very well ; they 're pleasant and do n't put on airs", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 75, "context": "`` You see , '' said the woman , `` I told her to come when she was hungry , and when she 'd come I 'd give her odd jobs to do ; an ' I found she was willing , and somehow I got to like her ; and the end of it was , I 've given her a place an ' a home , and she helps me , an ' behaves well , an ' is as thankful as a girl can be", "context_len": 88}}}, "queerly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 34, "context": "But Jennie wanted to walk with her mother too , and so the other two little girls dropped behind to pursue their way through the crooked streets where odd sights met their eyes ; queerly dressed women and children jostled them ; at the doors of houses swarthy faces and strange forms appeared", "context_len": 53}}}, "recently": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 63, "context": "Instructions were therefore given to the two knights that , while Georges was to be treated with all courtesy , he was to be strictly watched , though in such a manner that he should be in ignorance of it , and that , whenever he turned his steps in the direction of those parts of the defences where fresh works had been recently added and preparations made of which it was desirable the Turks should be kept in ignorance , he was to be met , as if by accident , by one of the knights told off for the purpose , and his steps diverted in another direction", "context_len": 110}}}, "blocks": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 28, "context": "He could use a saw to block the wheels out of a pine board , and he could use a hatchet to rough off the corners of the blocks , but he had to use his knife to give them any sort of roundness , and they were not very round then ; they were apt to be oval in shape , and they always wabbled", "context_len": 66}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The left half-back helps the left-tackle to make his quick turn , and then blocks off the opposing right-end , while I help make interference for the right-half , who 's got the ball", "context_len": 34}}}, "terriers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Yes , fox terriers look absurd with long tails , said Jock ; every one says so", "context_len": 17}}}, "clenched": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 11, "context": "Here Seabrooke 's hand , which lay upon his desk , clenched itself , and a dark scowl passed over his face , while Percy glanced over at him with suspicion and resentment written on every feature , and a battery of eyes turned in his direction , not one among them with friendly look for himself", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 31, "context": "Mark Penelly worked with all his might , and after wrapping Paul in the blanket and covering him with coats and oilskins , some of the spirit was trickled between his clenched teeth , and the men then rubbed his feet and hands", "context_len": 43}}}, "cruelty": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 98, "context": "or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing his victims to the tree with spike nails driven through the hands and feet into the tough timber", "context_len": 128}}}, "turrets": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "Jamie and Jackie had both fallen asleep in front of the fire , commenced daddy , and now instead of the fire they saw huge castles and towers and turrets and bridges and royal people", "context_len": 35}}}, "pods": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "reddish": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "Bartram , who saw it in 1775 , depicts it as the largest , most compact , and best situated Indian town he ever saw ; the habitations were large and neatly built , the walls of the houses consisted of a wooden frame , lathed and plastered inside and outside with a reddish clay , and roofed with cypress bark or shingles", "context_len": 63}}}, "eardrop": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "And sometimes I \u2019 ve been called Lady \u2019 s Eardrops because my shape is something like a lady \u2019 s eardrop , too", "context_len": 24}}}, "jewelry": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "Some years afterward , when he was obtaining conquests over the Persians , he took , among the spoils of one of his victories , a very beautiful and costly casket , which King Darius had used for his jewelry or for some other rich treasures", "context_len": 46}}}, "cooling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "Peggy and Bobby would have been quite content to go on adding a nail here or a shelf there , and further making improvements , but Archie , now that the chief work was over , found his interest cooling , and having got hold of a book on 'Balloons and Air-Ships , ' proposed no less daring a scheme than that he should construct a flying machine , and start it from the platform", "context_len": 75}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "This he could slit with his machete and secure as much as he wanted of the cooling delicious waters that these plants store up , a fact that , if known , would have saved the lives of many of those who have perished of thirst in the very shadow of the thorny plants", "context_len": 54}}}, "dining": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` Oh , Bridget , such a kitchen and such a table , and no white cloth on it ! They , the servants did n't seem to care , and they were nice and clean at the Home , and you 're -- well , you have things as nice as in the dining room , and to have things shoveled out of the kettle on your plate or cold on a dish ! Sometimes puddings were good , and the creams and frozen things were clean and nice", "context_len": 90}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "The previous year Edna had spent Thanksgiving Day with her Uncle Justus ; this year it would be quite a different thing to sit at table with a whole company of cousins instead of dining alone with Uncle Justus", "context_len": 39}}}, "humming": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 3, "context": "The wind was humming through the stays of the Roanoke's apologies for masts , and it smote the Sea Witch with a driving power , which heeled her until the copper of her hull gleamed like a belt of gold against the white-capped Atlantic", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Some of them had paper fringe pasted round the edges of their kites ; this made a fine rattling as the kite rose , and when the kite stood , at the end of its string , you could hear the humming if you put your ear to the twine", "context_len": 50}}}, "refreshment": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "On previous birthdays they had always been satisfied with lemonade as a drink , but Drusie , feeling that this was a special occasion , had considered that lemonade was , perhaps , hardly a suitable form of refreshment ; and so , from a recipe which she was proud to think was entirely out of her own head , she had concocted a bottle of red wine", "context_len": 68}}}, "fonder": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "His Mistress was a little girl named Inez and she was almost fonder of Peter Murray than of anything else in the world", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "I suppose it 's very noble ; but , all the same , little Agnes is fonder of me", "context_len": 19}}}, "invitations": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 41, "context": "Horner had received an invitation from the whole club , so had Miss Newman , and the other teachers , and many of the pupils who were outside the charmed circle were invited by their schoolmates who were free to give invitations , only Clara Adams was not considered for a moment by anyone , and she was very miserable over the fact", "context_len": 63}}}, "purse": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 29, "context": "She lived for others ; whether in the great work of her life , the use of her pen , the proceeds of which went to fill her charity purse , or in the simple act of leaving her quiet room , on a dull , rainy afternoon , to play a bright country dance or Scotch reel , and set the little ones dancing to vent their superfluous spirits", "context_len": 70}}}, "pennies": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Why , '' said Bessie , holding up her little finger , `` do n't you know I have a silver three cent piece ? Well , there 's three pennies in it -- mamma said so , -- but it 's only one piece of money , and I suppose it 's somehow that way about three Persons in one God , -- Father , Son , and Holy Ghost , -- three Persons in one God", "context_len": 79}}}, "bowls": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 74, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}}}, "hepatica": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "Of course you are , dear little violets , said the arbutus and hepatica families together", "context_len": 16}}}, "fearing": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 26, "context": "On the return of the knight with the news that it would be at least a fortnight before Genoa could fit out any galleys , and fearing that the pirates might at any moment put to sea , we procured some small Sardinian craft , and fitted them as fire ships ; with the captives we had rescued , and some Sard fishermen , we manned the three prizes , distributing the knights between them , and at night launched the fire ships against the corsairs , whose ships were crowded together", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "They had rather a mixed mind about them in the meantime , but perhaps they were not unlike other idolaters in both fearing and adoring their idols ; perhaps they came pretty near being Indians in that , and certainly they came nearer than they knew", "context_len": 46}}}, "graves": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 73, "context": "It was very still and peaceful in there , and she liked to sit and think how the Abbey must have looked in those strange , bygone days when the little steps had led to a dormitory , and the broken pillars had held up the roof of a church , whose tinkling bell had rung out at sunset , calling to prayer those old monks who slept so quietly in their forgotten graves", "context_len": 74}}}, "memorial": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 56, "context": "It is a timely gift , Sir Gervaise , for the siege has played havoc with the suit Genoa gave you ; it is sorely battered , dinted , and broken , and , although you can doubtless get it repaired , if I were you I would keep it in its present state as a memorial -- and there could be no prouder one -- of the part you bore in the siege", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 67, "context": "As a sequel to his wonderful story of the pursuit of the Alibamu by the Creeks and the final peaceable settling down of both , Milfort adds some points on the early doings and warrings of the Creeks , which had occurred but a limited number of years before his stay in the tribe , and were recounted to him by one of the m\u00edkos from their memorial beads , like the legendary migration :", "context_len": 75}}}, "drills": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 0, "context": "Drills and parades , from early morn till late in the afternoon , soon brought the detachment up to a state bordering upon perfection , and the word went round that the Thirty-somethingth reinforcements would be sent to France some weeks earlier than the usual time , thanks to the efficiency of all ranks", "context_len": 54}}}, "balcony": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 45, "context": "The following is a literal rendering of the above passage : `` An upper chamber ( keszr ) and ( or ? ) a kiosk ( kushk , a word explained by a modern Syrian dictionary as meaning ' [ a building ] like a balcony projecting from the level of the rest of the house , ' but by others as an isolated building or pavilion erected on the top of a house , i", "context_len": 76}}}, "roofs": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 102, "context": "But still she went on , but only slowly , because of the great press of people around her ; and suddenly an icy wind passed over them , and all fell to the earth shivering and terror-stricken , for they had never felt cold before , and they looked up to the mountain , and lo ! upon the very summit , at the door of the deserted wigwam , stood a terrible figure , clothed in white , and having a face as white as his robes , and his hair was like the long crystals that hang from the roofs of caves that the water goes through , and his eyes were like two great diamonds , white , yet blazing like the sun", "context_len": 127}}}, "robin": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 25, "context": "Round through the orchard she sped , bringing down a shower of cherry-blossom as she brushed against the low-growing trees , and greatly disturbing a robin , who was feeding a young family in a hole in the ivy , but without any sign of the truant", "context_len": 47}}}, "frightening": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 61, "context": "`` How are we to begin ? Shall we do penance like the old monks ? Do you know , Rosamund '' -- here Irene linked her thin , almost steel-like little hand inside Rosamund 's arm -- '' that I am a most voracious reader ? Father was a great collector of books , and when I am tired of frightening the servants , and terrifying Frosty , and annoying mother , I spend days at a time in his library swallowing down the contents of his books", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Ah , '' said Barty , `` how frightening ! '' and he felt quite alarmed", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` We ran away and hid because you looked so frightening", "context_len": 11}}}, "provider": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "He just knew so much about it all and was such a good business robin that he was a very fine provider", "context_len": 22}}}, "sharing": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 55, "context": "Should she give Dolly her own or not ? She wanted it very much herself ; but she had asked her Father in heaven to let her find some way to be kind to Lem and Dolly , and now was He not answering her prayer ? It had been very pleasant to think of sharing the delicious fruit with her own dear friends whom she loved so much , or even of giving a piece to Starr , who was always so kind and good to her ; but to give it all to this bad girl who had done so much cruel mischief to her and hers , was another thing", "context_len": 113}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "They are friends and neighbors in the best sense of the word ; too high-minded and preoccupied to be gossipy or prying , they are interested in each other 's affairs with the interest that means a sharing of each other 's joys and sorrows", "context_len": 45}}}, "shared": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 7, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}}}, "safely": {"freq": 100, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 100, "word_index": 102, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "rescued": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 61, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "It was clearly impossible to take thirteen prizes to Rhodes , for there would be but three or four knights to each , and were they to fall in with but one Moorish pirate , they might suffer great disaster , while , should they meet with a storm , they would fare badly indeed , as they could not depend upon the rescued Christians for the management of the sails and oars in heavy weather", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "A boat was at once prepared to go ashore , and Gervaise begged Fabricius Caretto , the senior of the rescued Italian knights , to endeavour to find out whether a swift sailing craft of some kind could be hired", "context_len": 40}}}, "flown": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 70, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "You \u2019 ve flown twin-engined planes in Advanced Training School and you \u2019 ll get the hang of your new B-26 on the way to Darwin", "context_len": 26}}}, "counsel": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` Quoth the Sultan , `` On what wise shall we rid ourselves of this man , seeing I have given him my word and a King 's word is his bond ? '' `` O my lord , '' answered the Vizier , `` my counsel is that thou require of him forty dishes of pure virgin gold , full of jewels , such as she [ 426 ] brought thee the other day , [ 427 ] and forty slave-girls to bear the dishes and forty black slaves", "context_len": 90}}}, "priory": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 31, "context": "Wilfrid at Aescendune , it seems in some sort my duty , following the example of many worthy brethren , to write some account of the origin and history of the priory over which it has pleased God to make me overseer , and to note , as occasion serves from time to time , such passing events as seem worthy of remembrance ; which record , deposited in the archives of the house , may preserve our memory when our bodies are but dust , and other brethren fill our places in the choir", "context_len": 95}}}, "uncle": {"freq": 303, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 297, "word_index": 112, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "Under this idea , Fanny , like a dear Quixote as she is , formed a grand plan of rushing up to town on Thursday by coach with uncle Charlton , who happened to be coming , and turning you off the music-stool , or snatching the spelling frame from your delicate hand instanter", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` You do n't make a very pleasant prospect for me to be in , '' said Bessie , looking from brother to uncle with grave displeasure , `` and if a little boy like you , Fred , says that to me when I am a big lady , I shall say , 'My dear , you are very impertinent", "context_len": 61}}}, "wealthy": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 34, "context": "Nevertheless , mine enemies found it an easy thing to bring witnesses to swear I was seen in Venner 's company , and pressed hard on my hiding-place ; so seeing I was not wealthy enough or easy enough to bribe their witnesses to refrain from lies , I e'en fled , and have the hue and cry after me for a dangerous plotter", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "We see the same analogy reigning at the present day , when the sons of the wealthy , who are born to fortune , substitute pride , and arrogance , and vicious self-indulgence and waste for the modesty , and prudence , and virtue of their sires , by means of which the fortune was acquired", "context_len": 56}}}, "thine": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` And she said to him , `` O my son , the Sultan received me with all courtesy , according to his wont , and meseemeth he meant fairly by us , but [ for ] thine accursed enemy the Vizier ; for that , after I had bespoken the Sultan in thy name , even as thou badest me , reminding him that the term for which he had appointed us was past and saying to him , 'If Thy Grace would vouchsafe to give commandment for the marriage of thy daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour with my son Alaeddin , ' -- he turned to the Vizier and spoke to him", "context_len": 113}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Quoth the Sultan , `` I marvel at thee how thou still deemest evil of Alaeddin ; but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 83, "context": "O misery ! to wake in darkness , wake From vision of unutterable joy ; Instead of trumpet-sound and song of Heaven , To hear the dull clock measuring out time , When I had seemed to touch Eternity ! In the first pang of disappointed hope , I wept that I could wake from such a dream ; Until Faith gently whispered , Wherefore weep To lose the faint dim shadow of a joy Of which the substance shall one day be thine ? Live in the hope , -- that hope shall brighten life , And sanctify it to its highest end", "context_len": 104}}}, "whither": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 60, "context": "It was undoubtedly by diligent practice in this direction that , after strict confinement to the interior of my hut for some five or six days , I was permitted , first of all , to wander at will about that portion of the town which was enclosed by the palisade , and ultimately to pass outside and go practically whither I would , always accompanied , however , by two armed guards", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 54, "context": "Then the Sultan and Alaeddin betook themselves to the latter 's palace ; [ 542 ] whereupon his bride , the Lady Bedrulbudour , came out to meet him , rejoicing in him , and kissed him between the eyes , and he went in with her to her pavilion ; [ 543 ] whither after a little came the Sultan and they sat down and the slave-girls brought sherbets", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "So the slave-girls laid the table and the Lady Bedrulbudour sat down and sought of the accursed sorcerer that he should eat with her ; but he refused and rising , entered the pavilion which she had given him , whither the slave-girls carried him the morning-meal", "context_len": 47}}}, "southern": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 77, "word_index": 20, "context": "The party thus opposed were inactive and silent while Alexander was in their country , on his first visit to southern Greece ; but after his return they began to contemplate more decisive action , and afterward , when they heard of his having undertaken so desperate an enterprise as going northward with his forces , and actually crossing the Danube , they considered him as so completely out of the way that they grew very courageous , and meditated open rebellion", "context_len": 82}}}, "requested": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 97, "context": "her small person between the fists of two fighting hobbledehoys , and seized a little boy by the boot who had ventured to climb up the stove-pipe ; she welcomed some of the parents who had begun to arrive for the entertainment , and found them comfortable places on the benches , even nursing one of the babies while its mother went to the rescue of an older child , who was being forcibly held down and sat upon by several of its companions ; and by the time the bell was rung , and the audience requested to take their places , she was almost as hot and pulled about as the Rector himself , but with a feeling of conscious virtue that made up for everything", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "First , however , he called for Ed Kerr to make a try through right-tackle , and when this had been accomplished , with a smashing force that temporarily demoralized the Boxer Hall players , Kindlings was once more requested to oblige", "context_len": 42}}}, "repaired": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 2, "context": "So she repaired to the palace and when the Sultan came to the Divan and saw her standing before him , he remembered his promise to her , that after three months he would marry his daughter to her son , and turning to the Vizier , said to him , `` O Vizier , yonder is the woman who presented us with the jewels and we gave her our word that after three months [ we would marry our daughter to her son ]", "context_len": 85}}}, "disguise": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 37, "context": "And do you mean to say , Sir Gervaise , that you , a knight of the Order , are willing to submit to the indignity of being treated as a slave ? To keep up the disguise long enough to be taken into the confidence of the plotters , you might have to stay there for some time ; and if the prison officials believe you to be but an ordinary slave , you will be put to work either on the walls or in one of the galleys", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 58, "context": "Any one would have thought , as I did , that such a thing was strange , if not suspicious , and the only way to find out whether there was anything in it was to mix with the slaves ; as I spoke Turkish well enough to do so I asked Sir John Kendall 's permission to disguise myself", "context_len": 60}}}, "innkeeper": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Tell us truly , old man , '' said the innkeeper , threateningly ; `` who is this esquire Humphrey , and who is the young lady that beareth him company ? Make haste with thine answer , or it shall be worse for thee", "context_len": 46}}}, "grooms": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 46, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "sole": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 42, "context": "He strode along behind the flying form of the little minister , inwardly furious at the saintly and exasperating person who forced him to seek out the company that was precisely the most painful and uncongenial to him , when his one sole idea was to hide himself in solitude like some wounded animal and there wrestle down the grief and horror that possessed him", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 1, "context": "The sole vow necessary , so far as I can see , would be that knights should remain unmarried and disposable at all times for service until ten years after making their profession , and that afterwards they should ever be ready to obey the summons to arms , on occasions when the safety of Rhodes , or the invasion of any Christian country by the Moslems , rendered their services needful , when they would come out just as the knights of Richard the Lion Heart went out as crusaders", "context_len": 91}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Writing home , she described drolly her absence from Batala as -- this strange episode of my life ; -- seven weeks acting Superintendent of the Orphanage , -- three of those weeks sole Missionary at Amritsar , -- and -- oh , bathos ! ten days an ayah -- for I had none other", "context_len": 55}}}, "succeeding": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 50, "context": "Had any other child friend along his route , no matter how friendly , questioned him day after day as to how he was getting on with his training for the race ? Had any other among them promised to be on hand at the latest delivery on the afternoon succeeding it , so as to learn just what the issue had been , and at a time when he would be able to stop and tell about it ? Would any one else in the world have thought of suggesting that he should give three short little whistles when he reached the Browns ' , in Remsen Street , so that she should know just how near he was ? Surely no one ; and it was", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "A tiny glimpse of the daily fighting , which she like all others had to go through , may be seen in the succeeding letter , written to her sister , Laura , a year or two before the death of old Mr", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "In the succeeding Noontide hours she had known still much of brightness , though they included her first great sorrow , and ended with her second", "context_len": 26}}}, "indifferently": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 39, "context": "For a moment I could scarce refrain from throwing myself upon him ; but reflecting that little would be gained thus , and that much might be obtained by strategy , I refrained , and , shrugging my shoulders indifferently , I turned from him and walked to the farther part of the room", "context_len": 54}}}, "hath": {"freq": 105, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 121, "context": "advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we will set over you guards to guard you ; but do not thou let slip this great honour , for that it hath fallen to none other than thyself", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 82, "context": "His mother was perplexed at his case , unknowing what had befallen him , and bethought herself that belike he was sick ; so she came up to him and questioned him , saying , `` O my son , an thou feel aught of pain or otherwhat , tell me , that I may go fetch thee a physician , more by token there is presently in the city a physician from the land of the Arabs , whom the Sultan hath sent to bring hither , and report saith of him that he is exceeding skilful ; so [ tell me ] if thou art sick , that I may go and call him to thee", "context_len": 118}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Burton , `` Ere this thou hast learned , O mother mine , that the Lamp which we possess hath become to us a stable income", "context_len": 26}}}, "inn": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 74, "context": "`` Ees , ees we 'd surely stay by her ; but 't is no fitting place here for a young lady ; why , there 's no company -- no coming and going ; and the coaches as used to come to the old Squires 's ; and the quality ; and they fare to have clean forgotten our young lady , dang 'em ! And Squire 's great house turned into an inn ! You think o ' that ! If so be as you goo into Hun'ston , you 'll see the name o ' it , The Royal Oak , and a great oak tree drawed for a sign over the front door", "context_len": 117}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The pony and trap were put up for the day at a quiet little inn midway between the two schools , and were always waiting for the children by a quarter past four , when , like the traditional donkey , they joyfully turned their noses towards home again", "context_len": 49}}}, "anon": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Ever and anon we looked forth from doors and windows into the black darkness without ; but although it was near midnight , neither sight nor sound told of aught amiss , and we were beginning to yield to fatigue , when I ascended the tower in company with Father Adhelm , to survey the scene for the last time", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "Ever and anon he raved in his sleep about Sweyn , Edric , his father , and Alfgar , mixing them up in his mind most strangely : but the object of his abhorrence was ever Edric , while he spoke of Alfgar , `` poor Alfgar ! '' as a father might speak of a son", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "When I mentioned that I was on my way to my uncle , Master Anderson , the younger of the twain gave his companion a wink that did not escape me , and remarked : `` Then , Master Aubrey , we \u2019 ll see more of thee anon , if I mistake not", "context_len": 54}}}, "restrain": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then , instead of attempting to restrain him , and worrying and checking him by useless efforts to hold him in , he gave him the rein freely , and animated and encouraged him with his voice , so that the horse flew across the plains at the top of his speed , the king and the courtiers looking on , at first with fear and trembling , but soon afterward with feelings of the greatest admiration and pleasure", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas must never complain ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must temper restrain -- When sust [ 91 ] pankah-wala won \u2019 t pull at the cane ; Must never be fanciful , foolish , or vain", "context_len": 39}}}, "whereabouts": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 39, "context": "Could it be that she had made a mistake , after all , and that the neglected child had some powerful though eccentric friend in the background -- perhaps some previously unknown relation , who had suddenly traced her whereabouts , and chose to provide for her in this mysterious and fantastic way ? Relations were sometimes very odd -- particularly rich old bachelor uncles , who did not care for having children near them", "context_len": 75}}}, "bystanders": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` It 's hard for most to follow God in such a dispensation as this , and yet my Lord and Master is as sweet and glorious to me now as He was in the time of my greatest prosperity ; '' and then , embracing his friends in farewell , he committed his spirit into the hands of God and was , the bystanders declared , `` not so much thrown off the ladder by the executioner , but went readily off himself", "context_len": 84}}}, "expectation": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 79, "context": "None could look back , not even to behold If those beloved were nigh ; one thrilling thought Rapt all the multitude , -- Can HE be near ? Then cries of terror rose -- I scarcely heard ; And buildings shook and rocked , and crashing fell , -- I scarcely marked their fall ; the trembling ground Rose like the billowy sea , -- I scarcely felt The motion ; such intensity of hope -- Joy -- expectation -- flooded all my soul ; A tide of living light , o \u2019 erwhelming all The hopes and fears , the cares and woes of earth", "context_len": 107}}}, "bold": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` She came up to my room -- I will confess to you that I was hiding from her , absolutely hiding , and shaking from head to foot , scarcely knowing what to do -- and she came in as bold as brass , and yet with a new sort of humility about her , and she said to me , 'Will you forgive me ? And if you forgive me , will you come downstairs and let me put my hand inside your arm ? ' And somehow , although it was the very last thing on earth that I wanted to do , I did it ; and now here I am , and I do n't feel nearly so much afraid of her as", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 73, "context": "Tell me an thou have a want , that I may accomplish it unto thee ; '' whereupon she kissed the earth a second time and called down blessings upon him , then answered , `` Ay , O King of the Age , as thy head liveth , I have indeed a want ; but before all things do thou give me thine assurance , [ 362 ] so I may make bold to prefer my suit to the hearing of our lord the Sultan , for that belike Thy Grace will find it a strange one", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "Yes , you said something like that to the captain ; and as though she would have given all the world if he had n't , but I did n't mean to be bold really , only I felt so sorry for Chris ; and then she proceeded to tell , as coherently as her emotions would allow , of her unexpected encounter with her old friend , and how dreadful it would have been if they could not have seen anything of each other just because Chris was a second-cabin passenger , and of how she had mustered all her courage and gone straight to the captain to see what could be done about it", "context_len": 116}}}, "tunic": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 36, "context": "A murmur of surprise and expectation now ran through the crowd , and the same bold hand bodily removed the head-rail and the robe beneath it ; and there stood old Bartlemy in his gray woollen tunic , his legs bare from the knees down , and his feet encased in skin shoes reaching to his ankles", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "A light material of a rather dark grey colour , nicely made up with a tunic bodice and belt , would be very useful to her", "context_len": 26}}}, "encased": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 60, "context": "Then , when she had at length cleared a sufficient space she lighted her cooking fire , taking care to use only dry wood , and thus make but little smoke , after which she proceeded to the margin of the river and brought back a large lump of damp clay , pieces of which she broke off and completely encased the birds in , and this she did with considerable care , I noticed", "context_len": 75}}}, "ankles": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 56, "context": "A murmur of surprise and expectation now ran through the crowd , and the same bold hand bodily removed the head-rail and the robe beneath it ; and there stood old Bartlemy in his gray woollen tunic , his legs bare from the knees down , and his feet encased in skin shoes reaching to his ankles", "context_len": 57}}}, "identity": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 27, "context": "Why , yes I do , Theodore for although it was years since he had seen him , the momentarily uncovered head had at once established his identity ; but how do you and Donald happen to be in each other 's company ? Marie-Celeste told me Donald was on a farm down in Oxfordshire , and that you -- well , that nobody knew where you were exactly", "context_len": 69}}}, "esquire": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Tell us truly , old man , '' said the innkeeper , threateningly ; `` who is this esquire Humphrey , and who is the young lady that beareth him company ? Make haste with thine answer , or it shall be worse for thee", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` They are like to be as little priest and his novice as they be esquire and young lady", "context_len": 19}}}, "groom": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 17, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Now let us groom the Prince", "context_len": 6}}}, "sirrah": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Then he rose and going forth the [ privy ] door of his palace , walked on till he encountered a peasant by the way and said to him , `` Harkye , sirrah , take my clothes and give me thine", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` And who are you , sirrah ? '' demanded Felgate , giving him a fierce look that cowed him for the time", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Now , listen , sirrah ! Say not one word that we are here , but directly he returns let us know", "context_len": 23}}}, "wherefore": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 77, "context": "When the Vizier heard the Sultan 's words , his tongue was tied for despite and he was overcome with exceeding chagrin , forasmuch as the King had promised him that he would marry his daughter to his son ; so , after a little , he said to him , `` O King of the age , Thy Grace condescended to promise me [ 371 ] that the Lady Bedrulbudour should be my son 's ; wherefore it behoveth thine exalted highness appoint a delay of three months , [ 372 ] and God willing , my son 's present shall be greater than this", "context_len": 106}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 98, "context": "`` The King , for all he knew that this was a thing whereto the Vizier might not avail , no , nor the greatest King , [ 373 ] nevertheless exercised his clemency [ 374 ] and granted him the delay he sought ; then , turning to the old woman , he said to her , `` Go to thy son and tell him I give him [ my ] word that my daughter shall be in his name ; [ 375 ] but needs must I take order for her equipment ; [ 376 ] wherefore it behoveth him grant us a delay of three months", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 84, "context": "The genie immediately appeared to him and said , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord ; '' whereupon quoth Alaeddin , `` I seek the Sultan 's daughter in marriage and he requireth of me forty dishes of pure gold , each ten pounds in weight and full of the jewels which be in the garden of the treasure , the forty dishes to be borne by forty slave girls and each slave-girl to be accompanied by a male slave ; wherefore I will have thee bring me this , all of it", "context_len": 96}}}, "thievery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "But the mule of the nurse is still there , wherefore there is no thievery , since he did take but his own", "context_len": 23}}}, "questioner": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "The mystery explained , his questioner had no further interest in the matter , and Gervaise , picking out a vacant place on the stone floor , sat down and looked round him", "context_len": 33}}}, "threateningly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "Will you tell that villain -- and he pointed to the captain threateningly -- I warn him that some day I will kill him like a dog ! Then , turning , he stepped into the bow of the boat , and the two men who rowed it at once pushed off", "context_len": 52}}}, "novice": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 63, "context": "One alone lingered to cut some limbs from a hemlock bush , a whole armful of which he brought with him , and while the rest were passing the water from the beach , and pouring on the blazing wood-pile , he was switching out the flames , as they ran towards the beach , with a dexterity that showed he was no novice in fire-fighting", "context_len": 66}}}, "elbowed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "What could he mean ? Donald and Marie-Celeste elbowed each other in their haste to discover , and in the next moment sure enough there he was right before them", "context_len": 30}}}, "agone": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "He knows all now : a day or two agone , when all the household had gone to hunt in the woods , I was alone with him in his chamber , and thought that at last I must discharge the painful task of telling him the truth", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Let me see : how many years will it be agone ? '", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` See , they come to drag me thither ; they all come -- Edward ; the victims whom I slew sixteen years agone in Cumbria ; the slain on St", "context_len": 31}}}, "bailiff": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 36, "context": "When the shout had died away , and the wine cups were emptied , Gervaise , who was sitting on Sir John Kendall 's right hand , would gladly have retained his seat , but the bailiff told him that he must say a few words , and after standing in embarrassed silence for a minute he said , Sir John Kendall , and brother knights , I can only say that I am very sensible of the kindness with which you have been pleased to regard what seems to me after all to have been a very ordinary affair", "context_len": 100}}}, "madman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "And he did act like a madman when he did discover that he had stopped the wrong persons , and prated of a reward from the king which he must lose", "context_len": 31}}}, "prated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "And he did act like a madman when he did discover that he had stopped the wrong persons , and prated of a reward from the king which he must lose", "context_len": 31}}}, "pounced": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 72, "context": "Even Toby seemed to forget himself for a moment or two ; and once , when the chickens were all flying over the grass , screaming and laughing , he sprang up from his post on the porch , where he had been quietly watching them , and came bounding down among them with a joyous bark , and seized hold of the fox by the coat tails , just as he pounced on Harry and Walter , as if he thought they had need of his help", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "pounced amber ) signifies also `` saffron '' ; but the obbligato juxtaposition of aloes and sandal-wood tends to show that what is meant is the well-known product of the sperm-whale", "context_len": 31}}}, "disconsolately": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "Mark managed after a good deal of trouble to postpone Texas ' hunting trip ; and the two struggled on through the day 's drills disconsolately , waiting to see what would happen next", "context_len": 34}}}, "hum": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 70, "context": "it grew darker and darker , came forth another and another star , and looked down on the world which God had loved so much , till the whole sky was brilliant with them ; the soft , cool sea-breeze came gently in at the windows , bringing with it the gentle plash of the waves upon the shore , mingled with the chirp of the crickets and the distant hum of voices from the far end of the piazza ; but no one came near or disturbed them ; and still the colonel sat with his face turned towards the sea , without either speaking or moving , till his wife , as she sat with her hand in his , wondered if he could be asleep", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "She don \u2019 t da \u2019 s \u2019 t talk about ye to hum , ye know , but w \u2019 en she \u2019 s away she kind o \u2019 finds opportetunities to discuss the subjec \u2019", "context_len": 37}}}, "escaping": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 31, "context": "He had calculated that he would be sold at one of the Levant ports , and had thought that with his knowledge of Turkish he should have no great difficulty in escaping from any master into whose hands he might fall , and taking his chance of either seizing a fishing boat , or of making his way in a trading ship to some district where the population was a mixed one , and where trade was winked at between the merchants there , and those at some of the Greek towns", "context_len": 92}}}, "din": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "In the hurly-burly and din that now arose old Bartlemy slipped out to the stables , got possession of his mule , and rode off unnoticed", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 55, "context": "The scene was indeed a terrible one , the roar of cannon , the shouts of the combatants , the screams of the poor wretches upon whom the terrible Greek fire fell , the clash of arms and the shouts and cries of the Turks as they pressed across the bridge , united in a din that thrilled with horror the spectators , both in the city and on St", "context_len": 70}}}, "unnoticed": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 20, "context": "One hope Alfgar had , and that not a faint one : he knew that the two theows had escaped unnoticed , and that they would give warning in time for either defence or escape ; their strength at Aescendune was but slight for the former , all the able-bodied men were absent at the seat of war", "context_len": 58}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "Mark Penelly had gone over to the other side of the lugger while the conversation was going on , and he did not face the man he looked upon as his rival ; while Harry , unnoticed by the busy fishers as he swam round , went on , touching the sides of the lugger as he lightly swam , but only the next moment to find himself entangled in a quantity of the thin mackerel net , which seemed somehow to descend upon him like a cloud , and before he could realise the fact he was under water , hopelessly fettered by the net , and feeling that if he could not extricate himself directly he should be a dead man", "context_len": 123}}}, "nobles": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 82, "context": "I do not for a moment deceive myself with the thought that , beyond the fact that her gift has made her feel an interest in me and my fate , she has any sentiment in the matter : probably , indeed , she looks back upon the gift as a foolish act of girlish enthusiasm that led her into making a promise that she now can not but find unpleasantly binding ; for it is but natural that among the young nobles of her own rank and country there must be some whom she would see with pleasure wearing her colours", "context_len": 102}}}, "situated": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 19, "context": "Bartram , who saw it in 1775 , depicts it as the largest , most compact , and best situated Indian town he ever saw ; the habitations were large and neatly built , the walls of the houses consisted of a wooden frame , lathed and plastered inside and outside with a reddish clay , and roofed with cypress bark or shingles", "context_len": 63}}}, "retinue": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "But how comes she to be alone in this ruined house , like an enchanted damosel of a fairy tale ? 'T is a strange plight for such a tenderly-nurtured maid , for old Sir Gyles guarded her as the very apple of his eye ! And what state did not he keep , and Hunstanton Hall ! And with what a retinue did he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for", "context_len": 128}}}, "livery": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 22, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Several fresh paying guests had arrived , and there was the little man in livery helping Emma to wait", "context_len": 19}}}, "beckoning": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 13, "context": "He looked in surprise at the commotion before the White Horse , and beckoning a retainer he said , `` Find me the meaning of this uproar", "context_len": 27}}}, "retainer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "One Boom , a retainer of Elfwyn , had been taken prisoner by the Danes , and by a very uncommon piece of good fortune had escaped with life from his ferocious captors", "context_len": 33}}}, "uproar": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 49, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "courtyard": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 86, "context": "Thanks be to Allah , the All Powerful , one of the men began , my lips are unsealed , and I can tell you the great news that our hour for escape from bondage is at hand ! We need not fear the warder there , he went on , as several eyes were turned apprehensively towards the guard , who , with his spear beside him , was leaning carelessly against the wall at the farther end , looking through the window into the courtyard ; he is with us", "context_len": 92}}}, "lordship": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "If you can keep private a while , his lordship makes no doubt it will all blow over , and he will use his influence to have Keith sent back to Scotland , or over sea on some errand", "context_len": 39}}}, "disguised": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 48, "context": "As for the guilt , it lieth with the accursed one , the surviving [ 661 ] brother of the Maugrabin enchanter , who feigneth himself to be Fatimeh the Recluse ; for lo , he hath slain Fatimeh in her cavern and hath donned her dress and disguised himself after her favour and fashion and is come hither , seeking thy destruction , so he may take vengeance on thee for his brother ; and he it is who taught thy wife to seek this of thee", "context_len": 88}}}, "guise": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "Now Lord De Launay was he who in the guise of a scullion had set Walter Skinner free , and all for the friendship he bore Josceline 's father", "context_len": 29}}}, "scullion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now Lord De Launay was he who in the guise of a scullion had set Walter Skinner free , and all for the friendship he bore Josceline 's father", "context_len": 29}}}, "garb": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 67, "context": "This much I had heard from report , and now , in spite of my cramped position , and faintness from want of food , I could not help looking with interest on the shattered walls , which still showed the black marks of the powder , though now , after a lapse of twenty years , their barrenness was beginning to be hidden by a kindly garb of ivy", "context_len": 70}}}, "escort": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 47, "context": "Their voices were subdued lest they should reach the king 's ears ; but , respectful as was the bearing of all the members of the group by the fire , they clearly split into two halves : on the one hand , the officers of the escort who were teasing the boy , and on the other , a group of gentlemen , some wearing the conventional ribbons and laces of a cavalier , others in the rough cloth of country wear , stained with the mud of country lanes , while the master and mistress of the house moved from one guest to another , evidently nervous at the doubtful honour that such a royal visit had brought to their roof", "context_len": 123}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 83, "context": "`` [ 428 ] So he said to Alaeddin 's mother , `` Go and tell thy son that I abide by the promise which I made him , but an if he avail unto my daughter 's dowry ; to wit , I require of him forty dishes of pure gold , which must all be full of jewels [ such as ] thou broughtest me [ erst ] , together with forty slave-girls to carry them and forty male slaves to escort and attend them", "context_len": 87}}}, "flouted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Not that he openly flouted the king 's authority , but that , at all times , he dared to pursue the course that seemed to him best", "context_len": 28}}}, "authority": {"freq": 76, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 52, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}}}, "pursue": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 100, "context": "that if he wished to marry his daughter he could do it without his consent ; as to the ransom , he was not in want of money ; in respect to Darius 's offering to give him up all west of the Euphrates , it was absurd for a man to speak of giving what was no longer his own ; that he had crossed too many seas in his military expeditions , since he left Macedon , to feel any concern about the rivers that he might find in his way ; and that he should continue to pursue Darius wherever he might retreat in search of safety and protection , and he had no fear but that he should find and conquer him at last", "context_len": 128}}}, "thwarting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Moreover , the king did not discover how much he owed to him for the thwarting of his plans", "context_len": 19}}}, "plans": {"freq": 146, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 142, "word_index": 122, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "This , doubtless , may be so , Sir Gervaise , the doge said , with a slight smile , but it is to the head that plans , rather than to the hand that strikes , that such success as you have achieved is due ; and the credit of this night attack is , as the cavalier Caretto tells me , wholly yours , for until you issued your final orders it seemed to him , and to the two good knights his companions , that there was naught to do but to remain in port and watch this corsair fleet sail away to carry out its work of destruction", "context_len": 112}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "PLANS FOR DEPARTURE 95", "context_len": 4}}}, "punished": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 15, "context": "Only one-half of the boys were implicated in that affair ; they had already been punished by the restrictions which had been placed upon them , and were to be further disgraced by the public reprimand which he intended to give them on the dismissal of the school ; and these culprits were probably dreading this or some other severe punishment which would be meted out to them by the report of their misconduct which would be sent home", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 73, "context": "Thus , as the news of the destruction of Thebes , and the selling of so large a portion of the inhabitants into slavery , spread over the land , its effect was to turn over so great a part of the population to a feeling of admiration of Alexander 's character , and confidence in his extraordinary powers , as to leave only a small minority disposed to take sides with the punished rebels , or resent the destruction of the city", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I want the guilty one punished , '' went on Professor Tines", "context_len": 13}}}, "overtook": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Where 's your camp ? '' asked William , who , as my readers will remember , was the son of the widow in whose house the chums found shelter during a blizzard that overtook them when they were on a hunting trip , as told in the second volume of this series entitled `` The Darewell Chums in the City", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He overtook Malcolm , swinging along with prodigious strides despite the tenacious slime", "context_len": 13}}}, "fugitives": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 67, "context": "The bridge , which they both had passed , was actually rising , when the foremost Dane leapt upon it , but was rewarded by a blow from the battle-axe of Alfgar , which sent him tumbling into the snow ; two or three others leapt forward and clung to the edge of the bridge , but fell into the ditch like the first ; the two fugitives entered , and the gate was closed", "context_len": 75}}}, "despairingly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Drusie , Drusie ! '' he shouted despairingly , as he was flung to the ground , and , fighting every inch of the way , was dragged and bumped nearer and nearer to the fort", "context_len": 37}}}, "stride": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Barry saluted and watched the officer \u2019 s tall , still youthful figure stride away in the twilight", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 41, "context": "It had a rhythmic rise and fall , slight of itself , but distinct , such as is made by a person carrying a lighted lantern as he walks , or possibly by a horseman whose animal is on the same stride", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "And Stanard bowed and strode away with joy in his very stride", "context_len": 12}}}, "dungeon": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 51, "context": "The roof was semicircular , and composed of minute bricks , seeming to form one solid mass ; the floor of tiling , arranged in patterns , which could still be obscurely traced by the light of the lamp left by the charity of Sidroc to the prisoner ; for the dungeon was of bad reputation ; lights had been seen there at unearthly hours , when the outer door was fast and no inmate existed", "context_len": 76}}}, "surrounded": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 89, "word_index": 96, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}}}, "saluting": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "The princess rose and saluting him , seated him by her side and said to him , `` O my Lady Fatimeh , I will have thee with me alway , that I may be blessed in thee and eke that I may learn of thee the ways of God-service and piety and model myself on thee", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Yes , sir , '' replied Fortescue , standing to attention and saluting as he recognized Captain Nicholson the S", "context_len": 21}}}, "spurred": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 27, "context": "The girl had a sort of barbaric love of brightness and softness ; and one day , as she looked over some fabrics for which Jane , spurred by the approach of the vacation and the fact that Lola was to have a part in the closing exercises of school , had sent to Denver , the girl said suddenly , `` How good my father is to me , tia ! ''", "context_len": 73}}}, "hast": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 84, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 77, "context": "and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm , that thou shouldst merit of him this boon ? Again , this that thou cravest is beyond thy condition ; [ 336 ] so it can not be that the king will grant thee that which thou seekest", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "vile": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` And he went on to excuse himself to him and kiss him ; and [ 624 ] Alaeddin said to him , `` O Lord of the Age , thou didst with me nothing contrary to the law and I also was guiltless of offence ; but the thing came all of that vile Maugrabin enchanter", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "Sir Gyles feared much General Harrison might be set on some rash action , and by throwing things into confusion , would leave the way open for the Cavaliers to join with the vile levelling party to root out all good in the land", "context_len": 44}}}, "knaves": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` And , in truth , we did pass some vile knaves riding fast to overtake ye", "context_len": 17}}}, "reined": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 22, "context": "But it was of no use ; the real pony held his neck straight out like a ewe , or , if reined up , like a camel , and he hung his big head at the end of it with no regard whatever for the ideal", "context_len": 47}}}, "couldst": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Why , what couldst thou do for the lad against us ? '' he said", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "O Alfgar ! couldst thou forgive him ? ''", "context_len": 9}}}, "fears": {"freq": 67, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 99, "context": "None could look back , not even to behold If those beloved were nigh ; one thrilling thought Rapt all the multitude , -- Can HE be near ? Then cries of terror rose -- I scarcely heard ; And buildings shook and rocked , and crashing fell , -- I scarcely marked their fall ; the trembling ground Rose like the billowy sea , -- I scarcely felt The motion ; such intensity of hope -- Joy -- expectation -- flooded all my soul ; A tide of living light , o \u2019 erwhelming all The hopes and fears , the cares and woes of earth", "context_len": 107}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 84, "context": "is coming ; we don \u2019 t know whether she is sticking half-way on the road , waiting for the horse which we offered to send twelve miles , if she required it ! Like the famous little pig , we have eaten all the bread and butter ; and if the grand lady arrives -- without that faithless kahar -- what shall we give her to eat ? I urged Florrie at least to send to the city for meat ; but she fears that in the absence of the cook the guest may arrive", "context_len": 96}}}, "shouldst": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Lord De Launay is , as thou shouldst know , the sworn friend of Lord De Aldithely , and he hath sent us to overtake ye and to carry ye safe to the ship at Dover", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 92, "context": "and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm , that thou shouldst merit of him this boon ? Again , this that thou cravest is beyond thy condition ; [ 336 ] so it can not be that the king will grant thee that which thou seekest", "context_len": 128}}}, "sworn": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "As Claudia 's sworn knight we have now a claim upon you , and for the short time that you remain here you must regard this as your home , although you must necessarily remain the guest of the doge", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 65, "context": "`` Gad ! '' he said , suddenly staring at a face that rose above the crowd near him , `` I was almost fool enough to think the fanatic 's prophecy was come true , and there was General Harrison come alive again ! That young fellow yonder is the very marrow of him ! Some one of his family , I dare be sworn , poor wretch , and doubtless of the same way of thinking", "context_len": 78}}}, "pace": {"freq": 109, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 109, "word_index": 5, "context": "Tucker could not quite keep pace with the new-fashioned unconventionalities of young-lady work in London ; and one of the object-sermons , which most impressed me in my College days , was the beautiful self-restraint which these two sisters -- no longer young -- imposed upon themselves , in deference to their aged Mother \u2019 s wishes , in regard to that outside work which inclination , or one might say conviction , as well as opportunity and qualifications , impelled them to participate in", "context_len": 85}}}, "troop": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 29, "context": "Once there were two chariots -- one held the band in red-and-blue uniforms , and was drawn by eighteen piebald horses ; and the other was drawn by a troop of Shetland ponies , and carried in a vast mythical sea-shell little boys in spangled tights and little girls in the gauze skirts and wings of fairies", "context_len": 57}}}, "elixir": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}}}, "transformed": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 19, "context": "Se looks jus ' as Marie-Celeste said , remarked Albert proudly , his sceptical spirit of the morning wholly transformed into one of profound admiration ; and Marie-Celeste , asking that she might hold the case in her own hand , and gazing entranced upon the dear little face looking out at her , said joyfully , Yes , she does look as I said , does n't she ? Then she reverently laid the miniature back upon the Queen 's lap , as though counting it quite too precious to be long out of royal keeping", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "It was the drowsiness of happy , well-fed childhood , and they sat in the fire glow and luxuriated in it until Sara found herself turning to look at her transformed bed", "context_len": 32}}}, "genial": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 1, "context": "The genial landlord , who had joined the group at the fire , was relating to a listening and an eager guest another story of the almost superhuman performances of the Union scout , Leroy , when suddenly the lad arose from the rocker and began to search the floor with his eyes", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` You 're almost as bad as Tom was ! '' declared Madge , and there was just a little change from her former genial tones", "context_len": 26}}}, "motley": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Escorted by two Landsturmers , and garbed in very motley attire , the New Zealander was marched through the streets to the railway station , and after a six-hour journey the train stopped at a small station that , from the name on the Fahrkartenausgabe , was called D\u00fcren", "context_len": 49}}}, "appreciably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Meanwhile the motley crowd that had started in pursuit from the White Horse had become appreciably thinned upon the road", "context_len": 20}}}, "thinned": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Meanwhile the motley crowd that had started in pursuit from the White Horse had become appreciably thinned upon the road", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "I tried a Batala trap ; it was of no use : I have bought an Amritsar one , and Mera Bhatija has bought another ; but the rats , I fear , will not be much thinned in numbers", "context_len": 40}}}, "rider": {"freq": 117, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 116, "word_index": 111, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "pitched": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 56, "context": "They were not very dense , and the timber was not very heavy ; the trees stood more like trees in a park than trees in a forest ; there was little or no undergrowth , except here and there a pawpaw thicket ; and there were sometimes grassy spaces between them , where the may-apples pitched their pretty tents in the spring", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "This fort had once been a summer-house , but it had long since been disused , and would , no doubt , have fallen into decay , had not the children hit upon the idea of making it the scene of their pitched battles , and had so propped it up and strengthened it that it was impossible to take except by surprise", "context_len": 63}}}, "imperfectly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 6, "context": "The parts of speech being but imperfectly differentiated , tenses can be expressed in nouns by adding suffixes : m\u00edko chief , mikot\u00e1ti , m\u00edko-\u014d m\u0101 one who was , has been chief ; m\u00edko-ta ` l\u00e1ni a future chief ; adsulagit\u00e1ti the defunct forefathers", "context_len": 45}}}, "saddled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "Another , in his haste , had but imperfectly saddled his horse , so that he was speedily at the side of the road with his horse gone", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He observed three men standing in front , with a saddled horse near them", "context_len": 14}}}, "mounts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Others had chosen poor mounts that could go but slowly , being waggoners ' horses and not accustomed to any but a slow motion", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Now he was standing in admiration before the great oak chair of State beneath the organ gallery , and now nothing loath he mounts the steps that lead to it and runs a finger along the curves of its elaborate carving , and then , with a most reverent air , touches the embroidered cross and garter with which it is decorated", "context_len": 62}}}, "fain": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` `` Ay , my lord , '' replied the Maugrabin , `` I am indeed a stranger and arrived but yesternight in this your town ; wherefore I beseech thee tell me of the divine gifts of this holy woman and where her place is , for that I have fallen into a calamity and would fain go to her and crave her of prayer , so haply God ( to whom belong might and majesty ) may deliver me from my stress , by means of her intercession", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Emily fain would ascertain whether she does so from love of religion , or only from love for her boy", "context_len": 20}}}, "halting": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 15, "context": "Supposedly the string of barges was `` manned '' by women , the diminishing group halting at each barge to prolong the conversation before the crews boarded their respective boats , until , by the clatter almost overhead , the fugitives knew that the last barge had received its complement -- two , perhaps three , buxom and stolid German women", "context_len": 61}}}, "betimes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` [ 346 ] `` Have no fear for that , O my son , '' answered she and rising , took the dish wherein were the jewels and wrapping it in a fine handkerchief , went forth betimes , so she might reach the Divan and enter , ere it became crowded", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "embarked": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 38, "context": "How came such a misfortune to happen to them ? It will create consternation here when it is known , for although it was not a state enterprise , the sultan himself and almost all the rich Moors embarked money in the fitting out of the ships , and were to have shares in the spoil taken", "context_len": 57}}}, "tidings": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` And she said to him , `` Glad tidings , O my son ! Let thy heart rejoice and thine eye be solaced in the attainment of thy desire , for that the Sultan accepteth thine offering , to wit , the bride gift and the dowry of the Lady Bedrulbudour , and she is thy bride and this , O my son , is the night of your [ 443 ] bridal and thy going in to the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 82}}}, "aught": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 67, "context": "[ 354 ] On this wise she abode a week 's space [ 355 ] and the Sultan saw her at each Divan ; so , when she went on the last day [ of the week ] and stood , according to her wont , before the Divan , till it was ended , but could not muster courage to enter [ 356 ] or say aught , the Sultan arose and entering the harem , turned to his chief Vizier , who was with him , and said to him , `` O Vizier , these six or seven days [ 357 ] past I have seen yonder old woman come hither at every Divan and I note that she still carrieth somewhat under her veil", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "Then the slave of the lamp came to him and taking him up , set him down in his house and said to him , `` O my lord , dost thou need aught ? '' `` Yes , '' answered Alaeddin ; `` I will have thee bring me eight-and-forty mamelukes , [ 454 ] four-and-twenty to walk before me and four-and-twenty to walk behind me , with their horses and clothes and arms , and let all that is upon them and their horses be of stuffs costly and precious exceedingly , such as are not found in kings ' treasuries", "context_len": 103}, "UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 113, "context": "but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed not to do aught against him , so he was dumb and could make the Sultan no answer", "context_len": 128}}}, "knighted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 81, "context": "I am sure , comrades , that there are few , if any , among us who would not infinitely prefer fighting the Moslems to spending our whole time in learning their language ; and I for one consider the fact that he has for nine months laboured so incessantly and assiduously that he has come , as Boswell says , to speak it like a native , is even more to his credit than the deed for which he was knighted", "context_len": 82}}}, "elude": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 46, "context": "It was always the same , no matter what the hour of day or night that I might choose to start upon my expeditions ; no surprise was ever displayed at my eccentricity in the choice of times , but I simply could not contrive to elude notice ; and at length it was borne in upon me that if I wished to effect my escape I must adopt tactics of a totally different kind", "context_len": 75}}}, "risking": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "Their eyes met , and in his surprise Harry Paul nearly let go , for he now for the first time realised the fact that he had been risking his life in an endeavour to save that of the man whom he had heard accused of an attempt to destroy him the night before", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "Moreover , could one or more of the guns be disabled permanently , others would be brought down and set in their place , so that nothing would be gained but a very short delay , which would be of no advantage to us , and certainly would in no way justify the risking of the life of so distinguished a young knight", "context_len": 63}}}, "spurs": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 56, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "His silence and absence were not spurs to affection , although she dwelt gratefully upon the fact that he should lavish so much upon her", "context_len": 25}}}, "kinsfolk": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 69, "context": "All his heart was with his English friends ; he felt that in going to the Danish camp he was really going to his death , for although within a few years the conversion of the Northmen took place , yet at this period their hatred of Christianity was simply ferocious , and his father belonged to the old heathen conservatives of his day , as did all his kinsfolk", "context_len": 70}}}, "archly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "You have heard , have n't you -- for Miss Aliyn was quite willing to change the subject -- of the Englishman who said to a young girl whom he met on the steamer , 'You Americans use nice so much , I think it 's a nasty word ; ' and of how she turned and archly said , 'And do you think nasty is a nice word ? '", "context_len": 71}}}, "scans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "net ( This file was produced from scans of public domain material produced by Microsoft for their Live Search Books site", "context_len": 21}}}, "site": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` This horsepath began at the Rock Landing on Ok\u00f3ni river , a British post just below Wilkinson and about four miles below Milledgeville , Georgia , passed Fort Hawkins built upon the Okm\u00falgi old fields , then the site of Macon , on the shore opposite , then Knoxville , then the old Creek agency on Flint river , then crossed Patsil\u0101\u00edka creek , the usual ford on Chatahuchi river lying between Kas\u00ed ta and Apat\u00e1-i Creek", "context_len": 78}}}, "howells": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "HOWELLS", "context_len": 1}}}, "criticism": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "But the very idea that such a course had evidently been suggested made the children uneasy , and kept them for several days at a pitch of sedate behaviour calculated to calm down Aunt Helen 's possible fears for their future welfare , and to render unnecessary the criticism of the most faithful and interfering of friends", "context_len": 57}}}, "portrait": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 54, "context": "and as Donald looked as though he thought he might , and as that was exactly the way he was expected to look , Marie-Celeste complacently continued : Well , first you go up a flight of steps , and you find yourself in a sort of vestibule ; and there 's a splendid portrait of the architect there -- the man who restored the old parts of the castle and added new parts to it and made it all beautiful as it is now ; and from this vestibule you go on and on from one grand room to another", "context_len": 101}}}, "heroines": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Well , my little girl thinks her small dog would like to show his appreciation of the act which saved his life the other day , and so I have prepared two medals for the heroines of that occasion ; they are not gold medals ; in fact they are not real medals and of no special value except that they represent her , and our , gratitude to the little girls who were the life savers", "context_len": 77}}}, "imaginary": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 9, "context": "The tables are divided down the center by an imaginary line , each part being a `` table '' ; first class men sit near the head , and so on down to the plebes , who find themselves at the center ( that is , after they have moved into camp , and been `` sized '' and assigned to companies ; before that they are `` beasts , '' herded apart , as has been said )", "context_len": 79}}}, "imperative": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 46, "context": "It will take a little while for them to ascertain that you are nowhere concealed in the town , but when that has been determined they will at once think of the river , and a party will be despatched in pursuit ; therefore it is imperative that we should secure as long a start as possible", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "is the present imperative of the verb to be ; he was , being , been , is , am , ai n't", "context_len": 23}}}, "impressions": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 5, "context": "Miss Wauton \u2019 s first impressions of her , jotted down as follows , do not give the impression of a very old lady , dearly as Charlotte Tucker loved to describe herself in those terms : Tall , slight , with lofty brow , sparkling eye , face constantly beaming with love and intelligence ; genius in every look ; figure frail and fairy-like , agile and graceful ; very brisk movements and light tread", "context_len": 76}}}, "literary": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 118, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}}}, "questionable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "Both Harry Herndon and myself had tried hard to impress the negro with the necessity of taking unusual precautions to secure the safety of the horses , for they had attracted the attention of the whole camp , which was full of questionable characters , some of whom would have answered to their names if Falstaff had appeared to call the roll of his ragamuffins", "context_len": 65}}}, "traveller": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 82, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}}}, "farces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "FARCES :", "context_len": 2}}}, "earliest": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "EARLIEST EXPERIENCES", "context_len": 2}, "UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 35, "context": "You and I , who are so much older , ought to try to encourage her ; for , after all , she has been a most sadly mismanaged child , allowed even from her earliest days to see that people were afraid of her , and thus the spirit of cruelty gained a strong hold ; but there is a great deal of good in her nature", "context_len": 68}}}, "appears": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 19, "context": "[ Footnote 638 : Keniz , a word which I can not find in any dictionary , but which appears to be the past participle ( in the secondary form for mecnouz , as ketil , slain , for mertoul , ) of keneza , a lost verb of which only the fourth form acneza , he drank from a cup ( kinz ) , survives , and to mean `` something drunk from a cup", "context_len": 76}}}, "eleventh": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 60, "context": "There is no need to tell that they were friendly after this , these two pilgrims on the heavenward way , -- the old woman and the little child , she who had begun to tread in her Master 's footsteps so early in life 's bright morning , and she who had not sought to follow him until the eleventh hour , when her day was almost ended", "context_len": 69}}}, "catches": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 38, "context": "I CALL it a Boy 's Town because I wish it to appear to the reader as a town appears to a boy from his third to his eleventh year , when he seldom , if ever , catches a glimpse of life much higher than the middle of a man , and has the most distorted and mistaken views of most things", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "He begins to unbutton his long gray overcoat , and then he takes it off and throws it into the ring , where one of the supes catches it", "context_len": 29}}}, "distorted": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "I CALL it a Boy 's Town because I wish it to appear to the reader as a town appears to a boy from his third to his eleventh year , when he seldom , if ever , catches a glimpse of life much higher than the middle of a man , and has the most distorted and mistaken views of most things", "context_len": 63}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "The ruffian had hardly hit the wall before the negro was upon him again , making a noise in his throat like some wild animal , his face distorted and the muscles of his arms and body standing out as prominently as if he were covered with huge wens or tumors", "context_len": 51}}}, "angels": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 55, "context": "They fascinated her with their crude drawing and colours still vivid and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined", "context_len": 128}}}, "ascending": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 34, "context": "And such a garden as it proved ! with its fountains and statues and vases , and the orangery on one side , and on the other three sides a beautiful sloping lawn , ascending from the level of the garden to the gray stonewall at the outer edge of the terrace ; and to think that here they were actually walking about in this beautiful garden , instead of merely peering through the fretwork of the iron gate , as some other little children with envious eyes were doing that very moment", "context_len": 93}}}, "grope": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "He may then indeed look up to the sky , and see heaven open , and angels ascending and descending ; but he can only grope about on the earth , and he knows nothing aright that goes on there beyond his small boy 's world", "context_len": 46}}}, "aright": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "He may then indeed look up to the sky , and see heaven open , and angels ascending and descending ; but he can only grope about on the earth , and he knows nothing aright that goes on there beyond his small boy 's world", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "He is firm and resolute , and if I read him aright , he will never forego his purpose of demanding from me the sum which I so foolishly promised his father", "context_len": 32}}}, "escapes": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 54, "context": "Naturally , I was very thankful that the awful day had passed over , and that its end found me still in the land of the living ; for `` while there is life there is hope , '' and in the course of my somewhat adventurous career I have seen so many extraordinary escapes from apparently inevitable disaster that the one piece of advice above all others which I would give to everybody is `` Never despair ! '' I can recall more than one occasion when , if I had abandoned hope and the effort which goes with it , I should not now have been alive to pen these words", "context_len": 113}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` It seemeth strange that he escapes their fire , '' remarked the bos \u2019 n , as the swimmer bobbed up and down amid the splash of the shot", "context_len": 30}}}, "describe": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 64, "context": "were descanting upon the palace and its magnificence and talking of Alaeddin 's grace and comeliness and his bounty and munificence and the goodliness of his manners and disposition ; so [ 555 ] he went up to one of those who were extolling Alaeddin on this wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world ,", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "He entered , knelt in a mental attitude easier to imagine than describe , but felt some heavenly dew fall upon his bleeding wounds ; he left without waiting to speak to any one at the conclusion of the service , and was crossing the quadrangle to the palace which occupied a portion of the site of modern Christ Church , when a heavy hand was laid upon his shoulder", "context_len": 70}}}, "minutely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "The landlady extended her hand eagerly , and taking the bill examined it minutely , and finally , as if satisfied with her scrutiny , thrust it into a probable pocket in the interior recesses of her dress", "context_len": 38}}}, "adapted": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 59, "context": "Ted , however , who , in spite of himself , had still considerable regard for appearances , could not resist the temptation of investing -- though Donald urgently protested against such extravagance -- in a suit of clothes , somewhat less conspicuous than the nautical blue jersey and wide-flapping trousers of Donald 's Sunday best , and better adapted to his new calling", "context_len": 64}}}, "channel": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 43, "context": "It had a river , the great Miami River , which was as blue as the sky when it was not as yellow as gold ; and it had another river , called the Old River , which was the Miami 's former channel , and which held an island in its sluggish loop ; the boys called it The Island ; and it must have been about the size of Australia ; perhaps it was not so large", "context_len": 79}}}, "island": {"freq": 303, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 301, "word_index": 28, "context": "Then he returned to Sir John , and joined the other knights , who were all too anxious to learn the latest news -- who had left the island , and who had come to it since they sailed -- to interest themselves in any way with the figure who had gone aft , supposing him , indeed , to be Sir John 's servant , the lantern suspended over the poop giving too feeble a light for his costume to be noted", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "That island isn \u2019 t big enough to shelter any vessel that could make such a wide wake , Barry Blake declared", "context_len": 22}}}, "loop": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "It had a river , the great Miami River , which was as blue as the sky when it was not as yellow as gold ; and it had another river , called the Old River , which was the Miami 's former channel , and which held an island in its sluggish loop ; the boys called it The Island ; and it must have been about the size of Australia ; perhaps it was not so large", "context_len": 79}}}, "culverts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "Then it had a Hydraulic , which brought the waters of Old River for mill power through the heart of the town , from a Big Reservoir and a Little Reservoir ; the Big Reservoir was as far off as the Second Lock , and the Hydraulic ran under mysterious culverts at every street-crossing", "context_len": 54}}}, "courses": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 48, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}}}, "seasons": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 2, "context": "Several bad seasons and a spell of disease among the cattle had made all the difference between profit and loss , and at the time this story begins Robert Vaughan realized that any unusual run of ill luck might bring matters to a crisis , and render vain the struggle of so many years", "context_len": 54}}}, "skates": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 57, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}}}, "pastured": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "When the excitement about the Mexican War was the highest , the company wanted a fort ; and they got a farmer to come and scale off the sod with his plough , in a grassy place there was near a piece of woods , where a good many cows were pastured", "context_len": 52}}}, "kites": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 0, "context": "Kites came in just about the time of the greatest heat in summer , and lasted a good while ; but could not have lasted as long as the heat , which began about the first of June , and kept on well through September ; no play could last so long as that , and I suppose kite-flying must have died into swimming after the Fourth of July", "context_len": 69}}}, "circuses": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "The house had been occupied by a physician , and under the eaves the children found heaps of phials full of doctor 's stuff ; the garret abounded in their own family boxes and barrels , but there was always room for a swing , which the boys used in training for their circuses", "context_len": 54}}}, "flocks": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 38, "context": "Oh , the boundless delight of those rolling miles of heather , which looked like a crimson sea spreading onward towards the horizon , and the delicious smell of the sweet-gale as they trod it underfoot ! Great flocks of plovers flew before them , screaming their 'pee-wit , pee-wit ! ' and here and there they roused a snipe or a woodcock , while wild little ponies scampered off like the wind , indignant at having their solitude disturbed", "context_len": 80}}}, "abundant": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` And the Lady Bedrulbudour said to him , `` Go and bring her to me ; marry , this long while past I have still heard of her gifts and excellences and have yearned to see her , so I may ask a blessing of her , for that the folk are beyond measure abundant [ in talk ] of her [ 646 ] virtues", "context_len": 66}}}, "battened": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "There were flocks of wild ducks on the Reservoirs and on Old River , and flocks of kildees on the Commons ; and there were squirrels in the woods , where there was abundant mast for the pigs that ran wild in them , and battened on the nuts under the hickory-trees", "context_len": 52}}}, "walnuts": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 52, "context": "He started early in the morning of some bright autumn day while the frost still bearded the grass in the back-yard , and bristled on the fence-tops and the roof of the wood-shed , and hurried off to the woods so as to get there before the other boys had got the walnuts", "context_len": 53}}}, "sweetish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "There were no other nuts except walnuts , white and black ; but there was no end to the small , sweetish acorns , which the boys called chinquepins ; they ate them , but I doubt if they liked them , except as boys like anything to eat", "context_len": 49}}}, "acorns": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 49, "context": "I know that \u2019 s so ; because , sometimes I want Rover to go to the woods , and he won \u2019 t ; I switch him , and he won \u2019 t ; I push him , and he won \u2019 t ; then I put some acorns in my pocket and run ahead , and he \u2019 ll get there as soon as I do", "context_len": 68}}}, "levels": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 60, "context": "In the meantime he did what he could with the family pony , and he had long rides in the woods with the other boy , who used to get his father 's horse when he was not using it on Sunday , and race with him through the dangling wild grape-vines and pawpaw thickets , and over the reedy levels of the river , their hearts both bounding with the same high hopes of a world that could never come true", "context_len": 82}}}, "quails": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Here the ealdorman Elfric met him with a large English army ; but when he saw the foe he fell sick , or feigned to be so ; and then the old proverb came true , `` When the general fails , the army quails", "context_len": 45}}}, "sumac": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "In the vast corn-fields stretching everywhere along the river levels there were quails ; and rabbits in the sumac thickets and turnip patches", "context_len": 23}}}, "thickets": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 54, "context": "In the meantime he did what he could with the family pony , and he had long rides in the woods with the other boy , who used to get his father 's horse when he was not using it on Sunday , and race with him through the dangling wild grape-vines and pawpaw thickets , and over the reedy levels of the river , their hearts both bounding with the same high hopes of a world that could never come true", "context_len": 82}}}, "turnip": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "They first planted a stout broomstick in the ground , and fixed the turnip head firmly upon the top of it ; two branches of trees tied on like cross-bars made a pair of shoulders and arms , and when covered with a sheet stolen from the linen-cupboard , it appeared as if the white figure were stretching out its imploring hands in a vain appeal for mercy", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Dame Eleanor 's remains were fished out of the corner of the five-acre field on Monday morning by Joe himself , who kicked her turnip head as ruthlessly as the Yorkists had used her husband 's at Mortimer 's Cross , and brought back the broomstick to the stable and the sheet to Nancy 's wash-tub", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He 'd made a many o ' they turnip lanterns in his time , and knowed 'em too well to be took in so easy", "context_len": 25}}}, "skate": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Yet he was a boy among boys , too ; he loved to swim , to skate , to fish , to forage , and passionately , above all , he loved to hunt ; but in everything he held himself in check , that he might hold the younger boys in check ; and my boy often repaid his conscientious vigilance with hard words and hard names , such as embitter even the most self-forgiving memories", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "In this case they sometimes ended in an acorn ; if this acorn was of brass , it transfigured the boy who wore that skate ; he might have been otherwise all rags and patches , but the brass acorn made him splendid from head to foot", "context_len": 47}}}, "canal": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was hard enough to get it to the head of the Basin , which left the canal half a mile or so to the eastward , and stretched down into the town , a sheet of smooth water , fifteen or twenty feet deep , and a hundred wide ; his sense ached with , the effort of conceiving of the other side of it", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 32, "context": "For a time my boy 's father was collector of tolls on it , but even when he was old enough to understand that his father held this State office ( the canal belonged to the State ) because he had been such a good Whig , and published the Whig newspaper , he could not grasp the notion of the distance which the canal-boats came out of and went into", "context_len": 71}}}, "breweries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "But besides these natural advantages for boys , there were artificial opportunities which the boys treated as if they had been made for them ; grist-mills on the river and canal , cotton-factories and saw-mills on the Hydraulic , iron-founderies by the Commons , breweries on the river-bank , and not too many school-houses", "context_len": 54}}}, "rows": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 43, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "For instance , it is they only who man small boats ; and when a small boat rows ashore , it is always possible that the guard may be careless -- that he will keep the boat at the landing place , instead of pushing off at once into deep water , as he ought to do -- and that in this way a chance will , sooner or later , come for springing ashore and making a dash for liberty", "context_len": 81}}}, "dim": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 70, "context": "O misery ! to wake in darkness , wake From vision of unutterable joy ; Instead of trumpet-sound and song of Heaven , To hear the dull clock measuring out time , When I had seemed to touch Eternity ! In the first pang of disappointed hope , I wept that I could wake from such a dream ; Until Faith gently whispered , Wherefore weep To lose the faint dim shadow of a joy Of which the substance shall one day be thine ? Live in the hope , -- that hope shall brighten life , And sanctify it to its highest end", "context_len": 104}, "UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 43, "context": "He remembered the fearful joy of the adventure , and the pride , too ; for he had somehow the notion that this young fellow was handsome and fine , and did him an honor by his notice -- so soon does some dim notion of worldly splendor turn us into snobs ! The next thing was his own attempt at skating , when he was set down from the bank by his brother , full of a vainglorious confidence in his powers , and appeared instantly to strike on the top of his head", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 94, "context": "Father Cuthbert , seated in a corner near the Yule log , with his brother-in-law and the Etheling , forgot all his apprehensions , and shared in the universal joy around him ; if his thoughts were sometimes with those who had once made Christmas bright to him -- if he thought of the bright-haired Bertric , who had been the soul of last Yuletide festivity at Aescendune , or of the desolated home there , he dismissed the subject from his mind at once , and suffered no hint to drop which could dim the mirth of his fellow guests", "context_len": 101}}}, "century": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 68, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "encamped": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 15, "context": "To these he represented that he belonged to a party of his tribe who had encamped for the day at a short distance from the village in order to rest their horses before riding into Tripoli , whither they were proceeding to exchange skins of animals taken in the chase , and some young horses , for cotton clothes , knives , and other articles of barter with the tribes beyond them", "context_len": 72}}}, "avenge": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 58, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "military": {"freq": 93, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 92, "word_index": 71, "context": "that if he wished to marry his daughter he could do it without his consent ; as to the ransom , he was not in want of money ; in respect to Darius 's offering to give him up all west of the Euphrates , it was absurd for a man to speak of giving what was no longer his own ; that he had crossed too many seas in his military expeditions , since he left Macedon , to feel any concern about the rivers that he might find in his way ; and that he should continue to pursue Darius wherever he might retreat in search of safety and protection , and he had no fear but that he should find and conquer him at last", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Hawkins , plainly show , that the initiative for war rested with the civil authority , and not with the military", "context_len": 21}}}, "tribes": {"freq": 196, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 184, "word_index": 54, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "The names of localities could not be pure inventions ; they prove by themselves , that tribes speaking Creek or Maskoki proper were encountered by the adventurous leader in the same tracts where we find them at the beginning of this nineteenth century", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "To call this loose assemblage of towns and tribes a military democracy , in the sense that the majority of the votes decided a question brought before the people in a manner that was binding for the citizens , is entirely wrong and misleading , for Indians regard their actions subject to their own decisions only , or , at the utmost , to those of their individual gens", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "TRIBES ON THE YAZOO RIVER", "context_len": 5}}}, "sentenced": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "It is unnecessary to dwell upon the account of my journey to Winton to repeat my evidence ; but on the return journey ( having heard both men sentenced to death ) , as we were passing through a wood between Twyford and Waltham , a pistol was fired at our coach , the ball shattering the glass and passing close to my uncle \u2019 s head", "context_len": 67}}}, "desertion": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 3, "context": "She treated this desertion with such open scorn and was so very unpleasant to those who had formerly been her friends , that they turned their backs upon her utterly , declaring that they would rather pay a fine every day in the week than be nice to Clara Adams", "context_len": 50}}}, "execution": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 87, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The miners raised him on their shoulders and bore him aloft in triumph to the hotel from which he had so recently been dragged to execution", "context_len": 26}}}, "reprieve": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The offence was only a desertion in name , and the reprieve was promptly granted , but it came fifteen minutes too late", "context_len": 23}}}, "memorable": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 2, "context": "Since the memorable time when Captain Cook sailed into Botany Bay in 1769 and saw the naked native Australian poising erect to hurl his peculiar weapon , the boomerang has continued to excite the curiosity and amazement of the civilized world ; and truly the finding of such a scientific weapon in the hands of this so-called lowest order of mankind is an astonishing fact , to be simply accepted as another oddity of this odd , topsy-turvy corner of the world", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The Alma had been fought on 20th September , 1854 , and on the 25th October was fought the battle of Balaclava , memorable for the `` Thin Red Line ''", "context_len": 31}}}, "eastward": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 2, "context": "It stretched eastward from the river to the Canal-Basin , with the market-house , the county buildings , and the stores and hotels on one street , and a few other stores and taverns scattering off on streets that branched from it to the southward ; but all this was a vast metropolis to my boy 's fancy , where he might get lost -- the sum of all disaster -- if he ventured away from the neighborhood of the house where he first lived , on its southwestern border", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 29, "context": "We now had leisure to observe that the storm had so far passed away that there were big breaks in the canopy of cloud overhead and away to the eastward , through which the stars were beginning to show themselves , affording enough light just to enable us to discern the two banks of the stream , but not sufficient to betray our presence to an observer at a greater distance than , say , a quarter of a mile", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "He waited with all a fisherman 's patience , and , used as he was to such scenes , he could not help feeling gladdened at the glorious sight that met his gaze , for , one by one , the stars had paled , till only that named after the morning shone out resplendent in the now grey west ; while to eastward all was blushing with bright red and gold and purple and orange , tints so wondrously beautiful and rich that Nature had enough to spare for sea as well as sky", "context_len": 95}}}, "county": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 15, "context": "It stretched eastward from the river to the Canal-Basin , with the market-house , the county buildings , and the stores and hotels on one street , and a few other stores and taverns scattering off on streets that branched from it to the southward ; but all this was a vast metropolis to my boy 's fancy , where he might get lost -- the sum of all disaster -- if he ventured away from the neighborhood of the house where he first lived , on its southwestern border", "context_len": 90}}}, "taverns": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "It stretched eastward from the river to the Canal-Basin , with the market-house , the county buildings , and the stores and hotels on one street , and a few other stores and taverns scattering off on streets that branched from it to the southward ; but all this was a vast metropolis to my boy 's fancy , where he might get lost -- the sum of all disaster -- if he ventured away from the neighborhood of the house where he first lived , on its southwestern border", "context_len": 90}}}, "scattering": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 76, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "There was a tremendous bellowing and thrashing about , a scattering of the embers in every direction , and then the half-blinded creature lumbered out and made for the jungle , forgetting in its own pain and bewilderment all about its human enemy", "context_len": 43}}}, "branched": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 39, "context": "It stretched eastward from the river to the Canal-Basin , with the market-house , the county buildings , and the stores and hotels on one street , and a few other stores and taverns scattering off on streets that branched from it to the southward ; but all this was a vast metropolis to my boy 's fancy , where he might get lost -- the sum of all disaster -- if he ventured away from the neighborhood of the house where he first lived , on its southwestern border", "context_len": 90}}}, "southward": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 87, "context": "As we sat down to partake , of it together , Ama informed me that one of her father 's largest canoes , manned by forty paddlers , and commanded by a chief whom she had recognised , had passed slowly down the river about an hour after I had composed myself to rest , the chief in charge intently scrutinising both banks , as they went , evidently in search of some indication of our presence , and had finally passed out of sight to the southward ; after which Ama had descended and taken a few hours ' rest", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "The road which ran across the river at this spot continued southward into Wessex , and , so far as we could learn , was free from danger , so I determined to send my brethren to Abingdon by easy stages along its course , while I turned back with Father Adhelm , to share the misfortunes of my kindred and lay brethren in the woods", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "-- Alexander marches southward", "context_len": 4}}}, "metropolis": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 52, "context": "It stretched eastward from the river to the Canal-Basin , with the market-house , the county buildings , and the stores and hotels on one street , and a few other stores and taverns scattering off on streets that branched from it to the southward ; but all this was a vast metropolis to my boy 's fancy , where he might get lost -- the sum of all disaster -- if he ventured away from the neighborhood of the house where he first lived , on its southwestern border", "context_len": 90}}}, "disaster": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 58, "context": "Naturally , I was very thankful that the awful day had passed over , and that its end found me still in the land of the living ; for `` while there is life there is hope , '' and in the course of my somewhat adventurous career I have seen so many extraordinary escapes from apparently inevitable disaster that the one piece of advice above all others which I would give to everybody is `` Never despair ! '' I can recall more than one occasion when , if I had abandoned hope and the effort which goes with it , I should not now have been alive to pen these words", "context_len": 113}}}, "southwestern": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 88, "context": "It stretched eastward from the river to the Canal-Basin , with the market-house , the county buildings , and the stores and hotels on one street , and a few other stores and taverns scattering off on streets that branched from it to the southward ; but all this was a vast metropolis to my boy 's fancy , where he might get lost -- the sum of all disaster -- if he ventured away from the neighborhood of the house where he first lived , on its southwestern border", "context_len": 90}}}, "political": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was the great political year of `` Tippecanoe and Tyler too , '' when the grandfather of our President Harrison was elected President ; but the wild hard-cider campaign roared by my boy 's little life without leaving a trace in it , except the recollection of his father wearing a linsey-woolsey hunting-shirt , belted at the waist and fringed at the skirt , as a Whig who loved his cause and honored the good old pioneer times was bound to do", "context_len": 83}}}, "elected": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 22, "context": "It was the great political year of `` Tippecanoe and Tyler too , '' when the grandfather of our President Harrison was elected President ; but the wild hard-cider campaign roared by my boy 's little life without leaving a trace in it , except the recollection of his father wearing a linsey-woolsey hunting-shirt , belted at the waist and fringed at the skirt , as a Whig who loved his cause and honored the good old pioneer times was bound to do", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "In 1489 Sir John Boswell became bailiff of the English langue , and Sir Fabricius Caretto was in 1513 elected grand master of the Order , and held the office eight years , dying in 1521", "context_len": 36}}}, "campaign": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 29, "context": "It was the great political year of `` Tippecanoe and Tyler too , '' when the grandfather of our President Harrison was elected President ; but the wild hard-cider campaign roared by my boy 's little life without leaving a trace in it , except the recollection of his father wearing a linsey-woolsey hunting-shirt , belted at the waist and fringed at the skirt , as a Whig who loved his cause and honored the good old pioneer times was bound to do", "context_len": 83}}}, "recollection": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 46, "context": "It was the great political year of `` Tippecanoe and Tyler too , '' when the grandfather of our President Harrison was elected President ; but the wild hard-cider campaign roared by my boy 's little life without leaving a trace in it , except the recollection of his father wearing a linsey-woolsey hunting-shirt , belted at the waist and fringed at the skirt , as a Whig who loved his cause and honored the good old pioneer times was bound to do", "context_len": 83}}}, "belted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "It was the great political year of `` Tippecanoe and Tyler too , '' when the grandfather of our President Harrison was elected President ; but the wild hard-cider campaign roared by my boy 's little life without leaving a trace in it , except the recollection of his father wearing a linsey-woolsey hunting-shirt , belted at the waist and fringed at the skirt , as a Whig who loved his cause and honored the good old pioneer times was bound to do", "context_len": 83}}}, "fringed": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 60, "context": "It was the great political year of `` Tippecanoe and Tyler too , '' when the grandfather of our President Harrison was elected President ; but the wild hard-cider campaign roared by my boy 's little life without leaving a trace in it , except the recollection of his father wearing a linsey-woolsey hunting-shirt , belted at the waist and fringed at the skirt , as a Whig who loved his cause and honored the good old pioneer times was bound to do", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Poor , simple Dolly had seen the gardener shielding his choice blossoms by a circlet of fringed paper ; and she would fain do as much for the stunted little favorite which was so lovely in her eyes", "context_len": 38}}}, "pioneer": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 77, "context": "It was the great political year of `` Tippecanoe and Tyler too , '' when the grandfather of our President Harrison was elected President ; but the wild hard-cider campaign roared by my boy 's little life without leaving a trace in it , except the recollection of his father wearing a linsey-woolsey hunting-shirt , belted at the waist and fringed at the skirt , as a Whig who loved his cause and honored the good old pioneer times was bound to do", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "I well remember a time when I longed to see Windsor and the Queen ; and Aunt Charlotte immediately said she was longing for the same thing , and gladly undertook to pioneer an expedition", "context_len": 35}}}, "ironical": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "I dare say he did not wear it often , and I fancy he wore it then in rather an ironical spirit , for he was a man who had slight esteem for outward shows and semblances ; but it remained in my boy 's mind , as clear a vision as the long cloak of blue broadcloth in which he must have seen his father habitually", "context_len": 67}}}, "esteem": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "I dare say he did not wear it often , and I fancy he wore it then in rather an ironical spirit , for he was a man who had slight esteem for outward shows and semblances ; but it remained in my boy 's mind , as clear a vision as the long cloak of blue broadcloth in which he must have seen his father habitually", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 44, "context": "So long as I live I shall prize and value it beyond any honour I may gain , and as I feel it next to my heart , it will ever recall to me that you gave it me as a pledge of your esteem and trust , and I will strive to the utmost so to bear myself that I may be worthy of the gift", "context_len": 67}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 88, "context": "I have often wondered why maidens should so carelessly bestow their favours upon every knight who begged for them , and have said to myself that when my time came I would grant it but once , and only then to one whom I deemed worthy of it in all ways -- one in whose loyalty and honour I could trust implicitly , and who would regard it as something sacred , deeming it an honour to wear it , as being the pledge of my trust and esteem", "context_len": 89}}}, "semblances": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "I dare say he did not wear it often , and I fancy he wore it then in rather an ironical spirit , for he was a man who had slight esteem for outward shows and semblances ; but it remained in my boy 's mind , as clear a vision as the long cloak of blue broadcloth in which he must have seen his father habitually", "context_len": 67}}}, "broadcloth": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "habitually": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 66, "context": "I dare say he did not wear it often , and I fancy he wore it then in rather an ironical spirit , for he was a man who had slight esteem for outward shows and semblances ; but it remained in my boy 's mind , as clear a vision as the long cloak of blue broadcloth in which he must have seen his father habitually", "context_len": 67}}}, "overcoat": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 23, "context": "Two moments later , the parlor door was thrown open , and there stood a tall , broad figure in a great fur overcoat , which , as well as his long , curly beard , was thickly powdered with snow", "context_len": 41}}}, "security": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` Because I know the secrets of the prison house -- knew them before the Danes had murdered the once happy dwellers in this garden of England , which they have made a howling wilderness ; hence I escaped the wrath of the furious parricide , whom the saints destroy , with ease , and laughed in security at their vain efforts to take me ; but we must waste no time ; it yet wants five hours to daybreak ; within those five hours we must reach the opposite shore", "context_len": 91}}}, "divined": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "He used to be allowed to go as far as the street corner , to enjoy it , when his father came home from the printing-office in the evening ; and one evening , never to be forgotten , after he had long been teasing for a little axe he wanted , he divined that his father had something hidden under his cloak", "context_len": 63}}}, "treasure": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 86, "word_index": 124, "context": "wrote to me several times , and in each letter said that he knew perfectly well that I meant to be free until the year was up , and that he was not going to worry me with overmuch love-making , or any nonsense of that sort ; but he thought I would like Switzerland , and the change would do me good , and although he would not say much , and would not even ask me to go out walking with him unless I wished it , yet I was to be certain of one thing , that he was ready to lay down his life for me , and that I was the one thought of his heart , the one treasure of his soul", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "She was teeming with life and energy ; -- whether it were over her favourite chess , when she would wait patiently but eagerly , thinking out each move ; or enjoying the small-talk of society , watching faces and reading characters , to treasure them up for painting in one of her forthcoming volumes ; or teaching a niece the beauties of sound and thought in the Italian of Dante ; or playing at some game of thought with young people ; or reading aloud one of her two favourite dearly-loved and untiringly-studied authors , Shakespeare and Boswell \u2019 s Life of Johnson", "context_len": 104}}}, "woodpile": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "He ran home and fell upon the woodpile with it , in a zeal that proposed to leave nothing but chips ; before he had gone far he learned that this is a world in which you can sate but never satisfy yourself with anything , even hard work", "context_len": 49}}}, "zeal": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 70, "context": "Peggy and Bobby as 'the children , ' with crumpled pinafores and smudged faces , were an equal success , for in watching Archie Peggy had forgotten her sudden shyness , and she now threw herself thoroughly into her part , howling most realistically when her nose was scrubbed in the wash-tub , or her hair combed with a fork ; while Bobby stole jam and resisted medicine with lifelike zeal", "context_len": 71}}}, "chips": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 20, "context": "He ran home and fell upon the woodpile with it , in a zeal that proposed to leave nothing but chips ; before he had gone far he learned that this is a world in which you can sate but never satisfy yourself with anything , even hard work", "context_len": 49}}}, "sate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "He ran home and fell upon the woodpile with it , in a zeal that proposed to leave nothing but chips ; before he had gone far he learned that this is a world in which you can sate but never satisfy yourself with anything , even hard work", "context_len": 49}}}, "abiding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Some of my readers may have found that out , too ; at any rate , my boy did not keep the family in firewood with his axe , and his abiding association with it in after-life was a feeling of weariness and disgust ; so I fancy that he must have been laughed at for it", "context_len": 57}}}, "association": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 35, "context": "The only person who was not won over by Archie 's friendly ways and frank American manners was Joe , who gloomily prophesied broken necks and kindred evils as the result of the children 's association with 'the young master from furrin parts ' ; but I fear there was a good deal of jealousy in this , for poor Joe had been a hero to the children in his modest way , and it was hard to find himself suddenly supplanted in their affections , especially by a rival with whom it was quite impossible to compete", "context_len": 98}}}, "weariness": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 36, "context": "A grand place , too , to work , you think ; and then you recall with a sigh that the light of that glorious mind has well-nigh gone out , 'neath the weight of physical weariness and infirmity , and then the solitary home begins to look a little like a prison in your eyes , as you realize how glad its inmate would be to exchange it for the Palace of that King whose divine intent for the world he has so marvellously interpreted for us all in the days when soul was still master of hand and brain", "context_len": 101}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "He tried to throw off his depression , assuring himself that it was due to weariness", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "For very weariness the se\u00f1ora nodded over her labor , and made many little appeals to the saints that they might guide aright the tortuous course of her double cotton", "context_len": 30}}}, "surfeit": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Besides the surfeit of this little axe , he could recall , when he grew up , the glory of wearing his Philadelphia suit , which one of his grandmothers had brought him Over the Mountains , as people said in those days , after a visit to her Pennsylvania German kindred beyond the Alleghanies", "context_len": 55}}}, "kindred": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 88, "context": "Guy thought he had never witnessed so sad a scene as when they laid the beautiful baby , that looked as pure and sweet as a white lily , in the rough coffin that some of the young men had hastily made , and carried him to a lonely spot , that perhaps no feet had ever trod before , and , breathing a prayer over him , left him to his long sleep , far from the place of his birth , or that for which his kindred were bound , and where never a tear would be dropped above him , or a sigh breathed", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "The Yamacraw chief Tomochichi , then banished from one of the Lower Creek towns , spoke in favor of making a treaty with the foreigners , and Yah\u00f3la ` l\u00e1ko , m\u00edko of Kaw\u00edta , allowed Tomochichi and his relatives `` to call the kindred , that love them , out of each of the Creek towns , that they may come together and make one town", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "She had only to glance up in the doctor 's eyes to know there was a kindred delight in his", "context_len": 20}}}, "beatified": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was of some beatified plaid in gay colors , and when once it was put on it never was laid aside for any other suit till it was worn out", "context_len": 31}}}, "plaid": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "At this Edna jumped and bustled around with such promptness that she was ready by the time Reliance came to the door neatly dressed in her bright plaid frock and scarlet hair ribbons", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "She made quite a picture in her short linsey-woolsey petticoat , with the check apron , her plaid shawl crossed over her cotton bodice , and the frilled white cap framing the kind old face , with its apple cheeks and soft white hair", "context_len": 44}}}, "worn": {"freq": 123, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 92, "word_index": 73, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "As the game began in the raw cold of the earliest spring , every boy had chapped hands , and nearly every one had the skin worn off the knuckle of his middle finger from resting it on the ground when he shot", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 5, "context": "His shoes , though well worn and patched , had been blacked and polished till they looked quite respectable ; the suit made from his father 's old uniform was still neat and whole , for Willie 's present quiet life was a great saving to his clothes , if that were any comfort ; his white collar was turned back and neatly tied with a black ribbon , and Mrs", "context_len": 71}}}, "testified": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "Clavering having reckoned up the marks , Mademoiselle Le Brun having given her testimony , Fraulein having given hers , and the English teachers having further testified to the industry of the pupils , the girls of the Upper school were to pass muster before Sir John , who was to decide without prejudice in favor of the lucky three who alone were to compete for the great Scholarship in October", "context_len": 71}}}, "shameful": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 89, "context": "He hesitated for a moment , not from any intention of refusing to give his uncle his confidence , or denying that he had been in trouble , but from a desire to frame his confession in the best manner possible ; but nothing came to his aid other than the plain , unvarnished truth ; nothing else , he felt , would serve his turn here with that steady , searching eye upon him ; and in a moment he had taken his resolve , and the whole shameful tale was poured into Colonel Rush 's ears", "context_len": 98}}}, "commended": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "High or low , the drivers had a great deal of leisure , which commended their calling to the boyish fancy ; and my boy saw them , with a longing to speak to them , even to approach them , never satisfied , while they amused the long summer afternoon in the shade of the tavern by a game of skill peculiar to them", "context_len": 65}}}, "shadowy": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 65, "context": "He loved equality and fraternity , and he came out to America towards the close of the last century to prospect for these as well as for a good location to manufacture Welsh flannels ; but after being presented to Washington , then President , at Philadelphia , and buying a tract of land somewhere near the District of Columbia , his phantom rolls a shadowy barrel of dollars on board ship at Baltimore , and sails back in the Flying Dutchman to South Wales", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 29, "context": "The sentry 's tramp grew clear and then died out again , which meant that the sentry was back in the hallway of the barracks , and then the shadowy form stepped out into the moonlight and ran swiftly and silently across the area and sprang up the steps to the porch of the building ; and there he stood and waited again until once more the sentry was far away -- then stepped into the doorway and crept softly up the stairs", "context_len": 83}}}, "palings": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was a quiet spot , with long gardens leading to old-fashioned houses on the one side , and the tall palings of a cricket-field on the other -- just the spot where nobody would be likely to come along and make a disturbance , and so evidently Jones minor seemed to think , for he held Bobby pinned against the wall with one hand , while with the other he amused himself by tweaking his ears , pulling his hair , and any other tortures which his ingenious mind could suggest at the moment", "context_len": 95}}}, "hollyhocks": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}}}, "savage": {"freq": 97, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 87, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was clear to me that this unsophisticated young savage had been dominated in her actions by one idea alone , that of saving me from a death of unspeakable horror ; she knew that , in so doing , she was cutting herself off for ever from her own people , to whom it would be impossible for her to return ; and , in her absolute simplicity , she had evidently thought that it would be the easiest thing in the world for her to throw in her lot with mine", "context_len": 93}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 75, "context": "The whole family was gathered together on the grass round the wood fire , the smoke of which rose up faintly blue against the russet of the beech-woods behind -- handsome , fierce-eyed men , lying slouching idly on the ground smoking short black pipes ; slatternly women with gay handkerchiefs tied round their black hair , bustling about with something savoury inside tin cans ; ragged brown-skinned little children , gnawing at bones with savage haste ; while a few disreputable dogs waited eagerly for the scraps that were thrown to them from time to time", "context_len": 97}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Once their enmity was turned from him , the Indians became as friendly and pleasant as they had been savage a few moments before", "context_len": 24}}}, "solar": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Like the savage , he dwells on an earth round which the whole solar system revolves , and he is himself the centre of all life on the earth", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "An upper cut caught van Eindhovengen full on the point of his chin , while almost simultaneously the Maori drove home a resounding blow on the Afrikander 's solar plexus", "context_len": 30}}}, "revolves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Like the savage , he dwells on an earth round which the whole solar system revolves , and he is himself the centre of all life on the earth", "context_len": 29}}}, "sensations": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 57, "context": "It was such a beautiful , stanch , great ship , with its large masts and spars and network of interlacing halyards , that its wideness meant more to her just then than even the wideness of the sea ; and she felt so safe and at home on it withal , that all the expected uncanny sensations had need to be postponed to some more favorable occasion", "context_len": 68}}}, "justice": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 82, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "All this time it must not be imagined that I had given up the quest for my father \u2019 s murderer ; indeed , as I grew older , my thoughts of bringing the villain to justice waxed hotter instead of waning", "context_len": 42}}}, "crueller": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "He lives under a law of favor or of fear , but never of justice , and the savage does not make a crueller idol than the child makes of the Power ruling over his world and having him for its chief concern", "context_len": 43}}}, "ruling": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "The question was , whether Alexander should attempt to take his father 's place in respect to all this general power , and undertake to sustain and carry on his vast projects , or whether he should content himself with ruling , in quiet , over his native country of Macedon", "context_len": 51}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "At last , when the teacher was driven desperate , and got in a rod ( which he said he was ashamed to use , but they left him no hope of ruling them by reason ) , the big boys fought him , and struck back when he began to whip them", "context_len": 53}}}, "supernal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "What remained to my boy of that faint childish consciousness was the idea of some sort of supernal Being who abode in the skies for his advantage and disadvantage , and made winter and summer , wet weather and dry , with an eye single to him ; of a family of which he was necessarily the centre , and of that far , vast , unknown Town , lurking all round him , and existing on account of him if not because of him", "context_len": 85}}}, "disadvantage": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 28, "context": "What remained to my boy of that faint childish consciousness was the idea of some sort of supernal Being who abode in the skies for his advantage and disadvantage , and made winter and summer , wet weather and dry , with an eye single to him ; of a family of which he was necessarily the centre , and of that far , vast , unknown Town , lurking all round him , and existing on account of him if not because of him", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "No sooner had the new teacher appeared than Clara set to work to do everything in her power to make Edna appear to disadvantage , by all sorts of mean innuendoes , by sly hints , by even open charges , till the child was almost in tears over the state of affairs", "context_len": 53}}}, "necessarily": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 10, "context": "You see , a gage of this kind does not necessarily mean that there is any deep feeling between the knight who bears it and the lady who bestows it ; it shows only that she , on her part , feels it an honour that her gage should be worn by a distinguished knight , and , on his part , that he considers it as somewhat more than a compliment , and wears it as a proof of regard on the part of one whose good opinion at least he values", "context_len": 93}}}, "existing": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 88, "context": "Among the twenty millions of people in the United States , there are perhaps two millions , between the ages of fifteen and twenty-five , who wish to become acquainted , in general , with the leading events in the history of the Old World , and of ancient times , but who , coming upon the stage in this land and at this period , have ideas and conceptions so widely different from those of other nations and of other times , that a mere republication of existing accounts is not what they require", "context_len": 95}}}, "relate": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Some of his memories reach a time earlier than his third year , and relate to the little Ohio River hamlet where he was born , and where his mother 's people , who were river-faring folk , all lived", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 75, "context": "So he told them how , when he sat alone , ill and dull and irritable , Ram Dass had tried to distract him by describing the passers by , and there was one child who passed oftener than any one else ; he had begun to be interested in her -- partly perhaps because he was thinking a great deal of a little girl , and partly because Ram Dass had been able to relate the incident of his visit to the attic in chase of the monkey", "context_len": 89}}}, "hamlet": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "With a falling tide our position was hopeless , and when daylight dawned and objects became visible , we found to our dismay that we were within half a mile of the shore , and in full view of the hamlet from which we had taken the galliot", "context_len": 48}}}, "flooded": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 81, "context": "None could look back , not even to behold If those beloved were nigh ; one thrilling thought Rapt all the multitude , -- Can HE be near ? Then cries of terror rose -- I scarcely heard ; And buildings shook and rocked , and crashing fell , -- I scarcely marked their fall ; the trembling ground Rose like the billowy sea , -- I scarcely felt The motion ; such intensity of hope -- Joy -- expectation -- flooded all my soul ; A tide of living light , o \u2019 erwhelming all The hopes and fears , the cares and woes of earth", "context_len": 107}}}, "trivial": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Every two or three years the river rose and flooded the village ; and his grandmother 's household was taken out of the second-story window in a skiff ; but no one minded a trivial inconvenience like that , any more than the Romans have minded the annual freshet of the Tiber for the last three or four thousand years", "context_len": 60}}}, "inconvenience": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 35, "context": "Every two or three years the river rose and flooded the village ; and his grandmother 's household was taken out of the second-story window in a skiff ; but no one minded a trivial inconvenience like that , any more than the Romans have minded the annual freshet of the Tiber for the last three or four thousand years", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Would you mind lifting me up and letting me stand on the side and wave my handkerchief at them ? -- if it wo n't inconvenience you , please", "context_len": 30}}}, "annual": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 15, "context": "Though I was then happily unaware of the fact , the period of the great annual festival , or Customs , was approaching , and the joy of the populace began to find vent in nocturnal concerts inordinately prolonged , the musical instruments consisting of tom-toms , each beaten by two , three , or four performers -- according to the size of the tom- tom -- with a monotony of cadence that soon became positively maddening , further aggravated by the discordant squealing of a number of flageolet- like instruments made of stout reeds", "context_len": 95}}}, "freshet": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 48, "context": "Every two or three years the river rose and flooded the village ; and his grandmother 's household was taken out of the second-story window in a skiff ; but no one minded a trivial inconvenience like that , any more than the Romans have minded the annual freshet of the Tiber for the last three or four thousand years", "context_len": 60}}}, "impression": {"freq": 106, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 106, "word_index": 5, "context": "The affair made a great impression but somehow did not increase Miss Newman 's popularity , for the idea of the drawings was hers and Clara could not forgive her for the position into which she had forced her , therefore she lost no opportunity of making it as unpleasant for her teacher as she could in the thousand and one ways a sly and unprincipled girl can , and her little pin-pricks were so annoying , that finally Dorothy and Edna , who had not particularly cared for the new teacher , began to stand up for her and to do as many kind things as they could", "context_len": 109}}}, "fleeting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "All his life he has never seen a peach-tree in bloom without a swelling of the heart , without some fleeting sense that", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "However , the hours of darkness were fleeting fast , so we pressed on along the shore , peering through the darkness to try and secure a safe shelter", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Though Gorswen was the most quiet little country spot you could find , it lay only four miles away from Warford , a rising inland watering-place , which boasted not only a Mayor and Corporation , but a pump-room and concert-hall , and had a large and fleeting population of visitors , and , to judge by its growing suburbs , an ever-increasing number of residents", "context_len": 66}}}, "infancy": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 65, "context": "He could no more remember learning to set type than he could remember learning to read ; and in after-life he could not come within smell of the ink , the dusty types , the humid paper , of a printing-office without that tender swelling of the heart which so fondly responds to any memory-bearing perfume : his youth , his boyhood , almost his infancy came back to him in it", "context_len": 72}}}, "erasing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Over the spot where the little house once stood , a railroad has drawn its erasing lines , and the house itself was long since taken down and built up brick by brick in quite another place ; but the blooming peach-tree glows before his childish eyes untouched by time or change", "context_len": 52}}}, "glows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Over the spot where the little house once stood , a railroad has drawn its erasing lines , and the house itself was long since taken down and built up brick by brick in quite another place ; but the blooming peach-tree glows before his childish eyes untouched by time or change", "context_len": 52}}}, "untouched": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Her fair hair , untouched by artificial adornment , hung like a shower of gold around her shoulders , while her eyes were of that delicate blue which seemed to reflect the deep summer sky ; but the sweet pensive expression of her face was that which attracted nearly all who knew her , and made her the object of general regard", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "The Virginia creepers -- always the first to change -- turned a bright crimson ; here and there a maple flung out a scarlet branch , like a gorgeous banner in the air ; while chestnuts and birch showed a few golden leaves , in beautiful contrast to the vivid green of the foliage which was yet untouched", "context_len": 58}}}, "tints": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 77, "context": "He waited with all a fisherman 's patience , and , used as he was to such scenes , he could not help feeling gladdened at the glorious sight that met his gaze , for , one by one , the stars had paled , till only that named after the morning shone out resplendent in the now grey west ; while to eastward all was blushing with bright red and gold and purple and orange , tints so wondrously beautiful and rich that Nature had enough to spare for sea as well as sky", "context_len": 95}}}, "reminiscence": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}}}, "perversely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "The idea that the Creeks knew anything of the devil of the Christian religion is a pure invention of the missionaries ; being christianized , they call him now : \u00edsti f\u00fatchig\u014d `` the man acting perversely , '' taso l\u00e4 ya , or : \u00edsti nikl\u00e9-idsha ats\u016b li `` the old person-burner '' ( \u00e1ni nikl\u00e9-idsh\u00e4s I burn somebody , something ) ; the Yuchi call him `` the swinging man , '' just as they call a ghost `` a hunting man", "context_len": 84}}}, "blended": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "Perversely blended with that vision of the blooming peach is a glimpse of a pet deer in the kitchen of the same little house , with his head up and his antlers erect , as if he meditated offence", "context_len": 39}}}, "antlers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Perversely blended with that vision of the blooming peach is a glimpse of a pet deer in the kitchen of the same little house , with his head up and his antlers erect , as if he meditated offence", "context_len": 39}}}, "meditated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 79, "context": "The party thus opposed were inactive and silent while Alexander was in their country , on his first visit to southern Greece ; but after his return they began to contemplate more decisive action , and afterward , when they heard of his having undertaken so desperate an enterprise as going northward with his forces , and actually crossing the Danube , they considered him as so completely out of the way that they grew very courageous , and meditated open rebellion", "context_len": 82}}}, "yawl": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "In the case which my boy seemed to remember , the passenger is a one-legged man , and he is standing in the yawl , with his crutch under his arm , and his cane in his other hand ; his family must be watching him from the house", "context_len": 49}}}, "captured": {"freq": 130, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 113, "word_index": 8, "context": "Should the worst happen , and you are captured by pirates , you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 68, "context": "During his comparatively brief service in the trenches he had been called upon to repel isolated raids , both by day and night ; he had taken part in several successful excursions over the top to harry the German trenches ; he had participated in one of the greatest actions on the Western Front ; but , for the first time , he was helping to man a captured position against a massive hostile counter-attack", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Costly robes , delicate embroidery , superb carpets , shawls , goldsmiths ' work , and no small amount of jewels , were among the spoil collected , and the bulk of the merchandise captured was , two days later , despatched in galleys to Genoa and Marseilles , to be sold for the benefit of the Order", "context_len": 58}}}, "checkered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He began a checkered career of uselessness when they were ferrying him over from Wheeling in a skiff , by trying to help wear the pantaloons of the boy who was holding him ; he put one of his fore-legs in at the watch-pocket ; but it was disagreeable to the boy and ruinous to the trousers", "context_len": 57}}}, "uselessness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 74, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}}}, "ferrying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He began a checkered career of uselessness when they were ferrying him over from Wheeling in a skiff , by trying to help wear the pantaloons of the boy who was holding him ; he put one of his fore-legs in at the watch-pocket ; but it was disagreeable to the boy and ruinous to the trousers", "context_len": 57}}}, "pantaloons": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 35, "context": "He belonged to a military company of big boys that had real wooden guns , such as the little boys never could get , and silk oil-cloth caps , and nankeen roundabouts , and white pantaloons with black stripes down the legs ; and once they marched out to a boy 's that had a father that had a farm , and he gave them all a free dinner in an arbor before the house ; bread and butter , and apple-butter , and molasses and pound cake , and peaches and apples ; it was splendid", "context_len": 97}}}, "ruinous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "The quaint timbered house , with its carved gables and red-tiled roof , was built in at one end into a kind of square tower or keep , with tiny turret windows and winding staircase , getting just a little ruinous in places , but held firmly together by masses of ivy , which clung round it like a green mantle", "context_len": 61}}}, "butted": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 6, "context": "He grew very tame , and butted children over , right and left , in the village streets ; and he behaved like one of the family whenever he got into a house ; he ate the sugar out of the bowl on the table , and plundered the pantry of its sweet cakes", "context_len": 54}}}, "plundered": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 38, "context": "His kingdom lost ; his family in captivity ; his beloved wife in the grave , where the sorrows and sufferings of separation from her husband had borne her ; his cities sacked ; his palaces and treasures plundered ; and now he himself , in the last hour of his extremity , abandoned and betrayed by all in whom he had placed his confidence and trust , his heart sunk within him in despair", "context_len": 75}}}, "steamboat": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "He is on the steamboat which is carrying the family down the Ohio River to Cincinnati , on their way to the Boy 's Town , and he is kneeling on the window-seat in the ladies ' cabin at the stern of the boat , watching the rain fall into the swirling yellow river and make the little men jump up from the water with its pelting drops", "context_len": 68}}}, "kneeling": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 47, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Late in the night Henry awoke , and , opening his eyes , saw his aunt kneeling by the side of the bed , and heard her thanking God that he had given her this child 's life , beseeching him , oh , so earnestly , that it might be the means of turning his young heart towards her , that there might be some one in the world to love her", "context_len": 73}}}, "swirling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 51, "context": "He is on the steamboat which is carrying the family down the Ohio River to Cincinnati , on their way to the Boy 's Town , and he is kneeling on the window-seat in the ladies ' cabin at the stern of the boat , watching the rain fall into the swirling yellow river and make the little men jump up from the water with its pelting drops", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` No use waiting any longer , '' called Fenn , rising up from behind his bush , and peering through the swirling flakes", "context_len": 24}}}, "pelting": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "He is on the steamboat which is carrying the family down the Ohio River to Cincinnati , on their way to the Boy 's Town , and he is kneeling on the window-seat in the ladies ' cabin at the stern of the boat , watching the rain fall into the swirling yellow river and make the little men jump up from the water with its pelting drops", "context_len": 68}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Everything was lovely and just as it should be , even to the pelting showers that came down with such swift suddenness as to almost soak them through before they could get under cover of waterproofs and umbrellas , and then a moment after left them stranded in brilliant sunshine , fairly steaming within the rubber coats which , with much difficulty , had but just been adjusted", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "It is a pelting day , and no other conveyance seems likely to pass ; while the artist is plainly unable to walk the distance which separates Grimhaggard Hall from the next town", "context_len": 33}}}, "headed": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 56, "context": "It was not until they passed by the two forts guarding the entrance that the flags fluttering at the mastheads of the prizes afforded to those on shore an intimation of the event that had taken place , and even then none supposed that this fleet of prizes had been taken by the one galley that headed them", "context_len": 58}}}, "scurrying": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 34, "context": "At the last , the girls all crowded around Edna to kiss her good-night and to make their farewells , and then , like a flock of birds , they all took flight , scurrying home by the light of their lanterns , some across the street , some down , some up", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "After the first howls of fright and the frantic scurrying of the Indians before that awful apparition , a deep silence again fell on the jungle", "context_len": 26}}}, "bustled": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 18, "context": "My uncle busied himself till all traces of his worry lost themselves in his activity ; my aunt bustled about the house , driving the servants hither and thither , bringing linen from the huge oak chest , furbishing the massive furniture , and causing a huge supply of viands to be prepared for the entertainment of our guests -- for the invited company numbered close on a score , so that our house would be filled to overflowing , and rooms had to be engaged at `` The Bell Inn '' and `` The Blue Posts ''", "context_len": 98}}}, "slips": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Take care , Bessie , take care ! '' called her brother , the Colonel ; `` that is loose gravel there ; if it slips with you , you are lost ; '' and , `` Come back , Bessie , come back ! '' called her husband from above , seeing the danger more plainly than any of the others", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 45, "context": "Porter also kindly gave them such seeds as would do to plant at this late season ; and papa , who had driven down to the village with the Colonel and Uncle Ruthven , came back with a number of verbenas , heliotropes , geranium slips and other pretty things , which were set out in the new gardens", "context_len": 59}}}, "vanishes": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "But they disappear like persons in a dream , and their fishing-time vanishes with them , and the swimming-time is in full possession of the river , and of all the other waters of the Boy 's Town", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "It \u2019 s too good to be true , finding you all here and Sweet Rosy O \u2019 Grady too ! I \u2019 m going to say hello to her before she vanishes in a pink fog , or something !", "context_len": 41}}}, "incident": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 77, "context": "So he told them how , when he sat alone , ill and dull and irritable , Ram Dass had tried to distract him by describing the passers by , and there was one child who passed oftener than any one else ; he had begun to be interested in her -- partly perhaps because he was thinking a great deal of a little girl , and partly because Ram Dass had been able to relate the incident of his visit to the attic in chase of the monkey", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "In spite of the frequent use to which they are put they do not relieve the sentence of its heaviness to any perceptible extent ; for what we call incident clauses and also many co-ordinate principal sentences are uniformly expressed by groups of words , the verb of which stands in the -t or -n verbal , which nearest corresponds to our participle in -ing , or to having ( h", "context_len": 71}}}, "influences": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 45, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}}}, "manufacturer": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "His great-grandfather was a prosperous manufacturer of Welsh flannels , who had founded his industry in a pretty town called The Hay , on the river Wye , in South Wales , where the boy saw one of his mills , still making Welsh flannels , when he visited his father 's birthplace a few years ago", "context_len": 57}}}, "flannels": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 33, "context": "He loved equality and fraternity , and he came out to America towards the close of the last century to prospect for these as well as for a good location to manufacture Welsh flannels ; but after being presented to Washington , then President , at Philadelphia , and buying a tract of land somewhere near the District of Columbia , his phantom rolls a shadowy barrel of dollars on board ship at Baltimore , and sails back in the Flying Dutchman to South Wales", "context_len": 85}}}, "founded": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 21, "context": "Oh , the Brotherhood of Knights ! That is what an Order is , you know , and this one was founded way back in the fourteenth century , in the time of Edward the Third ; and they say the way it came to be called the Order of the Garter was this : That King Edward was dancing with the Countess of Salisbury , when she had the misfortune to lose her garter ; and then as he stooped to pick it up , and saw every one smiling , he gallantly announced , 'that they should shortly see that garter advanced to so high an honor and renown as to account themselves happy to wear it", "context_len": 119}}}, "industry": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 26, "context": "Whit Monday was a holiday at school , however , and Peggy and Bobby , having the day at home , took a sudden fit of industry , and started to weed the shrubbery with the noble intention of having it raked over and tidied by teatime , being put somewhat on their mettle by Father 's remarks on the subject of sustained labour , and his laughing incredulity when they assured him he would find it all neatly finished when he returned from Warford that evening", "context_len": 87}}}, "birthplace": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "His great-grandfather was a prosperous manufacturer of Welsh flannels , who had founded his industry in a pretty town called The Hay , on the river Wye , in South Wales , where the boy saw one of his mills , still making Welsh flannels , when he visited his father 's birthplace a few years ago", "context_len": 57}}}, "convert": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 72, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "The first syllable , as pronounced by the Creek Indians , contains a clear , short a , and that the name was written Muscogee and not Mascogee , is not to be wondered at , for the English language , with its surd , indistinct and strongly modified vocalization , will convert the clearest a into a u", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The poor fellow had been thinking how he could earn his livelihood , -- he has a wife and four children , and of course religious begging would be for a convert both improper and unprofitable", "context_len": 36}}}, "equality": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "He loved equality and fraternity , and he came out to America towards the close of the last century to prospect for these as well as for a good location to manufacture Welsh flannels ; but after being presented to Washington , then President , at Philadelphia , and buying a tract of land somewhere near the District of Columbia , his phantom rolls a shadowy barrel of dollars on board ship at Baltimore , and sails back in the Flying Dutchman to South Wales", "context_len": 85}}}, "fraternity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "He loved equality and fraternity , and he came out to America towards the close of the last century to prospect for these as well as for a good location to manufacture Welsh flannels ; but after being presented to Washington , then President , at Philadelphia , and buying a tract of land somewhere near the District of Columbia , his phantom rolls a shadowy barrel of dollars on board ship at Baltimore , and sails back in the Flying Dutchman to South Wales", "context_len": 85}}}, "location": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 29, "context": "He loved equality and fraternity , and he came out to America towards the close of the last century to prospect for these as well as for a good location to manufacture Welsh flannels ; but after being presented to Washington , then President , at Philadelphia , and buying a tract of land somewhere near the District of Columbia , his phantom rolls a shadowy barrel of dollars on board ship at Baltimore , and sails back in the Flying Dutchman to South Wales", "context_len": 85}}}, "manufacture": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "He loved equality and fraternity , and he came out to America towards the close of the last century to prospect for these as well as for a good location to manufacture Welsh flannels ; but after being presented to Washington , then President , at Philadelphia , and buying a tract of land somewhere near the District of Columbia , his phantom rolls a shadowy barrel of dollars on board ship at Baltimore , and sails back in the Flying Dutchman to South Wales", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 45, "context": "Among the tribes of the Maskoki family , we notice the following ethnographic practices : the use of the red and white colors as symbols of war and peace , an extensive system of totemic gentes , the use of the Ilex cassine for the manufacture of the black drink , the erection of artificial mounds , the belief in a deity called `` Master of Life , '' and original sun-worship", "context_len": 72}}}, "tract": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 51, "context": "He loved equality and fraternity , and he came out to America towards the close of the last century to prospect for these as well as for a good location to manufacture Welsh flannels ; but after being presented to Washington , then President , at Philadelphia , and buying a tract of land somewhere near the District of Columbia , his phantom rolls a shadowy barrel of dollars on board ship at Baltimore , and sails back in the Flying Dutchman to South Wales", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Muskhogans '' inhabited the tract north of Pensacola", "context_len": 9}}}, "phantom": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "He loved equality and fraternity , and he came out to America towards the close of the last century to prospect for these as well as for a good location to manufacture Welsh flannels ; but after being presented to Washington , then President , at Philadelphia , and buying a tract of land somewhere near the District of Columbia , his phantom rolls a shadowy barrel of dollars on board ship at Baltimore , and sails back in the Flying Dutchman to South Wales", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Matt relapsed into silence , while the car continued to speed along the gloomy , tree-bordered road , following the long shafts of light like a phantom locomotive on gleaming rails", "context_len": 31}}}, "tradition": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 5, "context": "I fancy , from the tradition of the dollars , that he had made good affairs here with the stock of flannels he brought over with him ; but all is rather uncertain about him , especially the land he bought , though the story of it is pretty sure to fire some descendant of his in each new generation with the wish to go down to Washington , and oust the people there who have unrightfully squatted on the ancestral property", "context_len": 82}}}, "descendant": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "I fancy , from the tradition of the dollars , that he had made good affairs here with the stock of flannels he brought over with him ; but all is rather uncertain about him , especially the land he bought , though the story of it is pretty sure to fire some descendant of his in each new generation with the wish to go down to Washington , and oust the people there who have unrightfully squatted on the ancestral property", "context_len": 82}}}, "generation": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 16, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}}}, "oust": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 70, "context": "I fancy , from the tradition of the dollars , that he had made good affairs here with the stock of flannels he brought over with him ; but all is rather uncertain about him , especially the land he bought , though the story of it is pretty sure to fire some descendant of his in each new generation with the wish to go down to Washington , and oust the people there who have unrightfully squatted on the ancestral property", "context_len": 82}}}, "squatted": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 91, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}}}, "ancestral": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 80, "context": "I fancy , from the tradition of the dollars , that he had made good affairs here with the stock of flannels he brought over with him ; but all is rather uncertain about him , especially the land he bought , though the story of it is pretty sure to fire some descendant of his in each new generation with the wish to go down to Washington , and oust the people there who have unrightfully squatted on the ancestral property", "context_len": 82}}}, "unquestionable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "What is unquestionable is that this old gentleman went home and never came out here again ; but his son , who had inherited all his radicalism , sailed with his family for Boston in 1808 , when my boy 's father was a year old", "context_len": 46}}}, "radicalism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "What is unquestionable is that this old gentleman went home and never came out here again ; but his son , who had inherited all his radicalism , sailed with his family for Boston in 1808 , when my boy 's father was a year old", "context_len": 46}}}, "disastrous": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 33, "context": "From Boston he passed to one Quaker neighborhood after another , in New York , Virginia , and Ohio , setting up the machinery of woollen mills , and finally , after much disastrous experiment in farming , paused at the Boy 's Town , and established himself in the drug and book business : drugs and books are still sold together , I believe , in small places", "context_len": 69}}}, "experiment": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 79, "context": "But more incomprehensible than the existence of so bad a man in such a dark age is the renewed confidence ever accorded him , when , after more than once betraying the armies of his country into the hands of their foes , and fighting openly in the hostile cause , he is again forgiven , nay , received into favour , and sent once more to command the men he has already deceived , until he repeats the experiment , and when it fails is again admitted into confidence", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "But it was one thing to find them , and quite another to persuade them to swallow my medicine , and it was not until at length I administered a pretty stiff dose to myself that I prevailed upon a man to allow me to experiment upon him", "context_len": 48}}}, "drugs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "The door opened , and Edward left the room after a conference with the physician , who sat in a corner of the room compounding drugs at a small table ; a few minutes passed in silence , when he returned and held the door open for the bishop of London , who entered , bearing the viaticum , as the last communion of the sick was then called , and attended by an acolyte , who bore a lighted taper before him and carried a bell", "context_len": 87}}}, "righteous": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 38, "context": "I am much better as a rule ; but it came over me in church how proper people were , and they all of them talk about being miserable sinners , and every one looks so good and righteous , and knowing down deep in their hearts that every single one of them is a miserable sinner , except your darling , precious self ; and they all repeat the words , not feeling them a bit", "context_len": 77}}}, "grandson": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 71, "context": "So thither flocked the young and the old : the wood rangers and hunters from the forests of Newenham , where Herstan had right of wood cutting ; the men who wove baskets and hurdles of osier work from the river banks ; the theows who cultivated the home farm ; the ceorls who rented a hide of land here and a hide there -- all , the grandfather and the grandson , accepted the invitation to feast", "context_len": 78}}}, "abolitionist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "He had long ceased to be a Quaker , but he remained a Friend to every righteous cause ; and brought shame to his grandson 's soul by being an abolitionist in days when it was infamy to wish the slaves set free", "context_len": 43}}}, "infamy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "He had long ceased to be a Quaker , but he remained a Friend to every righteous cause ; and brought shame to his grandson 's soul by being an abolitionist in days when it was infamy to wish the slaves set free", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Fortunately none , save half a dozen knights of our langue , saw the affair , and you may be sure we shall say nothing about it ; and instead of Rivers ' name going down to infamy , it will appear in the list of those who died in the defence of Rhodes", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Alexander ordered him to be publicly scourged ; then he caused his face to be mutilated in a manner customary in those days , when a tyrant wished to stamp upon his victim a perpetual mark of infamy", "context_len": 38}}}, "constitutional": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "My boy 's father restored his self-respect in a measure by being a Henry Clay Whig , or a constitutional anti-slavery man", "context_len": 22}}}, "fervent": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "How odd it seemed ! She thought sometimes that she felt reprehensibly young , as if she was having girlhood over again in her heart , but it was a richer , wiser and more fervent girlhood , with the added experiences of the woman", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "After the death of Birinus , the zeal of his successors failed to evangelise the southeastern districts of Wessex , until , at length , came Wilfrid , fervent in zeal , and , stationing himself at Selsey , near Chichester , evangelised both Sussex and Wessex , sending out missionaries like-minded with himself , even into the most inaccessible wilds", "context_len": 61}}}, "doctrines": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "You also will join us ; I know it is long since you have heard the words of pure doctrine , yet there is a blessing in reserve for the seed of the righteous , and the filth of the Presbyterian doctrines you learned in Scotland shall not cleave unto your feet to make them stumble in the way", "context_len": 59}}}, "spiritual": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 83, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}}}, "hell": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` So did I at first , but I feel convinced that the young Dane who vanished so suspiciously must have deceived the prince concerning the presence of Edric in the Danish camp , and that we have no sufficient reason for thinking him such a child of hell as he would be could he betray his country thus cruelly", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` It was hell let loose , and our boys copped it", "context_len": 12}}}, "destroying": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 68, "context": "The vast throng then rolled along upon the top of the wall till they came to stairways and slopes by which they could descend into the city , and , pouring down through all these avenues , they spread over the streets , and satiated the hatred and rage , which had been gathering strength for seven long months , in bursting into houses , and killing and destroying all that came in their way", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "[ 80 ] Their hostilities with the Tangipahoa are referred to by the French annalists , and ended in the destroying of the Tangipahoa town by the Huma ; Margry IV , 168", "context_len": 33}}}, "fiends": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 47, "context": "Fifty men ! and how could we resist them ? The poor old gleemen expressed their readiness to fight for the old hall , and so did even the boys ; but these accursed pagans are the very spawn of the evil one , and fight like fiends , whom they equal in skill , so that I saw at once there was no chance in resistance", "context_len": 67}}}, "revengeful": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 61, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Have a brother about four years older than yourself , I should say ; and if your temper is hot , and your disposition revengeful , and you are a vain and ridiculous dreamer at the same time that you are eager to excel in feats of strength and games of skill , and to do everything that the other fellows do , and are ashamed to be better than the worst boy in the crowd , your brother can be of the greatest use to you , with his larger experience and wisdom", "context_len": 94}}}, "accessible": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "My boy preferred the company of good angels after dark , and especially about bedtime , and he usually made the effort to get himself into an accessible frame of mind before he slept ; by day he felt that he could look out for himself , and gave way to the natural man like other boys", "context_len": 57}}}, "unwholesome": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "There had been quite a spell of sickness in Gorswen at the end of the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change", "context_len": 128}}}, "affliction": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 82, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "Many facts therein given have been rejected by the Author as too harrowing in their nature ; and he has preferred to render the contemplation of woe and suffering less painful , by a display of those virtues of patience , resignation , and brave submission to the Divine will , which affliction never fails to bring out in the fold of Christ , whose promise stands ever fast , that the strength of His people shall be equal to their needs", "context_len": 82}}}, "precepts": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "I am afraid that the father would sometimes forget his own precepts , and tease a little ; when the mother went with him she was sometimes troubled at the warmth with which the controversy raged", "context_len": 36}}}, "controversy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Me eatee aftelward , '' said Ki Sing when he perceived that his presence at the table was the subject of controversy , but he was overruled by Richard Dewey , who possessed a large share of independence , and would not allow himself to be controlled or influenced by the prejudices of others", "context_len": 55}}}, "salvation": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 22, "context": "There is something so touching in his looking at such a moment to the Saviour , whose Blood , shed for his salvation , was at that moment trickling down in his view ; and there is something so sublime in our Lord \u2019 s conferring Eternal Life , -- such a gift , -- at the time when He was Himself undergoing the terrible sentence of death ! We may envy your dear suffering child , my Laura , when we think how soon , in human expectation , his eyes will behold the King in His beauty", "context_len": 99}}}, "abstract": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The grandmother seemed to be bored by it , and the boys , who cared nothing for salvation in the abstract , no matter how anxious they were about the main chance , certainly shared this feeling with her", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "In the eyes of the missionaries and Christian settlers , the paramount importance and abstract character of the Master of Breath made him appear as the centre of an almost monotheistic religion ; but on closer investigation it will be found that the Creeks believed in many genii and mythic animals besides , two of which were the isti-p\u00e1pa and the snake , which furnished the snake horn as a war-talisman", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "His little game had been spoiled , for rightly supposing that Jake did not know how much money there was in the wallet , he had intended to abstract at least half the contents and appropriate it to his own use", "context_len": 41}}}, "plainness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "She was a pale , little , large-eyed lady , who always wore a dress of Quakerish plainness , with a white kerchief crossed upon her breast ; and her aquiline nose and jutting chin almost met", "context_len": 37}}}, "kerchief": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}}}, "aquiline": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "She was a pale , little , large-eyed lady , who always wore a dress of Quakerish plainness , with a white kerchief crossed upon her breast ; and her aquiline nose and jutting chin almost met", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "His hair was a deep grizzled red , as also his beard , and his eyes were of the same tinge , his nose somewhat aquiline , and his whole features , weatherworn as they were , were those of one born to command , while they lacked the sheer brutality of expression so conspicuous in some of his subordinates", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Richardson rose beside his desk , on which he tapped lightly with his knuckles , and surveyed the scholars over his spectacles , which seemed to cling precariously to the tip of his thin , aquiline nose", "context_len": 37}}}, "jutting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "She was a pale , little , large-eyed lady , who always wore a dress of Quakerish plainness , with a white kerchief crossed upon her breast ; and her aquiline nose and jutting chin almost met", "context_len": 37}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "They cheered the objects as they floated by , and they were breathless with the excitement of seeing the men who caught fence-rails and cord-wood , and even saw-logs , with iron prongs at the points of long poles , as they stood on some jutting point of shore and stretched far out over the flood", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The current carried the raft toward the further side of the river , and before long it struck heavily against a jutting point of land", "context_len": 25}}}, "chin": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` So saying , he did off the Maugrabin 's chin veil and the Lady Bedrulbudour looked and saw a man whose beard covered his face ; whereupon she at once knew the truth and said to Alaeddin , `` O my beloved , twice have I cast thee into danger of death ; '' and he said to her , `` O Lady Bedrulbudour , thanks to thine eyes , [ 670 ] no harm [ hath betided me thereof ; nay , ] I accept with all joy everything that cometh to me through thee", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Certainly the poor old lady felt lonely enough , as , with her clasped hands lying upon her lap , her chin sunk upon her breast , and her eyes fixed upon the fire , she thought of the long , long ago , when she , too , was young , bright , and happy ; when those around lived only for her happiness", "context_len": 65}}}, "morals": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Oh ! if only every one might have his turn in this world of ours ; but since that is out of the question , let there at least be as much Coney Island for housemaids as is consistent with good morals and faithful discharge of their duties ; at least so thought one dear little mistress , with more heart , perhaps , than discretion , but a heart , all the same , that won every one to her and made life in her household move with infinite smoothness", "context_len": 91}}}, "aggrieved": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "He would not have allowed them to play where it could have aggrieved any one , for a prime article of his religion was to respect the religious feelings of others , even when he thought them wrong", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 11, "context": "Much offended at these remarks , Bobby stalked away in such aggrieved majesty that , as the best means to restore peace , Peggy suggested that they should walk on to a larger stream , which emptied itself into the lake about half a mile lower down", "context_len": 47}}}, "prime": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 6, "context": "Whilst Tip was still in his prime the family of children was further enriched by the possession of a goat ; but this did not belong to the whole family , or it was , at least nominally , the property of that eldest brother they all looked up to", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was still in the prime of life , still under fifty ; and an eager desire took hold of him to enter public life once more , to serve again his own country , as well as the eastern land of his adoption", "context_len": 44}}}, "notion": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 14, "context": "Well , now , let Harold , who is pretty well out of the notion of Ted already , come to hear of this last scrape , and , youngster as he is , I believe he 'd throw him over ; and Ted , you know , would n't stand any nonsense of that sort and would tell Harold 'to go his own way and welcome , ' and who knows what the upshot of that would be ! If Ted does not feel he must make an effort to lead a different sort of life for Harold 's sake , he may come to the conclusion that the thing 's not worth trying", "context_len": 115}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 81, "context": "The story , as I have said , was imagined from events in Irving 's history of the `` Conquest of Granada , '' a book which the boy loved hardly less than the monkish legends of `` Gesta Romanorum , '' and it concerned the rival fortunes of Hamet el Zegri and Boabdil el Chico , the uncle and nephew who vied with each other for the crumbling throne of the Moorish kingdom ; but I have not the least notion how it all ended", "context_len": 86}}}, "deadly": {"freq": 76, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 75, "word_index": 99, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Not a few dead bodies lying in the glare of the fire testified to their deadly skill", "context_len": 17}}}, "crime": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` `` Far be it '' ( as the Arabs say ) that we should do so cruel a wrong to so well and justly beloved a memory as that of Jacques Cazotte as to attempt to perpetuate the remembrance of a literary crime which one can hardly believe him to have committed in sober earnest ! Rather let us seek to bury in oblivion this his one offence and suffer kind Lethe with its beneficent waters to wash this `` adulterous blot '' from his else unsullied name", "context_len": 89}}}, "conventional": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 67, "context": "Their voices were subdued lest they should reach the king 's ears ; but , respectful as was the bearing of all the members of the group by the fire , they clearly split into two halves : on the one hand , the officers of the escort who were teasing the boy , and on the other , a group of gentlemen , some wearing the conventional ribbons and laces of a cavalier , others in the rough cloth of country wear , stained with the mud of country lanes , while the master and mistress of the house moved from one guest to another , evidently nervous at the doubtful honour that such a royal visit had brought to their roof", "context_len": 123}}}, "rebuked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Once , when their grandfather reported to him that the boys had been seen throwing stones on Sunday at the body of a dog lodged on some drift in the river , he rebuked them for the indecorum , and then ended the matter , as he often did , by saying , `` Boys , consider yourselves soundly thrashed", "context_len": 60}}}, "indecorum": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Once , when their grandfather reported to him that the boys had been seen throwing stones on Sunday at the body of a dog lodged on some drift in the river , he rebuked them for the indecorum , and then ended the matter , as he often did , by saying , `` Boys , consider yourselves soundly thrashed", "context_len": 60}}}, "thrashed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 17, "context": "Peggy does not feel the least little bit older than when she climbed the water-wheel , or thrashed Jones minor behind the paling of the cricket-field -- if growing old means losing your ideals , and your keen enthusiasm , and your hopefulness , and a certain appreciation of other people 's worth which is our birthright when we are young", "context_len": 61}}}, "behavior": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 12, "context": "He conformed his conduct to the principles and maxims which actuated the behavior of the shadowy people of these dry-as-dust tales ; he went about drunk with the fumes of fables about Roman emperors that never were , in an empire that never was ; and , though they tormented him by putting a mixed and impossible civilization in the place of that he knew from his Goldsmith , he was quite helpless to break from their influence", "context_len": 78}}}, "fantastic": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 51, "context": "Could it be that she had made a mistake , after all , and that the neglected child had some powerful though eccentric friend in the background -- perhaps some previously unknown relation , who had suddenly traced her whereabouts , and chose to provide for her in this mysterious and fantastic way ? Relations were sometimes very odd -- particularly rich old bachelor uncles , who did not care for having children near them", "context_len": 75}}}, "arrogant": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "Rage , blinding rage , at the man who had killed his father , at Japazy , the half-breed , the arrogant , heartless monster who lorded it over this ignorant people", "context_len": 32}}}, "pervading": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was simply a pervading influence ; and I am sure that in the father and mother it dignified life , and freighted motive and action here with the significance of eternal fate", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "There was a pleasant aroma of gingerbread pervading the house , and the fire in the open grate looked very cheerful", "context_len": 21}}}, "freighted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "One could not but think , What a mercy it is that we did not pass those large icebergs in the night ! Had our great emigrant-ship , freighted with 2000 tons of iron , dashed up against one of them , we should have gone to the bottom like lead", "context_len": 51}}}, "motive": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 50, "context": "Gervaise , who had , -- thanks partly to his skill with his weapons , but still more to the temper of the splendid suit of armour presented to him by Genoa , -- escaped without a scratch , volunteered to remain with him until next morning , his principal motive for making the request being his desire to escape from further congratulations and praise for the success of his plan", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "The law exercised by the gens was more powerful than all these temporary rulings , and , in fact , was the real motive power in the Indian community", "context_len": 29}}}, "eternal": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 67, "context": "The lych-gate was locked , but she toiled over the steep steps that spanned the wall , and wading through the long grass under the yew-trees , found the spot , all covered with flowers , which Lilian had pointed out on Sunday , where 'Mary , the wife of the Reverend Philip Howell , ' slept , 'in sure and certain hope of the resurrection to eternal life , ' and where the stonemason had been already busy with the newly-added line : 'Also Raymond , only son of the above", "context_len": 92}}}, "fate": {"freq": 160, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 158, "word_index": 123, "context": "my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction , you shall meet with the fate you so justly deserve", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "You can make up your minds I wo n't tempt fate that way again", "context_len": 14}}}, "choosing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 78, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Indeed , she did not care to realize anything of the sort , choosing , rather , to think of the Windsor of Isabel 's time as much the same as the Windsor of Victoria 's , and she would have been not a little grieved and surprised had any one insisted on pointing out to her in how many , many ways the old differed from the new", "context_len": 69}}}, "devils": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "When the children were taught that in every thought and in every deed they were choosing their portion with the devils or the angels , and that God himself could not save them against themselves , it often went in and out of their minds , as such things must with children ; but some impression remained and helped them to realize the serious responsibility they were under to their own after-selves", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "Then the boys saw him going about the streets , pale and tremulous , in a second-hand suit of his benefactor 's clothes , and fighting hard against the tempter that beset him on every side in that town ; and then some day they saw him dead drunk in a fence corner ; and they did not understand how seven devils worse than the first had entered in the place which had been swept and garnished for them", "context_len": 79}}}, "despised": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 121, "context": "in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war , and praising Little Van , whom he had hitherto despised as the `` Fox of Kinderhook", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "To say what this Hindu did of despised Missionaries , a band of weak-minded amiable enthusiasts , if not something more contemptible , -- as the world thinks them , -- showed moral courage", "context_len": 34}}}, "austerity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "Hartley know , he asked after a pause , that Ted ought not to be moved ? Will he be willing that he should stay ? for Harry stood in considerable awe of the master of the house , who , it could not be denied , was conducting himself through this whole affair with no little austerity of deportment", "context_len": 60}}}, "owlish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "At the same time , the father , who loved a joke almost as much as he loved a truth , and who despised austerity as something owlish , set them the example of getting all the harmless fun they could out of experience", "context_len": 44}}}, "essentially": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 61, "context": "As we grew older , we not only more fully understood the exceptional boundlessness of old-fashioned hospitality and kindness which that house and household exemplified thoroughly , but we came to understand somewhat of the heart-source whence issued that truest manifestation , of everyday religion , which evidences itself in an absolutely unselfish consecration , -- consistent , unreserved , and essentially practical , -- for everyday wear , and not only under stimulating environments", "context_len": 75}}}, "ludicrous": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "The young instructor , presenting , by the way , a ludicrous contrast in his shining uniform of gray and white and gold , with his three or four nervous and variously costumed pupils , takes the bayonet of his gun for a drill stick and marches `` his '' squad over into a secluded corner of the area and thus begins the above-mentioned instructions :", "context_len": 66}}}, "alleviation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Reade -- who could doubt him who looked into his face ? I guessed him to be what he was , one of the strongest , most faithful , bravest men who ever lived -- a man whose whole life was given up to the alleviation of the suffering of others", "context_len": 51}}}, "severally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}}}, "literal": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "[ Footnote 436 : The whole of the foregoing passage is so confused that I think it well to add here ( l ) a literal translation , as I read it : `` So the Vizier , yea , indeed , he marvelled at the greatness of that wealth more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more when he saw the Sultan that he was satisfied with ( or accepted of ) the bride-gift and the dowry ; however , it was not possible to him that he should gainsay the truth and should say to the Sultan , 'He is not worthy ; ' only , he practised with a device upon the Sultan so he should", "context_len": 128}}}, "application": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 31, "context": "I gathered from their remarks that they were seriously concerned at my condition , for it seemed that I was reserved for some very especial refinement of torture , the satisfactory application of which demanded that I must come to it in the possession of my full strength , which they feared had been seriously sapped by the suffering which I had already endured , and they freely expressed their concern lest , under existing circumstances , I should not furnish quite so much sport as was being expected of me", "context_len": 91}}}, "edifying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was very dark , and hung full of clothes , and his literal application of the text was not edifying ; he fancied , with a child 's vague suspicion , that it amused his father and mother ; I dare say it also touched them", "context_len": 47}}}, "upper": {"freq": 116, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 116, "word_index": 13, "context": "Of the Smith house , he could remember much more : the little upper room where the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the", "context_len": 128}}}, "bled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "Of the Smith house , he could remember much more : the little upper room where the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the", "context_len": 128}}}, "woodshed": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 61, "context": "Of the Smith house , he could remember much more : the little upper room where the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Every house , whether it had a flower-garden or not , had a woodshed , which was the place where a boy mostly received his friends , and made his kites and wagons , and laid his plots and plans for all the failures of his life", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The other boys waited in the woodshed when he went in to ask his mother whether he might do this or that , or go somewhere", "context_len": 26}}}, "traced": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 82, "context": "the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the mind by the capricious action of memory , while far greater events drop out of it", "context_len": 128}}}, "greased": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 89, "context": "the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the mind by the capricious action of memory , while far greater events drop out of it", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Then a frying pan was put on the stove , for they had brought along no regular griddle , it was greased , and Frank , who insisted on doing the rest , was allowed to pour out the batter , and do the turning", "context_len": 45}}}, "engraving": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 93, "context": "the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the mind by the capricious action of memory , while far greater events drop out of it", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Do you remember the well-known engraving of Una with her lion entering a witch \u2019 s cave ? Now , as I jogged along in my duli , [ 40 ] while Margaret rode on her white pony , she made me think of that picture of Una", "context_len": 48}}}, "capricious": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 115, "context": "the boys slept , and the narrow stairs which he often rolled down in the morning ; the front room where he lay sick with a fever , and was bled by the doctor , as people used to be in those days ; the woodshed where , one dreadful afternoon , when he had somehow been left alone in the house , he took it into his head that the family dog Tip was going mad ; the window where he traced the figure of a bull on greased paper from an engraving held up against the light : none of them important facts , but such as stick in the mind by the capricious action of memory , while far greater events drop out of it", "context_len": 128}}}, "accused": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 83, "context": "`` That big fellow on the bough there tells me he is delighted ; that he has at last found one that can understand his language , for he has heard so many ridiculous theories advanced by men with picks on their shoulders and books in their hands , as to what gold is , and how it came on the ground , that he has nearly burst his throat in trying to make them understand the truth , and has then been accused of making a 'senseless chatter", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "But amongst those who stood nearest to the place he must occupy were his betrothed , her mother , Bertha , and young Hermann , who had already got into several quarrels through his fierce espousing of the cause of the accused", "context_len": 42}}}, "tracing": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 3, "context": "Among the nations tracing their mythic origin to the earth , or what amounts to the same thing , to caves , deep holes , hills or mountains , are the Pomo of Northern California , who believe that their ancestors , the coyote-men , were created directly from a knoll of red earth , [ 135 ] still visible in their country ; the Nahua , whose seven tribes issued from Chicomoztoc or the `` Seven Caves", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Hannington has kindly made a tracing of the page in the bishop 's little pocket diary for 28th October , the day before his martyrdom took place", "context_len": 27}}}, "theory": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was weary , anxious work ; for of course our movements were being regulated by a theory that , for aught we knew to the contrary , might be all wrong ; and as day succeeded day without bringing the expected sail within our ken there were not wanting among us those who denounced the skipper 's plan as foolish , and argued that the proper thing would have been to go direct to the Gaboon , and look there for the Virginia", "context_len": 84}}}, "attain": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 107, "context": "son get these dishes of jewels ? Supposing , for the jewels and the dishes , that he return to the treasure and gather the whole from the trees , -- and withal methinketh not it is possible to him ; but say that he fetch them , -- whence [ shall he get ] the slaves and slave-girls ? '' And she gave not over talking to herself till she reached the house , where Alaeddin awaited her , and when she came in to him , she said to him , `` O my son , said I not to thee , 'Think not to attain to the Lady Bedrulbudour ' ? Indeed , this is a thing that is not possible unto folk like ourselves", "context_len": 128}}}, "fibbing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "It was called `` The Trippings of Tom Pepper ; or , the Effects of Romancing , '' and it showed how at many important moments the hero had been baulked of fortune by his habit of fibbing", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "My boy distinctly remembered that under one of these trees his elder brother first broached to him that awful scheme of reform about fibbing , and applied to their own lives the moral of `` The Trippings of Tom Pepper ; '' he remembered how a conviction of the righteousness of the scheme sank into his soul , and he could not withhold his consent", "context_len": 65}}}, "pledged": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 68, "context": "I do not say that every one was not before disposed to obey you in all things , willingly and cheerfully ; but he put it so strongly to them that they had volunteered specially for service in this galley , knowing well who was to be its commander , and the circumstance that the crew was to consist solely of young knights , and had therefore specially pledged their honour so to act that the enterprise should be in all respects a successful one", "context_len": 85}}}, "trial": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 97, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "deceits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Their great trial was having to take a younger brother with them whenever they wanted to go off with other boys ; and it had been their habit to get away from him by many little deceits which they could not practise now : to tell him that their mother wanted him ; or to send him home upon some errand to his pretended advantage that had really no object but his absence", "context_len": 73}}}, "absence": {"freq": 112, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 112, "word_index": 127, "context": "wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his absence", "context_len": 128}}}, "backsliding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "My boy and his brother groaned under their good resolution , I do not know how long ; but the day came when they could bear it no longer , though I can not give just the time or the terms of their backsliding", "context_len": 44}}}, "reform": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 21, "context": "My boy distinctly remembered that under one of these trees his elder brother first broached to him that awful scheme of reform about fibbing , and applied to their own lives the moral of `` The Trippings of Tom Pepper ; '' he remembered how a conviction of the righteousness of the scheme sank into his soul , and he could not withhold his consent", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 48, "context": "Why , mother has been writing to him , and writing to your mother , too ; and the one thing about you that I do n't quite like is that mother had evidently been thinking that you have been sent as a sort of Providence here to reform me", "context_len": 50}}}, "uprightness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` No , '' repeated Lena , still more vexed that he should suppose her to be capable of such an evasion , which to her sense of uprightness would have been as bad as speaking a falsehood by word of mouth ; `` no , of course I did not , that would have been telling her , would it not ? One can do a falsehood as well as tell it , '' and although she had intended no reflection upon her brother , no thrust at him , Percy was ashamed as he remembered how often , during the last few months , he had done this very thing ; how he had shuffled and evaded , and thought it no great harm as long", "context_len": 128}}}, "truthfulness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "That it should come to this -- that she , Peggy Vaughan , who , whatever might be her faults , had always held such an unstained reputation for honour and truthfulness , should be deemed capable of such a mean and discreditable action seemed too hard to be borne", "context_len": 50}}}, "absolute": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 22, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}, "NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 39, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I thought to myself with a dull ache , which never rose to absolute pain , how soon they would give me up , when they knew that I was engaged to Albert Fanning", "context_len": 34}}}, "sincerity": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 55, "context": "Arnold , that if he found out that people knew about a scrape like this , I believe he 'd grow perfectly reckless , and there would n't be any such thing as saving him ; and there was such suppressed earnestness in the young fellow 's voice that no one could have doubted his sincerity for a moment", "context_len": 59}}}, "wretchedness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "He seemed to forget for brief periods his misfortune and wretchedness , but he could not put his bitter thoughts aside for long , and whenever he tried to do so , they simply slunk in the background , to come swarming upon him again at the first opportunity", "context_len": 49}}}, "virtue": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 49, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}}}, "vice": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 50, "context": "Young Hermann 's sword broke against a Danish axe ; he rushed in and got within the swing of the weapon ; both wrestled for the deadly steel , they fell , rolled over and over on the grass ; at length Hermann grasped his opponent 's throat like a vice with his mailed hand , and held till the arms of his foe hung nerveless by the side and the face grew black , when , disengaging his right hand , he found his dagger , and drove it to the victim 's heart", "context_len": 95}}}, "superstitious": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 63, "context": "This was not , however , so impious as it would at first view seem , for the gods whom the ancients worshiped were , in fact , only deifications of old heroes and kings who had lived in early times , and had acquired a reputation for supernatural powers by the fame of their exploits , exaggerated in descending by tradition in superstitious times", "context_len": 65}}}, "genius": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 58, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "neighbor": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 83, "context": "`` I know I ought to be thankful , and so I mostly am , that I and my friends ai n't no worse off than we are , and I know it 's no good to be frettin ' and worryin ' about your trials , and settin ' yourself against the Lord 's will ; but I do say if I fall down and break my arm , there ai n't a grain of comfort in hearin ' that my next-door neighbor has broken both his", "context_len": 88}}}, "talons": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` asked the princess , `` and where shall we find its egg ? '' And the Moor said to her , `` O my lady , this is a great bird that taketh up camels and elephants in its talons and flieth with them , of its bigness and greatness ; it is mostly to be found in the mountain Caf and the craftsman who builded this palace [ 654 ] is able to bring its egg", "context_len": 78}}}, "radiating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Nicholas , though he was so pleasant and friendly in the poem about the night before Christmas , was known to some of the neighbor boys as Santa Claus ; they called it Centre Claws , and my boy imagined him with large talons radiating from the pit of his stomach", "context_len": 51}}}, "pit": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 49, "context": "Then he embraced Alaeddin and fell to kissing him and said to him , `` Excuse me , O my son , for that I was going [ 623 ] to bereave thee of thy life , through the wickedness of yonder accursed sorcerer who cast thee into this pit ; and indeed , O my son , I was excusable in that which I did with thee , inasmuch as I saw myself bereft of my daughter and mine only one , who is dearer to me than my kingdom , and thou knowest how fathers ' hearts yearn upon their children , more by token that I have but the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 114}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "From now on he must pit his wit against brute strength , trusting to his keen brain to win against odds that would otherwise be overwhelming", "context_len": 26}}}, "joked": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "Then , at five every clear day , afternoon tea with the captain , for which they had a standing invitation , and by means of which both she and Chris came to be on terms of wonderful intimacy with that august officer , so that they joked over the rare souchong and delicious little toasted cakes ( the secret of whose making was kept close-guarded by the steward ) with a familiarity that , to themselves at least , never ceased to be a wonder", "context_len": 86}}}, "represented": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 103, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "glassless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "But this was all nothing to the notion of Dowd 's spectacles , which his father sometimes joked him about , and which were represented by a pair of hollow , glassless iron rims which he had found in the street", "context_len": 41}}}, "rims": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "But this was all nothing to the notion of Dowd 's spectacles , which his father sometimes joked him about , and which were represented by a pair of hollow , glassless iron rims which he had found in the street", "context_len": 41}}}, "gruesome": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "For the space of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "We 've had an ugly accident , said one of the men , as the light from within fell upon them ; and then as Chris held the door wide open they pressed into the little sitting-room with their gruesome burden", "context_len": 41}}}, "ghastly": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 61, "context": "They may or may not have belonged to Dowd , and Dowd may have been an Irishman in the neighborhood , or he may not ; he may have died , or he may not ; but there was something in the mere gruesome mention of his spectacles which related itself to all the boy had conceived of the ghostly and ghastly , and all that was alarming in the supernatural ; he could never say in the least how or why", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The bodies of the dead were committed to the deep , the wounded attended to , and the decks cleaned of their ghastly stains , while a party of seamen were placed on board the prize to rig jury masts", "context_len": 40}}}, "supernatural": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 84, "context": "peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to laugh at the supernatural , she did not feel quite so brave as she pretended , and found little cold shivers stealing down her back when the wind rose suddenly , and began to wail round the cottage like a hungry creature waiting to be let in", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 65, "context": "of the Cabinet des Fees ( printed in 1793 , though antedated 1788 and 1789 ) do not form the first edition of Chavis and Cazotte 's so-called Sequel , which was in 1793 added , by way of supplement , to the Cabinet des Fees , having been first published in 1788 ( two years after the completion-in thirty-seven volumes-of that great storehouse of supernatural fiction ) under the title of `` Les Veillees Persanes '' or `` Les Veillees du Sultan Schahriar avec la Sultane Scheherazade , histoires incroyables , amusantes et morales , traduites par M", "context_len": 99}}}, "shadows": {"freq": 85, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 55, "context": "She meant to lay the hands together , as she remembered her mother 's had been laid long ago in the stricken gloom of the Kansas farmhouse which had been her home ; but suddenly there was a movement at her feet , and she stopped , having stumbled over some living thing in the shadows of the couch , something that stirred and struggled and gasped passionately , `` Vamos ! Vamos ! ''", "context_len": 75}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "But there was no room in Dorothy 's mind just then for musings either on nature or Ruskin , and it is to be feared that the dancing blue of the water and the purple shadows on the hills and golden glow of the sunset made little impression on her wholly preoccupied mind", "context_len": 53}}}, "projected": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "The spears were so long that when the men stood in close order , the rear ranks being brought up near to those before them , the points of the spears of eight or ten of the ranks projected in front , forming a bristling wall of points of steel , each one of which was held in its place by the strong arms of an athletic and well-trained soldier", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "With your photographs , Sedgwick , we shall have ample material for our projected treatise , which I trust should be ready for the September issue of the Arch\u00e6ologian ' -- and the little old gentleman sat down on a broken column , and pulled a sheaf of papers out of one of his many pockets", "context_len": 56}}}, "outlook": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 34, "context": "However , by leaving scraps of her clothing on every sharp thorn , and getting her hands and legs terribly scratched , she forced her way out at last ; and keeping a wary outlook on the fort , she tried to unloose the knots that bound Jim", "context_len": 48}}}, "peopled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then he traversed the Tallise `` province , '' peopled with many towns , and entered the great pueblo of Tallise on September 18th , to stay there twenty days", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He states that de Soto 's army usually marched five to six leagues a day in peopled countries , but when passing through deserted lands proceeded faster", "context_len": 27}}}, "shapes": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 65, "context": "In the Park Crescent Gardens , near Portland Place , -- their playground ; described by one friend in those days as a jungle , because of its unkempt condition , -- she would name one bed England , another France , another Germany , and so on , and would thus fix in the children \u2019 s minds their various positions , though the shapes and sizes of the beds were by no means always what they ought to have been", "context_len": 82}}}, "doom": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 48, "context": "This popular fear of agitators who might disturb the rule of the Merry Monarch chimed in very well with the feelings of the old cavaliers , who felt that heavy amends were due to them for the sorrows and hardships of the last twenty years , and no doom could be too awful for the murderers who had laid sacrilegious hands upon the sacred person of the king", "context_len": 68}}}, "invention": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 17, "context": "The idea that the Creeks knew anything of the devil of the Christian religion is a pure invention of the missionaries ; being christianized , they call him now : \u00edsti f\u00fatchig\u014d `` the man acting perversely , '' taso l\u00e4 ya , or : \u00edsti nikl\u00e9-idsha ats\u016b li `` the old person-burner '' ( \u00e1ni nikl\u00e9-idsh\u00e4s I burn somebody , something ) ; the Yuchi call him `` the swinging man , '' just as they call a ghost `` a hunting man", "context_len": 84}}}, "prisons": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 60, "context": "Will you let him sit down here by the helm ? My instructions are that he is not to mingle with the other slaves ; and as there are reasons why it is wished that his coming on board in this manner shall not be known to them , I myself am to take him up to one of the prisons , or at least to hand him over to the officer sent down from that prison to take up the captives allotted to it", "context_len": 85}}}, "misery": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 1, "context": "O misery ! to wake in darkness , wake From vision of unutterable joy ; Instead of trumpet-sound and song of Heaven , To hear the dull clock measuring out time , When I had seemed to touch Eternity ! In the first pang of disappointed hope , I wept that I could wake from such a dream ; Until Faith gently whispered , Wherefore weep To lose the faint dim shadow of a joy Of which the substance shall one day be thine ? Live in the hope , -- that hope shall brighten life , And sanctify it to its highest end", "context_len": 104}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "They watched the hapless creature with grave , unpitying , yet not unfriendly interest , too ignorant of the dark ills of life to know how deeply tragic was the spectacle that entertained them , and how awfully present in Morn 's contortions was the mystery of God 's ways with his children , some of whom he gives to happiness and some to misery", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "But in spite of these precautions , the nights were misery all the same", "context_len": 14}}}, "arrest": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 28, "context": "William Lang told of his visit to Oak Swamp , and how he had fled at the sight of the boys , fearing they wanted to cause his arrest , and he also mentioned his trips to the mud volcano , and how he had run away at the sound of some one stirring in the campers ' tent , likewise how he had led the boys a chase about the town , just prior to the accident on the pole", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 10, "context": "Several of the Turkish galleys , in their efforts to arrest the approach of the fire ships towards the bridge , became themselves involved in the flames ; but they were so far successful that when daylight broke the bridge was still intact and the combat at the breach continued to rage with determination and fury on both sides", "context_len": 59}}}, "constable": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "Of course no such thing ever happened ; but the boys believed it , and it froze my boy 's soul with fear ; all the more because this constable was a cabinet-maker and made coffins ; from his father 's printing-office the boy could hear the long slide of his plane over the wood , and he could smell the varnish on the boards", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 6, "context": "Jugged ! Jugged by a country constable , just when a telegram might save the day for us in New York ! Sufferin \u2019 cats ! Can \u2019 t we do something , pard ? We \u2019 re not going to let a couple of hayseeds knock us out like this , are we ?", "context_len": 55}}}, "cases": {"freq": 84, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 84, "word_index": 64, "context": "thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing", "context_len": 128}}}, "bolder": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "He grew bolder , after a while , concerning constables , but never concerning ghosts ; they shivered in the autumnal evenings among the tall stalks of the corn-field that stretched , a vast wilderness , behind the house to the next street , and they walked the night everywhere", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "My brother has taken a bolder part in upholding Missions , and spreading religious literature , than almost any one else in the country ; therefore , if Benares had followed the example of Delhi , the terrible event might have been attributed to his excess of zeal", "context_len": 48}}}, "constables": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "I do not mean that there was no law for the boys , for it was manifest to their terror in two officers whom they knew as constables , and who may have reigned one after another , or together , with full power of life and death over them , as they felt ; but who in a community mainly so peaceful acted upon Dogberry 's advice , and made and meddled with rogues as little as they could", "context_len": 80}}}, "autumnal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "He grew bolder , after a while , concerning constables , but never concerning ghosts ; they shivered in the autumnal evenings among the tall stalks of the corn-field that stretched , a vast wilderness , behind the house to the next street , and they walked the night everywhere", "context_len": 50}}}, "stalks": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 52, "context": "But the 'Ladies ' Column ' should have been a little more explicit as to the quantity of flavouring , for , when it came to a matter of eating , the herbs so entirely predominated that the mutton was utterly lost , and , as she had unfortunately cut up the stalks as well as the leaves , the mixture bore a horrible resemblance to chopped hay", "context_len": 68}}}, "tragical": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "Yet nothing more tragical , that he could remember , really happened while he lived in the Smith house than something he saw one bright sunny morning , while all the boys were hanging on the fence of the next house , and watching the martins flying down to the ground from their box in the gable", "context_len": 57}}}, "martins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Yet nothing more tragical , that he could remember , really happened while he lived in the Smith house than something he saw one bright sunny morning , while all the boys were hanging on the fence of the next house , and watching the martins flying down to the ground from their box in the gable", "context_len": 57}}}, "gable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 56, "context": "Yet nothing more tragical , that he could remember , really happened while he lived in the Smith house than something he saw one bright sunny morning , while all the boys were hanging on the fence of the next house , and watching the martins flying down to the ground from their box in the gable", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "The favorite kite , the kite that balanced best , took the wind best , and flew best , and that would stand all day when you got it up , was the house kite , which was made of three sticks , and shaped nearly in the form of the gable of a gambrel-roofed house , only smaller at the base than at the point where the roof would begin", "context_len": 71}}}, "crouching": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 48, "context": "This ended that game , but everybody was so warmed up to the fun that when it was proposed to carry on a game of Hide and Seek out of doors all agreed , and Edna was so convulsed with laughter to see her dignified , great-uncle Wilbur crouching behind a wood-pile and peeping fearfully over the top that she forgot to hide herself properly and was discovered by Ben in a moment", "context_len": 73}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 42, "context": "For a hundred yards or more the trail led over uneven rocky ground toward an immense rock , upon rounding which I found myself face to face with , and within half-a-dozen yards of , a splendid full-grown male leopard who was crouching over poor Ama 's motionless body , snarling savagely as he strove with his claws to remove a broken spear , the head of which was buried deep in his neck", "context_len": 74}}}, "indifference": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 63, "context": "What did it matter to me whether Albert Fanning paid for his household goods or not ? and how could it concern me what shop he chose to buy them at ? But I felt myself more or less in a trap , and knew the best way to prevent any crisis taking place was to put on an assumed air of absolute indifference , and to take the first possible opportunity of returning home", "context_len": 75}}}, "loops": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "There were other companies that used to come to town on the Fourth of July and Muster Day , from smaller places round about ; and some of them had richer uniforms : one company had blue coats with gold epaulets , and gold braid going down in loops on the sides of their legs ; all the soldiers , of course , had braid straight down the outer seams of their pantaloons", "context_len": 73}}}, "fatal": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 27, "context": "It was not of the least use in the world , and could not be , but the children were allowed to keep it till , one fatal day , when the mother had a number of other ladies to tea , as the fashion used to be in small towns , when they sat down to a comfortable gossip over dainty dishes of stewed chicken , hot biscuit , peach-preserves , sweet tomato-pickles , and pound-cake", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "As a scholar he shone ; as a business man he was a failure , owing to the fatal and all too common trait amongst Maoris of the educated class of pleasure in the spending of money , and , oddly enough , to an inherent tendency to relapse , if only temporarily , to an aboriginal existence", "context_len": 58}}}, "martin": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Suddenly she sprang into the air , and then darted away with a martin in her mouth , while my boy 's heart leaped into his own , and the other boys rushed after the cat", "context_len": 36}}}, "enduring": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 65, "context": "He ran home with it , and in his simple-hearted rapture he told his mother that as soon as he picked it up there came into his mind the words , `` He who picketh up this cap picketh up a fortune , '' and he could hardly wait for Monday to come and let him restore the cap to its owner and receive an enduring prosperity in reward of his virtue", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Charlotte \u2019 s affections for everything connected with her youth were of a very enduring nature", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "If her mother wished us to come here to be educated , I suppose she had her good reasons for it , and that Lucy should be such a goose is really past enduring", "context_len": 34}}}, "trampled": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 46, "context": "The tiny fences were pulled up , and scattered far and wide ; lady-slippers , mignonette , verbenas , and all the other simple flowers which had flourished so well , and given such pride and delight to the little gardeners , were rooted up and trampled into the earth ; and , worse than all , the beloved heliotrope and geranium were torn leaf from leaf and sprig from sprig , while their main stems had been twisted and bent , till no hope remained that even these could be revived", "context_len": 92}}}, "rank": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 107, "context": "son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not give them his daughter", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "First and above all , good conduct ; an unselfish , brave , noble character would rank very high indeed", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "'Such a speech from a woman in this age is rank heresy ! '", "context_len": 14}}}, "frolicked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "In their absence the June grass , which the children 's feet always kept trampled down so low , had flourished up in purple blossom , and now stood rank and tall ; and the mother threw herself on her knees in it , and tossed and frolicked with her little ones like a girl", "context_len": 55}}}, "removal": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was a hurried removal of the barriers on the other side of the door , and then the old squaw came rushing out and threw her arms about Bomba \u2019 s neck , crying and laughing in the same breath", "context_len": 41}}}, "principal": {"freq": 78, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 61, "context": "' Bobby took to carrying about an old horse-pistol which he had found in the lumber-room , and saying 'Oddsbodkins ' on all occasions , and Peggy put on such a general air of melodrama that it seemed scarcely possible for her to speak in plain prose ; Archie was impresario , stage-manager , scene-shifter , dresser , maker-up , and principal actor all combined , while Lilian waxed so enthusiastic that she even sacrificed the feathers out of her last summer 's best hat to adorn the slouched head-gear considered necessary for a due representation of Romeo", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 18, "context": "Merton would have spared the doctor the recital of any further disturbance until the morning ; but the principal , having observed all this , would not be put off ; the time was short , and if the matter were a serious one which required investigation , he must have knowledge of it at once", "context_len": 56}}}, "blinds": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "It was a brick house , at a corner of the principal street , and in the gable there were places for mock-windows where there had never been blinds put , but where the swallows had thickly built their nests", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Who is there ? '' Then the voice continued , `` If it is you , Irene , the door is bolted , and the blinds are down , and the shutters shut , so even if you break the glass you can not get in", "context_len": 47}}}, "improve": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 51, "context": "I have tried both , and find there is more real pleasure to be had from the intellectual than from the social side of life , for while the latter is apt to fail us just when we most require it , the former is `` warranted to wear well and improve with keeping , '' and , so far from being affected by the changes and chances of this world , sticks by us when health and wealth and even friends can fall away", "context_len": 85}}}, "havoc": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 14, "context": "It is a timely gift , Sir Gervaise , for the siege has played havoc with the suit Genoa gave you ; it is sorely battered , dinted , and broken , and , although you can doubtless get it repaired , if I were you I would keep it in its present state as a memorial -- and there could be no prouder one -- of the part you bore in the siege", "context_len": 74}}}, "naked": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 4, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "In the present instance a narrow circular course was arranged round the great square , a lane of about a yard in width being formed through the mass of spectators , and into this lane the victims , stripped naked , were introduced , one at a time , to run round and round until beaten to death by the bare fists of as many as could get in a blow at them", "context_len": 73}}}, "rafters": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 62, "context": "Both men were lost in an attempt to reach the island in a small craft in which they had sailed alone , so as to keep their secret , and thus all trace of the spot vanished till five years ago , when Pedro came across the rough chart and an account of the matter , which had been hidden in the rafters of his hut", "context_len": 66}}}, "studded": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "Moreover , she had with her eight and-forty slave-girls , whom Alaeddin had presented to her , in each one 's hand a great candle of camphor and ambergris , set in a candlestick of gold , studded with jewels ; and all the men and women in the palace went out with her and fared on before her , till they brought her to her bridegroom 's palace and carrying her up to her pavilion , [ 495 ] attired her in various robes [ 496 ] and displayed her", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Altogether the vehicle had a lordly air , easily matching that of the individual sitting alone on the purple cushions -- a man whose features were not very clear at the distance , although the yellowness of his beard , the glitter of his studded shirt-front , and whole consequential , expansive effect recalled to the doctor 's mind an image of the past , less ornate , indeed , and affluent , but of similar aspect", "context_len": 77}}}, "wasps": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 24, "context": "If they find the good people of Dorchester are awake , as the noise shows , they will swarm all over the neighbourhood like wasps after honey , to plunder the isolated houses and farms , and carry off all they can ; and this place is too conspicuous -- too much of a city on a hill -- to be hidden", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Last night I was in the pantry , and James told me a lot of things about her ; how she used to get wasps to sting him , and how she frightened away such a lot of servants from the place with leeches and toads , and all sorts of horrors", "context_len": 52}}}, "shingles": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 62, "context": "Bartram , who saw it in 1775 , depicts it as the largest , most compact , and best situated Indian town he ever saw ; the habitations were large and neatly built , the walls of the houses consisted of a wooden frame , lathed and plastered inside and outside with a reddish clay , and roofed with cypress bark or shingles", "context_len": 63}}}, "physician": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 106, "context": "the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change of air , ordering him off immediately to the high meadows of the Alps", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "-- Alexander 's physician Philip", "context_len": 5}}}, "phials": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The house had been occupied by a physician , and under the eaves the children found heaps of phials full of doctor 's stuff ; the garret abounded in their own family boxes and barrels , but there was always room for a swing , which the boys used in training for their circuses", "context_len": 54}}}, "chambers": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 52, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}}}, "basement": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "Below the garret there were two unimportant stories with chambers , dining-room , parlor , and so on ; then you came to the brick-paved kitchen in the basement , and a perfectly glorious cellar , with rats in it", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a basement kitchen with a dining room above", "context_len": 10}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was wrapped in flames from top to basement , and even as we looked one of the towers gave way , and fell upon the hall beneath , with hideous din , in headlong ruin", "context_len": 36}}}, "plot": {"freq": 76, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 42, "context": "Filelfo , who was greatly attached to D'Aubusson , saw by their manner that they wished to engage him in some intrigue , and , feigning great resentment and anger at his disgrace , led them on until they divulged the entire plot for D'Aubusson 's assassination , and made brilliant offers to him if he would afford them facilities for carrying it out , producing , in proof of their power to do so , a letter of the pasha , authorising them to make such promises in his name", "context_len": 91}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "For anything that I really know to the contrary , a lot of fellows when they get together now may plot good deeds of all kinds , but when more than a single one of them was together then they plotted mischief", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "What they can be going to do , or what plot they are hatching , I can not discover , only I fear that it is some design for vengeance upon the Danes -- some dark treachery plotted against those in our midst ; and , if such is the case , I can but feel uneasy for poor Alfgar", "context_len": 60}}}, "scale": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 48, "context": "Alexander 's was a simple burial procession , going from the palace where he died to the proper cemetery -- a march of a thousand miles , it is true , but all within his own dominions The greatness of it resulted simply from the magnitude of the scale on which every thing pertaining to the mighty here was performed , for it was nothing but a simple passage from the dwelling to the burial-ground on his own estates , after all", "context_len": 82}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "When the excitement about the Mexican War was the highest , the company wanted a fort ; and they got a farmer to come and scale off the sod with his plough , in a grassy place there was near a piece of woods , where a good many cows were pastured", "context_len": 52}}}, "fortunes": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 47, "context": "The story , as I have said , was imagined from events in Irving 's history of the `` Conquest of Granada , '' a book which the boy loved hardly less than the monkish legends of `` Gesta Romanorum , '' and it concerned the rival fortunes of Hamet el Zegri and Boabdil el Chico , the uncle and nephew who vied with each other for the crumbling throne of the Moorish kingdom ; but I have not the least notion how it all ended", "context_len": 86}}}, "editor": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 4, "context": "Years ago the general editor of this series stated , in various publications , the problems that region offers , and on finding the remarkable legend of Chekilli , translated it and published it , as pointing to a solution of some of the questions involved", "context_len": 46}}}, "overwhelmingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The fortunes of a Whig editor in a place so overwhelmingly democratic as the Boy 's Town were not such as could have warranted his living in a palace ; and he must have been poor , as the world goes now", "context_len": 42}}}, "democratic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The fortunes of a Whig editor in a place so overwhelmingly democratic as the Boy 's Town were not such as could have warranted his living in a palace ; and he must have been poor , as the world goes now", "context_len": 42}}}, "warranted": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 46, "context": "I have tried both , and find there is more real pleasure to be had from the intellectual than from the social side of life , for while the latter is apt to fail us just when we most require it , the former is `` warranted to wear well and improve with keeping , '' and , so far from being affected by the changes and chances of this world , sticks by us when health and wealth and even friends can fall away", "context_len": 85}}}, "abundance": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 28, "context": "The water-carriers lifted their heavy burdens -- every drop of water had to be taken into the trenches , for , although there are springs and water-holes in abundance close to the firing-line , the risk of contamination had to be carefully guarded against -- and the `` homeward '' trek began", "context_len": 52}}}, "employing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 46, "context": "As soon as his pageship was at an end , and he found that his duties included supervision of Turkish slaves , he felt the want of a knowledge of the language , and from that time devoted an hour a day to its study , employing one of the servants of the auberge , who was a man of rank and education at home , to instruct him", "context_len": 69}}}, "philosophy": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 24, "context": "This was not altogether because he was needed there , I dare say , but because it was part of his father 's Swedenborgian philosophy that every one should fulfil a use ; I do not know that when the boy wanted to go swimming , or hunting , or skating , it consoled him much to reflect that the angels in the highest heaven delighted in uses ; nevertheless , it was good for him to be of use , though maybe not so much use", "context_len": 87}}}, "fulfil": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 29, "context": "This was not altogether because he was needed there , I dare say , but because it was part of his father 's Swedenborgian philosophy that every one should fulfil a use ; I do not know that when the boy wanted to go swimming , or hunting , or skating , it consoled him much to reflect that the angels in the highest heaven delighted in uses ; nevertheless , it was good for him to be of use , though maybe not so much use", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Item , to the said George Anderson the sum of Twenty and five Pounds yearly , provided that the said George Anderson doth fulfil to the letter the instructions set forth by me and intrusted to the keeping of the aforesaid Master Whitehead , Lawyer of Midhurst", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I will be guided by you in all things , and fulfil thus the dying wish of the only being who ever loved me", "context_len": 24}}}, "consoled": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 92, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}}}, "highest": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 31, "context": "'' 'Speech , which , in the year 1735 , was delivered at Savannah , in Georgia , by Chekilli , Emperor of the Upper and Lower Creeks ; Antiche , highest Chief of the town of the Cowetas , Eliche , King ; Ousta , Head Chief of the Cussitaws , Tomechaw , War King ; Wali , War Captain of the Palachucolas , Poepiche , King ; Tomehuichi , Dog King of the Euchitaws ; Mittakawye , Head War Chief of the Okonees , Tuwechiche , King ; Whoyauni , Head War Chief of the Chehaws and of the Hokmulge Nation ; Stimelacoweche , King of the Osoches ; Opithli , King of the Jawocolos ; Ewenauki , King ; Tahmokmi , War Captain of the", "context_len": 128}}}, "reverend": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "If his mother did her own work , with help only now and then from a hired girl , that was the custom of the time and country ; and her memory was always the more reverend to him , because whenever he looked back at her in those dim years , he saw her about some of those household offices which are so beautiful to a child", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Quite right , reverend father ! as you say , success was impossible while spies and traitors commanded our forces", "context_len": 21}}}, "tenderest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "She was always the best and tenderest mother , and her love had the heavenly art of making each child feel itself the most important , while she was partial to none", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "In his own home he was charming ; full of wit , full of fun , full of gay spirits and laughter ; full also of the tenderest affection for his wife and children , an affection which was abundantly returned", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Her mother died when she was a very little baby , so that she was left to grow up without that tenderest and wisest of all care", "context_len": 27}}}, "partial": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 60, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "conception": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 30, "context": "His brother had once been taken to a theatre in Pittsburgh by one of their river-going uncles , and he often told about it ; but my boy formed no conception of the beautiful reality from his accounts of a burglar who jumped from a roof and was chased by a watchman with a pistol up and down a street with houses painted on a curtain", "context_len": 66}}}, "rarest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` One rarest night of all , the family sat up till two o'clock , listening to a novel that my boy long ago forgot the name of , if he ever knew its name", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "But there was more than one heart at that table for which those feeble , sickly plants had a value far beyond that of the richest and rarest exotic", "context_len": 29}}}, "novel": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 81, "context": "My boy 's brother went to all sorts of places that my boy was too shy to go to ; and he associated with much older boys , but there was one boy who , as I have said , was the dear friend of both of them , and that was the boy who came to learn the trade in their father 's printing-office , and who began an historical romance at the time my boy began his great Moorish novel", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 19, "context": "And when , at night , impressed with the sense of solemnity which night always imparts to strange and novel scenes , they looked up to the bright round moon , pleased with the expression of cheerfulness and companionship which beams always in her light , to find her suddenly waning , changing her form , withdrawing her bright beams , and looking down upon them with a lurid and murky light , it was not surprising that they felt an emotion of terror", "context_len": 84}}}, "forged": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 26, "context": "This envelope , he said , contains two important documents the written confession of Bradley Wentworth , that it was he , not I , who forged the check upon his uncle , and the last letter in which he repudiates my claim upon him for the sum he agreed to pay me", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The murder \u2019 s out ! The forged check has fallen into my uncle \u2019 s hands , and he was in a great rage , you may be sure", "context_len": 30}}}, "bewitched": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 1, "context": "It bewitched them , and though my boy could not afterwards recall a single fact or figure in it , he could bring before his mind 's eye every trait of its outward aspect", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "trait": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "As a scholar he shone ; as a business man he was a failure , owing to the fatal and all too common trait amongst Maoris of the educated class of pleasure in the spending of money , and , oddly enough , to an inherent tendency to relapse , if only temporarily , to an aboriginal existence", "context_len": 58}}}, "volunteer": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 1, "context": "One volunteer brought home a Mexican dog , that was smooth and hairless , with a skin like an elephant , and that was always shivering round with the cold ; he was not otherwise a remarkable dog , and I do not know that he ever felt even the warmth of friendship among the boys ; his manners were reserved and his temper seemed doubtful", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Once at camp , Harry Herndon drummed up as many of the Independents as would volunteer , and they had gone in search of me ; Whistling Jim heard them riding along the road as he was coming to the tavern", "context_len": 41}}}, "broadside": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 57, "context": "It was a splendid gun , but it would not stand cocked long , and he was resting it on the wall of the fort , ready to fire when the storming-party came on , throwing sods and yelling and holloing ; and all at once his gun went off , and a cow that was grazing broadside to the fort gave a frightened bellow , and put up her tail , and started for home", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "After another broadside the pirate backed her main topsail and hove to at less than a cable \u2019 s length off", "context_len": 21}}}, "authorship": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 13, "context": "Among the many volumes published during the first fifteen or twenty years of authorship were the following : -- The Giant-Killer , The Roby Family , The Young Pilgrim , History of a Needle , and Rambles of a Rat , before 1858 ; Flora , The Mine , Precepts in Practice , Idols in the Heart , and Whispering Unseen , before 1860 ; Pride and his Prisoners , The Shepherd of Bethlehem , My Neighbour \u2019 s Shoes , War and Peace , Light in the Robber \u2019 s Cave , and The Silver Casket , before 1864", "context_len": 100}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "But here he met with that sort of disappointment which more than any other dismays and baffles authorship ; a difference in the point of view", "context_len": 26}}}, "heredity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "As the father had once taught English grammar in six lessons , from a broadside of his own authorship , he may have expected the principle of heredity to help the boy ; and certainly he did dig the English grammar out of that blessed book , and the Spanish language with it , but after many long years , and much despair over the difference between a preposition and a substantive", "context_len": 72}}}, "preposition": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 68, "context": "As the father had once taught English grammar in six lessons , from a broadside of his own authorship , he may have expected the principle of heredity to help the boy ; and certainly he did dig the English grammar out of that blessed book , and the Spanish language with it , but after many long years , and much despair over the difference between a preposition and a substantive", "context_len": 72}}}, "substantive": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 71, "context": "As the father had once taught English grammar in six lessons , from a broadside of his own authorship , he may have expected the principle of heredity to help the boy ; and certainly he did dig the English grammar out of that blessed book , and the Spanish language with it , but after many long years , and much despair over the difference between a preposition and a substantive", "context_len": 72}}}, "glimpses": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 34, "context": "He had a start of several hundred feet , darkness was coming on , and he evidently knew the paths through the woods better than did the boys , for when they caught occasional glimpses of him he appeared to be running at full speed , whereas they had to go slowly , and pick their way", "context_len": 57}}}, "social": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 52, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}}}, "problem": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 30, "context": "Of the sun-worship , which underlies the religions of all the tribes in the Gulf territories , only slight intimations are contained in the Kasi'hta legend , and the important problem , whether the Creeks ever crossed the Mississippi river from west to east in their migrations , seems to be settled by it in the negative , although other legends may be adduced as speaking in its favor", "context_len": 69}}}, "denied": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 25, "context": "Why should he be in the jungle , he a white boy , whose rightful heritage of a life with his own kind had been denied him by a cruel fate ? Why did he not have a home like that of Frank Parkhurst , a father who was proud of him , a mother who loved him ? Why had he been fated to have his life placed every day in jeopardy ? He had been cheated of what belonged to him equally with every other boy of the white race", "context_len": 92}}}, "margin": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 53, "context": "There was nothing of an alarming character in sight , and therefore , as soon as I had descended to the ground , we both set out for the spot where we had hidden our canoe , launched her , and made our way out through the labyrinth of mangrove roots to the margin of the river , where we lay perdu until the darkness had completely fallen , when we boldly pushed off into the strength of the current , and steadily pursued our way", "context_len": 86}}}, "festival": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 94, "context": "upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant , of course , that I was to die by some exquisite refinement of torture , the nature of which would probably be too dreadful for description", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Most of the people had made haste to get well for Easter , for to the good folk of Gorswen that festival meant but one event -- the great fair of the borders , which had been held in the village every Easter Tuesday within the memory of even that wonderful person , the oldest inhabitant", "context_len": 56}}}, "provision": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 47, "context": "He preferred a sunny spot to have his chill in , a cosy fence-corner or a warm back door-step , or the like ; but as for the fever that followed the chill , he took no account of it whatever , or at least made no provision for it", "context_len": 50}}}, "alley": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 22, "context": "Dick hurried her into the coach with all speed , and signed to his servant to lead his horse down a retired alley , but the aspect of the gathering crowd was so threatening , and that of his attendant saints so grim , that he began to suspect that his only escape from being stoned by the unbelieving mob , or run through by a Fifth Monarchy corporal , would be to be laid by the heels in a city jail !", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I strolled down the alley day before yesterday", "context_len": 8}}}, "indignity": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "And do you mean to say , Sir Gervaise , that you , a knight of the Order , are willing to submit to the indignity of being treated as a slave ? To keep up the disguise long enough to be taken into the confidence of the plotters , you might have to stay there for some time ; and if the prison officials believe you to be but an ordinary slave , you will be put to work either on the walls or in one of the galleys", "context_len": 90}}}, "patronage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 85, "context": "Peggy and Bobby liked the fun of the preparations , though they crept out of the way of the visitors as much as possible , for to be shown up in the drawing-room involved an amount of dressing and tidying which did not fit in at all with their ideas of holiday enjoyment , and they much preferred a picnic tea in the orchard , with Rollo and the rabbits for company , to the more select charms of the best china and the stately patronage of the neighbouring dowagers", "context_len": 90}}}, "attending": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 36, "context": "Yes , he was outwardly a model character : he worked early and late ; he saved money ; he was never in debt ; he defrauded no man ; his evenings were spent either in attending lectures of working men like himself or studying the subjects he loved at home ; he never drank ; he never swore ; he was looked up to , and brought forward as an example to follow for many a poor drunken wretch", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Only two of their number had been killed outright , but there were four serious cases among the wounded , while eight or ten others had received wounds that required bandaging and attending to", "context_len": 34}}}, "restive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "No doubt she had enough without it , but there were circumstances of indignity or patronage attending the gift which were recognized in my boy 's home , and which helped afterwards to make him doubtful of all giving , except the humblest , and restive with a world in which there need be any giving at all", "context_len": 58}}}, "watercourses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "IT seems to me that the best way to get at the heart of any boy 's town is to take its different watercourses and follow them into it", "context_len": 29}}}, "foremost": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Those who are most proficient in turning phrases are not often among those foremost in battle , or wisest in council , and I can tell you that we women value deeds far higher than words", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 2, "context": "First and foremost was the king of Southern England , the valiant Ironside , and his attendant and friend Alfgar ; Elfwyn and Father Cuthbert from Aescendune , with the Lady Hilda and Ethelgiva ; Herstan , his wife Bertha , and son Hermann , from Clifton , with his sisters ; and Ethelm , the new bishop of Dorchester , the successor of the martyred Ednoth", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "That seemed to be Mark Penelly 's fate ; but no -- as he neared the gate in the Shangles he could be seen to turn over upon his back , keeping his head well out of the water , paddling with his hands , and feet foremost , showing from time to time amongst the foam , literally shooting like a canoe right between the rocks , to float directly after in smooth water , and calmly swim round towards the shore", "context_len": 83}}}, "tunnel": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 48, "context": "The day Barty finished reading his book he tucked it under his arm and ran into the wood to his secret place and played his tune , and in less than two minutes he turned round and saw the Good Wolf trotting towards him out of the green tunnel", "context_len": 49}}}, "planet": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "It is a long wooden tunnel , with two roadways , and a foot-path on either side of these ; there is a toll-house at each end , and from one to the other it is about as far as from the Earth to the planet Mars", "context_len": 47}}}, "western": {"freq": 100, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 99, "word_index": 85, "context": "A part of the Cheroki anciently dwelt in caves ; and concerning the caverns from which the Creeks claim to have issued , James Adair gives the following interesting disclosure : `` It is worthy of notice , that the Muskohgeh cave , out of which one of their politicians persuaded them their ancestors formerly ascended to their present terrestrial abode , lies in the Nanne Hamgeh old town , inhabited by the Mississippi-Nachee Indians , [ 150 ] which is one of the most western parts of their old-inhabited country", "context_len": 91}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "When d'Iberville ascended the Yazoo river in the same year , he found a village of this tribe on its right ( or western ) bank , four days ' travel from the Natchez landing", "context_len": 35}}}, "perspective": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "To be sure , the perspective of her cottages was apt to be rather peculiar at times , and the Welsh mountains turned out a more vivid shade of purple , and the fields a far more brilliant green than Nature ever painted them ; but it was all good practice , and the admiring Peggy thought that no Royal Academician could have produced such charming masterpieces", "context_len": 67}}}, "timbers": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 1, "context": "The timbers are of a hugeness to strike fear into the heart of the boldest little boy ; and there is something awful even about the dust in the roadways ; soft and thrillingly cool to the boy 's bare feet , it lies thick in a perpetual twilight , streaked at intervals by the sun that slants in at the high , narrow windows under the roof ; it has a certain potent , musty smell", "context_len": 77}}}, "hugeness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The timbers are of a hugeness to strike fear into the heart of the boldest little boy ; and there is something awful even about the dust in the roadways ; soft and thrillingly cool to the boy 's bare feet , it lies thick in a perpetual twilight , streaked at intervals by the sun that slants in at the high , narrow windows under the roof ; it has a certain potent , musty smell", "context_len": 77}}}, "boldest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "The timbers are of a hugeness to strike fear into the heart of the boldest little boy ; and there is something awful even about the dust in the roadways ; soft and thrillingly cool to the boy 's bare feet , it lies thick in a perpetual twilight , streaked at intervals by the sun that slants in at the high , narrow windows under the roof ; it has a certain potent , musty smell", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "As I rounded the rock and came in sight of him he rose to his feet , with his two front paws on Ama 's body , and bared his great fangs at me in a hideous grin , as he gave utterance to a snarling growl that might well have struck terror to the boldest", "context_len": 56}}}, "thrillingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "The timbers are of a hugeness to strike fear into the heart of the boldest little boy ; and there is something awful even about the dust in the roadways ; soft and thrillingly cool to the boy 's bare feet , it lies thick in a perpetual twilight , streaked at intervals by the sun that slants in at the high , narrow windows under the roof ; it has a certain potent , musty smell", "context_len": 77}}}, "perpetual": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 32, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}}}, "slants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "The timbers are of a hugeness to strike fear into the heart of the boldest little boy ; and there is something awful even about the dust in the roadways ; soft and thrillingly cool to the boy 's bare feet , it lies thick in a perpetual twilight , streaked at intervals by the sun that slants in at the high , narrow windows under the roof ; it has a certain potent , musty smell", "context_len": 77}}}, "potent": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 73, "context": "The timbers are of a hugeness to strike fear into the heart of the boldest little boy ; and there is something awful even about the dust in the roadways ; soft and thrillingly cool to the boy 's bare feet , it lies thick in a perpetual twilight , streaked at intervals by the sun that slants in at the high , narrow windows under the roof ; it has a certain potent , musty smell", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "289 ) , that `` at Sandy Point , the southern extremity of the peninsula , are large fields , being the lands formerly planted by the Colusa savages ; '' and that `` they were driven away from the continent by the Creeks , their more potent neighbors", "context_len": 49}}}, "musty": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 75, "context": "The timbers are of a hugeness to strike fear into the heart of the boldest little boy ; and there is something awful even about the dust in the roadways ; soft and thrillingly cool to the boy 's bare feet , it lies thick in a perpetual twilight , streaked at intervals by the sun that slants in at the high , narrow windows under the roof ; it has a certain potent , musty smell", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "So the old chest remained in the loft , as hidden there as when it had been buried in the earth , and Peggy came and went , never dreaming in the time that followed that these ancient , musty relics could in any way be bound up with the fate and fortunes of the Vaughans", "context_len": 56}}}, "hardier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The bridge has three piers , and at low water hardier adventurers than he wade out to the middle pier ; some heroes even fish there , standing all day on the loose rocks about the base of the pier", "context_len": 40}}}, "adventurers": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 24, "context": "He regarded Alexander as a mere boy -- energetic and daring it is true , and at the head of a desperate band of adventurers ; but he considered his whole force as altogether too insignificant to make any stand against such a vast military power as he was bringing against him", "context_len": 52}}}, "pier": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 57, "context": "But as it happened , the warmth of the wrapping , the influence of the spirit that had been poured liberally down his throat , and the chafing , combined with his naturally strong animal power to revive him from the state of insensibility into which he had fallen , and long before they reached the granite pier of the little harbour his eyes had opened , and he was staring in a peculiarly puzzled way at Mark Penelly , who still knelt beside him in the double character of medical man and nurse", "context_len": 94}}}, "shudders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He shudders to see them , and aches with wonder how they will get ashore", "context_len": 15}}}, "aches": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 80, "context": "`` [ 662 ] Therewith he disappeared , and as for Alaeddin , when he heard this , his wit fled from his head and his joints trembled at the cry wherewith the Marid cried out at him ; but he took heart and leaving his closet , went in straightway to his wife and feigned to her that his head irked him , of his knowledge that Fatimeh was renowned for the secret of healing [ 663 ] all aches and pains", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "' And one by one they go away weeping , the poor caballeros ! She is cruel , my Ana , being so beautiful ! Me , I own it -- though my heart aches to see the caballeros shedding tears ! ''", "context_len": 43}}}, "wades": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Once he is there when a big boy wades back from the middle pier , where he has been to rob a goose 's nest ; he has some loose silver change in his wet hand , and my boy understands that it has come out of one of the goose eggs", "context_len": 52}}}, "rob": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 17, "context": "Now Dolly had heard nothing of the cup , but she knew that Lem had meant to rob John Porter 's tree of its tempting fruit , and she was on her way to meet him at the `` Chief 's Head , '' according to his bidding , and have a share of the ill-gotten prize", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Rob", "context_len": 1}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Only that I wished he were living now to tame the cutthroats who live in our midst , and who murder and rob daily , just in mere sport , or to keep their hands in", "context_len": 37}}}, "goose": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 62, "context": "We knew that although you were dainty in your ways , and belonged to a higher social class than we did ( we are never going , either of us , to deny that ) , we knew that you were ignorant of these things , and had not our wisdom , and we thought Jane Mullins was a bit of a goose to have launched in such a hopeless undertaking", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Not that any very remarkable things were said , but one can laugh very easily , you know , and at almost nothing , when one 's heart is light as a feather and the goose hangs high , as the queer old saying has it", "context_len": 46}}}, "riches": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 64, "context": "and agate , he was amazed [ 505 ] and his wit was bewildered at that affluence and wealth and magnificence ; so he turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? Hast thou in all thy days seen aught like this ? Are there found with the greatest of the kings of the world riches and gold and jewels such as these we see in this palace ? '' `` O my lord the King , '' answered the Vizier , `` this is a thing beyond the competence of a king of the sons of Adam , nor might all the people of the earth together avail to build a palace like this ; nay , there", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "I have tried to give some notion of the general distribution of comfort which was never riches in the Boy 's Town ; but I am afraid that I could not paint the simplicity of things there truly without being misunderstood in these days of great splendor and great squalor", "context_len": 50}}}, "hapless": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "In this one he was a hapless , harmless creature , whom the boys knew as Solomon Whistler , perhaps because his name was Whistler , perhaps because he whistled ; though when my boy met him midway of the bridge , he marched swiftly and silently by , with his head high and looking neither to the right nor to the left , with an insensibility to the boy 's presence that froze his blood and shrivelled him up with terror", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "It was his brother , I suppose , who taught him to abhor it ; and perhaps it was his own suffering from it in part ; for he , too , sometimes shed bitter tears over such a knot , as I have seen hapless little wretches do , tearing at it with their nails and gnawing at it with their teeth , knowing that the time was passing when they could hope to hide the fact that they had been in swimming , and foreseeing no remedy but to cut off the sleeve above the knot , or else put on their clothes without the shirt , and trust to untying the knot when it got dry", "context_len": 119}}}, "midway": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 56, "context": "I 've seen it too , continued Donald , wondering whether an artist-proof and a waterproof had anything in common ; but the girls are n't often so handsome as that ; but I 'll tell you when they do look pretty as a picture : that 's on a clear Sunday morning , just about midway in the service , when the sun comes streaming through one of the choir windows in a great white shaft of light , I think they call it", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "He had yet to learn that it was a small town in Rhenish Prussia roughly midway between Aix-la-Chapelle and Cologne", "context_len": 20}}}, "insensibility": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "But as it happened , the warmth of the wrapping , the influence of the spirit that had been poured liberally down his throat , and the chafing , combined with his naturally strong animal power to revive him from the state of insensibility into which he had fallen , and long before they reached the granite pier of the little harbour his eyes had opened , and he was staring in a peculiarly puzzled way at Mark Penelly , who still knelt beside him in the double character of medical man and nurse", "context_len": 94}}}, "shrivelled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 77, "context": "In this one he was a hapless , harmless creature , whom the boys knew as Solomon Whistler , perhaps because his name was Whistler , perhaps because he whistled ; though when my boy met him midway of the bridge , he marched swiftly and silently by , with his head high and looking neither to the right nor to the left , with an insensibility to the boy 's presence that froze his blood and shrivelled him up with terror", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "It is only the sunshine of God 's presence that can mellow us thoroughly , and if people wilfully turn away from that they are bound to become shrivelled and sour", "context_len": 31}}}, "endowed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "To be a Christ Church Senior , with plenty of money and plenty of friends and a head that easily mastered enough learning to make a good showing , left little to be desired , especially when already endowed with a handsome face and a physique that every man envied -- at least , so thought Theodore Harris , and so thought and affirmed the half score of intimate friends who enjoyed many of the good things of this life through his bounty", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "[ Footnote 105 : The person specially appointed to lead the prayers of the congregation and paid out of the endowed revenues of the mosque to which he is attached", "context_len": 30}}}, "drafted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "On reaching the beach they were disarmed , divided equally between the galleys , and distributed among the rowers , filling up the places of those who had been killed by the fire of the Moslems , and of the men drafted into the prizes", "context_len": 45}}}, "ranged": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 80, "context": "Beyond the hedge lies a pasture meadow , where a flock of sheep are grazing , and encircling the meadow another hedge , less closely clipped , and so making bold to riot here and there in a snowy wealth of hawthorn blossom , A fine Alderney cow , with coat as well cared for as the gray mare 's in the stable , is also enjoying the sweet grass of the meadow , and the shining milk , pans ranged beneath the kitchen window bear witness to the generous service she renders", "context_len": 93}}}, "illimitable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "He only knew that Solomon Whistler lived at the poor-house beyond the eastern border of the town , and that he ranged between this sojourn and the illimitable wilderness north of the town on the western shore of the river", "context_len": 40}}}, "blend": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The crazy man was often in the boy 's dreams , the memories of which blend so with the memories of real occurrences : he could not tell later whether he once crossed the bridge when the footway had been partly taken up , and he had to walk on the girders , or whether he only dreamed of that awful passage", "context_len": 62}}}, "footway": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "The crazy man was often in the boy 's dreams , the memories of which blend so with the memories of real occurrences : he could not tell later whether he once crossed the bridge when the footway had been partly taken up , and he had to walk on the girders , or whether he only dreamed of that awful passage", "context_len": 62}}}, "girders": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "The crazy man was often in the boy 's dreams , the memories of which blend so with the memories of real occurrences : he could not tell later whether he once crossed the bridge when the footway had been partly taken up , and he had to walk on the girders , or whether he only dreamed of that awful passage", "context_len": 62}}}, "fearful": {"freq": 83, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 67, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "With a stealthy tread he vanished down the road , hugging the buildings as if fearful of meeting a benighted stranger in the now deserted village", "context_len": 26}}}, "cracks": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 2, "context": "The whip cracks every minute across some one 's back ; you are blinded by exhaustion and rage , and you feel that you would give the world if you could but burst your chain , rush on your taskmasters , and strike , if only one blow , before you are killed", "context_len": 53}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The ring-master and the clown manage to get the country-jake on to the broad platform on the horse 's back , and then the ring-master cracks his whip , and the two supes who have been holding the horse 's head let go , and the horse begins cantering round the ring", "context_len": 52}}}, "boards": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 44, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}}}, "playmates": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "It made his brain reel ; and he felt that he took his life in his hand whenever he entered the bridge , even when he had grown old enough to be making an excursion with some of his playmates to the farm of an uncle of theirs who lived two miles up the river", "context_len": 55}}}, "watermelons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The farmer gave them all the watermelons they wanted to eat , and on the way home , when they lay resting under the sycamores on the river-bank , Solomon Whistler passed by in the middle of the road , silent , swift , straight onward", "context_len": 46}}}, "sycamores": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "The boys exulted in the turbid spread of the stream , which filled its low western banks and stole over their tops , and washed into all the hollow places along its shores , and shone among the trunks of the sycamores on Delorac 's Island , which was almost of the geographical importance of The Island in Old River", "context_len": 60}}}, "afflicted": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "This we had waited for , and we held council , and decided that , having no home , the brethren should depart with the sacred vessels and treasure to the mother house at Abingdon , while I remained , as also Father Adhelm , to minister to our afflicted flock in the woods as best we might", "context_len": 58}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "Important benefits were thus conferred on the whole neighbourhood ; agriculture flourished , learning increased , a sanctuary for the oppressed was provided , and last , though not least in Ina 's eyes , a bulwark against Mercia was provided for the neighbourhood ; while the poor and the afflicted found their happiness in every way promoted by the neighbourhood of the monastery", "context_len": 64}}}, "mocked": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 103, "context": "`` For the sake of him who hath even now rendered up himself as a martyr for the Lord 's cause , I may not be angry with any word of yours , '' he answered sadly ; `` but I do entreat of you to take heed ! Would you lay down your arms and live in peace among your cattle and your corn , coached and complimented into effeminacy and foolishness ? Oh , for shame ! Rub your eyes and look about you ! What was the fate of the men of Sodom when they thought Lot was one that mocked when he warned them ! ''", "context_len": 110}}}, "frequented": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "The boys knew little or nothing of the river south of the bridge , and frequented mainly that mile-long stretch of it between the bridge and the dam , beyond which there was practically nothing for many years ; afterwards they came to know that this strange region was inhabited", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Fenn and Frank were for going to the one where they had previously had such good luck hunting wild turkeys , but Bart and Ned wanted to go to a less frequented part of the State , where larger game , such as an occasional bear or deer , could be had , and , in the end they carried their point , though it meant a longer trip , and necessitated going by railroad", "context_len": 75}}}, "dam": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 54, "context": "A short three miles now separated us from the town , and on approaching it I saw a large mound of earth , called the Town Mount , crowned by fortifications and fronted by a line of bastions and earthworks , which in turn were encircled with a moat that communicated with the mill dam on the right", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "We \u2019 ll have a channel cut here '' ( indicating a semicircle of about forty yards in diameter ) , `` and dam the stream on each side of this hole", "context_len": 32}}}, "accumulating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Just above the bridge the Hydraulic emptied into the river with a heart-shaking plunge over an immense mill-wheel ; and there was a cluster of mills at this point , which were useful in accumulating the waters into fishing-holes before they rushed through the gates upon the wheel", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "So many sensational occurrences had marked the last twenty-four hours of Bart Stirling 's career , that it seemed as though the accumulating series would never end", "context_len": 27}}}, "secondary": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "[ Footnote 638 : Keniz , a word which I can not find in any dictionary , but which appears to be the past participle ( in the secondary form for mecnouz , as ketil , slain , for mertoul , ) of keneza , a lost verb of which only the fourth form acneza , he drank from a cup ( kinz ) , survives , and to mean `` something drunk from a cup", "context_len": 76}}}, "flour": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Afterwards , all the coarse parts of the husk are taken away by means of sifting : this coarse part is called bran ; and the fine white inside is flour , of which bread is made", "context_len": 37}}}, "circumstance": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 52, "context": "I do not say that every one was not before disposed to obey you in all things , willingly and cheerfully ; but he put it so strongly to them that they had volunteered specially for service in this galley , knowing well who was to be its commander , and the circumstance that the crew was to consist solely of young knights , and had therefore specially pledged their honour so to act that the enterprise should be in all respects a successful one", "context_len": 85}}}, "oriole": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "They were as simply a part of his natural circumstance as the large cottonwood-tree which hung over the river from a point near by , and which seemed to have always an oriole singing in it", "context_len": 36}}}, "clay": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 94, "context": ", love , should we fear the act of departing ? How many , many , pass Jordan , as it were , dry-shod ? Remember how peacefully sweet Fanny sank to rest , -- dearest Mother , -- how my Letitia \u2019 s face was lighted up with a smile , -- how our Bible-woman at Batala sang aloud a happy hymn within a few hours of her going ! To me it seems such a simple thing for the -- I had almost said imprisoned soul , to leave its cottage of clay , -- for the bird , as soon as fledged , to spread its wings ! We are winged creatures , and it seems a humiliation to be creeping on earth so long", "context_len": 128}}}, "springs": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 3, "context": "Though called boiling springs in some parts of the country they do not boil or bubble on the surface , as a rule , though there is a constant supply of warm water from some subterranean source , so , that , as in the case with the spring Fenn was viewing , the water ran over from the pool , and trickled off through the woods", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Very well , then , we will weigh as soon as the land-breeze springs up , '' said the skipper", "context_len": 21}}}, "dammed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "It may be also that , now he was gone for whom and with whom she had written her plays , all zest in that direction was gone with him , and the gift of writing , like a river dammed up in one direction and forced to turn elsewhere , sought naturally a fresh outlet , -- an outlet with which there should be no overpoweringly sad associations", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Burton , `` see how the dammed villain broke every promise he made , certifying that he would soon work all good with me", "context_len": 25}}}, "ponds": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 61, "context": "Luckily Christmas Day was fine ( for a wet Christmas is enough to damp the stoutest spirits ) , but after that the weather indulged in such a variety of changes , alternately freezing and thawing from day to day , that there was no time for a safe crust of ice to form even on the smallest and shallowest of ponds", "context_len": 62}}}, "glassy": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The blue clay that formed them was full of springs , which the boys dammed up in little ponds and let loose in glassy falls upon their flutter-mills", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was now near noon , and about low tide ; the sun shone bright , the water was glassy , and they could plainly see the bottom , which was a reef of rocks covered with long kelps ; the largest of which now came to the top of the water , spreading their great red leaves over its surface", "context_len": 61}}}, "pins": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 53, "context": "Their uncle has sent with the book two butterfly-nets ; a pretty case filled with crooked scissors , tweezers , pincers , and all sorts of sharp steel instruments ; a pretty box , at the bottom of which are little round pieces of cork , glued in rows , with long large-headed pins to run through the butterflies ; and another little box , with a lot of small squares of glass , which are to be put over their wings to keep them open , and prevent them from fluttering and beating about", "context_len": 95}}}, "supported": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 19, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "wheels": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 121, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}}}, "instances": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 22, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "spin": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 76, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 69, "context": "The Maugrabin fell to examining it and knew that this all of it was the work of the Lamp ; so he said , `` Alack ! Alack ! Needs must I dig a pit for this accursed one , this tailor 's son , who could not come by a night 's supper ; but , an destiny enable me , I will send his mother back to spin at her wheel , like as she did erst , and as for him , it shall cost him [ 556 ] his life", "context_len": 94}}}, "dauntless": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "The Crimean War was ended ; and two years later came the outbreak of the Indian Mutiny , with its awful carnage , its heaps of slain , its tortured women and children , its heroic determination , its dauntless courage", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The bailiff was , as all knew , a man of dauntless courage , of wide experience and great judgment , and that he should believe that Rhodes would , although not in their time , inevitably fall , brought home to them for the first time the fact that their fortress was but an outpost of Europe , and one placed so distant from it that Christendom , in the hour of peril , might be unable to furnish them with aid", "context_len": 83}}}, "bubble": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Who that has merely seen a photograph , or many photographs , of the Falls , can drink in the beauty of the living , bounding , changing , glorious miracle of Nature , which is beheld here ? Yet Niagara itself is but a bubble , compared with the glory which shall be revealed", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "What ! -- the falcon crest and morion , -- the scar on his dark brow -- will not all this throw the poor ignorant thick-lipped hero quite into the shade ? Yes , -- if a sparkling bubble is more glorious than a diamond shut up in a black case", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Though called boiling springs in some parts of the country they do not boil or bubble on the surface , as a rule , though there is a constant supply of warm water from some subterranean source , so , that , as in the case with the spring Fenn was viewing , the water ran over from the pool , and trickled off through the woods", "context_len": 67}}}, "universe": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 75, "context": "Young Dewey shook hands all around , and then sat down on the bed , looking at Mark with a puzzled expression on his face , as much as to say , `` what on earth have I struck -- b'gee ? '' Mark saw his expression and undertook to inform him , making haste to start before the Parson could begin again on the relative merits of Boston and the rest of the civilized universe", "context_len": 76}}}, "mirrored": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "They met such disappointments with dauntless cheerfulness , and lightly turned from some bursting bubble to some other where the glory of the universe was still mirrored", "context_len": 27}}}, "strewn": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Taking the galleys in tow , they rowed out of the inlet , whose banks were strewn with half charred timbers , oars , and relics of the fight", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "Re-entering the great hall , the chamberlain and prince ascended the broad staircase which conducted to the upper chambers , and passing along a passage thickly strewn with rushes to deaden the sound , for carpets were unknown , they came to a door at the end , where the chamberlain paused and knocked", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}}}, "porous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The river shore was strewn not only with waste cotton , but with drift which the water had made porous , and which they called smoke-wood", "context_len": 26}}}, "cigars": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "They made cigars for their own use out of it , and it seemed to them that it might be generally introduced as a cheap and simple substitute for tobacco ; but they never got any of it into the market , not even the market of that world where the currency was pins", "context_len": 54}}}, "substitute": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 27, "context": "They made cigars for their own use out of it , and it seemed to them that it might be generally introduced as a cheap and simple substitute for tobacco ; but they never got any of it into the market , not even the market of that world where the currency was pins", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "If you could substitute the name Almighty for Hari , and Lord Jesus for Guru , [ 83 ] it might almost seem the composition of hermits in the early centuries , except that celibacy is not enjoined", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "We see the same analogy reigning at the present day , when the sons of the wealthy , who are born to fortune , substitute pride , and arrogance , and vicious self-indulgence and waste for the modesty , and prudence , and virtue of their sires , by means of which the fortune was acquired", "context_len": 56}}}, "currency": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 89, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}}}, "climate": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 39, "context": "But if our Church is to go on growing , as we hope and pray that it may , what would be the advantage of having a tiny chapel , which would not comfortably accommodate ourselves in a fiery climate , and in which there would be no room at all for heathen spectators ? We should be wanting a second ; and how could we procure a second clergyman ? Please thank dear Fred very , very much for his kindness in collecting , and assure him that we wish to make the money go as far as possible", "context_len": 100}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "At eleven o'clock they sat down to a more substantial meal ; but in that climate the heat was at that hour considerable , and as there were duties to be performed , there was no sitting long at table", "context_len": 40}}}, "caves": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 104, "context": "But still she went on , but only slowly , because of the great press of people around her ; and suddenly an icy wind passed over them , and all fell to the earth shivering and terror-stricken , for they had never felt cold before , and they looked up to the mountain , and lo ! upon the very summit , at the door of the deserted wigwam , stood a terrible figure , clothed in white , and having a face as white as his robes , and his hair was like the long crystals that hang from the roofs of caves that the water goes through , and his eyes were like two great diamonds , white , yet blazing like the sun", "context_len": 127}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "turf": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 44, "context": "Joe said the way to tell it was , right off the mouth of the cove there was a great , high rock , that came up out of the water , with three spruces on the top of it , and a little turf , but the sides were all bare ; and he said there were reefs and breakers all round it ; but I \u2019 ll bet , if we could find it , we could see the reefs break , and keep clear of them", "context_len": 89}}}, "blackening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They had the joy of choking and blackening over these flues , and they intended to live on corn and potatoes borrowed from the household stores of the boy whose house was nearest", "context_len": 33}}}, "flues": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "My young master was intensely interested as his uncle talked to him about the width of stalls , the size of windows , the proper kind of concrete flooring which should have wooden stall racks , the ventilating flues and rolling doors", "context_len": 42}}}, "parch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They never got so far as to parch the corn or to bake the potatoes in their caves , but there was the fire , and the draft was magnificent", "context_len": 30}}}, "bake": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 32, "context": "We 'll just put two or three dishes of this batter together in the pan , add a little more flour , and some salt , and it 'll be ready to bake , '' and , as he talked Fenn soon beat up the batter to the right consistency , for he had a knack of cooking", "context_len": 58}}}, "draft": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 27, "context": "They never got so far as to parch the corn or to bake the potatoes in their caves , but there was the fire , and the draft was magnificent", "context_len": 30}}}, "grizzled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` But I always wanted to be the kind of a seaman my father was , '' confided David , grateful for the cheer of this grizzled shipmate", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "His hair was a deep grizzled red , as also his beard , and his eyes were of the same tinge , his nose somewhat aquiline , and his whole features , weatherworn as they were , were those of one born to command , while they lacked the sheer brutality of expression so conspicuous in some of his subordinates", "context_len": 60}}}, "huddled": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 33, "context": "The light of the red flames painted the little , happy , foolish faces , so long since wrinkled and grizzled with age , or mouldered away to dust , as the boys huddled before them under the bank , and fed them with the drift , or stood patient of the heat and cold in the afternoon light of some vast Saturday waning to nightfall", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "For quite five seconds he remained thus , then his knees gave way under him , slowly and reluctantly , it seemed , he fell in a huddled heap face downwards in the mud", "context_len": 34}}}, "waning": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 63, "context": "The light of the red flames painted the little , happy , foolish faces , so long since wrinkled and grizzled with age , or mouldered away to dust , as the boys huddled before them under the bank , and fed them with the drift , or stood patient of the heat and cold in the afternoon light of some vast Saturday waning to nightfall", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "And when , at night , impressed with the sense of solemnity which night always imparts to strange and novel scenes , they looked up to the bright round moon , pleased with the expression of cheerfulness and companionship which beams always in her light , to find her suddenly waning , changing her form , withdrawing her bright beams , and looking down upon them with a lurid and murky light , it was not surprising that they felt an emotion of terror", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He had the waning courage of a little creature whose heart has all gone out of him", "context_len": 17}}}, "nightfall": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "The light of the red flames painted the little , happy , foolish faces , so long since wrinkled and grizzled with age , or mouldered away to dust , as the boys huddled before them under the bank , and fed them with the drift , or stood patient of the heat and cold in the afternoon light of some vast Saturday waning to nightfall", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 25, "context": "Our wants for the next few hours were consequently supplied , we had therefore no need to do anything further than just to wait for nightfall and then resume our voyage ; which we did , passing the intervening time in chatting together and discussing the various precautions which we must take in order to elude our pursuers , who , by the way , were now several miles ahead of us", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "Indeed , the little queen , whose realm was the hearts of her nearest and dearest , would have been obliged to abdicate , for a while at least , I fancy , had she not chosen before nightfall of that same day to bury her head in the lap of her very most loyal subject , and with tears and sohs confess to her extreme unreasonableness and avow her determination not soon again to be overtaken by such a sorry state of mind and temper", "context_len": 86}}}, "eventful": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 61, "context": "Harris , knowing that she would , gave the little girl on his knee a good , hard hug , and bundled her off for a word with her mamma , comfortably tucked up in a steamer-chair on deck , and then hurried her down to the saloon for the breakfast that she stood in sore need of after such an eventful morning", "context_len": 63}}}, "cloudless": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It is a cloudless morning , the white sands of the South-shore beaches shine like silver in the sunlight , and the fresh sea breeze that is stirring holds its own the whole length of Long Island , and blows its purifying way into every street and alley of the vast city that lies at its farther end", "context_len": 58}}}, "rains": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 29, "context": "Thrace was one great fertile basin or valley , sloping toward the center in every direction , so that all the streams from the mountains , increased by the rains which fell over the whole surface of the ground , flowed together into one river , which meandered through the center of the valley , and flowed out at last into the \u00c6gean Sea", "context_len": 64}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "It rains , and we have nothing to do , and so we are writing you a letter", "context_len": 18}}}, "gain": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 79, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}, "VERB": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 3, "context": "And suppose I gain admission to the presence , what shall I take by way of offering to the Sultan 's highness ? It [ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm", "context_len": 128}}}, "cheered": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 72, "context": "Her gruesome voice as she recounted what the goblins did to naughty boys made several small sinners in the back benches shake in their shoes -- many innocent infants felt they should never dare to go to bed in the dark again ; and the wild shriek with which she ended her announcement that 'the goblins are about ' quite brought down the house , and the children shouted and yelled and cheered as the Rector strove to get order once more , and wind up the proceedings with the National Anthem", "context_len": 92}}}, "objects": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 48, "context": "At the moment I took but scant notice of the circumstance , for the flickering flames of the fire which was always kindled upon such occasions played strange pranks with the lights and shadows , and often imparted a weird effect of movement to stationery and even inanimate objects ; but presently , happening to again glance in that direction , my eye was once more caught by the same queer wavering movement", "context_len": 73}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "The first objects he saw , were the camp-fires , which were smouldering slowly away as if the gray dawn that was peeping over the hills was putting them to shame", "context_len": 31}}}, "prongs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "They cheered the objects as they floated by , and they were breathless with the excitement of seeing the men who caught fence-rails and cord-wood , and even saw-logs , with iron prongs at the points of long poles , as they stood on some jutting point of shore and stretched far out over the flood", "context_len": 56}}}, "poles": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 34, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "exulted": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 49, "context": "Many persons have accordingly supposed that the plan of assassinating Philip was devised in Greece ; that Demosthenes was a party to it ; that Pausanias was the agent for carrying it into execution ; and that Demosthenes was so confident of the success of the plot , and exulted so much in this certainty , that he could not resist the temptation of thus anticipating its announcement", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Miles and Barney are welcome to catch us -- if they can , exulted McGlory , who was riding in front with Matt", "context_len": 23}}}, "turbid": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "This story does not mention any crossing by the Cha'hta of the turbid waters of the mighty Mississippi , but accounts quite satisfactorily for the mysterious inclination of the pole , for the prophet must have been careful to suspend the satchel with the war-physic always on the eastern side , so as to have the pole brought down in that direction by the weight of the pouch", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The shores are low and monotonous , and the river itself in a large part of its course is turbid and narrow", "context_len": 22}}}, "shores": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 67, "context": "When I say that you are free to choose your own cruising ground , I do not mean that you should go up the Levant , or to the east of the Mediterranean , but that you are not bound to keep close along the African coast , but may , should you obtain any information to warrant your doing so , seek the pirates along the shores of Spain , Sardinia , Corsica , or Sicily", "context_len": 77}}}, "trunks": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 50, "context": "They had a travelling uncle who came home to them at last ; but he staid away ten years and did not come home even to see his mother , and I think he ort to do n't you ? But now he is come and has brought so many trunks and boxes with such lots and lots of things and kurositys in them that he is 'most like a Norz ' Ark only better , and his gret coat and cap are made of the bears ' skins he shot and he tells us about the tigers and lions and I do n't like it and Fred and Harry do and Bessie do n't too", "context_len": 116}}}, "geographical": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "The boys exulted in the turbid spread of the stream , which filled its low western banks and stole over their tops , and washed into all the hollow places along its shores , and shone among the trunks of the sycamores on Delorac 's Island , which was almost of the geographical importance of The Island in Old River", "context_len": 60}}}, "gulfs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "Once the river rose to within a few feet of it , so that if the right piece of drift had been there to do its duty , the bridge might have been torn from its piers and swept down the raging tide into those unknown gulfs to the southward", "context_len": 50}}}, "grief": {"freq": 88, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 85, "word_index": 13, "context": "At the end of that time he roused himself from his stupor of grief and disappointment , and , abandoning the idea of any further attack upon the point that had cost him so dearly , he ordered the troops to move round and renew the attack upon the wall in front of the Jews ' quarter , and commence the construction of a battery on the edge of the great ditch facing the retrenchment behind the breach before effected", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` As for that braggart , Mosely , he 'll come to grief some of these days", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Indeed , O my father , there possessed me grief and sore chagrin , not only for my severance from thee , but also for the loss of my husband , to whom I shall be beholden all the days of my life , seeing he delivered me from that accursed enchanter", "context_len": 52}}}, "citizens": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 29, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "tedious": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 50, "context": "Language is not only the most general and important help to ethnology , but outside of race , it is also the most ancient of all ; ethnologists are well aware of this fact , but do not generally apply it to their studies , because they find it too tedious to acquire the language of unlettered tribes by staying long enough among them", "context_len": 64}}}, "rushes": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 39, "context": "There were foaming cataracts here and there among the rocks , just like Niagara on a small scale , and there were dear little quiet pools at the edges , where the still water was overhung by ferns and rushes , that sheltered caddice-worms , and boat-flies , and whirligig water-beetles , and all sorts of other delights for the collection", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}}}, "splashing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "It seemed to him that he had to wait an hour , and just when he was going to hollo , and tell his brother where the ducks were , the old smoothbore sent out a red flash and a white puff before he heard the report ; Tip tore loose from his grasp ; and he heard the splashing rise of the ducks , and the hurtling rush of their wings ; and he ran forward , yelling , `` How many did you hit ? Where are they ? Where are you ? Are they coming back ? It 's my turn now ! '' and making an outcry that would have frightened away a fleet of ironclads , but much less a flock of ducks", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The splashing was a failure for he fell down", "context_len": 9}}}, "diving": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 76, "context": "Dotted about the swelling surface there would be the heads of plenty of swimmers -- men and lads -- some going smoothly along , mounting the rollers as they came in , and descending softly into the hollows ; others again swimming to meet each wave , then rising a little , and with a plunge like a duck or one of the great bronze-black shags , or cormorants , that sat upon the rock-shelves , diving right through the mass of water , to come out fairly on the other side", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Later Rosy \u2019 s crew found out that Mickey was the lone survivor of a B-26 that had been sliced in two by a diving Zero fighter", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He stooped , made a diving scurry under one of them , and was lost to view", "context_len": 17}}}, "ignominy": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "There were not many fish in that part of the Miami ; my boy 's experience was full of the ignominy of catching shiners and suckers , or , at the best , mudcats , as they called the yellow catfish ; but there were boys , of those who cursed and swore , who caught sunfish , as they called the bream ; and there were men who were reputed to catch at will , as it were , silvercats and river-bass", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "And grant that Allah make [ the thing ] possible and I take courage and say to him , ' I desire to ally myself to thee by [ marrying ] thy daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour with my son Alaeddin , ' they will straightway deem me mad and will put me out with ignominy and reproach ; nay , I need not tell thee that by this I shall fall into danger of death , and not I only , but thou also", "context_len": 84}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The prisoner strode up and down the limited area , wrestling with self , bending the will by prayer to submit to ignominy and pain , for he knew now that his father had abandoned him , and that he had to apprehend the worst ; still he did not regret the choice he had made , and he felt , as he prayed , peace and confidence descend like heavenly dew upon his soul", "context_len": 75}}}, "shiners": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "There were not many fish in that part of the Miami ; my boy 's experience was full of the ignominy of catching shiners and suckers , or , at the best , mudcats , as they called the yellow catfish ; but there were boys , of those who cursed and swore , who caught sunfish , as they called the bream ; and there were men who were reputed to catch at will , as it were , silvercats and river-bass", "context_len": 83}}}, "suckers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "There were not many fish in that part of the Miami ; my boy 's experience was full of the ignominy of catching shiners and suckers , or , at the best , mudcats , as they called the yellow catfish ; but there were boys , of those who cursed and swore , who caught sunfish , as they called the bream ; and there were men who were reputed to catch at will , as it were , silvercats and river-bass", "context_len": 83}}}, "mudcats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "There were not many fish in that part of the Miami ; my boy 's experience was full of the ignominy of catching shiners and suckers , or , at the best , mudcats , as they called the yellow catfish ; but there were boys , of those who cursed and swore , who caught sunfish , as they called the bream ; and there were men who were reputed to catch at will , as it were , silvercats and river-bass", "context_len": 83}}}, "catfish": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "There were not many fish in that part of the Miami ; my boy 's experience was full of the ignominy of catching shiners and suckers , or , at the best , mudcats , as they called the yellow catfish ; but there were boys , of those who cursed and swore , who caught sunfish , as they called the bream ; and there were men who were reputed to catch at will , as it were , silvercats and river-bass", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was several years before the catfish got in , and then they were mud-cats ; but the boys had great luck with sunfish there and in the pools about the flood-gates , where there was always some leakage , and where my boy once caught a whole string of live fish which had got away from some other boy , perhaps weeks before ; they were all swimming about , in a lively way , and the largest hungrily took his bait", "context_len": 83}}}, "cursed": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 50, "context": "There were not many fish in that part of the Miami ; my boy 's experience was full of the ignominy of catching shiners and suckers , or , at the best , mudcats , as they called the yellow catfish ; but there were boys , of those who cursed and swore , who caught sunfish , as they called the bream ; and there were men who were reputed to catch at will , as it were , silvercats and river-bass", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "What a cursed fortune , Gervaise muttered , in Turkish , just when a road to freedom is open ! I have a good mind to say I am ill , and can not go till the morning", "context_len": 38}}}, "swore": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "There were not many fish in that part of the Miami ; my boy 's experience was full of the ignominy of catching shiners and suckers , or , at the best , mudcats , as they called the yellow catfish ; but there were boys , of those who cursed and swore , who caught sunfish , as they called the bream ; and there were men who were reputed to catch at will , as it were , silvercats and river-bass", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 14, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "The washerwoman 's little girl had heard footsteps behind her on the drive and a distinct clanking as of chains , while old Betty Carson swore on her Bible oath that she had seen something white moving about among the ruins , which groaned as in the expression of the keenest mental anguish ; and when Mr", "context_len": 57}}}, "sunfish": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "There were not many fish in that part of the Miami ; my boy 's experience was full of the ignominy of catching shiners and suckers , or , at the best , mudcats , as they called the yellow catfish ; but there were boys , of those who cursed and swore , who caught sunfish , as they called the bream ; and there were men who were reputed to catch at will , as it were , silvercats and river-bass", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Sometimes you could catch sunfish in the Hydraulic , but it was generally too swift , and the only thing you could depend upon was catching crawfish", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was one big sunfish that was not afraid of anything ; if you held a worm just over him he would jump out of the water and snatch it", "context_len": 30}}}, "bream": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "There were not many fish in that part of the Miami ; my boy 's experience was full of the ignominy of catching shiners and suckers , or , at the best , mudcats , as they called the yellow catfish ; but there were boys , of those who cursed and swore , who caught sunfish , as they called the bream ; and there were men who were reputed to catch at will , as it were , silvercats and river-bass", "context_len": 83}}}, "reputed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 70, "context": "There were not many fish in that part of the Miami ; my boy 's experience was full of the ignominy of catching shiners and suckers , or , at the best , mudcats , as they called the yellow catfish ; but there were boys , of those who cursed and swore , who caught sunfish , as they called the bream ; and there were men who were reputed to catch at will , as it were , silvercats and river-bass", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 80, "context": "Baulked of their prey , the mob still surged round us , and with a shout of : `` Let \u2019 em have the cat ! '' a great black object was sent flying towards us , and , striking my horse on the crupper , resolved itself into an enormous black cat , that spat and howled , digging its claws into the horse \u2019 s hide , and arching its back like the demon cat that is the reputed companion of every witch", "context_len": 85}}}, "respected": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 73, "context": "Randolph to have come as he had done , to have forced himself upon us , to have invited us to go out with him , to have -- -- and then I stopped , and a great lump rose in my throat , and I burst into tears , for in my heart of hearts I knew well that I did not think what he did intolerable at all , that I respected him , and -- but I did not dare to allow my thoughts to go any further", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 97, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "whiskey": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 46, "context": "As soon as the gauger had left them , he ran up to one of the barrels where he had seen a few drops fall from his instrument when he lifted it from the bunghole , and plunged the tip of his little finger into the whiskey , and then put it to his tongue", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "There were at least a dozen places where a man could get whiskey , but only one where he could get ice-cream , and the boys were mostly too poor and too shy to visit this resort", "context_len": 37}}}, "honorable": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 42, "context": "Philip wished to make this wedding not merely the means of confirming his reconciliation with his former wife and son , and establishing friendly relations with the King of Epirus : he also prized it as an occasion for paying marked and honorable attention to the princes and great generals of the other states of Greece", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Harwood what a good woman she was , and how willing she was to do any thing honorable for the support of herself and her little boy", "context_len": 27}}}, "brinks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "The boys respected them for their ability to drink whiskey , and thought it a fit and honorable thing that they should now and then fall into the river over the brinks where they had set their poles", "context_len": 38}}}, "disappear": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 72, "context": "Horrid , affected creatures ! And then to see Carter 's frightened eyes looking at me ! Have n't I seen them in my dreams until I am sick of the sight of them ? And Rosamund wants me to go and see them again ! Why , Carter , poor thing ! would nearly die of fright , and every one of the Leaves would get into their native trees and disappear from view ! Oh , Rosamund is all very well , but she is n't worth that ! I wish I had n't given those leeches back to the chemist", "context_len": 103}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "With the utmost audacity a U-boat had lain submerged across the track of the convoy , trusting to be able to launch her bolt and disappear before even the swift destroyers could take her bearings , and close upon the spot where the tips of her periscopes had appeared when the torpedo had been discharged", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Sun ? We are glad to see you after a week 's absence , even though you do mean to make us disappear beneath your warm rays", "context_len": 27}}}, "delights": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 58, "context": "Sometimes I ask myself , -- Now , in my present position , if I had no pleasure in religion , if everything connected with that were cut off , what would be left me ? -- what would life be to me ? O Leila , what a tasteless , what a bitter thing ! We want delights that will not grow old , that will never pall , that will be just as fresh and lovely at eighty as at eighteen", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "The one thing which delights me most is the way in which his faith has stood the hard hard test to which it has been put", "context_len": 26}}}, "continual": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 19, "context": "We shall still have to work hard and live carefully , but now that there will not be the continual drain of the interest on the mortgages going out , I shall be able to put more into the land , and carry out the improvements I have been longing to make for years , and in time the estate will pick up , so that I hope when I go to take my place among the old Crusaders in the transept I shall hand it on to the next generation of Vaughans in better condition than I found it", "context_len": 100}}}, "practice": {"freq": 163, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 163, "word_index": 71, "context": "these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about to work upon him by practice , that he might hinder him from giving his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour to Alaeddin , and accordingly said to him , [ 436 ] `` O my lord , all the treasures of the world were not worth a paring of thy daughter Bedrulbudour 's nails ; indeed , Thy Highness overrateth this upon her", "context_len": 128}}}, "shallow": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 47, "context": "Fiery dragons could not have kept them out ; gallynippers , whatever they were , certainly did not ; they were believed to abound at the bottom of the deep holes ; but the boys never stayed long in the deep holes , and they preferred the shallow places , where the river broke into a long ripple ( they called it riffle ) on its gravelly bed , and where they could at once soak and bask in the musical rush of the sunlit waters", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "In a shallow , muddy pool , about thirty feet in front of him , he saw a mass of writhing snakes , gray in color like the mud in which they wallowed", "context_len": 33}}}, "infested": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "The Gosport coaled and left for the Pacific , Until it was definitely established that German raiders no longer infested the route between Wellington and the Horn the presence of a few light cruisers was necessary , otherwise armed merchant-cruisers could effectually perform convoying duties , and release the `` pukka '' warships for other duties in home waters", "context_len": 59}}}, "dragons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Fiery dragons could not have kept them out ; gallynippers , whatever they were , certainly did not ; they were believed to abound at the bottom of the deep holes ; but the boys never stayed long in the deep holes , and they preferred the shallow places , where the river broke into a long ripple ( they called it riffle ) on its gravelly bed , and where they could at once soak and bask in the musical rush of the sunlit waters", "context_len": 86}}}, "abound": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "Fiery dragons could not have kept them out ; gallynippers , whatever they were , certainly did not ; they were believed to abound at the bottom of the deep holes ; but the boys never stayed long in the deep holes , and they preferred the shallow places , where the river broke into a long ripple ( they called it riffle ) on its gravelly bed , and where they could at once soak and bask in the musical rush of the sunlit waters", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "riffle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 62, "context": "Fiery dragons could not have kept them out ; gallynippers , whatever they were , certainly did not ; they were believed to abound at the bottom of the deep holes ; but the boys never stayed long in the deep holes , and they preferred the shallow places , where the river broke into a long ripple ( they called it riffle ) on its gravelly bed , and where they could at once soak and bask in the musical rush of the sunlit waters", "context_len": 86}}}, "gravelly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "Fiery dragons could not have kept them out ; gallynippers , whatever they were , certainly did not ; they were believed to abound at the bottom of the deep holes ; but the boys never stayed long in the deep holes , and they preferred the shallow places , where the river broke into a long ripple ( they called it riffle ) on its gravelly bed , and where they could at once soak and bask in the musical rush of the sunlit waters", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The gravelly side of the mountain sloped away here for a few feet , then fell sheer down in a tremendous precipice to the valley beneath ; and a foot or so below the edge grew this beautiful , tantalizing bunch of leaves", "context_len": 43}}}, "soak": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 75, "context": "Fiery dragons could not have kept them out ; gallynippers , whatever they were , certainly did not ; they were believed to abound at the bottom of the deep holes ; but the boys never stayed long in the deep holes , and they preferred the shallow places , where the river broke into a long ripple ( they called it riffle ) on its gravelly bed , and where they could at once soak and bask in the musical rush of the sunlit waters", "context_len": 86}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "Everything was lovely and just as it should be , even to the pelting showers that came down with such swift suddenness as to almost soak them through before they could get under cover of waterproofs and umbrellas , and then a moment after left them stranded in brilliant sunshine , fairly steaming within the rubber coats which , with much difficulty , had but just been adjusted", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I \u2019 ll soak him if he ever puts his hands on me again , declared Rollins , who was desirous of maintaining his reputation , yet hesitated before that dangerous look on Stone \u2019 s face", "context_len": 37}}}, "bask": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 77, "context": "Fiery dragons could not have kept them out ; gallynippers , whatever they were , certainly did not ; they were believed to abound at the bottom of the deep holes ; but the boys never stayed long in the deep holes , and they preferred the shallow places , where the river broke into a long ripple ( they called it riffle ) on its gravelly bed , and where they could at once soak and bask in the musical rush of the sunlit waters", "context_len": 86}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "Indeed , who knows of a more inspiriting sight anywhere than Eton College on an April morning ? The quaint old buildings seem to bask in the broad spring sunshine ; the trees that dot the grass-bare turf where the Upper School fronts the street are already casting tiny leaf-shadows , and on the other side , where the garden slopes down to the Thames , many a little branch and bush begins to glow with color", "context_len": 77}}}, "sunlit": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 84, "context": "Fiery dragons could not have kept them out ; gallynippers , whatever they were , certainly did not ; they were believed to abound at the bottom of the deep holes ; but the boys never stayed long in the deep holes , and they preferred the shallow places , where the river broke into a long ripple ( they called it riffle ) on its gravelly bed , and where they could at once soak and bask in the musical rush of the sunlit waters", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 56, "context": "It was in imagination , but his mental calculation was not far wrong , for within a few yards of where it might be expected , and not ten from where he was now sculling , he saw something roll up as it were to the surface , there was a gleam of white in the sunlit water , and then it was disappearing again , when , acting upon the impulse of the moment , Harry loosened his hold of the oar , took two steps forward over the thwarts , and leaped into the sea", "context_len": 97}}}, "floods": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "I have heard people in New England blame all the Western rivers for being yellow and turbid ; but I know that after the spring floods , when the Miami had settled down to its summer business with the boys , it was as clear and as blue as if it were spilled out of the summer sky", "context_len": 58}}}, "landlocked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The boys liked the riffle because they could stay in so long there , and there were little landlocked pools and shallows , where the water was even warmer , and they could stay in longer", "context_len": 36}}}, "clattering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "At most places under the banks there was clay of different colors , which they used for war-paint in their Indian fights ; and after they had their Indian fights they could rush screaming and clattering into the riffle", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "With another furious outburst the knight rushed out of the room , mounted his horse , and , followed by his two servants and the messenger of ill-tidings , rode furiously down the road to Midhurst , the noise of the horses \u2019 hoofs clattering on the frosty road testifying to the speed at which they were urged", "context_len": 58}}}, "sunburn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "When the stream had washed them clean down to their red sunburn or their leathern tan , they could paint up again and have more Indian fights", "context_len": 27}}}, "leathern": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "He shivered and felt wretched , with the soggy leathern pouches flapping his neck , like a grotesque tippet ; but ere long his incisors stopped their music , and the chills shook no more", "context_len": 35}}}, "associations": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 68, "context": "It may be also that , now he was gone for whom and with whom she had written her plays , all zest in that direction was gone with him , and the gift of writing , like a river dammed up in one direction and forced to turn elsewhere , sought naturally a fresh outlet , -- an outlet with which there should be no overpoweringly sad associations", "context_len": 69}}}, "outlawry": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "I do not know why my boy 's associations with Delorac 's Island were especially wild in their character , for nothing more like outlawry than the game of mumble-the-peg ever occurred there", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "He gives out he has sufficient evidence that you are corresponding with Johnson of Warriston and the other Scotch gentlemen under sentence of outlawry , and that he will see you at the gallows before he leaves you", "context_len": 38}}}, "bounds": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 105, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "If you were to get on a ship and sail away as far as you can see from here , you would still see just as much water before you , and the sea and the sky would still appear to touch each other : and however far you went it would always be so , until you came where the land bounds the ocean on the other side", "context_len": 69}}}, "civilization": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 57, "context": "He conformed his conduct to the principles and maxims which actuated the behavior of the shadowy people of these dry-as-dust tales ; he went about drunk with the fumes of fables about Roman emperors that never were , in an empire that never was ; and , though they tormented him by putting a mixed and impossible civilization in the place of that he knew from his Goldsmith , he was quite helpless to break from their influence", "context_len": 78}}}, "gauntlet": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "They might have reached it by the bridge , but the temper of the boys on the western shore was uncertain ; they would have had to run the gauntlet of their river-guard on the way up to it ; and they might have been friendly or they might not ; it would have depended a good deal on the size and number of the interlopers", "context_len": 66}}}, "depended": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 83, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}}}, "interlopers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "They might have reached it by the bridge , but the temper of the boys on the western shore was uncertain ; they would have had to run the gauntlet of their river-guard on the way up to it ; and they might have been friendly or they might not ; it would have depended a good deal on the size and number of the interlopers", "context_len": 66}}}, "blackbirds": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "But the blackbirds and the ring-ousels had been before them , so it took a long time to fill the can , especially as a good deal of the fruit found its way into the children 's mouths , leaving them with such purple lips and stained fingers that they resembled the babes in the wood", "context_len": 56}}}, "nested": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 73, "context": "She was above the clock , which was so far up in the air , against the summer sky which was always blue , that it made your neck ache to look up at it ; and the bell was so large that once when my boy was a very little fellow , and was in the belfry with his brother , to see if they could get some of the pigeons that nested there , and the clock began to strike , it almost smote him dead with the terror of its sound , and he felt his heart quiver with the vibration of the air between the strokes", "context_len": 110}}}, "clamor": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 41, "context": "When David returned to the deck an officer was already making reports of the temperature of the water , and Captain Thrasher was standing with head cocked and a hand at his ear , listening , on the chance that the clamor of the fog-whistle might fling back a telltale echo from some hidden mountain of ice that lay in ambush", "context_len": 61}}}, "roost": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "The short November day was already so near its end that the fowls were thinking of going to roost , though the hour was not late , and after watching them take their supper , which Edna helped Reliance to distribute , the two girls went on to the garden , now robbed of most of its vegetables", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "These branch towns were Intatchk\u00e1lgi , `` beaver-dam people '' ; [ 8 ] Padshil\u00e4\u00edka , `` pigeon roost '' ; and Tokog\u00e1lgi , `` tad-pole people '' , on Flint river and its side creeks ; while a few Yuchi had gone to the Upper Creeks and settled there at Sawan\u00f3gi", "context_len": 52}}}, "deafened": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "The shouting was so violent that the congregation effectually deafened themselves to the knocking that began to make itself heard at the door of the room , and it was not till the knocking changed to the clang of crowbars , and the door gave way before the assailants , that the excited fanatics realized that their enemies were upon them", "context_len": 61}}}, "rejoining": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Wounded Anzacs , on receiving the ten-days ' leave in England before rejoining their units , frequently make railway journeys running into hundreds of miles simply for the Purpose of `` looking up '' a remote blood relation in the Old Country , In Selwyn 's case his relation lived at a small town on the shores of False Bay , a distance of about twenty miles from Cape Town", "context_len": 70}}}, "resented": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 22, "context": "This reproof had greatly subdued Robert Rivers ; and as he felt that any display of his jealousy of Gervaise would be resented by the other knights , and might result in serious consequences to himself , he abstained from any exhibition of it when they returned to the auberge , although he could not bring himself to join in the congratulations offered to them", "context_len": 65}}}, "parleys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "For no reason under the sun they were afraid of him ; perhaps they thought he resented their parleys with the parrot", "context_len": 22}}}, "existed": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 6, "context": "There had for a long time existed a tradition among them , that when a daughter of a chief -- an only child , -- should love a brave of an unfriendly tribe , they would have power to change her into a flower or animal , and unless the brave should find the means within ten moons , or months , to break the enchantment , she would die , and with her every chieftain and his family", "context_len": 79}}}, "solely": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 15, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "industries": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "But he and the parrot existed solely to amuse and to frighten them ; and on their own side of the river , just opposite the island , there were established some small industries for their entertainment and advantage , on a branch of the Hydraulic", "context_len": 46}}}, "elastic": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 30, "context": "She inflicted upon him the most extreme tortures , and finally , when satiated with the pleasure of seeing him suffer , the story is that they chose four very elastic trees , growing at a little distance from each other , and bent down the tops of them toward the central point between them", "context_len": 55}}}, "shavings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}}}, "inviting": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 45, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "It did not look very inviting , nor did it seem to be much in use , but as it led away from the main highway , it broke the trail , and without hesitation he turned the horse 's head in the direction of Clyde Station", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 94, "context": "Could any one suppose for a moment , that he could hear all the beautiful plans fora Knight-of-the-Garter day discussed in his presence , and never make an effort to have a hand in it ? To be sure , the children had tried to keep the date a close-guarded secret , but Albert had got wind of it , all the same ; and here he was , bright and fresh as the day itself , marching up the path , his little blue sacque folded carefully over one arm , and an inviting luncheon hamper swinging from the other", "context_len": 101}}}, "forefinger": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 23, "context": "The boys in the Boy 's Town used to make the motion of swimming with both arms ; or they held up the forefinger and middle-finger in the form of a swallow-tail ; they did this when it was necessary to be secret about it , as in school , and when they did not want the whole crowd of boys to come along ; and often when they just pretended they did not want some one to know", "context_len": 79}}}, "extremity": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 35, "context": "Beginning at the southeast , we first meet the historic Timucua family , the tribes of which are extinct at the present time ; and after describing the Indians of the Floridian Peninsula , southern extremity , we pass over to the Yuchi , on Savannah river , to the Naktche , Taensa and the other stocks once settled along and beyond the mighty Uk'hina , or `` water road '' of the Mississippi river", "context_len": 75}}}, "sacrifice": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 105, "context": "'s service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut her ear to the cry of the needy , because to answer it would cost a sacrifice of her own wishes ? Should she bear the burdens of others only when they did not weigh heavily on herself ?", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 50, "context": "I am not saying a word in defence of his having become a renegade ; yet even a renegade may have some sort of heart , and now that he has been called upon to fight against Christians he may well have repented of his faults , and determined to sacrifice his position and prospects rather than aid in the attack on the city", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But was not any sacrifice worth while that would give him a chance to sail with Margaret ? David was suddenly brought face to face with a new problem which had come into his life without his being aware of it", "context_len": 41}}}, "cruelties": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "Children in factories and mines required to be protected from the cruelties to which they were subjected ; chimney sweeps needed to be guarded from the dangers to which they were exposed ; the hours of labour in factories were excessive ; thieves required to be shown a way of escape from their wretched life ; ragged schools and other institutions needed support", "context_len": 63}}}, "condemns": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "There are few cruelties which public opinion in the boys ' world condemns , but I am glad to remember , to their honor , that there were not many in that Boy 's Town who would tie shirts ; and I fervently hope that there is no boy now living who would do it", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- He condemns him to death", "context_len": 6}}}, "extinct": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 18, "context": "Beginning at the southeast , we first meet the historic Timucua family , the tribes of which are extinct at the present time ; and after describing the Indians of the Floridian Peninsula , southern extremity , we pass over to the Yuchi , on Savannah river , to the Naktche , Taensa and the other stocks once settled along and beyond the mighty Uk'hina , or `` water road '' of the Mississippi river", "context_len": 75}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was a common enough occurrence in that district , which had once abounded in volcanoes , most of them now extinct", "context_len": 22}}}, "miscreant": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "As the crime is probably extinct , I will say that in those wicked days , if you were such a miscreant , and there was some boy you hated , you stole up and tied the hardest kind of a knot in one arm or both arms of his shirt", "context_len": 51}}}, "degree": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 92, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He was sure that half a degree colder would congeal it", "context_len": 11}}}, "meanness": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 82, "context": "I will ask Celia about it , but what I do n't like is that you should start it in a spirit of trying to get the better of another girl , though I can see that it is the most natural thing in the world for you to feel as you do , and I can see that Clara has really brought it on herself , but I do want my dear little girls to be charitable and above the petty meanness that is actuating Clara", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Merry was a tall , slender youth , with a whitish hue that suggested dissipation , and a fine , scornful curve to his lips that suggested meanness no less clearly", "context_len": 31}}}, "abhor": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was his brother , I suppose , who taught him to abhor it ; and perhaps it was his own suffering from it in part ; for he , too , sometimes shed bitter tears over such a knot , as I have seen hapless little wretches do , tearing at it with their nails and gnawing at it with their teeth , knowing that the time was passing when they could hope to hide the fact that they had been in swimming , and foreseeing no remedy but to cut off the sleeve above the knot , or else put on their clothes without the shirt , and trust to untying the knot when it got dry", "context_len": 119}}}, "gnawing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "It was his brother , I suppose , who taught him to abhor it ; and perhaps it was his own suffering from it in part ; for he , too , sometimes shed bitter tears over such a knot , as I have seen hapless little wretches do , tearing at it with their nails and gnawing at it with their teeth , knowing that the time was passing when they could hope to hide the fact that they had been in swimming , and foreseeing no remedy but to cut off the sleeve above the knot , or else put on their clothes without the shirt , and trust to untying the knot when it got dry", "context_len": 119}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "My boy listened with a gnawing literary jealousy of a boy who had read a book that he had never heard of", "context_len": 22}}}, "foreseeing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 86, "context": "It was his brother , I suppose , who taught him to abhor it ; and perhaps it was his own suffering from it in part ; for he , too , sometimes shed bitter tears over such a knot , as I have seen hapless little wretches do , tearing at it with their nails and gnawing at it with their teeth , knowing that the time was passing when they could hope to hide the fact that they had been in swimming , and foreseeing no remedy but to cut off the sleeve above the knot , or else put on their clothes without the shirt , and trust to untying the knot when it got dry", "context_len": 119}}}, "remedy": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 88, "context": "It was his brother , I suppose , who taught him to abhor it ; and perhaps it was his own suffering from it in part ; for he , too , sometimes shed bitter tears over such a knot , as I have seen hapless little wretches do , tearing at it with their nails and gnawing at it with their teeth , knowing that the time was passing when they could hope to hide the fact that they had been in swimming , and foreseeing no remedy but to cut off the sleeve above the knot , or else put on their clothes without the shirt , and trust to untying the knot when it got dry", "context_len": 119}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Then there arose a great cry in Britain ; and every one demanded that something should be done to remedy this state of things", "context_len": 24}}}, "untying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 112, "context": "It was his brother , I suppose , who taught him to abhor it ; and perhaps it was his own suffering from it in part ; for he , too , sometimes shed bitter tears over such a knot , as I have seen hapless little wretches do , tearing at it with their nails and gnawing at it with their teeth , knowing that the time was passing when they could hope to hide the fact that they had been in swimming , and foreseeing no remedy but to cut off the sleeve above the knot , or else put on their clothes without the shirt , and trust to untying the knot when it got dry", "context_len": 119}}}, "explicitly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "There must have been a lurking anxiety in all the boys ' hearts when they went in without leave , or , as my boy was apt to do , when explicitly forbidden", "context_len": 33}}}, "forgiven": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "But more incomprehensible than the existence of so bad a man in such a dark age is the renewed confidence ever accorded him , when , after more than once betraying the armies of his country into the hands of their foes , and fighting openly in the hostile cause , he is again forgiven , nay , received into favour , and sent once more to command the men he has already deceived , until he repeats the experiment , and when it fails is again admitted into confidence", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "But the teacher took the hand of the snivelling wretch , and called him affectionately by name , and said they would try to get along without that , and sent him to his seat forgiven", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 67, "context": "But we must ask Him from our hearts ; and can we do so if those hearts are full of unkindness and hard feeling towards those who have injured us ? I know how hard it is for you both , my darlings ; I know by my own feelings how hard it is to forgive Lem and Dolly ; but I can not hope to be forgiven myself for what I have done wrong this day , unless I forgive them the harm they have done to me", "context_len": 89}}}, "heartlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was an awful moment , and it covered him with shame before his mates , who heartlessly rejoiced , as children do , in the doom which they are escaping", "context_len": 31}}}, "rejoiced": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 70, "context": "`` Then he returned to the khan in a woeful state of chagrin and colour and despite , for envy of Alaeddin , and [ 557 ] taking his geomantic instruments , [ 558 ] smote his [ tablet of ] sand , so he might learn where the lamp was , and found that it was in the palace and not with Alaeddin ; [ 559 ] whereat he rejoiced with an exceeding joy and said , `` Now it will be an easy matter for me to bereave this accursed of his life and I have a way to come at the lamp", "context_len": 105}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I never shall forget how rejoiced I was when we got into a pure atmosphere and healthy soil again , but it was weeks , yes , even months , before the effects of my poisoned draught passed entirely away", "context_len": 40}}}, "expiated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "That sin , at least , he fully expiated ; and I will whisper to the Young People here at the end of the chapter , that somehow , soon or late , our sins do overtake us , and insist upon being paid for", "context_len": 45}}}, "sins": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 76, "context": "On his first night my boy saw `` The Beacon of Death , '' `` Bombastes Furioso , '' and `` Black-eyed Susan , '' and he never afterwards saw less than three plays each night , and he never missed a night , as long as the theatre languished in the unfriendly air of that mainly Calvinistic community , where the theatre was regarded by most good people as the eighth of the seven deadly sins", "context_len": 77}}}, "sinning": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "That is not the best reason for not sinning , but it is well to know it , and to believe it in our acts as well as our thoughts", "context_len": 30}}}, "northward": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 67, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 51, "context": "The party thus opposed were inactive and silent while Alexander was in their country , on his first visit to southern Greece ; but after his return they began to contemplate more decisive action , and afterward , when they heard of his having undertaken so desperate an enterprise as going northward with his forces , and actually crossing the Danube , they considered him as so completely out of the way that they grew very courageous , and meditated open rebellion", "context_len": 82}}}, "collector": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` How are we to begin ? Shall we do penance like the old monks ? Do you know , Rosamund '' -- here Irene linked her thin , almost steel-like little hand inside Rosamund 's arm -- '' that I am a most voracious reader ? Father was a great collector of books , and when I am tired of frightening the servants , and terrifying Frosty , and annoying mother , I spend days at a time in his library swallowing down the contents of his books", "context_len": 89}}}, "tolls": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "For a time my boy 's father was collector of tolls on it , but even when he was old enough to understand that his father held this State office ( the canal belonged to the State ) because he had been such a good Whig , and published the Whig newspaper , he could not grasp the notion of the distance which the canal-boats came out of and went into", "context_len": 71}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Over this entrance looms the beautiful tower containing Great Tom , an old , old bell that tolls a curfew of one hundred and one strokes every night as a signal for the closing of the college", "context_len": 37}}}, "conceiving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "It was hard enough to get it to the head of the Basin , which left the canal half a mile or so to the eastward , and stretched down into the town , a sheet of smooth water , fifteen or twenty feet deep , and a hundred wide ; his sense ached with , the effort of conceiving of the other side of it", "context_len": 66}}}, "alternated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "The Basin was bordered on either side near the end by pork-houses , where the pork was cut up and packed , and then lay in long rows of barrels on the banks , with other long rows of salt-barrels , and yet other long rows of whiskey-barrels ; cooper-shops , where the barrels were made , alternated with the pork-houses", "context_len": 61}}}, "sterns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The water made a soft tuck-tucking at the sterns of the boat , and you could catch sunfish , if you were the right kind of a boy , or the wrong kind ; the luck seemed to go a good deal with boys who were not good for much else", "context_len": 51}}}, "planks": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 82, "context": "Her Spanish crew we soon found snugly hidden away in spacious quarters beneath the lazaret ; and , as to the name on her stern , we found that the piece of wood on which it was carved and painted was reversible , having Virginia , New Orleans , carved on one side of it and Preciosa , Havana , on the other , and that it could be unbolted and reversed in a few minutes by lifting a couple of movable planks in the after cabin", "context_len": 87}}}, "navigation": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "This charge , also , Genoa took upon itself , and put on board much stronger crews than Gervaise deemed necessary for the navigation of the ships", "context_len": 27}}}, "prohibited": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "No limit was placed on their swimming in the Basin , except that of the law which prohibited it in the daytime , as the Basin was quite in the heart of the town", "context_len": 34}}}, "southerly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "As daylight dawned upon the third day of the fight we continued our retreat , and as a faint southerly wind sprang up the enemy drew near with the intention of renewing the fight , concentrating their efforts on Albemarle \u2019 s ship , which , covering the retreat , presented an undaunted spectacle to our relentless foes", "context_len": 58}}}, "latitude": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "While Darius had been thus advancing to the northward into the latitude where he knew that Alexander must cross the rivers , Alexander himself , and his small but compact and fearless body of Grecian troops , were moving eastward , toward the same region to which Darius 's line of march was tending", "context_len": 54}}}, "rioted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "In the warm summer nights of that southerly latitude , the water swarmed with laughing , shouting , screaming boys , who plunged from the banks and rioted in the delicious water , diving and ducking , flying and following , safe in the art of swimming which all of them knew", "context_len": 52}}}, "illumine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "They turned somersaults from the decks of the canal-boats ; some of the boys could turn double somersaults , and one boy got so far as to turn a somersault and a half ; it was long before the time of electric lighting , but when he struck the water there came a flash that seemed to illumine the universe", "context_len": 60}}}, "amphibious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` It was a -- '' he paused and looked impressively at each one , `` a -- I hardly know how to describe it , for it seemed to be amphibious , having once lived on land , and yet I doubt if it will live there ever again", "context_len": 50}}}, "lasted": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 22, "context": "These tales are narrations of the exploits and adventures of certain great warriors at the siege of Troy -- a siege which lasted ten years -- and they are written with so much beauty and force , they contain such admirable delineations of character , and such graphic and vivid descriptions of romantic adventures , and picturesque and striking scenes , that they have been admired in every age by all who have learned to understand the language in which they are written", "context_len": 83}}}, "skated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "The Basin , however , unlike the river , had a winter as well as a summer climate , and one of the very first things that my boy could remember was being on the ice there , when a young man caught him up into his arms , and skated off with him almost as far away as the canal", "context_len": 61}}}, "worldly": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 31, "context": "When you went to Scotland first , you and he were of one mind , one heart , but when you came hither again , your head was stuffed full of worldly wisdom and time-serving devices , talking of a Lord Protector instead of the glory of God , and hand and glove with that cruel Cromwell who did throw my saint into prison ! Your heart was turned from those that reared you , and given to their enemies ! And now you can stand by unmoved and see him you once loved haled to prison and death ! ''", "context_len": 101}}}, "splendor": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "He remembered the fearful joy of the adventure , and the pride , too ; for he had somehow the notion that this young fellow was handsome and fine , and did him an honor by his notice -- so soon does some dim notion of worldly splendor turn us into snobs ! The next thing was his own attempt at skating , when he was set down from the bank by his brother , full of a vainglorious confidence in his powers , and appeared instantly to strike on the top of his head", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 7, "context": "There was not a flaw in this splendor to the young eyes that gloated on it , and that followed it in rapture through every turn and winding of its course in the Boy 's Town ; nor in the magnificence of the actors and actresses , who came riding two by two in their circus-dresses after the chariots , and looking some haughty and contemptuous , and others quiet and even bored , as if it were nothing to be part of such a procession", "context_len": 86}}}, "snobs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "He remembered the fearful joy of the adventure , and the pride , too ; for he had somehow the notion that this young fellow was handsome and fine , and did him an honor by his notice -- so soon does some dim notion of worldly splendor turn us into snobs ! The next thing was his own attempt at skating , when he was set down from the bank by his brother , full of a vainglorious confidence in his powers , and appeared instantly to strike on the top of his head", "context_len": 95}}}, "vainglorious": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 78, "context": "He remembered the fearful joy of the adventure , and the pride , too ; for he had somehow the notion that this young fellow was handsome and fine , and did him an honor by his notice -- so soon does some dim notion of worldly splendor turn us into snobs ! The next thing was his own attempt at skating , when he was set down from the bank by his brother , full of a vainglorious confidence in his powers , and appeared instantly to strike on the top of his head", "context_len": 95}}}, "instantly": {"freq": 127, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 127, "word_index": 86, "context": "He remembered the fearful joy of the adventure , and the pride , too ; for he had somehow the notion that this young fellow was handsome and fine , and did him an honor by his notice -- so soon does some dim notion of worldly splendor turn us into snobs ! The next thing was his own attempt at skating , when he was set down from the bank by his brother , full of a vainglorious confidence in his powers , and appeared instantly to strike on the top of his head", "context_len": 95}}}, "glazing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "They made a very little ice go a long way in the Boy 's Town ; and began to use it for skating as soon as there was a glazing of it on the Basin", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "His head was reeling , his brain confused and black spots danced before his glazing eyes", "context_len": 16}}}, "drowned": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 84, "context": "But just then , the thoughts of every one in the house except mother ( and the news was carefully kept from her ) , were full of a great and terrible catastrophe , and even I forgot all about the Duchess , for one of our largest Orient liners had foundered on some sunken rocks not far from Port Adelaide , off the coast of South Australia , and there had been a terrific shipwreck , and almost every one on board was drowned", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "Beyond the kitchen-garden lay pleasant , sunny fields , at the foot of which flowed a small stream that farther down joined the river in which Jumbo had been so nearly drowned", "context_len": 32}}}, "crazed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Why , your feet are frozen ! '' said the big boy , and he dragged off my boy 's skates , and the little one ran all the long mile home , crazed with terror , and not knowing what moment his feet might drop off there in the road", "context_len": 52}}}, "flannel": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 8, "context": "Granby made the most extraordinary flowers of red flannel and gilt paper , -- flowers whose likeness never grew in gardens or greenhouses of any known land ; had she not baked sugar cakes which were intended to represent men and women , pigs , horses , and cows ? Were not the branches looped with gay ribbons ? Did they not bear rosy-cheeked apples , an orange for each child , some cheap but much prized toys , and , better than all , several useful and greatly needed articles , which had been the gift of Mrs", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` And here is a cap of Franky 's , and some little flannel shirts , which I will roll up in the bundle , '' said Mrs", "context_len": 28}}}, "clamp": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "When you went to get a pair of skates forty or fifty years ago , you did not make your choice between a Barney & Berry and an Acme , which fastened on with the turn of a screw or the twist of a clamp", "context_len": 45}}}, "assortment": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 88, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "sizes": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 67, "context": "In the Park Crescent Gardens , near Portland Place , -- their playground ; described by one friend in those days as a jungle , because of its unkempt condition , -- she would name one bed England , another France , another Germany , and so on , and would thus fix in the children \u2019 s minds their various positions , though the shapes and sizes of the beds were by no means always what they ought to have been", "context_len": 82}}}, "solid": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 36, "context": "She was not long in discovering the landmarks of which she was in search , and a few minutes later the canoe was threading its tortuous way among a tangled mass of mangrove roots toward the solid bank of the river , landing upon which , we drew our light craft bodily up out of the water , concealing her beneath a broad overhanging mass of foliage which hid her so effectually that I would defy anybody but ourselves to find her", "context_len": 82}}}, "guttered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "You found an assortment of big and little sizes of solid wood bodies with guttered blades turning up in front with a sharp point , or perhaps curling over above the toe", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The candle had burnt low in the lantern , and even as she looked it gave a last flicker , and guttered out , leaving her in utter darkness", "context_len": 29}}}, "blades": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 59, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Guy was not sorry to see the thick grass become thinner and thinner , for he remembered that amid its clustering blades he had nearly lost his life , and therefore looked with much complacency upon the broad , shallow river , along which their course lay ; the sandy loam beneath their feet , and the sand hills that arose like great billows of earth , rolling in regular succession over the level surface", "context_len": 75}}}, "acorn": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "The butter did not come by any means as quickly as the children expected , even when Fanny took hold ; and , tired of waiting for it , they presently began to amuse themselves with sailing the acorn cups which they had picked up in their walk , in the stream which ran through the dairy", "context_len": 57}}}, "brass": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` She came up to my room -- I will confess to you that I was hiding from her , absolutely hiding , and shaking from head to foot , scarcely knowing what to do -- and she came in as bold as brass , and yet with a new sort of humility about her , and she said to me , 'Will you forgive me ? And if you forgive me , will you come downstairs and let me put my hand inside your arm ? ' And somehow , although it was the very last thing on earth that I wanted to do , I did it ; and now here I am , and I do n't feel nearly so much afraid of her as", "context_len": 128}}}, "transfigured": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "In this case they sometimes ended in an acorn ; if this acorn was of brass , it transfigured the boy who wore that skate ; he might have been otherwise all rags and patches , but the brass acorn made him splendid from head to foot", "context_len": 47}}}, "carpenter": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 42, "context": "Dad put an axe into my hand , and taught me to chop kindling before I was out of petticoats , and when we went up the Rockies shooting grizzlies we built log-cabins , and I can tell you there was no carpenter to fetch there nearer than a hundred miles , so I guess I ought to know how to handle a saw and fix up a bit of lumber", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "A man who can carpenter , garden , or put in panes of glass , may find his knowledge most useful", "context_len": 21}}}, "pierced": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` Later on , when the Sultan visits the enchanted palace for the first time , Alaeddin `` brought him to the high kiosk and he looked at the belvedere ( teyyareh , a square or round erection on the top of a house , either open at the sides or pierced with windows , our architectural term 'lantern ' ) and its casements ( shebabik , pl", "context_len": 68}}}, "saddler": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then you took them to a saddler , and got him to make straps for them ; that is , if you were rich , and your father let you have a quarter to pay for the job", "context_len": 38}}}, "straps": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 49, "context": "I tell you another thing you do , said the captain : negroes there use lots of baskets , that they carry on their heads , filled with oranges and other things ; they also use them in loading and unloading vessels , and sometimes they carry them by straps of green hide that go over their shoulders", "context_len": 58}}}, "slumping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}}}, "cooper": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Sally made a little bed-tick and pillow , which Charlie stuffed with the down of the cat-tail ( cooper \u2019 s ) flag", "context_len": 23}}}, "whizzing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 75, "context": "They were always coming off , or getting crosswise of your foot , or feeble-mindedly slumping down on one side of the wood ; but it did not matter , if you had a fire on the ice , fed with old barrels and boards and cooper 's shavings , and could sit round it with your skates on , and talk and tell stories , between your flights and races afar ; and come whizzing back to it from the frozen distance , and glide , with one foot lifted , almost among the embers", "context_len": 96}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "A most bewildering place they found this same subway to be , full of people rushing for trains , noisy from the whizzing of cars from out of cavernous dark places and departing into equally unknown darkness", "context_len": 37}}}, "malaria": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "They were good places for the fever-and-ague which people had in those days without knowing it was malaria , or suffering it to interfere much with the pleasure and business of life ; but they seemed to my boy bowers of delight , especially one where there was a bear , chained to a weeping-willow , and another where there was a fishpond with gold-fish in it", "context_len": 67}}}, "bowers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}}}, "fishpond": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "They were good places for the fever-and-ague which people had in those days without knowing it was malaria , or suffering it to interfere much with the pleasure and business of life ; but they seemed to my boy bowers of delight , especially one where there was a bear , chained to a weeping-willow , and another where there was a fishpond with gold-fish in it", "context_len": 67}}}, "keyhole": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Rosamund got up softly and locked the door , not a minute too soon , for she had scarcely done so before the handle was turned and the voice of Irene was heard outside crying through the keyhole , `` What changeling is in this room ? Which of you housemaids has dared to lock herself in ? Come out ! I 've got a big spider ready , and '' -- --", "context_len": 73}}}, "safer": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 43, "context": "Miss Allyn was not so sure of that ; but Albert was mounting the wall of the terrace , to which he had led the way , in rather dangerous fashion , and Miss Allyn hurrying to lift the little fellow to a safer level , the conversation ended abruptly", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "What had come about was quite natural and ordinary : the Tollivers were anxious to get further away from the scene of their recent crime , to a safer and more obscure haunt than the open camp in the woods", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "Indeed , from what he himself knew of Harold 's intense nature , he felt very sure that it would be far wiser and safer that he should never know of all that had happened -- not , at any rate , unless Ted , having had a chance to prove the strength of his new resolutions , chose some day himself to tell him", "context_len": 65}}}, "wanting": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 58, "context": "But if our Church is to go on growing , as we hope and pray that it may , what would be the advantage of having a tiny chapel , which would not comfortably accommodate ourselves in a fiery climate , and in which there would be no room at all for heathen spectators ? We should be wanting a second ; and how could we procure a second clergyman ? Please thank dear Fred very , very much for his kindness in collecting , and assure him that we wish to make the money go as far as possible", "context_len": 100}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "She talked a lot about my being careless and our wanting to play tricks on Mary , but she said she was `` glad to be able to think as highly of Margaret Vaughan as she had always done , '' and we were to go at once and set you free", "context_len": 52}}}, "incomparable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "It seemed that in one of the colleges somebody lived who Marie-Celeste would have given more to see , next to the Queen ( and , as you know , she had seen her without the asking ) , than any one else in England , and that was the man who calls himself Lewis Carroll , and who has written those incomparable books , Through the Looking-Glass and Alice in Wonderland", "context_len": 72}}}, "prow": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "When with Chris it was forward clear to the bow to lean over the rail and see the magnificent prow cut the water ; or way to the stern , to watch the far-shining train , the screws churned into white foam behind them ; or an hour 'midships , where the ever-varying amusements with which the steerage passengers beguile the weary hours can be looked down upon from the saloon deck of either first or second cabin", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "These were in vain , for her sharp prow struck them amidships , cutting the boat almost in two , and she sank like a stone , the galley , without a pause , making for another boat", "context_len": 38}}}, "hindmost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "The water curled away on either side of her sharp prow , that cut its way onward at the full rate of five miles an hour , and the team came swinging down the tow-path at a gallant trot , the driver sitting the hindmost horse of three , and cracking his long-lashed whip with loud explosions , as he whirled its snaky spirals in the air", "context_len": 67}}}, "cracking": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "The water curled away on either side of her sharp prow , that cut its way onward at the full rate of five miles an hour , and the team came swinging down the tow-path at a gallant trot , the driver sitting the hindmost horse of three , and cracking his long-lashed whip with loud explosions , as he whirled its snaky spirals in the air", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "It would try to butt them into the well when they leaned down to pull up the bucket from the curb ; and if it came out of the house , and saw a boy cracking nuts at the low flat stone the children had in the back-yard to crack nuts on , it would pretend that the boy was making motions to insult it , and before he knew what he was about it would fly at him and send him spinning head over heels", "context_len": 86}}}, "explosions": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 56, "context": "The water curled away on either side of her sharp prow , that cut its way onward at the full rate of five miles an hour , and the team came swinging down the tow-path at a gallant trot , the driver sitting the hindmost horse of three , and cracking his long-lashed whip with loud explosions , as he whirled its snaky spirals in the air", "context_len": 67}}}, "snaky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "The water curled away on either side of her sharp prow , that cut its way onward at the full rate of five miles an hour , and the team came swinging down the tow-path at a gallant trot , the driver sitting the hindmost horse of three , and cracking his long-lashed whip with loud explosions , as he whirled its snaky spirals in the air", "context_len": 67}}}, "spirals": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 63, "context": "The water curled away on either side of her sharp prow , that cut its way onward at the full rate of five miles an hour , and the team came swinging down the tow-path at a gallant trot , the driver sitting the hindmost horse of three , and cracking his long-lashed whip with loud explosions , as he whirled its snaky spirals in the air", "context_len": 67}}}, "volleyed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "All the boys in town were there , meekly proud to be ordered out of his way , to break and fly before his volleyed oaths and far before his horses ' feet ; and suddenly the captain pressed his foot on the spring and released the tow-rope", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The Trust magnate was in his usual hurry , and he volleyed these commands as if argument were out of the question :", "context_len": 23}}}, "oaths": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 48, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Think you that a man who has denied his faith and taken service with his enemies is to be trusted , whatever oaths he may take ?", "context_len": 27}}}, "swishing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "She was half-way across , and was feeling very well pleased , when something hurtled through the air with a loud , swishing sound , and the next moment she was jerked violently to the ground , while an exceedingly uncomfortable sensation round her waist told her that she had been caught by the lasso", "context_len": 55}}}, "steersman": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Keep just this distance if you can , '' said the gleeman , and drew an arrow suddenly to its head ; it whistled through the air , and the steersman , transfixed , rose , leapt in the boat , and fell in the sea a corpse", "context_len": 49}}}, "aport": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The driver kept on to the stable with unslackened speed , and the line followed him , swishing and skating over the water , while the steersman put his helm hard aport , and the packet rounded to , and swam softly and slowly up to her moorings", "context_len": 48}}}, "moorings": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "But our joy was turned to feelings of dismay when we perceived that two small ships had joined the Dutch men-of-war , and that they were brought to with reduced canvas immediately to windward of us , and were held by stern moorings only , so that their bows were pointed straight at our luckless vessel", "context_len": 56}}}, "arrives": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 54, "context": "is coming ; we don \u2019 t know whether she is sticking half-way on the road , waiting for the horse which we offered to send twelve miles , if she required it ! Like the famous little pig , we have eaten all the bread and butter ; and if the grand lady arrives -- without that faithless kahar -- what shall we give her to eat ? I urged Florrie at least to send to the city for meat ; but she fears that in the absence of the cook the guest may arrive", "context_len": 96}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "When , however , the time at length arrives , the most bold and desperate portion of the army are designated to lead the attack", "context_len": 25}}}, "heroic": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 48, "context": "How came it that this girl had guessed his secret at once when most people passed him , taking him but for a sturdy beggar ? What made her suspect him of being a fugitive ? Was her offer of shelter but careless good nature , or a heroic endeavour to save a hunted man ? At any rate he had not fallen so low as to draw suspicion on a woman , and a young woman to boot , although she was plainly no nervous , fanciful , fine lady , but a bright , resolute , country girl , with good health and high spirits gleaming from every flash of her bright eyes , and every turn of her auburn head", "context_len": 123}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 12, "context": "It had often been a sharp struggle to him , nursed in heroic times , learned in battle songs , and of the very blood of the vikings , to avoid the feeling that Christianity was not the religion of the brave ; now the difficulty was over , and who shall say that the first joy of the martyr 's soul was not the knowledge that his sufferings had already borne such fruit to God !", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Or if one can not do anything heroic , let him even do something that will bring him notoriety , and then --", "context_len": 23}}}, "drivers": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 90, "context": "David could not be spared to go with them , but all the children had been taught to drive , and even Bobby had a firm hand on the reins , and knew the rules of the road as well as many a more experienced coachman ; and I think , too , that Pixie had a sense of her responsibilities , and could be trusted not to get the wheel locked with a passing waggon , or to race too furiously down a steep hill , whatever feats her drivers might urge her to perform", "context_len": 96}}}, "tavern": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 41, "context": "One afternoon these brothers quarrelled a long time , and then the sober brother retired to the doorway of a pork-house , where he stood , probably brooding upon his injuries , when the drunkard , who had remained near the tavern , suddenly caught up an axe and flung it ; the boys saw it sail across the corner of the Basin , and strike in the door just above his brother 's head", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` You 'd better pick up and go with us , Shannon ; our company is going to raid the tavern to-night , and to-morrow we take the road", "context_len": 29}}}, "whiplash": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Ben \u2019 s ears were deaf to that sound , however ; he was wholly unaware that his name came snapping forth at the end of that cheer like a cracker at the end of a whiplash", "context_len": 37}}}, "target": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 68, "context": "John , who knew Charlie was too modest to do it himself , showed him the lookout in the top of the tree , the house , and all that was in it , and also told him how Charlie beat them firing at a mark , though they had guns , and he a bow and arrows ; and showed him the bullet-holes and arrow-marks in the target", "context_len": 69}}}, "feat": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 64, "context": "[ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm , that thou shouldst merit of him this boon ? Again , this that thou cravest is beyond thy condition ; [ 336 ]", "context_len": 128}}}, "pioneers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "Before the procession set out from Babylon an army of pioneers and workmen went forward to repair the roads , strengthen the bridges , and remove the obstacles along the whole line of route over which the train was to pass", "context_len": 41}}}, "thrower": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The thrower caught the axe by the end of the helve , and with a dextrous twirl sent it flying through the air , and struck its edge into whatever object he aimed at -- usually a tree", "context_len": 38}}}, "helve": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The thrower caught the axe by the end of the helve , and with a dextrous twirl sent it flying through the air , and struck its edge into whatever object he aimed at -- usually a tree", "context_len": 38}}}, "dextrous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The thrower caught the axe by the end of the helve , and with a dextrous twirl sent it flying through the air , and struck its edge into whatever object he aimed at -- usually a tree", "context_len": 38}}}, "twirl": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "The thrower caught the axe by the end of the helve , and with a dextrous twirl sent it flying through the air , and struck its edge into whatever object he aimed at -- usually a tree", "context_len": 38}}}, "aimed": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 18, "context": "Never in my life had I felt more deadly cool and self-possessed than I did then as I aimed steadily at the animal 's right eye ; I felt that I could not miss ; nor did I ; for while we thus stood motionlessly staring at each other , I released the string , and the next instant the great lithe beast sprang convulsively into the air , with the butt of my arrow protruding from his eye and the point buried deep in his brain", "context_len": 87}}}, "loafers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then there were certain genteel loafers , young men of good families , who hung about the principal hotel , and whom the boys believed to be fighters of singular prowess", "context_len": 31}}}, "controversies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "One was of the unhappy fraternity of town-drunkards , and somehow the boys thought him a finer fellow than the other , whom somehow they considered `` mean , '' and they were always of his side in their controversies", "context_len": 40}}}, "quarrelled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "One afternoon these brothers quarrelled a long time , and then the sober brother retired to the doorway of a pork-house , where he stood , probably brooding upon his injuries , when the drunkard , who had remained near the tavern , suddenly caught up an axe and flung it ; the boys saw it sail across the corner of the Basin , and strike in the door just above his brother 's head", "context_len": 75}}}, "brooding": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "One afternoon these brothers quarrelled a long time , and then the sober brother retired to the doorway of a pork-house , where he stood , probably brooding upon his injuries , when the drunkard , who had remained near the tavern , suddenly caught up an axe and flung it ; the boys saw it sail across the corner of the Basin , and strike in the door just above his brother 's head", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was interrupted in his brooding and his plans by a young girl , who came down toward him between the rows of springing corn", "context_len": 25}}}, "injuries": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 52, "context": "Mingled with many other things , giving an account of their summer among the mountains , and written in Maggie 's own droll , peculiar way , ran the story of Lem and Dolly , of their persecutions , and of the difficulty she and Bessie had had in forgiving their many injuries ; but all that was not new to the mother , who now read for the first time what Maggie had written during the last week", "context_len": 79}}}, "drunkard": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 34, "context": "One afternoon these brothers quarrelled a long time , and then the sober brother retired to the doorway of a pork-house , where he stood , probably brooding upon his injuries , when the drunkard , who had remained near the tavern , suddenly caught up an axe and flung it ; the boys saw it sail across the corner of the Basin , and strike in the door just above his brother 's head", "context_len": 75}}}, "manifest": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "son-in-law and to have his daughter to bride ? '' Now the Vizier marvelled at the greatness of these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about to work upon him by practice , that he might hinder him from giving his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour to Alaeddin , and accordingly said to him , [ 436 ] `` O my lord , all the treasures of the world were not", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "The Abbey boasted the reputation of maintaining what Peggy called ' a real , live , genuine ghost , ' though none of the family had ever caught a glimpse of it , it seeming to prefer to manifest itself to Joe and to chance visitors from the village who came up to the house on dark evenings", "context_len": 58}}}, "reigned": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 88, "context": "It is true that Alexander 's soldiers , about to leave their homes to go to another quarter of the globe , and into scenes of danger and death from which it was very improbable that many of them would ever return , had no other celestial protection to look up to than the spirits of ancient heroes , who , they imagined , had , somehow or other , found their final home in a sort of heaven among the summits of the mountains , where they reigned , in some sense , over human affairs ; but this , small as it seems to us , was a great deal to them", "context_len": 114}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Mark turned and followed him swiftly down the road toward Highland Falls , and quiet once more reigned about the post", "context_len": 21}}}, "meddled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 72, "context": "I do not mean that there was no law for the boys , for it was manifest to their terror in two officers whom they knew as constables , and who may have reigned one after another , or together , with full power of life and death over them , as they felt ; but who in a community mainly so peaceful acted upon Dogberry 's advice , and made and meddled with rogues as little as they could", "context_len": 80}}}, "rogues": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 74, "context": "I do not mean that there was no law for the boys , for it was manifest to their terror in two officers whom they knew as constables , and who may have reigned one after another , or together , with full power of life and death over them , as they felt ; but who in a community mainly so peaceful acted upon Dogberry 's advice , and made and meddled with rogues as little as they could", "context_len": 80}}}, "corporation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "From time to time it was known among the boys that you would be taken up if you went in swimming inside of the corporation line , and for a while they would be careful to keep beyond it ; but this could not last ; they were soon back in the old places , and I suppose no arrests were ever really made", "context_len": 64}}}, "arrests": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "From time to time it was known among the boys that you would be taken up if you went in swimming inside of the corporation line , and for a while they would be careful to keep beyond it ; but this could not last ; they were soon back in the old places , and I suppose no arrests were ever really made", "context_len": 64}}}, "coffins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Of course no such thing ever happened ; but the boys believed it , and it froze my boy 's soul with fear ; all the more because this constable was a cabinet-maker and made coffins ; from his father 's printing-office the boy could hear the long slide of his plane over the wood , and he could smell the varnish on the boards", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It 's rather like the cover of one of those old stone coffins in the churchyard , only smaller", "context_len": 19}}}, "plane": {"freq": 78, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 78, "word_index": 51, "context": "Of course no such thing ever happened ; but the boys believed it , and it froze my boy 's soul with fear ; all the more because this constable was a cabinet-maker and made coffins ; from his father 's printing-office the boy could hear the long slide of his plane over the wood , and he could smell the varnish on the boards", "context_len": 65}}}, "headsman": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 39, "context": "It is , however , abundantly evident from the general context that this is the author 's intention in the passage in question , Alaeddin 's head being metaphorically in the hands of ( or pledged to ) the headsman , inasmuch as he had engaged to return and suffer decapitation in case he should not succeed in recovering the princess within forty days", "context_len": 64}}}, "severity": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 30, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}}}, "exaggerate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "I can \u2019 t say he showed any sign of wishing to sneak out , and when I remark that his uncle didn \u2019 t have any trouble in lugging him off , I suppose I exaggerate , but I want to make sure the scamp doesn \u2019 t find an excuse for dodging a square , stand up fight", "context_len": 60}}}, "criminal": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 89, "context": "A girl at school mislaid a pencil which she thought she had lent him , and he began to have a morbid belief that he must have stolen it ; he became frantic with the mere dread of guilt ; he could not eat or sleep , and it was not till he went to make good the loss with a pencil which his grandfather gave him that the girl said she had found her pencil in her desk , and saved him from the despair of a self-convicted criminal", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 58, "context": "It represents the bare cost of a new copper pedestal to replace the one you shot to pieces last night , and it 's a wonder you are not in jail for murder , for had that cannon ball struck a human being -- Enough ! before I take up this outrage with the district attorney in its criminal phase , are you going to settle the damage , or are you not ? ''", "context_len": 75}}}, "consequence": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 74, "word_index": 26, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "guiltless": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "`` When Alaeddin heard their speech , wonderment took him and his tongue was tied , for that he knew not the cause ; then he turned to the eunuchs and officers and said , `` Prithee , sirs , [ 573 ] have you no knowledge of the cause of this commandment of the Sultan ? I know myself guiltless , forasmuch as I have done no sin against the Sultan nor against his realm", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` And he went on to excuse himself to him and kiss him ; and [ 624 ] Alaeddin said to him , `` O Lord of the Age , thou didst with me nothing contrary to the law and I also was guiltless of offence ; but the thing came all of that vile Maugrabin enchanter", "context_len": 57}}}, "obscure": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "If we elders stand here in the place of the Heavenly Father towards those younger children of His , He will not hold us guiltless when we obscure for them the important difference between a great and a small misdeed , or wring their souls , fear-clouded as they always are , with a sense of perdition for no real sin", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "It seemed to be a good deal of fun , but he did not dare to risk it ; and he had an obscure trouble of mind when , the last thing , four or five ponies were brought out with as many monkeys tied on their backs , and set to run a race round the ring", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "as she was in her own home ; '' but I confess that his explanation seems to me as obscure as the text", "context_len": 23}}}, "misdeed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "If we elders stand here in the place of the Heavenly Father towards those younger children of His , He will not hold us guiltless when we obscure for them the important difference between a great and a small misdeed , or wring their souls , fear-clouded as they always are , with a sense of perdition for no real sin", "context_len": 61}}}, "wring": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "They catch the rain , in such cases , with cloths , and afterward wring out the water ; though in this instance , as the historians of that day say , the soldiers did not wait for this tardy method of supply , but the whole detachment held back their heads and opened their mouths , to catch the drops of rain as they fell", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "perdition": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "That was because the boys had not the experience to enable them to think rightly , or to think at all about such things , or to know what images of perdition they had before their eyes ; and when they followed them and teased them , they did not know they were joining like fiends in the torment of lost souls", "context_len": 62}}}, "depend": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 60, "context": "It was clearly impossible to take thirteen prizes to Rhodes , for there would be but three or four knights to each , and were they to fall in with but one Moorish pirate , they might suffer great disaster , while , should they meet with a storm , they would fare badly indeed , as they could not depend upon the rescued Christians for the management of the sails and oars in heavy weather", "context_len": 76}}}, "crawfish": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "These abounded so that if you dropped a string with a bit of meat on it into the water anywhere , you could pull it up again with two or three crawfish hanging to it", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "One day he came up to my boy where he sat fishing for crawfish in the Hydraulic , with his bare legs dangling over the edge of a culvert , and , unawed by this august figure , asked him pleasantly what luck he had", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` He wo n't crawfish", "context_len": 5}}}, "designed": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 22, "context": "The boys could not begin to use them all for bait , which was the only use their Creator seemed to have designed them for ; but they had vaguely understood that people somewhere ate them , or something like them , though they had never known even the name of lobsters ; and they always intended to get their mothers to have them cooked for them", "context_len": 67}}}, "lobsters": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 69, "context": "`` He also states that the French post was three leagues from the mouth of Yazoo river , close to a village inhabited by a medley of Yazoo , Couroas and Ofogoula Indians , and mentions the tribes in the following order ( I , 163 ) : `` Yazoo Indians , about 100 huts ; further up , Coroas , about 40 huts ; Chactioumas or `` red lobsters '' , about 50 huts , on same river ; Ouf\u00e9-ouglas , about 60 huts ; Tapoussas , not over 25 huts", "context_len": 92}}}, "culvert": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "The Hydraulic ran alongside of the Academy , and at recess the boys had a good deal of fun with it , one way and another , sailing shingles with stones on them , and watching them go under one end of the culvert and come out of the other , or simply throwing rocks into the water", "context_len": 58}}}, "foothold": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 73, "context": "`` Well , first they would find , either by accident or after a search , a herd of buffaloes , grazing on a plateau , perhaps three or four hundred feet above the river , for such are very often found a mile or more in length along the Columbia or Missouri Rivers , which abruptly terminate , forming a precipice so perfectly perpendicular that neither man or beast can gain a foothold on their sides", "context_len": 77}}}, "cock": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "The fox was the emblem ( totem ) of the Democrats in the campaigns of 1840 and 1844 ; and in their processions they always had a fox chained to the hickory flag-poles which they carried round on their wagons , together with a cock , reconciled probably in a common terror", "context_len": 52}}}, "serenely": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 55, "context": "She travelled to the glorious East ; she met the rising sun , -- And even so her day of heavenly bliss was soon begun ; I knew \u2019 twas sunrise with my child , while night was o \u2019 er me weeping , E \u2019 er closed my weary day , my darling was serenely sleeping", "context_len": 57}}}, "currant": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "Conway contained several things which made the dinner much more of a feast than it would otherwise have been , for there was a jar of tomato soup , a small chicken pie with scalloped leaves and little balls of crust on top , some delicious pickles , a glass of currant jelly and another of cranberry sauce", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The sight of the roast mutton , and the currant tart with Devonshire cream , which formed the nursery dinner that day , made him shudder ; and going to his own room , he flung himself on the bed , and after having taken some of the soup which was brought to him , he fell asleep", "context_len": 58}}}, "dishonor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "In those days the boys attached peculiar ideas of dishonor to taking a dare", "context_len": 14}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Only my place ! How then can I live ? Why should I dishonor my own name and lose my reputation for you ? \u2019", "context_len": 25}}}, "challenged": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 9, "context": "In this case , the high spirit who was challenged felt nothing base in running up behind his unsuspecting friend and popping him into the water , and I have no doubt the victim considered the affair in the right light when he found that it was a dare", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Mallory , as the challenged party , has the right to name the time", "context_len": 14}}}, "popping": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "In this case , the high spirit who was challenged felt nothing base in running up behind his unsuspecting friend and popping him into the water , and I have no doubt the victim considered the affair in the right light when he found that it was a dare", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "As he did so he heard a loud popping sound , as if his ear-drums were bursting , and the next instant he could hear the distant rumble of the guns and the voices of men in his proximity", "context_len": 39}}}, "scaled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "In the early dawn of that day the Mahdi assaulted the town in overwhelming force -- whether helped by treachery is not exactly known ; and before his well-fed , well-trained hosts , the feeble worn-out garrison gave way , the walls were scaled , the city taken , and the hero who had won the affection of many nations fell amidst the people he had come to save", "context_len": 69}}}, "costume": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 39, "context": "As it wound through the streets in splendid array , the grand master and officials in all their robes of state , the knights in full armour and the mantles of the Order , while the inhabitants in gala costume lined the streets , windows , and housetops , the ladies waving scarves and scattering flowers down on the knights , the roar of great cannon on the south side of the city showed that the Turks had commenced the attack in another quarter", "context_len": 84}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was one of the empty rooms that they entered , however , and there they proceeded to costume one of their number , putting on a huge red sash , some medals , a few shoulder straps borrowed for the occasion , and , last of all , a false mustache", "context_len": 52}}}, "barefoot": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "No great harm was done ; everybody could swim , and a boy 's summer costume in that hot climate was made up of a shirt and trousers and a straw hat ; no boy who had any regard for his social standing wore shoes or stockings , and as they were all pretty proud , they all went barefoot from April till October", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "How can you walk in them ? I as often as not go barefoot all day long ; but then I am a wild thing , a changeling , and I suppose , after all , you are not", "context_len": 39}}}, "characterized": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "Notwithstanding , however , the extraordinary discretion and wisdom which characterized the mind of Alexander in his early years , he was often haughty and headstrong , and in cases where his pride or his resentment were aroused , he was sometimes found very impetuous and uncontrollable", "context_len": 47}}}, "extraction": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "Now , Orton Campbell knew very well that he had made this promise , but the payment of nine hundred dollars he dreaded as much as some of my readers would dread the extraction of half a dozen teeth", "context_len": 39}}}, "blacksmith": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 12, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}}}, "dispute": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 51, "context": "In this case , when the army was gradually drawing near to the river , the prodromi came in with the news that they had been to the river , and found the whole opposite shore , at the place of crossing , lined with Persian troops , collected there to dispute the passage", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 75, "context": "Mafuta hates you , I know , and would willingly ` smell you out ' if he dared ; but the people will not let him ; for where would they get any one else to play beautiful music to them if you were to die ? Besides , do you think I would allow any one to hurt you ? My father is the king ; no one , not even Mafuta , dare dispute his will ; and I have more influence than any one else with the king", "context_len": 91}}}, "stingy": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "Now , if it had been you or me , Jennie , that had so much given to us , maybe we 'd have been selfish and stingy like ; so the Lord saw it was n't best for us", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "If the owner of the orchard was supposed to be stingy you might do it to plague him", "context_len": 18}}}, "cheat": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "But thou knowest not , O my son , that the good of the Muslims , believers in the unity of God the Most High , is lawful spoil in the eyes of Jews ; nay , they still cheat the Muslims and especially this accursed one with whom thou dealest and into whose hands thou hast fallen", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "When [ 312 ] the goldsmith heard this , `` Out on this accursed one , '' cried he , `` who fleeceth the servants of God the Most High ! '' Then he looked at Alaeddin and said to him , `` O my son , this Jew is a cheat , who hath cheated thee and laughed at thee , for that the silver of this thy platter is pure and fine ; and I have weighed it and find its worth threescore diners and ten ; so , an it please thee take its price , take [ it ]", "context_len": 103}}}, "lied": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "A person must always hit a person who told him he lied ; but even if you called a Yankee a fighting liar ( the worst form of this insult ) , he would not hit you , but just call you a liar back", "context_len": 45}}}, "liar": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 44, "context": "Ah ! he exclaimed suddenly , the thought has just occurred to me of a way by which you can obtain confirmation of my story ; and , as I value your good opinion and would not be regarded as a boaster and a liar , I entreat you to take it", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Who wants to hint that Sergeant Mickey Rourke is a liar , now ?", "context_len": 14}}}, "representative": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "The little old gentleman turned out to be a very learned don from Oxford , with a terrific list of letters at the end of his name , and in the course of a few days a representative arrived from the Bodleian Library with a request to be allowed to inspect the books ( which by this time had been removed to a corner of the big drawing-room ) , and after a considerable amount of talking and writing it was arranged that they should be sent to London at once to be sold by auction", "context_len": 96}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 63, "context": "`` As to his pugilistic abilities , I am by no means so accurately informed , but if my conjecture be of any value whatsoever , I should be inclined to infer , from the fact that our enemies , the representatives of tyranny and oppression , who are endeavoring to reduce us to submission , have selected him as their champion and representative in arms , that -- -- ''", "context_len": 71}}}, "sectional": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "My boy long accepted these ideas of New England as truly representative of the sectional character", "context_len": 16}}}, "repairing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 32, "context": "There was the Little Reservoir first , and then a mile beyond it the Big Reservoir , and there was nearly always a large flat boat on each which was used for repairing the banks , but which the boys employed as a pleasure-barge", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Driggs kept a little shop where he did watch and clock repairing , and he was noted for his skill as an engraver", "context_len": 23}}}, "clandestine": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "It seemed in some natural way to belong to them , and yet they had a feeling of something clandestine in pushing out on the Reservoir in it", "context_len": 28}}}, "inferred": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "My boy then learned , for the first time , that there was such a book as the `` Arabian Nights ; '' one of the other boys told stories out of it , and he inferred that the sole copy in existence belonged to this boy", "context_len": 47}}}, "duplicated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "He knew that they all had school-books alike , but it did not occur to him that a book which was not a Reader or a Speller was ever duplicated", "context_len": 30}}}, "loll": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They did nothing with their boat except loll in it and tell stories , and as there was no current in the Reservoir , they must have remained pretty much in the same place ; but they had a sense of the wildest adventure , which mounted to frenzy , when some men rose out of the earth on the shore , and shouted at them , `` Hello , there ! What are you doing with that boat ? '' They must have had an oar ; at any rate , they got to the opposite bank , and , springing to land , fled somewhere into the vaguest past", "context_len": 111}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` No want loll down hillee , '' said the Chinaman , without , however , betraying any fear", "context_len": 19}}}, "frenzy": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "They did nothing with their boat except loll in it and tell stories , and as there was no current in the Reservoir , they must have remained pretty much in the same place ; but they had a sense of the wildest adventure , which mounted to frenzy , when some men rose out of the earth on the shore , and shouted at them , `` Hello , there ! What are you doing with that boat ? '' They must have had an oar ; at any rate , they got to the opposite bank , and , springing to land , fled somewhere into the vaguest past", "context_len": 111}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 85, "context": "coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to find the poor creature ; so that , his hair being wet , he was a sight to behold when Harry pulled him out , covered with mud from head to foot , and had to be sent behind in disgrace", "context_len": 128}}}, "vaguest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 109, "context": "They did nothing with their boat except loll in it and tell stories , and as there was no current in the Reservoir , they must have remained pretty much in the same place ; but they had a sense of the wildest adventure , which mounted to frenzy , when some men rose out of the earth on the shore , and shouted at them , `` Hello , there ! What are you doing with that boat ? '' They must have had an oar ; at any rate , they got to the opposite bank , and , springing to land , fled somewhere into the vaguest past", "context_len": 111}}}, "bases": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "There were large trees standing in the hollow which became the bed of the Reservoir , and these died when the water was let in around them , and gave the stretch of quiet waters a strange , weird look ; about their bases was the best kind of place for sunfish , and even for bass", "context_len": 57}}}, "bass": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}}}, "leakage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "It was several years before the catfish got in , and then they were mud-cats ; but the boys had great luck with sunfish there and in the pools about the flood-gates , where there was always some leakage , and where my boy once caught a whole string of live fish which had got away from some other boy , perhaps weeks before ; they were all swimming about , in a lively way , and the largest hungrily took his bait", "context_len": 83}}}, "nosing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "The great pleasure of fishing in these pools was that the waters were so clear you could see the fat , gleaming fellows at the bottom , nosing round your hook , and going off and coming back several times before they made up their minds to bite", "context_len": 48}}}, "defend": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 96, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I could see that Sir John was scanning the hill for a spot where we could best defend ourselves", "context_len": 19}}}, "celebrate": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 34, "context": "I do not know why there should have been so many empty tar-barrels in the Boy 's Town , or what they used so much tar for ; but there were barrels enough to celebrate all the Whig victories that the boys ever heard of , and more , too ; the boys did not always wait for the victories , but celebrated every election with bonfires , in the faith that it would turn out right", "context_len": 77}}}, "tolerable": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 74, "context": "Here and there Lilian ran , hunting in all Peggy 's favourite haunts -- now peeping into a hollow yew-tree , now peering at the top of a ladder , now rummaging in the tool-shed , then back through the sand-quarry into the stack-yard , where there was a very good chance that the young lady might be hidden away in some snug little hole among the hay ; but though Lilian got a tolerable amount of hay-seed into her hair , her efforts were fruitless , and she was just turning away , hot and out of breath , to give up the useless search , when the sound of a low , chuckling laugh attracted her to the barn", "context_len": 121}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 93, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}}}, "probable": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` This ( or a similar text ) is evidently the original of Galland 's translation of this episode and it is probable , therefore , that the French translator inserted the mention `` of a certain warm drink '' ( tea ) , out of that mistaken desire for local colouring at all costs which has led so many French authors ( especially those of our own immediate day ) astray", "context_len": 72}}}, "organism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It is not probable that any lower organism", "context_len": 8}}}, "corporal": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "Texas had rubbed that mantel fiercely ; yet , to get it so clean as not to soil the glove was almost impossible , and so the corporal first held up the finger to show the mark of dirt and then -- wrote down `` dust on mantel", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` This same morning -- and my friend Indian will substantiate my statement , for he was there -- a low , ignorant cadet corporal did enter into my room , for inspection , by Zeus , and after generally displaying his ill-manners , he turned to me and conveyed the extraordinary information that , according to rules , forsooth , I must be deprived of the dearest object of my affections , solace of my weary hours , my friend in time of need , my companion in sickness , which through all the trials of adversity has stuck to me closer than a brother , my only joy , my -- -- ''", "context_len": 115}}}, "sufferance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Oh , the child ought not be on sufferance but have the genial freedom of a home of her very own where she could live over the childhood she had missed and learn the glad inspiration of youth", "context_len": 38}}}, "dies": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 23, "context": "Driggs confessed that your father \u2019 s testimony concerning him at the trial was absolutely true that he did bring the packages of dies for making counterfeit money to your father , and , having deceived him regarding the contents of those packages , induced him to conceal them in his house , where they were found", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 13, "context": "'Oh yes , for I looked at the end chapter , and she dies beautiful , and they plant snowdrops on her grave ; and her big brother , what 's so unkind to her , gets drowned through goin ' boatin ' on Sunday , ' replied Polly , regarding Peggy as if she thought her courses might lead her to a similar watery fate", "context_len": 66}}}, "barbs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "Then seating himself on the side , he began talking and chatting to the men , who were shaking mackerel out of their dark-brown nets , where they hung caught by the gills , which acted like the barbs to their arrow-like flight through the sea against the drift-net , and prevented their return", "context_len": 54}}}, "captor": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 70, "context": "But , as luck would have it , the weather fell still lighter at sunset on our ninth day out ; and on the following morning at daybreak we found ourselves becalmed within three miles of a British cruiser , which promptly lowered her boats and despatched them to overhaul us ; and by breakfast-time I had the pleasure of finding myself once more under the British flag , our captor proving to be the corvette Cleopatra , by the captain and officers of which I was most kindly received when I had related to them my strange story", "context_len": 99}}}, "abreast": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 72, "context": "Others would make for one of the cinnamon-sailed luggers lying at anchor , to go round and back , or would get into one of the boats ; while some , more venturesome , or really more confident in their powers over the water , would go boldly out , perhaps a mile , to meet some lugger coming in from the fishing-ground , sure of being taken aboard and riding back abreast of the boulders where they had left their clothes", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "He ran home to show it and leave it in safe-keeping , and then hurried back , so as to walk with the other boys abreast of a great platform on wheels , where an old woman sat spinning inside of a log-cabin , and a pioneer in a hunting-shirt stood at the door , with his long rifle in his hand", "context_len": 62}}}, "vividly": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 42, "context": "My boy could dimly recall the day when the water was first let into the Hydraulic , and the little fellows ran along its sides to keep abreast of the current , as they easily could ; and he could see more vividly the tumult which a break in the embankment of the Little Reservoir caused", "context_len": 56}}}, "embankment": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 17, "context": "I suspect that in their secret hearts they would have been glad to have seen that whole embankment washed away , for the excitement 's sake , and for the hope of catching the fish that would be left flopping at the bottom of the Reservoir when the waters were drained out , I think that these waters were brought somehow from Old River , but I am not sure how", "context_len": 71}}}, "shovels": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "The whole town rushed to the spot , or at least all the boys in it did , and a great force of men besides , with shovels and wheelbarrows , and bundles of brush and straw , and heavy logs , and heaped them into the crevasse , and piled earth on them", "context_len": 54}}}, "wheelbarrows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "The whole town rushed to the spot , or at least all the boys in it did , and a great force of men besides , with shovels and wheelbarrows , and bundles of brush and straw , and heavy logs , and heaped them into the crevasse , and piled earth on them", "context_len": 54}}}, "crevasse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "The whole town rushed to the spot , or at least all the boys in it did , and a great force of men besides , with shovels and wheelbarrows , and bundles of brush and straw , and heavy logs , and heaped them into the crevasse , and piled earth on them", "context_len": 54}}}, "local": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 31, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "politician": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The men threw off their coats and all joined in the work ; a great local politician led off in his shirt-sleeves ; and it was as if my boy should now see the Emperor of Germany in his shirt-sleeves pushing a wheelbarrow , so high above all other men had that exalted Whig always been to him", "context_len": 58}}}, "exalted": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 81, "context": "When the Vizier heard the Sultan 's words , his tongue was tied for despite and he was overcome with exceeding chagrin , forasmuch as the King had promised him that he would marry his daughter to his son ; so , after a little , he said to him , `` O King of the age , Thy Grace condescended to promise me [ 371 ] that the Lady Bedrulbudour should be my son 's ; wherefore it behoveth thine exalted highness appoint a delay of three months , [ 372 ] and God willing , my son 's present shall be greater than this", "context_len": 106}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "His buff coat and crimson sash were like those worn by the boy , and the velvet cap he carried in his hand left uncovered curls as brown ; but instead of the childish calm of the boy 's hazel eyes , the older man 's glance now flashed with the fire of an eagle , now glowed with the exalted enthusiasm of a poet", "context_len": 65}}}, "prosperity": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 87, "context": "But , when he saw that she heartened herself for that which he set forth to her of its virtues , he feared lest she should talk of this to the folk , so he said to her , `` O my mother , beware lest thou bespeak any of the lamp and its uses , for that this is our fortune ; be careful [ 345 ] and exceed not in speech thereof to any one , lest we lose it and lose this our present prosperity , for that it is from it", "context_len": 95}}}, "equals": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 55, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The Hitchiti verb equals the Creek verb in the abundance of inflectional forms", "context_len": 13}}}, "suspect": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 43, "context": "Nor was Nancy the only one who indulged in a little innocent vanity , for Joe , too , had been so fastidious in his choice of a red-and-blue spotted necktie and a walking-stick with an ivory handle that the children began to suspect the blacksmith 's rosy-cheeked daughter must be at the bottom of it ; and even David had taken a long-tailed coat and a beaver hat out of the retirement of some mysterious bandboxes and had been quite snappy and particular with Mrs", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "How came it that this girl had guessed his secret at once when most people passed him , taking him but for a sturdy beggar ? What made her suspect him of being a fugitive ? Was her offer of shelter but careless good nature , or a heroic endeavour to save a hunted man ? At any rate he had not fallen so low as to draw suspicion on a woman , and a young woman to boot , although she was plainly no nervous , fanciful , fine lady , but a bright , resolute , country girl , with good health and high spirits gleaming from every flash of her bright eyes , and every turn of her auburn head", "context_len": 123}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "By this time , Your Honor , I was absolutely convinced that I was on the right trail , and thenceforth I shadowed the suspect with the persistence of a bloodhound , never once letting him escape from beneath my hawklike eye", "context_len": 42}}}, "flopping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "I was out in my canoe Bluebird , and did n't I pick up that poor gull baby pretty quick as he lay flopping in the water , then I overhauled the boat , trounced the boy who was quite astonished to find that a girl had fists , swam to my canoe , paddled home , mothered Gullie who made his home with the hens , and revelled in bread and milk", "context_len": 73}}}, "measureless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Once , also , when the boys had gone into the vast woods of that measureless continent which they called the Island , for pawpaws or for hickory-nuts , or maybe buckeyes , they got lost ; and while they ran about in terror , they heard the distant lowing and bellowing of cattle", "context_len": 54}}}, "pawpaws": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "The herd came slowly up , and when they reached the boys ' refuge they behaved with all the fury that could have been expected -- they trampled and tossed the bags that held the pawpaws or buckeyes or hickory-nuts ; they gored the trees where the boys hung trembling ; they pawed and tossed the soft earth below ; and then they must have gone away , and given them up as hopeless", "context_len": 74}}}, "buckeyes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "The herd came slowly up , and when they reached the boys ' refuge they behaved with all the fury that could have been expected -- they trampled and tossed the bags that held the pawpaws or buckeyes or hickory-nuts ; they gored the trees where the boys hung trembling ; they pawed and tossed the soft earth below ; and then they must have gone away , and given them up as hopeless", "context_len": 74}}}, "lowing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 91, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Animals were lowing , bellowing , bleating , whinnying , or squeaking in every note of the octave ; cocks crew , ducks quacked , dogs barked , and men talked and shouted in Welsh and English till a chance comer might have imagined himself in pandemonium", "context_len": 47}}}, "bellowing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 51, "context": "Once , also , when the boys had gone into the vast woods of that measureless continent which they called the Island , for pawpaws or for hickory-nuts , or maybe buckeyes , they got lost ; and while they ran about in terror , they heard the distant lowing and bellowing of cattle", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 70, "context": "Suddenly away flash wig and beard and false-face , the pantaloons are stripped off with the same movement , the actor stoops for the reins lying on the horse 's neck , and James Rivers , the greatest three-horse rider in the world nimbly capers on the broad pad , and kisses his hand to the shouting and cheering spectators as he dashes from the ring past the braying and bellowing brass-band into the dressing-room !", "context_len": 76}}}, "herd": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 27, "context": "The enormous crowd who had followed us up from the beach were not permitted to enter the palisaded enclosure , which was strictly taboo to the common herd ; our party therefore now consisted solely of those who had brought me up the river , four individuals who had joined us outside the gate -- and whom I took to be officials of some sort -- and my unworthy self ; and , for my own part , I would very willingly have waived the distinction of forming one of the party", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I dare say the far forefathers of the race knew this too , when they first began to herd their kine in the birthplace of the Aryan peoples ; and perhaps they learned then that if you killed a snake early in the day its tail would live till sundown", "context_len": 50}}}, "favorably": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 63, "context": "Clavering , just as they were approaching the house , `` I see you are all a little nervous , thinking that a somewhat strange test will be applied to you to-day , but I assure you , my dears , that nothing of the kind is intended , and I beg of you , as you wish to impress your kind host favorably , to be at any cost natural and true to yourselves", "context_len": 75}}}, "bags": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 4, "context": "Two sets of mail bags were approaching each other from points two thousand miles apart , and there were times when this approach was at the astounding speed of forty and even fifty miles an hour ! The average daily rate was two hundred and fifty miles a day , but where everything was favorable , or when an express rider was fleeing from the vengeful red men , his pony struck a gait of twenty-five miles and maintained it , when an untrained horse would have dropped in his tracks", "context_len": 91}}}, "gored": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "The herd came slowly up , and when they reached the boys ' refuge they behaved with all the fury that could have been expected -- they trampled and tossed the bags that held the pawpaws or buckeyes or hickory-nuts ; they gored the trees where the boys hung trembling ; they pawed and tossed the soft earth below ; and then they must have gone away , and given them up as hopeless", "context_len": 74}}}, "pawed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 52, "context": "The herd came slowly up , and when they reached the boys ' refuge they behaved with all the fury that could have been expected -- they trampled and tossed the bags that held the pawpaws or buckeyes or hickory-nuts ; they gored the trees where the boys hung trembling ; they pawed and tossed the soft earth below ; and then they must have gone away , and given them up as hopeless", "context_len": 74}}}, "hopeless": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 73, "context": "The herd came slowly up , and when they reached the boys ' refuge they behaved with all the fury that could have been expected -- they trampled and tossed the bags that held the pawpaws or buckeyes or hickory-nuts ; they gored the trees where the boys hung trembling ; they pawed and tossed the soft earth below ; and then they must have gone away , and given them up as hopeless", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 89, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}}}, "stagnant": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` No , '' said Harry ; `` there is rather too much sunshine and sparkle about Maggie to think that she would become a stagnant pool , full of ugly tempers and hateful faults , like 'toads and things", "context_len": 40}}}, "scum": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "The natives were friendly to the whites and fond of visiting them ; the old chiefs were orderly men , desirous and active in restraining the young `` braves '' from the licentiousness which they had contracted through their intercourse with the scum of the white colonists", "context_len": 47}}}, "skilled": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 8, "context": "The son of one of the tavern-keepers was skilled in catching them , and I fancy supplied them to his father 's table ; the important fact was his taking them , which he did by baiting a cluster of three hooks with red flannel , and dropping them at the end of a fish-line before a frog", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "baiting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The son of one of the tavern-keepers was skilled in catching them , and I fancy supplied them to his father 's table ; the important fact was his taking them , which he did by baiting a cluster of three hooks with red flannel , and dropping them at the end of a fish-line before a frog", "context_len": 58}}}, "hooks": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 38, "context": "The market-house , where the German butchers in their white aprons were standing behind their meat-blocks , was lit up with candles in sconces , that shone upon festoons of sausage and cuts of steak dangling from the hooks behind them ; but without , all was in a vague obscurity , broken only by the lanterns in the farmers ' wagons", "context_len": 62}}}, "fated": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Here all things in their place remain , As all were ordered ages since , Come , care and pleasure , hope and pain , And bring the fated fairy prince", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 63, "context": "Why should he be in the jungle , he a white boy , whose rightful heritage of a life with his own kind had been denied him by a cruel fate ? Why did he not have a home like that of Frank Parkhurst , a father who was proud of him , a mother who loved him ? Why had he been fated to have his life placed every day in jeopardy ? He had been cheated of what belonged to him equally with every other boy of the white race", "context_len": 92}}}, "croaker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The fated croaker plunged at the brilliant bait , and was caught in the breast ; even as a small boy , my boy thought it a cruel sight", "context_len": 29}}}, "succession": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 67, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}}}, "droll": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 45, "context": "Stanton understood it no better than she did , perhaps not so well ; but he was very much amused ; and as he thought these young nieces and nephews of his were very wonderful little beings , he told Aunt Patty many of their droll sayings and doings , making himself so agreeable and entertaining , that by the time his sister came in , the old lady had almost forgotten that she had cause to be offended , and was not only quite ready to meet Mrs", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "And so , trying to reason herself into better humor , as she thought the matter over , she began to see its droll side ( for Aunt Patty had a quick sense of fun ) and to find some amusement mingling with her vexation at the singular conduct of the children", "context_len": 52}}}, "unerring": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Slacken yet more ; '' and before the pursuers could recover their confusion , a third fell , then a fourth , before the unerring shafts", "context_len": 27}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` He knew the meaning of the words , for Father Cuthbert had taught him some Latin , and the conviction flashed upon him that , sooner or later , all the wrong and evil about him would be righted by the power of a judge as omnipotent as unerring", "context_len": 50}}}, "docile": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "MY boy had not a great deal to do with schools after his docile childhood", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He became very tractable and docile , yielding a ready submission to his master in every thing", "context_len": 17}}}, "hardihood": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "He had never quite the hardihood to play truant , but he was subject to sudden attacks of sickness , which came on about school-time and went off towards the middle of the forenoon or afternoon in a very strange manner", "context_len": 41}}}, "truant": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "He had never quite the hardihood to play truant , but he was subject to sudden attacks of sickness , which came on about school-time and went off towards the middle of the forenoon or afternoon in a very strange manner", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 46, "context": "Round through the orchard she sped , bringing down a shower of cherry-blossom as she brushed against the low-growing trees , and greatly disturbing a robin , who was feeding a young family in a hole in the ivy , but without any sign of the truant", "context_len": 47}}}, "forenoon": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 34, "context": "Her punishment was that she `` should return to the Gaol from whence she came , and there remain until Saturday next between the hours of Eleven and Twelve of the Clock in the forenoon , at which time she was to be brought to the public Whipping-post , and there receive Twenty Lashes with a Cat-of-Nine-Tails from the hands of the Common Beadle on her naked back till the same shall be bloody , and then return to the said Gaol and remain until her fees be paid ! ''", "context_len": 91}}}, "fathers": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 96, "context": "Then he embraced Alaeddin and fell to kissing him and said to him , `` Excuse me , O my son , for that I was going [ 623 ] to bereave thee of thy life , through the wickedness of yonder accursed sorcerer who cast thee into this pit ; and indeed , O my son , I was excusable in that which I did with thee , inasmuch as I saw myself bereft of my daughter and mine only one , who is dearer to me than my kingdom , and thou knowest how fathers ' hearts yearn upon their children , more by token that I have but the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 114}}}, "outgrow": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 64, "context": "Now you can understand , I think , how I have wanted to keep that boy for a friend , together with the other children who have proved themselves friendly ; and so realizing they were all growing older each year , I have tried in the books I have written since then to keep pace with them , that they might not perhaps outgrow me for a little while yet", "context_len": 71}}}, "completed": {"freq": 78, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 78, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` Now Alaeddin had meanwhile gone in to his bride , the Lady Bedrulbudour , to acquaint her with the coming of her father the Sultan ; and when he returned , the Sultan said to him , `` O my son Alaeddin , what is the reason that the lattice [ -work ] of yonder oriel [ 513 ] is not completed ? '' `` O King of the Age , '' replied Alaeddin , `` by reason of the haste made with the bridal , the craftsmen might not avail to [ 514 ] finish it", "context_len": 98}}}, "recovery": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 34, "context": "Nicholas , you saved the life of the grand master ; and in the order of the day he issued on the morning we left , granting you three months ' leave for the recovery of your wounds , he took the opportunity of recording , in the name of the council and himself , their admiration for the services rendered by you during the siege , and his own gratitude for saving his life when he lay helpless and surrounded by the Moslems -- a testimony of which any knight of Christendom might well feel proud", "context_len": 97}}}, "unprofitable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "But all through the years when he lived in the Boy 's Town he had intervals of schooling , which broke in upon the swimming and the skating , of course , but were not altogether unpleasant or unprofitable", "context_len": 39}}}, "endear": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "After that he went to school in the basement of a church , where a number of boys and girls were taught by a master who knew how to endear study at least to my boy", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Indeed , for a woman grown , this little lady had more of the cunning tricks of childhood than anyone I had ever seen -- the cute little ways that endear children to those who love them", "context_len": 37}}}, "schoolroom": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 22, "context": "So until almost midnight the entire seminary , realizing somehow that all rules were laid aside , crowded round Ermengarde in the schoolroom and heard read and re-read the letter containing a story which was quite as wonderful as any Sara herself had ever invented , and which had the amazing charm of having happened to Sara herself and the mystic Indian gentleman in the very next house", "context_len": 68}}}, "cheeses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Other paragraphs in the report read thus : `` Received anonymously five large cheeses ; received a box of dessert knives and forks , a cruet , a silver soup ladle and a silver cup ; from Clifton , twelve tons house coals ; from Bedminster , a monster loaf , 200 lbs", "context_len": 53}}}, "drowsy": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "With the fact of this garden is mixed a sense of drowsy heat and summer light , and that is all , except the blackboard at the end of the room and a big girl doing sums at it ; and the wonder why the teacher smiled when he read in one of the girls ' compositions a phrase about forging puddings and pies ; my boy did not know what forging meant , so he must have been very young", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Grandma continued the singing , rocking Edna in her arms till the child fell fast asleep , the drowsy hum of the tea-kettle , hanging on the crane , helping to make a lullaby", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "She give me a big bottle full , and I drops three or four into her milk , and she 'll never wake now till mornin ' , and then she 'll be drowsy like and I can hush her off any minute", "context_len": 43}}}, "blackboard": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "With the fact of this garden is mixed a sense of drowsy heat and summer light , and that is all , except the blackboard at the end of the room and a big girl doing sums at it ; and the wonder why the teacher smiled when he read in one of the girls ' compositions a phrase about forging puddings and pies ; my boy did not know what forging meant , so he must have been very young", "context_len": 81}}}, "sums": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 36, "context": "With the fact of this garden is mixed a sense of drowsy heat and summer light , and that is all , except the blackboard at the end of the room and a big girl doing sums at it ; and the wonder why the teacher smiled when he read in one of the girls ' compositions a phrase about forging puddings and pies ; my boy did not know what forging meant , so he must have been very young", "context_len": 81}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It could not be that all the ten sums she had taken so much pains over were wrong", "context_len": 18}}}, "forging": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "The two dived as one figure ; both took the water clean and low , with no perceptible splash ; two heads appeared a moment later , forging ahead side by side ; a cheer from the cadets arose , that drowned , for a moment , the roars of the storm ; and the race was on", "context_len": 58}}}, "puddings": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 76, "context": "`` Oh , Bridget , such a kitchen and such a table , and no white cloth on it ! They , the servants did n't seem to care , and they were nice and clean at the Home , and you 're -- well , you have things as nice as in the dining room , and to have things shoveled out of the kettle on your plate or cold on a dish ! Sometimes puddings were good , and the creams and frozen things were clean and nice", "context_len": 90}}}, "geography": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` It 's not my own finding out , '' said Maggie ; `` the other day my geography lesson was about rivers , and mamma told me all that , and Bessie heard too ; so when we first saw this brook farther down the mountain , we remembered what mamma said , and Aunt May said a very nice thing", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I must rush off ; I want to have another look through those geography questions ; there is no saying what Sir John Wallis may question us about to-night , and if I do n't get into the lucky three who are to compete for the Scholarship , I believe I 'll go off my head", "context_len": 56}}}, "science": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 28, "context": "But she did not wish to vex their kind uncle , who was a very learned man , and was always thinking of collections and museums , and science and experiments ; and she did not like to tell him that she would rather her little ones should learn about butterflies from the book with its beautiful pictures , and from watching them flying about , and settling on the flowers in the fields ; and that she did not think it could be a nice play for children to catch and kill the pretty harmless creatures", "context_len": 97}}}, "valued": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 45, "context": "I ca n't bear to have the Romney picture go , either , because it 's just like Peggy ; it might have been painted from her ' -- the vision of the family portraits and the armour of her ancestors being turned over and valued by oily gentlemen of the Hebrew persuasion adding a double sting to the trial", "context_len": 60}}}, "multitude": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 110, "context": "So they fared on with him in great state , with four of the Viziers for his sword-bearers , whilst all the troops and people of the city , both townsfolk and strangers , walked in procession before him , carrying flambeaux and drums and flutes and instruments of mirth and music , till they brought him to his palace , when he alighted and entering , sat down , as did also the Viziers and Amirs who were in his company , whilst the mamelukes brought sherbets and sweetmeats [ 493 ] and gave all who were with him in the procession to drink , albeit they were a multitude of folk whose number might not be told", "context_len": 119}}}, "praise": {"freq": 83, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 116, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}, "VERB": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 64, "context": "upon the palace and its magnificence and talking of Alaeddin 's grace and comeliness and his bounty and munificence and the goodliness of his manners and disposition ; so [ 555 ] he went up to one of those who were extolling Alaeddin on this wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both", "context_len": 128}}}, "disgraced": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 31, "context": "Only one-half of the boys were implicated in that affair ; they had already been punished by the restrictions which had been placed upon them , and were to be further disgraced by the public reprimand which he intended to give them on the dismissal of the school ; and these culprits were probably dreading this or some other severe punishment which would be meted out to them by the report of their misconduct which would be sent home", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "I am not going back to Sunnyside , for they would not accept me ; but , at the same time , I do not feel at all a disgraced girl ; and I should like the Merrimans to be friends with me , and I should still like sometimes to see Jane and Laura Everett , and some of the other girls -- not Lucy Merriman , for she is not in the least to my taste ; but even she does not greatly matter now that I am no longer living in the house with her", "context_len": 98}}}, "arithmetic": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 45, "context": "Such very Spartan fare was the result of his lecture that he wisely fixed her a weekly allowance , and left her to manage as best she could upon it , and this plan answered far better , for she had a natural aptitude for arithmetic , and soon learned to make the various items fall into their proper places , balancing her little account-book in quite a professional manner", "context_len": 70}}}, "mediocrities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He sank into the common herd of mediocrities ; and then , when his family went to live in another part of the town , he began to go to another school", "context_len": 32}}}, "estranged": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "The place so familiar by day was estranged to his eyes in that pale light , and he was glad of old Tip 's company , for it seemed a time when there might very well be ghosts about", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "-- His officers became estranged", "context_len": 5}}}, "grade": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "So two of the Order , though but of the inferior grade , are in the plot ? the grand master said ; and several of the overseers ? One of the villains is , of course , the man you saw this Greek talking with", "context_len": 46}}}, "beginners": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "They were mere beginners at the great game of war , while others , younger in years , were already their seniors in the profession of arms", "context_len": 27}}}, "raw": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 22, "context": "The children had made a nest of grass for him inside a basket , and fed him on worms and scraps of raw meat until he was old enough to fly , when he would follow them everywhere about the farmyard and outbuildings , calling 'Jack , Jack ! ' which , with the mewing of a cat , the gobble of the old turkey-cock , and a close imitation of David 's winter cough , made up the extent of his accomplishments", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Sometimes he stopped to cook the meat ; at other times he ate it raw", "context_len": 15}}}, "enviably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "In the singing-class there was a boy who wore his hair so enviably long that he could toss it on his neck as he wheeled in the march of the class round the room ; his father kept a store and he brought candy to school", "context_len": 46}}}, "happiest": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 77, "context": "I suppose you want to tell me that Westenra here , my pretty little girl , who is going to give herself to me on the 1st of June next year , does not care for me , but she will care for me by-and-by , for my feeling is that love like mine must be returned in the long run , and if after a year she do n't tell your Grace that she is the happiest little wife in the length and breadth of England , I shall be greatly surprised", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "Glad to tell you anything , '' assured the professor , happiest always when he was talking , and willing to talk for hours with anyone who would listen to him", "context_len": 31}}}, "promoted": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 57, "context": "Three times have you earned special credit ; upon the first occasion , the grand master -- no mean judge of conduct and character -- deemed you worthy of secular knighthood , an honour which has not , in my memory , been bestowed at Rhodes upon any young knight ; on the second , you were promoted to the command of a galley , though never before has such a command been given to any , save knights of long experience ; and now , for the third time , the councillors of one of the greatest of Italian cities are about to do you honour", "context_len": 107}}}, "bellows": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "They managed to construct a broom from some of the longest pieces of heather , and swept the crumbs neatly out at the front-door ; they hung up the frying-pan , the kettle , and the bellows in their accustomed places , and stacked the cups and plates in the old box which served as a cupboard", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "burning blow pigskin bellows blew blowing", "context_len": 6}}}, "chiefly": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 69, "context": "He is glad to have had a boyhood fully rounded out with all a boy 's interests and pleasures , and he is glad that his lines were cast in the Boy 's Town ; but he knows , or believes he knows , that whatever is good in him now came from what was good in him then ; and he is sure that the town was delightful chiefly because his home in it was happy", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "The first part of the entertainment was to be chiefly musical , so the proceedings began with a waltz by Lilian , who was always the Rector 's mainstay in making up a programme , and had helped at most of his Sunday-school concerts since she was ten years old", "context_len": 50}}}, "chemical": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "The odor grew more pronounced , for some sulphur springs are so strongly impregnated with that chemical in solution that the smell carries for miles , especially on a windy day", "context_len": 31}}}, "terrors": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 14, "context": "In fact , as I have said , he dwelt in a world of terrors ; and I doubt if some of the big boys who were taken there to be whipped underwent so much as he in being merely taken to the place where they had been whipped", "context_len": 49}}}, "underwent": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "In fact , as I have said , he dwelt in a world of terrors ; and I doubt if some of the big boys who were taken there to be whipped underwent so much as he in being merely taken to the place where they had been whipped", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "But the most awful calamity it ever underwent was its destruction in the first great Danish invasion , in the early days of King Alfred , when it was literally levelled with the ground , only , however , to arise in greater magnificence when the storm had passed away", "context_len": 50}}}, "tamely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "He knew what belonged to him as a small boy of honor , and one thing was , not to be tamely put back from a higher to a lower place in his studies", "context_len": 34}}}, "studies": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 24, "context": "Ever since the adventure with the pirates , the knight had exhibited an almost fatherly interest in him ; had encouraged him in his studies , ridden with him on such occasions as he had permitted himself a short holiday , and had , whenever they were together , related to him stories of war , sieges , battles , and escapes , from which he thought the young knight might gain lessons for his future guidance", "context_len": 77}}}, "dismissed": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 24, "context": "The other boys would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart", "context_len": 128}}}, "slate": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 43, "context": "The other boys would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart", "context_len": 128}}}, "inkstand": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "The other boys would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart", "context_len": 128}}}, "defiant": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 50, "context": "The other boys would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "Seabrooke went about the business of the day with all his accustomed regularity and precision , but with a sort of defiant and I-am-going-to-stick-to-it air about him which in itself incited the other boys to covert thrusts and innuendoes tending to throw distrust upon his version of the story and to make known their thorough sympathy with Percy , not only for his loss , but also for the aspersions cast upon him by the young pupil-teacher", "context_len": 77}}}, "comport": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 94, "context": "would have a right to mock you if you did not do it ; and as soon as the class was dismissed you went to your desk as haughtily as you could , and began putting your books and your slate and your inkstand together , with defiant glances at the teacher ; and then when twelve o'clock came , or four o'clock , and the school was let out , you tucked the bundle under your arm and marched out of the room , with as much majesty as could be made to comport with a chip hat and bare feet ; and as you passed the teacher you gave a twist of the head that was meant to carry dismay to the heart of your enemy", "context_len": 128}}}, "particulars": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 68, "context": "Of course they were all exceedingly anxious to hear the story of what had befallen me since the Dolphin and the Eros had parted company ; but I steadfastly refused to tell them anything beyond the bare fact that the Dolphin had gone down with all hands , explaining that the skipper had invited me to dine with him that day , and that they would learn all particulars then , as I gathered that it was his intention to invite them all to meet me", "context_len": 86}}}, "schoolboys": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "The girls cheered as lustily as schoolboys , the band in the corner burst forth with the gay strains of `` See the Conquering Hero Comes , '' and after a brief signal from Sir John there was suddenly heard outside the report of a small cannon , which was the intimation that the bonfires were to be lit", "context_len": 59}}}, "stalls": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 103, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}}}, "grisly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "My boy had a vision of her hanging from it ; but this was probably one of those grisly fancies that were always haunting him , and no fact at all", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Bomba has his hand on him now , and he reached down and touched the grisly coils", "context_len": 17}}}, "teamsters": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "Next he was ordered to run back to the village for some article that had been forgotten , next to carry water to the teamsters , then to run with messages from one person to another until he was so tired , he thoroughly envied George and Gus their comfortable seats in one of the baggage wagons , and was delighted at last to hear the signal to halt", "context_len": 69}}}, "beaux": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 90, "context": "Some he said were telling how a monster rattlesnake had come to visit them without any invitation , and that the only food he would eat , was the youngest and fattest of their families ; and that their constant intruders , the owls , had the same carnivorous tastes , besides which they rendered themselves particularly disagreeable , by standing in the doors and staring at every dog that went by , and even preventing the entrance of visitors , to the great distress of all the belles and beaux in town", "context_len": 93}}}, "disentangle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "My boy did not know what that meant ; in his own mind he could not disentangle the idea of bows from the idea of arrows ; but he was in love with the girl who caught her hand on the meat-hook , and secretly suffered much on account of her", "context_len": 51}}}, "arrows": {"freq": 128, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 122, "word_index": 7, "context": "As Ama , taking her bow and arrows , tripped lightly away toward the forest , I proceeded to shin up the tree , and presently , after some labour , found myself among its topmost branches , which towered high above those of all the other trees in the neighbourhood , and -- it being by this time broad daylight -- obtained a magnificent view of the surrounding country , extending for , as I estimated , some forty miles toward the south and east , while toward the north and west the view was shut off by high hills , through which the river wound its way", "context_len": 109}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "John , who knew Charlie was too modest to do it himself , showed him the lookout in the top of the tree , the house , and all that was in it , and also told him how Charlie beat them firing at a mark , though they had guns , and he a bow and arrows ; and showed him the bullet-holes and arrow-marks in the target", "context_len": 69}}}, "flushes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "She had black eyes , and her name long seemed to him the most beautiful name for a girl ; he said it to himself with flushes from his ridiculous little heart", "context_len": 32}}}, "intimacy": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 53, "context": "Yet I scarcely knew precisely what it was that I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether", "context_len": 128}}}, "gulf": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "It felt a very insecure bridge to poor Peggy , as she crept over on her hands and knees , trying not to look down into the dark gulf below , so frail and insecure that she shuddered for Bobby , who seemed so unnerved that she scarcely dared allow him to make the trial", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "I am an Historian , for I have been discussing old dates ; a Merchant , for I add plum to plum ; a Lawyer , for I have opened many a case ; a Lord Mayor , for the mace is before me ; and a Navigator , for I am led to seize and gulf ! What if I were to stay here altogether , or set up a new company with my fair hostesses ? Miss Ratty is cut out for a tragedy Queen", "context_len": 87}}}, "bridged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "There could have been little in common between her and the hard , domineering father until a great grief bridged the gulf that had grown between them", "context_len": 27}}}, "balance": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "A very pretty tail was made by tying twists of paper across a string a foot apart , till there were enough to balance the kite ; but this sort of tail was apt to get tangled , and the best tail was made of a long streamer of cotton rags , with a gay tuft of dog-fennel at the end", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 57, "context": "To very many of them death must have been welcome , and if we had not attacked them as we did , and they had sailed for Italy , hundreds , if not thousands , of Christians would have been killed , and as many more carried away into captivity ; so , you see , the balance is all in favour of the course we adopted", "context_len": 67}}}, "philosophized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The boy who philosophized the matter in this way was a merry , unlucky fellow , who fully tested the advantages and disadvantages of the free-school system", "context_len": 27}}}, "unlucky": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 88, "context": "It was only before dinner I said to Marion , 'You would n't like to be in Miss Wickham 's shoes to-night , would you , Marion ? You would n't like to be wearing the willow , would you , my girl ? ' And she said no , she would n't , but then she added , 'With my soul full of Art , mother , I always can have my resources , ' and that is where Marion believes , that if she were so unlucky as to be crossed in love , she would have the advantage of you , Miss Wickham , for you have plainly said that you have no soul for h'Art", "context_len": 119}}}, "tested": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 24, "context": "Joseph Smith must be immediately `` boosted '' up and balanced upon the cadet 's outstretched hand , there to be shaken and otherwise tested , while the man below made audible calculations by means of trigonometrical formulas as to what was his actual weight", "context_len": 45}}}, "disadvantages": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "The air was comparatively cool , a pleasing relief from the hot sunshine in the open ; but before long the disadvantages of the tunnel made themselves apparent", "context_len": 28}}}, "kindest": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 82, "context": "To his surprise some of the crew gave him a friendly greeting ; he was unable to understand a manner so at variance with their hatred to the Christians , until one of them said to him in a mixture of Greek and Italian , We have heard from our countrymen who were in the boat with you , that they received much kindness at your hands , and that of all the Christians they had served under , you were the kindest master", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Tucker retains an honoured place in memories of these and later days as the kindest and most liberal of old aunts , -- so she desired me to designate her , and at once adopted me into her very large circle of favoured nephews and nieces , -- the inexhaustible source of varied goodnesses , especially such as were of the most approved edible nature", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "To kindest pity now inclin 'd , See these children wish to give A trifle to the poor and blind , Thus assisting him to live", "context_len": 26}}}, "frequent": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 57, "word_index": 78, "context": "seen service under Prince Rupert ; Arthur Conolly , an Irish veteran who had served in the Low Countries , and who had come over from Chichester for the occasion ; Arthur Lewis , a gentleman of Bramshott ; Percy Young , an officer of the navy , who in his earlier days had lost a leg in the action of La Rochelle ; Herbert Collings , a master mariner of Gosport , who used to be a frequent visitor at our house , and who greatly interested me with the account of his adventures off the coast of Barbary ; and Giles Perrin , the landlord of the `` Flying Bull '' , who modestly seated himself on a stool in a remote corner of the room", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The luckless victims begin after breakfast , and at regular ( and frequent ) periods until night are turned out under the charge of some irascible yearling to be taught all manner of military maneuvers -- setting up drill , how to stand , to face , and , in fact , how to walk", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Later on they crossed the Mississippi to its western side , and are mentioned as wanderers on Bayou Crocodile and its environs ( 1806 ) , which they frequent even now , and on the Lake of Avoyelles", "context_len": 38}}}, "thinly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "Sometimes he would not cry ; but when he was caught in one of his frequent offences and called up before the teacher 's desk in the face of the whole school , and whipped over his thinly jacketed shoulders , he would take it without wincing , and go smiling to his seat , and perhaps be called back and whipped more for smiling", "context_len": 65}}}, "jacketed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Sometimes he would not cry ; but when he was caught in one of his frequent offences and called up before the teacher 's desk in the face of the whole school , and whipped over his thinly jacketed shoulders , he would take it without wincing , and go smiling to his seat , and perhaps be called back and whipped more for smiling", "context_len": 65}}}, "wincing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "Sometimes he would not cry ; but when he was caught in one of his frequent offences and called up before the teacher 's desk in the face of the whole school , and whipped over his thinly jacketed shoulders , he would take it without wincing , and go smiling to his seat , and perhaps be called back and whipped more for smiling", "context_len": 65}}}, "depraving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "He said he must teach them not to forget , and their punishment began ; surely the most hideous and depraving sight , except a hanging , that could be offered to children 's eyes", "context_len": 35}}}, "howled": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 57, "context": "Baulked of their prey , the mob still surged round us , and with a shout of : `` Let \u2019 em have the cat ! '' a great black object was sent flying towards us , and , striking my horse on the crupper , resolved itself into an enormous black cat , that spat and howled , digging its claws into the horse \u2019 s hide , and arching its back like the demon cat that is the reputed companion of every witch", "context_len": 85}}}, "rained": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 55, "context": "Several Danes strove to pass the breach ; one was already through , and Edmund attacked him ; meanwhile two others had crept through , but were cut off from their fellows , for the English rallied in front and presented an impenetrable barrier with their spears , while from the windows above the arrows rained upon the assailants", "context_len": 59}}}, "imploring": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 60, "context": "They first planted a stout broomstick in the ground , and fixed the turnip head firmly upon the top of it ; two branches of trees tied on like cross-bars made a pair of shoulders and arms , and when covered with a sheet stolen from the linen-cupboard , it appeared as if the white figure were stretching out its imploring hands in a vain appeal for mercy", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "He ran , and my boy followed with the other boys after him , till they overtook the culprit and brought him to bay against a high board fence ; and there my boy struck him in his imploring face", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Jane , listening , could only press those slender , clinging fingers in speechless comfort , and look up silently into the imploring eyes of her child -- eyes filled with tears and love", "context_len": 34}}}, "promising": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "One of them howled and shrieked , and leaped and danced , catching his back , his arms , his legs , as the strokes rained upon him , imploring , promising , and getting away at last with a wild effort to rub himself all over all at once", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 83, "context": "It was exactly as Edna had predicted ; Miss Eloise entered into the plan with the greatest eagerness , and when Ben had opened up his plot to her and had showed her how he had planned the scenes she said she would take a few minutes to think it over and then she thought she could give him some of the needed dialogue , and before they left Ben had taken down as much as was necessary for this first time , promising to come back for the rest", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 47, "context": "For reasons of his own the Rector kept Peggy late that evening , and they made a little tour of the garden , selecting what they called their prize plants , putting indiarubber bands round carnation-buds to keep them from bursting , and tying up the most promising stocks and asters with a scrupulous care , working until the light had almost failed and the sky stood out yellow against the outline of the cypress-trees", "context_len": 75}}}, "exasperated": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 3, "context": "He had been exasperated to a great degree by Flagg 's endeavor to drug him on the night of the expedition to Rice 's , and that with good reason ; and now his suspicions , nay , more than suspicions aroused that he was trying to make it appear that he , Seabrooke , had wrongfully kept Percy 's money and then pretended that the latter had taken it from him by stealth , enraged him beyond bounds", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Oh , you let me alone ! '' exclaimed the exasperated one", "context_len": 13}}}, "blows": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 72, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "It is a cloudless morning , the white sands of the South-shore beaches shine like silver in the sunlight , and the fresh sea breeze that is stirring holds its own the whole length of Long Island , and blows its purifying way into every street and alley of the vast city that lies at its farther end", "context_len": 58}}}, "contrived": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 115, "context": "`` And when , '' [ quoth she ] ; `` I saw this [ seeming ] lack of wit in him , I fell to laughing at him , unknowing his perfidy and his intent ; so I took an old lamp that was in my husband 's pavilion and sent it by the eunuch , who exchanged it with him for a new lamp ; and next day , O my father , at daybreak , we found ourselves in Africa , with the palace and all that was therein ; and I knew not the properties of the lamp which I had exchanged , till my husband Alaeddin came to us and contrived against the Maugrabin a device whereby he delivered us from him", "context_len": 127}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The consultation of the doctors had been so contrived that the principal boarders had been out when it took place , therefore I knew that Mrs", "context_len": 26}}}, "executioner": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 77, "context": "`` It 's hard for most to follow God in such a dispensation as this , and yet my Lord and Master is as sweet and glorious to me now as He was in the time of my greatest prosperity ; '' and then , embracing his friends in farewell , he committed his spirit into the hands of God and was , the bystanders declared , `` not so much thrown off the ladder by the executioner , but went readily off himself", "context_len": 84}}}, "inflicting": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 21, "context": "The defence , though even more obstinate and desperate than the first , was attended with less success , for after inflicting enormous losses upon the great army , commanded by the Sultan Solyman himself , the town was forced to yield ; for although the Grand Master L'Isle Adam , and most of his knights , would have preferred to bury themselves beneath the ruins rather than yield , they were deterred from doing so , by the knowledge that it would have entailed the massacre of the whole of the inhabitants , who had throughout the siege fought valiantly in the defence of the town", "context_len": 107}}}, "fiend": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "The bos \u2019 n , who was standing close to me , hastened to where the missile lay , and lifting it up he exclaimed : `` That \u2019 s where they have us ! \u2019 Tis a chain shot -- a new invention of that stubborn fiend de Wit ! ''", "context_len": 52}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 70, "context": "`` Oh , this was to have been my wedding day , my wedding day ! '' and he clasped his hands in agony ; then the thought of his master -- his slain lord -- returned , and he cried , `` O Edmund ! my master , my dear master , so good , so gentle , yet so brave ; who else could slay him ? what fiend else than Edric , the murderer Edric ? That they should think I , or any one else than Edric , could have done such a deed , such an evil deed ! ''", "context_len": 105}}}, "shrink": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 58, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "It has not taken you very long , Stone , he said , in a manner that made Ben shrink and shiver , to demonstrate beyond question that what Mr", "context_len": 30}}}, "blister": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Anyway , he did not blister your hand with a ruler , as some teachers did , or make you stand bent forward from the middle , with your head hanging down , so that the blood all ran into it", "context_len": 41}}}, "advances": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 43, "context": "Perceiving a serving maid at one of the windows , one of the soldiers began to make love to her in dumb show , kissing his hand and waving his iron headpiece to the damsel , who seemed nothing loath to accept his advances", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "In this way we are to go out to meet Alexander as he advances -- and all will be well", "context_len": 20}}}, "distinction": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Every expense connected with the school was to be met by this wonderful purse of money ; she was to be educated and called the Cherry Court Scholarship girl , and it was to be a wonderfully proud distinction , I can tell you , and at the end of the year Sir John Wallis was to give another purse of money , and at the end of that year another purse of money , so that the lucky girl who won the Scholarship was to be educated free of expense for three whole years", "context_len": 96}}}, "declamation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Under him my boy made great advances in reading and writing , and he won some distinction in declamation ; but the old difficulties with the arithmetic remained", "context_len": 28}}}, "ease": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 29, "context": "He failed to make anything out of the parts of speech in his grammar ; but one afternoon , while he sat in his stocking feet , trying to ease the chilblains which every boy used to have from his snow-soaked boots , before the days of india-rubbers , he found something in the back of his grammar which made him forget all about the pain", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 100, "context": "the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to demand her of the Sultan her father in the way of law and righteousness", "context_len": 128}}}, "chilblains": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "He failed to make anything out of the parts of speech in his grammar ; but one afternoon , while he sat in his stocking feet , trying to ease the chilblains which every boy used to have from his snow-soaked boots , before the days of india-rubbers , he found something in the back of his grammar which made him forget all about the pain", "context_len": 66}}}, "verses": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 6, "context": "There ought to have been more verses , but at this point Father had unfortunately got hold of the paper , and persisted in treating the poem in such a comic light that the indignant authoress had never found the heart to finish it , though the fragment was considered very talented by Aunt Helen , and carefully put away in an old work-box , with the first specimens of Peggy 's handwriting and one of Bobby 's little baby-curls", "context_len": 80}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "xxiii , verses 99 , l00 , of the Koran , `` Say , 'Lord , I take refuge in Thee from the suggestions of the devils , and I take refuge in thee , Lord , that ( i", "context_len": 40}}}, "stanzas": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "This was a part called Prosody , and it told how to make verses ; explained the feet , the accents , the stanzas -- everything that had puzzled him in his attempts to imitate the poems he had heard his father read aloud", "context_len": 44}}}, "poems": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 79, "context": "Harold 's principal achievement lay in The Walrus and the Carpenter , and Marie-Celeste 's in the recitation of Jabberwocky from Through the Looking-Glass ; for not only was she able to slip its almost unpronounceable words quite easily from her tongue , but she remembered the explanation of them given by Humpty Dumpty , when Alice appeals to him a little later on in the story , and he modestly informs her that he can explain all the poems that ever were invented , and a good many beside that have n't been invented just yet", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "She sang a few hymns she knew , she said over several , little poems she had learned and spelled a few words", "context_len": 23}}}, "charily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He conceived of its reasons at the first reading , so that I suppose nature had not dealt so charily with him concerning the rules of prosody as the rules of arithmetic ; and he lost no time in applying them in a poem of his own", "context_len": 47}}}, "prosody": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Heaven knows who wrote it or published it ; his father bought it with a number of other books at an auction , and the boy , who had about that time discovered the chapter on prosody in the back part of his grammar , made poems from it for years , and appeared in many transfigurations , as this and that god and demigod and hero upon imagined occasions in the Boy 's Town , to the fancied admiration of all the other fellows", "context_len": 85}}}, "applying": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 39, "context": "He conceived of its reasons at the first reading , so that I suppose nature had not dealt so charily with him concerning the rules of prosody as the rules of arithmetic ; and he lost no time in applying them in a poem of his own", "context_len": 47}}}, "visibly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "The afternoon air was heavy with the heat that quivered visibly above the great cast-iron wood stove in the centre of the schoolroom ; the boys drowsed in their seats , or hummed sleepily over their lessons ; the chilblains gnawed away at the poet 's feet , but heaven had opened to him , and he was rapt far from all the world of sense", "context_len": 66}}}, "drowsed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The afternoon air was heavy with the heat that quivered visibly above the great cast-iron wood stove in the centre of the schoolroom ; the boys drowsed in their seats , or hummed sleepily over their lessons ; the chilblains gnawed away at the poet 's feet , but heaven had opened to him , and he was rapt far from all the world of sense", "context_len": 66}}}, "seats": {"freq": 92, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 34, "context": "Here was a little cleared space among the trees , and a broad flat stone on which the fire was to be built ; while two or three great trunks and stumps formed excellent seats , -- excellent , that is to say , for those people who had both their limbs left to them , -- but the Colonel did not find them quite so comfortable ; so Starr slung a camp chair over his arm to have it ready for his master 's use when he should come", "context_len": 90}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Then she seats herself , and , clasping two fair little hands round one knee , just waits , letting eyes rove where they will and thoughts follow", "context_len": 28}}}, "gnawed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "The afternoon air was heavy with the heat that quivered visibly above the great cast-iron wood stove in the centre of the schoolroom ; the boys drowsed in their seats , or hummed sleepily over their lessons ; the chilblains gnawed away at the poet 's feet , but heaven had opened to him , and he was rapt far from all the world of sense", "context_len": 66}}}, "rapt": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 58, "context": "The afternoon air was heavy with the heat that quivered visibly above the great cast-iron wood stove in the centre of the schoolroom ; the boys drowsed in their seats , or hummed sleepily over their lessons ; the chilblains gnawed away at the poet 's feet , but heaven had opened to him , and he was rapt far from all the world of sense", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He , indeed , gave her and her sister his heart , but his mind was already gone , rapt from him by the adorable pirate who fought a losing fight with broadswords , two up and two down -- click-click , click-click -- and died all over the deck of the pirate ship in the opening piece", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "They fascinated her with their crude drawing and colours still vivid and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined", "context_len": 128}}}, "wield": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 50, "context": "The strong sentiment of love and devotion with which he inspired all around him at the commencement of his career , was now gone , and his generals and officers were continually planning schemes to depose him from the power which he seemed no longer to have the energy to wield ; or , at least , Alexander was continually suspecting that such plans were formed , and he was kept in a continual state of uneasiness and anxiety in discovering and punishing them", "context_len": 84}}}, "unconscious": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 8, "context": "Nothing could have been prettier than the wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he", "context_len": 128}}}, "imitation": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 49, "context": "He had purloined David 's scarf from the saddle-room , and dropped it into the horse-trough , had filled Bobby 's hat with pebbles , and devoured the queen-cakes which Lilian had placed on the kitchen window-sill to cool ; he had snatched Joe 's breast-pin -- a glittering imitation diamond -- from his Sunday tie , under the very nose of that injured youth , and had stolen so many small articles that if anything were missing the children would have a grand search for Master Jack 's hiding-place , and would generally turn out the lost treasure from among an odd collection of trifles -- scraps of bright-coloured rags , bits of broken glass , hairpins , pen-nibs , pencil-ends , together with pieces of bread", "context_len": 128}}}, "dismays": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "But here he met with that sort of disappointment which more than any other dismays and baffles authorship ; a difference in the point of view", "context_len": 26}}}, "baffles": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "But here he met with that sort of disappointment which more than any other dismays and baffles authorship ; a difference in the point of view", "context_len": 26}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Donald Pierce is sent to solve the mystery of his father \u2019 s disappearance , into the unmapped barrens where King Stively weaves his web of wickedness , and rules a territory the size of a small empire with a ruthlessness and cunning that baffles the best of the Mounted Police", "context_len": 51}}}, "sympathized": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "His father said the verses were well made , and he sympathized with him in his delight at having found out the way to make them , though he was not so much astonished as the boy that such a science as prosody should exist", "context_len": 45}}}, "exist": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 9, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Such proceedings as these could not of course long exist without the fame of them reaching the ears of the king , and I had only given some three or four performances when I was summoned to entertain his Majesty and his household , which I did in the great square before the palace , my audience numbering quite two thousand ; Banda and his numerous family being seated in a huge semicircle -- of which I was the centre -- in front of the palace , while the rest of the audience filled the remaining portion of the square", "context_len": 100}}}, "despaired": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The boy promised that he would try to realize heaven as a state ; but at the bottom of his heart he despaired of getting that idea into poetry", "context_len": 29}}}, "revered": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "But the dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "We have , however , only been allowed one very brief glimpse and kiss of the revered remains", "context_len": 18}}}, "seer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He revered Swedenborg ; he had a religious awe of the seer 's lithograph portrait in a full-bottom wig which hung in the front-room , but he did not see how even Swedenborg could have helped calling heaven a place if he had been making poetry", "context_len": 46}}}, "lithograph": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He revered Swedenborg ; he had a religious awe of the seer 's lithograph portrait in a full-bottom wig which hung in the front-room , but he did not see how even Swedenborg could have helped calling heaven a place if he had been making poetry", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` Come on , fellows , let 's get some of our stuff out ! I want my football pictures , '' and with that Sid rushed to the wall and yanked down the only bit of ornamentation he cared for -- a lithograph of a Rugby scrimmage", "context_len": 48}}}, "tyrannical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "It appeared to them that several of his acts were distinctly tyrannical , and were encroachments upon rights of theirs which the other teacher , with all his severity , had respected", "context_len": 32}}}, "encroachments": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 61, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "despotic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "My boy was inspired by the common mood to write a tragedy which had the despotic behavior of the new teacher for its subject , and which was intended to be represented by the boys in the hayloft of a boy whose father had a stable without any horse in it", "context_len": 51}}}, "hayloft": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "My boy was inspired by the common mood to write a tragedy which had the despotic behavior of the new teacher for its subject , and which was intended to be represented by the boys in the hayloft of a boy whose father had a stable without any horse in it", "context_len": 51}}}, "dagger": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 83, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}}}, "vitals": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 88, "context": "Accordingly , when she came up , he saw her and viewed her well , beyond his wish ; and from that time till now , O King of the Age , life hath not been pleasant to him [ 366 ] and he will e'en have me seek her of Thy Grace , [ 367 ] so thou mayst marry her with him , and I can not do away this conceit from his wit , for that the love of her hath gotten possession of his vitals , so that he saith to me , 'Know , O mother mine , that , except I attain my desire , assuredly I am a dead man", "context_len": 117}}}, "invented": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 59, "context": "At the same time , the boys were to be dressed as Roman conspirators , and one of them was to give the teacher a petition to read , while another plunged a dagger into his vitals , and still another shouted , `` Strike , Stephanos , strike ! '' It seemed to my boy that he had invented a situation which he had lifted almost bodily out of Goldsmith 's history ; and he did not feel that his lines ,", "context_len": 83}}}, "flee": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 45, "context": "Do you mind , Dick , '' she went on , in a calmer tone , as her memory wandered back to happier days , `` do you mind how I foresaw these evil times were at hand , and how I entreated him to flee ? Do you mind , last spring , when that letter came from New England from excellent Master Perrient , how I prayed him to hearken to it ? ''", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "modelled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "were too closely modelled upon Scott 's lines ,", "context_len": 9}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "No wonder that the boy saw with his eyes and heard with his ears and modelled himself as nearly as he could on the ideals of his hero ; and when Colonel Harrison signed the warrant for the king 's execution , the boy was as convinced a regicide as any of the judges whose names were written beside that of Harrison on the fatal parchment", "context_len": 66}}}, "realizing": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 12, "context": "Why do n't the fellow come back ? he muttered angrily , realizing , as he heard the clock strike half-past twelve , that he had been actually inconvenienced for a whole half hour ; and shortly after the fellow did come back , the dearest little fellow in the world too , by the way , and shut to the big front door and locked it as he had done night after night during the last two years , while Ted was up at Oxford , and he had been living alone with the servants in the pretty little home there at Windsor", "context_len": 104}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Board and expenses -- that is all , sir , and so the serious talk ended with this bit of pleasantry ; and Ted realizing that he had not been disappointed in feeling that Donald would somehow be able to help him , found himself entering into the new scheme with rather more hope than circumstances would seem to justify", "context_len": 60}}}, "persecution": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 102, "context": "dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of the sunset", "context_len": 128}}}, "usurper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He must have come next after that usurper , but at any rate the word had been passed round , even before school took up the first morning he began , that he was to be resisted to the death", "context_len": 40}}}, "resisted": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 66, "context": "Appalled by the disaster that had befallen them , by the terrible effect of the broadsides , poured in at a few yards ' distance , and by the sight of so many of the dreaded warriors of the Cross , some of the corsairs threw down their arms and flung themselves on the deck or into the hold , crying for quarter ; those who resisted fell either under the swords of the knights , the vengeful axes of the late captives , or the pikes of the Sards ; but the great bulk , leaping from the bow or stern , swam ashore", "context_len": 105}}}, "friendliest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "He could not have had any notion of what was in the air , for in that opening speech to the school which a new teacher always used to make , he talked to the boys in the friendliest manner , and with more sense and reason than they could feel , though I hope they felt some secret shame for the way they meant to behave", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It was good of Grico to keep these for Bomba till he should come back again , said the boy in the friendliest of tones , as he stowed away the treasures in his pouch", "context_len": 35}}}, "scuffling": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 14, "context": "It rolled over on its side and lay there , and there was a scuffling and a couple of black legs were to be seen kicking themselves loose , and a pair of black arms twisting themselves from under it , and a little black wrinkled face and head with cunning , bright eyes pushed themselves out , and the minute Barty saw them he shouted aloud with glee :", "context_len": 70}}}, "slates": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "The truth was , however , that it was a sound which had called her back -- a real sound -- the click of the skylight as it fell in closing after a lithe white figure which slipped through it and crouched down close by upon the slates of the roof -- just near enough to see what happened in the attic , but not near enough to be seen", "context_len": 70}}}, "wads": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Why in the name of old King Log did my father send me to spend the summer in this eerie place ? Is there no country air south of these wads of Canadian forest ? ''", "context_len": 37}}}, "babel": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "As they did so , a babel of shouts and cries rose from the dark mass of ships , which swelled into a tumult of alarm as on Ralph 's order , Throw your torches into the straw ! a flash of flame leapt up from each boat", "context_len": 48}}}, "reasoning": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` In spite of all her wisdom , this reasoning of Gervassen convinced Alstarnah , who soon after stood up before all the people and bade them farewell , saying that she was going to dwell in the wigwam of the mighty chieftain , Gervassen", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "Finally Ted , who finds himself simply reasoning in a circle , decides to lay the whole matter before Donald ; for Donald , boy that he is , has opinions of his own which he does not fear to express , and , what is more , Ted in desperation feels that he simply must turn to somebody", "context_len": 59}}}, "titter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` But you were n't at all likely to be in his place or any other where arrows were flying , '' interrupted Gus , with a laugh , which quickly subsided into a smothered titter as George looked at him , with the remark : `` You had better mind your bones", "context_len": 53}}}, "hookey": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "His despair of now hiding the fact that he had been playing hookey was his own affair , but they reasoned with him that the offence would be overlooked in the anxiety which his disaster must arouse", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Why , that Patsy 's a reg'lar bum ! He 's called me names -- he plays hookey too , and he tried to trip me up and I give him a left-hander , and he called me a stinking pup and ever so many nasty names and then we went at it", "context_len": 54}}}, "savages": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 70, "context": "dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of the sunset", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": ", and in Margry V , 442 , where P\u00e9nicaut alleges to have studied the languages of the Louisiana savages pretty thoroughly for five years , `` surtout le Mobilien , qui est le principal et qu'on entend par toutes les nations", "context_len": 42}}}, "penknife": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "'Now , put your foot down upon that , and I will draw a line round it ; then , if I cut it out with my penknife it will make quite a good sole -- enough to save you from the stones , at any rate", "context_len": 47}}}, "reproof": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 1, "context": "This reproof had greatly subdued Robert Rivers ; and as he felt that any display of his jealousy of Gervaise would be resented by the other knights , and might result in serious consequences to himself , he abstained from any exhibition of it when they returned to the auberge , although he could not bring himself to join in the congratulations offered to them", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 87, "context": "The melancholy faces of the two little girls grieved their older friends ; and , besides , it was sad to know that children like Lem and Dolly should take delight in such wicked , wanton mischief , and to know that there seemed to be no way to do them good ; since they only came near those who were weaker and younger than themselves to do them harm , and ran from those who were older and wiser , in fear of the punishment and reproof their wickedness deserved", "context_len": 91}}}, "dwindling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "This fired my boy 's wicked little heart with furious resentment ; he gathered up his books after school , and took them home ; a good many other boys had done it , and the school was dwindling", "context_len": 39}}}, "thrill": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 44, "context": "I dare say it was of the size of the ordinary city omnibus , but it looked as large to the boys then as a Pullman car would look to a boy now ; and they assembled for its arrivals and departures with a thrill of civic pride such as hardly any other fact of the place could impart", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Don \u2019 t try to thrill \u2019 em by dropping an egg on the town pump , said Hap Newton", "context_len": 20}}}, "escapade": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 98, "context": "and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say afterwards upon the danger of such heedless practical jokes", "context_len": 128}}}, "snivelling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "But the teacher took the hand of the snivelling wretch , and called him affectionately by name , and said they would try to get along without that , and sent him to his seat forgiven", "context_len": 36}}}, "barbarous": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "republic": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 110, "context": "him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought , of God , why did He not own us ? What were General Harrison 's dreams of a pure republic , but vanity ? Who can say if his dreams of heaven were any truer ? ''", "context_len": 128}}}, "barred": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 59, "context": "Knights and slaves , men , women , and children , and even the inmates of the convents and nunneries , aided in the work , and when at last the outer wall fell , and the Turks thought that success was at hand , the pasha saw with astonishment and dismay that entry to the city was still barred by a work as formidable as that which he had destroyed at an enormous expenditure of ammunition", "context_len": 77}}}, "nailing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 109, "context": "or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing his victims to the tree with spike nails driven through the hands and feet into the tough timber", "context_len": 128}}}, "dangling": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 49, "context": "In the meantime he did what he could with the family pony , and he had long rides in the woods with the other boy , who used to get his father 's horse when he was not using it on Sunday , and race with him through the dangling wild grape-vines and pawpaw thickets , and over the reedy levels of the river , their hearts both bounding with the same high hopes of a world that could never come true", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "This was called the `` Beacon of Death , '' and the scene represented the forecastle of the pirate ship with a lantern dangling from the rigging , to lure unsuspecting merchantmen to their doom", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Peggy came hurrying up in great excitement , tripping as usual over her dangling bootlace", "context_len": 15}}}, "pleasantly": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Did any of the boys and girls ever try the experiment of speaking to him pleasantly ? I am quite sure the boys do their best to annoy him in any way they can contrive , and even some of the girls tease him slyly and call him names , I am told", "context_len": 54}}}, "basely": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "While Ben rejoiced and lifted silent thanks to God for his narrow escape from a shameful death , he felt no satisfaction in the knowledge that the men who had basely conspired against them had suffered the like terrible fate", "context_len": 40}}}, "tingle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "'Just imagine , Paul , ' the letter went on , ' I , of all possible people , a missionary 's wife ! But the fact of the matter is , my precious saint , your splendid , consecrated life made me tingle with shame to my finger tips when I thought of my aimless existence , and when I remembered how you took up your cross and followed your Master to Sleepy Hollow , there seemed to be no reason why I should not follow Him to Africa", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "If I had only known who he was , I should have said something that would have made his cheeks tingle", "context_len": 21}}}, "forgiveness": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 46, "context": "A very happy , grateful thing , the dear mamma thought it too ; and very happy , grateful tears were those which dimmed her eyes as she read her little daughter 's simple story , and then thanked God that the lessons of love and forgiveness which were given to her little ones fell not upon stony ground , but took root and bore precious fruit in those tender young hearts", "context_len": 72}}}, "differ": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 50, "context": "Hers was a high ideal of filial submission ; and though she had reached an age when she had a right to an independent opinion , yet obedience to them ranked in her mind before the necessity to decide for herself , in a question where opinions might so greatly differ", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Western and Eastern boys differ considerably , however ; and no doubt the explanation resides in this fact", "context_len": 18}}}, "govern": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 87, "context": "Dear old mammy , who nursed every one of us , from Ruthven down to myself , and whom mother gave to Margaret as a treasure past all price when Harry was born , -- poor mammy , who considers herself quite as much one of the family as any Stanton , Duncan , or Bradford among us all , -- to talk of dismissing her ! But nothing less would satisfy Aunt Patty ; and Margaret gently claiming the right to correct her own children and govern her own household as she saw fit , and Henry firmly upholding his wife , Aunt Patty departed that very afternoon in a tremendous passion , and has never entered the house since", "context_len": 121}}}, "communities": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 15, "context": "Everywhere and always the world of boys is outside of the laws that govern grown-up communities , and it has its unwritten usages , which are handed down from old to young , and perpetuated on the same level of years , and are lived into and lived out of , but are binding , through all personal vicissitudes , upon the great body of boys between six and twelve years old", "context_len": 72}}}, "unwritten": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Everywhere and always the world of boys is outside of the laws that govern grown-up communities , and it has its unwritten usages , which are handed down from old to young , and perpetuated on the same level of years , and are lived into and lived out of , but are binding , through all personal vicissitudes , upon the great body of boys between six and twelve years old", "context_len": 72}}}, "usages": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "Everywhere and always the world of boys is outside of the laws that govern grown-up communities , and it has its unwritten usages , which are handed down from old to young , and perpetuated on the same level of years , and are lived into and lived out of , but are binding , through all personal vicissitudes , upon the great body of boys between six and twelve years old", "context_len": 72}}}, "perpetuated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Everywhere and always the world of boys is outside of the laws that govern grown-up communities , and it has its unwritten usages , which are handed down from old to young , and perpetuated on the same level of years , and are lived into and lived out of , but are binding , through all personal vicissitudes , upon the great body of boys between six and twelve years old", "context_len": 72}}}, "personal": {"freq": 104, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 104, "word_index": 34, "context": "Captain Riley 's narrative of his shipwreck on the coast of Africa , and his captivity among the Arabs , was a book which my boy and his brother prized with a kind of personal interest , because their father told them that he had once seen a son of Captain Riley when he went to get his appointment of collector at Columbus , and that this son was named William Willshire Riley , after the good English merchant , William Willshire , who had ransomed Captain Riley", "context_len": 88}}}, "vicissitudes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "Everywhere and always the world of boys is outside of the laws that govern grown-up communities , and it has its unwritten usages , which are handed down from old to young , and perpetuated on the same level of years , and are lived into and lived out of , but are binding , through all personal vicissitudes , upon the great body of boys between six and twelve years old", "context_len": 72}}}, "violate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "No boy can violate them without losing his standing among the other boys , and he can not enter into their world without coming under them", "context_len": 26}}}, "obeys": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "He must do this , and must not do that ; he obeys , but he does not know why , any more than the far-off savages from whom his customs seem mostly to have come", "context_len": 36}}}, "invisible": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 48, "context": "Alden could make out in the reflection the stranger \u2019 s hands ( one of which grasped the knotted bridle reins ) , the pommel of his saddle , and the tuft of hair at the base of his pony \u2019 s neck , but everything else was invisible in the darkness", "context_len": 52}}}, "ideals": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "No wonder that the boy saw with his eyes and heard with his ears and modelled himself as nearly as he could on the ideals of his hero ; and when Colonel Harrison signed the warrant for the king 's execution , the boy was as convinced a regicide as any of the judges whose names were written beside that of Harrison on the fatal parchment", "context_len": 66}}}, "superstitions": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Here , then , my boy , on this spot where thou wert once cradled , renounce all these Christian follies and superstitions , and thou shalt go back with me to the camp of King Sweyn , where thou shalt be received as the descendant of warrior kings , and shalt forget that thou , the falcon , wert ever the inmate of the dovecote", "context_len": 67}}}, "ferocity": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 43, "context": "The first-named was the bearded ruffian who had so nearly settled my account in the valley near Petersfield , and now , knowing full well that his neck was already in the hangman \u2019 s noose , his demeanour was one of sullen ferocity , and , though he was heavily manacled , his appearance was like that of a savage beast awaiting its opportunity to spring", "context_len": 67}}}, "depravity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "But just as they were rolling into Grasmere , the off-leader , with the total depravity peculiar to animal nature , struck the only stone visible within a hundred yards on that perfect roadway , laming himself instantly and in most pronounced fashion", "context_len": 43}}}, "credible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "It has its own ideals and superstitions , and these are often of a ferocity , a depravity , scarcely credible in after-life", "context_len": 23}}}, "penetrate": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 31, "context": "The river was so distant , so broad and rapid , and its banks were bordered and defended by such ferocious foes , that to cross its eddying tide , and penetrate into the unknown and unexplored regions beyond , leaving the broad , and deep , and rapid stream to cut off the hopes of retreat , implied the possession of extreme self-reliance , courage , and decision", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "A man can sometimes penetrate plans -- ''", "context_len": 8}}}, "revenge": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "The boys ' gardens had suffered some , but not so much as those of the little girls ; whether it was that Dolly fancied Maggie and Bessie had been the most to blame for Lem 's imprisonment , and so chose first to revenge herself on them ; whether it was that their gardens lay nearer to her hand and she had been interrupted in her wicked work before she had quite destroyed the boys ' , -- could not be known", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 24, "context": "In his abject misery and dread , Mark Penelly saw , in the stern gaze before him , anger and a vindictive desire for revenge ; he saw therein fierce hate , and an implacable , unchanging condemnation ; he felt that Harry was sustaining him there where he had dragged him to make his sufferings more acute , and that , after holding him up for a while , he would loosen his hold , causing him to sink at once into the deep water by the rocks , and be swept away by the tremendous current", "context_len": 98}}}, "inflict": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 74, "context": "The first great law was that , whatever happened to you through another boy , whatever hurt or harm he did you , you were to right yourself upon his person if you could ; but if he was too big , and you could not hope to revenge yourself , then you were to bear the wrong , not only for that time , but for as many times as he chose to inflict it", "context_len": 76}}}, "tormentor": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "To tell the teacher or your mother , or to betray your tormentor to any one outside of the boys ' world , was to prove yourself a cry-baby , without honor or self-respect , and unfit to go with the other fellows", "context_len": 43}}}, "unfit": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "It could only be possible were there , not one but many , traitors among the officials ; if this is so , then indeed am I a dull ass , and unfit for my duty here , of which I shall pray you to relieve me , and to order such punishment as the council may deem just to be allotted to me for having so signally been hoodwinked", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hartley will not think me wholly unfit in future to act as guardian on your drives", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Now that they were wounded they bemoaned the fact that their injuries were not greater , and envied those of their comrades who were permanently disabled and unfit for further service in the field", "context_len": 34}}}, "residents": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 77, "context": "How came that picture in the dwelling of Japazy , the half-breed ? Why had a similar picture been in the hut of Sobrinini ? What mysterious link was there between the lovely original of that picture and Sobrinini , the witch woman , Japazy , the master of Jaguar Island , Jojasta , the medicine man of the Moving Mountain , and Casson , his friend and former protector ? Somewhere , some time , those residents of the Amazonian jungle had known the mysterious Bartow , his wife , Laura , and perhaps the little child named Bonny", "context_len": 100}}}, "adopted": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 123, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "Here the apostle of Wessex , Birinus , had converted and baptized Cynegils , king of that country , Oswald , the saintly king of Northumbria , being present , and receiving him fresh from the regenerating waters as his adopted son", "context_len": 42}}}, "conclusions": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 51, "context": "When you moved away from a neighborhood you did not lose all your rights in it ; you did not have to fight when you went back to see the boys , or anything ; but if one of them met you in your new precincts you might have to try conclusions with him ; and perhaps , if he was a boy who had been in the habit of whipping you , you were quite ready to do so", "context_len": 80}}}, "sovereign": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 62, "context": "My birthday eve they kept in high style , the men smoking long clay pipes , till our dining-room was enveloped in a cloud of tobacco smoke , so that my aunt declared that her best hangings would reek for days like a London coffee tavern , whereat Sir George pacified her by saying that the weed of Virginia is ever a sovereign safeguard against the plague !", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was written by his own hand , and expressed his thanks as a temporal sovereign for the great benefit to the commerce of his subjects by the destruction of the corsair fleet , and as the head of the Christian Church for the blow struck at the Moslems", "context_len": 49}}}, "witnessed": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 53, "context": "But although I had passed through the day unscathed , so far as actual bodily injury was concerned , it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything", "context_len": 128}}}, "combat": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 45, "context": "Several of the Turkish galleys , in their efforts to arrest the approach of the fire ships towards the bridge , became themselves involved in the flames ; but they were so far successful that when daylight broke the bridge was still intact and the combat at the breach continued to rage with determination and fury on both sides", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "We must send up and drive in as many of these as possible , and of course we shall send messengers to Tempe ; but it will need a very large force to combat these pirates , who will be able to come down and plunder and destroy , and then retire to the hills , whence it will be hard to dislodge them", "context_len": 64}}}, "shamed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 7, "context": "The children , she said , had shamed her by their greater patience and submission under the disappointment she had so unintentionally brought upon them , and now she felt that the ill-temper she had shown had brought reproach on the Master whom she really wished to serve , and destroyed the little influence she had been able to gain with the children", "context_len": 63}}}, "wrongs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "The home instruction was all against fighting ; my boy was taught that it was not only wicked , but foolish ; that if it was wrong to strike , it was just as wrong to strike back ; that two wrongs never made a right , and so on", "context_len": 50}}}, "trials": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 95, "context": "`` This same morning -- and my friend Indian will substantiate my statement , for he was there -- a low , ignorant cadet corporal did enter into my room , for inspection , by Zeus , and after generally displaying his ill-manners , he turned to me and conveyed the extraordinary information that , according to rules , forsooth , I must be deprived of the dearest object of my affections , solace of my weary hours , my friend in time of need , my companion in sickness , which through all the trials of adversity has stuck to me closer than a brother , my only joy , my -- -- ''", "context_len": 115}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "We are having such an interesting time , a heart-warming time ! There is to be a Confirmation to-morrow ; and oh , through what fiery trials some of the dear candidates have come ! There is B -- n ,", "context_len": 41}}}, "perplexities": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` And they tell him everything , what they have read and studied , and the little troubles and differences and perplexities , and he listens and explains and laughs with them when it is funny , and everything is so nice", "context_len": 42}}}, "bullies": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` More beef into it , bullies , '' urged the bos \u2019 n to the capstan men , and , as the strain on the cables increased , the rest of the crew , in obedience to an order , doubled along the sloping decks , as well as they were able , towards the bows , the whole vessel trembling with the motion", "context_len": 65}}}, "fame": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 9, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The young warriors , anxious to obtain fame and war-titles , joined the war-parties on the call of a leader", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "He wished to secure their favor as a means of aiding him up the steep path to fame and power which he was attempting to climb", "context_len": 26}}}, "range": {"freq": 93, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "There were some boys of such standing as bullies and such wide fame that they could range all neighborhoods of the town not only without fear of being molested , or made to pass under the local yoke anywhere , but with such plenary powers of intimidation that the other boys submitted to them without question", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 88, "word_index": 75, "context": "It happened that one night , when , as now was frequently the case , I had been summoned to entertain the king and his household by `` obliging them with a little music , '' I was playing some soft , plaintive air -- I forget what -- when , chancing to glance toward Ama , who , seated on the ground on the extreme left of the semicircle , was well within my range of vision , I fancied I saw some moving object close to her left hand , which was resting lightly on the ground", "context_len": 99}}}, "neighborhoods": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "There were some boys of such standing as bullies and such wide fame that they could range all neighborhoods of the town not only without fear of being molested , or made to pass under the local yoke anywhere , but with such plenary powers of intimidation that the other boys submitted to them without question", "context_len": 56}}}, "molested": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "There were some boys of such standing as bullies and such wide fame that they could range all neighborhoods of the town not only without fear of being molested , or made to pass under the local yoke anywhere , but with such plenary powers of intimidation that the other boys submitted to them without question", "context_len": 56}}}, "yoke": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 16, "context": "One of the oxen , stupid as the species always is , became entangled in his yoke and would have been drowned had not the African urged his pony alongside , where with a swift , powerful wrench , some shouting and a savage blow or two with the butt of his gun , he straightened out matters and saved the valuable animal", "context_len": 63}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I was standing on the piazza , looking down the road , and thinking if it was not best to yoke up a team of oxen and go in search of my boy , when I saw the sledge coming up the side of the lake", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I , said John , mean to paint my steers \u2019 yoke , my gunning float , sled , and the boat father made me , if we can get enough ; and I \u2019 ll paint my bedroom , then put some into whitewash and paint the walls", "context_len": 49}}}, "plenary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "There were some boys of such standing as bullies and such wide fame that they could range all neighborhoods of the town not only without fear of being molested , or made to pass under the local yoke anywhere , but with such plenary powers of intimidation that the other boys submitted to them without question", "context_len": 56}}}, "intimidation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "There were some boys of such standing as bullies and such wide fame that they could range all neighborhoods of the town not only without fear of being molested , or made to pass under the local yoke anywhere , but with such plenary powers of intimidation that the other boys submitted to them without question", "context_len": 56}}}, "attended": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 67, "word_index": 14, "context": "The defence , though even more obstinate and desperate than the first , was attended with less success , for after inflicting enormous losses upon the great army , commanded by the Sultan Solyman himself , the town was forced to yield ; for although the Grand Master L'Isle Adam , and most of his knights , would have preferred to bury themselves beneath the ruins rather than yield , they were deterred from doing so , by the knowledge that it would have entailed the massacre of the whole of the inhabitants , who had throughout the siege fought valiantly in the defence of the town", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was not largely attended , for Florence had made but few acquaintances during her stay in the city", "context_len": 19}}}, "fighters": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 47, "context": "There was Captain Poynings standing unmoved amid the crash and din of the fight , the master standing by the wheel , his head bound with a blood-stained scarf , several men , still in death , encumbering her decks , while amid the throng of excited fighters a continuous procession of wounded was winding its way towards the main hatch", "context_len": 61}}}, "retainers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "They seemed to know by instinct that this was Buz Simpson , and they stood patiently by while he kicked their marbles out of the ring and broke up their game , and , after staying awhile to cover them with ignominy and insult , passed on with his retainers to other fields of conquest", "context_len": 55}}}, "outrage": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 51, "context": "It represents the bare cost of a new copper pedestal to replace the one you shot to pieces last night , and it 's a wonder you are not in jail for murder , for had that cannon ball struck a human being -- Enough ! before I take up this outrage with the district attorney in its criminal phase , are you going to settle the damage , or are you not ? ''", "context_len": 75}}}, "efficient": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 41, "context": "My first impulse was to carry the body down to where the soil was softer , and there dig a grave for it ; but while I was considering this plan , it occurred to me that , with no more efficient tool than a spear to serve as a shovel , it would be practically impossible for me to bury the body deep enough to protect it from the jackals and hyaenas ; and I therefore determined that , instead of burying it , I would burn it", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "To get as efficient a corps as possible , Alexander dismounted five hundred of the cavalry , and gave their horses to five hundred men -- officers and others -- selected for their strength and courage from among the foot soldiers", "context_len": 41}}}, "weakness": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 94, "context": "Fortunately , the sun 's rays came down hot and bright , and the rock grew warmer , so that by degrees the terribly void look began to leave Mark Penelly 's face , and at last , when Harry held out his hand , saying , `` Do you feel better ? '' Mark Penelly caught it in both of his , clung to it , and , turning half over on his face , laid his forehead against it , and , forgetting his years of manhood , lay there in his weakness , and sobbed and cried like a child", "context_len": 103}}}, "gross": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "\u2019 [ 63 ] What gross , fearful ignorance ! I heard on good authority that in one place in India , not the Panjab , offerings are actually made to a dead European , who was a special object of dread to the Natives , and whom they therefore wish to propitiate as a kind of demon ! Do not the poor , deluded creatures want teachers ? I find the women in general very gentle and courteous , and quite willing to listen when they are spoken to on the subject of religion", "context_len": 95}}}, "tripped": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 9, "context": "As Ama , taking her bow and arrows , tripped lightly away toward the forest , I proceeded to shin up the tree , and presently , after some labour , found myself among its topmost branches , which towered high above those of all the other trees in the neighbourhood , and -- it being by this time broad daylight -- obtained a magnificent view of the surrounding country , extending for , as I estimated , some forty miles toward the south and east , while toward the north and west the view was shut off by high hills , through which the river wound its way", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The foremost went down under the chair with which I struck him , the second one tripped over the fallen body and also went down with my assistance", "context_len": 28}}}, "sacredly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "There were some things so base that a boy could not do them ; and what happened out of doors , and strictly within the boy 's world , had to be kept sacredly secret among the boys", "context_len": 38}}}, "beguiled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "For instance , if you had been beguiled , as a little boy , into being the last in the game of snap-the-whip , and the snap sent you rolling head over heels on the hard ground , and skinned your nose and tore your trousers , you could cry from the pain without disgrace , and some of the fellows would come up and try to comfort you ; but you were bound in honor not to appeal to the teacher , and you were expected to use every device to get the blood off you before you went in , and to hide the tear in your trousers", "context_len": 110}}}, "skinned": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "For instance , if you had been beguiled , as a little boy , into being the last in the game of snap-the-whip , and the snap sent you rolling head over heels on the hard ground , and skinned your nose and tore your trousers , you could cry from the pain without disgrace , and some of the fellows would come up and try to comfort you ; but you were bound in honor not to appeal to the teacher , and you were expected to use every device to get the blood off you before you went in , and to hide the tear in your trousers", "context_len": 110}}}, "ruled": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 70, "context": "The historical romance which he had begun to write before the impassioned days of the theatre seems to have been lost sight of at this time , though it was an enterprise that he was so confident of carrying forward that he told all his family and friends about it , and even put down the opening passages of it on paper which he cut in large quantity , and ruled himself , so as to have it exactly suitable", "context_len": 80}}}, "boring": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "So it was with no great confidence as to the ultimate outcome that he made his way back to the cabin , surrounded by the lowering bucks of the Araos tribe and feeling the suspicious gaze of Lodo boring into his back", "context_len": 42}}}, "shaft": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 68, "context": "He had a notion that if he could get up a chariot by sawing down the sides of a store-box for the body , and borrowing the hind-wheels of the baby 's willow wagon , and then , drawn by the family dog Tip at a mad gallop , come suddenly whirling round the corner of the school-house , wearing spangled circus-tights and bearing Apollo 's bow and shaft , while a silken scarf which he had seen in a bureau-drawer at home blew gallantly out behind him , it would have a fine effect with the boys", "context_len": 98}}}, "tube": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 50, "context": "With some show of hesitation the man surrendered the thing , and upon inspection I found it to be a reed of about a foot in length , with a mouthpiece shaped something like that of a whistle , and with four small holes drilled in the length of the tube , whereby an expert performer might produce seven distinct tones ; but the tones were not consecutive , and the instrument was altogether a very poor and inefficient affair", "context_len": 80}}}, "thumb": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 48, "context": "But that Lord Grey of Wigmore was a disciple of Old Nick himself , and what does he do but catch hold of Dame Eleanor 's daughter , which were n't more ' an a little maid like Miss Peggy there , and put the screw on her thumb , thinkin ' it would loosen her mother 's tongue to hear her shriek", "context_len": 63}}}, "abominable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` How ! you must be beside yourself to come to me -- me ! -- Rupert ! on such an errand ! Because , forsooth , I exchanged civilities with one I held an honourable enemy , you dare to expect my interest on behalf of a regicide ! I vow , sir , every man who even witnessed that most abominable and unnatural murder should swing , did it depend on me", "context_len": 74}}}, "lawful": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 60, "context": "`` Upwards it leads to the banqueting hall , and you can comprehend my escape this evening , '' said he ; `` but our path is now downwards , unless you would like to go up and see the drunken beasts of murderers snoring off their debauch upon the floor as they fell ; oh , that it were lawful for a Christian man to cut their throats as they lie ; many innocent lives would be saved thereby , which those brutes will live to destroy", "context_len": 88}}}, "briers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "It was perfectly good form , also , to get a boy , if you could , to shut his eyes , and then lead him into a mud-puddle or a thicket of briers or nettles , or to fool him in any heartless way , such as promising to pump easy when he put his mouth to the pump-spout , and then coming down on the pump-handle with a rush that flooded him with water and sent him off blowing the tide from his nostrils like a whale", "context_len": 89}}}, "nettles": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was perfectly good form , also , to get a boy , if you could , to shut his eyes , and then lead him into a mud-puddle or a thicket of briers or nettles , or to fool him in any heartless way , such as promising to pump easy when he put his mouth to the pump-spout , and then coming down on the pump-handle with a rush that flooded him with water and sent him off blowing the tide from his nostrils like a whale", "context_len": 89}}}, "heartless": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 43, "context": "It was perfectly good form , also , to get a boy , if you could , to shut his eyes , and then lead him into a mud-puddle or a thicket of briers or nettles , or to fool him in any heartless way , such as promising to pump easy when he put his mouth to the pump-spout , and then coming down on the pump-handle with a rush that flooded him with water and sent him off blowing the tide from his nostrils like a whale", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "I gave you three sovereigns to change into postoffice orders for my mother , and she -- she never had them ; she never got any of my letters , she thought me cold , heartless , unfeeling -- she , my mother , the one I love best in the world", "context_len": 52}}}, "pump": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "It was perfectly good form , also , to get a boy , if you could , to shut his eyes , and then lead him into a mud-puddle or a thicket of briers or nettles , or to fool him in any heartless way , such as promising to pump easy when he put his mouth to the pump-spout , and then coming down on the pump-handle with a rush that flooded him with water and sent him off blowing the tide from his nostrils like a whale", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was a town pump there for you , so that you would not have to go into the house for a drink when you got thirsty , and perhaps be set to doing something ; and there were plenty of boards for teeter and see-saw ; and somehow that neighborhood seemed to understand boys , and did not molest them in any way", "context_len": 64}}}, "whale": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 88, "context": "It was perfectly good form , also , to get a boy , if you could , to shut his eyes , and then lead him into a mud-puddle or a thicket of briers or nettles , or to fool him in any heartless way , such as promising to pump easy when he put his mouth to the pump-spout , and then coming down on the pump-handle with a rush that flooded him with water and sent him off blowing the tide from his nostrils like a whale", "context_len": 89}}}, "stung": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "And mingling their voices they would sing , `` Infanta ! Infanta Lolita ! '' until Lola , stung to rage , turned upon them wildly ; whereat their delighted cries served to send her flying homeward", "context_len": 37}, "VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 25, "context": "-- As I was engaged yesterday with a party of our boys , I was interrupted by hearing that my poor dear Ayah had been stung -- bitten , as the people incorrectly say -- by a scorpion", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` That you 're stung ! Eh , Stumpy , my lad ? '' cried Bart good naturedly , clapping his chum on the back", "context_len": 25}}}, "deceit": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "Edric , who is steeped in stratagems and deceit , plotted against his life again and again , whereupon Edmund broke up the camp in indignation , and took a separate course with all the warriors who would follow his standard", "context_len": 41}}}, "allowable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "This did not prove him a cry-baby ; it was allowable , like crying when you stumped your toe", "context_len": 19}}}, "playground": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "For instance , if you appeared on the playground with an apple , and all the boys came whooping round , `` You know me , Jimmy ! '' `` You know your uncle ! '' `` You know your grandfather ! '' and you began to sell out bites at three pins for a lady-bite and six pins for a hog-bite , and a boy bought a lady-bite and then took a hog-bite , he was held in contempt , and could by no means pass it off for a good joke on you ; it was considered mean", "context_len": 100}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The sons of Nippon won \u2019 t be using their little mountain playground as long as our fliers can keep an eye on it", "context_len": 24}}}, "whooping": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "For instance , if you appeared on the playground with an apple , and all the boys came whooping round , `` You know me , Jimmy ! '' `` You know your uncle ! '' `` You know your grandfather ! '' and you began to sell out bites at three pins for a lady-bite and six pins for a hog-bite , and a boy bought a lady-bite and then took a hog-bite , he was held in contempt , and could by no means pass it off for a good joke on you ; it was considered mean", "context_len": 100}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` 'They left this place , and came to a creek , called Wattoolahawka hutche , Whooping-creek , so called from the whooping of cranes , a great many being there", "context_len": 31}}}, "bites": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "For instance , if you appeared on the playground with an apple , and all the boys came whooping round , `` You know me , Jimmy ! '' `` You know your uncle ! '' `` You know your grandfather ! '' and you began to sell out bites at three pins for a lady-bite and six pins for a hog-bite , and a boy bought a lady-bite and then took a hog-bite , he was held in contempt , and could by no means pass it off for a good joke on you ; it was considered mean", "context_len": 100}}}, "contempt": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 79, "context": "For instance , if you appeared on the playground with an apple , and all the boys came whooping round , `` You know me , Jimmy ! '' `` You know your uncle ! '' `` You know your grandfather ! '' and you began to sell out bites at three pins for a lady-bite and six pins for a hog-bite , and a boy bought a lady-bite and then took a hog-bite , he was held in contempt , and could by no means pass it off for a good joke on you ; it was considered mean", "context_len": 100}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "The officers who formed the escort appeared , however , to trouble very little about the sentiments of the crowd , and from good nature or contempt went about their own affairs , allowing the country squires and their wives to show their loyal devotion in any fashion they pleased", "context_len": 50}}}, "stoning": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "My boy was not allowed to stone them ; but when he was with the other fellows , and his elder brother was not along , he could not help stoning them", "context_len": 32}}}, "inanimate": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "They were always stoning something , birds , or dogs , or mere inanimate marks , but most of the time they were stoning one another", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "At the moment I took but scant notice of the circumstance , for the flickering flames of the fire which was always kindled upon such occasions played strange pranks with the lights and shadows , and often imparted a weird effect of movement to stationery and even inanimate objects ; but presently , happening to again glance in that direction , my eye was once more caught by the same queer wavering movement", "context_len": 73}}}, "singly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "Since , however , this adventure has taught us that a single leader like Hassan sails with at least nine ships under his orders , it is clear that in future our galleys must not adventure singly among the islands", "context_len": 40}}}, "whim": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "They stoned a boy who left a group singly , and it was lawful for him to throw stones back at the rest , if the whim took him , when he got a little way off", "context_len": 37}}}, "skull": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 54, "context": "Marching up to what I conjectured to be the king 's house -- from the fact that it was not only by far the largest dwelling in the enclosure , but was also distinguished by an exclusive embellishment in the form of a row of a dozen poles , each surmounted by a human skull , planted upright in the ground before it -- we halted at a distance of some twenty paces from the entrance , with our backs turned toward the crucifixion tree , the leafless branches of which overshadowed us , and waited", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "'Send him a letter with a coffin drawed out at the top , or a skull and cross-bones , to say as how that 's what 's waitin ' for un , if he comes to Gorswen", "context_len": 37}}}, "informal": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "And so it happened that when the informal assembly dissolved for supper it dissolved with but one idea in the mind of every cadet in the party -- that Mark Mallory must be taken down !", "context_len": 36}}}, "scuffles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Their fights were mostly informal scuffles , on and off in a flash , and conducted with none of the ceremony which I have read of concerning the fights of English boys", "context_len": 32}}}, "conducted": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was nearly noon when I was conducted back to my hut after my futile attempt to cure the king ; and it was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant", "context_len": 128}}}, "moral": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 1, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "My boy distinctly remembered that under one of these trees his elder brother first broached to him that awful scheme of reform about fibbing , and applied to their own lives the moral of `` The Trippings of Tom Pepper ; '' he remembered how a conviction of the righteousness of the scheme sank into his soul , and he could not withhold his consent", "context_len": 65}}}, "superiority": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 56, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}}}, "champions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 61, "context": "You have been selected from among many other candidates , and you have not only to justify the choice , but to uphold the reputation and honour of the young knights of your Order , by all of whom your doings will be regarded with special interest , as reflecting credit not only upon yourselves individually , but as representatives and champions of them all", "context_len": 65}}}, "preparatory": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "It took a great while to bring the champions to blows , and I have known the mere preparatory insults of a fight of this kind to wear out the spirit of the combatants and the patience of the spectators , so that not a blow was struck , finally , and the whole affair fell through", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Phil was practically assured of a place , for he had played the game at a preparatory school , and had as good a reputation in regard to filling the position of quarter-back as Tom had in the pitching box", "context_len": 40}}}, "insults": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 19, "context": "It took a great while to bring the champions to blows , and I have known the mere preparatory insults of a fight of this kind to wear out the spirit of the combatants and the patience of the spectators , so that not a blow was struck , finally , and the whole affair fell through", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Thar \u2019 s no use of throwing away powder and ball , he said ; we \u2019 re likely to need all we \u2019 ve got afore we see Salt Lake , and them insults don \u2019 t hurt", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "When a man insults me I kill him", "context_len": 8}}}, "combatants": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 14, "context": "The smoke of the guns lay so heavy on the water , and the combatants were so intent upon the struggle at the breach , that Gervaise steered his galley into the midst of the Turkish vessels laden with troops ready to disembark , without attracting any notice ; then , standing upon the taffrail , he signalled to the two outside boats to throw off their ropes and make for the Turkish ship nearest to them", "context_len": 77}}}, "quarrelsome": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "With these long , awkward legs , he can scamper out of the way , and with those sharp claws , he fights , for he is a quarrelsome little fellow", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "We have the cruel and lurking Chicasa , the powerful and ingenious but treacherous and corruptible Cha'hta , the magnanimous and hospitable , proud and revengeful Creek , the aggressive Alibamu , the quarrelsome Y\u00e1massi , and the self-willed , independent Seminole , jealous of the enjoyment of his savage freedom in the swamps and everglades of the semi-tropical peninsula", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "Stone is a great deal older than we are ; but since we all know that she does not take much pains to make Mamie a good and pleasant child , do you not think that this ought to make us more patient with her when she is fretful and quarrelsome ? ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "scorned": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Tell your mother I scorned her advice the day we were caught in the rain , and paid well for my folly , for I was very ill ; but there was a good , kind doctor , who came and cured me , and now he is going to 'take care of me and my money , and make me behave myself", "context_len": 64}}}, "seesaw": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "When they were having fun they did not want to have any girls around ; but in the back-yard a boy might play teeter or seesaw , or some such thing , with his sisters and their friends , without necessarily losing caste , though such things were not encouraged", "context_len": 50}}}, "caste": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "To those friends who thought he lost caste thereby he had always the same argument -- that he saw no reason why the cultivation of fields should counteract the habits of refinement and good breeding to which he had been reared ; that in the colonies educated gentlemen set to work to labour with their hands , and are thought none the worse of : so why not in England , where land is good and markets are plentiful , especially when it involved the keeping of a fine old property which had been in the family for so many hundreds of years ?", "context_len": 104}}}, "slighting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "On the other hand , a boy was bound to defend them against anything that he thought slighting or insulting ; and you did not have to verify the fact that anything had been said or done ; you merely had to hear that it had", "context_len": 46}}}, "insulting": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "On the other hand , a boy was bound to defend them against anything that he thought slighting or insulting ; and you did not have to verify the fact that anything had been said or done ; you merely had to hear that it had", "context_len": 46}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "We passed through numerous villages , the inhabitants of which flocked out to see us , though they behaved courteously , and refrained from insulting us , a contrast to the behaviour of our own countrymen to their Dutch prisoners", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "De minute he did dat , he turned to you and said , I beg your pardon ! \u2019 but you thinked he said something insulting , but he didn \u2019 t", "context_len": 32}}}, "verify": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 87, "context": "You could do something by turning your roundabout inside out , and rolling your trousers up as far as they would go ; but what a fellow wanted to make him a real circus actor was a long pair of white cotton stockings , and I never knew a fellow that got a pair ; I heard of many a fellow who was said to have got a pair ; but when you came down to the fact , they vanished like ghosts when you try to verify them", "context_len": 89}}}, "accusation": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 25, "context": "He knew not then that the example before him would nerve him in moments of severest trial , then fast approaching , that the one accusation urged against the Christians , which he had felt most keenly , that of cowardice , was answered in the weak yet valiant boy , who found strength in the name of Christ to endure all for His sake ; neither did his fierce countrymen know that they were preparing a disappointment for the pagan Anlaf , and for all those of his house and lineage", "context_len": 92}}}, "culprit": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 10, "context": "It really seemed too bad to leave the poor little culprit so long in suspense , for to a child 's mind the agony of waiting is often far worse than the dreaded punishment , and childhood 's sorrows are so overwhelming and hopeless that they almost eclipse those of later years", "context_len": 52}}}, "champion": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 71, "context": "But if he caught sight of a dog that he did not know , he would drop that stick and start for that dog as far off as he could see him , spilling the Henry Clay Club out of the wagon piecemeal as he went , and never stopping till he mixed up the strange dog in a fight where it would have been hard to tell which was either champion and which was the club wagon", "context_len": 78}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I suppose there 's no one else at school who would champion you ? '", "context_len": 15}}}, "brutal": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 52, "context": "What he saw caused a fierce look to come to his face and his freckled cheeks to flush ; for in the midst of the group was Hunk Rollins , a look of vicious pleasure on his face , holding little Jimmy Jones by the ear , which he was twisting with brutal pleasure , showing his ugly teeth as he laughed at the tortured lad \u2019 s cries and pleadings", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "This last comer appeared to be in a state of great excitement , and his brutal , overbearing nature was clearly in evidence", "context_len": 23}}}, "weeping": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 62, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 56, "context": "Now the princess , of the excess of her grief for her separation from her husband and the Sultan her father and of her sore distress at that which had betided her with the accursed Maugrabin enchanter , used every day to arise , at the first peep of dawn , [ 586 ] and sit weeping ; nay , she slept not anights and forswore meat and drink", "context_len": 69}}}, "victor": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 51, "context": "Justice , law , liberty were solid facts ; those were the watchwords General Harrison had taught him ; for those he had lived , to those he would be true , whether good or evil fortune awaited him , whether there were , indeed , a heavenly reward for the victor , or but the abyss of forgetfulness at the end of the strife", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "VICTOR 140", "context_len": 2}}}, "retribution": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 61, "context": "But , in carrying this terrible decision into effect , he acted in a manner so deliberate , discriminating , and cautious , as to diminish very much the irritation and resentment which it would otherwise have caused , and to give it its full moral effect as a measure , not of angry resentment , but of calm and deliberate retribution -- just and proper , according to the ideas of the time", "context_len": 74}}}, "irreparable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "In fact , it seems best to be very careful how we try to do justice in this world , and mostly to leave retribution of all kinds to God , who really knows about things ; and content ourselves as much as possible with mercy , whose mistakes are not so irreparable", "context_len": 53}}}, "crullers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "They would follow a boy to his door , and wait for him to come out ; and they would sometimes get him to go in and ask his mother for crullers or sugar-cakes ; when they came to see him they never went indoors for him , but stood on the sidewalk and called him with a peculiar cry , something like `` E-oo-we , e-oo-we ! '' and threw stones at trees , or anything , till he came out", "context_len": 82}}}, "reasonable": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 49, "context": "I do not know Claudia 's thoughts , Caretto remarked drily , but I do know that last year she refused to listen to at least a score of excellent offers for her hand , including one from a son of the doge himself , and that without any reasonable cause assigned by her , to the great wonderment of all , seeing that she does not appear to have any leaning whatever towards a life in a nunnery", "context_len": 79}}}, "sovereignty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The mother represented the family sovereignty ; the father was seldom seen , and he counted for little or nothing among the outside boys", "context_len": 24}}}, "according": {"freq": 119, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 119, "word_index": 41, "context": "[ 354 ] On this wise she abode a week 's space [ 355 ] and the Sultan saw her at each Divan ; so , when she went on the last day [ of the week ] and stood , according to her wont , before the Divan , till it was ended , but could not muster courage to enter [ 356 ] or say aught , the Sultan arose and entering the harem , turned to his chief Vizier , who was with him , and said to him , `` O Vizier , these six or seven days [ 357 ] past I have seen yonder old woman come hither at every Divan and I note that she still carrieth somewhat under her veil", "context_len": 128}}}, "outwitted": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 105, "context": "Jerusalem , what is this ! bellowed a voice , and Joe Griffin leaped out from the midst of the burning pile ; the brands rolled off the back of his woollen shirt , which was thoroughly singed , while a fox-skin cap he wore was scorched to a crisp , as was his hair ; he ran round and round , as though he was mad , blowing his fingers ( which where slightly burned ) , and slapping them on his thighs , while on his face was a mingled expression of pain , arising from the burn , and anger at being outwitted", "context_len": 106}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "OUTWITTED", "context_len": 1}}}, "suggest": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 70, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I heard her suggest to Mr", "context_len": 6}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "He smiled in a sickly way at Bart , and once made a movement as if inclined to get out and once more suggest to the young express agent that he `` forget it", "context_len": 34}}}, "concealment": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 26, "context": "Caretto did not wish the Turks to imagine that he doubted their good faith , and while directing the main body of knights to remain in concealment near the breach , he placed two on sentry duty on the crest of the ruins , and , with four other knights and Gervaise , went down in complete armour to salute the officer in command of the burying party , as he landed from the boats", "context_len": 75}}}, "conformed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "He conformed his conduct to the principles and maxims which actuated the behavior of the shadowy people of these dry-as-dust tales ; he went about drunk with the fumes of fables about Roman emperors that never were , in an empire that never was ; and , though they tormented him by putting a mixed and impossible civilization in the place of that he knew from his Goldsmith , he was quite helpless to break from their influence", "context_len": 78}}}, "society": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 76, "context": "She did not talk nearly so much as in former times of her son ; perhaps he had told her that I was not to be worried , but she was very good and very nice , and I got sincerely attached to her : and I never saw the Duchess nor Lady Thesiger nor my old friends , although I heard that the Duchess was fairly well again , and was out and going into society ; and every one now seemed certain that Jim Randolph had gone to the bottom in the Star of Hope , but by universal consent the boarders decided that the news should be kept from mother , and mother grew much better", "context_len": 119}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I am a country boy and not used to society , but I do n't believe Cousin Ida will care for that", "context_len": 23}}}, "roundabouts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "He belonged to a military company of big boys that had real wooden guns , such as the little boys never could get , and silk oil-cloth caps , and nankeen roundabouts , and white pantaloons with black stripes down the legs ; and once they marched out to a boy 's that had a father that had a farm , and he gave them all a free dinner in an arbor before the house ; bread and butter , and apple-butter , and molasses and pound cake , and peaches and apples ; it was splendid", "context_len": 97}}}, "blacked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "His shoes , though well worn and patched , had been blacked and polished till they looked quite respectable ; the suit made from his father 's old uniform was still neat and whole , for Willie 's present quiet life was a great saving to his clothes , if that were any comfort ; his white collar was turned back and neatly tied with a black ribbon , and Mrs", "context_len": 71}}}, "dandy": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "My boy had his secret longing to be a dandy , and once he was so taken with a little silk hat at the hat-store that he gave his father no peace till he got it for him", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Me -- nowhere ! '' chuckled Bart , winking deliberately and then , walking over to the horse , he fondled his long ears , with the remark : `` If I had a dandy rig like you 've got here , I bet I 'd go somewheres , though ! ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "compromise": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "If Alfgar had been a modern Christian , he might have said , conscientiously enough , that he believed they would be judged by their light , but no such compromise in belief was possible then", "context_len": 36}}}, "unstylish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "After that he wore it several times with streaming tears ; and then he was allowed to lay it aside , and compromise on an unstylish cap of velvet , which he had despised before", "context_len": 35}}}, "tassel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Joe drew aside his jacket , and disclosed to the children 's delighted eyes the sweetest little round fuzzy ball of black fluff , just like a tiny woolly bear , with tan chest and paws , and a wagging morsel of a tail like a black tassel", "context_len": 48}}}, "babyish": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "A bow kite could be made with half a barrel hoop carried over the top of a cross , but it was troublesome to make , and it did not fly very well , and somehow it was thought to look babyish ; but it was held in greater respect than the two-stick kite , which only the smallest boys played with , and which was made by fastening two sticks in the form of a cross", "context_len": 77}}}, "peak": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` On the very summit of yonder peak , which seems to rise at least a thousand feet above its neighbors , and where the sun shone the warmest , the grains and fruits were most luxuriant , and the deer larger and tamer than in any other place , lived an old man , the chief of all the tribes that lived between the mountains and the great ocean in the far west", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Well to the northward rose a peak , whose white crest showed it was always crowned with snow", "context_len": 18}}}, "removable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The most desired kind of cap was a flat one of blue broadcloth , with a patent-leather peak , and a removable cover of oil-cloth , silk if you were rich , cotton if you were poor ; when you had pulled the top of such a cap over on one side , you were dressed for conquest , especially if you wore your hair long", "context_len": 66}}}, "marred": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 33, "context": "The slender , slightly bowed figure in the velvet coat and blue ribbon , with soft curls that flowed from beneath a plumed hat , the sad eyes , the regular features only marred by a look of weakness and almost peevishness about the mouth ; the boy had seen them all often enough in pictures , but to-day he stood for the first time in the presence of a king , of King Charles the First of England", "context_len": 79}}}, "toes": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 59, "context": "He was a rounded and curved creature -- not an angle anywhere about him ; his chin was a dimple , his lips rose-buds , his eyes sapphires ; his little head was a mass of tangled golden curls ; sunshine seemed to kiss him all over -- hair , eyes , lips , even to the small pink toes -- for he had pulled off his shoes and stockings , which were held tightly in two fat hands", "context_len": 79}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` I 'll fix it up , fustrate , '' exclaimed Lem ; `` but now , I say , mister , '' and Lem hitched up his ragged pantaloons , scratched his head , and dug his bare toes into a patch of moss in an unwonted fit of shame", "context_len": 51}}}, "ardently": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 16, "context": "At one time boots with long , sharp-pointed toes were the fashion , and he so ardently desired a pair of these that fate granted his prayer , but in the ironical spirit which fate usually shows when granting a person 's prayers", "context_len": 43}}}, "granting": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 64, "context": "and disposition ; so [ 555 ] he went up to one of those who were extolling Alaeddin on this wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both far and near have heard ? How is it thou hast heard nought of this nor of the name of Alaeddin , whom Our Lord", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Nor had she to wait long before the granting of her wish", "context_len": 12}}}, "martyrdom": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "If I had to suffer martyrdom , in no form could it appear more attractive than by being thrown over Niagara ! To be launched into eternity , shrouded in that cascade of diamonds , would rouse such a thrilling sense of the beautiful and the sublime , that half one \u2019 s fears would be swallowed up in something almost like joy", "context_len": 63}}}, "sliding": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "A few weeks ' scuffling over the gravelly ground , or a single day 's steady sliding made them the subjects for half-soling , and then it was a question of only a very little time", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 50, "context": "It was so steep that in places they were obliged to sit down and slide , clutching at the trees as they passed , to save themselves from falling , and bringing down such showers of small loose stones and shale that it seemed as if the whole hillside were sliding too , and Lilian was very relieved when they all got safely to the bottom", "context_len": 66}}}, "distribution": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 82, "context": "Then came a hurried discussion as to the destination of the booty , some , including the parish clerk , Fallowfield , who had gained the upper regions by means of the tackle , urging that it had best be taken and placed in the tower of Worth Church , the others insisting that it would be best to make one journey do , and convey it as close to Wareham as possible , where their accomplices could make arrangements for its distribution", "context_len": 83}}}, "misunderstood": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "In the confusion , orders were misunderstood , and instead of all the slaves on one side rowing whilst those on the other side backed , all order was lost , and long before the craft for which the galley was making had got round , the latter was upon her", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` 'Sta 'ueno ! '' they would whisper , watching Edith May , who upon such occasions was wont to enliven things by bursting into tears , and who commonly brought upon the following day a note from her mother , stating that Edith May must be excused for missing in spelling because she had not been at all well and had misunderstood the word", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` I do n't say you knew him , '' declared Bart , fearing he had been misunderstood , `` but you certainly greeted him", "context_len": 25}}}, "squalor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "I have tried to give some notion of the general distribution of comfort which was never riches in the Boy 's Town ; but I am afraid that I could not paint the simplicity of things there truly without being misunderstood in these days of great splendor and great squalor", "context_len": 50}}}, "richest": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` Hearkening and obedience , '' replied the genie and disappearing , brought him in the twinkling of an eye all that he had commanded him withal , whilst in his hand he held a stallion , whose like is not among the horses of the Arabs of the Arabs , [ 456 ] with housings of the richest stuffs brocaded with gold ; whereupon Alaeddin called his mother forthright and delivered her the twelve slave-girls and gave her the [ twelve ] suits , [ 457 ] so she might dress herself [ 458 ] and go with them to the Sultan 's palace", "context_len": 105}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Bring me also twelve slave-girls , who must be unique in loveliness and clad in the richest of raiment , so they may attend my mother to the Sultan 's palace , and let each slave-girl have with her a suit of apparel fit for the wearing of kings ' wives", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "It waves with fields of corn and grain , and is adorned with groves of the most luxuriant growth and richest verdure", "context_len": 22}}}, "distinctions": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 69, "context": "The majority of them are laid down in phonetically defective missionary alphabets , about which we are prompted to repeat what the citizens of the young colony of Mexico wrote to the government of Spain , in Cortez 's time : `` Send to us pious and Christian men , as preachers , bishops and missionaries , but do not send us scholars , who , with their pettifogging distinctions and love of contention , create nothing but disorder and strife", "context_len": 81}}}, "repressed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "There were distinctions among the grown people , and no doubt there were the social cruelties which are the modern expression of the savage spirit otherwise repressed by civilization ; but these were unknown among the boys", "context_len": 37}}}, "prowess": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 49, "context": "Now he was riding a stallion whose like is not among the horses of the Arabs of the Arabs [ 490 ] and his bride the Lady Bedrulbudour was looking upon him from the window of her pavilion , and when she saw his grace and goodliness and knightly prowess , she was overcome with his love and was like to fly for joy in him", "context_len": 66}}}, "underclothing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The best of them had not an overcoat ; and underclothing was unknown among them", "context_len": 15}}}, "buttoned": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "I think the coat was gray , and the skirts were buttoned back with buff , but I will not be sure of this ; and somehow I can not say how the officers differed from the privates in dress ; it was impossible for them to be more magnificent", "context_len": 50}}}, "roundabout": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "You could do something by turning your roundabout inside out , and rolling your trousers up as far as they would go ; but what a fellow wanted to make him a real circus actor was a long pair of white cotton stockings , and I never knew a fellow that got a pair ; I heard of many a fellow who was said to have got a pair ; but when you came down to the fact , they vanished like ghosts when you try to verify them", "context_len": 89}}}, "comforter": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "He worked for their father , and for them , and very hard too , sometimes , but he was still their playfellow , George 's guide , Gus ' friend , little Aggie 's comforter , and singer of songs , and teller of stories to all", "context_len": 48}}}, "mythology": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Her stern gallery was upheld by a row of gilt figures representing that hero of mythology , Master Atlas , of whom my father used to speak ; while over the gallery towered three enormous lanterns , and above everything was a maze of spars and rigging that confused me not a little , though before long I was well acquainted with the names of all of them", "context_len": 68}}}, "perpetually": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "ABOUT the time fate cursed him with a granted prayer in those boots , my boy was deep in the reading of a book about Grecian mythology which he found perpetually fascinating ; he read it over and over without ever thinking of stopping merely because he had already been through it twenty or thirty times", "context_len": 56}}}, "gods": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 58, "context": "His influence and ascendency seemed firmly established now in the country of the Greeks , and in due time he returned to Macedon , and celebrated at \u00c6g\u00e6 , which was at this time his capital , the establishment and confirmation of his power , by games , shows , spectacles , illuminations , and sacrifices to the gods , offered on a scale of the greatest pomp and magnificence", "context_len": 70}}}, "goddesses": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 95, "context": "The big drawing-room had been shut up for many years ; the Queen-Anne chairs and china-cabinets were swathed in wrappers , and the ornaments put away in boxes ; but sometimes the children would steal in and open the shutters to look at the portraits which hung upon the oak-panelled walls -- stately gentlemen with wigs and lace frills , whose eyes seemed to follow you about the room ; haughty dames with powdered hair and patches ; stiff little girls in hoops and mittens , and pretty young ladies attired as shepherdesses or classic goddesses , with cupids and nymphs in the background", "context_len": 104}}}, "demigods": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It had pictures of all the gods and goddesses , demigods and heroes ; and he tried to make poems upon their various characters and exploits", "context_len": 26}}}, "exploits": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 6, "context": "These tales are narrations of the exploits and adventures of certain great warriors at the siege of Troy -- a siege which lasted ten years -- and they are written with so much beauty and force , they contain such admirable delineations of character , and such graphic and vivid descriptions of romantic adventures , and picturesque and striking scenes , that they have been admired in every age by all who have learned to understand the language in which they are written", "context_len": 83}}}, "coveted": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 42, "context": "She could hardly believe it , hardly believe that in spite of her enforced absence from school , in spite of her inability to hand in her compositions for so many weeks , she had been the one to receive this much coveted opportunity , and that she was not only free to bestow it upon her own little country-woman , but that her own credit would redound to that of Miss Ashton", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "Ben Stone , whose appearance in Oakdale had been so unfavorable , whose days there had been so filled with trouble and strife , found himself the hero of the village and the coveted friend of those lads who had once regarded him with doubt and aversion", "context_len": 47}}}, "chariot": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 11, "context": "He had a notion that if he could get up a chariot by sawing down the sides of a store-box for the body , and borrowing the hind-wheels of the baby 's willow wagon , and then , drawn by the family dog Tip at a mad gallop , come suddenly whirling round the corner of the school-house , wearing spangled circus-tights and bearing Apollo 's bow and shaft , while a silken scarf which he had seen in a bureau-drawer at home blew gallantly out behind him , it would have a fine effect with the boys", "context_len": 98}}}, "sawing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "He had a notion that if he could get up a chariot by sawing down the sides of a store-box for the body , and borrowing the hind-wheels of the baby 's willow wagon , and then , drawn by the family dog Tip at a mad gallop , come suddenly whirling round the corner of the school-house , wearing spangled circus-tights and bearing Apollo 's bow and shaft , while a silken scarf which he had seen in a bureau-drawer at home blew gallantly out behind him , it would have a fine effect with the boys", "context_len": 98}}}, "spangled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "He had a notion that if he could get up a chariot by sawing down the sides of a store-box for the body , and borrowing the hind-wheels of the baby 's willow wagon , and then , drawn by the family dog Tip at a mad gallop , come suddenly whirling round the corner of the school-house , wearing spangled circus-tights and bearing Apollo 's bow and shaft , while a silken scarf which he had seen in a bureau-drawer at home blew gallantly out behind him , it would have a fine effect with the boys", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Once there were two chariots -- one held the band in red-and-blue uniforms , and was drawn by eighteen piebald horses ; and the other was drawn by a troop of Shetland ponies , and carried in a vast mythical sea-shell little boys in spangled tights and little girls in the gauze skirts and wings of fairies", "context_len": 57}}}, "silken": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 72, "context": "He had a notion that if he could get up a chariot by sawing down the sides of a store-box for the body , and borrowing the hind-wheels of the baby 's willow wagon , and then , drawn by the family dog Tip at a mad gallop , come suddenly whirling round the corner of the school-house , wearing spangled circus-tights and bearing Apollo 's bow and shaft , while a silken scarf which he had seen in a bureau-drawer at home blew gallantly out behind him , it would have a fine effect with the boys", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "If Miss Maggie do n't object , maybe he 'd lie a bit on your knee , and let you feel his nice long ears and silken hair", "context_len": 28}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "But he restrained himself with an effort , and his tone was silken as he replied :", "context_len": 17}}}, "robbers": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 35, "context": "I have noticed that men do not undertake half so many things as boys do ; and instead of wanting to be circus-actors and Indians , and soldiers , and boat-drivers , and politicians and robbers , and to run off , and go in swimming all the time , and out hunting and walnutting , they keep to a very few things , and are glad then if they can do them", "context_len": 73}}}, "outlaws": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "Some of the fellows wished to be highway robbers and outlaws ; one who intended to be a pirate afterwards got so far in a maritime career as to invent a steam-engine governor now in use on the seagoing steamers ; my boy was content to be simply a god , the god of poetry and sunshine", "context_len": 57}}}, "pirate": {"freq": 116, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "Some of the fellows wished to be highway robbers and outlaws ; one who intended to be a pirate afterwards got so far in a maritime career as to invent a steam-engine governor now in use on the seagoing steamers ; my boy was content to be simply a god , the god of poetry and sunshine", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 108, "word_index": 38, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "maritime": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Some of the fellows wished to be highway robbers and outlaws ; one who intended to be a pirate afterwards got so far in a maritime career as to invent a steam-engine governor now in use on the seagoing steamers ; my boy was content to be simply a god , the god of poetry and sunshine", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "In double-column line ahead the greyhounds of the sea tore to meet the approaching troopships , then , at a signal from the senior officer , the destroyers `` broke line '' , tearing hither and thither seemingly without order or reason -- zigzagging , pirouetting , and crossing each others ' bows as if participating in an intricate maritime dance", "context_len": 61}}}, "invent": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 43, "context": "Who but she could climb to the very highest branch of the tallest tree ? Who but she could swing so high that she seemed almost to turn a somersault in the air before she came down again ? Who but she could invent the most daring games ? And then , when all other things failed , who but she could tell such weird stories ? Her eyes shone ; her lips were wreathed in smiles", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "They each had a rubber doll and the child would dance them up and down and make them turn somersaults and stand on their heads , and invent every sort of grotesque action", "context_len": 33}}}, "seagoing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Some of the fellows wished to be highway robbers and outlaws ; one who intended to be a pirate afterwards got so far in a maritime career as to invent a steam-engine governor now in use on the seagoing steamers ; my boy was content to be simply a god , the god of poetry and sunshine", "context_len": 57}}}, "failing": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 24, "context": "Not but what he had full three hours to spare , only the Allyns , who were invited to luncheon at the Castle , failing to reach there before the arrival of the brake , he felt that nothing but his own timely precaution had spared him a similar disappointment", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "He was always in demand , notwithstanding this failing , -- the economical captains never hesitating to go one hand short when they had Flour ( alias James Peterson ) for cook , as he was always ready to lend a hand , and was worth three common men in bad weather", "context_len": 52}}}, "stated": {"freq": 67, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "In the Boy 's Town they had regular games and plays , which came and went in a stated order", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 2, "context": "She further stated that , upon awaking , she had again gone aloft to take another long and careful look round , but had seen nothing more of our pursuers , and was therefore inclined to believe that we were now reasonably safe , provided , of course , that we did all our travelling at night , kept a sharp look-out , and were careful not to allow ourselves to be fallen in with by other users of the river", "context_len": 81}}}, "skilful": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 97, "context": "His mother was perplexed at his case , unknowing what had befallen him , and bethought herself that belike he was sick ; so she came up to him and questioned him , saying , `` O my son , an thou feel aught of pain or otherwhat , tell me , that I may go fetch thee a physician , more by token there is presently in the city a physician from the land of the Arabs , whom the Sultan hath sent to bring hither , and report saith of him that he is exceeding skilful ; so [ tell me ] if thou art sick , that I may go and call him to thee", "context_len": 118}}}, "pocketful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "As my boy was skilful at marbles , he was able to start out in the morning with his toy , or the marble he shot with , and a commy , or a brown marble of the Lowest value , and come home at night with a pocketful of white-alleys and blood-alleys , striped plasters find bull's-eyes , and crystals , clear and clouded", "context_len": 65}}}, "plasters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "As my boy was skilful at marbles , he was able to start out in the morning with his toy , or the marble he shot with , and a commy , or a brown marble of the Lowest value , and come home at night with a pocketful of white-alleys and blood-alleys , striped plasters find bull's-eyes , and crystals , clear and clouded", "context_len": 65}}}, "crystals": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 97, "context": "But still she went on , but only slowly , because of the great press of people around her ; and suddenly an icy wind passed over them , and all fell to the earth shivering and terror-stricken , for they had never felt cold before , and they looked up to the mountain , and lo ! upon the very summit , at the door of the deserted wigwam , stood a terrible figure , clothed in white , and having a face as white as his robes , and his hair was like the long crystals that hang from the roofs of caves that the water goes through , and his eyes were like two great diamonds , white , yet blazing like the sun", "context_len": 127}}}, "gambling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "His gambling was not approved of at home , but it was allowed him because of the hardness of his heart , I suppose , and because it was not thought well to keep him up too strictly ; and I suspect it would have been useless to forbid his playing for keeps , though he came to have a bad conscience about it before he gave it up", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Oh , probably Gerhart wanted him to go to some sporty gambling affair", "context_len": 14}}}, "hardness": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 26, "context": "But there was a something , very difficult to define , in the expression of these people that I did not at all like , a hardness about the mouth , and a cruel glint in the eyes -- especially of the men -- which looked at me in a manner that suggested all sorts of unpleasant possibilities , and excited within me a distinct longing to be almost anywhere rather than where I was", "context_len": 75}}}, "forbid": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 48, "context": "His gambling was not approved of at home , but it was allowed him because of the hardness of his heart , I suppose , and because it was not thought well to keep him up too strictly ; and I suspect it would have been useless to forbid his playing for keeps , though he came to have a bad conscience about it before he gave it up", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 10, "context": "And if need comes you should hide -- which God forbid -- you see this little stair in the corner ? It leads up to the great attic that is full of lumber , where you could play hide-and-seek with a regiment ; and were you pressed there -- see '' -- and she ran lightly up the stair and pushed open the door into the lumber-room", "context_len": 67}}}, "hollowed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "There were three kinds of games at marbles which the boys played : one with a long ring marked out on the ground , and a base some distance off , which you began to shoot from ; another with a round ring , whose line formed the base ; and another with holes , three or five , hollowed in the earth at equal distances from each other , which was called knucks", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Sure enough just ahead of them was a hollowed place in the bank which might easily be called a cave", "context_len": 20}}}, "distances": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 65, "context": "There were three kinds of games at marbles which the boys played : one with a long ring marked out on the ground , and a base some distance off , which you began to shoot from ; another with a round ring , whose line formed the base ; and another with holes , three or five , hollowed in the earth at equal distances from each other , which was called knucks", "context_len": 74}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The frequent removals of towns to new sites , lying at short distances only , may be easily explained by the unhealthiness of the old site , produced by the constant accumulation of refuse and filth around the towns , which never had anything like sewers or efficient regulations of sanitary police", "context_len": 52}}}, "shots": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 21, "context": "Sir Richard , finding himself in this condition after fifteen hours ' hard fighting , and having received about eight hundred shots from great guns , besides various assaults from the enemy , and seeing , moreover , no way by which he might prevent his ship falling into the hands of the Spanish , commanded the master gunner , whom he knew was a most resolute man , to split and sink the ship", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "In obedience to an order from the captain our men cast loose a pair of swivel guns , for it was evident that the buccaneer was getting out of range , and her shots no longer disturbed the water", "context_len": 39}}}, "twitch": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "Aunt Helen fondly imagined herself to be a great disciplinarian , but her own lively youth was still such a recent remembrance that her eyes were wont to twinkle and the corners of her mouth to twitch in the middle of her severest scoldings , and the children always had a feeling that so long as they did not do anything rude or wrong , or run into any very imminent danger , their escapades were secretly condoned by their aunt , who admired pluck and spirit , however much she might feel it incumbent upon her to lecture them", "context_len": 100}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was the arm which was grasping the middle of the bow , and the hurt caused an involuntary twitch that spoiled the aim of the archer", "context_len": 27}}}, "chapped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "As the game began in the raw cold of the earliest spring , every boy had chapped hands , and nearly every one had the skin worn off the knuckle of his middle finger from resting it on the ground when he shot", "context_len": 43}}}, "knuckle": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "As the game began in the raw cold of the earliest spring , every boy had chapped hands , and nearly every one had the skin worn off the knuckle of his middle finger from resting it on the ground when he shot", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "For my part , I would n't be in such a hurry to knuckle to people who waited seventeen years to find whether I was in the land of the living before they said , `` How d 'ye do", "context_len": 40}}}, "effeminate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "You could use a knuckle-dabster of fur or cloth to rest your hand on , but it was considered effeminate , and in the excitement you were apt to forget it , anyway", "context_len": 33}}}, "incessant": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 21, "context": "Nicholas , and the danger that would arise from the construction of the battery was at once perceived , and an incessant fire opened upon it from the guns on the wall round the grand master 's palace", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Frequent stoppages were the only respite to the otherwise incessant jolting", "context_len": 11}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Owing to incessant rains , I could not explore the sites to their full extent , but found a flat mound south of St", "context_len": 24}}}, "blackbird": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Below them the pink apple-blossom spread like a rosy cloud against the bluest of skies , and a blackbird in a neighbouring bush was trilling his loudest", "context_len": 27}}}, "edged": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 14, "context": "A great many points were always coming up : whether a boy took-up or edged beyond the very place where his toy lay when he shot ; whether he knuckled down , or kept his hand on the ground in shooting ; whether , when another boy 's toy drove one marble against another and knocked both out of the ring , he holloed `` Fen doubs ! '' before the other fellow holloed `` Doubs ! '' whether a marble was in or out of the ring , and whether the umpire 's decision was just or not", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "From the bottom of the bait can Barry took a folded square of muslin and the sharp edged spearhead", "context_len": 19}}}, "knuckled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "A great many points were always coming up : whether a boy took-up or edged beyond the very place where his toy lay when he shot ; whether he knuckled down , or kept his hand on the ground in shooting ; whether , when another boy 's toy drove one marble against another and knocked both out of the ring , he holloed `` Fen doubs ! '' before the other fellow holloed `` Doubs ! '' whether a marble was in or out of the ring , and whether the umpire 's decision was just or not", "context_len": 99}}}, "holloed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "They thought more of the marker than of the drummer ; for the marker carried a little flag , and when the officers holloed out , `` By the left flank -- left ! Wheel ! '' he set his flag against his shoulder , and stood marking time with his feet till the soldiers all got by him , and then he ran up to the front rank , with the flag fluttering behind him", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "If ever a boy holloed something at him when he was out with his crier 's bell , he turned and ran as hard as he could , and as if from the constable himself", "context_len": 35}}}, "doubs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "umpire": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 92, "context": "A great many points were always coming up : whether a boy took-up or edged beyond the very place where his toy lay when he shot ; whether he knuckled down , or kept his hand on the ground in shooting ; whether , when another boy 's toy drove one marble against another and knocked both out of the ring , he holloed `` Fen doubs ! '' before the other fellow holloed `` Doubs ! '' whether a marble was in or out of the ring , and whether the umpire 's decision was just or not", "context_len": 99}}}, "twirling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}}}, "sidewalks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "This helped a boy to wear out his boots very rapidly , but that was what his boots were made for , just as the sidewalks were made for the boys ' marble-rings , and a citizen 's character for cleverness or meanness was fixed by his walking round or over the rings", "context_len": 53}}}, "cleverness": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 58, "context": "She kept a severe eye on the small children , patted them firmly on the back when they choked , and refused to allow them to grab at the cake , regulating their tea according to her own notions of what was good for them , and turning stolen lumps of sugar out of their pockets with the cleverness of a detective", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Of course they might by great cleverness trace him until they found him , and then they would tear him from her arms ; but never , until this happened , would she relinquish him", "context_len": 35}}}, "batters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Two-cornered cat was played by four boys : two to bat , and two behind the batters to catch and pitch", "context_len": 21}}}, "hitting": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 40, "context": "But , after a few blows had been exchanged , Dick 's rising temper supplied the strength that had been lessened by hardship , while Astbury , unwieldy and out of condition , soon lost his breath , and , hitting out wildly , gave Dick an opening for a good straight left-hander , that sent his opponent crashing on the ground", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "HITTING THE BULL \u2019 S-EYE", "context_len": 5}}}, "undertaken": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Foot-ball was always played with a bladder , and it came in season with the cold weather when the putting up of beef began ; the business was practically regarded by the boys as one undertaken to supply them with bladders for foot-balls", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 29, "context": "He declared that he was absolutely unable to give any names whatever of those concerned in the plot , save those of the two overseers , as these had undertaken the work of suborning the warders and guards , though he admitted that he had on several occasions spoken to slaves as the gangs were on their way back to the prison , and had told them to be prepared to take part in a plan that was on foot for their rescue from slavery", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 127, "context": "each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the operation was undertaken", "context_len": 128}}}, "bladders": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "Foot-ball was always played with a bladder , and it came in season with the cold weather when the putting up of beef began ; the business was practically regarded by the boys as one undertaken to supply them with bladders for foot-balls", "context_len": 43}}}, "touches": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 51, "context": "Now he was standing in admiration before the great oak chair of State beneath the organ gallery , and now nothing loath he mounts the steps that lead to it and runs a finger along the curves of its elaborate carving , and then , with a most reverent air , touches the embroidered cross and garter with which it is decorated", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 75, "context": "The old lady nodded and smiled , as if she were glad to see them so happy , but Nonesuch seemed not only surprised , but displeased , at finding himself in such jolly company ; and , after some shaking of his head and putting back of his ears , stood stock still in the middle of the road ; nor could all Aunt Patty 's coaxing or scolding , or even some gentle touches of the whip , persuade him to go on , till the whole party were out of sight", "context_len": 94}}}, "thrills": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "Here we present three crackerjack stories of baseball , football , and basketball , written in the vernacular of the boy of to-day , full of action , suspense and thrills , in language every boy will understand , and which we know will be enthusiastically endorsed by all boys", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Had he felt any further doubt it was solved on the instant by a repetition of the cry , this time clearer , and plainly to be interpreted into that agonising appeal that thrills the hearts of weak and strong alike -- the one word `` Help ! ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "inmost": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Something thrills through him then from the heart of her inmost being that makes him feel kin with her , and cousin to all her dumb children of the grass and trees", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Bridget in her inmost heart hoped he had the worst of it", "context_len": 12}}}, "leaps": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 7, "context": "She was up the yew-tree in two leaps and a bound , and by crawling along an overhanging bough , clinging to a branch , and making a long arm , she managed to seize puss by the scruff of her neck , and release her from her dangerous position", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The country-jake comes stumbling awkwardly into the ring , but he is perfectly sober , and he boldly leaps astride the mule , which tries all its arts to shake him off , plunging , kicking , rearing", "context_len": 38}}}, "plunges": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Then , at a signal repeated four times , they throw some of it into the river , and every man , at a like signal , plunges into the water , to pick up four stones from the bottom", "context_len": 40}}}, "sweeter": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 25, "context": "If you had come to see mother sometimes -- mother , who is just as much a lady as she ever was , who is sweeter and more beautiful than she ever was -- you might have done us a great service , and I could have loved you , oh ! so dearly ; but you have forsaken us , because we are no longer in your set", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 39, "context": "The Scholarship money would have done more good to a poorer girl '' -- and here Sir John had quickly to suppress a sigh , for was he not thinking of Kitty -- Kitty , who had never looked sweeter than during this evening of defeat , who had never , never been nearer to his heart ?", "context_len": 58}}}, "bliss": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 87, "context": "Yet there was a wondrous beauty still lingering over them ; they seemed etherialised -- as if an angel 's smile had last stirred their lines , when the spirit went forth , and left its imprint of wonder , joy , and awe thereon ; and Alfgar instinctively turned from them to the blue depths of heaven above , where a few stars were visible , although dimmed by the moonlight ; and he seemed to trace his beloved Bertric 's passage to the realms of bliss", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "It would have been richly enough for him to pass the least word with them ; a look , a smile from them would have been bliss ; but he shrank out of their way ; and once when he met them in the street , and they seemed to be going to speak to him , he ran so that they could not", "context_len": 64}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "If dear Otho thought that he might possibly be permitted to watch over his Mother , and help to make her happy , and be one of the first to welcome her to bliss , -- perhaps the real bitterness of death would for him seem taken away", "context_len": 48}}}, "foretaste": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "The boys liked to be at the circus early so as to make sure of the grand entry of the performers into the ring , where they caracoled round on horseback , and gave a delicious foretaste of the wonders to come", "context_len": 42}}}, "reconciled": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 63, "context": "' I should be Juliet , you know , stretched out on the bier , and Archie would have to be Romeo , and come in , and think I was dead , and stab himself , and then of course I should wake up and stab myself , too ; and Peggy and Bobby could be the Montagues and Capulets , getting reconciled over our dead bodies", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "And this thought made him the more reconciled to the apparent injustice of which he was the victim , and he prayed for his father , that God would enlighten him with the true light", "context_len": 35}}}, "deathless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "It is all like some glimpse , some foretaste of the heavenly time when the earth and her sons shall be reconciled in a deathless love , and they shall not be thankless , nor she a step-mother any more", "context_len": 40}}}, "thankless": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "It is all like some glimpse , some foretaste of the heavenly time when the earth and her sons shall be reconciled in a deathless love , and they shall not be thankless , nor she a step-mother any more", "context_len": 40}}}, "drawback": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "About the only drawback to going barefoot was stumping your toe , which you were pretty sure to do when you first took off your shoes and before you had got used to your new running weight", "context_len": 37}}}, "stumping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "The terrified children took to their heels , and I do n't really know what might have happened , only fortunately the familiar figure of old Barlow , the village cobbler , appeared stumping down the road in the distance", "context_len": 40}}}, "involved": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 46, "context": "While things were in this state -- the Thebans having understood that Alexander had been killed at the north , and supposing that , at all events , if this report should not be true , he was , without doubt , still far away , involved in contentions with the barbarian nations , from which it was not to be expected that he could be very speedily extricated -- the whole city was suddenly thrown into consternation by the report that a large Macedonian army was approaching from the north , with Alexander at its head , and that it was , in fact , close upon them", "context_len": 109}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "In 1706 , when expecting to become involved in a conflict with the Chicasa and Alibamu Indians , the Tonica tribe , or a part of it , fled southward to the towns of the Huma , and massacred a number of these near the site where New Orleans was built afterwards ( French , Hist", "context_len": 56}}}, "tag": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "With the races came the other plays which involved running , like hide-and-go-whoop , and tag , and dog-on-wood , and horse , which I dare say the boys of other times and other wheres know by different names", "context_len": 39}}}, "wheres": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "With the races came the other plays which involved running , like hide-and-go-whoop , and tag , and dog-on-wood , and horse , which I dare say the boys of other times and other wheres know by different names", "context_len": 39}}}, "molest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 59, "context": "There was a town pump there for you , so that you would not have to go into the house for a drink when you got thirsty , and perhaps be set to doing something ; and there were plenty of boards for teeter and see-saw ; and somehow that neighborhood seemed to understand boys , and did not molest them in any way", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "Her heart was full of solemn yet not sad thoughts ; peace , sweet peace , was the subject of her meditations , and she thought with gratitude of Him who had hitherto preserved Mercia from the foe , who had indeed for nearly two years ceased to molest England", "context_len": 50}}}, "whirligig": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "In a vacant lot behind one of the houses there was a whirligig , that you could ride on and get sick in about a minute ; it was splendid", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "There were foaming cataracts here and there among the rocks , just like Niagara on a small scale , and there were dear little quiet pools at the edges , where the still water was overhung by ferns and rushes , that sheltered caddice-worms , and boat-flies , and whirligig water-beetles , and all sorts of other delights for the collection", "context_len": 61}}}, "broached": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}}}, "withhold": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "My boy distinctly remembered that under one of these trees his elder brother first broached to him that awful scheme of reform about fibbing , and applied to their own lives the moral of `` The Trippings of Tom Pepper ; '' he remembered how a conviction of the righteousness of the scheme sank into his soul , and he could not withhold his consent", "context_len": 65}}}, "conclave": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "' And when God took her , and we thought our hearts would break , we all sat in a conclave together , and we determined to follow our mother 's rule , and to do the right because it was right and because we loved her", "context_len": 47}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "was actually present at the solemn conclave held by us six M", "context_len": 12}}}, "feasibility": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Under the same tree , and very likely at the same time , a solemn conclave of boys , all the boys there were , discussed the feasibility of tying a tin can to a dog 's tail , and seeing how he would act", "context_len": 45}}}, "tying": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 47, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "He tried frantically to burst the cords that secured his arms , but the tying had been too securely done", "context_len": 20}}}, "suspicions": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 9, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}}}, "lured": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "The lot finally fell to a young yellow dog belonging to one of the boys , and the owner at once ran home to get him , and easily lured him back to the other boys with flatteries and caresses", "context_len": 40}}}, "caresses": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "She went quickly across the room and never paused until she found herself in the woman 's arms , and showed her love by so many quaint and delicate little caresses , and had such a dainty and bewitching way about her , that no human could have held out against her", "context_len": 52}}}, "pretext": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "The flatteries and caresses were not needed , for a dog is always glad to go with boys , upon any pretext , and so far from thinking that he does them a favor , he feels himself greatly honored", "context_len": 40}}}, "engagement": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` I do not wish to dissuade you ; indeed , I can not myself see how you can get out of this most mistaken engagement , for the man has behaved well , and I am the first to acknowledge that ; but has it ever occurred to you that you do a man an absolute and terrible injustice when you marry him , loving with all your heart and soul another man ? Do you think it is fair to him ? Do n't you think he ought at least to know this ? ''", "context_len": 97}}}, "misgiving": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 56, "context": "The other boys did not ask how he came to know its name , or where he got it ; boys never ask anything that it would be well for them to know ; but they accepted his theory , and his further statement that it was of a mildness singularly adapted to learners , without misgiving", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 55, "context": "What a sleepless night I have passed , what anxiety , what excitement ! and yet how unlike is he to what I had imagined ! so timid , so petulant ! and that perpetual punning ! It matters not , however , -- his title to our services remains the same ! A strange misgiving is on my soul ; is it the shadow of approaching danger , or only the fear of it ? The Colonel gave me a strange meaning look as he passed me this morning , and said , You are early up , Miss Ratty ; I fear that your rest was broken last night", "context_len": 111}}}, "dissipated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "If there had been any question with the boys as to whether he would enter fully into the spirit of the affair , it must have been instantly dissipated by the dog 's behavior when he felt the loop tighten on his tail , and looked round to see what the matter was", "context_len": 53}}}, "tighten": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "If there had been any question with the boys as to whether he would enter fully into the spirit of the affair , it must have been instantly dissipated by the dog 's behavior when he felt the loop tighten on his tail , and looked round to see what the matter was", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "But on the other hand they could not tighten until the snake , lashing wildly about , could find some stump or tree to encircle with its tail and use it as a lever", "context_len": 34}}}, "whizzed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 1, "context": "It whizzed through the air , and drank the life blood of the poor beast , which bounded a few steps , staggered , and fell , when in a moment Alfgar ended its struggles by drawing his knife across its throat , while young Hermann , a true hunter by instinct , clapped his hands with joy", "context_len": 58}}}, "hurtled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "Springing on to a projecting ledge just outside one of the loopholes , Jim 's head was already above the level of the summit , and his outstretched arm was within a foot of the flagstaff , when something hurtled through the air , and , to Jim 's intense astonishment , a coil of rope fell heavily over his shoulders , and slipped to his waist", "context_len": 67}}}, "projectors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "As an experiment it was successful beyond the wildest dreams of its projectors , though it would have been a sort of relief if the dog had taken some other road , for variety , or had even reversed his course", "context_len": 41}}}, "reversed": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 71, "context": "Her Spanish crew we soon found snugly hidden away in spacious quarters beneath the lazaret ; and , as to the name on her stern , we found that the piece of wood on which it was carved and painted was reversible , having Virginia , New Orleans , carved on one side of it and Preciosa , Havana , on the other , and that it could be unbolted and reversed in a few minutes by lifting a couple of movable planks in the after cabin", "context_len": 87}}}, "impulse": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 71, "context": "It was in imagination , but his mental calculation was not far wrong , for within a few yards of where it might be expected , and not ten from where he was now sculling , he saw something roll up as it were to the surface , there was a gleam of white in the sunlit water , and then it was disappearing again , when , acting upon the impulse of the moment , Harry loosened his hold of the oar , took two steps forward over the thwarts , and leaped into the sea", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` How could I have left him ? And how could we be urgent to him to fly when we could not know what extraordinary impulse one of his virtue or courage may have had on his mind ? Forget not how he did answer to your entreaties , saying that he would not stir a foot , nor turn his back as though he repented he had been engaged in that great work , or were ashamed of the service of so glorious and great a God ! We could not seek to change such a resolve", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Quiet and self-contained and little given to impulse as he was , Seabrooke , when roused to anger or resentment , was a very lion in his wrath , and there was one thing which he could never tolerate or overlook , and that was any attempt to take an unfair advantage of him", "context_len": 54}}}, "ignore": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 65, "context": "One of his river-faring uncles was visiting with his family at the boy 's home when he laid out the scheme of his great fiction of `` Hamet el Zegri , '' and the kindly young aunt took an interest in it which he poorly rewarded a few months later , when she asked how the story was getting on , and he tried to ignore the whole matter , and showed such mortification at the mention of it that the poor lady was quite bewildered", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Harris should forbid Marie-Celeste to see him , or should just calmly ignore the fact that he was on board at all , it would be pretty hard to bear", "context_len": 30}}}, "powerless": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 50, "context": "Goaded by his words , the men made a general rush upon Gervaise , and , in spite of his desperate efforts , threw him on to the deck and bound him ; then the captain , seizing a heavy stick in his left hand , his right being still powerless , showered blows upon him until Gervaise almost lost consciousness", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 29, "context": "In the big castle , let us hope , since we may not know , that even crowned heads were resting easily , and that the level rays were powerless at that early hour to waken them to that sense of great uneasiness supposed to be inseparable from the lot of the nobly born", "context_len": 54}}}, "enthusiast": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Yet even the mothers were powerless in a case like that , and the enthusiast had to be left to his fate", "context_len": 22}}}, "lifeless": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "She shook her head rather sadly when she saw the pretty nets which were to stop the butterflies from flying about so happily , and the pins and tweezers which were to turn them into lifeless specimens in a glass box", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "They were bending over the lifeless cat that had been pierced through with an arrow , when a rustling among the branches and a low growl warned Bomba that the second jaguar had turned its attention to its new foes and was about to attack", "context_len": 45}}}, "touchingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Eventually he got well again , and lived to be a solemn warning to the boys ; he was touchingly distrustful of their advances for a time , but he finally forgot and forgave everything", "context_len": 35}}}, "distrustful": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Eventually he got well again , and lived to be a solemn warning to the boys ; he was touchingly distrustful of their advances for a time , but he finally forgot and forgave everything", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Although encouraged by this first successful meeting with the Creeks , the colonists knew so well the fickleness of the Indian character that they were distrustful of the steadiness of their promises , and thus sought to renew the friendly relations with them as often as possible", "context_len": 47}}}, "seriously": {"freq": 77, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 77, "word_index": 53, "context": "When I told her we had a three months ' vacation , she never said , `` How delightful ! '' as most girls would , but calmly inquired what I took up in the holidays , and when I groaned at the very thought of taking up anything , she said so seriously , `` But you do n't let your mind lie fallow for three whole months ? '' And then she sighed a little , and added , half to herself , `` Some girls would give all the world for such a chance to read", "context_len": 99}}}, "inventing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "So Maggie was told , and went capering round the room in frantic delight at the news , inventing , as usual , so many plans and pleasures that might fit in with this new arrangement , that Bessie was better satisfied than ever , and even forgave Uncle Ruthven the kiss", "context_len": 52}}}, "ingenious": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 88, "context": "It was a quiet spot , with long gardens leading to old-fashioned houses on the one side , and the tall palings of a cricket-field on the other -- just the spot where nobody would be likely to come along and make a disturbance , and so evidently Jones minor seemed to think , for he held Bobby pinned against the wall with one hand , while with the other he amused himself by tweaking his ears , pulling his hair , and any other tortures which his ingenious mind could suggest at the moment", "context_len": 95}}}, "beech": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "A grassy glade under a tall beech seemed intended by Nature for a summer-house , for there was a large flat stone in the middle , which served for a table , and a circle of little stones round , just high enough for seats , so that you might imagine Queen Mab and her fairies dined here on moonlight nights , with the squirrels for guests and the bats and owls for waiters", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Cut me a whip , then , -- a beech one ; you \u2019 ll find a good one on that sapling", "context_len": 22}}}, "launched": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 79, "context": "On the return of the knight with the news that it would be at least a fortnight before Genoa could fit out any galleys , and fearing that the pirates might at any moment put to sea , we procured some small Sardinian craft , and fitted them as fire ships ; with the captives we had rescued , and some Sard fishermen , we manned the three prizes , distributing the knights between them , and at night launched the fire ships against the corsairs , whose ships were crowded together", "context_len": 92}}}, "bulged": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "Then as his glance fell upon the people coming up the hill , the officers in their lead , his eyes bulged with terror , he grasped Bart 's arm , let out an unearthly yell of fear , and by sheer force carried Bart pell-mell down the other side of the hill with him", "context_len": 55}}}, "holloing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "Of course the only way was to climb for the coon , which would be out at the point of a high and slender limb , and would bite you awfully , even if the limb did not break under you , while the boys kept whooping and yelling and holloing out what to do , and Tip the dog just howled with excitement", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "It was a splendid gun , but it would not stand cocked long , and he was resting it on the wall of the fort , ready to fire when the storming-party came on , throwing sods and yelling and holloing ; and all at once his gun went off , and a cow that was grazing broadside to the fort gave a frightened bellow , and put up her tail , and started for home", "context_len": 76}}}, "esteemed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "Some few of the boys had humming-tops ; but though these pleased by their noise , they were not much esteemed , and could make no head against the good old turnip-shaped tops , solid and weighty , that you could wind up with a stout cotton cord , and launch with perfect aim from the flat button held between your fore finger and middle finger", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Soon afterward , Philadelphia \u2019 s most esteemed lawyer , Andrew Hamilton , arranged for him to print the laws and votes of the government", "context_len": 25}}}, "weighty": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "Some few of the boys had humming-tops ; but though these pleased by their noise , they were not much esteemed , and could make no head against the good old turnip-shaped tops , solid and weighty , that you could wind up with a stout cotton cord , and launch with perfect aim from the flat button held between your fore finger and middle finger", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "Aylmer the great , observed how depressed Florence was when she received letters from Dawlish , noted her feverish anxiety to deport herself well , to lead a life of excellent conduct , and , above all things , to struggle through the weighty themes which had to be mastered in order to win the great Scholarship", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Sir Aubrey Wentworth , forsooth , '' he cried , `` and not a word in writing to prove your right ! Nay , good sirs , I can not grant you your desires on so weighty a matter with so light a claim", "context_len": 45}}}, "cord": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 47, "context": "So rapt was he in the grandeur of the scene that he had forgotten all about the object of his journey , but he was brought back to the matter-of-fact present by a tremendous snatch which jerked his arm hanging over the side , and made the cord cut so violently into his hand that he was glad to give the line a twist and set it free to run for some distance before he began to check it a little", "context_len": 81}}}, "control": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 54, "context": "Ah , the old besetting sin , strengthened by long habit and indulgence , what a tyrant it had become , and how hard she had to struggle with it , how often was she overcome ! Yes , well might little Bessie be thankful that wise and tender teachers had taught her to control that passionate temper , which later might have proved such a misery to herself and her friends", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 80, "context": "It is not to be supposed that I was allowed to spin my yarn without interruption ; on the contrary , I was bombarded with a continuous fire of questions for the elucidation of points that I had failed to make quite clear ; and when I had finished the captain was pleased to express himself as perfectly satisfied with all that I had done , and that the loss of the Dolphin was due to causes entirely beyond my control", "context_len": 81}}}, "hoop": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "A bow kite could be made with half a barrel hoop carried over the top of a cross , but it was troublesome to make , and it did not fly very well , and somehow it was thought to look babyish ; but it was held in greater respect than the two-stick kite , which only the smallest boys played with , and which was made by fastening two sticks in the form of a cross", "context_len": 77}}}, "troublesome": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 22, "context": "A bow kite could be made with half a barrel hoop carried over the top of a cross , but it was troublesome to make , and it did not fly very well , and somehow it was thought to look babyish ; but it was held in greater respect than the two-stick kite , which only the smallest boys played with , and which was made by fastening two sticks in the form of a cross", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Well , the gentle Sweyn has taken your evasion very much to heart , and earnestly desires to repossess himself of your person ; but for this , my easiest plan would have been to rid myself of so troublesome a witness in a more speedy manner , and you might ere this have fed the fishes of the Thames", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 96, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}}}, "fastening": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 68, "context": "A bow kite could be made with half a barrel hoop carried over the top of a cross , but it was troublesome to make , and it did not fly very well , and somehow it was thought to look babyish ; but it was held in greater respect than the two-stick kite , which only the smallest boys played with , and which was made by fastening two sticks in the form of a cross", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "They took the iron from the spars , and Joe , who had worked in a blacksmith shop , took it over to the main land to a shop , and made their fastening", "context_len": 34}}}, "jeers": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 67, "context": "Her missile flew far above the man \u2019 s head , and , running backwards in a vain endeavour to catch it , he fell heavily into a large trough of pigs \u2019 mash , where , half-stunned by the force of his fall , he lay wallowing in the sticky mess , till he was helped out by his comrades , to the accompaniment of their jeers and laughter", "context_len": 70}}}, "balanced": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 7, "context": "The favorite kite , the kite that balanced best , took the wind best , and flew best , and that would stand all day when you got it up , was the house kite , which was made of three sticks , and shaped nearly in the form of the gable of a gambrel-roofed house , only smaller at the base than at the point where the roof would begin", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "For once in this little fit of down-heartedness , she seems to have somewhat lost her usual balanced view of the comparative unimportance of seemingly successful results", "context_len": 27}}}, "outline": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 99, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}}}, "taut": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "The outline of all these kites was given , and the sticks stayed in place by a string carried taut from stick to stick , which was notched at the ends to hold it ; sometimes the sticks were held with a tack at the point of crossing , and sometimes they were mortised into one another ; but this was apt to weaken them", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "If there was not much surface wind you might have to run a little way , but as soon as the kite caught the upper currents it straightened itself , pulled the twine taut , and steadily mounted , while you gave it more and more twine ; if the breeze was strong , the cord burned as it ran through your hands ; till at last the kite stood still in the sky , at such a height that the cord holding it sometimes melted out of sight in the distance", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "But the implement must have been injured in the fall of the tree , for as Bomba drew it taut the frayed string broke with an ominous snap", "context_len": 28}}}, "notched": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "The outline of all these kites was given , and the sticks stayed in place by a string carried taut from stick to stick , which was notched at the ends to hold it ; sometimes the sticks were held with a tack at the point of crossing , and sometimes they were mortised into one another ; but this was apt to weaken them", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Pools , swelled by the recent rain , were frequent , some of them so deep that it was necessary to cross them by notched trunks of trees , the crude bridges of the jungle", "context_len": 35}}}, "ends": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 70, "context": "In my collection I have them varying from almost straight to a shape like that of the letter V , nearly straight , curved , plain , ornamented , some with strange carvings , and all varying according to different sections of the country and individual tribes , each having its own make or style , showing respectively rough crudeness or considerable finish , and being especially characteristic in the ends or points -- all of which a boomerang connoisseur will distinguish at once , and locate as to tribe and section", "context_len": 92}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 7, "context": "But , though all is well that ends well , Peggy was still so tear-stained and upset that she did not feel equal to facing her school-fellows , however sympathetic they might be , so she escaped with Lilian through the side-door into the street , feeling she would never be really happy or at ease again till she was back in that haven of home where she was always appreciated and understood , and pouring out her troubles to Aunt Helen in the sanctuary of the Rose Parlour", "context_len": 89}}}, "mortised": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "The outline of all these kites was given , and the sticks stayed in place by a string carried taut from stick to stick , which was notched at the ends to hold it ; sometimes the sticks were held with a tack at the point of crossing , and sometimes they were mortised into one another ; but this was apt to weaken them", "context_len": 65}}}, "weaken": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 63, "context": "The outline of all these kites was given , and the sticks stayed in place by a string carried taut from stick to stick , which was notched at the ends to hold it ; sometimes the sticks were held with a tack at the point of crossing , and sometimes they were mortised into one another ; but this was apt to weaken them", "context_len": 65}}}, "pasted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "But after the kite was pasted , and dried in the sun , or behind the kitchen stove , if you were in very much of a hurry ( and you nearly always were ) , it had to be hung , with belly-bands and tail-bands ; that is , with strings carried from stick to stick over the face and at the bottom , to attach the cord for flying it and to fasten on the tail by", "context_len": 79}}}, "paste": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 71, "context": "She looked lovingly at the cows , lazily chewing their cud in the sunshine ; she felt sorry for her step-mother , as she strove to woo slumber to Polly 's wakeful eyes with the same lullaby which had done duty for the whole six ; she even found it in her heart to kiss Lemuel , who , with his ready talent for the unusual , was busily cramming mud paste into the seams of the little trunk which held her worldly all", "context_len": 84}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Hold him tight , Gerhart ; I 'm going to paste him another", "context_len": 13}}}, "mixing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 60, "context": "Tresham was last night saying to me , that it seemed to him it would not be a difficult matter for one who spoke Turkish well , to issue at night on the other side of the town , and to make his way round to the battery , disguised of course as a Turkish soldier , and then , mixing with the artillery men , to drive a spike into one of the touch holes", "context_len": 76}}}, "courted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "There was one jealous old fellow , a pet rooster and a great beauty , who would take his supper from no hand but that of his mistress ; and flying on the bench beside her , he courted her notice and a supper by himself", "context_len": 46}}}, "attach": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 66, "context": "But after the kite was pasted , and dried in the sun , or behind the kitchen stove , if you were in very much of a hurry ( and you nearly always were ) , it had to be hung , with belly-bands and tail-bands ; that is , with strings carried from stick to stick over the face and at the bottom , to attach the cord for flying it and to fasten on the tail by", "context_len": 79}}}, "pitching": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "Charlie , leaping into the water , caught at the child , but , missing it , grasped one wheel , which upset the cart in an instant , pitching the screaming child into the water , from which it was instantly rescued by Charlie , who , however , had to swim for it", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "It was due to a great catch which Phil made in the deciding championship game , almost as much as to Tom 's wonderful pitching , that Randall had the banner , and Captain Holly Cross , of the eleven , had marked Phil for one of his men during the season which was about to open on the gridiron", "context_len": 60}}}, "darting": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 29, "context": "With his body bent , his head thrust forward , and his nostrils working , he slowly passed along the inner face of the crowd , his shifty eyes darting hither and thither , until his gaze happened to fall upon one of the individuals for whom he was looking , when he would come to a halt , appear to be following a scent , and finally stretch forth his spear and lightly smite some man or woman on the head with it", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then , with a quick , steady pull , he drew the darting , curving , shining fish from its home , and landed it among the weeds on the shore", "context_len": 31}}}, "dart": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 101, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 51, "context": "At first sight nothing seems more frail than a herring or mackerel net , one of those slight pieces of mesh-work that , in a continuation of lengths perhaps half-a-mile long , is let down into the sea to float with the tide , ready for the shoals of fish that dart against it as it forms a filmy wall across their way", "context_len": 63}}}, "twists": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "And while he holds that slippery throat with the clutch of desperation , as the snake twists and writhes and tries to bite , it may be well , for the benefit of those who have not read the preceding volumes of this series , to tell who Bomba was and what had been his adventures up to the time at which this story opens", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He twists my arm round , too , and hammers at it , and he keeps a buckle-strap in his pocket specially for me , so he says", "context_len": 28}}}, "streamer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "A very pretty tail was made by tying twists of paper across a string a foot apart , till there were enough to balance the kite ; but this sort of tail was apt to get tangled , and the best tail was made of a long streamer of cotton rags , with a gay tuft of dog-fennel at the end", "context_len": 61}}}, "tuft": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 21, "context": "When my turn came I leapt with all the buoyancy of youth on to the lowermost crag , grasping an overhanging tuft of reeds and grass to gain the next step ; but the reeds were as sharp as a razor , and before I realized it the palm of my hand was cut to the bone , and the blood poured in a stream down my arm and over my doublet", "context_len": 72}}}, "experimental": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Dog-fennel was added or taken away till just the right weight was got ; and when this was done , after several experimental tests , the kite was laid flat on its face in the middle of the road , or on a long stretch of smooth grass ; the bands were arranged , and the tail stretched carefully out behind , where it would not catch on bushes", "context_len": 69}}}, "tests": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Dog-fennel was added or taken away till just the right weight was got ; and when this was done , after several experimental tests , the kite was laid flat on its face in the middle of the road , or on a long stretch of smooth grass ; the bands were arranged , and the tail stretched carefully out behind , where it would not catch on bushes", "context_len": 69}}}, "unwound": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "You unwound a great length of twine , running backward , and letting the twine slip swiftly through your hands till you had run enough out ; then you seized the ball , and with one look over your shoulder to see that all was right , started swiftly forward", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The boy slowly unwound a long piece of string from the package and brought to view a piece of soap", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The coils unwound and fell in a heap at the boy \u2019 s feet", "context_len": 14}}}, "gracefully": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 25, "context": "What a noisy , merry , frolicksome stream this was , gurgling and splashing , rushing and tumbling in its rocky bed ; now leaping gracefully in a miniature waterfall over some narrow ledge , now rippling and singing about the roots of the trees and over the pebbles that lay in its course , now flashing in the sunlight , and now hiding in a crevice of the rocks as if it were playing at Bopeep", "context_len": 77}}}, "fennel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Here , they also found four herbs or roots , which sang and disclosed their virtues : First , Pasaw , the rattle-snake root ; Second , Micoweanochaw red-root ; Third , Sowatchko , which grows like wild fennel ; and Fourth , Eschalapootchke , little tobacco", "context_len": 47}}}, "roasting": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "More chipping , then more roasting , and the growing boomerang was now and again tossed carelessly on the ground just to see how it would act , while he glanced at it sideways , gave it a poke with his foot , and reminded me of a sedate old tom cat playing with a mouse", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "Do you go over to our house , and ask John to give you some turnip-seed , and sow it on that ground you burned over when you was roasting Joe Griffin , and see what turnips will grow there ; you can hack the seed in with the hoe ; turnips will sell first rate in the West Indies ; I \u2019 ll tell them they are Yankee yams", "context_len": 70}}}, "peaceably": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 8, "context": "Many of these settlers have acquired their lands peaceably , but others by the strong arms of their ancestors in periods of ancient strife ; and these have been allowed to keep their possessions for generations , so that if they did not retain their heathen customs we might forget they were not Englishmen", "context_len": 54}}}, "debate": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 3, "context": "Besides the wild debate of the rival excellences of the different kites , there were always quarrels from getting the strings crossed ; for , as the boys got their kites up , they drew together for company and for an easier comparison of their merits", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The chief and principal warriors had annual meetings in the public square of some central town , on public affairs ; they drank \u00e1ssi , exchanged tobacco , and then proceeded to debate", "context_len": 33}}}, "quarrels": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 33, "context": "The first command was obeyed at once , but Maggie did not find the second quite so easy ; and she lay awake for some time imagining all kinds of possible and impossible quarrels with Aunt Patty , and inventing a chapter about her for `` The Complete Family", "context_len": 49}}}, "merits": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 66, "context": "Young Dewey shook hands all around , and then sat down on the bed , looking at Mark with a puzzled expression on his face , as much as to say , `` what on earth have I struck -- b'gee ? '' Mark saw his expression and undertook to inform him , making haste to start before the Parson could begin again on the relative merits of Boston and the rest of the civilized universe", "context_len": 76}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "I will tell you , Ben Ibyn , rather than excite your displeasure , though I would much have preferred not to do so , for you speak so much more highly of the affair than it merits", "context_len": 38}}}, "entangled": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was only a mean boy who would try to cross another fellow 's string ; but sometimes accidents would happen ; two kites would become entangled , and both would have to be hauled in , while their owners cried and scolded , and the other fellows cheered and laughed", "context_len": 51}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 68, "context": "Mark Penelly had gone over to the other side of the lugger while the conversation was going on , and he did not face the man he looked upon as his rival ; while Harry , unnoticed by the busy fishers as he swam round , went on , touching the sides of the lugger as he lightly swam , but only the next moment to find himself entangled in a quantity of the thin mackerel net , which seemed somehow to descend upon him like a cloud , and before he could realise the fact he was under water , hopelessly fettered by the net , and feeling that if he could not extricate himself directly he should be a dead man", "context_len": 123}}}, "scolded": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 51, "context": "But the dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of", "context_len": 128}}}, "swifter": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "Nobody had ever seen a blacksnake do it , and nobody had ever seen a hoop-snake , but the boys believed there was such a snake , and that he would take his tail in his mouth , when he got after a person , and roll himself along swifter than the fastest race-horse could run", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` 'At that time , there was a bird of large size , blue in color , with a long tail , and swifter than an eagle , which came every day and killed and ate their people", "context_len": 38}}}, "waver": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Sometimes the kite-string would break , and the kite would waver and fall like a bird shot in the wing ; and the owner of the kite , and all the fellows who had no kites , would run to get it where it came down , perhaps a mile or more away", "context_len": 53}}}, "cropped": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "She catechized Lilian freely about her housekeeping arrangements , hoped Nancy did her duty , and did not neglect to sweep out corners , told Bobby that his irrepressible curls looked girlish , and his hair ought to be cropped close every week , plied Peggy with embarrassing questions on the subject of fine needlework and stocking-darning , and drank four cups of tea in the meantime , with the air of one conferring a favour thereby", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "A heavy , well-built man , with a closely cropped beard and a kindly face , stepped from the deck to the tow-path", "context_len": 23}}}, "fringe": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 5, "context": "Some of them had paper fringe pasted round the edges of their kites ; this made a fine rattling as the kite rose , and when the kite stood , at the end of its string , you could hear the humming if you put your ear to the twine", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "So one February evening found him struggling across the moorlands that fringe the coast of Norfolk between Hunstanton and Lynn", "context_len": 20}}}, "slit": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The messengers were cut out of thick paper , with a slit at one side , so as to slip over the string , which would be pulled level long enough to give the messenger a good start , and then released , when the wind would catch the little circle , and drive it up the long curving incline till it reached the kite", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The ears were slit and stretched to an enormous circumference , an operation which caused them incredible pain and was adopted from the Sh\u00e1wano or some other northern nation", "context_len": 29}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "This he could slit with his machete and secure as much as he wanted of the cooling delicious waters that these plants store up , a fact that , if known , would have saved the lives of many of those who have perished of thirst in the very shadow of the thorny plants", "context_len": 54}}}, "messenger": {"freq": 85, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 84, "word_index": 17, "context": "Matt recounted the manner in which he had been beguiled into the open country by the supposed messenger ; and he told about the accident to the taxicab , the revelation that the supposed youth was a girl , the finding of the driver , the passing of the red touring car with McGlory in the tonneau , the work of Dimmock and Sanders , a mile west of Rye , and the journey through Connecticut and into Massachusetts , finishing with his meeting with McGlory", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Neither could any other messenger approach their dwellings safely at night", "context_len": 11}}}, "incline": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 9, "context": "They had just reached the side of a little incline when Peggy 's bootlace , which she had neglected to fasten all the morning , tripped her up , and over she went , rolling into a prickly gorse-bush , while Bobby , who was so close behind that he could not stop himself , fell over her , and collapsed into a boggy hollow , where he lay panting for breath until Peggy picked herself up and hauled him out", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Do n't be afraid of hitting him , '' the Parson had said , afraid that Mark 's kind-heartedness would incline him to mercy", "context_len": 25}}}, "unlimited": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 47, "context": "For a time they could hold the walls ; but as their number became reduced by the fire of the Turkish cannon and the battles at the breaches , they would at last be too weak any longer to repel the onslaughts of foes with an almost unlimited supply of soldiers", "context_len": 51}}}, "imagination": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 102, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}}}, "fulfilment": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 58, "context": "My boy would as soon have thought of his father 's doing a wrong thing as of his brother 's doing it ; and his brother was a calm light of common-sense , of justice , of truth , while he was a fantastic flicker of gaudy purposes which he wished to make shine before men in their fulfilment", "context_len": 59}}}, "hardships": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 11, "context": "So he said in himself , `` I have suffered many hardships for the sake of the lamp and have endured fatigues such as none but I might brook , [ 549 ] and now yonder accursed one taketh it without stress and it is evident [ 550 ] [ that ] , an he have learned the use thereof , there will be none in the world richer than he", "context_len": 71}}}, "weeding": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Perhaps Maggie and Fred might not have kept their plots in such good order , or worked so perseveringly at weeding , raking , and watering , had it not been for Harry and Bessie , who never forgot to go each day and look at their plants , and never suffered a weed to do more than show the tip of its head above the soil", "context_len": 67}}}, "saws": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was not the constant clatter of machinery and the rasping of dozens of circular saws in the adjoining factories that kept him awake , but the excitement of the day , culminating in the discovery of his elder brother , whom he had regarded as dead for months past", "context_len": 50}}}, "bucks": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 28, "context": "Putting on a new pair of moccasins ( stillipa\u00ed a ) , he leaves the cabin , and during twelve moons abstains from eating the meat of young bucks , of turkey-cocks , fowls , peas and salt , and is also forbidden to pick his ears and to scratch his head with his fingers , but must use a small splinter to perform these operations", "context_len": 66}}}, "sawyers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "There were public sawyers who did most of the wood-sawing ; and they came up with their bucks on their shoulders , and asked for the job almost as soon as the wood was unloaded before your door", "context_len": 38}}}, "unloaded": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "There were public sawyers who did most of the wood-sawing ; and they came up with their bucks on their shoulders , and asked for the job almost as soon as the wood was unloaded before your door", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Unless the stuff was unloaded there was little chance of discovery , since the bargee could not see the spot from where he stood to steer", "context_len": 26}}}, "unfriendly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 31, "context": "There had for a long time existed a tradition among them , that when a daughter of a chief -- an only child , -- should love a brave of an unfriendly tribe , they would have power to change her into a flower or animal , and unless the brave should find the means within ten moons , or months , to break the enchantment , she would die , and with her every chieftain and his family", "context_len": 79}}}, "ills": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "They watched the hapless creature with grave , unpitying , yet not unfriendly interest , too ignorant of the dark ills of life to know how deeply tragic was the spectacle that entertained them , and how awfully present in Morn 's contortions was the mystery of God 's ways with his children , some of whom he gives to happiness and some to misery", "context_len": 65}}}, "tragic": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "They watched the hapless creature with grave , unpitying , yet not unfriendly interest , too ignorant of the dark ills of life to know how deeply tragic was the spectacle that entertained them , and how awfully present in Morn 's contortions was the mystery of God 's ways with his children , some of whom he gives to happiness and some to misery", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "Boswell was a devotedly good and also an able man ; a minister , not in the Scottish Presbyterian Church , but in some smaller religious body ; and his death took place in a somewhat tragic manner , before the date of his daughter \u2019 s marriage to Mr", "context_len": 50}}}, "contortions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "They watched the hapless creature with grave , unpitying , yet not unfriendly interest , too ignorant of the dark ills of life to know how deeply tragic was the spectacle that entertained them , and how awfully present in Morn 's contortions was the mystery of God 's ways with his children , some of whom he gives to happiness and some to misery", "context_len": 65}}}, "weakly": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "Do you remember the name of Thaurepas ( ? ) , the blacky General who weakly surrendered his post to the French ? What do you think the grateful Monsieurs did to him ? Nailed epaulettes on his shoulders and a cocked hat on his head , and then threw him with his wife and children into the sea ! Would one believe such things of men in the 19th century ? I should like to know something of the present state of Hayti , and whether the throne is filled by a son of Henri I", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 13, "context": "The plebe was about a hundred yards from the shore , and swimming weakly ; the girl , still unconscious , was floating upon her back -- and her rescuer , holding her by the arms -- was slowly towing her toward the shore", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` No , I thank you , '' responded Mark , smiling weakly", "context_len": 13}}}, "engage": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 17, "context": "Filelfo , who was greatly attached to D'Aubusson , saw by their manner that they wished to engage him in some intrigue , and , feigning great resentment and anger at his disgrace , led them on until they divulged the entire plot for D'Aubusson 's assassination , and made brilliant offers to him if he would afford them facilities for carrying it out , producing , in proof of their power to do so , a letter of the pasha , authorising them to make such promises in his name", "context_len": 91}}}, "liquor": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 8, "context": "He also brought Ben a cocoanut to keep liquor in ; the end of it , where the eyes are , was made in the shape of a negro \u2019 s face ; the two little round places , where we bore to let the milk out , serving the purpose of eyes , with eyebrows cut over them , and filled with some red matter ; in the mouth was a lead pipe to drink from ; large ears were also made , and a nose ; the figure looked somewhere between a monkey and a negro , funny enough , and was full of rum", "context_len": 107}}}, "preachers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "But all this , as you can plainly see , has nothing whatever to do with the title of this chapter , so it behooves us , as the preachers used to say , to turn our back on Harold and the charms of the renowned old college , and our faces toward the ocean and a far-off land -- far off , that is , as far as Windsor and the English are concerned , but very near and dear to the hearts of some of the rest of us", "context_len": 91}}}, "remotely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He was a big boy , whom my boy remotely heard of , but never saw , for he lived in another part of the town ; but his adventure was known to all the boys , and his heroism rated high among them", "context_len": 44}}}, "heroism": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then Alfgar saw what was the marvellous power of Christianity , and beheld a heroism utterly beyond the fierce excitement which nerved his countrymen for their scenes of carnage and blood ; not one of his pagan friends could have suffered as calmly , as patiently -- it seemed easier for the sufferer to bear than for Alfgar to look on ; once or twice the latter gave audible vent to his emotions , but the look which Bertric turned upon him spoke volumes , and he restrained himself lest he should add to the pain of the victim", "context_len": 99}}}, "rated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Belden 's faultless attire and distinguished bearing , had rated him at once as one of those high and mighty Londoners , and had expected that he would of course entrench himself on the back seat of the little turnout and , preserving a dignified silence , condescendingly allow himself to be driven about and to be very much bored into the bargain -- all of which , it must be confessed , would have been more in keeping with Mr", "context_len": 81}}}, "annul": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "It took nothing from this , in their eyes , that he was found , homesick and crying in Cincinnati , and was glad to come back -- the great fact was that he had run off ; nothing could change or annul that", "context_len": 44}}}, "alighting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 50, "context": "Then he took leave of the Sultan and going forth , mounted and rode , with his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "crupper": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "Baulked of their prey , the mob still surged round us , and with a shout of : `` Let \u2019 em have the cat ! '' a great black object was sent flying towards us , and , striking my horse on the crupper , resolved itself into an enormous black cat , that spat and howled , digging its claws into the horse \u2019 s hide , and arching its back like the demon cat that is the reputed companion of every witch", "context_len": 85}}}, "piebald": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Then , if you were ever seen away from home , you were seen tumbling through a hoop and alighting on the crupper of a barebacked piebald , and if you ever came home you came home in a gilded chariot , and you flashed upon the domestic circle in flesh-colored tights and spangled breech-cloth", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Once there were two chariots -- one held the band in red-and-blue uniforms , and was drawn by eighteen piebald horses ; and the other was drawn by a troop of Shetland ponies , and carried in a vast mythical sea-shell little boys in spangled tights and little girls in the gauze skirts and wings of fairies", "context_len": 57}}}, "gilded": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 36, "context": "Albert , who preferred that everything should be done decently and in order , placed a chair for Marie-Celeste on the other side of the Queen 's little table , and then seated himself on the gilded sofa beside Miss Belmore , in such a comfortable , snuggling-up way that Miss Belmore had to put one arm right round him and give him a sound little kiss by way of punishment , which Albert was courteous enough not to resent , notwithstanding he considered that sort of treatment somewhat humiliating for a boy of four", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 38, "context": "Each of the lofty ships bore the ensign of its commander ; some carried at the prow the figures of lions , some of bulls , dolphins , dragons , or armed warriors , gaudily painted or even gilded ; while others bore from their mast the ensign of voracious birds -- the eagle , the raven -- which appeared to stretch their wings as the flag expanded in the wind", "context_len": 71}}}, "domestic": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 15, "context": "It is well said that no man is utterly bad , and perhaps he possessed domestic virtues which were thought unworthy of the attention of the chroniclers ; but as they picture him -- now prompting Ethelred to deeds of treachery against the Danes , now joining those Danes themselves , and surpassing them in cruelty -- now seeking pretended reconciliation , only to betray his foe more surely , and in all this aided and supported by the weak , unprincipled king -- as thus pictured there is scarcely a blacker character in history", "context_len": 95}}}, "tights": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "Once there were two chariots -- one held the band in red-and-blue uniforms , and was drawn by eighteen piebald horses ; and the other was drawn by a troop of Shetland ponies , and carried in a vast mythical sea-shell little boys in spangled tights and little girls in the gauze skirts and wings of fairies", "context_len": 57}}}, "performance": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was too much for the boys , but the performance of the big dive was looked upon as the passing of a lad from boyhood into the manly stage , upon which he entered through the Shangles Gate , and then swam back , coming , as it were , of age amidst the shouts of his companions to swim ashore and land upon the big boulders , where the boys bathed and learned to swim in the calm weather , gazing the while in admiration at their older companions", "context_len": 91}}}, "strict": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 71, "context": "Then there was Pixie , the pony , who would come whinnying up from the further side of a field to poke her soft nose into the children 's pockets for pieces of bread or lumps of sugar ; there were numerous cats who lived in the barns and stables , and Tabbyskins , the stately gray Persian , who usually sat sunning herself on the pigsty wall , keeping a strict eye on naughty Jack , who was wont to harry her if he got the chance", "context_len": 88}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Maybe he \u2019 s sent him to one of them strict military schools , where he \u2019 ll be ruled with a rod of iron", "context_len": 25}}}, "feats": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 45, "context": "Have a brother about four years older than yourself , I should say ; and if your temper is hot , and your disposition revengeful , and you are a vain and ridiculous dreamer at the same time that you are eager to excel in feats of strength and games of skill , and to do everything that the other fellows do , and are ashamed to be better than the worst boy in the crowd , your brother can be of the greatest use to you , with his larger experience and wisdom", "context_len": 94}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 88, "context": "David could not be spared to go with them , but all the children had been taught to drive , and even Bobby had a firm hand on the reins , and knew the rules of the road as well as many a more experienced coachman ; and I think , too , that Pixie had a sense of her responsibilities , and could be trusted not to get the wheel locked with a passing waggon , or to race too furiously down a steep hill , whatever feats her drivers might urge her to perform", "context_len": 96}}}, "representation": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 95, "context": "' Bobby took to carrying about an old horse-pistol which he had found in the lumber-room , and saying 'Oddsbodkins ' on all occasions , and Peggy put on such a general air of melodrama that it seemed scarcely possible for her to speak in plain prose ; Archie was impresario , stage-manager , scene-shifter , dresser , maker-up , and principal actor all combined , while Lilian waxed so enthusiastic that she even sacrificed the feathers out of her last summer 's best hat to adorn the slouched head-gear considered necessary for a due representation of Romeo", "context_len": 98}}}, "fortnight": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "For a fortnight beforehand they worked themselves up for the arrival of the circus into a fever of fear and hope , for it was always a question with a great many whether they could get their fathers to give them the money to go in", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 16, "context": "On the return of the knight with the news that it would be at least a fortnight before Genoa could fit out any galleys , and fearing that the pirates might at any moment put to sea , we procured some small Sardinian craft , and fitted them as fire ships ; with the captives we had rescued , and some Sard fishermen , we manned the three prizes , distributing the knights between them , and at night launched the fire ships against the corsairs , whose ships were crowded together", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 71, "context": "Having concluded my reading of old Russell , how can I do better than employ the interval before the arrival of the Indian letters in sitting down and writing to my fair absent sister ? Colonel Sykes let me know last night that Robin would not come by this mail , which was , he says , only from Bombay , so that letters being all we must expect before Saturday fortnight , you need not hurry home on account of Robin \u2019 s return", "context_len": 85}}}, "beforehand": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was to be held in a hall in Haddonfield , and many were the precautions taken by the committee to prevent any of the hated sophomores from attending , or getting to the place beforehand , lest they might , by some untoward act , `` put it on the blink , '' as Holly Cross used to say", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` It has been a hard fight , old friend , '' said the familiar king , `` but we have conquered ; for my part , I would far sooner have stood out against him , battle-axe in hand , than have met this struggle , could I have foreseen it beforehand ; but now I have given him the kiss of peace , peace it must be ; he has no more to dread from me", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "A fool might -- I beg pardon -- were not all our plans known beforehand ? Did not all our enterprises fail ? Were not all our ambushes anticipated ? Did we not fall into all theirs ? If they had had a prophet like Elisha , who told the king of Israel all Benhadad said in his council chamber , they could n't have managed better", "context_len": 67}}}, "commoner": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "It was pitiful to see the blackened ruins of churches and palace , which had not been rebuilt since the Danish raid of 1010 , but the commoner dwellings were rising with rapidity from their ashes , or had already risen , for the shelter of the earthworks and other fortifications was not to be despised , and prevented the place from being utterly abandoned", "context_len": 65}}}, "dime": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The full price was two bits , and the half-price was a bit , or a Spanish real , then a commoner coin than the American dime in the West ; and every boy , for that time only , wished to be little enough to look young enough to go in for a bit", "context_len": 55}}}, "editors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Editors of newspapers had a free ticket for every member of their families ; and my boy was sure of going to the circus from the first rumor of its coming", "context_len": 31}}}, "rumor": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 57, "context": "But Percy was popular , Seabrooke was not ; and even the masters were inclined to believe that the latter must have been careless and forgetful and mislaid the money , while believing he had put it in the place he indicated , and presently -- no one knew exactly how it started or could trace the rumor to its source -- presently it began to be bruited about among the boys that Seabrooke was keeping it for his own use and had never intended to return it to Percy , and was now making him his scape-goat", "context_len": 98}}}, "thrilled": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 21, "context": "He ended as they drew rein before the farmhouse where they were to pass the night , and the boy , thrilled and awed , had no voice to answer , but the grasp of his uncle 's hand , and the memory of his uncle 's words remained with him , as a consecration of his new life as a soldier , and moulded his doings and beliefs for all his life after", "context_len": 74}}}, "uniforms": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "They have worn the rock smooth , so that it glistens like glass in the morning sun , for , as if aware of the folly of urging on its regiments of well-mounted cavalry to come dashing in upon the wild white-maned sea-horses , or the more sober lines of heavy infantry in uniforms of green and blue , the sea has for countless ages bombarded Carn Du with stone-shot in the shape of great boulders", "context_len": 76}}}, "ponies": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 71, "context": "Dey 're trained to behave jus ' of dere own selves without any driver , and when dey get out on de road dey always do behave ; and then in the moment 's pause that followed , Marie-Celeste , remembering certain recent performances of her own , wondered if her father wished that a certain little girl , of whom he had some knowledge , more closely resembled these royal ponies , who , once trained to behave , according to Albert , never dreamed of taking the bit in their teeth or of kicking over the traces", "context_len": 99}}}, "mythical": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "It is wholly legendary , in its first portion even mythical ; it is of a comparatively remote age , exceedingly instructive for ethnography and for the development of religious ideas ; it is full of that sort of na\u00efvet\u00e9 which we like so much to meet in the mental productions of our aborigines , and affords striking instances of the debasing and brutalizing influence of the unrestricted belief in the supernatural and miraculous", "context_len": 74}}}, "gauze": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 113, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "flaw": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was not a flaw in this splendor to the young eyes that gloated on it , and that followed it in rapture through every turn and winding of its course in the Boy 's Town ; nor in the magnificence of the actors and actresses , who came riding two by two in their circus-dresses after the chariots , and looking some haughty and contemptuous , and others quiet and even bored , as if it were nothing to be part of such a procession", "context_len": 86}}}, "magnificence": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 41, "context": "When the Sultan entered the palace and viewed its building and ordinance and saw its stones , which were of jade and agate , he was amazed [ 505 ] and his wit was bewildered at that affluence and wealth and magnificence ; so he turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? Hast thou in all thy days seen aught like this ? Are there found with the greatest of the kings of the world riches and gold and jewels such as these we see in this palace ? '' `` O my lord the King , '' answered the Vizier , `` this is a thing beyond the competence of a king of the sons of Adam", "context_len": 128}}}, "actors": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 43, "context": "There was not a flaw in this splendor to the young eyes that gloated on it , and that followed it in rapture through every turn and winding of its course in the Boy 's Town ; nor in the magnificence of the actors and actresses , who came riding two by two in their circus-dresses after the chariots , and looking some haughty and contemptuous , and others quiet and even bored , as if it were nothing to be part of such a procession", "context_len": 86}}}, "actresses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "There was not a flaw in this splendor to the young eyes that gloated on it , and that followed it in rapture through every turn and winding of its course in the Boy 's Town ; nor in the magnificence of the actors and actresses , who came riding two by two in their circus-dresses after the chariots , and looking some haughty and contemptuous , and others quiet and even bored , as if it were nothing to be part of such a procession", "context_len": 86}}}, "contemptuous": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 65, "context": "There was not a flaw in this splendor to the young eyes that gloated on it , and that followed it in rapture through every turn and winding of its course in the Boy 's Town ; nor in the magnificence of the actors and actresses , who came riding two by two in their circus-dresses after the chariots , and looking some haughty and contemptuous , and others quiet and even bored , as if it were nothing to be part of such a procession", "context_len": 86}}}, "bills": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 81, "context": "`` I was wondering , '' rather hesitated Sara -- '' you know , you say I have so much money -- I was wondering if I could go to see the bun-woman , and tell her that if , when hungry children -- particularly on those dreadful days -- come and sit on the steps , or look in at the window , she would just call them in and give them something to eat , she might send the bills to me", "context_len": 84}}}, "tumbler": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "The boys tried to make them out by the pictures and names on the bills : which was Rivers , the bare-back rider , and which was O'Dale , the champion tumbler ; which was the India-rubber man , which the ring-master , which the clown", "context_len": 46}}}, "clown": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 59, "context": "He did not get home till nearly ten o'clock , though , and he saw the sides of the tent dropped before the people got out ; that was a great thing ; and what was greater yet , and reflected a kind of splendor on the boy at second hand , was that the jour printer and the clown turned out to be old friends", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The ring-master and the clown manage to get the country-jake on to the broad platform on the horse 's back , and then the ring-master cracks his whip , and the two supes who have been holding the horse 's head let go , and the horse begins cantering round the ring", "context_len": 52}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was the only company they ever really got together , for everybody wanted to be captain and lieutenant , just as they wanted to be clown and ring-master in a circus", "context_len": 32}}}, "ropes": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 28, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "skeleton": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 65, "context": "Covered with dust , gasping with the fatigue of a three hours ' run beside the procession , but fresh at heart as in the beginning , they arrived with it on the Commons , where the tent-wagons were already drawn up , and the ring was made , and mighty men were driving the iron-headed tent-stakes , and stretching the ropes of the great skeleton of the pavilion which they were just going to clothe with canvas", "context_len": 78}}}, "pavilion": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}}}, "clothe": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 75, "context": "Covered with dust , gasping with the fatigue of a three hours ' run beside the procession , but fresh at heart as in the beginning , they arrived with it on the Commons , where the tent-wagons were already drawn up , and the ring was made , and mighty men were driving the iron-headed tent-stakes , and stretching the ropes of the great skeleton of the pavilion which they were just going to clothe with canvas", "context_len": 78}}}, "fetch": {"freq": 84, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 77, "word_index": 62, "context": "Alaeddin 's mother returned home , shaking her head and saying , `` Whence shall my poor son get these dishes of jewels ? Supposing , for the jewels and the dishes , that he return to the treasure and gather the whole from the trees , -- and withal methinketh not it is possible to him ; but say that he fetch them , -- whence [ shall he get ] the slaves and slave-girls ? '' And she gave not over talking to herself till she reached the house , where Alaeddin awaited her , and when she came in to him , she said to him , `` O my son , said I not to thee , 'Think not to attain to the Lady", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "I \u2019 ll fetch y \u2019 u a warm supper by and by , for I bought a piece of lamb to stew a-purpose , an \u2019 Sadie is tendin \u2019 it", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Accordingly the Sultan bade straightway fetch jewellers and goldsmiths and commanded to give them from the treasury all that they needed of gold and jewels and [ precious ] metals ; so they came and he bade them do that which was wanting of the lattice-work of the [ unfinished ] oriel", "context_len": 52}}}, "richly": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 78, "context": "But he had been in the kitchen that evening , when Maggie and Bessie had gone to the porch to speak to his father for Lem , and he had heard all that had passed ; and now , as he remembered how sweetly and generously the two dear little girls had pleaded for the boy who had treated them so badly , he could not resolve to give him even a part of the punishment he so richly deserved", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "She always had some interesting book on hand , and kept her mind richly stored with knowledge , being specially fond of history", "context_len": 23}}}, "tickets": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` My husband will not be down till to-morrow evening in time to take the train for Fall River , and meantime I have these three little girls on my hands and no man to look after us , so if you will come along to see about tickets and things I should be pleased", "context_len": 55}}}, "brimming": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The other boys were proud to pass a word with them as they went by with their brimming buckets ; fellows who had money to go in would have been glad to carry water just for the glory of coming close to the circus-men", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "As a result , they were both brimming over with fun and merriment ; and as there is , fortunately , nothing in the world more contagious than good spirits , the other members of the party were equally merry without in the least knowing why", "context_len": 46}}}, "twos": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 38, "context": "The best place for them was in some woods-pasture where the trees stood free of one another , and around them , in among the tall , frosty grass , the tumbled nuts lay scattered in groups of twos and threes , or fives , some still yellowish-green in their hulls , and some black , but all sending up to the nostrils of the delighted boy the incense of their clean , keen , wild-woody smell , to be a memory forever", "context_len": 83}}}, "threes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "The best place for them was in some woods-pasture where the trees stood free of one another , and around them , in among the tall , frosty grass , the tumbled nuts lay scattered in groups of twos and threes , or fives , some still yellowish-green in their hulls , and some black , but all sending up to the nostrils of the delighted boy the incense of their clean , keen , wild-woody smell , to be a memory forever", "context_len": 83}}}, "debating": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 82, "context": "When I see five or six boys now lying under a tree on the grass , and they fall silent as I pass them , I have no right to say that they are not arranging to go and carry some poor widow 's winter wood into her shed and pile it neatly up for her , and wish to keep it a secret from everybody ; but forty years ago I should have had good reason for thinking that they were debating how to tie a piece of her clothes-line along the ground so that when her orphan boy came out for an armload of wood after dark , he would trip on it and send his wood flying all over the yard", "context_len": 124}}}, "sawdust": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "They stood about in twos and threes , and lay upon the grass in groups debating whether a tan-bark ring was better than a sawdust ring ; there were different opinions", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 63, "context": "I never was away from home more than a day in my life till I was married , and it 's been nothing but work ever since , and now to be laid here like a useless log , with everything going hotfoot to destruction ! It 's a good thing you 've come at last , for the children are makin ' sawdust and splinters of every bit of crockery in the house , and that Martha Spriggs has no more management than a settin ' hen", "context_len": 88}}}, "wagons": {"freq": 101, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 101, "word_index": 105, "context": "Among other things were two or three peacock feathers , a turkey wing , some pebbles from the brook where papa used to sail his boats when he was a boy , a piece of rusty tin pipe , which , because it looked black and smoky , and came from the field where the burnt barn had stood , they persuaded themselves must be a part of the very leader down which papa slid when he ran for the ladder to save his little brother , -- all these , and other treasures of like value , were carefully collected and stowed in the wagons , to be carried to the Lake House , and thence to the city", "context_len": 120}}}, "streamers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "The wagons were left standing outside of the tent ; but when it was up , the horses were taken into the dressing-room , and then the boys , with many a backward look at the wide spread of canvas , and the flags and streamers floating over it from the centre-pole ( the centre-pole was revered almost like a distinguished personage ) , ran home to dinner so as to get back good and early , and be among the first to go in", "context_len": 85}}}, "personage": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 78, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}}}, "doorkeepers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "All round , before the circus doors were open , the doorkeepers of the side-shows were inviting people to come in and see the giants and fat woman and boa-constrictors , and there were stands for peanuts and candy and lemonade ; the vendors cried , `` Ice-cold lemonade , from fifteen hundred miles under ground ! Walk up , roll up , tumble up , any way to get up ! '' The boys thought this brilliant drolling , but they had no time to listen after the doors were open , and they had no money to spend on side-shows or dainties , anyway", "context_len": 106}}}, "lemonade": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 27, "context": "They sat laughing over the rhymes and chattering as only schoolgirls know how until Aunt Helen came in to announce that a light repast of cake and lemonade awaited them in the dining-room , and to gently hint that , if Warford were to be reached before darkness had fallen , it was getting time for the six bicycles to be set in motion", "context_len": 64}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "He was asked to be one of the boys who held the banner-tassels , and he felt this a great compliment somehow , though he was so young that he had afterwards only the vaguest remembrance of marching in the procession , and going to a raw and chilly grove somewhere , and having untimely lemonade and cake", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "We have had ginger ale and lemonade , and chocolate and such things", "context_len": 13}}}, "vendors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "All round , before the circus doors were open , the doorkeepers of the side-shows were inviting people to come in and see the giants and fat woman and boa-constrictors , and there were stands for peanuts and candy and lemonade ; the vendors cried , `` Ice-cold lemonade , from fifteen hundred miles under ground ! Walk up , roll up , tumble up , any way to get up ! '' The boys thought this brilliant drolling , but they had no time to listen after the doors were open , and they had no money to spend on side-shows or dainties , anyway", "context_len": 106}}}, "dainties": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 103, "context": "All round , before the circus doors were open , the doorkeepers of the side-shows were inviting people to come in and see the giants and fat woman and boa-constrictors , and there were stands for peanuts and candy and lemonade ; the vendors cried , `` Ice-cold lemonade , from fifteen hundred miles under ground ! Walk up , roll up , tumble up , any way to get up ! '' The boys thought this brilliant drolling , but they had no time to listen after the doors were open , and they had no money to spend on side-shows or dainties , anyway", "context_len": 106}}}, "amaze": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "When the eunuchs and chamberlains and captains of the guard saw them , wonder took them and they were breathless for amaze at this sight , the like whereof they had never in their lives seen , and especially at the slave girls , each one of whom would ravish the wit of an anchorite", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "But there was staying at the hotel a lady who used to amaze Maggie and Bessie very much", "context_len": 18}}}, "hooking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "There was not a boy in the crowd that my boy went with who would have been allowed to hook into a circus by his parents ; yet hooking in was an ideal that was cherished among them , that was talked of , and that was even sometimes attempted , though not often", "context_len": 54}}}, "risks": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 15, "context": "Mistress Felgate gave her permission with the stipulation that her husband must run no needless risks , the compact was sealed by a kiss bestowed by her gallant and unabashed bridegroom , and the conversation was resumed , while mirth and gaiety reigned supreme", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Because everyone goes and risks his life just for the sake of gratifying his vanity , '' replied Harry Paul , `` I do n't see why I should go and do the same", "context_len": 35}}}, "vigilance": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 57, "context": "For the first few days of my captivity my brain was literally seething with schemes for effecting my escape , most of them wildly impossible , I admit ; but some there were that seemed to promise just a ghost of a chance of success -- until I attempted to put them into effect , when the vigilance of my guards -- with the fear of crucifixion , head downward , before their eyes -- invariably baffled me", "context_len": 78}}}, "massive": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 40, "context": "My uncle busied himself till all traces of his worry lost themselves in his activity ; my aunt bustled about the house , driving the servants hither and thither , bringing linen from the huge oak chest , furbishing the massive furniture , and causing a huge supply of viands to be prepared for the entertainment of our guests -- for the invited company numbered close on a score , so that our house would be filled to overflowing , and rooms had to be engaged at `` The Bell Inn '' and `` The Blue Posts ''", "context_len": 98}}}, "patrolled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "He ran great risks , and if he escaped the vigilance of the massive circus-man who patrolled the outside of the tent with a cowhide and a bulldog , perhaps he merited the fame he was sure to win", "context_len": 39}}}, "cowhide": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "He ran great risks , and if he escaped the vigilance of the massive circus-man who patrolled the outside of the tent with a cowhide and a bulldog , perhaps he merited the fame he was sure to win", "context_len": 39}}}, "merited": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "Living on grass , and in the woods , with the milk from the house , he had not that protuberance of belly which swine reared in sties possess , and really merited Charlie \u2019 s encomium of being handsome ; he jumped up on his master and rubbed against his legs , with low grunts , expressive of satisfaction", "context_len": 60}}}, "notions": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` I did n't want to be settin ' myself up , but I was n't goin ' to have her take no false notions about me , so I says , 'Yes , ma'am , but when a body 's sick , and ai n't no appetite to eat only what one forces one 's self to , I do n't think it no sin to spend a bit for a nice nourishin ' mouthful", "context_len": 76}}}, "morality": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 110, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "outgrows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He outgrows them , and other boys grow into them , and then outgrow them as he did", "context_len": 18}}}, "boyhood": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 55, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}}}, "plains": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 78, "word_index": 37, "context": "Pshaw ! dat ain \u2019 t nuffin , he said when the wife of Abner Fleming complimented him ; I \u2019 spects to do de same thing a good many more times afore we gits across de plains ; de fac \u2019 is , I \u2019 m on to dem Injins and dey \u2019 ll find it out ; when dey wokes up Jethro Mix dey wokes up de wrong passenger and dere \u2019 s gwine to be trouble in de land", "context_len": 82}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "True they had passed over a wonderful country , but the alkali plains seemed small in comparison to the desert , over which they had passed some weeks before , and all the grandeur of the Sierra Nevada Mountains could not awaken in them one iota of the enthusiasm with which they had greeted the first glimpse of the snow-capped summits of the Rocky Mountains", "context_len": 65}}}, "retrospect": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 77, "context": "He knew what it was to be without peace ; for having got into debt when he was first in the army , and knowing the distress it caused his family at home , his mind was so troubled that he wrote to his mother : `` Oh , what agony I have endured ! What sleepless nights I have passed since the perusal of that letter ! The review of my past life , especially the retrospect of the last two years , has at last quite startled me , and at the same time disgusted me", "context_len": 98}}}, "melon": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` But Cousin Alexander had seen the melon in its glory , before it was taken from the vine ; and , in spite of its present distressed appearance , Fred found a handsome six-bladed knife placed beside the fragments , -- '' A blade for each piece , and the handle thrown in , '' said pleased Fred ; adding , that he thought Cousin Alexander wanted an excuse for giving presents", "context_len": 73}}}, "watermelon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "None of them would have taken an apple out of a market-wagon , or stolen a melon from a farmer who came to town with it ; but they would all have thought it fun , if not right , to rob an orchard or hook a watermelon out of a patch", "context_len": 52}}}, "foray": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "This would have been a foray into the enemy 's country , and the fruit of the adventure would have been the same as the plunder of a city , or the capture of a vessel belonging to him on the high seas", "context_len": 43}}}, "plunder": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 54, "context": "Our defences are all complete ; our recent peace with Egypt has enabled us to fill up our magazines with provisions of all kinds ; the inhabitants of the Island have had ample warning to move into the town , carrying with them everything of value ; so the Turks will obtain but little plunder , and will be able to gather no means of subsistence on the island , as every animal has been driven within the walls , and even the unripe corn has been reaped and brought in", "context_len": 91}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "If they find the good people of Dorchester are awake , as the noise shows , they will swarm all over the neighbourhood like wasps after honey , to plunder the isolated houses and farms , and carry off all they can ; and this place is too conspicuous -- too much of a city on a hill -- to be hidden", "context_len": 62}}}, "seas": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 68, "context": "that if he wished to marry his daughter he could do it without his consent ; as to the ransom , he was not in want of money ; in respect to Darius 's offering to give him up all west of the Euphrates , it was absurd for a man to speak of giving what was no longer his own ; that he had crossed too many seas in his military expeditions , since he left Macedon , to feel any concern about the rivers that he might find in his way ; and that he should continue to pursue Darius wherever he might retreat in search of safety and protection , and he had no fear but that he should find and conquer him at last", "context_len": 128}}}, "exploit": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 73, "context": "My dear Caretto , the grand master exclaimed , it is almost a resurrection , for we have all long mourned you as dead ; and your return to us at the present time is indeed fortunate ; for upon whose judgment and aid could I better rely than those of my old comrade in arms ? Then , turning to Gervaise , he went on : It was a daring and brilliant exploit indeed , Sir Gervaise , and in due time honour shall be paid to you and your brave companions , to whom and to you I now tender the thanks of the Order", "context_len": 107}}}, "enclosed": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 39, "context": "Using the butts , and occasionally the points , of their spears freely in order to force a passage through the steadily growing crowd , my escort slowly made their way toward that part of the town which was enclosed by the palisade ; and , as they did so , I studied the faces of those who thronged about me , with the object of forming some idea , if I could , of the fate that I might expect at their hands", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 65, "context": "The letter gave instructions for me to be sent to my uncle \u2019 s at Portsmouth , to be provided for until I could choose for myself what I should be , at the same time exhorting me to serve faithfully His Majesty King Charles II or his lawful successor , and to abstain from vain or idle longings to break the seals of the enclosed package till the stipulated time limit had expired", "context_len": 74}}}, "forbade": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "As a person who enclosed bounds and forbade trespass , the circus-man constituted himself the enemy of every boy who respected himself , and challenged him to practise any sort of strategy", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 24, "context": "He knew that his father abhorred it , but he had never been explicitly forbidden to smoke or chew , for his father seldom forbade him anything explicitly , and he gave himself such freedom of choice in the matter that when the boy with the tobacco began to offer it around , he judged it right to take a chew with the rest", "context_len": 64}}}, "trespass": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "As a person who enclosed bounds and forbade trespass , the circus-man constituted himself the enemy of every boy who respected himself , and challenged him to practise any sort of strategy", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "All this , however , was exceedingly detrimental to the reputation of Mafuta , the chief witch-doctor of the community , who found his power and influence rapidly waning , and he soon discovered means to make me understand that I must cease to trespass upon what he deemed his own exclusive sphere of operations , on pain of making him my mortal enemy", "context_len": 64}}}, "constituted": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 30, "context": "Meanwhile , during the time that Percy was closeted with his uncle in the library , that portion of the members of the `` Cheeryble Sisters ' Club '' which constituted the choice band of `` Inseparables , '' namely Maggie and Bessie Bradford , Belle Powers , Lily Norris , and Fanny Leroy , having joined forces on their way to Miss Ashton 's , had called in to see Lena", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "He had no objection to the scheme on any score except that he was afraid it might absorb all his time and thoughts ; for he was so constituted that he could never see a human creature , particularly a human creature in trouble , without taking that person 's part and endeavoring if possible to set wrong right", "context_len": 59}}}, "strategy": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 73, "context": "The English hero divided his army into three divisions : The right wing , where he posted around his own person the chosen band whom he had trained during the last few years of retirement ; the left wing , chiefly composed of the men of Wessex ; the centre , the weakest and newest recruits , whom he posted there with as deep a design as led Hannibal to use the same strategy at Cannae", "context_len": 76}}}, "attempted": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 3, "context": "If ever I attempted such a thing -- and I did , very frequently -- a party of the king 's guards was certain to turn up , in the most exasperatingly casual and unexpected manner , and join me , under the pretence , as they made me understand , that it was extremely dangerous to venture alone beyond the confines of the town , if I pretended that I was engaged in hunting for animals , or plants to be used in my medical practice", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "A nearly fatal result to Alden followed the attempted slaying of an enormous bull by shooting him in the head instead of just behind the fore leg , but the mistake was not repeated", "context_len": 34}}}, "hooked": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "He felt in despair , for he was all eagerness now to see the monster he had hooked , when a happy thought suggested itself , and in an instant he had made three or four hitches round one of the oars with the end of the line , and cast it overboard", "context_len": 53}}}, "ignobly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Once , when a fellow really hooked in , and joined the crowd that had ignobly paid , one of the fellows could not stand it", "context_len": 26}}}, "doorkeeper": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "He asked him just how and where he got in , and then he went to the door , and got back his money from the doorkeeper upon the plea that he did not feel well ; and in five or ten minutes he was back among the boys , a hero of such moral grandeur as would be hard to describe", "context_len": 62}}}, "thievish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Not one of the fellows saw him as he really was -- a little lying , thievish scoundrel", "context_len": 18}}}, "scoundrel": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 5, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` You scoundrel ! '' he ejaculated between his set teeth , and with his eyes actually blazing , `` you stole this , did you ? '' -- flourishing the note before the now terrified Lewis , who , taken thus by surprise , had no time to collect his wits and assume an appearance of unconcern and innocence", "context_len": 60}}}, "entry": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 64, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "caracoled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The boys liked to be at the circus early so as to make sure of the grand entry of the performers into the ring , where they caracoled round on horseback , and gave a delicious foretaste of the wonders to come", "context_len": 42}}}, "horseback": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 47, "context": "Moreover , he fell to going everywhile [ 539 ] to the chase and the horse course and to practicing horsemanship and archery [ 540 ] before the Sultan , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour redoubled in love of him , whenassoever she saw him disporting himself a horseback , and thought in herself that God had wrought exceeding graciously by her in that there had befallen her what befell with the Vizier 's son , so He might keep her for her true bridegroom Alaeddin", "context_len": 85}}}, "united": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 52, "context": "The scene was indeed a terrible one , the roar of cannon , the shouts of the combatants , the screams of the poor wretches upon whom the terrible Greek fire fell , the clash of arms and the shouts and cries of the Turks as they pressed across the bridge , united in a din that thrilled with horror the spectators , both in the city and on St", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 64, "context": "Gervaise made first for the man lying beneath the mast ; as he came up to him , the sailor opened his eyes and murmured , Water ! Gervaise called out to one of the servants to bring water from the galley , and , as soon as it came , poured some between the man 's lips , and the knights by their united efforts lifted the mast from across his body", "context_len": 73}}}, "varied": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 54, "context": "And do you think that people are to be all massed in heaven , losing their various identities , their differing tastes , their separate natures ? Going from this lower world so full of its adaptations , where colour and form take on a thousand changes , and life and pursuit can be varied almost at will , to a mere dead level of perfect felicity ? To leave earth where no two things are alike , and go to heaven to find no two different ! The Lord 's preparations mean more than that", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 23, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}}}, "knives": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 56, "context": "There were but five or six men to be seen on the deck of the wreck , and these had , as the boat approached , come down to the rocks as if to meet those who came to their aid ; but as the knights leapt out , they threw themselves suddenly upon them with knives and scimitars that had hitherto been concealed beneath their garments , while at the same moment a crowd of men appeared on the deck of the ship , and , leaping down , ran forward with drawn swords", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The very knives he saw used for their meals had served as daggers to despatch the wounded or the helpless prisoner", "context_len": 21}}}, "askew": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "There never was more than one ring in those days ; and you were not tempted to break your neck and set your eyes forever askew , by trying to watch all the things that went on at once in two or three rings", "context_len": 44}}}, "preferences": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "It is certainly true , said Her Majesty a little gravely , that the rulers of a great country like England have often to set aside their own preferences ; but these are better times than those in which the little Isabel lived , and the idea of a king marrying a little girl of eight , no matter for what reason , would hardly be tolerated now , you know", "context_len": 71}}}, "trained": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 27, "context": "The English hero divided his army into three divisions : The right wing , where he posted around his own person the chosen band whom he had trained during the last few years of retirement ; the left wing , chiefly composed of the men of Wessex ; the centre , the weakest and newest recruits , whom he posted there with as deep a design as led Hannibal to use the same strategy at Cannae", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 18, "context": "The big boys have known all along that he was not a real country-jake ; but when the trained mule begins , and shakes everybody off , just like the horse , and another country-jake gets up , and offers to bet that he can ride that mule , nobody can tell whether he is a real country-jake or not", "context_len": 60}}}, "audience": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 51, "word_index": 96, "context": "her small person between the fists of two fighting hobbledehoys , and seized a little boy by the boot who had ventured to climb up the stove-pipe ; she welcomed some of the parents who had begun to arrive for the entertainment , and found them comfortable places on the benches , even nursing one of the babies while its mother went to the rescue of an older child , who was being forcibly held down and sat upon by several of its companions ; and by the time the bell was rung , and the audience requested to take their places , she was almost as hot and pulled about as the Rector himself , but with a feeling of conscious virtue that made up for everything", "context_len": 128}}}, "stirs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "I can recommend their Scotch highballs , and as for cocktails , they put up a dry Martini here that goes down like oil , and stirs you up like a torchlight procession", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` Let me see , what shall we be fined for ? I shall have to get some light upon that , too , but I think it would be a good plan that any girl who voluntarily stirs up a fuss with another at school must pay a fine of not less than one cent", "context_len": 56}}}, "reels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The little fellows are just sure the country-jake is going to fall off , he reels and totters so ; but the big boys tell them to keep watching out ; and pretty soon the country-jake begins to straighten up", "context_len": 40}}}, "totters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The little fellows are just sure the country-jake is going to fall off , he reels and totters so ; but the big boys tell them to keep watching out ; and pretty soon the country-jake begins to straighten up", "context_len": 40}}}, "unbutton": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "He begins to unbutton his long gray overcoat , and then he takes it off and throws it into the ring , where one of the supes catches it", "context_len": 29}}}, "flourishes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Then he sticks a short pipe into his mouth , and pulls on an old wool hat , and flourishes a stick that the supe throws to him , and you see that he is an Irishman just come across the sea ; and then off goes another coat , and he comes out a British soldier in white duck trousers and red coat", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was agreeable , and so the Parson told his story , embellishing it with many flourishes , classical allusions and geological metaphors", "context_len": 23}}}, "soldier": {"freq": 141, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 129, "word_index": 67, "context": "I often think of all these things , and sometimes talk them over with comrades , and there are few who do not hold , with me , that it would be far better that we should become a purely military Order , like some of the military Orders in the courts of the European sovereigns , than remain as we are , half monk , half soldier -- a mixture that , so far as I can see , accords but badly with either morality or public repute", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 15, "context": "All followers of the Prophet have an equal chance , and one may be a soldier today , a bey tomorrow , and a pasha a year hence , if he be brave , or astute , or capable in any way beyond his fellows", "context_len": 45}}}, "hips": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 8, "context": "Ana , with her hands on her broad hips , short and thick as a squat brown jug with its handles akimbo , -- Ana , with her great clay-colored face and tiny , glittering eyes , with her thick , pale lips and coarse , black hair , -- surely none but a mother could view in Ana such charms as bedewed Se\u00f1ora Vigil 's eyes only to think of !", "context_len": 72}}}, "hornpipe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "That comes off , and he is an American sailor , with his hands on his hips dancing a hornpipe", "context_len": 20}}}, "stripped": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 103, "context": "I know a dozen rich Moors in Tripoli or Tunis who would be glad to have him ; and we agreed that we would run down to the African coast for awhile , for that galley has been altogether too busy of late for our comfort , and will be all the more active after this little affair ; besides , people in these islands have got so scared that one ca n't get within ten miles of any of them now without seeing their signal smokes rising on the hills , and finding , when they land , the villages deserted and stripped of everything worth carrying away", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "When they reached the cabin they were set upon by the combined party , their horses were taken from them , they were tied to trees , where they were kept in great pain all night , and in the morning stripped of the greater part of their money and sent adrift", "context_len": 52}}}, "actor": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 62, "context": "' Bobby took to carrying about an old horse-pistol which he had found in the lumber-room , and saying 'Oddsbodkins ' on all occasions , and Peggy put on such a general air of melodrama that it seemed scarcely possible for her to speak in plain prose ; Archie was impresario , stage-manager , scene-shifter , dresser , maker-up , and principal actor all combined , while Lilian waxed so enthusiastic that she even sacrificed the feathers out of her last summer 's best hat to adorn the slouched head-gear considered necessary for a due representation of Romeo", "context_len": 98}}}, "stoops": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Suddenly away flash wig and beard and false-face , the pantaloons are stripped off with the same movement , the actor stoops for the reins lying on the horse 's neck , and James Rivers , the greatest three-horse rider in the world nimbly capers on the broad pad , and kisses his hand to the shouting and cheering spectators as he dashes from the ring past the braying and bellowing brass-band into the dressing-room !", "context_len": 76}}}, "capers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "Suddenly away flash wig and beard and false-face , the pantaloons are stripped off with the same movement , the actor stoops for the reins lying on the horse 's neck , and James Rivers , the greatest three-horse rider in the world nimbly capers on the broad pad , and kisses his hand to the shouting and cheering spectators as he dashes from the ring past the braying and bellowing brass-band into the dressing-room !", "context_len": 76}}}, "kisses": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "Suddenly away flash wig and beard and false-face , the pantaloons are stripped off with the same movement , the actor stoops for the reins lying on the horse 's neck , and James Rivers , the greatest three-horse rider in the world nimbly capers on the broad pad , and kisses his hand to the shouting and cheering spectators as he dashes from the ring past the braying and bellowing brass-band into the dressing-room !", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 77, "context": "There would have been exclamations of admiration and delight and much wondering as to who could have placed them there , -- '' some good fairy perhaps who knew that these were the very things that were wanted , '' and such speeches , all of which Maggie and Bessie would have enjoyed highly , and at last it would be asked if they could possibly have made them , and then would have come thanks and kisses", "context_len": 78}}}, "braying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "Suddenly away flash wig and beard and false-face , the pantaloons are stripped off with the same movement , the actor stoops for the reins lying on the horse 's neck , and James Rivers , the greatest three-horse rider in the world nimbly capers on the broad pad , and kisses his hand to the shouting and cheering spectators as he dashes from the ring past the braying and bellowing brass-band into the dressing-room !", "context_len": 76}}}, "shakes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "The big boys have known all along that he was not a real country-jake ; but when the trained mule begins , and shakes everybody off , just like the horse , and another country-jake gets up , and offers to bet that he can ride that mule , nobody can tell whether he is a real country-jake or not", "context_len": 60}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "And when suitors throng about her she only shakes her head", "context_len": 11}}}, "offers": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "Constant references as to his fondness for female society , offers to see him home to protect him on the way , tender inquiries as to the state of his eyes and the condition of his ears , filled him with confusion , while large portraits of Jones in the clutches of an imaginary Amazon , executed with schoolboy talent and vigour , adorned the walls of the playground and the palings of the cricket-field", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 51, "context": "Filelfo , who was greatly attached to D'Aubusson , saw by their manner that they wished to engage him in some intrigue , and , feigning great resentment and anger at his disgrace , led them on until they divulged the entire plot for D'Aubusson 's assassination , and made brilliant offers to him if he would afford them facilities for carrying it out , producing , in proof of their power to do so , a letter of the pasha , authorising them to make such promises in his name", "context_len": 91}}}, "betokening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "This is always the last thing in the performance , and the boys have seen with heavy hearts many signs openly betokening the end which they knew was at hand", "context_len": 30}}}, "everyday": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 19, "context": "The polishing of the darkened gold of her Will she had long known in the small unavoidable frictions of everyday life ; and the plunging of that Will into furnace-heat , and the straightening of its crookedness by means of heavy successive blows , she had begun to know in the death of her dear Father , and would soon know more fully through other sorrows coming after", "context_len": 68}}}, "overcoats": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "The actors have come out of the dressing-room door , some in their everyday clothes , and some with just overcoats on over their circus-dresses , and they lounge about near the band-stand watching the performance in the ring", "context_len": 39}}}, "eke": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "Some of the people are already getting up to go out , and stand for this last act , and will not mind the shouts of `` Down in front ! Down there ! '' which the boys eagerly join in , to eke out their bliss a little longer by keeping away even the appearance of anything transitory in it", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "When the Marid heard Alaeddin 's words , his face frowned and he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "transitory": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "Some of the people are already getting up to go out , and stand for this last act , and will not mind the shouts of `` Down in front ! Down there ! '' which the boys eagerly join in , to eke out their bliss a little longer by keeping away even the appearance of anything transitory in it", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It was one of those transitory yet indelibly-stamped impressions that are frequently formed in times of imminent danger", "context_len": 18}}}, "stumbling": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 3, "context": "The country-jake comes stumbling awkwardly into the ring , but he is perfectly sober , and he boldly leaps astride the mule , which tries all its arts to shake him off , plunging , kicking , rearing", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Alas ! I could but feel how much reason the ill lives of Christians had given him to form this opinion , and of the curse pronounced upon those who shall put a stumbling block in their brother 's way", "context_len": 40}}}, "arts": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "Accordingly these wicked sorcerers found constant pretexts for involving the tribes in war , especially if they supposed that the only daughter of a chieftain loved a brave of another tribe ; but for many years all their arts were in vain , for the Indians were so passionate and revengeful that immediately an affront was given or received , violent hatred vanquished love , and the chiefs and their families were saved", "context_len": 73}}}, "plunging": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 88, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "In her plan to give Joe a fright , she had never thought of Father , who returned by the same road ; and now that horrible white object was stationed just in the very danger-spot where a plunging horse might mean a matter of almost certain death , for the wall of the five-acre field abutted the road on one side , and on the other there was only a narrow patch of grass between the steep bank which shelved down sheer into the river , while the closed gate stopped any chance of a dash forward", "context_len": 98}}}, "convulsive": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "At last , with one convulsive spring , it flings him from its back , and dashes into the dressing-room , while the country-jake picks himself up and vanishes among the crowd", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 23, "context": "I shut my eyes and screamed in terror , and on opening them I saw -- oh , merciful Heaven ! -- a convulsive form lying in the road , while the Roundhead stood watching me intently , the smoke from his petronel hanging round like a pall , and slowly ascending in the chill night air", "context_len": 57}}}, "flings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "At last , with one convulsive spring , it flings him from its back , and dashes into the dressing-room , while the country-jake picks himself up and vanishes among the crowd", "context_len": 32}}}, "admission": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 4, "context": "And suppose I gain admission to the presence , what shall I take by way of offering to the Sultan 's highness ? It [ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm", "context_len": 128}}}, "clamoring": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "Between Jennie and the baby came three little boys , sturdy , healthy children , always clamoring for bread and butter , and frequent calls for bread and butter were becoming a serious matter in the policeman 's household ; for provisions were high , and it was not as easy to feed eight mouths as it had been to feed four", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then she gave the babies a crust of bread to stop their clamoring while she crumbed up some in the saucepan and kept stirring it so that it should n't scorch , taking out part , presently", "context_len": 37}}}, "chooses": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 59, "context": "As you say , the dog would not let the hungry oxen eat the hay he could not use himself , but because Aunt Patty did not choose to buy the grove , we have no right to suppose she would not make , or has not made some other good use of her money , and if she chooses to keep that a secret , she has a right to do so", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "These words were addressed to the saddle , which he flung impatiently to the ground : whoever chooses can find you ; I don \u2019 t tote you another rod", "context_len": 30}}}, "rivalry": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 26, "context": "The cause of the warring , or the pretense for it , against `` some other Indians from the west '' is curiously similar to the rivalry in athletic sports , which took place between the western Iroquois and their subdivisions , and finally led to the destruction of the Erie or K\u00e1'hkwa Indians ( Cusick , Johnson )", "context_len": 59}}}, "rounders": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "After dinner one of the elder girls suggested rounders , and the game grew so enthralling that time flew by until the bell , ringing for afternoon school , sent the players , hot and rosy with their exertions , hurrying up the great staircase to their classrooms", "context_len": 48}}}, "printers": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 51, "context": "When they got through with it my boy 's brother made himself a ramrod out of a straight piece of hickory , or at least as straight as the gun-barrel , which was rather sway-backed , and had a little twist to one side , so that one of the jour printers said it was a first-rate gun to shoot round a corner with", "context_len": 64}}}, "printer": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 56, "context": "He did not get home till nearly ten o'clock , though , and he saw the sides of the tent dropped before the people got out ; that was a great thing ; and what was greater yet , and reflected a kind of splendor on the boy at second hand , was that the jour printer and the clown turned out to be old friends", "context_len": 66}}}, "grease": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "The New Englander had stripped , and had anointed himself from head to foot with a dark , offensive-smelling grease , which , he assured us , would keep him immune from insect bites , and at the same time render him nearly invisible", "context_len": 44}}}, "dripped": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 82, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}}}, "shaved": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "They knew the fellows who were helping the priest ; one of them was the boy who stood on his head till he had to have it shaved ; they would have liked to mock him then and there for wearing a petticoat , and most of them had the bitterest scorn and hate for Catholics in their hearts ; but they were afraid of the sacristan , and they behaved very well as long as they were in the church ; but as soon as they got out they whooped and yelled , and stoned the sacristan when he ran after them", "context_len": 103}}}, "rail": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 14, "context": "When with Chris it was forward clear to the bow to lean over the rail and see the magnificent prow cut the water ; or way to the stern , to watch the far-shining train , the screws churned into white foam behind them ; or an hour 'midships , where the ever-varying amusements with which the steerage passengers beguile the weary hours can be looked down upon from the saloon deck of either first or second cabin", "context_len": 78}}}, "practising": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 5, "context": "And here he was , practising among a few Mexicans and miners , tending their bruises , doling them out quinine , and taking pay of a dollar a month from every man , sick or well , enrolled on the mine books , and frequently getting nothing at all from such as were not therein enrolled", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Why , going over my studies with her , and learning to do things about the house ; and then my practising , too ?", "context_len": 25}}}, "forming": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 24, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "And that is the story of the forming of West Point 's first and only secret society , a society which was destined to introduce some very , very exciting incidents into West Point 's dignified history , the Seven Devils , B", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "The full form of the tribal name is Bayuk-\u00f3kla or river-tribe , creek- or bayou-people ; the Cha'hta word for a smaller river , or river forming part of a delta is b\u00e1yuk , contr", "context_len": 35}}}, "despicable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "You could get girls , but even as spectators girls were a little too despicable ; they did not know anything ; they had no sense ; if a follow got hurt they cried", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was a pity , indeed , that Dimmock should have forced his daughter into such a tangle of deception ; and doubly a pity that one so young and fair could have played the despicable part so boldly , and given her false answers without a tremor , or a pang of conscience", "context_len": 54}}}, "strain": {"freq": 85, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 79, "word_index": 2, "context": "But the strain was too much for Peggy , and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "The long wished-for moon at length arose , hours after Guy had expected her , but too soon he thought when she made her appearance , for the camp fires grew dim beneath her rays , and he had to strain his aching eyes to see them at all", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "No one in that crowd knew under what a strain Bart Keene was at that moment", "context_len": 16}}}, "minstrels": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 60, "context": "The last spark had died out of the paper in the grate and left only black tinder ; the table was left bare , the golden plates and richly embroidered napkins , and the garlands were transformed again into old handkerchiefs , scraps of red and white paper , and discarded artificial flowers all scattered on the floor ; the minstrels in the minstrel gallery had stolen away , and the viols and bassoons were still", "context_len": 76}}}, "strolling": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "There were only two kinds of show known by that name in the Boy 's Town : a Nigger Show , or a performance of burnt-cork minstrels ; and an Animal Show , or a strolling menagerie ; and the boys always meant a menagerie when they spoke of a show , unless they said just what sort of show", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "There had once been a theatre in the Boy 's Town , when a strolling company came up from Cincinnati , and opened for a season in an empty pork-house", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 11, "context": "Harris that evening , as in company with Marie-Celeste they were strolling home from an hour spent in the little churchyard where the great poet Southey is buried , I think it is beautiful to realize what a grand part Providence plays in the world", "context_len": 45}}}, "menagerie": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` Bradford , '' he continued , in the most mournful tones , `` since we are not to go over to the policeman 's this afternoon , I had thought we might have some other little frolic ; but of course , none of us are in spirits for the visit to the menagerie I had intended to propose", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Visitors are politely requested not to feed or annoy the animals , '' quoted Bart , from some menagerie sign", "context_len": 21}}}, "unquestionably": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "Belden 's hand when Marie-Celeste made his acquaintance had proved to be Dixon 's Royal Windsor ; and as soon as the terms of their friendship were unquestionably established , she made so bold as to ask many questions regarding its contents ; for what could have more interest for a Windsor-bound little maiden than the story of the Royal Castle ? And the best part of it was that the book happened to be the second volume , and therefore contained the history of Madame la Petite Reine , as the little French Isabel was called", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The scent of the ursus species is by no means as fine as that of many other animals , but this one unquestionably was on the track of something and it looked very much as if that something was an American youngster crouching behind a boulder a few rods off and scared almost out of his wits", "context_len": 57}}}, "processional": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It made a processional entry into the town almost as impressive as the circus 's , and the boys went out to meet it beyond the corporation line in the same way", "context_len": 32}}}, "impressive": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "The huge cathedral church towered above the masses , rude in architecture , yet still impressive in its proportions , while another church , scarcely smaller in its dimensions , rose from the banks lower down the stream , below the bridge , and the wooden steeple of a third was visible above the roofs of the houses in the western part of the city", "context_len": 65}}}, "giraffe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "It always had two elephants , at least , and four or five camels , and sometimes there was a giraffe", "context_len": 21}}}, "keepers": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 114, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "halters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "These headed the procession , the elephants in the very front , with their keepers at their heads , and then the camels led by halters dangling from their sneering lips and contemptuous noses", "context_len": 34}}}, "sneering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "These headed the procession , the elephants in the very front , with their keepers at their heads , and then the camels led by halters dangling from their sneering lips and contemptuous noses", "context_len": 34}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "He had noticed a little earlier his big brother , Ira , a loutish , overgrown fellow who had gone around with his hands in his pockets sneering at the innocent fun the smaller boys were indulging in , and bragging about his own especial Fourth of July supply of fireworks which were to come from some mysterious source not clearly defined", "context_len": 62}}}, "tigers": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 75, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}}}, "meaner": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "A beautiful portrait of the Queen of Bohemia , that could come from no meaner brush than that of Vandyke , hung on the wall , while beneath it a table was heaped with dusty bottles and jars , retorts , and powder-flasks", "context_len": 43}}}, "leopards": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}}}, "pumas": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 53, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}}}, "panthers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}}}, "jackals": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 104, "context": "Neither Peggy nor Bobby had ever seen a wild-beast show before , for those visits to the Zoo , which are the joy of little Londoners , do not fall to the share of country children , and Archie had quite a lively time keeping them out of harm 's way , for Peggy declared the leopard looked so exactly like Tabbyskins at home that she must positively try to stroke it , and was under the rope and up to the bars before Archie could seize her by her skirts and drag her back , while Bobby 's curiosity on the subject of jackals and hyenas nearly led to the ejection of the whole party from the tent", "context_len": 119}}}, "hyenas": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 106, "context": "Neither Peggy nor Bobby had ever seen a wild-beast show before , for those visits to the Zoo , which are the joy of little Londoners , do not fall to the share of country children , and Archie had quite a lively time keeping them out of harm 's way , for Peggy declared the leopard looked so exactly like Tabbyskins at home that she must positively try to stroke it , and was under the rope and up to the bars before Archie could seize her by her skirts and drag her back , while Bobby 's curiosity on the subject of jackals and hyenas nearly led to the ejection of the whole party from the tent", "context_len": 119}}}, "wolves": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 53, "context": "Guy was greatly saddened by the sufferings of the child , and by the grief of its parents , and shuddered when he saw the bones of animals which lay by thousands bleaching upon the desert , and once was filled with horror on coming across a human skull , which the prairie wolves had dragged from some shallow grave , and separated far from its kindred bones", "context_len": 68}}}, "ostriches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 84, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}}}, "emus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 86, "context": "After these began to come the show-wagons , with pictures on their sides , very flattered portraits of the wild beasts and birds inside ; lions first , then tigers ( never meaner than Royal Bengal ones , which the boys understood to be a superior breed ) , then leopards , then pumas and panthers ; then bears , then jackals and hyenas ; then bears and wolves ; then kangaroos , musk-oxen , deer , and such harmless cattle ; and then ostriches , emus , lyre-birds , birds-of-Paradise and all the rest", "context_len": 95}}}, "dramatized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "From time to time the boys ran back from the elephants and camels to get what good they could out of the scenes in which these hidden wonders were dramatized in acts of rapine or the chase , but they always came forward to the elephants and camels again", "context_len": 49}}}, "rapine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "From time to time the boys ran back from the elephants and camels to get what good they could out of the scenes in which these hidden wonders were dramatized in acts of rapine or the chase , but they always came forward to the elephants and camels again", "context_len": 49}}}, "denial": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 46, "context": "She reproached him for having appealed to Hannah , a servant in his father 's house , for aid ; and in her turn had to hear his reproaches for believing that he would condescend to such a thing , and received an emphatic and solemn denial that he had been guilty of this , or that he had ever let Hannah know of the straits he was in", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "He would have denied it could he have found words in which to do it , had he had time to frame a denial , but he was so entirely off his guard , so confounded by Seabrooke 's sudden accusation and this evidence of the dastardly deed he had performed that he was utterly overwhelmed , and stood speechless , and the picture of detected guilt", "context_len": 67}}}, "draped": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "Ten minutes , twenty minutes , passed , and the sun was within a hand's- breadth of the horizon when a man emerged from the `` palace , '' bearing a massive chair of ebony , quaintly-carved , and draped with a magnificent leopard 's skin , which he placed immediately before the open door , midway between the house and ourselves , and departed", "context_len": 65}}}, "waded": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 40, "context": "A boy had heard tell that bladders would float a person , and thought he would walk on the water with them ; so he went down to the pond , tied the bladders on to his feet , and waded into the water : they found him , a few hours afterwards , feet up and head down , as dead as a herring ; and that would have been the way with our craft", "context_len": 76}}}, "wading": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 18, "context": "The lych-gate was locked , but she toiled over the steep steps that spanned the wall , and wading through the long grass under the yew-trees , found the spot , all covered with flowers , which Lilian had pointed out on Sunday , where 'Mary , the wife of the Reverend Philip Howell , ' slept , 'in sure and certain hope of the resurrection to eternal life , ' and where the stonemason had been already busy with the newly-added line : 'Also Raymond , only son of the above", "context_len": 92}}}, "squirt": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Neptune was a favorite with the boys , though they believed that the Tremont could squirt farther , and they had a belief in its quiet efficiency which was fostered by its reticence in public", "context_len": 36}}}, "poke": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 21, "context": "Then there was Pixie , the pony , who would come whinnying up from the further side of a field to poke her soft nose into the children 's pockets for pieces of bread or lumps of sugar ; there were numerous cats who lived in the barns and stables , and Tabbyskins , the stately gray Persian , who usually sat sunning herself on the pigsty wall , keeping a strict eye on naughty Jack , who was wont to harry her if he got the chance", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "More chipping , then more roasting , and the growing boomerang was now and again tossed carelessly on the ground just to see how it would act , while he glanced at it sideways , gave it a poke with his foot , and reminded me of a sedate old tom cat playing with a mouse", "context_len": 56}}}, "bars": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 82, "context": "Neither Peggy nor Bobby had ever seen a wild-beast show before , for those visits to the Zoo , which are the joy of little Londoners , do not fall to the share of country children , and Archie had quite a lively time keeping them out of harm 's way , for Peggy declared the leopard looked so exactly like Tabbyskins at home that she must positively try to stroke it , and was under the rope and up to the bars before Archie could seize her by her skirts and drag her back , while Bobby 's curiosity on the subject of jackals and hyenas nearly led to the ejection of the whole party from the tent", "context_len": 119}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "'Therefore , they thought it good you hear a play , And frame your mind to mirth and merriment , Which bars a thousand harms , and lengthens life", "context_len": 29}}}, "lion": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 10, "context": "Yet , even at this awful crisis , Edmund 's lion heart did not wholly fail him , as he left the field where lay all the flower of the Anglo-Saxon race : the brave and faithful Ulfketyl , Earl Ethelweard , Earl Godwin , Elfric the ealdorman , and well nigh all the great men of England , all sleeping in death", "context_len": 63}}}, "dramatic": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 40, "context": "might have reached a larger circle , might have gained a more extensive influence , if she had less anxiously pressed so very much didactic talk into her tales , -- if too she had more studiously cultivated her own dramatic instincts , and had more closely studied human nature", "context_len": 50}}}, "climax": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 8, "context": "It did keep up till one morning the climax was reached when Miss Newman came into her school-room to find on the board a very good caricature of herself , with under it written : `` Ugly , old Miss New , '' in scrawling letters", "context_len": 46}}}, "curtained": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "If you had the courage you could go up the ladder into the curtained tower on the elephant 's back , and ride round the ring with some of the other fellows ; but my boy at least never had the courage ; and he never was of those who mounted the trick pony and were shaken off as soon as they got on", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Who sprinkled the kohl of enchantment upon her eyes And gathered the bloom of the rose from her cheeks , fruit-wise ? And who was it let down the curtained night of her hair And eke through its glooms made the light of her forehead rise ?", "context_len": 47}}}, "risk": {"freq": 130, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 4, "context": "He is ready to risk his life on behalf of a slave , and can strike down men with his unarmed hand ; he is as gentle in his manner as a woman ; and now it seems he can talk Arabic , and although it was in his power to keep this secret he tells it rather than overhear words that are not meant for his ear", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 94, "word_index": 97, "context": "or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length , as well because it is an important one as to show the almost insuperable difficulties that beset the", "context_len": 128}}}, "cocked": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "It was a splendid gun , but it would not stand cocked long , and he was resting it on the wall of the fort , ready to fire when the storming-party came on , throwing sods and yelling and holloing ; and all at once his gun went off , and a cow that was grazing broadside to the fort gave a frightened bellow , and put up her tail , and started for home", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Do you remember the name of Thaurepas ( ? ) , the blacky General who weakly surrendered his post to the French ? What do you think the grateful Monsieurs did to him ? Nailed epaulettes on his shoulders and a cocked hat on his head , and then threw him with his wife and children into the sea ! Would one believe such things of men in the 19th century ? I should like to know something of the present state of Hayti , and whether the throne is filled by a son of Henri I", "context_len": 97}}}, "beliefs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 68, "context": "He ended as they drew rein before the farmhouse where they were to pass the night , and the boy , thrilled and awed , had no voice to answer , but the grasp of his uncle 's hand , and the memory of his uncle 's words remained with him , as a consecration of his new life as a soldier , and moulded his doings and beliefs for all his life after", "context_len": 74}}}, "zebra": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "I do not know why the zebra should have had this repute , for he certainly never did anything to deserve it ; but , for the matter of that , he was like all the other animals", "context_len": 38}}}, "repute": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "on this wise and said to him , `` Prithee , fair youth , who is this whom you describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both far and near have heard ? How is it thou hast heard nought of this nor of the name of Alaeddin , whom Our Lord increase in glory and prosper ? '' Quoth the Maugrabin , `` Marry , it is the utmost", "context_len": 128}}}, "fiercest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 41, "context": "First , before all things , Sir Knight , let me express to you all our deep gratitude and our admiration of the gallant deed that you have accomplished in thus , single handed , capturing three vessels belonging to the fiercest and most dreaded of the corsairs of Tripoli", "context_len": 50}}}, "retaken": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was always hoped that some of the fiercest animals would get away and have to be hunted down , and retaken after they had killed a lot of dogs", "context_len": 30}}}, "roamed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "If the elephants , some of them , had gone crazy , it would have been something , for then they would have roamed up and down the turnpike smashing buggies and wagons , and had to be shot with the six-pound cannon that was used to celebrate the Fourth of July with", "context_len": 53}}}, "turnpike": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "If the elephants , some of them , had gone crazy , it would have been something , for then they would have roamed up and down the turnpike smashing buggies and wagons , and had to be shot with the six-pound cannon that was used to celebrate the Fourth of July with", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The child herself , however , did not seem to be troubled with the slightest fear , for she walked on as steadily as if the beam had been a plain turnpike road , giving a shout of triumph as she reached the cross-bar , and slid down the ladder on to the ground", "context_len": 54}}}, "smashing": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "If the elephants , some of them , had gone crazy , it would have been something , for then they would have roamed up and down the turnpike smashing buggies and wagons , and had to be shot with the six-pound cannon that was used to celebrate the Fourth of July with", "context_len": 53}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "As the Prussian officer raised his revolver to fire point-blank at Sergeant Fortescue , Malcolm plunged his bayonet into the Hun 's side , while Fortescue reciprocated the service by shooting a German who was about to deal Rifleman Carr a smashing blow with the butt of his rifle before the latter could disengage his blade", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "SMASHING THE LINE", "context_len": 3}}}, "cannon": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 2, "context": "But the cannon of that period were so cumbrous and slowly worked , and so inaccurate in their aim , that the advantage of occupying a position that would prevent an enemy from firing down into a town was considered to be more than counterbalanced by the weakening of the garrison by the abstraction of the force required to man the detached work , and by the risk of their being surrounded and cut off without the garrison of the town being able to aid them", "context_len": 86}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He recognized what the foolhardy escapade might have cost had that whirling cannon ball met a human , instead of an inanimate , target", "context_len": 24}}}, "trodden": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was a tiny hand , that of your precious little child , which pointed it out , and showed me the way by which I must go ; but my feet have so long trodden the road which leads to death , that now , when I would set my face the other way , they falter and stumble", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "They were so many that they cumbered the house , and were trodden under foot ; and sometimes they brought him to open shame , as when his brother picked one of them up , and began to read it out loud with affected admiration", "context_len": 45}}}, "camel": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 67, "context": "I had never , never even by my most intimate friends , been spoken of as the `` little thing , '' for I was a tall girl and somewhat large in my ideas , and if anything rather masculine in my mind , and to be spoken of as a little thing , and by Albert Fanning , was about the final straw which broke the camel 's back", "context_len": 70}}}, "negro": {"freq": 73, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Of course you could have negro shows , and the boys often had them ; but they were not much fun , and you were always getting the black on your shirt-sleeves", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "He lay on the floor and rolled about in an agony of pain , and the negro stood over him , apparently waiting for a fitting opportunity to put in the finishing touch , but his hard head had done the work for the time being", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 56, "context": "In the third French war against the Naktche a large body of Cha'hta warriors served as allies under the French commander , and on January 27 , 1730 , before daylight , made a furious onslaught on their principal village , killing sixty enemies and rescuing fifty-nine French women and children and one hundred and fifty negro slaves previously captured by the tribe ( Claiborne , Mississippi , I , 45", "context_len": 71}}}, "persuasion": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 33, "context": "Christmas was the day that was kept in all churches and families , whether they were Methodists or Episcopalians , Baptists or Universalists , Catholics or Protestants ; and among boys of whatever persuasion it was kept in a fashion that I suppose may have survived from the early pioneer times , when the means of expressing joy were few and primitive", "context_len": 62}}}, "survived": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 45, "context": "Christmas was the day that was kept in all churches and families , whether they were Methodists or Episcopalians , Baptists or Universalists , Catholics or Protestants ; and among boys of whatever persuasion it was kept in a fashion that I suppose may have survived from the early pioneer times , when the means of expressing joy were few and primitive", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "But against them he matched his own courage and cunning , and had so far survived", "context_len": 16}}}, "expressing": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 39, "context": "He tells me that he has heard from Ben Ibyn , the Berber , that you are a slave , and has asked me to inquire of the sultan the price that he will take for your ransom , expressing his willingness to pay whatever may be demanded , and charging me to defray the sum and to make arrangements by which you may return to Europe", "context_len": 67}}}, "pistols": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 57, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "torpedoes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was nearly eight o'clock when Bart got through with his supper , did his house chores , mended a broken toy pistol for one junior brother , made up a list of purchases of torpedoes , baby-crackers and punk for the other , and helped his sisters in various ways", "context_len": 51}}}, "ammunition": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 45, "context": "As the heaviest part of his work at headquarters was over , and as pretty much everything had depended on the reply to General Forrest 's requisition on his superior officer -- who , unfortunately , chanced to be General Bragg -- for arms and ammunition , Harry had no difficulty in securing leave of absence for the day ; and so , when all the arrangements had been made , we set out the next evening for the house where Jack Bledsoe lay", "context_len": 84}}}, "commonest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "There were all sorts of pistols ; but the commonest was one that the boys called a Christmas-crack ; it was of brass , and when it burst the barrel curled up like a dandelion stem when you split it and put it in water", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The grand master himself set the example , and , throwing aside his robes and armour , laboured with pick and shovel like the commonest labourer", "context_len": 26}}}, "stem": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 18, "context": "She had a moment 's struggle with herself ; then , breaking one of the bananas from the stem , she went a little nearer to Dolly and held it out at arm 's length , for she feared the mischievous girl too much to go very close to her", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "Whoever it was had lost his nerve and was in some peril , for though not above a hundred yards or so from the shore he was in the race of a fierce current that at certain periods of the tide ran so swiftly amongst the rocks that a strongly-manned boat could not stem its force", "context_len": 56}}}, "trophy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "It was a great glory to him , in a martial point of view , to have such trophies of his victory in his train ; and , of course , the more highly he honored the personages , the more glorious the trophy appeared", "context_len": 45}}}, "exploded": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 34, "context": "But Master Archie evidently thought discretion the better part of valour , for he dived through the doorway like a rabbit into a burrow , his overwrought feelings so far overcoming him that he exploded into a tremendous cock-crow as he sought the friendly shelter of Sky Cottage", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Although his arms and legs were unprotected , the man had practically escaped injury from the bombs , since the fragments of the exploded missile flew upwards", "context_len": 27}}}, "thumbs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Some fellows would hold a cracker in their hands till it exploded ; nearly everybody had burned thumbs , and some of the boys had their faces blackened with powder", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It \u2019 ll be swell fun pulling up close to his wing tip and giving him the old thumbs down \u2019 signal", "context_len": 22}}}, "blackened": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 24, "context": "Some of those boys had hands as hard as horn , cracked open at the knuckles and in the palms , and the crevices blackened with earth or grime ; and they taught my boy to believe that he was an inferior and unmanly person , almost of the nature of a cry-baby , because his hands were not horn-like , and cracked open , and filled with dirt", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 88, "context": "The twinkling lights in the harbor were still shining when the little girls went to bed , but before morning a wilder light was blazing from the point where old Cap'n Si 's little house stood , and , the next morning when the children looked across to where yesterday they had seen the old man sitting on the bench outside his door , the smoke curling from the chimney and the flowers in his little garden making a brave showing , they beheld but a heap of blackened ruins", "context_len": 90}}}, "celebrating": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "She completely recognized the fidelity and sense of her loyal son , allowed Bart to lead her into the house , and tried to be calm and cheerful when he bade her good-bye , and , evading celebrating groups of his boy friends , made his way down to the ruined express shed", "context_len": 53}}}, "lowly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 12, "context": "It is more suited for an Earl and Countess than for two lowly Missionaries ; and yet our rent is only a little more than 20 a year ! Certainly , we have had to make that very necessary article , a fireplace , and to build servants \u2019 huts ; but the house is grand ! It seems unnatural to be the lady of it", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "On the grass around this lowly table the family were seated , all cheerful and all by the labors of the day blessed with an appetite that rendered their first meal in camp perfectly delicious", "context_len": 35}}}, "awed": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "He ended as they drew rein before the farmhouse where they were to pass the night , and the boy , thrilled and awed , had no voice to answer , but the grasp of his uncle 's hand , and the memory of his uncle 's words remained with him , as a consecration of his new life as a soldier , and moulded his doings and beliefs for all his life after", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 38, "context": "A strange minstrel , a young gleeman , had been admitted that night -- one whose chain and robes bespoke him of the privileged class -- and he sang in a voice which thrilled all the revellers into awed silence", "context_len": 40}}}, "incense": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 68, "context": "The best place for them was in some woods-pasture where the trees stood free of one another , and around them , in among the tall , frosty grass , the tumbled nuts lay scattered in groups of twos and threes , or fives , some still yellowish-green in their hulls , and some black , but all sending up to the nostrils of the delighted boy the incense of their clean , keen , wild-woody smell , to be a memory forever", "context_len": 83}}}, "grimness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "When they had burst their pistols or fired off their crackers , the boys sometimes huddled into the back part of the Catholic church and watched the service , awed by the dim altar lights , the rising smoke of incense , and the grimness of the sacristan , an old German , who stood near to keep order among them", "context_len": 61}}}, "sacristan": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "When they had burst their pistols or fired off their crackers , the boys sometimes huddled into the back part of the Catholic church and watched the service , awed by the dim altar lights , the rising smoke of incense , and the grimness of the sacristan , an old German , who stood near to keep order among them", "context_len": 61}}}, "bitterest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "They knew the fellows who were helping the priest ; one of them was the boy who stood on his head till he had to have it shaved ; they would have liked to mock him then and there for wearing a petticoat , and most of them had the bitterest scorn and hate for Catholics in their hearts ; but they were afraid of the sacristan , and they behaved very well as long as they were in the church ; but as soon as they got out they whooped and yelled , and stoned the sacristan when he ran after them", "context_len": 103}}}, "whooped": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 90, "context": "They knew the fellows who were helping the priest ; one of them was the boy who stood on his head till he had to have it shaved ; they would have liked to mock him then and there for wearing a petticoat , and most of them had the bitterest scorn and hate for Catholics in their hearts ; but they were afraid of the sacristan , and they behaved very well as long as they were in the church ; but as soon as they got out they whooped and yelled , and stoned the sacristan when he ran after them", "context_len": 103}}}, "yelled": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 92, "context": "They knew the fellows who were helping the priest ; one of them was the boy who stood on his head till he had to have it shaved ; they would have liked to mock him then and there for wearing a petticoat , and most of them had the bitterest scorn and hate for Catholics in their hearts ; but they were afraid of the sacristan , and they behaved very well as long as they were in the church ; but as soon as they got out they whooped and yelled , and stoned the sacristan when he ran after them", "context_len": 103}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "'Gee up ! Let her go ! Hooray ! ' yelled Bobby , striking such a whack on Brindle 's heaving side that she settled the matter by suddenly lying down to roll , and depositing her encumbrances in the miry ditch", "context_len": 42}}}, "prejudice": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 53, "context": "Clavering having reckoned up the marks , Mademoiselle Le Brun having given her testimony , Fraulein having given hers , and the English teachers having further testified to the industry of the pupils , the girls of the Upper school were to pass muster before Sir John , who was to decide without prejudice in favor of the lucky three who alone were to compete for the great Scholarship in October", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "Bradford would be only too ready to forgive and forget all that was disagreeable in the past , and she also felt that they would do nothing to prejudice the minds of their children against her", "context_len": 36}}}, "crucifix": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "I held up the crucifix , which hung at my girdle , on high ; I threw my arm over his head , and abjured them under the name of Christ , and as they feared the curse of the Church , to forbear", "context_len": 44}}}, "thorns": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 31, "context": "He liked to go to the Catholic church , though he was afraid of the painted figure that hung full length on the wooden crucifix , with the blood-drops under the thorns on its forehead , and the red wound in its side", "context_len": 43}}}, "nicholas": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 0, "context": "Nicholas , you saved the life of the grand master ; and in the order of the day he issued on the morning we left , granting you three months ' leave for the recovery of your wounds , he took the opportunity of recording , in the name of the council and himself , their admiration for the services rendered by you during the siege , and his own gratitude for saving his life when he lay helpless and surrounded by the Moslems -- a testimony of which any knight of Christendom might well feel proud", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "NICHOLAS", "context_len": 1}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Nicholas until he had got rid of his five fire ships , then to advance and launch his craft against the Turks", "context_len": 22}}}, "sordid": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "Death was easy enough to face among the high enthusiasms of the battlefield ; but here , in the city , where the busy world was eating and drinking and making money among these sordid surroundings , what radiance of a celestial city could flash from opened gates to support a victim through a torturing death ? Could faith win a victory even here ?", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "This was an incident which suggested strange things to her sordid mind", "context_len": 12}}}, "origin": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 23, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "They worshiped the sun in the shape of a stuffed deer raised upon the end of a high beam planted in the ground ; this gave , probably , origin to their name A\u00efs , for B", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Possibly the Creek word \u014d sta four , in the sense of `` four allied tribes , '' has given origin to this tribal name ( ost\u00e1ka in Alibamu )", "context_len": 30}}}, "consolation": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 18, "context": "But through the long dull hours of sewing in Aunt Isham 's dressing-room , her unfailing treasure of consolation was in repeating to herself all the teachings she had received from her grandfather -- words that could never be breathed aloud in Madam Isham 's house ; of liberty , and the rights of the people to representation and civil justice , teachings that were drawn from writings as far asunder as Bishop Taylor 's `` Liberty of Prophesying , '' and Mr", "context_len": 83}}}, "logwood": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "The parlor had what was called a flowered-carpet or gay pattern of ingrain on its floor , and the other rooms had rag-carpets , woven by some woman who had a loom for the work , and dyed at home with such native tints as butternut and foreign colors as logwood", "context_len": 51}}}, "calico": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "Papa and Uncle John have gone over to see them ; and mamma and Aunt Helen have gone to the village to buy some flannel and calico to make things for the poor little children who have lost theirs", "context_len": 39}}}, "pattern": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 106, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "That 's the very pattern we saw Aunt Annie working the day that 'bomnable Miss Adams pulled your hair", "context_len": 19}}}, "outlasted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But most Easter eggs never outlasted Easter Day", "context_len": 8}}}, "stumped": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Drat ye ! snarled Tige Fletcher , his face contorted with rage as he stumped forward , shaking his crooked cane at Ben Stone", "context_len": 24}}}, "observance": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 82, "context": "The Sultan was amazed when he saw Alaeddin in this regal array and beheld his grace and goodliness and the mamelukes standing in attendance upon him in all their comeliness and fair favour ; yea , and his wonderment redoubled when Alaeddin 's mother came up attired in rich and costly raiment , as she were a queen , and he saw twelve slave-girls in her service , preceding her , their hands clasped behind their backs , with all worship and observance", "context_len": 83}}}, "pin": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "About the best thing you could do then was to write April Fool on a piece of paper and pin it to a fellow 's back , or maybe a girl 's , if she was a big girl , and stuck-up , or anything", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 109, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}}}, "brickbats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "I do not suppose there is a boy now living who is silly enough to play this trick on anybody , or mean enough to fill an old hat with rocks and brickbats , and dare a fellow to kick it ; but in the Boy 's Town there were some boys who did this ; and then the fellow had to kick the hat , or else come under the shame of having taken a dare", "context_len": 77}}}, "tassels": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "The trees were clothed with the pale , tender green of April , and a cuckoo , the first of the year , flew out of the copse , and , cuckooing loudly , sped over to where the larch-trees were bursting out into a crowd of tassels", "context_len": 48}}}, "banners": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 19, "context": "In the window sat a raccoon , which was the Whig emblem , and which , on all their banners , was painted with the legend , `` That same old Coon ! '' to show that they had not changed at all since the great days when they elected the pioneer , General Harrison , president of the United States", "context_len": 61}}}, "poisoned": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 11, "context": "At any rate , the May Party was apt to be poisoned at its source by questions of class ; and I think it might have been in the talk about precedence , and who should be what , that my boy first heard that such and such a girl 's father was a mechanic , and that it was somehow dishonorable to be a mechanic", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "I never shall forget how rejoiced I was when we got into a pure atmosphere and healthy soil again , but it was weeks , yes , even months , before the effects of my poisoned draught passed entirely away", "context_len": 40}}}, "precedence": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 45, "context": "Whether the powers that rule the entrance to the same came to the conclusion that to a little girl of twelve and a little boy of four the term of general public could not honestly be applied , or whether all rules of procedure and precedence were magnanimously waived in their favor , certain it is that the little card in question bore the incredible inscription : Admit Master Albert Allyn and his little friend , Miss Marie-Celeste Harris , to the East Terrace between the hours of twelve and three on Thursday", "context_len": 93}}}, "mechanic": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "If a member read something of interest in history , morality , Poetry , physic , travels , mechanic arts , he shared his new knowledge with his fellow members", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "THE man who stood before Gerald was dressed like a mechanic in a working suit somewhat the worse for wear , but he had an honest , intelligent face that inspired confidence", "context_len": 32}}}, "dishonorable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 61, "context": "At any rate , the May Party was apt to be poisoned at its source by questions of class ; and I think it might have been in the talk about precedence , and who should be what , that my boy first heard that such and such a girl 's father was a mechanic , and that it was somehow dishonorable to be a mechanic", "context_len": 66}}}, "grove": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "He was asked to be one of the boys who held the banner-tassels , and he felt this a great compliment somehow , though he was so young that he had afterwards only the vaguest remembrance of marching in the procession , and going to a raw and chilly grove somewhere , and having untimely lemonade and cake", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 41, "context": "She told him how she had heard them all talking of the policeman and his troubles , and how much she had wished that she was able to help him ; how she had thought that the desire to have the grove was only a fancy , right in itself perhaps , but not to be indulged if she could better spend the money for the good of others ; and how , without taking much time to consider the matter , she had decided to give it up", "context_len": 89}}}, "reserves": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "In response to a signal from an observing aeroplane the barrage suddenly parted , some of the guns surging round to the right , others lifting and pounding away at a mass of German reserves", "context_len": 35}}}, "firing": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 98, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}, "NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 41, "context": "When the ceremony was over , Gervaise was solemnly invested with the command of the galley by the grand marshal of the navy ; then the shores were struck away , and the galley glided into the water , amid the firing of guns , the blowing of trumpets , and the cheers of the spectators who had gathered at the port to witness the ceremony", "context_len": 66}}}, "wakened": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 66, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}}}, "salutes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Everybody in town had been wakened by the salutes fired from the six-pounder on the river-bank , and by the noise of guns and pistols ; and right after breakfast you heard that the Butler Guards were out , and you ran up to the court-house yard with the other fellows to see if it was true", "context_len": 57}}}, "festive": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 60, "context": "Archie hung up a Japanese lantern in the doorway , and fixed a Union-Jack on one side , and the star-spangled banner of the United States on the other , and Peggy found enough Michaelmas daisies and white asters to put a wreath all round the railing of the veranda , which rather suggested harvest decorations , but looked very festive all the same", "context_len": 64}}}, "preparation": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 6, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "oration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "There was going to be an oration and a public dinner , and they were already setting the tables under the locust-trees", "context_len": 22}}}, "gingerbread": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "None of the girls have had gingerbread , and I am sure it would be liked", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Jones , whom she had just been saying she did not like , praising and petting her and promising gingerbread men ; and oh , Mr", "context_len": 26}}}, "spree": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "After a pause she said , `` I did remember that yesterday morning ; and knowing that you 'd be frightfully dumpy -- oh , mummy ! you know you never are cheerful -- I thought I 'd have a spree on my own account", "context_len": 45}}}, "unmolested": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "As at present war has not been formally declared between us and the sultan , peaceful traders , as you know , carry on their avocations unmolested either by the warships of the Turks or by ours ; they do not enter our ports without a special permit , and the crews are never allowed to land , in order that no detailed account of our fortifications may be taken to the sultan", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 46, "context": "But Wesley felt no uneasiness , `` knowing , '' as he writes , `` that God would take care of us ; and He did so , for before we came to the spot all the highwaymen were taken , and so we went on unmolested , and came safe to Bristol ''", "context_len": 54}}}, "sorrow": {"freq": 92, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 40, "context": "The boys did not think it at all out of the way for him to be in that state ; they took it as they took the preparations for the public dinner , and no sense of the shame and sorrow it meant penetrated their tough ignorance of life", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 82, "word_index": 15, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was her sister Mary who first spoke about it , having seen with sorrow how changed the once happy blind sister had become , and longing to lighten her burden", "context_len": 31}}}, "drunkards": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 83, "context": "I meant to have begun with older people , and should not have thought of you ; but when I heard that you were all on here together , it seemed to me that the road was pinted out ; that you had no bad habits to break off , and that it would be beginning at the root of the tree ; for if there were no young folks growing up to drink , there would be no old ones to die drunkards", "context_len": 84}}}, "novelty": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 60, "context": "The limbs of the great tree swayed gently in the westerly breeze , and the moonbeams came slanting through them most delightfully , as the boys lay listening to the moan of the night wind , the sound of the surf along the shore , and watched the clouds as they coursed by the moon , all heightened by the novelty of their situation", "context_len": 64}}}, "intemperance": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "At these , not only did he preach the Gospel of Christ , but he made a point of telling the men the blessings of temperance ; and it was by his influence that later on a society was formed in the regiment , and various attractions were placed before the men to keep them from intemperance", "context_len": 57}}}, "strenuous": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 20, "context": "Malcolm and Dick found bayonet exercise exciting work -- thrusting at suspended sacks stuffed with straw called for strength and strenuous activity -- while at the ranges both lads gained a high percentage of bulls , and in a very short while the `` crossed rifles `` , denoting marksmanship , ornamented the sleeves of their uniforms", "context_len": 57}}}, "drunkenness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "My aunt regarded the sin of drunkenness with the greatest horror ; she rarely mentions it in her books , and generally , where it is touched upon , she writes with the deepest pathos , as in The Great Impostor", "context_len": 41}}}, "abstinent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The boys did not know it , but there was a very strenuous fight in the community against the drunkenness that was so frequent ; and there were perhaps more people who were wholly abstinent then than there are now", "context_len": 40}}}, "forces": {"freq": 82, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 80, "word_index": 54, "context": "The party thus opposed were inactive and silent while Alexander was in their country , on his first visit to southern Greece ; but after his return they began to contemplate more decisive action , and afterward , when they heard of his having undertaken so desperate an enterprise as going northward with his forces , and actually crossing the Danube , they considered him as so completely out of the way that they grew very courageous , and meditated open rebellion", "context_len": 82}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` I did n't want to be settin ' myself up , but I was n't goin ' to have her take no false notions about me , so I says , 'Yes , ma'am , but when a body 's sick , and ai n't no appetite to eat only what one forces one 's self to , I do n't think it no sin to spend a bit for a nice nourishin ' mouthful", "context_len": 76}}}, "passions": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 99, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}}}, "tasted": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 37, "context": "Yes , she wanted it very much ; but there was poor Dolly who wanted it very much too , -- who said she had had nothing to eat all day yesterday , who probably had never tasted such a fruit ; for she had asked what they were when she saw them , -- who , even Bessie 's innocent eyes could see , looked very ill", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He smelled to 'em and tasted 'em , and he said as there wor poison in 'em ; and he threw 'em , bottle and h'all , out of winder", "context_len": 30}}}, "outnumbered": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "But the battle continued to rage , and , though he was hampered by his helmet and sorely outnumbered , the valour displayed by the holder of the fort might yet have gained him the day , if Jim , warned by a cry from Hal that the water in the barrel was giving out , had not succeeded in grasping the flagstaff", "context_len": 63}}}, "adder": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 61, "context": "The pity for the boys was that they saw the drunkards every day , and the temperance men only now and then ; and out of the group of boys who were my boy 's friends , many kindly fellows came to know how strong drink could rage , how it could bite like the serpent , and sting like an adder", "context_len": 62}}}, "scholars": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 62, "context": "The majority of them are laid down in phonetically defective missionary alphabets , about which we are prompted to repeat what the citizens of the young colony of Mexico wrote to the government of Spain , in Cortez 's time : `` Send to us pious and Christian men , as preachers , bishops and missionaries , but do not send us scholars , who , with their pettifogging distinctions and love of contention , create nothing but disorder and strife", "context_len": 81}}}, "butchers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The market-house , where the German butchers in their white aprons were standing behind their meat-blocks , was lit up with candles in sconces , that shone upon festoons of sausage and cuts of steak dangling from the hooks behind them ; but without , all was in a vague obscurity , broken only by the lanterns in the farmers ' wagons", "context_len": 62}}}, "sconces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The market-house , where the German butchers in their white aprons were standing behind their meat-blocks , was lit up with candles in sconces , that shone upon festoons of sausage and cuts of steak dangling from the hooks behind them ; but without , all was in a vague obscurity , broken only by the lanterns in the farmers ' wagons", "context_len": 62}}}, "festoons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The market-house , where the German butchers in their white aprons were standing behind their meat-blocks , was lit up with candles in sconces , that shone upon festoons of sausage and cuts of steak dangling from the hooks behind them ; but without , all was in a vague obscurity , broken only by the lanterns in the farmers ' wagons", "context_len": 62}}}, "cuts": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "Hartley very seriously ; and then as she looked up and saw , now that dust and grime and the stains from two or three slight cuts were removed , that the face above was a good face , after all , her heart went out in sympathy , and she added gently , but we 'll hope for the best , dear -- we 'll hope for the best", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "Whatever one gives to be made or mended is made or mended in the verandah ; and the darzi , as he cuts out , clips , and sews , talks -- perhaps with the pankah-wala , perhaps a stranger , perhaps the munshi ( tutor ) whose pupil is not quite ready to take her lesson", "context_len": 57}}}, "steak": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The market-house , where the German butchers in their white aprons were standing behind their meat-blocks , was lit up with candles in sconces , that shone upon festoons of sausage and cuts of steak dangling from the hooks behind them ; but without , all was in a vague obscurity , broken only by the lanterns in the farmers ' wagons", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 10, "context": "There was nearly always hot biscuit for supper , with steak , and with coffee such as nobody but a boy 's mother ever knew how to make ; and just as likely as not there was some kind of preserves ; at any rate , there was apple-butter", "context_len": 49}}}, "obscurity": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 51, "context": "My prison -- if such it was -- was a tolerably spacious affair , measuring about twenty feet long by fifteen feet wide , and it was absolutely empty ; also , there being no windows to the building , and the light entering only by the open door , the obscurity , on entering , seemed profound , although a few minutes sufficed to enable one 's eyes to grow accustomed to it , when , at least during daylight , it was possible to see clearly enough for all practical purposes", "context_len": 93}}}, "lanterns": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 35, "context": "Her stern gallery was upheld by a row of gilt figures representing that hero of mythology , Master Atlas , of whom my father used to speak ; while over the gallery towered three enormous lanterns , and above everything was a maze of spars and rigging that confused me not a little , though before long I was well acquainted with the names of all of them", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Others , arming themselves with a medley of weapons , hurried to the fortress , from which a gun was fired and then lanterns hoisted as a signal when a vessel was expected", "context_len": 33}}}, "fined": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Let me see , what shall we be fined for ? I shall have to get some light upon that , too , but I think it would be a good plan that any girl who voluntarily stirs up a fuss with another at school must pay a fine of not less than one cent", "context_len": 56}}}, "peaches": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 90, "context": "He belonged to a military company of big boys that had real wooden guns , such as the little boys never could get , and silk oil-cloth caps , and nankeen roundabouts , and white pantaloons with black stripes down the legs ; and once they marched out to a boy 's that had a father that had a farm , and he gave them all a free dinner in an arbor before the house ; bread and butter , and apple-butter , and molasses and pound cake , and peaches and apples ; it was splendid", "context_len": 97}}}, "melons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "People would walk along the line of wagons , where the butter and eggs , apples and peaches and melons , were piled up inside near the tail-boards , and stop where they saw something they wanted , and stand near so as to lay hands on it the moment the bell rang", "context_len": 53}}}, "cantaloupes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There was a smell of cantaloupes in the air , along the line of wagons , that morning , and so it must have been towards the end of the summer", "context_len": 31}}}, "lengthen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "The sandals were anything but comfortable , and her feet hurt dreadfully on the stones , while every gorse-bush she passed seemed to be stretching out spiky fingers to scratch her bare legs ; she was tired after her morning 's adventures on the moors , and the eight miles seemed to lengthen out to an interminable vista , in spite of the way being downhill ; sundry bumps and bruises , which she had never noticed at the time , began to ache now , and the bee-stings on her neck smarted , until she hardly knew how to bear the pain", "context_len": 103}}}, "eves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "After the nights began to lengthen and to be too cold for the farmers to sleep in their wagons , as they did in summer on the market eves , the market time was changed to midday", "context_len": 37}}}, "midday": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "After the nights began to lengthen and to be too cold for the farmers to sleep in their wagons , as they did in summer on the market eves , the market time was changed to midday", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 34, "context": "Seeing that I was utterly exhausted , he allowed me to lie down in front of his oven , and , in spite of the hardness of my couch , I slept soundly till midday , when I was aroused by Greville Drake and some of the late crew of the Gannet , who were being entertained in the town till they could be conveyed to their homes", "context_len": 68}}}, "frayed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Then it was fun to count the wagons on both sides of the street clear to where they frayed off into wood-wagons , and to see the great heaps of apples and cabbages , and potatoes and turnips , and all the other fruits and vegetables which abounded in that fertile country", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "But the implement must have been injured in the fall of the tree , for as Bomba drew it taut the frayed string broke with an ominous snap", "context_len": 28}}}, "coops": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "There was a great variety of poultry for sale , and from time to time the air would be startled with the clamor of fowls transferred from the coops where they had been softly crr-crring in soliloquy to the hand of a purchaser who walked off with them and patiently waited for their well-grounded alarm to die away", "context_len": 58}}}, "soliloquy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "There was a great variety of poultry for sale , and from time to time the air would be startled with the clamor of fowls transferred from the coops where they had been softly crr-crring in soliloquy to the hand of a purchaser who walked off with them and patiently waited for their well-grounded alarm to die away", "context_len": 58}}}, "purchaser": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "There was a great variety of poultry for sale , and from time to time the air would be startled with the clamor of fowls transferred from the coops where they had been softly crr-crring in soliloquy to the hand of a purchaser who walked off with them and patiently waited for their well-grounded alarm to die away", "context_len": 58}}}, "rounds": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 94, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Thirty rounds from the Stokes 's mortars in the short space of two minutes played havoc with the garrisons", "context_len": 19}}}, "weigh": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 62, "context": "Pigs , poultry , and young animals had to be fed , and the eggs gathered in daily ; the dairy claimed constant attention , for the pans must be scalded and the pails polished bright , and though Joe did the milking and churning , it was Lilian 's business to superintend the making up of the butter , and to weigh each pound with her own hands and print it with the Abbey stamp", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The last ship to get under weigh was The Revenge , as Sir Richard waited for the men left on the island , who would have otherwise been captured", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 123, "context": "'s service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut her ear to the cry of the needy , because to answer it would cost a sacrifice of her own wishes ? Should she bear the burdens of others only when they did not weigh heavily on herself ?", "context_len": 128}}}, "steelyards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "All the time the market-master was making his rounds ; and if he saw a pound roll of butter that he thought was under weight , he would weigh it with his steelyards , and if it was too light he would seize it", "context_len": 44}}}, "confiscation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "My boy once saw a confiscation of this sort with such terror as he would now , perhaps , witness an execution", "context_len": 22}}}, "witness": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 63, "context": "When the ceremony was over , Gervaise was solemnly invested with the command of the galley by the grand marshal of the navy ; then the shores were struck away , and the galley glided into the water , amid the firing of guns , the blowing of trumpets , and the cheers of the spectators who had gathered at the port to witness the ceremony", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 4, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "Mark Penelly watched him as he swam ashore easily and well , and the bitter feelings of dislike which had for the time being lain in abeyance before the scene of peril of which he had been witness , began once more to grow stronger , completely changing the appearance of his face as now , to get rid of the thoughts that troubled him , he took hold of one of the sweeps and began to row", "context_len": 78}}}, "musters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "MUSTERS AND ELECTIONS", "context_len": 3}}}, "troops": {"freq": 178, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 173, "word_index": 127, "context": "had hay in his right shoe , and straw in his left , because a Dutchman was too dumb , as the boys said for stupid , to know his feet apart any other way ; and that the Dutch officers had to call out to the men when they were marching , `` Up mit de hay-foot , down mit de straw-foot -- links , links , links ! '' ( Left , left , left ! ) But the boys honored even these imperfect intelligences so much in their quality of soldiers that they would any of them have been proud to be marker in the Dutch company ; and they followed the Dutchmen round in their march as fondly as any other body of troops", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "I wuz comin ' atter you anyhow , but Marse Harry holla 'd at me an ' tol ' me fer ter fin ' you an ' say dat de troops gwineter move in de mornin ' an ' our comp'ny starts fust", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "The Persian cavalry covered the plain , and were ready to dash upon the Macedonian troops the moment they should emerge from the water and attempt to ascend the bank", "context_len": 30}}}, "revel": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "How the Mummy would enjoy it , how she would revel in the good food and the lovely house ! What a red-letter day it would be to her all her life , for all the rest of her years ! How Sukey and Ann Pratt and the neighbors down at Dawlish would respect her for evermore ! And doubtless the Mummy 's dress might be managed , and -- but what about Aunt Susan ? Would Aunt Susan ever forgive her ? She dared not run the risk of her displeasure ; too much depended on keeping her in a good humor", "context_len": 103}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` O yes , my father is keeping high festival ; a number of his countrymen are visiting him and holding revel ; this morning they drove in a number of oxen , I know not whence , and slaughtered two on the spot , and they have broached several barrels of mead ; they will keep the feast all day , and before night my father will not be in a state to miss me ; I always absent myself if I can on such occasions", "context_len": 87}}}, "cockerel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "I forget just what their uniform was , but there were white pantaloons in it , and a tuft of white-and-red cockerel plumes that almost covered the front of the hat , and swayed when the soldier walked , and blew in the wind", "context_len": 44}}}, "differed": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 92, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}}}, "privates": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "I think the coat was gray , and the skirts were buttoned back with buff , but I will not be sure of this ; and somehow I can not say how the officers differed from the privates in dress ; it was impossible for them to be more magnificent", "context_len": 50}}}, "platoons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "They walked backwards in front of the platoons , with their swords drawn , and held in their white-gloved hands at hilt and point , and kept holloing , `` Shoulder-r-r -- arms ! Carry -- arms ! Present -- arms ! '' and then faced round , and walked a few steps forward , till they could think of something else to make the soldiers do", "context_len": 67}}}, "swords": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 71, "context": "Appalled by the disaster that had befallen them , by the terrible effect of the broadsides , poured in at a few yards ' distance , and by the sight of so many of the dreaded warriors of the Cross , some of the corsairs threw down their arms and flung themselves on the deck or into the hold , crying for quarter ; those who resisted fell either under the swords of the knights , the vengeful axes of the late captives , or the pikes of the Sards ; but the great bulk , leaping from the bow or stern , swam ashore", "context_len": 105}}}, "hilt": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 21, "context": "They walked backwards in front of the platoons , with their swords drawn , and held in their white-gloved hands at hilt and point , and kept holloing , `` Shoulder-r-r -- arms ! Carry -- arms ! Present -- arms ! '' and then faced round , and walked a few steps forward , till they could think of something else to make the soldiers do", "context_len": 67}}}, "drummer": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "He told them the most delightful and interesting stories of people and things that he had seen while he was in India , being always careful not to tell anything that might shock or grieve them , from the day that he was speaking of the sad death of a little drummer boy , when , to his great surprise and distress , both children broke into a violent fit of crying , and it was some time before they could be pacified", "context_len": 83}}}, "marker": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "The fellows used to wonder how he got to be marker , and to plan how they could get to be markers in other companies , if not in the Butler Guards", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 105, "context": "had hay in his right shoe , and straw in his left , because a Dutchman was too dumb , as the boys said for stupid , to know his feet apart any other way ; and that the Dutch officers had to call out to the men when they were marching , `` Up mit de hay-foot , down mit de straw-foot -- links , links , links ! '' ( Left , left , left ! ) But the boys honored even these imperfect intelligences so much in their quality of soldiers that they would any of them have been proud to be marker in the Dutch company ; and they followed the Dutchmen round in their march as fondly as any other body of troops", "context_len": 128}}}, "flank": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 49, "context": "These have ground and polished off every scrap of seaweed , every barnacle , limpet , and sea-anemone , leaving the rock all smooth and bare , while the boulders lie piled to the east in a heap , where the waves that try to take the rock in flank leap amongst them , and roll them over higher and higher , to come rumbling down as if they were tiny pebbles instead of rounded masses of granite and spar-veined stone a quarter , half , and a hundredweight each", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Marie-Celeste looked after him a moment with a most amused smile , and then growing to feel more at home amid royal surroundings , turned to investigate the little miniature elephants that flank the steps leading down from the eastern terrace", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Relieved of the flank attack , he had at last got the hold upon his opponent \u2019 s throat for which he had been seeking , and in a few moments more the fight was over", "context_len": 36}}}, "marking": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "They thought more of the marker than of the drummer ; for the marker carried a little flag , and when the officers holloed out , `` By the left flank -- left ! Wheel ! '' he set his flag against his shoulder , and stood marking time with his feet till the soldiers all got by him , and then he ran up to the front rank , with the flag fluttering behind him", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The marking of the linguistic areas by dots , pointing to the tribal settlements , answers much better the purpose than the coloring of large areas , which conveys the erroneous impression that the population was scattered all over a certain country", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Belden , sat just at the entrance of the main stairway , a cigar poised in his left hand , a book balanced in his right ; the book closed for the moment , with his forefinger marking the place , and his elbow resting on the arm of his steamer-chair", "context_len": 51}}}, "companies": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 61, "context": "The tables are divided down the center by an imaginary line , each part being a `` table '' ; first class men sit near the head , and so on down to the plebes , who find themselves at the center ( that is , after they have moved into camp , and been `` sized '' and assigned to companies ; before that they are `` beasts , '' herded apart , as has been said )", "context_len": 79}}}, "richer": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 14, "context": "Peggy grew up without many of the luxuries which fall to the lot of richer girls , but she was none the worse for that , and I think , when she saw how bored and uninterested Maud Middleton was in the midst of her round of London society and foreign travel , she felt that it is sometimes better to have the capacity for enjoyment than to have the means without the happy talent of being able to appreciate your good things", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The essay was rich in metaphor and still richer in quotation", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` Then he thanked her for her kindness and the pains she had taken [ 383 ] and said to her , `` By Allah , O my mother , it is as I were in a tomb and now thou hast raised me up therefrom ; and I praise God the Most High , for I am presently certified that there is none richer or happier than I in the world", "context_len": 72}}}, "epaulets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "There were other companies that used to come to town on the Fourth of July and Muster Day , from smaller places round about ; and some of them had richer uniforms : one company had blue coats with gold epaulets , and gold braid going down in loops on the sides of their legs ; all the soldiers , of course , had braid straight down the outer seams of their pantaloons", "context_len": 73}}}, "braid": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "Marie-Celeste could not possibly discover whether the tone was encouraging or no , but in any case she had no words with which to continue , so awe-inspiring proved the blue coat , gold braid , and the other insignia of the captain 's office", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "outer": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 68, "context": "There were other companies that used to come to town on the Fourth of July and Muster Day , from smaller places round about ; and some of them had richer uniforms : one company had blue coats with gold epaulets , and gold braid going down in loops on the sides of their legs ; all the soldiers , of course , had braid straight down the outer seams of their pantaloons", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 88, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}}}, "seams": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 74, "context": "She looked lovingly at the cows , lazily chewing their cud in the sunshine ; she felt sorry for her step-mother , as she strove to woo slumber to Polly 's wakeful eyes with the same lullaby which had done duty for the whole six ; she even found it in her heart to kiss Lemuel , who , with his ready talent for the unusual , was busily cramming mud paste into the seams of the little trunk which held her worldly all", "context_len": 84}}}, "plumed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "One Muster Day , a captain of one of the country companies came home with my boy 's father to dinner ; he was in full uniform , and he put his plumed helmet down on the entry table just like any other hat", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "Now that the danger was past , Drake and I could not help laughing at our cavalier companion and his fair burden , for the hag had clasped him tightly round the waist with her skinny arms , while the cat , perched on her shoulders , was rubbing its head against the back of Felgate \u2019 s plumed hat , so that it was being continually thrust over his eyes despite his frequent attempts to place it firmly on his head", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The slender , slightly bowed figure in the velvet coat and blue ribbon , with soft curls that flowed from beneath a plumed hat , the sad eyes , the regular features only marred by a look of weakness and almost peevishness about the mouth ; the boy had seen them all often enough in pictures , but to-day he stood for the first time in the presence of a king , of King Charles the First of England", "context_len": 79}}}, "helmet": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "He was dimly conscious of khaki-clad forms crashing heavily to the ground on either side , of a whizz of flying metal that sent his steel helmet spinning , of a sharp , burning pain in his left wrist , and of a dozen other mental and physical sensations", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 49, "context": "Once Drusie , who had wriggled herself through the thick hawthorns at the back of the fort , was within an ace of taking the flag ; but , just as she had climbed up on the roof , the defender , whose face was completely hidden by his helmet , made a grab at her , and she was obliged to fly for her life", "context_len": 66}}}, "mit": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` The very first Christmas what I makes my fortunes I vill put a four-masted Yankee ship in your stockings , boy , mit stores and crew ready for sea , and this granddaddy of yours walkin ' up and down the poop , so ? ''", "context_len": 47}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "But if you does your duty and sticks by your friends you will wear the blue coat mit the brass stripes on the sleeve , and you will be glad you stayed by steam", "context_len": 34}}}, "intelligences": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 86, "context": "had hay in his right shoe , and straw in his left , because a Dutchman was too dumb , as the boys said for stupid , to know his feet apart any other way ; and that the Dutch officers had to call out to the men when they were marching , `` Up mit de hay-foot , down mit de straw-foot -- links , links , links ! '' ( Left , left , left ! ) But the boys honored even these imperfect intelligences so much in their quality of soldiers that they would any of them have been proud to be marker in the Dutch company ; and they followed the Dutchmen round in their march as fondly as any other body of troops", "context_len": 128}}}, "fondly": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 121, "context": "had hay in his right shoe , and straw in his left , because a Dutchman was too dumb , as the boys said for stupid , to know his feet apart any other way ; and that the Dutch officers had to call out to the men when they were marching , `` Up mit de hay-foot , down mit de straw-foot -- links , links , links ! '' ( Left , left , left ! ) But the boys honored even these imperfect intelligences so much in their quality of soldiers that they would any of them have been proud to be marker in the Dutch company ; and they followed the Dutchmen round in their march as fondly as any other body of troops", "context_len": 128}}}, "muster": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 43, "context": "Clavering having reckoned up the marks , Mademoiselle Le Brun having given her testimony , Fraulein having given hers , and the English teachers having further testified to the industry of the pupils , the girls of the Upper school were to pass muster before Sir John , who was to decide without prejudice in favor of the lucky three who alone were to compete for the great Scholarship in October", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 58, "context": "[ 354 ] On this wise she abode a week 's space [ 355 ] and the Sultan saw her at each Divan ; so , when she went on the last day [ of the week ] and stood , according to her wont , before the Divan , till it was ended , but could not muster courage to enter [ 356 ] or say aught , the Sultan arose and entering the harem , turned to his chief Vizier , who was with him , and said to him , `` O Vizier , these six or seven days [ 357 ] past I have seen yonder old woman come hither at every Divan and I note that she still carrieth somewhat under her veil", "context_len": 128}}}, "tents": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 50, "context": "But they would not , and all those upon the mountains died , and their curses came down , and rested upon the rich and powerful who had refused to help them , and upon the day that the last of the wanderers perished a voice was heard in the tents of the yellow men , and it said , 'As ye refused to leave your lands to aid your brethren , ye shall rest in the ground till strangers shall bear thee hence , and as ye have refused to toil , or bless in your life time , ye shall do both after death", "context_len": 106}}}, "ram": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "Screening himself in the shadow cast by the wagon , he proceeded to ram a charge down the barrel , while the guide did as he said he would do", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "The first warning of the arrival of reinforcements was not the traditional trumpet call , nor the roll of a drum , nor even the tramp of soldiers , but a muttered `` Wow ! '' This was followed by Texas himself , bursting through the bushes like a battering ram", "context_len": 51}}}, "aptly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Those chaps -- 'island carrions , desperate of their bones ' , as Will Shakespeare aptly puts it -- are our ancestors , Malcolm , whether we are New Zealanders , Australians , or Canadians , and although we are up against a big thing I have n't the faintest doubt that blood will tell , as it did in those days", "context_len": 62}}}, "national": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 38, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}}}, "veterans": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "They wished there could be a war with England , just to see ; and their national feeling was kept hot by the presence of veterans of the War of 1812 at all the celebrations", "context_len": 35}}}, "salute": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 69, "context": "'Of course he will ! I wonder you can ask such a question ! Heaven would n't be heaven unless we found Rollo there ! We 'll wrap his body in the Union Jack , and pick all our best flowers to strew round him ; and you might fire off your old pistol over his grave , if Father will let you have any gunpowder -- a parting salute , you know , like they do for officers , ' said Peggy , cheering up a little at the thought of arranging the obsequies", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "Caretto did not wish the Turks to imagine that he doubted their good faith , and while directing the main body of knights to remain in concealment near the breach , he placed two on sentry duty on the crest of the ruins , and , with four other knights and Gervaise , went down in complete armour to salute the officer in command of the burying party , as he landed from the boats", "context_len": 75}}}, "obscurely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "The roof was semicircular , and composed of minute bricks , seeming to form one solid mass ; the floor of tiling , arranged in patterns , which could still be obscurely traced by the light of the lamp left by the charity of Sidroc to the prisoner ; for the dungeon was of bad reputation ; lights had been seen there at unearthly hours , when the outer door was fast and no inmate existed", "context_len": 76}}}, "hereditary": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 18, "context": "Sp\u00e1ne or Spanish gens ; m\u00edngos or chiefs could be chosen from this gens only , and were hereditary in the female line ; sh\u0103-\u00e9 or racoon gens ; second chiefs or headmen were selected from it ; kuishto or tiger gens ; ko-intch\u016bsh or catamount gens ; n\u00e1ni or fish gens ; \u00edssi or deer gens ; hal\u00f3ba or ? gens ; fosh\u00e9 or bird gens ; hunshkon\u00e9 or skunk gens , the least respected of them all", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Each division took up rapidly the position assigned ; for in this domestic war men fought side by side with those they had known from childhood , and were commanded immediately by their own hereditary chieftains", "context_len": 36}}}, "rooster": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 90, "context": "The soft spring air floated in through the open window , and she heard the birds twitter and the frogs peep : she heard Abraham Lincoln , the old horse that she used to ride to water before she grew big enough to work , whinney over his hay ; and Goliath , the young giant that had come to take his place in the farm work , answer him sonorously : the dog barked lazily as a nighthawk swept by , and in the distant hen-yard she heard a rooster crow", "context_len": 92}}}, "plume": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 60, "context": "Once as the party , who were all growing somewhat tired , were rather quiet , they suddenly heard a long , loud chirrup ; and looking round to the side whence the noise came , there , upon a heap of stones , sat a large gray squirrel , with his tail curled gracefully over his back like a plume , and seeming to call attention to himself by his song", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "An endless passivity seemed a dreary outlook to her active soul , which was sighing to plume its cramped wings , and soar among the endless possibilities of earth : it seemed strange that there should be no wonders to explore in heaven", "context_len": 43}}}, "whittled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Well , I 'll cut another club , and have it in readiness , '' Frank thought , and , as there were no more suitable dead limbs that would serve , he whittled off with his knife , a tough green branch , that would answer as a club", "context_len": 51}}}, "laths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was pretty easy to make swords out of laths , but guns again were hard to realize", "context_len": 18}}}, "finery": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "She was the older daughter , a strong , healthy girl of seventeen , who never helped her mother about the work , who dressed in such cheap finery as she could obtain by hook or crook , who took music lessons on a rented melodeon paid for out of her mother \u2019 s hard earnings , who felt herself to be a lady unfortunately born out of her sphere , and who was unquestionably ashamed of her surviving parent and her brother and sister", "context_len": 85}}}, "eking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "In these troubles the fellows ' mothers had to suffer almost as much as the fellows themselves , the fellows teased them so much for bits of finery that they thought they could turn to account in eking out a uniform", "context_len": 41}}}, "hatters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Once it came to quite a lot of fellows getting their mothers to ask their fathers if they would buy them some little soldier-hats that one of the hatters had laid in , perhaps after a muster , when he knew the boys would begin recruiting", "context_len": 46}}}, "recruiting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Once it came to quite a lot of fellows getting their mothers to ask their fathers if they would buy them some little soldier-hats that one of the hatters had laid in , perhaps after a muster , when he knew the boys would begin recruiting", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "One of these people was an officer of the army who was staying a while in the Boy 's Town , and perhaps had something to do with recruiting troops for the Mexican War", "context_len": 34}}}, "argued": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 63, "context": "It was weary , anxious work ; for of course our movements were being regulated by a theory that , for aught we knew to the contrary , might be all wrong ; and as day succeeded day without bringing the expected sail within our ken there were not wanting among us those who denounced the skipper 's plan as foolish , and argued that the proper thing would have been to go direct to the Gaboon , and look there for the Virginia", "context_len": 84}}}, "tomfoolery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "My boy was by when his mother asked his father , and stood with his heart in his mouth , while the question was argued ; it was decided against him , both because his father hated the tomfoolery of the thing , and because he would not have the child honor any semblance of soldiering , even such a feeble image of it as a boys ' company could present", "context_len": 71}}}, "semblance": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 86, "context": "He never let on that he suspected for a moment that he was not entirely alone , but , walking over to a tree stump , where , spread out on a newspaper , was the remains of a lunch , he acted delighted at the discovery , picked up a hunk of bread in one hand , a piece of cheese in the other , and , throwing himself on the green sward at full length , proceeded to munch the eatables , with every semblance of satisfaction", "context_len": 89}}}, "soldiering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "My boy was by when his mother asked his father , and stood with his heart in his mouth , while the question was argued ; it was decided against him , both because his father hated the tomfoolery of the thing , and because he would not have the child honor any semblance of soldiering , even such a feeble image of it as a boys ' company could present", "context_len": 71}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Already soldiering had hardened his hands considerably , but stoking , he decided , had proved to be far more strenuous than bayonet exercise , if an aching back , stiff muscles , and galls as big as half-crowns were any criterion", "context_len": 42}}}, "chapeau": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "But , after all , a paper chapeau , with a panache of slitted paper , was no bad soldier-hat ; it went far to constitute a whole uniform ; and it was this that the boys devolved upon at last", "context_len": 41}}}, "panache": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "But , after all , a paper chapeau , with a panache of slitted paper , was no bad soldier-hat ; it went far to constitute a whole uniform ; and it was this that the boys devolved upon at last", "context_len": 41}}}, "constitute": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "But , after all , a paper chapeau , with a panache of slitted paper , was no bad soldier-hat ; it went far to constitute a whole uniform ; and it was this that the boys devolved upon at last", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "In fact , it is probable that Alexander 's slaughter of the Persian army at Arbela , and subsequent spoliation of Susa , constitute , taken together , the most gigantic case of murder and robbery which was ever committed by man ; so that , in performing these deeds , the great hero attained at last to the glory of having perpetrated the grandest and most imposing of all human crimes", "context_len": 72}}}, "devolved": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "But , after all , a paper chapeau , with a panache of slitted paper , was no bad soldier-hat ; it went far to constitute a whole uniform ; and it was this that the boys devolved upon at last", "context_len": 41}}}, "marshalling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "I can not understand how my boy came to hold either office ; perhaps the fellows found that the only way to keep the company together was to take turn-about ; but , at any rate , he was marshalling his forces near his grandfather 's gate one evening when his grandfather came home to tea", "context_len": 56}}}, "apparition": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 60, "context": "He was about to launch his battle-axe through the air in search of the daring minstrel , when the same dread expression of unutterable agony we have before mentioned passed over his face ; he trembled as an aspen , and sank , as one paralysed , into his chair , while his glaring eyes seemed to behold some horrid apparition unseen by all beside", "context_len": 65}}}, "attack": {"freq": 307, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 75, "word_index": 69, "context": "I had with me one of my old galley slaves , who had been taken into Ben Ibyn 's employment ; and when the pirate came up with two of his crew , and furiously attacked me as soon as I threw off my disguise , it would have gone hard with me had he not stood by me , and killed one of them who was about to attack me in the rear", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 228, "word_index": 53, "context": "This , doubtless , may be so , Sir Gervaise , the doge said , with a slight smile , but it is to the head that plans , rather than to the hand that strikes , that such success as you have achieved is due ; and the credit of this night attack is , as the cavalier Caretto tells me , wholly yours , for until you issued your final orders it seemed to him , and to the two good knights his companions , that there was naught to do but to remain in port and watch this corsair fleet sail away to carry out its work of destruction", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "At Pensford the rabble made a bull savage , and then tried to make it attack his congregation ; at Whitechapel they drove cows among the listeners and threw stones , one of which hit Wesley between the eyes ; but after he had wiped away the blood he went on with his address , telling the people that `` God hath not given us the spirit of fear ''", "context_len": 70}}}, "warlike": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 74, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 31, "context": "Besides , what should I do if I left the Order without home , country , or means , and with naught to do but to sell my sword to some warlike monarch ? Besides , Caretto , I love the Order , and deem it the highest privilege to fight against the Moslems , and to uphold the banner of the Cross", "context_len": 63}}}, "volunteers": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 1, "context": "The volunteers were mustered in at the Boy 's Town ; and the boys , who understood that they were real soldiers , and were going to a war where they might get killed , suffered a disappointment from the plain blue of their uniform and the simplicity of their caps , which had not the sign of a feather in them", "context_len": 62}}}, "mustered": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 98, "context": "Yes , you said something like that to the captain ; and as though she would have given all the world if he had n't , but I did n't mean to be bold really , only I felt so sorry for Chris ; and then she proceeded to tell , as coherently as her emotions would allow , of her unexpected encounter with her old friend , and how dreadful it would have been if they could not have seen anything of each other just because Chris was a second-cabin passenger , and of how she had mustered all her courage and gone straight to the captain to see what could be done about it", "context_len": 116}}}, "feather": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 59, "context": "The volunteers were mustered in at the Boy 's Town ; and the boys , who understood that they were real soldiers , and were going to a war where they might get killed , suffered a disappointment from the plain blue of their uniform and the simplicity of their caps , which had not the sign of a feather in them", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 75, "context": "dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of the sunset", "context_len": 128}}}, "effaced": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "At any rate , it was celebrated throughout the world in its day , and its existence and its greatness made an impression on the human mind which has not yet been effaced", "context_len": 33}}}, "paraded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Fortescue , Selwyn , and Carr were also amongst the volunteers , and after breakfast twenty men paraded in dungarees to take their `` trick '' below", "context_len": 27}}}, "civic": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 46, "context": "I dare say it was of the size of the ordinary city omnibus , but it looked as large to the boys then as a Pullman car would look to a boy now ; and they assembled for its arrivals and departures with a thrill of civic pride such as hardly any other fact of the place could impart", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The civic centre was the court-house , with the county buildings about it in the court-house yard ; and the great thing in the court-house was the town clock", "context_len": 29}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "America \u2019 s earliest lending library had come into being , the first of many civic benefits which Franklin initiated over the years", "context_len": 23}}}, "alloyed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But this civic satisfaction was alloyed for my boy by the consciousness that both his father and his grandfather abhorred the war that the volunteers were going to", "context_len": 28}}}, "abhorred": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "He knew that his father abhorred it , but he had never been explicitly forbidden to smoke or chew , for his father seldom forbade him anything explicitly , and he gave himself such freedom of choice in the matter that when the boy with the tobacco began to offer it around , he judged it right to take a chew with the rest", "context_len": 64}}}, "opposed": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 3, "context": "The party thus opposed were inactive and silent while Alexander was in their country , on his first visit to southern Greece ; but after his return they began to contemplate more decisive action , and afterward , when they heard of his having undertaken so desperate an enterprise as going northward with his forces , and actually crossing the Danube , they considered him as so completely out of the way that they grew very courageous , and meditated open rebellion", "context_len": 82}}}, "annexation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "wanton": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 58, "context": "His grandfather , as an Abolitionist , and his father , as a Henry Clay Whig , had both been opposed to the annexation of Texas ( which the boy heard talked of without knowing in the least what annexation meant ) , and they were both of the mind that the war growing out of it was wanton and wicked", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The melancholy faces of the two little girls grieved their older friends ; and , besides , it was sad to know that children like Lem and Dolly should take delight in such wicked , wanton mischief , and to know that there seemed to be no way to do them good ; since they only came near those who were weaker and younger than themselves to do them harm , and ran from those who were older and wiser , in fear of the punishment and reproof their wickedness deserved", "context_len": 91}}}, "majority": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 37, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}}}, "whilst": {"freq": 95, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 24, "context": "Then he took leave of the Sultan and going forth , mounted and rode , with his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like ,", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 26, "context": "Thou sayst , O my mother , that I have no gift to present to the Sultan , according to the wont of the folk , whilst in fact I have by me a gift and an offering , the like whereof methinketh no king ever possessed , no , nor aught to match therewith ; for [ 338 ] thou must know , O my mother , that the fruits , which I brought in the purses [ 339 ] from the treasure and which I deemed glass or crystal , are very jewels , methinketh all the kings of the world may not compass the least of them , and I , of my companying with the jewellers , know that they are precious stones", "context_len": 127}, "UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 36, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}}}, "filial": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hers was a high ideal of filial submission ; and though she had reached an age when she had a right to an independent opinion , yet obedience to them ranked in her mind before the necessity to decide for herself , in a question where opinions might so greatly differ", "context_len": 51}}}, "appeased": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}}}, "quoting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 21, "context": "Although Charlotte Tucker \u2019 s Indian life lay still in the future , this seems to be the right place for quoting a few words from her pen , written after years of toil in the East", "context_len": 37}}}, "wisest": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}}}, "wittiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The pieces were called `` The Biglow Papers , '' and his father read them out of a Boston newspaper , and thought them the wisest and wittiest things that ever were", "context_len": 32}}}, "politics": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 64, "context": "the Cordilleras ; they may float in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war , and praising Little Van , whom he had hitherto despised", "context_len": 128}}}, "urge": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 92, "context": "David could not be spared to go with them , but all the children had been taught to drive , and even Bobby had a firm hand on the reins , and knew the rules of the road as well as many a more experienced coachman ; and I think , too , that Pixie had a sense of her responsibilities , and could be trusted not to get the wheel locked with a passing waggon , or to race too furiously down a steep hill , whatever feats her drivers might urge her to perform", "context_len": 96}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was a long journey that Bomba had in prospect to reach Jojasta , but the urge to go was so strong that the lad determined to attempt it", "context_len": 29}}}, "election": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 64, "context": "I do not know why there should have been so many empty tar-barrels in the Boy 's Town , or what they used so much tar for ; but there were barrels enough to celebrate all the Whig victories that the boys ever heard of , and more , too ; the boys did not always wait for the victories , but celebrated every election with bonfires , in the faith that it would turn out right", "context_len": 77}}}, "clubs": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "They usually had no such easy time getting them as they always would in a story ; they did not find them under the trees , or ready to drop off , but they had to knock them off with about six or seven clubs or rocks to every walnut , and they had to pound the hulls so hard to get the nuts out that sometimes they cracked the nuts", "context_len": 71}}}, "ash": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The business of a Whig club among the boys was to raise ash flag-poles , in honor of Henry Clay 's home at Ashland , and to learn the Whig songs and go about singing them", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "The terms for maize disagree but apparently , and seem to be reducible to one radix , atch or ash ; the terms for dog agree in all dialects -- hence , the Maskoki tribes planted maize and kept dogs before , probably many centuries before they separated ; and the term \u00edfa went over from them to the Timucua", "context_len": 60}}}, "bonfires": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 66, "context": "I do not know why there should have been so many empty tar-barrels in the Boy 's Town , or what they used so much tar for ; but there were barrels enough to celebrate all the Whig victories that the boys ever heard of , and more , too ; the boys did not always wait for the victories , but celebrated every election with bonfires , in the faith that it would turn out right", "context_len": 77}}}, "tar": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "I do not know why there should have been so many empty tar-barrels in the Boy 's Town , or what they used so much tar for ; but there were barrels enough to celebrate all the Whig victories that the boys ever heard of , and more , too ; the boys did not always wait for the victories , but celebrated every election with bonfires , in the faith that it would turn out right", "context_len": 77}}}, "victories": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 67, "context": "There was long consultation over this in the English camp ; but in spite of Edmund 's own feelings it was decided to receive Edric , since Canute 's fate would seem to be quite decided if England were united by the union of those southern English who had fought under Canute with Edric , and the men of Mercia and Wessex who had won the previous victories", "context_len": 68}}}, "celebrated": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 66, "context": "Thus the time passed on until the first day of the Customs dawned , when , having received a more than usually substantial meal , I was stripped of the few rags that still covered my nakedness , and , with my hands tightly bound behind me by a thin but strong raw-hide rope , was led forth to the great square wherein the Customs were celebrated , and firmly bound to one of the posts , the erection of which I had witnessed a week earlier", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` And call it 'The Happy Family , ' '' said Fred , mischievously , `` after those celebrated bears and dogs and cats and mice who live together in the most peaceable manner so long as they have no teeth and claws , but who immediately fall to and eat one another up as soon as these are allowed to grow", "context_len": 62}}}, "sewed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "I have a nice little pot buried in a safe place on a certain key ; I 've got a map here '' -- and he thumped his broad chest -- '' here , sewed in the lining of my coat , and the place marked with a cross ; and I tell you , sir , there 's enough gold in that pot to fit out the snuggest little pinnace any man need want to see", "context_len": 77}}}, "turpentine": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "whir": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "When a ball was lighted a boy caught it quickly up , and threw it , and it made a splendid streaming blaze through the air , and a thrilling whir as it flew", "context_len": 34}}}, "dusky": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "The way they used to build their bonfires was to set one tar-barrel on top of another , as high as the biggest boy could reach , and then drop a match into them ; in a moment a dusky , smoky flame would burst from the top , and fly there like a crimson flag , while all the boys leaped and danced round it , and hurrahed for the Whig candidates", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 1, "context": "The dusky enemies must have believed it was an experienced Express Rider who was coming from the east , and it would seem likely to them that he would direct his pony as the animal was now directing himself", "context_len": 39}}}, "crimson": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 58, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 32, "context": "In the grate there was a glowing , blazing fire ; on the hob was a little brass kettle hissing and boiling ; spread upon the floor was a thick , warm crimson rug ; before the fire a folding-chair , unfolded , and with cushions on it ; by the chair a small folding-table , unfolded , covered with a white cloth , and upon it spread small covered dishes , a cup , a saucer , a teapot ; on the bed were new warm coverings and a satin-covered down quilt ; at the foot a curious wadded silk robe , a pair of quilted slippers , and some books", "context_len": 112}}}, "hurrahed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "The way they used to build their bonfires was to set one tar-barrel on top of another , as high as the biggest boy could reach , and then drop a match into them ; in a moment a dusky , smoky flame would burst from the top , and fly there like a crimson flag , while all the boys leaped and danced round it , and hurrahed for the Whig candidates", "context_len": 73}}}, "candidates": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 72, "context": "The way they used to build their bonfires was to set one tar-barrel on top of another , as high as the biggest boy could reach , and then drop a match into them ; in a moment a dusky , smoky flame would burst from the top , and fly there like a crimson flag , while all the boys leaped and danced round it , and hurrahed for the Whig candidates", "context_len": 73}}}, "buckeye": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The reason why they wore buckeyes was that the buckeye was the emblem of Ohio , and Ohio , they knew , was a Whig state", "context_len": 26}}}, "emblem": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 17, "context": "The notice that each of the seven plants distributed to the Indians belonged , or was the emblem of a certain gens or division of people , is gathered from this passage only , and probably refers to the ingredients of some war-physic , which only a limited number of the gentes may have been entitled to contribute to the annual p\u00faskita", "context_len": 62}}}, "elections": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "He was sometimes in the court-house yard , at elections and celebrations ; and once he came from school at recess with some other boys and explored the region of the jail", "context_len": 32}}}, "mob": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "One night , out of pure zeal for the common good , they wished to mob the negro quarter of the town , because the `` Dumb Negro '' ( a deaf-mute of color who was a very prominent personage in their eyes ) was said to have hit a white boy", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 4, "context": "It is not the mob of Genoa only who regard the services that you have rendered as extraordinary , but it is the opinion of the councillors and authorities of the Republic , and of those who , like myself , have borne our share in warfare , that not only is the service great , but that it is due to the singular ability with which you , in command of only a single galley , have wholly destroyed or captured the fleet that threatened our commerce", "context_len": 88}}}, "prominent": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 64, "context": "overpoweringly strong , I detected fastened to the trunk of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "The Duke of Wellington himself was present ; prominent still in the minds of men as the Deliverer of Europe , only twenty years earlier , from a tyrant \u2019 s thraldom", "context_len": 32}}}, "contests": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` And the king proposes to enrol you amongst his chosen warriors on that day ; he has marked the skill you have displayed in the mimic contests with spear or sword , your skill as a horseman , and he wishes to see whether in actual battle you will fulfil the promise of the parade ground", "context_len": 57}}}, "cordially": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 12, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}}}, "hardiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "My boy never had the heart to look on , but he shared the excitement of the affair , and rejoiced in the triumph of Whig principles in these contests as cordially as the hardiest witness", "context_len": 36}}}, "polls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The fighting must have come from the drinking , which began as soon as the polls were opened , and went on all day and night with a devotion to principle which is now rarely seen", "context_len": 36}}}, "transacted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "In fact , the politics of the Boy 's Town seem to have been transacted with an eye single to the diversion of the boys ; or if not that quite , they were marked by traits of a primitive civilization among the men", "context_len": 44}}}, "traits": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 47, "context": "Alfred , her eldest son -- for Edmund was the offspring of an early amour of the king -- was on the other side of the bed , a well-made youth , combining in his features the haughty bearing of his Norman maternal ancestors with the English traits of his father ; but now his expression was one of distress and anxiety , which was yet more deeply shared by his younger brother , Edward , who even at this period manifested that strong sense of religious obligation and that early devotion which in later years caused him to be numbered amongst canonised saints", "context_len": 104}}}, "traditions": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 6, "context": "But alas ! there were no traditions of victory to encourage them ; only gloomy remembrances of defeat ; and , but for the stern call of duty which bade them , as men and Christians , go to the succour of their brethren , the majority would have preferred to remain at home and abide the worst , although they knew full well that submission utterly failed to mitigate the ferocious cruelty of their oppressors , who slew alike the innocent babe and the grey-haired grandsire", "context_len": 87}}}, "hospitality": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 16, "context": "As we grew older , we not only more fully understood the exceptional boundlessness of old-fashioned hospitality and kindness which that house and household exemplified thoroughly , but we came to understand somewhat of the heart-source whence issued that truest manifestation , of everyday religion , which evidences itself in an absolutely unselfish consecration , -- consistent , unreserved , and essentially practical , -- for everyday wear , and not only under stimulating environments", "context_len": 75}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` You sang one of your own national songs , boy , '' he exclaimed , `` and never piped a note for the country offering you hospitality", "context_len": 28}}}, "lingered": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 2, "context": "One alone lingered to cut some limbs from a hemlock bush , a whole armful of which he brought with him , and while the rest were passing the water from the beach , and pouring on the blazing wood-pile , he was switching out the flames , as they ran towards the beach , with a dexterity that showed he was no novice in fire-fighting", "context_len": 66}}}, "barbecue": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "I suppose boys , though , were not allowed at the dinner ; all that my boy saw of the barbecue were the heaps of loaves and hams left over , that piled the floor in one of the rooms to the ceiling", "context_len": 43}}}, "profusion": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "The huge Yule log burnt upon the hearth as it had done since it was lighted the night before ; a profusion of torches turned night into day ; the tables groaned with the weight of the good cheer ; in short , all was there which could express joy and thanksgiving", "context_len": 52}}}, "contributed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 31, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}}}, "hams": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Every Whig family contributed loaves of bread and boiled hams ; the Whig farmers brought in barrels of cider and wagon-loads of apples ; there were heaps of pies and cakes ; sheep were roasted whole , and young roast pigs , with oranges in their mouths , stood in the act of chasing one another over the long tables which were spread in one of the largest pork-houses , where every comer was freely welcome", "context_len": 76}}}, "cider": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 18, "context": "Every Whig family contributed loaves of bread and boiled hams ; the Whig farmers brought in barrels of cider and wagon-loads of apples ; there were heaps of pies and cakes ; sheep were roasted whole , and young roast pigs , with oranges in their mouths , stood in the act of chasing one another over the long tables which were spread in one of the largest pork-houses , where every comer was freely welcome", "context_len": 76}}}, "roasted": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 34, "context": "Every Whig family contributed loaves of bread and boiled hams ; the Whig farmers brought in barrels of cider and wagon-loads of apples ; there were heaps of pies and cakes ; sheep were roasted whole , and young roast pigs , with oranges in their mouths , stood in the act of chasing one another over the long tables which were spread in one of the largest pork-houses , where every comer was freely welcome", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "He realized at last that he was very hungry , and , thinking that his weakness was partly due to this , he took from his pouch some of the roasted meat and ate with a relish", "context_len": 37}}}, "comer": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 72, "context": "Every Whig family contributed loaves of bread and boiled hams ; the Whig farmers brought in barrels of cider and wagon-loads of apples ; there were heaps of pies and cakes ; sheep were roasted whole , and young roast pigs , with oranges in their mouths , stood in the act of chasing one another over the long tables which were spread in one of the largest pork-houses , where every comer was freely welcome", "context_len": 76}}}, "nominated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "He remained an ardent Whig till his eleventh year , when his father left the party because the Whigs had nominated , as their candidate for president , General Taylor , who had won his distinction in the Mexican war , and was believed to be a friend of slavery , though afterwards he turned out otherwise", "context_len": 57}}}, "candidate": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` Right at the very start ! 'Baby ' Edwards was telling me the other day how way last year this fellow met with an accident -- fell off the express or something -- and while he was staying down at the Falls Baby and a couple of other fellows thought he was a candidate , and started in to haze him", "context_len": 62}}}, "purity": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "There is indeed the ridiculous idea of people having to pass through 840,000 states of existence , -- unless the probation be shortened by meditation , purity , and the repetition of God \u2019 s name , -- but this fearful number of births is regarded as very tiresome indeed", "context_len": 50}}}, "capital": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 126, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "and I have just come from a loiter at the Botanical Gardens , which showed us that we need be under no great concern , were hemp and flax exterminated from the vegetable world , and silkworms to leave off being spinsters , as we could dress cheaply and well on plantain fibre , have capital paper and excellent ropes , etc", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 93, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}}}, "preferring": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "His faith in the purity of the Whigs had been much shaken by their behavior in trying to make capital out of a war they condemned ; and he had been bitterly disappointed by their preferring Taylor to Tom Corwin , the favorite of the anti-slavery Whigs", "context_len": 47}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "They , at any rate , would not be the ones to acquaint them with the fact that even queens sometimes grow to be grandmothers , taking more comfort in rocking-chairs than thrones , vastly preferring lace caps to crowns , and behaving in general like other dear grandmothers the world over", "context_len": 52}}}, "allegiance": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 13, "context": "A council was at once held , and it became apparent that the allegiance of the greater part of Wessex depended upon Edmund 's prompt appearance amongst them , while , on the other hand , the rapid approach of Canute made his presence in the city very essential to the safety of the inhabitants", "context_len": 55}}}, "grounded": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 22, "context": "Some made their way to the rocks and landed the soldiers there ; others got alongside the mole ; but the majority grounded in the shallow water of the harbour , and the troops , leaping out , waded to the foot of the breach", "context_len": 45}}}, "invading": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "They sheltered the retreating Naktche against the pursuing French , [ 58 ] besieged the commander Denys at Fort Natchitoches , though they were repulsed there with considerable loss , defeated the French invading their country at Amalahta ( 1736 ) , at the Long House , or Tch\u00faka fal\u00e1ya ( Adair , p", "context_len": 54}}}, "lust": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "The stories they had both heard of predatory bands of Danes who had wandered far from their main body , and had sought gratification for their lust for plunder and blood in remote spots where the inhabitants dwelt in fancied security , came to their minds , and also the inquiries which had been made in the Danish camp concerning their home and the circumstances of St", "context_len": 67}}}, "conquerors": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "The old pile had been badly preserved by the Saxon conquerors , but it had been built of that solid architecture which seems almost to defy the assaults of time , and which in some cases , after fifteen centuries , preserves all its characteristics , and promises yet to preserve them , when our frailer erections lie crumbled in the dust", "context_len": 62}}}, "declaimed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` So shall it be with yours ! '' he had declaimed", "context_len": 12}}}, "loftiest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` You may carry them to the loftiest peaks of the Cordilleras ; they may float in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "peaks": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` You may carry them to the loftiest peaks of the Cordilleras ; they may float in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "insolent": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` You may carry them to the loftiest peaks of the Cordilleras ; they may float in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war ,", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "The officers now debated as to the best course to pursue -- whether to follow the buccaneer , which , undermanned as we were , was hazardous and reckless , or to return with all haste to Port Royal , report our loss , and join with the rest of the fleet in the capture of the insolent pirates", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` You insolent , unmanageable child ! '' she cried", "context_len": 10}}}, "halls": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` You may carry them to the loftiest peaks of the Cordilleras ; they may float in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "weakest": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` You may carry them to the loftiest peaks of the Cordilleras ; they may float in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "warriors": {"freq": 142, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 142, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` You may carry them to the loftiest peaks of the Cordilleras ; they may float in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "wrench": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 70, "context": "-- Mine own Laura , how could you write regarding the little meeting , at which you and sweet Margaret were , Would you not like to be in my shoes at the time , and hold your darling friend in your arms ? I would much rather have been in Margaret \u2019 s shoes , and have held some one else in my arms , -- only for the wrench that would have followed ! But O love , we are travelling in the same train , only in different carriages ; and I am thankful that though we can not see each other , we can as it were talk to each other out of the windows", "context_len": 119}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "Nascanora shall not know , broke in Bomba , as he lightened somewhat his pressure on the man \u2019 s chest and bored him through and through with his eyes as though to wrench the truth from him", "context_len": 38}}}, "opposing": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 79, "context": "in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war , and praising Little Van , whom he had hitherto despised as the `` Fox of Kinderhook", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Then the quarter-back rattled off a string of signals for Jerry Jackson , the left half-back , to take the ball through the opposing left tackle and end", "context_len": 28}}}, "praising": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 113, "context": "in insolent triumph in the halls of Montezuma ; but the weakest hand in Mexico , uplifted in prayer , can call down a power against you before which the iron hearts of your warriors shall be turned into ashes ! '' It must have been a terrible wrench for him to part from the Whig boys in politics , and the wrench must have been a sudden one at last ; he was ashamed of his father for opposing the war , and then , all at once , he was proud of him for it , and was roaring out songs against Taylor as the hero of that war , and praising Little Van , whom he had hitherto despised as the `` Fox of Kinderhook", "context_len": 128}}}, "campaigns": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "The fox was the emblem ( totem ) of the Democrats in the campaigns of 1840 and 1844 ; and in their processions they always had a fox chained to the hickory flag-poles which they carried round on their wagons , together with a cock , reconciled probably in a common terror", "context_len": 52}}}, "processions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "The fox was the emblem ( totem ) of the Democrats in the campaigns of 1840 and 1844 ; and in their processions they always had a fox chained to the hickory flag-poles which they carried round on their wagons , together with a cock , reconciled probably in a common terror", "context_len": 52}}}, "hickory": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 20, "context": "When they got through with it my boy 's brother made himself a ramrod out of a straight piece of hickory , or at least as straight as the gun-barrel , which was rather sway-backed , and had a little twist to one side , so that one of the jour printers said it was a first-rate gun to shoot round a corner with", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "He had a hickory nut in his mouth , to make his voice sound deep and hoarse , and was supported on either side by one lad in a purple sheet , and another one in yellow , the trio forming the `` Mystic Three", "context_len": 45}}}, "signal": {"freq": 147, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 85, "context": "I know a dozen rich Moors in Tripoli or Tunis who would be glad to have him ; and we agreed that we would run down to the African coast for awhile , for that galley has been altogether too busy of late for our comfort , and will be all the more active after this little affair ; besides , people in these islands have got so scared that one ca n't get within ten miles of any of them now without seeing their signal smokes rising on the hills , and finding , when they land , the villages deserted and stripped of everything worth carrying away", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 121, "word_index": 71, "context": "Chris , having instantly surmised the object of Donald 's disappearance , determined that he should have all the time needed ; and nothing was easier , under conditions that called naturally for so many explanations , than to engage the children in such an absorbing conversation on the spot as to make no move toward the cottage ; but the ring of Donald 's feet on the path was the signal that it was safe to lead the way in that direction", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` He sneaked up from below as soon as the coast was clear , to signal his mates if anybody caught them at work with the boat", "context_len": 27}}}, "coopers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "There were coopers , hatters , shoemakers , blacksmiths , bakers , tinners , and others , all hard at work ; and from time to time they threw out to the crowd something they had made", "context_len": 37}}}, "shoemakers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "There were coopers , hatters , shoemakers , blacksmiths , bakers , tinners , and others , all hard at work ; and from time to time they threw out to the crowd something they had made", "context_len": 37}}}, "blacksmiths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "There were coopers , hatters , shoemakers , blacksmiths , bakers , tinners , and others , all hard at work ; and from time to time they threw out to the crowd something they had made", "context_len": 37}}}, "bakers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "There were coopers , hatters , shoemakers , blacksmiths , bakers , tinners , and others , all hard at work ; and from time to time they threw out to the crowd something they had made", "context_len": 37}}}, "rifle": {"freq": 147, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 141, "word_index": 38, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "Though he often came under the fire of jeers and taunts -- more trying to most men than the rifle bullets of the enemy -- he experienced a new joy which increased and deepened", "context_len": 34}}}, "raccoon": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "In the window sat a raccoon , which was the Whig emblem , and which , on all their banners , was painted with the legend , `` That same old Coon ! '' to show that they had not changed at all since the great days when they elected the pioneer , General Harrison , president of the United States", "context_len": 61}}}, "coons": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "During his captivity the coon bit his keeper repeatedly through the thumb , and upon the whole seemed to prefer him to any other food ; I do not really know what coons eat in a wild state , but this captive coon tasted the blood of nearly that whole family of children", "context_len": 53}}}, "elect": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 29, "context": "Nevertheless , you shall say to Sister Harrison all that is in your heart , and she shall act as the Lord shall direct her , and if she elect to go forth into desert places and await the consummation of our hopes afar off , in fasting and prayer , in that fashion also she may serve the Good Old Cause", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "'That is her name , ' said Richard Everidge , with a tender reverence in his voice , 'and she deserves it , for she is among the aristocracy of the elect", "context_len": 32}}}, "governors": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "In 1688 a number of Apalachi chiefs ( caciques ) addressed a letter of complaint to Charles the Second , king of Spain ( 1700 ) , concerning the exactions to which their former governors had subjected them , and other topics relating to their actual condition", "context_len": 47}}}, "presidents": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Even in my boy 's time the boys kept coons just for the pleasure of it , and without meaning to elect Whig governors and presidents with them", "context_len": 28}}}, "traded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "I do not know how they got them -- they traded for them , perhaps , with fellows in the country that had caught them , or perhaps their fathers bought them in market ; some people thought they were very good to eat , and , like poultry and other things for the table , they may have been brought alive to market", "context_len": 64}}}, "chain": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 91, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}}}, "gnaw": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` You 're a nobler fellow than the Spartan that let the wolf gnaw his vitals rather than cry out , '' replied the young man , `` and though you wo n't join us , Guy , I do n't doubt but you 'll find good fortune somewhere", "context_len": 49}}}, "prefer": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 75, "context": "Tell me an thou have a want , that I may accomplish it unto thee ; '' whereupon she kissed the earth a second time and called down blessings upon him , then answered , `` Ay , O King of the Age , as thy head liveth , I have indeed a want ; but before all things do thou give me thine assurance , [ 362 ] so I may make bold to prefer my suit to the hearing of our lord the Sultan , for that belike Thy Grace will find it a strange one", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Which wouldst thou prefer , carpentering or trying to persuade thy fellow-men to give up drinking , and to become teetotalers ? '' he asked", "context_len": 26}}}, "captive": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 46, "context": "It appeared that he was ashore at the moment making arrangements for the shipment of a cargo of slaves on the next day ; but the chief mate was aboard , and upon representing myself to him as a shipwrecked Englishman who had been carried away captive into the interior , and had just effected my escape , he gave me permission to remain , saying that he had no doubt Captain Duquesne would receive me if I were willing to work my passage to Martinique", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 20, "context": "Windsor surrendered to a blast of trumpets , and a few days later the little Queen was yielded up a captive into Henry 's hands , and was carried with faithful Simonette , her Saracen maid , to the Castle of Ledes ; but Ledes , fortunately , proved to be a beautiful castle , with a large garden , and she was not treated harshly or unkindly", "context_len": 68}}}, "remembering": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Besides biting and getting away , he never did the slightest thing worth remembering ; as there was no election , he did not even take part in a Whig procession", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 40, "context": "Dey 're trained to behave jus ' of dere own selves without any driver , and when dey get out on de road dey always do behave ; and then in the moment 's pause that followed , Marie-Celeste , remembering certain recent performances of her own , wondered if her father wished that a certain little girl , of whom he had some knowledge , more closely resembled these royal ponies , who , once trained to behave , according to Albert , never dreamed of taking the bit in their teeth or of kicking over the traces", "context_len": 99}}}, "yelling": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 48, "context": "Of course the only way was to climb for the coon , which would be out at the point of a high and slender limb , and would bite you awfully , even if the limb did not break under you , while the boys kept whooping and yelling and holloing out what to do , and Tip the dog just howled with excitement", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 78, "context": "It seemed to him that he had to wait an hour , and just when he was going to hollo , and tell his brother where the ducks were , the old smoothbore sent out a red flash and a white puff before he heard the report ; Tip tore loose from his grasp ; and he heard the splashing rise of the ducks , and the hurtling rush of their wings ; and he ran forward , yelling , `` How many did you hit ? Where are they ? Where are you ? Are they coming back ? It 's my turn now ! '' and making an outcry that would have frightened away a fleet of ironclads , but much less a flock of ducks", "context_len": 128}}}, "shifting": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 60, "context": "Guy 's only comfort was , and , perhaps , too , that of the poor baby 's father and mother , that he could not be quite alone , even when they left him , for God would watch over him ; and he could not but rejoice that they had not been forced to leave him in the shifting sands of the desert , but that a green tree bent over him , and grass would spring above the sod in which he lay", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "As they had approached on this side of the boiling spring on a previous occasion , much closer to the water than they now were , it was evident that there had been a shifting of the earth underneath the surface", "context_len": 41}}}, "grab": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 26, "context": "She kept a severe eye on the small children , patted them firmly on the back when they choked , and refused to allow them to grab at the cake , regulating their tea according to her own notions of what was good for them , and turning stolen lumps of sugar out of their pockets with the cleverness of a detective", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 53, "context": "Once Drusie , who had wriggled herself through the thick hawthorns at the back of the fort , was within an ace of taking the flag ; but , just as she had climbed up on the roof , the defender , whose face was completely hidden by his helmet , made a grab at her , and she was obliged to fly for her life", "context_len": 66}}}, "backed": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 17, "context": "The boys hunted rats with him at night , when they came out of the stables that backed down to the Hydraulic , for water ; and a dog who liked above all things to lie asleep on the back-step , by day , and would no more think of chasing a pig out of the garden than he would think of sitting up all night with a coon , would get frantic about rats , and would perfectly wear himself out hunting them on land and in the water , and keep on after the boys themselves were tired", "context_len": 100}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "She sat in a high backed chair and Marilla thought she looked like a princess , and her gown made the child think of the beautiful dresses in the fairy place", "context_len": 31}}}, "truest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 88, "context": "Harris to defy credulity was that Ted should have fallen into the hands of the Hartleys , for in what other little cottage in all England could such a transformation have been wrought ? Where else could he have been brought into such close touch with all the old home interests as he had been there , first through Chris and afterward through Donald and Marie-Celeste , and where else could he have come to see so clearly that he had been wilfully trampling upon all that is truest and best in life ? Fritz , said Mrs", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "A few years ago , when my first story saw the light , a little fellow , a stranger to me then , but who has since proved himself the truest of friends , wrote me a most welcome letter", "context_len": 40}}}, "trustful": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "He loved the whole human race , and as a watch-dog he was a failure through his trustful nature ; he would no more have bitten a person than he would have bitten a pig ; but where other dogs were concerned , he was a lion", "context_len": 47}}}, "nickname": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 15, "context": "She was the first girl in Cherry Court School who had dared to adopt a nickname for any of her companions , and Florence , who had begun by being indignant , could not help laughing now as the saucy creature fixed her with her bright eyes", "context_len": 47}}}, "battle": {"freq": 221, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 221, "word_index": 17, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "spilling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "But if he caught sight of a dog that he did not know , he would drop that stick and start for that dog as far off as he could see him , spilling the Henry Clay Club out of the wagon piecemeal as he went , and never stopping till he mixed up the strange dog in a fight where it would have been hard to tell which was either champion and which was the club wagon", "context_len": 78}}}, "piecemeal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "But if he caught sight of a dog that he did not know , he would drop that stick and start for that dog as far off as he could see him , spilling the Henry Clay Club out of the wagon piecemeal as he went , and never stopping till he mixed up the strange dog in a fight where it would have been hard to tell which was either champion and which was the club wagon", "context_len": 78}}}, "smilingly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 74, "context": "Will you tell it to us , Marie-Celeste ? And Marie-Celeste , nothing loath , and willing at last to substantiate her claims in the ears of doubting Albert , rested a hand comfortably on either arm of her chair , and commenced , preceding her narration with the request , You will correct me , wo n't you , if you find I do not tell it right ? to which Her Majesty smilingly acceded , first asking Miss Belmore to hand her a little jewelled miniature case from among the other treasures on the table", "context_len": 97}}}, "fragments": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 45, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}}}, "vehicle": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 2, "context": "Altogether the vehicle had a lordly air , easily matching that of the individual sitting alone on the purple cushions -- a man whose features were not very clear at the distance , although the yellowness of his beard , the glitter of his studded shirt-front , and whole consequential , expansive effect recalled to the doctor 's mind an image of the past , less ornate , indeed , and affluent , but of similar aspect", "context_len": 77}}}, "profess": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 82, "context": "When the damsel saw him , she opened him the privy door and he went in to the Lady Bedrulbudour [ 596 ] and said to her , `` Harkye , I will have thee dress and tire thyself and put away melancholy from thee ; and when the accursed Maugrabin cometh to thee , do thou receive him with 'Welcome and fair welcome ' and go to meet him with a smiling face and bid him come sup with thee and profess to him that thou hast forgotten thy beloved Alaeddin and thy father and that thou lovest him with an exceeding love", "context_len": 104}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Of the three religions in the Panjab , I think Sikhism by far the best ; but then the race of those who profess it in purity seems to be dying away", "context_len": 32}}}, "endearing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "He was an intensely loving and lovable man ; his wonderful sweetness and evenness of temper , never disturbed by heavy work or pressing cares , endearing him to all with whom he came in contact", "context_len": 36}}}, "qualities": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 92, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}}}, "brag": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "As to his mind , it was perhaps not much to brag of , and he certainly had some defects of character", "context_len": 22}}}, "defects": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Buckner 's Creek Grammar , with its numerous defects , I have extracted but a few conjugational forms of the verb isita to take , but have availed myself of some linguistic manuscripts of Mrs", "context_len": 35}}}, "incurably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He was incurably lazy , and his laziness grew upon him as he grew older , till hardly anything but the sight of a gun or a bone would move him", "context_len": 31}}}, "laziness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Self-indulgence , laziness , cowardliness were unknown factors in her existence , and could never have held her back ; but here too there was probably an innate sense of lack of power ; and here too she never through life took to the occupation , as to the manner born", "context_len": 51}}}, "ardor": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 43, "context": "It seemed strange to see men of such age , standing , and experience , obeying the orders of such a boy ; but there was something in the genius , the power , and the enthusiasm of Alexander 's character which inspired ardor in all around him , and made every one eager to join his standard and to aid in the execution of his plans", "context_len": 67}}}, "muzzles": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Once a year as long as the family lived in the Boy 's Town , the children were anxious about Tip when the dog-law was put in force , and the constables went round shooting all the dogs that were found running at large without muzzles", "context_len": 46}}}, "unseasonable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "At this time , when Tip was in danger of going mad and biting people , he showed a most unseasonable activity , and could hardly be kept in bounds", "context_len": 30}}}, "activity": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 14, "context": "My uncle busied himself till all traces of his worry lost themselves in his activity ; my aunt bustled about the house , driving the servants hither and thither , bringing linen from the huge oak chest , furbishing the massive furniture , and causing a huge supply of viands to be prepared for the entertainment of our guests -- for the invited company numbered close on a score , so that our house would be filled to overflowing , and rooms had to be engaged at `` The Bell Inn '' and `` The Blue Posts ''", "context_len": 98}}}, "rouse": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 59, "context": "That it came off they knew , and there was much talk of what this one or that one wore , of how late they stayed and how many dances they had , but that was all , and the stay-at-homes decided that , after all they had not missed much , and if Clara 's intention was to rouse their envy she failed of her purpose", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Sir ! sir ! rouse yourself , '' and she shook the helpless man gently by the shoulder", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "And there are few things which sooner rouse the evil passions and dislike of others than this trick of fault-finding where we have no right or need to do so , or of meddling with that which does not concern us", "context_len": 41}}}, "forays": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "A dog whose sole delight at other moments was to bask in the summer sun , or dream by the winter fire , would now rouse himself to an interest in everything that was going on in the dangerous world , and make forays into it at all unguarded points", "context_len": 50}}}, "unguarded": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 56, "context": "The hostess who brought Dick 's bowl of onion broth at this moment destroyed his chance of feigning sleep , and he had to resign himself to endure his companion 's conversation which flowed on , garnished with oaths and cant phrases in three or four different languages , without any interruption , till by an unguarded movement Richard exposed his face to the light of the fire , and the stranger stared a moment , and then sprang up exclaiming , `` Body o ' me if it be not Measter Dick himself ! ''", "context_len": 96}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "There was no call for him to go any farther , since the sentinel there must be as vigilant as himself , and it was not wise to leave his own charge unguarded even for a few minutes", "context_len": 38}}}, "inexorable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}}}, "offender": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 67, "context": "For a dentist , he gave an upward twist of the hand from his jaw , and uttered a howl which left no doubt that he meant tooth-pulling ; and for what would happen to a boy if he kept on misbehaving , he crossed his fingers before his face and looked through them in a way that brought the jail-window clearly before the eyes of the offender", "context_len": 68}}}, "unmuzzled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The boy was playing before his door , when a constable came by with his rifle on his shoulder , and asked him if he had seen any unmuzzled dogs about ; and partly from pride at being addressed by a constable , partly from a nervous fear of refusing to answer , and partly from a childish curiosity to see what would happen , he said , `` Yes ; one over there by the pork-house", "context_len": 77}}}, "refusing": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 11, "context": "He hesitated for a moment , not from any intention of refusing to give his uncle his confidence , or denying that he had been in trouble , but from a desire to frame his confession in the best manner possible ; but nothing came to his aid other than the plain , unvarnished truth ; nothing else , he felt , would serve his turn here with that steady , searching eye upon him ; and in a moment he had taken his resolve , and the whole shameful tale was poured into Colonel Rush 's ears", "context_len": 98}}}, "haunches": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 32, "context": "The little dog , who had at first seemed to fear some harm threatened its master , frisked back and forth before them , barking frantically , finally sitting up on its haunches with its forward paws drooping , its mouth open and its protruding tongue quivering ; for at last it appeared to comprehend that there was really no danger , and this affair was one over which even a small yellow dog should laugh and be happy", "context_len": 79}}}, "peril": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 26, "context": "Yet I scarcely knew precisely what it was that I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Turned , though he knew it not , toward a more grisly peril than he had yet encountered !", "context_len": 19}}}, "flowed": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 40, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Bomba stared up at the sky while hope once more flowed like a warm flood into his heart", "context_len": 18}}}, "enriched": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Whilst Tip was still in his prime the family of children was further enriched by the possession of a goat ; but this did not belong to the whole family , or it was , at least nominally , the property of that eldest brother they all looked up to", "context_len": 50}}}, "nominally": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "Whilst Tip was still in his prime the family of children was further enriched by the possession of a goat ; but this did not belong to the whole family , or it was , at least nominally , the property of that eldest brother they all looked up to", "context_len": 50}}}, "mistaking": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "I do not know how they came by the goat , any more than I know how they came by Tip ; I only know that there came a time when it was already in the family , and that before it was got rid of it was a presence there was no mistaking", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}}}, "impulses": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 44, "context": "The truth was , that Alexander , though he had the sagacity to see that he was placed in circumstances where prodigious moral effect in strengthening his position would be produced by an act of great severity , was swayed by so many generous impulses , which raised him above the ordinary excitements of irritation and revenge , that he had every desire to make the suffering as light , and to limit it by as narrow bounds , as the nature of the case would allow", "context_len": 87}}}, "purposes": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 38, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}}}, "performances": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 31, "context": "Such proceedings as these could not of course long exist without the fame of them reaching the ears of the king , and I had only given some three or four performances when I was summoned to entertain his Majesty and his household , which I did in the great square before the palace , my audience numbering quite two thousand ; Banda and his numerous family being seated in a huge semicircle -- of which I was the centre -- in front of the palace , while the rest of the audience filled the remaining portion of the square", "context_len": 100}}}, "chewed": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "It was detected in its stolen joys just as it had chewed the ribbon of a best bonnet up to the bonnet , and was chased into the back-yard ; but , as it had swallowed the ribbon without being able to swallow the bonnet , it carried that with it", "context_len": 51}}}, "dangled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "The leaves had dropped from the trees overhead , and the branches outlined themselves against the blue sky , and dangled from their outer stems clusters of the unfallen fruit , as large as oranges , and only wanting a touch to send them plumping down into the grass where sometimes their fat hulls burst , and the nuts almost leaped into the boys ' hands", "context_len": 66}}}, "glut": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Washing-day was a perfect feast for it , for then it would banquet on the shirt-sleeves and stockings that dangled from the clothes-line , and simply glut itself with the family linen and cotton", "context_len": 34}}}, "default": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "In default of these dainties , Nanny would gladly eat a chip-hat ; she was not proud ; she would eat a split-basket , if there was nothing else at hand", "context_len": 31}}}, "orgy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 69, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}}}, "pumpkin": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Says she , ' a bit av a chicken will do and ye can make a pumpkin pie the day before , so what with a few pertaties and a taste of stewed tomats he 'll do bravely", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Pauline smiled to herself , as she cut generous slices of pumpkin pie to go with the doughnuts and bread and butter in the different dinner pails", "context_len": 27}}}, "visiting": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 60, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 25, "context": "He spent a busy time in the Crimea , doing plenty of hard work in the trenches ; and when off duty engaged in hospital visiting , tract and book distributing , attending prayer meetings and mission services , constant in his Bible reading , and always endeavouring to do good to others", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "She always deprecated any attempt to judge of her work by the number of Zenanas on her visiting list ; and indeed it would not be fair to do so , as she did not undertake regular teaching in them", "context_len": 40}}}, "exasperation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 66, "context": "What the fate of the rest of us was to be there was nothing to indicate , but I had no doubt that it would be something quite as dreadful as fire ; and I had fully made up my mind that when my turn came I would endeavour , by insult and invective , to goad my tormentors to such a state of fury and exasperation as should provoke them to finish me off quickly", "context_len": 76}}}, "banishment": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "The mother was always saying that the goat should not stay in the house another day , but she had not the heart to insist on its banishment , the children were so fond of it", "context_len": 36}}}, "butt": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "It would try to butt them into the well when they leaned down to pull up the bucket from the curb ; and if it came out of the house , and saw a boy cracking nuts at the low flat stone the children had in the back-yard to crack nuts on , it would pretend that the boy was making motions to insult it , and before he knew what he was about it would fly at him and send him spinning head over heels", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 71, "context": "Never in my life had I felt more deadly cool and self-possessed than I did then as I aimed steadily at the animal 's right eye ; I felt that I could not miss ; nor did I ; for while we thus stood motionlessly staring at each other , I released the string , and the next instant the great lithe beast sprang convulsively into the air , with the butt of my arrow protruding from his eye and the point buried deep in his brain", "context_len": 87}}}, "curb": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 20, "context": "It would try to butt them into the well when they leaned down to pull up the bucket from the curb ; and if it came out of the house , and saw a boy cracking nuts at the low flat stone the children had in the back-yard to crack nuts on , it would pretend that the boy was making motions to insult it , and before he knew what he was about it would fly at him and send him spinning head over heels", "context_len": 86}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "Why do n't you write and tell me about it , and about Sky Cottage , too ? I shall have heaps of school news for you when I come home , and I have thought of several fresh things we can make ; but I sha n't tell you what they are till I see you , so curb your curiosity until the holidays", "context_len": 65}}}, "motions": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 61, "context": "It would try to butt them into the well when they leaned down to pull up the bucket from the curb ; and if it came out of the house , and saw a boy cracking nuts at the low flat stone the children had in the back-yard to crack nuts on , it would pretend that the boy was making motions to insult it , and before he knew what he was about it would fly at him and send him spinning head over heels", "context_len": 86}}}, "bonnets": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "That day they all laid off their bonnets on the hall-table , and the goat , after demurely waiting and watching with its faded eyes , which saw everything and seemed to see nothing , discerned a golden opportunity , and began to make such a supper of bonnet-ribbons as perhaps never fell to a goat 's lot in life before", "context_len": 61}}}, "discerned": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 36, "context": "All rose to look at them , not without anxiety ; as yet they were far distant , across a wild moor , but as they drew nearer , and their standards could be more clearly discerned , it became gradually evident that it was a reinforcement ; and so it proved , for heralds , galloping forward , announced the men of Dorsetshire", "context_len": 64}}}, "bonnet": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 48, "context": "By-and-by the rustle of a very rich silk caused her to turn her attention again to the outside world , and she observed a lady of about forty-five years of age , richly dressed in deep mourning , with a good deal of crape and a widow 's bonnet , walking up the church", "context_len": 54}}}, "apprehension": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` Her accent was sharp with anxiety as she added , `` But of course he sends the -- board-money for me -- he would remember that ? '' Evidently she recalled the Se\u00f1ora Vigil 's questions and doubts on this subject , for there was such intensity of apprehension in her look that Jane felt herself full of pain", "context_len": 60}}}, "unravel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "For he would leave no stone unturned , he said , emphatically , to unravel the mystery ; and if nothing came to light before to-night , he should at once place the matter in competent hands for its solution", "context_len": 40}}}, "mourned": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 20, "context": "My dear Caretto , the grand master exclaimed , it is almost a resurrection , for we have all long mourned you as dead ; and your return to us at the present time is indeed fortunate ; for upon whose judgment and aid could I better rely than those of my old comrade in arms ? Then , turning to Gervaise , he went on : It was a daring and brilliant exploit indeed , Sir Gervaise , and in due time honour shall be paid to you and your brave companions , to whom and to you I now tender the thanks of the Order", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Father had refused to allow gunpowder , so the pistol was useless , but Peggy placed a wreath of white jonquils picked from her own garden upon the grave , and dropped so many tears over it that I do not think any dog could have been more truly mourned and regretted", "context_len": 52}}}, "plaintive": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 42, "context": "It happened that one night , when , as now was frequently the case , I had been summoned to entertain the king and his household by `` obliging them with a little music , '' I was playing some soft , plaintive air -- I forget what -- when , chancing to glance toward Ama , who , seated on the ground on the extreme left of the semicircle , was well within my range of vision , I fancied I saw some moving object close to her left hand , which was resting lightly on the ground", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was the ceaseless burden of his plaintive song , -- '' If I only could see mother 's face ! If I only could see mother 's face ! ''", "context_len": 31}}}, "interchange": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "When he called `` Nanny '' it answered him instantly with a plaintive `` Baa ! '' and then , after a vain interchange of lamentations , he had to come away , and console himself as he could with the pets that were left him", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "George , speaking of this in later years , continues : But habit is strong ; and even now , when I repair to the stables for my horse , I interchange bows with the coachman and the ostlers and all the little idle urchins whom I encounter in the mews", "context_len": 51}}}, "hutch": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "Partly because Jumbo was so old , and partly because he was also very bad tempered , he lived by himself in a comfortable , roomy hutch , with a soft bed of hay at one end and a great wide space at the other , in which he took his meals and looked out of the door at the other rabbits", "context_len": 62}}}, "bantam": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "My boy had a bantam hen that was perfectly white , and so tame that she would run up to him whenever he came into the yard , and follow him round like a dog", "context_len": 35}}}, "fearlessly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 5, "context": "Willie had been going on fearlessly , listening to his gentle little guide ; but at the sound of the lady 's voice he started , and as she kept repeating her cautions , he grew nervous and uneasy ; while Bessie , instead of watching his steps and taking heed to her own , kept glancing up at her aunt with an uncomfortable sense of being watched by those sharp eyes", "context_len": 72}}}, "pecked": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "When she had chickens she taught them to be just as fond of him , and the tiny little balls of yellow down tumbled fearlessly about in his hands , and pecked the crumbs of bread between his fingers", "context_len": 39}}}, "clambered": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 22, "context": "As they got older they ran with their mother to meet him , and when he sat down on the grass they clambered over him and crept into his shirt-bosom , and crooned softly , as they did when their mother hovered them", "context_len": 43}}}, "crooned": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 32, "context": "Bartow , she said , and there was that softened light in her wild eyes that had appeared there while she was singing the tender lullaby that Bartow \u2019 s wife had crooned to Bonny , this is where you sleep while you remain with Sobrinini on her island", "context_len": 49}}}, "coop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "The boy loved them better than anything he ever had ; he always saw them safe in the coop at night , and he ran out early in the morning to see how they had got through the night , and to feed them", "context_len": 44}}}, "bereavements": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "It was a real grief ; it was long before he could think of his loss without tears ; and I am not sure there is so much difference of quality in our bereavements ; the loss can hurt more or it can hurt less , but the pang must be always the same in kind", "context_len": 56}}}, "featherless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "They had to be much more carefully looked after than chickens when they were young , they were so helpless in their nests , such mere weak wads of featherless flesh", "context_len": 31}}}, "pigeon": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 101, "context": "e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon express for one \u2019 s thoughts before one can express them at all ? Better have wit to cover ignorance , than silence to conceal sense", "context_len": 128}}}, "hogshead": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "At times there would be a great dry stub as big as a hogshead , and the fire , getting in at the roots , would run up the inside , and roar and blaze from the top like a dozen chimneys", "context_len": 42}}}, "snatch": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "So rapt was he in the grandeur of the scene that he had forgotten all about the object of his journey , but he was brought back to the matter-of-fact present by a tremendous snatch which jerked his arm hanging over the side , and made the cord cut so violently into his hand that he was glad to give the line a twist and set it free to run for some distance before he began to check it a little", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 73, "context": "Startled by the red-coated Britishers rising up at the word of their leader , they broke and fled ; and the men of the 93rd , who , but a little before , had made up their minds to die where they stood , saw as in a dream their enemies scattered and broken ; and the cloud of horsemen which had threatened to engulf and annihilate them , make no effort to snatch the victory which seemed within their grasp", "context_len": 81}}}, "trifling": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 14, "context": "Hartley 's , Ted has detained her for a moment , to ask some trifling question , and just as she is about to make a reply , Chris , returning from his daily ride into Nuneham for the mail , swings into the room with his breezy , postman-like air , and empties the contents of the little Hartley mail-bag upon the table", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "It might seem a trifling thing that he should leave the heavier one behind for the sake of the saving in weight , but such was the fact , though the difference was slight", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Horace sat silent for a good while after Annie had gone away ; at last he said , 'Poor innocent little lamb ! and she is so earnestly seeking forgiveness for the trifling fault which is far more the sin of another than her own , while I -- ' There he stopped , and indeed it seemed as if he had been speaking more to himself than to me", "context_len": 70}}}, "hairless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "One volunteer brought home a Mexican dog , that was smooth and hairless , with a skin like an elephant , and that was always shivering round with the cold ; he was not otherwise a remarkable dog , and I do not know that he ever felt even the warmth of friendship among the boys ; his manners were reserved and his temper seemed doubtful", "context_len": 66}}}, "fry": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "When the sun was high above their heads and all the jungle seemed to fry and crackle beneath the heat of it , Bomba and his companions sat down to eat the last of the jaguar meat and some berries and nuts they had gathered by the way", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` No small fry", "context_len": 4}}}, "salamander": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "But the pony never had any trouble with the climate of Southern Ohio ( which is indeed hot enough to fry a salamander in summer ) ; and though his temper was no better than other ponies ' , he was perfectly approachable", "context_len": 43}}}, "sorrel": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "For answer I swung myself on my horse and , bidding the negro to follow if he desired , put spurs to the sorrel and went flying in the direction of the tavern", "context_len": 33}}}, "haunch": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "So Bomba had to be content with bringing out a haunch of meat and sitting beside Polulu and talking to him , while the latter munched away contentedly", "context_len": 28}}}, "arched": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "It was a quarter of a mile wide , and half a mile in length , and had been carefully attended to by owner after owner for generations ; so that groups of water-lilies grew here , and swans arched their proud white necks and spread out their feathered plumes", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Clavering had made her statement Florence had cast one anxious , half-despairing glance in Kitty 's direction , and Kitty had slowly raised her arched eyebrows and looked at her friend with compassion and distress", "context_len": 35}}}, "checks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "My boy had an ideal of a pony , conceived from pictures in his reading-books at school , that held its head high and arched its neck , and he strove by means of checks and martingales to make this real pony conform to the illustrations", "context_len": 46}}}, "martingales": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "My boy had an ideal of a pony , conceived from pictures in his reading-books at school , that held its head high and arched its neck , and he strove by means of checks and martingales to make this real pony conform to the illustrations", "context_len": 46}}}, "conform": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "My boy had an ideal of a pony , conceived from pictures in his reading-books at school , that held its head high and arched its neck , and he strove by means of checks and martingales to make this real pony conform to the illustrations", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "There is a tradition that when the Creek people incorporated tribes of other nations into their confederacy , these tribes never kept up their own customs and peculiarities for any length of time , but were subdued in such a manner as to conform with the dominant race", "context_len": 48}}}, "ewe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "But it was of no use ; the real pony held his neck straight out like a ewe , or , if reined up , like a camel , and he hung his big head at the end of it with no regard whatever for the ideal", "context_len": 47}}}, "caparison": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "His caparison was another mortification and failure", "context_len": 7}}}, "embroidered": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 4, "context": "His tunic was finely embroidered in colours around the neck , and was below of spotless white , secured by a belt richly gilded , whereon was a sheath for the dagger or knife , which was used for all occasions , whether in battle or in meal time , the haft being inlaid with precious stones", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 29, "context": "The last spark had died out of the paper in the grate and left only black tinder ; the table was left bare , the golden plates and richly embroidered napkins , and the garlands were transformed again into old handkerchiefs , scraps of red and white paper , and discarded artificial flowers all scattered on the floor ; the minstrels in the minstrel gallery had stolen away , and the viols and bassoons were still", "context_len": 76}}}, "morocco": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "Bradford came back with a small , rather worn , red morocco case , and as soon as they were all quietly settled , he opened it", "context_len": 27}}}, "stirrups": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "What he had was the framework of a Mexican saddle , covered with rawhide , and cushioned with a blanket ; the stirrups were Mexican too , and clumsily fashioned out of wood", "context_len": 33}}}, "framework": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "We can not enter more closely into the secret which gave the martyr his strength ; we know not the visions of heavenly joy which may have overpowered the present pain , we know not whether He who gave this elaborate framework of flesh and blood , nerve and sinew , miraculously suspended the full operation of His laws , as is elsewhere recorded of other martyrs", "context_len": 67}}}, "rawhide": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "What he had was the framework of a Mexican saddle , covered with rawhide , and cushioned with a blanket ; the stirrups were Mexican too , and clumsily fashioned out of wood", "context_len": 33}}}, "cushioned": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "It was a pretty affair , the low basket , softly cushioned , the black ponies with their bright , glittering harness , and the jaunty groom in his neat livery ; but Bessie had no wish to get in it when Miss Adams said , `` Come , Bessie , jump in and take a ride", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "As Guy saw no sheets or blankets upon the divans , he was at a loss to know how the sleepers would keep warm , until his mother raised the cushioned lid of one of the boxes , and showed him a quantity of coverlets and blankets , packed tightly therein", "context_len": 51}}}, "clumsily": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 15, "context": "The great gray horse , wakened by the opening of the stable door , rose clumsily to his feet , and stared , a little frightened , across his manger toward the visitor who came so early", "context_len": 37}}}, "calmed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "After the red men had flitted away like red leaves their memory remained with the boys , and a plague of bows and arrows raged among them , and it was a good while before they calmed down to their old desire of having a gun", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The gates were opened wider and wider , till the waters poured a heavy cataract into the lock , where the boat tossed on their increasing volume , and at last calmed themselves to the level within", "context_len": 37}}}, "lonesome": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 40, "context": "There was a large , round lamp suspended from the center of the wagon , and as Guy looked at his mother 's cheerful surroundings he could not but wonder that she sighed when he spoke of the dark , lonesome lodgings they had left , until he suddenly remembered that she had been nursing the heavy , fretful baby , and trying to amuse Aggie all the day", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "What makes such very rich people seem so dreadfully lonesome ? She explained that Arthur 's boy friends were all out of town , and that he did n't have many anyhow", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "The chums felt a little lonesome after his departure , but it soon wore off , for there was much to do , to get in wood and water , straighten up the camp , and prepare for a storm , which , according to all the evidences , was soon to break", "context_len": 53}}}, "javelin": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "Just before he reached the land , he took his place upon the prow , and threw a javelin at the shore as he approached it , a symbol of the spirit of defiance and hostility with which he advanced to the frontiers of the eastern world", "context_len": 47}}}, "herding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The pony early gave the boys to understand that they could get very little out of him in the way of herding the family cow", "context_len": 25}}}, "subscribers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "He had a great dislike to any fuss being made about him personally ; and , though 1500 was subscribed during his life to erect a memorial , it was , at his earnest desire , either returned to the subscribers or spent in assisting poor debtors", "context_len": 47}}}, "competition": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 52, "context": "Then , too , it was an open secret that she was Sir John 's favorite ; it had been whispered by more than one visitor to another that it was on account of Kitty Sharston that this great fuss had been made , that the Scholarship had been opened to the competition of the school , that the girls were here , that they themselves were here -- it was all on account of this slim little girl with the big eyes and the sweet pathetic face ; and reminiscences of Sir John and Kitty 's father together side by side , shoulder to shoulder , in the trenches before Sebastopol arose in the memory of one or two visitors present", "context_len": 122}}}, "comrade": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 53, "context": "My dear Caretto , the grand master exclaimed , it is almost a resurrection , for we have all long mourned you as dead ; and your return to us at the present time is indeed fortunate ; for upon whose judgment and aid could I better rely than those of my old comrade in arms ? Then , turning to Gervaise , he went on : It was a daring and brilliant exploit indeed , Sir Gervaise , and in due time honour shall be paid to you and your brave companions , to whom and to you I now tender the thanks of the Order", "context_len": 107}}}, "romances": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "Hall ! And with what a retinue did he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "romance": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 92, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "Did n't they come sailing over from Denmark and Normandy , and all sorts of places , to settle down in England , which was a new country then , just as we 're thinking of going out to Australia ? Five hundred years hence we shall be quite ancient history ourselves , and folks can romance over our tombstones if they feel inclined", "context_len": 64}}}, "sallying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "It was while matters were in this very unsatisfactory state that I became aware that some event of extreme importance was imminent in the town ; for upon sallying forth from my residence on a certain morning and crossing the great square , in the centre of which stood Banda 's crucifixion tree , I saw that a number of men were engaged in setting up some forty stout , quaintly-carved posts in a circle round about the tree", "context_len": 79}}}, "charger": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "When he arrived , he would have dismounted at the door , but there came up to him one of the Amirs , whom the Sultan had deputed to that office , and said to him , `` O my lord , the commandment is that thou enter , riding on thy charger , so thou mayst alight at the door of the Divan", "context_len": 64}}}, "champed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "He would have given almost anything to be a Spaniard , and he lived in a dream of some day sallying out upon the Vega before Granada , in silk and steel , with an Arabian charger under him that champed its bit", "context_len": 43}}}, "rides": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "In the meantime he did what he could with the family pony , and he had long rides in the woods with the other boy , who used to get his father 's horse when he was not using it on Sunday , and race with him through the dangling wild grape-vines and pawpaw thickets , and over the reedy levels of the river , their hearts both bounding with the same high hopes of a world that could never come true", "context_len": 82}}}, "pawpaw": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "In the meantime he did what he could with the family pony , and he had long rides in the woods with the other boy , who used to get his father 's horse when he was not using it on Sunday , and race with him through the dangling wild grape-vines and pawpaw thickets , and over the reedy levels of the river , their hearts both bounding with the same high hopes of a world that could never come true", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "They were not very dense , and the timber was not very heavy ; the trees stood more like trees in a park than trees in a forest ; there was little or no undergrowth , except here and there a pawpaw thicket ; and there were sometimes grassy spaces between them , where the may-apples pitched their pretty tents in the spring", "context_len": 63}}}, "reedy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "In the meantime he did what he could with the family pony , and he had long rides in the woods with the other boy , who used to get his father 's horse when he was not using it on Sunday , and race with him through the dangling wild grape-vines and pawpaw thickets , and over the reedy levels of the river , their hearts both bounding with the same high hopes of a world that could never come true", "context_len": 82}}}, "hewn": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The white people had hewn space for their streets and houses , for their fields and farmsteads , out of the woods , but where the woods had been left they were of immemorial age", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was by far the oldest part of the house ; the great beams of the roof , roughly hewn out with an axe and black with age , had been a portion of the ancient castle , and so had the mullioned windows , with their deep , old-fashioned seats and diamond panes , filled with green , uneven glass", "context_len": 61}}}, "farmsteads": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The white people had hewn space for their streets and houses , for their fields and farmsteads , out of the woods , but where the woods had been left they were of immemorial age", "context_len": 35}}}, "immemorial": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "She had put on a large blue apron , for cherries stain a good deal when they are as luscious as those in Cherry Court orchard , and quantities had to be picked , for it was the custom from time immemorial for each of the guests to take a basket of cherries away with them , and the baskets themselves -- long , low , broad , and ornamental -- were filled now first with cherry-leaves , and then with fruit , by the excited and happy girls", "context_len": 89}}}, "dense": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 66, "context": "From this land Ben had hauled his spars , and logs for boards , leaving the tops of the trees and all the brush ; in addition to this there was left quite a growth of other trees , that were not fit for timber ; these he and Joe had cut early in the spring , so that the soil was completely covered with a dense mass of combustible matter , as dry as tinder", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 33, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now , however , the pastures had given place to dense evergreens which made my young master shudder", "context_len": 18}}}, "undergrowth": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 55, "context": "Peggy shrieked , such a shriek of ghastly terror that it might have been heard a mile away , and mercifully it was heard , for there was an answering call from the wood , followed by a rustle of branches and dead leaves , and the keeper and his son burst through the thick undergrowth , and came scrambling over the fence , almost before the echo of her cry had died away", "context_len": 74}}}, "grassy": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 49, "context": "Then , taking a bow and quiver of arrows , together with a brace of spears , out of the canoe , and signing to me to do the same , Ama led the way through the dense growth bordering the river bank , until we reached an open grassy space of about twenty acres , sparsely dotted here and there with magnificent trees ; and here Ama signified that we were to camp for the day", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "Neither of them was there , now , however ; and so Helen proposed that they should climb over , and go down the grassy glade , which would bring them on to a small knoll , from whence they could command a view of the house and the wide lawn that lay in front of it", "context_len": 57}}}, "spaces": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "They were not very dense , and the timber was not very heavy ; the trees stood more like trees in a park than trees in a forest ; there was little or no undergrowth , except here and there a pawpaw thicket ; and there were sometimes grassy spaces between them , where the may-apples pitched their pretty tents in the spring", "context_len": 63}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Other privileged towns possessed a covered square , by which term is meant a bridging over of the entrance spaces left between the four buildings by means of canes laid on poles", "context_len": 32}}}, "prairie": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 43, "context": "Aggie looked in the direction indicated by her brother , and crying : `` Oh , the dreadful rats , '' was about to run away , when Guy stopped her , telling her , laughingly , that they were the wonderful little prairie dogs , of which they had heard so much", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 5, "context": "All the beasts of the prairie had he slain in his terrible anger , and all the people had fled to the mountains for food , thither he thought he would follow them , and he sat down upon a ridge of sand , to strengthen his bow , and sharpen his arrows , when , lo ! quite unmindful of him , a thousand little creatures he had fancied too insignificant to notice , sprang forth from their holes , and gathered in groups for their daily gossip", "context_len": 89}}}, "savannahs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Perhaps , at no very great distance of time , it had been a prairie country , with those wide savannahs of waving grass that took the eyes of the first-comers in the Ohio wilderness with an image of Nature long tamed to the hand of man", "context_len": 47}}}, "tamed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Perhaps , at no very great distance of time , it had been a prairie country , with those wide savannahs of waving grass that took the eyes of the first-comers in the Ohio wilderness with an image of Nature long tamed to the hand of man", "context_len": 47}}}, "conjecture": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` As to his pugilistic abilities , I am by no means so accurately informed , but if my conjecture be of any value whatsoever , I should be inclined to infer , from the fact that our enemies , the representatives of tyranny and oppression , who are endeavoring to reduce us to submission , have selected him as their champion and representative in arms , that -- -- ''", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "What first led her to adopt the style of fiction , by which she was soon to become known , it is possible at least to conjecture", "context_len": 27}}}, "encompassed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "At some points it was nearer and at some farther ; but , nearer or farther , the forest encompassed the town , and it called the boys born within its circuit , as the sea calls the boys born by its shore , with mysterious , alluring voices , kindling the blood , taking the soul with love for its strangeness", "context_len": 62}}}, "circuit": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "At some points it was nearer and at some farther ; but , nearer or farther , the forest encompassed the town , and it called the boys born within its circuit , as the sea calls the boys born by its shore , with mysterious , alluring voices , kindling the blood , taking the soul with love for its strangeness", "context_len": 62}}}, "strangeness": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "The strangeness of the house and street , and the necessity of meeting the boys of the neighborhood , and paying with his person for his standing among them , kept my boy interested for a time , and he did not realize at first how much he missed the Boy 's Town and all the familiar fellowships there , and all the manifold privileges of the place", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "And in Miss Tucker \u2019 s eyes the very strangeness and romance of the place made up for its deficiency in warmth and comfort", "context_len": 24}}}, "recapture": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "There was not a boy in the Boy 's Town who would not gladly have turned from the town and lived in the woods if his mother had let him ; and in every vague plan of running off the forest had its place as a city of refuge from pursuit and recapture", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Jerry Ford , and his pals , recapture the pirate ship and again secure their missing treasure", "context_len": 17}}}, "solitary": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 43, "context": "A grand place , too , to work , you think ; and then you recall with a sigh that the light of that glorious mind has well-nigh gone out , 'neath the weight of physical weariness and infirmity , and then the solitary home begins to look a little like a prison in your eyes , as you realize how glad its inmate would be to exchange it for the Palace of that King whose divine intent for the world he has so marvellously interpreted for us all in the days when soul was still master of hand and brain", "context_len": 101}}}, "sylvan": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The pioneer days were still so close to those times that the love of solitary adventure which took the boys ' fathers into the sylvan wastes of the great West might well have burned in the boys ' hearts ; and if their ideal of life was the free life of the woods , no doubt it was because their near ancestors had lived it", "context_len": 65}}}, "trappers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "At any rate , that was their ideal , and they were always talking among themselves of how they would go farther West when they grew up , and be trappers and hunters", "context_len": 33}}}, "hunters": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 11, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}}}, "hopelessly": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 101, "context": "Mark Penelly had gone over to the other side of the lugger while the conversation was going on , and he did not face the man he looked upon as his rival ; while Harry , unnoticed by the busy fishers as he swam round , went on , touching the sides of the lugger as he lightly swam , but only the next moment to find himself entangled in a quantity of the thin mackerel net , which seemed somehow to descend upon him like a cloud , and before he could realise the fact he was under water , hopelessly fettered by the net , and feeling that if he could not extricate himself directly he should be a dead man", "context_len": 123}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Did the Queen 's mother come out and ask you to come in ? whispered Marie-Celeste , detaining Albert by main force , while she straightened his necktie and gave his hopelessly frowsy curls a rearranging touch", "context_len": 37}}}, "bandit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "I do not remember any boy but one who meant to be a sailor ; they lived too hopelessly far from the sea ; and I dare say the boy who invented the marine-engine governor , and who wished to be a pirate , would just as soon have been a bandit of the Osage", "context_len": 55}}}, "whites": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 30, "context": "Holmes said she really smiled , and then a horrible thing happened , like a fit , and her mouth all curled up and her eyes rolled up to the whites and Marilla screamed and fainted and the old lady was dead in a minute , and then the child fainted several times and they put her in her own bed -- we 'd had her down stairs", "context_len": 68}}}, "northwestern": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 7, "context": "The quarters of Anlaf were in the northwestern angle of the camp ; they consisted of huts hastily constructed from the material which the neighbouring woods supplied , and one or two tents , the best of which , stolen property , appertained to the chieftain", "context_len": 46}}}, "crowding": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 32, "context": "Now , by the decree of destiny , Fatimeh came down on the morrow to the city and the enchanter , going forth the Khan in the morning , saw the folk crowding together ; so he went up , to see what was toward , and found Fatimeh standing , whilst every one who had a pain or an ache came to her , seeking her blessing and soliciting her prayers , and whenas she stroked him , he was made whole of his ailment", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then , for the much crowding upon him and the clamour of the folk , the Lady Bedrulbudour heard and said to her women , `` See what is to do and what is the cause of this noise", "context_len": 39}}}, "braves": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 40, "context": "In 1781 , on the 1st of February , Milfort , great war-chief of the Creeks , left his home at Little Talassi , half a league above the ancient Fort Toulouse , at the head of two hundred young braves , to visit the legendary caves on Red river , from which the nation had issued in bygone times", "context_len": 60}}}, "squaws": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 29, "context": "That , however , he did not greatly apprehend , as they soon perceived the Indians were but a small party of middle-aged , or old men , and squaws , and it is seldom such a party attempts to molest any number of travelers", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "His nose is crushed , and the squaws laugh at him behind his back , though they do not dare to smile where he can see them", "context_len": 27}}}, "squatting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "There they were , crowding the decks , in their blankets and moccasins , braves and squaws and pappooses , standing about or squatting in groups , not saying anything , and looking exactly like the pictures", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The barrage had passed on and was `` squatting '' at about two hundred yards distance", "context_len": 16}}}, "quarters": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 92, "context": "Naturally this called forth inquiry , and it soon became known that Percy had entrusted to Seabrooke 's keeping a large sum of money , lest he should himself be tempted to spend any portion of it , as it was to be reserved for a special purpose ; that Seabrooke before going to the dinner on the previous evening had put it , as he supposed , in a secure place , and that this morning the money was gone , while he had discovered slight but unmistakable evidence that his quarters had been ransacked in search of it", "context_len": 100}}}, "filthy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Many put the cause of the disease , rightly enough , to the filthy habits of the poorer people , and hearing that salt water was a preventive , scores of the inhabitants repaired daily to the seashore , plunging into the bitterly cold waves in the hope of staving off the malady", "context_len": 53}}}, "withered": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "They were of all ages , from old , withered men to children of six or seven , but they were all alike grave and unsmiling ; the old men were not a whit more dignified than the children , and the children did not enter into their sport with more zeal and ardor than the wrinkled sages who shared it", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 4, "context": "Betsy Owen was a withered , cross-grained old dame , who by dint of the knowledge of the uses of some simple herbs and a good deal of cunning , had contrived to establish a reputation something between a witch and a quack doctor", "context_len": 44}}}, "unsmiling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "They were of all ages , from old , withered men to children of six or seven , but they were all alike grave and unsmiling ; the old men were not a whit more dignified than the children , and the children did not enter into their sport with more zeal and ardor than the wrinkled sages who shared it", "context_len": 61}}}, "whit": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 56, "context": "Harris , to whom the idea had first occurred , suggested it , Marie-Celeste had put her two arms round her father 's neck , declaring he was just a darling and yet , when you come to think of it , he was the very same old curmudgeon of a papa , and not one whit altered either , who had been so soundly berated for insisting that it would be better for Donald to have some easy work to do than to idle away the whole summer", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The magnificence of the Ramsey 's dinner far outdid Aunt Elizabeth 's , but Edna did not enjoy it one whit the more , although it was very delightful to be served by a man in livery , and to have such exquisite china and glass to look at during the meal", "context_len": 52}}}, "sages": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "They were of all ages , from old , withered men to children of six or seven , but they were all alike grave and unsmiling ; the old men were not a whit more dignified than the children , and the children did not enter into their sport with more zeal and ardor than the wrinkled sages who shared it", "context_len": 61}}}, "envied": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 81, "context": "The folk marvelled at Alaeddin 's bounty and at the excess of his munificence and were amazed when they saw that which graced him of beauty and goodliness and his courtliness and dignity ; yea , they extolled the perfection of the Compassionate One for this His noble creature and all of them great and small [ 462 ] called down blessings on him , albeit they knew him for the son of such an one the tailor ; yet none envied him , but all said , `` He is deserving", "context_len": 92}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 78, "context": "To the uninitiated this may seem a not particularly inhumane form of inflicting the death punishment ; but I , who saw the whole remaining part of the day spent in doing some fifty poor wretches to death in that fashion , can tell a very different story ; there is no need to enter into details , but I may say that those who were weakest , and who succumbed most quickly , were to be most envied", "context_len": 79}}}, "ranging": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "In fact they were , old and young alike , savages , and the boys who looked on and envied them were savages in their ideal of a world where people spent their lives in hunting and fishing and ranging the woods , and never grew up into the toils and cares that can alone make men of boys", "context_len": 59}}}, "toils": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "In fact they were , old and young alike , savages , and the boys who looked on and envied them were savages in their ideal of a world where people spent their lives in hunting and fishing and ranging the woods , and never grew up into the toils and cares that can alone make men of boys", "context_len": 59}}}, "nations": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 52, "context": "While things were in this state -- the Thebans having understood that Alexander had been killed at the north , and supposing that , at all events , if this report should not be true , he was , without doubt , still far away , involved in contentions with the barbarian nations , from which it was not to be expected that he could be very speedily extricated -- the whole city was suddenly thrown into consternation by the report that a large Macedonian army was approaching from the north , with Alexander at its head , and that it was , in fact , close upon them", "context_len": 109}}}, "barbarians": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 65, "context": "Many of those to whom he spoke have since died the martyr 's or the patriot 's death , but as yet no evil has reached us at Aescendune , although many parts of Wessex , nay , all the sea coast and the banks of the great rivers have been wasted with fire and sword , and the money which has been given the barbarians has been worse than wasted , for they only come for more", "context_len": 78}}}, "flitted": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "After the red men had flitted away like red leaves their memory remained with the boys , and a plague of bows and arrows raged among them , and it was a good while before they calmed down to their old desire of having a gun", "context_len": 46}}}, "normal": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 54, "context": "In places it was eight feet deep , with sand-bags on either side in addition , In others , in marshy spots , where the high-explosive shells had spitefully disturbed the tranquillity of meandering streams and carried the sluggish water to overflow and swamp the surrounding ground , the `` trench '' was above normal ground-level , with a lofty and broad wall of sand-bags to right and left", "context_len": 69}}}, "bullets": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 28, "context": "Helen dashed off to do as she was told , but as she was flying across the clearing she was suddenly brought up by a perfect hailstorm of bullets , which played round her in all directions , and caused her to fly back to the camp with the astounding information that it was not Hal who had been defending the fort , but somebody else", "context_len": 66}}}, "buckshot": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then you would have to use buckshot , if you could get them anywhere near the right size , or small marbles ; but a pistol was always a makeshift , and you never could hit anything with it , not even a board fence ; it always kicked , or burst , or something", "context_len": 55}}}, "makeshift": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Then you would have to use buckshot , if you could get them anywhere near the right size , or small marbles ; but a pistol was always a makeshift , and you never could hit anything with it , not even a board fence ; it always kicked , or burst , or something", "context_len": 55}}}, "gunsmith": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "It began by being simply an old gun-barrel , which my boy 's brother bought of another boy who was sick of it for a fip , as the half-real piece was called , and it went on till it got a lock from one gunsmith and a stock from another , and was a complete gun", "context_len": 57}}}, "gunsmiths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "But this took time ; perhaps a month ; for the gunsmiths would only work at it in their leisure ; they were delinquent subscribers , and they did it in part pay for their papers", "context_len": 36}}}, "ramrod": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 13, "context": "When they got through with it my boy 's brother made himself a ramrod out of a straight piece of hickory , or at least as straight as the gun-barrel , which was rather sway-backed , and had a little twist to one side , so that one of the jour printers said it was a first-rate gun to shoot round a corner with", "context_len": 64}}}, "scraped": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "There were distinct signs that the perpetrators of the outrage had sought to obliterate all traces of their dastardly work : the name of the ship had been scraped off the boat 's bows , her air-tight tanks had been stove in , yet in spite of this precaution the boat still remained awash", "context_len": 54}}}, "smoothbore": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "It seemed to him that he had to wait an hour , and just when he was going to hollo , and tell his brother where the ducks were , the old smoothbore sent out a red flash and a white puff before he heard the report ; Tip tore loose from his grasp ; and he heard the splashing rise of the ducks , and the hurtling rush of their wings ; and he ran forward , yelling , `` How many did you hit ? Where are they ? Where are you ? Are they coming back ? It 's my turn now ! '' and making an outcry that would have frightened away a fleet of ironclads , but much less a flock of ducks", "context_len": 128}}}, "mortified": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 28, "context": "Hartley , he said , taking his stand at the end of the table , and leaning a little wearily against the wall at his back , how mortified I am about what has happened , and how sorry that we should have put you to all this trouble ; and the bother of it is , Mrs", "context_len": 58}}}, "sneaked": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 57, "context": "But as he looked at the individual whom he had mistaken for a deer he felt a second tremor of excitement , for , as he had a glimpse of his face Bart was made aware that the man was none other than the one about whom there was such a mystery -- the man who had sneaked into the schoolhouse the night the diamond bracelet was stolen -- the midnight visitor at the camp , perhaps", "context_len": 77}}}, "shinny": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "He could not shut his left eye and keep his right eye open ; so he had to take aim with both eyes , or else with the left eye , which was worse yet , till one day when he was playing shinny ( or hockey ) at school , and got a blow over his left eye from a shinny-stick", "context_len": 62}}}, "hockey": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "He could not shut his left eye and keep his right eye open ; so he had to take aim with both eyes , or else with the left eye , which was worse yet , till one day when he was playing shinny ( or hockey ) at school , and got a blow over his left eye from a shinny-stick", "context_len": 62}}}, "stanched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He found that he could do it perfectly ; by help of his handkerchief , he stanched his wound , and made himself presentable , with the glassy pool before the pump for a mirror , and went joyfully back to school", "context_len": 42}}}, "presentable": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "Haven , an ex-editor , had written up a good `` puff '' for a local paper , inserted gratis an exciting comment and anticipation in reference to the impending sale , and Darry and Bob had printed fifteen hundred dodgers on their home press , very neat and presentable in appearance , and these had been judiciously distributed for miles around , and posted up in stores and depots", "context_len": 70}}}, "suicidal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a suicidal sap-sucker , which had suffered him to steal upon it so close that it could not escape even the vagaries of that wandering gun-barrel , and was blown into such small pieces that the boy could bring only a few feathers of it away", "context_len": 48}}}, "vagaries": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "She had a full cargo , consisting of seven hundred and thirty negroes , all young males , on board ; and as she was a remarkably fast and well-built ship she was a prize worth having , to say nothing of the credit that we should win by putting a stop to her vagaries", "context_len": 55}}}, "trophies": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 13, "context": "From the roofs were dangling on the inside heterogeneous emblems of peace and trophies of war , as eagles ' feathers , swans ' wings , wooden scalping knives , war clubs , red-painted wands , bunches of hoops on which to dry their scalps , bundles of a war-physic called snake-root ( s\u00ednika in Cheroki ) , baskets , etc", "context_len": 61}}}, "boasted": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 28, "context": "Though Gorswen was the most quiet little country spot you could find , it lay only four miles away from Warford , a rising inland watering-place , which boasted not only a Mayor and Corporation , but a pump-room and concert-hall , and had a large and fleeting population of visitors , and , to judge by its growing suburbs , an ever-increasing number of residents", "context_len": 66}}}, "minutest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "It was a great disappointment to him to do this -- yet he was able to write , `` It is my duty to acknowledge an overruling and directing Providence in all the very minutest things , by being in whatever state I am therewith content", "context_len": 46}}}, "faithfully": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 10, "context": "So , if you will promise , little girl , faithfully , that never , never shall those debts which I paid for you , be paid back again to me ; if you will allow me , for the whole of my life , to feel that I was the one who saved Westenra in her hour of bitter need ; I was the one who helped her mother in her last moments to go down to the grave in peace , if you will promise all that , Westenra , there is an end of everything else", "context_len": 99}}}, "symbols": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "Among the tribes of the Maskoki family , we notice the following ethnographic practices : the use of the red and white colors as symbols of war and peace , an extensive system of totemic gentes , the use of the Ilex cassine for the manufacture of the black drink , the erection of artificial mounds , the belief in a deity called `` Master of Life , '' and original sun-worship", "context_len": 72}}}, "hallowed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Frideswide 's ; there wax tapers shed a hallowed light on the sternly composed features of him who had been the bulwark of England ; and there choking sobs and bitter sighs every hour rent the air , and bore witness to a nation 's grief", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "Alfgar cast a longing glance behind at the spot where he knew all that was mortal of poor Bertric was left , to be , so far as the Danes cared , the prey of the wolf or the kite ; but the young Dane knew well that , if any were yet alive at Aescendune , the hallowed temple of the martyr would not want its due honour", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "He could bear it no longer , the contrast was too painful , he must break the sweet charm , the hallowed song , for the sky was reddening yet more luridly behind him , and each moment he expected to see Dorchester burst forth into flames", "context_len": 47}}}, "plentiful": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 78, "context": "To those friends who thought he lost caste thereby he had always the same argument -- that he saw no reason why the cultivation of fields should counteract the habits of refinement and good breeding to which he had been reared ; that in the colonies educated gentlemen set to work to labour with their hands , and are thought none the worse of : so why not in England , where land is good and markets are plentiful , especially when it involved the keeping of a fine old property which had been in the family for so many hundreds of years ?", "context_len": 104}}}, "grain": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 74, "context": "`` I know I ought to be thankful , and so I mostly am , that I and my friends ai n't no worse off than we are , and I know it 's no good to be frettin ' and worryin ' about your trials , and settin ' yourself against the Lord 's will ; but I do say if I fall down and break my arm , there ai n't a grain of comfort in hearin ' that my next-door neighbor has broken both his", "context_len": 88}}}, "twitter": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "The soft spring air floated in through the open window , and she heard the birds twitter and the frogs peep : she heard Abraham Lincoln , the old horse that she used to ride to water before she grew big enough to work , whinney over his hay ; and Goliath , the young giant that had come to take his place in the farm work , answer him sonorously : the dog barked lazily as a nighthawk swept by , and in the distant hen-yard she heard a rooster crow", "context_len": 92}}}, "doves": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "Accustomed to little quiet schoolgirl parties , she had not thought there would be such a large and fashionable assembly of guests , and had hoped that she would have her dear Maud to herself for a short time , at any rate , and be shown the doves , and the white pony , and the Indian cabinet , and the other treasures which her friend had so often described to her during the dancing-class", "context_len": 76}}}, "committed": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 50, "context": "They had been the staunchest Indian supporters of the new British colony , and had sent 28 men of auxiliary troops to Colonel Barnwell , to defeat the Tuscarora insurrection on the coast of North Carolina ( 1712-13 ) , when they suddenly revolted on April 15th , 1715 , committed the most atrocious deeds against helpless colonists , and showed themselves to be quite the reverse of what their name indicates ( y\u00e1masi , y\u00e1massi , the Creek term for mild , gentle , peaceable [ 44 ] )", "context_len": 90}}}, "unpardonable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "When he brought them back , it seemed to my boy that he had committed the unpardonable sin , and that something awful would surely happen to him", "context_len": 28}}}, "forms": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 36, "context": "Peggy , hard at work behind the scenes , put her eye to a hole in the curtain , and surveyed the prospect below , where children big and little were engaged in jumping over the forms , chasing each other round the stove , and generally acting more like monkeys at the Zoo than civilized human beings , while the Rector strove in vain to collect them at the empty end of the room to play games , and the curate , a shy young man fresh from Oxford , looked as if he would have preferred to wrestle with a consignment of heathens from the Cannibal Islands", "context_len": 109}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 56, "context": "At first sight nothing seems more frail than a herring or mackerel net , one of those slight pieces of mesh-work that , in a continuation of lengths perhaps half-a-mile long , is let down into the sea to float with the tide , ready for the shoals of fish that dart against it as it forms a filmy wall across their way", "context_len": 63}}}, "exchanging": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "They stopped for a moment outside , and then , after exchanging a few words , separated , the Greek going in the direction of the quarter in which lay the house of Vrados , while the other walked towards Gervaise", "context_len": 41}}}, "pressman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "But he just kept on rolling the forms of type and exchanging insults with the pressman ; and at the first faint twitter of doves ' wings he would be off again", "context_len": 32}}}, "snipe": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "Oh , the boundless delight of those rolling miles of heather , which looked like a crimson sea spreading onward towards the horizon , and the delicious smell of the sweet-gale as they trod it underfoot ! Great flocks of plovers flew before them , screaming their 'pee-wit , pee-wit ! ' and here and there they roused a snipe or a woodcock , while wild little ponies scampered off like the wind , indignant at having their solitude disturbed", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "He might do something in the way of defense with his pistol , but of what avail against a party of Indians armed with rifles , or possibly bows and arrows ? All they would have to do would be to remain beyond his range and snipe him at their leisure", "context_len": 51}}}, "yellowhammer": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "You could hardly take the time to wash the powder-grime off your hands and face before you rushed to the table ; and if you had brought home a yellowhammer you left it with your gun on the back porch , and perhaps the cat got it and saved you the trouble of cleaning it", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "A few minutes ' search resulted in a delighted 'S'sh ! ' from Bobby , for on a little ledge of rock under an overhanging tussock of grass was the cosiest , cunningest nest in the world , and the yellowhammer herself sat on it , looking at them with her bright little eyes , half undecided whether to stay or to fly away in alarm", "context_len": 66}}}, "yellowhammers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "They distinguished , again , in its case ; and decided that it was not a woodpecker , and might be killed ; sometimes they thought that woodpeckers were so nearly yellowhammers that they might be killed , but they had never heard of any one 's eating a woodpecker , and so they could not quite bring themselves to it", "context_len": 61}}}, "pastures": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "It was a fine summer 's evening towards the end of the month of July , and the sun was slowly setting behind the wood-crowned range of hills in the west , where the forest terminated the pastures of Aescendune ; the cattle were returning to their stalls ; the last load of hay was being transferred from the wain to the rick , and all things spoke of the calm and rest of a sweet night , fragrant with the breath of honeysuckle and wild brier , when nature herself seems to court luxurious repose", "context_len": 96}}}, "reliance": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 46, "context": "He felt some solicitude in giving his young son so important a charge , but he endeavored to guard against the danger of an unfortunate result by putting the ablest generals on Alexander 's side , while he reserved those on whom he could place less reliance for his own", "context_len": 50}}}, "rails": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 36, "context": "One glance sufficed to show that whatever the wreck was , it was not a terrible one , for there were no telescoped coaches , and , in fact , none seemed to have left the rails , while as for the engine that , too , seemed to be in its usual place at the head of the coaches", "context_len": 60}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Drake and I stood side by side conversing in low tones as we clung to the weather rails , the lively motion of the little craft making it a difficult matter to keep one \u2019 s feet", "context_len": 37}}}, "column": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 66, "context": "But from the meanest to the best swimmer there , every one paused to watch Mark Penelly standing statue-like up against the black rock , waiting till a great ninth wave came majestically rolling in , sweeping over the outer rocks -- the Shangles -- and then with a boom leaping at Carn Du , running up it , as it were , in a mighty column of water , some twenty feet even on a calm day", "context_len": 78}}}, "nipple": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "You poured hot water into the muzzle and blew it through the nipple , till it began to show clear ; then you wiped it dry with soft rags wound on your gun-screw , and then oiled it with greasy tow", "context_len": 41}}}, "footsore": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "But the great pleasure of all , in hunting , was getting home tired and footsore in the evening , and smelling the supper almost as soon as you came in sight of the house", "context_len": 35}}}, "mallard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "His brother had an alarm-clock , which he set at about four , and he was up the instant it rang , and pulling my boy out of bed , where he would rather have stayed than shot the largest mallard duck in the world", "context_len": 45}}}, "raked": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "Whit Monday was a holiday at school , however , and Peggy and Bobby , having the day at home , took a sudden fit of industry , and started to weed the shrubbery with the noble intention of having it raked over and tidied by teatime , being put somewhat on their mettle by Father 's remarks on the subject of sustained labour , and his laughing incredulity when they assured him he would find it all neatly finished when he returned from Warford that evening", "context_len": 87}}}, "ticked": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "They raked the ashes off the bed of coals in the fireplace , and while the embers ticked and bristled , and flung out little showers of sparks , they hustled on their clothes , and ran down the back stairs into the yard with their guns", "context_len": 47}}}, "bristled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 23, "context": "He started early in the morning of some bright autumn day while the frost still bearded the grass in the back-yard , and bristled on the fence-tops and the roof of the wood-shed , and hurried off to the woods so as to get there before the other boys had got the walnuts", "context_len": 53}}}, "sparks": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 30, "context": "Then mamma told how all this ground was once covered with just such beautiful woods as they had passed through , and how they were set on fire by the sparks from a train of cars , how the fire spread for miles and miles , and burned for many days ; and the people could do nothing to stop it , until God sent a change of wind and a heavy rain which put it out", "context_len": 77}}}, "hustled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "But Lilian popped down on the grass , and pulled off her shoes and stockings as the most speedy way of solving the difficulty , and hustled Miss Peggy home with all possible haste , to be dried ; for the water was very cold , and her teeth were chattering in her head , while her dripping garments left such a moist track as she went that Bobby unfeelingly compared her to a watering-cart", "context_len": 75}}}, "kissing": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 29, "context": "If so be you laid hands if it were but on a hen , if you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "At another time nurse would have ordered them to shut their eyes and not speak another word ; but to-night she seemed to think it quite right and natural that they should be so very wide awake , and not only gave them an extra amount of petting and kissing , but told them stories of Uncle Ruthven 's pranks when he was a boy , and of his wonderful sayings and doings , till mamma , coming up and finding this going on , was half inclined to find fault with the old woman herself", "context_len": 96}}}, "bulks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "When they started , he knew where they were going , and he rushed ahead through the silent little sleeping town , and led the way across the wide Commons , where the cows lay in dim bulks on the grass , and the geese waddled out of his way with wild clamorous cries , till they came in sight of the Reservoir", "context_len": 63}}}, "waddled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "When they started , he knew where they were going , and he rushed ahead through the silent little sleeping town , and led the way across the wide Commons , where the cows lay in dim bulks on the grass , and the geese waddled out of his way with wild clamorous cries , till they came in sight of the Reservoir", "context_len": 63}}}, "clamorous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "When they started , he knew where they were going , and he rushed ahead through the silent little sleeping town , and led the way across the wide Commons , where the cows lay in dim bulks on the grass , and the geese waddled out of his way with wild clamorous cries , till they came in sight of the Reservoir", "context_len": 63}}}, "tufts": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 59, "context": "He was surprised to find that they were not as large as elephants , but on the contrary about the size of a cow , which animal they closely resembled in the shape of their bodies , and limbs ; but their hair , instead of being of the same length all over their body , grew in shaggy tufts upon the back and sides , and lengthened into a sweeping mane upon the neck", "context_len": 75}}}, "bets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then he began to make those bets with himself that a boy hopes he will lose : he bet that his brother would not hit any of them ; he bet that he did not even see them ; he bet that if he did see them and got a shot at them , they would not come back so that he could get a chance himself to kill any", "context_len": 70}}}, "hollo": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "It seemed to him that he had to wait an hour , and just when he was going to hollo , and tell his brother where the ducks were , the old smoothbore sent out a red flash and a white puff before he heard the report ; Tip tore loose from his grasp ; and he heard the splashing rise of the ducks , and the hurtling rush of their wings ; and he ran forward , yelling , `` How many did you hit ? Where are they ? Where are you ? Are they coming back ? It 's my turn now ! '' and making an outcry that would have frightened away a fleet of ironclads , but much less a flock of ducks", "context_len": 128}}}, "hurtling": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 67, "context": "It seemed to him that he had to wait an hour , and just when he was going to hollo , and tell his brother where the ducks were , the old smoothbore sent out a red flash and a white puff before he heard the report ; Tip tore loose from his grasp ; and he heard the splashing rise of the ducks , and the hurtling rush of their wings ; and he ran forward , yelling , `` How many did you hit ? Where are they ? Where are you ? Are they coming back ? It 's my turn now ! '' and making an outcry that would have frightened away a fleet of ironclads , but much less a flock of ducks", "context_len": 128}}}, "outcry": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 110, "context": "It seemed to him that he had to wait an hour , and just when he was going to hollo , and tell his brother where the ducks were , the old smoothbore sent out a red flash and a white puff before he heard the report ; Tip tore loose from his grasp ; and he heard the splashing rise of the ducks , and the hurtling rush of their wings ; and he ran forward , yelling , `` How many did you hit ? Where are they ? Where are you ? Are they coming back ? It 's my turn now ! '' and making an outcry that would have frightened away a fleet of ironclads , but much less a flock of ducks", "context_len": 128}}}, "fleet": {"freq": 148, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 148, "word_index": 117, "context": "It seemed to him that he had to wait an hour , and just when he was going to hollo , and tell his brother where the ducks were , the old smoothbore sent out a red flash and a white puff before he heard the report ; Tip tore loose from his grasp ; and he heard the splashing rise of the ducks , and the hurtling rush of their wings ; and he ran forward , yelling , `` How many did you hit ? Where are they ? Where are you ? Are they coming back ? It 's my turn now ! '' and making an outcry that would have frightened away a fleet of ironclads , but much less a flock of ducks", "context_len": 128}}}, "ironclads": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 119, "context": "It seemed to him that he had to wait an hour , and just when he was going to hollo , and tell his brother where the ducks were , the old smoothbore sent out a red flash and a white puff before he heard the report ; Tip tore loose from his grasp ; and he heard the splashing rise of the ducks , and the hurtling rush of their wings ; and he ran forward , yelling , `` How many did you hit ? Where are they ? Where are you ? Are they coming back ? It 's my turn now ! '' and making an outcry that would have frightened away a fleet of ironclads , but much less a flock of ducks", "context_len": 128}}}, "foraging": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 14, "context": "The instinct of getting , of hoarding , was the motive of all their foraging ; they had no other idea of property than the bounty of nature ; and this was well enough as far as it went , but their impulse was not to share this bounty with others , but to keep it each for himself", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "While halting for these foraging parties to return , a Persian nobleman came into the camp , and informed Alexander that Darius and the forces accompanying him were encamped about two days ' march in advance , but that Bessus was in command -- the conspiracy having been successful , and Darius having been deposed and made a prisoner", "context_len": 59}}}, "auger": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 54, "context": "But all the time they kept looking out for a good sugar-tree , and when they saw a maple stained black from the branches down with the sap running from the little holes that the sap-suckers had made , they burst into a shout , and dashed forward , and the fellow with the auger began to bore away , while the other fellows stood round and told him how , and wanted to make him let them do it", "context_len": 80}}}, "spiles": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "They never could get their spiles to work , and the water gathered so slowly in the holes they bored , and some of the fellows took such long turns , that it was very little fun", "context_len": 37}}}, "straws": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "He would choose one of the roots near the foot of the tree , and chop a clean , square hole in it ; the sap flew at each stroke of his axe , and it rose so fast in the well he made that the thirstiest boy could not keep it down , and three or four boys , with their heads jammed tight together and their straws plunged into its depths , lay stretched upon their stomachs and drank their fill at once", "context_len": 85}}}, "suck": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "Without wasting a moment , I applied my lips to the double wound , intending to suck the poison from it , even as I had done in my own case ; but another startling scream from the girl caused me to look up , and , following the direction of her terrified glance , I looked behind me and beheld the king himself , his eyes ablaze with demoniac fury , in the very act of raising a spear that he had snatched from the hand of one of his guards , to drive it through my body", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "By a great strain he lifted his head a trifle and heard the thick suck of the ooze as it reluctantly released its prey", "context_len": 24}}}, "whittle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "They always brought lots of tin buckets to take the sap home in , and the big boys made the little fellows carry these , for they had to keep their own hands free to whittle the elder sticks into the form of spouts , and to push the pith out and make them hollow", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Drat that corn ! If it don \u2019 t stop botherin \u2019 me purty soon , I \u2019 ll whittle the whole toe off", "context_len": 24}}}, "spouts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "They always brought lots of tin buckets to take the sap home in , and the big boys made the little fellows carry these , for they had to keep their own hands free to whittle the elder sticks into the form of spouts , and to push the pith out and make them hollow", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Another is that the force of water , flowing from some mountain lake , by an underground passage , spouts up through the boiling spring , being heated in some manner in its passage", "context_len": 34}}}, "pith": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "They always brought lots of tin buckets to take the sap home in , and the big boys made the little fellows carry these , for they had to keep their own hands free to whittle the elder sticks into the form of spouts , and to push the pith out and make them hollow", "context_len": 55}}}, "milky": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "This was rather a pleasant amusement , and she soon had a nice little pile of those which were either milky white , which showed some faint color , or which shone with spots of mica or quartz", "context_len": 38}}}, "snarling": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 26, "context": "The two rolled over together , and Peggy clung trembling to the gatepost as she watched the confused heap at her feet , Rollo scratching , snarling , and biting like a wild beast , and the tramp kicking , fighting , and swearing in a way which made her blood go cold to hear", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 50, "context": "For a hundred yards or more the trail led over uneven rocky ground toward an immense rock , upon rounding which I found myself face to face with , and within half-a-dozen yards of , a splendid full-grown male leopard who was crouching over poor Ama 's motionless body , snarling savagely as he strove with his claws to remove a broken spear , the head of which was buried deep in his neck", "context_len": 74}}}, "chuckling": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "They could hear the blue-jays snarling at one another , and the yellowhammer chuckling ; on some dead tree a redheaded woodpecker hammered noisily , and if the boys had only had a gun with them they could have killed lots of things", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 114, "context": "Here and there Lilian ran , hunting in all Peggy 's favourite haunts -- now peeping into a hollow yew-tree , now peering at the top of a ladder , now rummaging in the tool-shed , then back through the sand-quarry into the stack-yard , where there was a very good chance that the young lady might be hidden away in some snug little hole among the hay ; but though Lilian got a tolerable amount of hay-seed into her hair , her efforts were fruitless , and she was just turning away , hot and out of breath , to give up the useless search , when the sound of a low , chuckling laugh attracted her to the barn", "context_len": 121}}}, "redheaded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "They could hear the blue-jays snarling at one another , and the yellowhammer chuckling ; on some dead tree a redheaded woodpecker hammered noisily , and if the boys had only had a gun with them they could have killed lots of things", "context_len": 43}}}, "axes": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 78, "context": "Appalled by the disaster that had befallen them , by the terrible effect of the broadsides , poured in at a few yards ' distance , and by the sight of so many of the dreaded warriors of the Cross , some of the corsairs threw down their arms and flung themselves on the deck or into the hold , crying for quarter ; those who resisted fell either under the swords of the knights , the vengeful axes of the late captives , or the pikes of the Sards ; but the great bulk , leaping from the bow or stern , swam ashore", "context_len": 105}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` I axes your pardon , sir ; no offence meant", "context_len": 11}}}, "felling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Now and then they passed near some woodchoppers , whose axes made a pleasant sound , without frightening any of the wild things , they had got so used to them ; sometimes the boys heard the long hollow crash of a tree they were felling", "context_len": 46}}}, "bees": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 64, "context": "not , ' said Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner , and the", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "When de kitchen-fire am blazin ' , an ' Aunt Dinah stews an ' bakes , All de piccaninnies gather just to smell her pies an ' cakes ; An ' dey cluster round de window like de bees upon a comb , Till Auntie Dinah she get mad , an ' turn an ' drive 'em home ! Shoo , shoo ! piccaninnies , etc", "context_len": 66}}}, "wove": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "So thither flocked the young and the old : the wood rangers and hunters from the forests of Newenham , where Herstan had right of wood cutting ; the men who wove baskets and hurdles of osier work from the river banks ; the theows who cultivated the home farm ; the ceorls who rented a hide of land here and a hide there -- all , the grandfather and the grandson , accepted the invitation to feast", "context_len": 78}}}, "intent": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` And when , '' [ quoth she ] ; `` I saw this [ seeming ] lack of wit in him , I fell to laughing at him , unknowing his perfidy and his intent ; so I took an old lamp that was in my husband 's pavilion and sent it by the eunuch , who exchanged it with him for a new lamp ; and next day , O my father , at daybreak , we found ourselves in Africa , with the palace and all that was therein ; and I knew not the properties of the lamp which I had exchanged , till my husband Alaeddin came to us and contrived against the Maugrabin a device whereby he delivered us from him", "context_len": 127}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 71, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Good-night , Chris , rather absent-mindedly , and with eyes and thoughts still intent upon the letter", "context_len": 17}}}, "spile": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "downward": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 69, "context": "For the first few days of my captivity my brain was literally seething with schemes for effecting my escape , most of them wildly impossible , I admit ; but some there were that seemed to promise just a ghost of a chance of success -- until I attempted to put them into effect , when the vigilance of my guards -- with the fear of crucifixion , head downward , before their eyes -- invariably baffled me", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "He paused in a listening attitude , but the only sounds that came to him were those of the wind howling through the leafless branches of the trees , and the swish of the snowflakes as they swirled downward", "context_len": 39}}}, "bunted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Then some of them got out their straws , and began to suck the sap up from the hole through them , and to quarrel and push , till they agreed to take turn-about , and others got the auger and bunted for another blackened tree", "context_len": 46}}}, "stroke": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 29, "context": "He would choose one of the roots near the foot of the tree , and chop a clean , square hole in it ; the sap flew at each stroke of his axe , and it rose so fast in the well he made that the thirstiest boy could not keep it down , and three or four boys , with their heads jammed tight together and their straws plunged into its depths , lay stretched upon their stomachs and drank their fill at once", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 70, "context": "Neither Peggy nor Bobby had ever seen a wild-beast show before , for those visits to the Zoo , which are the joy of little Londoners , do not fall to the share of country children , and Archie had quite a lively time keeping them out of harm 's way , for Peggy declared the leopard looked so exactly like Tabbyskins at home that she must positively try to stroke it , and was under the rope and up to the bars before Archie could seize her by her skirts and drag her back , while Bobby 's curiosity on the subject of jackals and hyenas nearly led to the ejection of the whole party from the tent", "context_len": 119}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` You are bow , and you are stroke , '' continued the Second Mate , addressing two of the men", "context_len": 21}}}, "thirstiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "He would choose one of the roots near the foot of the tree , and chop a clean , square hole in it ; the sap flew at each stroke of his axe , and it rose so fast in the well he made that the thirstiest boy could not keep it down , and three or four boys , with their heads jammed tight together and their straws plunged into its depths , lay stretched upon their stomachs and drank their fill at once", "context_len": 85}}}, "jammed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 63, "context": "He would choose one of the roots near the foot of the tree , and chop a clean , square hole in it ; the sap flew at each stroke of his axe , and it rose so fast in the well he made that the thirstiest boy could not keep it down , and three or four boys , with their heads jammed tight together and their straws plunged into its depths , lay stretched upon their stomachs and drank their fill at once", "context_len": 85}}}, "stomachs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 78, "context": "He would choose one of the roots near the foot of the tree , and chop a clean , square hole in it ; the sap flew at each stroke of his axe , and it rose so fast in the well he made that the thirstiest boy could not keep it down , and three or four boys , with their heads jammed tight together and their straws plunged into its depths , lay stretched upon their stomachs and drank their fill at once", "context_len": 85}}}, "dip": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "But by this time it would be pretty late in the afternoon ; and they would have to put it off till some other day , when they intended to bring something to dip the water out with ; the buckets they had brought were all too big", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "They lay by the fire listening to the dip of Ben \u2019 s oars , and the rumble of Uncle Isaac \u2019 s cart , till both died away in the distance", "context_len": 32}}}, "mandrake": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The next thing after going for sugar-water was gathering may-apples , as they called the fruit of the mandrake in that country", "context_len": 22}}}, "pullet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "They grew to their full size , nearly as large as a pullet 's egg , some time in June , and they were gathered green , and carried home to be ripened in the cornmeal-barrel", "context_len": 36}}}, "ripened": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "The long leaves , and the shining silk of the corn waved in the breeze that softly played about the lofty summits , lovely flowers opened , and rich fruits ripened in the warm sunshine that ever fell upon them", "context_len": 40}}}, "ripe": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was somewhat neglected , it is true , but perhaps it looked none the less picturesque for that , and certainly no one would be disposed to quarrel with the beautiful ripe strawberries and the sweet little yellow gooseberries with the hairy skins , or the big red plums that hung upon the old brick walls", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Midget and Bess , '' came in Fred 's clear tones from a little distance , `` come over here ; here are lots of berries , lying on top of one another almost , ripe and sweet ; and calling out , 'Come pick me ! ' They hang low , so we 'll leave them for you , and it 's nice and shady too", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Fred displayed an enormous melon which had been ripe for some days , and was now rather too mellow and soft , and , having been jolted somewhat severely on its ride down the mountain , had fallen to pieces , presenting , as joking Fred said , `` a very melon choly sight", "context_len": 54}}}, "watery": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 70, "context": "When again closely pressed by the pursuing Moskoquis , who had defeated them more than once , the Alibamu crossed over to the left side of the river ; but this did not save them from pursuit , for the Moskoquis followed them to the opposite side , defeated them in a sharp encounter , and drove them in the direction of Mississippi river , in which many found a watery grave in their hasty flight", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "He seemed to be stupefied with terror , and stared from one to the other of his captors with red and watery eyes that begged for mercy", "context_len": 27}}}, "straggling": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 30, "context": "But the boys gathered them every spring , in the pleasant open woods where they grew , just beyond the densest shade of the trees , among the tall , straggling grasses ; and they had that joyous sense of the bounty of nature in hoarding them up which is one of the sweetest and dearest experiences of childhood", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The coach now descended a long declivity , at the bottom of which lay a straggling village , which , I was told , boasted of the name of Horndean", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "The sergeant saluted , and told his story : The troop was riding in a straggling manner , one of the men , who had a prisoner bound behind him ( he with the scarred face ) , being in the rear", "context_len": 42}}}, "joyous": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 55, "context": "Even Toby seemed to forget himself for a moment or two ; and once , when the chickens were all flying over the grass , screaming and laughing , he sprang up from his post on the porch , where he had been quietly watching them , and came bounding down among them with a joyous bark , and seized hold of the fox by the coat tails , just as he pounced on Harry and Walter , as if he thought they had need of his help", "context_len": 88}}}, "hoarding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "But the boys gathered them every spring , in the pleasant open woods where they grew , just beyond the densest shade of the trees , among the tall , straggling grasses ; and they had that joyous sense of the bounty of nature in hoarding them up which is one of the sweetest and dearest experiences of childhood", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The instinct of getting , of hoarding , was the motive of all their foraging ; they had no other idea of property than the bounty of nature ; and this was well enough as far as it went , but their impulse was not to share this bounty with others , but to keep it each for himself", "context_len": 59}}}, "rejoices": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Through this the boy comes close to the heart of the mother of us all , and rejoices in the wealth she never grudges to those who are willing to be merely rich enough", "context_len": 34}}}, "grudges": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Through this the boy comes close to the heart of the mother of us all , and rejoices in the wealth she never grudges to those who are willing to be merely rich enough", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Settle your personal grudges off the football field ; that \u2019 s the thing to do", "context_len": 16}}}, "map": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 20, "context": "I have a nice little pot buried in a safe place on a certain key ; I 've got a map here '' -- and he thumped his broad chest -- '' here , sewed in the lining of my coat , and the place marked with a cross ; and I tell you , sir , there 's enough gold in that pot to fit out the snuggest little pinnace any man need want to see", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "His questioner bent over a map spread out on the table in front of him", "context_len": 15}}}, "blackberries": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "There were indeed lots of blackberries in the spot to which Fred had called them ; and , screened from the rays of the sun , they picked them with comfort ; besides which , many a large berry which they did not pick themselves found its way into their pails ; so that , by the time Hafed came with his offering to Maggie , her own berries made quite a show , and she steadily refused to take his", "context_len": 81}}}, "oaks": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "Mosses and lichens , of all shades of gray , green and brown , covered their weather-beaten sides , while their tops were crowned with oaks , maples , pines and firs", "context_len": 32}}}, "heavier": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 57, "context": "Stanton , who , lying over the edge , watched him , God only knows , with what sickening hope ; the loose soil crumbled and slid beneath him ; but , light and sinewy as he was , his bare feet , trained to all kinds of mountain climbing , took hold where those of a heavier person , with shoes upon them , must have faltered and slipped past all recovery", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was harder work now that the board was heavier by reason of the added weight , and once or twice Edna was afraid that after all her efforts would be in vain", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` For , after all , they 're only the corners , '' said Maggie , `` and maybe when she tried to bear the policeman 's burden , and felt bad about the grove , that made her burden heavier , and so squeezed out her corners a little more , and they scratched her neighbors , who ought not to mind if that was the reason", "context_len": 68}}}, "guilt": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 24, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "The celebration of the p\u00faskita had a favorable influence upon the minds of the people , for it was a signal of amnesty , absolving the Indian of all crimes , murder excepted , and seemed to bury guilt itself in oblivion", "context_len": 42}}}, "reasoned": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 1, "context": "So reasoned this unprincipled boy , and he had actually made up his mind to make no attempt to restore the money to a place where it might be found , but to retain it for himself , when the doctor 's address and a dread that his crime might after all be detected , decided him to return to his first intentions", "context_len": 63}}}, "overlooked": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 14, "context": "Edna was always such a conscientious child about her lessons that Miss Ashurst rather overlooked the fact that upon this occasion she was not quite as glib as usual , and she took her seat with a feeling of great relief , determining that she would not forget her lessons another Saturday", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Retracing their way to the main dug-out , they found a hitherto overlooked passage running at right angles to the longer walls", "context_len": 22}}}, "arouse": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "At length , after a more than usually intense paroxysm of pain had passed , Gouroo , Banda 's favourite wife , who was present , and whose virulent animosity I had been unfortunate enough to arouse , bent over the patient and whispered something in his ear , the purport of which I could not catch", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Why should such a natural and simple request arouse Japazy \u2019 s ire ? Jojasta had told him something", "context_len": 19}}}, "alternates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "He was prepared to make the most of this , and his groans grew louder as he drew near home in the arms of the boys who took turns , two and two , in carrying him the whole long way from Dayton Lane , with a terrified procession of alternates behind them", "context_len": 53}}}, "deliver": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` `` O my son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not", "context_len": 128}}}, "haws": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "Once when the fellows were gathering haws out there they began to have fun with a flock of turkeys , especially the gobblers , and one boy got an old gobbler to following him while he walked slowly backward , and teased him", "context_len": 43}}}, "wormy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "They were rather nice , with a pleasant taste between a small apple and a rose seed-pod ; only you had to throw most of them away because they were wormy", "context_len": 31}}}, "gobblers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Once when the fellows were gathering haws out there they began to have fun with a flock of turkeys , especially the gobblers , and one boy got an old gobbler to following him while he walked slowly backward , and teased him", "context_len": 43}}}, "gobbler": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Once when the fellows were gathering haws out there they began to have fun with a flock of turkeys , especially the gobblers , and one boy got an old gobbler to following him while he walked slowly backward , and teased him", "context_len": 43}}}, "backing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 39, "context": "This seemed to rather shake the nerve of her skipper , for the next moment her studdingsails collapsed and came down altogether , regular man-o'-war fashion -- showing her to be strongly manned ; but instead of rounding-to and backing her main-yard , as we thought she intended , she braced sharp up on the port tack and endeavoured to escape to windward", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "All this while the ship was left to herself , the topsails backing and filling , and the spanker moving from side to side", "context_len": 24}}}, "pignuts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The woods were pretty full of the kind of hickory-trees called pignuts , and the boys gathered the nuts , and even ate their small , bitter kernels ; and around the Poor-House woods there were some shag-barks , but the boys did not go for them because of the bull and the crazy people", "context_len": 55}}}, "walnut": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Long before it was time to go walnutting , the boys began knocking off the nuts and trying whether they were ripe enough ; and just as soon as the kernels began to fill out , the fellows began making walnut wagons", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 49, "context": "They usually had no such easy time getting them as they always would in a story ; they did not find them under the trees , or ready to drop off , but they had to knock them off with about six or seven clubs or rocks to every walnut , and they had to pound the hulls so hard to get the nuts out that sometimes they cracked the nuts", "context_len": 71}}}, "hatchet": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 20, "context": "He could use a saw to block the wheels out of a pine board , and he could use a hatchet to rough off the corners of the blocks , but he had to use his knife to give them any sort of roundness , and they were not very round then ; they were apt to be oval in shape , and they always wabbled", "context_len": 66}}}, "roundness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "He could use a saw to block the wheels out of a pine board , and he could use a hatchet to rough off the corners of the blocks , but he had to use his knife to give them any sort of roundness , and they were not very round then ; they were apt to be oval in shape , and they always wabbled", "context_len": 66}}}, "oval": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 58, "context": "He could use a saw to block the wheels out of a pine board , and he could use a hatchet to rough off the corners of the blocks , but he had to use his knife to give them any sort of roundness , and they were not very round then ; they were apt to be oval in shape , and they always wabbled", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The banquet room was beautiful as well ; there was a great oval table with a chandelier shedding a thousand lights from the gorgeous prisms", "context_len": 25}}}, "axles": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "It split in the axles when he was making the linchpin holes , and the wheels had to be kept on by linchpins that were tied in ; the wheels themselves split , and had to be strengthened by slats nailed across the rifts", "context_len": 44}}}, "hubs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He whittled the axles out with his knife , and he made the hubs with it", "context_len": 16}}}, "linchpin": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "He could get a tongue ready-made if he used a broom-handle or a hoop-pole , but that had in either case to be whittled so it could be fastened to the wagon ; he even bored the linchpin holes with his knife if he could not get a gimlet ; and if he could not get an auger , he bored the holes through the wheels with a red-hot poker , and then whittled them large enough with his knife", "context_len": 80}}}, "gimlet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "He could get a tongue ready-made if he used a broom-handle or a hoop-pole , but that had in either case to be whittled so it could be fastened to the wagon ; he even bored the linchpin holes with his knife if he could not get a gimlet ; and if he could not get an auger , he bored the holes through the wheels with a red-hot poker , and then whittled them large enough with his knife", "context_len": 80}}}, "poker": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 69, "context": "He could get a tongue ready-made if he used a broom-handle or a hoop-pole , but that had in either case to be whittled so it could be fastened to the wagon ; he even bored the linchpin holes with his knife if he could not get a gimlet ; and if he could not get an auger , he bored the holes through the wheels with a red-hot poker , and then whittled them large enough with his knife", "context_len": 80}}}, "splitting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "The two ends of the withe formed a handle about fourteen inches long , and were bound together with strips of buffalo hide , which rendered it strong and firm , totally preventing it from either splitting down , or breaking when used , as no doubt it often was , with great force , upon the heads of unfortunate enemies", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Anyhow , half an hour ago I seemed to come out of a stupor , my head fairly splitting , and my stomach burning as though I 'd taken poison", "context_len": 30}}}, "linchpins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It split in the axles when he was making the linchpin holes , and the wheels had to be kept on by linchpins that were tied in ; the wheels themselves split , and had to be strengthened by slats nailed across the rifts", "context_len": 44}}}, "strengthened": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 80, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "It seems to me that it is partly because death is hushed up and ignored between them that it rests such a burden on the soul ; but if children were told as soon as they are old enough that death is a part of nature , and not a calamitous accident , they would be somewhat strengthened to meet it", "context_len": 61}}}, "slats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "It split in the axles when he was making the linchpin holes , and the wheels had to be kept on by linchpins that were tied in ; the wheels themselves split , and had to be strengthened by slats nailed across the rifts", "context_len": 44}}}, "rifts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "It split in the axles when he was making the linchpin holes , and the wheels had to be kept on by linchpins that were tied in ; the wheels themselves split , and had to be strengthened by slats nailed across the rifts", "context_len": 44}}}, "width": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 22, "context": "In the present instance a narrow circular course was arranged round the great square , a lane of about a yard in width being formed through the mass of spectators , and into this lane the victims , stripped naked , were introduced , one at a time , to run round and round until beaten to death by the bare fists of as many as could get in a blow at them", "context_len": 73}}}, "upsetting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "Joe set to work again with a will , clearing out the earth well from under the side of the stone ; then , putting his fingers beneath it , he gave a mighty jerk of his strong arms , and up it came , nearly upsetting him with the force of the recoil", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "He was puzzled , for , save a few luggers swinging from the little buoys that dotted the surface of the sea , there was not a sign of an accident by the upsetting of a boat , or of any one struggling in the water", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "'Oh , thank you ! Of course we 'll promise ! ' cried Peggy , clapping her hands , and flying off in the seventh heaven of joy to inform Bobby of the delightful prospect , nearly upsetting Nancy and the breakfast-tray in her mad career , and causing that worthy girl to wish devoutly that schools had no holidays", "context_len": 60}}}, "bearded": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 21, "context": "I did not tell you , wife , he went on , turning to her , that the reason why he bearded Hassan today was because the corsair brutally struck a little female captive ; thus , you see , he , at the risk of his life , and when himself a captive , carried out his vows to protect the defenceless", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "The first-named was the bearded ruffian who had so nearly settled my account in the valley near Petersfield , and now , knowing full well that his neck was already in the hangman \u2019 s noose , his demeanour was one of sullen ferocity , and , though he was heavily manacled , his appearance was like that of a savage beast awaiting its opportunity to spring", "context_len": 67}}}, "fives": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "The best place for them was in some woods-pasture where the trees stood free of one another , and around them , in among the tall , frosty grass , the tumbled nuts lay scattered in groups of twos and threes , or fives , some still yellowish-green in their hulls , and some black , but all sending up to the nostrils of the delighted boy the incense of their clean , keen , wild-woody smell , to be a memory forever", "context_len": 83}}}, "hulls": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 50, "context": "The best place for them was in some woods-pasture where the trees stood free of one another , and around them , in among the tall , frosty grass , the tumbled nuts lay scattered in groups of twos and threes , or fives , some still yellowish-green in their hulls , and some black , but all sending up to the nostrils of the delighted boy the incense of their clean , keen , wild-woody smell , to be a memory forever", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The rest of the little slugs struck the log hulls or missed entirely", "context_len": 13}}}, "outlined": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 12, "context": "The leaves had dropped from the trees overhead , and the branches outlined themselves against the blue sky , and dangled from their outer stems clusters of the unfallen fruit , as large as oranges , and only wanting a touch to send them plumping down into the grass where sometimes their fat hulls burst , and the nuts almost leaped into the boys ' hands", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "It was evident that his pitch of enthusiasm , or perhaps physical endurance , was not equal to that of his companion , for he panted with heat as he held the other end of the yard measure , and gave vent to occasional grunts of disapprobation as he toiled painfully to the tops of mounds to get bird's-eye views of the outlined cells , or take snap-shots of the remains of the windows and columns", "context_len": 76}}}, "plumping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "The leaves had dropped from the trees overhead , and the branches outlined themselves against the blue sky , and dangled from their outer stems clusters of the unfallen fruit , as large as oranges , and only wanting a touch to send them plumping down into the grass where sometimes their fat hulls burst , and the nuts almost leaped into the boys ' hands", "context_len": 66}}}, "hull": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "When they had got them all heaped up like a pile of grape-shot at the foot of the tree , they began to hull them , with blows of a stick , or with stones , and to pick the nuts from the hulls , where the grubs were battening on their assured ripeness , and to toss them into a little heap , a very little heap indeed compared with the bulk of that they came from", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 42, "context": "With voice no louder than if he were talking across a dinner table , the master of the liner waved away the tugs that swarmed out to help him , and with flawless judgment turned the six hundred feet of vibrant steel hull almost in its own length and laid her alongside her pier as delicately as a fisherman handles a dory", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "Marvin ; de way I knowed whar he was , was dat I knowed it ; I felt he had flopped ober to de left , so I knowed jes \u2019 de p \u2019 int to aim at ; dat \u2019 s de skience ob de hull bus \u2019 ness", "context_len": 50}}}, "battening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "When they had got them all heaped up like a pile of grape-shot at the foot of the tree , they began to hull them , with blows of a stick , or with stones , and to pick the nuts from the hulls , where the grubs were battening on their assured ripeness , and to toss them into a little heap , a very little heap indeed compared with the bulk of that they came from", "context_len": 78}}}, "ripeness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "When they had got them all heaped up like a pile of grape-shot at the foot of the tree , they began to hull them , with blows of a stick , or with stones , and to pick the nuts from the hulls , where the grubs were battening on their assured ripeness , and to toss them into a little heap , a very little heap indeed compared with the bulk of that they came from", "context_len": 78}}}, "bulk": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 94, "context": "Appalled by the disaster that had befallen them , by the terrible effect of the broadsides , poured in at a few yards ' distance , and by the sight of so many of the dreaded warriors of the Cross , some of the corsairs threw down their arms and flung themselves on the deck or into the hold , crying for quarter ; those who resisted fell either under the swords of the knights , the vengeful axes of the late captives , or the pikes of the Sards ; but the great bulk , leaping from the bow or stern , swam ashore", "context_len": 105}}}, "gloried": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` When the priestess , Alstarnah , saw this man she thought no more of the magician 's words or of her own power , but gloried in the beauty that had been given her , and said , 'He will surely love me , for there is not upon all the earth a woman as fair", "context_len": 57}}}, "stain": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 14, "context": "The knight opened it , disclosing a lace handkerchief marked with a deep brown stain , to which was fastened a piece of parchment inscribed : `` Stained with y e blood of y e Martyr His M tie King Charles '' , the jewelled hilt of a sword , a ring , and several papers", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "She had put on a large blue apron , for cherries stain a good deal when they are as luscious as those in Cherry Court orchard , and quantities had to be picked , for it was the custom from time immemorial for each of the guests to take a basket of cherries away with them , and the baskets themselves -- long , low , broad , and ornamental -- were filled now first with cherry-leaves , and then with fruit , by the excited and happy girls", "context_len": 89}}}, "staining": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Gervaise thought the plan an excellent one ; and he was soon transformed , Muley shaving that part of the hair that would have shown below the turban , and then staining him a deep brownish black , from the waist upwards , together with his feet and his legs up to his knee , and darkening his eyebrows , eyelashes , and moustache", "context_len": 64}}}, "bushel": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "In a story , they would always have had good luck , and always gone home with half a bushel of walnuts ; but this is a history , and so I have to own that they usually went home with about two quarts of walnuts rattling round under their shoes and stockings in the bottom of the wagon", "context_len": 59}}}, "quarts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "In a story , they would always have had good luck , and always gone home with half a bushel of walnuts ; but this is a history , and so I have to own that they usually went home with about two quarts of walnuts rattling round under their shoes and stockings in the bottom of the wagon", "context_len": 59}}}, "gallon": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "But they made just as much preparation for drying the nuts on the wood-shed roof whether they got half a gallon or half a bushel ; for they did not intend to stop gathering them till they had two or three barrels", "context_len": 42}}}, "intend": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 26, "context": "When the winter came on , Alexander and his army were about three or four hundred miles from home ; and , as he did not intend to advance much farther until the spring should open , he announced to the army that all those persons , both officers and soldiers who had been married within the year , might go home if they chose , and spend the winter with their brides , and return to the army in the spring", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "We \u2019 re to get a good fat commission for placing that property , and I don \u2019 t intend to let the commission slip through my fingers", "context_len": 28}}}, "cleat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "They nailed a cleat across the roof to keep them from rolling off , and they spread them out thin , so that they could look more than they were , and dry better", "context_len": 34}}}, "undertakings": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "The religious instincts of the human heart demand some sort of sympathy and aid , real or imaginary , from the invisible world , in great and solemn undertakings , and in every momentous crisis in its history", "context_len": 38}}}, "undertaking": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 16, "context": "Perhaps they undertook too much ; but the failure was a part of the pleasure of undertaking a great deal , and if they had not failed they would have left nothing for the men to do ; and a more disgusting thing than a world full of idle men who had done everything there was to do while they were boys , I can not imagine", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 19, "context": "Oh , well , said Marie-Celeste with a sigh , as though becoming oppressed with the greatness of her undertaking ; besides the Banqueting Hall there 's the Grand Reception-Room , with a beautiful plate-glass window forming almost all of one end of it , and there 's the Waterloo Room , filled with portraits of officers who fought there , and then , in a place they call the Grand Vestibule , there 's a splendid statue of the Queen", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Often part of her so-called holiday was spent in looking after or in acting as companion to somebody else , -- or in undertaking work during the absence of other Missionaries from their posts", "context_len": 34}}}, "disgusting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Perhaps they undertook too much ; but the failure was a part of the pleasure of undertaking a great deal , and if they had not failed they would have left nothing for the men to do ; and a more disgusting thing than a world full of idle men who had done everything there was to do while they were boys , I can not imagine", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "He expected to become drunk instantly , if not to end a town-drunkard there on the spot ; but the whiskey only tasted very disgusting ; and he was able to get home without help", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "'How disgusting ! Who cares for old books ? ' exclaimed Bobby , whose heart had been set on stolen jewels , smuggled valuables , or daggers and firearms at the least", "context_len": 32}}}, "prevented": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 42, "context": "But , oh , what exquisite torment was mine when , my bonds being released , the blood once more began to circulate through my benumbed members ! I could have screamed aloud with the excruciating agony , had not my pride prevented me ; and it was a full hour before I had sufficiently recovered the use of my hands to enable me to convey food and drink to my lips", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "In fact , whatever the dinner-parties may have seemed to herself , her own presence , her bright smile and sparkling conversation , effectually prevented sensations of dulness on the part of others who were there", "context_len": 36}}}, "politicians": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "A part of the Cheroki anciently dwelt in caves ; and concerning the caverns from which the Creeks claim to have issued , James Adair gives the following interesting disclosure : `` It is worthy of notice , that the Muskohgeh cave , out of which one of their politicians persuaded them their ancestors formerly ascended to their present terrestrial abode , lies in the Nanne Hamgeh old town , inhabited by the Mississippi-Nachee Indians , [ 150 ] which is one of the most western parts of their old-inhabited country", "context_len": 91}}}, "doubts": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It is very curious , but it is true ; and I advise any boy who doubts it to watch his father awhile", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 20, "context": "While she could go with a clear and perfectly easy conscience , able to enjoy herself , and untroubled by doubts , she probably did so without harm to herself , so long as her life was not given to pleasures , \u2019 that is to say , so long as she did not unduly love these things , or allow them to occupy a wrong place in her life", "context_len": 70}}}, "peeling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "He may turn out to be like an onion when he is grown up , and be nothing but hulls , that you keep peeling off , one after another , till you think you have got down to the heart , at last , and then you have got down to nothing", "context_len": 53}}}, "conforming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "But I am certain that this was the case with him , and that while he was joyfully sharing the wild sports and conforming to the savage usages of the boy 's world about him , he was dwelling in a wholly different world within him , whose wonders no one else knew", "context_len": 53}}}, "dwelling": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 29, "context": "The poor fellow seemed to have gone out too far , and , in his ignorance , had been drawn into the fierce current -- one that no one dwelling about Carn Du would have ventured to approach ; and , unless help were soon afforded , there would be a dead body cast up somewhere by a weedy cove just about the turn of the tide", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 12, "context": "I was not permitted to show myself in the doorway of my dwelling , or even to approach it nearly enough to see what was going on ; and in this dreadful solitude , waiting and hoping for I knew not what impossible happening to occur and effect my deliverance , each day seemed to drag itself out to the length of a month -- until the darkness came ; and then , with the realisation of the fact that I was so much nearer to a hideous fate , the hours seemed suddenly to have sped with lightning swiftness", "context_len": 100}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Else the lava floods may come and roll over the dwelling place of the tribe", "context_len": 15}}}, "avow": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "Indeed , the little queen , whose realm was the hearts of her nearest and dearest , would have been obliged to abdicate , for a while at least , I fancy , had she not chosen before nightfall of that same day to bury her head in the lap of her very most loyal subject , and with tears and sohs confess to her extreme unreasonableness and avow her determination not soon again to be overtaken by such a sorry state of mind and temper", "context_len": 86}}}, "witchery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "It was all vague and vast , and it came out of the books that he read , and that filled his soul with their witchery , and often held him aloof with their charm in the midst of the plays from which they could not lure him wholly away , or at all away", "context_len": 55}}}, "aloof": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "It was all vague and vast , and it came out of the books that he read , and that filled his soul with their witchery , and often held him aloof with their charm in the midst of the plays from which they could not lure him wholly away , or at all away", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "It is worth remarking that , while she kept aloof from many entertainments out of the house , she never , either then or in later years , refused to join in home-parties , or failed to do her utmost to entertain the guests", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "They therefore remained firm in their original simplicity and integrity , and after some respectful but ineffectual remonstrances , they stood aloof , alienated from their commander in heart , and condemning very strongly , among themselves , his wickedness and folly", "context_len": 42}}}, "lure": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "It was all vague and vast , and it came out of the books that he read , and that filled his soul with their witchery , and often held him aloof with their charm in the midst of the plays from which they could not lure him wholly away , or at all away", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Every boy who knows the lure of exploring and who loves to rig up huts and caves and tree-houses to fortify himself against imaginary enemies will enjoy these books , for they give a vivid chronicle of the doings and inventions of a group of boys who are shipwrecked and have to make themselves snug and safe in tropical islands where the dangers are too real for play", "context_len": 68}}}, "enchantment": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 88, "context": "the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How now ? Deemest thou Alaeddin worthy to be bridegroom to my daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour ? Hast thou seen and considered this royal building and all these riches which man 's wit can not comprehend ? '' The Vizier , of his envy of Alaeddin , answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , indeed this palace and its building and all these riches may not be but by means of enchantment , for that no man among men , no , not the mightiest of them in dominion or the greatest in wealth , might avail to upraise and stablish [ the like of ] this building in one night", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Who sprinkled the kohl of enchantment upon her eyes And gathered the bloom of the rose from her cheeks , fruit-wise ? And who was it let down the curtained night of her hair And eke through its glooms made the light of her forehead rise ?", "context_len": 47}}}, "naturalized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "First of them was Goldsmith 's `` History of Greece , '' which made him an Athenian of Pericles 's time , and Goldsmith 's `` History of Rome , '' which naturalized him in a Roman citizenship chiefly employed in slaying tyrants ; from the time of Appius Claudius down to the time of Domitian , there was hardly a tyrant that he did not slay", "context_len": 67}}}, "citizenship": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "First of them was Goldsmith 's `` History of Greece , '' which made him an Athenian of Pericles 's time , and Goldsmith 's `` History of Rome , '' which naturalized him in a Roman citizenship chiefly employed in slaying tyrants ; from the time of Appius Claudius down to the time of Domitian , there was hardly a tyrant that he did not slay", "context_len": 67}}}, "slaying": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "First of them was Goldsmith 's `` History of Greece , '' which made him an Athenian of Pericles 's time , and Goldsmith 's `` History of Rome , '' which naturalized him in a Roman citizenship chiefly employed in slaying tyrants ; from the time of Appius Claudius down to the time of Domitian , there was hardly a tyrant that he did not slay", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "A nearly fatal result to Alden followed the attempted slaying of an enormous bull by shooting him in the head instead of just behind the fore leg , but the mistake was not repeated", "context_len": 34}}}, "tyrants": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "tyrant": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 16, "context": "Ah , the old besetting sin , strengthened by long habit and indulgence , what a tyrant it had become , and how hard she had to struggle with it , how often was she overcome ! Yes , well might little Bessie be thankful that wise and tender teachers had taught her to control that passionate temper , which later might have proved such a misery to herself and her friends", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "In the centre , where the main streets crossed , was the royal tent , with the raven banner floating therefrom ; and there , at that moment , was the savage tyrant Sweyn in person", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Nay , who told the brutal tyrant the truth", "context_len": 10}}}, "test": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 47, "context": "The question should be , -- not , How much may I allow myself ? -- but , How little can I do with ? This was the question asked by Miss Tucker , and she set herself bravely , as the years went on , to test and to prove how much or how little was truly needed", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 57, "context": "But finding herself thus unfettered , the thought came up , -- Why not devote the Evening of her life to Missionary work ? Why not set an example to others who , like herself , might with advancing years be left free of ties ? Or at least , why not put the matter to the test of actual trial , and prove whether or not elderly women , and not younger ones only , might go forth to work among the Heathen ?", "context_len": 85}}}, "nourished": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The fancy nourished upon", "context_len": 4}}}, "amidst": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 61, "context": "In the early dawn of that day the Mahdi assaulted the town in overwhelming force -- whether helped by treachery is not exactly known ; and before his well-fed , well-trained hosts , the feeble worn-out garrison gave way , the walls were scaled , the city taken , and the hero who had won the affection of many nations fell amidst the people he had come to save", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 69, "context": "Then that dear friend of his brother and himself , the apprentice who knew all about `` Monte Cristo , '' came to work with them and live with them again , and that was a great deal ; but he did not bring the Boy 's Town with him ; and when they each began to write a new historical romance , the thought of the beloved scenes amidst which they had planned their first was a pang that nothing could assuage", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 53, "context": "It was too much for the boys , but the performance of the big dive was looked upon as the passing of a lad from boyhood into the manly stage , upon which he entered through the Shangles Gate , and then swam back , coming , as it were , of age amidst the shouts of his companions to swim ashore and land upon the big boulders , where the boys bathed and learned to swim in the calm weather , gazing the while in admiration at their older companions", "context_len": 91}}}, "robust": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 9, "context": "This girl of three and twenty , never very robust , had marvellous strength of will ; and , her mind being set on attempting the rescue , she prevailed over both her father 's judgment and her mother 's entreaties ; and into that awful sea the boat was at length launched", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}}}, "criticisms": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "3 , and in her brother \u2019 s house , she was wont to read aloud her own stories to her young nephews and nieces , for the sake of their criticisms , \u2019 and perhaps quite as much for the sake of amusing them", "context_len": 45}}}, "folios": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "The boy greeting him with a flash of white teeth , made no formality over admitting an entire stranger , but at once introduced him into a little book-closet on the ground floor , where a gentleman was busily engaged in unpacking folios from a great sea chest ; and as he turned to receive the visitor , Dick , to his inexpressible relief , saw a face that had been familiar to him in Scotland", "context_len": 76}}}, "priceless": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 32, "context": "Its roses are bound together with a little blue garter , which she was quick to recognize , and she knows very well she has need to thank Uncle Selden for this priceless souvenir of that happy Knight-of-the-Garter party", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "in Gorswen at the end of the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change of air , ordering him off immediately to", "context_len": 128}}}, "treatise": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "With your photographs , Sedgwick , we shall have ample material for our projected treatise , which I trust should be ready for the September issue of the Arch\u00e6ologian ' -- and the little old gentleman sat down on a broken column , and pulled a sheaf of papers out of one of his many pockets", "context_len": 56}}}, "literally": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 51, "context": "Certain tones in the man 's hearty voice reminded me of the boy 's sweet notes , and was n't there a queer suggestion of each other when I stood near them ? We Shetland ponies as I have said before are very close to human beings for our ancestors were literally brought up with the children in the crofters ' huts , and my mother has often told me that her mother had many a lick at the family platter and many a time her soft muzzle was buried in the children 's necks", "context_len": 95}}}, "fragment": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 47, "context": "There ought to have been more verses , but at this point Father had unfortunately got hold of the paper , and persisted in treating the poem in such a comic light that the indignant authoress had never found the heart to finish it , though the fragment was considered very talented by Aunt Helen , and carefully put away in an old work-box , with the first specimens of Peggy 's handwriting and one of Bobby 's little baby-curls", "context_len": 80}}}, "auction": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 95, "context": "The little old gentleman turned out to be a very learned don from Oxford , with a terrific list of letters at the end of his name , and in the course of a few days a representative arrived from the Bodleian Library with a request to be allowed to inspect the books ( which by this time had been removed to a corner of the big drawing-room ) , and after a considerable amount of talking and writing it was arranged that they should be sent to London at once to be sold by auction", "context_len": 96}}}, "demigod": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": "Heaven knows who wrote it or published it ; his father bought it with a number of other books at an auction , and the boy , who had about that time discovered the chapter on prosody in the back part of his grammar , made poems from it for years , and appeared in many transfigurations , as this and that god and demigod and hero upon imagined occasions in the Boy 's Town , to the fancied admiration of all the other fellows", "context_len": 85}}}, "winged": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 113, "context": ", love , should we fear the act of departing ? How many , many , pass Jordan , as it were , dry-shod ? Remember how peacefully sweet Fanny sank to rest , -- dearest Mother , -- how my Letitia \u2019 s face was lighted up with a smile , -- how our Bible-woman at Batala sang aloud a happy hymn within a few hours of her going ! To me it seems such a simple thing for the -- I had almost said imprisoned soul , to leave its cottage of clay , -- for the bird , as soon as fledged , to spread its wings ! We are winged creatures , and it seems a humiliation to be creeping on earth so long", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "The rooks croaked drearily to each other as they winged their way inland , and the gulls circled wailing over the heath before taking their flight to roost on some lonely sand-bank , and no other sound broke the monotonous plunge of the cold waves", "context_len": 45}}}, "castle": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 69, "context": "and as Donald looked as though he thought he might , and as that was exactly the way he was expected to look , Marie-Celeste complacently continued : Well , first you go up a flight of steps , and you find yourself in a sort of vestibule ; and there 's a splendid portrait of the architect there -- the man who restored the old parts of the castle and added new parts to it and made it all beautiful as it is now ; and from this vestibule you go on and on from one grand room to another", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 87, "context": "wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he could reach , in most uncourtly fashion", "context_len": 128}}}, "apotheoses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "Perhaps it was because he thought that his grandmother , who used to tell the children about her life in Wales , and show them the picture of a castle where she had once slept when she was a girl , would appreciate him in these apotheoses", "context_len": 47}}}, "retrospective": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "They fired my boy with a retrospective longing to have been present at the Battle of Navarino , when the allied ships of the English , French , and Russians destroyed the Turkish fleet ; but it seemed to him that he could not have borne to have the allies impose a king upon the Greeks , when they really wanted a republic , and so he was able to console himself for having been absent", "context_len": 76}}}, "allied": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "They fired my boy with a retrospective longing to have been present at the Battle of Navarino , when the allied ships of the English , French , and Russians destroyed the Turkish fleet ; but it seemed to him that he could not have borne to have the allies impose a king upon the Greeks , when they really wanted a republic , and so he was able to console himself for having been absent", "context_len": 76}}}, "destroyed": {"freq": 78, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 72, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "He said that the Persians had , under the ancestors of Darius , crossed the Hellespont , invaded Greece , laid waste the country , and destroyed cities and towns , and had thus done them incalculable injury ; and that Darius himself had been plotting against his ( Alexander 's ) life , and offering rewards to any one who would kill him", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "This he shortened to five or six inches and it served quite well as a substitute for the one destroyed", "context_len": 20}}}, "allies": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 49, "context": "They fired my boy with a retrospective longing to have been present at the Battle of Navarino , when the allied ships of the English , French , and Russians destroyed the Turkish fleet ; but it seemed to him that he could not have borne to have the allies impose a king upon the Greeks , when they really wanted a republic , and so he was able to console himself for having been absent", "context_len": 76}}}, "impose": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "They fired my boy with a retrospective longing to have been present at the Battle of Navarino , when the allied ships of the English , French , and Russians destroyed the Turkish fleet ; but it seemed to him that he could not have borne to have the allies impose a king upon the Greeks , when they really wanted a republic , and so he was able to console himself for having been absent", "context_len": 76}}}, "harden": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "But the boys were taught to harden their constitutions against the inclemencies of the seasons and the privations in war , and this result they most successfully attained by the so-called initiation , and also by continued bodily exercise before and after that solemn period of their lives", "context_len": 48}}}, "strife": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 75, "context": "Despite the fact that he was on the losing side , my father was not backward in declaring his attachment to His Gracious Majesty King Charles II ; and although our neighbours , even the Roundheads , were favourably disposed to him , making allowance for his fiery temper , yet with strangers who passed along the great highway betwixt London Town and Portsmouth , honest Owen \u2019 s outspoken declarations oft led to wordy strife , and on occasions ended in blows", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` That wars are necessary evils , only justifiable when fighting , as you , my lord , have done , for home and altar , but they are no true children of the Prince of Peace who delight in bloodshed and strife", "context_len": 43}}}, "distinguishable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Who are your pals ? Beg pardon , sir , I did n't know you were an officer ! '' ( This to Captain Nicholson , who , owing to the dirt and grime in which he was smothered , was hardly distinguishable from the others", "context_len": 46}}}, "reluctance": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 75, "context": "This question reminded him again of his determination to leave Oakdale directly ; and , knowing the good woman had regarded the room as engaged by him for the time of the fall term of school , and also feeling that to leave her thus and so deprive her of the rent money she expected to receive for weeks to come would be a poor return for her kindness , he hesitated in confusion and reluctance to tell her the truth", "context_len": 81}}}, "shipwreck": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 6, "context": "Captain Riley 's narrative of his shipwreck on the coast of Africa , and his captivity among the Arabs , was a book which my boy and his brother prized with a kind of personal interest , because their father told them that he had once seen a son of Captain Riley when he went to get his appointment of collector at Columbus , and that this son was named William Willshire Riley , after the good English merchant , William Willshire , who had ransomed Captain Riley", "context_len": 88}}}, "prized": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 76, "context": "Granby made the most extraordinary flowers of red flannel and gilt paper , -- flowers whose likeness never grew in gardens or greenhouses of any known land ; had she not baked sugar cakes which were intended to represent men and women , pigs , horses , and cows ? Were not the branches looped with gay ribbons ? Did they not bear rosy-cheeked apples , an orange for each child , some cheap but much prized toys , and , better than all , several useful and greatly needed articles , which had been the gift of Mrs", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "That Percy had been in some serious difficulty , that he was in some way heavily involved , was very evident ; likewise that Hannah knew of this and had sacrificed her much prized savings to rescue him", "context_len": 38}}}, "appointment": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 58, "context": "Captain Riley 's narrative of his shipwreck on the coast of Africa , and his captivity among the Arabs , was a book which my boy and his brother prized with a kind of personal interest , because their father told them that he had once seen a son of Captain Riley when he went to get his appointment of collector at Columbus , and that this son was named William Willshire Riley , after the good English merchant , William Willshire , who had ransomed Captain Riley", "context_len": 88}}}, "ransomed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 85, "context": "Captain Riley 's narrative of his shipwreck on the coast of Africa , and his captivity among the Arabs , was a book which my boy and his brother prized with a kind of personal interest , because their father told them that he had once seen a son of Captain Riley when he went to get his appointment of collector at Columbus , and that this son was named William Willshire Riley , after the good English merchant , William Willshire , who had ransomed Captain Riley", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Slowly , slowly the words dropped from her lips ; then came one or two fluttering sighs ; and a little ransomed soul , thirsting for the water of life , had flown away , and was safe within the bosom of Him who has said , `` Suffer little children to come unto me", "context_len": 55}}}, "picturesque": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was a very picturesque scene ; the mingled torchlight , moonlight , and heat-lightning , -- the quaint , white-robed crowd , -- the man who has dared to break through so much , who calls himself a Luther , telling idolaters of the folly of idol-worship", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I have no power with regard to picturesque writing", "context_len": 9}, "NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 21, "context": "There had been quite a spell of sickness in Gorswen at the end of the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change", "context_len": 128}}}, "vein": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 51, "context": "She was notified by the Dauntless Company that two entries , the fourth and fifth east , had entered her property , in which she had never suspected the presence of coal , and that the owners were prepared to negotiate with her suitable terms for the right of working the vein in question", "context_len": 54}}}, "sincere": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 104, "context": "it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached L'Esperance , and the extraordinary story I had heard while aboard her", "context_len": 128}}}, "stateliness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The whole narrative was very interesting ; it had a vein of sincere and earnest piety in it which was not its least charm , and it was written in a style of old-fashioned stateliness which was not without its effect with the boys", "context_len": 44}}}, "robbed": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 49, "context": "He judged Harry Paul , in fact , by the same measure as he would have meted out to an enemy himself ; and so terrible were his thoughts , so horrifying to him was the thought of the death from which he had escaped , that he was robbed of all energy ; he had not strength to do more than hang there clinging to the weeds with desperate clutch , and , with only his head out of water , gaze up in Harry 's stern eyes", "context_len": 89}}}, "accomplice": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "Their father once told them that Robinson Crusoe had robbed the real narrative of Alexander Selkirk of the place it ought to have held in the remembrance of the world ; and my boy had a feeling of guilt in reading it , as if he were making himself the accomplice of an impostor", "context_len": 54}}}, "impostor": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "Their father once told them that Robinson Crusoe had robbed the real narrative of Alexander Selkirk of the place it ought to have held in the remembrance of the world ; and my boy had a feeling of guilt in reading it , as if he were making himself the accomplice of an impostor", "context_len": 54}}}, "oddly": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 40, "context": "As a scholar he shone ; as a business man he was a failure , owing to the fatal and all too common trait amongst Maoris of the educated class of pleasure in the spending of money , and , oddly enough , to an inherent tendency to relapse , if only temporarily , to an aboriginal existence", "context_len": 58}}}, "inferior": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 47, "context": "He acted , however , himself , in this high position , with great energy and with complete success ; and , at the same time , with all that modesty of deportment , and that delicate consideration for the officers under him -- who , though inferior in rank , were yet his superiors in age and experience -- which his position rendered proper , but which few persons so young as he would have manifested in circumstances so well calculated to awaken the feelings of vanity and elation", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "The male population was divided into a class of head-men or chiefs , recruited by popular election , the selection being made among the most valorous men and the best orators in their councils ; and in two classes of `` yeomen '' : the `` warriors '' and the `` fighting men , '' these being inferior to the warriors", "context_len": 61}}}, "purblind": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "He did not know the name of this book , or who wrote it ; from which I imagine that much of his reading was of the purblind sort that ignorant grown-up people do , without any sort of literary vision", "context_len": 41}}}, "lent": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 7, "context": "Perhaps he lost it ; perhaps he lent it ; at any rate it was gone , and he never got it back , and he never knew what book it was till thirty years afterwards , when he picked up from a friend 's library-table a copy of `` Gesta Romanorum , '' and recognized in this collection of old monkish legends the long-missing treasure of his boyhood", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 12, "context": "A girl at school mislaid a pencil which she thought she had lent him , and he began to have a morbid belief that he must have stolen it ; he became frantic with the mere dread of guilt ; he could not eat or sleep , and it was not till he went to make good the loss with a pencil which his grandfather gave him that the girl said she had found her pencil in her desk , and saved him from the despair of a self-convicted criminal", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "The sound lent wings to Bomba \u2019 s feet , and the faithful slaves kept pace with him , no matter how fast he went", "context_len": 25}}}, "monkish": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "The story , as I have said , was imagined from events in Irving 's history of the `` Conquest of Granada , '' a book which the boy loved hardly less than the monkish legends of `` Gesta Romanorum , '' and it concerned the rival fortunes of Hamet el Zegri and Boabdil el Chico , the uncle and nephew who vied with each other for the crumbling throne of the Moorish kingdom ; but I have not the least notion how it all ended", "context_len": 86}}}, "legends": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 62, "context": "Perhaps he lost it ; perhaps he lent it ; at any rate it was gone , and he never got it back , and he never knew what book it was till thirty years afterwards , when he picked up from a friend 's library-table a copy of `` Gesta Romanorum , '' and recognized in this collection of old monkish legends the long-missing treasure of his boyhood", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 58, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}}}, "construction": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 12, "context": "Nancy 's evenings for some time past had been absorbed in the construction of a bright heliotrope gown with gilt buttons , and she had walked into Warford on her day out , and spent a month 's wages upon a hat , which was such a marvellous erection of flowers and feathers combined with lace , chiffon , and ribbons , that it was calculated to leave her rivals , like the Queen of Sheba , with no more spirit in them", "context_len": 83}}}, "spirituality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "These stories , without beauty of invention , without art of construction or character , without spirituality in their crude materialization , which were read aloud in the refectories of medi\u00e6val cloisters while the monks sat at meat , laid a spell upon the soul of the boy that governed his life", "context_len": 52}}}, "crude": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "They fascinated her with their crude drawing and colours still vivid and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "Farther along the street stood a square , old-fashioned , story-and-a-half house , with a more modern ell and shed adjoining , and a wretched sagging barn , that lurched on its foundations , and was only kept from toppling farther , and possibly falling , by long , crude timber props , set against its side", "context_len": 57}}}, "materialization": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "These stories , without beauty of invention , without art of construction or character , without spirituality in their crude materialization , which were read aloud in the refectories of medi\u00e6val cloisters while the monks sat at meat , laid a spell upon the soul of the boy that governed his life", "context_len": 52}}}, "refectories": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "These stories , without beauty of invention , without art of construction or character , without spirituality in their crude materialization , which were read aloud in the refectories of medi\u00e6val cloisters while the monks sat at meat , laid a spell upon the soul of the boy that governed his life", "context_len": 52}}}, "cloisters": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "It had been part of the plan to attend the five-o'clock service in Christ Church Cathedral ; and after spending a half hour or so in wandering through the cloisters and gaining something of an idea of the college as a whole , they went early into the cathedral , that they might also stroll for a while through the beautiful old church whose history dates as far back as the middle of the eighth century", "context_len": 76}}}, "monks": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 43, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}}}, "governed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 49, "context": "If he had known just what Raca meant , he might have called him Raca , for he was not so much afraid of the council ; but , as it was , his brother escaped that insult , and held through all a rein upon him , and governed him through his scruples as well as his fears", "context_len": 59}}}, "maxims": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "He conformed his conduct to the principles and maxims which actuated the behavior of the shadowy people of these dry-as-dust tales ; he went about drunk with the fumes of fables about Roman emperors that never were , in an empire that never was ; and , though they tormented him by putting a mixed and impossible civilization in the place of that he knew from his Goldsmith , he was quite helpless to break from their influence", "context_len": 78}}}, "actuated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He conformed his conduct to the principles and maxims which actuated the behavior of the shadowy people of these dry-as-dust tales ; he went about drunk with the fumes of fables about Roman emperors that never were , in an empire that never was ; and , though they tormented him by putting a mixed and impossible civilization in the place of that he knew from his Goldsmith , he was quite helpless to break from their influence", "context_len": 78}}}, "fumes": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 28, "context": "He conformed his conduct to the principles and maxims which actuated the behavior of the shadowy people of these dry-as-dust tales ; he went about drunk with the fumes of fables about Roman emperors that never were , in an empire that never was ; and , though they tormented him by putting a mixed and impossible civilization in the place of that he knew from his Goldsmith , he was quite helpless to break from their influence", "context_len": 78}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Does the air seem pure ? No petrol fumes hanging around , for instance ? ''", "context_len": 16}}}, "fables": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "He conformed his conduct to the principles and maxims which actuated the behavior of the shadowy people of these dry-as-dust tales ; he went about drunk with the fumes of fables about Roman emperors that never were , in an empire that never was ; and , though they tormented him by putting a mixed and impossible civilization in the place of that he knew from his Goldsmith , he was quite helpless to break from their influence", "context_len": 78}}}, "emperors": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "He conformed his conduct to the principles and maxims which actuated the behavior of the shadowy people of these dry-as-dust tales ; he went about drunk with the fumes of fables about Roman emperors that never were , in an empire that never was ; and , though they tormented him by putting a mixed and impossible civilization in the place of that he knew from his Goldsmith , he was quite helpless to break from their influence", "context_len": 78}}}, "empire": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 40, "context": "He conformed his conduct to the principles and maxims which actuated the behavior of the shadowy people of these dry-as-dust tales ; he went about drunk with the fumes of fables about Roman emperors that never were , in an empire that never was ; and , though they tormented him by putting a mixed and impossible civilization in the place of that he knew from his Goldsmith , he was quite helpless to break from their influence", "context_len": 78}}}, "tormented": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 49, "context": "He conformed his conduct to the principles and maxims which actuated the behavior of the shadowy people of these dry-as-dust tales ; he went about drunk with the fumes of fables about Roman emperors that never were , in an empire that never was ; and , though they tormented him by putting a mixed and impossible civilization in the place of that he knew from his Goldsmith , he was quite helpless to break from their influence", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Little by little David 's confidence in Arthur 's safety began to reassure the tormented mother", "context_len": 16}}}, "prophecy": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Gad ! '' he said , suddenly staring at a face that rose above the crowd near him , `` I was almost fool enough to think the fanatic 's prophecy was come true , and there was General Harrison come alive again ! That young fellow yonder is the very marrow of him ! Some one of his family , I dare be sworn , poor wretch , and doubtless of the same way of thinking", "context_len": 78}}}, "prophecies": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Then , glowing with enterprise , she proceeded to extend and embellish her property in a way which speedily set the town by the ears , and aroused every one to dark prophecies as to what must happen when her money should all be gone , and nothing left her but to face poverty in the palatial five-room dwelling now growing up around the pine homestead of the past", "context_len": 69}}}, "clove": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "Amanda showed her how to put first a big fat raisin in the center of the cake , then a current for the turtle 's head , four cloves were then stuck in , part way under the raisin , thus making the feet , and for the tail , another clove with the sharp end out", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "So they plied their finger-tips and touching their strings , struck up with plaintive airs , till they clove in sunder the hearts of the listeners , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour redoubled in wonderment and said in herself , `` Never in my life heard I the like of these songs ; '' so that she forgot to eat and fell to listening", "context_len": 63}}}, "syllable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Heaven knows what form he expected this to take ; but when he found himself in the store , he lost all courage ; his tongue clove to the roof of his mouth , and he could not utter a syllable of the fine phrases he had made to himself", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "The first syllable , as pronounced by the Creek Indians , contains a clear , short a , and that the name was written Muscogee and not Mascogee , is not to be wondered at , for the English language , with its surd , indistinct and strongly modified vocalization , will convert the clearest a into a u", "context_len": 59}}}, "phrases": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 41, "context": "The hostess who brought Dick 's bowl of onion broth at this moment destroyed his chance of feigning sleep , and he had to resign himself to endure his companion 's conversation which flowed on , garnished with oaths and cant phrases in three or four different languages , without any interruption , till by an unguarded movement Richard exposed his face to the light of the fire , and the stranger stared a moment , and then sprang up exclaiming , `` Body o ' me if it be not Measter Dick himself ! ''", "context_len": 96}}}, "showcase": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Why , this is ours , '' and he tossed the cap into a loose pile of hats by the showcase , and the boy slunk out , cut to the heart and crushed to the dust", "context_len": 38}}}, "slunk": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Why , this is ours , '' and he tossed the cap into a loose pile of hats by the showcase , and the boy slunk out , cut to the heart and crushed to the dust", "context_len": 38}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 55, "context": "The body of the dead kitten was securely tied round his neck with a piece of rope , and , like the albatross in the ballad of the 'Ancient Mariner , ' it proved such a weight of shame that the guilty dog did not dare to show his face to his friends , but slunk away to hide his dishonoured head in dark corners of the barn or stackyard", "context_len": 70}}}, "quads": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "At least he could fight his brother , and , when he ran , could stone him ; and he could throw quads and quoins , and pieces of riglet at the jour printers when the story spread to them , and one of them would begin , `` He who picketh -- ''", "context_len": 54}}}, "quoins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "At least he could fight his brother , and , when he ran , could stone him ; and he could throw quads and quoins , and pieces of riglet at the jour printers when the story spread to them , and one of them would begin , `` He who picketh -- ''", "context_len": 54}}}, "localize": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He was not different from other boys in his desire to localize , to realize , what he read ; and he was always contriving in fancy scenes and encounters of the greatest splendor , in which he bore a chief part", "context_len": 42}}}, "contriving": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "He was not different from other boys in his desire to localize , to realize , what he read ; and he was always contriving in fancy scenes and encounters of the greatest splendor , in which he bore a chief part", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Fanning was in his element , and was so pleased with his own consummate common sense and his own skill in getting the right things into the right corners , and in showing me what a mind he had for contriving and for making money go as far as possible , that I allowed him to talk to his heart 's content", "context_len": 62}}}, "encounters": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "He was not different from other boys in his desire to localize , to realize , what he read ; and he was always contriving in fancy scenes and encounters of the greatest splendor , in which he bore a chief part", "context_len": 42}}}, "thrones": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "principalities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "magicians": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "magnificently": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 75, "context": "The Captain took off his big hat with a great flourish and made a bow right down to the ground , then the second pirate took off his big hat with a great flourish and made a bow right down to the ground , then the third pirate took off his hat , and the fourth and the fifth , until all six had taken off their hats with a flourish and made the most magnificently polite bow any one had ever seen", "context_len": 83}}}, "apparelled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "outwardly": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 4, "context": "Yes , he was outwardly a model character : he worked early and late ; he saved money ; he was never in debt ; he defrauded no man ; his evenings were spent either in attending lectures of working men like himself or studying the subjects he loved at home ; he never drank ; he never swore ; he was looked up to , and brought forward as an example to follow for many a poor drunken wretch", "context_len": 80}}}, "direst": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "bashfulness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "Inwardly he was all thrones , principalities , and powers , the foe of tyrants , the friend of good emperors , and the intimate of magicians , and magnificently apparelled ; outwardly he was an incorrigible little sloven , who suffered in all social exigencies from the direst bashfulness , and wished nothing so much as to shrink out of the sight of men if they spoke to him", "context_len": 70}}}, "revealing": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 54, "context": "True , these men were his guests , and it might be considered that it was against his interests to denounce them , but he knew his claim for entertainment would be allowed him out of the funds found in possession of the party , with probably a liberal addition as a compensation for revealing their real character", "context_len": 58}}}, "squabbled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "He could not help revealing sometimes to the kindness of his father and mother the world of foolish dreams one half of him lived in , while the other half swam , and fished , and hunted , and ran races , and played tops and marbles , and squabbled and scuffled in the Boy 's Town", "context_len": 57}}}, "scuffled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "He could not help revealing sometimes to the kindness of his father and mother the world of foolish dreams one half of him lived in , while the other half swam , and fished , and hunted , and ran races , and played tops and marbles , and squabbled and scuffled in the Boy 's Town", "context_len": 57}}}, "directed": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 13, "context": "Go down to the big apple-tree in the corner of the meadow , directed Donald , half under his breath , and , look here ! you had better take this with you , dragging a steamer rug from the couch , and flinging it out after him , and I 'll come down just as soon as ever I can and let you know how things are going and then Donald drew the shutters noiselessly to and sped back to the lane at as tight a run as he had left it", "context_len": 93}}}, "prose": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "' Bobby took to carrying about an old horse-pistol which he had found in the lumber-room , and saying 'Oddsbodkins ' on all occasions , and Peggy put on such a general air of melodrama that it seemed scarcely possible for her to speak in plain prose ; Archie was impresario , stage-manager , scene-shifter , dresser , maker-up , and principal actor all combined , while Lilian waxed so enthusiastic that she even sacrificed the feathers out of her last summer 's best hat to adorn the slouched head-gear considered necessary for a due representation of Romeo", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "After so much dissipation , it was quite hard to settle down to plain prose again ; but school had already re-opened , and the children had stolen an extra week of holidays to join in the festivities , so it was high time they were setting to work once more", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He changed the essays into verse , endeavored to put them back to prose", "context_len": 14}}}, "sorrows": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 55, "context": "So thought Aunt Patty , and then said to herself , if she had but a short time upon earth , was there not more reason that she should spend it in doing all she could for her Master 's service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The death of Letitia , nearly twenty years later , was one of the heaviest sorrows she ever had to endure", "context_len": 21}}}, "recline": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` There were some of Moore 's songs , too , that he was fond of , such as `` When in death I shall calm recline , '' and `` It was noon and on flowers that ranged all around", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "When the time came , the roads being especially bad with the heavy rains , Miss Tucker performed her journey from Amritsar to Batala in what she called a most luxurious conveyance , -- the big , heavy Government d\u00e2k gari , [ 64 ] in which one can recline at ease , as if in a bed", "context_len": 58}}}, "declaim": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` He learned these by heart , to declaim at school , where he spoke , `` On the banks of the Danube fair Adelaide hied , '' from Campbell ; but he could hardly speak the `` Soldier 's Dream '' for the lump that came into his throat at the lines ,", "context_len": 54}}}, "hied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` He learned these by heart , to declaim at school , where he spoke , `` On the banks of the Danube fair Adelaide hied , '' from Campbell ; but he could hardly speak the `` Soldier 's Dream '' for the lump that came into his throat at the lines ,", "context_len": 54}}}, "o'er": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Loud they sung in notes of gladness , Ever o'er the sweet refrain ; 'Jesus loves us ! we shall never Lose His tender care again", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Thoughts of my sin much more humble shall make me , For thy forgiveness I 'll love thee the more ; So keep me humble until thou shall take me Where sin and sorrow forever are o'er", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 68, "context": "`` Yes , they would if they were hungry , but Lammie-noo has probably not been here more than a couple of nights , and if the bears have been roaming in some other direction they would not get wind of the fact that there was a nice plump lamb on the old Lonesome Hill farm -- -- Come up higher , and we 'll spy the landscape o'er", "context_len": 69}}}, "fulness": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 23, "context": "'It is terrible to see you suffer so , my lady ! ' she cried , one morning , when , in the fulness of her strength , she had gone from the laughing sunshine into the shadowed room , where every ray of light fell like a blow upon the invalid 's quivering nerves", "context_len": 55}}}, "dawning": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` 'Stay , stay with us ! Stay ! Thou art weary and worn ! ' And fain was their war-broken soldier to stay ; But sorrow returned at the dawning of morn , And the voice in my dreaming ear melted away ! ''", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "Another paper of copied extracts has a particular interest , because it seems to show , even then , a dawning sense in the mind of Charlotte Tucker of the needs of heathen and semi-heathen lands", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "At first there was no recognition , but as they gazed fixedly a dawning wonderment came into their eyes", "context_len": 19}}}, "morn": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 69, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Could you not keep the poor fellow with you all night ? I fear his father is in some danger , as well he may be , acting as wickedly as he did this very morn", "context_len": 37}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Michael , this poor lad , so soon to be called to meet the Judge who had entertained him as a guest at His holy Table that Christmas morn", "context_len": 29}}}, "pangs": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 90, "context": ", for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed , with feelings of envy , that many of my fellow-sufferers had already succumbed and become unconscious , if indeed they were not dead", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Does the eagle mourn over the death of the dove , or heed what pangs the kid may suffer which writhes beneath its talons ? If you are of the race of warrior kings , act like one", "context_len": 39}}}, "heartbreak": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "She worked herself up nearly to the point of heartbreak when she thought of what Father and Aunt Helen would think about it , and it seemed to her as though the very Crusaders and the lady and gentleman in the Elizabethan ruffs would look at her from their tombs in the church next Sunday with grave disapproval in their eyes", "context_len": 61}}}, "dreamer": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "Have a brother about four years older than yourself , I should say ; and if your temper is hot , and your disposition revengeful , and you are a vain and ridiculous dreamer at the same time that you are eager to excel in feats of strength and games of skill , and to do everything that the other fellows do , and are ashamed to be better than the worst boy in the crowd , your brother can be of the greatest use to you , with his larger experience and wisdom", "context_len": 94}}}, "versions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "But he could not make anything either of Byron or Cowper ; and he did not even try to read the little tree-calf volumes of Homer and Virgil which his father had in the versions of Pope and Dryden ; the small copperplates with which they were illustrated conveyed no suggestion to him", "context_len": 53}}}, "imitated": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "Afterwards he read Goldsmith 's `` Deserted Village , '' and he formed a great passion for Pope 's `` Pastorals , '' which he imitated in their easy heroics ; but till he came to read Longfellow , and Tennyson , and Heine , he never read any long poem without more fatigue than pleasure", "context_len": 56}}}, "heroics": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Afterwards he read Goldsmith 's `` Deserted Village , '' and he formed a great passion for Pope 's `` Pastorals , '' which he imitated in their easy heroics ; but till he came to read Longfellow , and Tennyson , and Heine , he never read any long poem without more fatigue than pleasure", "context_len": 56}}}, "acquired": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "His father used to say that the taste for poetry was an acquired taste , like the taste for tomatoes , and that he would come to it yet ; but he never came to it , or so much of it as some people seemed to do , and he always had his sorrowful misgivings as to whether they liked it as much as they pretended", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 33, "context": "There was the question about Hannah , also : how the money was to be returned to her , in the uncertainty as to how much she knew , and how she had acquired any knowledge of Percy 's predicament ; for that she knew something of it Lena was convinced ; and yet the child was equally sure that that letter had never been out of her own keeping", "context_len": 70}}}, "tomatoes": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "His father used to say that the taste for poetry was an acquired taste , like the taste for tomatoes , and that he would come to it yet ; but he never came to it , or so much of it as some people seemed to do , and he always had his sorrowful misgivings as to whether they liked it as much as they pretended", "context_len": 67}}}, "misgivings": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 55, "context": "His father used to say that the taste for poetry was an acquired taste , like the taste for tomatoes , and that he would come to it yet ; but he never came to it , or so much of it as some people seemed to do , and he always had his sorrowful misgivings as to whether they liked it as much as they pretended", "context_len": 67}}}, "flavor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "I think , too , that it should be a flavor , a spice , a sweet , a delicate relish in the high banquet of literature , and never a chief dish ; and I should not know how to defend my boy for trying to make long poems of his own at the very time when he found it so hard to read other people 's long poems", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Aylmer the less thought of the hearty tea she and Florence would make at home , the shrimps and the brown bread and butter , and the honey and the strong tea with a little cream to flavor it ; nevertheless , her beady black eyes were fixed on her sister-in-law now with a look which almost signified adoration", "context_len": 59}}}, "spice": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "I think , too , that it should be a flavor , a spice , a sweet , a delicate relish in the high banquet of literature , and never a chief dish ; and I should not know how to defend my boy for trying to make long poems of his own at the very time when he found it so hard to read other people 's long poems", "context_len": 70}}}, "relish": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 20, "context": "I think , too , that it should be a flavor , a spice , a sweet , a delicate relish in the high banquet of literature , and never a chief dish ; and I should not know how to defend my boy for trying to make long poems of his own at the very time when he found it so hard to read other people 's long poems", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "His closest friend was a boy who was probably never willingly at school in his life , and who had no more relish of literature or learning in him than the open fields , or the warm air of an early spring day", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "The Spaniards did not relish this , and were going to board the tiny English craft , but again they were forestalled ; for Cochrane with all his men took the Gamo by storm , killed some , and frightened others ; and ere long a marvellous sight was witnessed at Minorca , the great Gamo was brought by the Speedy into the harbour , with over 263 men on board , hale and hearty , whilst Cochrane never had a fifth of that number !", "context_len": 86}}}, "vocation": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "At present , Gervaise , your vocation lies wholly in the way of fighting , but it may be that the time will come when you may have other aims and ambitions , and when the vows of the Order will gall you", "context_len": 43}}}, "flaunting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "After first flaunting his skill in it before the boys , and getting one of them into trouble by writing a love-letter for him to a girl at school , and making the girl cry at a thing so strange and puzzling as a love-letter in rhyme , he preferred to conceal his gift", "context_len": 54}}}, "puzzling": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 41, "context": "After first flaunting his skill in it before the boys , and getting one of them into trouble by writing a love-letter for him to a girl at school , and making the girl cry at a thing so strange and puzzling as a love-letter in rhyme , he preferred to conceal his gift", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "He , however , soon had her afloat , and , taking one of the oars , he stood up in the stern and sculled her out with that peculiar fish-tail motion which is so puzzling to one not used to the custom", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "A green patch and goggles , a deep brown face-stain , and a pair of thin artistically made `` side-burns '' comprised a puzzling make-up", "context_len": 25}}}, "disgraces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "But such disgraces are soon lived down , even at ten years , and a great new experience which now came to him possibly helped the boy to forget", "context_len": 29}}}, "amusements": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 53, "context": "When with Chris it was forward clear to the bow to lean over the rail and see the magnificent prow cut the water ; or way to the stern , to watch the far-shining train , the screws churned into white foam behind them ; or an hour 'midships , where the ever-varying amusements with which the steerage passengers beguile the weary hours can be looked down upon from the saloon deck of either first or second cabin", "context_len": 78}}}, "burglar": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "His brother had once been taken to a theatre in Pittsburgh by one of their river-going uncles , and he often told about it ; but my boy formed no conception of the beautiful reality from his accounts of a burglar who jumped from a roof and was chased by a watchman with a pistol up and down a street with houses painted on a curtain", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Sleep refused to come , and she lay awake hour after hour , imagining every sound to be a burglar breaking into the premises , and wondering how Peggy could slumber so peacefully in the other little white bed", "context_len": 39}}}, "impartially": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "She laid down her work to wipe her eyes , and , observing two of her sons grappling in fraternal war at the house corner , she arose to cuff each one impartially , exclaiming , `` Ea , muchachos ! You fight before my very eyes , eh ? Take that ! and that ! '' Waddling reluctantly back to her sewing , she saw Lola standing in the white-pillared porch of the big adobe house beyond , and a gleam of inspiration crossed the se\u00f1ora 's dark , fat face", "context_len": 92}}}, "tacitly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "These pretty little creatures took the leading parts in `` Bombastes Furioso , '' the first night my boy ever saw a play , and he instantly fell impartially in love with both of them , and tacitly remained their abject slave for a great while after", "context_len": 47}}}, "abject": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "These pretty little creatures took the leading parts in `` Bombastes Furioso , '' the first night my boy ever saw a play , and he instantly fell impartially in love with both of them , and tacitly remained their abject slave for a great while after", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "In his abject misery and dread , Mark Penelly saw , in the stern gaze before him , anger and a vindictive desire for revenge ; he saw therein fierce hate , and an implacable , unchanging condemnation ; he felt that Harry was sustaining him there where he had dragged him to make his sufferings more acute , and that , after holding him up for a while , he would loosen his hold , causing him to sink at once into the deep water by the rocks , and be swept away by the tremendous current", "context_len": 98}}}, "slave": {"freq": 124, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 108, "word_index": 2, "context": "Then the slave of the lamp came to him and taking him up , set him down in his house and said to him , `` O my lord , dost thou need aught ? '' `` Yes , '' answered Alaeddin ; `` I will have thee bring me eight-and-forty mamelukes , [ 454 ] four-and-twenty to walk before me and four-and-twenty to walk behind me , with their horses and clothes and arms , and let all that is upon them and their horses be of stuffs costly and precious exceedingly , such as are not found in kings ' treasuries", "context_len": 103}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 41, "context": "When the eunuchs and chamberlains and captains of the guard saw them , wonder took them and they were breathless for amaze at this sight , the like whereof they had never in their lives seen , and especially at the slave girls , each one of whom would ravish the wit of an anchorite", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 110, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}}}, "displace": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Whoever dares these boots displace Shall meet Bombastes face to face , ''", "context_len": 14}}}, "bereft": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "if the boy had not already been bereft of his senses by the melodrama preceding the burlesque , he must have been transported by her beauty , her grace , her genius", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 74, "context": "Then he embraced Alaeddin and fell to kissing him and said to him , `` Excuse me , O my son , for that I was going [ 623 ] to bereave thee of thy life , through the wickedness of yonder accursed sorcerer who cast thee into this pit ; and indeed , O my son , I was excusable in that which I did with thee , inasmuch as I saw myself bereft of my daughter and mine only one , who is dearer to me than my kingdom , and thou knowest how fathers ' hearts yearn upon their children , more by token that I have but the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 114}}}, "melodrama": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "' Bobby took to carrying about an old horse-pistol which he had found in the lumber-room , and saying 'Oddsbodkins ' on all occasions , and Peggy put on such a general air of melodrama that it seemed scarcely possible for her to speak in plain prose ; Archie was impresario , stage-manager , scene-shifter , dresser , maker-up , and principal actor all combined , while Lilian waxed so enthusiastic that she even sacrificed the feathers out of her last summer 's best hat to adorn the slouched head-gear considered necessary for a due representation of Romeo", "context_len": 98}}}, "preceding": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 44, "context": "Will you tell it to us , Marie-Celeste ? And Marie-Celeste , nothing loath , and willing at last to substantiate her claims in the ears of doubting Albert , rested a hand comfortably on either arm of her chair , and commenced , preceding her narration with the request , You will correct me , wo n't you , if you find I do not tell it right ? to which Her Majesty smilingly acceded , first asking Miss Belmore to hand her a little jewelled miniature case from among the other treasures on the table", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Donald , she said on her second visit , for the one preceding had naturally been limited to the ordinary themes of first acquaintance , I wish you would tell me a little more about yourself", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 88, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "burlesque": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "He was apt to be ashamed of his literary efforts after the first moment , and he shuddered at his brother 's burlesque of the high romantic vein in which most of his neverended beginnings were conceived", "context_len": 37}}}, "transported": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 105, "context": "When the Lady Bedrulbudour saw this , she cried out and said to him , `` What hath this holy anchoress done , that thou burthenest thyself with the sore burden of her blood ? Hast thou no fear of God , that thou dost this and hast slain Fatimeh , who was a holy woman and whose divine gifts were renowned ? '' Quoth he to her , `` I have not slain Fatimeh ; nay , I have slain him who slew her ; for that this is the brother of the accursed Maugrabin enchanter , who took thee and by his sorcery transported the palace with thee to the land of Africa", "context_len": 115}}}, "adorable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "He , indeed , gave her and her sister his heart , but his mind was already gone , rapt from him by the adorable pirate who fought a losing fight with broadswords , two up and two down -- click-click , click-click -- and died all over the deck of the pirate ship in the opening piece", "context_len": 58}}}, "broadswords": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "He , indeed , gave her and her sister his heart , but his mind was already gone , rapt from him by the adorable pirate who fought a losing fight with broadswords , two up and two down -- click-click , click-click -- and died all over the deck of the pirate ship in the opening piece", "context_len": 58}}}, "forecastle": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 81, "context": "Some hours later the captain returned , and upon my repeating to him the yarn which I had spun to the mate he not only very readily consented to my working my passage , but also offered me two excellent suits of clothes , two shirts , two pairs of stockings , a pair of shoes , and a worsted cap in exchange for my leopard-skin , which offer I gladly accepted ; and that night found me domiciled in the forecastle of L'Esperance as one of her crew", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Far forward amid a tangle of rigging and broken spars , three seamen sprawled upon the forecastle head", "context_len": 18}}}, "rigging": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 24, "context": "With the approach of night both fleets withdrew ; but for us there was little rest , as all hands were employed reeving fresh rigging , splicing spars , and plugging shot holes , while our dead were committed to the deep , and the wounded transhipped to one of the smaller vessels", "context_len": 53}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 46, "context": "Her stern gallery was upheld by a row of gilt figures representing that hero of mythology , Master Atlas , of whom my father used to speak ; while over the gallery towered three enormous lanterns , and above everything was a maze of spars and rigging that confused me not a little , though before long I was well acquainted with the names of all of them", "context_len": 68}}}, "merchantmen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "Spring came and went , yet to my great mortification I was not sent to join a ship , though in the interval I engaged in a private venture -- a few gentlemen of Hampshire having fitted out a small vessel to prey upon Dutch merchantmen", "context_len": 46}}}, "dialogue": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 63, "context": "It was exactly as Edna had predicted ; Miss Eloise entered into the plan with the greatest eagerness , and when Ben had opened up his plot to her and had showed her how he had planned the scenes she said she would take a few minutes to think it over and then she thought she could give him some of the needed dialogue , and before they left Ben had taken down as much as was necessary for this first time , promising to come back for the rest", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I 'm no good at all when it comes to dialogue , '' he told them", "context_len": 17}}}, "meaninglessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Afterwards , the boy remembered nothing of the story , but a scrap of the dialogue meaninglessly remained with him ; and when the pirate captain appeared with his bloody crew and said , hoarsely , `` Let us go below and get some brandy ! '' the boy would have bartered all his hopes of bliss to have been that abandoned ruffian", "context_len": 63}}}, "bloody": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 8, "context": "Did Edric remember the place , and the bloody event which only he and one other present connected with the spot ? -- for Edmund had been far away , and the matter had been hushed up , as far as was possible , by all the power and influence Ethelred could exert in his favourite 's cause , or rather his own , for he , the royal villain , shared the ill-gotten spoil", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 73, "context": "Her punishment was that she `` should return to the Gaol from whence she came , and there remain until Saturday next between the hours of Eleven and Twelve of the Clock in the forenoon , at which time she was to be brought to the public Whipping-post , and there receive Twenty Lashes with a Cat-of-Nine-Tails from the hands of the Common Beadle on her naked back till the same shall be bloody , and then return to the said Gaol and remain until her fees be paid ! ''", "context_len": 91}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "One of them bloody Aichi float planes has opened up on us", "context_len": 12}}}, "bartered": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` [ 590 ] Quoth he , `` How came this about ? '' So she acquainted him with the whole matter from first to last , telling him how they had bartered the old lamp for a new one ; `` and next morning , '' added she , `` we found ourselves in this country and he who had cozened me and changed the lamp told me that he had wroughten these tricks upon us of the might of his magic , by means of the lamp and that he is a Maugrabin from Africa [ 591 ] and that we are now in his native land", "context_len": 109}}}, "villain": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 69, "context": "Did Edric remember the place , and the bloody event which only he and one other present connected with the spot ? -- for Edmund had been far away , and the matter had been hushed up , as far as was possible , by all the power and influence Ethelred could exert in his favourite 's cause , or rather his own , for he , the royal villain , shared the ill-gotten spoil", "context_len": 75}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Oh , you villain ! Aint it enough to go robbin ' orchards and melon patches , and farmers ' wagons market-days , but you must be fighting and knocking down babies like these to get what 's not your own ? If you do n't see the inside of the county jail for this , my name 's not Susan Jones", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I \u2019 ll wager \u2019 tis none other than that villain Increase Joyce", "context_len": 14}}}, "glutted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 76, "context": "None of our friends or kindred were on board the fleet ; and we traders have rather reason to rejoice , for , in the first place , so severe a lesson will keep the corsairs in their ports for a long time ; and in the second , had the fleet succeeded according to general expectation , so great a store of European goods would have been brought home that the market would have been glutted , and the goods in our storehouses would have lost all their value", "context_len": 90}}}, "midnight": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 70, "context": "But as he looked at the individual whom he had mistaken for a deer he felt a second tremor of excitement , for , as he had a glimpse of his face Bart was made aware that the man was none other than the one about whom there was such a mystery -- the man who had sneaked into the schoolhouse the night the diamond bracelet was stolen -- the midnight visitor at the camp , perhaps", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "About midnight the bright moonlight streaming in through the window woke Master Rollo , who , having had a refreshing nap , was now very wide awake , and ready for anything", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 61, "context": "The wind was fair , but light , and they steered for the lone spruce on Birch Point , and , passing it , kept on to the north-east , having resolved to run the shore along , keeping a bright lookout for the high rock with the spruce on its summit , till they judged by the tide it was midnight , when , if they could not find the place , they would go ashore and camp , continuing their search in the morning", "context_len": 86}}}, "languished": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "On his first night my boy saw `` The Beacon of Death , '' `` Bombastes Furioso , '' and `` Black-eyed Susan , '' and he never afterwards saw less than three plays each night , and he never missed a night , as long as the theatre languished in the unfriendly air of that mainly Calvinistic community , where the theatre was regarded by most good people as the eighth of the seven deadly sins", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "' I am afraid the house-keeping languished while Lilian painted scenery on large sheets of blue grocery paper", "context_len": 18}}}, "eighth": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 71, "context": "On his first night my boy saw `` The Beacon of Death , '' `` Bombastes Furioso , '' and `` Black-eyed Susan , '' and he never afterwards saw less than three plays each night , and he never missed a night , as long as the theatre languished in the unfriendly air of that mainly Calvinistic community , where the theatre was regarded by most good people as the eighth of the seven deadly sins", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 74, "context": "It had been part of the plan to attend the five-o'clock service in Christ Church Cathedral ; and after spending a half hour or so in wandering through the cloisters and gaining something of an idea of the college as a whole , they went early into the cathedral , that they might also stroll for a while through the beautiful old church whose history dates as far back as the middle of the eighth century", "context_len": 76}}}, "blotted": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}}}, "consumed": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 81, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}}}, "effulgent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The whole day long he dwelt in a dream of it that blotted out , or rather consumed with more effulgent brightness , all the other day-dreams he had dreamed before , and his heart almost burst with longing to be a villain like those villains on the stage , to have a moustache -- a black moustache -- such as they wore at a time when every one off the stage was clean shaven , and somehow to end bloodily , murderously , as became a villain", "context_len": 88}}}, "moustache": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 63, "context": "Gervaise thought the plan an excellent one ; and he was soon transformed , Muley shaving that part of the hair that would have shown below the turban , and then staining him a deep brownish black , from the waist upwards , together with his feet and his legs up to his knee , and darkening his eyebrows , eyelashes , and moustache", "context_len": 64}}}, "shaven": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 74, "context": "The whole day long he dwelt in a dream of it that blotted out , or rather consumed with more effulgent brightness , all the other day-dreams he had dreamed before , and his heart almost burst with longing to be a villain like those villains on the stage , to have a moustache -- a black moustache -- such as they wore at a time when every one off the stage was clean shaven , and somehow to end bloodily , murderously , as became a villain", "context_len": 88}}}, "bloodily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 80, "context": "The whole day long he dwelt in a dream of it that blotted out , or rather consumed with more effulgent brightness , all the other day-dreams he had dreamed before , and his heart almost burst with longing to be a villain like those villains on the stage , to have a moustache -- a black moustache -- such as they wore at a time when every one off the stage was clean shaven , and somehow to end bloodily , murderously , as became a villain", "context_len": 88}}}, "murderously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 82, "context": "The whole day long he dwelt in a dream of it that blotted out , or rather consumed with more effulgent brightness , all the other day-dreams he had dreamed before , and his heart almost burst with longing to be a villain like those villains on the stage , to have a moustache -- a black moustache -- such as they wore at a time when every one off the stage was clean shaven , and somehow to end bloodily , murderously , as became a villain", "context_len": 88}}}, "portray": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "I dare say this was not quite a wholesome frame of mind for a boy of ten years ; but I do not defend it ; I only portray it", "context_len": 30}}}, "dwell": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` So it was agreed that her spirit should dwell as a prairie dog , and before long out sprang one from a reeking cauldron of herbs , and they took it to the holes of the prairie dogs and left it there , placing beside it a terrible serpent , that all others might be afraid to approach it , and an owl at the door , as a sentinel that would stand looking patiently for an enemy both night and day , and never breathe to the gossips around her the tale of the princess that was prisoned within", "context_len": 101}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` So [ 649 ] saying , she arose and carrying the sorcerer to the place which she had appointed him wherein to abide , said to him , `` O my lady Fatimeh , here shalt thou dwell ; this pavilion is in thy name and thou shalt abide therein in all quiet and ease of privacy", "context_len": 58}}}, "villainy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Bart was shocked at this exhibition of cold-blooded villainy on the part of a representative member of the community , although he had never had much use for the pompous , domineering old tyrant , who now led the way through the silent Streets of Pleasantville as meek as a lamb", "context_len": 51}}}, "reformed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "Being the boy he was , he was destined somehow to dwell half the time in a world of dreamery ; and I have tried to express how , when he had once got enough of villainy , he reformed his ideals and rather liked virtue", "context_len": 46}}}, "riot": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "How the cheers broke forth then ! What a riot of color from the grandstands ! How the flags , ribbons and banners waved ! How the gay youths and grave men yelled themselves hoarse ! How the girls ' shrill voices sounded over the field !", "context_len": 47}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Beyond the hedge lies a pasture meadow , where a flock of sheep are grazing , and encircling the meadow another hedge , less closely clipped , and so making bold to riot here and there in a snowy wealth of hawthorn blossom , A fine Alderney cow , with coat as well cared for as the gray mare 's in the stable , is also enjoying the sweet grass of the meadow , and the shining milk , pans ranged beneath the kitchen window bear witness to the generous service she renders", "context_len": 93}}}, "perpetrated": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 62, "context": "In fact , it is probable that Alexander 's slaughter of the Persian army at Arbela , and subsequent spoliation of Susa , constitute , taken together , the most gigantic case of murder and robbery which was ever committed by man ; so that , in performing these deeds , the great hero attained at last to the glory of having perpetrated the grandest and most imposing of all human crimes", "context_len": 72}}}, "manager": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 40, "context": "As told in the first volume of this series , entitled `` The Rival Pitchers , '' Tom had no sooner reached Randall than he incurred the enmity of Fred Langridge , a rich youth from Chicago , who was manager of the 'varsity ball nine , and also its pitcher", "context_len": 51}}}, "divine": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 24, "context": "Their principal mythic power is the `` Master of Life '' or `` Holder of Breath , '' in Creek Is\u00e1kita imm\u00edssi , a divine being , which is as thoroughly North American as Jahve , an ancient sun- and thunder-god , is of Semitic , and Dyaus , Zeus , Jupiter , the Sky-god , is of Aryan origin", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "Charlie lost no time putting into execution Captain Rhines \u2019 s directions in respect to the pig , having first enjoined upon them the greatest secrecy , not even permitting them to tell Ben and Sally of his plots and suspicions , lest Joe , who was very quick of perception , should divine what was in store for him", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 58, "context": "When the Lady Bedrulbudour saw this , she cried out and said to him , `` What hath this holy anchoress done , that thou burthenest thyself with the sore burden of her blood ? Hast thou no fear of God , that thou dost this and hast slain Fatimeh , who was a holy woman and whose divine gifts were renowned ? '' Quoth he to her , `` I have not slain Fatimeh ; nay , I have slain him who slew her ; for that this is the brother of the accursed Maugrabin enchanter , who took thee and by his sorcery transported the palace with thee to the land of Africa", "context_len": 115}}}, "symbolical": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "They were on easy terms with the roller-boy , the wretch who shot turtle-doves with no regard for their symbolical character , and they joked with him , in a light give-and-take that smote my boy with an anguish of envy", "context_len": 41}}}, "shrank": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then the boys and girls shrank away , for had they not heard of her pranks , and might they not expect toads and frogs to come out of their mouths , and other horrors to happen if Miss Irene had anything to do with them ? They therefore began to decline Irene 's cake , and to say , `` No , thank you , miss , '' in a very timid voice when delicious-looking pieces of bread-and-butter and jam were offered to them", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` No , '' she said , severely , as poor frightened Maggie shrank before the glance of her eye , `` you will not go out to-day , nor do you deserve it", "context_len": 34}}}, "drama": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Perhaps it was because his soul was so soaked , and , as it were , water-logged with the drama , that it could only drift sluggishly in that welter of emotions , and make for no point , no port , where it could recover itself and direct its powers again", "context_len": 52}}}, "welter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Perhaps it was because his soul was so soaked , and , as it were , water-logged with the drama , that it could only drift sluggishly in that welter of emotions , and make for no point , no port , where it could recover itself and direct its powers again", "context_len": 52}}}, "port": {"freq": 136, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 127, "word_index": 97, "context": "This , doubtless , may be so , Sir Gervaise , the doge said , with a slight smile , but it is to the head that plans , rather than to the hand that strikes , that such success as you have achieved is due ; and the credit of this night attack is , as the cavalier Caretto tells me , wholly yours , for until you issued your final orders it seemed to him , and to the two good knights his companions , that there was naught to do but to remain in port and watch this corsair fleet sail away to carry out its work of destruction", "context_len": 112}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "The passengers were forbidden to climb to the bridge , and they seldom thought of the quiet men in blue who , two at a time , were always pacing that canvas-screened pathway to guide the Roanoke to port", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "The small sally port , as it would have been called in later times , was opened , and two men were in each case posted together all round the building , under cover of trees , at convenient distances", "context_len": 40}}}, "impassioned": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The historical romance which he had begun to write before the impassioned days of the theatre seems to have been lost sight of at this time , though it was an enterprise that he was so confident of carrying forward that he told all his family and friends about it , and even put down the opening passages of it on paper which he cut in large quantity , and ruled himself , so as to have it exactly suitable", "context_len": 80}}}, "quantity": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 71, "context": "Mark Penelly had gone over to the other side of the lugger while the conversation was going on , and he did not face the man he looked upon as his rival ; while Harry , unnoticed by the busy fishers as he swam round , went on , touching the sides of the lugger as he lightly swam , but only the next moment to find himself entangled in a quantity of the thin mackerel net , which seemed somehow to descend upon him like a cloud , and before he could realise the fact he was under water , hopelessly fettered by the net , and feeling that if he could not extricate himself directly he should be a dead man", "context_len": 123}}}, "suitable": {"freq": 57, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 79, "context": "The historical romance which he had begun to write before the impassioned days of the theatre seems to have been lost sight of at this time , though it was an enterprise that he was so confident of carrying forward that he told all his family and friends about it , and even put down the opening passages of it on paper which he cut in large quantity , and ruled himself , so as to have it exactly suitable", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "We have bread brought in regularly ; for I did not think the heavy , solid German home-made bread suitable for India", "context_len": 22}}}, "el": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` the thirsty one ( es szadi ) and the goer-forth by day or in the morning '' ( el ghadi ) ; but this is most probably a mistranscription for the common phrase es sari ( the goer by night ) wa ' l ghadi , often used in the sense of `` comers and goers '' simply", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "Kanoun is the Syrian name of two winter months , December ( Kanoun el awwal or first ) and January ( Kanoun eth thani or second )", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 28, "context": "One of his river-faring uncles was visiting with his family at the boy 's home when he laid out the scheme of his great fiction of `` Hamet el Zegri , '' and the kindly young aunt took an interest in it which he poorly rewarded a few months later , when she asked how the story was getting on , and he tried to ignore the whole matter , and showed such mortification at the mention of it that the poor lady was quite bewildered", "context_len": 86}}}, "nephew": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 79, "word_index": 60, "context": "The story , as I have said , was imagined from events in Irving 's history of the `` Conquest of Granada , '' a book which the boy loved hardly less than the monkish legends of `` Gesta Romanorum , '' and it concerned the rival fortunes of Hamet el Zegri and Boabdil el Chico , the uncle and nephew who vied with each other for the crumbling throne of the Moorish kingdom ; but I have not the least notion how it all ended", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 23, "context": "If he had asked to be a brother , I should have welcomed him ; but I really could not have as a nephew one to whom we look up as a head-pastor , a kind of bishop ! I don \u2019 t think that my nephews should be more than forty years old", "context_len": 54}}}, "vied": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "The story , as I have said , was imagined from events in Irving 's history of the `` Conquest of Granada , '' a book which the boy loved hardly less than the monkish legends of `` Gesta Romanorum , '' and it concerned the rival fortunes of Hamet el Zegri and Boabdil el Chico , the uncle and nephew who vied with each other for the crumbling throne of the Moorish kingdom ; but I have not the least notion how it all ended", "context_len": 86}}}, "crumbling": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 68, "context": "The story , as I have said , was imagined from events in Irving 's history of the `` Conquest of Granada , '' a book which the boy loved hardly less than the monkish legends of `` Gesta Romanorum , '' and it concerned the rival fortunes of Hamet el Zegri and Boabdil el Chico , the uncle and nephew who vied with each other for the crumbling throne of the Moorish kingdom ; but I have not the least notion how it all ended", "context_len": 86}}}, "throne": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 58, "context": "They had stories to tell him about the famous hanging gardens , which were artificially constructed in the most magnificent manner , on arches raised high in the air ; and about a vine made of gold , with all sorts of precious stones upon it instead of fruit , which was wrought as an ornament over the throne on which the King of Persia often gave audience ; of the splendid palaces and vast cities of the Persians ; and the banquets , and f\u00eates , and magnificent entertainments and celebrations which they used to have there", "context_len": 98}}}, "cumbered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "They were so many that they cumbered the house , and were trodden under foot ; and sometimes they brought him to open shame , as when his brother picked one of them up , and began to read it out loud with affected admiration", "context_len": 45}}}, "beginnings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "He was apt to be ashamed of his literary efforts after the first moment , and he shuddered at his brother 's burlesque of the high romantic vein in which most of his neverended beginnings were conceived", "context_len": 37}}}, "fiction": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 66, "context": "of the Cabinet des Fees ( printed in 1793 , though antedated 1788 and 1789 ) do not form the first edition of Chavis and Cazotte 's so-called Sequel , which was in 1793 added , by way of supplement , to the Cabinet des Fees , having been first published in 1788 ( two years after the completion-in thirty-seven volumes-of that great storehouse of supernatural fiction ) under the title of `` Les Veillees Persanes '' or `` Les Veillees du Sultan Schahriar avec la Sultane Scheherazade , histoires incroyables , amusantes et morales , traduites par M", "context_len": 99}}}, "aunt": {"freq": 138, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 136, "word_index": 78, "context": "not to let our left hand , know that which is done by our right hand ; but are we not also commanded so to let our light shine before men that they may see our good works and glorify our Father in heaven ? And can we do so , or truly show our love to him , if we hide the services rendered for his sake behind a mask of coldness and reserve ? My dear aunt , for his sake , for your own , for the sake of the affection and confidence which I wish my children to feel for you , and which I believe you wish to gain , let me satisfy them that it was really you who did this thing", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "One afternoon , their uncle and aunt went out to visit some friends , giving the boys permission to amuse themselves out of doors as long as they pleased", "context_len": 29}}}, "rewarded": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 45, "context": "One of his river-faring uncles was visiting with his family at the boy 's home when he laid out the scheme of his great fiction of `` Hamet el Zegri , '' and the kindly young aunt took an interest in it which he poorly rewarded a few months later , when she asked how the story was getting on , and he tried to ignore the whole matter , and showed such mortification at the mention of it that the poor lady was quite bewildered", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "At the gymnasium I made a close inspection of the pilfered lockers , being there to obtain a clew of some sort , a desire which was amply rewarded", "context_len": 29}}}, "usefulness": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Stirling was resting easily , she reported , in very little pain or discomfort , but his career of usefulness and work was over -- the doctors expressed an opinion that he would never regain his eyesight", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 41, "context": "I dare say that if a crowd of the Young People 's readers , from eight to eleven years old , got together , they would choose the best boy among them to lead them on in works of kindness and usefulness ; but I am very sorry to say that in the Boy 's Town such a crowd of boys would have followed the lead of the worst boy as far as they dared", "context_len": 75}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas should sow well the grain , Dark babies should fondle , dark women should train , And Bibis and Begums at times entertain ; Should smile and should soothe , but not flatter or feign ; And to usefulness thus they may hope to attain", "context_len": 49}}}, "gaudy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "My boy would as soon have thought of his father 's doing a wrong thing as of his brother 's doing it ; and his brother was a calm light of common-sense , of justice , of truth , while he was a fantastic flicker of gaudy purposes which he wished to make shine before men in their fulfilment", "context_len": 59}}}, "forage": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Yet he was a boy among boys , too ; he loved to swim , to skate , to fish , to forage , and passionately , above all , he loved to hunt ; but in everything he held himself in check , that he might hold the younger boys in check ; and my boy often repaid his conscientious vigilance with hard words and hard names , such as embitter even the most self-forgiving memories", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "'All right , ' cried Peggy , rushing off with enthusiasm to rouse Lilian from the piano and forage in the pantry for a supply of jam and butter", "context_len": 29}}}, "repaid": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 50, "context": "What would be thought of an English soldier who , on being ordered to some lonely and difficult post , were , instead of going at once , to begin to calculate whether it were worth while , -- whether the cost and trouble of his going would be sufficiently repaid by results ? Yet such is the spirit in which certain soldiers of the Cross -- somewhat faithless soldiers , surely ! -- are disposed to regard this great Marching Order of our Captain and King", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "You have beautiful nails , child , said Babette , the instant she took Marie-Celeste 's extended hands in hers ; and this compliment from so high and experienced an authority made Marie-Celeste at once feel repaid for all the dainty care her mother had always insisted upon", "context_len": 48}}}, "conscientious": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 60, "context": "Yet he was a boy among boys , too ; he loved to swim , to skate , to fish , to forage , and passionately , above all , he loved to hunt ; but in everything he held himself in check , that he might hold the younger boys in check ; and my boy often repaid his conscientious vigilance with hard words and hard names , such as embitter even the most self-forgiving memories", "context_len": 77}}}, "embitter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 71, "context": "Yet he was a boy among boys , too ; he loved to swim , to skate , to fish , to forage , and passionately , above all , he loved to hunt ; but in everything he held himself in check , that he might hold the younger boys in check ; and my boy often repaid his conscientious vigilance with hard words and hard names , such as embitter even the most self-forgiving memories", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "But affection can not excuse what is unworthy ; and Lola 's thoughts ran vaguely with a distrust which did something to embitter the wholesome tides of life", "context_len": 28}}}, "council": {"freq": 100, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 98, "word_index": 87, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The term fire evidently refers to council fires , which had to be kindled ceremonially by the friction of two pieces of wood", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "A stormy scene had meanwhile taken place in an interior chamber of the palace of the bishop , which had been metamorphosed into a council chamber for the king", "context_len": 29}}}, "rein": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "If he had known just what Raca meant , he might have called him Raca , for he was not so much afraid of the council ; but , as it was , his brother escaped that insult , and held through all a rein upon him , and governed him through his scruples as well as his fears", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 26, "context": "Then , instead of attempting to restrain him , and worrying and checking him by useless efforts to hold him in , he gave him the rein freely , and animated and encouraged him with his voice , so that the horse flew across the plains at the top of his speed , the king and the courtiers looking on , at first with fear and trembling , but soon afterward with feelings of the greatest admiration and pleasure", "context_len": 79}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Twice these coursers of the plains passed so near the camp that they exchanged greetings with the emigrants but neither did more than rein his pony down to a walk", "context_len": 30}}}, "inventions": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "Every boy who knows the lure of exploring and who loves to rig up huts and caves and tree-houses to fortify himself against imaginary enemies will enjoy these books , for they give a vivid chronicle of the doings and inventions of a group of boys who are shipwrecked and have to make themselves snug and safe in tropical islands where the dangers are too real for play", "context_len": 68}}}, "enterprises": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "A fool might -- I beg pardon -- were not all our plans known beforehand ? Did not all our enterprises fail ? Were not all our ambushes anticipated ? Did we not fall into all theirs ? If they had had a prophet like Elisha , who told the king of Israel all Benhadad said in his council chamber , they could n't have managed better", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "These deities having been engaged in the same enterprises themselves , and animated with the same hopes and fears , the soldiers imagined that the semi-human divinities invoked by them would take an interest in their dangers , and rejoice is their success", "context_len": 43}}}, "nothingness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "Alfgar gazed with reverence , as well as love , upon the calm features from which the expression of pain had wholly passed ; the light of the fire , mingling strangely with that of the rising full moon , illumined them in this their first day of nothingness , for the spirit which had lived and dwelt in the tabernacle of clay had fled", "context_len": 65}}}, "awaits": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "His brother was full of inventions and enterprises beyond most other boys , and his undertakings came to the same end of nothingness that awaits all boyish endeavor", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The bottomless pit awaits ye , '' threatened Sandy , and then , one at a time , the four were carefully lowered over the side of the wagon , down into some depths , as they supposed , but in reality only a short distance , so strangely are distances rendered when one is blindfolded", "context_len": 56}}}, "endeavor": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "He had been exasperated to a great degree by Flagg 's endeavor to drug him on the night of the expedition to Rice 's , and that with good reason ; and now his suspicions , nay , more than suspicions aroused that he was trying to make it appear that he , Seabrooke , had wrongfully kept Percy 's money and then pretended that the latter had taken it from him by stealth , enraged him beyond bounds", "context_len": 79}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "This island divided the current of the stream , and Alexander , in attempting to attack it , found that it would be best to endeavor to effect a landing upon the upper point of it", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "we throve together , and have ever mutually endeavor \u2019 d to make each other happy", "context_len": 16}}}, "fireworks": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 50, "context": "He had noticed a little earlier his big brother , Ira , a loutish , overgrown fellow who had gone around with his hands in his pockets sneering at the innocent fun the smaller boys were indulging in , and bragging about his own especial Fourth of July supply of fireworks which were to come from some mysterious source not clearly defined", "context_len": 62}}}, "contract": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 49, "context": "Jack Borden , the junior partner in a fairly successful law firm , his wife an averagely nice , sensible body , Miss Florence , her husband 's sister , a bright girl of three and twenty , whose lover was in South America on a five years ' contract , with one year yet to serve", "context_len": 57}}}, "cemetery": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "Alexander 's was a simple burial procession , going from the palace where he died to the proper cemetery -- a march of a thousand miles , it is true , but all within his own dominions The greatness of it resulted simply from the magnitude of the scale on which every thing pertaining to the mighty here was performed , for it was nothing but a simple passage from the dwelling to the burial-ground on his own estates , after all", "context_len": 82}}}, "blasting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "He intended to make fireworks and sell them ; he meant to raise silk-worms ; he prepared to take the contract of clearing the new cemetery grounds of stumps by blasting them out with gunpowder", "context_len": 35}}}, "gunpowder": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 65, "context": "'Of course he will ! I wonder you can ask such a question ! Heaven would n't be heaven unless we found Rollo there ! We 'll wrap his body in the Union Jack , and pick all our best flowers to strew round him ; and you might fire off your old pistol over his grave , if Father will let you have any gunpowder -- a parting salute , you know , like they do for officers , ' said Peggy , cheering up a little at the thought of arranging the obsequies", "context_len": 95}}}, "selling": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 24, "context": "Some of the grit of the old Crusaders was left in his blood , and instead of taking his solicitor 's advice , and selling the place for what it would fetch , he resolved to farm the land himself , and by using every care and economy to free the property , and raise it to its former level in the county", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "He ceased not to go daily to the market of the jewellers and to clap up acquaintance with the folk , making friends with them and questioning them of buying and selling and giving and taking and dear and cheap , till , one day of the days , he arose in the morning and donning his clothes , went forth , intending , as of wont , for the jewellers ' market ; but , as he went , he heard the crier proclaiming aloud on this wise , `` By commandment of the Lord of Beneficence , the king of the age and monarch of the time and the tide , let all the folk shut their shops and stores and enter their houses , for", "context_len": 128}}}, "overflowed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 47, "context": "I shall tell his aunt so the next time I see her , and I do n't care who hears me ! ' she added , catching sight of a grinning face on the veranda , for Archie had stolen out to see the fun , and overflowed in such gurgles of delight at this sally that Lilian trembled for the result", "context_len": 62}}}, "cellars": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "They did not do this before it had overflowed the street , and run into the cellars of the nearest houses", "context_len": 21}}}, "rafts": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 25, "context": "The water stood two feet deep in the kitchen of my boy 's house , and the yard was flooded so that the boys made rafts and navigated it for a whole day", "context_len": 33}}}, "navigated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "The water stood two feet deep in the kitchen of my boy 's house , and the yard was flooded so that the boys made rafts and navigated it for a whole day", "context_len": 33}}}, "nankeen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "He belonged to a military company of big boys that had real wooden guns , such as the little boys never could get , and silk oil-cloth caps , and nankeen roundabouts , and white pantaloons with black stripes down the legs ; and once they marched out to a boy 's that had a father that had a farm , and he gave them all a free dinner in an arbor before the house ; bread and butter , and apple-butter , and molasses and pound cake , and peaches and apples ; it was splendid", "context_len": 97}}}, "arbor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 71, "context": "He belonged to a military company of big boys that had real wooden guns , such as the little boys never could get , and silk oil-cloth caps , and nankeen roundabouts , and white pantaloons with black stripes down the legs ; and once they marched out to a boy 's that had a father that had a farm , and he gave them all a free dinner in an arbor before the house ; bread and butter , and apple-butter , and molasses and pound cake , and peaches and apples ; it was splendid", "context_len": 97}}}, "sod": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 81, "context": "Guy 's only comfort was , and , perhaps , too , that of the poor baby 's father and mother , that he could not be quite alone , even when they left him , for God would watch over him ; and he could not but rejoice that they had not been forced to leave him in the shifting sands of the desert , but that a green tree bent over him , and grass would spring above the sod in which he lay", "context_len": 86}}}, "stormed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 41, "context": "They took the pieces of sod , and built them up into the walls of a fort about fifteen feet square ; they intended to build them higher than their heads , but they got so eager to have the works stormed that they could not wait , and they commenced having the battle when they had the walls only breast high", "context_len": 62}}}, "sods": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "It was a splendid gun , but it would not stand cocked long , and he was resting it on the wall of the fort , ready to fire when the storming-party came on , throwing sods and yelling and holloing ; and all at once his gun went off , and a cow that was grazing broadside to the fort gave a frightened bellow , and put up her tail , and started for home", "context_len": 76}}}, "grazing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 14, "context": "Beyond the hedge lies a pasture meadow , where a flock of sheep are grazing , and encircling the meadow another hedge , less closely clipped , and so making bold to riot here and there in a snowy wealth of hawthorn blossom , A fine Alderney cow , with coat as well cared for as the gray mare 's in the stable , is also enjoying the sweet grass of the meadow , and the shining milk , pans ranged beneath the kitchen window bear witness to the generous service she renders", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "To the northward country was undulating or level , mostly covered with the billowy , succulent lush grass , which makes the region one of the finest grazing grounds in the world", "context_len": 32}}}, "miller": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 22, "context": "He came down feet foremost , however , and they sauntered off along the road to the old water-mill , where the miller 's man was slinging a sack of flour on to a patient donkey who stood , with drooping ears , eyeing the burden which he must carry up far into the mountains , while his mistress , a little black-eyed Welshwoman , poured forth a torrent of gossip in high-pitched tones", "context_len": 74}}}, "scow": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Ben and Charlie went off in the large canoe , and caught and cured fish to last them through the winter , and , getting a scow , brought on hay enough to winter their stock", "context_len": 36}}}, "disputing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Aylmer the less , chirruping away as she spoke , and dancing up to a neighboring looking-glass to take a side view of her own dress ; `` we are not disputing that point", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Fred and the colonel were very fond of joking and sparring in this fashion , but Bessie always looked very sober while it was going on ; for she could not bear anything that sounded like disputing , even in play ; and perhaps she was about right", "context_len": 48}}}, "halfway": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "Once the fellows were disputing whether you could jump halfway across a narrow stream , and then jump back , without touching your feet to the other shore", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "As soon as darkness came on , a large body of slaves were marched down from the town , and , under the direction of the knights , laboured all night at the mound , removing great quantities of the fallen stones and rubbish in a line halfway up it , and piling them above so as to form a scarp across the mound that would need ladders to ascend", "context_len": 70}}}, "preserving": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 39, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "sealing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "My boy 's brother had a scheme for preserving ripe fruit , by sealing it up in a stone jug and burying the jug in the ground , and not digging it up till Christmas", "context_len": 35}}}, "burying": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 78, "context": "coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to find the poor creature ; so that , his hair being wet , he was a sight to behold when Harry pulled him out , covered with mud from head to foot , and had to be sent behind in disgrace", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` And I could n't bear , sir , '' he said , `` that the old folks should n't have a decent burying", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "BURYING THE HATCHET or the Young Brave of the Delawares By Rev", "context_len": 12}}}, "bakery": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "In answer to my question he told me that I was in Wareham , and a few minutes afterwards I was sitting in a bakery , eagerly devouring a half-loaf and a cup of milk that a kindly baker provided for me", "context_len": 42}}}, "brewery": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "There was a fat boy learning the trade there , and they called him the dough-baby , because he looked so white and soft ; and the boy whose father had a mill said that down at the German brewery they had a Dutch boy that they were teaching to drink beer , so they could tell how much beer a person could drink if he was taken early ; but perhaps this was not true", "context_len": 76}}}, "beer": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 72, "context": "There was , first , a broiled fish , fresh from the river , with boiled yams ; then a few roast plantains -- not more than a dozen , I think ; then the roast rib of a cow ; a few handfuls of boiled rice ; and -- yes , I think that was all , except a bowl of jaro ' -- the latter being a kind of native beer", "context_len": 73}}}, "associated": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 86, "context": "legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all service time , and if she did not behave properly it might come down in great wrath and devour her", "context_len": 128}}}, "types": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "He could no more remember learning to set type than he could remember learning to read ; and in after-life he could not come within smell of the ink , the dusty types , the humid paper , of a printing-office without that tender swelling of the heart which so fondly responds to any memory-bearing perfume : his youth , his boyhood , almost his infancy came back to him in it", "context_len": 72}}}, "jealousy": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 54, "context": "The only person who was not won over by Archie 's friendly ways and frank American manners was Joe , who gloomily prophesied broken necks and kindred evils as the result of the children 's association with 'the young master from furrin parts ' ; but I fear there was a good deal of jealousy in this , for poor Joe had been a hero to the children in his modest way , and it was hard to find himself suddenly supplanted in their affections , especially by a rival with whom it was quite impossible to compete", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "As Benjamin \u2019 s skill began to surpass his own , his attitude changed to resentment and jealousy", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "It was jealousy which made you so unhappy", "context_len": 8}}}, "steadfast": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "Something was surely amiss ; but the girl as yet fully realized only one fact -- that tia , always so steadfast and strong and cheerful , had gone hastily from the room in the agitation of one who struggled with unaccustomed tears", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Oh , may our steadfast courage so In danger \u2019 s hour be seen , Sir ; And let the tide flow , And let the world go , We ll be true to our Faith and our Queen , Sir ! \u2019", "context_len": 43}}}, "lofty": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 38, "context": "in the face of the carelessness with which the text treats kushk as synonymous now with keszr or teyyareh and now with liwan or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 59, "context": "In places it was eight feet deep , with sand-bags on either side in addition , In others , in marshy spots , where the high-explosive shells had spitefully disturbed the tranquillity of meandering streams and carried the sluggish water to overflow and swamp the surrounding ground , the `` trench '' was above normal ground-level , with a lofty and broad wall of sand-bags to right and left", "context_len": 69}}}, "millionaires": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "He was one of those boys who grow into the men who seem commoner in America than elsewhere , and who succeed far beyond our millionaires and statesmen in realizing the ideal of America in their nobly simple lives", "context_len": 39}}}, "statesmen": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "He was one of those boys who grow into the men who seem commoner in America than elsewhere , and who succeed far beyond our millionaires and statesmen in realizing the ideal of America in their nobly simple lives", "context_len": 39}}}, "nobly": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 64, "context": "Here , in the name of the Republic , Battista Fragoso announced to him that , by the unanimous decision of the council , he had been elected a noble of Genoa ; an honour , he added , on only one or two previous occasions in the history of the Republic bestowed upon any but of princely rank , but which he had nobly earned by the great service he had rendered to the State", "context_len": 76}}}, "schoolmates": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 9, "context": "Seizing the first opportunity of slipping away from his schoolmates , he rushed upstairs to the dormitory with the design of throwing the note under Seabrooke 's bed or bureau , where it might be supposed to have fallen ; but seeing the trunk standing there ready packed , the impulse had taken him to put it in that , and without reflecting -- perhaps hardly caring -- that this would place Seabrooke in a still more embarrassing position , he thrust the note within , as Charlie Henderson saw , and fled from the room", "context_len": 96}}}, "closest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "When maidens of different antecedents , rank , age , temperament , and -- in minor matters -- opinions , are brought together in closest proximity , in a climate which tries the temper , there is at least a possibility of some slight rubs , which without the soothing ointment brought by the Peacemaker may even develop into sores", "context_len": 60}}}, "imaginings": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "She had lived such a life of imaginings that she was quite equal to accepting any wonderful thing that happened , and almost to cease , in a short time , to find it bewildering", "context_len": 35}}}, "passive": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 88, "context": "`` Beside these stones , which were kept constantly hot , they made Mahdrusa sit down , and while she still wept , her tears fell upon the stones , and a great vapor arose , which the sorcerers condensed upon clay vessels into drops of water as pure as crystal , and with them and the herbs that hung around , made a decoction so powerful that when they had forced Mahdrusa to drink it , she lost all power and reason , and her spirit lay passive in the hands of her tormentors", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "The verb forms a considerable number of tenses and incorporates the prefixed object-pronoun , the interrogative and the negative particle ; it has a form for the passive and one for the reflective voice", "context_len": 34}}}, "belated": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "He lived in a belated log-cabin that stood in the edge of a corn-field on the river-bank , and he seemed , one day when my boy went to find him there , to have a mother , who smoked a cob-pipe , and two or three large sisters who hulked about in the one dim , low room", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "His bayonet , darting above the other 's belated guard , caught the Hun fairly in the lower part of his chest", "context_len": 22}}}, "toleration": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Good Parson Perrient had painted Providence plantation as a sort of paradise , where the liberty and toleration dreamt of by a few in England were the law for all ; but was that refuge open to him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "void": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "Fortunately , the sun 's rays came down hot and bright , and the rock grew warmer , so that by degrees the terribly void look began to leave Mark Penelly 's face , and at last , when Harry held out his hand , saying , `` Do you feel better ? '' Mark Penelly caught it in both of his , clung to it , and , turning half over on his face , laid his forehead against it , and , forgetting his years of manhood , lay there in his weakness , and sobbed and cried like a child", "context_len": 103}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "As for me , I rather hope Stone does come out all right , for if he stays in the school he may play football , and I reckon a stocky chap like him will just about fill an aching void in the right wing of the line", "context_len": 48}}}, "socially": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "As an instance of Fanny \u2019 s peculiar gentleness , it is told that one Sunday , when she saw a man trying to sell things , she went up and remonstrated with him , speaking very seriously , but in so mild and courteous a manner , so entirely as she would have spoken to one who was socially on her own level , that he was utterly unable to take offence", "context_len": 73}}}, "morally": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "This would not be a sign that they were morally any worse than the boys who read Harper 's Young People , and who would every one die rather than do such a cruel thing , but that they had not really thought much about it", "context_len": 46}}}, "listed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "With the falling tide the Prince Royal listed heavily to starboard , so that her guns were for the most part unworkable , and her great sides were exposed an easy target for the enemy", "context_len": 35}}}, "foraged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "He never had any plans himself , and never any will but to go in swimming ; he neither hunted nor foraged ; he did not even fish ; and I suppose that money could not have hired him to run races", "context_len": 42}}}, "sandy": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "Guy was not sorry to see the thick grass become thinner and thinner , for he remembered that amid its clustering blades he had nearly lost his life , and therefore looked with much complacency upon the broad , shallow river , along which their course lay ; the sandy loam beneath their feet , and the sand hills that arose like great billows of earth , rolling in regular succession over the level surface", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 49, "context": "It was so quaint to sit right inside the chimney corner , and smell the delicious blue peat smoke that was curling its way to the turf roof overhead , and to look out through the open doorway at the silver lake , sending gentle ripples over the little sandy beach , and always the purple waste of heather beyond , with the mountains rising up , tier after tier , into the dim distance", "context_len": 75}}}, "fume": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "The two boys soaked themselves in the river together , and then they lay on the sandy shore , or under some tree , and talked ; but my boy could not have talked to him about any of the things that were in his books , or the fume of dreams they sent up in his mind", "context_len": 58}}}, "intensity": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 73, "context": "None could look back , not even to behold If those beloved were nigh ; one thrilling thought Rapt all the multitude , -- Can HE be near ? Then cries of terror rose -- I scarcely heard ; And buildings shook and rocked , and crashing fell , -- I scarcely marked their fall ; the trembling ground Rose like the billowy sea , -- I scarcely felt The motion ; such intensity of hope -- Joy -- expectation -- flooded all my soul ; A tide of living light , o \u2019 erwhelming all The hopes and fears , the cares and woes of earth", "context_len": 107}}}, "lax": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "Would she be terribly shocked ? I doubt if the little Mummy has the highest principles in the world ; in fact , I do n't doubt , for I am quite certain that the Mummy 's principles are a little lax , but there , she is the Mummy , and I love her", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "My companion often called my attention to the fact that military discipline seemed very lax at this great fortress ; so when , forty-one years later , it was captured by a coup de main by Admirals Rooke and Shovel , the news of its falling an easy prey to us did not come as a great surprise", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "This served to increase our bitterness at heart , and , curiously enough , our guards became particularly lax in their duties", "context_len": 22}}}, "aimlessness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "He must rather have soothed against his soft , caressing ignorance the ache of his fantastic spirit , and reposed his intensity of purpose in that lax and easy aimlessness", "context_len": 30}}}, "mentally": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 48, "context": "This was not easy , for though everybody knew how much the two were together , it was a different thing to sit with him as if he thought him just as good as any boy , and to help him get his lessons , and stay him mentally as well as socially", "context_len": 53}}}, "freer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "He struggled through one day , and maybe another ; but it was a failure from the first moment , and my boy breathed freer when his friend came one half-day , and then never came again", "context_len": 37}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Ki Sing was cook and general servant to the little party , and performed his duties in a very satisfactory manner -- better than either Ben or Bradley could have done -- and left his white employers freer to work at the more congenial occupation of searching for gold", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He had a freer hand now and composed many articles for the Courant", "context_len": 13}}}, "humanity": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 23, "context": "It happened to be a local holiday in Cumberland , and as a result here and there they encountered a solitary specimen of humanity prone upon his back or his face , just as it chanced , by the roadside , or , not quite so badly off as that , reeling along to wherever home might be in that apparently houseless region", "context_len": 63}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}}}, "popularity": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 14, "context": "The affair made a great impression but somehow did not increase Miss Newman 's popularity , for the idea of the drawings was hers and Clara could not forgive her for the position into which she had forced her , therefore she lost no opportunity of making it as unpleasant for her teacher as she could in the thousand and one ways a sly and unprincipled girl can , and her little pin-pricks were so annoying , that finally Dorothy and Edna , who had not particularly cared for the new teacher , began to stand up for her and to do as many kind things as they could", "context_len": 109}}}, "potential": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "My boy tried to sleep , but the more he reflected upon his chances of getting through the night alive the smaller they seemed ; and so he woke up his potential murderer from the sweetest and soundest slumber , and said he was going home , but he was afraid ; and the boy had to go and wake his father", "context_len": 62}}}, "murderer": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 76, "context": "`` Oh , this was to have been my wedding day , my wedding day ! '' and he clasped his hands in agony ; then the thought of his master -- his slain lord -- returned , and he cried , `` O Edmund ! my master , my dear master , so good , so gentle , yet so brave ; who else could slay him ? what fiend else than Edric , the murderer Edric ? That they should think I , or any one else than Edric , could have done such a deed , such an evil deed ! ''", "context_len": 105}}}, "soundest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "My boy tried to sleep , but the more he reflected upon his chances of getting through the night alive the smaller they seemed ; and so he woke up his potential murderer from the sweetest and soundest slumber , and said he was going home , but he was afraid ; and the boy had to go and wake his father", "context_len": 62}}}, "flux": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then , towards the end of the week they heard that he had the flux ; and on Friday , just before school let out , the teacher -- it was the one that whipped so , and that the fellows all liked -- rapped on his desk , and began to speak very solemnly to the scholars", "context_len": 58}}}, "resolve": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 66, "context": "But he had been in the kitchen that evening , when Maggie and Bessie had gone to the porch to speak to his father for Lem , and he had heard all that had passed ; and now , as he remembered how sweetly and generously the two dear little girls had pleaded for the boy who had treated them so badly , he could not resolve to give him even a part of the punishment he so richly deserved", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 97, "context": "`` How could I have left him ? And how could we be urgent to him to fly when we could not know what extraordinary impulse one of his virtue or courage may have had on his mind ? Forget not how he did answer to your entreaties , saying that he would not stir a foot , nor turn his back as though he repented he had been engaged in that great work , or were ashamed of the service of so glorious and great a God ! We could not seek to change such a resolve", "context_len": 98}}}, "sunken": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "But just then , the thoughts of every one in the house except mother ( and the news was carefully kept from her ) , were full of a great and terrible catastrophe , and even I forgot all about the Duchess , for one of our largest Orient liners had foundered on some sunken rocks not far from Port Adelaide , off the coast of South Australia , and there had been a terrific shipwreck , and almost every one on board was drowned", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 22, "context": "There had been no mother 's quickened ear to mark the warning cough , no mother 's loving eye to see the sunken cheek , no mother 's tender hand to guard her child from damp and cold ; and so the trouble had gone on unheeded and unchecked , till the night spent in the Ice Glen had finished the mischief already at work", "context_len": 65}}}, "wan": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "She was lying with her sunken eyes fixed on the marigolds , which stood on a small table beside the bed ; and , oh , how wan , white , and wasted she looked ! Yet there was a look of perfect peace on the poor face ; and , when the children came in , she turned to them with a bright smile", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 58, "context": "There had been quite a spell of sickness in Gorswen at the end of the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change", "context_len": 128}}}, "perforated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "Most of them walked in the procession ; but some of them were waiting beside the open grave , that was dug near the grave of that man who believed there was a hole through the earth from pole to pole , and had a perforated stone globe on top of his monument", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Three days later Rosy O \u2019 Grady \u2019 s sunburned crew had lost ten or fifteen pounds apiece , but the roadway of perforated steel was completed", "context_len": 27}}}, "cutter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "MY boy used to be afraid of this monument , which stood a long time , or what seemed to him a long time , in the yard of the tombstone cutter before it was put up at the grave of the philosopher who imagined the earth as hollow as much of the life is on it", "context_len": 57}}}, "philosopher": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "No ; turn round ; if it 's all the same to you we 'll go there now ; and this last a little gruffly ; for one has to be a good deal of a philosopher to continue on the friendliest of terms with a man that has just informed you that you look listless and lazy", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The same interposition in behalf of the poet 's family and descendants spoke directly to the heart of every poet , orator , historian , and philosopher throughout the country , and tended to make all the lovers of literature his friends", "context_len": 42}}}, "gravestones": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "My boy had to go by the yard when he went to see his grandmother , and even at high noon the sight of the officer 's monument , and the other gravestones standing and leaning about , made his flesh creep and his blood run cold", "context_len": 47}}}, "eased": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 40, "context": "When there were other boys with him he would stop at the door of the shed , where a large , fair German was sawing slabs of marble with a long saw that had no teeth , and that he eased every now and then with water from a sponge he kept by him ; but if the boy was alone , and it was getting at all late in the afternoon , he always ran by the place as fast as he could", "context_len": 84}}}, "feign": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas should sow well the grain , Dark babies should fondle , dark women should train , And Bibis and Begums at times entertain ; Should smile and should soothe , but not flatter or feign ; And to usefulness thus they may hope to attain", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was , therefore , better to feign a belief in his story , to obtain all the information possible from him , and at the same time to prevent his gaining any knowledge of affairs that would be of the slightest use to the Turks", "context_len": 46}}}, "import": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 69, "context": "As Florence slowly , very slowly , as if each step was weighted with lead , crept forward to the front of the dais without any of that look of triumph and pleasure which ought to have marked her face at such a moment , Bertha Keys threw back her own head and allowed her watchful light blue eyes to follow the girl , while a smile of sardonic import curled her lips", "context_len": 73}}}, "forbearing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "In one of his school-books was a story that began , `` Charles was an honest boy , but Robert was the name of a thief , '' and it went on to show how Charles grew up in the respect and affection of all who knew him by forbearing to steal some oranges which their owner had set for safe-keeping at the heels of his horse , while Robert was kicked at once ( there was a picture that showed him holding his stomach with both hands ) , and afterwards came to a bad end , through attempting to take one", "context_len": 103}}}, "attempting": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 99, "context": "In one of his school-books was a story that began , `` Charles was an honest boy , but Robert was the name of a thief , '' and it went on to show how Charles grew up in the respect and affection of all who knew him by forbearing to steal some oranges which their owner had set for safe-keeping at the heels of his horse , while Robert was kicked at once ( there was a picture that showed him holding his stomach with both hands ) , and afterwards came to a bad end , through attempting to take one", "context_len": 103}}}, "outgrew": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "My boy conceived from the tale that the name of Robert was necessarily associated with crime ; it was long before he outgrew the prejudice ; and this tale and others of a like vindictive virtuousness imbued him with such a desire to lead an upright life that he was rather a bother to his friends with his scruples", "context_len": 59}}}, "vindictive": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "In his abject misery and dread , Mark Penelly saw , in the stern gaze before him , anger and a vindictive desire for revenge ; he saw therein fierce hate , and an implacable , unchanging condemnation ; he felt that Harry was sustaining him there where he had dragged him to make his sufferings more acute , and that , after holding him up for a while , he would loosen his hold , causing him to sink at once into the deep water by the rocks , and be swept away by the tremendous current", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` This is rough , Ben , '' he managed to say as the two , side by side , were hurried along by the vindictive crowd", "context_len": 27}}}, "virtuousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "My boy conceived from the tale that the name of Robert was necessarily associated with crime ; it was long before he outgrew the prejudice ; and this tale and others of a like vindictive virtuousness imbued him with such a desire to lead an upright life that he was rather a bother to his friends with his scruples", "context_len": 59}}}, "imbued": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "My boy conceived from the tale that the name of Robert was necessarily associated with crime ; it was long before he outgrew the prejudice ; and this tale and others of a like vindictive virtuousness imbued him with such a desire to lead an upright life that he was rather a bother to his friends with his scruples", "context_len": 59}}}, "mislaid": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "But Percy was popular , Seabrooke was not ; and even the masters were inclined to believe that the latter must have been careless and forgetful and mislaid the money , while believing he had put it in the place he indicated , and presently -- no one knew exactly how it started or could trace the rumor to its source -- presently it began to be bruited about among the boys that Seabrooke was keeping it for his own use and had never intended to return it to Percy , and was now making him his scape-goat", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` I put on all the official dignity I could assume , but was very polite all the time , informed her that mislaid , delayed and irregular express matter were common occurrences , that the company was responsible for its contracts , counted you one of its most reliable agents , and assured her that very possibly within twenty-four hours she would find her trunk delivered safe and sound at its destination", "context_len": 73}}}, "pencil": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 36, "context": "At the last moment , smitten by regret because fancied necessity seemed to compel him to leave without bidding the kind widow good-by , he seized a piece of brown paper and the stub of a pencil and sat down to write a few words of farewell Jerry urging him to hasten even while he was scribbling", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Here is pencil and paper", "context_len": 5}}}, "morbid": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "A girl at school mislaid a pencil which she thought she had lent him , and he began to have a morbid belief that he must have stolen it ; he became frantic with the mere dread of guilt ; he could not eat or sleep , and it was not till he went to make good the loss with a pencil which his grandfather gave him that the girl said she had found her pencil in her desk , and saved him from the despair of a self-convicted criminal", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "The damp , unhealthy atmosphere made our heads swim , so for a time the work was suspended and we gained the upper air , where a crowd of morbid countrymen were dividing their attention between the corpse of the unfortunate Increase Joyce and the gaping hole from which we had emerged", "context_len": 52}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` And I do not mean to be morbid about it ; only , at the time , my conscience troubled me , and your poor aunty had a very bad time", "context_len": 32}}}, "keenest": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 35, "context": "His hair was only beginning to turn grey , and was falling away a trifle from his temples , and he was very upright , and very thin , and had keen eyes , the keenest eyes I had ever looked at , small , grey and bright , and those eyes seemed to look through you , as though they were forcing a gimlet into the very secrets of your soul", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "He seemed to be looking at the youth himself , though that was impossible because Alden must have been invisible to the keenest of eyes while wrapped in the shadow", "context_len": 30}}}, "gravestone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "This was a brown-paper parcel which he found behind a leaning gravestone in the stone-cutter 's yard , and which he could not help peeping into", "context_len": 26}}}, "raisins": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 50, "context": "He went away sorrowing , without touching a raisin ( he felt that the touch must have stricken him with death ) , and far heavier in soul than the hardened accomplices of his sin , of whom he believed himself the worst in having betrayed the presence of the raisins to them", "context_len": 53}}}, "swooped": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "He swooped upon Edmund , who caused his horse to start aside , avoided the stroke , and then , guiding his horse by his knees , and raising his axe in both hands , cleft his antagonist to the chin before he could recover", "context_len": 45}}}, "raisin": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "He went away sorrowing , without touching a raisin ( he felt that the touch must have stricken him with death ) , and far heavier in soul than the hardened accomplices of his sin , of whom he believed himself the worst in having betrayed the presence of the raisins to them", "context_len": 53}}}, "implored": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 3, "context": "In vain he implored them not to stain their souls with this misdeed ; neither the law nor the prophets availed ; neither the awful shadow of the prison which he cast upon them , nor the fear of the last judgment which he invoked", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Had I done so , Albert would probably have gone on his knees on the spot and implored of me there and then to make him the happiest of men", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "'Oh ! ca n't we keep him for a pet ? ' implored Peggy , with an eye on the ever-increasing menagerie", "context_len": 22}}}, "prophets": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "In vain he implored them not to stain their souls with this misdeed ; neither the law nor the prophets availed ; neither the awful shadow of the prison which he cast upon them , nor the fear of the last judgment which he invoked", "context_len": 45}}}, "availed": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 109, "context": "but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed not to do aught against him , so he was dumb and could make the Sultan no answer", "context_len": 128}}}, "judgment": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 61, "word_index": 113, "context": "each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the operation was undertaken", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Sigeferth and Morcar , whom Edric slew at Oxford , have been here , and they said they would come back and drag me with them to some judgment seat ; now take thine axe , Edmund , my son , and slay them when they enter ; they want killing again", "context_len": 52}}}, "invoked": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "So Alaeddin 's mother entered with them and when they came before the Sultan , they all did obeisance to him with the utmost courtliness and gravity and invoked on him glory and prosperity ; then , raising the dishes from their heads , they set them down before him and stood with their hands clasped behind them , after they had removed the covers", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "invoked blessings upon him in the manner familiar to readers of the Nights", "context_len": 13}}}, "sorrowing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "He went away sorrowing , without touching a raisin ( he felt that the touch must have stricken him with death ) , and far heavier in soul than the hardened accomplices of his sin , of whom he believed himself the worst in having betrayed the presence of the raisins to them", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "They were ever afterwards fully persuaded that but for his timely arrival they would have been robbed and murdered , and their bodies hidden away from their sorrowing relatives , like Eugene Aram 's victim , or the little princes in the Tower , and they felt quite an affection for Barlow in consequence , so much so that Bobby determined to wear out his boots as fast as possible , that he might put some business in the old man 's way", "context_len": 83}}}, "hardened": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 24, "context": "And fearful places he found them to be ! Boys who were taken to gaol for the first time were put with old and hardened criminals ; the prisons were dirty and ill-smelling ; the dungeons were dark and unhealthy ; and , unless prisoners could afford to pay for comforts , they were obliged to sleep on cold bare floors , even delicate women not being exempted from such cruel treatment", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 3, "context": "Was I getting hardened ? Was I getting injured ? I put these questions to myself now and then , but I think without any great seriousness -- I was sure that my plan was , on the whole , sensible , and I would not reproach myself for what I had done", "context_len": 53}}}, "accomplices": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 76, "context": "Then came a hurried discussion as to the destination of the booty , some , including the parish clerk , Fallowfield , who had gained the upper regions by means of the tackle , urging that it had best be taken and placed in the tower of Worth Church , the others insisting that it would be best to make one journey do , and convey it as close to Wareham as possible , where their accomplices could make arrangements for its distribution", "context_len": 83}}}, "jokingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "He used to talk to himself when he was little , but one day his mother said to him jokingly , `` Do n't you know that he who talks to himself has the devil for a listener ? '' and after that he never dared whisper above his breath when he was alone , though his father and mother had both taught him that there was no devil but his own evil will", "context_len": 74}}}, "warts": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Other boys played with toads just to show that they were not afraid of having warts ; but every one knew that if you killed a toad , your cow would give bloody milk", "context_len": 34}}}, "forefathers": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}}}, "kine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I dare say the far forefathers of the race knew this too , when they first began to herd their kine in the birthplace of the Aryan peoples ; and perhaps they learned then that if you killed a snake early in the day its tail would live till sundown", "context_len": 50}}}, "sundown": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "I dare say the far forefathers of the race knew this too , when they first began to herd their kine in the birthplace of the Aryan peoples ; and perhaps they learned then that if you killed a snake early in the day its tail would live till sundown", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Then he waited till two of the three months were past , when his mother went out one day of the days , at sundown , to buy oil , and saw the markets closed and the city all decorated and the folk setting candles and flowers in their windows and saw troops , horse and foot , and mounted eunuchs drawn up in state , with cressets and lustres burning", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "My uncle fired and shot one of them , and they went off ; but my grandfather died about sundown", "context_len": 20}}}, "ancestor": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` It is written in the most immortal document , '' continued the Parson , without noticing the interruption , `` that ever emanated from the mind of man , the Declaration of Independence ( signed , by the way , by an ancestor of my stepmother ) , that among the inalienable privileges of man , co-ordinate with life and liberty itself , is the pursuit of happiness", "context_len": 69}}}, "thawing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Once , in a corn-field near the Little Reservoir , the boys found on a thawing day of early spring knots and bundles of snakes writhen and twisted together , in the torpor of their long winter sleep", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Luckily Christmas Day was fine ( for a wet Christmas is enough to damp the stoutest spirits ) , but after that the weather indulged in such a variety of changes , alternately freezing and thawing from day to day , that there was no time for a safe crust of ice to form even on the smallest and shallowest of ponds", "context_len": 62}}}, "writhen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Once , in a corn-field near the Little Reservoir , the boys found on a thawing day of early spring knots and bundles of snakes writhen and twisted together , in the torpor of their long winter sleep", "context_len": 38}}}, "torpor": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "There was no one left alive who had the power to rouse Richard from the torpor that possessed him ; the numb misery that had fallen on him when he saw General Harrison die had never again lifted from his heart and brain", "context_len": 43}}}, "haunted": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 49, "context": "He felt better after this and went in ; but that night the excitement of the adventure and the effects of his immersion were sufficient to keep him awake hour after hour , while when he dropped off into an uneasy slumber it was for his mind to be haunted by dreams in which he was being dragged down into the depths of the sea by a strange monster that clung to his limbs and writhed about him , making him shudder as he felt the chilling embrace", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` My father says there are n't such things as haunted houses , and he ought to know", "context_len": 18}}}, "nightmares": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "If you will only give me back my key and let me lock my door I would n't have such dreadful nightmares", "context_len": 22}}}, "poisonous": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 52, "context": "This was the first time he had had an opportunity offered him to try his gun , as they had seen no living creature upon the desert of alkali which they had occupied more than a week in crossing , and but few among the prickly pears and sage-brush that succeeded the poisonous salts", "context_len": 54}}}, "blacksnakes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "There were no poisonous snakes in the region of the Boy 's Town , but there were some large blacksnakes , and the boys said that if a blacksnake got the chance he would run up your leg , and tie himself round your body so that you could not breathe", "context_len": 51}}}, "blacksnake": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Nobody had ever seen a blacksnake do it , and nobody had ever seen a hoop-snake , but the boys believed there was such a snake , and that he would take his tail in his mouth , when he got after a person , and roll himself along swifter than the fastest race-horse could run", "context_len": 56}}}, "fastest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "Nobody had ever seen a blacksnake do it , and nobody had ever seen a hoop-snake , but the boys believed there was such a snake , and that he would take his tail in his mouth , when he got after a person , and roll himself along swifter than the fastest race-horse could run", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 125, "context": "as it might be here '' -- arranging a tankard at the corner of the table -- '' with a good high road leading up to it from the sea , as it might be my tobacco pipe '' -- laying it down with care ; `` and if you 'll believe me , sir , we took and run races , as it might be along my tobacco pipe , and as soon as them Spaniards was 'ware of our coming , they took and ran out by 'tother gate , and left the town empty ! There was seven churches all chock full of gold and silver idols and candle-sticks , and such like : 't was just who 'd fill his pockets fastest ! ''", "context_len": 128}}}, "puddle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "No one had ever seen it happen , but every one knew that if you put long horsehairs into a puddle of water and let them stay , they would turn into hair-snakes ; and when you drank out of a spring you had to be careful not to swallow a hair-snake , or it would remain in your stomach and grow there", "context_len": 63}}}, "lizard": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "dwarfs": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 35, "context": "It would be singular indeed , if the Creeks were the only Indians of America who believed solely in the Great Spirit and not also in a number of lesser conceptions of imagination , as dwarfs , giants , ogres , fairies , hobgoblins and earth-spirits", "context_len": 46}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "ground thumb beads dwarfs crooked crown worked", "context_len": 7}}}, "joints": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 11, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}}}, "rattlesnake": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 8, "context": "Some he said were telling how a monster rattlesnake had come to visit them without any invitation , and that the only food he would eat , was the youngest and fattest of their families ; and that their constant intruders , the owls , had the same carnivorous tastes , besides which they rendered themselves particularly disagreeable , by standing in the doors and staring at every dog that went by , and even preventing the entrance of visitors , to the great distress of all the belles and beaux in town", "context_len": 93}}}, "charmed": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 20, "context": "What a bright account you give of your dear busy young party ! Tell dear Otho that I shall be charmed if he makes the discovery of a magenta-coloured caterpillar , or a mauve earwig ; and that as it will be ten times as curious as the Spongmenta Padella , it ought to have a Latin name ten times as long", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "'As I advised you in my last , we were on our way to Farningham , when something went wrong with the engine , and we had to stop here for repairs , and mamma was so charmed with this little village that she decided to stay awhile ; she says it seems to suit her better than any place she has seen ; poor mamma , I wish I could find some place where she would be satisfied", "context_len": 79}}}, "rebel": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "and if ever he tried to rebel against this slavery , and went by a pin in the path , his fears tormented him till he came back and picked it up", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "I was told that the horseman was supposed to be the arch-traitor , Old Noll , but a clever hewer of wood had caused all likeness of the great rebel to disappear , and had graven instead the features of honest George , now created Duke of Albemarle", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "George spoke of himself as having then been a bit of a rebel \u2019 ; adding , But my republican zeal was very much cooled by the French Revolution ; and if a spark of it had remained , our own most contemptible revolution of 1830 would have extinguished it , and have fixed me for life a determined Conservative", "context_len": 60}}}, "isle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "He had an abject terror of dying , but it seemed to him that if a person could die right in the centre isle of the church -- the Methodist church where his mother used to go before she became finally a New Churchwoman -- the chances of that person 's going straight to heaven would be so uncommonly good that he need have very little anxiety about it", "context_len": 69}}}, "revelation": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "Matt recounted the manner in which he had been beguiled into the open country by the supposed messenger ; and he told about the accident to the taxicab , the revelation that the supposed youth was a girl , the finding of the driver , the passing of the red touring car with McGlory in the tonneau , the work of Dimmock and Sanders , a mile west of Rye , and the journey through Connecticut and into Massachusetts , finishing with his meeting with McGlory", "context_len": 86}}}, "darkling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "He asked his mother if she did not think so too , holding by her hand as they came out of church together , and he noticed the sort of gravity and even pain with which she and his father received this revelation of his darkling mind", "context_len": 47}}}, "doctrine": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "You also will join us ; I know it is long since you have heard the words of pure doctrine , yet there is a blessing in reserve for the seed of the righteous , and the filth of the Presbyterian doctrines you learned in Scotland shall not cleave unto your feet to make them stumble in the way", "context_len": 59}}}, "singularity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "They tried to teach him what they thought of such things ; but though their doctrine caught his fancy and flattered his love of singularity , he was not proof against the crude superstitions of his mates", "context_len": 37}}}, "sinner": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 56, "context": "I am much better as a rule ; but it came over me in church how proper people were , and they all of them talk about being miserable sinners , and every one looks so good and righteous , and knowing down deep in their hearts that every single one of them is a miserable sinner , except your darling , precious self ; and they all repeat the words , not feeling them a bit", "context_len": 77}}}, "heightened": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 57, "context": "The limbs of the great tree swayed gently in the westerly breeze , and the moonbeams came slanting through them most delightfully , as the boys lay listening to the moan of the night wind , the sound of the surf along the shore , and watched the clouds as they coursed by the moon , all heightened by the novelty of their situation", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "To please these dear cousins was his burning desire , therefore he thought he must create a sensation , so with a heightened color he announced solemnly , `` We were after the lamb", "context_len": 34}}}, "believers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "But thou knowest not , O my son , that the good of the Muslims , believers in the unity of God the Most High , is lawful spoil in the eyes of Jews ; nay , they still cheat the Muslims and especially this accursed one with whom thou dealest and into whose hands thou hast fallen", "context_len": 58}}}, "ascension": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Those were the days when the minds of many were stirred by this fear or hope ; the believers had their ascension robes ready , and some gave away their earthly goods so as not to be cumbered with anything in their heavenward flight", "context_len": 44}}}, "heavenward": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Those were the days when the minds of many were stirred by this fear or hope ; the believers had their ascension robes ready , and some gave away their earthly goods so as not to be cumbered with anything in their heavenward flight", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "There is no need to tell that they were friendly after this , these two pilgrims on the heavenward way , -- the old woman and the little child , she who had begun to tread in her Master 's footsteps so early in life 's bright morning , and she who had not sought to follow him until the eleventh hour , when her day was almost ended", "context_len": 69}}}, "jest": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` It 's jest cause He 's so wonderful beautiful His own self , '' she would say ; and it was agony to her , greater even than it would be to a mother to hear her little child abused , to have a word breathed against Him", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I did but jest , '' she answered ; `` but the common folk have much talk of ghosts in this house since it hath been left so desolate , and so they shun it ; and if any man saw or heard you here , 't is more likely they would hold you for some dead Cremer or Inglethorpe than for a mortal man", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 10, "context": "No matter what I \u2019 m doin \u2019 , I jest drop an \u2019 don \u2019 t know a thing for a minute or two ; then , after I come out of it , I \u2019 m gen \u2019 rally a little while piecin \u2019 things together", "context_len": 48}}}, "tint": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 44, "context": "In the early morning , before the activities of the day have begun , this wharf is a deserted and forbidding place , and on one such early morning in September , with chill air and cloudy skies , and not even a rose tint in the dull east , there was no one to be seen throughout the whole length of the wharf save one slowly moving boy", "context_len": 69}}}, "prevailing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "At home , my boy heard his father jest at the crazy notion , and make fun of the believers ; but abroad , among the boys , he took the tint of the prevailing gloom", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Numbers of people in their picturesque costumes were threading their way to the village of A -- -- , white being the prevailing colour of the men \u2019 s costumes , gay red that of the women \u2019 s , with a fair sprinkling of green , a touch of yellow and blue , and here and there a grand display of glittering gold", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It must have acted powerfully against the prevailing practice of family revenge , and served to establish a state of safety for the lives of individuals", "context_len": 26}}}, "presentiments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He early heard of forebodings and presentiments , and he tried hard against his will to have them , because he was so afraid of having them", "context_len": 27}}}, "trance": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "For the same reason he did his best , or his worst , to fall into a trance , in which he should know everything that was going on about him , all the preparations for his funeral , all the sorrow and lamentation , but should be unable to move or speak , and only be saved at the last moment by some one putting a mirror to his lips and finding a little blur of mist on it", "context_len": 80}}}, "blur": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 75, "context": "For the same reason he did his best , or his worst , to fall into a trance , in which he should know everything that was going on about him , all the preparations for his funeral , all the sorrow and lamentation , but should be unable to move or speak , and only be saved at the last moment by some one putting a mirror to his lips and finding a little blur of mist on it", "context_len": 80}}}, "phantasmal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Once , he woke up in the night and found the full moon shining into his room in a very strange and phantasmal way , and washing the floor with its pale light , and somehow it came into his mind that he was going to die when he was sixteen years old", "context_len": 53}}}, "perverse": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "He could then only have been nine or ten , but the perverse fear sank deep into his soul , and became an increasing torture till he passed his sixteenth birthday and entered upon the year in which he had appointed himself to die", "context_len": 44}}}, "sixteenth": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "In the sixteenth century the Timucua inhabited the northern and middle portion of the peninsula of Florida , and although their exact limits to the north are unknown , they held a portion of Florida bordering on Georgia , and some of the coast islands in the Atlantic Ocean , as Guale ( then the name of Amelia ) and others", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The two new figures , confronted with each other , in like manner , furnish a fifteenth figure , which , being confronted with the first of the primaries , gives a sixteenth and last figure , completing the series", "context_len": 40}}}, "appointed": {"freq": 83, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 78, "word_index": 71, "context": "`` And she said to him , `` O my son , the Sultan received me with all courtesy , according to his wont , and meseemeth he meant fairly by us , but [ for ] thine accursed enemy the Vizier ; for that , after I had bespoken the Sultan in thy name , even as thou badest me , reminding him that the term for which he had appointed us was past and saying to him , 'If Thy Grace would vouchsafe to give commandment for the marriage of thy daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour with my son Alaeddin , ' -- he turned to the Vizier and spoke to him", "context_len": 113}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "So they entered all , for that it was the appointed time of the morning-meal and they had set one table for the Sultan and the Lady Bedrulbudour and Alaeddin and another for the Vizier and the officers of state and grandees of the realm and captains and chamberlains and deputies", "context_len": 51}}}, "confessed": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was growing quite dark before Father returned , and I think , though neither of them would have confessed it , the children were both rather relieved to hear his cheery whistle outside the door , for it was just a little eerie sitting by the peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "Just as we reached the summit , where the road makes a vast horseshoe curve round the dark , forbidding cavity known as the Devil \u2019 s Punch Bowl , a heavy rainstorm came on , blotting out the horizon , while a vivid flash of lightning , followed at a short interval by a tremendous clap of thunder , startled our horses , and , be it confessed , ourselves as well", "context_len": 73}}}, "seventeenth": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "It is too late for you to die at sixteen , '' and all the long-gathering load of misery dropped from the boy 's soul , and he lived till his seventeenth birthday and beyond it without further trouble", "context_len": 39}}}, "presentiment": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "Meanwhile it seemed as if the earth had opened and swallowed both Increase Joyce and the box ( for in my mind the two were inseparably associated ) , though I had a presentiment that I should obtain satisfaction in the end", "context_len": 42}}}, "beards": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "In the world you will find a great many grown-up boys , with gray beards and grandchildren , who think that they have been different their whole lives through from other people , and are the victims of destiny", "context_len": 39}}}, "victims": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 64, "context": "night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me , died out , and I began to prepare", "context_len": 128}}}, "essentials": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "The first thing you have to learn here below is that in essentials you are just like every one else , and that you are different from others only in what is not so much worth while", "context_len": 37}}}, "perversion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "If you have anything in common with your fellow-creatures , it is something that God gave you ; if you have anything that seems quite your own , it is from your silly self , and is a sort of perversion of what came to you from the Creator who made you out of himself , and had nothing else to make any one out of", "context_len": 66}}}, "flatters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "There is not really any difference between you and your fellow-creatures ; but only a seeming difference that flatters and cheats you with a sense of your strangeness , and makes you think you are a remarkable fellow", "context_len": 38}}}, "cheats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "There is not really any difference between you and your fellow-creatures ; but only a seeming difference that flatters and cheats you with a sense of your strangeness , and makes you think you are a remarkable fellow", "context_len": 38}}}, "fails": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 84, "context": "But more incomprehensible than the existence of so bad a man in such a dark age is the renewed confidence ever accorded him , when , after more than once betraying the armies of his country into the hands of their foes , and fighting openly in the hostile cause , he is again forgiven , nay , received into favour , and sent once more to command the men he has already deceived , until he repeats the experiment , and when it fails is again admitted into confidence", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Here the ealdorman Elfric met him with a large English army ; but when he saw the foe he fell sick , or feigned to be so ; and then the old proverb came true , `` When the general fails , the army quails", "context_len": 45}}}, "succeeds": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 51, "context": "Now and then men have managed to become possessed of a file , and have , by long and patient work , sawn through a chain , and have , when a galley has been lying near our own shore , sprung overboard and escaped ; but for every attempt that succeeds there must be twenty failures , for the chains are frequently examined , and woe be to the man who is found to have been tampering with his", "context_len": 80}}}, "wasp": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Good day , or rather night , to you , Thane of Aescendune , '' said he to Elfwyn ; `` we have had a fair night 's work , and destroyed a big wasp 's nest ; have you come for your share in the spoil ? ''", "context_len": 50}}}, "sleeps": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Are you going to have the bed on which your mother sleeps sold under her , and she dying , or are you not ? I can help you , I have plenty of money , I have a lot of loose cash in the bank which may as well go in your direction as any other", "context_len": 58}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "' I think the dormouse has the best of it , ' said Lilian , 'for he sleeps straight on , and never seems to feel the frost at all", "context_len": 30}}}, "contrary": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 48, "context": "I must cure the king , or it would be so much the worse for me ! And how was I to cure him ? My knowledge of disease was of the slightest and most amateurish kind , and , for aught that I could tell to the contrary , might not even be sufficient to enable me to diagnose the case correctly , much less to treat it successfully ! However , there was no use in meeting trouble half-way ; the only possible course was to obey the summons forthwith , and do my best , leaving the result in the hands of Providence", "context_len": 106}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Aggie , on the contrary was so anxious to have a nearer view of the Indians , when she found them so much less terrible than she had imagined , that she begged her mother to allow her to stand with Guy outside the wagon , and after some little hesitation , Mrs", "context_len": 53}}}, "plotted": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 37, "context": "What they can be going to do , or what plot they are hatching , I can not discover , only I fear that it is some design for vengeance upon the Danes -- some dark treachery plotted against those in our midst ; and , if such is the case , I can but feel uneasy for poor Alfgar", "context_len": 60}}}, "armload": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 104, "context": "When I see five or six boys now lying under a tree on the grass , and they fall silent as I pass them , I have no right to say that they are not arranging to go and carry some poor widow 's winter wood into her shed and pile it neatly up for her , and wish to keep it a secret from everybody ; but forty years ago I should have had good reason for thinking that they were debating how to tie a piece of her clothes-line along the ground so that when her orphan boy came out for an armload of wood after dark , he would trip on it and send his wood flying all over the yard", "context_len": 124}}}, "ambitions": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 37, "context": "And as he grew more weary , more desperate of escape from his pursuers , he soon ceased to think at all ; political dreams , sorrow for the dead , hopes of finding new friends and ambitions in a new world , all were forgotten , the spirit within him was dulled by suffering ; only the poor body cried incessantly for rest , for food , for warmth , and most often craved in vain", "context_len": 77}}}, "commit": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I should think you \u2019 d commit suicide , I should , upon my word", "context_len": 15}}}, "sublimity": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "I have often read in stories of boys who were fond of nature , and loved her sublimity and beauty , but I do not believe boys are ever naturally fond of nature", "context_len": 33}}}, "aspects": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "They want to make use of the woods and fields and rivers ; and when they become men they find these aspects of nature endeared to them by association , and so they think that they were dear for their own sakes ; but the taste for nature is as purely acquired as the taste for poetry or the taste for tomatoes", "context_len": 62}}}, "purely": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 39, "context": "I often think of all these things , and sometimes talk them over with comrades , and there are few who do not hold , with me , that it would be far better that we should become a purely military Order , like some of the military Orders in the courts of the European sovereigns , than remain as we are , half monk , half soldier -- a mixture that , so far as I can see , accords but badly with either morality or public repute", "context_len": 89}}}, "rainbow": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 65, "context": "There was the giant mora tree , two hundred feet high , aglow with clusters of scarlet blossoms , feathery palms , the bright yellow trumpet flower with blooms so large that they were worn as hats by the Indian women and children , huge fuchsias with their purplish tubular bells , heliotrope , verbenas , orchids , glowing with all the colors of the rainbow", "context_len": 66}}}, "profit": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 18, "context": "Bobby had a pretty set of bantams , whose small eggs afforded him much delight and some slight profit , for Aunt Helen bought them from him at threepence a dozen -- a transaction which he always recorded in chalk upon the hen-house door , the pennies being carefully put by towards the purchase of a pair of fantail pigeons , which was at present the summit of his ambition", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The king left on Monday without making any efforts to profit by the Etheling 's discovery at Carisbrooke ; but we could not in conscience let the matter rest", "context_len": 29}}}, "perfume": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 55, "context": "He could no more remember learning to set type than he could remember learning to read ; and in after-life he could not come within smell of the ink , the dusty types , the humid paper , of a printing-office without that tender swelling of the heart which so fondly responds to any memory-bearing perfume : his youth , his boyhood , almost his infancy came back to him in it", "context_len": 72}}}, "noisome": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Marry , that which befell me was yet worse , for that , instead of sleeping with my bride in bed , I lay in the draught-house , a place dark and frightful , noisome of smell and accursed , and my ribs were straitened [ 411 ] with cold", "context_len": 50}}}, "stench": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "But a minute or two later , as we drew nearer the tree , the stench meanwhile becoming almost overpoweringly strong , I detected fastened to the trunk of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may", "context_len": 128}}}, "loathsome": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 95, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "A sign , and we were hurried from the burgomaster \u2019 s presence , and , with a file of pikemen surrounding us , we were taken , not to that loathsome dungeon , but into the open air", "context_len": 39}}}, "putrefaction": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Alexander marched his troops rapidly off the ground , leaving , as the direct result of the battle , a wide extent of country depopulated and desolate , with this vast mass of putrefaction and pestilence reigning in awful silence and solitude in the midst of it", "context_len": 47}}}, "acquire": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 64, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "impale": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "As Bart turned , he saw the village magnate ten feet away , planted like a rock , and extending his big golden-headed cane as if it was a spear and he was poising to immediately impale a victim", "context_len": 39}}}, "flinching": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "To bait a hook they tear an angle-worm into small pieces , or impale a grub without flinching ; they go to the slaughter-house and see beeves knocked in the head without a tremor", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "He met the gaze of the multitude without flinching , and his general demeanour impressed many in his favour", "context_len": 19}}}, "beeves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "To bait a hook they tear an angle-worm into small pieces , or impale a grub without flinching ; they go to the slaughter-house and see beeves knocked in the head without a tremor", "context_len": 34}}}, "tremor": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 18, "context": "But as he looked at the individual whom he had mistaken for a deer he felt a second tremor of excitement , for , as he had a glimpse of his face Bart was made aware that the man was none other than the one about whom there was such a mystery -- the man who had sneaked into the schoolhouse the night the diamond bracelet was stolen -- the midnight visitor at the camp , perhaps", "context_len": 77}}}, "encounter": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 20, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 62, "context": "Yes , you said something like that to the captain ; and as though she would have given all the world if he had n't , but I did n't mean to be bold really , only I felt so sorry for Chris ; and then she proceeded to tell , as coherently as her emotions would allow , of her unexpected encounter with her old friend , and how dreadful it would have been if they could not have seen anything of each other just because Chris was a second-cabin passenger , and of how she had mustered all her courage and gone straight to the captain to see what could be done about it", "context_len": 116}}}, "unclean": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Thanks be to God for our preservation in that den of unclean lions ! '' said the gleeman ; `` but had they known who was amongst them , he would have had scant chance of escape", "context_len": 38}}}, "discomfort": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 54, "context": "Lilian was busy trying to grow up suddenly and be ready to take Aunt Helen 's place , for she was old enough now to realize how much responsibility would rest with her when she must manage the reins of the household alone , and she was determined that Father should feel as little discomfort as possible from the change", "context_len": 60}}}, "hastened": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 82, "context": "When the Sultan examined it and saw that it was unfinished , he said , `` Woe is me for thee , O casement , that thou art not perfect ! '' Then , turning to the Vizier , he said to him , `` Knowest thou the reason of the lack of completion of this casement and its lattices ? '' `` O [ 512 ] my lord , '' answered the Vizier , `` methinketh it is because Thy Grace hastened upon Alaeddin with the wedding and he had no time to complete it", "context_len": 96}}}, "crevices": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 57, "context": "At last the weather became very cold , and being without a fire , his sufferings were great , until his ferocious gaoler , finding him quite stiffened , brought up a brazier of coals , which saved his prisoner 's life , while it filled the room with smoke , which could only escape by the crevices in walls and roof , for to open a window would have been as bad as to dispense with the fire , such was the state of the outer air", "context_len": 88}}}, "grime": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "Hartley very seriously ; and then as she looked up and saw , now that dust and grime and the stains from two or three slight cuts were removed , that the face above was a good face , after all , her heart went out in sympathy , and she added gently , but we 'll hope for the best , dear -- we 'll hope for the best", "context_len": 70}}}, "unmanly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "Some of those boys had hands as hard as horn , cracked open at the knuckles and in the palms , and the crevices blackened with earth or grime ; and they taught my boy to believe that he was an inferior and unmanly person , almost of the nature of a cry-baby , because his hands were not horn-like , and cracked open , and filled with dirt", "context_len": 69}}}, "dirt": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` She made you take back old Miss Mapes ' handkercher , and made me go and tell Miss Jones she was sorry for unhookin ' her clothes-line and lettin ' down the clothes in the dirt ; and , oh ! do you think , there 's the biggest kind of a squash down in Todd 's cornfield , and I was a goin ' to get it for him , and Dol coaxed me not", "context_len": 77}}}, "tenderness": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 14, "context": "Has somebody stepped on his poor 'ittle toe ? '' he added with mock tenderness , as if he was talking to a little child ; `` never mind , hold up your head , or you 'll put the fire out with your tears ; just see how they make it fizzle : why , how salt they must be ! ''", "context_len": 63}}}, "generosity": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 84, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}}}, "wasteful": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "They are wasteful with what they do not want at the moment ; but their instinct is to get and not to give", "context_len": 23}}}, "flatter": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "In the Boy 's Town , if a fellow appeared at his gate with a piece of bread spread with apple-butter and sugar on top , the other fellows flocked round him and tried to flatter him out of bites of it , though they might be at that moment almost bursting with surfeit", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Walmer is much flatter", "context_len": 4}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas should sow well the grain , Dark babies should fondle , dark women should train , And Bibis and Begums at times entertain ; Should smile and should soothe , but not flatter or feign ; And to usefulness thus they may hope to attain", "context_len": 49}}}, "wheedled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "To get a bite was so much clear gain , and when they had wheedled one from the owner of the bread , they took as large a bite as their mouths could stretch to , and they had neither shame nor regret for their behavior , but mocked his just resentment", "context_len": 52}}}, "hoarded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was not practical to carry much fodder for the animals , but even that scant supply was hoarded against the inevitable rainy day", "context_len": 24}}}, "imprison": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}}}, "accumulate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "At intervals the ash-hoists had to be supplied with still-smouldering embers , for so quickly did the heaps of ashes accumulate , that , unless removed constantly , they would seriously hamper the fireman at work in the already-congested space", "context_len": 40}}}, "pens": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}}}, "counterfeit": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 121, "context": "It might be well enough to catch bees in hollyhocks , and imprison them in underground cells with flowers for them to make honey from ; but why accumulate fire-flies and even dor-bugs in small brick pens ? Why heap together mussel-shells ; and what did a boy expect to do with all the marbles he won ? You could trade marbles for tops , but they were not money , like pins ; and why were pins money ? Why did the boys instinctively choose them for their currency , and pay everything with them ? There were certain very rigid laws about them , and a bent pin could not be passed among the boys any more than a counterfeit coin among men", "context_len": 125}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Concealed in the attic they discovered a bundle , or package , and this contained dies for the making of counterfeit money", "context_len": 22}}}, "calculation": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was in imagination , but his mental calculation was not far wrong , for within a few yards of where it might be expected , and not ten from where he was now sculling , he saw something roll up as it were to the surface , there was a gleam of white in the sunlit water , and then it was disappearing again , when , acting upon the impulse of the moment , Harry loosened his hold of the oar , took two steps forward over the thwarts , and leaped into the sea", "context_len": 97}}}, "forecast": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "From time to time several boys got together and decided to keep store , and then other boys decided to buy of them with pins ; but there was no calculation in the scheme ; and though I have read of boys , especially in English books , who made a profit out of their fellows , I never knew any boy who had enough forecast to do it", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "As time passed , and Lola , with her arm in a sling , began to sit up and to creep about , there was little in her manner to show the wisdom of Jane 's cheerful forecast", "context_len": 38}}}, "improvident": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "They were too wildly improvident for anything of the kind , and if they had any virtue at all it was scorn of the vice of stinginess", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "And there the yellow men , in their new forms , waited for thousands of years , and there many of them are waiting still for the pick of the miner to bring them forth into their new life , to curse the wicked and improvident , and to bless the poor and needy", "context_len": 54}}}, "stinginess": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "They were too wildly improvident for anything of the kind , and if they had any virtue at all it was scorn of the vice of stinginess", "context_len": 27}}}, "venturesome": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 32, "context": "Others would make for one of the cinnamon-sailed luggers lying at anchor , to go round and back , or would get into one of the boats ; while some , more venturesome , or really more confident in their powers over the water , would go boldly out , perhaps a mile , to meet some lugger coming in from the fishing-ground , sure of being taken aboard and riding back abreast of the boulders where they had left their clothes", "context_len": 82}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "It was a great setback to the enthusiasm of the girls , but as Reliance told Esther Ann , she should not have tried so venturesome a thing at the very outset", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The feat had been seen hundreds of times ; every swimmer who had attained manhood could do it ; and at times it was hard work to keep back the venturesome boys", "context_len": 32}}}, "courageous": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 76, "context": "The party thus opposed were inactive and silent while Alexander was in their country , on his first visit to southern Greece ; but after his return they began to contemplate more decisive action , and afterward , when they heard of his having undertaken so desperate an enterprise as going northward with his forces , and actually crossing the Danube , they considered him as so completely out of the way that they grew very courageous , and meditated open rebellion", "context_len": 82}}}, "painting": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 48, "context": "She was teeming with life and energy ; -- whether it were over her favourite chess , when she would wait patiently but eagerly , thinking out each move ; or enjoying the small-talk of society , watching faces and reading characters , to treasure them up for painting in one of her forthcoming volumes ; or teaching a niece the beauties of sound and thought in the Italian of Dante ; or playing at some game of thought with young people ; or reading aloud one of her two favourite dearly-loved and untiringly-studied authors , Shakespeare and Boswell \u2019 s Life of Johnson", "context_len": 104}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "He has no talent , thank Heaven , for painting or for poetry , or for any fal-lal of that kind , his talent lies in a sound business direction", "context_len": 30}}}, "natives": {"freq": 125, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 125, "word_index": 19, "context": "Why did he never see that look upon the face of Casson ? Why did the faces of the natives always wear to Bomba the same dull and stupid look , as dull and stupid , Bomba thought , and often more so , than the faces of his jungle friends ? Why were the faces of the Indians always the same , except when they darkened and grew fierce and stern ? Why did beauty not appeal to them as it did to him ?", "context_len": 86}}}, "tidily": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was between six and seven o'clock when Agnes , neatly and tidily dressed , came downstairs , accompanied by Irene , who led her straight into the drawing room", "context_len": 30}}}, "canes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "'Way down in Georgia where de sugar am grow , All de piccaninnies want to suck de canes , you know ! An ' dey 're hidin ' round an ' peepin ' , like de 'possum all de day , Till Uncle Sambo bring his stick an ' chase 'em all away !", "context_len": 54}}}, "reject": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "They would rather go to school than fish , or hunt , or swim , any day ; and if one of their teachers were ever to offer them a holiday , they would reject it by a vote of the whole school", "context_len": 43}}}, "dreads": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "As that country is friendly with us at present , since Egypt dreads the ever increasing power of the Turks , it will be but a question of ransom , for I have secret agents there who will inform me without delay of the arrival of a Christian captive", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Turning a bend in the river , a light assailed Bomba \u2019 s eyes , a fearful light , a light such as the native of the jungle dreads above all others", "context_len": 32}}}, "owning": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "But while I am owning to a good deal that seems to me lamentably wrong in the behavior of the Boy 's Town boys , I ought to remember one or two things to their credit", "context_len": 36}}}, "lamentably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "But while I am owning to a good deal that seems to me lamentably wrong in the behavior of the Boy 's Town boys , I ought to remember one or two things to their credit", "context_len": 36}}}, "resenting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 56, "context": "Hartley personally , he sought him out , where he was at work in a corner of the meadow , and the result , as he had anticipated , was a very plain talk -- so unsparingly and pointedly plain that Harry winced a good deal in the process , and once or twice came near resenting a mode of procedure that seemed very much akin to knocking a fellow when he 's down", "context_len": 74}}}, "respectful": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 15, "context": "Their voices were subdued lest they should reach the king 's ears ; but , respectful as was the bearing of all the members of the group by the fire , they clearly split into two halves : on the one hand , the officers of the escort who were teasing the boy , and on the other , a group of gentlemen , some wearing the conventional ribbons and laces of a cavalier , others in the rough cloth of country wear , stained with the mud of country lanes , while the master and mistress of the house moved from one guest to another , evidently nervous at the doubtful honour that such a royal visit had brought to their roof", "context_len": 123}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "The officer , however , in farther interviews , gave him such an account of the kind and respectful treatment which the ladies had received from Alexander , during all the time of their captivity , as greatly to relieve his mind , and to afford him a high degree of comfort and consolation", "context_len": 54}}}, "abusing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "There was sternness as he expressed his sentiments regarding the person with a bullying inclination who took pleasure in abusing those physically weaker than himself ; and , although Sam Rollins \u2019 name was not mentioned , every one knew at whom those open remarks were directed", "context_len": 47}}}, "impudent": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "They would not have dreamed of uttering a rude or impudent word to her ; they obeyed her , and they were even eager to serve her , if she asked a favor of them", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Well , if here is n't another beggar for crumbs , '' chattered an impudent little voice near me , and looking round I saw a chipmunk sitting on a sun dial and washing his face and paws for supper", "context_len": 41}}}, "obeyed": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 16, "context": "As soon as the vessel was under way he abused his followers hotly for not having obeyed his orders to capture the knights without bloodshed ; but they pleaded that it was as much as they had been able to do to capture Gervaise in that way , and that they could never have overcome the four together , before the boats would have had time to come from the ship", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "Clitus obeyed , saying , as he moved away , `` He is right not to bear freeborn men at his table who can only tell him the truth", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Da-rah ! again commanded her father , and this time the child obeyed and went back weeping to the group of women", "context_len": 22}}}, "cheating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "I never knew a boy who was even reputed to have stolen anything , among all the boys , high and low , who met together and played in a perfect social equality ; and cheating in any game was despised", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Peggy had been accustomed from her babyhood to 'play fair , ' so she opened her eyes wide to see such deliberate cheating", "context_len": 23}}}, "invade": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 98, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}}}, "chivalrously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "But the standard of honesty was chivalrously high among those boys ; and I believe that if ever we have the equality in this world which so many good men have hoped for , theft will be unknown", "context_len": 38}}}, "theft": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 53, "context": "Leacraft as he had with the boys , insisting that the whole affair , the abstraction of the money and the placing it in Seabrooke 's trunk , was `` only a joke ; '' but the doctor altogether refused to look upon it in any other light than that of an unmitigated theft and an atrocious attempt to fasten it upon another when he feared detection for himself", "context_len": 69}}}, "poverty": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 53, "context": "Then , glowing with enterprise , she proceeded to extend and embellish her property in a way which speedily set the town by the ears , and aroused every one to dark prophecies as to what must happen when her money should all be gone , and nothing left her but to face poverty in the palatial five-room dwelling now growing up around the pine homestead of the past", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "In a short time she made him feel quite at ease , which also seemed surprising when he thought of it ; for to him , accustomed to poverty all his life , that library was like a room in a palace", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "It is the fashion to be eccentric now , it is poverty that crushes , you must know that", "context_len": 19}}}, "scales": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 48, "context": "If the enemy remained at a distance , and threw javelins or darts at the phalanx , they fell harmless , stopped by the shields which the soldiers wore upon the left arm , and which were held in such a manner as to form a system of scales , which covered and protected the whole mass , and made the men almost invulnerable", "context_len": 64}}}, "belfry": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "She was above the clock , which was so far up in the air , against the summer sky which was always blue , that it made your neck ache to look up at it ; and the bell was so large that once when my boy was a very little fellow , and was in the belfry with his brother , to see if they could get some of the pigeons that nested there , and the clock began to strike , it almost smote him dead with the terror of its sound , and he felt his heart quiver with the vibration of the air between the strokes", "context_len": 110}}}, "quiver": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 100, "context": "She was above the clock , which was so far up in the air , against the summer sky which was always blue , that it made your neck ache to look up at it ; and the bell was so large that once when my boy was a very little fellow , and was in the belfry with his brother , to see if they could get some of the pigeons that nested there , and the clock began to strike , it almost smote him dead with the terror of its sound , and he felt his heart quiver with the vibration of the air between the strokes", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "Aylmer the great ? Was she , her real mother , the mother who had nursed her as a baby , who had put up with her childish troubles , to have nothing whatever to do with her in the future ? Notwithstanding that crown of glory which seemed to quiver over the forehead of the little widow , she did not like this aspect of the question", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "He stood upright , his machete was at his side ; his bow was still intact , his quiver full of arrows , and in his pouch was his greatly prized revolver , once again fully loaded", "context_len": 37}}}, "vibration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 103, "context": "She was above the clock , which was so far up in the air , against the summer sky which was always blue , that it made your neck ache to look up at it ; and the bell was so large that once when my boy was a very little fellow , and was in the belfry with his brother , to see if they could get some of the pigeons that nested there , and the clock began to strike , it almost smote him dead with the terror of its sound , and he felt his heart quiver with the vibration of the air between the strokes", "context_len": 110}}}, "professor": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 54, "word_index": 20, "context": "After the death of his teacher Orphy falls upon hard times , but eventually his talent is recognized by a professor of music who takes him to Europe , and there , under peculiar circumstances , he plays on his home-made gourd fiddle before no less a personage than Her Majesty , Queen Victoria", "context_len": 54}}}, "exhibition": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 85, "context": "Then she repeated -- 'Where are you going , my pretty maid ? ' and she had a little milk pail on her arm , and she managed to keep the two parts wonderfully distinct -- it was remarkable in a child not three years old , and when she said -- 'Then I wo n't marry you , my pretty maid ' and answered so pertly -- 'Nobody asked you , sir , she said , ' it would have done credit to an exhibition", "context_len": 86}}}, "dentists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was the same gas that dentists now give when they draw teeth ; but it was then used to make people merry and truthful , to make them laugh and say just what they thought", "context_len": 36}}}, "harrowed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "My boy was too young to know whether it did either ; but he was exactly the right age , when on another night there was a large picture of Death on a Pale Horse shown , to be harrowed to the bottom of his soul by its ghastliness", "context_len": 49}}}, "arguing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "When he was much older , his father urged him to go to the court-house and hear the great Corwin , whose Mexican War speech he had learned so much of by heart , arguing a case ; but the boy was too bashful to go in when he got to the door , and came back and reported that he was afraid they would make him swear", "context_len": 68}}}, "explored": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 15, "context": "Shelly Beach came to be a familiar spot , the grove was more than once explored , the drives up and down the coast became old acquaintances , while Cap'n Si grew to be as well known as any member of Jennie 's family", "context_len": 44}}}, "spire": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "It was the Episcopal church , and he struggled for some meaning in the word Episcopal ; he knew that the Seceder church was called so because the spire was cedar ; a boy who went to Sunday-school there told him so", "context_len": 42}}}, "cedar": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 45, "context": "He strolled about the open space , pausing now and then to survey through his glasses the snowy peaks which towered far into the sky to the westward , or at the lower hills to the north , where the gnarled pine , the dwarfed cedar , the rushing torrents and the gorges made up one of the many wild regions which abound over thousands of square miles of area", "context_len": 70}}}, "roughcast": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The store had a high , sharp gable to the street , and showed its timbers through the roughcast of its wall , which was sprinkled with broken glass that glistened in the sun", "context_len": 34}}}, "sprinkled": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "At first Jetty did not even have power to shake himself , but before many minutes , his dripping coat was freed of many drops of water , which freely sprinkled the girls , who laughing ran at a safe distance , and then Reliance wrapped him up in her jersey and carried him away from the scene of his late disaster", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "[ Footnote 600 : Reshoush ( for reshash ) , `` anything sprinkled , '' i", "context_len": 16}}}, "glistened": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "Aunt Bessie 's own eyes glistened as she lifted the sobbing child upon her lap , while Maggie stood beside her , holding Bessie 's hand in one of her own , and with her pocket-handkerchief wiping the tears that streamed from her little sister 's eyes", "context_len": 47}}}, "shifted": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 56, "context": "Had Ben set the fire at first along the whole line of the brush , there would have been burnt ground between him and the mass of fire , which would have cut off the communication , and he would have been safe ; but he set it on one corner , and when the wind shifted , the flame driven by it dried the moisture from the grass , and made rapid progress towards the house , while a large strip of dry grass made a bridge for the fire to travel on", "context_len": 94}}}, "dealer": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then the boys found another toy store ; but they considered the dealer mean ; he asked very high prices , and he said , when a boy hung back from buying a thing that it was `` a very superior article , '' and the boys had that for a by-word , and they holloed it at the storekeeper 's boy when they wanted to plague him", "context_len": 68}}}, "bakeries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There were two bakeries , and at the American bakery there were small sponge-cakes , which were the nicest cakes in the world , for a cent apiece ; at the Dutch bakery there were pretzels , with salt and ashes sticking on them , that the Dutch boys liked ; but the American boys made fun of them , and the bread at the Dutch bakery was always sour", "context_len": 70}}}, "pretzels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "There were two bakeries , and at the American bakery there were small sponge-cakes , which were the nicest cakes in the world , for a cent apiece ; at the Dutch bakery there were pretzels , with salt and ashes sticking on them , that the Dutch boys liked ; but the American boys made fun of them , and the bread at the Dutch bakery was always sour", "context_len": 70}}}, "yeast": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The boys went to the brewery , to get yeast for their mothers ; and they liked to linger among the great heaps of malt , and the huge vats wreathed in steam , and sending out a pleasant smell", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "They took a boy 's bottle and filled it with foaming yeast , and then took his cent , all in a silence so oppressive that he scarcely dared to breathe", "context_len": 31}}}, "malt": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The boys went to the brewery , to get yeast for their mothers ; and they liked to linger among the great heaps of malt , and the huge vats wreathed in steam , and sending out a pleasant smell", "context_len": 40}}}, "vats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "The boys went to the brewery , to get yeast for their mothers ; and they liked to linger among the great heaps of malt , and the huge vats wreathed in steam , and sending out a pleasant smell", "context_len": 40}}}, "wreathed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 74, "context": "Who but she could climb to the very highest branch of the tallest tree ? Who but she could swing so high that she seemed almost to turn a somersault in the air before she came down again ? Who but she could invent the most daring games ? And then , when all other things failed , who but she could tell such weird stories ? Her eyes shone ; her lips were wreathed in smiles", "context_len": 77}}}, "steam": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 24, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "One day when she went out to the Vanguard that vessel was getting up steam ready to go away , having received sudden orders to put out to sea", "context_len": 29}}}, "vapory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The floors were always wet , and the fat , pale Dutchmen , working about in the vapory air , never spoke to the boys , who were afraid of them", "context_len": 31}}}, "foaming": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 75, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "Over an hour passed before they brought the refractory animals back , and an exciting time the boys had watching the race , and shouting and hurrahing when the foaming , panting creatures rushed into the camp , followed by their almost breathless pursuers", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was the foaming water , there the black rocks , that were swept over by the roaring wave , but now showing plainly amidst a sheet of white surf , with beyond them a comparatively smooth surface , through which a current seems to run", "context_len": 46}}}, "oppressive": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "Our task was rendered doubly difficult , first by the oppressive heat , and secondly by the fact that , like the Mediterranean , these waters are practically tideless , so that the difference between the rise and fall can be measured by the span of a man \u2019 s hand", "context_len": 51}}}, "fronts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "When there was no fire , the companies sometimes pulled them up through the town to the Basin bank , and practised with them against the roofs and fronts of the pork-houses", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Indeed , who knows of a more inspiriting sight anywhere than Eton College on an April morning ? The quaint old buildings seem to bask in the broad spring sunshine ; the trees that dot the grass-bare turf where the Upper School fronts the street are already casting tiny leaf-shadows , and on the other side , where the garden slopes down to the Thames , many a little branch and bush begins to glow with color", "context_len": 77}}}, "firemen": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 22, "context": "The cadet had seen enough of the fire-room force , a hundred and fifty strong , to know that the coal-passers and firemen were as brutal and disorderly men ashore as could be found in the slums of a great seaport", "context_len": 41}}}, "fireman": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 9, "context": "My boy would have liked to speak to a fireman , but he never dared ; and the foreman of the Neptune , which was the larger and feebler of the engines , was a figure of such worshipful splendor in his eyes that he felt as if he could not be just a common human being", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It will serve as a sample of the dangers which beset the fireman daily in the pursuit of his duty", "context_len": 20}}}, "foreman": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 18, "context": "My boy would have liked to speak to a fireman , but he never dared ; and the foreman of the Neptune , which was the larger and feebler of the engines , was a figure of such worshipful splendor in his eyes that he felt as if he could not be just a common human being", "context_len": 57}}}, "feebler": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "My boy would have liked to speak to a fireman , but he never dared ; and the foreman of the Neptune , which was the larger and feebler of the engines , was a figure of such worshipful splendor in his eyes that he felt as if he could not be just a common human being", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "She struggled , but each struggle was feebler than the last", "context_len": 11}}}, "glove": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 52, "context": "When you went to Scotland first , you and he were of one mind , one heart , but when you came hither again , your head was stuffed full of worldly wisdom and time-serving devices , talking of a Lord Protector instead of the glory of God , and hand and glove with that cruel Cromwell who did throw my saint into prison ! Your heart was turned from those that reared you , and given to their enemies ! And now you can stand by unmoved and see him you once loved haled to prison and death ! ''", "context_len": 101}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Edna sat very close to her mother on the sofa , her hand stroking the smooth kid glove she wore", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` And the glove money too , mamma ? '' asked Maggie", "context_len": 12}}}, "trumpet": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 19, "context": "It did not make any difference how close he was to the men , he shouted everything through the trumpet ; and when they manned the breaks and began to pump , he roared at them , `` Down on her , down on her , boys ! '' so that you would have thought the Neptune could put out the world if it was burning up", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "I remember that I was shoeless , along with the greater part of my command , though the weather was bitter cold , and my feet were bleeding , and yet when I heard that trumpet voice , ordering us from the wagons to make one more stand , I never thought of my feet", "context_len": 55}}}, "manned": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 23, "context": "As we sat down to partake , of it together , Ama informed me that one of her father 's largest canoes , manned by forty paddlers , and commanded by a chief whom she had recognised , had passed slowly down the river about an hour after I had composed myself to rest , the chief in charge intently scrutinising both banks , as they went , evidently in search of some indication of our presence , and had finally passed out of sight to the southward ; after which Ama had descended and taken a few hours ' rest", "context_len": 101}}}, "splutter": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Instead of that there was usually a feeble splutter from the nozzle , and sometimes none at all , even if the hose did not break ; it was fun to see the hose break", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The old man continued to splutter and snarl and flourish his cane , upon which Tuttle and Cooper made a pretense of skurrying around in great haste for rocks to pelt him with , and he beat a hasty retreat toward his wretched hovel", "context_len": 44}}}, "nozzle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Instead of that there was usually a feeble splutter from the nozzle , and sometimes none at all , even if the hose did not break ; it was fun to see the hose break", "context_len": 35}}}, "hose": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` I am going to bring up the water-barrel , pump it full from the stream , fit the biggest hose to it , and let fly into the fort", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The hose played upon besieged and besiegers alike , and all the combatants were being drenched to the skin", "context_len": 19}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I mean to hose Dodds out of the fort", "context_len": 10}}}, "efficiency": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "It is very strikingly characteristic of the mingling of poetic sentiment and enthusiasm with calm and calculating business efficiency , which shone conspicuously so often in Alexander 's career , that when he arrived at Sestos , and found that the ships were there , and the army safe , and that there was no enemy to oppose his landing on the Asiatic shore , he left Parmenio to conduct the transportation of the troops across the water , while he himself went away in a single galley on an excursion of sentiment and romantic adventure", "context_len": 96}}}, "fostered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "The Neptune was a favorite with the boys , though they believed that the Tremont could squirt farther , and they had a belief in its quiet efficiency which was fostered by its reticence in public", "context_len": 36}}}, "reticence": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "The Neptune was a favorite with the boys , though they believed that the Tremont could squirt farther , and they had a belief in its quiet efficiency which was fostered by its reticence in public", "context_len": 36}}}, "gilding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was small and black , but the Neptune was large , and painted of a gay color lit up with gilding that sent the blood leaping through a boy 's veins", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The great hall is floored and lined with oak , while the chapel -- the Priory Church the people call it -- excels for limning and gilding , as well as for the beauty of its tapestry , any church in this part of Mercia", "context_len": 45}}}, "sensibilities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Before the Hydraulic was opened , the pork-houses were the chief public attraction to the boys , and they haunted them , with a thrilling interest in the mysteries of pork-packing which none of their sensibilities revolted from", "context_len": 38}}}, "revolted": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 43, "context": "They had been the staunchest Indian supporters of the new British colony , and had sent 28 men of auxiliary troops to Colonel Barnwell , to defeat the Tuscarora insurrection on the coast of North Carolina ( 1712-13 ) , when they suddenly revolted on April 15th , 1715 , committed the most atrocious deeds against helpless colonists , and showed themselves to be quite the reverse of what their name indicates ( y\u00e1masi , y\u00e1massi , the Creek term for mild , gentle , peaceable [ 44 ] )", "context_len": 90}}}, "factories": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was not the constant clatter of machinery and the rasping of dozens of circular saws in the adjoining factories that kept him awake , but the excitement of the day , culminating in the discovery of his elder brother , whom he had regarded as dead for months past", "context_len": 50}}}, "eclipsed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "Nancy 's hat was a conspicuous object among the smart throng , for Lilian , after one peep at the fair , had charitably kept house herself , and allowed her hand-maiden to take a holiday , and the damsel seemed to have collected so many admirers that the luckless Sally was entirely eclipsed", "context_len": 54}}}, "hardship": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 22, "context": "But , after a few blows had been exchanged , Dick 's rising temper supplied the strength that had been lessened by hardship , while Astbury , unwieldy and out of condition , soon lost his breath , and , hitting out wildly , gave Dick an opening for a good straight left-hander , that sent his opponent crashing on the ground", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Of course it was considered extremely important that the child destined to be a general should become robust and powerful in constitution from his earliest years , and that he should be inured to hardship and fatigue", "context_len": 37}}}, "quieted": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 40, "context": "My boy 's brother was so crazy to try mill-life that he was allowed to do so for a few weeks ; but a few weeks were enough of it , and pretty soon the feeling about the mills all quieted down , and the boys contented themselves with their flumes and their wheel-pits , and the head-gates that let the water in on the wheels ; sometimes you could find fish under the wheels when the mills were not running", "context_len": 81}}}, "flumes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "My boy 's brother was so crazy to try mill-life that he was allowed to do so for a few weeks ; but a few weeks were enough of it , and pretty soon the feeling about the mills all quieted down , and the boys contented themselves with their flumes and their wheel-pits , and the head-gates that let the water in on the wheels ; sometimes you could find fish under the wheels when the mills were not running", "context_len": 81}}}, "forbidding": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The mill-doors all had `` No Admittance '' painted on them ; and the mere sight of the forbidding words would have been enough to keep my boy away , for he had a great awe of any sort of authority ; but once he went into the mill to see his brother ; and another time he and some other boys got into an empty mill , where they found a painter on an upper floor painting a panorama of `` Paradise Lost", "context_len": 84}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 46, "context": "By degrees , though , the cloud that had been over his understanding seemed to float away , and as they were nearing the harbour he began to recall the urgings he had received to leap from Carn Du , which now stood up black and forbidding on his left ; the swim out to the lugger and round ; and then -- '' Well , how do you feel now , lad ? '' said the master", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "In the early morning , before the activities of the day have begun , this wharf is a deserted and forbidding place , and on one such early morning in September , with chill air and cloudy skies , and not even a rose tint in the dull east , there was no one to be seen throughout the whole length of the wharf save one slowly moving boy", "context_len": 69}}}, "panorama": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 38, "context": "Then , going into the ruins , she climbed up on to the old wall , from which the best view of the house and its surroundings could be obtained , and gazed with new eyes at the panorama of her home ; and as her glance travelled slowly from orchard to stackyard , from meadow to garden , back to the ivy-clad tower and the red gabled roof , ' I think , ' she said slowly , 'that if we had to leave the Abbey , it would break my heart ! '", "context_len": 95}}}, "disputed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` This masterpiece must have been several hundred feet long ; the boys disputed whether it would reach to the sawmill they could see from the windows if it was stretched out ; and my boy was surprised by the effects which the painter got out of some strips of tinsel which he was attaching to the scenery of the lake of fire and brimstone at different points", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It is a disputed fact whether they ever observed the custom of flattening their children 's heads , like some of their neighbors", "context_len": 23}}}, "tinsel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` This masterpiece must have been several hundred feet long ; the boys disputed whether it would reach to the sawmill they could see from the windows if it was stretched out ; and my boy was surprised by the effects which the painter got out of some strips of tinsel which he was attaching to the scenery of the lake of fire and brimstone at different points", "context_len": 68}}}, "attaching": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` This masterpiece must have been several hundred feet long ; the boys disputed whether it would reach to the sawmill they could see from the windows if it was stretched out ; and my boy was surprised by the effects which the painter got out of some strips of tinsel which he was attaching to the scenery of the lake of fire and brimstone at different points", "context_len": 68}}}, "scenery": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 55, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "infernal": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 22, "context": "They figured it out that all hands on deck would be pulled over to the port side and kept there by their infernal row , while their pals dumped the tobacco out of the starboard side", "context_len": 36}}}, "paints": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "On Sunday afternoons she would take us up to her room , in order that my Mother might rest in peace from the children ; and there we always spent a delightful time , looking over her dressing-case with its treasures , and listening to the histories of each trinket and curiosity , or messing with her paints", "context_len": 58}}}, "favorable": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 54, "context": "Two sets of mail bags were approaching each other from points two thousand miles apart , and there were times when this approach was at the astounding speed of forty and even fifty miles an hour ! The average daily rate was two hundred and fifty miles a day , but where everything was favorable , or when an express rider was fleeing from the vengeful red men , his pony struck a gait of twenty-five miles and maintained it , when an untrained horse would have dropped in his tracks", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was a night favorable for Indian cunning to do its work", "context_len": 12}}}, "edifice": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 24, "context": "Besides the bridge , the school-houses , the court-house and jail , the port-houses and the mills , there was only one other public edifice in their town that concerned the boys , or that they could use in accomplishing the objects of their life , and this was the hall that was built while my boy could remember its rise , for public amusements", "context_len": 65}}}, "accomplishing": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 39, "context": "Besides the bridge , the school-houses , the court-house and jail , the port-houses and the mills , there was only one other public edifice in their town that concerned the boys , or that they could use in accomplishing the objects of their life , and this was the hall that was built while my boy could remember its rise , for public amusements", "context_len": 65}}}, "mesmerism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "It was in this hall that he first saw a play , and then saw so many plays , for he went to the theatre every night ; but for a long time it seemed to be devoted to the purposes of mesmerism", "context_len": 43}}}, "hypnotism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "A professor highly skilled in that science , which has reappeared in these days under the name of hypnotism , made a sojourn of some weeks in the town , and besides teaching it to classes of learners who wished to practise it , gave nightly displays of its wonders", "context_len": 50}}}, "learners": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "The other boys did not ask how he came to know its name , or where he got it ; boys never ask anything that it would be well for them to know ; but they accepted his theory , and his further statement that it was of a mildness singularly adapted to learners , without misgiving", "context_len": 57}}}, "nightly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "A professor highly skilled in that science , which has reappeared in these days under the name of hypnotism , made a sojourn of some weeks in the town , and besides teaching it to classes of learners who wished to practise it , gave nightly displays of its wonders", "context_len": 50}}}, "mesmerized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` How would you know that you were mesmerized , and how would you help yourself ? ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "mockingly": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 37, "context": "For when she encased herself in cold silence , and stalked home with lifted head and unseeing eyes , often a little throng of Mexican children would walk behind her , imitating her stately gait and calling mockingly , `` Ea ! ea ! See the madamisela ! See the princess ! She is sister to the king -- that one ! Vah ! vah ! vah ! ''", "context_len": 69}}}, "dreamlike": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "I finish this off in Montreal , a very handsome , thriving-looking city , with far grander buildings than Quebec : but it wants the dreamlike , exquisite beauty of its sister", "context_len": 32}}}, "mulberries": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "labyrinth": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Besides this attraction , there was a labyrinth , or puzzle , as the boys called it , of paths that wound in and out among bushes , so that when you got inside you were lucky if you could find your way out", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "There was nothing of an alarming character in sight , and therefore , as soon as I had descended to the ground , we both set out for the spot where we had hidden our canoe , launched her , and made our way out through the labyrinth of mangrove roots to the margin of the river , where we lay perdu until the darkness had completely fallen , when we boldly pushed off into the strength of the current , and steadily pursued our way", "context_len": 86}}}, "perish": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 73, "context": "or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing his victims to the tree with spike nails driven through the hands and feet into the tough timber", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "His bold curious eyes let nothing pass unnoticed , yet when the torture of the half-dead victim was ended even his lips were somewhat white , though he shouted and waved his hat with the loyal rabble who cheered and cheered again at the headsman 's final speech : `` So perish all King Charles 's enemies", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "They raised the neighborhood , and followed the horse \u2019 s tracks to William Griffin \u2019 s door , and then it got dark , and they lost them ; however , they hunted in the slough holes , and all about , a good part of the night , for it was cold , and they knew if he laid out he \u2019 d perish", "context_len": 66}}}, "saloon": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 70, "context": "When with Chris it was forward clear to the bow to lean over the rail and see the magnificent prow cut the water ; or way to the stern , to watch the far-shining train , the screws churned into white foam behind them ; or an hour 'midships , where the ever-varying amusements with which the steerage passengers beguile the weary hours can be looked down upon from the saloon deck of either first or second cabin", "context_len": 78}}}, "luxuries": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 7, "context": "Peggy grew up without many of the luxuries which fall to the lot of richer girls , but she was none the worse for that , and I think , when she saw how bored and uninterested Maud Middleton was in the midst of her round of London society and foreign travel , she felt that it is sometimes better to have the capacity for enjoyment than to have the means without the happy talent of being able to appreciate your good things", "context_len": 83}}}, "arising": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 96, "context": "Jerusalem , what is this ! bellowed a voice , and Joe Griffin leaped out from the midst of the burning pile ; the brands rolled off the back of his woollen shirt , which was thoroughly singed , while a fox-skin cap he wore was scorched to a crisp , as was his hair ; he ran round and round , as though he was mad , blowing his fingers ( which where slightly burned ) , and slapping them on his thighs , while on his face was a mingled expression of pain , arising from the burn , and anger at being outwitted", "context_len": 106}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "\u2019 Rather curiously , she spoke of herself in old age as having been when young subject to very low spirits \u2019 ; or more strictly , she said that she would have been so subject , but for the counteracting influences of religion \u2019 and work , \u2019 the latter arising from the former", "context_len": 55}}}, "afforded": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 37, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "unjust": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 68, "context": "He had done what it must have been harder to do than to stand out against it ; he had publicly owned that he was mistaken in regard to Taylor , who had not become the tool of the slaveholders , but had obeyed the highest instincts of the party and served the interests of freedom , though he was himself a slaveholder and the hero of an unjust war", "context_len": 70}}}, "omnibus": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "I dare say it was of the size of the ordinary city omnibus , but it looked as large to the boys then as a Pullman car would look to a boy now ; and they assembled for its arrivals and departures with a thrill of civic pride such as hardly any other fact of the place could impart", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` See here , '' she said , `` I 'll put ye all into the crib , and there ye may play omnibus till I come back", "context_len": 28}}}, "arrivals": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "I dare say it was of the size of the ordinary city omnibus , but it looked as large to the boys then as a Pullman car would look to a boy now ; and they assembled for its arrivals and departures with a thrill of civic pride such as hardly any other fact of the place could impart", "context_len": 59}}}, "departures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "I dare say it was of the size of the ordinary city omnibus , but it looked as large to the boys then as a Pullman car would look to a boy now ; and they assembled for its arrivals and departures with a thrill of civic pride such as hardly any other fact of the place could impart", "context_len": 59}}}, "impart": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "illusion": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "It was this furious speed which gave the child his earliest illusion of trees and fences racing by while the stage seemed to stand still", "context_len": 25}}}, "pave": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "When it started back , he thought it would never get off the city pave and out from between its lines of houses into the free country", "context_len": 27}}}, "commerce": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 50, "context": "My brother has already spoken of you to me , Sir Gervaise , the viscount said , when the young knight was presented to him ; although indeed there was no occasion for him to do so , since the name of the knight who two years ago saved the commerce of Italy from ruin , and with a single galley destroyed or captured a great fleet of over twenty Barbary pirates , and thus for a time put a stop to the depredations of the infidels , is known throughout Europe", "context_len": 92}}}, "princelier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Nearly all the houses had vegetable gardens , and some of them had flower-gardens that appeared princelier pleasaunces to my boy than he has ever seen since in Europe or America", "context_len": 31}}}, "nasturtiums": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "Very likely they were not so vast or so splendid as they looked to him then ; but one of them at least had beds of tulips and nasturtiums , and borders of flags and pinks , with clumps of tiger-lilies and hollyhocks ; and in the grassy yard beside it there were high bushes full of snow-balls , and rose-trees with moss-roses on them", "context_len": 65}}}, "pinks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "' I suppose not , ' said Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner", "context_len": 128}}}, "superb": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "In this superb domain there were two summer-houses and a shed where bee-hives stood ; at the end of the garden was a bath-house , and you could have a shower-bath , if you were of a mind to bring the water for it from the pump in the barn-yard", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Costly robes , delicate embroidery , superb carpets , shawls , goldsmiths ' work , and no small amount of jewels , were among the spoil collected , and the bulk of the merchandise captured was , two days later , despatched in galleys to Genoa and Marseilles , to be sold for the benefit of the Order", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "It was all so vivid and Marilla made such pretty gestures with her hands and swayed her head to and fro , that they could fairly see the palace , and the banquet was superb with its lights and flowers and beautiful adornments", "context_len": 43}}}, "unequalled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "But this was all on a scale of unequalled magnificence ; and most of the houses , which were mostly of wood , just had a good big yard with plum-trees and cherry-trees in it ; and a vegetable garden at one side that the boy hated to weed", "context_len": 49}}}, "weed": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "Whit Monday was a holiday at school , however , and Peggy and Bobby , having the day at home , took a sudden fit of industry , and started to weed the shrubbery with the noble intention of having it raked over and tidied by teatime , being put somewhat on their mettle by Father 's remarks on the subject of sustained labour , and his laughing incredulity when they assured him he would find it all neatly finished when he returned from Warford that evening", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 67, "context": "63 : `` They live in the centre of an uneven and large nitrous savannah ; have in it one town , long one mile and a half , very narrow and irregular ; this they divide into seven ( towns ) , by the names of Melattaw 'hat and feather , ' Chatelaw 'copper town , ' Chukafalaya 'long town , ' Tuckahaw ' a certain weed , ' Ashuck hooma 'red grass", "context_len": 74}}}, "arbors": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Chief Chicote informs me , that the names in question were distributed by the `` beloved men '' or ist'-ats\u00e1kalgi while sitting in their cabins or arbors on two opposite sides of the square", "context_len": 34}}}, "currants": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "They were delicious grapes ; and there were black currants , which the grandfather liked , because he had liked them when he was a boy himself in the old country , but which no Boy 's Town boy could have been induced to take as a gracious gift", "context_len": 49}}}, "plots": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "Perhaps Maggie and Fred might not have kept their plots in such good order , or worked so perseveringly at weeding , raking , and watering , had it not been for Harry and Bessie , who never forgot to go each day and look at their plants , and never suffered a weed to do more than show the tip of its head above the soil", "context_len": 67}}}, "tolerably": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 10, "context": "My prison -- if such it was -- was a tolerably spacious affair , measuring about twenty feet long by fifteen feet wide , and it was absolutely empty ; also , there being no windows to the building , and the light entering only by the open door , the obscurity , on entering , seemed profound , although a few minutes sufficed to enable one 's eyes to grow accustomed to it , when , at least during daylight , it was possible to see clearly enough for all practical purposes", "context_len": 93}}}, "mischance": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "Alaeddin , seeing her with the dish in her hand , knew that most like some mischance had betided her , but cared not to question her till she entered and setting down the dish , told him what had passed and finally said to him , `` God be praised , O my son , I mustered courage to find myself a place in the Divan , albeit I could not win to speak with the Sultan to day ; but to-morrow , an it please God the Most High , I will bespeak him", "context_len": 96}}}, "interiors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Yet those interiors were not of an oppressive grandeur , and one was much like another", "context_len": 16}}}, "ingrain": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The parlor had what was called a flowered-carpet or gay pattern of ingrain on its floor , and the other rooms had rag-carpets , woven by some woman who had a loom for the work , and dyed at home with such native tints as butternut and foreign colors as logwood", "context_len": 51}}}, "woven": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "And now what does the boy do but suddenly exchange his vertical position for one quite the reverse , and turn all his attention to the carpet ; for did not Harold say it was woven in some special way on purpose ? Yes , sure enough ! here is the Cross of St", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 58, "context": "And her bosom throbbed at the sight of the little , stony face now lifted upon her from the dusk of the floor -- a face with a fierce gleam in its dark eyes , and clouded with a wild array of black hair in which was knotted and twisted a fantastic faja of green wool , narrowly woven", "context_len": 59}}}, "loom": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 44, "context": "Gorswen , having taken its annual holiday at Easter , made no account of Whit-week , and went on with its work as usual , for the agricultural labourer does not claim so much in the way of pleasure as his brethren of the loom or the forge , and is content with an occasional fair or village feast to break the monotony of his daily life", "context_len": 67}}}, "butternut": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "The parlor had what was called a flowered-carpet or gay pattern of ingrain on its floor , and the other rooms had rag-carpets , woven by some woman who had a loom for the work , and dyed at home with such native tints as butternut and foreign colors as logwood", "context_len": 51}}}, "fireplaces": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Fred and Charlie began now to search among the long grass for some traces of the Indian village , but found only charred wood , and stones which had formed rude fireplaces , blackened by smoke", "context_len": 36}}}, "housewife": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Within the little cottage all is as prim and dainty and neat as without , for the sweet-faced old housewife gives as close heed to the household as the gudeman of the house to the flock and the cow and the hedgerows", "context_len": 42}}}, "lamps": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 27, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}}}, "heating": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "There was an electric stove here and he soon had water heating and was making me the nicest mess of oatmeal gruel I ever tasted", "context_len": 25}}}, "enemies": {"freq": 170, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 170, "word_index": 32, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}}}, "strangest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "Among these politicians the Whigs were sacred in my boy 's eyes , but the Democrats appeared like enemies of the human race ; and one of the strangest things that ever happened to him was to find his father associating with men who came out of the Democratic party at the time he left the Whig party , and joining with them in a common cause against both", "context_len": 69}}}, "associating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Among these politicians the Whigs were sacred in my boy 's eyes , but the Democrats appeared like enemies of the human race ; and one of the strangest things that ever happened to him was to find his father associating with men who came out of the Democratic party at the time he left the Whig party , and joining with them in a common cause against both", "context_len": 69}}}, "joining": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 46, "context": "It is well said that no man is utterly bad , and perhaps he possessed domestic virtues which were thought unworthy of the attention of the chroniclers ; but as they picture him -- now prompting Ethelred to deeds of treachery against the Danes , now joining those Danes themselves , and surpassing them in cruelty -- now seeking pretended reconciliation , only to betray his foe more surely , and in all this aided and supported by the weak , unprincipled king -- as thus pictured there is scarcely a blacker character in history", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "It is a grievous pity that our elders all set their will against even the younger members of the community joining in a dance", "context_len": 24}}}, "statesman": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "It is always well to remember that the man who serves his country as a good citizen , as a soldier , as a statesman , or in any other walk of life , deserves our admiration as much as the missionary or the minister of the Gospel -- each and all such are servants of the great King", "context_len": 59}}}, "keystones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "There were other people in that town who wanted the whole sidewalk , and these were the professional drunkards , whom the boys regarded as the keystones , if not corner-stones , of the social edifice", "context_len": 36}}}, "slavish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I do not think any boy realized the tragedy of those hopeless , wasted , slavish lives", "context_len": 17}}}, "plagued": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 58, "context": "Now , Miss Adams always said that she was afraid of nobody , and did not care what people said of her ; but as she watched the delicate little child , who she knew had been brought by her parents to the sea-shore that she might gain health and strength , she felt sorry that she had plagued her so , and thought that she would like to make it up with her", "context_len": 74}}}, "outcasts": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "Some of the town-drunkards were the outcasts of good homes , which they had desolated , and some had merely destroyed in themselves that hope of any home which is the light of heaven in every human heart ; but from time to time a good man held out a helping hand to one of them , and gave him the shelter of his roof , and tried to reclaim him", "context_len": 71}}}, "desolated": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Some of the town-drunkards were the outcasts of good homes , which they had desolated , and some had merely destroyed in themselves that hope of any home which is the light of heaven in every human heart ; but from time to time a good man held out a helping hand to one of them , and gave him the shelter of his roof , and tried to reclaim him", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 72, "context": "Father Cuthbert , seated in a corner near the Yule log , with his brother-in-law and the Etheling , forgot all his apprehensions , and shared in the universal joy around him ; if his thoughts were sometimes with those who had once made Christmas bright to him -- if he thought of the bright-haired Bertric , who had been the soul of last Yuletide festivity at Aescendune , or of the desolated home there , he dismissed the subject from his mind at once , and suffered no hint to drop which could dim the mirth of his fellow guests", "context_len": 101}}}, "reclaim": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 69, "context": "Some of the town-drunkards were the outcasts of good homes , which they had desolated , and some had merely destroyed in themselves that hope of any home which is the light of heaven in every human heart ; but from time to time a good man held out a helping hand to one of them , and gave him the shelter of his roof , and tried to reclaim him", "context_len": 71}}}, "tempter": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "Then the boys saw him going about the streets , pale and tremulous , in a second-hand suit of his benefactor 's clothes , and fighting hard against the tempter that beset him on every side in that town ; and then some day they saw him dead drunk in a fence corner ; and they did not understand how seven devils worse than the first had entered in the place which had been swept and garnished for them", "context_len": 79}}}, "beset": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 126, "context": "or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length , as well because it is an important one as to show the almost insuperable difficulties that beset the", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "' I 'm that beset I dunno where I 'm standin ' , for Miss Hardin 's been as crooked as a snake fence , an ' as contrairy as a yearlin ' colt , an ' the childern dew train awful", "context_len": 42}}}, "garnished": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "The hostess who brought Dick 's bowl of onion broth at this moment destroyed his chance of feigning sleep , and he had to resign himself to endure his companion 's conversation which flowed on , garnished with oaths and cant phrases in three or four different languages , without any interruption , till by an unguarded movement Richard exposed his face to the light of the fire , and the stranger stared a moment , and then sprang up exclaiming , `` Body o ' me if it be not Measter Dick himself ! ''", "context_len": 96}}}, "dandies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Besides the town-drunkards there were other persons in whom the boys were interested , like the two or three dandies , whom their splendor in dress had given a public importance in a community of carelessly dressed men", "context_len": 38}}}, "impassable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "It was likely that ere long they might be called upon to stand a siege , but , acquainted as they were with the strength of the place -- its deep and seemingly impassable moat , its massive walls , and protecting towers and bastions -- it had seemed to them that Rhodes was capable of withstanding all assaults , however numerous the foe , however oft repeated the invasion", "context_len": 70}}}, "pantomime": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "The girls were all talking about parties at school , after the Christmas holidays , and I had n't been to a single one , or the pantomime either , and we never get to any of the concerts at the Spa Gardens", "context_len": 43}}}, "graphic": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 47, "context": "These tales are narrations of the exploits and adventures of certain great warriors at the siege of Troy -- a siege which lasted ten years -- and they are written with so much beauty and force , they contain such admirable delineations of character , and such graphic and vivid descriptions of romantic adventures , and picturesque and striking scenes , that they have been admired in every age by all who have learned to understand the language in which they are written", "context_len": 83}}}, "jaw": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 48, "context": "There was Charley Tuttle , called Chub for short , a roly-poly , round-faced , laughing chap , who was munching peanuts ; Tim Davis , nicknamed Spotty , even more freckled than Ben , thin-legged , sly-faced , and minus the two front teeth of his upper jaw ; Sam Rollins , a big , hulking , low-browed fellow , who lost no opportunity to bully smaller boys , generally known as Hunk ; Berlin Barker , a cold blond , rather good-looking , but proud and distant in his bearing ; and others who did not impress the new boy at all with their personalities", "context_len": 107}}}, "misbehaving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "For a dentist , he gave an upward twist of the hand from his jaw , and uttered a howl which left no doubt that he meant tooth-pulling ; and for what would happen to a boy if he kept on misbehaving , he crossed his fingers before his face and looked through them in a way that brought the jail-window clearly before the eyes of the offender", "context_len": 68}}}, "negroes": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 12, "context": "I tell you another thing you do , said the captain : negroes there use lots of baskets , that they carry on their heads , filled with oranges and other things ; they also use them in loading and unloading vessels , and sometimes they carry them by straps of green hide that go over their shoulders", "context_len": 58}}}, "fidelity": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 26, "context": "They had been grievously led away by Mahomet , whom Gervaise regarded as a false teacher ; but as he had seen innumerable instances of the fidelity of the Moslems to their creed , and the punctuality and devotion with which the slaves said their daily prayers , exposed though they were to the scorn and even the anger of their taskmasters , he had quite lost , during his nine months of constant association with Suleiman Ali , the bigoted hatred of Mohammedanism so universal at the time", "context_len": 89}}}, "swarthy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "I even found , when once our eyes met ( his grave and dark , mine , I daresay , bold enough and determined enough ) , that my heart beat fast , and the colour flew into my face ; then , strange to say , the colour came into his face , dying his swarthy cheek just for a moment , but leaving it the next paler than ever", "context_len": 71}}}, "darker": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 10, "context": "The men were swarthy , handsome fellows , not much darker than Spaniards , and they were so little afraid of the chances which were often such fatal mischances to colored people in that day that one of them travelled through the South , and passed himself in very good company as a Cherokee Indian of rank and education", "context_len": 59}}}, "mischances": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The men were swarthy , handsome fellows , not much darker than Spaniards , and they were so little afraid of the chances which were often such fatal mischances to colored people in that day that one of them travelled through the South , and passed himself in very good company as a Cherokee Indian of rank and education", "context_len": 59}}}, "mayors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Of mayors and magistrates , such as there must have been , they knew nothing , and they had not the least notion what the Whigs whom they were always trying to elect were to do when they got into office", "context_len": 41}}}, "magistrates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Of mayors and magistrates , such as there must have been , they knew nothing , and they had not the least notion what the Whigs whom they were always trying to elect were to do when they got into office", "context_len": 41}}}, "outcome": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 54, "context": "But when Gouroo whispered to my father , hinting at your incapacity , and suggesting that Mafuta should be sent for , my suspicions began to take definite shape , and , although I was not able to verify those suspicions , I finally made up my mind that the whole occurrence was the outcome of a plot between Gouroo and Mafuta -- your only enemies -- to ruin you", "context_len": 70}}}, "constabulary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "But though he was hail-fellow with every boy in town , and although every boy rejoiced in his impudence , he was so panoplied in the awfulness of his relation to the constabulary functions that , however remote it was , no boy would have thought of trifling with him when he was on duty", "context_len": 55}}}, "merciless": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "The sounds of the oars , the rattling of the chains of the miserable galley slaves , and the sharp crack of the whip of the merciless taskmaster could be distinctly heard , while ever and anon a gun would be fired , merely to impress upon us the fruitlessness of resistance", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Are they merciless as ever ? ''", "context_len": 8}}}, "crier": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 64, "context": "folk , making friends with them and questioning them of buying and selling and giving and taking and dear and cheap , till , one day of the days , he arose in the morning and donning his clothes , went forth , intending , as of wont , for the jewellers ' market ; but , as he went , he heard the crier proclaiming aloud on this wise , `` By commandment of the Lord of Beneficence , the king of the age and monarch of the time and the tide , let all the folk shut their shops and stores and enter their houses , for that the Lady Bedrulbudour , daughter of the Sultan , purposeth to go to the bath , and whoso", "context_len": 128}}}, "proclamation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "In the Boy 's Town the crier carried a good-sized bell ; when he started out he rang it till he reached the street corner , and then he stopped , and began some such proclamation as , `` O , yes ! O , yes ! O , yes ! There will be an auction this evening at early candle-light , at Brown & Robinson 's store ! Dry goods , boots and shoes , hats and caps , hardware , queen 's ware , and so forth , and so forth", "context_len": 93}}}, "hardware": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 80, "context": "In the Boy 's Town the crier carried a good-sized bell ; when he started out he rang it till he reached the street corner , and then he stopped , and began some such proclamation as , `` O , yes ! O , yes ! O , yes ! There will be an auction this evening at early candle-light , at Brown & Robinson 's store ! Dry goods , boots and shoes , hats and caps , hardware , queen 's ware , and so forth , and so forth", "context_len": 93}}}, "ware": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 84, "context": "In the Boy 's Town the crier carried a good-sized bell ; when he started out he rang it till he reached the street corner , and then he stopped , and began some such proclamation as , `` O , yes ! O , yes ! O , yes ! There will be an auction this evening at early candle-light , at Brown & Robinson 's store ! Dry goods , boots and shoes , hats and caps , hardware , queen 's ware , and so forth , and so forth", "context_len": 93}}}, "deputy": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 7, "context": "The first person called upon was the deputy sheriff , who , questioned by Lawyer Frances , stated that upon the previous night he was at Stickney \u2019 s store shortly after supper , where Bern Hayden found him and told him that there had been a robbery , adding the request that he should at once find Ben Stone , whom Hayden suspected , and search him", "context_len": 68}}}, "reckless": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 124, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "I accordingly fitted to my bow the stoutest arrow in my quiver , and dashed forward in a fury of rage and grief , absolutely reckless of consequences to myself , and animated by but one impulse -- the determination to slay the beast , whatever it might be , that had wrought this evil to my faithful and gentle companion", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` I did n't mean to do anything reckless", "context_len": 9}}}, "quips": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "This deputy was no other than that reckless boy who used to run out from the printing-office and shoot the turtle-doves ; and he decorated his proclamation with quips and quirks of his own invention , and with personal allusions to his employer , who was auctioneer as well as constable", "context_len": 51}}}, "quirks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "This deputy was no other than that reckless boy who used to run out from the printing-office and shoot the turtle-doves ; and he decorated his proclamation with quips and quirks of his own invention , and with personal allusions to his employer , who was auctioneer as well as constable", "context_len": 51}}}, "allusions": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 78, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}}}, "employer": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 6, "context": "I might tell him that my employer -- and I would mention some merchant whose name would carry weight with him -- has received from the interior a large consignment of slaves , among whom are three or four girls , who would fetch high prices in Egypt , and as he believes they have been captured from a tribe within the limits of the sultan 's territory , he is anxious to get rid of them , and will either dispose of them all cheaply in a lot , or will hand them over to him to take to Egypt to sell , giving him a large commission for carrying them there and disposing of them", "context_len": 117}}}, "auctioneer": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "This deputy was no other than that reckless boy who used to run out from the printing-office and shoot the turtle-doves ; and he decorated his proclamation with quips and quirks of his own invention , and with personal allusions to his employer , who was auctioneer as well as constable", "context_len": 51}}}, "panoplied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "But though he was hail-fellow with every boy in town , and although every boy rejoiced in his impudence , he was so panoplied in the awfulness of his relation to the constabulary functions that , however remote it was , no boy would have thought of trifling with him when he was on duty", "context_len": 55}}}, "awfulness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "But though he was hail-fellow with every boy in town , and although every boy rejoiced in his impudence , he was so panoplied in the awfulness of his relation to the constabulary functions that , however remote it was , no boy would have thought of trifling with him when he was on duty", "context_len": 55}}}, "functions": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "But though he was hail-fellow with every boy in town , and although every boy rejoiced in his impudence , he was so panoplied in the awfulness of his relation to the constabulary functions that , however remote it was , no boy would have thought of trifling with him when he was on duty", "context_len": 55}}}, "remote": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "But though he was hail-fellow with every boy in town , and although every boy rejoiced in his impudence , he was so panoplied in the awfulness of his relation to the constabulary functions that , however remote it was , no boy would have thought of trifling with him when he was on duty", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 123, "context": "seen service under Prince Rupert ; Arthur Conolly , an Irish veteran who had served in the Low Countries , and who had come over from Chichester for the occasion ; Arthur Lewis , a gentleman of Bramshott ; Percy Young , an officer of the navy , who in his earlier days had lost a leg in the action of La Rochelle ; Herbert Collings , a master mariner of Gosport , who used to be a frequent visitor at our house , and who greatly interested me with the account of his adventures off the coast of Barbary ; and Giles Perrin , the landlord of the `` Flying Bull '' , who modestly seated himself on a stool in a remote corner of the room", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The war was at present raging in the southern counties , but ever and anon the marauders made a forced march , and sacked some helpless town remote from the seat of war", "context_len": 33}}}, "gauger": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "As soon as the gauger had left them , he ran up to one of the barrels where he had seen a few drops fall from his instrument when he lifted it from the bunghole , and plunged the tip of his little finger into the whiskey , and then put it to his tongue", "context_len": 55}}}, "gauging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The boys knew just one other official , and that was the gauger , whom they watched at a respectful distance , when they found him employed with his mysterious instruments gauging the whiskey in the long rows of barrels on the Basin bank", "context_len": 44}}}, "irresistible": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 18, "context": "His delight in it all , and na\u00efve unconsciousness of anything unusual in his behavior , are altogether irresistible , and Harold and Marie-Celeste , after a whispered conference , decide to suspend Knight-of-the-Garter reminiscences for the time being , and make the tour of the castle with him", "context_len": 49}}}, "instrument": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 71, "context": "With some show of hesitation the man surrendered the thing , and upon inspection I found it to be a reed of about a foot in length , with a mouthpiece shaped something like that of a whistle , and with four small holes drilled in the length of the tube , whereby an expert performer might produce seven distinct tones ; but the tones were not consecutive , and the instrument was altogether a very poor and inefficient affair", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "All these years she was being prepared and made ready , till she should be as a sharpened instrument \u2019 in the Hand of her Master , fitted for the work which He would give her to do", "context_len": 38}}}, "bunghole": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "As soon as the gauger had left them , he ran up to one of the barrels where he had seen a few drops fall from his instrument when he lifted it from the bunghole , and plunged the tip of his little finger into the whiskey , and then put it to his tongue", "context_len": 55}}}, "shower": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 64, "context": "it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to find the poor creature ; so that , his hair being wet , he was a sight to behold when Harry pulled him out , covered with mud from", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "It was therefore of the greatest importance to burn this land , and get it into grass as soon as possible ; but Ben hesitated a long time , fearing that he might burn himself up , it was so dry , and hoping that a shower would come to wet the grass , so the fire would not run", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The whole troop of children had joined us , and what pet names they did shower on the happy animal", "context_len": 20}}}, "chew": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 49, "context": "One morning after a rain , when the boys were having fun in one of those open canal-boats with the loose planks which the over-night shower had set afloat , a fellow came up and said he had got some tobacco that was the best kind to learn to chew with", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "There was a tall and pious-looking man , and two or three civilians who had no particular points about them ; and then there was a burly man , who sat with his hands in his pockets and did nothing but chew tobacco and gaze in the fire , uttering not one word until some of the company fell to discussing Captain Leroy , the famous Union scout", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "arrears": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "If they had only smoked , it would have been something , but they did not even smoke ; and the boy felt that he had a long arrears of manliness to bring up , and that he should have to retrieve his family in spite of itself from the shame of not using tobacco in any form", "context_len": 58}}}, "manliness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` It shows what you are , '' answered Seabrooke , continuing his reproaches , instead of giving the straightforward answer which he considered unnecessary , `` that you have not the decent manliness to demand that which rightfully belonged to you because you were ashamed of your own folly and weakness , but must go and ransack in my quarters to find your money", "context_len": 65}}}, "retrieve": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "If they had only smoked , it would have been something , but they did not even smoke ; and the boy felt that he had a long arrears of manliness to bring up , and that he should have to retrieve his family in spite of itself from the shame of not using tobacco in any form", "context_len": 58}}}, "judged": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 1, "context": "He judged Harry Paul , in fact , by the same measure as he would have meted out to an enemy himself ; and so terrible were his thoughts , so horrifying to him was the thought of the death from which he had escaped , that he was robbed of all energy ; he had not strength to do more than hang there clinging to the weeds with desperate clutch , and , with only his head out of water , gaze up in Harry 's stern eyes", "context_len": 89}}}, "mildness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "The other boys did not ask how he came to know its name , or where he got it ; boys never ask anything that it would be well for them to know ; but they accepted his theory , and his further statement that it was of a mildness singularly adapted to learners , without misgiving", "context_len": 57}}}, "chewing": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 8, "context": "She looked lovingly at the cows , lazily chewing their cud in the sunshine ; she felt sorry for her step-mother , as she strove to woo slumber to Polly 's wakeful eyes with the same lullaby which had done duty for the whole six ; she even found it in her heart to kiss Lemuel , who , with his ready talent for the unusual , was busily cramming mud paste into the seams of the little trunk which held her worldly all", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The chewing and tearing up of these afforded him much sport , as did also the bare feet of his mistress , when she attempted to interfere", "context_len": 27}}}, "quid": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "He let the quid rest in his cheek as he had seen men do , when he was not crushing it between his teeth , and for some moments he poled his plank up and down the canal-boat with a sense of triumph that nothing marred", "context_len": 46}}}, "launching": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "However she said nothing , but got out her slim blue canoe , put a paddle in it , and casting a glance at Big Chief , who was launching a larger canoe and calling out directions to the younger children , who after all had been permitted to come", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "There was but one dry dock , and in it lay the Providence ; and on a slip , being nearly fit for launching , stood a large ship of seventy-six guns , her name having but recently been changed to the Royal Oak", "context_len": 44}}}, "acknowledged": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 33, "context": "We are all just now in a state of indignation about your pork ! Don \u2019 t suppose that it is any fault in the pork ; on the contrary , it is acknowledged to be the most refined pork ever known ; and Mother says that if she shut her eyes , she would not know that she was not eating chicken ! ! We had a beautiful roast of it one day at luncheon ; and Mother cut off a choice bit , to be reserved for our table , cold , while the servants were indulged with the rest of that joint", "context_len": 105}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "He was a general favorite with the small boys , always ready to assist or advise them , and an acknowledged leader with those of his own age", "context_len": 28}}}, "aptness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "He found it as sweet as he had been told it was , and he acknowledged the aptness of its name of molasses-tobacco ; it seemed to him a golden opportunity to acquire a noble habit on easy terms", "context_len": 39}}}, "crushing": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 56, "context": "I am quite sure that among the fifty knights , there is not one who does not feel well content that he has not only done his duty to the Order , but has gained a share in the credit and honour that will certainly be given to all who have taken a part in so crushing a defeat of the corsairs", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "At each of her guns on the starboard side were men lying prone on the deck , waiting for the signal to trice up the ports , run the guns out , and deliver a crushing broadside", "context_len": 37}}}, "poled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "He let the quid rest in his cheek as he had seen men do , when he was not crushing it between his teeth , and for some moments he poled his plank up and down the canal-boat with a sense of triumph that nothing marred", "context_len": 46}}}, "haggard": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "Meanwhile , one of those whom he strove in vain to forget for the time drew nearer and nearer ; a haggard figure , wan and worn by painful imprisonment , the garments dishevelled , the hair matted , the whole figure wild with excitement , he drew near the outer gate", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "At the call , the old crone ceased paddling for a moment and turned her haggard face in the direction of the tree", "context_len": 23}}}, "confession": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 95, "context": "and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say afterwards upon the danger of such heedless practical jokes", "context_len": 128}}}, "ague": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 17, "context": "This was a singularly fortunate and opportune discovery , for I had already observed that fever and ague were very prevalent among the inhabitants , and I hoped that if by means of a decoction of cinchona bark I could effect a cure , I might be able very materially to improve and strengthen my position in the town", "context_len": 59}}}, "guest": {"freq": 90, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 63, "context": "Good Parson Perrient had painted Providence plantation as a sort of paradise , where the liberty and toleration dreamt of by a few in England were the law for all ; but was that refuge open to him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought ,", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 36, "context": "When the table was all cleared , the black-haired girl who had been eyeing Dallas with much curiosity and a kind of serious interest , came to her father and said , `` Mother thinks our guest would like to be shown round the place", "context_len": 45}}}, "lapse": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}}}, "duly": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 3, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "I therefore at once applied for leave , and , having obtained it , secured a passage in a merchant vessel that was on the point of sailing for Liverpool , where I duly arrived after an uneventful passage of twenty-seven days", "context_len": 42}}}, "quinine": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "And here he was , practising among a few Mexicans and miners , tending their bruises , doling them out quinine , and taking pay of a dollar a month from every man , sick or well , enrolled on the mine books , and frequently getting nothing at all from such as were not therein enrolled", "context_len": 57}}}, "chills": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "For the space of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very", "context_len": 128}}}, "cosy": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 12, "context": "He preferred a sunny spot to have his chill in , a cosy fence-corner or a warm back door-step , or the like ; but as for the fever that followed the chill , he took no account of it whatever , or at least made no provision for it", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "The baby ceased its endless wailing , and with Jennie to keep things trim after they had once been put in order , the whole household put on its old air of cosy neatness", "context_len": 34}}}, "fevers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "The miasm which must have filled the air of the place from so many natural and artificial bodies of fresh water showed itself in low fevers , which were not so common as ague , but common enough", "context_len": 38}}}, "intermittent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The only long sickness that my boy could remember was intermittent fever , which seemed to last many weeks , and which was a kind of bewilderment rather than a torment", "context_len": 31}}}, "compensation": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 52, "context": "True , these men were his guests , and it might be considered that it was against his interests to denounce them , but he knew his claim for entertainment would be allowed him out of the funds found in possession of the party , with probably a liberal addition as a compensation for revealing their real character", "context_len": 58}}}, "affectionate": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 20, "context": "Bobby naturally regarded him with an admiration which bordered on worship , and trotted about at his heels like an affectionate poodle , while Peggy found herself living from Saturday to Saturday to continue the delightful series of projects which her enterprising friend lost no time in starting , and wondering sometimes how they had managed to exist before they knew him", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 18, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "She was very resolute ; very persevering ; very affectionate ; reserved , yet demonstrative ; untidy , yet methodical ; exceedingly anxious for the happiness of all around ; apt often to think people better than they really were ; generous to a fault ; unselfishly ready at all times to put her own wishes aside ; vehement and impulsive , yet never in a hurry or flurry ; unyielding , yet tender ; severe , yet frisky", "context_len": 79}}}, "petting": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "At another time nurse would have ordered them to shut their eyes and not speak another word ; but to-night she seemed to think it quite right and natural that they should be so very wide awake , and not only gave them an extra amount of petting and kissing , but told them stories of Uncle Ruthven 's pranks when he was a boy , and of his wonderful sayings and doings , till mamma , coming up and finding this going on , was half inclined to find fault with the old woman herself", "context_len": 96}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "Then what petting followed for little Roy ! what feeding him with the choicest bits ! until at last the little fellow , worn out from his walk and fit of crying , fell asleep in his sister 's arms", "context_len": 40}}}, "malady": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "You know how great is the mortality among the slaves -- how they pine away and die from no material malady that can be detected , but simply from hopelessness and weariness of life , aided , undoubtedly , in the case of the galley slaves , by sleeping in the damp night air after an exposure all day to the full heat of the sun", "context_len": 66}}}, "fans": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "She sits by his bed and fans him ; she smooths the pillow and turns its cool side up under his hot and aching head ; she cooks dainty dishes to tempt his sick appetite , and brings them to him herself", "context_len": 42}}}, "smooths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "She sits by his bed and fans him ; she smooths the pillow and turns its cool side up under his hot and aching head ; she cooks dainty dishes to tempt his sick appetite , and brings them to him herself", "context_len": 42}}}, "drifts": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 41, "context": "Just before the boys reached that phenomenon , the marks vanished altogether , coming to an abrupt stop in the snow , but it was evident that this was due to the wind covering the tracks with white crystals from the drifts , and not because the man had mysteriously vanished", "context_len": 51}}}, "yells": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 70, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}}}, "descends": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "He hears the yells and shouts of the boys in the street without a pang of envy or longing ; in his weakness , his helplessness , he becomes a gentle and innocent child again ; and heaven descends to him out of his mother 's heart", "context_len": 47}}}, "burdened": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "At this time he had a large family of little children , and he had got nothing beyond a comfortable living from the newspaper which he had published for eight years ; if he must give that up , he must begin life anew heavily burdened", "context_len": 46}}}, "publish": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 46, "context": "We have the most sure word of prophecy that the Day of the Lord is at hand ; therefore the persecuted remnant who do expect the coming of the Fifth and only Monarch , are even now assembled with their swords upon their thighs , to publish their glorious gospel and go forth conquering and to conquer", "context_len": 57}}}, "printing": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 53, "context": "I might track her easy , I suppose , but since she did n't seem to wish it to be known who she was or where she came from , I would n't feel it was showing my gratitude for the obligations she 's laid me under , and you see by the printing she do n't wish to be tracked even by her handwriting", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "Good-by , now , printing a hearty little kiss on his grateful face , and remember your promise ; and then , carefully lifting the tray , she sped back to the cottage , cheerily calling , Yes , I 'm coming , to Donald , who was on his way to meet her", "context_len": 54}}}, "acquiescence": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Rebellion against lawful authority , rank disobedience and deception were to be laid at Percy 's door , not to speak of the pitiable weakness which had suffered him to be led into this wrong , and the enormity of his at least passive acquiescence when Flagg had stolen Seabrooke 's letter ; still worse his own destruction of it , almost involuntary though it was", "context_len": 66}}}, "courageously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "The elder brother knew all the anxiety of the time , and shared it fully with the mother , whose acquiescence in what the father thought right was more than patient ; she abode courageously in the suspense , the uncertainty of the time ; and she hoped for something from the father 's endeavors in the different ways he turned", "context_len": 61}}}, "suspense": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 127, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}}}, "endeavors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "The elder brother knew all the anxiety of the time , and shared it fully with the mother , whose acquiescence in what the father thought right was more than patient ; she abode courageously in the suspense , the uncertainty of the time ; and she hoped for something from the father 's endeavors in the different ways he turned", "context_len": 61}}}, "introduce": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 46, "context": "We are going to have a fine day , Albert asserted , resuming his seat on the steps , and with the authority of one who knows ; and the matter of the weather being thus satisfactorily disposed of , Marie-Celeste made so bold as to introduce another subject ; and as it chanced to meet with Albert 's approval , they chatted merrily together for ever so long", "context_len": 69}}}, "inaction": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "At one time there was much talk in the family of using the fibre of a common weed in making paper , which he thought he could introduce ; perhaps it was the milk-weed ; but he could not manage it , somehow ; and after a year of inaction he decided to go into another newspaper", "context_len": 57}}}, "publicly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "He had done what it must have been harder to do than to stand out against it ; he had publicly owned that he was mistaken in regard to Taylor , who had not become the tool of the slaveholders , but had obeyed the highest instincts of the party and served the interests of freedom , though he was himself a slaveholder and the hero of an unjust war", "context_len": 70}}}, "tool": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "My first impulse was to carry the body down to where the soil was softer , and there dig a grave for it ; but while I was considering this plan , it occurred to me that , with no more efficient tool than a spear to serve as a shovel , it would be practically impossible for me to bury the body deep enough to protect it from the jackals and hyaenas ; and I therefore determined that , instead of burying it , I would burn it", "context_len": 89}}}, "slaveholders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "He had done what it must have been harder to do than to stand out against it ; he had publicly owned that he was mistaken in regard to Taylor , who had not become the tool of the slaveholders , but had obeyed the highest instincts of the party and served the interests of freedom , though he was himself a slaveholder and the hero of an unjust war", "context_len": 70}}}, "instincts": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 46, "context": "He had done what it must have been harder to do than to stand out against it ; he had publicly owned that he was mistaken in regard to Taylor , who had not become the tool of the slaveholders , but had obeyed the highest instincts of the party and served the interests of freedom , though he was himself a slaveholder and the hero of an unjust war", "context_len": 70}}}, "slaveholder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "He had done what it must have been harder to do than to stand out against it ; he had publicly owned that he was mistaken in regard to Taylor , who had not become the tool of the slaveholders , but had obeyed the highest instincts of the party and served the interests of freedom , though he was himself a slaveholder and the hero of an unjust war", "context_len": 70}}}, "elation": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 89, "context": "He acted , however , himself , in this high position , with great energy and with complete success ; and , at the same time , with all that modesty of deportment , and that delicate consideration for the officers under him -- who , though inferior in rank , were yet his superiors in age and experience -- which his position rendered proper , but which few persons so young as he would have manifested in circumstances so well calculated to awaken the feelings of vanity and elation", "context_len": 90}}}, "comparable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was a much larger place and was even considered a city , though it was not comparable to Cincinnati , so long the only known city in the world", "context_len": 30}}}, "compositor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "My boy was twelve years old by that time , and was already a swift compositor , though he was still so small that he had to stand on a chair to reach the case in setting type on Taylor 's inaugural message", "context_len": 43}}}, "inaugural": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "My boy was twelve years old by that time , and was already a swift compositor , though he was still so small that he had to stand on a chair to reach the case in setting type on Taylor 's inaugural message", "context_len": 43}}}, "stature": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Lord ha \u2019 mercy on me if \u2019 tis not young Master Wentworth ! '' he exclaimed , showing that , though grown in stature , I had not outgrown the appearance of my boyhood ; and when Drake told him of my newly found title , the old man tottered away to let the frequenters of the inn know the news ; whereat we , unwilling to tarry longer , pushed on towards the towering heights of Hindhead", "context_len": 80}}}, "demeanor": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "But what he lacked in stature he made up in gravity of demeanor ; and he got the name of `` The Old Man '' from the printers as soon as he began to come about the office , which he did almost as soon as he could walk", "context_len": 49}}}, "essay": {"freq": 64, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 51, "context": "It was Faith 's duty to make this room ready for breakfast ; and , as a rule , Warden would have been angry with her for its present state of neglect ; but this morning he said nothing , only when his eyes rested on the torn pieces of the essay he uttered a groan , and , stooping down , he picked them all up and put them in the grate", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Put your essay away for the present", "context_len": 7}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "Her father was seated by the table , the gas flaring high over his head ; he was trying laboriously to put some of his torn essay together ; he heard the movement at the door , but without looking up called out harshly -- '' Go away ; I ca n't be disturbed", "context_len": 54}}}, "disappointing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "Here was a disappointing beginning for these earnest young people ! At first it seemed quite probable they would not even be allowed to land ; and though permission was after a time obtained , yet in six weeks they were told they must go elsewhere , as they would not be permitted to settle", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Julia has few opportunities of seeing her family and he insists that I must not think of disappointing her on his account", "context_len": 22}}}, "sixth": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "His first attempt in literature , an essay on the vain and disappointing nature of human life , he set up and printed off himself in his sixth or seventh year ; and the printing-office was in some sort his home , as well as his school , his university", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 2, "context": "At the sixth shot the miserable villain made a feeble attempt to regain his former position , but ere he had ascended another two feet a shot struck him in the back of the head , and he tumbled to the bottom of the bank a hideously disfigured corpse", "context_len": 49}}}, "university": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "His first attempt in literature , an essay on the vain and disappointing nature of human life , he set up and printed off himself in his sixth or seventh year ; and the printing-office was in some sort his home , as well as his school , his university", "context_len": 50}}}, "humid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "He could no more remember learning to set type than he could remember learning to read ; and in after-life he could not come within smell of the ink , the dusty types , the humid paper , of a printing-office without that tender swelling of the heart which so fondly responds to any memory-bearing perfume : his youth , his boyhood , almost his infancy came back to him in it", "context_len": 72}}}, "responds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "He could no more remember learning to set type than he could remember learning to read ; and in after-life he could not come within smell of the ink , the dusty types , the humid paper , of a printing-office without that tender swelling of the heart which so fondly responds to any memory-bearing perfume : his youth , his boyhood , almost his infancy came back to him in it", "context_len": 72}}}, "chartered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Nothing consoled him but the fact that they were going all the way to the new place in a canal-boat , which his father chartered for the trip", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The latter , being a chartered humorist , signalled No", "context_len": 10}}}, "veil": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 64, "context": "that had happened to him , adding , `` And most like thou heardest the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to", "context_len": 128}}}, "realities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "His character was naturally more logical and less high strung than that of his hero ; and as the stern realities of life claimed the attention of the young soldier , the ecstatic glories of his uncle 's visions faded from his mind , his work absorbed and satisfied him , and he forgot to dream of ideal republics , or even of the Celestial City , in the practical interest of helping to conquer and to govern Scotland", "context_len": 79}}}, "outlines": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 48, "context": "He ceased to be that eager comrade he had once been ; sometimes he left his book with a sigh ; and he saw much of the outer world through a veil of fancies quivering like an autumn haze between him and its realities , softening their harsh outlines , and giving them a fairy coloring", "context_len": 56}}}, "coloring": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 55, "context": "He ceased to be that eager comrade he had once been ; sometimes he left his book with a sigh ; and he saw much of the outer world through a veil of fancies quivering like an autumn haze between him and its realities , softening their harsh outlines , and giving them a fairy coloring", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Oh , yes , mamma , I remember her ever so well , '' answered poor Maggie , coloring all over her face and neck , and looking as if the remembrance of Aunt Patty were a great distress", "context_len": 40}}}, "strutted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Upon which the rooster refused to eat at all , and after pecking one or two of the smaller chickens pretty severely , he strutted away with his neck stretched very straight , and expressing his displeasure in a loud and by no means pleasant voice", "context_len": 46}}}, "provisioned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "They were sufficiently provisioned without the game , however ; the mother had baked bread , and boiled a ham , and provided sugar-cakes in recognition of the holiday character of the voyage , and they had the use of the boat cooking-stove for their tea and coffee", "context_len": 48}}}, "breasts": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 19, "context": "But when to question after question Kitty Sharston gave a correct reply , the furore and excitement in the breasts of several of the spectators rose to the highest pitch , for Kitty 's soft voice , her gentle answers , her correct and lady-like utterances impressed everyone favorably", "context_len": 49}}}, "pressure": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 57, "context": "The Danes who had attacked the house of Herstan were so far in advance of their countrymen that they were forced to retreat instantly before the superior force which came to the rescue of the besieged ; and they fell back , at first in some order , but shortly , owing to the darkness and the pressure of their foes , in utter confusion", "context_len": 65}}}, "wider": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "It was wider here , and deeper , and ran faster towards the sea", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "Miss Forster was not sorry to find so plausible an excuse for keeping her pet nephew at the Willows , and the young man himself had no objections , being fuller than ever of ingenious schemes , only he had transferred the seat of his operations to the Abbey , as offering a wider scope in the way of material , and having the further advantage of a number of appreciative assistants", "context_len": 72}}}, "cataract": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 37, "context": "It was cool and dark there , for the trees grew low over the stream , and the water , escaping from the race above , poured down by the side of the wheel in a foaming cataract", "context_len": 38}}}, "seasickness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "They had made the whole forty miles in less than two days , and were all as well as when they started , without having suffered for a moment from seasickness", "context_len": 31}}}, "necessity": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 15, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}}}, "fellowships": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "The strangeness of the house and street , and the necessity of meeting the boys of the neighborhood , and paying with his person for his standing among them , kept my boy interested for a time , and he did not realize at first how much he missed the Boy 's Town and all the familiar fellowships there , and all the manifold privileges of the place", "context_len": 68}}}, "manifold": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "The strangeness of the house and street , and the necessity of meeting the boys of the neighborhood , and paying with his person for his standing among them , kept my boy interested for a time , and he did not realize at first how much he missed the Boy 's Town and all the familiar fellowships there , and all the manifold privileges of the place", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 61, "context": "Then he bade carry the slave-girls and their burdens to the pavilion of the Lady Bedrulbudour ; so the damsels took up the dishes and entered ; whereupon Alaeddin 's mother came forward and said to the Sultan , `` O my lord , this is no great matter for the Lady Bedrulbudour 's exalted rank ; nay , she deserveth manifold this", "context_len": 63}}}, "privileges": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 48, "context": "The strength he had shown in the struggle on shore , and the manner in which he had hurled , bound as he was , two of their comrades to the deck , had won for him the respect of his captors , and he was therefore allowed privileges not granted to the seamen of the vessel that had had the ill fortune to be cast on shore so close to the spot where the corsair was hiding", "context_len": 78}}}, "dearly": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 34, "context": "At the end of that time he roused himself from his stupor of grief and disappointment , and , abandoning the idea of any further attack upon the point that had cost him so dearly , he ordered the troops to move round and renew the attack upon the wall in front of the Jews ' quarter , and commence the construction of a battery on the edge of the great ditch facing the retrenchment behind the breach before effected", "context_len": 80}}}, "yonder": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 109, "context": "[ 354 ] On this wise she abode a week 's space [ 355 ] and the Sultan saw her at each Divan ; so , when she went on the last day [ of the week ] and stood , according to her wont , before the Divan , till it was ended , but could not muster courage to enter [ 356 ] or say aught , the Sultan arose and entering the harem , turned to his chief Vizier , who was with him , and said to him , `` O Vizier , these six or seven days [ 357 ] past I have seen yonder old woman come hither at every Divan and I note that she still carrieth somewhat under her veil", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then he asked Alaeddin , `` What is the cause that the lattice of yonder kiosk ( kushk ) is not complete ? '' The defective part is soon after referred to , no less than four times , as `` the lattice of the kiosk '' ( sheriyyetu ' l kushk ) , thus showing that , in the writer 's mind , kushk , liwan and shubbak were synonymous terms for the common Arab projecting square-sided window , made of latticework , and I have therefore rendered the three words , when they occur in this sense , by our English `` oriel , '' to whose modern meaning ( a window that juts out , so as to form a small apartment ) , they", "context_len": 128}}}, "heartache": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The first months there was a dumb heartache at the bottom of every pleasure and excitement", "context_len": 16}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Then he would listen to the words that would give him the information for which his soul yearned or else doom him once more to heartache and despair", "context_len": 28}}}, "apprentice": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then that dear friend of his brother and himself , the apprentice who knew all about `` Monte Cristo , '' came to work with them and live with them again , and that was a great deal ; but he did not bring the Boy 's Town with him ; and when they each began to write a new historical romance , the thought of the beloved scenes amidst which they had planned their first was a pang that nothing could assuage", "context_len": 83}}}, "assuage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 82, "context": "Then that dear friend of his brother and himself , the apprentice who knew all about `` Monte Cristo , '' came to work with them and live with them again , and that was a great deal ; but he did not bring the Boy 's Town with him ; and when they each began to write a new historical romance , the thought of the beloved scenes amidst which they had planned their first was a pang that nothing could assuage", "context_len": 83}}}, "cholera": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 4, "context": "During the summer the cholera came ; the milkman , though naturally a cheerful person , said that the people around where he lived were dying off like flies ; and the funerals , three and four , five and six , ten and twelve a day , passed before the door ; and all the brooding horror of the pestilence sank deep into the boy 's morbid soul", "context_len": 69}}}, "milkman": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "During the summer the cholera came ; the milkman , though naturally a cheerful person , said that the people around where he lived were dying off like flies ; and the funerals , three and four , five and six , ten and twelve a day , passed before the door ; and all the brooding horror of the pestilence sank deep into the boy 's morbid soul", "context_len": 69}}}, "funerals": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "During the summer the cholera came ; the milkman , though naturally a cheerful person , said that the people around where he lived were dying off like flies ; and the funerals , three and four , five and six , ten and twelve a day , passed before the door ; and all the brooding horror of the pestilence sank deep into the boy 's morbid soul", "context_len": 69}}}, "pestilence": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 60, "context": "During the summer the cholera came ; the milkman , though naturally a cheerful person , said that the people around where he lived were dying off like flies ; and the funerals , three and four , five and six , ten and twelve a day , passed before the door ; and all the brooding horror of the pestilence sank deep into the boy 's morbid soul", "context_len": 69}}}, "reconciling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "Was he afraid to die , she asked him , when he knew that heaven was so much better , and he would be in the care of such love as never could come to him on earth ? He could only gasp back that he was afraid to die ; and she could only turn from reconciling him with the other world to assuring him that he was in no danger of leaving this", "context_len": 75}}}, "rests": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "It seems to me that it is partly because death is hushed up and ignored between them that it rests such a burden on the soul ; but if children were told as soon as they are old enough that death is a part of nature , and not a calamitous accident , they would be somewhat strengthened to meet it", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` In 1763 the remnants , about eighty families , went to Havannah from their last possessions at Cayos Vacos and Cayo Hueso ( hueso , bone ) , where Romans saw the rests of their stone habitations ( p", "context_len": 40}}}, "calamitous": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "It seems to me that it is partly because death is hushed up and ignored between them that it rests such a burden on the soul ; but if children were told as soon as they are old enough that death is a part of nature , and not a calamitous accident , they would be somewhat strengthened to meet it", "context_len": 61}}}, "revisit": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "They all long to revisit their country and their homes , and to enjoy , for the remainder of their lives , the fruits of all their toils", "context_len": 28}}}, "figured": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 47, "context": "When supper was over , to satisfy Jimmy , Ben was compelled to tell about the football game , and this he did with such modesty that the listeners , who had not witnessed the contest , were given no inkling as to how conspicuously he had figured in it", "context_len": 50}}}, "dazzle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "He fancied that if the boys did not find him grown , they would find him somehow changed , and that he would dazzle them with the light accumulated by his residence in a city", "context_len": 35}}}, "accumulated": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 7, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "The whole world looked on with wonder to see such a course of conquest , pursued so successfully by so young a man , and with so small an army , gaining continual victories , as it did , over such vast numbers of foes , and making conquests of such accumulated treasures of wealth and splendor", "context_len": 57}}}, "residence": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 112, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}}}, "refusal": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "He went to all the places that were so dear to him ; but he felt in them the same kind of refusal , or reluctance , that he felt in the boys", "context_len": 33}}}, "bruised": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 65, "context": "Lem ran like a hare , and was out of sight among the trees in an instant ; while Dolly , finding the boys were gaining upon her , threw herself upon the ground when she came to the brow of a steep hill , and rolled over and over until she reached the foot , not heeding the stones which must have hurt and bruised her as she went", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "Lemuel burst into the room in riotous distress with a bruised knee , the result of his attempt to imitate the Prodigal Son , which had ended in an ignominious head-over-heels tumble into the midst of his swinish friends", "context_len": 39}}}, "unwashed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He at least was not changed ; he was as unwashed and as unkempt as ever ; but he seemed shy of my poor boy", "context_len": 25}}}, "affect": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 45, "context": "The man might have been mistaken for a circus performer , in his brilliant uniform , with plated horn , pistol , scabbard and belt , gay , flower-worked leggings , jingling spurs and fine boots with high heels , such as cowmen and rustlers affect", "context_len": 46}}}, "outsider": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The place that he had once had in their lives was filled ; he was an outsider , who might be suffered among them , but he was no longer of them", "context_len": 32}}}, "jovially": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "At tea-time his grandfather came home and gravely made him welcome ; the uncle who was staying with them was jovially kind", "context_len": 22}}}, "longingly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "Oh ! I did hope that he would soon get brown and ruddy like the other kiddies -- my own dear little lad , and I ventured to take a step into the room and stretched my head out longingly toward him", "context_len": 42}}}, "deaths": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 6, "context": "There had been a good many deaths from cholera during the day , and the grandfather prayed for grace and help amidst the pestilence that walketh in darkness and wasteth at noonday in such a way that the boy felt there would be very little of either for him unless he got home at once", "context_len": 55}}}, "prayed": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "There had been a good many deaths from cholera during the day , and the grandfather prayed for grace and help amidst the pestilence that walketh in darkness and wasteth at noonday in such a way that the boy felt there would be very little of either for him unless he got home at once", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 28, "context": "Then he took leave of the Sultan and going forth , mounted and rode , with his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like ,", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Moreover the Viziers and Amirs and officers of state and grandees of the realm invoked blessings on him and the Sultan gave him joy [ 502 ] and prayed God prosper him", "context_len": 32}}}, "noonday": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "There had been a good many deaths from cholera during the day , and the grandfather prayed for grace and help amidst the pestilence that walketh in darkness and wasteth at noonday in such a way that the boy felt there would be very little of either for him unless he got home at once", "context_len": 55}}}, "simpler": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "He could not sleep for excitement that night , and he came and spent the time talking on quite equal terms with the steersman , one of the canalers whom he had admired afar in earlier and simpler days", "context_len": 39}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Nothing could be simpler and yet more graceful than the dress , the long flowing sleeves falling away from the elbow and showing the young molded arms distinctly", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "If you , blind and alone , save for Pilot , could avoid pursuers so long , surely together we must find it a simpler matter", "context_len": 26}}}, "clumsy": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 93, "context": "have got some good points , and if I were your daughter , Aunt Susan -- and I am heartily glad I 'm not your daughter ; I would much , much rather be Mummy 's daughter , poor as she is -- but if I were your daughter you would dress me in such a fashion that my good points would come out , for I have good points ; a nice complexion , fine hair and plenty of it , and fairly good eyes , and my figure would not look clumsy if I wore proper stays and properly-made dresses ; and my feet would not be like clodhoppers , if I had fine well-made boots and silk stockings ; and my hands -- -- ''", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "They found upon trial , that although she was clumsy in working , she minded her helm , and sailed beyond their most sanguine expectations", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The current was stronger than he had thought , and the clumsy raft was borne along upon the surface at a surprising rate of speed", "context_len": 25}}}, "experienced": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 50, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 58, "context": "She did not soothe or pet her overmuch ; she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little", "context_len": 128}}}, "revisited": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Every corner of the Abbey had to be revisited to feel again the joy of possession ; all the animals must be petted as a kind of apology for having even thought of parting with them ; and Peggy felt as though she would like to tell the birds and the flowers that they were not going away after all , and give them a chance of joining in the general rejoicings", "context_len": 72}}}, "environment": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "While surely , to a thoughtful mind , the constant sight of so much loveliness around tends to have a more ennobling effect than an environment of bricks and mortar and smoky chimneys , whatever the Londoners may say", "context_len": 39}}}, "bitterness": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 46, "context": "The shouts swelled into a deep roar of execration , as the sledge on which the prisoner lay bound neared the place of execution , and Richard Harrison , struggling to keep his place as close to the victim as he might , thought with grim bitterness of the day when this same mob , silent and cowed , had seen General Harrison ride back from the scaffold at Whitehall", "context_len": 70}}}, "sinners": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 16, "context": "Her gruesome voice as she recounted what the goblins did to naughty boys made several small sinners in the back benches shake in their shoes -- many innocent infants felt they should never dare to go to bed in the dark again ; and the wild shriek with which she ended her announcement that 'the goblins are about ' quite brought down the house , and the children shouted and yelled and cheered as the Rector strove to get order once more , and wind up the proceedings with the National Anthem", "context_len": 92}}}, "manlier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "There are some great fools and great sinners who try to believe in after-life that they are the manlier men because they have been silly and mischievous boys , but he has never believed that", "context_len": 35}}}, "hemmed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "Now you know yourself that , although you took a great deal of pains , the hem was rather crooked and some of the stitches quite long , yet grandpapa was more pleased with that one than with the whole dozen which Aunt Helen hemmed , and which were beautifully done , because he knew that you had done the best you could , and that it was a great effort for you", "context_len": 73}}}, "inexperience": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The town was small and the boys there were hemmed in by their inexperience and ignorance ; but the simple home was large with vistas that stretched to the ends of the earth , and it was serenely bright with a father 's reason and warm with a mother 's love", "context_len": 51}}}, "vistas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The town was small and the boys there were hemmed in by their inexperience and ignorance ; but the simple home was large with vistas that stretched to the ends of the earth , and it was serenely bright with a father 's reason and warm with a mother 's love", "context_len": 51}}}, "laugher": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Page 190 , `` laugher '' changed to `` laughter '' ( great a laughter )", "context_len": 16}}}, "illustration": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 37, "context": "Abundant material for the study of ethnography is on hand for the earlier and later periods of the Creek nation ; but here we have to restrict ourselves to some points which are especially adapted to the illustration of the migration legends", "context_len": 42}}}, "zip": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 4, "context": "Abner Fleming felt the zip of a bullet which grazed his forehead , and one of his neighbors on the other side of the circle was scraped by a pellet of lead which abraded an arm", "context_len": 36}}}, "gutenberg": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 0, "context": "gutenberg", "context_len": 1}}}, "debts": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 18, "context": "So , if you will promise , little girl , faithfully , that never , never shall those debts which I paid for you , be paid back again to me ; if you will allow me , for the whole of my life , to feel that I was the one who saved Westenra in her hour of bitter need ; I was the one who helped her mother in her last moments to go down to the grave in peace , if you will promise all that , Westenra , there is an end of everything else", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "assist": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 79, "context": "Gervaise waved his hand , as he neared the end of the harbour , to the officer on the walls , and while the six knights who were with him ran off to tell the master of the galleys to prepare two of them to leave the port instantly , Gervaise explained to the officer in charge of the wall at that point the plan that he was charged to carry out , and asked for twenty knights to assist him", "context_len": 81}}}, "displeased": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 26, "context": "The old lady nodded and smiled , as if she were glad to see them so happy , but Nonesuch seemed not only surprised , but displeased , at finding himself in such jolly company ; and , after some shaking of his head and putting back of his ears , stood stock still in the middle of the road ; nor could all Aunt Patty 's coaxing or scolding , or even some gentle touches of the whip , persuade him to go on , till the whole party were out of sight", "context_len": 94}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 32, "context": "She looked something like a well-groomed pony herself , and the girls were much amused at her way of greeting them , and so thoroughly pleased that peals of laughter reached the displeased ears of Lucy , who was waiting with Annie and Laura in the porch", "context_len": 47}}}, "industrious": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 6, "context": "On the loom I was most industrious , working from early in the morning often till ten , and sometimes later , at night ; and she not only did all the house work , but wound the bobbins for three weavers -- myself , uncle , and grandfather ; and yet , with all this apparently hard lot , these were happy days", "context_len": 64}}}, "obtain": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 51, "context": "Our defences are all complete ; our recent peace with Egypt has enabled us to fill up our magazines with provisions of all kinds ; the inhabitants of the Island have had ample warning to move into the town , carrying with them everything of value ; so the Turks will obtain but little plunder , and will be able to gather no means of subsistence on the island , as every animal has been driven within the walls , and even the unripe corn has been reaped and brought in", "context_len": 91}}}, "poorer": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "You think I have done something unworthy of your god-daughter , but do n't you really , really believe that you would have a much poorer , more contemptible , more worthless sort of god-daughter if she were now to break her bond to the man who saved her mother at considerable expense -- the man who was so good , so kind , so faithful ? Would you really counsel me to break my bond ? ''", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Many put the cause of the disease , rightly enough , to the filthy habits of the poorer people , and hearing that salt water was a preventive , scores of the inhabitants repaired daily to the seashore , plunging into the bitterly cold waves in the hope of staving off the malady", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "To help his still poorer fellow creatures in suffering , to restore them to strength and teach them to be useful members of society , or to comfort them and make the path easier over the river to the other country ; this was his highest aim", "context_len": 47}}}, "scanty": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 33, "context": "There was no garden , only a low wall on which the milk-cans and most of the family crockery seemed to be taking an airing ; but a patch of potatoes and a scanty half-acre of oats lay beyond the little homestead , roughly railed off to keep out the marauding sheep", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 34, "context": "Then , with loud yells of triumph , the stormers rushed the position on three sides simultaneously , bombarded the pill-boxes with grenade and smoke-bombs , and soon had the satisfaction of seeing the scanty remnant of a once numerous garrison come forth in fear and terror , accepting their conquerors ' assurances that their lives would be spared", "context_len": 59}}}, "employ": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 14, "context": "Having concluded my reading of old Russell , how can I do better than employ the interval before the arrival of the Indian letters in sitting down and writing to my fair absent sister ? Colonel Sykes let me know last night that Robin would not come by this mail , which was , he says , only from Bombay , so that letters being all we must expect before Saturday fortnight , you need not hurry home on account of Robin \u2019 s return", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "It is queer to think of you as in the employ of 'Uncle Sam , ' said Ted , who by this time had come to be on most friendly terms with Chris", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "From some cause he quarreled with those above him , and left their employ three or four years before we find him acting as guide for the emigrant train of Abner Fleming", "context_len": 32}}}, "privation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "In this way he thought that the army of Alexander would be reduced by privation and want , and would , in the end , fall an easy prey", "context_len": 29}}}, "charged": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 68, "context": "Gervaise waved his hand , as he neared the end of the harbour , to the officer on the walls , and while the six knights who were with him ran off to tell the master of the galleys to prepare two of them to leave the port instantly , Gervaise explained to the officer in charge of the wall at that point the plan that he was charged to carry out , and asked for twenty knights to assist him", "context_len": 81}}}, "indigent": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` he increased ( or exceeded ) in the salaries ( or allowances ) of the poor and the indigent '' ( zada fi jewanicki ' l fukera wa ' l mesakin )", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "This change of mind was symbolized by the custom of the women of breaking to pieces all the household utensils of the past year , and replacing them by new ones ; the men refitted all their property so as to look new , and it was considered extremely disgraceful , even for the most indigent , to eat any of the new maize before the annual busk ( Sketch , pp", "context_len": 72}}}, "accept": {"freq": 92, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 89, "word_index": 12, "context": "I am not going back to Sunnyside , for they would not accept me ; but , at the same time , I do not feel at all a disgraced girl ; and I should like the Merrimans to be friends with me , and I should still like sometimes to see Jane and Laura Everett , and some of the other girls -- not Lucy Merriman , for she is not in the least to my taste ; but even she does not greatly matter now that I am no longer living in the house with her", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "I am quite sure that in your case your disclaimer is wholly sincere and unaffected ; but take my advice , accept the honours the world may pay you as not undeserved , determining only in your mind that if you deem them excessive , you will at least do all in your power to show that they are not ill bestowed", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "The Outram of the book is just the Outram who was the admiration of our girlhood , -- generous , chivalrous , noble ! One feels how much pain that fine spirit would have been saved , had he realised how little it really matters whether good service be appreciated or not by man , if the great Leader accept it , -- if all be done as to Him Who never overlooks or misunderstands ! To our own Master we stand or fall ; let earthly superiors say what they will", "context_len": 92}}}, "consumption": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 49, "context": "When Alexander was a young man in Macedon , before his father 's death , he was one day present at the offering of sacrifices , and one of his teachers and guardians , named Leonnatus , who was standing by , thought he was rather profuse in his consumption of frankincense and myrrh", "context_len": 54}}}, "uncharitable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "That would not be so hard to do if it were not for leaving you alone in this uncharitable world", "context_len": 20}}}, "shocked": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 54, "context": "I never was too good -- was I , mother dear ? Perhaps , darling , I 'd have become an awfully naughty Rosamund had it not been for Irene -- poor little wild Irene ; but she was really and truly much naughtier than I ever thought of being , and her example shocked me and pulled me up , and I resolved to try to be good for her sake", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "( Do you remember dear Fanny \u2019 s lovely definition of that word ? ) When I was in Edinburgh , dear -- -- was surprised , and I think a little shocked , at my father \u2019 s daughter going in omnibuses", "context_len": 43}}}, "speaker": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Then , '' resumed the speaker , `` if so be as that ye are all content , how comes it that one of ye must needs taake bloodmoney from the gaugers ? And how comes it that dree [ 1 ] of our \u2019 n have been stuck wi \u2019 a Bridport dagger ? `` [ 2 ]", "context_len": 60}}}, "induce": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 76, "context": "Her mother sprang up and kissed her rapturously , crying -- 'Is n't she the dearest and sweetest thing and the smartest ! Think of her learning that and acting it off so completely , and not three years old ! She is smarter than Violet ' -- and then Violet set up such a howl ! Her mother pacified her by saying Marilla should tell her a piece , and after several efforts Cinderella did induce her to say by a great deal of prompting 'Milkman , Milkman , where have you been ? ' Think of the wear on the child 's nerves , and she looked so tired", "context_len": 111}}}, "throbbing": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 49, "context": "However , he bathed my hand in seawater , although the salt did make it smart mightily , and , tying it with a strip of wet linen , he told me to keep quiet , so as not unduly to excite my head , which was by now throbbing like to burst", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "The former was content to lie there gazing into his enemy 's eyes , for his strength was returning with every breath ; that breathing was less laboured , and , in place of his heart throbbing and jumping , sending hot gushes of blood , as it were , choking to his throat , it began to settle steadily down to its ordinary labours in the breast of a strongly-built , healthy , temperate man", "context_len": 76}}}, "kitten": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 86, "context": "That was all there was in it , and yet can you not surmise how even that letter was calculated to work great consternation in the mind of some one in the little thatched cottage -- some one who never saw the letter itself , and who did not so much as know of its existence until it had been read and re-read and thought over and answered , but who when one day he was made acquainted with its contents felt as weak as a kitten for hours afterward ? He happened to be lying on the lounge in the living-room at the time , the same lounge to which he had been carried more dead than alive apparently , just four weeks before", "context_len": 125}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Dorothy was playing with her kitten out on the side porch", "context_len": 11}}}, "frail": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 33, "context": "The sad procession passed through the streets , the people flocked into the church , and in the presence of all the `` wise men '' of London , they solemnly committed the frail tabernacle in which the living spirit had sinned and suffered to the parent earth , where the rush and roar of a mighty city should ever peal around it", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 48, "context": "Eliot , as he led the boy into a handsome room , where there were long shelves of books , and great comfortable leather-covered chairs , and costly Turkish rugs on the hardwood floor , with a wood fire burning cheerfully in an open fireplace , and a frail , sweet-faced woman sitting amid piled-up cushions in an invalid \u2019 s chair near a table , on which stood a shaded lamp and lay many books and magazines", "context_len": 78}}}, "indulge": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 29, "context": "Marie-Celeste was up betimes the next morning -- that is , if betimes means bright and early , and , stopping for a few minutes on her way to indulge in a savory cup of arrowroot , which the stewardess had made ready for her , she passed on up the stairs and out on to the saloon deek , looking as fresh and sweet in her dress of sailor-blue as a fair little morning-glory", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "I suppose you don \u2019 t indulge yourself in that way , Wentworth added , with a faint smile", "context_len": 19}}}, "antics": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "Baby ceased her loud cries , and stretched out her dimpled arms to her uncle , with a beseeching whimper ; Hafed paused in his antics , and stood like a statue at sight of his master ; and the three other children all turned to him with exclamations of `` Oh , Uncle Ruthven ; we 're so glad ! '' and `` Please do n't leave us , '' from Maggie and Bessie ; and `` Make dat Patty be off wiz herself , '' from Franky", "context_len": 89}}}, "preventing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 75, "context": "Some he said were telling how a monster rattlesnake had come to visit them without any invitation , and that the only food he would eat , was the youngest and fattest of their families ; and that their constant intruders , the owls , had the same carnivorous tastes , besides which they rendered themselves particularly disagreeable , by standing in the doors and staring at every dog that went by , and even preventing the entrance of visitors , to the great distress of all the belles and beaux in town", "context_len": 93}}}, "quarreling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "He did so , and as she watched him closely , she saw , with surprise , that he amused Agnes by his lively stories , the baby by his antics , and was successful not only in preventing Gus and George from quarreling , but in keeping friendly with them himself", "context_len": 52}}}, "impulsive": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 7, "context": "Again , one of her eager and impulsive temperament could not have been naturally free from a clinging to her own way , and from a certain vigorous self-seeking ; and many a bitter conflict must have been gone through , before friends could , with an all but unanimous voice , speak of hers as a peculiarly unselfish character", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "She was very resolute ; very persevering ; very affectionate ; reserved , yet demonstrative ; untidy , yet methodical ; exceedingly anxious for the happiness of all around ; apt often to think people better than they really were ; generous to a fault ; unselfishly ready at all times to put her own wishes aside ; vehement and impulsive , yet never in a hurry or flurry ; unyielding , yet tender ; severe , yet frisky", "context_len": 79}}}, "project": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 9, "context": "Father , however , getting to hear of the project , forbade it so emphatically that the disgusted aeronaut was obliged to give way , and consoled himself by constructing a fire-balloon out of gay strips of pink and green tissue-paper , which , ignited by methylated spirits , was to be set off with great effect on Peggy 's birthday", "context_len": 61}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Suddenly , with a swirl of beaten water , a huge vessel loomed out of the mist , her flying jibboom seeming to project right over our stern", "context_len": 28}}}, "laggards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Remember , I 'll have no laggards in my train", "context_len": 10}}}, "dampen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "There seemed to her little prospect of freedom or pleasure in becoming a servant ; yet she said but little about it to Guy as she did not wish to dampen the ardor of his feelings , fearing that the stern reality of an emigrant 's life would soon throw a cloud over his blissful hopes", "context_len": 56}}}, "impulsiveness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Even Guy himself sometimes felt half inclined to repent his impulsiveness , for George Harwood constantly reminded him of it by calling him `` Young America '' and asking him if he had no other servants to hire out", "context_len": 39}}}, "taunts": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "Gardner , to whom , with others , he was giving lessons in languages ; and as a result of his intercourse with this man he became again the same simple loving believer that he had been when he learnt to read the Bible at his mother 's knee , or braved the taunts of his school-fellows", "context_len": 57}}}, "pleasanter": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 31, "context": "Her friendships were so keen and her affections so warm , that I think she found the world a very pleasant place to be in , and made it better and pleasanter for other people too ( which is , after all , the grand secret of happiness ) , and the energy and enterprising spirits which had caused the tomboy pranks of her childhood were qualities which , turned to good use , proved of the utmost service to her in after-years", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "It is pleasanter to drive a team of eight horses over cleared land than to grub out dockweed and thistles all alone in one corner", "context_len": 25}}}, "travelers": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 55, "context": "The slope was steadily upward for a fourth of a mile , when it reached a nearly level plateau , and wound in and out among rocks , stunted pines , gnarled cedars , and ravines , interspersed with valleys and comparatively smooth stretches , with now and then a mountain torrent across which the travelers made their way with difficulty", "context_len": 61}}}, "caravans": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "They were real gipsies , too , not the fair-haired hybrid pretenders who go about in neat caravans with muslin curtains to the windows , and wicker baskets slung on the top , but a dark-eyed , Spanish-looking crew , who put up a tent with a pole and a ragged blanket , and stewed their supper in a black caldron hung from three sticks set in the ground", "context_len": 69}}}, "remind": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 17, "context": "The perpetual play upon words , and the almost rollicking fun and nonsense of the whole , remind one of her earlier effort , The Pretender , already given at length ; though the later-written farce is in some respects scarcely equal to the girlish achievement", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Remind me of it , Mark , and I 'll tell it to you some day", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Did the figure remind you of anyone , father ? What was it like ?", "context_len": 15}}}, "mentioning": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 36, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}}}, "oxen": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 51, "word_index": 95, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Why , mamma , you know the dog in the manger could not eat the hay himself , and would not let the oxen eat it ; and Aunt Patty would not buy the grove , or tell papa what was the reason ; so was she not like the dog in the manger ? ''", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They had mules , however , in addition to oxen and horses", "context_len": 12}}}, "turbans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Those great , strong , covered wagons do n't look much like the queer old caravans did I guess , and neither the mules or oxen are like camels , besides the drivers have n't any turbans on their heads , and the people altogether look much more like Christians than Arabs", "context_len": 53}}}, "comparisons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Guy was quite abashed , and not daring to make any other comparisons , asked Gus to tell him the name of the owner of each wagon as it passed", "context_len": 30}}}, "baggage": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Louis , and the other six are baggage wagons", "context_len": 9}, "NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 55, "context": "Next he was ordered to run back to the village for some article that had been forgotten , next to carry water to the teamsters , then to run with messages from one person to another until he was so tired , he thoroughly envied George and Gus their comfortable seats in one of the baggage wagons , and was delighted at last to hear the signal to halt", "context_len": 69}}}, "halt": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 68, "context": "Next he was ordered to run back to the village for some article that had been forgotten , next to carry water to the teamsters , then to run with messages from one person to another until he was so tired , he thoroughly envied George and Gus their comfortable seats in one of the baggage wagons , and was delighted at last to hear the signal to halt", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 52, "context": "But , singularly enough , no one seemed desirous to avail himself of his neighbour 's generosity ; and the reason soon became apparent ; for presently , in the midst of their wild bounding round the inner edge of the tightly packed mass of spectators , they came to a sudden halt , and Mafuta , advancing alone , proceeded to `` smell out '' those who were supposed to be inimical to the king 's or his own authority , or against whom either of them had a secret grudge", "context_len": 92}}}, "wearisome": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "Although they had been traveling all day they were but a few miles from the village , and the people in spite of the wearisome labors of the day scarcely realized that they had begun a long and perilous journey", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I do hope that you may not find teaching the wearisome task which I sometimes do", "context_len": 16}}}, "labors": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 38, "context": "Could it be that her father had come at last ? That , after all her waiting , she was to see him and talk with him and sob out on his breast her appreciation of his long labors in her behalf , his kindness , unselfishness and goodness ?", "context_len": 50}}}, "perilous": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 32, "context": "The colour which his unlucky presence among the congregation at Coleman Street on the day of General Harrisons execution , had given to his enemy 's accusations , had made his position perilous in the extreme , for General Monck , and his other secret friends , considered that he had wilfully disregarded their warnings , and were not inclined to exert their influence in his behalf", "context_len": 67}}}, "unhitching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Some were building fires for themselves , some unhitching the horses from the traces , unyoking the oxen , and giving them water and feed", "context_len": 25}}}, "unyoking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Some were building fires for themselves , some unhitching the horses from the traces , unyoking the oxen , and giving them water and feed", "context_len": 25}}}, "spark": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 83, "context": "Dinner at length being over ( though later than usual , on account of the time occupied in baking the pie , and later , still , by reason of the goodness of it ) , they prepared to start , taking with them an axe to build a camp , tinder in a horn , flint , steel , and matches , which were made by dipping splinters of wood into melted brimstone , and which would burn when touched to the spark in the tinder", "context_len": 87}}}, "reprimanded": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "Edna thought it was , and now that she was ready to pop into bed , Reliance left her with a happy `` Good-night ! '' It was like sunshine in the house to have such a dear little girl as Edna , she thought as she went downstairs , and though Amanda reprimanded her sharply for not being in bed , she did not answer back , for , in fact , she scarcely heard her , so busy was she with pleasant thoughts , and so excited over the idea of the club", "context_len": 95}}}, "negligence": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Harwood , who was wishing to warm some milk for the baby reprimanded him severely for his negligence", "context_len": 18}}}, "vexation": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 44, "context": "And so , trying to reason herself into better humor , as she thought the matter over , she began to see its droll side ( for Aunt Patty had a quick sense of fun ) and to find some amusement mingling with her vexation at the singular conduct of the children", "context_len": 52}}}, "milking": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 34, "context": "The garden left behind , there was the lane to be passed through , and here some real cause for fear in Edna 's opinion , for the cows that Ira had just finished milking were coming through the bars he had let down", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Pigs , poultry , and young animals had to be fed , and the eggs gathered in daily ; the dairy claimed constant attention , for the pans must be scalded and the pails polished bright , and though Joe did the milking and churning , it was Lilian 's business to superintend the making up of the butter , and to weigh each pound with her own hands and print it with the Abbey stamp", "context_len": 76}}}, "fizzle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "Has somebody stepped on his poor 'ittle toe ? '' he added with mock tenderness , as if he was talking to a little child ; `` never mind , hold up your head , or you 'll put the fire out with your tears ; just see how they make it fizzle : why , how salt they must be ! ''", "context_len": 63}}}, "raillery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Guy had the good sense neither to get angry , or to cry , at this raillery , although he found it hard to abstain from doing both", "context_len": 28}}}, "abstain": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "The letter gave instructions for me to be sent to my uncle \u2019 s at Portsmouth , to be provided for until I could choose for myself what I should be , at the same time exhorting me to serve faithfully His Majesty King Charles II or his lawful successor , and to abstain from vain or idle longings to break the seals of the enclosed package till the stipulated time limit had expired", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "I am really but a boy yet , though by good fortune pushed strangely forward , and I am glad indeed to receive council from a knight of vastly greater experience than myself and , in future , however much I may be conscious in my own mind that anything I have done is greatly overrated , I will at least abstain from protest", "context_len": 64}}}, "impervious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was covered with thick oil-cloth , which was quite impervious to rain ; on the floor was a carpet , over head a curious sort of rack that held all manner of useful things , guns , fishing poles and lines , game bags , baskets of fruit , sewing materials , books ; and even glass-ware and crockery", "context_len": 60}}}, "carpet": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 63, "context": "It [ 481 ] was now grown high day ; so the Sultan arose from sleep and opening a window of his pavilion , looked forth and saw buildings [ 482 ] before his palace ; whereupon he fell to rubbing his eyes and opening them wide and looking farther , saw a magnificent palace , that bewildered the wits , and a carpet spread therefrom to his own palace ; as on like wise did the doorkeepers and all who were in the palace , and their wits were bewildered at the sight", "context_len": 94}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "In many cases the walls of the buildings which surround the quadrangles on the four sides are almost hid beneath a luxurious growth of English ivy , while from April to December the lawns that carpet them are green with the wonderful depth of color peculiar to lawns that have been cultivated for centuries", "context_len": 54}}}, "rack": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 9, "context": "He was standing on his tip-toes , for the rack was high , and was just taking down one of the small hampers , when there came a sudden crash , followed by a ripping , tearing sound , and before Bart knew what had happened he was deposited in a heap on top of Fenn , who , in turn , was mixed up with Frank and Ned", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "And so the hours rolled on , which brought the dreaded morn nearer and nearer ; and the victim , comforted by prayer , but without hope in this world , slept , and thought no longer of the torturer 's knife , or felt the cruel anticipations which would rack the waiting mind", "context_len": 54}}}, "divans": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "As Guy saw no sheets or blankets upon the divans , he was at a loss to know how the sleepers would keep warm , until his mother raised the cushioned lid of one of the boxes , and showed him a quantity of coverlets and blankets , packed tightly therein", "context_len": 51}}}, "benches": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 64, "context": ", and the girls to play 'Drop the Handkerchief ' ; she boldly interposed her small person between the fists of two fighting hobbledehoys , and seized a little boy by the boot who had ventured to climb up the stove-pipe ; she welcomed some of the parents who had begun to arrive for the entertainment , and found them comfortable places on the benches , even nursing one of the babies while its mother went to the rescue of an older child , who was being forcibly held down and sat upon by several of its companions ; and by the time the bell was rung , and the audience requested to take their places , she was almost as hot and pulled about as the Rector", "context_len": 128}}}, "lid": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 32, "context": "To-morrow morning , then , my dear , if your Father will be at liberty , I shall give myself the pleasure of calling upon him ; ' and regretfully closing the lid , he tore himself away from the old chest , like a reluctant lover from his mistress , and took his departure , leaving Peggy , nearly wild with excitement , to rush tumultuously into the house with her wonderful piece of news", "context_len": 76}}}, "therein": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 54, "context": "When [ 413 ] the Vizier heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 50, "context": "Now his mother was with the Lady Bedrulbudour , and when he came up and did off her veil , she fell to gazing upon the bride 's beauty and grace and looked at the pavilion , the which was all wroughten [ 497 ] of gold and jewels and therein were golden lustres , all embossed with emeralds and rubies ; and she said in herself , `` Methought the Sultan 's palace was magnificent ; but , for this pavilion [ 498 ] alone , I doubt me the greatest of the Chosroes and the kings never owned its match ; nor , methinketh , might all mankind avail to make the like thereof", "context_len": 116}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "' So now , O my mother , arise , as I bade thee , and fetch me the China dish whereof I bespoke thee , that we may range of these jewels therein and see how they show", "context_len": 39}}}, "suspended": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 37, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Malcolm and Dick found bayonet exercise exciting work -- thrusting at suspended sacks stuffed with straw called for strength and strenuous activity -- while at the ranges both lads gained a high percentage of bulls , and in a very short while the `` crossed rifles `` , denoting marksmanship , ornamented the sleeves of their uniforms", "context_len": 57}}}, "nursing": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 64, "context": "girls to play 'Drop the Handkerchief ' ; she boldly interposed her small person between the fists of two fighting hobbledehoys , and seized a little boy by the boot who had ventured to climb up the stove-pipe ; she welcomed some of the parents who had begun to arrive for the entertainment , and found them comfortable places on the benches , even nursing one of the babies while its mother went to the rescue of an older child , who was being forcibly held down and sat upon by several of its companions ; and by the time the bell was rung , and the audience requested to take their places , she was almost as hot and pulled about as the Rector himself , but", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 49, "context": "Now this was monkish work , and men who devoted themselves solely to such a life of charity as that in our Hospital at Jerusalem , might well renounce all human pleasures ; but when the great change was made by Master Raymond du Puy , and from a nursing body we became a brotherhood in arms , it seems to me that the vows of celibacy were no longer needful or desirable", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Miss Nightingale thus learnt nursing very thoroughly , and when she came back to England turned her knowledge to account by taking charge of an institution in London", "context_len": 28}}}, "fretful": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 58, "context": "There was a large , round lamp suspended from the center of the wagon , and as Guy looked at his mother 's cheerful surroundings he could not but wonder that she sighed when he spoke of the dark , lonesome lodgings they had left , until he suddenly remembered that she had been nursing the heavy , fretful baby , and trying to amuse Aggie all the day", "context_len": 69}}}, "whips": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "An hour after midnight our mainsail , though treble-reefed , parted with a report that was heard above the storm , the torn canvas streaming out to lee \u2019 ard like so many whips ; and simultaneously our bowsprit carried away close to the gammonings , with the result that the ship yawed , then shot up into the wind", "context_len": 60}}}, "tenderhearted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` But we will have no hurting or crying this time , '' replied Guy as he helped Aggie out of the wagon , thinking what a tenderhearted girl she must be to cry to see George Harwood whipped , he was sure that he should not , `` for , '' said Guy to himself , `` we should never cry over what we think will do people good", "context_len": 70}}}, "stoves": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Two cooking stoves were sending up from their black pipes thick spirals of smoke , while half a dozen clouds of the same arose from as many fires , around which were gathered men and women busily engaged in preparing the evening meal", "context_len": 43}}}, "pipes": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 44, "context": "The whole family was gathered together on the grass round the wood fire , the smoke of which rose up faintly blue against the russet of the beech-woods behind -- handsome , fierce-eyed men , lying slouching idly on the ground smoking short black pipes ; slatternly women with gay handkerchiefs tied round their black hair , bustling about with something savoury inside tin cans ; ragged brown-skinned little children , gnawing at bones with savage haste ; while a few disreputable dogs waited eagerly for the scraps that were thrown to them from time to time", "context_len": 97}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Madge stepped to the steam pipes in the room , and with her pencil tapped several times in a peculiar way", "context_len": 21}}}, "busily": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 68, "context": "She looked lovingly at the cows , lazily chewing their cud in the sunshine ; she felt sorry for her step-mother , as she strove to woo slumber to Polly 's wakeful eyes with the same lullaby which had done duty for the whole six ; she even found it in her heart to kiss Lemuel , who , with his ready talent for the unusual , was busily cramming mud paste into the seams of the little trunk which held her worldly all", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Edna came down to find her busily taking up the ashes", "context_len": 11}}}, "beefsteaks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Tea and coffee were steaming , beefsteaks broiling , slices of bacon sputtering in the frying pans , each and every article sending forth most appetizing odors", "context_len": 27}}}, "broiling": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "You have not worked as a galley slave , but I have , and I can assure you that I used to feel the hours when we were lying broiling in the sun , doing nothing , much more trying than those during which I was at work", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "One of the men was preparing breakfast and the odor of the steaming coffee and broiling venison was the sweetest perfume that could greet a hungry person", "context_len": 27}}}, "sputtering": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 67, "context": "And now , whether Miss Kitty saw through the crack of the drawer that her young master was fondling a new pet , or whether she only guessed at it , or whether she thought it hard that fun should be going on in which she had no share , can not be told ; but just then there came from her prison-place such a hissing and sputtering and scratching that every one of the children set up a shout of laughter", "context_len": 82}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He knew the language of the fluttering signal flags by day and the sputtering Coston lights and winking lamps by night", "context_len": 21}}}, "appetizing": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "The blankets were dry and comforting , the food really appetizing , and , having made a satisfying meal , the brothers slept soundly after forty hours of unceasing vigilance", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Hope the post will arrive before we start , '' remarked Carr as he turned to enter the hut , from which wafted the appetizing odour of frying eggs and bacon , the fumes of cheap kerosene notwithstanding", "context_len": 39}}}, "odors": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 64, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}}}, "coverings": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 87, "context": "In the grate there was a glowing , blazing fire ; on the hob was a little brass kettle hissing and boiling ; spread upon the floor was a thick , warm crimson rug ; before the fire a folding-chair , unfolded , and with cushions on it ; by the chair a small folding-table , unfolded , covered with a white cloth , and upon it spread small covered dishes , a cup , a saucer , a teapot ; on the bed were new warm coverings and a satin-covered down quilt ; at the foot a curious wadded silk robe , a pair of quilted slippers , and some books", "context_len": 112}}}, "requisite": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Joseph at Glastonbury , and can produce , in the time requisite for a messenger to go and return , an attestation to that effect", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "She was not too old , many years later , for Batala boys ; but plainly she had not the requisite gifts for managing or winning rough English village lads", "context_len": 30}}}, "edibles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "There were boxes too , of tea , coffee , rice , crackers and many other edibles , and in one corner , quite apart from these a number of flasks of powder", "context_len": 33}}}, "flasks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The antiquarians did not seem to be discussing anything very learned just at present , for they had drawn out flasks , and packets of sandwiches , and were engaged in picnicking upon the stones in a truly modern fashion , while occasional bursts of laughter were wafted along the air", "context_len": 51}}}, "spades": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 12, "context": "They had a little garden house too , where they kept their spades , rakes and other tools , for each child had a plot of ground for its own , and every fall they had a flower and fruit show , when their father and mother gave prizes , not only for the best flowers and fruit , but also to those whose gardens had been neatly kept during the summer", "context_len": 72}}}, "valuable": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 62, "context": "I ask for no earthly remuneration ; my position in life renders me independent of any exertions of my own ; I pray but for God \u2019 s blessing upon my attempts to instruct His lambs in the things which concern their everlasting welfare ; and deeply gratified should I feel , were my little work to be classed among the numerous valuable publications which you have already given to the world", "context_len": 72}}}, "buffalo": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 61, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "But Aggie , herself entertained no such fears , and after looking at the old man curiously for some little time , approached him slowly and examined his strange dress , the circular shield covered with buffalo hide that was strapped on his left arm , and the formidable war-club that lay at his side", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Shagbark , however , was not partial to buffalo meat , which many of his friends found coarse and tough", "context_len": 20}}}, "brandishing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 58, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}}}, "wrested": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "But do not land unless it is absolutely necessary , for you might fall a victim to the fanaticism of its inhabitants , and no knight has ever set foot on shore there since the ill fated day when the Moslems wrested it from us , bathed the ruined walls with the best blood of our Order and the Templars ' , and destroyed the last hope of our ever recovering the Holy Sepulchre", "context_len": 74}}}, "administered": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 4, "context": "The herb medicines are administered to him by the \u00edsti pak\u0101 dsha ` l\u00e1ko or `` great leader , '' who , when speaking of him , says : pusidshedsh\u0113 yi sanatchumitch\u00e4 tch\u00e4-is , [ 123 ] `` I am passing him through the physicking process repeatedly , '' or : n\u00e1ki om\u00e1lga imaki ` l\u00e4 dsh\u00e4yi s\u00e1 ` lit \u00f3m\u00e4s , tch\u00ed , `` I am teaching him all the matters proper for him to think of", "context_len": 78}}}, "piteously": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Only that you go away ! We -- we want to be by ourselves , here -- '' suddenly she broke off , glancing piteously toward the couch , and crying out in a changed , husky voice , `` Madre mia ! muerta ! muerta ! ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "cowardice": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 40, "context": "He knew not then that the example before him would nerve him in moments of severest trial , then fast approaching , that the one accusation urged against the Christians , which he had felt most keenly , that of cowardice , was answered in the weak yet valiant boy , who found strength in the name of Christ to endure all for His sake ; neither did his fierce countrymen know that they were preparing a disappointment for the pagan Anlaf , and for all those of his house and lineage", "context_len": 92}}}, "unperceived": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Encouraged by the dead silence that reigned within the fort , she flattered herself that her stealthy approach was unperceived by the enemy , and so , after pausing for a moment , she advanced still farther and gained another bush", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Having had the good fortune to get unperceived past a sentry who was closely questioning a native , they came into the principal street of Lucknow , jostling against the armed rebels , who would have killed them in a moment had their suspicion been aroused", "context_len": 46}}}, "henceforth": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 80, "context": "`` So he broke away from the sorcerers and plunged the enchanted one into the boiling cauldron , unheeding the agony he suffered or the cries of the little animal he held , and in a moment the moon plunged beneath the horizon ; Mahdrusa arose from her long sleep ; the sorcerers fell into the boiling cauldron and were consumed ; and all the people shouted for joy , and with one accord cried that the Ohoolees should from henceforth be their brothers , and the young brave who had rescued Mahdrusa , their chieftain , when her father was called to the happy hunting grounds", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Englishmen are henceforth at least a match for their oppressors , and the result of the long contest is the conversion of their foes to Christianity , their king setting the example , and the union of the two races -- not the submission of one to the other", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "I will make him pay more heavily than the sultan would , Ben Ibyn said sternly ; I will speak to my friends among the merchants , and henceforth no Berber will buy aught from him ; and we have hitherto been his best customers", "context_len": 45}}}, "especial": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 24, "context": "I gathered from their remarks that they were seriously concerned at my condition , for it seemed that I was reserved for some very especial refinement of torture , the satisfactory application of which demanded that I must come to it in the possession of my full strength , which they feared had been seriously sapped by the suffering which I had already endured , and they freely expressed their concern lest , under existing circumstances , I should not furnish quite so much sport as was being expected of me", "context_len": 91}}}, "defender": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 27, "context": "Thou diest a traitor \u2019 s death ; -- but wert thou ours , Then ev \u2019 ry bush around the fatal spot Should hold an armed defender , ev \u2019 ry knoll Conceal an ambushed friend , and at a word A wall of steel should bristle round thy breast ; Then swords should clash with swords , and they who came To shed thy blood lie weltering in their own", "context_len": 72}}}, "molests": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I shall make him from henceforth Aggie 's especial defender , and he can strike whoever molests her , whether it be an Indian or any one else", "context_len": 28}}}, "sullenly": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 12, "context": "But the girl just took what she could get that day as sullenly as you please , never speaking a word of thanks , and making no promises , though she did look mighty proud of her new clothes , and hugged herself up in them as if she were glad to feel herself in something warm and comfortable", "context_len": 59}}}, "tablecloth": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "Nancy had to dry her washing as best she could , for all the clothes-horses in the establishment were needed for side-wings , and the dining-room tablecloth , being green , was generally missing , having to do duty for such occasions as ' a grove , ' ' a glade in the forest , ' or the garden scene in 'Much Ado about Nothing", "context_len": 65}}}, "congregated": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "After the crowd has thickly congregated at the bank , each person places a grain of `` old man 's tobacco '' on the head and others in each ear", "context_len": 30}}}, "entreating": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "Bedin aden ; it is n't fair ; bedin aden , Dorothy , please , he urged with all the breath hurrying and excitement had left him ; and Dorothy , at sight of his anxious , entreating face , resolved to begin again , first bringing the interrupted measure to a close with a brief concluding improvisation of her own", "context_len": 61}}}, "blessing": {"freq": 61, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 69, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 45, "context": "Another day Has past ! another weary weary day , And I am so much nearer to my grave ! Oh that I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in", "context_len": 128}}}, "aggie": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Aggie whispered to Guy , as she bade him `` good-night , '' that after that prayer she should not be afraid of the Indians , and went very contentedly to her mother 's wagon , while Guy followed Gus and George to the one in which they were to sleep", "context_len": 51}}}, "prairies": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "Then in succession he passed through Grenada , Logchain , Seneca , Ash Point , Guittard \u2019 s , Marysville , Hollenburg , thence following Little Blue Valley to Rock Creek , Big Sandy , Liberty Farm , across prairies to Thirty-Two Mile Creek , over the divide , sand hills and plains to Platte River , and then westward and up that valley to Fort Kearny", "context_len": 67}}}, "lodging": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 69, "context": "Then the king shouted something to his women-folk which caused them to scuttle back into the palace like so many rabbits ; and the next moment the two guards who had me in charge marched me off to an empty hut behind the palace -- which was , luckily , to windward of the crucifixion tree , the odour from which therefore did not reach as far as my lodging -- and , having signed to me to enter , mounted guard , one on each side of the door", "context_len": 90}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "These circumstances occurred at a time when the army was preparing for a march , the various generals lodging in tents pitched for the purpose", "context_len": 25}}}, "smouldering": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 29, "context": "Alexander himself ascended one of the eminences , from whence he could look down upon the great plain beyond , which was dimly illuminated in every part by the smouldering fires of the Persian encampment", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Arrived at the camp , fresh fuel was put on the smouldering fire , the embers were blown into a lively flame , and then the black fellow began operations by splitting the crook into slabs , cutting them thinner and thinner until of the required thickness", "context_len": 47}}}, "extinguished": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 48, "context": "George spoke of himself as having then been a bit of a rebel \u2019 ; adding , But my republican zeal was very much cooled by the French Revolution ; and if a spark of it had remained , our own most contemptible revolution of 1830 would have extinguished it , and have fixed me for life a determined Conservative", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "freshly": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` O Aescendune , once happy Aescendune ! '' was the thought , the bitter thought , as each hour placed a larger barrier of space between Alfgar and his late home ; all its happy memories came freshly back upon him , and particularly the thought of Ethelgiva , his betrothed , from whom he was so ruthlessly torn , torn as if he left part of himself behind", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The mingled odours of corned beef and cabbage issued from one of the pots and permeated the freshly ironed clothes", "context_len": 20}}}, "budding": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "There was the busy , noisy camp before him , and beyond it the calm beauty of freshly budding forests , standing forth in bold relief from the blue sky which bore on its bosom the golden sphere whence emanate all light and heat , God 's gifts that make our earth so lovely and so fruitful", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Presently a clerk beckoned him , and the door of the inner office was closed behind the budding shipping merchant", "context_len": 20}}}, "sphere": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "There was the busy , noisy camp before him , and beyond it the calm beauty of freshly budding forests , standing forth in bold relief from the blue sky which bore on its bosom the golden sphere whence emanate all light and heat , God 's gifts that make our earth so lovely and so fruitful", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 69, "context": "She was the older daughter , a strong , healthy girl of seventeen , who never helped her mother about the work , who dressed in such cheap finery as she could obtain by hook or crook , who took music lessons on a rented melodeon paid for out of her mother \u2019 s hard earnings , who felt herself to be a lady unfortunately born out of her sphere , and who was unquestionably ashamed of her surviving parent and her brother and sister", "context_len": 85}}}, "emanate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "There was the busy , noisy camp before him , and beyond it the calm beauty of freshly budding forests , standing forth in bold relief from the blue sky which bore on its bosom the golden sphere whence emanate all light and heat , God 's gifts that make our earth so lovely and so fruitful", "context_len": 57}}}, "fruitful": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "Most children would have thought the life of constant toil which Guy led very wretched indeed ; but he did not , for he had daily the gratification of perceiving that the great object of their journey across the plains was being gradually accomplished ; his mother 's health was slowly becoming strengthened , by every step they took toward the snowy mountains , beyond which lay the fruitful valleys in which they hoped to find a home", "context_len": 78}}}, "assisting": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 14, "context": "Harwood did not tell him to do so , he remained contentedly behind , assisting his mother to take care of the baby , and anxiously wondering when she would become strong and well , for she still looked as pale and weak as when they left W -- --", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The plan of Thais was carried fully into effect , every half-intoxicated guest assisting , by putting fire to the immense pile wherever they could get access to it", "context_len": 29}}}, "pitied": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 58, "context": "To be sure , the apology for a tree was not dressed with glittering balls , rich bonbons , or rows of tapers ; its branches bore no expensive toys , rare books , or lovely pictures ; but the owner and the little ones for whose delight she dressed it , were quite satisfied , and only pitied those who had no tree at all", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "If you forgot for a moment , and your temper overcame you , I think he knew how you had struggled with it this morning , and so pitied and forgave , sending the grace and strength you needed as soon as you saw your own want of it", "context_len": 49}}}, "unused": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "Being unused to these solitudes and this wild life , he was frightened to death , and like a little dog out alone in the cold and darkness he was barking to keep his courage up", "context_len": 36}}}, "pailful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "So when he went to the spring for water , he brought also a pailful for them , and when he had a leisure moment , he did any little chores for them that he could", "context_len": 36}}}, "wended": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Slowly and steadily the well-trained mules and the patient oxen wended their way towards the Missouri River , and so for nearly two weeks the march was kept up with no incident occurring to break its monotony , save the daily excitement of breaking camp at noon and after a tiresome walk of a dozen miles or more , building the watch fires at night , and talking over the events of the day", "context_len": 74}}}, "occurring": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 32, "context": "Slowly and steadily the well-trained mules and the patient oxen wended their way towards the Missouri River , and so for nearly two weeks the march was kept up with no incident occurring to break its monotony , save the daily excitement of breaking camp at noon and after a tiresome walk of a dozen miles or more , building the watch fires at night , and talking over the events of the day", "context_len": 74}}}, "monotony": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 22, "context": "But the dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of", "context_len": 128}}}, "wearied": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 16, "context": "Could they have supplied the place of the fallen by fresh men , until they had wearied the defenders out , they would have succeeded , but they were evidently not in strength to do this so they slowly yielded , until the deadly struggle ceased , and silence resumed her empire , while the besieged repaired the damage the defences had sustained", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "Ram Dass had been lying flat upon the slates , looking in at the skylight , when the banquet had come to its disastrous conclusion ; he had been sure of the profoundness of Sara 's wearied sleep ; and then , with a dark lantern , he had crept into the room , while his companion remained outside and handed the things to him", "context_len": 65}}}, "shirked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Every one found something for Guy to do , and as he never shirked his work as many boys do , be found but little time for rest , and none for play", "context_len": 33}}}, "nook": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 64, "context": "a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and sent such", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "WHEN Sunday afternoon came they all met as had been arranged , in the lovely nook the Colonel had chosen for them", "context_len": 22}}}, "jests": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Because of its happy absurdity the name long clung to Jane ; but despite such small jests every one respected her sterling traits , -- every one , that is , except Se\u00f1ora Vigil , who lived hard by in a mud house like a bird 's nest , and who cherished a grudge against her neighbor", "context_len": 58}}}, "clapping": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 71, "context": "`` No , indeed ! Nurse , with Maggie on one arm and Bessie on the other , fairly dancing about the room in her efforts to save the former from Aunt Patty 's clutches , both terrified babies screaming at the top of their voices , both old women scolding at the top of theirs ; while Fred , the monkey , young as he was , stood by , clapping his hands and setting them at each other as if they had been two cats", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "There was a good deal of giggling and whispered conversation behind the scenes as the actors hastily concluded their preparations , but at length , in response to the enthusiastic clapping and stamping of the audience , the curtain was drawn aside by two Sunday-school teachers , and the play began", "context_len": 51}}}, "upheld": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Some of the men hastened to man the sea-boat , but the upheld hand of the Commander indicated that they were to `` stand fast ''", "context_len": 26}}}, "pillars": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}}}, "hangings": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 59, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}}}, "intoxicated": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 73, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "Though I never drank a daily dram , and sometimes not for six months , and was never intoxicated in my life , I \u2019 ve strong thoughts -- yes , I \u2019 ve very strong thoughts -- of leaving it off altogether", "context_len": 43}}}, "dries": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 89, "context": "So Guy told her about the wonderful bowers in the centre of large roses where the butterflies rest at night , of the great parlor in the middle of all , whose walls are of the palest rose and whose ceiling is upheld by pillars of gold , and of the bed chambers on either hand with their crimson hangings and their atmosphere of odors so sweet that the very butterflies sometimes become intoxicated with its deliciousness , and sleep until the rude sun opens their chamber doors and dries the dew-drops upon their wings", "context_len": 95}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Cotton gloves are a comfort in the season when kid shrivels and dries", "context_len": 13}}}, "parlors": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Only Lily , ma'am , and Mamie Stone , and a few more of the little ones from the hotel ; they were running about and making a great noise in the hall and parlors , and I thought I could keep them quiet for a while if Mr", "context_len": 50}}}, "revellers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": ", for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed , with feelings of envy , that many of my fellow-sufferers had already succumbed and become unconscious , if indeed they were not dead", "context_len": 128}}}, "dancers": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "But as the natives danced on , as tireless apparently as the ghosts that Bomba half-thought them , the mist began to clear and the sun struck through , causing the moisture upon the dark bodies of the dancers to glisten", "context_len": 41}}}, "recounting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "For a long time they patiently stood watching the dancers , and recounting to each other the origin of the tiny lamps they held", "context_len": 24}}}, "brightest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "It had the brightest of brown eyes , the pinkest of tongues , and the shrillest of barks , and it was altogether such a dear , enchanting , soft , curly morsel of puppyhood that Peggy took it to her arms and her heart at the same moment", "context_len": 49}}}, "commented": {"freq": 70, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 7, "context": "Florence 's change in circumstances were much commented upon by the other girls , and there is no doubt that in her neatly-fitting dress with her abundant pocket-money she did appear a more gracious and a more agreeable girl than she had done in the old days when her frock was shabby , her pinafore ugly , her pocket-money almost nil", "context_len": 61}}}, "shingle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I took the spark from a shingle roof , beneath which lay four little children asleep , '' she modestly answered", "context_len": 22}}}, "modestly": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 115, "context": "seen service under Prince Rupert ; Arthur Conolly , an Irish veteran who had served in the Low Countries , and who had come over from Chichester for the occasion ; Arthur Lewis , a gentleman of Bramshott ; Percy Young , an officer of the navy , who in his earlier days had lost a leg in the action of La Rochelle ; Herbert Collings , a master mariner of Gosport , who used to be a frequent visitor at our house , and who greatly interested me with the account of his adventures off the coast of Barbary ; and Giles Perrin , the landlord of the `` Flying Bull '' , who modestly seated himself on a stool in a remote corner of the room", "context_len": 128}}}, "fadeless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` That is a pretty story , '' said Aggie , contentedly , `` and I am going to bed now to dream all night of the good fly , and her fadeless lamp", "context_len": 34}}}, "delaying": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But there was no delaying or turning back then , and so they slowly continued their way , pausing but once to give a farewell cheer for the flag that floated from the fort , and to look at their rifles and say , `` We are ready for whatever may come ! ''", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "-- I have been delaying writing till I could give you news from Batala , -- that place towards which Missionary eyes longingly turn , as those of the Germans did towards Strasburg", "context_len": 33}}}, "rifles": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 11, "context": "( We would say to our readers , that the scythe rifles in those days were not made as at present , by putting sand or emery upon wood , with cement ; but they scratched the wood and made it rough , then smeared it with tallow , and put fine sand on it , which adhered to the tallow and the scratches", "context_len": 64}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Malcolm and Dick found bayonet exercise exciting work -- thrusting at suspended sacks stuffed with straw called for strength and strenuous activity -- while at the ranges both lads gained a high percentage of bulls , and in a very short while the `` crossed rifles `` , denoting marksmanship , ornamented the sleeves of their uniforms", "context_len": 57}}}, "sunbeams": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Spirits came and took the ebony from her hair , and covered it with gold ; they brought blue from the skies and prisoned it in her eyes ; the white stars laid their light upon her face , and sunbeams rendered her smile so warm and tender that it gladdened all upon whom it fell", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "There is actually a fireplace in it ! -- and the sunbeams stream in", "context_len": 14}}}, "halo": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Little Roy 's cheeks were slightly flushed ; his tangled yellow hair , ruffled more than ever by his running in the wind , surrounded his head like a halo ; and as gradually it dawned upon him that all those people surrounding him were strangers , his blue eyes filled with tears", "context_len": 53}}}, "vegetation": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 15, "context": "Then a gentle breeze would come and ruffle the surface of this vast sea of vegetation , and immediately a hundred shades , varying from the deepest green to the lightest gold , would dance up and down each separate blade , producing the most wonderful chaos of colors", "context_len": 49}}}, "deepest": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 27, "context": "Also his energy in teaching was reflected by her own burning desire , in old age , to speak on all occasions to the Natives of their deepest needs , and never to miss an opportunity of trying to lead some poor Hindu or Muhammadan to Christ , always with the vivid sense upon her , when she met man or woman , that the call to herself might come before they could meet again , and so a second opportunity might never recur", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Lights shone from the cabin windows , and red lamps gleamed from the bows of the boats ; but the tow-path , where Joe walked , was wrapped in the deepest gloom", "context_len": 32}}}, "blade": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 20, "context": "Why do n't you shout Westward Ho ! and come along o ' me ? I know many a roaring blade that would be proud to ship under such a captain as you 'd make ! '' Then leaning forward , he continued in a solemn whisper , `` What though I seem no better than a beggar -- cavado , cleaned out , as the Spaniards say -- if I could but get a loan of as much as would carry me across sea , I 'd be a rich man again", "context_len": 93}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "chaos": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 46, "context": "Then a gentle breeze would come and ruffle the surface of this vast sea of vegetation , and immediately a hundred shades , varying from the deepest green to the lightest gold , would dance up and down each separate blade , producing the most wonderful chaos of colors", "context_len": 49}}}, "refresh": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "And in the garden summer-house , where , a few days back , the secret meeting had been held , the materials for a most sumptuous feast were in readiness to refresh the weary warriors when the day 's work was done", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Some of the boys strolled in and helped me refresh ; but I have n't forgot my pals", "context_len": 18}}}, "traveler": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 84, "context": "`` First , Gervassen fell , almost at the side of Alstarnah : then , one by one , all the rest of the people sank down and were buried by the soft , white snow , until at last not one remained to tell of the verdure that once crowned the mountains where the North King still reigns , or of the people he slew with terrible arrows of snow , like those he still loves to throw in derision upon any daring traveler that attempts to invade his dominions", "context_len": 91}}}, "handsful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Guy gathered them by handsful and gave them to Aggie , who wove them into long wreaths which she hung around the wagon , when she declared it looked like a fairy bower", "context_len": 33}}}, "wreaths": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 19, "context": "Archie , who naturally was to the fore on such an occasion , managed to climb up and hang wreaths round the heads of the cherubs at the top of the Jacobean monument , which gave their weeping faces quite a festive appearance for once", "context_len": 45}}}, "bower": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 19, "context": "But these , after all , were not very great troubles , and were soon remedied ; for another bower was built beneath two tall althea bushes near the house , where no evil disposed person would dare to come ; and the boys took care not to leave their boats where they could be meddled with again", "context_len": 58}}}, "graze": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 62, "context": "The walk which Bobby had proposed this afternoon was somewhat of a scramble , for the country rose behind the Abbey into undulating hills , which were fairly steep , though not so high as the Welsh mountains , and were covered for the most part with gorse and rough grass , where the sheep and young bullocks were turned out to graze", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Ef I did n't know de diffunce I 'd turn myse ' f out ter graze wid de dry cattle , an ' stay wid um all thoo de season", "context_len": 30}}}, "belt": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 25, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Is your belt very loose ? '' asked the Good Wolf", "context_len": 12}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Some day I will tell you of our great clay belt further north", "context_len": 14}}}, "lagging": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Oh , did she ? All right ! Here , take the flask and hurry along , or mamma will give it to you for lagging so", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The man started away , but his lagging footsteps showed that he had something on his mind , and in a few moments I heard him coming back", "context_len": 28}}}, "exultant": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 14, "context": "I think he would not have gone so fast had he heard George 's exultant laugh as he turned to Gus with the remark , `` Is n't it jolly he 's gone , but if you tell that I sent him away , I 'll break your bones", "context_len": 49}}}, "specks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "George very boldly told them to `` come on , '' but Gus went close to Guy , and declared that such mere specks as they saw in the distance could n't be Indians ; yet was suddenly most anxious to know whether they were cannibals , and if so , whether he looked a tempting morsel or not", "context_len": 59}}}, "accomplish": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 11, "context": "Tell me an thou have a want , that I may accomplish it unto thee ; '' whereupon she kissed the earth a second time and called down blessings upon him , then answered , `` Ay , O King of the Age , as thy head liveth , I have indeed a want ; but before all things do thou give me thine assurance , [ 362 ] so I may make bold to prefer my suit to the hearing of our lord the Sultan , for that belike Thy Grace will find it a strange one", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 70, "context": "[ 361 ] Accordingly , one day of the days , she went to the Divan , as of her wont , and stood before the Sultan ; and when he saw her , he called his Vizier and said to him , `` Yonder is the woman of whom I bespoke thee yesterday ; bring her now before me , so I may see what her suit is and accomplish unto her her occasion", "context_len": 75}}}, "affright": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 67, "context": "`` When [ 409 ] the Sultan heard his daughter 's story , he was sore concerned and his eyes brimmed with tears ; then , sheathing the sword and coming up to her , he kissed her and said to her , `` O my daughter , why didst thou not tell me yesterday , so I might have warded off from thee the torment and affright which have befallen thee this night ? But no matter ; arise and put away from thee this thought , and to-night I will set over thee those who shall guard thee , so there shall not again befall thee that which befell yesternight", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "They were walking slowly past the baggage wagons when to his surprise , and affright Guy saw a puff of smoke , issue from the back part of the one in which he usually slept", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Leastwise I never care to go yonder missen , for strange noises affright the whole countryside ! ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "east": {"freq": 153, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 13, "context": "As the day went on , he saw that the coastline was now east and west , and felt that he must be off the most northerly point of the promontory ; he accordingly laid his course to the northeast , which would take him close to Cape Saloman , the most easterly point of Crete , and from two hundred and fifty to three hundred miles distant", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 108, "word_index": 94, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "Of the sun-worship , which underlies the religions of all the tribes in the Gulf territories , only slight intimations are contained in the Kasi'hta legend , and the important problem , whether the Creeks ever crossed the Mississippi river from west to east in their migrations , seems to be settled by it in the negative , although other legends may be adduced as speaking in its favor", "context_len": 69}}}, "ejaculations": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "At first he ran wildly about , uttering broken ejaculations of alarm , then he sat down and burst into tears , it was so dreadful to be on that vast prairie alone", "context_len": 33}}}, "overcharged": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "He soon grew calm for his tears relieved his overcharged heart", "context_len": 11}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Schwitz has overcharged us for certain ; and I do n't believe it will stand in law", "context_len": 17}}}, "oppressed": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 13, "context": "Oh , well , said Marie-Celeste with a sigh , as though becoming oppressed with the greatness of her undertaking ; besides the Banqueting Hall there 's the Grand Reception-Room , with a beautiful plate-glass window forming almost all of one end of it , and there 's the Waterloo Room , filled with portraits of officers who fought there , and then , in a place they call the Grand Vestibule , there 's a splendid statue of the Queen", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "But to sail the South seas ! to harry the Spaniard ! to free the oppressed Indians ! A sort of fever seemed to possess him , and rouse him from the apathy that had fallen on him", "context_len": 38}}}, "vows": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 2, "context": "If our vows had not forbidden our fighting I should have called him to account long ago ; but the only thing else to do was for me to lodge a formal complaint before the bailiff , of his continually offensive bearing and manner , which I could not bring myself to do , and indeed there was no special matter that would have seemed to justify me , no single speech that in itself would warrant such grave action on my part", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "I \u2019 ve got to down Stone , and I will ; but I can \u2019 t afford to hurt myself while I \u2019 m about it , and , with Eliot taking the stand he vows he will take , it will be necessary for me to try other tactics", "context_len": 51}}}, "solitude": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 32, "context": "I was not permitted to show myself in the doorway of my dwelling , or even to approach it nearly enough to see what was going on ; and in this dreadful solitude , waiting and hoping for I knew not what impossible happening to occur and effect my deliverance , each day seemed to drag itself out to the length of a month -- until the darkness came ; and then , with the realisation of the fact that I was so much nearer to a hideous fate , the hours seemed suddenly to have sped with lightning swiftness", "context_len": 100}}}, "indicate": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 15, "context": "What the fate of the rest of us was to be there was nothing to indicate , but I had no doubt that it would be something quite as dreadful as fire ; and I had fully made up my mind that when my turn came I would endeavour , by insult and invective , to goad my tormentors to such a state of fury and exasperation as should provoke them to finish me off quickly", "context_len": 76}}}, "reproached": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 1, "context": "She reproached him for having appealed to Hannah , a servant in his father 's house , for aid ; and in her turn had to hear his reproaches for believing that he would condescend to such a thing , and received an emphatic and solemn denial that he had been guilty of this , or that he had ever let Hannah know of the straits he was in", "context_len": 69}}}, "trustfulness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Oh ! how bitterly he reproached himself for his foolish trustfulness in George Harwood , and again for ever having persuaded his mother to undertake such a perilous journey", "context_len": 29}}}, "pall": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 72, "context": "In despair he lifted his voice and halloed wildly , crying for help from God and man , but no answer came , while still the sky grew a deeper blue , the sun a more glorious scarlet , till at last when it had gained its utmost magnificence , it suddenly dropped beneath the prairie , the green grass grew darker and darker , and at last lay like a black pall around poor Guy , as he stood alone in the awful solitude", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "Sometimes I ask myself , -- Now , in my present position , if I had no pleasure in religion , if everything connected with that were cut off , what would be left me ? -- what would life be to me ? O Leila , what a tasteless , what a bitter thing ! We want delights that will not grow old , that will never pall , that will be just as fresh and lovely at eighty as at eighteen", "context_len": 83}}}, "wolf": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 64, "word_index": 65, "context": "Two young theows , whose fathers had gone to the war , but who had been left behind as being too young to share its dangers , although in the flush of early youth , accompanied them , and were soon loaded with the lighter game their masters had killed , while a deer they had slain was hung in the trees , where a wolf could not reach it , and where wayfarers were not likely to pass until the sportsmen should return for their own", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Was that wolf a dangerous beast ? '' asked Dallas sharply", "context_len": 12}}}, "ferocious": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 21, "context": "At last the weather became very cold , and being without a fire , his sufferings were great , until his ferocious gaoler , finding him quite stiffened , brought up a brazier of coals , which saved his prisoner 's life , while it filled the room with smoke , which could only escape by the crevices in walls and roof , for to open a window would have been as bad as to dispense with the fire , such was the state of the outer air", "context_len": 88}}}, "entreat": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Alas , alas ! and did I not kneel and entreat my dearest husband to heed the words of those good men if he would not mine ? How happy we might have been , even in a hut among the savages ! And you , too , Dick , '' she said tenderly , `` you would have liked well to follow Master Perrient 's leading ; and my dear husband was ready to have you go , seeing all he and Sir Gyles Perrient had set their minds upon for your happiness", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` For the sake of him who hath even now rendered up himself as a martyr for the Lord 's cause , I may not be angry with any word of yours , '' he answered sadly ; `` but I do entreat of you to take heed ! Would you lay down your arms and live in peace among your cattle and your corn , coached and complimented into effeminacy and foolishness ? Oh , for shame ! Rub your eyes and look about you ! What was the fate of the men of Sodom when they thought Lot was one that mocked when he warned them ! ''", "context_len": 110}}}, "prostrated": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "Mother was prostrated , and I thought she would surely die then ; but she finally rallied , although she carried a constant pain in her heart , as if the bullet that slew my father had lodged in her breast", "context_len": 41}}}, "petitions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "So , in the agony of his terror , he prostrated himself upon the ground , and offered up his petitions", "context_len": 21}}}, "phenomenon": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 60, "context": "Whenever an eclipse of the moon takes place , it is , of course , when the moon is full , so that the eclipse is always a sudden , and , among an ignorant people , an unexpected waning of the orb in the height of its splendor ; and as such people know not the cause of the phenomenon , they are often extremely terrified", "context_len": 67}}}, "rapturously": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "Suddenly he paused , and burst into a fit of laughter , exclaiming rapturously ; `` It is no moon ; it is a camp fire ! There ! I can count one , two , three , of them , They are the fires of our own camp", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Her mother sprang up and kissed her rapturously , crying -- 'Is n't she the dearest and sweetest thing and the smartest ! Think of her learning that and acting it off so completely , and not three years old ! She is smarter than Violet ' -- and then Violet set up such a howl ! Her mother pacified her by saying Marilla should tell her a piece , and after several efforts Cinderella did induce her to say by a great deal of prompting 'Milkman , Milkman , where have you been ? ' Think of the wear on the child 's nerves , and she looked so tired", "context_len": 111}}}, "headlong": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "For two or three strides it seemed that he must plunge headlong with it , and then he regained his equilibrium and brought a gasping chorus of cries from the southern side of the field as he ran on toward Clearport \u2019 s goal", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 106, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}}}, "retarded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "The moon he had so anxiously looked for , gave no indication of her presence in the heavens , and so Guy 's progress was much retarded for the want of light , for the stars were often overwhelmed by great banks of clouds , and gave but a feeble ray at best", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "As a wheel landed in a rut full of thick mud , their pace was momentarily retarded", "context_len": 17}}}, "ray": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 89, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}}}, "repress": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 6, "context": "The Danes found it hard to repress their laughter at the thought of the reception which awaited the travellers at home ; they had no idea of spoiling it by attacking them , although the numbers were about equal ; besides , they had got all the plunder and spoil , and a battle would only endanger the success already obtained", "context_len": 61}}}, "prowling": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "Even the bear meat is safe , '' and he looked to where it was suspended on a tree limb , by means of a long rope , a precaution taken to keep it out of the way of prowling animals", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 52, "context": "How queer it is , he said to himself , that I should feel constrained in this way to run out to Windsor ! Land knows ! I have no desire to come to be on intimate terms of acquaintance with Evelyn 's boys ; and what would be the satisfaction of prowling around just to see where they live ? Their father gave me up after that time he spoke his mind so freely about my aimless life -- as he was pleased to call it -- and there is no reason whatever why I should bother myself about my sister 's children , since she , poor thing ! is dead and gone , and they have enough of this world 's goods to make them", "context_len": 128}}}, "primer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "He especially remembered one he had read in an old primer , of little Harry who was eaten by lions for saying `` I wo n't '' to his mother", "context_len": 30}}}, "stray": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 52, "context": "He was thankful to know , that there were no lions on the prairies , and that he had never said `` I wo n't , '' to his mother , but he very much feared he had said things just as bad , and that prairie wolves , or even a stray bear , might be lying in wait to devour him for it", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "The habitat of an animal is the region where he makes his home : sometimes a wild beast takes it into his head to stray a good many miles from where he has been brought up and educated", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Why , pard , this isn \u2019 t a time to be sorry about anything ! You \u2019 ve still got the colonel \u2019 s gold in your safe , and I \u2019 m the happiest stray in all New York ! You hear that , Matt ? and he whirled and caught his chum by both hands", "context_len": 58}}}, "precipitate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Without pausing even for an instant to listen , he set off at full speed toward the still glowing fires , till his precipitate flight was arrested by some obstacle , over which he fell , reaching the ground with a shock that almost stunned him", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Before the officer could perform this duty the rear-guard was compelled to retreat on the main body in the most precipitate manner", "context_len": 22}}}, "arrested": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 54, "context": "The wood-pile was composed mostly of logs eight feet in length : while the others poured water on the ends of the sticks , Ben , catching hold of them , dragged them from the pile to a safe distance from the house , and , after a long and desperate struggle , they arrested the progress of the flames", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They are said to speak with an abundance of arrested sounds or voice-checks , from which they start again with a jerk of the voice", "context_len": 25}}}, "stunned": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 63, "context": "The English tourist had been awakened by the discussion , and comprehending from what he heard that Gerald was in a tight place , he hastily threw on his clothes , and at the right time darted out of the cabin , seized Jake by the collar with one hand while with the other he planted a blow in his face , nearly stunned him , and dragging him from Gerald hurled him forcibly upon the ground six feet away", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "Still I did not speak , and I am sure my silence , and the stunned subdued heavy expression on my face , vexed Jane more than any amount of words I might have uttered", "context_len": 35}}}, "ankle": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 32, "context": "Then , in batches of ten at a time , forty more victims were triced up to the boughs of this accursed tree by raw-hide ropes fastened to one wrist or one ankle , in such positions that their bodies showed clearly against the bright background of sky ; and , while thus suspended , whosoever would was at liberty to shoot at them with bows and arrows , the great object being , apparently , to pierce the body with as many arrows as possible without inflicting a mortal injury", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Children stood ankle deep in water , pumping hour after hour , and their work was sometimes prolonged for thirty-six hours continuously ; so that it was no wonder the children died early , that they suffered much from disease , and led cheerless , wretched lives", "context_len": 47}}}, "sprained": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "He thought at first that his leg was broken , but after a careful examination , came to the conclusion that his ankle was sprained , but even a broken leg would not have been a greater misfortune then , for he was unable to walk , and was suffering the most excruciating pain", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 60, "context": "Father missed you , but thought you were somewhere about the wagons , and I dared not tell him you were not ; but Gus turned coward during the afternoon , and told that I had sent you away -- and then did n't I catch it ? '' and George grimaced most dolefully , pointing to poor Guy 's sprained ankle , and declaring that the pain of that was nothing to what he had had in his back for days past", "context_len": 83}}}, "excruciating": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "He thought at first that his leg was broken , but after a careful examination , came to the conclusion that his ankle was sprained , but even a broken leg would not have been a greater misfortune then , for he was unable to walk , and was suffering the most excruciating pain", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "But , oh , what exquisite torment was mine when , my bonds being released , the blood once more began to circulate through my benumbed members ! I could have screamed aloud with the excruciating agony , had not my pride prevented me ; and it was a full hour before I had sufficiently recovered the use of my hands to enable me to convey food and drink to my lips", "context_len": 72}}}, "indications": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 64, "context": "-- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me , died out , and I began to prepare myself , as best I could , to", "context_len": 128}}}, "evils": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 27, "context": "The only person who was not won over by Archie 's friendly ways and frank American manners was Joe , who gloomily prophesied broken necks and kindred evils as the result of the children 's association with 'the young master from furrin parts ' ; but I fear there was a good deal of jealousy in this , for poor Joe had been a hero to the children in his modest way , and it was hard to find himself suddenly supplanted in their affections , especially by a rival with whom it was quite impossible to compete", "context_len": 98}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I sha'n't , if she evils my Bessie , '' said Maggie , stoutly", "context_len": 15}}}, "bemoaning": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "bemoan": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "But he had not long to bemoan her presence , and to say , that she hid the light of home from him , for she soon plunged into a great bank of clouds ; a fearful blast of wind swept by , and Guy was drenched with rain", "context_len": 49}}}, "blast": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 46, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "The cold east wind , for it was but the beginning of March , in the year of grace 1660 , whistled through the clump of gaunt pine trees that marked the summit of the hill , and , despite the fact that each of us wore a thick doublet , the chilly blast cut us like a knife", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "He entered the hall at first unnoticed , but the merry laughter and cheerful conversation withered before his presence , as of one who came to blast it", "context_len": 28}}}, "cheerily": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 31, "context": "But working with a will sometimes means nothing more than determination to do one 's duty ; and from what we happen to know , Chris would much have preferred setting cheerily forth on his round in Uncle Sam 's far-away city , and Donald was probably dreaming of the blue boundless sea and the steamer ploughing its way in the teeth of a driving nor'easter", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They went on deck as Captain Bracewell called out cheerily :", "context_len": 11}}}, "icicle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "Graham , it 's cold enough up there to kill his mother , make an icicle of her before Christmas , you know you said last night it was", "context_len": 29}}}, "consuming": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "He had no idea where he was , and talked constantly to his mother , whom he fancied was beside him , entreating her to put out the fire that was consuming him", "context_len": 33}}}, "signaling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Suddenly he heard his name called , and realizing his position , and springing to his feet , in spite of his wounded limb , halloed loudly , waving his white handkerchief and signaling frantically to a horseman that appeared in the distance", "context_len": 43}}}, "horseman": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 6, "context": "At the same moment , another horseman , on another side , had his sword raised , and would have killed Alexander before he could have turned to defend himself , had no help intervened ; but just at this instant a third combatant , one of Alexander 's friends , seeing the danger , brought down so terrible a blow upon the shoulder of this second assailant as to separate his arm from his body", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Moreover , since the force at the station had increased from three to four men , one of them must be the horseman whom Alden met , and who refused to speak a word to him", "context_len": 36}}}, "unheard": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "For a few dreadful moments he was unheard , and unseen , then a shout of joy , answered his screams , and the horseman galloped rapidly toward him , and in a few minutes the poor boy lay fainting , but saved , in the arms of James Graham !", "context_len": 51}}}, "screams": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 20, "context": "The scene was indeed a terrible one , the roar of cannon , the shouts of the combatants , the screams of the poor wretches upon whom the terrible Greek fire fell , the clash of arms and the shouts and cries of the Turks as they pressed across the bridge , united in a din that thrilled with horror the spectators , both in the city and on St", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The moment the little one set eyes on him , she screams out as joyful as you please , 'Oh , it 's my soldier , it 's my soldier ! '", "context_len": 32}}}, "regained": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 29, "context": "We were subject to scoffs and blows ; now , thank God , we have swords to strike with , and though as yet our arms may not have regained their full strength , we could at least bear a share in a fray", "context_len": 44}}}, "exceed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 46, "context": "But , on the other side , emigration was favored by the Creek communities themselves through the practice observed by some of their number to send away a part of their young men to form branch villages , whenever the number of the inhabitants began to exceed two hundred", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 69, "context": "But , when he saw that she heartened herself for that which he set forth to her of its virtues , he feared lest she should talk of this to the folk , so he said to her , `` O my mother , beware lest thou bespeak any of the lamp and its uses , for that this is our fortune ; be careful [ 345 ] and exceed not in speech thereof to any one , lest we lose it and lose this our present prosperity , for that it is from it", "context_len": 95}}}, "grimaced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "Father missed you , but thought you were somewhere about the wagons , and I dared not tell him you were not ; but Gus turned coward during the afternoon , and told that I had sent you away -- and then did n't I catch it ? '' and George grimaced most dolefully , pointing to poor Guy 's sprained ankle , and declaring that the pain of that was nothing to what he had had in his back for days past", "context_len": 83}}}, "declaring": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 25, "context": "Harris , to whom the idea had first occurred , suggested it , Marie-Celeste had put her two arms round her father 's neck , declaring he was just a darling and yet , when you come to think of it , he was the very same old curmudgeon of a papa , and not one whit altered either , who had been so soundly berated for insisting that it would be better for Donald to have some easy work to do than to idle away the whole summer", "context_len": 89}}}, "exposure": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 60, "context": "But Dolly turned sulky in her turn , and refused to say a word more ; and Lem , knowing it was useless to try to make her speak when she did not choose , strolled into the woods to see if he could find any berries for his breakfast ; while she , still shivering from her night 's exposure in the Ice Glen , tried to kindle a fire from the wet sticks which lay around ; and finding this in vain , crept to her wretched bed , and tried to warm herself there", "context_len": 97}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "He received no answer ; Charlie was fast asleep ; and all three of them were soon buried in those refreshing slumbers that succeed to exercise and exposure in the open air", "context_len": 32}}}, "fretted": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 1, "context": "It fretted him sorely when they camped at night , to see how hard she must have worked while he lay ill ; yet he could but perceive that she looked better and stronger than she had done since his father 's death , and joyfully felt that the excitement and toil of a journey across the plains would restore his mother to health , whatever might be the effect upon him", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The droves seen in the distance seemed often to cover a fourth of the horizon , and their dark , shaggy backs as they cropped the herbage and hitched continually forward , were like the fretted waves of the sea", "context_len": 40}}}, "sorely": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 58, "context": "Therefore , as soon as we engaged her , and I found that she was full of troops and could not be captured without heavy loss , and that two of her consorts might arrive before we accomplished it , it was plainly my duty to abandon the attempt , although , you may guess , it went sorely against the grain to give the order , especially as I knew that a host would be looking on from St", "context_len": 80}}}, "perceive": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 53, "context": "I feel nearly mad when I think of your sufferings ; but never mind , once the Scholarship is won and I am declared to the world to be the Cherry Court Scholarship girl , once I am crowned queen on the great day of the Scholarship competition , I shall , I perceive well , be able to do exactly what I like with Aunt Susan , and then be sure you shall not want", "context_len": 76}}}, "regaining": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "I could now see how near I had been to regaining my freedom , for just beyond the place where my tour of exploration had abruptly terminated was the entrance to the cave , skilfully hidden by a heavy screen of painted canvas that , even at a short distance , would deceive all who were not acquainted with the secret", "context_len": 61}}}, "degrees": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 21, "context": "Fortunately , the sun 's rays came down hot and bright , and the rock grew warmer , so that by degrees the terribly void look began to leave Mark Penelly 's face , and at last , when Harry held out his hand , saying , `` Do you feel better ? '' Mark Penelly caught it in both of his , clung to it , and , turning half over on his face , laid his forehead against it , and , forgetting his years of manhood , lay there in his weakness , and sobbed and cried like a child", "context_len": 103}}}, "thinner": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 21, "context": "Randolph went away , she had been taking less and less pains with her dress ; her hair was rough and thinner than ever ; her little round figure had fallen away ; she seemed to have aged by many years", "context_len": 41}}}, "clustering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Guy was not sorry to see the thick grass become thinner and thinner , for he remembered that amid its clustering blades he had nearly lost his life , and therefore looked with much complacency upon the broad , shallow river , along which their course lay ; the sandy loam beneath their feet , and the sand hills that arose like great billows of earth , rolling in regular succession over the level surface", "context_len": 75}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Still farther down , the valleys with their glistening ponds and streams , and the villages clustering here and there , their houses and churches looking from this height almost as small as toys ; while far in the distance , flashing in the sunlight , rolled the noble river up whose waters they had come that morning", "context_len": 58}}}, "complacency": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 34, "context": "Guy was not sorry to see the thick grass become thinner and thinner , for he remembered that amid its clustering blades he had nearly lost his life , and therefore looked with much complacency upon the broad , shallow river , along which their course lay ; the sandy loam beneath their feet , and the sand hills that arose like great billows of earth , rolling in regular succession over the level surface", "context_len": 75}}}, "loam": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "Guy was not sorry to see the thick grass become thinner and thinner , for he remembered that amid its clustering blades he had nearly lost his life , and therefore looked with much complacency upon the broad , shallow river , along which their course lay ; the sandy loam beneath their feet , and the sand hills that arose like great billows of earth , rolling in regular succession over the level surface", "context_len": 75}}}, "billows": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 63, "context": "Guy was not sorry to see the thick grass become thinner and thinner , for he remembered that amid its clustering blades he had nearly lost his life , and therefore looked with much complacency upon the broad , shallow river , along which their course lay ; the sandy loam beneath their feet , and the sand hills that arose like great billows of earth , rolling in regular succession over the level surface", "context_len": 75}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The Roanoke heaved up her black side until he saw smears of a yellow liquid trickle from several pipes , and spread out over the frothing billows in shimmering sheets", "context_len": 30}}}, "oasis": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "George and Gus thought the country most dreary and wretched , and would scarcely believe Guy , when he told them of a desert called Sahara , that had not even a blade of grass upon it , save an occasional oasis , many miles apart , and which were often sought for , by the weary traveler , as he had himself sought the camp , during his terrible night on the prairie", "context_len": 74}}}, "contended": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "The lad looked up sadly , yet firmly ; the expression of his face , whereon filial awe contended with yet higher feelings of duty , was very touching", "context_len": 29}}}, "cannibals": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 45, "context": "George very boldly told them to `` come on , '' but Gus went close to Guy , and declared that such mere specks as they saw in the distance could n't be Indians ; yet was suddenly most anxious to know whether they were cannibals , and if so , whether he looked a tempting morsel or not", "context_len": 59}}}, "proceed": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 66, "context": "In due course she was adjudicated upon and condemned by the Mixed Commission ; but I did not remain at Sierra Leone for that to take place ; for upon our arrival we found that a packet had come in from England a few days previously bringing letters for me , acquainting me with the sad news of my father 's death and urging me to proceed home immediately to supervise the winding up of his affairs , and to assume the management of the very important property that he had left behind him", "context_len": 94}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "I bear orders to the fleet at Portsmouth that they all , with the exception of the Naseby , the name of which giveth offence to His Majesty , proceed to the Downs , there to welcome our sovereign lord", "context_len": 40}}}, "apprehend": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 42, "context": "The prisoner strode up and down the limited area , wrestling with self , bending the will by prayer to submit to ignominy and pain , for he knew now that his father had abandoned him , and that he had to apprehend the worst ; still he did not regret the choice he had made , and he felt , as he prayed , peace and confidence descend like heavenly dew upon his soul", "context_len": 75}}}, "palm": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 48, "context": "When my turn came I leapt with all the buoyancy of youth on to the lowermost crag , grasping an overhanging tuft of reeds and grass to gain the next step ; but the reeds were as sharp as a razor , and before I realized it the palm of my hand was cut to the bone , and the blood poured in a stream down my arm and over my doublet", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "If Pearl Dimmock gets into your clothes and tries to palm herself off as Joe McGlory , the bank people are going to get suspicious", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was like a palm tree but it had great balls hanging from it and something queer was going on high up among the branches", "context_len": 25}}}, "posture": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "However , mounted messengers were sent off at once , up and down the coast , to give warning to the inhabitants of the towns to put themselves into a posture of defence , and to the villagers to fly with their wives and families into the interior as soon as they saw galleys of doubtful appearance approaching", "context_len": 58}}}, "indicative": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "They , upon this , stopped their horses , and consulted together a few moments , then fell into a posture indicative of rest", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Her very walk was indicative of this ; the elastic springiness of every step", "context_len": 14}}}, "hue": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 55, "context": "Nevertheless , mine enemies found it an easy thing to bring witnesses to swear I was seen in Venner 's company , and pressed hard on my hiding-place ; so seeing I was not wealthy enough or easy enough to bribe their witnesses to refrain from lies , I e'en fled , and have the hue and cry after me for a dangerous plotter", "context_len": 64}}}, "pennants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The hair of the men was very long , and streamed like black pennants , upon the wind", "context_len": 18}}}, "warrior": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 43, "context": "Swan , after whose report this passage is quoted ( Schoolcraft V , 279 ) states that the title of the chief of the confederacy was the great beloved man , while Milfort , who was himself invested with the charge of great warrior of the nation , styles him `` Le Tastan\u00e9gy ou grand chef de guerre , '' adding , however , that in his time he was the highest authority in civil and military affairs ( M\u00e9moire , Note to p", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` Here , then , my boy , on this spot where thou wert once cradled , renounce all these Christian follies and superstitions , and thou shalt go back with me to the camp of King Sweyn , where thou shalt be received as the descendant of warrior kings , and shalt forget that thou , the falcon , wert ever the inmate of the dovecote", "context_len": 67}}}, "osage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Guy thought that the men as they sat proudly upon their beautiful horses , holding in their hands long bows made of the tough wood of the osage orange , which is as supple as elastic , looked very noble and fine", "context_len": 42}}}, "sinews": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "Of average height , Anlaf possessed vast muscular powers ; his sinews stood out like tight cords , and his frame , although robust , was yet such that there seemed no useless flesh about him", "context_len": 36}}}, "flexible": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "With the anti-gas apparatus he had been able to withstand the choking fumes , until a chance splinter of metal had perforated the flexible pipe between the Hun 's mouth and the oxygen-container hidden under his back-plate", "context_len": 37}}}, "triangular": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "There was another rush to the ship \u2019 s side , and I saw a dozen dark forms struggling in a smother of foam-lashed water under our quarter , while the triangular fins of several sharks showed that the culminating tragedy had occurred", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Look there , Crayle , he said , pointing to a black triangular fin that showed above the oncoming wave", "context_len": 20}}}, "intersecting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The arrows were about twenty inches long , of flexible wood , with a triangular point of iron at one end , and two feathers intersecting each other at right angles , at the opposite extremity", "context_len": 36}}}, "diverted": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 106, "context": "Instructions were therefore given to the two knights that , while Georges was to be treated with all courtesy , he was to be strictly watched , though in such a manner that he should be in ignorance of it , and that , whenever he turned his steps in the direction of those parts of the defences where fresh works had been recently added and preparations made of which it was desirable the Turks should be kept in ignorance , he was to be met , as if by accident , by one of the knights told off for the purpose , and his steps diverted in another direction", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The arrival of another wayfarer next diverted my thoughts", "context_len": 9}}}, "tattooed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Their faces were tattooed in the most uncouth devices , and altogether they appeared quite hideous , as they sat upon their horses , in the same position as the men , regarding with much interest the movements of their chief who had been made to understand that he might come alone to the train", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Let your men bare his back , good sir , and if he hath not the sign of the Jolly Roger tattooed there , I \u2019 ll take back my word", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I was always under the impression that New Zealanders were black , tattooed savages", "context_len": 14}}}, "uncouth": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "Their faces were tattooed in the most uncouth devices , and altogether they appeared quite hideous , as they sat upon their horses , in the same position as the men , regarding with much interest the movements of their chief who had been made to understand that he might come alone to the train", "context_len": 55}}}, "propriety": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "It almost shocks my sense of propriety , young girls travelling at night , -- it is funny even to an old lady , hurrying up and down a bustling platform amongst Natives", "context_len": 33}}}, "protestations": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 57, "context": "Irene had not been in church since she was a tiny child , when she had screamed loudly , uttered naughty words , declared that the clergyman had no right to come in in his night-gown , and , in short , disgraced herself so thoroughly that she was carried out amidst a tempest of tears and protestations", "context_len": 58}}}, "token": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 63, "context": "His mother was perplexed at his case , unknowing what had befallen him , and bethought herself that belike he was sick ; so she came up to him and questioned him , saying , `` O my son , an thou feel aught of pain or otherwhat , tell me , that I may go fetch thee a physician , more by token there is presently in the city a physician from the land of the Arabs , whom the Sultan hath sent to bring hither , and report saith of him that he is exceeding skilful ; so [ tell me ] if thou art sick , that I may go and call him to thee", "context_len": 118}}}, "ornaments": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 23, "context": "The big drawing-room had been shut up for many years ; the Queen-Anne chairs and china-cabinets were swathed in wrappers , and the ornaments put away in boxes ; but sometimes the children would steal in and open the shutters to look at the portraits which hung upon the oak-panelled walls -- stately gentlemen with wigs and lace frills , whose eyes seemed to follow you about the room ; haughty dames with powdered hair and patches ; stiff little girls in hoops and mittens , and pretty young ladies attired as shepherdesses or classic goddesses , with cupids and nymphs in the background", "context_len": 104}}}, "daub": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "At that the old man was delighted , for the Indians are very fond of beads and all kinds of ornaments , and of paints , with which they daub their faces and arms in the most grotesque manner , upon any grand occasion", "context_len": 44}}}, "grotesque": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "They had gone but a few yards farther , Bomba \u2019 s eyes straining to detect the faintly marked trail , when there was a thud , and on the ground before them , directly in their path , appeared a figure so grotesque in form and ugly in face that Bomba took a startled step backward and the two slaves fell to the ground in a fit of shuddering terror", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 18, "context": "Alden \u2019 s belief was that the monstrous animal would keep on with his ponderous gait in the grotesque attempt to overtake the fleet footed horse which was safe beyond his reach ; but to the consternation of the watcher the brute halted at the very spot where the fugitive had landed when he dropped from the saddle", "context_len": 58}}}, "disdained": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "But the old chief disdained to exhibit any satisfaction , and smoked the pipe , that had been offered him , in the most indifferent manner while the presents were being procured from the wagons", "context_len": 35}}}, "exhibit": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "Professor Long had heard of my cousin , and knew him to be harmless , but William got so excited on the subject of turtles that the professor concluded it would not be best to exhibit the school collection , so he refused", "context_len": 43}}}, "procured": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 39, "context": "On the return of the knight with the news that it would be at least a fortnight before Genoa could fit out any galleys , and fearing that the pirates might at any moment put to sea , we procured some small Sardinian craft , and fitted them as fire ships ; with the captives we had rescued , and some Sard fishermen , we manned the three prizes , distributing the knights between them , and at night launched the fire ships against the corsairs , whose ships were crowded together", "context_len": 92}}}, "hesitation": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 75, "context": "You may meet with vessels on your way south , and doubtless some of them cruise off the barren coast I speak of , to intercept traders to and from Egypt and Acre , and other Syrian ports ; for the trade carried on is considerable , and , although of the same religion , the Turks are disposed to view the Egyptians as enemies rather than as friends , and would have even less hesitation in plundering them than in robbing their own countrymen", "context_len": 85}}}, "grunt": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 67, "context": "Just what he said to the boys , Edna never knew , but it is a matter of comment that from that day on there were no more tricks played on old Nathan Keener , and though the big stick was not so much in evidence , it was a long time before any of the Elderflowers made any headway in winning even so much as a grunt from him", "context_len": 70}}}, "evident": {"freq": 144, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 143, "word_index": 8, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "That was exactly what Donald felt sure of , but he contrived to say , I did n't suppose you 'd see the need of its being kept -- I 'm glad if you do ; but there was no real gladness evident , for Donald 's tone was hopeless in the extreme", "context_len": 53}}}, "shield": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "With a cry of grief and fury Gervaise sprang to him , receiving as he did so several blows on his armour and shield intended for the fallen knight , and , standing across him , showered his blows with such strength and swiftness that the janissaries shrank back before the sweep of the flashing steel", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 89, "context": "Can he speak thus who hath so long espoused The Royal cause , and served that cause so well ? Who , girt with honours , well deserved , hath stood One in a noble Brotherhood of Fame ! Where are the Cavaliers who fought with thee In battle , side by side , who with thee shared The feast , and drained the wine-cup to your King ? Where are they now ? what , gone ? not one remains , T \u2019 assert thy innocence , or shield thee from An ignominious death", "context_len": 95}}}, "formidable": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 82, "context": "I may here mention that I never again saw the party of savages who brought me up the river , and I was therefore strengthened in the conclusion at which I had arrived that they had gone to all the trouble of conveying me that long distance in order that they might make a present of me , possibly as a peace-offering from their tribe , to King Banda , who , I soon had reason to believe , was a decidedly formidable potentate , as African kings went", "context_len": 89}}}, "withe": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "The two ends of the withe formed a handle about fourteen inches long , and were bound together with strips of buffalo hide , which rendered it strong and firm , totally preventing it from either splitting down , or breaking when used , as no doubt it often was , with great force , upon the heads of unfortunate enemies", "context_len": 61}}}, "groove": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Searching farther , they found a most singular-shaped stone , with an edge like an axe , and near the top a groove nearly half an inch in depth all around it ; also , a pipe , a piece of bone pointed at one end , and in the other a hole , and a tooth pointed , exceedingly hard and white", "context_len": 63}}}, "prettiest": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` Oh , that is all very fine , '' he said , `` but come now ; what have I done to make myself obnoxious ? There is the old lady upstairs , she has taken no end of a fancy to you , she says you are the most charming and the prettiest girl she has ever seen , and what have you to say against my mother ? Let me tell you that she has come to this house on purpose to make your acquaintance", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "At the time of our introduction to the little maid in question she has donned the prettiest of white embroidered dresses and a broad white sash ( which she first tied in a great bow in front and then pulled round to where it belongs in the back ) , and has come down to the front steps to watch for somebody", "context_len": 62}}}, "frowning": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Dear me , mother , '' said Florence , frowning slightly , `` what is the good of going over that now ? Uncle Tom has been in his grave for the last six years , has n't he ? and Aunt Susan rules the roost", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Their glances met and clashed , and , startled by the strange look on Ben \u2019 s face , Jimmy glanced round , discovering the frowning , dark-faced intruder", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "' '' Judge not the Lord by feeble sense , But trust Him for His grace : Behind a frowning providence He hides a smiling face", "context_len": 26}}}, "levelled": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 57, "context": "The rigid simplicity which she cultivated was , no doubt , partly a matter of pure economy , that she might have the more to give away , -- partly a matter of her innate generosity ; but partly also it arose from a deep-rooted desire to remove the reproach , which has of late been often levelled at the ease and luxury , real or supposed , of many Missionaries in India or elsewhere", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "At the sight of six levelled weapons the crowd drew back ; then , satisfied that the cowards were properly cowed , Felgate jumped from his horse , made his way to the ducking stool , and cut the bonds that held the old crone to the chair", "context_len": 48}}}, "obedience": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 109, "context": "[ 358 ] Hast thou any knowledge of her , O Vizier , and knowest thou what is her want ? '' `` O our lord the Sultan , '' replied the Vizier , `` verily women are little of wit ; and most like this woman cometh to complain to thee of her husband or one of her folk , '' The Sultan was not content with the Vizier 's reply , but bade him , an she came again to the Divan , bring her before him forthright ; [ 359 ] whereupon the Vizier laid his hand on his head and answered , `` Hearkening and obedience , O our lord the Sultan", "context_len": 116}}}, "galloping": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 56, "context": "All rose to look at them , not without anxiety ; as yet they were far distant , across a wild moor , but as they drew nearer , and their standards could be more clearly discerned , it became gradually evident that it was a reinforcement ; and so it proved , for heralds , galloping forward , announced the men of Dorsetshire", "context_len": 64}}}, "arrow": {"freq": 143, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 135, "word_index": 74, "context": "Never in my life had I felt more deadly cool and self-possessed than I did then as I aimed steadily at the animal 's right eye ; I felt that I could not miss ; nor did I ; for while we thus stood motionlessly staring at each other , I released the string , and the next instant the great lithe beast sprang convulsively into the air , with the butt of my arrow protruding from his eye and the point buried deep in his brain", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "When Alden looked back as he did several times , he saw the warrior still on his perch , and watching him , but the huge bow in his hand was not raised nor was another arrow drawn to the head , while the horseman was within reach of the primitive weapon", "context_len": 52}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The speed of Dick rapidly carried him and his rider beyond reach of any bow and arrow ever devised", "context_len": 19}}}, "juncture": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 2, "context": "At this juncture the Vizier presented himself and as he entered , he espied the new palace and the carpet and marvelled also ; so , when he came in to the Sultan , the twain fell to talking of this strange matter and marvelling , for that they saw a thing which amazed the beholder and dilated the heart ; and they said , `` Verily , methinketh kings may not avail unto the building of the like of this palace", "context_len": 82}}}, "signaled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "Thar \u2019 s only one way to explanify it , he answered ; the varmint whose scalp ye left on top of his head was so thankful that he signaled ahead to the other three varmints not to hurt ye , \u2019 cause ye and him war friends", "context_len": 48}}}, "retire": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 40, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "He had been known to go to the largest dealers in Christ-church and purchase the swiftest car procurable , drive it at breakneck speed until he collided with something , and then sell the remains and retire to his pah until he found an opportunity for another exuberance of pecuniary extravagance", "context_len": 51}}}, "freak": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "There he sat motionless for a long time , trying to divine by what baleful freak of circumstances he had been brought to this wretched plight ; but , without knowledge of the facts to work upon , he found himself floundering helplessly and blindly in a mire of uncertainty", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "That venerable gentleman -- antiquarian , scientist and profound scholar -- had a queer little place at the edge of the town where he raised wonderful bees , and grew freak squashes inside glass molds in every grotesque shape imaginable", "context_len": 40}}}, "notwithstanding": {"freq": 67, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 81, "context": "Albert , who preferred that everything should be done decently and in order , placed a chair for Marie-Celeste on the other side of the Queen 's little table , and then seated himself on the gilded sofa beside Miss Belmore , in such a comfortable , snuggling-up way that Miss Belmore had to put one arm right round him and give him a sound little kiss by way of punishment , which Albert was courteous enough not to resent , notwithstanding he considered that sort of treatment somewhat humiliating for a boy of four", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "There were several other humpies near by in the bush , and whenever my black fellow threw the boomerang the other fellows would shout kout kout ! meaning look out ! and the women would seize the little naked blacks , and cuff them , and tumble them into the humpies in a most unceremonious manner ; notwithstanding , their little black heads were soon peeping out again", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "Like a flock of sheep the Huns , with hands still upraised , shuffled on the first of their long trek to captivity -- to some delectable spot in England , where , far from the sound of the guns , there is food in plenty for Hun prisoners of war , German U-boats notwithstanding", "context_len": 55}}}, "lodges": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "But , notwithstanding that peace had been restored , they were all glad when the chief took up his presents and went back to his motley followers , and even more so , when they put their horses to their utmost speed , and returned to their lodges ; where no doubt they gave to their tribe an astounding account of the adventure of their chief in the camp of the white man", "context_len": 73}}}, "astounding": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 60, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Aunt Patty ! Was it possible ? At this astounding and alarming news , Bessie plumped down again in the bed beside Maggie , amazed at herself for having dared to speak so boldly to that terrible person", "context_len": 38}}}, "drearily": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The rooks croaked drearily to each other as they winged their way inland , and the gulls circled wailing over the heath before taking their flight to roost on some lonely sand-bank , and no other sound broke the monotonous plunge of the cold waves", "context_len": 45}}}, "breakfasts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Of all the fires he built ; the oxen he fed ; the water he carried , and even the breakfasts and suppers he helped to cook", "context_len": 27}}}, "gratification": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 80, "context": "But in carrying a city by storm , which takes place usually at an unexpected time , and often in the night , the maddened and victorious assaulter suddenly burst into the sacred scenes of domestic peace , and seclusion , and love -- the very worst of men , filled with the worst of passions , stimulated by the resistance they have encountered , and licensed by their victory to give all these passions the fullest and most unrestricted gratification", "context_len": 81}}}, "valleys": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 69, "context": "Most children would have thought the life of constant toil which Guy led very wretched indeed ; but he did not , for he had daily the gratification of perceiving that the great object of their journey across the plains was being gradually accomplished ; his mother 's health was slowly becoming strengthened , by every step they took toward the snowy mountains , beyond which lay the fruitful valleys in which they hoped to find a home", "context_len": 78}}}, "saddened": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "Guy was greatly saddened by the sufferings of the child , and by the grief of its parents , and shuddered when he saw the bones of animals which lay by thousands bleaching upon the desert , and once was filled with horror on coming across a human skull , which the prairie wolves had dragged from some shallow grave , and separated far from its kindred bones", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` The fiends ! '' said Bertric bitterly ; and then seeing Alfgar 's saddened face , said , `` Oh , I beg pardon , '' which made matters worse", "context_len": 31}}}, "bleaching": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Guy was greatly saddened by the sufferings of the child , and by the grief of its parents , and shuddered when he saw the bones of animals which lay by thousands bleaching upon the desert , and once was filled with horror on coming across a human skull , which the prairie wolves had dragged from some shallow grave , and separated far from its kindred bones", "context_len": 68}}}, "temple": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 57, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "One league from there , in the direction up stream , was Talomeco town , the `` temple '' of which is described as a wonderful and curious structure by Garcilaso", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "mode": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 85, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}}}, "sepulture": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "The idea that the body of the poor little baby should meet such a fate , filled him with sorrow , and although it had always seemed to him a natural and peaceful thing that the temple of clay should rest under its native dust , after the flight of the soul , he thought that the Indian mode of sepulture , of which they saw examples every day , by far the best", "context_len": 74}}}, "examples": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 66, "context": "The idea that the body of the poor little baby should meet such a fate , filled him with sorrow , and although it had always seemed to him a natural and peaceful thing that the temple of clay should rest under its native dust , after the flight of the soul , he thought that the Indian mode of sepulture , of which they saw examples every day , by far the best", "context_len": 74}}}, "burial": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "An old French lady , looking at a picture of the burial of Harold , and , I suppose , feeling that the subject might be painful to me as a Saxon , politely assured me of her regret at that monarch \u2019 s death ! Let bygones be bygones , say I", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "Alexander 's was a simple burial procession , going from the palace where he died to the proper cemetery -- a march of a thousand miles , it is true , but all within his own dominions The greatness of it resulted simply from the magnitude of the scale on which every thing pertaining to the mighty here was performed , for it was nothing but a simple passage from the dwelling to the burial-ground on his own estates , after all", "context_len": 82}}}, "forked": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "What a sight he saw ! Heavy clouds of smoke were pouring up the stairway from the lower floor of the barn , while forked flames darted through them , showing that a fierce fire was raging below", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` And she thrust the forked pole into Edna 's hands and then sprang up the bank , while Edna crouched down , as near the water as possible , in order to make best use of her pole", "context_len": 39}}}, "weapons": {"freq": 82, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 82, "word_index": 57, "context": "He snatched his dagger from its sheath , and struck down one assailant ; but ere he could raise it to strike again , another leapt on to his back , and clung there until the rest rushed up , when he shouted , Take him alive ! take him alive ! and , throwing down their weapons , half a dozen of the pirates flung themselves upon Gervaise , and strove to pull him to the ground , until at last , in spite of his desperate resistance , they succeeded in doing so", "context_len": 95}}}, "summons": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 90, "context": "I must cure the king , or it would be so much the worse for me ! And how was I to cure him ? My knowledge of disease was of the slightest and most amateurish kind , and , for aught that I could tell to the contrary , might not even be sufficient to enable me to diagnose the case correctly , much less to treat it successfully ! However , there was no use in meeting trouble half-way ; the only possible course was to obey the summons forthwith , and do my best , leaving the result in the hands of Providence", "context_len": 106}}}, "attentively": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 2, "context": "And very attentively everybody listened to the story , as I told it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached", "context_len": 128}}}, "laughingly": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 19, "context": "The chums had an early breakfast the next morning -- their last breakfast of the year , as Ned laughingly remarked -- and then , with Fenn 's arm well wrapped up , so he would not take cold in it , and each of the other lads carrying a gun , they started off for the town of Cannistota", "context_len": 60}}}, "resembled": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 57, "context": "He found three thrushes ' nests low down in the elder-bushes , all filled with gaping yellow mouths , the pretty little chaffinch 's up in the ivy-tree , with only two speckly eggs as yet , and Jenny Wren 's household , hidden away in a bank , full of so many children that she surely resembled the old woman who lived in a shoe , and it was a marvel how she could remember which little chirping atom she had fed last", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "Dey 're trained to behave jus ' of dere own selves without any driver , and when dey get out on de road dey always do behave ; and then in the moment 's pause that followed , Marie-Celeste , remembering certain recent performances of her own , wondered if her father wished that a certain little girl , of whom he had some knowledge , more closely resembled these royal ponies , who , once trained to behave , according to Albert , never dreamed of taking the bit in their teeth or of kicking over the traces", "context_len": 99}}}, "inspection": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 124, "context": "It seems that in the old days , when Lewis Carroll loved to play host to the children , they would often come to take afternoon tea in his lodgings , and then likely as not , if the light were good , he would spirit them into a 'room fitted up for the purpose and take their pictures ; and then , if they promised to be good and not to bother , they might follow him into the queer-smelling little room where he made the pictures come out , and they would be permitted to have a look at the dripping glass plate , from which they could seldom make head nor tail , held up against the dark-room 's lantern for inspection", "context_len": 125}}}, "translate": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` This is a difficult passage to translate , owing to its elliptical form ; but the meaning is that the princess wished to assure Alaeddin that what had happened was not due to any slackening in the warmth of her affection for him", "context_len": 44}}}, "chirruping": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "Running along a branch he curved his tail over his head , and sitting down , gave a cheerful , chirruping call , which perhaps meant that there was no danger ; for in a moment the whole tree seemed to be alive with the rest of the family", "context_len": 49}}}, "fattest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Some he said were telling how a monster rattlesnake had come to visit them without any invitation , and that the only food he would eat , was the youngest and fattest of their families ; and that their constant intruders , the owls , had the same carnivorous tastes , besides which they rendered themselves particularly disagreeable , by standing in the doors and staring at every dog that went by , and even preventing the entrance of visitors , to the great distress of all the belles and beaux in town", "context_len": 93}}}, "intruders": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "Some he said were telling how a monster rattlesnake had come to visit them without any invitation , and that the only food he would eat , was the youngest and fattest of their families ; and that their constant intruders , the owls , had the same carnivorous tastes , besides which they rendered themselves particularly disagreeable , by standing in the doors and staring at every dog that went by , and even preventing the entrance of visitors , to the great distress of all the belles and beaux in town", "context_len": 93}}}, "carnivorous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "Some he said were telling how a monster rattlesnake had come to visit them without any invitation , and that the only food he would eat , was the youngest and fattest of their families ; and that their constant intruders , the owls , had the same carnivorous tastes , besides which they rendered themselves particularly disagreeable , by standing in the doors and staring at every dog that went by , and even preventing the entrance of visitors , to the great distress of all the belles and beaux in town", "context_len": 93}}}, "belles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 88, "context": "Some he said were telling how a monster rattlesnake had come to visit them without any invitation , and that the only food he would eat , was the youngest and fattest of their families ; and that their constant intruders , the owls , had the same carnivorous tastes , besides which they rendered themselves particularly disagreeable , by standing in the doors and staring at every dog that went by , and even preventing the entrance of visitors , to the great distress of all the belles and beaux in town", "context_len": 93}}}, "continuously": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 65, "context": "He is without a rival among the younger knights of the convent in his skill in arms , and for strength and activity in all exercises ; he seems to care nothing for the ordinary amusements in which they join at Rhodes , and for nine months was scarcely ever seen by those in the auberge , save when they gathered for meals , so continuously did he work to acquire a perfect command of Turkish", "context_len": 76}}}, "grievance": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "All this may have been very true , for the excited little creatures talked so continuously that I am sure they must have had some grievance , and the children thought it must be the owls that stood solemnly at the entrance of many of the burrows", "context_len": 47}}}, "burrows": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Close to my feet was a large rabbit hole , and knowing from past experience that these animals frequently throw up flints and other stones from their burrows , I plunged my hand into the newly excavated earth , and to my delight found a large jagged flint , and soon after my armoury consisted of five good-sized stones", "context_len": 59}}}, "rattlesnakes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "They did not see the rattlesnakes , so even Aggie somewhat doubted the tales of their ferocity , which Guy said the little prairie dogs related", "context_len": 26}}}, "chatterers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "'No one would look for her in their miserable holes , ' said he , 'and they are such chatterers that the magpies , themselves , would not have patience to listen to them", "context_len": 34}}}, "hillocks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "But although these little creatures were such chatterers , they appeared very industrious , for many hillocks of sand indicated where their homes were burrowed", "context_len": 25}}}, "burrowed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 29, "context": "So , tired as he was , he got to his feet and made his way cautiously inland until he came to a thorn thicket , into which he burrowed , not without scratches on arms and legs", "context_len": 38}}}, "apex": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Each little hole was occupied by a pair of dogs , one of which was often seen perched on the apex like a sentinel", "context_len": 24}}}, "sentinel": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 70, "context": "`` So it was agreed that her spirit should dwell as a prairie dog , and before long out sprang one from a reeking cauldron of herbs , and they took it to the holes of the prairie dogs and left it there , placing beside it a terrible serpent , that all others might be afraid to approach it , and an owl at the door , as a sentinel that would stand looking patiently for an enemy both night and day , and never breathe to the gossips around her the tale of the princess that was prisoned within", "context_len": 101}}}, "stalked": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 52, "context": "I would n't have minded being Margaret , for instance , in the `` Daisy Chain , '' to lie on a sofa , and just look beautiful , and set a good example to all your family , and keep on telling somebody you could n't marry him , while he stalked about the room with an air of profound melancholy , and said he could n't take to anybody else", "context_len": 72}}}, "naturalists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Naturalists have never paid much attention to them , curious as they are", "context_len": 13}}}, "seconded": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 47, "context": "A cannon was at once fired to give notice to the other batteries to be in readiness , and as soon as the dark mass of boats was made out the guns of the fort opened a destructive fire upon them , and a moment later were seconded by those from the fortress ; these , however , were at present being fired almost at random , as the Turkish boats could not be made out at that distance", "context_len": 79}}}, "teller": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "For an hour they were delighted listeners , for it seemed as if this teller of tales had never been in better spirits nor had she ever told a more entrancing story , and when at last it came to an end there were many long drawn `` Ahs '' which showed that no one was ready to have her stop", "context_len": 61}}}, "conversed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` They met there every day , drank the \u00e1ssi or black drink , continued deliberations on public and domestic affairs , attended to complaints and redressed them ; then conversed about news while smoking , or amused themselves at playing `` roll the bullet '' in a sort of ten-pin alley", "context_len": 52}}}, "diseases": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 66, "context": "96 ) : `` Creeks make the boys swim in the coldest weather ; make them frequently undergo scratching from head to foot , through the skin , with broken glass or gar-fish teeth [ 120 ] , so as to make them all in a gore of blood , and then wash them with cold water ; this is with them the arcanum against all diseases ; but when they design it as a punishment to the boys , they dry-scratch them , i", "context_len": 85}}}, "destinies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Such was the man to whom the destinies of the English army were now confided , and such the king who ruled the unhappy land -- cruel as he was cowardly", "context_len": 31}}}, "veneration": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "But , though Alexander was thus willing to impress his ignorant soldiers with a mysterious veneration for his fictitious divinity , he was not deceived himself on the subject ; he sometimes even made his pretensions to the divine character a subject of joke", "context_len": 44}}}, "dupes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "They were accordingly held in great veneration by their simple-minded dupes , as are their few descendants , which even at this day practice in a lesser degree the arts of their forefathers", "context_len": 33}}}, "descendants": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 66, "context": "The defeat of the Turks created such enthusiasm throughout Europe that when the grand prior of England laid before the king letters he had received from the grand master and Sir John Kendall , speaking in the highest terms of the various great services Gervaise had rendered to the Order , Edward granted his request that the act of attainder against Sir Thomas Tresham and his descendants should be reversed and the estates restored to Gervaise", "context_len": 76}}}, "lesser": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 45, "context": "In little more than ten minutes from the moment of starting we swept out of the tributary stream into the main river , the current of which was also flowing pretty rapidly , though not , of course , so swiftly as that of the lesser stream ; and now , as we pushed off into mid-channel , we found time to exchange a few remarks", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "Although the range to his left did not sweep around far enough to cross the course he was pursuing , Alden could not help wondering whether a turn in the trail would not force him to pass through the spur as he had done in the case of the lesser range behind him", "context_len": 53}}}, "chiefs": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Well , it happened that when these men were more powerful among the tribes than the chiefs themselves , that they combined together to wrest from the hands of these the commands that they held , in order that they might hold the people both in bodily and mental subjection", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 94, "context": "`` The Sultan was perplexed at this her claim , more by token that he saw her in poor case , as she were the meanest of the folk ; but the present which she had made him was exceeding magnificent [ and indeed ] beyond price ; [ 420 ] so he turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How deemest thou ? What shall we do ? [ 421 ] It is true I gave her my word , but meseemeth they are poor folk and not of the chiefs of the people", "context_len": 98}}}, "wrest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 58, "context": "At the first cry as was raised by that child Dame Eleanor went clean off her head , and , breakin ' away from them as held her , she seized up a dagger and stabbed herself through the breast , with a awful shout of laughter , shuttin ' her lips for ever from them as would wrest the secret from her", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Wentworth eyed Gerald , doubting whether he should not seize him then and there and wrest from him the paper if he proved to have it , but there was something in the resolute look of the boy that daunted him , man though he was , and he decided that it would be better to have recourse to a little strategy", "context_len": 62}}}, "commands": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Then he returned to his pavilion and sent at once for the Vizier , who came and stood before him , awaiting his commands ; and the Sultan said to him , `` O Vizier , how deemest thou of this affair ? Most like thy son hath told thee what happened to him and to my daughter", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "I washed and dressed , as much by feeling as by sight , opened my venetian shutters , and walked out into the verandah which commands a fine view of both Falls", "context_len": 32}}}, "mental": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was in imagination , but his mental calculation was not far wrong , for within a few yards of where it might be expected , and not ten from where he was now sculling , he saw something roll up as it were to the surface , there was a gleam of white in the sunlit water , and then it was disappearing again , when , acting upon the impulse of the moment , Harry loosened his hold of the oar , took two steps forward over the thwarts , and leaped into the sea", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}}}, "subjection": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Well , it happened that when these men were more powerful among the tribes than the chiefs themselves , that they combined together to wrest from the hands of these the commands that they held , in order that they might hold the people both in bodily and mental subjection", "context_len": 51}}}, "moons": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 57, "context": "There had for a long time existed a tradition among them , that when a daughter of a chief -- an only child , -- should love a brave of an unfriendly tribe , they would have power to change her into a flower or animal , and unless the brave should find the means within ten moons , or months , to break the enchantment , she would die , and with her every chieftain and his family", "context_len": 79}}}, "pretexts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "Accordingly these wicked sorcerers found constant pretexts for involving the tribes in war , especially if they supposed that the only daughter of a chieftain loved a brave of another tribe ; but for many years all their arts were in vain , for the Indians were so passionate and revengeful that immediately an affront was given or received , violent hatred vanquished love , and the chiefs and their families were saved", "context_len": 73}}}, "involving": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 71, "context": "May it please Your Honor , said Sleuth loftily , I have been requested by the attorney for the defense to tell my story in my own way , and ere I have finished I will demonstrate to your satisfaction and the satisfaction of every person present that every word I speak has a bearing upon the matter and is necessary to explain the reasons which led up to my deduction involving the before-mentioned Timothy Davis in a network of his own weaving , from which I think he will scarcely escape with ease", "context_len": 94}}}, "affront": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "Accordingly these wicked sorcerers found constant pretexts for involving the tribes in war , especially if they supposed that the only daughter of a chieftain loved a brave of another tribe ; but for many years all their arts were in vain , for the Indians were so passionate and revengeful that immediately an affront was given or received , violent hatred vanquished love , and the chiefs and their families were saved", "context_len": 73}}}, "hatred": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 25, "context": "To his surprise some of the crew gave him a friendly greeting ; he was unable to understand a manner so at variance with their hatred to the Christians , until one of them said to him in a mixture of Greek and Italian , We have heard from our countrymen who were in the boat with you , that they received much kindness at your hands , and that of all the Christians they had served under , you were the kindest master", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 81, "context": "They had been grievously led away by Mahomet , whom Gervaise regarded as a false teacher ; but as he had seen innumerable instances of the fidelity of the Moslems to their creed , and the punctuality and devotion with which the slaves said their daily prayers , exposed though they were to the scorn and even the anger of their taskmasters , he had quite lost , during his nine months of constant association with Suleiman Ali , the bigoted hatred of Mohammedanism so universal at the time", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "The Turks are warlike and ambitious ; they have conquered Syria , and war with Egypt and Persia ; but the Moorish states are small , they have no thought of conquest , and might live peaceably with Europe were it not for the hatred excited against them by the corsairs", "context_len": 51}}}, "vanquished": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "Accordingly these wicked sorcerers found constant pretexts for involving the tribes in war , especially if they supposed that the only daughter of a chieftain loved a brave of another tribe ; but for many years all their arts were in vain , for the Indians were so passionate and revengeful that immediately an affront was given or received , violent hatred vanquished love , and the chiefs and their families were saved", "context_len": 73}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "It appears long to have been a maxim of their policy to give equal liberty and protection to tribes conquered by themselves , as well as to those vanquished by others , although many individuals taken in war are slaves among them , and their children are called of the slave race , and can not arrive to much honorary distinction in the country , on that account", "context_len": 68}}}, "obtaining": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 86, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "craved": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 74, "context": "And as he grew more weary , more desperate of escape from his pursuers , he soon ceased to think at all ; political dreams , sorrow for the dead , hopes of finding new friends and ambitions in a new world , all were forgotten , the spirit within him was dulled by suffering ; only the poor body cried incessantly for rest , for food , for warmth , and most often craved in vain", "context_len": 77}}}, "victorious": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 70, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}}}, "consternation": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 23, "context": "That was all there was in it , and yet can you not surmise how even that letter was calculated to work great consternation in the mind of some one in the little thatched cottage -- some one who never saw the letter itself , and who did not so much as know of its existence until it had been read and re-read and thought over and answered , but who when one day he was made acquainted with its contents felt as weak as a kitten for hours afterward ? He happened to be lying on the lounge in the living-room at the time , the same lounge to which he had been carried more dead than alive apparently , just four weeks before", "context_len": 125}}}, "maiden": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 53, "context": "Belden 's hand when Marie-Celeste made his acquaintance had proved to be Dixon 's Royal Windsor ; and as soon as the terms of their friendship were unquestionably established , she made so bold as to ask many questions regarding its contents ; for what could have more interest for a Windsor-bound little maiden than the story of the Royal Castle ? And the best part of it was that the book happened to be the second volume , and therefore contained the history of Madame la Petite Reine , as the little French Isabel was called", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 51, "context": "And then when it comes to be well known with its family of dear little daughters , I hope that , once in a while , people who have had little children and lost them , and people who have never had them at all , and now and then a maiden lady , or even an old bachelor , will come down there and carry off one or more of the little girls , to bring them up as their own in their own homes , and so room will be made for others", "context_len": 95}}}, "fairest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "She was more graceful than the fairest flower that grew upon the prairie ; her hair was longer than the grass by the riverside and blacker than the night ; her eyes were like those of the young fawn , and her voice was sweeter than a breeze laden with the song of birds", "context_len": 54}}}, "riverside": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "She was more graceful than the fairest flower that grew upon the prairie ; her hair was longer than the grass by the riverside and blacker than the night ; her eyes were like those of the young fawn , and her voice was sweeter than a breeze laden with the song of birds", "context_len": 54}}}, "blacker": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 91, "context": "It is well said that no man is utterly bad , and perhaps he possessed domestic virtues which were thought unworthy of the attention of the chroniclers ; but as they picture him -- now prompting Ethelred to deeds of treachery against the Danes , now joining those Danes themselves , and surpassing them in cruelty -- now seeking pretended reconciliation , only to betray his foe more surely , and in all this aided and supported by the weak , unprincipled king -- as thus pictured there is scarcely a blacker character in history", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "There was one , I never saw blacker eyes than hers ; they were as black as sloes , and I always admired black eyes , because I am fair , you know , and the mater is fair", "context_len": 39}}}, "fawn": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "A pair of ears were there also , a black nose too ; in short , the whole of some animal 's pretty head ; and , before the little girls had time to call out or run away , a beautiful little fawn sprang out from the bushes and ran to them as if he was glad to see them", "context_len": 61}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Where is the good , '' she said to herself , `` of trying to please this horrid Aunt Susan , and I quite hate Mummy to fawn on her the way she is doing", "context_len": 36}}}, "befell": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 67, "context": "the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to demand her of the Sultan her father in the way of law and righteousness", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "waged": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "So that was the father of Phyllis and Marjorie Norton , that vulgar man with the loud voice and the coarse red face , and those pretty frocks and handsome carriages were all paid for out of the ruin of poor people 's homes ; for the lower parts of Warford had a bad character , and the clergy waged ceaseless war against the terrible curse of drink", "context_len": 68}}}, "increase": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 79, "context": "oriels , [ 651 ] and said to him , `` How deemest thou , O my Lady Fatimeh , of this wonderful pavilion ? '' [ 652 ] `` By Allah , O my daughter , '' replied he , `` it is indeed marvellous in the extreme , [ 653 ] nor methinketh is its like found in the world ; nay , it is magnificent exceedingly ; but oh , for one thing which would far increase it in beauty and adornment ! '' And the princess said to him , `` O my Lady Fatimeh , what is lacking to it and what is this thing which would adorn it ? Tell me of it ; I had thought that it was altogether perfect", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 81, "context": "replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both far and near have heard ? How is it thou hast heard nought of this nor of the name of Alaeddin , whom Our Lord increase in glory and prosper ? '' Quoth the Maugrabin , `` Marry , it is the utmost of my wish to look upon the palace ; so , an thou wouldst do me a kindness , direct me thither , for that I am a stranger", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Sunday enigmas greatly increase knowledge of Scripture , and also help to make the holy day pass pleasantly", "context_len": 18}}}, "rejoicing": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Quoth the queen , `` Rise , O my daughter , and put away these illusions from thy thought ; nay , don thy clothes and see the rejoicing that is toward in the town on thine account and the festivities that they celebrate in the kingdom for thy sake and hear the drums and the singing and look upon the decorations , all in honour of thy nuptials , O my daughter", "context_len": 74}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 30, "context": "Then the Sultan and Alaeddin betook themselves to the latter 's palace ; [ 542 ] whereupon his bride , the Lady Bedrulbudour , came out to meet him , rejoicing in him , and kissed him between the eyes , and he went in with her to her pavilion ; [ 543 ] whither after a little came the Sultan and they sat down and the slave-girls brought sherbets", "context_len": 70}}}, "eagerness": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Her rescue was attempted with a thousand times more eagerness than before , and one day Anoctah , the bravest of all the Gheelees , led her in triumph to her father 's wigwam and demanded his reward", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 90, "context": "canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have heard of , they had never actually seen a white man before , a conclusion which I afterwards found to be correct", "context_len": 128}}}, "bravest": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 56, "context": "When Napoleon 's Old Guard was advancing up the hill , the only sight they could see was the duke and a few mounted officers , till a voice was heard , `` Up , guards , and at them ! '' And the best men in the whole French army , the pick of the bravest of the brave , fell back before the onset of the British guards", "context_len": 70}}}, "wigwam": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 69, "context": "But still she went on , but only slowly , because of the great press of people around her ; and suddenly an icy wind passed over them , and all fell to the earth shivering and terror-stricken , for they had never felt cold before , and they looked up to the mountain , and lo ! upon the very summit , at the door of the deserted wigwam , stood a terrible figure , clothed in white , and having a face as white as his robes , and his hair was like the long crystals that hang from the roofs of caves that the water goes through , and his eyes were like two great diamonds , white , yet blazing like the sun", "context_len": 127}}}, "entreated": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` `` Hearkening and obedience , '' answered the genie and was gone ; whereupon Alaeddin arose , -- and indeed he had scarce believed that the thing should succeed with him , -- and when he saw the Lady Bedrulbudour in his house , he entreated her with respect , albeit he had long burned for love of her , and said to her , `` O princess of the fair , think not that I have brought thee hither to soil shine honour", "context_len": 85}}}, "lodge": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 7, "context": "But he was many leagues from the lodge in which it lay , and he knew that by his own power he could not hope to reach it in time , so he called upon the good fairy again , and she turned a rabbit into a fleet courser that bore the lover and the enchanted maiden , over the prairie with the swiftness of wind", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "If our vows had not forbidden our fighting I should have called him to account long ago ; but the only thing else to do was for me to lodge a formal complaint before the bailiff , of his continually offensive bearing and manner , which I could not bring myself to do , and indeed there was no special matter that would have seemed to justify me , no single speech that in itself would warrant such grave action on my part", "context_len": 83}}}, "befallen": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 33, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "She bespoke him and he answered her neither yea nor nay ; then she brought him the morning-meal , as he abode on this wise , and said to him , `` O my son , what hath betided thee ? Doth there ail thee aught ? Tell me what hath befallen thee , for that , against thy wont , I bespeak thee and thou answerest me not", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "When he returned , the King said to him , `` What hast thou seen ? Where is thy palace and where is my daughter , my heart 's darling and mine only one , than whom I have none other ? '' And Alaeddin answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , I have no knowledge thereof , neither know I what hath befallen", "context_len": 69}}}, "mourns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Now these men had reasoned together over her strange malady , and said , 'She mourns so much over her betrothal to Anoctah because she loves a brave of an unfriendly tribe", "context_len": 33}}}, "betrothal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Now these men had reasoned together over her strange malady , and said , 'She mourns so much over her betrothal to Anoctah because she loves a brave of an unfriendly tribe", "context_len": 33}}}, "enchantments": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Let us then take her from her father , and place her in the great medicine lodge where we can work our enchantments upon her , and make ourselves rulers of all the tribes", "context_len": 34}}}, "herbs": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` So it was agreed that her spirit should dwell as a prairie dog , and before long out sprang one from a reeking cauldron of herbs , and they took it to the holes of the prairie dogs and left it there , placing beside it a terrible serpent , that all others might be afraid to approach it , and an owl at the door , as a sentinel that would stand looking patiently for an enemy both night and day , and never breathe to the gossips around her the tale of the princess that was prisoned within", "context_len": 101}}}, "incantations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` So in the night they took her from her father 's wigwam into the great medicine lodge , which was hung about with the herbs they used in their incantations , and had in the centre a great heap of stones , within which was a fire burning", "context_len": 49}}}, "vapor": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Beside these stones , which were kept constantly hot , they made Mahdrusa sit down , and while she still wept , her tears fell upon the stones , and a great vapor arose , which the sorcerers condensed upon clay vessels into drops of water as pure as crystal , and with them and the herbs that hung around , made a decoction so powerful that when they had forced Mahdrusa to drink it , she lost all power and reason , and her spirit lay passive in the hands of her tormentors", "context_len": 95}}}, "crystal": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 90, "context": "Thou sayst , O my mother , that I have no gift to present to the Sultan , according to the wont of the folk , whilst in fact I have by me a gift and an offering , the like whereof methinketh no king ever possessed , no , nor aught to match therewith ; for [ 338 ] thou must know , O my mother , that the fruits , which I brought in the purses [ 339 ] from the treasure and which I deemed glass or crystal , are very jewels , methinketh all the kings of the world may not compass the least of them , and I , of my companying with the jewellers , know that they are precious stones", "context_len": 127}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 70, "context": "He used also to go to the market of the goldsmiths and the market of the jewellers , and there he would sit and look upon the different kinds of jewels and see them bought and sold ; whereby he became aware that the fruits of the trees , wherewith he had filled the purses , [ 318 ] whenas he was in the treasure , were neither glass nor crystal , but jewels , and knew that he had happened upon great wealth , such as kings might nowise compass", "context_len": 91}}}, "decoction": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` Beside these stones , which were kept constantly hot , they made Mahdrusa sit down , and while she still wept , her tears fell upon the stones , and a great vapor arose , which the sorcerers condensed upon clay vessels into drops of water as pure as crystal , and with them and the herbs that hung around , made a decoction so powerful that when they had forced Mahdrusa to drink it , she lost all power and reason , and her spirit lay passive in the hands of her tormentors", "context_len": 95}}}, "tormentors": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 94, "context": "`` Beside these stones , which were kept constantly hot , they made Mahdrusa sit down , and while she still wept , her tears fell upon the stones , and a great vapor arose , which the sorcerers condensed upon clay vessels into drops of water as pure as crystal , and with them and the herbs that hung around , made a decoction so powerful that when they had forced Mahdrusa to drink it , she lost all power and reason , and her spirit lay passive in the hands of her tormentors", "context_len": 95}}}, "demurred": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` But she is a witch ; beware of the evil eye , '' demurred Drake , who , like all West-countrymen , deeply believed in witchcraft and sorcery , far more so than dwellers in other parts of England", "context_len": 40}}}, "fearless": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 42, "context": "It was broad daylight , it was true , and Meg was only a poor , ragged child , but her face was so solemn , and her big eyes shone with so intense a light , and she was so absolutely fearless before him , that he felt impressed , even just a trifle afraid -- something as he would have felt had he been looking at an accusing angel", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 97, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}}}, "magpies": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "The three flew upstairs chattering like magpies , and when up they flew around excitedly so as to get down again to ask more questions , though this they were not able to do as the very first relay of guests arrived before they were quite dressed", "context_len": 47}}}, "reeking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` So it was agreed that her spirit should dwell as a prairie dog , and before long out sprang one from a reeking cauldron of herbs , and they took it to the holes of the prairie dogs and left it there , placing beside it a terrible serpent , that all others might be afraid to approach it , and an owl at the door , as a sentinel that would stand looking patiently for an enemy both night and day , and never breathe to the gossips around her the tale of the princess that was prisoned within", "context_len": 101}}}, "cauldron": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` So it was agreed that her spirit should dwell as a prairie dog , and before long out sprang one from a reeking cauldron of herbs , and they took it to the holes of the prairie dogs and left it there , placing beside it a terrible serpent , that all others might be afraid to approach it , and an owl at the door , as a sentinel that would stand looking patiently for an enemy both night and day , and never breathe to the gossips around her the tale of the princess that was prisoned within", "context_len": 101}}}, "gossips": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 89, "context": "`` So it was agreed that her spirit should dwell as a prairie dog , and before long out sprang one from a reeking cauldron of herbs , and they took it to the holes of the prairie dogs and left it there , placing beside it a terrible serpent , that all others might be afraid to approach it , and an owl at the door , as a sentinel that would stand looking patiently for an enemy both night and day , and never breathe to the gossips around her the tale of the princess that was prisoned within", "context_len": 101}}}, "sharers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` And that was how the rattlesnake and owl became sharers in the homes of the prairie dogs , and it was with these awful companions that the spirit of Mahdrusa spent many weary days", "context_len": 35}}}, "ascended": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 55, "context": "A part of the Cheroki anciently dwelt in caves ; and concerning the caverns from which the Creeks claim to have issued , James Adair gives the following interesting disclosure : `` It is worthy of notice , that the Muskohgeh cave , out of which one of their politicians persuaded them their ancestors formerly ascended to their present terrestrial abode , lies in the Nanne Hamgeh old town , inhabited by the Mississippi-Nachee Indians , [ 150 ] which is one of the most western parts of their old-inhabited country", "context_len": 91}}}, "purified": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Meanwhile her body lay in the medicine lodge of her people , and the sorcerers said that her soul had ascended to the stars , where , in ten moons , she would be purified from her sin and return to her body , or that it would die , and moulder away", "context_len": 53}}}, "desires": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 48, "context": "To be able thus to tell their story to their general , and to see him listening to it with interest and pleasure , filled their hearts with pride and joy ; and the whole army was inspired with the highest spirit of enthusiasm , and with eager desires to have another opportunity occur in which they could encounter danger and death in the service of such a leader", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 16, "context": "Well , the gentle Sweyn has taken your evasion very much to heart , and earnestly desires to repossess himself of your person ; but for this , my easiest plan would have been to rid myself of so troublesome a witness in a more speedy manner , and you might ere this have fed the fishes of the Thames", "context_len": 60}}}, "toiled": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 69, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Alexander made congratulatory addresses to his soldiers on the occasion of their having now at last before them , what they had so long toiled and labored to attain , the whole concentrated force of the Persian empire", "context_len": 38}}}, "ceaselessly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "Sparrows and chickadees , and other birds which make their home with us during the winter , were hopping merrily over the leafless branches , and twittering ceaselessly to one another , as if they were telling of the happy time near at hand , when the warm south winds would blow , and the trees and bushes be covered with their beautiful green summer dress", "context_len": 66}}}, "slew": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 83, "context": "When the Lady Bedrulbudour saw this , she cried out and said to him , `` What hath this holy anchoress done , that thou burthenest thyself with the sore burden of her blood ? Hast thou no fear of God , that thou dost this and hast slain Fatimeh , who was a holy woman and whose divine gifts were renowned ? '' Quoth he to her , `` I have not slain Fatimeh ; nay , I have slain him who slew her ; for that this is the brother of the accursed Maugrabin enchanter , who took thee and by his sorcery transported the palace with thee to the land of Africa", "context_len": 115}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Sigeferth and Morcar , whom Edric slew at Oxford , have been here , and they said they would come back and drag me with them to some judgment seat ; now take thine axe , Edmund , my son , and slay them when they enter ; they want killing again", "context_len": 52}}}, "ridge": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 40, "context": "All the beasts of the prairie had he slain in his terrible anger , and all the people had fled to the mountains for food , thither he thought he would follow them , and he sat down upon a ridge of sand , to strengthen his bow , and sharpen his arrows , when , lo ! quite unmindful of him , a thousand little creatures he had fancied too insignificant to notice , sprang forth from their holes , and gathered in groups for their daily gossip", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "strengthen": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 45, "context": "All the beasts of the prairie had he slain in his terrible anger , and all the people had fled to the mountains for food , thither he thought he would follow them , and he sat down upon a ridge of sand , to strengthen his bow , and sharpen his arrows , when , lo ! quite unmindful of him , a thousand little creatures he had fancied too insignificant to notice , sprang forth from their holes , and gathered in groups for their daily gossip", "context_len": 89}}}, "sharpen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "All the beasts of the prairie had he slain in his terrible anger , and all the people had fled to the mountains for food , thither he thought he would follow them , and he sat down upon a ridge of sand , to strengthen his bow , and sharpen his arrows , when , lo ! quite unmindful of him , a thousand little creatures he had fancied too insignificant to notice , sprang forth from their holes , and gathered in groups for their daily gossip", "context_len": 89}}}, "unmindful": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 59, "context": "All the beasts of the prairie had he slain in his terrible anger , and all the people had fled to the mountains for food , thither he thought he would follow them , and he sat down upon a ridge of sand , to strengthen his bow , and sharpen his arrows , when , lo ! quite unmindful of him , a thousand little creatures he had fancied too insignificant to notice , sprang forth from their holes , and gathered in groups for their daily gossip", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I watched the scene , for the moment unmindful of my dangerous position , but drawn by an indescribable feeling to watch the last moments of a doubly-dyed rogue", "context_len": 29}}}, "angered": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 48, "context": "When the King heard her story , he fell a-laughing , of his clemency , [ 368 ] and asked her , `` What is that thou hast with thee and what is that bundle ? '' [ 369 ] Whereupon she , seeing that he was not angered at her words , but laughed , opened the handkerchief forthright and proffered him the dish of jewels", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Allowing the carcasses for Shagbark to look after , the angered Alden turned upon the servant and exclaimed :", "context_len": 19}}}, "amongst": {"freq": 141, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 51, "context": "These have ground and polished off every scrap of seaweed , every barnacle , limpet , and sea-anemone , leaving the rock all smooth and bare , while the boulders lie piled to the east in a heap , where the waves that try to take the rock in flank leap amongst them , and roll them over higher and higher , to come rumbling down as if they were tiny pebbles instead of rounded masses of granite and spar-veined stone a quarter , half , and a hundredweight each", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 60, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 27, "context": "Bertha 's apparent lot in life was to be a teacher -- her own wish was to cringe to those in power , to obtain a footing amongst those who were likely to aid her , and she had not been a week in the school before she made up her mind that of all the girls at Cherry Court School no one was so likely to help her in the future as Florence Aylmer", "context_len": 75}}}, "cactus": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "I took her a delicate slice of cactus myself , but I dropped it in a terrible fright , for a great serpent darted towards me , and an owl sprang forward and devoured my youngest brother before he had time to utter a squeak", "context_len": 45}}}, "squeak": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "I took her a delicate slice of cactus myself , but I dropped it in a terrible fright , for a great serpent darted towards me , and an owl sprang forward and devoured my youngest brother before he had time to utter a squeak", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "'No more Latin , no more Greek , no more cane to make me squeak ! ' sang Bobby on the fifteenth of July , springing out of the pony-trap before Pixie had stopped , and taking a flying leap over the pump-trough in the exuberance of his spirits", "context_len": 49}}}, "guardian": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was a sight which I thought might gladden their guardian angels to see him and his sister kneeling with clasped hands at their uncle Elfric 's tomb , and when service was over , they made me tell them the old old story about the first Elfric , the brother of my father , and how my father rescued him when the old castle was burnt iii", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 42, "context": "Still faster he went , Polulu padding beside him and keeping away by his presence not only jungle enemies but Bomba \u2019 s friends , the monkeys and the parrots , who gave him a wide berth when they saw the grim guardian that kept pace with him", "context_len": 48}}}, "supposing": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 21, "context": "While things were in this state -- the Thebans having understood that Alexander had been killed at the north , and supposing that , at all events , if this report should not be true , he was , without doubt , still far away , involved in contentions with the barbarian nations , from which it was not to be expected that he could be very speedily extricated -- the whole city was suddenly thrown into consternation by the report that a large Macedonian army was approaching from the north , with Alexander at its head , and that it was , in fact , close upon them", "context_len": 109}}}, "conjurer": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 48, "context": "He kindly gave Father the benefit of his experience of farming in Colorado ( greatly to the latter 's edification ) ; he amused Lilian with his funny stories , and was a source of open-mouthed wonder to Nancy , who thought his achievements only second to a conjurer she had once seen at Gorswen fair", "context_len": 56}}}, "pouch": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 23, "context": "Withdrawing behind the line , the grand master sat down on a fragment of stone , and , drawing a tablet from a pouch in his girdle , he wrote on it , In all things carry out the instructions of Sir Gervaise Tresham : he is acting by my orders and authority , and has full power in all respects", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 67, "context": "This story does not mention any crossing by the Cha'hta of the turbid waters of the mighty Mississippi , but accounts quite satisfactorily for the mysterious inclination of the pole , for the prophet must have been careful to suspend the satchel with the war-physic always on the eastern side , so as to have the pole brought down in that direction by the weight of the pouch", "context_len": 68}}}, "briars": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}}}, "hare": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "Lem ran like a hare , and was out of sight among the trees in an instant ; while Dolly , finding the boys were gaining upon her , threw herself upon the ground when she came to the brow of a steep hill , and rolled over and over until she reached the foot , not heeding the stones which must have hurt and bruised her as she went", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "I think with you , that Sir Gervaise has fully justified his insistence in this matter , which I own I considered to be hare brained folly", "context_len": 27}}}, "undaunted": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 24, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "But the boys were undaunted ; and , in spite of all their pain and suffering , sang hymns of praise till their tongues could utter no more", "context_len": 28}}}, "foes": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 79, "word_index": 18, "context": "At the moment when I began to doubt whether we could much longer withstand the swarms of fresh foes who continued to pour against us , while we were overcome by heat and labour , Sir Gervaise , who had throughout been fighting at my side , offered to swim into the port , to fit out a dozen of the merchant craft there as fire ships , and to tow them round into the midst of the Turkish vessels behind the two galleys that were lying ready for service", "context_len": 90}}}, "unscathed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "But although I had passed through the day unscathed , so far as actual bodily injury was concerned , it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "The process of `` smelling out '' being at an end , and those who had passed the ordeal unscathed being relieved of all further apprehension , the enormous crowd which had gathered to witness the `` sports '' settled down to thoroughly enjoy itself", "context_len": 45}}}, "fangs": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 31, "context": "As I rounded the rock and came in sight of him he rose to his feet , with his two front paws on Ama 's body , and bared his great fangs at me in a hideous grin , as he gave utterance to a snarling growl that might well have struck terror to the boldest", "context_len": 56}}}, "asunder": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "He caught the serpent in his hands , and seizing its jaws , tore it asunder , and wrapped its writhing body around his wound , while at the same moment the fairy called up a terrible wind that blew the owl far away , and to the arms of the young warrior , the little prairie dog that held the soul of Mahdrusa", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 9, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "'You goose ! Vipers do n't squeal , at any rate , ' said Archie , whose friendship had reached a degree of intimacy that was distinctly brotherly ; and gingerly pulling asunder the neatly-woven grass , he disclosed to view a plump yellow dormouse , whom they had evidently disturbed in his winter quarters", "context_len": 55}}}, "writhing": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 20, "context": "He caught the serpent in his hands , and seizing its jaws , tore it asunder , and wrapped its writhing body around his wound , while at the same moment the fairy called up a terrible wind that blew the owl far away , and to the arms of the young warrior , the little prairie dog that held the soul of Mahdrusa", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 85, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "leagues": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` He also states that the French post was three leagues from the mouth of Yazoo river , close to a village inhabited by a medley of Yazoo , Couroas and Ofogoula Indians , and mentions the tribes in the following order ( I , 163 ) : `` Yazoo Indians , about 100 huts ; further up , Coroas , about 40 huts ; Chactioumas or `` red lobsters '' , about 50 huts , on same river ; Ouf\u00e9-ouglas , about 60 huts ; Tapoussas , not over 25 huts", "context_len": 92}}}, "courser": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "But he was many leagues from the lodge in which it lay , and he knew that by his own power he could not hope to reach it in time , so he called upon the good fairy again , and she turned a rabbit into a fleet courser that bore the lover and the enchanted maiden , over the prairie with the swiftness of wind", "context_len": 66}}}, "swiftness": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 99, "context": "I was not permitted to show myself in the doorway of my dwelling , or even to approach it nearly enough to see what was going on ; and in this dreadful solitude , waiting and hoping for I knew not what impossible happening to occur and effect my deliverance , each day seemed to drag itself out to the length of a month -- until the darkness came ; and then , with the realisation of the fact that I was so much nearer to a hideous fate , the hours seemed suddenly to have sped with lightning swiftness", "context_len": 100}}}, "accord": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 73, "context": "`` So he broke away from the sorcerers and plunged the enchanted one into the boiling cauldron , unheeding the agony he suffered or the cries of the little animal he held , and in a moment the moon plunged beneath the horizon ; Mahdrusa arose from her long sleep ; the sorcerers fell into the boiling cauldron and were consumed ; and all the people shouted for joy , and with one accord cried that the Ohoolees should from henceforth be their brothers , and the young brave who had rescued Mahdrusa , their chieftain , when her father was called to the happy hunting grounds", "context_len": 107}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Alaeddin did that which behoveth and befitteth unto kings of obeisance and invocation and said to him , `` O our lord the Sultan , thy Grace 's munificence hath vouchsafed [ 464 ] to accord me the Lady Bedrulbudour thy daughter , albeit I am unworthy of this great favour , for that I am of the lowliest of thy slaves ; wherefore I beseech God that He keep and continue thee", "context_len": 73}}}, "marriage": {"freq": 65, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 64, "context": "] the Vizier heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we will set over", "context_len": 128}}}, "yawning": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` And they lived happily together all their lives , '' quoted Aggie , from the fairy tales she had heard , `` Why , Guy , that was a long story , '' she added yawning , `` and it has made me so sleepy I shall go to bed", "context_len": 51}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Anything seemed better than sitting still in the darkness watching the growing water , so the children went up the cave again to where the chasm lay yawning across their path", "context_len": 31}}}, "slapping": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 79, "context": "Jerusalem , what is this ! bellowed a voice , and Joe Griffin leaped out from the midst of the burning pile ; the brands rolled off the back of his woollen shirt , which was thoroughly singed , while a fox-skin cap he wore was scorched to a crisp , as was his hair ; he ran round and round , as though he was mad , blowing his fingers ( which where slightly burned ) , and slapping them on his thighs , while on his face was a mingled expression of pain , arising from the burn , and anger at being outwitted", "context_len": 106}}}, "indignant": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 34, "context": "There ought to have been more verses , but at this point Father had unfortunately got hold of the paper , and persisted in treating the poem in such a comic light that the indignant authoress had never found the heart to finish it , though the fragment was considered very talented by Aunt Helen , and carefully put away in an old work-box , with the first specimens of Peggy 's handwriting and one of Bobby 's little baby-curls", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 74, "context": "Oh , the boundless delight of those rolling miles of heather , which looked like a crimson sea spreading onward towards the horizon , and the delicious smell of the sweet-gale as they trod it underfoot ! Great flocks of plovers flew before them , screaming their 'pee-wit , pee-wit ! ' and here and there they roused a snipe or a woodcock , while wild little ponies scampered off like the wind , indignant at having their solitude disturbed", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "But the stalk gave way , and Jumbo fell heavily across Pepper 's neck , who , indignant at such a liberty , gave a squeak and darted away", "context_len": 29}}}, "shirk": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Look out we do n't get in it ourselves , '' cautioned Frank , but it was from no desire to shirk any danger in rescuing his chum that he was thus thoughtful", "context_len": 34}}}, "reception": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 15, "context": "As he thought of his visit to Roger Eliot \u2019 s home , of his reception by Roger \u2019 s family , of that dinner in the handsome dining room , and of Roger \u2019 s earnest pledge on hearing his story to stand by him and be his friend , a strange and wonderful feeling of lightness and exuberant happiness possessed him and made him long to shout and sing", "context_len": 71}}}, "dissatisfied": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` I should say -- not , '' answered Tom Hadley in a dissatisfied tone , bringing out the last word after a slight pause ; `` but I do n't see why I should n't carry the bag part of the time", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Johnson , but she thought the place an excellent one , and that it was a bad thing to change girls about , making them dissatisfied everywhere , but I meant to bide my time , and find an opportunity", "context_len": 40}}}, "antelopes": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 39, "context": "George saw that , and , greatly delighted , said : `` I told Gus I did n't believe you would back out , and we will have such a jolly time ! You know there are numbers of antelopes on the plains here , and I heard James Graham say this morning , that there would be sure to be a great many of them go down to that little creek to drink just as soon as the moon rose", "context_len": 81}}}, "pa": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "That was what I was doing when pa thought me so studious to-day , and I found out how to hunt them at night , and it 's just as easy as can be", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` You would n't let him have it , would you , pa ? ''", "context_len": 15}}}, "studious": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "What I mean is that we can 'be diligent and studious ' as the old copy-books used to have it , speak well of his school , and not carry tales home that will make our families think we are martyrs and that he is an ogre , or someone to be feared constantly", "context_len": 54}}}, "partridges": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 94, "context": "They said there were great hills of strange-looking rocks , with a flat between them , and a brook running through it ; that the Indians used to live there ; and you could see the stones where they made their fires , and find arrow-heads , and Indian things that were buried there ; and Uncle Isaac knew where ; that somewhere along the side of the brook there was red paint , as good as ever was , and that Uncle Isaac had a room painted with it ; that there were partridges there , and way back was a pond , that the brook ran out of , with pickerel in it", "context_len": 115}}}, "unwilling": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 19, "context": "Penelly bit his lips and said nothing , while Zekle went maundering on about his duty , and how unwilling he was to take the money , till , seeing an awkward look in his victim 's eyes , he concluded that he had better go , and went out , turning at the door to tell Penelly that he might be quite comfortable now , and wishing him good-night", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 67, "context": "`` Lord ha \u2019 mercy on me if \u2019 tis not young Master Wentworth ! '' he exclaimed , showing that , though grown in stature , I had not outgrown the appearance of my boyhood ; and when Drake told him of my newly found title , the old man tottered away to let the frequenters of the inn know the news ; whereat we , unwilling to tarry longer , pushed on towards the towering heights of Hindhead", "context_len": 80}}}, "exhibiting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "They descended to the water , without exhibiting any signs of suspicion or fear , for the boys , quite by accident , had got to the leeward of them -- that is , where the wind would not pass from them to the antelopes , and give to the keen animals notice of their presence", "context_len": 56}}}, "inducements": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "No inducements or threats , even to the breaking of his bones , would induce Gus to touch one", "context_len": 19}}}, "securing": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 52, "context": "As the heaviest part of his work at headquarters was over , and as pretty much everything had depended on the reply to General Forrest 's requisition on his superior officer -- who , unfortunately , chanced to be General Bragg -- for arms and ammunition , Harry had no difficulty in securing leave of absence for the day ; and so , when all the arrangements had been made , we set out the next evening for the house where Jack Bledsoe lay", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "That occasion called for every available hand , for the securing bolts of two of our deck guns had broken adrift , and the huge ungainly weapons charged to and fro across the ship , carrying destruction in their passage", "context_len": 40}}}, "wiry": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 12, "context": "Well , I ain \u2019 t partic \u2019 lar , replied the wiry fellow , who despite his youthful looks , was a veteran of the plains ; I expected to ride , if anything happened to Dick , but this young chap seems to have set his heart on it and I don \u2019 t want to spoil his fun", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The first named Harper was wiry and slight of frame , while the other two were of ordinary stature", "context_len": 19}}}, "leeward": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "They descended to the water , without exhibiting any signs of suspicion or fear , for the boys , quite by accident , had got to the leeward of them -- that is , where the wind would not pass from them to the antelopes , and give to the keen animals notice of their presence", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 58, "context": "Not to be outdone in politeness , we hoisted our colours also ; and for the next quarter of an hour the two craft continued to close , the chase stolidly maintaining her course , while we , under the skipper 's skilful conning , continued to edge very gradually away , as the other vessel sped to leeward , checking our weather braces by a few inches at a time until our yards were all but square", "context_len": 78}}}, "stoop": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 74, "context": "He was not like some people that find comfort in talking over perplexing questions , and it certainly was a very perplexing question to him , how he was to support his mother in the strange country to which he had induced her to come , for though young , Guy was too wise to think that gold lay all over the land , and all that any one had to do was to stoop and pick it up , though many older than himself in the train still believed that old fable , which deceived many in the time of Cortez , over two hundred years before", "context_len": 108}, "NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 15, "context": "The first was a lean , spare man , with gray side-whiskers and a slight stoop , whose rather sporting attire accorded ill with his sharp , keen face ; while the second , the one on the better horse , was stout and ruddy of countenance , a man who looked as though he would be more at home in a bar-parlour than in a drawing-room , and who held himself with a complacent air , as one who is satisfied both with the world in general and himself in particular", "context_len": 92}}}, "concussion": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 17, "context": "Breathless from the violent shock , blinded by the shower of dust , deafened by the terrific concussion , and with his sense of smell deadened to everything but the acrid fumes of the burst shell , he was at a loss to know what had happened", "context_len": 47}}}, "stampeded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "That ain \u2019 t what I \u2019 m driving at , but if a hoss had been wounded or killed , he would have kicked up such a rumpus he \u2019 d stampeded all the others ; they \u2019 d have scattered over the perarie and upset things so promiscuous that we \u2019 d never got \u2019 em agin", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "As she listened to the crashing of the swollen tide , another noise seemed to mingle with the sound of the mountain waters -- a sound of bellowing and trampling , as of a stampeded herd", "context_len": 36}}}, "believing": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 32, "context": "But Percy was popular , Seabrooke was not ; and even the masters were inclined to believe that the latter must have been careless and forgetful and mislaid the money , while believing he had put it in the place he indicated , and presently -- no one knew exactly how it started or could trace the rumor to its source -- presently it began to be bruited about among the boys that Seabrooke was keeping it for his own use and had never intended to return it to Percy , and was now making him his scape-goat", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` We were marching along with our father , '' they said , `` believing that what the ancients had told us was true -- that the world had no end ; but all at once the world said to us , ' I am finished , there is no more of me '", "context_len": 54}}}, "enraged": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "And so Baby had no resource but flight ; he took to his heels , the enraged plebe a few feet behind ; and in half a minute more the pair were lost to sight and sound , far distant in the woods , Indian still pursuing", "context_len": 47}}}, "profitless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Guy , on his part , was very much vexed with George , for having brought him on such a profitless adventure ; but though he was suffering very much from his rashness , the whole thing appeared to him so ridiculous , that he laughed long and heartily", "context_len": 49}}}, "rashness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "Guy , on his part , was very much vexed with George , for having brought him on such a profitless adventure ; but though he was suffering very much from his rashness , the whole thing appeared to him so ridiculous , that he laughed long and heartily", "context_len": 49}}}, "impending": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 15, "context": "The Tyrians , finding themselves pressed more and more , and seeing that the dangers impending became more and more formidable every day , at length concluded to send a great number of the women and children away to Carthage , which was a great commercial city in Africa", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "Haven , an ex-editor , had written up a good `` puff '' for a local paper , inserted gratis an exciting comment and anticipation in reference to the impending sale , and Darry and Bob had printed fifteen hundred dodgers on their home press , very neat and presentable in appearance , and these had been judiciously distributed for miles around , and posted up in stores and depots", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 83, "context": "Thank goodness you 've got your senses left , said Chris , turning to the young fellow , who really seemed anxious to render every possible service ; and if we get them into the room there you can put them to bed , ca n't you ? while I go for the doctor ; and in a voice scarcely audible from mortification the young fellow replied that he thought he could ; so after some difficulty in making them understand the move impending , the two men were successfully landed in the best spare-room", "context_len": 95}}}, "incur": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Harwood 's impending wrath , as well as angry at himself for having done anything to incur it", "context_len": 18}}}, "rudely": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 33, "context": "I made the very best of him , and I went on still further , and told her of the man who had burst into mother 's presence in the drawing-room , and rudely demanded payment for his debt , and then how I had yielded , and told Albert Fanning that I would marry him , and how , after that , everything was smooth , and all the worries about money had disappeared as if by magic", "context_len": 79}}}, "termination": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` The story of the girl 's life is charmingly told , and eventually , her father , a man who , despite some failings , is generous and well-meaning , reappears in the character of a wealthy mine owner , and brings the story to an unlooked for and happy termination", "context_len": 52}}}, "panther": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 23, "context": "Inferior in strength to Gervaise , he was as active as a cat , and he leapt back with the spring of a panther , avoiding the sweeping blow with which Gervaise had hoped to finish the conflict at once ; the latter found himself therefore engaged in a desperate fight with his three assailants", "context_len": 55}}}, "transfixed": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "Besides , it had taken so much courage to nerve herself up to the mere asking of the question , that she found she had none in reserve , and stood transfixed in the doorway , her little face aflame with embarrassment", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "But of course there was only the merest little suggestion of a sympathetic thought to spend on the poor , white lady , with Chris Hartley but just discovered , and after that one instant of transfixed surprise she sped toward him , both hands extended ; and over the gate that divides the first from the second cabin they indulged in the heartiest shaking of hands possible , while hats for the moment were expected to look out for themselves", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Keep just this distance if you can , '' said the gleeman , and drew an arrow suddenly to its head ; it whistled through the air , and the steersman , transfixed , rose , leapt in the boat , and fell in the sea a corpse", "context_len": 49}}}, "glaring": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 33, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Before a week was over , both ourselves and the cattle were quite worn down with our tiresome march across the glaring , blinding desert", "context_len": 25}}}, "crouch": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "They had to crouch down , in uncomfortable positions , not moving , for fear of betraying themselves , and , of course , it was out of the question to talk", "context_len": 32}}}, "claws": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 61, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Teeth tearing , claws going like piston rods , the ferocious brutes fell to the ground and rolled over and over , growling , spitting , biting , each trying to get a strangle-hold on the other \u2019 s throat", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Evidently he was the only rooster on the place , and he had his claws full with all those hens", "context_len": 20}}}, "struggles": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 9, "context": "Since then Christ feels for all the pains and struggles through which we go for his sake , since he can make allowance for all our weakness and failures ; and as he is so ready to give us help in our temptations , so much the more ought we who are not only tempted , but too apt , in spite of our best efforts , to fall into sin , to show to others all the kindness and sympathy we may at any time need for ourselves", "context_len": 89}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "While most of the onlookers jeered , it was evident that some of his friends were present , and at one time it looked as if a rescue was about to be attempted ; but the soldiers kept back the press , and in spite of his violent struggles the prisoner was brought underneath the gallows , where a rope was deftly passed round his neck", "context_len": 66}}}, "delivery": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 46, "context": "Had any other child friend along his route , no matter how friendly , questioned him day after day as to how he was getting on with his training for the race ? Had any other among them promised to be on hand at the latest delivery on the afternoon succeeding it , so as to learn just what the issue had been , and at a time when he would be able to stop and tell about it ? Would any one else in the world have thought of suggesting that he should give three short little whistles when he reached the Browns ' , in Remsen Street , so that she should know just how near he was ? Surely no one ; and it was", "context_len": 128}}}, "deliverer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "The boys uttered cries of joy for their delivery from the terrible death with which the panther had threatened one , or perhaps even all , of them ; but they were very much frightened to see that their deliverer was Mr", "context_len": 42}}}, "reports": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 11, "context": "When David returned to the deck an officer was already making reports of the temperature of the water , and Captain Thrasher was standing with head cocked and a hand at his ear , listening , on the chance that the clamor of the fog-whistle might fling back a telltale echo from some hidden mountain of ice that lay in ambush", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Jefferys ( I , 144 ) reports the Yazoos to be the allies of the `` Cherokees , who are under the protection of Great Britain", "context_len": 26}}}, "fearfully": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 71, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}}}, "disobey": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was this , rather than the capture of these three vessels , that induced us to disobey the general instructions we had been given to cruise along the northern coast of Africa , and determined us to push north to give warning along the coast from Naples to Genoa of the danger that threatened , and , if possible , to enable Genoa to fit out her galleys to encounter the corsairs", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Poor mammy ! She knew her master 's word was not to be disputed , and dared not disobey ; but I think she has never quite forgiven him for that , and still looks upon it as hard that when , as she said , she had a chance 'to speak her mind to Mrs", "context_len": 57}}}, "sorrowfully": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 34, "context": "When the fire had entirely died down , and I had satisfied myself that there was nothing left of poor Ama to be desecrated by fang of beast or beak of bird , I sorrowfully retired from the fatal spot , carrying the leopard 's skin with me , and making my way with some difficulty to the place where the canoe lay concealed , sprang in and shoved off", "context_len": 70}}}, "surmised": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 34, "context": "But I soon discovered that even this small measure of mercy was to be denied me ; food and drink were indeed to be served out to us -- in order , as I surmised , that we might meet the ordeal in store for us with unabated strength -- but the night was to be passed , as the day had been , secured to the sacrificial post , exposed , naked and helpless , to the elements and to the myriad insect plagues that attacked us unceasingly", "context_len": 89}}}, "wringing": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 21, "context": "Beyond the fact that a lady in white was supposed to haunt both the ruins and the oak-wood , weeping and wringing her hands in orthodox ghostly fashion , the children had not been able to learn much of the story , for there were so many and divergent accounts of it , all told with uncertainty as to names and dates , and in that very oracular -- not to say muddled -- style sometimes indulged in by rustic historians", "context_len": 81}}}, "convulsions": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "She wor tuk wid convulsions last evening , and when the doctor come he said it wor the drops", "context_len": 19}}}, "sporting": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "The first was a lean , spare man , with gray side-whiskers and a slight stoop , whose rather sporting attire accorded ill with his sharp , keen face ; while the second , the one on the better horse , was stout and ruddy of countenance , a man who looked as though he would be more at home in a bar-parlour than in a drawing-room , and who held himself with a complacent air , as one who is satisfied both with the world in general and himself in particular", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "For that instant the sporting instincts of the men triumphed over racial prejudices , and for the rest of the voyage -- and after -- the Maoris and Afrikanders `` hit it off '' splendidly", "context_len": 35}}}, "babe": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 82, "context": "But alas ! there were no traditions of victory to encourage them ; only gloomy remembrances of defeat ; and , but for the stern call of duty which bade them , as men and Christians , go to the succour of their brethren , the majority would have preferred to remain at home and abide the worst , although they knew full well that submission utterly failed to mitigate the ferocious cruelty of their oppressors , who slew alike the innocent babe and the grey-haired grandsire", "context_len": 87}}}, "threat": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 35, "context": "Aunt Helen had broadly hinted that an hour 's practice of much-neglected pieces upon the piano in the Rose Parlour would be a profitable means of employing the time , and the fear lest this threat should become an actual command caused Peggy to shun the neighbourhood of the house with elaborate care , and betake herself , with a selection of pets , to the barn", "context_len": 67}}}, "disobedient": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "Harwood 's reproachful glance , when he had said he had been deceived in him ; and he wondered if he would carry into execution the threat he had made before they left home , and greatly feared that he would , for he felt that he had been quite disobedient , and seemingly ungrateful enough , to be left alone on the prairie", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "I know he will , if you go to him and tell him how sorry you are , and promise him you will never be so wild and disobedient again", "context_len": 30}}}, "seemingly": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was likely that ere long they might be called upon to stand a siege , but , acquainted as they were with the strength of the place -- its deep and seemingly impassable moat , its massive walls , and protecting towers and bastions -- it had seemed to them that Rhodes was capable of withstanding all assaults , however numerous the foe , however oft repeated the invasion", "context_len": 70}}}, "coffin": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 32, "context": "Guy thought he had never witnessed so sad a scene as when they laid the beautiful baby , that looked as pure and sweet as a white lily , in the rough coffin that some of the young men had hastily made , and carried him to a lonely spot , that perhaps no feet had ever trod before , and , breathing a prayer over him , left him to his long sleep , far from the place of his birth , or that for which his kindred were bound , and where never a tear would be dropped above him , or a sigh breathed", "context_len": 107}}}, "trod": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 57, "context": "Guy thought he had never witnessed so sad a scene as when they laid the beautiful baby , that looked as pure and sweet as a white lily , in the rough coffin that some of the young men had hastily made , and carried him to a lonely spot , that perhaps no feet had ever trod before , and , breathing a prayer over him , left him to his long sleep , far from the place of his birth , or that for which his kindred were bound , and where never a tear would be dropped above him , or a sigh breathed", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "Although the new-comers trod stealthily , the stillness of the air and the conducting properties of the calm water carried the sound of their footfalls with disconcerting clearness", "context_len": 28}}}, "birth": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 81, "context": "Guy thought he had never witnessed so sad a scene as when they laid the beautiful baby , that looked as pure and sweet as a white lily , in the rough coffin that some of the young men had hastily made , and carried him to a lonely spot , that perhaps no feet had ever trod before , and , breathing a prayer over him , left him to his long sleep , far from the place of his birth , or that for which his kindred were bound , and where never a tear would be dropped above him , or a sigh breathed", "context_len": 107}}}, "brokenhearted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Poor little Aggie was quite brokenhearted at the loss of her poor little playfellow , and , quite forgetting her anger went to Guy for comfort", "context_len": 26}}}, "playfellow": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "Who can tell all that goes on in the mind of a little child , or what it understands of death ? In a vague way Peggy knew that her playfellow had 'gone to heaven , where mother is , ' but she did not think of that as any cause for sorrow , nor did she connect him for an instant with the place where she stood , but , with her nurse 's words still troubling her , she knelt down and searched among the white flowers that hid the bare earth beneath", "context_len": 95}}}, "disobeyed": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 66, "context": "If she only could just once go to Sunday-school and hear about Jesus , and learn that He was really alive , and that she could take Roy to Him ! Oh ! if only it were possible that Roy might receive this great and wonderful blessing , why it would be worth even her father 's great anger , should he learn that she had disobeyed him", "context_len": 68}}}, "summon": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` I grieve to say , '' he continued , `` that there is but one upon whom our suspicions can now fall with any shadow of probability -- one who is now absent , for I thought it well not to summon him to this council ; and before naming him , I must recall to you , Elfwyn , and to you , Herstan , the solemn oath we have all three taken to disregard all appeals of natural affection , and to ascertain the truth , God being our helper", "context_len": 93}}}, "intrusting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Harwood , who treated him very coldly , seldom asking him to do anything , and never intrusting the care of even the slightest article to him", "context_len": 27}}}, "incurred": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "As told in the first volume of this series , entitled `` The Rival Pitchers , '' Tom had no sooner reached Randall than he incurred the enmity of Fred Langridge , a rich youth from Chicago , who was manager of the 'varsity ball nine , and also its pitcher", "context_len": 51}}}, "displeasure": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 91, "context": "How the Mummy would enjoy it , how she would revel in the good food and the lovely house ! What a red-letter day it would be to her all her life , for all the rest of her years ! How Sukey and Ann Pratt and the neighbors down at Dawlish would respect her for evermore ! And doubtless the Mummy 's dress might be managed , and -- but what about Aunt Susan ? Would Aunt Susan ever forgive her ? She dared not run the risk of her displeasure ; too much depended on keeping her in a good humor", "context_len": 103}}}, "ado": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "There the sentries , who were stationed at the door , had some ado to keep back the curious throng , and many a sturdy country farmer shouldered his way into the house in the wake of his squire to catch a glimpse of his king , the ill-fated King Charles , who was to rest that night at Farnham on his last journey from the prison at Hurst Castle to the scaffold at Whitehall", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Indians ! '' exclaimed George and Gus , turning pale , and with out more ado , rushing from the spot , not only as they said `` to tell mother , '' but to gain a place of safety", "context_len": 41}}}, "grieved": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 13, "context": "When [ 413 ] the Vizier heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 45, "context": "Indeed , she did not care to realize anything of the sort , choosing , rather , to think of the Windsor of Isabel 's time as much the same as the Windsor of Victoria 's , and she would have been not a little grieved and surprised had any one insisted on pointing out to her in how many , many ways the old differed from the new", "context_len": 69}}}, "glare": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` It does look like snow , '' returned Guy , looking intently in the direction she indicated , `` but it is now June , and we certainly ought not to encounter such a fall as that appears to be , besides , there is a perfect glare of sunshine there ! Ah , I have it ! That is not snow , but alkali ! ''", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Not a bird peeped or stirred , except to glare at a big white rabbit who came loping easily down the hill from the wood , and went noiselessly under the veranda", "context_len": 32}}}, "alkali": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "`` It does look like snow , '' returned Guy , looking intently in the direction she indicated , `` but it is now June , and we certainly ought not to encounter such a fall as that appears to be , besides , there is a perfect glare of sunshine there ! Ah , I have it ! That is not snow , but alkali ! ''", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` What is alkali ? '' asked Aggie", "context_len": 8}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "True they had passed over a wonderful country , but the alkali plains seemed small in comparison to the desert , over which they had passed some weeks before , and all the grandeur of the Sierra Nevada Mountains could not awaken in them one iota of the enthusiasm with which they had greeted the first glimpse of the snow-capped summits of the Rocky Mountains", "context_len": 65}}}, "encamp": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Parmenio thought , therefore , that the Persians would not dare to remain and encamp many days in the vicinity of Alexander 's army , and that , accordingly , if they waited a little , the enemy would retreat , and Alexander could then cross the river without incurring the danger of a battle", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "san\u00f3dsh\u00e4s I encamp near , or with somebody ; that of Ishiagaska ( Tchiagaska ? ) by \u00edka ak\u00e1ska his scraped or shaved head ; or issi ak\u00e1ska his hair ( on body ) removed", "context_len": 35}}}, "sulphur": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "The water was impregnated with sulphur and salt , and though there was not an eruption when the writer was present , there were marks on surrounding trees showing where mud had been hurled to a height of thirty or forty feet", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The odor grew more pronounced , for some sulphur springs are so strongly impregnated with that chemical in solution that the smell carries for miles , especially on a windy day", "context_len": 31}}}, "impregnates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "A fall of rain usually brings it forth from the earth it impregnates in great quantities , and it looks very beautiful", "context_len": 22}}}, "particles": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "Some of the lumps began to shift , while particles of coal dust , filtering through the interstices , floated in the already-stifling air , causing intense irritation to the fugitives ' eyes and throats", "context_len": 35}}}, "assume": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 80, "context": "In due course she was adjudicated upon and condemned by the Mixed Commission ; but I did not remain at Sierra Leone for that to take place ; for upon our arrival we found that a packet had come in from England a few days previously bringing letters for me , acquainting me with the sad news of my father 's death and urging me to proceed home immediately to supervise the winding up of his affairs , and to assume the management of the very important property that he had left behind him", "context_len": 94}}}, "salts": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 63, "context": "The horses pranced , swung a little to one side and stopped short , and I heard some one say , 'So , Barney , so Pet ! ' I did n't know what happened next but the first thing I knew I was lying on the lounge in the sitting-room , my mother bending over me , and holding a bottle of salts to my nose , 'Oh , dear , oh , dear , ' my mother was crying , 'another minute and the child might have been killed", "context_len": 91}}}, "partaken": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "The food and drink provided for me were of an especially nourishing character , and when at length I had partaken of as much as I could force down my throat I was again lashed to my stake , but this time so carefully that , while for me to loose my bonds was an impossibility , the circulation of blood was in nowise impeded ; and for even this small mercy I was inexpressibly grateful", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Notwithstanding their Puritanical garb , most of the troopers had , even at this early hour , partaken of spirituous drinks , and , judging by their gestures and talk , were evidently anticipating the restoration of His Majesty King Charles", "context_len": 41}}}, "partaking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Graham , `` yet it was only a very short time afterwards that I was nearly killed by partaking of it", "context_len": 21}}}, "defined": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 61, "context": "He had noticed a little earlier his big brother , Ira , a loutish , overgrown fellow who had gone around with his hands in his pockets sneering at the innocent fun the smaller boys were indulging in , and bragging about his own especial Fourth of July supply of fireworks which were to come from some mysterious source not clearly defined", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "In the clear sun everything was sharply defined", "context_len": 8}}}, "sickened": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "Their passage had been quite unopposed ; all the fighting men were in Wessex ; and those who had seen the Danish party had fled with terror -- they had not stopped long to plunder , but had speared one or two unfortunate victims who fell in their way , a sight which sickened Alfgar", "context_len": 55}}}, "impregnated": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "The water was impregnated with sulphur and salt , and though there was not an eruption when the writer was present , there were marks on surrounding trees showing where mud had been hurled to a height of thirty or forty feet", "context_len": 42}}}, "quaffed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Many of the people drank it , and became very sick ; the weary oxen quaffed it from the little pools , formed by the rain , by the wayside , and daily two or three died , and we were compelled to leave them to bleach as white as the alkali around them", "context_len": 54}}}, "wayside": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Many of the people drank it , and became very sick ; the weary oxen quaffed it from the little pools , formed by the rain , by the wayside , and daily two or three died , and we were compelled to leave them to bleach as white as the alkali around them", "context_len": 54}}}, "bleach": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "Many of the people drank it , and became very sick ; the weary oxen quaffed it from the little pools , formed by the rain , by the wayside , and daily two or three died , and we were compelled to leave them to bleach as white as the alkali around them", "context_len": 54}}}, "agonies": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 50, "context": "Alfgar leapt nimbly aside , and before his bulky but clumsy antagonist could recover his guard , passed his keen sword beneath the left arm , through the body , and the giant staggered and fell , a bloody foam rising to his lips , as he quivered in the agonies of death", "context_len": 53}}}, "abstinence": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "One day , one of my comrades died , he had borne the torture attending abstinence as long as possible , and then had drank to repletion , and been poisoned", "context_len": 31}}}, "repletion": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "One day , one of my comrades died , he had borne the torture attending abstinence as long as possible , and then had drank to repletion , and been poisoned", "context_len": 31}}}, "imprudent": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "I was doing a most imprudent thing to linger by the side of the pyre , for the smoke , in the first place , and the light of the flames when it fell dark , could scarcely have failed to attract to the spot any savages who might have been in the neighbourhood , when my plight would probably have been as bad as ever ; but at that moment my sorrow at the loss of my companion overcame every other feeling , and , for the moment at least , I was quite indifferent as to what befell me", "context_len": 101}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Mallory , on the whole , though some of his actions have been , I think , imprudent", "context_len": 18}}}, "convulsed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "This ended that game , but everybody was so warmed up to the fun that when it was proposed to carry on a game of Hide and Seek out of doors all agreed , and Edna was so convulsed with laughter to see her dignified , great-uncle Wilbur crouching behind a wood-pile and peeping fearfully over the top that she forgot to hide herself properly and was discovered by Ben in a moment", "context_len": 73}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "Then from out the fringe of trees that lined the shore shot a small canoe , paddled with frantic energy by a withered old woman , her straggling locks streaming behind her head , her face convulsed with fear", "context_len": 39}}}, "quieting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 46, "context": "Nothing used to delight me so much as to tell ghost stories to my younger brothers and sisters and leave them without explaining them , when often the poor little creatures would become nearly convulsed with terror , and my mother would find great trouble in quieting them", "context_len": 48}}}, "malicious": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "I had often been scolded , and even whipped for my malicious mischief but all to no purpose , and at last no notice was taken of me , and I thought my father and mother had made up their mind to let me tell horrible stories until I was tired of them", "context_len": 53}}}, "persuasions": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "But in spite of this , and all Bessie 's persuasions and assurances that the thing was quite impossible , Willie was not to be convinced that the voice he had twice heard was not that of the old lady who had left the money in the Bible ; and he did not cease regretting that Jennie had not seen her", "context_len": 61}}}, "cower": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Generally the nurse came in the middle of it and carried them away to bed , where they would cower under the blankets and tremble at every sound", "context_len": 28}}}, "tinware": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Just then I heard a terrible crash , as if all the crockery and tinware upon the kitchen dressers had tumbled down", "context_len": 23}}}, "dressers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "It looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and", "context_len": 128}}}, "towered": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 39, "context": "As Ama , taking her bow and arrows , tripped lightly away toward the forest , I proceeded to shin up the tree , and presently , after some labour , found myself among its topmost branches , which towered high above those of all the other trees in the neighbourhood , and -- it being by this time broad daylight -- obtained a magnificent view of the surrounding country , extending for , as I estimated , some forty miles toward the south and east , while toward the north and west the view was shut off by high hills , through which the river wound its way", "context_len": 109}}}, "menaced": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "The poor Chinaman , though Dewey did not suspect it , was at that moment in a very uncomfortable position indeed , and he himself was menaced by a peril already near at hand against which his helpless condition allowed of no defence", "context_len": 43}}}, "derided": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "I was convinced that this revelation from the spirit world was made to me alone , and I was overwhelmed by the fear that I was to be carried away bodily , to answer before the ghosts I had derided", "context_len": 40}}}, "begone": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "Such was the wrathful force of this voice which , with so little courtesy , bade the intruder begone , as fairly to stagger the well-meaning visitor", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "But begone -- your time is not yet ; none but the elect may abide the fury of the Lord 's foemen", "context_len": 22}}}, "balm": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "But I make no doubt that the sight of these your loyal neighbours whom you have called in , and the very blessings of the poor folk in the street , are somewhat of a balm to his heart , also I can not deny that those gentlemen '' -- looking over at the officers -- '' have used us very civilly during the day 's ride ; methinks his Majesty finds himself more at ease with them than with those crop-eared parliament men and their preachers", "context_len": 87}}}, "gem": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now it is to me of value beyond the richest gem on earth ; it is a proof of the faith and loyalty of the knight I most esteem and honour , and so in giving it to you again , I part with it with a pang , for I have far greater reason to prize it than you can have", "context_len": 62}}}, "doth": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` When [ 592 ] she had made an end of her story , Alaeddin said to her , `` Tell me , what does this accursed one purpose with thee ; what saith he to thee and of what doth he bespeak thee and what is his will of thee ? '' `` Every day , '' answered the princess , `` he cometh to me once and no more and seeketh to draw me to his love , willing me take him in thy stead and forget and renounce thee ; nay , he told me that my father the Sultan had cut off thy head", "context_len": 108}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Item , it is my will if the above named Aubrey my sonne doth dye without heires or before he come to the age of XXI years , the residue shall remain to my sister Margaret Anderson and her heires forever", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 32, "context": "When the Marid heard Alaeddin 's words , his face frowned and he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "gleams": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I answered her , 'Though the rose is fair , Though the diamond gleams like a lesser sun ; Oh , ne'er can they , e'en in thought compare , With my chosen beauty , my purest one", "context_len": 39}}}, "ne'er": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` I answered her , 'Though the rose is fair , Though the diamond gleams like a lesser sun ; Oh , ne'er can they , e'en in thought compare , With my chosen beauty , my purest one", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Heaven help him , '' quoth Spurius Laritus , `` And bring him safe to shore ! For such a gallant feat of arms Has ne'er been seen before", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "'Nay , his mother were no fool , neither , and she knowed full well she 'd ne'er see him again , no more than you 'd see a duck if you gave it in charge of the fox", "context_len": 39}}}, "e'en": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Accordingly , when she came up , he saw her and viewed her well , beyond his wish ; and from that time till now , O King of the Age , life hath not been pleasant to him [ 366 ] and he will e'en have me seek her of Thy Grace , [ 367 ] so thou mayst marry her with him , and I can not do away this conceit from his wit , for that the love of her hath gotten possession of his vitals , so that he saith to me , 'Know , O mother mine , that , except I attain my desire , assuredly I am a dead man", "context_len": 117}}}, "purest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` I answered her , 'Though the rose is fair , Though the diamond gleams like a lesser sun ; Oh , ne'er can they , e'en in thought compare , With my chosen beauty , my purest one", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I always agree with good old Francis Bacon that `` God Almighty first planted a garden , and indeed it is the purest of human pleasures", "context_len": 26}}}, "woe": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 90, "context": "and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say afterwards upon the danger of such heedless practical jokes", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "Now and then men have managed to become possessed of a file , and have , by long and patient work , sawn through a chain , and have , when a galley has been lying near our own shore , sprung overboard and escaped ; but for every attempt that succeeds there must be twenty failures , for the chains are frequently examined , and woe be to the man who is found to have been tampering with his", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Longstone is a desolate rock , swept by the northern gales ; and woe betide the ship driven on its pitiless shores !", "context_len": 23}}}, "blest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` 'Yes , as I look on a kneeling child , Of those I think , whom our Saviour blest , And I know of all things fair and mild , The pure , young heart of a child is best", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Kind pity then , thou blessed gift , Help and relieve the sore distress 'd ; And up to heav'n his heart he 'll lift , That you with mercy may be blest", "context_len": 33}}}, "bidding": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 44, "context": "No one could appreciate what new life had opened up for the old couple with the coming of Chris and Ted and Donald , and now two were proposing to go at once , and only five weeks more , and Chris would be bidding them farewell on his way to the Majestic down at Liverpool , and on which it had been arranged that Donald at the same time should once more put to sea", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 13, "context": "All this have they suffered in pursuit of duty , or at the bidding of conscience ; and of such and of brave Joseph Ford there comes to us across the ages -- a saying spoken long ago , to the effect that `` he that loseth his life shall save it '' : and we need to remember that saying in such cases as that of Fireman Ford", "context_len": 69}}}, "pail": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then she repeated -- 'Where are you going , my pretty maid ? ' and she had a little milk pail on her arm , and she managed to keep the two parts wonderfully distinct -- it was remarkable in a child not three years old , and when she said -- 'Then I wo n't marry you , my pretty maid ' and answered so pertly -- 'Nobody asked you , sir , she said , ' it would have done credit to an exhibition", "context_len": 86}}}, "agitated": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 29, "context": "The piercing cry , `` Help ! Edmund , the king , is slain ! '' roused the household -- Elfwyn , Herstan , Hermann , the ladies , agitated beyond measure ; the household guard ; and , last of all , Edric", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "' I suppose not , ' said Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner", "context_len": 128}}}, "endeavoring": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "endeavored": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 72, "context": "Harwood said , to divide so small a force , and they were spared the necessity of doing so by the savages themselves , who enraged at the death of one of their number , and confident of success , rode boldly up to the very sides of the wagons , and with showers of arrows , and brandishing their war-clubs , uttering at the same time the most dreadful yells , endeavored to overcome the white men and gain possession of the animals , that snorting and plunging with terror at the unusual rounds of shouting and firing were striving vainly to break their bounds", "context_len": 106}}}, "repair": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 62, "context": "It was certainly Peggy who suggested sitting on the top of the haystack , and making it sway backwards and forwards like a swing , a delightful sensation while it lasted , but which ended suddenly in the collapse of the whole top of the stack , much to Father 's wrath , for it took David a couple of hours to repair the damage , and certainly did not sweeten the old man 's already crusty temper", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 67, "context": "I told them that you had captured three of the corsairs with a single galley , and that if they could send you ten others you would probably be able to make head against the pirates ; but , as I have told you , Genoa is at peace with all the world ; her war galleys are laid up , and most of them would need repair and recaulking before they would be fit to send to sea", "context_len": 79}}}, "comically": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` I did n't smoke last night , '' retorted George , `` I only tried to , and to try to smoke and to do it are two very different things , I can tell you , '' and George grimaced most comically at the remembrance of some very extraordinary sensations he had experienced , both before and after the fire", "context_len": 62}}}, "telescope": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 80, "context": "We were raising her very fast now that both ships were steering upon converging lines ; I could already see nearly to the foot of her topsails ; and I settled myself comfortably , determined to remain where I was until I could absolutely identify her , although even at this time I had scarcely a shadow of a doubt that it was the long-sought Virginia , or rather the Preciosa , that I held in the field of my telescope", "context_len": 81}}}, "bidden": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was almost sunset when Peggy , having seen the last of Archie 's contrivances , and bidden good-bye to kind Miss Forster , passed by the Rectory hedge , and hearing the brisk sound of the mowing-machine , pushed open the little gate and went in , knowing she was always sure of a welcome", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "So they went till they came to Alaeddin and said to him , `` O our lord Alaeddin , blame us not , for that the Sultan hath bidden us carry thee to him , bound and shackled ; wherefore we beseech thee of excusement , for that we are under a royal commandment and may not gainsay it", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` If I could only go too , '' mourned Polly , having nothing to do but go slowly back and shut herself into Mamsie 's room , as bidden", "context_len": 30}}}, "delay": {"freq": 84, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 85, "context": "When the Vizier heard the Sultan 's words , his tongue was tied for despite and he was overcome with exceeding chagrin , forasmuch as the King had promised him that he would marry his daughter to his son ; so , after a little , he said to him , `` O King of the age , Thy Grace condescended to promise me [ 371 ] that the Lady Bedrulbudour should be my son 's ; wherefore it behoveth thine exalted highness appoint a delay of three months , [ 372 ] and God willing , my son 's present shall be greater than this", "context_len": 106}, "VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 10, "context": "At last , when he felt that he must not delay another moment , he took leave of her , saying as he went , Well , as usual you have set me thinking , my little friend , but as though he were grateful for the same ; and Marie-Celeste , turning back to finish the copying of the Prince Imperial 's prayer , wondered in her practical little way if anything would come of the thinking , and if so , if she would ever happen to hear what it was ; and yet at the same time not a little sceptical as to any tangible result whatsoever", "context_len": 110}}}, "carelessness": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 5, "context": "in the face of the carelessness with which the text treats kushk as synonymous now with keszr or teyyareh and now with liwan or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this", "context_len": 128}}}, "barricade": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Harwood 's signals , during the formation of the barricade had been totally disregarded , and the savages in all the hideousness of paint and warlike decorations were riding rapidly around the camp in a gradually decreasing circle", "context_len": 38}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "She would go round with Nancy and a candle , carefully locking all the doors and securing the shutters , peering fearfully into cupboards and starting at her own shadow on the wall ; and , having finally retreated to her bedroom , would barricade the door with a tin box , and place the poker handy on a chair by her bedside", "context_len": 63}}}, "stationed": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 30, "context": "In her plan to give Joe a fright , she had never thought of Father , who returned by the same road ; and now that horrible white object was stationed just in the very danger-spot where a plunging horse might mean a matter of almost certain death , for the wall of the five-acre field abutted the road on one side , and on the other there was only a narrow patch of grass between the steep bank which shelved down sheer into the river , while the closed gate stopped any chance of a dash forward", "context_len": 98}}}, "signals": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 4, "context": "Could those odd sounding signals which the single warrior sent forward from his perch on the rocks have had anything to do with it ? Did they cause the forbearance of his comrades farther up the gorge ? That such should be the case seemed incredible , but days afterward Alden submitted the question to Shagbark", "context_len": 56}}}, "formation": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 49, "context": "The spreading out of the people from the Tallapoosa river to the Chatahutchi and from there to the Savannah must have involved a warfare , struggling , migration and settling down of several centuries , for the advance of the Maskoki proper in this direction was tantamount to the formation of the Maskoki confederacy by subduing or incorporating the tribes standing in their way , and to the still more lengthy process of settling among them", "context_len": 76}}}, "disregarded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "The colour which his unlucky presence among the congregation at Coleman Street on the day of General Harrisons execution , had given to his enemy 's accusations , had made his position perilous in the extreme , for General Monck , and his other secret friends , considered that he had wilfully disregarded their warnings , and were not inclined to exert their influence in his behalf", "context_len": 67}}}, "hideousness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Harwood 's signals , during the formation of the barricade had been totally disregarded , and the savages in all the hideousness of paint and warlike decorations were riding rapidly around the camp in a gradually decreasing circle", "context_len": 38}}}, "decorations": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` `` How knewest thou this , '' asked Alaeddin , `` that the Vizier 's son goeth in this night to the Lady Bedrulbudour ? '' So she told him all she had seen of the decorations in the city , whenas she went to buy the oil , and how the eunuchs and chiefs of the state were drawn up awaiting the Vizier 's son , against he should come forth of the bath , for that this was the night of his going in", "context_len": 87}}}, "decreasing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Harwood 's signals , during the formation of the barricade had been totally disregarded , and the savages in all the hideousness of paint and warlike decorations were riding rapidly around the camp in a gradually decreasing circle", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Tukabatchi was an ancient capital , decreasing in population in Hawkins ' time , but still able to raise one hundred and sixteen warriors", "context_len": 24}}}, "doubtfully": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 28, "context": "As she swung the gate open , and passed from the field into the lane , she saw a sight which for a moment made her hang back doubtfully , for a tramp lay stretched out full length asleep in the sunshine , his tattered clothes and broken boots a strange contrast to the bed of white daisies and celandine upon which he lay", "context_len": 64}}}, "weapon": {"freq": 107, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 105, "word_index": 87, "context": "As he stepped through the gateway one of the pirates sprang past him , but as he did so , a figure leapt out from beyond the wall , and plunged a dagger into his back , while at the same moment , by cutting down another pirate , Gervaise rid himself of one of his assailants in front ; but as he did so , he himself received a severe wound on the left shoulder from Hassan , who , before he could again raise his weapon , sprang upon him , and tried to hurl him to the ground", "context_len": 101}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "There he was without bow or arrow or revolver -- his only weapon the machete", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "He saw the gleam of the Indian \u2019 s steel , drew his own machete and with one stroke sent his enemy \u2019 s weapon whizzing off into the underbrush", "context_len": 30}}}, "feint": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "As soon as he gained the top of the ridge , though some feet below me , I made a sudden rush towards him , intending to make a feint and then run towards the troopers", "context_len": 36}}}, "dividing": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "She took down the dividing curtain between herself and Jane Denton without asking any one 's permission ; and she slept in the bed intended for Jane , and rearranged the drawers , putting them into another part of the room ; and complained about the wardrobe , saying that she would like it put opposite the door instead of in its present position", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Chatahuchi river is the water-course dividing , in its lower portion , the State of Alabama from that of Georgia", "context_len": 20}}}, "sallied": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 62, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}}}, "indescribable": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 37, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "regardless": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "Meanwhile Tige , utterly regardless of the commotion he was causing , or to how great an extent he was injuring his previous high reputation , swam steadily along , dragging the half-drowned pig after him , till he got among the birds , when , taking one in his mouth , he swam to the White Bull ; where Ben , who had watched the whole proceeding , relieved him from the harness , when he swam off and brought in the remainder", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 34, "context": "As Harry Paul disappeared in the swift current the boat rocked and danced , and was sent many feet away by the impulse it received ; but as he rose to the surface , regardless of everything but the drowning man he was striving to save , the boat swept by him , lightened of its load , and was whirled slowly round and round", "context_len": 65}}}, "inactive": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "The party thus opposed were inactive and silent while Alexander was in their country , on his first visit to southern Greece ; but after his return they began to contemplate more decisive action , and afterward , when they heard of his having undertaken so desperate an enterprise as going northward with his forces , and actually crossing the Danube , they considered him as so completely out of the way that they grew very courageous , and meditated open rebellion", "context_len": 82}}}, "spectator": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 44, "context": "He never thought of firing his gun , and had no idea which party had the advantage , he , in fact , felt perfectly overwhelmed , not with fear , but horror , and quite regardless of his danger , remained an inactive spectator of the scene , until he beheld Mr", "context_len": 53}}}, "glittered": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 13, "context": "At the mention of the `` cat '' the man \u2019 s eyes glittered ominously , then , instantly relapsing into his subservient manner , he jabbered in broken English :", "context_len": 31}}}, "attracted": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 116, "context": "Here and there Lilian ran , hunting in all Peggy 's favourite haunts -- now peeping into a hollow yew-tree , now peering at the top of a ladder , now rummaging in the tool-shed , then back through the sand-quarry into the stack-yard , where there was a very good chance that the young lady might be hidden away in some snug little hole among the hay ; but though Lilian got a tolerable amount of hay-seed into her hair , her efforts were fruitless , and she was just turning away , hot and out of breath , to give up the useless search , when the sound of a low , chuckling laugh attracted her to the barn", "context_len": 121}}}, "adversary": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 20, "context": "The commander of the Spanish fleet agreed to this readily , not only because ( knowing the disposition of his adversary ) he feared further loss to his own side by prolonging the fight , but because he greatly admired the valour of Sir Richard Grenville , and desired to save his life", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "He felt sort of bruised and beaten though he had knocked down his adversary and would have stamped on him if his mother had not appeared at that moment and carried him off", "context_len": 33}}}, "apprehended": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 59, "context": "But little notice was taken of them at first , for the members of the train were too busy attending to the wounded , and examining their own hurts , to think of Indians , unless it was to look occasionally to satisfy themselves that they were really gone , and that there was no farther trouble to be apprehended from them", "context_len": 62}}}, "embraced": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 2, "context": "Then he embraced Alaeddin and fell to kissing him and said to him , `` Excuse me , O my son , for that I was going [ 623 ] to bereave thee of thy life , through the wickedness of yonder accursed sorcerer who cast thee into this pit ; and indeed , O my son , I was excusable in that which I did with thee , inasmuch as I saw myself bereft of my daughter and mine only one , who is dearer to me than my kingdom , and thou knowest how fathers ' hearts yearn upon their children , more by token that I have but the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 114}}}, "repay": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 43, "context": "Truly I am deeply thankful to my good friend , Suleiman Ali ; but for reasons of my own I am not desirous of being ransomed at present , especially at such a cost , which I should feel bound in honour to repay to him ; therefore , I pray you to write to him , saying that while I thank him from my heart for his kindness , I am not able to avail myself of it", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "I could , -- yes , I could arm in thy behalf A thousand gallant hands , might I but say , The injured will on the oppressor turn , Unite the love of freedom with revenge , A thousand-fold repay the debt he owes To your brave confidence ; in Ravensby Ye will destroy a foe and win a friend ! \u2019 Could I speak thus -- --", "context_len": 69}}}, "suffocated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Laughed at in his sufferings by the savages , almost suffocated by the bad smells about the hut , taken out at times to be the sport of his captors , unable to eat , full of aches and pains , he was yet able to look up and say , `` Let the Lord do as He sees fit , '' and to read his Bible and feel refreshed", "context_len": 70}}}, "subsided": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 48, "context": "As this seemed the most humane suggestion , Master Dormouse was tucked up in bed once more , and , still protesting , was carried to a sunny bank under an apple-tree , and stowed away under a protecting clump of leaves , where his plaintive voice gradually subsided , and he settled down for five months of oblivion , to ignore the winter frosts and storms until the April sunshine should tempt him out of his lair", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "When the first feeling of surprise and wonder had a little subsided , Alexander explained to the assembly the nature of the conspiracy , and the circumstances connected with the self-execution of one of the guilty participators in it", "context_len": 39}}}, "harnessing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I say , the men are harnessing the teams , '' exclaimed George", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I suppose you know nothing about harnessing , '' she said to Dallas", "context_len": 14}}}, "plentifully": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "K\u00f3sa is the name of a small forest-bird , resembling a sparrow ; but the name of the town and river could possibly be an ancient form of \u014d sa , \u014ds\u00e1 , 'os\u00e1 poke or pokeweed , a plant with red berries , which grows plentifully and to an enormous height throughout the South", "context_len": 55}}}, "possess": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 47, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "Living on grass , and in the woods , with the milk from the house , he had not that protuberance of belly which swine reared in sties possess , and really merited Charlie \u2019 s encomium of being handsome ; he jumped up on his master and rubbed against his legs , with low grunts , expressive of satisfaction", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed not to do aught against him , so he was dumb and could make the Sultan no answer", "context_len": 128}}}, "debt": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 14, "context": "He knew what it was to be without peace ; for having got into debt when he was first in the army , and knowing the distress it caused his family at home , his mind was so troubled that he wrote to his mother : `` Oh , what agony I have endured ! What sleepless nights I have passed since the perusal of that letter ! The review of my past life , especially the retrospect of the last two years , has at last quite startled me , and at the same time disgusted me", "context_len": 98}}}, "refractory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Over an hour passed before they brought the refractory animals back , and an exciting time the boys had watching the race , and shouting and hurrahing when the foaming , panting creatures rushed into the camp , followed by their almost breathless pursuers", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "At the same time that Williams and Fischer were discussing the case of this particularly refractory plebe , there were other cadets doing likewise , but with far different sentiments and views", "context_len": 32}}}, "hurrahing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Over an hour passed before they brought the refractory animals back , and an exciting time the boys had watching the race , and shouting and hurrahing when the foaming , panting creatures rushed into the camp , followed by their almost breathless pursuers", "context_len": 44}}}, "pursuers": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 29, "context": "She further stated that , upon awaking , she had again gone aloft to take another long and careful look round , but had seen nothing more of our pursuers , and was therefore inclined to believe that we were now reasonably safe , provided , of course , that we did all our travelling at night , kept a sharp look-out , and were careful not to allow ourselves to be fallen in with by other users of the river", "context_len": 81}}}, "quest": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 21, "context": "In 1559 another Spanish leader , Tristan de Luna , disembarked in or near Mobile bay , then went north in quest of gold and treasure , reached Nanipacna , or `` pueblo Santa Cruz de Nanipacna , '' and from there arrived , after experiencing many privations and trials , among the Co\u00e7as , who were then engaged in warfare with the Napochies ( nanpissa ? cf", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "Already the lust for gold had entered into his soul , and he was ready to hazard everything for the sake of that which had cost the lives of hundreds of men in these seas -- the quest of hidden treasure", "context_len": 41}}}, "prickly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "They had just reached the side of a little incline when Peggy 's bootlace , which she had neglected to fasten all the morning , tripped her up , and over she went , rolling into a prickly gorse-bush , while Bobby , who was so close behind that he could not stop himself , fell over her , and collapsed into a boggy hollow , where he lay panting for breath until Peggy picked herself up and hauled him out", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` There was a rush down the hill to the big chestnut tree about whose roots lay the prickly burs which the frost had opened to show the shining brown nuts within", "context_len": 32}}}, "pears": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "This was the first time he had had an opportunity offered him to try his gun , as they had seen no living creature upon the desert of alkali which they had occupied more than a week in crossing , and but few among the prickly pears and sage-brush that succeeded the poisonous salts", "context_len": 54}}}, "sufferers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "I knew that Sadiq was speaking about sickness , castor-oil , and quinine , and people going about to look after the sufferers ; but I could not get at his full meaning ; and as he was clearly on business , I thought it better to call in C", "context_len": 50}}}, "motherless": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Indeed , the latter became so very ill that , for two or three days , Guy feared he should soon be motherless", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "Folks had said that the Rector , like many a man who marries late in life , had made an idol of his motherless boy , and they had said , again , that the father 's heart was broken and the print of death was on his face as he stood by his child 's open grave", "context_len": 58}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 69, "context": "could I bear it to come home and not find you here , -- never again to see you smile when I come in ; never to hear you say , ' I 'm so glad you 've come , Tom ; ' never to get the kiss that puts heart into me after a hard day 's work ? And the babies , -- would you wish them motherless ? To be sure , you ca n't do for them what you once did , but that will all come right yet ; and there 's the mother 's eye to overlook and see that things do n't go too far wrong ; here 's the mother voice and the mother smile for them to turn to", "context_len": 128}}}, "additionally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It was a good thing for him that he was obliged to work additionally hard , else he might also have fallen ill from excessive grief", "context_len": 26}}}, "excessive": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 72, "context": "Bi ' l ghewali may be a clerical error for bi ' l ghalibi , `` for the most part , in general , '' in which case we may read , `` Folk in general abound [ in talk ] anent her virtues ; '' or bi ' l ghewali may perhaps be used in the sense ( of which use , however , I know no instance ) of 'in excessive estimation , ' in which latter case the passage might be rendered , `` Folk abound in setting a high value on ( or extolling ) her virtues", "context_len": 101}}}, "pronounced": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 62, "context": "And certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 22, "context": "His heart sank , for hardly half a dozen steps were taken when \u2019 Ceph limped again : the halt grew more pronounced , and suddenly he hobbled one or two steps on three legs , holding the remaining hoof clear of the ground as he did so", "context_len": 48}}}, "enviously": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "There was no boulevard at West Point , but the area in barracks served the purpose , and Mark could not help noticing that as he went the yearlings were gazing enviously at him , and the plebes with undisguised admiration", "context_len": 41}}}, "skillful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "By some skillful manoeuvering , they managed to approach within gun-shot of the deer , of which there were five or six , brousing quietly", "context_len": 25}}}, "buffaloes": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Graham uttered an exclamation of delight , `` Was there ever such good luck before ? '' he cried , `` Those are buffaloes ! I had no idea we should find them here so early", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Well , first they would find , either by accident or after a search , a herd of buffaloes , grazing on a plateau , perhaps three or four hundred feet above the river , for such are very often found a mile or more in length along the Columbia or Missouri Rivers , which abruptly terminate , forming a precipice so perfectly perpendicular that neither man or beast can gain a foothold on their sides", "context_len": 77}}}, "advised": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 2, "context": "'As I advised you in my last , we were on our way to Farningham , when something went wrong with the engine , and we had to stop here for repairs , and mamma was so charmed with this little village that she decided to stay awhile ; she says it seems to suit her better than any place she has seen ; poor mamma , I wish I could find some place where she would be satisfied", "context_len": 79}}}, "inclinations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "I know that you love an active life ; and as , for nine months , you have put a rein on your inclinations , and have devoted yourself wholly to study , so that you might be of greater use to the Order , you have a good right to any employment in which your knowledge can be utilised", "context_len": 60}}}, "awaiting": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 73, "context": "`` `` Nay , '' answered she , `` but I am of this city ; '' and he said to her , `` Thou art of this city and knowest not that this is the night of the going in of the Grand Vizier 's son to the Lady Bedrulbudour , the Sultan 's daughter ? Nay , he is presently in the bath and yonder Amirs and troops are drawn up awaiting him , against he come forth , so they may carry him in procession to the palace of the Sultan 's daughter", "context_len": 96}}}, "shaggy": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 58, "context": "He was surprised to find that they were not as large as elephants , but on the contrary about the size of a cow , which animal they closely resembled in the shape of their bodies , and limbs ; but their hair , instead of being of the same length all over their body , grew in shaggy tufts upon the back and sides , and lengthened into a sweeping mane upon the neck", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "He had a little rough shepherd 's hut there , just sufficient to form a shelter at night-time , and every now and then he would make an expedition to look after his sheep , or the tiny shaggy ponies which were turned out loose to wander almost wild upon the moors", "context_len": 52}}}, "mane": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 71, "context": "He was surprised to find that they were not as large as elephants , but on the contrary about the size of a cow , which animal they closely resembled in the shape of their bodies , and limbs ; but their hair , instead of being of the same length all over their body , grew in shaggy tufts upon the back and sides , and lengthened into a sweeping mane upon the neck", "context_len": 75}}}, "adornment": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "This adornment took from them the peaceful expression of the majority of our domestic cattle , and gave them instead the terrible one of the untamed lion", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 84, "context": "oriels , [ 651 ] and said to him , `` How deemest thou , O my Lady Fatimeh , of this wonderful pavilion ? '' [ 652 ] `` By Allah , O my daughter , '' replied he , `` it is indeed marvellous in the extreme , [ 653 ] nor methinketh is its like found in the world ; nay , it is magnificent exceedingly ; but oh , for one thing which would far increase it in beauty and adornment ! '' And the princess said to him , `` O my Lady Fatimeh , what is lacking to it and what is this thing which would adorn it ? Tell me of it ; I had thought that it was altogether perfect", "context_len": 128}}}, "untamed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "This adornment took from them the peaceful expression of the majority of our domestic cattle , and gave them instead the terrible one of the untamed lion", "context_len": 27}}}, "imbedded": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "Guy did not at first notice their horns , which were small , and almost imbedded in their thick , woolly hair , but it did not need a second look to assure him that they could do a great deal of harm , if once called into service", "context_len": 49}}}, "woolly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Guy did not at first notice their horns , which were small , and almost imbedded in their thick , woolly hair , but it did not need a second look to assure him that they could do a great deal of harm , if once called into service", "context_len": 49}}}, "hoofs": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 11, "context": "The dust of the prairie began to rise from beneath their hoofs in clouds , through which Guy could indistinctly see the buffaloes dashing forward , one turning occasionally upon some audacious man who had fired upon him , who would then wheel his horse quickly and escape from the reach of the infuriated animal , which would continue its flight or fall to the earth , with a terrible bellow", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "With another furious outburst the knight rushed out of the room , mounted his horse , and , followed by his two servants and the messenger of ill-tidings , rode furiously down the road to Midhurst , the noise of the horses \u2019 hoofs clattering on the frosty road testifying to the speed at which they were urged", "context_len": 58}}}, "horsemen": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 59, "context": "Startled by the red-coated Britishers rising up at the word of their leader , they broke and fled ; and the men of the 93rd , who , but a little before , had made up their minds to die where they stood , saw as in a dream their enemies scattered and broken ; and the cloud of horsemen which had threatened to engulf and annihilate them , make no effort to snatch the victory which seemed within their grasp", "context_len": 81}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I never use them things , but my eyes tell me a blamed sight more than that can tell ye ; them horsemen ye obsarved are Injins", "context_len": 27}}}, "indistinctly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The dust of the prairie began to rise from beneath their hoofs in clouds , through which Guy could indistinctly see the buffaloes dashing forward , one turning occasionally upon some audacious man who had fired upon him , who would then wheel his horse quickly and escape from the reach of the infuriated animal , which would continue its flight or fall to the earth , with a terrible bellow", "context_len": 71}}}, "audacious": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 18, "context": "He was absent on the third of these little excursions of his , and was surreptitiously amusing his audacious little self by seeing how it seemed to sit in the Oueen 's own stall , when hark ! -- yes , that was going to be The Roseate Hues , and with a bound that came near bringing the royal draperies with him he was out of the stall in a trice and fairly scrambling up the organ stairs", "context_len": 79}}}, "infuriated": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "The dust of the prairie began to rise from beneath their hoofs in clouds , through which Guy could indistinctly see the buffaloes dashing forward , one turning occasionally upon some audacious man who had fired upon him , who would then wheel his horse quickly and escape from the reach of the infuriated animal , which would continue its flight or fall to the earth , with a terrible bellow", "context_len": 71}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 0, "context": "Infuriated by Gervaise 's interference and threatening attitude , the corsair drew his long knife ; but before he could strike , Gervaise caught his wrist ; the knife fell from his hand , and Gervaise kicked it through the open gangway into the sea", "context_len": 45}}}, "retreating": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "Alexander did not know that Darius was so near ; and as for Darius , he was confident that Alexander was retreating before him ; for , as the Macedonian army was so small , and his own forces constituted such an innumerable host , the idea that Alexander would remain to brave a battle was , in his opinion , entirely out of the question", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He had known from the sound of their retreating footsteps that they were not between him and his goal", "context_len": 19}}}, "slaughter": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "Harwood , who exclaimed that it was a miracle that he was alive , and asked him if he was n't killed , and then shouted out for somebody to go in pursuit of the horse , which was galloping away in the opposite direction to the buffaloes , which were suffered to depart without any further attempt being made to slaughter them", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 9, "context": "In fact , it is probable that Alexander 's slaughter of the Persian army at Arbela , and subsequent spoliation of Susa , constitute , taken together , the most gigantic case of murder and robbery which was ever committed by man ; so that , in performing these deeds , the great hero attained at last to the glory of having perpetrated the grandest and most imposing of all human crimes", "context_len": 72}}}, "bruises": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 70, "context": "The sandals were anything but comfortable , and her feet hurt dreadfully on the stones , while every gorse-bush she passed seemed to be stretching out spiky fingers to scratch her bare legs ; she was tired after her morning 's adventures on the moors , and the eight miles seemed to lengthen out to an interminable vista , in spite of the way being downhill ; sundry bumps and bruises , which she had never noticed at the time , began to ache now , and the bee-stings on her neck smarted , until she hardly knew how to bear the pain", "context_len": 103}}}, "carcasses": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "Guy was surprised and delighted to find that his fool-hardy escapade had brought upon him no injury except a few bruises , which , however , did not prevent him from assisting the men to take into camp the carcasses of the three buffaloes they had slain", "context_len": 47}}}, "choicest": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 47, "context": "We had heard that he was in the neighborhood of Chattanooga , giving his men and horses a much-needed rest ; but on the way news came to us that , in spite of his brilliant achievements in the field , he had been deprived of the choicest regiments of his brigade -- men whom he had trained and seasoned to war", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "as our choicest young Natives , converts or descendants of converts ; one is the grandson of a martyr ! \u2019 These boys or their friends paid fees , when they could , which was not always ; and the fees , though perhaps sufficient to cover their food , were by no means sufficient to cover the cost of a good education", "context_len": 63}}}, "harass": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Then the choicest parts of the bodies were cut up and salted , and the rest left to the hungry wolves , who are the natural enemies of the buffalo -- one of which , when wounded , they will often follow , and harass to death", "context_len": 47}}}, "contradictory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "[ Footnote 480 : The description of the famous upper hall with the four-and-twenty windows is one of the most contused and incoherent parts of the Nights and well-nigh defies the efforts of the translator to define the exact nature of the building described by the various and contradictory passages which refer to it", "context_len": 54}}}, "sketch": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 4, "context": "As only a slight sketch of Guy 's wonderful escape had been given by Harwood upon his return to camp , the children were anxious to hear a full account of it , and as soon as the dishes were washed , the fuel for the night brought in and Guy was at liberty to take his usual seat by the fire , they called upon him to tell them all about it", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "Some of my readers may desire to know more about Dewey and his two friends , and I will sketch for their benefit the events to which Bradley referred", "context_len": 29}}}, "liberty": {"freq": 76, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 15, "context": "Good Parson Perrient had painted Providence plantation as a sort of paradise , where the liberty and toleration dreamt of by a few in England were the law for all ; but was that refuge open to him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought ,", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Accordingly , although the inhabitants made no opposition to his entrance , he marched in with the phalanx formed , and gave the soldiers liberty to kill and plunder as they pleased", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Tell me about that gull , will you ? '' asked Dallas as he gave me more liberty", "context_len": 19}}}, "discomfiture": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 80, "context": "With the latter he did as he had done the past night , to wit , he took him and couched him in the draught-house , where he deft him parched for excess of fright and dismay ; whilst Alaeddin arose and placing the sword between himself and the Lady Bedrulbudour , lay down and slept till the morning , when the genie appeared and restored the twain to their place , leaving Alaeddin full of joy at [ the discomfiture of ] the Vizier 's son", "context_len": 87}}}, "earnestness": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 17, "context": "But Harrison 's expression did not respond ; he continued to speak with grave , almost severe earnestness , and the surprise with which the king heard him quickly froze into a look of offence , and then abruptly his Majesty dismissed Major Harrison with a slight inclination of his head , and came forward to the supper-table ; while Harrison , with a silent greeting to his friends by the fire , called Dick , and left the room", "context_len": 80}}}, "plateau": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Well , first they would find , either by accident or after a search , a herd of buffaloes , grazing on a plateau , perhaps three or four hundred feet above the river , for such are very often found a mile or more in length along the Columbia or Missouri Rivers , which abruptly terminate , forming a precipice so perfectly perpendicular that neither man or beast can gain a foothold on their sides", "context_len": 77}}}, "terminate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` Well , first they would find , either by accident or after a search , a herd of buffaloes , grazing on a plateau , perhaps three or four hundred feet above the river , for such are very often found a mile or more in length along the Columbia or Missouri Rivers , which abruptly terminate , forming a precipice so perfectly perpendicular that neither man or beast can gain a foothold on their sides", "context_len": 77}}}, "precipice": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 84, "context": "If we had it we could go on , we could go on at least till the end of the season , and there would be an excuse to take your mother to the country , and she might never know , never ; but it wants two months to the end of the season , and the house is full , and every one is in the height of good humour , and yet they are all walking on the brink of a precipice ; the earth is eaten away beneath us , and any moment the whole thing may topple through", "context_len": 103}}}, "perpendicular": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` Well , first they would find , either by accident or after a search , a herd of buffaloes , grazing on a plateau , perhaps three or four hundred feet above the river , for such are very often found a mile or more in length along the Columbia or Missouri Rivers , which abruptly terminate , forming a precipice so perfectly perpendicular that neither man or beast can gain a foothold on their sides", "context_len": 77}}}, "crowned": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 63, "context": "in the face of the carelessness with which the text treats kushk as synonymous now with keszr or teyyareh and now with liwan or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this", "context_len": 128}}}, "decoys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Toward this precipice a young warrior wrapped in a buffalo robe , and crowned with the head and horns , decoys the game , while the others chase them forward , riding their swiftest horses , bearing their best arms , and uttering their wildest shouts", "context_len": 47}}}, "decoy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "The whole herd maddened by the hunters , will usually follow the decoy -- their fancied leader -- when suddenly he will hide beneath some cliff , the buffaloes will rush on , seeing no danger ahead , or unable to check their headlong career , and thus very often a hundred or more will spring over the precipice , and be dashed to pieces on the rocks below", "context_len": 69}}}, "jerking": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "He sat firmly in the saddle , leaning slightly forward and now and then jerking down his hat which was in danger of being whisked off by the wind", "context_len": 29}}}, "sentence": {"freq": 68, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 67, "word_index": 64, "context": "And certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "On page 114 , word `` of '' added to sentence ``", "context_len": 12}}}, "mellow": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 18, "context": "Fred displayed an enormous melon which had been ripe for some days , and was now rather too mellow and soft , and , having been jolted somewhat severely on its ride down the mountain , had fallen to pieces , presenting , as joking Fred said , `` a very melon choly sight", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It is only the sunshine of God 's presence that can mellow us thoroughly , and if people wilfully turn away from that they are bound to become shrivelled and sour", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Ah ! some kind of liquid , I should imagine , '' and he shook the box , its contents echoing out a mellow , gurgling sound", "context_len": 28}}}, "gloaming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Little Allie , tired with roaming , Fell asleep one summer day ; In the soft , and mellow gloaming , That the fairies haunt , they say", "context_len": 29}}}, "glen": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 44, "context": "They were forbidden to go there without some grown person , but this command was scarcely needed by the little girls , since Bessie could not have made her way alone without the help of some stronger hand ; and though Maggie thought the glen a great curiosity , she did not like the chill and gloom of the place , and was glad to come out once more into the bright sunshine which met them at the entrance", "context_len": 79}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Down the little path from the glen above solemnly marched a very small girl indeed , so round and fat and chubby that she looked nearly as broad as she was long", "context_len": 32}}}, "clearer": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` 'Child ! ' they said , as slow around her One by one they floated on , 'Look into the clearer ether , Close beside the setting sun ! ' Then she looked , and lo ! the cloudlets Parted back and showed her there , Myriad angels , sinless spirits Sporting in a garden fair", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Now , dearest Fred , could there be a clearer opening than at Batala for an earnest Christian man , whether in Orders or not ? I am not thinking of you , for I would not have any one subject to headaches come to this feverish land ; but I am thinking of your brother collegians", "context_len": 57}}}, "ether": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` 'Child ! ' they said , as slow around her One by one they floated on , 'Look into the clearer ether , Close beside the setting sun ! ' Then she looked , and lo ! the cloudlets Parted back and showed her there , Myriad angels , sinless spirits Sporting in a garden fair", "context_len": 57}}}, "sinless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` 'Child ! ' they said , as slow around her One by one they floated on , 'Look into the clearer ether , Close beside the setting sun ! ' Then she looked , and lo ! the cloudlets Parted back and showed her there , Myriad angels , sinless spirits Sporting in a garden fair", "context_len": 57}}}, "twining": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Joe walked as much as possible at Old Charlie \u2019 s head , urging him gently at times , putting his arm caressingly over the beast \u2019 s drooping neck , or twining his hand in the long , wet mane", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "A sharp snatch at his line , followed by a long peculiar drag , told him what was at his bait ; and after a little giving and taking , he drew a heavy twining conger eel over the boat 's edge , having no little difficulty in preventing it from tangling his line , for it was quite a yard in length , and proportionately thick", "context_len": 67}}}, "alarms": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "The worst of it was that he infected Nancy with his alarms to such an extent that she could scarcely be persuaded to put her nose out at the back-door after dusk ( except on her evening out , when her sweetheart came to fetch her ) , which was distinctly inconvenient if a pail of water were wanted during the evening , or she happened to have left the coal-box standing in the yard", "context_len": 75}}}, "refrain": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 64, "context": "Bradford and Miss Rush did all they could to make the children feel kindly towards her , it was some days before things went at all smoothly between the old lady and the little ones , and Annie Stanton , seeing the consequence of her thoughtlessness , had more than once reason to regret it , and to take to herself a lesson to refrain from evil speaking", "context_len": 68}}}, "peerless": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` 'Here the flow'rets bloom forever ; Here the sun of God doth shine ; Here doth flow the crystal river , Giving all a life divine ! ' Then the peerless vision faded , And the fairies stole away ; All the dell with gloom was shaded , Darkness 'round sweet Allie lay", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "No matter how well mounted a party of Indians might be , none could overhaul the peerless Dick , whose graceful legs were again doubling under him with marvelous rapidity and carrying him and his burden as an eagle bears its eaglet on its broad back", "context_len": 46}}}, "dell": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "Torrents descended from these mountains , forming streams which flowed in currents more and more deep and slow as they descended into the plains , and combining at last into one central river , which flowed to the eastward , and escaped from the environage of mountains through a most celebrated dell called the Vale of Tempe", "context_len": 57}}}, "shaded": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 102, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 70, "context": "Eliot , as he led the boy into a handsome room , where there were long shelves of books , and great comfortable leather-covered chairs , and costly Turkish rugs on the hardwood floor , with a wood fire burning cheerfully in an open fireplace , and a frail , sweet-faced woman sitting amid piled-up cushions in an invalid \u2019 s chair near a table , on which stood a shaded lamp and lay many books and magazines", "context_len": 78}}}, "redeeming": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` Then she woke from out her slumber , And she said -- within her heart -- 'Shall I join that happy number ? In their joyous song take part ? ' Then she prayed that God would lead her In the path to heaven above , And that she might dwell forever Blessed by Christ 's redeeming love", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Though chagrined by the loss of their prisoner , the capture of one of his rescuers was a redeeming feature of the fray , and the latest captive was brought before the officers for the purpose of being interrogated", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "One character in the tale has always puzzled historians -- a character , so far as the author knows , absolutely without redeeming trait -- Edric Streorn", "context_len": 27}}}, "rover": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Rover followed them round , rooting under the stumps for worms , and once in a while would shove his nose on a hot coal , which would make him run away squealing", "context_len": 33}}}, "defense": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 30, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}}}, "verdant": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "As they looked around upon the verdant dell , and upon the snow-capped mountains that arose in the distance , all the arid plain they had passed , -- the desert of alkali , and the hills of sand -- seemed like a dream , so great was the contrast between them and Virginia Dale", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The horses \u2019 hoofs echoed under the dark gateway and clattered across the drawbridge , the town of Portsmouth was left behind , and the dense cluster of timbered and red-tiled houses gave place to verdant fields and clumps of tall trees that even now were beginning to burst into leaf", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Again under cover , their mercurial spirits rose , and when at length the rain ceased , and the train rumbled betwixt the red-earthed , verdant coombes of Devon , bathed in brilliant sunshine , the Anzacs unanimously voted that there were worse places on earth than the Old Country", "context_len": 50}}}, "arid": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "As they looked around upon the verdant dell , and upon the snow-capped mountains that arose in the distance , all the arid plain they had passed , -- the desert of alkali , and the hills of sand -- seemed like a dream , so great was the contrast between them and Virginia Dale", "context_len": 55}}}, "enraptured": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "She is an inveterate relic-hunter ; is enraptured with Bunker Hill and the Old South ; delights in Cornhill , and wherever she can find a crooked old street that reminds her of Washington ; and pokes about all the old cemeteries , until I feel as eerie as Coleridge 's ancient mariner", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "A brilliant future seemed opening before her enraptured gaze", "context_len": 9}}}, "hermit": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "He was the black sheep of an otherwise respectable family , went off and spent his portion in riotous living , afterward bought a tract of ground above Harlem , turned hermit , raised geese and ducks and pigs , married and had three daughters and they in turn married , glad , I suppose , to get away from the penurious living", "context_len": 63}}}, "verdure": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` First , Gervassen fell , almost at the side of Alstarnah : then , one by one , all the rest of the people sank down and were buried by the soft , white snow , until at last not one remained to tell of the verdure that once crowned the mountains where the North King still reigns , or of the people he slew with terrible arrows of snow , like those he still loves to throw in derision upon any daring traveler that attempts to invade his dominions", "context_len": 91}}}, "vale": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "Years , years ago , so many years that all remembrance of the time is now lost , and only vague reports of it remain , the snowy mountains we now see were covered with verdure , even more luxuriant than that which makes this vale so beautiful", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` The Shepherd sought his sheep ; The Father sought his child ; They followed me o'er vale and hill , O'er deserts waste and wild", "context_len": 26}}}, "summits": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 81, "context": "It is true that Alexander 's soldiers , about to leave their homes to go to another quarter of the globe , and into scenes of danger and death from which it was very improbable that many of them would ever return , had no other celestial protection to look up to than the spirits of ancient heroes , who , they imagined , had , somehow or other , found their final home in a sort of heaven among the summits of the mountains , where they reigned , in some sense , over human affairs ; but this , small as it seems to us , was a great deal to them", "context_len": 114}}}, "wigwams": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "They could follow the game over any ground , and spear the fish in any stream they chose , so that hunger never entered their wigwams ; and in course of time they became so puffed up with their good fortune that they called themselves 'gods , ' and the neighboring tribes bowed and worshipped them", "context_len": 56}}}, "irreverent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Although General Harrison 's strong personal fascination and unselfish ardour still commanded his nephew 's affection and even admiration , the young man 's irreverent common sense could not help viewing these new Fifth Monarchy opinions held by his uncle and his uncle 's friends as fitter for Bedlam than for the pulpit or the parliament house", "context_len": 57}}}, "summit": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 51, "word_index": 61, "context": "But still she went on , but only slowly , because of the great press of people around her ; and suddenly an icy wind passed over them , and all fell to the earth shivering and terror-stricken , for they had never felt cold before , and they looked up to the mountain , and lo ! upon the very summit , at the door of the deserted wigwam , stood a terrible figure , clothed in white , and having a face as white as his robes , and his hair was like the long crystals that hang from the roofs of caves that the water goes through , and his eyes were like two great diamonds , white , yet blazing like the sun", "context_len": 127}}}, "warmest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` On the very summit of yonder peak , which seems to rise at least a thousand feet above its neighbors , and where the sun shone the warmest , the grains and fruits were most luxuriant , and the deer larger and tamer than in any other place , lived an old man , the chief of all the tribes that lived between the mountains and the great ocean in the far west", "context_len": 74}}}, "grains": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` On the very summit of yonder peak , which seems to rise at least a thousand feet above its neighbors , and where the sun shone the warmest , the grains and fruits were most luxuriant , and the deer larger and tamer than in any other place , lived an old man , the chief of all the tribes that lived between the mountains and the great ocean in the far west", "context_len": 74}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Next he removed the wad over the powder , and poured the black grains out into his hand", "context_len": 18}}}, "soars": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "His beard was like silver , and his face bore the marks of that wisdom which can only come with age , yet his form was not bent , and his eyes were as strong as the eagle 's , that soars up and looks in the face of the sun", "context_len": 51}}}, "centuries": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}}}, "inheritor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 88, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}}}, "successor": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 95, "context": "`` He was indeed wonderful , and the wisest man upon the earth ; he knew all secrets of the land , and sea , and air , and from them he had gained the elixir that still kept the blood warm in his veins after the lapse of centuries , but he could not get from them contentment , -- his soul at last wearied of the habit of clay it had worn so long , and he began a search for one worthy to be the inheritor of his wisdom , and the successor of his power , that he might lie down and be at rest", "context_len": 109}}}, "instructed": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 29, "context": "Your Honor , said Lawyer Marsh , the defense , having no further witnesses and desiring none , rests here , with the request that the deputy sheriff be instructed to keep a close watch upon Timothy Davis until a warrant may be sworn out for his arrest", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "Inform him , then , that from the present time he will be excused from all work , except , of course , to join in ceremonials when all are required to be present ; and if you , Sir John , will pick out from among the servitors here one who is well instructed and educated , and capable of writing as well as reading his language , I will similarly relieve him of all other work , and place him at the disposal of young Tresham", "context_len": 88}}}, "lastly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 56, "context": "In the first place , I am well treated here , and my position is not an unpleasant one ; secondly , the sum required for ransom is altogether preposterous ; thirdly , I am not without hopes that I may some day find other means of freeing myself without so great a sacrifice ; and lastly , that I have a reason which I can not mention , why , at present , I would not quit Tripoli , even were I free tomorrow", "context_len": 85}}}, "priestess": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "Then the priestess went to her wigwam on the high mountains and sat down and thought of the last words the dead man had said to her , 'Beware of him who reigneth at the northernmost part of the earth , for if thou showest weakness or any human passion he will have dominion over thee and all thy people", "context_len": 60}}}, "magician": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "So the headhunters had achieved their end at last ! They had captured the helpless old man whom their superstition had led them to regard as a Man of Evil , a magician whose spells had brought blight on their crops and sickness to their people", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 28, "context": "That the latter had finally triumphed , after a terrible struggle , and after yielding one important point to his enemy , which was , that if the magician or any of his successors yielded to human passion , the help of the spirits should be withdrawn from them , and their dominion and people left to the power of the terrible North King", "context_len": 64}}}, "decrepit": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "Yet , although in appearance a very Methuselah in age , this individual had a pair of piercing black eyes that glowed and sparkled with all the fire and passion of early manhood , and , bowed as he was , and decrepit as he appeared to be , he tottered across the intervening space with extraordinary agility , and seated himself in the second chair", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "During the conversation a lean and decrepit horse , led by a boy of about ten or eleven years of age , arrived at the wharf", "context_len": 26}}}, "northernmost": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Then the priestess went to her wigwam on the high mountains and sat down and thought of the last words the dead man had said to her , 'Beware of him who reigneth at the northernmost part of the earth , for if thou showest weakness or any human passion he will have dominion over thee and all thy people", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Atchina-\u00e1lgi , or `` Cedar Grove , '' the northernmost of all the Creek settlements , near the Hillabi-Etowa trail , on a side creek of Tallapoosa river and forty miles above Niuya ' \u00e1 a", "context_len": 36}}}, "dominion": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 105, "context": "the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How now ? Deemest thou Alaeddin worthy to be bridegroom to my daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour ? Hast thou seen and considered this royal building and all these riches which man 's wit can not comprehend ? '' The Vizier , of his envy of Alaeddin , answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , indeed this palace and its building and all these riches may not be but by means of enchantment , for that no man among men , no , not the mightiest of them in dominion or the greatest in wealth , might avail to upraise and stablish [ the like of ] this building in one night", "context_len": 128}}}, "communing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "She lived alone communing with spirits , and at sundry times appearing among the people to astonish them by her wisdom which as years advanced , become a thousand times more potent than had been that of the old magician", "context_len": 40}}}, "astonish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "She lived alone communing with spirits , and at sundry times appearing among the people to astonish them by her wisdom which as years advanced , become a thousand times more potent than had been that of the old magician", "context_len": 40}}}, "ebony": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Ten minutes , twenty minutes , passed , and the sun was within a hand's- breadth of the horizon when a man emerged from the `` palace , '' bearing a massive chair of ebony , quaintly-carved , and draped with a magnificent leopard 's skin , which he placed immediately before the open door , midway between the house and ourselves , and departed", "context_len": 65}}}, "mortal": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 89, "context": "Then , in batches of ten at a time , forty more victims were triced up to the boughs of this accursed tree by raw-hide ropes fastened to one wrist or one ankle , in such positions that their bodies showed clearly against the bright background of sky ; and , while thus suspended , whosoever would was at liberty to shoot at them with bows and arrows , the great object being , apparently , to pierce the body with as many arrows as possible without inflicting a mortal injury", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 15, "context": "Alfgar cast a longing glance behind at the spot where he knew all that was mortal of poor Bertric was left , to be , so far as the Danes cared , the prey of the wolf or the kite ; but the young Dane knew well that , if any were yet alive at Aescendune , the hallowed temple of the martyr would not want its due honour", "context_len": 69}}}, "e'er": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "'Alstarnah must no passion own , That mortal e'er before hath known", "context_len": 12}}}, "renowned": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "But all this , as you can plainly see , has nothing whatever to do with the title of this chapter , so it behooves us , as the preachers used to say , to turn our back on Harold and the charms of the renowned old college , and our faces toward the ocean and a far-off land -- far off , that is , as far as Windsor and the English are concerned , but very near and dear to the hearts of some of the rest of us", "context_len": 91}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 61, "context": "When the Lady Bedrulbudour saw this , she cried out and said to him , `` What hath this holy anchoress done , that thou burthenest thyself with the sore burden of her blood ? Hast thou no fear of God , that thou dost this and hast slain Fatimeh , who was a holy woman and whose divine gifts were renowned ? '' Quoth he to her , `` I have not slain Fatimeh ; nay , I have slain him who slew her ; for that this is the brother of the accursed Maugrabin enchanter , who took thee and by his sorcery transported the palace with thee to the land of Africa", "context_len": 115}}}, "excelled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "She excelled in all outdoor sports , having a keen eye and a true stroke , and was soon absorbed in making her hoops and helping on her partners , two quiet little girls considerably younger than herself , who seemed never to have played before , to judge by their absolute lack of skill", "context_len": 55}}}, "pomp": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 62, "context": "As for Alaeddin , he went out that day and saw the rejoicings toward in the city and the palace and fell a-laughing , especially when he heard the folk speak of the honour which had betided the Vizier 's son and the greatness of his good luck , in that he was become the Sultan 's son-in-law , and the exceeding pomp used in his marriage and bridal festivities ; and he said in himself , `` Ye know not , good simple folk that ye are , [ 403 ] what befell him last night , that ye envy him", "context_len": 102}}}, "ruins": {"freq": 78, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 77, "word_index": 86, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It did not take very long for the children to be hauled up by Joe at the top , and they found themselves standing among the Abbey ruins in the early gray of the dawn , with Aunt Helen rushing to catch them in her arms , and Lilian hugging them and sobbing over them by turns , while Nancy , her face all blotched and swollen with crying , kept hovering round to put in a kiss whenever the others gave her a chance , and even old David cleared his throat hard , and 'blessed the Lord they were safe", "context_len": 102}}}, "exclaim": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "With great joy she placed her hand in his , but at the moment she was about to speak , she felt an icy wind blow over her and a voice exclaim : 'Beware of the King of the North ! Pity thy people ! '", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "For Nora had also had a tidy fit , and had been turning out her desk , and she now drew forth a book with such a very blank and rueful face that Peggy might well exclaim", "context_len": 37}}}, "pleading": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` She fled to her wigwam in terror , and for days refused to admit the chieftain , who stood without pleading for an answer but at length she ventured to glance at him through a tiny hole in the buffalo hide that formed the walls of her tent , and in an instant all her love for her people and all fear of the warning voice vanished , and she promised to be Gervassen 's bride", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "There was a penetrating sweetness in the pleading , but she saw the red of surprise mount to the very edge of the man 's hair , and almost a frown settle between the eyes", "context_len": 35}}}, "infatuated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Again came the icy wind and the voice , but so infatuated was she that they failed to turn her from her purpose , although her lover asked the meaning of them", "context_len": 33}}}, "triumphed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "What did it matter whether he himself played football or not ? True , he would have enjoyed doing so , but , to a certain extent , he had triumphed over the fellow who had tried to drive him out of school , and might it not be best if that satisfied him ? Discord on the team was a serious misfortune , and only for Eliot \u2019 s persistence he would have taken himself away already", "context_len": 78}}}, "yielding": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 39, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "He was hastening in a direction opposite to them , but not at such a headlong pace , as though he had a certain dignity to maintain which forbade too much yielding to fright", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Fanny \u2019 s gentle , yielding nature went no farther than being troubled", "context_len": 13}}}, "successors": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "That the latter had finally triumphed , after a terrible struggle , and after yielding one important point to his enemy , which was , that if the magician or any of his successors yielded to human passion , the help of the spirits should be withdrawn from them , and their dominion and people left to the power of the terrible North King", "context_len": 64}}}, "withdrawn": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` And one of the best , '' I said impulsively , and as I thought of all that was going to happen to that most precious mother , and how soon that presence would be withdrawn from our mortal gaze , and how soon that spirit would go to the God who gave it , tears sprang to my eyes , and even Mrs", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "The turtle seemed undecided about it , for some time after Fenn had placed it on the ground it did not move , but remained with head , legs and tail withdrawn into the protecting shell", "context_len": 36}}}, "exists": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` 'It can not be that he exists , ' returned the warrior , 'else he would have endeavored to enter the land over which my tribe is scattered , and never , never has one of his subjects been seen or heard of upon it", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "A paucity of materials exists as to the early part of the life ; but in later years the difficulty is of a precisely opposite description , arising from a superabundance of details", "context_len": 33}}}, "descent": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 16, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}}}, "clogging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "Then he and all the people were filled with still greater terror and turned to flee down the mountains , but the snow flakes -- the deadly arrows of the North King , came faster and faster , falling before as well as behind them , clogging the feet and chilling the life-blood of the people Alstarnah had betrayed", "context_len": 59}}}, "reigns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` First , Gervassen fell , almost at the side of Alstarnah : then , one by one , all the rest of the people sank down and were buried by the soft , white snow , until at last not one remained to tell of the verdure that once crowned the mountains where the North King still reigns , or of the people he slew with terrible arrows of snow , like those he still loves to throw in derision upon any daring traveler that attempts to invade his dominions", "context_len": 91}}}, "dominions": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 90, "context": "`` First , Gervassen fell , almost at the side of Alstarnah : then , one by one , all the rest of the people sank down and were buried by the soft , white snow , until at last not one remained to tell of the verdure that once crowned the mountains where the North King still reigns , or of the people he slew with terrible arrows of snow , like those he still loves to throw in derision upon any daring traveler that attempts to invade his dominions", "context_len": 91}}}, "unloading": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Those that had been over the route before said they got along remarkably well , while those to whom the experience was new , declared that with the breaking down of some wagons , the unloading of others , and letting them and goods they contained down the precipices by ropes , and the accidents attendant upon such work , they found the journey anything but delightful", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Colonel Harrington 's buckboard was backed to the platform and its driver was unloading a large trunk", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "I tell you another thing you do , said the captain : negroes there use lots of baskets , that they carry on their heads , filled with oranges and other things ; they also use them in loading and unloading vessels , and sometimes they carry them by straps of green hide that go over their shoulders", "context_len": 58}}}, "precipices": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 48, "context": "Those that had been over the route before said they got along remarkably well , while those to whom the experience was new , declared that with the breaking down of some wagons , the unloading of others , and letting them and goods they contained down the precipices by ropes , and the accidents attendant upon such work , they found the journey anything but delightful", "context_len": 67}}}, "buttercups": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "She gave Bobby a leg up on to Brindle 's broad back , and had just succeeded in scrambling inelegantly behind him , when the astonished quadruped paused in her task of cropping the succulent grass and buttercups , and realizing that something unusual was oppressing her , fled in mad career over the meadow , with the delighted children clinging desperately to her horns", "context_len": 65}}}, "perplexing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Now , this discovery of Ted 's , that Chris was a friend of Marie-Celeste , and the perplexing state of mind that followed , had transpired , you understand , two weeks previous to this particular chapter , and Ted , you remember , is lying on the chintz-covered lounge in the living-room , having gained strength enough in the mean time to walk from his bed to the lounge unaided", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "All day long Ben \u2019 s mind dwelt on the perplexing problem , and gradually he came to believe there was only one solution ; he must give up school , leave Oakdale , and find a job of some sort by which he could support himself and Jerry", "context_len": 49}}}, "fable": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Bessie had heard many a fairy tale , many a fable , and had never objected to them , though she always preferred to listen to stories which were , or might be , true ; but somehow , no one could tell why , this fancy about the rock seemed to shock her sense of truth , and from this time she could never be persuaded to call it the `` Chief 's Head", "context_len": 75}}}, "pleasurably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 72, "context": "But although Guy was so uncertain as to what his fate would be in California , he soon became as anxious to reach it as the rest , for nothing for many weeks occurred to break the monotony of their journey , and the only excitement they had at all was in looking out for Indians , which were said to be very plenty upon their route , and in being constantly pleasurably disappointed in not coming upon any", "context_len": 79}}}, "encountering": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "One day , indeed , they were greatly surprised by the descent of a terrific rain storm upon them , for they had never dreamed of encountering rain in that elevated region , where not even a drop of dew was found in the early morning", "context_len": 46}}}, "torrents": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 49, "context": "He strolled about the open space , pausing now and then to survey through his glasses the snowy peaks which towered far into the sky to the westward , or at the lower hills to the north , where the gnarled pine , the dwarfed cedar , the rushing torrents and the gorges made up one of the many wild regions which abound over thousands of square miles of area", "context_len": 70}}}, "deluge": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 105, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}}}, "saddles": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 90, "context": "Moreover , he brought him into a place , which he found full of coffers overflowing with royal raiment , such as ravished the wit , gold-inwoven stuffs , Indian and Chinese , and brocades , and he showed him also many other places , all full of that which beggareth description , till at last he brought him into a stable , wherein were horses whose like is not found with the kings of the world ; and therewithin he showed him a storehouse , full of housings and saddles of price , all broidered with pearls and precious stones and so forth", "context_len": 104}}}, "expend": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "It is remarkable how closely nature follows a rule in her most perfect work ; here were two figures , built by her a thousand miles apart , racing there , and each striving with might and main , yet the sum total of the energy that each was able to expend so nearly alike that yard by yard they struggled on , without an inch of difference between them", "context_len": 70}}}, "spur": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "It was about two o \u2019 clock when the yokes were adjusted to the necks of the oxen , the horsemen swung into their saddles , and the cavalcade headed for Fort Laramie , on the other side of the mountain spur which bears the latter half of that name", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 48, "context": "At the same instant , a wiry pony , with flashing eyes and dilated nostrils and fine muscles aquiver , made a tremendous leap which carried him almost to the middle of the street , and heading toward the river , plunged away under the prick of the spur , on a dead run", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "There were many places where the hedge-hog could not go close to the stumps , because the large spur roots rolled it off : around these Charlie hacked the grain in with a hoe", "context_len": 34}}}, "maintained": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 78, "context": "Two sets of mail bags were approaching each other from points two thousand miles apart , and there were times when this approach was at the astounding speed of forty and even fifty miles an hour ! The average daily rate was two hundred and fifty miles a day , but where everything was favorable , or when an express rider was fleeing from the vengeful red men , his pony struck a gait of twenty-five miles and maintained it , when an untrained horse would have dropped in his tracks", "context_len": 91}}}, "expressly": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 2, "context": "It seemed expressly formed for the enjoyment of small boys and girls , for as it lay across a corner , you had only to walk up the road to get to the top , then settling yourself firmly with feet straight in front , you let go , and slid like a bolt from an arrow down -- down -- till you found your feet on the road again , and could climb up once more and repeat the performance", "context_len": 81}}}, "iota": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Whatever had to be done , she was ready to do it , -- not one iota more ready to write her books , or to visit in the Workhouse , than to teach the Robins , \u2019 to amuse visitors , old or young , to entertain guests at dinner or luncheon , to take her part in a family glee , \u2019 to join in merry games , to conduct friends on sight-seeing expeditions", "context_len": 76}}}, "enthusiasm": {"freq": 76, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 73, "word_index": 55, "context": "I do not for a moment deceive myself with the thought that , beyond the fact that her gift has made her feel an interest in me and my fate , she has any sentiment in the matter : probably , indeed , she looks back upon the gift as a foolish act of girlish enthusiasm that led her into making a promise that she now can not but find unpleasantly binding ; for it is but natural that among the young nobles of her own rank and country there must be some whom she would see with pleasure wearing her colours", "context_len": 102}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "I say not but that by a mighty effort of all Christendom , Palestine might be wrested from the infidels , as it was wrested before ; but the past shows us that while men or nations can be stirred to enthusiasm for a time , the fire does not last long , and once again the faithful few would be overwhelmed by the odds that would be brought against them , while Europe looked on impassive , if not indifferent", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 72, "context": "But who could long be grave at the outset of so promising an expedition ! The idea of a leisurely driving trip through the lovely Lake Country , stopping here and there , as the spirit moved them , at the comfortable little inns and hotels that abound in the region , had been such a supremely delightful idea , even in mere anticipation , that now that they were actually off enthusiasm knew no bounds , and mirth was literally unconfined", "context_len": 82}}}, "deserts": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then he went forth , wandering in the deserts and knowing not whitherward he should aim , and ceased not going till he came to a river ; whereupon , his hope being cut off for stress of chagrin that possessed him , he thought to cast himself into the stream ; but , for that he was a pious Muslim , professing the unity of God , he feared God in himself and stood on the bank ; of the stream to perform the ablution", "context_len": 86}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` The Shepherd sought his sheep ; The Father sought his child ; They followed me o'er vale and hill , O'er deserts waste and wild", "context_len": 26}}}, "vales": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "In fact they were too weary of their long journey to look around them for enjoyment , but rather looked forward to it , when all deserts of alkali , of sand and sage-brush being past , they might by the rivers and in the peaceful vales of California find rest and plenty", "context_len": 53}}}, "placer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Oh , do n't say that , '' exclaimed John , cheerfully , `` you just come along with me and my partners , we are going straight to the placer diggings , and we 'll take care of you until you can do for yourself , which wo n't be long , you may be sure ; I should n't wonder if you 're as rich as Rothschild in a few years", "context_len": 74}}}, "diggings": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Oh , do n't say that , '' exclaimed John , cheerfully , `` you just come along with me and my partners , we are going straight to the placer diggings , and we 'll take care of you until you can do for yourself , which wo n't be long , you may be sure ; I should n't wonder if you 're as rich as Rothschild in a few years", "context_len": 74}}}, "mines": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 13, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}}}, "exultantly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "Many of the young men were going to the placer diggings or the deep mines , and spoke exultantly of the rich harvest they would surely glean", "context_len": 27}}}, "harvest": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 20, "context": "Eating and drinking were over , and the old gleeman , striking his harp , was beginning a song of harvest home , when in rushed the two young theows who had gone out with Alfgar and Bertric , with the startling intelligence that there was a band of Northmen lurking in the woods , who had seized their young lords , and were , they thought , bent on attacking the place", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 54, "context": "Archie hung up a Japanese lantern in the doorway , and fixed a Union-Jack on one side , and the star-spangled banner of the United States on the other , and Peggy found enough Michaelmas daisies and white asters to put a wreath all round the railing of the veranda , which rather suggested harvest decorations , but looked very festive all the same", "context_len": 64}}}, "glean": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Many of the young men were going to the placer diggings or the deep mines , and spoke exultantly of the rich harvest they would surely glean", "context_len": 27}}}, "quartz": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "This was rather a pleasant amusement , and she soon had a nice little pile of those which were either milky white , which showed some faint color , or which shone with spots of mica or quartz", "context_len": 38}}}, "purchase": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 47, "context": "When the latter , in obedience to the order , came in from the garden , the officer said in Italian , It having come to the ears of the sultan my master that the merchant Ben Ibyn has ventured , contrary to the law , to purchase a Christian slave brought secretly into the town , he has declared the slave to be forfeited and I am commanded to take him at once to the slaves ' quarter", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 51, "context": "Dawson and free the policeman from debt ! What happiness this would bring to those poor people ! What pleasure it would give little Maggie and Bessie ! But how could she do it ? She had not the means at present , unless , indeed , she put off the purchase of the grove for a year or two , and took part of the sum she had so carefully laid by for that purpose , and if she did so , she might never have back the grove", "context_len": 90}}}, "unsettled": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` While the pole was in an unsettled condition , a part of their tribe moved on east , and got with the Creek Indians , but so soon as the majority of the tribe settled at the Old Fields , they sent for the party that had gone east , who answered that they were very tired , and would rest where they were awhile", "context_len": 66}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "He met with no squalls this autumn , for when the weather looked at all unsettled he could work with Ben in the woods , and fall down the large pines for him to hew , which he dearly loved to do ; and , as it took a long time to hew out a large stick of timber , he had ample time to cut them down and trim them out", "context_len": 72}}}, "provide": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 44, "context": "Could it be that she had made a mistake , after all , and that the neglected child had some powerful though eccentric friend in the background -- perhaps some previously unknown relation , who had suddenly traced her whereabouts , and chose to provide for her in this mysterious and fantastic way ? Relations were sometimes very odd -- particularly rich old bachelor uncles , who did not care for having children near them", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Would not Benjamin \u2019 s father provide the necessary backing ? It was very unlikely , Benjamin commented", "context_len": 18}}}, "obligations": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 41, "context": "I might track her easy , I suppose , but since she did n't seem to wish it to be known who she was or where she came from , I would n't feel it was showing my gratitude for the obligations she 's laid me under , and you see by the printing she do n't wish to be tracked even by her handwriting", "context_len": 65}}}, "treating": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 24, "context": "There ought to have been more verses , but at this point Father had unfortunately got hold of the paper , and persisted in treating the poem in such a comic light that the indignant authoress had never found the heart to finish it , though the fragment was considered very talented by Aunt Helen , and carefully put away in an old work-box , with the first specimens of Peggy 's handwriting and one of Bobby 's little baby-curls", "context_len": 80}}}, "presumption": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "repressing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "Among others , the presence of so many ladies of rank would have the excellent effect of discountenancing and repressing extravagances and dissolute habits , which are but too common , and are a shame to the Order", "context_len": 38}}}, "partners": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Oh , do n't say that , '' exclaimed John , cheerfully , `` you just come along with me and my partners , we are going straight to the placer diggings , and we 'll take care of you until you can do for yourself , which wo n't be long , you may be sure ; I should n't wonder if you 're as rich as Rothschild in a few years", "context_len": 74}}}, "strive": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 51, "context": "So long as I live I shall prize and value it beyond any honour I may gain , and as I feel it next to my heart , it will ever recall to me that you gave it me as a pledge of your esteem and trust , and I will strive to the utmost so to bear myself that I may be worthy of the gift", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "He held the man then firmly and swam on , feeling himself moment by moment grow more weary , for he was swimming in his clinging clothes , and unless help soon came he knew that he must loosen his grasp and strive to save his own life", "context_len": 48}}}, "nobler": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "Why should I thus urge life upon thee , -- why Seek to preserve thee in thine own despite ? O thou art worthy of a nobler cause ; I see in thee one who can nobly dare , Firmly resolve , and boldly execute ; -- And what a bright career before thee lies -- --", "context_len": 57}}}, "invaluable": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 69, "context": "There 'll be no time for whims and fancies , or even for lessons ; for there is to be an enormous tea-fight , as I call it , for the young folk of the parish in the schoolhouse this afternoon , and games afterwards , and recitations ; and if you , Rosamund , can recite as well as Lucy has described , why , you will be invaluable", "context_len": 70}}}, "wages": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 38, "context": "Nancy 's evenings for some time past had been absorbed in the construction of a bright heliotrope gown with gilt buttons , and she had walked into Warford on her day out , and spent a month 's wages upon a hat , which was such a marvellous erection of flowers and feathers combined with lace , chiffon , and ribbons , that it was calculated to leave her rivals , like the Queen of Sheba , with no more spirit in them", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Wages up there are about the highest thing in sight", "context_len": 10}}}, "inadvertently": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "The man advanced , picked the torn mackerel off the heap , where he had inadvertently thrown it , and the work went on , till as the master raised his eyes to where Penelly sat , he saw how pale and strange he looked", "context_len": 45}}}, "dismissing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 64, "context": "Dear old mammy , who nursed every one of us , from Ruthven down to myself , and whom mother gave to Margaret as a treasure past all price when Harry was born , -- poor mammy , who considers herself quite as much one of the family as any Stanton , Duncan , or Bradford among us all , -- to talk of dismissing her ! But nothing less would satisfy Aunt Patty ; and Margaret gently claiming the right to correct her own children and govern her own household as she saw fit , and Henry firmly upholding his wife , Aunt Patty departed that very afternoon in a tremendous passion , and has never entered the house since", "context_len": 121}}}, "final": {"freq": 83, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 72, "context": "It is true that Alexander 's soldiers , about to leave their homes to go to another quarter of the globe , and into scenes of danger and death from which it was very improbable that many of them would ever return , had no other celestial protection to look up to than the spirits of ancient heroes , who , they imagined , had , somehow or other , found their final home in a sort of heaven among the summits of the mountains , where they reigned , in some sense , over human affairs ; but this , small as it seems to us , was a great deal to them", "context_len": 114}}}, "separation": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 59, "context": "In the morning Alaeddin arose and aroused his wife , whereupon her women came to her and dressed her and busked her and adorned her ; whilst he , on his part , donned the richest of raiment , [ 620 ] and both were like to fly for joy at their reunion with each other , after their separation , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour was especially glad , for that she looked to see her father that day", "context_len": 79}}}, "diverged": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Regret was expressed for the loss of Tasker and Keene , both of whom were highly esteemed by all their shipmates ; and then the conversation diverged to the topic of the audacious Virginia-Preciosa , which , protected by the very ingenious fraud of the double sets of papers and the double crews , was still merrily pursuing her way and bidding defiance to everybody", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "When their paths at last diverged , Hondura and Bomba clasped each other by the shoulders and vowed eternal friendship", "context_len": 20}}}, "dwellings": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 71, "context": "It is true that the walls rose directly from the surface of the water , and of course a considerable part of their elevation was required to bring them up to the level of the surface of the land ; and then , in addition to this , they had to be carried up the whole ordinary height of a city wall to afford the usual protection to the edifices and dwellings within", "context_len": 73}}}, "meadows": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 124, "context": "the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change of air , ordering him off immediately to the high meadows of the Alps", "context_len": 128}}}, "degraded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Too much puffed up in his own conceit , he certainly was ; but we must remember how ignorant he was , and even this was better than that he should feel himself the miserable , degraded outcast of a few weeks since , whose `` hand was against every man , and every man 's hand against him", "context_len": 59}}}, "employers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "Loring , for although she called herself a servant , and was paid as such , she did not feel degraded by it , for she knew she was earning an honest living , and was respected as a friend by her employers , while Guy was looked upon almost as a son", "context_len": 53}}}, "singer": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 34, "context": "I never in my life had heard such a sweet even flow of song from the mouth of any living being , and I had once belonged to the son of a famous concert singer and had in her home heard many beautiful voices -- why ! it was like the lovely upward gush of a fountain -- clear , pure and exquisite", "context_len": 63}}}, "indulgent": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "He had never , he asseverated , spoken or written to any one concerning this , save herself ; if he had done so it would have been to his indulgent uncle , Colonel Rush , to whom he would have applied", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Uncle Fritz , who had spent all his boyhood on a New England farm near Franconia , and taken many a trip on a White Mountain coach by the side of an indulgent driver , had early mastered the secret of competent four-in-hand driving , and was therefore first to take his seat on the driver 's almost perpendicular cushion", "context_len": 60}}}, "routine": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 51, "context": "Once or twice he thought of asking permission to leave the ship long enough to run over to Brooklyn , but new notions of discipline had been pounded into him by the events of the homeward voyage , and he decided to take his detention on board as part of the routine which made good sailors `` in steam", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "Bart went home , got his breakfast , told his mother a trunk had got lost and he might have to go down the road to look it up , returned to the express office , found Darry Haven and McCarthy on duty , gave them some routine directions , and left the place", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Affairs at the little express office had settled down to routine when , one morning , Darry Haven dropped into the place", "context_len": 22}}}, "revive": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 37, "context": "But as it happened , the warmth of the wrapping , the influence of the spirit that had been poured liberally down his throat , and the chafing , combined with his naturally strong animal power to revive him from the state of insensibility into which he had fallen , and long before they reached the granite pier of the little harbour his eyes had opened , and he was staring in a peculiarly puzzled way at Mark Penelly , who still knelt beside him in the double character of medical man and nurse", "context_len": 94}}}, "frolicking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 86, "context": "`` And it means no , and thank you , and if you please , and I love you , and everything else he wants to say , '' said Maggie , catching up her frisky pet in her arms and giving him a hug , which he returned by putting his cold nose in her face , after which he struggled to be put down again , for so glad was he to be free this pleasant morning that he wished to show it by frolicking about on his own four feet", "context_len": 93}}}, "plums": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 49, "context": "It was somewhat neglected , it is true , but perhaps it looked none the less picturesque for that , and certainly no one would be disposed to quarrel with the beautiful ripe strawberries and the sweet little yellow gooseberries with the hairy skins , or the big red plums that hung upon the old brick walls", "context_len": 57}}}, "chasm": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "They were dipped into the rain-water barrel by means of a rope and pulley , and they were cast from `` the terrible height into the awful chasm , '' which ordeal consisted merely in being pushed down a space of about three feet , upon some hay , but being blindfolded was supposed to make up for the difference", "context_len": 60}}}, "preliminary": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` My dears , '' she said , `` I wo n't keep you a minute , but I have just had a letter from Sir John Wallis , and he wishes me to say that he would like the girls who are to compete for the preliminary examination for the Scholarship to write their answers to the English History questions", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Fritz had already been used to a furious bombardment as a preliminary of a `` big stunt ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "fibs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` And let us have no more preliminary fibs , '' said George , `` you are the greatest fellow for them , you know , Guy", "context_len": 27}}}, "theories": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` That big fellow on the bough there tells me he is delighted ; that he has at last found one that can understand his language , for he has heard so many ridiculous theories advanced by men with picks on their shoulders and books in their hands , as to what gold is , and how it came on the ground , that he has nearly burst his throat in trying to make them understand the truth , and has then been accused of making a 'senseless chatter", "context_len": 89}}}, "conjectures": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` 'And all the time , ' says he , 'their chatter was far more senseless than mine , and so they would think if they had heard all of us laugh over their conjectures about a matter we knew all about , for birds have legends as well as men , and there 's none better remembered than that of the `` Enchanted Yellow Men", "context_len": 66}}}, "battled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "They were the most numerous and powerful people on all the earth , yet none could tell why they were feared , for they had never battled with their neighbors , or shown great courage in the chase", "context_len": 38}}}, "tawny": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "It had been the whines and barking of the fox that he had heard , and the groans had come when death followed the stroke of the sharp claws of the wildcat , for it was that savage and tawny beast that now glared at Frank -- a wildcat disturbed at its meal", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "To his horror , instead of seeing her stretched out dead , or writhing in the final struggle , the lad saw the big , tawny body bounding over the snow toward him", "context_len": 33}}}, "brethren": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 70, "context": "But they would not , and all those upon the mountains died , and their curses came down , and rested upon the rich and powerful who had refused to help them , and upon the day that the last of the wanderers perished a voice was heard in the tents of the yellow men , and it said , 'As ye refused to leave your lands to aid your brethren , ye shall rest in the ground till strangers shall bear thee hence , and as ye have refused to toil , or bless in your life time , ye shall do both after death", "context_len": 106}}}, "prospered": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 60, "context": "I am just a homely body , and can only do the rough homely work ; I did n't tell them that it was because I had a lady who had the face of an angel and the ways of a queen in the drawing-room , and a young lady , the princess , her daughter , that the boarding-house prospered", "context_len": 61}}}, "spear": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "They could follow the game over any ground , and spear the fish in any stream they chose , so that hunger never entered their wigwams ; and in course of time they became so puffed up with their good fortune that they called themselves 'gods , ' and the neighboring tribes bowed and worshipped them", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 65, "context": "Thanks be to Allah , the All Powerful , one of the men began , my lips are unsealed , and I can tell you the great news that our hour for escape from bondage is at hand ! We need not fear the warder there , he went on , as several eyes were turned apprehensively towards the guard , who , with his spear beside him , was leaning carelessly against the wall at the farther end , looking through the window into the courtyard ; he is with us", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 93, "context": "was being taken for granted , will you please tell me what is a tilt-yard ? and what it was dat de knignts would not tome to ? and what was dat little white ting of the Queen 's dat de King carried ? and impatiently as Marie-Celeste brooked the interruption , there was nothing for it but to wait while Her Majesty explained that the tilt-yard was a sort of riding-school for the knights , where they practised for the tournaments , and that the tournaments were occasions when the knights , spear in hand , came together to ride against each other , with a great many people looking on , and when the one who unseated all those who rode against him won the prize", "context_len": 128}}}, "barren": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 18, "context": "You may meet with vessels on your way south , and doubtless some of them cruise off the barren coast I speak of , to intercept traders to and from Egypt and Acre , and other Syrian ports ; for the trade carried on is considerable , and , although of the same religion , the Turks are disposed to view the Egyptians as enemies rather than as friends , and would have even less hesitation in plundering them than in robbing their own countrymen", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "If there is water , the most barren sands will clothe themselves with some species of vegetation , which , in its decay , will form a soil that will nourish more and more fully each succeeding generation of plants", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Here , however , the landscape was barren in the extreme", "context_len": 11}}}, "herb": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The herb medicines are administered to him by the \u00edsti pak\u0101 dsha ` l\u00e1ko or `` great leader , '' who , when speaking of him , says : pusidshedsh\u0113 yi sanatchumitch\u00e4 tch\u00e4-is , [ 123 ] `` I am passing him through the physicking process repeatedly , '' or : n\u00e1ki om\u00e1lga imaki ` l\u00e4 dsh\u00e4yi s\u00e1 ` lit \u00f3m\u00e4s , tch\u00ed , `` I am teaching him all the matters proper for him to think of", "context_len": 78}}}, "woes": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 104, "context": "None could look back , not even to behold If those beloved were nigh ; one thrilling thought Rapt all the multitude , -- Can HE be near ? Then cries of terror rose -- I scarcely heard ; And buildings shook and rocked , and crashing fell , -- I scarcely marked their fall ; the trembling ground Rose like the billowy sea , -- I scarcely felt The motion ; such intensity of hope -- Joy -- expectation -- flooded all my soul ; A tide of living light , o \u2019 erwhelming all The hopes and fears , the cares and woes of earth", "context_len": 107}}}, "arise": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 42, "context": "Now the princess , of the excess of her grief for her separation from her husband and the Sultan her father and of her sore distress at that which had betided her with the accursed Maugrabin enchanter , used every day to arise , at the first peep of dawn , [ 586 ] and sit weeping ; nay , she slept not anights and forswore meat and drink", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 79, "context": "`` When [ 409 ] the Sultan heard his daughter 's story , he was sore concerned and his eyes brimmed with tears ; then , sheathing the sword and coming up to her , he kissed her and said to her , `` O my daughter , why didst thou not tell me yesterday , so I might have warded off from thee the torment and affright which have befallen thee this night ? But no matter ; arise and put away from thee this thought , and to-night I will set over thee those who shall guard thee , so there shall not again befall thee that which befell yesternight", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "' So now , O my mother , arise , as I bade thee , and fetch me the China dish whereof I bespoke thee , that we may range of these jewels therein and see how they show", "context_len": 39}}}, "invaders": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was the custom of the invaders to burn all their resting places when they left them , and to slay all captives , save such as could be held to ransom , or a few whom they detained in slavery , till they died a worse death from want and ill usage", "context_len": 53}}}, "destitute": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` But they were very near the Great Spirit , and he heard all their woes , and he sent a messenger down to the yellow men bidding them arise , slay the invaders and restore the destitute to their homes again", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "Yelf , who has suffered so much from liquor , and is so destitute , bring it on to treat the mourners , and old Jonathan Smullen ( who is going as fast as he can in the same way as Yelf ) drink it , it kind of went against my feelings", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Herstan and all his family , who had returned in peace to their dwelling , have come to us homeless and destitute", "context_len": 22}}}, "curses": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 15, "context": "But they would not , and all those upon the mountains died , and their curses came down , and rested upon the rich and powerful who had refused to help them , and upon the day that the last of the wanderers perished a voice was heard in the tents of the yellow men , and it said , 'As ye refused to leave your lands to aid your brethren , ye shall rest in the ground till strangers shall bear thee hence , and as ye have refused to toil , or bless in your life time , ye shall do both after death", "context_len": 106}}}, "wanderers": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 69, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "perished": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 43, "context": "But they would not , and all those upon the mountains died , and their curses came down , and rested upon the rich and powerful who had refused to help them , and upon the day that the last of the wanderers perished a voice was heard in the tents of the yellow men , and it said , 'As ye refused to leave your lands to aid your brethren , ye shall rest in the ground till strangers shall bear thee hence , and as ye have refused to toil , or bless in your life time , ye shall do both after death", "context_len": 106}}}, "hence": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 83, "context": "But they would not , and all those upon the mountains died , and their curses came down , and rested upon the rich and powerful who had refused to help them , and upon the day that the last of the wanderers perished a voice was heard in the tents of the yellow men , and it said , 'As ye refused to leave your lands to aid your brethren , ye shall rest in the ground till strangers shall bear thee hence , and as ye have refused to toil , or bless in your life time , ye shall do both after death", "context_len": 106}, "UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 39, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 45, "context": "Our young readers must bear in mind that these canoes could only go before the wind , or a little quartering , and therefore could not , like a boat , be luffed sharp into the wind , and beat out clear of danger ; hence the boys preferred to take the sail in , and trust to their oars , with which they could , if they saw a breaker , pull away from it", "context_len": 76}}}, "miner": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 30, "context": "And there the yellow men , in their new forms , waited for thousands of years , and there many of them are waiting still for the pick of the miner to bring them forth into their new life , to curse the wicked and improvident , and to bless the poor and needy", "context_len": 54}}}, "curse": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "And there the yellow men , in their new forms , waited for thousands of years , and there many of them are waiting still for the pick of the miner to bring them forth into their new life , to curse the wicked and improvident , and to bless the poor and needy", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 65, "context": "So that was the father of Phyllis and Marjorie Norton , that vulgar man with the loud voice and the coarse red face , and those pretty frocks and handsome carriages were all paid for out of the ruin of poor people 's homes ; for the lower parts of Warford had a bad character , and the clergy waged ceaseless war against the terrible curse of drink", "context_len": 68}}}, "needy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "And there the yellow men , in their new forms , waited for thousands of years , and there many of them are waiting still for the pick of the miner to bring them forth into their new life , to curse the wicked and improvident , and to bless the poor and needy", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 96, "context": "'s service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut her ear to the cry of the needy , because to answer it would cost a sacrifice of her own wishes ? Should she bear the burdens of others only when they did not weigh heavily on herself ?", "context_len": 128}}}, "conceit": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 72, "context": "Accordingly , when she came up , he saw her and viewed her well , beyond his wish ; and from that time till now , O King of the Age , life hath not been pleasant to him [ 366 ] and he will e'en have me seek her of Thy Grace , [ 367 ] so thou mayst marry her with him , and I can not do away this conceit from his wit , for that the love of her hath gotten possession of his vitals , so that he saith to me , 'Know , O mother mine , that , except I attain my desire , assuredly I am a dead man", "context_len": 117}}}, "judge": {"freq": 96, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 19, "context": "Three times have you earned special credit ; upon the first occasion , the grand master -- no mean judge of conduct and character -- deemed you worthy of secular knighthood , an honour which has not , in my memory , been bestowed at Rhodes upon any young knight ; on the second , you were promoted to the command of a galley , though never before has such a command been given to any , save knights of long experience ; and now , for the third time , the councillors of one of the greatest of Italian cities are about to do you honour", "context_len": 107}, "VERB": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 21, "context": "Randolph glance down in my direction once or twice , and I am sure , although he was not able to judge of the difference , the fact of seeing me once more in my properly made black evening dress relieved his mind , for he looked quite contented , and turned in a cheerful manner to my mother , and when dinner was over , and we returned to the drawing-room , I was lucky enough to be able to escape Mrs", "context_len": 83}}}, "idleness": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 6, "context": "As for Alaeddin , he left idleness and the commerce of striplings and took to consorting with grown men ; [ 316 ] nay , he would go every day to the market of the merchants and sit with the great and the small of them and question of the ways and fashions of commerce and the prices of articles of merchandise [ 317 ] and otherwhat", "context_len": 67}}}, "oppose": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 57, "context": "It is very strikingly characteristic of the mingling of poetic sentiment and enthusiasm with calm and calculating business efficiency , which shone conspicuously so often in Alexander 's career , that when he arrived at Sestos , and found that the ships were there , and the army safe , and that there was no enemy to oppose his landing on the Asiatic shore , he left Parmenio to conduct the transportation of the troops across the water , while he himself went away in a single galley on an excursion of sentiment and romantic adventure", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "bye": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "At the top of the incline the rider waved a good bye to his new acquaintances , and the horse of his own accord struck into a gallop which speedily carried him out of sight around a sweeping curve in the trail", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "interpret": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "I do believe it is the one that told us the pretty story , -- and , listen , to what he is singing , 'Good bye ! ' why , I even can interpret that , 'Good boy ! good bye ! Guy Loring ! Guy , Good bye ! ' ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "typographic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "-- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- -- - Transcriber 's note : Obvious typographic errors have been corrected", "context_len": 35}}}, "meade": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "MEADE", "context_len": 1}}}, "chicago": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "CHICAGO :", "context_len": 2}}}, "rambling": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 56, "context": "In a moment she had forgotten how tired and dusty she was ; forgotten how far she had journeyed since the morning she said good-bye to Uncle Joe and old Benita and Don ; had forgotten everything but the garden lying , half in shade , half in sunshine , below , -- the big , rambling , old-fashioned garden , of which the one at home was a faint reproduction", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Your Honor , interrupted Lawyer Frances , what bearing can all this rambling , second-hand information have upon the case ? I think we are wasting valuable time", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Dolly was awake , but quite wild , and talked in a rambling way of silver cups and angels , of gardens and music , of the Ice Glen and the dark , dark night", "context_len": 35}}}, "aesthetic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "In parts at least it must have been quite a hundred years old , and even the modern portion was not built according to the ideas of the present day , for in 1870 people were not so aesthetic as they are now , and the lines of beauty and grace were not considered all essential to happiness", "context_len": 58}}}, "essential": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 46, "context": "The captain will then light the train of powder in the hold , throw the torch among the straw , and see that , if possible , the men fasten her to the Turk ; but if this can not be done , it is not essential , for in the confusion the enemy will not be able to get out of the way of the fire ship as it drives down against her", "context_len": 74}}}, "ornament": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 55, "context": "They had stories to tell him about the famous hanging gardens , which were artificially constructed in the most magnificent manner , on arches raised high in the air ; and about a vine made of gold , with all sorts of precious stones upon it instead of fruit , which was wrought as an ornament over the throne on which the King of Persia often gave audience ; of the splendid palaces and vast cities of the Persians ; and the banquets , and f\u00eates , and magnificent entertainments and celebrations which they used to have there", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "It was only a bundle of wool wrapped in red and white tissue paper , but the tissue paper was soon twisted into the form of little dishes , and was combined with the remaining flowers to ornament the candlestick which was to light the feast", "context_len": 46}}}, "artistic": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 109, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "designs": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 39, "context": "Assured as he was that the Indians of whom he had caught glimpses during the afternoon , and the reports of whose guns he had heard not far away , were drawing near the camp with the most sinister designs , he set out to checkmate them so far as it was possible", "context_len": 53}}}, "lawn": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 32, "context": "And such a garden as it proved ! with its fountains and statues and vases , and the orangery on one side , and on the other three sides a beautiful sloping lawn , ascending from the level of the garden to the gray stonewall at the outer edge of the terrace ; and to think that here they were actually walking about in this beautiful garden , instead of merely peering through the fretwork of the iron gate , as some other little children with envious eyes were doing that very moment", "context_len": 93}}}, "shrubbery": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 33, "context": "Whit Monday was a holiday at school , however , and Peggy and Bobby , having the day at home , took a sudden fit of industry , and started to weed the shrubbery with the noble intention of having it raked over and tidied by teatime , being put somewhat on their mettle by Father 's remarks on the subject of sustained labour , and his laughing incredulity when they assured him he would find it all neatly finished when he returned from Warford that evening", "context_len": 87}}}, "overhanging": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 91, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 63, "context": "She was not long in discovering the landmarks of which she was in search , and a few minutes later the canoe was threading its tortuous way among a tangled mass of mangrove roots toward the solid bank of the river , landing upon which , we drew our light craft bodily up out of the water , concealing her beneath a broad overhanging mass of foliage which hid her so effectually that I would defy anybody but ourselves to find her", "context_len": 82}}}, "acres": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 54, "context": "Then , taking a bow and quiver of arrows , together with a brace of spears , out of the canoe , and signing to me to do the same , Ama led the way through the dense growth bordering the river bank , until we reached an open grassy space of about twenty acres , sparsely dotted here and there with magnificent trees ; and here Ama signified that we were to camp for the day", "context_len": 77}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "That indeed belongs to me and the eighty acres adjoining , but it would be difficult to sell it , nor do I care to do so", "context_len": 27}}}, "clavering": {"freq": 96, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 85, "word_index": 0, "context": "Clavering before I leave and arrange that you are to stay with her until October ; then if you win the Scholarship your future is arranged ; you take your three years ' education , and then by hook or by crook , my darling , you come out to me to India , for by then , unless I am vastly mistaken , I shall have got into a hill station where it will be safe for you to stay with me", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 0, "context": "Clavering was so proud of her cherries and so afraid that the neighbors might be tempted to help themselves to the luscious fruit , that she kept the door locked between the cherry garden and the other , and only those girls who were very privileged were allowed to sit in it", "context_len": 52}}}, "feature": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}}}, "stimulus": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I quite agree with you , and to work for such a prize would be an immense stimulus ; but then , you know , the feast comes on so soon , and there are only three weeks in which to prepare", "context_len": 43}}}, "merest": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 7, "context": "But of course there was only the merest little suggestion of a sympathetic thought to spend on the poor , white lady , with Chris Hartley but just discovered , and after that one instant of transfixed surprise she sped toward him , both hands extended ; and over the gate that divides the first from the second cabin they indulged in the heartiest shaking of hands possible , while hats for the moment were expected to look out for themselves", "context_len": 81}}}, "tug": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 67, "context": "For Cherry Court School was old-fashioned according to our modern ideas , and one of its old-fashioned plans was to give holidays at the end of June instead of the end of July , so that the girls had the longest , finest days at home , and came back to work at the end of August refreshed and strengthened , and prepared for a good long tug at lessons of all sorts until Christmas", "context_len": 75}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` I tell 'ee I did n't let no net trail overboard , '' cried the man angrily , as he seized a long oar and began to tug at it , dropping it into the water every time with a heavy splash", "context_len": 43}}}, "sections": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 40, "context": "In my collection I have them varying from almost straight to a shape like that of the letter V , nearly straight , curved , plain , ornamented , some with strange carvings , and all varying according to different sections of the country and individual tribes , each having its own make or style , showing respectively rough crudeness or considerable finish , and being especially characteristic in the ends or points -- all of which a boomerang connoisseur will distinguish at once , and locate as to tribe and section", "context_len": 92}}}, "numbered": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 100, "context": "Alfred , her eldest son -- for Edmund was the offspring of an early amour of the king -- was on the other side of the bed , a well-made youth , combining in his features the haughty bearing of his Norman maternal ancestors with the English traits of his father ; but now his expression was one of distress and anxiety , which was yet more deeply shared by his younger brother , Edward , who even at this period manifested that strong sense of religious obligation and that early devotion which in later years caused him to be numbered amongst canonised saints", "context_len": 104}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` I think not ; I fear he is numbered amongst the dead ; I have over and over again inquired of Danish prisoners whether they knew aught of him ; they all said he had not been known in their ranks for years", "context_len": 44}}}, "resident": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Moreover , her husband objected to his having a resident tutor for various reasons , and they thought Rudolph would overtax his strength if he went into Boston every day", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "I am sorry to say that I hear my friend Vrados has been arrested ; but there can be no doubt about his loyalty , and he will assuredly be able to explain to the satisfaction of the council how this man became a resident at his house", "context_len": 48}}}, "instruct": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 33, "context": "I ask for no earthly remuneration ; my position in life renders me independent of any exertions of my own ; I pray but for God \u2019 s blessing upon my attempts to instruct His lambs in the things which concern their everlasting welfare ; and deeply gratified should I feel , were my little work to be classed among the numerous valuable publications which you have already given to the world", "context_len": 72}}}, "seventies": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "On these occasions the young girls had to act turn about as hostess , pouring out tea , receiving the visitors , seeing them out again , and entering into what was considered in the early seventies polite conversation", "context_len": 39}}}, "deportment": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 43, "context": "Davenport discouraged the acquaintance as much as possible , never feeling easy even for the life and limbs of her progeny when they were in the society of 'those terrible young Vaughans , ' and revenging herself by scathing remarks upon manners and deportment , which were extremely trying to the feelings of Aunt Helen , who naturally thought her children superior in every way to 'those mealy-faced little Davenports , who look as if they had not the strength or spirits to enjoy themselves , even if they were allowed to try", "context_len": 93}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "They had a special master to teach them deportment in all its different branches , and once a week they spent an evening in Mrs", "context_len": 25}}}, "code": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 48, "context": "So he thought , and as he came to the last words his voice rang out clear and full , and when he ceased the applause was great , and Kitty 's eyes shone , although she dared not meet anyone , for it was part of the code of honor amongst the three girls that the judges should not guess who had written each individual essay", "context_len": 67}}}, "signifies": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 47, "context": "These names were written down in 1559 , and do not show much affinity with Timucua ; but since they are the only remnants of the Calusa language , I present the full list : `` Tampa , Tomo , Tuchi , Sogo , No ( which signifies 'beloved village ' ) , Sinapa , Sinaesta , Metamapo , Sacaspada , Calaobe , Estame , Yagua , Guaya , Guevu , Muspa , Casitoa , Tatesta , Coyovea , Jutun , Tequemapo , Comachica , Quiseyove and two others in the vicinity", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "The term seman\u00f3le , or isti siman\u00f3le , signifies separatist or runaway , and as a tribal name points to the Indians who left the Creek , especially the Lower Creek settlements , for Florida , to live , hunt and fish there in entire independence", "context_len": 46}}}, "thorough": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 75, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}}}, "backbone": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "Already the Irishman had been led to respect his opponent , and , even though his backbone had weakened not a whit , he could not withstand the charge which swept him from his pins and spun him aside", "context_len": 39}}}, "numbering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "Such proceedings as these could not of course long exist without the fame of them reaching the ears of the king , and I had only given some three or four performances when I was summoned to entertain his Majesty and his household , which I did in the great square before the palace , my audience numbering quite two thousand ; Banda and his numerous family being seated in a huge semicircle -- of which I was the centre -- in front of the palace , while the rest of the audience filled the remaining portion of the square", "context_len": 100}}}, "luscious": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "She had put on a large blue apron , for cherries stain a good deal when they are as luscious as those in Cherry Court orchard , and quantities had to be picked , for it was the custom from time immemorial for each of the guests to take a basket of cherries away with them , and the baskets themselves -- long , low , broad , and ornamental -- were filled now first with cherry-leaves , and then with fruit , by the excited and happy girls", "context_len": 89}}}, "privileged": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "Carroll wrote you about ! called Marie-Celeste , for she and Harold had had no misgivings whatever about making their way into a room to which they had been granted privileged entrance ; and after a reconnoitring tour round its borders had naturally brought up at the portfolio , to which their attention had been specially directed in Mr", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 87, "context": "But oh , what a solemnising thought it is ! -- The likeness to Him , which we know will be apparent in another world , to begin in this ! The glass of our souls , so spotted and dusty , -- spotted with sin , and dusty with pettiness , -- to be cleansed and polished , so as to receive such an Image ! But you and I , love , have caught a glimpse of that Image in those whom we have been privileged to know ; have we not ? \u2019", "context_len": 96}}}, "discussing": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 10, "context": "Striding in among the group of boys who were still discussing the very question of the disappearance of the money which had been the main topic of interest ever since the loss was discovered , the bank-note in his hand , he advanced directly to Flagg , who was taking an active part in the conversation -- that is , he had been doing so within the last few moments , since he had returned after a short absence from the school-room , looking , as more than one of the boys observed , `` flushed and rather flurried", "context_len": 99}}}, "respectively": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "Their names were Mabel and Alice Cunningham , two handsome dark-eyed girls , aged respectively seventeen and fifteen ; Florence Aylmer , who was also fifteen and the romp of the school ; Mary Bateman , a stolid-looking girl of fourteen ; Bertha Kennedy , who had only lately been raised to the rank of the Upper school ; Edith King , a handsome , graceful girl , who competed with Mabel for the honors of the head of her class ; and Kitty Sharston , who had only lately come , and who had some Irish blood in her , and was very daring and very much inclined to break the rules", "context_len": 113}}}, "competed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 69, "context": "Their names were Mabel and Alice Cunningham , two handsome dark-eyed girls , aged respectively seventeen and fifteen ; Florence Aylmer , who was also fifteen and the romp of the school ; Mary Bateman , a stolid-looking girl of fourteen ; Bertha Kennedy , who had only lately been raised to the rank of the Upper school ; Edith King , a handsome , graceful girl , who competed with Mabel for the honors of the head of her class ; and Kitty Sharston , who had only lately come , and who had some Irish blood in her , and was very daring and very much inclined to break the rules", "context_len": 113}}}, "honors": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 74, "context": "Their names were Mabel and Alice Cunningham , two handsome dark-eyed girls , aged respectively seventeen and fifteen ; Florence Aylmer , who was also fifteen and the romp of the school ; Mary Bateman , a stolid-looking girl of fourteen ; Bertha Kennedy , who had only lately been raised to the rank of the Upper school ; Edith King , a handsome , graceful girl , who competed with Mabel for the honors of the head of her class ; and Kitty Sharston , who had only lately come , and who had some Irish blood in her , and was very daring and very much inclined to break the rules", "context_len": 113}}}, "hobbledehoy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "She was a hobbledehoy sort of girl , having outstripped her years , which were only thirteen , and was considered by some of her companions very plain and by others very fascinating", "context_len": 33}}}, "outstripped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "But for Bomba \u2019 s eyes , he might have exulted in the thought that he had outstripped the Indians in the race , that he had reached the hut in time to warn Casson", "context_len": 35}}}, "thirteen": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was clearly impossible to take thirteen prizes to Rhodes , for there would be but three or four knights to each , and were they to fall in with but one Moorish pirate , they might suffer great disaster , while , should they meet with a storm , they would fare badly indeed , as they could not depend upon the rescued Christians for the management of the sails and oars in heavy weather", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 48, "context": "Two days ' rowing took them to Bastia , where their approach created unbounded excitement until the banner of the Order was seen floating from the stern of the Santa Barbara , while smaller flags , that had been hastily manufactured , flew from the mastheads of the thirteen prizes", "context_len": 50}}}, "restrictions": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "Only one-half of the boys were implicated in that affair ; they had already been punished by the restrictions which had been placed upon them , and were to be further disgraced by the public reprimand which he intended to give them on the dismissal of the school ; and these culprits were probably dreading this or some other severe punishment which would be meted out to them by the report of their misconduct which would be sent home", "context_len": 79}}}, "petty": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 81, "context": "I will ask Celia about it , but what I do n't like is that you should start it in a spirit of trying to get the better of another girl , though I can see that it is the most natural thing in the world for you to feel as you do , and I can see that Clara has really brought it on herself , but I do want my dear little girls to be charitable and above the petty meanness that is actuating Clara", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of", "context_len": 128}}}, "pinafore": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 9, "context": "She put the dainty blue frock , the white pinafore , the little shoes and socks , into a box which was well hidden away under the bed ; then while Roy still slept she slipped out , and purchased at a pawnbroker 's for a shilling , a set of little garments such as her own child , were he alive , would wear", "context_len": 65}}}, "pinafores": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 52, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}}}, "handfuls": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 43, "context": "There was , first , a broiled fish , fresh from the river , with boiled yams ; then a few roast plantains -- not more than a dozen , I think ; then the roast rib of a cow ; a few handfuls of boiled rice ; and -- yes , I think that was all , except a bowl of jaro ' -- the latter being a kind of native beer", "context_len": 73}}}, "kitty": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Kitty now walked quickly to her desk , glanced at the questions , and wrote the answers in a good bold , firm hand", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "The commotion being over , with the exception of an occasional spit from the drawer , as if kitty were still conscious of the presence of her foe , Bessie walked up to Mrs", "context_len": 34}}}, "adopt": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 68, "context": "It was always the same , no matter what the hour of day or night that I might choose to start upon my expeditions ; no surprise was ever displayed at my eccentricity in the choice of times , but I simply could not contrive to elude notice ; and at length it was borne in upon me that if I wished to effect my escape I must adopt tactics of a totally different kind", "context_len": 75}}}, "oak": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 109, "context": "the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner , and the oak dresser , with its rows of blue willow-pattern plates ; and back again at last to where Mrs", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "Then there 's a room they call the Guard Room , where they have suits of ancient armor ; and there 's a great oak writing-table in it made from the wood of the old Arctic ship Resolute ; and it tells in an inscription on it how she was abandoned by the English , and how she was found by an American whaling-ship captain three years afterwards , who got her free from the ice", "context_len": 76}}}, "positively": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 67, "context": "Neither Peggy nor Bobby had ever seen a wild-beast show before , for those visits to the Zoo , which are the joy of little Londoners , do not fall to the share of country children , and Archie had quite a lively time keeping them out of harm 's way , for Peggy declared the leopard looked so exactly like Tabbyskins at home that she must positively try to stroke it , and was under the rope and up to the bars before Archie could seize her by her skirts and drag her back , while Bobby 's curiosity on the subject of jackals and hyenas nearly led to the ejection of the whole party from the tent", "context_len": 119}}}, "demure": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "A brawny , large , and handsome man was the father , on whose shoulder the little fellow was riding , while a demure , pale-faced sister of about ten , walked by the side of the two", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Excuse me , sir ; you mean Maggie Bradford , '' said Bessie , in her own quiet , demure little way , still unable to shake off her conviction that Maggie and no one but Maggie must be the winner , and believing that Mr", "context_len": 47}}}, "suppressing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "A further accession resulted from the arrival of the Y\u00e1massi , whom Governor Craven had driven into Georgia and into the arms of their enemies , the Spaniards of Florida , after suppressing the revolt of 1715 in which they had participated", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was impossible to suppress her ; she was past suppressing", "context_len": 11}}}, "ladylike": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 63, "context": "I will do all I can to make you happy , and I think it but fair to put all three of you on your guard , for please understand that the Scholarship is given , not only for scholarly attainments and correct deportment , but also for those lofty traits of character which are a greater possession to any woman than either ladylike manners or great accomplishments", "context_len": 68}}}, "dolly": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 0, "context": "Dolly had heard of God before , but not as the kind , loving Father , -- the merciful , gracious Saviour , -- who stands ready to receive all who will turn to Him , who comes after us when we go from Him , and who had now put out His pitying hand to draw to Himself this poor little stricken lamb who had wandered so far from his fold", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Do n't you yemember how Colonel Yush told us he had to sew on his buttons ? But I did not mean he made the dolly 's clothes , only maybe he gave us the dolls , and Mrs", "context_len": 40}}}, "cherub": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "She had a round cherub face and two great big blue eyes , and short hair , which she wore in a curly mop all over her head", "context_len": 28}}}, "everyone": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 71, "context": "But Marie-Celeste succeeded in getting through the first few hours without telling : the two hours with Harold on the train , a very trying half hour when she was all alone with her mother , and another trying half hour the next morning , when she was sitting in the breakfast-room with Dorothy ; and after that the worst was over , so many delightful things came along to claim everyone 's thought and attention", "context_len": 76}}}, "roguishly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Her blue eyes rolled roguishly round until they fixed themselves on Edith King 's face , then she looked again at Kitty as solemn as possible", "context_len": 26}}}, "guesses": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "He had prepared a simple , witty preface to actual business , telling many truths of people who had spent a few cents for what had turned out to be worth many dollars , and inviting a good many guesses by hinting what might be in the heap upon which all eyes were fixed intently", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` She never guesses that it all goes to the little Mummy , that it helps to cheer her life and to give her some of the comforts she needs , '' thought the anxious girl ; `` but why , why does not Mummy write ? ''", "context_len": 48}}}, "unfold": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "The small plot of grass behind the white palings was quite green now , and some of the buds on the hardier bushes were beginning to unfold their young leaves", "context_len": 30}}}, "unopened": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` If I acted as I ought , '' said Florence , `` I would take this box just as it stands unopened to Cherry Court School to-morrow", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The box was in the tiny sitting-room still unopened", "context_len": 9}}}, "shilling": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 25, "context": "I 'm not in the way to know much , sir -- preferring to be civil at any cost than to lose the probable extra shilling the young un is an Eton boy , and the older one studies up to Hoxford", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 46, "context": "She put the dainty blue frock , the white pinafore , the little shoes and socks , into a box which was well hidden away under the bed ; then while Roy still slept she slipped out , and purchased at a pawnbroker 's for a shilling , a set of little garments such as her own child , were he alive , would wear", "context_len": 65}}}, "gripped": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 17, "context": "But Alaeddin was watching him and waited till he had all to-drawn the dagger , when he gripped him by the hand and taking the knife from him , planted [ 669 ] it in his heart", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The two loosely gripped hands in front , for be it remembered the method described was the old fashioned way of wrestling , and is still popular in many parts of the country", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "GRIPPED", "context_len": 1}}}, "unfolded": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "Just then , the murmurs of applause having died away , Sir John took up the first of the envelopes , opened it , unfolded the sheet of paper which lay within , and commenced to read", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "When the packages were unfolded Miss Minchin 's countenance wore suddenly a singular expression", "context_len": 14}}}, "skim": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "To go there each day with Fanny , and see her churn , or skim the rich cream from the milk , or roll the beautiful yellow butter into dainty little pats for the table ; or , to have the butter spaddles put into their own hands , and help Fanny in this last piece of work ; or sometimes even to pack the butter down into the great stone pots , -- all these were pleasures which never lost their charm", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "How would it do fur us to ride ahead and fix it dat way ? Den we could come back and skim ober de kentry like a couple ob muskeeters", "context_len": 30}}}, "undecided": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Middleton , and shy Maggie , not caring to put forth in his presence any further disclaimer to the still undecided honors which her sister and friends seemed determined to put upon her head , smiled doubtfully", "context_len": 37}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "A few minutes ' search resulted in a delighted 'S'sh ! ' from Bobby , for on a little ledge of rock under an overhanging tussock of grass was the cosiest , cunningest nest in the world , and the yellowhammer herself sat on it , looking at them with her bright little eyes , half undecided whether to stay or to fly away in alarm", "context_len": 66}}}, "piteous": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 5, "context": "Albert Fanning gave me a piteous glance , but I could not reply to it just then , and I let them both go away , and felt myself a wretch for being so cold to them , and for their society so thoroughly", "context_len": 44}}}, "term": {"freq": 161, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 161, "word_index": 7, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "carolling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "She had a very sweet voice , and was carolling wild notes now high up in the air -- '' Begone , dull care ; you and I shall never agree", "context_len": 31}}}, "gratify": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 46, "context": "They had been condemned to die ; but , owing to a slight difference between the admiral and the governor of Jamaica , their fate was yet undecided , the former wishing to send them to England to grace Execution Dock , the latter desiring to gratify the inhabitants of Port Royal by stringing up the prisoners in front of the castle", "context_len": 62}}}, "tarpaulin": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "Here a thought occurred to me to spring from my hiding place and make a dash for freedom , but the weight of the tarpaulin , which was securely lashed down , prevented me ; so I was perforce obliged to remain , though firmly resolved to free myself at the first favourable opportunity", "context_len": 54}}}, "restraint": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 49, "context": "I am glad , continued Fred , that I took up with Uncle Isaac \u2019 s advice , and staid at home , for , had I gone to Salem , I should probably have found other companions as bad as Pete Clash , and being away from all restraint , been worse than ever , and perhaps have come to the gallows", "context_len": 63}}}, "horribly": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 26, "context": "'If you want to break your own neck , you sha n't break Bobby 's , at any rate ! Do n't you know what a horribly dangerous thing you have been doing ? And the idea of your walking along there with your boot-lace dangling down in that way ! You are really getting too old for these silly tricks ; one ca n't look after you like a baby", "context_len": 71}}}, "coaxed": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 74, "context": "`` She made you take back old Miss Mapes ' handkercher , and made me go and tell Miss Jones she was sorry for unhookin ' her clothes-line and lettin ' down the clothes in the dirt ; and , oh ! do you think , there 's the biggest kind of a squash down in Todd 's cornfield , and I was a goin ' to get it for him , and Dol coaxed me not", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Never was a birdie coaxed with more pretty names than was this one during the next ten minutes ; and he seemed to like them well , for , after answering with one or two more half-timid `` cheeps , '' he broke into a soft trill , which soon swelled into a clear , sweet song of joy", "context_len": 59}}}, "embrace": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 87, "context": "He felt better after this and went in ; but that night the excitement of the adventure and the effects of his immersion were sufficient to keep him awake hour after hour , while when he dropped off into an uneasy slumber it was for his mind to be haunted by dreams in which he was being dragged down into the depths of the sea by a strange monster that clung to his limbs and writhed about him , making him shudder as he felt the chilling embrace", "context_len": 88}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}}}, "restlessly": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 39, "context": "Yes , it had come , and it was a long letter closely written on foreign paper , and Kitty took a very long time reading it , so long that little Dolly slipped off her lap and wandered restlessly to the window and stood there gazing out into the court , and then back again into the softly-shaded room , with the slanting rays of the afternoon sun making bars of light across the oak", "context_len": 76}}}, "troubling": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "He said he would send you a telegram as soon as he had made arrangements , as there was no good troubling you before", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 77, "context": "Who can tell all that goes on in the mind of a little child , or what it understands of death ? In a vague way Peggy knew that her playfellow had 'gone to heaven , where mother is , ' but she did not think of that as any cause for sorrow , nor did she connect him for an instant with the place where she stood , but , with her nurse 's words still troubling her , she knelt down and searched among the white flowers that hid the bare earth beneath", "context_len": 95}}}, "fees": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 86, "context": "Her punishment was that she `` should return to the Gaol from whence she came , and there remain until Saturday next between the hours of Eleven and Twelve of the Clock in the forenoon , at which time she was to be brought to the public Whipping-post , and there receive Twenty Lashes with a Cat-of-Nine-Tails from the hands of the Common Beadle on her naked back till the same shall be bloody , and then return to the said Gaol and remain until her fees be paid ! ''", "context_len": 91}}}, "perceptible": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 22, "context": "In spite of the frequent use to which they are put they do not relieve the sentence of its heaviness to any perceptible extent ; for what we call incident clauses and also many co-ordinate principal sentences are uniformly expressed by groups of words , the verb of which stands in the -t or -n verbal , which nearest corresponds to our participle in -ing , or to having ( h", "context_len": 71}}}, "loathed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}}}, "dormitories": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "They were not , for instance , required to talk French in the dormitories , and they were always allowed , provided they got into bed within certain limits and dressed within certain limits , to have freedom when in their rooms", "context_len": 42}}}, "knocker": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "Miss Forster was a little elderly lady , with a neat , bird-like appearance , and a brisk , cheerful manner , who seemed to match the prim , square house with its green door and brass knocker , and white sun-blinds over the windows", "context_len": 45}}}, "renting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "The habit of the family at this time , while spending the main part of the year at Portland Place , was to go to some country place in the summer , for several weeks , sometimes renting a house where they could stay all together , sometimes breaking into smaller parties", "context_len": 52}}}, "unmarried": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "The sole vow necessary , so far as I can see , would be that knights should remain unmarried and disposable at all times for service until ten years after making their profession , and that afterwards they should ever be ready to obey the summons to arms , on occasions when the safety of Rhodes , or the invasion of any Christian country by the Moslems , rendered their services needful , when they would come out just as the knights of Richard the Lion Heart went out as crusaders", "context_len": 91}}}, "benevolent": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Indeed , that was but the germ in a thoughtful , benevolent mind , of principles that were to be widely extended in after years", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Captain John , in his Sunday black , loomed like a benevolent Viking", "context_len": 13}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Something was left to the housekeeper and one or two distant relatives , and all the rest went to benevolent institutions ; I was left out", "context_len": 26}}}, "bowing": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 18, "context": "One thing which puzzled Barty very much was that though the pirates were so polite that they kept bowing all the time they looked as fierce as ever , and when the Captain said such polite things , his voice was so rough and savage that it made you almost jump out of your skin when he began to speak", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Without a word , Nathan took the stick and went in , Ben bowing and scraping and saying , `` Thank you for a very good time , '' then receiving no reply , not even a grunt , he added , `` Not at all , the pleasure is entirely mine", "context_len": 52}}}, "extending": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 71, "context": "As Ama , taking her bow and arrows , tripped lightly away toward the forest , I proceeded to shin up the tree , and presently , after some labour , found myself among its topmost branches , which towered high above those of all the other trees in the neighbourhood , and -- it being by this time broad daylight -- obtained a magnificent view of the surrounding country , extending for , as I estimated , some forty miles toward the south and east , while toward the north and west the view was shut off by high hills , through which the river wound its way", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was just a quarter of seven when he reached a half-street extending along and facing the railroad tracks for a single square", "context_len": 23}}}, "estimable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` The same ; she is the daughter of Major Sharston , a very estimable man", "context_len": 16}}}, "baronet": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` We can manage it by means of a sort of preliminary canter , '' said the baronet , in a musing tone ; `` I am sure we can work the thing up", "context_len": 34}}}, "musing": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "It did Aunt Patty no harm , but a great deal of good , that long afternoon 's musing in the silent house , where no patter of children 's feet , nor any sound of young voices was heard ; for baby had gone to her grandmamma , so that even her soft coo and joyous crow were missing for some hours", "context_len": 63}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` When I was in Macedon , before setting out on this expedition , while I was revolving the subject in my mind , musing day after day on the means of conquering Asia , one night I had a remarkable dream", "context_len": 42}}}, "endurable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "Nothing made the dreadful things she had often to encounter in town seem more endurable than the sweet-peas , the roses , the green trees , the green grass , the fragrance and perfume of the country ; and when she saw her little niece -- for she was very fond of Lucy -- looking discontented and unhappy , Mrs", "context_len": 60}}}, "crook": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "She was the older daughter , a strong , healthy girl of seventeen , who never helped her mother about the work , who dressed in such cheap finery as she could obtain by hook or crook , who took music lessons on a rented melodeon paid for out of her mother \u2019 s hard earnings , who felt herself to be a lady unfortunately born out of her sphere , and who was unquestionably ashamed of her surviving parent and her brother and sister", "context_len": 85}}}, "prizes": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 86, "word_index": 68, "context": "On the return of the knight with the news that it would be at least a fortnight before Genoa could fit out any galleys , and fearing that the pirates might at any moment put to sea , we procured some small Sardinian craft , and fitted them as fire ships ; with the captives we had rescued , and some Sard fishermen , we manned the three prizes , distributing the knights between them , and at night launched the fire ships against the corsairs , whose ships were crowded together", "context_len": 92}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "In the case of a knight hospitaller it can only be the latter ; it is in his case merely a sign that he has so distinguished himself that some maiden feels a pride that her gift should be carried into battle by him , and , on his part , that he too is proud of the gift so bestowed by one whose goodwill he prizes", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The prize-giving is of real importance ; for we give prizes instead of money , as the Government gives", "context_len": 19}}}, "scholarships": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Girls were not accustomed to big prizes in those days , and scholarships were only in vogue in the few public schools which were then in existence", "context_len": 27}}}, "vogue": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "conferred": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 61, "context": "Instead of doing either of these things , Alexander decided on the very bold step of setting out himself , at the head of an army , to march into southern Greece , for the purpose of presenting in person , and , if necessary , of enforcing his claim to the same post of honor and power which had been conferred upon his father", "context_len": 65}}}, "compete": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 97, "context": "The only person who was not won over by Archie 's friendly ways and frank American manners was Joe , who gloomily prophesied broken necks and kindred evils as the result of the children 's association with 'the young master from furrin parts ' ; but I fear there was a good deal of jealousy in this , for poor Joe had been a hero to the children in his modest way , and it was hard to find himself suddenly supplanted in their affections , especially by a rival with whom it was quite impossible to compete", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "They both did compete , and perhaps Rosamund did not exactly put forth her full powers ; but , anyhow , in the end Irene won , and her delight was beyond bounds", "context_len": 33}}}, "qualify": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "There were three tests which were to qualify for the prize", "context_len": 11}}}, "unselfish": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 52, "context": "As we grew older , we not only more fully understood the exceptional boundlessness of old-fashioned hospitality and kindness which that house and household exemplified thoroughly , but we came to understand somewhat of the heart-source whence issued that truest manifestation , of everyday religion , which evidences itself in an absolutely unselfish consecration , -- consistent , unreserved , and essentially practical , -- for everyday wear , and not only under stimulating environments", "context_len": 75}}}, "neglected": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 18, "context": "They had just reached the side of a little incline when Peggy 's bootlace , which she had neglected to fasten all the morning , tripped her up , and over she went , rolling into a prickly gorse-bush , while Bobby , who was so close behind that he could not stop himself , fell over her , and collapsed into a boggy hollow , where he lay panting for breath until Peggy picked herself up and hauled him out", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 46, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}}}, "educational": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "It was to be held by the girl who was best in conduct , best in deportment , and best in her educational work , in the following October , and she was to hold it for three years , and what do you think the scholarship was ?", "context_len": 49}}}, "munificent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "I hoped that it would bring me something particularly nice ; and what should come to-day but your dear loving letter , and the first halves of your munificent contribution to our schools ! How very kind and liberal my Laura is ! I had been speaking to Mr", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "in the outer quadrangle , with all its panoply of robes of state and globe and sceptre , appears to look a little more chipper than ever and a trifle more conscious of the distinction of being the munificent founder of so glorious an institution", "context_len": 45}}}, "consist": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 58, "context": "I do not say that every one was not before disposed to obey you in all things , willingly and cheerfully ; but he put it so strongly to them that they had volunteered specially for service in this galley , knowing well who was to be its commander , and the circumstance that the crew was to consist solely of young knights , and had therefore specially pledged their honour so to act that the enterprise should be in all respects a successful one", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Ah ! '' remarked I , trying to look as though I knew all about it ; `` and of what did thy breakfast consist ? ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "accompanying": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` In the first place I am not at all certain that the king would give you leave ; and , even if he did , you would not be permitted to go alone ; and where would you find men willing to absent themselves from the Customs for the sake of accompanying you ? There is not a man in the town who would consent to do so", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 81, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}}}, "parchment": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 65, "context": "No wonder that the boy saw with his eyes and heard with his ears and modelled himself as nearly as he could on the ideals of his hero ; and when Colonel Harrison signed the warrant for the king 's execution , the boy was as convinced a regicide as any of the judges whose names were written beside that of Harrison on the fatal parchment", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The knight opened it , disclosing a lace handkerchief marked with a deep brown stain , to which was fastened a piece of parchment inscribed : `` Stained with y e blood of y e Martyr His M tie King Charles '' , the jewelled hilt of a sword , a ring , and several papers", "context_len": 56}}}, "thirdly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "In the first place , I am well treated here , and my position is not an unpleasant one ; secondly , the sum required for ransom is altogether preposterous ; thirdly , I am not without hopes that I may some day find other means of freeing myself without so great a sacrifice ; and lastly , that I have a reason which I can not mention , why , at present , I would not quit Tripoli , even were I free tomorrow", "context_len": 85}}}, "locket": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 45, "context": "The prize itself was to consist of a free education at Cherry Court School for the space of three years ; accompanying it was a certificate in parchment , which in itself was to be considered a very high honor ; and thirdly , a locket set with a beautiful ruby to represent a cherry , which was the badge of the school", "context_len": 63}}}, "ruby": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 50, "context": "The prize itself was to consist of a free education at Cherry Court School for the space of three years ; accompanying it was a certificate in parchment , which in itself was to be considered a very high honor ; and thirdly , a locket set with a beautiful ruby to represent a cherry , which was the badge of the school", "context_len": 63}}}, "winner": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 61, "context": "They were still friends as far as Kitty was concerned , for she was a generous-hearted girl , and although the winning of the prize meant everything almost in her life , did another girl take it from her fairly and honorably in open fight , she would resign it without a trace of ill-will or any sore feeling towards the winner", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "He stayed with the race like a little man , overhauled all the other ponies and passed the judges ' stand a two lengths winner", "context_len": 25}}}, "scroll": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Oh , was there anything so splendid ! A lovely , lovely gold locket with a ruby cherry on the right side and a wonderful inscription on the left side , and a parchment scroll , father , in which the full particulars of the great Scholarship were written down , and besides that , a purse of money", "context_len": 60}}}, "inform": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 50, "context": "Young Dewey shook hands all around , and then sat down on the bed , looking at Mark with a puzzled expression on his face , as much as to say , `` what on earth have I struck -- b'gee ? '' Mark saw his expression and undertook to inform him , making haste to start before the Parson could begin again on the relative merits of Boston and the rest of the civilized universe", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "k\u00ed ` l\u00e4s I know , ki ` l\u00eddsh\u00e4s I inform , apprize , i-uki ` lku\u00eddsh\u00e4s I explain myself", "context_len": 20}}}, "sward": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 73, "context": "He never let on that he suspected for a moment that he was not entirely alone , but , walking over to a tree stump , where , spread out on a newspaper , was the remains of a lunch , he acted delighted at the discovery , picked up a hunk of bread in one hand , a piece of cheese in the other , and , throwing himself on the green sward at full length , proceeded to munch the eatables , with every semblance of satisfaction", "context_len": 89}}}, "greenest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "In the very centre of the garden is a grass sward , the greenest grass you ever saw , father , and , oh , so smooth -- as smooth as velvet , and on this grass , lit with fairy lamps , the girls dance all kinds of stately , wonderful , old-fashioned dances , and the neighbors sit round and watch , and then at the end we all go into the house , into the great oak hall in the middle , and Mrs", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}}}, "feasts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "For instance , she began speedily to sit upon the floor as Natives do ; and at Indian gatherings or feasts she would not only sit as they sat , but would share their food", "context_len": 35}}}, "digressing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "But there , how I am digressing , father !", "context_len": 10}}}, "assembling": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Clavering gave out the programme for the day -- the usual sort , you know , the dancing on the lawn in the evening , and the crowds of spectators , and the assembling in the big hall for the prizes to be given out to all the lucky girls who had won them", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "The shabby room in Coleman Street , where the Fifth Monarchy men were in the habit of assembling , was crowded with men , and the first glance showed with what ominous intentions the congregation were assembled", "context_len": 37}}}, "rostrum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Clavering 's place on the little rostrum , as they call it , and he spoke in such a loud , penetrating , and yet beautiful voice , and he said that he , with Mrs", "context_len": 36}}}, "penetrating": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "Beneath the battery of wondering eyes turned upon him Sleuth bore himself proudly , for he felt that at last his hour had come the hour in which he would demonstrate to the confusion of those who had sneered at him that he really possessed the keen , penetrating , analytical mind of a great detective", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 45, "context": "We judge that Sally and her 'Bijah , who , though little , is `` sizable '' enough to meet her wants , will make the acquaintance of those who like a book that is not over-intellectualized nor yet lacking in soundness of heart and penetrating vision into human nature", "context_len": 50}}}, "scholarship": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "It was to be held by the girl who was best in conduct , best in deportment , and best in her educational work , in the following October , and she was to hold it for three years , and what do you think the scholarship was ?", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Lighton that every man on the team was A1 when it came to scholarship", "context_len": 14}}}, "devoid": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "Yet I scarcely knew precisely what it was that I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "The day was intensely hot , causing him to suffer terribly from thirst ; and one of his horsekeepers at length brought him some milk , -- a deed of mercy , which shows that one man at least was not devoid of gratitude", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "By his side , similarly dressed , stood his son , the hopeful Canute , the future King of England , then only in his twelfth year , but already showing himself a true cub of the old tiger in fierceness and valour , yet not devoid of nobler and gentler virtues , as he afterwards showed", "context_len": 57}}}, "florence": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 87, "word_index": 0, "context": "Florence 's change in circumstances were much commented upon by the other girls , and there is no doubt that in her neatly-fitting dress with her abundant pocket-money she did appear a more gracious and a more agreeable girl than she had done in the old days when her frock was shabby , her pinafore ugly , her pocket-money almost nil", "context_len": 61}}}, "reconstructing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The boy , in reconstructing the cabin , had made loopholes on all four sides through which the weapons could be discharged", "context_len": 22}}}, "discontented": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 31, "context": "Very few of us are heroes when it really comes to the point , and I grieve to say that she looked so glum , and was generally so grumpy and discontented over the arrangement , that unselfish Lilian , divining the cause , instantly proposed to give up her own part to Peggy , and train a little village girl for the second 'child ' instead", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "She lay still a moment , wondering what it was that had troubled her last night ; and just as she remembered about the baby , she heard a little discontented sound at her side", "context_len": 35}}}, "allows": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "If that Leeville constable had known as much as the law allows , he \u2019 d have given the lads a chance to tell their side of the story ; and for him to refuse to let them send such an important telegram was an outrage", "context_len": 46}}}, "agog": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "I am sorry that I have kept you waiting so long , but at present Genoa , and , indeed , all Europe , is agog at the news of the defeat of the Turks , and Italy especially sees clearly enough that , had Rhodes fallen , she would have been the next object of attack by Mahomet ; therefore the ladies would not hear of my leaving them until I had told them something at least of the events of the siege , and also how it came about that you were there to share in the defence", "context_len": 100}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Well , go on , pet , I am all agog to hear", "context_len": 14}}}, "cease": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 15, "context": "Peace ! Peace ! The whole wide world rejoiceth now , Let war and discord cease ; Christ reigneth from the manger , Away with strife and danger ; Our God , before whom angels bow , Each taught this lesson by his birth , Good will to men , and peace on earth", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 28, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` I must insist that , hereafter , when I , or any other member of the faculty approaches , all indiscriminate knocking or kicking of balls cease until we have passed on", "context_len": 33}}}, "depressed": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "How the engagement would end we knew not , but our spirits were greatly depressed with our misfortunes , and one and all , having seen that the courage and fortitude of our enemies had been unduly depreciated by our leaders , were far from sanguine as to the prospects of a victory of our hitherto redoubtable fleet", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "Aylmer the great , observed how depressed Florence was when she received letters from Dawlish , noted her feverish anxiety to deport herself well , to lead a life of excellent conduct , and , above all things , to struggle through the weighty themes which had to be mastered in order to win the great Scholarship", "context_len": 57}}}, "plainest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "-- I shall eat very little and be satisfied with the plainest food", "context_len": 13}}}, "patron": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "George 's protection , and from that time he became the patron saint of England , and that means , Albert ( for Albert looked the question he longed to ask ) , that England regarded him as the saint who would help her most and be her special guardian", "context_len": 50}}}, "stamped": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 19, "context": "But O Sir , how Miss Sophy trembled and turned as white as a lily , and Miss Ratty stamped and sent me to the village -- at that hour , your honour , company in the house -- the ground covered with frost -- I subject to the rheumatics -- and what for , d \u2019 ye think ? to get her twopenceworth of shoe-ribbon , your honour ; and when I brought it , would you believe it ? -- she roared out that it was too narrow and sent me back again", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Ink-lined to be fresh , '' added Dewey , `` as the stamped egg remarked when it was dated three days after it was laid", "context_len": 26}}}, "chaise": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "Just as she was in the act of doing so the chaise drew up at the front door , a tall soldierly man got out , he came into the porch , and just as he was about to ring the bell , his eyes met those of Florence", "context_len": 49}}}, "soldierly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Just as she was in the act of doing so the chaise drew up at the front door , a tall soldierly man got out , he came into the porch , and just as he was about to ring the bell , his eyes met those of Florence", "context_len": 49}}}, "lashes": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 12, "context": "She was fair , but her eyes were black , with dark lashes curling over them , for her grandmother was an Italian , you know ; and her head was put on her shoulders in a pretty sort of way , and she had a cunning , sweet look on her face that just made people love her", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "How beautiful her eyes were today -- a sort of gray-violet , and the bronze lashes almost curled", "context_len": 18}}}, "wistful": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 44, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}}}, "bloomed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "Harris 's , fell under the spell , and bloomed out in a most surprising and delightful manner , and by the time the first week was over felt as though he had known them all all his life , and , indeed , very much regretted that such was not in truth the case", "context_len": 55}}}, "thrived": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "They had for some reason , too , thrived rather better in their new home , and the two buds Lem had pointed out to Mrs", "context_len": 26}}}, "hedge": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 85, "context": "Joseph 's tree at Glastonbury , and that he was there on pilgrimage when Alfgar saw him -- saw him , mark you -- at the Danish camp on the borders of Sussex ; and I saw men , I wo n't mention names , who had more than once taken reward to slay the innocent , look as if they would go down on their knees to this holy thorn , which was n't a holy thorn at all , but plucked from some hedge hard at hand", "context_len": 89}}}, "mignonette": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 15, "context": "The tiny fences were pulled up , and scattered far and wide ; lady-slippers , mignonette , verbenas , and all the other simple flowers which had flourished so well , and given such pride and delight to the little gardeners , were rooted up and trampled into the earth ; and , worse than all , the beloved heliotrope and geranium were torn leaf from leaf and sprig from sprig , while their main stems had been twisted and bent , till no hope remained that even these could be revived", "context_len": 92}}}, "raptures": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "The Major made no remark about the garden , which in itself was somewhat curious , for strangers were always in raptures over this old-world garden , with its yew-trees cut in quaint shapes , and its high walls , and its flowers , which seemed , every one of them , to belong to the past", "context_len": 57}}}, "anyone": {"freq": 131, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 130, "word_index": 107, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Anyone would like to be wakened by a bird who was tame enough to sit on one 's wrist and sing", "context_len": 21}}}, "cheapest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "`` Although it means a great deal to me , a great deal more than anyone can guess , '' thought Florry to herself , `` for Aunt Susan is never very kind to the dear little mother , and she makes such a compliment of giving her that money term after term , and she insists on doing everything in the very cheapest way", "context_len": 65}}}, "holland": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}}}, "coarsest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "These shirts were of the coarsest check , and Hannah would obtain tenpence for each", "context_len": 15}}}, "serge": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 20, "context": "It is brown holland all during the summer , the coarsest brown holland , and it is the coarsest blue serge during the winter ; never , never anything else -- no style , no fashion , no pretty ribbons , not even a cherry ribbon for my hair , and so little pocket-money , oh ! so little -- only a penny a week", "context_len": 65}}}, "penny": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 62, "context": "It is brown holland all during the summer , the coarsest brown holland , and it is the coarsest blue serge during the winter ; never , never anything else -- no style , no fashion , no pretty ribbons , not even a cherry ribbon for my hair , and so little pocket-money , oh ! so little -- only a penny a week", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}}}, "linked": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` How are we to begin ? Shall we do penance like the old monks ? Do you know , Rosamund '' -- here Irene linked her thin , almost steel-like little hand inside Rosamund 's arm -- '' that I am a most voracious reader ? Father was a great collector of books , and when I am tired of frightening the servants , and terrifying Frosty , and annoying mother , I spend days at a time in his library swallowing down the contents of his books", "context_len": 89}}}, "reunion": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 52, "context": "In the morning Alaeddin arose and aroused his wife , whereupon her women came to her and dressed her and busked her and adorned her ; whilst he , on his part , donned the richest of raiment , [ 620 ] and both were like to fly for joy at their reunion with each other , after their separation , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour was especially glad , for that she looked to see her father that day", "context_len": 79}}}, "trenches": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 109, "context": "Then , too , it was an open secret that she was Sir John 's favorite ; it had been whispered by more than one visitor to another that it was on account of Kitty Sharston that this great fuss had been made , that the Scholarship had been opened to the competition of the school , that the girls were here , that they themselves were here -- it was all on account of this slim little girl with the big eyes and the sweet pathetic face ; and reminiscences of Sir John and Kitty 's father together side by side , shoulder to shoulder , in the trenches before Sebastopol arose in the memory of one or two visitors present", "context_len": 122}}}, "bygone": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was very still and peaceful in there , and she liked to sit and think how the Abbey must have looked in those strange , bygone days when the little steps had led to a dormitory , and the broken pillars had held up the roof of a church , whose tinkling bell had rung out at sunset , calling to prayer those old monks who slept so quietly in their forgotten graves", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "That such a cave existed the children knew well , for Joe had once been inside , and had told them wonderful stories of smugglers in bygone days , and of kegs of brandy , and bales of silk and lace , and boxes of tea and tobacco , which were still supposed to be hidden in its depths , only waiting for some enterprising spirit to discover their hiding-place and bring them to light again", "context_len": 76}}}, "inherit": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 55, "context": "At this the knight laid down a paper which I recognized as the one that had been read by him under such strange circumstances at Rake , some eight years before , and the light flashed across me -- the senseless jargon that he had read was part of a secret code whereby I should inherit my fortune", "context_len": 58}}}, "cheeky": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Oh , I have lots of faults ; I can be so cheeky when I like , and so naughty about rules , but somehow nothing , nothing ever frightens me , except the thought of going to Helen Dartmoor", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` It is rather cheeky of you to call me Irene ; but I do n't much mind", "context_len": 18}}}, "frightens": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Oh , I have lots of faults ; I can be so cheeky when I like , and so naughty about rules , but somehow nothing , nothing ever frightens me , except the thought of going to Helen Dartmoor", "context_len": 41}}}, "retain": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 88, "context": "At the council this morning it was urged that some signal mark of honour should be granted ; but I interposed , saying that you had already received exceptional promotion , and that it would not be for your good , or that of the Order , for so young a knight to be raised to an official position of a character usually held by seniors , and that I was perfectly sure you would prefer remaining in command of your galley to any promotion whatever that would retain you on the Island", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas must topis [ 92 ] retain , Must guard against sunstroke , to health such a bane ; And midst frogs and mosquitoes must patient remain , Yes , e \u2019 en when tormented , must smile through their pain ; And , with courage like that of the knights of Charlemagne , By Mission Miss Sahibas snakes should be slain", "context_len": 65}}}, "endangered": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "I like to see the students indulge in sports , but when it comes to such rough play that the life of one of my teachers is endangered , it is time to call a halt", "context_len": 36}}}, "seasoned": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` You need not be a scrap anxious , my love , '' he added ; `` the place would not suit a young thing like you , but a seasoned old subject like myself is safe enough", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "We had heard that he was in the neighborhood of Chattanooga , giving his men and horses a much-needed rest ; but on the way news came to us that , in spite of his brilliant achievements in the field , he had been deprived of the choicest regiments of his brigade -- men whom he had trained and seasoned to war", "context_len": 62}}}, "untold": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "The degree of dialectic difference points approximately to the date of the separation of the respective communities , and untold centuries must have elapsed since the two main branches of the family were torn asunder , for Cha'hta differs about as much from Creek as the literary German does from Icelandic", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "I longed most passionately to see him , and so I was torn from hour to hour and from minute to minute with longings and doubts and despairs ; but all through everything , I kept my secret untold within my breast", "context_len": 42}}}, "possibility": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 22, "context": "Some years before the end , when in severe illness she thought herself to be passing away , she spoke of the possibility that her long correspondence about Batala might be so employed , and earnestly hoped that , if it were so , no one-sided account should be given , but that shadow as well as sunshine , the dark as well as the bright aspect , should be frankly presented", "context_len": 72}}}, "cleverer": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I am afraid , father , dear , there are other girls much cleverer than your Kitty , who know a vast lot more , and who are very full of zeal", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But he did a cleverer thing even than this", "context_len": 9}}}, "vastly": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 29, "context": "Marie-Celeste straightened up a little , as though to show she did not quite approve , and then she replied , with an air of childish dignity that was vastly amusing , Because it was his family motto that helped Leonard ( he 's the boy in the story I spoke about ) ever so much , and that taught him to be cheerful and contented , and it seems to me -- this last very slowly and thoughtfully -- that you are very much like Leonard , only grown up", "context_len": 91}}}, "favoritism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Of course , you must compete fairly with the other girls , Kitty , '' said her father ; `` if I thought there was any special favoritism in this , well -- '' His bronzed cheeks flushed , an indignant light fired his eyes", "context_len": 46}}}, "honorably": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I will win the Scholarship honorably and in open fight", "context_len": 10}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "They were still friends as far as Kitty was concerned , for she was a generous-hearted girl , and although the winning of the prize meant everything almost in her life , did another girl take it from her fairly and honorably in open fight , she would resign it without a trace of ill-will or any sore feeling towards the winner", "context_len": 62}}}, "vow": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` How ! you must be beside yourself to come to me -- me ! -- Rupert ! on such an errand ! Because , forsooth , I exchanged civilities with one I held an honourable enemy , you dare to expect my interest on behalf of a regicide ! I vow , sir , every man who even witnessed that most abominable and unnatural murder should swing , did it depend on me", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 2, "context": "The sole vow necessary , so far as I can see , would be that knights should remain unmarried and disposable at all times for service until ten years after making their profession , and that afterwards they should ever be ready to obey the summons to arms , on occasions when the safety of Rhodes , or the invasion of any Christian country by the Moslems , rendered their services needful , when they would come out just as the knights of Richard the Lion Heart went out as crusaders", "context_len": 91}}}, "adverse": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "After that glance , one may feel less surprised to hear of failures among young and untrained converts , the whole pull of whose previous lives has been utterly adverse to Christianity ; not to speak of the baneful effects of a surrounding heathen atmosphere , always present after conversion", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "In early times its tribe was the most important among the Lower Creeks , adverse to warfare , a `` peace or white town , '' and called by the people T\u00e1lua ` l\u00e1ko , Great Town", "context_len": 37}}}, "deport": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Aylmer the great , observed how depressed Florence was when she received letters from Dawlish , noted her feverish anxiety to deport herself well , to lead a life of excellent conduct , and , above all things , to struggle through the weighty themes which had to be mastered in order to win the great Scholarship", "context_len": 57}}}, "momentous": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 57, "context": "You might not care much for it , but to me it would be a delight to follow our friends on Ted 's break as they rolled merrily out from town on the bright Monday morning succeeding their two days ' stay at Oxford , and to keep with them all the way ; not that anything momentous or wildly exciting happened on the trip , only that if it were possible to put all its charm onto paper , there is no question but you would enjoy it", "context_len": 89}}}, "possesses": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "Each girl will please pack a small bag , if she possesses such a useful commodity , and we must walk as fast as ever we can to the station , for my poor dear husband has no end of things for me to attend to to-day , and the moment we get to Dartford we shall have to bustle about , I can tell you", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Creek possesses all sounds of the general Maskoki alphabet ; but here and in Hitchiti the gutturals g , k , are often pronounced with the tongue resting upon the fore or alveolar part of the palate", "context_len": 37}}}, "clasp": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was a hurried clasp in the strongest , bravest arms in all the world , a brief kiss on her cheek , a look in her father 's eyes which was enough to stimulate the highest in any girl 's heart , and then the parting was over", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "There was less drag upon him here from the stream ; his breath came more freely , and with it returning strength , sufficient to enable him to climb right out of the water , lie face downwards upon the rock , and , stretching down his hands , clasp the wrists of his companion , whose fingers seemed to have grown into the tough weed to which they clung", "context_len": 70}}}, "unhealthy": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 34, "context": "For the Major knew that , strong as he was , he was going to a part of India where brave men as strong as he are stricken down year after year by the unhealthy climate , and three years even at the best was a long time to part with a girl like Kitty , particularly when she was the only child he had , the light of his eyes , the darling of his heart", "context_len": 77}}}, "zephyrs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "One after another they peeped out and saw the sun on the grass and heard the birds sing and felt the soft zephyrs of the summer breeze blowing on their cheeks", "context_len": 31}}}, "stimulating": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Clavering had a wonderful way with her pupils , a very stimulating way , and she so arranged her prizes and her certificates that no girl who had really worked , who had really taken pains , was excluded from distinction", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 73, "context": "As we grew older , we not only more fully understood the exceptional boundlessness of old-fashioned hospitality and kindness which that house and household exemplified thoroughly , but we came to understand somewhat of the heart-source whence issued that truest manifestation , of everyday religion , which evidences itself in an absolutely unselfish consecration , -- consistent , unreserved , and essentially practical , -- for everyday wear , and not only under stimulating environments", "context_len": 75}}}, "certificates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Clavering had a wonderful way with her pupils , a very stimulating way , and she so arranged her prizes and her certificates that no girl who had really worked , who had really taken pains , was excluded from distinction", "context_len": 41}}}, "excluded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 86, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "tackled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "Kitty Sharston was too new a scholar to expect to get any reward in the ordinary sense of this term , but , all the same , she had worked fairly well , and during the last three weeks had tackled her studies and regulated her conduct like a veritable little Trojan", "context_len": 52}}}, "regulated": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was weary , anxious work ; for of course our movements were being regulated by a theory that , for aught we knew to the contrary , might be all wrong ; and as day succeeded day without bringing the expected sail within our ken there were not wanting among us those who denounced the skipper 's plan as foolish , and argued that the proper thing would have been to go direct to the Gaboon , and look there for the Virginia", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "Frank was inclined to blame himself , and , after that , Fenn , because the latter had put the soap powder into the cocoanut box , but the amateur cook 's chums were good-natured over his failure , and comforted him with the proverb `` accidents will happen in the best of regulated camps", "context_len": 55}}}, "stead": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 86, "context": "`` When [ 592 ] she had made an end of her story , Alaeddin said to her , `` Tell me , what does this accursed one purpose with thee ; what saith he to thee and of what doth he bespeak thee and what is his will of thee ? '' `` Every day , '' answered the princess , `` he cometh to me once and no more and seeketh to draw me to his love , willing me take him in thy stead and forget and renounce thee ; nay , he told me that my father the Sultan had cut off thy head", "context_len": 108}}}, "punctuality": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "They had been grievously led away by Mahomet , whom Gervaise regarded as a false teacher ; but as he had seen innumerable instances of the fidelity of the Moslems to their creed , and the punctuality and devotion with which the slaves said their daily prayers , exposed though they were to the scorn and even the anger of their taskmasters , he had quite lost , during his nine months of constant association with Suleiman Ali , the bigoted hatred of Mohammedanism so universal at the time", "context_len": 89}}}, "testimony": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 86, "context": "Nicholas , you saved the life of the grand master ; and in the order of the day he issued on the morning we left , granting you three months ' leave for the recovery of your wounds , he took the opportunity of recording , in the name of the council and himself , their admiration for the services rendered by you during the siege , and his own gratitude for saving his life when he lay helpless and surrounded by the Moslems -- a testimony of which any knight of Christendom might well feel proud", "context_len": 97}}}, "offering": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 37, "context": "Alexander replied , that if he wished to marry his daughter he could do it without his consent ; as to the ransom , he was not in want of money ; in respect to Darius 's offering to give him up all west of the Euphrates , it was absurd for a man to speak of giving what was no longer his own ; that he had crossed too many seas in his military expeditions , since he left Macedon , to feel any concern about the rivers that he might find in his way ; and that he should continue to pursue Darius wherever he might retreat in search of safety and protection , and he had no fear but that he should find and conquer", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 16, "context": "And suppose I gain admission to the presence , what shall I take by way of offering to the Sultan 's highness ? It [ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm", "context_len": 128}}}, "resign": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 83, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "I need hardly say that I shall be glad indeed to have the company and aid of three such well known knights of the Order , and would , could I do so , gladly resign my command into your experienced hands", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I don \u2019 t need any of your new-fangled notions ; when my eyes go back on me , I \u2019 ll resign and hike over the divide", "context_len": 28}}}, "fanned": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "Her bowsprit pointed straight at Captain Bracewell , and fanned by the breath of the guests as she passed them , the Sea Witch glided without swerving from her course to the mossy bank in front of the captain 's plate", "context_len": 41}}}, "injudicious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Aylmer was not a woman of lofty principle nor of strong courage , and some of the jealous thoughts in Florence 's heart had been fanned into flame by her mother 's injudicious words", "context_len": 34}}}, "dormitory": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 16, "context": "Seizing the first opportunity of slipping away from his schoolmates , he rushed upstairs to the dormitory with the design of throwing the note under Seabrooke 's bed or bureau , where it might be supposed to have fallen ; but seeing the trunk standing there ready packed , the impulse had taken him to put it in that , and without reflecting -- perhaps hardly caring -- that this would place Seabrooke in a still more embarrassing position , he thrust the note within , as Charlie Henderson saw , and fled from the room", "context_len": 96}}}, "cubicle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Each girl had her little cubicle or division curtained off from her fellows , where she could sleep and where she could retire , if necessary , into a sort of semi-solitude", "context_len": 32}}}, "division": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 10, "context": "Imagine for an instant the drudgery of working a long division sum with leaden type and raised , figures ; think of all the difficulty of placing the figures , and the chances of doing the sum wrong ; and then it will not cause surprise that the blind girl could never enjoy arithmetic , although in mental calculation she showed herself later on to be very clever", "context_len": 68}}}, "chatted": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 49, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}}}, "limits": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 62, "context": "I might tell him that my employer -- and I would mention some merchant whose name would carry weight with him -- has received from the interior a large consignment of slaves , among whom are three or four girls , who would fetch high prices in Egypt , and as he believes they have been captured from a tribe within the limits of the sultan 's territory , he is anxious to get rid of them , and will either dispose of them all cheaply in a lot , or will hand them over to him to take to Egypt to sell , giving him a large commission for carrying them there and disposing of them", "context_len": 117}}}, "healthier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "They never dreamt of abusing these privileges , and better , healthier , brighter girls could not be found in the length and breadth of England", "context_len": 26}}}, "examinations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Well , it is going to be a splendid day , '' said Alice , `` and , you know , there are no lessons of any sort ; all the examinations are over and the results will be known to-night ; the day is to be a long and happy one -- no lessons , nothing to do except to wander about and please ourselves ; pack our trunks , of course , which will be truly a delightful occupation", "context_len": 82}}}, "studying": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 44, "context": "Yes , he was outwardly a model character : he worked early and late ; he saved money ; he was never in debt ; he defrauded no man ; his evenings were spent either in attending lectures of working men like himself or studying the subjects he loved at home ; he never drank ; he never swore ; he was looked up to , and brought forward as an example to follow for many a poor drunken wretch", "context_len": 80}}}, "exam": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` But , never mind , '' she muttered now softly under her breath , `` I shall spend most of the hours in studying ; there is so much to get through before the Scholarship exam", "context_len": 37}}}, "odious": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "Once she was safe out of the neighborhood of those odious girls , as she was pleased to call them , she thought all would be easy enough", "context_len": 28}}}, "plight": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 25, "context": "But how comes she to be alone in this ruined house , like an enchanted damosel of a fairy tale ? 'T is a strange plight for such a tenderly-nurtured maid , for old Sir Gyles guarded her as the very apple of his eye ! And what state did not he keep , and Hunstanton Hall ! And with what a retinue did he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for", "context_len": 128}}}, "corded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "He ascended the narrow , uncarpeted stairs to his small , uncarpeted room over the kitchen , where a loose board rattled beneath his feet , and the dull light from a single window showed him the old-fashioned , low-posted , corded bedstead with its straw tick , coarse sheets and patchwork quilt pushed back beneath the sloping rafters of the roof", "context_len": 62}}}, "flushing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "There , said the widow , when Ben finished eating and sat back , flushing as he realized he had left not a morsel before him , now I know y \u2019 u feel better", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "And I thought -- '' flushing daintily , `` that maybe Jane might marry , and you would want some one in her place and if it was -- me , '' rather tremulously -- '' I could come -- I love you so", "context_len": 44}}}, "cottony": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "Mademoiselle Le Brun is going into Hilchester immediately after breakfast , and I am going to ask her to get me the best she can , but , of course , she will get nothing worth having for sixpence -- a yard and a half at the most of some horrid cottony stuff which will look perfectly dreadful", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "If it were not for the chance of being one of the lucky three in the Scholarship competition I would n't appear on the scenes at all , I vow I would not , with that horrid bit of cottony cherry-colored ribbon -- yes , I vow I would n't", "context_len": 50}}}, "shabbily": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "It is very hard on a girl to be dressed as shabbily as I am , and to be twitted by her companions for what she can not help ; and although you kept me at Cherry Court School , there have been times over and over when I hated you , Aunt Susan , and but for my dear little Mummy I would have left the school and earned my bread as a dressmaker or a servant", "context_len": 78}}}, "partner": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 5, "context": "Jack Borden , the junior partner in a fairly successful law firm , his wife an averagely nice , sensible body , Miss Florence , her husband 's sister , a bright girl of three and twenty , whose lover was in South America on a five years ' contract , with one year yet to serve", "context_len": 57}}}, "minuet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` You know we are to open the proceedings by dancing the old-fashioned minuet , '' continued Mary Bateman ; `` on the lawn , of course , with the colored lamps lighting us up", "context_len": 35}}}, "comical": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 12, "context": "They were in the very midst of one of Archie 's most comical adventures , when a shout was heard underneath the tree , and going out on to the veranda , they beheld Nancy struggling timorously up the staircase , her evident anxiety to make some communication overcoming her natural abhorrence of such an airy structure", "context_len": 57}}}, "proceedings": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 1, "context": "Such proceedings as these could not of course long exist without the fame of them reaching the ears of the king , and I had only given some three or four performances when I was summoned to entertain his Majesty and his household , which I did in the great square before the palace , my audience numbering quite two thousand ; Banda and his numerous family being seated in a huge semicircle -- of which I was the centre -- in front of the palace , while the rest of the audience filled the remaining portion of the square", "context_len": 100}}}, "mademoiselle": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` One moment , mademoiselle ; I wo n't keep you any time , '' she called out to the governess , who nodded back to her with a pleased smile on her face , for Kitty was a universal favorite", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "If there is any change from the half-crown you can let me have it back , can you not , mademoiselle ? ''", "context_len": 23}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "universal": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 100, "context": "She did not talk nearly so much as in former times of her son ; perhaps he had told her that I was not to be worried , but she was very good and very nice , and I got sincerely attached to her : and I never saw the Duchess nor Lady Thesiger nor my old friends , although I heard that the Duchess was fairly well again , and was out and going into society ; and every one now seemed certain that Jim Randolph had gone to the bottom in the Star of Hope , but by universal consent the boarders decided that the news should be kept from mother , and mother grew much better", "context_len": 119}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then he covered them with river mud , the universal and healing ointment of the jungle", "context_len": 16}}}, "extracted": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "It chanced one day that the Maugrabin 's brother was minded to know how it was with his brother ; so he fetched his sand-board and smote it and extracted its figures ; then he considered them and examining them throughly , found his brother in the house of the tomb ; [ 628 ] whereat he mourned and was certified that he was indeed dead", "context_len": 66}}}, "prescribed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "Then the young girl rushed upstairs to her dormitory , unlocked her little private drawer , took out her sealskin purse , extracted one of the new half-crowns , and was down again by the little governess cart , whispering eagerly to Mademoiselle Le Brun , within the prescribed time", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "And the voluble but kind-hearted woman led the way to the dormitory of the older boys , where all was quiet and in order , and installed her patient on Percy Neville 's bed , covered him , gave him the medicine prescribed for his relief , and having made him as comfortable as circumstances would permit , left him to the coveted rest and quiet in the half-darkened room", "context_len": 70}}}, "daresay": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 18, "context": "I even found , when once our eyes met ( his grave and dark , mine , I daresay , bold enough and determined enough ) , that my heart beat fast , and the colour flew into my face ; then , strange to say , the colour came into his face , dying his swarthy cheek just for a moment , but leaving it the next paler than ever", "context_len": 71}}}, "satiny": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Why , Florry , '' she exclaimed , `` she has got you three yards , and it is absolutely beautiful , satiny and smooth , and not a scrap of cotton in the ribbon , and such a sweet color", "context_len": 42}}}, "ornamental": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 69, "context": "She had put on a large blue apron , for cherries stain a good deal when they are as luscious as those in Cherry Court orchard , and quantities had to be picked , for it was the custom from time immemorial for each of the guests to take a basket of cherries away with them , and the baskets themselves -- long , low , broad , and ornamental -- were filled now first with cherry-leaves , and then with fruit , by the excited and happy girls", "context_len": 89}}}, "acquiring": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 21, "context": "Your knowledge may prove of much assistance to the Order , and believe me , the time you have spent in acquiring it may prove of much greater advantage to you in your career than if you had occupied it in performing even the most valiant deeds , and that at some future time it will ensure your appointment to a responsible office here", "context_len": 64}}}, "racking": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "The terrible rheumatic pains were better , it is true , and she could now be moved without causing her so much agony ; but she had a racking cough and much fever , and showed , in many other ways , how very sick she was", "context_len": 47}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But he went home racking his brains to see if something could not be done toward keeping her", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The children knew nothing of this , however , and if mother 's face was sadder than usual , they thought it was the old racking pain in her bones", "context_len": 30}}}, "complied": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Ben was obliging , and , finding he could give pleasure , he generally complied with the request of the miners and rehearsed such songs as he knew , so that he was considered a decided acquisition by the little company , and his popularity was unbounded", "context_len": 47}}}, "distressed": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` But Cousin Alexander had seen the melon in its glory , before it was taken from the vine ; and , in spite of its present distressed appearance , Fred found a handsome six-bladed knife placed beside the fragments , -- '' A blade for each piece , and the handle thrown in , '' said pleased Fred ; adding , that he thought Cousin Alexander wanted an excuse for giving presents", "context_len": 73}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 78, "context": "`` Beware , woman ! '' shrieked Maggie , and Bessie , thinking it time for her to come to her sister 's aid , joined in the cry , `` Beware , woman ! '' while Franky , always ready to take part in any disturbance , struck at Aunt Patty with his whip , and shouted , `` 'Ware , woman ! '' and Hafed , knowing nothing but that this old lady had alarmed and distressed his young charge , and that it was his duty to protect them , raised his voice in a whoop of defiance , and snatching up the hearth-brush , brandished it in a threatening manner as he danced wildly about her", "context_len": 120}}}, "worthies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 54, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "chapters": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 3, "context": "This book contains chapters on Introductions , Invitations , Cards and Calling , Dress , Correspondence and Reading , Conversation , Dinners , Balls , Table Etiquette , Receptions , Luncheons , Suppers , Engagements , Weddings , The Young Wife , etc", "context_len": 43}}}, "immensity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The immensity of the sum intrusted to his charge perturbed him a trifle", "context_len": 13}}}, "twopence": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "You are very nice to look at , and you have no nonsense about you , and it 's my belief that you never cared twopence about that high-falutin ' young man , and that now he has gone , you 'll just know where your bread is buttered", "context_len": 49}}}, "envelopes": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 35, "context": "He has sent over the questions in this envelope , and you can all read them , and you are to write your answers in advance , and fold them up and put them into envelopes for him to open and read to-night", "context_len": 43}}}, "comprehensive": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "His observations , however , were so exceedingly cursory and so impartially comprehensive , including the path at his feet every whit as carefully as the sky above him , that Marie-Celeste had difficulty in preserving proper decorum", "context_len": 38}}}, "faintest": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 58, "context": "Warden laughed again disagreeably , and , shutting the door on Davis , returned to his work ; but the little incident and the burning , angry words of the despairing man shook him unpleasantly , and his temper , never one of the best , was in such a ruffled condition , that it only wanted the faintest provocation to kindle it into a blaze", "context_len": 66}}}, "paced": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 13, "context": "In the evenings , long after the others were in bed , she paced up and down the kitchen , spinning Deacon Croaker 's wool into smooth , even threads , but her heart ached as she prayed for her boy , and often , when in the still watches of the night Polly kept her vigils with pain , she heard her cry softly : --", "context_len": 67}}}, "quickening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "As she did so she suddenly perceived with a quickening of her heart 's pulses that Kitty through an oversight had left the key in her desk ; all the other girls had locked their desks ; but Kitty , who was generally careful enough in this matter , had left the key in hers", "context_len": 55}}}, "pulses": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "As she did so she suddenly perceived with a quickening of her heart 's pulses that Kitty through an oversight had left the key in her desk ; all the other girls had locked their desks ; but Kitty , who was generally careful enough in this matter , had left the key in hers", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Bomba \u2019 s pulses beat fast", "context_len": 6}}}, "oversight": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "As she did so she suddenly perceived with a quickening of her heart 's pulses that Kitty through an oversight had left the key in her desk ; all the other girls had locked their desks ; but Kitty , who was generally careful enough in this matter , had left the key in hers", "context_len": 55}}}, "postboy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Just at that moment Florence , who had approached the window in her restless pacing up and down , saw the postboy enter the courtyard", "context_len": 25}}}, "contrive": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 56, "context": "Meanwhile I had practically abandoned my attempts to effect my escape , for the time being at least ; for the conviction had at length been forced upon me that neither Banda nor his people would ever willingly let me go , and that , therefore , before engaging in any further attempts , I must contrive to disarm suspicion completely , and create the impression that I had at length resigned myself to live out my life in this remote African town , and with savages only for my companions", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` I know 'd they 'd crowd us out'n house an ' home ef they got a chance ; I could ' a ' took oath to it ! Cuss 'em , an ' contrive 'em , both sides on 'em , all an ' similar ! They 'd as lief make a hoss-stable out'n the house as not , an ' I built it wi ' my two han 's", "context_len": 71}}}, "wasting": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 64, "context": "he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions , but she can not look on me as", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I saw that the galley would be up in five minutes , and had no fear of their wasting time to look for me", "context_len": 24}}}, "intolerable": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 37, "context": "Had my stepping down -- oh , had my stepping down led to this ? It was a bitter thought , and yet when I looked at mother , and felt that I had saved her from intolerable anguish and perhaps sudden death , I felt that it was worth while", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 67, "context": "Randolph to have come as he had done , to have forced himself upon us , to have invited us to go out with him , to have -- -- and then I stopped , and a great lump rose in my throat , and I burst into tears , for in my heart of hearts I knew well that I did not think what he did intolerable at all , that I respected him , and -- but I did not dare to allow my thoughts to go any further", "context_len": 91}}}, "prohibition": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "Benedict into Abingdon , Glastonbury , Ely , and other great houses , which , by its absolute prohibition of monastic idleness , and its wise regulations , caused the religious houses of that period to become the central points of civilisation and learning in the land", "context_len": 47}}}, "devotedly": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "The yell of the Moslems rose less frequently ; they sacrificed their lives as freely and devotedly as those who led the first onset had done ; but as the hours wore on , the assurance of victory died out , and a doubt as to whether it was possible to break through the line of their terrible foes gained ground", "context_len": 61}}}, "cheaply": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "How was it you managed to get it so cheaply ? ''", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 85, "context": "I might tell him that my employer -- and I would mention some merchant whose name would carry weight with him -- has received from the interior a large consignment of slaves , among whom are three or four girls , who would fetch high prices in Egypt , and as he believes they have been captured from a tribe within the limits of the sultan 's territory , he is anxious to get rid of them , and will either dispose of them all cheaply in a lot , or will hand them over to him to take to Egypt to sell , giving him a large commission for carrying them there and disposing of them", "context_len": 117}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "A hurried council of war was held on board the flagship , and here Albemarle made the first great mistake of his life ; for it is reported he held the Dutch so cheaply that he ordered Prince Rupert to take twenty vessels of our fleet and make to the westward to find and engage the French , while he relied on his remaining fifty-four ships to meet the formidable array of Dutchmen", "context_len": 73}}}, "midsummer": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 84, "context": "Then he turned from the window , put on his coat , which was lying in readiness beside him , strolled out from the club , called for a hansom , directing the driver to take him to the station , and never for one minute admitted to himself that he had risen a whole hour earlier in order to do this very thing , or that he was acting on any stronger impulse than that of a passing fancy , born of the midsummer day , and desire for a little variety", "context_len": 93}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Besides , Harry Allyn , who , as you know , is in dead earnest about his new leaf , is up at Oxford delving away , midsummer though it is , at some back work that was sadly neglected in the spring term , and has actual need to be made up", "context_len": 53}}}, "apace": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "News travels apace , and in less than an hour it was all over our village that Joyce had by some means obtained a file , cut through his fetters , and , after a murderous attack on his jailer , had broken out of Midhurst Jail , and was last seen making his way towards the bleak Sussex Downs", "context_len": 60}}}, "superintended": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "She suggested , among other things , a valance and curtains for the little brass bedstead , already purchased , and then went herself and selected a soft , white material and superintended their making", "context_len": 35}}}, "shiny": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "The farmer with the plug hat he-hawed at the top of his voice , the miserable owner of the eggs got mad at him , some words ensued , the farmer started after him , the egg owner ran , once outside fired an egg which struck the smooth , shiny tile with a splatter , and the farmer came back into the express office holding his nose , bareheaded , and looking for his rejected straw head-covering", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": ") And , Margaret , dey will get here in time for luncheon , and I would have a very good luncheon , Margaret , and everything all b'ight and shiny", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I did n't think the less of him , either , that I saw his eyes mighty shiny as he and the baby held fast to each other", "context_len": 28}}}, "becomingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Mabel and Alice Cunningham were in soft pink dresses , a little paler in shade than the cherry-colored ribbons which as a matter of course they would wear , and one and all of the girls of the Upper school were becomingly and suitably dressed , with the exception of poor Florence ; but Florence 's muslin dress was coarse in texture and badly made , and notwithstanding the soft cherry-colored ribbons , she did not look her best", "context_len": 79}}}, "suitably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Mabel and Alice Cunningham were in soft pink dresses , a little paler in shade than the cherry-colored ribbons which as a matter of course they would wear , and one and all of the girls of the Upper school were becomingly and suitably dressed , with the exception of poor Florence ; but Florence 's muslin dress was coarse in texture and badly made , and notwithstanding the soft cherry-colored ribbons , she did not look her best", "context_len": 79}}}, "texture": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 61, "context": "Mabel and Alice Cunningham were in soft pink dresses , a little paler in shade than the cherry-colored ribbons which as a matter of course they would wear , and one and all of the girls of the Upper school were becomingly and suitably dressed , with the exception of poor Florence ; but Florence 's muslin dress was coarse in texture and badly made , and notwithstanding the soft cherry-colored ribbons , she did not look her best", "context_len": 79}}}, "coaching": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I am so dreadfully sorry , '' she said ; `` I meant to give you such a coaching in the reign of Queen Elizabeth all this afternoon , Florry , but there , it ca n't be helped", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "I knew the Duncans had met with some losses this year , that their two eldest sons , for whom the bungalow was built , had gone abroad , and that maybe they would let Miss Newman have it in exchange for coaching Rudolph", "context_len": 44}}}, "dunce": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Oh , I know you are anything but a dunce , '' replied Kitty ; `` I do n't take you for one , I assure you , Florence , only I did hope that I might help you in English History , for that is my strong point", "context_len": 50}}}, "applauded": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 12, "context": "A violin solo followed from the village tailor , who was much applauded , most of the hearers being persuaded that not even the band in the Warford Public Gardens could surpass 'Bill Evans and his fiddle", "context_len": 37}}}, "strains": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 43, "context": "And then one or two of them recited , and one or two of them sang songs , and then there was a great chorus in which all the girls joined , and then they danced Sir Roger de Coverley to the merry strains of a string band , and presently the great occasion of all came when the girls , followed by the guests , entered the great central hall of Cherry Court , and the prizes were given away", "context_len": 81}}}, "central": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 69, "context": "And then one or two of them recited , and one or two of them sang songs , and then there was a great chorus in which all the girls joined , and then they danced Sir Roger de Coverley to the merry strains of a string band , and presently the great occasion of all came when the girls , followed by the guests , entered the great central hall of Cherry Court , and the prizes were given away", "context_len": 81}}}, "portfolio": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 110, "context": "Dodgson no longer cares to see as much of the little people as formerly ; in fact , he rather runs away from them when they seek him out ; and when he received Dorothy 's letter , what did he do but write her that he was very sorry to say that he would not be at home on the afternoon in question , but that if it would be any pleasure to her little friends to see his rooms , she might bring them there and welcome , and that he would leave some old photographs that he thought would interest them ready to her hand in a portfolio on the writing-table", "context_len": 114}}}, "sketches": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "Florence obtained two prizes , a beautiful edition of Scott 's poems , and also a little portfolio full of some pretty water-color drawings , for Florence had a great taste for art , and had managed to come out at the head of the school with her own water-color sketches", "context_len": 51}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Romans ( 1775 ) sketches the training of the Creek youths in the following words ( p", "context_len": 17}}}, "cable": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 45, "context": "The shrill notes of the bosn \u2019 s whistle had hardly died away when the rigging was alive with men ; the canvas was spread from the yards as if by magic , and all that remained was to break the anchor out , the cable already being hove short", "context_len": 50}}}, "expects": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 35, "context": "He has heard that young Atherton is very sharp and clever ; of course he will try to regain his rights by any means that may present themselves ; so I really believe that Papa expects him to appear some day or other through the key-hole", "context_len": 46}}}, "regards": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 47, "context": "Though I have often often done wrong since , and shed many many tears , I have never quite lost the light shed on me then , and now it brightens all the future , so that I can scarcely say that I have any care as regards myself -- the Lord will take care of me in advancing age -- in the last sickness -- in what is called death , ( it is only its shadow )", "context_len": 79}}}, "cablegram": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "And so when the hour was over and the brief examination was made , and the names of the successful competitors called out , and Kitty Sharston 's name appeared at the head of the list , she could only look at Sir John , and think of the cablegram , and not feel at all elated , although her companions clustered around her and shook her hand and wished her joy", "context_len": 72}}}, "resolving": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "She only knew that her heart was beating wildly , and she was thinking all the time of that cablegram which would comfort her father when he reached Malta , and resolving as surely girl never resolved before not to disappoint him , to give him if she could , if it were any way within her power , that supreme pleasure", "context_len": 62}}}, "supreme": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "She only knew that her heart was beating wildly , and she was thinking all the time of that cablegram which would comfort her father when he reached Malta , and resolving as surely girl never resolved before not to disappoint him , to give him if she could , if it were any way within her power , that supreme pleasure", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 93, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 42, "context": "As Philip had been made commander-in-chief of the Grecian armies which were about to undertake the conquest of Asia , and as , of course , his influence and power in all that related to that vast enterprise would be paramount and supreme ; and as all were ambitious to have a large share in the glory of that expedition , and to participate , as much as possible , in the power and in the renown which seemed to be at Philip 's disposal , all were , of course , very anxious to secure his favor", "context_len": 98}}}, "elated": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 56, "context": "And so when the hour was over and the brief examination was made , and the names of the successful competitors called out , and Kitty Sharston 's name appeared at the head of the list , she could only look at Sir John , and think of the cablegram , and not feel at all elated , although her companions clustered around her and shook her hand and wished her joy", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "Aylmer the less , as the mother of the heroine , minced about with her head in the air , so elated , so excited , so carried out of herself , that not the grandest county lady present had power to awe her", "context_len": 44}}}, "clustered": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 61, "context": "And so when the hour was over and the brief examination was made , and the names of the successful competitors called out , and Kitty Sharston 's name appeared at the head of the list , she could only look at Sir John , and think of the cablegram , and not feel at all elated , although her companions clustered around her and shook her hand and wished her joy", "context_len": 72}}}, "banish": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "Harold was indulging in this meditation as he stood waiting by the break for the rest of the party , for thinking comes very easy when one has nothing to do ; but wise are the folk , big or little , who , like Harold , resolve to banish uncomfortable thoughts from the mind when convinced that thinking is not in the least likely to better them", "context_len": 68}}}, "hating": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` For , although I mean to win the Scholarship -- yes , I shall win it ; I have made up my mind on that point -- I can not help more or less hating Kitty Sharston , and Mrs", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "It is an awful pity , said Ted , echoing Donald 's words , and hating his own record more than any one else could hate it ; but all that is left me is to try and mend matters", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` You must n't think of that young lady upstairs as hating you , '' said the woman , after she had brushed the hat and had asked me if I felt strong enough to walk a mile or more", "context_len": 40}}}, "stint": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "It will be Poverty Castle , my loves , and we 'll have to stint and scrape and contrive ; but at any rate we 'll be merry when we can be merry , and we 'll forget our troubles in doing good to others", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "There is no stint of food , and it is no worse than one would have , were one labouring in the fields", "context_len": 23}}}, "unwieldy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Alexander 's troops , being a comparatively small and compact body , and being accustomed to move with great promptness and celerity , could easily evade any attempt of such an unwieldy mass of forces to oppose his crossing at any particular point upon the stream", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "But , after a few blows had been exchanged , Dick 's rising temper supplied the strength that had been lessened by hardship , while Astbury , unwieldy and out of condition , soon lost his breath , and , hitting out wildly , gave Dick an opening for a good straight left-hander , that sent his opponent crashing on the ground", "context_len": 62}}}, "abundantly": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 5, "context": "It is , however , abundantly evident from the general context that this is the author 's intention in the passage in question , Alaeddin 's head being metaphorically in the hands of ( or pledged to ) the headsman , inasmuch as he had engaged to return and suffer decapitation in case he should not succeed in recovering the princess within forty days", "context_len": 64}}}, "dumpling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` That prim , dumpling sort of woman ? Oh , all right , go ; but do n't stay long with her", "context_len": 23}}}, "beady": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "Aylmer the less thought of the hearty tea she and Florence would make at home , the shrimps and the brown bread and butter , and the honey and the strong tea with a little cream to flavor it ; nevertheless , her beady black eyes were fixed on her sister-in-law now with a look which almost signified adoration", "context_len": 59}}}, "brusque": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was the same brusque query which the cashier put so many times a day that its use had become a habit", "context_len": 22}}}, "luggage": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 51, "context": "He just entered the drawing-room quickly one morning after breakfast , and shook hands with mother and shook hands with me , and said that he would be back again before either of us had missed him , and then went downstairs , and I watched behind the curtain as his luggage was put on the roof of the cab", "context_len": 60}}}, "porter": {"freq": 63, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 63, "word_index": 0, "context": "Porter to Lem ; `` not that you deserve it , if it was only on account of the apples , and I did mean to keep you here till to-morrow night at least ; but those dear little girls that you 've plagued so , have begged you off , and I could n't refuse them", "context_len": 57}}}, "shingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Aylmer , leaning upon Florence , who was head and shoulders taller than her parent , walked down the little shingly beach , and a moment afterwards entered the cottage door", "context_len": 31}}}, "sunburnt": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Oh , you look just as well as ever , sunburnt -- so much the better", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The other , Keeping , did not appear to be of the same debased kind as his companion , though his matted red hair and sunburnt face and arms betokened a villain whose existence had been of an out-door kind", "context_len": 40}}}, "docking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "That was the way I managed , just docking the butcher 's bill and the greengrocer 's bill", "context_len": 18}}}, "greengrocer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` I expected Pattens to send a man in , for I had met him once or twice , and he told me that his bill was not paid , and that he did not mean to supply any more meat , and what Pattens said the baker and greengrocer said too , and so did Allthorp the grocer , and so did the fishmonger , Merriman , and so did all the other tradespeople , and if one spoke to me , so did they all", "context_len": 87}}}, "lintel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` It is rather a small bed for two , '' she reflected , as she entered the room , stooping to get beneath the lintel of the door ; `` but never mind , it 's Mummy 's little room and Mummy 's bed , and I am happy , happy as the day is long", "context_len": 57}}}, "tidied": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 72, "context": "she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little creature whom every servant and every governess alike dreaded", "context_len": 128}}}, "muzzy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "I get muzzy in the head unless I sleep enough", "context_len": 10}}}, "muzziness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Do you ever suffer from muzziness in the head , dear ? ''", "context_len": 13}}}, "downside": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I have turned and re-turned my best dress -- I turned it upside down last year , and downside up this year , and back to front and front to back , and I am trimming it now with frills which I have cut another old skirt up to make , and I really can not do anything more with it", "context_len": 61}}}, "trimming": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "I have turned and re-turned my best dress -- I turned it upside down last year , and downside up this year , and back to front and front to back , and I am trimming it now with frills which I have cut another old skirt up to make , and I really can not do anything more with it", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "When he had dressed them , Muley went out and returned with some Turkish garments , consisting of a pair of baggy trousers of yellow cotton , a white shirt of the same material , and a sleeveless jacket of blue cloth embroidered with yellow trimming ; a pair of yellow slippers completed the costume", "context_len": 55}}}, "frills": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 58, "context": "The big drawing-room had been shut up for many years ; the Queen-Anne chairs and china-cabinets were swathed in wrappers , and the ornaments put away in boxes ; but sometimes the children would steal in and open the shutters to look at the portraits which hung upon the oak-panelled walls -- stately gentlemen with wigs and lace frills , whose eyes seemed to follow you about the room ; haughty dames with powdered hair and patches ; stiff little girls in hoops and mittens , and pretty young ladies attired as shepherdesses or classic goddesses , with cupids and nymphs in the background", "context_len": 104}}}, "stylish": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 46, "context": "Mamma used to dress me in white dresses with short sleeves that came just to my elbow , tied round with pink or blue ribbons , and a sash to match , tied on one side in front , and I knew it was pretty and stylish , and used to walk around with my head in the air , and people would laugh and say I was awfully cunning", "context_len": 70}}}, "sells": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "his edition of Alaeddin ; `` Rewwas ( for raa '' s ) signifies not only 'he who sells cooked heads , ' but also 'he who makes a business of cooking heads", "context_len": 33}}}, "buys": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "This is not a very good worm country as the soil is stony , so he buys worms from his sister 's pit and makes worm hash", "context_len": 27}}}, "wardrobes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "After rummaging a little further and discovering frocks and coats of unfamiliar cut hanging in the closets and wardrobes , and coming upon mouldy slippers , and queer-looking hats in other places , they concluded they must go", "context_len": 38}}}, "shocking": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 14, "context": "That is true enough , Gervaise admitted ; and to my mind it is shocking that four-fifths at least of the Order , pledged to oppose the infidels , should be occupied with the inglorious work of looking after the manors and estates of the society throughout Europe , while one-fifth , at most , are here performing the duties to which all are sworn", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "The death of Dymnus had been kept a secret , so that the appearance of his body was an unexpected as well as a shocking sight", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a shocking state of existence , nor did it grow better as the children got older", "context_len": 18}}}, "stingiest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Peter Grimm , notoriously the stingiest man in Pleasantville , who raised the sourest apples in the town and spent most of his time watching the boys and picking up what fruit rolled outside of the fence , bided his time with watchful ferret eyes until a promising-looking package came along", "context_len": 51}}}, "offended": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 78, "context": "Stanton understood it no better than she did , perhaps not so well ; but he was very much amused ; and as he thought these young nieces and nephews of his were very wonderful little beings , he told Aunt Patty many of their droll sayings and doings , making himself so agreeable and entertaining , that by the time his sister came in , the old lady had almost forgotten that she had cause to be offended , and was not only quite ready to meet Mrs", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "Hall laughed and offered Franky his stick to ride on , but the little boy would not take it ; and when he found he could not have the sugar-plums , walked away with an offended air , which amused the old gentleman very much , though it distressed his sisters , who thought him very impolite", "context_len": 57}}}, "crabs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Fish is cheap and plentiful in Dawlish , and we can get dear little crabs for fourpence apiece", "context_len": 18}}}, "fourpence": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then she told him the story of the bun shop , and the fourpence she picked up out of the sloppy mud , and the child who was hungrier than herself", "context_len": 31}}}, "fishwife": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` We will go down to the fishwife after breakfast , and get her to boil some for us in time for supper , '' said the mother ; `` and now , Florence , if you are quite disposed to listen , I may as well get over this bad business", "context_len": 52}}}, "allude": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 31, "context": "Horace was touched then , and her words took hold of him I could see , though he tried to seem impatient and vexed , and would not permit me to allude to them", "context_len": 34}}}, "aggravating": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "Lawrence , without intending it , was constantly fretting and aggravating those around her while Maggie and Bessie , who thought that all their mamma did or said was quite perfect , were amazed and indignant when they heard her rules and wishes questioned and found fault with , and sometimes even set aside by Aunt Patty , if she thought another way better", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "For the symptoms of the night before had developed in a most aggravating way", "context_len": 14}}}, "stocks": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 56, "context": "Beginning at the southeast , we first meet the historic Timucua family , the tribes of which are extinct at the present time ; and after describing the Indians of the Floridian Peninsula , southern extremity , we pass over to the Yuchi , on Savannah river , to the Naktche , Taensa and the other stocks once settled along and beyond the mighty Uk'hina , or `` water road '' of the Mississippi river", "context_len": 75}}}, "shares": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 52, "context": "How came such a misfortune to happen to them ? It will create consternation here when it is known , for although it was not a state enterprise , the sultan himself and almost all the rich Moors embarked money in the fitting out of the ships , and were to have shares in the spoil taken", "context_len": 57}}}, "dividends": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "declares": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "He is wounded sorely , Gervaise , and the leech declares that one at least of his wounds is mortal ; still , I can not bring myself to believe that so great a hero will be taken away in the moment of victory , after having done such marvels for the cause not only of the Order , but of all Christendom", "context_len": 63}}}, "lieu": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "He kept a fragment of pencil continually in the corner of his mouth , like a cigar , and Peggy noticed that when he accidentally mislaid his pocket-book he jotted down notes upon his cuffs , as if it were quite an ordinary occurrence to use them in lieu of paper", "context_len": 51}}}, "payment": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 35, "context": "I made the very best of him , and I went on still further , and told her of the man who had burst into mother 's presence in the drawing-room , and rudely demanded payment for his debt , and then how I had yielded , and told Albert Fanning that I would marry him , and how , after that , everything was smooth , and all the worries about money had disappeared as if by magic", "context_len": 79}}}, "moors": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "The sandals were anything but comfortable , and her feet hurt dreadfully on the stones , while every gorse-bush she passed seemed to be stretching out spiky fingers to scratch her bare legs ; she was tired after her morning 's adventures on the moors , and the eight miles seemed to lengthen out to an interminable vista , in spite of the way being downhill ; sundry bumps and bruises , which she had never noticed at the time , began to ache now , and the bee-stings on her neck smarted , until she hardly knew how to bear the pain", "context_len": 103}}}, "dreariest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It is up amongst the Yorkshire moors , in the dreariest spot , I make no doubt", "context_len": 17}}}, "removes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` No , no , dear , that 's just it ; and she says also that when she removes you from Cherry Court School she will allow me fifteen pounds a year more than I have at present , which will make my income of sixty-five pounds instead of fifty", "context_len": 51}}}, "pettish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` I should judge from the sort of clothes I have now , '' said Florence , giving her foot a pettish kick against the obnoxious blue serge , `` I should judge they did not cost five pounds a year", "context_len": 41}}}, "obnoxious": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 115, "context": "she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little creature whom every servant and every governess alike dreaded", "context_len": 128}}}, "depends": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 5, "context": "The success of our expedition depends upon you rather than upon me , and as I feel assured of your warm cooperation I have no fear as to what the result will be , if Dame Fortune will but favour us by throwing in our way some of those scourges of the sea in search of whom we are about to set out", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` The number sacrificed depends , of course , upon how many can be found , '' answered Ama ; `` but generally there are at least three hundred ; this time it is hoped that there may be many more", "context_len": 41}}}, "wiping": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` And to think of him , '' she said , wiping the tears of joy from her eyes , -- '' to think of him asking for his old mammy 'most before he had done with his greetings to the gentlefolks ! And him putting his arm about me and giving me a kiss as hearty as he used when he was a boy ; and him been all over the world seein ' all sorts of sights and doin 's", "context_len": 82}}}, "workhouse": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "Aylmer , wiping the moisture from her brow as she spoke , `` that we are the greatest worry to her , both of us , and that she does not care a pin for either of us , but that she does not want to have it said that her husband 's people are in the workhouse , and that is why she is doing what she is doing", "context_len": 70}}}, "halfpenny": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` Oh , Mummy , '' said Florence , `` can you bear her ? When you tell me those sort of things I just long to throw her gifts in her face and to say boldly , 'We wo n't take another halfpenny from you , we will go to the workhouse to spite you , we 'll tell every one we can that we are connected with you", "context_len": 70}}}, "vex": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 6, "context": "But she did not wish to vex their kind uncle , who was a very learned man , and was always thinking of collections and museums , and science and experiments ; and she did not like to tell him that she would rather her little ones should learn about butterflies from the book with its beautiful pictures , and from watching them flying about , and settling on the flowers in the fields ; and that she did not think it could be a nice play for children to catch and kill the pretty harmless creatures", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Ay , madam , 't is easier to keep silence than to testify ; and I would not willingly vex you , but I desire that you should know me in my true colours", "context_len": 35}}}, "jiffy": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 25, "context": "I screamed out , The fire is coming right down on Ben \u2019 s house , and he \u2019 ll be burnt out in a jiffy ! \u2019 We were almost abreast of the harbor , and , hauling the sheets aft , shot her right on to the beach", "context_len": 50}}}, "matchmaker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I enjoy taking handsome girls about , and I am a right good matchmaker", "context_len": 14}}}, "unturned": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "For he would leave no stone unturned , he said , emphatically , to unravel the mystery ; and if nothing came to light before to-night , he should at once place the matter in competent hands for its solution", "context_len": 40}}}, "heir": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 14, "context": "As he had no family , there was , of course , no direct heir to the crown , and , in case of any misfortune happening by which his life should be lost , Macedon would become at once the prey of contending factions , which would immediately arise , each presenting its own candidate for the vacant throne", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He was heir to all the instincts , traditions and ambitions of his race", "context_len": 14}}}, "properties": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 98, "context": "`` And when , '' [ quoth she ] ; `` I saw this [ seeming ] lack of wit in him , I fell to laughing at him , unknowing his perfidy and his intent ; so I took an old lamp that was in my husband 's pavilion and sent it by the eunuch , who exchanged it with him for a new lamp ; and next day , O my father , at daybreak , we found ourselves in Africa , with the palace and all that was therein ; and I knew not the properties of the lamp which I had exchanged , till my husband Alaeddin came to us and contrived against the Maugrabin a device whereby he delivered us from him", "context_len": 127}}}, "chit": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Oh , I am to talk sense , am I ? What a little chit it is ! but it is a dear little thing in its way , although saucy", "context_len": 32}}}, "tacit": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "Belden to do aught but look and listen , and drop a word of assent now and then , when Marie-Celeste saw fit to impart her information in a somewhat interrogative form ; and in this way they went on from monument to monument , giving of course but a passing glance to many and stopping longest , by tacit agreement , at those which had some special charm or attraction for Marie-Celeste", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` With this tacit rebuke she left him , and thereafter the doctor respected her wishes", "context_len": 16}}}, "alluded": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 29, "context": "But the characteristics of race and language , which are the most important for ethnology , because they are the most ancient in their origin , are not often alluded to by them , and when the modern sciences of anthropology and ethnology had been established on solid principles many of these southern races had already disappeared or intermingled , and scientific inquiry came too late for their investigation", "context_len": 69}}}, "plead": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "Berlin looked a trifle startled as the captain of the eleven appeared , but into Hayden \u2019 s eyes there sprang a light of satisfaction ; for he fancied Eliot had come to temporize , possibly to plead", "context_len": 38}}}, "repression": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Although the boy still spoke with a sort of grim calmness , Roger fancied he detected in his forced repression the cry of a desperate , despairing heart", "context_len": 28}}}, "sentiments": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 110, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "extravagance": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "Put away from thee this extravagance : [ 326 ] who shall undertake an affair like this and demand it of the Sultan ? Meknoweth not how thou wilt do to make this request of the Sultan , and if thou speak sooth , [ 327 ] by whom wilt thou make it ? '' `` O my mother , '' rejoined Alaeddin , `` by whom [ should I make ] a request like this , when thou art at hand , and whom have I trustier [ 328 ] than thyself ? Wherefore my intent is that thou shalt make this request for me", "context_len": 106}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "My companion , as I soon found , was given to extravagance , and frequently indulged in drink , so that he found it hard work to make both ends meet", "context_len": 31}}}, "stouter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "She 's grown stouter than ever , she promises to be a very large woman in her old age ; and what a pompous way she does walk ! I do declare -- well , that beats everything -- she is walking to the hotel , not even taking a carriage", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The first three craft we captured offered a much stouter resistance , and we lost two of our number ; but in the other affair no knight was killed , or even seriously wounded , and believe me , Countess , I feel absolutely ashamed at the fuss that is made over it", "context_len": 53}}}, "pompous": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "The conductor , a pompous , self-contained old fellow , started towards Bart to haul him over the coals , but Bart wisely walked farther down the platform , the conductor gave the go-ahead signal and shook his fist sternly at Bart , while the latter with a gay , relieved laugh waved him back a cheery , courteous good-by", "context_len": 60}}}, "greeting": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 2, "context": "The boy greeting him with a flash of white teeth , made no formality over admitting an entire stranger , but at once introduced him into a little book-closet on the ground floor , where a gentleman was busily engaged in unpacking folios from a great sea chest ; and as he turned to receive the visitor , Dick , to his inexpressible relief , saw a face that had been familiar to him in Scotland", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 11, "context": "To his surprise some of the crew gave him a friendly greeting ; he was unable to understand a manner so at variance with their hatred to the Christians , until one of them said to him in a mixture of Greek and Italian , We have heard from our countrymen who were in the boat with you , that they received much kindness at your hands , and that of all the Christians they had served under , you were the kindest master", "context_len": 84}}}, "disturb": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 80, "context": "The Middletons had a fine collection of orchids and rare plants , which were much admired by the young visitors , though simple Peggy could not help thinking they were not half so pretty as the roses and lilies in the old garden at home , and certainly the grand Scotch gardener was not nearly so nice as David or Joe , for he seemed quite to resent their presence and followed them about grimly , for fear they should disturb anything , or pluck any of the fruit or flowers", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Sit down , my warriors , disturb not the feast", "context_len": 11}}}, "punctual": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Now then , darling , let us have our own dinner , because we must be punctual ; it would never do to keep Susan waiting", "context_len": 26}}}, "behooves": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "I have this afternoon had a meeting of your knights , and have urged upon them very strongly that , having volunteered to serve under you , they must obey your orders as promptly and willingly as if you were the senior knight of the Order , and that it behooves them specially upon the present occasion , when the crew is composed entirely of young knights , to show themselves worthy of the honour that has been done to them by entrusting a galley of the Order to their charge", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "But all this , as you can plainly see , has nothing whatever to do with the title of this chapter , so it behooves us , as the preachers used to say , to turn our back on Harold and the charms of the renowned old college , and our faces toward the ocean and a far-off land -- far off , that is , as far as Windsor and the English are concerned , but very near and dear to the hearts of some of the rest of us", "context_len": 91}}}, "waiters": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 73, "context": "A grassy glade under a tall beech seemed intended by Nature for a summer-house , for there was a large flat stone in the middle , which served for a table , and a circle of little stones round , just high enough for seats , so that you might imagine Queen Mab and her fairies dined here on moonlight nights , with the squirrels for guests and the bats and owls for waiters", "context_len": 74}}}, "corridor": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 33, "context": "Be that as it may , a very sweet-faced lady met them at the door as they entered , and saying reassuringly , Come this way , children , led them through a corridor resplendent with statues and portraits , and thence by a wide folding-door into a large room , with windows looking out over the Long Walk and away to the grand old Windsor Forest", "context_len": 67}}}, "waiter": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 13, "context": "I reckon , neighbor , and he cocked up his eye at the waiter , that I \u2019 ll trouble you for a seltzer lemonade , mixed with a pickled cherry and the cross-section of a ripe orange", "context_len": 38}}}, "confronting": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 47, "context": "And what more natural , when you find yourself at the foot of a flight of steps , than to walk up them , no matter if the place does chance to be Windsor Castle ; and then if at the top you find an open door confronting you , what more natural than to walk in , particularly if there happens to be no one to say you nay , and you have half a mind , besides , to seek an audience of the Queen , and report the ungracious conduct of an ungracious little American , who has been unworthily permitted to tread the paths of the royal garden", "context_len": 112}}}, "prophesying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Aylmer the less was quite right in prophesying her sister-in-law would be a large woman in the future ; she was a large woman now , stoutly built and very fat about the face", "context_len": 34}}}, "pasty": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Her face was pasty in complexion without a scrap of color in it , and her eyes were of too light a blue to redeem the general insipidity of her appearance ; but when she spoke that insipidity vanished , for her lips were very firm , and were apt to utter incisive words , and at such moments her pale blue eyes would flash with a light fire which was full of sarcasm , and might even rise to positive cruelty", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I hated her the moment I saw her face , it was so white and pasty ; and she was n't at all interesting", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "But oh , Nancy , stop ! Let me look ! These are not onions ; they 're the gladiolus bulbs that David has just taken up from the garden ! What a mercy you did not put them into the pasty ! We might all have been poisoned ! '", "context_len": 51}}}, "redeem": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "not more reason that she should spend it in doing all she could for her Master 's service , in helping those of his children on whom he had laid pain and sorrows ? She had been wishing that she might be able to prove her love and gratitude for the great mercy that had been shown to her , that she might yet redeem the wasted years , the misspent life which lay behind her , and now when the Lord had given her the opportunity for which she had been longing , should she turn her back upon it , should she shut her ear to the cry of the needy , because to answer it would cost a sacrifice of her own wishes ? Should", "context_len": 128}}}, "insipidity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "incisive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "Her face was pasty in complexion without a scrap of color in it , and her eyes were of too light a blue to redeem the general insipidity of her appearance ; but when she spoke that insipidity vanished , for her lips were very firm , and were apt to utter incisive words , and at such moments her pale blue eyes would flash with a light fire which was full of sarcasm , and might even rise to positive cruelty", "context_len": 82}}}, "sarcasm": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 82, "context": "my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction , you shall meet with the fate you so justly deserve", "context_len": 128}}}, "oblige": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` Well , sir , if 't was a matter of working my passage , I 'd be bound to go where there would be ships trading the right way ; but if I was with a gentleman as would oblige me with a loan , 't is easy to take ship from Harwich , or find one lying in Yarmouth Roads that would carry us part way , and then we could take passage from some French or Spanish port", "context_len": 81}}}, "ominous": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then he lifted the head of the sofa , and warned by an ominous creaking , he lowered it gently to the floor of the college room which he and his chum , Sid Henderson , were about to leave , with the assistance of Phil Clinton to help them move", "context_len": 51}}}, "signified": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 68, "context": "Then , taking a bow and quiver of arrows , together with a brace of spears , out of the canoe , and signing to me to do the same , Ama led the way through the dense growth bordering the river bank , until we reached an open grassy space of about twenty acres , sparsely dotted here and there with magnificent trees ; and here Ama signified that we were to camp for the day", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "What this signified , as well as the meaning of the display itself , was beyond him", "context_len": 17}}}, "adoration": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 58, "context": "Aylmer the less thought of the hearty tea she and Florence would make at home , the shrimps and the brown bread and butter , and the honey and the strong tea with a little cream to flavor it ; nevertheless , her beady black eyes were fixed on her sister-in-law now with a look which almost signified adoration", "context_len": 59}}}, "unavoidable": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "The polishing of the darkened gold of her Will she had long known in the small unavoidable frictions of everyday life ; and the plunging of that Will into furnace-heat , and the straightening of its crookedness by means of heavy successive blows , she had begun to know in the death of her dear Father , and would soon know more fully through other sorrows coming after", "context_len": 68}}}, "deprecating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I have , Susan , I have , '' said the widow , in an eager , deprecating voice", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Neither one had the slightest idea of drawing and knew that their results would be absurd , but they labored away and finally with half deprecating , half amused expressions showed their drawings to one another", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Hartley said , Oh , my dear ! in a most deprecating way ; but there was no gainsaying the evident sincerity of the declaration", "context_len": 25}}}, "profited": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "It was not so much the money that influenced him , for youth is apt to be indifferent to worldly considerations , but his heart was filled with resentment against this man who had profited by his father \u2019 s sacrifice , and then deliberately refused to fulfil the contract he had made", "context_len": 53}}}, "folding": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 27, "context": "Finally he resolved to take matters into his own hands by suddenly slipping away from Marie-Celeste 's grasp ; and then drawing off a little , and folding both hands behind his back , as though neither of them were to be longer at anybody 's disposal , he said aggressively : And -- and now what are you afraid of , Marie-Celeste ? Do you sink somebody 's goin ' to soot you from de top of one of de towers if you speak out loud ?", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "He did n't have a mother-in-law , this one , but he slept in a folding bed , and , b'gee , that bed used to shut up oftener than the mother-in-law did n't", "context_len": 34}}}, "admittance": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I have been fortunate enough to gain you admittance into an excellent school for the daughters of the poor clergy", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "They were refused admittance to the cell wherein he was confined by Edric , who had assumed the direction of all things , and whose claim , such is the force of impudence , seemed to be tacitly allowed by the thanes and ealdormen of Wessex", "context_len": 46}}}, "clergy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "So that was the father of Phyllis and Marjorie Norton , that vulgar man with the loud voice and the coarse red face , and those pretty frocks and handsome carriages were all paid for out of the ruin of poor people 's homes ; for the lower parts of Warford had a bad character , and the clergy waged ceaseless war against the terrible curse of drink", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Oh , nonsense , Florence , how would you stand the disgrace ? and the clergy school , you know -- do n't forget , Florence , what it means", "context_len": 31}}}, "remuneration": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "I ask for no earthly remuneration ; my position in life renders me independent of any exertions of my own ; I pray but for God \u2019 s blessing upon my attempts to instruct His lambs in the things which concern their everlasting welfare ; and deeply gratified should I feel , were my little work to be classed among the numerous valuable publications which you have already given to the world", "context_len": 72}}}, "smattering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "And he was cunning enough to make this count for all that it was worth and considerably more , considering that , after all , he had gained only the merest smattering of learning", "context_len": 34}}}, "snub": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "My nose is snub and my mouth is wide , but I have got some good points , and if I were your daughter , Aunt Susan -- and I am heartily glad I 'm not your daughter ; I would much , much rather be Mummy 's daughter , poor as she is -- but if I were your daughter you would dress me in such a fashion that my good points would come out , for I have good points ; a nice complexion , fine hair and plenty of it , and fairly good eyes , and my figure would not look clumsy if I wore proper stays and properly-made dresses ; and my feet would not be like clodhoppers , if I had fine", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The first fact to impress itself upon me was that these people among whom I now found myself were of an entirely different race from the negro , properly so-called -- the woolly-pated , high cheek-boned , ebony-skinned individual with snub nose and thick lips usually met with aboard a slaver", "context_len": 51}}}, "clodhoppers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 110, "context": "have got some good points , and if I were your daughter , Aunt Susan -- and I am heartily glad I 'm not your daughter ; I would much , much rather be Mummy 's daughter , poor as she is -- but if I were your daughter you would dress me in such a fashion that my good points would come out , for I have good points ; a nice complexion , fine hair and plenty of it , and fairly good eyes , and my figure would not look clumsy if I wore proper stays and properly-made dresses ; and my feet would not be like clodhoppers , if I had fine well-made boots and silk stockings ; and my hands -- -- ''", "context_len": 128}}}, "deathbed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Mabel , I pity you ; I should like to wash my hands of your daughter , but I can not forget my promise to my poor dead husband , who begged me on his deathbed not to allow either of you to starve", "context_len": 45}}}, "animates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "' And I promised him , and I mean as long as the breath animates this feeble frame to keep my word", "context_len": 22}}}, "per": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 37, "context": "He had been ordered to take some cheese in the forenoon , so he bought a piece at about eightpence a pound ; and as he munched it came this thought : cheese wholesale cost but fivepence per pound ; would it not be possible to buy a piece wholesale and sell it to his friends , so that he too might have the benefit of getting it at this low price ?", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Price per volume , 35 cents , postpaid", "context_len": 8}}}, "twitted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "It is very hard on a girl to be dressed as shabbily as I am , and to be twitted by her companions for what she can not help ; and although you kept me at Cherry Court School , there have been times over and over when I hated you , Aunt Susan , and but for my dear little Mummy I would have left the school and earned my bread as a dressmaker or a servant", "context_len": 78}}}, "earned": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 4, "context": "Three times have you earned special credit ; upon the first occasion , the grand master -- no mean judge of conduct and character -- deemed you worthy of secular knighthood , an honour which has not , in my memory , been bestowed at Rhodes upon any young knight ; on the second , you were promoted to the command of a galley , though never before has such a command been given to any , save knights of long experience ; and now , for the third time , the councillors of one of the greatest of Italian cities are about to do you honour", "context_len": 107}}}, "dressmaker": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 61, "context": "She looked very nice in that silk , almost pretty , and as all its accompaniments were perfect , the lace ruffles round the neck , the lace hanging over her hands , the trimmings of every sort just as they ought to be , the hat which she was to wear with the dress , specially chosen by the London dressmaker for the purpose , no one could look more elegant than Florence did as she stood in the hall of Cherry Court School just before she started for Cherry Court Park", "context_len": 93}}}, "wafers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It contained three very small cups of weak tea , and about five tiny wafers of the thinnest bread and butter", "context_len": 21}}}, "thinnest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "They both wore their thinnest morning frocks and had pinned their hair up in little pug knots on the tops of their heads", "context_len": 23}}}, "basin": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 5, "context": "Thrace was one great fertile basin or valley , sloping toward the center in every direction , so that all the streams from the mountains , increased by the rains which fell over the whole surface of the ground , flowed together into one river , which meandered through the center of the valley , and flowed out at last into the \u00c6gean Sea", "context_len": 64}}}, "judicious": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 59, "context": "But one wet afternoon , finding David alone in the harness-room , where the old man had lighted a fire to make some wonderful decoction of foxgloves wherewith to doctor a cow with a strained leg , they seated themselves on a sack of potatoes in front of the cheerful blaze , and with the aid of a little judicious flattery and coaxing managed to cajole him into a true and circumstantial account of the family ghost", "context_len": 77}}}, "subsequent": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 9, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Alexander determined to make use of this box as a depository for his beautiful copy of Homer , and he always carried it with him , thus protected , in all his subsequent campaigns", "context_len": 34}}}, "correction": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Florence , '' she said , `` you need much correction ; you are a very bombastic , disagreeable , silly , ignorant girl , but I will own it -- I do admire spirit , you have a look of your father , and I was very fond of poor John ; not as fond of him as I was of my own dear Tom , but still I respected him", "context_len": 73}}}, "bombastic": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Florence , '' she said , `` you need much correction ; you are a very bombastic , disagreeable , silly , ignorant girl , but I will own it -- I do admire spirit , you have a look of your father , and I was very fond of poor John ; not as fond of him as I was of my own dear Tom , but still I respected him", "context_len": 73}}}, "eligible": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "He did an extraordinary thing , and at the Cherry Feast , which always ends the summer term at the school , had a preliminary examination , and dear Flo , with two other girls , is eligible to compete for the great Scholarship", "context_len": 44}}}, "fawning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I prefer her rudeness to your fawning , Mabel , and that I will say frankly", "context_len": 17}}}, "expired": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 73, "context": "The letter gave instructions for me to be sent to my uncle \u2019 s at Portsmouth , to be provided for until I could choose for myself what I should be , at the same time exhorting me to serve faithfully His Majesty King Charles II or his lawful successor , and to abstain from vain or idle longings to break the seals of the enclosed package till the stipulated time limit had expired", "context_len": 74}}}, "abilities": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` As to his pugilistic abilities , I am by no means so accurately informed , but if my conjecture be of any value whatsoever , I should be inclined to infer , from the fact that our enemies , the representatives of tyranny and oppression , who are endeavoring to reduce us to submission , have selected him as their champion and representative in arms , that -- -- ''", "context_len": 71}}}, "average": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "As for the characters of some of the converts , we only have to look at home , and to see for ourselves what the average civilised and well-taught and highly-trained Englishman is -- how very far in a large majority of cases from being either blameless , or saintly , or entirely faithful to his Baptismal vows", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 38, "context": "Two sets of mail bags were approaching each other from points two thousand miles apart , and there were times when this approach was at the astounding speed of forty and even fifty miles an hour ! The average daily rate was two hundred and fifty miles a day , but where everything was favorable , or when an express rider was fleeing from the vengeful red men , his pony struck a gait of twenty-five miles and maintained it , when an untrained horse would have dropped in his tracks", "context_len": 91}}}, "atrocious": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "They had been the staunchest Indian supporters of the new British colony , and had sent 28 men of auxiliary troops to Colonel Barnwell , to defeat the Tuscarora insurrection on the coast of North Carolina ( 1712-13 ) , when they suddenly revolted on April 15th , 1715 , committed the most atrocious deeds against helpless colonists , and showed themselves to be quite the reverse of what their name indicates ( y\u00e1masi , y\u00e1massi , the Creek term for mild , gentle , peaceable [ 44 ] )", "context_len": 90}}}, "measurements": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Or , stay , I have not the least doubt that your taste is atrocious ; give me her measurements , and I shall write to my own dressmaker in London", "context_len": 31}}}, "frankness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "The countess greeted him with great cordiality ; but Claudia came forward with a timidity that contrasted strangely with the outspoken frankness he remembered in the girl", "context_len": 27}}}, "labelled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Do you remember the picture , '' she said , `` in the comic paper about the six boys and girls going gaily to pick mushrooms and it was labelled , 'They thought they knew mushrooms ' ? ''", "context_len": 40}}}, "hints": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 34, "context": "No sooner had the new teacher appeared than Clara set to work to do everything in her power to make Edna appear to disadvantage , by all sorts of mean innuendoes , by sly hints , by even open charges , till the child was almost in tears over the state of affairs", "context_len": 53}}}, "unprovided": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Her death may come suddenly or gradually , there is just a possibility that she may know when she is dying , and at such a time , to know also that you are unprovided for , will give her great and terrible anxiety", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "On the afternoon of the third day a knock was heard at the door , for as yet it was unprovided with a bell", "context_len": 24}}}, "cringe": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Bertha 's apparent lot in life was to be a teacher -- her own wish was to cringe to those in power , to obtain a footing amongst those who were likely to aid her , and she had not been a week in the school before she made up her mind that of all the girls at Cherry Court School no one was so likely to help her in the future as Florence Aylmer", "context_len": 75}}}, "propitiate": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "\u2019 [ 63 ] What gross , fearful ignorance ! I heard on good authority that in one place in India , not the Panjab , offerings are actually made to a dead European , who was a special object of dread to the Natives , and whom they therefore wish to propitiate as a kind of demon ! Do not the poor , deluded creatures want teachers ? I find the women in general very gentle and courteous , and quite willing to listen when they are spoken to on the subject of religion", "context_len": 95}}}, "snob": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Yes , I will get the Scholarship , and I will stoop to Aunt Susan 's ways -- I will cringe to her if necessary ; I will do my best to propitiate Sir John Wallis , and I will act like a snob in every sense of the word", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` You 'll suffer for this ! I protest against this sale -- I demand that you do not deliver that package , you young snob ! you -- ''", "context_len": 30}}}, "hasty": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 70, "context": "Whether it was that he had not seen just what had happened -- as might very well have been the case , since the whole thing seemed to have occurred in the space of a single instant -- and was under the impression that I had suddenly gone mad and was attacking his daughter , I know not , but it is certain that Ama 's scream , and certain hasty words uttered by her , only barely saved me from his fury", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "When the Lady Bedrulbudour knew that her father was angry and saw the naked sword in his hand , she was like to swoon for fear ; [ 405 ] so she raised her head and said to him , `` Dear [ 406 ] my father , be not wroth with me , neither be thou hasty in thine anger , for that I am excusable in that which thou hast seen from me", "context_len": 75}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Oh , say , that \u2019 s too bad , Stone , old chap ! Now don \u2019 t be hasty ; let \u2019 s think this matter over", "context_len": 29}}}, "screwdriver": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Now , then , '' she said , in a hasty voice , `` let us get the screwdriver and open the box", "context_len": 24}}}, "tissue": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "Utter silence met this tissue of impudence , defiance , truth and falsehood , and he saw plainly enough that he was believed to have committed the theft of Percy 's money for theft itself , pure and simple , and that fear of detection only had induced him to make the effort at restoration", "context_len": 55}}}, "shimmering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Then came the sweet voices of the children and their teacher , hymn after hymn of infant praise floating in , as it seemed , on that soft , shimmering sunshine , and filling the little room with music", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Inside the area the hot and shimmering pavement echoed to the tread of the plebe company at drill ; outside the street was deserted except for one solitary figure with whom our story has to do", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Some of them dropped on the floor and started to crawl away , leaving a shimmering track", "context_len": 17}}}, "girlish": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 54, "context": "I do not for a moment deceive myself with the thought that , beyond the fact that her gift has made her feel an interest in me and my fate , she has any sentiment in the matter : probably , indeed , she looks back upon the gift as a foolish act of girlish enthusiasm that led her into making a promise that she now can not but find unpleasantly binding ; for it is but natural that among the young nobles of her own rank and country there must be some whom she would see with pleasure wearing her colours", "context_len": 102}}}, "flowered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The silk dress was followed by a flowered barege , and this by one or two cottons , all equally well made , quite suitable for a young girl , and the sort of dress which would give to Florence 's somewhat clumsy figure a new grace", "context_len": 47}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Well , it 's different and there are two big parlors and some curious things that I never saw before and chairs in beautiful light blue , all flowered , and some tall vases and marble statues", "context_len": 37}}}, "cottons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The silk dress was followed by a flowered barege , and this by one or two cottons , all equally well made , quite suitable for a young girl , and the sort of dress which would give to Florence 's somewhat clumsy figure a new grace", "context_len": 47}}}, "label": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "Bart helped carry it in , dumped it on the scales , went to the desk , got the receipt book , and reading the label on the trunk found that it was directed to Mrs", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Why do n't you label it , Phil , so a fellow would know what it is ? ''", "context_len": 20}}}, "underlinen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Under the pretty serge were half a dozen white embroidered aprons , and below them piles and piles of underlinen , all beautifully embroidered , silk stockings , little shoes , plenty of gloves , handkerchiefs , also embroidered with Florence 's name", "context_len": 43}}}, "wardrobe": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 4, "context": "The keepers of the wardrobe , one after another , have cared for them carefully , Albert , for nearly five hundred years , Miss Belmore explained ; and it is only by a special order from the Queen that they can ever be taken out of the precious chest where they are stored for a single moment , except twice a year or so , to be cleaned and brushed", "context_len": 71}}}, "enhance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I think so , love , for it will spread the news , and it will greatly enhance my position in the place", "context_len": 24}}}, "teas": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "The hut had to be swept and dusted , late flowers to be gleaned from the garden to decorate the tea-table , cups and saucers packed up and conveyed in baskets , together with the little tin kettle and the methylated spirit lamp , as they could scarcely light a fire on the platform like they did for picnic teas in the woods", "context_len": 63}}}, "discarded": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "The last spark had died out of the paper in the grate and left only black tinder ; the table was left bare , the golden plates and richly embroidered napkins , and the garlands were transformed again into old handkerchiefs , scraps of red and white paper , and discarded artificial flowers all scattered on the floor ; the minstrels in the minstrel gallery had stolen away , and the viols and bassoons were still", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "While the wounded were being removed , a couple of Prussian Poles , who were employed as ambulance-men , placed Malcolm on a stretcher , and threw a discarded greatcoat over his legs , not realizing that he was an enemy , since the remnants of his khaki uniform were indistinguishable from the field-grey after they had been `` chromed '' by the fumes of bursting shells", "context_len": 67}}}, "intends": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 23, "context": "A certain objective purpose is inherent in these legends , which is more of a practical than of a historical character ; it intends to trace the tribal friendship existing between the Kasi ta and the Chicasa , or a portion of the latter , to remote ages", "context_len": 48}}}, "elegant": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 71, "context": "She looked very nice in that silk , almost pretty , and as all its accompaniments were perfect , the lace ruffles round the neck , the lace hanging over her hands , the trimmings of every sort just as they ought to be , the hat which she was to wear with the dress , specially chosen by the London dressmaker for the purpose , no one could look more elegant than Florence did as she stood in the hall of Cherry Court School just before she started for Cherry Court Park", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Trains , elegant in the house , would never do in Zenanas", "context_len": 12}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The ladies wanted to go out to match some trimming and view some elegant new party frocks that might do for a wedding gown", "context_len": 24}}}, "intellectual": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 17, "context": "I have tried both , and find there is more real pleasure to be had from the intellectual than from the social side of life , for while the latter is apt to fail us just when we most require it , the former is `` warranted to wear well and improve with keeping , '' and , so far from being affected by the changes and chances of this world , sticks by us when health and wealth and even friends can fall away", "context_len": 85}}}, "obtains": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "If Florence plays her cards well and obtains the Scholarship she will have a good deal to say of Aylmer Hall in the future", "context_len": 24}}}, "enclose": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "The total want of all society , except that which the walls of Grimhaggard Hall have the honour constantly to enclose , may perhaps have an effect upon the lady \u2019 s spirits not altogether exhilarating ; but when your brother returns from College , perhaps he may be accompanied by some of his fellow-students", "context_len": 55}}}, "herewith": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` I enclose herewith a five-pound note , and please ask Florence to exchange her third-class ticket for a first-class one , and telegraph to the station-master at Hilchester to have a carriage waiting for her , in order to take her back to the school as my niece ought to arrive", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Inclosed herewith you will find an order on the bank to deliver the bars to you", "context_len": 16}}}, "exchange": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 67, "context": "A grand place , too , to work , you think ; and then you recall with a sigh that the light of that glorious mind has well-nigh gone out , 'neath the weight of physical weariness and infirmity , and then the solitary home begins to look a little like a prison in your eyes , as you realize how glad its inmate would be to exchange it for the Palace of that King whose divine intent for the world he has so marvellously interpreted for us all in the days when soul was still master of hand and brain", "context_len": 101}, "NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 115, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "encircled": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 45, "context": "A short three miles now separated us from the town , and on approaching it I saw a large mound of earth , called the Town Mount , crowned by fortifications and fronted by a line of bastions and earthworks , which in turn were encircled with a moat that communicated with the mill dam on the right", "context_len": 58}}}, "neatest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "She was wearing the neat and beautifully fitting serge , a white linen collar encircled her throat , and was fastened by the neatest of studs , and white linen cuffs also encircled her wrists ; her figure was shown off to the best advantage", "context_len": 45}}}, "studs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "She was wearing the neat and beautifully fitting serge , a white linen collar encircled her throat , and was fastened by the neatest of studs , and white linen cuffs also encircled her wrists ; her figure was shown off to the best advantage", "context_len": 45}}}, "sweetbread": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Is there any little thing you would fancy -- I am sure Sukey would run to the butcher 's -- a sweetbread or anything ? ''", "context_len": 26}}}, "accusing": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 69, "context": "It was broad daylight , it was true , and Meg was only a poor , ragged child , but her face was so solemn , and her big eyes shone with so intense a light , and she was so absolutely fearless before him , that he felt impressed , even just a trifle afraid -- something as he would have felt had he been looking at an accusing angel", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "The two fellows seemed to realize that they were considered responsible for the whole unhappy affair ; indeed , the doctor had told them so pretty plainly , and they were themselves rather anxious to be off and away from such an accusing and uncomfortable atmosphere", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "And as he proceeded the unfavorable impression that had prevailed concerning him was gradually dispelled ; for surely he did not speak like a desperate character or a thief ; nor was Lawyer Frances , by his sternest and most accusing cross-questioning , able to confuse the lad or shake him in his statements", "context_len": 54}}}, "reviewed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "What with sickness and fighting and losses encountered on the way up the river , Baker 's force was now reduced to about five hundred men , in place of the twelve hundred whom he had once reviewed at Gondokoro", "context_len": 40}}}, "assigned": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 15, "context": "For nearly a week I was kept closely confined to the hut which had been assigned to me , never being permitted to go beyond the door of the building , where , when the sun had worked round far enough to cause the building to cast a shadow , I soon got into the way of sitting for an hour or two , doing my best to ingratiate myself with the inhabitants of the place , many of whom used to come and stare at me with never-ceasing curiosity and wonder , and with whom I used to laugh and chat , although of course neither party understood a word of what was said by the other", "context_len": 118}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Each plane will lay a two-ton , delayed action bomb directly on an assigned target , from mast-head height", "context_len": 19}}}, "alter": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 21, "context": "He had never dreamed this would be so ; he had never meant it should be ; but that did not alter the fact that , flattered and made much of ever since he went up to Oxford , he had somehow had little time to think of his mother , and , sorrier than that , little inclination", "context_len": 59}}}, "intellect": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 42, "context": "An artist of repute , who saw her in the later days of her Indian career , has said unhesitatingly , in reply to a query on this subject , -- Plain ! No ! A face with such a look of intellect as Miss Tucker \u2019 s could never be plain", "context_len": 52}}}, "scholarly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "I will do all I can to make you happy , and I think it but fair to put all three of you on your guard , for please understand that the Scholarship is given , not only for scholarly attainments and correct deportment , but also for those lofty traits of character which are a greater possession to any woman than either ladylike manners or great accomplishments", "context_len": 68}}}, "plodding": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Mary has not as brilliant an intellect as Kitty , and in some ways is not as scholarly as you are , Florence , but she is very plodding and persevering , and as a rule gets to the head of her class", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 42, "context": "The emigrant trains were disposed to keep to certain paths , where the face of the country compelled a closing in , but in other sections the respective courses were separated by miles , and , as has been shown the parties plodding across the plains , even though their routes were parallel , were often so far apart that for days they saw nothing of one another", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Hello , Sandy ! '' called Fenn , as he saw the president of the Shamma Shig secret society plodding along through the snow", "context_len": 24}}}, "persevering": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "Mary has not as brilliant an intellect as Kitty , and in some ways is not as scholarly as you are , Florence , but she is very plodding and persevering , and as a rule gets to the head of her class", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "She was very resolute ; very persevering ; very affectionate ; reserved , yet demonstrative ; untidy , yet methodical ; exceedingly anxious for the happiness of all around ; apt often to think people better than they really were ; generous to a fault ; unselfishly ready at all times to put her own wishes aside ; vehement and impulsive , yet never in a hurry or flurry ; unyielding , yet tender ; severe , yet frisky", "context_len": 79}}}, "themes": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 48, "context": "The thoughts of his father 's plight , a haunting dread that Colonel Harrington might make him some trouble , and the uncertainty of continued work in the express service , all combined to depress his mind with anxiety and suspense , and he tried to dismiss the themes by whistling a quiet , soothing tune as he started to get the hammer to put the padlock in place", "context_len": 69}}}, "exercises": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 71, "context": "I am afraid this time her stone was bigger , for it bounced with such effect on his shoulder , that he turned round with a flow of language far from elegant , shaking his fist at the tree with so much fury that Peggy was in terror lest he should climb up to find the author of the assault ; but he evidently thought the day too hot for such exercises , and with a final abusive epithet walked away muttering curses on all the children in creation", "context_len": 89}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "On occasion he went swimming in the Thames with a co-worker named Wygate , and once on an excursion to Chelsea he dazzled Wygate and his other companions with a display of the water exercises which he had invented in his childhood", "context_len": 42}}}, "assemble": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 42, "context": "The speeches were over at length , and a last farewell round of the ruins having been made , the society finally took its departure , with the intention of viewing the church , and an injunction for all the members to assemble for tea at the Bluebell Arms at half-past four precisely", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "463 , in the following terms : `` The great Coweta town , on Chatahuchi or Apalachucly river , twelve miles above Apalachucla town , is called the bloody town , where the micos , chiefs and warriors assemble , when a general war is proposed , and here captives and state malefactors are put to death", "context_len": 57}}}, "conversazione": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Their companions , those of the Upper school , were invited to assemble in the drawing-room on the occasions of the weekly conversazione , as it was called , and a special subject was then introduced , which the girls were obliged to handle as deftly and as well as they could", "context_len": 52}}}, "unworthy": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 68, "context": "The enormous crowd who had followed us up from the beach were not permitted to enter the palisaded enclosure , which was strictly taboo to the common herd ; our party therefore now consisted solely of those who had brought me up the river , four individuals who had joined us outside the gate -- and whom I took to be officials of some sort -- and my unworthy self ; and , for my own part , I would very willingly have waived the distinction of forming one of the party", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "It is well said that no man is utterly bad , and perhaps he possessed domestic virtues which were thought unworthy of the attention of the chroniclers ; but as they picture him -- now prompting Ethelred to deeds of treachery against the Danes , now joining those Danes themselves , and surpassing them in cruelty -- now seeking pretended reconciliation , only to betray his foe more surely , and in all this aided and supported by the weak , unprincipled king -- as thus pictured there is scarcely a blacker character in history", "context_len": 95}}}, "exclude": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "Mother says it is very conceited of a person to think she can satisfy every need of a friend , and that it shows only love of self , and not love of your friend , when you want to exclude others from her friendship , and I am sure I do n't want to be either selfish or conceited , and I should hate to be called a jealous person", "context_len": 71}}}, "thirtieth": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Never a doubt nor a scruple entered Richard 's mind , even on that memorable thirtieth of January , when on the scaffold at Whitehall the King --", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Fontanedo states that he was prisoner in these parts from his thirteenth to his thirtieth year ; that he knew four languages , but was not familiar with those of A\u00efs and Teaga , not having been there", "context_len": 39}}}, "reassembled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Scholarship was to be competed for on the thirtieth of October , and the girls reassembled at Cherry Court School about the fifteenth of August", "context_len": 26}}}, "fifteenth": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "'No more Latin , no more Greek , no more cane to make me squeak ! ' sang Bobby on the fifteenth of July , springing out of the pony-trap before Pixie had stopped , and taking a flying leap over the pump-trough in the exuberance of his spirits", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "The two new figures , confronted with each other , in like manner , furnish a fifteenth figure , which , being confronted with the first of the primaries , gives a sixteenth and last figure , completing the series", "context_len": 40}}}, "recommenced": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "Three weeks after the school had recommenced , some time therefore in the first week in September , Mary Bateman , who had been bending for a long time over her desk with her hands pressed to her temples and her cheeks somewhat flushed , suddenly raised her eyes and encountered the fixed stare of Kitty Sharston", "context_len": 57}}}, "temples": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 17, "context": "His hair was only beginning to turn grey , and was falling away a trifle from his temples , and he was very upright , and very thin , and had keen eyes , the keenest eyes I had ever looked at , small , grey and bright , and those eyes seemed to look through you , as though they were forcing a gimlet into the very secrets of your soul", "context_len": 72}}}, "mathematics": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` I must do my best , '' said Florence , in a gloomy voice ; `` there is not the least doubt that I shall beat Kitty Sharston in mathematics and arithmetic , and as to Mary Bateman , she has not a scrap of imagination in her composition", "context_len": 50}}}, "pallid": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 48, "context": "At that instant the thing upon which my gaze was fixed , and which looked like half a fathom of stiff tarred lanyard , darted with lightning swiftness at the girl and coiled itself about her shapely bare arm , while a piercing scream rang out from her pallid lips", "context_len": 50}}}, "blunt": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "What a long note I have written ! Pay me back by a review of my Tragedy , and be as blunt as ever you like ; for if you tell me that my poor lady is very stupid , instead of rather stupid , you will only make me smile", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 58, "context": "You know country people always keep their best parlours done up in must and green paper ; but the princess says , `` Nothing is too good for Polly and the boys ! '' They just idolize her , and I fancy they have good reason to , for , as Stephen said , in his queer , blunt way , `` she comes as near to an angel as any mortal ever will", "context_len": 74}}}, "downright": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "Poor Sigard ! he could not bear that , and he said one day when I was trying to cheer him , 'No , father , I shall never be able to strike a good downright blow again , and I can not live until I die a cow 's death in my bed ; I will die as my fathers have died before me when they could no longer fight", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 57, "context": "I have not the slightest doubt that you have faults , but I am willing to run the risk of finding them out ; and I have no doubt that I have faults too , but I do not think that they are too prominent , and , at any rate , I am a real , downright son of Britain , an honest , good-hearted , well-meaning man", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "During the rest of the meal Sir John devoted himself to Mary ; her frank , commonplace face , her downright manners , her total absence of all self-consciousness pleased him", "context_len": 31}}}, "discuss": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 33, "context": "She don \u2019 t da \u2019 s \u2019 t talk about ye to hum , ye know , but w \u2019 en she \u2019 s away she kind o \u2019 finds opportetunities to discuss the subjec \u2019", "context_len": 37}}}, "needlework": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 54, "context": "She catechized Lilian freely about her housekeeping arrangements , hoped Nancy did her duty , and did not neglect to sweep out corners , told Bobby that his irrepressible curls looked girlish , and his hair ought to be cropped close every week , plied Peggy with embarrassing questions on the subject of fine needlework and stocking-darning , and drank four cups of tea in the meantime , with the air of one conferring a favour thereby", "context_len": 77}}}, "gardening": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "Mother thinks that a girl ought to be purely domestic ; she is very particular about needlework , and she would like every girl to be able to make a shirt well , and to be able to cook and preserve , and know a little about gardening , and know a great deal about keeping a house in perfect order", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Baring , has succeeded in making these young Natives like not only cricket , but gardening", "context_len": 16}}}, "openings": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 15, "context": "When the spark was blazing up , four young men entered the area at the openings of its four corners , each holding a stick of wood ; they approached the new fire with high reverence , and placed the ends of their sticks to it `` in a very formal manner", "context_len": 52}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "\u2019 s footsteps , and to give the Evenings of their lives to Mission-work , openings enough for them might be found", "context_len": 22}}}, "lectures": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "He will spend the money which he would have spent on my education at Cherry Court School in keeping me at Girton , where I shall attend the University lectures at Cambridge , and learn as much as a man learns", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 37, "context": "Yes , he was outwardly a model character : he worked early and late ; he saved money ; he was never in debt ; he defrauded no man ; his evenings were spent either in attending lectures of working men like himself or studying the subjects he loved at home ; he never drank ; he never swore ; he was looked up to , and brought forward as an example to follow for many a poor drunken wretch", "context_len": 80}}}, "learns": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "Billy Smith , son of an eminent explorer , learns to pilot a seaplane aboard a Naval Air Station , and immediately fares forth upon a series of the most exciting cruises and mysterious quests by air , by land and by sea that have ever fallen to the lot of man", "context_len": 52}}}, "womanly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "Now , I had always loved the Duchess , not at all because she was a duchess , but because she was a woman with a very womanly heart and a very sweet way , and my whole heart went out to her now -- to her gracious appearance , to her gentle , refined tone of voice , to the look in her eyes", "context_len": 65}}}, "warped": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 85, "context": "She is not the least like my dear mother , and I never loved her , and I know that the best in me will not be brought to the fore if I am with her ; and I sha n't learn those things which would delight dear father ; I shall not know modern languages , nor be a good musical scholar , nor be able to sing nicely , and I -- I shall hate that life , and my nature may be warped , and I -- but , oh ! I will win the Scholarship", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The man \u2019 s reason was so warped that he regarded everyone with a sullen hatred", "context_len": 16}}}, "charmingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "I do n't suppose , when all is said and done , that it will make any difference in his affection whether you can speak French and read German or not , and I am certain he wo n't kiss you less often because you do not play charmingly and because you do not sing divinely", "context_len": 56}}}, "divinely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "I do n't suppose , when all is said and done , that it will make any difference in his affection whether you can speak French and read German or not , and I am certain he wo n't kiss you less often because you do not play charmingly and because you do not sing divinely", "context_len": 56}}}, "college": {"freq": 113, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 113, "word_index": 45, "context": "And all this while the wilful little queen was trying to convince herself that it was ready for Donald 's sake , when the truth was that the long walks with Donald , when Harold -- who was making up some necessary back work at college -- was not at her service , were what she was determined not to give up , and the reading aloud in the evenings , when Donald was such a delightful listener ; and , in fact , the hundred and one little amusing things that Donald was continually doing , and that made the days go by in such happy , merry fashion", "context_len": 110}}}, "twined": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 51, "context": "One flash ends all ! O Clara , when my soul Hath ceased to suffer , can it cease to love ? Methinks , when quitting Earth , \u2019 twill still retain Her image , who was more than Earth to me ! It is a portion of my being , twined With every thought and feeling ; thou wilt weep , My Clara ; thou canst not believe him false To faith and friends , who is so true to thee", "context_len": 82}}}, "heaved": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was as smooth as a pond , except where , with a low moan , it heaved up and beat against Carn Du , falling back with an angry hiss as if of disappointment , while all above looked calm and dark and starlit", "context_len": 45}}}, "grander": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The sides of the ships were also gay with bright colours , and as the warriors embarked and hung up their bright shields , grander sight was never seen", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I finish this off in Montreal , a very handsome , thriving-looking city , with far grander buildings than Quebec : but it wants the dreamlike , exquisite beauty of its sister", "context_len": 32}}}, "attainments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "I will do all I can to make you happy , and I think it but fair to put all three of you on your guard , for please understand that the Scholarship is given , not only for scholarly attainments and correct deportment , but also for those lofty traits of character which are a greater possession to any woman than either ladylike manners or great accomplishments", "context_len": 68}}}, "selves": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "Dey 're trained to behave jus ' of dere own selves without any driver , and when dey get out on de road dey always do behave ; and then in the moment 's pause that followed , Marie-Celeste , remembering certain recent performances of her own , wondered if her father wished that a certain little girl , of whom he had some knowledge , more closely resembled these royal ponies , who , once trained to behave , according to Albert , never dreamed of taking the bit in their teeth or of kicking over the traces", "context_len": 99}}}, "fretting": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 8, "context": "Lawrence , without intending it , was constantly fretting and aggravating those around her while Maggie and Bessie , who thought that all their mamma did or said was quite perfect , were amazed and indignant when they heard her rules and wishes questioned and found fault with , and sometimes even set aside by Aunt Patty , if she thought another way better", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Mamma told her that the way to bear a trial was not to sit fretting over it and thinking how bad it was , but to look at its bright side , and see what good we or others might gain from it", "context_len": 43}}}, "sympathies": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 32, "context": "Do you care enough for me to desert your comrades and fling your principles to the four winds ? Do I care enough for you to leave my people and give my sympathies to your side ? '' She was smiling as she spoke , but I knew that she was very serious , and I made no reply", "context_len": 59}}}, "tingling": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "Kitty had shown her Sir John 's letter , and she had felt a queer tingling pain at her heart as she read it ; but then a sort of defiance , which was growing more and more in her character day by day , arose to her aid , and she determined that she would not give Sir John one loophole to find out anything amiss in her conduct", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "That \u2019 s what adds to the fun ; the speed itself gives a man a thrill and the possibility of ambush , a treacherous shot or an open attack sets the blood tingling", "context_len": 34}}}, "defiance": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 100, "context": "`` Beware , woman ! '' shrieked Maggie , and Bessie , thinking it time for her to come to her sister 's aid , joined in the cry , `` Beware , woman ! '' while Franky , always ready to take part in any disturbance , struck at Aunt Patty with his whip , and shouted , `` 'Ware , woman ! '' and Hafed , knowing nothing but that this old lady had alarmed and distressed his young charge , and that it was his duty to protect them , raised his voice in a whoop of defiance , and snatching up the hearth-brush , brandished it in a threatening manner as he danced wildly about her", "context_len": 120}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Mark Mallory 's conduct shows that he 's gotten the idea into his head that not only can he avoid being hazed , but even turn the tables upon us and bid us defiance", "context_len": 34}}}, "loophole": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 61, "context": "Kitty had shown her Sir John 's letter , and she had felt a queer tingling pain at her heart as she read it ; but then a sort of defiance , which was growing more and more in her character day by day , arose to her aid , and she determined that she would not give Sir John one loophole to find out anything amiss in her conduct", "context_len": 70}}}, "amiss": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 66, "context": "Kitty had shown her Sir John 's letter , and she had felt a queer tingling pain at her heart as she read it ; but then a sort of defiance , which was growing more and more in her character day by day , arose to her aid , and she determined that she would not give Sir John one loophole to find out anything amiss in her conduct", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Ever and anon we looked forth from doors and windows into the black darkness without ; but although it was near midnight , neither sight nor sound told of aught amiss , and we were beginning to yield to fatigue , when I ascended the tower in company with Father Adhelm , to survey the scene for the last time", "context_len": 60}}}, "accompaniments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "She looked very nice in that silk , almost pretty , and as all its accompaniments were perfect , the lace ruffles round the neck , the lace hanging over her hands , the trimmings of every sort just as they ought to be , the hat which she was to wear with the dress , specially chosen by the London dressmaker for the purpose , no one could look more elegant than Florence did as she stood in the hall of Cherry Court School just before she started for Cherry Court Park", "context_len": 93}}}, "ruffles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "She looked very nice in that silk , almost pretty , and as all its accompaniments were perfect , the lace ruffles round the neck , the lace hanging over her hands , the trimmings of every sort just as they ought to be , the hat which she was to wear with the dress , specially chosen by the London dressmaker for the purpose , no one could look more elegant than Florence did as she stood in the hall of Cherry Court School just before she started for Cherry Court Park", "context_len": 93}}}, "interior": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 26, "context": "I might tell him that my employer -- and I would mention some merchant whose name would carry weight with him -- has received from the interior a large consignment of slaves , among whom are three or four girls , who would fetch high prices in Egypt , and as he believes they have been captured from a tribe within the limits of the sultan 's territory , he is anxious to get rid of them , and will either dispose of them all cheaply in a lot , or will hand them over to him to take to Egypt to sell , giving him a large commission for carrying them there and disposing of them", "context_len": 117}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 38, "context": "We simply broke open the lumps of baked clay with our spears and took out the birds -- minus the skin and feathers , which adhered to the clay -- and , splitting them open , removed the interior organs and devoured the flesh , which I found done to a turn , and particularly rich and juicy in flavour", "context_len": 60}}}, "exclamations": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 93, "context": "canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have heard of , they had never actually seen a white man before , a conclusion which I afterwards found to be correct", "context_len": 128}}}, "thrice": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "The cry was taken up by the usher and repeated thrice ere there hobbled into the well of the court an object that could scarce lay claim to being called a man", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "They slept that night and on the morrow Alaeddin 's mother arose and went with the dish to the Sultan 's Divan , but found it closed ; so she asked the folk and they said to her , `` The Sultan holdeth a Divan but thrice a week ; '' wherefore she was compelled [ 352 ] to return home", "context_len": 61}}}, "dimpling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "She was a sedate-looking little girl with big blue eyes and straight , mouse-colored hair , but upon this occasion she was dimpling and smiling as she handed a tiny , three-cornered note to Edna", "context_len": 35}}}, "depute": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Kitty , my dear , '' said Sir John , turning to Kitty Sharston , `` you have been here before and I depute to you the task of doing the honors", "context_len": 33}}}, "gardeners": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "The tiny fences were pulled up , and scattered far and wide ; lady-slippers , mignonette , verbenas , and all the other simple flowers which had flourished so well , and given such pride and delight to the little gardeners , were rooted up and trampled into the earth ; and , worse than all , the beloved heliotrope and geranium were torn leaf from leaf and sprig from sprig , while their main stems had been twisted and bent , till no hope remained that even these could be revived", "context_len": 92}}}, "grates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` And I meant to be amiable to-day , '' said poor Florence , `` but somehow everything grates", "context_len": 19}}}, "sketching": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "But there , we are to be amiable to-day , are we not ? Look at Mary sitting under that tree and sketching as if her life depended on it", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Have you been sketching it , Miss Bateman ? ''", "context_len": 11}}}, "peregrinations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "They had nearly completed their peregrinations , having wandered over many acres of cultivated and lovely land , when the luncheon bell summoned them back to the house", "context_len": 28}}}, "luncheons": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Tucker , since her husband \u2019 s death , had made one difference , in that she no longer gave dinner-parties ; but luncheons were in full swing , to any extent ; and Charlotte \u2019 s powers of entertaining were still in abundant requisition", "context_len": 45}}}, "constrained": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "When he became tired of his constrained position he got off and walked a quarter or half a mile , and experienced the needed relief", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "How queer it is , he said to himself , that I should feel constrained in this way to run out to Windsor ! Land knows ! I have no desire to come to be on intimate terms of acquaintance with Evelyn 's boys ; and what would be the satisfaction of prowling around just to see where they live ? Their father gave me up after that time he spoke his mind so freely about my aimless life -- as he was pleased to call it -- and there is no reason whatever why I should bother myself about my sister 's children , since she , poor thing ! is dead and gone , and they have enough of this world 's goods to make them", "context_len": 128}}}, "evenly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "Physically two fighters were never more evenly matched ; no one , to look at them , could have picked the winner , for both were giants", "context_len": 27}}}, "responsibilities": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "David could not be spared to go with them , but all the children had been taught to drive , and even Bobby had a firm hand on the reins , and knew the rules of the road as well as many a more experienced coachman ; and I think , too , that Pixie had a sense of her responsibilities , and could be trusted not to get the wheel locked with a passing waggon , or to race too furiously down a steep hill , whatever feats her drivers might urge her to perform", "context_len": 96}}}, "talents": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 65, "context": "The fire-balloon , however , was the success of the evening , for it shot straight up , and floated across the sky like a beautiful meteor , its pink and green sides giving a charming effect , till at length it dwindled away and became a mere speck in the distance , leaving the children more full of admiration than ever for Archie 's talents", "context_len": 66}}}, "gong": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` I wonder where Mary is , '' she said , and just as she uttered the words the silver gong in the hall sounded , and the three ladies hurried down to luncheon", "context_len": 34}}}, "smudge": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Still no sign of Mary , but just as they were all wondering with regard to her absence , the door was opened , and a girl , with a smudge on her face and her hat pushed crooked on her head , entered the room", "context_len": 46}}}, "grenadier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` What , the grenadier ? '' said Sir John , with a smile", "context_len": 14}}}, "contracted": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 7, "context": "The city itself , however , though contracted in extent on account of the small dimensions of the island , was very compactly built and strongly fortified , and it contained a vast number of stately and magnificent edifices , which were filled with stores of wealth that had been accumulated by the mercantile enterprise and thrift of many generations", "context_len": 60}}}, "frown": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` So , sir , '' she said , with an angry frown , `` I 'll teach you to run away without leave ! '' and taking the poor dog by the back of the neck , she thrashed him with the horse-whip she held in her other hand", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "There was a penetrating sweetness in the pleading , but she saw the red of surprise mount to the very edge of the man 's hair , and almost a frown settle between the eyes", "context_len": 35}}}, "devote": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 43, "context": "Why do you mention so often the mere handwriting of your letters ? Do you think that I see anything in them but the kindness of her who has , in the midst of all her engagements , found so much time to devote to me ? My own Mother too -- how very good to me she has been ! I am grateful to her for all her most kind endeavours to set my mind quite at ease on the subject of the poor little Robins", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Then , when I am free , I can tell you how I love and honour you , and how , as I have in the past devoted my life to the Order , so I will in the future devote it to your happiness", "context_len": 45}}}, "interfered": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 27, "context": "He meant to be faithful , but of course he was not to be trusted without a good deal of watching to make sure he was not interfered with , and there were folks enough , bad boys and girls , who were always ready to meddle with him and set him up to some mischief , just for the bit of fun it would make for themselves", "context_len": 68}}}, "inattentive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "As these thoughts came to the restless and unhappy girl 's mind she only played with her food , became distrait and inattentive , and had to be spoken to once or twice by Mrs", "context_len": 35}}}, "ensure": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 56, "context": "Your knowledge may prove of much assistance to the Order , and believe me , the time you have spent in acquiring it may prove of much greater advantage to you in your career than if you had occupied it in performing even the most valiant deeds , and that at some future time it will ensure your appointment to a responsible office here", "context_len": 64}}}, "gipsy": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 6, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}}}, "strongly": {"freq": 83, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 83, "word_index": 125, "context": "of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very strongly influence him", "context_len": 128}}}, "devise": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "Gervaise , although taken aback by this sudden change in his fortunes , was not so cast down as he might otherwise have been ; he would now be free to carry out any plan for escape that he might devise , and by his being addressed in Italian it was evident to him that his knowledge of Turkish was unsuspected", "context_len": 61}}}, "auspicious": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Now , brother mariners , said John , who was in high feather at this auspicious beginning of their enterprise , we \u2019 ve got a fishing-ground of our own , marks of our own , all written down in a birch-bark book , and can come when we like", "context_len": 50}}}, "laurels": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "The happy winner of the Scholarship shall be truly crowned with laurels , bonfires are to be lighted in her honor , and the whole country-side is to be invited to attend the great function , which I propose to take place , not at the school , but in this house", "context_len": 52}}}, "function": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "The second part of the name , -chiqui , is a term foreign to Yuchi , but found in all the dialects of Maskoki in the function of house , dwelling , ( tch\u00faku , tch\u00f3ko , and in the eastern or Apalachian dialects , tch\u00edki ) and has to be rendered here in the collective sense of houses , town", "context_len": 61}}}, "extend": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 13, "context": "Your little ones have given me a lesson in their innocent wish to extend to others the benefits which God has heaped upon them ; now can not you help me to put it into practice ? I am still so much of a stranger in my own city that I should scarcely know where to begin the task of carrying help to those who need it ; but you were always a hand to know the claims and deserts of the poor", "context_len": 83}}}, "capacity": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 90, "context": "May I suggest , sir , that they should be those appropriate to a small merchant ? This might seem to account for my not being placed with the other slaves who may be on board the galley , as it would be supposed that I was set apart in order that I should be sent to one of the auberges as a servant ; and my afterwards being herded with the others would be explained by its being found that there was no opening for me in such a capacity", "context_len": 91}}}, "exactitude": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "She performed her duties with exactitude and despatch", "context_len": 8}}}, "despatch": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 37, "context": "There was now a short breathing time for the besieged ; but the depression which the failure of their efforts excited among the Turks , was shortly dispelled by the arrival of a ship , with a despatch from Constantinople , in which the pasha was informed that the sultan himself was about to proceed to Rhodes with a reinforcement of a hundred thousand men , and a fresh park of artillery", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "Moreover , in any case it would be necessary to despatch a vessel to Genoa , in order that it might be known that the danger was averted , and that there was no longer any necessity for getting the galleys ready for sea", "context_len": 44}}}, "incapable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "Eliot , taking Ben \u2019 s hand in both her own thin hands , mere words are quite incapable of expressing my feelings , but I wish I might somehow make you know how deeply grateful I am to you for your noble and heroic action in saving my helpless little girl from those cruel dogs", "context_len": 56}}}, "accompanies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Bertha had the fair complexion which often accompanies a certain shade of red hair , and but for the expression in her eyes she might have been a fairly good-looking girl", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The bay of the dog seems close at hand , and the trampling of human feet accompanies it", "context_len": 18}}}, "watchful": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 55, "context": "As Florence slowly , very slowly , as if each step was weighted with lead , crept forward to the front of the dais without any of that look of triumph and pleasure which ought to have marked her face at such a moment , Bertha Keys threw back her own head and allowed her watchful light blue eyes to follow the girl , while a smile of sardonic import curled her lips", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "But gradually these habits were overcome , and he grew in holiness , becoming watchful of his conduct , praying more fervently , living nearer to Christ", "context_len": 27}}}, "exertions": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 16, "context": "I ask for no earthly remuneration ; my position in life renders me independent of any exertions of my own ; I pray but for God \u2019 s blessing upon my attempts to instruct His lambs in the things which concern their everlasting welfare ; and deeply gratified should I feel , were my little work to be classed among the numerous valuable publications which you have already given to the world", "context_len": 72}}}, "heiress": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 65, "context": "I know that it is not only sinful , but mad , for me to think so frequently of her , but at least I am not mad enough to think that I can either win the heart or aspire to the hand of one who is , you say , so beautiful , and who is , moreover , as I know , the heiress to wide estates", "context_len": 69}}}, "advancement": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "Sir John Boswell told me in confidence that he understood that Rivers did not at first volunteer to come out in response to the appeal of the grand master , but that the grand prior informed him that unless he took this opportunity of retrieving his character , he might give up all hope of ever obtaining advancement", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Few of them who sought to make their careers in this way had the grit and patience to endure the machine-like routine in which advancement lay years and years ahead", "context_len": 30}}}, "nameless": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Speaking to a friend , long after in old age , she said that in her young days at home , \u2019 when a certain nameless gentleman was supposed to be paying his addresses to Fanny , the other sisters were very indignant \u2019 at the idea of any man wishing to break into their sisterly circle", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Bart halted with a nameless shock , for the utterance was distinctly human and curdling", "context_len": 15}}}, "feverish": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 18, "context": "Aylmer the great , observed how depressed Florence was when she received letters from Dawlish , noted her feverish anxiety to deport herself well , to lead a life of excellent conduct , and , above all things , to struggle through the weighty themes which had to be mastered in order to win the great Scholarship", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Thank God , I am in perfect health , not in the slightest degree feverish", "context_len": 15}}}, "mastered": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 20, "context": "To be a Christ Church Senior , with plenty of money and plenty of friends and a head that easily mastered enough learning to make a good showing , left little to be desired , especially when already endowed with a handsome face and a physique that every man envied -- at least , so thought Theodore Harris , and so thought and affirmed the half score of intimate friends who enjoyed many of the good things of this life through his bounty", "context_len": 83}}}, "suggests": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "Burton suggests , with great probability , that the name , as it stands in the text , is a contraction , by a common elliptical process , of the more acceptable , form Zein-ud-din ul Asnam , i", "context_len": 39}}}, "bengaline": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "I have consulted my dressmaker , Madame le Rouge , and she suggests white bengaline , simply made and suitable to a young girl", "context_len": 24}}}, "simplest": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 9, "context": "Yours , my dear Florence , will be the simplest dress in the school , and yet far and away the most elegant , for what we have to aim at now is the extreme simplicity of graceful youth", "context_len": 39}}}, "dishonored": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Were I to see you dishonored , I should never hold up my head again , and , as far as you are concerned , would wash my hands of you forever", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "I hope I may burn up right in this shed to-night if I 'm not telling the truth when I say to you that I never touched a dishonored penny , never harmed a soul , never did a wrong thing knowingly", "context_len": 42}}}, "pleurisy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Nancy prophesied quinsy and pleurisy and rheumatic fever , but the Vaughans were a hardy race , and not even a cold resulted , in spite of her gloomy prognostications", "context_len": 30}}}, "chemist": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 102, "context": "Horrid , affected creatures ! And then to see Carter 's frightened eyes looking at me ! Have n't I seen them in my dreams until I am sick of the sight of them ? And Rosamund wants me to go and see them again ! Why , Carter , poor thing ! would nearly die of fright , and every one of the Leaves would get into their native trees and disappear from view ! Oh , Rosamund is all very well , but she is n't worth that ! I wish I had n't given those leeches back to the chemist", "context_len": 103}}}, "keenly": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 36, "context": "He knew not then that the example before him would nerve him in moments of severest trial , then fast approaching , that the one accusation urged against the Christians , which he had felt most keenly , that of cowardice , was answered in the weak yet valiant boy , who found strength in the name of Christ to endure all for His sake ; neither did his fierce countrymen know that they were preparing a disappointment for the pagan Anlaf , and for all those of his house and lineage", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "In that case , I can affirm with certainty , that you will keenly appreciate any alleviation , however small , of your lot", "context_len": 24}}}, "susceptible": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` Well , it is good of you to trust me , '' said Hughie , softening in spite of himself , for such a bold , handsome , independent girl as Rosamund had never addressed him in such a way before ; and , like all lads , he was susceptible to a girl 's influence", "context_len": 57}}}, "flattery": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` What do you mean by that ? '' said Florence , who was keenly susceptible to flattery", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 60, "context": "But one wet afternoon , finding David alone in the harness-room , where the old man had lighted a fire to make some wonderful decoction of foxgloves wherewith to doctor a cow with a strained leg , they seated themselves on a sack of potatoes in front of the cheerful blaze , and with the aid of a little judicious flattery and coaxing managed to cajole him into a true and circumstantial account of the family ghost", "context_len": 77}}}, "cultivating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` You are the only girl in the school whose friendship is worth cultivating , '' she said ; `` you have go and courage , and some day you will be very handsome ; yes , I feel sure of it", "context_len": 42}}}, "tighter": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "Grasping the rope with both hands , he kept the knot from growing tighter ; then sliding through the noose with the slipperiness of an eel , he dropped to the ground", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` That \u2019 s more than I can tell , '' replied Drake , and drawing our cloaks tighter around our shivering bodies , we rode down the hill , silent and depressed , through the driving rain , towards the town of Godalming", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "The children clung together tighter than ever and held their breath , for there certainly was the most extraordinary noise to be heard -- a kind of mixture between groaning and sighing , with a rattling sound as of dragging chains", "context_len": 41}}}, "sovereigns": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 55, "context": "I often think of all these things , and sometimes talk them over with comrades , and there are few who do not hold , with me , that it would be far better that we should become a purely military Order , like some of the military Orders in the courts of the European sovereigns , than remain as we are , half monk , half soldier -- a mixture that , so far as I can see , accords but badly with either morality or public repute", "context_len": 89}}}, "postal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "In 1870 postal orders , much simpler things , were unknown", "context_len": 11}}}, "stile": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 79, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}}}, "corridors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Did you post my letter ? '' said Florence , who met her in one of the corridors", "context_len": 19}}}, "overburdened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The feeling that her money would relieve her mother contrived to ease her overburdened conscience , and she was more cheerful and happy-looking that evening", "context_len": 25}}}, "reflective": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "The verb forms a considerable number of tenses and incorporates the prefixed object-pronoun , the interrogative and the negative particle ; it has a form for the passive and one for the reflective voice", "context_len": 34}}}, "essays": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The programme for the great day is to be given out also this afternoon , and I believe Sir John intends to read the three Scholarship essays aloud , and the guests present are then to vote with regard to the fortunate winner", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 35, "context": "While it was going on very soft music was to be played on a distant organ ; the competitors were then to go forward and to stand in front of the judges while the three essays were read aloud by no less a person than Sir John himself", "context_len": 48}}}, "tendencies": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 59, "context": "Mention has several times been made of Miss Tucker \u2019 s readiness to give ; and when one recalls the abounding generosity of her father , not to speak of the story of her grandmother on the Boswell side giving away to a beggar the last coin in the house , one can hardly be surprised at the generous tendencies of Charlotte Tucker \u2019 s character", "context_len": 66}}}, "confide": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 27, "context": "She was very happy with Roy , but she was puzzled how to act ; she dared not leave him alone at home , she dared not confide her secret to the neighbours , still less did she dare to take him with her into the streets , for by this time surely his description would be printed up by the police courts , and no rags could dim the beauty of his lovely little face", "context_len": 76}}}, "sympathize": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` I often wonder beyond words why I am given such a very naughty child -- a child who understands me so very little , who can not sympathize with my sorrows and can not understand my griefs , and who contrives to make others miserable", "context_len": 46}}}, "magnetism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Bertha had a rare power of magnetism , and could influence almost any girl who had not sufficiently high principles to withstand her power", "context_len": 24}}}, "withstand": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 13, "context": "At the moment when I began to doubt whether we could much longer withstand the swarms of fresh foes who continued to pour against us , while we were overcome by heat and labour , Sir Gervaise , who had throughout been fighting at my side , offered to swim into the port , to fit out a dozen of the merchant craft there as fire ships , and to tow them round into the midst of the Turkish vessels behind the two galleys that were lying ready for service", "context_len": 90}}}, "attacking": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 51, "context": "Whether it was that he had not seen just what had happened -- as might very well have been the case , since the whole thing seemed to have occurred in the space of a single instant -- and was under the impression that I had suddenly gone mad and was attacking his daughter , I know not , but it is certain that Ama 's scream , and certain hasty words uttered by her , only barely saved me from his fury", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Each recess was piled high with rubble , the result of the stupendous concussion , while a dozen intact machine-guns had been prevented from being brought into action against the attacking infantry", "context_len": 32}}}, "passable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "As the daylight broadened out , however , the light enabled the Christian gunners to aim with far greater accuracy than had before been possible , and , concentrating their fire upon the bridge , across which reinforcements continued to press to the support of the assailants , they succeeded in sinking so many of the boats that it was no longer passable", "context_len": 63}}}, "translation": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 26, "context": "[ Footnote 436 : The whole of the foregoing passage is so confused that I think it well to add here ( l ) a literal translation , as I read it : `` So the Vizier , yea , indeed , he marvelled at the greatness of that wealth more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more when he saw the Sultan that he was satisfied with ( or accepted of ) the bride-gift and the dowry ; however , it was not possible to him that he should gainsay the truth and should say to the Sultan , 'He is not worthy ; ' only , he practised with a device upon the Sultan so he should", "context_len": 128}}}, "reassembling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Dinner passed , and the moment for the reassembling of the school for afternoon work arrived", "context_len": 16}}}, "communication": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 51, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "effective": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 50, "context": "To be sure , the catherine-wheel stuck , and utterly refused to turn , in spite of all pokings and proddings , and the rockets only fizzled off near the ground , instead of shooting up as they ought to have done ; but the squibs and crackers were quite effective , and a train of gunpowder , laid down to represent a fiery serpent , blazed away in fine style", "context_len": 71}}}, "plaited": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 60, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "objections": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 27, "context": "Miss Forster was not sorry to find so plausible an excuse for keeping her pet nephew at the Willows , and the young man himself had no objections , being fuller than ever of ingenious schemes , only he had transferred the seat of his operations to the Abbey , as offering a wider scope in the way of material , and having the further advantage of a number of appreciative assistants", "context_len": 72}}}, "roughness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Bobby , almost equally afflicted , had the added trial of trying to conceal his grief , for he regarded tears as unmanly , and the result was a peculiar shortness and roughness of manner , with frequent rushings away to the barn when his feelings overcame him", "context_len": 48}}}, "capacities": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "He felt within himself powers and capacities qualifying him to take his father 's place , and reap for himself the harvest of glory and power which seemed to await the Grecian armies in the coming campaign", "context_len": 37}}}, "unsigned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "It is to be written without the aid of books of reference , and when finished is to be unsigned and put into a blank envelope", "context_len": 26}}}, "containing": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 7, "context": "So the kind nurse opened the paper containing the biscuits , and spread it on the flat stone on which the children sat ; next she pulled two broad mullein leaves , and put a handful of berries on each , and then having produced the drinking cup she always carried when the children went on an expedition , she asked John where she should find a stream , and one being near at hand as usual , the cup was soon filled and placed beside the other things", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "As he spoke , he pulled out a pail and jug from beneath the head-board of the canoe , -- one containing coffee , the other bread , meat , and two apple pies , which Sally had made the evening before , of some apples Uncle Isaac brought over to them", "context_len": 52}}}, "mathematical": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 61, "context": "`` Further particulars with regard to the competition I will let you know in a few days , but I may as well say now that most of the examination will be viv\u00e2 voce , and will consist of eight questions relating to the study of the French language , eight questions on the study of the German tongue , eight mathematical questions , eight arithmetical questions , eight questions on English History , and eight on English Literature", "context_len": 79}}}, "arithmetical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "`` Further particulars with regard to the competition I will let you know in a few days , but I may as well say now that most of the examination will be viv\u00e2 voce , and will consist of eight questions relating to the study of the French language , eight questions on the study of the German tongue , eight mathematical questions , eight arithmetical questions , eight questions on English History , and eight on English Literature", "context_len": 79}}}, "innermost": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "In addition , a piece of music will be played by each girl and a song sung by each ; but the final and most searching test of all will be the essay , which in itself will contain , I doubt not , the innermost heart of the competitor , for she can not truly write on Heroism without understanding something of what a hero or heroine should be", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` O my mother , '' replied Alaeddin , `` thou speakest justly and deemest that which is true , [ 337 ] and it behoveth me consider all that whereof thou mindest me ; but , O my mother , the love of the Sultan 's daughter , the Lady Bedrulbudour , hath entered into the innermost of my heart ; and there can be no rest for me , except I obtain her", "context_len": 75}}}, "handwriting": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 8, "context": "Why do you mention so often the mere handwriting of your letters ? Do you think that I see anything in them but the kindness of her who has , in the midst of all her engagements , found so much time to devote to me ? My own Mother too -- how very good to me she has been ! I am grateful to her for all her most kind endeavours to set my mind quite at ease on the subject of the poor little Robins", "context_len": 87}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "It seems to have come later into the possession of Charlotte ; and when she went to India it was presented by her to her sister Laura , -- the envelope which contained it having in Charlotte \u2019 s handwriting the following inscription : --", "context_len": 45}}}, "diction": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Burton in his estimate of these tales , which seem to me , even in Caussin de Perceval 's corrector rendering and in his own brilliant and masterly version , very inferior , in style , conduct and diction , to those of `` the old Arabian Nights , '' whilst I think `` Chavis and Cazotte 's Continuation '' utterly unworthy of republication , whether in part or `` in its entirety", "context_len": 73}}}, "testify": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "And the grace of her diction will to a certain extent testify to her ladylike deportment and the entire breadth of her education", "context_len": 23}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 29, "context": "She was to receive the purse which would contain the expenses for one year at Cherry Court School , and the parchment scroll , which through all time would testify to her ability and her triumph , was to be put into her hand", "context_len": 44}}}, "postbag": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Ah , I see you have written a letter to your mother ; shall I put it in the postbag for you ? ''", "context_len": 25}}}, "receptacle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "' And taking a basket as a receptacle for any weeds that might offend his eye , he led the way , Peggy trotting after him with her little tongue wagging freely in a lively account of her latest adventures , and the marvels which her new friend Archie was constructing in Miss Forster 's garden", "context_len": 56}}}, "postmark": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "She would like to have asked where Victor had gone , for she felt some affection for the boy whom she had known since he wore knickerbockers , but she reflected that when letters were received the postmark would reveal what she desired to know", "context_len": 45}}}, "enquiries": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Although I could n't understand all the conversation between those two fellows , I managed to learn that they decided to go to the nearest village and get the police to make enquiries of the whereabouts of a certain Karl Hoeffer -- evidently one of the two men who gave us an unpleasant five minutes", "context_len": 56}}}, "regretted": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 52, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "foolhardy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "She had taken the two sovereigns in a momentary and strong impulse , and had since to a certain extent regretted her foolhardy and wicked deed", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Oh , Louis , indeed you 'd better not , '' said Edna in fear lest he be too foolhardy", "context_len": 21}}}, "consequences": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 33, "context": "This reproof had greatly subdued Robert Rivers ; and as he felt that any display of his jealousy of Gervaise would be resented by the other knights , and might result in serious consequences to himself , he abstained from any exhibition of it when they returned to the auberge , although he could not bring himself to join in the congratulations offered to them", "context_len": 65}}}, "underhand": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "If she could do that , if by means of some underhand action on her part she could win the Scholarship for Florence , Florence would help her in the future , and even if Bertha 's theft was known to her , would never dare to betray her", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "We used to think and say , How she would have admired such a deed ! -- How she would have grieved at such a want of courage ! if anything mean or underhand were done", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 70, "context": "Her light flaxen hair and pale , fair complexion gave her a smooth , shining appearance , and somehow Peggy always thought her manners were smooth and shining too , for she had a way of wriggling out of any little difficulty and unpleasantness , so that the blame rested upon other people , and was always ready to take a mean advantage , or play some of those little underhand tricks which schoolgirls know only too well", "context_len": 78}}}, "purloining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It is well known that it is the first step which costs , and Bertha 's first theft was followed by the purloining of several letters from poor Mrs", "context_len": 29}}}, "financial": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "Thus in May , 1892 , when the financial year of the institution began , they had in hand for their School , Bible , Missionary and Tract funds only 17 8s", "context_len": 32}}}, "transmit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "She hoped soon to have another two pounds to transmit to her by means of a post-office order", "context_len": 18}}}, "remittance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Aylmer the great was thoroughly generous now to Florence , and never a letter arrived which did not contain a money remittance", "context_len": 22}}}, "manuscript": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "Florence slipped softly down to the oak parlor ; having secured a candle and a box of matches , she lit the candle and placed it on her desk , and , taking out a sheet of manuscript paper , she pressed her face on her hands , once again uttered a wild , passionate prayer , and then , dipping the pen in the ink , waited for inspiration", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` 'If you like to leave your manuscript or your drawings , ' I say to them , 'you can do so , but as to the chance of their being accepted , well , look for yourselves", "context_len": 38}}}, "foiled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 52, "context": "An alliance concluded by the Alibamu with the Mobilians did not last long , for in 1708 they arrived with a host of Cheroki , Abika and Kataba Indians , in the vicinity of the French fort on Mobile Bay , where Naniabas , Tohomes and Mobilians had settled , but were foiled in their attack upon the Mobilians through the watchfulness of the tribe and of the French colonists", "context_len": 70}}}, "largely": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 62, "context": "As soon as the battery was completed , gangs of men , accustomed to mining operations , set to work in its rear to drive sloping passages downwards , opening into the face of the great cutting , and through these vast quantities of earth and stones were poured , so as to afford a passage across it , the depth being largely diminished by the great pile of rubbish that had already fallen from the breached wall", "context_len": 78}}}, "echoing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 25, "context": "The storm raged all night : the rain poured down in a driving flood ; the lightning flashed ; the thunder pealed without rest , echoing from one to another of the mountain-peaks in a long , heavy roll ; and the wind blew in furious gusts , shaking even Mr", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "Such stormy -- oh , such stormy weather as we have had , night after night ! There have been such thunder and lightning , and rushing blast , and banging of doors and windows , as if in this great echoing house there were pistol practice", "context_len": 47}}}, "darn": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "This is a very disconnected sort of note , a sort of patchwork , for my ears are as much employed as my hand , and I have every now and then a message to darn in , -- then , O my chilblains ! But I am determined to complain of nothing , for I am so overloaded with blessings", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "People did n't call Joan of Arc and Grace Darling tomboys , though they did other things besides stay at home and darn stockings", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "' I wish we lived up here , and then we should n't need to go to school , and darn stockings , and do all kinds of things we hate ! '", "context_len": 33}}}, "probability": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` I grieve to say , '' he continued , `` that there is but one upon whom our suspicions can now fall with any shadow of probability -- one who is now absent , for I thought it well not to summon him to this council ; and before naming him , I must recall to you , Elfwyn , and to you , Herstan , the solemn oath we have all three taken to disregard all appeals of natural affection , and to ascertain the truth , God being our helper", "context_len": 93}}}, "fidget": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` I wish to goodness , Bertha , '' she said , `` you would n't sit there just like a statue ; you fidget me dreadfully", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Oh , no , it 's me you ought to call cruel , '' said Maggie , in a very doleful voice ; `` you know I am such a fidget , Walter , and I ca n't help it", "context_len": 41}}}, "insinuating": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "This church is enough in itself to keep a man awake if he has any thoughts to think , no matter how dull the sermon might happen to be ; but then I know -- with an insinuating shrug of the shoulders -- some men , and boys too I suppose , never do have any thoughts to think", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Three dollars , did you say ? '' he inquired , in an insinuating and soothing , but strictly business tone", "context_len": 22}}}, "imaginative": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Now , what a pity that is ; but there is a great deal of writing besides the imaginative type", "context_len": 21}}}, "threaded": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` All the willing and the wishing in the world wo n't make the impossible come to pass , '' retorted Bertha , and now she once more threaded her darning-needle and took out another stocking from the basket", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "So it was with extreme caution that Bomba threaded his way through the jungle , his eyes darting from right to left , plumbing the recesses of every thicket , piercing the foliage of every tree", "context_len": 36}}}, "provides": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "It was held in the Town Hall , which proved none too large to hold comfortably the surprising number of curious persons who flocked thither ; for in any small country town that is somewhat removed from larger places those inhabitants who can spare the time to do so seldom fail to attend such an affair , which provides for their more or less uneventful lives a certain sort of entertainment and a topic out of the ordinary for discussion", "context_len": 80}}}, "sacrificing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "And that reminds me , Dick , '' she added , with a sudden access of gravity , `` if you had not been clever enough to save my life when the snake bit me , you would most certainly have been one of the victims ; indeed it was with a view to sacrificing you at the Customs that my father accepted you from the Igbo", "context_len": 67}}}, "worship": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 80, "context": "The Sultan was amazed when he saw Alaeddin in this regal array and beheld his grace and goodliness and the mamelukes standing in attendance upon him in all their comeliness and fair favour ; yea , and his wonderment redoubled when Alaeddin 's mother came up attired in rich and costly raiment , as she were a queen , and he saw twelve slave-girls in her service , preceding her , their hands clasped behind their backs , with all worship and observance", "context_len": 83}}}, "owes": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 3, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "magazine": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 47, "context": "Peggy turned out the drawer by the simple process of emptying it on the table , and disclosed a very miscellaneous collection of literature -- socialist pamphlets , agnostic newspapers , and radical tracts were mixed up with teetotal treatises , missionary leaflets , and the parish magazine", "context_len": 48}}}, "magazines": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 18, "context": "Our defences are all complete ; our recent peace with Egypt has enabled us to fill up our magazines with provisions of all kinds ; the inhabitants of the Island have had ample warning to move into the town , carrying with them everything of value ; so the Turks will obtain but little plunder , and will be able to gather no means of subsistence on the island , as every animal has been driven within the walls , and even the unripe corn has been reaped and brought in", "context_len": 91}}}, "letterpress": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The letterpress is in very able hands , -- a very pretty little fairy-tale for grown-up children , -- that \u2019 s all the rage now , you know , in this enlightened age", "context_len": 34}}}, "awakening": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Yet I could not divest my mind of the conviction that my abrupt awakening had been caused by a cry for help from Ama having reached my ears ; and , seizing my weapons , I set out in search of her", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 54, "context": "And so to all outward appearances there was no change whatever in this particular old bachelor , and the little sermon Marie-Celeste had unconsciously preached on the steamer and the reading of the Story of a Short Life had only given him a glimpse of what a noble thing life might be , without awakening any real determination to make his own life noble", "context_len": 64}}}, "couched": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "True , the Vizier 's son had fared worse than she , for that he was couched in the draught-house ; but he belied [ 401 ] the matter and put away that tribulation from his thought , of his fear lest he should lose his bride and his rank , [ 402 ] more by token that all the folk envied him his lot , for the much increase of honour it brought him , as also for the exceeding beauty and loveliness of the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 88}}}, "smuggle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Florence had that weight of care ever at her heart , and Bertha was wondering by what means she could smuggle the letter to Mrs", "context_len": 25}}}, "wit": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` Then the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How now ? Deemest thou Alaeddin worthy to be bridegroom to my daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour ? Hast thou seen and considered this royal building and all these riches which man 's wit can not comprehend ? '' The Vizier , of his envy of Alaeddin , answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , indeed this palace and its building and all these riches may not be but by means of enchantment , for that no man among men , no , not the mightiest of them in dominion or the greatest in wealth , might avail to upraise and stablish [ the like of ] this building in", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 81, "context": "his mamelukes before him and behind him , whilst the folk all prayed for him and said , `` By Allah , he is deserving ! '' till he came to his house and alighting from his stallion , entered his chamber and rubbed the lamp ; whereupon the genie stood before him and said to him , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` I desire of thee an important service , to wit , that thou build me with all speed a palace before that of the Sultan , which shall be marvellous in its building , never saw kings its like , and be it complete with all its requisites of kingly and magnificent furniture and so forth", "context_len": 128}}}, "ingenuity": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 65, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "writings": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 67, "context": "But through the long dull hours of sewing in Aunt Isham 's dressing-room , her unfailing treasure of consolation was in repeating to herself all the teachings she had received from her grandfather -- words that could never be breathed aloud in Madam Isham 's house ; of liberty , and the rights of the people to representation and civil justice , teachings that were drawn from writings as far asunder as Bishop Taylor 's `` Liberty of Prophesying , '' and Mr", "context_len": 83}}}, "reveal": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 49, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "worlds": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 100, "context": "Then what is that field compared to all England , or England to Europe , or Europe to the whole world ? Neither your little head , nor the wisest man \u2019 s , can imagine how many blossoms and how many insects there are on this great globe , -- it makes one almost giddy to think of it , -- and then to consider that all the world itself is only like a speck in God \u2019 s Creation , that there are said to be eighty millions of fixed stars , each of which has very likely worlds moving round it", "context_len": 104}}}, "talent": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 62, "context": "She looked lovingly at the cows , lazily chewing their cud in the sunshine ; she felt sorry for her step-mother , as she strove to woo slumber to Polly 's wakeful eyes with the same lullaby which had done duty for the whole six ; she even found it in her heart to kiss Lemuel , who , with his ready talent for the unusual , was busily cramming mud paste into the seams of the little trunk which held her worldly all", "context_len": 84}}}, "reveals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "A closer study of this legend reveals many points of importance for the better understanding of Tchikilli 's narrative , as both have evidently been derived from the same original report", "context_len": 31}}}, "disinterested": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "They were not wholly disinterested in this , it is true", "context_len": 11}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Pretty good dive , '' commented a bystander , who was somewhat more disinterested", "context_len": 15}}}, "shockingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "It was shockingly mutilated , and it was evident the man had made a brave defense", "context_len": 16}}}, "remainder": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 83, "context": "Meanwhile Tige , utterly regardless of the commotion he was causing , or to how great an extent he was injuring his previous high reputation , swam steadily along , dragging the half-drowned pig after him , till he got among the birds , when , taking one in his mouth , he swam to the White Bull ; where Ben , who had watched the whole proceeding , relieved him from the harness , when he swam off and brought in the remainder", "context_len": 84}}}, "buzzing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was a buzzing noise in her ears", "context_len": 8}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was much buzzing and whispering among the pupils as they marched to their classrooms -- whispering which the teachers and principal thought best to ignore under the circumstances , for the morning announcement had been an unusual one", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "The head man of the tribe and his followers were soon buzzing about his ears like a swarm of wasps ; but seeing he was not to be frightened by their threats they showed themselves ready enough in the future to supply him with cattle in return for payment", "context_len": 49}}}, "buns": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 87, "context": "I am a working-woman myself and can not afford to do much on my own account , and there 's sights of trouble on every side ; but , if you 'll excuse me , I 'm bound to say I 've given away many a bit of bread since that wet afternoon , just along o ' thinking of you -- an ' how wet an ' cold you was , an ' how hungry you looked ; an ' yet you gave away your hot buns as if you was a princess", "context_len": 94}}}, "commissions": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 91, "context": "Darling -- my own darling Letitia ! Oh , when she looked so happy , did she not see the angels -- or her beloved Father -- or the Bedwells and old Rodman whom she had so tended , -- perhaps all coming to welcome her , -- or the loving Saviour Himself ? I do not grudge her to Him ; but oh , what a wealth of love I have ( apparently ) lost in that one young heart ! Her last parcel of letters to me contained sweet commissions for her poor", "context_len": 95}}}, "queenly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Frances , in her queenly apparel , presented him with a bag which contained a Commission to defend England , -- a business which , one is disposed to think , he had already pretty well accomplished ! The Duke received this offering graciously ; and a day or two later the following playful letter arrived from him to Mr", "context_len": 60}}}, "conscious": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 121, "context": "her small person between the fists of two fighting hobbledehoys , and seized a little boy by the boot who had ventured to climb up the stove-pipe ; she welcomed some of the parents who had begun to arrive for the entertainment , and found them comfortable places on the benches , even nursing one of the babies while its mother went to the rescue of an older child , who was being forcibly held down and sat upon by several of its companions ; and by the time the bell was rung , and the audience requested to take their places , she was almost as hot and pulled about as the Rector himself , but with a feeling of conscious virtue that made up for everything", "context_len": 128}}}, "confectioner": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "There were four big freezers of ice cream , one for delivery at the town confectioner 's , one at the drug store soda fountain , and two for the picnic grounds , where an afternoon celebration was on the programme", "context_len": 41}}}, "creams": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 82, "context": "`` Oh , Bridget , such a kitchen and such a table , and no white cloth on it ! They , the servants did n't seem to care , and they were nice and clean at the Home , and you 're -- well , you have things as nice as in the dining room , and to have things shoveled out of the kettle on your plate or cold on a dish ! Sometimes puddings were good , and the creams and frozen things were clean and nice", "context_len": 90}}}, "retraced": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 43, "context": "Forswear Square Toes ; repent thee of Republicanism , and I 'll stand godfather to thy new life ! So go and get thee wisdom ! '' And the young fellow turned , laughing , back to his work , while Richard sadly retraced his steps to Mrs", "context_len": 48}}}, "execute": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "Why should I thus urge life upon thee , -- why Seek to preserve thee in thine own despite ? O thou art worthy of a nobler cause ; I see in thee one who can nobly dare , Firmly resolve , and boldly execute ; -- And what a bright career before thee lies -- --", "context_len": 57}}}, "reverse": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 110, "context": "The party who had brought me to this remote spot were of an entirely different race from those among whom I now found myself , and the fact that we were making our way toward what was obviously the aristocratic part of the town , coupled with the expressive conversation carried on by the leader of my custodians with three or four individuals who had joined us , led me to surmise -- although of course I did not understand a word of what was said -- that I had been brought up the river as a peace- offering or something of that sort , which conclusion was again the reverse of reassuring", "context_len": 113}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "And by-and-by Lucy forgot herself , and could not help laughing when Rosamund seized her round the waist and whirled her round and round , and taught her to reverse , and instructed her in one or two other matters unknown to Lucy up to the present", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Now here is a brush for you -- no , do n't rub your Prince the right way of the hair -- the reverse way to get out the dandruff and dry dirt", "context_len": 33}}}, "bedeck": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "There were no lack of pretty dresses now for Florence Aylmer to bedeck herself in", "context_len": 15}}}, "donned": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 44, "context": "As for the guilt , it lieth with the accursed one , the surviving [ 661 ] brother of the Maugrabin enchanter , who feigneth himself to be Fatimeh the Recluse ; for lo , he hath slain Fatimeh in her cavern and hath donned her dress and disguised himself after her favour and fashion and is come hither , seeking thy destruction , so he may take vengeance on thee for his brother ; and he it is who taught thy wife to seek this of thee", "context_len": 88}}}, "bangle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "A little ruffle of lace surrounded her girlish throat , and on her arm she slipped a gold bangle , Mrs", "context_len": 21}}}, "latest": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 45, "context": "Had any other child friend along his route , no matter how friendly , questioned him day after day as to how he was getting on with his training for the race ? Had any other among them promised to be on hand at the latest delivery on the afternoon succeeding it , so as to learn just what the issue had been , and at a time when he would be able to stop and tell about it ? Would any one else in the world have thought of suggesting that he should give three short little whistles when he reached the Browns ' , in Remsen Street , so that she should know just how near he was ? Surely no one ; and it was", "context_len": 128}}}, "wildflower": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Yes , she is a distinguished-looking girl , '' said Sir John , just glancing at Florence , and then looking away again , `` but Kitty is my choice ; give me the little wildflower Kitty", "context_len": 38}}}, "respond": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 51, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They draw out one \u2019 s affections , and respond to them", "context_len": 12}}}, "refinement": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 113, "context": "upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant , of course , that I was to die by some exquisite refinement of torture , the nature of which would probably be too dreadful for description", "context_len": 128}}}, "skipped": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hanging on his arm , Edna skipped along to the house to find that it was quite too early to think of sandwiches , though the lamps were lighted in all but the living-room where a cheerful fire made the place light enough", "context_len": 43}}}, "trays": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "[ Footnote 431 : Here szewani ( trays ) instead of , as before , szuhoun ( dishes )", "context_len": 19}}}, "partake": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "As we sat down to partake , of it together , Ama informed me that one of her father 's largest canoes , manned by forty paddlers , and commanded by a chief whom she had recognised , had passed slowly down the river about an hour after I had composed myself to rest , the chief in charge intently scrutinising both banks , as they went , evidently in search of some indication of our presence , and had finally passed out of sight to the southward ; after which Ama had descended and taken a few hours ' rest", "context_len": 101}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Then , and not until then , did she hail me , asking whether the expected pursuers were in sight ; and upon my replying in the negative , she informed me that breakfast was ready , and invited me to come down and partake of it", "context_len": 47}}}, "hostesses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 76, "context": "I am an Historian , for I have been discussing old dates ; a Merchant , for I add plum to plum ; a Lawyer , for I have opened many a case ; a Lord Mayor , for the mace is before me ; and a Navigator , for I am led to seize and gulf ! What if I were to stay here altogether , or set up a new company with my fair hostesses ? Miss Ratty is cut out for a tragedy Queen", "context_len": 87}}}, "wrung": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was a public confession , wrung from a proud and hard man in the moment of his deep humiliation and thankfulness , and doubtless the angels in heaven recording it rejoiced", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Poor wretch ! '' said Harry to himself , as he gazed down at the helpless man , and , loosening and taking off his woollen jersey , he wrung it tightly , getting out as much water as he could , and then drew it on the stony cold figure lying in the washed-up dry brown weed", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Faith 's words had wrung her heart", "context_len": 7}}}, "unladylike": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Oh , come , come , '' said Sir John , truly shocked ; `` let me tell you that is a very unladylike way of speaking and scarcely fair to your aunt , who is doing so much for you", "context_len": 42}}}, "selected": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 34, "context": "Sp\u00e1ne or Spanish gens ; m\u00edngos or chiefs could be chosen from this gens only , and were hereditary in the female line ; sh\u0103-\u00e9 or racoon gens ; second chiefs or headmen were selected from it ; kuishto or tiger gens ; ko-intch\u016bsh or catamount gens ; n\u00e1ni or fish gens ; \u00edssi or deer gens ; hal\u00f3ba or ? gens ; fosh\u00e9 or bird gens ; hunshkon\u00e9 or skunk gens , the least respected of them all", "context_len": 79}}}, "sensation": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 78, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}}}, "encouraging": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 29, "context": "If the captain thought so , however , he succeeded marvelously well in hiding his feelings , trying his best all the time to brace his teammates up , encouraging the equally staunch , chiding a few who showed symptoms of wavering , and entreating one or two who apparently had lost heart", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 20, "context": "Blue Crest perched on the leaf bed and sang a little thrilling song which Barty knew was meant to be encouraging and was also full of good advice if he could have understood it", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "In spite of the pain caused by the rough-and-ready surgery , the father grasped his son \u2019 s hand , encouraging and comforting the boy , as the surgeon probed for the bullet", "context_len": 33}}}, "brimmed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` When [ 409 ] the Sultan heard his daughter 's story , he was sore concerned and his eyes brimmed with tears ; then , sheathing the sword and coming up to her , he kissed her and said to her , `` O my daughter , why didst thou not tell me yesterday , so I might have warded off from thee the torment and affright which have befallen thee this night ? But no matter ; arise and put away from thee this thought , and to-night I will set over thee those who shall guard thee , so there shall not again befall thee that which befell yesternight", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The two wore broad brimmed hats , which partly hid their features , but when the elder spoke , Alden fancied there was something familiar in his voice", "context_len": 28}}}, "dipping": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 67, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "'If only we can manage to cross , for the water is rather full to-day , ' she added , with a lively remembrance of her former dipping", "context_len": 28}}}, "inspiration": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 83, "context": "She laid down her work to wipe her eyes , and , observing two of her sons grappling in fraternal war at the house corner , she arose to cuff each one impartially , exclaiming , `` Ea , muchachos ! You fight before my very eyes , eh ? Take that ! and that ! '' Waddling reluctantly back to her sewing , she saw Lola standing in the white-pillared porch of the big adobe house beyond , and a gleam of inspiration crossed the se\u00f1ora 's dark , fat face", "context_len": 92}}}, "sealed": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "But last night when I took up the Bible to read a chapter before we went to bed , out drops a sealed packet with my name printed on it", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 74, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}}}, "invidious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` It is invidious to speak of a lady who is soon to be one 's guest , '' he said , `` but I also have met Mrs", "context_len": 29}}}, "critical": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 21, "context": "On came the attacking party ; he faced them , and with a coolness that did him credit at such a critical moment he picked out the one that he could most easily capture , and was in the act of hurling the lasso , when , up from the very midst of the hawthorn bushes at the back of the fort , Hal 's voice was heard again", "context_len": 69}}}, "unexpected": {"freq": 89, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 84, "word_index": 61, "context": "Yes , you said something like that to the captain ; and as though she would have given all the world if he had n't , but I did n't mean to be bold really , only I felt so sorry for Chris ; and then she proceeded to tell , as coherently as her emotions would allow , of her unexpected encounter with her old friend , and how dreadful it would have been if they could not have seen anything of each other just because Chris was a second-cabin passenger , and of how she had mustered all her courage and gone straight to the captain to see what could be done about it", "context_len": 116}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "They separated , McGlory rather dazed and skeptical about his prospective fortune , and Matt more confident and highly delighted over his chum \u2019 s unexpected good luck", "context_len": 28}}}, "catechized": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "She catechized Lilian freely about her housekeeping arrangements , hoped Nancy did her duty , and did not neglect to sweep out corners , told Bobby that his irrepressible curls looked girlish , and his hair ought to be cropped close every week , plied Peggy with embarrassing questions on the subject of fine needlework and stocking-darning , and drank four cups of tea in the meantime , with the air of one conferring a favour thereby", "context_len": 77}}}, "extravagant": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 22, "context": "My love , how can you think of sending me another dress for winter ? Do you think me so careless and extravagant as to have worn out the graceful Grey already ? I never take it into a duli ; I keep my faithful Green for such rough work", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "The fable has led to a proverb which prevails in every language in Europe , by which all extravagant and unheard-of exertions to accomplish an end is said to be a piling of Pelion upon Ossa", "context_len": 36}}}, "fifth": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 13, "context": "Indeed , they knew so little about him way back even in the fifth century , that one of the popes , when he made up a list of the saints , said 'he was one of those whose names are justly reverenced among men , but whose actions are known only to God", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 81, "context": "The Spaniards did not relish this , and were going to board the tiny English craft , but again they were forestalled ; for Cochrane with all his men took the Gamo by storm , killed some , and frightened others ; and ere long a marvellous sight was witnessed at Minorca , the great Gamo was brought by the Speedy into the harbour , with over 263 men on board , hale and hearty , whilst Cochrane never had a fifth of that number !", "context_len": 86}}}, "milliner": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "James Ralph borrowed money from him and then split up with him without paying him back , in a quarrel over a pretty milliner", "context_len": 24}}}, "chokes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was good of Sir John to send her that twenty pounds , and yet -- and yet it chokes me to think of it", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "And she was in danger of making so much noise with her hysterical chokes and gurgles that her sister was obliged to go to her and apply salts and sal volatile to quiet her , instead of pouring forth her indignation at her audacity", "context_len": 44}}}, "ordeal": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 41, "context": "But I soon discovered that even this small measure of mercy was to be denied me ; food and drink were indeed to be served out to us -- in order , as I surmised , that we might meet the ordeal in store for us with unabated strength -- but the night was to be passed , as the day had been , secured to the sacrificial post , exposed , naked and helpless , to the elements and to the myriad insect plagues that attacked us unceasingly", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "You went through that ordeal very well , let me tell you", "context_len": 12}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "They were dipped into the rain-water barrel by means of a rope and pulley , and they were cast from `` the terrible height into the awful chasm , '' which ordeal consisted merely in being pushed down a space of about three feet , upon some hay , but being blindfolded was supposed to make up for the difference", "context_len": 60}}}, "greatness": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 60, "context": "`` So the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? He that can in so short a time avail unto riches like these , is he not worthy to be the Sultan 's son-in-law and to have his daughter to bride ? '' Now the Vizier marvelled at the greatness of these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about", "context_len": 128}}}, "composing": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "I got him into bed with some difficulty , and sat by him , after giving him a composing draught -- for I never travel without a few simples at hand , in case of sickness amongst those to whom I minister", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 102, "context": "People knew the time of their passing , and looked out for them daily ; kindly women would come to cottage doorways to nod and smile ; the inn-keeper at the Halfway Arms set his clock by their arrival ; they had struck up quite a friendship with the postman , two milkmen , and the driver of a fish-cart , while the old man who broke stones by the roadside always nodded and gave them 'Good-morning , ' and fired a facetious remark or two after them , which the children imagined he must relieve the monotony of his work by composing during the intervals of their coming and going", "context_len": 111}}}, "submit": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 22, "context": "And do you mean to say , Sir Gervaise , that you , a knight of the Order , are willing to submit to the indignity of being treated as a slave ? To keep up the disguise long enough to be taken into the confidence of the plotters , you might have to stay there for some time ; and if the prison officials believe you to be but an ordinary slave , you will be put to work either on the walls or in one of the galleys", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "If she won the Scholarship all would indeed be well ; but if she failed she could at least be good , she could at least submit", "context_len": 27}}}, "prim": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "Miss Forster was a little elderly lady , with a neat , bird-like appearance , and a brisk , cheerful manner , who seemed to match the prim , square house with its green door and brass knocker , and white sun-blinds over the windows", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 45, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "excitable": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "The young express agent well knew the erratic temperment of his singular friend , but Baker had been so placid and natural up to the present moment , and this excitable outburst was so vivid and unaccountable , that Bart felt sure that there was some important reason for the same", "context_len": 51}}}, "decorous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "Of course , you are only a child yet , but I trust if I have the care of you , which I fully expect to have -- for it is scarcely likely you will for a single moment win this ridiculous Scholarship -- I trust that I shall send you out to your father a well-mannered and decorous woman", "context_len": 60}}}, "abhorrent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "I have the greatest dislike to the manners of the present day , and the new sort of girl who is growing up so rapidly in our midst is thoroughly abhorrent to me", "context_len": 33}}}, "lass": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "The burden of this house , and with so delicate a mother as your good Ma , must be heavy upon a young lass like you , but Albert tells me -- but there ! I wo n't say any more just now , for you 'll blush , and I do n't want you to blush , and I do n't want to encourage those hopes that may never be realised", "context_len": 72}}}, "exaggerated": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "'s report was an exaggerated one as to the number of missiles thrown into the mouth of the tunnel , the fact that the defenders were still able to offer resistance was a perplexing problem", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "This was not , however , so impious as it would at first view seem , for the gods whom the ancients worshiped were , in fact , only deifications of old heroes and kings who had lived in early times , and had acquired a reputation for supernatural powers by the fame of their exploits , exaggerated in descending by tradition in superstitious times", "context_len": 65}}}, "attained": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 29, "context": "On the other hand , he felt perfectly confident that the city could be successfully defended , and that at a cost of life far less than would be attained by a victory in the open field , while the blow that would be inflicted upon the prestige and power of the enemy , by being ignominiously compelled to retire to their ships , after the failure of all their attacks , would be as great as if their army had been defeated in the field", "context_len": 86}}}, "richness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "From all this it becomes evident , that the Creek verb surpasses in its large power of polysynthesis the Algonkin , Dakota and Kalapuya verb , and in the richness of its forms approaches closely to the Iroquois verb , which is poorer in tenses , but has an impersonal conjugation and fourteen persons to each tense of the finite verb", "context_len": 61}}}, "scrimped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "whatsoever": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 75, "context": "If it is possible that any little friend of these stories of mine has never happened to have read them , then let me urge you at once to give Aunt Bess or Uncle Jack no rest till both are in your keeping , with your name written very legibly across the fly-leaf of each , so that you can keep them for your very own till you 've no more use for any books whatsoever", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 109, "context": "At last , when he felt that he must not delay another moment , he took leave of her , saying as he went , Well , as usual you have set me thinking , my little friend , but as though he were grateful for the same ; and Marie-Celeste , turning back to finish the copying of the Prince Imperial 's prayer , wondered in her practical little way if anything would come of the thinking , and if so , if she would ever happen to hear what it was ; and yet at the same time not a little sceptical as to any tangible result whatsoever", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "His mother had begged him to promise to do whatsoever she asked , and he had hesitated , wishing to know first what it was that she wanted", "context_len": 28}}}, "bewildering": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The long , bewildering , beautiful day was over and the three candidates for the coming competition were being dressed for the occasion", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "He made an effort again and again , but each time it was weaker , and at last , with the noise of many waters in his ears , and a bewildering rush of memories through his brain , all seemed to be growing very dark around him , and then he knew no more", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "Her three cousins -- Gwendolyn , Russell , and Belle -- had greeted her cordially as soon as the train drew up in a station which , for size and grandeur , surpassed her wildest dreams , and then escorted her between a bewildering panorama of flashing lights , brilliant shop windows , swiftly moving cars , and people in an endless stream to another depot , for her Uncle Robert resided in the suburbs", "context_len": 75}}}, "mandate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "The very beautiful dress which Aunt Susan had purchased for Florence in London was , after all , not to be used on this occasion , for Sir John had given forth his mandate that each of the three candidates was to be dressed exactly alike , and as this was his supreme wish he further said that he himself would purchase the dresses for the occasion", "context_len": 67}}}, "cashmere": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "More than once on her way to school that first day she looked at the breadths of her scarlet cashmere with a gratified eye ; and catching her at this , Ana Vigil had sighed disapprovingly , saying , `` It is too good for every day -- that dress", "context_len": 50}}}, "swansdown": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The material was the finest white cashmere edged with swansdown , and each girl had clasped round her waist a belt of massive silver , also Sir John 's present", "context_len": 30}}}, "unbound": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then his eyes fell upon Pirah , the young daughter of Hondura , who , herself unbound , came running toward him with outstretched arms", "context_len": 25}}}, "fillet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Their hair was unbound and hung down their backs , being kept in its place on the head by a narrow fillet of silver", "context_len": 24}}}, "molded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Nothing could be simpler and yet more graceful than the dress , the long flowing sleeves falling away from the elbow and showing the young molded arms distinctly", "context_len": 28}}}, "classical": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "a keszr , in its classical meaning of 'upper chamber , ' in which sense Lane indeed gives it as synonymous with the Turkish koushk , variant kushk , ) with four-and-twenty estrades ( liwan , a raised recess , generally a square-shaped room , large or small , open on the side facing the main saloon ) , all of it of emeralds and rubies and other jewels , and one estrade its kiosk was not finished", "context_len": 78}}}, "sandals": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 1, "context": "The sandals were anything but comfortable , and her feet hurt dreadfully on the stones , while every gorse-bush she passed seemed to be stretching out spiky fingers to scratch her bare legs ; she was tired after her morning 's adventures on the moors , and the eight miles seemed to lengthen out to an interminable vista , in spite of the way being downhill ; sundry bumps and bruises , which she had never noticed at the time , began to ache now , and the bee-stings on her neck smarted , until she hardly knew how to bear the pain", "context_len": 103}}}, "choicer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The girls wore sandals on their feet , and altogether nothing could be choicer and prettier than the dresses which Sir John had devised for them", "context_len": 26}}}, "devised": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 12, "context": "Many persons have accordingly supposed that the plan of assassinating Philip was devised in Greece ; that Demosthenes was a party to it ; that Pausanias was the agent for carrying it into execution ; and that Demosthenes was so confident of the success of the plot , and exulted so much in this certainty , that he could not resist the temptation of thus anticipating its announcement", "context_len": 68}}}, "attired": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 90, "context": "The big drawing-room had been shut up for many years ; the Queen-Anne chairs and china-cabinets were swathed in wrappers , and the ornaments put away in boxes ; but sometimes the children would steal in and open the shutters to look at the portraits which hung upon the oak-panelled walls -- stately gentlemen with wigs and lace frills , whose eyes seemed to follow you about the room ; haughty dames with powdered hair and patches ; stiff little girls in hoops and mittens , and pretty young ladies attired as shepherdesses or classic goddesses , with cupids and nymphs in the background", "context_len": 104}}}, "laurel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "It is because in the moment of triumph the brow of the young victor -- victress , do n't you say ? no , of course , victor -- will be crowned with a laurel wreath", "context_len": 36}}}, "wreath": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 42, "context": "Archie hung up a Japanese lantern in the doorway , and fixed a Union-Jack on one side , and the star-spangled banner of the United States on the other , and Peggy found enough Michaelmas daisies and white asters to put a wreath all round the railing of the veranda , which rather suggested harvest decorations , but looked very festive all the same", "context_len": 64}}}, "snort": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 13, "context": "He smelled about it for a moment , and then , with a snort of disgust , turned about , and dashing his heels upon it , sent it flying over the cliff , then walked quietly to the sledge , and placed himself ready to be harnessed , with an air which said , 'That can do no more harm", "context_len": 61}}}, "heathenish": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I hate the heathenish sort of dress , '' she exclaimed , `` but if it comes to that , I believe that Catherine Sharston will look just as well with a chaplet of leaves round her head as anyone else in the room", "context_len": 45}}}, "chaplet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` I hate the heathenish sort of dress , '' she exclaimed , `` but if it comes to that , I believe that Catherine Sharston will look just as well with a chaplet of leaves round her head as anyone else in the room", "context_len": 45}}}, "recesses": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 12, "context": "The light shone out in the gathering darkness , and illumined the recesses of the bushes around , and the faces of a large body of men reclining on the bank , or engaged in the task of sharpening their arms while their supper was roasting", "context_len": 46}}}, "dais": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 23, "context": "As Florence slowly , very slowly , as if each step was weighted with lead , crept forward to the front of the dais without any of that look of triumph and pleasure which ought to have marked her face at such a moment , Bertha Keys threw back her own head and allowed her watchful light blue eyes to follow the girl , while a smile of sardonic import curled her lips", "context_len": 73}}}, "judges": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 57, "context": "So he thought , and as he came to the last words his voice rang out clear and full , and when he ceased the applause was great , and Kitty 's eyes shone , although she dared not meet anyone , for it was part of the code of honor amongst the three girls that the judges should not guess who had written each individual essay", "context_len": 67}}}, "crucial": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "There was not to be the slightest indication of who the successful winner was to be until the crucial moment , and the examination from first to last was expected to occupy about an hour and a half", "context_len": 38}}}, "incandescent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "A flood of light from many incandescent burners poured down upon this dais , making it one of dazzling light", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Stores and houses were draped with flags and bunting , while from several electric-light poles men were stringing long wires , with small incandescent bulbs of various colors fastened on at intervals", "context_len": 32}}}, "burners": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "The people are all charcoal burners and woodmen in winter , and I was therefore able to get together some thirty or forty axes and hatchets , which will be ample , with the arms we took from the Moors , to equip the ninety Christians", "context_len": 46}}}, "occupy": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 63, "context": "While she could go with a clear and perfectly easy conscience , able to enjoy herself , and untroubled by doubts , she probably did so without harm to herself , so long as her life was not given to pleasures , \u2019 that is to say , so long as she did not unduly love these things , or allow them to occupy a wrong place in her life", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He is now a knight commander , and will assuredly one day occupy one of the highest posts in the Order", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The Creek Indians or Maskoki proper occupy , in historic times , a central position among the other tribes of their affiliation , and through their influence and physical power , which they attained by forming a comparatively strong and permanent national union , have become the most noteworthy of all the Southern tribes of the United States territories", "context_len": 59}}}, "gallery": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 17, "context": "There was a large oak staircase , with wide , shallow steps , leading to a panelled gallery , where hung swords , and rusty armour , and moth-eaten tapestry , and many an ancient relic of the past ; while in the best bedroom was a great carved four-post bed , hung with faded yellow curtains , where Queen Elizabeth herself was said to have slept in much state for two nights on her journey from Shrewsbury to Wrexham", "context_len": 80}}}, "votes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 21, "context": "To call this loose assemblage of towns and tribes a military democracy , in the sense that the majority of the votes decided a question brought before the people in a manner that was binding for the citizens , is entirely wrong and misleading , for Indians regard their actions subject to their own decisions only , or , at the utmost , to those of their individual gens", "context_len": 69}}}, "professors": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 32, "context": "We 're not all boys , for the father of one of my chums , the fellow who owns the yacht , is with us , so is one of the college professors , and Edna , you will never guess who is one of the party", "context_len": 47}}}, "jury": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 8, "context": "The judges would retire , something like a jury at a court of justice , on hearing the essays , to give their votes for the lucky winner of the Scholarship , and then Sir John was to crown the successful girl with glory", "context_len": 44}}}, "encircle": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "Fanning 's had a wonderfully clinging effect ; it seemed to encircle my fingers something like an octopus , and she pulled me gently towards the corner she had in view , and presently had pinned me there , seating herself well in front of me , so that there was no possible escape", "context_len": 54}}}, "discontent": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 27, "context": "Easter will soon be here , and Archie will come home , and then we shall all have glorious times again ! ' And Peggy 's momentary discontent vanished like snow in sunshine at the enthralling prospect of several new projects which her ingenious friend intended carrying out , and of the picnics , woodland scrambles , and other delights which the holidays would bring in their train", "context_len": 68}}}, "influenced": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 49, "context": "But he went to Rugby , and at Christmas he brought some of his school-fellows back with him , and they paid the most absurd attentions to Florence and Janet , and they snubbed me ; and I suppose Roger , poor dear ! was weak enough to be influenced by them , for he took no notice of me either , so you can just imagine what a bad time I had", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "'You will find the housekeeping comes easier as you grow older , and in the meantime remember you are not only educating yourself , but bringing up the younger ones , who look to you now instead of to me for example , and who will be far more influenced by what you do and what you are than by any amount of good advice you may bestow upon them", "context_len": 70}}}, "vulgarly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "For a wild moment Bertha Keys felt inclined to say , `` It is impossible ; I am engaged with my pupils , and can not give you any of my time , '' but a glance into Florence 's face showed her , as she vulgarly expressed it , `` the fat was in the fire , '' and she had better face the position at once", "context_len": 68}}}, "crisis": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 51, "context": "What did it matter to me whether Albert Fanning paid for his household goods or not ? and how could it concern me what shop he chose to buy them at ? But I felt myself more or less in a trap , and knew the best way to prevent any crisis taking place was to put on an assumed air of absolute indifference , and to take the first possible opportunity of returning home", "context_len": 75}}}, "unfeeling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "I gave you three sovereigns to change into postoffice orders for my mother , and she -- she never had them ; she never got any of my letters , she thought me cold , heartless , unfeeling -- she , my mother , the one I love best in the world", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 87, "context": "At this the hard hearts relented altogether , and Harold rushed out and gave Albert a toss in the air that was very threatening to the eggs in the luncheon basket ; and as soon as he was on terra firma again Marie-Celeste gave him a good hard hug , and both begged his pardon half a dozen times over for ever assuming for a moment that he was too little , and intimated that they felt very small indeed themselves to think they had been so unfeeling as to plan not to include him in the expedition", "context_len": 98}}}, "deny": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 44, "context": "But I make no doubt that the sight of these your loyal neighbours whom you have called in , and the very blessings of the poor folk in the street , are somewhat of a balm to his heart , also I can not deny that those gentlemen '' -- looking over at the officers -- '' have used us very civilly during the day 's ride ; methinks his Majesty finds himself more at ease with them than with those crop-eared parliament men and their preachers", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "I told you the horse was stolen , and the man you got him of stood right there an \u2019 didn \u2019 t deny it , either ! I said it was my father \u2019 s horse , an \u2019 it is !", "context_len": 43}}}, "tat": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "He 'll probably give you tit for tat every time , anyhow", "context_len": 12}}}, "develop": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 57, "context": "When maidens of different antecedents , rank , age , temperament , and -- in minor matters -- opinions , are brought together in closest proximity , in a climate which tries the temper , there is at least a possibility of some slight rubs , which without the soothing ointment brought by the Peacemaker may even develop into sores", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Should the infantry attack develop and be successful his rescue would be merely a question of time", "context_len": 17}}}, "salary": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 26, "context": "If I would consent to take his guilt upon myself , he agreed to pay over to my wife five hundred dollars annually out of his salary of fifteen hundred dollars , and when he inherited his uncle \u2019 s estate , he furthermore agreed to pay over to me twenty thousand dollars", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 48, "context": "Schwitz had promised that his charges should be small , but how he never would give in his bill , the policeman and his wife thinking all the while that it was kindness which kept him from doing so ; how it had taken every cent of his salary to pay the other expenses of illness , and keep the family barely warmed and fed ; of the disappointment of their hopes for Willie for , at least , some time to come ; and finally of the terrible bill which Dr", "context_len": 92}}}, "temperament": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 100, "context": "each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the operation was undertaken", "context_len": 128}}}, "patted": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 10, "context": "She kept a severe eye on the small children , patted them firmly on the back when they choked , and refused to allow them to grab at the cake , regulating their tea according to her own notions of what was good for them , and turning stolen lumps of sugar out of their pockets with the cleverness of a detective", "context_len": 62}}}, "allotted": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 82, "context": "Will you let him sit down here by the helm ? My instructions are that he is not to mingle with the other slaves ; and as there are reasons why it is wished that his coming on board in this manner shall not be known to them , I myself am to take him up to one of the prisons , or at least to hand him over to the officer sent down from that prison to take up the captives allotted to it", "context_len": 85}}}, "violet": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "Very slowly and gradually the storm worked its way toward the zenith , gathering intensity as it rose , and at length -- probably about ten o'clock -- the first drops of rain , hot and heavy , like gouts of blood , began to fall , quickly increasing to a drenching downpour , accompanied by lightning , green , rose-tinted , violet , sun-bright , that lighted up the town until every object , however minute , was as clearly visible as in broad daylight , while the ceaseless crashing of the thunder was unspeakably appalling", "context_len": 97}}}, "brocade": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Quoth the slave , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord ; '' and Alaeddin said to him , `` I will have thee bring me a carpet Of fine brocade , all inwoven with gold , and spread it from my palace to that of the Sultan , so the Lady Bedrulbudour , whenas she cometh hither , may walk thereon and not upon the earth", "context_len": 70}}}, "displaying": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` This same morning -- and my friend Indian will substantiate my statement , for he was there -- a low , ignorant cadet corporal did enter into my room , for inspection , by Zeus , and after generally displaying his ill-manners , he turned to me and conveyed the extraordinary information that , according to rules , forsooth , I must be deprived of the dearest object of my affections , solace of my weary hours , my friend in time of need , my companion in sickness , which through all the trials of adversity has stuck to me closer than a brother , my only joy , my -- -- ''", "context_len": 115}}}, "adopting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 104, "context": "or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing his victims to the tree with spike nails driven through the hands and feet into the tough timber", "context_len": 128}}}, "vulgar": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 12, "context": "So that was the father of Phyllis and Marjorie Norton , that vulgar man with the loud voice and the coarse red face , and those pretty frocks and handsome carriages were all paid for out of the ruin of poor people 's homes ; for the lower parts of Warford had a bad character , and the clergy waged ceaseless war against the terrible curse of drink", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "She saw that you were a hard-hearted , worldly woman , and that I was a weak fool , and that we were both of us vulgar and mean enough to grovel on our knees for her money , and behave ill to her because it was taken from her -- though she behaved herself like a little princess even when she was a beggar", "context_len": 65}}}, "insinuate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "She could talk about Florence and her great abilities from time to time , and gently insinuate little hints with regard to the girl 's unfortunate position and her great kindness in adopting her", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I do n't insinuate , mind you , that any of the fellows tampered with the ironwork , but all the same the bunk collapsed , and our Padre subsided heavily upon poor little Nicholson", "context_len": 35}}}, "undoubted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Thus people would think her a most good-natured woman as well as a very rich one , the aunt of a girl of undoubted genius -- yes , a great deal might follow in the train of such consequences", "context_len": 39}}}, "motives": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 41, "context": "But that 's the way with kings and queens ; they always have to do the things that 's wise , no matter how they may feel about it , do n't they ? for Marie-Celeste , to whom even the motives of royal conduct were of deepest interest , felt one could hardly ask for a more reliable source of information than the Queen 's own mother", "context_len": 68}}}, "applause": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 25, "context": "So he thought , and as he came to the last words his voice rang out clear and full , and when he ceased the applause was great , and Kitty 's eyes shone , although she dared not meet anyone , for it was part of the code of honor amongst the three girls that the judges should not guess who had written each individual essay", "context_len": 67}}}, "reconcile": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "This was quite a new gospel to Peggy , and though she could not altogether reconcile it with her clinging love for the home of so many generations of Vaughans , it did her good in that it gave her a fresh aspect of life , for it is always wise to look at things from another person 's point of view , as well as your own , and she had a great respect for Archie 's opinions", "context_len": 79}}}, "improving": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "I try to attack a few `` improving '' books when I can manage it , and I have begun to read Carlyle 's `` French Revolution '' to Father in the evenings , but I am sorry to say it generally sends him to sleep", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Nothing improving , all entertaining", "context_len": 5}}}, "inimitable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "But most of the guests scarcely looked at Florence , for their eyes were attracted by the sweet expression , the inimitable grace of Kitty Sharston", "context_len": 26}}}, "dewy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "Florence 's cheeks were deeply flushed , her eyes so bright that they looked dark as night ; but Kitty , equally excited , her heart beating , every nerve highly strung , only showed her excitement by a dewy look in the great big grey eyes , and a wild-rose bloom on the delicate cheeks", "context_len": 56}}}, "necks": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 40, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "craned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Necks were craned forward and ears were at attention point", "context_len": 10}}}, "bridled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Aylmer the great bridled and glanced with her cold eyes at the proudest of the county folk , as much as to say , `` There 's genius for you", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "The horse which Alden was to ride for the next station was allowed to wait , saddled and bridled , and ready to start the moment called upon", "context_len": 28}}}, "proudest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Aylmer the great bridled and glanced with her cold eyes at the proudest of the county folk , as much as to say , `` There 's genius for you", "context_len": 30}}}, "scored": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 8, "context": "Aylmer ; and when his own child Mary scored a point , as he expressed it , a gratified flush rose to his old cheeks , and he dropped his eyes , not caring to look at the girl whom he loved best in the world", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Belden would be to do a good turn to somebody and Ted once more scored himself a fool to have seriously considered the thing for a moment", "context_len": 27}}}, "utterances": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "But when to question after question Kitty Sharston gave a correct reply , the furore and excitement in the breasts of several of the spectators rose to the highest pitch , for Kitty 's soft voice , her gentle answers , her correct and lady-like utterances impressed everyone favorably", "context_len": 49}}}, "bolt": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 53, "context": "It seemed expressly formed for the enjoyment of small boys and girls , for as it lay across a corner , you had only to walk up the road to get to the top , then settling yourself firmly with feet straight in front , you let go , and slid like a bolt from an arrow down -- down -- till you found your feet on the road again , and could climb up once more and repeat the performance", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Listen , '' he said , sitting bolt upright ; `` I have got a rattling good plan in my head , but , '' throwing a severe glance in Tommy 's direction , `` there must be no more disobedience , or the whole thing will be spoiled", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Do n't you bolt , '' ordered Buck Tolliver , advancing -- '' come back here , kid", "context_len": 19}}}, "compressed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 50, "context": "Also of another point in her character , stern determination , -- the resolute folding in of her arms and hands , as she paced along a road or up and down a garden , -- drawing herself up to her full height the while , with sparkling eye and compressed lips", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "He was of slight figure , dark mustache , flashing hazel eyes , flowing hair and closely compressed lips , and he sat his steed with perfect grace", "context_len": 28}}}, "disapproving": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Miss Helen Dartmoor sat bolt upright , her lips firmly compressed , and a disapproving expression in her eyes ; but Miss Helen Dartmoor did not count", "context_len": 27}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "Then a tall and enormously stout man , clad in a leopard-skin moucha , and with a handsome leopard- skin cloak on his shoulders , came forth from the palace , leaning upon the shoulders of two other men , and advanced toward the chair which had first been placed in position , into which he subsided heavily , casting a strongly disapproving glance at the second chair as he did so", "context_len": 72}}}, "keener": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "No torturer ever seemed to take a keener pleasure in the throes of his victim , than Edric in the mental agony he kindled in the breast of his unhappy prisoner", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "Harris had said to her more than once , that there 's nothing but sin itself has so many heavy hearts to answer for as thoughtlessness ; and thoughtfulness , next to love , has lightened and brightened more hearts than anything else in the world and Marie-Celeste knew how thoughtless she had been to press home upon Harold in any way a keener sense of his own great loss", "context_len": 70}}}, "van": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 32, "context": "The girls one by one had approached the piano and played each her trial piece and had sung her trial song , and still it seemed to everyone that Kitty led the van ; for her music , although not quite so showy and brilliant as Florence 's , was marked with true musical expression , and her song , a sweet old English ballad , came purely and freely from her young lips", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "During the second round the Maori kept strictly on the defensive , while van Eindhovengen tired himself considerably in making blind and ineffectual rushes at his nimble opponent", "context_len": 28}}}, "ballad": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 63, "context": "`` I wonder , '' he cried , `` if you can remember how , a many years ago , Sir Gyles carried you up to London , and you lay for a week at our house at Highgate ? I had never seen his like ! He seemed to me the very model of the old courtier of the Queen in the ballad ; he was so worshipful an old gentleman , and carried such a train of old servants riding with him", "context_len": 84}}}, "acquitted": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "At Gallipoli he acquitted himself manfully , was mentioned for gallantry in an affair at Quinn 's Post , and was brought back to Alexandria in a hospital ship , with a wound sufficiently dangerous to smash many a man up completely", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Jones \u2019 gate , where twenty boys cheered the acquitted lad , who paused upon the steps to look back at them , his plain face illumined by an expression of joy which made it seem actually comely", "context_len": 38}}}, "warbling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Bateman raised his head and listened with real pleasure as the wild warbling notes of `` Annie Laurie '' sounded through the old hall", "context_len": 24}}}, "draperies": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 60, "context": "He was absent on the third of these little excursions of his , and was surreptitiously amusing his audacious little self by seeing how it seemed to sit in the Oueen 's own stall , when hark ! -- yes , that was going to be The Roseate Hues , and with a bound that came near bringing the royal draperies with him he was out of the stall in a trice and fairly scrambling up the organ stairs", "context_len": 79}}}, "murmurs": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "It was blue over the house ; but in the distance , coming rapidly nearer and nearer , was a terrible black cloud -- a cloud almost as black as ink -- and already there were murmurs in the trees and cawings among the birds , the breeze growing stronger and stronger -- the prelude to a great agitation of nature", "context_len": 61}}}, "originality": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "She was chiefly remarkable for her spring and energy , her originality and cleverness , her wild spirits , and her lofty determination", "context_len": 23}}}, "immature": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was the work of an immature mind , but here and there was a delicate touch which pointed to the possibility of future genius", "context_len": 25}}}, "allusion": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then after a slight allusion to her successive yoke-fellows \u2019 at Batala , she adds brightly : And I look forward for the greater part of 1880 to going side by side with Babu Singha , the converted Hindu Head-master , \u2019 -- with kind mention also of his wife and children", "context_len": 52}}}, "adjusted": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Then , one day of the days , he smote the sand and extracting the figures , set them down after the most approved fashion [ 545 ] and adjusted [ 546 ] them , so he might see and certify himself of the death of Alaeddin and the safe keeping of the lamp under the earth ; and he looked well into [ 547 ] the figures , both mothers and daughters , [ 548 ] but saw not the lamp , whereupon rage overrode him and he smote the sand a second time , that he might certify himself of Alaeddin 's death , but saw him not in the treasure ; whereat he redoubled in wrath , and yet more when it was certified", "context_len": 128}}}, "communicating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "He was , however , now convinced , by the terror which Dymnus had manifested , and by his suicide , that all was true , and he asked Alexander 's pardon for not having taken immediate measures for communicating promptly the information he had received", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "On one side were recesses that formed , as they afterwards discovered , the lower part of the lift-tunnel communicating with the open air", "context_len": 24}}}, "element": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 4, "context": "Fanning was in his element , and was so pleased with his own consummate common sense and his own skill in getting the right things into the right corners , and in showing me what a mind he had for contriving and for making money go as far as possible , that I allowed him to talk to his heart 's content", "context_len": 62}}}, "metaphor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Parable , allegory , and metaphor were the very language in which she thought ; and her thoughts always seemed naturally to clothe themselves in those figures of speech in which the children of the East are wont to express themselves", "context_len": 41}}}, "quotation": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 56, "context": "In Chapter IX , `` these follows have tied me hand and foot '' has been changed to `` these fellows have tied me hand and foot '' ; a missing period has been inserted after `` '' It 'll do as far as it goes , Mosely , '' said Bradley '' ; a superfluous quotation mark has been removed following `` echoed Tom Hadley from the other tree", "context_len": 70}}}, "cull": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "'The busy birds with nice selection cull Soft thistle-down , gray moss , and scattered wool ; Far from each prying eye the nest prepare , Formed of warm moss and lined with softer hair", "context_len": 35}}}, "logical": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "His character was naturally more logical and less high strung than that of his hero ; and as the stern realities of life claimed the attention of the young soldier , the ecstatic glories of his uncle 's visions faded from his mind , his work absorbed and satisfied him , and he forgot to dream of ideal republics , or even of the Celestial City , in the practical interest of helping to conquer and to govern Scotland", "context_len": 79}}}, "acclamations": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "Paul 's Cathedral , amidst tears of joy , and cries which even the sanctity of the place could not wholly restrain , `` God bless King Edmund ! '' The solemn oath of fidelity was administered , and when all was over , with mingled tears and acclamations , those who had met to bury the late king greeted with joy his son and successor", "context_len": 66}}}, "undeserved": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "I am quite sure that in your case your disclaimer is wholly sincere and unaffected ; but take my advice , accept the honours the world may pay you as not undeserved , determining only in your mind that if you deem them excessive , you will at least do all in your power to show that they are not ill bestowed", "context_len": 62}}}, "adjudged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "It was adjudged that the maiden was too young to fix her own fate", "context_len": 14}}}, "universally": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "The questions have been so universally well answered that there would have been a difficulty in giving the prize to any one when all three so very nearly had earned it , were it not for the trial essay ; but the trial essay has removed all doubt", "context_len": 48}}}, "motto": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 37, "context": "Marie-Celeste straightened up a little , as though to show she did not quite approve , and then she replied , with an air of childish dignity that was vastly amusing , Because it was his family motto that helped Leonard ( he 's the boy in the story I spoke about ) ever so much , and that taught him to be cheerful and contented , and it seems to me -- this last very slowly and thoughtfully -- that you are very much like Leonard , only grown up", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It 's queer , said Marie-Celeste , but I believe -- yes , I 'm sure that very motto stands at the head of one of the chapters in my book", "context_len": 31}}}, "breezy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "Hartley 's , Ted has detained her for a moment , to ask some trifling question , and just as she is about to make a reply , Chris , returning from his daily ride into Nuneham for the mail , swings into the room with his breezy , postman-like air , and empties the contents of the little Hartley mail-bag upon the table", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "He was rather a nice-looking boy , with merry brown eyes , a freckled nose , and frizzy chestnut hair , which stood up like a mop all over his head , and he had a particularly jolly , breezy air about him", "context_len": 43}}}, "mary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Mary shook her head and fell back", "context_len": 7}}}, "weighted": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 12, "context": "As Florence slowly , very slowly , as if each step was weighted with lead , crept forward to the front of the dais without any of that look of triumph and pleasure which ought to have marked her face at such a moment , Bertha Keys threw back her own head and allowed her watchful light blue eyes to follow the girl , while a smile of sardonic import curled her lips", "context_len": 73}}}, "sardonic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "As Florence slowly , very slowly , as if each step was weighted with lead , crept forward to the front of the dais without any of that look of triumph and pleasure which ought to have marked her face at such a moment , Bertha Keys threw back her own head and allowed her watchful light blue eyes to follow the girl , while a smile of sardonic import curled her lips", "context_len": 73}}}, "hurrahs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Cheers , acclamations , hurrahs , every sort of congratulation filled the air", "context_len": 13}}}, "congratulation": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "Soft-hearted Joe wept at the good news , but it was for joy this time ; and so many people sent kind messages of congratulation , and expressed such pleasure at hearing they were to remain at the Abbey , that the Vaughans were quite surprised to find how popular they were in the neighbourhood", "context_len": 55}}}, "filigree": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "As he spoke he stepped to the table and brought back a small crown of filigree silver", "context_len": 17}}}, "ensuing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "Patient Lilian was struggling to teach Bobby a Scripture lesson , for his form-master had decreed that the names of the books of the Old Testament must be repeated without a slip immediately after prayers on the ensuing Monday morning", "context_len": 40}}}, "intimation": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 51, "context": "The girls cheered as lustily as schoolboys , the band in the corner burst forth with the gay strains of `` See the Conquering Hero Comes , '' and after a brief signal from Sir John there was suddenly heard outside the report of a small cannon , which was the intimation that the bonfires were to be lit", "context_len": 59}}}, "exhausting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "The day was over , the long , exciting , exhausting evening had come to an end", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Bomba waited till several days had elapsed and his aged charges had become rested and strengthened after their exhausting experiences before he broached the subject that was nearest his heart", "context_len": 30}}}, "heartiness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Now , that 's the way to talk , '' said I with some heartiness ; `` but if I ever find the stable-door unlocked again I 'll take it for granted that you have changed your opinion about our young friend", "context_len": 43}}}, "conceive": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 69, "context": "Be not concerned for the gift , for these be exceeding precious jewels , and know , O my mother , that I have gone many a time to the market of the jewellers and have seen them sell jewels , that had not an hundredth part [ 341 ] of the beauty of these of ours , at exceeding high prices such as man 's wit can not conceive", "context_len": 70}}}, "collect": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 66, "context": "Peggy , hard at work behind the scenes , put her eye to a hole in the curtain , and surveyed the prospect below , where children big and little were engaged in jumping over the forms , chasing each other round the stove , and generally acting more like monkeys at the Zoo than civilized human beings , while the Rector strove in vain to collect them at the empty end of the room to play games , and the curate , a shy young man fresh from Oxford , looked as if he would have preferred to wrestle with a consignment of heathens from the Cannibal Islands", "context_len": 109}}}, "minced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Aylmer the less , as the mother of the heroine , minced about with her head in the air , so elated , so excited , so carried out of herself , that not the grandest county lady present had power to awe her", "context_len": 44}}}, "grandest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "In fact , it is probable that Alexander 's slaughter of the Persian army at Arbela , and subsequent spoliation of Susa , constitute , taken together , the most gigantic case of murder and robbery which was ever committed by man ; so that , in performing these deeds , the great hero attained at last to the glory of having perpetrated the grandest and most imposing of all human crimes", "context_len": 72}}}, "henceforward": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Aylmer the less , for was it possible that Susan really meant to take her child from her altogether ? Was Florence henceforward to be considered by the world as the daughter of Mrs", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "O joy for England ! the day of her captivity was turned ; henceforward she might hope", "context_len": 17}}}, "forefront": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Yet his desire to be in the forefront of the attack was so great that he would not take in any sail on The Victory , and thus rendered it impossible for the other vessels to obey his orders", "context_len": 39}}}, "grudged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` As if I grudged the Scholarship to your niece ; far from that , '' he answered ; `` she is a remarkable girl ; any girl who could write that essay possesses genius", "context_len": 35}}}, "proportioned": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "She glanced at it for a moment in a puzzled way , a solitary young figure , tall and well proportioned , a head of dark hair , eyes very bright , a face somewhat pale now from excessive emotion , pathetic lines round the mouth", "context_len": 46}}}, "separates": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "It is a pelting day , and no other conveyance seems likely to pass ; while the artist is plainly unable to walk the distance which separates Grimhaggard Hall from the next town", "context_len": 33}}}, "decoration": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 57, "context": "At this wonder took her ; [ 384 ] he went to an oilman 's shop there open and buying oil of him , said to him , `` [ I conjure thee ] by thy life , O uncle , tell me what is toward to-day in this city , that the folk are making this decoration and the markets [ are shut ] and the houses all adorned and the troops drawn up in state ? '' Quoth he , `` O woman , methinketh thou art a stranger and art not of this city", "context_len": 97}}}, "meditations": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "Lady Jane was in the midst of her meditations , and a more confused , distracted poor woman it would be difficult to find in the length and breadth of the land , when suddenly she heard a step in the hall , a frank young voice -- not Irene 's , but bright and young and full of courage -- and the next instant Rosamund Cunliffe entered the room", "context_len": 70}}}, "insistent": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Now I know that my Mamsie is sick and you 're keeping it from me , '' cried Joel , in a loud , insistent voice , `` and I shall go and find her ; so there , Polly Pepper", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The members seemed to take copious notes in pocket-books , and asked occasional questions , one literary-looking lady , in spectacles and a large black hat with nodding feathers , being particularly insistent , and volunteering so much information that she threatened to usurp the place of the lecturer , and had to be gently suppressed , while an old gentleman distributed pamphlets broadcast , declaring he had had them specially printed for the occasion", "context_len": 75}}}, "comprehend": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` Then the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How now ? Deemest thou Alaeddin worthy to be bridegroom to my daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour ? Hast thou seen and considered this royal building and all these riches which man 's wit can not comprehend ? '' The Vizier , of his envy of Alaeddin , answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , indeed this palace and its building and all these riches may not be but by means of enchantment , for that no man among men , no , not the mightiest of them in dominion or the greatest in wealth , might avail to upraise and stablish [ the like of ] this building in", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The only thing he could not quite comprehend was , why they had thought it worth while to imprison him , when murder would seem the more convenient mode of removing an unpleasant witness", "context_len": 34}}}, "sacrificed": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 74, "context": "' Bobby took to carrying about an old horse-pistol which he had found in the lumber-room , and saying 'Oddsbodkins ' on all occasions , and Peggy put on such a general air of melodrama that it seemed scarcely possible for her to speak in plain prose ; Archie was impresario , stage-manager , scene-shifter , dresser , maker-up , and principal actor all combined , while Lilian waxed so enthusiastic that she even sacrificed the feathers out of her last summer 's best hat to adorn the slouched head-gear considered necessary for a due representation of Romeo", "context_len": 98}}}, "meanest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` The Sultan was perplexed at this her claim , more by token that he saw her in poor case , as she were the meanest of the folk ; but the present which she had made him was exceeding magnificent [ and indeed ] beyond price ; [ 420 ] so he turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How deemest thou ? What shall we do ? [ 421 ] It is true I gave her my word , but meseemeth they are poor folk and not of the chiefs of the people", "context_len": 98}}}, "monitor": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "Now , the conscience of Kitty and the conscience of Mary would have risen up in hot protest , and the temptation would not have been a temptation to them , but it was to you because of the poor health of your little monitor", "context_len": 45}}}, "shunted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "There was more discussion , and , if it did nothing more , it served a good turn , for it shunted the thoughts of the lads into new channels , and they began to feel sleepy", "context_len": 37}}}, "proposal": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 14, "context": "Had not she herself hated Albert 's father for a whole fortnight after his proposal , and had she not been glad , very glad , in the end to creep into his great heart for shelter ? Did she suppose that I also would be glad to creep into Albert the second 's great heart for shelter ? Oh , it was all unbearable", "context_len": 65}}}, "reassure": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "The wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his", "context_len": 128}}}, "weakling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` My dear Florence , I ask in order to reassure you that , sensitive and keen as you think your little inward monitor , it is at best but a poor weakling", "context_len": 33}}}, "sane": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Was I mad to go to him -- to beard the lion in his den ? I did not know ; I only knew that sane or mad , I must do what I had made up my mind to do", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "He knew that the savage beasts of the jungle , like the savage men of the jungle , had fear of all that was not sane and shunned it", "context_len": 29}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It could not be true , it was not even sane to expect that he might walk the quarter-deck of the Sea Witch , a bigger , finer ship than he had ever been master of in his prime", "context_len": 39}}}, "catastrophe": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 32, "context": "But just then , the thoughts of every one in the house except mother ( and the news was carefully kept from her ) , were full of a great and terrible catastrophe , and even I forgot all about the Duchess , for one of our largest Orient liners had foundered on some sunken rocks not far from Port Adelaide , off the coast of South Australia , and there had been a terrific shipwreck , and almost every one on board was drowned", "context_len": 85}}}, "sounding": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 8, "context": "Aunt Patty 's loud , angry voice seemed sounding in her ears , uttering the most violent threats , and she thought of the old lady herself almost as if she had been some terrible monster , ready to tear in pieces her own poor frightened little self , clinging about nurse 's neck", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "Could those odd sounding signals which the single warrior sent forward from his perch on the rocks have had anything to do with it ? Did they cause the forbearance of his comrades farther up the gorge ? That such should be the case seemed incredible , but days afterward Alden submitted the question to Shagbark", "context_len": 56}}}, "eyeballs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Then Florence Aylmer fell on her knees , and the tears which all this time had lain like a dead weight against her eyeballs , were loosened , and she sobbed as she had never sobbed before in all her life", "context_len": 41}}}, "loosened": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 77, "context": "It was in imagination , but his mental calculation was not far wrong , for within a few yards of where it might be expected , and not ten from where he was now sculling , he saw something roll up as it were to the surface , there was a gleam of white in the sunlit water , and then it was disappearing again , when , acting upon the impulse of the moment , Harry loosened his hold of the oar , took two steps forward over the thwarts , and leaped into the sea", "context_len": 97}}}, "quieter": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "The voice which kept speaking to her was now quieter ; it ceased to say , `` Refuse the Evil , '' but once again through the silent room she seemed to hear the echo of the words , calm , great , all knowing , `` Choose the Good , choose the Good , '' and then she hastily , very hastily got into her clothes , for it seemed to her that there was nothing else worth while in all the world but the following , the obeying of this voice", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "To do Peggy justice , I think she was right , for though she delighted in outdoor life , she was in no sense a rough or ill-mannered child , and loved pretty things and dainty ways as well as quieter Lilian ; but it was a case of a dog with a bad name , for however indignantly she might remonstrate , people had got into the habit of dubbing her 'tomboy , ' and at that valuation she seemed likely to remain", "context_len": 84}}}, "echo": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 34, "context": "The voice which kept speaking to her was now quieter ; it ceased to say , `` Refuse the Evil , '' but once again through the silent room she seemed to hear the echo of the words , calm , great , all knowing , `` Choose the Good , choose the Good , '' and then she hastily , very hastily got into her clothes , for it seemed to her that there was nothing else worth while in all the world but the following , the obeying of this voice", "context_len": 93}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Again the sound was heard , this time nearer ; but almost immediately it was answered by another faint beating , another , and yet another , till the sea seemed to echo with the rapid roll of drums", "context_len": 39}}}, "obeying": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 89, "context": "The voice which kept speaking to her was now quieter ; it ceased to say , `` Refuse the Evil , '' but once again through the silent room she seemed to hear the echo of the words , calm , great , all knowing , `` Choose the Good , choose the Good , '' and then she hastily , very hastily got into her clothes , for it seemed to her that there was nothing else worth while in all the world but the following , the obeying of this voice", "context_len": 93}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 44, "context": "And when he said further that what was done , was done in the name of the parliament , which was the only lawful authority , for that the generality of the people in England , Scotland , and Ireland had owned it by obeying it , and foreign States by sending embassies to it , they were cut to the heart and desired to silence him", "context_len": 67}}}, "saner": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "On the contrary , I think I have got it back again ; I never felt saner than I do now , but -- but you must help me , and there is no time to lose", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Rogers from his boyhood , and had a strong personal liking for the affectionate and unselfish little man as well as a real admiration for the saner points in his doctrines", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "It seemed as if some healing touch had awakened him from a fevered dream , and recalled him to saner and earlier memories than those that tortured him ; and when he stretched his weary limbs on the unwonted luxury of a bed , the old dreams awoke and bore him company all night long", "context_len": 55}}}, "depict": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}}}, "conference": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 59, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}}}, "upshot": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 74, "context": "Well , now , let Harold , who is pretty well out of the notion of Ted already , come to hear of this last scrape , and , youngster as he is , I believe he 'd throw him over ; and Ted , you know , would n't stand any nonsense of that sort and would tell Harold 'to go his own way and welcome , ' and who knows what the upshot of that would be ! If Ted does not feel he must make an effort to lead a different sort of life for Harold 's sake , he may come to the conclusion that the thing 's not worth trying", "context_len": 115}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The six yearlings made for camp on a run , and there an interesting conference was held with a few more choice spirits , the upshot being that the whole crew set out for barracks again in high spirits , and looking forward to a jolly lark", "context_len": 47}}}, "sadder": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "Yes , rather , drawled the other with cool effrontery ; and knowing he had scotched his man , the doctor turned on his heel , and the two men started off in the direction of the Nuneham station , neither sadder nor wiser , it is to be feared , for the lesson of the night 's experience", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The children knew nothing of this , however , and if mother 's face was sadder than usual , they thought it was the old racking pain in her bones", "context_len": 30}}}, "interview": {"freq": 47, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 70, "context": "There remained only one inmate of the cottage yet to be encountered -- the gentleman about whom Chris had told him , and who had met with the driving accident a few weeks back ; but the gentleman in question bad his own ideas as to the time and place when that dreaded encounter was to be gotten through with , and Donald was not to be favored with an interview that evening", "context_len": 73}}}, "educating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "'You will find the housekeeping comes easier as you grow older , and in the meantime remember you are not only educating yourself , but bringing up the younger ones , who look to you now instead of to me for example , and who will be far more influenced by what you do and what you are than by any amount of good advice you may bestow upon them", "context_len": 70}}}, "claims": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 22, "context": "Will you tell it to us , Marie-Celeste ? And Marie-Celeste , nothing loath , and willing at last to substantiate her claims in the ears of doubting Albert , rested a hand comfortably on either arm of her chair , and commenced , preceding her narration with the request , You will correct me , wo n't you , if you find I do not tell it right ? to which Her Majesty smilingly acceded , first asking Miss Belmore to hand her a little jewelled miniature case from among the other treasures on the table", "context_len": 97}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "Reade , the lawyer , he has what is called `` bought up the mortgages , '' and now he claims all the money back , and I 'm afraid , from what you heard to-day , he will take the Abbey if he does not get it", "context_len": 48}}}, "transcribed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The voluminous pages of Father Cuthbert 's diary for the years of bitter woe and misery which followed can not be fully transcribed ; they would fill a volume themselves , and we must content ourselves with a few extracts , which will probably interest our readers , and carry on the thread of the history to the place where our narrative will again flow free and uninterrupted", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Aristotle had a beautiful copy of Homer 's poems prepared expressly for Alexander , taking great pains to have it transcribed with perfect correctness , and in the most elegant manner", "context_len": 31}}}, "colleague": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "This book has been transcribed for Project Gutenberg by Distributed Proofreaders , in memory of our friend and colleague Emmy * * * Mentor extraordinaire , and so much more * * *", "context_len": 33}}}, "extraordinaire": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "This book has been transcribed for Project Gutenberg by Distributed Proofreaders , in memory of our friend and colleague Emmy * * * Mentor extraordinaire , and so much more * * *", "context_len": 33}}}, "tweezers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Their uncle has sent with the book two butterfly-nets ; a pretty case filled with crooked scissors , tweezers , pincers , and all sorts of sharp steel instruments ; a pretty box , at the bottom of which are little round pieces of cork , glued in rows , with long large-headed pins to run through the butterflies ; and another little box , with a lot of small squares of glass , which are to be put over their wings to keep them open , and prevent them from fluttering and beating about", "context_len": 95}}}, "pincers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Their uncle has sent with the book two butterfly-nets ; a pretty case filled with crooked scissors , tweezers , pincers , and all sorts of sharp steel instruments ; a pretty box , at the bottom of which are little round pieces of cork , glued in rows , with long large-headed pins to run through the butterflies ; and another little box , with a lot of small squares of glass , which are to be put over their wings to keep them open , and prevent them from fluttering and beating about", "context_len": 95}}}, "squares": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 70, "context": "Their uncle has sent with the book two butterfly-nets ; a pretty case filled with crooked scissors , tweezers , pincers , and all sorts of sharp steel instruments ; a pretty box , at the bottom of which are little round pieces of cork , glued in rows , with long large-headed pins to run through the butterflies ; and another little box , with a lot of small squares of glass , which are to be put over their wings to keep them open , and prevent them from fluttering and beating about", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Public squares still exist at the present time in some of the pure-blood towns of the Creek nation , Indian Territory , and the busk , in its ancient , though slightly modified form , is annually celebrated in them", "context_len": 40}}}, "chases": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "In the beautiful book there are pretty coloured pictures of the fine butterflies that they may meet with in their chases , with the names of each kind printed underneath , so that they will know them all when they catch them", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "She chases me out with a stick", "context_len": 7}}}, "specimens": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 68, "context": "There ought to have been more verses , but at this point Father had unfortunately got hold of the paper , and persisted in treating the poem in such a comic light that the indignant authoress had never found the heart to finish it , though the fragment was considered very talented by Aunt Helen , and carefully put away in an old work-box , with the first specimens of Peggy 's handwriting and one of Bobby 's little baby-curls", "context_len": 80}}}, "collections": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "But she did not wish to vex their kind uncle , who was a very learned man , and was always thinking of collections and museums , and science and experiments ; and she did not like to tell him that she would rather her little ones should learn about butterflies from the book with its beautiful pictures , and from watching them flying about , and settling on the flowers in the fields ; and that she did not think it could be a nice play for children to catch and kill the pretty harmless creatures", "context_len": 97}}}, "museums": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "But she did not wish to vex their kind uncle , who was a very learned man , and was always thinking of collections and museums , and science and experiments ; and she did not like to tell him that she would rather her little ones should learn about butterflies from the book with its beautiful pictures , and from watching them flying about , and settling on the flowers in the fields ; and that she did not think it could be a nice play for children to catch and kill the pretty harmless creatures", "context_len": 97}}}, "experiments": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "But she did not wish to vex their kind uncle , who was a very learned man , and was always thinking of collections and museums , and science and experiments ; and she did not like to tell him that she would rather her little ones should learn about butterflies from the book with its beautiful pictures , and from watching them flying about , and settling on the flowers in the fields ; and that she did not think it could be a nice play for children to catch and kill the pretty harmless creatures", "context_len": 97}}}, "corks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Now that we have ended all this War of Words , And fall to drawing corks instead of swords , Now the Pretender may his Captors mock , And view with glee a match without the lock , Let each resentful thought and feeling cease , And General Harmony conclude the Piece !", "context_len": 53}}}, "sportsman": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "But the bears seemed to have warning of the approach of the young Nimrods , for none was in evidence , though there were tracks in the snow , which Bart , enthusiastic sportsman that he was , followed hopefully for some distance , until they disappeared down in a deep gulch , where even he did not think it wise to follow", "context_len": 63}}}, "daisy": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "They are going to the daisy field for their first chase ; it is a beautiful meadow , full of flowers , which the butterflies are very fond of", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 78, "context": "wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he could reach , in most uncourtly fashion", "context_len": 128}}}, "peacocks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Why , you might be a regular king among them , sir , like Solomon in his glory , sitting there in golden jewels among apes and peacocks , leastways currasows , and as many queens as you please", "context_len": 40}}}, "donkeys": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "A small cart , laden with brooms and coarse crockery , was tilted up by the hedge , while a couple of worn-out old donkeys , with clogs tied to their legs , cropped the grass close by , with dejection in their drooping ears", "context_len": 45}}}, "slits": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Minnie finds that there are holes in the ground , hidden under the grass , which make her trip , and there are disagreeable plants growing among the daisies , which sting her legs , and even some that tear little slits in her frock but when one goes a-hunting one must not be particular ; another time she will ask for a pair of gaiters , like Bertie \u2019 s , and a very thick frock", "context_len": 77}}}, "gaiters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "Minnie finds that there are holes in the ground , hidden under the grass , which make her trip , and there are disagreeable plants growing among the daisies , which sting her legs , and even some that tear little slits in her frock but when one goes a-hunting one must not be particular ; another time she will ask for a pair of gaiters , like Bertie \u2019 s , and a very thick frock", "context_len": 77}}}, "inquisitive": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 26, "context": "Really , said Ted at last , able to stand it no longer , and looking pathetically toward Chris , I do n't mean to be inquisitive , but do I understand you that the father of your friend , Marie-Celeste , is coming here to your cottage to recruit from some illness , and that you plan to entertain him by putting him to work on the farm ?", "context_len": 70}}}, "nearby": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "[ Illustration : Bertie holds his net , watching a butterfly that is perched on a plant nearby", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "The three stepped out of the group with alacrity , and followed Fischer over to a seat nearby , while the rest of the gang stood and stared in surprise , speculating as to what this could possibly mean", "context_len": 39}}}, "cleverly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 27, "context": "Peggy liked to see Rollo working with the sheep , the tips of his ears twitching and his faithful brown eyes bright with intelligence , as he cleverly sorted the lambs which Father had pointed out from the rest of the flock , and drove them neatly into the enclosure , coming up whimpering with pride for the praise which he knew awaited his efforts", "context_len": 65}}}, "steals": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "His chief business , answered Gerald , is to get drunk , and when he can \u2019 t raise money any other way he steals it", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Time steals on very quickly when we are fully occupied , and winter seemed to slide into spring , and daffodils to replace the snowdrops in the garden before they had realized the welcome change , and Easter drew near once more , with all its delights of violets , young lambs , and early blossom", "context_len": 56}}}, "mocking": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "But she could never be still very long , and in the midst of her most earnest and fascinating stories she would rush from one end of the field to the other , or turn a somersault , or climb a tree and look down at Rosamund with her laughing , mocking face from the midst of the branches", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "Suddenly they heard a mocking laugh , and , looking up , saw that Pedro had slipped away and was jeering at them from the top of the rocks , and , what was more , he spoke like an Englishman , without any of his former accent", "context_len": 48}}}, "imprudently": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 108, "context": "He ran after the children with little short , sharp barks , worrying at their heels till Aunt Helen declared there would soon not be a whole stocking left in the establishment ; he had a fray at once with Jack , but was much worsted by that worthy , returning to Peggy for protection with his scrap of a tail between his legs ; he tore Bobby 's cricket-ball to pieces , licked the polish off Father 's boots , devoured Aunt Helen 's goloshes , and nestled cosily down to sleep on the top of Nancy 's best Sunday hat , which that luckless damsel had imprudently left in an open band-box on the kitchen settle", "context_len": 118}}}, "rim": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 20, "context": "The German 's bullet , fired at a range of ten yards , had struck the upper part of the rim and deflected upwards , completely penetrating the head-dress , while the wearer escaped with a scalp wound , rendering him unconscious for a quarter of an hour", "context_len": 48}}}, "laughs": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 50, "context": "I wish you could see how gentle these great , strong men are with her : Stephen wo n't let any one but himself carry her to bed , and Lemuel is always ready to push her about in her wheel chair , and talk nonsense to her till she laughs and cries together", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` And they tell him everything , what they have read and studied , and the little troubles and differences and perplexities , and he listens and explains and laughs with them when it is funny , and everything is so nice", "context_len": 42}}}, "browsing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "But the Donkey is not going to waste his time in staring up into the sky , and very wisely goes back to his browsing", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "They had already caught sight of two mustangs which were browsing near the Golden Gulch Hotel , and the sight of these useful animals excited the envy and longing of Bill Mosely", "context_len": 32}}}, "popped": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 55, "context": "But the strain was too much for Peggy , and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say", "context_len": 128}}}, "jumps": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Minnie , who certainly was not expecting anything of the kind , is so taken by surprise that she jumps up on her feet ; and the butterfly , quite as much astonished at what he has done , flies off again as quickly as before", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "'Here goes ! ' cried gallant Bobby at last , leading the way as though he were charging at the enemy , and with four jumps and a long leap he managed to get over dry-shod , with Rollo at his heels , barking loud and furiously", "context_len": 47}}}, "tripping": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "[ Illustration : Bertie tripping and about to fall , net in hand , as Minnie falls to the ground", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "George 's was to love it , and she had soon gained a standing permission to go there whenever she liked ; and that was very often -- so often , in fact , that any one who saw her one lovely May morning tripping down the walk from the Little Castle , as though bent upon some special errand , could easily have guessed her destination", "context_len": 67}}}, "ditch": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 89, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}}}, "dirtied": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "My poor -- -- came particularly badly off , for he not only had three times his raiment dirtied , but his hand rather severely hurt", "context_len": 26}}}, "pops": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "] When it was night , your honour , what sees I through the chink of the kitchen door in the passage but the three young Ladies lugging along a great bundle , and stopping and panting and puffing ? So says I , I \u2019 ll see to the bottom of this , so I pops out suddenly and says , Can I help you , Misses ? \u2019 quite civil like", "context_len": 73}}}, "container": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "[ Illustration : Bertie holds net down while Minnie pulls something out of a container in her hand", "context_len": 18}}}, "exact": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 19, "context": "' And the mothers o ' the babies wor going off , when Jesus said -- I remember the exact words , for we was got to larn 'em off book -- ` Suffer the little children to come unto Me , and do n't forbid 'em ; ' and He tuk them 'ere little babies in His arms and kissed 'em", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "A cadet is forbidden in any way to lay hands upon the plebe ; he is forbidden to give any degrading command or exact any menial service ; and the penalty for breaking these rules is dismissal", "context_len": 37}}}, "operation": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 125, "context": "each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the operation was undertaken", "context_len": 128}}}, "hastening": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 16, "context": "Ca n't you walk faster , Donald ? But meantime , the uncle in question was hastening to put himself in Ted 's way with all possible speed , or rather in Donald 's , which , as it happened , was one and the same thing", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "But we have no right to complain ; you have done your duty and more , and I doubt not that should Mahomet again lay siege to Rhodes , we may count on your hastening here to aid us ?", "context_len": 40}}}, "absorbed": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 9, "context": "Nancy 's evenings for some time past had been absorbed in the construction of a bright heliotrope gown with gilt buttons , and she had walked into Warford on her day out , and spent a month 's wages upon a hat , which was such a marvellous erection of flowers and feathers combined with lace , chiffon , and ribbons , that it was calculated to leave her rivals , like the Queen of Sheba , with no more spirit in them", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "The father was thinking as he watched them that it was worth a great deal to have his only son learn lessons of unselfishness ; to see him more absorbed in the welfare of others than in his own interests", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 46, "context": "His character was naturally more logical and less high strung than that of his hero ; and as the stern realities of life claimed the attention of the young soldier , the ecstatic glories of his uncle 's visions faded from his mind , his work absorbed and satisfied him , and he forgot to dream of ideal republics , or even of the Celestial City , in the practical interest of helping to conquer and to govern Scotland", "context_len": 79}}}, "wantonly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "When they are older she will explain to them that though it is not wrong for learned men to make painful experiments , in order to gain useful knowledge , no one else should wantonly destroy even the least of God \u2019 s creatures", "context_len": 44}}}, "typos": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Minor punctuation typos have been silently corrected", "context_len": 7}}}, "capitals": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 68, "context": "But Lilian had kept her surprise in the background , and it was only when all was ready that she opened a basket , and proudly drew out her masterpiece , a substantial-looking cake , with a cut-paper frill , and white icing on the top , on which in pink sugary letters were inscribed the words : 'Success to Sky Cottage ! ' Certainly some of the capitals were a little staggery , and the ' y ' had strayed into the pink border round the edge , but it was felt to be a triumph of culinary art all the same , and gave quite a grace to the table", "context_len": 112}}}, "paine": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Not to be opened under paine of my displeasure till my sonne attaine the age of XXI years", "context_len": 18}}}, "spearman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Spearman", "context_len": 1}}}, "williams": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Williams , '' said he , `` you will understand is said with all possible politeness and good feeling , but it must be said", "context_len": 25}}}, "liner": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 18, "context": "With voice no louder than if he were talking across a dinner table , the master of the liner waved away the tugs that swarmed out to help him , and with flawless judgment turned the six hundred feet of vibrant steel hull almost in its own length and laid her alongside her pier as delicately as a fisherman handles a dory", "context_len": 62}}}, "promenade": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "The band on the promenade deck was lustily playing `` home again , home again , from a foreign shore , '' as the tugs poked their noses against the black side of the ocean cripple and began to nudge her into her berth", "context_len": 44}}}, "immigrants": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "Down in the steerage quarters hundreds of hysterical immigrants were running to and fro with prayers and screams , but a squad of hard-fisted seamen soon herded them like sheep and threatened death to any who should try to force a way to the boat deck", "context_len": 46}}}, "salty": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "Many wild animals will dig and tear at anything the salty hand of man has passed over , and in the back part of the room I noticed an old table worried up and down its seams by the teeth probably of this little creature who had found food crumbs in the cracks", "context_len": 53}}}, "throngs": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "The Mexicans came in throngs , filling the air with a chorus of `` Caspitas ! '' and `` Carambas ! '' None of them called Lola `` Infanta '' nowadays unless it were in a spirit of friendly pleasantry ; and she herself had lost much of the air which had brought this contemptuous honor upon her childish head", "context_len": 60}}}, "airy": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 55, "context": "They were in the very midst of one of Archie 's most comical adventures , when a shout was heard underneath the tree , and going out on to the veranda , they beheld Nancy struggling timorously up the staircase , her evident anxiety to make some communication overcoming her natural abhorrence of such an airy structure", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` After all , this is a small sort of school , and the rooms are low and by no means airy", "context_len": 22}}}, "spanned": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "The lych-gate was locked , but she toiled over the steep steps that spanned the wall , and wading through the long grass under the yew-trees , found the spot , all covered with flowers , which Lilian had pointed out on Sunday , where 'Mary , the wife of the Reverend Philip Howell , ' slept , 'in sure and certain hope of the resurrection to eternal life , ' and where the stonemason had been already busy with the newly-added line : 'Also Raymond , only son of the above", "context_len": 92}}}, "rugged": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Midway of the bridge was the wheel-house , in which a rugged quartermaster seemed to be playing with the spokes set round a small brass rim while he kept his eyes on the swaying compass card before him", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 97, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Ah , dear , an ' it 's glad enough I am to get ye back , but you 've not grown very fat an ' rugged looking , but them babies do beat all ! They 're quite ginteel one may say , an how they do run and talk ! You 'll have your hands full , I 'm thinkin ' , if they 're goin ' to copy Jack ! ''", "context_len": 75}}}, "quartermaster": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 28, "context": "It would be so much more fun to fool the learned Parson ! And in a minute or two half the party , including the lord high chief quartermaster , was on its way back to barracks to hunt up the new victim , while the rest stayed to resuscitate Indian and to write out a list of questions for the `` mental examination", "context_len": 64}}}, "spokes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Midway of the bridge was the wheel-house , in which a rugged quartermaster seemed to be playing with the spokes set round a small brass rim while he kept his eyes on the swaying compass card before him", "context_len": 38}}}, "ponderous": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 10, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}}}, "rudder": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 21, "context": "As Uncle Isaac had finished his planting , he now came to work with them ; they made the windlass , rudder , and spars ; they also sheathed the bow and stern with boards , where she entered and left the water , so as to diminish the friction somewhat", "context_len": 51}}}, "trimly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Trimly clad in blue , his taut jersey was lettered across the chest with the word CADET", "context_len": 17}}}, "jersey": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 47, "context": "Ted , however , who , in spite of himself , had still considerable regard for appearances , could not resist the temptation of investing -- though Donald urgently protested against such extravagance -- in a suit of clothes , somewhat less conspicuous than the nautical blue jersey and wide-flapping trousers of Donald 's Sunday best , and better adapted to his new calling", "context_len": 64}}}, "lettered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Trimly clad in blue , his taut jersey was lettered across the chest with the word CADET", "context_len": 17}}}, "peevish": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "He had no difficulty in getting half-a-dozen damaged mackerel down in the harbour -- fish that had been torn by the nets ; but he was only just in time , for in the soft grey light he could see the gulls already busy floating down on their ghostly-looking wings in the gloom , uttering a mournful , peevish wail , and carrying off fragments of fish for their morning meal", "context_len": 71}}}, "seafaring": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "David was proud that he had been able to play a part in a deed of real seafaring , such as he had thought was no longer to be found in steamers", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Once when at school he walked twelve miles to get a sight of the sea and a taste of the salt air ; and such was his desire for a seafaring career that although his father was at first very much opposed to the idea , yet when he found how strongly Franklin had set his heart upon a sailor 's life , he got him a place on a war-ship where John took part in the battle of Copenhagen", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` If it will cheer up you and your wife to see us plain seafaring folks , we will accept , with hearty thanks", "context_len": 24}}}, "sonny": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "I \u2019 m sorry for ye , sonny , said Uncle Billy , after an awkward pause ; but I tell ye they aint no use o \u2019 yer goin \u2019 hum ; they don \u2019 t ixpect ye , an \u2019 they don \u2019 t want ye", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Go it sonny , '' cried a man laughing , but she gained on him and took him by the arm with a jerk that nearly capsized them both", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Do n't , do n't , sonny , '' said his mother , laying her hand lovingly on his head", "context_len": 21}}}, "loafing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Young David Downes went slowly down the stairway that led to the boat deck , but his loafing gait was quickened by a strong voice in his ear :", "context_len": 29}}}, "quickened": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 2, "context": "As he quickened his steps , he suddenly realized that he must pass the wretched little tumble-down home of Tige Fletcher , a dirty , crabbed , old recluse , who hated boys because he had been taunted and tormented by them , and who kept two fierce dogs , which were regarded as vicious and dangerous", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "There had been no mother 's quickened ear to mark the warning cough , no mother 's loving eye to see the sunken cheek , no mother 's tender hand to guard her child from damp and cold ; and so the trouble had gone on unheeded and unchecked , till the night spent in the Ice Glen had finished the mischief already at work", "context_len": 65}}}, "landsman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Another soft-baked landsman that has made up his mind to quit us , eh ? ''", "context_len": 16}}}, "cadet": {"freq": 120, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 114, "word_index": 8, "context": "There was more in the life of a cadet than cleaning paint and brass , but was he always going to be in hot water for doing the right thing at the wrong time ? Before he went to sleep he heard the police launch return , and stepped on deck long enough to see four prisoners hauled on to the landing stage", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` This same morning -- and my friend Indian will substantiate my statement , for he was there -- a low , ignorant cadet corporal did enter into my room , for inspection , by Zeus , and after generally displaying his ill-manners , he turned to me and conveyed the extraordinary information that , according to rules , forsooth , I must be deprived of the dearest object of my affections , solace of my weary hours , my friend in time of need , my companion in sickness , which through all the trials of adversity has stuck to me closer than a brother , my only joy , my -- -- ''", "context_len": 115}}}, "commander": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 91, "word_index": 48, "context": "I do not say that every one was not before disposed to obey you in all things , willingly and cheerfully ; but he put it so strongly to them that they had volunteered specially for service in this galley , knowing well who was to be its commander , and the circumstance that the crew was to consist solely of young knights , and had therefore specially pledged their honour so to act that the enterprise should be in all respects a successful one", "context_len": 85}}}, "viewed": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 64, "context": "And most like thou heardest the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to demand her of the Sultan her father in the", "context_len": 128}}}, "despot": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He was the chosen counselor of Japazy as far as that haughty despot permitted any one to advise him", "context_len": 19}}}, "overbearing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "The maid , however , standing stiff and straight , with a towel thrown over her arm and a whisk-broom in hand ready to attack them , looked so very formidable that Marie-Celeste begged Ainslee not to leave them ; and Ainslee , herself appreciating the overbearing self-importance of the maid Babette , was good enough to accede to her request", "context_len": 61}}}, "scurried": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 3, "context": "While the Indians scurried around in the forest to find jaboty eggs , nuts , and fruit for the morning meal , Bomba climbed a great tree on the bank of the river to look back on the trail they had traversed", "context_len": 42}}}, "worthless": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Of course she does n't know all I suffered , what with taking up one worthless claim after another month in and out -- if you mean that ! Why , I actually thought one time of giving up prospecting and settling down to day 's work ! Yes 'm ! It was sure enough that grub-stake you gave me last Fourth of July that brought me my first luck ! I put it right into Pony Gulch and my pick struck free-milling ore the first blow ! Some of the stuff runs ninety dollars to the ton and some higher", "context_len": 102}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Why did n't you stop your engines , you worthless , cowardly scoundrel ? ''", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "You think I have done something unworthy of your god-daughter , but do n't you really , really believe that you would have a much poorer , more contemptible , more worthless sort of god-daughter if she were now to break her bond to the man who saved her mother at considerable expense -- the man who was so good , so kind , so faithful ? Would you really counsel me to break my bond ? ''", "context_len": 78}}}, "rebellion": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 81, "context": "The party thus opposed were inactive and silent while Alexander was in their country , on his first visit to southern Greece ; but after his return they began to contemplate more decisive action , and afterward , when they heard of his having undertaken so desperate an enterprise as going northward with his forces , and actually crossing the Danube , they considered him as so completely out of the way that they grew very courageous , and meditated open rebellion", "context_len": 82}}}, "shift": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 56, "context": "I do not believe I am a real missionary , and I have a theory -- it may not be a very noble one , '' and a soft color suffused her fine face , `` that people who bring children into the world ought to be made to feel the responsibility of them and not shift them on society at large , trusting Providence to take care of them", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "The warmth of the comfortable fire had already begun to thaw his frozen wits , and he made shift to stammer a word of thanks as he fumbled with the spoon", "context_len": 31}}}, "wholesale": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Why did n't I ? I think it was because I was afraid if I made it so wholesale they would decline to come at all , and I thought once they did come it would be easy to persuade them to extend their visit , if it proved to be the best place for Miss Eloise", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "wharves": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "Restlessly haunting the wharves in his leisure hours , David had happened to discover that the famous Black Star Line steamers were compelled by act of Congress to carry a certain number of apprentices or `` cadets , '' to be trained until they were fit for berths as junior officers", "context_len": 51}}}, "cadets": {"freq": 121, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 121, "word_index": 5, "context": "The manner in which the cadets dine has not as yet been described in these pages ; perhaps here is just as good a place as any to picture the historic mess hall where Lee and Grant and Sherman once dined , and toward which on that Saturday afternoon were marching not only the group we have just left , but also the object of all their dislike , the B", "context_len": 71}}}, "berths": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "Restlessly haunting the wharves in his leisure hours , David had happened to discover that the famous Black Star Line steamers were compelled by act of Congress to carry a certain number of apprentices or `` cadets , '' to be trained until they were fit for berths as junior officers", "context_len": 51}}}, "junior": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 4, "context": "Jack Borden , the junior partner in a fairly successful law firm , his wife an averagely nice , sensible body , Miss Florence , her husband 's sister , a bright girl of three and twenty , whose lover was in South America on a five years ' contract , with one year yet to serve", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "Would these young knights , the youngest of whom could be but a year his junior , serve willingly under his orders ? And , above all , would they be able to emulate the deeds of experienced warriors , and would the galley worthily maintain the fame of the Order ?", "context_len": 52}}}, "appointments": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "Albert , who had already become familiar with the appointments of this apartment , stepped at once to the table , near which an elderly lady was sitting , and laying his sailor-hat , nothing loath , atop of a miniature of His Royal Highness the Prince of Wales , announced cavalierly , And -- and now , this is my little American friend , Marie-Celeste", "context_len": 66}}}, "placard": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "A placard suspended within proclaims the fact that it is the smallest church in all England , and beneath it a contribution-box , of dimensions out of all proportion to the surroundings , invites spare shillings for the maintenance of the lonely little parish", "context_len": 44}}}, "marine": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Proceeding to England , and posing as a mercantile marine officer , forged documents and an engaging manner procured him an introduction to Whitehall , with the result that he was given a commission in the Royal Naval Reserve and appointed to an armed merchant cruiser", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "Either the kangaroo objected to the marine 's mistake in the matter of gender or else he was disinclined to be forced to perform , for , as the Corporal grasped the animal 's short fore paws , Panjie let rip with one of his powerful hind legs", "context_len": 48}}}, "superintendent": {"freq": 67, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 67, "word_index": 62, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}}}, "references": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 1, "context": "Constant references as to his fondness for female society , offers to see him home to protect him on the way , tender inquiries as to the state of his eyes and the condition of his ears , filled him with confusion , while large portraits of Jones in the clutches of an imaginary Amazon , executed with schoolboy talent and vigour , adorned the walls of the playground and the palings of the cricket-field", "context_len": 75}}}, "applicants": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Partly because he was the son of a ship-master and partly because of his frank and manly bearing , David Downes was asked for his references , and a few days later he received orders to join the Roanoke over the heads of thirty-odd applicants", "context_len": 45}}}, "completing": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 37, "context": "The two new figures , confronted with each other , in like manner , furnish a fifteenth figure , which , being confronted with the first of the primaries , gives a sixteenth and last figure , completing the series", "context_len": 40}}}, "grievances": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "Davenport that country lanes were unpleasant to drive along in the dark , so drawing on her gloves she routed her groom , a small , depressed-looking boy , out of the harness-room , where he was retailing his grievances to the awe-stricken Joe , and tucked her black skirts safely into her pony-carriage , assuring the children that it should not be long before she looked them up again , as she had promised their aunt to keep an eye on them after they were left alone", "context_len": 88}}}, "threaten": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 74, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}}}, "seaman": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 11, "context": "He was a man of great strength , and a thorough seaman , but he often shipped as cook , because he had higher wages ; and a most excellent cook he was : he was also perfectly honest , and , like most very powerful men , of an excellent disposition ; but he would get drunk whenever the opportunity offered , insomuch that they often put him in jail , and locked him up till the vessel was ready for sea", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` But I always wanted to be the kind of a seaman my father was , '' confided David , grateful for the cheer of this grizzled shipmate", "context_len": 28}}}, "voyages": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 74, "context": "But I hope that if , on our return , we bring back all our slaves in good condition and health , the contrast between their appearance and that of the slaves in most other galleys will be so marked that the admiral may consider it would be well to order awnings to be fixed to all the vessels of the Order , and even to grant to all slaves , when away on voyages , the little indulgences I have given them here", "context_len": 84}}}, "chubby": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 69, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}}}, "mutiny": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 62, "context": "They now took the apples that were left , put them in the canoe , and piling a great heap of slabs on the table , set it on fire , and pulled away by the light of it , Charlie steering , and singing to them an old English song about one Parker , who was hung at the yard-arm for mutiny at the -- --", "context_len": 67}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "But alas ! I for one know to a Certainty that in the little castle there was rebellion almost amounting to mutiny , and that one curly , uncrowned head , that need not have had a care in all the world , was tossing uneasily on its pillow", "context_len": 49}}}, "pretences": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Ah , Scapegrace ! dare you present yourself before me ? Under what false and shameful pretences have you entered this house ?", "context_len": 23}}}, "mushy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` You are a mushy lot , you are", "context_len": 9}}}, "moodily": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` A nice , sociable set they are , '' he said moodily", "context_len": 13}}}, "sunlight": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 49, "context": "There had been quite a spell of sickness in Gorswen at the end of the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The boy -- for he was no more than that , being fourteen years old at the most -- presented a striking picture as he stood there with a ray of sunlight falling athwart his face", "context_len": 36}}}, "royals": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "The rarefaction of the air bothered me a good deal , producing something of the effect of a mirage , and causing the royals of the distant vessel to stand up clear of the horizon as though there were nothing beneath them ; yet , as she rose and fell with the 'scend of the sea , shapeless snow- white blotches appeared and vanished again beneath them occasionally", "context_len": 68}}}, "westward": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 50, "context": "A hurried council of war was held on board the flagship , and here Albemarle made the first great mistake of his life ; for it is reported he held the Dutch so cheaply that he ordered Prince Rupert to take twenty vessels of our fleet and make to the westward to find and engage the French , while he relied on his remaining fifty-four ships to meet the formidable array of Dutchmen", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 34, "context": "The assurance that it was much better to set out in the spring than in the autumn had not a feather \u2019 s weight with them : they would have been glad to head westward in the midst of a December snowstorm", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "He wandered back to the station where he sat down on one of the stools that had been brought outside and gloomily watched the lumbering wagons as they swung slowly westward under the strenuous pull of the oxen", "context_len": 38}}}, "bos'n": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 19, "context": "While he scoured , cleaned , and trotted this way and that at the beck and call of the bos'n , a bebuttoned small boy in a bob-tailed jacket hailed him with this brief message :", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "But he had to watch the bos'n battle hand-over-hand toward the wreck , the line trailing in his wake", "context_len": 19}}}, "vas": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Mein Gott , boy , it vas full up mit splendid kinds of seamanship , what that old bundle of sticks and canvas out yonder never heard about", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` A cadet vas good for somethings if you give him a chance", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "skipper": {"freq": 91, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 89, "word_index": 57, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "She was by this time dead ahead of us ; and the skipper thereupon gave orders to bear away four points and set the larboard studdingsails ; at the same time instructing the look-out to give us instant warning of any change in the stranger 's course or amount of sail set", "context_len": 52}}}, "quitter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "I always knew a hot pilot of his stripe would be a quitter when the real test came", "context_len": 18}}}, "seamanship": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "When the mooring-buoy had been thrown overboard , the rope had caught between the bobstay and the stem , with the result that for the last hour the three raw amateurs in salt-water seamanship had been simply keeping their craft straining at the end of the buoy-rope", "context_len": 47}}}, "scornful": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "With a scornful sniff for what she considered to be great weakness on Drusie 's part , Helen returned to her place , where , in spite of her declaration that she did not intend to play any more , she continued to field", "context_len": 44}}}, "speck": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 75, "context": "Then what is that field compared to all England , or England to Europe , or Europe to the whole world ? Neither your little head , nor the wisest man \u2019 s , can imagine how many blossoms and how many insects there are on this great globe , -- it makes one almost giddy to think of it , -- and then to consider that all the world itself is only like a speck in God \u2019 s Creation , that there are said to be eighty millions of fixed stars , each of which has very likely worlds moving round it", "context_len": 104}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` Ef I said dat , Marse Cally , I must ' a ' been dreamin ' ; I wuz mighty nigh fast asleep when we started back des now , an ' ef you 'd ' a ' lissened right close I speck you 'd ' a ' hearn me a sno'in '", "context_len": 54}}}, "aft": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 21, "context": "Giving her my hand to steady her in entering the crank little craft , I waited until she had seated herself aft and taken the steering paddle in her hand , then , with a powerful push that sent the canoe , stern-first , far out into the rapidly flowing stream , I sprang in over the bows , seized a paddle , and proceeded to force the craft off-shore into the strength of the current", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "Then he returned to Sir John , and joined the other knights , who were all too anxious to learn the latest news -- who had left the island , and who had come to it since they sailed -- to interest themselves in any way with the figure who had gone aft , supposing him , indeed , to be Sir John 's servant , the lantern suspended over the poop giving too feeble a light for his costume to be noted", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "I screamed out , The fire is coming right down on Ben \u2019 s house , and he \u2019 ll be burnt out in a jiffy ! \u2019 We were almost abreast of the harbor , and , hauling the sheets aft , shot her right on to the beach", "context_len": 50}}}, "gale": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 45, "word_index": 2, "context": "Although the gale had entirely abated , there was still a brisk wind blowing , and it was evident to the captain of the corsair that under such circumstances he could outsail the galley that had long been searching for him ; when , therefore , the Santa Barbara came in sight , just as he and his crew had finished stripping the wreck of its contents , the idea had occurred to him to attempt to entice some of the knights to land", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` The canvas was soon made secure , and then , while the wind whipped itself into a gale outside , the boys fell asleep in their warm tent , Fenn 's last thoughts being about a place where he had seen the three turtles", "context_len": 45}}}, "overside": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then as she lay at rest , a rope ladder was dropped overside , and young Arthur Cochran swarmed down it as if he had been the pet monkey saved from the yacht", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "He wanted very much to know what was going on overside in this mysterious fashion , but there was no one in hailing distance , and the watch officer , judging by the noise in the pier , had his hands full", "context_len": 42}}}, "inky": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Rapidly we drifted shorewards , where , in the inky blackness , a line of phosphorescent light denoted the breaking of the boiling water upon an ironbound coast", "context_len": 28}}}, "lee": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 56, "context": "One evening there were signs of a change of weather , and by morning it was blowing a furious gale from the north ; in spite of the efforts of the rowers , the galley narrowly escaped being driven ashore ; but she at last gained the shelter of an island , and anchored under its lee , the slaves being utterly worn out by continuous exertion", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "I remember seeing the lieutenant , the bos \u2019 n , Greville Drake , and about a score of the men , but , huddled on the lee side of the bulwarks , I remained , chilled to the bone and drenched by the drifting spray , hardly conscious of my peril or the presence of my shipmates in distress", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "An hour after midnight our mainsail , though treble-reefed , parted with a report that was heard above the storm , the torn canvas streaming out to lee \u2019 ard like so many whips ; and simultaneously our bowsprit carried away close to the gammonings , with the result that the ship yawed , then shot up into the wind", "context_len": 60}}}, "scrambling": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 70, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "To throw himself down , or go rushing and scrambling over the rocks and every thing else that lay in his way , as he would have done in another place , would not do here , where a false step or a slip would carry him to certain death ; and , in a moment , John Porter had his hand upon his collar , and giving him a rough shake , ordered him to give up the cup", "context_len": 80}}}, "errands": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 4, "context": "Sara was sent on errands in all weathers , and scolded and driven hither and thither ; she was scarcely allowed to speak to Ermengarde and Lottie ; Lavinia sneered at the increasing shabbiness of her clothes ; and the other girls stared curiously at her when she appeared in the schoolroom", "context_len": 52}}}, "combers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "The white-crested combers were towering higher than her sides , and as he started to cross the well deck a wall of green water crashed over the bow , picked him up , and tossed him against a hatch , where he clung bruised and strangling until the torrent passed", "context_len": 50}}}, "towering": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 76, "context": "`` Lord ha \u2019 mercy on me if \u2019 tis not young Master Wentworth ! '' he exclaimed , showing that , though grown in stature , I had not outgrown the appearance of my boyhood ; and when Drake told him of my newly found title , the old man tottered away to let the frequenters of the inn know the news ; whereat we , unwilling to tarry longer , pushed on towards the towering heights of Hindhead", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "In the midst of it all Mafuta sprang from his chair in a towering rage , and , addressing a few remarks to the king which seemed to make the latter feel rather uncomfortable , took himself off to his fetish-house , within which he vanished", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "There lay the carrier , a long , dim shape that grew rapidly huger until the speedboat paused close to her towering side", "context_len": 23}}}, "strangling": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "While the jungle boy struggles desperately to loose that strangling hold , it may be well , for the benefit of those who have not read the preceding volumes of this series , to tell who Bomba was and what had been his adventures up to the time this story opens", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "He started to turn around , clutched at his neck in a strangling kind of a way , tottered , reeled , and plunged forward on his face against a heap of cinders", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It came up sputtering and strangling and started to scramble up the bank", "context_len": 13}}}, "torrent": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 68, "context": "He came down feet foremost , however , and they sauntered off along the road to the old water-mill , where the miller 's man was slinging a sack of flour on to a patient donkey who stood , with drooping ears , eyeing the burden which he must carry up far into the mountains , while his mistress , a little black-eyed Welshwoman , poured forth a torrent of gossip in high-pitched tones", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The rushing torrent bears along The straw on its surface thrown , Sir ; But the rock in its midst stands firm and strong , Although it stand alone , Sir", "context_len": 31}}}, "barnacle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "These have ground and polished off every scrap of seaweed , every barnacle , limpet , and sea-anemone , leaving the rock all smooth and bare , while the boulders lie piled to the east in a heap , where the waves that try to take the rock in flank leap amongst them , and roll them over higher and higher , to come rumbling down as if they were tiny pebbles instead of rounded masses of granite and spar-veined stone a quarter , half , and a hundredweight each", "context_len": 90}}}, "reeling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 51, "context": "It happened to be a local holiday in Cumberland , and as a result here and there they encountered a solitary specimen of humanity prone upon his back or his face , just as it chanced , by the roadside , or , not quite so badly off as that , reeling along to wherever home might be in that apparently houseless region", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Bart felt his brain reeling , but , by a strong effort he held himself together", "context_len": 16}}}, "vigil": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Keep quiet , he may , '' ordered Bart , and though the storm raged , they kept up their vigil half an hour longer", "context_len": 26}}}, "skyline": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Ten minutes later Drake and I were spurring hotly towards York , Felgate , by reason of having but one jack boot and wet clothes , being compelled to stay behind , and before long the massive towers of the Minster showed above the skyline", "context_len": 45}}}, "windward": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Captain Thrasher also clawed his way to the windward side of the bridge and stared hard at the sea", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "But our joy was turned to feelings of dismay when we perceived that two small ships had joined the Dutch men-of-war , and that they were brought to with reduced canvas immediately to windward of us , and were held by stern moorings only , so that their bows were pointed straight at our luckless vessel", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 51, "context": "Then the king shouted something to his women-folk which caused them to scuttle back into the palace like so many rabbits ; and the next moment the two guards who had me in charge marched me off to an empty hut behind the palace -- which was , luckily , to windward of the crucifixion tree , the odour from which therefore did not reach as far as my lodging -- and , having signed to me to enter , mounted guard , one on each side of the door", "context_len": 90}}}, "wreck": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 15, "context": "There were but five or six men to be seen on the deck of the wreck , and these had , as the boat approached , come down to the rocks as if to meet those who came to their aid ; but as the knights leapt out , they threw themselves suddenly upon them with knives and scimitars that had hitherto been concealed beneath their garments , while at the same moment a crowd of men appeared on the deck of the ship , and , leaping down , ran forward with drawn swords", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "People in India can not help thinking a great deal about it , because five minutes \u2019 carelessness may wreck health for life ; yet it is a great matter for us , if possible , to keep from sinking to the languid can not -do-anything point", "context_len": 47}}}, "splintered": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "With a pair of glasses from the rack in the wheel-house , David fancied he could make out some kind of a signal streaming from the splintered stump of a mast", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "The boys found quite a throng around the engine , and they could now see that the cab , on one side , was splintered , and that the forward end of the coach next to the engine , which was the baggage car , was also damaged", "context_len": 48}}}, "founder": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "in the outer quadrangle , with all its panoply of robes of state and globe and sceptre , appears to look a little more chipper than ever and a trifle more conscious of the distinction of being the munificent founder of so glorious an institution", "context_len": 45}}}, "veering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I 've been veering this way and that in my mind , but the things I 've learned about duty in the last year kind of help me to make a good finish of it", "context_len": 36}}}, "hulk": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then for the third and last time the Roanoke began to weave a path around the water-logged hulk , which was so close at hand that the castaways could be counted", "context_len": 31}}}, "astern": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 30, "context": "Hardly had the body of men reached the foremast when the Gannet glided forward and entered the deep waters of the harbour , the two ropes on her quarter trailing astern , and the mob of excited negroes who had manned them were shouting and dancing on the banks of the creek", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 65, "context": "The boys began to feel a kind of awe stealing over them , as the last glimpse of it faded from their sight , and they found themselves rushing into the unknown waste , for they were steering straight out to sea , without compass , or any guide other than to keep before the wind till the daylight should reveal to them the land astern", "context_len": 66}}}, "tanks": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "There were distinct signs that the perpetrators of the outrage had sought to obliterate all traces of their dastardly work : the name of the ship had been scraped off the boat 's bows , her air-tight tanks had been stove in , yet in spite of this precaution the boat still remained awash", "context_len": 54}}}, "valves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The orders he sent below had to do with tanks , valves , pipes , and strainers", "context_len": 17}}}, "strainers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The orders he sent below had to do with tanks , valves , pipes , and strainers", "context_len": 17}}}, "engineer": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 27, "context": "In addition to this he had arranged with a renegade German , known as Maitre Georges , a man of very great ability as an artilleryman and engineer , to desert to the city , and there do all in his power to assist the besiegers , both by affording them information and by giving bad advice to the besieged", "context_len": 60}}}, "pitiful": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 31, "context": "She had lost her hat , and the wind had tossed up her curls , her cheeks were pale and streaked with tears , and her big brown eyes had a pitiful look in them that would have softened a tiger , let alone a man that had half a dozen little ones of his own at home ; while every now and then the great heavy sighs came struggling up , as if she had almost cried her heart out", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "It was a scene Salvator Rosa would have loved to paint : the firelight bringing out in strong relief the huge limbs of the oak trees , the bronzed faces of those dread warriors , which no pitiful or tender feelings ever seemed to visit", "context_len": 45}}}, "smashed": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 38, "context": "The day after the fight , when he was being assisted to take off his uniform , he found that a small pistol which had been put in his pocket without his knowledge was broken , his watch smashed , and his side bruised", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` And I 'm going to sue the railroad company for my smashed foot", "context_len": 14}}}, "wedder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "If the wedder do n't moderate pretty quick , it vas good-by and Davy Jones 's locker for them poor fellers", "context_len": 21}}}, "locker": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 27, "context": "He then identified one of the watches and both of the rings as belonging to him , further stating that the money left by him in his locker was a five dollar bill , a two dollar bill , and change which must have amounted to nearly a dollar and a half", "context_len": 52}}}, "armful": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "One alone lingered to cut some limbs from a hemlock bush , a whole armful of which he brought with him , and while the rest were passing the water from the beach , and pouring on the blazing wood-pile , he was switching out the flames , as they ran towards the beach , with a dexterity that showed he was no novice in fire-fighting", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Papa and Uncle Ruthven each took an armful too ; so , when they were all laid together , there was quite a pile", "context_len": 24}}}, "bunting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "On several side streets , as well as on the main ones , flags and bunting were in evidence , and colored electric lights were being strung", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "He made it a point to pass by the ruins of the old express shed , and he found there what he expected to find -- the missing flag from the switch shanty ; only the rod was bare , the little piece of red bunting having been burned away", "context_len": 50}}}, "halliard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "One by one he bent the bright flags to a halliard ; they crept aloft , broke out of stops , and snapped in the wind", "context_len": 26}}}, "international": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "As to the numbers who flocked to hear some of his addresses they can best be realised by those who have attended an international football match , when 20,000 persons are actually assembled in one field , or at a review , when a like number of people are together", "context_len": 50}}}, "davits": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "The davits were swung inboard , the boat resting on `` chocks '' or hinged pieces of wood shaped to fit the lower strakes of the boat", "context_len": 27}}}, "smash": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 36, "context": "At Gallipoli he acquitted himself manfully , was mentioned for gallantry in an affair at Quinn 's Post , and was brought back to Alexandria in a hospital ship , with a wound sufficiently dangerous to smash many a man up completely", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 94, "context": "I knew exactly or nearly to a nicety , and if I did n't my mother did , what the dinners cost which we ate night after night in your dining-room , and what the furniture must have cost , and what the breakfast cost , and the hundred and one things which were necessary to keep up an establishment of that kind , and I said to the mater , 'Look you here , mater , the incomings are so and so , and the outgoings are so and so , and a smash is inevitable", "context_len": 97}}}, "funnels": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then as the seas began to smash against her weather side , she rolled until it seemed as if her funnels must be jerked out by the roots", "context_len": 28}}}, "arc": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Not very long , '' answered the king ; `` perhaps while the sun has been climbing thus far , '' -- describing with his hand the arc of a circle measuring about seven degrees , or , say , half an hour -- '' I had just finished my breakfast when the pains seized me", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "A drizzling fog filled the harbor , and the arc lights on the pier were no more than vague blobs of sickly yellow", "context_len": 23}}}, "smoother": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The great circle had been half-way covered before the cadet happened to notice that a band of smoother water was stretching to leeward of the steamer , and that as if by a miracle the huge combers were ceasing to break", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The flight leader , Captain Bartlett , took his bombers up to 20,000 , where it was colder but smoother", "context_len": 20}}}, "eddying": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "An eddying gust brought him a strong smell of oil , and he went to the rail and stared down at the sea", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "The river was so distant , so broad and rapid , and its banks were bordered and defended by such ferocious foes , that to cross its eddying tide , and penetrate into the unknown and unexplored regions beyond , leaving the broad , and deep , and rapid stream to cut off the hopes of retreat , implied the possession of extreme self-reliance , courage , and decision", "context_len": 69}}}, "smears": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "I paint pictures , too , sometimes , when Isabella do n't want me , ' confided the juvenile artist ; and to judge from the smears of paint upon her pinafore , she had evidently been pursuing that art with more vigour than discretion", "context_len": 45}}}, "frothing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The Roanoke heaved up her black side until he saw smears of a yellow liquid trickle from several pipes , and spread out over the frothing billows in shimmering sheets", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "On he went down the river , the canoe caught now by one current , now by another , sometimes dipping to one side at so sharp an angle that it seemed it must capsize , then righting itself and dancing on again over the frothing black water -- a frail barrier between Bomba and destruction", "context_len": 56}}}, "ringed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Slowly the Roanoke plunged and rolled on her circular course until she had ringed the wreck with a streak of oily calm", "context_len": 22}}}, "murk": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The gray sky was dusky and the horizon was shutting down nearer and nearer in mist and murk", "context_len": 18}}}, "wallowed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "In a shallow , muddy pool , about thirty feet in front of him , he saw a mass of writhing snakes , gray in color like the mud in which they wallowed", "context_len": 33}}}, "weave": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "But , all the same , their faith in the result was supreme ; for what could a wizard not do who could weave fairy-tales so wonderfully as not to have them seem like fairy-tales at all", "context_len": 37}}}, "sprawled": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 3, "context": "Poor Stanard sat sprawled out on top of the earthworks , where he sat down in amazement and confusion when he discovered that Mark was not on hand ; and there he sat yet , too much amazed and confused to move or say anything", "context_len": 45}}}, "deepening": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "The reign of Ethelred the Unready , from his accession , after the murder of his innocent brother , until the scene depicted in the nineteenth chapter of the tale , was a tragedy ever deepening", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "While waiting , the chauffeur had lighted the gas lamps of the car , and , with the machine again under way , they blazed a golden path through the deepening autumn darkness", "context_len": 33}}}, "onset": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 27, "context": "France and Auvergne , led by Ralph Harcourt , boarded at the bow , the other five langues at the poop ; and so impetuous was their onset that the pirates , who had still scarce recovered from their surprise at being hastily aroused from sleep to repel the attack of the foe who had so suddenly sprung out from the darkness upon them , offered but a feeble resistance", "context_len": 70}}}, "diminished": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then rushing onward , the diminished breakers came to the second protecting streak and their menace was still further lessened", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 63, "context": "As soon as the battery was completed , gangs of men , accustomed to mining operations , set to work in its rear to drive sloping passages downwards , opening into the face of the great cutting , and through these vast quantities of earth and stones were poured , so as to afford a passage across it , the depth being largely diminished by the great pile of rubbish that had already fallen from the breached wall", "context_len": 78}}}, "protecting": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "He had not meant that any one should know it , such a modest little knight was he ; but alas ! the weakness of drowsiness had overtaken the valiant little fellow , and in the disorder thereon attendant the shapely little limb had thrust itself forth from the folds of the protecting kilt , and there was the garter plainly visible to the most casual passer-by", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 41, "context": "It was likely that ere long they might be called upon to stand a siege , but , acquainted as they were with the strength of the place -- its deep and seemingly impassable moat , its massive walls , and protecting towers and bastions -- it had seemed to them that Rhodes was capable of withstanding all assaults , however numerous the foe , however oft repeated the invasion", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "As this seemed the most humane suggestion , Master Dormouse was tucked up in bed once more , and , still protesting , was carried to a sunny bank under an apple-tree , and stowed away under a protecting clump of leaves , where his plaintive voice gradually subsided , and he settled down for five months of oblivion , to ignore the winter frosts and storms until the April sunshine should tempt him out of his lair", "context_len": 78}}}, "menace": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 23, "context": "Although the troops on board were going to fight a common foe -- a foe that victorious would speedily prove more than a menace to Australia , to United South Africa , and to civilization in general -- there was a certain amount of misunderstanding between the Afrikanders and their brothers-in-arms", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "One , consisting of picked warriors under the leadership of Lodo , with Grico as his lieutenant , proceeded toward the Giant Cataract to give battle to the headhunters and remove forever , if possible , that menace to the peaceful tribes of the region", "context_len": 45}}}, "toppled": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 21, "context": "There was a short tug of war ; then a sharp sound of tearing cloth ; and while the gallant defender toppled backwards into the stream , carrying the greater part of the flag with him , Jim fell down on the other side , bearing with him the flagstaff and the fluttering remnant of the Union Jack", "context_len": 58}}}, "washes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "We had a large flat-bottomed boat on the river which washes the monastery garden ; they had placed all the sacred vessels and the treasure of the priory therein , and had sent the novices and lay brethren to seek their safety with the rest in the woods , only the brethren , properly so called , remaining", "context_len": 58}}}, "problems": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Years ago the general editor of this series stated , in various publications , the problems that region offers , and on finding the remarkable legend of Chekilli , translated it and published it , as pointing to a solution of some of the questions involved", "context_len": 46}}}, "baffled": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 96, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "Neram and Ashati dragged him further into the depths of the cavern , while , with a shriek of rage , the baffled monkey , seeking to follow , dashed instead into the rock surface at the side", "context_len": 38}}}, "leaden": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "It is not remarkable that he hesitated over this , or that as the shadows once more thickened in that room he sat for a long time on his trunk , his elbows on his knees and his chin on his hands , gazing blankly at the one leaden window", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "The days now dragged themselves away upon leaden feet , yet -- apparent paradox -- with frightful rapidity ; for I now no longer had a household to attend to my wants ; my meals were brought to me with unfailing regularity by my guards , but they had apparently been forbidden to communicate with me , for not a word could I get out of them , good , bad , or indifferent", "context_len": 74}}}, "ruddy": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "The first was a lean , spare man , with gray side-whiskers and a slight stoop , whose rather sporting attire accorded ill with his sharp , keen face ; while the second , the one on the better horse , was stout and ruddy of countenance , a man who looked as though he would be more at home in a bar-parlour than in a drawing-room , and who held himself with a complacent air , as one who is satisfied both with the world in general and himself in particular", "context_len": 92}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 67, "context": "While we waited in suspense , we saw a fierce light flash forth from the hall , and perceived that , having plundered it of all that was portable , they had fired it in many places at once ; and while we looked , we saw our own once happy home share the same fate , and emulate the hall in sending forth its volume of ruddy flame towards the skies", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Oh ! I did hope that he would soon get brown and ruddy like the other kiddies -- my own dear little lad , and I ventured to take a step into the room and stretched my head out longingly toward him", "context_len": 42}}}, "amidships": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "Her sides were painted in red , white , and blue horizontal stripes , while amidships her name was displayed in letters six feet in height -- in conformity with an arbitrary regulation made by an unscrupulous nation whose U-boats did not hesitate to torpedo at sight , despite the distinguishing marks of neutral craft", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Finally , when he could stand the suspense no longer , he tiptoed across the deck , moved aft until he was amidships between the saloon deck houses , and crouched on a bench against the rail", "context_len": 37}}}, "shoved": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 68, "context": "When the fire had entirely died down , and I had satisfied myself that there was nothing left of poor Ama to be desecrated by fang of beast or beak of bird , I sorrowfully retired from the fatal spot , carrying the leopard 's skin with me , and making my way with some difficulty to the place where the canoe lay concealed , sprang in and shoved off", "context_len": 70}}}, "automatic": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "But I think the crowning achievement was an automatic currying-brush , which was to be wound up and fixed on to the horse 's back , and was to do the work 'in just half the time old Fleming takes pottering over it", "context_len": 43}}}, "shattered": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 40, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 34, "context": "This much I had heard from report , and now , in spite of my cramped position , and faintness from want of food , I could not help looking with interest on the shattered walls , which still showed the black marks of the powder , though now , after a lapse of twenty years , their barrenness was beginning to be hidden by a kindly garb of ivy", "context_len": 70}}}, "seamen": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 53, "context": "The strength he had shown in the struggle on shore , and the manner in which he had hurled , bound as he was , two of their comrades to the deck , had won for him the respect of his captors , and he was therefore allowed privileges not granted to the seamen of the vessel that had had the ill fortune to be cast on shore so close to the spot where the corsair was hiding", "context_len": 78}}}, "madly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 93, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}}}, "foundering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "As the liner 's life-boat drew nearer the foundering hulk , the men at the oars could see how fearful was the plight of the handful of survivors", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "They actually put the ship up for auction as she was foundering", "context_len": 12}}}, "survivors": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 23, "context": "At least half of the raiders had been incapacitated , and among the wounded had been Nascanora himself , and all the unwounded survivors were at that moment rushing pell-mell through the jungle , frantically eager to put as many miles as possible between themselves and the cabin , where the white man \u2019 s magic had been capped by the calling of the jungle beasts to help", "context_len": 68}}}, "seaweed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "These have ground and polished off every scrap of seaweed , every barnacle , limpet , and sea-anemone , leaving the rock all smooth and bare , while the boulders lie piled to the east in a heap , where the waves that try to take the rock in flank leap amongst them , and roll them over higher and higher , to come rumbling down as if they were tiny pebbles instead of rounded masses of granite and spar-veined stone a quarter , half , and a hundredweight each", "context_len": 90}}}, "jagged": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 46, "context": "Close to my feet was a large rabbit hole , and knowing from past experience that these animals frequently throw up flints and other stones from their burrows , I plunged my hand into the newly excavated earth , and to my delight found a large jagged flint , and soon after my armoury consisted of five good-sized stones", "context_len": 59}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Where all before was smooth heaving water all was now rushing foam , as the broken wave raced back , as if to pass between two narrow jagged pieces of rock rising up like a gateway some fifty yards away before the next wave came in", "context_len": 46}}}, "spars": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 7, "context": "From this land Ben had hauled his spars , and logs for boards , leaving the tops of the trees and all the brush ; in addition to this there was left quite a growth of other trees , that were not fit for timber ; these he and Joe had cut early in the spring , so that the soil was completely covered with a dense mass of combustible matter , as dry as tinder", "context_len": 76}}}, "straining": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 13, "context": "They had gone but a few yards farther , Bomba \u2019 s eyes straining to detect the faintly marked trail , when there was a thud , and on the ground before them , directly in their path , appeared a figure so grotesque in form and ugly in face that Bomba took a startled step backward and the two slaves fell to the ground in a fit of shuddering terror", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "When the mooring-buoy had been thrown overboard , the rope had caught between the bobstay and the stem , with the result that for the last hour the three raw amateurs in salt-water seamanship had been simply keeping their craft straining at the end of the buoy-rope", "context_len": 47}}}, "knotting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "And hurriedly taking off her pinafore , she tore it into strips , and knotting them together in a kind of rope , threw one end of it across to him", "context_len": 31}}}, "heaving": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Where all before was smooth heaving water all was now rushing foam , as the broken wave raced back , as if to pass between two narrow jagged pieces of rock rising up like a gateway some fifty yards away before the next wave came in", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 69, "context": "I could , of course , leap through a port into the sea , taking my chance of being picked up by the pursuing craft , which I fondly hoped would be the avenging Gannet ; but I did not know what distance separated us , and even then , in the eagerness of the chase , there was little likelihood of their noticing me , still less of heaving to and picking me up", "context_len": 75}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "'Gee up ! Let her go ! Hooray ! ' yelled Bobby , striking such a whack on Brindle 's heaving side that she settled the matter by suddenly lying down to roll , and depositing her encumbrances in the miry ditch", "context_len": 42}}}, "awash": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then a sea picked up the swimmer and flung him on the broken deck that was awash with the sea", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "There were distinct signs that the perpetrators of the outrage had sought to obliterate all traces of their dastardly work : the name of the ship had been scraped off the boat 's bows , her air-tight tanks had been stove in , yet in spite of this precaution the boat still remained awash", "context_len": 54}}}, "tensely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Those in the boat feared that he had been killed or crippled by the shock , and waited tensely until his hoarse shout came back to them", "context_len": 27}}}, "poop": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 66, "word_index": 71, "context": "Then he returned to Sir John , and joined the other knights , who were all too anxious to learn the latest news -- who had left the island , and who had come to it since they sailed -- to interest themselves in any way with the figure who had gone aft , supposing him , indeed , to be Sir John 's servant , the lantern suspended over the poop giving too feeble a light for his costume to be noted", "context_len": 83}}}, "protector": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 69, "context": "How came that picture in the dwelling of Japazy , the half-breed ? Why had a similar picture been in the hut of Sobrinini ? What mysterious link was there between the lovely original of that picture and Sobrinini , the witch woman , Japazy , the master of Jaguar Island , Jojasta , the medicine man of the Moving Mountain , and Casson , his friend and former protector ? Somewhere , some time , those residents of the Amazonian jungle had known the mysterious Bartow , his wife , Laura , and perhaps the little child named Bonny", "context_len": 100}}}, "warily": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "He went forward now more slowly , more cautiously , pausing to look about him warily when the lightning illumined the jungle", "context_len": 22}}}, "bulwark": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 36, "context": "Important benefits were thus conferred on the whole neighbourhood ; agriculture flourished , learning increased , a sanctuary for the oppressed was provided , and last , though not least in Ina 's eyes , a bulwark against Mercia was provided for the neighbourhood ; while the poor and the afflicted found their happiness in every way promoted by the neighbourhood of the monastery", "context_len": 64}}}, "cradled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Here , then , my boy , on this spot where thou wert once cradled , renounce all these Christian follies and superstitions , and thou shalt go back with me to the camp of King Sweyn , where thou shalt be received as the descendant of warrior kings , and shalt forget that thou , the falcon , wert ever the inmate of the dovecote", "context_len": 67}}}, "gunwale": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "She sat in the stern , waving her hands in helpless terror , and even as the spectators stared , the boat gibed with terrific violence , and a volume of water poured in over the gunwale", "context_len": 37}}}, "overboard": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 8, "context": "Many will , we may calculate , jump overboard and swim ashore rather than face us , and we shall be able , at any rate , to capture three or four of their craft , for , as they come out , one by one , we can all close round them ; and with nearly fifty knights , ninety released captives , burning for vengeance , and some fifty or sixty fishermen , for those from the fire ships will , of course , join us -- we shall make short work of them , and may even hope to entirely destroy their fleet", "context_len": 106}, "NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 42, "context": "Now and then men have managed to become possessed of a file , and have , by long and patient work , sawn through a chain , and have , when a galley has been lying near our own shore , sprung overboard and escaped ; but for every attempt that succeeds there must be twenty failures , for the chains are frequently examined , and woe be to the man who is found to have been tampering with his", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Two of the pirates had fallen dead across him , and in the ardour of their attack on the knights , he had lain there unnoticed until the return of Sir Louis and his comrades had driven the pirates overboard", "context_len": 40}}}, "deft": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Maggie will cut it for you , '' said the lady ; and , under her mother 's direction , Maggie 's deft little fingers soon prepared the paper to suit Dolly", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "With a deft movement the trapped rifleman removed the paper from his pocket and conveyed it to his mouth , and before the approaching Huns were upon him he had swallowed the paper", "context_len": 33}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "With the latter he did as he had done the past night , to wit , he took him and couched him in the draught-house , where he deft him parched for excess of fright and dismay ; whilst Alaeddin arose and placing the sword between himself and the Lady Bedrulbudour , lay down and slept till the morning , when the genie appeared and restored the twain to their place , leaving Alaeddin full of joy at [ the discomfiture of ] the Vizier 's son", "context_len": 87}}}, "quickness": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 13, "context": "As you will learn from Sir John , Gervaise Tresham afterwards showed a quickness of wit that was the means of saving the lives of those with him , and I may say also of all with me , for had they failed to reach the rendezvous we should have fallen easy victims to the five ships Hassan Ali brought against us", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Though the name is of difficult analysis , it is said to refer to quickness , velocity ( of the water-course ? )", "context_len": 23}}}, "stimulants": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Briggs rejoiced to find that both had life in them , and forced stimulants between their locked and pallid lips , while his men rowed toward the bow of the wreck", "context_len": 31}}}, "briggs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Briggs :", "context_len": 2}}}, "clubbed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "He had never fought for his life in water like this which clubbed and choked him", "context_len": 16}}}, "groped": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 33, "context": "To say that Ben was touched , would be to fail utterly in expressing the smallest degree of his feelings , yet he was a silent , undemonstrative fellow , and now he groped in vain for satisfactory words with which to thank the widow", "context_len": 45}}}, "nerved": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 21, "context": "Then Alfgar saw what was the marvellous power of Christianity , and beheld a heroism utterly beyond the fierce excitement which nerved his countrymen for their scenes of carnage and blood ; not one of his pagan friends could have suffered as calmly , as patiently -- it seemed easier for the sufferer to bear than for Alfgar to look on ; once or twice the latter gave audible vent to his emotions , but the look which Bertric turned upon him spoke volumes , and he restrained himself lest he should add to the pain of the victim", "context_len": 99}}}, "swoop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Then David felt the bow rise , rearing higher and higher , until it hung for a moment and descended in a long , sickening swoop as if it were heading straight for the bottom", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Right overhead the leading aircraft of the V formation seemed to swoop", "context_len": 12}}}, "heading": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 47, "context": "With a favourable wind the fleet arrived off the Forelands and thence beat up for the Downs , where we were in a position to meet either the Dutch or the French squadrons ; but off Dover we learned from a fishing boat that Beaufort was seen heading back towards Brest in order to refit some of his ships , so that for the time our chances of smelling powder were very remote", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "Sitting beside him , cross-legged on the ground , he asked the youth his name , where he was from and how he came to be with the party heading for the other side of the continent", "context_len": 37}}}, "bight": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "There was barely time to make fast a bight of the line under the sailor 's shoulders before , clinging to each other , the two were washed out to sea", "context_len": 31}}}, "guessing": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 73, "context": "`` Why you see , '' Edna began , `` Nettie has been taken into the club , and when her time comes to have the club meeting she wo n't have any way of getting the refreshments , so we thought and thought of what we could do to get some money , and last night I saw in the Children 's Corner of the Times that they would give prizes for guessing a puzzle , you know those puzzles , Cousin Ben", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "But the questions could not be answered by standing in the midst of the plain and guessing and staring", "context_len": 19}}}, "throes": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "An involuntary cry of horror broke from us ; then , to our surprise , we saw the man reappear , brandishing a sheath-knife , while the shark , in its last throes , floated belly uppermost , a skilful thrust of the knife having practically disembowelled it", "context_len": 48}}}, "seething": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "For the first few days of my captivity my brain was literally seething with schemes for effecting my escape , most of them wildly impossible , I admit ; but some there were that seemed to promise just a ghost of a chance of success -- until I attempted to put them into effect , when the vigilance of my guards -- with the fear of crucifixion , head downward , before their eyes -- invariably baffled me", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Land ! To feel the solid earth beneath him after the nightmare of that awful journey through the seething waters", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "With a fearful bellow of rage and pain , the great brute leaped half out of the water and fell back , only to churn the water into a seething whirlpool", "context_len": 31}}}, "slumped": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Remember how I nearly slumped in Latin before the big ball game last year , and only just got through by the skin of my teeth in time to play ? Do n't let that happen to you", "context_len": 39}}}, "hoist": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` So I hoist the signal for permission to part company ; and if my senior officer will comply , I \u2019 ll weigh anchor with you to-morrow", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Up -- hoist ! ''", "context_len": 5}}}, "stewards": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "Several stewards and the ship 's doctor were waiting to care for the rescued , and as David limped forward he caught a glimpse of the slender girl being borne toward the staterooms of the second cabin", "context_len": 37}}}, "staterooms": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Several stewards and the ship 's doctor were waiting to care for the rescued , and as David limped forward he caught a glimpse of the slender girl being borne toward the staterooms of the second cabin", "context_len": 37}}}, "somethings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` A cadet vas good for somethings if you give him a chance", "context_len": 13}}}, "ovation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "They reached the Gulch about sunset , and were welcomed in such noisy style by the miners that it might almost be called an ovation", "context_len": 25}}}, "schooner": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Here they fitted the rigging , and altered the ship \u2019 s sails into those of a schooner ; and so well versed were Captain Rhines and his son in all nautical matters , that , by dint of splicing and piecing , they managed to get all the standing rigging , and nearly all the running gear , out of the materials of the wreck", "context_len": 66}}}, "galley": {"freq": 265, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 263, "word_index": 35, "context": "I know a dozen rich Moors in Tripoli or Tunis who would be glad to have him ; and we agreed that we would run down to the African coast for awhile , for that galley has been altogether too busy of late for our comfort , and will be all the more active after this little affair ; besides , people in these islands have got so scared that one ca n't get within ten miles of any of them now without seeing their signal smokes rising on the hills , and finding , when they land , the villages deserted and stripped of everything worth carrying away", "context_len": 109}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "Would these young knights , the youngest of whom could be but a year his junior , serve willingly under his orders ? And , above all , would they be able to emulate the deeds of experienced warriors , and would the galley worthily maintain the fame of the Order ?", "context_len": 52}}}, "spoiling": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "The Danes found it hard to repress their laughter at the thought of the reception which awaited the travellers at home ; they had no idea of spoiling it by attacking them , although the numbers were about equal ; besides , they had got all the plunder and spoil , and a battle would only endanger the success already obtained", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Bradford smiled , and did not look as if she thought the `` spoiling '' would hurt her children very much ; and now , with a loud `` whoa , '' Mr", "context_len": 33}}}, "muscle": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 89, "context": "For there was something very stirring in the act , and a stranger to the place would hold his breath in dread as he saw Mark Penelly , who was the finest swimmer at the port of Carn Du , climb up the side of the great black rock upon some fine summer evening , then go round along the narrow shelf of shaley stone , till he stood alone there forty feet above the sea , his white figure as he rested against the black rock , every muscle standing out from his well-knit frame , and his arms crossed , looking like some antique statue in its niche", "context_len": 110}}}, "scoured": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 48, "context": "This chanced to be the first mishap ; but then could you really call an accident a mishap that simply necessitated a three-days ' stay in the beautiful Wordsworth district ? Our sunshiny little party , at any rate , chose not so to regard it , and scoured the whole lovely region on foot , reading Wordsworth 's poetry in their halts by the roadside , and growing familiar with every foot of the lanes he so dearly loved", "context_len": 80}}}, "beck": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "While he scoured , cleaned , and trotted this way and that at the beck and call of the bos'n , a bebuttoned small boy in a bob-tailed jacket hailed him with this brief message :", "context_len": 36}}}, "steward": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 68, "context": "Then , at five every clear day , afternoon tea with the captain , for which they had a standing invitation , and by means of which both she and Chris came to be on terms of wonderful intimacy with that august officer , so that they joked over the rare souchong and delicious little toasted cakes ( the secret of whose making was kept close-guarded by the steward ) with a familiarity that , to themselves at least , never ceased to be a wonder", "context_len": 86}}}, "pitiable": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "The folk and the people of the city marvelled at this strange thing , especially when they saw the Vizier and his son go forth the palace in a pitiable plight for stress of chagrin and despite , and they fell to asking , `` What hath happened and why is the marriage avoided and the rejoicings broken off ? '' But none knew what was to do save Alaeddin , the suitor , [ 419 ] who laughed in his sleeve", "context_len": 82}}}, "promotion": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 50, "context": "I did not dare to send any message to you , for the sultan pretended great anger against me , and used the opportunity to squeeze me hardly ; but I have frequently made inquiries about you , and was glad indeed to find that even in prison you received promotion ; had it been otherwise -- had I found that you were in misery -- I would have endeavoured , whatever the risk , to aid you to escape", "context_len": 80}}}, "stammer": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "The warmth of the comfortable fire had already begun to thaw his frozen wits , and he made shift to stammer a word of thanks as he fumbled with the spoon", "context_len": 31}}}, "repeating": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 10, "context": "Some hours later the captain returned , and upon my repeating to him the yarn which I had spun to the mate he not only very readily consented to my working my passage , but also offered me two excellent suits of clothes , two shirts , two pairs of stockings , a pair of shoes , and a worsted cap in exchange for my leopard-skin , which offer I gladly accepted ; and that night found me domiciled in the forecastle of L'Esperance as one of her crew", "context_len": 89}}}, "noddle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I guess I knocked some of the hero nonsense out of his noddle , and now I want you to work him hard and watch how he takes his medicine", "context_len": 30}}}, "craftily": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "While avoiding or refusing to answer his questions , the two men had craftily sought to lead him into compromising statements ; failing in which , they disappointedly told each other that his attempt at slickness would do him no good", "context_len": 41}}}, "awnings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "But I hope that if , on our return , we bring back all our slaves in good condition and health , the contrast between their appearance and that of the slaves in most other galleys will be so marked that the admiral may consider it would be well to order awnings to be fixed to all the vessels of the Order , and even to grant to all slaves , when away on voyages , the little indulgences I have given them here", "context_len": 84}}}, "lashings": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "99 to the effect that when boat-lowering practice was again resorted to it would be advisable to provide ring-bolts and securing lashings to prevent the soldiers falling overboard ; meanwhile he would make sure of the two he had picked up by keeping them on board the Gosport until her arrival at Port Stanley with the transport under her charge", "context_len": 60}}}, "limply": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "David was more than willing , and as he busied himself with stays and lashings he cast his eye aft until he could see the gray-haired skipper of the Pilgrim huddled limply in a chair , a forlorn picture of misery and weakness", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Gerald knocked on the door , which after a slight delay , was opened by a tall , gaunt woman attired in a soiled calico dress which hung limply about her thin and bony figure", "context_len": 35}}}, "tremulously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "And I thought -- '' flushing daintily , `` that maybe Jane might marry , and you would want some one in her place and if it was -- me , '' rather tremulously -- '' I could come -- I love you so", "context_len": 44}}}, "masts": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was such a beautiful , stanch , great ship , with its large masts and spars and network of interlacing halyards , that its wideness meant more to her just then than even the wideness of the sea ; and she felt so safe and at home on it withal , that all the expected uncanny sensations had need to be postponed to some more favorable occasion", "context_len": 68}}}, "seamed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` She 's too weak to talk much , but she smiled at me , '' and the ship-master 's seamed face suddenly became radiant", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "No sounds came from their lips , but their stifled sobs and the heaving of their naked shoulders , seamed and scarred by the strokes of their taskmasters ' whips , told the young knights , who stood unhelmeted and silent around , how deep was their emotion", "context_len": 48}}}, "radiant": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 12, "context": "Captain Thrasher chanced to catch a glimpse of the lad with the radiant face , who was leaning over the rail of the deck below him", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "Hartley , not divining that , simply answered , his kind face radiant as a boy 's , The largest yet , Marie-Celeste -- enough to take me home for two months this summer , and pay Bradford , besides , for doing double work while I 'm gone", "context_len": 49}}}, "livelier": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "The children had returned after dinner to their point of vantage on the churchyard wall , and found as much amusement in the sight below as in the livelier scenes of the morning", "context_len": 33}}}, "persisted": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 22, "context": "There ought to have been more verses , but at this point Father had unfortunately got hold of the paper , and persisted in treating the poem in such a comic light that the indignant authoress had never found the heart to finish it , though the fragment was considered very talented by Aunt Helen , and carefully put away in an old work-box , with the first specimens of Peggy 's handwriting and one of Bobby 's little baby-curls", "context_len": 80}}}, "granddaughter": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 45, "context": "She was askin ' some more questions about my sick friend , when somebody pulls the door-bell as furious , and when it was opened , there was a servant-gal lookin ' as scared as anything , and she tells the old lady her little granddaughter was lost , and could n't be found nowhere , and was she here , and did they know anything about her ? Well , they did n't know nothin ' , and the old lady said she 'd be round right away , and she herself looked scared ready to drop , and I see she had n't no more thought for me nor my belongin 's , nor could n't be expected to , so I just takes my leave", "context_len": 128}}}, "spar": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 49, "context": "In a short time he heard a dull moaning sound , a puff of wind struck the boat , but in a few seconds died out ; it was sufficient to give the light craft stern way , and she drifted backwards , the rope tightening , until the spar lay across her bows , and some twenty yards away", "context_len": 60}}}, "forbore": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The broken ship-master fell to brooding again , and there was so much grief in his tired eyes and uncertain voice that David forbore to ask him any more questions", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "As Tom Hadley 's special phrase could not come in here appropriately , he forbore to make any remark", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "He probably could have slain it with one of his arrows , but he forbore , glad enough to be rid so suddenly of an awful problem", "context_len": 27}}}, "nearing": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 22, "context": "By degrees , though , the cloud that had been over his understanding seemed to float away , and as they were nearing the harbour he began to recall the urgings he had received to leap from Carn Du , which now stood up black and forbidding on his left ; the swim out to the lugger and round ; and then -- '' Well , how do you feel now , lad ? '' said the master", "context_len": 78}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Again the master appealed , pointing to the now rapidly nearing cliffs", "context_len": 12}}}, "spick": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "At last the little toilettes were completed , even to the reformation of Albert 's curls around an ivory curling stick ; and with embroidered dress and well-starched kilt none the worse for the decorous experiences of the morning , they emerged from the little boudoir as spick and span as from the depths of the traditional bandbox", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Half an hour later Drusie and Jim , having fed all the animals , were loitering on the sunny terrace together when Hal , looking very spick and span in a clean suit of flannels , came out with his bat under his arm", "context_len": 44}}}, "adrift": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 51, "context": "When they reached the cabin they were set upon by the combined party , their horses were taken from them , they were tied to trees , where they were kept in great pain all night , and in the morning stripped of the greater part of their money and sent adrift", "context_len": 52}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Their ship and companions perished in tempest at sea , the boys are adrift in a small open boat when they spy a ship", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 56, "context": "And very attentively everybody listened to the story , as I told it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached", "context_len": 128}}}, "slenderest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Captain Bracewell was fit for many active years afloat , but he was too old to begin at the foot of the ladder in steam vessels , and there was the slenderest hope of his finding a command in the kind of a ship he had lost", "context_len": 47}}}, "snuggled": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 45, "context": "Never had a night seemed so long ; the only one who thoroughly enjoyed himself was the puppy , who , delighted to receive so much attention , pursued his diversions until the sun was well in at the window , when at length he snuggled into Peggy 's pillow , and composed himself to sleep , leaving his weary guardians only time for a brief rest before Nancy 's unwelcome tap was heard at the door", "context_len": 77}}}, "ahoy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` David Downes , ahoy ! Here 's my Margaret that wants to know the fine big boy I 've been telling her so much about", "context_len": 26}}}, "lamely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "So he finished very lamely", "context_len": 5}}}, "renown": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 110, "context": "Oh , the Brotherhood of Knights ! That is what an Order is , you know , and this one was founded way back in the fourteenth century , in the time of Edward the Third ; and they say the way it came to be called the Order of the Garter was this : That King Edward was dancing with the Countess of Salisbury , when she had the misfortune to lose her garter ; and then as he stooped to pick it up , and saw every one smiling , he gallantly announced , 'that they should shortly see that garter advanced to so high an honor and renown as to account themselves happy to wear it", "context_len": 119}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "This , then , was the gallant Montague , a man who , under the Commonwealth , had earned renown when fighting with Blake the fleets of Holland and of Spain , and whose prompt action in co-operating with Monk and taking command of the fleet sent to fetch the king from Holland did much to earn the royal gratitude and favour", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "After this fashion he won himself great renown in all the realm and the most of the chiefs of the state and the Amirs used to eat at his table and swore not but by his precious life", "context_len": 38}}}, "donna": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Then the prima donna of a grand-opera company volunteered to collect a fund which should be divided among the heroes and the castaways", "context_len": 23}}}, "volunteered": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 21, "context": "I have this afternoon had a meeting of your knights , and have urged upon them very strongly that , having volunteered to serve under you , they must obey your orders as promptly and willingly as if you were the senior knight of the Order , and that it behooves them specially upon the present occasion , when the crew is composed entirely of young knights , to show themselves worthy of the honour that has been done to them by entrusting a galley of the Order to their charge", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "'Perhaps I might read you something else , then ? ' volunteered Peggy , determined to be a ministering angel , despite the evident unwillingness of her prot\u00e9g\u00e9", "context_len": 28}}}, "fund": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 46, "context": "Of course , all this meant money ; but with the exception of the previous summer , when Theodore 's guests had cost him such a pretty penny , Harold had conscientiously lived a good way inside his income , so that there was a reserve fund to draw on , on demand", "context_len": 53}}}, "heaping": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Joe carefully replaced the lid , and shovelled on the soil again , heaping it up , and smoothing it with the flat of his spade , in imitation of the village sexton", "context_len": 33}}}, "squared": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 41, "context": "The work of transforming the man-of-war into a seemingly peaceful merchantman was quickly performed , and long before the corsair ( for such there was no doubt she was ) came within range the Gannet was floundering along with yards badly squared , for all the world like a helpless trader , her course having been previously altered as if she were intent on running away", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "On the one side there was the open sea , on the other the piled-up granite , which rose up like hand-built buttresses , composed of vast squared masses rising tier upon tier", "context_len": 33}}}, "resonance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "But instead of the expected thanks for the generous gift , he squared his slouching shoulders and standing as if he were on his own quarter-deck , his deep voice rang out with its old-time resonance :", "context_len": 37}}}, "aye": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "steamed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "But the steamer did not mind how any of her passengers were feeling -- she must make the best possible record , no matter who was glad or sorry ; and on she steamed , past lonely and beautiful Holyhead , and then through the wide Irish Sea ( that seems indeed a veritable ocean in its wideness ) , until land once more was sighted and the harbor reached , and the anchor dropped off the wonderful docks at Liverpool", "context_len": 81}}}, "jubilant": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "There is no need to tell the rest in detail , but simply to say that while Mark and his roommate gazed on in blank despair , their jubilant enemy made out a list of at least a dozen charges , which he knew would aggregate to at least half of the demerit maximum , and for every one of which there was some slight basis of justification", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was a jubilant chorus of shouts as the party struck the incline , and in a few minutes they were on ground above the swirling waters of the mighty river", "context_len": 31}}}, "lavish": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "On the day after Paleologus , who was himself a renegade Greek , had established his camp , he sent in a herald to summon the city to surrender , at the same time making lavish promises that the lives and property of the native population should be respected , and that they should be allowed to continue to reside there , to enjoy the full exercise of their religion and of all other rights they possessed", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "I would not allow him to give me a flashy or showy ring , as he wanted to do , and I think he would gladly have spent a couple of hundred pounds on my engagement-ring , but I would not have it , not until the whole thing was known , then he might lavish jewellery on me as much as he pleased for all I cared", "context_len": 68}}}, "tugs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "With voice no louder than if he were talking across a dinner table , the master of the liner waved away the tugs that swarmed out to help him , and with flawless judgment turned the six hundred feet of vibrant steel hull almost in its own length and laid her alongside her pier as delicately as a fisherman handles a dory", "context_len": 62}}}, "flawless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "With voice no louder than if he were talking across a dinner table , the master of the liner waved away the tugs that swarmed out to help him , and with flawless judgment turned the six hundred feet of vibrant steel hull almost in its own length and laid her alongside her pier as delicately as a fisherman handles a dory", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The Peaks stood bald and pink against the flawless sky", "context_len": 10}}}, "vibrant": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "With voice no louder than if he were talking across a dinner table , the master of the liner waved away the tugs that swarmed out to help him , and with flawless judgment turned the six hundred feet of vibrant steel hull almost in its own length and laid her alongside her pier as delicately as a fisherman handles a dory", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It was the gay and vibrant melody that poor Casson had tried to sing", "context_len": 14}}}, "dory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "With voice no louder than if he were talking across a dinner table , the master of the liner waved away the tugs that swarmed out to help him , and with flawless judgment turned the six hundred feet of vibrant steel hull almost in its own length and laid her alongside her pier as delicately as a fisherman handles a dory", "context_len": 62}}}, "era": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "David thrilled at the sight , and thought of Captain Bracewell , as fine a seaman in his way , but belonging to another era of the ocean", "context_len": 28}}}, "unnerved": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "There was about Marie-Celeste at all times the same implicit childish confidence that unnerved the bold robber in Editha 's Burglar , and yet she herself was always quick to discover when this same confidence was being taken advantage of , and when she would best fly to cover", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "It felt a very insecure bridge to poor Peggy , as she crept over on her hands and knees , trying not to look down into the dark gulf below , so frail and insecure that she shuddered for Bobby , who seemed so unnerved that she scarcely dared allow him to make the trial", "context_len": 55}}}, "stranded": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "No longer a stranded derelict , but a leader of men , gravely rejoicing in the strength and beauty of the Sea Witch , Captain Bracewell looked every inch a proper seaman to command this queen of the old-time Yankee merchant marine", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 46, "context": "Everything was lovely and just as it should be , even to the pelting showers that came down with such swift suddenness as to almost soak them through before they could get under cover of waterproofs and umbrellas , and then a moment after left them stranded in brilliant sunshine , fairly steaming within the rubber coats which , with much difficulty , had but just been adjusted", "context_len": 68}}}, "smuggling": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "The three conspirators proposed `` borrowing '' a few specimens , smuggling them into Stumpy 's collection , which was kept in a shack in his back yard , and , after witnessing his surprise , they would take Professor Long 's reptiles back to the school", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "Fortunately I was spared the ordeal of attending the trial , but I heard that the gallows or transportation to the West Indies accounted for the whole of the rascally crew , against whom the barbaric crime of wrecking , as well as smuggling , was proved right up to the hilt", "context_len": 52}}}, "fared": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 2, "context": "So they fared on with him in great state , with four of the Viziers for his sword-bearers , whilst all the troops and people of the city , both townsfolk and strangers , walked in procession before him , carrying flambeaux and drums and flutes and instruments of mirth and music , till they brought him to his palace , when he alighted and entering , sat down , as did also the Viziers and Amirs who were in his company , whilst the mamelukes brought sherbets and sweetmeats [ 493 ] and gave all who were with him in the procession to drink , albeit they were a multitude of folk whose number might not be told", "context_len": 119}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I have travelled far and not fared over-sumptuously ; but now I am near my journey 's end , and I must not linger on the way", "context_len": 27}}}, "precise": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 48, "context": "He was a cunning little object as he strode a few feet down the path , his hat on the back of his golden curls , a soft , red silk sash knotted soldier-like at his side , and his hands folded behind him , in evident and precise imitation of some older observer of the elements", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "Letters of Charlotte Tucker , referring to the event , have not come to hand ; but she must have felt the separation very keenly , whatever might have been the precise reasons which led to the move", "context_len": 38}}}, "shipmate": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` But I always wanted to be the kind of a seaman my father was , '' confided David , grateful for the cheer of this grizzled shipmate", "context_len": 28}}}, "stanch": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was such a beautiful , stanch , great ship , with its large masts and spars and network of interlacing halyards , that its wideness meant more to her just then than even the wideness of the sea ; and she felt so safe and at home on it withal , that all the expected uncanny sensations had need to be postponed to some more favorable occasion", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Two of the other officers were endeavouring to stanch the blood that flowed from his forehead", "context_len": 16}}}, "partnership": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "David smiled at the `` we '' of this stanch partnership of the Pilgrim , and as soon as he was off watch he wrote a long reply , in which he told Margaret that his uncle 's death made him feel as if he kind of belonged to their little family , for he had nobody else to care for and be of service to", "context_len": 66}}}, "detention": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "Once or twice he thought of asking permission to leave the ship long enough to run over to Brooklyn , but new notions of discipline had been pounded into him by the events of the homeward voyage , and he decided to take his detention on board as part of the routine which made good sailors `` in steam", "context_len": 59}}}, "drizzling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "He passed through all the streets inflaming the curiosity of the watchmen ; the darkness ( for there were very few lamps or lights of any kind , in those days , for public use ) was intense , a drizzling rain was falling , and at length , weary , wet , and dispirited , he returned to the palace , and found that the council , tired of waiting , had at length broken up", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "But as regards skating , the children were doomed to disappointment , for , with the usual perversity of the English climate , the weather changed , and Christmas Eve brought a mild wind and drizzling rain", "context_len": 37}}}, "blobs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "A drizzling fog filled the harbor , and the arc lights on the pier were no more than vague blobs of sickly yellow", "context_len": 23}}}, "riotous": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "The other was a heliotrope which had been given to Bessie by her papa , and which also had been little more than a sprig when she first had it ; but it was now a flourishing little plant , not running riotous in the way Maggie 's geranium did , but fresh and fair , with a cluster or two of delicate , perfumed blossoms , and the promise of more", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Rows of Mexican children squatted silent and stolid against the red walls , unmoved by those excitements of closing day which stirred their American mates to riotous glee", "context_len": 28}}}, "roustabouts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Accordin ' to reports , you , being a well-dressed and unknown young stranger , was rescued from a gang of drunken roustabouts by two policemen , a big red automobile , and a prominent citizen whose name was withheld at his request , as the bright reporter puts it", "context_len": 50}}}, "unseemly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}}}, "odder": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Vell , den , me speak to Senhor Capitan an \u2019 three odder", "context_len": 14}}}, "patrol": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 34, "context": "The cadet could hear seamen running from the other side of the ship to re-enforce the peace party , and presently one of them dashed up the pier as if to call the police patrol boat , which lay at the next dock", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "We have , therefore , only to overcome the eight or ten men who patrol the prison ; and as we shall have the arms of the guards , some thirty in number , we shall make quick work of them", "context_len": 41}}}, "dock": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 28, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Dad will dock Big Chief 's weekly pocket money for this", "context_len": 11}}}, "disorderly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "Mallory , I do not think it has ever happened to me to find a room so utterly disorderly , or a cadet so negligent ! Look at that bluebook ; it belongs upon the mantelpiece , and I see it on the bed -- -- ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "slums": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}}}, "seaport": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "The cadet had seen enough of the fire-room force , a hundred and fifty strong , to know that the coal-passers and firemen were as brutal and disorderly men ashore as could be found in the slums of a great seaport", "context_len": 41}}}, "disquieted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "For instance , -- having noticed that girls in an evening party , glancing at a mirror , were apt to be disquieted to find their dresses disorganised , she resolved to have no mirrors at all in her rooms , hoping thereby to secure greater peace of mind among her guests", "context_len": 52}}}, "deserter": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "But every word he spoke but added to the grand master 's suspicions ; for if the man considered that the capture of the city was morally certain , it would be simply throwing away his life to enter it as a deserter", "context_len": 43}}}, "hailing": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 22, "context": "He wanted very much to know what was going on overside in this mysterious fashion , but there was no one in hailing distance , and the watch officer , judging by the noise in the pier , had his hands full", "context_len": 42}}}, "tiptoed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Finally , when he could stand the suspense no longer , he tiptoed across the deck , moved aft until he was amidships between the saloon deck houses , and crouched on a bench against the rail", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "With infinite caution Bomba tiptoed to the other side of the bed", "context_len": 12}}}, "poking": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` That is Beaver Meadow , '' said Cassowary , `` and that lazy little river poking through it as if looking for the shortest way to the lake is Fawn River", "context_len": 32}}}, "discern": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 49, "context": "We now had leisure to observe that the storm had so far passed away that there were big breaks in the canopy of cloud overhead and away to the eastward , through which the stars were beginning to show themselves , affording enough light just to enable us to discern the two banks of the stream , but not sufficient to betray our presence to an observer at a greater distance than , say , a quarter of a mile", "context_len": 80}}}, "ports": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 13, "context": "He had calculated that he would be sold at one of the Levant ports , and had thought that with his knowledge of Turkish he should have no great difficulty in escaping from any master into whose hands he might fall , and taking his chance of either seizing a fishing boat , or of making his way in a trading ship to some district where the population was a mixed one , and where trade was winked at between the merchants there , and those at some of the Greek towns", "context_len": 92}}}, "resume": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 28, "context": "Our wants for the next few hours were consequently supplied , we had therefore no need to do anything further than just to wait for nightfall and then resume our voyage ; which we did , passing the intervening time in chatting together and discussing the various precautions which we must take in order to elude our pursuers , who , by the way , were now several miles ahead of us", "context_len": 72}}}, "coaling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "She was hovering under one of the lower ports , which had been left open to resume coaling at daylight", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Amboina City , with its piers , its big coaling station and its naval installations , offered the biggest group of targets", "context_len": 22}}}, "blots": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Two or three men were moving like dark blots in the little craft", "context_len": 13}}}, "muffled": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "I could hear the sound of the brushwood being replaced and the rough farewell greetings of the smugglers , and , by the jolting of the cart and the muffled noise of the wheels , I knew that the route lay across a grassy down", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 49, "context": "They were slightly veiled ; but , as Gervaise afterwards learnt , Ben Ibyn was not a Moor , but a Berber , a people who do not keep their women in close confinement as do the Moors , but allow them to go abroad freely without being entirely muffled up", "context_len": 51}}}, "complex": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "He really knew so little of this vast and complex structure called a steamship that almost any surprising performance might happen among her eight decks", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "d'Iberville reports , that at the time of his visit ( March 1699 ) the villages of the Naktche made up one town only , and formed a complex of contiguous villages called Th\u00e9lo\u00ebl or Th\u00e9co\u00ebl [ 27 ] ( Margry IV , 179 )", "context_len": 45}}}, "structure": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Oh ! '' Edna thought that Reliance was very clever to know all this before they had even reached the mill which now loomed up before them , a grey stone structure in a little nest of trees which climbed the hill behind it , and spread along the sides of the stream , flowing on to join the river", "context_len": 61}}}, "steamship": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Without any further words he hurried out of the house , and drove at once to the office of the steamship company , where he secured passage by the earliest vessel eastward bound", "context_len": 33}}}, "recross": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "He rose from his seat and turned to recross the deck , when he was tripped and thrown on his back so suddenly that there was no time to cry out before some one was kneeling on his chest , with a choking grip on his neck", "context_len": 47}}}, "repetition": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 21, "context": "Very little alteration will make them go very well to the air , for I have tried them ; and the repetition of the last words , which your sweet music requires , suits lines the whole emphasis of which falls on the closing words ; at least I fancy so", "context_len": 51}}}, "weakened": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 3, "context": "Their repeated disasters weakened every man 's heart , and gave rise to a well-founded belief that there was treachery in their midst , and that plans decided even in their secret councils were made known to the Danes", "context_len": 39}}}, "savagely": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 51, "context": "For a hundred yards or more the trail led over uneven rocky ground toward an immense rock , upon rounding which I found myself face to face with , and within half-a-dozen yards of , a splendid full-grown male leopard who was crouching over poor Ama 's motionless body , snarling savagely as he strove with his claws to remove a broken spear , the head of which was buried deep in his neck", "context_len": 74}}}, "heave": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "'It bea n't no light weight , Miss Peggy , whatever it be , ' groaned Joe , for it was as much as he could manage to heave the heavy chest from its resting-place on to the grass above", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 37, "context": "While the latter was growing moment by moment more refulgent , the former caught the wondrous dyes , till the water seemed everywhere like molten gold with ruddy and empurpled reflections where the sea gave a gentle heave", "context_len": 38}}}, "brawn": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "And I have here besides a noble collar of brawn that would turn even a heathen to a better mind ! But , indeed , sir , '' she added , changing her banter to a winning tone of apology", "context_len": 40}}}, "intruder": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 19, "context": "The prince flung his pen on the floor , and leaned back in his chair to look at the intruder , who stood by the door inwardly cursing himself for having been such a fool as to force himself into such a position", "context_len": 43}}}, "explanations": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 79, "context": "each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the operation was undertaken", "context_len": 128}}}, "handcuffed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Without waiting for explanations they fell upon the captive whom Captain Thrasher had tucked under one arm , and handcuffed him in a twinkling", "context_len": 24}}}, "murdering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 61, "context": "He 's an English colonel that lives at the -- -- Hotel ; and they tell wonderful stories about him , -- how brave he is , and what a lot of battles he 's fought , and how , with just a handful of soldiers , he defended a hospital full of sick men against a great force of them murdering Sepoys , and brought every man of them safe off", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` They are in Wessex , plundering , murdering , and burning", "context_len": 12}}}, "blotch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The fog had begun to lift , and a black blotch was moving out toward the middle of the river", "context_len": 20}}}, "enos": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Enos told me not to budge from the gangway , and I went over to see what was going on", "context_len": 20}}}, "gang": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 67, "context": "Among the missiles hurled into the town were balls filled with Greek fire , but the houses being entirely built of stone , no conflagrations of importance were caused by them , as a band of knights was organised specially to watch for these bombs , and whenever one of them was seen to fall , they hurried from their lookout to the spot , with a gang of slaves carrying baskets of earth and buckets of water , and quenched the flames before they had made any great headway", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Waugh remarked that `` thae twa lads were ower young to gang together , '' so they were separated", "context_len": 19}}}, "surprisingly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Do you know , '' said the doctor , handling Tesuque , `` that this thing is surprisingly well-modeled ? The Mexicans can do anything with adobe , but this has something about it beyond the reach of most of them", "context_len": 42}}}, "chug": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The officer took to his heels , and in a surprisingly short time the captain saw a launch dart out from the pier beyond the Roanoke , her engines `` chug chugging '' at top speed", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "That \u2019 s my chug cart , and the man pointed to a red touring car at the curb", "context_len": 19}}}, "chugging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The officer took to his heels , and in a surprisingly short time the captain saw a launch dart out from the pier beyond the Roanoke , her engines `` chug chugging '' at top speed", "context_len": 36}}}, "gangways": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "In full marching order the men trooped up the gangways , divested themselves of their packs , and made themselves as comfortable as possible for their twelve or fourteen hours ' voyage", "context_len": 32}}}, "dumping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Dumping the lad on a divan , the master of the liner pawed him over from head to foot to make sure no bones were broken , and then remarked with great severity :", "context_len": 34}}}, "divan": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "Dumping the lad on a divan , the master of the liner pawed him over from head to foot to make sure no bones were broken , and then remarked with great severity :", "context_len": 34}}}, "disobeying": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "Bessie had a quick temper , and was apt to fly into a passion ; while Maggie was exceedingly careless and forgetful , sometimes disobeying her parents from sheer heedlessness , and a moment 's want of thought", "context_len": 38}}}, "budge": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "My first intention was to refuse to budge from the carriage , to fiercely demand that the coachman should turn round and drive me straight back again to mother , but on second thoughts , I reflected that I should lose a good deal of dignity by this proceeding , and the best possible plan was to appear as if nothing at all extraordinary had occurred , and to follow Mrs", "context_len": 71}}}, "oiling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Enos is a good seaman , but his brain needs oiling", "context_len": 11}}}, "blackguards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "That row on the dock was all a blind , put up by two or three of those fire-room blackguards from Antwerp , who stand in with the gang of tobacco smugglers", "context_len": 32}}}, "smugglers": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 40, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}}}, "pals": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 3, "context": "Who are your pals ? Beg pardon , sir , I did n't know you were an officer ! '' ( This to Captain Nicholson , who , owing to the dirt and grime in which he was smothered , was hardly distinguishable from the others", "context_len": 46}}}, "bunkers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "At other bunkers a similar operation was performed by their comrades , the `` trimmers '' being specially instructed to remove the coal in a methodical manner , so that there was slight possibility of the remaining contents being thrown out by the roll of the vessel", "context_len": 47}}}, "decoyed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "And because the police patrol boat berths close by us , they even decoyed the whole squad away for a little while", "context_len": 22}}}, "squad": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 33, "context": "There were two reasons : In the first place , Mark Mallory , together with the rest of the plebes , was being bullied and tormented just then in the course of a squad drill ; and , in the second place , one of the committee , Cadet Spencer , was engaged in doing the bullying , having been appointed `` on duty over plebes", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "What manner of torture is squad drill has already been shown ; and so the reader should have some idea of what our five friends were going through", "context_len": 28}}}, "tuppenny": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Do you expect me to sit here yarning all night with a tuppenny cadet ? Go to your bunk and report to me in the morning", "context_len": 26}}}, "nuisance": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 7, "context": "As this would have been a horrible nuisance , especially as I wanted to enjoy in quiet the view of the city , with its castle and walls , I acceded at once to his request , which seemed to me a reasonable one", "context_len": 44}}}, "thugs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` The police are watching that bunch of thugs", "context_len": 9}}}, "ruction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They had something to do with the ruction last night , but they ca n't be identified", "context_len": 17}}}, "identified": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` For by a most lucky chance I have to-day obtained what I believe to be trustworthy information to the effect that she was sighted four days ago , bound for the Gaboon river -- or perhaps it would be more correct to say that she was sighted steering east , and identified by the master of a brig who knows her perfectly well , and who has since arrived here , and that there is authentic information to the effect that she is this time bound for the Gaboon", "context_len": 90}}}, "theatricals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "David looked solemn at this , but it was too much like playing theatricals to let himself believe that he was in any kind of danger along the water front of New York", "context_len": 33}}}, "blockaded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The bridge cars were blockaded by an accident , and after fidgeting for half an hour David decided to walk across", "context_len": 21}}}, "fidgeting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` I am afraid you would not like me to say it , '' said Bessie , fidgeting on her hard seat", "context_len": 22}}}, "mustache": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 51, "context": "It was one of the empty rooms that they entered , however , and there they proceeded to costume one of their number , putting on a huge red sash , some medals , a few shoulder straps borrowed for the occasion , and , last of all , a false mustache", "context_len": 52}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "His brierwood was between his lips , and from the thicket of whisker and mustache the blue vapor issued as if from the funnel of a small steamcraft", "context_len": 28}}}, "flighty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The old man is kind of flighty and I could n't ha ' held him here with a hawser", "context_len": 19}}}, "hawser": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "At irregular intervals the barges had to be passed through locks , and since the locks admitted only two boats at a time , and the hawser had to be cast off before the gate opened and secured again when the lower level was reached , progress was tediously slow", "context_len": 50}}}, "berthed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "The train , upon arrival at the place of embarkation , ran straight into the docks , and brought up close to one of the many transports that were berthed there with banked fires ready to sail at any hour of the day or night", "context_len": 45}}}, "glum": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 34, "context": "' I have n't seen the colour of a ten-dollar bill in as many years , and you put it in your pocket as cool as a cucumber , and go about looking as glum as a herring", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Very few of us are heroes when it really comes to the point , and I grieve to say that she looked so glum , and was generally so grumpy and discontented over the arrangement , that unselfish Lilian , divining the cause , instantly proposed to give up her own part to Peggy , and train a little village girl for the second 'child ' instead", "context_len": 67}}}, "shipping": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 67, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "That the latter should be collecting these things for mercenary purposes with the design , when he should have enough , of shipping them overseas and selling them at fabulous prices , and then following them to live for the rest of his life in wealth and luxury amid the civilization whose value he knew , did not enter Bomba \u2019 s mind", "context_len": 63}}}, "touchy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He is that touchy about taking favors from anybody that it 's plumb unnatural", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "I believe the fellow 's just as ashamed of this last year at Oxford as I am , but you know , Dorothy , as well as I do ( as , alas ! Dorothy did know to her sorrow ) , that Ted 's awfully touchy and sensitive , and it takes a very little thing to turn him one way or the other", "context_len": 65}}}, "favors": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 33, "context": "After beating off another attack of the headhunters , Bomba took Casson down the river and delivered him to the care of Pipina , an old squaw for whom he had done many favors , and set out to find Jojasta", "context_len": 41}}}, "drag": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 92, "context": "Neither Peggy nor Bobby had ever seen a wild-beast show before , for those visits to the Zoo , which are the joy of little Londoners , do not fall to the share of country children , and Archie had quite a lively time keeping them out of harm 's way , for Peggy declared the leopard looked so exactly like Tabbyskins at home that she must positively try to stroke it , and was under the rope and up to the bars before Archie could seize her by her skirts and drag her back , while Bobby 's curiosity on the subject of jackals and hyenas nearly led to the ejection of the whole party from the tent", "context_len": 119}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "A sharp snatch at his line , followed by a long peculiar drag , told him what was at his bait ; and after a little giving and taking , he drew a heavy twining conger eel over the boat 's edge , having no little difficulty in preventing it from tangling his line , for it was quite a yard in length , and proportionately thick", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 85, "context": "At first they had all floundered hopelessly about , and it had taken some practice even to slide a few steps ; but long before they had reached the stage of skimming over the frozen surface at the rate of eight or ten miles an hour , which had been their fond ambition , the snow had melted into moist and dirty slush , which was particularly trying , as they had just decided to make a sledge out of an old packing-case , and drag Lilian round the pasture", "context_len": 90}}}, "chirpy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Oh , she 's feelin ' fairly chirpy , and she went off with granddaddy as proud and cocksure as if they were expectin ' to be offered command of a liner to-morrow", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` A chirpy little sausage-eater ! '' exclaimed Captain Nicholson , who , like his men , was not backward in acknowledging bravery even in an enemy", "context_len": 27}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Glad to see you so chirpy , boys , '' remarked the Brigadier", "context_len": 14}}}, "cocksure": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Oh , she 's feelin ' fairly chirpy , and she went off with granddaddy as proud and cocksure as if they were expectin ' to be offered command of a liner to-morrow", "context_len": 34}}}, "becket": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 0, "context": "Becket , '' thundered the captain , and the mate ducked down the ladder , as if he had been shot at", "context_len": 22}}}, "nautical": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "Here they fitted the rigging , and altered the ship \u2019 s sails into those of a schooner ; and so well versed were Captain Rhines and his son in all nautical matters , that , by dint of splicing and piecing , they managed to get all the standing rigging , and nearly all the running gear , out of the materials of the wreck", "context_len": 66}}}, "explorers": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "The early explorers of North American countries all followed these Indian trails : Narvaez , Hernando de Soto , Tristan de Luna , Juan del Pardo , Lederer and Lawson , because they were led along these tracks by their Indian guides", "context_len": 42}}}, "bearings": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 13, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}}}, "policemen": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "Accordin ' to reports , you , being a well-dressed and unknown young stranger , was rescued from a gang of drunken roustabouts by two policemen , a big red automobile , and a prominent citizen whose name was withheld at his request , as the bright reporter puts it", "context_len": 50}}}, "wayfarers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 73, "context": "Two young theows , whose fathers had gone to the war , but who had been left behind as being too young to share its dangers , although in the flush of early youth , accompanied them , and were soon loaded with the lighter game their masters had killed , while a deer they had slain was hung in the trees , where a wolf could not reach it , and where wayfarers were not likely to pass until the sportsmen should return for their own", "context_len": 87}}}, "pilot": {"freq": 55, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 27, "context": "Nor had Captain Thrasher ever felt more pride and confidence in the power , speed , and seaworthiness of the Roanoke than when he dropped the Dutch pilot off Flushing a few days later and signalled `` full speed ahead , '' with Sandy Hook a week away and waiting wives and sweethearts `` hauling on the towline", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "Billy Smith , son of an eminent explorer , learns to pilot a seaplane aboard a Naval Air Station , and immediately fares forth upon a series of the most exciting cruises and mysterious quests by air , by land and by sea that have ever fallen to the lot of man", "context_len": 52}}}, "hereabouts": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 22, "context": "I know where we be , for yon \u2019 s the Tilly Whim Caves , and nought but smugglers and wreckers bide hereabouts", "context_len": 23}}}, "definite": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Neither of the New Zealanders could give a definite reply , but , to cheer the men up , Peter expressed his opinion that another thirty miles would see them in Dutch territory", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 27, "context": "But when Gouroo whispered to my father , hinting at your incapacity , and suggesting that Mafuta should be sent for , my suspicions began to take definite shape , and , although I was not able to verify those suspicions , I finally made up my mind that the whole occurrence was the outcome of a plot between Gouroo and Mafuta -- your only enemies -- to ruin you", "context_len": 70}}}, "scuppered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Do you think I 'm scuppered yet ? '' snorted Mr", "context_len": 12}}}, "snorted": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 2, "context": "Once he snorted so loudly that the bow-legged man , glancing uneasily toward the farmhouse , made haste to close the stable door and lead the horse to the bars , where he could more readily mount him", "context_len": 38}}}, "traversed": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 22, "context": "The culminating crest of the main range is the Wind River Mountains in the west-central part of the State , which is traversed by numerous other ranges , including the Big Horn in the north-central section , the Laramie Mountains already mentioned , the Medicine Bow in the south ; north of them the Sweetwater and Rattlesnake ranges , and in the west the Teton , Shoshone and Gros Ventres mountains", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "So he rose at once and equipping himself for travel , set out and traversed plains and deserts and mountains months and months , till he came to the land of China [ and entering ] the city of the sultanate , wherein was Alaeddin , repaired to the Strangers ' Khan , where he hired him a lodging and rested there a little", "context_len": 64}}}, "slowed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "As soon as he was sure that the street was unfamiliar he slowed his pace , looked around him , and not wishing to enter a saloon , went over to a gaudily placarded `` oyster house", "context_len": 37}}}, "gaudily": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Each of the lofty ships bore the ensign of its commander ; some carried at the prow the figures of lions , some of bulls , dolphins , dragons , or armed warriors , gaudily painted or even gilded ; while others bore from their mast the ensign of voracious birds -- the eagle , the raven -- which appeared to stretch their wings as the flag expanded in the wind", "context_len": 71}}}, "placarded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "As soon as he was sure that the street was unfamiliar he slowed his pace , looked around him , and not wishing to enter a saloon , went over to a gaudily placarded `` oyster house", "context_len": 37}}}, "screens": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "He erected an immense wooden catapult , which threw huge pieces of rock into the midst of the Turkish works , crushing down the wooden screens erected to hide their approaches , breaking in the covered ways , and causing great loss of life among the besiegers", "context_len": 47}}}, "unlovely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He had recognized the unlovely face of the man who yelled at him as that of one of the Roanoke firemen who had stared at him from the pier in the morning", "context_len": 32}}}, "ruffians": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "blindly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 11, "context": "For two or three minutes we twisted hither and thither , blindly , so far as I was concerned , and then another flash came which enabled me to see that my companion was , as I had already suspected , my faithful little friend Ama , and that she was conducting me , by a somewhat circuitous route , toward the gate in the palisade", "context_len": 66}}}, "doubled": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` More beef into it , bullies , '' urged the bos \u2019 n to the capstan men , and , as the strain on the cables increased , the rest of the crew , in obedience to an order , doubled along the sloping decks , as well as they were able , towards the bows , the whole vessel trembling with the motion", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The Express Rider having gone upon the ferry boat , the attention of the crowd was shifted to the two youths , confronting each other with doubled fists and savage countenances", "context_len": 31}}}, "echoes": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 18, "context": "With a weird , hideous scream that rang through the silent jungle and startled it into a hundred echoes , Bomba left his shelter , flinging the torch ahead of him and dashed down upon that sinister ring of figures about the hut", "context_len": 43}}}, "fugitive": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 34, "context": "How came it that this girl had guessed his secret at once when most people passed him , taking him but for a sturdy beggar ? What made her suspect him of being a fugitive ? Was her offer of shelter but careless good nature , or a heroic endeavour to save a hunted man ? At any rate he had not fallen so low as to draw suspicion on a woman , and a young woman to boot , although she was plainly no nervous , fanciful , fine lady , but a bright , resolute , country girl , with good health and high spirits gleaming from every flash of her bright eyes , and every turn of her auburn head", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 59, "context": "In doing so , he went back to the cause of Jerry \u2019 s flight from the home of his dead uncle , explaining how the blind lad had been pursued even to Oakdale , and how while purchasing that pair of shoes Ben had learned that a man had arrived in the town and made inquiries for the fugitive", "context_len": 60}}}, "bruising": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He was still stiff from the bruising ordeal of the Pilgrim wreck , and his legs felt benumbed , while the panting firemen seemed to be overhauling him inch by inch", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "He was violently dragged along , some trying to force him one way , some another , dashing him against trees in their hurry , and bruising and wounding him without thought or consideration", "context_len": 34}}}, "overhauling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Bart 's idea met with favor and , when lessons for the day were over , the four chums were overhauling cots , inspecting the big tent and seeing if the portable stove was in good condition", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "The Roanoke was almost ready to proceed straight to Southampton for a thorough overhauling after the patch-work repairs made to enable her to cross the Atlantic in safety", "context_len": 28}}}, "revolver": {"freq": 82, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 80, "word_index": 33, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I always carried a Colt \u2019 s revolver with two cylinders , and often had to use both of them", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "So , though he stepped swiftly , he stepped softly , constantly on the alert , bow and arrows , machete and revolver ready at hand in case of need , eyes searching out every moving shadow in the underbrush", "context_len": 40}}}, "stumble": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 59, "context": "It was a tiny hand , that of your precious little child , which pointed it out , and showed me the way by which I must go ; but my feet have so long trodden the road which leads to death , that now , when I would set my face the other way , they falter and stumble", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "You also will join us ; I know it is long since you have heard the words of pure doctrine , yet there is a blessing in reserve for the seed of the righteous , and the filth of the Presbyterian doctrines you learned in Scotland shall not cleave unto your feet to make them stumble in the way", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Don \u2019 t stumble on the doorstool", "context_len": 7}}}, "rapping": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Far away David heard the rapping of nightsticks against the pavement", "context_len": 11}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "The next instant the brothers were shaking hands and rapping out questions , to the surprise of the other occupants of the hut , who had suspicions that they were the victims of a practical joke", "context_len": 36}}}, "nightsticks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Far away David heard the rapping of nightsticks against the pavement", "context_len": 11}}}, "pavement": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 53, "context": "So desperate was their attack that the latter were forced to use their swords , which indeed , burning with rage at the treachery of these men , they were not backward in doing , and in less than a minute the five traitors lay , with cloven heads , dead on the pavement", "context_len": 54}}}, "footfalls": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "He heard ghostly footfalls among the ruins and mysterious taps on the stable-window when he was suppering the horses , which , in spite of Lilian 's reassurances , he persisted in regarding as a warning , though for which of his numerous relatives it was intended kept him in a state of perpetual doubt and uneasiness", "context_len": 57}}}, "lined": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 40, "context": "As it wound through the streets in splendid array , the grand master and officials in all their robes of state , the knights in full armour and the mantles of the Order , while the inhabitants in gala costume lined the streets , windows , and housetops , the ladies waving scarves and scattering flowers down on the knights , the roar of great cannon on the south side of the city showed that the Turks had commenced the attack in another quarter", "context_len": 84}}}, "warehouses": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "To be sure , one has need to overlook , in quite too literal fashion , the warehouses that front the water below the bluff , and here and there an unsightly elevator , but why let the eye rest on these , with the dancing blue water beneath you , and the Jersey hills beyond , and beyond that again , like as not , a glorious sunset", "context_len": 69}}}, "bolted": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Who is there ? '' Then the voice continued , `` If it is you , Irene , the door is bolted , and the blinds are down , and the shutters shut , so even if you break the glass you can not get in", "context_len": 47}}}, "beacons": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` You forget that when the last invasion of our pagan foes was over , it was agreed in the Witan that a set of beacons should be prepared , in readiness to fire , on the tops of the hills , and that if the Danes appeared again , they should be fired everywhere , in which case Mercia was to hold herself in readiness to come to the aid of Wessex or East Anglia , whichever the foe might be harrying", "context_len": 83}}}, "awning": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "At one end there was a gay blue and white awning spread , and under it was a table which looked as if it were piled with the kind of good things you have at a tea party", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "The admiral tells me that never before has a galley returned with the slaves in such good health and condition , and that unquestionably your plan of erecting an awning to shelter them from the midday heat and the night dews has had a most beneficial effect on their health ; he has recommended its general adoption", "context_len": 57}}}, "squirmed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "They went in search of Tiddlywinks ' mother , but Madam Pittypat objected to being made a baby of , for , though she was gentle enough , she squirmed and twisted herself out of every garment they tried upon her , and , at the first opportunity , walked off in a most dignified manner , as though she would say : `` Such a way to treat the mother of a family ! ''", "context_len": 76}}}, "shipboard": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "She wore a blue dress and white apron , but she was not a Roanoke stewardess nor was this place anything like the bunk-room on shipboard", "context_len": 26}}}, "banged": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 52, "context": "They had seen him off at the station -- Harold and she -- and Donald , never allowing himself for a moment to regard this whole affair in any light but the true one , kept a stiff upper lip to the last , and smiled the cheeriest good-by as the guard banged the carriage-door and the train glided out from the depot", "context_len": 63}}}, "chided": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` You must be quiet and not fret , '' chided the nurse", "context_len": 13}}}, "bump": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "She had been tired out with the railroad journey , entertaining as it was , then the excitement of meeting them all again , the bump on her forehead when she had come down so hard on Pansy 's head , and the screams that seemed like a stab going from temple to temple tired her inexpressibly", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Pausing in the doorway , I bowed my head to the company , then I went in , picking my steps carefully so as not to bump into chairs or tables", "context_len": 31}}}, "addresses": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "Speaking to a friend , long after in old age , she said that in her young days at home , \u2019 when a certain nameless gentleman was supposed to be paying his addresses to Fanny , the other sisters were very indignant \u2019 at the idea of any man wishing to break into their sisterly circle", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The author , having had some opportunity to become acquainted with the position , the ideas , and the intellectual wants of those whom he addresses , presents the result of his labors to them , with the hope that it may be found successful in accomplishing its design", "context_len": 49}}}, "quavered": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` But the captain probably signed on another cadet to take my place , first thing this morning , '' quavered the patient , `` and -- and I -- I 'm adrift and dis -- disgraced", "context_len": 37}}}, "dis": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 37, "context": "It \u2019 ll be jes \u2019 like him to kick when he sees me standing with my back toward de Injins , and dere ain \u2019 t any use ob habing any quarrels at a time like dis", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "What can I do for you dis mornin \u2019 ?", "context_len": 10}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Shagbark tole me how dem critters chaw and stomp and bite a feller ; I knowed dat if I brunged down dis one , it would make de oder two so mad dey \u2019 d come at me afore I could load up ag \u2019 in , and you wouldn \u2019 t hab any Jeth any more", "context_len": 57}}}, "framed": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` The one over the mantel is called 'The Signing of the Declaration of Independence , ' and that small framed affair by the chimney is a key to it , for it tells the names of the different men who figure in the picture", "context_len": 45}}}, "tread": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 75, "context": "Miss Wauton \u2019 s first impressions of her , jotted down as follows , do not give the impression of a very old lady , dearly as Charlotte Tucker loved to describe herself in those terms : Tall , slight , with lofty brow , sparkling eye , face constantly beaming with love and intelligence ; genius in every look ; figure frail and fairy-like , agile and graceful ; very brisk movements and light tread", "context_len": 76}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 34, "context": "Another day Has past ! another weary weary day , And I am so much nearer to my grave ! Oh that I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "And the guide moved away with his noiseless tread melting from sight in the gloom", "context_len": 15}}}, "scat": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Marie-Celeste opened her eyes wide for wonder at the novel sight of a coach and four , but with no reins anywhere about the harness , and not so much as the suggestion of a scat for the coachman", "context_len": 39}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "Edna would fain have spent the morning in the kitchen from which issued delectable odors , but Amanda had declared she wanted all the room there was , that she had scatted out the cats and dogs and she would have to scat out children , too , if they came bothering around", "context_len": 53}}}, "dickens": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "' I say ! What the dickens ! Great Scott ! Are you hurt ? ' exclaimed the boy , regarding with much astonishment the crumpled heap of sailor frock , brown curls , and splinters of willow-wood that had suddenly descended at his feet", "context_len": 45}}}, "shave": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was a close shave , but that message of ours did the trick ! The gold \u2019 s here , and Tibbits has been done -- done to a turn ! If there weren \u2019 t so many people around , I \u2019 d yell", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "He would have just comfortable time to shave , dress , and check over his few personal effects that had been sent from the New Guinea airport", "context_len": 27}}}, "withheld": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "Accordin ' to reports , you , being a well-dressed and unknown young stranger , was rescued from a gang of drunken roustabouts by two policemen , a big red automobile , and a prominent citizen whose name was withheld at his request , as the bright reporter puts it", "context_len": 50}}}, "reporter": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "Accordin ' to reports , you , being a well-dressed and unknown young stranger , was rescued from a gang of drunken roustabouts by two policemen , a big red automobile , and a prominent citizen whose name was withheld at his request , as the bright reporter puts it", "context_len": 50}}}, "ferry": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 36, "context": "Joseph , Missouri , on that afternoon in April , 1860 , when Alexander Carlyle , the first Pony Express rider , dashed out of the stables and galloped full speed down the street to the ferry , amid the huzzahs of the excited multitudes", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "So said Robert Moffat as he stood with his mother on the Firth of Forth waiting for the boat to ferry him across", "context_len": 23}}}, "bowled": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 19, "context": "Do you know , he carried you right back to the advance dressing-station , and that you were both bowled over by a shell just the other side of Hannebeke stream ? That 's how you got it in the foot , and Te Paheka had a chunk taken off his shoulder", "context_len": 52}}}, "beam": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 59, "context": "Father had a newspaper and a box of matches in his pocket , so , with the help of the dry heather , they soon had a glowing red blaze , and swung the kettle on a hook , fastened to the end of a long chain , which hung , in true Welsh fashion , from a great beam fixed across the chimney", "context_len": 64}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Sweet bud of the morning , what poet can speak The glories that beam in thy eye ? The rosebuds that bloom on thy fat little cheek , -- And thy round head so stuffed full of Latin and Greek , Arithmetic and Geology", "context_len": 44}}}, "gasolene": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The skipper of the gasolene liner , he being the aforesaid prominent citizen , hopped out to pick you up , and had you aboard just as the police came up", "context_len": 31}}}, "aforesaid": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "For , at every chance he got , and at times when there was no opportunity but such as he made , Ford would quote his aforesaid uncle , upon any and all subjects , to the no small disapproval of his college mates", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Item , to the said George Anderson the sum of Twenty and five Pounds yearly , provided that the said George Anderson doth fulfil to the letter the instructions set forth by me and intrusted to the keeping of the aforesaid Master Whitehead , Lawyer of Midhurst", "context_len": 47}}}, "harked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Smugglers and wreckers ! Instantly my mind harked back to the scene in the court at Winton , when Master Joseph Hawkes gave testimony against the two rascally Dorset smugglers", "context_len": 30}}}, "orderlies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then he spun around and announced with a shout that brought nurses and orderlies hurrying from the near-by wards :", "context_len": 20}}}, "wards": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 69, "context": "The hayoy\u00e1lgi , coming from the four corners of the world to light the sacred fire , the symbol of the sun , are the winds fanning it to a higher flame , and the purpose of the story is to make an oracular power of the sacred flame , by which the Holder of Breath , or Great Spirit , could be placed in communication with his Indian wards , and enabled to take care of them", "context_len": 78}}}, "wireless": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 1, "context": "Her wireless instrument was chattering to the world beyond the sky line that she was in sore straits , but if any steamers passed within unseen hailing distance they were not equipped to talk through the air", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "Presently it transpired that the transport had received a wireless message ordering her to return to Southampton , as four German submarines had been reported lying in wait at a distance of ten Miles south of the Nab Lightship", "context_len": 39}}}, "waistcoat": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "I want all the s d to go into my own pocket '' -- and here he slapped his waistcoat loudly , and uttered a harsh laugh , which showed all his broken teeth in a most disagreeable manner", "context_len": 39}}}, "orderly": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 31, "context": "The dress fastened , Jennie went slowly and unwillingly about her task of putting away her own and her brother 's clothes ; not doing so in her usual neat and orderly manner but folding them carelessly and tumbling them into the drawers in a very heedless fashion", "context_len": 48}}}, "rejoin": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 9, "context": "My brother sent a portion of his men to rejoin the army , but feels himself justified in entering at once on his winter quarters with the remainder ; in fact , since my arrival at Abingdon , the troops have all been dismissed for the winter , and the Danes have , as I said , retired to the Wight", "context_len": 61}}}, "repentant": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "I beg of you to forgive me , and to forgive me although I am not particularly repentant", "context_len": 18}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Texas was repentant through and through by that time ; he grabbed up his cap savagely and made for the door , with a reckless disregard for sore joints", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "I am not , however , one who thinks there is no chance of reform for a boy or man who has done wrong , and I think it is a fatal mistake to turn a cold shoulder on the repentant wrongdoer", "context_len": 42}}}, "shipshape": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "It would cost a sight of cash to do it all shipshape -- not that that 's much odds to me if a thing takes my fancy", "context_len": 27}}}, "cochran": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 0, "context": "Cochran might be , but he could learn nothing from the nurse , except that he was the wealthy gentleman who had brought him to the hospital in his automobile", "context_len": 30}}}, "overnight": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "They had been warned overnight that the coast beyond the cape was so precipitous , that there would be no villages at which to make inquiries , and arranged with Fosco that the ships should anchor north of the cape , and that he should go on at once to inspect the next bay", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Tell the able seaman that I was n't as crazy as I seemed when I bought the Sea Witch overnight", "context_len": 21}}}, "mightily": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "However , he bathed my hand in seawater , although the salt did make it smart mightily , and , tying it with a strip of wet linen , he told me to keep quiet , so as not unduly to excite my head , which was by now throbbing like to burst", "context_len": 53}}}, "roanoke": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Roanoke", "context_len": 1}}}, "rereading": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Next forenoon he was rereading this message for something like the tenth time when Captain Bracewell was shown into the room", "context_len": 21}}}, "tenth": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Cousins to the ninth and tenth degree turned up for the fair , distant relations from remote districts or former inhabitants who had left the neighbourhood and 'got on ' in other places availed themselves of cheap excursions , and visited their early home , partly for the holiday , and partly for the sake of meeting everyone else", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "The night preceding the tenth of June he passed at his Cutcherry or Office ; and in the early morning news was brought that his own house had been set on fire", "context_len": 32}}}, "derelict": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "No longer a stranded derelict , but a leader of men , gravely rejoicing in the strength and beauty of the Sea Witch , Captain Bracewell looked every inch a proper seaman to command this queen of the old-time Yankee merchant marine", "context_len": 42}}}, "shipmaster": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The derelict shipmaster smiled , and said to David :", "context_len": 10}}}, "leathery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "There is an extra room in our -- in our -- '' He hesitated , and a bit of color came into his leathery cheek as he finished : `` We can find a room for you close by us", "context_len": 40}}}, "welfare": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 43, "context": "I ask for no earthly remuneration ; my position in life renders me independent of any exertions of my own ; I pray but for God \u2019 s blessing upon my attempts to instruct His lambs in the things which concern their everlasting welfare ; and deeply gratified should I feel , were my little work to be classed among the numerous valuable publications which you have already given to the world", "context_len": 72}}}, "pitifully": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 58, "context": "It was easy to see that they wanted to take care of him , but , for his own part , he felt a kind of guardian care for the welfare of the two `` Pilgrims , '' and he was very glad of the chance to be with them at a time when Captain Bracewell was so pitifully unlike his reliant self", "context_len": 63}}}, "lavishly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "Inside the carriage a candlelamp burned , throwing a dim light on the occupants ; and , brief as was my glimpse , I saw that they were lavishly attired , and wore lovelocks under their plumed beaver hats", "context_len": 39}}}, "befriended": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 61, "context": "They were pursued by strange men , when they landed to witness a large grain elevator on fire , and eventually they succeeded in causing the breaking up of a gang of Chinese smugglers , and a band of scoundrels who were secretly taking valuable minerals from a cave , under land owned by a man whom the boys had once befriended", "context_len": 62}}}, "telephoned": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 23, "context": "Ramsey to bring you all the way out here , besides his clerks told me that he had not returned , but had telephoned from New York that he would arrive this evening", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Ramsey or any of her party came in or telephoned inquiries , that they were to be told instantly the little girls were there", "context_len": 24}}}, "cyclone": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "Baby this time had no friend to hide behind ; Harris was lying on the ground , face down , as a man might do to protect himself in a cyclone", "context_len": 31}}}, "motherly": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 31, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}}}, "portly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 47, "context": "I discovered , upon reaching home , that it would be quite impossible for me to manage my property and at the same time follow the sea ; at my mother 's earnest entreaty , therefore , I gave up the latter ; and am now a portly grey-headed county squire , a J", "context_len": 54}}}, "director": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Why , of course , he had seen his portrait and his name as the head of a great Trust , and a director in railroads , banks , and corporations by the dozen", "context_len": 34}}}, "railroads": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` Well , '' said Lem , `` you give me these clothes , and now I 'd just as lieve he 'd give me one of his old hats and a red shirt ; so I 'd be decent-like ; and then I 'd like him to get me to be an engine driver on one of them railroads", "context_len": 60}}}, "corporations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Why , of course , he had seen his portrait and his name as the head of a great Trust , and a director in railroads , banks , and corporations by the dozen", "context_len": 34}}}, "curt": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}}}, "mackerel": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 75, "context": "Mark Penelly had gone over to the other side of the lugger while the conversation was going on , and he did not face the man he looked upon as his rival ; while Harry , unnoticed by the busy fishers as he swam round , went on , touching the sides of the lugger as he lightly swam , but only the next moment to find himself entangled in a quantity of the thin mackerel net , which seemed somehow to descend upon him like a cloud , and before he could realise the fact he was under water , hopelessly fettered by the net , and feeling that if he could not extricate himself directly he should be a dead man", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "At first sight nothing seems more frail than a herring or mackerel net , one of those slight pieces of mesh-work that , in a continuation of lengths perhaps half-a-mile long , is let down into the sea to float with the tide , ready for the shoals of fish that dart against it as it forms a filmy wall across their way", "context_len": 63}}}, "scoundrels": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 39, "context": "They were pursued by strange men , when they landed to witness a large grain elevator on fire , and eventually they succeeded in causing the breaking up of a gang of Chinese smugglers , and a band of scoundrels who were secretly taking valuable minerals from a cave , under land owned by a man whom the boys had once befriended", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Here , what are you scoundrels doing to my old sofa ? '' exclaimed a voice", "context_len": 17}}}, "hale": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 72, "context": "The Spaniards did not relish this , and were going to board the tiny English craft , but again they were forestalled ; for Cochrane with all his men took the Gamo by storm , killed some , and frightened others ; and ere long a marvellous sight was witnessed at Minorca , the great Gamo was brought by the Speedy into the harbour , with over 263 men on board , hale and hearty , whilst Cochrane never had a fifth of that number !", "context_len": 86}}}, "ambulance": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "In front of a partly demolished barn , over which was flying a Geneva Cross flag , covered ambulance motors were being filled up with wounded , who , their injuries attended to , were being dispatched to the base hospital", "context_len": 41}}}, "scrape": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 25, "context": "Well , now , let Harold , who is pretty well out of the notion of Ted already , come to hear of this last scrape , and , youngster as he is , I believe he 'd throw him over ; and Ted , you know , would n't stand any nonsense of that sort and would tell Harold 'to go his own way and welcome , ' and who knows what the upshot of that would be ! If Ted does not feel he must make an effort to lead a different sort of life for Harold 's sake , he may come to the conclusion that the thing 's not worth trying", "context_len": 115}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "\u2019 Get a whole lot of pitch wood , and burn it in some tight thing , so as to keep in the smoke ; the black will stick to the sides , and you can scrape it off , as good lampblack as you can buy , and better than half of it", "context_len": 54}}}, "damaged": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 7, "context": "He had no difficulty in getting half-a-dozen damaged mackerel down in the harbour -- fish that had been torn by the nets ; but he was only just in time , for in the soft grey light he could see the gulls already busy floating down on their ghostly-looking wings in the gloom , uttering a mournful , peevish wail , and carrying off fragments of fish for their morning meal", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The balloon had been so damaged that it did not drag along , and though most of the instruments were smashed their lives were saved", "context_len": 25}}}, "coddle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I am not very strong , you know , and they sort of coddle me , and trot me around to health resorts like a set of china done up in cotton", "context_len": 32}}}, "resorts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I am not very strong , you know , and they sort of coddle me , and trot me around to health resorts like a set of china done up in cotton", "context_len": 32}}}, "demanding": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 76, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "millionaire": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 27, "context": "In the second volume of the series , called `` Ned Wilding 's Disappearance , '' a story was told of how Ned tried to become a millionaire on his own account", "context_len": 32}}}, "tutors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "David demanded to know all about his automobiles , his horses , and his yacht , his trips to Florida and California , his private tutors , and his several homes among which he flitted to and fro like an uneasy bird", "context_len": 42}}}, "magnate": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 24, "context": "The back room was now nearly full of cases of fruit , a grand boxed-up display of fireworks for Colonel Harrington , the village magnate , another for a local club , some minor boxes for private family use , and extra orders from the city for the village storekeepers", "context_len": 50}}}, "kidnapping": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "He seemed more like the son of some professional man only moderately well off -- what did he mean by the kidnapping story ?", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "After the kidnapping traders had been harrying the islands , one of the chiefs said that , if the bishop would only bring a man-of-war and get him vengeance on his adversaries , he would be exalted like his Father above", "context_len": 41}}}, "lordly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "Altogether the vehicle had a lordly air , easily matching that of the individual sitting alone on the purple cushions -- a man whose features were not very clear at the distance , although the yellowness of his beard , the glitter of his studded shirt-front , and whole consequential , expansive effect recalled to the doctor 's mind an image of the past , less ornate , indeed , and affluent , but of similar aspect", "context_len": 77}}}, "stubborn": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 57, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "suntanned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "It seemed as rash to question the orders of this lordly parent as to disobey Captain Thrasher , but there was a look of stubborn resolution in the suntanned jaw of the young sailor and he was not to be so easily driven", "context_len": 43}}}, "kinfolk": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The kinfolk and friends of those on board had been kept in a state of panicky alarm , day after day , by the flaring newspaper head-lines which sent the Roanoke to the bottom and raised her again , in hourly `` extras", "context_len": 43}}}, "astonishing": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 28, "context": "How very great and wonderful He must be ! It seems surprising that He should care for every one on this little ball , -- how much more astonishing that He should have condescended to come and live upon it , to have appeared as a feeble Child in one of the worlds that He had made , and then actually to die , like one of the creatures that He had formed ! Is not God \u2019 s power wonderful , and His love more wonderful still ?", "context_len": 89}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "Yet , astonishing to say , I was marvellously successful , all things considered , for when at a loss I administered pills compounded of meal dough and strongly flavoured with the first harmless substance that came to hand , and so profound was the belief of these people in my ability that at least half of them were cured by the wonderful power of faith alone", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "And the boss says they 've thinned out astonishing , and they can talk a good bit", "context_len": 17}}}, "galled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Still more he rejoiced at being appointed his instructor , as it relieved him from all menial work which , although preferable to that to which the bulk of the slaves were condemned , yet galled his spirit infinitely", "context_len": 39}}}, "domineering": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "The authorities of the army , who now had the proud and domineering spirit which had so long excited their hatred and envy completely in their power , listened for a time to what Philotas had to say in his own justification", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Bart was shocked at this exhibition of cold-blooded villainy on the part of a representative member of the community , although he had never had much use for the pompous , domineering old tyrant , who now led the way through the silent Streets of Pleasantville as meek as a lamb", "context_len": 51}}}, "towing": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 48, "context": "When he judged it to be about ten o'clock he went down to the shore again , took off his clothes and made them into a bundle ; then , wading out into the water to within fifty yards of the felucca , swam off to it , towing the bundle behind him", "context_len": 53}}}, "misjudgments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Cochran 's hasty misjudgments , the cadet 's night was restless and slightly fevered", "context_len": 14}}}, "fevered": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Cochran 's hasty misjudgments , the cadet 's night was restless and slightly fevered", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "It seemed as if some healing touch had awakened him from a fevered dream , and recalled him to saner and earlier memories than those that tortured him ; and when he stretched his weary limbs on the unwonted luxury of a bed , the old dreams awoke and bore him company all night long", "context_len": 55}}}, "thatch": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 65, "context": "the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner , and the oak dresser , with its rows of blue willow-pattern plates ; and back again at last to where Mrs", "context_len": 128}}}, "dispelled": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 52, "context": "The wedding rejoicings continued all that day , with dancing-women and singing-women , and all the instruments of mirth and minstrelsy were smitten , whilst the queen and the Vizier and his son were exceeding assiduous in keeping up the festivities , so the Lady Bedrulbudour should rejoice and her chagrin be dispelled ; nay , they left nought that day of that which exciteth unto liesse but they did it before her , so she should leave what was in her mind and be cheered", "context_len": 86}}}, "seafarer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Perhaps he had a trace of the superstition which can be found in the hardest-headed seafarer", "context_len": 16}}}, "sheerest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The listener chewed the ends of his mustache for a while , and then his chin dropped and his mouth stayed open in sheerest amazement", "context_len": 25}}}, "stark": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Where they had met to pray , there they lay in death ! With a deep sigh Edmund recognised Ednoth , bishop of Dorchester , lying stark and stiff in his bloody robes", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "And to talk of buying her as if she were the toy which had floated on the dinner table ! It was all stark nonsense , yet his kindled imagination could not help painting bright pictures", "context_len": 36}}}, "florid": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "As Ben \u2019 s chained tongue was seeking to free itself a stout , square , bald-headed , florid man , with a square-trimmed tuft of iron-gray whiskers on his chin , appeared in the doorway of a lighted room off the hall , and a healthy , hearty voice cried :", "context_len": 52}}}, "quoth": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` `` O my son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Therewith he left him and returning to the Sultan , told him that the Lady Bedrulbudour 's story was true ; whereupon quoth the Sultan , `` Since the case is thus , we need no wedding-festivities", "context_len": 38}}}, "refineries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Cochran has just bought out the Columbia sugar refineries , hook , line , and sinker", "context_len": 16}}}, "sinker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "For some time I found no bottom with four fathoms , which was the available length of the line , and I was on the point of giving up the task with a feeling that we were clear of the shallows , when I felt the sinker touch bottom", "context_len": 49}}}, "fetches": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "I often says there ai n't a better husband and father to be found than Sergeant Richards , look the city through ; and you do deserve the best , that 's a fact , if it was gold and diamonds ; not that you would n't have a better use for them than to eat 'em ; diamonds fetches a heap , they tell me , but never havin ' had none of my own , I ca n't rightly tell of my own showin '", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Dat \u2019 s de way I allers fetches \u2019 em ; I frows myself on my back and dey \u2019 re gone !", "context_len": 23}}}, "cargoes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "The five prizes turned out , when their cargoes were landed , to be much more valuable than the cursory examination made by the knights had warranted them in expecting", "context_len": 30}}}, "controls": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Think , my son , over all I have told you ; you will decide like one who yet controls his senses , and not disgrace your aged father", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 21, "context": "Barry found The Colonel \u2019 s Lady as Hap had named their new craft , strangely quick and light on the controls , compared with her big sister Rosy", "context_len": 29}}}, "robbin": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Oh , you villain ! Aint it enough to go robbin ' orchards and melon patches , and farmers ' wagons market-days , but you must be fighting and knocking down babies like these to get what 's not your own ? If you do n't see the inside of the county jail for this , my name 's not Susan Jones", "context_len": 63}}}, "charter": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Is it too late to patch it up ? Ca n't we charter a hack and overhaul Stanley P", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "We have n't any charter but we can have one , I reckon", "context_len": 13}}}, "hack": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "Harris , only it seems to me that people are often in too much of a hurry to make use of the word , for you ca n't he certain until you are able to look hack upon a thing whether it was surely of God 's ordering or man 's short-sighted scheming", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 43, "context": "Do you go over to our house , and ask John to give you some turnip-seed , and sow it on that ground you burned over when you was roasting Joe Griffin , and see what turnips will grow there ; you can hack the seed in with the hoe ; turnips will sell first rate in the West Indies ; I \u2019 ll tell them they are Yankee yams", "context_len": 70}}}, "prodigal": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Leonnatus reproved him for this extravagance , and told him that when he became master of the countries where these costly gums were procured , he might be as prodigal of them as he pleased , but that in the mean time it would be proper for him to be more prudent and economical", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "And I have resolved to seek once more , as a prodigal son , the land of my birth , where science is fostered instead of being repressed as in this hotbed of prejudice and ignorance", "context_len": 36}}}, "error": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 8, "context": "Bi ' l ghewali may be a clerical error for bi ' l ghalibi , `` for the most part , in general , '' in which case we may read , `` Folk in general abound [ in talk ] anent her virtues ; '' or bi ' l ghewali may perhaps be used in the sense ( of which use , however , I know no instance ) of 'in excessive estimation , ' in which latter case the passage might be rendered , `` Folk abound in setting a high value on ( or extolling ) her virtues", "context_len": 101}}}, "jolted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "Fred displayed an enormous melon which had been ripe for some days , and was now rather too mellow and soft , and , having been jolted somewhat severely on its ride down the mountain , had fallen to pieces , presenting , as joking Fred said , `` a very melon choly sight", "context_len": 54}}}, "hazards": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "Remember that ere long every sword may be of the utmost consequence in the defence of Rhodes , and that even the capture of pirates may be too dearly purchased ; but that , at the same time , the honour of the flag of the Order must be upheld at all hazards", "context_len": 53}}}, "careers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Further particulars of the careers of Sir Colin Campbell , John Cassell , General Gordon , Sir Henry Havelock , Joseph Livesey , David Livingstone , Robert Moffat , George Moore , Florence Nightingale , Lord Shaftesbury , Agnes Weston , and other men and women whose example has benefited the country , will be found in an attractive series of books issued under the title of The World 's Workers", "context_len": 71}}}, "grit": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 74, "context": "Following the course of the brook for some distance , they came to where the soil changed to a stiff clay , and the brook was obstructed by an old beaver-dam , causing the water in many places to stand in little pools , in the bottom of which , and in the shelves of the rock which formed the bed of the brook , was a sediment of yellow mud , devoid of grit , and fine as flour", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Look out ; there may be another fire , '' retorted Gerhart with a grin , and the sophomores could only grit their teeth", "context_len": 25}}}, "mastery": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 35, "context": "The light of day was rapidly fading ; a light breeze brought down the remaining leaves from the trees , or whirled them about in all directions ; winter was plainly about to assume the mastery of the scene , as was evident from the clothing the people wore , the thick fur and warm woollen cloaks which covered their light tunics", "context_len": 62}}}, "navigating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Because he was strong , quick , and obedient , the navigating officers took a genuine interest in his welfare", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "On going on deck I found that , as the next officer fit for duty after Drake , I was put in charge of the starboard watch , and had to take my share in the responsibility of navigating the Gannet to the nearest port", "context_len": 45}}}, "taffrail": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 54, "context": "The smoke of the guns lay so heavy on the water , and the combatants were so intent upon the struggle at the breach , that Gervaise steered his galley into the midst of the Turkish vessels laden with troops ready to disembark , without attracting any notice ; then , standing upon the taffrail , he signalled to the two outside boats to throw off their ropes and make for the Turkish ship nearest to them", "context_len": 77}}}, "chart": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 38, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}}}, "cargo": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 16, "context": "It appeared that he was ashore at the moment making arrangements for the shipment of a cargo of slaves on the next day ; but the chief mate was aboard , and upon representing myself to him as a shipwrecked Englishman who had been carried away captive into the interior , and had just effected my escape , he gave me permission to remain , saying that he had no doubt Captain Duquesne would receive me if I were willing to work my passage to Martinique", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The farther was a cargo vessel of six thousand tons", "context_len": 10}}}, "derricks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The bos'n taught him what few tricks of old-fashioned seamanship were left to learn in a vessel whose spars were no more than cargo derricks", "context_len": 25}}}, "intricate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 58, "context": "In double-column line ahead the greyhounds of the sea tore to meet the approaching troopships , then , at a signal from the senior officer , the destroyers `` broke line '' , tearing hither and thither seemingly without order or reason -- zigzagging , pirouetting , and crossing each others ' bows as if participating in an intricate maritime dance", "context_len": 61}}}, "organization": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 29, "context": "He would always turn the conversation from them to inquire about the geographical position of the different Persian countries , the various routes leading into the interior , the organization of the Asiatic armies , their system of military tactics , and , especially , the character and habits of Artaxerxes , the Persian king", "context_len": 55}}}, "myriad": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "And he realized what it must mean to be that calm and ever-ready man in the captain 's cabin , whose mind was in touch with every one of these myriad activities by night and day", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 82, "context": "But I soon discovered that even this small measure of mercy was to be denied me ; food and drink were indeed to be served out to us -- in order , as I surmised , that we might meet the ordeal in store for us with unabated strength -- but the night was to be passed , as the day had been , secured to the sacrificial post , exposed , naked and helpless , to the elements and to the myriad insect plagues that attacked us unceasingly", "context_len": 89}}}, "activities": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "In the early morning , before the activities of the day have begun , this wharf is a deserted and forbidding place , and on one such early morning in September , with chill air and cloudy skies , and not even a rose tint in the dull east , there was no one to be seen throughout the whole length of the wharf save one slowly moving boy", "context_len": 69}}}, "waifs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "And then Sara , leaning on the counter , made her little proposal concerning the dreadful days and the hungry waifs and the buns", "context_len": 24}}}, "shuttle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Every time the Roanoke wove her way back to New York , like a giant shuttle plying over a vast blue carpet , the cadet was with Margaret and her grandfather as often as he was allowed ashore", "context_len": 38}}}, "yearned": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` And the Lady Bedrulbudour said to him , `` Go and bring her to me ; marry , this long while past I have still heard of her gifts and excellences and have yearned to see her , so I may ask a blessing of her , for that the folk are beyond measure abundant [ in talk ] of her [ 646 ] virtues", "context_len": 66}}}, "stevedore": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Captain Bracewell had not found the ship for which he yearned , but his former owners had given him a berth as stevedore on their wharf , and in faithful drudgery he earned a living and a home for Margaret", "context_len": 40}}}, "drudgery": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "Imagine for an instant the drudgery of working a long division sum with leaden type and raised , figures ; think of all the difficulty of placing the figures , and the chances of doing the sum wrong ; and then it will not cause surprise that the blind girl could never enjoy arithmetic , although in mental calculation she showed herself later on to be very clever", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "'Blessed be drudgery , for it is the grey angel of success", "context_len": 12}}}, "barge": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 2, "context": "As each barge passed there was a lively exchange of greetings between their crews and those of the stationary boats ; but , in spite of the fact that the Carrs had picked up several German words during their period of captivity , the hidden listeners were unable to understand the conversation , beyond the knowledge that it referred largely to the air raid of the previous night", "context_len": 68}}}, "regrets": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 43, "context": "The Christmas holidays being over , and Archie in the rudest of health , Miss Forster had no more excuse for keeping that young gentleman at home , and after much packing and preparation , he departed to school , amid the fond regrets of his aunt and the lamentations of his friends at the Abbey , to whom he promised to write as often as time and the regulations of his house would allow him", "context_len": 76}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Certainly , though he now regrets it", "context_len": 7}}}, "anchor": {"freq": 62, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 11, "context": "Others would make for one of the cinnamon-sailed luggers lying at anchor , to go round and back , or would get into one of the boats ; while some , more venturesome , or really more confident in their powers over the water , would go boldly out , perhaps a mile , to meet some lugger coming in from the fishing-ground , sure of being taken aboard and riding back abreast of the boulders where they had left their clothes", "context_len": 82}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 54, "context": "We will send a boat ashore to try and find out from the natives they may come across whether any vessels , bearing the appearance of Moorish corsairs , have been seen passing up the coast , and also to find out what bays and inlets there are where they would be likely to anchor", "context_len": 55}}}, "kindle": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 68, "context": "But Dolly turned sulky in her turn , and refused to say a word more ; and Lem , knowing it was useless to try to make her speak when she did not choose , strolled into the woods to see if he could find any berries for his breakfast ; while she , still shivering from her night 's exposure in the Ice Glen , tried to kindle a fire from the wet sticks which lay around ; and finding this in vain , crept to her wretched bed , and tried to warm herself there", "context_len": 97}}}, "fidgety": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Grandfather misses you more than he thinks he lets me see , and he is almost as fidgety as when we landed from the dear old Pilgrim", "context_len": 27}}}, "coastwise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "After being chief officer in that coastwise steamer for three years , he was silly enough to play a joke on his skipper in Charleston last week", "context_len": 27}}}, "barber": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The captain was in a barber shop ashore , getting his whiskers cut off for the summer season", "context_len": 18}}}, "whiskers": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 16, "context": "At the gate they paused , for it was shut , and he of the gray whiskers , after fumbling at the latch with his whip , was evidently preparing with much reluctance to dismount , when , spying Peggy , he called to her , and asked her to open it", "context_len": 52}}}, "pricked": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 57, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}}}, "explosion": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 10, "context": "Before the debris flung high in the air by the explosion had fallen , the pirate ship had sunk beneath the waves , taking our flying jibboom and part of the jibboom with her , while a heavy pall of smoke covered the place where a moment before she was lying like a wounded animal at bay", "context_len": 57}}}, "smack": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "Kept out of sight , hoping , in a manner o ' speaking , that the Dutchy would have a smack at us , and then she 'd butt in", "context_len": 30}}}, "hackman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Of course , the hackman had not expected to be caught this way , but he had to go or else the captain would have broken his neck for him , at least that is what he said he would do", "context_len": 41}}}, "cab": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 59, "context": "He just entered the drawing-room quickly one morning after breakfast , and shook hands with mother and shook hands with me , and said that he would be back again before either of us had missed him , and then went downstairs , and I watched behind the curtain as his luggage was put on the roof of the cab", "context_len": 60}}}, "fathom": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "At that instant the thing upon which my gaze was fixed , and which looked like half a fathom of stiff tarred lanyard , darted with lightning swiftness at the girl and coiled itself about her shapely bare arm , while a piercing scream rang out from her pallid lips", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Lad , '' he exclaimed , `` I would I could fathom this mystery ! Thy father \u2019 s slayer is no mean reaver or cutpurse ; yet , though we have him safe by the heels , manacled and leg-ironed , and threaten him with the thumbscrews , never a word can be wrung from him", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "exaggerator": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Becket says they were a fathom long , but he is a terrible exaggerator , as you know", "context_len": 18}}}, "margaret": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "MARGARET", "context_len": 1}}}, "misunderstandings": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "But , Peggy , take my advice , and if you quarrel with anyone , go and fight it out at once , and get it over , and do n't let misunderstandings make the breach so wide that nothing can ever mend it again", "context_len": 45}}}, "lilies": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "' I suppose not , ' said Peggy , settling herself on a low wooden seat , at a safe distance from the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner", "context_len": 128}}}, "mariner": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 69, "context": "seen service under Prince Rupert ; Arthur Conolly , an Irish veteran who had served in the Low Countries , and who had come over from Chichester for the occasion ; Arthur Lewis , a gentleman of Bramshott ; Percy Young , an officer of the navy , who in his earlier days had lost a leg in the action of La Rochelle ; Herbert Collings , a master mariner of Gosport , who used to be a frequent visitor at our house , and who greatly interested me with the account of his adventures off the coast of Barbary ; and Giles Perrin , the landlord of the `` Flying Bull '' , who modestly seated himself on a stool in a remote corner of the room", "context_len": 128}}}, "boisterously": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "David read the letter to the bos'n , expecting sympathy , but that hard-hearted mariner laughed boisterously , and said :", "context_len": 21}}}, "vat": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` He got vat was comin ' to him , the red-headed old sundowner", "context_len": 14}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` It was vat you call a tight squeak , '' explained David 's faithful shipmate , the bos'n", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "sundowner": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The red-headed old sundowner ! He is a rascal , is that Becket man ! ''", "context_len": 16}}}, "ho": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 1, "context": "Heigh ho ! how dark it is getting ! Just the sort of place to meet with a ghost of Hamlet , not the sort of hamlet that I \u2019 m looking after , for I have done with theatrical effects , -- I wish that I had done with the effects of cold", "context_len": 54}}}, "trudged": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 5, "context": "Up the steep bank they trudged , through fields of bracken higher than Bobby 's head , getting out at last upon a white road that wound along the top of a cliff , with a stream roaring below , till round a sudden turn they came upon Pengarth , a prim little village , nestling in a hollow , and consisting of a short street and a big chapel and school-house", "context_len": 72}}}, "rascal": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` That young'un 'll get in prison some day ; you see ! He 's a rascal through and through , a mean dirty spalpeen , a holy terror ! And if they set to blaming you , I 'll threaten to leave ; that I will", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 16, "context": "But active as he was , he soon found he was no match for the young rascal in front of him , whose feet scarcely seemed to touch the ground , and who threw himself headlong over fences and hedges , as though he had forgotten he had a neck and limbs which might be broken", "context_len": 56}}}, "hoodooed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` It seems as if all my family is hoodooed about keeping their berths afloat", "context_len": 15}}}, "tuned": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Clean and tuned up after her overhauling , the crack ship of the Black Star Line was fit for a record run across the Atlantic", "context_len": 25}}}, "seaworthiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Nor had Captain Thrasher ever felt more pride and confidence in the power , speed , and seaworthiness of the Roanoke than when he dropped the Dutch pilot off Flushing a few days later and signalled `` full speed ahead , '' with Sandy Hook a week away and waiting wives and sweethearts `` hauling on the towline", "context_len": 58}}}, "signalled": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 57, "context": "The smoke of the guns lay so heavy on the water , and the combatants were so intent upon the struggle at the breach , that Gervaise steered his galley into the midst of the Turkish vessels laden with troops ready to disembark , without attracting any notice ; then , standing upon the taffrail , he signalled to the two outside boats to throw off their ropes and make for the Turkish ship nearest to them", "context_len": 77}}}, "sweethearts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "Nor had Captain Thrasher ever felt more pride and confidence in the power , speed , and seaworthiness of the Roanoke than when he dropped the Dutch pilot off Flushing a few days later and signalled `` full speed ahead , '' with Sandy Hook a week away and waiting wives and sweethearts `` hauling on the towline", "context_len": 58}}}, "towline": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "Nor had Captain Thrasher ever felt more pride and confidence in the power , speed , and seaworthiness of the Roanoke than when he dropped the Dutch pilot off Flushing a few days later and signalled `` full speed ahead , '' with Sandy Hook a week away and waiting wives and sweethearts `` hauling on the towline", "context_len": 58}}}, "girlhood": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 15, "context": "The Outram of the book is just the Outram who was the admiration of our girlhood , -- generous , chivalrous , noble ! One feels how much pain that fine spirit would have been saved , had he realised how little it really matters whether good service be appreciated or not by man , if the great Leader accept it , -- if all be done as to Him Who never overlooks or misunderstands ! To our own Master we stand or fall ; let earthly superiors say what they will", "context_len": 92}}}, "cordial": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` I am no fit company for quiet folks and dainty maidens ; but , '' he added rising , `` it hath been a cordial to see the face of a friend , and the memory of it will abide long with me", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "I had already poured a rich cordial down his throat , and he was coming to himself , my brother aiding me , when the sheriff , grand in his robe and chain of office , came up", "context_len": 38}}}, "unbent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Now and then he unbent a trifle at sea and asked David if Captain Bracewell had found another ship", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Lola often unbent to talk", "context_len": 5}}}, "resolute": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 3, "context": "She was very resolute ; very persevering ; very affectionate ; reserved , yet demonstrative ; untidy , yet methodical ; exceedingly anxious for the happiness of all around ; apt often to think people better than they really were ; generous to a fault ; unselfishly ready at all times to put her own wishes aside ; vehement and impulsive , yet never in a hurry or flurry ; unyielding , yet tender ; severe , yet frisky", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 96, "context": "How came it that this girl had guessed his secret at once when most people passed him , taking him but for a sturdy beggar ? What made her suspect him of being a fugitive ? Was her offer of shelter but careless good nature , or a heroic endeavour to save a hunted man ? At any rate he had not fallen so low as to draw suspicion on a woman , and a young woman to boot , although she was plainly no nervous , fanciful , fine lady , but a bright , resolute , country girl , with good health and high spirits gleaming from every flash of her bright eyes , and every turn of her auburn head", "context_len": 123}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "The arms of the men are light , and they are not accustomed to fighting on foot ; and on a sudden incursion of an enemy at midnight into their camp , their horses and their horsemanship are alike useless , and they fall an easy prey to resolute invaders", "context_len": 50}}}, "outshining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "In his eyes , Captain Stephen Thrasher was the most enviable man alive , far outshining presidents and kings", "context_len": 19}}}, "masterful": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "The union in him of strength with gentleness , of a masterful intellect with a spirit of yielding courtesy , of nobility with playfulness , of generosity with self-restraint , of real religious conviction and experience with frolicsome gaiety , made a combination not more rare than beautiful", "context_len": 48}}}, "shuffle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Once on deck , the new-fledged able seaman danced a shuffle and cracked his heels together", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "He call me by my name , an ' den it come over me dat we done got mix ' up in de shuffle an ' dat I wuz shootin ' at you", "context_len": 33}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He saw Lem Wacker shuffle into view , glance keenly around , fix his eye on Baker , and steal into the room and sit down directly behind that mysterious individual", "context_len": 31}}}, "mettle": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 53, "context": "Whit Monday was a holiday at school , however , and Peggy and Bobby , having the day at home , took a sudden fit of industry , and started to weed the shrubbery with the noble intention of having it raked over and tidied by teatime , being put somewhat on their mettle by Father 's remarks on the subject of sustained labour , and his laughing incredulity when they assured him he would find it all neatly finished when he returned from Warford that evening", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "This kick was highly resented by that young person , who , as she said to herself , stiffened her neck on the spot and determined to show what mettle she was made of", "context_len": 34}}}, "toilers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "For twelve or fourteen hours a day these poor little toilers had to sit in the mines , opening and shutting trap doors as the coal was pushed along in barrows", "context_len": 31}}}, "millions": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 42, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}}}, "resourceful": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Quick-witted and resourceful , Slim , after strenuous efforts to gain the world \u2019 s endurance record , faces more odds when he makes a perilous trip carrying a cargo of gold", "context_len": 32}}}, "pygmies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "They were like bold and resourceful pygmies among the mighty monsters of clanging steel which they were ready to tame and check at the call from above", "context_len": 27}}}, "monsters": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "So , with a sigh , he restrained the impulse and , edging his way to the right , he made a wide circle about the nest of snakes , watching the ground carefully , lest one of the monsters should cross his path", "context_len": 44}}}, "shrouded": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Through a long night the Roanoke groped her way over a shrouded sea on which the fog hung so thick that the ghostly figures on the bridge could not see the bow of their own ship", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "If I had to suffer martyrdom , in no form could it appear more attractive than by being thrown over Niagara ! To be launched into eternity , shrouded in that cascade of diamonds , would rouse such a thrilling sense of the beautiful and the sublime , that half one \u2019 s fears would be swallowed up in something almost like joy", "context_len": 63}}}, "blackness": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 1, "context": "The blackness that precedes the dawn is always greatest ; the shore was invisible , and our sole guide as to direction was the wind , which we hoped still blew from the same quarter", "context_len": 35}}}, "cautious": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 46, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}}}, "fluttered": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 3, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "longitude": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 78, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}}}, "icebergs": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "But on the next day , the 4th , we beheld icebergs indeed , -- I believe more than a hundred , and some , O how glorious ! Our eyes were satiated with beauty", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "thanked rocks moved friends helping good-bye over apart icebergs", "context_len": 9}}}, "temperature": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 14, "context": "When David returned to the deck an officer was already making reports of the temperature of the water , and Captain Thrasher was standing with head cocked and a hand at his ear , listening , on the chance that the clamor of the fog-whistle might fling back a telltale echo from some hidden mountain of ice that lay in ambush", "context_len": 61}}}, "fling": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 61, "context": "`` Now , sir , I warrant you know by experience that a broadsword is a good enough thing to have in your fist on the field of battle ; but , the war over , 't is neither fit for a lady 's chamber nor for a courtier 's duello ; 't is but a commodity of rusty iron to fling in the lumber-room", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Well , well , young folks must have their fling , I suppose", "context_len": 13}}}, "telltale": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "When David returned to the deck an officer was already making reports of the temperature of the water , and Captain Thrasher was standing with head cocked and a hand at his ear , listening , on the chance that the clamor of the fog-whistle might fling back a telltale echo from some hidden mountain of ice that lay in ambush", "context_len": 61}}}, "ambush": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 60, "context": "When David returned to the deck an officer was already making reports of the temperature of the water , and Captain Thrasher was standing with head cocked and a hand at his ear , listening , on the chance that the clamor of the fog-whistle might fling back a telltale echo from some hidden mountain of ice that lay in ambush", "context_len": 61}}}, "sparking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Before long David was ordered to stand by the wireless operator 's room and fetch to the bridge any messages that might leap from his rattling , sparking instruments", "context_len": 29}}}, "pearly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Irene opened those deep sapphire-blue eyes , which were one of her greatest charms , to their fullest extent ; her little mouth pouted , and some pearly teeth showed beneath", "context_len": 31}}}, "starless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "As the chill and dripping air changed from pearly gray to starless gloom , the hoarse and frequent whistle seemed to be appealing for guidance on this sightless sea", "context_len": 29}}}, "unceasingly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 70, "context": "The hop-gathering was over , and the hoppers -- a motley crew -- had returned to the slums of Stafford and Birmingham , the men not sorry to cease hard work , but the poor , draggled women and the little children with a wistful good-bye to the green fields , and all , I think , with a half sigh of regret for the Rector , who had toiled unceasingly among them during their brief stay , hoping , if by ever so little , to raise the hopeless lives and the sodden minds to a knowledge of higher things", "context_len": 101}}}, "vigilant": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 41, "context": "It looked as if the Indian while crawling over the ground and hidden by the grass , had changed his mind and deliberately risen to his feet , where he must have known he would be in full view of the vigilant white men", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Watch out , Billy , watch out , '' muttered the vigilant Fischer to himself , as he caught the gleam in Mark 's eye", "context_len": 26}}}, "indicator": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Language is not an absolute indicator of race , but it is more so in America than elsewhere , for the large number of linguistic families in the western hemisphere proves that the populations speaking their dialects have suffered less than in the eastern by encroachment , foreign admixture , forcible alteration or entire destruction", "context_len": 55}}}, "screws": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "When with Chris it was forward clear to the bow to lean over the rail and see the magnificent prow cut the water ; or way to the stern , to watch the far-shining train , the screws churned into white foam behind them ; or an hour 'midships , where the ever-varying amusements with which the steerage passengers beguile the weary hours can be looked down upon from the saloon deck of either first or second cabin", "context_len": 78}}}, "rivets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The Roanoke shook herself as if her rivets were pulling out , as the engines strove to hold her back , but the momentum of the vast bulk could not be checked on the instant", "context_len": 35}}}, "momentum": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 22, "context": "It was somewhat of a surprise , therefore , for him to find his book flying out of one hand with a momentum that swept the cigar out of the other ; but he did not need to look far or long for an explanation", "context_len": 45}}}, "grinding": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He was so near to the grinding wheels of the vehicle and kicking hoofs of the horses that Bart relaxed the bridles", "context_len": 22}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": ", but a fine , thoughtful young Man , -- who had been given a New Testament , which he is reading every day , and who sat down on the floor , and quietly , gravely , asked me to explain difficulties which he had met with in his reading , such as Daniel \u2019 s abomination of desolation , the two women grinding , etc", "context_len": 67}}}, "rending": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 23, "context": "Ki Sing ? '' asked Mosely , who was disposed , like the cat , to play with his victim before turning and rending him", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "There came a rending , splintering crash that nearly shattered his eardrums , a detonation that seemed to shake the earth", "context_len": 21}}}, "berg": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Now a bold iceberg rose before us , reminding me of pictures of Gibraltar ; but this berg was all of snow , [ 22 ] and , as well as we could guess , about 150 feet high", "context_len": 39}}}, "collision": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 10, "context": "The man had not been so badly stunned by his collision with the wall but that he could turn over , and by the time the negro reached him he had drawn his pistol half-way from his pocket ; but that was all", "context_len": 43}}}, "grind": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "Uncle Isaac told him there was a vessel going to Salem with timber , and he would send by the captain , who was a relative of his , and get one for him , and then grind it for him , and put in a good white-oak handle , and bend it just right", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "Before he jumped on the train , however , he had whispered , as the last of many entreaties : I know it 's all for my sake , Marie-Celeste , but all the same , it 's an awful grind on me the way you 're acting ; and if you do n't come to see it so pretty soon , your father and mother will wish they had never let you do anything for me", "context_len": 77}}}, "scores": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 36, "context": "Thursday morning , the grand morning , has arrived , -- nay , it is nearly eleven o \u2019 clock , and the children of six schools , their teachers and their mothers , and perhaps scores of women besides , will be on the tiptoe of expectation , -- and our kahar has never returned ! ! ! We don \u2019 t know whether Mrs", "context_len": 66}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "It is the one thing except death to look forward to , and there is not a man among us who does not think of it scores of times a day ; but , small as the chance is , it is greatest for those who behave best", "context_len": 48}}}, "bulkheads": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Before the steamer had ceased to grind against the pale and ghastly ice that towered above her , the water-tight doors in the scores of bulkheads were being closed by men who knew their stations in such a time as this", "context_len": 41}}}, "panic": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 42, "context": "The wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Immediately there was a panic amongst the workmen , and , throwing down their tools , they betook themselves off , vowing that neither the king nor the king \u2019 s enemies would make them resume work till the Essex had gone", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The sensation akin to panic had passed", "context_len": 7}}}, "steerage": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 57, "context": "When with Chris it was forward clear to the bow to lean over the rail and see the magnificent prow cut the water ; or way to the stern , to watch the far-shining train , the screws churned into white foam behind them ; or an hour 'midships , where the ever-varying amusements with which the steerage passengers beguile the weary hours can be looked down upon from the saloon deck of either first or second cabin", "context_len": 78}}}, "hysterical": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 7, "context": "Down in the steerage quarters hundreds of hysterical immigrants were running to and fro with prayers and screams , but a squad of hard-fisted seamen soon herded them like sheep and threatened death to any who should try to force a way to the boat deck", "context_len": 46}}}, "herded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 69, "context": "May I suggest , sir , that they should be those appropriate to a small merchant ? This might seem to account for my not being placed with the other slaves who may be on board the galley , as it would be supposed that I was set apart in order that I should be sent to one of the auberges as a servant ; and my afterwards being herded with the others would be explained by its being found that there was no opening for me in such a capacity", "context_len": 91}}}, "carpenters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Added to the dismal noises came the dull thud of the carpenters \u2019 hammers and mallets as they drove plugs into the shot holes betwixt wind and water , while the creaking of the ship \u2019 s pumps betokened that she was leaking freely", "context_len": 44}}}, "crews": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The order came from the bridge for the boat crews to stand by , ready to abandon ship if need be", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 40, "word_index": 51, "context": "As at present war has not been formally declared between us and the sultan , peaceful traders , as you know , carry on their avocations unmolested either by the warships of the Turks or by ours ; they do not enter our ports without a special permit , and the crews are never allowed to land , in order that no detailed account of our fortifications may be taken to the sultan", "context_len": 73}}}, "drill": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 34, "context": "There were two reasons : In the first place , Mark Mallory , together with the rest of the plebes , was being bullied and tormented just then in the course of a squad drill ; and , in the second place , one of the committee , Cadet Spencer , was engaged in doing the bullying , having been appointed `` on duty over plebes", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "The luckless victims begin after breakfast , and at regular ( and frequent ) periods until night are turned out under the charge of some irascible yearling to be taught all manner of military maneuvers -- setting up drill , how to stand , to face , and , in fact , how to walk", "context_len": 55}}}, "relentless": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 56, "context": "As daylight dawned upon the third day of the fight we continued our retreat , and as a faint southerly wind sprang up the enemy drew near with the intention of renewing the fight , concentrating their efforts on Albemarle \u2019 s ship , which , covering the retreat , presented an undaunted spectacle to our relentless foes", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "With relentless activity they hunted down the audacious rebels who had dared to send Charles the First to the scaffold , and few were so fortunate as to escape the fate decreed for a regicide", "context_len": 35}}}, "compartments": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Marshalled in military formation , the `` walking cases '' boarded the carriages , which were similar to the fourth-class compartments of the German State Railways -- hard wooden seats not excepted", "context_len": 32}}}, "bulkhead": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "The barrel staves , set slantwise between the sloping bank of coal and the after bulkhead , served as a roof , while , to camouflage their place of concealment , coal was piled on the boards until the new level was about the same as the original one", "context_len": 49}}}, "iceberg": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "Now a bold iceberg rose before us , reminding me of pictures of Gibraltar ; but this berg was all of snow , [ 22 ] and , as well as we could guess , about 150 feet high", "context_len": 39}}}, "eternity": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "If I had to suffer martyrdom , in no form could it appear more attractive than by being thrown over Niagara ! To be launched into eternity , shrouded in that cascade of diamonds , would rouse such a thrilling sense of the beautiful and the sublime , that half one \u2019 s fears would be swallowed up in something almost like joy", "context_len": 63}}}, "cabins": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 77, "context": "You who are so kindly anxious about my comfort will be pleased to know that I have a very fair amount of wraps , and am more likely to suffer from heat than cold , seeing that my cabin port-hole is never opened , and that the only way of ventilating it is by leaving the door open , -- a thing not to be thought of at night , as ladies \u2019 and gentlemen \u2019 s cabins are not at all in separate parts of the vessel", "context_len": 88}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "To my surprise , it was not a calf but a boy that stood in the doorway , evidently a city boy for he was smartly dressed and not clad in overalls like the children in the distance , or poor clothes like the children in the few log cabins we had passed on the way to this lonely place", "context_len": 60}}}, "sombre": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "When I had finished there was a dead pause , during which the howling blast without , as it dashed the hail against the casement , seemed a fitting accompaniment to our sombre thoughts", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "She watched the Vigils playing , the kids gamboling , the magpies squabbling ; but never a lighter look stirred the chill calm of her little , russet-toned features , or the sombre depths of her dark , long eyes", "context_len": 40}}}, "cavern": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 13, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "crumpled": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 9, "context": "Peggy and Bobby as 'the children , ' with crumpled pinafores and smudged faces , were an equal success , for in watching Archie Peggy had forgotten her sudden shyness , and she now threw herself thoroughly into her part , howling most realistically when her nose was scrubbed in the wash-tub , or her hair combed with a fork ; while Bobby stole jam and resisted medicine with lifelike zeal", "context_len": 71}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 1, "context": "Shagbark crumpled up some dry fragments from a plug of tobacco , in the palm of his hand , punched them into the bowl of his pipe , switched a match along the side of his buckskins , applied the tiny flame , and rode to the head of the company without another word", "context_len": 54}}}, "barometer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "I then looked at the barometer , and whilst I was doing so my head fell on my left shoulder", "context_len": 20}}}, "straits": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 65, "context": "She reproached him for having appealed to Hannah , a servant in his father 's house , for aid ; and in her turn had to hear his reproaches for believing that he would condescend to such a thing , and received an emphatic and solemn denial that he had been guilty of this , or that he had ever let Hannah know of the straits he was in", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "I am even now going the rounds of the yard , so if you will bear me company you can see for yourself to what straits we are put", "context_len": 29}}}, "promptness": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "Alexander 's troops , being a comparatively small and compact body , and being accustomed to move with great promptness and celerity , could easily evade any attempt of such an unwieldy mass of forces to oppose his crossing at any particular point upon the stream", "context_len": 46}}}, "befall": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 106, "context": "`` When [ 409 ] the Sultan heard his daughter 's story , he was sore concerned and his eyes brimmed with tears ; then , sheathing the sword and coming up to her , he kissed her and said to her , `` O my daughter , why didst thou not tell me yesterday , so I might have warded off from thee the torment and affright which have befallen thee this night ? But no matter ; arise and put away from thee this thought , and to-night I will set over thee those who shall guard thee , so there shall not again befall thee that which befell yesternight", "context_len": 112}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Should aught befall you , tell your captors that if they bring you to me I will pay any ransom that they could , in fairness , require", "context_len": 28}}}, "senior": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 41, "context": "I have this afternoon had a meeting of your knights , and have urged upon them very strongly that , having volunteered to serve under you , they must obey your orders as promptly and willingly as if you were the senior knight of the Order , and that it behooves them specially upon the present occasion , when the crew is composed entirely of young knights , to show themselves worthy of the honour that has been done to them by entrusting a galley of the Order to their charge", "context_len": 91}}}, "poring": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "She \u2019 s poring all day long over a lot of different sorts of learnings ; I don \u2019 t remember their names , but they all ends in oddity", "context_len": 30}}}, "disasters": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Their repeated disasters weakened every man 's heart , and gave rise to a well-founded belief that there was treachery in their midst , and that plans decided even in their secret councils were made known to the Danes", "context_len": 39}}}, "irritable": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 15, "context": "So he told them how , when he sat alone , ill and dull and irritable , Ram Dass had tried to distract him by describing the passers by , and there was one child who passed oftener than any one else ; he had begun to be interested in her -- partly perhaps because he was thinking a great deal of a little girl , and partly because Ram Dass had been able to relate the incident of his visit to the attic in chase of the monkey", "context_len": 89}}}, "lunatic": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Resentful wrath nearly choked him , and for a few moments he raged against Eliot like a lunatic", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was as though he felt himself in some way impelled to ask a question which proclaimed him a pitiful lunatic on the face of it", "context_len": 26}}}, "afire": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I ca n't go in search of the confounded lunatic even if he is afire", "context_len": 16}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Set him afire ! '' echoed the crowd , in a whisper", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` So he smote the sand once more and examining its figures , saw that Alaeddin had gotten him exceeding wealth and had married the Sultan 's daughter ; whereat he was all afire for rage and envy and arising then and there , equipped himself for travel and set out for the land of China", "context_len": 56}}}, "caved": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "What right has he to ask help of me when my bows are caved in like an old hat , with no chance at all of getting under way before night , and my ship half full of water ? I 'm trying to find help myself", "context_len": 47}}}, "winging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The vultures had gathered as though in obedience to a signal and were winging their way above the trees", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` I hope there is no mistake as to the accuracy of your information ; because , if there is , you know , we shall have got ourselves into a rather awkward mess by firing upon and winging that craft ! ''", "context_len": 43}}}, "spreading": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "At Pisa the authorities at first treated my story as a fiction , and , I believe , took me for an impostor ; but on the news spreading , some knights came forward and recognised me", "context_len": 37}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 32, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}}}, "improbable": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "lookout": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 60, "context": "Among the missiles hurled into the town were balls filled with Greek fire , but the houses being entirely built of stone , no conflagrations of importance were caused by them , as a band of knights was organised specially to watch for these bombs , and whenever one of them was seen to fall , they hurried from their lookout to the spot , with a gang of slaves carrying baskets of earth and buckets of water , and quenched the flames before they had made any great headway", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Dat ain \u2019 t nuffin , he said airily ; de sarpint furgot dat I was on de lookout fur him and as soon as he fired and missed , why , I plugged him ; \u2019 tain \u2019 t wuth speaking \u2019 bout", "context_len": 44}}}, "heartstrings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "While the liner was by no means out of danger , the situation of the Restless people fairly tore at the captain 's heartstrings", "context_len": 24}}}, "pored": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "The author dedicates this tale to his brother , engaged , like himself , in that most responsible task , the education of youth , in memory of those happy days when they pored together in rapturous delight over old legend or romantic lore in their father 's home at that very Clifton ( now Clifton Hampden ) familiar to hearers or readers of the tale as the home of Herstan , and the scene of the heroic defence of the English dwelling against the Danes", "context_len": 86}}}, "charts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "It appears from old charts , that Creek towns , or at least towns having Creek names , also existed west of Coosa river , as on Canoe creek : Litafatcha , and on Cahawba river : T\u00e1lua h\u00e1dsho , `` Crazy Town , '' together with ruins of other villages above this", "context_len": 53}}}, "delivered": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 11, "context": "'' 'Speech , which , in the year 1735 , was delivered at Savannah , in Georgia , by Chekilli , Emperor of the Upper and Lower Creeks ; Antiche , highest Chief of the town of the Cowetas , Eliche , King ; Ousta , Head Chief of the Cussitaws , Tomechaw , War King ; Wali , War Captain of the Palachucolas , Poepiche , King ; Tomehuichi , Dog King of the Euchitaws ; Mittakawye , Head War Chief of the Okonees , Tuwechiche , King ; Whoyauni , Head War Chief of the Chehaws and of the Hokmulge Nation ; Stimelacoweche , King of the Osoches ; Opithli , King of the Jawocolos ; Ewenauki , King ; Tahmokmi , War Captain of the", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` I have done it now , '' said the Duchess , `` God knows what will be the consequence , but I have at least delivered my soul", "context_len": 29}}}, "directness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The directness of his aim , the full certainty of his thought were brought to a stand-still ; all movement was arrested by the terrible certainty that Faith was not there", "context_len": 31}}}, "dimpled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "With the touch of a passing breeze , the maple trees of the yard waved their hands gayly to him , and in the distance beyond the football field Lake Woodrim dimpled and laughed in the golden sunshine", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Baby ceased her loud cries , and stretched out her dimpled arms to her uncle , with a beseeching whimper ; Hafed paused in his antics , and stood like a statue at sight of his master ; and the three other children all turned to him with exclamations of `` Oh , Uncle Ruthven ; we 're so glad ! '' and `` Please do n't leave us , '' from Maggie and Bessie ; and `` Make dat Patty be off wiz herself , '' from Franky", "context_len": 89}}}, "hoisted": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 8, "context": "Not to be outdone in politeness , we hoisted our colours also ; and for the next quarter of an hour the two craft continued to close , the chase stolidly maintaining her course , while we , under the skipper 's skilful conning , continued to edge very gradually away , as the other vessel sped to leeward , checking our weather braces by a few inches at a time until our yards were all but square", "context_len": 78}}}, "lounged": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "She was growing used now to the new order of things and smiled a welcome to Belle from the velvet lounging chair in which she , Pauline Harding , who had never lounged in her life , was beginning to feel perfectly at home", "context_len": 44}}}, "thwarts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 90, "context": "It was in imagination , but his mental calculation was not far wrong , for within a few yards of where it might be expected , and not ten from where he was now sculling , he saw something roll up as it were to the surface , there was a gleam of white in the sunlit water , and then it was disappearing again , when , acting upon the impulse of the moment , Harry loosened his hold of the oar , took two steps forward over the thwarts , and leaped into the sea", "context_len": 97}}}, "cruise": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 57, "context": "I wish to gain experience and to do service to the Order , and so far from pleasing me , promotion would trouble and distress me , and , could it have been done , I would most gladly have sent home the prizes , instead of going to Genoa , and would myself have continued the cruise", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "You may meet with vessels on your way south , and doubtless some of them cruise off the barren coast I speak of , to intercept traders to and from Egypt and Acre , and other Syrian ports ; for the trade carried on is considerable , and , although of the same religion , the Turks are disposed to view the Egyptians as enemies rather than as friends , and would have even less hesitation in plundering them than in robbing their own countrymen", "context_len": 85}}}, "arduous": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 69, "context": "Year after year she persisted in her steady round of Zenana visits ; not , like the average district-visitor of England , going once a fortnight or once a week into her district , -- which was the whole city of Batala , -- but day after day giving hours to the work , never daunted because results seemed small , never apparently even tempted to throw up her arduous task in despair", "context_len": 73}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "As the next few weeks would be spent in scraping , breaming , and pitching the hull , the officers were allowed to take up quarters ashore , and right glad was I to have the chance of having a spell on dry land after so long and arduous a time afloat", "context_len": 52}}}, "quitting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "One flash ends all ! O Clara , when my soul Hath ceased to suffer , can it cease to love ? Methinks , when quitting Earth , \u2019 twill still retain Her image , who was more than Earth to me ! It is a portion of my being , twined With every thought and feeling ; thou wilt weep , My Clara ; thou canst not believe him false To faith and friends , who is so true to thee", "context_len": 82}}}, "caw": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was arranged that Fred should caw like a crow in succession ; at the first summons they were to get ready ; at the second , Charlie and John were to fire ; but Fred was to fire as they rose", "context_len": 42}}}, "wot": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "I know as He will answer us , fur I told Him all about Charlie , and it wor arter-wards as I larned wot a deal He ha ' done fur him", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 16, "context": "You ca n't get it at no chemist 's , Hannah Searles , and the neighbour wot give it me ha ' gone to Ameriky ; but see yere , fur I real feels for disturbed and worrited mothers , I 'll give yer a tiny drop in this yere bottle , and you can take it to her ; ten drops ull settle that baby off as sound as a nut", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` I do n't know wot h'ever you mean , '' replied Faith ; `` how could we put our little Roy into a place when we do n't know wherever he is ? We do n't want to put our little Roy anywhere , only jest to bring him home", "context_len": 51}}}, "telegraphy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "Wireless telegraphy was too recent an aid to sea-faring to seem real to these simple sailors ; this was the first time its workings had touched their lives , and they were not ready to take the burning yacht on faith unseen", "context_len": 42}}}, "southing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He had run down his fifteen miles of southing , but the blue horizon line was without a speck to mar it", "context_len": 22}}}, "mar": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "I told them I should regard your report of their individual conduct with the same attention and respect with which I should that of any other commander , and that they might greatly make or mar their future prospects in the Order by their conduct during the cruise", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "I 'm goin ' ter stay up fer hours an ' hours , an ' if yer do n't keep right on tellin ' stories quick , I 'll holler , an ' that 'll make mar mad , an ' then she 'll send par up with a stick ter beat me", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "You \u2019 ve played a brilliant game , Hayden , and you can \u2019 t afford to let your temper and your hatred of Stone mar your record", "context_len": 28}}}, "routes": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 50, "context": "The emigrant trains were disposed to keep to certain paths , where the face of the country compelled a closing in , but in other sections the respective courses were separated by miles , and , as has been shown the parties plodding across the plains , even though their routes were parallel , were often so far apart that for days they saw nothing of one another", "context_len": 68}}}, "perplexed": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 79, "context": "The Sultan wondered with an exceeding wonderment and was confounded at the beauty of the girls and their loveliness , which overpassed description ; his wit was bewildered , when he saw the golden dishes , full of jewels that dazzled the sight , and he was amazed at this marvel , so that he became as one dumb , unable to speak aught , of the excess of his wonderment ; nay , his wit was the more perplexed , forasmuch as this had all been accomplished in an hour 's time", "context_len": 93}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 45, "context": "When [ 413 ] the Vizier heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He scratched his head and looked perplexed", "context_len": 7}}}, "banner": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 11, "context": "The camp , with all its rich booty and the great banner of the pasha , fell into the hands of the victors , who , satisfied with their success , and exhausted by their efforts , made no attempt to follow the flying foe , or to hinder their embarkation ; for even now the Turks , enormously outnumbering them as they did , might be driven by despair to a resistance so desperate as once again to turn the tide of victory", "context_len": 84}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Here , you let that banner alone ! '' cried Tom , and he gave such a jerk to the chair on which Sid was standing that the objector to things artistic toppled to the floor with a resounding crash", "context_len": 41}}}, "prophet": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 33, "context": "This story does not mention any crossing by the Cha'hta of the turbid waters of the mighty Mississippi , but accounts quite satisfactorily for the mysterious inclination of the pole , for the prophet must have been careful to suspend the satchel with the war-physic always on the eastern side , so as to have the pole brought down in that direction by the weight of the pouch", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Weatherford , the leader of the insurgent Creeks , and their prophet Hilis'-h\u00e1ko resided there ; the forces gathered at this place by them were defeated December 23d , 1813", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Their great leader and prophet [ 139 ] directed all their movements , carried the hobuna or sacred bag ( containing 'medicines ' ) and a long white pole as the badge of his authority", "context_len": 35}}}, "skyward": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "He was a good prophet , for while he stared , the smoke suddenly spread skyward like a huge fan , hung for a moment , and then vanished , except for tattered fringes of vapor that drifted slowly to leeward", "context_len": 41}}}, "fan": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 41, "context": "Random walked out into Liberty Street , rounded a corner , entered a door , passed through a barroom , and finally piloted the cowboy into a small apartment , furnished with two chairs , a table , and an electric fan", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "I did sit down ; I sat where some of the summer breeze coming in from across the Square garden could fan my hot cheeks", "context_len": 25}}}, "tattered": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "As she swung the gate open , and passed from the field into the lane , she saw a sight which for a moment made her hang back doubtfully , for a tramp lay stretched out full length asleep in the sunshine , his tattered clothes and broken boots a strange contrast to the bed of white daisies and celandine upon which he lay", "context_len": 64}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "And he knew by the clothes , which , though tattered into shreds , were similar to those that had been worn by Gillis and Dorn , by the men of the Parkhurst family", "context_len": 34}}}, "fringes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "Jack Frost threw photographs of fairyland upon the windows , and hung the roofs with fringes of crystal pendants , while the snowflakes piled themselves over the fences and made a shroud for the trees , and every day Pauline , with this strange peace in her heart , did her housework to the glory of God", "context_len": 57}}}, "grimy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "Nearly fifty people were lounging about , regarding the new arrival with curious interest , while on the adjoining barge stood about a dozen men in grimy overalls , with planks , barrows , and spades , in readiness to commence work", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "'Oh , you are in a mess ! ' she cried , trying to wipe the mud off his coat with her pocket-handkerchief , and getting almost as grimy as he was in the process", "context_len": 35}}}, "blistered": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "I was faint , weary , and hungry ; my clothes were in rags , my hands blistered , and my face blackened with powder", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The engineer raised a pair of hands which were raw with burns , and felt of his blistered face", "context_len": 19}}}, "hamper": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 71, "context": "It was a strange reaction as he hung there -- he , the brave and daring swimmer , famed for his dives off Carn Du , held up by the man he had always denounced as a terrible coward ; whom he had hated from boyhood almost , without cause , and whom really , under the impulse of a horrible temptation , he had on the previous night tried to hamper in his swimming , though not really to drown", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 15, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "transfer": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "Although within range of hostile guns , the `` dump '' had so far escaped serious damage , To bring the supplies nearer the lines by mechanical transfer would be to court disaster , so every ounce of food had to be carried by squads detailed for that purpose", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 18, "context": "Alexander considered it somewhat uncertain whether the people of all these states and cities would be disposed to transfer readily , to so youthful a prince as he , the high commission which his father , a very powerful monarch and soldier , had extorted from them with so much difficulty", "context_len": 51}}}, "porpoise": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "The mate was busy somewhere about the deck , and the captain was leaning over the quarter rail , watching his opportunity to strike a porpoise which had come under the ship \u2019 s counter", "context_len": 35}}}, "sputtered": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "First over the gunwale was a very stout derelict in dripping blue trousers , who puffed like a porpoise as he sputtered :", "context_len": 23}}}, "juggler": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "He had been a juggler `` when he was young ; '' he was used to tossing heavy weights ; in fact , he just happened to know that he could throw three hundred pounds exactly twelve feet , the height of the ceiling", "context_len": 44}}}, "spitting": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 41, "context": "On Willie 's knee was a Maltese kitten purring away contentedly ; but the moment she caught sight of Flossy , she sprang from her resting-place , and , scampering into a corner , put up her back , and began spitting and hissing in a very impolite manner", "context_len": 49}}}, "cinders": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "Scarcely was this accomplished , when the roof was discovered to be on fire ; the violence of the wind had blown off a blanket , and the cinders catching had kindled in the dry bark", "context_len": 36}}}, "undismayed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "While this undismayed survivor babbled on as if his tongue were hung in the middle , David was trying to drag from the raft a ragged man who lay limp and face downward", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "As before , the boats swept up to the foot of the breach , the Turks leaped out , and , undismayed by the storm of shot , climbed up to the assault", "context_len": 33}}}, "survivor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "While this undismayed survivor babbled on as if his tongue were hung in the middle , David was trying to drag from the raft a ragged man who lay limp and face downward", "context_len": 33}}}, "babbled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was pleasant work wandering among the green meadows picking the sweet-smelling flowers , while the larks sang their loudest overhead , and the little brook babbled by on its path to the river -- more especially pleasant when they remembered that by this time next week school would have begun again , with its attendant woes of Latin grammar and French composition", "context_len": 63}}}, "raft": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 23, "context": "Here and there a dead sheep or pig drifted by , or a portion of a haystack ; a hurdle floating like a raft bore on it a few disconsolate fowls , clucking dismally ; while an occasional wash-tub or upturned table showed that the river must have already flooded some of the low-lying cottages higher up on its course", "context_len": 60}}}, "inboard": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "After strenuous efforts the guns were secured , but at a cost of four men washed overboard and five injured , either by the heavy seas that came tumbling inboard , or else by the wild career of the derelict weapons", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "With a hoarse `` Yo ho ! '' the spar was thrust forward , and just as its weight was on the point of overbalancing the weight of the seamen on the inboard part , the extremity touched the edge of the rocks", "context_len": 43}}}, "frizzled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The other crowd stood it as long as they could , and then when it was take to the water or be frizzled where they stood , they pitched the boats over and got away", "context_len": 35}}}, "confiding": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "Like most children , with the instinctive dread of being laughed at , she never thought of confiding her difficulty to an older person , but solving the problem according to her own quaint ideas , she dodged the vigilance of Ellen , and trotted off alone to the churchyard", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Of course I do , more touched than he would have cared to admit by her confiding friendliness ; but I want you to wait , he added , while I try to answer your question about our family motto", "context_len": 40}}}, "fender": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "You have done things uncommonly well , and now you 'll just have the goodness to sit with your feet on the fender in the drawing-room , and give yourself a right good toasting while Miss Wickham and I are examining the house", "context_len": 43}}}, "dulled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "These words seemed to awaken the dulled understanding of the father", "context_len": 11}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "And as he grew more weary , more desperate of escape from his pursuers , he soon ceased to think at all ; political dreams , sorrow for the dead , hopes of finding new friends and ambitions in a new world , all were forgotten , the spirit within him was dulled by suffering ; only the poor body cried incessantly for rest , for food , for warmth , and most often craved in vain", "context_len": 77}}}, "stupor": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 11, "context": "At the end of that time he roused himself from his stupor of grief and disappointment , and , abandoning the idea of any further attack upon the point that had cost him so dearly , he ordered the troops to move round and renew the attack upon the wall in front of the Jews ' quarter , and commence the construction of a battery on the edge of the great ditch facing the retrenchment behind the breach before effected", "context_len": 80}}}, "clammy": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Ye have one more chance , candidates , '' went on the president , as he touched the foreheads of the four with something cold and clammy -- a hand , from the feel of it , but it was only a rubber glove , filled with cracked ice", "context_len": 50}}}, "lamenting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He tried to struggle to his feet , but muscular hands gripped his heaving shoulders and he fell back lamenting :", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "She continued to study hard and perseveringly , in preparation for fuller work , often lamenting her own slowness in learning to speak ; and already she was making herself known and beloved by a few Indians , -- either Christian , or disposed towards Christianity", "context_len": 46}}}, "mourning": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 44, "context": "They always keep your beautiful baby-clothes , and the gold locket round your neck with the portrait inside , and then , when you 're just grown up , you turn out to be the only daughter of a duke , who has been mourning for you ever since you were lost", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 2, "context": "Enough of mourning ; for that , an I sat grieving a thousand years , what were the profit ? Alaeddin can not return from the tomb and I have considered and believe [ 601 ] that which thou saidst to me yesterday , to wit , that most like my father the Sultan hath slain him , in the excess of his grief for my loss", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "So they tuned their instruments and touched their strings and struck up with plaintive [ 518 ] airs that dilated the mourning heart", "context_len": 23}}}, "swine": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 24, "context": "Living on grass , and in the woods , with the milk from the house , he had not that protuberance of belly which swine reared in sties possess , and really merited Charlie \u2019 s encomium of being handsome ; he jumped up on his master and rubbed against his legs , with low grunts , expressive of satisfaction", "context_len": 60}}}, "glimpsed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "A mere second lieutenant had no place in such company ! Then he glimpsed Captain O \u2019 Grady in a wheelchair , chatting with the highest-ranking officer", "context_len": 27}}}, "collapse": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "It was certainly Peggy who suggested sitting on the top of the haystack , and making it sway backwards and forwards like a swing , a delightful sensation while it lasted , but which ended suddenly in the collapse of the whole top of the stack , much to Father 's wrath , for it took David a couple of hours to repair the damage , and certainly did not sweeten the old man 's already crusty temper", "context_len": 78}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "I 'll try to be comfortable until your return , dearest , '' and with a mocking smile Tom Parsons sank down into an easy chair that threatened to collapse under his substantial bulk", "context_len": 34}}}, "groping": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 8, "context": "And suddenly all the lad \u2019 s long groping for the truth , his passionate eagerness to learn the facts concerning his parents , the many disappointments he had suffered and the realization of his desperate loneliness rushed over him in an overwhelming flood , and filled him with emotion that found vent in a headlong torrent of words", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "And 'bout dis ? groping in the side-pocket of his sacque , and producing a little circle of blue ribbon", "context_len": 20}}}, "decency": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "During those miserable months of hiding he had but one sad satisfaction -- that of knowing that Prince Rupert had kept his promise and the mangled remains of Thomas Harrison were restored to his widow , and laid in decency in Newcastle churchyard", "context_len": 43}}}, "cub": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "By his side , similarly dressed , stood his son , the hopeful Canute , the future King of England , then only in his twelfth year , but already showing himself a true cub of the old tiger in fierceness and valour , yet not devoid of nobler and gentler virtues , as he afterwards showed", "context_len": 57}}}, "aback": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 11, "context": "It would be difficult to say who were the most taken aback : the Boches at the sight of a khaki-clad man who might or might not be the foremost of a party of trench raiders , or Malcolm on finding himself confronted by a score of fully-armed Germans", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "Gervaise , although taken aback by this sudden change in his fortunes , was not so cast down as he might otherwise have been ; he would now be free to carry out any plan for escape that he might devise , and by his being addressed in Italian it was evident to him that his knowledge of Turkish was unsuspected", "context_len": 61}}}, "veered": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 6, "context": "Crossing the Missouri , the messenger veered slightly to the southwest , holding to the course until he struck the old military road , forty-odd miles distant , where he shifted to the northwest and crossed the Kickapoo Reservation", "context_len": 39}}}, "bombarded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "It is not to be supposed that I was allowed to spin my yarn without interruption ; on the contrary , I was bombarded with a continuous fire of questions for the elucidation of points that I had failed to make quite clear ; and when I had finished the captain was pleased to express himself as perfectly satisfied with all that I had done , and that the loss of the Dolphin was due to causes entirely beyond my control", "context_len": 81}}}, "bribes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Briggs , and he bombarded him with threats , bribes , and pleadings , until the rockets from the Roanoke soared into the clear night and the yacht 's people shouted at the welcome sight", "context_len": 35}}}, "pleadings": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 70, "context": "What he saw caused a fierce look to come to his face and his freckled cheeks to flush ; for in the midst of the group was Hunk Rollins , a look of vicious pleasure on his face , holding little Jimmy Jones by the ear , which he was twisting with brutal pleasure , showing his ugly teeth as he laughed at the tortured lad \u2019 s cries and pleadings", "context_len": 71}}}, "repairs": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 31, "context": "'As I advised you in my last , we were on our way to Farningham , when something went wrong with the engine , and we had to stop here for repairs , and mamma was so charmed with this little village that she decided to stay awhile ; she says it seems to suit her better than any place she has seen ; poor mamma , I wish I could find some place where she would be satisfied", "context_len": 79}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Rosy will be laid up for a couple of weeks \u2019 repairs anyway , Chick added in a loud whisper , and so will Tony and Cracker", "context_len": 27}}}, "bedraggled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "David saw the bedraggled magnate swaying against the door of the captain 's room as he begged :", "context_len": 18}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "The angry , surprised swan dived his head down in search of him ; but the current , which swept round here with some force , carried Jumbo away , and finally flung him , a bedraggled and most unhappy-looking rabbit , on to a corner of the island", "context_len": 49}}}, "reimburse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` But I 'll reimburse the company", "context_len": 7}}}, "vibrating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "When David went aft in the late evening with the fourth officer to set the log over the stern , the liner was vibrating to the steady thrust of her engines , and her broad wake foamed white in the starlit darkness", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The last vibrating tintinnabulations of the clock died away as Bart discovered his enemy", "context_len": 14}}}, "foamed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "When David went aft in the late evening with the fourth officer to set the log over the stern , the liner was vibrating to the steady thrust of her engines , and her broad wake foamed white in the starlit darkness", "context_len": 42}}}, "ay": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 110, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "What should you say to a roof made of a single leaf , large enough to shelter twelve men from a scorching sun ? Or to one of snow ; ay , to roof , walls , floor , all of snow , -- making a warm , comfortable home too ? ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "bygones": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "chockfull": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` But I am just chockfull of hope that we will hear from Arthur", "context_len": 14}}}, "panicky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The kinfolk and friends of those on board had been kept in a state of panicky alarm , day after day , by the flaring newspaper head-lines which sent the Roanoke to the bottom and raised her again , in hourly `` extras", "context_len": 43}}}, "flaring": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "The kinfolk and friends of those on board had been kept in a state of panicky alarm , day after day , by the flaring newspaper head-lines which sent the Roanoke to the bottom and raised her again , in hourly `` extras", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Her father was seated by the table , the gas flaring high over his head ; he was trying laboriously to put some of his torn essay together ; he heard the movement at the door , but without looking up called out harshly -- '' Go away ; I ca n't be disturbed", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}}}, "hourly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "The kinfolk and friends of those on board had been kept in a state of panicky alarm , day after day , by the flaring newspaper head-lines which sent the Roanoke to the bottom and raised her again , in hourly `` extras", "context_len": 43}}}, "extras": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "The kinfolk and friends of those on board had been kept in a state of panicky alarm , day after day , by the flaring newspaper head-lines which sent the Roanoke to the bottom and raised her again , in hourly `` extras", "context_len": 43}}}, "cripple": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Well , thou hast passed unscathed through five great battles , or at least without any serious wound ; but remember all are not so fortunate , and many a poor cripple sighs over Penn , Sherston , Brentford , Otford , or Assingdun", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "For Mark knew how Dewey had come there ; he had swum out , cripple though he was , to hunt for him ; and with his one well arm , poor gallant Dewey was finding trouble in getting back", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "The painful freeing of his right hand , the fear that in the fall he might have sustained an injury that would cripple him and leave him a prey to the first beast of the jungle that might roam that way , the dread that he might never be able to free himself from those ruthless , imprisoning branches had blinded him to another and more imminent peril that threatened", "context_len": 70}}}, "nudge": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "The band on the promenade deck was lustily playing `` home again , home again , from a foreign shore , '' as the tugs poked their noses against the black side of the ocean cripple and began to nudge her into her berth", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "But the more wary Peggy , seeing the folly of such revelations , gave him a nudge , and the emphatic hint : 'Shut up ! '", "context_len": 27}}}, "prodded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "David Downes was looking for friends on the wharf , but he scanned the masses of upturned faces in vain , until the bos'n prodded him in the ribs , and said :", "context_len": 33}}}, "welcomes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "There were his `` dearest folks , '' as he called them , and he was as rich in welcomes as any of the passengers who were making so much joyful noise along the decks below", "context_len": 36}}}, "homecoming": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "She would not go to Dorothy 's for fear she should miss her mother and sister , and likewise for the reason that she felt it would be a very flat report she would have to make to Dorothy of her homecoming", "context_len": 42}}}, "finance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Gentlemen high in the world of finance , and managers of his great interests had flocked aboard to greet him and to offer their aid and sympathy", "context_len": 27}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "His father admired Franklin and was willing to finance them", "context_len": 10}}}, "genuinely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "' I 'll fetch Carlo , ' said Maud , who was genuinely fond of her pets , and liked to show them to an appreciative audience", "context_len": 27}}}, "wonted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "Yet , full as London was of hopes and fears , of mad gaiety and black despair , the October day was as sweet and still as any day of any autumn ; the late roses blossomed as of old in the gardens of the Strand , and vine-leaves wreathed the citizens ' with their wonted coronals of ruby and gold", "context_len": 61}}}, "abruptness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "How do you do , dear ? said the lady , extending her hand , which Marie-Celeste , her cheeks aflame with the unexpected abruptness of Albert 's introduction , took in hers , in a pretty deferential sort of way , as though fully conscious of the dignity of her surroundings", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Belden , and the carriage came to a standstill with paralyzing abruptness Now , turn your wheel and let me out", "context_len": 21}}}, "beacon": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 36, "context": "Below the house the meadow sloped down to a river , where a stone bridge led to the village , with its pretty thatched cottages and Norman church , whose square tower stood up like a beacon for the surrounding country ; and away in the distance , tier upon tier , rose the Welsh mountains , fading from green to purple or from purple to misty mauve , till the last were lost in the hazy blue of the sky", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "`` I thought that it was midnight of Christmas Eve , and that I was attending mass , when , just as the words were sung by the choir , 'Pax in terra , ' the scene suddenly changed , and I stood in the dark on the chalk hills which overlook the Solent ; by my side was a beacon ready laid for firing", "context_len": 65}}}, "topped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "A line of khaki topped the parapet , leapt into the open , and broke into a steady double", "context_len": 19}}}, "skilfully": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 79, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}}}, "piloted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Random walked out into Liberty Street , rounded a corner , entered a door , passed through a barroom , and finally piloted the cowboy into a small apartment , furnished with two chairs , a table , and an electric fan", "context_len": 42}}}, "thump": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Becket was first to reach him , and he proceeded to thump David 's chest and pound his back with the exhortation :", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` She has -- she 's called Dovey 'cause most people think doves are so dear , but we know they 're the worst fighters in the lot , do n't we , Dovey ? '' and Cassowary shook her sturdy young sister , who gave her a good thump on the back", "context_len": 53}}}, "exhortation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "Shall he go forth and sit lazily while his brethren fight for Canaan ? It may be that godly exhortation and the example of valiant men may turn him from the error of his ways ere it be too late", "context_len": 40}}}, "stave": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "There is ruin before us ; I can scarcely stave it off for another week , and I have not had a line from Mr", "context_len": 25}}}, "anywheres": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` All sound and fit for duty ? The collision did n't stave you in anywheres ? ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "shoving": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "It was pretty to see her as she stood by the colonel , looking up at him with her eyes so full of love and pleasure , and a bright color in her cheeks ; while Maggie , almost as much delighted , ran to the heavy arm-chair in which Grandpapa Duncan usually sat , and began tugging and shoving at it with all her might", "context_len": 66}}}, "mariners": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "Now , brother mariners , said John , who was in high feather at this auspicious beginning of their enterprise , we \u2019 ve got a fishing-ground of our own , marks of our own , all written down in a birch-bark book , and can come when we like", "context_len": 50}}}, "explosive": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "No need for the destroyers to tear at full speed across the ever-widening circle of oil ; no need for explosive grapnels to trail over the downward path of the vertically-descending pirate craft", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "His own impression was that it had something to do with the discovery of the explosive in the bunker", "context_len": 19}}}, "chuckles": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Becket went off into a series of explosive chuckles , from which he was diverted by the appearance of the bos'n who declared in the most threatening voice :", "context_len": 29}}}, "loafer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` The red-headed loafer again ? I vill protect my whiskers mit my life", "context_len": 14}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` What are you doing here , you Chinese loafer ? ''", "context_len": 12}}}, "discharged": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 54, "context": "With the utmost audacity a U-boat had lain submerged across the track of the convoy , trusting to be able to launch her bolt and disappear before even the swift destroyers could take her bearings , and close upon the spot where the tips of her periscopes had appeared when the torpedo had been discharged", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Two more rushed at him ; one he shot , the second received the discharged pistol full in the face", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Slowly and confidently he pulled the trigger , and worked the lever that ejected the discharged shell , and pumped a new bullet into place", "context_len": 25}}}, "temporary": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 80, "context": "I noted that while those who had not succumbed to their sufferings were fed as they stood still bound to their posts , those who had become unconscious were temporarily released , and revived by being copiously soused with water , and , further , were allowed to eat and drink while seated on the ground , before they were lashed up afresh ; and I took the hint , feigning insensibility for the sake of the few minutes of temporary relief that I hoped thus to win", "context_len": 88}}}, "blithely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` I was vexed , but my ill-humor seemed to add to her high spirits , and she talked away quite blithely", "context_len": 22}}}, "birdcage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Margaret called blithely from the birdcage of a kitchen", "context_len": 9}}}, "fruiter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` There 's a master wanted in a Jamaica fruiter , '' observed Mr", "context_len": 14}}}, "festal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "To enter from the chill darkness outside , and find one 's self confronted by a totally unanticipated festal board , draped with red , adorned with white napery , and wreathed with flowers , was to feel that the preparations were brilliant indeed", "context_len": 44}}}, "quadrant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Becket demanded Captain John 's old-fashioned quadrant with which to measure off the exact number of degrees of pie each was entitled to , and nearly upset the table before this mathematical problem was adjusted", "context_len": 35}}}, "landings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "And may all your future landings be as happy as this one , Captain Barry Blake of the Flying Fortress !", "context_len": 21}}}, "flurried": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "He had been late for several days at the inn-yard , and had arrived looking so flurried and peculiar that , although he had laughed it off and made some excuse , she was certain that things were not as they should be", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 98, "context": "Striding in among the group of boys who were still discussing the very question of the disappearance of the money which had been the main topic of interest ever since the loss was discovered , the bank-note in his hand , he advanced directly to Flagg , who was taking an active part in the conversation -- that is , he had been doing so within the last few moments , since he had returned after a short absence from the school-room , looking , as more than one of the boys observed , `` flushed and rather flurried", "context_len": 99}}}, "girlie": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Well , there 's one thing , little girlie , said her father ; there are wonderful places in England , which I mean you shall see ; and how long we stay in Windsor depends --", "context_len": 37}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Thank you , girlie , '' said her mother , `` I believe I will resign in your favor", "context_len": 20}}}, "presenting": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 4, "context": "The young instructor , presenting , by the way , a ludicrous contrast in his shining uniform of gray and white and gold , with his three or four nervous and variously costumed pupils , takes the bayonet of his gun for a drill stick and marches `` his '' squad over into a secluded corner of the area and thus begins the above-mentioned instructions :", "context_len": 66}}}, "rumbled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Again under cover , their mercurial spirits rose , and when at length the rain ceased , and the train rumbled betwixt the red-earthed , verdant coombes of Devon , bathed in brilliant sunshine , the Anzacs unanimously voted that there were worse places on earth than the Old Country", "context_len": 50}}}, "remorseful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was pitiful to hear this pleading , remorseful speech from such a man as Stanley P", "context_len": 17}}}, "twitching": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 15, "context": "Peggy liked to see Rollo working with the sheep , the tips of his ears twitching and his faithful brown eyes bright with intelligence , as he cleverly sorted the lambs which Father had pointed out from the rest of the flock , and drove them neatly into the enclosure , coming up whimpering with pride for the praise which he knew awaited his efforts", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "'We 'll build a little house , ' said Peggy , anxious to prevent the suspicious twitching of her charge 's mouth from developing into a roar , and taking up some bricks and loose stones which lay under the wall", "context_len": 41}}}, "dire": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 63, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 35, "context": "Meanwhile , since I was almost naked , and was hoping soon to find myself once more among civilised people , it occurred to me that the skin of the leopard which had wrought this dire tragedy might be of use to me as material out of which to fashion some sort of a garment ; and , therefore , while the flames of the pyre were still blazing brilliantly I utilised their light to enable me to strip the pelt off the great carcase", "context_len": 85}}}, "mansion": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 26, "context": "Then ( says our author ) , the geomant proceeds to examine the sixteen figures thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it", "context_len": 128}}}, "livable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Captain John 's flat was far more cheery and livable than these gloomy apartments , thought David , as he followed his host up the echoing marble staircase to the second story", "context_len": 32}}}, "apartments": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 8, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}}}, "blush": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 67, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 29, "context": "Never before had the boy dined in such a room and at such a table , and the fear that he might do some awkward thing to make him blush with shame was painful upon him", "context_len": 36}}}, "weathers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "Sara was sent on errands in all weathers , and scolded and driven hither and thither ; she was scarcely allowed to speak to Ermengarde and Lottie ; Lavinia sneered at the increasing shabbiness of her clothes ; and the other girls stared curiously at her when she appeared in the schoolroom", "context_len": 52}}}, "fiddle": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 42, "context": "After the death of his teacher Orphy falls upon hard times , but eventually his talent is recognized by a professor of music who takes him to Europe , and there , under peculiar circumstances , he plays on his home-made gourd fiddle before no less a personage than Her Majesty , Queen Victoria", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "He had felt that this time must come , but now the dread of it passed from him instantly , and he was almost frightened by that feeling of eager fierceness and uncontrollable rage which had possessed him in the hour when he was led to wreak physical violence on Hayden for the destruction of little Jerry \u2019 s fiddle", "context_len": 60}}}, "loyalty": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 55, "context": "I have often wondered why maidens should so carelessly bestow their favours upon every knight who begged for them , and have said to myself that when my time came I would grant it but once , and only then to one whom I deemed worthy of it in all ways -- one in whose loyalty and honour I could trust implicitly , and who would regard it as something sacred , deeming it an honour to wear it , as being the pledge of my trust and esteem", "context_len": 89}}}, "yankee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "YANKEE TOPSAILS", "context_len": 2}}}, "atone": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "She seemed to be letting go her hold on life , and her husband , as if seeking to atone for the years in which he had allowed his worldly interests to absorb his time and thought , was seldom away from her", "context_len": 43}}}, "posted": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 64, "context": "Haven , an ex-editor , had written up a good `` puff '' for a local paper , inserted gratis an exciting comment and anticipation in reference to the impending sale , and Darry and Bob had printed fifteen hundred dodgers on their home press , very neat and presentable in appearance , and these had been judiciously distributed for miles around , and posted up in stores and depots", "context_len": 70}}}, "moneybags": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Cochran , the moneybags vat we picked up , he vas very mad mit our old man at first , but he cool down by and by and see vat a idiot he vas", "context_len": 34}}}, "befriend": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "I wrote some time since to a man who is rich and prosperous , and is under great obligations to me , telling him about you and asking him , as I had a right to ask him , to befriend you", "context_len": 42}}}, "enlist": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Whether intentionally or not , the man had slipped back into his Staffordshire accent and dropped the strongest of his oaths , and Dick could not prevent a feeling of bitter amusement at seeing that this drunken ne'er-do-well , whom his uncle had persuaded to enlist in the hopes of drilling him into a decent life , had yielded to the influence of General Harrison 's character just as he himself had done", "context_len": 73}}}, "yarns": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Grandfather told her all sorts of cheerful yarns about people who were not heard of at sea for weeks and weeks , and then came into port all safe and smiling", "context_len": 31}}}, "leans": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "She seemed to have faith in that simple , quiet way of his , when he leans forward and looks you straight in the eyes as he talks", "context_len": 28}}}, "initials": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 13, "context": "That would have taken too long , but , after quickly scratching his initials on the back of the turtle 's shell , together with the date , so he would know the reptile again , Fenn replaced it on the ground , and started off through the woods in the indicated direction", "context_len": 53}}}, "cuffed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Becket 's message , and was thanked for the one and cuffed over the head for the other", "context_len": 18}}}, "valve": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "In consequence of the rotary motion of the balloon , which had continued without ceasing since the earth was left , the valve line had become twisted , and he had to leave the car , and to mount into the ring above to adjust it", "context_len": 46}}}, "mopped": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "Cochran was not strong enough to come , '' panted her husband as he mopped his face", "context_len": 17}}}, "buoyant": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 10, "context": "Grief does not for long hold its sway over the buoyant spirit of youth , and , in spite of the heavy blow that I had sustained , my boyish disposition speedily reasserted itself , and I looked forward with undisguised eagerness to my journey to my new home in Portsmouth town", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was not desirable to load her till near the time of starting , in order that the cargo might not water-soak , as the great object was to render her as buoyant as possible", "context_len": 35}}}, "lavished": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "And this boy of his was a weakling , used to the constant care and luxury which wealth had lavished on him", "context_len": 22}}}, "swapped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Cochran had become lost in his own thoughts as he stared from a wheel-house window , while David swapped briny yarns with the mate", "context_len": 24}}}, "briny": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Cochran had become lost in his own thoughts as he stared from a wheel-house window , while David swapped briny yarns with the mate", "context_len": 24}}}, "piling": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 52, "context": "As soon as darkness came on , a large body of slaves were marched down from the town , and , under the direction of the knights , laboured all night at the mound , removing great quantities of the fallen stones and rubbish in a line halfway up it , and piling them above so as to form a scarp across the mound that would need ladders to ascend", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The fable has led to a proverb which prevails in every language in Europe , by which all extravagant and unheard-of exertions to accomplish an end is said to be a piling of Pelion upon Ossa", "context_len": 36}}}, "stitch": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 21, "context": "My head was sore , but the surgeon removed the bandage , clipped the hair about the wound , took a stitch or two that hurt worse than the original blow , and in an hour I had forgotten the sabre-cut", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Even if you had a needle and thread to stitch them together they would break to pieces because they are so dry and brittle", "context_len": 24}}}, "glimpsing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "David had been at sea for more than a year without glimpsing such a noble picture as this", "context_len": 18}}}, "mizzen": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "She was coming along very fast , however ; and presently , when she took a rather broad sheer , I caught a momentary glimpse of two royals and just the head of a third -- the mizzen -- proving conclusively that she was full-rigged -- as was the Virginia", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "This time fortune did not smile on us , for as the shallop was on the Frenchman \u2019 s lee a spurt of flame burst from the man-of-war , immediately followed by a deafening roar , and with it our mizzen mast went by the board with a terrific crash", "context_len": 50}}}, "interval": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 16, "context": "Having concluded my reading of old Russell , how can I do better than employ the interval before the arrival of the Indian letters in sitting down and writing to my fair absent sister ? Colonel Sykes let me know last night that Robin would not come by this mail , which was , he says , only from Bombay , so that letters being all we must expect before Saturday fortnight , you need not hurry home on account of Robin \u2019 s return", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "M'Turk , M'Kane , and Selwyn at three paces interval", "context_len": 10}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was a critical moment , but in the brief interval Jethro recalled one of Shagbark \u2019 s reminiscences , in which he told of dodging every return bullet during a night attack by rolling aside the instant he fired his gun", "context_len": 42}}}, "surging": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 31, "context": "Before the men could be sent forward a pioneer battalion , composed mostly of Maoris , whose skill at rapid digging-in had won the admiration of the High Command , came surging up to assist in the consolidation of the captured position", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The Second Mate looked at the surging water , and considered the erratic rolling motion of the lofty hull", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Unexpectedly Bomba found himself on the edge of the river , the same surging , turbulent stream that had brought him to Jaguar Island", "context_len": 24}}}, "frisky": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` And it means no , and thank you , and if you please , and I love you , and everything else he wants to say , '' said Maggie , catching up her frisky pet in her arms and giving him a hug , which he returned by putting his cold nose in her face , after which he struggled to be put down again , for so glad was he to be free this pleasant morning that he wished to show it by frolicking about on his own four feet", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "To be harnessed in the little pony-carriage , and trot about with the old lady for her daily drive , was all the work to which Nonesuch was used ; and when he found Frankie perched upon him , he was very much displeased , and began a series of antics and prancings which were more becoming some frisky pony than a sober , well-behaved donkey", "context_len": 66}}}, "sheered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "He got almost to the point of telling me something , and then he sheered off on another tack , rubbed his red head , sighed , looked out of the window , and muttered something about guessing he 'd have to see you first", "context_len": 45}}}, "agile": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "Miss Wauton \u2019 s first impressions of her , jotted down as follows , do not give the impression of a very old lady , dearly as Charlotte Tucker loved to describe herself in those terms : Tall , slight , with lofty brow , sparkling eye , face constantly beaming with love and intelligence ; genius in every look ; figure frail and fairy-like , agile and graceful ; very brisk movements and light tread", "context_len": 76}}}, "shrilly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "Her nerves quivered as she listened to Mrs Harding shrilly droning the worn-out lullaby to the sleepless Polly , and Lemuel demanding to have Jack the Giant Killer told to him six times in succession", "context_len": 35}}}, "accounted": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 7, "context": "Harris , for instance , how he accounted for this , he would probably have laughed good-naturedly at the question , and answered : Why , easily enough ! How could it be otherwise with this glorious weather , this beautiful country , and our jolly little party ! But the real secret of what made the party so jolly was , in fact , quite beyond Mr", "context_len": 68}}}, "stentorian": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Mallory ! Mallory ! '' roared the excited plebes , backed up by an occasional `` Wow ! '' in the stentorian tones of the mighty Texan , who , by this time , was on the verge of epilepsy", "context_len": 41}}}, "rigger": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The tug dropped farther astern , and the towering square rigger began to slacken her rushing speed as her mighty yards were swung round", "context_len": 24}}}, "slacken": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "The surface was rough in many places , but not once did \u2019 Ceph slacken his splendid pace , which must have risen to twenty miles an hour", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Slow work ! Heart-breaking work ! If only the rain would stop , the torrential downpour slacken for a while , he might yet get free", "context_len": 26}}}, "razor": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "When my turn came I leapt with all the buoyancy of youth on to the lowermost crag , grasping an overhanging tuft of reeds and grass to gain the next step ; but the reeds were as sharp as a razor , and before I realized it the palm of my hand was cut to the bone , and the blood poured in a stream down my arm and over my doublet", "context_len": 72}}}, "coddled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "She went out and Edna , feeling that she had been coddled long enough , took a seat on a low chair , and pretty soon dinner was announced , the two eating it very happily together", "context_len": 37}}}, "reef": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The wind increased again after sunrise , and being unable to reef the sail single handed he managed partially to brail it up", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was now near noon , and about low tide ; the sun shone bright , the water was glassy , and they could plainly see the bottom , which was a reef of rocks covered with long kelps ; the largest of which now came to the top of the water , spreading their great red leaves over its surface", "context_len": 61}}}, "tactfully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then Arthur found time to stare hard at David , who was tactfully keeping in the background", "context_len": 17}}}, "jiggered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Well , I 'll be jiggered ! It 's you , is it ? '' shouted Arthur", "context_len": 18}}}, "abaft": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Cochran and his boy sat in the skipper 's little room abaft the wheel-house and talked to their heart 's content", "context_len": 21}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "One struck the hull just below the water-line on the starboard side , nearly abaft the foremost funnel ; the second bumped heavily under her port quarter", "context_len": 27}}}, "scorning": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "At first scorning the idea that Uncle Sam could render personal aid , the Huns were beginning to realize that the Americans were doing something great , and not merely `` talking big ''", "context_len": 34}}}, "newsboys": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Every one knows the familiar look of Smith 's bookstalls , with their energetic clerks , and their armies of pushing newsboys , and perchance think they were born with the railways and have grown up with them", "context_len": 38}}}, "emblazoned": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Clad in a pair of shorts of a vivid orange hue -- for Te Paheka shared with the rest of the Maoris a love of brilliant colour -- and with a silk red ensign emblazoned with the New Zealand stars round his waist , Te Paheka grinned amicably at the Afrikander", "context_len": 51}}}, "castaway": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I have n't had a chance to see you more than a minute , '' exclaimed the jubilant castaway", "context_len": 20}}}, "kidnap": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Well , the boy suffered more particularly because the old aunt at intervals made silly attempts to kidnap him , thereby angering his father and making him keep the boy shut up with old servants", "context_len": 35}}}, "goatee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Dried-up little man with a white goatee ? ''", "context_len": 9}}}, "docks": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Be sure to take Captain John down to see her when she docks", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 78, "context": "But the steamer did not mind how any of her passengers were feeling -- she must make the best possible record , no matter who was glad or sorry ; and on she steamed , past lonely and beautiful Holyhead , and then through the wide Irish Sea ( that seems indeed a veritable ocean in its wideness ) , until land once more was sighted and the harbor reached , and the anchor dropped off the wonderful docks at Liverpool", "context_len": 81}}}, "frosted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Cochran 's head and ask him to buy the Sea Witch and put Captain John in her , instead of the frosted old pippin that is master of her", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "Lilian transformed the pulpit into a perfect bower , weaving in holly and laurels till the Rector hoped she would leave room for him inside it ; and Peggy and Bobby helped -- or hindered -- the schoolmistress as she adorned the font with a wonderful arrangement of cotton-wool and frosted leaves", "context_len": 52}}}, "pippin": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "If you were to ask Clem McAllister why he named it that he would say , 'Because she is such a pippin , ' meaning a beauty , and that is all there is of it", "context_len": 36}}}, "sweating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Ahoy , there , '' he shouted , pushing open the door , and alighting fairly on top of David before the driver could pull up his sweating steed", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "When the natives gathered in this structure for sweating , either for promoting their health or as a religious ceremony , they developed steam by throwing water on heated stones , then danced around the fire , and went to plunge into the chilling waves of the river flowing past their town", "context_len": 52}}}, "steed": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 37, "context": "Often he went for hours without looking back to note in what order the company were following him , but now he had turned like Alden did a few minutes before , and , without checking his steed or shifting his course , was gazing fixedly to the rear", "context_len": 49}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "To Brighton or Worthing , and all with full speed , Which way are you going so mightily fast ? Take care of the reins , for your mettlesome steed Might stumble and lay you too low at the last", "context_len": 40}}}, "shipwrecked": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "It appeared that he was ashore at the moment making arrangements for the shipment of a cargo of slaves on the next day ; but the chief mate was aboard , and upon representing myself to him as a shipwrecked Englishman who had been carried away captive into the interior , and had just effected my escape , he gave me permission to remain , saying that he had no doubt Captain Duquesne would receive me if I were willing to work my passage to Martinique", "context_len": 86}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 48, "context": "Every boy who knows the lure of exploring and who loves to rig up huts and caves and tree-houses to fortify himself against imaginary enemies will enjoy these books , for they give a vivid chronicle of the doings and inventions of a group of boys who are shipwrecked and have to make themselves snug and safe in tropical islands where the dangers are too real for play", "context_len": 68}}}, "dotes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Mother dotes on the dear little sister , and she is a dear , and Captain Bracewell is a copper-fastened A1 old-time Yankee sailor , that you read about in books", "context_len": 31}}}, "ton": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 98, "context": "`` Of course she does n't know all I suffered , what with taking up one worthless claim after another month in and out -- if you mean that ! Why , I actually thought one time of giving up prospecting and settling down to day 's work ! Yes 'm ! It was sure enough that grub-stake you gave me last Fourth of July that brought me my first luck ! I put it right into Pony Gulch and my pick struck free-milling ore the first blow ! Some of the stuff runs ninety dollars to the ton and some higher", "context_len": 102}}}, "wince": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Elated as he was to learn that all the yacht 's crew had been accounted for , the praise of Margaret made David wince a trifle in spite of himself", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Then she threw back her head and laughed , a harsh , cackling laugh that caused Bomba to wince and shrink back as from the sharp thrust of a knife", "context_len": 30}}}, "ungenerous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Beating down this ungenerous emotion with a very manly spirit , David cordially agreed :", "context_len": 15}}}, "stages": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 40, "context": "The road which ran across the river at this spot continued southward into Wessex , and , so far as we could learn , was free from danger , so I determined to send my brethren to Abingdon by easy stages along its course , while I turned back with Father Adhelm , to share the misfortunes of my kindred and lay brethren in the woods", "context_len": 66}}}, "rattles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Sauga is a hard-shelled fruit or gourd , similar to a cocoa-nut , used for making rattles ; sa\u00fak\u00e4s I am rattling", "context_len": 23}}}, "luckier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Cochran , sends you his warmest regards , '' said Arthur , `` and wishes you a luckier voyage than the last", "context_len": 22}}}, "fidgeted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was like hunting a needle in a hay-stack to look for him , and Arthur fidgeted where he was until the deck officer warned him that it was time to go ashore", "context_len": 33}}}, "tally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I had to tally a lot of stores that just came aboard for the paint room , '' panted David", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` I 'll put a tally on the beauty", "context_len": 9}}}, "regretful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Warren returned with some sewing and without evincing undue curiosity led Marilla to talk of her past , though the child really knew very little about her mother and seemed to have no tender or regretful regard for this Mrs", "context_len": 40}}}, "comradeship": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "To me it seemed very remarkable indeed that the latter by which , if you please , I refer to Bernard Hayden should impart such information to Spotty Davis , with whom he had never been on terms of close comradeship , while withholding the desired knowledge from me", "context_len": 49}}}, "await": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "[ 594 ] Marvel thou not at this and let one of thy slave-girls abide await at the privy door , to open to me forthright , when she seeth me coming ; and I will cast about for a device whereby I may slay this accursed one", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 33, "context": "Now the Sultan had summoned to his presence the chiefs of his state and telling them that he had passed his word for the marriage of his daughter to Alaeddin , bade them await the latter , commanding them that , when he came , they should all go out to meet him ; moreover , he assembled the amirs and viziers and chamberlains and guards and captains of the troops and they were all awaiting Alaeddin at the door of the palace", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "At half past ten Sir John Kendall went round among the knights and bade fifty of them arm themselves quietly , and proceed , one by one , down to the gate , and there await orders", "context_len": 37}}}, "merrier": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Certainly , the more beasts the merrier for me", "context_len": 10}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was really a merrier repast than Edna had ever eaten under that roof , for instead of eating his dinner in silence as he generally did , Uncle Justus was quite talkative and actually attempted to joke once in a while", "context_len": 42}}}, "thereupon": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Thereupon they fenced in the fields selected for planting by means of rails and pickets , so as to prevent the wild animals from eating the maize-crop , and apportioned some of the land to each family [ 145 ] in the tribe", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "The unhappy victim , thus `` smelled out , '' would thereupon be instantly taken in charge by Mafuta 's followers , and the process would be repeated until all those whose removal was desired had been gathered in", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "She was by this time dead ahead of us ; and the skipper thereupon gave orders to bear away four points and set the larboard studdingsails ; at the same time instructing the look-out to give us instant warning of any change in the stranger 's course or amount of sail set", "context_len": 52}}}, "model": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` I wonder , '' he cried , `` if you can remember how , a many years ago , Sir Gyles carried you up to London , and you lay for a week at our house at Highgate ? I had never seen his like ! He seemed to me the very model of the old courtier of the Queen in the ballad ; he was so worshipful an old gentleman , and carried such a train of old servants riding with him", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "This is supposed to be a model country stable", "context_len": 9}}}, "christened": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Their first thought each day on coming home from school was to rush off to see how 'Sky Cottage , ' as they had christened it , had progressed in their absence , and their grief was loud if anything hindered Archie from the prosecution of his labours on Saturdays , while they quite envied Lilian being able to run out any time she liked and take a peep at the operations", "context_len": 72}}}, "miniature": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 87, "context": "Will you tell it to us , Marie-Celeste ? And Marie-Celeste , nothing loath , and willing at last to substantiate her claims in the ears of doubting Albert , rested a hand comfortably on either arm of her chair , and commenced , preceding her narration with the request , You will correct me , wo n't you , if you find I do not tell it right ? to which Her Majesty smilingly acceded , first asking Miss Belmore to hand her a little jewelled miniature case from among the other treasures on the table", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "It seems that opposite the Nag 's Head Inn the Church of England has built a tiny edifice , and as though to apologize for the apparent unreasonableness of building any church there whatsoever , they have made a most miniature affair of it", "context_len": 44}}}, "tagged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "For the bridge of the liner Arthur found a most dashing miniature captain in blue , who was tagged , in honor of the absent friend , `` Captain David Downes", "context_len": 31}}}, "winsomely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Margaret , dressed in white , with a rose in her fair hair , was winsomely girlish , enjoying every moment of this red-letter night", "context_len": 25}}}, "costly": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 89, "context": "Then the slave of the lamp came to him and taking him up , set him down in his house and said to him , `` O my lord , dost thou need aught ? '' `` Yes , '' answered Alaeddin ; `` I will have thee bring me eight-and-forty mamelukes , [ 454 ] four-and-twenty to walk before me and four-and-twenty to walk behind me , with their horses and clothes and arms , and let all that is upon them and their horses be of stuffs costly and precious exceedingly , such as are not found in kings ' treasuries", "context_len": 103}}}, "perils": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 81, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Notwithstanding the scorching heat of an Indian summer , -- in spite , too , of the fact that a number of the men were obliged to march in heavy garments utterly unsuited to the climate ; though death , disease , and a thousand perils lay in front of them , -- not a man of Havelock 's `` Ironsides '' but was impatient to push onward to death or victory", "context_len": 72}}}, "pensively": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Leaning heavily on the handle of his hoe , Joe gazed pensively away to the west", "context_len": 16}}}, "senorita": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` There was a black-eyed senorita in Valparaiso", "context_len": 8}}}, "hereby": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Conway read : `` I hereby appoint Edna Conway to be my proxy and to vote upon any question which may come up before this meeting", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` \u2019 -- -- Mine desires \u2019 '' resumed Master Whitehead , receiving the interruption with a deprecatory cough , `` \u2019 I doe hereby give full directions in soe that the treasures of mine house at Holwick may come to him by right", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "When the latter saw Alaeddin , he accosted him and said to him , `` O my son , what wilt thou ? This many a time have I seen thee pass hereby and betake thyself to such an one , a Jew , and I have seen thee give him certain things", "context_len": 53}}}, "values": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "The whole question of our duty as Christians , on this point as on all others , hinges here , -- Are we doing , or are we not doing , that which God wills us to do ? All theories respecting outlays , values , results , sink into utter insignificance beside this question", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 92, "context": "You see , a gage of this kind does not necessarily mean that there is any deep feeling between the knight who bears it and the lady who bestows it ; it shows only that she , on her part , feels it an honour that her gage should be worn by a distinguished knight , and , on his part , that he considers it as somewhat more than a compliment , and wears it as a proof of regard on the part of one whose good opinion at least he values", "context_len": 93}}}, "conspiracy": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 45, "context": "While halting for these foraging parties to return , a Persian nobleman came into the camp , and informed Alexander that Darius and the forces accompanying him were encamped about two days ' march in advance , but that Bessus was in command -- the conspiracy having been successful , and Darius having been deposed and made a prisoner", "context_len": 59}}}, "presuming": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "He guessed he knew how a queen ought to look in her robes of velvet and ermine , and with characteristic self-sufficiency had at once settled it in his venturesome little mind that this was the Queen 's mother ; and Marie-Celeste , presuming he knew whereof he spoke , simply took him at his word", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "You are , according to our custom here , considered almost a woman , and had not Sir Gervaise belonged to a religious Order , and were he of a presuming disposition , he might well have gathered a meaning from your words far beyond what you intended , and have even entertained a presumptuous hope that you were not indifferent to his merits", "context_len": 64}}}, "secrecy": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 25, "context": "Charlie lost no time putting into execution Captain Rhines \u2019 s directions in respect to the pig , having first enjoined upon them the greatest secrecy , not even permitting them to tell Ben and Sally of his plots and suspicions , lest Joe , who was very quick of perception , should divine what was in store for him", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "If Lottie made any discoveries , she could be bound to secrecy also", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "It is just like Aunt Patty to do a thing in this way ; and her very secrecy and her unwillingness to confess why she would not have the grove , or what she intended to do with the money , convinced me that she was sacrificing herself for the good of some other person or persons", "context_len": 57}}}, "prompting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "It was a daring prospect to imagine that he , a mere boy , would be allowed to succeed to a grown man 's position and salary -- and yet Bart had placed himself in line for it with every prompting of diligence and duty", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 85, "context": "Her mother sprang up and kissed her rapturously , crying -- 'Is n't she the dearest and sweetest thing and the smartest ! Think of her learning that and acting it off so completely , and not three years old ! She is smarter than Violet ' -- and then Violet set up such a howl ! Her mother pacified her by saying Marilla should tell her a piece , and after several efforts Cinderella did induce her to say by a great deal of prompting 'Milkman , Milkman , where have you been ? ' Think of the wear on the child 's nerves , and she looked so tired", "context_len": 111}}}, "congratulations": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 62, "context": "Gervaise , who had , -- thanks partly to his skill with his weapons , but still more to the temper of the splendid suit of armour presented to him by Genoa , -- escaped without a scratch , volunteered to remain with him until next morning , his principal motive for making the request being his desire to escape from further congratulations and praise for the success of his plan", "context_len": 71}}}, "toot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Why do n't you toot your whistle and salute us , Captain Downes ? Too haughty and stuck-up , I suppose , like all you steamer captains", "context_len": 28}}}, "captains": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 67, "context": "Now the Sultan had summoned to his presence the chiefs of his state and telling them that he had passed his word for the marriage of his daughter to Alaeddin , bade them await the latter , commanding them that , when he came , they should all go out to meet him ; moreover , he assembled the amirs and viziers and chamberlains and guards and captains of the troops and they were all awaiting Alaeddin at the door of the palace", "context_len": 83}}}, "clipper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The blood of a long line of sailor ancestors thrilled in Margaret 's veins , as she clasped her hands and leaned forward to waft her breath against the white sails of the clipper ship", "context_len": 35}}}, "dipped": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 2, "context": "They were dipped into the rain-water barrel by means of a rope and pulley , and they were cast from `` the terrible height into the awful chasm , '' which ordeal consisted merely in being pushed down a space of about three feet , upon some hay , but being blindfolded was supposed to make up for the difference", "context_len": 60}}}, "furrowed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The Sea Witch dipped to this fair gale , gathered headway , and furrowed the pond with a wake of tiny ripples", "context_len": 22}}}, "bowsprit": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 38, "context": "An hour after midnight our mainsail , though treble-reefed , parted with a report that was heard above the storm , the torn canvas streaming out to lee \u2019 ard like so many whips ; and simultaneously our bowsprit carried away close to the gammonings , with the result that the ship yawed , then shot up into the wind", "context_len": 60}}}, "swerving": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "Her bowsprit pointed straight at Captain Bracewell , and fanned by the breath of the guests as she passed them , the Sea Witch glided without swerving from her course to the mossy bank in front of the captain 's plate", "context_len": 41}}}, "mossy": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "This pretty , leafy house was furnished with a table formed of an old mossy stump , and with a couple of small logs for seats , and here the children used to play , as Bessie said with `` the greatest cunningness", "context_len": 43}}}, "manikin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Cochran tossed the luckless manikin into the water , where he sank to the bottom without a struggle , and reposed against a rock with arms calmly folded across his chest", "context_len": 31}}}, "superstition": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 16, "context": "It would seem incredible that such a mind as his could really admit such an absurd superstition as the story of his divine origin , and we must therefore suppose that he gave this direction in order that the place of his interment might confirm the idea of his superhuman nature in the general opinion of mankind", "context_len": 57}}}, "omen": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "All this while poor wayward Ted , whose name you must have guessed almost from the first , was lying wholly oblivious to everything about him , muttering now and then a few delirious , incoherent words , and yet by degrees subsiding into a gentle , regular breathing that the professional ear was quick to detect , and that was full of good omen for the waking in the morning", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "This was an omen , but what was the signification of it ? Gordius did not know , and he accordingly went to a neighboring town in order to consult the prophets and soothsayers", "context_len": 34}}}, "husky": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Say , Al , am you dar ? asked Jethro in a husky , half-whisper , as he peeped round the rim of the wheel at the figure dimly visible in the shadow , and almost within arm \u2019 s reach", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 23, "context": "She read aloud to an audience consisting of the two dolls and the three kittens , she sang hymns , in rather a husky voice to be sure , and she stood at the window a long time watching the people pass by on their way to and from church", "context_len": 50}}}, "mutter": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Engaged ! Good heavens ! '' I heard Lady Thesiger mutter , and then she sat very still , and fixed her eyes for a moment on my face , with a sort of glance which seemed to say , `` Are you quite absolutely mad ? ''", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Round and round the ring the two men went , until the South Africans yelled to their man to hurry up and the Anzacs began to mutter impatiently", "context_len": 28}}}, "imposing": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 67, "context": "In fact , it is probable that Alexander 's slaughter of the Persian army at Arbela , and subsequent spoliation of Susa , constitute , taken together , the most gigantic case of murder and robbery which was ever committed by man ; so that , in performing these deeds , the great hero attained at last to the glory of having perpetrated the grandest and most imposing of all human crimes", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "Somehow he had not given much thought to this subject of first and second class till on that first morning out , when he found the low iron gate imposing itself so resolutely between himself and his little friend ; but then he realized at a bound how much there was in it", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "Harold , who had been all through the stables of the Duke of Westminster at Eton Hall , had described something much finer than this -- imposing buildings surrounding a courtyard paved with bevel-edged squares of stone , with not so much as a whisp of hay or straw to be seen anywhere , and in the centre a noble statue of a high-spirited horse , rearing and pulling hard at the bridle , held in the hand of a stalwart groom , who seems fully equal to the occasion", "context_len": 90}}}, "solidly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The ramparts whereon he was standing were of Roman workmanship , built so solidly that they had resisted every attack of man or of time ; while down below lay the ruins of a magnificent villa , once occupied by the Roman governor of the island", "context_len": 46}}}, "ballasted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I am a business man , and you are pretty solidly ballasted yourself", "context_len": 13}}}, "dismiss": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 46, "context": "The thoughts of his father 's plight , a haunting dread that Colonel Harrington might make him some trouble , and the uncertainty of continued work in the express service , all combined to depress his mind with anxiety and suspense , and he tried to dismiss the themes by whistling a quiet , soothing tune as he started to get the hammer to put the padlock in place", "context_len": 69}}}, "potentates": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 3, "context": "The princes and potentates of all the neighboring countries assembled to partake of his hospitality , to be entertained by the games , the plays , the spectacles , and the feastings , and to unite in swelling his court and doing him honor", "context_len": 44}}}, "tactful": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Then the English mistress , Miss Archer , knew so much , and was so tactful and charming ; and Mademoiselle Omont knew her own tongue so beautifully , and was also such a perfect German scholar ! In short , the seven girls had their work cut out for them , and there was not a minute 's pause to allow ambition and envy and jealousy to creep in", "context_len": 70}}}, "dumps": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Not only did the advance and support trenches get it hot , but for miles behind the lines hostile shells were dropping promiscuously , on the chance of blowing up one or more of the numerous dumps and otherwise hampering the supply columns", "context_len": 43}}}, "sleepless": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Her nerves quivered as she listened to Mrs Harding shrilly droning the worn-out lullaby to the sleepless Polly , and Lemuel demanding to have Jack the Giant Killer told to him six times in succession", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "What a sleepless night I have passed , what anxiety , what excitement ! and yet how unlike is he to what I had imagined ! so timid , so petulant ! and that perpetual punning ! It matters not , however , -- his title to our services remains the same ! A strange misgiving is on my soul ; is it the shadow of approaching danger , or only the fear of it ? The Colonel gave me a strange meaning look as he passed me this morning , and said , You are early up , Miss Ratty ; I fear that your rest was broken last night", "context_len": 111}}}, "kindled": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "And to talk of buying her as if she were the toy which had floated on the dinner table ! It was all stark nonsense , yet his kindled imagination could not help painting bright pictures", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 22, "context": "At the moment I took but scant notice of the circumstance , for the flickering flames of the fire which was always kindled upon such occasions played strange pranks with the lights and shadows , and often imparted a weird effect of movement to stationery and even inanimate objects ; but presently , happening to again glance in that direction , my eye was once more caught by the same queer wavering movement", "context_len": 73}}}, "longshoremen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Next morning Captain Bracewell trudged off to his gang of longshoremen on a North River pier", "context_len": 16}}}, "inspect": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 50, "context": "The little old gentleman turned out to be a very learned don from Oxford , with a terrific list of letters at the end of his name , and in the course of a few days a representative arrived from the Bodleian Library with a request to be allowed to inspect the books ( which by this time had been removed to a corner of the big drawing-room ) , and after a considerable amount of talking and writing it was arranged that they should be sent to London at once to be sold by auction", "context_len": 96}}}, "deciding": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 25, "context": "Albert gave Marie-Celeste a look which said as plainly as words : That jus ' American little girls could be awful mean , and evidently deciding it would be best to leave that kind of a girl to herself , turned on his heel and walked straight off toward the castle with a consequential air , and as though bent on reporting such unseemly conduct to Her Majesty in person", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "When it came to the crucial moment he could not `` make good , '' and Tom was put in his place , in the pitching box , and by phenomenal work won the deciding game", "context_len": 36}}}, "spindle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "If you will give me enough to think about , I wo n't have time to bother with my weak chest and spindle legs", "context_len": 24}}}, "investigate": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 26, "context": "Marie-Celeste looked after him a moment with a most amused smile , and then growing to feel more at home amid royal surroundings , turned to investigate the little miniature elephants that flank the steps leading down from the eastern terrace", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Well , I guess you \u2019 d better get off and let me take charge of the horse , and we \u2019 ll investigate this matter a little", "context_len": 28}}}, "scrawled": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 4, "context": "The letter was roughly scrawled on a pad , and when Texas finished reading it he flung it on the floor and went and glared at himself in the mirror", "context_len": 30}}}, "stub": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "At the last moment , smitten by regret because fancied necessity seemed to compel him to leave without bidding the kind widow good-by , he seized a piece of brown paper and the stub of a pencil and sat down to write a few words of farewell Jerry urging him to hasten even while he was scribbling", "context_len": 57}}}, "scanning": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 13, "context": "For another hour he pressed rapidly through the jungle , his eyes sharply scanning every tree and covert , for dusk was coming on and the beasts of prey would soon be starting on their nightly mission of death", "context_len": 39}}}, "rumpled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Noon found him waiting in the ante-room of his father 's private offices , chewing his pencil stub and scanning many rumpled pages of calculations", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Albert , you may be sure , was standing as close as possible to the foot of the steps , and tumbled curls and rumpled collar soon bore witness to an exceedingly hearty exchange of greetings", "context_len": 36}}}, "calculations": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 34, "context": "Up went the smoke , the heather catching fire at once , in went the stick , and out came the bees in an angry swarm ; but something had gone wrong in the calculations , for instead of falling stupefied on to the grass , they flew unharmed through the smoke , and fell upon their tormentors with a buzz of indignation", "context_len": 63}}}, "confidential": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Now , what were you saying to the Mumsy-pums ? I do n't allow any one to talk in a confidential way to my Mumsy-pums except myself", "context_len": 28}}}, "secretary": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 11, "context": "Sir Knights , he said , I have heard from my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "hereafter": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` The coal over there makes too much slack , anyhow , '' said the mine boss , `` so we intend hereafter to stick to the west", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 67, "context": "The captain and master could not , however , see the matter in this light , and besought Sir Richard to have a care of them , declaring that the Spaniards would be ready to treat with them ; and that , as there were a number of gallant men yet living whose wounds were not mortal , they might do their country and prince acceptable service hereafter", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "But now you know something of what the crests on the ceiling of the Banqueting Hall mean , and the shields in the panels along the sides , that are waiting for the crests of the Knights that may hereafter be admitted into the Order", "context_len": 45}}}, "stationery": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "At the moment I took but scant notice of the circumstance , for the flickering flames of the fire which was always kindled upon such occasions played strange pranks with the lights and shadows , and often imparted a weird effect of movement to stationery and even inanimate objects ; but presently , happening to again glance in that direction , my eye was once more caught by the same queer wavering movement", "context_len": 73}}}, "sheer": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 82, "context": "In her plan to give Joe a fright , she had never thought of Father , who returned by the same road ; and now that horrible white object was stationed just in the very danger-spot where a plunging horse might mean a matter of almost certain death , for the wall of the five-acre field abutted the road on one side , and on the other there was only a narrow patch of grass between the steep bank which shelved down sheer into the river , while the closed gate stopped any chance of a dash forward", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "At the bottom of the meadows which bounded the Abbey land the river took a sharp turn past a few bold cliffs which rose almost sheer out of the water , and by scrambling along the rocks at the base it was possible to get round this headland and reach the low entrance of the cave , which was raised only a few feet above the level of the river", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Sure enough , he turned about , and going to a spot where the mountain fell sheer down a hundred feet or so , he pawed away the snow , and there , half on , half over the edge of the precipice , hung Ted , his clothes caught by a bush , and holding him back from sure destruction", "context_len": 62}}}, "plied": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 44, "context": "She catechized Lilian freely about her housekeeping arrangements , hoped Nancy did her duty , and did not neglect to sweep out corners , told Bobby that his irrepressible curls looked girlish , and his hair ought to be cropped close every week , plied Peggy with embarrassing questions on the subject of fine needlework and stocking-darning , and drank four cups of tea in the meantime , with the air of one conferring a favour thereby", "context_len": 77}}}, "jabs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He did not look happy as he plied the sail-needle with vicious jabs , while he thought , half aloud :", "context_len": 21}}}, "cabled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "After dancing along the deck with a cabled message in his fist , he sat down on the edge of his bunk to think things over", "context_len": 26}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "If anything should happen to you out there -- one has to consider such a thing -- the money can then be cabled to the governor", "context_len": 26}}}, "skylights": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "In the spacious after-cabin , bright with the summer sun which flooded through the open skylights , Margaret was saying almost the last of her good-bys", "context_len": 26}}}, "bouquets": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}}}, "unspoken": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 33, "context": "At length we discovered that if we gave our parole we should be allowed comparative freedom within the city ; if not , well -- a meaning shrug of the shoulders completed the unspoken sentence", "context_len": 35}}}, "sadness": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 66, "context": "The little black cow with the long horns looked strange to eyes accustomed to the large red and white cows at the Abbey , but her milk seemed sweet and good , and the woman sang a song in Welsh while she milked it , to a strange , haunting kind of melody that , like most of the Celtic music , had a touch of sadness and pathos about it", "context_len": 71}}}, "prouder": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "I give it to you now as a woman , far prouder than before that you should wear her gage , and not blind to the meaning of the emblem", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "It is a timely gift , Sir Gervaise , for the siege has played havoc with the suit Genoa gave you ; it is sorely battered , dinted , and broken , and , although you can doubtless get it repaired , if I were you I would keep it in its present state as a memorial -- and there could be no prouder one -- of the part you bore in the siege", "context_len": 74}}}, "overlook": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 102, "context": "could I bear it to come home and not find you here , -- never again to see you smile when I come in ; never to hear you say , ' I 'm so glad you 've come , Tom ; ' never to get the kiss that puts heart into me after a hard day 's work ? And the babies , -- would you wish them motherless ? To be sure , you ca n't do for them what you once did , but that will all come right yet ; and there 's the mother 's eye to overlook and see that things do n't go too far wrong ; here 's the mother voice and the mother smile for them to turn to", "context_len": 128}}}, "boatman": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 74, "context": "The moment before Harry Paul seemed to have been exerting his full strength to force the boat through the water , but an accession of strength came to him , and with a few fierce thrusts he drove her bows into the edge of the current , which gave it so quick a snatch that it was whirled round , and its occupant nearly lost his footing ; but he was too practised a boatman for that", "context_len": 77}}}, "figuring": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Moreover , he knew that some day it were far from improbable that he himself would be figuring in a similar scene as a chief actor , or rather chief victim", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "y\u00e1ntuntun hitchk\u00e1tigan beyond sight , is an instance of a postposition figuring as preposition , and is connected with the objective case of a noun", "context_len": 25}}}, "shoreward": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "David watched the boat move shoreward , until it dodged behind a string of barges , and then he returned to Margaret in the cabin", "context_len": 25}}}, "barges": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 4, "context": "Supposedly the string of barges was `` manned '' by women , the diminishing group halting at each barge to prolong the conversation before the crews boarded their respective boats , until , by the clatter almost overhead , the fugitives knew that the last barge had received its complement -- two , perhaps three , buxom and stolid German women", "context_len": 61}}}, "drudging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "And you will be just drudging along in the Roanoke for years and years , before you can get that far", "context_len": 21}}}, "retires": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Besides , he stands a fine chance to be master of the Sea Witch when Captain John retires", "context_len": 18}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Like a poor wounded deer which retires to his thicket to die , Alfgar threw himself down upon the bed of straw", "context_len": 22}}}, "nutshell": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Which course do you want me to take ? The one I ought to steer , or the one I want to follow ? There 's the whole thing in a nutshell", "context_len": 32}}}, "dearer": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "If so , it was the mistake of burning love and self-devotion ; and one can well believe that such a mistake must be dearer to the Heart of our Lord than the correct attitude of those who always decide on the safe and comfortable side", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 85, "context": "Then he embraced Alaeddin and fell to kissing him and said to him , `` Excuse me , O my son , for that I was going [ 623 ] to bereave thee of thy life , through the wickedness of yonder accursed sorcerer who cast thee into this pit ; and indeed , O my son , I was excusable in that which I did with thee , inasmuch as I saw myself bereft of my daughter and mine only one , who is dearer to me than my kingdom , and thou knowest how fathers ' hearts yearn upon their children , more by token that I have but the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 114}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "But yet in God 's sight that poor drunkard , struggling , though struggling feebly , to repent , was far nearer , far dearer than this Pharisee , who had never yet known love , human or divine", "context_len": 39}}}, "fogy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` It is n't always easy to get your true bearings , my boy , and maybe I did wrong in trying to persuade you to sail with an old fogy like me", "context_len": 33}}}, "whisker": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` You 've got what you wanted , and what more do you want ? Why , I did n't look as dismal as all this when my last skipper chased me ashore , with his one whisker whistlin ' in the wind", "context_len": 43}}}, "scrubber": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` And what would we do with the useless little paint scrubber aboard a real ship ? '' exclaimed Mr", "context_len": 20}}}, "anchorage": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "When it became light it was seen that the Turkish ships had sailed away to their previous anchorage on the other side of the Island , and although at considerable intervals the great cannon hurled their missiles against the fort , it was evident that , for the time at least , the attack was not to be pressed at that point", "context_len": 62}}}, "thresh": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Promptly on the stroke of ten , as if she were moved by clockwork , the decks trembled to the thresh of her giant screws , hawsers came writhing in to the rattle of donkey-engines fore and aft , and the black hull of the liner slid slowly past her pier", "context_len": 51}}}, "hawsers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Promptly on the stroke of ten , as if she were moved by clockwork , the decks trembled to the thresh of her giant screws , hawsers came writhing in to the rattle of donkey-engines fore and aft , and the black hull of the liner slid slowly past her pier", "context_len": 51}}}, "rattle": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 39, "context": "His captive was , however , soon safe in the large basket , and he had hardly closed the lid and placed a boulder used as ballast upon it before a tug at his other line made the thole-pin rattle , and after a little hauling he dragged in a gloriously-coloured gurnard , whose outspread fins looked like the wings of some lovely butterfly", "context_len": 64}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "But wouldn \u2019 t the whole game just naturally rattle your spurs ? What \u2019 s the good of it ? How are Tibbits , Dimmock , and the rest going to make anything by their work ?", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "He had already begun turning his little boat , and then , bending to his task , he forced it through the water as he stood up in the stern , making the rippling waves rattle and splash against her bows as a line of foam parted on either side", "context_len": 50}}}, "nothings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 74, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}}}, "jibs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now I hear the captain say to the mate , 'Set your jibs", "context_len": 13}}}, "staysails": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "' And next it is , 'Set your staysails", "context_len": 9}}}, "topsails": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "We were raising her very fast now that both ships were steering upon converging lines ; I could already see nearly to the foot of her topsails ; and I settled myself comfortably , determined to remain where I was until I could absolutely identify her , although even at this time I had scarcely a shadow of a doubt that it was the long-sought Virginia , or rather the Preciosa , that I held in the field of my telescope", "context_len": 81}}}, "apologies": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "At the last minute Father had been obliged to send his regrets and apologies , for the veterinary surgeon had arrived to doctor a sick horse , and he could not possibly leave the stables , so the tea-party must perforce begin without him , for the days were growing short now , and there was no time to spare", "context_len": 60}}}, "bribed": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 49, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}}}, "foremast": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "Hardly had the body of men reached the foremast when the Gannet glided forward and entered the deep waters of the harbour , the two ropes on her quarter trailing astern , and the mob of excited negroes who had manned them were shouting and dancing on the banks of the creek", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Most of the foremast hands were aloft in the rigging , I myself being in the maintop", "context_len": 17}}}, "chanties": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "It was a hard task that he set them , but by hook and crook he had gathered a dozen deep-water `` shell-backs '' of the old breed among his thirty foremast hands , and they knew the old-time sailors ' chanties", "context_len": 42}}}, "braces": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 63, "context": "Not to be outdone in politeness , we hoisted our colours also ; and for the next quarter of an hour the two craft continued to close , the chase stolidly maintaining her course , while we , under the skipper 's skilful conning , continued to edge very gradually away , as the other vessel sped to leeward , checking our weather braces by a few inches at a time until our yards were all but square", "context_len": 78}}}, "lusty": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 52, "context": "A horse is quick to learn what kind of man holds the reins , and woe to him whom the equine despises ! Bucephalus would not have needed any enlightenment had Harper or any one of the regular riders been in the saddle , but he wasn \u2019 t sure about the lusty young fellow who was trying to lord it over him", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The men feared no interruption , for they were singing a lusty song in broad Dorset dialect , the chorus of which ran :", "context_len": 24}}}, "topsail": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "There was a shower of splinters and the broken spar fell , till brought up by the strain on the topsail and t \u2019 gallant sail , and at the same time the halyards of the foresail parted , bringing that sail down to the deck with a run", "context_len": 49}}}, "chanced": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 36, "context": "As the heaviest part of his work at headquarters was over , and as pretty much everything had depended on the reply to General Forrest 's requisition on his superior officer -- who , unfortunately , chanced to be General Bragg -- for arms and ammunition , Harry had no difficulty in securing leave of absence for the day ; and so , when all the arrangements had been made , we set out the next evening for the house where Jack Bledsoe lay", "context_len": 84}}}, "shipmates": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 30, "context": "There were Sir George Lee , greyer and more bent than of yore , Master Hugh Salesbury , Lawyer Whitehead , all from the neighbourhood of Rake ; my old shipmates , Greville Drake and Felgate , the latter accompanied by his young wife , whom he had lately wedded , and several others who had been friends of my father long before I could remember", "context_len": 66}}}, "insane": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "The swift-falling darkness , the knowledge that danger was all about them , that insane enemies were skulking perhaps behind a tree , leering at them from branches overhead , crouching in ambush behind a concealing bush or shrub , at any moment to reach out a hairy arm -- all these things combined to fill them with terror", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 6, "context": "Roscoe was kept in a private insane asylum , though he had full possession of his reason , only he was made ill by drugs constantly administered to him by men who had an interest in keeping him out of the way", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "It would not surprise me at all if he should make his appearance with some needy physician who for a fee will be ready to pronounce me insane", "context_len": 28}}}, "deporting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` If you please , sir , two of those insane steerage passengers we are deporting have broken out , and are running amuck below", "context_len": 25}}}, "amuck": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "As though a taxicab , minus its driver and running amuck into a stone wall , was not enough hard luck to throw across the path of Motor Matt , he had also to deal with a young woman masquerading in man \u2019 s attire", "context_len": 45}}}, "unobserved": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "The sharks still kept off ; our gunners ceased to fire , and the running bowline was dropped from our catheads for the man to be hauled on board , when , within fifty yards from us , he suddenly disappeared , and over the spot darted yet another huge shark that , unobserved , had lurked under our bows", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "I could easily hide a piece of white cotton a yard or so long in my clothes , scanty as these are , and could certainly manage , unobserved , to drop it outside the window", "context_len": 36}}}, "unmoved": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 87, "context": "When you went to Scotland first , you and he were of one mind , one heart , but when you came hither again , your head was stuffed full of worldly wisdom and time-serving devices , talking of a Lord Protector instead of the glory of God , and hand and glove with that cruel Cromwell who did throw my saint into prison ! Your heart was turned from those that reared you , and given to their enemies ! And now you can stand by unmoved and see him you once loved haled to prison and death ! ''", "context_len": 101}}}, "dummy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Do n't stand like a dummy ! Below with you ! ''", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The preliminary course with dummy bombs had been completed , and now came the exciting part of this particular branch of training -- hurling live Mills ' bombs", "context_len": 28}}}, "bedlam": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A bedlam of noises came from the steerage quarters , groans and shrieks and prayers", "context_len": 15}}}, "tussles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "David pitched in with the momentum of a centre-rush , and after several sharp tussles looked around him to find that his doughty comrades had done their duty well", "context_len": 29}}}, "doughty": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The doughty warrior , who had not quailed before the charge of the jaguar , seemed to shrink into himself , and Bomba had a new glimpse into the ruthless character with whom he was soon to deal", "context_len": 38}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Truly it seemed well nigh impossible that any one who was like to be on that craft could have performed so doughty a deed", "context_len": 24}}}, "lunatics": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 66, "context": "I sez he ai n't in , but I asks Miss Lilian , and she tells 'em `` Yes , '' and there they all is , pokin ' about amongst the nettles , and grubbin ' up stones , and stickin ' bits of mortar in their pockets , and dodgin ' about with yard measures , for all the world like a set of lunatics", "context_len": 67}}}, "steadied": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "Behind and below him were the knives and pistols of the pirates , above him was I , covering his trembling body with a pistol that I steadied against the edge of the lubber \u2019 s hole", "context_len": 37}}}, "mutinous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "They were lazy and mutinous ; the sentries went to sleep , the scouts were unreliable , they were full of complaints ; whilst round about him were the natives , ready to steal , maim , and murder whenever they could get an opportunity", "context_len": 45}}}, "outburst": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 43, "context": "I begged them for my sake to maintain an absolute silence as to that affair , and I have no doubt that they have done so , for in the fury the news would excite , they might fall victims to the first outburst , though , of course , wholly innocent of any share in the misfortune", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "With another furious outburst the knight rushed out of the room , mounted his horse , and , followed by his two servants and the messenger of ill-tidings , rode furiously down the road to Midhurst , the noise of the horses \u2019 hoofs clattering on the frosty road testifying to the speed at which they were urged", "context_len": 58}}}, "glinted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "David looked around at the speck of blue ocean which glinted through an open porthole", "context_len": 15}}}, "porthole": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "He fitted an arrow to the string and took aim through a porthole at the nearest figure", "context_len": 17}}}, "disconsolate": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "Here and there a dead sheep or pig drifted by , or a portion of a haystack ; a hurdle floating like a raft bore on it a few disconsolate fowls , clucking dismally ; while an occasional wash-tub or upturned table showed that the river must have already flooded some of the low-lying cottages higher up on its course", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Churchill , Professor Tines , and the proctor moved away , leaving a very disconsolate group of football candidates on the gridiron", "context_len": 22}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Why , you disconsolate little monkey ! Are there not enough grown people here already , making children of themselves for your amusement , but you must want the colonel too ? If he was here , he could not play with you , poor fellow ! ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "sulking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The disconsolate David , sulking in the steerage , was not wise enough to know that in this trying hour he was doing that which would have made his `` dearest folks '' happy in this big boy of theirs", "context_len": 40}}}, "pyramid": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Still I 've heard some more far-fetched than that -- one a friend of mine read on an Egyptian pyramid and brought home to tell for new", "context_len": 27}}}, "fleck": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "And how the pony did go ! With a snort he flirted his head , as if he would shake off everything that held him back as easily as he blew aside the fleck of foam that alighted like a snowflake on the knee of his rider", "context_len": 47}}}, "pseudonym": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Frederick Garrison '' is a pseudonym used by Upton Sinclair", "context_len": 10}}}, "academy": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 69, "word_index": 48, "context": "The man who built it and attempted to run it had failed to find business profitable , and in time it was purchased at a low price by Urian Eliot , Roger \u2019 s father , who moved it to its present location and pledged it to the academy as long as the scholars should continue to use it as a gymnasium", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Several of the boys , some of them in football clothes , for there was to be practice immediately after school , had paused in a little group a short distance from the academy steps and were watching to note the result of Roger \u2019 s appeal to the new scholar", "context_len": 51}}}, "mallory": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 0, "context": "Mallory , '' said he , `` just as sure as I 'm alive this thing shall stop ! Keep up heart now , and we 'll make a fight for it ! While there 's life there 's hope , they say -- and , by Heaven , you sha n't be expelled ! ''", "context_len": 56}}}, "lieut": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "LIEUT", "context_len": 1}}}, "yearling": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "I was a yearling once myself , and I 'm a cadet still , and there 's not the least use trying to pull the wool over my eyes", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 77, "context": "Dewey was debating whether discretion were not really the whole of valor , and whether it were not better to `` run away and live to fight -- or run away -- some other day ; '' and Indian was actually doubling up his fat little fists about to strike the first blow in his fat little life ; when suddenly came a shout behind them , and a moment later a strong hand seized the advancing yearling by the back of his collar and flung him head first to the ground", "context_len": 92}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` You see yesterday morning when he did n't turn up to fight that 'ere yearling fellow Williams , I thought 't was cause he was scared", "context_len": 27}}}, "insulted": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 35, "context": "When he had got out of hearing of his late captors he turned to Hadley and said , `` I hate that man ! He has put a stain on my honor ; he has insulted and outraged me", "context_len": 39}}}, "mightiest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 101, "context": "the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How now ? Deemest thou Alaeddin worthy to be bridegroom to my daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour ? Hast thou seen and considered this royal building and all these riches which man 's wit can not comprehend ? '' The Vizier , of his envy of Alaeddin , answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , indeed this palace and its building and all these riches may not be but by means of enchantment , for that no man among men , no , not the mightiest of them in dominion or the greatest in wealth , might avail to upraise and stablish [ the like of ] this building in one night", "context_len": 128}}}, "violated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The mightiest traditions of the academy had been violated , `` trampled beneath the dust , '' and that by two or three vile and uncivilized `` beasts '' -- '' plebes '' -- new cadets of scarcely a week 's experience", "context_len": 42}}}, "uncivilized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The mightiest traditions of the academy had been violated , `` trampled beneath the dust , '' and that by two or three vile and uncivilized `` beasts '' -- '' plebes '' -- new cadets of scarcely a week 's experience", "context_len": 42}}}, "plebes": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 34, "context": "The tables are divided down the center by an imaginary line , each part being a `` table '' ; first class men sit near the head , and so on down to the plebes , who find themselves at the center ( that is , after they have moved into camp , and been `` sized '' and assigned to companies ; before that they are `` beasts , '' herded apart , as has been said )", "context_len": 79}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "Saturday afternoon is also a holiday time for the yearling , too , and he is accustomed to amuse himself with variety shows and concerts , recitations and exhibition drills , continuous performances that are free , given by the `` beasts , '' the `` trained animals , '' or plebes", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "And those plebes shall pay for this ! ''", "context_len": 9}}}, "yearlings": {"freq": 56, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 56, "word_index": 46, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}}}, "inherent": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "As a scholar he shone ; as a business man he was a failure , owing to the fatal and all too common trait amongst Maoris of the educated class of pleasure in the spending of money , and , oddly enough , to an inherent tendency to relapse , if only temporarily , to an aboriginal existence", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "hazers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "And the third class , the yearlings , by inherent right the guardians of West Point 's honor , and the hazers of the plebe , had vowed that those plebes must be punished as never had plebes been punished before", "context_len": 41}}}, "plebe": {"freq": 79, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 76, "word_index": 25, "context": "Now , I want you to understand in the first place that I know of this contemptible trick , and that also I know the plebe , who 's worth more than a dozen of you ; and that if he gets a demerit from any one of you again I 'll make you pay for it as sure as I 'm alive", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` `` Indian '' is the fourth and only other plebe present , and Indian is horrified , as usual , and mutters `` Bless my soul '' at intervals", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It usually takes the luckless plebe a week to get that idea hammered out of his head , and another week besides to learn the correct method", "context_len": 27}}}, "vowed": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 64, "context": "This was a new experience , and she could do nothing , try as she would , to terrify Rosamund , who laughed when she assured her that she was a changeling , and might perhaps take any form at any time , and might return to her real home with the fairies at any moment ; who laughed still more merrily when she vowed she would upset the boat", "context_len": 70}}}, "graduation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The first and third classes of cadets had gone into summer camp the previous day , immediately after the graduation exercises", "context_len": 21}}}, "inspections": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "From that date , the middle of June to July 1 , they have a comparative holiday , with no drills and no duties except guard-mounting , dress parade toward evening , and inspections", "context_len": 34}}}, "mornings": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 10, "context": "She used to study with Madame de Coucy in the mornings and go for walks among the flowers out in the garden there in the afternoon , and way beyond it too sometimes , and Richard would often come down from London for a visit , and he taught her English courtly ways and to play the mandolin ( Albert looked significantly toward the quaint mandolin , with a faded blue ribbon attached to it , that was lying among the other treasures on the table ) ; and when the King could not come for a regular visit , he would just ride down for a word and kiss", "context_len": 110}}}, "chairman": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` We have met , '' began the chairman , as soon as this formality was over -- '' we have met , I believe , to consider the cases of three 'beasts , ' Powers , Stanard and Mallory , by name ( a low groan from the class ) , and to consider the best method of reducing them to submission", "context_len": 63}}}, "boosted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Joseph Smith must be immediately `` boosted '' up and balanced upon the cadet 's outstretched hand , there to be shaken and otherwise tested , while the man below made audible calculations by means of trigonometrical formulas as to what was his actual weight", "context_len": 45}}}, "onto": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 77, "context": "You might not care much for it , but to me it would be a delight to follow our friends on Ted 's break as they rolled merrily out from town on the bright Monday morning succeeding their two days ' stay at Oxford , and to keep with them all the way ; not that anything momentous or wildly exciting happened on the trip , only that if it were possible to put all its charm onto paper , there is no question but you would enjoy it", "context_len": 89}}}, "contusions": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Bud Smith was a tall , heavily-built youth with a face covered by court-plaster and `` contusions , '' as the results of a West Point fight are officially designated by the hospital surgeon", "context_len": 35}}}, "designated": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` The subterranean dwellings , mentioned and visited by Milfort as being the legendary home of the `` Moskoquis , '' are not mentioned here ; and in French colonial times the `` Forks of Red river '' designated the confluence of Washita and Red rivers", "context_len": 46}}}, "surgeon": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 44, "context": "Touching my cap , I slipped up on to the poop , as in duty bound , to report myself to the first lieutenant , who gave me as hearty a welcome as the skipper had done , and then joined Copplestone , the surgeon , and one or two others who were obviously waiting to have a word with me , and retired with them to the gunroom , where my return was celebrated in due form", "context_len": 78}}}, "mopping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "And Bud illustrated his statement by gently mopping his `` contusions '' with a damp handkerchief", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The decks were still wet from their daily mopping , the folded steamer chairs were ranged five deep beneath the cabin windows , and nothing seemed to be quite in shape yet save her own tidy little self", "context_len": 38}}}, "formality": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 96, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}}}, "reducing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` We have met , '' began the chairman , as soon as this formality was over -- '' we have met , I believe , to consider the cases of three 'beasts , ' Powers , Stanard and Mallory , by name ( a low groan from the class ) , and to consider the best method of reducing them to submission", "context_len": 63}}}, "submission": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` We have met , '' began the chairman , as soon as this formality was over -- '' we have met , I believe , to consider the cases of three 'beasts , ' Powers , Stanard and Mallory , by name ( a low groan from the class ) , and to consider the best method of reducing them to submission", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 67, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}}}, "restate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I do n't think it is necessary for me to restate the complaints against them , for you are probably all as familiar with the incidents as I", "context_len": 28}}}, "disciplined": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 68, "context": "And now it was seen how his merits were recognised , and how he had already gained the love of his people , for , from the territory of the Hwiccas , and all the extreme west of Mercia , men flocked to his standard until he was at the head of an army almost as numerous as that he had lost at Assingdun , only less perfectly disciplined and officered", "context_len": 71}}}, "defeating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "He organized armies , crossed the boundary between Europe and Asia , and spent the twelve years of his career in a most triumphant military incursion into the very center and seat of Asiatic power , destroying the Asiatic armies , conquering the most splendid cities , defeating or taking captive the kings , and princes , and generals that opposed his progress", "context_len": 63}}}, "cavalry": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 24, "context": "She was accompanied by a very clean-looking officer on horseback , and he , in turn , was followed by a small escort of cavalry -- I did not take the trouble to count them , for my eyes were all for the lady ; and it was left to Harry Herndon to realize the fact that we were in something of a pickle should the officer take advantage of the position in which he found us", "context_len": 77}}}, "offenders": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "And then , last of all , but of all the offenders most insolent and lawless , comes -- -- ''", "context_len": 21}}}, "lawless": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Bill Mosely was naturally lazy ; any sort of work he considered beneath him , and he desired to avoid all possible trouble in the lawless and vagabond life which he had chosen", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "His life had been a lawless one , but he was not the thorough-going scoundrel that Bill Mosely was , and would have been glad if circumstances had favored a more creditable mode of life", "context_len": 35}}}, "hisses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Disappointed at not getting the crumbs , the swans slowly turned round and were sailing away again when they caught sight of Jumbo , and with angry hisses and long necks outstretched they bore down upon him as he swam about half-way between the island and the bank", "context_len": 48}}}, "barracks": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 24, "context": "The sentry 's tramp grew clear and then died out again , which meant that the sentry was back in the hallway of the barracks , and then the shadowy form stepped out into the moonlight and ran swiftly and silently across the area and sprang up the steps to the porch of the building ; and there he stood and waited again until once more the sentry was far away -- then stepped into the doorway and crept softly up the stairs", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "With which words he sprang down the stairs and , leaving the cadet officer to gaze at him in surprise , hurried up the street to barracks", "context_len": 27}}}, "icehouse": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "he is , he deliberately locked Bull Harris and Baby Edwards up in an icehouse , with the intention of making them absent at taps and compelling them to remain imprisoned all night", "context_len": 33}}}, "forcing": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 62, "context": "His hair was only beginning to turn grey , and was falling away a trifle from his temples , and he was very upright , and very thin , and had keen eyes , the keenest eyes I had ever looked at , small , grey and bright , and those eyes seemed to look through you , as though they were forcing a gimlet into the very secrets of your soul", "context_len": 72}}}, "hazed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "In that time most of the students had settled in their new rooms , the freshmen found their places , some were properly hazed , and that ordeal for others was postponed until a future date , much to the misery of the fledglings", "context_len": 44}}}, "unprovoked": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` The class desires to express , in the first place , its entire displeasure , both as a class and as individuals , at your unprovoked conduct toward two of its members", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` And did the two members tell you the attack was unprovoked ? ''", "context_len": 14}}}, "falsehood": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 12, "context": "Utter silence met this tissue of impudence , defiance , truth and falsehood , and he saw plainly enough that he was believed to have committed the theft of Percy 's money for theft itself , pure and simple , and that fear of detection only had induced him to make the effort at restoration", "context_len": 55}}}, "omit": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 54, "context": "I know that the knights , so far as bravery and devotion are concerned , will each and every one do his best , and will die at their posts before yielding a foot ; but while fighting like paladins they will think of naught else , and , however hardly pressed , will omit to send to me for reinforcements", "context_len": 61}}}, "gainsay": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 64, "context": "the Vizier , yea , indeed , he marvelled at the greatness of that wealth more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more when he saw the Sultan that he was satisfied with ( or accepted of ) the bride-gift and the dowry ; however , it was not possible to him that he should gainsay the truth and should say to the Sultan , 'He is not worthy ; ' only , he practised with a device upon the Sultan so he should not let him give his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour to Alaeddin , and this [ Footnote was ] that he said to him , etc , '' -- and also ( 2 ) the version", "context_len": 128}}}, "inasmuch": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 94, "context": "he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward , [ 660 ] I excuse you , for that ye are innocent", "context_len": 128}}}, "predominant": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "One was predominant , and seemed to be the most popular", "context_len": 11}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}}}, "convey": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 70, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}}}, "challenge": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` He has great gifts , '' said the lad , `` and I trust he is treated accordingly ; but I doubt it , '' and with that he turned away from the piano with a snap of thumb and finger that sounded for all the world like a challenge", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was that the headhunters must be present in large force , or they would not have dared thus to challenge the wrath of the most powerful native tribe of the district", "context_len": 32}}}, "appoint": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "I may add that the Admiral has -- in order that no knight greatly your senior should be placed under your command -- determined that he will appoint to it only young knights , who will , we are assured , gladly serve under one who has so distinguished himself , feeling certain that , under his command , they will have ample opportunities against the infidels to prove themselves worthy of the Order", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Conway read : `` I hereby appoint Edna Conway to be my proxy and to vote upon any question which may come up before this meeting", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 83, "context": "When the Vizier heard the Sultan 's words , his tongue was tied for despite and he was overcome with exceeding chagrin , forasmuch as the King had promised him that he would marry his daughter to his son ; so , after a little , he said to him , `` O King of the age , Thy Grace condescended to promise me [ 371 ] that the Lady Bedrulbudour should be my son 's ; wherefore it behoveth thine exalted highness appoint a delay of three months , [ 372 ] and God willing , my son 's present shall be greater than this", "context_len": 106}}}, "adjourned": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "This meeting stands adjourned", "context_len": 4}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "With a sigh of relief Lilian had adjourned with Peggy to the kitchen to help Nancy to wash up , when the back-door was softly pushed ajar", "context_len": 27}}}, "unfortunates": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Meanwhile the night had fallen so intensely dark that the completion of the task of feeding us unfortunates had to be accomplished by torchlight ; and we had not been very long left to ourselves before the faint flickering of distant lightning and the low muttering and grumbling of thunder warned us to expect a storm of more than ordinary violence", "context_len": 61}}}, "bony": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "Gerald knocked on the door , which after a slight delay , was opened by a tall , gaunt woman attired in a soiled calico dress which hung limply about her thin and bony figure", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 18, "context": "But she got no further , for with a snarl the old man reached out one long , bony arm and grabbed her by the shoulder , raising his stick threateningly , `` I 'll larn ye , ye little varmint , '' he began", "context_len": 45}}}, "straying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "In a chair by the window sat a still more curious figure , a lank , bony individual with ill-fitted , straying clothes and a long , sharp face", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Even those in the open fields were careful to keep together ; any man straying more than twenty yards from the rest of the party being liable to be shot by the numerous armed guards", "context_len": 35}}}, "labeled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Upon his big , bulging knees rested a leather-bound volume labeled `` Dana 's Geology , '' and opened at the Tertiary fossiliferous strata of the Hudson River Valley", "context_len": 29}}}, "fossiliferous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Upon his big , bulging knees rested a leather-bound volume labeled `` Dana 's Geology , '' and opened at the Tertiary fossiliferous strata of the Hudson River Valley", "context_len": 29}}}, "strata": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Upon his big , bulging knees rested a leather-bound volume labeled `` Dana 's Geology , '' and opened at the Tertiary fossiliferous strata of the Hudson River Valley", "context_len": 29}}}, "classify": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He was trying to classify a Cyatho phylloid coral which he had just had the luck to find", "context_len": 18}}}, "phylloid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He was trying to classify a Cyatho phylloid coral which he had just had the luck to find", "context_len": 18}}}, "coral": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "The Parson had left the library for a walk , his beloved Dana under his arm and the cyathophylloid coral in one of his pockets", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "And of all their wide possessions there were none so valued by them both , as the little coral heart set in pearls that she , as a girl , had given him , and he had so faithfully brought back to her", "context_len": 43}}}, "soles": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "The soles of his boots were worn to pieces , and so long a time did he wear them without a change that the uppers stuck firmly to his legs ; and , though the boots were soaked in hot water , the skin came away when they were taken off", "context_len": 51}}}, "spokesman": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "Ashati and Neram would have been nothing now but bones buried in the heart of the Moving Mountain if you had not come to their help , replied Ashati , who seemed to be the spokesman for the two", "context_len": 39}}}, "individuals": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 62, "context": "The party who had brought me to this remote spot were of an entirely different race from those among whom I now found myself , and the fact that we were making our way toward what was obviously the aristocratic part of the town , coupled with the expressive conversation carried on by the leader of my custodians with three or four individuals who had joined us , led me to surmise -- although of course I did not understand a word of what was said -- that I had been brought up the river as a peace- offering or something of that sort , which conclusion was again the reverse of reassuring", "context_len": 113}}}, "grossly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Then I desire to express , in the first place , my entire displeasure , both as a class and as an individual , at being thus grossly misrepresented", "context_len": 30}}}, "misrepresented": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Then I desire to express , in the first place , my entire displeasure , both as a class and as an individual , at being thus grossly misrepresented", "context_len": 30}}}, "contemptible": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 28, "context": "You think I have done something unworthy of your god-daughter , but do n't you really , really believe that you would have a much poorer , more contemptible , more worthless sort of god-daughter if she were now to break her bond to the man who saved her mother at considerable expense -- the man who was so good , so kind , so faithful ? Would you really counsel me to break my bond ? ''", "context_len": 78}}}, "chum": {"freq": 148, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 143, "word_index": 29, "context": "The day of the midnight visit , when the Darewell Chums , together with Bart 's sister Alice ( who wanted to be a trained nurse ) and her chum , Jennie Smith , were in a drug store getting soda and cream , Fenn had pulled from his pocket , together with his handkerchief , a small mud turtle", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "Here \u2019 s a brown and white Happy Family for you ! Natives and Europeans can hardly chum together ; yet it would be absurd to have three cooks for us", "context_len": 31}}}, "depositing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "'Gee up ! Let her go ! Hooray ! ' yelled Bobby , striking such a whack on Brindle 's heaving side that she settled the matter by suddenly lying down to roll , and depositing her encumbrances in the miry ditch", "context_len": 42}}}, "forsooth": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` I do know , and I shall , forsooth , be very happy to tell you about him", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` How ! you must be beside yourself to come to me -- me ! -- Rupert ! on such an errand ! Because , forsooth , I exchanged civilities with one I held an honourable enemy , you dare to expect my interest on behalf of a regicide ! I vow , sir , every man who even witnessed that most abominable and unnatural murder should swing , did it depend on me", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` This same morning -- and my friend Indian will substantiate my statement , for he was there -- a low , ignorant cadet corporal did enter into my room , for inspection , by Zeus , and after generally displaying his ill-manners , he turned to me and conveyed the extraordinary information that , according to rules , forsooth , I must be deprived of the dearest object of my affections , solace of my weary hours , my friend in time of need , my companion in sickness , which through all the trials of adversity has stuck to me closer than a brother , my only joy , my -- -- ''", "context_len": 115}}}, "proportions": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 85, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "proportion": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 66, "context": "I hear that there was a talk of freeing all , and that we , instead of embarking at first , should make for the other prisons , burst open the doors , and rescue the others ; but by the time we could do so the knights would be all in arms , and our enterprise would fail altogether , for as but a small proportion of us can obtain weapons , we could not hope to overcome them", "context_len": 80}}}, "designation": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "pugilistic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` As to his pugilistic abilities , I am by no means so accurately informed , but if my conjecture be of any value whatsoever , I should be inclined to infer , from the fact that our enemies , the representatives of tyranny and oppression , who are endeavoring to reduce us to submission , have selected him as their champion and representative in arms , that -- -- ''", "context_len": 71}}}, "infer": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` As to his pugilistic abilities , I am by no means so accurately informed , but if my conjecture be of any value whatsoever , I should be inclined to infer , from the fact that our enemies , the representatives of tyranny and oppression , who are endeavoring to reduce us to submission , have selected him as their champion and representative in arms , that -- -- ''", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The Creeks infer , from the parallel Creek form has\u00f3ti , `` sunshine , '' that Kas\u00ed'hta really meant `` light , '' or `` bright splendor of the sun ; '' anciently , this term was used for the sun himself , `` as the old people say", "context_len": 49}}}, "representatives": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "An approach to its reconstruction could be attempted by carefully comparing the lexicon and grammatic forms of the dialects presently spoken , and an individual acquainted with them all , or at least with their four representatives , might also compose a comparative grammar of these dialects as spoken at the present epoch of their development , which would reveal many points concerning the ancient or historic shape of the language once common to all these tribes", "context_len": 77}}}, "tyranny": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` As to his pugilistic abilities , I am by no means so accurately informed , but if my conjecture be of any value whatsoever , I should be inclined to infer , from the fact that our enemies , the representatives of tyranny and oppression , who are endeavoring to reduce us to submission , have selected him as their champion and representative in arms , that -- -- ''", "context_len": 71}}}, "oppression": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` As to his pugilistic abilities , I am by no means so accurately informed , but if my conjecture be of any value whatsoever , I should be inclined to infer , from the fact that our enemies , the representatives of tyranny and oppression , who are endeavoring to reduce us to submission , have selected him as their champion and representative in arms , that -- -- ''", "context_len": 71}}}, "reduce": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 80, "context": "he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward , [ 660 ] I excuse you , for that ye are innocent", "context_len": 128}}}, "student": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` You are no student of Randall College at all ! I 'll take you to Proctor Zane , and he 'll give you in charge of an officer ! Perhaps you are a thief , and have stolen that camera ! ''", "context_len": 43}}}, "fossil": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` And I wish , '' added the Boston student , `` that I could contrive to account for the presence of this Cyathodhylloid fossil in a sandstone of Tertiary origin", "context_len": 31}}}, "sandstone": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` And I wish , '' added the Boston student , `` that I could contrive to account for the presence of this Cyathodhylloid fossil in a sandstone of Tertiary origin", "context_len": 31}}}, "tattoo": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "And it usually happened that most of the unfortunates were already sound asleep by the time `` tattoo '' was sounded , that is , unless the unfortunates had been still more unfortunate , unfortunate enough to fall into the clutches of the merciless yearling", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Mark did not come up until `` tattoo '' sounded , and so Texas had plenty of time", "context_len": 18}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "And the three of them spent the rest of that evening , up to tattoo , discussing their revenge , thinking up a thousand pretexts upon which Cadet Mallory might be `` skinned", "context_len": 33}}}, "rebels": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 74, "context": "Thus , as the news of the destruction of Thebes , and the selling of so large a portion of the inhabitants into slavery , spread over the land , its effect was to turn over so great a part of the population to a feeling of admiration of Alexander 's character , and confidence in his extraordinary powers , as to leave only a small minority disposed to take sides with the punished rebels , or resent the destruction of the city", "context_len": 83}}}, "fealty": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "The council lasted from August 11th to 21st , and terminated in a treaty , by which the towns renewed their `` fealty '' to the king of Great Britain , and confirmed their cessions of territory , while Oglethorpe engaged that the British should not encroach upon their reserved lands , and that their traders should deal fairly and honestly with the Indians", "context_len": 64}}}, "classically": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Which , '' added the Parson 's solemn voice , `` might be somewhat more classically expressed by the sesquipedalian Hellenic vocable -- ahem ! -- Megalacephalomania", "context_len": 28}}}, "yea": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "The folk marvelled at Alaeddin 's bounty and at the excess of his munificence and were amazed when they saw that which graced him of beauty and goodliness and his courtliness and dignity ; yea , they extolled the perfection of the Compassionate One for this His noble creature and all of them great and small [ 462 ] called down blessings on him , albeit they knew him for the son of such an one the tailor ; yet none envied him , but all said , `` He is deserving", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "[ Footnote 436 : The whole of the foregoing passage is so confused that I think it well to add here ( l ) a literal translation , as I read it : `` So the Vizier , yea , indeed , he marvelled at the greatness of that wealth more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more when he saw the Sultan that he was satisfied with ( or accepted of ) the bride-gift and the dowry ; however , it was not possible to him that he should gainsay the truth and should say to the Sultan , 'He is not worthy ; ' only , he practised with a device upon the Sultan so he should", "context_len": 128}}}, "drilling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "These were days of work for the plebes at West Point -- days of drilling and practicing from sunrise to night , until mind and body were exhausted", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "Whether intentionally or not , the man had slipped back into his Staffordshire accent and dropped the strongest of his oaths , and Dick could not prevent a feeling of bitter amusement at seeing that this drunken ne'er-do-well , whom his uncle had persuaded to enlist in the hopes of drilling him into a decent life , had yielded to the influence of General Harrison 's character just as he himself had done", "context_len": 73}}}, "practicing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "Moreover , he fell to going everywhile [ 539 ] to the chase and the horse course and to practicing horsemanship and archery [ 540 ] before the Sultan , whilst the Lady Bedrulbudour redoubled in love of him , whenassoever she saw him disporting himself a horseback , and thought in herself that God had wrought exceeding graciously by her in that there had befallen her what befell with the Vizier 's son , so He might keep her for her true bridegroom Alaeddin", "context_len": 85}}}, "scant": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 84, "context": ", for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed , with feelings of envy , that many of my fellow-sufferers had already succumbed and become unconscious , if indeed they were not dead", "context_len": 128}}}, "tactical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "When `` taps '' came half an hour later , meaning lights out and all quiet , there was usually scant need for the round of the watchful `` tac , '' as the tactical officer is designated", "context_len": 38}}}, "sentry": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` If that fellow , '' continued Corporal Jasper , `` had any reason on earth for getting up at midnight , dodging sentry and running out of barracks , to stay till reveille , except to avoid fighting you that morning , now , by jingo ! I want to know what it is ! The class sent me to ask him , and he simply said he would n't tell , that 's all", "context_len": 76}}}, "tirelessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "And , this duty over , the officer made his way to his own home ; and after that there was nothing awake except the lonely sentry who marched tirelessly up and down the halls", "context_len": 35}}}, "area": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 43, "context": "The sentry 's tramp grew clear and then died out again , which meant that the sentry was back in the hallway of the barracks , and then the shadowy form stepped out into the moonlight and ran swiftly and silently across the area and sprang up the steps to the porch of the building ; and there he stood and waited again until once more the sentry was far away -- then stepped into the doorway and crept softly up the stairs", "context_len": 83}}}, "guardhouse": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The only trouble was that the tall , sedate-looking officer of the day , in his spotless uniform of gray and white and gold with a dazzling red sash thrown in , strode out of the guardhouse just then ; a moment later came the cry , `` New cadets turn out ! '' and Indian drew a breath of relief at being delivered from his uncomfortable situation", "context_len": 68}}}, "hallway": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "The sentry 's tramp grew clear and then died out again , which meant that the sentry was back in the hallway of the barracks , and then the shadowy form stepped out into the moonlight and ran swiftly and silently across the area and sprang up the steps to the porch of the building ; and there he stood and waited again until once more the sentry was far away -- then stepped into the doorway and crept softly up the stairs", "context_len": 83}}}, "occupant": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 29, "context": "Suddenly I felt my finger in contact with a small roll of paper that had been carefully pushed under the leather , and then I remembered that the last occupant of the chair was the short-haired man -- the man who had the general appearance of a prize-fighter", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "The moment before Harry Paul seemed to have been exerting his full strength to force the boat through the water , but an accession of strength came to him , and with a few fierce thrusts he drove her bows into the edge of the current , which gave it so quick a snatch that it was whirled round , and its occupant nearly lost his footing ; but he was too practised a boatman for that", "context_len": 77}}}, "ssh": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ssh ! ''", "context_len": 3}}}, "thumping": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 105, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "And then man and animal plunged into the night and disappeared , though the fast diminishing thumping of hoofs was heard for some seconds later", "context_len": 25}}}, "incredulity": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 68, "context": "Whit Monday was a holiday at school , however , and Peggy and Bobby , having the day at home , took a sudden fit of industry , and started to weed the shrubbery with the noble intention of having it raked over and tidied by teatime , being put somewhat on their mettle by Father 's remarks on the subject of sustained labour , and his laughing incredulity when they assured him he would find it all neatly finished when he returned from Warford that evening", "context_len": 87}}}, "recklessly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 54, "context": "I wonder why Baker was so excited when he heard that name ? I wonder why Lem Wacker bid it up ? Is he aware of the mystery surrounding Baker ? Has this package got something to do with it ? Wacker looked as though he had struck a prosperous streak , and bragged recklessly about the lot of money he could get", "context_len": 63}}}, "unhurt": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "He and Fred had found two Mitsubishi bombers with engines apparently unhurt and wings not too badly damaged , though the tail assemblies , fuselages and undercarriages were in sad shape", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Now , sink me ! \u2019 tis a fair exchange : give us the five and take your enterprising \u2019 ( how he sneered when he said this ) \u2019 king \u2019 s men unhurt , or else , for every man of ours who dances at the end of a rope , five of yours shall dangle from our yardarm", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "They swerved in their course , swerved sufficiently to enable woman and child to pass by unhurt , but they knocked Meg down and the carriage-wheels went over her", "context_len": 29}}}, "mused": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 18, "context": "Now , if I should have a flat sail on my right and left like a kite , mused Alden , giving rein to his whimsical fancy , this speed would lift us clear and we should skim through the air like a swallow", "context_len": 44}}}, "expulsion": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 48, "context": "Mary opened the box , but dropped it with a howl of dismay , for in place of the tempting sweetmeats she had expected lay a writhing mass of fat green caterpillars , newly picked from the gooseberry-bushes , a subtle revenge on Bobby 's part for his expulsion from the sanctum", "context_len": 52}}}, "winsome": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "Though she had not again tried him for a bedfellow , he was still the companion of her walks , and the most winsome playmate on wet days , and Peggy firmly believed that he understood every word she said to him", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 119, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}}}, "bureau": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 29, "context": "Seizing the first opportunity of slipping away from his schoolmates , he rushed upstairs to the dormitory with the design of throwing the note under Seabrooke 's bed or bureau , where it might be supposed to have fallen ; but seeing the trunk standing there ready packed , the impulse had taken him to put it in that , and without reflecting -- perhaps hardly caring -- that this would place Seabrooke in a still more embarrassing position , he thrust the note within , as Charlie Henderson saw , and fled from the room", "context_len": 96}}}, "tiptoe": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 45, "context": "Thursday morning , the grand morning , has arrived , -- nay , it is nearly eleven o \u2019 clock , and the children of six schools , their teachers and their mothers , and perhaps scores of women besides , will be on the tiptoe of expectation , -- and our kahar has never returned ! ! ! We don \u2019 t know whether Mrs", "context_len": 66}}}, "sally": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I think , '' said Bradley , not smiling at this sally of Ben 's , `` that our Chinese friend has fallen in with some rough fellows who have done him harm", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 55, "context": "I shall tell his aunt so the next time I see her , and I do n't care who hears me ! ' she added , catching sight of a grinning face on the veranda , for Archie had stolen out to see the fun , and overflowed in such gurgles of delight at this sally that Lilian trembled for the result", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Let us settle our plans , then , before we sally out from here ; we could not find a safer place for talking", "context_len": 24}}}, "wane": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "After Texas had chased two yearlings into the woods , and Mark had stretched out Bull -- that was Bull 's third time that afternoon -- the ardor of the eight began to wane", "context_len": 34}}}, "lighten": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "But my dear nephew Bertric , Saint Bertric we must assuredly call him , oh how it will lighten the grief of his parents and sister to know how gloriously he died for Christ ! One could envy him his crown", "context_len": 41}}}, "streaks": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 19, "context": "They could not sit down in a chair without smutting it all over ; and their faces were in streaks of white and black , where the perspiration ran down and washed away the smut", "context_len": 35}}}, "fighter": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Git up thar , you prize fighter you ! ''", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 14, "context": "Its proprietor was one Silas Green , once a switchman , later a prize fighter , always a hard drinker , and latterly so crippled with rheumatism and liquor that he was just able to get about", "context_len": 37}}}, "encasing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The `` Parson '' had not , for Texas found him engaged in encasing his long , bony legs in a pair of trousers that would have held a dozen such", "context_len": 31}}}, "accoutered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Are you accoutered for the combat ? '' he whispered , in a sepulchral tone , sleepily brushing his long black hair from his eyes", "context_len": 26}}}, "detour": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "For my part , flushed with my success , I slowly edged away , intending to make a detour , regain the road , and then retrace my steps towards Petersfield ; for I knew what sort of road I had already passed , but was in ignorance of the distance to which this wild valley extended", "context_len": 57}}}, "inclosure": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "At the rear was a covered inclosure and from the rough , stone chimney , built at one end on the outside of the main building , rose a spiral of smoke proof that the cabin had occupants", "context_len": 38}}}, "flunked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` It 's ten to one he 's flunked , '' put in Bull Harris , grinning joyfully", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` It 's evident he 's flunked", "context_len": 7}}}, "speculating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "The three stepped out of the group with alacrity , and followed Fischer over to a seat nearby , while the rest of the gang stood and stared in surprise , speculating as to what this could possibly mean", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "At supper nobody talked of anything else , and everybody in the room was eying Mark and his stalwart opponent and speculating as to what the chances would be", "context_len": 29}}}, "earthworks": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 47, "context": "It was pitiful to see the blackened ruins of churches and palace , which had not been rebuilt since the Danish raid of 1010 , but the commoner dwellings were rising with rapidity from their ashes , or had already risen , for the shelter of the earthworks and other fortifications was not to be despised , and prevented the place from being utterly abandoned", "context_len": 65}}}, "humiliation": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 120, "context": ", love , should we fear the act of departing ? How many , many , pass Jordan , as it were , dry-shod ? Remember how peacefully sweet Fanny sank to rest , -- dearest Mother , -- how my Letitia \u2019 s face was lighted up with a smile , -- how our Bible-woman at Batala sang aloud a happy hymn within a few hours of her going ! To me it seems such a simple thing for the -- I had almost said imprisoned soul , to leave its cottage of clay , -- for the bird , as soon as fledged , to spread its wings ! We are winged creatures , and it seems a humiliation to be creeping on earth so long", "context_len": 128}}}, "gall": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Oh , he comes of a fine family ! And he has the gall to come here among decent fellows to try to attend the academy here ! Wait till my father hears of this ! He \u2019 ll have something to say about it", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "At present , Gervaise , your vocation lies wholly in the way of fighting , but it may be that the time will come when you may have other aims and ambitions , and when the vows of the Order will gall you", "context_len": 43}}}, "wormwood": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The position of humiliation in which Mark 's conduct had placed him was gall and wormwood to him , and he was fast working himself into a temper of the Texas style", "context_len": 32}}}, "suiting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "And first , I 'll see what kind of a day we are going to have ; and suiting the action to the word , he stepped off a little distance to take an observation", "context_len": 35}}}, "coyote": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` You coyote ! '' he roared", "context_len": 7}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "Some heroes , West Point 's bravest , lie buried there , and Indian was dejectedly wondering if those same heroes would ever have stuck through plebe days in barracks if they had had a drill master like that `` red-headed coyote , '' Chick Spencer", "context_len": 46}}}, "pranced": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "The horses pranced , swung a little to one side and stopped short , and I heard some one say , 'So , Barney , so Pet ! ' I did n't know what happened next but the first thing I knew I was lying on the lounge in the sitting-room , my mother bending over me , and holding a bottle of salts to my nose , 'Oh , dear , oh , dear , ' my mother was crying , 'another minute and the child might have been killed", "context_len": 91}}}, "uncomplimentary": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "But he did n't , at least , not then ; and Texas pranced up to his room and flung himself into a chair , muttering uncomplimentary remarks about Mark and West Point and everything in it", "context_len": 37}}}, "aflame": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "How do you do , dear ? said the lady , extending her hand , which Marie-Celeste , her cheeks aflame with the unexpected abruptness of Albert 's introduction , took in hers , in a pretty deferential sort of way , as though fully conscious of the dignity of her surroundings", "context_len": 52}}}, "prancing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "It was very inspiring and exciting to see the three coaches , that were to convey some royal guests to the depot , leave the courtyard one after the other , the horses in each case prancing in wildest fashion and perfectly free , apparently , with the exception of the one mounted leader , to do any outlandish thing that they chose", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The carriage and prancing horses were on the woman , who was too tipsy either to see them or to save herself", "context_len": 22}}}, "sneaks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` I jest tell you , sah , they ai n't no room in my room fo ' a coward that sneaks off when he 's got a fight", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Serves you right to be tripped up by a woman ! You ain \u2019 t men , you \u2019 re sneaks !", "context_len": 22}}}, "interrupting": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 34, "context": "'There 's the butcher 's boy at the back-door , miss , ' observed Nancy one morning , a few days after the wedding , putting her head into the Rose Parlour , and interrupting the 'Bridal March ' from 'Lohengrin ' which Lilian was trying over on the piano", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` There 's where you are wrong , Ben , '' cried his mother , eagerly , and guilty of interrupting , `` it is Mr", "context_len": 26}}}, "stinging": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "I \u2019 ll mighty soon show you , replied Alden , who , without an instant \u2019 s hesitation , launched his right fist at the face of the other ; but he neatly dodged the blow and delivered so stinging a one on the cheek of Alden that he reeled for several paces", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "Nonesuch had raced with Frankie to the very edge of this little rivulet , and then stood still for a moment as if considering what he would do next , when a hand , holding a long , thorny switch , was suddenly put forth from the clump of bushes , and Nonesuch received a stinging blow across his haunches", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "If she could only have thought of a suitable retort , she would have said something stinging ; but usually our smartest remarks occur to us when the occasion has long passed by , and perhaps it is all the better , for we are saved from bitter words , which , once spoken , are not easily forgotten by the hearers , however keenly we may regret them ourselves", "context_len": 70}}}, "physically": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 21, "context": "There was sternness as he expressed his sentiments regarding the person with a bullying inclination who took pleasure in abusing those physically weaker than himself ; and , although Sam Rollins \u2019 name was not mentioned , every one knew at whom those open remarks were directed", "context_len": 47}}}, "angrier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "At least , it had no such effect in this instance , for the more I was silent , the louder and faster she talked , and , apparently , the angrier she became", "context_len": 34}}}, "aiming": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 29, "context": "Then for an hour she lay quiet , while the knights , divesting themselves of their mantles and armour , worked one of the guns on the poop , aiming at a floating barrel moored for the purpose a mile out at sea", "context_len": 43}}}, "prod": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Mark moved his head to one side and let the blow pass , stopping the rush with a firm prod in the other 's chest ; then he stepped aside and waited for another rush", "context_len": 35}}}, "roommate": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "There is no need to tell the rest in detail , but simply to say that while Mark and his roommate gazed on in blank despair , their jubilant enemy made out a list of at least a dozen charges , which he knew would aggregate to at least half of the demerit maximum , and for every one of which there was some slight basis of justification", "context_len": 68}}}, "capped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "At least half of the raiders had been incapacitated , and among the wounded had been Nascanora himself , and all the unwounded survivors were at that moment rushing pell-mell through the jungle , frantically eager to put as many miles as possible between themselves and the cabin , where the white man \u2019 s magic had been capped by the calling of the jungle beasts to help", "context_len": 68}}}, "gritted": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 28, "context": "Mark noticed that the cadets were looking at him at breakfast ; and he noticed that the members of his own class were rather distant , but he gritted his teeth and made up his mind to face it out", "context_len": 40}}}, "lunge": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "As a fact there was but little choice in the matter , it was fight or die with the two , for at the same instant Gus Murray , wild with rage , had leaped forward and made a savage lunge at the dude", "context_len": 44}}}, "planting": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 45, "context": "They decided to have quite a garden on the platform next spring , and to grow seeds in pots , and persuade nasturtiums and canary-creepers to climb up the walls , and they made a beginning by hauling up a box of soil , and planting some ivy , which they hoped in time would cover the whole roof", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "As the days grew longer and lighter , the garden also claimed attention , and the children were busy digging , raking , and planting , for this year there were to be special classes in the flower-show for exhibitors under fifteen , and both Peggy and Bobby had secret hopes of a prize", "context_len": 54}}}, "invincible": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "All this pomp and pageantry , with inefficient weapons , and inefficient men to wield them , will be of no avail against their invincible courage and energy ; and the best disposition that you can make of all your gold , and silver , and other treasures , is to send it away and procure good soldiers with it , if indeed gold and silver will procure them", "context_len": 69}}}, "pummeled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The farmer , after a five-minute gallant stand , was finally knocked down -- from behind -- and once down he was being fast pummeled into nothingness", "context_len": 27}}}, "paralyzed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "Edna , paralyzed with fright , looked on with affrighted eyes , but presently found voice to quaver out , `` Please do n't hurt her ! Oh , please do n't ! ''", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He was so paralyzed with horror that he could not move", "context_len": 11}}}, "needless": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 35, "context": "What schoolboy of fourteen would not like to visit India \u2019 ? Young Henry seized upon the idea ; and the said aunt , under the impression that she was kindly relieving his father of needless school expenses , actually shipped the lad off as middy in a merchant vessel bound for India , not waiting to write and ask his father \u2019 s permission", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Needless to say , our further stay in the Low Countries was hurried as quickly as possible , and next day a stout little brig conveyed us from Rotterdam to London", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Jerry put himself to needless trouble by running away ere his uncle \u2019 s will was read ; for in that will , which was drawn up barely two months before Asher Rand \u2019 s death , and which was found in Mr", "context_len": 43}}}, "interviewed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "She must be interviewed and told of the change which had taken place in the fortunes of her pupil", "context_len": 19}}}, "satisfactorily": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 36, "context": "We are going to have a fine day , Albert asserted , resuming his seat on the steps , and with the authority of one who knows ; and the matter of the weather being thus satisfactorily disposed of , Marie-Celeste made so bold as to introduce another subject ; and as it chanced to meet with Albert 's approval , they chatted merrily together for ever so long", "context_len": 69}}}, "branded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` We can not allow it , '' said Jasper , emphatically , `` unless you consent to explain your action and can succeed in doing it satisfactorily , which you will pardon me for saying I doubt very much , you stand before the academy branded as a coward", "context_len": 50}}}, "philosophize": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Cadet Powers was released from the hospital `` cured '' that evening after supper , and he limped upstairs to his room , and sat down to think about himself , and to philosophize upon the vanities of life and the follies of ambition", "context_len": 44}}}, "vanities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Cadet Powers was released from the hospital `` cured '' that evening after supper , and he limped upstairs to his room , and sat down to think about himself , and to philosophize upon the vanities of life and the follies of ambition", "context_len": 44}}}, "follies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Here , then , my boy , on this spot where thou wert once cradled , renounce all these Christian follies and superstitions , and thou shalt go back with me to the camp of King Sweyn , where thou shalt be received as the descendant of warrior kings , and shalt forget that thou , the falcon , wert ever the inmate of the dovecote", "context_len": 67}}}, "wrenching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Oh ! '' cried Texas , springing up from his chair and wrenching a dilapidated shoulder", "context_len": 17}}}, "dilapidated": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a dilapidated door at the best , and would long since have yielded to the beast \u2019 s attack had it not been for some barriers placed against it on the other side", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "He had the same squat , sturdy figure , the coarse black hair dangling about his bare shoulders , and growing low upon his forehead , the naked chest , the frowsy hunting shirt of deerskin , with leggings and dilapidated moccasins", "context_len": 42}}}, "helpful": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 17, "context": "You say you wish to learn to dress yourself , that you may be a useful and helpful little girl ; but you make more trouble than you save when you tear the buttons and strings from your clothes , or knot and fray your shoe-laces", "context_len": 46}}}, "disregard": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Harold and Marie-Celeste were at first very much disgusted at his utter disregard of the object of their visit , but disgust gradually gave way to amusement , and the tale of the chivalrous old knights was even suspended for awhile , that they might watch the little fellow 's peculiar methods of letting nothing escape him", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 76, "context": "`` I grieve to say , '' he continued , `` that there is but one upon whom our suspicions can now fall with any shadow of probability -- one who is now absent , for I thought it well not to summon him to this council ; and before naming him , I must recall to you , Elfwyn , and to you , Herstan , the solemn oath we have all three taken to disregard all appeals of natural affection , and to ascertain the truth , God being our helper", "context_len": 93}}}, "convince": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "And all this while the wilful little queen was trying to convince herself that it was ready for Donald 's sake , when the truth was that the long walks with Donald , when Harold -- who was making up some necessary back work at college -- was not at her service , were what she was determined not to give up , and the reading aloud in the evenings , when Donald was such a delightful listener ; and , in fact , the hundred and one little amusing things that Donald was continually doing , and that made the days go by in such happy , merry fashion", "context_len": 110}}}, "hones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Hones ' now , Mark , '' and as Mark looked into the other 's pleading gray eyes he saw that Texas meant it", "context_len": 24}}}, "debated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The officers now debated as to the best course to pursue -- whether to follow the buccaneer , which , undermanned as we were , was hazardous and reckless , or to return with all haste to Port Royal , report our loss , and join with the rest of the fleet in the capture of the insolent pirates", "context_len": 59}}}, "widowed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Payne had a colored youth who had been turned over to him when an infant by his widowed mother , she having consented to become the wife of a big , lazy darky , with no love for other folks \u2019 children", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Hamilton , who had long been in failing health , passed away ; and Charlotte seems , either in anticipation of the event , or directly after , to have had some floating ideas of making a home with her widowed favourite sister", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "We should have expected that Sysigambis , the bereaved and widowed mother of Darius , would have been among those who would have exulted most highly at the conqueror 's death ; but history tells us that , instead of this , she mourned over it with a protracted and inconsolable grief", "context_len": 52}}}, "flirt": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "Deprived thus of his balancing poles , a quick flirt of the pony to one side would have flung him into the water , but \u2019 Ceph , if he was aware of it ( and it would seem he ought to have been ) , did not seize the chance", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Armstrong , too , became strangely mysterious , wreathing her face in smiles now and then , and now and then looking strangely sour and disappointed , and Marion Armstrong began to flirt with a young German who had arrived", "context_len": 40}}}, "reveille": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` The friendships of this evil generation will avail us little when the trumpet of the Lord of Hosts doth sound the reveille , and those poor bones yonder live once more , ay , and that dead hand beckon us on to victory", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` If that fellow , '' continued Corporal Jasper , `` had any reason on earth for getting up at midnight , dodging sentry and running out of barracks , to stay till reveille , except to avoid fighting you that morning , now , by jingo ! I want to know what it is ! The class sent me to ask him , and he simply said he would n't tell , that 's all", "context_len": 76}}}, "yo": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Time to have me call you names and to lick me blue , for the same which I have jest thanked yo , '' added Texas , his eyes suspiciously moist", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "What you all laughing at ? What \u2019 s de matter wid you , Tony Burke ? If yo \u2019 doan \u2019 shet up straight off , I \u2019 ll frow you down so hard you \u2019 ll make a bulge on toder side de yarth", "context_len": 46}}}, "hip": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 36, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "You know dat las ' time we went at um ? Well , suh , I wuz shootin ' at a man right at me , an ' he knock my han ' down des ez I pull de trigger , an ' de ball cotch him right 'twix de hip an ' de knee", "context_len": 55}}}, "brim": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 27, "context": "There was a very large cup , called the bowl of Hercules , which he now called for , and , after having filled it to the brim , he drank it off to the health of one of the company present , a Macedonian named Proteas", "context_len": 47}}}, "alleged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` In my excursions into the various fields of knowledge I have never yet had occasion to investigate the alleged discoveries of phrenological experimentalists , and yet -- -- ''", "context_len": 30}}}, "discoveries": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "An hour later , these two were tramping over the place , hand in hand , making all sorts of discoveries , leaving Willis deep in a book and the older people chatting cozily before the open fire", "context_len": 38}}}, "phrenological": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` In my excursions into the various fields of knowledge I have never yet had occasion to investigate the alleged discoveries of phrenological experimentalists , and yet -- -- ''", "context_len": 30}}}, "experimentalists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` In my excursions into the various fields of knowledge I have never yet had occasion to investigate the alleged discoveries of phrenological experimentalists , and yet -- -- ''", "context_len": 30}}}, "perception": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "Charlie lost no time putting into execution Captain Rhines \u2019 s directions in respect to the pig , having first enjoined upon them the greatest secrecy , not even permitting them to tell Ben and Sally of his plots and suspicions , lest Joe , who was very quick of perception , should divine what was in store for him", "context_len": 60}}}, "delineated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` And yet I had , I think , sufficient perception of character as delineated by the outlines of physiognomy to recognize at once the fact that the person to whom we refer is in no way a coward", "context_len": 39}}}, "physiognomy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` And yet I had , I think , sufficient perception of character as delineated by the outlines of physiognomy to recognize at once the fact that the person to whom we refer is in no way a coward", "context_len": 39}}}, "refer": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 51, "context": "[ Footnote 480 : The description of the famous upper hall with the four-and-twenty windows is one of the most contused and incoherent parts of the Nights and well-nigh defies the efforts of the translator to define the exact nature of the building described by the various and contradictory passages which refer to it", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Please don \u2019 t refer to that , Mr", "context_len": 9}}}, "originate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` And how did this encounter originate ? '' inquired the Parson", "context_len": 12}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Another tale is , that they did not originate on this continent ; that when they arrived from their country they landed at the `` Jagged Rock , '' tch\u00e1to tcha \u00e0 a ` l\u00e1ko , and brought the metallic plates with them , which they preserve to the present day with anxious care", "context_len": 54}}}, "unaccountable": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "They were a tangible reality , however , and no delusion of the senses , and his ready mind took in the fact that someone had in an unaccountable manner rescued them from the burning express shed , and mysteriously restored them to the proper representative of the express company in the nature of a vast surprise", "context_len": 57}}}, "instinctive": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 38, "context": "They made an interesting group as they clustered round the girls from Sunnyside and bade them welcome , Maud taking the lead , and finally attaching herself to Rosamund ; for she guessed , in some sort of instinctive way , that Rosamund had more character than the other school-girls , and would be more likely to interest her", "context_len": 59}}}, "intuition": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "Bishop , appealed to these simple savages ; a sentimental ditty , such as `` The Anchor 's weighed '' or `` Tom Bowling , '' would hold them breathless and entranced ; `` Rule , Britannia ! '' or `` Should He upbraid '' set them quivering with excitement ; and they seemed to know by intuition that `` The Sailor 's Hornpipe '' was written to be danced to , and they danced to it accordingly a wild , furious , mad fandango in which the extraordinary nature of the gambols of the performers was only equalled by the ecstasy of their enjoyment", "context_len": 105}}}, "adequate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` And yet somehow I had an instinctive intuition , so to speak , that there was an adequate reason", "context_len": 20}}}, "trustworthy": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` For by a most lucky chance I have to-day obtained what I believe to be trustworthy information to the effect that she was sighted four days ago , bound for the Gaboon river -- or perhaps it would be more correct to say that she was sighted steering east , and identified by the master of a brig who knows her perfectly well , and who has since arrived here , and that there is authentic information to the effect that she is this time bound for the Gaboon", "context_len": 90}}}, "demonstrated": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "And one is apt to find that such impressions are trustworthy , as , indeed , was most obviously demonstrated and consistently maintained by the German philosopher , Immanuel Kant", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 10, "context": "Stoker laughed at Walker , but the little chap soon demonstrated that he was on the field to do his handsomest as long as he lasted ; and , despite the greater weight of the opposing end , he was able to keep the fellow busy", "context_len": 46}}}, "consistently": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 101, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}}}, "expostulated": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I should think you 'd be ashamed to bring such truck into a decent college , '' expostulated Langridge", "context_len": 20}}}, "slyly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "And it 's a pity about me , is n't it , Marie-Celeste , said Dorothy slyly , for the author might feel that as I am your friend he ought to put mein somewhere , and that would make it a little more about girls , you see , and probably spoil the story", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` Did any of the boys and girls ever try the experiment of speaking to him pleasantly ? I am quite sure the boys do their best to annoy him in any way they can contrive , and even some of the girls tease him slyly and call him names , I am told", "context_len": 54}}}, "gird": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He might gird up his loins and take his sword in his hand and sally forth , to vindicate his honor and the honor of his sworn friends and allies", "context_len": 30}}}, "loins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He might gird up his loins and take his sword in his hand and sally forth , to vindicate his honor and the honor of his sworn friends and allies", "context_len": 30}}}, "vindicate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "The crowd were getting impatient , and readily seconded the words of Jim Brown when he followed up Bill Mosely 's speech by a suggestion that they proceed at once to vindicate justice by a summary execution", "context_len": 37}}}, "slothfulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "And instead what does he do ? In slothfulness and cowardice he sits and suffers beneath the rod of tyranny and oppression ! ''", "context_len": 24}}}, "suffers": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I have been with many people suffering as your mother suffers , but I never before came across any one quite so gentle , so resigned , so happy , so peaceful , -- it is the peace of God", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "O dear ! O dear ! Why did we trust that sust [ 60 ] kahar , -- or eat up all the bread ? O how shall the bari Bibi ever be fed ? I must go and try to cheer up poor Florrie , who suffers from her head , in addition to being in this fix", "context_len": 59}}}, "unprecedented": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "That Harold should deliberately assert himself in this way was such an unprecedented performance that he knew not what to say", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "This he has consented to do , feeling , as we all feel , that although such an appointment is unprecedented for a young knight , yet in the present case such an exception may well be made", "context_len": 38}}}, "antiquity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "In the eastern and western branch ( also among the Naktche people ) the children belong to the gens of the mother , a custom which differs from that of the Yuchi and dates from high antiquity", "context_len": 37}}}, "speakers": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "It was an afternoon late in June , and the two speakers were discussing some ice cream at `` the Dutchwoman 's '' and waiting for the call to quarters before dress parade", "context_len": 33}}}, "perplexity": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 36, "context": "Then he proceeded to look closely till at last he was certified that there was neither trace nor sign left of the palace and knew not what was come of it ; whereupon he redoubled in perplexity and smote hand upon hand and his tears ran down upon his beard , for that he knew not what had befallen his daughter", "context_len": 61}}}, "verge": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 50, "context": "How I hated the house ; how I hated the dreary , and yet beautifully-furnished drawing-room ; how the heat oppressed me , and seemed to take away the remainder of my strength ! I wondered if it were true , that I was only two-and-twenty , just on the verge of womanhood", "context_len": 53}}}, "grizzly": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 5, "context": "She had a quantity of grizzly grey hair , which was turned back from her expansive forehead ; a cap of almost every colour in the rainbow bedizened her head , she wore diamond pendants in her ears , and had a flashing diamond brooch fastening the front of her dress", "context_len": 51}}}, "spotless": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "The only trouble was that the tall , sedate-looking officer of the day , in his spotless uniform of gray and white and gold with a dazzling red sash thrown in , strode out of the guardhouse just then ; a moment later came the cry , `` New cadets turn out ! '' and Indian drew a breath of relief at being delivered from his uncomfortable situation", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 15, "context": "His tunic was finely embroidered in colours around the neck , and was below of spotless white , secured by a belt richly gilded , whereon was a sheath for the dagger or knife , which was used for all occasions , whether in battle or in meal time , the haft being inlaid with precious stones", "context_len": 57}}}, "drilled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "With some show of hesitation the man surrendered the thing , and upon inspection I found it to be a reed of about a foot in length , with a mouthpiece shaped something like that of a whistle , and with four small holes drilled in the length of the tube , whereby an expert performer might produce seven distinct tones ; but the tones were not consecutive , and the instrument was altogether a very poor and inefficient affair", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was the evening dress parade of perhaps the best drilled body of troops in the country , and West Point was out in holiday attire to see it", "context_len": 29}}}, "pleases": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 14, "context": "One of them dropped on one knee and said , 'Madame , if God pleases , you shall be our Queen and lady ; ' and then she made a little courtesy like this , and answered without a word from anybody , 'Sir , if it please God and my lord and father , I shall be most happy , for I am told the Queen of England is a very great lady", "context_len": 74}}}, "concerts": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 32, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "recitations": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 47, "context": "There 'll be no time for whims and fancies , or even for lessons ; for there is to be an enormous tea-fight , as I call it , for the young folk of the parish in the schoolhouse this afternoon , and games afterwards , and recitations ; and if you , Rosamund , can recite as well as Lucy has described , why , you will be invaluable", "context_len": 70}}}, "continuous": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 26, "context": "It is not to be supposed that I was allowed to spin my yarn without interruption ; on the contrary , I was bombarded with a continuous fire of questions for the elucidation of points that I had failed to make quite clear ; and when I had finished the captain was pleased to express himself as perfectly satisfied with all that I had done , and that the loss of the Dolphin was due to causes entirely beyond my control", "context_len": 81}}}, "hazing": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 53, "context": "They were sitting -- half a dozen of them -- beneath the shade trees of Trophy Point at the northern end of the parade ground ; they were waiting for dinner , and the afternoon , which , being Saturday , was a holiday and for which they had planned some particular delicious hazing adventure", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 42, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was more hazing that night , in which Phil and Tom had a share , but Sid would not come out , saying he had to study", "context_len": 28}}}, "abolished": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "The latter stated his case at some length , and reiterated his demand that football be abolished", "context_len": 17}}}, "stringent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "As you are aware , it is a stringent rule , which even in so exceptional a case we should not be justified in breaking , that a knight must reside in the Island for five years previous to being promoted to a commandery", "context_len": 44}}}, "measures": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 56, "context": "I sez he ai n't in , but I asks Miss Lilian , and she tells 'em `` Yes , '' and there they all is , pokin ' about amongst the nettles , and grubbin ' up stones , and stickin ' bits of mortar in their pockets , and dodgin ' about with yard measures , for all the world like a set of lunatics", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He did not mean to measure our forgiveness any more than He measures His own", "context_len": 15}}}, "degrading": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "There was , moreover , nothing degrading in it , and while the overseers had plied their whips freely on the backs of many of his companions , he had not only escaped , but had , he felt , succeeded in pleasing his masters", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "The man is John Howard , who travelled tens of thousands of miles , and spent many years in visiting gaols all over England and the Continent , and in endeavouring to render prison life less degrading and brutalising", "context_len": 39}}}, "menial": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Still more he rejoiced at being appointed his instructor , as it relieved him from all menial work which , although preferable to that to which the bulk of the slaves were condemned , yet galled his spirit infinitely", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Our host soon appeared , cringing and bowing like the menial that he was", "context_len": 14}}}, "penalty": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 42, "context": "What can I do ? -- my father will not let me be baptized , and I dare not oppose his will ; yet I sometimes think I ought to chance all , and to die , if death should be the penalty", "context_len": 43}}}, "dismissal": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "A cadet is forbidden in any way to lay hands upon the plebe ; he is forbidden to give any degrading command or exact any menial service ; and the penalty for breaking these rules is dismissal", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "Only one-half of the boys were implicated in that affair ; they had already been punished by the restrictions which had been placed upon them , and were to be further disgraced by the public reprimand which he intended to give them on the dismissal of the school ; and these culprits were probably dreading this or some other severe punishment which would be meted out to them by the report of their misconduct which would be sent home", "context_len": 79}}}, "deviling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The results are supposed to be a complete stopping of `` deviling '' in all its forms", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Indian , it seemed , was always thought of whenever there was any deviling to be done", "context_len": 17}}}, "yanks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The actual result has been that when a yearling wants to `` lay hands upon the plebe '' he does it on the sly -- perhaps `` yanks '' him , as one peculiar form of nocturnal torture is termed", "context_len": 40}}}, "nocturnal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Though I was then happily unaware of the fact , the period of the great annual festival , or Customs , was approaching , and the joy of the populace began to find vent in nocturnal concerts inordinately prolonged , the musical instruments consisting of tom-toms , each beaten by two , three , or four performers -- according to the size of the tom- tom -- with a monotony of cadence that soon became positively maddening , further aggravated by the discordant squealing of a number of flageolet- like instruments made of stout reeds", "context_len": 95}}}, "wily": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "Every now and again the wily enemy would stop firing , and give a tug to the two ropes which bound his unfortunate captives , and they would be jerked a foot or two nearer the fort", "context_len": 37}}}, "corps": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Indian , '' as he was now called by the whole corps , was a rara avis among plebes , being an innocent , gullible person who believed implicitly everything that was told him , and could be scared to death by a word", "context_len": 45}}}, "gullible": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Indian , '' as he was now called by the whole corps , was a rara avis among plebes , being an innocent , gullible person who believed implicitly everything that was told him , and could be scared to death by a word", "context_len": 45}}}, "implicitly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 61, "context": "I have often wondered why maidens should so carelessly bestow their favours upon every knight who begged for them , and have said to myself that when my time came I would grant it but once , and only then to one whom I deemed worthy of it in all ways -- one in whose loyalty and honour I could trust implicitly , and who would regard it as something sacred , deeming it an honour to wear it , as being the pledge of my trust and esteem", "context_len": 89}}}, "ascertain": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 33, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "After considering for a time , Gervaise thought that it would be best for him to follow this other man when he left , and ascertain who he was , rather than to keep a watch on the movements of the Greek , who , as likely as not , would now return to the merchant 's", "context_len": 57}}}, "geological": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "Mark and Texas , it chanced , had gone out for a walk ; `` Parson '' Stanard had , wandered over to the library building to `` ascertain the extent of their geological literature , '' and to get some information , if possible , about a most interesting question which was just then troubling him", "context_len": 57}}}, "dreading": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "Only one-half of the boys were implicated in that affair ; they had already been punished by the restrictions which had been placed upon them , and were to be further disgraced by the public reprimand which he intended to give them on the dismissal of the school ; and these culprits were probably dreading this or some other severe punishment which would be meted out to them by the report of their misconduct which would be sent home", "context_len": 79}}}, "visitation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "And poor Joe Smith was all alone in his room , dreading some visitation of evil", "context_len": 16}}}, "indian": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 0, "context": "Indian got up second ; he gazed about him , his rage still burning ; he gave one snort , shook his head clear of the soil as an angry bull might ; and then made another savage rush at Baby", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Indian was hauled up , terrified , to explain ; he described his hazing , but steadfastly refused names -- which was good West Point etiquette -- he vowed he knew nothing about the fire -- which was the truth -- also West Point etiquette", "context_len": 45}}}, "trigonometry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Do n't know 'em yet ? Bah , what will you do when you come to trigonometry with a hundred and fourteen formulas to learn every night ? Have you learned to stand on your head yet ? What ! Did n't I tell you to do it ? Who taught you to stand on your feet , anyhow ? Why do n't you answer me , eh ? Let 's see you get up on that mantelpiece", "context_len": 78}}}, "formulas": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Do n't know 'em yet ? Bah , what will you do when you come to trigonometry with a hundred and fourteen formulas to learn every night ? Have you learned to stand on your head yet ? What ! Did n't I tell you to do it ? Who taught you to stand on your feet , anyhow ? Why do n't you answer me , eh ? Let 's see you get up on that mantelpiece", "context_len": 78}}}, "flap": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 62, "context": "Hens do n't crow ? What do you know about hens , anyway ? Were you ever a hen ? Well , why were n't you ? Were you ever a goose , then ? No ? Well , you certainly look like it ! Why do n't you crow when we tell you ? What kind of crowing is that -- flap your arms , there", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` A little before ten o'clock God brought me safe to Wednesbury , having lost only one flap of my waistcoat , and a little skin from one of my hands", "context_len": 31}}}, "crowing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 58, "context": "Hens do n't crow ? What do you know about hens , anyway ? Were you ever a hen ? Well , why were n't you ? Were you ever a goose , then ? No ? Well , you certainly look like it ! Why do n't you crow when we tell you ? What kind of crowing is that -- flap your arms , there", "context_len": 67}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Miss Baby looked for a moment as if she had half a mind to resent this liberty , but thought better of it , and presently was crowing and smiling in the kind old face , which looked so pleasantly at her", "context_len": 42}}}, "amazingly": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 17, "context": "All hesitation and doubt seemed to have left him , and at everything he attempted he was amazingly sure and so swift that not a few of the boys who had fancied him heavy and awkward gasped with astonishment and confessed to one another that they had sized him up wrong", "context_len": 51}}}, "presumptuous": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 36, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}}}, "interspersed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 37, "context": "The slope was steadily upward for a fourth of a mile , when it reached a nearly level plateau , and wound in and out among rocks , stunted pines , gnarled cedars , and ravines , interspersed with valleys and comparatively smooth stretches , with now and then a mountain torrent across which the travelers made their way with difficulty", "context_len": 61}}}, "ejaculation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Soon a solitary figure emerged , and searched anxiously amongst the fallen and blackened walls , uttering one anxious ejaculation , `` My son ! I seek my son ! '' and Alfgar knew his father", "context_len": 36}}}, "gravitation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "He was commanded to fall down , and he fell ; he was commanded to fall up , and he protested that the law of gravitation -- -- '' Bah ! why do n't you get the law repealed ? '' He wiped off a smile from his terrified face and threw it under the bed", "context_len": 56}}}, "repealed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "He was commanded to fall down , and he fell ; he was commanded to fall up , and he protested that the law of gravitation -- -- '' Bah ! why do n't you get the law repealed ? '' He wiped off a smile from his terrified face and threw it under the bed", "context_len": 56}}}, "lung": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "He strove his very best to go to sleep , amid a variety of suggestions , such as which eyes to shut and which lung to breathe through", "context_len": 28}}}, "expelled": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 53, "context": "Mallory , '' said he , `` just as sure as I 'm alive this thing shall stop ! Keep up heart now , and we 'll make a fight for it ! While there 's life there 's hope , they say -- and , by Heaven , you sha n't be expelled ! ''", "context_len": 56}}}, "preposterous": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "In the first place , I am well treated here , and my position is not an unpleasant one ; secondly , the sum required for ransom is altogether preposterous ; thirdly , I am not without hopes that I may some day find other means of freeing myself without so great a sacrifice ; and lastly , that I have a reason which I can not mention , why , at present , I would not quit Tripoli , even were I free tomorrow", "context_len": 85}}}, "direful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "And almost before the poor Indian could realize what they were doing , or going to do , the whole crowd arose gravely and marched in silence out of the room , bent upon their direful mission of having the Army Board expel Indian because he had never heard of Joseph Smith , the Mormon prophet", "context_len": 56}}}, "expel": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "And almost before the poor Indian could realize what they were doing , or going to do , the whole crowd arose gravely and marched in silence out of the room , bent upon their direful mission of having the Army Board expel Indian because he had never heard of Joseph Smith , the Mormon prophet", "context_len": 56}}}, "lark": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 73, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}}}, "marauding": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "They entered the building , causing dire fear to several anxious-looking plebes who were peering out of the windows and wondering if this particular marauding party was bound in their direction", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "There was no garden , only a low wall on which the milk-cans and most of the family crockery seemed to be taking an airing ; but a patch of potatoes and a scanty half-acre of oats lay beyond the little homestead , roughly railed off to keep out the marauding sheep", "context_len": 52}}}, "medals": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was one of the empty rooms that they entered , however , and there they proceeded to costume one of their number , putting on a huge red sash , some medals , a few shoulder straps borrowed for the occasion , and , last of all , a false mustache", "context_len": 52}}}, "exhibited": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 11, "context": "Ever since the adventure with the pirates , the knight had exhibited an almost fatherly interest in him ; had encouraged him in his studies , ridden with him on such occasions as he had permitted himself a short holiday , and had , whenever they were together , related to him stories of war , sieges , battles , and escapes , from which he thought the young knight might gain lessons for his future guidance", "context_len": 77}}}, "stupidity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "He of the scarred face was quickly captured , being well-nigh winded with the effects of the stone I had thrown ; but the third was a more determined quarry , though , in his stupidity , instead of climbing upwards ( being far lighter than the soldiers , encumbered as they were with breast-pieces and riding-boots ) , he must needs make for the road , where he raced off at breakneck speed", "context_len": 74}}}, "allegations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` In order to consider these very grave allegations , '' continued the other , `` a special meeting of the Army Board was first convened , with the following result : ''", "context_len": 33}}}, "convened": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "One day they were going into the town in this way , at a time when it happened that there was an assembly convened , which was in a state of great perplexity on account of the civil dissensions and contests which prevailed in the country", "context_len": 46}}}, "pompously": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "An unusual equipage was turning in from the Trinidad road -- an equipage on which leather and varnish shone , and harness brasses flashed , while the dust rolled pompously after it in a freakish fantasy of postilions and outriders", "context_len": 40}}}, "ascertained": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 13, "context": "The hands pointed to one o'clock , when it was , as Tom ascertained by looking at his watch , barely nine ; but a little thing like that did not seem to give the clock any concern", "context_len": 38}}}, "conditional": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Cadet Joseph Smith , of Indianapolis , Indiana , it has just been ascertained , was admitted to the duties of conditional cadet through an error of the examining board", "context_len": 31}}}, "ahem": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` If I remember correctly , we were occupied when last interrupted , by -- ahem ! a rather facetious observation upon the subject of our solitary lunar satellite and quadruped of the genus Bos -- occupied I say in considering the position which the metropolis of Boston has obtained -- -- ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "superiors": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}}}, "bogus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The bogus officer desired time to change his uniform , as he knew it would be risky to cross the parade in his borrowed clothing", "context_len": 25}}}, "unanimous": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 18, "context": "Here , in the name of the Republic , Battista Fragoso announced to him that , by the unanimous decision of the council , he had been elected a noble of Genoa ; an honour , he added , on only one or two previous occasions in the history of the Republic bestowed upon any but of princely rank , but which he had nobly earned by the great service he had rendered to the State", "context_len": 76}}}, "unutterably": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Indeed , I confess the latter version seems to me so curiously and perversely and unutterably bad that I can not conceive how Cazotte can have perpetrated it and can only regard it as a bad joke on his part", "context_len": 41}}}, "excite": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 32, "context": "Since the memorable time when Captain Cook sailed into Botany Bay in 1769 and saw the naked native Australian poising erect to hurl his peculiar weapon , the boomerang has continued to excite the curiosity and amazement of the civilized world ; and truly the finding of such a scientific weapon in the hands of this so-called lowest order of mankind is an astonishing fact , to be simply accepted as another oddity of this odd , topsy-turvy corner of the world", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "In this breaker lay the ball ; when the wave curled over and broke , it would come towards the shore and excite hope ; then the recoil would carry it back again : thus it tantalized them", "context_len": 38}}}, "bedecked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The lord high chief quartermaster presently slipped in , once more bedecked with medals and mustache", "context_len": 16}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Gorry ! ain \u2019 t it queer , Al ? asked his companion , after the gaily bedecked rider had dashed by on his way to the ferry", "context_len": 28}}}, "businesslike": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "The Parson , however , did not act as if curiosity had brought him ; with a businesslike air and a solemn visage he strode down the company street , and , heedless of the cadets who had gathered at the tent doors to see him , halted in front of one before which he saw `` Billy '' Williams standing", "context_len": 61}}}, "surgeons": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The surgeons first very carefully cut off the wooden shaft of the arrow , and then , enlarging the wound by incisions , they drew out the barbed point", "context_len": 29}}}, "cutlets": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "She now deliberately turned her back upon Irene , and continued to eat her cutlets without taking the least notice of her", "context_len": 22}}}, "cutlet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "I had never greater difficulty in keeping my composure than I had during that awful meal , but I did eat a cutlet when it was put on my plate , and I did manage to talk to my neighbour , a new boarder who had come up from the country , and did not know her way about anywhere", "context_len": 60}}}, "estimated": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 76, "context": "As Ama , taking her bow and arrows , tripped lightly away toward the forest , I proceeded to shin up the tree , and presently , after some labour , found myself among its topmost branches , which towered high above those of all the other trees in the neighbourhood , and -- it being by this time broad daylight -- obtained a magnificent view of the surrounding country , extending for , as I estimated , some forty miles toward the south and east , while toward the north and west the view was shut off by high hills , through which the river wound its way", "context_len": 109}}}, "jockey": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "Next us is a sorrel pony , his jockey is red and green , next him a white pony , colors black and gold", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "I 'm jockey number one and my colors are blue and white", "context_len": 12}}}, "slaughtered": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` O yes , my father is keeping high festival ; a number of his countrymen are visiting him and holding revel ; this morning they drove in a number of oxen , I know not whence , and slaughtered two on the spot , and they have broached several barrels of mead ; they will keep the feast all day , and before night my father will not be in a state to miss me ; I always absent myself if I can on such occasions", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}}}, "hogs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "But he carried all that stock and land to the store ( except one acre and a half ) in a two-quart jug , and died drunk among the hogs", "context_len": 30}}}, "audible": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 68, "context": "Then Alfgar saw what was the marvellous power of Christianity , and beheld a heroism utterly beyond the fierce excitement which nerved his countrymen for their scenes of carnage and blood ; not one of his pagan friends could have suffered as calmly , as patiently -- it seemed easier for the sufferer to bear than for Alfgar to look on ; once or twice the latter gave audible vent to his emotions , but the look which Bertric turned upon him spoke volumes , and he restrained himself lest he should add to the pain of the victim", "context_len": 99}}}, "trigonometrical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Joseph Smith must be immediately `` boosted '' up and balanced upon the cadet 's outstretched hand , there to be shaken and otherwise tested , while the man below made audible calculations by means of trigonometrical formulas as to what was his actual weight", "context_len": 45}}}, "weights": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "I would also submit that it were well to pick men with some strength for this labour , for many are so weak that they are well nigh useless in the moving of heavy weights ; and lastly , I would humbly submit to you that if men are to do good work they must be fed", "context_len": 57}}}, "perspire": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "And in the course of that you must manage to perspire fifteen pounds of weight ( enough to make you go through the ceiling )", "context_len": 25}}}, "lag": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Two minutes there , two minutes ! Come now , hurry up ! Do n't begin to lag there ! Why do n't you stop that panting ? There goes the first drop of perspiration", "context_len": 36}}}, "presiding": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "But the boys enjoyed it , and , his good nature restored by presiding at the function , Sandy told how he and his friends had been much alarmed at finding Bart and his companions missing , and how they had searched in vain for them", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` I suppose it 's all settled , old fellow ! You are lucky with the prospect of such a home and such a presiding -- shall I call her the goddess of the hearth ? That room is a perfect gem , and you three people are to the manner born", "context_len": 52}}}, "reduction": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "There was an abundance of fallen boughs and twigs in the adjacent jungle to enable me to build a funeral pyre ; and I should have the melancholy satisfaction of actually watching the reduction of the body to impalpable ashes", "context_len": 40}}}, "resuscitate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "It would be so much more fun to fool the learned Parson ! And in a minute or two half the party , including the lord high chief quartermaster , was on its way back to barracks to hunt up the new victim , while the rest stayed to resuscitate Indian and to write out a list of questions for the `` mental examination", "context_len": 64}}}, "secluded": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 54, "context": "The young instructor , presenting , by the way , a ludicrous contrast in his shining uniform of gray and white and gold , with his three or four nervous and variously costumed pupils , takes the bayonet of his gun for a drill stick and marches `` his '' squad over into a secluded corner of the area and thus begins the above-mentioned instructions :", "context_len": 66}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Yet I was deeply puzzled to understand what motive could have brought a war party so far , and why they had passed so many flourishing homes to come to poor secluded Aescendune", "context_len": 33}}}, "unison": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Spotless and glittering in their uniforms were the cadets , and they went through all manner of difficult evolutions in perfect unison , marching with lines as straight and even as the eye could wish", "context_len": 35}}}, "ordnance": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 15, "context": "The only chance they had of bringing me down was by training a piece of ordnance on the top ; but either they did not possess a cannon capable of being elevated to that height , or else they feared that the damage done would be greater than the success of getting rid of me", "context_len": 55}}}, "comments": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "Again the procession started with the same comments as before ; this time , however , the door was not locked , and the party entered , sought out another room where stood several solemn cadets at attention , respectfully saluting the superintendent of ordnance , ex-lord high", "context_len": 48}}}, "respectfully": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 39, "context": "Again the procession started with the same comments as before ; this time , however , the door was not locked , and the party entered , sought out another room where stood several solemn cadets at attention , respectfully saluting the superintendent of ordnance , ex-lord high", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He bears himself , as a young knight should do , respectfully towards his seniors , and is ever ready to do a service to any one", "context_len": 27}}}, "corals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "When are cyathophylloid corals to be found in fossiliferous sandstone of Tertiary origin ? ''", "context_len": 15}}}, "forthwith": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 16, "context": "She soon found to her sorrow , however , that she was mistaken : for Donald forthwith launched forth into such a detailed account of Lord Nelson 's history , from his voyage as a boy to the North Pole , to his last great , glorious battle , that the patience of that young lady , who was rather more eager at all times to impart information than to receive it , was sorely tried", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "When he reached it and entered , the eunuchs went in and acquainted the Sultan with his presence ; which [ 501 ] when he heard , he arose forthwith and coming to meet Alaeddin , embraced him and kissing him , as he were his son , seated him on his right hand", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 91, "context": "I must cure the king , or it would be so much the worse for me ! And how was I to cure him ? My knowledge of disease was of the slightest and most amateurish kind , and , for aught that I could tell to the contrary , might not even be sufficient to enable me to diagnose the case correctly , much less to treat it successfully ! However , there was no use in meeting trouble half-way ; the only possible course was to obey the summons forthwith , and do my best , leaving the result in the hands of Providence", "context_len": 106}}}, "discourse": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "And the prince had much discourse with Major Harrison , for so your uncle was then , and when he bade him farewell he gave him a gallant compliment , saying he never received such satisfaction in such unhappiness , and that if ever it were in his power he would repay it", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "In the evening we discourse with Queen Elizabeth , Leicester , Paul Buys , and Olden Barneveldt , etc", "context_len": 19}}}, "researches": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 45, "context": "Traditions on early migrations , which have originated in the people to which they refer and bear the imprint of genuineness , not that of a late fabrication by conjurers or mixed-bloods , usually contain indications of importance which are confirmed by arch\u00e6ologic and linguistic researches", "context_len": 46}}}, "undiscovered": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "Certainly he was not the one by whom Charlie would choose to be faced , and seeing that he was not perceived , he hesitated whether he should speak and reveal his presence , or pretend to be still asleep and trust to silence and good fortune to remain undiscovered", "context_len": 50}}}, "reread": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The Parson knitted his brows in perplexity and reread the question", "context_len": 11}}}, "redden": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "It seemed above and below -- to redden the skies , the frozen treetops with their glittering snow wreaths , and the smooth surface beneath alike", "context_len": 26}}}, "indignation": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 17, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Penelly did not speak , and Zekle remained silent , but he was evidently moved to indignation at what had been said , for he kept lifting his big oar and chopping it down in the water as if he were trying to take off the master 's head", "context_len": 49}}}, "warfare": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 46, "context": "It is not the mob of Genoa only who regard the services that you have rendered as extraordinary , but it is the opinion of the councillors and authorities of the Republic , and of those who , like myself , have borne our share in warfare , that not only is the service great , but that it is due to the singular ability with which you , in command of only a single galley , have wholly destroyed or captured the fleet that threatened our commerce", "context_len": 88}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "In early times its tribe was the most important among the Lower Creeks , adverse to warfare , a `` peace or white town , '' and called by the people T\u00e1lua ` l\u00e1ko , Great Town", "context_len": 37}}}, "missionary": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 41, "context": "The soil in the neighbourhood of the station was light and needed plenty of water , but the stream which supplied them with the necessary moisture for their vegetables was diverted from its channel by the natives , so that the missionary 's garden was nearly burnt up by the hot sun", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 20, "context": "'Just imagine , Paul , ' the letter went on , ' I , of all possible people , a missionary 's wife ! But the fact of the matter is , my precious saint , your splendid , consecrated life made me tingle with shame to my finger tips when I thought of my aimless existence , and when I remembered how you took up your cross and followed your Master to Sleepy Hollow , there seemed to be no reason why I should not follow Him to Africa", "context_len": 90}}}, "missionaries": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 20, "context": "The idea that the Creeks knew anything of the devil of the Christian religion is a pure invention of the missionaries ; being christianized , they call him now : \u00edsti f\u00fatchig\u014d `` the man acting perversely , '' taso l\u00e4 ya , or : \u00edsti nikl\u00e9-idsha ats\u016b li `` the old person-burner '' ( \u00e1ni nikl\u00e9-idsh\u00e4s I burn somebody , something ) ; the Yuchi call him `` the swinging man , '' just as they call a ghost `` a hunting man", "context_len": 84}}}, "shrinks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "If a plebe 's swelled head shrinks at the rate of three inches a day , how many months will it be before it fits his brains ? ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "ameliorated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` It is obvious , very obvious , that my condition will in no way be ameliorated by creating a suspicion in trying to make a forceful exit through that locked door", "context_len": 32}}}, "forceful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` It is obvious , very obvious , that my condition will in no way be ameliorated by creating a suspicion in trying to make a forceful exit through that locked door", "context_len": 32}}}, "efficacious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "You say that they take place six days hence ; it will be strange indeed if our united ingenuity is not equal to the task of devising some simple yet efficacious plan", "context_len": 32}}}, "propitiously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` It would appear , '' he said , gravely , as he took in the situation , `` that the 'high-thundering , Olympian Zeus ' smiles propitiously upon my plan", "context_len": 31}}}, "shank": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "And with this classic remark he stuck one long shank out of the window , followed it with another just as long , and stood upon the cornice over the door of the building , which chanced to be in reach", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Although he used only the weakest pressure , \u2019 Ceph winced when the friendly fingers glided over the slim shank , as if the touch was painful", "context_len": 27}}}, "cornice": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "And with this classic remark he stuck one long shank out of the window , followed it with another just as long , and stood upon the cornice over the door of the building , which chanced to be in reach", "context_len": 41}}}, "sagely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 58, "context": "From there he half slid , half tumbled to the ground , arose , arranged his necktie carefully , gazed about him solemnly to hear if any one had seen him , and finally set out at a brisk pace for barracks , taking great , long strides , swinging his great , long arms , and talking sagely to himself in the meanwhile", "context_len": 64}}}, "alliance": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 1, "context": "An alliance concluded by the Alibamu with the Mobilians did not last long , for in 1708 they arrived with a host of Cheroki , Abika and Kataba Indians , in the vicinity of the French fort on Mobile Bay , where Naniabas , Tohomes and Mobilians had settled , but were foiled in their attack upon the Mobilians through the watchfulness of the tribe and of the French colonists", "context_len": 70}}}, "justified": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 46, "context": "Nancy , too , proved somewhat of a trial , for though a hard-working and most kind-hearted girl , she had not been gifted by Providence with either brains or a memory , and was capable of making the most astonishing mistakes , which she invariably justified by declaring she was sure she had done it so 'in Miss Vaughan 's time", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "He had entertained this wish before , but his father would not consent , and , indeed as there was some danger of losing one \u2019 s way in case of a sudden fog , his apprehensions were justified", "context_len": 39}}}, "hypothesis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` When the other two members of our -- ahem ! -- alliance are made aware of the extraordinary condition of affairs , '' he muttered , `` I think that I am justified in my hypothesis when I say there will be some excitement", "context_len": 45}}}, "penalties": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 82, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}}}, "sedately": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "The two were listening to the music of the band at the Saturday afternoon hop in the Academy Building , and also watching several cadets paying penalties by marching sedately back and forth in the area", "context_len": 36}}}, "methodical": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "She was very resolute ; very persevering ; very affectionate ; reserved , yet demonstrative ; untidy , yet methodical ; exceedingly anxious for the happiness of all around ; apt often to think people better than they really were ; generous to a fault ; unselfishly ready at all times to put her own wishes aside ; vehement and impulsive , yet never in a hurry or flurry ; unyielding , yet tender ; severe , yet frisky", "context_len": 79}}}, "embellishing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was agreeable , and so the Parson told his story , embellishing it with many flourishes , classical allusions and geological metaphors", "context_len": 23}}}, "metaphors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It was agreeable , and so the Parson told his story , embellishing it with many flourishes , classical allusions and geological metaphors", "context_len": 23}}}, "rebuke": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahiba must know every lane , Climb ladder-like stairs , without fearing a sprain ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must speak very plain , Must rebuke and encourage , must teach and explain ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must grasp well the rein ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must not look for gain , Though doctoring sick folk , like Jenner or Quain", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "It is many moons since the eyes of men have rested upon that city , returned the Indian sternly , seeming by his manner to rebuke the boy \u2019 s enthusiasm", "context_len": 31}}}, "sheepishly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` It is best to deliberate , to think over our plan of attack , '' returned the Parson , calmly , and with a mild rebuke in his tone , which reminded Texas of his promise never to get excited again , made him sit down sheepishly", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I was a-'pretendin ' , ' miss , '' she answered a little sheepishly ; `` I was tryin ' to see it like you do", "context_len": 27}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Polulu had bared his teeth when he first saw it , and had sheepishly abandoned his hostile attitude when Bomba had emerged and laughed at him", "context_len": 26}}}, "ratiocinating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "And then there was silence , deep and impressive , while everybody got his `` ratiocinating apparatus , '' as the Parson called it , to work", "context_len": 27}}}, "chemicals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Look here , Parson , '' said he , `` what 's the name of all those chemicals of yours that you hid up the chimney for fear the cadet officers 'd make you give 'em up ? ''", "context_len": 40}}}, "unpronounceable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Harold 's principal achievement lay in The Walrus and the Carpenter , and Marie-Celeste 's in the recitation of Jabberwocky from Through the Looking-Glass ; for not only was she able to slip its almost unpronounceable words quite easily from her tongue , but she remembered the explanation of them given by Humpty Dumpty , when Alice appeals to him a little later on in the story , and he modestly informs her that he can explain all the poems that ever were invented , and a good many beside that have n't been invented just yet", "context_len": 97}}}, "gee": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Never mind why ! But I 've got something in my pocket that 'll -- gee whiz ! Hurry up ! ''", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Oh , gee , but this is going to be a fight ! '' was the verdict of every one of them", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Gee ! when I saw it was full of water , was n't I scared for a minute ? Then I said to myself , 'Edna 's not such a fool as to stay and get drowned", "context_len": 37}}}, "conversing": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` Come on board , sir , '' remarked I , touching my cap as I passed in through the gangway of the Eros and found myself face to face with Captain Perry and the master , who were walking the quarter-deck side by side and conversing earnestly , while the first lieutenant , from the break of the poop , was carrying on the work of the ship", "context_len": 69}}}, "dissolves": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Just wait till enough of that dissolves , '' said Mark", "context_len": 12}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "There is a flight of iron steps in the center , and at the foot of these steps , three times every day , the battalion breaks ranks and dissolves into a mob of actively bounding figures", "context_len": 37}}}, "predicament": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 39, "context": "There was the question about Hannah , also : how the money was to be returned to her , in the uncertainty as to how much she knew , and how she had acquired any knowledge of Percy 's predicament ; for that she knew something of it Lena was convinced ; and yet the child was equally sure that that letter had never been out of her own keeping", "context_len": 70}}}, "timed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "organized": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 1, "context": "He organized armies , crossed the boundary between Europe and Asia , and spent the twelve years of his career in a most triumphant military incursion into the very center and seat of Asiatic power , destroying the Asiatic armies , conquering the most splendid cities , defeating or taking captive the kings , and princes , and generals that opposed his progress", "context_len": 63}}}, "brigade": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 54, "context": "March , 1853 , saw Sir Colin Campbell in England ; but though he had passed his sixtieth year , most of which had been spent in his country 's service , his rest was not of long duration , as in 1854 he went out to the Crimea in command of the Highland brigade , consisting of the 42nd , 79th , and 93rd regiments", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He must have come here , knowing that this dug-out was the Hun brigade head-quarters , and changed into a Boche rig-out", "context_len": 22}}}, "infantry": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 70, "context": "So , once more , by the matter of a few hours , Konrad von Feldoffer escaped a well-merited death ; while , through ignorance of the terrific preparations made for the blowing up of Messines Ridge , he had unwittingly done the Allies a good turn ; for instead of withdrawing the troops the Hun commander had concentrated a thousand on the mined ridge in order to repel an infantry attack that threatened only in the minds of the German staff", "context_len": 82}}}, "imminent": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 70, "context": "Aunt Helen fondly imagined herself to be a great disciplinarian , but her own lively youth was still such a recent remembrance that her eyes were wont to twinkle and the corners of her mouth to twitch in the middle of her severest scoldings , and the children always had a feeling that so long as they did not do anything rude or wrong , or run into any very imminent danger , their escapades were secretly condoned by their aunt , who admired pluck and spirit , however much she might feel it incumbent upon her to lecture them", "context_len": 100}}}, "crevice": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 66, "context": "What a noisy , merry , frolicksome stream this was , gurgling and splashing , rushing and tumbling in its rocky bed ; now leaping gracefully in a miniature waterfall over some narrow ledge , now rippling and singing about the roots of the trees and over the pebbles that lay in its course , now flashing in the sunlight , and now hiding in a crevice of the rocks as if it were playing at Bopeep", "context_len": 77}}}, "ax": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Prompt and quick to act , some heroic young cadet leaped up the steps and burst in the door with an ax , though it was not locked and needed only a turn of the knob to open it", "context_len": 39}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "My gracious ! I should say not ! Why , Ben he \u2019 s gittin \u2019 to be \u2019 most like one of my fambly , though sometimes it \u2019 s hard work makin \u2019 him come down to eat with us when I ax him", "context_len": 46}}}, "craven": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He had fought his way through the craven crowd , his coat tails flying and his long elbows working", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Ye are now in the pit , whence there is no escape , '' went on Sandy , `` but , if ye are true knights , and no craven cowards ye will come to no harm", "context_len": 38}}}, "imperiled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The crowd waited in breathless suspense -- one minute -- two -- and still the imperiled lad stood at the window and the hero did not appear", "context_len": 27}}}, "ladders": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 67, "context": "As soon as darkness came on , a large body of slaves were marched down from the town , and , under the direction of the knights , laboured all night at the mound , removing great quantities of the fallen stones and rubbish in a line halfway up it , and piling them above so as to form a scarp across the mound that would need ladders to ascend", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The Good Wolf looked serious for a second or so and then began to walk up as calmly as if he had used rope ladders all his life", "context_len": 28}}}, "diligent": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` `` O my lord the king , '' answered Alaeddin , `` I would fain build the Lady Bedrulbudour a palace , besorting her rank and station , and it may not be that I should go in to her without this ; but , God willing , the building shall , by the diligent endeavour of thy slave and by Thy Grace 's auspice , [ 472 ] be right speedily despatched", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "What I mean is that we can 'be diligent and studious ' as the old copy-books used to have it , speak well of his school , and not carry tales home that will make our families think we are martyrs and that he is an ogre , or someone to be feared constantly", "context_len": 54}}}, "investigation": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 29, "context": "It would be useless to try the highways , she knew , so accordingly her search must be in the by-ways , and she made a little tour of investigation round all the back streets between the inn and the Grammar School , but without success , and she was just thinking she must have missed him , and had better return to the inn-yard , when a fortunate chance prompted her to turn up a retired avenue which lay between the two main roads", "context_len": 85}}}, "steadfastly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "Of course they were all exceedingly anxious to hear the story of what had befallen me since the Dolphin and the Eros had parted company ; but I steadfastly refused to tell them anything beyond the bare fact that the Dolphin had gone down with all hands , explaining that the skipper had invited me to dine with him that day , and that they would learn all particulars then , as I gathered that it was his intention to invite them all to meet me", "context_len": 86}}}, "etiquette": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "[ Footnote 116 : So that they might hang down and hide his feet and hands , it being a point of Arab etiquette for an inferior scrupulously to avoid showing either of these members in presenting himself ( especially for the first time ) before his superior", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Baker ; most of all I 'd like to see Marilla , but it would n't be etiquette to call", "context_len": 20}}}, "bluff": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 24, "context": "To be sure , one has need to overlook , in quite too literal fashion , the warehouses that front the water below the bluff , and here and there an unsightly elevator , but why let the eye rest on these , with the dancing blue water beneath you , and the Jersey hills beyond , and beyond that again , like as not , a glorious sunset", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "There was that in his face , however , that showed plainly enough that he had begun already , though he did not in the least suspect it ; an earnest , thoughtful look that even bluff old Mr", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "That Taensa landing , at the foot of a bluff nine hundred feet high ( 150 toises ) , was about 32 5 Lat", "context_len": 24}}}, "scarecrow": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "It is well , Claudia , he said , laughing , that you did not see me at the time I was rescued , for I was such a scarecrow that you would never have been able to regard me with due and proper respect afterwards", "context_len": 46}}}, "defy": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 2, "context": "Harris to defy credulity was that Ted should have fallen into the hands of the Hartleys , for in what other little cottage in all England could such a transformation have been wrought ? Where else could he have been brought into such close touch with all the old home interests as he had been there , first through Chris and afterward through Donald and Marie-Celeste , and where else could he have come to see so clearly that he had been wilfully trampling upon all that is truest and best in life ? Fritz , said Mrs", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "A mad desire to go right away from the Leaves , to dash on to the moors , and defy Rosamund and Miss Frost , had just for a moment taken possession of her", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He seemed to have met his match in the young express agent , and dared not defy him", "context_len": 18}}}, "cadetship": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Of course that got him dead stuck on himself , and then he goes and wins a cadetship here and thinks he can run the earth", "context_len": 26}}}, "vehement": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "She was very resolute ; very persevering ; very affectionate ; reserved , yet demonstrative ; untidy , yet methodical ; exceedingly anxious for the happiness of all around ; apt often to think people better than they really were ; generous to a fault ; unselfishly ready at all times to put her own wishes aside ; vehement and impulsive , yet never in a hurry or flurry ; unyielding , yet tender ; severe , yet frisky", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "He left his room , but such vehement sounds of sweeping and scrubbing sounded from the kitchen that , when Richard reached the foot of the stair , he held discretion the better part of valour , and strolled out of the door into the bright morning air", "context_len": 48}}}, "battalion": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 1, "context": "A battalion picture-theatre , billiard rooms , voluntary swimming parades , boxing , and a variety of other indoor and outdoor games contributed to the men 's enjoyment ; and , although discipline was well enforced , there was a total absence of irritating petty restrictions that form a constant source of annoyance to the men of the New Armies of the Motherland", "context_len": 63}}}, "campus": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 5, "context": "As they were crossing the campus , which was thronged with players , visitors and some of the Boxer Hall team and its supporters , Wallops , the messenger , came along with a telegram in his hand", "context_len": 38}}}, "attire": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 20, "context": "The first was a lean , spare man , with gray side-whiskers and a slight stoop , whose rather sporting attire accorded ill with his sharp , keen face ; while the second , the one on the better horse , was stout and ruddy of countenance , a man who looked as though he would be more at home in a bar-parlour than in a drawing-room , and who held himself with a complacent air , as one who is satisfied both with the world in general and himself in particular", "context_len": 92}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The prettiness of her dress on that particular occasion was no doubt accentuated by the fact that in general Charlotte did not attire herself becomingly ; and also by the fact of another young lady being present as a second star of the morning", "context_len": 44}}}, "evolutions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Spotless and glittering in their uniforms were the cadets , and they went through all manner of difficult evolutions in perfect unison , marching with lines as straight and even as the eye could wish", "context_len": 35}}}, "parades": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "A battalion picture-theatre , billiard rooms , voluntary swimming parades , boxing , and a variety of other indoor and outdoor games contributed to the men 's enjoyment ; and , although discipline was well enforced , there was a total absence of irritating petty restrictions that form a constant source of annoyance to the men of the New Armies of the Motherland", "context_len": 63}}}, "drawled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "Yes , rather , drawled the other with cool effrontery ; and knowing he had scotched his man , the doctor turned on his heel , and the two men started off in the direction of the Nuneham station , neither sadder nor wiser , it is to be feared , for the lesson of the night 's experience", "context_len": 59}}}, "yank": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Yes , I will ! Them ole cadets git after me , now , by jingo , I 'll go out there an ' yank some of 'em out that parade an ' lick them all t'once", "context_len": 38}}}, "commandant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Only , if you do n't want to get me into a regular row with the camp commandant , take care to slip back before morning roll-call", "context_len": 27}}}, "review": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "I think it a good plan at the end of a year to review the whole , to try and find out what especial lesson has been set one to learn in it", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 69, "context": "He knew what it was to be without peace ; for having got into debt when he was first in the army , and knowing the distress it caused his family at home , his mind was so troubled that he wrote to his mother : `` Oh , what agony I have endured ! What sleepless nights I have passed since the perusal of that letter ! The review of my past life , especially the retrospect of the last two years , has at last quite startled me , and at the same time disgusted me", "context_len": 98}}}, "rigamarole": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "tomcat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` I jest believe if I had somebody to teach me 'cept that 'ere yellin ' tomcat of a Cadet Spencer I 'd learn in a jiffy , dog on his boots ! ''", "context_len": 34}}}, "chevrons": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "Cadet Murray looked handsome in his spotless uniform of gray and white , with his chevrons of gold ; he strode in with a stern and haughty look which speedily changed to one of displeasure as he gazed about him at the room", "context_len": 43}}}, "martinet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Jest think o ' that little martinet a yellin ' at me an ' tellin ' me I did n't have any sense", "context_len": 23}}}, "rared": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "To-day , for instance , d'you remember , he was tryin ' to show Indian how to march an ' move his legs , an ' Indian got twisted up into a knot ; an ' durnation , jist because I laughed , why he rared round an ' bucked fo ' an hour ! What 's the harm in laughing , anyhow ? ''", "context_len": 65}}}, "bucked": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "To-day , for instance , d'you remember , he was tryin ' to show Indian how to march an ' move his legs , an ' Indian got twisted up into a knot ; an ' durnation , jist because I laughed , why he rared round an ' bucked fo ' an hour ! What 's the harm in laughing , anyhow ? ''", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "The latter bucked until tired , then spun around as if on a pivot and finally dashed off on a dead run", "context_len": 22}}}, "archway": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "On the side of the archway a notice board announced it to be the old Westgate , through which the armies of Edward III and Henry V marched to embark upon the expedition that ended respectively in the victories of Cr\u00e9cy and Agincourt", "context_len": 43}}}, "stunning": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "His cane had fallen from his hand , his head was thrown up as if he had been struck a stunning blow under the chin , and even at the distance he was , Bart could see that his usually red-puffed face was the color of chalk", "context_len": 47}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "'It must be stunning to have a bicycle , ' said Bobby , regarding with envious eye the row of bright machines neatly stacked against the wall , and spinning the pedals with a not too gentle hand", "context_len": 38}}}, "magnitude": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 45, "context": "Alexander 's was a simple burial procession , going from the palace where he died to the proper cemetery -- a march of a thousand miles , it is true , but all within his own dominions The greatness of it resulted simply from the magnitude of the scale on which every thing pertaining to the mighty here was performed , for it was nothing but a simple passage from the dwelling to the burial-ground on his own estates , after all", "context_len": 82}}}, "thunderclap": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "And then came the thunderclap", "context_len": 5}}}, "surge": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "To lower away with a practised crew manning the falls would entail a certain amount of risk should the boat surge against the ship 's side ; with a crowd of raw amateurs the danger was magnified threefold", "context_len": 38}}}, "twitched": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "First , the hearth , on which no spot or speck was to be seen , must be brushed up anew ; next , the corner of the table-cloth was to be twitched into place , and a knife laid more exactly into straight line ; then a ball , belonging to one of the younger children , was picked up and put in the toy-basket , with the reminder to little Tommy that father was coming , and the room must be kept in good order", "context_len": 87}}}, "controlled": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` Me eatee aftelward , '' said Ki Sing when he perceived that his presence at the table was the subject of controversy , but he was overruled by Richard Dewey , who possessed a large share of independence , and would not allow himself to be controlled or influenced by the prejudices of others", "context_len": 55}}}, "clinched": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 42, "context": "He aimed another blow with all his might ; Mark dodged ; the other tried again , and again the plebe leaped to one side ; this repeated again and again was the story of the next minute , and the yearlings clinched their hands in disappointment and rage", "context_len": 49}}}, "emotionless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He clinched his hands behind him and bit his tongue , and when he spoke he was calm and emotionless", "context_len": 20}}}, "murray": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Murray '' gazed into space , and Mark turned without another word and strode over to where his friend was sitting", "context_len": 21}}}, "coherently": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "Yes , you said something like that to the captain ; and as though she would have given all the world if he had n't , but I did n't mean to be bold really , only I felt so sorry for Chris ; and then she proceeded to tell , as coherently as her emotions would allow , of her unexpected encounter with her old friend , and how dreadful it would have been if they could not have seen anything of each other just because Chris was a second-cabin passenger , and of how she had mustered all her courage and gone straight to the captain to see what could be done about it", "context_len": 116}}}, "dynamite": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` I 'd as soon think of sending a dynamite bomb , '' laughed Mark", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "At one time I -- that is my chums and I -- were accused of blowing up the school tower with dynamite , and -- -- ''", "context_len": 27}}}, "bomb": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 62, "word_index": 31, "context": "With levelled bayonets Malcolm , Selwyn , and half a dozen riflemen advanced towards the door in the rear of the pill-box , while M'Turk and M'Kane , each brandishing a bomb , ran close to the wall immediately by the side of the machine-gun aperture", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "The Jap guns had shot up the field so you couldn \u2019 t get any planes off to bomb them", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "As they headed for their new target at ten thousand feet , more bomb bursts tossed up white fountains of sea water around the farther warships", "context_len": 26}}}, "diplomacy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "Half idly , Richard asked more questions and grew interested in the man 's tales , for the fellow 's varied experiences had given him a sort of shrewd cunning , which in a higher walk of life might have been almost worthy the name of diplomacy , and he knew how to fit his tale to his audience", "context_len": 59}}}, "venturing": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "Ever mindful of the inestimable value of the mail treasure , he adjusted them with much care about his neck , somewhat as if they were life preservers , and holding his light rifle in his hand , he stepped cautiously into the current like an elephant venturing upon a rickety bridge", "context_len": 52}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "Joe \u2019 s mother , pitifully silent and anxious-eyed , not venturing to question the wisdom or oppose the will of her husband , went every day to place fresh flowers in Joe \u2019 s room", "context_len": 36}}}, "prospecting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` Of course she does n't know all I suffered , what with taking up one worthless claim after another month in and out -- if you mean that ! Why , I actually thought one time of giving up prospecting and settling down to day 's work ! Yes 'm ! It was sure enough that grub-stake you gave me last Fourth of July that brought me my first luck ! I put it right into Pony Gulch and my pick struck free-milling ore the first blow ! Some of the stuff runs ninety dollars to the ton and some higher", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He loved better than simple work the precarious excitement of prospecting -- an occupation which , except in isolated and accidental instances , can not be pursued to any good save with the aid of science and capital", "context_len": 38}}}, "visage": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "The Parson , however , did not act as if curiosity had brought him ; with a businesslike air and a solemn visage he strode down the company street , and , heedless of the cadets who had gathered at the tent doors to see him , halted in front of one before which he saw `` Billy '' Williams standing", "context_len": 61}}}, "heedless": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "The Parson , however , did not act as if curiosity had brought him ; with a businesslike air and a solemn visage he strode down the company street , and , heedless of the cadets who had gathered at the tent doors to see him , halted in front of one before which he saw `` Billy '' Williams standing", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 125, "context": "and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say afterwards upon the danger of such heedless practical jokes", "context_len": 128}}}, "unwinding": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Williams had been engaged in vigorously drying his face ; he paused , and gazed up out of the towel in surprise , and one of his tent mates , Cadet Captain Fischer , ceased unwinding himself from his long red sash and stared", "context_len": 44}}}, "brawny": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Pleased to meet you , '' said Williams , stretching out a long , brawny arm", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "A brawny , large , and handsome man was the father , on whose shoulder the little fellow was riding , while a demure , pale-faced sister of about ten , walked by the side of the two", "context_len": 38}}}, "skinny": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I have not far to go now -- my home is down there , '' indicating with a skinny finger the rain-blotted heathery pit beneath us", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Now that the danger was past , Drake and I could not help laughing at our cavalier companion and his fair burden , for the hag had clasped him tightly round the waist with her skinny arms , while the cat , perched on her shoulders , was rubbing its head against the back of Felgate \u2019 s plumed hat , so that it was being continually thrust over his eyes despite his frequent attempts to place it firmly on his head", "context_len": 82}}}, "prehensile": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The Parson drew up his `` prehensile muscles , '' as he called them", "context_len": 14}}}, "excessively": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "Both father and son were excessively fond of money , and the large amount of the fortune of the young lady , who stood to the father in the relation of ward , had excited the covetousness of both", "context_len": 39}}}, "humidity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` It is not that the temperature is excessively altitudinous , '' responded the Parson , `` but the presence of a larger proportion of humidity retards perspiratory exudation", "context_len": 29}}}, "retards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` It is not that the temperature is excessively altitudinous , '' responded the Parson , `` but the presence of a larger proportion of humidity retards perspiratory exudation", "context_len": 29}}}, "perspiratory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` It is not that the temperature is excessively altitudinous , '' responded the Parson , `` but the presence of a larger proportion of humidity retards perspiratory exudation", "context_len": 29}}}, "exudation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` It is not that the temperature is excessively altitudinous , '' responded the Parson , `` but the presence of a larger proportion of humidity retards perspiratory exudation", "context_len": 29}}}, "fulfill": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Mallory desires to know -- if you will pardon my abruptness in proceeding immediately to the matter in hand -- to know if it is not possible for you to fulfill a certain -- er -- engagement which you had with him", "context_len": 42}}}, "tapped": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 24, "context": "Once more we were taken into the presence of the magistrate , who , phlegmatic as ever , merely raised one eyebrow slightly and tapped the book in front of him with the feathered end of his pen", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "short flowers soft stopped tapped pull pick voice string", "context_len": 9}}}, "profusely": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` And , by gum , how about me ? '' enquired a lusty voice , as Riflemen Joliffe , bleeding profusely from the head , sat up and vainly attempted to regain his feet", "context_len": 35}}}, "suddenness": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 6, "context": "Peggy was so amazed by the suddenness of the attack that for the moment she offered no resistance ; but , finding a storm of blows descending on her head like hail , she managed to squirm out of Betsy 's ungentle grasp , and fled after Bobby down the lane , followed by a shower of epithets from the gate , where the old woman stood shaking her fist until long after the children were out of sight", "context_len": 79}}}, "conspicuous": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 27, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}}}, "resents": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "In accordance with Indian etiquette , which resents a sudden intrusion , he clapped his hands and shouted", "context_len": 18}}}, "directs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Absolutely , until the class so directs", "context_len": 8}}}, "culminating": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "There was another rush to the ship \u2019 s side , and I saw a dozen dark forms struggling in a smother of foam-lashed water under our quarter , while the triangular fins of several sharks showed that the culminating tragedy had occurred", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The culminating crest of the main range is the Wind River Mountains in the west-central part of the State , which is traversed by numerous other ranges , including the Big Horn in the north-central section , the Laramie Mountains already mentioned , the Medicine Bow in the south ; north of them the Sweetwater and Rattlesnake ranges , and in the west the Teton , Shoshone and Gros Ventres mountains", "context_len": 71}}}, "stroll": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 54, "context": "It had been part of the plan to attend the five-o'clock service in Christ Church Cathedral ; and after spending a half hour or so in wandering through the cloisters and gaining something of an idea of the college as a whole , they went early into the cathedral , that they might also stroll for a while through the beautiful old church whose history dates as far back as the middle of the eighth century", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "Tea was over , and they had all gone out into the garden again for a last stroll before bed-time , when they saw him come running across the field , which was separated from the lawn by a sunk fence", "context_len": 41}}}, "closers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "When their rather ragged and scattered company fell in behind the corps at barracks , they were all talking about it , at least when the file closers were n't near", "context_len": 31}}}, "eying": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "At supper nobody talked of anything else , and everybody in the room was eying Mark and his stalwart opponent and speculating as to what the chances would be", "context_len": 29}}}, "classmate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "And the plebes feared it would be that way , too , and yet there were a few at the tables discussing the matter in whispers , venturesome enough to say that perhaps maybe their classmate might win and to wonder what on earth would happen to him if he did", "context_len": 51}}}, "irate": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Poor Texas was the subject of a ten-minute discourse upon `` impertinence and presumption , '' because he was guilty of the heinous offense of bursting out laughing in the midst of one of the irate little drillmaster 's tirades", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "There were swarms of mischievous boys on the banks , whose sole amusement seemed to be throwing stones at the irate bargees , until one of the women grew so furious that she leapt upon the coal that screened the New Zealanders ' retreat , and picking up fragments hurled them at her tormentors", "context_len": 54}}}, "thundering": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` If you do n't , '' said the other , looking serious , `` by jingo ! we 'll be in a thundering fix", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a thundering good job when he slung that dud bomb at Featherstone Camp , or I might n't be here now", "context_len": 23}}}, "walkover": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "There 's nobody in the class can beat you , and that plebe 'll have a walkover", "context_len": 17}}}, "badgered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` It certainly is something , '' Ned had insisted , and they had badgered Fenn until he finally admitted that he was now collecting mud turtles , and had a number of them in a pen at home", "context_len": 39}}}, "meeker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` about it , he is bullied the more ; if he shows fight , he has all he wants , and is made meeker still", "context_len": 26}}}, "athlete": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 65, "context": "Now , here was Mark Mallory -- to say nothing of Texas -- who had come up to the Point with an absurd notion of his own dignity , who had outwitted the yearlings at every turn , been sent to Coventry -- and did n't care a hang , and now was on the point of trying to `` lick '' the finest all-around athlete in the whole third class", "context_len": 71}}}, "meddle": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 46, "context": "He meant to be faithful , but of course he was not to be trusted without a good deal of watching to make sure he was not interfered with , and there were folks enough , bad boys and girls , who were always ready to meddle with him and set him up to some mischief , just for the bit of fun it would make for themselves", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The pashas here , and the sultans of the Moors , are all alike ; if they once meddle in an affair they take all the profit , and think they do well by giving you a tithe of it", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Every one of us at court finds his new seat so slippery that he dare not wag a finger for fear of being upset -- and I know none there who dare meddle with my Lord Lauderdale 's favourite", "context_len": 40}}}, "ambassador": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Howard refused at first to meet the emperor 's wishes ; but , on the English ambassador representing good might come of the visit , Howard went to see his majesty , and remained with him two hours in conversation , during which time he made the emperor acquainted with the bad state of some of the Austrian prisons", "context_len": 59}}}, "combatant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "At the same moment , another horseman , on another side , had his sword raised , and would have killed Alexander before he could have turned to defend himself , had no help intervened ; but just at this instant a third combatant , one of Alexander 's friends , seeing the danger , brought down so terrible a blow upon the shoulder of this second assailant as to separate his arm from his body", "context_len": 76}}}, "hoe": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "'Pawliney , ' piped her father in his shrill voice , 'where 's Lemuel ? I told him to take the horse to the forge , and hoe the potatoes , and weed the onions , and go to the woods for a load", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 39, "context": "And when they reached the cottage , or rather the little lane that runs down between the hedgerows , who did they see at once but Chris himself , busy at work in the garden , and Donald , hoe in hand , close beside him , cutting vigorously at the weeds round some hop-vines , and both working away with such a will and such a farmer-like air that it looked as though both had mistaken their calling", "context_len": 79}}}, "calamity": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` `` Ay , my lord , '' replied the Maugrabin , `` I am indeed a stranger and arrived but yesternight in this your town ; wherefore I beseech thee tell me of the divine gifts of this holy woman and where her place is , for that I have fallen into a calamity and would fain go to her and crave her of prayer , so haply God ( to whom belong might and majesty ) may deliver me from my stress , by means of her intercession", "context_len": 90}}}, "sentries": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 85, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}}}, "ramparts": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 24, "context": "From the breach clouds of dust rose from under the feet of the combatants , mingling with the smoke of the cannon on the ramparts , the fort , and Turkish ships , and at times entirely hid from the sight of the anxious spectators on the walls of the town and fortress , and of the still more numerous throng of Turks on St", "context_len": 65}}}, "sponges": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "Indians use the bare hand and arm -- utensils in common stable use among us whites are the metal curry-combs , bristle body-brushes , corn-brushes , rub rags , sponges , whisks and hoof picks", "context_len": 35}}}, "towels": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I have several dozens of towels to be hemmed , and I intended that Jane should do them all ; but I will keep out one dozen for you , and will pay you five cents apiece", "context_len": 38}}}, "shanks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I am coming to that , '' said the Parson , gravely , stretching out his long shanks , drawing up his trousers , and displaying his sea-green socks", "context_len": 30}}}, "breastworks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "About the same time Stanard 's long shanks appeared over the breastworks , and Indian tumbled over a moment later", "context_len": 20}}}, "referee": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "He sank to the ground just as Eliot was tackled and dragged down and the referee \u2019 s whistle shrilled the signal which told that the half was over", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Are you all ready ? '' asked the referee , and Captain Holly Cross cast a quick eye on his team , which , spread out on their field , was like an aggregation of eager foxhounds , waiting for the start", "context_len": 43}}}, "timekeeper": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "The cool , deliberate voice of the timekeeper calling off the fateful ten seconds silenced all other sounds of approbation or encouragement to the fallen man", "context_len": 26}}}, "suspenders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Williams was by this time stripped to the waist , his suspenders tied about him", "context_len": 15}}}, "sized": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "The tables are divided down the center by an imaginary line , each part being a `` table '' ; first class men sit near the head , and so on down to the plebes , who find themselves at the center ( that is , after they have moved into camp , and been `` sized '' and assigned to companies ; before that they are `` beasts , '' herded apart , as has been said )", "context_len": 79}}}, "unkept": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The ring is surrounded by earthworks , now grass-grown and trodden down , unkept since the Revolutionary days , when West Point was a Gibraltar", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It was characteristic of its proprietor -- ricketty , disorderly , the yard unkept and grown over with weeds", "context_len": 19}}}, "caissons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Old cannon , caissons and wagon wheels are scattered about inside , together with ramparts and wire chevaux-de-friezes which the cadets are practiced in constructing", "context_len": 25}}}, "constructing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 35, "context": "One thing only the players felt to be missing in their entertainments , and that was the very important feature of an audience ; for what is the use of learning up parts , and constructing scenery , if there is nobody to come and watch you act ? Peggy sounded Father on the subject of a children 's party , but he did not rise at all to the occasion", "context_len": 71}}}, "southwest": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 45, "context": "A few hours later the chieftains were all summoned to a council in the king 's tent , and when , after a short session , they came forth , the general order was given to break up the encampment , and move towards the southwest for the winter , for all the resources of the country around were exhausted", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "At the time of his recall he was at the island of \u00c6gina , which is about forty miles southwest of Athens , in one of the gulfs of the \u00c6gean Sea", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Course is southwest by south , Curly Levitt \u2019 s voice came over the phone", "context_len": 15}}}, "outlooks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The `` outlooks , '' posted for safety , are neglecting their duty recklessly for the same reason , and looking in altogether", "context_len": 23}}}, "neglecting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 76, "context": "From Norfolk Island , in 1870 , he wrote to his sister when he was holding an ordination : `` At such times as these , when one is specially engaged in solemn work , there is much heart searching ; and I can not tell you how my conscience accuses me of such systematic selfishness during many long years -- I mean I see how I was all along making self the centre , and neglecting all kinds of duties -- social and others -- in consequence ''", "context_len": 89}}}, "mutters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` `` Indian '' is the fourth and only other plebe present , and Indian is horrified , as usual , and mutters `` Bless my soul '' at intervals", "context_len": 30}}}, "generals": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 27, "context": "The strong sentiment of love and devotion with which he inspired all around him at the commencement of his career , was now gone , and his generals and officers were continually planning schemes to depose him from the power which he seemed no longer to have the energy to wield ; or , at least , Alexander was continually suspecting that such plans were formed , and he was kept in a continual state of uneasiness and anxiety in discovering and punishing them", "context_len": 84}}}, "skirmish": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The two began cautiously , like a pair of skillful generals sending out a skirmish line to test the enemy 's strength and resource", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The skirmish was short , and I reckon it was the tray of chuck that did the work for the shuffer", "context_len": 21}}}, "resource": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 51, "context": "I am right glad to hear you speak so warmly of Tresham , Sir John , for I regard him as my special protege , and am pleased indeed to find that at this outset of his career he has proved himself not only a brave knight , but full of resource , and quick at invention", "context_len": 57}}}, "sparring": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then for the rest of the round the excited cadets were treated to an exhibition of sparring such as they had never seen in their lives", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Fred and the colonel were very fond of joking and sparring in this fashion , but Bessie always looked very sober while it was going on ; for she could not bear anything that sounded like disputing , even in play ; and perhaps she was about right", "context_len": 48}}}, "harmlessly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "In the briefest conceivable time the whites discharged their weapons , but in that flitting interval , their enemies had shifted their position , and the bullets whizzed harmlessly past in the grass , or buried themselves in the earth", "context_len": 40}}}, "feinting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Feinting , dodging and parrying , the springing pair seemed everywhere at once , and their fists in a thousand places", "context_len": 21}}}, "parrying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Feinting , dodging and parrying , the springing pair seemed everywhere at once , and their fists in a thousand places", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "How do you come to be here , Cousin Theodore ? parrying question with question , and drawing her little figure to its full height , in resentment of the tone in which Ted had spoken", "context_len": 36}}}, "imperturbable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "The crowd was thrilled ; even the imperturbable Fischer was moved to exclamation , and Texas in half a minute had seen more skill than his whole experience had shown him in his life", "context_len": 34}}}, "gad": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "He went forward meekly , took the gad that Blixey handed to him , and resumed the monotonous task of urging on the tired and miserable horses", "context_len": 27}}}, "skillfully": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Hughie stumbled to his feet ; Irene sat in the stern , took the ropes , and skillfully guided the boat into the centre of the stream", "context_len": 27}}}, "nearness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "Oh , that she had little Bessie 's simple faith ! that this feeling of the Saviour 's nearness , this constant looking to him for help and guidance , which were shown by this little one , were hers also ! She bethought herself of a hymn , which she had heard Mrs", "context_len": 54}}}, "stagger": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 46, "context": "Bobby suggested floating them down the stream , but , as Lilian pointed out , they would promptly sink to the bottom ; so in the end Archie hoisted a sack upon his back , and , with Lilian to steady it behind , managed to stagger across in safety , coming back for the other when the first had been successfully landed", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "While Texas muttered and pranced about for joy , Mark dealt his opponent another blow which made him stagger ; he caught himself upon one knee , and Mark stepped back and waited for him to rise", "context_len": 37}}}, "classmates": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 32, "context": "It had been impossible to induce Gracie to take any part or to show any interest in the competition , and she had resolutely refused to come with the rest of her classmates this morning , and there was no obligation upon her to do so , as it was now holiday time and this was something outside of the regular school duties", "context_len": 63}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He has classmates quite as rich as he who are perfectly steady , and doing credit to their families", "context_len": 19}}}, "flunking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` He 's flunking ! '' cried one of them -- Bull Harris -- '' He 's afraid ! ''", "context_len": 20}}}, "prettily": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 77, "context": "Then as soon as it is ready all the institutions for children in London are to be told about it , and whenever a little girl comes along who seems to be too fine , in the best sense of the word , for the life of the ordinary institution , down she is to go to Cranford , to be cared for in the Home ; and it is to be a home , Harold , prettily furnished , with rooms for ten children , and everything as dainty as can be", "context_len": 93}}}, "endurance": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was evident that his pitch of enthusiasm , or perhaps physical endurance , was not equal to that of his companion , for he panted with heat as he held the other end of the yard measure , and gave vent to occasional grunts of disapprobation as he toiled painfully to the tops of mounds to get bird's-eye views of the outlined cells , or take snap-shots of the remains of the windows and columns", "context_len": 76}}}, "madder": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` He 's getting madder now , '' whispered Mark to Stanard , as he sat down to rest", "context_len": 19}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "And papa said I was naughty , and that woman said she would not have such a noise there , and I must go away if I was not quiet , and that made me madder", "context_len": 36}}}, "goaded": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}}}, "surer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "For they were both clinging to one faith , both looking to one hope , and the hand of the younger had drawn the feet of the elder to a firmer and surer foothold upon the Rock of Ages , on which both were resting", "context_len": 45}}}, "fischer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Fischer gazed at his watch and saw that there was yet half a minute , and at the same moment he heard a resounding thump", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Fischer ! '' cried the three , in obvious surprise", "context_len": 10}}}, "resounding": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "An upper cut caught van Eindhovengen full on the point of his chin , while almost simultaneously the Maori drove home a resounding blow on the Afrikander 's solar plexus", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "Down , down , down at a tremendous speed they went ; the earth appeared to be coming up to them with awful swiftness ; and a minute or two later with a resounding crash they struck the ground at Newhaven close to the sea", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Here , you let that banner alone ! '' cried Tom , and he gave such a jerk to the chair on which Sid was standing that the objector to things artistic toppled to the floor with a resounding crash", "context_len": 41}}}, "rallied": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 36, "context": "Several Danes strove to pass the breach ; one was already through , and Edmund attacked him ; meanwhile two others had crept through , but were cut off from their fellows , for the English rallied in front and presented an impenetrable barrier with their spears , while from the windows above the arrows rained upon the assailants", "context_len": 59}}}, "groggy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Williams rallied , and made a desperate fight for his life , but at the close of that round he was what a professional reporter would have termed `` groggy", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "He didn \u2019 t say anything to you personally , did he , Sleuth ? Wonder how you got by ? Morehead had you groggy in that last smash", "context_len": 29}}}, "swathing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The speaker was a lad with brown , curly hair and a laughing , merry face , at present , however , half covered with a swathing of bandages", "context_len": 29}}}, "sturdily": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 54, "context": "I think if it had not been for the fact that she knew Bobby was peeping at her from over the wall , Peggy would have turned tail too , but as it was , she felt bound to carry out her project , and under the fire of his laughing eyes she walked sturdily towards the strangers", "context_len": 58}}}, "proffered": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "Marie-Celeste gave a very audible sigh of relief as she stepped up the two steps into the room , but she refused the proffered seat with the dignity of a little princess", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 61, "context": "When the King heard her story , he fell a-laughing , of his clemency , [ 368 ] and asked her , `` What is that thou hast with thee and what is that bundle ? '' [ 369 ] Whereupon she , seeing that he was not angered at her words , but laughed , opened the handkerchief forthright and proffered him the dish of jewels", "context_len": 67}}}, "goner": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "I \u2019 m a goner ! he wailed , throwing himself forward so as to be as flat as possible on the back of his animal", "context_len": 26}}}, "spunky": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Say , but you 're a spunky chap ! '' remarked Dewey , eying Mark with an admiring expression", "context_len": 20}}}, "burly": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 24, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "scar": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "He still retained his flowing lovelocks , while the lower part of his weather-worn face was adorned by a greyish beard of Van Dyck cut , which failed to hide a portion of a long , whitish scar that extended from his left eyebrow to his cheek bone -- the legacy of a pike-thrust in the sanguinary encounter of Cropredy Bridge", "context_len": 61}}}, "lammed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Betcher life , b'gee ! He tried to make me climb that tree , and , b'gee , says I , ' I wo n't , b'gee ! ' Then I lammed him one in the eye -- -- ''", "context_len": 41}}}, "bandage": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 29, "context": "One of the men had his left arm in a sling , the empty sleeve being pinned to his coat ; the other , in addition to wearing a bandage round his forehead , walked with a pronounced limp and leant heavily upon a rubber-shod walking-stick", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Say , Lieutenant , came Fred Marmon \u2019 s reply , did you ever try to bandage a man \u2019 s seat with a roll of one-inch gauze ? I might do it if Soapy would hold still , but he \u2019 s wiggling like a worm on a fishhook", "context_len": 50}}}, "nicknamed": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 10, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}}}, "laundryman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Pretty well done up , as the laundryman remarked , to borrow your style of illustration , '' Mark responded , laughing", "context_len": 23}}}, "chaps": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 1, "context": "Those chaps -- 'island carrions , desperate of their bones ' , as Will Shakespeare aptly puts it -- are our ancestors , Malcolm , whether we are New Zealanders , Australians , or Canadians , and although we are up against a big thing I have n't the faintest doubt that blood will tell , as it did in those days", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 19, "context": "Howsomever , they say as a jap cruiser 'as got the hang of the 'Un , an ' you chaps 'ave been done out of a visit to Davy Jones this time", "context_len": 32}}}, "enjoined": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Charlie lost no time putting into execution Captain Rhines \u2019 s directions in respect to the pig , having first enjoined upon them the greatest secrecy , not even permitting them to tell Ben and Sally of his plots and suspicions , lest Joe , who was very quick of perception , should divine what was in store for him", "context_len": 60}}}, "organize": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "The sound of a drum from barracks put a stop to further business then , but before supper there was a spare half hour , and during that time the seven conspirators met in Mark 's room to `` organize", "context_len": 40}}}, "injustice": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` I do not wish to dissuade you ; indeed , I can not myself see how you can get out of this most mistaken engagement , for the man has behaved well , and I am the first to acknowledge that ; but has it ever occurred to you that you do a man an absolute and terrible injustice when you marry him , loving with all your heart and soul another man ? Do you think it is fair to him ? Do n't you think he ought at least to know this ? ''", "context_len": 97}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` No , no , dearest madam , '' cried Dick , `` you know in your own heart you do me injustice", "context_len": 23}}}, "prompted": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 106, "context": "younger , extreme carelessness had been her besetting fault , and , as is the manner of this `` little fox , '' had created much trouble for herself and for others ; but having become convinced that it was her duty to cure herself of this , she had set to work to do it in such earnest that that which had been a burden and a care to her was fast becoming a settled habit , and it was but seldom now that any act or word of heedlessness could be laid to her charge , while her ever obliging disposition and loving heart prompted many a deed of kindness which she never failed to carry out if it were in her power to do so", "context_len": 128}}}, "formula": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Heels together , eyes to the front , chest out '' -- they knew the whole formula by this time", "context_len": 21}}}, "devastation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Thus the devastation and misery which the making of these conquests brought upon Europe and Asia were continued for many years , during the slow and terrible process of their return to their original condition", "context_len": 35}}}, "salutation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "Feeling confident that the deception would not be noticed , he kept his course without swerving , and passed some of the fishing boats within hailing distance , waving his hand and shouting the usual Arab salutation to their crews", "context_len": 40}}}, "outrageous": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "And I do swear by the bones of my ancestors , by the hounds of Diana , forsooth even by Jupiter lapis and the Gemini , that never while I inspire the atmosphere of existence will I submit myself to so outrageous an imposition -- -- ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "imposition": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "And I do swear by the bones of my ancestors , by the hounds of Diana , forsooth even by Jupiter lapis and the Gemini , that never while I inspire the atmosphere of existence will I submit myself to so outrageous an imposition -- -- ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "immortal": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` It is written in the most immortal document , '' continued the Parson , without noticing the interruption , `` that ever emanated from the mind of man , the Declaration of Independence ( signed , by the way , by an ancestor of my stepmother ) , that among the inalienable privileges of man , co-ordinate with life and liberty itself , is the pursuit of happiness", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 74, "context": "'We make our plans for this world , ' he said softly , speaking as much to himself as to Peggy , 'and say we will do this or that , but sometimes God takes it out of our hands and arranges it for us ; but His ways are better than our ways , and His thoughts than our thoughts , and , after all , death is but the gate to life immortal", "context_len": 75}}}, "document": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` It is written in the most immortal document , '' continued the Parson , without noticing the interruption , `` that ever emanated from the mind of man , the Declaration of Independence ( signed , by the way , by an ancestor of my stepmother ) , that among the inalienable privileges of man , co-ordinate with life and liberty itself , is the pursuit of happiness", "context_len": 69}}}, "emanated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 99, "context": "As we drew near to the exceedingly narrow gate in the palisade , which had been thrown open to admit us -- and which , I presently saw , was strongly guarded by a number of warriors armed with heavy , broad-bladed spears , murderous-looking swords , and small round shields , or targets , of wood covered with what looked like crocodile hide -- I became sensible of a horrible charnel-house smell ; but it was not until we had passed through the gate , and were inside the palisaded enclosure , that I discovered from whence it emanated", "context_len": 100}}}, "stepmother": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` It is written in the most immortal document , '' continued the Parson , without noticing the interruption , `` that ever emanated from the mind of man , the Declaration of Independence ( signed , by the way , by an ancestor of my stepmother ) , that among the inalienable privileges of man , co-ordinate with life and liberty itself , is the pursuit of happiness", "context_len": 69}}}, "inalienable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` It is written in the most immortal document , '' continued the Parson , without noticing the interruption , `` that ever emanated from the mind of man , the Declaration of Independence ( signed , by the way , by an ancestor of my stepmother ) , that among the inalienable privileges of man , co-ordinate with life and liberty itself , is the pursuit of happiness", "context_len": 69}}}, "divinities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "These deities having been engaged in the same enterprises themselves , and animated with the same hopes and fears , the soldiers imagined that the semi-human divinities invoked by them would take an interest in their dangers , and rejoice is their success", "context_len": 43}}}, "buccaneers": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 26, "context": "Instead , I journey to the Rhine to join the German freebooters , or else to the Spanish Main to throw in my lot with the buccaneers of the Indies -- it matters not which ; but ere I go I have an account to settle with the Lord of Holwick", "context_len": 51}}}, "cradle": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 23, "context": "Next , Tom read how Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea , in a rough stable and laid not in a pretty cradle such as their baby brothers and sisters slept in , but in a manger where the wise men of the east came and worshipped Him : and how after Joseph and Mary had been told by God to fly into the land of Egypt with the infant Saviour , the wicked king , Herod , killed all the dear little babies in the land , with the hope that Jesus might be among them", "context_len": 98}}}, "peroration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Jeremiah Powers , '' continued Mark , cutting the Parson off in his peroration", "context_len": 14}}}, "inconspicuous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "' And Indian echoed 'Bless my soul ! ' and crept into the room and sat down in an inconspicuous corner", "context_len": 21}}}, "facetious": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 82, "context": "People knew the time of their passing , and looked out for them daily ; kindly women would come to cottage doorways to nod and smile ; the inn-keeper at the Halfway Arms set his clock by their arrival ; they had struck up quite a friendship with the postman , two milkmen , and the driver of a fish-cart , while the old man who broke stones by the roadside always nodded and gave them 'Good-morning , ' and fired a facetious remark or two after them , which the children imagined he must relieve the monotony of his work by composing during the intervals of their coming and going", "context_len": 111}}}, "lunar": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` If I remember correctly , we were occupied when last interrupted , by -- ahem ! a rather facetious observation upon the subject of our solitary lunar satellite and quadruped of the genus Bos -- occupied I say in considering the position which the metropolis of Boston has obtained -- -- ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "satellite": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "This story is so much about Peggy and her satellite Bobby that we have rather neglected Lilian , and she deserves a chapter all to herself ; for she was one of the sweetest , gentlest , most unselfish of girls , who filled a quiet niche in the little world of her home which would have been sadly empty without her", "context_len": 62}}}, "quadruped": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "She gave Bobby a leg up on to Brindle 's broad back , and had just succeeded in scrambling inelegantly behind him , when the astonished quadruped paused in her task of cropping the succulent grass and buttercups , and realizing that something unusual was oppressing her , fled in mad career over the meadow , with the delighted children clinging desperately to her horns", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "You are the only quadruped without running water in your stall", "context_len": 11}}}, "genus": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Well , the parts I used to like best were the verses ; for Harold , after the manner of the genus who pride themselves on early outgrowing many of the best things of life , relegated the books to the days of his early childhood ; the stories themselves always seemed more meant for girls than for boys", "context_len": 59}}}, "summary": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "concise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "extensive": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "introductions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "formal": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 31, "context": "If our vows had not forbidden our fighting I should have called him to account long ago ; but the only thing else to do was for me to lodge a formal complaint before the bailiff , of his continually offensive bearing and manner , which I could not bring myself to do , and indeed there was no special matter that would have seemed to justify me , no single speech that in itself would warrant such grave action on my part", "context_len": 83}}}, "perorations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "promulgations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "clarified": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "conciseness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "eminently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 72, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "advantageous": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 73, "context": "`` Well ! Since it is the obvious and , in fact , natural desire of the company assembled , so expressed by them , that I should immediately proceed to a summary and concise statement of the matter in hand , pausing for no extensive introductions or formal perorations , but endeavoring assiduously to impart to my promulgations a certain clarified conciseness which in matters of this peculiar nature is so eminently advantageous -- -- ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "emphatic": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 43, "context": "She reproached him for having appealed to Hannah , a servant in his father 's house , for aid ; and in her turn had to hear his reproaches for believing that he would condescend to such a thing , and received an emphatic and solemn denial that he had been guilty of this , or that he had ever let Hannah know of the straits he was in", "context_len": 69}}}, "stoic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "As I stated , an outrage had been offered me such as neither Parmenides , nor Socrates , nor even Zeno , stoic of stoics , could have borne", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The stoic calm of the Indian vanished", "context_len": 7}}}, "stoics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "As I stated , an outrage had been offered me such as neither Parmenides , nor Socrates , nor even Zeno , stoic of stoics , could have borne", "context_len": 29}}}, "hotbed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "And I have resolved to seek once more , as a prodigal son , the land of my birth , where science is fostered instead of being repressed as in this hotbed of prejudice and ignorance", "context_len": 36}}}, "substantiate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` This same morning -- and my friend Indian will substantiate my statement , for he was there -- a low , ignorant cadet corporal did enter into my room , for inspection , by Zeus , and after generally displaying his ill-manners , he turned to me and conveyed the extraordinary information that , according to rules , forsooth , I must be deprived of the dearest object of my affections , solace of my weary hours , my friend in time of need , my companion in sickness , which through all the trials of adversity has stuck to me closer than a brother , my only joy , my -- -- ''", "context_len": 115}}}, "deprived": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` This same morning -- and my friend Indian will substantiate my statement , for he was there -- a low , ignorant cadet corporal did enter into my room , for inspection , by Zeus , and after generally displaying his ill-manners , he turned to me and conveyed the extraordinary information that , according to rules , forsooth , I must be deprived of the dearest object of my affections , solace of my weary hours , my friend in time of need , my companion in sickness , which through all the trials of adversity has stuck to me closer than a brother , my only joy , my -- -- ''", "context_len": 115}}}, "solace": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 73, "context": "`` This same morning -- and my friend Indian will substantiate my statement , for he was there -- a low , ignorant cadet corporal did enter into my room , for inspection , by Zeus , and after generally displaying his ill-manners , he turned to me and conveyed the extraordinary information that , according to rules , forsooth , I must be deprived of the dearest object of my affections , solace of my weary hours , my friend in time of need , my companion in sickness , which through all the trials of adversity has stuck to me closer than a brother , my only joy , my -- -- ''", "context_len": 115}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Her handmaid used to go in to her at the time of the Salutation , [ 587 ] so she might dress her , and that morning , by the decree of destiny , the damsel opened the window at that time , thinking to solace her mistress with the sight of the trees and streams", "context_len": 56}}}, "adversity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 97, "context": "`` This same morning -- and my friend Indian will substantiate my statement , for he was there -- a low , ignorant cadet corporal did enter into my room , for inspection , by Zeus , and after generally displaying his ill-manners , he turned to me and conveyed the extraordinary information that , according to rules , forsooth , I must be deprived of the dearest object of my affections , solace of my weary hours , my friend in time of need , my companion in sickness , which through all the trials of adversity has stuck to me closer than a brother , my only joy , my -- -- ''", "context_len": 115}}}, "wrought": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 64, "context": "my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm , that thou shouldst merit of him this boon ? Again , this that thou cravest is beyond thy condition ; [ 336 ] so it can not be that the", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "She followed the letters with the tip of her finger , tracing them on the glass and at last spelling out the name of `` Annabel Lisle , wrought in her seventh year", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Yea , this accursed one was his brother and came to this country and wrought these frauds , slaying Fatimeh and donning her clothes and coming hither , so he might take vengeance on me for his brother", "context_len": 38}}}, "mitigating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` My one refuge from the cares of life , '' continued the solemn Parson , `` the one mitigating circumstance in this life of tribulation , the only -- -- ''", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Mitigating circumstances or otherwise , reduced to rock-bottom level , the three non-coms , had overstayed their leave , and were actually deserters , from a military point of view", "context_len": 30}}}, "tribulation": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 33, "context": "True , the Vizier 's son had fared worse than she , for that he was couched in the draught-house ; but he belied [ 401 ] the matter and put away that tribulation from his thought , of his fear lest he should lose his bride and his rank , [ 402 ] more by token that all the folk envied him his lot , for the much increase of honour it brought him , as also for the exceeding beauty and loveliness of the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 88}}}, "drillmaster": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` New cadets turn out ! '' which meant drill ; and it lasted after that , too , so that Cadet Spencer , drillmaster , `` on duty over plebes , '' spent the next hour or two in wondering what on earth his charges kept snickering at", "context_len": 49}}}, "charges": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 5, "context": "Schwitz had promised that his charges should be small , but how he never would give in his bill , the policeman and his wife thinking all the while that it was kindness which kept him from doing so ; how it had taken every cent of his salary to pay the other expenses of illness , and keep the family barely warmed and fed ; of the disappointment of their hopes for Willie for , at least , some time to come ; and finally of the terrible bill which Dr", "context_len": 92}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "He is such a dear , kind man , that I do not like to hurt his feelings ; but really , Westenra , he charges me so little that it quite goes to my heart", "context_len": 36}}}, "snickering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` New cadets turn out ! '' which meant drill ; and it lasted after that , too , so that Cadet Spencer , drillmaster , `` on duty over plebes , '' spent the next hour or two in wondering what on earth his charges kept snickering at", "context_len": 49}}}, "impertinence": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "Poor Texas was the subject of a ten-minute discourse upon `` impertinence and presumption , '' because he was guilty of the heinous offense of bursting out laughing in the midst of one of the irate little drillmaster 's tirades", "context_len": 40}}}, "heinous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Being personally acquainted with the before-mentioned parties , what was more natural than that , on hearing that this heinous crime had been committed , I should become profoundly interested in the case and should resolve to give it my earnest attention with the determination of solving the deep , dark mystery appertaining thereunto ?", "context_len": 55}}}, "offense": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 23, "context": "Poor Texas was the subject of a ten-minute discourse upon `` impertinence and presumption , '' because he was guilty of the heinous offense of bursting out laughing in the midst of one of the irate little drillmaster 's tirades", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It had been a mystery to him why people so often took offense and set to arguing the opposite side of the question", "context_len": 23}}}, "tirades": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Poor Texas was the subject of a ten-minute discourse upon `` impertinence and presumption , '' because he was guilty of the heinous offense of bursting out laughing in the midst of one of the irate little drillmaster 's tirades", "context_len": 40}}}, "periods": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 19, "context": "Opinions here vary a good deal among the friends that knew her best ; but those who at different periods of her life lived for any length of time under the same roof , will be able to recall certain instances of an absence of tact , a lack of quick understanding of the feelings of others , which certainly never arose from want of a desire to understand", "context_len": 69}}}, "irascible": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "The luckless victims begin after breakfast , and at regular ( and frequent ) periods until night are turned out under the charge of some irascible yearling to be taught all manner of military maneuvers -- setting up drill , how to stand , to face , and , in fact , how to walk", "context_len": 55}}}, "maneuvers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "The luckless victims begin after breakfast , and at regular ( and frequent ) periods until night are turned out under the charge of some irascible yearling to be taught all manner of military maneuvers -- setting up drill , how to stand , to face , and , in fact , how to walk", "context_len": 55}}}, "instructor": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 2, "context": "The young instructor , presenting , by the way , a ludicrous contrast in his shining uniform of gray and white and gold , with his three or four nervous and variously costumed pupils , takes the bayonet of his gun for a drill stick and marches `` his '' squad over into a secluded corner of the area and thus begins the above-mentioned instructions :", "context_len": 66}}}, "variously": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "The young instructor , presenting , by the way , a ludicrous contrast in his shining uniform of gray and white and gold , with his three or four nervous and variously costumed pupils , takes the bayonet of his gun for a drill stick and marches `` his '' squad over into a secluded corner of the area and thus begins the above-mentioned instructions :", "context_len": 66}}}, "costumed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The young instructor , presenting , by the way , a ludicrous contrast in his shining uniform of gray and white and gold , with his three or four nervous and variously costumed pupils , takes the bayonet of his gun for a drill stick and marches `` his '' squad over into a secluded corner of the area and thus begins the above-mentioned instructions :", "context_len": 66}}}, "bayonet": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 37, "context": "The young instructor , presenting , by the way , a ludicrous contrast in his shining uniform of gray and white and gold , with his three or four nervous and variously costumed pupils , takes the bayonet of his gun for a drill stick and marches `` his '' squad over into a secluded corner of the area and thus begins the above-mentioned instructions :", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Never again would he use either bayonet or boxing-gloves , for his right arm was totally incapacitated", "context_len": 17}}}, "marches": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 46, "context": "The young instructor , presenting , by the way , a ludicrous contrast in his shining uniform of gray and white and gold , with his three or four nervous and variously costumed pupils , takes the bayonet of his gun for a drill stick and marches `` his '' squad over into a secluded corner of the area and thus begins the above-mentioned instructions :", "context_len": 66}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "This ceremony over , the battalion marches back to camp , a good half mile , in the broiling sun or pouring rain , as the case may be", "context_len": 29}}}, "strikes": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 16, "context": "To be of easy remembrance , there must be something connected with these events which forcibly strikes the imagination and in later times stands out as the principal fact , while minor features of its occurrence disappear or become subject to alterations in the progress of time", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "This , doubtless , may be so , Sir Gervaise , the doge said , with a slight smile , but it is to the head that plans , rather than to the hand that strikes , that such success as you have achieved is due ; and the credit of this night attack is , as the cavalier Caretto tells me , wholly yours , for until you issued your final orders it seemed to him , and to the two good knights his companions , that there was naught to do but to remain in port and watch this corsair fleet sail away to carry out its work of destruction", "context_len": 112}}}, "exclusive": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 87, "context": "Then , by grace of his own idea of fitness , Harold had added to the other furnishings a Dresden china toilet-set , and in this he was perhaps far wiser than he knew , for is there anything so well calculated to captivate at sight the heart of a dainty little maiden as the mysterious round-topped boxes that compose the dainty outfit of the ideal dressing-table ? Then , to crown it all , a pair of ponies and a basket-phaeton had been purchased for the exclusive use of the guests that were to be", "context_len": 96}}}, "interruptions": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 32, "context": "Many , whose favourite occupations are , to say the least , no whit more pressing than were her books , are exceedingly tenacious of their time , and exceedingly impatient of interruptions ; and with too many the home-calls come second to all personal interests", "context_len": 46}}}, "witticisms": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "That is the way the marching exercise goes , exclusive , of course , of all interruptions , comments and witticisms on the instructor 's part", "context_len": 26}}}, "stiffness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 61, "context": "Mark declined , however , saying that he was quite well ; and soon after , in spite of the opposition he met with from the master , who said it was foolishness , Harry Paul plunged overboard , and swam to the bathing-place , where he dressed ; and , saving that he was suffering from a peculiar sensation of stiffness , he was not much the worse", "context_len": 69}}}, "rubs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The plebe begins to get used to it after the first week or so , when the stiffness rubs off , and then a certain amount of rivalry begins to spring up among various squads , and everybody settles down to the business of learning", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "When maidens of different antecedents , rank , age , temperament , and -- in minor matters -- opinions , are brought together in closest proximity , in a climate which tries the temper , there is at least a possibility of some slight rubs , which without the soothing ointment brought by the Peacemaker may even develop into sores", "context_len": 60}}}, "squads": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "Although within range of hostile guns , the `` dump '' had so far escaped serious damage , To bring the supplies nearer the lines by mechanical transfer would be to court disaster , so every ounce of food had to be carried by squads detailed for that purpose", "context_len": 49}}}, "settles": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "Belden told me on the steamer , said Marie-Celeste , with the air of one who settles down for a good talk with a familiar friend , of some musician who heard some one strike two or three chords and then suddenly stop , and after that he ; could not get a wink of sleep till he jumped out of bed and rushed to his piano and struck the chord that belonged at the end of the others", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "And best of all , Chris himself is just the same thoughtful fellow he left them , and at once settles down to a general supervision of the farm , that leaves the old man free to smoke his brier-wood pipe and read the news from morning till night , if he cares to", "context_len": 54}}}, "consolidated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 48, "context": "They advised him to seek a wife among the princesses of Greece , and then to settle down quietly to the duties of domestic life , and to the government of his kingdom for a few years ; then , when every thing should have become settled and consolidated in Greece , and his family was established in the hearts of his countrymen , he could leave Macedon more safely", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Bowen 's map it appears that Chicasa Indians also lived on Savannah river ( above the Yuchi ) for some time , and many Cheroki must have lived within the boundaries of the consolidated Creek confederacy", "context_len": 36}}}, "graveyard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was wandering through the graveyard , which is situated in a lonely portion of the post , way off in the northwestern corner", "context_len": 24}}}, "dejectedly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "Some heroes , West Point 's bravest , lie buried there , and Indian was dejectedly wondering if those same heroes would ever have stuck through plebe days in barracks if they had had a drill master like that `` red-headed coyote , '' Chick Spencer", "context_len": 46}}}, "toadying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Bull Harris smiled benignly upon his toadying echo , while the rest of the gang nodded approvingly", "context_len": 17}}}, "hie": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "And swiftly , forsooth , will I hie me to my companions and inform them of this insufferable indignity", "context_len": 19}}}, "insufferable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "And swiftly , forsooth , will I hie me to my companions and inform them of this insufferable indignity", "context_len": 19}}}, "brambles": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "She had climbed up the bank to reach a particularly luscious looking cluster from the long , trailing brambles overhead , when voices below attracted her attention , and , peeping through the hedge , she saw two horsemen riding slowly along in the bridle-path beneath", "context_len": 46}}}, "captors": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 26, "context": "If I am captured I trust that an opportunity of escape will soon present itself , for I should , if taken , conceal from my captors the fact that I understand their language , and should thus , if I could evade my guard , have every chance of escaping , as in a native dress I could meet and converse with those hunting for me , without their having a suspicion of my being the white slave for whom they were in search", "context_len": 85}}}, "witticism": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` He , he ! the only good Indian 's a dead Indian , he , he ! '' chimed in Baby , chuckling at his own witticism", "context_len": 28}}}, "rounder": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Poor Indian 's fat , round eyes stared wider and rounder every minute ; his equally round , red face grew redder , and his gasping exclamations more frequent and violent", "context_len": 31}}}, "trembles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` See , he trembles ! Behold how the victim pales ! ''", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "My hand trembles with the heat , for it is warm walking at this hour , and I always walk fast when I walk in the streets alone", "context_len": 28}}}, "pales": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` See , he trembles ! Behold how the victim pales ! ''", "context_len": 13}}}, "ascends": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "One first ascends five steps to the substantial platform on which the house is built , and then twenty-nine steps to our apartments", "context_len": 23}}}, "advancing": {"freq": 54, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 53, "word_index": 76, "context": "Dewey was debating whether discretion were not really the whole of valor , and whether it were not better to `` run away and live to fight -- or run away -- some other day ; '' and Indian was actually doubling up his fat little fists about to strike the first blow in his fat little life ; when suddenly came a shout behind them , and a moment later a strong hand seized the advancing yearling by the back of his collar and flung him head first to the ground", "context_len": 92}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In front and in the rear of the advancing German infantry the hail of shells descended like a giant twin portcullis , while the intervening space was thick with shrapnel", "context_len": 30}}}, "dimples": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "At this Bessie broke into a little low laugh , and the dimples showed themselves at the corners of Maggie 's mouth , though she pursed up her lips , and drew down her eyebrows in her determination not to smile", "context_len": 41}}}, "coil": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 76, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}}}, "articulate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was no articulate cry , simply a wild howl", "context_len": 10}}}, "bullfrog": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "And it died into a gasp that Bull Harris described as `` the sigh of a homesick bullfrog", "context_len": 18}}}, "wail": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 56, "context": "'Oh , no , no ! We ca n't go back over that hateful plank again and sit watching the water come up ! I would rather be drowned here than there ! Oh , Father , Father ! do come and find us ! ' And Bobby sat down upon the ground with such a wail of despair that Peggy at last lost her self-control and found herself joining in his sobs", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 115, "context": "peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to laugh at the supernatural , she did not feel quite so brave as she pretended , and found little cold shivers stealing down her back when the wind rose suddenly , and began to wail round the cottage like a hungry creature waiting to be let in", "context_len": 128}}}, "survey": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 12, "context": "He strolled about the open space , pausing now and then to survey through his glasses the snowy peaks which towered far into the sky to the westward , or at the lower hills to the north , where the gnarled pine , the dwarfed cedar , the rushing torrents and the gorges made up one of the many wild regions which abound over thousands of square miles of area", "context_len": 70}}}, "likened": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "In fact , it can be well likened to the dungeons of the castle at Newark ( which was dismantled by the rebels ) , where a dismal hole some twenty feet below ground is only accessible by a rope ladder dropped through a narrow opening above", "context_len": 47}}}, "calliope": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "Each wail was louder and more unearthly , more mirth-provoking to the unpitying cadets , until at last , when Bull Harris finished and stepped back to survey his work , the frightened plebe could be likened to nothing less than a steam calliope", "context_len": 44}}}, "performer": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And so , without giving the performer chance to breathe even , they set to work diligently collecting sticks and leaves", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}}}, "collecting": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 53, "context": "At the same time that we learned , from a dying man left on board the ship the pirates captured yesterday , of the course they had taken , and were so enabled to follow them , we also learned that they were on their way to join a corsair fleet that was collecting at some point on the eastern side of Sardinia , with the intention of sweeping the coast of Italy", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 83, "context": "But if our Church is to go on growing , as we hope and pray that it may , what would be the advantage of having a tiny chapel , which would not comfortably accommodate ourselves in a fiery climate , and in which there would be no room at all for heathen spectators ? We should be wanting a second ; and how could we procure a second clergyman ? Please thank dear Fred very , very much for his kindness in collecting , and assure him that we wish to make the money go as far as possible", "context_len": 100}}}, "unabated": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "But I soon discovered that even this small measure of mercy was to be denied me ; food and drink were indeed to be served out to us -- in order , as I surmised , that we might meet the ordeal in store for us with unabated strength -- but the night was to be passed , as the day had been , secured to the sacrificial post , exposed , naked and helpless , to the elements and to the myriad insect plagues that attacked us unceasingly", "context_len": 89}}}, "prolong": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "Supposedly the string of barges was `` manned '' by women , the diminishing group halting at each barge to prolong the conversation before the crews boarded their respective boats , until , by the clatter almost overhead , the fugitives knew that the last barge had received its complement -- two , perhaps three , buxom and stolid German women", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Well , he continued , I needn \u2019 t prolong the story , for it is always the same", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 101, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}}}, "perspiring": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "So , while the red-faced , perspiring victim panted , grunted , howled , and wriggled , they piled the wood about him with exasperating slowness , rearranging , inspecting , and discussing the probable effect of each and every stick of wood they laid on", "context_len": 46}}}, "exasperating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "He strode along behind the flying form of the little minister , inwardly furious at the saintly and exasperating person who forced him to seek out the company that was precisely the most painful and uncongenial to him , when his one sole idea was to hide himself in solitude like some wounded animal and there wrestle down the grief and horror that possessed him", "context_len": 65}}}, "slowness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "So , while the red-faced , perspiring victim panted , grunted , howled , and wriggled , they piled the wood about him with exasperating slowness , rearranging , inspecting , and discussing the probable effect of each and every stick of wood they laid on", "context_len": 46}}}, "rearranging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "So , while the red-faced , perspiring victim panted , grunted , howled , and wriggled , they piled the wood about him with exasperating slowness , rearranging , inspecting , and discussing the probable effect of each and every stick of wood they laid on", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Did the Queen 's mother come out and ask you to come in ? whispered Marie-Celeste , detaining Albert by main force , while she straightened his necktie and gave his hopelessly frowsy curls a rearranging touch", "context_len": 37}}}, "inspecting": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "So , while the red-faced , perspiring victim panted , grunted , howled , and wriggled , they piled the wood about him with exasperating slowness , rearranging , inspecting , and discussing the probable effect of each and every stick of wood they laid on", "context_len": 46}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 23, "context": "But who has kept all dese old tings so long , and how old are dey anyway ? asked more practical Albert , inspecting with curious , critical gaze a little necklace of hammered gold and silver which Miss Belmore had dropped into his lap as one of the few treasures his rather inquisitive touch would not damage", "context_len": 58}}}, "fagots": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was done , at last , however , and the result was a great pile of fagots surrounding and half covering the unfortunate lad", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "They were fagots selected as being the driest that could be found in the dry and sun-parched clearing", "context_len": 18}}}, "driest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Its waters are pure , its current gentle ; it runs about two miles an hour , and has 15-18 feet depth in the driest season of the year", "context_len": 29}}}, "crescendo": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Indian responded with a crescendo in C minor", "context_len": 8}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "The noise grew louder and louder , so that it resembled the squealing of a kitten , and all four began to turn over the moss with eager fingers , till , with a cry , Archie drew out a small round ball of dried grass , about the size of Bobby 's fist , from which issued such crescendo squeaks that there could be no mistake as to the locality of the sound", "context_len": 74}}}, "minor": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "It was a quiet spot , with long gardens leading to old-fashioned houses on the one side , and the tall palings of a cricket-field on the other -- just the spot where nobody would be likely to come along and make a disturbance , and so evidently Jones minor seemed to think , for he held Bobby pinned against the wall with one hand , while with the other he amused himself by tweaking his ears , pulling his hair , and any other tortures which his ingenious mind could suggest at the moment", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 5, "context": "'Some days are set in minor key , and the Lord calls me where the waves run high ; but so long as I am sure it is the Lord , what does it matter ? Not one good thing has failed of all that He has promised , and soldiers do not mind a few sword thrusts when they are marching to victory", "context_len": 64}}}, "torch": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 15, "context": "The captain will then light the train of powder in the hold , throw the torch among the straw , and see that , if possible , the men fasten her to the Turk ; but if this can not be done , it is not essential , for in the confusion the enemy will not be able to get out of the way of the fire ship as it drives down against her", "context_len": 74}}}, "mocks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` He mocks us ! '' cried the spokesman", "context_len": 9}}}, "scorns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` He scorns to answer", "context_len": 5}}}, "untrustworthy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Indeed , there is no telling how long the hand-shaking might have lasted but that the hats proved untrustworthy in the stiff northern wind that was blowing , Chris catching his on the fly and Marie-Celeste 's saved almost as narrowly", "context_len": 41}}}, "dryness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Bull 's `` management '' proved rather untrustworthy ; for Bull had forgotten to take into account the dryness of the twigs , and also another factor", "context_len": 27}}}, "factor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Bull 's `` management '' proved rather untrustworthy ; for Bull had forgotten to take into account the dryness of the twigs , and also another factor", "context_len": 27}}}, "extinguishing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "There were two of them ; one tore a knife from his pocket and cut the rope in a dozen places , the other flung off his jacket and wrapped it quickly about Indian 's feet , extinguishing the flames", "context_len": 40}}}, "bonds": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 64, "context": "although I had passed through the day unscathed , so far as actual bodily injury was concerned , it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond", "context_len": 128}}}, "extinguish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "He had tugged at his bonds savagely ; when he was cut free he had torn loose from the friendly stranger who had knelt to extinguish the fire , and made a savage rush at the badly scared cadets", "context_len": 39}}}, "windmill": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "Miss Forster opened her eyes wide enough , as it was , to see a neat telephone , made of two empty cocoa-tins and a piece of waxed string , fixed up between the house and the stables , while a small windmill on the scullery roof turned the coffee-grinder in the kitchen , to the huge amazement and delight of the cook", "context_len": 63}}}, "hurricane": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 48, "context": "And very attentively everybody listened to the story , as I told it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached", "context_len": 128}}}, "emerge": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "He was restless and uneasy at the thought that , as his father was in the prime and vigor of manhood , many long years must elapse before he could emerge from this confined and subordinate condition", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "At last , when he was beginning to think that Sobrinini had again laid a spell on him and that the voices were ghostly voices , to be heard by none but himself , he saw a dark form emerge from the mist and after it another and another", "context_len": 49}}}, "diabolical": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "She was a long , rakish , yellow-sided craft , evidently built for speed , and her audacity puzzled us mightily ; but knowing the diabolical cunning of these freebooters , we were determined to take no chance of a surprise", "context_len": 41}}}, "shucks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Ye can steal back and forth on the inside of the circle of wagons , fur that will help keep yer peepers peeled , but ba \u2019 rs and beavers ! no man \u2019 s wuth shucks if he can \u2019 t stay awake till midnight , and them as comes on duty then will have had \u2019 nough sleep to last \u2019 em till daylight", "context_len": 66}}}, "bespoke": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 84, "context": "`` Quoth he , `` Speak ; what is the news ? '' And she said to him , `` The Sultan hath gone from his promise to thee in the matter of his daughter , the Lady Bedrulbudour , for that this very night the Vizier 's son goeth in to her ; and indeed methought at the time , [ 385 ] O my son , the Vizier would change the Sultan 's mind , even as I told thee that he bespoke him privily before me", "context_len": 89}}}, "possessing": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 35, "context": "The events , however , that had taken place during the expedition had impressed him greatly with the utility of a knowledge of Turkish , for had it not been for Sir John Boswell 's possessing some acquaintance with the language , it would have been impossible to communicate with the rowers of their boat , or to have arranged the plan by which they had escaped the pirates", "context_len": 69}}}, "drawl": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` The taller was a big slouchy-looking chap in clothes that evidently bespoke the farmer , and possessing a drawl which quite as clearly indicated the situation of the farm -- the prairies", "context_len": 33}}}, "despise": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The companion was even less awe-inspiring , for one had to look at him but an instant to see that he was one of the creatures whom all well-regulated boys despise -- a dude", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 28, "context": "And now , what can we say ? How can we hope to win our foes to God and Christ when we set at naught his precepts and despise his example ? ''", "context_len": 33}}}, "dude": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 43, "context": "As a fact there was but little choice in the matter , it was fight or die with the two , for at the same instant Gus Murray , wild with rage , had leaped forward and made a savage lunge at the dude", "context_len": 44}}}, "compulsion": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "He wore a high collar , ridiculously high ; he was slender and delicate looking , with the correct Fifth Avenue stoop to his shoulders and an attitude to his arms which showed that he had left his cane behind only on compulsion when he `` struck the Point", "context_len": 49}}}, "chastise": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Dye know , I ca n't see why we plebes suffer so , bah Jove ! I was thinking aw , do n't ye know , if some of us would band together we could -- aw -- chastise the deuced cadets and -- -- ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "daunted": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The rest of the crowd , not in the least daunted by Murray 's miss , rushed in to the attack ; and a moment later a wild scrimmage was in progress , a scrimmage which defied the eye to comprehend and the pen to describe", "context_len": 46}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 55, "context": "Year after year she persisted in her steady round of Zenana visits ; not , like the average district-visitor of England , going once a fortnight or once a week into her district , -- which was the whole city of Batala , -- but day after day giving hours to the work , never daunted because results seemed small , never apparently even tempted to throw up her arduous task in despair", "context_len": 73}}}, "scrimmage": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` I do n't say anything against the heathen ; he 's squarer than many a white man I 've met with , and he 's worth a dozen such men as Bill Mosely and Tom Hadley ; but , all the same , he would n't be much in a scrimmage", "context_len": 52}}}, "hammers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Added to the dismal noises came the dull thud of the carpenters \u2019 hammers and mallets as they drove plugs into the shot holes betwixt wind and water , while the creaking of the ship \u2019 s pumps betokened that she was leaking freely", "context_len": 44}}}, "tactics": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 69, "context": "It was always the same , no matter what the hour of day or night that I might choose to start upon my expeditions ; no surprise was ever displayed at my eccentricity in the choice of times , but I simply could not contrive to elude notice ; and at length it was borne in upon me that if I wished to effect my escape I must adopt tactics of a totally different kind", "context_len": 75}}}, "acrobat": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The messenger leaped like an acrobat into place , caught up the reins , touched with his spurs the flanks of the animal , which instantly responded with a bound , and was off on a headlong run", "context_len": 38}}}, "punch": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "He \u2019 s a real smart boy , too , said John ( giving Fred a punch under the table ) ; he catches all the fish they eat , and a good many to sell , and has made lots of baskets , and sent them to the West Indies by father", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` I 'll punch your head ! '' he cried to Tom , who promptly ensconced himself behind the bed", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "'She lade the villin flat on the erth , and I just wish you cud have seen her punch his hed , ' he wrote", "context_len": 25}}}, "plowed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "Jane , with her rugged , middle-aged face , baggy blouse , hob-nailed shoes and man 's hat , was so unfeminine a figure as she plowed and planted her little vega , that some village wag had once referred to her as `` Annie Laurie", "context_len": 46}}}, "chronicle": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The writer wishes it were his privilege to chronicle the fact that the two put the eight to flight ; or that Indian , having put the Baby `` to sleep , '' returned to perform yet greater prodigies of valor", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Every boy who knows the lure of exploring and who loves to rig up huts and caves and tree-houses to fortify himself against imaginary enemies will enjoy these books , for they give a vivid chronicle of the doings and inventions of a group of boys who are shipwrecked and have to make themselves snug and safe in tropical islands where the dangers are too real for play", "context_len": 68}}}, "prodigies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "The writer wishes it were his privilege to chronicle the fact that the two put the eight to flight ; or that Indian , having put the Baby `` to sleep , '' returned to perform yet greater prodigies of valor", "context_len": 41}}}, "valor": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "Dewey was debating whether discretion were not really the whole of valor , and whether it were not better to `` run away and live to fight -- or run away -- some other day ; '' and Indian was actually doubling up his fat little fists about to strike the first blow in his fat little life ; when suddenly came a shout behind them , and a moment later a strong hand seized the advancing yearling by the back of his collar and flung him head first to the ground", "context_len": 92}}}, "providence": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "Still I am inclined to believe , even at this stage of the proceeding , that our coming over here this summer has indeed been a beautiful providence and a few weeks later , for good and sufficient reasons , there was not a shadow of doubt on that score left in the mind of any one", "context_len": 57}}}, "wilted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "The second bud had half opened into another scragly , stunted flower , about which not even the most loving eyes could see the slightest beauty , and , in spite of the care which Maggie and Bessie had given them , the leaves of both plants were wilted and drooping", "context_len": 51}}}, "conquer": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 124, "context": "that if he wished to marry his daughter he could do it without his consent ; as to the ransom , he was not in want of money ; in respect to Darius 's offering to give him up all west of the Euphrates , it was absurd for a man to speak of giving what was no longer his own ; that he had crossed too many seas in his military expeditions , since he left Macedon , to feel any concern about the rivers that he might find in his way ; and that he should continue to pursue Darius wherever he might retreat in search of safety and protection , and he had no fear but that he should find and conquer him at last", "context_len": 128}}}, "reinforcements": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 81, "context": "I do not , of course , wish you to interfere in any way in the conduct of the defence , in which you will take such share as you can ; but you are specially to observe how matters go , and if you see that the knights are pressed and in sore need of assistance to enable them to hold the post , you will at once bring the news to me , and I will hurry there with reinforcements", "context_len": 82}}}, "traditional": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 56, "context": "At last the little toilettes were completed , even to the reformation of Albert 's curls around an ivory curling stick ; and with embroidered dress and well-starched kilt none the worse for the decorous experiences of the morning , they emerged from the little boudoir as spick and span as from the depths of the traditional bandbox", "context_len": 58}}}, "battering": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "The first warning of the arrival of reinforcements was not the traditional trumpet call , nor the roll of a drum , nor even the tramp of soldiers , but a muttered `` Wow ! '' This was followed by Texas himself , bursting through the bushes like a battering ram", "context_len": 51}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Again and again her men went battering against the wall of flesh and blood , until , with what seemed a superhuman effort , Holly Cross was shoved over the line for a touch-down", "context_len": 34}}}, "warpath": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 45, "context": "Reassured now , but still not abandoning his habitual caution , he moved forward until from the shelter of a bush he could see encamped in a clearing , while they prepared their midday meal , a large party of natives , evidently on the warpath , as they were armed to the teeth", "context_len": 54}}}, "odds": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 64, "context": "I say not but that by a mighty effort of all Christendom , Palestine might be wrested from the infidels , as it was wrested before ; but the past shows us that while men or nations can be stirred to enthusiasm for a time , the fire does not last long , and once again the faithful few would be overwhelmed by the odds that would be brought against them , while Europe looked on impassive , if not indifferent", "context_len": 81}}}, "damages": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "I should like nothing better than a stay there myself , but there is much to do to repair the damages caused by the siege , and to place the city in a state of defence should the Turks again lay siege to it ; and methinks Mahomet will not sit down quietly under the heavy reverse his troops have met with", "context_len": 62}}}, "hostilities": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 43, "context": "From time immemorial the walk had been barred to freshmen unless , in the annual rush , they succeeded in defeating the sophomores , and , as this seldom occurred , few freshmen used the walk , save on Sundays , when all hostilities were suspended , in honor of the day", "context_len": 52}}}, "sesquipedalian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Which , '' added the Parson 's solemn voice , `` might be somewhat more classically expressed by the sesquipedalian Hellenic vocable -- ahem ! -- Megalacephalomania", "context_len": 28}}}, "vocable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Which , '' added the Parson 's solemn voice , `` might be somewhat more classically expressed by the sesquipedalian Hellenic vocable -- ahem ! -- Megalacephalomania", "context_len": 28}}}, "gratis": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Haven , an ex-editor , had written up a good `` puff '' for a local paper , inserted gratis an exciting comment and anticipation in reference to the impending sale , and Darry and Bob had printed fifteen hundred dodgers on their home press , very neat and presentable in appearance , and these had been judiciously distributed for miles around , and posted up in stores and depots", "context_len": 70}}}, "coincidence": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was a pure coincidence that the New Zealander and he were on the same boat ; it might be that the latter 's visit to the bridge was utterly unconnected with him", "context_len": 33}}}, "factory": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 11, "context": "At the outskirts of the town was a large , newly-erected factory , from which Gotha machines , their wings folded for transit , were being taken away in large motor-lorries , while sandwiched between the building and the outskirts of the town proper was a large barbed-wire compound within which were rows of wooden huts", "context_len": 56}}}, "prattled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Dewey could have prattled on at this merry rate for an hour , for he knew more jokes -- good ones -- and could make up more bad ones on the spur of the moment than half a dozen ordinary mortals", "context_len": 41}}}, "doomsday": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Dewey could have prattled on that way till doomsday with unfailing good humor", "context_len": 13}}}, "unfailing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "But through the long dull hours of sewing in Aunt Isham 's dressing-room , her unfailing treasure of consolation was in repeating to herself all the teachings she had received from her grandfather -- words that could never be breathed aloud in Madam Isham 's house ; of liberty , and the rights of the people to representation and civil justice , teachings that were drawn from writings as far asunder as Bishop Taylor 's `` Liberty of Prophesying , '' and Mr", "context_len": 83}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "The days now dragged themselves away upon leaden feet , yet -- apparent paradox -- with frightful rapidity ; for I now no longer had a household to attend to my wants ; my meals were brought to me with unfailing regularity by my guards , but they had apparently been forbidden to communicate with me , for not a word could I get out of them , good , bad , or indifferent", "context_len": 74}}}, "guyed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "But by this time Bull had perceived that he was being guyed , and he turned away with an angry exclamation", "context_len": 21}}}, "yew": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` I 'm 'bliged to yew fellows , '' put in the farmer , getting up from his seat with a lazy groan", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "There \u2019 s rest beneath the yew ; I know There \u2019 s deeper Rest in realms above ; The Saviour \u2019 s Arm the valley through Will me uphold with strengthening love ; My hope His Righteousness ; my buckler , faith ; Why should I fear to tread the shades of death ?", "context_len": 55}}}, "immaculate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` And mine 's Chauncey Van Rensallear Mount-Bonsall , do n't ye know , '' said the other , putting on his immaculate white gloves", "context_len": 25}}}, "cuff": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Chauncey 's cuff button was found at last -- he vowed he would n't go to dinner without it -- and then the party started in earnest , the two strangers giving a graphic and characteristic account of the scrimmage we have just witnessed", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "She laid down her work to wipe her eyes , and , observing two of her sons grappling in fraternal war at the house corner , she arose to cuff each one impartially , exclaiming , `` Ea , muchachos ! You fight before my very eyes , eh ? Take that ! and that ! '' Waddling reluctantly back to her sewing , she saw Lola standing in the white-pillared porch of the big adobe house beyond , and a gleam of inspiration crossed the se\u00f1ora 's dark , fat face", "context_len": 92}}}, "deuced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` Dye know , I ca n't see why we plebes suffer so , bah Jove ! I was thinking aw , do n't ye know , if some of us would band together we could -- aw -- chastise the deuced cadets and -- -- ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "characteristic": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 99, "context": "The next thing , and sometimes a better thing than knowing your favorite author , is to know how and where he lives ; and it was a matter of supreme delight to Marie-Celeste that henceforth when she should open Lewis Carroll 's books she should be able to picture him working away here in his study , and just as he really looked , too , for by chance or accidents full-length photograph stood on the mantel , which Dorothy , from her visita few years before , was able to pronounce an excellent likeness , and very characteristic", "context_len": 100}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 50, "context": "Aunt Helen sometimes thought the two must have got changed , and that Peggy should have been the boy and Bobby the girl ; for though the latter was not without courage , it was certainly Peggy who had the most of that enterprising spirit which is generally thought a characteristic of the masculine mind", "context_len": 55}}}, "outvoted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` We do , we do , '' cried all but Elfwyn and Herstan ; but they were utterly outvoted , and the order was given to the captain of the hus-carles to arrest Alfgar", "context_len": 35}}}, "unobtrusive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "Fanny was par excellence the gentle sister ; very sweet , very unselfish ; always the one who would silently take the most uncomfortable chair in the room ; always the one to put others forward , yet in so quiet and unobtrusive a fashion that the fact was often not remarked until afterwards", "context_len": 54}}}, "graduated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Horner did not believe in showy commencements , and when the girls were graduated they simply received their diplomas after a few simple exercises , and then the school was dismissed", "context_len": 31}}}, "holy": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 61, "context": "And when I am not always there perhaps you 'll think of me , and how faithful I am to you , and how truly , truly I love you ; and you will think , too , of what you are to me , a poem , yes , that 's the right word , a beautiful poem , something holy , something that makes a new man of me , the most lovely bit of a thing I ever saw", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` `` Ay , my lord , '' replied the Maugrabin , `` I am indeed a stranger and arrived but yesternight in this your town ; wherefore I beseech thee tell me of the divine gifts of this holy woman and where her place is , for that I have fallen into a calamity and would fain go to her and crave her of prayer , so haply God ( to whom belong might and majesty ) may deliver me from my stress , by means of her intercession", "context_len": 90}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Now the saints help thy lying tongue , '' thus irreverently he spoke , `` do holy men travel like robbers in dead of night ? ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "infected": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "The worst of it was that he infected Nancy with his alarms to such an extent that she could scarcely be persuaded to put her nose out at the back-door after dusk ( except on her evening out , when her sweetheart came to fetch her ) , which was distinctly inconvenient if a pail of water were wanted during the evening , or she happened to have left the coal-box standing in the yard", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Brett were strictly forbidden to have any communication with the infected house ; Rosamund and Irene were equally forbidden to go near Sunnyside", "context_len": 23}}}, "surrender": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 28, "context": "On the day after Paleologus , who was himself a renegade Greek , had established his camp , he sent in a herald to summon the city to surrender , at the same time making lavish promises that the lives and property of the native population should be respected , and that they should be allowed to continue to reside there , to enjoy the full exercise of their religion and of all other rights they possessed", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 42, "context": "Only one of the enemy reached our deck , and he was dragged on board by the clever cast of a running bowline thrown by a seaman , who , seizing his captive in his powerful grasp , demanded and obtained his surrender at the point of a gleaming knife , while his comrades roared with laughter at the hapless Dutchman \u2019 s discomfiture", "context_len": 64}}}, "et": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 17, "context": "[ Footnote 555 : Here Galland says , `` Il entra dans le lien le plus fameux et le plus frequente par les personnel de grande distinction , ou l'on s'assembloit pour boire d'une certaine boisson chance qui luy etoit connue des son premier voyage", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "I don \u2019 t s \u2019 pose y \u2019 u have et anything since y \u2019 u was locked up ; but I cal \u2019 lated you \u2019 d git off , an \u2019 I \u2019 ve got Sadie tendin \u2019 a big roast , an \u2019 we \u2019 ll have a feed that \u2019 ll give y \u2019 u injunjesshun , which I guess y \u2019 u can stand once if Joel , my late departed , could endure it all his born days", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 93, "context": "of the Cabinet des Fees ( printed in 1793 , though antedated 1788 and 1789 ) do not form the first edition of Chavis and Cazotte 's so-called Sequel , which was in 1793 added , by way of supplement , to the Cabinet des Fees , having been first published in 1788 ( two years after the completion-in thirty-seven volumes-of that great storehouse of supernatural fiction ) under the title of `` Les Veillees Persanes '' or `` Les Veillees du Sultan Schahriar avec la Sultane Scheherazade , histoires incroyables , amusantes et morales , traduites par M", "context_len": 99}}}, "decorum": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` ' Veni , vidi , vici , ' '' remarked the Parson , `` or else ' Dulce et decorum est pro patria mori , ' in the immortal words of Horace , poet of the Sabine farm", "context_len": 39}}}, "pro": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` ' Veni , vidi , vici , ' '' remarked the Parson , `` or else ' Dulce et decorum est pro patria mori , ' in the immortal words of Horace , poet of the Sabine farm", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "He would make friends with no one forward , and sometimes would even show his large white teeth upon a too familiar caress from the captain , his master pro tem", "context_len": 31}}}, "expresses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "Since Indian character expresses itself in the most pronounced , self-willed independence , the power of the authorities was more of a persuasive than of a constraining or commanding nature", "context_len": 30}}}, "cultivation": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "To those friends who thought he lost caste thereby he had always the same argument -- that he saw no reason why the cultivation of fields should counteract the habits of refinement and good breeding to which he had been reared ; that in the colonies educated gentlemen set to work to labour with their hands , and are thought none the worse of : so why not in England , where land is good and markets are plentiful , especially when it involved the keeping of a fine old property which had been in the family for so many hundreds of years ?", "context_len": 104}}}, "slang": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}}}, "verb": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 47, "context": "[ Footnote 638 : Keniz , a word which I can not find in any dictionary , but which appears to be the past participle ( in the secondary form for mecnouz , as ketil , slain , for mertoul , ) of keneza , a lost verb of which only the fourth form acneza , he drank from a cup ( kinz ) , survives , and to mean `` something drunk from a cup", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Some forms of the finite verb represent true verbs , while others , like the Creek forms , with tcha- , tchi- , pu- , etc", "context_len": 26}}}, "freshest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` By George , he 's the freshest plebe that ever struck this place ! ''", "context_len": 16}}}, "benignly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Bull Harris smiled benignly upon his toadying echo , while the rest of the gang nodded approvingly", "context_len": 17}}}, "confound": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` That fellow Mallory seems to get the best of us everything we try ; confound him ! ''", "context_len": 19}}}, "indefinite": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "This suggestion was good , only rather indefinite , which indefiniteness was remarked by one of the crowd , Merry Vance , the cadet who had interposed the same objection before", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 46, "context": "I could plan nothing , I could hope nothing , but it seemed to me that my journey to Switzerland was indefinitely postponed , and that my future from being settled in every detail , month , week , hour , and all , was as indefinite and vague and shadowy as though I were standing on the brink of the other world", "context_len": 63}}}, "indefiniteness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "This suggestion was good , only rather indefinite , which indefiniteness was remarked by one of the crowd , Merry Vance , the cadet who had interposed the same objection before", "context_len": 31}}}, "whitish": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "He still retained his flowing lovelocks , while the lower part of his weather-worn face was adorned by a greyish beard of Van Dyck cut , which failed to hide a portion of a long , whitish scar that extended from his left eyebrow to his cheek bone -- the legacy of a pike-thrust in the sanguinary encounter of Cropredy Bridge", "context_len": 61}}}, "curve": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 14, "context": "Just as we reached the summit , where the road makes a vast horseshoe curve round the dark , forbidding cavity known as the Devil \u2019 s Punch Bowl , a heavy rainstorm came on , blotting out the horizon , while a vivid flash of lightning , followed at a short interval by a tremendous clap of thunder , startled our horses , and , be it confessed , ourselves as well", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "It stood on the high shore of Coosa river , forty-six feet above its waters , where the two rivers approach each other within a quarter of a mile , to curve out again", "context_len": 34}}}, "doubling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 41, "context": "Dewey was debating whether discretion were not really the whole of valor , and whether it were not better to `` run away and live to fight -- or run away -- some other day ; '' and Indian was actually doubling up his fat little fists about to strike the first blow in his fat little life ; when suddenly came a shout behind them , and a moment later a strong hand seized the advancing yearling by the back of his collar and flung him head first to the ground", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "137 , speaks of the `` sepulchres or tumuli of the Yamasees who were here slain in the last decisive battle , the Creeks having driven them to this point , between the doubling of the river ( St", "context_len": 39}}}, "loosen": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 72, "context": "In his abject misery and dread , Mark Penelly saw , in the stern gaze before him , anger and a vindictive desire for revenge ; he saw therein fierce hate , and an implacable , unchanging condemnation ; he felt that Harry was sustaining him there where he had dragged him to make his sufferings more acute , and that , after holding him up for a while , he would loosen his hold , causing him to sink at once into the deep water by the rocks , and be swept away by the tremendous current", "context_len": 98}}}, "authorities": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 108, "context": "you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been seized while travelling with a safe conduct from the governor of Syria", "context_len": 128}}}, "defying": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Defying her as usual , and trying how far he could go , without punishment , he had called her `` bad old sing , '' and many other naughty names ; but finding this did not bring the expected scolding , he had pulled off first one and then the other of his slippers and thrown them at the old lady", "context_len": 62}}}, "discouraging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` That 's what I did ! '' responded Murray , brightening up at the mention of a less discouraging incident", "context_len": 21}}}, "apologize": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 23, "context": "It seems that opposite the Nag 's Head Inn the Church of England has built a tiny edifice , and as though to apologize for the apparent unreasonableness of building any church there whatsoever , they have made a most miniature affair of it", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Then the father said , Joseph , I had intended to whip you ; but it \u2019 s a hard and unpleasant duty , and I \u2019 m inclined to try you once more without it , if you \u2019 ll apologize and make a new promise not to go fishing again without my permission", "context_len": 55}}}, "flatly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "Here Father Adhelm and I landed , and , giving Wiglaf our blessing , bade him depart in peace , which the good soul flatly refused to do until assured of our safety", "context_len": 33}}}, "assembly": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 23, "context": "The spectacle of the body , and the statement of the king , produced a scene of great and universal excitement in the assembly , and this excitement was raised to the highest pitch by the announcement which Alexander now made , that he had reason to believe that Philotas and his father Parmenio , officers who had enjoyed his highest favor , and in whom he had placed the most unbounded confidence , were the authors and originators of the whole design", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Then , on the following Saturday , they led him to their hustings ( or assembly ) , and shamefully slaughtered him , casting upon him bones and the horns of oxen", "context_len": 32}}}, "dissolved": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 64, "context": "heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we will set over you guards to", "context_len": 128}}}, "evolving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The baby 's sweet little brain , evolving the interesting problem , struck an idea which , so to speak , brought down the house", "context_len": 25}}}, "incredulously": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 15, "context": "But when he came to M he paused suddenly ; he gazed at the list incredulously , then cleared his throat , took a long foreboding breath and began :", "context_len": 30}}}, "demerits": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 21, "context": "After the first shock of horror had passed the crowd had sat down and made a calculation ; they found fifty-five demerits due that day , which , together with ten previously given , left thirty-five to go , and then -- why it made them sick to think of what would happen !", "context_len": 54}}}, "vowing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "prevails": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The fable has led to a proverb which prevails in every language in Europe , by which all extravagant and unheard-of exertions to accomplish an end is said to be a piling of Pelion upon Ossa", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I wonder whether the same remissness prevails elsewhere", "context_len": 8}}}, "inflexible": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "These demerits are assigned according to a regular and inflexible schedule ; thus for being late at roll call , a minor offense , a cadet receives two demerits , while a serious offense , such as disobedience of orders or sitting down on post while on sentry duty , brings ten units of trouble in its wake", "context_len": 58}}}, "schedule": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "These demerits are assigned according to a regular and inflexible schedule ; thus for being late at roll call , a minor offense , a cadet receives two demerits , while a serious offense , such as disobedience of orders or sitting down on post while on sentry duty , brings ten units of trouble in its wake", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It is n't schedule , you know", "context_len": 7}}}, "receives": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "These demerits are assigned according to a regular and inflexible schedule ; thus for being late at roll call , a minor offense , a cadet receives two demerits , while a serious offense , such as disobedience of orders or sitting down on post while on sentry duty , brings ten units of trouble in its wake", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "How astonished we should be if French or drawing masters asked such questions in England ! I have been asked what salary my nephew receives", "context_len": 25}}}, "units": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "Wounded Anzacs , on receiving the ten-days ' leave in England before rejoining their units , frequently make railway journeys running into hundreds of miles simply for the Purpose of `` looking up '' a remote blood relation in the Old Country , In Selwyn 's case his relation lived at a small town on the shores of False Bay , a distance of about twenty miles from Cape Town", "context_len": 70}}}, "forwarded": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 59, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}}}, "assigns": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "The report is read out after parade that same day and posted in a certain place the next day ; and four days later the superintendent assigns the demerits in all cases where `` explanations '' have not been received", "context_len": 40}}}, "disarranged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The plans which had cost her so much , and which she had thought would give such satisfaction , were all disarranged", "context_len": 22}}}, "bedding": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 36, "context": "Men who had prospected for precious metals all summer in the mountains now bundled their pots and pans and blankets back to shelter for the winter ; the long-eared burros , lost in great rolls of bedding , stood about the tipple awaiting the result of their masters ' interviews with the mine boss , concerning work and the occupancy of any `` shack '' that might still be empty", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Most of the floor space was occupied by long trestle tables , while in one corner was the large stack of blankets and bedding upon which Malcolm and M'Turk had fallen", "context_len": 31}}}, "readjusted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "They readjusted things in the comfortable little phaeton and tucked the lap-robe about them in trimmer fashion , and then the ponies , feeling a tightening grasp on the lines , and intuitively conscious of a whip poised at an easily descending angle , wisely saw fit to make up for lost time", "context_len": 53}}}, "deemed": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 87, "context": "Thou sayst , O my mother , that I have no gift to present to the Sultan , according to the wont of the folk , whilst in fact I have by me a gift and an offering , the like whereof methinketh no king ever possessed , no , nor aught to match therewith ; for [ 338 ] thou must know , O my mother , that the fruits , which I brought in the purses [ 339 ] from the treasure and which I deemed glass or crystal , are very jewels , methinketh all the kings of the world may not compass the least of them , and I , of my companying with the jewellers , know that they are precious stones", "context_len": 127}}}, "marchers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Reports are made by the army officers , and also by cadets themselves , file closers , section marchers and others", "context_len": 21}}}, "consequent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Such seemed to be the substance of the complaint of the warriors on guard , from which Alfgar felt justified in believing in the escape of the theows , and the consequent deliverance of the people , if not of the place", "context_len": 42}}}, "parley": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "After a short parley at the door , the visitors were conducted by Nancy to the side-gate , where they all filed into the ruins , from whence a lively hum of conversation could plainly be heard", "context_len": 37}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "She took no time to parley , but raising a rifle which she carried at her side , aimed at the foremost ruffian , and an instant later a sharp pain in his shoulder told him he had been hit", "context_len": 40}}}, "squeamish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "She was generally sound , with no especial delicacy ; she did not suffer from any tendency to headache ; she was not fussy , or self-indulgent , or dainty as to her eating , or particular as to personal comforts , or squeamish as to her surroundings , or shy in making new friends , or afraid of toil and trouble", "context_len": 62}}}, "effectually": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 71, "context": "She was not long in discovering the landmarks of which she was in search , and a few minutes later the canoe was threading its tortuous way among a tangled mass of mangrove roots toward the solid bank of the river , landing upon which , we drew our light craft bodily up out of the water , concealing her beneath a broad overhanging mass of foliage which hid her so effectually that I would defy anybody but ourselves to find her", "context_len": 82}}}, "squelched": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "If Cadet Corporal Murray had any doubts about the necessity for putting this very dirty scheme into practice , or if his not over squeamish conscience was the least bit troubled by the prospect , something happened that same evening which effectually squelched such ideas", "context_len": 45}}}, "avenged": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` And as for you , you white-livered young cub , '' he added , addressing me , `` our captain here has given you to me , and , let me say , Dick Swyre will be avenged", "context_len": 39}}}, "snowdrift": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "His father , one stormy night a few years previous , had picked up Green half frozen to death in a snowdrift , where he had fallen in a drunken stupor", "context_len": 31}}}, "blithe": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Cadet Murray listened to this blithe recital with a frowning brow", "context_len": 11}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "For it is written of him that `` he was always as sour as if he had swallowed a pint of vinegar except when he was being shot at , -- and then he was as blithe as a schoolboy out for a holiday ''", "context_len": 45}}}, "recital": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "Merton would have spared the doctor the recital of any further disturbance until the morning ; but the principal , having observed all this , would not be put off ; the time was short , and if the matter were a serious one which required investigation , he must have knowledge of it at once", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` It 's -- let me see , we have called him Pip constantly -- '' he groped for the rest of the recital Jasper had given him one day concerning the lad so thrown upon their sympathy", "context_len": 38}}}, "dated": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 16, "context": "In a letter addressed by Commander Lemoyne d'Iberville to the Minister of the French Navy , dated from Bayogoulas , February 26th , 1700 , he states that an English fur-trader and Indian slave-jobber had just visited the Tonica , who are on a river emptying into the Mississippi , twenty leagues above the Taensa Indians , at some distance from the Chicasa , and 170 leagues from the Gulf of Mexico", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The sheet is dated 1844 ; and the passages are selected from a book of the day , called Savage Life and Scenes", "context_len": 23}}}, "inlaid": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Handsome bed , too -- an inlaid bed -- and it shut up whenever it was laid in , b'gee", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "His tunic was finely embroidered in colours around the neck , and was below of spotless white , secured by a belt richly gilded , whereon was a sheath for the dagger or knife , which was used for all occasions , whether in battle or in meal time , the haft being inlaid with precious stones", "context_len": 57}}}, "mortals": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "Dewey could have prattled on at this merry rate for an hour , for he knew more jokes -- good ones -- and could make up more bad ones on the spur of the moment than half a dozen ordinary mortals", "context_len": 41}}}, "discretion": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 124, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "confront": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "No other thought entered the head of the terrified Lewis than that Seabrooke himself had seen him at his shameful work , and that he had chosen to confront and convict him with it here in the presence of the rest of the school", "context_len": 44}}}, "timely": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 10, "context": "They were ever afterwards fully persuaded that but for his timely arrival they would have been robbed and murdered , and their bodies hidden away from their sorrowing relatives , like Eugene Aram 's victim , or the little princes in the Tower , and they felt quite an affection for Barlow in consequence , so much so that Bobby determined to wear out his boots as fast as possible , that he might put some business in the old man 's way", "context_len": 83}}}, "slink": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Occasionally Franklin caught sight of Sir William Keith on the street , The former governor would look uncomfortable and slink away", "context_len": 21}}}, "vials": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The angry yearling hurried back to camp , nursing his feelings as he went ; there he poured out the vials of his wrath into the ears of his two sympathetic companions , Bull and the Baby", "context_len": 37}}}, "bombshell": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "And with this , the indignant captain turned upon his heel , and strode off , leaving the yearlings as if a bombshell had landed in their midst", "context_len": 28}}}, "scheduled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "plebe for fair ; that he 'd set to work to make war on the yearlings , and had put them to rout in style ; also , incidentally , that he was scheduled to fight Billy Williams , the yearling 's pet athlete", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Seven days behind scheduled time the Awarua crawled into Port Stanley harbour", "context_len": 12}}}, "unconsciousness": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "His delight in it all , and na\u00efve unconsciousness of anything unusual in his behavior , are altogether irresistible , and Harold and Marie-Celeste , after a whispered conference , decide to suspend Knight-of-the-Garter reminiscences for the time being , and make the tour of the castle with him", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Got it this time , Malcolm ! '' murmured Dick faintly , as his chum knelt beside him , and with a queer smile on his face Selwyn passed into unconsciousness", "context_len": 32}}}, "whack": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "'Gee up ! Let her go ! Hooray ! ' yelled Bobby , striking such a whack on Brindle 's heaving side that she settled the matter by suddenly lying down to roll , and depositing her encumbrances in the miry ditch", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Smash ! went the plates on the floor , and whack went Big Chief 's empty hands against Dallas ' shoulders", "context_len": 21}}}, "presentably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "After that came the march to mess hall , the plebe company , which was by this time able to march presentably though rather stiffly , falling in behind the rest of the corps", "context_len": 34}}}, "ranks": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 62, "context": "All being now ready , the gate in the palisade was thrown open , a conch- shell was blown , and the waiting inhabitants began to pour into the enclosure with all the eagerness and excitement of an audience crowding into the unreserved portions of a theatre , and in a very short time the great square was full , the front ranks pressing close up to a cordon of armed guards that had been drawn round the circle of posts", "context_len": 81}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The soldier who goes on a forlorn-hope expedition ranks higher in the minds of men than the soldier who remains in camp ; and the pioneer is counted worthy of more honour than the settler", "context_len": 35}}}, "chambermaids": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The elevator boy or one of the chambermaids will show you where it is , if you would rather go there", "context_len": 21}}}, "policing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Cadets do their own room cleaning , `` policing , '' as it is called , and they do it well , too", "context_len": 23}}}, "barer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "A simpler , barer place than a room in barracks it would be hard to imagine", "context_len": 16}}}, "bedstead": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 15, "context": "The chamber occupied by the king was a long roomy place , containing a single bedstead of carved wood , surmounted by the usual distinctive canopy , from which tapestried hangings depended , and upon which scriptural subjects were woven ; the furniture of the room partook of the usual meagreness of the times", "context_len": 54}}}, "washstand": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "Besides the bed , there was in the room for furniture a broken-backed rocking-chair ; a small table with a split top , on which stood a common kerosene hand-lamp ; a dingy white earthen water pitcher and bowl the former with a circular piece broken out of its nose sitting on a washstand , made of a long box stood on one end , with a muslin curtain hanging in front of it", "context_len": 74}}}, "inventory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Bare white walls -- no pictures allowed -- and no wall paper -- a black fireplace , a plain table , an iron bedstead , a washstand , two chairs , and a window is about the entire inventory", "context_len": 39}}}, "precision": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 14, "context": "Seabrooke went about the business of the day with all his accustomed regularity and precision , but with a sort of defiant and I-am-going-to-stick-to-it air about him which in itself incited the other boys to covert thrusts and innuendoes tending to throw distrust upon his version of the story and to make known their thorough sympathy with Percy , not only for his loss , but also for the aspersions cast upon him by the young pupil-teacher", "context_len": 77}}}, "infringement": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "There is a `` bluebook '' -- learned by heart -- to tell where ; and there are penalties for every infringement", "context_len": 22}}}, "accordance": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 7, "context": "No post could have been more in accordance with the desire of Gervaise , for the portion of the wall defended by the English langue was far removed from the point selected by the Turks for their first attack , the sea front being defended half by the langue of Italy , and half by that of Castile", "context_len": 58}}}, "responsible": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 50, "context": "For the space of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very", "context_len": 128}}}, "insubordination": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "At length , Alexander , learning how strong and how extensive the spirit of insubordination was becoming , summoned his officers to his own tent , and then ordering the whole army to gather around , he went out to meet them", "context_len": 42}}}, "hairs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "Sweyn stood at the door of his tent , leaning upon his ponderous battle-axe ; around him were two or three warriors , whose grey hairs had not softened the look of ferocity so plainly stamped upon their faces", "context_len": 39}}}, "notebook": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "Attached to his belt was a sheath-knife , while in contrast to his up-country appearance he carried in the breast-pocket of his shirt a canvas-covered notebook , a couple of pencils , and a fountain-pen", "context_len": 35}}}, "states": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 10, "context": "518 , in describing the mounds of the country , states `` that the region lying between Savanna river and Oakmulge , east and west , and from the sea coast ( of the Atlantic ) to the Cherokee or Apalachean mountains ( filled with these mounds ) was possessed by the Cherokees since the arrival of the Europeans ; but they were afterwards dispossessed by the Muscogulges , and all that country was probably , many ages preceding the Cherokee invasion , inhabited by one nation or confederacy ( unknown to the Cherokees , Creeks )", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 15, "context": "Swan , after whose report this passage is quoted ( Schoolcraft V , 279 ) states that the title of the chief of the confederacy was the great beloved man , while Milfort , who was himself invested with the charge of great warrior of the nation , styles him `` Le Tastan\u00e9gy ou grand chef de guerre , '' adding , however , that in his time he was the highest authority in civil and military affairs ( M\u00e9moire , Note to p", "context_len": 84}}}, "negligent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Mallory , I do not think it has ever happened to me to find a room so utterly disorderly , or a cadet so negligent ! Look at that bluebook ; it belongs upon the mantelpiece , and I see it on the bed -- -- ''", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` When the princess heard this , she hastened to embrace him and kissed him , saying , `` O my beloved , all this was of my love for thee and I knew not what I did ; [ 671 ] nor indeed am I negligent of thy love", "context_len": 50}}}, "sterner": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "But , generally speaking , the Muhammadans seem to be much sterner opponents of Truth than the Hindus", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "First , the mischievous puppy started a little black and white rabbit , and sent it scampering away as fast as its feet could carry it , rushing after it among all the underbrush and briars , and never heeding the coaxing calls of his little mistresses or the louder and sterner voices of their brothers ; then coming back he rushed into a brook which ran by the way , and after rolling himself in it till the water was dripping from his silky coat , he shook himself and sent a shower of drops over the clean white dresses of the little girls ; and then finding the hole of a wood-chuck , he began scratching and burying himself in the earth in a frenzy to", "context_len": 128}}}, "delinquencies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "So I 've decided that in future no demerits shall be given for the first three weeks , and that delinquencies shall be punished by extra hours and other penalties", "context_len": 30}}}, "remonstrated": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 31, "context": "As an instance of Fanny \u2019 s peculiar gentleness , it is told that one Sunday , when she saw a man trying to sell things , she went up and remonstrated with him , speaking very seriously , but in so mild and courteous a manner , so entirely as she would have spoken to one who was socially on her own level , that he was utterly unable to take offence", "context_len": 73}}}, "aggregate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "There is no need to tell the rest in detail , but simply to say that while Mark and his roommate gazed on in blank despair , their jubilant enemy made out a list of at least a dozen charges , which he knew would aggregate to at least half of the demerit maximum , and for every one of which there was some slight basis of justification", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "I often think if each professing Christian took hold of one poor beggar and tried to elevate him , we should solve the problem a great deal sooner than by starting so many societies to improve them in the aggregate", "context_len": 40}}}, "demerit": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 78, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}}}, "maximum": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "There is no need to tell the rest in detail , but simply to say that while Mark and his roommate gazed on in blank despair , their jubilant enemy made out a list of at least a dozen charges , which he knew would aggregate to at least half of the demerit maximum , and for every one of which there was some slight basis of justification", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "A springless Cape cart drove them at the maximum pace obtainable by the wiry horse and the vociferous exhortations of the native `` boy ''", "context_len": 25}}}, "justification": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 67, "context": "There is no need to tell the rest in detail , but simply to say that while Mark and his roommate gazed on in blank despair , their jubilant enemy made out a list of at least a dozen charges , which he knew would aggregate to at least half of the demerit maximum , and for every one of which there was some slight basis of justification", "context_len": 68}}}, "excused": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 90, "context": "For since Flossy was stolen , he had been very careful as to the company he kept , and looked with a very suspicious eye upon any one who wore a ragged coat , which was not very just of Flossy , since a ragged coat may cover as true and honest a heart as ever beat ; but as the poor puppy knew no better , and had received some hard treatment at the hands of those whose miserable garments covered hard and cruel hearts , he must be excused for thinking that the one was a sign of the other", "context_len": 102}}}, "dumfounded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was a minute at least before either of them spoke ; they were both too dumfounded", "context_len": 17}}}, "revolvers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "As for Texas , his faithful friend and roommate , Texas said not one word ; but he went to the chimney , up which he had hidden his sixteen revolvers for safety , calmly selected two of the biggest , and having examined the cartridges , tucked them safely away in his rear pockets", "context_len": 55}}}, "cartridges": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 45, "context": "As for Texas , his faithful friend and roommate , Texas said not one word ; but he went to the chimney , up which he had hidden his sixteen revolvers for safety , calmly selected two of the biggest , and having examined the cartridges , tucked them safely away in his rear pockets", "context_len": 55}}}, "postpone": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` You would make a splendid Jeanne d'Arc , with the light of high and holy purpose in her eyes ; but as this is the last Saturday in the year that we shall have the chance of a ride to Forest Glen and home by moonlight , I move that we postpone our rhapsodies until a more convenient season", "context_len": 60}}}, "foreboding": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "But although she essayed a pleased smile at these things , and listened with enforced attention to the speeches and the music , there were present with her foreboding and unrest", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "No foreboding whisper in her heart spoke of what that visit to Moultan , so lightly mentioned , would mean to them all", "context_len": 23}}}, "improperly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Same -- Bedding improperly arranged at A", "context_len": 8}}}, "subdue": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "Philip , instead of being gratified at the present , was rather disposed to be displeased that they had sent him an animal of so fiery and apparently vicious a nature that nobody dared to attempt to subdue him", "context_len": 39}}}, "expressions": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` into the business was hailed with pleasure by the workmen : he was so full of tact and sympathy ; and sometimes , when his father had raised a storm of ill-feeling by some hasty expressions , he was able to bring peace and calm by his pleasant and genial manner", "context_len": 52}}}, "disapproval": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 117, "context": "of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very strongly influence him", "context_len": 128}}}, "downfall": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "He loved the Church of England as he loved the Queen and the Parliament ; and Audrey had wondered and admired as she realized how he had endured to see the downfall of one cherished institution after another , still full of hope in the future of England , and of faith that the Divine Providence would bring good out of evil", "context_len": 62}}}, "striven": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}}}, "thereof": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 64, "context": "the Sultan 's highness ? It [ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm , that thou shouldst merit of him this boon ? Again , this that thou cravest is beyond", "context_len": 128}}}, "incidentally": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}}}, "principals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}}}, "witnesses": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 51, "context": "Having striven to realize this for half an hour , they got together and swore a solemn oath , first , that if Mark were dismissed , a joint statement of the reasons thereof , incidentally mentioning each and every act of hazing done by the yearlings , naming principals , witnesses , time and place , should be forwarded to the superintendent , signed by the six ; and second , that every yearling who gave a demerit should be `` licked until he could n't stand up", "context_len": 89}}}, "cowboy": {"freq": 72, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 70, "word_index": 2, "context": "While the cowboy sat in his chair in front of the blackboard , wondering how long he could wait for Random or Griggs and yet be at the Flatiron Building as per appointment with Matt , a man sauntered in , looked at an office boy who was just going out with an armful of ticker tape , and then approached McGlory", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` That fellow Texas '' was `` making a fool of himself '' by dancing about in wild excitement , and raising a series of cowboy whoops in behalf of his friend , and of plebes in general", "context_len": 38}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He was a strapping middle-aged man in corduroys and cowboy hat with holes punched in it", "context_len": 16}}}, "labored": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 26, "context": "The late Major Chorpenning , remembered as one of the most prominent of freighters across the plains , told me that more than once he had labored through the mountains in the depth of winter when the snow under his feet was sixty feet deep ! He was in Salt Lake City , talking with Brigham Young , when word came that the mail rider westward had been killed by Indians", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The handwriting of the announcement was crude and labored , and the boy felt sure he had never seen it before", "context_len": 21}}}, "crosses": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "There was plenty of the coarse black cloth which they wear in summer -- in winter , of course , they are clad in sheepskins ; and I have sufficient white cotton cloth to make the crosses", "context_len": 37}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "I saw one at the summit of the hills which the trackway crosses between our county and Oxfordshire , when I last returned form Beranbyrig v , and I think that one gives the present alarm", "context_len": 36}}}, "retiring": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 73, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The more retiring ones , like Alcinda , contented themselves with beginning their ministrations to relatives or those they knew , but it was to adventurous spirits like Esther Ann and Reliance that a difficult case such as old Nathan Keener appealed", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Elmslie was eager as a girl , settling where the different pictures were to be hung , jumping up on chairs , and keeping us up beyond our usual hour for retiring , for she could not bear to leave the picture-question unsettled", "context_len": 43}}}, "skinning": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Skinning the seal , and especially talking about it , had consumed so much time , that they determined to devote what little of the day was left to playing ball , especially as Charlie was very fond of the sport , and seldom had any one to play with him", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "But it was some time before the natives had so far recovered from their fright as to set about resuming the skinning and cutting up of the dead jaguar", "context_len": 29}}}, "furlough": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "Like the great Missionary Swartz , she never went home on furlough ; and she never took more than a month \u2019 s [ 37 ] holiday in the year , but remained at her post , hot weather and cold weather , sometimes eleven months , sometimes twelve months in the year , during her whole service", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Wicks Merritt 's evidently forgotten I was on furlough once myself , '' he said", "context_len": 16}}}, "jollying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Oh , nothing much ; only a lot of nonsense -- jollying as usual", "context_len": 15}}}, "polishing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "Their faces and hands were scrubbed with wholly unnecessary vigor , and in Albert 's case even ears , and then both children were thrust on to the little low seat again , and drawing a stool in front of them , Babette laid an elaborate manicure set open upon her lap , and gave her whole mind to the shaping and polishing of their nails -- a process in which Albert took great interest , and which was accomplished , it must be confessed , most dexterously and with great expedition", "context_len": 92}}}, "rout": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 117, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "He felt sure that the next day would witness the rout and capture of the Confederate who had for so long harassed the Federals in Tennessee", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Rout the beggars out of it", "context_len": 6}}}, "whacks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Tell Billy I hope the plebe does him ; tell him I say that if Mallory once whacks him on the head with that right arm of his he 'll see more stars from the lick than the Lick telescope can show -- -- ' ''", "context_len": 46}}}, "pun": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I do n't know whether that 's because of the pun , '' laughed Fischer , `` or because of your recollection of the blow", "context_len": 26}}}, "tit": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He 'll probably give you tit for tat every time , anyhow", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "It was made of the softest moss and lichens , fashioned together in the shape of a bottle with the neck downwards ; for the tit must have some place in which to bestow her long tail , and she builds her home to suit her person", "context_len": 47}}}, "unfairly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Look you , Ned Crocker , have ye at any time been unfairly done by ? No ? Then why did ye blab on the run we made nigh Dancing Ledge , when Thompson , John Light , and Long Will of Corfe were taken ? ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "gunstock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` By George ! '' cried the latter at last , striking his gunstock in the ground", "context_len": 17}}}, "confoundedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "And it 's perfectly outrageous , that 's what it is ! The plebe 's confoundedly fresh , of course , but he 's a gentleman for all that , and he do n't deserve one-quarter of the demerits he 's gotten", "context_len": 42}}}, "justifiable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` That wars are necessary evils , only justifiable when fighting , as you , my lord , have done , for home and altar , but they are no true children of the Prince of Peace who delight in bloodshed and strife", "context_len": 43}}}, "labor": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 59, "context": "I wouldn \u2019 t ; he \u2019 s a particular old toad , and would rather have it just as it grew ; but if you touch it , he \u2019 ll think you \u2019 ve taken off some where you ought not to , and spilte it ; he \u2019 ll no more thank you for saving him labor on a float-piece , than a feller would thank you for courting a girl for him ; he \u2019 d rather do it himself", "context_len": 84}}}, "cronies": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "He was gone half an hour , came back looking wise and excited , joined his cronies again , and at midnight was helpless", "context_len": 24}}}, "northern": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 71, "word_index": 5, "context": "The spot was on the northern borders of the ancient kingdom of Sussex -- the land of the Saxon Ella -- a spot marvelously favoured by nature , occupying the summit of a low hill , which commanded a wide prospect on all sides , while itself almost impregnable when fortified , as it was , by ditches and mounds , dug in the usual Danish fashion , for the Danes owed much of their success to their skill in fortification", "context_len": 81}}}, "humorous": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 36, "context": "When Keimer took him to his new landlady , Ben was dressed in his best , a handsome , husky well-mannered young man , about five feet ten inches , with a wide mouth and a humorous light in his brown eyes", "context_len": 42}}}, "appreciatively": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Baker took a mild interest in what was going on , smiling appreciatively once in a while when Bart made a witty hit or an unusually good sale", "context_len": 28}}}, "spruced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "They had spruced up out of sight ; but it did n't take me very long to find something wrong , I tell you", "context_len": 24}}}, "hilarity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "And when the reciter commenced a string of verses portraying the catastrophe in the cabin shared by Lieutenant Nicholson and the Padre , the former 's `` Hear , hear ! '' and the latter 's deep bass laugh were heard above the roars of hilarity", "context_len": 46}}}, "vance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Vance", "context_len": 1}}}, "alacrity": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 48, "context": "I have particularly noticed that when we have been on board , and have been laughing and chatting together before we got under way , their manner changed directly the first order was given , and that all the commands were carried out with as much goodwill and alacrity as if they were under Ricord himself", "context_len": 56}}}, "sternness": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 2, "context": "There was sternness as he expressed his sentiments regarding the person with a bullying inclination who took pleasure in abusing those physically weaker than himself ; and , although Sam Rollins \u2019 name was not mentioned , every one knew at whom those open remarks were directed", "context_len": 47}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 84, "context": "my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction , you shall meet with the fate you so justly deserve", "context_len": 128}}}, "interference": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 51, "context": "Each was bent at the elbow with the hand close to the chest , like a professional runner , but the right hand was empty , while the fingers of the left were closed about the huge bow which he was obliged to hold diagonally before him , to prevent its interference with his running", "context_len": 55}}}, "historic": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 66, "context": "An approach to its reconstruction could be attempted by carefully comparing the lexicon and grammatic forms of the dialects presently spoken , and an individual acquainted with them all , or at least with their four representatives , might also compose a comparative grammar of these dialects as spoken at the present epoch of their development , which would reveal many points concerning the ancient or historic shape of the language once common to all these tribes", "context_len": 77}}}, "dined": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 56, "context": "A grassy glade under a tall beech seemed intended by Nature for a summer-house , for there was a large flat stone in the middle , which served for a table , and a circle of little stones round , just high enough for seats , so that you might imagine Queen Mab and her fairies dined here on moonlight nights , with the squirrels for guests and the bats and owls for waiters", "context_len": 74}}}, "candid": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "'My stars ! Is n't she a terror ? I shall have to keep clear of the Willows every afternoon next week , for I know she 'll make a point of calling and telling poor Aunt Mary her candid opinion of me", "context_len": 43}}}, "observer": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 66, "context": "We now had leisure to observe that the storm had so far passed away that there were big breaks in the canopy of cloud overhead and away to the eastward , through which the stars were beginning to show themselves , affording enough light just to enable us to discern the two banks of the stream , but not sufficient to betray our presence to an observer at a greater distance than , say , a quarter of a mile", "context_len": 80}}}, "credulous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They tell stories at West Point of credulous candidates who have `` swallowed '' that , and believed that the cadet battalion was composed of disobedient cadets , about to be locked up in confinement", "context_len": 35}}}, "confinement": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 13, "context": "It was undoubtedly by diligent practice in this direction that , after strict confinement to the interior of my hut for some five or six days , I was permitted , first of all , to wander at will about that portion of the town which was enclosed by the palisade , and ultimately to pass outside and go practically whither I would , always accompanied , however , by two armed guards", "context_len": 73}}}, "actively": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "All these monarchs I have actively served ; and when the call of duty has not taken me to the high seas , or on service in some foreign land , my leisure time has always been spent in the quietness of Holwick", "context_len": 43}}}, "corporals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The dinner is upon the table when the cadets enter ; the corporals are charged with the duty of carving , and the luckless plebe is expected to help everybody to water upon demand , and eats nothing until that duty has been attended to", "context_len": 45}}}, "carving": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "The right in there of the sexton evidently referred to Braye Chapel , within a few feet of the door by which he had entered ; and glancing in through the open-work carving of the partition enclosing it , he discovered Marie-Celeste seated on a cushion on the floor , her back against the wall , busily writing away on the portfolio on her lap", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "'Nay , this be older nor smugglers , ' said Joe , with a glance at the solid workmanship and the quaint carving on the old lid , 'unless they made use of an old thing for their own purposes", "context_len": 40}}}, "speculate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` There was a new phase of the question they had to speculate upon now , and that was what the `` beast '' could possibly have done to move to such unholy wrath so important a personage as the senior captain of the Battalion", "context_len": 45}}}, "unholy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` There was a new phase of the question they had to speculate upon now , and that was what the `` beast '' could possibly have done to move to such unholy wrath so important a personage as the senior captain of the Battalion", "context_len": 45}}}, "incurring": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "Parmenio thought , therefore , that the Persians would not dare to remain and encamp many days in the vicinity of Alexander 's army , and that , accordingly , if they waited a little , the enemy would retreat , and Alexander could then cross the river without incurring the danger of a battle", "context_len": 55}}}, "attracting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "The smoke of the guns lay so heavy on the water , and the combatants were so intent upon the struggle at the breach , that Gervaise steered his galley into the midst of the Turkish vessels laden with troops ready to disembark , without attracting any notice ; then , standing upon the taffrail , he signalled to the two outside boats to throw off their ropes and make for the Turkish ship nearest to them", "context_len": 77}}}, "clinch": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` All the same , '' continued the other , `` I wish we could get up a scheme to get him in disgrace , so as to clinch it", "context_len": 30}}}, "moping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I thought he 'd be up in barracks , moping , to-day ! ''", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He did not understand moping ; he had no sympathy with gloom", "context_len": 12}}}, "sickest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "I did want mother awfully , when I was feeling the sickest , but I tried to think how lovely everyone was to me , and of how nice it was to be in this lovely cool place by the sea , instead of in the hot city , and I did n't feel so", "context_len": 55}}}, "malignant": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 25, "context": "He did not sleep well that night ; he was disturbed by dreams , in which he lived over again that desperate struggle with his malignant enemy the struggle that had brought upon him the great trouble of his life", "context_len": 40}}}, "bystander": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "It is dangerous , too , in the hands of a beginner , for it is then that it shows off , and is liable to run wild and chase some bystander in a most vigorous manner", "context_len": 37}}}, "piqued": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "So it was ; certain of the cadets , being piqued at the evident superiority which that B", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "He left word that he 'd not be home till six , mum , so you need n't be thinking of leaving till half-past five , mum ; and so it was plainly evident that Lewis Carroll wanted to run no risk of seeing them at either end of their visit , and Dorothy could not help feeling a little piqued", "context_len": 61}}}, "impromptu": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "Next he started to carry outside and pile neatly at the blind end of the building all the boards , boxes and other debris littering up the room , swept it , and selected two packing cases and nailed them up into a convenient impromptu desk , manufactured a bench seat out of some loose boards , set his pen , ink and paper in order , and felt quite ready for business", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Not to be outdone in pluck , Peggy essayed to follow with equal style , but her foot slipped on a particularly green and moss-grown boulder , and in a moment she had plunged , tea-cakes and all , into an impromptu bath", "context_len": 43}}}, "surlily": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "The newcomer was a burly , good-natured farmer , who , after giving the three men a cheery salutation , which they returned surlily enough , sat down opposite to me", "context_len": 31}}}, "whoops": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` That fellow Texas '' was `` making a fool of himself '' by dancing about in wild excitement , and raising a series of cowboy whoops in behalf of his friend , and of plebes in general", "context_len": 38}}}, "betraying": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 104, "context": "She did not call to me , or announce her return in any way , but set to work to mow a circle of about ten feet in diameter in the long grass ; and then , having produced fire by rubbing two pieces of wood together , she proceeded very carefully to burn off the short grass left inside the circle , setting fire to it , allowing it to burn for a few seconds , and then beating it out again with a branch , in order that the fire might not spread and burn us out , to say nothing of betraying our presence by the smoke that it would raise", "context_len": 114}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "He would not betray and desert his friends in anticipation of their deserting and betraying him", "context_len": 16}}}, "golly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Golly , but that 's a long swim ! ''", "context_len": 10}}}, "thunderstorm": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "By the time that the proceedings of the day were over and the enormous crowd began to disperse , it became evident that a more than usually violent tropical thunderstorm was brewing , although it might be some hours yet before it would burst over the blood-stained town", "context_len": 48}}}, "prediction": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "I think you will all agree with me that the prediction is already in a fair way of being fulfilled , and that the services he has rendered to the Order justify us , his comrades of the English langue , in feeling proud of him", "context_len": 46}}}, "obscured": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 13, "context": "But after twelve o \u2019 clock the wind changed to south-east ; clouds obscured the moon ; and , while the boys were quietly sleeping , a gust of wind struck the tree , covering them with showers of leaves , while the rain dashed in sheets upon their faces", "context_len": 50}}}, "thence": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 116, "context": "Among other things were two or three peacock feathers , a turkey wing , some pebbles from the brook where papa used to sail his boats when he was a boy , a piece of rusty tin pipe , which , because it looked black and smoky , and came from the field where the burnt barn had stood , they persuaded themselves must be a part of the very leader down which papa slid when he ran for the ladder to save his little brother , -- all these , and other treasures of like value , were carefully collected and stowed in the wagons , to be carried to the Lake House , and thence to the city", "context_len": 120}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The skipper therefore carefully laid down upon his chart the supposititious course of the Virginia from the Gaboon to Cape Palmas , and thence onward to the Caribbean Sea ; and then shaped a course to enable us to fall in with her on the latter , at a spot about one hundred miles to the westward of Palmas", "context_len": 59}}}, "swimmers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Dotted about the swelling surface there would be the heads of plenty of swimmers -- men and lads -- some going smoothly along , mounting the rollers as they came in , and descending softly into the hollows ; others again swimming to meet each wave , then rising a little , and with a plunge like a duck or one of the great bronze-black shags , or cormorants , that sat upon the rock-shelves , diving right through the mass of water , to come out fairly on the other side", "context_len": 92}}}, "damper": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Even the damper of his idol 's displeasure could not change Master Edwards ' mind , however ; he and nearly the whole crowd with him made a dash down the shore for a vantage point to see the finish", "context_len": 40}}}, "vantage": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "The rush always took place on a small knoll , or hill , back of the gymnasium , and it was the object of the freshmen to take possession of this point of vantage , and maintain it for half an hour against the rush of the sophomores", "context_len": 48}}}, "epilepsy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` Mallory ! Mallory ! '' roared the excited plebes , backed up by an occasional `` Wow ! '' in the stentorian tones of the mighty Texan , who , by this time , was on the verge of epilepsy", "context_len": 41}}}, "snail": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I want exercise , you young snail , '' I tried to tell him by starting slowly up the gentle slope of the barnyard , and then turning to look round at him", "context_len": 34}}}, "partisans": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Onward went the two heads , still side by side , seeming to creep through the water at a snail 's pace to the excited partisans on the shore", "context_len": 29}}}, "incoherently": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "The race was at its most exciting stage -- the finish , and the cadets were dancing about , half in a frenzy , yelling incoherently , at the two still struggling lads , when some one , nobody knew just who , chanced to glance for one brief instant up the river", "context_len": 53}}}, "sailboat": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Help ! Help ! '' called a faint voice from the distant sailboat", "context_len": 14}}}, "catboat": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Right in the teeth of the wind , in the center of the river , was a small catboat , driven downstream , before the gale , with the speed of a locomotive", "context_len": 33}}}, "downstream": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Right in the teeth of the wind , in the center of the river , was a small catboat , driven downstream , before the gale , with the speed of a locomotive", "context_len": 33}}}, "gibed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "She sat in the stern , waving her hands in helpless terror , and even as the spectators stared , the boat gibed with terrific violence , and a volume of water poured in over the gunwale", "context_len": 37}}}, "violence": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 65, "context": "Early on the morrow we weighed anchor , and , under the guidance of Pedro , towed the Gannet , there being no wind , through a gap in the reef , so that her new berth was within half a league of the landingplace -- though there was an inner reef close inshore , on which the sea brake , though not with such violence as on the outer reef", "context_len": 71}}}, "racers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The racers were not slow to notice it ; both of them turned to gaze behind them , and to take in the situation", "context_len": 24}}}, "ants": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "Before it was half finished there was a very distinct commotion upon the beach ; half the naked children , who had been playing in the water , were racing up to the town as fast as their legs would carry them , shouting as they went , while from every hut the inhabitants came pouring out , like ants from a disturbed nest , and began to hurry down to the beach", "context_len": 73}}}, "contributing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "She still steadily refrained from going out to parties at other people \u2019 s houses ; but she never failed to be present at any party in their own house , not only making her appearance , but contributing her utmost to the entertainment of guests", "context_len": 46}}}, "humbled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "A blinding sheet of driving rain , that darkened the air and drove against the river , and rose again in clouds of spray ; a gale that lashed the water into fury ; and darkness that shut out the river , and the boat , and the swimmer , and left nothing but a humbled group of shivering cadets", "context_len": 60}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "Yes , the passion had come quickly , but it had been quickly conquered , and as Aunt Patty looked at her , these words came to her mind : `` Greater is he that ruleth his spirit than he that taketh a city ; '' and she stood humbled before this little child", "context_len": 54}}}, "watchers": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` And so was I , '' said he , `` and I have persuaded the king to place guards and watchers all through the coasts opposite the Wight , and with Edric 's aid we elaborated a goodly plan", "context_len": 40}}}, "precludes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The surprise of the helpless watchers on the shore precludes description", "context_len": 11}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The syntax is remarkably simple and uniform ; the multiplicity of grammatic forms precludes the formation of many syntactic rules , just as in Sanscrit", "context_len": 25}}}, "enacted": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 74, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}}}, "wronged": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 32, "context": "A man has said that it is a terrible thing to die with a wrong upon one 's soul ; but that it is agony to see another die whom you have wronged , to know that your act can never be atoned for now", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Are you goin ' to let him insult us ? '' asked Mosely in the tone of a wronged man", "context_len": 21}}}, "atoned": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "There was something I suddenly remembered that I had to see to that very minute , stammered Donald , shaking bands with Marie-Celeste and Harold at one and the same moment ; but you may just believe I 'm glad to see you and the warmth of Donald 's welcome fully atoned for the few moments of unexplained delay", "context_len": 59}}}, "records": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 12, "context": "Many old traditions of historic importance must have been embodied in these records ; but the only one given by Milfort , referring to the emigration of the Creeks from their ancient cave-homes along Red river , is so mixed up with incredible matter , that the fixation of the events , as far as then remembered , must have taken place many generations after the arrival of the Creeks in their Alabama homes", "context_len": 74}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "George went out to India ; and two years later a paper of extracts from different letters , in her handwriting , records the sister \u2019 s loving pride in the warm opinions sent home about that brother", "context_len": 38}}}, "cataclysm": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "If he were doomed to die in this terrible cataclysm , he would die with that picture next his heart !", "context_len": 21}}}, "pent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "It came from the north , and it had a full sweep down the valley of the Hudson , pent in and focused to one point by the mountains on each side", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "In one case , the fleet , which had come along the sea , accompanying the march of the army on land , was pent up in a harbor by a stronger Persian fleet outside", "context_len": 35}}}, "focused": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "In rolling tones and with magnificent gestures he was paying a lofty tribute to the immortal Stars and Stripes waving just over his head , when , his eyes lowering , they focused straight in a fixed stare on Bart", "context_len": 40}}}, "foam": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 21, "context": "First , there was the strip of smooth , white sand , on which the waves were breaking into beautiful snowy foam , with such a pleasant sound ; then came another space full of pebbles and stones and sea-weed , with a few shells and here and there a great rock ; then more rocks and stones with a coarse kind of grass growing between them ; and beyond these , a few rough fir trees which looked as if they found it hard work to grow there", "context_len": 89}}}, "raindrops": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "It flung the raindrops in gusts against it , and caught them up in spray and whirled them on ; and this , to the echoing crashes of the thunder and the dull , lurid gleam of the lightning that played in the rear", "context_len": 44}}}, "crashes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "It flung the raindrops in gusts against it , and caught them up in spray and whirled them on ; and this , to the echoing crashes of the thunder and the dull , lurid gleam of the lightning that played in the rear", "context_len": 44}}}, "lurid": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 68, "context": "And when , at night , impressed with the sense of solemnity which night always imparts to strange and novel scenes , they looked up to the bright round moon , pleased with the expression of cheerfulness and companionship which beams always in her light , to find her suddenly waning , changing her form , withdrawing her bright beams , and looking down upon them with a lurid and murky light , it was not surprising that they felt an emotion of terror", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "There is , however , something very solemn and awful in a great conflagration at night , and very few incendiaries can gaze upon the fury of the lurid and frightful flames which they have caused to ascend without some misgivings and some remorse", "context_len": 44}}}, "laboring": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "What they had done was this : The boy was apparently laboring under some indecision , as if wishing to remain on the top of the hill", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "A detonation sounded above the noise of the laboring motor , and something whistled viciously past Matt \u2019 s ear", "context_len": 20}}}, "chancing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "It happened that one night , when , as now was frequently the case , I had been summoned to entertain the king and his household by `` obliging them with a little music , '' I was playing some soft , plaintive air -- I forget what -- when , chancing to glance toward Ama , who , seated on the ground on the extreme left of the semicircle , was well within my range of vision , I fancied I saw some moving object close to her left hand , which was resting lightly on the ground", "context_len": 99}}}, "cleave": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "You also will join us ; I know it is long since you have heard the words of pure doctrine , yet there is a blessing in reserve for the seed of the righteous , and the filth of the Presbyterian doctrines you learned in Scotland shall not cleave unto your feet to make them stumble in the way", "context_len": 59}}}, "boom": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 49, "context": "But from the meanest to the best swimmer there , every one paused to watch Mark Penelly standing statue-like up against the black rock , waiting till a great ninth wave came majestically rolling in , sweeping over the outer rocks -- the Shangles -- and then with a boom leaping at Carn Du , running up it , as it were , in a mighty column of water , some twenty feet even on a calm day", "context_len": 78}}}, "weakening": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "But the cannon of that period were so cumbrous and slowly worked , and so inaccurate in their aim , that the advantage of occupying a position that would prevent an enemy from firing down into a town was considered to be more than counterbalanced by the weakening of the garrison by the abstraction of the force required to man the detached work , and by the risk of their being surrounded and cut off without the garrison of the town being able to aid them", "context_len": 86}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Notice had been sent down early to the tower ; and all the knights who could be spared , without too greatly weakening the garrison , went up to attend it ; the service was conducted with all the pomp and ceremony possible , and after it was over a great procession was formed to proceed to the shrine , where a picture of the Virgin held in special reverence by the Order was placed", "context_len": 75}}}, "yore": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 69, "context": "In what strange world had he been living ? Had that sullen , desperate wretch been indeed Dick Harrison ? He realized that he was alive ; he could still enjoy the common comforts of food and fire ; he could think ; he could plan ! His feet were once more treading solid earth ; his brain began to spin anew the projects that had delighted him of yore ; his heart began to stir with the hopes of old", "context_len": 81}}}, "battling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "There was just such a hero then battling with the waves as now --", "context_len": 14}}}, "sallow": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Mark Penelly 's face grew a shade more sallow , but he said nothing , only knelt down by a pile of loose net , and watched the young man , whom he looked upon as his rival , till Harry , swimming gracefully and well , came right up and answered the hail of the fishermen with a cheery shout", "context_len": 61}}}, "shielding": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "Indeed , for the sake of shielding you , I have told the Hartleys that your name is Morris , and it rests with you to tell them your right name some day if you choose ; hut the doctor knows the truth about things -- he had to know", "context_len": 50}}}, "drizzle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "While the anxious cadets raced up and down the shore , each striving to catch a glimpse of Mallory , the dark clouds sailed past and the rain settled into an ordinary drizzle", "context_len": 33}}}, "straggler": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "So they lay in cover until the last straggler had disappeared in the direction of Aescendune , and then continued their course , with many a jest at the expense of the English", "context_len": 33}}}, "dosing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` It 's Judge Fuller 's daughter , '' said Fischer , who was busily dosing the unconscious figure with a flask of reddish liquid surreptitiously produced by one of the cadets", "context_len": 32}}}, "prostrate": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "Nevertheless , after the first few days , when many of the men were prostrate with sea-sickness , the troops soon accustomed themselves to their new conditions , and settled down with the fixed determination to make the voyage a sort of maritime picnic", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 57, "context": "Both sides would certainly have claimed the victory , for both held a portion of the flag , had not Drusie , scrambling out of the hawthorn bushes into which she had been tossed , jumped into the middle of the stream , and snatched the part that he still held out of the hand of the prostrate , half-drowned enemy", "context_len": 61}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Edmund , on the other hand , `` Victory , my men , or a warrior 's grave ! We will not live to see England prostrate beneath the tyrant any longer", "context_len": 32}}}, "swum": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 4, "context": "Meanwhile the latter had swum right out to the fishing lugger , where he was taken on board , and it being one of his father 's boats , he was soon furnished with a blue jersey and a pair of rough flannel trousers , for he did not care about swimming back", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` ] `` Why , it 's a porpoise , and a good big 'un too ! Steady , lads ; steady ! She 's swum into the net that trailed overboard", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "An Indian warrior having swum the stream , stood for a moment on the shore", "context_len": 15}}}, "overzealous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Half a dozen had started without being asked ; and Mark 's overzealous friend was grabbed by the hair and arms and feet and rushed in in great style", "context_len": 29}}}, "fractured": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Yes , I guess with a ruffle or two , a little insertion , and a bit of old lace , it will hide the fractured places , Phil", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "To a man not a sailor by profession the knowledge that only a comparatively thin steel plate , and fractured at that , is between him and death by drowning is apt to be decidedly disconcerting", "context_len": 36}}}, "fainted": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 29, "context": "The butchery of the sentence for treason was carried out to the bitter end , yet of the onlookers there were but a few women who sobbed hysterically or fainted , and but one or two men who pushed their way back , sick with the sight and smell of the shambles", "context_len": 52}}}, "bloodless": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Fainted ? '' echoed the surgeon , as he noticed the young man 's white lips and bloodless cheek", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "His face was bloodless and twitching , his whole frame a-quake", "context_len": 11}}}, "colonel": {"freq": 309, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 309, "word_index": 39, "context": "If so be you laid hands if it were but on a hen , if you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old", "context_len": 128}}}, "whacked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Some one with an oath whacked me over the head with a sabre , my horse stumbled in the darkness , and down I went into chaos", "context_len": 27}}}, "miraculous": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 73, "context": "It is wholly legendary , in its first portion even mythical ; it is of a comparatively remote age , exceedingly instructive for ethnography and for the development of religious ideas ; it is full of that sort of na\u00efvet\u00e9 which we like so much to meet in the mental productions of our aborigines , and affords striking instances of the debasing and brutalizing influence of the unrestricted belief in the supernatural and miraculous", "context_len": 74}}}, "consistent": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 38, "context": "Oh ! if only every one might have his turn in this world of ours ; but since that is out of the question , let there at least be as much Coney Island for housemaids as is consistent with good morals and faithful discharge of their duties ; at least so thought one dear little mistress , with more heart , perhaps , than discretion , but a heart , all the same , that won every one to her and made life in her household move with infinite smoothness", "context_len": 91}}}, "fee": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Young Fischer , who 's a splendid man himself , I 'll tell you , informed me yesterday that the plebe had earned his admission fee by bringing help to a wrecked train and telegraphing the account to a New York paper", "context_len": 42}}}, "radical": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Peggy turned out the drawer by the simple process of emptying it on the table , and disclosed a very miscellaneous collection of literature -- socialist pamphlets , agnostic newspapers , and radical tracts were mixed up with teetotal treatises , missionary leaflets , and the parish magazine", "context_len": 48}}}, "contemplating": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` Do n't touch 'em , I want to see in what direction they are traveling , '' called Fenn , who , as soon as he had placed in a safe place the turtle he had caught , came over to where his chums were contemplating the other two", "context_len": 50}}}, "notoriety": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Or if one can not do anything heroic , let him even do something that will bring him notoriety , and then --", "context_len": 23}}}, "boulevard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was no boulevard at West Point , but the area in barracks served the purpose , and Mark could not help noticing that as he went the yearlings were gazing enviously at him , and the plebes with undisguised admiration", "context_len": 41}}}, "bubbly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Bless my soul , but I 'm glad ! '' piped in the little round bubbly voice of `` Indian", "context_len": 21}}}, "pshaw": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Reminds me of a story I once heard , b'gee -- pshaw , what 's the use of trying to tell a good story with everybody trying to shout at once", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Therefore one morning when Louis was seen coming in the gate , Dorothy gave an impatient `` Oh , pshaw ! I thought we were going to have a nice pleasant time to-day , and here comes Louis", "context_len": 38}}}, "modesty": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "With the true modesty natural to all high minds he felt that he would a great deal rather rescue a girl than be praised and made generally uncomfortable for it", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "He acted , however , himself , in this high position , with great energy and with complete success ; and , at the same time , with all that modesty of deportment , and that delicate consideration for the officers under him -- who , though inferior in rank , were yet his superiors in age and experience -- which his position rendered proper , but which few persons so young as he would have manifested in circumstances so well calculated to awaken the feelings of vanity and elation", "context_len": 90}}}, "likelihood": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 60, "context": "I could , of course , leap through a port into the sea , taking my chance of being picked up by the pursuing craft , which I fondly hoped would be the avenging Gannet ; but I did not know what distance separated us , and even then , in the eagerness of the chase , there was little likelihood of their noticing me , still less of heaving to and picking me up", "context_len": 75}}}, "faction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "She is in my Sunday-school class , '' returned Jennie , but she said nothing more , yet both the other two felt quite sure that there was no likelihood of Jennie 's going over to the other faction", "context_len": 39}}}, "agnes": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 0, "context": "Agnes in the end confesses herself guilty of the crime for which he is condemned to death ; -- in time to save his name from lasting disgrace , though not in time to save his life", "context_len": 37}}}, "wealthier": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "The common people of Pennsylvania at this time were pleading for paper money , such as was used in Massachusetts and other colonies , but the wealthier citizens opposed it", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The Leather Apron Club , the town \u2019 s wealthier citizens nicknamed the Junto , because of the humble working class background of its membership", "context_len": 25}}}, "adams": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Adams , '' he read from the tag , `` a queer name , and no one answering to it here in Pleasantville", "context_len": 23}}}, "edna": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 0, "context": "Edna at first decided to take her doll Ada `` because she is more used to traveling , '' she said , but at the last moment she changed her mind saying that Ada had been on so many journeys that she thought someone else should have a chance and , therefore , it was her new doll , Virginia , who was dressed for the trip", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 0, "context": "Edna wondered if she would ever cease to dislike her , and insisted to Dorothy that they ought to try to persuade her to come into the club", "context_len": 28}}}, "rector": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 24, "context": "Now , Rosamund 's interest in this expedition was on no account to examine the `` Leaves , '' as naughty Irene called the rector 's daughters ; but she wanted to watch Miss Carter , and if possible to get a word with her , and to induce her to tell her something more with regard to the wild and mischievous girl whom , nevertheless , she could not get for a moment out of her head", "context_len": 78}}}, "despatched": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 13, "context": "She further mentioned that , as she felt sure her father would have despatched a party in pursuit of us , which , she expected , would by this time be , not far behind us , it would be very desirable to keep a watch for them , since it was important that we should know as much as possible of their movements ; and she accordingly suggested that I should climb a particularly lofty tree which she indicated , and keep a look-out for them , while she went off into the forest to seek the wherewithal to furnish a breakfast", "context_len": 103}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "\u2019 In the present day a boy so despatched might be sent back again ; but in those days India was separated from England by a vast gulf of distance and of time", "context_len": 33}}}, "dessert": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 53, "context": "Moreover , there was always a supply of preserved ginger taken from a queer jar with twisted handles , and there was also an especially toothsome cake which the captain 's housekeeper served , so Edna felt that the feast in store for her , quite made up for the poverty of a dessert of boiled rice and sugar", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Other paragraphs in the report read thus : `` Received anonymously five large cheeses ; received a box of dessert knives and forks , a cruet , a silver soup ladle and a silver cup ; from Clifton , twelve tons house coals ; from Bedminster , a monster loaf , 200 lbs", "context_len": 53}}}, "ginger": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 9, "context": "Moreover , there was always a supply of preserved ginger taken from a queer jar with twisted handles , and there was also an especially toothsome cake which the captain 's housekeeper served , so Edna felt that the feast in store for her , quite made up for the poverty of a dessert of boiled rice and sugar", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "We have had ginger ale and lemonade , and chocolate and such things", "context_len": 13}}}, "nourishing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "A little sick bugler was stowed away in an undesirable second-cabin state-room that had remained unengaged ; and Chris , noticing that a bowl of broth or some sort of nourishing food was carried thither three times a day , but that apart from this no one ever entered or left the state-room , questioned the steward , and as soon as he learned the facts , made his own way in , to the great delight of the lonely little fellow", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "It can not be denied that as they sat before the blazing fire , and ate the nourishing , comfortable food , they felt a kind of rapturous awe , and looked into each other 's eyes with something like doubt", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "The food and drink provided for me were of an especially nourishing character , and when at length I had partaken of as much as I could force down my throat I was again lashed to my stake , but this time so carefully that , while for me to loose my bonds was an impossibility , the circulation of blood was in nowise impeded ; and for even this small mercy I was inexpressibly grateful", "context_len": 76}}}, "waffles": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Lizzie had waffles for supper , and Frank ate ten pieces , '' spoke up Charlie", "context_len": 16}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Edna enjoyed the prospect of chicken and waffles too much to long too ardently for the next day", "context_len": 18}}}, "favored": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 67, "context": "There remained only one inmate of the cottage yet to be encountered -- the gentleman about whom Chris had told him , and who had met with the driving accident a few weeks back ; but the gentleman in question bad his own ideas as to the time and place when that dreaded encounter was to be gotten through with , and Donald was not to be favored with an interview that evening", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Harold , conjecturing that the children might be sent home in this courtly fashion , was on hand on the steps to receive the favored recipients of royal hospitality", "context_len": 29}}}, "charitable": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 77, "context": "I will ask Celia about it , but what I do n't like is that you should start it in a spirit of trying to get the better of another girl , though I can see that it is the most natural thing in the world for you to feel as you do , and I can see that Clara has really brought it on herself , but I do want my dear little girls to be charitable and above the petty meanness that is actuating Clara", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I know you began it all with a view to charitable ends ; but he who does evil that good may come sets his foot in a crooked path , of which none can see the close", "context_len": 37}}}, "actuating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 85, "context": "I will ask Celia about it , but what I do n't like is that you should start it in a spirit of trying to get the better of another girl , though I can see that it is the most natural thing in the world for you to feel as you do , and I can see that Clara has really brought it on herself , but I do want my dear little girls to be charitable and above the petty meanness that is actuating Clara", "context_len": 87}}}, "expatiate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "She had n't seen Cousin Ben yet so she went out to hunt him up , but discovering that he was hard at work over his studies she concluded not to disturb him but to go with the boys to hear them expatiate upon the qualities of the new pigeons , of the trade they had made with another boy and of various things which had been going on at their school", "context_len": 72}}}, "attic": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 55, "word_index": 83, "context": "So he told them how , when he sat alone , ill and dull and irritable , Ram Dass had tried to distract him by describing the passers by , and there was one child who passed oftener than any one else ; he had begun to be interested in her -- partly perhaps because he was thinking a great deal of a little girl , and partly because Ram Dass had been able to relate the incident of his visit to the attic in chase of the monkey", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "It must be admitted that the attic was rather delighted in , and that its coldness and bareness quite sank into insignificance when Melchisedec was remembered , and one heard about the sparrows and things one could see if one climbed on the table and stuck one 's head and shoulders out of the skylight", "context_len": 55}}}, "odor": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 45, "context": "At the foot of the throne was the coffin , made , it is said , of solid gold , and containing , besides the body , a large quantity of the most costly spices and aromatic perfumes , which filled the air with their odor", "context_len": 46}}}, "camphor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Moreover , she had with her eight and-forty slave-girls , whom Alaeddin had presented to her , in each one 's hand a great candle of camphor and ambergris , set in a candlestick of gold , studded with jewels ; and all the men and women in the palace went out with her and fared on before her , till they brought her to her bridegroom 's palace and carrying her up to her pavilion , [ 495 ] attired her in various robes [ 496 ] and displayed her", "context_len": 91}}}, "stored": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 53, "context": "The keepers of the wardrobe , one after another , have cared for them carefully , Albert , for nearly five hundred years , Miss Belmore explained ; and it is only by a special order from the Queen that they can ever be taken out of the precious chest where they are stored for a single moment , except twice a year or so , to be cleaned and brushed", "context_len": 71}}}, "detract": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "There was , too , an odor of camphor and of soap , a quantity of the latter being stored up there , but these things did not in the least detract from the place in the eyes of the girls", "context_len": 41}}}, "faulty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was rather wobbly , but a bit of wood was put under the faulty leg , and it did very well", "context_len": 22}}}, "rickety": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 41, "context": "One perfectly good chair was brought up for the president , the rest were content to be seated on whatever came handy , two chairs very much gone as to backs , one with the bottom entirely through , and a rickety camp stool made up the remainder of the furniture , but Agnes had taken care that there were flowers on the table and that pens , pencils and paper were supplied", "context_len": 73}}}, "pencils": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 68, "context": "One perfectly good chair was brought up for the president , the rest were content to be seated on whatever came handy , two chairs very much gone as to backs , one with the bottom entirely through , and a rickety camp stool made up the remainder of the furniture , but Agnes had taken care that there were flowers on the table and that pens , pencils and paper were supplied", "context_len": 73}}}, "organizers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "She also brought up some books `` to make it look more literary , '' she said , and the organizers of the club were delighted", "context_len": 26}}}, "bundled": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 13, "context": "Men who had prospected for precious metals all summer in the mountains now bundled their pots and pans and blankets back to shelter for the winter ; the long-eared burros , lost in great rolls of bedding , stood about the tipple awaiting the result of their masters ' interviews with the mine boss , concerning work and the occupancy of any `` shack '' that might still be empty", "context_len": 70}}}, "closeted": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "Meanwhile , during the time that Percy was closeted with his uncle in the library , that portion of the members of the `` Cheeryble Sisters ' Club '' which constituted the choice band of `` Inseparables , '' namely Maggie and Bessie Bradford , Belle Powers , Lily Norris , and Fanny Leroy , having joined forces on their way to Miss Ashton 's , had called in to see Lena", "context_len": 72}}}, "smitten": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "The wedding rejoicings continued all that day , with dancing-women and singing-women , and all the instruments of mirth and minstrelsy were smitten , whilst the queen and the Vizier and his son were exceeding assiduous in keeping up the festivities , so the Lady Bedrulbudour should rejoice and her chagrin be dispelled ; nay , they left nought that day of that which exciteth unto liesse but they did it before her , so she should leave what was in her mind and be cheered", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The lepers of whom we read in the Bible were obliged to dwell alone outside the camp ; and even king Uzziah , when smitten with leprosy , mighty monarch though he was , had to give up his throne and dwell by himself to the end of his days", "context_len": 50}}}, "affects": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "Why is it that a man of six feet looks double the size of a man of five , though in reality only one fifth larger ? It is an ocular deception which affects every one , but is not readily explained", "context_len": 42}}}, "endorsement": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "He never shall , Marie-Celeste said slowly , giving her full endorsement to that part of the proceeding , and Ted inwardly pronounced her a dearer child than ever", "context_len": 29}}}, "fines": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was rather a favourite habit of his to punish himself by fines for bad behaviour", "context_len": 16}}}, "voluntarily": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "And so , she went on in a softer tone , you have actually brought my gage home ! Oh , Sir Gervaise , -- and her eyes filled with tears -- my cousin has told me ! How could you have been so foolish as to remain voluntarily in captivity , that you might recover the gage a child had given you ?", "context_len": 64}}}, "sillies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "However , we do n't care anything about those poor little sillies", "context_len": 12}}}, "understandingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then she whispered something to Dorothy who nodded understandingly , retired to the back of the attic and returned with two plates , one of delicious little cakes and the other of caramels to which full justice was done", "context_len": 39}}}, "caramels": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "Then she whispered something to Dorothy who nodded understandingly , retired to the back of the attic and returned with two plates , one of delicious little cakes and the other of caramels to which full justice was done", "context_len": 39}}}, "furnish": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 100, "context": "She further mentioned that , as she felt sure her father would have despatched a party in pursuit of us , which , she expected , would by this time be , not far behind us , it would be very desirable to keep a watch for them , since it was important that we should know as much as possible of their movements ; and she accordingly suggested that I should climb a particularly lofty tree which she indicated , and keep a look-out for them , while she went off into the forest to seek the wherewithal to furnish a breakfast", "context_len": 103}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Fred saucily asked him if he had come home to set up an upholsterer 's shop , knowing he could himself furnish plenty of stuffing for mattresses and sofas", "context_len": 29}}}, "peeps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "However , by dint of peeps at the book between whiles , after devoting to it all the time she had before school was called to order , she managed to get through the recitation , yet not without many misgivings and a rapid beating of the heart when Miss Ashurst called upon her", "context_len": 54}}}, "whiles": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "However , by dint of peeps at the book between whiles , after devoting to it all the time she had before school was called to order , she managed to get through the recitation , yet not without many misgivings and a rapid beating of the heart when Miss Ashurst called upon her", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 77, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "devoting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "However , by dint of peeps at the book between whiles , after devoting to it all the time she had before school was called to order , she managed to get through the recitation , yet not without many misgivings and a rapid beating of the heart when Miss Ashurst called upon her", "context_len": 54}}}, "recitation": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 17, "context": "Harold 's principal achievement lay in The Walrus and the Carpenter , and Marie-Celeste 's in the recitation of Jabberwocky from Through the Looking-Glass ; for not only was she able to slip its almost unpronounceable words quite easily from her tongue , but she remembered the explanation of them given by Humpty Dumpty , when Alice appeals to him a little later on in the story , and he modestly informs her that he can explain all the poems that ever were invented , and a good many beside that have n't been invented just yet", "context_len": 97}}}, "glib": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Edna was always such a conscientious child about her lessons that Miss Ashurst rather overlooked the fact that upon this occasion she was not quite as glib as usual , and she took her seat with a feeling of great relief , determining that she would not forget her lessons another Saturday", "context_len": 52}}}, "determining": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "We gather from his statements , that at Odshi-ap\u00f3fa or `` Hickory Ground , '' which is a white town also , the `` priest , or fire-maker of the town '' had the privilege of determining the days of the busk , and that in doing so he was led by the ripening of the maize-crop and by the growth of the cassine-shrub", "context_len": 64}}}, "enrolled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` And indeed it did appeal so strongly to some of the older girls that before the week was out several new members were enrolled , and it was decided to change the time of meeting to Friday afternoon so that those in the city might have their convenience considered while the girls living in the country could easily stay in till a later hour", "context_len": 65}}}, "joyously": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 16, "context": "The children had their hooples , which they trundled merrily before them and Flossy went capering joyously along , sometimes running ahead , for a short distance , and then rushing back to his little mistresses , and if any rough boys made their appearance , keeping very close at their side till all danger was past", "context_len": 57}}}, "disbanded": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "Horner were by no means unaware of what was going on and they smiled to see how pleasant an atmosphere prevailed in the school all except in the unfortunate Neighborhood Club which they would have gladly disbanded", "context_len": 37}}}, "standards": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "All rose to look at them , not without anxiety ; as yet they were far distant , across a wild moor , but as they drew nearer , and their standards could be more clearly discerned , it became gradually evident that it was a reinforcement ; and so it proved , for heralds , galloping forward , announced the men of Dorsetshire", "context_len": 64}}}, "martyrs": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "What I mean is that we can 'be diligent and studious ' as the old copy-books used to have it , speak well of his school , and not carry tales home that will make our families think we are martyrs and that he is an ogre , or someone to be feared constantly", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "They fascinated her with their crude drawing and colours still vivid and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined", "context_len": 128}}}, "ogre": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 46, "context": "What I mean is that we can 'be diligent and studious ' as the old copy-books used to have it , speak well of his school , and not carry tales home that will make our families think we are martyrs and that he is an ogre , or someone to be feared constantly", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "I suppose my friend here , the doctor , has explained my errand -- the rest is -- you are the little white queen and I am an ogre come to capture you and take you away", "context_len": 37}}}, "created": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 28, "context": "When she was much younger , extreme carelessness had been her besetting fault , and , as is the manner of this `` little fox , '' had created much trouble for herself and for others ; but having become convinced that it was her duty to cure herself of this , she had set to work to do it in such earnest that that which had been a burden and a care to her was fast becoming a settled habit , and it was but seldom now that any act or word of heedlessness could be laid to her charge , while her ever obliging disposition and loving heart prompted many a deed of kindness which she never failed to carry out if it were in her", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Indeed , I find in my pony life that if you despise any created thing it surprises you", "context_len": 18}}}, "liable": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 24, "context": "It is dangerous , too , in the hands of a beginner , for it is then that it shows off , and is liable to run wild and chase some bystander in a most vigorous manner", "context_len": 37}}}, "furnishing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "Off they started down the wide street bordered by maples , now shorn of their leaves , but furnishing a carpet of yellow underfoot , past the church , the store , the schoolhouse and on to the old brown house sitting back behind an orchard of gnarled , crooked apple trees", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Ramsey insisted upon furnishing , or at least lending from her own home , so the next week saw the inmates happy as two birds in a nest", "context_len": 28}}}, "entertaining": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 10, "context": "She had been tired out with the railroad journey , entertaining as it was , then the excitement of meeting them all again , the bump on her forehead when she had come down so hard on Pansy 's head , and the screams that seemed like a stab going from temple to temple tired her inexpressibly", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 55, "context": "Stanton understood it no better than she did , perhaps not so well ; but he was very much amused ; and as he thought these young nieces and nephews of his were very wonderful little beings , he told Aunt Patty many of their droll sayings and doings , making himself so agreeable and entertaining , that by the time his sister came in , the old lady had almost forgotten that she had cause to be offended , and was not only quite ready to meet Mrs", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "One of her brothers remarks : She was very sociable , lively , and threw her whole heart into the kindly entertaining of guests of all ages", "context_len": 27}}}, "conway": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 0, "context": "Conway to her aunt", "context_len": 4}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Conway when he comes in that his daughter Edna is at home , '' said Ben and then he hung up the receiver", "context_len": 23}}}, "trolley": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 4, "context": "Tom thought the fifteen-mile trolley ride to Fairview was an unusually long one , but , as a matter of fact , it was soon accomplished , for he caught an express , and about eight o'clock that night arrived in the town where the co-educational institution was located", "context_len": 49}}}, "reconsider": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Knowing nothing of this , however , she continued to urge him to reconsider his determination to give up school and leave Oakdale", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` You 'd better reconsider that , stranger , '' said Jack , pulling out a revolver and fingering it significantly", "context_len": 21}}}, "cud": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` She was tellin ' me this mornin ' thot it would be aisy , and I cud have me afthernoon the same as usual , for he 'd not be in", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "She looked lovingly at the cows , lazily chewing their cud in the sunshine ; she felt sorry for her step-mother , as she strove to woo slumber to Polly 's wakeful eyes with the same lullaby which had done duty for the whole six ; she even found it in her heart to kiss Lemuel , who , with his ready talent for the unusual , was busily cramming mud paste into the seams of the little trunk which held her worldly all", "context_len": 84}}}, "av": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "Says she , ' a bit av a chicken will do and ye can make a pumpkin pie the day before , so what with a few pertaties and a taste of stewed tomats he 'll do bravely", "context_len": 38}}}, "unburdened": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Her little face wore a very serious and troubled look every time she looked at Uncle Justus that evening and the next day at recess she unburdened her heart to Dorothy and Jennie", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "She had scarcely unburdened herself of all her happenings , before in swarmed Uncle Bert and his family", "context_len": 18}}}, "frugal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "What girl of nine does not delight in such an experience as spending the night with a friend ? The thought of two Thanksgiving dinners , though one might be rather a frugal one , had its charm , too", "context_len": 40}}}, "prophesied": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 22, "context": "The only person who was not won over by Archie 's friendly ways and frank American manners was Joe , who gloomily prophesied broken necks and kindred evils as the result of the children 's association with 'the young master from furrin parts ' ; but I fear there was a good deal of jealousy in this , for poor Joe had been a hero to the children in his modest way , and it was hard to find himself suddenly supplanted in their affections , especially by a rival with whom it was quite impossible to compete", "context_len": 98}}}, "agreeing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "Of course Edna had told Celia about all this , and indeed it had been talked over at the club , all the girls agreeing that it was a perfectly lovely thing for Edna to do , so she came in quite exalted by all the approval", "context_len": 47}}}, "thanksgiving": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` My dear little girl , '' he replied , `` if that is the way you feel , I can only say that I am delighted beyond measure that you want to stay , and you will give me a greater cause for thanksgiving than I have expected or deserved , '' and he drew her to his knee", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 51, "context": "The huge Yule log burnt upon the hearth as it had done since it was lighted the night before ; a profusion of torches turned night into day ; the tables groaned with the weight of the good cheer ; in short , all was there which could express joy and thanksgiving", "context_len": 52}}}, "scalloped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Conway contained several things which made the dinner much more of a feast than it would otherwise have been , for there was a jar of tomato soup , a small chicken pie with scalloped leaves and little balls of crust on top , some delicious pickles , a glass of currant jelly and another of cranberry sauce", "context_len": 58}}}, "pickles": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "Conway contained several things which made the dinner much more of a feast than it would otherwise have been , for there was a jar of tomato soup , a small chicken pie with scalloped leaves and little balls of crust on top , some delicious pickles , a glass of currant jelly and another of cranberry sauce", "context_len": 58}}}, "cranberry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "Conway contained several things which made the dinner much more of a feast than it would otherwise have been , for there was a jar of tomato soup , a small chicken pie with scalloped leaves and little balls of crust on top , some delicious pickles , a glass of currant jelly and another of cranberry sauce", "context_len": 58}}}, "sauce": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "Conway contained several things which made the dinner much more of a feast than it would otherwise have been , for there was a jar of tomato soup , a small chicken pie with scalloped leaves and little balls of crust on top , some delicious pickles , a glass of currant jelly and another of cranberry sauce", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Then to think how good Uncle Isaac and his wife were to come down there and bring all those good things , just that we boys might have a good time ! Wasn \u2019 t that apple pudding and sauce good ?", "context_len": 42}}}, "greenhouse": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 6, "context": "The gardener had not opened the greenhouse ; but I found much more than I should have expected in the beginning of December , -- even rosebuds", "context_len": 27}}}, "toasty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` What was it yez were sayin ' about thim little toasty crusts for the soup", "context_len": 16}}}, "crusts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` What was it yez were sayin ' about thim little toasty crusts for the soup", "context_len": 16}}}, "overseeing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` She felt very important to be overseeing this piece of cookery and went in to call her uncle , feeling very much pleased at what had been accomplished", "context_len": 29}}}, "cookery": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "The rest giving a hearty and vigorous approval , she set to work to bake cakes in honour of the occasion , preserving such a halo of mystery round her cookery that the others were consumed with curiosity , and felt ready for any surprises", "context_len": 45}}}, "repast": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "They sat laughing over the rhymes and chattering as only schoolgirls know how until Aunt Helen came in to announce that a light repast of cake and lemonade awaited them in the dining-room , and to gently hint that , if Warford were to be reached before darkness had fallen , it was getting time for the six bicycles to be set in motion", "context_len": 64}}}, "talkative": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was really a merrier repast than Edna had ever eaten under that roof , for instead of eating his dinner in silence as he generally did , Uncle Justus was quite talkative and actually attempted to joke once in a while", "context_len": 42}}}, "cupboard": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 1, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then dressing , he took a couple of lines from a cupboard down-stairs , and went out", "context_len": 17}}}, "wicker": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "They were real gipsies , too , not the fair-haired hybrid pretenders who go about in neat caravans with muslin curtains to the windows , and wicker baskets slung on the top , but a dark-eyed , Spanish-looking crew , who put up a tent with a pole and a ragged blanket , and stewed their supper in a black caldron hung from three sticks set in the ground", "context_len": 69}}}, "outdid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The magnificence of the Ramsey 's dinner far outdid Aunt Elizabeth 's , but Edna did not enjoy it one whit the more , although it was very delightful to be served by a man in livery , and to have such exquisite china and glass to look at during the meal", "context_len": 52}}}, "inviolable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "It had been made an inviolable rule that not more than three articles were to be served , but when there were ice cream , delicious cakes and bon-bons , surely these were quite enough", "context_len": 35}}}, "elaborate": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 20, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Now he was standing in admiration before the great oak chair of State beneath the organ gallery , and now nothing loath he mounts the steps that lead to it and runs a finger along the curves of its elaborate carving , and then , with a most reverent air , touches the embroidered cross and garter with which it is decorated", "context_len": 62}}}, "attentions": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 41, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}}}, "evans": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 0, "context": "Evans , '' he hailed , as he recognized the regular flagman on duty for whom Wacker had been substituting for three days past", "context_len": 24}}}, "brilliancy": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "The tropical brilliancy of the girl , by reason of her red frock and the red ribbons in her hair , excites the jealousy of the little Mexicans and the paler children from the mining end of the town , and in their disapproval they style her `` Infanta", "context_len": 49}}}, "toll": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "The mountaineers had been accustomed to exact tribute here of all who passed , having a sort of right , derived from ancient usage , to the payment of a toll", "context_len": 31}}}, "freight": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 41, "context": "[ A ] In 1859 , Ben Holladay had sixteen large steamers running between San Francisco and Panama , Oregon , China and Japan , operated 5,000 miles of daily stage coaches , with 500 coaches and express wagons , 500 freight wagons , 5,000 horses and mules with oxen beyond counting", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "It was , however , forty feet from any tracks , and for convenience sake , when the railroad gave up the burned building which they had occupied for unclaimed freight storage , it had been turned over to the express people", "context_len": 42}}}, "unprotected": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 48, "context": "I shall merely say that I have intelligence of the arrival here of a fleet of Moorish corsairs , of whose strength I am ignorant , but that assuredly their intention is to make a raid on the commerce of the coast , and perhaps to land at unprotected places", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Will it be safe to leave our stuff in camp , unprotected ? ''", "context_len": 15}}}, "perishing": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 35, "context": "M\u00fcller , with the following comment : `` My wife and I having , through the exceeding riches of God 's grace , been brought to the Lord Jesus , wish to lay aside the perishing gold of the world for the unsearchable riches of Christ , and send the enclosed for the support of the orphans ''", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "When it was sufficiently close to the shore William Darling sprang out to help the weary perishing creatures , whilst Grace was left to manage the boat unaided", "context_len": 28}}}, "unfasten": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "The cart came to a sudden stop in an archway under the keep , and , with a hurried warning : `` Look alive ; the sun \u2019 s nearly up '' , the men proceeded to unfasten the tarpaulin", "context_len": 40}}}, "removing": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 67, "context": "Then , if that was so , would not the very best thing Faith could do be to go to Him first herself , and tell Him that Roy was lost -- that he had gone quite , quite far away , and ask Him to help her to find him ? She placed the Bible on the table , got a duster , and , tenderly removing its dust , opened it", "context_len": 73}}}, "rubbers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` She slipped off the garment and hung it over the back of a chair , then she removed her rubbers and came over to the fire to dry the edge of her skirt", "context_len": 34}}}, "wanderer": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "It was obvious that he was nothing better than a pirate , but he managed to gloss over the barbarities of the life so well , and to dwell on its picturesque and adventurous side so successfully , that Dick began insensibly to soften in his judgment of the wanderer", "context_len": 50}}}, "greenhouses": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "Granby made the most extraordinary flowers of red flannel and gilt paper , -- flowers whose likeness never grew in gardens or greenhouses of any known land ; had she not baked sugar cakes which were intended to represent men and women , pigs , horses , and cows ? Were not the branches looped with gay ribbons ? Did they not bear rosy-cheeked apples , an orange for each child , some cheap but much prized toys , and , better than all , several useful and greatly needed articles , which had been the gift of Mrs", "context_len": 99}}}, "fatherless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Oh , '' Edna felt very sorry that Nettie was fatherless , but she did not know exactly what to say about it", "context_len": 24}}}, "shamefacedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` There is n't so very much to get , '' returned Nettie a little shamefacedly", "context_len": 16}}}, "thickening": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The shadows were thickening and night was at hand as he crossed the fields and reached the road to the north of the academy", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It seemed to grow dark suddenly , but it was due to the thickening of the storm clouds overhead", "context_len": 19}}}, "flanked": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Oh , I guess we can tackle a slice , '' remarked William , and Fenn went proudly to the stove , where the pudding was being kept hot , and soon had it on the table , flanked by two bowls of savory sauce", "context_len": 46}}}, "warmed": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 62, "context": "Schwitz had promised that his charges should be small , but how he never would give in his bill , the policeman and his wife thinking all the while that it was kindness which kept him from doing so ; how it had taken every cent of his salary to pay the other expenses of illness , and keep the family barely warmed and fed ; of the disappointment of their hopes for Willie for , at least , some time to come ; and finally of the terrible bill which Dr", "context_len": 92}}}, "bookcase": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 17, "context": "The walls were adorned with school drawings and prize maps , fastened up with tacks ; a bookcase of much-read volumes filled the space between the fireplace and the window ; and in the corner might be found a miscellaneous collection of cricket-bats , fishing-rods , tennis-racquets , bows and arrows , croquet-mallets , sticks , balls and ninepins , and other articles very dear to the children 's hearts , but which Nancy generally classed impartially under the head of 'lumber", "context_len": 82}}}, "engravings": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "There must be so many lovely places in this great world ! Over the ocean where Miss Armitage had been , and she recalled the castles and palaces and beautiful woods , and peasants dancing on the green and laughing ; that she had seen in the portfolio of engravings", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "ENGRAVINGS", "context_len": 1}}}, "rocker": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "The genial landlord , who had joined the group at the fire , was relating to a listening and an eager guest another story of the almost superhuman performances of the Union scout , Leroy , when suddenly the lad arose from the rocker and began to search the floor with his eyes", "context_len": 53}}}, "haircloth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The chairs matched the sofa , one being a comfortable rocker with cover of haircloth", "context_len": 15}}}, "barring": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "[ Footnote 258 : A proverbial expression , meaning that , as he did not absolutely kill Alaeddin , though doing what was ( barring a miracle ) certain to cause his death , he could not be said to be his slayer ; a piece of casuistry not peculiar to the East , cf", "context_len": 55}}}, "shutters": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 23, "context": "It looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and", "context_len": 128}}}, "escorted": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 39, "context": "Her three cousins -- Gwendolyn , Russell , and Belle -- had greeted her cordially as soon as the train drew up in a station which , for size and grandeur , surpassed her wildest dreams , and then escorted her between a bewildering panorama of flashing lights , brilliant shop windows , swiftly moving cars , and people in an endless stream to another depot , for her Uncle Robert resided in the suburbs", "context_len": 75}}}, "panes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "It was by far the oldest part of the house ; the great beams of the roof , roughly hewn out with an axe and black with age , had been a portion of the ancient castle , and so had the mullioned windows , with their deep , old-fashioned seats and diamond panes , filled with green , uneven glass", "context_len": 61}}}, "banked": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "Toward the ca\u00f1on rose the tipple and fans of the Dauntless colliery , banked in slack and slate , and surrounded by paintless mine-houses , while to the right swept the ugly shape of the company 's store", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The train , upon arrival at the place of embarkation , ran straight into the docks , and brought up close to one of the many transports that were berthed there with banked fires ready to sail at any hour of the day or night", "context_len": 45}}}, "snowed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "Black had been snowed in , for her sister lived quite a distance from the station , but she had at last been able to get some one of the neighbors to bring her across , as he had to go to the doctor 's , and was willing to take her the short distance further", "context_len": 56}}}, "impossibility": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 48, "context": "Lilian 's experience of concerts was confined to an occasional afternoon performance in the Warford Assembly Rooms , or the military band in the Spa gardens ; but she bravely hid her longings for better things , for she knew that a musical education would be an utter impossibility in the family circumstances , and that Father had strained a point already to allow her to learn from Herr Frankenburg , who was the most expensive teacher in the school", "context_len": 80}}}, "housewifely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "With housewifely pride , Audrey had dished up her country fare , and smiled to see her guest 's enjoyment of it", "context_len": 22}}}, "footstep": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "So Aunt Patty sat , and sadly thought , unconscious of the wistful pair of eyes which watched her from the other room , until by and by a gentle footstep came stealing round her chair , a soft little hand timidly slipped itself into her own , and she turned to see Bessie 's sweet face looking at her , half in pity , half in wonder", "context_len": 68}}}, "evidence": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 81, "word_index": 21, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}}}, "shovel": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` She went to the back door to view the prospect , and tried to shovel away some of the snow , but it was slow work", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 50, "context": "My first impulse was to carry the body down to where the soil was softer , and there dig a grave for it ; but while I was considering this plan , it occurred to me that , with no more efficient tool than a spear to serve as a shovel , it would be practically impossible for me to bury the body deep enough to protect it from the jackals and hyaenas ; and I therefore determined that , instead of burying it , I would burn it", "context_len": 89}}}, "wearying": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 99, "context": "or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length , as well because it is an important one as to show the almost insuperable difficulties that beset the", "context_len": 128}}}, "rummage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "'There might be something underneath , ' said Peggy , beginning to rummage the chest to the very bottom ; but her hopes were soon dashed , for a further search did not bring anything more to light", "context_len": 38}}}, "codfish": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` There are two potatoes , but they are n't very big , '' she said , `` and there is some codfish", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "They would have gone hungry had not some of the passengers hauled in a batch of codfish", "context_len": 17}}}, "desserts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "She was n't compelled to eat her whole breakfast off of oatmeal , and always had such lovely desserts for dinner", "context_len": 21}}}, "cupful": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "Aunt Hetty drank a good cupful , hot , and the remainder was set out on the window sill", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "measured": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 26, "context": "She is sometimes almost ashamed of feeling , as she says , so ridiculously young , but after all one 's age should not always be measured by one 's years , and wherever she may go , or whatever she may do , Peggy will keep that most priceless of possessions , the heart of a little child , without which , equally in this world or the next , it is impossible to enter into the Kingdom of Heaven", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Ben had now opportunity to see who his deliverers were , and to thank them , which he did in no measured terms", "context_len": 23}}}, "laborious": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 32, "context": "After some time the rice was transferred to another and larger saucepan and was soon boiling `` like mad , '' then the eggs were fried and after a somewhat anxious and laborious period of time the dinner was pronounced ready", "context_len": 41}}}, "foundation": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 13, "context": "French was the common language , for as the Order was of French foundation , and three of the seven langues belonged to that country , most of the high dignitaries being chosen from their ranks , it was natural that the French language should be the general medium of communication between them", "context_len": 53}}}, "jangling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Her faith was not without foundation for along in the middle of the afternoon they heard jangling bells , and ran to the front window to see the milkman in a huge sleigh , his milk cans in the body of it", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "At that moment above the jangling , clumping activity of the yards there arose on the night air one frightful , piercing shriek", "context_len": 23}}}, "snyder": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Snyder , '' said Nettie , `` I am so glad you have come", "context_len": 14}}}, "middles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Snyder 's pocket with a promise of sure delivery , and he went off , his horses plunging through the deep drifts up to their middles", "context_len": 26}}}, "provender": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There 's a lot of provender in that basket and we 'll be as jolly as they make 'em", "context_len": 19}}}, "scuttles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Got a candle ? Where are the coal scuttles ? One of you hold the light and show me your coal bin and up comes your coal", "context_len": 27}}}, "disturbances": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "DISTURBANCES", "context_len": 1}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "The Almighty , to whom my brother attributes the glory , has hitherto watched over Benares in so marked a manner , that it remained quiet in the midst of disturbances ; and my young niece has bravely ridden through it by her father \u2019 s side , giving confidence to the timid by her fearlessness", "context_len": 56}}}, "dazzlingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was so dazzlingly bright when the little girls arose that they thought it must be much later than it was", "context_len": 21}}}, "muffins": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 90, "context": "`` The fire was THERE '' , pointing with her finger , `` an ' the table was before it ; an ' the lamp was there , an ' the light looked rosy red ; an ' there was a satin cover on your bed , an ' a warm rug on the floor , an ' everythin ' looked beautiful ; an ' '' -- she paused a second , and laid her hand on her stomach tenderly -- '' there WAS soup an ' sandwiches an ' muffins -- there WAS", "context_len": 94}}}, "apprenticeship": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 12, "context": "Hartley , the little maid said proudly , confident that her long apprenticeship had made her fully equal to the occasion , and inwardly rejoicing in the full sense of responsibility", "context_len": 31}}}, "nettie": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Nettie considered this for some time before she quite took in the sense of it", "context_len": 15}}}, "fresher": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I never saw fresher eggs than your hens lay , '' he said , looking at Nettie with a serious face", "context_len": 22}}}, "interestedly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Do you think you will be able to walk more and more ? '' asked Edna interestedly", "context_len": 18}}}, "select": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 16, "context": "As you are aware , the sultan has the choice of any one slave he may select from each batch brought in , and assuredly he would choose this one , did it come to his ears , or to the ears of one of his officers , that a Christian knight had been landed", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 76, "context": "Peggy and Bobby liked the fun of the preparations , though they crept out of the way of the visitors as much as possible , for to be shown up in the drawing-room involved an amount of dressing and tidying which did not fit in at all with their ideas of holiday enjoyment , and they much preferred a picnic tea in the orchard , with Rollo and the rabbits for company , to the more select charms of the best china and the stately patronage of the neighbouring dowagers", "context_len": 90}}}, "uninterruptedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Matters went on at school uninterruptedly , until just before Christmas , when it was suddenly made known that Miss Ashurst was to be married , and that another teacher would take her place after the holidays", "context_len": 37}}}, "rivals": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 69, "context": "Nancy 's evenings for some time past had been absorbed in the construction of a bright heliotrope gown with gilt buttons , and she had walked into Warford on her day out , and spent a month 's wages upon a hat , which was such a marvellous erection of flowers and feathers combined with lace , chiffon , and ribbons , that it was calculated to leave her rivals , like the Queen of Sheba , with no more spirit in them", "context_len": 83}}}, "innuendoes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Seabrooke went about the business of the day with all his accustomed regularity and precision , but with a sort of defiant and I-am-going-to-stick-to-it air about him which in itself incited the other boys to covert thrusts and innuendoes tending to throw distrust upon his version of the story and to make known their thorough sympathy with Percy , not only for his loss , but also for the aspersions cast upon him by the young pupil-teacher", "context_len": 77}}}, "caricature": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "It came out later that the drawings were the proof of the child 's guilt , for they were done in the same style as the caricature and because they were so much better than the rest it was evident that only Clara could have made the figure on the board", "context_len": 51}}}, "scrawling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "It did keep up till one morning the climax was reached when Miss Newman came into her school-room to find on the board a very good caricature of herself , with under it written : `` Ugly , old Miss New , '' in scrawling letters", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Jake Amsden affixed his signature in a large scrawling hand , and the two witnesses subscribed after him", "context_len": 18}}}, "clara": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Clara came into the school-room late , and slipped into her seat after the exercises had begun", "context_len": 17}}}, "fiercer": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "The Thane and his children , accompanied by Alfgar , paused on their homeward road , and when the drowsy tinkling ceased , deep silence seemed to fall over the landscape , while the night darkened -- if darkness it could be called when the moonbeams succeeded to the fiercer light of the glowing orb of day", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Each moment their number increased , and the war cry of the Order rose louder and fiercer above the din", "context_len": 20}}}, "impertinent": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 60, "context": "`` You do n't make a very pleasant prospect for me to be in , '' said Bessie , looking from brother to uncle with grave displeasure , `` and if a little boy like you , Fred , says that to me when I am a big lady , I shall say , 'My dear , you are very impertinent", "context_len": 61}}}, "gentler": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "By his side , similarly dressed , stood his son , the hopeful Canute , the future King of England , then only in his twelfth year , but already showing himself a true cub of the old tiger in fierceness and valour , yet not devoid of nobler and gentler virtues , as he afterwards showed", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` For Uncle Justus had never shown the boys his gentler side and they stood in great awe of him , scuttling out of sight whenever they saw him coming", "context_len": 30}}}, "separately": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 50, "context": "When they reached the dining-room , Miss Belmore was already seated at the table , ready to receive them ; but as places were set for only three , two little hearts were again doomed to disappointment , for two little minds , without any sort of consultation , had separately arrived at the conclusion that all that elaborate preparation could certainly mean nothing less than luncheon with Her Majesty in person", "context_len": 72}}}, "undertone": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 17, "context": "My father spoke truly , for though the stranger had uttered his lengthy speech but in an undertone , yet I , with the curiosity of youth , did not fail to hear , much to my mystification", "context_len": 38}}}, "suspecting": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 60, "context": "The strong sentiment of love and devotion with which he inspired all around him at the commencement of his career , was now gone , and his generals and officers were continually planning schemes to depose him from the power which he seemed no longer to have the energy to wield ; or , at least , Alexander was continually suspecting that such plans were formed , and he was kept in a continual state of uneasiness and anxiety in discovering and punishing them", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "They had unfortunately given him a bed in a chamber which looked towards his home : he had chanced to wake , had looked from the window , seen the flames , and had started thither at once , swimming the moat when he could not cross the drawbridge -- suspecting , doubtless , that he was surrounded by treachery", "context_len": 60}}}, "illustrate": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 62, "context": "The tale now presented to the indulgence of the public is the second of a series of tales , each complete in itself , which , as stated in the preface to the first of the series , have been told to the senior boys of a large school , in order to secure their interest in historical characters , and to illustrate great epochs in human affairs by the aid of fiction", "context_len": 73}}}, "outright": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was as much as they could do to keep from laughing outright , they were so very funny , but they signed their names in the corner as Miss Newman directed them to do , and handed them in", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` It would do a great deal of good , but instead of giving the money outright how would you children like to buy materials to make fancy articles for the bazar ? In that way I have n't a doubt but you would get a better return", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "There was never any telling where Maggie 's ideas would carry her , and at the thought of the droll fate she had imagined for Aunt Patty , Miss Rush fairly laughed outright , and even Bessie smiled , after which she said she would go up-stairs and talk a little to her mother , which always did her good when she was in trouble", "context_len": 65}}}, "perked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Clara perked up and looked around with a little smirk", "context_len": 10}}}, "smirk": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then she turned and made him a low bow , a hideous smirk cracking the wrinkles of her withered face", "context_len": 20}}}, "acknowledgment": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 42, "context": "MY DEAR SIR , -- When Queen Elizabeth gave me that beautiful bag on Friday night , I was not aware that it contained a Letter Patent which I prize highly ; and for which I ought to have returned my grateful acknowledgment at the time it was delivered", "context_len": 49}}}, "unprincipled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 65, "context": "The affair made a great impression but somehow did not increase Miss Newman 's popularity , for the idea of the drawings was hers and Clara could not forgive her for the position into which she had forced her , therefore she lost no opportunity of making it as unpleasant for her teacher as she could in the thousand and one ways a sly and unprincipled girl can , and her little pin-pricks were so annoying , that finally Dorothy and Edna , who had not particularly cared for the new teacher , began to stand up for her and to do as many kind things as they could", "context_len": 109}}}, "annoying": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 75, "context": "The affair made a great impression but somehow did not increase Miss Newman 's popularity , for the idea of the drawings was hers and Clara could not forgive her for the position into which she had forced her , therefore she lost no opportunity of making it as unpleasant for her teacher as she could in the thousand and one ways a sly and unprincipled girl can , and her little pin-pricks were so annoying , that finally Dorothy and Edna , who had not particularly cared for the new teacher , began to stand up for her and to do as many kind things as they could", "context_len": 109}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 70, "context": "`` How are we to begin ? Shall we do penance like the old monks ? Do you know , Rosamund '' -- here Irene linked her thin , almost steel-like little hand inside Rosamund 's arm -- '' that I am a most voracious reader ? Father was a great collector of books , and when I am tired of frightening the servants , and terrifying Frosty , and annoying mother , I spend days at a time in his library swallowing down the contents of his books", "context_len": 89}}}, "adherents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "She had won her way into the hearts of most of the girls , and there were only two or three of Clara 's most adoring adherents who still called her `` a pet '' and said she was at the bottom of all Clara 's trouble", "context_len": 47}}}, "hatefulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` You see , '' she said , `` if she could once find out what doing to others really means she maybe would get over all her hatefulness", "context_len": 29}}}, "noised": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was speedily noised about in the mining-camp that a party of horse-thieves had had the audacity to visit the settlement , and were even now guests of the Golden Gulch Hotel", "context_len": 32}}}, "associate": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 53, "context": "Lawyer Frances , however , was far from it ; and immediately , by various questions , he tried to show that Bern , knowing the dangerous and desperate character of Stone , had tried to get him dropped from the school because he did not believe he was a fit person to associate with the academy scholars", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "The Sultan proceeded to make familiar with Alaeddin and to talk with him , and Alaeddin answered him with all courtliness and fluency , as he had been bred in kings ' palaces or as he were their constant associate ; [ 471 ] and the more the talk was prolonged between them , the more gladness and joy redoubled on the Sultan for that which he heard of the goodliness of Alaeddin 's answers and the sweetness of his speech", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Finally Rosy herself was described as the plane that sighted convoy , sank same , and retired to a desert island , where she became a sort of Empress Jones , too proud to come home and associate with her sister Fortresses", "context_len": 42}}}, "furnishings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then , by grace of his own idea of fitness , Harold had added to the other furnishings a Dresden china toilet-set , and in this he was perhaps far wiser than he knew , for is there anything so well calculated to captivate at sight the heart of a dainty little maiden as the mysterious round-topped boxes that compose the dainty outfit of the ideal dressing-table ? Then , to crown it all , a pair of ponies and a basket-phaeton had been purchased for the exclusive use of the guests that were to be", "context_len": 96}}}, "showiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "MacDonald did not believe in showiness", "context_len": 6}}}, "banjo": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "He kept everyone going , never allowed a dull moment , and if nothing else was planned , he would pick up his banjo and give a funny coon song , so that it was no wonder Mrs", "context_len": 38}}}, "prelude": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "It was blue over the house ; but in the distance , coming rapidly nearer and nearer , was a terrible black cloud -- a cloud almost as black as ink -- and already there were murmurs in the trees and cawings among the birds , the breeze growing stronger and stronger -- the prelude to a great agitation of nature", "context_len": 61}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The roar of a jaguar might prelude its spring", "context_len": 9}}}, "quizzing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "He has been quizzing me all day about my orphan asylum party , but I know he liked my going", "context_len": 20}}}, "honorary": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 59, "context": "It appears long to have been a maxim of their policy to give equal liberty and protection to tribes conquered by themselves , as well as to those vanquished by others , although many individuals taken in war are slaves among them , and their children are called of the slave race , and can not arrive to much honorary distinction in the country , on that account", "context_len": 68}}}, "discoursed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "We frequently discoursed on the probable events that were occurring at home , but our guards refrained from giving us any information on the subject , though one or two of our new custodians spoke a few words of broken English at intervals", "context_len": 43}}}, "offset": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "We are square now , Joe , said the captain ; your scorching will do to offset the fright you gave me , when I thought I had shot Ben , having put one bullet through the window , and the other into a milk-pan of eggs , on the dresser", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "He knew well enough what he ought to do , namely , seize the drowning man by the hair , and then turn upon his own back and float , drawing the other after him ; but on trying this a difficulty met him at the offset : the man 's hair was very short ; but he got over it by grasping his ears , and then , throwing himself back , he struck out with his legs so as to keep afloat and go with the racing current", "context_len": 90}}}, "voted": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 20, "context": "The question of decorations was much discussed , for while Father suggested hanging the walls with sacking , and Lilian voted for garlanding them with wild flowers , both ideas were rejected , the one as too prosaic and the other as not sufficiently durable , and it was not until Peggy conceived the brilliant thought of lining their dwelling with moss that a satisfactory solution was arrived at", "context_len": 69}}}, "dilemma": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "Getting a hint of the dilemma , the Rector came to the rescue , and invited the performers to give an exhibition of their skill and talent on the occasion of the Sunday-school tea-party , which was to take place early in the New Year", "context_len": 45}}}, "columns": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 19, "context": "To stand stealthily among these dark and somber mountains , with the defiles and passes below filled with the columns of his small but undaunted army , and to look onward , a few miles beyond , and see the countless fires of the vast hosts which had got between him and all hope of retreat to his native land ; to feel , as he must have done , that his fate , and that of all who were with him , depended upon the events of the day that was soon to dawn -- to see and feel these things must have made this night one of the most exciting and solemn scenes in the conqueror 's life", "context_len": 120}}}, "quickest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Why , of course , but you see the main thing is to get it done as quickly as possible , for the one who gets the answer to the puzzle the quickest and who has the clearest answer will get the first prize", "context_len": 45}}}, "clearest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "But wherever their thoughts may have been , they instantly both stopped thinking , for first they heard the familiar bugle-call of the steamer ring out on the air in the clearest sort of a whistle ; and then -- could they believe their eyes ? -- there stood Marie-Celeste and Harold right before them on the other side of the hawthorn", "context_len": 62}}}, "soonest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Oh , I think so , for it does n't say you must n't have any help ; it just says the one who sends it in the soonest", "context_len": 30}}}, "busying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "And indeed it would appear that this small maiden -- small even for her six years -- did think so ; for as she talked she was trotting about the room , busying herself with arranging half a dozen trifles , which her quick eye spied out , and which , according to her way of thinking , were not just in proper order", "context_len": 64}}}, "kiddies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "He watched him as a cat would watch a mouse , and ate everything he ate and drank whatever he drank -- -- No one had coffee or tea , they had milk , cocoa , buttermilk , and cold water , which latter the kiddies drank as if it had been something that would make them super-children", "context_len": 58}}}, "puzzles": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 80, "context": "`` Why you see , '' Edna began , `` Nettie has been taken into the club , and when her time comes to have the club meeting she wo n't have any way of getting the refreshments , so we thought and thought of what we could do to get some money , and last night I saw in the Children 's Corner of the Times that they would give prizes for guessing a puzzle , you know those puzzles , Cousin Ben", "context_len": 84}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` What puzzles me , '' rejoined Dick , `` is how any of us came out of it alive", "context_len": 20}}}, "laudable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "With the laudable intention of keeping the men 's minds fully occupied during the hours of leisure , the officers had arranged for the boxing-tournament at an early stage of the voyage", "context_len": 32}}}, "crocuses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "I know the grass is green and the sky is blue , and the crocuses and violets are coming out just as they used to when I could see , but I do n't want them to tell me of it all the time ; and they forget , and it makes me feel worse", "context_len": 55}}}, "uppermost": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "An involuntary cry of horror broke from us ; then , to our surprise , we saw the man reappear , brandishing a sheath-knife , while the shark , in its last throes , floated belly uppermost , a skilful thrust of the knife having practically disembowelled it", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "The Tonica village , the lowest of them , was two days ' travel from Thysia , the uppermost ( Margry IV , 180", "context_len": 24}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "I saw that he was ill at ease , but we did not mention the subject , which I am sure was uppermost in both our minds , lest we should alarm the gentle ones", "context_len": 35}}}, "aroma": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was a pleasant aroma of gingerbread pervading the house , and the fire in the open grate looked very cheerful", "context_len": 21}}}, "chickabiddy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Well , chickabiddy , '' she said , `` where have you been ? You are as rosy as an apple", "context_len": 22}}}, "woodsy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "The boys scampered off out of doors to scour the place for nuts or to dive into unfrequented woodsy places , while the girls gathered around the crowing baby , in high good-humor with herself and the world at large", "context_len": 40}}}, "terrace": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 107, "context": "Never in all this world was there a happier little host than Harold Harris when he found how kindly his guests from across the water were taking to the life at Windsor ; but who would not have taken kindly to it , I should like to know ? The Queen herself , in her great castle on the hill , could not have planned more for the comfort of her guests than did Harold in his little castle beneath it ; and , indeed , this name of Little Castle had somehow attached itself to the pretty stone house , with its round tower and moat-shaped terrace", "context_len": 108}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "baking": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` We 've got to get up good and early , '' Reliance remarked , `` for there 's a heap to be done , even if we are ahead with the baking", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "Dinner at length being over ( though later than usual , on account of the time occupied in baking the pie , and later , still , by reason of the goodness of it ) , they prepared to start , taking with them an axe to build a camp , tinder in a horn , flint , steel , and matches , which were made by dipping splinters of wood into melted brimstone , and which would burn when touched to the spark in the tinder", "context_len": 87}}}, "goody": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 6, "context": "You see he was n't a goody boy at all , she said , when all was told , just brave and grand", "context_len": 23}}}, "unbeknownst": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "That 's a good deal to spring on a fellow unbeknownst", "context_len": 11}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Brown , sometimes made so bold as to steal in unbeknownst and curl up on the cool marble in a dark corner of the choir , and Albert , who knew the corner well , at once slipped away in the hope of finding him", "context_len": 45}}}, "occupying": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 24, "context": "But the cannon of that period were so cumbrous and slowly worked , and so inaccurate in their aim , that the advantage of occupying a position that would prevent an enemy from firing down into a town was considered to be more than counterbalanced by the weakening of the garrison by the abstraction of the force required to man the detached work , and by the risk of their being surrounded and cut off without the garrison of the town being able to aid them", "context_len": 86}}}, "crusty": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 76, "context": "It was certainly Peggy who suggested sitting on the top of the haystack , and making it sway backwards and forwards like a swing , a delightful sensation while it lasted , but which ended suddenly in the collapse of the whole top of the stack , much to Father 's wrath , for it took David a couple of hours to repair the damage , and certainly did not sweeten the old man 's already crusty temper", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "He was not generally a very attractive person , being snuffy in appearance and crusty in temper , but to-night no fairy prince could have been more appreciated , and they flew to him , each seizing one of his horny hands with an enthusiasm which much surprised him", "context_len": 49}}}, "kneading": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` She went on kneading her bread while the children watched her", "context_len": 12}}}, "raspberry": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "Just at first they almost gibbered because they did not know what to say , but when they were taken below and allowed to wash the smoke and powder off their faces and hands , and then were given cups of tea and muffins and raspberry jam , and then were shown all over the beautiful ship , they could not help but begin to be calm", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "It 's raspberry shortcake", "context_len": 4}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 79, "context": "Here was a quantity of ground covered with brush , logs , and bushes : to have hauled all this away would have been an endless job , and after that the ground could neither be ploughed nor planted , being entirely matted with green roots , and cold and sour ; besides , the moment the sun was let into it , sprouts would begin to spring up from the stumps , and weeds , blackberry , and raspberry bushes from the ground , and cover it all over", "context_len": 90}}}, "shrub": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 38, "context": "The swift-falling darkness , the knowledge that danger was all about them , that insane enemies were skulking perhaps behind a tree , leering at them from branches overhead , crouching in ambush behind a concealing bush or shrub , at any moment to reach out a hairy arm -- all these things combined to fill them with terror", "context_len": 59}}}, "donate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Rudolph is going to donate some photographs and will take orders for others ; then some of the boys are going into the woods for tiny little cedar and pine trees which we are going to plant in pots", "context_len": 39}}}, "ale": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "[ Handwritten : We agree to abstain from all Liquors of an Intoxicating Quality , whether ale porter Wine , or Ardent Spirits , except as Medicine", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 69, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}}}, "chunks": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "In a twinkling the emigrants were gazing upon his back , the ends of the fluttering handkerchief , fringes of clothing , streaming tail and flying hoofs of the pony , which flung chunks of earth into the air as he skimmed away on the wings of the wind", "context_len": 49}}}, "crumb": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The refreshments received even more praise than Edna had predicted , for not a crumb of gingerbread , not a single maple-sugar cream , nor a drop of raspberry shrub was left , and the honorary member went home in an exalted frame of mind", "context_len": 45}}}, "handsomer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "No , Charlie , they are only singed on the outside ; there was not power enough in burning grass to heat the roots , as though they had stood in the woods among the brush ; and the trees on the bank will be replaced by others , and perhaps handsomer ones", "context_len": 53}}}, "grudging": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "It was given rather a grudging glance , but it was really too pretty not to be admired and Clara replied with a show of indifference , `` It is quite pretty , is n't it ? ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "spurned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "There was a time when Clara might have spurned even this kind offer , setting it down as `` trying to get in '' with her , but her pride and vanity had received a blow when the Neighborhood Club was broken up and she cast forth , and she took the offer in the spirit in which it was meant", "context_len": 61}}}, "proposing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "No one could appreciate what new life had opened up for the old couple with the coming of Chris and Ted and Donald , and now two were proposing to go at once , and only five weeks more , and Chris would be bidding them farewell on his way to the Majestic down at Liverpool , and on which it had been arranged that Donald at the same time should once more put to sea", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I thought of proposing that", "context_len": 6}}}, "affable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "They did not know much about her home life , for she was not the affable person Miss Ashurst had been", "context_len": 21}}}, "delayed": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 1, "context": "We delayed our departure on one excuse and another , until finally Bill , who was to be our guide , grew irritable ; and even then we made a further delay while Jack pencilled a note to his colonel , which Harry was to take charge of as long as there was danger of his capture by roving bands of Federals , and then it was to be given to the guide , who thought he could insure its delivery", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Barry reefed back sharply , gaining altitude in the few precious seconds before the delayed action blast arrived", "context_len": 18}}}, "unpretty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Yes , I do ; she is so unpretty", "context_len": 10}}}, "flippantly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` She looks like a cross old thing , '' returned Celia rather flippantly", "context_len": 14}}}, "pondering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was easy to see , from the grave look on Marie-Celeste 's face , that she was still pondering the sad predicament of no particular father or mother , but she asked , Where was the hospital , Donald ?", "context_len": 41}}}, "spine": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 21, "context": "Jane came and gave her a bath , rubbed her softly but thoroughly across the hips and up and down the spine , holding her up with one strong arm", "context_len": 30}}}, "gentlest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "This story is so much about Peggy and her satellite Bobby that we have rather neglected Lilian , and she deserves a chapter all to herself ; for she was one of the sweetest , gentlest , most unselfish of girls , who filled a quiet niche in the little world of her home which would have been sadly empty without her", "context_len": 62}}}, "wistfulness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "But a better arrangement than that was made , for when Margaret and Jennie heard of the affair they were so eager to be included in the party , that Miss Newman noticing their wistfulness , asked if they , too , would come", "context_len": 44}}}, "pleasantest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` That is just like the Ramseys , '' said Agnes , `` they always come forward at just the right moment and do the thing that makes it pleasantest all around", "context_len": 32}}}, "screened": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "There were indeed lots of blackberries in the spot to which Fred had called them ; and , screened from the rays of the sun , they picked them with comfort ; besides which , many a large berry which they did not pick themselves found its way into their pails ; so that , by the time Hafed came with his offering to Maggie , her own berries made quite a show , and she steadily refused to take his", "context_len": 81}}}, "bamboo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "One end of the porch was screened by a bamboo shade", "context_len": 11}}}, "pillows": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 15, "context": "She found beautiful new flowers growing in her room , whimsical little gifts tucked under pillows , and once , as they sat together in the evening , they heard the scratch of a heavy paw on the door , and when Sara went to find out what it was , there stood a great dog -- a splendid Russian boarhound -- with a grand silver and gold collar bearing an inscription", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The cradle was Jennie 's especial charge , and she never suffered any one else to arrange it ; but now she pulled the clothes and pillows about as if they had done something to offend her", "context_len": 37}}}, "wavy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "He is a noble looking youth truly , Isaac , with his fair , wavy hair , his grey eyes , and white skin ; truly , all my neighbours will envy me such a possession", "context_len": 36}}}, "tallest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Dorothy I know her by her hair , and Margaret because she is the tallest of them , so of course the one left must be Jennie", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "Who but she could climb to the very highest branch of the tallest tree ? Who but she could swing so high that she seemed almost to turn a somersault in the air before she came down again ? Who but she could invent the most daring games ? And then , when all other things failed , who but she could tell such weird stories ? Her eyes shone ; her lips were wreathed in smiles", "context_len": 77}}}, "finely": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 15, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "widen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "They had not a word to say for they had not understood how a little ripple of kindness may widen till it touches an unknown shore", "context_len": 26}}}, "merriest": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "Along the perfect English road they scampered , and out to Virginia Water , at the merriest pace , and then home again at a better pace still , so alluring to their pony imaginations were the box stalls and oats that lay in that direction", "context_len": 46}}}, "anticipation": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 63, "context": "But who could long be grave at the outset of so promising an expedition ! The idea of a leisurely driving trip through the lovely Lake Country , stopping here and there , as the spirit moved them , at the comfortable little inns and hotels that abound in the region , had been such a supremely delightful idea , even in mere anticipation , that now that they were actually off enthusiasm knew no bounds , and mirth was literally unconfined", "context_len": 82}}}, "wrapt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The children listened in wrapt attention", "context_len": 6}}}, "ramsey": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ramsey at the Parker House , and to get ready for their journey home , but there was a chance to walk through the botanical gardens and the Commons , to look across at the gilded dome of the State House , and to see the church where the great Phillips Brooks had preached", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ramsey appeared to say that if all three were to sleep in one bed , as they had begged to be allowed to do this last night , they must stop chattering and go to sleep", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ramsey a grateful look , for what she said was very true", "context_len": 12}}}, "smoothly": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 21, "context": "Dotted about the swelling surface there would be the heads of plenty of swimmers -- men and lads -- some going smoothly along , mounting the rollers as they came in , and descending softly into the hollows ; others again swimming to meet each wave , then rising a little , and with a plunge like a duck or one of the great bronze-black shags , or cormorants , that sat upon the rock-shelves , diving right through the mass of water , to come out fairly on the other side", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "It may be just as good , or better than the old one , but it 's got to take time to work off the rough spots and fit in smoothly", "context_len": 31}}}, "asphalt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "It runs very smoothly and we would not go too fast nor on any of the streets which are not asphalt", "context_len": 21}}}, "rehearsals": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "A surprise it was indeed , and because of her presence , or because it is generally so , they all did much better than at any of their rehearsals and received such applause as quite overpowered them", "context_len": 38}}}, "woefully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` And there will have to be ever so many rehearsals , '' said Agnes woefully to Celia as they were talking it over together on the Conways ' porch", "context_len": 30}}}, "situations": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Ben has a lovely little plot and all the ideas he has given us about the scenes and the dressing of the characters and the funny situations are mighty good , '' returned Agnes , `` it does seem as if between us all we ought to be able to do the rest when we have eighteen regular members in the club and two honorary ones", "context_len": 67}}}, "stunt": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 40, "context": "With hands upraised and cries of `` Kamerad '' on their lips the surrendering men passed between the advancing troops , who saluted them with ironical advice to `` Cut it out , and not so much of your Kamerad stunt ! ''", "context_len": 43}}}, "shorthand": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "If one of you girls will go with me to present me to the lady , I can take down what she says in shorthand and knock it off on the type-writer afterward", "context_len": 33}}}, "slightingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` For a girl , '' Hal said slightingly ; `` yes , that is just it", "context_len": 17}}}, "athletic": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 3, "context": "His manly and athletic form , his frank and open manners , his spirit , his generosity , and a certain air of confidence , independence , and conscious superiority , which were combined , as they always are in the case of true greatness , with an unaffected and unassuming modesty -- these and other traits , which were obvious to all who saw him , in the person and character of Alexander , made every one his friend", "context_len": 80}}}, "forego": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "He is firm and resolute , and if I read him aright , he will never forego his purpose of demanding from me the sum which I so foolishly promised his father", "context_len": 32}}}, "mauled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "However , I can forego the delights of being mauled for one afternoon , I reckon , and am at your service , fair lady", "context_len": 25}}}, "henchman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Often in my younger days had old Henry Martin and Master Collings told me tales of a buccaneering Captain Kidd and his bloodthirsty henchman , a renegade Scotsman called Angus Priddys , whose career was ended at Execution Dock ; so I formed a conclusion that these smugglers were men whose illicit dealings were not the worst of their accomplishments", "context_len": 60}}}, "interfering": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 50, "context": "Yet the Author has distinctly felt that fiction must always , in such cases , be subordinate to truth , and that it is only legitimately used as a vehicle of instruction when it fills up the gaps in the outline , without contradicting them in any respect , or interfering with their due order and sequence", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "But the very idea that such a course had evidently been suggested made the children uneasy , and kept them for several days at a pitch of sedate behaviour calculated to calm down Aunt Helen 's possible fears for their future welfare , and to render unnecessary the criticism of the most faithful and interfering of friends", "context_len": 57}}}, "commencements": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Horner did not believe in showy commencements , and when the girls were graduated they simply received their diplomas after a few simple exercises , and then the school was dismissed", "context_len": 31}}}, "diplomas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Horner did not believe in showy commencements , and when the girls were graduated they simply received their diplomas after a few simple exercises , and then the school was dismissed", "context_len": 31}}}, "accuse": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 3, "context": "I would n't accuse no one ; but them nets was safe aboard one minute , and the next minute twenty fathom was atop of you ; and if we had n't hauled you out you would n't have been talking to me just now", "context_len": 45}}}, "partiality": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Charlie was a pattern scholar , a boy after Seabrooke 's own heart , because of his sincere efforts to do right ; and hence he had found favor in his eyes , and he had shown many little tokens of partiality toward the child which had won for him the younger boy 's gratitude and affection", "context_len": 57}}}, "broomstick": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "They first planted a stout broomstick in the ground , and fixed the turnip head firmly upon the top of it ; two branches of trees tied on like cross-bars made a pair of shoulders and arms , and when covered with a sheet stolen from the linen-cupboard , it appeared as if the white figure were stretching out its imploring hands in a vain appeal for mercy", "context_len": 68}}}, "chats": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "The two had little chats once in a while and , to do Clara justice , she did her best to be pleasant whenever Edna gave her the chance", "context_len": 29}}}, "gist": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "But why should we imagine it to have been a mistake ? The true gist of the matter is not , after all , to be found in the question as to which particular type of work she might be best fitted for intellectually", "context_len": 44}}}, "responses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "She told the other girls what Clara had said and the gist of most of the responses was `` Good riddance to bad rubbish", "context_len": 24}}}, "riddance": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "She told the other girls what Clara had said and the gist of most of the responses was `` Good riddance to bad rubbish", "context_len": 24}}}, "prick": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "There was a locomotive standing on the track , and when the horses saw it they began to prick up their ears and to dance a little ; so James turned their heads and drove them up by the side of the depot , where they could not see it", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "At the same instant , a wiry pony , with flashing eyes and dilated nostrils and fine muscles aquiver , made a tremendous leap which carried him almost to the middle of the street , and heading toward the river , plunged away under the prick of the spur , on a dead run", "context_len": 54}}}, "woebegone": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "His second , which was fortunately the one upon which he acted , was to stand and see the thing through , giving himself over to his fate with an air of most woebegone resignation to whatever might be in store for him", "context_len": 43}}}, "rightest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Mother , '' she said with a woebegone face , `` why do the rightest things always be the hardest ones ? ''", "context_len": 24}}}, "dearie": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Well , well , good-bye , my dearie , '' said the stout old woman , and she embraced me with her voluminous arms , and patted me on my cheek", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` But , dearie , I think I ought to be with you , because these dolls for the bazar should be dressed very neatly , and not botchily as they might be if you were doing them merely for yourselves", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "Just you keep out of their way as much as you can , dearie , or they 'll do you a mischief if they find the chance", "context_len": 27}}}, "chidden": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "What sort of a Golden Rule would it be that allowed a little girl to be chidden for carrying out its precepts", "context_len": 22}}}, "fuzzy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Jennie in a fuzzy grayish brown frock with a hood , made a dear little Pussy Willow , Edna in pink with her rosy cheeks was the very picture of Pinky Blooms , Dorothy 's golden head made a lovely centre for Daisy White , while as for Ben , the big giant , he was the roughest , toughest old Pine Knot one could imagine", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Joe drew aside his jacket , and disclosed to the children 's delighted eyes the sweetest little round fuzzy ball of black fluff , just like a tiny woolly bear , with tan chest and paws , and a wagging morsel of a tail like a black tassel", "context_len": 48}}}, "grayish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Jennie in a fuzzy grayish brown frock with a hood , made a dear little Pussy Willow , Edna in pink with her rosy cheeks was the very picture of Pinky Blooms , Dorothy 's golden head made a lovely centre for Daisy White , while as for Ben , the big giant , he was the roughest , toughest old Pine Knot one could imagine", "context_len": 66}}}, "roughest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "Jennie in a fuzzy grayish brown frock with a hood , made a dear little Pussy Willow , Edna in pink with her rosy cheeks was the very picture of Pinky Blooms , Dorothy 's golden head made a lovely centre for Daisy White , while as for Ben , the big giant , he was the roughest , toughest old Pine Knot one could imagine", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "It \u2019 s many a long year since I drew an arrow to the head ; but I \u2019 ve seen the time it would have been as much as any of your lives were worth to have run across the roughest ground you ever saw , within thirty yards of my arrow ; that is , if I was prepared to harm you", "context_len": 64}}}, "demonstrations": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "They soon spread the tale of the speedy coming of the sultan with vast reinforcements , and as the pasha had on the previous day caused salutes to be fired , and other demonstrations to be made , the news was readily credited , and caused the greatest dismay among the defenders", "context_len": 52}}}, "complimentary": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "At any rate , I wrote a complimentary letter this morning from the grand master to the governor of Syria , and the bailiff of Spain , who was , as you know , for ten years a prisoner among the Turks , read it through at D'Aubusson 's request , to see that there was no error in it , and was good enough to pass it without alteration", "context_len": 70}}}, "tilde": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Text in small caps is marked with a tilde", "context_len": 9}}}, "corrections": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "As long as we stay above the ceiling , I can make corrections by shooting the sun", "context_len": 17}}}, "blanchard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "BLANCHARD", "context_len": 1}}}, "copyright": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Copyright 1911 by George W", "context_len": 5}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "A new series of instructive copyright stories for boys of High School Age", "context_len": 13}}}, "pug": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "They both wore their thinnest morning frocks and had pinned their hair up in little pug knots on the tops of their heads", "context_len": 23}}}, "sticky": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 50, "context": "Her missile flew far above the man \u2019 s head , and , running backwards in a vain endeavour to catch it , he fell heavily into a large trough of pigs \u2019 mash , where , half-stunned by the force of his fall , he lay wallowing in the sticky mess , till he was helped out by his comrades , to the accompaniment of their jeers and laughter", "context_len": 70}}}, "postman": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 49, "context": "People knew the time of their passing , and looked out for them daily ; kindly women would come to cottage doorways to nod and smile ; the inn-keeper at the Halfway Arms set his clock by their arrival ; they had struck up quite a friendship with the postman , two milkmen , and the driver of a fish-cart , while the old man who broke stones by the roadside always nodded and gave them 'Good-morning , ' and fired a facetious remark or two after them , which the children imagined he must relieve the monotony of his work by composing during the intervals of their coming and going", "context_len": 111}}}, "fanning": {"freq": 190, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 189, "word_index": 26, "context": "The hayoy\u00e1lgi , coming from the four corners of the world to light the sacred fire , the symbol of the sun , are the winds fanning it to a higher flame , and the purpose of the story is to make an oracular power of the sacred flame , by which the Holder of Breath , or Great Spirit , could be placed in communication with his Indian wards , and enabled to take care of them", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Fanning , are packing your things", "context_len": 6}}}, "fidgets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I never saw such little fidgets", "context_len": 6}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Before my dear Albert became so wealthy , I used to get into my bus , and do my hour of shop-gazing a-most every afternoon , but now it fidgets the coachman if I ask him to pull up the horses too often", "context_len": 43}}}, "impetuous": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 24, "context": "France and Auvergne , led by Ralph Harcourt , boarded at the bow , the other five langues at the poop ; and so impetuous was their onset that the pirates , who had still scarce recovered from their surprise at being hastily aroused from sleep to repel the attack of the foe who had so suddenly sprung out from the darkness upon them , offered but a feeble resistance", "context_len": 70}}}, "scan": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` She opened the letter with what seemed to the children a great lack of haste , and began to scan the lines , two pairs of eager eyes watching her the while", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "These , too , he had to scan , lest any innocent looking bough should suddenly come to life and prove to be the body of an anaconda ready to dart down like lightning upon its prey", "context_len": 37}}}, "intervening": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Turning in that direction , keeping on hands and knees and motioning Doto to follow , Bomba made his way through the intervening thickets with as much speed as possible", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 38, "context": "Our wants for the next few hours were consequently supplied , we had therefore no need to do anything further than just to wait for nightfall and then resume our voyage ; which we did , passing the intervening time in chatting together and discussing the various precautions which we must take in order to elude our pursuers , who , by the way , were now several miles ahead of us", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "Yet , although in appearance a very Methuselah in age , this individual had a pair of piercing black eyes that glowed and sparkled with all the fire and passion of early manhood , and , bowed as he was , and decrepit as he appeared to be , he tottered across the intervening space with extraordinary agility , and seated himself in the second chair", "context_len": 66}}}, "husbands": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "The joy of the women and children to whom their husbands and fathers were thus restored was very touching ; all seemed willing to forget the destruction of their homes , since they had been spared to each other , and I , to whom , by my vows , such love is unknown , yet could but feel how holy a thing is family affection", "context_len": 66}}}, "unburden": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "She was not long in finding her mother , and in plying her with questions upon the all-important subject , but she received no further assurance than had been given her in the beginning and was fain to exercise her patience and unburden herself to her sister Celia , who was interested and sympathetic", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "I think , during those early struggles , her correspondence with Aunt Helen was her greatest help and comfort , for to that dearest of friends she could unburden all her worries and perplexities , and be sure of sympathy", "context_len": 40}}}, "coolest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "But even her favorite fairy tales failed to fix her attention , so she returned to the house to find everyone given up to napping and the place so still that finally in the coolest corner of the library where a little breeze found its way through the open windows , she herself fell asleep", "context_len": 55}}}, "misplaced": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 117, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}}}, "bestow": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 69, "context": "Nevertheless , I know that it was spoken of last night , and although it has not yet been finally settled , and will not be until the council meet this afternoon , I should not tell you if I did not think that it was as good as agreed upon ; and I am pleased to be the first to whisper to you that it is intended to bestow upon you an honour that is jealously guarded and seldom granted , even to crowned heads , unless as a token of gratitude for some signal service done to the Republic", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Tell him that for certain reasons we wish Ahmet to pass the night for the present in the prison , and arrange with him on what excuse we can best bestow him there without exciting suspicion", "context_len": 36}}}, "rapturous": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 41, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}}}, "dubiously": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I should say so , '' returned Hadley , but it was noticeable that he spoke rather dubiously , and not with his usual positiveness", "context_len": 26}}}, "determinedly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "As Jennie had pictured it the visit was to be one long season of delight , so Edna said determinedly", "context_len": 20}}}, "outdone": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 3, "context": "Not to be outdone in politeness , we hoisted our colours also ; and for the next quarter of an hour the two craft continued to close , the chase stolidly maintaining her course , while we , under the skipper 's skilful conning , continued to edge very gradually away , as the other vessel sped to leeward , checking our weather braces by a few inches at a time until our yards were all but square", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It was an unwritten law that each draft should attempt to sing all the way to The Summit , and the Thirty-somethingth was not going to be outdone", "context_len": 28}}}, "waken": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "By now she could see well enough to find her clothes , but , fearing lest she should waken her mother , she determined to go to the bathroom at the end of the hall rather than use the wash-stand in the room where she was , so she gathered up her clothing in her arms , and went down the entry , made her toilet and crept down stairs", "context_len": 70}}}, "expressman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then just as they were about to sit down to breakfast , the expressman came for the trunk , and next Dorothy arrived all impatience", "context_len": 25}}}, "gainsaying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Hartley said , Oh , my dear ! in a most deprecating way ; but there was no gainsaying the evident sincerity of the declaration", "context_len": 25}}}, "untrod": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Ramsey 's private office , they felt that here began their untrod way", "context_len": 13}}}, "satchels": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "They read several of the fairy tales ; they worked over their puzzles ; they watched from the windows , and finally decided to put on their hats and pack up such of their belongings as they had taken from their satchels so they might be all ready", "context_len": 48}}}, "elevator": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 32, "context": "To be sure , one has need to overlook , in quite too literal fashion , the warehouses that front the water below the bluff , and here and there an unsightly elevator , but why let the eye rest on these , with the dancing blue water beneath you , and the Jersey hills beyond , and beyond that again , like as not , a glorious sunset", "context_len": 69}}}, "whizz": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The boy led the way to the elevator and down they went to the first floor of the big office building , then to the street where an automobile stood to whizz them off to the station", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "He was dimly conscious of khaki-clad forms crashing heavily to the ground on either side , of a whizz of flying metal that sent his steel helmet spinning , of a sharp , burning pain in his left wrist , and of a dozen other mental and physical sensations", "context_len": 49}}}, "chauffeur": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 3, "context": "Ramsey directed the chauffeur to see about the trunks while he conducted the little girls to the waiting-room where he left them , returning in a moment to hurry them to the train , and the second part of their journey began", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Stevie lived quite a distance below and had brought no end of playthings , had an auto wagon that two could sit in , though only one could be chauffeur", "context_len": 31}}}, "footstools": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` I never was in a parlor car before , '' whispered Dorothy to Edna as the porter turned their seat to a proper angle and adjusted their footstools", "context_len": 29}}}, "vouchsafe": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 89, "context": "son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not give them his daughter", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "There are some words at line-breaks in the original where it is not clear if they should be hyphenated or not : woe-begone , vouchsafe , fireplace , lobster-pots , tip-toeing , homeless , haystack , homecoming ;", "context_len": 38}}}, "undesirable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "lorgnette": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` What have you ? '' asked the lady putting up her lorgnette", "context_len": 13}}}, "beckon": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` The friendships of this evil generation will avail us little when the trumpet of the Lord of Hosts doth sound the reveille , and those poor bones yonder live once more , ay , and that dead hand beckon us on to victory", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "You have only to mount your horse , beckon to your negro , and follow the path you will find at the back of the house", "context_len": 26}}}, "withering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The inquisitive lady gave the speaker a withering look and sank to her place with an air of great dignity while Edna returned to her place by Dorothy", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Fritz , lying perdu , while the crowd of Anzacs poured onward , would resurrect his tic-tocs and direct a withering machine-gun fire into the backs of the luckless men", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "We have a great hankering to go , both me and Marion , and seeing that we are all in this house -- one family , so to speak -- it do n't seem fair , do it , that all the favour should go to one ? '' -- here she cast a withering glance at me", "context_len": 58}}}, "determine": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 34, "context": "Some persons think that Alexander watched the countenance of his physician while he was reading the letter , and that he was led to take the medicine by his confidence in his power to determine the guilt or the innocence of a person thus accused by his looks", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "It is a case , doubtless , of concussion of the brain , he said at last ; just how serious I can not at once determine , but , first thing , Mrs", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "She had really had an honest desire to keep her generous self-sacrifice a secret , that it should not be published abroad to all the world ; but there was , also , an obstinate little corner in her heart which made her determine to keep it from her nephew , lest he should oppose it", "context_len": 56}}}, "disclose": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 19, "context": "There were patches of snow on the ground about the sycamore , but an examination of them did not disclose any human footprints , though there were squirrel and rabbit tracks which gave the boys hope that they would get plenty of game when they went to their winter camp", "context_len": 50}}}, "unfolding": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "Lawyer Whitehead , whose name did not belie his appearance , adjusted his horn spectacles , and , unfolding a parchment , read the will , which is as follows : --", "context_len": 32}}}, "emergency": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 35, "context": "A body of fifty English soldiers was passing on the road , which lay at the distance of a few hundred yards only , travelling at a considerable speed , as if they anticipated the emergency of Aescendune , and hurried to the rescue", "context_len": 44}}}, "worming": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "So through the crowded streets , worming their way among lines of heavy teams , across car-tracks , and into queer-looking neighborhoods they were taken , arriving just in time to be taken on board the boat before she should move off", "context_len": 42}}}, "goners": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` If you children had not been all ready we should have been goners", "context_len": 14}}}, "boundaries": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 45, "context": "A special court of justice was speedily organised , wherein Edric presided as ealdorman of Mercia , for Oxenford properly was a Mercian city , although , lying on the debateable land , it was frequently claimed by Wessex as the border land changed its boundaries", "context_len": 46}}}, "stateroom": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 33, "context": "To this the captain assented , and the Portuguese , having been deprived of his knife , and searched for any concealed weapons he might have had , was taken below to the stateroom , whither repaired the captain , two lieutenants , and the master", "context_len": 46}}}, "prink": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "No stopping to prink at the last moment , no forgetting something when it is too late to go back for it", "context_len": 22}}}, "models": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "She had five darlings of her own , in whom she could see no fault , and whom she invariably held up as models of good behaviour to all the children of her acquaintance", "context_len": 34}}}, "heed": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` For the sake of him who hath even now rendered up himself as a martyr for the Lord 's cause , I may not be angry with any word of yours , '' he answered sadly ; `` but I do entreat of you to take heed ! Would you lay down your arms and live in peace among your cattle and your corn , coached and complimented into effeminacy and foolishness ? Oh , for shame ! Rub your eyes and look about you ! What was the fate of the men of Sodom when they thought Lot was one that mocked when he warned them ! ''", "context_len": 110}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Alas , alas ! and did I not kneel and entreat my dearest husband to heed the words of those good men if he would not mine ? How happy we might have been , even in a hut among the savages ! And you , too , Dick , '' she said tenderly , `` you would have liked well to follow Master Perrient 's leading ; and my dear husband was ready to have you go , seeing all he and Sir Gyles Perrient had set their minds upon for your happiness", "context_len": 95}}}, "queried": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 8, "context": "Who can that possibly be with Albert ? queried Harold , naturally mystified at the appearance of a gentleman , in the easy costume of house coat and slippers , standing complacently in the doorway of the Little Castle", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "'What makes P'liney so different ? ' queried Leander of Stephen and John , as they rested from their daily task of cutting wood", "context_len": 24}}}, "anticipations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "And so the hours rolled on , which brought the dreaded morn nearer and nearer ; and the victim , comforted by prayer , but without hope in this world , slept , and thought no longer of the torturer 's knife , or felt the cruel anticipations which would rack the waiting mind", "context_len": 54}}}, "dales": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Instead of rolling meadows , hills and dales , were long stretches of salt marsh , sand dunes and beyond all the great expanse of ocean", "context_len": 26}}}, "stretches": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 43, "context": "The slope was steadily upward for a fourth of a mile , when it reached a nearly level plateau , and wound in and out among rocks , stunted pines , gnarled cedars , and ravines , interspersed with valleys and comparatively smooth stretches , with now and then a mountain torrent across which the travelers made their way with difficulty", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I suppose I am not the only man in the world that has been caught in the desert that sometimes stretches its barren wastes between love and duty", "context_len": 28}}}, "marsh": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Hasten , Aubrey ! Our place is on board the Gannet , '' quoth Drake , and alternately running and walking we hurried out of the town , crossed the causeway over the marsh , and arrived at the mud dock , where the vessel lay", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Hidden by the rank marsh grass that formed a perfect covert , the three lay motionless , not a breath betraying their hiding place", "context_len": 24}}}, "dunes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Instead of rolling meadows , hills and dales , were long stretches of salt marsh , sand dunes and beyond all the great expanse of ocean", "context_len": 26}}}, "squeals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "With many little squeals of apprehension , the two who lacked experience , ventured to the edge of the water , but Jennie dashed in , letting the waves completely cover her , and with such an example the other two soon became braver and began to enjoy themselves hugely", "context_len": 50}}}, "braver": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "Maggie was afraid at first , and cried when she was carried into the water ; but the second time she was braver , and she soon came to like it almost as well as Bessie , who never was ready to come out when it was thought she had been in long enough", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "When the cannon cease to sound and swords are sheathed , there is no longer animosity between brave men ; and no braver than those whose bodies lie stretched there , breathed the air of heaven", "context_len": 36}}}, "hugely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "With many little squeals of apprehension , the two who lacked experience , ventured to the edge of the water , but Jennie dashed in , letting the waves completely cover her , and with such an example the other two soon became braver and began to enjoy themselves hugely", "context_len": 50}}}, "undivided": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 78, "context": "There are terms which occur in all dialects in the same or nearly the same form , as h\u00e1si sun , \u00edtchu , \u00edssi deer , \u00f3fi , \u00edfa dog , the terms for chief , black , yellow , bird , snake , buffalo , turtle , fox ( also in Cheroki : tsu'hl\u00e1 ) , the numerals and the personal pronouns ; they must , therefore , have been once the common property of the still undivided , primordial tribe", "context_len": 82}}}, "seasick": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Sister was so afraid I would be seasick , but I was not , in fact it was the most glorious trip I ever had , and we can go back the same way", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It is n't everyone who can go in a sailing vessel without being seasick , and I am a little doubtful for Dorothy and Miss Eloise , but in your present condition I am very sure it would be anything but a pleasure to you", "context_len": 45}}}, "overtax": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Hartley would not be good enough to take him to board for the rest of the summer , and if Chris would not contrive to keep him occupied about the farm in some way that should not overtax his newly gained strength", "context_len": 42}}}, "baths": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now , with nourishing diet and some salt in the baths we shall have her about again , though I 've had several patients lie weeks in this state in the hot weather", "context_len": 33}}}, "preference": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 35, "context": "Whatever may be the real foundation of this confused narrative , it seems that the Alibamu reached their later seats from a country lying to the west or southwest , and that they showed a preference for river-junctions , for this enabled them to take fish in two rivers simultaneously", "context_len": 50}}}, "pennant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Her hopes ran toward winning the Lake League pennant in baseball , but as her nine had been at the bottom of the list for several seasons , the chances were dubious", "context_len": 32}}}, "pinky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Oh , yes , ever so many , and some of them are so pretty , tiny little pinky ones", "context_len": 21}}}, "dearies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I am just sending a line to your mothers , dearies , '' she said to Edna and Dorothy", "context_len": 20}}}, "relied": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 60, "context": "A hurried council of war was held on board the flagship , and here Albemarle made the first great mistake of his life ; for it is reported he held the Dutch so cheaply that he ordered Prince Rupert to take twenty vessels of our fleet and make to the westward to find and engage the French , while he relied on his remaining fifty-four ships to meet the formidable array of Dutchmen", "context_len": 73}}}, "swop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "I 've seen a good bit of towns and been to considerable many ports , but I ai n't seen nawthin ' I 'd swop this place fur", "context_len": 28}}}, "knotty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Edna and Dorothy thought him a most interesting person with his red face , his white tuft of beard under his chin and his great knotty hands", "context_len": 27}}}, "seaweeds": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Then Cap'n Si said it was about time they were starting back , so they did not have time to explore the grove and had not gathered half the seaweeds they had intended to , though they were not so much disturbed at this lack because Jennie said , `` Oh , well , do n't bother , we can come here any time , and there will be just as many things as there are now", "context_len": 77}}}, "basketful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then Dodds arrived with a basketful of good things for the feast , and a very merry feast it was", "context_len": 20}}}, "supplies": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 14, "context": "The besiegers have to invest the city on all sides to cut off all supplies of provisions , and then , in those days , they had to construct engines to make a breach somewhere in the walls , through which an assaulting party could attempt to force their way in", "context_len": 51}}}, "solved": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 10, "context": "The mystery of how they were to be driven was solved in a moment , however , when a faultlessly equipped groom threw himself astride of one of the leaders , and the stablemen , standing at the bridles of the four-in-hand , at one and the same moment let go their hold , and sprang quickly out of the way", "context_len": 61}}}, "horny": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "He was not generally a very attractive person , being snuffy in appearance and crusty in temper , but to-night no fairy prince could have been more appreciated , and they flew to him , each seizing one of his horny hands with an enthusiasm which much surprised him", "context_len": 49}}}, "shelly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Shelly Beach came to be a familiar spot , the grove was more than once explored , the drives up and down the coast became old acquaintances , while Cap'n Si grew to be as well known as any member of Jennie 's family", "context_len": 44}}}, "rowboat": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "At four in the afternoon five weatherworn and mud-bespattered travellers arrived at Chatham , where a rowboat took us to Albemarle \u2019 s flagship , which lay at anchor in Gillingham Reach", "context_len": 32}}}, "confidences": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "She was a great favorite with everybody , and with none more than the three little girls to whom she gave confidences she withheld from older persons , and they came to know a great deal about the circumstances of herself and sister", "context_len": 43}}}, "mortgages": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 124, "context": "grow , when they were not there to watch it all , for most of us have a kind of feeling that we are the important centre around which Nature turns , instead of only mere spectators of her varying moods , and sometimes it felt so impossible that such an utter upheaval in their lives could really come to pass that she would have to shake herself to believe that it was not all a bad dream ; but as she noticed the quiet preparations that went on , and the added worry on Father 's face , she realized that it was only too true , and that every day was bringing them nearer to that terrible twenty-fifth of July when the mortgages would fall due", "context_len": 128}}}, "fractions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I do n't believe I ever shall get through fractions , let alone interest", "context_len": 15}}}, "bazar": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 33, "context": "It was not a long one , for the yacht was to leave the harbor early and there was not much time left though Edna managed to tell about the fire and the bazar , and to send a great many messages to all at home whom Uncle Justus would see before she herself would", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "55 : `` have a bazaar for her '' bazaar changed to bazar ;", "context_len": 14}}}, "scooping": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "' I 've won ! ' declared Bobby in triumph , hurriedly swallowing the last morsel , and scooping up a delicious draught of water to wash it down", "context_len": 29}}}, "mussel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Edna employed herself in making little piles of sand , scooping it up with a purple mussel shell", "context_len": 18}}}, "institution": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 49, "context": "Then as soon as it is ready all the institutions for children in London are to be told about it , and whenever a little girl comes along who seems to be too fine , in the best sense of the word , for the life of the ordinary institution , down she is to go to Cranford , to be cared for in the Home ; and it is to be a home , Harold , prettily furnished , with rooms for ten children , and everything as dainty as can be", "context_len": 93}}}, "reciter": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` This is an evident mistake ( due to some ignorant copyist or reciter of the story ) of the same kind as that to be found at the commencement of the story of Ghanim ben Eyoub , ( see my Book of the Thousand Nights and One Night , Vol I", "context_len": 52}}}, "program": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Well , we 're in here , what 's next on the program ? '' asked Ned , of Bart", "context_len": 21}}}, "miffed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` She walked away toward the stables , Dorothy joined her and Edna was left with her cousin feeling half indignant with Louis and half miffed with the girls", "context_len": 29}}}, "tenacious": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "Many , whose favourite occupations are , to say the least , no whit more pressing than were her books , are exceedingly tenacious of their time , and exceedingly impatient of interruptions ; and with too many the home-calls come second to all personal interests", "context_len": 46}}}, "morrison": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Morrison", "context_len": 1}}}, "confirmation": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "His influence and ascendency seemed firmly established now in the country of the Greeks , and in due time he returned to Macedon , and celebrated at \u00c6g\u00e6 , which was at this time his capital , the establishment and confirmation of his power , by games , shows , spectacles , illuminations , and sacrifices to the gods , offered on a scale of the greatest pomp and magnificence", "context_len": 70}}}, "thwarted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 66, "context": "Bomba wondered whether he would ever see that strange and wonderful city or others like it , wondered if he would always have to spend his life in the jungle , with none save Casson and a few natives for his friends , to none of whom he could speak of the longings that obsessed him , of the aspirations that seemed doomed forever to be thwarted", "context_len": 67}}}, "marplot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Edna was fond of him in a way , but she feared he would prove a marplot if he spent much time at the Ramsey 's , and she began to wonder how long he and his mother were to be at the hotel", "context_len": 44}}}, "awkwardness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "He looked quite brisk , and bright , and like his usual self , except that in some extraordinary way his awkwardness and self-consciousness had completely left him ; he was evidently absorbed with some business on hand , which made him a new man for the time", "context_len": 48}}}, "friction": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "Their hands , too , were sore and parboiled from the friction of the line and constant soaking in the water , especially those of John and Fred , who did not know how to take out the hook without putting their fingers into the fish \u2019 s mouth , and scratching and cutting them with his teeth and gills", "context_len": 60}}}, "continuing": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 80, "context": "The wind was fair , but light , and they steered for the lone spruce on Birch Point , and , passing it , kept on to the north-east , having resolved to run the shore along , keeping a bright lookout for the high rock with the spruce on its summit , till they judged by the tide it was midnight , when , if they could not find the place , they would go ashore and camp , continuing their search in the morning", "context_len": 86}}}, "meaningly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` But with these wretched wooden shoes -- -- '' and he pointed meaningly at his feet", "context_len": 17}}}, "clamored": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now , too , the bell of the red-brick school clamored loudly of mornings ; and dark , taciturn Mexican children , and paler , noisier children from the mining end of town , bubbled out of every door", "context_len": 39}}}, "decline": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 51, "context": "Then the boys and girls shrank away , for had they not heard of her pranks , and might they not expect toads and frogs to come out of their mouths , and other horrors to happen if Miss Irene had anything to do with them ? They therefore began to decline Irene 's cake , and to say , `` No , thank you , miss , '' in a very timid voice when delicious-looking pieces of bread-and-butter and jam were offered to them", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Marie-Celeste looked away over the tops of the fine old trees that just reach to the terraces from the steep decline of the slopes below , way to the lovely meadows , and then turned to look up at the castle , leaning comfortably against the wall at her back", "context_len": 50}}}, "bungalow": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 42, "context": "A story has been told of an officer , himself a hearty supporter of Missions , who received a very unfavourable impression of one particular Missionary from observing the large amount of comfortable furniture which arrived at the said Missionary \u2019 s bungalow , for the latter \u2019 s use", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Well , my dear , '' spoke up Miss Newman , `` I am sure Eloise will not object to your knowing that when we get into our little bungalow she is to have a porch party of her own every day", "context_len": 43}}}, "bungalows": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "[ 31 ] I have now lived in four bungalows , and all have elegant rooms , and there is such an air of refinement that I have great doubts whether it would be the correct thing to put out my hand and take a slice of bread off a plate", "context_len": 51}}}, "imposed": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 17, "context": "Merrily laughed the bonny blue-bell at my proposition that , in addition to the fine of 100 imposed on Mission Miss Sahibas who marry within three years of coming out , it should be part of the contract that they should have all their hair shaved off on the day before the wedding", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Tucker could not quite keep pace with the new-fashioned unconventionalities of young-lady work in London ; and one of the object-sermons , which most impressed me in my College days , was the beautiful self-restraint which these two sisters -- no longer young -- imposed upon themselves , in deference to their aged Mother \u2019 s wishes , in regard to that outside work which inclination , or one might say conviction , as well as opportunity and qualifications , impelled them to participate in", "context_len": 85}}}, "radiance": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 21, "context": "When it was full , his mother looked at the dish , but could not gaze fixedly thereon , for the radiance of the jewels and their lustre and the excess of their flashing ; so she shut her eyes and her wit was confounded at them ; yet was she not certified that their value was in very deed so great as her son had said , but bethought her that his speech might be true in that their like was not found with kings", "context_len": 86}}}, "harmonious": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "We can hardly compare her to anything else ; so varied , so harmonious , so lovely were the rays of light which she reflected", "context_len": 25}}}, "expectancy": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "Here Lady Jane herself was waiting in a state of nervous expectancy , and here was Miss Frost , very pale , shaky , and troubled , declaring stoutly to Lady Jane that `` they '' were running about inside her still", "context_len": 42}}}, "adding": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 41, "word_index": 34, "context": "However , yesterday , I saw the Lady Bedrulbudour , the Sultan 's daughter , as she went to the bath ; '' and he told her all that had happened to him , adding , `` And most like thou heardest the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her", "context_len": 128}}}, "tutor": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 45, "context": "Whatever one gives to be made or mended is made or mended in the verandah ; and the darzi , as he cuts out , clips , and sews , talks -- perhaps with the pankah-wala , perhaps a stranger , perhaps the munshi ( tutor ) whose pupil is not quite ready to take her lesson", "context_len": 57}}}, "losses": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 23, "context": "The defence , though even more obstinate and desperate than the first , was attended with less success , for after inflicting enormous losses upon the great army , commanded by the Sultan Solyman himself , the town was forced to yield ; for although the Grand Master L'Isle Adam , and most of his knights , would have preferred to bury themselves beneath the ruins rather than yield , they were deterred from doing so , by the knowledge that it would have entailed the massacre of the whole of the inhabitants , who had throughout the siege fought valiantly in the defence of the town", "context_len": 107}}}, "duncan": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 0, "context": "Duncan heard about Jemmy Bent , he proposed a little plan to the children , that pleased them very much", "context_len": 20}}}, "spreads": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The boys used to have spreads there , and would cook all sorts of messes", "context_len": 15}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "May the Lord keep you as one of His `` hidden ones , '' my darling , and make you to realise that He who `` holdeth the height of the hills , '' spreads the hush of His presence over the valleys", "context_len": 43}}}, "messes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The boys used to have spreads there , and would cook all sorts of messes", "context_len": 15}}}, "relay": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "The three flew upstairs chattering like magpies , and when up they flew around excitedly so as to get down again to ask more questions , though this they were not able to do as the very first relay of guests arrived before they were quite dressed", "context_len": 47}}}, "entrancing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "For an hour they were delighted listeners , for it seemed as if this teller of tales had never been in better spirits nor had she ever told a more entrancing story , and when at last it came to an end there were many long drawn `` Ahs '' which showed that no one was ready to have her stop", "context_len": 61}}}, "carriages": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 91, "context": "-- Mine own Laura , how could you write regarding the little meeting , at which you and sweet Margaret were , Would you not like to be in my shoes at the time , and hold your darling friend in your arms ? I would much rather have been in Margaret \u2019 s shoes , and have held some one else in my arms , -- only for the wrench that would have followed ! But O love , we are travelling in the same train , only in different carriages ; and I am thankful that though we can not see each other , we can as it were talk to each other out of the windows", "context_len": 119}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "To Malcolm 's surprise the Red Cross carriages disgorged a battalion of fully-equipped troops", "context_len": 14}}}, "prop": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Would you like to sit up in bed ? I can put something over your shoulders and prop you up with pillows , or how would you like to get into my bed ? There is more room and you can look out of the window", "context_len": 46}}}, "unceremoniously": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 53, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}}}, "liberally": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 73, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "inmates": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 7, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "discord": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "Peace ! Peace ! The whole wide world rejoiceth now , Let war and discord cease ; Christ reigneth from the manger , Away with strife and danger ; Our God , before whom angels bow , Each taught this lesson by his birth , Good will to men , and peace on earth", "context_len": 54}}}, "sandcastle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "If that 's what they are going to do you and I can go down to the beach and build a sandcastle or go fishing or something", "context_len": 27}}}, "lief": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` I know 'd they 'd crowd us out'n house an ' home ef they got a chance ; I could ' a ' took oath to it ! Cuss 'em , an ' contrive 'em , both sides on 'em , all an ' similar ! They 'd as lief make a hoss-stable out'n the house as not , an ' I built it wi ' my two han 's", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` I think , '' he added , `` as I 'd as lief 'ave my chance wid the h'Almighty as yourn , when h'all is reckoned up", "context_len": 28}}}, "boulders": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 67, "context": "It was too much for the boys , but the performance of the big dive was looked upon as the passing of a lad from boyhood into the manly stage , upon which he entered through the Shangles Gate , and then swam back , coming , as it were , of age amidst the shouts of his companions to swim ashore and land upon the big boulders , where the boys bathed and learned to swim in the calm weather , gazing the while in admiration at their older companions", "context_len": 91}}}, "scraggy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "They set off together along the beach , but though they climbed over great boulders and scrambled around scraggy roots of trees the place was ever beyond them", "context_len": 28}}}, "robber": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` Is it true , '' said Nora , `` that her papa gave all his money to a friend to put in a mine that had diamonds in it , and then the friend thought he had lost it all and ran away because he felt as if he was a robber ? ''", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Well , I guess he has a bad little robber girl of his own that he has taken it to , '' said Maggie", "context_len": 25}}}, "hoard": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "Still , he resolved to push the inquiry , in the hope of eliciting some information that might be of value , for already a plan had come into his mind which was in accordance with his general character and reputation -- that of relieving the invalid of his hoard of gold-dust", "context_len": 52}}}, "leak": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "I think not so badly of men of our faith as to believe that any one would betray the secret for the sake of obtaining his own freedom and a big reward ; but secrets , when known by many , are apt to leak out", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Papa saw the carpenter \u2019 s wife , who told him that the leak could not be got at because of the coals , that they would not get to Boulogne , but must return in two hours", "context_len": 38}}}, "avail": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` So the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? He that can in so short a time avail unto riches like these , is he not worthy to be the Sultan 's son-in-law and to have his daughter to bride ? '' Now the Vizier marvelled at the greatness of these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 113, "context": "the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How now ? Deemest thou Alaeddin worthy to be bridegroom to my daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour ? Hast thou seen and considered this royal building and all these riches which man 's wit can not comprehend ? '' The Vizier , of his envy of Alaeddin , answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , indeed this palace and its building and all these riches may not be but by means of enchantment , for that no man among men , no , not the mightiest of them in dominion or the greatest in wealth , might avail to upraise and stablish [ the like of ] this building in one night", "context_len": 128}}}, "mica": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "This was rather a pleasant amusement , and she soon had a nice little pile of those which were either milky white , which showed some faint color , or which shone with spots of mica or quartz", "context_len": 38}}}, "compose": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 59, "context": "Then , by grace of his own idea of fitness , Harold had added to the other furnishings a Dresden china toilet-set , and in this he was perhaps far wiser than he knew , for is there anything so well calculated to captivate at sight the heart of a dainty little maiden as the mysterious round-topped boxes that compose the dainty outfit of the ideal dressing-table ? Then , to crown it all , a pair of ponies and a basket-phaeton had been purchased for the exclusive use of the guests that were to be", "context_len": 96}}}, "meddling": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 38, "context": "My weary and aching frame soothed and refreshed by the pelting rain , I must have fallen into a kind of doze , for I was suddenly startled into full consciousness by the feeling that some one was meddling with my bonds , which , the next moment , severed by a sharp knife , fell from my limbs", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Well , '' said Maggie , slowly , as if she were considering , `` maybe her temper corner is not so sharp as it used to be , but her meddling corner is very bad , -- yes , very bad indeed ; and it scratches like everything", "context_len": 50}}}, "furthest": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 88, "context": "The air seemed to have grown more close and sultry while the children were within the cave , and , just as they reached the mouth , a low , grumbling sound , which they had heard for some time , but not taken much notice of , broke into a crash of thunder that seemed to make the ground shake beneath them , while at the same instant a brilliant streak of lightning flashed zigzag across the sky , lighting up the gloom behind them to its furthest recess", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Uncle Bert and his family take the morning train because they have the furthest to go , and Aunt Lucia wants to get home with the children before dark", "context_len": 29}}}, "bayberry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Me got an onion patch , massa , -- my ole woman raise him ; got some bayberry taller , -- Spaniards buy him quick to put in de candle ; make him hard so he no melt", "context_len": 38}}}, "enveloping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "But all at once it was made evident to her that she could not see , for a chill grey fog had crept in , and was enveloping land and sea", "context_len": 31}}}, "sloped": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "Below the house the meadow sloped down to a river , where a stone bridge led to the village , with its pretty thatched cottages and Norman church , whose square tower stood up like a beacon for the surrounding country ; and away in the distance , tier upon tier , rose the Welsh mountains , fading from green to purple or from purple to misty mauve , till the last were lost in the hazy blue of the sky", "context_len": 81}}}, "cove": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 15, "context": "Joe said the way to tell it was , right off the mouth of the cove there was a great , high rock , that came up out of the water , with three spruces on the top of it , and a little turf , but the sides were all bare ; and he said there were reefs and breakers all round it ; but I \u2019 ll bet , if we could find it , we could see the reefs break , and keep clear of them", "context_len": 89}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "While they were thus engaged , Fred , who was steering , exclaimed , I see a smoke in Captain Rhines \u2019 s cove", "context_len": 24}}}, "stubbly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "There was no talk of calling Cap'n Si that morning , for he would not be looking for the flag to be run up , instead he was lying helpless on a cot , his hands swathed in cotton , and his stubbly beard singed by the fire he had vainly tried to put out", "context_len": 55}}}, "wetter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "If it was possible for weather to be worse than it had been the day before , it was worse this day -- wetter , muddier , colder", "context_len": 28}}}, "centered": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 34, "context": "The grass , short and stubbly , was strung with fine mist and at each step Edna grew wetter and wetter , but she did not heed this , for her whole thought was centered upon Louis , and she was imagining all sorts of things", "context_len": 46}}}, "imagining": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "No , I 'm not going to take any savings of yours , said Ted , imagining that Donald might so have understood him ; but I want to put the case to you , and have you tell me what to do ; and Donald listened attentively while Ted put his case plainly and without any mental reservations whatever", "context_len": 60}}}, "disclosed": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 29, "context": "'What on earth has that stupid Nancy given me a hot bottle for on such a warm evening ? ' he exclaimed ; and flinging down the clothes he disclosed to view , not the expected earthenware bottle , but the shrinking and apologetic form of Master Rollo , who , if ever a dog could be said to own a conscience , surely showed he knew he had offended , and repented heartily of his sins", "context_len": 77}}}, "landmarks": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 104, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "hut": {"freq": 160, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 159, "word_index": 11, "context": "It was nearly noon when I was conducted back to my hut after my futile attempt to cure the king ; and it was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Indeed , for the sake of shielding you , I have told the Hartleys that your name is Morris , and it rests with you to tell them your right name some day if you choose ; hut the doctor knows the truth about things -- he had to know", "context_len": 50}}}, "impetus": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` This gave her a new impetus and she hurried on , and presently was surprised to see that it was not a fisherman 's house at all , but a small bungalow , set with back to a grove of trees and facing a small strip of beach", "context_len": 49}}}, "soaking": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Soaking blankets in salt water , they spread them on the roof of the house , wet the ground around it , and urged to desperation by the fear of losing their home , beat out the flame from the grass with hemlock boughs , which is the best way to stop fire that is running in grass", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Their hands , too , were sore and parboiled from the friction of the line and constant soaking in the water , especially those of John and Fred , who did not know how to take out the hook without putting their fingers into the fish \u2019 s mouth , and scratching and cutting them with his teeth and gills", "context_len": 60}}}, "flimsy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "My dear child , why did you come out in this fog wearing that flimsy gingham ? And no rubbers , no coat ? What were you thinking of ? ''", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "That 's the way with girls , eh ? As long as they can have a lot of flimsy laces and ribbons and flowers they 're as happy as birds", "context_len": 30}}}, "forlornly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "A happier child , with more childish ways , might not so fully have compassed Jane 's awakening ; for this had been in proportion to the needs of the one who so forlornly made plea for entrance", "context_len": 38}}}, "censure": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "It was a very exciting afternoon , taking it all in all , and was the means of bringing some ridicule and some censure upon the little club", "context_len": 28}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He began to censure himself for taking the independent course he had pursued", "context_len": 13}}}, "kimono": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Here she comes , '' cried Edna trying to get to the door in such haste that she forgot to hold up the blue kimono and came near to sprawling at full length", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Holding up the blue kimono so it would not drag on the floor , she went out into the little shed , annexed to the back of the bungalow", "context_len": 29}}}, "calmer": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 13, "context": "Do you mind , Dick , '' she went on , in a calmer tone , as her memory wandered back to happier days , `` do you mind how I foresaw these evil times were at hand , and how I entreated him to flee ? Do you mind , last spring , when that letter came from New England from excellent Master Perrient , how I prayed him to hearken to it ? ''", "context_len": 76}}}, "sou'wester": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Rudolph will like to go , for he has his sou'wester and rubber boots , while as for me I am used to going out in all sorts of weather", "context_len": 30}}}, "refrigerator": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "So Miss Newman did not waste time in getting ready , but started forth in a very few minutes , and then Miss Eloise sent Edna out into the little shed to report upon what she might find in the refrigerator", "context_len": 41}}}, "annexed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Holding up the blue kimono so it would not drag on the floor , she went out into the little shed , annexed to the back of the bungalow", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The debate lasted for a long while , but all present were agreed that the villain Joyce must have annexed it for some particular motive , though \u2019 twas evident that robbery was not intended , the box being of some worthless metal", "context_len": 43}}}, "soggy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "He shivered and felt wretched , with the soggy leathern pouches flapping his neck , like a grotesque tippet ; but ere long his incisors stopped their music , and the chills shook no more", "context_len": 35}}}, "forks": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 44, "context": "In it he had brought tubs to salt the fish in , knives to split , and salt to salt them ; a kettle , pork , potatoes , new cider , apples , cheese , bowls , spoons and plates , knives and forks , and some eggs to roast in the ashes", "context_len": 54}}}, "blueberry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "There was quite a large section of blueberry pie , and there were some slices of bread already cut", "context_len": 19}}}, "sprawling": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 29, "context": "Once down , he seemed in no hurry to get up , and Dick , having satisfied himself that the fellow was more frightened than hurt , left him sprawling in the mud with his twenty shillings scattered round him , and , as Bunyan would have put it , `` went joyfully on his way , and was troubled no more by him at that time", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "To have the pollywog for her own , or to have him any where near her , was the last thing Aunt Patty desired , for she had a great fear of snakes , toads , frogs and such creatures ; and now she started back in dismay as the sprawling thing was laid upon her handsome black silk dress", "context_len": 60}}}, "waterproof": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "I 've seen it too , continued Donald , wondering whether an artist-proof and a waterproof had anything in common ; but the girls are n't often so handsome as that ; but I 'll tell you when they do look pretty as a picture : that 's on a clear Sunday morning , just about midway in the service , when the sun comes streaming through one of the choir windows in a great white shaft of light , I think they call it", "context_len": 85}}}, "inexcusable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "Marie-Celeste waited with a decidedly martyr-like air till this inexcusable whispering was through with , chiming in again at the first opportunity", "context_len": 22}}}, "thoughtlessness": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "Harris had said to her more than once , that there 's nothing but sin itself has so many heavy hearts to answer for as thoughtlessness ; and thoughtfulness , next to love , has lightened and brightened more hearts than anything else in the world and Marie-Celeste knew how thoughtless she had been to press home upon Harold in any way a keener sense of his own great loss", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "It was thoughtlessness rather than anything else that I dreaded ; but now that the admiral himself has spoken to them , there is no fear that anything will occur to give us trouble", "context_len": 34}}}, "freezing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Then I would advise you to go straight home to your mother , '' said Miss Newman in her most freezing manner , `` and I hope it is the last time Edna ever trusts herself to your tender mercies", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Luckily Christmas Day was fine ( for a wet Christmas is enough to damp the stoutest spirits ) , but after that the weather indulged in such a variety of changes , alternately freezing and thawing from day to day , that there was no time for a safe crust of ice to form even on the smallest and shallowest of ponds", "context_len": 62}}}, "trusts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Then I would advise you to go straight home to your mother , '' said Miss Newman in her most freezing manner , `` and I hope it is the last time Edna ever trusts herself to your tender mercies", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "You may be like a very little child , that can \u2019 t even ask for what it needs , but yet trusts and fears not", "context_len": 26}}}, "surrey": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "And there at the station was Miss Florence who gave her a cordial welcome , and the big surrey drove them and three other passengers to their destination", "context_len": 28}}}, "laconic": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "While the Indians talked in their guttural language , sometimes sitting for many minutes of silence between their laconic sentences , Bomba fretted and fumed , eager to be once more on the trail of the headhunters", "context_len": 37}}}, "compromised": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "The enemy dreaded was his own kith and kin ; and although all his sympathies were with his English friends , from whom he had received more kindness and love than he had ever known elsewhere , yet he seemed to feel compromised by the deeds of his kindred , whose savage cruelty no Christianity had as yet softened", "context_len": 59}}}, "entertainers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "He brought a warm coat of his sister 's to wrap Edna up in , and they set off after Edna had given earnest thanks to her entertainers", "context_len": 28}}}, "marrow": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` Gad ! '' he said , suddenly staring at a face that rose above the crowd near him , `` I was almost fool enough to think the fanatic 's prophecy was come true , and there was General Harrison come alive again ! That young fellow yonder is the very marrow of him ! Some one of his family , I dare be sworn , poor wretch , and doubtless of the same way of thinking", "context_len": 78}}}, "paled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "He waited with all a fisherman 's patience , and , used as he was to such scenes , he could not help feeling gladdened at the glorious sight that met his gaze , for , one by one , the stars had paled , till only that named after the morning shone out resplendent in the now grey west ; while to eastward all was blushing with bright red and gold and purple and orange , tints so wondrously beautiful and rich that Nature had enough to spare for sea as well as sky", "context_len": 95}}}, "tumultuous": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 24, "context": "In striking contrast to this mournful scene of sorrow in the palace of Sysigambis , there was an exhibition of the most wild and tumultuous joy in the streets , and in all the public places of resort in the city of Athens , when the tidings of the death of the great Macedonian king arrived there", "context_len": 57}}}, "barker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Barker", "context_len": 1}}}, "cruising": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "During the whole of the month of May the English fleet remained cruising betwixt Gravelines and Dover , till Albemarle began to revile the Dutch for their cowardice in fearing to leave their harbours , while of de Beaufort we had neither signs nor tidings", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "When I say that you are free to choose your own cruising ground , I do not mean that you should go up the Levant , or to the east of the Mediterranean , but that you are not bound to keep close along the African coast , but may , should you obtain any information to warrant your doing so , seek the pirates along the shores of Spain , Sardinia , Corsica , or Sicily", "context_len": 77}}}, "chums": {"freq": 214, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 212, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` The mysterious stranger who entered the school just before we did -- the man who shot against me at the gallery ! Fellows , it 's the same man -- we must catch him ! '' and , as he had done that night in the shooting gallery , Bart darted after the stranger , followed by his chums , Fenn still carrying the turtle", "context_len": 66}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` It would gratify me very much , '' he said , `` if you and I could be chums as your Dad and I were", "context_len": 26}}}, "goodly": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Yes , yes , '' she murmured abstractedly ; `` who could fail to love him ? so valiant and so goodly to look upon , so tender unto his friends , and to me his poor wife , and ever was the inward joy in his bosom breaking forth in praises to God -- and yet '' -- turning wildly on Dick -- '' yet you will let him die ! You are as hard as the nether millstone ! Dick , do not shake your head , you must go ! You must force Prince Rupert to hear you", "context_len": 102}}}, "impressively": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` It was a -- '' he paused and looked impressively at each one , `` a -- I hardly know how to describe it , for it seemed to be amphibious , having once lived on land , and yet I doubt if it will live there ever again", "context_len": 50}}}, "intuitions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` You are a very clever child , Miss Ramsey , but your intuitions fail you upon this occasion", "context_len": 19}}}, "tousling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Ben Barker , you are just the worst tease , '' said Edna , tousling his hair", "context_len": 18}}}, "pommel": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Oh , you mean , mean thing ! '' Edna began to pommel him with her fist and the others joined in", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Alden could make out in the reflection the stranger \u2019 s hands ( one of which grasped the knotted bridle reins ) , the pommel of his saddle , and the tuft of hair at the base of his pony \u2019 s neck , but everything else was invisible in the darkness", "context_len": 52}}}, "yachting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It seemed very queer to see Uncle Justus in yachting cap and flannels when he had always appeared in most severe dress , and never on any occasion wore such a frivolous thing as a cap", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Edna and Ben were the center of interest that evening , for when Edna was not telling something that went on at Ramsey 's , Ben was relating some of his yachting experiences", "context_len": 33}}}, "frivolous": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "There were gentlemen , young , old and middle-aged , some in tourist tweeds , some in boating flannels , and some in sober black , most of them with books or road-maps in their hands , while a sprinkling of ladies , both grave and frivolous , in light summer costumes and with gay parasols , completed the party", "context_len": 60}}}, "schoolmaster": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "In the upper benches of the pavilion were ranged the flower and chivalry of the town -- the families of the mine boss , the liveryman , the lawyer , the schoolmaster and several visiting personages", "context_len": 36}}}, "pendulum": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "As for Edna , she was so divided between her desire to be with her favorite cousin Ben and with Uncle Justus , of whom she was very fond , that she swung between her two desires like a pendulum till Ben caught her and pretended he was going to throw her overboard because she would not walk with him up to the house", "context_len": 64}}}, "squabble": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "By the time this pretended squabble was over Uncle Justus was well ahead with Mr", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "She and I had a squabble in the passage ; she said I was not to come in because I had my red dress on", "context_len": 25}}}, "satellites": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Ramsey , so the three little girls attended Ben like satellites", "context_len": 11}}}, "prior": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 74, "context": "William Lang told of his visit to Oak Swamp , and how he had fled at the sight of the boys , fearing they wanted to cause his arrest , and he also mentioned his trips to the mud volcano , and how he had run away at the sound of some one stirring in the campers ' tent , likewise how he had led the boys a chase about the town , just prior to the accident on the pole", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 14, "context": "The defeat of the Turks created such enthusiasm throughout Europe that when the grand prior of England laid before the king letters he had received from the grand master and Sir John Kendall , speaking in the highest terms of the various great services Gervaise had rendered to the Order , Edward granted his request that the act of attainder against Sir Thomas Tresham and his descendants should be reversed and the estates restored to Gervaise", "context_len": 76}}}, "establish": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 33, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}}}, "aggravated": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 79, "context": "Though I was then happily unaware of the fact , the period of the great annual festival , or Customs , was approaching , and the joy of the populace began to find vent in nocturnal concerts inordinately prolonged , the musical instruments consisting of tom-toms , each beaten by two , three , or four performers -- according to the size of the tom- tom -- with a monotony of cadence that soon became positively maddening , further aggravated by the discordant squealing of a number of flageolet- like instruments made of stout reeds", "context_len": 95}}}, "gosling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Gosling Island is a pretty place , and I think you will all enjoy the sail", "context_len": 16}}}, "infusion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "They will not miss an old fogy like me , but young blood like Ben 's gives a great infusion of spirits", "context_len": 22}}}, "dizziness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "She was so disappointed and tried to persuade herself that the feeling of dizziness would pass away , and that she would be better by the time they were ready to start", "context_len": 32}}}, "sicker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Oh , but , '' Edna went on , `` I shall be much sicker if you stay , because I shall feel as if it were all on my account", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It makes me sicker just to think of it", "context_len": 9}}}, "emma": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Emma and her children , as also Edwy , the son of Ethelred by his first wife , were the chief mourners , nay , the only real ones", "context_len": 29}}}, "darken": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The afternoon shadows were again to darken around Charlotte Tucker ; and one blow after another had to fall", "context_len": 19}}}, "restful": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "After all it was rather pleasant and restful to lie there undisturbed , to know that nothing was expected of her , and that she did not have to pretend to feel better than she really was", "context_len": 37}}}, "undisturbed": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` But I really must have an interview with you , it is of the highest importance , -- when can I see you alone ? When can you give me an hour of your time quite undisturbed ? ''", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "\u2019 She found them , to her great relief , quite serene , \u2019 as serene as she was herself in her funny position , \u2019 -- the clock ticking placidly on , undisturbed by the jar", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "Watchful Chris thought he began to detect signs of weariness in Donald 's occasional answers , and as soon as he felt sure of it he bundled Marie-Celeste off in a hurry , and pinning a shawl over the port-hole , left the little convalescent for a nap undisturbed in his darkened state-room", "context_len": 53}}}, "putter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "lamb": {"freq": 71, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 78, "context": "Happy were happy about it , even if Dolly was dead , because papa and mamma , and all our grown-up , wise people , think she is happy with Jesus ; and we hope our Father will let it be a little jewel to carry to Him , when the angel takes us over the river , and the Elder Brother will say we did it unto Him , because we did it to His poor little lamb that did not know about Him", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Talker just told me there 's a lamb missing and I thought I 'd come up to look for him", "context_len": 20}}}, "babes": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "But the blackbirds and the ring-ousels had been before them , so it took a long time to fill the can , especially as a good deal of the fruit found its way into the children 's mouths , leaving them with such purple lips and stained fingers that they resembled the babes in the wood", "context_len": 56}}}, "sucklings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings , Thou hast perfected praise , '' murmured the old lady to herself , when the door had closed behind the children", "context_len": 31}}}, "adroit": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "Thais did all in her power to captivate and please Alexander during the feast by her vivacity , her wit , her adroit attentions to him , and the display of her charms , and at length , when he himself , as well as the other guests , were excited with wine , she asked him to allow her to have the pleasure of going herself and setting fire , with her own hands , to the great palace of the Persian kings in the city", "context_len": 87}}}, "singed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "Jerusalem , what is this ! bellowed a voice , and Joe Griffin leaped out from the midst of the burning pile ; the brands rolled off the back of his woollen shirt , which was thoroughly singed , while a fox-skin cap he wore was scorched to a crisp , as was his hair ; he ran round and round , as though he was mad , blowing his fingers ( which where slightly burned ) , and slapping them on his thighs , while on his face was a mingled expression of pain , arising from the burn , and anger at being outwitted", "context_len": 106}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Archie had even contrived to manufacture a few fireworks , having shut himself up in a room over the stables at the Willows , a combination of peculiar odours and a singed eyebrow alone testifying to his occupation", "context_len": 38}}}, "subscribed": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 19, "context": "He had a great dislike to any fuss being made about him personally ; and , though 1500 was subscribed during his life to erect a memorial , it was , at his earnest desire , either returned to the subscribers or spent in assisting poor debtors", "context_len": 47}}}, "forthcoming": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 104, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Again and again he has not known where to turn for the next meal for his orphans ; but , as if by a miracle , supplies have been always forthcoming", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 93, "context": "upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant , of course , that I was to die by some exquisite refinement of torture , the nature of which would probably be too dreadful for description", "context_len": 128}}}, "subscription": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 7, "context": "How are you coming on with your subscription scheme to raise funds to hire a football coach for your team , Roger ?", "context_len": 23}}}, "contribution": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 27, "context": "Lilian put out a very tempting looking selection of rock-buns and ginger-nuts , and Archie produced a tin of real Scotch shortbread and some macaroons , a contribution from his aunt ; so with bread-and-butter , and a pot of the newly-made blackberry jam , there was quite a noble display", "context_len": 51}}}, "postage": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 20, "context": "A newspaper cost sevenpence ; there were no national schools or Sunday schools , no penny publications , no penny postage , no railways , no gas , and no free libraries , and no free education ! Yet so resolute was he in his desire for education that , though he was not even allowed a candle after the elders went to bed , he would sit up till late at night reading by the glow of the embers", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Sometimes it was postage stamps , or postmarks , and again minerals , or jackknives , or butterflies", "context_len": 18}}}, "savings": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 9, "context": "No , I 'm not going to take any savings of yours , said Ted , imagining that Donald might so have understood him ; but I want to put the case to you , and have you tell me what to do ; and Donald listened attentively while Ted put his case plainly and without any mental reservations whatever", "context_len": 60}}}, "tricky": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Oh , that was the way , was it ? Well , how is the tricky head to-day ? ''", "context_len": 21}}}, "balky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He would n't budge any more than a balky mule", "context_len": 10}}}, "boastful": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Yes , my lady , though it is too late in the day to make boastful vaunts , and it would have spoiled Uncle Justus 's little game if both of us had come", "context_len": 35}}}, "vaunts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Yes , my lady , though it is too late in the day to make boastful vaunts , and it would have spoiled Uncle Justus 's little game if both of us had come", "context_len": 35}}}, "chipped": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "All the fellows chipped in and I have quite a wad here which I am instructed to turn over to the proper authorities", "context_len": 23}}}, "wad": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "Taking out a cartridge , and bracing himself in the crotch of a limb so as to have both hands free , he dug out , with his knife , the wad that held the shot in place", "context_len": 38}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Bart had managed to wad the cotton in his mouth into a compact wet mass , enabling him to speak", "context_len": 20}}}, "specifying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The boys said never mind specifying names , just say it is from the Pippin", "context_len": 15}}}, "subtle": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 29, "context": "Perhaps it was because he was one of those rare characters in whose presence we can feel certain of perfect sympathy , or perhaps it grew from a more subtle and silent bond , felt keenly by the child , though never spoken of , for Peggy could remember a time when the Rector 's hair was raven black , and there had been a little Raymond Howell playing about on the smooth lawns of the old garden", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Did the child feel the subtle atmosphere ?", "context_len": 8}}}, "intrude": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 4, "context": "And I 'll never intrude on her grand friends , so that her grand friends can come to see her as often as they like ; and as to my mother , she is a right-down good sort , though she was n't born in the purple like yourself , your Grace ; so , as far as I am concerned , I do not know what you have to say to me", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I hope I don \u2019 t intrude", "context_len": 7}}}, "dressy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Well , anyhow , it is much more dressy than yours ; it is more colory , and it is embroidered", "context_len": 22}}}, "contradicted": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "The poor child had no one to tell her how foolish and sinful this was , or to warn her that she was laying up trouble for herself and her friends , for her father would never suffer her to be contradicted or corrected", "context_len": 44}}}, "wrapper": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 15, "context": "Conway turned down the covers of her own bed , slipped Edna into her flannel wrapper , threw a shawl around her and carried her across the room to deposit her in the big bed", "context_len": 35}}}, "dorothy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Dorothy thinks I ought to wear one of my white frocks and I think my gray linen would be better", "context_len": 20}}}, "handbag": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "She was too excited to eat much breakfast , and when they were told that Mack was at the door with the automobile she clutched her little handbag very tightly , for it contained the precious dollar which was to buy so many things that day", "context_len": 46}}}, "intuitively": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "They readjusted things in the comfortable little phaeton and tucked the lap-robe about them in trimmer fashion , and then the ponies , feeling a tightening grasp on the lines , and intuitively conscious of a whip poised at an easily descending angle , wisely saw fit to make up for lost time", "context_len": 53}}}, "inexpensive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The dolls being such a very important matter , it was decided to get these off their minds at once , and therefore to a big , though inexpensive shop they went", "context_len": 32}}}, "lovelier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Oh '' she cried , `` it 's lovelier than the real Cinderella , and you danced with the Prince ? Can you dance -- truly ? ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "restaurant": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "McGlory told of his call at the Liberty Street office , of meeting Random , of his talk with Random in the restaurant , of Random \u2019 s going with him to the Flatiron Building , of the failure to find Matt , and of the yarn told by the driver of the red car", "context_len": 55}}}, "broiled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "There was , first , a broiled fish , fresh from the river , with boiled yams ; then a few roast plantains -- not more than a dozen , I think ; then the roast rib of a cow ; a few handfuls of boiled rice ; and -- yes , I think that was all , except a bowl of jaro ' -- the latter being a kind of native beer", "context_len": 73}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The bear steak , broiled with pieces of bacon stuck on it , was voted most delicious , and Fenn ate so much that he said it made him sleepy", "context_len": 30}}}, "charlotte": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Then each chose her own dessert , Dorothy taking a chocolate eclair , Edna peach ice-cream and Jennie charlotte russe", "context_len": 20}}}, "botchily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` But , dearie , I think I ought to be with you , because these dolls for the bazar should be dressed very neatly , and not botchily as they might be if you were doing them merely for yourselves", "context_len": 41}}}, "littlest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "He told the children to bid Dolly good-by , which they did ; the girl replying in a more gentle tone than she had yet used , and then calling Bessie back , saying , `` Here , littlest one", "context_len": 40}}}, "scouted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "But Percy and Seabrooke had both scouted the idea ; no one , they both said , knew that the former had intrusted his money to Seabrooke ; no one had been present at the time , and both declared that they had spoken of it to no one", "context_len": 49}}}, "systematic": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 54, "context": "From Norfolk Island , in 1870 , he wrote to his sister when he was holding an ordination : `` At such times as these , when one is specially engaged in solemn work , there is much heart searching ; and I can not tell you how my conscience accuses me of such systematic selfishness during many long years -- I mean I see how I was all along making self the centre , and neglecting all kinds of duties -- social and others -- in consequence ''", "context_len": 89}}}, "underclothes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` This white stuff will do for their underclothes , '' she told the others", "context_len": 15}}}, "brunette": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Jennie decided upon a thin bit of yellow for her doll of decidedly brunette type and they set to work", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "He had had the color of youth in his cheeks , in spite of the swarthiness of his skin , and I had admired the combination -- your light-haired man is for everything that has a touch of the brunette -- but now he had gone white", "context_len": 47}}}, "progressed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "Their first thought each day on coming home from school was to rush off to see how 'Sky Cottage , ' as they had christened it , had progressed in their absence , and their grief was loud if anything hindered Archie from the prosecution of his labours on Saturdays , while they quite envied Lilian being able to run out any time she liked and take a peep at the operations", "context_len": 72}}}, "sighs": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 67, "context": "She had lost her hat , and the wind had tossed up her curls , her cheeks were pale and streaked with tears , and her big brown eyes had a pitiful look in them that would have softened a tiger , let alone a man that had half a dozen little ones of his own at home ; while every now and then the great heavy sighs came struggling up , as if she had almost cried her heart out", "context_len": 81}}}, "purchases": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 68, "context": "Now you can take him into the servants ' quarters , Muley , and tell them that he is a new slave whom I have purchased , and that henceforth it will be his duty to wait upon my wife , to whom I have presented him as her special attendant , and that he will accompany her and my daughters when they go abroad to make their purchases or visit their friends", "context_len": 73}}}, "contributions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Dorothy and Edna did not fail to write home about the fire and the bazar and made their request for contributions of money or fancy articles", "context_len": 26}}}, "centerpiece": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` The two were sitting on the porch with Jennie just inside by the window busily working away at an embroidered centerpiece she was doing in outline", "context_len": 27}}}, "lecturers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Here concerts were given , dances took place , lecturers found a platform", "context_len": 13}}}, "gypsy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Tea was served in a tent outside near a gypsy camp where pretended fortunes were told by a pretty girl with dark eyes , whose costume made one almost believe she really belonged to that wandering race", "context_len": 37}}}, "blooms": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}}}, "bouquet": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "A bower of green in one corner of the hall sheltered the flower girls who offered all kinds of blooms , from a bunch of field flowers to a bouquet of American Beauty roses", "context_len": 34}}}, "donated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "Two years before , Colonel Harrington had erected at his own expense a lofty flagpole at the side of the cannon and donated an elegant flag", "context_len": 26}}}, "prompt": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "And HE who looks on the heart , HE who gauges our actions not by results but by the motives which prompt them , -- HE , we may well believe , honoured His servant for her faithful work in His Service", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 34, "context": "This , then , was the gallant Montague , a man who , under the Commonwealth , had earned renown when fighting with Blake the fleets of Holland and of Spain , and whose prompt action in co-operating with Monk and taking command of the fleet sent to fetch the king from Holland did much to earn the royal gratitude and favour", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "His instructions were that , `` after quelling all disturbances at Allahabad , he should not lose a moment in supporting Sir Henry Lawrence at Lucknow , and Sir Hugh Wheeler at Cawnpore ; and that he should take prompt measures for dispersing and utterly destroying all mutineers and insurgents ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "rummaged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` She rummaged around in the boxes at the back , and finally produced what she was looking for which pleased Edna greatly , and it was handed over to her", "context_len": 31}}}, "wedged": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "He worked the canoe in as far toward the island as he dared and wedged it tightly among the rushes , trusting that they would hold it for him until he returned", "context_len": 32}}}, "crocheted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 73, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "worsted": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "Some hours later the captain returned , and upon my repeating to him the yarn which I had spun to the mate he not only very readily consented to my working my passage , but also offered me two excellent suits of clothes , two shirts , two pairs of stockings , a pair of shoes , and a worsted cap in exchange for my leopard-skin , which offer I gladly accepted ; and that night found me domiciled in the forecastle of L'Esperance as one of her crew", "context_len": 89}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "He ran after the children with little short , sharp barks , worrying at their heels till Aunt Helen declared there would soon not be a whole stocking left in the establishment ; he had a fray at once with Jack , but was much worsted by that worthy , returning to Peggy for protection with his scrap of a tail between his legs ; he tore Bobby 's cricket-ball to pieces , licked the polish off Father 's boots , devoured Aunt Helen 's goloshes , and nestled cosily down to sleep on the top of Nancy 's best Sunday hat , which that luckless damsel had imprudently left in an open band-box on the kitchen settle", "context_len": 118}}}, "thingamabob": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Let 's see about the worsted thingamabob first", "context_len": 8}}}, "attendance": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 23, "context": "The Sultan was amazed when he saw Alaeddin in this regal array and beheld his grace and goodliness and the mamelukes standing in attendance upon him in all their comeliness and fair favour ; yea , and his wonderment redoubled when Alaeddin 's mother came up attired in rich and costly raiment , as she were a queen , and he saw twelve slave-girls in her service , preceding her , their hands clasped behind their backs , with all worship and observance", "context_len": 83}}}, "priced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` So they are , '' put in the lady in attendance , `` but because they are the highest priced they have not been sold yet", "context_len": 27}}}, "wormed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Bomba dropped on his stomach and wormed his way through the brush until he reached the outermost edge of the zone of light cast by the fire", "context_len": 27}}}, "overhear": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 59, "context": "He is ready to risk his life on behalf of a slave , and can strike down men with his unarmed hand ; he is as gentle in his manner as a woman ; and now it seems he can talk Arabic , and although it was in his power to keep this secret he tells it rather than overhear words that are not meant for his ear", "context_len": 68}}}, "giddy": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 55, "context": "Then what is that field compared to all England , or England to Europe , or Europe to the whole world ? Neither your little head , nor the wisest man \u2019 s , can imagine how many blossoms and how many insects there are on this great globe , -- it makes one almost giddy to think of it , -- and then to consider that all the world itself is only like a speck in God \u2019 s Creation , that there are said to be eighty millions of fixed stars , each of which has very likely worlds moving round it", "context_len": 104}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I have only ceased to be giddy for the time being , '' I said", "context_len": 16}}}, "cashier": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 25, "context": "Leaning against a writing desk at the wall , he watched his companion as she boldly made her way to the railing behind which the cashier transacted his business", "context_len": 29}}}, "eatables": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 82, "context": "He never let on that he suspected for a moment that he was not entirely alone , but , walking over to a tree stump , where , spread out on a newspaper , was the remains of a lunch , he acted delighted at the discovery , picked up a hunk of bread in one hand , a piece of cheese in the other , and , throwing himself on the green sward at full length , proceeded to munch the eatables , with every semblance of satisfaction", "context_len": 89}}}, "patronized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The fishpond was out of doors and had been so greatly patronized that it was a very difficult matter to keep it stocked with fish of proper quality , and latterly there had been frequent raids upon the candy stand for such things as might serve for fish", "context_len": 48}}}, "latterly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` I am not like to mistake the colours of Master Harrison -- or Captain Harrison , is it not ? '' answered the girl ; `` and whatever differences did latterly divide us in mind , though not in love , from General Harrison , you must needs know we were all for the Parliament here -- my grandfather , my father , and I ; that is how I came to guess you for one in hiding from the king 's men ; but for your own sake I would have you careful , lest even walls should have ears", "context_len": 102}}}, "raids": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 16, "context": "During his comparatively brief service in the trenches he had been called upon to repel isolated raids , both by day and night ; he had taken part in several successful excursions over the top to harry the German trenches ; he had participated in one of the greatest actions on the Western Front ; but , for the first time , he was helping to man a captured position against a massive hostile counter-attack", "context_len": 75}}}, "correspondingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "They were a quaint little pair , for the boy 's trousers were very long for his short legs and his shirt sleeves were correspondingly short for his arms", "context_len": 29}}}, "starched": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "At the ball Frances made her appearance dressed as Queen Elizabeth , -- very neat and very stately , \u2019 -- while Charlotte represented the star of the morning , \u2019 in a dress of pure muslin , full and well starched , so nicely made and so beautifully white that the impression of it lasts still in the mind of a brother , after the lapse of more than half a century", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "He was young in years only , being a stiff , grave , wooden-faced man , who in his starched manners was a close copy of his father", "context_len": 28}}}, "interpreted": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 85, "context": "A grand place , too , to work , you think ; and then you recall with a sigh that the light of that glorious mind has well-nigh gone out , 'neath the weight of physical weariness and infirmity , and then the solitary home begins to look a little like a prison in your eyes , as you realize how glad its inmate would be to exchange it for the Palace of that King whose divine intent for the world he has so marvellously interpreted for us all in the days when soul was still master of hand and brain", "context_len": 101}}}, "sis": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` There 's no specially bad news , '' he said , `` but it 's this way : She has a malady which , sooner or later , unless it is conquered , will -- will take her away from me -- and sis", "context_len": 45}}}, "devouring": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "But now they are there , all of them , as merry and jocular as you like , running about the place , racing here and there , and devouring all our best fruit , tramping in and out of the greenhouses and conservatory , and making such a noise just over your study", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Mrs Mason hurried away , and Faith still sat still ; but a devouring wish was now possessing her", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Finally , the army was obliged to draw back , and to abandon all that was combustible in the vast construction they had reared , to the devouring flames", "context_len": 29}}}, "silenced": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 4, "context": "It bore down and silenced , for the time , every other suffering , so that his first cry , when his enemies came around him with shouts of exultation , was not for his life , not for mercy , not for relief from the pain and anguish of his wounds -- he begged them to give him some water", "context_len": 61}}}, "benefactors": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "The sight of the rows of pikemen with their eighteen-feet weapons riveted my attention till I was recalled to my senses by being dismissed by my benefactors , who gave me in charge of a sour-visaged soldier , with instructions to take me to the house of Master Anderson in St", "context_len": 51}}}, "trudge": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Do you suppose he is going to spend it all at the fish-pond ? '' said Dorothy as she watched him trudge off", "context_len": 24}}}, "salable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "By this time the tables in the hall were swept of nearly everything salable , and the tea had given out in the tea-tent", "context_len": 24}}}, "toddling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "She was a very little girl , and could just walk alone , and while she was toddling about after her father , she fell down and knocked her head against the corner of a door", "context_len": 36}}}, "toddlers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They were not long in catching up with the toddlers", "context_len": 10}}}, "tape": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 56, "context": "While the cowboy sat in his chair in front of the blackboard , wondering how long he could wait for Random or Griggs and yet be at the Flatiron Building as per appointment with Matt , a man sauntered in , looked at an office boy who was just going out with an armful of ticker tape , and then approached McGlory", "context_len": 62}}}, "toddle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` You 'd better toddle home , '' said Jennie", "context_len": 10}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Come on , Stoney , let \u2019 s toddle up to the acad", "context_len": 13}}}, "funnier": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` And it 's a great deal jolly funnier than if you caught them and shut them up in a cage , is it not ? '' said Bessie", "context_len": 29}}}, "reverence": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 107, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Sir , '' cried Dick , with a gleam of comprehension that almost amounted to reverence , `` that may be London fashion ; we country folk hang the broadsword in the place of honour , and account it the prime treasure of the house", "context_len": 46}}}, "relics": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 40, "context": "So the old chest remained in the loft , as hidden there as when it had been buried in the earth , and Peggy came and went , never dreaming in the time that followed that these ancient , musty relics could in any way be bound up with the fate and fortunes of the Vaughans", "context_len": 56}}}, "subway": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` That was like looking for a needle in a haystack , '' said the lady , `` for you did n't know which of the subway stations they meant , did you ? There are a great many , you know", "context_len": 42}}}, "embattled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Then , ho , for Lexington ! We must take a subway car , and seek the 'rude bridge ' where 'the embattled farmers stood to fire the shot heard 'round the world", "context_len": 34}}}, "jostled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "But Jennie wanted to walk with her mother too , and so the other two little girls dropped behind to pursue their way through the crooked streets where odd sights met their eyes ; queerly dressed women and children jostled them ; at the doors of houses swarthy faces and strange forms appeared", "context_len": 53}}}, "retrace": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}}}, "denser": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "With one consent they stood still and looked around as if hoping to see a familiar face , but here was a denser crowd of foreigners and only the dark eyes of Russians and Poles met theirs", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Accordingly , dropping on her hands and knees , she left the safe shelter of the denser part of the Wilderness , and crawled out to a bush", "context_len": 28}}}, "leered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I do n't like it a bit here , '' said Dorothy as a hideous old woman leered down at them", "context_len": 22}}}, "accosted": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "Gervaise went up to the palace , and while waiting in the great hall until summoned before the council he was warmly accosted by several knights , -- some of whom were quite strangers to him , -- who all joined in congratulating him on the immense service he had done to the Order", "context_len": 54}}}, "clerical": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "Bi ' l ghewali may be a clerical error for bi ' l ghalibi , `` for the most part , in general , '' in which case we may read , `` Folk in general abound [ in talk ] anent her virtues ; '' or bi ' l ghewali may perhaps be used in the sense ( of which use , however , I know no instance ) of 'in excessive estimation , ' in which latter case the passage might be rendered , `` Folk abound in setting a high value on ( or extolling ) her virtues", "context_len": 101}}}, "taxicab": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 27, "context": "Matt recounted the manner in which he had been beguiled into the open country by the supposed messenger ; and he told about the accident to the taxicab , the revelation that the supposed youth was a girl , the finding of the driver , the passing of the red touring car with McGlory in the tonneau , the work of Dimmock and Sanders , a mile west of Rye , and the journey through Connecticut and into Massachusetts , finishing with his meeting with McGlory", "context_len": 86}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "What taxicab ? the man asked , drawing one hand across his forehead", "context_len": 13}}}, "cavernous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "A most bewildering place they found this same subway to be , full of people rushing for trains , noisy from the whizzing of cars from out of cavernous dark places and departing into equally unknown darkness", "context_len": 37}}}, "confusing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "You always think of the right things at the right time , instead of thinking of the right things at the wrong time or the wrong things at the right time , which is very confusing", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "But it could not leap like Bomba , and with the torpor of its long sleep confusing it and the light of the fire blinding it , plunged headlong into the flames", "context_len": 32}}}, "jostling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The roaring of passing trains was so confusing , the jostling of the crowd was so unpleasant that the children held fast to one another and hurried up the steps and into the open air", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Having had the good fortune to get unperceived past a sentry who was closely questioning a native , they came into the principal street of Lucknow , jostling against the armed rebels , who would have killed them in a moment had their suspicion been aroused", "context_len": 46}}}, "tension": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 1, "context": "The tension was relieved by the woman , who looked at both the young fellows , and then turned to the surgeon and asked almost unconcernedly , `` Ai n't war a hell of a thing ? ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "haystack": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was certainly Peggy who suggested sitting on the top of the haystack , and making it sway backwards and forwards like a swing , a delightful sensation while it lasted , but which ended suddenly in the collapse of the whole top of the stack , much to Father 's wrath , for it took David a couple of hours to repair the damage , and certainly did not sweeten the old man 's already crusty temper", "context_len": 78}}}, "notify": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` Well , '' answered Bart desperately , somewhat weary and nervous over the ordeal through which he had gone , `` the chief reason was that if I told that , I 'd have to tell why we did n't notify the police", "context_len": 44}}}, "plaintively": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I am always getting lost , it seems to me , '' said Edna plaintively , `` and yet I am never really lost , or I was n't before this time , only people will keep thinking I am", "context_len": 41}}}, "mimicked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` You did n't mean to , '' mimicked Ben in a mocking voice", "context_len": 14}}}, "hysterics": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` There 's the way of succumbing like , and going into hysterics , and making no end of a scene , and the man stays on all the same , and the neighbours get wind of it , and the ruin 's complete in no time , so to speak", "context_len": 51}}}, "vindictively": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` I wish that old window was in the bottom of the sea before I ever stopped to look in , '' presently said Dorothy vindictively", "context_len": 26}}}, "heedlessness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 90, "context": "younger , extreme carelessness had been her besetting fault , and , as is the manner of this `` little fox , '' had created much trouble for herself and for others ; but having become convinced that it was her duty to cure herself of this , she had set to work to do it in such earnest that that which had been a burden and a care to her was fast becoming a settled habit , and it was but seldom now that any act or word of heedlessness could be laid to her charge , while her ever obliging disposition and loving heart prompted many a deed of kindness which she never failed to carry out if it were in her power to do so", "context_len": 128}}}, "moralizing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But she was spared moralizing upon this to Dorothy , for Ben returned saying that Mrs", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "You 'd forgive this bit of what the critics call moralizing -- it is all the outcome of that remark of Mrs", "context_len": 22}}}, "sunniest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "He was quite grumpy about it and the little girls were most unhappy at being under his displeasure , for Ben was usually the sunniest of persons and rarely gave them a cross look", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "We put her in the sunniest room , and when we found that her nerves were so terribly shaken that she could scarcely sleep alone , one of my sisters had a little bed made up in the room and slept with her at night", "context_len": 45}}}, "culprits": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "Only one-half of the boys were implicated in that affair ; they had already been punished by the restrictions which had been placed upon them , and were to be further disgraced by the public reprimand which he intended to give them on the dismissal of the school ; and these culprits were probably dreading this or some other severe punishment which would be meted out to them by the report of their misconduct which would be sent home", "context_len": 79}}}, "parkway": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "There was a drive around the beautiful parkway that evening and a visit to the splendid library that night", "context_len": 19}}}, "botanical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Ramsey at the Parker House , and to get ready for their journey home , but there was a chance to walk through the botanical gardens and the Commons , to look across at the gilded dome of the State House , and to see the church where the great Phillips Brooks had preached", "context_len": 54}}}, "dome": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 64, "context": ", his face frowned and he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward , [ 660 ] I excuse you ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "whiled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Ben whiled away the time by asking them ridiculous conundrums which made them so hilarious that more than one fellow traveller smiled in sympathy with their merry laughs", "context_len": 28}}}, "conundrums": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Ben whiled away the time by asking them ridiculous conundrums which made them so hilarious that more than one fellow traveller smiled in sympathy with their merry laughs", "context_len": 28}}}, "advertisement": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ Transcriber 's Note : The advertisement for `` Famous Alger Books '' has been moved from its position before the main text to the rear of the book", "context_len": 29}}}, "malted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I would n't ; I 'd use Horlick 's malted milk", "context_len": 12}}}, "roasts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Then this is the answer : I 'd put my roasts through a course of Browning", "context_len": 17}}}, "limerick": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Now each of you try to make up a limerick and I 'll do the same", "context_len": 16}}}, "limericks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "They kept up the limericks for some time , though Dorothy found it such hard work that she finally refused to try any more , and Ben looking at his watch decided it was time to go into the dining-car for dinner", "context_len": 42}}}, "limbered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` The old chap had a good time while he was with us , '' Ben told her , `` and I think it limbered him up a lot", "context_len": 29}}}, "ramrods": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` No , he was stiff from eating too many ramrods", "context_len": 11}}}, "closets": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Bradford was busy unpacking trunks and arranging drawers and closets ; for the family had just come home from the sea-shore , where they had been spending the summer ; but she was so surprised to hear Maggie say this that she turned around with her hands full , to look at her little daughter", "context_len": 55}}}, "disremember": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "When they learns that their mother was going to town they says we 'll go to one of the neighbors , I disremember which one it was , but they says they wo n't be back to lunch , bein ' as they do n't like to ate without the ithers", "context_len": 51}}}, "release": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 62, "context": "But , whatever the order may have been , Mafuta evidently objected to it , for no sooner had it been spoken than he sprang to his feet , and with quite marvellous agility , hurried to me and seized me by the left arm , saying in an angry voice something to the guards that I interpreted as an order to release their hold upon me", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Come , confess , Aunt Patty , '' he said ; `` acknowledge that it is to you this good fellow who has just left us owes his freedom from debt , his child 's eyesight , his release from cares which were almost too much even for his hopeful spirit ; acknowledge that you have generously sacrificed a long-cherished desire , given up the fruits of much saving and self-denial , to make those happy in whom you could have had no interest save as creatures and children of one common Father", "context_len": 94}}}, "bearlike": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then Ben came in and there were more explanations , and next the boys came rushing upstairs to give boisterous bearlike hugs and to tell Edna she looked fine as silk , and so the hours went on till it was time for Mr", "context_len": 44}}}, "hugs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Then Ben came in and there were more explanations , and next the boys came rushing upstairs to give boisterous bearlike hugs and to tell Edna she looked fine as silk , and so the hours went on till it was time for Mr", "context_len": 44}}}, "scuttling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` For Uncle Justus had never shown the boys his gentler side and they stood in great awe of him , scuttling out of sight whenever they saw him coming", "context_len": 30}}}, "murky": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It seemed warm and murky after the sharp fresh from the sea , and Edna , for all her excitement , was ready for bed early", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 70, "context": "And when , at night , impressed with the sense of solemnity which night always imparts to strange and novel scenes , they looked up to the bright round moon , pleased with the expression of cheerfulness and companionship which beams always in her light , to find her suddenly waning , changing her form , withdrawing her bright beams , and looking down upon them with a lurid and murky light , it was not surprising that they felt an emotion of terror", "context_len": 84}}}, "bazaar": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` `` The poor , '' adds he , `` at Cairo buy sheepsheads and for a trifle have them boiled in the bazaar by persons who are not only cooks , but sellers of sheepsheads , and are therefore called raa '' s , or in the Egyptian dialect rewwas", "context_len": 51}}}, "comma": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "59 : `` remarked Dorothy ; '' I do n't '' Added space between comma and double quotation mark ;", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Why all this '' Period changed to comma ;", "context_len": 9}}}, "today": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 6, "context": "When I went to him again today , he said that he had learnt from the governor of the prison and from the head mason that you were almost beyond price , that you had been raised to the position of superintendent of the slaves employed in the building of his palace , and that you were a man of such skill that he would not part with you at any price until the work was finished", "context_len": 77}}}, "coma": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "133 : `` we are getting today '' today changed to to-day ; `` '' Some day , '' she said `` '' Added coma after said ;", "context_len": 28}}}, "superfluous": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 68, "context": "She lived for others ; whether in the great work of her life , the use of her pen , the proceeds of which went to fill her charity purse , or in the simple act of leaving her quiet room , on a dull , rainy afternoon , to play a bright country dance or Scotch reel , and set the little ones dancing to vent their superfluous spirits", "context_len": 70}}}, "hyphenated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "There are some words at line-breaks in the original where it is not clear if they should be hyphenated or not : woe-begone , vouchsafe , fireplace , lobster-pots , tip-toeing , homeless , haystack , homecoming ;", "context_len": 38}}}, "grandma": {"freq": 95, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 78, "word_index": 54, "context": "Ben told such a funny story about the lady by the willow tree , that Edna could never look at the picture again without laughing , but he had scarcely finished it before some one called out : `` Bedtime for little folks ! '' and all the younger ones trooped off upstairs , grandma herself leading the way to see that each one was tucked in comfortably", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` She gathered up her booty and piled it on the table , then some one proposed a game , and they amused themselves till grandma sent out for nuts , cider , apples and cakes , which feast ended the entertainment , though it is safe to say it lasted more than an hour", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "We are not going to have a real , regular set-the-table supper , because grandma thinks Amanda and Reliance should have some holiday , too , but we are going to have sandwiches and cakes and nuts and apples and cider and a whole lot of things ; something like a party you know", "context_len": 54}}}, "grandpa": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 19, "context": "The two little girls followed Ben into the next room and before long everyone was trying to escape from grandpa who was as eager for a game of Blind Man 's Buff as anybody , and who at last caught Becky , who in turn caught Howard Colby because he did n't try to get out of her way", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Why , Pinky Blooms , I was on my way to grandpa 's , thought I would come to take mother back to-morrow , and , as it was a fine afternoon , I concluded , to walk up from the station", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The girls proudly pinned on their medals and wore them home , for very soon came grandpa to say they must get ready to go", "context_len": 25}}}, "workshop": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "Lafitte , and was informed , much to his disappointment , that he was at his workshop on Long Island , and would probably not be in the city for two or three days", "context_len": 34}}}, "tickling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "I therefore came to the conclusion that if vomiting could be freely induced almost immediate relief ought to follow ; and I accordingly prescribed the only emetic which I could think of at the moment , namely , copious draughts of warm water , followed by tickling the back of the throat with a feather", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Stop your tickling , Prince , '' he said ; `` here 's a bit of sugar for you", "context_len": 20}}}, "pill": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "I hope he is n't any kin to them and I am sorry he is to us , for he is a pill", "context_len": 23}}}, "meowed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Of course , if he fussed and meowed , I would take off the things and let him go", "context_len": 19}}}, "silvery": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "There one moment stood the statuesque figure , white as a cameo cut in the black rock , the next moment there was a gleam of something flashing through the air , and passing into the deep blue wave , which , as if by the contact of the figure , broke into silvery foam , rushing back like a vast cascade towards the Shangles", "context_len": 65}}}, "cozy": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` She is always well up in her studies , is n't she ? You remember that I did n't have the usual visit last summer , and as Alice is going to stay we could all have a nice cozy time together", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Very well , Letty , '' she said , `` well have a nice , little , cozy chat all to ourselves after awhile when this impatient young person has had her subject discussed", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "The house looked cozy and cheerful , yellow chrysanthemums in tall vases graced the hall and library ; in the latter , an open grate fire glowed , and Edna looked around complacently", "context_len": 33}}}, "jealously": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Are there any girls there ? '' asked Dorothy , a little jealously", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 76, "context": "Nevertheless , I know that it was spoken of last night , and although it has not yet been finally settled , and will not be until the council meet this afternoon , I should not tell you if I did not think that it was as good as agreed upon ; and I am pleased to be the first to whisper to you that it is intended to bestow upon you an honour that is jealously guarded and seldom granted , even to crowned heads , unless as a token of gratitude for some signal service done to the Republic", "context_len": 101}}}, "churns": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Around the room ran a broad shelf , also of stone , and on this were placed the bright tin pans , most of them now full of milk , and in one corner were two or three churns", "context_len": 39}}}, "snugly": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 112, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}}}, "maples": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "But there was grandpa himself waiting to help them off the train , to see that their trunks were safely stowed into the big farm wagon , and at last to tuck them snugly into the carriage which was to bear them to the white house set in behind a stately row of maples", "context_len": 54}}}, "clambering": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "The pirate could swim very well and in a few minutes came clambering over the side of the ship again , but he looked very wet and ashamed and sneaked down into the hold as if he knew better than to come back to the party", "context_len": 46}}}, "generations": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 27, "context": "This was quite a new gospel to Peggy , and though she could not altogether reconcile it with her clinging love for the home of so many generations of Vaughans , it did her good in that it gave her a fresh aspect of life , for it is always wise to look at things from another person 's point of view , as well as your own , and she had a great respect for Archie 's opinions", "context_len": 79}}}, "willis": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Willis who listened interestedly to what they had to tell", "context_len": 10}}}, "furbish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Willis , `` and we must furbish up once in a while", "context_len": 12}}}, "unpacking": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Bradford was busy unpacking trunks and arranging drawers and closets ; for the family had just come home from the sea-shore , where they had been spending the summer ; but she was so surprised to hear Maggie say this that she turned around with her hands full , to look at her little daughter", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "The boy greeting him with a flash of white teeth , made no formality over admitting an entire stranger , but at once introduced him into a little book-closet on the ground floor , where a gentleman was busily engaged in unpacking folios from a great sea chest ; and as he turned to receive the visitor , Dick , to his inexpressible relief , saw a face that had been familiar to him in Scotland", "context_len": 76}}}, "vases": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 14, "context": "And such a garden as it proved ! with its fountains and statues and vases , and the orangery on one side , and on the other three sides a beautiful sloping lawn , ascending from the level of the garden to the gray stonewall at the outer edge of the terrace ; and to think that here they were actually walking about in this beautiful garden , instead of merely peering through the fretwork of the iron gate , as some other little children with envious eyes were doing that very moment", "context_len": 93}}}, "silhouette": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` The silhouette , you mean ? Yes , that is she , and she is the same one who did that sampler you see hanging between the windows", "context_len": 29}}}, "sampler": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` The silhouette , you mean ? Yes , that is she , and she is the same one who did that sampler you see hanging between the windows", "context_len": 29}}}, "samplers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I am glad I do n't have to do samplers", "context_len": 11}}}, "urn": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Miss Carter gave utterance to one piercing scream , laid the cup which she had been filling from a huge urn hastily on the table , and disappeared from view", "context_len": 30}}}, "decipher": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "Not only was a light showing from the Pomfret Castle ; it was blinking , sending a message in Morse , although the signalman of the cruiser was unable to decipher the code", "context_len": 33}}}, "almshouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` No , I was n't in an orphan asylum , if that 's what you mean , but I reckon I would have had to go there or else to the almshouse", "context_len": 33}}}, "vittles": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "It ain \u2019 t right for a big , growin \u2019 youngster like you to be alwus a-eatin \u2019 cold vittles , \u2019 specially when he \u2019 s studyin \u2019 hard", "context_len": 31}}}, "greats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then there are the greats ; great Aunt Emmeline and her brother , Wilbur Merrifield , and his daughter , Cousin Becky", "context_len": 22}}}, "nineteen": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 40, "context": "An interesting reference to this event is found in a letter of Charlotte Tucker \u2019 s , written February 15 , 1890 : As I went in my duli to villages this morning , I thought , One hundred and nineteen years ago a precious Baby was born in a distant island ; and I thanked God for our beloved and honoured Father", "context_len": 63}}}, "sweetheart": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 41, "context": "The worst of it was that he infected Nancy with his alarms to such an extent that she could scarcely be persuaded to put her nose out at the back-door after dusk ( except on her evening out , when her sweetheart came to fetch her ) , which was distinctly inconvenient if a pail of water were wanted during the evening , or she happened to have left the coal-box standing in the yard", "context_len": 75}}}, "fillings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I heard Amanda say that she would make the fillings to-day for pumpkin , lemon and apple ; she has the crust all done", "context_len": 24}}}, "plum": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 6, "context": "Your grandma was talking about having plum pudding , but Amanda said she did n't see the sense of having it when it was n't Christmas , and there would be such lots of other things , all the nuts and apples and such things", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` I do n't know what it is , '' declared Frank , indignantly , `` only I know it is n't plum pudding", "context_len": 24}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Is this a joke , or what ? I thought you were going to give us plum pudding ! ''", "context_len": 21}}}, "cleanly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 21, "context": "Eliot was ready for this , and , seeming to gauge the distance the ball would travel , he took it cleanly and easily , shooting past the first man who came at him , dodging the second , and bringing the spectators to their feet by a run that carried him to Clearport \u2019 s thirty yard line ere he was forced out of bounds", "context_len": 66}}}, "distribute": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "The short November day was already so near its end that the fowls were thinking of going to roost , though the hour was not late , and after watching them take their supper , which Edna helped Reliance to distribute , the two girls went on to the garden , now robbed of most of its vegetables", "context_len": 58}}}, "celery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Is your mistress in the kitchen ? he asked of Martha , who was arranging some sweet peas in a celery glass as a decoration for the table", "context_len": 28}}}, "discoverer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Edna felt that she was quite a discoverer when she came across some tiny yellow tomatoes which the frost had not yet touched , and which she gathered in triumph to carry back to her mother", "context_len": 36}}}, "chestnut": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 9, "context": "Harris were out for a drive behind Harold 's chestnut ponies , and , as usual , when something important had need to be talked over , the ponies did pretty much as they liked , and that meant , I am ashamed to say ( for they were quite too young to so much as think of being lazy ) , keeping up the merest pretence of a trot for a while , and then subsiding into a walk altogether", "context_len": 81}}}, "chestnuts": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 35, "context": "The Virginia creepers -- always the first to change -- turned a bright crimson ; here and there a maple flung out a scarlet branch , like a gorgeous banner in the air ; while chestnuts and birch showed a few golden leaves , in beautiful contrast to the vivid green of the foliage which was yet untouched", "context_len": 58}}}, "nut": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 71, "context": "You ca n't get it at no chemist 's , Hannah Searles , and the neighbour wot give it me ha ' gone to Ameriky ; but see yere , fur I real feels for disturbed and worrited mothers , I 'll give yer a tiny drop in this yere bottle , and you can take it to her ; ten drops ull settle that baby off as sound as a nut", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "As the little party moved swiftly on , a large castanha nut struck Ashati on the shoulder with such force that he was felled to the ground", "context_len": 27}}}, "burs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` The girls did not roam far from the house but the boys wandered much further afield , bringing caps and pockets full of nuts , and clothes full of burs and stick-tights , even Ben brought back a hoard of persimmons touched by the frost and as sweet as honey", "context_len": 51}}}, "improvised": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` I suppose we 'd better be going back , '' said Reliance when she lifted the improvised bag to her arm", "context_len": 22}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The thirteen able-bodied members of the party worked feverishly , with shovels improvised from pieces of wreckage , to fill in the last gaping bomb craters", "context_len": 26}}}, "skimmed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 55, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The skimmed milk goes to the pigs", "context_len": 7}}}, "excusing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Then excusing himself for a few minutes , he ran to the station where the agent and his companion were curiously watching the movements of the emigrants", "context_len": 27}}}, "dehorned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Your grandpa 's do n't have ; he keeps only dehorned cattle", "context_len": 13}}}, "rend": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` She would really have like to try her hand if there had been but one cow , but when there were six , how could a young person be certain that one of the number would not turn and rend her ? To be sure , they were much less fearsome without horns , but still they were too big and dreadful to be entirely trusted", "context_len": 67}}}, "fearsome": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` She would really have like to try her hand if there had been but one cow , but when there were six , how could a young person be certain that one of the number would not turn and rend her ? To be sure , they were much less fearsome without horns , but still they were too big and dreadful to be entirely trusted", "context_len": 67}}}, "earthen": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 77, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "He ordered all the houses in rear of the wall to be levelled ; a deep semicircular ditch was then dug , and behind this a new wall , constructed of the stones and bricks from the houses destroyed , was built , and backed with an earthen rampart of great thickness and solidity", "context_len": 54}}}, "crocks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "If it should turn cold suddenly now , we 'd have to bring it in , '' Amanda told her , as she carefully lifted the earthen crocks into place", "context_len": 30}}}, "tarry": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 69, "context": "`` Lord ha \u2019 mercy on me if \u2019 tis not young Master Wentworth ! '' he exclaimed , showing that , though grown in stature , I had not outgrown the appearance of my boyhood ; and when Drake told him of my newly found title , the old man tottered away to let the frequenters of the inn know the news ; whereat we , unwilling to tarry longer , pushed on towards the towering heights of Hindhead", "context_len": 80}}}, "enlarge": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Edna swung the big key on her finger by its string and trotted along by the side of Reliance , asking many questions , and delighting to hear Reliance enlarge upon the all-important subject of the Thanksgiving festivities", "context_len": 38}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Already there were breaks in the door that the puma was trying to enlarge with its claws so that it could push its body through", "context_len": 25}}}, "candlelight": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "I expect to be up before daylight , myself , and I reckon Ira will be milking by candlelight , '' she added , as she entered the kitchen door", "context_len": 30}}}, "freshened": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "All day the felucca made rapid progress , for although the wind freshened , Gervaise did not lessen sail as before , being now accustomed to the boat and confident of her powers", "context_len": 33}}}, "fatiguing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "It had been an exciting and fatiguing day and she was in the land of dreams in a few minutes , not even having visions of keys , spring-houses or Thanksgiving dinners , but of the mother cat and her three kittens who were climbing chestnut trees and throwing down chestnuts to her", "context_len": 53}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The Afrikander , realizing that he was fatiguing himself with futile efforts , adopted semi-defensive tactics , in the hope that the Maori would close", "context_len": 25}}}, "farmyard": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "She was not used to farmyard sounds and could not tell if it were a lusty rooster , an insistent guinea-fowl or a gobbling turkey whose voice first reached her", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 73, "context": "What a pleasant ride that was : out of the field where the bars had been let down ; past other fields ready , or nearly ready , for the harvesting ; pale green oats , and golden wheat , the white , sweet-scented buckwheat , and the tall Indian corn ; then through the orchard where a flock of sheep were feeding , past the locust grove , and then into the farmyard ; stopping at last between the open doors of the great barn !", "context_len": 87}}}, "gobbling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "She was not used to farmyard sounds and could not tell if it were a lusty rooster , an insistent guinea-fowl or a gobbling turkey whose voice first reached her", "context_len": 30}}}, "overshadowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "She lay still for a few minutes , with an uncertain feeling of something not very pleasant overshadowing her , then she remembered the key", "context_len": 25}}}, "meow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then came a plaintive meow and she discovered one of the three kittens out on an adventuring tour", "context_len": 18}}}, "adventuring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then came a plaintive meow and she discovered one of the three kittens out on an adventuring tour", "context_len": 18}}}, "ambled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "They stumbled along clumsily , following one another over the rail , and ambled on to another set of bars where they stood till Ira should let them through", "context_len": 29}}}, "fleeing": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 62, "context": "Two sets of mail bags were approaching each other from points two thousand miles apart , and there were times when this approach was at the astounding speed of forty and even fifty miles an hour ! The average daily rate was two hundred and fifty miles a day , but where everything was favorable , or when an express rider was fleeing from the vengeful red men , his pony struck a gait of twenty-five miles and maintained it , when an untrained horse would have dropped in his tracks", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Only a short distance separated the fleeing car from the gate , but between the gate and the car was one of the guards", "context_len": 24}}}, "banging": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "At first , Edna did not realize that they were not making for the spot where she stood and she took to her heels , fleeing frantically back to the garden , banging the gate behind her and standing still waiting till the cows were through and the bars up again", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Such stormy -- oh , such stormy weather as we have had , night after night ! There have been such thunder and lightning , and rushing blast , and banging of doors and windows , as if in this great echoing house there were pistol practice", "context_len": 47}}}, "doggone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` What 's took you up so airly ? Why , I jest got through milkin ' , and , doggone it , it ai n't skeerce light yit", "context_len": 29}}}, "reassuringly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "Be that as it may , a very sweet-faced lady met them at the door as they entered , and saying reassuringly , Come this way , children , led them through a corridor resplendent with statues and portraits , and thence by a wide folding-door into a large room , with windows looking out over the Long Walk and away to the grand old Windsor Forest", "context_len": 67}}}, "betwixt": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "Ever and anon I looked back , and found that they were increasing their pace into almost a trot ; so , directly I had put the brow of the hill betwixt them and me , I ran steadily but not too swiftly down the road", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 59, "context": "Despite the fact that he was on the losing side , my father was not backward in declaring his attachment to His Gracious Majesty King Charles II ; and although our neighbours , even the Roundheads , were favourably disposed to him , making allowance for his fiery temper , yet with strangers who passed along the great highway betwixt London Town and Portsmouth , honest Owen \u2019 s outspoken declarations oft led to wordy strife , and on occasions ended in blows", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 47, "context": "Thenceforward , whenassoever the price of one platter was spent , he would carry another to the old goldsmith , and on this wise he and his mother increased in substance ; but they ceased not to live at their sufficiency , [ 313 ] midwise [ betwixt rich and poor ] , [ 314 ] without excessive spending [ 315 ] or squandering", "context_len": 64}}}, "pattered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "He swung his lantern so its rays would shed a broad light along the way , and Edna pattered along just behind him , trying very hard to keep up with his long strides", "context_len": 34}}}, "hinges": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 34, "context": "If there were more girls like you in the world I should n't be surprised if there were a lot of good men too ; and the world could be oiled on all its hinges , so to speak , so that it would n't creak and jump and fret one at every turn as it seems to have an unpleasant habit of doing at the present moment", "context_len": 68}}}, "spry": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "My pockets were comfortably lined , and I had not a debt in the world , and I was feeling pretty spry , you may be sure , and thinking , 'Albert Fanning , the time has come for you to take a mate ; the time has come for you and your sweetheart to meet , and to have a right good time , and a happy life afterwards", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` You wo n't catch a Chinaman spraining his ankle , '' said Bradley ; `` they 're too spry for that", "context_len": 22}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Bradford ; `` folks pretty spry ? ''", "context_len": 8}}}, "traipsing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Now , you stay right here and do n't go running about in the cold ; you 'll be down sick traipsing about in the wet grass , and then where will your Thanksgiving be ? ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "buttermilk": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "He watched him as a cat would watch a mouse , and ate everything he ate and drank whatever he drank -- -- No one had coffee or tea , they had milk , cocoa , buttermilk , and cold water , which latter the kiddies drank as if it had been something that would make them super-children", "context_len": 58}}}, "advising": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Since receiving the telegram advising him to be ready to leave for Florida on short notice , Phil and his sister had had word that their mother had rallied somewhat , but that no permanent hope was held out for her recovery", "context_len": 42}}}, "sausages": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "He stopped to look at a few loaves of bread and a string of sausages that were displayed in the window of a cheap store", "context_len": 25}}}, "griddle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then a frying pan was put on the stove , for they had brought along no regular griddle , it was greased , and Frank , who insisted on doing the rest , was allowed to pour out the batter , and do the turning", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Oh punk ! '' groaned Bart , as he pushed his plate away from him , `` and I was counting on griddle cakes ! ''", "context_len": 27}}}, "buckwheat": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "What a pleasant ride that was : out of the field where the bars had been let down ; past other fields ready , or nearly ready , for the harvesting ; pale green oats , and golden wheat , the white , sweet-scented buckwheat , and the tall Indian corn ; then through the orchard where a flock of sheep were feeding , past the locust grove , and then into the farmyard ; stopping at last between the open doors of the great barn !", "context_len": 87}}}, "delectable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Like a flock of sheep the Huns , with hands still upraised , shuffled on the first of their long trek to captivity -- to some delectable spot in England , where , far from the sound of the guns , there is food in plenty for Hun prisoners of war , German U-boats notwithstanding", "context_len": 55}}}, "scatted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Edna would fain have spent the morning in the kitchen from which issued delectable odors , but Amanda had declared she wanted all the room there was , that she had scatted out the cats and dogs and she would have to scat out children , too , if they came bothering around", "context_len": 53}}}, "junction": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` They had spread out into two branch settlements : Hitchit\u00fadshi or Little Hitchiti , on both sides of Flint river , below the junction of Kitchofuni creek , which passes through a county named after it ; and Tutal\u00f3si on Tutalosi creek , a branch of Kitchofuni creek , twenty miles west of Hitchit\u00fadshi ( Hawkins , p", "context_len": 59}}}, "tousled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "How are you Pinky Blooms ? '' He darted at Edna , tousled her hair , picked her up and slung her over his shoulder as if she were a bag of meal , and dropped her on the top step of the porch , she laughing and protesting the while", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` You said you had n't one , '' remarked Champ and he withdrew his tousled head", "context_len": 17}}}, "valise": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Their trunks were left , but each had taken a valise , filled with clothing , as may be presumed , for the bureau drawers were empty", "context_len": 27}}}, "slinking": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "'Now then ! ' cried the man , with a horrible oath , 'none of your slinking off ! You give me what you have there , or I 'll break every bone in your body , and worse ! Here ! Hand it over , quick ! '", "context_len": 49}}}, "pretense": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "The cause of the warring , or the pretense for it , against `` some other Indians from the west '' is curiously similar to the rivalry in athletic sports , which took place between the western Iroquois and their subdivisions , and finally led to the destruction of the Erie or K\u00e1'hkwa Indians ( Cusick , Johnson )", "context_len": 59}}}, "tramping": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 45, "context": "First of all , he went into a cotton factory , and later to a velveteen factory ; then , having a taste for carpentering , he took to it as a trade , though he was at best but a rough unskilled workman , tramping about the country , and doing odd jobs wherever he could get them", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then there arose a sudden tramping of bare feet upon the dry earth , and from somewhere in the rear of the palace there swung into view a hundred picked warriors , armed like those who had mounted guard at the palisade gate , who formed up behind and on each side of the chairs with very commendable military precision", "context_len": 60}}}, "cozily": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Thereupon Fenn gave over watching the turtles , and , a little later , seated cozily in the tent , the chums partook of a supper of canned baked beans , with hard-tack or pilot biscuit , and coffee", "context_len": 39}}}, "pout": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` And , '' said Maggie , with just the least little pout , `` I do n't believe she is a dame pilgrim , and I do n't believe she is in the narrow path , not a bit ! ''", "context_len": 42}}}, "houseful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "What a houseful it was , to be sure , and what long , long tables in the dining-room", "context_len": 19}}}, "compute": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It would be quite a task if one were to try to compute the number of buckwheat cakes consumed at the long tables the next morning , and there might have been more but that Charlie stopped Frank in the act of helping himself to a further supply by saying : `` Look here , son , if you keep on eating cakes you wo n't give your Thanksgiving dinner any show at all", "context_len": 74}}}, "unfrequented": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "The grand master left me to choose my course ; but I think there is more danger by the coast -- where pirates may be hiding in unfrequented bays , in readiness to pounce upon a passing craft -- than in the open sea , where we should have at least the advantage that we could not be taken by surprise , and might make a race of it", "context_len": 69}}}, "braved": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 54, "context": "They do not know that they are heroes -- that 's the best of it ! It is a fact to be thankful for that everywhere throughout the land , beneath the rough jackets of our artisans and labourers , beat hearts as true and fearless as those which have stormed the fort or braved the dangers of the battlefield", "context_len": 60}}}, "flakiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Grandma served the flakiest of noble chicken-pies at her side of the table , while Aunt Alice served an oyster-pie of the same proportions and quite as delicious", "context_len": 28}}}, "wishbones": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "To Cousin Becky 's lot fell one of the wishbones , and to Edna 's joy she had the other", "context_len": 20}}}, "wishbone": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "You make a head , you know , and then the wishbone has two legs and you dress it up so it is a pen-wiper", "context_len": 25}}}, "digest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` This was not a very clear description , but Lulie was satisfied , especially as at that moment Ben came to them and said that everyone was going to play games , in order that their dinners might properly digest", "context_len": 41}}}, "forfeits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` We are going to begin with something easy , like forfeits , and work up to the real snappy ones after", "context_len": 22}}}, "snappy": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 81, "context": "Nor was Nancy the only one who indulged in a little innocent vanity , for Joe , too , had been so fastidious in his choice of a red-and-blue spotted necktie and a walking-stick with an ivory handle that the children began to suspect the blacksmith 's rosy-cheeked daughter must be at the bottom of it ; and even David had taken a long-tailed coat and a beaver hat out of the retirement of some mysterious bandboxes and had been quite snappy and particular with Mrs", "context_len": 86}}}, "afield": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "The second night after crossing the Missouri , when the wagons had been placed in a circle , the animals allowed to browse on the luxuriant grass , so well guarded that they could not wander afield , he came back and sat down among the group that were eating from the food spread on a blanket", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "What did he care where he was going , and entering the house with a latch-key , he tossed his hat on to a hook and started upstairs , his thoughts already far afield from all that concerned his younger brother", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Remember , Elizabeth , that Woodford covers a fairly wide area ; you mustn \u2019 t roam too far afield alone", "context_len": 21}}}, "persimmons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` The girls did not roam far from the house but the boys wandered much further afield , bringing caps and pockets full of nuts , and clothes full of burs and stick-tights , even Ben brought back a hoard of persimmons touched by the frost and as sweet as honey", "context_len": 51}}}, "divvy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Come here , Edna , '' he said , `` let 's divvy up", "context_len": 15}}}, "seductive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` No , that coming supper party sounds too seductive ; I 'll wait so that I can do it justice", "context_len": 21}}}, "thereafter": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 97, "context": "Zein-ud-din ( Adornment of the Faith ) [ he ] of the Images , Zein ( adornment ) not being a name used by the Arabic-speaking races , unless with some such addition as ud-Din ( `` of the Faith '' ) , and the affix ul Asnam ( `` [ He ] of the Images '' ) being a sobriquet arising from the circumstances of the hero 's after-life , unless its addition , as recommended by the astrologers , is meant as an indication of the latter 's fore-knowledge of what was to befall him thereafter", "context_len": 98}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Hartley wisely determined to keep his own counsel ; and for many minutes thereafter the heavy breathing of the men asleep in the next room and the crackling of the wood upon the andirons were the only sounds that broke the silence", "context_len": 42}}}, "dissent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "This was all so satisfactory that there was not a murmur of dissent , and grandma began : `` It was when I was about ten years old that I went one day with my father to the nearest village", "context_len": 40}}}, "tilt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "My father stopped the horses and ran back to pick up the parcel , but before he could get to the buggy , the horses took fright at a piece of paper blowing along the road in front of them and off they started , full tilt , down the road", "context_len": 51}}}, "dale": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "I managed to hold on to the reins but my young hands were not strong enough to control the wild creatures , and I thought every minute would be my last , for up hill and down dale we went at such a pace I had never known", "context_len": 48}}}, "jounce": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Over a stump would jounce the buggy , and I would nearly pitch out", "context_len": 14}}}, "musingly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Yet who knows what even she might do ? '' thought the mother , as having seen each little birdling safe in its nest , she went slowly and musingly down stairs to join the rest of the family , thinking as she went of Bessie 's simple prayer , `` who knows what even she might do ? for --", "context_len": 62}}}, "tarlatan": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I wore white tarlatan trimmed with forget-me-nots , '' said Aunt Emmeline , `` and I danced my first dance with Steve Hardesty", "context_len": 24}}}, "rosebuds": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Sweet bud of the morning , what poet can speak The glories that beam in thy eye ? The rosebuds that bloom on thy fat little cheek , -- And thy round head so stuffed full of Latin and Greek , Arithmetic and Geology", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The gardener had not opened the greenhouse ; but I found much more than I should have expected in the beginning of December , -- even rosebuds", "context_len": 27}}}, "inoffensive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 101, "context": "Peggy and Bobby loathed the little Davenports , who were mild , pale , neat-looking little girls , so alike that each one seemed merely a copy of the next , a size smaller , and who always wore gloves , even in the garden , and never dreamed of tearing their pinafores , or using slang , and went on prim little walks with their governess , instead of scrambling over the fields ; and , I grieve to say , that on the few occasions when they met , they had taken such a positive pleasure in leading their inoffensive companions into places which resulted in soiled dresses and dirty boots , that Mrs", "context_len": 116}}}, "creeps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` My head aches and there are cold creeps running up and down my back , '' Edna told her", "context_len": 20}}}, "snuggling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I do n't think I want any breakfast , '' said Edna , snuggling down with a grateful feeling for the warmth and quiet", "context_len": 25}}}, "dozed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "All night he kept at his post , but finding the wind perfectly steady he lashed the tiller so as to keep the boat 's head in the direction in which he was steering , and dozed for some hours , waking up occasionally to assure himself that she was keeping her course", "context_len": 53}}}, "drowsily": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "Captain Bill had gone off up the wharf to a canal store , Blixey was busy in the cabin , and the horses were drowsily munching oats from baskets tied under their noses", "context_len": 33}}}, "doze": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "My weary and aching frame soothed and refreshed by the pelting rain , I must have fallen into a kind of doze , for I was suddenly startled into full consciousness by the feeling that some one was meddling with my bonds , which , the next moment , severed by a sharp knife , fell from my limbs", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "But , just as they were about to doze off , there came an exclamation of dismay from Bart", "context_len": 19}}}, "decree": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 4, "context": "Now , by the decree of destiny , Fatimeh came down on the morrow to the city and the enchanter , going forth the Khan in the morning , saw the folk crowding together ; so he went up , to see what was toward , and found Fatimeh standing , whilst every one who had a pain or an ache came to her , seeking her blessing and soliciting her prayers , and whenas she stroked him , he was made whole of his ailment", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "The gate thou hast defended with thy blood , To-morrow casts thee forth , led out to die ; And the proud towers coldly will look down Upon the closing scene ; for hearts more hard And more impregnable decree thy doom", "context_len": 42}}}, "gravel": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Take care , Bessie , take care ! '' called her brother , the Colonel ; `` that is loose gravel there ; if it slips with you , you are lost ; '' and , `` Come back , Bessie , come back ! '' called her husband from above , seeing the danger more plainly than any of the others", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Stanton hold on , could that cruel gravel bear them both , till that should be ? God , in whom alone they trusted , only knew", "context_len": 27}}}, "stair": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 44, "context": "And when she stepped over each one , it went right back to its place in the sky ; but it left a long light behind it , like the shooting star we saw the other night ; and at the top of the stair of stars was a soft , white cloud ; and when Dolly came to it , a hand came out of the cloud , and took her in , and we knew she was quite safe , and would never come back again", "context_len": 88}}}, "tablets": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "In the waiting room at the head quarters of the London Fire Brigade , in Southwark Street , London , is an oak board on which are fixed a number of brass tablets , bearing the names of men who are entitled to a place on this `` Roll of Honour ''", "context_len": 52}}}, "phial": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` She went to the bureau and found the little phial she was in search of", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "The result was a fumble , the phial slipped from Sid 's grasp , Tom made a grab for it , but missed , and Phil , with only one good hand , could do nothing", "context_len": 36}}}, "pellets": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "After shaking out a few pellets in her hand , she brought them to Edna with a glass of water and the child took the dose obediently , for she knew these small tablets of old", "context_len": 36}}}, "crocheting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Her mother was sitting by the window crocheting where the sun was streaming in", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Just a week to-day , Harold , said Aunt Lou , looking up from a great mass of crocheting , that would soon be a full-grown afghan ; I hope it has n't seemed more like a month to you , dear", "context_len": 42}}}, "deposit": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "Conway turned down the covers of her own bed , slipped Edna into her flannel wrapper , threw a shawl around her and carried her across the room to deposit her in the big bed", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "Almost before he knew it he was within sight of the boiling spring , and he checked his pace , hoping to come upon the buck licking the salty deposit from the rocks in the little stream that flowed from the place where the mud volcano was", "context_len": 47}}}, "corkscrew": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "Most of it had been carried away to build the large barns and stables , but the foundations could still be plainly traced , with here and there part of a wall thickly covered with ivy , the ruins of a shattered column , a delicate little piece of window tracery , or a few steps of corkscrew staircase", "context_len": 59}}}, "uncovering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "The crowd was dispersing , Darry was counting up the heap of bank notes and coin in the cash box , Bob was gloating and wild with delight as uncovering his purchase he brought to light a new bicycle", "context_len": 39}}}, "tureen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "It was a charming sight to see this happy , clean , and well-bred family sitting at this long table with no walls between them and the lovely lake that was shining and beautiful as the sides of the big silver soup tureen from which Mrs", "context_len": 46}}}, "solicitously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "But in spite of her belief that she could eat every bit of what was before her , Edna could do no more than manage the broth and one piece of toast , Reliance watching her solicitously while she ate", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Stirling solicitously , and said his mother was then on her way to see Mrs", "context_len": 15}}}, "peckish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` You 're not very peckish , are you ? '' she said", "context_len": 13}}}, "scalawag": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` You old scalawag , '' cried the young man , `` what were you up to ? If you are yearning to hit somebody , take a fellow your own size", "context_len": 32}}}, "marshmallow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Fox , and I wanted to tell you that the lady by the willow has had another accident ; she dropped her last chocolate marshmallow and the dog stepped on it", "context_len": 31}}}, "eyelash": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Has n't she found her eyelash yet ? ''", "context_len": 10}}}, "raining": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` I 've seen it three times before , '' remarked another -- the inevitable grouser of the company ; `` and , every time it 's been raining cats and dogs", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "But he says , if it keeps on raining , he thinks it would be better to send it , because it is not kind to keep them waiting when they feel so badly about Willie , and this will make them so glad", "context_len": 44}}}, "annoys": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "How did you stand all the racket this morning ? If a noisy noise annoys an oyster , how much of a noisy noise does it take to annoy Pinky Blooms ? That sounds like a problem in mental arithmetic , but it is n't", "context_len": 45}}}, "ducky": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Sorry I ca n't , ducky dear , for I am off by the morning train to a football game which I ca n't miss", "context_len": 26}}}, "contritely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I know it was a hasty thing to do , '' said Bart , contritely , `` but I could n't help it", "context_len": 24}}}, "hymns": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 48, "context": "He studied the language of the tribes amongst whom he was ministering ; and soon the people were able to sing in their own tongue , `` There is a fountain filled with blood , '' `` Jesus shall reign where'er the sun , '' and other beautiful hymns which delight the hearts of those in our own land", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "As he was led forth to execution he sang hymns nearly all the way", "context_len": 14}}}, "melodeon": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "She was the older daughter , a strong , healthy girl of seventeen , who never helped her mother about the work , who dressed in such cheap finery as she could obtain by hook or crook , who took music lessons on a rented melodeon paid for out of her mother \u2019 s hard earnings , who felt herself to be a lady unfortunately born out of her sphere , and who was unquestionably ashamed of her surviving parent and her brother and sister", "context_len": 85}}}, "wheezed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Edna enjoyed this performance very much , but after a while grandma was tired of an instrument that skipped notes and wheezed like an old horse , so they went back to the big chair by the open fire", "context_len": 39}}}, "crane": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Uncle Isaac , said he , mother hasn \u2019 t got any crane ; all the way she hangs her pot over the fire is by a birch withe , with a chain at the end ; and sometimes it burns off above the chain : the other day it broke , and liked to have scalded the baby to death", "context_len": 61}}}, "lullaby": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "She looked lovingly at the cows , lazily chewing their cud in the sunshine ; she felt sorry for her step-mother , as she strove to woo slumber to Polly 's wakeful eyes with the same lullaby which had done duty for the whole six ; she even found it in her heart to kiss Lemuel , who , with his ready talent for the unusual , was busily cramming mud paste into the seams of the little trunk which held her worldly all", "context_len": 84}}}, "postmaster": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "About the first of June they were released , came back to Millville , found their old shack burned down , and since then , the postmaster understood , had camped out in the woods , giving the town a wide berth -- in fact , only occasionally appearing , to buy a little flour , sugar or coffee , or , mostly , tobacco", "context_len": 65}}}, "reassured": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 11, "context": "Where are de children , anyway ? asked Albert , wonderfully reassured by Donald 's courteous reception ; wo n't you fin ' dem for me , please , Donald , and tell dem I wo n't be a badder , nor ask queshuns , and I 'll jus ' eat my own lunch and --", "context_len": 56}}}, "shorn": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Off they started down the wide street bordered by maples , now shorn of their leaves , but furnishing a carpet of yellow underfoot , past the church , the store , the schoolhouse and on to the old brown house sitting back behind an orchard of gnarled , crooked apple trees", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Cropping , and afterwards washing the animal with grease and paraffin , were the only remedies , and but temporary ; for , shorn of its warm fur , the monkey caught pneumonia and succumbed", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "One bite of the alligator \u2019 s jaws and a leg would be severed as though shorn by shears", "context_len": 19}}}, "underfoot": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Off they started down the wide street bordered by maples , now shorn of their leaves , but furnishing a carpet of yellow underfoot , past the church , the store , the schoolhouse and on to the old brown house sitting back behind an orchard of gnarled , crooked apple trees", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Oh , the boundless delight of those rolling miles of heather , which looked like a crimson sea spreading onward towards the horizon , and the delicious smell of the sweet-gale as they trod it underfoot ! Great flocks of plovers flew before them , screaming their 'pee-wit , pee-wit ! ' and here and there they roused a snipe or a woodcock , while wild little ponies scampered off like the wind , indignant at having their solitude disturbed", "context_len": 80}}}, "schoolhouse": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 60, "context": "But as he looked at the individual whom he had mistaken for a deer he felt a second tremor of excitement , for , as he had a glimpse of his face Bart was made aware that the man was none other than the one about whom there was such a mystery -- the man who had sneaked into the schoolhouse the night the diamond bracelet was stolen -- the midnight visitor at the camp , perhaps", "context_len": 77}}}, "gnarled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "The slope was steadily upward for a fourth of a mile , when it reached a nearly level plateau , and wound in and out among rocks , stunted pines , gnarled cedars , and ravines , interspersed with valleys and comparatively smooth stretches , with now and then a mountain torrent across which the travelers made their way with difficulty", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "He strolled about the open space , pausing now and then to survey through his glasses the snowy peaks which towered far into the sky to the westward , or at the lower hills to the north , where the gnarled pine , the dwarfed cedar , the rushing torrents and the gorges made up one of the many wild regions which abound over thousands of square miles of area", "context_len": 70}}}, "rotting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Up the rotting pillars of the porch a woodbine still clambered , and around the door , lilac bushes kept their green", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": ") A legend , which evidently originated from the name already existing , relates that wayfarers passing there had left a large provision-basket ( s\u00e1ka ) at this locality , which was upset and left rotting , so that finally it became flattened out : from pat\u00e4\u00eddsh\u00e4s I spread out something ; pat\u00e1yi , partic", "context_len": 55}}}, "woodbine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Up the rotting pillars of the porch a woodbine still clambered , and around the door , lilac bushes kept their green", "context_len": 22}}}, "arresting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "At length , however , when the people of the city saw it gradually increasing in size and advancing toward them , they concluded that they must engage in earnest in the work of arresting its progress", "context_len": 37}}}, "lagged": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "The beautiful Holstein turned in her calm way and with her friends lagged up to the top of the hill and over to a low-lying field that would be green and fresh in spite of the slight drought they had been having", "context_len": 42}}}, "spooky": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Is n't it lovely and spooky ? '' said Esther Ann , tingling with excitement", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Pony-Boy , '' he said , laying a timid finger on the tip of one of my ears , `` I 'm not afraid exactly , but I do n't like spooky woods and queer silent waiting people", "context_len": 39}}}, "flopped": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "Marvin ; de way I knowed whar he was , was dat I knowed it ; I felt he had flopped ober to de left , so I knowed jes \u2019 de p \u2019 int to aim at ; dat \u2019 s de skience ob de hull bus \u2019 ness", "context_len": 50}}}, "combs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "The others gathered around her to see boxes in which were the treasures of a forgotten owner , -- strings of beads , half-worn white kid gloves , a fan with ivory sticks , combs , and ornaments of various kinds", "context_len": 41}}}, "conclusively": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "She was coming along very fast , however ; and presently , when she took a rather broad sheer , I caught a momentary glimpse of two royals and just the head of a third -- the mizzen -- proving conclusively that she was full-rigged -- as was the Virginia", "context_len": 50}}}, "buckle": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Back up , Pony , '' she said , and she showed Dallas how to lift the shafts and buckle straps until I and my smart tan harness were firmly attached to the cart", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 38, "context": "Ten minutes should be ample time for all there is to be done , and even if the alarm is given at once , we shall be away long before the knights can be aroused from sleep , buckle on their armour , and get down to the port", "context_len": 49}}}, "enthusiastically": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 45, "context": "Here we present three crackerjack stories of baseball , football , and basketball , written in the vernacular of the boy of to-day , full of action , suspense and thrills , in language every boy will understand , and which we know will be enthusiastically endorsed by all boys", "context_len": 50}}}, "rummaging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Here and there Lilian ran , hunting in all Peggy 's favourite haunts -- now peeping into a hollow yew-tree , now peering at the top of a ladder , now rummaging in the tool-shed , then back through the sand-quarry into the stack-yard , where there was a very good chance that the young lady might be hidden away in some snug little hole among the hay ; but though Lilian got a tolerable amount of hay-seed into her hair , her efforts were fruitless , and she was just turning away , hot and out of breath , to give up the useless search , when the sound of a low , chuckling laugh attracted her to the barn", "context_len": 121}}}, "respective": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 57, "context": "Most of the knights went ashore at once , but Gervaise , under the excuse that he wished to see that everything was in order before landing , remained on board until it was time to go to the banquet , being sure that by that time the knights would have fully told the story at their respective auberges , and that there would be no more questions to answer", "context_len": 70}}}, "remarking": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 47, "context": "Thank you , very much , as though Ted had done her the greatest personal favor ; and then , seeming to feel that their talk had come to a natural end , she asked quite casually , Will you have the custard now ? and Ted remarking quite as casually , Yes , thank you , I will , she lifted the tray carefully into his lap", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Topaze the grog tub was used as a table for signing the pledge book , one sailor remarking ( to the tub ) : `` Sixty odd nails in your coffin to-day , old fellow ! If they all hold firm I would not give much for your life", "context_len": 49}}}, "quarreled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "He was distressed beyond words at the dreadful fate that had overtaken the youth with whom he quarreled and whom he was anxious to meet that the wrangle might be fought to a finish", "context_len": 34}}}, "heirs": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "They died within a few weeks of one another and there were no nearer heirs than distant cousins who have never troubled themselves to look after the place", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "Lands to mercy ! Now ain \u2019 t that jest amazin \u2019 ! Here I \u2019 ve been entertainin \u2019 under my roof a couple of heirs to wealth ! Three thousand dollars ! Fifteen hundred dollars apiece ! Why , it puts y \u2019 u both beyond the touch of the tooth of avarice", "context_len": 55}}}, "tramps": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Our captain had procured the animal for a friend of his , who lived somewhere in the country , and wanted such a dog to keep off tramps and other trespassers", "context_len": 31}}}, "vagrant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Even if it is not valued by the present owners , it should not be left for tramps or any chance vagrant to make use of", "context_len": 26}}}, "sobered": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "The children jumped up in fits of laughter , but they sobered down considerably at the sight of the shocked faces of Aunt Helen and Mrs", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Bart , half decided that he was demented , watched him disappear in the direction of a cheap eating house just beyond the tracks , and started homewards more or less sobered and thoughtful over the peculiar incident", "context_len": 38}}}, "dishonest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "He is probably in confidential relations with you , and he might play some dishonest trick on you", "context_len": 18}}}, "burglary": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` My father says you could n't call it by any other name , and that to break into a house is burglary", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "I do not know his name , but I have seen him several times , '' and Bart thought it best to reveal to his superior all he knew about the roustabout who had warned him of the burglary , who had assisted him in rescuing his father from the burning express shed , and who had vanished suddenly as people began to crowd to the scene of the blaze", "context_len": 70}}}, "shivery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "But he kept up a shivery thought of wondering if the ball he had thrown at Marilla had really hurt her", "context_len": 21}}}, "adventurous": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 46, "context": "Naturally , I was very thankful that the awful day had passed over , and that its end found me still in the land of the living ; for `` while there is life there is hope , '' and in the course of my somewhat adventurous career I have seen so many extraordinary escapes from apparently inevitable disaster that the one piece of advice above all others which I would give to everybody is `` Never despair ! '' I can recall more than one occasion when , if I had abandoned hope and the effort which goes with it , I should not now have been alive to pen these words", "context_len": 113}}}, "chanted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "The while the birds from each tree and bush chanted their Maker 's praise , and the sweet fresh green of springtide enlivened the scene , as if to welcome us pilgrims to our home", "context_len": 35}}}, "clue": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 42, "context": "The slaves had not , from their benches , been able to see what had passed on board the dismantled vessel , but from the order and the change of course , they had no doubt that the knights had obtained some clue to the direction taken by the corsairs who had captured and sacked the ship", "context_len": 57}}}, "laundry": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "My impression is , that he started a laundry in San Francisco , made enough money for a Chinaman to retire upon , and went back to his native land to live in competence , the happy husband of a high-born Chinese maiden with incredibly small feet", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Two of us can go around by the mill , two of us can go to the butcher 's and Alcinda can go to the laundry place", "context_len": 28}}}, "sluice": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The mill pond and dam and sluice and all those are higher up", "context_len": 13}}}, "pooh": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Oh , pooh , I do n't get dizzy , '' returned Reliance", "context_len": 14}}}, "reaches": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 41, "context": "I could not tell anything about the sense of hearing ; the perfect stillness of the regions six miles from the earth -- and at that time we were between six and seven miles high -- is such that no sound reaches the ear", "context_len": 44}}}, "shove": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "Rover followed them round , rooting under the stumps for worms , and once in a while would shove his nose on a hot coal , which would make him run away squealing", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "This was very near the edge of the rock , where a slight push might throw him over , and it came into the bad man 's heart to climb up softly behind him , and , with a sudden shove , to send him down upon the rocks below", "context_len": 50}}}, "ordering": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 117, "context": "the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change of air , ordering him off immediately to the high meadows of the Alps", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "Harris , only it seems to me that people are often in too much of a hurry to make use of the word , for you ca n't he certain until you are able to look hack upon a thing whether it was surely of God 's ordering or man 's short-sighted scheming", "context_len": 53}}}, "sopping": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` He is so sopping wet I am afraid he will take cold , '' said Reliance", "context_len": 17}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Stone recovered to find some one sopping his face with a cool , dripping sponge", "context_len": 15}}}, "footbridge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "To Edna 's satisfaction a small footbridge was discovered a short distance below and on this they crossed , reaching the miller 's house just after", "context_len": 26}}}, "speculatively": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "For a moment he stood still , staring at Matt speculatively , as though trying to guess who he was and where he had come from ; then , suddenly , he whirled and broke from Matt \u2019 s grasp , running farther back into the bushes", "context_len": 47}}}, "advertise": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I meant to take him home and advertise for his owner , but when I came to look for him , the dog was gone , though Jeb was there", "context_len": 30}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "During the greater part of that Sunday the brothers remained in the little room , having many things to talk about and being unwilling to advertise for the general public the fact that Jerry was in Oakdale", "context_len": 37}}}, "approving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Reliance gave an account of the rescue and received approving nods", "context_len": 11}}}, "nods": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Reliance gave an account of the rescue and received approving nods", "context_len": 11}}}, "unhitch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I was just going to unhitch , but there is my buggy all ready , and I guess the quickest way to get you back to the village is to take you there behind Dolly", "context_len": 35}}}, "mourn": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "So much for Alaeddin and the Lady Bedrulbudour ; and as for the Sultan , after he had released Alaeddin , he ceased not to mourn for the loss of his daughter and to sit and weep for her , like a woman , at every time and tide ; for that she was his only one and he had none other than her", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Does the eagle mourn over the death of the dove , or heed what pangs the kid may suffer which writhes beneath its talons ? If you are of the race of warrior kings , act like one", "context_len": 39}}}, "tardiness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` After which speech , the miller drove off , and the rescuers darted across the street to their home , where the tardiness of their appearance was entirely forgiven after they had told their story", "context_len": 36}}}, "dabbling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "Peggy tied her boots together by the laces , and putting her stockings inside , slung them over her back in true fisher-boy fashion , while she sat dabbling her feet in a waterfall , and watching Bobby 's frantic efforts to catch a dragon-fly", "context_len": 45}}}, "mustard": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "The tiny grain of mustard seed dropped by that little hand had taken root on a hard and stony ground , it might be ; but I could only pray that the dews of heaven might fall upon it , and cause it to grow and bring forth fruit", "context_len": 49}}}, "nauseous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` She did indeed , and when she was a little girl it was even worse , for instead of lemonade to drink , she was made to take a very bitter dose of herb tea , or a dreadful mess called composition which had every sort of nauseous thing in it you can think of", "context_len": 56}}}, "turtles": {"freq": 81, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 80, "word_index": 37, "context": "His proposal was that the three of them should pay a surreptitious visit to the school that Friday night , and , from the room of Professor Long , the science teacher , take a number of turtles , snakes and small alligators which the instructor kept for the use of his class in biology", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "A diamond thief ! Oh , dear ! Mud turtles ! '' and again the lineman seemed overcome", "context_len": 18}}}, "cookies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Maybe you 'd like a little lunch too ; and here 's some biscuits , and a couple of cookies your mother told me to bring lest you should be hungry", "context_len": 32}}}, "amanda": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Amanda showed her how to put first a big fat raisin in the center of the cake , then a current for the turtle 's head , four cloves were then stuck in , part way under the raisin , thus making the feet , and for the tail , another clove with the sharp end out", "context_len": 57}}}, "cloves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Amanda showed her how to put first a big fat raisin in the center of the cake , then a current for the turtle 's head , four cloves were then stuck in , part way under the raisin , thus making the feet , and for the tail , another clove with the sharp end out", "context_len": 57}}}, "misbehavior": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The people about here know she comes of good stock and that the poverty of the family was due more to misfortune than misbehavior", "context_len": 24}}}, "challis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Edna had finally decided not to wear her best white frock , but had on a pretty blue challis , quite suited to the occasion , her mother told her", "context_len": 30}}}, "comers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` the thirsty one ( es szadi ) and the goer-forth by day or in the morning '' ( el ghadi ) ; but this is most probably a mistranscription for the common phrase es sari ( the goer by night ) wa ' l ghadi , often used in the sense of `` comers and goers '' simply", "context_len": 59}}}, "cologne": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then they let down a rope ladder and a bottle of smelling salts and some eau de cologne to restore the Impolite Captain , and by the time he was restored and assisted up the rope ladder all the Polite Pirates were standing lined up on deck ready to receive him and his crew with low sweeping bows", "context_len": 58}}}, "savers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 76, "context": "Well , my little girl thinks her small dog would like to show his appreciation of the act which saved his life the other day , and so I have prepared two medals for the heroines of that occasion ; they are not gold medals ; in fact they are not real medals and of no special value except that they represent her , and our , gratitude to the little girls who were the life savers", "context_len": 77}}}, "edging": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "In a few minutes more we had lifted the heads of her courses above the horizon , still edging away and keeping her about four points on our port bow ; and presently , as we watched her , we saw the Stars and Stripes go soaring up to her gaff-end", "context_len": 51}}}, "unlaced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Her mother left the room , and while Edna unlaced her shoes , she listened for her return", "context_len": 18}}}, "accorded": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Quoth the Sultan , `` I marvel at thee how thou still deemest evil of Alaeddin ; but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed", "context_len": 128}}}, "ungraciously": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "The officer thanked us ungraciously , though why he took that attitude I was unable to discover , and we were on the point of joining our comrades when the lady remarked : `` You 'll probably know me again when you see me , Mr", "context_len": 46}}}, "girlies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I was thinking , girlies , that as long as there are so many elderly and old people in the village , some of whom are poor and some who are partial invalids , that it would be a very sweet thing if you little girls could form yourselves into a club which would help to make their lives a little less sad", "context_len": 63}}}, "invalids": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 43, "context": "There had been quite a spell of sickness in Gorswen at the end of the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change", "context_len": 128}}}, "spider": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 66, "context": "Rosamund got up softly and locked the door , not a minute too soon , for she had scarcely done so before the handle was turned and the voice of Irene was heard outside crying through the keyhole , `` What changeling is in this room ? Which of you housemaids has dared to lock herself in ? Come out ! I 've got a big spider ready , and '' -- --", "context_len": 73}}}, "doorsill": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Conway paid no heed to the comment , `` you could leave a big apple on the doorsill for him , and so on , till in time I will venture to say he will learn that you wish him well and are trying to be friends", "context_len": 47}}}, "gallivanting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Where did you Fortress men get the idea that you could desert Sweet Rosy O \u2019 Grady and go gallivanting off with a silly little B-26 ? No wonder you-all had to come home in a Jap crate ! What happened , anyway ?", "context_len": 44}}}, "skylarking": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "He appeared to be a keen young officer , knowing the ins and outs of everything , yet withal he was of a roguish disposition , and given to skylarking", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` No skylarking , and do n't go to eating a lot of trash", "context_len": 14}}}, "pacified": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 59, "context": "Her mother sprang up and kissed her rapturously , crying -- 'Is n't she the dearest and sweetest thing and the smartest ! Think of her learning that and acting it off so completely , and not three years old ! She is smarter than Violet ' -- and then Violet set up such a howl ! Her mother pacified her by saying Marilla should tell her a piece , and after several efforts Cinderella did induce her to say by a great deal of prompting 'Milkman , Milkman , where have you been ? ' Think of the wear on the child 's nerves , and she looked so tired", "context_len": 111}}}, "voting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Mother , '' she said , `` is n't it too bad that Reliance ca n't go ? She says she would n't mind so much if it were not for the voting , but you see if she is n't there , she will lose her vote , and we do so want the Elderflower plan to be the one", "context_len": 62}}}, "proxy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Conway read : `` I hereby appoint Edna Conway to be my proxy and to vote upon any question which may come up before this meeting", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` It is some one appointed in the place of another to do what would otherwise be done by the first person ; for instance , in this case you could be proxy for Reliance and vote for her", "context_len": 39}}}, "legal": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 4, "context": "Though he had no legal right to interfere with her , even as the representative of his father , she knew the unscrupulous character of the man , and that he would not have spent time and money in a visit to California unless he had a strong hope of carrying her back with him", "context_len": 55}}}, "indisputable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "She signed her name with a flourish , and Edna , armed with the indisputable right to take her place , started off for Hewlett 's old blacksmith shop", "context_len": 29}}}, "storage": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "It was , however , forty feet from any tracks , and for convenience sake , when the railroad gave up the burned building which they had occupied for unclaimed freight storage , it had been turned over to the express people", "context_len": 42}}}, "prominence": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "The things that are to be explained , the points that are to be brought out , the comparative degree of prominence to be given to the various particulars , will all be different , on account of the difference in the situation , the ideas , and the objects of these new readers , compared with those of the various other classes of readers which former authors have had in view", "context_len": 72}}}, "prospective": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "They separated , McGlory rather dazed and skeptical about his prospective fortune , and Matt more confident and highly delighted over his chum \u2019 s unexpected good luck", "context_len": 28}}}, "ministrations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The more retiring ones , like Alcinda , contented themselves with beginning their ministrations to relatives or those they knew , but it was to adventurous spirits like Esther Ann and Reliance that a difficult case such as old Nathan Keener appealed", "context_len": 42}}}, "littler": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "So she set off with Reliance , and felt quite at home since she knew all the girls of her own age , and older , and , as she said , `` the littler ones do n't count", "context_len": 39}}}, "session": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 24, "context": "A few hours later the chieftains were all summoned to a council in the king 's tent , and when , after a short session , they came forth , the general order was given to break up the encampment , and move towards the southwest for the winter , for all the resources of the country around were exhausted", "context_len": 60}}}, "doorstep": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 35, "context": "Peggy , who always liked to be first and foremost , ran on before the others to ask for the milk , and was greeted by a furious barking from a collie-dog who guarded the doorstep , while a small , shock-headed girl peeped shyly from behind the shelter of his rough back", "context_len": 53}}}, "measly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 21, "context": "He has no apple trees except an old one that is nearly dead and never has but a few little , measly , knerly apples on it ; that 's why I thought he 'd like these", "context_len": 37}}}, "glowering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "For the space of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very", "context_len": 128}}}, "varmint": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "A red varmint never takes chances when he has a show of getting what he wants without it ; there ain \u2019 t \u2019 nough of \u2019 em to ride up and open fire ; don \u2019 t ye see that if they hold off till darkness , believing as how we haven \u2019 t an idee they \u2019 re within a thousand miles , and we ain \u2019 t keeping a lookout , they believe they can play thunder with us ?", "context_len": 83}}}, "esther": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Esther screamed", "context_len": 2}}}, "affrighted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Edna , paralyzed with fright , looked on with affrighted eyes , but presently found voice to quaver out , `` Please do n't hurt her ! Oh , please do n't ! ''", "context_len": 34}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Mar -- Marse Cal -- Cally -- lem -- lem me tell you 'bout it ! '' he cried , affrighted ; and then , ashamed of my silly display of temper , I turned him loose", "context_len": 38}}}, "quaver": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "' When quite a tiny child she had much preferred the tragedy of Red Riding Hood to the brighter fate of the princesses who lived happily ever afterwards , and , with the tears streaming down her fat little cheeks , would quaver out 'Tell it again ! '", "context_len": 49}}}, "mut": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Here , you old mut , what are you doing to my sister ? '' he cried , at the same time trying to wrest the stick from the old man 's grasp", "context_len": 34}}}, "wielded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "But in one place a gigantic warrior succeeded in hewing an aperture with his axe , wielded by giant strength , and all might have been lost had not Edmund perceived it , and rushed to its defence , collecting by his shout half-a-dozen followers", "context_len": 45}}}, "loosing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "He was not dead , for , as Harry Paul turned him so that he floated on his back with his face above water , the drowning man began to make frantic clutches with his hands , so that it was only by loosing his hold and getting behind that Harry Paul avoided what would have been a deadly embrace", "context_len": 60}}}, "yearning": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 77, "context": "the crier proclaiming that none should open his shop nor stand in the road , so the Lady Bedrulbudour might pass to the bath ; but I saw her even as she is , for that , when she came to the door of the bath , she lifted her veil , and when I noted her favour and viewed that noble form of hers , there befell me , O my mother , a passion of yearning for love of her and desire of her [ 324 ] usurped mine every part ; nor can I ever more have ease , except I get her , and I purpose , therefore , to demand her of the Sultan her father in the way of law and righteousness", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "One might call the Granth the book of yearning , and I feel humiliated that I , with Gospel light , should in spiritual contemplation and longing for closest communion with the Deity come so far behind these poor Sikhs", "context_len": 40}}}, "grapple": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` He squared off , but the old man had neither strength nor desire to grapple with such a masterful opponent , and he slunk back against his door", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Snarling and spitting , they scrambled up and faced each other , and the next moment were engaged in a life and death grapple", "context_len": 24}}}, "pestered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "I ain \u2019 t much on letter-writing , Uncle Joe answered , but I \u2019 ll make a try at it now and then ; and you \u2019 re going to be so busy doing the things you \u2019 re wanting to do that you won \u2019 t have much time to be pestered with the goings-on out here", "context_len": 59}}}, "eyeteeth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I guess I cut my eyeteeth a good many years ago , and can catch 'em at their tricks", "context_len": 19}}}, "everlastingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Well , I 'll be everlastingly switched , '' he exclaimed , `` Edna , my child , what are you doing in this mix-up ? ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "switched": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "Shagbark crumpled up some dry fragments from a plug of tobacco , in the palm of his hand , punched them into the bowl of his pipe , switched a match along the side of his buckskins , applied the tiny flame , and rode to the head of the company without another word", "context_len": 54}}}, "zealous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Under the zealous and indefatigable tuition of this young damsel I made astonishingly rapid progress as a student of the language spoken by those around me , and was soon able to converse in it with a very fair amount of freedom", "context_len": 42}}}, "overreach": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "You have something of a case , I admit , but you happened to overreach yourself this time", "context_len": 18}}}, "chirk": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "They knew you were lonely and had n't much chance to run around with the boys , or run an automobile , so they thought they would chirk you up a little by presenting you with a large , sweet , juicy , red apple", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "' I had n't felt so chirk as I might all day , and it just went to the soft place in my heart ; and it gave me a lesson , too , that I sha'n't forget in a hurry", "context_len": 41}}}, "dissect": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "To prove this to you , I will now dissect this large , sweet , juicy , red apple", "context_len": 19}}}, "pared": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` He carefully cut the apple , which Edna had given him , pared and quartered it , stuck a piece on the end of his knife and offered it to the old man , who pushed it away contemptuously", "context_len": 40}}}, "quartered": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "Troopers were dismounting and leading their horses away to their stables , or were lounging at the doors of the houses where they were quartered , and a crowd of curious country folk and villagers gathered to stare at them , and even to put questions to the more affable-looking of the steel-coated soldiers", "context_len": 54}}}, "acknowledge": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` I do not wish to dissuade you ; indeed , I can not myself see how you can get out of this most mistaken engagement , for the man has behaved well , and I am the first to acknowledge that ; but has it ever occurred to you that you do a man an absolute and terrible injustice when you marry him , loving with all your heart and soul another man ? Do you think it is fair to him ? Do n't you think he ought at least to know this ? ''", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "harangue": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hugh and Irene had an earnest harangue on the subject of pony-driving , and Miss Frost , much relieved at such an unceremonious introduction , turned her attention to little Agnes", "context_len": 31}}}, "stuffed": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 72, "context": "wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his absence", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Sweet bud of the morning , what poet can speak The glories that beam in thy eye ? The rosebuds that bloom on thy fat little cheek , -- And thy round head so stuffed full of Latin and Greek , Arithmetic and Geology", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "On the edge of the top shelf was a squirrel stuffed , sitting up with his tail over his back , just as natural as life", "context_len": 26}}}, "batting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "How his batting brings them first place in the League and how he secures his appointment to West Point make CRACKER STANTON an up-to-the-minute baseball story no lover of the game will want to put down until the last word is read", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Dodds was batting , but as his back was toward them , the children could not see his face", "context_len": 19}}}, "relaxed": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 31, "context": "Meat will be served out to you daily , when it can be obtained , and for those of you who hold that the strict tenets of your religion may be relaxed while engaged in such severe labour , a ration of wine will also be served out ; and such other indulgences as are compatible with the discipline and safety of the ship , will also be granted to you", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "She had not noticed Jane , who sat opposite , mute and relaxed , like one in whom hope and resolution flag and fail ; but Jane 's deep eyes followed Lola 's swift motion , and her look changed a little at the girl 's air of eager joy", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The vigilance of our guards had somewhat relaxed , and we were allowed to remain in one another \u2019 s company for a much longer period", "context_len": 26}}}, "treaty": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 36, "context": "It stipulated a cession of the lands between the Savannah and Altamaha rivers , and of some islands on the Atlantic coast , to the British ; it further stipulated promises to enter into a commercial treaty at a later date , to place themselves under the general government of Great Britain , to live in peace with the colonies , to capture runaway slaves and deliver them at Charleston , Savannah or Palachukla garrison for a consideration", "context_len": 78}}}, "grouch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "If you will promise to be a little less free with that stick and not get a grouch on you every time a boy looks your way , they will promise to play no more tricks", "context_len": 36}}}, "scraping": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Without a word , Nathan took the stick and went in , Ben bowing and scraping and saying , `` Thank you for a very good time , '' then receiving no reply , not even a grunt , he added , `` Not at all , the pleasure is entirely mine", "context_len": 52}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}}}, "curmudgeon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "Harris , to whom the idea had first occurred , suggested it , Marie-Celeste had put her two arms round her father 's neck , declaring he was just a darling and yet , when you come to think of it , he was the very same old curmudgeon of a papa , and not one whit altered either , who had been so soundly berated for insisting that it would be better for Donald to have some easy work to do than to idle away the whole summer", "context_len": 89}}}, "setback": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was a great setback to the enthusiasm of the girls , but as Reliance told Esther Ann , she should not have tried so venturesome a thing at the very outset", "context_len": 32}}}, "outset": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "It was a great setback to the enthusiasm of the girls , but as Reliance told Esther Ann , she should not have tried so venturesome a thing at the very outset", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "But who could long be grave at the outset of so promising an expedition ! The idea of a leisurely driving trip through the lovely Lake Country , stopping here and there , as the spirit moved them , at the comfortable little inns and hotels that abound in the region , had been such a supremely delightful idea , even in mere anticipation , that now that they were actually off enthusiasm knew no bounds , and mirth was literally unconfined", "context_len": 82}}}, "ridicule": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "So great was the ridicule , however , which followed , that he gave up his serious thoughts for the time , though it is related that he kept an alms-box , into which he put money whenever his conscience accused him of wrong-doing", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "If he was awkward with his fork , no one noticed it , and finally he quite forgot his embarrassment in the realization of the , to him , remarkable fact that he was among friends , none of whom were seeking to discover his shortcomings that they might laugh over them and ridicule him behind his back", "context_len": 58}}}, "fisticuffs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It is n't the first time that he has had a fisticuffs with a member of this family", "context_len": 18}}}, "uncontrolled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "With a touch of gipsy blood in her veins , with the most ungoverned , uncontrolled passions , she yet was capable of a devotion , of an affection self-absorbing , self-forgetful", "context_len": 32}}}, "pricks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Bessie had been taken from the wagon that she might rest herself by running about a little after her long ride , and now she and Maggie , as well as Hafed , forgot pricks and scratches in the pleasure of watching the brook , and feeling its cool , clear waters trickle through their fingers", "context_len": 56}}}, "snappish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He has grown so morose and snappish that no one takes the trouble to do more than nod to him nowadays", "context_len": 21}}}, "tumbledown": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "With good money after bad all the time there is nothing left but that little tumbledown house he lives in", "context_len": 20}}}, "appropriated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 46, "context": "But the doctor said there might be even a worse interpretation put upon the disappearance of the money , an interpretation he was both to entertain , but which must occur to all , namely , that some one had succumbed to temptation , and had appropriated the missing sum , which one of their number had been so positive he left in a safe place", "context_len": 66}}}, "abed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Wherefore I do presently command thee , as thou art a loyal servant of the lamp , that this night , whenas thou seest the bride and bridegroom abed together , thou take them up in their bed [ and bring them ] hither", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "So after Jack and the babies were abed she used to read , unless Jack would n't go to sleep and torment her with questions that were unanswerable", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The Sultan went up to his daughter , the Lady Bedrulbudour , as she lay abed , and raising the curtain , gave her good morning and kissed her between the eyes and asked her how she did", "context_len": 38}}}, "standpoint": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 60, "context": "It is not hard to realise what the thought must have been to Charlotte of losing this dearly-loved companion , -- her room-mate and the constant sharer of her thoughts and interests from very infancy ; nor is it difficult to believe how bravely she would put aside the recollection of herself , viewing the question from Laura \u2019 s standpoint alone", "context_len": 62}}}, "pricking": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "He had drawn the reins and sat looking down on it , while Old Charlie , pricking up his ears in glad recognition of the familiar sight , pawed the ground impatiently", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "At the same instant the flag rod clattered to the boards , and a second later , rubbing his face free from sudden pricking grains of powder , Bart saw what had happened", "context_len": 33}}}, "blistering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` To keep 'em from blistering , '' Amanda told her", "context_len": 11}}}, "cornfields": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "They live as they can , by robbing orchards , hen-roosts , dairies and cornfields during the summer ; picking up odd bits , and stealing whatever they can lay their fingers on in the winter , half starved and half frozen the most of the time", "context_len": 47}}}, "pails": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 50, "context": "There were indeed lots of blackberries in the spot to which Fred had called them ; and , screened from the rays of the sun , they picked them with comfort ; besides which , many a large berry which they did not pick themselves found its way into their pails ; so that , by the time Hafed came with his offering to Maggie , her own berries made quite a show , and she steadily refused to take his", "context_len": 81}}}, "frisking": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Bunny and their family were generally to be seen frisking about all over and among the boughs , waiting for the nice breakfast which was sure to be provided for them by the kind young master who had chosen them for his pets", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He smiled when he saw me swimming and frisking and said , `` The Prince is braver than I am", "context_len": 20}}}, "outspread": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "Edna picked it up but it had no desire to stay when this , of all hours in the day , was the best to play in , so it scrambled down from her arms and was off like a flash , darting half way up a tree , with ears back and claws outspread", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "His captive was , however , soon safe in the large basket , and he had hardly closed the lid and placed a boulder used as ballast upon it before a tug at his other line made the thole-pin rattle , and after a little hauling he dragged in a gloriously-coloured gurnard , whose outspread fins looked like the wings of some lovely butterfly", "context_len": 64}}}, "titters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was very quiet in the living-room , or did she hear whispers , and subdued titters ? Was Reliance or maybe Ben going to play a trick on her ? She heard a sudden `` Hush ! Hush ! '' as she reached the door of the living-room , but she made up her mind that she would appear perfectly unconcerned , and entered the room in a very don't-care sort of manner", "context_len": 74}}}, "plumped": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "But the strain was too much for Peggy , and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say", "context_len": 128}}}, "ornate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "Altogether the vehicle had a lordly air , easily matching that of the individual sitting alone on the purple cushions -- a man whose features were not very clear at the distance , although the yellowness of his beard , the glitter of his studded shirt-front , and whole consequential , expansive effect recalled to the doctor 's mind an image of the past , less ornate , indeed , and affluent , but of similar aspect", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "In the Dominion he found that he was `` up against something '' in which an ornate classical education did not count", "context_len": 22}}}, "contribute": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "The notice that each of the seven plants distributed to the Indians belonged , or was the emblem of a certain gens or division of people , is gathered from this passage only , and probably refers to the ingredients of some war-physic , which only a limited number of the gentes may have been entitled to contribute to the annual p\u00faskita", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "I trust that she will not leave this house , in which we have all enjoyed so much happiness ; and I feel assured that you will all tenderly watch over her , and contribute by every means in your power to her future comfort", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And the Queen did more than contribute money : orders for work were sent from Windsor Castle , Osborne and Balmoral ; and the blind people delighted in saying that they were making brooms for the Queen", "context_len": 37}}}, "souvenirs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Oh , they 're nothing but some little souvenirs , '' remarked Esther Ann loftily", "context_len": 16}}}, "loftily": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "May it please Your Honor , said Sleuth loftily , I have been requested by the attorney for the defense to tell my story in my own way , and ere I have finished I will demonstrate to your satisfaction and the satisfaction of every person present that every word I speak has a bearing upon the matter and is necessary to explain the reasons which led up to my deduction involving the before-mentioned Timothy Davis in a network of his own weaving , from which I think he will scarcely escape with ease", "context_len": 94}}}, "booty": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 7, "context": "The camp , with all its rich booty and the great banner of the pasha , fell into the hands of the victors , who , satisfied with their success , and exhausted by their efforts , made no attempt to follow the flying foe , or to hinder their embarkation ; for even now the Turks , enormously outnumbering them as they did , might be driven by despair to a resistance so desperate as once again to turn the tide of victory", "context_len": 84}}}, "incredibly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "Simultaneously with the appearance of the guards -- for such they were -- there emerged from the fetish-house a man who appeared to be incredibly old , for his hair and beard were as white as snow , and his once stalwart form was now bowed and wizened with the passage of , as it seemed to me , hundreds of years", "context_len": 62}}}, "racketed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Oh , we just racketed around", "context_len": 7}}}, "stunts": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "Then I galloped to the beach and wading out swam carefully to the edge of the crib , thinking perhaps I could aid with teeth or tail , for one of my water stunts is to bring ashore children who pretend they are drowning", "context_len": 44}}}, "graced": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "When she raised the veil from her face and Alaeddin saw her , he said , `` Verily , her fashion glorifieth the Great Creator and extolled be the perfection of Him who made her and graced her with this beauty and goodliness ! '' And his back was cloven in sunder , [ 321 ] when he saw her ; his thought was confounded and his understanding [ 322 ] dazed and the love of her gat hold upon his whole heart ; so he turned back and returning home , went in to his mother , like one distraught", "context_len": 101}}}, "complacently": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 25, "context": "and as Donald looked as though he thought he might , and as that was exactly the way he was expected to look , Marie-Celeste complacently continued : Well , first you go up a flight of steps , and you find yourself in a sort of vestibule ; and there 's a splendid portrait of the architect there -- the man who restored the old parts of the castle and added new parts to it and made it all beautiful as it is now ; and from this vestibule you go on and on from one grand room to another", "context_len": 101}}}, "hello": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Oh , hello , Dorothy", "context_len": 5}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "It \u2019 s too good to be true , finding you all here and Sweet Rosy O \u2019 Grady too ! I \u2019 m going to say hello to her before she vanishes in a pink fog , or something !", "context_len": 41}}}, "tinkle": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "Beside Silver Brook , which ran down through the border of the woods , he paused to listen to the tinkle and gurgle of the water", "context_len": 26}}}, "gladder": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "The next excitement was the coming of her father , for whom Dorothy watched and who appeared almost gladder than anyone that his wife and little girl were at home again", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "I think maybe if I take Old Charlie back with me they -- they won \u2019 t be so hard on me ; they -- they \u2019 ll be gladder to -- to --", "context_len": 34}}}, "scrapping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Conway on the other with the evening paper , the boys reading , or scrapping in the hall , Celia in the next room at the piano , and Edna herself with the Children 's Page of the paper spread out before her where she lay at full length on the big rug before the fire", "context_len": 56}}}, "unlock": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 45, "context": "What could Theodore Harris be writing to Marie-Celeste about , and who could the other two letters be from ? Those were the absorbing questions of the hour ; and at last Marie-Celeste is back again on the little seat beside her , ready to unlock her precious secrets , and with the three mysterious letters spread , one upon the other , open in her lap", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Did Casson know her ? Would the mention of her name unlock the door of his memory , that door that he had tried so desperately but fruitlessly to open ?", "context_len": 31}}}, "alternative": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "It meant the shattering of all his plans , but he faced the alternative bravely , and even became a bit more relieved and cheerful when he had decided to accept it as the only thing that could be done", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "Before taking down the spar and sail , he had deliberated as to whether it would be better to run before the coming gale or to lie to , and had decided on the latter alternative , as , were it to continue to blow long , he might be driven on to the Egyptian coast", "context_len": 56}}}, "burt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "BURT", "context_len": 1}}}, "tracking": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Another hour \u2019 s careful tracking brought him close to the creek , where he saw our comrades being escorted on board by the buccaneers in gangs , twenty-seven all told , so that they were apparently all alive and well", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "TRACKING THE THIEF", "context_len": 3}}}, "foiling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Foiling a Thief 58", "context_len": 4}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "FOILING A THIEF", "context_len": 3}}}, "standish": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 0, "context": "Standish turned ghastly pale as he saw the menacing glances of those around him", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Standish seemed to take no special pains to cultivate their acquaintance as he might naturally have been expected to do", "context_len": 20}}}, "interviewing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "How well you 'd look interviewing authors and artists when they come here with their manuscripts", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Amongst other matters which Jane Mullins took upon her own broad shoulders was the interviewing of all strangers who came to inquire about the house", "context_len": 25}}}, "foothills": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 46, "context": "The man inside the cabin having cleared away the dishes and set matters to right , sat down on a bench and began mending his clothes ; two others had gone off to look after the horses , which were grazing some distance away among the foothills", "context_len": 47}}}, "reclining": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "The light shone out in the gathering darkness , and illumined the recesses of the bushes around , and the faces of a large body of men reclining on the bank , or engaged in the task of sharpening their arms while their supper was roasting", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Jane wrapped the little girl in the kimona and lifted her up in the reclining chair", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The first to recover , he waited until M'Kane regained consciousness , and , having placed M'Turk and Selwyn in a reclining position , set off to find an egress and bring assistance", "context_len": 33}}}, "attest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "On one side is a bureau , a few portraits are on the walls , a pine bedstead and an easy-chair , in which is reclining a man of middle age whose wasted form and hollow cheeks attest the ravages of consumption", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Will you officially attest to the correctness of these , Mr", "context_len": 12}}}, "ravages": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "On one side is a bureau , a few portraits are on the walls , a pine bedstead and an easy-chair , in which is reclining a man of middle age whose wasted form and hollow cheeks attest the ravages of consumption", "context_len": 42}}}, "canyon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Five minutes later the sound of hoofs was heard outside , and a boy of sixteen galloped up from the canyon on the left , and , jumping off at the portal , tethered his pony and pushed open the door of the cabin", "context_len": 44}}}, "portal": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "Five minutes later the sound of hoofs was heard outside , and a boy of sixteen galloped up from the canyon on the left , and , jumping off at the portal , tethered his pony and pushed open the door of the cabin", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The outer portal stood open , but sentinels of the hus-carles were posted thereat , who at once came forward as Edmund paused at the gate", "context_len": 26}}}, "tethered": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "Five minutes later the sound of hoofs was heard outside , and a boy of sixteen galloped up from the canyon on the left , and , jumping off at the portal , tethered his pony and pushed open the door of the cabin", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Alejandro turned off in a sulk , and , leaving Ana to her own resources , went toward the place where the ponies and burros were tethered", "context_len": 27}}}, "permanent": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It may relieve me a little , Gerald , but I am past permanent help", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 40, "context": "The Creek Indians or Maskoki proper occupy , in historic times , a central position among the other tribes of their affiliation , and through their influence and physical power , which they attained by forming a comparatively strong and permanent national union , have become the most noteworthy of all the Southern tribes of the United States territories", "context_len": 59}}}, "deceive": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 52, "context": "I could now see how near I had been to regaining my freedom , for just beyond the place where my tour of exploration had abruptly terminated was the entrance to the cave , skilfully hidden by a heavy screen of painted canvas that , even at a short distance , would deceive all who were not acquainted with the secret", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I do not for a moment deceive myself with the thought that , beyond the fact that her gift has made her feel an interest in me and my fate , she has any sentiment in the matter : probably , indeed , she looks back upon the gift as a foolish act of girlish enthusiasm that led her into making a promise that she now can not but find unpleasantly binding ; for it is but natural that among the young nobles of her own rank and country there must be some whom she would see with pleasure wearing her colours", "context_len": 102}}}, "wrinkling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I appreciate his confidence , '' said the big man , wrinkling his eyebrows in an amused way", "context_len": 19}}}, "indebted": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 3, "context": "Greatly am I indebted to you , and sorry indeed am I , that I am unable to reward you now for the great service that you have rendered me", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I have reason to feel indebted to him , '' said Dewey", "context_len": 13}}}, "reparation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "But , father , the reparation ought to have been made to you , not to me", "context_len": 17}}}, "misjudged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "She was vexed at being misjudged by those from whom she had so carefully concealed what she had done , for she saw plainly enough that they all thought her opposition of the morning was owing to the spirit of contradiction she had so often shown", "context_len": 46}}}, "misjudge": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Above all things , do n't misjudge us , '' and with that she was gone , closing the door behind her", "context_len": 23}}}, "pliant": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Campbell darkly intimated that should her opposition continue he would procure from two pliant physicians a certificate of her insanity and have her confined in that most terrible of prisons , a mad-house", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The pronunciation of Cha'hta is so delicate and pliant that only a superior scientific alphabet can approximately express its peculiar sounds and intonations", "context_len": 23}}}, "desirous": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 8, "context": "But , singularly enough , no one seemed desirous to avail himself of his neighbour 's generosity ; and the reason soon became apparent ; for presently , in the midst of their wild bounding round the inner edge of the tightly packed mass of spectators , they came to a sudden halt , and Mafuta , advancing alone , proceeded to `` smell out '' those who were supposed to be inimical to the king 's or his own authority , or against whom either of them had a secret grudge", "context_len": 92}}}, "ungenerously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He has behaved so ungenerously about the whole matter", "context_len": 9}}}, "investments": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "His name is Bradley Wentworth , and he lives in the town of Seneca , Illinois , where he has large investments , and is a prominent man", "context_len": 28}}}, "lucrative": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Divested of his disguise , no longer a fear-filled roustabout fugitive , Bart 's strange friend had found a steady , lucrative position at the hotel , and Bart felt that he had certainly been the means of doing some real good in the world every time he looked at the happy , contented face of his prot\u00e9g\u00e9", "context_len": 58}}}, "monthly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "In her little sketch Charlotte says of him , -- Careless of his own comfort , restricting his personal expenses to a very narrow compass , he gave to the Missionary cause at the rate of forty pounds monthly , and one year even more \u2019 ; adding that with shrinking from ostentation \u2019 he had never given his name on these occasions", "context_len": 63}}}, "tarnished": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "You see that if my uncle finds out what has happened I am a ruined man , for he will dismiss me from his employment with a tarnished name", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "After the soldiers were weary with the work of slaughtering the wretched inhabitants of the city , they found that many still remained alive , and Alexander tarnished the character for generosity and forbearance for which he had thus far been distinguished by the cruelty with which he treated them", "context_len": 50}}}, "inheritance": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 28, "context": "The children , however , knew little of the shadow which haunted their home , for they lived as yet in that happy thoughtless paradise which is the inheritance of true childhood , where a new rabbit in the hutch or an extra treat on a holiday is of far more importance than any grown-up affair", "context_len": 56}}}, "annually": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "If I would consent to take his guilt upon myself , he agreed to pay over to my wife five hundred dollars annually out of his salary of fifteen hundred dollars , and when he inherited his uncle \u2019 s estate , he furthermore agreed to pay over to me twenty thousand dollars", "context_len": 53}}}, "furthermore": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "If I would consent to take his guilt upon myself , he agreed to pay over to my wife five hundred dollars annually out of his salary of fifteen hundred dollars , and when he inherited his uncle \u2019 s estate , he furthermore agreed to pay over to me twenty thousand dollars", "context_len": 53}}}, "distasteful": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "She was dressed in her usual prim and proper style , and Rosamund could not help owning to a distasteful feeling when she saw her", "context_len": 25}}}, "admitting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "The boy greeting him with a flash of white teeth , made no formality over admitting an entire stranger , but at once introduced him into a little book-closet on the ground floor , where a gentleman was busily engaged in unpacking folios from a great sea chest ; and as he turned to receive the visitor , Dick , to his inexpressible relief , saw a face that had been familiar to him in Scotland", "context_len": 76}}}, "agreement": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 60, "context": "Belden to do aught but look and listen , and drop a word of assent now and then , when Marie-Celeste saw fit to impart her information in a somewhat interrogative form ; and in this way they went on from monument to monument , giving of course but a passing glance to many and stopping longest , by tacit agreement , at those which had some special charm or attraction for Marie-Celeste", "context_len": 73}}}, "infant": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 23, "context": "Report says that when he came to Rake to settle down , some twelve or thirteen years back -- I being but an infant in arms , -- he did gain a lasting reputation by outmatching one Caleb James , a notorious bully , at his own game , breaking his pate with his own staff on the roadside hard by Milland Church", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 70, "context": "Next , Tom read how Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judea , in a rough stable and laid not in a pretty cradle such as their baby brothers and sisters slept in , but in a manger where the wise men of the east came and worshipped Him : and how after Joseph and Mary had been told by God to fly into the land of Egypt with the infant Saviour , the wicked king , Herod , killed all the dear little babies in the land , with the hope that Jesus might be among them", "context_len": 98}}}, "compact": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "Mistress Felgate gave her permission with the stipulation that her husband must run no needless risks , the compact was sealed by a kiss bestowed by her gallant and unabashed bridegroom , and the conversation was resumed , while mirth and gaiety reigned supreme", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 15, "context": "Bartram , who saw it in 1775 , depicts it as the largest , most compact , and best situated Indian town he ever saw ; the habitations were large and neatly built , the walls of the houses consisted of a wooden frame , lathed and plastered inside and outside with a reddish clay , and roofed with cypress bark or shingles", "context_len": 63}}}, "hindrance": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "Her very warm-heartedness and impetuosity were now and then somewhat of a hindrance , -- as when , on her first arrival , going into a Zenana , she pressed forward and eagerly shook hands with a bibi , -- an Indian lady , -- forgetting the difference of Indian customs and English ones", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "I was told that Batala is a place where we could not read the Bible : but I have copied a great deal into my Bible picture-book ; and there is no let or hindrance that I can see in showing the pictures , and reading the descriptions , which are God \u2019 s own Word", "context_len": 56}}}, "calming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "My uncle was at first resolved upon having you arrested , but I succeeded in calming him down", "context_len": 18}}}, "authorized": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "SETH KENDALL : I have received your letter , and must express my surprise at your trusting my son , knowing well that he is a minor , and that I have not authorized his running up a bill with you", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Taking no chances this time , Malcolm and his companions went on board a couple of hours before the authorized time", "context_len": 21}}}, "prosecute": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "Rosencamp , disappointed , cowed , more angry than ever , knowing that he could not prosecute Joe , made his way to the door and out to the tow-path amid the jeers of the waiting crowd", "context_len": 37}}}, "installments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Of course I will see that the note is paid by installments and let him think that the money comes from you", "context_len": 22}}}, "inestimable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "He has won a victory that not only reflects the highest honour upon the Order , but is an inestimable service to Italy , and has freed her from a corsair fleet that would have been a scourge to her , both at sea and to the towns and villages along the coast", "context_len": 53}}}, "lenient": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "But this did not make Seabrooke any more lenient in his judgment", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then when at noon , by special permission of a very lenient guardsman , the children establish themselves for luncheon on a terrace beneath the shade of the Round Tower , Marie-Celeste and Albert and Harold agree that they had never had such fun -- never !", "context_len": 47}}}, "disinherited": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Wentworth \u2019 s urgent request , in order that Wentworth might not be disinherited by his uncle", "context_len": 17}}}, "moderately": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "Alfgar drank moderately , for sooth to say it was invigorating and welcome that cold day , but Higbald finished the bowl then and there , and then staggering down , drew the outer bolt in such a way that it missed the staple , which fact he was too drunk to perceive", "context_len": 53}}}, "injure": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Of course I could do nothing to injure mother , '' I said , `` I will try and be patient ; but Jane , Jane , do you know really what this means ? Can you not guess that there are things that even for a mother , a dying mother , a girl ought not to do ? ''", "context_len": 62}}}, "wentworth": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Wentworth send you the five hundred dollars he promised you annually ? asked Gerald", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 0, "context": "Wentworth did not ordinarily keep early hours , but he had been fatigued by his walks during the day , partly because he had traversed considerable ground , but partly on account of the high altitude which made the air rarer , and exertion more difficult", "context_len": 46}}}, "stipulated": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Why , the fact is , Uncle Job , it 's the major that lets me have the money on my note , but he stipulated that I should have an indorser , and he particularly mentioned you", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 69, "context": "The letter gave instructions for me to be sent to my uncle \u2019 s at Portsmouth , to be provided for until I could choose for myself what I should be , at the same time exhorting me to serve faithfully His Majesty King Charles II or his lawful successor , and to abstain from vain or idle longings to break the seals of the enclosed package till the stipulated time limit had expired", "context_len": 74}}}, "alleging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I have received from you a letter , asking me to send you twenty thousand dollars , alleging that some years since I promised to give you that sum upon the death of my uncle", "context_len": 35}}}, "regularly": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 28, "context": "No real substantive verb being extant , its want is supplied by \u00f3m\u00e4s , m\u00f3m\u00e4s , t\u00f3y\u00e4s I am so , I am such ; these are conjugated regularly , and when connected with the verbals in -t ( -at , -it , -ut ) of any verb , compose a periphrastic conjugation which displays itself in an almost infinite number of forms", "context_len": 63}}}, "nowise": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "The food and drink provided for me were of an especially nourishing character , and when at length I had partaken of as much as I could force down my throat I was again lashed to my stake , but this time so carefully that , while for me to loose my bonds was an impossibility , the circulation of blood was in nowise impeded ; and for even this small mercy I was inexpressibly grateful", "context_len": 76}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 89, "context": "He used also to go to the market of the goldsmiths and the market of the jewellers , and there he would sit and look upon the different kinds of jewels and see them bought and sold ; whereby he became aware that the fruits of the trees , wherewith he had filled the purses , [ 318 ] whenas he was in the treasure , were neither glass nor crystal , but jewels , and knew that he had happened upon great wealth , such as kings might nowise compass", "context_len": 91}}}, "perused": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "The boys had gone camping several times before , both winter and summer , and had a very complete outfit , as is known to those who have perused the other volumes of this series", "context_len": 35}}}, "epistle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "WHAT do you think of that letter , Gerald ? asked his father , when the boy had perused the epistle which had been handed to him", "context_len": 27}}}, "scathing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Davenport discouraged the acquaintance as much as possible , never feeling easy even for the life and limbs of her progeny when they were in the society of 'those terrible young Vaughans , ' and revenging herself by scathing remarks upon manners and deportment , which were extremely trying to the feelings of Aunt Helen , who naturally thought her children superior in every way to 'those mealy-faced little Davenports , who look as if they had not the strength or spirits to enjoy themselves , even if they were allowed to try", "context_len": 93}}}, "declining": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "A cold , wet , and misty evening , and above all to one whose pockets are not lined ! My foolish fancy for the Stage has brought me to a declining stage , if not a stage of decline", "context_len": 40}}}, "paltry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "A slight tingling pain in my hand made me look down , and I saw that one of my fingers had been cut off , so that one of the wretches could steal a paltry silver ring I was wearing", "context_len": 40}}}, "forger": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "I don \u2019 t think he would have offered anything , if he had not known that my father had letters proving that he was innocent , and Wentworth himself the forger", "context_len": 32}}}, "pulmonary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Yes ; the pure air has been favorable to my pulmonary difficulties", "context_len": 12}}}, "hemorrhage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "My father has had a serious hemorrhage , and he choked , unable to finish the sentence", "context_len": 17}}}, "repudiates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "This envelope , he said , contains two important documents the written confession of Bradley Wentworth , that it was he , not I , who forged the check upon his uncle , and the last letter in which he repudiates my claim upon him for the sum he agreed to pay me", "context_len": 53}}}, "lawyer": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 19, "context": "There was Mester Vaughan givin ' interest fair on them mortgages , and I 've heard as th ' lawyer hisself said as he were safe as th ' bank to pay regular , and what call had Mester Norton to buy 'em up , and ask for th ' principal back , when it were n't in reason as he could raise it ? '", "context_len": 66}}}, "recluse": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "As he quickened his steps , he suddenly realized that he must pass the wretched little tumble-down home of Tige Fletcher , a dirty , crabbed , old recluse , who hated boys because he had been taunted and tormented by them , and who kept two fierce dogs , which were regarded as vicious and dangerous", "context_len": 57}}}, "redress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "To him , Gerald , was left the task of obtaining redress for a great wrong", "context_len": 16}}}, "considerations": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "There were bodies of troops distinguished from the rest , and occupying positions of high honor , but these were selected and advanced above the others , not on account of their courage , or strength , or superior martial efficiency , but from considerations connected with their birth , and rank , and other aristocratic qualities", "context_len": 57}}}, "rightfully": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` It shows what you are , '' answered Seabrooke , continuing his reproaches , instead of giving the straightforward answer which he considered unnecessary , `` that you have not the decent manliness to demand that which rightfully belonged to you because you were ashamed of your own folly and weakness , but must go and ransack in my quarters to find your money", "context_len": 65}}}, "unfastening": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "He reached the shore of the lake , and , unfastening a boat which he kept there for his own use , he pushed it out from the shore , and then suffered it to float lazily over the smooth surface of the lake while he prepared his fishing tackle", "context_len": 50}}}, "soliloquized": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` This will be a tight hole to squeeze through , '' soliloquized the new arrival , as he noted the elaborate precautions taken against any attempt on the part of the prisoners to escape", "context_len": 35}}}, "serves": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 10, "context": "It is always well to remember that the man who serves his country as a good citizen , as a soldier , as a statesman , or in any other walk of life , deserves our admiration as much as the missionary or the minister of the Gospel -- each and all such are servants of the great King", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "Wilfrid at Aescendune , it seems in some sort my duty , following the example of many worthy brethren , to write some account of the origin and history of the priory over which it has pleased God to make me overseer , and to note , as occasion serves from time to time , such passing events as seem worthy of remembrance ; which record , deposited in the archives of the house , may preserve our memory when our bodies are but dust , and other brethren fill our places in the choir", "context_len": 95}}}, "replying": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "I guess I 'm tiring you with talking so much , said Chris ; I 'll tell you all about her some other time ; and Ted , replying , Well , somehow or other , I do seem to get exhausted precious easily , turned over and closed his eyes", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Then , and not until then , did she hail me , asking whether the expected pursuers were in sight ; and upon my replying in the negative , she informed me that breakfast was ready , and invited me to come down and partake of it", "context_len": 47}}}, "resemble": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 61, "context": "Save that your lips lack the thickness , and your nose is straighter than those of Nubians , no one would doubt but that you were one of that race ; and this is of little consequence , as many of them are of mixed blood , and , though retaining their dark colour , have features that in their outline resemble those of the Arabs", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Lucy did not in the least resemble her father , but took more after her mother , who was round and fat , and proportionately commonplace", "context_len": 26}}}, "referring": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 22, "context": "Many old traditions of historic importance must have been embodied in these records ; but the only one given by Milfort , referring to the emigration of the Creeks from their ancient cave-homes along Red river , is so mixed up with incredible matter , that the fixation of the events , as far as then remembered , must have taken place many generations after the arrival of the Creeks in their Alabama homes", "context_len": 74}}}, "winced": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 42, "context": "Hartley personally , he sought him out , where he was at work in a corner of the meadow , and the result , as he had anticipated , was a very plain talk -- so unsparingly and pointedly plain that Harry winced a good deal in the process , and once or twice came near resenting a mode of procedure that seemed very much akin to knocking a fellow when he 's down", "context_len": 74}}}, "disinheritance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "But to resume : I saved you from disgrace and disinheritance", "context_len": 11}}}, "repayment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "A thousand dollars in repayment of my great sacrifice ! Have riches made you narrow and mean ?", "context_len": 18}}}, "arrogantly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "There is some difference between the position of my son and yours , said Wentworth arrogantly", "context_len": 16}}}, "nobility": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "The union in him of strength with gentleness , of a masterful intellect with a spirit of yielding courtesy , of nobility with playfulness , of generosity with self-restraint , of real religious conviction and experience with frolicsome gaiety , made a combination not more rare than beautiful", "context_len": 48}}}, "paragon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Oh , I have no doubt your son is a paragon , said Wentworth with a sneer", "context_len": 17}}}, "documents": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 33, "context": "To offer a history of the Creek tribe from its discovery down to our epoch to the readers does not lie within the scope of this volume , and for want of sufficient documents illustrating the earlier periods it could be presented in a fragmentary manner only", "context_len": 47}}}, "demise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "IS he dead ? Wentworth asked himself , with sudden hope , for the demise of Warren Lane would remove all danger", "context_len": 22}}}, "hartshorn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Your father has fainted , he said , and I am looking for some restorative have you any salts , or hartshorn ?", "context_len": 23}}}, "insensible": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 16, "context": "Words led to blows , with the result that three of his opponents were picked up insensible , while Kennedy was taken to the military hospital with a broken wrist and a nasty contusion of the forehead , caused by the nail-shod boot of an eighteen-stone antagonist", "context_len": 47}}}, "defer": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "Well , this beats all , said Harold to himself ; what is going to happen next , I wonder ? But he had the graciousness to defer his own greeting to Uncle Everett until he assisted Aunt Lou and Dorothy and Marie-Celeste to dismount , by aid of the brake 's steps , and which much practice , by the way , enabled them to accomplish very skilfully", "context_len": 69}}}, "rely": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 14, "context": "But , more than from any efforts on your part or mine , I rely upon Suleiman Ali , who will , I am sure , as soon as he is set on shore , lose no time in acquainting the pasha of your capture , and in calling upon him to interfere in your favour", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "My dear Caretto , the grand master exclaimed , it is almost a resurrection , for we have all long mourned you as dead ; and your return to us at the present time is indeed fortunate ; for upon whose judgment and aid could I better rely than those of my old comrade in arms ? Then , turning to Gervaise , he went on : It was a daring and brilliant exploit indeed , Sir Gervaise , and in due time honour shall be paid to you and your brave companions , to whom and to you I now tender the thanks of the Order", "context_len": 107}}}, "confessing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "Your confession over your own signature that you forged the check , a crime attributed to my father , and confessing that he bore the blame to screen you", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Should she ask the children for it when they came home ? To do so , would be the same as confessing that she had done wrong , and that was the hardest thing in the world for the proud old lady to do", "context_len": 44}}}, "encroach": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "The council lasted from August 11th to 21st , and terminated in a treaty , by which the towns renewed their `` fealty '' to the king of Great Britain , and confirmed their cessions of territory , while Oglethorpe engaged that the British should not encroach upon their reserved lands , and that their traders should deal fairly and honestly with the Indians", "context_len": 64}}}, "distrust": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "Others suppose that the act was an expression of his implicit faith in the integrity and fidelity of his servant , and that he intended it as testimony , given in a very pointed and decisive , and , at the same time , delicate manner , that he was not suspicious of his friends , or easily led to distrust their faithfulness", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 6, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Seabrooke went about the business of the day with all his accustomed regularity and precision , but with a sort of defiant and I-am-going-to-stick-to-it air about him which in itself incited the other boys to covert thrusts and innuendoes tending to throw distrust upon his version of the story and to make known their thorough sympathy with Percy , not only for his loss , but also for the aspersions cast upon him by the young pupil-teacher", "context_len": 77}}}, "barter": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "When they were finished , the Maugrabin paid him their price , even that which he sought , and taking the lamps , carried them to the khan , where he laid them in a basket and fell to going round about in the markets and thoroughfares of the city and crying out , `` Ho ! who will barter an old lamp for a new lamp ? '' When the folk heard him crying this , they laughed at him and said , `` Certes , this man is mad , since he goeth about , bartering new lamps for old", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 66, "context": "To these he represented that he belonged to a party of his tribe who had encamped for the day at a short distance from the village in order to rest their horses before riding into Tripoli , whither they were proceeding to exchange skins of animals taken in the chase , and some young horses , for cotton clothes , knives , and other articles of barter with the tribes beyond them", "context_len": 72}}}, "pottage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Yes : I will not barter my son \u2019 s inheritance for a mess of pottage", "context_len": 16}}}, "shrewdness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "He answered this and received a prompt reply , directing him thereafter to always report such happenings at once , but his zeal and shrewdness were heartily commended , and a check for twenty-five dollars for extra services was inclosed", "context_len": 40}}}, "canoe": {"freq": 123, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 122, "word_index": 22, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "In the first place they removed everything from the house to the beach ; then they hauled Charlie \u2019 s canoe up to the house , and filled it with water ; they also filled all the barrels , troughs , and tubs about the premises , and drove the cattle to the beach , lest the fire should run into the woods", "context_len": 63}}}, "cornbread": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "With boiled potatoes , cornbread and coffee , the meal was by no means to be despised", "context_len": 17}}}, "temptations": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 42, "context": "Since then Christ feels for all the pains and struggles through which we go for his sake , since he can make allowance for all our weakness and failures ; and as he is so ready to give us help in our temptations , so much the more ought we who are not only tempted , but too apt , in spite of our best efforts , to fall into sin , to show to others all the kindness and sympathy we may at any time need for ourselves", "context_len": 89}}}, "amounting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "But alas ! I for one know to a Certainty that in the little castle there was rebellion almost amounting to mutiny , and that one curly , uncrowned head , that need not have had a care in all the world , was tossing uneasily on its pillow", "context_len": 49}}}, "dutiful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "She was remarkable for her intelligence and dutiful conduct ; and from the age of ten felt deep religious convictions , and was constant in her daily prayers and Bible reading", "context_len": 31}}}, "compensations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The enforced detention at Southampton , was , however , not without certain compensations", "context_len": 14}}}, "complacent": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He said this in a complacent tone", "context_len": 7}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 74, "context": "The first was a lean , spare man , with gray side-whiskers and a slight stoop , whose rather sporting attire accorded ill with his sharp , keen face ; while the second , the one on the better horse , was stout and ruddy of countenance , a man who looked as though he would be more at home in a bar-parlour than in a drawing-room , and who held himself with a complacent air , as one who is satisfied both with the world in general and himself in particular", "context_len": 92}}}, "unequally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "How unequally fortune is distributed ! thought Mr", "context_len": 8}}}, "directing": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 31, "context": "Then he turned from the window , put on his coat , which was lying in readiness beside him , strolled out from the club , called for a hansom , directing the driver to take him to the station , and never for one minute admitted to himself that he had risen a whole hour earlier in order to do this very thing , or that he was acting on any stronger impulse than that of a passing fancy , born of the midsummer day , and desire for a little variety", "context_len": 93}}}, "insupportably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He found the cabin insupportably dull , and would like to have wandered around with Gerald as a guide", "context_len": 19}}}, "conciliatory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I am a stranger just arrived , answered Wentworth in a conciliatory tone , for he did not feel the most absolute confidence in this man with his brigandish look", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Ted had not been as angry as Harold had expected , and Harold , sorry for his thrust about money matters , had wound up by being rather conciliatory , and he was happier , on the whole , than he had been any time for a twelvemonth", "context_len": 48}}}, "intrusive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The numerous incorporations of foreign elements have not been able to alter the purity of their language ; the number of intrusive words is very small , and the grammar has repelled every foreign intrusion", "context_len": 35}}}, "policy": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 10, "context": "It appears long to have been a maxim of their policy to give equal liberty and protection to tribes conquered by themselves , as well as to those vanquished by others , although many individuals taken in war are slaves among them , and their children are called of the slave race , and can not arrive to much honorary distinction in the country , on that account", "context_len": 68}}}, "neighborly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I went round to make a neighborly call , but he told me he didn \u2019 t feel like talking , and left me on the outside of the cabin lookin \u2019 like a fool ! and the backwoodsman spat to express his disgust", "context_len": 44}}}, "backwoodsman": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "If he could have seen the handsomely appointed quarters I had been used to -- the paved floors and fine stalls ! Poor lad ! where had he been brought up to think this rough log cabin of some early backwoodsman a suitable place for a Shetland pony ?", "context_len": 49}}}, "spat": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "Baulked of their prey , the mob still surged round us , and with a shout of : `` Let \u2019 em have the cat ! '' a great black object was sent flying towards us , and , striking my horse on the crupper , resolved itself into an enormous black cat , that spat and howled , digging its claws into the horse \u2019 s hide , and arching its back like the demon cat that is the reputed companion of every witch", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Her nose and tail and belly turrets spat", "context_len": 8}}}, "whisky": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I \u2019 ve got some whisky inside", "context_len": 7}, "NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 8, "context": "He poured out for himself a teaspoonful of whisky , but did not replenish Gerald \u2019 s glass , as Amsden was not likely to notice the omission", "context_len": 28}}}, "sociably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Have you lived here long ? asked Wentworth sociably", "context_len": 9}}}, "wallet": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 7, "context": "For the grandfather there is a new wallet with twenty five-pound notes laid between its leather-scented covers , and for the grandmother a labor-saving gift that will never cease to be a marvel -- a wonder-working churn that turns Bess 's milk to butter in just twelve seconds over a minute", "context_len": 51}}}, "inconsiderately": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "In doing this , however , he put himself forward so rashly and inconsiderately that his ladder was broken , and while the rest retreated he was left alone upon the wall , whence he descended into the town , and was immediately surrounded by enemies", "context_len": 46}}}, "habitual": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 8, "context": "Reassured now , but still not abandoning his habitual caution , he moved forward until from the shelter of a bush he could see encamped in a clearing , while they prepared their midday meal , a large party of natives , evidently on the warpath , as they were armed to the teeth", "context_len": 54}}}, "vacantly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Zekle Wynn stared vacantly round at the speakers , and then , putting his hand to his head , he walked thoughtfully away", "context_len": 23}}}, "identify": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 44, "context": "We were raising her very fast now that both ships were steering upon converging lines ; I could already see nearly to the foot of her topsails ; and I settled myself comfortably , determined to remain where I was until I could absolutely identify her , although even at this time I had scarcely a shadow of a doubt that it was the long-sought Virginia , or rather the Preciosa , that I held in the field of my telescope", "context_len": 81}}}, "signature": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 44, "context": "D'Aubusson had , indeed , for some years been the real head of the community , but every question had , if only as a matter of form , to be referred to the grand master , in order to obtain his approval and signature", "context_len": 45}}}, "jake": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Jake screwed up his face and pored over the signature", "context_len": 10}}}, "fastidious": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "Nor was Nancy the only one who indulged in a little innocent vanity , for Joe , too , had been so fastidious in his choice of a red-and-blue spotted necktie and a walking-stick with an ivory handle that the children began to suspect the blacksmith 's rosy-cheeked daughter must be at the bottom of it ; and even David had taken a long-tailed coat and a beaver hat out of the retirement of some mysterious bandboxes and had been quite snappy and particular with Mrs", "context_len": 86}}}, "eighty": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 45, "context": "The noise of a few gun-shots brought out of these spacious cavities a large number of bisons , wild oxen and wild horses , which ran , frightened as they were by the unusual explosions , head over heels , over precipices of more than eighty feet of perpendicular height into the slimy waters of Red river", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 79, "context": "Sometimes I ask myself , -- Now , in my present position , if I had no pleasure in religion , if everything connected with that were cut off , what would be left me ? -- what would life be to me ? O Leila , what a tasteless , what a bitter thing ! We want delights that will not grow old , that will never pall , that will be just as fresh and lovely at eighty as at eighteen", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "Aunt Hetty Vanderveer who was queer and going on to eighty , who could n't live with a relative for they always wanted to borrow her money , got tangled up in a house on which she had a mortgage , and called her grandnephew , Mr", "context_len": 47}}}, "attesting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "If you want any certificate attesting your prowess you have only to refer to Jake Amsden", "context_len": 16}}}, "ownership": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 58, "context": "So saying , I descended the poop ladder and , entering the cabin , made my way to the skipper 's state-room , and , opening a desk which I found there , soon discovered the genuine set of papers declaring the ship 's name to be the Preciosa , her port of registry Havana , and her ownership Spanish", "context_len": 60}}}, "broaches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Then when Wentworth broaches the subject again I will tell him , both for you and myself , that I decline his offer", "context_len": 23}}}, "capabilities": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 21, "context": "Although she never published anything during her Father \u2019 s lifetime -- whether because she was slow to recognise her own capabilities , or because he failed to encourage the idea , does not distinctly appear , -- her pen was often busy", "context_len": 43}}}, "locality": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 70, "context": "The noise grew louder and louder , so that it resembled the squealing of a kitten , and all four began to turn over the moss with eager fingers , till , with a cry , Archie drew out a small round ball of dried grass , about the size of Bobby 's fist , from which issued such crescendo squeaks that there could be no mistake as to the locality of the sound", "context_len": 74}}}, "visionary": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "But when he returned home on flying visits , he found to his dismay that his uncle 's visionary hopes were growing instead of fading ; and from desiring a merely republican England , General Harrison had begun to dream of a theocracy as complete as that of the early Jews , and to look forward to the immediate inauguration of an earthly Reign of the Saints , under the sceptre of Christ Himself , as the Fifth and last of the great monarchies of the world", "context_len": 87}}}, "speculation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 92, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "negotiation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "But as soon as it is taken up , the m\u00edko and his council may interpose , and by their prudent counsels stop it , and proceed to adjust the misunderstanding by negotiation", "context_len": 33}}}, "revolving": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` When I was in Macedon , before setting out on this expedition , while I was revolving the subject in my mind , musing day after day on the means of conquering Asia , one night I had a remarkable dream", "context_len": 42}}}, "primeval": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "In hot blood civilized men went back to primeval instincts and fought like wild beasts , clawing , tearing and gouging when it was too close work for the bayonet", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It refers to the coming up of primeval man from a lower world into a pre\u00ebxistent upper world , through some orifice", "context_len": 22}}}, "emboldens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "That emboldens me to mention to you a plan which has occurred to me", "context_len": 14}}}, "reap": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 22, "context": "Only in a few spots was there any appearance of cultivation , for who would sow when they knew not who should reap ? Not one lonely country house , such as abounded in the days of Edgar the Pacific , did they see standing , although they passed the blackened ruins of many an abode , showing where once the joys of home held sway", "context_len": 66}}}, "acre": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "There was a plat of land belonging to his house rather more than an acre in extent , but land was cheap in Hampton , and it is doubtful whether both house and lot would have brought , if thrown into the market , over one thousand dollars", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "James , \u2019 the old man would say in the morning , do you go into the barn-yard , and look in the north-east corner , and you will find a hoe ; take that hoe , and go down to the western field , and begin to hoe on the acre piece , and stick two punkin seeds in every other hill", "context_len": 63}}}, "patrimony": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "In these things Jane developed a most prodigal pride , freely expending upon them the little patrimony which had been put in the Trinidad bank against her old age", "context_len": 29}}}, "firmer": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "For they were both clinging to one faith , both looking to one hope , and the hand of the younger had drawn the feet of the elder to a firmer and surer foothold upon the Rock of Ages , on which both were resting", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "I am not poorly , though I look thin ; I think that I am stronger in health and firmer in spirit now than I have been almost all this trying year ; and for this I am thankful", "context_len": 39}}}, "accommodated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "Nothing loth to have a share in the afternoon 's fun , the children dropped from the wall , and each accommodated with one of Archie 's arms , they went the round of the fair in true village fashion", "context_len": 40}}}, "surrendered": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 16, "context": "Do you remember the name of Thaurepas ( ? ) , the blacky General who weakly surrendered his post to the French ? What do you think the grateful Monsieurs did to him ? Nailed epaulettes on his shoulders and a cocked hat on his head , and then threw him with his wife and children into the sea ! Would one believe such things of men in the 19th century ? I should like to know something of the present state of Hayti , and whether the throne is filled by a son of Henri I", "context_len": 97}}}, "fatigued": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 18, "context": "They reached the island about eight o \u2019 clock in the evening , with all their treasures , fatigued , but happy , having enjoyed themselves to the top of their bent , and with enough to think and talk about to last them half the winter", "context_len": 47}}}, "altitude": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 35, "context": "Wentworth did not ordinarily keep early hours , but he had been fatigued by his walks during the day , partly because he had traversed considerable ground , but partly on account of the high altitude which made the air rarer , and exertion more difficult", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The Cha'hta trace their mythic origin from the `` Stooping , Leaning or Winding Hill , '' N\u00e1ni W\u00e1ya , a mound of fifty feet altitude , situated in Winston county , Mississippi , on the headwaters of Pearl river", "context_len": 40}}}, "rarer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Wentworth did not ordinarily keep early hours , but he had been fatigued by his walks during the day , partly because he had traversed considerable ground , but partly on account of the high altitude which made the air rarer , and exertion more difficult", "context_len": 46}}}, "deigned": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "Two negroes , in addition to those killed by the fall of the gun , were lost in the sudden and brief incident , yet the only remark the overseer deigned to make was : `` And they cost a hundred pieces of eight but a month ago ! ''", "context_len": 50}}}, "significant": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 75, "context": "It was all very well for her to tell me that I must cure him ; but suppose that I could not , suppose that the sickness -- whatever it might be -- should run its course and fail to yield to my treatment , what then ? The reason , so urgently expressed by her , why I must effect a cure -- '' for your own sake , Dick , '' -- was significant enough of the direction in which her apprehensions pointed ; there was no necessity for me to inquire what she meant", "context_len": 97}}}, "dishonesty": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "Whatever their faults and shortcomings -- and some of these had been grave enough -- such an idea , such an implication as this had never before presented itself to them -- that there was a thief in their midst , that one of their number had been guilty of flagrant dishonesty , of an absolute theft , and that of a large sum", "context_len": 64}}}, "pocketbook": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "I take you to witness , Gerald , said Wentworth , this is my pocketbook , which this man Amsden stole from me last night", "context_len": 25}}}, "torpid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Because I was more asleep than awake , and my mind was too torpid to reason upon what I saw", "context_len": 20}}}, "tallied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The description tallied exactly with Jake Amsden , the man with whom he had had a conference the day before", "context_len": 20}}}, "evasively": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "I spoke to him , said Wentworth evasively , for he did not care to mention the subject of their conversation", "context_len": 21}}}, "burglars": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "Lord Shaftesbury brought before Parliament a scheme for assisting young thieves to emigrate ; and the grown-up burglars and vagabonds , seeing how much in earnest he was , invited him to a meeting", "context_len": 34}}}, "lynched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Only last year a man living five miles away was lynched for stealing a horse", "context_len": 15}}}, "recovering": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 70, "context": "But do not land unless it is absolutely necessary , for you might fall a victim to the fanaticism of its inhabitants , and no knight has ever set foot on shore there since the ill fated day when the Moslems wrested it from us , bathed the ruined walls with the best blood of our Order and the Templars ' , and destroyed the last hope of our ever recovering the Holy Sepulchre", "context_len": 74}}}, "shoddy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He was a dirty looking negro of medium size , dressed in a shoddy suit , common enough in appearance , but with a look of cunning in his small round eyes", "context_len": 32}}}, "belied": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "True , the Vizier 's son had fared worse than she , for that he was couched in the draught-house ; but he belied [ 401 ] the matter and put away that tribulation from his thought , of his fear lest he should lose his bride and his rank , [ 402 ] more by token that all the folk envied him his lot , for the much increase of honour it brought him , as also for the exceeding beauty and loveliness of the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 88}}}, "saloons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "There was a counter , such as is usually found in saloons , and Mr", "context_len": 15}}}, "hiccoughs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "But he was immediately put to confusion by a voice from behind the bar ; a voice interrupted by hiccoughs : Who \u2019 s callin \u2019 me ? Is it you , Pete ?", "context_len": 34}}}, "stupefied": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 40, "context": "Up went the smoke , the heather catching fire at once , in went the stick , and out came the bees in an angry swarm ; but something had gone wrong in the calculations , for instead of falling stupefied on to the grass , they flew unharmed through the smoke , and fell upon their tormentors with a buzz of indignation", "context_len": 63}}}, "contact": {"freq": 43, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 25, "context": "Carefully feeling with both hands , I discovered that I was standing in an angle , and right in the corner my hand came in contact with an object that , on inspection , proved to be a gun ; also , by the smoothness of the barrel I knew that it had recently been in use , there being no rust on the ironwork", "context_len": 65}}}, "inebriate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The inebriate paid his money , chuckled and took the box to one side , hugging it like a pet child , reached over and picked up the hatchet from inside the railing , and pried open the corner of the box", "context_len": 42}}}, "gulped": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I 'm an awful baby , my Pony , '' he gulped , `` but I ca n't help it", "context_len": 21}}}, "stuttered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Now you drink with me ! stuttered Jake , who was too befuddled to recognize the man who had treated him", "context_len": 21}}}, "befuddled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now you drink with me ! stuttered Jake , who was too befuddled to recognize the man who had treated him", "context_len": 21}}}, "teaspoonful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He poured out for himself a teaspoonful of whisky , but did not replenish Gerald \u2019 s glass , as Amsden was not likely to notice the omission", "context_len": 28}}}, "replenish": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "Once they halted at a filling station to replenish their supply of gasoline , but the man in charge of the supply tank was kept adroitly in ignorance of the fact that there was a prisoner in the tonneau", "context_len": 39}}}, "omission": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "He poured out for himself a teaspoonful of whisky , but did not replenish Gerald \u2019 s glass , as Amsden was not likely to notice the omission", "context_len": 28}}}, "extravagantly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Bradley had paid Mosely in his own coin , and boasted of his prowess even more extravagantly than that braggadocio , claiming to have killed from seventy to eighty men in the course of his experience", "context_len": 36}}}, "champagne": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "I 've warned Harris against them time and again , but when they planned this afternoon to drive up to Nuneham in Harris 's trap for a champagne supper , I took to the scheme , and I had n't the moral courage to decline myself or to persuade Ted to do so", "context_len": 53}}}, "sipped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "After that he led her into the deanery -- those are rooms set aside for different uses in connection with the chapel -- and there he gave her a royal box of candies , and sat down and ate some with her , and tried to joke with her , and sipped a little wine , and then another long farewell , and he was gone , never to see the little Queen again", "context_len": 74}}}, "moderation": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "Religious , and yet allowing all harmless and innocent amusements both for Sundays and weekdays ; temperate , but permitting the use of beer and wine in moderation", "context_len": 28}}}, "presume": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 17, "context": "Now , suppose it were possible for any one to write a story about me ; I presume they would have to give a sort of girl 's name to the story ; but would that mean that it was all about girls ? Well , I guess not ; and Marie-Celeste laughed as she realized how wide such an estimate would fall of the mark", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Moreover , whoso presenteth himself before the Sultan and craveth favours of him , it behoveth him take in his hand somewhat that sorteth with the royal dignity ; and as I said to thee , how canst thou presume to present thyself before the Sultan and seek of him his daughter , without aught thou mayst proffer him of that which sorteth with his rank ? ''", "context_len": 68}}}, "sicken": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 125, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Does not your heart sicken for Lucknow ? \u2019", "context_len": 9}}}, "solicitous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "When they were within a few rods of his home , Gerald , impatient and always solicitous about the invalid , ran forward , leaving Mr", "context_len": 26}}}, "significantly": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 61, "context": "She used to study with Madame de Coucy in the mornings and go for walks among the flowers out in the garden there in the afternoon , and way beyond it too sometimes , and Richard would often come down from London for a visit , and he taught her English courtly ways and to play the mandolin ( Albert looked significantly toward the quaint mandolin , with a faded blue ribbon attached to it , that was lying among the other treasures on the table ) ; and when the King could not come for a regular visit , he would just ride down for a word and kiss", "context_len": 110}}}, "harshly": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "You are laboring under a delusion , boy ! said Wentworth harshly", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 65, "context": "Windsor surrendered to a blast of trumpets , and a few days later the little Queen was yielded up a captive into Henry 's hands , and was carried with faithful Simonette , her Saracen maid , to the Castle of Ledes ; but Ledes , fortunately , proved to be a beautiful castle , with a large garden , and she was not treated harshly or unkindly", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "He did n't like to judge the trader harshly , but it did seem as if he was quite indifferent in the matter", "context_len": 23}}}, "attributed": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 49, "context": "M\u00fcller cast about in his mind as to why this should be so , and he discovered that whilst he had asked God for money to open the Home and for helpers , he had forgotten to pray that the children might be sent ; and to this he attributed such a strange occurrence", "context_len": 54}}}, "doubting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Will you tell it to us , Marie-Celeste ? And Marie-Celeste , nothing loath , and willing at last to substantiate her claims in the ears of doubting Albert , rested a hand comfortably on either arm of her chair , and commenced , preceding her narration with the request , You will correct me , wo n't you , if you find I do not tell it right ? to which Her Majesty smilingly acceded , first asking Miss Belmore to hand her a little jewelled miniature case from among the other treasures on the table", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "And then when they reached the cottage , there stood dear old Mis , Hartley , looking the picture of motherliness in her snow-white cap and kerchief ; and the welcome that she gave Donald made him feel beyond all doubting that he had but exchanged one dear home for another ; and that meant worlds to a boy who had come to know for the first time what a dear place home might be", "context_len": 75}}}, "recourse": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "Wentworth eyed Gerald , doubting whether he should not seize him then and there and wrest from him the paper if he proved to have it , but there was something in the resolute look of the boy that daunted him , man though he was , and he decided that it would be better to have recourse to a little strategy", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "used by him and ( unable to obtain a more perfect copy ) he seems to have endeavoured to supply the place of the missing portions by incorporating in his translation a number of Persian , Turkish and Arabic Tales , which had no connection with his original and for which it is generally supposed that he probably had recourse to Oriental MSS", "context_len": 63}}}, "moderating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Look here , Gerald , he said , moderating his tone and moving further away , as if all thoughts of violence had left him , I will have a few plain words with you", "context_len": 35}}}, "compromising": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "Good Parson Perrient had painted Providence plantation as a sort of paradise , where the liberty and toleration dreamt of by a few in England were the law for all ; but was that refuge open to him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "molehill": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "I remember that I was in a little trouble , and being young made a mountain out of a molehill", "context_len": 20}}}, "uttermost": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "And so this portfolio , extended to its uttermost , was literally stuffed with pictures ; and what did they discover , to their surprised delight , lying right on the top of the pile , but three or four unmistakable photographs of Harry and Dorothy Allyn , which had evidently been placed there by design", "context_len": 56}}}, "farthing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "If you do n't leave the room at once I must just put you out I may as well tell you plainly that I do n't believe a word you say , and not one farthing will you ever get from me", "context_len": 42}}}, "integrity": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "Others suppose that the act was an expression of his implicit faith in the integrity and fidelity of his servant , and that he intended it as testimony , given in a very pointed and decisive , and , at the same time , delicate manner , that he was not suspicious of his friends , or easily led to distrust their faithfulness", "context_len": 63}}}, "impugn": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "He praised Parmenio , who had been Philip 's greatest general , and began to impugn the justice of his late condemnation and death", "context_len": 24}}}, "perverseness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Wentworth was not a patient man , and the boy \u2019 s perverseness , as he called it , provoked him , and brought out his sterner and more disagreeable qualities", "context_len": 31}}}, "provoked": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` She provoked us , hateful old thing ! I know I ought not to say that about the rain , Aunt Bessie , 'cause it 's God 's rain , and he can send it if he chooses ; but it was not her business to meddle about , and I am a great deal more sorry for your speaking so kind than for all the scolding", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I will let you do just what you choose with any thing of mine , Bessie , and not be a bit provoked", "context_len": 23}}}, "blackmailer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "It is a delusion of his and yours , returned Wentworth , I can only look upon your attitude as that of a blackmailer", "context_len": 24}}}, "blackmail": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "If a man owed you money , and you presented your claim , would you consider it blackmail ?", "context_len": 19}}}, "enmity": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 52, "context": "Bomba could but remember that on his onetime visit to the Araos tribe , when his life had been in danger from the suspicion of Hondura and his braves , it had been little Pirah who had stood between him and a horrible fate , and by her innocent friendship had changed enmity to confidence and trust", "context_len": 57}}}, "approves": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "I shall do what I consider right , and if my own conscience approves I shall seek no other approval", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "The council appoints the Great Warrior , approves or rejects the nominations for a m\u00edko 's assistant , and gives advice in law , war or peace questions", "context_len": 28}}}, "repelling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "The Thracian forces had assembled in the defiles , with other troops from the northern countries , to arrest Alexander 's march , and he had some difficulty in repelling them", "context_len": 31}}}, "resistance": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 58, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "conveyance": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 31, "context": "When the time came , the roads being especially bad with the heavy rains , Miss Tucker performed her journey from Amritsar to Batala in what she called a most luxurious conveyance , -- the big , heavy Government d\u00e2k gari , [ 64 ] in which one can recline at ease , as if in a bed", "context_len": 58}}}, "dependence": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` There is a tiny room which you could have next to mine , and we could have the doors open , and I promise to be awfully careful of you , if you really like it best , '' said Irene , who felt more and more charmed at the dependence of this small creature", "context_len": 56}}}, "desolation": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 47, "context": "Their father had wished to keep this secret , but they begged so to go and see the gardens , that he thought it best to take them and let them know the worst at once ; and they were so astonished when they saw the utter desolation of their own beds , and the difference between them and those which lay around , and asked so many questions , that he was obliged to tell them", "context_len": 77}}}, "tourist": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 2, "context": "The English tourist had been awakened by the discussion , and comprehending from what he heard that Gerald was in a tight place , he hastily threw on his clothes , and at the right time darted out of the cabin , seized Jake by the collar with one hand while with the other he planted a blow in his face , nearly stunned him , and dragging him from Gerald hurled him forcibly upon the ground six feet away", "context_len": 80}}}, "suicide": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "He was , however , now convinced , by the terror which Dymnus had manifested , and by his suicide , that all was true , and he asked Alexander 's pardon for not having taken immediate measures for communicating promptly the information he had received", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "To rough it , \u2019 to brave the climate , to be reckless of hardships , would in the majority of instances be tantamount to suicide", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But he realized at once that this would be suicide", "context_len": 10}}}, "particle": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 41, "context": "Two men appointed to that office then bring flowers of `` old man 's tobacco , '' \u00edsti atch\u00fali p\u00e1kpagi , prepared on the first day of the busk , in a pan to the m\u00edko 's cabin , and a particle of it is given to every person present", "context_len": 50}}}, "palatable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "It is on light , frothy , palatable morsels that I and my wife will live in the future , eh , eh ? You 're pleased with the look of the place , ai n't you", "context_len": 37}}}, "relished": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "An aching void ! sneered Piper , who had not relished Cooper \u2019 s words or manner", "context_len": 17}}}, "obliging": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 101, "context": "younger , extreme carelessness had been her besetting fault , and , as is the manner of this `` little fox , '' had created much trouble for herself and for others ; but having become convinced that it was her duty to cure herself of this , she had set to work to do it in such earnest that that which had been a burden and a care to her was fast becoming a settled habit , and it was but seldom now that any act or word of heedlessness could be laid to her charge , while her ever obliging disposition and loving heart prompted many a deed of kindness which she never failed to carry out if it were in her power to do so", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "While we were waiting , the bailiff , who was now most civil and obliging , placed in my hands an object that had been found in Joyce \u2019 s pocket", "context_len": 31}}}, "prolonged": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 5, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "Though I was then happily unaware of the fact , the period of the great annual festival , or Customs , was approaching , and the joy of the populace began to find vent in nocturnal concerts inordinately prolonged , the musical instruments consisting of tom-toms , each beaten by two , three , or four performers -- according to the size of the tom- tom -- with a monotony of cadence that soon became positively maddening , further aggravated by the discordant squealing of a number of flageolet- like instruments made of stout reeds", "context_len": 95}}}, "brooke": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 41, "context": "Their chosen retreat was the precincts of the old castle -- old even then -- for it had been once a British stronghold , commanding the route of the Phoenician tin merchants across the island , whence its name `` Caer brooke , '' or the `` fort on the stream", "context_len": 51}}}, "royally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Well , you \u2019 ll be entertained so royally here , Motor Matt , that you \u2019 ll forget all the unpleasant things that have happened to you", "context_len": 28}}}, "settee": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "He ate a little himself , for he had been up long enough to have a good appetite , and seated himself on the settee in front of the cabin", "context_len": 30}}}, "clearness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "From his seat he could see at a distance of twenty-five miles the snowy top of Pike \u2019 s Peak , looking on account of the clearness of the atmosphere not more than five miles away", "context_len": 36}}}, "intermediate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "You asked me to tell you of anything she ever needs ; and I think you may like to know that she has no intermediate dress for everyday use ; nothing between the dark green cashmere and a very pale kind of Chinese silk", "context_len": 44}}}, "wooded": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was a beautiful little sheet of water that lay there at her feet , with its densely wooded banks and its wilderness still uninvaded by civilization ; and just across the lake the setting sun was crimsoning the chimneys and pointed gables of the only house upon that farther bank", "context_len": 51}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The annalist P\u00e9nicaut , who visited these parts in 1704 , states that the nine villages were situated in a delightful country , swarming with buffaloes , drained by rivulets and partly wooded", "context_len": 33}}}, "apprehensions": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "Father Cuthbert , seated in a corner near the Yule log , with his brother-in-law and the Etheling , forgot all his apprehensions , and shared in the universal joy around him ; if his thoughts were sometimes with those who had once made Christmas bright to him -- if he thought of the bright-haired Bertric , who had been the soul of last Yuletide festivity at Aescendune , or of the desolated home there , he dismissed the subject from his mind at once , and suffered no hint to drop which could dim the mirth of his fellow guests", "context_len": 101}}}, "peacefulness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "In the strange peacefulness which brooded over her she walked home between the pi\u00f1on-sprinkled hills , where doves were crooning and the far bleating of an upland herd echoed among the barren ridges", "context_len": 33}}}, "contemplation": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "Many facts therein given have been rejected by the Author as too harrowing in their nature ; and he has preferred to render the contemplation of woe and suffering less painful , by a display of those virtues of patience , resignation , and brave submission to the Divine will , which affliction never fails to bring out in the fold of Christ , whose promise stands ever fast , that the strength of His people shall be equal to their needs", "context_len": 82}}}, "hostile": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "But more incomprehensible than the existence of so bad a man in such a dark age is the renewed confidence ever accorded him , when , after more than once betraying the armies of his country into the hands of their foes , and fighting openly in the hostile cause , he is again forgiven , nay , received into favour , and sent once more to command the men he has already deceived , until he repeats the experiment , and when it fails is again admitted into confidence", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 45, "context": "It is a serious undertaking to get a great body of men and horses across a broad and rapid river , when the people of the country have done all in their power to remove or destroy all possible means of transit , and when hostile bands are on the opposite bank , to embarrass and impede the operations by every mode in their power", "context_len": 65}}}, "earshot": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "But it was too late now to retreat , for Donald was having an animated talk with the buttons of the door of the Reform Club ; and Ted , who stood just out of earshot , was the victim of all sorts of uncomfortable sensations as to what the result might be", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "For so long indeed as her footsteps echoed in the passage her pupils obeyed her order , but the moment she might reasonably be believed to be out of earshot a low murmur began among the little heads bent so discreetly over the arithmetic books", "context_len": 45}}}, "malevolence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Oh , you \u2019 re puttin \u2019 on frills , are you ? retorted Jake , halting where he stood , and eying the boy with evident malevolence", "context_len": 28}}}, "sinister": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 5, "context": "The arrangement somehow had a sinister , suggestive appearance that made me feel vaguely uncomfortable ; and abandoning the intention , whatever it may have been , that took me to the spot , I returned to my house , and , as soon as Ama made her appearance , asked her what it meant", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "Assured as he was that the Indians of whom he had caught glimpses during the afternoon , and the reports of whose guns he had heard not far away , were drawing near the camp with the most sinister designs , he set out to checkmate them so far as it was possible", "context_len": 53}}}, "jackanapes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` This is no laughing matter , you pack of jackanapes , '' bawled the now infuriated ship 's officer", "context_len": 20}}}, "shan": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 38, "context": "If I had the chance I \u2019 d shoot that confounded \u2019 Ceph ! he exclaimed ; if a horse knew how to grin , how he is grinning over the youngster he fooled ! Well , I shan \u2019 t bother with you , that \u2019 s certain", "context_len": 49}}}, "akimbo": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "She had arranged the dishes and placed the old chair at the table , after which , as had become habitual with her on rising from the wash-tub , she wiped her hands on her apron and rested them on her hips , her arms akimbo", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Ana , with her hands on her broad hips , short and thick as a squat brown jug with its handles akimbo , -- Ana , with her great clay-colored face and tiny , glittering eyes , with her thick , pale lips and coarse , black hair , -- surely none but a mother could view in Ana such charms as bedewed Se\u00f1ora Vigil 's eyes only to think of !", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "She looked at the solitary lamp with its grotesquely hideous ornament of red flannel , at Susan 's expressionless , freckled face , at the boys in their copper-toed boots and overalls , at the good-natured , but hopelessly common-place Martha Spriggs , with her thin hair drawn tight into a knob the size of a bullet , and her bare arms akimbo", "context_len": 63}}}, "thrash": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "Vaughan laughed so much that he had not the heart to thrash the little rogue , but next morning he devised a punishment for him which Peggy declared was far worse", "context_len": 31}}}, "anticipated": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 27, "context": "Hartley personally , he sought him out , where he was at work in a corner of the meadow , and the result , as he had anticipated , was a very plain talk -- so unsparingly and pointedly plain that Harry winced a good deal in the process , and once or twice came near resenting a mode of procedure that seemed very much akin to knocking a fellow when he 's down", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Perhaps to David the anticipated change meant as much as to anyone , for he was growing too old to seek a new master , and dreaded the inevitable time when he would be shelved from work , and placed on the parish list , to the self-respecting poor always the bitterest sting of old age", "context_len": 56}}}, "incredulous": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "At first they were almost incredulous when I told them that every ship of the pirate fleet had been either destroyed or captured , and that the fleet in the offing consisted entirely of your galley and the thirteen corsairs she had captured", "context_len": 43}}}, "wuss": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Every time I git sorter billious and downcast an \u2019 dejec \u2019 ed I look \u2019 round till I find somebody that \u2019 s wuss off than I be , an \u2019 then I take holt an \u2019 try to give them a lift , an \u2019 that cheers me up an \u2019 makes me feel thankful an \u2019 content with my lot", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "He \u2019 s been up to things wuss \u2019 n that , and his father before him was a jailbird", "context_len": 20}}}, "eluding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "For a time he succeeded in eluding the grasp of his antagonist , and the delay only infuriated Amsden the more", "context_len": 21}}}, "antagonist": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 23, "context": "The knowledge thus acquired , it was believed , imparted to the person a full conjuring power and charmer 's influence over the antagonist , and enabled him to conquer the hostile warriors at a distance ( hup\u00e1-i ) and before reaching them , or to make them come near enough for easy capture", "context_len": 54}}}, "pinioned": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 59, "context": "Even if he had had an open sharp knife in his hand he could hardly have cut himself free , and in the horror of those brief moments he found that his struggles were sending him deeper and deeper , and that unconsciously he had wound himself still farther in the net , till his arms and legs were pinioned in the cold , slimy bonds , which clung to and wrapped round him more and more", "context_len": 77}}}, "comprehending": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "The English tourist had been awakened by the discussion , and comprehending from what he heard that Gerald was in a tight place , he hastily threw on his clothes , and at the right time darted out of the cabin , seized Jake by the collar with one hand while with the other he planted a blow in his face , nearly stunned him , and dragging him from Gerald hurled him forcibly upon the ground six feet away", "context_len": 80}}}, "nonchalantly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 67, "context": "Certainly it is true that the thief might have retained the missing sum , but does it not seem remarkable that he should have done so and left behind him in his flight the larger amount hidden in that room ? It is likewise true that the beforesaid Davis might have learned from the before-mentioned Hayden just what sum of money was recovered , but , being nonchalantly questioned regarding this , he denied it", "context_len": 75}}}, "ceremoniously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Allow me to introduce myself , said the Englishman , raising his hat as ceremoniously as if he were addressing a Chicago millionaire", "context_len": 23}}}, "floored": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "Why , Ben ! exclaimed his father in angry amazement , what \u2019 s got into yer ? Hev you been drinkin \u2019 ? Why , you can \u2019 t fight more \u2019 n an old cow ! To be floored by a little chap like that !", "context_len": 48}}}, "powerfully": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 3, "context": "A tall , powerfully built man , clad in buckskin and carrying a rifle , followed by two young men , slighter in figure , but quite as tall , strode from the woods , and halted when they caught sight of Gerald and his companion", "context_len": 46}}}, "overwhelm": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` But what ? Is it a noble impulse which will spring to life in rich gratitude when I tell her my story ? Or will a mere hurt , passionate vanity rise to overwhelm us all in its acrid swell ? I shall soon know", "context_len": 46}}}, "unprepared": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "It could only mean that an attack of unusual fierceness was being planned ; and so , that it might not find him unprepared , he cast an eye round the fort to see if he could strengthen it in any way", "context_len": 42}}}, "prudence": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 40, "context": "We see the same analogy reigning at the present day , when the sons of the wealthy , who are born to fortune , substitute pride , and arrogance , and vicious self-indulgence and waste for the modesty , and prudence , and virtue of their sires , by means of which the fortune was acquired", "context_len": 56}}}, "unruffled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "At the foot of the slope lay a small lake , its unruffled surface reflecting with startling distinctness the foliage that lined its shores , and the two white clouds that hung above in the blue sky", "context_len": 37}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Still unruffled , Piper proceeded :", "context_len": 6}}}, "reg": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "It kep \u2019 him from workin \u2019 reg \u2019 ler an \u2019 kinder sp \u2019 iled his prospects , poor man ! an \u2019 left me in straightened circumstances when he passed away", "context_len": 33}}}, "lar": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "I say , stranger , you \u2019 re a reg \u2019 lar steam en gine", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Well , I ain \u2019 t partic \u2019 lar , replied the wiry fellow , who despite his youthful looks , was a veteran of the plains ; I expected to ride , if anything happened to Dick , but this young chap seems to have set his heart on it and I don \u2019 t want to spoil his fun", "context_len": 61}}}, "arbitrate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I \u2019 ll arbitrate between you", "context_len": 6}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Leading Bostonians often consulted him about public affairs , or asked him to arbitrate disputes", "context_len": 15}}}, "unfeigned": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "He was interrupted by a childish voice , and saw Herstan 's little son , a boy of twelve years , touching his garment , and looking at him with unfeigned admiration", "context_len": 32}}}, "rightful": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "Why should he be in the jungle , he a white boy , whose rightful heritage of a life with his own kind had been denied him by a cruel fate ? Why did he not have a home like that of Frank Parkhurst , a father who was proud of him , a mother who loved him ? Why had he been fated to have his life placed every day in jeopardy ? He had been cheated of what belonged to him equally with every other boy of the white race", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "No , I promise , Albert ; and Margaret coming in just then to announce luncheon , the blue garter was surreptitiously removed and left for the time being on the library table , and was not thought of again by its rightful owner", "context_len": 44}}}, "squire": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 38, "context": "There the sentries , who were stationed at the door , had some ado to keep back the curious throng , and many a sturdy country farmer shouldered his way into the house in the wake of his squire to catch a glimpse of his king , the ill-fated King Charles , who was to rest that night at Farnham on his last journey from the prison at Hurst Castle to the scaffold at Whitehall", "context_len": 75}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "I \u2019 ll do it , squire", "context_len": 7}}}, "wallets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "All the necessary preparations for departure were shortly made -- the theows and ceorls were collected together , beasts of burden selected to carry the necessary baggage , the wallets filled with provisions", "context_len": 33}}}, "asseverated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "He had never , he asseverated , spoken or written to any one concerning this , save herself ; if he had done so it would have been to his indulgent uncle , Colonel Rush , to whom he would have applied", "context_len": 42}}}, "compensate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "As I \u2019 ve knocked you down twice , perhaps it is only fair to compensate you for affording me such a chance for healthful exercise", "context_len": 26}}}, "affording": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "We now had leisure to observe that the storm had so far passed away that there were big breaks in the canopy of cloud overhead and away to the eastward , through which the stars were beginning to show themselves , affording enough light just to enable us to discern the two banks of the stream , but not sufficient to betray our presence to an observer at a greater distance than , say , a quarter of a mile", "context_len": 80}}}, "healthful": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Full of healthful energy , it was a relief to her to help the solitary maid in her household work ; that was the least dull part of her new life ; and , in the kitchen , the Queen of Hunstanton could still rule imperiously over the old cowman , and make the dairywoman tremble before her royal displeasure", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "She noticed how fresh and rosy he looked after his healthful , unbroken slumbers , and she said to herself that a little more sleep would do him still greater good", "context_len": 31}}}, "coins": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 93, "context": "A cupboard in the corner was devoted to a kind of museum , where they kept their collection of birds ' eggs , a few butterflies , moths , and beetles , Lilian 's pressed wild-flowers , a box full of shells and fossils which they had brought home from their one never-to-be-forgotten visit to the seaside , a curious plaited basket filled with bone rings , shell bracelets , and other curiosities , sent by a sailor cousin from the South Sea Islands , and an odd assortment of stones , old coins , foreign stamps , crests and postmarks , which represented landmarks in the history of past fads", "context_len": 111}}}, "insinuatingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Then , as he turned , a sudden idea struck him , and he asked insinuatingly : Wouldn \u2019 t you like to knock me down ag \u2019 in , stranger ?", "context_len": 32}}}, "ag": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "Shagbark tole me how dem critters chaw and stomp and bite a feller ; I knowed dat if I brunged down dis one , it would make de oder two so mad dey \u2019 d come at me afore I could load up ag \u2019 in , and you wouldn \u2019 t hab any Jeth any more", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Course , concluded the officer , we give the feller warnin \u2019 that anything he said might be used as evidence ag \u2019 in \u2019 him , and I ruther guess he kept it in mind ; for , \u2019 though we talked with him considerable on the way back to Oakdale , he didn \u2019 t make no slip-ups , and he pertended all the time not to know nothin \u2019 at all \u2019 bout the robbery", "context_len": 78}}}, "brightened": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 37, "context": "Harris had said to her more than once , that there 's nothing but sin itself has so many heavy hearts to answer for as thoughtlessness ; and thoughtfulness , next to love , has lightened and brightened more hearts than anything else in the world and Marie-Celeste knew how thoughtless she had been to press home upon Harold in any way a keener sense of his own great loss", "context_len": 70}}}, "hospitably": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Partly from curiosity , the permission was given , and Ben , in plain , simple language , told the story of how they had received Mosely and Hadley hospitably , and awoke in the morning to find that they had stolen their horses", "context_len": 44}}}, "woolen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "I am , as you will judge from my appearance , a working-man , and have ever since I attained my majority been employed in woolen mills", "context_len": 27}}}, "accommodate": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 67, "context": "And now we were to welcome the well-known and gifted authoress into our house ! This was a privilege ; and earnestly did we look forward to the pleasure of receiving her ; though at the same time we were perhaps conscious of a slight shadow of doubt crossing our minds , as to how far one of Miss Tucker \u2019 s age would be able to accommodate herself to the new surroundings , and bear the trials incident to life and work in a tropical climate", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "But if our Church is to go on growing , as we hope and pray that it may , what would be the advantage of having a tiny chapel , which would not comfortably accommodate ourselves in a fiery climate , and in which there would be no room at all for heathen spectators ? We should be wanting a second ; and how could we procure a second clergyman ? Please thank dear Fred very , very much for his kindness in collecting , and assure him that we wish to make the money go as far as possible", "context_len": 100}}}, "maintain": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 67, "context": "So soon as the news of the death of Ethelred travelled abroad , the bishops , abbots , ealdormen , and thanes of southern England , despairing of the cause of the house of Cerdic , met together at Southampton , and renouncing Ethelred and his descendants , elected Canute to be their king , while he swore that both in things spiritual and temporal he would maintain their liberties", "context_len": 70}}}, "schemed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Though I did not expect it he schemed a revenge", "context_len": 10}}}, "instituted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 86, "context": "She was frequently in and out , as often as three or four times a day , and very seldom indeed less than twice ; moreover , she seemed exceedingly anxious to become my instructress in her own language , and as I had already felt heavily handicapped on several occasions by my inability to converse freely with those around me I made no demur , although I must confess that I at length began to view with vague disquietude the extreme freedom of intercourse thus instituted by the young woman", "context_len": 91}}}, "resulted": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 63, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}}}, "attested": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` The gleeman ! the false gleeman the Etheling Edmund ! '' in various energetic cries , attested his fame , and the hatred of his foes", "context_len": 27}}}, "asserted": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 10, "context": "We are going to have a fine day , Albert asserted , resuming his seat on the steps , and with the authority of one who knows ; and the matter of the weather being thus satisfactorily disposed of , Marie-Celeste made so bold as to introduce another subject ; and as it chanced to meet with Albert 's approval , they chatted merrily together for ever so long", "context_len": 69}}}, "imprisonment": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 37, "context": "The boys ' gardens had suffered some , but not so much as those of the little girls ; whether it was that Dolly fancied Maggie and Bessie had been the most to blame for Lem 's imprisonment , and so chose first to revenge herself on them ; whether it was that their gardens lay nearer to her hand and she had been interrupted in her wicked work before she had quite destroyed the boys ' , -- could not be known", "context_len": 83}}}, "shadowed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "By this time , Your Honor , I was absolutely convinced that I was on the right trail , and thenceforth I shadowed the suspect with the persistence of a bloodhound , never once letting him escape from beneath my hawklike eye", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "'It is terrible to see you suffer so , my lady ! ' she cried , one morning , when , in the fulness of her strength , she had gone from the laughing sunshine into the shadowed room , where every ray of light fell like a blow upon the invalid 's quivering nerves", "context_len": 55}}}, "assert": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 84, "context": "Can he speak thus who hath so long espoused The Royal cause , and served that cause so well ? Who , girt with honours , well deserved , hath stood One in a noble Brotherhood of Fame ! Where are the Cavaliers who fought with thee In battle , side by side , who with thee shared The feast , and drained the wine-cup to your King ? Where are they now ? what , gone ? not one remains , T \u2019 assert thy innocence , or shield thee from An ignominious death", "context_len": 95}}}, "hounded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "I found employment elsewhere , but was hounded down again , and by the same man", "context_len": 16}}}, "denouncing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "But before denouncing me , he came to me , and offered to keep silent if I would pay him a hundred and fifty dollars", "context_len": 25}}}, "treacherous": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 45, "context": "It seems to me a hare brained scheme , lad , Sir John , who had just joined him , said , as they issued from the auberge ; though I own , from what the bailiff tells me , that there must be some treacherous plot on hand , and when that is the case it is necessary that it should be probed to the bottom", "context_len": 67}}}, "divulged": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "Filelfo , who was greatly attached to D'Aubusson , saw by their manner that they wished to engage him in some intrigue , and , feigning great resentment and anger at his disgrace , led them on until they divulged the entire plot for D'Aubusson 's assassination , and made brilliant offers to him if he would afford them facilities for carrying it out , producing , in proof of their power to do so , a letter of the pasha , authorising them to make such promises in his name", "context_len": 91}}}, "treachery": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 25, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "This ill-omened anniversary we sang dirge and mass for the souls of those who were slain by treachery seven years ago", "context_len": 21}}}, "extort": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The torture had not been , as was then the usual custom , applied to extort information ; partly because his story was probable , still more because the grand master and council did not wish that more publicity should be given to the affair , and were glad that it should be allowed to drop without any further trial of the delinquents", "context_len": 63}}}, "bribe": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 40, "context": "Nevertheless , mine enemies found it an easy thing to bring witnesses to swear I was seen in Venner 's company , and pressed hard on my hiding-place ; so seeing I was not wealthy enough or easy enough to bribe their witnesses to refrain from lies , I e'en fled , and have the hue and cry after me for a dangerous plotter", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "They did not , however , seem willing , either of them , to trust to an impartial decision of the question , but each offered the judge a bribe to induce him to decide in her favor", "context_len": 38}}}, "unmerciful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Then at last , in my despair and anger , I lay in wait for Haynes , and gave him an unmerciful beating until he roared for mercy", "context_len": 28}}}, "persistently": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` From the moment when I first became aware of my father 's illness I was not entirely free from suspicion ; and when at length I saw that your efforts to cure him were only partially successful , and that his symptoms persistently recurred , I was convinced that there was foul play somewhere , though why , I could not at first imagine", "context_len": 65}}}, "condemning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "They therefore remained firm in their original simplicity and integrity , and after some respectful but ineffectual remonstrances , they stood aloof , alienated from their commander in heart , and condemning very strongly , among themselves , his wickedness and folly", "context_len": 42}}}, "untenanted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "The woods near the shore were first searched , but were found untenanted", "context_len": 13}}}, "expose": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` Margaret , '' she said , severely , `` it is not possible that you mean to add to your folly by letting your children go to that low place , after such weather as we have had ! You do n't know what you may expose them to , especially that delicate child , whom you can never expect to be strong while you are so shamefully careless of her ; '' and she looked at Bessie , who felt very angry", "context_len": 84}}}, "incriminate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "He was the man who stole the wallet and put it in my trunk to incriminate me", "context_len": 17}}}, "misfortunes": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 56, "context": "The road which ran across the river at this spot continued southward into Wessex , and , so far as we could learn , was free from danger , so I determined to send my brethren to Abingdon by easy stages along its course , while I turned back with Father Adhelm , to share the misfortunes of my kindred and lay brethren in the woods", "context_len": 66}}}, "boxing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "In the center was piled a collection of baseball bats , tennis racquets , boxing gloves , foils , catching gloves , a football , some running trousers , a couple of sweaters , and a nondescript collection of books", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "A battalion picture-theatre , billiard rooms , voluntary swimming parades , boxing , and a variety of other indoor and outdoor games contributed to the men 's enjoyment ; and , although discipline was well enforced , there was a total absence of irritating petty restrictions that form a constant source of annoyance to the men of the New Armies of the Motherland", "context_len": 63}}}, "tenant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "His office was in a little ell part in a rather inviting looking house , and he took his meals with the tenant", "context_len": 23}}}, "mover": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was the first time in many years that he had seen the man who was the prime mover in the events that had brought about his financial ruin", "context_len": 29}}}, "blighted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "That false accusation of yours has blighted my life", "context_len": 9}}}, "unjustly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 60, "context": "Though it is complete in itself , and may be read independently , the chief characters introduced will be recognized as old friends by the readers of `` The Young Explorer , '' the volume just preceding , not omitting Ki Sing , the faithful Chinaman , whose virtues may go far to diminish the prejudice which , justly or unjustly , is now felt toward his countrymen", "context_len": 68}}}, "exonerating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "His orders were obeyed , and after asking questions as to details he wrote out a confession exonerating John Carter and laying the blame on the right party", "context_len": 28}}}, "subscribe": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The Native Christians not unfrequently subscribe to give a parting gift to a Missionary whom they love , when starting for England ; but I suppose they thought that , in my case , if they waited for that they would never give me anything , and that it was no harm to present me with something for not going away ! Mr", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Station-people subscribe to help in the distribution of this food", "context_len": 10}}}, "affixed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Indeed , he never unpursed them until that same letter was sealed and directed and the stamp affixed with a very determined little air , as though firmly resolved that the thing he had done should brook no undoing", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "An hour passed away ; the body remained affixed to the tree ; the night grew darker , and the hour approached when , under ordinary circumstances , people retired to rest , and the band commenced its preparations for carrying out the attack upon Aescendune", "context_len": 46}}}, "persecute": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "Carter , said Noel Brooke , as he handed him the paper , keep this carefully , and whenever that scoundrel who has made it his business to persecute you engages again in the same work you can show this document , and it will be a satisfactory answer to his base charges", "context_len": 53}}}, "engages": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Carter , said Noel Brooke , as he handed him the paper , keep this carefully , and whenever that scoundrel who has made it his business to persecute you engages again in the same work you can show this document , and it will be a satisfactory answer to his base charges", "context_len": 53}}}, "untarnished": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Now I shall be able to leave Oscar an untarnished name !", "context_len": 12}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "Our share is but in the present , and I trust the flag of the Order will float over Rhodes as long , at least , as the lifetime of the youngest of us , and that we may bequeath the duty of upholding the Cross untarnished to those who come after us ; and we can then leave the issue in God 's hands", "context_len": 65}}}, "agency": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` This horsepath began at the Rock Landing on Ok\u00f3ni river , a British post just below Wilkinson and about four miles below Milledgeville , Georgia , passed Fort Hawkins built upon the Okm\u00falgi old fields , then the site of Macon , on the shore opposite , then Knoxville , then the old Creek agency on Flint river , then crossed Patsil\u0101\u00edka creek , the usual ford on Chatahuchi river lying between Kas\u00ed ta and Apat\u00e1-i Creek", "context_len": 78}}}, "crafty": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "Meanwhile the crafty Edric , who excused himself from attendance on the solemnities , tarried at Oxenford , and with him tarried also Elfwyn , Herstan , and the other friends of the unfortunate prisoner , to secure , as they were able , that justice should be rendered him", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Be on your guard , '' he said , `` as men who know that the welfare of England depends upon them ; if you see the least movement on the part of the crafty Canute , rouse the camp at once ; they are not unlikely to attack us by night if they can surprise us , not otherwise", "context_len": 61}}}, "institute": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Clay MacCauley in 1880 visited the Seminoles settled in the southern parts of the peninsula , to take their census and institute ethnologic researches", "context_len": 24}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Are you going to institute proceedings against this boy ? If you are , he will be placed under bonds , and I shall become his bondsman", "context_len": 27}}}, "subjoined": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "In the comparative table subjoined I have received only such terms that answer to this requisite", "context_len": 16}}}, "remonstrate": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 61, "context": "To do Peggy justice , I think she was right , for though she delighted in outdoor life , she was in no sense a rough or ill-mannered child , and loved pretty things and dainty ways as well as quieter Lilian ; but it was a case of a dog with a bad name , for however indignantly she might remonstrate , people had got into the habit of dubbing her 'tomboy , ' and at that valuation she seemed likely to remain", "context_len": 84}}}, "estimate": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 26, "context": "You can read for yourself from the reproduction of the last page of his diary , written on the 14th December , 1884 , his own estimate of the length of time he could hold out ; and , though he managed to keep back the enemy for another month , yet on the 26th January , 1885 , whilst yet Sir Charles Wilson and the British troops were fighting their way up the river Nile to his relief , Khartoum fell", "context_len": 82}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "I soon found that travelling down-stream , with the current in our favour , was a very different matter from travelling up-stream against it ; and within the next twenty-four hours I was able to estimate that we were now proceeding about three times as rapidly as was the case when I was making the upward journey", "context_len": 57}}}, "exceeded": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 3, "context": "Nothing could have exceeded the air of importance with which Albert was striding along the streets of Windsor , and notwithstanding the shortness of his legs , his valet de chambre , in the shape of a newly acquired French nurse , had difficulty in keeping up with him", "context_len": 49}}}, "interpolated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "DEAR FATHER : I meant to write you last week but was too busy Not with your studies , I \u2019 ll be bound , interpolated his father besides there isn \u2019 t much to write about here", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "stuffy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I am sure I would learn more in that way than I shall from the stuffy books I am studying here under that old mummy , Dr", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "This done , we were sent on board a frigate and placed in a dark , stuffy hole below the waterline", "context_len": 21}}}, "tangent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "Victor has all the advantages that money can command , and a brilliant prospect for the future if he will only act in accordance with my wishes , and yet he is ready to start off at a tangent and roam round the world with some scapegrace companion", "context_len": 48}}}, "scapegrace": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "Besides , when all is said , Ted is Harold 's brother , so that , scapegrace or no , we ought not to deliberately turn our backs , at a time too when matters have reached a crisis , and one wonders how they will go with him", "context_len": 49}}}, "headstrong": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "I wish he were more like Lane \u2019 s boy I don \u2019 t like him , for he is obstinate and headstrong , and utterly unreasonable in his demands upon me , but he is steady and correct in his habits , and if he were in Victor \u2019 s place would never give me any uneasiness", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Overgrown and headstrong does n't sound very nice , Marie-Celeste said slowly ; I 'm really not a bit afraid about Harold -- I love him already , but I do n't feel sure about Ted , somehow", "context_len": 38}}}, "unreasonable": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 26, "context": "I wish he were more like Lane \u2019 s boy I don \u2019 t like him , for he is obstinate and headstrong , and utterly unreasonable in his demands upon me , but he is steady and correct in his habits , and if he were in Victor \u2019 s place would never give me any uneasiness", "context_len": 58}}}, "tribute": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 45, "context": "The Northern and Western papers praised the scout very highly , and some of them said that if there were more such men in the army the cause of the Union would progress more rapidly ; whereas the Southern papers , though paying a high tribute to the dash and courage of the scout , were highly abusive", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "They say the Danes have gone into winter quarters on the Thames , and that all the neighbourhood pays them tribute", "context_len": 21}}}, "illegible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was addressed in an almost illegible scrawl and appeared to be written by a person of defective education", "context_len": 19}}}, "scrawl": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 10, "context": "Then came the mournful news ; and a hasty short scrawl conveyed the first intimation of it from Charlotte Tucker to her niece , Leila \u2019 Hamilton ; a note without any formal beginning : --", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Bomba knew something of the history of Grico , and hope sprang in him anew as the giant caboclo made his way through the sullen group and peered at that faint scrawl upon the wall", "context_len": 35}}}, "defective": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "Then he asked Alaeddin , `` What is the cause that the lattice of yonder kiosk ( kushk ) is not complete ? '' The defective part is soon after referred to , no less than four times , as `` the lattice of the kiosk '' ( sheriyyetu ' l kushk ) , thus showing that , in the writer 's mind , kushk , liwan and shubbak were synonymous terms for the common Arab projecting square-sided window , made of latticework , and I have therefore rendered the three words , when they occur in this sense , by our English `` oriel , '' to whose modern meaning ( a window that juts out , so as to form a small apartment ) , they", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "`` So he came down forthright and went to meet the Sultan , who [ 529 ] said to him , when he saw him , `` Wherefore , O my son , hast thou done thus , and why sufferedst thou not the jewellers complete the lattice-work of the oriel , [ 530 ] so there might not remain a place in thy palace [ 531 ] defective ? '' `` O King of the Age , '' answered Alaeddin , `` I left it not imperfect but of my free will , nor did I lack of ableness to complete it", "context_len": 103}}}, "audacity": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "Three days after we left the island the Sea Wolf , for such is the name of the buccaneer \u2019 s ship , hove to in sight of Port Royal , and with the greatest audacity Pedro , or Red Peter , to give him the name he is generally known by , went ashore under a flag of truce , taking me with him as hostage", "context_len": 67}}}, "drakes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "You speak of me as a rich man , and I admit that you are correct in doing so , but I do not propose to have you make ducks and drakes of my money", "context_len": 35}}}, "authorize": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I have written him that I didn \u2019 t authorize your running up such a bill , and that he must be content with fifty per cent of it , or else go unpaid", "context_len": 34}}}, "unpaid": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "I have written him that I didn \u2019 t authorize your running up such a bill , and that he must be content with fifty per cent of it , or else go unpaid", "context_len": 34}}}, "reproduced": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 6, "context": "And much the same thought is reproduced in Charlotte Tucker \u2019 s own clever and amusing little book , My Neighbour \u2019 s Shoes , -- when , as Archie gazes into the mirror , he says of himself , One thing is evident ; as I can \u2019 t be admired for my beauty , I must make myself liked in some other way", "context_len": 65}}}, "influenza": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "He was not at recitations , but I received a note saying he was sick with the influenza", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "It is influenza", "context_len": 3}}}, "absented": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "When the next morning Victor also absented himself I sent around to his boarding-house , and learned that he and a school friend of his Arthur Grigson had not been seen for twenty-four hours", "context_len": 34}}}, "presumed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "I presumed the steamer was doomed , and wished to secure my small baggage , for I am a poor man and couldn \u2019 t afford to lose it", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "And now , by Percy 's confession , one part of the mystery was solved ; but there still remained that of Hannah 's presumed knowledge that he was in trouble and had been in sore need of money", "context_len": 39}}}, "premeditated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Whether the order to advance was premeditated , or given on the spur of the moment , few of the New Zealanders knew", "context_len": 23}}}, "clew": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 14, "context": "But we \u2019 ve run down the man who did it , from one clew to another , and the deputy sheriff \u2019 s got him in a wagon out here in the road in front of the house now", "context_len": 40}}}, "explains": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 1, "context": "Byington explains it by helping people , allies , in the Cha'hta ap\u0103l\u0103tchi \u00f3kla , but the original form of the name is Apala tchi \u00f3kli , not ap\u00e1latchi ; - tchi is a Hitchiti suffix of adjectives , and ap\u00e1lui in that dialect means `` on the other side of", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "All this is making very free with State belongings , and one of the guides , in charge of a small party of visitors , starts toward him in a decidedly menacing manner ; but Harold intercepts him and explains , and the guide , himself much amused , decides to leave unmolested this gallant little tar of Her Majesty 's", "context_len": 61}}}, "detective": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 61, "context": "She kept a severe eye on the small children , patted them firmly on the back when they choked , and refused to allow them to grab at the cake , regulating their tea according to her own notions of what was good for them , and turning stolen lumps of sugar out of their pockets with the cleverness of a detective", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 9, "context": "A golf caddy who has a leaning toward amateur detective work , together with his younger cousin , are accidentally mixed up in the strange loss , or theft , of a valuable diamond , known as Green Fire", "context_len": 39}}}, "predict": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "I predict that it won \u2019 t be long before you hear from him", "context_len": 14}}}, "interrogated": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 3, "context": "I had already interrogated the two churls ; their story was but too evidently true ; and I learned that they had discovered the footmarks of the Northmen in crossing a ford that afterwards , while returning hastily home , they stumbled upon them , and Alfgar and Bertric were taken", "context_len": 51}}}, "illiterate": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "It will be well to remember that in Indian and all illiterate languages the sounds of the same organ-class are interchangeable ; thus , a word may be correctly pronounced and written in six , ten , or twelve different ways", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The alternating processes observed here also occur in most other Indian and illiterate languages : tch , dsh alternate with ts , ds , h with k , ; g with the other gutturals , b with p , d with t , \u0101 with e , o with u", "context_len": 50}}}, "amanuensis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Bancroft reflected in alarm that he might be sick and unable to write for himself , and had employed an illiterate amanuensis", "context_len": 22}}}, "detect": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 64, "context": "barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me , died out , and I began to prepare myself , as best I could", "context_len": 128}}}, "postmarked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I noticed that it was postmarked in Colorado", "context_len": 8}}}, "vouchsafed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` decided ( or demanded ) that thou be bountiful to ( or grace ) me with ; '' but icledsa is here used in the colloquial sense of `` willed , vouchsafed", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "Alaeddin did that which behoveth and befitteth unto kings of obeisance and invocation and said to him , `` O our lord the Sultan , thy Grace 's munificence hath vouchsafed [ 464 ] to accord me the Lady Bedrulbudour thy daughter , albeit I am unworthy of this great favour , for that I am of the lowliest of thy slaves ; wherefore I beseech God that He keep and continue thee", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "When Alaeddin found himself released and saw the Sultan sitting , he went up to him and said to him , `` O my lord , since Thy Grace hath bountifully vouchsafed me my life , [ 577 ] favour me [ yet farther ] and tell me the manner of my offence", "context_len": 53}}}, "reproduce": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "The mysterious letter ( not to keep the reader in doubt ) was written by our old acquaintance Jake Amsden , and we will reproduce it here , correcting the orthography , which deviated considerably from the standards set by the best writers", "context_len": 43}}}, "correcting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "The mysterious letter ( not to keep the reader in doubt ) was written by our old acquaintance Jake Amsden , and we will reproduce it here , correcting the orthography , which deviated considerably from the standards set by the best writers", "context_len": 43}}}, "orthography": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 30, "context": "The mysterious letter ( not to keep the reader in doubt ) was written by our old acquaintance Jake Amsden , and we will reproduce it here , correcting the orthography , which deviated considerably from the standards set by the best writers", "context_len": 43}}}, "deviated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The mysterious letter ( not to keep the reader in doubt ) was written by our old acquaintance Jake Amsden , and we will reproduce it here , correcting the orthography , which deviated considerably from the standards set by the best writers", "context_len": 43}}}, "correspondent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "If an anxious correspondent offered a big price to the carrier to accept a thin missive after the pouch had been made up , he was refused and obliged to wait for the next messenger", "context_len": 35}}}, "coffers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Moreover , he brought him into a place , which he found full of coffers overflowing with royal raiment , such as ravished the wit , gold-inwoven stuffs , Indian and Chinese , and brocades , and he showed him also many other places , all full of that which beggareth description , till at last he brought him into a stable , wherein were horses whose like is not found with the kings of the world ; and therewithin he showed him a storehouse , full of housings and saddles of price , all broidered with pearls and precious stones and so forth", "context_len": 104}}}, "precipitous": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 13, "context": "They had been warned overnight that the coast beyond the cape was so precipitous , that there would be no villages at which to make inquiries , and arranged with Fosco that the ships should anchor north of the cape , and that he should go on at once to inspect the next bay", "context_len": 54}}}, "dismount": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "Well , this beats all , said Harold to himself ; what is going to happen next , I wonder ? But he had the graciousness to defer his own greeting to Uncle Everett until he assisted Aunt Lou and Dorothy and Marie-Celeste to dismount , by aid of the brake 's steps , and which much practice , by the way , enabled them to accomplish very skilfully", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I waited for him to come up and dismount , and then I bade him knock at the door , and when it was opened I told him to stand by the horses", "context_len": 33}}}, "observances": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Madam , said Gerald , lifting his hat with quite unnecessary politeness , for the woman before him knew nothing of social observances , is this a hotel ?", "context_len": 29}}}, "consents": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Brooke , he added , this lady consents to entertain us", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "You can do lessons with her , my dear , if your mother consents to your staying , for she is very highly educated , though she really has no control over Irene", "context_len": 33}}}, "backwoods": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "What a sporty lot they were ! In the boat-house beside a big launch there were canoes , racing skiffs , aqua-planes , life preservers , fishing tackle and many other things useful in life in the backwoods", "context_len": 38}}}, "commendable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "And certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most", "context_len": 128}}}, "selecting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "For reasons of his own the Rector kept Peggy late that evening , and they made a little tour of the garden , selecting what they called their prize plants , putting indiarubber bands round carnation-buds to keep them from bursting , and tying up the most promising stocks and asters with a scrupulous care , working until the light had almost failed and the sky stood out yellow against the outline of the cypress-trees", "context_len": 75}}}, "jerky": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "What do you want for supper ? asked the woman in a quick , jerky way", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "At first I talked excitedly with the driver , a sour-faced , wizened man , whose short jerky answers , spoken in broadest Sussex , did not encourage conversation ; so presently I dropped all attempt at talking , and took note of the various places and persons we met on the road", "context_len": 53}}}, "antelope": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 36, "context": "Possibly he felt that he was blamable in the matter , for it had been his awful words that caused the senseless panic of the colored youth , and made him flee from before a harmless antelope , when the lad had a loaded rifle in his hands and knew how to use it", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "But where did you get antelope meat ? You didn \u2019 t shoot the animal yourself ?", "context_len": 17}}}, "sportsmanlike": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "She brought out a large rifle , which her mother took from her and raised to her shoulder in fine , sportsmanlike fashion", "context_len": 23}}}, "contagious": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "As a result , they were both brimming over with fun and merriment ; and as there is , fortunately , nothing in the world more contagious than good spirits , the other members of the party were equally merry without in the least knowing why", "context_len": 46}}}, "bust": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Borden brought home something in a paper bag and Jack begged the bag `` to bust , '' watching his father as he shook out a leather strap cut in thongs and said --", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "Then there 's a chair besides , that 's made from an elm-tree that grew where the English beat Napoleon on the field of Waterloo ; and in another part of the room , on a piece of a mast , there 's a great colossal bust of Lord Nelson ; and I 'm ashamed to say I do n't know anything about him , but we ought to , Donald", "context_len": 71}}}, "rill": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Now the warrior 's cup of joy was full , When he drank the blood of his foe , Where the slain lay thick on the gory hill , And torrents of blood from every rill reddened the river below , For Odin 's hall is the Northman 's heaven -- ''", "context_len": 53}}}, "accommodations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "She knew the accommodations of Hickory Farm , and it was easier to leave them where she had met them first , at the entrance of what would always be to her the city of delights", "context_len": 36}}}, "ablution": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 85, "context": "Then he went forth , wandering in the deserts and knowing not whitherward he should aim , and ceased not going till he came to a river ; whereupon , his hope being cut off for stress of chagrin that possessed him , he thought to cast himself into the stream ; but , for that he was a pious Muslim , professing the unity of God , he feared God in himself and stood on the bank ; of the stream to perform the ablution", "context_len": 86}}}, "sacrifices": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 55, "context": "His influence and ascendency seemed firmly established now in the country of the Greeks , and in due time he returned to Macedon , and celebrated at \u00c6g\u00e6 , which was at this time his capital , the establishment and confirmation of his power , by games , shows , spectacles , illuminations , and sacrifices to the gods , offered on a scale of the greatest pomp and magnificence", "context_len": 70}}}, "privations": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 5, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "graduate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "The girl graduate at once jumped to the conclusion that there was a story behind the old scrap-book , else why should it be preserved by her father , who had been a Confederate soldier ? This idea no sooner took shape than she became insistently inquisitive", "context_len": 47}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "In his youth he had been given a thoroughly sound college education , and had gone to England in order to graduate", "context_len": 22}}}, "compunction": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Afterward , a dark , adder-like compunction glided through the flowery expanse of her joy in Tesuque , as she wondered if there was not something heathenish in his lordly enshrinement upon a Christian mantelpiece", "context_len": 35}}}, "congenial": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 43, "context": "Ki Sing was cook and general servant to the little party , and performed his duties in a very satisfactory manner -- better than either Ben or Bradley could have done -- and left his white employers freer to work at the more congenial occupation of searching for gold", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Every part of her being seemed to expand In the congenial atmosphere", "context_len": 12}}}, "replenished": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "7 bunker -- -- Let me see , Jephson , '' he continued , addressing the engineer ; `` that was replenished at Sierra Leone , was n't it ? ''", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Which of them had been the wiser -- which was the better off ? The fellow maundered on , taking a drink at his replenished tankard now and then", "context_len": 29}}}, "monstrous": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 7, "context": "Alden \u2019 s belief was that the monstrous animal would keep on with his ponderous gait in the grotesque attempt to overtake the fleet footed horse which was safe beyond his reach ; but to the consternation of the watcher the brute halted at the very spot where the fugitive had landed when he dropped from the saddle", "context_len": 58}}}, "relaxing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Three months later comes another letter , still further relaxing her secrecy , and still on the subject of the volume of poems \u2019 : --", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Without any relaxing of his scowl , the Indian grunted Ugh and pointed to the jaboty slung over Bomba \u2019 s shoulder", "context_len": 22}}}, "marrying": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Is this a time to be `` marrying and giving in marriage '' ? Edmund is frantic about it", "context_len": 19}}}, "sue": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Oh , is it that way ? Then I must sue humbly to the power above and present my case , for indeed , if you did n't want your fortune you would stop the wheels of division and perhaps be accused of contempt of court -- which you do n't know a word about", "context_len": 56}}}, "buckskin": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "A tall , powerfully built man , clad in buckskin and carrying a rifle , followed by two young men , slighter in figure , but quite as tall , strode from the woods , and halted when they caught sight of Gerald and his companion", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He was dressed in a soft flannel shirt open at the neck , buckskin riding-breeches , leggings , and strong laced boots , the latter provided with spurs", "context_len": 28}}}, "slighter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "A tall , powerfully built man , clad in buckskin and carrying a rifle , followed by two young men , slighter in figure , but quite as tall , strode from the woods , and halted when they caught sight of Gerald and his companion", "context_len": 46}}}, "landlord": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 106, "context": "seen service under Prince Rupert ; Arthur Conolly , an Irish veteran who had served in the Low Countries , and who had come over from Chichester for the occasion ; Arthur Lewis , a gentleman of Bramshott ; Percy Young , an officer of the navy , who in his earlier days had lost a leg in the action of La Rochelle ; Herbert Collings , a master mariner of Gosport , who used to be a frequent visitor at our house , and who greatly interested me with the account of his adventures off the coast of Barbary ; and Giles Perrin , the landlord of the `` Flying Bull '' , who modestly seated himself on a stool in a remote corner of the room", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "And pay damages to de landlord ? Not much , nein , I guess not , and the stout German walked away", "context_len": 22}}}, "filed": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 21, "context": "After a short parley at the door , the visitors were conducted by Nancy to the side-gate , where they all filed into the ruins , from whence a lively hum of conversation could plainly be heard", "context_len": 37}}}, "trenching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The three men filed into the cabin , and their wants were provided for without trenching upon the antelope they had brought with them", "context_len": 24}}}, "prejudiced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "May I ask why you are prejudiced against my countrymen ?", "context_len": 11}}}, "contend": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "It happened , however , that a short time afterward there was another opportunity to contend for the same bull , and Paris , disguising himself as a prince , appeared in the lists , conquered every competitor , and bore away the bull again to his home in the fastnesses of the mountain", "context_len": 54}}}, "heights": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 77, "context": "`` Lord ha \u2019 mercy on me if \u2019 tis not young Master Wentworth ! '' he exclaimed , showing that , though grown in stature , I had not outgrown the appearance of my boyhood ; and when Drake told him of my newly found title , the old man tottered away to let the frequenters of the inn know the news ; whereat we , unwilling to tarry longer , pushed on towards the towering heights of Hindhead", "context_len": 80}}}, "depreciation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Once a young girl , at table , being vexed by words said in depreciation of a near relative , showed her feelings very decisively", "context_len": 25}}}, "nettle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Noel Brooke felt that it was foolish , but this good-natured depreciation of his abilities didn \u2019 t fail to nettle him", "context_len": 22}}}, "scientific": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 49, "context": "Since the memorable time when Captain Cook sailed into Botany Bay in 1769 and saw the naked native Australian poising erect to hurl his peculiar weapon , the boomerang has continued to excite the curiosity and amazement of the civilized world ; and truly the finding of such a scientific weapon in the hands of this so-called lowest order of mankind is an astonishing fact , to be simply accepted as another oddity of this odd , topsy-turvy corner of the world", "context_len": 82}}}, "languid": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "She had put on a languid , affected manner , and sailed about , shaking back her long curls , and trying to be very grown-up and stylish , and she did not take any more notice of Peggy , nor come up to introduce her to other people , and make her feel happy and at home", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I wonder why ? '' said Jane , and she looked up with a languid curiosity , which was all she could ever rise to , in her light-blue eyes", "context_len": 31}}}, "paroxysm": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "At length , after a more than usually intense paroxysm of pain had passed , Gouroo , Banda 's favourite wife , who was present , and whose virulent animosity I had been unfortunate enough to arouse , bent over the patient and whispered something in his ear , the purport of which I could not catch", "context_len": 57}}}, "pluckiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Excuse me , stranger ! You \u2019 re the pluckiest man I \u2019 ve met in many a long day", "context_len": 20}}}, "antagonists": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "He sat back and looked on with an air of intense interest , while the two ill-matched antagonists prepared for the trial", "context_len": 22}}}, "contestants": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "And when a body of contestants in any game get the idea that they are bound to succeed , it is doubly difficult to stop them", "context_len": 26}}}, "ocular": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Why is it that a man of six feet looks double the size of a man of five , though in reality only one fifth larger ? It is an ocular deception which affects every one , but is not readily explained", "context_len": 42}}}, "untrained": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Ben began to move his arms in a flail-like way common to those who are untrained in the art of fighting , and advanced with the utmost confidence to the fray", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 83, "context": "Two sets of mail bags were approaching each other from points two thousand miles apart , and there were times when this approach was at the astounding speed of forty and even fifty miles an hour ! The average daily rate was two hundred and fifty miles a day , but where everything was favorable , or when an express rider was fleeing from the vengeful red men , his pony struck a gait of twenty-five miles and maintained it , when an untrained horse would have dropped in his tracks", "context_len": 91}}}, "fray": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 36, "context": "He ran after the children with little short , sharp barks , worrying at their heels till Aunt Helen declared there would soon not be a whole stocking left in the establishment ; he had a fray at once with Jack , but was much worsted by that worthy , returning to Peggy for protection with his scrap of a tail between his legs ; he tore Bobby 's cricket-ball to pieces , licked the polish off Father 's boots , devoured Aunt Helen 's goloshes , and nestled cosily down to sleep on the top of Nancy 's best Sunday hat , which that luckless damsel had imprudently left in an open band-box on the kitchen settle", "context_len": 118}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "You say you wish to learn to dress yourself , that you may be a useful and helpful little girl ; but you make more trouble than you save when you tear the buttons and strings from your clothes , or knot and fray your shoe-laces", "context_len": 46}}}, "puny": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "It seemed to Ben , therefore , that he had better throw his long arms around his puny opponent , and , fairly lifting him off the ground , hold him helpless at his mercy", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Jimmy Jones , a puny , pale-faced child of eight , permanently lamed by hip disease , which made one leg shorter than the other , was hanging on the rickety gate , as usual , and seemed to be waiting Ben \u2019 s appearance , hobbling out to meet him when he came along the road", "context_len": 57}}}, "magnanimously": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "Whether the powers that rule the entrance to the same came to the conclusion that to a little girl of twelve and a little boy of four the term of general public could not honestly be applied , or whether all rules of procedure and precedence were magnanimously waived in their favor , certain it is that the little card in question bore the incredible inscription : Admit Master Albert Allyn and his little friend , Miss Marie-Celeste Harris , to the East Terrace between the hours of twelve and three on Thursday", "context_len": 93}}}, "miscarried": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "My plans miscarried , as any man 's are liable to", "context_len": 11}}}, "evaded": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 108, "context": "that he should suppose her to be capable of such an evasion , which to her sense of uprightness would have been as bad as speaking a falsehood by word of mouth ; `` no , of course I did not , that would have been telling her , would it not ? One can do a falsehood as well as tell it , '' and although she had intended no reflection upon her brother , no thrust at him , Percy was ashamed as he remembered how often , during the last few months , he had done this very thing ; how he had shuffled and evaded , and thought it no great harm as long as he did not put his falsehood into actual words", "context_len": 128}}}, "undersized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Ain \u2019 t you ashamed of bein \u2019 beaten back by a little chap like that ! Where \u2019 s your pluck ? Are you goin \u2019 to let a little undersized Britisher do you up afore your own father and brother ?", "context_len": 43}}}, "eternally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "No , dad , I \u2019 ll be eternally walloped if I will", "context_len": 13}}}, "walloped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "No , dad , I \u2019 ll be eternally walloped if I will", "context_len": 13}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I should n't care if I was in pieces after the way we walloped them ! Come on over here", "context_len": 21}}}, "avoiding": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 25, "context": "Inferior in strength to Gervaise , he was as active as a cat , and he leapt back with the spring of a panther , avoiding the sweeping blow with which Gervaise had hoped to finish the conflict at once ; the latter found himself therefore engaged in a desperate fight with his three assailants", "context_len": 55}}}, "paralyze": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Again he succeeded in avoiding the close hug by which Ben intended to paralyze and render him powerless , and took the opportunity to get in a couple of sledge-hammer blows , one of which took effect on Ben \u2019 s chin", "context_len": 42}}}, "poplar": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Like a tall poplar he swayed for a moment , and then , falling backward , measured his length upon the ground", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "They also made bark canoes and canoes of poplar [ 18 ] or pine , from thirty to forty feet long and about two feet broad , with flat bottoms and sides", "context_len": 32}}}, "gritting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Hold ! hold ! '' pleaded Holly , and , with gritting teeth and tense muscles , his men did hold", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "A mist seemed to come before his eyes , but with a gritting of his teeth he got more control of himself , and then he saw clear", "context_len": 28}}}, "vanquish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It looked to Gerald as if his friend would find it a hard job to vanquish this backwoods giant", "context_len": 19}}}, "thews": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Wal , stranger , how do you feel about it ? asked Abe , as he saw Brooke apparently taking stock of his thews and sinews", "context_len": 26}}}, "recoil": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "Joe set to work again with a will , clearing out the earth well from under the side of the stone ; then , putting his fingers beneath it , he gave a mighty jerk of his strong arms , and up it came , nearly upsetting him with the force of the recoil", "context_len": 54}}}, "derisive": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Abe \u2019 s rather sluggish temperament was angered by his brother \u2019 s derisive laugh , and he too lost his head", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The cowboy who had sprung upon the horse turned and greeted him with derisive laughter", "context_len": 15}}}, "chalk": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 39, "context": "Bobby had a pretty set of bantams , whose small eggs afforded him much delight and some slight profit , for Aunt Helen bought them from him at threepence a dozen -- a transaction which he always recorded in chalk upon the hen-house door , the pennies being carefully put by towards the purchase of a pair of fantail pigeons , which was at present the summit of his ambition", "context_len": 70}}}, "disc": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "At a sign from the Lieutenant the sergeant in charge of the escort deftly removed the prisoner 's identity disc and handed it to his superior officer for inspection", "context_len": 29}}}, "marksmen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "I wondered whether by any chance the fatal wounds were actually inflicted by interested persons who desired to put as speedy an end as possible to the sufferings of their unfortunate friends ; and if so , whether the idea would occur to Ama to enlist the services of a few good marksmen in my behalf when my turn should come", "context_len": 61}}}, "encomium": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Living on grass , and in the woods , with the milk from the house , he had not that protuberance of belly which swine reared in sties possess , and really merited Charlie \u2019 s encomium of being handsome ; he jumped up on his master and rubbed against his legs , with low grunts , expressive of satisfaction", "context_len": 60}}}, "marksman": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Had Alden possessed his own gun , he would have brought down the redskin , for the distance was not great , and we remember he was a fine marksman , but the new weapon did not feel precisely like the one to which he was accustomed", "context_len": 47}}}, "sleeper": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "Bessie , always a light sleeper , was the first to be roused by his sweet notes , that soft , half-doubtful little trill with which he began ; but , as it rose into a gush of joyous music , Maggie , too , stirred , and opened her eyes", "context_len": 51}}}, "whinny": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a whinny from one of the horses , that were tethered to a tree at the rear part of the cabin", "context_len": 23}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "He would run to meet Ted , and follow him about the fields just as your little Flossy there follows you ; or if he was in the stable would whinny with delight the moment he heard his step", "context_len": 39}}}, "cowboys": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "The two cowboys turned quickly , expecting to encounter a man , but instead saw only a tall , gaunt woman in a white night-dress , with her long , disheveled hair hanging down her back", "context_len": 36}}}, "sombreros": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Two cowboys wearing large flapping sombreros , had untied the horses , and were leading them away", "context_len": 17}}}, "incautious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Ay , ay , down with them ! '' repeated their incautious leader , seizing Felgate \u2019 s horse by the bridle and attempting to force it on its haunches", "context_len": 31}}}, "unarmed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "This incautious speech betrayed the fact that he was unarmed , and made the two thieves feel secure", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "He is ready to risk his life on behalf of a slave , and can strike down men with his unarmed hand ; he is as gentle in his manner as a woman ; and now it seems he can talk Arabic , and although it was in his power to keep this secret he tells it rather than overhear words that are not meant for his ear", "context_len": 68}}}, "recognizing": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 32, "context": "Selden ( circumstances permitting , it is better to call people by their real names ) had discovered Donald from the dining-room window just as he was descending the steps , and recognizing him instantly flung his napkin onto the table , and hurrying from the room seized his hat from the rack as he passed", "context_len": 56}}}, "disheveled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The two cowboys turned quickly , expecting to encounter a man , but instead saw only a tall , gaunt woman in a white night-dress , with her long , disheveled hair hanging down her back", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Meanwhile Irene , in the red dress she had worn all night , very much crumpled , very much disheveled and soiled , sat down and fixed her bright eyes on her parent", "context_len": 33}}}, "imprecation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Oh , how hollow the mirth and revelry that night ! How he loathed the singing , the drunken shouting , the fierce imprecation over the wine cup -- the sensuality , which now distinguished his bloodthirsty companions", "context_len": 38}}}, "disabled": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 10, "context": "Moreover , could one or more of the guns be disabled permanently , others would be brought down and set in their place , so that nothing would be gained but a very short delay , which would be of no advantage to us , and certainly would in no way justify the risking of the life of so distinguished a young knight", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "While the engineer , firemen , and some of the brakemen worked over the disabled engine , another trainman walked back to the nearest telegraph office to summon a relief engine", "context_len": 31}}}, "problematical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "What further ramification the conversation might have taken is problematical , but it was interrupted just then by the arrival of Ed Kerr , who seemed in much of a hurry", "context_len": 31}}}, "strangle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Still Bomba \u2019 s muscles were iron and he forged forward doggedly , ignoring the thorns that tore at him , the roots that tried to trip him up , the vines that sought to strangle him", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Only those bred as Bomba had been among the very wild beasts of that tangled region could have hoped to free himself of that strangle hold", "context_len": 26}}}, "scoot": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` She may kick , you see , and soon as you light her you want to scoot", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Jest load and fire as fast as we kin till we \u2019 ve tumbled \u2019 em all ober on dere heads , and den scoot fur camp", "context_len": 27}}}, "reloading": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "Not only that , but he reasoned that the Indian would not shift his place for more than two or three feet , before reloading and trying another shot", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Jethro had not quite finished reloading , and by the time he was ready , it was useless for him to fire", "context_len": 22}}}, "exhilarated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The adventure , which would have terrified most women , only exhilarated her", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He was in a joyous , exhilarated mood , and they were glad for Bomba \u2019 s sake", "context_len": 18}}}, "betook": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then the Sultan and Alaeddin betook themselves to the latter 's palace ; [ 542 ] whereupon his bride , the Lady Bedrulbudour , came out to meet him , rejoicing in him , and kissed him between the eyes , and he went in with her to her pavilion ; [ 543 ] whither after a little came the Sultan and they sat down and the slave-girls brought sherbets", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then he fared on in the high road till he came to the city and entering , betook himself to the drug-market , where for two diners he bought of [ one of ] the druggists two drachms of rare strong henbane , the son of its minute , [ 595 ] and retracing his steps , returned to the palace", "context_len": 61}}}, "tendered": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "on the two documents , tendered them with exaggerated courtesy to his visitor , who he was well aware knew his name perfectly , and said , with the faintest suggestion of mimicry :", "context_len": 34}}}, "northwest": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 27, "context": "He saw a dark line far out at sea ; it came across the water rapidly , and presently a gentle breeze began to blow from the northwest ; it gradually increased in strength , and when , in the afternoon , he stopped at a village , the waves were breaking upon the shore", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 12, "context": "At that epoch the Moskoquis formed a powerful separate republic in the northwest of Mexico ; they succored him with a numerous body of warriors , but were frightfully decimated by the Spaniards , who dismembered Montezuma 's domain , and almost completely depopulated it", "context_len": 45}}}, "highwaymen": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "But Wesley felt no uneasiness , `` knowing , '' as he writes , `` that God would take care of us ; and He did so , for before we came to the spot all the highwaymen were taken , and so we went on unmolested , and came safe to Bristol ''", "context_len": 54}}}, "learner": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Noel Brooke had become more and more attached to his young secretary , whom he not only found an agreeable companion , but intelligent and an eager learner", "context_len": 28}}}, "oral": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "It gave great delight to the children to recognize an old friend in a new dress , and as interest was aroused , but little difficulty was encountered in recognizing words that were indeed `` new '' in their sight vocabulary , but old servants in their oral vocabulary", "context_len": 49}}}, "landlords": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "You rich Englishmen are not obliged to turn time into money like us poor landlords", "context_len": 15}}}, "bantering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Rather taken aback by his bantering tone , Peggy was at a loss what to answer , but the other old gentleman , noticing her confusion , came to the rescue", "context_len": 31}}}, "shifty": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "With his body bent , his head thrust forward , and his nostrils working , he slowly passed along the inner face of the crowd , his shifty eyes darting hither and thither , until his gaze happened to fall upon one of the individuals for whom he was looking , when he would come to a halt , appear to be following a scent , and finally stretch forth his spear and lightly smite some man or woman on the head with it", "context_len": 84}}}, "scrutinizing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "While scrutinizing the ridge which showed a considerable growth of dwarfed pine , Alden was startled to observe a thin column of smoke issuing from a point on the crest", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` I am not hungry , '' Hal muttered , flushing under her scrutinizing gaze", "context_len": 15}}}, "jostle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Brooke managed to jostle him a little in going on board the steamer", "context_len": 13}}}, "qualified": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "He did what very few monarchs have done , either before or since ; in securing for himself the pleasures of friendship , and of intimate social communion with a heart kindred to his own , without ruining himself by committing to a favorite powers which he was not qualified to wield", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "This dignitary was appointed by the m\u00edko and his counsellors , and selected by them among the best qualified warriors", "context_len": 20}}}, "beardless": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Private secretary ! Couldn \u2019 t he find a person better qualified for the position than a beardless boy from the hills of Colorado ?", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Dost tell me that a beardless youth could with one galley overcome a great fleet , commanded by the most noted captains on our coast ?", "context_len": 26}}}, "communicate": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "I thought when that gave out he would come home , or at any rate communicate with me", "context_len": 18}, "VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 64, "context": "tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me , died out , and I began to prepare myself , as best I could , to meet whatever fate might have in store for me with the", "context_len": 128}}}, "cabinet": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 58, "context": "Accustomed to little quiet schoolgirl parties , she had not thought there would be such a large and fashionable assembly of guests , and had hoped that she would have her dear Maud to herself for a short time , at any rate , and be shown the doves , and the white pony , and the Indian cabinet , and the other treasures which her friend had so often described to her during the dancing-class", "context_len": 76}}}, "photograph": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 73, "context": "The next thing , and sometimes a better thing than knowing your favorite author , is to know how and where he lives ; and it was a matter of supreme delight to Marie-Celeste that henceforth when she should open Lewis Carroll 's books she should be able to picture him working away here in his study , and just as he really looked , too , for by chance or accidents full-length photograph stood on the mantel , which Dorothy , from her visita few years before , was able to pronounce an excellent likeness , and very characteristic", "context_len": 100}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` What do you want to photograph such scary things as foxes for , anyhow ? ''", "context_len": 17}}}, "emotional": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` You sent the things to me , '' she said , in a joyful emotional little voice , `` the beautiful , beautiful things ? YOU sent them ! ''", "context_len": 31}}}, "promenading": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "But I do not like promenading at this hour in winter ! Is it a country fashion ? I am very cold , and tired , and sleepy , and I would rather retire to rest", "context_len": 36}}}, "estimation": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 73, "context": "Bi ' l ghewali may be a clerical error for bi ' l ghalibi , `` for the most part , in general , '' in which case we may read , `` Folk in general abound [ in talk ] anent her virtues ; '' or bi ' l ghewali may perhaps be used in the sense ( of which use , however , I know no instance ) of 'in excessive estimation , ' in which latter case the passage might be rendered , `` Folk abound in setting a high value on ( or extolling ) her virtues", "context_len": 101}}}, "nationality": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 37, "context": "The great majority were Italians and Spaniards ; the former could proceed by land or sea to their respective homes , while the Spaniards would have no long time to wait before a vessel of their own nationality entered the port , even if one were not lying there when they arrived", "context_len": 52}}}, "repels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Yes , but the boy is headstrong and repels my advances", "context_len": 11}}}, "objectionable": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 6, "context": "Irene , no longer in the objectionable red frock , but now dressed as a pretty young girl of her age ought to be dressed , walked by Rosamund 's side and chatted about books , about music , about all sorts of things , the existence of which she had scarcely known a few weeks ago", "context_len": 57}}}, "recede": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "Slowly the canoe yielded to their efforts , as with desperate energy , they strained at the line , and began to recede from the surf", "context_len": 26}}}, "inherits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Unless he inherits the same tendency , said Wentworth significantly", "context_len": 10}}}, "tendency": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 16, "context": "She was generally sound , with no especial delicacy ; she did not suffer from any tendency to headache ; she was not fussy , or self-indulgent , or dainty as to her eating , or particular as to personal comforts , or squeamish as to her surroundings , or shy in making new friends , or afraid of toil and trouble", "context_len": 62}}}, "suggestively": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Warren Lane seemed to my uncle and myself a thoroughly upright man , but here he paused suggestively", "context_len": 18}}}, "forgery": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "It is only just over a hundred years since John Howard died ; yet in his day persons could be put to death for stealing a horse or a sheep , for robbing dwellings , for defrauding creditors , for forgery , for wounding deer , for killing or maiming cattle , for stealing goods to the value of five shillings , or even for cutting a band in a hop plantation", "context_len": 72}}}, "practicable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 43, "context": "Might not this occur to some of the company in some of their cooler moments ? They decided in a secret conference that it would be best for them to get away early the next morning -- that is , as early as practicable -- before any change had come over the minds of their new friends", "context_len": 57}}}, "discharge": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 41, "context": "The Maxims began hurling nickel at the rate of 450 shots a minute , with the idea of either disabling the periscope or churning up the water in its vicinity , in order to make it impossible for the U-boat to discharge a torpedo with any degree of accuracy", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 44, "context": "Oh ! if only every one might have his turn in this world of ours ; but since that is out of the question , let there at least be as much Coney Island for housemaids as is consistent with good morals and faithful discharge of their duties ; at least so thought one dear little mistress , with more heart , perhaps , than discretion , but a heart , all the same , that won every one to her and made life in her household move with infinite smoothness", "context_len": 91}}}, "proofs": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "Was Christianity worth the sacrifice ? Where were the absolute proofs of its truth ? If it were of God , why did He not protect His people ? The heathen Saxons had been victorious over the Christian Britons ; and now that they had become Christian , the heathen Danes were victorious over them", "context_len": 55}}}, "shortened": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 6, "context": "Having reached this spot , we shortened sail to our three topsails , spanker , and jib , and slowly worked to windward along that course , tacking every two hours until we had worked up to within sight of the cape , and then bearing up and running off to leeward for a distance of one hundred miles again , keeping a hand aloft on the main-royal yard as look-out from dawn to dark", "context_len": 75}}}, "proving": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 71, "context": "But , as luck would have it , the weather fell still lighter at sunset on our ninth day out ; and on the following morning at daybreak we found ourselves becalmed within three miles of a British cruiser , which promptly lowered her boats and despatched them to overhaul us ; and by breakfast-time I had the pleasure of finding myself once more under the British flag , our captor proving to be the corvette Cleopatra , by the captain and officers of which I was most kindly received when I had related to them my strange story", "context_len": 99}}}, "unscrupulous": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "Her sides were painted in red , white , and blue horizontal stripes , while amidships her name was displayed in letters six feet in height -- in conformity with an arbitrary regulation made by an unscrupulous nation whose U-boats did not hesitate to torpedo at sight , despite the distinguishing marks of neutral craft", "context_len": 55}}}, "deferentially": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "As Bradley Wentworth paced the deck with thoughtful brow , Samuel Standish , who was always drawn towards men whom he suspected to be wealthy , stepped up , and asked deferentially : General , may I ask you for a light ? for Wentworth chanced to be smoking", "context_len": 49}}}, "adventurer": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "He kept whistling a jolly air , with a sidelong glance observed that his recent companions had turned back towards their camp in the clearing , and then , dropping his assumption of the reckless young adventurer , stared seriously ahead and began to figure out the situation in all its details", "context_len": 52}}}, "memorandum": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "As he neared the `` new '' express shed , however , he passed around to its rear , and glancing out of a window there Bart saw that he had come to a halt , and was drawing a diagram of the tracks on a blank page in his memorandum book", "context_len": 52}}}, "needful": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 71, "context": "The sole vow necessary , so far as I can see , would be that knights should remain unmarried and disposable at all times for service until ten years after making their profession , and that afterwards they should ever be ready to obey the summons to arms , on occasions when the safety of Rhodes , or the invasion of any Christian country by the Moslems , rendered their services needful , when they would come out just as the knights of Richard the Lion Heart went out as crusaders", "context_len": 91}}}, "promenaded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The two promenaded the deck together , and were evidently on the most cordial terms", "context_len": 15}}}, "artful": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "And that 's the way ; they are both so sly and artful , they are seldom or never caught in the act ; so though when a melon patch or hen-roost is robbed , or some fine young trees are hacked to pieces , every one feels sure it was Lem or Dolly who did the mischief , yet it is difficult to prove it on them", "context_len": 68}}}, "overrated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "I am really but a boy yet , though by good fortune pushed strangely forward , and I am glad indeed to receive council from a knight of vastly greater experience than myself and , in future , however much I may be conscious in my own mind that anything I have done is greatly overrated , I will at least abstain from protest", "context_len": 64}}}, "virtuous": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` Good faith , '' he cried ; `` 't is worth an hour lost to learn that Tumble-down Dick had one follower , and , I warrant , a faithful one ! Aller Teufel ! thou art as good a lad as I have seen in this most virtuous and loyal city", "context_len": 53}}}, "disease": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 28, "context": "I must cure the king , or it would be so much the worse for me ! And how was I to cure him ? My knowledge of disease was of the slightest and most amateurish kind , and , for aught that I could tell to the contrary , might not even be sufficient to enable me to diagnose the case correctly , much less to treat it successfully ! However , there was no use in meeting trouble half-way ; the only possible course was to obey the summons forthwith , and do my best , leaving the result in the hands of Providence", "context_len": 106}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "But the quiet gentle woman herself feared neither death , disease nor hard work ; the only thing she did not like was the fuss the people made about her", "context_len": 30}}}, "instituting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Meanwhile the officers had been instituting an investigation as to the person who had raised the cry", "context_len": 17}}}, "typical": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 64, "context": "detected fastened to the trunk of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to", "context_len": 128}}}, "calumny": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I assure you , gentlemen , he protested , this is a base calumny", "context_len": 14}}}, "voiced": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "A ducking will do him no harm , said the cowboy , and he evidently voiced the sentiment of his fellow passengers", "context_len": 22}}}, "intrusion": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 47, "context": "Indeed , I have an idea that Captain Revell inclines to apply the same rule to every one aboard of his great steamer , to passengers as well as to officers and crew , and so perhaps regarded the advent of Marie-Celeste in the light of an intrusion", "context_len": 48}}}, "compel": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 56, "context": "Your fate seemed sealed -- unless I could contrive a scheme to save you ; but I could think of nothing ; and the anticipation of your death made me feel so utterly wretched , that at last I entreated my father that , if he would not spare you , he would at least not compel me to witness your sufferings", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Unless you do as I suggest , I shall attach your property and compel you to raise the money in the way I indicate", "context_len": 24}}}, "harsher": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "You are mistaken ! I hope you will not compel me to use a harsher word", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It means persecution , beating , cruelty , hard words and harsher deeds", "context_len": 13}}}, "unoffending": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "I appeal to the officers of this boat to prevent an outrage upon an unoffending man", "context_len": 16}}}, "appearances": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 73, "context": "The Eton boys in their tall hats ( atoning , as it were , for the extreme briefness of their jackets ) and wide-rolling linen collars were skurrying through the streets as though they had the right of way , as indeed they have in dear old royal Windsor ; and here and there the flowing gown of a colleger spread itself to the April wind and floated out behind , to all appearances as glad as any peacock to show what it could do in that direction", "context_len": 88}}}, "fickle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The fickle crowd changed at once and such cries were heard as It \u2019 s a shame ! It \u2019 s an outrage ! Samuel Standish was released", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Suddenly the minds of the fickle multitude veered round to the two accusers , and shouts arose : `` The boy 's right ! Hang the thieves ! ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "lobby": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "He and his chum , Matt King , known far and wide as Motor Matt , were in the lobby of the big hotel in which they had established themselves when they first arrived in New York", "context_len": 37}}}, "conciliate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}}}, "salesman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I am a traveling salesman", "context_len": 5}}}, "agents": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` I put on all the official dignity I could assume , but was very polite all the time , informed her that mislaid , delayed and irregular express matter were common occurrences , that the company was responsible for its contracts , counted you one of its most reliable agents , and assured her that very possibly within twenty-four hours she would find her trunk delivered safe and sound at its destination", "context_len": 73}}}, "evasive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Gerald returned an evasive answer", "context_len": 5}}}, "cultivate": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "Indeed , both she and Miss Fanny gave one the impression of being too incessantly though quietly busy about everything that promoted the happiness of other people , to ever become stout , or to cultivate dress and appearances , beyond what was consistent with the aims and duties and requirements of a fully occupied home-life", "context_len": 56}}}, "tank": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 48, "context": "If you were here , dear Aunt , it would interest you to walk about , leaning on my arm , and see poor men in their rags , women and children , carrying baskets of earth on their heads , to fill up that part of the tank which is nearest to the house", "context_len": 55}}}, "licks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "She licks him awful", "context_len": 4}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I 've read of 'salt licks , ' where deer congregate , but I never figured out that our boiling spring might be one", "context_len": 24}}}, "inhaling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Gerald followed the boy , inhaling , as he went up , musty and disagreeable odors , and felt that if it had not been on an errand of mercy he would have been inclined to retreat and make his way back to the street", "context_len": 45}}}, "confirm": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "It would seem incredible that such a mind as his could really admit such an absurd superstition as the story of his divine origin , and we must therefore suppose that he gave this direction in order that the place of his interment might confirm the idea of his superhuman nature in the general opinion of mankind", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "But if , before you begin to do so , I tell them that they can speak the truth with reference to me , they will , I am sure , confirm my story , incredible as it may now appear to you", "context_len": 43}}}, "urchin": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Had the boy been a man he would have been on his guard , but who could be suspicious of a street urchin , whose story seemed natural enough", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Clean to the muzzle ! '' bubbled over an excited urchin", "context_len": 12}}}, "design": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 19, "context": "Seizing the first opportunity of slipping away from his schoolmates , he rushed upstairs to the dormitory with the design of throwing the note under Seabrooke 's bed or bureau , where it might be supposed to have fallen ; but seeing the trunk standing there ready packed , the impulse had taken him to put it in that , and without reflecting -- perhaps hardly caring -- that this would place Seabrooke in a still more embarrassing position , he thrust the note within , as Charlie Henderson saw , and fled from the room", "context_len": 96}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 71, "context": "96 ) : `` Creeks make the boys swim in the coldest weather ; make them frequently undergo scratching from head to foot , through the skin , with broken glass or gar-fish teeth [ 120 ] , so as to make them all in a gore of blood , and then wash them with cold water ; this is with them the arcanum against all diseases ; but when they design it as a punishment to the boys , they dry-scratch them , i", "context_len": 85}}}, "maneuver": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "In fact the boy who had proved so unworthy of his compassion was by this time in the street , laughing aloud at his successful maneuver", "context_len": 26}}}, "fusillade": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "In the midst of a regular mob of panting , yelling , and shouting men , and preceded by a terrific fusillade of Mills 's bombs , Malcolm found himself struggling through masses of partly-severed barbed wire and up on the hostile parapet", "context_len": 43}}}, "jailer": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "News travels apace , and in less than an hour it was all over our village that Joyce had by some means obtained a file , cut through his fetters , and , after a murderous attack on his jailer , had broken out of Midhurst Jail , and was last seen making his way towards the bleak Sussex Downs", "context_len": 60}}}, "choleric": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Bring out that rascally Portuguese , trice him up , and give him five dozen ! '' cried the captain , a strain of his choleric ancestor betraying itself", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Men were clustered like flies in the boats on the boat deck , others swarmed up the shrouds , to the choleric but ineffectual protests of the ship 's officers", "context_len": 30}}}, "screwing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "After this , Peggy and Bobby were determined to try and obtain a sight of their ancestress , and with much screwing up of their courage stole out one evening , and crept fearfully round the ruins , clutching each other 's hands very tightly , and jumping at every sound", "context_len": 51}}}, "temporize": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "Berlin looked a trifle startled as the captain of the eleven appeared , but into Hayden \u2019 s eyes there sprang a light of satisfaction ; for he fancied Eliot had come to temporize , possibly to plead", "context_len": 38}}}, "thronging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The pirates thronging the other side of their ship were intent only on overcoming the resistance of the knights , and even the discharge of cannon had not called their attention to their foe , until the latter , shouting the war cry of the Order , fell suddenly upon them", "context_len": 51}}}, "knights": {"freq": 500, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 499, "word_index": 83, "context": "This , doubtless , may be so , Sir Gervaise , the doge said , with a slight smile , but it is to the head that plans , rather than to the hand that strikes , that such success as you have achieved is due ; and the credit of this night attack is , as the cavalier Caretto tells me , wholly yours , for until you issued your final orders it seemed to him , and to the two good knights his companions , that there was naught to do but to remain in port and watch this corsair fleet sail away to carry out its work of destruction", "context_len": 112}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "The grand master had advanced Gervaise a sum equal to half a year 's income of his commandery , and with this he had purchased a stock of the best wines , and various other luxuries , to supplement the rations supplied from the funds of the Order to knights when at sea", "context_len": 53}}}, "unresistingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Some were cut down almost unresistingly , but the great majority , running to the bow or stern , threw themselves overboard and swam to the other ships", "context_len": 28}}}, "grapnels": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "The pirate ship on the other side of Santoval 's galley instantly threw off the grapnels and thrust off from her side , and , immediately hauling in the sheets of the big sail , began at once to draw away , while her three consorts made for the mouth of the bay", "context_len": 53}}}, "consorts": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 32, "context": "Therefore , as soon as we engaged her , and I found that she was full of troops and could not be captured without heavy loss , and that two of her consorts might arrive before we accomplished it , it was plainly my duty to abandon the attempt , although , you may guess , it went sorely against the grain to give the order , especially as I knew that a host would be looking on from St", "context_len": 80}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "As far as colour goes I should put you down as a Nubian ; but your hair is of a hue that consorts but badly with that of your flesh", "context_len": 30}}}, "aiding": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 39, "context": "The wind was blowing freshly , and was rapidly increasing in strength , so that , in spite of the efforts of the galley slaves , the pirates gradually drew away , running straight before the wind , and aiding the effects of the sails with oars", "context_len": 47}}}, "hopelessness": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "You know how great is the mortality among the slaves -- how they pine away and die from no material malady that can be detected , but simply from hopelessness and weariness of life , aided , undoubtedly , in the case of the galley slaves , by sleeping in the damp night air after an exposure all day to the full heat of the sun", "context_len": 66}}}, "galleys": {"freq": 119, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 119, "word_index": 76, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}}}, "opportunely": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "A flash of lightning , that came most opportunely at this moment , revealed a small light canoe hauled up on the beach , with a couple of paddles , a sheaf of spears , bows and arrows , and a few other oddments in it", "context_len": 46}}}, "bulwarks": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 55, "context": "Several of the boats were sunk by a well-directed fire from our swivel guns , but eight or nine gained the side of the Prince Royal , and , passing under the comparative shelter afforded by our lofty stern , boarded us on the starboard side , where , owing to the list , our bulwarks were much lower than on the other side", "context_len": 64}}}, "grievously": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 3, "context": "They had been grievously led away by Mahomet , whom Gervaise regarded as a false teacher ; but as he had seen innumerable instances of the fidelity of the Moslems to their creed , and the punctuality and devotion with which the slaves said their daily prayers , exposed though they were to the scorn and even the anger of their taskmasters , he had quite lost , during his nine months of constant association with Suleiman Ali , the bigoted hatred of Mohammedanism so universal at the time", "context_len": 89}}}, "paynims": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "One of the paynims ' cannonballs has carried off both my legs below the knee", "context_len": 15}}}, "cannonballs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "If we do not do so , he said , they will assuredly think that it is the corsair fleet advancing to attack and burn the vessels in port , and you may be saluted as you approach by a shower of cannonballs", "context_len": 43}}}, "leech": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "He is wounded sorely , Gervaise , and the leech declares that one at least of his wounds is mortal ; still , I can not bring myself to believe that so great a hero will be taken away in the moment of victory , after having done such marvels for the cause not only of the Order , but of all Christendom", "context_len": 63}}}, "searing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The leech has been searing the wounds with a hot iron , and says that he thinks I shall get over it ; but if so I fear that my fighting days are past , unless , indeed , I fight seated on a chair", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The effort gave his ankle a wrench that sent a pain through his leg like the searing of a hot iron", "context_len": 21}}}, "recaptured": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "We had all kept watch during the night , thinking the pirates might obtain boats and make an attack upon us ; and , with but twenty of us fit to wield a sword , our position would have been a bad one , and at any rate they might have recaptured the prizes", "context_len": 54}}}, "similarly": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 71, "context": "Inform him , then , that from the present time he will be excused from all work , except , of course , to join in ceremonials when all are required to be present ; and if you , Sir John , will pick out from among the servitors here one who is well instructed and educated , and capable of writing as well as reading his language , I will similarly relieve him of all other work , and place him at the disposal of young Tresham", "context_len": 88}}}, "ascend": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 69, "context": "As soon as darkness came on , a large body of slaves were marched down from the town , and , under the direction of the knights , laboured all night at the mound , removing great quantities of the fallen stones and rubbish in a line halfway up it , and piling them above so as to form a scarp across the mound that would need ladders to ascend", "context_len": 70}}}, "embark": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "We expected that you would be here before noon ; but we decided that , should they get here before you , we from our galley would embark on Santoval 's , as it was better to fight in one strongly manned boat than to divide our forces", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The instant the commander knew what was required , he bade six of the knights embark with Gervaise in a boat moored behind the tower , and row up the harbour to the spot where the shipping was all massed together , protected by the high ground of the fortress from the Turkish fire", "context_len": 54}}}, "fetching": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 101, "context": "Frightened almost out of their wits , the children shrieked and ran , but stopped before they had gone twenty yards , for the noise had developed into a very decided 'Hee-haw ! ' and the moon , bursting through the clouds , revealed the long nose and twirling ears of the tinker 's broken-winded old donkey , who , dragging a clog on his leg , was giving vent to his feelings in his own peculiar fashion , his master having turned him into the ruins for a stolen feed during the night , no doubt with the intention of fetching him in the morning before anyone was up and about", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "She had established herself over by the ditch , and Jane could see her fetching water in a can and mixing it with a queer kind of adobe which she got half-way up the hill", "context_len": 35}}}, "rowers": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 51, "context": "The events , however , that had taken place during the expedition had impressed him greatly with the utility of a knowledge of Turkish , for had it not been for Sir John Boswell 's possessing some acquaintance with the language , it would have been impossible to communicate with the rowers of their boat , or to have arranged the plan by which they had escaped the pirates", "context_len": 69}}}, "gunners": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 13, "context": "As the daylight broadened out , however , the light enabled the Christian gunners to aim with far greater accuracy than had before been possible , and , concentrating their fire upon the bridge , across which reinforcements continued to press to the support of the assailants , they succeeded in sinking so many of the boats that it was no longer passable", "context_len": 63}}}, "riddled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Come back hyar , said the guide sternly ; git behind this wagon if ye don \u2019 t want to git riddled by the varmints", "context_len": 25}}}, "grievous": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 27, "context": "Since you have been away , your friend Harcourt has been appointed a commander of a galley , and Sir John Boswell , being incapacitated by the grievous wounds he received during the siege , has accepted a rich commandery in England , and sailed but two days since to take up his charge", "context_len": 54}}}, "prestige": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "On the other hand , he felt perfectly confident that the city could be successfully defended , and that at a cost of life far less than would be attained by a victory in the open field , while the blow that would be inflicted upon the prestige and power of the enemy , by being ignominiously compelled to retire to their ships , after the failure of all their attacks , would be as great as if their army had been defeated in the field", "context_len": 86}}}, "leaders": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 25, "context": "It would have been a blow to the prestige of the Order , and would have brought such strength to Hassan Ali and other pirate leaders that nothing short of sending out a fleet would have recovered our ascendancy ; and as every ducat we can spare has to be spent on the fortifications , it would have been a misfortune indeed had we been obliged to fit out such an expedition at present", "context_len": 74}}}, "ascendancy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "It would have been a blow to the prestige of the Order , and would have brought such strength to Hassan Ali and other pirate leaders that nothing short of sending out a fleet would have recovered our ascendancy ; and as every ducat we can spare has to be spent on the fortifications , it would have been a misfortune indeed had we been obliged to fit out such an expedition at present", "context_len": 74}}}, "ducat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "It would have been a blow to the prestige of the Order , and would have brought such strength to Hassan Ali and other pirate leaders that nothing short of sending out a fleet would have recovered our ascendancy ; and as every ducat we can spare has to be spent on the fortifications , it would have been a misfortune indeed had we been obliged to fit out such an expedition at present", "context_len": 74}}}, "fortifications": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 53, "context": "It would have been a blow to the prestige of the Order , and would have brought such strength to Hassan Ali and other pirate leaders that nothing short of sending out a fleet would have recovered our ascendancy ; and as every ducat we can spare has to be spent on the fortifications , it would have been a misfortune indeed had we been obliged to fit out such an expedition at present", "context_len": 74}}}, "resound": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It is true there are five prizes to show for it , and we have struck Hassan Ali a blow that will resound through the Levant ; but the cost is heavy", "context_len": 32}}}, "disable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Sir John Boswell had been wounded , but not so seriously as to altogether disable him , and he was in command of one of the prizes , having Sir Adam Tedbond , Harcourt , Gervaise , and a German knight , with him", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Come on , '' and Langridge and his crony disappeared in the darkness , talking , meanwhile , of a dastardly plot they had evolved to disable Phil Clinton", "context_len": 29}}}, "disembarked": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "In 1559 another Spanish leader , Tristan de Luna , disembarked in or near Mobile bay , then went north in quest of gold and treasure , reached Nanipacna , or `` pueblo Santa Cruz de Nanipacna , '' and from there arrived , after experiencing many privations and trials , among the Co\u00e7as , who were then engaged in warfare with the Napochies ( nanpissa ? cf", "context_len": 68}}}, "disarmed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "On reaching the beach they were disarmed , divided equally between the galleys , and distributed among the rowers , filling up the places of those who had been killed by the fire of the Moslems , and of the men drafted into the prizes", "context_len": 45}}}, "disinclined": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "I before thought it probable that a good many of them would have remained hidden , trusting to be able to make a raft after we had left , and so get away , believing rightly enough that we should be disinclined to search every foot of the island for them", "context_len": 51}}}, "anchored": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 53, "context": "One evening there were signs of a change of weather , and by morning it was blowing a furious gale from the north ; in spite of the efforts of the rowers , the galley narrowly escaped being driven ashore ; but she at last gained the shelter of an island , and anchored under its lee , the slaves being utterly worn out by continuous exertion", "context_len": 67}}}, "rendezvous": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 31, "context": "You were , he tells me , the means of saving his life and that of your comrade , and , by thus enabling him to bear to the place of rendezvous the news of Sir Louis 's danger , were also the means of saving the lives of Sir Louis and his companions , and of bringing home in safety the prizes he had taken", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "As soon as the meal concluded , the Moslem captives were questioned one by one as to the rendezvous at which the pirate fleet was to assemble ; all , however , protested that the place was known only to the three commanders , all of whom had fallen in the fight", "context_len": 52}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Three ships on their way up were captured by a galley of our Order , and her commander having obtained news of the whereabouts of the spot where the corsairs were to rendezvous , found them all lying together in a small inlet , and launched against them a number of fishing boats fitted out as fire ships", "context_len": 58}}}, "acclamation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "The little fleet anchored that night at the rendezvous , and after two more days ' rowing reached Rhodes , where the appearance of the three galleys , followed by their five prizes , was greeted with great acclamation", "context_len": 39}}}, "damped": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "But elated as we are at our success , our gladness is sorely damped by the state of the grand master , and the loss of so many of our comrades , though , indeed , our langue has suffered less than any of the others , for the brunt of the attacks on St", "context_len": 55}}}, "sultan": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 31, "context": "Sir Knights , he said , I have heard from my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction ,", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "When the latter , in obedience to the order , came in from the garden , the officer said in Italian , It having come to the ears of the sultan my master that the merchant Ben Ibyn has ventured , contrary to the law , to purchase a Christian slave brought secretly into the town , he has declared the slave to be forfeited and I am commanded to take him at once to the slaves ' quarter", "context_len": 79}}}, "infidels": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 86, "context": "My brother has already spoken of you to me , Sir Gervaise , the viscount said , when the young knight was presented to him ; although indeed there was no occasion for him to do so , since the name of the knight who two years ago saved the commerce of Italy from ruin , and with a single galley destroyed or captured a great fleet of over twenty Barbary pirates , and thus for a time put a stop to the depredations of the infidels , is known throughout Europe", "context_len": 92}}}, "secular": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 29, "context": "Three times have you earned special credit ; upon the first occasion , the grand master -- no mean judge of conduct and character -- deemed you worthy of secular knighthood , an honour which has not , in my memory , been bestowed at Rhodes upon any young knight ; on the second , you were promoted to the command of a galley , though never before has such a command been given to any , save knights of long experience ; and now , for the third time , the councillors of one of the greatest of Italian cities are about to do you honour", "context_len": 107}}}, "knighthood": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 30, "context": "Three times have you earned special credit ; upon the first occasion , the grand master -- no mean judge of conduct and character -- deemed you worthy of secular knighthood , an honour which has not , in my memory , been bestowed at Rhodes upon any young knight ; on the second , you were promoted to the command of a galley , though never before has such a command been given to any , save knights of long experience ; and now , for the third time , the councillors of one of the greatest of Italian cities are about to do you honour", "context_len": 107}}}, "forfeit": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Then , Sir John , will you bestow it upon them ? The Order , as an Order , does not bestow the honour , but its members do not forfeit their right as knights to bestow it individually , and none among us are more worthy of admitting them to your rank than yourself", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 78, "context": "It was not until Hassan had stepped down into the boat that I knew he was going to take any one with him ; then he suddenly told two of his men to take their places by him , saying to me , as he did so , ' I know not whether this message is a snare ; but mind , if I see any signs of treachery , your life at any rate will pay the forfeit", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Handcuffed and under a strong escort , the spy arrested under the name of Pieter Waas was hurried along the slippery quay -- the bent , dejected figure of a man who , although uncondemned , knows that his life is forfeit", "context_len": 42}}}, "individually": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` May the great God whom I worship grant to my country , and for the benefit of Europe in general , a great and glorious victory ; and may no misconduct in any one tarnish it , and may humanity after victory be the predominant feature in the British fleet ! For myself individually , I commit my life to Him that made me , and may His blessing alight on my endeavours for serving my country faithfully ! To Him I resign myself , and the just cause which is entrusted to me to defend", "context_len": 97}}}, "commanders": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 21, "context": "And , although Lord Howard would himself have gone into battle even against such odds as that , yet the other commanders were greatly opposed to so rash an enterprise ; and the master of his own ship said he would rather jump into the sea than conduct her Majesty 's ship and the rest to be a prey to the enemy", "context_len": 62}}}, "worthily": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "Two names have been specially brought before me by commander Ricord , and by the good knight Sir John Boswell ; they are those of two young companions who , though knights of our Order , have not yet received secular knighthood , and this , in the opinion of these two knights , they have right worthily won", "context_len": 59}}}, "helmets": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "Oh , but it is a wonderful place ! and there are probably other rooms just as wonderful ; so a little closer look at the brass shields and the helmets , and the portraits of the sovereigns ranged along one side , and then , wholly unsuspicious of any disapproval , he walks over to the children and remarks that now he would like to see the other rooms , please", "context_len": 72}}}, "knighting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "D'Aubusson went on , turning to the knights around him , I am about , comrades , to undertake the office of knighting them", "context_len": 24}}}, "sponsors": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "The grand prior drew his sword , and the two young knights knelt before him , their sponsors standing beside them", "context_len": 21}}}, "affirmative": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "Knowing that Nancy was capable of starting immediately for Warford to change the article in question for one yet more costly , Peggy hastened to answer in the affirmative , and Bobby likewise assuring her that it would 'take the cake , and no mistake , ' it was carefully folded up again in its sheets of white tissue-paper , and put by until the great day should arrive", "context_len": 69}}}, "accolade": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "He speaks Turkish as well as our own tongue , and , as I told you , we received the accolade at the hands of the grand master , a year and a half ago", "context_len": 35}}}, "bestows": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "You see , a gage of this kind does not necessarily mean that there is any deep feeling between the knight who bears it and the lady who bestows it ; it shows only that she , on her part , feels it an honour that her gage should be worn by a distinguished knight , and , on his part , that he considers it as somewhat more than a compliment , and wears it as a proof of regard on the part of one whose good opinion at least he values", "context_len": 93}}}, "enabling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "You were , he tells me , the means of saving his life and that of your comrade , and , by thus enabling him to bear to the place of rendezvous the news of Sir Louis 's danger , were also the means of saving the lives of Sir Louis and his companions , and of bringing home in safety the prizes he had taken", "context_len": 66}}}, "foresee": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "And I foresee that the spirit of emulation , and the desire to show that , though still but professed knights , they are capable of performing as valiant deeds as their elders , will make the galley under your command one of the most successful in the Order", "context_len": 49}}}, "auberge": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 26, "context": "He had been puzzled when , four days before , he had received an order from the grand master that Ahmet , a servitor in the auberge of the English langue , should be permitted to pass the night in his house , with authority to move freely and without question , at any hour , in the courtyard of the gaol , and to depart at any hour , secretly and without observation , by the private gate", "context_len": 79}}}, "langue": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 30, "context": "He had been puzzled when , four days before , he had received an order from the grand master that Ahmet , a servitor in the auberge of the English langue , should be permitted to pass the night in his house , with authority to move freely and without question , at any hour , in the courtyard of the gaol , and to depart at any hour , secretly and without observation , by the private gate", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The Order will be a loser , for you would assuredly have risen to the grand priorage of your langue some day", "context_len": 22}}}, "cursory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "The five prizes turned out , when their cargoes were landed , to be much more valuable than the cursory examination made by the knights had warranted them in expecting", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "His observations , however , were so exceedingly cursory and so impartially comprehensive , including the path at his feet every whit as carefully as the sky above him , that Marie-Celeste had difficulty in preserving proper decorum", "context_len": 38}}}, "desperadoes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "They contained , indeed , an accumulation of the most valuable contents of the prizes taken by the pirates for a long time previously ; and as these desperadoes preyed upon Turkish commerce as well as Christian , the goods consisted largely of Eastern manufactures of all kinds", "context_len": 48}}}, "preyed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "They contained , indeed , an accumulation of the most valuable contents of the prizes taken by the pirates for a long time previously ; and as these desperadoes preyed upon Turkish commerce as well as Christian , the goods consisted largely of Eastern manufactures of all kinds", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "This impression grew after an attempt had been made to burn my uncle \u2019 s house , and I had been deliberately pushed from the quayside into the Camber by a seaman ; and these incidents so preyed upon my mind that I was unfeignedly glad when I was asked if I should like to go to sea", "context_len": 58}}}, "manufactures": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "They contained , indeed , an accumulation of the most valuable contents of the prizes taken by the pirates for a long time previously ; and as these desperadoes preyed upon Turkish commerce as well as Christian , the goods consisted largely of Eastern manufactures of all kinds", "context_len": 48}}}, "embroidery": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 77, "context": "Did these belong to her ? asked Marie-Celeste , standing in open-eyed wonder before a mosaic table , which had been cleared to make room for a quaint collection of foreign-looking , childish possessions -- a mandolin , a well-worn little missal , a remarkable doll , a necklace or two , numerous little childish trinkets , and thrown over a chair , standing close to the table , a little gown of white silk and exquisite embroidery , yellow and limp with age , but none the less dainty and lovely", "context_len": 92}}}, "carpets": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "Costly robes , delicate embroidery , superb carpets , shawls , goldsmiths ' work , and no small amount of jewels , were among the spoil collected , and the bulk of the merchandise captured was , two days later , despatched in galleys to Genoa and Marseilles , to be sold for the benefit of the Order", "context_len": 58}}}, "goldsmiths": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "He used also to go to the market of the goldsmiths and the market of the jewellers , and there he would sit and look upon the different kinds of jewels and see them bought and sold ; whereby he became aware that the fruits of the trees , wherewith he had filled the purses , [ 318 ] whenas he was in the treasure , were neither glass nor crystal , but jewels , and knew that he had happened upon great wealth , such as kings might nowise compass", "context_len": 91}}}, "mainland": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 17, "context": "Moreover , it is the nearest point for cutting off ships coming down between Corsica and the mainland , and they can , besides , snap up those proceeding from the south to Marseilles , as these , for the most part , pass between Sardinia and Corsica", "context_len": 48}}}, "ingratiate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "For nearly a week I was kept closely confined to the hut which had been assigned to me , never being permitted to go beyond the door of the building , where , when the sun had worked round far enough to cause the building to cast a shadow , I soon got into the way of sitting for an hour or two , doing my best to ingratiate myself with the inhabitants of the place , many of whom used to come and stare at me with never-ceasing curiosity and wonder , and with whom I used to laugh and chat , although of course neither party understood a word of what was said by the other", "context_len": 118}}}, "reprove": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "Richardson did not reprove him , but dismissed him with the rest of the class when the successful ones had demonstrated their problems", "context_len": 23}}}, "befits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "The next time that I have reason to complain of you , I shall bring the matter before the grand master , and represent to him that it were best to send you home , since you can not comport yourself to the servants of the auberge as befits a knight of the Order", "context_len": 54}}}, "hauteur": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "We have always borne the reputation of being specially kind to our servants , and it is intolerable that one , who has been but a short time only a professed knight , should behave with a hauteur and insolence that not even the oldest among us would permit himself", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "insolence": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "We have always borne the reputation of being specially kind to our servants , and it is intolerable that one , who has been but a short time only a professed knight , should behave with a hauteur and insolence that not even the oldest among us would permit himself", "context_len": 50}}}, "duelling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "More than once I have heard you address Sir Gervaise Tresham in a manner which , were not duelling forbidden by our rules , might bring about bloodshed ; and from what I have seen when I have been watching the exercises , he is as much your superior in arms as he is in manner and disposition", "context_len": 58}}}, "bloodshed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "As soon as the vessel was under way he abused his followers hotly for not having obeyed his orders to capture the knights without bloodshed ; but they pleaded that it was as much as they had been able to do to capture Gervaise in that way , and that they could never have overcome the four together , before the boats would have had time to come from the ship", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Accustomed though we were to scenes of bloodshed and violence , this weird outburst , the concentrated expression of mortal agony , held us spellbound", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "You can not wish to involve a woman in your schemes of bloodshed ! ''", "context_len": 15}}}, "abstained": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "This reproof had greatly subdued Robert Rivers ; and as he felt that any display of his jealousy of Gervaise would be resented by the other knights , and might result in serious consequences to himself , he abstained from any exhibition of it when they returned to the auberge , although he could not bring himself to join in the congratulations offered to them", "context_len": 65}}}, "nigh": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 114, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Keep me , my Father , oh , keep me from falling ! I had not sinned , had I felt thou wert nigh ; Speak , when the voice of the tempter is calling So that temptation before thee may fly", "context_len": 43}}}, "enabled": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 12, "context": "Our defences are all complete ; our recent peace with Egypt has enabled us to fill up our magazines with provisions of all kinds ; the inhabitants of the Island have had ample warning to move into the town , carrying with them everything of value ; so the Turks will obtain but little plunder , and will be able to gather no means of subsistence on the island , as every animal has been driven within the walls , and even the unripe corn has been reaped and brought in", "context_len": 91}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "At the same time that we learned , from a dying man left on board the ship the pirates captured yesterday , of the course they had taken , and were so enabled to follow them , we also learned that they were on their way to join a corsair fleet that was collecting at some point on the eastern side of Sardinia , with the intention of sweeping the coast of Italy", "context_len": 73}}}, "unduly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 54, "context": "While she could go with a clear and perfectly easy conscience , able to enjoy herself , and untroubled by doubts , she probably did so without harm to herself , so long as her life was not given to pleasures , \u2019 that is to say , so long as she did not unduly love these things , or allow them to occupy a wrong place in her life", "context_len": 70}}}, "refectory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "No doubt he had a happy thought , which turned out well ; but I can not see that there was anything wonderful in it , and it seemed to me unfair that one who is a mere boy should receive higher praise than yourself , who , as I heard Sir John and Sir Adam Tedbond say last night at the refectory , bore yourself right gallantly", "context_len": 68}}}, "scimitar": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "When you see a Moslem rushing at you , scimitar in hand , and know that if you do not cut him down he will cut you down , you naturally strike as hard and as quickly as you can", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "In a measure he had an advantage , wearing his breastplate ; yet as the scimitar is rarely used save for cutting , the armour did not serve him as readily as it would have done if he had been pitted against a man armed with a pointed sword", "context_len": 49}}}, "swordsman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "For what reason should you feel a contempt for him ? Although so much younger , he is a better swordsman and a better rider than you are", "context_len": 28}}}, "unassuming": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 50, "context": "His manly and athletic form , his frank and open manners , his spirit , his generosity , and a certain air of confidence , independence , and conscious superiority , which were combined , as they always are in the case of true greatness , with an unaffected and unassuming modesty -- these and other traits , which were obvious to all who saw him , in the person and character of Alexander , made every one his friend", "context_len": 80}}}, "considerate": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "No one has ever seen him out of temper ; he is always kind and considerate to the servants , and when in command of parties of slaves at the public works never says a harsh word to them , but treats them as if they were human beings , and not brute beasts", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 42, "context": "My feelings were relieved by Whistling Jim , who informed me that he had placed a very substantial ration in my holsters ; and I am free to say that , after Colonel Ryder , the negro was the most thoughtful and considerate person I have ever seen", "context_len": 48}}}, "arrogance": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "We see the same analogy reigning at the present day , when the sons of the wealthy , who are born to fortune , substitute pride , and arrogance , and vicious self-indulgence and waste for the modesty , and prudence , and virtue of their sires , by means of which the fortune was acquired", "context_len": 56}}}, "naught": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 68, "context": "I had a kind master for some months , and was treated as a friend rather than as a slave ; afterwards , I had the good fortune to be made the head of the labourers at the buildings in the sultan 's palace , and although I certainly worked with them , the labour was not greater than one could perform without distress , and I had naught to complain of as to my condition", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 21, "context": "Besides , what should I do if I left the Order without home , country , or means , and with naught to do but to sell my sword to some warlike monarch ? Besides , Caretto , I love the Order , and deem it the highest privilege to fight against the Moslems , and to uphold the banner of the Cross", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 90, "context": "This , doubtless , may be so , Sir Gervaise , the doge said , with a slight smile , but it is to the head that plans , rather than to the hand that strikes , that such success as you have achieved is due ; and the credit of this night attack is , as the cavalier Caretto tells me , wholly yours , for until you issued your final orders it seemed to him , and to the two good knights his companions , that there was naught to do but to remain in port and watch this corsair fleet sail away to carry out its work of destruction", "context_len": 112}}}, "cooled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "George spoke of himself as having then been a bit of a rebel \u2019 ; adding , But my republican zeal was very much cooled by the French Revolution ; and if a spark of it had remained , our own most contemptible revolution of 1830 would have extinguished it , and have fixed me for life a determined Conservative", "context_len": 60}}}, "assuredly": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 111, "context": "Accordingly , when she came up , he saw her and viewed her well , beyond his wish ; and from that time till now , O King of the Age , life hath not been pleasant to him [ 366 ] and he will e'en have me seek her of Thy Grace , [ 367 ] so thou mayst marry her with him , and I can not do away this conceit from his wit , for that the love of her hath gotten possession of his vitals , so that he saith to me , 'Know , O mother mine , that , except I attain my desire , assuredly I am a dead man", "context_len": 117}}}, "attaining": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "It is said that Alexander , finding that the various objects of human ambition which he had been so rapidly attaining by his victories and conquests for the past few years were insufficient to satisfy him , began now to aspire for some supernatural honors , and he accordingly conceived the design of having himself declared to be the son of a god", "context_len": 63}}}, "virtually": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Harcourt himself thought no more of the matter , though he laughingly told Gervaise that Rivers was by no means gratified at their both attaining the honour of secular knighthood , which virtually placed them over his head", "context_len": 38}}}, "galling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "Of course it is otherwise with you , who are as old as most of the other professed knights serving here ; but with me it is quite different , and as Rivers , somehow , has never been very friendly with me , of course it is doubly galling to him", "context_len": 52}}}, "rallying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The first news that the knights heard on their return from their expedition was that the Grand Master Orsini was seriously ill , and that , at his advanced age , the doctors feared there was little hope of his rallying", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Grico \u2019 s courage was so well established that he would have no trouble in rallying many bucks of other tribes under his leadership", "context_len": 24}}}, "invested": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 37, "context": "Swan , after whose report this passage is quoted ( Schoolcraft V , 279 ) states that the title of the chief of the confederacy was the great beloved man , while Milfort , who was himself invested with the charge of great warrior of the nation , styles him `` Le Tastan\u00e9gy ou grand chef de guerre , '' adding , however , that in his time he was the highest authority in civil and military affairs ( M\u00e9moire , Note to p", "context_len": 84}}}, "feebleness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "I can not say how far it is away , for I do not know at what height it hangs above the water ; but I should imagine , from the feebleness of the light , that it must be some two miles distant", "context_len": 44}}}, "ceremonies": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 38, "context": "[ 113 ] Milfort was installed into the charge of `` Great Warrior of the Nation '' in the Kaw\u00edta council-house by solemn orations , the smoking of the pipe , the drinking of the \u00e1ssi-decoct and other ceremonies , [ 114 ] and then conducted to the `` great house", "context_len": 51}}}, "surpassed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "Every knight of the Order in Rhodes was present , together with a number of the leading natives of the Island ; and although Gervaise had , since his arrival on the Island , seen many stately ceremonies , this far surpassed anything he had previously beheld", "context_len": 47}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Her three cousins -- Gwendolyn , Russell , and Belle -- had greeted her cordially as soon as the train drew up in a station which , for size and grandeur , surpassed her wildest dreams , and then escorted her between a bewildering panorama of flashing lights , brilliant shop windows , swiftly moving cars , and people in an endless stream to another depot , for her Uncle Robert resided in the suburbs", "context_len": 75}}}, "interviews": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "Men who had prospected for precious metals all summer in the mountains now bundled their pots and pans and blankets back to shelter for the winter ; the long-eared burros , lost in great rolls of bedding , stood about the tipple awaiting the result of their masters ' interviews with the mine boss , concerning work and the occupancy of any `` shack '' that might still be empty", "context_len": 70}}}, "dexterity": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 57, "context": "One alone lingered to cut some limbs from a hemlock bush , a whole armful of which he brought with him , and while the rest were passing the water from the beach , and pouring on the blazing wood-pile , he was switching out the flames , as they ran towards the beach , with a dexterity that showed he was no novice in fire-fighting", "context_len": 66}}}, "martial": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 54, "context": "They had bathed , and combed their matted hair , which was alone sufficient to transform them , but the feeling that they were once more free men , and knights of an honoured Order , had done even more to effect the change ; and although they looked thin and worn , the martial bearing had come back naturally as they donned their knightly robes and buckled on swords", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "The subjects interested him ; the accounts of the contentions , the rivalries , the exploits of these warriors , the delineations of their character and springs of action , and the narrations of the various incidents and events to which such a war gave rise , were all calculated to captivate the imagination of a young martial hero", "context_len": 59}}}, "interpreter": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 19, "context": "Listening , the three chums could hear the stern tones of the Major and the bass voice of the interpreter , for several of the South Africans spoke nothing but Taal -- a dialect comprised largely of Dutch , with a sprinkling of Zulu and Kaffir words", "context_len": 47}}}, "chaplain": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 87, "context": "the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change of air , ordering him off immediately to the high meadows of the Alps", "context_len": 128}}}, "conscientiously": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 52, "context": "It was not very easy reading , for the print had faded till it was almost the colour of the yellow leaves , and the 's 's ' were all long , so that Peggy found herself continually reading 'fins ' instead of 'sins ' ; but she did her best , conscientiously , and the old man nodded in his chair , sitting up briskly , however , when he felt her reproachful eyes upon him", "context_len": 77}}}, "utility": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "The events , however , that had taken place during the expedition had impressed him greatly with the utility of a knowledge of Turkish , for had it not been for Sir John Boswell 's possessing some acquaintance with the language , it would have been impossible to communicate with the rowers of their boat , or to have arranged the plan by which they had escaped the pirates", "context_len": 69}}}, "decisive": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 32, "context": "The party thus opposed were inactive and silent while Alexander was in their country , on his first visit to southern Greece ; but after his return they began to contemplate more decisive action , and afterward , when they heard of his having undertaken so desperate an enterprise as going northward with his forces , and actually crossing the Danube , they considered him as so completely out of the way that they grew very courageous , and meditated open rebellion", "context_len": 82}}}, "akin": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "With a feeling of complete confidence , akin to that of a child for its mother , the New Zealanders literally clung to the skirts of the barrage , at the same time adjuring the distant artillerymen to `` Push it along and let 's get on ! ''", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "Hartley personally , he sought him out , where he was at work in a corner of the meadow , and the result , as he had anticipated , was a very plain talk -- so unsparingly and pointedly plain that Harry winced a good deal in the process , and once or twice came near resenting a mode of procedure that seemed very much akin to knocking a fellow when he 's down", "context_len": 74}}}, "intercourse": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 26, "context": "Hawkins , who attempted to introduce some unity among the towns for the purpose of facilitating the transaction of business of the nation , and their intercourse with the United States Government , proposed various measures , as the classing of the towns into nine districts ; these were adopted at Tukabatchi by the chiefs of the nation , on November 27th , 1799", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 84, "context": "She was frequently in and out , as often as three or four times a day , and very seldom indeed less than twice ; moreover , she seemed exceedingly anxious to become my instructress in her own language , and as I had already felt heavily handicapped on several occasions by my inability to converse freely with those around me I made no demur , although I must confess that I at length began to view with vague disquietude the extreme freedom of intercourse thus instituted by the young woman", "context_len": 91}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Some of the southern septs had intercourse with them , mainly through the French colonists", "context_len": 15}}}, "deservedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Were we to send him out with the next galleys that start , I should be accused of favouritism , and the lad , who is now deservedly popular with all , would be regarded with envy , and possibly even with dislike", "context_len": 43}}}, "ceremonials": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Inform him , then , that from the present time he will be excused from all work , except , of course , to join in ceremonials when all are required to be present ; and if you , Sir John , will pick out from among the servitors here one who is well instructed and educated , and capable of writing as well as reading his language , I will similarly relieve him of all other work , and place him at the disposal of young Tresham", "context_len": 88}}}, "servitors": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "Inform him , then , that from the present time he will be excused from all work , except , of course , to join in ceremonials when all are required to be present ; and if you , Sir John , will pick out from among the servitors here one who is well instructed and educated , and capable of writing as well as reading his language , I will similarly relieve him of all other work , and place him at the disposal of young Tresham", "context_len": 88}}}, "persevere": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Tell the lad that I hope he will persevere until he obtains a complete knowledge of the tongue", "context_len": 18}}}, "landowner": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He had been a large landowner in Syria , and held a high official position in the province , but had been captured in a galley on his way to Constantinople , whither he was proceeding on an official mission", "context_len": 40}}}, "province": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 19, "context": "The auriferous ledges of the Cheroki country were said to be within `` the extreme confines of the Apalachi province '' ( Fontanedo , 1559 ) , and the Apalachi found by Narvaez was fifteen days ' march north of Aute , [ 49 ] a roadstead or harbor on the Gulf of Mexico , though the Indians had stated to him that it lay at a distance of nine days ' travel only", "context_len": 74}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "preferable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "They would only drag you out , and when they saw that there was naught the matter with you , would suspect that there must be some reason why you did not want to go , when , as every one knows , the position of the servitors is in every way preferable to ours", "context_len": 55}}}, "approbation": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was partly to assure you of my approbation that I sent for you , partly to inform you that I have appointed you to proceed with Suleiman Ali as the knight in charge of the vessel , and to receive the ransom agreed on , upon your handing him over", "context_len": 51}}}, "banter": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "Perhaps they would look killing if Margaret wore them ! She would not believe me , -- her playful banter , her arch smile , so reminded me of my Laura ! Margaret went on exactly as you would have done", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "At first Lorimer had tried to banter him out of the plan , insisting that the guardianship would be sufficient", "context_len": 20}}}, "mania": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Sometimes they pretended that his mania , as they considered it , arose from the fact that he was determined to become a renegade , and was fitting himself for a high position in the Turkish army", "context_len": 37}}}, "renegade": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 10, "context": "On the day after Paleologus , who was himself a renegade Greek , had established his camp , he sent in a herald to summon the city to surrender , at the same time making lavish promises that the lives and property of the native population should be respected , and that they should be allowed to continue to reside there , to enjoy the full exercise of their religion and of all other rights they possessed", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "In addition to this he had arranged with a renegade German , known as Maitre Georges , a man of very great ability as an artilleryman and engineer , to desert to the city , and there do all in his power to assist the besiegers , both by affording them information and by giving bad advice to the besieged", "context_len": 60}}}, "dervish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "At other times they insisted that his intention was to become a Turkish dervish , or to win a great Turkish heiress and settle in Syria", "context_len": 26}}}, "ransom": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 22, "context": "Alexander replied , that if he wished to marry his daughter he could do it without his consent ; as to the ransom , he was not in want of money ; in respect to Darius 's offering to give him up all west of the Euphrates , it was absurd for a man to speak of giving what was no longer his own ; that he had crossed too many seas in his military expeditions , since he left Macedon , to feel any concern about the rivers that he might find in his way ; and that he should continue to pursue Darius wherever he might retreat in search of safety and protection , and he had no fear but that he should find and conquer", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 43, "context": "But he has arranged that if at the end of ten days I have not succeeded in finding out anything , he will send down to the prison , and under the pretence that he wants to ask me some questions about what ransom my father would be likely to pay for me , he will have me up to the auberge , and there I can dye myself afresh", "context_len": 70}}}, "acceded": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 75, "context": "Will you tell it to us , Marie-Celeste ? And Marie-Celeste , nothing loath , and willing at last to substantiate her claims in the ears of doubting Albert , rested a hand comfortably on either arm of her chair , and commenced , preceding her narration with the request , You will correct me , wo n't you , if you find I do not tell it right ? to which Her Majesty smilingly acceded , first asking Miss Belmore to hand her a little jewelled miniature case from among the other treasures on the table", "context_len": 97}}}, "ransoms": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The request was at once acceded to , as it was the policy of the knights to accept ransoms for their prisoners , both because the sums so gained were useful , and because they were themselves compelled sometimes to pay ransom for members of the Order", "context_len": 47}}}, "pasha": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 80, "context": "Filelfo , who was greatly attached to D'Aubusson , saw by their manner that they wished to engage him in some intrigue , and , feigning great resentment and anger at his disgrace , led them on until they divulged the entire plot for D'Aubusson 's assassination , and made brilliant offers to him if he would afford them facilities for carrying it out , producing , in proof of their power to do so , a letter of the pasha , authorising them to make such promises in his name", "context_len": 91}}}, "provinces": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 2, "context": "The Muslim provinces of North-Western Africa , extending from the north-western boundary of Egypt to Cape Nun on the Mogador Coast , were known under the general name of El Meghreb ( modern Barbary ) and were divided into three parts , to wit ( 1 ) El Meghreb el Jewwaniy , Inner , i", "context_len": 55}}}, "anchorite": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 54, "context": "When the eunuchs and chamberlains and captains of the guard saw them , wonder took them and they were breathless for amaze at this sight , the like whereof they had never in their lives seen , and especially at the slave girls , each one of whom would ravish the wit of an anchorite", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Why , you old anchorite , have n't I a right to ? It 's a pity you would n't get a girl yourself ! ''", "context_len": 27}}}, "performing": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 47, "context": "In fact , it is probable that Alexander 's slaughter of the Persian army at Arbela , and subsequent spoliation of Susa , constitute , taken together , the most gigantic case of murder and robbery which was ever committed by man ; so that , in performing these deeds , the great hero attained at last to the glory of having perpetrated the grandest and most imposing of all human crimes", "context_len": 72}}}, "pleasing": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 17, "context": "I wish to gain experience and to do service to the Order , and so far from pleasing me , promotion would trouble and distress me , and , could it have been done , I would most gladly have sent home the prizes , instead of going to Genoa , and would myself have continued the cruise", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 46, "context": "They made no secret of the fact that my inquisitive glances were unwelcome , and so much did they scowl that I turned hastily away and looked out of the window , where the brilliant sunshine , beating down on the crowded courtyard , made a pleasing contrast to the dismal trio within", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "She was a girl of probably not more than sixteen years of age , but for all that a woman , and , as compared with the rest , a very pretty woman too ; quite light in colour , exquisitely shaped , and with a most pleasing expression of countenance , especially when she smiled , as she generally did when my eyes happened to meet hers", "context_len": 68}}}, "goodwill": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 64, "context": "In the case of a knight hospitaller it can only be the latter ; it is in his case merely a sign that he has so distinguished himself that some maiden feels a pride that her gift should be carried into battle by him , and , on his part , that he too is proud of the gift so bestowed by one whose goodwill he prizes", "context_len": 67}}}, "reconquered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They have , I must admit , been usually reconquered , but such might not be always the case ; and if such an event happened in Syria , this man 's influence and goodwill might be of great advantage to us , as it might well suit us to ally ourselves with Syria against Constantinople", "context_len": 56}}}, "ally": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "And grant that Allah make [ the thing ] possible and I take courage and say to him , ' I desire to ally myself to thee by [ marrying ] thy daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour with my son Alaeddin , ' they will straightway deem me mad and will put me out with ignominy and reproach ; nay , I need not tell thee that by this I shall fall into danger of death , and not I only , but thou also", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "The admiral received us kindly , and on receipt of our news ordered a signal to be flown recalling all officers and men belonging to the fleet who were on shore , and ere sunset the English squadron was making its way towards the Nore to chase and destroy the Hollanders \u2019 ally", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "He offered him any sum he might name as a ransom for his mother , wife , and child , and agreed that if he would deliver them up to him on the payment of the ransom , and depart from his dominions , he would thenceforth regard him as an ally and a friend", "context_len": 55}}}, "obliterated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "He admitted that , for a slave , he had been kindly and gently treated , and added that any unpleasant memories he might have retained had been obliterated by the nine months of pleasant companionship spent with you", "context_len": 39}}}, "companionship": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 38, "context": "And when , at night , impressed with the sense of solemnity which night always imparts to strange and novel scenes , they looked up to the bright round moon , pleased with the expression of cheerfulness and companionship which beams always in her light , to find her suddenly waning , changing her form , withdrawing her bright beams , and looking down upon them with a lurid and murky light , it was not surprising that they felt an emotion of terror", "context_len": 84}}}, "eschews": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It seems to me that when a young knight of seventeen eschews all pleasure , refrains from volunteering for service at sea , and spends his whole time in study , he does distinguish himself , and that very greatly", "context_len": 40}}}, "refrains": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It seems to me that when a young knight of seventeen eschews all pleasure , refrains from volunteering for service at sea , and spends his whole time in study , he does distinguish himself , and that very greatly", "context_len": 40}}}, "volunteering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "The members seemed to take copious notes in pocket-books , and asked occasional questions , one literary-looking lady , in spectacles and a large black hat with nodding feathers , being particularly insistent , and volunteering so much information that she threatened to usurp the place of the lecturer , and had to be gently suppressed , while an old gentleman distributed pamphlets broadcast , declaring he had had them specially printed for the occasion", "context_len": 75}}}, "sentences": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 36, "context": "In spite of the frequent use to which they are put they do not relieve the sentence of its heaviness to any perceptible extent ; for what we call incident clauses and also many co-ordinate principal sentences are uniformly expressed by groups of words , the verb of which stands in the -t or -n verbal , which nearest corresponds to our participle in -ing , or to having ( h", "context_len": 71}}}, "incessantly": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 49, "context": "I am sure , comrades , that there are few , if any , among us who would not infinitely prefer fighting the Moslems to spending our whole time in learning their language ; and I for one consider the fact that he has for nine months laboured so incessantly and assiduously that he has come , as Boswell says , to speak it like a native , is even more to his credit than the deed for which he was knighted", "context_len": 82}}}, "alteration": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 69, "context": "At any rate , I wrote a complimentary letter this morning from the grand master to the governor of Syria , and the bailiff of Spain , who was , as you know , for ten years a prisoner among the Turks , read it through at D'Aubusson 's request , to see that there was no error in it , and was good enough to pass it without alteration", "context_len": 70}}}, "infidel": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 79, "context": "We shall not this voyage have the encouragement and confidence inspired by the presence of those who have long and valiantly borne the standard of the Order ; but , on the other hand , we have to show that we are worthy of the confidence reposed in us , and that the young knights of the Order can be trusted to emulate the deeds of those who have rendered the name of the Hospitallers a terror to the infidel", "context_len": 80}}}, "thickest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "Then she linked her hand through Jane Denton 's arm , called to Laura Everett to follow her , while Annie Millar , Laura 's special friend , immediately turned to join the little group , and the four children soon found themselves in the shade of one of the thickest plantations in Sunnyside", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "D'Aubusson himself , although , in spite of the remonstrances of the knights , always in the thickest of the fray , was yet ever watchful , and quickly perceived where the defenders were hotly pressed , and where support was most needed", "context_len": 43}}}, "hottest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Six weeks \u2019 holiday in the year was permitted by the Society under which she worked , and she would seldom take more than a month of this in the hottest weather , that she might be able to get away for a few days at some other time , without infringing on her full ten months and a half of work", "context_len": 62}}}, "estates": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 78, "context": "Alexander 's was a simple burial procession , going from the palace where he died to the proper cemetery -- a march of a thousand miles , it is true , but all within his own dominions The greatness of it resulted simply from the magnitude of the scale on which every thing pertaining to the mighty here was performed , for it was nothing but a simple passage from the dwelling to the burial-ground on his own estates , after all", "context_len": 82}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Of course , in Genoa I was well known , though my family estates lie near Mantua , and my acquaintances flocked round me and urged me to stay until the galleys were ready for sea", "context_len": 36}}}, "irritated": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "But , dearest children , did we not all determine not to allow ourselves to be irritated and vexed by such things as have taken place this morning ? This is almost the first trial of the kind we have had", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` I do n't believe there 's any such word , '' while Big Chief shook an irritated fist at her", "context_len": 21}}}, "affectation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Gervaise especially was glad at his leaving the Island , for after he received the honour of knighthood , Rivers made a point of always addressing him with an affectation of deference and respect that often tried his temper to the utmost", "context_len": 42}}}, "deference": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "Tucker could not quite keep pace with the new-fashioned unconventionalities of young-lady work in London ; and one of the object-sermons , which most impressed me in my College days , was the beautiful self-restraint which these two sisters -- no longer young -- imposed upon themselves , in deference to their aged Mother \u2019 s wishes , in regard to that outside work which inclination , or one might say conviction , as well as opportunity and qualifications , impelled them to participate in", "context_len": 85}}}, "justify": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 66, "context": "If our vows had not forbidden our fighting I should have called him to account long ago ; but the only thing else to do was for me to lodge a formal complaint before the bailiff , of his continually offensive bearing and manner , which I could not bring myself to do , and indeed there was no special matter that would have seemed to justify me , no single speech that in itself would warrant such grave action on my part", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "I think you will all agree with me that the prediction is already in a fair way of being fulfilled , and that the services he has rendered to the Order justify us , his comrades of the English langue , in feeling proud of him", "context_len": 46}}}, "quarterstaffs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "I used to wish over and over again that we could but meet in some quiet spot in England , both unarmed , and could there settle the matter in good English fashion , with our fists , or even with a couple of quarterstaffs", "context_len": 45}}}, "unknightly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 106, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "sneers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "His sneers and petty insults were not sufficient to justify the drawing of blood , and there has been enough of that shed for the last twenty years in England without two brother knights betaking themselves to their swords against each other", "context_len": 42}}}, "betaking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "His sneers and petty insults were not sufficient to justify the drawing of blood , and there has been enough of that shed for the last twenty years in England without two brother knights betaking themselves to their swords against each other", "context_len": 42}}}, "assailing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "If so , we may have him back here again , especially if the Turks carry out their threat of assailing us , for an appeal will be made to all the grand priors for knights to aid in the defence", "context_len": 41}}}, "priors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "If so , we may have him back here again , especially if the Turks carry out their threat of assailing us , for an appeal will be made to all the grand priors for knights to aid in the defence", "context_len": 41}}}, "trader": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 50, "context": "The work of transforming the man-of-war into a seemingly peaceful merchantman was quickly performed , and long before the corsair ( for such there was no doubt she was ) came within range the Gannet was floundering along with yards badly squared , for all the world like a helpless trader , her course having been previously altered as if she were intent on running away", "context_len": 66}}}, "traders": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 14, "context": "None of our friends or kindred were on board the fleet ; and we traders have rather reason to rejoice , for , in the first place , so severe a lesson will keep the corsairs in their ports for a long time ; and in the second , had the fleet succeeded according to general expectation , so great a store of European goods would have been brought home that the market would have been glutted , and the goods in our storehouses would have lost all their value", "context_len": 90}}}, "avocations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "As at present war has not been formally declared between us and the sultan , peaceful traders , as you know , carry on their avocations unmolested either by the warships of the Turks or by ours ; they do not enter our ports without a special permit , and the crews are never allowed to land , in order that no detailed account of our fortifications may be taken to the sultan", "context_len": 73}}}, "warships": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "As at present war has not been formally declared between us and the sultan , peaceful traders , as you know , carry on their avocations unmolested either by the warships of the Turks or by ours ; they do not enter our ports without a special permit , and the crews are never allowed to land , in order that no detailed account of our fortifications may be taken to the sultan", "context_len": 73}}}, "detailed": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 22, "context": "She soon found to her sorrow , however , that she was mistaken : for Donald forthwith launched forth into such a detailed account of Lord Nelson 's history , from his voyage as a boy to the North Pole , to his last great , glorious battle , that the patience of that young lady , who was rather more eager at all times to impart information than to receive it , was sorely tried", "context_len": 76}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 45, "context": "Although within range of hostile guns , the `` dump '' had so far escaped serious damage , To bring the supplies nearer the lines by mechanical transfer would be to court disaster , so every ounce of food had to be carried by squads detailed for that purpose", "context_len": 49}}}, "brawls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Moreover , brawls might arise between them and the native population , or they might aid slaves to escape", "context_len": 19}}}, "population": {"freq": 58, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 58, "word_index": 67, "context": "He had calculated that he would be sold at one of the Levant ports , and had thought that with his knowledge of Turkish he should have no great difficulty in escaping from any master into whose hands he might fall , and taking his chance of either seizing a fishing boat , or of making his way in a trading ship to some district where the population was a mixed one , and where trade was winked at between the merchants there , and those at some of the Greek towns", "context_len": 92}}}, "consists": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "Such land is called alluvial ; it is always level , and , as it consists of successive deposits from the turbid waters of the river , made in the successive inundations , it forms always a very rich soil , deep and inexhaustible , and is , of course , extremely fertile", "context_len": 53}}}, "denounce": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "True , these men were his guests , and it might be considered that it was against his interests to denounce them , but he knew his claim for entertainment would be allowed him out of the funds found in possession of the party , with probably a liberal addition as a compensation for revealing their real character", "context_len": 58}}}, "safeguard": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "Should the worst happen , and you are captured by pirates , you will of course in the first place show them your safe conduct , and if I find that you do not return I shall send at once to the governor of Syria , complaining of your capture when furnished with his safeguard , and requesting him to order a search for you to be made at every port on the coast , with instructions that you are to be at once released , and either sent to him for return hither , or placed on board a craft bound for any Christian port ; while you , on your part , will endeavour to acquaint the Turkish authorities with the fact that you have been", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "Stupendous though the task was , not a man flinched , although several groused at the lack of consideration on the part of the Staff to send them against a prepared position in a practically-unbroken state ; which showed that the troops were generally ignorant of the measures taken to safeguard them", "context_len": 52}}}, "acquainting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "In due course she was adjudicated upon and condemned by the Mixed Commission ; but I did not remain at Sierra Leone for that to take place ; for upon our arrival we found that a packet had come in from England a few days previously bringing letters for me , acquainting me with the sad news of my father 's death and urging me to proceed home immediately to supervise the winding up of his affairs , and to assume the management of the very important property that he had left behind him", "context_len": 94}}}, "converse": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 55, "context": "She was frequently in and out , as often as three or four times a day , and very seldom indeed less than twice ; moreover , she seemed exceedingly anxious to become my instructress in her own language , and as I had already felt heavily handicapped on several occasions by my inability to converse freely with those around me I made no demur , although I must confess that I at length began to view with vague disquietude the extreme freedom of intercourse thus instituted by the young woman", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "So much did the lonely boy enjoy converse with those who loved him that it was with reluctance he finally sent them away , so that he could go on with his hunting", "context_len": 33}}}, "fanaticism": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "But do not land unless it is absolutely necessary , for you might fall a victim to the fanaticism of its inhabitants , and no knight has ever set foot on shore there since the ill fated day when the Moslems wrested it from us , bathed the ruined walls with the best blood of our Order and the Templars ' , and destroyed the last hope of our ever recovering the Holy Sepulchre", "context_len": 74}}}, "pounce": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "The grand master left me to choose my course ; but I think there is more danger by the coast -- where pirates may be hiding in unfrequented bays , in readiness to pounce upon a passing craft -- than in the open sea , where we should have at least the advantage that we could not be taken by surprise , and might make a race of it", "context_len": 69}}}, "goodbye": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then we bade him goodbye and a happy journey to Valhalla ; he was as cheerful as if he were going to his bridal ; we tried to appear as if we were too , but it tore my heart all the same", "context_len": 43}}}, "involves": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "[ Footnote 584 : I suppose the verb which I render `` caused [ sleep ] get the mastery , '' to be ghelleba , II of gheleba , as the only way of making sense of this passage , though this reading involves some irregularity from a grammatical point of view", "context_len": 52}}}, "swarms": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 15, "context": "At the moment when I began to doubt whether we could much longer withstand the swarms of fresh foes who continued to pour against us , while we were overcome by heat and labour , Sir Gervaise , who had throughout been fighting at my side , offered to swim into the port , to fit out a dozen of the merchant craft there as fire ships , and to tow them round into the midst of the Turkish vessels behind the two galleys that were lying ready for service", "context_len": 90}}}, "pashas": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The pashas and the officers of the ports have not the power , even had they the will , to put down piracy in their districts , and indeed are , as often as not , participators in the spoils", "context_len": 40}}}, "piracy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The pashas and the officers of the ports have not the power , even had they the will , to put down piracy in their districts , and indeed are , as often as not , participators in the spoils", "context_len": 40}}}, "participators": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The pashas and the officers of the ports have not the power , even had they the will , to put down piracy in their districts , and indeed are , as often as not , participators in the spoils", "context_len": 40}}}, "spoils": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 41, "context": "Besides the treasures , Alexander found also at Susa a number of trophies which had been brought by Xerxes from Greece ; for Xerxes had invaded Greece some hundred years before Alexander 's day , and had brought to Susa the spoils and the trophies of his victories", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` But do n't you think , old chap -- and I know you 'll excuse my mentioning it , '' said Tom -- '' do n't you think that it rather spoils , well , we 'll say the artistic beauty of it ? ''", "context_len": 46}}}, "expended": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 60, "context": "Under such circumstances , although victory might be possible , the chances of defeat would be far greater , and while victory could be only won at a great sacrifice of life , defeat would mean annihilation to the garrison , and the loss of the city upon whose fortifications such an enormous amount of money and labour had been expended", "context_len": 61}}}, "fortress": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 51, "context": "The bailiff was , as all knew , a man of dauntless courage , of wide experience and great judgment , and that he should believe that Rhodes would , although not in their time , inevitably fall , brought home to them for the first time the fact that their fortress was but an outpost of Europe , and one placed so distant from it that Christendom , in the hour of peril , might be unable to furnish them with aid", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It is indeed a grand and stately fortress", "context_len": 8}}}, "inlet": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 34, "context": "What with Egypt and the sultan their hands are too full for them to act as the police of the sea , and the consequence is that from every port , bay , and inlet , pirate craft set out -- some mere rowboats , some , like those under the command of Hassan Ali , veritable fleets", "context_len": 58}}}, "rowboats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "What with Egypt and the sultan their hands are too full for them to act as the police of the sea , and the consequence is that from every port , bay , and inlet , pirate craft set out -- some mere rowboats , some , like those under the command of Hassan Ali , veritable fleets", "context_len": 58}}}, "fleets": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "This , then , was the gallant Montague , a man who , under the Commonwealth , had earned renown when fighting with Blake the fleets of Holland and of Spain , and whose prompt action in co-operating with Monk and taking command of the fleet sent to fetch the king from Holland did much to earn the royal gratitude and favour", "context_len": 62}}}, "coasters": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "Thus the humblest coasters and the largest merchant craft go alike in fear of them , and I would that the sultan and Egypt and your Order would for two or three years put aside their differences , and confine their efforts to sweeping the seas of these pests , to storming their strongholds , and to inflicting such punishment upon them as that , for a very long time to come , peaceful merchants might carry on their trade without fear", "context_len": 82}}}, "confine": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "Thus the humblest coasters and the largest merchant craft go alike in fear of them , and I would that the sultan and Egypt and your Order would for two or three years put aside their differences , and confine their efforts to sweeping the seas of these pests , to storming their strongholds , and to inflicting such punishment upon them as that , for a very long time to come , peaceful merchants might carry on their trade without fear", "context_len": 82}}}, "pests": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "Thus the humblest coasters and the largest merchant craft go alike in fear of them , and I would that the sultan and Egypt and your Order would for two or three years put aside their differences , and confine their efforts to sweeping the seas of these pests , to storming their strongholds , and to inflicting such punishment upon them as that , for a very long time to come , peaceful merchants might carry on their trade without fear", "context_len": 82}}}, "storming": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "Thus the humblest coasters and the largest merchant craft go alike in fear of them , and I would that the sultan and Egypt and your Order would for two or three years put aside their differences , and confine their efforts to sweeping the seas of these pests , to storming their strongholds , and to inflicting such punishment upon them as that , for a very long time to come , peaceful merchants might carry on their trade without fear", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "A good illustration of this is afforded by the answer that he sent to Darius , about the time of the storming of Tyre , in reply to a second communication which he had received from him proposing terms of peace", "context_len": 41}}}, "strongholds": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "Thus the humblest coasters and the largest merchant craft go alike in fear of them , and I would that the sultan and Egypt and your Order would for two or three years put aside their differences , and confine their efforts to sweeping the seas of these pests , to storming their strongholds , and to inflicting such punishment upon them as that , for a very long time to come , peaceful merchants might carry on their trade without fear", "context_len": 82}}}, "merchants": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 81, "context": "He had calculated that he would be sold at one of the Levant ports , and had thought that with his knowledge of Turkish he should have no great difficulty in escaping from any master into whose hands he might fall , and taking his chance of either seizing a fishing boat , or of making his way in a trading ship to some district where the population was a mixed one , and where trade was winked at between the merchants there , and those at some of the Greek towns", "context_len": 92}}}, "safest": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "From a thorough comparative study of the Yuchi language , the Maskoki dialects and the local nomenclature of the country , we can alone expect any reliable information upon the extent and the area of territory anciently held by the Yuchi ; but at present it is safest to locate their `` priscan home '' upon both sides of Lower Savannah river", "context_len": 62}}}, "uninhabited": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Thence , till you get to Palestine , the country is utterly barren and uninhabited , while , running up the coast to Palestine , there are , save at Jaffa , no ports to speak of until you arrive at Acre ; and besides , the inhabitants there , even if pirates , would not venture to disregard the pasha 's safe conduct", "context_len": 64}}}, "intercept": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "You may meet with vessels on your way south , and doubtless some of them cruise off the barren coast I speak of , to intercept traders to and from Egypt and Acre , and other Syrian ports ; for the trade carried on is considerable , and , although of the same religion , the Turks are disposed to view the Egyptians as enemies rather than as friends , and would have even less hesitation in plundering them than in robbing their own countrymen", "context_len": 85}}}, "plundering": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 77, "context": "You may meet with vessels on your way south , and doubtless some of them cruise off the barren coast I speak of , to intercept traders to and from Egypt and Acre , and other Syrian ports ; for the trade carried on is considerable , and , although of the same religion , the Turks are disposed to view the Egyptians as enemies rather than as friends , and would have even less hesitation in plundering them than in robbing their own countrymen", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Destroying and plundering , the mighty host moved on its way , crossing into Hampshire , and doing , as the chronicle says , `` their old wont", "context_len": 28}}}, "inshore": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "By this time the day had dawned , and we could see that the ship \u2019 s bows were close inshore , so that had our bowsprit and jibboom remained they would have been touching the rocks , up which the broken water dashed in a terrific manner that made any attempt to swim ashore a matter of utter impossibility", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "Early on the morrow we weighed anchor , and , under the guidance of Pedro , towed the Gannet , there being no wind , through a gap in the reef , so that her new berth was within half a league of the landingplace -- though there was an inner reef close inshore , on which the sea brake , though not with such violence as on the outer reef", "context_len": 71}}}, "pursuer": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 48, "context": "Your Honor , smiled Marsh , we are seeking to establish the motive for the sudden flight of Ben Stone from this town , and we hope to show beyond doubt that he did not run away because he had committed theft , but because he knew this pursuer of his brother had arrived and feared unreasonably , doubtless that it boded harm to the blind lad", "context_len": 67}}}, "stating": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` 'Sta 'ueno ! '' they would whisper , watching Edith May , who upon such occasions was wont to enliven things by bursting into tears , and who commonly brought upon the following day a note from her mother , stating that Edith May must be excused for missing in spelling because she had not been at all well and had misunderstood the word", "context_len": 65}}}, "aga": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "I have received a letter from the pasha , stating that he had written to the grand master respecting the ransom of the aga , and sending me the amount which Suleiman 's son had offered", "context_len": 36}}}, "fairness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "As a signal was about to be given Phil went over to the coach , and , in a spirit of the utmost fairness , complained that the opposing center was continually offending in the matter of playing off side", "context_len": 40}}}, "trusty": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was difficult , nay , almost impossible , to get a trusty messenger through that multitude of fierce and bloodthirsty foes ; and yet it was of the utmost importance that Sir Colin should have some one to tell him what was passing within the Residency , and show him the best route by which his troops could approach", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "This feeling was produced as well by the intrigues of the British traders residing among them as by their hatred of the Cha'hta , who had entered into friendly relations with the French colonists , though they could not , by any means , be called their trusty allies", "context_len": 49}}}, "mantle": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 17, "context": "The only time he actually said anything was when he requested that I would not wear the mantle of the Order while in port , as sailors were a turbulent race , and it might lead to an attack upon me ; and as he was responsible to the pasha that his safe conduct should be respected , it would be necessary , if I declined to follow his advice , to keep a strong body of soldiers on board", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "So we took one of our ships , stuffed it full with things that would burn easily , made a funereal pile on the deck , and laid him thereon in state , with a mantle fit for a king thrown over him", "context_len": 43}}}, "insignia": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 123, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}}}, "turbulent": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "The only time he actually said anything was when he requested that I would not wear the mantle of the Order while in port , as sailors were a turbulent race , and it might lead to an attack upon me ; and as he was responsible to the pasha that his safe conduct should be respected , it would be necessary , if I declined to follow his advice , to keep a strong body of soldiers on board", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "They were considered as outcasts , on account of the turbulent and lawless elements which made up a large part of them", "context_len": 22}}}, "treasurer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "When it was the morning , Alaeddin arose and his treasurer brought him a costly suit of the richest of kings ' raiment ; so he donned it and sat down ; whereupon coffee was brought him with ambergris and he drank thereof and called for the horses", "context_len": 48}}}, "uneventful": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "It was held in the Town Hall , which proved none too large to hold comfortably the surprising number of curious persons who flocked thither ; for in any small country town that is somewhat removed from larger places those inhabitants who can spare the time to do so seldom fail to attend such an affair , which provides for their more or less uneventful lives a certain sort of entertainment and a topic out of the ordinary for discussion", "context_len": 80}}}, "cruisers": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 89, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}}}, "wager": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "There has been more than one wager made that you would be captured ; but I , for my part , was confident that your good fortune would not desert you", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 1, "context": "I wager my head that they \u2019 ll both of them come over here and make it : what a good time they will have puttering over it , and passing their compliments upon each other ! It \u2019 s my opinion , that when them old men die they won \u2019 t be buried with their wives , but alongside of each other", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Wake up ! you 've struck a higher rung on the ladder , and I 'll wager they 'll boost you fast", "context_len": 22}}}, "bastion": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Within a few minutes after the return of the grand master and his party ashore , the flags of the Order were run up to the flagstaffs of every fort and bastion : the bells of the churches chimed out a triumphant peal , and a salute was fired from the guns of the three water forts , while along the wall facing the port , the townspeople waved numberless gay flags as a welcome to the galley", "context_len": 78}}}, "seafront": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Of course , I only saw the seafront , and it was upon the land side that the attack was made", "context_len": 21}}}, "breaches": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "For a time they could hold the walls ; but as their number became reduced by the fire of the Turkish cannon and the battles at the breaches , they would at last be too weak any longer to repel the onslaughts of foes with an almost unlimited supply of soldiers", "context_len": 51}}}, "impregnable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 48, "context": "The spot was on the northern borders of the ancient kingdom of Sussex -- the land of the Saxon Ella -- a spot marvelously favoured by nature , occupying the summit of a low hill , which commanded a wide prospect on all sides , while itself almost impregnable when fortified , as it was , by ditches and mounds , dug in the usual Danish fashion , for the Danes owed much of their success to their skill in fortification", "context_len": 81}}}, "assaults": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 86, "context": "The company on board The Revenge was gradually getting less and less ; the Spanish ships , meanwhile , as they received a sufficient evidence of The Revenge's powers of destruction , dropped off , and their places were taken by others ; and thus it happened that ere the morning fifteen ships had been engaged , and all were so little pleased with the entertainment provided that they were far more willing to listen to proposals for an honourable arrangement than to make any more assaults", "context_len": 87}}}, "tens": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "Perhaps some years hence a Nihilist crop may rise from tens of thousands of sharp conceited lads whom the Government so carefully educate without God ! They can not possibly all get the prizes in life which they look for ; they won \u2019 t dig , -- so will naturally swell the dangerous classes", "context_len": 55}}}, "relinquish": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "Yes , said Donald very cordially ; and Marie-Celeste said yes as cordially as was possible , considering it meant she should again relinquish her province of story-teller ; but Harold , wholly unconscious , proceeded", "context_len": 36}}}, "threatens": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Certainly not in our time -- not until the Moslem power , which threatens not only us , but all Europe , has crumbled to dust", "context_len": 26}}}, "impassive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 76, "context": "I say not but that by a mighty effort of all Christendom , Palestine might be wrested from the infidels , as it was wrested before ; but the past shows us that while men or nations can be stirred to enthusiasm for a time , the fire does not last long , and once again the faithful few would be overwhelmed by the odds that would be brought against them , while Europe looked on impassive , if not indifferent", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "For over an hour they remained , and on coming on deck we noticed that Captain Poynings and his officers looked highly pleased , though the Portuguese still wore an impassive look", "context_len": 32}}}, "stoutest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 63, "context": "Of course , it was not very nice for the backs of boots and knickerbockers , and frocks and pinafores were apt to get sadly torn if they caught on a projecting angle ; but what child ever thought of clothes when a twenty-foot slide might be enjoyed ? Certainly not Peggy or Bobby , whose well-worn garments were generally made of the stoutest and most serviceable materials", "context_len": 68}}}, "prevail": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Those who have seen how things verily are in heathen lands , those who have witnessed the awful and desperate cruelties which there prevail , know what the argument is worth as to the present life", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 8, "context": "As soon as the agricultural interests began to prevail over the military , through the approach of the colonial settlements , this feature had to disappear , and the social order also changed from the gens or into that of civitas", "context_len": 41}}}, "overpowering": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 31, "context": "But I foresee that this can not last : the strongest defences , the stoutest hearts , and the bravest of hearts , can not in the long run prevail against overpowering numbers", "context_len": 33}}}, "repel": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 68, "context": "So , once more , by the matter of a few hours , Konrad von Feldoffer escaped a well-merited death ; while , through ignorance of the terrific preparations made for the blowing up of Messines Ridge , he had unwittingly done the Allies a good turn ; for instead of withdrawing the troops the Hun commander had concentrated a thousand on the mined ridge in order to repel an infantry attack that threatened only in the minds of the German staff", "context_len": 82}}}, "assault": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 49, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "With its labyrinths of wire and nests of skilfully-hidden machine-guns Messines Ridge was far more difficult to assault than in the earlier stages of the war , when French won and lost the important position", "context_len": 35}}}, "betake": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "Aunt Helen had broadly hinted that an hour 's practice of much-neglected pieces upon the piano in the Rose Parlour would be a profitable means of employing the time , and the fear lest this threat should become an actual command caused Peggy to shun the neighbourhood of the house with elaborate care , and betake herself , with a selection of pets , to the barn", "context_len": 67}}}, "succumb": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "As at Acre , we may repel assault after assault , we may cause army after army to betake themselves again to their ships ; but , as a rock is overwhelmed by the rising tide , so must Rhodes succumb at last , if left by Europe to bear alone the brunt of Moslem invasion", "context_len": 56}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "I am so afraid that Agnes , who has a little bit of me in her nature , will succumb utterly and show Irene that she is afraid of her", "context_len": 30}}}, "brunt": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 52, "context": "As at Acre , we may repel assault after assault , we may cause army after army to betake themselves again to their ships ; but , as a rock is overwhelmed by the rising tide , so must Rhodes succumb at last , if left by Europe to bear alone the brunt of Moslem invasion", "context_len": 56}}}, "fortify": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Every boy who knows the lure of exploring and who loves to rig up huts and caves and tree-houses to fortify himself against imaginary enemies will enjoy these books , for they give a vivid chronicle of the doings and inventions of a group of boys who are shipwrecked and have to make themselves snug and safe in tropical islands where the dangers are too real for play", "context_len": 68}}}, "bequeath": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Our share is but in the present , and I trust the flag of the Order will float over Rhodes as long , at least , as the lifetime of the youngest of us , and that we may bequeath the duty of upholding the Cross untarnished to those who come after us ; and we can then leave the issue in God 's hands", "context_len": 65}}}, "upholding": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 90, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "My brother has taken a bolder part in upholding Missions , and spreading religious literature , than almost any one else in the country ; therefore , if Benares had followed the example of Delhi , the terrible event might have been attributed to his excess of zeal", "context_len": 48}}}, "siege": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 98, "context": "The defence , though even more obstinate and desperate than the first , was attended with less success , for after inflicting enormous losses upon the great army , commanded by the Sultan Solyman himself , the town was forced to yield ; for although the Grand Master L'Isle Adam , and most of his knights , would have preferred to bury themselves beneath the ruins rather than yield , they were deterred from doing so , by the knowledge that it would have entailed the massacre of the whole of the inhabitants , who had throughout the siege fought valiantly in the defence of the town", "context_len": 107}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Alexander stopped to besiege it , and the siege delayed him two months", "context_len": 13}}}, "moat": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 34, "context": "It was likely that ere long they might be called upon to stand a siege , but , acquainted as they were with the strength of the place -- its deep and seemingly impassable moat , its massive walls , and protecting towers and bastions -- it had seemed to them that Rhodes was capable of withstanding all assaults , however numerous the foe , however oft repeated the invasion", "context_len": 70}}}, "bastions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "It was likely that ere long they might be called upon to stand a siege , but , acquainted as they were with the strength of the place -- its deep and seemingly impassable moat , its massive walls , and protecting towers and bastions -- it had seemed to them that Rhodes was capable of withstanding all assaults , however numerous the foe , however oft repeated the invasion", "context_len": 70}}}, "withstanding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "It was likely that ere long they might be called upon to stand a siege , but , acquainted as they were with the strength of the place -- its deep and seemingly impassable moat , its massive walls , and protecting towers and bastions -- it had seemed to them that Rhodes was capable of withstanding all assaults , however numerous the foe , however oft repeated the invasion", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "More than one who tried to spring into close quarters fell cleft to the chin , and , ere his assailants could combine for a general rush , a body of knights , who had just beaten off their assailants , fell upon the ranks of the janissaries with a force and fury there was no withstanding , and the chosen troops of the sultan for the first time broke and fled", "context_len": 72}}}, "oft": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 71, "context": "Despite the fact that he was on the losing side , my father was not backward in declaring his attachment to His Gracious Majesty King Charles II ; and although our neighbours , even the Roundheads , were favourably disposed to him , making allowance for his fiery temper , yet with strangers who passed along the great highway betwixt London Town and Portsmouth , honest Owen \u2019 s outspoken declarations oft led to wordy strife , and on occasions ended in blows", "context_len": 83}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Although I had oft journeyed across this bleak hill , never before had I seen it under such depressing circumstances", "context_len": 20}}}, "inevitably": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "The bailiff was , as all knew , a man of dauntless courage , of wide experience and great judgment , and that he should believe that Rhodes would , although not in their time , inevitably fall , brought home to them for the first time the fact that their fortress was but an outpost of Europe , and one placed so distant from it that Christendom , in the hour of peril , might be unable to furnish them with aid", "context_len": 83}}}, "outpost": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 55, "context": "The bailiff was , as all knew , a man of dauntless courage , of wide experience and great judgment , and that he should believe that Rhodes would , although not in their time , inevitably fall , brought home to them for the first time the fact that their fortress was but an outpost of Europe , and one placed so distant from it that Christendom , in the hour of peril , might be unable to furnish them with aid", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "The Smiths , father and son are ordered to find him , and the trail leads to an outpost rubber plantation , where Billy is lost in the jungle and captured by the cannibal Mangeroma Indians", "context_len": 36}}}, "tombs": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "supine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "If Christendom chooses to be supine , let Christendom suffer , say I", "context_len": 13}}}, "someday": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I shall fight neither better nor worse , friend Harcourt , because it may be that someday the Moslems are , as the bailiff seems to think , destined to lord it here", "context_len": 33}}}, "sherbet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "You have plenty of light and music , and it is pleasant to see families sitting together and enjoying themselves ; and if one is disposed for a cup of wine or of cool sherbet , they are delighted to give it , for they all are pleased when one of us joins a group", "context_len": 55}}}, "pledge": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "I might walk there twenty years by myself without making an acquaintance , and I do not pledge myself to join in these intimacies of yours", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 83, "context": "I have often wondered why maidens should so carelessly bestow their favours upon every knight who begged for them , and have said to myself that when my time came I would grant it but once , and only then to one whom I deemed worthy of it in all ways -- one in whose loyalty and honour I could trust implicitly , and who would regard it as something sacred , deeming it an honour to wear it , as being the pledge of my trust and esteem", "context_len": 89}}}, "intimacies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "I might walk there twenty years by myself without making an acquaintance , and I do not pledge myself to join in these intimacies of yours", "context_len": 26}}}, "celibacy": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 66, "context": "Now this was monkish work , and men who devoted themselves solely to such a life of charity as that in our Hospital at Jerusalem , might well renounce all human pleasures ; but when the great change was made by Master Raymond du Puy , and from a nursing body we became a brotherhood in arms , it seems to me that the vows of celibacy were no longer needful or desirable", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Their surprise was great when they heard that the knights were bound to celibacy", "context_len": 14}}}, "chivalry": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` Noble gentleman ! to entrap a woman and then declare she shall not be entrapped ! To gain whatever honor there may be in a woman 's capture by running ahead of his ruffians and capturing her himself ! This is Southern manliness -- this is Southern chivalry ! I am glad I know it for what it really is", "context_len": 61}}}, "enclosure": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 91, "context": "As we drew near to the exceedingly narrow gate in the palisade , which had been thrown open to admit us -- and which , I presently saw , was strongly guarded by a number of warriors armed with heavy , broad-bladed spears , murderous-looking swords , and small round shields , or targets , of wood covered with what looked like crocodile hide -- I became sensible of a horrible charnel-house smell ; but it was not until we had passed through the gate , and were inside the palisaded enclosure , that I discovered from whence it emanated", "context_len": 100}}}, "stonework": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Clumps of shrubs and flowers , surrounded by stonework some eight or ten inches high enclosing the earth in which they grew , were scattered here and there", "context_len": 28}}}, "enclosing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "The right in there of the sexton evidently referred to Braye Chapel , within a few feet of the door by which he had entered ; and glancing in through the open-work carving of the partition enclosing it , he discovered Marie-Celeste seated on a cushion on the floor , her back against the wall , busily writing away on the portfolio on her lap", "context_len": 65}}}, "cords": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 74, "context": "In the same letter , speaking of a young Indian , who had eagerly said to her that the Bible is the light of our eyes , and the root of our faith , \u2019 she sadly remarked that it was almost sickening \u2019 to think what the young Muhammadan would have to endure , did he openly confess Christ , \u2019 -- even while earnestly hoping that he would be constrained by the cords of love \u2019 to leave all and come forward", "context_len": 84}}}, "lute": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "A girl was playing a lute , and another singing to her accompaniment", "context_len": 13}}}, "saucily": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Are you waiting to be invited in , Sir Knight ? she went on saucily", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Indeed , your visit came pat to the minute , for I was just shutting up for the night when you must needs get in the way of the gate , '' and she laughed saucily", "context_len": 36}}}, "fortresses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "He knew of caves , of crevices only wide enough for himself to squeeze through , of deserted cabins , of hollow trees , of a host of similar hiding places and temporary fortresses", "context_len": 34}}}, "fosse": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "That great fosse would seem as if it could defy the efforts of an army to cross ; and yet the past has shown that even the strongest defences , held with the greatest bravery , can be carried by generals with immense armies , and careless how they sacrifice them so that they do but succeed", "context_len": 57}}}, "armies": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 9, "context": "As Philip had been made commander-in-chief of the Grecian armies which were about to undertake the conquest of Asia , and as , of course , his influence and power in all that related to that vast enterprise would be paramount and supreme ; and as all were ambitious to have a large share in the glory of that expedition , and to participate , as much as possible , in the power and in the renown which seemed to be at Philip 's disposal , all were , of course , very anxious to secure his favor", "context_len": 98}}}, "beholding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Assuredly , even the senior knights of the Order would rejoice at the opportunity of beholding a fortress so intimately connected with the past history of the Order", "context_len": 28}}}, "intimately": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "In giving the story of Charlotte Tucker , and of the growth of the Church at Batala , with which she was so intimately associated , it is of very real importance to show frankly both sides of the picture , -- the dark side , as well as the bright ; the cloudy as well as the sunshiny", "context_len": 59}}}, "accomplishment": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 46, "context": "There is little recorded of Simeon 's early life to indicate the character of the future leader of men ; for , to `` jump over half a dozen chairs in succession , and snuff a candle with his feet , '' is an ordinary schoolboy accomplishment", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Never had that accomplishment stood him in better stead", "context_len": 9}}}, "permanently": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "Moreover , could one or more of the guns be disabled permanently , others would be brought down and set in their place , so that nothing would be gained but a very short delay , which would be of no advantage to us , and certainly would in no way justify the risking of the life of so distinguished a young knight", "context_len": 63}}}, "yearly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 26, "context": "Vaughan , and stopped to give him some good advice as to the general upbringing of his family , even suggesting that Peggy -- for a yearly consideration -- should be transplanted to Pendlefield Rectory , to share the studies and maternal care of the five little Davenports , a proposal which he declined with a haste that was perhaps more emphatic than polite", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "She therefore kept to a certain sum of money yearly for her own expenses , while giving much away in addition ; she made her clothes last as long as it was possible for them to hold together ; she had hardly any furniture in her rooms ; and she refused all luxuries , including some things which in India are commonly reckoned not luxuries , but absolute necessaries", "context_len": 69}}}, "hasten": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 51, "context": "At the last moment , smitten by regret because fancied necessity seemed to compel him to leave without bidding the kind widow good-by , he seized a piece of brown paper and the stub of a pencil and sat down to write a few words of farewell Jerry urging him to hasten even while he was scribbling", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Now , sirrah , on thy life , hasten ! Provender for my beast ; a cup of spiced ale for myself", "context_len": 23}}}, "impossibilities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "But even they can not accomplish impossibilities", "context_len": 7}}}, "onslaughts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "For a time they could hold the walls ; but as their number became reduced by the fire of the Turkish cannon and the battles at the breaches , they would at last be too weak any longer to repel the onslaughts of foes with an almost unlimited supply of soldiers", "context_len": 51}}}, "inglorious": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "That is true enough , Gervaise admitted ; and to my mind it is shocking that four-fifths at least of the Order , pledged to oppose the infidels , should be occupied with the inglorious work of looking after the manors and estates of the society throughout Europe , while one-fifth , at most , are here performing the duties to which all are sworn", "context_len": 65}}}, "manors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "That is true enough , Gervaise admitted ; and to my mind it is shocking that four-fifths at least of the Order , pledged to oppose the infidels , should be occupied with the inglorious work of looking after the manors and estates of the society throughout Europe , while one-fifth , at most , are here performing the duties to which all are sworn", "context_len": 65}}}, "revenues": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 76, "context": "Withal , O my son , of regard for thy wish , needs must I take courage and go ; but , O my son , if the King receive me and honour me for the gift 's sake and I seek of him that which thou wilt in [ 343 ] the matter of marrying his daughter and he ask me , after the wont of the folk , what are thy possessions and thy revenues , what shall I say to him ? And most like , O my son , he will ask me of this ere he ask me of thyself", "context_len": 105}}}, "fraction": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 24, "context": "The prepaid charges on the Martin & Company consignment were seven dollars and seventy-five cents , or five cents for every hundred dollars or fraction of it over the first fifty dollars , which was charged for at regular tariff rates , twenty-five cents", "context_len": 44}}}, "armament": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "A similar warning had been given a month previously , but the fleet had sailed past the island , being bound for Phineka , which was the rendezvous where Mahomet 's great armament was to assemble", "context_len": 36}}}, "bantered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "After they had left , with the promise that it would not be long before they repeated their visit , Ralph bantered Gervaise on preferring the society of the merchant to that of his daughters", "context_len": 35}}}, "sapient": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "What should he want to listen for , most sapient knight ?", "context_len": 12}}}, "sensibly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "The heart of the old warrior was sensibly affected by this appeal , but not knowing the strength of Christian principle , he could not reconcile it with facts , and he walked sadly away", "context_len": 35}}}, "discernment": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "He would not have minded this much , however , only that , being endowed with average qualities of discernment , he soon realized he had been obliged to take a back seat in more senses than one", "context_len": 38}}}, "spies": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 41, "context": "Other people meet and talk , and enjoy what society there is in the city , without troubling their heads for a moment as to where people come from or what their business is here , still less whether they are spies", "context_len": 42}}}, "insufficient": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 68, "context": "Again , in 1889 , when a dear friend of mine , Pastor and Teacher in the United States of North America , with whom I had come out to India in 1869 , had decided to return to India as a Missionary , in order to join and to help me in the multifarious work at Clarkabad , and he found that the money in hand was insufficient to pay for his and his family \u2019 s voyage from Germany , and Miss Tucker heard of it , she immediately sent me 100 , with the direction to forward that sum to him , on condition that he had not left Germany again for America", "context_len": 116}}}, "straightway": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 62, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` [ 662 ] Therewith he disappeared , and as for Alaeddin , when he heard this , his wit fled from his head and his joints trembled at the cry wherewith the Marid cried out at him ; but he took heart and leaving his closet , went in straightway to his wife and feigned to her that his head irked him , of his knowledge that Fatimeh was renowned for the secret of healing [ 663 ] all aches and pains", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "Hardly had the genie set them down and gone out when the Sultan came to visit his daughter ; and when the Vizier 's son heard the door open , he straightway sprang out of bed , knowing that none might enter but the Sultan , and donned his clothes , [ 394 ] albeit this irked him sore , for that he would fain have warmed himself a little , having had no time [ to do so ] since he left the draught-house", "context_len": 85}}}, "legitimate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Lewis was upon business which necessitated haste and secrecy ; and knowing that all the other legitimate occupants of the dormitory were below stairs , he never gave a thought to the possibility that there might be some one else there , and believed himself quite alone", "context_len": 47}}}, "pry": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I do n't know your secrets , '' continued Bart earnestly , `` and I certainly shall not pry into them without your permission , but I want to repay your kindness in some way", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Now I don \u2019 t want to be curi \u2019 s an \u2019 pry inter nobody \u2019 s affairs , declared the widow ; but I do think you \u2019 d better trust me an \u2019 tell me about this business", "context_len": 41}}}, "distraction": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "Whilst she was thus engaged , the accursed Maugrabin presented himself and was exceeding rejoiced to see her on this wise , more by token that she received him with a smiling face , contrary to her wont ; so he redoubled in distraction for her love and longing for her", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Merriman , round and cherubic , looking as though no care had ever troubled her ; and there was Lucy , fair almost as the Singletons , with that particularly prim face which aggravated Rosamund , and which would certainly drive Irene to distraction", "context_len": 44}}}, "profession": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 32, "context": "The sole vow necessary , so far as I can see , would be that knights should remain unmarried and disposable at all times for service until ten years after making their profession , and that afterwards they should ever be ready to obey the summons to arms , on occasions when the safety of Rhodes , or the invasion of any Christian country by the Moslems , rendered their services needful , when they would come out just as the knights of Richard the Lion Heart went out as crusaders", "context_len": 91}}}, "fanatic": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was impossible to escape and leave this heroic fanatic to his fate ; but words were wasted on John Rogers , so , suddenly seizing the minister 's slight form in his stalwart arms , Dick thrust him up on the high window-sill and , swinging himself up beside him , dropped with his prisoner into the soft mud of a back lane", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Gad ! '' he said , suddenly staring at a face that rose above the crowd near him , `` I was almost fool enough to think the fanatic 's prophecy was come true , and there was General Harrison come alive again ! That young fellow yonder is the very marrow of him ! Some one of his family , I dare be sworn , poor wretch , and doubtless of the same way of thinking", "context_len": 78}}}, "obsolete": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The vow of poverty is long since obsolete", "context_len": 8}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Active : bat\u0101 plilis I strike ( now ) by one blow bat\u0101 spilis I strike ( now ) by several blows Passive : tchab\u00e1tapkas I am struck once , by one blow tchab\u00e1taspkas I am struck more than once ( obsolete ) Reciprocal : itibat\u00e1plikas we strike each other once itibat\u00e1spigas we strike each other repeatedly Reflective : ilbat\u0101 plilis I strike myself by one blow ilbat\u00e1spilis I strike myself by several blows Causative : batapl\u00eddshilis I cause to strike once batasp\u00eddshilis I cause to strike repeatedly", "context_len": 88}}}, "exceeding": {"freq": 40, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 69, "context": "son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not give them his daughter", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "You must now only think of the far more exceeding and eternal weight of glory", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "With all her exceeding kindness , hers could hardly be described as the true sympathetic temperament", "context_len": 16}}}, "scandal": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Trouble enough ; and she 's awful bad herself with the rheumatics , and a sickly baby , and a blind boy , and debts to pay , and that scandal of a doctor , and no way of laying up much ; for the children must be fed and warmed , bless their hearts ! and a police-sergeant 's pay ai n't no great ; yes , yes , honey , lots of trouble and no help for it as I see", "context_len": 84}}}, "incumbent": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "But I see not that it is in any way incumbent on us to give up all the pleasures of life", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 94, "context": "Aunt Helen fondly imagined herself to be a great disciplinarian , but her own lively youth was still such a recent remembrance that her eyes were wont to twinkle and the corners of her mouth to twitch in the middle of her severest scoldings , and the children always had a feeling that so long as they did not do anything rude or wrong , or run into any very imminent danger , their escapades were secretly condoned by their aunt , who admired pluck and spirit , however much she might feel it incumbent upon her to lecture them", "context_len": 100}}}, "asceticism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "But to affect asceticism is out of date and ridiculous", "context_len": 10}}}, "ascetic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I have certainly no wish to be an ascetic , Ralph", "context_len": 11}}}, "energies": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 8, "context": "His whole heart , and soul , and energies were concentrated , waiting and listening for a little voice , for the sight of a dear golden head , the return of the blue-eyed boy who was his own , and whom now that he had lost , he knew , indeed , to be bone of his bone , flesh of his flesh", "context_len": 64}}}, "snuffing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "When I see danger , I will do my best to ward it off , Gervaise ; but I can not go about with my nose in the air , snuffing danger like a hunting dog in pursuit of game", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Dick did not notice him , but kept up his snuffing as if begging an answer", "context_len": 16}}}, "recurred": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 23, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}}}, "overseers": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 24, "context": "He declared that he was absolutely unable to give any names whatever of those concerned in the plot , save those of the two overseers , as these had undertaken the work of suborning the warders and guards , though he admitted that he had on several occasions spoken to slaves as the gangs were on their way back to the prison , and had told them to be prepared to take part in a plan that was on foot for their rescue from slavery", "context_len": 85}}}, "effected": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 54, "context": "It appeared that he was ashore at the moment making arrangements for the shipment of a cargo of slaves on the next day ; but the chief mate was aboard , and upon representing myself to him as a shipwrecked Englishman who had been carried away captive into the interior , and had just effected my escape , he gave me permission to remain , saying that he had no doubt Captain Duquesne would receive me if I were willing to work my passage to Martinique", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 79, "context": "At the end of that time he roused himself from his stupor of grief and disappointment , and , abandoning the idea of any further attack upon the point that had cost him so dearly , he ordered the troops to move round and renew the attack upon the wall in front of the Jews ' quarter , and commence the construction of a battery on the edge of the great ditch facing the retrenchment behind the breach before effected", "context_len": 80}}}, "tax": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "If I were to require to be nursed at night -- which , thank God , I have not done -- I should feel inclined to run off somewhere or other , so as not to tax the strength of my nieces", "context_len": 42}}}, "confer": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "I am always ready to die , '' answered the pasha ; `` and so far from fearing your putting me to death you would confer a favour on me by so doing", "context_len": 33}}}, "recounted": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 5, "context": "Her gruesome voice as she recounted what the goblins did to naughty boys made several small sinners in the back benches shake in their shoes -- many innocent infants felt they should never dare to go to bed in the dark again ; and the wild shriek with which she ended her announcement that 'the goblins are about ' quite brought down the house , and the children shouted and yelled and cheered as the Rector strove to get order once more , and wind up the proceedings with the National Anthem", "context_len": 92}}}, "traitor": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 3, "context": "Thou diest a traitor \u2019 s death ; -- but wert thou ours , Then ev \u2019 ry bush around the fatal spot Should hold an armed defender , ev \u2019 ry knoll Conceal an ambushed friend , and at a word A wall of steel should bristle round thy breast ; Then swords should clash with swords , and they who came To shed thy blood lie weltering in their own", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Sir monk , '' was the reply , `` are you traitor to your king that you thus league yourself with his deadly enemies ? All that is done this night is done by his order", "context_len": 37}}}, "witted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "I will myself give the matter consideration , but as you have shown yourself so quick witted in following up the matter so far , I rely upon you more than myself to carry it farther", "context_len": 36}}}, "commend": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 68, "context": "`` In the Name of God , Amen , I , Owen Wentworth , late of Holwick in the countie of Yorks '' [ here followed some word that had been erased and `` yeoman '' written above ] `` being whole of bodie and perfect of mynde , do ordaine and make this my last will and testament in manner and forme followinge : First , I commend my soule into the handes of Almightie God my Creator , and my bodie to be buried in the churchyarde at Trotton", "context_len": 91}}}, "participate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 63, "context": "As Philip had been made commander-in-chief of the Grecian armies which were about to undertake the conquest of Asia , and as , of course , his influence and power in all that related to that vast enterprise would be paramount and supreme ; and as all were ambitious to have a large share in the glory of that expedition , and to participate , as much as possible , in the power and in the renown which seemed to be at Philip 's disposal , all were , of course , very anxious to secure his favor", "context_len": 98}}}, "plotters": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "And do you mean to say , Sir Gervaise , that you , a knight of the Order , are willing to submit to the indignity of being treated as a slave ? To keep up the disguise long enough to be taken into the confidence of the plotters , you might have to stay there for some time ; and if the prison officials believe you to be but an ordinary slave , you will be put to work either on the walls or in one of the galleys", "context_len": 90}}}, "involve": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 47, "context": "The dead was safe from further outrage , but the living were still at the mercy of private malice and public panic , and Richard found that to linger any longer near his old home would be but to draw suspicion on his friends , and even involve them in the fate that threatened himself", "context_len": 55}}}, "stains": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Here it is ! cried John , who was the first to perceive it ; here is the yellow stuff ; only see how it stains my hands", "context_len": 28}}}, "despatching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "I should think there would be no difficulty in obtaining such a suit , as garments of all kinds are brought here in prizes , and are bought up by some of the Greek merchants , who afterwards find opportunities of despatching them by craft trading among the islands", "context_len": 49}}}, "trading": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 60, "context": "He had calculated that he would be sold at one of the Levant ports , and had thought that with his knowledge of Turkish he should have no great difficulty in escaping from any master into whose hands he might fall , and taking his chance of either seizing a fishing boat , or of making his way in a trading ship to some district where the population was a mixed one , and where trade was winked at between the merchants there , and those at some of the Greek towns", "context_len": 92}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Well , sir , if 't was a matter of working my passage , I 'd be bound to go where there would be ships trading the right way ; but if I was with a gentleman as would oblige me with a loan , 't is easy to take ship from Harwich , or find one lying in Yarmouth Roads that would carry us part way , and then we could take passage from some French or Spanish port", "context_len": 81}}}, "brained": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "It seems to me a hare brained scheme , lad , Sir John , who had just joined him , said , as they issued from the auberge ; though I own , from what the bailiff tells me , that there must be some treacherous plot on hand , and when that is the case it is necessary that it should be probed to the bottom", "context_len": 67}}}, "probed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "It seems to me a hare brained scheme , lad , Sir John , who had just joined him , said , as they issued from the auberge ; though I own , from what the bailiff tells me , that there must be some treacherous plot on hand , and when that is the case it is necessary that it should be probed to the bottom", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "In spite of the pain caused by the rough-and-ready surgery , the father grasped his son \u2019 s hand , encouraging and comforting the boy , as the surgeon probed for the bullet", "context_len": 33}}}, "strangled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "It had originally been a grove of nut trees , and though some of these still flourished and bore nuts that had not their equal for size and flavour in all the country-side , they had for the most part been strangled by blackberry bushes and briers , and smothered by masses of wild clematis", "context_len": 55}}}, "masthead": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "I should decorate the galley with all the flags on board : should set every one to work to make great flags with the cross of the Order to hoist to the masthead of the prizes , instead of the little things that are now flying ; and under them we will hoist the flags of the corsairs , among which are those of Tripoli , Tunis , and Algiers", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Malcolm and the rest of the party halted and watched a masthead signalling-lamp that was blinking rapidly on the cruiser", "context_len": 20}}}, "dye": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 67, "context": "But he has arranged that if at the end of ten days I have not succeeded in finding out anything , he will send down to the prison , and under the pretence that he wants to ask me some questions about what ransom my father would be likely to pay for me , he will have me up to the auberge , and there I can dye myself afresh", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "He had no fear of a recognition , for the darkly stained skin and the black hair had so completely altered him that when he had looked at himself in a mirror , after the application of the dye , he was surprised to find that he would not have known it to be his own face", "context_len": 57}}}, "fade": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "That \u2019 s what it is , said Shagbark ; ye \u2019 re looking at the Medicine Bow Mountains , which lay a good many miles south ; afore long they \u2019 ll fade out of yer sight ; see anything else ?", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "She stayed a long , long time among the crumbling old walls , watching the gold fade gradually out of the sky", "context_len": 22}}}, "afresh": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 62, "context": "I noted that while those who had not succumbed to their sufferings were fed as they stood still bound to their posts , those who had become unconscious were temporarily released , and revived by being copiously soused with water , and , further , were allowed to eat and drink while seated on the ground , before they were lashed up afresh ; and I took the hint , feigning insensibility for the sake of the few minutes of temporary relief that I hoped thus to win", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 94, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Gaston , drawing the child to her , sat on the stair-landing and said nothing ; but sorrow and sympathy , struggling for the mastery in her heart , sent the bitter tears afresh to her eyes", "context_len": 37}}}, "renew": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 44, "context": "At the end of that time he roused himself from his stupor of grief and disappointment , and , abandoning the idea of any further attack upon the point that had cost him so dearly , he ordered the troops to move round and renew the attack upon the wall in front of the Jews ' quarter , and commence the construction of a battery on the edge of the great ditch facing the retrenchment behind the breach before effected", "context_len": 80}}}, "unclasped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Gervaise unclasped his mantle and gave it to Sir John , and then followed him on board", "context_len": 17}}}, "paces": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 72, "context": "Marching up to what I conjectured to be the king 's house -- from the fact that it was not only by far the largest dwelling in the enclosure , but was also distinguished by an exclusive embellishment in the form of a row of a dozen poles , each surmounted by a human skull , planted upright in the ground before it -- we halted at a distance of some twenty paces from the entrance , with our backs turned toward the crucifixion tree , the leafless branches of which overshadowed us , and waited", "context_len": 96}}}, "mingle": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "Will you let him sit down here by the helm ? My instructions are that he is not to mingle with the other slaves ; and as there are reasons why it is wished that his coming on board in this manner shall not be known to them , I myself am to take him up to one of the prisons , or at least to hand him over to the officer sent down from that prison to take up the captives allotted to it", "context_len": 85}}}, "captives": {"freq": 80, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 80, "word_index": 62, "context": "Many will , we may calculate , jump overboard and swim ashore rather than face us , and we shall be able , at any rate , to capture three or four of their craft , for , as they come out , one by one , we can all close round them ; and with nearly fifty knights , ninety released captives , burning for vengeance , and some fifty or sixty fishermen , for those from the fire ships will , of course , join us -- we shall make short work of them , and may even hope to entirely destroy their fleet", "context_len": 106}}}, "conveying": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "I may here mention that I never again saw the party of savages who brought me up the river , and I was therefore strengthened in the conclusion at which I had arrived that they had gone to all the trouble of conveying me that long distance in order that they might make a present of me , possibly as a peace-offering from their tribe , to King Banda , who , I soon had reason to believe , was a decidedly formidable potentate , as African kings went", "context_len": 89}}}, "servitor": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "He had been puzzled when , four days before , he had received an order from the grand master that Ahmet , a servitor in the auberge of the English langue , should be permitted to pass the night in his house , with authority to move freely and without question , at any hour , in the courtyard of the gaol , and to depart at any hour , secretly and without observation , by the private gate", "context_len": 79}}}, "overmuch": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "She did not soothe or pet her overmuch ; she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little", "context_len": 128}}}, "gangs": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 53, "context": "He declared that he was absolutely unable to give any names whatever of those concerned in the plot , save those of the two overseers , as these had undertaken the work of suborning the warders and guards , though he admitted that he had on several occasions spoken to slaves as the gangs were on their way back to the prison , and had told them to be prepared to take part in a plan that was on foot for their rescue from slavery", "context_len": 85}}}, "rower": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "He is a well built young fellow , Sir John , the officer remarked , and should make a good rower in a galley", "context_len": 24}}}, "elmo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Elmo", "context_len": 1}}}, "vaulted": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "He fell on his knees , and did not rise until the temptation was conquered , and then he walked steadily into the great vaulted room , of Roman construction , which served as the banqueting hall , and took his usual place by his father 's side", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 14, "context": "He succeeded in facing them sideways , ran their heads into some brush , vaulted over them , and , landing safely on his feet in front of them , grabbed them near the bits and held them snorting and trembling at a standstill", "context_len": 44}}}, "clings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "We have heard nothing of Alfgar -- we fear there is no hope ; but the prince clings to it , and says his dream will come true , and that Alfgar has yet a great work to do", "context_len": 39}}}, "wills": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 34, "context": "The whole question of our duty as Christians , on this point as on all others , hinges here , -- Are we doing , or are we not doing , that which God wills us to do ? All theories respecting outlays , values , results , sink into utter insignificance beside this question", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Still , as ever , she was guided and restrained by her Mother \u2019 s wishes , yielding her own desires when the two wills , or the two judgments , happened to lie in opposite directions", "context_len": 37}}}, "quarrying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I was with the gang quarrying stones , and a mass of rock fell upon it", "context_len": 16}}}, "infirmary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I have been in the infirmary for weeks , and I own that the Christian dogs treated me well", "context_len": 19}}}, "accursed": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 87, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 42, "context": "Then he embraced Alaeddin and fell to kissing him and said to him , `` Excuse me , O my son , for that I was going [ 623 ] to bereave thee of thy life , through the wickedness of yonder accursed sorcerer who cast thee into this pit ; and indeed , O my son , I was excusable in that which I did with thee , inasmuch as I saw myself bereft of my daughter and mine only one , who is dearer to me than my kingdom , and thou knowest how fathers ' hearts yearn upon their children , more by token that I have but the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 114}}}, "overseer": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 23, "context": "This officer tells me that you are skilled in masonry , the governor said , and has desired that you shall be appointed overseer of the gang whose duty it is to move the stones , saying he is sure that with half the slaves now employed you would get as much work done as at present", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "Wilfrid at Aescendune , it seems in some sort my duty , following the example of many worthy brethren , to write some account of the origin and history of the priory over which it has pleased God to make me overseer , and to note , as occasion serves from time to time , such passing events as seem worthy of remembrance ; which record , deposited in the archives of the house , may preserve our memory when our bodies are but dust , and other brethren fill our places in the choir", "context_len": 95}}}, "vacancies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I hear that he had been set aside to be appointed a servitor , but there are no vacancies , so they sent him here till one should occur ; and I was ordered to make him useful in other ways in the meantime", "context_len": 44}}}, "rye": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "The island being mostly forest , they had not a very large place in which to play , as part of that cleared was sown with winter rye , which had grown so much on the new , strong land , as to make it difficult to find the ball", "context_len": 50}}}, "enforced": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "A battalion picture-theatre , billiard rooms , voluntary swimming parades , boxing , and a variety of other indoor and outdoor games contributed to the men 's enjoyment ; and , although discipline was well enforced , there was a total absence of irritating petty restrictions that form a constant source of annoyance to the men of the New Armies of the Motherland", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 13, "context": "She could hardly believe it , hardly believe that in spite of her enforced absence from school , in spite of her inability to hand in her compositions for so many weeks , she had been the one to receive this much coveted opportunity , and that she was not only free to bestow it upon her own little country-woman , but that her own credit would redound to that of Miss Ashton", "context_len": 73}}}, "fountain": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 56, "context": "I never in my life had heard such a sweet even flow of song from the mouth of any living being , and I had once belonged to the son of a famous concert singer and had in her home heard many beautiful voices -- why ! it was like the lovely upward gush of a fountain -- clear , pure and exquisite", "context_len": 63}}}, "restoring": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 63, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}}}, "locking": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 14, "context": "Now Alaeddin had forgotten the lamp in his pavilion , [ 565 ] without locking it up in his treasury [ as was his wont ] , and one of the girls had seen it ; so she said to the princess , `` O my lady , methinketh I have seen an old lamp in my lord Alaeddin 's pavilion ; let us barter it with this man for a new one , so we may see an his speech be true or leasing", "context_len": 85}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Even the locking of the door , after Matt had entered the room , did not appear to have aroused any suspicions in McGlory \u2019 s mind", "context_len": 27}}}, "undertones": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "A few men sat in groups together , talking in undertones , but the greater number threw themselves down on the rushes , either to sleep or to think alone", "context_len": 30}}}, "precisely": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 4, "context": "Yet I scarcely knew precisely what it was that I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether", "context_len": 128}}}, "moody": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "Many , after years of captivity , had grown altogether reckless , and it was among these that there was most talking ; the younger men seemed , for the most part , silent and moody", "context_len": 36}}}, "exert": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 52, "context": "Did Edric remember the place , and the bloody event which only he and one other present connected with the spot ? -- for Edmund had been far away , and the matter had been hushed up , as far as was possible , by all the power and influence Ethelred could exert in his favourite 's cause , or rather his own , for he , the royal villain , shared the ill-gotten spoil", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I \u2019 m the coach , and as long as I continue in that capacity I \u2019 ll exert my authority to pull any man out of the game", "context_len": 29}}}, "hells": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Some men are foolish and obstinate , but , so far from doing any good , this only brings trouble on themselves ; they come in for punishment daily , they are closely watched , and their lives made hells for them", "context_len": 42}}}, "darkly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "He had no fear of a recognition , for the darkly stained skin and the black hair had so completely altered him that when he had looked at himself in a mirror , after the application of the dye , he was surprised to find that he would not have known it to be his own face", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "Campbell darkly intimated that should her opposition continue he would procure from two pliant physicians a certificate of her insanity and have her confined in that most terrible of prisons , a mad-house", "context_len": 33}}}, "indelible": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "If you could send one with a coloured cover , it could do either on bed or sofa ; and I think it might be well to put her name on it in indelible ink , for she is so very likely to give away such a desirable thing", "context_len": 49}}}, "swerve": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Sometimes they went ahead at full speed for a few hundred yards , as if to chase an adversary ; then they would swerve aside , the slaves on one side rowing , while those on the other backed , so as to make a rapid turn", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Suddenly there was a lessening of the speed , a swerve to the right , a quick stop , and the touring car was nosing a big iron gate , hung between square brick pillars", "context_len": 35}}}, "immovable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "On a rickety platform at the end of the of the room a preacher in a Geneva gown was holding forth in the most violent language of the sect , and all around the grim listeners hung on his words with immovable attention , leaning on their pikes or holding their drawn swords across their knees", "context_len": 56}}}, "divesting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then for an hour she lay quiet , while the knights , divesting themselves of their mantles and armour , worked one of the guns on the poop , aiming at a floating barrel moored for the purpose a mile out at sea", "context_len": 43}}}, "mantles": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 29, "context": "As it wound through the streets in splendid array , the grand master and officials in all their robes of state , the knights in full armour and the mantles of the Order , while the inhabitants in gala costume lined the streets , windows , and housetops , the ladies waving scarves and scattering flowers down on the knights , the roar of great cannon on the south side of the city showed that the Turks had commenced the attack in another quarter", "context_len": 84}}}, "pauses": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "Afterwards there had been music , Russell at the piano , and Gwendolyn and Belle with their violins , and she had sat upon the sofa by the gracious , new-found friend , who stroked her rough hand gently with her white jewelled fingers , and talked to her softly , in the pauses of the music , of what her mother was like as a girl", "context_len": 67}}}, "despatches": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "But I can tell you that when you have to row from sunrise to sunset , with perhaps but one or two pauses for a few minutes , it is a different thing altogether , especially if the galley is carrying despatches , and speed is necessary", "context_len": 47}}}, "taskmasters": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 61, "context": "They had been grievously led away by Mahomet , whom Gervaise regarded as a false teacher ; but as he had seen innumerable instances of the fidelity of the Moslems to their creed , and the punctuality and devotion with which the slaves said their daily prayers , exposed though they were to the scorn and even the anger of their taskmasters , he had quite lost , during his nine months of constant association with Suleiman Ali , the bigoted hatred of Mohammedanism so universal at the time", "context_len": 89}}}, "sawn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Now and then men have managed to become possessed of a file , and have , by long and patient work , sawn through a chain , and have , when a galley has been lying near our own shore , sprung overboard and escaped ; but for every attempt that succeeds there must be twenty failures , for the chains are frequently examined , and woe be to the man who is found to have been tampering with his", "context_len": 80}}}, "tampering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 77, "context": "Now and then men have managed to become possessed of a file , and have , by long and patient work , sawn through a chain , and have , when a galley has been lying near our own shore , sprung overboard and escaped ; but for every attempt that succeeds there must be twenty failures , for the chains are frequently examined , and woe be to the man who is found to have been tampering with his", "context_len": 80}}}, "abhorrence": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}}}, "bey": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "All followers of the Prophet have an equal chance , and one may be a soldier today , a bey tomorrow , and a pasha a year hence , if he be brave , or astute , or capable in any way beyond his fellows", "context_len": 45}}}, "astute": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "All followers of the Prophet have an equal chance , and one may be a soldier today , a bey tomorrow , and a pasha a year hence , if he be brave , or astute , or capable in any way beyond his fellows", "context_len": 45}}}, "warders": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 35, "context": "He declared that he was absolutely unable to give any names whatever of those concerned in the plot , save those of the two overseers , as these had undertaken the work of suborning the warders and guards , though he admitted that he had on several occasions spoken to slaves as the gangs were on their way back to the prison , and had told them to be prepared to take part in a plan that was on foot for their rescue from slavery", "context_len": 85}}}, "inferiors": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}}}, "fetters": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 33, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}}}, "padlocks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "For an hour or two , when we are not on a long passage , the padlocks are unfastened , in order that we may jump over and bathe , and exercise our limbs ; but at this time the knights are always on guard , and as we are without arms we are altogether powerless", "context_len": 56}}}, "corrupted": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 89, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "ravings": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` My son , '' said the bishop , in a deep calm voice , which seemed to still the ravings of the king , `` think of thy sins , repent , confess ; the Church hath power to loose in her Lord 's name , Who came to save sinners", "context_len": 52}}}, "dullest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Think you that if such a man as that were to know that there is hope , he would not be so changed that even the dullest would observe it ? I see you are a brisk young fellow , and I say to you , keep up your courage", "context_len": 50}}}, "observe": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 37, "context": "I do not , of course , wish you to interfere in any way in the conduct of the defence , in which you will take such share as you can ; but you are specially to observe how matters go , and if you see that the knights are pressed and in sore need of assistance to enable them to hold the post , you will at once bring the news to me , and I will hurry there with reinforcements", "context_len": 82}}}, "shackles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now , the first thing is to free the slaves of their shackles ; there are many white faces among them", "context_len": 21}}}, "vacancy": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 63, "context": "But all the same mother and I laughed and were cheerful , and the visitors who came into the drawing-room that afternoon thought what a picturesque group mother and I made , and what a lovely room it was , and how much superior to most boarding-houses ; and they inquired , more than one of them , when there would be a vacancy , and said they would write to Miss Mullins on the subject", "context_len": 76}}}, "penance": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 41, "context": "Oh , I do n't think he would like that kind of a governess any better than we should ourselves -- ' privately hoping that Father 's notions of self-sacrifice would not make him see fit to inflict so great a penance on himself and his family", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` How are we to begin ? Shall we do penance like the old monks ? Do you know , Rosamund '' -- here Irene linked her thin , almost steel-like little hand inside Rosamund 's arm -- '' that I am a most voracious reader ? Father was a great collector of books , and when I am tired of frightening the servants , and terrifying Frosty , and annoying mother , I spend days at a time in his library swallowing down the contents of his books", "context_len": 89}}}, "unsupportable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "As to the penance , Sir John , it has been nothing unsupportable", "context_len": 13}}}, "filth": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "You also will join us ; I know it is long since you have heard the words of pure doctrine , yet there is a blessing in reserve for the seed of the righteous , and the filth of the Presbyterian doctrines you learned in Scotland shall not cleave unto your feet to make them stumble in the way", "context_len": 59}}}, "surety": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "No , I have as yet heard nothing of a surety -- and yet enough to show me that my suspicions were justified , and that there is a plot of some sort on foot , and he related to the two knights the conversation he had had with the galley slave", "context_len": 52}}}, "surmises": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "My friend has commissioned me to see this matter through , for if his worst surmises are fulfilled Miss Mullins , and you also , Miss Wickham , and of course your mother , may find yourselves in an uncomfortable position", "context_len": 41}}}, "insistence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I think with you , that Sir Gervaise has fully justified his insistence in this matter , which I own I considered to be hare brained folly", "context_len": 27}}}, "overpower": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 57, "context": "And yet this morning , daughter , he braved , unarmed , the anger of Hassan the corsair , on the deck of his own ship ; and when the pirate called upon his men to seize him he threw one overboard , struck two more on to the deck , and it needed eight men to overpower him", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}}}, "loopholes": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "Springing on to a projecting ledge just outside one of the loopholes , Jim 's head was already above the level of the summit , and his outstretched arm was within a foot of the flagstaff , when something hurtled through the air , and , to Jim 's intense astonishment , a coil of rope fell heavily over his shoulders , and slipped to his waist", "context_len": 67}}}, "crossbars": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "There are six small loopholes -- one on each side of the door , and two in each of the side walls ; they are but four inches across and three feet in length , and there are two crossbars to each ; they are four feet from the floor", "context_len": 50}}}, "masquerading": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "As though a taxicab , minus its driver and running amuck into a stone wall , was not enough hard luck to throw across the path of Motor Matt , he had also to deal with a young woman masquerading in man \u2019 s attire", "context_len": 45}}}, "implicated": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "Only one-half of the boys were implicated in that affair ; they had already been punished by the restrictions which had been placed upon them , and were to be further disgraced by the public reprimand which he intended to give them on the dismissal of the school ; and these culprits were probably dreading this or some other severe punishment which would be meted out to them by the report of their misconduct which would be sent home", "context_len": 79}}}, "farthest": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "On the third evening , after the allowance of broth and bread had been consumed , and the door was closed and locked upon them for the night , three or four of the galley slaves , after talking eagerly together , beckoned to the others to gather round them at the end of the room farthest from the door", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 10, "context": "The stranger took up the lamp and walked to the farthest recess of the dungeon , where , concealed amongst the rude carvings with which the builders had ornamented the wall , was a rose carved in stone", "context_len": 38}}}, "insure": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 78, "context": "We delayed our departure on one excuse and another , until finally Bill , who was to be our guide , grew irritable ; and even then we made a further delay while Jack pencilled a note to his colonel , which Harry was to take charge of as long as there was danger of his capture by roving bands of Federals , and then it was to be given to the guide , who thought he could insure its delivery", "context_len": 81}}}, "unsealed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Thanks be to Allah , the All Powerful , one of the men began , my lips are unsealed , and I can tell you the great news that our hour for escape from bondage is at hand ! We need not fear the warder there , he went on , as several eyes were turned apprehensively towards the guard , who , with his spear beside him , was leaning carelessly against the wall at the farther end , looking through the window into the courtyard ; he is with us", "context_len": 92}}}, "bondage": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 81, "context": "Then Alaeddin brought them up to the upper pavilion , [ 621 ] where they sat down and the Sultan proceeded to question the princess of her case and of that which had befallen her , whilst [ 622 ] she acquainted him with all that had happened to her and said to him , `` O my father , I breathed not till yesterday , when I saw my husband , and he it is who delivered me from the bondage of a Maugrabin , an accursed sorcerer , methinketh there is not a filthier than he on the face of the earth ; and but for my beloved Alaeddin , I had not won free of him and thou hadst not seen me all thy life", "context_len": 128}}}, "warder": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "Thanks be to Allah , the All Powerful , one of the men began , my lips are unsealed , and I can tell you the great news that our hour for escape from bondage is at hand ! We need not fear the warder there , he went on , as several eyes were turned apprehensively towards the guard , who , with his spear beside him , was leaning carelessly against the wall at the farther end , looking through the window into the courtyard ; he is with us", "context_len": 92}}}, "avowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Two of our taskmasters belonging to the Order have been bribed by large sums of money , and several of the overseers , who are half of our blood , have eagerly embraced the prospect of returning to their mothers ' country , and of avowing openly their belief in our religion", "context_len": 52}}}, "guards": {"freq": 99, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 98, "word_index": 40, "context": "It was nearly noon when I was conducted back to my hut after my futile attempt to cure the king ; and it was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "His dog probably guards his parcels , when he has occasion to stop at a house and leave his cart", "context_len": 20}}}, "duplicates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Duplicates of the keys have been made , and will be hidden in a spot known to some of our party , close to the gate", "context_len": 26}}}, "forts": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 89, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}}}, "embarking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "I hear that there was a talk of freeing all , and that we , instead of embarking at first , should make for the other prisons , burst open the doors , and rescue the others ; but by the time we could do so the knights would be all in arms , and our enterprise would fail altogether , for as but a small proportion of us can obtain weapons , we could not hope to overcome them", "context_len": 80}}}, "silencing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "One is to row to each of the forts to carry off the party that will have been engaged in silencing its cannon", "context_len": 23}}}, "exulting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "He had come from the gymnasium and onto the football field feeling his heart exulting with a new-found pleasure in life ; and now this boy , whom he had believed so far away , whom he had hoped never again to see , rose before him to push aside the happiness almost within his grasp", "context_len": 56}}}, "speedy": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 18, "context": "But Lilian popped down on the grass , and pulled off her shoes and stockings as the most speedy way of solving the difficulty , and hustled Miss Peggy home with all possible haste , to be dried ; for the water was very cold , and her teeth were chattering in her head , while her dripping garments left such a moist track as she went that Bobby unfeelingly compared her to a watering-cart", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "I wondered whether by any chance the fatal wounds were actually inflicted by interested persons who desired to put as speedy an end as possible to the sufferings of their unfortunate friends ; and if so , whether the idea would occur to Ama to enlist the services of a few good marksmen in my behalf when my turn should come", "context_len": 61}}}, "advent": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 13, "context": "The deep , deep bay of the mastiff sometimes gave warning of the advent of a stranger , or of the step heard from the distance , in the still deep night ; but this was all that Alfgar could learn of the outer world , from which he was banished at so critical a moment", "context_len": 56}}}, "unbarred": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "There were two long narrow windows at the end , unbarred , for they were too small for the human body to pass through them ; they looked upon the valley and , river beneath , for although the dungeon was below the level of the courtyard , it was above that of the neighbourhood", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "On the day when I send you word that Hassan is here , I will see that the gate of my garden is unbarred at night , and will place the garments down just behind it", "context_len": 36}}}, "unquestioned": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "Had it not been for that , he would have attempted to escape at the first opportunity , and such an opportunity could not fail to present itself ere long , for he had but to manage to possess himself of Moslem garments to be able to move about unquestioned in any Turkish town", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He could suspend any officer from duty , and had unquestioned power of life and death over the crews", "context_len": 19}}}, "buckled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 67, "context": "They had bathed , and combed their matted hair , which was alone sufficient to transform them , but the feeling that they were once more free men , and knights of an honoured Order , had done even more to effect the change ; and although they looked thin and worn , the martial bearing had come back naturally as they donned their knightly robes and buckled on swords", "context_len": 70}}}, "humiliations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "Whether your news be good or bad , whether you have found that it is a general rising of the slaves that is intended , or a plot by which a handful of slaves may seize a boat and escape , the gratitude of the Order is no less due to you for the hardships and humiliations you have undergone on its behalf", "context_len": 63}}}, "undergone": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "Whether your news be good or bad , whether you have found that it is a general rising of the slaves that is intended , or a plot by which a handful of slaves may seize a boat and escape , the gratitude of the Order is no less due to you for the hardships and humiliations you have undergone on its behalf", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "After having been so long on board , and being weak in body through the hardships I had undergone , I felt weary and ill before half the ascent was completed ; so , while my shipmates proceeded , I was obliged to sit down to recover my breath", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "His physical and his nervous system had undergone so great a strain and fatigue that he could not at once relapse into slumber", "context_len": 23}}}, "liberated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "The whole of the slaves there are to be liberated at twelve o'clock tonight , are to seize the three water towers and to spike the guns , to burn all the shipping in the harbour , to make off with six galleys , and destroy the rest", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "There were , as I have mentioned , five men in each watch -- one of the two liberated slaves , a Genoese , who spoke no English , being in mine , while the other , a negro , was placed in the second", "context_len": 45}}}, "spike": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The whole of the slaves there are to be liberated at twelve o'clock tonight , are to seize the three water towers and to spike the guns , to burn all the shipping in the harbour , to make off with six galleys , and destroy the rest", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 116, "context": "or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing his victims to the tree with spike nails driven through the hands and feet into the tough timber", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "traitors": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 13, "context": "It could only be possible were there , not one but many , traitors among the officials ; if this is so , then indeed am I a dull ass , and unfit for my duty here , of which I shall pray you to relieve me , and to order such punishment as the council may deem just to be allotted to me for having so signally been hoodwinked", "context_len": 70}}}, "suborned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The less stir made the better , for no one can say whether they may not have suborned some of the servitors to send instant news of any unusual movements in any of the auberges", "context_len": 35}}}, "torches": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 64, "context": "were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all service", "context_len": 128}}}, "newly": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` Lord ha \u2019 mercy on me if \u2019 tis not young Master Wentworth ! '' he exclaimed , showing that , though grown in stature , I had not outgrown the appearance of my boyhood ; and when Drake told him of my newly found title , the old man tottered away to let the frequenters of the inn know the news ; whereat we , unwilling to tarry longer , pushed on towards the towering heights of Hindhead", "context_len": 80}}}, "purposely": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` The Sultan `` goes round in the kiosk '' and seeing `` the casement ( shubbak ) , which Alaeddin had purposely left defective , without completion , '' said to the Vizier , `` Knowest thou the reason ( or cause ) of the lack of completion of this casement and its lattices ? '' ( shearihi , or quaere , `` [ this ] lattice , '' the copyist having probably omitted by mistake the diacritical points over the final ha )", "context_len": 85}}}, "saints": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 14, "context": "They fascinated her with their crude drawing and colours still vivid and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined", "context_len": 128}}}, "entail": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "Miss Tucker was much distressed about the Afghan war ; not because of any possible peril or discomfort to herself , but because her judgment disapproved of it as a whole , and also because of the sufferings which she knew it must entail upon the soldiers", "context_len": 47}}}, "condemn": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 61, "context": "As for me , my father knows that there is only one person -- myself -- who would dare to do what I have done for you to-night ; and even were I to succeed in returning to my own quarters undetected -- which is exceedingly doubtful -- his anger at your loss will be so great that he would assuredly condemn me to take your place at the stake", "context_len": 70}}}, "spiked": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 95, "context": "I have learned that at twelve tonight there will be a general rising of the slaves in this prison , and that , aided by treachery , they will free themselves from their fetters , overpower and slay such of the guards in their rooms as have not been bribed , throw open the gates , make their way down to the port , burn all the shipping there , and make off in the six galleys manned by them , having first overpowered the sentries in the three forts commanding the entrance , and spiked the guns", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "In an instant the ca\u00f1on was a wild tumult of thunder and roaring water , and Lola , barely keeping her feet , had laid hold of a pi\u00f1on on the lower slope and was burying her head in the spiked branches", "context_len": 42}}}, "stupefaction": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Major Sturgis received them in amazement bordering upon stupefaction , and counted them over three times", "context_len": 16}}}, "hesitatingly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` You do n't happen to love her well enough to join her side , do you ? '' This question was put hesitatingly , and , as I thought , with some shy hope that it would receive consideration", "context_len": 40}}}, "deceiving": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "And when it was too late he found that the desertion of the fort was only a cleverly-planned trick on the part of its defender , who had crashed noisily into the bushes , in the hope of deceiving the attacking party into the belief that the fort was empty", "context_len": 50}}}, "deem": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 75, "context": "maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions , but she can not look on me as all a stranger , even if she hold no memory of those old days at Highgate", "context_len": 128}}}, "signally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 67, "context": "It could only be possible were there , not one but many , traitors among the officials ; if this is so , then indeed am I a dull ass , and unfit for my duty here , of which I shall pray you to relieve me , and to order such punishment as the council may deem just to be allotted to me for having so signally been hoodwinked", "context_len": 70}}}, "hoodwinked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 69, "context": "It could only be possible were there , not one but many , traitors among the officials ; if this is so , then indeed am I a dull ass , and unfit for my duty here , of which I shall pray you to relieve me , and to order such punishment as the council may deem just to be allotted to me for having so signally been hoodwinked", "context_len": 70}}}, "matured": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The plot has been matured , not as a consequence of any laxity of discipline in the prison , but from deliberate treachery , against which no mortal being can guard", "context_len": 31}}}, "laxity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The plot has been matured , not as a consequence of any laxity of discipline in the prison , but from deliberate treachery , against which no mortal being can guard", "context_len": 31}}}, "connivance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The traitors are two of the officials who , being members of the Order , none would suspect of connivance in such a deed", "context_len": 24}}}, "emissary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "With them are several -- I know not how many -- under officials , warders , and guards ; all these have been bribed by an emissary from Constantinople , now in the town , and who is doubtless furnished with large means", "context_len": 43}}}, "cell": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "Mechanically he cast his eyes around the cell , and tried to trace out the pattern of the flooring , when he saw that the central figure , around which the circles and squares converged , was justice , with the scales , and the motto , `` Fiat justitia", "context_len": 50}}}, "clashing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The knights were enjoined to move as noiselessly as possible , and to avoid all clashing of arms against armour", "context_len": 20}}}, "deaden": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "Re-entering the great hall , the chamberlain and prince ascended the broad staircase which conducted to the upper chambers , and passing along a passage thickly strewn with rushes to deaden the sound , for carpets were unknown , they came to a door at the end , where the chamberlain paused and knocked", "context_len": 54}}}, "buttress": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "There 's a black mass of rock standing out like a buttress just to the west of the little harbour , running right into the sea , and going down straight like a wall into the deep clear water at its foot , as if to say to the waves , `` Thus far may you come , and no farther", "context_len": 61}}}, "bared": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "As I rounded the rock and came in sight of him he rose to his feet , with his two front paws on Ama 's body , and bared his great fangs at me in a hideous grin , as he gave utterance to a snarling growl that might well have struck terror to the boldest", "context_len": 56}}}, "executed": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 56, "context": "Constant references as to his fondness for female society , offers to see him home to protect him on the way , tender inquiries as to the state of his eyes and the condition of his ears , filled him with confusion , while large portraits of Jones in the clutches of an imaginary Amazon , executed with schoolboy talent and vigour , adorned the walls of the playground and the palings of the cricket-field", "context_len": 75}}}, "cloven": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "So desperate was their attack that the latter were forced to use their swords , which indeed , burning with rage at the treachery of these men , they were not backward in doing , and in less than a minute the five traitors lay , with cloven heads , dead on the pavement", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Two of our men went down before him , one having his skull cloven by a lightning sweep of the corsair \u2019 s razorlike blade , the other having his sword arm cut completely through at the wrist", "context_len": 38}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 85, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "arch": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "He hurried on as fast as his weary limbs could carry him , with a terror of the icy darkness that was closing in like the shadow of death descending upon him , and almost at a run he reached his goal , and stood on the balustraded stone arch that crossed the ice-encumbered moat of the old house", "context_len": 59}}}, "fastenings": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 14, "context": "The rest of the day was spent in cutting firewood , seeing to the fastenings of the tents , putting up cots , arranging their baggage and food supplies , and in putting together their shot guns and rifles , for each lad had two weapons", "context_len": 46}}}, "crimes": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 47, "context": "And certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most", "context_len": 128}}}, "daggers": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "The conflict in the yard told them that the plot had been discovered , and as they saw us approaching the house , they dared not meet the punishment of their crimes , and have fallen by their own daggers", "context_len": 40}}}, "incite": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The attempt has been detected , and has failed ; but were it talked of , it might incite others to repeat the attempt , and possibly with better success", "context_len": 30}}}, "revolt": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 34, "context": "A further accession resulted from the arrival of the Y\u00e1massi , whom Governor Craven had driven into Georgia and into the arms of their enemies , the Spaniards of Florida , after suppressing the revolt of 1715 in which they had participated", "context_len": 42}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` That will be too much ! We 'll revolt ! ''", "context_len": 12}}}, "accurate": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 51, "context": "Children can not too soon begin to know the wild flowers , and here they are told in a charming way where and when to look for each of fifty widely distributed common flowering plants ; also how they get their names , and how to know them from the remarkably accurate drawings of Mr", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "A series of stories brimming with hardy adventure , vivid and accurate in detail , and with a good foundation of probability", "context_len": 22}}}, "cowed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then , having thoroughly cowed the malcontents by taking more pains in the use of the stick than in any business for the last twelve months ( as he afterwards expressed it ) , he sent for the guard and clapped three of the ringleaders in the stocks", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "The shouts swelled into a deep roar of execration , as the sledge on which the prisoner lay bound neared the place of execution , and Richard Harrison , struggling to keep his place as close to the victim as he might , thought with grim bitterness of the day when this same mob , silent and cowed , had seen General Harrison ride back from the scaffold at Whitehall", "context_len": 70}}}, "annals": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 43, "context": "Concerning the gentes ( al\u00e4\u00edkita ) of the Creek people , it is important to notice that in their towns each group of houses contained people of one gens only , [ 94 ] and these gentes are often mentioned in their local annals ; and that the gens of each individual was determined by that of his mother", "context_len": 59}}}, "forfeited": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "When the latter , in obedience to the order , came in from the garden , the officer said in Italian , It having come to the ears of the sultan my master that the merchant Ben Ibyn has ventured , contrary to the law , to purchase a Christian slave brought secretly into the town , he has declared the slave to be forfeited and I am commanded to take him at once to the slaves ' quarter", "context_len": 79}}}, "shrunk": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Not one of them but would have freely risked his life in the service of the Order , but there were few who would not have shrunk from the idea of living as a slave among the slaves , sharing their tasks , and subject to the orders of men of inferior rank and often brutal manners", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "Ben could not doubt the honest candor of his companion , but he shrunk from unbosoming himself , dreading to narrate the unpleasant story of the events which had made both Bern Hayden and his father his uncompromising enemies and had forced him to flee like a criminal from his native village in order to escape being sent to the State Reformatory", "context_len": 62}}}, "flambeaux": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "So they fared on with him in great state , with four of the Viziers for his sword-bearers , whilst all the troops and people of the city , both townsfolk and strangers , walked in procession before him , carrying flambeaux and drums and flutes and instruments of mirth and music , till they brought him to his palace , when he alighted and entering , sat down , as did also the Viziers and Amirs who were in his company , whilst the mamelukes brought sherbets and sweetmeats [ 493 ] and gave all who were with him in the procession to drink , albeit they were a multitude of folk whose number might not be told", "context_len": 119}}}, "robed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "A loud shout rose from the assembled knights , for upon the return of the party who had been away , the rest of those at the auberge had hastily robed themselves and descended to the hall to gather the news", "context_len": 41}}}, "bailiffs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "If we stop now we will have the whole house of cards about our ears ; the tradespeople will sue for their money , the bailiffs will be in and will take possession of the furniture , even the very bed your mother sleeps on will be taken from under her", "context_len": 51}}}, "blushes": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 82, "context": "At this the little limb was suddenly drawn up , that the tell-tale garter might be hid from view ; and then , able to stand it no longer , Albert looked up entreatingly to the children above him , and blushingly explained , Dorothy made it for me , just for a bit of fun , you know ; and then sure to a certainty that he never , never would hear the end of that blue garter , buried his blushes in Timothy 's long silky coat , and rued the hour when Dorothy had so merrily abetted his desire for this particular bit of fun", "context_len": 108}}}, "congratulating": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 42, "context": "Gervaise went up to the palace , and while waiting in the great hall until summoned before the council he was warmly accosted by several knights , -- some of whom were quite strangers to him , -- who all joined in congratulating him on the immense service he had done to the Order", "context_len": 54}}}, "exceptional": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 28, "context": "At the council this morning it was urged that some signal mark of honour should be granted ; but I interposed , saying that you had already received exceptional promotion , and that it would not be for your good , or that of the Order , for so young a knight to be raised to an official position of a character usually held by seniors , and that I was perfectly sure you would prefer remaining in command of your galley to any promotion whatever that would retain you on the Island", "context_len": 93}}}, "manor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Lights appeared in two or three places , and soon it became evident that the ruthless foe had gained their object , as the barns and stables lit up in all directions , and the manor house was surrounded by the double conflagration , so that every object was as distinctly visible as in open daylight", "context_len": 56}}}, "revenue": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` With terrific exertion , '' he writes , `` in two or three years ' time I may with God 's administration make a good province -- with a good army and a fair revenue and peace , and an increased trade , -- also have suppressed slave raids", "context_len": 50}}}, "completion": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 53, "context": "When the Sultan examined it and saw that it was unfinished , he said , `` Woe is me for thee , O casement , that thou art not perfect ! '' Then , turning to the Vizier , he said to him , `` Knowest thou the reason of the lack of completion of this casement and its lattices ? '' `` O [ 512 ] my lord , '' answered the Vizier , `` methinketh it is because Thy Grace hastened upon Alaeddin with the wedding and he had no time to complete it", "context_len": 96}}}, "emulation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "And I foresee that the spirit of emulation , and the desire to show that , though still but professed knights , they are capable of performing as valiant deeds as their elders , will make the galley under your command one of the most successful in the Order", "context_len": 49}}}, "reside": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 59, "context": "On the day after Paleologus , who was himself a renegade Greek , had established his camp , he sent in a herald to summon the city to surrender , at the same time making lavish promises that the lives and property of the native population should be respected , and that they should be allowed to continue to reside there , to enjoy the full exercise of their religion and of all other rights they possessed", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "All that is left of them -- about fifty-five Indians , of a parentage strongly mixed with white blood , reside at Charenton , St", "context_len": 25}}}, "humiliated": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "One might call the Granth the book of yearning , and I feel humiliated that I , with Gospel light , should in spiritual contemplation and longing for closest communion with the Deity come so far behind these poor Sikhs", "context_len": 40}}}, "bestowal": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` But , '' he said in conclusion , `` in comparing the compositions written at the commencement of the term of trial and those last submitted to Miss Ashton , I had , from my own unbiassed judgment , and before I had learned the choice of your teacher , decided that the one best entitled to the prize and the bestowal of this art education is Miss Bessie Bradford", "context_len": 71}}}, "emulate": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "We shall not this voyage have the encouragement and confidence inspired by the presence of those who have long and valiantly borne the standard of the Order ; but , on the other hand , we have to show that we are worthy of the confidence reposed in us , and that the young knights of the Order can be trusted to emulate the deeds of those who have rendered the name of the Hospitallers a terror to the infidel", "context_len": 80}}}, "raises": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 65, "context": "It is not only when we try to amuse them that the children love us best ( who has not sometimes seen the look of almost contempt in the eyes of a little one for the good-natured elder who plays the fool for his benefit ? ) , and often the companion most cared for and sought after by a child is he who unconsciously raises the standard of the eager , growing soul", "context_len": 74}}}, "aspirants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "As Ralph had declared would be the case , the young knights in the other auberges were as anxious as those of England to be enrolled among the crew of the new galley , and the bailiffs had some trouble in choosing among the aspirants", "context_len": 45}}}, "comply": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 55, "context": "Moffat enough to let her understand that he had unwittingly seen something he was not intended to see , and she , knowing enough of boys in general and of that senior class in particular , to be sure that Charlie would not go scot free , if the truth were known , hastened to comply with his request", "context_len": 59}}}, "proficiency": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "Very few were selected outside the rank of professed knights , and as great pains were taken to comply with the grand master 's wishes that only young knights of good conduct and disposition , and distinguished by their proficiency in warlike exercises , should be chosen , the crew was in every way a picked one", "context_len": 57}}}, "inflicted": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 124, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}}}, "unpunished": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Even the warders and guards were allowed to go unpunished , although their offence was a much more serious one", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Do you think that a slave is to mutiny on board my ship , and escape unpunished ?", "context_len": 18}}}, "avowed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Seeing the hopelessness of denial , he had boldly avowed his share in the matter , and had acknowledged that he was acting as agent for the sultan , and had been supplied with ample funds before leaving Constantinople", "context_len": 39}}}, "suborning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "He declared that he was absolutely unable to give any names whatever of those concerned in the plot , save those of the two overseers , as these had undertaken the work of suborning the warders and guards , though he admitted that he had on several occasions spoken to slaves as the gangs were on their way back to the prison , and had told them to be prepared to take part in a plan that was on foot for their rescue from slavery", "context_len": 85}}}, "publicity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "The torture had not been , as was then the usual custom , applied to extort information ; partly because his story was probable , still more because the grand master and council did not wish that more publicity should be given to the affair , and were glad that it should be allowed to drop without any further trial of the delinquents", "context_len": 63}}}, "delinquents": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "The torture had not been , as was then the usual custom , applied to extort information ; partly because his story was probable , still more because the grand master and council did not wish that more publicity should be given to the affair , and were glad that it should be allowed to drop without any further trial of the delinquents", "context_len": 63}}}, "liberation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "In the city generally it was only known that a plot had been discovered for the liberation and escape of some of the slaves ; and , outside the members of the Order , none were aware of its extent and dangerous character", "context_len": 43}}}, "sieges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "Ever since the adventure with the pirates , the knight had exhibited an almost fatherly interest in him ; had encouraged him in his studies , ridden with him on such occasions as he had permitted himself a short holiday , and had , whenever they were together , related to him stories of war , sieges , battles , and escapes , from which he thought the young knight might gain lessons for his future guidance", "context_len": 77}}}, "pilgrims": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "We were founded , you know , simply as a body of monks , bound to devote ourselves solely to the care of the sick , and to give hospitality to pilgrims in Palestine", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "Dear little pilgrims ! there were jewels in their way such as they did not dream of ; but it was only earnest seeking such as theirs which could find them ; for they lay hidden beneath many a thorn and bramble and unsightly weed ; and they were to be found only by the help of this very jewel of prayer which shone so brightly that its light guided the little feet to the dark places where the hidden gems lay", "context_len": 82}}}, "renounce": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "Now this was monkish work , and men who devoted themselves solely to such a life of charity as that in our Hospital at Jerusalem , might well renounce all human pleasures ; but when the great change was made by Master Raymond du Puy , and from a nursing body we became a brotherhood in arms , it seems to me that the vows of celibacy were no longer needful or desirable", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 90, "context": "`` When [ 592 ] she had made an end of her story , Alaeddin said to her , `` Tell me , what does this accursed one purpose with thee ; what saith he to thee and of what doth he bespeak thee and what is his will of thee ? '' `` Every day , '' answered the princess , `` he cometh to me once and no more and seeketh to draw me to his love , willing me take him in thy stead and forget and renounce thee ; nay , he told me that my father the Sultan had cut off thy head", "context_len": 108}}}, "du": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 43, "context": "Now this was monkish work , and men who devoted themselves solely to such a life of charity as that in our Hospital at Jerusalem , might well renounce all human pleasures ; but when the great change was made by Master Raymond du Puy , and from a nursing body we became a brotherhood in arms , it seems to me that the vows of celibacy were no longer needful or desirable", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 81, "context": "of the Cabinet des Fees ( printed in 1793 , though antedated 1788 and 1789 ) do not form the first edition of Chavis and Cazotte 's so-called Sequel , which was in 1793 added , by way of supplement , to the Cabinet des Fees , having been first published in 1788 ( two years after the completion-in thirty-seven volumes-of that great storehouse of supernatural fiction ) under the title of `` Les Veillees Persanes '' or `` Les Veillees du Sultan Schahriar avec la Sultane Scheherazade , histoires incroyables , amusantes et morales , traduites par M", "context_len": 99}}}, "brotherhood": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 54, "context": "Now this was monkish work , and men who devoted themselves solely to such a life of charity as that in our Hospital at Jerusalem , might well renounce all human pleasures ; but when the great change was made by Master Raymond du Puy , and from a nursing body we became a brotherhood in arms , it seems to me that the vows of celibacy were no longer needful or desirable", "context_len": 73}}}, "crusaders": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 90, "context": "The sole vow necessary , so far as I can see , would be that knights should remain unmarried and disposable at all times for service until ten years after making their profession , and that afterwards they should ever be ready to obey the summons to arms , on occasions when the safety of Rhodes , or the invasion of any Christian country by the Moslems , rendered their services needful , when they would come out just as the knights of Richard the Lion Heart went out as crusaders", "context_len": 91}}}, "methinks": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 46, "context": "I should like nothing better than a stay there myself , but there is much to do to repair the damages caused by the siege , and to place the city in a state of defence should the Turks again lay siege to it ; and methinks Mahomet will not sit down quietly under the heavy reverse his troops have met with", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "But I make no doubt that the sight of these your loyal neighbours whom you have called in , and the very blessings of the poor folk in the street , are somewhat of a balm to his heart , also I can not deny that those gentlemen '' -- looking over at the officers -- '' have used us very civilly during the day 's ride ; methinks his Majesty finds himself more at ease with them than with those crop-eared parliament men and their preachers", "context_len": 87}}}, "garrison": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 84, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "It was a complete change when , with his head full of Utopian dreams , `` more of an antique Roman '' than an Englishman , Dick was sent off to serve under General Monck in the army that was to administer as well as to garrison Scotland", "context_len": 48}}}, "discountenancing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Among others , the presence of so many ladies of rank would have the excellent effect of discountenancing and repressing extravagances and dissolute habits , which are but too common , and are a shame to the Order", "context_len": 38}}}, "extravagances": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Among others , the presence of so many ladies of rank would have the excellent effect of discountenancing and repressing extravagances and dissolute habits , which are but too common , and are a shame to the Order", "context_len": 38}}}, "dissolute": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Among others , the presence of so many ladies of rank would have the excellent effect of discountenancing and repressing extravagances and dissolute habits , which are but too common , and are a shame to the Order", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Already , in defiance of their oaths , too many knights lead idle and dissolute lives", "context_len": 16}}}, "scandals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Knights possessing commanderies throughout Europe would be no worse stewards for being married men , and scandals , such as contributed largely to the downfall of the Templars , would be avoided", "context_len": 32}}}, "disposable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The sole vow necessary , so far as I can see , would be that knights should remain unmarried and disposable at all times for service until ten years after making their profession , and that afterwards they should ever be ready to obey the summons to arms , on occasions when the safety of Rhodes , or the invasion of any Christian country by the Moslems , rendered their services needful , when they would come out just as the knights of Richard the Lion Heart went out as crusaders", "context_len": 91}}}, "causes": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "As a fighting body we should be in no way inferior to what we are now , and we should be more liked and more respected throughout Europe , for naturally the sight of so many men leading a luxurious life in commanderies causes a feeling against them", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 12, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "dispensation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` It 's hard for most to follow God in such a dispensation as this , and yet my Lord and Master is as sweet and glorious to me now as He was in the time of my greatest prosperity ; '' and then , embracing his friends in farewell , he committed his spirit into the hands of God and was , the bystanders declared , `` not so much thrown off the ladder by the executioner , but went readily off himself", "context_len": 84}}}, "dispensations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Of course , dispensations are not common ; but doubtless any knight when he had served his term of active service could , especially if his request were backed by the grand master , obtain from the Pope a dispensation of his vows", "context_len": 43}}}, "rewarding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "If he had a commandery it would make a vacancy , and give the grand prior , or the grand master , or the council , in whosoever 's gift it might be , an opportunity of rewarding services or of gratifying some powerful family", "context_len": 45}}}, "gratifying": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 41, "context": "If he had a commandery it would make a vacancy , and give the grand prior , or the grand master , or the council , in whosoever 's gift it might be , an opportunity of rewarding services or of gratifying some powerful family", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The incident was much more gratifying to me than all the honour paid me by the Republic , and during the long months of my captivity it has recurred to me so frequently that I have in vain endeavoured to chase it from my thoughts , as sinful thus to allow myself constantly to think of any woman", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The end of the stern combat with the waters was particularly gratifying , as several men had previously lost their lives in crossing the same river", "context_len": 26}}}, "domains": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "Taking my life in my hands , I penetrated their fortalice , and administered the last sacrament to her ; but they threatened my life for entering their domains , and , perhaps , had I been but a simple priest , and not also , small boast as it is , the son of a powerful English thane , whom they feared to offend , I had died in doing my duty", "context_len": 73}}}, "willingness": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 34, "context": "The watch was duly set ; campfires were lighted , and joints of meat suspended over them ; barrels of wine and mead were broached , for all the country around contributed with loving willingness to the support of its defenders ; and when hunger was appeased the patriotic song arose from the various fires , and stirring legends of the glorious days of old , when Danes and Norsemen fled before the English arms , nerved the courage of the men for the morrow 's stern conflict", "context_len": 88}}}, "shamelessly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "All this is a scandal , and it were better by far to do away with all oaths , save that of obedience and willingness to war with the infidel , than to make vows that all men know are constantly and shamelessly broken", "context_len": 44}}}, "kith": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "But see what greater pleasure it would give to my life were you my son , for whom I could lay by such funds as I could well spare , instead of spending all my appointments on myself , and having neither kith nor kin to give a sigh of regret when the news comes that I have fallen in some engagement with the infidels", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 68, "context": "Farwell was no doubt a most desirable addition , and all were delighted that he could come ; but the place belonged by rights to Ted -- wilful , wandering , selfish Ted , who might have added so much to their pleasure if he had not chosen to turn his back upon them all and prefer any company in the world , apparently , to that of kith and kin and old friends at Windsor", "context_len": 76}}}, "courts": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 51, "context": "I often think of all these things , and sometimes talk them over with comrades , and there are few who do not hold , with me , that it would be far better that we should become a purely military Order , like some of the military Orders in the courts of the European sovereigns , than remain as we are , half monk , half soldier -- a mixture that , so far as I can see , accords but badly with either morality or public repute", "context_len": 89}}}, "monk": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "I often think of all these things , and sometimes talk them over with comrades , and there are few who do not hold , with me , that it would be far better that we should become a purely military Order , like some of the military Orders in the courts of the European sovereigns , than remain as we are , half monk , half soldier -- a mixture that , so far as I can see , accords but badly with either morality or public repute", "context_len": 89}}}, "accords": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 80, "context": "I often think of all these things , and sometimes talk them over with comrades , and there are few who do not hold , with me , that it would be far better that we should become a purely military Order , like some of the military Orders in the courts of the European sovereigns , than remain as we are , half monk , half soldier -- a mixture that , so far as I can see , accords but badly with either morality or public repute", "context_len": 89}}}, "aims": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 46, "context": "Indeed , both she and Miss Fanny gave one the impression of being too incessantly though quietly busy about everything that promoted the happiness of other people , to ever become stout , or to cultivate dress and appearances , beyond what was consistent with the aims and duties and requirements of a fully occupied home-life", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Do n't you know that `` he who aims for perfectness in a trifle , is trying to do that trifle holily ? '' '", "context_len": 25}}}, "shipwrights": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The shipwrights will have finished tomorrow , and the next day we shall take possession of her , and begin to practise , so that each man shall know his duties , and the galley slaves learn to row well , before we have orders to sail", "context_len": 47}}}, "marshal": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "When the ceremony was over , Gervaise was solemnly invested with the command of the galley by the grand marshal of the navy ; then the shores were struck away , and the galley glided into the water , amid the firing of guns , the blowing of trumpets , and the cheers of the spectators who had gathered at the port to witness the ceremony", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "A mighty noise above stairs of moving furniture and the patter of light footsteps came to Harrison as he basked by the great fire ; and it was not till the evening was growing late that Audrey reappeared , and , dropping a curtsey with a charming air of demureness , prayed leave to marshal his worship to his bedchamber", "context_len": 60}}}, "navy": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 22, "context": "When the ceremony was over , Gervaise was solemnly invested with the command of the galley by the grand marshal of the navy ; then the shores were struck away , and the galley glided into the water , amid the firing of guns , the blowing of trumpets , and the cheers of the spectators who had gathered at the port to witness the ceremony", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "in me , Lawyer Whitehead , Howard Hobbs and Jack Alexander of Iping , both of whom had seen service under Prince Rupert ; Arthur Conolly , an Irish veteran who had served in the Low Countries , and who had come over from Chichester for the occasion ; Arthur Lewis , a gentleman of Bramshott ; Percy Young , an officer of the navy , who in his earlier days had lost a leg in the action of La Rochelle ; Herbert Collings , a master mariner of Gosport , who used to be a frequent visitor at our house , and who greatly interested me with the account of his adventures off the coast of Barbary ; and Giles Perrin , the landlord of the ``", "context_len": 128}}}, "trumpets": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "Windsor surrendered to a blast of trumpets , and a few days later the little Queen was yielded up a captive into Henry 's hands , and was carried with faithful Simonette , her Saracen maid , to the Castle of Ledes ; but Ledes , fortunately , proved to be a beautiful castle , with a large garden , and she was not treated harshly or unkindly", "context_len": 68}}}, "ebon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "The dye by this time had entirely worn off his face , and although his hair was still several shades darker than of old , it differed even more widely from the ebon hue that it had been when he was in prison", "context_len": 43}}}, "detecting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Thus , although he recognised three or four men upon the benches who had been fellow occupants of his cell , he had no fear whatever of their detecting in the commander of the galley their late companion in misfortune", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Donald and Harold were on the eve of laughing outright , but Marie-Celeste , detecting a suspicious blinking in the long curling lashes of the eyelids , kept them still by an imperative gesture", "context_len": 34}}}, "defending": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 59, "context": "Helen dashed off to do as she was told , but as she was flying across the clearing she was suddenly brought up by a perfect hailstorm of bullets , which played round her in all directions , and caused her to fly back to the camp with the astounding information that it was not Hal who had been defending the fort , but somebody else", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The different parties at the table took up the subject , and began to dispute , the old men taking the part of Philip and former days , and the younger defending Alexander", "context_len": 33}}}, "admiral": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 16, "context": "They had been condemned to die ; but , owing to a slight difference between the admiral and the governor of Jamaica , their fate was yet undecided , the former wishing to send them to England to grace Execution Dock , the latter desiring to gratify the inhabitants of Port Royal by stringing up the prisoners in front of the castle", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 42, "context": "But I hope that if , on our return , we bring back all our slaves in good condition and health , the contrast between their appearance and that of the slaves in most other galleys will be so marked that the admiral may consider it would be well to order awnings to be fixed to all the vessels of the Order , and even to grant to all slaves , when away on voyages , the little indulgences I have given them here", "context_len": 84}}}, "nomination": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Hitherto you have given me , from the time you reached the Island , naught but reason for satisfaction at my nomination of you as page , and I have no fear that you will fail this time", "context_len": 38}}}, "suspend": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 39, "context": "This story does not mention any crossing by the Cha'hta of the turbid waters of the mighty Mississippi , but accounts quite satisfactorily for the mysterious inclination of the pole , for the prophet must have been careful to suspend the satchel with the war-physic always on the eastern side , so as to have the pole brought down in that direction by the weight of the pouch", "context_len": 68}}}, "corsairs": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 68, "word_index": 121, "context": "They said that if caught they would be either killed or made slaves of , and I could not contradict them , but said that , in the first place , as I was ready to run the risk , there was no reason why they should n't do so , and in the second , there was no chance whatever of their being taken , as , if discovered , we should get so long a start that we could either escape them altogether or run the boat ashore at some point where the trees came down to the water 's edge , carry the boat up and hide it , and then move up into the hills until the corsairs had gone", "context_len": 124}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "A similar report was given at Lunasei ; there were certainly no corsairs lying behind Cape Bellavista , or news would assuredly have reached the village", "context_len": 26}}}, "depredations": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 83, "context": "My brother has already spoken of you to me , Sir Gervaise , the viscount said , when the young knight was presented to him ; although indeed there was no occasion for him to do so , since the name of the knight who two years ago saved the commerce of Italy from ruin , and with a single galley destroyed or captured a great fleet of over twenty Barbary pirates , and thus for a time put a stop to the depredations of the infidels , is known throughout Europe", "context_len": 92}}}, "impetuosity": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "Her very warm-heartedness and impetuosity were now and then somewhat of a hindrance , -- as when , on her first arrival , going into a Zenana , she pressed forward and eagerly shook hands with a bibi , -- an Indian lady , -- forgetting the difference of Indian customs and English ones", "context_len": 54}}}, "undue": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 50, "context": "That was not the only reason ; the other was , that already a great deal too much has been said about an affair in which , as I have told you , I owed everything to good fortune , and am heartily sick of receiving what I consider altogether undue praise", "context_len": 52}}}, "harshness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 58, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}}}, "firmness": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 39, "context": "During the time of waiting she had a certain influence over Irene ; not , perhaps , so much as on the first day , when that young lady , charmed , bewildered , and amazed by Rosamund 's firmness , had followed her lead unflinchingly", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was with some difficulty that Rosamund persuaded Irene to obey her orders ; but firmness won the day", "context_len": 19}}}, "unmerited": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "I feel that it is a heavy responsibility and will spare no pains to justify the unmerited honour that has been bestowed upon me", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Morris was welcome to stay , he felt he ought to be able to bear with the rest , no matter how humiliating and , in a measure , unmerited", "context_len": 30}}}, "terminated": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was a fine summer 's evening towards the end of the month of July , and the sun was slowly setting behind the wood-crowned range of hills in the west , where the forest terminated the pastures of Aescendune ; the cattle were returning to their stalls ; the last load of hay was being transferred from the wain to the rick , and all things spoke of the calm and rest of a sweet night , fragrant with the breath of honeysuckle and wild brier , when nature herself seems to court luxurious repose", "context_len": 96}}}, "blunder": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Ah ! he broke off , seeing a slight smile on the young knight 's face , you think my orders contradictory ? It may be so ; but you know what I mean , and I fear not that you will blunder in carrying them out", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "The good lady was no less troubled to find what a blunder she had made , and set off for Captain Rhines \u2019 s , to thank John in person , and ask him to apologize for her to the others", "context_len": 41}}}, "benefits": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "When the Marid heard Alaeddin 's words , his face frowned and he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "unquestioning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "I am sure , quite sure , that it has been your dear little Bessie 's unquestioning faith , her love and trust in the power and goodness of the Almighty and , more than all , her firm belief that one for whom he had done so much , and preserved through so many dangers , must of necessity have a double share of faith and love , which has touched his heart", "context_len": 74}}}, "uphold": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "You have been selected from among many other candidates , and you have not only to justify the choice , but to uphold the reputation and honour of the young knights of your Order , by all of whom your doings will be regarded with special interest , as reflecting credit not only upon yourselves individually , but as representatives and champions of them all", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Friends ! out upon them ! They mock the name ; it were not thus , if thou Hadst drawn thy gallant sword with those who wear No chains but those of Virtue , those who own No earthly Monarch , and uphold no power But that of Liberty ; whose friendship lasts Not only when the red wine sparkles high , And revelry and song profane the night ; If such had been thy comrades and thy friends , Thou hadst not been forsaken thus", "context_len": 86}}}, "reflecting": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 62, "context": "Seizing the first opportunity of slipping away from his schoolmates , he rushed upstairs to the dormitory with the design of throwing the note under Seabrooke 's bed or bureau , where it might be supposed to have fallen ; but seeing the trunk standing there ready packed , the impulse had taken him to put it in that , and without reflecting -- perhaps hardly caring -- that this would place Seabrooke in a still more embarrassing position , he thrust the note within , as Charlie Henderson saw , and fled from the room", "context_len": 96}}}, "convent": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "He is without a rival among the younger knights of the convent in his skill in arms , and for strength and activity in all exercises ; he seems to care nothing for the ordinary amusements in which they join at Rhodes , and for nine months was scarcely ever seen by those in the auberge , save when they gathered for meals , so continuously did he work to acquire a perfect command of Turkish", "context_len": 76}}}, "attendants": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 14, "context": "Here , after the king had availed himself of the bath , and his attendants had divested themselves of their travel-stained attire , the throne of the king was placed at the head of the board , and a seat for the bishop on his right hand , and for Edric on his left", "context_len": 54}}}, "wines": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "The grand master had advanced Gervaise a sum equal to half a year 's income of his commandery , and with this he had purchased a stock of the best wines , and various other luxuries , to supplement the rations supplied from the funds of the Order to knights when at sea", "context_len": 53}}}, "supplement": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "The grand master had advanced Gervaise a sum equal to half a year 's income of his commandery , and with this he had purchased a stock of the best wines , and various other luxuries , to supplement the rations supplied from the funds of the Order to knights when at sea", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "of the Cabinet des Fees ( printed in 1793 , though antedated 1788 and 1789 ) do not form the first edition of Chavis and Cazotte 's so-called Sequel , which was in 1793 added , by way of supplement , to the Cabinet des Fees , having been first published in 1788 ( two years after the completion-in thirty-seven volumes-of that great storehouse of supernatural fiction ) under the title of `` Les Veillees Persanes '' or `` Les Veillees du Sultan Schahriar avec la Sultane Scheherazade , histoires incroyables , amusantes et morales , traduites par M", "context_len": 99}}}, "rations": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 7, "context": "Carefully setting apart a portion of our rations , we soon secured enough food to last us for a couple of days , and one evening , directly the guards had visited us for the last time that night , Greville climbed on Felgate \u2019 s shoulders and attacked the crumbling mortar that kept the bars in position", "context_len": 58}}}, "overwhelming": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "In the early dawn of that day the Mahdi assaulted the town in overwhelming force -- whether helped by treachery is not exactly known ; and before his well-fed , well-trained hosts , the feeble worn-out garrison gave way , the walls were scaled , the city taken , and the hero who had won the affection of many nations fell amidst the people he had come to save", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "Yet the grief and horror was still only in the background of his mind , his brain felt numbed , though an instinctive dread warned him that they lurked there ready at the first opportunity to spring out on him with overwhelming force", "context_len": 43}}}, "compensated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "But there was no mistaking the ardour and enthusiasm in their faces , and the lack of breadth and weight , that years alone would give to them , was compensated by skill in their weapons , acquired by long and severe training , and by the activity and tireless energy of youth", "context_len": 53}}}, "cooperation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The success of our expedition depends upon you rather than upon me , and as I feel assured of your warm cooperation I have no fear as to what the result will be , if Dame Fortune will but favour us by throwing in our way some of those scourges of the sea in search of whom we are about to set out", "context_len": 63}}}, "scourges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "The success of our expedition depends upon you rather than upon me , and as I feel assured of your warm cooperation I have no fear as to what the result will be , if Dame Fortune will but favour us by throwing in our way some of those scourges of the sea in search of whom we are about to set out", "context_len": 63}}}, "taxed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "The pair had never had any secrets before , so Peggy waited at first for Bobby to tell her , but as the confidence did not seem to be forthcoming , she one day boldly taxed him with it", "context_len": 39}}}, "quota": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 48, "context": "A minute later he was running down the hill to the town , accompanied by three or four other knights ; they made direct for the outer port , where two galleys were lying in readiness , leapt on board one of them , which already contained its quota of knights , and at once rowed out of the port", "context_len": 60}}}, "tenets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Meat will be served out to you daily , when it can be obtained , and for those of you who hold that the strict tenets of your religion may be relaxed while engaged in such severe labour , a ration of wine will also be served out ; and such other indulgences as are compatible with the discipline and safety of the ship , will also be granted to you", "context_len": 71}}}, "ration": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "Meat will be served out to you daily , when it can be obtained , and for those of you who hold that the strict tenets of your religion may be relaxed while engaged in such severe labour , a ration of wine will also be served out ; and such other indulgences as are compatible with the discipline and safety of the ship , will also be granted to you", "context_len": 71}}}, "indulgences": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 78, "context": "But I hope that if , on our return , we bring back all our slaves in good condition and health , the contrast between their appearance and that of the slaves in most other galleys will be so marked that the admiral may consider it would be well to order awnings to be fixed to all the vessels of the Order , and even to grant to all slaves , when away on voyages , the little indulgences I have given them here", "context_len": 84}}}, "compatible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "Meat will be served out to you daily , when it can be obtained , and for those of you who hold that the strict tenets of your religion may be relaxed while engaged in such severe labour , a ration of wine will also be served out ; and such other indulgences as are compatible with the discipline and safety of the ship , will also be granted to you", "context_len": 71}}}, "knightly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "They had bathed , and combed their matted hair , which was alone sufficient to transform them , but the feeling that they were once more free men , and knights of an honoured Order , had done even more to effect the change ; and although they looked thin and worn , the martial bearing had come back naturally as they donned their knightly robes and buckled on swords", "context_len": 70}}}, "fellowship": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "But I must also bear testimony , not only to the bravery displayed by Sir Gervaise , Sir Ralph Harcourt , his lieutenant , and every one of the knights his crew , but to the admirable discipline , order , and good fellowship on board the galley , which would have done credit to the most experienced commander and to the most veteran knights of the Order", "context_len": 68}}}, "french": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "French of Agra , who utterly refused to allow the Christian Natives to be banished from the town , as was proposed by some faint-hearted people there", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 0, "context": "French maps place it on the same river , where `` Chicachae '' fort stood above , and call it : `` Yauana , dernier village des Choctaws", "context_len": 28}}}, "dignitaries": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "French was the common language , for as the Order was of French foundation , and three of the seven langues belonged to that country , most of the high dignitaries being chosen from their ranks , it was natural that the French language should be the general medium of communication between them", "context_len": 53}}}, "erected": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 45, "context": "in the face of the carelessness with which the text treats kushk as synonymous now with keszr or teyyareh and now with liwan or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this", "context_len": 128}}}, "divested": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 0, "context": "Divested of his disguise , no longer a fear-filled roustabout fugitive , Bart 's strange friend had found a steady , lucrative position at the hotel , and Bart felt that he had certainly been the means of doing some real good in the world every time he looked at the happy , contented face of his prot\u00e9g\u00e9", "context_len": 58}}}, "siesta": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Most of the people will be taking their siesta at present , and we shall get through the streets without being mobbed ; for I can assure you that the mantle of the Order is just at present in such high favour that I had a hard task to wend my way through the streets to my cousin 's house", "context_len": 60}}}, "prejudicial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "In port the slaves always sleep in the prison , but at sea they must rest on their benches ; and to do so with clothes soaked with the heavy dew must be a severe trial , and most prejudicial to the health", "context_len": 43}}}, "ducats": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Florence sent a casket containing a document bestowing upon him the freedom of the city , and an order upon the treasury for five thousand ducats that had been voted to him by the grand council of the Republic ; while Ferdinand , King of Naples , bestowed on him the grand cross of the Order of St", "context_len": 58}}}, "prevalent": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "This was a singularly fortunate and opportune discovery , for I had already observed that fever and ague were very prevalent among the inhabitants , and I hoped that if by means of a decoction of cinchona bark I could effect a cure , I might be able very materially to improve and strengthen my position in the town", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "And more than this , there is a rare kindliness of spirit and an open-handed hospitality prevalent among the people", "context_len": 20}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` I suppose they 'll charge a fortune for a supper , '' said Mosely , whose economical spirit was troubled by the exorbitant prices then prevalent in California , `` but we must have it at any cost", "context_len": 39}}}, "utilitarian": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "When , however , they had the question put to them , as Gervaise had done , they were ready to give a hearty agreement , although it was the utilitarian rather than the humanitarian side of the question that recommended it to them", "context_len": 44}}}, "humanitarian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "When , however , they had the question put to them , as Gervaise had done , they were ready to give a hearty agreement , although it was the utilitarian rather than the humanitarian side of the question that recommended it to them", "context_len": 44}}}, "islet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "When they reached it , the children paused to gaze down at the river , which there broadened out into a sort of lake , with a grassy islet in the centre", "context_len": 32}}}, "indulgence": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 6, "context": "The tale now presented to the indulgence of the public is the second of a series of tales , each complete in itself , which , as stated in the preface to the first of the series , have been told to the senior boys of a large school , in order to secure their interest in historical characters , and to illustrate great epochs in human affairs by the aid of fiction", "context_len": 73}}}, "conversations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "Fanning , I also will take the present opportunity of telling you that you and I have nothing in common , that I dislike your singling me out for special conversations of any kind , and that I hope in the future you will clearly understand that I do not wish you to do so", "context_len": 55}}}, "lawgivers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The Mohammedans held in reverence the lawgivers and prophets of the Old Testament , and even regarded Christ Himself as being a prophet", "context_len": 23}}}, "creed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 32, "context": "They had been grievously led away by Mahomet , whom Gervaise regarded as a false teacher ; but as he had seen innumerable instances of the fidelity of the Moslems to their creed , and the punctuality and devotion with which the slaves said their daily prayers , exposed though they were to the scorn and even the anger of their taskmasters , he had quite lost , during his nine months of constant association with Suleiman Ali , the bigoted hatred of Mohammedanism so universal at the time", "context_len": 89}}}, "bigoted": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 80, "context": "They had been grievously led away by Mahomet , whom Gervaise regarded as a false teacher ; but as he had seen innumerable instances of the fidelity of the Moslems to their creed , and the punctuality and devotion with which the slaves said their daily prayers , exposed though they were to the scorn and even the anger of their taskmasters , he had quite lost , during his nine months of constant association with Suleiman Ali , the bigoted hatred of Mohammedanism so universal at the time", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "For the rest , Batala was a stronghold of bigoted Muhammadanism", "context_len": 11}}}, "advisable": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 18, "context": "Gangs of men , as usual , worked in the breach ; but , as it was deemed advisable that there should be no outward show of activity that would lead the Turks to suspect that their design had been discovered , neither reinforcements of men nor munitions were sent along the mole ; everything being taken out by boats , which , rowing closely along under the wall , were hidden from the view of the Turks", "context_len": 78}}}, "concessions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The majority approved of the steps Gervaise had taken , but some asserted that these concessions would only lead them to look for more , and would create discontent among the crews of other galleys not so favoured", "context_len": 38}}}, "create": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 63, "context": "Meanwhile I had practically abandoned my attempts to effect my escape , for the time being at least ; for the conviction had at length been forced upon me that neither Banda nor his people would ever willingly let me go , and that , therefore , before engaging in any further attempts , I must contrive to disarm suspicion completely , and create the impression that I had at length resigned myself to live out my life in this remote African town , and with savages only for my companions", "context_len": 91}}}, "affirm": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "A most uninteresting city , this city of Brooklyn , some people affirm ; even those of us who love it best can not claim that it is great in anything but bigness but there are homes there we will match against homes the world over , not for show or for luxury , but for pure and transcendent comfort", "context_len": 60}}}, "brighten": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 94, "context": "O misery ! to wake in darkness , wake From vision of unutterable joy ; Instead of trumpet-sound and song of Heaven , To hear the dull clock measuring out time , When I had seemed to touch Eternity ! In the first pang of disappointed hope , I wept that I could wake from such a dream ; Until Faith gently whispered , Wherefore weep To lose the faint dim shadow of a joy Of which the substance shall one day be thine ? Live in the hope , -- that hope shall brighten life , And sanctify it to its highest end", "context_len": 104}}}, "monetary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Even viewed from a merely monetary point of view it must pay well to render him as content as possible with his lot", "context_len": 23}}}, "mortality": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "You know how great is the mortality among the slaves -- how they pine away and die from no material malady that can be detected , but simply from hopelessness and weariness of life , aided , undoubtedly , in the case of the galley slaves , by sleeping in the damp night air after an exposure all day to the full heat of the sun", "context_len": 66}}}, "innovations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "There is no reason why our galley should not be a model one , and though , like enough , the seniors will laugh at our making innovations , D'Aubusson is a reformer , and will certainly support anything that he sees to be beneficial , from whatever quarter it comes", "context_len": 51}}}, "reformer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "There is no reason why our galley should not be a model one , and though , like enough , the seniors will laugh at our making innovations , D'Aubusson is a reformer , and will certainly support anything that he sees to be beneficial , from whatever quarter it comes", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Once or twice the emperor was angered by Howard 's plainness of speech , but told the ambassador afterwards that he liked the prison reformer all the better for his honesty", "context_len": 31}}}, "beneficial": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "The admiral tells me that never before has a galley returned with the slaves in such good health and condition , and that unquestionably your plan of erecting an awning to shelter them from the midday heat and the night dews has had a most beneficial effect on their health ; he has recommended its general adoption", "context_len": 57}}}, "viols": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 71, "context": "The last spark had died out of the paper in the grate and left only black tinder ; the table was left bare , the golden plates and richly embroidered napkins , and the garlands were transformed again into old handkerchiefs , scraps of red and white paper , and discarded artificial flowers all scattered on the floor ; the minstrels in the minstrel gallery had stolen away , and the viols and bassoons were still", "context_len": 76}}}, "obtainable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` For , '' quoth Felgate to the servile landlord , `` we have a desire to know more of this old castle , and methinks that good fishing is obtainable in this stream", "context_len": 34}}}, "sighting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "A few hours , however , after sighting the coast of Africa , a dark object was seen ahead", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Two can play at that game ! muttered Alden , bringing the rifle of Dick Lightfoot to his shoulder and sighting at the miscreant", "context_len": 24}}}, "merchantman": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 66, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "sacked": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "His kingdom lost ; his family in captivity ; his beloved wife in the grave , where the sorrows and sufferings of separation from her husband had borne her ; his cities sacked ; his palaces and treasures plundered ; and now he himself , in the last hour of his extremity , abandoned and betrayed by all in whom he had placed his confidence and trust , his heart sunk within him in despair", "context_len": 75}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "He tried to call up his cooler judgment , but in vain ; pictures of sunny seas and waving palm groves , of gallant fights and sacked towns danced before him , and his broken slumbers only wove the fancies into dreams", "context_len": 42}}}, "quicken": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Nor did he observe three boys who were coming along the path from the academy at a run , having been led to quicken their steps by the cries of the girl and the barking of the dogs", "context_len": 38}}}, "suppositions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "As soon as the vessels were close enough for those on the poop of the galley to look down on to the deck of the other craft , it was seen that Ralph 's suppositions were correct", "context_len": 37}}}, "opponents": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 42, "context": "The question is , ought we to pursue them at once , or ought we to coast along until we find Visconti 's galley ? Three of these Tripoli pirates , crowded as they always are with men , would prove serious opponents , yet we might engage them with a fair hope of victory", "context_len": 55}}}, "committing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 55, "context": "One morning in June I was called into our living-room , and found myself confronted by a gold-laced individual , who , drawing a paper from his pocket , read in a sonorous voice a summons for me to attend at the courthouse as a witness against Dick Swyre and Caleb Keeping , presented for committing a murderous attack upon divers of the king \u2019 s subjects on the highway", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "This mere committing of the subject to the attention of the soothsayers had a great effect among all the soldiers of the army", "context_len": 23}}}, "notorious": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 41, "context": "Report says that when he came to Rake to settle down , some twelve or thirteen years back -- I being but an infant in arms , -- he did gain a lasting reputation by outmatching one Caleb James , a notorious bully , at his own game , breaking his pate with his own staff on the roadside hard by Milland Church", "context_len": 63}}}, "loath": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "For Aunt Lou an up-stairs sitting-room was converted into a little study ; for although Aunt Lou herself was rather loath to confess it , it was nevertheless somewhat generally known that she was very fond of writing stories for children", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "Will you tell it to us , Marie-Celeste ? And Marie-Celeste , nothing loath , and willing at last to substantiate her claims in the ears of doubting Albert , rested a hand comfortably on either arm of her chair , and commenced , preceding her narration with the request , You will correct me , wo n't you , if you find I do not tell it right ? to which Her Majesty smilingly acceded , first asking Miss Belmore to hand her a little jewelled miniature case from among the other treasures on the table", "context_len": 97}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "They had gone on for perhaps a mile , seeing no sign of their quarry , but loath to give up , when there was a sudden darkening of the atmosphere , the wind increased in violence , and then the air was again filled with flying flakes , so thick that the lads could not see ten feet ahead", "context_len": 60}}}, "concurrence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Sir Ralph Harcourt and I are of one mind in the matter , but as the decision is a grave one we should be loath to act upon it without your concurrence", "context_len": 32}}}, "alternatives": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Before him the alternatives were sharply defined : Apostasy , and his ancestral honours , with all that the sword of the conqueror could give ; and on the other hand , the martyr 's lingering agony , but the hope of everlasting life after death", "context_len": 46}}}, "larboard": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "Floating some five feet above her usual loadline , the ship was towed into the mouth of a muddy creek , and there careened till the whole of the bottom on the larboard side , with the exception of the keel and a few of the lower planks , was clear of the water , disclosing a sloping wall of barnacle-covered timbers", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "From her build and rig my craft would lay closer to the wind than the Gannet , so I ordered her to be kept on the starboard tack for two hours , then on the larboard tack for another two hours , and so on , hoping by these means to keep within sight of our escort when the gale moderated", "context_len": 61}}}, "dismantled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "The slaves had not , from their benches , been able to see what had passed on board the dismantled vessel , but from the order and the change of course , they had no doubt that the knights had obtained some clue to the direction taken by the corsairs who had captured and sacked the ship", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "There have been civil dissensions , and fighting between rival factions , and in consequence her ships are all dismantled and laid up", "context_len": 23}}}, "hangs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Uncle Isaac , said he , mother hasn \u2019 t got any crane ; all the way she hangs her pot over the fire is by a birch withe , with a chain at the end ; and sometimes it burns off above the chain : the other day it broke , and liked to have scalded the baby to death", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Not that any very remarkable things were said , but one can laugh very easily , you know , and at almost nothing , when one 's heart is light as a feather and the goose hangs high , as the queer old saying has it", "context_len": 46}}}, "broadening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The lantern was still burning , and , in a quarter of an hour , the knights on the poop were able , in the broadening light , to make out three shadowy forms some two miles ahead of them", "context_len": 40}}}, "decreased": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "They had a different feast on hand -- to feast their eyes upon the varying outlines of the rugged Cornish coast ; for as the distance decreased the monotonous aspect gave place to one of intense interest", "context_len": 37}}}, "consort": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "There was no time to be lost , for the largest of the three pirate ships , which had , directly it was seen that her consort was captured , poured two heavy broadsides into the prize , was now approaching -- rowing but slowly , however , for the third vessel to come up", "context_len": 55}}}, "evading": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "She completely recognized the fidelity and sense of her loyal son , allowed Bart to lead her into the house , and tried to be calm and cheerful when he bade her good-bye , and , evading celebrating groups of his boy friends , made his way down to the ruined express shed", "context_len": 53}}}, "grated": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "A moment later the guns poured their iron contents among the pirates who clustered thickly on the forecastle and poop , and as the vessels grated together the knights sprang on board the corsair", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The room was meagrely furnished , with a table , a cot , and two chairs , and there were two grated windows overlooking the rear of the premises", "context_len": 29}}}, "corsair": {"freq": 49, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 47, "word_index": 101, "context": "This , doubtless , may be so , Sir Gervaise , the doge said , with a slight smile , but it is to the head that plans , rather than to the hand that strikes , that such success as you have achieved is due ; and the credit of this night attack is , as the cavalier Caretto tells me , wholly yours , for until you issued your final orders it seemed to him , and to the two good knights his companions , that there was naught to do but to remain in port and watch this corsair fleet sail away to carry out its work of destruction", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "When I came upon the ships in the dark , I thought at first that I had lighted on the pirates , but on letting the boat drift closely by them I soon saw they were not corsair galleys", "context_len": 39}}}, "hatches": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "All who did so were , without the loss of a minute 's time , thrown down into the hold of their ship , and the hatches secured over them", "context_len": 30}}}, "broadsides": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Appalled by the disaster that had befallen them , by the terrible effect of the broadsides , poured in at a few yards ' distance , and by the sight of so many of the dreaded warriors of the Cross , some of the corsairs threw down their arms and flung themselves on the deck or into the hold , crying for quarter ; those who resisted fell either under the swords of the knights , the vengeful axes of the late captives , or the pikes of the Sards ; but the great bulk , leaping from the bow or stern , swam ashore", "context_len": 105}}}, "reloaded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "As she did so , Ralph discharged the eight guns of the prize , which he had at once reloaded , into the bow of the corsair , the shot raking the crowded deck from end to end", "context_len": 38}}}, "raking": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Perhaps Maggie and Fred might not have kept their plots in such good order , or worked so perseveringly at weeding , raking , and watering , had it not been for Harry and Bessie , who never forgot to go each day and look at their plants , and never suffered a weed to do more than show the tip of its head above the soil", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "As the days grew longer and lighter , the garden also claimed attention , and the children were busy digging , raking , and planting , for this year there were to be special classes in the flower-show for exhibitors under fifteen , and both Peggy and Bobby had secret hopes of a prize", "context_len": 54}}}, "missiles": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 2, "context": "Among the missiles hurled into the town were balls filled with Greek fire , but the houses being entirely built of stone , no conflagrations of importance were caused by them , as a band of knights was organised specially to watch for these bombs , and whenever one of them was seen to fall , they hurried from their lookout to the spot , with a gang of slaves carrying baskets of earth and buckets of water , and quenched the flames before they had made any great headway", "context_len": 90}}}, "boarders": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 103, "context": "She did not talk nearly so much as in former times of her son ; perhaps he had told her that I was not to be worried , but she was very good and very nice , and I got sincerely attached to her : and I never saw the Duchess nor Lady Thesiger nor my old friends , although I heard that the Duchess was fairly well again , and was out and going into society ; and every one now seemed certain that Jim Randolph had gone to the bottom in the Star of Hope , but by universal consent the boarders decided that the news should be kept from mother , and mother grew much better", "context_len": 119}}}, "treble": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "We are sure to be greatly outnumbered , and , when we first spring on board , we must cut our way across the deck , and then form ourselves in a double or treble line across it , and , so fighting , gradually force them before us", "context_len": 49}}}, "exultation": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 41, "context": "Now the commons and the folk , one and all , when they saw Alaeddin , rejoiced in him with an exceeding joy and were glad for his deliverance ; but the ignominy which had befallen him and shame and the exultation of the envious had bowed down his head ; so he went forth and fell to going round about the city , perplexed anent his case and unknowing how all this had happened", "context_len": 75}}}, "cleft": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "Determined , self-willed he is ; you look , the next time you see him , at his chin , you observe the cleft in the middle ; there never was a chin like that yet without a mind according -- a mind , so to speak , set on the duty ahead of it -- a mind that is determined to conquer", "context_len": 63}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "Following the direction of his finger , we perceived the dorsal fins of two enormous sharks as they cleft their way towards the swimmer ; but , frightened by the splash of the shots , they contented themselves by swimming in large semicircles between us and the fugitive", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "More than one who tried to spring into close quarters fell cleft to the chin , and , ere his assailants could combine for a general rush , a body of knights , who had just beaten off their assailants , fell upon the ranks of the janissaries with a force and fury there was no withstanding , and the chosen troops of the sultan for the first time broke and fled", "context_len": 72}}}, "yataghan": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Gervaise sprang forward to take the place of a young Italian knight , who staggered back , with his helmet cleft by a heavy blow from the keen yataghan of the pirate captain", "context_len": 33}}}, "dismayed": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "The Creeks , much dismayed at the departure of their friends , and filled with aversion against the British and Spaniards , were compelled to open their towns to the English traders , to obtain the needed articles of European manufacture", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Whereupon Ted straightway found himself feeling very much dismayed at the announcement , and his heart misgave him , as hearts have a way of doing when the time has come for mere intention to take the more definite form of action", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "Peggy and Bobby tried , as Archie expressed it , to 'do a slope , ' but in vain , for , saying she had not seen them for a long time , and should like to talk to them , their unwelcome visitor took the dismayed pair into custody like a female policeman , and whirled them sternly along before her", "context_len": 62}}}, "recoiled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "The dense masses desisted , recoiled , and attempted to flee through the barrage , while death and wounds took heavy toll", "context_len": 22}}}, "defenders": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 68, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "remnant": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 35, "context": "Then , with loud yells of triumph , the stormers rushed the position on three sides simultaneously , bombarded the pill-boxes with grenade and smoke-bombs , and soon had the satisfaction of seeing the scanty remnant of a once numerous garrison come forth in fear and terror , accepting their conquerors ' assurances that their lives would be spared", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "Their roving disposition impelled them to leave this settlement also , and to migrate to the fertile Alachua plains , where they built Cuscowilla on the banks of a lake , and had to defend it against the attacks of the Tomocos , Utinas , Calloosas ( ? ) , Yamases and other remnant tribes of Florida , and the more northern refugees from Carolina , all of whom were helped by the Spaniards", "context_len": 74}}}, "assailants": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 56, "context": "As he stepped through the gateway one of the pirates sprang past him , but as he did so , a figure leapt out from beyond the wall , and plunged a dagger into his back , while at the same moment , by cutting down another pirate , Gervaise rid himself of one of his assailants in front ; but as he did so , he himself received a severe wound on the left shoulder from Hassan , who , before he could again raise his weapon , sprang upon him , and tried to hurl him to the ground", "context_len": 101}}}, "disdaining": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then the awful war cry of the Danes arose from earth to heaven , chilling the very blood and , disdaining all further concealment , the murderous warriors rushed forward , doubtless expecting to find the place almost undefended , and to carry the defences at a rush", "context_len": 48}}}, "marauders": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "As there were no marks of blood , it was supposed that they were suddenly pounced upon by a party of hidden marauders , who had been watching them from some hiding place , and had thrown themselves upon the knights before they had had time to draw their swords", "context_len": 50}}}, "tribesmen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "An attempt had then been made to ascertain whether they had been carried to Tripoli ; but the bey had little authority over the various tribesmen along the coast , and only replied that no such captives had been sold in the city", "context_len": 43}}}, "ransoming": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "And , moreover , Hassan , the Arab said grimly , it has doubtless not escaped you that as the Sultan of Turkey is fitting out an expedition to destroy the community of Rhodes , the chance of their ransoming their comrade is a very slight one", "context_len": 47}}}, "inscribed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "But Lilian had kept her surprise in the background , and it was only when all was ready that she opened a basket , and proudly drew out her masterpiece , a substantial-looking cake , with a cut-paper frill , and white icing on the top , on which in pink sugary letters were inscribed the words : 'Success to Sky Cottage ! ' Certainly some of the capitals were a little staggery , and the ' y ' had strayed into the pink border round the edge , but it was felt to be a triumph of culinary art all the same , and gave quite a grace to the table", "context_len": 112}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The knight opened it , disclosing a lace handkerchief marked with a deep brown stain , to which was fastened a piece of parchment inscribed : `` Stained with y e blood of y e Martyr His M tie King Charles '' , the jewelled hilt of a sword , a ring , and several papers", "context_len": 56}}}, "emaciated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "All were greatly emaciated , and their faces showed signs of the sufferings they had undergone", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Her voice could then be heard calling , `` Frosty ! Frosty ! come at once ; '' and then a thin and very emaciated woman was seen coming out of a summer-house just beyond", "context_len": 35}}}, "scarred": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "No sounds came from their lips , but their stifled sobs and the heaving of their naked shoulders , seamed and scarred by the strokes of their taskmasters ' whips , told the young knights , who stood unhelmeted and silent around , how deep was their emotion", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "He of the scarred face was quickly captured , being well-nigh winded with the effects of the stone I had thrown ; but the third was a more determined quarry , though , in his stupidity , instead of climbing upwards ( being far lighter than the soldiers , encumbered as they were with breast-pieces and riding-boots ) , he must needs make for the road , where he raced off at breakneck speed", "context_len": 74}}}, "capturing": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Noble gentleman ! to entrap a woman and then declare she shall not be entrapped ! To gain whatever honor there may be in a woman 's capture by running ahead of his ruffians and capturing her himself ! This is Southern manliness -- this is Southern chivalry ! I am glad I know it for what it really is", "context_len": 61}}}, "complement": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "Supposedly the string of barges was `` manned '' by women , the diminishing group halting at each barge to prolong the conversation before the crews boarded their respective boats , until , by the clatter almost overhead , the fugitives knew that the last barge had received its complement -- two , perhaps three , buxom and stolid German women", "context_len": 61}}}, "matted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "They had bathed , and combed their matted hair , which was alone sufficient to transform them , but the feeling that they were once more free men , and knights of an honoured Order , had done even more to effect the change ; and although they looked thin and worn , the martial bearing had come back naturally as they donned their knightly robes and buckled on swords", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "Here was a quantity of ground covered with brush , logs , and bushes : to have hauled all this away would have been an endless job , and after that the ground could neither be ploughed nor planted , being entirely matted with green roots , and cold and sour ; besides , the moment the sun was let into it , sprouts would begin to spring up from the stumps , and weeds , blackberry , and raspberry bushes from the ground , and cover it all over", "context_len": 90}}}, "scoffs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "We were subject to scoffs and blows ; now , thank God , we have swords to strike with , and though as yet our arms may not have regained their full strength , we could at least bear a share in a fray", "context_len": 44}}}, "veteran": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 33, "word_index": 45, "context": "There were present , besides Sir George Lee , who appeared to take a great interest in me , Lawyer Whitehead , Howard Hobbs and Jack Alexander of Iping , both of whom had seen service under Prince Rupert ; Arthur Conolly , an Irish veteran who had served in the Low Countries , and who had come over from Chichester for the occasion ; Arthur Lewis , a gentleman of Bramshott ; Percy Young , an officer of the navy , who in his earlier days had lost a leg in the action of La Rochelle ; Herbert Collings , a master mariner of Gosport , who used to be a frequent visitor at our house , and who greatly interested me with the account of his", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 63, "context": "But I must also bear testimony , not only to the bravery displayed by Sir Gervaise , Sir Ralph Harcourt , his lieutenant , and every one of the knights his crew , but to the admirable discipline , order , and good fellowship on board the galley , which would have done credit to the most experienced commander and to the most veteran knights of the Order", "context_len": 68}}}, "fabulous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "That the latter should be collecting these things for mercenary purposes with the design , when he should have enough , of shipping them overseas and selling them at fabulous prices , and then following them to live for the rest of his life in wealth and luxury amid the civilization whose value he knew , did not enter Bomba \u2019 s mind", "context_len": 63}}}, "oppressors": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 81, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}}}, "anticipating": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 65, "context": "Many persons have accordingly supposed that the plan of assassinating Philip was devised in Greece ; that Demosthenes was a party to it ; that Pausanias was the agent for carrying it into execution ; and that Demosthenes was so confident of the success of the plot , and exulted so much in this certainty , that he could not resist the temptation of thus anticipating its announcement", "context_len": 68}}}, "shackled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "So they went till they came to Alaeddin and said to him , `` O our lord Alaeddin , blame us not , for that the Sultan hath bidden us carry thee to him , bound and shackled ; wherefore we beseech thee of excusement , for that we are under a royal commandment and may not gainsay it", "context_len": 59}}}, "landfall": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A good landfall , Gervaise , Ralph said", "context_len": 8}}}, "inlets": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "We will send a boat ashore to try and find out from the natives they may come across whether any vessels , bearing the appearance of Moorish corsairs , have been seen passing up the coast , and also to find out what bays and inlets there are where they would be likely to anchor", "context_len": 55}}}, "cape": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 16, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}}}, "villagers": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "However , mounted messengers were sent off at once , up and down the coast , to give warning to the inhabitants of the towns to put themselves into a posture of defence , and to the villagers to fly with their wives and families into the interior as soon as they saw galleys of doubtful appearance approaching", "context_len": 58}}}, "speediest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` This is to me the easiest of all things ; so be easy , for it is enough that thou tell me of that which thou wishest and I will fetch it thee from the abysses of the world on the speediest wise", "context_len": 44}}}, "seaports": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I shall be greatly obliged if you will undertake this mission to the seaports", "context_len": 14}}}, "authoritatively": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Pansy was leaning over her with a book crying authoritatively -- '' Read to me , read to me", "context_len": 20}}}, "syndic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "In each place you will , if possible , see the syndic himself , and such of his council as can be got quickly together", "context_len": 25}}}, "northeastern": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 20, "context": "The Cheroki language was spoken in many dialects before the people emigrated to the lands allotted to them in the northeastern part of the Indian Territory , and even now a difference may be observed between the Western Cheroki and the Eastern or Mountain Cheroki , which is the language of the people that remained in the hills of Western North Carolina and Eastern Tennessee", "context_len": 65}}}, "papal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Pray them at Ostia to send off news down the coast , or to request the papal authorities to despatch mounted messengers", "context_len": 22}}}, "calms": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Thus , at the earliest , it must be from six to seven days before you can bring us the news there ; if you meet with calms or foul winds you may be well nigh double that time", "context_len": 39}}}, "northwards": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "They said the peasants had told them that several strange craft , using both oars and sails , had been noticed passing northwards , and that so strong was the opinion that these were either Algerines or Tunisians that , for the last three or four days , none of the fishing craft had ventured to put to sea", "context_len": 59}}}, "coastline": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "As the day went on , he saw that the coastline was now east and west , and felt that he must be off the most northerly point of the promontory ; he accordingly laid his course to the northeast , which would take him close to Cape Saloman , the most easterly point of Crete , and from two hundred and fifty to three hundred miles distant", "context_len": 68}}}, "indentation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "They thought the most likely places for corsairs to rendezvous would be in a deep indentation north of Cape Bellavista , or behind Cape Comino", "context_len": 25}}}, "maps": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 9, "context": "The walls were adorned with school drawings and prize maps , fastened up with tacks ; a bookcase of much-read volumes filled the space between the fireplace and the window ; and in the corner might be found a miscellaneous collection of cricket-bats , fishing-rods , tennis-racquets , bows and arrows , croquet-mallets , sticks , balls and ninepins , and other articles very dear to the children 's hearts , but which Nancy generally classed impartially under the head of 'lumber", "context_len": 82}}}, "overtaking": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "And very attentively everybody listened to the story , as I told it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached", "context_len": 128}}}, "entrap": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Noble gentleman ! to entrap a woman and then declare she shall not be entrapped ! To gain whatever honor there may be in a woman 's capture by running ahead of his ruffians and capturing her himself ! This is Southern manliness -- this is Southern chivalry ! I am glad I know it for what it really is", "context_len": 61}}}, "shepherd": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 9, "context": "One day she came across her father 's old shepherd , looking as miserable as could be ; and , on inquiring the cause , found that a mischievous boy had thrown a stone at his favourite dog , which had broken its leg , and he was afraid it would have to be killed", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The attendant cruiser was circling round at top speed , as if to shepherd her flock before entrusting them to the care of another", "context_len": 24}}}, "traffic": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "If they had not been anxious to conceal their near presence , they would have been likely to put into this bay in search of plunder and captives ; for Tempe , one of the largest of the Sardinian towns , lies but a short distance away , and there must be a considerable amount of traffic", "context_len": 57}}}, "reaped": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 87, "context": "Our defences are all complete ; our recent peace with Egypt has enabled us to fill up our magazines with provisions of all kinds ; the inhabitants of the Island have had ample warning to move into the town , carrying with them everything of value ; so the Turks will obtain but little plunder , and will be able to gather no means of subsistence on the island , as every animal has been driven within the walls , and even the unripe corn has been reaped and brought in", "context_len": 91}}}, "splits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "As you know , Sir Gervaise , the bay opposite this island splits up into two , running a long way inland , like the fangs of a great tooth", "context_len": 30}}}, "strait": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "His eyes might stare dully at the page , and his lips might keep repeating words printed there , but his thoughts persistently dwelt on the desperate strait into which he had fallen , and he speculated on the probable course that would be pursued by Lemuel Hayden", "context_len": 48}}}, "contradict": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "They said that if caught they would be either killed or made slaves of , and I could not contradict them , but said that , in the first place , as I was ready to run the risk , there was no reason why they should n't do so , and in the second , there was no chance whatever of their being taken , as , if discovered , we should get so long a start that we could either escape them altogether or run the boat ashore at some point where the trees came down to the water 's edge , carry the boat up and hide it , and then move up into the hills until the corsairs had gone", "context_len": 124}}}, "combustibles": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 16, "context": "The rest of you will go on board these native craft , and see that the combustibles are fairly distributed among them , the wood and straw soaked with pitch and oil , as also the sails and ropes , and that the decks are well coated ; this is a most important duty", "context_len": 54}}}, "smear": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "If we smear these craft you are going to fetch with pitch and oil , and fill the holds with combustibles , and so turn them into fire ships , we may at least do the pirates a tremendous lot of harm", "context_len": 42}}}, "headpieces": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` What with their breastplates and headpieces their running would be like to that of Goliath of Gath", "context_len": 18}}}, "olive": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "As he spoke he ran down over the boulders , and plunged off a rock into the clear sea , his white figure being traceable against the olive brown sea-wrack waving far below , as he swam for some distance below the surface , and then rose , shook the water from his eyes , and struck out for the lugger lying becalmed in the offing", "context_len": 66}}}, "inroads": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "In this neighbourhood we have as yet been preserved from the evils of war , but for many years past the Danes , those evil men , have renewed their inroads , as they used to make them before the great King Alfred pacified the country", "context_len": 46}}}, "dissensions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "One day they were going into the town in this way , at a time when it happened that there was an assembly convened , which was in a state of great perplexity on account of the civil dissensions and contests which prevailed in the country", "context_len": 46}}}, "factions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "As he had no family , there was , of course , no direct heir to the crown , and , in case of any misfortune happening by which his life should be lost , Macedon would become at once the prey of contending factions , which would immediately arise , each presenting its own candidate for the vacant throne", "context_len": 60}}}, "wrangled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "All talked , wrangled , and protested", "context_len": 7}}}, "cope": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "They said that it was absurd to suppose that they could , at a moment 's notice , fit out ships to cope with a fleet of corsairs ; and their sole idea was to man the forts , and to repel an attack", "context_len": 44}}}, "elapse": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Another eighteen years had , however , yet to elapse before she would go out to India , to follow in his steps , and to render to Hindustan a loving return for this year of horrors", "context_len": 37}}}, "harry": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 81, "context": "Then there was Pixie , the pony , who would come whinnying up from the further side of a field to poke her soft nose into the children 's pockets for pieces of bread or lumps of sugar ; there were numerous cats who lived in the barns and stables , and Tabbyskins , the stately gray Persian , who usually sat sunning herself on the pigsty wall , keeping a strict eye on naughty Jack , who was wont to harry her if he got the chance", "context_len": 88}}}, "splashes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "As soon as they did so , they ceased rowing , and the splashes of the anchors as they fell into the water were heard", "context_len": 25}}}, "anchors": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "His example animated the men , who immediately began to load their muskets and serve out boarding pikes and broadswords , while the master took steps to lay out a couple of anchors in readiness to warp the ship into deep water directly the floodtide should release her", "context_len": 48}}}, "faggots": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 70, "context": "Of course I was but one of many who were to gasp out their lives in this dreadful Aceldama ; and in a very short time each post , or stake , was decorated with its own separate victim , some of whom , it seemed , were to perish by the torture of fire , for after the victims had been secured to the stakes , huge bundles of faggots , composed of dry twigs and branches , were piled around some of them", "context_len": 85}}}, "pikes": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 86, "context": "Appalled by the disaster that had befallen them , by the terrible effect of the broadsides , poured in at a few yards ' distance , and by the sight of so many of the dreaded warriors of the Cross , some of the corsairs threw down their arms and flung themselves on the deck or into the hold , crying for quarter ; those who resisted fell either under the swords of the knights , the vengeful axes of the late captives , or the pikes of the Sards ; but the great bulk , leaping from the bow or stern , swam ashore", "context_len": 105}}}, "charcoal": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "There was charcoal on board , and a flat stone served as a hearth in the bottom of the boat , but he had no means of lighting a fire , for this the fishermen would have brought off when they came on board in the morning", "context_len": 47}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "As it was the month of June , and warm , the men were lucky to be able to have a fire , considering the scarcity of fuel and the difficulty of conveying wood and charcoal up to the firing-line", "context_len": 40}}}, "woodmen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The people are all charcoal burners and woodmen in winter , and I was therefore able to get together some thirty or forty axes and hatchets , which will be ample , with the arms we took from the Moors , to equip the ninety Christians", "context_len": 46}}}, "hatchets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The people are all charcoal burners and woodmen in winter , and I was therefore able to get together some thirty or forty axes and hatchets , which will be ample , with the arms we took from the Moors , to equip the ninety Christians", "context_len": 46}}}, "equip": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "The people are all charcoal burners and woodmen in winter , and I was therefore able to get together some thirty or forty axes and hatchets , which will be ample , with the arms we took from the Moors , to equip the ninety Christians", "context_len": 46}}}, "sheepskins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "There was plenty of the coarse black cloth which they wear in summer -- in winter , of course , they are clad in sheepskins ; and I have sufficient white cotton cloth to make the crosses", "context_len": 37}}}, "detachment": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 47, "context": "They catch the rain , in such cases , with cloths , and afterward wring out the water ; though in this instance , as the historians of that day say , the soldiers did not wait for this tardy method of supply , but the whole detachment held back their heads and opened their mouths , to catch the drops of rain as they fell", "context_len": 66}}}, "ravaging": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Another fleet of Danes , under Heming and Eglaf , has joined the former fleet , and both together are ravaging Wessex as far northward as Berkshire ; we have sent all the men we can spare to join the army , but the king , persuaded by Edric , will not give the Etheling Edmund any command therein", "context_len": 59}}}, "madness": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "Since it would be madness for the whole crush to keep together , we 've arranged to separate , if we do get clear , and work in pairs", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Orton Campbell , '' she retorted with spirit , `` to charge with madness those who oppose us", "context_len": 18}}}, "insanity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "They were ready for any encounter in which there was a chance , however faint , of success ; but all saw that for a single galley to attack one of the largest corsair fleets that had ever set out , would be nothing short of insanity", "context_len": 47}}}, "extricate": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 114, "context": "Mark Penelly had gone over to the other side of the lugger while the conversation was going on , and he did not face the man he looked upon as his rival ; while Harry , unnoticed by the busy fishers as he swam round , went on , touching the sides of the lugger as he lightly swam , but only the next moment to find himself entangled in a quantity of the thin mackerel net , which seemed somehow to descend upon him like a cloud , and before he could realise the fact he was under water , hopelessly fettered by the net , and feeling that if he could not extricate himself directly he should be a dead man", "context_len": 123}}}, "coaster": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "He now kept a sharp lookout for distant sails , for he was out of the ordinary course a coaster would take , and would have attracted the attention of any corsair coming out from the land ; the sea , however , remained clear of ships", "context_len": 47}}}, "sleeveless": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "There can be but little sewing required for these sleeveless gowns , nor need it be carefully done", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "When he had dressed them , Muley went out and returned with some Turkish garments , consisting of a pair of baggy trousers of yellow cotton , a white shirt of the same material , and a sleeveless jacket of blue cloth embroidered with yellow trimming ; a pair of yellow slippers completed the costume", "context_len": 55}}}, "gowns": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "I do not think that I left out anything ; our jumping on dearest Mother \u2019 s bed ; the new Silver ; [ 1 ] the Holly and the Mistletoe ; the Christmas Box ; the choosing the gowns ; the Cake , etc", "context_len": 45}}}, "coated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "The rest of you will go on board these native craft , and see that the combustibles are fairly distributed among them , the wood and straw soaked with pitch and oil , as also the sails and ropes , and that the decks are well coated ; this is a most important duty", "context_len": 54}}}, "endured": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The released Christians were no less exultant at the prospect of taking vengeance for the sufferings they had so long endured , and the scene on board all four ships was most animated", "context_len": 33}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 18, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "dictate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "But you must speak to her from yourself , and tell her that she is not to dictate any terms to us with regard to the pupils who come to be educated at Sunnyside", "context_len": 34}}}, "painters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "He noted their glorious appearance in the water , their bodies of a bright green , mixed with a silver colour , and their tails of a shining golden yellow , and wondered at the vulgar error of the painters , who always represent this fish monstrously crooked and deformed", "context_len": 50}}}, "grape": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "As soon as the boats were alongside the galley , the guns of the four vessels opened fire with grape into the crowded ships , now lit up by the flames as clearly as at noontide , while the battle cry of the Order sounded high above the din", "context_len": 49}}}, "noontide": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "For her parents \u2019 sake she had dutifully held back , during the noontide and early afternoon of her history , from much that she would fain have done ; and though the latter part of her afternoon \u2019 had been full and busy , with freedom to do what she willed , yet even this was not enough", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was the noontide heat , and two Danish warriors reclined under the shadow of an ancient beech , hard by the entrenched camp of the Danes , a few days after the arrival of Alfgar therein", "context_len": 37}}}, "hinder": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 76, "context": "these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about to work upon him by practice , that he might hinder him from giving his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour to Alaeddin , and accordingly said to him , [ 436 ] `` O my lord , all the treasures of the world were not worth a paring of thy daughter Bedrulbudour 's nails ; indeed , Thy Highness overrateth this upon her", "context_len": 128}}}, "appalled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Appalled by the disaster that had befallen them , by the terrible effect of the broadsides , poured in at a few yards ' distance , and by the sight of so many of the dreaded warriors of the Cross , some of the corsairs threw down their arms and flung themselves on the deck or into the hold , crying for quarter ; those who resisted fell either under the swords of the knights , the vengeful axes of the late captives , or the pikes of the Sards ; but the great bulk , leaping from the bow or stern , swam ashore", "context_len": 105}}}, "vengeful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 77, "context": "Appalled by the disaster that had befallen them , by the terrible effect of the broadsides , poured in at a few yards ' distance , and by the sight of so many of the dreaded warriors of the Cross , some of the corsairs threw down their arms and flung themselves on the deck or into the hold , crying for quarter ; those who resisted fell either under the swords of the knights , the vengeful axes of the late captives , or the pikes of the Sards ; but the great bulk , leaping from the bow or stern , swam ashore", "context_len": 105}}}, "poops": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "A few of these had been killed by the broadsides ; but the guns had been aimed at the poops and forecastles , where the corsairs were clustered together , and consequently the number of galley slaves who had fallen was comparatively small", "context_len": 43}}}, "forecastles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "A few of these had been killed by the broadsides ; but the guns had been aimed at the poops and forecastles , where the corsairs were clustered together , and consequently the number of galley slaves who had fallen was comparatively small", "context_len": 43}}}, "malefactors": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "463 , in the following terms : `` The great Coweta town , on Chatahuchi or Apalachucly river , twelve miles above Apalachucla town , is called the bloody town , where the micos , chiefs and warriors assemble , when a general war is proposed , and here captives and state malefactors are put to death", "context_len": 57}}}, "uninjured": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 7, "context": "Some gained the boats that still remained uninjured , and rowed for the opposite shore ; the greater number rushed into the water and strove to recross it either by swimming or by the aid of the debris of the shattered boats", "context_len": 42}}}, "ostensible": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "One lovely April morning Peggy started off alone , on the ostensible errand of going to pay half a crown which was owing to old Williams , the mole-catcher , but the more real one of gathering primroses and hunting about for birds ' nests", "context_len": 45}}}, "engaging": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "Meanwhile I had practically abandoned my attempts to effect my escape , for the time being at least ; for the conviction had at length been forced upon me that neither Banda nor his people would ever willingly let me go , and that , therefore , before engaging in any further attempts , I must contrive to disarm suspicion completely , and create the impression that I had at length resigned myself to live out my life in this remote African town , and with savages only for my companions", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I 'm sure we had no thought of deceiving you , '' responded Harry with one of his engaging smiles", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Proceeding to England , and posing as a mercantile marine officer , forged documents and an engaging manner procured him an introduction to Whitehall , with the result that he was given a commission in the Royal Naval Reserve and appointed to an armed merchant cruiser", "context_len": 46}}}, "underlying": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "People are on the whole expected to go to Church , -- or , if Dissenters , just as much to go to Chapel , -- and though the going to Church , as a matter of course , does not at all indicate the lack of deeper reasons , of purer motives underlying , it does make the going a very easy matter", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Despite her somewhat fragile appearance , and an appetite commonly small , there must have been a marvellous amount of underlying strength , -- of the wiriness \u2019 which often belongs to delicate-looking people", "context_len": 34}}}, "arguments": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "Easily swayed as he was in general , he was not to be influenced in the only right direction now , and all Lena 's arguments were thrown away", "context_len": 29}}}, "plaudits": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Are we not bound to humility ? And here you are all longing for the plaudits of the nobles and ladies of Genoa !", "context_len": 24}}}, "guerdon": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "I regarded it not as a lightly given favour , the result of a passing fancy by one who gave favours freely , but as a pledge of friendship and as a guerdon for what I had done , and therefore , more to be honoured than the gifts of a Republic freed from a passing danger", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Why must thou go to shore ? We will feed and guerdon thee well if thou wilt stay with us", "context_len": 21}}}, "herds": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "Early on Tuesday morning the real business of the day started , for then the shepherds began to bring down their flocks of sheep from the surrounding mountains , the cattle-drovers came with their sleek cows and long-horned young bullocks , and whole herds of rough little Welsh ponies were driven with much noise and shouting from the high moorlands over the border", "context_len": 63}}}, "goats": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 26, "context": "Just now , too , as Alejandro Vigil , a ragged lad with a scarlet cap on his black head , went by , driving his goats to pasture , he had said `` rogue ! '' under his breath", "context_len": 40}}}, "dislodge": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "We must send up and drive in as many of these as possible , and of course we shall send messengers to Tempe ; but it will need a very large force to combat these pirates , who will be able to come down and plunder and destroy , and then retire to the hills , whence it will be hard to dislodge them", "context_len": 64}}}, "harassing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "I should say that you had best avoid a pitched battle with them , but keep on harassing them by night and day , cutting off all who separate themselves from the main body , until at last they are completely worn out", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "This has been a harassing and eventful day", "context_len": 8}}}, "boars": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "We are all hunters , for there are wild boars and stags in the forest , and wolves too , and wild sheep on the higher mountains", "context_len": 27}}}, "stags": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "We are all hunters , for there are wild boars and stags in the forest , and wolves too , and wild sheep on the higher mountains", "context_len": 27}}}, "pike": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "Evidently the Dutchmen thought our losses under fire had been greater than they actually were ; but they soon realized their mistake , and with thrust of pike , swordthrusts , musket and pistol shots , they were swept aside as fast as their heads appeared above the bulwarks", "context_len": 49}}}, "unbounded": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 66, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The delight of Neram at the meeting was unbounded , and that of Bomba was scarcely less great", "context_len": 18}}}, "manufactured": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "He had manufactured a marvellous hat-grip , warranted to defy the windiest of weather , and presented it to the housemaid ; but when the poor girl tried to take off her Sunday hat , she found it so tightly fixed to her hair that it took the combined efforts of the other servants , aided by the liberal use of a pair of scissors , to remove the construction from her head", "context_len": 73}}}, "ruse": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "The bills , however , gave him a chance to pull himself together , as he made a ruse of carefully examining them , while his heart thumped like a trip-hammer at the thought of Uncle Fritz coming down to Nuneham and finding him stranded there , helpless , good-for-nothing fellow that he felt himself to be", "context_len": 57}}}, "valuables": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Even then the inhabitants feared to put out , believing that the flags were but a ruse , and numbers of them fled at once , with their families and valuables , to the mountains", "context_len": 35}}}, "allayed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "It changed their agitation and terror into a feeling of suspense , in awaiting the answer of the soothsayers , which was far less painful and dangerous ; and at length , when the answer came , it allayed their anxiety and fear altogether", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "At length , however , Alexander 's fears were so far allayed that he concluded to enter the city", "context_len": 19}}}, "annihilated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "As soon as it was understood that the galley of the knights had not only captured the thirteen corsairs , but had destroyed eleven others , and had thus annihilated a fleet that was intended to prey upon the commerce of Italy , and ravage the western coast , the alarm was succeeded by the wildest enthusiasm", "context_len": 57}}}, "ravage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "As soon as it was understood that the galley of the knights had not only captured the thirteen corsairs , but had destroyed eleven others , and had thus annihilated a fleet that was intended to prey upon the commerce of Italy , and ravage the western coast , the alarm was succeeded by the wildest enthusiasm", "context_len": 57}}}, "flotilla": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 55, "context": "With her quick-firers manned , in case a U-boat lurked in the vicinity , the Angiboo stopped until the last of the passengers and crew of the Koning der Zee gained her deck ; then , quickly increasing speed to twenty-five knots , the destroyer shaped a south-westerly course to rejoin the rest of the flotilla", "context_len": 56}}}, "picturesquely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "By the time Ralph had obtained the fresh meat and stores he came ashore to purchase , the greater part of the population were gathered on the shore , and a flotilla of boats put out with him , filled with picturesquely dressed men and women", "context_len": 46}}}, "musicians": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The galley was the first attraction , and , mounting her sides , the ladies presented their offerings of fruit , while the men cheered , and waved their hats ; many musicians came out in the boats , and these played on bagpipes and three-reeded flutes a succession of airs peculiar to the island", "context_len": 55}}}, "bagpipes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "The galley was the first attraction , and , mounting her sides , the ladies presented their offerings of fruit , while the men cheered , and waved their hats ; many musicians came out in the boats , and these played on bagpipes and three-reeded flutes a succession of airs peculiar to the island", "context_len": 55}}}, "flutes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "So they fared on with him in great state , with four of the Viziers for his sword-bearers , whilst all the troops and people of the city , both townsfolk and strangers , walked in procession before him , carrying flambeaux and drums and flutes and instruments of mirth and music , till they brought him to his palace , when he alighted and entering , sat down , as did also the Viziers and Amirs who were in his company , whilst the mamelukes brought sherbets and sweetmeats [ 493 ] and gave all who were with him in the procession to drink , albeit they were a multitude of folk whose number might not be told", "context_len": 119}}}, "nestling": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "There , at nightfall , the Christians relieved the slaves for some hours at their benches , and the next morning the circle of hills round Genoa , with the city nestling at their feet on the water 's edge , and climbing for some distance up their slopes , was in view", "context_len": 53}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 55, "context": "Up the steep bank they trudged , through fields of bracken higher than Bobby 's head , getting out at last upon a white road that wound along the top of a cliff , with a stream roaring below , till round a sudden turn they came upon Pengarth , a prim little village , nestling in a hollow , and consisting of a short street and a big chapel and school-house", "context_len": 72}}}, "slopes": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 18, "context": "The vast throng then rolled along upon the top of the wall till they came to stairways and slopes by which they could descend into the city , and , pouring down through all these avenues , they spread over the streets , and satiated the hatred and rage , which had been gathering strength for seven long months , in bursting into houses , and killing and destroying all that came in their way", "context_len": 75}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "Indeed , who knows of a more inspiriting sight anywhere than Eton College on an April morning ? The quaint old buildings seem to bask in the broad spring sunshine ; the trees that dot the grass-bare turf where the Upper School fronts the street are already casting tiny leaf-shadows , and on the other side , where the garden slopes down to the Thames , many a little branch and bush begins to glow with color", "context_len": 77}}}, "breakfasted": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "While Caretto was away , the knights and crews breakfasted , and the former put on their armour and gayest attire , in readiness for the landing", "context_len": 27}}}, "gayest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "While Caretto was away , the knights and crews breakfasted , and the former put on their armour and gayest attire , in readiness for the landing", "context_len": 27}}}, "feted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "But you must put up with the disagreeables as well as the advantages of being commander , and must submit to be honoured and feted here , as well as getting no end of credit at Rhodes", "context_len": 37}}}, "deserving": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 64, "context": "to the Sultan 's highness ? It [ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm , that thou shouldst merit of him this boon ? Again , this that thou cravest is", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The cowboy was a fine fellow , a lad of sterling worth , and fortune could not have visited her favors upon one more deserving", "context_len": 25}}}, "batteries": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 12, "context": "A cannon was at once fired to give notice to the other batteries to be in readiness , and as soon as the dark mass of boats was made out the guns of the fort opened a destructive fire upon them , and a moment later were seconded by those from the fortress ; these , however , were at present being fired almost at random , as the Turkish boats could not be made out at that distance", "context_len": 79}}}, "squadron": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 12, "context": "Joined by several others of like nature , Lewis had collected a squadron of seven swift vessels ; but the Assurance had captured two of the ships , and Lewis , with four of his fellow rogues , formed the party of captives whose fate now hung in the balance as they lay in irons in the castle", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The appearance of a Flying Fortress squadron high overhead furnished a welcome change of thought", "context_len": 15}}}, "bruited": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 67, "context": "But Percy was popular , Seabrooke was not ; and even the masters were inclined to believe that the latter must have been careless and forgetful and mislaid the money , while believing he had put it in the place he indicated , and presently -- no one knew exactly how it started or could trace the rumor to its source -- presently it began to be bruited about among the boys that Seabrooke was keeping it for his own use and had never intended to return it to Percy , and was now making him his scape-goat", "context_len": 98}}}, "offing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 65, "context": "As he spoke he ran down over the boulders , and plunged off a rock into the clear sea , his white figure being traceable against the olive brown sea-wrack waving far below , as he swam for some distance below the surface , and then rose , shook the water from his eyes , and struck out for the lugger lying becalmed in the offing", "context_len": 66}}}, "dockyard": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 43, "context": "Through his influence , his intimacy with Sir Thomas Middleton , and also through the interest which Sir Thomas showed towards me , the matter was an easy one , and before August was out I found myself being escorted down to the dockyard to join the Gannet", "context_len": 48}}}, "decorate": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "I should decorate the galley with all the flags on board : should set every one to work to make great flags with the cross of the Order to hoist to the masthead of the prizes , instead of the little things that are now flying ; and under them we will hoist the flags of the corsairs , among which are those of Tripoli , Tunis , and Algiers", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 80, "context": "of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very strongly influence him", "context_len": 128}}}, "pealed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "The storm raged all night : the rain poured down in a driving flood ; the lightning flashed ; the thunder pealed without rest , echoing from one to another of the mountain-peaks in a long , heavy roll ; and the wind blew in furious gusts , shaking even Mr", "context_len": 51}}}, "balconies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "On landing , the party waited until the knights had joined them , and then proceeded up the street to the ducal palace , amidst enthusiastic cheering from the crowd that lined the road , occupied the windows and balconies , and even scrambled on the housetops , the ladies waving their handkerchiefs and scarves", "context_len": 55}}}, "tapestries": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "The ships in the port were all decked with flags , and the front windows and balconies of every house were hung with tapestries and bright curtains", "context_len": 27}}}, "doge": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 12, "context": "This , doubtless , may be so , Sir Gervaise , the doge said , with a slight smile , but it is to the head that plans , rather than to the hand that strikes , that such success as you have achieved is due ; and the credit of this night attack is , as the cavalier Caretto tells me , wholly yours , for until you issued your final orders it seemed to him , and to the two good knights his companions , that there was naught to do but to remain in port and watch this corsair fleet sail away to carry out its work of destruction", "context_len": 112}}}, "gala": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "As it wound through the streets in splendid array , the grand master and officials in all their robes of state , the knights in full armour and the mantles of the Order , while the inhabitants in gala costume lined the streets , windows , and housetops , the ladies waving scarves and scattering flowers down on the knights , the roar of great cannon on the south side of the city showed that the Turks had commenced the attack in another quarter", "context_len": 84}}}, "duke": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 19, "context": "When Napoleon 's Old Guard was advancing up the hill , the only sight they could see was the duke and a few mounted officers , till a voice was heard , `` Up , guards , and at them ! '' And the best men in the whole French army , the pick of the bravest of the brave , fell back before the onset of the British guards", "context_len": 70}}}, "reflects": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He has won a victory that not only reflects the highest honour upon the Order , but is an inestimable service to Italy , and has freed her from a corsair fleet that would have been a scourge to her , both at sea and to the towns and villages along the coast", "context_len": 53}}}, "scourge": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "Others , whose fortunes had been ruined , and whose friends and relatives had been destroyed , in the course , or in the sequel of his victories , rejoiced that he who had been such a scourge and curse to others , had himself sunk , at last under the just judgment of Heaven", "context_len": 55}}}, "cavaliers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "`` The late Owen Wentworth , by reason of his excessive loyalty to His Majesty the King , and of his careful thought for the proper upbringing of his son during the past troublous times , did part with a portion of his family estates and personal property in order to furnish His Majesty with such aid as all loyal cavaliers were bound by their duty to give", "context_len": 68}}}, "massed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 10, "context": "And do you think that people are to be all massed in heaven , losing their various identities , their differing tastes , their separate natures ? Going from this lower world so full of its adaptations , where colour and form take on a thousand changes , and life and pursuit can be varied almost at will , to a mere dead level of perfect felicity ? To leave earth where no two things are alike , and go to heaven to find no two different ! The Lord 's preparations mean more than that", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Our massed antiaircraft , plus the umbrella of our land-based fighter planes , will beat off any air attack our enemies may make", "context_len": 23}}}, "subcommander": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Any credit that is due to me is shared equally by my subcommander here , Sir Ralph Harcourt , and indeed by every knight of my company", "context_len": 27}}}, "cavalier": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 73, "context": "Their voices were subdued lest they should reach the king 's ears ; but , respectful as was the bearing of all the members of the group by the fire , they clearly split into two halves : on the one hand , the officers of the escort who were teasing the boy , and on the other , a group of gentlemen , some wearing the conventional ribbons and laces of a cavalier , others in the rough cloth of country wear , stained with the mud of country lanes , while the master and mistress of the house moved from one guest to another , evidently nervous at the doubtful honour that such a royal visit had brought to their roof", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Now that the danger was past , Drake and I could not help laughing at our cavalier companion and his fair burden , for the hag had clasped him tightly round the waist with her skinny arms , while the cat , perched on her shoulders , was rubbing its head against the back of Felgate \u2019 s plumed hat , so that it was being continually thrust over his eyes despite his frequent attempts to place it firmly on his head", "context_len": 82}}}, "ducal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "On landing , the party waited until the knights had joined them , and then proceeded up the street to the ducal palace , amidst enthusiastic cheering from the crowd that lined the road , occupied the windows and balconies , and even scrambled on the housetops , the ladies waving their handkerchiefs and scarves", "context_len": 55}}}, "housetops": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "As it wound through the streets in splendid array , the grand master and officials in all their robes of state , the knights in full armour and the mantles of the Order , while the inhabitants in gala costume lined the streets , windows , and housetops , the ladies waving scarves and scattering flowers down on the knights , the roar of great cannon on the south side of the city showed that the Turks had commenced the attack in another quarter", "context_len": 84}}}, "scarves": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "As it wound through the streets in splendid array , the grand master and officials in all their robes of state , the knights in full armour and the mantles of the Order , while the inhabitants in gala costume lined the streets , windows , and housetops , the ladies waving scarves and scattering flowers down on the knights , the roar of great cannon on the south side of the city showed that the Turks had commenced the attack in another quarter", "context_len": 84}}}, "municipal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "At the palace were assembled all the municipal authorities , and the congratulations given on board were here repeated", "context_len": 19}}}, "seacoast": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "John , who had saved the commerce and seacoast of Italy from the greatest danger that had menaced them since the days when the Northern rovers had desolated the shores of the Mediterranean", "context_len": 33}}}, "rovers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "The Gannet first sailed through the Straits of Gibraltar to the Mediterranean Sea , for the purpose of keeping an eye on the Algerine rovers , who had again begun , in spite of the sharp lesson taught them by Admiral Blake , to molest peaceful traders", "context_len": 47}}}, "banqueting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Upwards it leads to the banqueting hall , and you can comprehend my escape this evening , '' said he ; `` but our path is now downwards , unless you would like to go up and see the drunken beasts of murderers snoring off their debauch upon the floor as they fell ; oh , that it were lawful for a Christian man to cut their throats as they lie ; many innocent lives would be saved thereby , which those brutes will live to destroy", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Taking the torches from the banqueting halls , they sallied forth , alarming the city with their shouts , and with the flashing of the lights they bore", "context_len": 28}}}, "courtly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Most of the young knights , belonging as they did to noble families , and accustomed from childhood to courtly ceremonies and festivities , were quite at home here", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "She used to study with Madame de Coucy in the mornings and go for walks among the flowers out in the garden there in the afternoon , and way beyond it too sometimes , and Richard would often come down from London for a visit , and he taught her English courtly ways and to play the mandolin ( Albert looked significantly toward the quaint mandolin , with a faded blue ribbon attached to it , that was lying among the other treasures on the table ) ; and when the King could not come for a regular visit , he would just ride down for a word and kiss", "context_len": 110}}}, "inexpedient": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "While feeling the need to draw for herself some line of demarcation between things expedient and things inexpedient , she does not appear to have fallen into the error , so common amongst really earnest and excellent people , of counting that the line which she rightly drew for herself must of necessity be the only right line for everybody else", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Alfgar had volunteered to share the perils of his adopted lord , but was kindly told that it would be inexpedient", "context_len": 21}}}, "dubbed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I see that you wear gilded spurs , Sir Ralph , another lady said ; you must therefore be a dubbed knight ?", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "Dommett , the boatkeeper , received me without signs of surprise , and on telling him of my misadventure , he merely ejaculated a loud `` Well done '' , which was a favourite expression of his , no matter what caused him to make it , insomuch that on board he was dubbed by his messmates `` Well done Dommett ''", "context_len": 62}}}, "countess": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 12, "context": "When Claudia shortly afterwards left the room , he said to the countess , Excuse me for breaking in , Agatha , but I felt that it was much better to agree with her , and not to make overmuch of the matter ; she is just of an age to make some one a hero , and she could hardly have chosen a better subject for her worship", "context_len": 69}}}, "indirectly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "He has a head to plan , and , in the case I speak of , a happy thought of his not only saved the lives of ourselves and Sir John Boswell , but , indirectly , was the means of preventing two of our galleys being captured by the corsairs", "context_len": 51}}}, "signoras": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Look round the hall , signoras , and see if any of you can pick him out from the rest of us", "context_len": 22}}}, "vestige": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "How can we choose ? the countess said laughingly , when none of them resemble our ideal hero ? Most of them are pleasant and courtly looking youths , but as yet there is scarce a vestige of hair on their faces , and one could not fancy any of them as the destroyer of the fleet of corsairs", "context_len": 59}}}, "destroyer": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 53, "context": "How can we choose ? the countess said laughingly , when none of them resemble our ideal hero ? Most of them are pleasant and courtly looking youths , but as yet there is scarce a vestige of hair on their faces , and one could not fancy any of them as the destroyer of the fleet of corsairs", "context_len": 59}}}, "staunch": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "If the captain thought so , however , he succeeded marvelously well in hiding his feelings , trying his best all the time to brace his teammates up , encouraging the equally staunch , chiding a few who showed symptoms of wavering , and entreating one or two who apparently had lost heart", "context_len": 53}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Peggy shook her head , for speech seemed almost impossible at that moment , and she broke away from the keeper 's eager inquiries to kneel down by Rollo 's side , trying vainly to staunch the crimson stream that was draining his life away", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Meg was her companion and staunch friend , but it seemed no hardship in Meg 's eyes to counsel Faith to pawn her neat and good clothes , and to receive in exchange garments in which her father would scarcely recognise her", "context_len": 42}}}, "introducing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` How is Westenra ? '' and then I found myself introducing him and his mother to Lady Thesiger , and Lady Thesiger gave a haughty little bow , and then sat down , with her eyes very bright , to watch events", "context_len": 43}}}, "signora": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "So you can well understand , signora , that I feel awkward in speech , and I pray you to make allowance for my ignorance of the language of courtesy , such as would naturally be expected in a knight , even though belonging to a religious Order", "context_len": 48}}}, "gaiety": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 13, "context": "Yet , full as London was of hopes and fears , of mad gaiety and black despair , the October day was as sweet and still as any day of any autumn ; the late roses blossomed as of old in the gardens of the Strand , and vine-leaves wreathed the citizens ' with their wonted coronals of ruby and gold", "context_len": 61}}}, "receptions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "At Rhodes there are state receptions and religious pageants , but a meeting such as this , is , of course , impossible in a convent ; and since I was eleven years old I think I have only once spoken to a woman", "context_len": 44}}}, "pageants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "At Rhodes there are state receptions and religious pageants , but a meeting such as this , is , of course , impossible in a convent ; and since I was eleven years old I think I have only once spoken to a woman", "context_len": 44}}}, "proficient": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Those who are most proficient in turning phrases are not often among those foremost in battle , or wisest in council , and I can tell you that we women value deeds far higher than words", "context_len": 36}}}, "recount": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "Twice that day had Gervaise to recount his adventures , the first time to Sir John Kendall and the knights of his auberge , the second to the grand master", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Burton boldly amplifies , `` the folk recount her manifestations in many cases of difficulty", "context_len": 16}}}, "thinness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But , bad as the thinness was , it was nothing to the dirt", "context_len": 14}}}, "perfumes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "At the foot of the throne was the coffin , made , it is said , of solid gold , and containing , besides the body , a large quantity of the most costly spices and aromatic perfumes , which filled the air with their odor", "context_len": 46}}}, "abbreviate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He was interrupted several times when he attempted to abbreviate the story , or to omit some of the details , and there were exclamations of surprise at his proposal to personate a Turkish prisoner , and to share the lot of the slaves in their prison , and on the benches of the galley", "context_len": 55}}}, "personate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "He was interrupted several times when he attempted to abbreviate the story , or to omit some of the details , and there were exclamations of surprise at his proposal to personate a Turkish prisoner , and to share the lot of the slaves in their prison , and on the benches of the galley", "context_len": 55}}}, "jot": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "But accidents , he had come to learn , were almost daily occurrences in the jungle , and he did not abate a jot of his vigilance , his keen eyes keeping on the lookout everywhere -- at the water for snags or alligators , at either shore for animal or human enemies , on the trees that overhung the stream for lurking anacondas", "context_len": 64}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Some friends who knew her best in India have been requested to jot down their recollections , and have most kindly responded", "context_len": 22}}}, "errant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Why , Sir Gervaise , it seems to me that you have been born two centuries too late , and that you should have been a knight errant , instead of being sworn to obey orders , and bound to celibacy", "context_len": 41}}}, "gage": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 88, "context": "Knowing that I was bound hither , a certain very beautiful young lady of noble family , to whom I had the honour of being introduced , prayed me that if you should by any chance have escaped from captivity -- and she said that she was convinced that you would , when you heard that Rhodes was threatened , assuredly endeavour to escape and to come hither to take a share in the defence -- I was to tell you that she trusted you still bore her gage , and that she , on her part , had held fast to the promise she made you", "context_len": 107}}}, "considers": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 39, "context": "Dear old mammy , who nursed every one of us , from Ruthven down to myself , and whom mother gave to Margaret as a treasure past all price when Harry was born , -- poor mammy , who considers herself quite as much one of the family as any Stanton , Duncan , or Bradford among us all , -- to talk of dismissing her ! But nothing less would satisfy Aunt Patty ; and Margaret gently claiming the right to correct her own children and govern her own household as she saw fit , and Henry firmly upholding his wife , Aunt Patty departed that very afternoon in a tremendous passion , and has never entered the house since", "context_len": 121}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Bartram ( 1775 ) states that Tuccabatche spoke Muscogulge , and the Census of 1832 considers it the largest town among the Creeks , with three hundred and eighty-six houses", "context_len": 30}}}, "unstained": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "That it should come to this -- that she , Peggy Vaughan , who , whatever might be her faults , had always held such an unstained reputation for honour and truthfulness , should be deemed capable of such a mean and discreditable action seemed too hard to be borne", "context_len": 50}}}, "pearls": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 97, "context": "Moreover , he brought him into a place , which he found full of coffers overflowing with royal raiment , such as ravished the wit , gold-inwoven stuffs , Indian and Chinese , and brocades , and he showed him also many other places , all full of that which beggareth description , till at last he brought him into a stable , wherein were horses whose like is not found with the kings of the world ; and therewithin he showed him a storehouse , full of housings and saddles of price , all broidered with pearls and precious stones and so forth", "context_len": 104}}}, "disapprovingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "More than once on her way to school that first day she looked at the breadths of her scarlet cashmere with a gratified eye ; and catching her at this , Ana Vigil had sighed disapprovingly , saying , `` It is too good for every day -- that dress", "context_len": 50}}}, "deeming": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "I kept the secret from all on board the corsair , and meant to have done the same here , deeming that if none knew that I spoke the language it would greatly aid me should I ever see an opportunity of making my escape ; but , Ben Ibyn , you have behaved so kindly to me that I feel it would not be honourable to keep it a secret from you , and to allow you and the ladies to talk freely before me , thinking that I was altogether ignorant of what you were saying", "context_len": 98}}}, "doublet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 71, "context": "When my turn came I leapt with all the buoyancy of youth on to the lowermost crag , grasping an overhanging tuft of reeds and grass to gain the next step ; but the reeds were as sharp as a razor , and before I realized it the palm of my hand was cut to the bone , and the blood poured in a stream down my arm and over my doublet", "context_len": 72}}}, "dispel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "However , she made an effort to dispel the cloud on her face , and , when Gervaise ceased speaking , said , This has been a somewhat more serious business than I intended , Sir Gervaise", "context_len": 37}}}, "womanhood": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` The wind is certainly chilly , '' she replied , and then , moved by embarrassment , or stirred by the motherly instinct that constitutes more than half the charm of womanhood , she leaned over and tucked the lap-robe about my knees , and then fell back in her place , laughing gleefully , as a child might have laughed", "context_len": 62}}}, "braggart": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "A man may believe himself a Solon , or a Roland ; others may consider him as a fool , or an empty braggart ; and it must be taken that the general opinion of the public is the judgment from which there is no appeal", "context_len": 46}}}, "councillors": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 92, "context": "Three times have you earned special credit ; upon the first occasion , the grand master -- no mean judge of conduct and character -- deemed you worthy of secular knighthood , an honour which has not , in my memory , been bestowed at Rhodes upon any young knight ; on the second , you were promoted to the command of a galley , though never before has such a command been given to any , save knights of long experience ; and now , for the third time , the councillors of one of the greatest of Italian cities are about to do you honour", "context_len": 107}}}, "competent": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "As our councillors , therefore , all competent judges , are unanimous in their opinion that you have deserved the highest honours that Genoa can bestow upon you , it is useless for you to set up your own opinion to the contrary", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 78, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "gains": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 38, "context": "Panting , Stone heard Sage calling the signal , and at the sound of the key number every nerve in his body went taut as a bow-string ; for it was the play by which the most effective gains had been made in the first half Hayden was to go through Clearport \u2019 s right wing with the ball", "context_len": 59}}}, "underestimate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "I will presently narrate to you the whole story ; it will come better from my lips than from those of Sir Gervaise , whose disposition is to wholly underestimate the merit of the action he has performed", "context_len": 38}}}, "overrate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "It is good to be modest , Sir Gervaise , and it is better to underestimate than to overrate one 's own merits , but it is not well to carry the feeling to an extreme", "context_len": 36}}}, "undertakes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "They tell me that he is always in earnest in everything he undertakes", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "To clear his young cousin of suspicion , the older boy undertakes to solve the mystery which deepens when one man disappears and another is found murdered on the golf course", "context_len": 31}}}, "gallantry": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "At Gallipoli he acquitted himself manfully , was mentioned for gallantry in an affair at Quinn 's Post , and was brought back to Alexandria in a hospital ship , with a wound sufficiently dangerous to smash many a man up completely", "context_len": 42}}}, "misunderstand": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "He went on to say that such a thing had occurred upon the previous afternoon , and that through undue haste on his own part , which he now regretted , he had been led to misunderstand the situation and condemn the wrong person", "context_len": 44}}}, "donor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "I do not think that many knights , however true they might be to the donor , would have suffered months of slavery in order to regain a token , lost by no fault or carelessness of their own ; and no lady could have blamed or held them in any way dishonoured by the loss", "context_len": 56}}}, "suitor": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 72, "context": "The folk and the people of the city marvelled at this strange thing , especially when they saw the Vizier and his son go forth the palace in a pitiable plight for stress of chagrin and despite , and they fell to asking , `` What hath happened and why is the marriage avoided and the rejoicings broken off ? '' But none knew what was to do save Alaeddin , the suitor , [ 419 ] who laughed in his sleeve", "context_len": 82}}}, "heiresses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Yes , I know , Agatha , that the child is one of the richest heiresses in Italy , but for that very reason it needs not that her husband should have wide possessions", "context_len": 34}}}, "possessions": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 73, "context": "Withal , O my son , of regard for thy wish , needs must I take courage and go ; but , O my son , if the King receive me and honour me for the gift 's sake and I seek of him that which thou wilt in [ 343 ] the matter of marrying his daughter and he ask me , after the wont of the folk , what are thy possessions and thy revenues , what shall I say to him ? And most like , O my son , he will ask me of this ere he ask me of thyself", "context_len": 105}}}, "amenable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "At any rate , Agatha , as I said before , he may never cross her path again , and you may , a year or two hence , find her perfectly amenable to your wishes", "context_len": 36}}}, "princely": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "Here , in the name of the Republic , Battista Fragoso announced to him that , by the unanimous decision of the council , he had been elected a noble of Genoa ; an honour , he added , on only one or two previous occasions in the history of the Republic bestowed upon any but of princely rank , but which he had nobly earned by the great service he had rendered to the State", "context_len": 76}}}, "casket": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "Florence sent a casket containing a document bestowing upon him the freedom of the city , and an order upon the treasury for five thousand ducats that had been voted to him by the grand council of the Republic ; while Ferdinand , King of Naples , bestowed on him the grand cross of the Order of St", "context_len": 58}}}, "presentation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Each presentation was accompanied by the plaudits of the assembly , and by the no less warm acclamations of the knights", "context_len": 21}}}, "workmanship": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 9, "context": "The ramparts whereon he was standing were of Roman workmanship , built so solidly that they had resisted every attack of man or of time ; while down below lay the ruins of a magnificent villa , once occupied by the Roman governor of the island", "context_len": 46}}}, "staffs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "When the harbour was in sight , the ships were ordered to proceed in single file , the galley leading the way with a huge banner of the Order floating from her stern , and smaller flags on staffs at each side", "context_len": 42}}}, "piratical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "They are Moors , your Excellency ; they form part of an expedition fitted out by the corsairs of Algiers , Tripoli , Tunis , and other piratical strongholds , for the purpose of destroying the commerce and ravaging the coasts of Western Italy", "context_len": 44}}}, "coasts": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 56, "context": "He did not like to leave it behind him and proceed to the eastward ; for , in case of any reverses happening to him , the Tyrians would be very likely to act decidedly against him , and their power on the Mediterranean would enable them to act very efficiently against him on all the coasts of Greece and Asia Minor", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He coasts along its banks , keeping up stream", "context_len": 9}}}, "liveliest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The grand master , and the knights with him , uttered an exclamation of joy , and , as the three knights named stepped forward , embraced them with the liveliest pleasure", "context_len": 32}}}, "resurrection": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "My dear Caretto , the grand master exclaimed , it is almost a resurrection , for we have all long mourned you as dead ; and your return to us at the present time is indeed fortunate ; for upon whose judgment and aid could I better rely than those of my old comrade in arms ? Then , turning to Gervaise , he went on : It was a daring and brilliant exploit indeed , Sir Gervaise , and in due time honour shall be paid to you and your brave companions , to whom and to you I now tender the thanks of the Order", "context_len": 107}}}, "warnings": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 54, "context": "The colour which his unlucky presence among the congregation at Coleman Street on the day of General Harrisons execution , had given to his enemy 's accusations , had made his position perilous in the extreme , for General Monck , and his other secret friends , considered that he had wilfully disregarded their warnings , and were not inclined to exert their influence in his behalf", "context_len": 67}}}, "operations": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 15, "context": "As soon as the battery was completed , gangs of men , accustomed to mining operations , set to work in its rear to drive sloping passages downwards , opening into the face of the great cutting , and through these vast quantities of earth and stones were poured , so as to afford a passage across it , the depth being largely diminished by the great pile of rubbish that had already fallen from the breached wall", "context_len": 78}}}, "distributing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 70, "context": "On the return of the knight with the news that it would be at least a fortnight before Genoa could fit out any galleys , and fearing that the pirates might at any moment put to sea , we procured some small Sardinian craft , and fitted them as fire ships ; with the captives we had rescued , and some Sard fishermen , we manned the three prizes , distributing the knights between them , and at night launched the fire ships against the corsairs , whose ships were crowded together", "context_len": 92}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "He spent a busy time in the Crimea , doing plenty of hard work in the trenches ; and when off duty engaged in hospital visiting , tract and book distributing , attending prayer meetings and mission services , constant in his Bible reading , and always endeavouring to do good to others", "context_len": 53}}}, "entrust": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "There were some of you who deemed it rash to entrust a galley to so young a commander and so youthful a crew", "context_len": 23}}}, "devising": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The plan was wholly of his own devising , its execution solely due to his arrangement of the details , and that without the slightest suggestion on the part of myself or my comrades", "context_len": 34}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "Harrison asked no question , she did not even answer his words ; for a moment she doubted if she had heard him ; but then she spoke : spoke with a calmness that startled him till he realized that she dreamt even yet that her husband might escape , and was too completely absorbed in devising schemes for his deliverance to have time to realize her own misery or measure her own powerlessness", "context_len": 74}}}, "narrate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "Ben could not doubt the honest candor of his companion , but he shrunk from unbosoming himself , dreading to narrate the unpleasant story of the events which had made both Bern Hayden and his father his uncompromising enemies and had forced him to flee like a criminal from his native village in order to escape being sent to the State Reformatory", "context_len": 62}}}, "flagstaffs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Within a few minutes after the return of the grand master and his party ashore , the flags of the Order were run up to the flagstaffs of every fort and bastion : the bells of the churches chimed out a triumphant peal , and a salute was fired from the guns of the three water forts , while along the wall facing the port , the townspeople waved numberless gay flags as a welcome to the galley", "context_len": 78}}}, "peal": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 42, "context": "Within a few minutes after the return of the grand master and his party ashore , the flags of the Order were run up to the flagstaffs of every fort and bastion : the bells of the churches chimed out a triumphant peal , and a salute was fired from the guns of the three water forts , while along the wall facing the port , the townspeople waved numberless gay flags as a welcome to the galley", "context_len": 78}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "The sad procession passed through the streets , the people flocked into the church , and in the presence of all the `` wise men '' of London , they solemnly committed the frail tabernacle in which the living spirit had sinned and suffered to the parent earth , where the rush and roar of a mighty city should ever peal around it", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then he too burst into a jolly peal", "context_len": 8}}}, "townspeople": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 67, "context": "Within a few minutes after the return of the grand master and his party ashore , the flags of the Order were run up to the flagstaffs of every fort and bastion : the bells of the churches chimed out a triumphant peal , and a salute was fired from the guns of the three water forts , while along the wall facing the port , the townspeople waved numberless gay flags as a welcome to the galley", "context_len": 78}}}, "numberless": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 69, "context": "Within a few minutes after the return of the grand master and his party ashore , the flags of the Order were run up to the flagstaffs of every fort and bastion : the bells of the churches chimed out a triumphant peal , and a salute was fired from the guns of the three water forts , while along the wall facing the port , the townspeople waved numberless gay flags as a welcome to the galley", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "At length , however , the Macedonians , in their march , came upon the brow of a range of hills , from which they looked down upon numberless and endless lines of infantry and cavalry , and ranges after ranges of tents , which filled the plain", "context_len": 48}}}, "chargeable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "You must let me know how much you paid for the vessels you used as fire ships , and to the Sards ; this is an expense chargeable to the general service", "context_len": 32}}}, "erecting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "The admiral tells me that never before has a galley returned with the slaves in such good health and condition , and that unquestionably your plan of erecting an awning to shelter them from the midday heat and the night dews has had a most beneficial effect on their health ; he has recommended its general adoption", "context_len": 57}}}, "dews": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "The admiral tells me that never before has a galley returned with the slaves in such good health and condition , and that unquestionably your plan of erecting an awning to shelter them from the midday heat and the night dews has had a most beneficial effect on their health ; he has recommended its general adoption", "context_len": 57}}}, "adoption": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 56, "context": "The admiral tells me that never before has a galley returned with the slaves in such good health and condition , and that unquestionably your plan of erecting an awning to shelter them from the midday heat and the night dews has had a most beneficial effect on their health ; he has recommended its general adoption", "context_len": 57}}}, "cruised": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "For some weeks she cruised backwards and forwards along the coast and among the islands", "context_len": 15}}}, "unavailing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "Again we tacked , but our efforts to find deeper water were unavailing , and at last the galliot ran aground with a slight shock on a bed of soft sand", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "In vain they attempted to launch it down the beach ; their united efforts were unavailing", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "And he persisted in his unavailing regret", "context_len": 7}}}, "abated": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 5, "context": "Although the gale had entirely abated , there was still a brisk wind blowing , and it was evident to the captain of the corsair that under such circumstances he could outsail the galley that had long been searching for him ; when , therefore , the Santa Barbara came in sight , just as he and his crew had finished stripping the wreck of its contents , the idea had occurred to him to attempt to entice some of the knights to land", "context_len": 84}}}, "scimitars": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "There were but five or six men to be seen on the deck of the wreck , and these had , as the boat approached , come down to the rocks as if to meet those who came to their aid ; but as the knights leapt out , they threw themselves suddenly upon them with knives and scimitars that had hitherto been concealed beneath their garments , while at the same moment a crowd of men appeared on the deck of the ship , and , leaping down , ran forward with drawn swords", "context_len": 95}}}, "sheath": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "He snatched his dagger from its sheath , and struck down one assailant ; but ere he could raise it to strike again , another leapt on to his back , and clung there until the rest rushed up , when he shouted , Take him alive ! take him alive ! and , throwing down their weapons , half a dozen of the pirates flung themselves upon Gervaise , and strove to pull him to the ground , until at last , in spite of his desperate resistance , they succeeded in doing so", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Some of the others now drew their weapons , but the captain shouted to them to sheath them", "context_len": 18}}}, "outsail": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Although the gale had entirely abated , there was still a brisk wind blowing , and it was evident to the captain of the corsair that under such circumstances he could outsail the galley that had long been searching for him ; when , therefore , the Santa Barbara came in sight , just as he and his crew had finished stripping the wreck of its contents , the idea had occurred to him to attempt to entice some of the knights to land", "context_len": 84}}}, "stripping": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 61, "context": "Although the gale had entirely abated , there was still a brisk wind blowing , and it was evident to the captain of the corsair that under such circumstances he could outsail the galley that had long been searching for him ; when , therefore , the Santa Barbara came in sight , just as he and his crew had finished stripping the wreck of its contents , the idea had occurred to him to attempt to entice some of the knights to land", "context_len": 84}}}, "entice": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 77, "context": "Although the gale had entirely abated , there was still a brisk wind blowing , and it was evident to the captain of the corsair that under such circumstances he could outsail the galley that had long been searching for him ; when , therefore , the Santa Barbara came in sight , just as he and his crew had finished stripping the wreck of its contents , the idea had occurred to him to attempt to entice some of the knights to land", "context_len": 84}}}, "furled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "While some brought the things into the house , others furled the vessel \u2019 s sails , and carried out an anchor astern , to hold the vessel when she should float , as it was half ebb when they ran her on", "context_len": 43}}}, "portable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "While we waited in suspense , we saw a fierce light flash forth from the hall , and perceived that , having plundered it of all that was portable , they had fired it in many places at once ; and while we looked , we saw our own once happy home share the same fate , and emulate the hall in sending forth its volume of ruddy flame towards the skies", "context_len": 72}}}, "gourd": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A gourd full of water was placed to Gervaise 's lips by one of the men of a kinder disposition than the rest", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Although the origin from the earth is certainly the most natural that could suggest itself to primitive man , there are a number of nations claiming provenience from the sky ( the Tukabatchi were let down from the sky in a gourd or calabash ) : from the sun ( Yuchi ) , from the moon , from the sea , from the ashes of fire ( Sh\u00e1wano ) , from eggs ( Quichhua ) or certain plants", "context_len": 78}}}, "parched": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "With the latter he did as he had done the past night , to wit , he took him and couched him in the draught-house , where he deft him parched for excess of fright and dismay ; whilst Alaeddin arose and placing the sword between himself and the Lady Bedrulbudour , lay down and slept till the morning , when the genie appeared and restored the twain to their place , leaving Alaeddin full of joy at [ the discomfiture of ] the Vizier 's son", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 100, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}}}, "tightness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "He drank it thankfully , for he was parched with thirst excited by the pain caused by the tightness with which he had been bound", "context_len": 25}}}, "pendant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "What have you got here that you are ready to risk your life for ? He looked at the little chain and its pendant with an air of disappointment", "context_len": 29}}}, "sultans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The pashas here , and the sultans of the Moors , are all alike ; if they once meddle in an affair they take all the profit , and think they do well by giving you a tithe of it", "context_len": 40}}}, "tithe": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Now Alden Payne would not have had a tithe of the sense with which we have credited him all along , if he had forgotten for one moment the peril which he faced from the moment he came up with the inanimate form of Dick Lightfoot", "context_len": 46}}}, "smokes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 86, "context": "I know a dozen rich Moors in Tripoli or Tunis who would be glad to have him ; and we agreed that we would run down to the African coast for awhile , for that galley has been altogether too busy of late for our comfort , and will be all the more active after this little affair ; besides , people in these islands have got so scared that one ca n't get within ten miles of any of them now without seeing their signal smokes rising on the hills , and finding , when they land , the villages deserted and stripped of everything worth carrying away", "context_len": 109}}}, "scuttle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was pitch dark below , although the scuttle had been left open in order to allow a certain amount of air to reach the captives ; Gervaise , therefore , felt his way about cautiously , and lay down as soon as he found a clear space", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then the king shouted something to his women-folk which caused them to scuttle back into the palace like so many rabbits ; and the next moment the two guards who had me in charge marched me off to an empty hut behind the palace -- which was , luckily , to windward of the crucifixion tree , the odour from which therefore did not reach as far as my lodging -- and , having signed to me to enter , mounted guard , one on each side of the door", "context_len": 90}}}, "closeness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Several of their intimate friends and scores of mere acquaintances had made the great sacrifice , but for the first time Malcolm realized the closeness of the Great War", "context_len": 29}}}, "poignant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The majority of the captives had been some time in their floating prison , and their first poignant grief had settled down into a dull and despairing acceptance of their fate ; the sailors , newly captured , had for hours raved and cursed , but , worn out by their struggle with the elements , and their rage and grief , they had now fallen asleep", "context_len": 67}}}, "acceptance": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "The majority of the captives had been some time in their floating prison , and their first poignant grief had settled down into a dull and despairing acceptance of their fate ; the sailors , newly captured , had for hours raved and cursed , but , worn out by their struggle with the elements , and their rage and grief , they had now fallen asleep", "context_len": 67}}}, "raved": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "The majority of the captives had been some time in their floating prison , and their first poignant grief had settled down into a dull and despairing acceptance of their fate ; the sailors , newly captured , had for hours raved and cursed , but , worn out by their struggle with the elements , and their rage and grief , they had now fallen asleep", "context_len": 67}}}, "inability": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "She was frequently in and out , as often as three or four times a day , and very seldom indeed less than twice ; moreover , she seemed exceedingly anxious to become my instructress in her own language , and as I had already felt heavily handicapped on several occasions by my inability to converse freely with those around me I made no demur , although I must confess that I at length began to view with vague disquietude the extreme freedom of intercourse thus instituted by the young woman", "context_len": 91}}}, "snare": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 57, "context": "It was not until Hassan had stepped down into the boat that I knew he was going to take any one with him ; then he suddenly told two of his men to take their places by him , saying to me , as he did so , ' I know not whether this message is a snare ; but mind , if I see any signs of treachery , your life at any rate will pay the forfeit", "context_len": 79}}}, "variance": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 100, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "decreed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Therefore , it is but right now Allah has decreed that you in turn should be a slave to the true believers , that you should receive the same mercy you gave to Moslems when they were in your power", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "They went for a walk in the afternoon , did some shooting at targets , for Bart decreed that the game must have a holiday as well as the hunters , and at night , inside the snug tent , with the fire blazing brightly in the stove , and the cold wind blowing outside , they spent a jolly evening , singing songs and telling stories", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "But Bomba will come back , if it is so decreed , and then he will talk and feast with Polulu again", "context_len": 22}}}, "ex": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He called up one of the ex slaves who was a native of Tripoli , and who had now taken his place as a member of the crew , and asked him a number of questions", "context_len": 36}}}, "deter": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "As soon as he perceived that the captain entertained the idea of returning to engage the galley , the man felt that if he were to avoid a return into captivity he must deter him from taking such a step", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Would his experiences and narrow escape deter him from further enterprises or merely whet his appetite for other surprising adventures ?", "context_len": 21}}}, "alleviate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "He had early taken the opportunity of saying to one of the released galley slaves , I pray you , if you have any feeling of kindness towards me for the efforts I made to alleviate your condition , say no word of my knowledge of Turkish , and ask the others also to remain silent on this point", "context_len": 59}}}, "irritation": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 54, "context": "The truth was , that Alexander , though he had the sagacity to see that he was placed in circumstances where prodigious moral effect in strengthening his position would be produced by an act of great severity , was swayed by so many generous impulses , which raised him above the ordinary excitements of irritation and revenge , that he had every desire to make the suffering as light , and to limit it by as narrow bounds , as the nature of the case would allow", "context_len": 87}}}, "brutally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "I did not tell you , wife , he went on , turning to her , that the reason why he bearded Hassan today was because the corsair brutally struck a little female captive ; thus , you see , he , at the risk of his life , and when himself a captive , carried out his vows to protect the defenceless", "context_len": 63}}}, "restraining": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 24, "context": "The natives were friendly to the whites and fond of visiting them ; the old chiefs were orderly men , desirous and active in restraining the young `` braves '' from the licentiousness which they had contracted through their intercourse with the scum of the white colonists", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Fenn darted forward , and would have taken after the man on the run , only Bart put forth a restraining hand", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "So I laid a restraining hand on the negro and bade him drop the almost lifeless body", "context_len": 17}}}, "bind": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 8, "context": "Ashton ; then continued , `` we all bind ourselves , do we not , to do as Bessie wishes and to keep this little transaction a secret among ourselves , making no mention to Lena Neville that the prize was not awarded to her in the first instance ? ''", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Bind his hands and we will go", "context_len": 7}}}, "disfiguring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Even in his passion the pirate had avoided striking him on the head , as a disfiguring mark on the face would diminish his value", "context_len": 25}}}, "diminish": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "But , in carrying this terrible decision into effect , he acted in a manner so deliberate , discriminating , and cautious , as to diminish very much the irritation and resentment which it would otherwise have caused , and to give it its full moral effect as a measure , not of angry resentment , but of calm and deliberate retribution -- just and proper , according to the ideas of the time", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "He could not imagine whatever had suddenly brought it about , after all the neglect of the summer ; but that did not in the least diminish his delight", "context_len": 29}}}, "mitigated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Well , I thank heaven , '' I exclaimed , with some feeling , `` that I was never at the mercy of more than one woman , and that fact was mitigated somewhat", "context_len": 35}}}, "treasured": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "There seemed little doubt about it , for the egg really looked quite different to the others ; so the treasured find was safely put away in the small box , to be shown to Joe , who was wise in such lore , though he only knew the birds by their country names , and had never heard of such a science as ornithology", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "It contained these words -- My precious Charlotte , you have been such a comfort to me ! \u2019 No wonder the loving utterance was treasured up by the daughter through the rest of her life", "context_len": 36}}}, "stiffening": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "After a time he got up and walked stiffly and painfully up and down the deck , knowing that this was the best plan to prevent the limbs from stiffening", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The dog dropped to the ground and lay still , its muscles twitching and its limbs stiffening ; for that final blow had broken its neck", "context_len": 26}}}, "prodigious": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 21, "context": "The truth was , that Alexander , though he had the sagacity to see that he was placed in circumstances where prodigious moral effect in strengthening his position would be produced by an act of great severity , was swayed by so many generous impulses , which raised him above the ordinary excitements of irritation and revenge , that he had every desire to make the suffering as light , and to limit it by as narrow bounds , as the nature of the case would allow", "context_len": 87}}}, "regulation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "I should ask twice as much , the captain replied , if it were not for the regulation that one slave from each cargo brought in belongs to the sultan , and his officers would as a matter of course choose this fellow , for the others are merely such as are sold in the market every day", "context_len": 58}}}, "bearable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "We have borne toil , and hardship , and blows , the heat of the sun by day , and the damp by night , but we would humbly represent to you that since we were placed in the galley commanded by this knight our lot has been made bearable by his humanity and kindness", "context_len": 55}}}, "humane": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "While the chase lasted they had , so I afterwards learned , suspended their prisoners over the stern , thus preventing our humane captain from opening fire ; but , now the chase was at an end , there was no further use for the doomed men", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "As this seemed the most humane suggestion , Master Dormouse was tucked up in bed once more , and , still protesting , was carried to a sunny bank under an apple-tree , and stowed away under a protecting clump of leaves , where his plaintive voice gradually subsided , and he settled down for five months of oblivion , to ignore the winter frosts and storms until the April sunshine should tempt him out of his lair", "context_len": 78}}}, "brutality": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "His hair was a deep grizzled red , as also his beard , and his eyes were of the same tinge , his nose somewhat aquiline , and his whole features , weatherworn as they were , were those of one born to command , while they lacked the sheer brutality of expression so conspicuous in some of his subordinates", "context_len": 60}}}, "ointment": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "If any of his wounds need ointment , see that he has it", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "When maidens of different antecedents , rank , age , temperament , and -- in minor matters -- opinions , are brought together in closest proximity , in a climate which tries the temper , there is at least a possibility of some slight rubs , which without the soothing ointment brought by the Peacemaker may even develop into sores", "context_len": 60}}}, "baggy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "When he had dressed them , Muley went out and returned with some Turkish garments , consisting of a pair of baggy trousers of yellow cotton , a white shirt of the same material , and a sleeveless jacket of blue cloth embroidered with yellow trimming ; a pair of yellow slippers completed the costume", "context_len": 55}}}, "veiled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "They were slightly veiled ; but , as Gervaise afterwards learnt , Ben Ibyn was not a Moor , but a Berber , a people who do not keep their women in close confinement as do the Moors , but allow them to go abroad freely without being entirely muffled up", "context_len": 51}}}, "expectations": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 6, "context": "Contrary to the New Zealanders ' expectations , the barge , beyond stopping to land the guard , did not tie up for the night within the limits of the town ; but , maintaining a two-miles-an-hour pace , held on until the lengthening shadows announced the close of another day", "context_len": 51}}}, "chivalrous": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "The Outram of the book is just the Outram who was the admiration of our girlhood , -- generous , chivalrous , noble ! One feels how much pain that fine spirit would have been saved , had he realised how little it really matters whether good service be appreciated or not by man , if the great Leader accept it , -- if all be done as to Him Who never overlooks or misunderstands ! To our own Master we stand or fall ; let earthly superiors say what they will", "context_len": 92}}}, "batch": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 19, "context": "As you are aware , the sultan has the choice of any one slave he may select from each batch brought in , and assuredly he would choose this one , did it come to his ears , or to the ears of one of his officers , that a Christian knight had been landed", "context_len": 55}}}, "wildfire": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Only do not for the present let any of your friends see him ; they would spread the news like wildfire , and in a week every soul in the town would know that you had a good looking Christian slave , and the sultan 's officer would be sending for me to ask how I obtained him", "context_len": 58}}}, "turban": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "Gervaise thought the plan an excellent one ; and he was soon transformed , Muley shaving that part of the hair that would have shown below the turban , and then staining him a deep brownish black , from the waist upwards , together with his feet and his legs up to his knee , and darkening his eyebrows , eyelashes , and moustache", "context_len": 64}}}, "politic": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "He had intended to conceal his knowledge , Ben Ibyn went on , which would have been politic ; but when he found that my intentions were kind , he told us that he knew our tongue , and now revealed his knowledge , as he thought it would be dishonourable to listen to our talk , leaving us under the impression that he could not understand us", "context_len": 68}}}, "shaving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Gervaise thought the plan an excellent one ; and he was soon transformed , Muley shaving that part of the hair that would have shown below the turban , and then staining him a deep brownish black , from the waist upwards , together with his feet and his legs up to his knee , and darkening his eyebrows , eyelashes , and moustache", "context_len": 64}}}, "brownish": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Gervaise thought the plan an excellent one ; and he was soon transformed , Muley shaving that part of the hair that would have shown below the turban , and then staining him a deep brownish black , from the waist upwards , together with his feet and his legs up to his knee , and darkening his eyebrows , eyelashes , and moustache", "context_len": 64}}}, "eyelashes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "Jones was laughing so ! He was not laughing out loud now , but he was shaking all over , and when Maggie peeped at him from under her eyelashes , he twinkled his eyes at her , as much as to say , `` Now , what do you think of her ? '' Right glad was she when Harry called her to take her turn at the swing , and she could run away out of sight of Mr", "context_len": 81}}}, "thickness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "Save that your lips lack the thickness , and your nose is straighter than those of Nubians , no one would doubt but that you were one of that race ; and this is of little consequence , as many of them are of mixed blood , and , though retaining their dark colour , have features that in their outline resemble those of the Arabs", "context_len": 66}}}, "retaining": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 50, "context": "Save that your lips lack the thickness , and your nose is straighter than those of Nubians , no one would doubt but that you were one of that race ; and this is of little consequence , as many of them are of mixed blood , and , though retaining their dark colour , have features that in their outline resemble those of the Arabs", "context_len": 66}}}, "fanatical": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "Now , you know the fellow is quite able to forge or trump up evidence enough to be mighty unpleasant , so Lord Monck prays you give no colour to anything he may say , by frequenting the company of any suspicious or fanatical people", "context_len": 45}}}, "nubian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Nubian slaves were no uncommon sight in the town , and in wealthy Moorish families were commonly employed in places of trust , and especially as attendants in the harems", "context_len": 30}}}, "harems": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Nubian slaves were no uncommon sight in the town , and in wealthy Moorish families were commonly employed in places of trust , and especially as attendants in the harems", "context_len": 30}}}, "faiths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "There is no reason why , because our faiths are different , we should be constantly fighting", "context_len": 17}}}, "nay": {"freq": 66, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 107, "context": "of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very strongly influence him", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 68, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "boaster": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Ah ! he exclaimed suddenly , the thought has just occurred to me of a way by which you can obtain confirmation of my story ; and , as I value your good opinion and would not be regarded as a boaster and a liar , I entreat you to take it", "context_len": 52}}}, "beseech": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 64, "context": "Alaeddin [ 329 ] waited till his mother had made an end of her speech and said to her , `` O my mother , all that thou thinkest I know ; marry , I know full well that I am the son of poor folk , nor may all this thy talk anywise avail to move me from my purpose ; but I beseech thee , an I be thy very son and thou love me , do me this kindness ; else wilt thou lose me , for death hasteneth upon me , an I attain not my wish of the beloved of my heart", "context_len": 107}}}, "communicated": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 22, "context": "I had hoped , he said , that the corsair would have taken me to Syria , for there I could have communicated with Suleiman , who would , I am sure , have given me such shelter and aid as he was able , in the event of my making my escape from slavery and finding myself unable to leave by sea", "context_len": 63}}}, "unsuspected": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 80, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "You have rendered us a great service indeed by your vigilance , and showed great prudence by allowing the Turk to believe that he had accomplished his mission unsuspected", "context_len": 29}}}, "betrayal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "He had no doubt that his betrayal was due to one of the gardeners , who had several times shown him signs of ill will , being doubtless jealous of the immunity he enjoyed from hard labour , and who must , he thought , have crept up and overheard some conversation ; but in that case it was singular that the fact of his knowledge of Turkish had not been mentioned", "context_len": 72}}}, "immunity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "He had no doubt that his betrayal was due to one of the gardeners , who had several times shown him signs of ill will , being doubtless jealous of the immunity he enjoyed from hard labour , and who must , he thought , have crept up and overheard some conversation ; but in that case it was singular that the fact of his knowledge of Turkish had not been mentioned", "context_len": 72}}}, "regulations": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 69, "context": "The Christmas holidays being over , and Archie in the rudest of health , Miss Forster had no more excuse for keeping that young gentleman at home , and after much packing and preparation , he departed to school , amid the fond regrets of his aunt and the lamentations of his friends at the Abbey , to whom he promised to write as often as time and the regulations of his house would allow him", "context_len": 76}}}, "masons": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "This was the work to which the gang was set that morning , and it was not long before the vigour with which Gervaise worked , and the strength he displayed in moving the heavy stones , attracted the attention of the overseers and of the head of the masons", "context_len": 50}}}, "smuggled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "mason": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 24, "context": "When I went to him again today , he said that he had learnt from the governor of the prison and from the head mason that you were almost beyond price , that you had been raised to the position of superintendent of the slaves employed in the building of his palace , and that you were a man of such skill that he would not part with you at any price until the work was finished", "context_len": 77}}}, "lever": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 57, "context": "After much consideration as to what had best be done , he took the anchor rope , which was some sixty yards in length , fastened one end to each end of the spar , and then lashed the middle of the rope to the bow of the felucca ; then , using an oar as a lever , he with great labour managed to launch the spar over the bow , with the sail still attached to it", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "( prenez la peine de vous lever et de m'apporter , etc", "context_len": 12}}}, "masonry": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 51, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "transporting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "So far from there being any falling off in the work , the head mason found that , even though the walls began to rise and the labour of transporting the stones into their positions became greater , the masons were never kept standing", "context_len": 44}}}, "heaviest": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "As the heaviest part of his work at headquarters was over , and as pretty much everything had depended on the reply to General Forrest 's requisition on his superior officer -- who , unfortunately , chanced to be General Bragg -- for arms and ammunition , Harry had no difficulty in securing leave of absence for the day ; and so , when all the arrangements had been made , we set out the next evening for the house where Jack Bledsoe lay", "context_len": 84}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "These sticks measure from a foot and a half to three feet and a half in length , the fighting and hunting ones being the largest and heaviest", "context_len": 28}}}, "appliances": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Gervaise did not content himself with giving orders , but worked at the heaviest jobs , and , little by little , introduced many of the appliances used by the skilled masons of Rhodes in transporting and lifting heavy stones", "context_len": 40}}}, "arcade": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Gradually his own position improved : he was treated as an overseer , and was permitted to sleep under an arcade that ran along one side of the yard , instead of being confined in the close and stifling cell", "context_len": 40}}}, "maliciously": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I must tell you I have kept a good conscience towards God , neither did I act maliciously toward any person , but as I judged them to be enemies to God and his people", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Penelly says as you 're the biggest coward as ever stepped , '' said another maliciously", "context_len": 16}}}, "amulet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "I told you that some day I would kill you ; but even now I am willing to forgive you and to allow you to depart in peace , if you will restore the amulet you took from me", "context_len": 39}}}, "defray": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "He tells me that he has heard from Ben Ibyn , the Berber , that you are a slave , and has asked me to inquire of the sultan the price that he will take for your ransom , expressing his willingness to pay whatever may be demanded , and charging me to defray the sum and to make arrangements by which you may return to Europe", "context_len": 67}}}, "threefold": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "To lower away with a practised crew manning the falls would entail a certain amount of risk should the boat surge against the ship 's side ; with a crowd of raw amateurs the danger was magnified threefold", "context_len": 38}}}, "secondly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "In the first place , I am well treated here , and my position is not an unpleasant one ; secondly , the sum required for ransom is altogether preposterous ; thirdly , I am not without hopes that I may some day find other means of freeing myself without so great a sacrifice ; and lastly , that I have a reason which I can not mention , why , at present , I would not quit Tripoli , even were I free tomorrow", "context_len": 85}}}, "meditating": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "But the beauty of it was , she continued , after meditating a moment over the Queen 's answer , that little Isabel was really a darling , and that the King called her 'his dear little sister , ' and really loved her ; because sometimes kings and queens do not love each other at all", "context_len": 57}}}, "precautions": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 47, "context": "Our wants for the next few hours were consequently supplied , we had therefore no need to do anything further than just to wait for nightfall and then resume our voyage ; which we did , passing the intervening time in chatting together and discussing the various precautions which we must take in order to elude our pursuers , who , by the way , were now several miles ahead of us", "context_len": 72}}}, "approximating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Gervaise had for some time taken to staining his face , hands , and legs with walnut juice , beginning with a weak solution , and very gradually increasing the strength until he had reached a shade approximating to that of the lighter coloured portion of the population", "context_len": 48}}}, "hurl": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 96, "context": "As he stepped through the gateway one of the pirates sprang past him , but as he did so , a figure leapt out from beyond the wall , and plunged a dagger into his back , while at the same moment , by cutting down another pirate , Gervaise rid himself of one of his assailants in front ; but as he did so , he himself received a severe wound on the left shoulder from Hassan , who , before he could again raise his weapon , sprang upon him , and tried to hurl him to the ground", "context_len": 101}}}, "girt": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "Can he speak thus who hath so long espoused The Royal cause , and served that cause so well ? Who , girt with honours , well deserved , hath stood One in a noble Brotherhood of Fame ! Where are the Cavaliers who fought with thee In battle , side by side , who with thee shared The feast , and drained the wine-cup to your King ? Where are they now ? what , gone ? not one remains , T \u2019 assert thy innocence , or shield thee from An ignominious death", "context_len": 95}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then the tension broke , and the nervous force that had girt him about when he was on the gridiron gave way , and he sobbed brokenly", "context_len": 27}}}, "wares": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "consignment": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "I might tell him that my employer -- and I would mention some merchant whose name would carry weight with him -- has received from the interior a large consignment of slaves , among whom are three or four girls , who would fetch high prices in Egypt , and as he believes they have been captured from a tribe within the limits of the sultan 's territory , he is anxious to get rid of them , and will either dispose of them all cheaply in a lot , or will hand them over to him to take to Egypt to sell , giving him a large commission for carrying them there and disposing of them", "context_len": 117}}}, "territory": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 50, "word_index": 67, "context": "I might tell him that my employer -- and I would mention some merchant whose name would carry weight with him -- has received from the interior a large consignment of slaves , among whom are three or four girls , who would fetch high prices in Egypt , and as he believes they have been captured from a tribe within the limits of the sultan 's territory , he is anxious to get rid of them , and will either dispose of them all cheaply in a lot , or will hand them over to him to take to Egypt to sell , giving him a large commission for carrying them there and disposing of them", "context_len": 117}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "In the southern part of the Cha'hta territory several tribes , represented to be of Cha'hta lineage , appear as distinct from the main body , and are always mentioned separately", "context_len": 31}}}, "disposing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 114, "context": "I might tell him that my employer -- and I would mention some merchant whose name would carry weight with him -- has received from the interior a large consignment of slaves , among whom are three or four girls , who would fetch high prices in Egypt , and as he believes they have been captured from a tribe within the limits of the sultan 's territory , he is anxious to get rid of them , and will either dispose of them all cheaply in a lot , or will hand them over to him to take to Egypt to sell , giving him a large commission for carrying them there and disposing of them", "context_len": 117}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "That invention fell into the hands of Asher Rand , who , on learning its value , was sorely tempted and kept its existence a secret , finally disposing of it to a concern that pays a royalty upon it of three thousand dollars yearly", "context_len": 45}}}, "huts": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 61, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Out of the lines of tin huts the two thousand five hundred men comprising the draft poured forth like bees", "context_len": 20}}}, "singled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "Bern Hayden likewise practiced well , putting all his usual snap and dash into everything he did , his accomplishments plainly demonstrating why he had been generally singled out as the fellow who would certainly be chosen as captain of next year \u2019 s team", "context_len": 45}}}, "sheik": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There is my master the sheik of the Beni Kalis awaiting you", "context_len": 12}}}, "unsuspectingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Hassan entered it unsuspectingly , followed by his two men", "context_len": 10}}}, "gateway": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 5, "context": "As he stepped through the gateway one of the pirates sprang past him , but as he did so , a figure leapt out from beyond the wall , and plunged a dagger into his back , while at the same moment , by cutting down another pirate , Gervaise rid himself of one of his assailants in front ; but as he did so , he himself received a severe wound on the left shoulder from Hassan , who , before he could again raise his weapon , sprang upon him , and tried to hurl him to the ground", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Where the Laramie River rushes eastward through the mountain range , they turned in the opposite direction toward the famous South Pass , that wide gateway through the great Rocky Mountains", "context_len": 31}}}, "girdle": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 26, "context": "Withdrawing behind the line , the grand master sat down on a fragment of stone , and , drawing a tablet from a pouch in his girdle , he wrote on it , In all things carry out the instructions of Sir Gervaise Tresham : he is acting by my orders and authority , and has full power in all respects", "context_len": 61}}}, "pinning": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "Gervaise , who had half risen to his knees , was unable to avoid the blow , but threw himself forward , his weight partly pinning the corsair 's shoulders to the ground , and the blow passed behind him , inflicting but a slight wound in the back ; then , with his right hand , which was now free , he grasped Hassan by the throat with a grip of iron", "context_len": 73}}}, "convulsively": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 64, "context": "Never in my life had I felt more deadly cool and self-possessed than I did then as I aimed steadily at the animal 's right eye ; I felt that I could not miss ; nor did I ; for while we thus stood motionlessly staring at each other , I released the string , and the next instant the great lithe beast sprang convulsively into the air , with the butt of my arrow protruding from his eye and the point buried deep in his brain", "context_len": 87}}}, "bargained": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 5, "context": "Maggie and Bessie had not bargained for the thorns , and felt somehow as if they had been rather imposed upon ; but they picked away more carefully", "context_len": 28}}}, "sashes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "The man took one of the sashes of the corsairs , tore it into strips , and bandaged the wound ; then with another he made a sling for the arm", "context_len": 31}}}, "amply": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I am greatly mistaken if I do not find in the sashes of these three villains sufficient to repay me amply for my share in this evening 's work", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "Brett 's calm voice continued : `` I am a very plain old woman ; I have no youth to boast of , and no looks to boast of ; but I think I have got a somewhat capacious heart , and it is amply willing to take you all in if you wish to come", "context_len": 56}}}, "chink": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "] When it was night , your honour , what sees I through the chink of the kitchen door in the passage but the three young Ladies lugging along a great bundle , and stopping and panting and puffing ? So says I , I \u2019 ll see to the bottom of this , so I pops out suddenly and says , Can I help you , Misses ? \u2019 quite civil like", "context_len": 73}}}, "sheathed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "As Uncle Isaac had finished his planting , he now came to work with them ; they made the windlass , rudder , and spars ; they also sheathed the bow and stern with boards , where she entered and left the water , so as to diminish the friction somewhat", "context_len": 51}}}, "boatmen": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "He had with him some oaten cakes he had bought there , a string of fish he had purchased from the boatmen , and with these and the dates he thought he could manage for four or five days at least", "context_len": 41}}}, "fruitless": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 85, "context": "Here and there Lilian ran , hunting in all Peggy 's favourite haunts -- now peeping into a hollow yew-tree , now peering at the top of a ladder , now rummaging in the tool-shed , then back through the sand-quarry into the stack-yard , where there was a very good chance that the young lady might be hidden away in some snug little hole among the hay ; but though Lilian got a tolerable amount of hay-seed into her hair , her efforts were fruitless , and she was just turning away , hot and out of breath , to give up the useless search , when the sound of a low , chuckling laugh attracted her to the barn", "context_len": 121}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "At length , worn out by our fruitless endeavours to extricate ourselves , we waited motionless in the slime till the grinning faces of the pikemen appeared above the bank", "context_len": 30}}}, "scaling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Not until the search proved absolutely fruitless would it be seriously suspected that he had , either by passing through the gates in disguise , or by scaling the walls , made for the interior", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Swarming across the mass of debris in the ditch , they ascended the breach without opposition , and their scaling ladders were placed against the new wall before the knights could hurry up to its defence", "context_len": 36}}}, "unacquainted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "None knew that he could speak Arabic , and it would be so hopeless an undertaking for any one unacquainted with the language to traverse the country without being detected , that the Moors would be slow to believe that he had embarked upon such adventure", "context_len": 46}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "I shall make a point , however , of sending him a copy of my report in the Arch\u00e6ologian , where I trust he will find many items of information respecting the origin and history of the Abbey with which perchance he may be unacquainted", "context_len": 45}}}, "traverse": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "None knew that he could speak Arabic , and it would be so hopeless an undertaking for any one unacquainted with the language to traverse the country without being detected , that the Moors would be slow to believe that he had embarked upon such adventure", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Bending to avoid the flying fragments that were descending like rain , Malcolm , regardless of his sprained foot , bolted round the traverse , and before he was fully aware of the fact he had blundered right into a party of Huns", "context_len": 43}}}, "searchers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "He had walked for eight hours , and calculated that he must be fully thirty miles from Tripoli , and that not until evening would searchers overtake him", "context_len": 28}}}, "dates": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 11, "context": "I am an Historian , for I have been discussing old dates ; a Merchant , for I add plum to plum ; a Lawyer , for I have opened many a case ; a Lord Mayor , for the mace is before me ; and a Navigator , for I am led to seize and gulf ! What if I were to stay here altogether , or set up a new company with my fair hostesses ? Miss Ratty is cut out for a tragedy Queen", "context_len": 87}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "We can not expect to reconstruct the parent Maskoki language spoken at that time but very imperfectly , since the oldest text known to exist in any of the dialects dates from A", "context_len": 33}}}, "roadside": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 19, "context": "But the strain was too much for Peggy , and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "Report says that when he came to Rake to settle down , some twelve or thirteen years back -- I being but an infant in arms , -- he did gain a lasting reputation by outmatching one Caleb James , a notorious bully , at his own game , breaking his pate with his own staff on the roadside hard by Milland Church", "context_len": 63}}}, "quenching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "After quenching his thirst at a well in front of the mosque , he retraced his steps until beyond the village , then struck out into the country , made a detour , came down into the road again , and continued his journey eastward", "context_len": 45}}}, "mosque": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "After quenching his thirst at a well in front of the mosque , he retraced his steps until beyond the village , then struck out into the country , made a detour , came down into the road again , and continued his journey eastward", "context_len": 45}}}, "tapering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Each boat carried a single short mast , to the top of which was attached a long tapering spar , on which the sail was furled", "context_len": 26}}}, "feluccas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Gervaise knew that these small feluccas were generally fast sailors and fair sea boats , and resolved to seize one of them , trusting that when once the sail was shaken out he would be able to manage it single handed", "context_len": 41}}}, "oaten": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He had with him some oaten cakes he had bought there , a string of fish he had purchased from the boatmen , and with these and the dates he thought he could manage for four or five days at least", "context_len": 41}}}, "felucca": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 48, "context": "After much consideration as to what had best be done , he took the anchor rope , which was some sixty yards in length , fastened one end to each end of the spar , and then lashed the middle of the rope to the bow of the felucca ; then , using an oar as a lever , he with great labour managed to launch the spar over the bow , with the sail still attached to it", "context_len": 79}}}, "brailed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "All day long he kept about the same distance from the land , and at night , instead of keeping on his course , brailed up the sail entirely , and allowed the vessel to drift , as he knew that before morning he should lose the coast if he continued as he was going", "context_len": 55}}}, "replenishing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "This relieved him of his chief anxiety , for he had been unable to think of any plan for replenishing his supply , or to concoct a likely tale that , were he obliged to go on shore , would account for his being alone in a craft of that size", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "She got it boiling briskly , and filled up the pan several times with water ; then , giving it a final replenishing , she ran off to practise a few scales and exercises", "context_len": 34}}}, "concoct": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "This relieved him of his chief anxiety , for he had been unable to think of any plan for replenishing his supply , or to concoct a likely tale that , were he obliged to go on shore , would account for his being alone in a craft of that size", "context_len": 51}}}, "brail": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The wind increased again after sunrise , and being unable to reef the sail single handed he managed partially to brail it up", "context_len": 23}}}, "constructed": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 15, "context": "They had stories to tell him about the famous hanging gardens , which were artificially constructed in the most magnificent manner , on arches raised high in the air ; and about a vine made of gold , with all sorts of precious stones upon it instead of fruit , which was wrought as an ornament over the throne on which the King of Persia often gave audience ; of the splendid palaces and vast cities of the Persians ; and the banquets , and f\u00eates , and magnificent entertainments and celebrations which they used to have there", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "A very large and elaborately constructed carriage was built to convey the body", "context_len": 13}}}, "steering": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` For by a most lucky chance I have to-day obtained what I believe to be trustworthy information to the effect that she was sighted four days ago , bound for the Gaboon river -- or perhaps it would be more correct to say that she was sighted steering east , and identified by the master of a brig who knows her perfectly well , and who has since arrived here , and that there is authentic information to the effect that she is this time bound for the Gaboon", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "They now took the apples that were left , put them in the canoe , and piling a great heap of slabs on the table , set it on fire , and pulled away by the light of it , Charlie steering , and singing to them an old English song about one Parker , who was hung at the yard-arm for mutiny at the -- --", "context_len": 67}}}, "promontory": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "As the day went on , he saw that the coastline was now east and west , and felt that he must be off the most northerly point of the promontory ; he accordingly laid his course to the northeast , which would take him close to Cape Saloman , the most easterly point of Crete , and from two hundred and fifty to three hundred miles distant", "context_len": 68}}}, "lessen": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` As this consoling assurance seemed not to lessen Jane 's alarm , he went on cheerfully to say , `` There is n't one in my body has n't been splintered by these broncos ! Tinker 'em up and they 're better than new", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Well ! Alec exclaimed ; and she felt for the moment his approval of her lessen", "context_len": 16}}}, "trended": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "He judged , from what he had heard on the coast , that he must be nearly off Cape Tejones , behind which lies the town of Bengasi , and was confirmed in the belief on finding half an hour later that the coast , which had run nearly north and south , trended sharply away to the northeast", "context_len": 59}}}, "northeast": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 39, "context": "As the day went on , he saw that the coastline was now east and west , and felt that he must be off the most northerly point of the promontory ; he accordingly laid his course to the northeast , which would take him close to Cape Saloman , the most easterly point of Crete , and from two hundred and fifty to three hundred miles distant", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The Taensa guide who accompanied Lemoyne d'Iberville , up the Yazoo river in March 1699 , enumerated the villages seen on its low banks in their succession from southwest to northeast , as follows ( Margry IV , 180 ) :", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The Acolapissa Indians appear under various names in the country northwest to northeast of New Orleans", "context_len": 16}}}, "northerly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "As the day went on , he saw that the coastline was now east and west , and felt that he must be off the most northerly point of the promontory ; he accordingly laid his course to the northeast , which would take him close to Cape Saloman , the most easterly point of Crete , and from two hundred and fifty to three hundred miles distant", "context_len": 68}}}, "easterly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "As the day went on , he saw that the coastline was now east and west , and felt that he must be off the most northerly point of the promontory ; he accordingly laid his course to the northeast , which would take him close to Cape Saloman , the most easterly point of Crete , and from two hundred and fifty to three hundred miles distant", "context_len": 68}}}, "smoothness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 90, "context": "Oh ! if only every one might have his turn in this world of ours ; but since that is out of the question , let there at least be as much Coney Island for housemaids as is consistent with good morals and faithful discharge of their duties ; at least so thought one dear little mistress , with more heart , perhaps , than discretion , but a heart , all the same , that won every one to her and made life in her household move with infinite smoothness", "context_len": 91}}}, "halliards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "This was not difficult , as its weight brought it down on to the deck as soon as he slackened the halliards ; he unhooked it from the block , and then lashed the sail securely to it", "context_len": 38}}}, "unhooked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "This was not difficult , as its weight brought it down on to the deck as soon as he slackened the halliards ; he unhooked it from the block , and then lashed the sail securely to it", "context_len": 38}}}, "deliberated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Before taking down the spar and sail , he had deliberated as to whether it would be better to run before the coming gale or to lie to , and had decided on the latter alternative , as , were it to continue to blow long , he might be driven on to the Egyptian coast", "context_len": 56}}}, "spanning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The clouds had risen so fast that their edge was nearly overhead , spanning the sky like a great arch", "context_len": 20}}}, "gales": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "He had not been out in many of the gales that at times sweep the eastern waters of the Mediterranean with terrible violence , but had seen enough of them to know that it was no ordinary one that he was about to encounter", "context_len": 44}}}, "tightening": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "In a short time he heard a dull moaning sound , a puff of wind struck the boat , but in a few seconds died out ; it was sufficient to give the light craft stern way , and she drifted backwards , the rope tightening , until the spar lay across her bows , and some twenty yards away", "context_len": 60}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "They readjusted things in the comfortable little phaeton and tucked the lap-robe about them in trimmer fashion , and then the ponies , feeling a tightening grasp on the lines , and intuitively conscious of a whip poised at an easily descending angle , wisely saw fit to make up for lost time", "context_len": 53}}}, "deafening": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 26, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "This time fortune did not smile on us , for as the shallop was on the Frenchman \u2019 s lee a spurt of flame burst from the man-of-war , immediately followed by a deafening roar , and with it our mizzen mast went by the board with a terrific crash", "context_len": 50}}}, "flatten": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "There was , as yet , but little sea ; the force of the wind seemed to flatten the water , and the instant a wave lifted its head it was cut off as if by a knife , and carried away in spray", "context_len": 44}}}, "commending": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "The principal continued by commending with some warmth the individual whose impulses led him , regardless of personal danger or the chance of being misunderstood , to stand up in defense of one who was being mistreated and abused", "context_len": 39}}}, "breasted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "The resolute boy breasted the waves ; but so far from being sucked in , he found it impossible to reach the spot where the ball lay , and the suction began , by reason of the wind , which blew directly in his face , and the sea , that , beyond the influence of the breaker , drove directly to the shore ; and , worn out with effort , he returned exhausted to the boat", "context_len": 78}}}, "enveloped": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 20, "context": "My birthday eve they kept in high style , the men smoking long clay pipes , till our dining-room was enveloped in a cloud of tobacco smoke , so that my aunt declared that her best hangings would reek for days like a London coffee tavern , whereat Sir George pacified her by saying that the weed of Virginia is ever a sovereign safeguard against the plague !", "context_len": 68}}}, "breakwater": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The spar and sail acted not only as a floating anchor , but as a breakwater , and the white crested waves , which came on as if they would break upon the boat , seemed robbed of half their violence by the obstruction to their course , and passed under the felucca without breaking", "context_len": 55}}}, "obstruction": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "The spar and sail acted not only as a floating anchor , but as a breakwater , and the white crested waves , which came on as if they would break upon the boat , seemed robbed of half their violence by the obstruction to their course , and passed under the felucca without breaking", "context_len": 55}}}, "taxing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was a heavy task , taxing his strength to the utmost , but after a deal of labour it was got on board , and then raised to its position at the masthead ; the sail was shaken out , and the felucca again put on her course", "context_len": 49}}}, "subsistence": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 65, "context": "Our defences are all complete ; our recent peace with Egypt has enabled us to fill up our magazines with provisions of all kinds ; the inhabitants of the Island have had ample warning to move into the town , carrying with them everything of value ; so the Turks will obtain but little plunder , and will be able to gather no means of subsistence on the island , as every animal has been driven within the walls , and even the unripe corn has been reaped and brought in", "context_len": 91}}}, "unripe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 83, "context": "Our defences are all complete ; our recent peace with Egypt has enabled us to fill up our magazines with provisions of all kinds ; the inhabitants of the Island have had ample warning to move into the town , carrying with them everything of value ; so the Turks will obtain but little plunder , and will be able to gather no means of subsistence on the island , as every animal has been driven within the walls , and even the unripe corn has been reaped and brought in", "context_len": 91}}}, "prevents": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The number of his assailants prevents the archers on the Turkish craft using their bows", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The rest the paternal Government prevents me getting rid of ; but it 's let , so I think I 'm good for about four hundred a year", "context_len": 28}}}, "archers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "And now the Danish archers , drawing nearer , sent fresh flights of arrows on those who were labouring on the house top , and , killing several , drove the others away", "context_len": 33}}}, "distract": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 22, "context": "So he told them how , when he sat alone , ill and dull and irritable , Ram Dass had tried to distract him by describing the passers by , and there was one child who passed oftener than any one else ; he had begun to be interested in her -- partly perhaps because he was thinking a great deal of a little girl , and partly because Ram Dass had been able to relate the incident of his visit to the attic in chase of the monkey", "context_len": 89}}}, "unequal": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "The two galleys made for the breach , and the knights leapt out as soon as the boats grounded , and , wading ashore , joined the group that had so long and gallantly sustained the unequal fight", "context_len": 38}}}, "riddle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "They will riddle him with their spears when he comes up ; we shall have nothing to do but to avenge him", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "What an uncomfortable puzzle it all was ! and who was to read the answer to the riddle ? Had Seabrooke lost the money ? Had Percy been guilty of possessing himself of his own property by such unjustifiable means ? Or was one of their number an actual thief ?", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Madam , '' he said at length , `` may I pray you to resolve me the riddle how I find you dwelling in Norfolk ? We heard you had departed to the New England plantations near two years ago , with your honoured father", "context_len": 46}}}, "forbear": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Maybe they 're hunting for the one of their number who wears the diamond bracelet , '' suggested Bart , with a laugh , for , in spite of the gravity of the loss , he could not forbear an occasional joke at Fenn 's rather odd theory", "context_len": 49}}}, "bloodstains": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "In those dark clothes , soaked with water as they are , I did not see the bloodstains ; but I ought to have looked for them , for surely no one could have gone through that fight -- altogether unprotected with armour too -- without being wounded", "context_len": 48}}}, "fasted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Also order me some wine and food , Sir John ; I have touched nothing save water for twenty-four hours , and before that fasted somewhat strictly", "context_len": 27}}}, "mooted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "A convention was arranged with the chiefs of the Lower Creeks at Savannah in 1735 , during which the legend of the Kasi ta migration was delivered , but it does not appear whether any new treaty stipulations were mooted or not at that meeting", "context_len": 45}}}, "roundly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Jealous of the erection of this advanced trade-post by their hereditary enemy , the British asked for permission to build a fort on Og\u012btchi river , twenty miles west of Augusta , Georgia , but were roundly , and in unmistakable terms , refused by the Creek towns", "context_len": 48}}}, "sufficed": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 63, "context": "My prison -- if such it was -- was a tolerably spacious affair , measuring about twenty feet long by fifteen feet wide , and it was absolutely empty ; also , there being no windows to the building , and the light entering only by the open door , the obscurity , on entering , seemed profound , although a few minutes sufficed to enable one 's eyes to grow accustomed to it , when , at least during daylight , it was possible to see clearly enough for all practical purposes", "context_len": 93}}}, "retrieving": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "Sir John Boswell told me in confidence that he understood that Rivers did not at first volunteer to come out in response to the appeal of the grand master , but that the grand prior informed him that unless he took this opportunity of retrieving his character , he might give up all hope of ever obtaining advancement", "context_len": 58}}}, "viscount": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "My brother has already spoken of you to me , Sir Gervaise , the viscount said , when the young knight was presented to him ; although indeed there was no occasion for him to do so , since the name of the knight who two years ago saved the commerce of Italy from ruin , and with a single galley destroyed or captured a great fleet of over twenty Barbary pirates , and thus for a time put a stop to the depredations of the infidels , is known throughout Europe", "context_len": 92}}}, "bearer": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "The fact was , Albert was intrusted with a most important piece of information -- the bearer of a message that had cleared his own mental horizon from so much as the vestige of a cloud , and which he felt sure would bring equal joy to the others for whom it was intended", "context_len": 54}}}, "undisciplined": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "D'Aubusson , however , knew that between the undisciplined hordes that gathered in countless numbers to oppose the crusaders , and the troops of Mahomet , well trained in warfare , who had borne his standard victoriously in numerous battles , there was but little comparison", "context_len": 46}}}, "hordes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "D'Aubusson , however , knew that between the undisciplined hordes that gathered in countless numbers to oppose the crusaders , and the troops of Mahomet , well trained in warfare , who had borne his standard victoriously in numerous battles , there was but little comparison", "context_len": 46}}}, "victoriously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "D'Aubusson , however , knew that between the undisciplined hordes that gathered in countless numbers to oppose the crusaders , and the troops of Mahomet , well trained in warfare , who had borne his standard victoriously in numerous battles , there was but little comparison", "context_len": 46}}}, "annihilation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Under such circumstances , although victory might be possible , the chances of defeat would be far greater , and while victory could be only won at a great sacrifice of life , defeat would mean annihilation to the garrison , and the loss of the city upon whose fortifications such an enormous amount of money and labour had been expended", "context_len": 61}}}, "entreaties": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` How could I have left him ? And how could we be urgent to him to fly when we could not know what extraordinary impulse one of his virtue or courage may have had on his mind ? Forget not how he did answer to your entreaties , saying that he would not stir a foot , nor turn his back as though he repented he had been engaged in that great work , or were ashamed of the service of so glorious and great a God ! We could not seek to change such a resolve", "context_len": 98}}}, "sortie": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Therefore the grand master , with the full assent of his leaders , turned a deaf ear to the entreaties of the younger knights , that they might be allowed to make a sortie", "context_len": 34}}}, "perfecting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "Then for the first time the tone of the organ rang out full and strong ; and the visitors in the chapel below looked up with rapt faces to the gallery , as though for them , as for Marie-Celeste , the sweet music seemed to lend the last perfecting touch to the holy enchantment of the place", "context_len": 58}}}, "disembarking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "After disembarking his company , he offered sacrifices to the gods , and then proceeded to visit the places which had been the scenes of the events which Homer had described", "context_len": 31}}}, "eminence": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "But the saddest scene in the flight lay on a gentle eminence , commanding a view of the field , whose deformities night mercifully shrouded from view , although the murmurs of the wounded reached them even there in one long subdued wailing moan", "context_len": 44}}}, "construct": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 64, "context": "Peggy and Bobby would have been quite content to go on adding a nail here or a shelf there , and further making improvements , but Archie , now that the chief work was over , found his interest cooling , and having got hold of a book on 'Balloons and Air-Ships , ' proposed no less daring a scheme than that he should construct a flying machine , and start it from the platform", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Incited by an account of the tree-dwellings in one of Miss Forster 's books of travel , Archie determined to emulate them , and construct such an elevated establishment for themselves", "context_len": 31}}}, "cumbrous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "But the cannon of that period were so cumbrous and slowly worked , and so inaccurate in their aim , that the advantage of occupying a position that would prevent an enemy from firing down into a town was considered to be more than counterbalanced by the weakening of the garrison by the abstraction of the force required to man the detached work , and by the risk of their being surrounded and cut off without the garrison of the town being able to aid them", "context_len": 86}}}, "inaccurate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "But the cannon of that period were so cumbrous and slowly worked , and so inaccurate in their aim , that the advantage of occupying a position that would prevent an enemy from firing down into a town was considered to be more than counterbalanced by the weakening of the garrison by the abstraction of the force required to man the detached work , and by the risk of their being surrounded and cut off without the garrison of the town being able to aid them", "context_len": 86}}}, "counterbalanced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "But the cannon of that period were so cumbrous and slowly worked , and so inaccurate in their aim , that the advantage of occupying a position that would prevent an enemy from firing down into a town was considered to be more than counterbalanced by the weakening of the garrison by the abstraction of the force required to man the detached work , and by the risk of their being surrounded and cut off without the garrison of the town being able to aid them", "context_len": 86}}}, "abstraction": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "But the cannon of that period were so cumbrous and slowly worked , and so inaccurate in their aim , that the advantage of occupying a position that would prevent an enemy from firing down into a town was considered to be more than counterbalanced by the weakening of the garrison by the abstraction of the force required to man the detached work , and by the risk of their being surrounded and cut off without the garrison of the town being able to aid them", "context_len": 86}}}, "detached": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 61, "context": "But the cannon of that period were so cumbrous and slowly worked , and so inaccurate in their aim , that the advantage of occupying a position that would prevent an enemy from firing down into a town was considered to be more than counterbalanced by the weakening of the garrison by the abstraction of the force required to man the detached work , and by the risk of their being surrounded and cut off without the garrison of the town being able to aid them", "context_len": 86}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "While all turned in surprise at this unexpected interruption , a small girl , not more than six or seven years old , detached herself from the group and rushed toward Bomba", "context_len": 32}}}, "marquees": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "On the summit was the great tent of the pasha ; round this were the marquees of the other commanders , while the encampments of the troops stretched far away along the upper slopes of the hill", "context_len": 37}}}, "encampments": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "The galley soon came back and reported that , beyond the Gates of Syria , they saw the whole country , which was nearly level land , though gently rising from the sea , covered with the vast encampments of the Persian army", "context_len": 43}}}, "truce": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 59, "context": "Three days after we left the island the Sea Wolf , for such is the name of the buccaneer \u2019 s ship , hove to in sight of Port Royal , and with the greatest audacity Pedro , or Red Peter , to give him the name he is generally known by , went ashore under a flag of truce , taking me with him as hostage", "context_len": 67}}}, "inhabitant": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 55, "context": "Most of the people had made haste to get well for Easter , for to the good folk of Gorswen that festival meant but one event -- the great fair of the borders , which had been held in the village every Easter Tuesday within the memory of even that wonderful person , the oldest inhabitant", "context_len": 56}}}, "artilleryman": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "In addition to this he had arranged with a renegade German , known as Maitre Georges , a man of very great ability as an artilleryman and engineer , to desert to the city , and there do all in his power to assist the besiegers , both by affording them information and by giving bad advice to the besieged", "context_len": 60}}}, "besiegers": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 45, "context": "In addition to this he had arranged with a renegade German , known as Maitre Georges , a man of very great ability as an artilleryman and engineer , to desert to the city , and there do all in his power to assist the besiegers , both by affording them information and by giving bad advice to the besieged", "context_len": 60}}}, "herald": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "On the day after Paleologus , who was himself a renegade Greek , had established his camp , he sent in a herald to summon the city to surrender , at the same time making lavish promises that the lives and property of the native population should be respected , and that they should be allowed to continue to reside there , to enjoy the full exercise of their religion and of all other rights they possessed", "context_len": 77}}}, "disaffection": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "A disaffection in a small part of an army may be put down by decisive measures ; but when the determination to resist is universal , it is useless for any commander , however imperious and absolute in temper , to attempt to withstand it", "context_len": 45}}}, "establishment": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 32, "word_index": 31, "context": "He ran after the children with little short , sharp barks , worrying at their heels till Aunt Helen declared there would soon not be a whole stocking left in the establishment ; he had a fray at once with Jack , but was much worsted by that worthy , returning to Peggy for protection with his scrap of a tail between his legs ; he tore Bobby 's cricket-ball to pieces , licked the polish off Father 's boots , devoured Aunt Helen 's goloshes , and nestled cosily down to sleep on the top of Nancy 's best Sunday hat , which that luckless damsel had imprudently left in an open band-box on the kitchen settle", "context_len": 118}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "1 General Hospital , Brockenhurst -- an establishment known as Tin Town -- two men in `` hospital blues '' were slowly making their way", "context_len": 25}}}, "reconnaissance": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "Moreover , Shagbark was likely to return at any moment from his reconnaissance and it would be just like him to shoot down Jethro", "context_len": 24}}}, "adviser": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Maitre Georges , who was acting as the military adviser of the pasha , saw at once that the weakest point of the defence was Fort St", "context_len": 27}}}, "battery": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 64, "context": "At the end of that time he roused himself from his stupor of grief and disappointment , and , abandoning the idea of any further attack upon the point that had cost him so dearly , he ordered the troops to move round and renew the attack upon the wall in front of the Jews ' quarter , and commence the construction of a battery on the edge of the great ditch facing the retrenchment behind the breach before effected", "context_len": 80}}}, "workmen": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 3, "context": "Numbers of the workmen were killed , but the erection of the battery was pushed on night and day , and ere long three of the immense cannon that had been brought from Constantinople , -- where sixteen of them had been cast under the direction of Maitre Georges -- were placed in position", "context_len": 54}}}, "erection": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 78, "context": "Thus the time passed on until the first day of the Customs dawned , when , having received a more than usually substantial meal , I was stripped of the few rags that still covered my nakedness , and , with my hands tightly bound behind me by a thin but strong raw-hide rope , was led forth to the great square wherein the Customs were celebrated , and firmly bound to one of the posts , the erection of which I had witnessed a week earlier", "context_len": 87}}}, "diameter": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 28, "context": "She did not call to me , or announce her return in any way , but set to work to mow a circle of about ten feet in diameter in the long grass ; and then , having produced fire by rubbing two pieces of wood together , she proceeded very carefully to burn off the short grass left inside the circle , setting fire to it , allowing it to burn for a few seconds , and then beating it out again with a branch , in order that the fire might not spread and burn us out , to say nothing of betraying our presence by the smoke that it would raise", "context_len": 114}}}, "paladins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "I know that the knights , so far as bravery and devotion are concerned , will each and every one do his best , and will die at their posts before yielding a foot ; but while fighting like paladins they will think of naught else , and , however hardly pressed , will omit to send to me for reinforcements", "context_len": 61}}}, "derogatory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Nay , even did they think of it , they probably would not send , deeming that to do so would be derogatory , and might be taken as an act of cowardice", "context_len": 33}}}, "interposing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Caretto had shown so much tact after his release from the Moors , and had so willingly aided him in any capacity allotted to him , without in the slightest degree interposing his council unasked , that Gervaise had come to like him greatly , even before their arrival at Genoa", "context_len": 51}}}, "unasked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Caretto had shown so much tact after his release from the Moors , and had so willingly aided him in any capacity allotted to him , without in the slightest degree interposing his council unasked , that Gervaise had come to like him greatly , even before their arrival at Genoa", "context_len": 51}}}, "cemented": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Circumstances there had brought them closely together , and their friendship had been cemented during their voyage to Rhodes", "context_len": 19}}}, "staff": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 81, "context": "So , once more , by the matter of a few hours , Konrad von Feldoffer escaped a well-merited death ; while , through ignorance of the terrific preparations made for the blowing up of Messines Ridge , he had unwittingly done the Allies a good turn ; for instead of withdrawing the troops the Hun commander had concentrated a thousand on the mined ridge in order to repel an infantry attack that threatened only in the minds of the German staff", "context_len": 82}}}, "solicitation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Yes , and am on it still ; and it is by his orders and not by my own solicitation that I am here", "context_len": 24}}}, "resisting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Bomba was all these in one now , as he fought with the Indian , straining backward and forward , resisting the pressure upon his knife arm , striving with all the power in him to drive downward the shining point of his machete , to sink it to the hilt in his enemy \u2019 s flesh", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The ruler Montezuma saw the impossibility of resisting , and called to his aid the neighboring tribes", "context_len": 17}}}, "contingency": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Living as they did in an unsettled age , and far more prepared than we should be for such a contingency , yet the sense of the rapid approach of a cruel and remorseless foe struck terror into many hearts", "context_len": 40}}}, "exorbitant": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` I suppose they 'll charge a fortune for a supper , '' said Mosely , whose economical spirit was troubled by the exorbitant prices then prevalent in California , `` but we must have it at any cost", "context_len": 39}}}, "damsel": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 106, "context": "He ran after the children with little short , sharp barks , worrying at their heels till Aunt Helen declared there would soon not be a whole stocking left in the establishment ; he had a fray at once with Jack , but was much worsted by that worthy , returning to Peggy for protection with his scrap of a tail between his legs ; he tore Bobby 's cricket-ball to pieces , licked the polish off Father 's boots , devoured Aunt Helen 's goloshes , and nestled cosily down to sleep on the top of Nancy 's best Sunday hat , which that luckless damsel had imprudently left in an open band-box on the kitchen settle", "context_len": 118}}}, "houris": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "For shame , Cavalier ! As if a Christian knight would care for a Moslem maiden , even were she as fair as the houris of their creed !", "context_len": 29}}}, "trinket": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "On Sunday afternoons she would take us up to her room , in order that my Mother might rest in peace from the children ; and there we always spent a delightful time , looking over her dressing-case with its treasures , and listening to the histories of each trinket and curiosity , or messing with her paints", "context_len": 58}}}, "jeeringly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` And when one fellow cried out jeeringly , `` Where is now your Good Old Cause ? '' he , with a cheerful smile , clapped his hand on his breast saying , `` Here it is , and I am going to seal it with my blood", "context_len": 49}}}, "possessor": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "It will be so in your case : the lady is a great heiress , and , as the possessor of wide lands , your influence in Northern Italy may be very valuable to us , and in case of need you will , like my brother De Monteuil , be able to bring a gathering of men-at-arms to our aid", "context_len": 61}}}, "fairer": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "She was fairer than the Mexicans , although not fair in the fashion of Edith May , but with a faint citron hue which , better than pink and white , befitted the extreme darkness of her hair and eyes", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "He had arrived in the nick of time and could not have asked a fairer target than that presented by the brute", "context_len": 22}}}, "dames": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "sinful": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "Whatever else they do n't hold with , they hold on to their sinful pride , they quite forget that they are worms of the dust , that their fall will come , and when it comes it 's bitter , that 's what I say ; that 's what I have said to Marion , when Marion has been a little put out , poor dear , with the marked and silly attentions of one who never meant anything at all", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "A love for music was , beside her affection for Lem , the one soft spot in poor Dolly 's sinful , hardened heart ; but the practised voices of the two ladies had not half the charm for her of the simple , childish tones which had first sung to her the hymn which had taken such hold upon her fancy , or rather on her heart", "context_len": 68}}}, "confessor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "I am speaking to you as to a confessor , and just as I have kept her amulet hidden from all , so is the thought of her a secret I would not part with for my life", "context_len": 38}}}, "decrease": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It does not decrease my chances , for , as I know , there are no chances ; but it renders it more easy for me to know that it is so", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Some think that not one of her inhabitants -- 120,000 -- has altogether escaped the fever , and many have died ; but I am thankful to write that the sickness is on the decrease", "context_len": 35}}}, "aspire": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "I know that it is not only sinful , but mad , for me to think so frequently of her , but at least I am not mad enough to think that I can either win the heart or aspire to the hand of one who is , you say , so beautiful , and who is , moreover , as I know , the heiress to wide estates", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "It is said that Alexander , finding that the various objects of human ambition which he had been so rapidly attaining by his victories and conquests for the past few years were insufficient to satisfy him , began now to aspire for some supernatural honors , and he accordingly conceived the design of having himself declared to be the son of a god", "context_len": 63}}}, "absolved": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "As to the vow of celibacy , you know that ere now knights have been absolved from their vows , and methinks that , after the service you have rendered to Italy by ridding the sea of those corsairs , his Holiness would make no difficulty in granting any request that you might make him in that or any other direction", "context_len": 61}}}, "ridding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "As to the vow of celibacy , you know that ere now knights have been absolved from their vows , and methinks that , after the service you have rendered to Italy by ridding the sea of those corsairs , his Holiness would make no difficulty in granting any request that you might make him in that or any other direction", "context_len": 61}}}, "monarch": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 64, "context": "heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing his victims to the tree with", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was impatient to be monarch himself", "context_len": 7}}}, "wonderment": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 38, "context": "The Sultan was amazed when he saw Alaeddin in this regal array and beheld his grace and goodliness and the mamelukes standing in attendance upon him in all their comeliness and fair favour ; yea , and his wonderment redoubled when Alaeddin 's mother came up attired in rich and costly raiment , as she were a queen , and he saw twelve slave-girls in her service , preceding her , their hands clasped behind their backs , with all worship and observance", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 68, "context": "The Sultan still marvelled in himself at Alaeddin 's mother , how she had come to him in poor clothes , whilst her son had command of this exceeding wealth ; and as for the folk , who came to the Sultan 's palace , to gaze upon the wedding-festivities , when they saw Alaeddin 's palace and the goodliness of its building , there took them great wonderment how so magnificent a building had been upreared in one night and they fell all to praying for Alaeddin and saying , `` God prosper him ! By Allah , he is deserving", "context_len": 102}}}, "nunnery": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 78, "context": "I do not know Claudia 's thoughts , Caretto remarked drily , but I do know that last year she refused to listen to at least a score of excellent offers for her hand , including one from a son of the doge himself , and that without any reasonable cause assigned by her , to the great wonderment of all , seeing that she does not appear to have any leaning whatever towards a life in a nunnery", "context_len": 79}}}, "artillery": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 63, "context": "Tresham was last night saying to me , that it seemed to him it would not be a difficult matter for one who spoke Turkish well , to issue at night on the other side of the town , and to make his way round to the battery , disguised of course as a Turkish soldier , and then , mixing with the artillery men , to drive a spike into one of the touch holes", "context_len": 76}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "She would be one of the choice delicate palfreys , yoked to artillery , who break down and give extra work to the already fully-taxed horses", "context_len": 26}}}, "battlements": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 58, "context": "In response to the gun , three companies of musketeers , with drums beating and matches burning , marched from their quarters to the fort , followed ten minutes later by a large body of pikemen , their arms glittering in the light as they passed by the huge wood fires that had been hastily kindled on the battlements", "context_len": 59}}}, "breached": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 76, "context": "As soon as the battery was completed , gangs of men , accustomed to mining operations , set to work in its rear to drive sloping passages downwards , opening into the face of the great cutting , and through these vast quantities of earth and stones were poured , so as to afford a passage across it , the depth being largely diminished by the great pile of rubbish that had already fallen from the breached wall", "context_len": 78}}}, "reload": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "The exclamation was caused by the discovery that after firing at the dusky archer in the ravine , he had neglected to reload his rifle , a piece of forgetfulness for which Shagbark never would have forgiven him", "context_len": 38}}}, "quarried": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "No stones ever quarried by man could long resist such tremendous blows", "context_len": 12}}}, "starred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "In some places , you see , the stones are starred and cracked , in others the shock seems to have pulverised the spot where it struck ; but , worse , still , the whole face of the wall is shaken", "context_len": 42}}}, "demolished": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "In front of a partly demolished barn , over which was flying a Geneva Cross flag , covered ambulance motors were being filled up with wounded , who , their injuries attended to , were being dispatched to the base hospital", "context_len": 41}}}, "craving": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "Gervaise , on reaching the palace , heard that D'Aubusson was at present engaged in examining no less a person than Maitre Georges , the right hand of Paleologus , who had soon after daybreak presented himself before the wall on the other side of the town , declaring that he had left the Turkish service , and craving to be admitted", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "At last the craving of his stomach became so insistent that he took down a square tin box from a shelf behind the calico curtain and opened it on the little table", "context_len": 32}}}, "defection": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "There was no question that if the renegade came in good faith , his defection would be a serious blow to the assailants , and that his well known skill and experience would greatly benefit the defenders", "context_len": 37}}}, "repented": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 65, "context": "`` How could I have left him ? And how could we be urgent to him to fly when we could not know what extraordinary impulse one of his virtue or courage may have had on his mind ? Forget not how he did answer to your entreaties , saying that he would not stir a foot , nor turn his back as though he repented he had been engaged in that great work , or were ashamed of the service of so glorious and great a God ! We could not seek to change such a resolve", "context_len": 98}}}, "suite": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "When Maitre Georges came out from the palace , laughing and talking with the two knights who had entered with him , it was evident that he was well pleased with his reception by the grand master , who had assigned to him a suite of apartments in the guest house", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "After talking for upwards of an hour , the countess told Caretto that she had several matters on which she needed his counsel , and retired with him to the next room of the suite opening from the apartment in which they had been sitting", "context_len": 45}}}, "georges": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Georges had , in his conversation with him , laid great stress on the strength of the Turkish army , the excellent quality of the troops , and the enormous battering train that had been prepared", "context_len": 36}}}, "stress": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 112, "context": "`` Then she told her mother the queen all that had betided her that night and how they had taken her bridegroom , leaving her alone , and how after a little there came another youth and lay down in the place of her bridegroom , putting a sword between himself and her ; `` and in the morning '' [ quoth she ] `` he who had brought us thither returned and taking us up , carried us back to our place here : and hardly had he brought us hither and left us when my father the Sultan entered and I had neither heart nor tongue to answer him for stress of fright and trembling which possessed me", "context_len": 120}}}, "anecdote": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}}}, "knave": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was somewhat of a knave as Benjamin would learn later , and he knew little about his trade", "context_len": 19}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "They addressed each other as 'Ho , knave ! ' or 'Prithee , kind wench ! ' and would answer the simplest question by 'Yea , certes ! ' or 'A n't please thee , my lord", "context_len": 37}}}, "extracting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Then , one day of the days , he smote the sand and extracting the figures , set them down after the most approved fashion [ 545 ] and adjusted [ 546 ] them , so he might see and certify himself of the death of Alaeddin and the safe keeping of the lamp under the earth ; and he looked well into [ 547 ] the figures , both mothers and daughters , [ 548 ] but saw not the lamp , whereupon rage overrode him and he smote the sand a second time , that he might certify himself of Alaeddin 's death , but saw him not in the treasure ; whereat he redoubled in wrath , and yet more when it was certified", "context_len": 128}}}, "tubes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It would be too dangerous ; and even were a spike driven in , the Turks would have no great difficulty in extracting it , for the tubes are so big that a man might crawl in and drive the spike up from the inside", "context_len": 45}}}, "bombardment": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 24, "context": "After a quarter of an hour 's desperate conflict the Turks were driven down the partial breach effected in the wall by the last bombardment , and the Christians were again the masters of their ramparts", "context_len": 36}}}, "approaches": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 33, "context": "From all this it becomes evident , that the Creek verb surpasses in its large power of polysynthesis the Algonkin , Dakota and Kalapuya verb , and in the richness of its forms approaches closely to the Iroquois verb , which is poorer in tenses , but has an impersonal conjugation and fourteen persons to each tense of the finite verb", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "A spirited horse knows immediately when any one approaches him in a timid or cautious manner", "context_len": 16}}}, "impede": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 64, "context": "was concerned , it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed", "context_len": 128}}}, "abeyance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Mark Penelly watched him as he swam ashore easily and well , and the bitter feelings of dislike which had for the time being lain in abeyance before the scene of peril of which he had been witness , began once more to grow stronger , completely changing the appearance of his face as now , to get rid of the thoughts that troubled him , he took hold of one of the sweeps and began to row", "context_len": 78}}}, "intermission": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 56, "context": "`` Why , my dear brother , '' said Simeon , `` I am sure you will pardon me ; you know it is all love , my brother -- but , indeed , it was just as if you were knocking on a warming-pan -- tin , tin , tin , tin , without any intermission ! ''", "context_len": 59}}}, "corpse": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 51, "context": "It took me the remainder of the day to collect as much dry and combustible material as I considered would be needful to accomplish the complete incineration of the body , and to build the pyre ; but it was done at last ; and then , once more raising the corpse in my arms , I gently placed it on the top", "context_len": 63}}}, "basilisk": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "A fragment of one of the stone balls from a basilisk struck off D'Aubusson 's helmet", "context_len": 16}}}, "terminating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "It was an island some three miles in extent , and about half that distance in breadth , a line of rugged hills running from east to west , terminating in low cliffs", "context_len": 33}}}, "watergate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "If you will give me leave , sir , I will go out at the watergate , swim up the inner harbour , and in a very short time turn a few of the craft lying there into fire ships , and tow them out with a couple of galleys", "context_len": 50}}}, "tablet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` Then he returned to the khan in a woeful state of chagrin and colour and despite , for envy of Alaeddin , and [ 557 ] taking his geomantic instruments , [ 558 ] smote his [ tablet of ] sand , so he might learn where the lamp was , and found that it was in the palace and not with Alaeddin ; [ 559 ] whereat he rejoiced with an exceeding joy and said , `` Now it will be an easy matter for me to bereave this accursed of his life and I have a way to come at the lamp", "context_len": 105}}}, "protected": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 37, "context": "Regret was expressed for the loss of Tasker and Keene , both of whom were highly esteemed by all their shipmates ; and then the conversation diverged to the topic of the audacious Virginia-Preciosa , which , protected by the very ingenious fraud of the double sets of papers and the double crews , was still merrily pursuing her way and bidding defiance to everybody", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "They huddled together , turned up the collars of their coats , and tried to crawl to spots more or less protected", "context_len": 22}}}, "saturated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "Then barrels were broken open , old sails and faggots saturated with oil and pitch , and in little more than a quarter of an hour after his arrival , Gervaise had the satisfaction of seeing that the ten boats were all filled with combustibles , and ready to be set on fire", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "In less than two dozen strokes he found that his saturated sleeves hampered his arms", "context_len": 15}}}, "disembark": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "The smoke of the guns lay so heavy on the water , and the combatants were so intent upon the struggle at the breach , that Gervaise steered his galley into the midst of the Turkish vessels laden with troops ready to disembark , without attracting any notice ; then , standing upon the taffrail , he signalled to the two outside boats to throw off their ropes and make for the Turkish ship nearest to them", "context_len": 77}}}, "aground": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Again we tacked , but our efforts to find deeper water were unavailing , and at last the galliot ran aground with a slight shock on a bed of soft sand", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "A few days after leaving port yellow fever decimated the crew , and the survivors , unable to handle the ship , ran her aground on a small cay in the Rosario Channel , between the Isla de Pinos and Cuba", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Fancy these glorious wanderers from Greenland or Labrador , with the sea-spray dashing against their sides , showing that they were aground ; for , as you are aware , the mass of ice below water is far greater than that which is visible above it", "context_len": 46}}}, "redouble": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "A shout to the rowers made them redouble their efforts", "context_len": 10}}}, "concentrated": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 58, "context": "So , once more , by the matter of a few hours , Konrad von Feldoffer escaped a well-merited death ; while , through ignorance of the terrific preparations made for the blowing up of Messines Ridge , he had unwittingly done the Allies a good turn ; for instead of withdrawing the troops the Hun commander had concentrated a thousand on the mined ridge in order to repel an infantry attack that threatened only in the minds of the German staff", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 2, "context": "With a concentrated roar , the concussion of which was distinctly felt over the greater part of south-eastern England , the explosive contents of a series of mine-chambers were fired simultaneously", "context_len": 31}}}, "reanimated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Nicholas , reanimated by the sight of the discomfiture of the Turkish fleet , with a loud shout rushed down from the spot which they had held for so many hours , drove their assailants before them , and flung themselves upon the crowd assembled at the foot of the breach", "context_len": 51}}}, "repulsed": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 24, "context": "They sheltered the retreating Naktche against the pursuing French , [ 58 ] besieged the commander Denys at Fort Natchitoches , though they were repulsed there with considerable loss , defeated the French invading their country at Amalahta ( 1736 ) , at the Long House , or Tch\u00faka fal\u00e1ya ( Adair , p", "context_len": 54}}}, "relinquished": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "Seeing themselves abandoned by the ships , a panic seized them , and as the knights rushed down upon them they relinquished all thoughts of resistance , and dashed into the shallow water", "context_len": 33}}}, "sustained": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 17, "context": "The painful freeing of his right hand , the fear that in the fall he might have sustained an injury that would cripple him and leave him a prey to the first beast of the jungle that might roam that way , the dread that he might never be able to free himself from those ruthless , imprisoning branches had blinded him to another and more imminent peril that threatened", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "Whit Monday was a holiday at school , however , and Peggy and Bobby , having the day at home , took a sudden fit of industry , and started to weed the shrubbery with the noble intention of having it raked over and tidied by teatime , being put somewhat on their mettle by Father 's remarks on the subject of sustained labour , and his laughing incredulity when they assured him he would find it all neatly finished when he returned from Warford that evening", "context_len": 87}}}, "meed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "They all worshiped Rosamund ; and , truth to tell , Rosamund could not but enjoy her meed of popularity", "context_len": 20}}}, "fops": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "She will look on the gay and idle young fops of Genoa with greater disdain than ever", "context_len": 17}}}, "disdain": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "I know I \u2019 m not handsome , Eliot , and could never be much of a favorite ; but the fact that we lived in such humble circumstances and that my father seemed so worthless caused the boys who dared do so to treat me with disdain", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas must work might and main , And therefore good nourishment should not disdain , -- Or danger is great of their going insane", "context_len": 27}}}, "armistice": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Two hours later Caretto and Gervaise were roused by the arrival of a hundred knights in place of the previous garrison ; these bore the news that the pasha had sent in a flag of truce to ask for an armistice until sundown , to enable him to carry off for burial the bodies of those who had fallen in the attack", "context_len": 62}}}, "reinforcement": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 59, "context": "There was now a short breathing time for the besieged ; but the depression which the failure of their efforts excited among the Turks , was shortly dispelled by the arrival of a ship , with a despatch from Constantinople , in which the pasha was informed that the sultan himself was about to proceed to Rhodes with a reinforcement of a hundred thousand men , and a fresh park of artillery", "context_len": 72}}}, "corpses": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "But a minute or two later , as we drew nearer the tree , the stench meanwhile becoming almost overpoweringly strong , I detected fastened to the trunk of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may", "context_len": 128}}}, "uncover": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "If , sir , I and the knights with me do not uncover our heads , it is from no want of respect for the dead , but solely because we dare not stand bareheaded under the fierce rays of the sun", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "He had been on hand when Pickle searched Stone \u2019 s room , and had seen the officer uncover the stolen property and take possession of Ben \u2019 s note of farewell to Mrs", "context_len": 34}}}, "bareheaded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 69, "context": "The farmer with the plug hat he-hawed at the top of his voice , the miserable owner of the eggs got mad at him , some words ensued , the farmer started after him , the egg owner ran , once outside fired an egg which struck the smooth , shiny tile with a splatter , and the farmer came back into the express office holding his nose , bareheaded , and looking for his rejected straw head-covering", "context_len": 78}}}, "complimenting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "The officers were taken on board the admiral \u2019 s , where de Ruyter himself accepted our surrender , complimenting us on our gallant defence , and permitting the senior officers to retain their swords", "context_len": 35}}}, "withstood": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` If any blessed spirit did speak to me , it were indeed a grace and a light shining in darkness ; but as they be evil spirits , they can scarce be more dangerous than when I withstood them in the flesh at Worcester fight and Dunbar", "context_len": 48}}}, "bugbear": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "Tales have come down to us from our fathers of the marvellous prowess of the knights of your Order , and how at Smyrna , at Acre , and elsewhere , they performed such feats of valour that their name is still used by Turkish mothers as a bugbear to frighten their children", "context_len": 53}}}, "predecessors": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "688 ) , was so angry at the long delay in building the monastery , that at first he revoked the grant of his predecessors to Hean , but becoming reconciled , gave all his energy to the work , and Cloveshoo x , or Abingdon , became a monastic town , and its history commences as a house of God from Ina , about A", "context_len": 66}}}, "represents": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "It represents the bare cost of a new copper pedestal to replace the one you shot to pieces last night , and it 's a wonder you are not in jail for murder , for had that cannon ball struck a human being -- Enough ! before I take up this outrage with the district attorney in its criminal phase , are you going to settle the damage , or are you not ? ''", "context_len": 75}}}, "scarp": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "As soon as darkness came on , a large body of slaves were marched down from the town , and , under the direction of the knights , laboured all night at the mound , removing great quantities of the fallen stones and rubbish in a line halfway up it , and piling them above so as to form a scarp across the mound that would need ladders to ascend", "context_len": 70}}}, "repulse": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "Enlisting in the first contingent , he had taken part in the repulse of the first Turkish invasion of Egypt and the heroic yet ill-starred Gallipoli campaign without receiving as much as a scratch , and having hardly spent a day in hospital", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "For three days after this terrible repulse the Turks were inactive , the pasha remaining shut up in his tent , refusing to see any one , or to issue orders", "context_len": 31}}}, "shrine": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 58, "context": "Notice had been sent down early to the tower ; and all the knights who could be spared , without too greatly weakening the garrison , went up to attend it ; the service was conducted with all the pomp and ceremony possible , and after it was over a great procession was formed to proceed to the shrine , where a picture of the Virgin held in special reverence by the Order was placed", "context_len": 75}}}, "chapel": {"freq": 52, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 91, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "And did you ever hear , Albert , about a little French girl who was made Queen of England , and came to live in the castle when she was only eight years old , and who used to come to this very chapel ?", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` I agree with you , '' went on Tom easily , `` but I 'm afraid I ca n't report to you after chapel to-morrow , or , rather , to-day , Mr", "context_len": 34}}}, "basilisks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "During the night the Turks had transported their great basilisks , with other large pieces of artillery , from the camp to the rising ground on the south side of the city , and had opened fire against the wall covering the Jews ' quarter , and at the same time against the tower of St", "context_len": 56}}}, "mortars": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "From other commanding spots huge mortars were hurling great fragments of rock and other missiles broadcast into the town", "context_len": 19}}}, "broadcast": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "From other commanding spots huge mortars were hurling great fragments of rock and other missiles broadcast into the town", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "The members seemed to take copious notes in pocket-books , and asked occasional questions , one literary-looking lady , in spectacles and a large black hat with nodding feathers , being particularly insistent , and volunteering so much information that she threatened to usurp the place of the lecturer , and had to be gently suppressed , while an old gentleman distributed pamphlets broadcast , declaring he had had them specially printed for the occasion", "context_len": 75}}}, "shelters": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 46, "context": "The langue of Provence was in charge of this part of the wall , and , leaving them for the present to bear the brunt of the storm , the grand master sent the knights who could be spared , to assist the inhabitants to erect shelters against the storm of missiles falling in the town", "context_len": 56}}}, "celerity": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 7, "context": "And certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most", "context_len": 128}}}, "vaults": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "The work was performed with great celerity by the inhabitants , aided by the gangs of slaves , and in two or three days the townspeople were all in shelter , either in these sheds , in the vaults of the churches , or in other strongly constructed buildings", "context_len": 49}}}, "conflagrations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Among the missiles hurled into the town were balls filled with Greek fire , but the houses being entirely built of stone , no conflagrations of importance were caused by them , as a band of knights was organised specially to watch for these bombs , and whenever one of them was seen to fall , they hurried from their lookout to the spot , with a gang of slaves carrying baskets of earth and buckets of water , and quenched the flames before they had made any great headway", "context_len": 90}}}, "bombs": {"freq": 45, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 44, "context": "Among the missiles hurled into the town were balls filled with Greek fire , but the houses being entirely built of stone , no conflagrations of importance were caused by them , as a band of knights was organised specially to watch for these bombs , and whenever one of them was seen to fall , they hurried from their lookout to the spot , with a gang of slaves carrying baskets of earth and buckets of water , and quenched the flames before they had made any great headway", "context_len": 90}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "With rifle-shots , bayonets , clubbed weapons , and bombs they contested their ground", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Only three nights ago we heard bombs dropping on Julich , which is but a few miles away", "context_len": 19}}}, "quenched": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 80, "context": "Among the missiles hurled into the town were balls filled with Greek fire , but the houses being entirely built of stone , no conflagrations of importance were caused by them , as a band of knights was organised specially to watch for these bombs , and whenever one of them was seen to fall , they hurried from their lookout to the spot , with a gang of slaves carrying baskets of earth and buckets of water , and quenched the flames before they had made any great headway", "context_len": 90}}}, "avert": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "The mother was terrified , and , to avert these calamities , gave the child to a slave as soon as it was born , and ordered him to destroy it", "context_len": 31}}}, "semicircular": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The roof was semicircular , and composed of minute bricks , seeming to form one solid mass ; the floor of tiling , arranged in patterns , which could still be obscurely traced by the light of the lamp left by the charity of Sidroc to the prisoner ; for the dungeon was of bad reputation ; lights had been seen there at unearthly hours , when the outer door was fast and no inmate existed", "context_len": 76}}}, "rampart": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 48, "context": "He ordered all the houses in rear of the wall to be levelled ; a deep semicircular ditch was then dug , and behind this a new wall , constructed of the stones and bricks from the houses destroyed , was built , and backed with an earthen rampart of great thickness and solidity", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "They make a very rampart ! And if that were forced , the ceiling is all broken at that end , so 'twere easy to scramble up on the rafters and lie hid under the tiles", "context_len": 36}}}, "solidity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "He ordered all the houses in rear of the wall to be levelled ; a deep semicircular ditch was then dug , and behind this a new wall , constructed of the stones and bricks from the houses destroyed , was built , and backed with an earthen rampart of great thickness and solidity", "context_len": 54}}}, "convents": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Knights and slaves , men , women , and children , and even the inmates of the convents and nunneries , aided in the work , and when at last the outer wall fell , and the Turks thought that success was at hand , the pasha saw with astonishment and dismay that entry to the city was still barred by a work as formidable as that which he had destroyed at an enormous expenditure of ammunition", "context_len": 77}}}, "nunneries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Knights and slaves , men , women , and children , and even the inmates of the convents and nunneries , aided in the work , and when at last the outer wall fell , and the Turks thought that success was at hand , the pasha saw with astonishment and dismay that entry to the city was still barred by a work as formidable as that which he had destroyed at an enormous expenditure of ammunition", "context_len": 77}}}, "expenditure": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 74, "context": "Knights and slaves , men , women , and children , and even the inmates of the convents and nunneries , aided in the work , and when at last the outer wall fell , and the Turks thought that success was at hand , the pasha saw with astonishment and dismay that entry to the city was still barred by a work as formidable as that which he had destroyed at an enormous expenditure of ammunition", "context_len": 77}}}, "assassination": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "Filelfo , who was greatly attached to D'Aubusson , saw by their manner that they wished to engage him in some intrigue , and , feigning great resentment and anger at his disgrace , led them on until they divulged the entire plot for D'Aubusson 's assassination , and made brilliant offers to him if he would afford them facilities for carrying it out , producing , in proof of their power to do so , a letter of the pasha , authorising them to make such promises in his name", "context_len": 91}}}, "deserters": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "To this end he employed two men , one a Dalmatian , the other an Albanian ; these presented themselves before the walls as deserters , and as there was no reason for suspecting their tale , they were admitted within the gates , and welcomed as having escaped from enforced service", "context_len": 52}}}, "credited": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "They soon spread the tale of the speedy coming of the sultan with vast reinforcements , and as the pasha had on the previous day caused salutes to be fired , and other demonstrations to be made , the news was readily credited , and caused the greatest dismay among the defenders", "context_len": 52}}}, "negotiations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "He had been at first incensed at the indignity which had been put upon his sister by Philip 's treatment of her ; but Philip now tried to appease his anger , also , by friendly negotiations and messages", "context_len": 39}}}, "secretaries": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "He had no means of detecting the imposture , for it was very customary in those days for letters to be written by secretaries , and to be authenticated solely by the seal", "context_len": 33}}}, "imperilled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "Sir Knights , he said , I have heard from my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "advocated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 67, "context": "my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction , you shall meet with the fate you so justly deserve", "context_len": 128}}}, "intrigues": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 112, "context": "my secretary your opinions in the matter of a surrender , and since you are in such terror of the Moslem sultan , you have my full permission to leave the town ; and , more than that , I will myself secure your safe departure , which might be imperilled if your comrades or even the inhabitants of the town came to learn that you had advocated surrender ; but , he went on , changing his tone from that of sarcasm to sternness , if you remain with us , see that the word surrender never again passes your lips , and be assured that , should you continue your intrigues , in that direction , you shall meet with the fate you so justly deserve", "context_len": 128}}}, "renewal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Feeling that the lesson had been sufficiently severe , and that henceforth there would be no renewal of intrigues for a surrender , D'Aubusson forgave them , and promised them a place in the van when next the Moslems attacked", "context_len": 40}}}, "intrigue": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Filelfo , who was greatly attached to D'Aubusson , saw by their manner that they wished to engage him in some intrigue , and , feigning great resentment and anger at his disgrace , led them on until they divulged the entire plot for D'Aubusson 's assassination , and made brilliant offers to him if he would afford them facilities for carrying it out , producing , in proof of their power to do so , a letter of the pasha , authorising them to make such promises in his name", "context_len": 91}}}, "feigning": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 17, "context": "The hostess who brought Dick 's bowl of onion broth at this moment destroyed his chance of feigning sleep , and he had to resign himself to endure his companion 's conversation which flowed on , garnished with oaths and cant phrases in three or four different languages , without any interruption , till by an unguarded movement Richard exposed his face to the light of the fire , and the stranger stared a moment , and then sprang up exclaiming , `` Body o ' me if it be not Measter Dick himself ! ''", "context_len": 96}}}, "facilities": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 59, "context": "Filelfo , who was greatly attached to D'Aubusson , saw by their manner that they wished to engage him in some intrigue , and , feigning great resentment and anger at his disgrace , led them on until they divulged the entire plot for D'Aubusson 's assassination , and made brilliant offers to him if he would afford them facilities for carrying it out , producing , in proof of their power to do so , a letter of the pasha , authorising them to make such promises in his name", "context_len": 91}}}, "watchdogs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "As it seems probable , Sir Fabricius , he said , after the first greetings to the knight , that the Turks will favour you with another visit , I have brought you five watchdogs", "context_len": 35}}}, "assign": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "My conclusions there stated as to the probable date of the original work have since been completely confirmed by the fact that experts assign Galland 's original ( imperfect ) copy of the Arabic text to the latter part of the fourteenth century , on the evidence of the handwriting , etc", "context_len": 52}}}, "lingo": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "Ay , ay , sir , I have been in the water , and that Italian commander told me to come straight up here to tell the grand master all about the story ; and right glad am I to have met you , for I should have made but a poor fist of it alone ; I do n't know more of their lingo than just to talk a few words of it", "context_len": 74}}}, "fathoms": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "It looked a very strange craft , and as I saw by the way it was bearing that it would come ashore about five or six fathoms from where I was sitting , I slid quietly off the rock , put my sword down by me handy for action , and waited", "context_len": 52}}}, "witchcraft": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "I was glad to see him , for I had begun to think that there was witchcraft in the thing moving along by itself , but I can tell you I was savage with myself for not having guessed there was a man swimming behind and pushing it on", "context_len": 49}}}, "unknotted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "I unknotted the rope from it and carried it ashore ; then it struck me that the Turks might take it into their heads to give a pull on it in the morning , and if they did ; they would find out that their game , whatever it was , had been found out ; so I got hold of a stone of about twenty pound weight , and fastened the rope 's end round it", "context_len": 77}}}, "solves": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Bob Dexter , while on a vacation , captures a band of criminals , and solves a mystery in which millions of dollars in gems and jewelry had been stolen", "context_len": 30}}}, "crowns": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "They , at any rate , would not be the ones to acquaint them with the fact that even queens sometimes grow to be grandmothers , taking more comfort in rocking-chairs than thrones , vastly preferring lace caps to crowns , and behaving in general like other dear grandmothers the world over", "context_len": 52}}}, "munitions": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "Gangs of men , as usual , worked in the breach ; but , as it was deemed advisable that there should be no outward show of activity that would lead the Turks to suspect that their design had been discovered , neither reinforcements of men nor munitions were sent along the mole ; everything being taken out by boats , which , rowing closely along under the wall , were hidden from the view of the Turks", "context_len": 78}}}, "cauldrons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "sheaves": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "darts": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 30, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "[ Suddenly darts towards Charles and pulls back his hood", "context_len": 10}}}, "spikes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "staves": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "Barrels of Greek fire and pitch , cauldrons for heating the latter , a store of firewood , great balls of cotton steeped in oil and turpentine , sheaves of darts , spikes on short staves , that were , after darkness fell , to be thrust in among the fallen masonry to form a chevaux-de-frise -- these , and all other matters that the ingenuity of the defenders could suggest , were landed at the water gate of the fort , while the garrison was strengthened by the addition of a large number of knights", "context_len": 96}}}, "disconcerted": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "The king was Charles the Sixth of France , explained the Queen , who was not going to have her little story-teller disconcerted if she could help it ; but Marie-Celeste confessed with perfect honesty , I am afraid I had forgotten that name ; and Albert felt ashamed of himself , and confided in a whisper to Miss Belmore dat he dessed he would n't be so mean aden", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It certainly did , for the disconcerted freshman and the pretty girls soon left the hall", "context_len": 16}}}, "persevered": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "\u2019 It is of infinitely greater importance to note with what absolute self-devotion Miss Tucker entered into the toil , with what resolution she persevered in the face of obstacles , with what eagerness she did the very utmost within her power", "context_len": 42}}}, "destructive": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "A cannon was at once fired to give notice to the other batteries to be in readiness , and as soon as the dark mass of boats was made out the guns of the fort opened a destructive fire upon them , and a moment later were seconded by those from the fortress ; these , however , were at present being fired almost at random , as the Turkish boats could not be made out at that distance", "context_len": 79}}}, "random": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 65, "context": "A cannon was at once fired to give notice to the other batteries to be in readiness , and as soon as the dark mass of boats was made out the guns of the fort opened a destructive fire upon them , and a moment later were seconded by those from the fortress ; these , however , were at present being fired almost at random , as the Turkish boats could not be made out at that distance", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Random just as I left the office to come after you", "context_len": 11}}}, "surmount": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The short ladders that they had brought with them enabled them to surmount the escarpments so laboriously made , and with loud shouts of Allah ! they flung themselves upon the defenders on the crest of the breach", "context_len": 38}}}, "escarpments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The short ladders that they had brought with them enabled them to surmount the escarpments so laboriously made , and with loud shouts of Allah ! they flung themselves upon the defenders on the crest of the breach", "context_len": 38}}}, "battleaxes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The knights were ranged three deep ; those in the front were armed with swords and battleaxes , while those in the other two lines thrust their spears out between the swordsmen , covering them with a hedge of steel points", "context_len": 41}}}, "swordsmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The knights were ranged three deep ; those in the front were armed with swords and battleaxes , while those in the other two lines thrust their spears out between the swordsmen , covering them with a hedge of steel points", "context_len": 41}}}, "gaps": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "Yet the Author has distinctly felt that fiction must always , in such cases , be subordinate to truth , and that it is only legitimately used as a vehicle of instruction when it fills up the gaps in the outline , without contradicting them in any respect , or interfering with their due order and sequence", "context_len": 57}}}, "convoyed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "They were convoyed by the two galleys , each with a full complement of knights , and these soon became engaged in a fierce fight with the Turkish vessels that bore down to arrest the course of the fire ships", "context_len": 40}}}, "intact": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 42, "context": "Several of the Turkish galleys , in their efforts to arrest the approach of the fire ships towards the bridge , became themselves involved in the flames ; but they were so far successful that when daylight broke the bridge was still intact and the combat at the breach continued to rage with determination and fury on both sides", "context_len": 59}}}, "animating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "The Turks there were led by a brave young prince named Ibrahim , a near relative of the sultan , with whom he was a great favourite , and he was ever in the front line of the assailants , his splendid bravery animating the soldiers to continue their efforts", "context_len": 50}}}, "concentrating": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "As the daylight broadened out , however , the light enabled the Christian gunners to aim with far greater accuracy than had before been possible , and , concentrating their fire upon the bridge , across which reinforcements continued to press to the support of the assailants , they succeeded in sinking so many of the boats that it was no longer passable", "context_len": 63}}}, "debris": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 23, "context": "Next he started to carry outside and pile neatly at the blind end of the building all the boards , boxes and other debris littering up the room , swept it , and selected two packing cases and nailed them up into a convenient impromptu desk , manufactured a bench seat out of some loose boards , set his pen , ink and paper in order , and felt quite ready for business", "context_len": 73}}}, "retrenchment": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 74, "context": "At the end of that time he roused himself from his stupor of grief and disappointment , and , abandoning the idea of any further attack upon the point that had cost him so dearly , he ordered the troops to move round and renew the attack upon the wall in front of the Jews ' quarter , and commence the construction of a battery on the edge of the great ditch facing the retrenchment behind the breach before effected", "context_len": 80}}}, "woodwork": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 6, "context": "The knights at once set the woodwork of the battery on fire , hurled the guns down into the ditch , and then returned triumphantly into the town , the dashing feat completely reinstating them in the good opinion of the grand master and their comrades", "context_len": 46}}}, "reinstating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The knights at once set the woodwork of the battery on fire , hurled the guns down into the ditch , and then returned triumphantly into the town , the dashing feat completely reinstating them in the good opinion of the grand master and their comrades", "context_len": 46}}}, "ejected": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "The incident showed the pasha that he must neglect no precautions , and , accordingly , he commenced his works at a distance from the walls , and pushed his approaches regularly forward until he again established a battery on the site of that from which his troops had been so unceremoniously ejected", "context_len": 53}}}, "harassed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "Like the Apalachis , the tribe of the heathen Taouachas had quitted the Spanish territory for being harassed by the Alibamu , and fled southwest to the French , who settled them on Mobile river , one league above the Apalachis ( 1710 ; in Margry V , 485-487 )", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "At the same moment eight cannon-balls , five at least of which hit him , were thrown at the harassed defender , whose helmet was now full of sand and water", "context_len": 31}}}, "numerical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "While forming the approaches , the workmen had been constantly harassed by the fire from the guns on the walls , suffering considerable loss of life ; but their numerical superiority was so vast that the loss in no way affected the plans of the pasha", "context_len": 46}}}, "mining": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "As soon as the battery was completed , gangs of men , accustomed to mining operations , set to work in its rear to drive sloping passages downwards , opening into the face of the great cutting , and through these vast quantities of earth and stones were poured , so as to afford a passage across it , the depth being largely diminished by the great pile of rubbish that had already fallen from the breached wall", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 16, "context": "One of the greatest engineering feats of the Great War -- in fact , the greatest mining operation in the history of the world 's battles -- had been successfully carried out , a task compared with which the great mine of Beaumont Hamel paled into insignificance", "context_len": 47}}}, "extorted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "Alexander considered it somewhat uncertain whether the people of all these states and cities would be disposed to transfer readily , to so youthful a prince as he , the high commission which his father , a very powerful monarch and soldier , had extorted from them with so much difficulty", "context_len": 51}}}, "counteract": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "To those friends who thought he lost caste thereby he had always the same argument -- that he saw no reason why the cultivation of fields should counteract the habits of refinement and good breeding to which he had been reared ; that in the colonies educated gentlemen set to work to labour with their hands , and are thought none the worse of : so why not in England , where land is good and markets are plentiful , especially when it involved the keeping of a fine old property which had been in the family for so many hundreds of years ?", "context_len": 104}}}, "catapult": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 49, "context": "Grimly determined to make it warm for them when they did close with him , the defender sprang on to the roof , and , regardless of the fact that he was exposing himself recklessly , took up his stand by the flagstaff , and , throwing down his catapult , whirled his lasso wildly round his head", "context_len": 58}}}, "galleries": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "It made Peggy quite sad to think that the little creatures were mostly bought for the mines , and that , once broken and trained , they would never see daylight again , but spend their lives drawing trucks up and down the low galleries underground , having said good-bye to their native moorlands for ever", "context_len": 56}}}, "exits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "At the same time galleries were driven below the breach , opening into the ditch , where their exits were concealed by masses of rubbish", "context_len": 25}}}, "diminishing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "He was quite a hundred yards from him yet ; but rapidly diminishing the distance now , for the boat seemed to tear along ; but Harry 's heart sank lower and lower , and the chilly feeling of despair grew more strong as , just when he had reduced the distance to about fifty yards , he saw a hand appear for a moment above the water , and then disappear , leaving the glistening surface perfectly blank", "context_len": 79}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Malcolm was reluctantly obliged to admit that the probability of rescue from that direction was of a diminishing nature", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "And then man and animal plunged into the night and disappeared , though the fast diminishing thumping of hoofs was heard for some seconds later", "context_len": 25}}}, "defeats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "His former defeats had , however , taught him that success could not be always calculated upon , however good its prospect might appear ; and although he had no real hope that the defenders would yield , he sent a formal summons for them to do so", "context_len": 48}}}, "impaled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Stakes , on which the knights , when taken prisoners , were to be impaled , were prepared and sharpened , and each soldier carried a coil of rope with which to secure his captive", "context_len": 35}}}, "feints": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "I \u2019 ll help you soak him ! added Jethro , bounding to the side of his master , putting up his big hands , see-sawing with them , ducking his head , and making several feints from a safe distance", "context_len": 41}}}, "parapet": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 16, "context": "Traces of poison gas lurked in the traverses , pungent fumes from bursting shells wafted over parapet and parados , while the report , passed on from various successive occupants of this section of the line , that a dozen dead Huns had been buried under the floor of the support trench -- the old first-line trench of a Prussian regiment -- seemed to find definite confirmation", "context_len": 67}}}, "couch": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 64, "context": "was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant , of course , that I was to die by some exquisite refinement of torture , the nature of which would probably be", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "Seeing that I was utterly exhausted , he allowed me to lie down in front of his oven , and , in spite of the hardness of my couch , I slept soundly till midday , when I was aroused by Greville Drake and some of the late crew of the Gannet , who were being entertained in the town till they could be conveyed to their homes", "context_len": 68}}}, "instantaneous": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "The Church too frequently scratches the hard ground with an impatient hand , drops in a few seeds , and immediately breaks into lamentations , because no instantaneous harvest springs forth", "context_len": 31}}}, "contending": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Earth here is so fair , with bold crags draperied with the richest foliage , that one could imagine her contending for the palm with water ; but water carries the victory at Niagara ; Earth but serves to frame and set off her magnificence", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "As he had no family , there was , of course , no direct heir to the crown , and , in case of any misfortune happening by which his life should be lost , Macedon would become at once the prey of contending factions , which would immediately arise , each presenting its own candidate for the vacant throne", "context_len": 60}}}, "mace": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "I am an Historian , for I have been discussing old dates ; a Merchant , for I add plum to plum ; a Lawyer , for I have opened many a case ; a Lord Mayor , for the mace is before me ; and a Navigator , for I am led to seize and gulf ! What if I were to stay here altogether , or set up a new company with my fair hostesses ? Miss Ratty is cut out for a tragedy Queen", "context_len": 87}}}, "clashed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "Scimitars clashed upon his helmet and armour ; but at every blow he struck a Turk fell , and for each foot he gained a knight sprang on to the wall and joined him", "context_len": 34}}}, "separating": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "On one occasion , after he had spent a whole night in drinking and carousing , the guests , when the usual time arrived for separating , proposed that , instead of this , they should begin anew , and commence a second banquet at the end of the first", "context_len": 50}}}, "isolating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Then the chosen band , separating from the rest , flung themselves upon the grand master , the suddenness and fury of their attack isolating him and Gervaise from the knights around", "context_len": 32}}}, "combine": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "More than one who tried to spring into close quarters fell cleft to the chin , and , ere his assailants could combine for a general rush , a body of knights , who had just beaten off their assailants , fell upon the ranks of the janissaries with a force and fury there was no withstanding , and the chosen troops of the sultan for the first time broke and fled", "context_len": 72}}}, "bleeding": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 27, "context": "I remember that I was shoeless , along with the greater part of my command , though the weather was bitter cold , and my feet were bleeding , and yet when I heard that trumpet voice , ordering us from the wagons to make one more stand , I never thought of my feet", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 22, "context": "He entered , knelt in a mental attitude easier to imagine than describe , but felt some heavenly dew fall upon his bleeding wounds ; he left without waiting to speak to any one at the conclusion of the service , and was crossing the quadrangle to the palace which occupied a portion of the site of modern Christ Church , when a heavy hand was laid upon his shoulder", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "All the imperfections of his character now faded away ; he seemed so brave , yet so loving , so invincible in combat , yet so gentle and forgiving , as he had shown in forgiving even -- even -- even -- said Alfgar to his own wounded bleeding heart -- even in forgiving his murderer", "context_len": 56}}}, "hewing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "The whole of the defenders of the breach now fell upon the rear of the struggling mass , hewing them down almost without resistance , while the cannon from the walls and towers kept up an unceasing fire until the last survivors of what had become a massacre , succeeded in gaining their works beyond the ditch , and fled to their camp", "context_len": 63}}}, "unceasing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "The whole of the defenders of the breach now fell upon the rear of the struggling mass , hewing them down almost without resistance , while the cannon from the walls and towers kept up an unceasing fire until the last survivors of what had become a massacre , succeeded in gaining their works beyond the ditch , and fled to their camp", "context_len": 63}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "The blankets were dry and comforting , the food really appetizing , and , having made a satisfying meal , the brothers slept soundly after forty hours of unceasing vigilance", "context_len": 30}}}, "massacre": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 86, "context": "The defence , though even more obstinate and desperate than the first , was attended with less success , for after inflicting enormous losses upon the great army , commanded by the Sultan Solyman himself , the town was forced to yield ; for although the Grand Master L'Isle Adam , and most of his knights , would have preferred to bury themselves beneath the ruins rather than yield , they were deterred from doing so , by the knowledge that it would have entailed the massacre of the whole of the inhabitants , who had throughout the siege fought valiantly in the defence of the town", "context_len": 107}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "There might be a dozen , a score , or half a hundred who were formulating if they had not already formulated a plan to surprise and massacre every one of the whites", "context_len": 33}}}, "postern": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "From every gateway and postern the knights now poured out , and , gathering together , advanced to the attack of St", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Then pass out by the postern gate , on the angle nearest the church ; I fear the danger is great , but he must be told that the foe is near , or he may fall into their hands", "context_len": 41}}}, "heartbroken": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The greater portion of the disorganised troops had made no pause at their camp , but had continued their headlong flight to the harbour , where their ships were moored , Paleologus himself , heartbroken and despairing at his failure , sharing their flight", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "And there , two heartbroken ladies , a mother and a daughter , came often to pray , not only for the soul of the departed king , but also for the discovery of his murderers and the clearing of the innocent , for neither Hilda nor Ethelgiva for one moment doubted the spotless innocence of Alfgar", "context_len": 57}}}, "victors": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "The camp , with all its rich booty and the great banner of the pasha , fell into the hands of the victors , who , satisfied with their success , and exhausted by their efforts , made no attempt to follow the flying foe , or to hinder their embarkation ; for even now the Turks , enormously outnumbering them as they did , might be driven by despair to a resistance so desperate as once again to turn the tide of victory", "context_len": 84}}}, "embarkation": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 50, "context": "The camp , with all its rich booty and the great banner of the pasha , fell into the hands of the victors , who , satisfied with their success , and exhausted by their efforts , made no attempt to follow the flying foe , or to hinder their embarkation ; for even now the Turks , enormously outnumbering them as they did , might be driven by despair to a resistance so desperate as once again to turn the tide of victory", "context_len": 84}}}, "outnumbering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "The camp , with all its rich booty and the great banner of the pasha , fell into the hands of the victors , who , satisfied with their success , and exhausted by their efforts , made no attempt to follow the flying foe , or to hinder their embarkation ; for even now the Turks , enormously outnumbering them as they did , might be driven by despair to a resistance so desperate as once again to turn the tide of victory", "context_len": 84}}}, "tending": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "Seabrooke went about the business of the day with all his accustomed regularity and precision , but with a sort of defiant and I-am-going-to-stick-to-it air about him which in itself incited the other boys to covert thrusts and innuendoes tending to throw distrust upon his version of the story and to make known their thorough sympathy with Percy , not only for his loss , but also for the aspersions cast upon him by the young pupil-teacher", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In defiance of the Puritan regulations against anything tending towards the lost cause , my father , though ruined by confiscations and sequestration , endeavoured to maintain the appearance of a careless and social demeanour , ever cherishing a hope that each day seemed nearer fulfilment", "context_len": 46}}}, "faintness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "This much I had heard from report , and now , in spite of my cramped position , and faintness from want of food , I could not help looking with interest on the shattered walls , which still showed the black marks of the powder , though now , after a lapse of twenty years , their barrenness was beginning to be hidden by a kindly garb of ivy", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Suddenly Felgate staggered , as if seized with faintness , and fell on his hands and knees", "context_len": 17}}}, "marvels": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 60, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "dangerously": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 67, "context": "Now it was all up-hill , and the wind blowing such a gale that she had need to bend way over , holding firmly to her sailor hat the while , to make any headway whatever ; and now in a trice it was very much down-hill indeed , and the little knees had to be stiffly braced to prevent her ladyship from bowling along at a dangerously rapid pace", "context_len": 70}}}, "gorget": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "The term im\u00fakfa is Hitchiti , for ( 1 ) shell ; ( 2 ) metallic ornament of concave shape ; Hawkins interprets the name by `` gorget made of a conch", "context_len": 32}}}, "assoil": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "May God assoil his soul ! Gervaise said earnestly", "context_len": 9}}}, "courtier": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Had he remained at home , and turned courtier , instead of entering the Order , he might have died honoured , without any one ever coming to doubt his courage", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` I wonder , '' he cried , `` if you can remember how , a many years ago , Sir Gyles carried you up to London , and you lay for a week at our house at Highgate ? I had never seen his like ! He seemed to me the very model of the old courtier of the Queen in the ballad ; he was so worshipful an old gentleman , and carried such a train of old servants riding with him", "context_len": 84}}}, "prognostications": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Long before Gervaise was out of hospital , the glad tidings that D'Aubusson would recover , in spite of the prognostications of the leech , spread joy through the city , and at about the same time that Gervaise left the hospital the grand master was able to sit up", "context_len": 50}}}, "potentate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 85, "context": "or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing his victims to the tree with spike nails driven through the hands and feet into the tough timber", "context_len": 128}}}, "caskets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The tents of King Darius and his household were inconceivably splendid , and were filled with gold and silver vessels , caskets , vases , boxes of perfumes , and every imaginable article of luxury and show", "context_len": 37}}}, "temporal": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 64, "context": "So soon as the news of the death of Ethelred travelled abroad , the bishops , abbots , ealdormen , and thanes of southern England , despairing of the cause of the house of Cerdic , met together at Southampton , and renouncing Ethelred and his descendants , elected Canute to be their king , while he swore that both in things spiritual and temporal he would maintain their liberties", "context_len": 70}}}, "gems": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 80, "context": "Dear little pilgrims ! there were jewels in their way such as they did not dream of ; but it was only earnest seeking such as theirs which could find them ; for they lay hidden beneath many a thorn and bramble and unsightly weed ; and they were to be found only by the help of this very jewel of prayer which shone so brightly that its light guided the little feet to the dark places where the hidden gems lay", "context_len": 82}}}, "treasury": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 6, "context": "The genie carried him into a treasury full of all manner of gold and silver and precious jewels past count or reckoning , price or estimation ; then he brought him into another place , where he saw all the requisites of the table , platters and spoons and ewers and basins and cups , of gold and silver , and thence to the kitchen , where he found cooks , [ 476 ] with their cooking-gear and utensils , all on like wise of gold and silver", "context_len": 88}}}, "rust": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 61, "context": "Carefully feeling with both hands , I discovered that I was standing in an angle , and right in the corner my hand came in contact with an object that , on inspection , proved to be a gun ; also , by the smoothness of the barrel I knew that it had recently been in use , there being no rust on the ironwork", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "And because you have no time for reading is not any reason why your thinking powers need rust away", "context_len": 19}}}, "healed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "He was , of course , reproved by his colonel ; but who could be seriously angry with a youngster for such conduct ? So when he was sent back to England to get healed of his wounds , he was made a captain at the early age of twenty-one", "context_len": 50}}}, "taint": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "He said -- and a momentary smile crossed the grand master 's face -- that he thought a change might benefit you also , for he was sure that the air here had scarce recovered from the taint of blood", "context_len": 40}}}, "recruit": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "Really , said Ted at last , able to stand it no longer , and looking pathetically toward Chris , I do n't mean to be inquisitive , but do I understand you that the father of your friend , Marie-Celeste , is coming here to your cottage to recruit from some illness , and that you plan to entertain him by putting him to work on the farm ?", "context_len": 70}}}, "refit": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "With a favourable wind the fleet arrived off the Forelands and thence beat up for the Downs , where we were in a position to meet either the Dutch or the French squadrons ; but off Dover we learned from a fishing boat that Beaufort was seen heading back towards Brest in order to refit some of his ships , so that for the time our chances of smelling powder were very remote", "context_len": 73}}}, "diffident": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "I \u2019 d asked y \u2019 u down to supper with us , but you \u2019 re so kinder backward an \u2019 diffident , that I thought p \u2019 raps y \u2019 u wouldn \u2019 t come , an \u2019 Mamie said she knowed y \u2019 u wouldn \u2019 t", "context_len": 50}}}, "fitness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then , by grace of his own idea of fitness , Harold had added to the other furnishings a Dresden china toilet-set , and in this he was perhaps far wiser than he knew , for is there anything so well calculated to captivate at sight the heart of a dainty little maiden as the mysterious round-topped boxes that compose the dainty outfit of the ideal dressing-table ? Then , to crown it all , a pair of ponies and a basket-phaeton had been purchased for the exclusive use of the guests that were to be", "context_len": 96}}}, "recording": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "Nicholas , you saved the life of the grand master ; and in the order of the day he issued on the morning we left , granting you three months ' leave for the recovery of your wounds , he took the opportunity of recording , in the name of the council and himself , their admiration for the services rendered by you during the siege , and his own gratitude for saving his life when he lay helpless and surrounded by the Moslems -- a testimony of which any knight of Christendom might well feel proud", "context_len": 97}}}, "mobbed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Most of the people will be taking their siesta at present , and we shall get through the streets without being mobbed ; for I can assure you that the mantle of the Order is just at present in such high favour that I had a hard task to wend my way through the streets to my cousin 's house", "context_len": 60}}}, "wend": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Still hurrying me forward , Ama led the way through the grove and along its edge , until we eventually reached a narrow bush path , through which it was necessary to wend our way circumspectly , for it was now as black as a wolf 's mouth , save when an occasional flicker of lightning from the now fast-receding storm momentarily lit up our surroundings", "context_len": 66}}}, "cordiality": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "The countess greeted him with great cordiality ; but Claudia came forward with a timidity that contrasted strangely with the outspoken frankness he remembered in the girl", "context_len": 27}}}, "timidity": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 7, "context": "On the contrary , I felt a timidity that was almost childish ; it was a feeling that carried me away back to my boyhood , when I refused to go into a room where there was a company of little girls", "context_len": 42}}}, "outspoken": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 69, "context": "Despite the fact that he was on the losing side , my father was not backward in declaring his attachment to His Gracious Majesty King Charles II ; and although our neighbours , even the Roundheads , were favourably disposed to him , making allowance for his fiery temper , yet with strangers who passed along the great highway betwixt London Town and Portsmouth , honest Owen \u2019 s outspoken declarations oft led to wordy strife , and on occasions ended in blows", "context_len": 83}}}, "widened": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "Going along , they came to the two great trees which were connected by a common root , making a natural bridge across the brook , which , above them , widened out into a little basin", "context_len": 37}}}, "blaming": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I am not blaming you , my dear Westenra , '' said Jane ; `` only it is very , very hard to go on toiling , toiling all day and almost all night , and to feel at the same time that the thing can not pay , that it can never pay", "context_len": 55}}}, "scoffed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "I had often scoffed at knights of an order like ours wearing ladies ' favours", "context_len": 15}}}, "noblest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "Had you then been what you are now , I might have been foolish enough to think of it in another light , regardless of the fact that you are a rich heiress of one of the noblest families in Italy , and I a knight with no possessions save my sword", "context_len": 52}}}, "impetuously": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 20, "context": "An officer of the guards , who had his position in the procession near the king , was seen advancing impetuously toward him , through the space which separated him from the rest , and , before the spectators had time even to wonder what he was going to do , he stabbed him to the heart", "context_len": 57}}}, "endorse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I will return to Rhodes tomorrow , and ask the grand master to forward to the Pope and endorse my petition , that I may be released from my vows to the Order", "context_len": 33}}}, "wooing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "But I am bound by no vows , and can acknowledge that you have long been the lord of my life , and that so long as you wore the heart I had given you , so long would I listen to the wooing of no other", "context_len": 47}}}, "importune": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "My mother has long known , and knowing also that I am not given to change , has ceased to importune me to listen to other offers", "context_len": 27}}}, "loser": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` You 'd better let me buy your horse , and then if we do n't meet again , or anything happens to it , you wo n't be the loser", "context_len": 31}}}, "provincial": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "I by no means disapprove , D'Aubusson went on , of knights leaving us when they have performed their active service , for in civil life they sometimes have it in their power to render better service to the Order than if passing their lives in the quiet duties of a provincial commandery", "context_len": 53}}}, "recommendation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "My recommendation alone would be sufficient ; but as , moreover , he is himself under an obligation to you , he will do so without hesitation", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` Oh , my dear child , how often I have explained all to you ! There 's Laura Everett , my dear friend Lady Everett 's only daughter ; then there is Annie Millar , whom I do not know anything about -- but she is a friend of Laura 's , and that alone is recommendation enough", "context_len": 59}}}, "incapacitated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "At least half of the raiders had been incapacitated , and among the wounded had been Nascanora himself , and all the unwounded survivors were at that moment rushing pell-mell through the jungle , frantically eager to put as many miles as possible between themselves and the cabin , where the white man \u2019 s magic had been capped by the calling of the jungle beasts to help", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Never again would he use either bayonet or boxing-gloves , for his right arm was totally incapacitated", "context_len": 17}}}, "wedding": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 87, "context": "When the Sultan examined it and saw that it was unfinished , he said , `` Woe is me for thee , O casement , that thou art not perfect ! '' Then , turning to the Vizier , he said to him , `` Knowest thou the reason of the lack of completion of this casement and its lattices ? '' `` O [ 512 ] my lord , '' answered the Vizier , `` methinketh it is because Thy Grace hastened upon Alaeddin with the wedding and he had no time to complete it", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "absolution": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Gervaise was well received at Rome , and the Pope , after reading the grand master 's letter , and learning from him his reason for wishing to leave the Order , without hesitation granted him absolution from his vows", "context_len": 40}}}, "cathedral": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "The huge cathedral church towered above the masses , rude in architecture , yet still impressive in its proportions , while another church , scarcely smaller in its dimensions , rose from the banks lower down the stream , below the bridge , and the wooden steeple of a third was visible above the roofs of the houses in the western part of the city", "context_len": 65}}}, "republics": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "His character was naturally more logical and less high strung than that of his hero ; and as the stern realities of life claimed the attention of the young soldier , the ecstatic glories of his uncle 's visions faded from his mind , his work absorbed and satisfied him , and he forgot to dream of ideal republics , or even of the Celestial City , in the practical interest of helping to conquer and to govern Scotland", "context_len": 79}}}, "attainder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "The defeat of the Turks created such enthusiasm throughout Europe that when the grand prior of England laid before the king letters he had received from the grand master and Sir John Kendall , speaking in the highest terms of the various great services Gervaise had rendered to the Order , Edward granted his request that the act of attainder against Sir Thomas Tresham and his descendants should be reversed and the estates restored to Gervaise", "context_len": 76}}}, "beleaguered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "When , in 1522 , forty-two years after the first siege , Rhodes was again beleaguered , Gervaise , who had , on the death of the countess , become Count of Forli , raised a large body of men-at-arms , and sent them , under the command of his eldest son , to take part in the defence", "context_len": 59}}}, "deterred": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 72, "context": "The defence , though even more obstinate and desperate than the first , was attended with less success , for after inflicting enormous losses upon the great army , commanded by the Sultan Solyman himself , the town was forced to yield ; for although the Grand Master L'Isle Adam , and most of his knights , would have preferred to bury themselves beneath the ruins rather than yield , they were deterred from doing so , by the knowledge that it would have entailed the massacre of the whole of the inhabitants , who had throughout the siege fought valiantly in the defence of the town", "context_len": 107}}}, "entailed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 84, "context": "The defence , though even more obstinate and desperate than the first , was attended with less success , for after inflicting enormous losses upon the great army , commanded by the Sultan Solyman himself , the town was forced to yield ; for although the Grand Master L'Isle Adam , and most of his knights , would have preferred to bury themselves beneath the ruins rather than yield , they were deterred from doing so , by the knowledge that it would have entailed the massacre of the whole of the inhabitants , who had throughout the siege fought valiantly in the defence of the town", "context_len": 107}}}, "stronghold": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "Solyman had suffered such enormous losses that he was glad to grant favourable conditions , and the knights sailed away from the city they had held so long and with such honour , and afterwards established themselves in Malta , where they erected another stronghold , which in the end proved an even more valuable bulwark to Christendom than Rhodes had been", "context_len": 62}}}, "unconquered": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": ", tired , but unconquered , held their wickets", "context_len": 9}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "His spirit seemed unconquered even by the blow which decapitated him , and he drove on", "context_len": 16}}}, "intrepidity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Frontispiece I ran at my father \u2019 s murderer and rained blow after blow upon his head and body They clambered up our sides with the greatest intrepidity The chest is hoisted to the surface", "context_len": 35}}}, "athwart": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 10, "context": "The action began in a strong wind that , blowing athwart the tide , raised such a steep sea that most of our ships were unable to open their lee \u2019 ard lower-deck ports , a misfortune that more than outbalanced our advantage in having the weather gauge", "context_len": 48}}}, "forges": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was dark ere I set out homewards , and on the summit of the hill I stopped to look across the coombe , where flickered the innumerable wood fires of the iron smelters \u2019 forges", "context_len": 36}}}, "fading": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "The light of day was rapidly fading ; a light breeze brought down the remaining leaves from the trees , or whirled them about in all directions ; winter was plainly about to assume the mastery of the scene , as was evident from the clothing the people wore , the thick fur and warm woollen cloaks which covered their light tunics", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 57, "context": "Below the house the meadow sloped down to a river , where a stone bridge led to the village , with its pretty thatched cottages and Norman church , whose square tower stood up like a beacon for the surrounding country ; and away in the distance , tier upon tier , rose the Welsh mountains , fading from green to purple or from purple to misty mauve , till the last were lost in the hazy blue of the sky", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "But when he returned home on flying visits , he found to his dismay that his uncle 's visionary hopes were growing instead of fading ; and from desiring a merely republican England , General Harrison had begun to dream of a theocracy as complete as that of the early Jews , and to look forward to the immediate inauguration of an earthly Reign of the Saints , under the sceptre of Christ Himself , as the Fifth and last of the great monarchies of the world", "context_len": 87}}}, "summers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "I remember that evening well ; its stirring incidents are graven on my memory as if they had happened but yesterday , though nigh on twoscore and ten winters and summers have passed over my head since the eventful year of which I write", "context_len": 44}}}, "sparely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Although only seventeen years of age , he was a full inch over six feet in height , and , although broad across the shoulders , was sparely built yet supple of frame", "context_len": 33}}}, "harmonized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Tall but sparely built , his frame betokened a strength of body that harmonized with the determination of character that made itself known by the glance of his steel-coloured eyes", "context_len": 30}}}, "outmatching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Report says that when he came to Rake to settle down , some twelve or thirteen years back -- I being but an infant in arms , -- he did gain a lasting reputation by outmatching one Caleb James , a notorious bully , at his own game , breaking his pate with his own staff on the roadside hard by Milland Church", "context_len": 63}}}, "pate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "Report says that when he came to Rake to settle down , some twelve or thirteen years back -- I being but an infant in arms , -- he did gain a lasting reputation by outmatching one Caleb James , a notorious bully , at his own game , breaking his pate with his own staff on the roadside hard by Milland Church", "context_len": 63}}}, "hardiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Moreover , as proof of his hardiness , is there not the testimony of the worthy Master Hugh Salesbury , the chirurgeon of Lyss -- the same whose son fell in Torrington \u2019 s action off Beachy Head , -- to the effect that though practice was slack around Lyss , yet he perforce would have to give up if none were better patients than honest Owen Wentworth", "context_len": 68}}}, "chirurgeon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Moreover , as proof of his hardiness , is there not the testimony of the worthy Master Hugh Salesbury , the chirurgeon of Lyss -- the same whose son fell in Torrington \u2019 s action off Beachy Head , -- to the effect that though practice was slack around Lyss , yet he perforce would have to give up if none were better patients than honest Owen Wentworth", "context_len": 68}}}, "perforce": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "Moreover , as proof of his hardiness , is there not the testimony of the worthy Master Hugh Salesbury , the chirurgeon of Lyss -- the same whose son fell in Torrington \u2019 s action off Beachy Head , -- to the effect that though practice was slack around Lyss , yet he perforce would have to give up if none were better patients than honest Owen Wentworth", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Here a thought occurred to me to spring from my hiding place and make a dash for freedom , but the weight of the tarpaulin , which was securely lashed down , prevented me ; so I was perforce obliged to remain , though firmly resolved to free myself at the first favourable opportunity", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "At the last minute Father had been obliged to send his regrets and apologies , for the veterinary surgeon had arrived to doctor a sick horse , and he could not possibly leave the stables , so the tea-party must perforce begin without him , for the days were growing short now , and there was no time to spare", "context_len": 60}}}, "patients": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 62, "context": "This of course was bad , for I was in no position to make any man my enemy , much less an individual of such power and influence as Mafuta ; nevertheless I continued to prescribe for all who came to me , trusting that if ever it should come to a struggle between Mafuta and myself , the gratitude of my patients would suffice to turn the scale in my favour", "context_len": 72}}}, "attachment": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 19, "context": "Despite the fact that he was on the losing side , my father was not backward in declaring his attachment to His Gracious Majesty King Charles II ; and although our neighbours , even the Roundheads , were favourably disposed to him , making allowance for his fiery temper , yet with strangers who passed along the great highway betwixt London Town and Portsmouth , honest Owen \u2019 s outspoken declarations oft led to wordy strife , and on occasions ended in blows", "context_len": 83}}}, "declarations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 70, "context": "Despite the fact that he was on the losing side , my father was not backward in declaring his attachment to His Gracious Majesty King Charles II ; and although our neighbours , even the Roundheads , were favourably disposed to him , making allowance for his fiery temper , yet with strangers who passed along the great highway betwixt London Town and Portsmouth , honest Owen \u2019 s outspoken declarations oft led to wordy strife , and on occasions ended in blows", "context_len": 83}}}, "wordy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 74, "context": "Despite the fact that he was on the losing side , my father was not backward in declaring his attachment to His Gracious Majesty King Charles II ; and although our neighbours , even the Roundheads , were favourably disposed to him , making allowance for his fiery temper , yet with strangers who passed along the great highway betwixt London Town and Portsmouth , honest Owen \u2019 s outspoken declarations oft led to wordy strife , and on occasions ended in blows", "context_len": 83}}}, "confiscations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "In defiance of the Puritan regulations against anything tending towards the lost cause , my father , though ruined by confiscations and sequestration , endeavoured to maintain the appearance of a careless and social demeanour , ever cherishing a hope that each day seemed nearer fulfilment", "context_len": 46}}}, "sequestration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "In defiance of the Puritan regulations against anything tending towards the lost cause , my father , though ruined by confiscations and sequestration , endeavoured to maintain the appearance of a careless and social demeanour , ever cherishing a hope that each day seemed nearer fulfilment", "context_len": 46}}}, "cherishing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "In defiance of the Puritan regulations against anything tending towards the lost cause , my father , though ruined by confiscations and sequestration , endeavoured to maintain the appearance of a careless and social demeanour , ever cherishing a hope that each day seemed nearer fulfilment", "context_len": 46}}}, "greyish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "He still retained his flowing lovelocks , while the lower part of his weather-worn face was adorned by a greyish beard of Van Dyck cut , which failed to hide a portion of a long , whitish scar that extended from his left eyebrow to his cheek bone -- the legacy of a pike-thrust in the sanguinary encounter of Cropredy Bridge", "context_len": 61}}}, "eyebrow": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "He still retained his flowing lovelocks , while the lower part of his weather-worn face was adorned by a greyish beard of Van Dyck cut , which failed to hide a portion of a long , whitish scar that extended from his left eyebrow to his cheek bone -- the legacy of a pike-thrust in the sanguinary encounter of Cropredy Bridge", "context_len": 61}}}, "legacy": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 50, "context": "He still retained his flowing lovelocks , while the lower part of his weather-worn face was adorned by a greyish beard of Van Dyck cut , which failed to hide a portion of a long , whitish scar that extended from his left eyebrow to his cheek bone -- the legacy of a pike-thrust in the sanguinary encounter of Cropredy Bridge", "context_len": 61}}}, "sanguinary": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 56, "context": "He still retained his flowing lovelocks , while the lower part of his weather-worn face was adorned by a greyish beard of Van Dyck cut , which failed to hide a portion of a long , whitish scar that extended from his left eyebrow to his cheek bone -- the legacy of a pike-thrust in the sanguinary encounter of Cropredy Bridge", "context_len": 61}}}, "hanger": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "He was dressed in a dark-blue suit , relieved by a deep collar of Mechlin lace , while , on account of the severity of the weather , he was further attired in a long cloak that barely concealed the end of a short hanger -- a necessary weapon in these troublous times", "context_len": 53}}}, "troublous": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` The late Owen Wentworth , by reason of his excessive loyalty to His Majesty the King , and of his careful thought for the proper upbringing of his son during the past troublous times , did part with a portion of his family estates and personal property in order to furnish His Majesty with such aid as all loyal cavaliers were bound by their duty to give", "context_len": 68}}}, "dags": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I also knew that he carried two long dags , or Scottish pistols , yet of these there was no outward sign", "context_len": 22}}}, "chapman": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "I told him I was waiting for the chapman who travelled between Winton , Petersfield , and Portsmouth , and at the same time ordered a meal , for the morning ride had made me hungry", "context_len": 36}}}, "flints": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Close to my feet was a large rabbit hole , and knowing from past experience that these animals frequently throw up flints and other stones from their burrows , I plunged my hand into the newly excavated earth , and to my delight found a large jagged flint , and soon after my armoury consisted of five good-sized stones", "context_len": 59}}}, "hostelry": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "As we entered the doorway of the `` Flying Bull '' -- an old hostelry that has sheltered all sorts and conditions of men , from kings and queens even to the arch-traitor Old Noll himself , and the sign of which , painted by a limner who had learned his art in the time of the last crusade , had swung in the breeze for nigh on four hundred years -- we were greeted with a chorus of welcome from the score or so of persons assembled in the large stone-flagged common room", "context_len": 94}}}, "queens": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 28, "context": "As we entered the doorway of the `` Flying Bull '' -- an old hostelry that has sheltered all sorts and conditions of men , from kings and queens even to the arch-traitor Old Noll himself , and the sign of which , painted by a limner who had learned his art in the time of the last crusade , had swung in the breeze for nigh on four hundred years -- we were greeted with a chorus of welcome from the score or so of persons assembled in the large stone-flagged common room", "context_len": 94}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "They , at any rate , would not be the ones to acquaint them with the fact that even queens sometimes grow to be grandmothers , taking more comfort in rocking-chairs than thrones , vastly preferring lace caps to crowns , and behaving in general like other dear grandmothers the world over", "context_len": 52}}}, "limner": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "As we entered the doorway of the `` Flying Bull '' -- an old hostelry that has sheltered all sorts and conditions of men , from kings and queens even to the arch-traitor Old Noll himself , and the sign of which , painted by a limner who had learned his art in the time of the last crusade , had swung in the breeze for nigh on four hundred years -- we were greeted with a chorus of welcome from the score or so of persons assembled in the large stone-flagged common room", "context_len": 94}}}, "crusade": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "As we entered the doorway of the `` Flying Bull '' -- an old hostelry that has sheltered all sorts and conditions of men , from kings and queens even to the arch-traitor Old Noll himself , and the sign of which , painted by a limner who had learned his art in the time of the last crusade , had swung in the breeze for nigh on four hundred years -- we were greeted with a chorus of welcome from the score or so of persons assembled in the large stone-flagged common room", "context_len": 94}}}, "barley": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}}}, "huckstering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Man , '' retorted another , `` doth thy reasoning not rise above the price of petty huckstering , Obadiah Blow-the-trumpet-in-Zion ? Heed him not , good Master Wentworth", "context_len": 30}}}, "sided": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` None , though rumour hath it that the fleet at Portsmouth hath sided with Monk , and that John Tippets , the mayor , hath called out the train bands and manned the ordnance on the Platform and the Square Tower", "context_len": 42}}}, "mayor": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` None , though rumour hath it that the fleet at Portsmouth hath sided with Monk , and that John Tippets , the mayor , hath called out the train bands and manned the ordnance on the Platform and the Square Tower", "context_len": 42}}}, "marketplace": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "Meanwhile the news that the Duke of Beaufort \u2019 s squadron was really in the Channel had spread abroad , causing the timid inhabitants of Rye to make preparations for a hurried exodus , while the trainbands were called out by beat of drum , and had assembled in the marketplace", "context_len": 51}}}, "forfend": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "God forfend that this land be not again drenched in blood ! ''", "context_len": 13}}}, "pitted": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "In a measure he had an advantage , wearing his breastplate ; yet as the scimitar is rarely used save for cutting , the armour did not serve him as readily as it would have done if he had been pitted against a man armed with a pointed sword", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "His fears thrown to the wind , and the exhilarating sensation of unfettered action surging through his veins , Malcolm found himself scrambling over sand-bags and leaping into the pitted No Man 's Land", "context_len": 34}}}, "smallpox": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Young maaster , '' he replied , `` for certain prevention of agues , fever , smallpox , plague , and all divers illnesses , for certain proof against the evil eye , there is nowt that can compare with the little finger of a murderer", "context_len": 47}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` We have n't been exposed to smallpox , '' went on Tom , `` but we are both suffering from a severe attack of Uncleitis , so if you do n't want to catch it you 'd better keep away from us", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "jerkin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He wore a buff-coloured jerkin , opened at the neck for comfort \u2019 s sake , and frayed and soiled from the wearing of armour , his breast- and back-plates of dull steel having been removed", "context_len": 36}}}, "broadsword": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Now , sir , I warrant you know by experience that a broadsword is a good enough thing to have in your fist on the field of battle ; but , the war over , 't is neither fit for a lady 's chamber nor for a courtier 's duello ; 't is but a commodity of rusty iron to fling in the lumber-room", "context_len": 65}}}, "petronel": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "I shut my eyes and screamed in terror , and on opening them I saw -- oh , merciful Heaven ! -- a convulsive form lying in the road , while the Roundhead stood watching me intently , the smoke from his petronel hanging round like a pall , and slowly ascending in the chill night air", "context_len": 57}}}, "bandolier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "These , together with a steel helmet with metal guards , and a heavy broadsword , lay on the settle within arm \u2019 s length , while a petronel and a well-weighted bandolier hung across the back of a chair on which the man \u2019 s feet , encased in long Spanish boots , rested", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "An hour later they returned , and rolling our blankets they fastened them bandolier fashion across one shoulder and under the other", "context_len": 22}}}, "trooper": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "In a moment a party of men had run from the cover of the brushwood , freed the captive , and , after hamstringing the trooper \u2019 s horse , had made their escape to the depths of the forest before the rest of the escort could realize what had occurred", "context_len": 51}}}, "unsay": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Either unsay those words or else swallow them ! ''", "context_len": 11}}}, "tankards": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 78, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}}}, "clattered": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "The horses \u2019 hoofs echoed under the dark gateway and clattered across the drawbridge , the town of Portsmouth was left behind , and the dense cluster of timbered and red-tiled houses gave place to verdant fields and clumps of tall trees that even now were beginning to burst into leaf", "context_len": 51}}}, "choler": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "For my own part , I remained by my father \u2019 s side , unable to take my eyes off his antagonist , and , at the same time , knowing that my father in his choler would brook no interference from me", "context_len": 43}}}, "spiced": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Now , sirrah , on thy life , hasten ! Provender for my beast ; a cup of spiced ale for myself", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then they tried several other pretty dances , and spiced them with much laughter", "context_len": 14}}}, "dispatch": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "Bart hustled the various packages up for sale and disposition with briskness and dispatch , and Darry was more than busy keeping tab on his record book and piling the cash into the tin box", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Now , Sedgewyke , I pray you dispatch that horse and let us hasten on , unless we wish to be benighted on the highway", "context_len": 25}}}, "stools": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 62, "context": "It looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and", "context_len": 128}}}, "thereon": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 44, "context": "Yet there was a wondrous beauty still lingering over them ; they seemed etherialised -- as if an angel 's smile had last stirred their lines , when the spirit went forth , and left its imprint of wonder , joy , and awe thereon ; and Alfgar instinctively turned from them to the blue depths of heaven above , where a few stars were visible , although dimmed by the moonlight ; and he seemed to trace his beloved Bertric 's passage to the realms of bliss", "context_len": 88}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "Indeed , O King , my tongue faileth to thank thee [ as were behoving ] for the greatness of this boon , overpassing its competence , [ 465 ] wherewith thou hast favoured me , and I beseech Thy Grace to vouchsafe me ground , such as is meet , so I may build thereon a palace that shall be fit for the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 66}}}, "gulp": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Joliffe , you are a proper white man ! '' declared Fortescue , deftly knocking off the head of the bottle and draining the contents at a gulp", "context_len": 29}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Jerry was hungry , indeed ; really , the little fellow was almost starved , and it was with no small difficulty that he repressed the eager desire to gulp his food", "context_len": 32}}}, "yon": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Behold ! Behold ! Like white-winged angels rise the radiant throng That from yon cemetery \u2019 s gloomy verge Have burst , immortal -- glorious -- undefiled ! Bright as the sun their crowns celestial shine , Yet I behold them with undazzled eye", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Another day Has past ! another weary weary day , And I am so much nearer to my grave ! Oh that I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "We 've lived hither and yon around New Mexico these ten years past , and I aint been very successful ; though things will be different now that I 've decided to pull out for the gold regions ! ''", "context_len": 40}}}, "footpads": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` How far down yon road is \u2019 t to Petersfield ? And is one like to meet aught of footpads , drawlatches , or vagrants of that condition ? ''", "context_len": 31}}}, "vagrants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` How far down yon road is \u2019 t to Petersfield ? And is one like to meet aught of footpads , drawlatches , or vagrants of that condition ? ''", "context_len": 31}}}, "implied": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 95, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` That 's all right -- make fun if you want to , '' said Fenn , a bit sharply , `` but it 's no joke to be under the disgrace of the implied accusation that we stole the bracelet", "context_len": 41}}}, "yeoman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` In the Name of God , Amen , I , Owen Wentworth , late of Holwick in the countie of Yorks '' [ here followed some word that had been erased and `` yeoman '' written above ] `` being whole of bodie and perfect of mynde , do ordaine and make this my last will and testament in manner and forme followinge : First , I commend my soule into the handes of Almightie God my Creator , and my bodie to be buried in the churchyarde at Trotton", "context_len": 91}}}, "cornet": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "Amidst renewed outbursts of cheering the subaltern retired for repairs and renewals , while the lady judges were fortunately spared the task of bestowing the palm upon the cornet champion of the Thirty-somethingths", "context_len": 33}}}, "regiment": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 8, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}}}, "enrol": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` And the king proposes to enrol you amongst his chosen warriors on that day ; he has marked the skill you have displayed in the mimic contests with spear or sword , your skill as a horseman , and he wishes to see whether in actual battle you will fulfil the promise of the parade ground", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Come , then , at once , '' said Drake , `` to the village , and enrol every man who can use mattock and spade", "context_len": 27}}}, "stripling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "First , at Edgehill , when I , a mere stripling , lay under the hoofs of Rupert \u2019 s horse", "context_len": 21}}}, "mystification": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 94, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "wouldst": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 73, "context": "`` O my son , '' replied she , `` true it is that the present is exceedingly costly and precious and as thou sayest , none hath the like thereof ; but who shall dare to come forward and seek of the Sultan his daughter Bedrulbudour ? Nay , I dare not adventure myself and say to him , ' I want thy daughter , ' whenas he asketh me , 'What wouldst thou ? ' Marry , O my son , , my tongue will be tied", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 112, "context": "replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both far and near have heard ? How is it thou hast heard nought of this nor of the name of Alaeddin , whom Our Lord increase in glory and prosper ? '' Quoth the Maugrabin , `` Marry , it is the utmost of my wish to look upon the palace ; so , an thou wouldst do me a kindness , direct me thither , for that I am a stranger", "context_len": 128}}}, "sire": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 48, "context": "His headpiece and armour received the blows as lightly as if they were from a straw , and with an oath he smote me heavily on the chest with the butt of his pistol , so that I reeled , fell backward across the body of my murdered sire , and struck my head on the frosty road", "context_len": 58}}}, "eminent": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "Billy Smith , son of an eminent explorer , learns to pilot a seaplane aboard a Naval Air Station , and immediately fares forth upon a series of the most exciting cruises and mysterious quests by air , by land and by sea that have ever fallen to the lot of man", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "They sat a long while over their dessert of fruit and nuts , and then the guest said he would have to go as he wanted to attend a lecture by an eminent surgeon", "context_len": 34}}}, "thud": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 25, "context": "They had gone but a few yards farther , Bomba \u2019 s eyes straining to detect the faintly marked trail , when there was a thud , and on the ground before them , directly in their path , appeared a figure so grotesque in form and ugly in face that Bomba took a startled step backward and the two slaves fell to the ground in a fit of shuddering terror", "context_len": 71}}}, "brushwood": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 14, "context": "In a moment a party of men had run from the cover of the brushwood , freed the captive , and , after hamstringing the trooper \u2019 s horse , had made their escape to the depths of the forest before the rest of the escort could realize what had occurred", "context_len": 51}}}, "coppice": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "When but half the distance was covered , I heard the sound of the crackling of the dry brushwood in a coppice on our left , followed by the cry of a bird and the fluttering of its wings as it flew over our heads", "context_len": 45}}}, "covert": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "The group of officers and visitors who were gathered round the hearth , spoke to each other in low tones as they glanced with looks of curiosity , and even covert amusement , at two gentlemen who stood in the recessed window , in earnest talk", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "For another hour he pressed rapidly through the jungle , his eyes sharply scanning every tree and covert , for dusk was coming on and the beasts of prey would soon be starting on their nightly mission of death", "context_len": 39}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Seabrooke went about the business of the day with all his accustomed regularity and precision , but with a sort of defiant and I-am-going-to-stick-to-it air about him which in itself incited the other boys to covert thrusts and innuendoes tending to throw distrust upon his version of the story and to make known their thorough sympathy with Percy , not only for his loss , but also for the aspersions cast upon him by the young pupil-teacher", "context_len": 77}}}, "authoritative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "As we neared the clump a voice , authoritative , harsh , and yet familiar , shouted :", "context_len": 18}}}, "unsheathed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "For the second time that night my father unsheathed his hanger , and , pushing me behind him , advanced towards the man", "context_len": 23}}}, "freebooters": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Instead , I journey to the Rhine to join the German freebooters , or else to the Spanish Main to throw in my lot with the buccaneers of the Indies -- it matters not which ; but ere I go I have an account to settle with the Lord of Holwick", "context_len": 51}}}, "trysting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Dost remember twenty years aback -- the trysting place under the Holmwood Oak ? -- Ah !", "context_len": 17}}}, "moonbeams": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 15, "context": "The limbs of the great tree swayed gently in the westerly breeze , and the moonbeams came slanting through them most delightfully , as the boys lay listening to the moan of the night wind , the sound of the surf along the shore , and watched the clouds as they coursed by the moon , all heightened by the novelty of their situation", "context_len": 64}}}, "unslung": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "The man swung off the yard and slid down as far as the cross-trees , while I unslung my glass and brought it to bear upon the stranger", "context_len": 28}}}, "clap": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 56, "context": "Just as we reached the summit , where the road makes a vast horseshoe curve round the dark , forbidding cavity known as the Devil \u2019 s Punch Bowl , a heavy rainstorm came on , blotting out the horizon , while a vivid flash of lightning , followed at a short interval by a tremendous clap of thunder , startled our horses , and , be it confessed , ourselves as well", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "He ceased not to go daily to the market of the jewellers and to clap up acquaintance with the folk , making friends with them and questioning them of buying and selling and giving and taking and dear and cheap , till , one day of the days , he arose in the morning and donning his clothes , went forth , intending , as of wont , for the jewellers ' market ; but , as he went , he heard the crier proclaiming aloud on this wise , `` By commandment of the Lord of Beneficence , the king of the age and monarch of the time and the tide , let all the folk shut their shops and stores and enter their houses , for", "context_len": 128}}}, "demon": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 75, "context": "Baulked of their prey , the mob still surged round us , and with a shout of : `` Let \u2019 em have the cat ! '' a great black object was sent flying towards us , and , striking my horse on the crupper , resolved itself into an enormous black cat , that spat and howled , digging its claws into the horse \u2019 s hide , and arching its back like the demon cat that is the reputed companion of every witch", "context_len": 85}}}, "cudgel": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "He was turning as he said , when Astbury , with an oath , sprang forward , flourishing his cudgel ; but he had forgotten that the young officer was no novice at sword-play , and a turn of Dick 's wrist sent the ruffian 's stick flying over the hedge", "context_len": 51}}}, "headpiece": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "His headpiece and armour received the blows as lightly as if they were from a straw , and with an oath he smote me heavily on the chest with the butt of his pistol , so that I reeled , fell backward across the body of my murdered sire , and struck my head on the frosty road", "context_len": 58}}}, "multitudes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "The very fact of this whole-hearted girl giving up her life for the cause of Christ , and the pathos of her untimely end , did more to touch the hearts of multitudes than perhaps the most apparently successful accomplishment of her mission would have done", "context_len": 46}}}, "vomit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I lay still , my temples throbbing like to burst , and my head swimming till I felt ready to vomit", "context_len": 21}}}, "cupping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "No more letting of blood or cupping is needful", "context_len": 9}}}, "posset": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A hot posset will not come amiss , good Mistress Heatherington , ere I take my leave , for \u2019 tis cold abroad", "context_len": 23}}}, "repose": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 95, "context": "It was a fine summer 's evening towards the end of the month of July , and the sun was slowly setting behind the wood-crowned range of hills in the west , where the forest terminated the pastures of Aescendune ; the cattle were returning to their stalls ; the last load of hay was being transferred from the wain to the rick , and all things spoke of the calm and rest of a sweet night , fragrant with the breath of honeysuckle and wild brier , when nature herself seems to court luxurious repose", "context_len": 96}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "Zotenberg 's note is unintelligible , in consequence of his having neglected to explain that the passage in question is a common Egyptian proverb , meaning ( says Burckhardt ) , `` the person whose fortune is entrusted to the hands of strangers can not enjoy repose", "context_len": 47}}}, "indecision": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "A wave of indecision swept over the people present ; but , in spite of extreme physical pain , I had raised myself on my elbow , and in reply I repudiated the Roundhead \u2019 s taunt", "context_len": 37}}}, "repudiated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "A wave of indecision swept over the people present ; but , in spite of extreme physical pain , I had raised myself on my elbow , and in reply I repudiated the Roundhead \u2019 s taunt", "context_len": 37}}}, "taunt": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "A wave of indecision swept over the people present ; but , in spite of extreme physical pain , I had raised myself on my elbow , and in reply I repudiated the Roundhead \u2019 s taunt", "context_len": 37}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "But most I came to hate Bern Hayden , who was a leader among the village boys , and who never lost a chance to taunt and deride me and call me the son of a jail-bird", "context_len": 37}}}, "furlong": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "That man shot my father with a petronel not a furlong from this house", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 0, "context": "Furlong shook her head wisely ; and I am sure a dewy moisture came to her eyes , but I did not wait to say anything more , but ran off in the direction of mother 's room", "context_len": 38}}}, "roadway": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was a good thing that the children were in a place of safety , for anyone down in the roadway below stood a very fair chance of being trampled to death by the frightened , plunging herd which surged up the street , scattering the spectators like leaves before a storm", "context_len": 52}}}, "unhand": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Were ye told to unhand him , dolts ? '' he shouted", "context_len": 13}}}, "dolts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Were ye told to unhand him , dolts ? '' he shouted", "context_len": 13}}}, "unawares": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "He had not taken the trouble to open his eyes , but his paws were crossed , and his pink mouth was open , giving vent to loud disapproval of the bumpings to which he had been rudely subjected unawares", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I want you to promise in the meantime , though , that if you come upon me unawares , or when I 'm asleep , or under any circumstances whatever , you will turn your head away and not look at my face", "context_len": 43}}}, "henchmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Then the two henchmen grappled with the Roundhead , and all three rolled in a heap on the floor", "context_len": 19}}}, "grappled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "The officers held a consultation -- Sir George Ascue was not one of them ; whether he was killed or wounded I did not at that time know -- and the opinion of the council was that if we were grappled by the fire ships our fate would no doubt be a glorious one , but of little use to His Majesty the King", "context_len": 64}}}, "pallor": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 22, "context": "He was too much a man of the world to show any of his emotions , but I saw by a certain pallor round his mouth , and a rather dragged look about his eyes , that he was suffering , and I could not imagine why", "context_len": 47}}}, "lurked": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 4, "context": "Traces of poison gas lurked in the traverses , pungent fumes from bursting shells wafted over parapet and parados , while the report , passed on from various successive occupants of this section of the line , that a dozen dead Huns had been buried under the floor of the support trench -- the old first-line trench of a Prussian regiment -- seemed to find definite confirmation", "context_len": 67}}}, "winded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "He of the scarred face was quickly captured , being well-nigh winded with the effects of the stone I had thrown ; but the third was a more determined quarry , though , in his stupidity , instead of climbing upwards ( being far lighter than the soldiers , encumbered as they were with breast-pieces and riding-boots ) , he must needs make for the road , where he raced off at breakneck speed", "context_len": 74}}}, "countryman": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "At that moment the door opened and a sturdy countryman entered , pulling his forelock as a mark of respect to Sir George , and handed him a petronel which I recognized only too well", "context_len": 35}}}, "forelock": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "At that moment the door opened and a sturdy countryman entered , pulling his forelock as a mark of respect to Sir George , and handed him a petronel which I recognized only too well", "context_len": 35}}}, "enquire": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was on a Tuesday morning when Sir George came to the cottage to enquire how I progressed , and to tell me that he was taking me to the courthouse at Midhurst on the following Monday morning , should I be well enough to bear the journey", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Whereupon the Sultan acquainted him with all that his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour had told him and said to him , `` It is now my will that thou enquire of thy son the truth of the case , for it may be my daughter knoweth not for fright what happened to her , though methinketh her tale is all true", "context_len": 62}}}, "courthouse": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "One morning in June I was called into our living-room , and found myself confronted by a gold-laced individual , who , drawing a paper from his pocket , read in a sonorous voice a summons for me to attend at the courthouse as a witness against Dick Swyre and Caleb Keeping , presented for committing a murderous attack upon divers of the king \u2019 s subjects on the highway", "context_len": 70}}}, "slayer": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Lad , '' he exclaimed , `` I would I could fathom this mystery ! Thy father \u2019 s slayer is no mean reaver or cutpurse ; yet , though we have him safe by the heels , manacled and leg-ironed , and threaten him with the thumbscrews , never a word can be wrung from him", "context_len": 58}}}, "reaver": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Lad , '' he exclaimed , `` I would I could fathom this mystery ! Thy father \u2019 s slayer is no mean reaver or cutpurse ; yet , though we have him safe by the heels , manacled and leg-ironed , and threaten him with the thumbscrews , never a word can be wrung from him", "context_len": 58}}}, "cutpurse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Lad , '' he exclaimed , `` I would I could fathom this mystery ! Thy father \u2019 s slayer is no mean reaver or cutpurse ; yet , though we have him safe by the heels , manacled and leg-ironed , and threaten him with the thumbscrews , never a word can be wrung from him", "context_len": 58}}}, "manacled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "The first-named was the bearded ruffian who had so nearly settled my account in the valley near Petersfield , and now , knowing full well that his neck was already in the hangman \u2019 s noose , his demeanour was one of sullen ferocity , and , though he was heavily manacled , his appearance was like that of a savage beast awaiting its opportunity to spring", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Looking back , the manacled lad saw Jerry standing there , his face hidden in his hands , the yellow dog gazing up sympathetically at him , a spectacle never to be forgotten ; and the frightful injustice of fate seemed to crush and smother the last spark of hope and strength in Ben \u2019 s soul", "context_len": 57}}}, "thumbscrews": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` Lad , '' he exclaimed , `` I would I could fathom this mystery ! Thy father \u2019 s slayer is no mean reaver or cutpurse ; yet , though we have him safe by the heels , manacled and leg-ironed , and threaten him with the thumbscrews , never a word can be wrung from him", "context_len": 58}}}, "feud": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "I will pursue them with unrelenting vengeance , with the death feud , till I have destroyed the accursed race utterly", "context_len": 21}}}, "twixt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Was there ever a feud \u2019 twixt thy sire and him ? ''", "context_len": 13}}}, "moonlit": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Was this God 's 'best ' for her ? The letter dropped from her hand , and she sat for hours motionless , her eyes taking in every detail of the pretty moonlit room , until it was indelibly engraved upon her memory", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Down the moonlit street they sped , their footsteps ringing out on the frosty night", "context_len": 15}}}, "wealthiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Sir George listened attentively , and , proud of being privileged to talk to so exalted a personage as the wealthiest man for miles around Rake , I let my tongue run wild for the space of nigh on an hour", "context_len": 41}}}, "babbling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Don \u2019 t stand there babbling like a drunken mummer at Martinmas fair ! '' he shouted , with a round oath", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "He babbled as much as of old ; but his babbling was now intelligible to other creatures besides the flies", "context_len": 20}}}, "mummer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Don \u2019 t stand there babbling like a drunken mummer at Martinmas fair ! '' he shouted , with a round oath", "context_len": 23}}}, "dolt": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Where did I look ? Now , out on me for a dolt ! I delved every night for more than a fortnight , till the countryside rang with tales of the ghost of Holwick , and none would venture near o \u2019 nights , and hardly by day", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Where ! Why , in the castle , ye dolt ! ''", "context_len": 13}}}, "testifying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "With another furious outburst the knight rushed out of the room , mounted his horse , and , followed by his two servants and the messenger of ill-tidings , rode furiously down the road to Midhurst , the noise of the horses \u2019 hoofs clattering on the frosty road testifying to the speed at which they were urged", "context_len": 58}}}, "churchyard": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 112, "context": "There 's a fine place round by the old abbot 's house , where the ground is soft , and we could dig easy ; and I 've a cousin in the slate-quarries at Bethogwen as has promised many a time to cut me a little tombstone as a present , if ever I was wantin ' one , so I 'll ask him to bring it next time he comes , and put Rollo 's name on it , and the day , and as how he died defendin ' you ; and we 'll fix it up nice , and plant flowers round , just as if it was the churchyard", "context_len": 113}}}, "belie": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Lawyer Whitehead , whose name did not belie his appearance , adjusted his horn spectacles , and , unfolding a parchment , read the will , which is as follows : --", "context_len": 32}}}, "erased": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` In the Name of God , Amen , I , Owen Wentworth , late of Holwick in the countie of Yorks '' [ here followed some word that had been erased and `` yeoman '' written above ] `` being whole of bodie and perfect of mynde , do ordaine and make this my last will and testament in manner and forme followinge : First , I commend my soule into the handes of Almightie God my Creator , and my bodie to be buried in the churchyarde at Trotton", "context_len": 91}}}, "testament": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` In the Name of God , Amen , I , Owen Wentworth , late of Holwick in the countie of Yorks '' [ here followed some word that had been erased and `` yeoman '' written above ] `` being whole of bodie and perfect of mynde , do ordaine and make this my last will and testament in manner and forme followinge : First , I commend my soule into the handes of Almightie God my Creator , and my bodie to be buried in the churchyarde at Trotton", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Testament 8", "context_len": 2}}}, "forme": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` In the Name of God , Amen , I , Owen Wentworth , late of Holwick in the countie of Yorks '' [ here followed some word that had been erased and `` yeoman '' written above ] `` being whole of bodie and perfect of mynde , do ordaine and make this my last will and testament in manner and forme followinge : First , I commend my soule into the handes of Almightie God my Creator , and my bodie to be buried in the churchyarde at Trotton", "context_len": 91}}}, "item": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 0, "context": "Item , to the said George Anderson the sum of Twenty and five Pounds yearly , provided that the said George Anderson doth fulfil to the letter the instructions set forth by me and intrusted to the keeping of the aforesaid Master Whitehead , Lawyer of Midhurst", "context_len": 47}}}, "wedded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "Good Parson Perrient had painted Providence plantation as a sort of paradise , where the liberty and toleration dreamt of by a few in England were the law for all ; but was that refuge open to him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "intrusted": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "The fact was , Albert was intrusted with a most important piece of information -- the bearer of a message that had cleared his own mental horizon from so much as the vestige of a cloud , and which he felt sure would bring equal joy to the others for whom it was intended", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "The ist'-ats\u00e1kalgi called out young men from the side opposite to them , and imparted one of the five titles to be mentioned below , according to their free choice , and simultaneously intrusted each with some office connected with the busk", "context_len": 42}}}, "hereinafter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Item , to all persons hereinafter named '' [ here followed a long list of names , embracing all present and many besides ] , `` provided that they pay me the last respects due to me , I give XX * s *", "context_len": 45}}}, "residue": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The residue , which is no inconsiderable amount , he hid in a secure place , taking good care that it should come to his son on his attaining the age of twenty-one , provided that he showed great promise of filling his position in a right and proper manner", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Item , it is my will if the above named Aubrey my sonne doth dye without heires or before he come to the age of XXI years , the residue shall remain to my sister Margaret Anderson and her heires forever", "context_len": 42}}}, "doe": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` \u2019 -- -- Mine desires \u2019 '' resumed Master Whitehead , receiving the interruption with a deprecatory cough , `` \u2019 I doe hereby give full directions in soe that the treasures of mine house at Holwick may come to him by right", "context_len": 44}}}, "whereof": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 112, "context": "the safe keeping of the lamp under the earth ; and he looked well into [ 547 ] the figures , both mothers and daughters , [ 548 ] but saw not the lamp , whereupon rage overrode him and he smote the sand a second time , that he might certify himself of Alaeddin 's death , but saw him not in the treasure ; whereat he redoubled in wrath , and yet more when it was certified to him that the lad was alive upon the surface of the earth and he knew that he had come forth from under the ground and had gotten the lamp , on account whereof he himself had suffered toil and torment such as passeth man 's power to endure", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 60, "context": "`` The Vizier arose and looked from the window towards Alaeddin 's palace , but could see nothing , neither palace nor aught else ; so his wit was bewildered and he was amazed and returned to the Sultan , who said to him , `` Now knowest thou the cause of my distress and seest Alaeddin his palace , whereof thou saddest that it was shut", "context_len": 67}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": "describe and praise ? '' `` O man , '' replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both far and near have heard ? How is it thou hast heard nought of this nor of the name of Alaeddin , whom Our Lord increase in glory and prosper ? '' Quoth the Maugrabin , `` Marry , it is the utmost of my wish to look upon the palace ; so , an thou wouldst do me a kindness ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "worded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "The following ancient hunting song may serve as a specimen of the female dialect of Hitchiti ; the ending -i of the verbs , standing instead of -is of the male dialect , proves it to be worded in that archaic form of speech", "context_len": 44}}}, "missive": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 20, "context": "Early in the morning , before the high mass , whereat the neighbourhood is generally present , I received a missive from the sheriff , bidding me , in the name of the King , to exhort my people to remain at home tonight , since danger is afoot , and there is likely , he says , to be a rising on the part of the pagans who dwell amongst us", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The few words of Apalachi were drawn from the missive sent , A", "context_len": 13}}}, "vellum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "This he broke and withdrew the contents , revealing yet another sealed missive and a sheet of vellum written in my father \u2019 s hand", "context_len": 25}}}, "exhorting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The letter gave instructions for me to be sent to my uncle \u2019 s at Portsmouth , to be provided for until I could choose for myself what I should be , at the same time exhorting me to serve faithfully His Majesty King Charles II or his lawful successor , and to abstain from vain or idle longings to break the seals of the enclosed package till the stipulated time limit had expired", "context_len": 74}}}, "longings": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "Lilian 's experience of concerts was confined to an occasional afternoon performance in the Warford Assembly Rooms , or the military band in the Spa gardens ; but she bravely hid her longings for better things , for she knew that a musical education would be an utter impossibility in the family circumstances , and that Father had strained a point already to allow her to learn from Herr Frankenburg , who was the most expensive teacher in the school", "context_len": 80}}}, "seals": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "The letter gave instructions for me to be sent to my uncle \u2019 s at Portsmouth , to be provided for until I could choose for myself what I should be , at the same time exhorting me to serve faithfully His Majesty King Charles II or his lawful successor , and to abstain from vain or idle longings to break the seals of the enclosed package till the stipulated time limit had expired", "context_len": 74}}}, "enquiring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then Sir George Lee spoke , enquiring where was the small metal box that my father had mentioned", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I was unable to utter a word , and stood still , feeling considerably uneasy under his enquiring gaze", "context_len": 19}}}, "twas": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Mebbe \u2019 twas an oversight we wa \u2019 n \u2019 t give \u2019 orders to fetch ye , for you might serve as a witness against him ; but , having neither authority nor room , we won \u2019 t cumber ourselves with ye", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "She travelled to the glorious East ; she met the rising sun , -- And even so her day of heavenly bliss was soon begun ; I knew \u2019 twas sunrise with my child , while night was o \u2019 er me weeping , E \u2019 er closed my weary day , my darling was serenely sleeping", "context_len": 57}}}, "robbery": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 77, "context": "Course , concluded the officer , we give the feller warnin \u2019 that anything he said might be used as evidence ag \u2019 in \u2019 him , and I ruther guess he kept it in mind ; for , \u2019 though we talked with him considerable on the way back to Oakdale , he didn \u2019 t make no slip-ups , and he pertended all the time not to know nothin \u2019 at all \u2019 bout the robbery", "context_len": 78}}}, "oaken": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Master Whitehead then gave to Sir George an oaken box which my father had mentioned in his will", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "It has a vaulted roof , and a minstrels ' gallery , and a huge chimney filled with blazing oaken logs , and it is brilliant with waxen tapers twinkling on every side", "context_len": 33}}}, "disclosing": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 55, "context": "Floating some five feet above her usual loadline , the ship was towed into the mouth of a muddy creek , and there careened till the whole of the bottom on the larboard side , with the exception of the keel and a few of the lower planks , was clear of the water , disclosing a sloping wall of barnacle-covered timbers", "context_len": 62}}}, "jewelled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 86, "context": "Will you tell it to us , Marie-Celeste ? And Marie-Celeste , nothing loath , and willing at last to substantiate her claims in the ears of doubting Albert , rested a hand comfortably on either arm of her chair , and commenced , preceding her narration with the request , You will correct me , wo n't you , if you find I do not tell it right ? to which Her Majesty smilingly acceded , first asking Miss Belmore to hand her a little jewelled miniature case from among the other treasures on the table", "context_len": 97}}}, "peruse": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "I am sure that your dear Mother and you would peruse with interest Keshab Sen \u2019 s lecture , or rather the review of it in the Statesman which I sent home", "context_len": 32}}}, "unmeaning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Again he read these unmeaning words , his brows knitting in undisguised perplexity ; then he handed the paper to the lawyer , who , after several vain attempts to produce a proper sentence , turned it over in his hand", "context_len": 41}}}, "handshakes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The rest of the assembled company followed his example , and , with an arm aching with the result of vigorous handshakes , I was left alone with Mistress Heatherington", "context_len": 30}}}, "reasserted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Grief does not for long hold its sway over the buoyant spirit of youth , and , in spite of the heavy blow that I had sustained , my boyish disposition speedily reasserted itself , and I looked forward with undisguised eagerness to my journey to my new home in Portsmouth town", "context_len": 52}}}, "ascent": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 39, "context": "A day spent in Midhurst , where I was well fitted out with clothes , helped to make the time pass , and on the evening previous to the eventful day of my departure , I climbed the steep ascent of Rake Hill to bid farewell to some of my friends who dwelt on the by-road towards Lyss", "context_len": 58}}}, "smelters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "For usually the Dauntless pursued no vigorous labor in summer , but merely kept the water out of its slope and `` took up '' and sold to various smelters such `` slack '' as it had made during the winter", "context_len": 41}}}, "selfsame": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "And older people than Donald have thought the selfsame thing , as they have looked down the same matchless avenue , with its wonderful far-reaching vista of branching elms , and its perfect driveway diminishing to a thread in the distance , with here and there a flock of grazing sheep roaming its ample grass-grown borders , and finding rich and abundant pasture", "context_len": 63}}}, "cavalcade": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "It was about two o \u2019 clock when the yokes were adjusted to the necks of the oxen , the horsemen swung into their saddles , and the cavalcade headed for Fort Laramie , on the other side of the mountain spur which bears the latter half of that name", "context_len": 50}}}, "starlight": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 19, "context": "As I drew to the side of the highway to watch them pass , I could see in the starlight that there was a body of horse , some dozen at least , surrounding a carriage", "context_len": 36}}}, "accoutred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The horsemen were accoutred in breast- and back-plates and steel helmets , and from their sour visages I knew them to be Roundheads", "context_len": 23}}}, "visages": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}}}, "beaver": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Beaver are making a dam across the river and soon they 'll have to be checked for they would stop up the water and make it so deep that the cows would drown when crossing from one meadow to another", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 67, "context": "Nor was Nancy the only one who indulged in a little innocent vanity , for Joe , too , had been so fastidious in his choice of a red-and-blue spotted necktie and a walking-stick with an ivory handle that the children began to suspect the blacksmith 's rosy-cheeked daughter must be at the bottom of it ; and even David had taken a long-tailed coat and a beaver hat out of the retirement of some mysterious bandboxes and had been quite snappy and particular with Mrs", "context_len": 86}}}, "jovial": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Whether they journeyed as prisoners I could not tell , though from the careless jovial expression of their faces it seemed otherwise ; but before I could remark much else the party had galloped past , and were well on their way along this southern highway towards Portsmouth", "context_len": 48}}}, "wain": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "It was a fine summer 's evening towards the end of the month of July , and the sun was slowly setting behind the wood-crowned range of hills in the west , where the forest terminated the pastures of Aescendune ; the cattle were returning to their stalls ; the last load of hay was being transferred from the wain to the rick , and all things spoke of the calm and rest of a sweet night , fragrant with the breath of honeysuckle and wild brier , when nature herself seems to court luxurious repose", "context_len": 96}}}, "wizened": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "At first I talked excitedly with the driver , a sour-faced , wizened man , whose short jerky answers , spoken in broadest Sussex , did not encourage conversation ; so presently I dropped all attempt at talking , and took note of the various places and persons we met on the road", "context_len": 53}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "Simultaneously with the appearance of the guards -- for such they were -- there emerged from the fetish-house a man who appeared to be incredibly old , for his hair and beard were as white as snow , and his once stalwart form was now bowed and wizened with the passage of , as it seemed to me , hundreds of years", "context_len": 62}}}, "broadest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "At first I talked excitedly with the driver , a sour-faced , wizened man , whose short jerky answers , spoken in broadest Sussex , did not encourage conversation ; so presently I dropped all attempt at talking , and took note of the various places and persons we met on the road", "context_len": 53}}}, "gallows": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 18, "context": "If so be you laid hands if it were but on a hen , if you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old", "context_len": 128}}}, "daubed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The corpse , daubed with pitch , was encircled with iron hoops like a cage , and as the wind howled over the hilltop the chain that suspended the cage creaked horribly", "context_len": 32}}}, "hoops": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 118, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "hilltop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "But Tom , Sid , Phil , Dutch Housenlager , Ed Kerr and others of the second-year class were strong in the belief of their power to oust their rivals from the hilltop", "context_len": 33}}}, "mutilated": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 15, "context": "Alexander ordered him to be publicly scourged ; then he caused his face to be mutilated in a manner customary in those days , when a tyrant wished to stamp upon his victim a perpetual mark of infamy", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "If others who did not know me could not see the brand , I \u2019 d feel it , just as I feel He lifted his hand , and his fingers touched his mutilated left ear", "context_len": 36}}}, "prevention": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Young maaster , '' he replied , `` for certain prevention of agues , fever , smallpox , plague , and all divers illnesses , for certain proof against the evil eye , there is nowt that can compare with the little finger of a murderer", "context_len": 47}}}, "illnesses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Young maaster , '' he replied , `` for certain prevention of agues , fever , smallpox , plague , and all divers illnesses , for certain proof against the evil eye , there is nowt that can compare with the little finger of a murderer", "context_len": 47}}}, "nowt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Young maaster , '' he replied , `` for certain prevention of agues , fever , smallpox , plague , and all divers illnesses , for certain proof against the evil eye , there is nowt that can compare with the little finger of a murderer", "context_len": 47}}}, "carts": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "By this time the square tower of Petersfield Church was in sight , and soon after we drew up in the courtyard of the `` Red Lion '' , where , since it was market day , there were numbers of carts and wagons from the countryside for miles around", "context_len": 50}}}, "mistook": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "In the midst of the bustle and noise I saw that mingled with the countryfolk were several soldiers , while in a corner of the courtyard was a ponderous coach , which , if I mistook not , was the very one that passed me yesternight at Rake Hill", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "As we shortly discovered , the Union commanders in Tennessee mistook General Forrest 's movement to the neighborhood of Chattanooga for a retreat ; for , shortly after he moved in that direction , an ambitious Federal officer asked and received permission to enter Northern Alabama with a force large enough to worry the Confederate leader if he could be found", "context_len": 61}}}, "smith": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "Moffat in those days was not only teacher and preacher , but carpenter , smith , cooper , tailor , shoemaker , miller , baker and gardener !", "context_len": 28}}}, "chisel": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "On the messenger \u2019 s return we used the cold chisel to such good purpose that the massive lid flew open with a clang and a groan , disclosing a number of canvas sacks filled with coins of all sorts", "context_len": 40}}}, "troopers": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 34, "context": "A moment later there was a sound of harsh voices , the noise of stones striking against steel , more pistol-shots , and then quietness , broken at length by the return of the troopers bearing between them a man who moaned and cursed lustily as he was carried by none too tender hands", "context_len": 54}}}, "spirituous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Notwithstanding their Puritanical garb , most of the troopers had , even at this early hour , partaken of spirituous drinks , and , judging by their gestures and talk , were evidently anticipating the restoration of His Majesty King Charles", "context_len": 41}}}, "restoration": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "Why will you not openly share with us the pleasure we must all feel at the blind boy 's restoration to sight ? Did you not see dear Bessie 's wistful look at you as she bade you good-night ? These little ones can not understand why there should be any reason to hide such kindness as you have shown to these people , or why you should refuse to show an interest you really feel", "context_len": 76}}}, "casement": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 30, "context": "This went on till it grew unbearable , so , leaping out of bed , Harry went to the window , drew up the blind , and threw open the casement , to lean out and gaze at the grey sea , that looked so dark in the early dawn of morning", "context_len": 52}}}, "missile": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 1, "context": "Her missile flew far above the man \u2019 s head , and , running backwards in a vain endeavour to catch it , he fell heavily into a large trough of pigs \u2019 mash , where , half-stunned by the force of his fall , he lay wallowing in the sticky mess , till he was helped out by his comrades , to the accompaniment of their jeers and laughter", "context_len": 70}}}, "wallowing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Her missile flew far above the man \u2019 s head , and , running backwards in a vain endeavour to catch it , he fell heavily into a large trough of pigs \u2019 mash , where , half-stunned by the force of his fall , he lay wallowing in the sticky mess , till he was helped out by his comrades , to the accompaniment of their jeers and laughter", "context_len": 70}}}, "carter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Having carried all my belongings into the inn , the old carter bade me farewell ; and as the sound of his footsteps was lost in the outside din , I felt as if the last link that bound me to the home of my childhood was severed", "context_len": 48}}}, "link": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "How came that picture in the dwelling of Japazy , the half-breed ? Why had a similar picture been in the hut of Sobrinini ? What mysterious link was there between the lovely original of that picture and Sobrinini , the witch woman , Japazy , the master of Jaguar Island , Jojasta , the medicine man of the Moving Mountain , and Casson , his friend and former protector ? Somewhere , some time , those residents of the Amazonian jungle had known the mysterious Bartow , his wife , Laura , and perhaps the little child named Bonny", "context_len": 100}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Their college course completed , they were assisting in the survey of the Wairakato valley , where a projected line was about to be commenced to link up the east and west coasts of South Island", "context_len": 36}}}, "severed": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 26, "context": "Squeers remarked of her Yorkshire pie , it seemed 'quite a pity to cut into it ' ; but , seizing the knife , Peggy boldly severed the 's ' and the ' u , ' and with Aunt Helen 's wedding festivities fresh in their memories , the company drank the health of Sky Cottage in tea , clinking their cups together over the table in imitation of old Squire Henley", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "The four chums had gathered up the severed ropes , and , folding the bandages up in them , hid them all under a big stone", "context_len": 26}}}, "panelled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "In the opposite wall were other recesses , panelled and furnished with rich curtains and hangings", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "There was a large oak staircase , with wide , shallow steps , leading to a panelled gallery , where hung swords , and rusty armour , and moth-eaten tapestry , and many an ancient relic of the past ; while in the best bedroom was a great carved four-post bed , hung with faded yellow curtains , where Queen Elizabeth herself was said to have slept in much state for two nights on her journey from Shrewsbury to Wrexham", "context_len": 80}}}, "livid": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "His face , which time and evil passions had deeply wrinkled , was of a deadly paleness ; his eyes were encircled by a livid tint , and stared as if they would start from their orbits ; his breathing was rapid and interrupted , but at the moment when Edmund entered he was silent", "context_len": 55}}}, "weal": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": "( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the operation was", "context_len": 128}}}, "copious": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "The members seemed to take copious notes in pocket-books , and asked occasional questions , one literary-looking lady , in spectacles and a large black hat with nodding feathers , being particularly insistent , and volunteering so much information that she threatened to usurp the place of the lecturer , and had to be gently suppressed , while an old gentleman distributed pamphlets broadcast , declaring he had had them specially printed for the occasion", "context_len": 75}}}, "reek": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Three large tankards , lying upset on the floor , showed that their refreshment had been copious , while the reek of strong spirits hung around them like an invisible cloud", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "My birthday eve they kept in high style , the men smoking long clay pipes , till our dining-room was enveloped in a cloud of tobacco smoke , so that my aunt declared that her best hangings would reek for days like a London coffee tavern , whereat Sir George pacified her by saying that the weed of Virginia is ever a sovereign safeguard against the plague !", "context_len": 68}}}, "wayfarer": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Richard crouched over the hearth , drying his wet clothes and awaiting without much expectation of satisfaction the supper the slatternly hostess promised , when a heavy step without , and a violent rattle of the door-latch , told that another wayfarer was coming to share his wretched lair", "context_len": 49}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "To the sightless wayfarer it was a marvel beyond words , almost beyond comprehension", "context_len": 14}}}, "excuses": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 23, "context": "After the twins were born they tried a grown nursemaid who bored them by sitting around when she was upstairs and making many excuses to get down to the kitchen , where she disputed with Bridget who declared one or the other of them must go , and they simply could not give up Bridget", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He found excuses to scold Benjamin , and sometimes gave him blows", "context_len": 12}}}, "bethink": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Ah , bethink me ! Didst pass Carver , the chapman , on your way hither ? ''", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` `` O my son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not", "context_len": 128}}}, "accommodation": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 19, "context": "But , when the captain heard Miss Weston was there to keep an appointment , he put out the accommodation ladder , took her on board , had the notice piped that she had come to give an address ; and soon a crowd of sailors was swarming round her in the upper deck battery , standing , sitting , lying , kneeling -- all earnestly listening", "context_len": 67}}}, "hostel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` None , young sir ; but there is ample accommodation here for man and beast till Thursday , when a wagon from Alton to Portsmouth passes this hostel", "context_len": 29}}}, "chalky": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Having paid my account in gold , and received a quantity of silver change , which the landlord counted slowly into my hand , I bade the kindly farmer good-bye , and set off southward along a dusty , chalky road", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Passing through this , I found myself on a steep bank , at the bottom of which a white chalky road led towards a town some miles away , the towers of whose churches were plainly visible in the morning light , while away to the right was a large expanse of water which I guessed correctly was the harbour of Poole", "context_len": 62}}}, "causeway": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "After examining carefully all the circumstances of the case , Alexander conceived the very bold plan of building a broad causeway from the main-land to the island on which the city was founded , out of the ruins of old Tyre , and then marching his army over upon it to the walls of the city , where he could then plant his engines and make a breach", "context_len": 68}}}, "downs": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 18, "context": "Never was fairy tale listened to with more rapt attention than Marie-Celeste 's narration of the ups and downs of Ted 's life as she knew them , and never was heart more gladly grateful than hers when she realized that these good friends were more than willing , for the sake of the end in view , to condone the deception practised upon them", "context_len": 65}}}, "treeless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Two miles brought me to the foot of the downs , where all signs of cultivation terminated abruptly , and only a treeless expanse of turf-covered hills met my eye", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Jane 's house was broad and heavy also -- a rough , paintless `` shack , '' which she had built after her own ideals on a treeless `` forty '' just beyond the limits of Aguilar", "context_len": 37}}}, "onwards": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "The troopers formed up again , the prisoners firmly bound to two of their number , and the cavalcade passed onwards , leaving by the roadside a motionless object that had once been a man", "context_len": 35}}}, "habitation": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 64, "context": "Father returned , and I think , though neither of them would have confessed it , the children were both rather relieved to hear his cheery whistle outside the door , for it was just a little eerie sitting by the peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to laugh at the supernatural , she", "context_len": 128}}}, "outdistance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` Do n't think I believe anything against tia ! '' she heard herself saying sternly , as the gate slipped from her impetuous hand and she rushed away , the quarry of emotions which no speed , however swift , could outdistance", "context_len": 43}}}, "grimed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was the one with the scarred face , and , being in addition grimed with sweat and dust , and panting heavily , he presented a truly ferocious aspect", "context_len": 30}}}, "quarry": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 29, "context": "He of the scarred face was quickly captured , being well-nigh winded with the effects of the stone I had thrown ; but the third was a more determined quarry , though , in his stupidity , instead of climbing upwards ( being far lighter than the soldiers , encumbered as they were with breast-pieces and riding-boots ) , he must needs make for the road , where he raced off at breakneck speed", "context_len": 74}}}, "blaspheming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "When he saw the open road with no sign of his quarry he stopped , swearing and blaspheming horribly , till his fellow rogues came up", "context_len": 26}}}, "weaponless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "In spite of my terror I kept them in view , and , being weaponless , I looked around for some means of defence", "context_len": 24}}}, "excavated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "Close to my feet was a large rabbit hole , and knowing from past experience that these animals frequently throw up flints and other stones from their burrows , I plunged my hand into the newly excavated earth , and to my delight found a large jagged flint , and soon after my armoury consisted of five good-sized stones", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "On all sides rose little mounds of excavated earth , showing how vigorously Joyce had pursued his quest for the hidden treasure , each mound being thirty-two paces from the wall", "context_len": 31}}}, "hurdle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Here and there a dead sheep or pig drifted by , or a portion of a haystack ; a hurdle floating like a raft bore on it a few disconsolate fowls , clucking dismally ; while an occasional wash-tub or upturned table showed that the river must have already flooded some of the low-lying cottages higher up on its course", "context_len": 60}}}, "undoing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "As I came more and more to my senses , I knew that these papers had been the cause of my undoing ; I could see in it , as plain as day , the hand of Jane Ryder , and I was truly sorry", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` That dog will be our undoing", "context_len": 7}}}, "outdistanced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "It raced the other , outdistanced it , seemed bound for the furthest heights , never swerving from a true , straight line", "context_len": 23}}}, "midriff": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Then suddenly I turned , and , throwing one of the largest stones , hit him fairly in the midriff", "context_len": 20}}}, "cursing": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 42, "context": "At the whiz of the bullets , though they passed well above us , I threw myself on the bottom of the boat ; but the firing had the opposite effect on the bos \u2019 n \u2019 s mate , for , cursing horribly , he raised himself and seized an oar", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 86, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "whining": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 25, "context": "In the warm nights in summer they crawl out on the rocks , and lie and play , and you may hear them growling and whining like so many dogs ; they also , in the winter time , lie on the ice cakes and float about , and when alarmed they slide into the water in an instant", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` The poor fellow thought it hard he should not have his share of fun , and was making a pitiful whining and whimpering , '' said Mr", "context_len": 28}}}, "trussed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He lay on the ground whining dismally , while the soldiers hastily trussed him up with a length of stout cord", "context_len": 21}}}, "disturbance": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` Beware , woman ! '' shrieked Maggie , and Bessie , thinking it time for her to come to her sister 's aid , joined in the cry , `` Beware , woman ! '' while Franky , always ready to take part in any disturbance , struck at Aunt Patty with his whip , and shouted , `` 'Ware , woman ! '' and Hafed , knowing nothing but that this old lady had alarmed and distressed his young charge , and that it was his duty to protect them , raised his voice in a whoop of defiance , and snatching up the hearth-brush , brandished it in a threatening manner as he danced wildly about her", "context_len": 120}}}, "sergeant": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 36, "word_index": 6, "context": "Striding over to the body the sergeant turned it over on its back , made sure that life was extinct , then returned to the door of the coach , and , saluting , said : `` Trooper Jenkins \u2019 s shot , sir , brought the rogue down", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "After the next volley some of the men plunged into the underwood , encouraged by the voice of the sergeant shouting : `` After him , men , at all costs ; he can not be far off", "context_len": 38}}}, "rascals": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 32, "context": "Hastily I told my story , and hardly had I finished , when the elder of the two officers called to the sergeant : `` Quickly , Sedgewyke ! Secure those other rascals ! ''", "context_len": 35}}}, "encumbered": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "He of the scarred face was quickly captured , being well-nigh winded with the effects of the stone I had thrown ; but the third was a more determined quarry , though , in his stupidity , instead of climbing upwards ( being far lighter than the soldiers , encumbered as they were with breast-pieces and riding-boots ) , he must needs make for the road , where he raced off at breakneck speed", "context_len": 74}}}, "breakneck": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 72, "context": "He of the scarred face was quickly captured , being well-nigh winded with the effects of the stone I had thrown ; but the third was a more determined quarry , though , in his stupidity , instead of climbing upwards ( being far lighter than the soldiers , encumbered as they were with breast-pieces and riding-boots ) , he must needs make for the road , where he raced off at breakneck speed", "context_len": 74}}}, "grassland": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Too late the wretch saw his mistake , and again ran from the road towards a steep bank of chalk that towered up for nearly fifty feet above the stretch of level grassland at its base", "context_len": 36}}}, "snuffbox": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "The elder man gave the sergeant the promised reward , then , turning to his companion , with a low bow , presented him with the snuffbox", "context_len": 27}}}, "outwit": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The thought made him fiercely determined to outwit the enemy , to bring all these people through the gantlet of Jap boats , planes , and shore patrols", "context_len": 28}}}, "irregular": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 5, "context": "The building was large and irregular , and had been added to at various times , the hall , looking over the river , forming its most conspicuous portion ; but it had not originally been built for purposes of defence , and could not have endured the Danish assault for a moment , but for external defences , utterly independent of the building , which had been recently added ; a mound , surmounted by crossed palisades , skilfully strengthened by osier bands , and a deep outer ditch , now full of snow , surrounded the building on three sides", "context_len": 102}}}, "holsters": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "My feelings were relieved by Whistling Jim , who informed me that he had placed a very substantial ration in my holsters ; and I am free to say that , after Colonel Ryder , the negro was the most thoughtful and considerate person I have ever seen", "context_len": 48}}}, "avalanche": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Right through he plunged , staggering along , half pulled , half shoved , until it was impossible to gain another inch , and Kindlings was buried out of sight under an avalanche of players", "context_len": 35}}}, "projection": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "See , said Marie-Celeste , pushing the seat of the stall from beneath , so that it folded up against the back , thereby bringing to view a queer little wooden projection about six inches wide", "context_len": 36}}}, "butts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Using the butts , and occasionally the points , of their spears freely in order to force a passage through the steadily growing crowd , my escort slowly made their way toward that part of the town which was enclosed by the palisade ; and , as they did so , I studied the faces of those who thronged about me , with the object of forming some idea , if I could , of the fate that I might expect at their hands", "context_len": 84}}}, "hideously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "At the sixth shot the miserable villain made a feeble attempt to regain his former position , but ere he had ascended another two feet a shot struck him in the back of the head , and he tumbled to the bottom of the bank a hideously disfigured corpse", "context_len": 49}}}, "disfigured": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "At the sixth shot the miserable villain made a feeble attempt to regain his former position , but ere he had ascended another two feet a shot struck him in the back of the head , and he tumbled to the bottom of the bank a hideously disfigured corpse", "context_len": 49}}}, "ceremonious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "With this ceremonious display the tragedy was brought to a close , and the two officers , learning that I was on my way to Portsmouth , consented to let me ride with them", "context_len": 34}}}, "twain": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 87, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "declivity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The coach now descended a long declivity , at the bottom of which lay a straggling village , which , I was told , boasted of the name of Horndean", "context_len": 30}}}, "firearms": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "Everybody knows , that when the Spaniards conquered Mexico , they experienced but little difficulty in subduing the peaceable nation inhabiting those southwestern countries by means of their firearms , which proved to be far superior to the bows and arrows of their opponents , and against which courage availed almost nothing", "context_len": 52}}}, "unsheathing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Draw , comrades , draw ! '' shouted Felgate , and , unsheathing our swords , we urged our horses through the crowd till we reined up abreast of our chivalrous friend", "context_len": 33}}}, "underwood": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "Dense foliage enclosed us on either side , and , save for the noise of the men \u2019 s footsteps , and the occasional crackle of dry underwood , not a sound either of man or beast broke the stillness of the forest", "context_len": 43}}}, "hamstringing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "In a moment a party of men had run from the cover of the brushwood , freed the captive , and , after hamstringing the trooper \u2019 s horse , had made their escape to the depths of the forest before the rest of the escort could realize what had occurred", "context_len": 51}}}, "chagrined": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "When [ 413 ] the Vizier heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` The chagrined dancers turned to the casement , to behold a circle of mocking faces", "context_len": 16}}}, "poll": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then after giving his young poll a good rubbing down with two military brushes that he took out of a hand-bag , he turned to the door where some one was knocking", "context_len": 32}}}, "sinewy": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Cal swung one of his sinewy legs over the other knee , struck a fly crawling several feet away , with a well aimed volley of discolored spittle , and said :", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Stanton , who , lying over the edge , watched him , God only knows , with what sickening hope ; the loose soil crumbled and slid beneath him ; but , light and sinewy as he was , his bare feet , trained to all kinds of mountain climbing , took hold where those of a heavier person , with shoes upon them , must have faltered and slipped past all recovery", "context_len": 73}}}, "scuffle": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "He was took sudden , you understand -- in some sort of fit like ; and young master -- that 's Passon Perrient as they calls him , our young missis ' father -- and missis , they was away at Ipswich , and come back all of a scuffle and finds him dead ; and by all I hear , not the value of a penny-piece in the house in money -- plenty of silver and pewter you understand , but no money whatsumever", "context_len": 85}}}, "crudely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "His sinewy arms were tattooed with various devices , while on his chest , exposed during the scuffle , a death \u2019 s-head and cross-bones were crudely drawn", "context_len": 28}}}, "surly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 51, "context": "And then Ted asked , as had she : Are you sure it was Belden ? You see , Donald , he continued , I 've an old bachelor uncle whose name is Selden -- my mother 's brother -- and who answers to your description to a dot -- a surly old customer , who would do little enough for me , or any one else , I imagine", "context_len": 70}}}, "benighted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Now , Sedgewyke , I pray you dispatch that horse and let us hasten on , unless we wish to be benighted on the highway", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "With a stealthy tread he vanished down the road , hugging the buildings as if fearful of meeting a benighted stranger in the now deserted village", "context_len": 26}}}, "hilliest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "To one living in the hilliest and most picturesque part of Hampshire and Sussex this first glimpse came as a disappointment", "context_len": 21}}}, "dreariness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "Save for a few trees and some scattered houses there was little to break the dreariness of it , while , the tide being out ( as I afterwards learnt ) , long expanses of mud on either side increased this aspect of monotonous desolation", "context_len": 45}}}, "expanses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Save for a few trees and some scattered houses there was little to break the dreariness of it , while , the tide being out ( as I afterwards learnt ) , long expanses of mud on either side increased this aspect of monotonous desolation", "context_len": 45}}}, "fronted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "A short three miles now separated us from the town , and on approaching it I saw a large mound of earth , called the Town Mount , crowned by fortifications and fronted by a line of bastions and earthworks , which in turn were encircled with a moat that communicated with the mill dam on the right", "context_len": 58}}}, "dominated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was clear to me that this unsophisticated young savage had been dominated in her actions by one idea alone , that of saving me from a death of unspeakable horror ; she knew that , in so doing , she was cutting herself off for ever from her own people , to whom it would be impossible for her to return ; and , in her absolute simplicity , she had evidently thought that it would be the easiest thing in the world for her to throw in her lot with mine", "context_len": 93}}}, "fortification": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 80, "context": "The spot was on the northern borders of the ancient kingdom of Sussex -- the land of the Saxon Ella -- a spot marvelously favoured by nature , occupying the summit of a low hill , which commanded a wide prospect on all sides , while itself almost impregnable when fortified , as it was , by ditches and mounds , dug in the usual Danish fashion , for the Danes owed much of their success to their skill in fortification", "context_len": 81}}}, "riveted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "The sight of the rows of pikemen with their eighteen-feet weapons riveted my attention till I was recalled to my senses by being dismissed by my benefactors , who gave me in charge of a sour-visaged soldier , with instructions to take me to the house of Master Anderson in St", "context_len": 51}}}, "gabled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Soon I found myself at the door of a tall , gabled house , where , without waiting , my guide left me", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 67, "context": "Then , going into the ruins , she climbed up on to the old wall , from which the best view of the house and its surroundings could be obtained , and gazed with new eyes at the panorama of her home ; and as her glance travelled slowly from orchard to stackyard , from meadow to garden , back to the ivy-clad tower and the red gabled roof , ' I think , ' she said slowly , 'that if we had to leave the Abbey , it would break my heart ! '", "context_len": 95}}}, "irregularity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "[ Footnote 584 : I suppose the verb which I render `` caused [ sleep ] get the mastery , '' to be ghelleba , II of gheleba , as the only way of making sense of this passage , though this reading involves some irregularity from a grammatical point of view", "context_len": 52}}}, "verbosity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "His wife , my father \u2019 s sister , was tall , sparely built , and somewhat inclined to verbosity", "context_len": 20}}}, "waxed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 64, "context": ", `` How sayst thou , O Vizier ? He that can in so short a time avail unto riches like these , is he not worthy to be the Sultan 's son-in-law and to have his daughter to bride ? '' Now the Vizier marvelled at the greatness of these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about to work upon him by practice , that he might hinder him", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Miss Forster opened her eyes wide enough , as it was , to see a neat telephone , made of two empty cocoa-tins and a piece of waxed string , fixed up between the house and the stables , while a small windmill on the scullery roof turned the coffee-grinder in the kitchen , to the huge amazement and delight of the cook", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "' Bobby took to carrying about an old horse-pistol which he had found in the lumber-room , and saying 'Oddsbodkins ' on all occasions , and Peggy put on such a general air of melodrama that it seemed scarcely possible for her to speak in plain prose ; Archie was impresario , stage-manager , scene-shifter , dresser , maker-up , and principal actor all combined , while Lilian waxed so enthusiastic that she even sacrificed the feathers out of her last summer 's best hat to adorn the slouched head-gear considered necessary for a due representation of Romeo", "context_len": 98}}}, "unrestrained": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "When I gained the gunroom I told the news with unrestrained enthusiasm , and my remaining companions , now reduced to two in number , Greville Drake and Alan Wood , though not slow in offering their congratulations , did not conceal the fact that my good fortune was their disappointment", "context_len": 51}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Then , unrestrained , the team shot down the street without guide or hindrance and with the speed of the wind", "context_len": 21}}}, "reproaches": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "She reproached him for having appealed to Hannah , a servant in his father 's house , for aid ; and in her turn had to hear his reproaches for believing that he would condescend to such a thing , and received an emphatic and solemn denial that he had been guilty of this , or that he had ever let Hannah know of the straits he was in", "context_len": 69}}}, "spouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "It did not take me long to ascertain that the pair were ill-assorted , and when on certain occasions their dispute waxed hot , my uncle was invariably driven from the house by the unrestrained reproaches of his spouse", "context_len": 39}}}, "bandy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "While we were looking on with astonishment at this busy scene , a short , thick-set man , whose portly body was ill supported by a pair of bandy legs , came towards the place where we stood", "context_len": 38}}}, "ne": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "An exile from home , splendor dazzles in vain ; O ! give me my lowly thatch cottage again ; A charm from the skies seems to hallow us there , Which , seek through the world , is ne \u2019 er met with elsewhere", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "I ne \u2019 er have feared the eye of mortal man , Why should I shrink from his ?", "context_len": 19}}}, "lacking": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 102, "context": "oriels , [ 651 ] and said to him , `` How deemest thou , O my Lady Fatimeh , of this wonderful pavilion ? '' [ 652 ] `` By Allah , O my daughter , '' replied he , `` it is indeed marvellous in the extreme , [ 653 ] nor methinketh is its like found in the world ; nay , it is magnificent exceedingly ; but oh , for one thing which would far increase it in beauty and adornment ! '' And the princess said to him , `` O my Lady Fatimeh , what is lacking to it and what is this thing which would adorn it ? Tell me of it ; I had thought that it was altogether perfect", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "When it was morning , Alaeddin went up to view the jewelers ' work and saw that they had not completed half the lacking lattice-work ; whereupon he bade them incontinent undo all that they had wrought and restore the jewels to their owners", "context_len": 44}}}, "creditors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "It is only just over a hundred years since John Howard died ; yet in his day persons could be put to death for stealing a horse or a sheep , for robbing dwellings , for defrauding creditors , for forgery , for wounding deer , for killing or maiming cattle , for stealing goods to the value of five shillings , or even for cutting a band in a hop plantation", "context_len": 72}}}, "tier": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "A noble sight she made , this immense yellow-painted hull , with her double tier of gunports and her towering stern , richly ornamented with gilded quarter badges and richly carved galleries", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "badges": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "A noble sight she made , this immense yellow-painted hull , with her double tier of gunports and her towering stern , richly ornamented with gilded quarter badges and richly carved galleries", "context_len": 32}}}, "anticipate": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was Oakes who had seemed to anticipate the play , and Oakes who flung himself at Hayden ; but it was Stone , interfering for the runner , who was brought down by the right half back of the locals", "context_len": 41}}}, "twelfth": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "By his side , similarly dressed , stood his son , the hopeful Canute , the future King of England , then only in his twelfth year , but already showing himself a true cub of the old tiger in fierceness and valour , yet not devoid of nobler and gentler virtues , as he afterwards showed", "context_len": 57}}}, "effigy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "The gilded effigy of his sainted father was restored to its niche in the Square Tower at Portsmouth , where all persons passing were ordered to uncover", "context_len": 27}}}, "sainted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Englishmen ! brethren ! '' he cried , `` you see your foe , the ruthless destroyers of your land and kinsfolk ; the pagan murderers of your archbishop , the sainted Alphege", "context_len": 34}}}, "niche": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 109, "context": "For there was something very stirring in the act , and a stranger to the place would hold his breath in dread as he saw Mark Penelly , who was the finest swimmer at the port of Carn Du , climb up the side of the great black rock upon some fine summer evening , then go round along the narrow shelf of shaley stone , till he stood alone there forty feet above the sea , his white figure as he rested against the black rock , every muscle standing out from his well-knit frame , and his arms crossed , looking like some antique statue in its niche", "context_len": 110}}}, "exceptions": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 74, "context": "Why should not other middle-aged ladies go out , as she went out ? -- not necessarily always to attempt full Zenana work ; but to be protectors , housekeepers , nurses , to younger and more active ladies ? Whether it would be right to use any portion of Mission-funds for such a purpose may be doubted ; and in many a case Mission rooms could not be spared ; but there are exceptions as to the latter", "context_len": 79}}}, "merrymaking": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "All the good people here will communicate , and the evening will be given up to such merrymaking as is befitting amongst Christians", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Suddenly , above the noise of the revellers , came a shrill whistle , and as if by magic the din of merrymaking gave place to an almost oppressive silence", "context_len": 30}}}, "sonorous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "Master Fallowfield , who , as I afterwards learned from the conversation , was the parish clerk of Worth Matravers church on Sabbaths and holydays , and an arrant smuggler at other times , took the paper and read in a sonorous voice a message from a neighbouring justice to the ill-fated James Harker , telling him that the reward due to the informer Crocker would be paid at any time after Martinmas", "context_len": 73}}}, "bolts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "That occasion called for every available hand , for the securing bolts of two of our deck guns had broken adrift , and the huge ungainly weapons charged to and fro across the ship , carrying destruction in their passage", "context_len": 40}}}, "corresponding": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "From these primaries they extract four fresh figures by confronting each point with the corresponding point in the next figure , and counting for each pair a single or double point , according to one of two rules , i", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 28, "context": "Then ( says our author ) , the geomant proceeds to examine the sixteen figures thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A conjunction corresponding in every respect to our and exists in none of the Maskoki dialects", "context_len": 16}}}, "hangman": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "On the third or fourth day of the feast he took him to hunt in a wood where he had prepared an ambuscade , and while all the rest were engaged in the chase , the common hangman of Shrewsbury , one Godwin `` port hund , '' or the town 's hound , bribed by Edric to commit the crime , sprang from behind a bush , and foully assassinated the innocent ealdorman", "context_len": 74}}}, "noose": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "The first-named was the bearded ruffian who had so nearly settled my account in the valley near Petersfield , and now , knowing full well that his neck was already in the hangman \u2019 s noose , his demeanour was one of sullen ferocity , and , though he was heavily manacled , his appearance was like that of a savage beast awaiting its opportunity to spring", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "She was within a few yards of the fort when Jim reached her side , and grasping the rope with both hands , he was in the act of widening the noose when he was struck heavily across the shoulders by a second lasso , and before he could even throw up his arms they were bound tightly to his side", "context_len": 61}}}, "debased": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The other , Keeping , did not appear to be of the same debased kind as his companion , though his matted red hair and sunburnt face and arms betokened a villain whose existence had been of an out-door kind", "context_len": 40}}}, "concluding": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 9, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "fettered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 102, "context": "Mark Penelly had gone over to the other side of the lugger while the conversation was going on , and he did not face the man he looked upon as his rival ; while Harry , unnoticed by the busy fishers as he swam round , went on , touching the sides of the lugger as he lightly swam , but only the next moment to find himself entangled in a quantity of the thin mackerel net , which seemed somehow to descend upon him like a cloud , and before he could realise the fact he was under water , hopelessly fettered by the net , and feeling that if he could not extricate himself directly he should be a dead man", "context_len": 123}}}, "imprecations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "I was the first witness called , and on concluding my evidence , which dealt solely with the first prisoner , Swyre leant across the front of the dock , raised his fettered hands , and with a terrible oath poured out the most frightful imprecations against me , vowing that sooner or later his mates would doubly avenge themselves on my miserable carcass , till at length , by dint of blows liberally bestowed by his custodians , he was restrained , though his low cursing and threats were distinctly audible during the rest of the trial", "context_len": 98}}}, "custodians": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "The party who had brought me to this remote spot were of an entirely different race from those among whom I now found myself , and the fact that we were making our way toward what was obviously the aristocratic part of the town , coupled with the expressive conversation carried on by the leader of my custodians with three or four individuals who had joined us , led me to surmise -- although of course I did not understand a word of what was said -- that I had been brought up the river as a peace- offering or something of that sort , which conclusion was again the reverse of reassuring", "context_len": 113}}}, "prosecution": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "Their first thought each day on coming home from school was to rush off to see how 'Sky Cottage , ' as they had christened it , had progressed in their absence , and their grief was loud if anything hindered Archie from the prosecution of his labours on Saturdays , while they quite envied Lilian being able to run out any time she liked and take a peep at the operations", "context_len": 72}}}, "usher": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The cry was taken up by the usher and repeated thrice ere there hobbled into the well of the court an object that could scarce lay claim to being called a man", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Then was Jane 's time to laugh with sheer happiness , to throw open gate and door and usher her guest into the old room where Tesuque sat and the Navajo blanket still covered the couch as of yore , and nothing was altered except that now other rooms opened brightly on all sides , and in one a piano displayed its white teeth in beaming welcome", "context_len": 67}}}, "fetid": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Yet there was no mistaking the fact that Hawkes was or had been a sailor , for a strong odour of tar , which was a pleasant relief to the fetid atmosphere of the crowded court , hovered around him like a cloud", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Here , lighted by the dismal glimmer of a few ship \u2019 s lanterns , a ghastly sight met my eyes , while a hot , fetid stench filled the gloomy region like a cloud", "context_len": 35}}}, "socket": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "One eye was covered with a patch , the other appeared to be fixed in its socket , inasmuch as whenever he looked he had to turn his head straight in that direction", "context_len": 33}}}, "pigtail": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "' I owe Mary something , ' she said , 'for she tied my hair-ribbon to the back of the desk on Friday , and when I tried to get up I was held fast by my pigtail", "context_len": 38}}}, "deficiencies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "Father did not encourage her to seek outside help , thinking it better that the Abbey should manage its own affairs , even at the cost of a little inconvenience , and kindly shut his eyes to many small deficiencies , knowing that time was the best remedy , and that old heads do not naturally grow on young shoulders", "context_len": 60}}}, "prosecuting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "But Jim Brown , who seemed to be acting as prosecuting attorney , had already summoned the two men to come forward and testify", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Your Honor , said the prosecuting attorney , the chirography is that of a person writing in great haste , and therefore somewhat difficult to read", "context_len": 26}}}, "hitched": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 29, "context": "He cut down a scrubby spruce , and trimming off the limbs within six or eight inches of the trunk , sharpened the points of them ; he then hitched the oxen to this hedge-hog , as he called it , and hauled it over the ground , thus scratching the earth over the grain", "context_len": 55}}}, "nether": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 79, "context": "`` Yes , yes , '' she murmured abstractedly ; `` who could fail to love him ? so valiant and so goodly to look upon , so tender unto his friends , and to me his poor wife , and ever was the inward joy in his bosom breaking forth in praises to God -- and yet '' -- turning wildly on Dick -- '' yet you will let him die ! You are as hard as the nether millstone ! Dick , do not shake your head , you must go ! You must force Prince Rupert to hear you", "context_len": 102}}}, "peculiarities": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` I know you do not like her -- I mean I know she is in every way your opposite ; but if you only would take no notice of her little peculiarities , and accept her as she really is , you would soon find good points in her", "context_len": 50}}}, "ard": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 29, "context": "An hour after midnight our mainsail , though treble-reefed , parted with a report that was heard above the storm , the torn canvas streaming out to lee \u2019 ard like so many whips ; and simultaneously our bowsprit carried away close to the gammonings , with the result that the ship yawed , then shot up into the wind", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The action began in a strong wind that , blowing athwart the tide , raised such a steep sea that most of our ships were unable to open their lee \u2019 ard lower-deck ports , a misfortune that more than outbalanced our advantage in having the weather gauge", "context_len": 48}}}, "bout": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 75, "context": "Course , concluded the officer , we give the feller warnin \u2019 that anything he said might be used as evidence ag \u2019 in \u2019 him , and I ruther guess he kept it in mind ; for , \u2019 though we talked with him considerable on the way back to Oakdale , he didn \u2019 t make no slip-ups , and he pertended all the time not to know nothin \u2019 at all \u2019 bout the robbery", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 15, "context": "The bishop received me very kindly , and talked to me a great deal a bout the happy days of Dunstan , when peace and plenty ruled everywhere ; but I led the conversation to the point I aimed at , and told him frankly how alarmed we were at Abingdon about Edmund 's tidings", "context_len": 55}}}, "ledge": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 37, "word_index": 33, "context": "What a noisy , merry , frolicksome stream this was , gurgling and splashing , rushing and tumbling in its rocky bed ; now leaping gracefully in a miniature waterfall over some narrow ledge , now rippling and singing about the roots of the trees and over the pebbles that lay in its course , now flashing in the sunlight , and now hiding in a crevice of the rocks as if it were playing at Bopeep", "context_len": 77}}}, "bales": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "That such a cave existed the children knew well , for Joe had once been inside , and had told them wonderful stories of smugglers in bygone days , and of kegs of brandy , and bales of silk and lace , and boxes of tea and tobacco , which were still supposed to be hidden in its depths , only waiting for some enterprising spirit to discover their hiding-place and bring them to light again", "context_len": 76}}}, "wreckers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` When I reported the matter to the authorities a body of men were sent from Wareham and Poole ; but though they discovered the caves , not a trace of the wreckers , their spoils , or the remains of the Speedie was to be found", "context_len": 47}}}, "indelibly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "Was this God 's 'best ' for her ? The letter dropped from her hand , and she sat for hours motionless , her eyes taking in every detail of the pretty moonlit room , until it was indelibly engraved upon her memory", "context_len": 43}}}, "misdeeds": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 62, "context": "So I preached upon the Saint of the day , who was pre-eminently a man of peace , and I took occasion to tell my people that there were many hurtful men about , who , like their master , Satan , were seeking whom they might devour , and that , like that master , they chose the night for their misdeeds , seeing they loved darkness rather than light", "context_len": 71}}}, "assizes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "The rest of the evidence was soon concluded , proving without doubt that both men were members of a notorious band of Dorset smugglers , whose misdeeds had caused the utmost consternation for years past ; and the case was settled by sending both prisoners to the assizes at Winchester", "context_len": 50}}}, "shattering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "It is unnecessary to dwell upon the account of my journey to Winton to repeat my evidence ; but on the return journey ( having heard both men sentenced to death ) , as we were passing through a wood between Twyford and Waltham , a pistol was fired at our coach , the ball shattering the glass and passing close to my uncle \u2019 s head", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "I sat in the drawing-room wondering what was going to happen , feeling that something must happen soon -- a great catastrophe -- a great shattering of that castle in the air which I had built so proudly a few months ago", "context_len": 42}}}, "surmise": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "That was all there was in it , and yet can you not surmise how even that letter was calculated to work great consternation in the mind of some one in the little thatched cottage -- some one who never saw the letter itself , and who did not so much as know of its existence until it had been read and re-read and thought over and answered , but who when one day he was made acquainted with its contents felt as weak as a kitten for hours afterward ? He happened to be lying on the lounge in the living-room at the time , the same lounge to which he had been carried more dead than alive apparently , just four weeks before", "context_len": 125}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was right in his surmise , but little did he dream of the circumstances which were to attend that memorable meeting", "context_len": 22}}}, "gibbeted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I was taken to see them gibbeted on Southsea beach", "context_len": 10}}}, "townsfolk": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "So they fared on with him in great state , with four of the Viziers for his sword-bearers , whilst all the troops and people of the city , both townsfolk and strangers , walked in procession before him , carrying flambeaux and drums and flutes and instruments of mirth and music , till they brought him to his palace , when he alighted and entering , sat down , as did also the Viziers and Amirs who were in his company , whilst the mamelukes brought sherbets and sweetmeats [ 493 ] and gave all who were with him in the procession to drink , albeit they were a multitude of folk whose number might not be told", "context_len": 119}}}, "arrant": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "Master Fallowfield , who , as I afterwards learned from the conversation , was the parish clerk of Worth Matravers church on Sabbaths and holydays , and an arrant smuggler at other times , took the paper and read in a sonorous voice a message from a neighbouring justice to the ill-fated James Harker , telling him that the reward due to the informer Crocker would be paid at any time after Martinmas", "context_len": 73}}}, "jeered": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "While most of the onlookers jeered , it was evident that some of his friends were present , and at one time it looked as if a rescue was about to be attempted ; but the soldiers kept back the press , and in spite of his violent struggles the prisoner was brought underneath the gallows , where a rope was deftly passed round his neck", "context_len": 66}}}, "criminals": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 25, "context": "And fearful places he found them to be ! Boys who were taken to gaol for the first time were put with old and hardened criminals ; the prisons were dirty and ill-smelling ; the dungeons were dark and unhealthy ; and , unless prisoners could afford to pay for comforts , they were obliged to sleep on cold bare floors , even delicate women not being exempted from such cruel treatment", "context_len": 72}}}, "quayside": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "This impression grew after an attempt had been made to burn my uncle \u2019 s house , and I had been deliberately pushed from the quayside into the Camber by a seaman ; and these incidents so preyed upon my mind that I was unfeignedly glad when I was asked if I should like to go to sea", "context_len": 58}}}, "burthen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "She was of six hundred tons burthen and pierced for fifty guns", "context_len": 12}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "This decided us , and , trusting that the downpour would deaden the force of the wind , we launched a small boat and pulled off to a galliot of about twenty tons burthen", "context_len": 34}}}, "figurehead": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Her figurehead represented a man on horseback trampling on a Dutchman , a Frenchman , a Spaniard , and an Algerine", "context_len": 21}}}, "trampling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "The wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "As she listened to the crashing of the swollen tide , another noise seemed to mingle with the sound of the mountain waters -- a sound of bellowing and trampling , as of a stampeded herd", "context_len": 36}}}, "hewer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "I was told that the horseman was supposed to be the arch-traitor , Old Noll , but a clever hewer of wood had caused all likeness of the great rebel to disappear , and had graven instead the features of honest George , now created Duke of Albemarle", "context_len": 48}}}, "likeness": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 95, "context": "The next thing , and sometimes a better thing than knowing your favorite author , is to know how and where he lives ; and it was a matter of supreme delight to Marie-Celeste that henceforth when she should open Lewis Carroll 's books she should be able to picture him working away here in his study , and just as he really looked , too , for by chance or accidents full-length photograph stood on the mantel , which Dorothy , from her visita few years before , was able to pronounce an excellent likeness , and very characteristic", "context_len": 100}}}, "longboat": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 62, "context": "And very attentively everybody listened to the story , as I told it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached", "context_len": 128}}}, "quarterdeck": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 54, "context": "Resistance was hopeless , but the pirate appeared anxious to take us alive , and , partially stunned by a blow from a handspike , I was thrown into one of the boats and taken on board our captor , where , together with five survivors , I was placed under guard on her quarterdeck", "context_len": 55}}}, "subordinates": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "His hair was a deep grizzled red , as also his beard , and his eyes were of the same tinge , his nose somewhat aquiline , and his whole features , weatherworn as they were , were those of one born to command , while they lacked the sheer brutality of expression so conspicuous in some of his subordinates", "context_len": 60}}}, "midshipman": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "Thus commenced the three years \u2019 cruise of my first ship , His Majesty \u2019 s ship Gannet , and I soon accustomed myself to the routine , showing a keen interest in the duties of a midshipman ; and ere long I could vie with my messmates in the most hazardous tasks that fell to their lot", "context_len": 58}}}, "midshipmen": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 35, "context": "Of the ship \u2019 s officers only three had gone to their last account -- the master , who had fallen a victim to the dreaded `` yellow jack '' , and two of the midshipmen", "context_len": 36}}}, "ins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "He appeared to be a keen young officer , knowing the ins and outs of everything , yet withal he was of a roguish disposition , and given to skylarking", "context_len": 30}}}, "outs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "He appeared to be a keen young officer , knowing the ins and outs of everything , yet withal he was of a roguish disposition , and given to skylarking", "context_len": 30}}}, "withal": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "Alaeddin 's mother returned home , shaking her head and saying , `` Whence shall my poor son get these dishes of jewels ? Supposing , for the jewels and the dishes , that he return to the treasure and gather the whole from the trees , -- and withal methinketh not it is possible to him ; but say that he fetch them , -- whence [ shall he get ] the slaves and slave-girls ? '' And she gave not over talking to herself till she reached the house , where Alaeddin awaited her , and when she came in to him , she said to him , `` O my son , said I not to thee , 'Think not to attain to the Lady", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 31, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 96, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "orlop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Having inspected the waist of the ship , the main and upper gundecks , he led me below to the orlop deck , where right aft was situated the gunroom or midshipmen \u2019 s mess", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Below the orlop deck we went , passing down a steep ladder to the flats , or part of the ship immediately above the ballast", "context_len": 25}}}, "purser": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 32, "context": "The lieutenants were all either killed or dangerously wounded ; the master , though he remained at his post during the engagement , had fallen through loss of blood ; and the purser , who took his part in the fight as bravely as the rest , had had his left arm shattered above the elbow", "context_len": 56}}}, "dips": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Here , illuminated by the glimmer of a couple of purser \u2019 s dips , or tallow candles , was the place where for the next two or three years I was to live and sleep -- otherwise my floating home", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "I expect , said Charlie , Uncle Isaac is there , and has got a fire -- won \u2019 t that be good ? -- to dry our wet clothes ; and won \u2019 t he laugh when he comes to see all these fish ? We couldn \u2019 t have carried fifty weight more ; she almost dips her side under every time she rolls", "context_len": 66}}}, "tallow": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Here , illuminated by the glimmer of a couple of purser \u2019 s dips , or tallow candles , was the place where for the next two or three years I was to live and sleep -- otherwise my floating home", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "I wish that I had never left the tallow business , that I do , for all this murthering work", "context_len": 20}}}, "cockroaches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Innumerable cockroaches crawled across the floor or attempted to climb the sides of the cabin , till pinked by a well-directed thrust with a sail-needle", "context_len": 25}}}, "pinked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Innumerable cockroaches crawled across the floor or attempted to climb the sides of the cabin , till pinked by a well-directed thrust with a sail-needle", "context_len": 25}}}, "mentor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "But my tour of the Gannet was by no means finished , my mentor evidently meaning to make me thoroughly acquainted with the ship", "context_len": 24}}}, "ballast": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "We transferred her double crew to the Eros , where they were carefully secured in the hold on top of the ballast , and , a strong prize crew being put on board by Captain Perry , we were not long in clearing away the wreck and putting everything back into place again , being ready to make sail by one bell in the first watch", "context_len": 66}}}, "amidship": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The amidship portion of this space is termed the cockpit , and , though nearly empty , it did not require much imagination on my part to see the forms of mangled seamen dimly outlined in the feeble glimmer of the lantern , young Drake telling me of some of the ghastly sights of the cockpit during action in a highly-worded and realistic style", "context_len": 64}}}, "cockpit": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "He , refusing the comfort of his own cabin , preferred to share with his gallant crew the horrors of the cockpit , and lay , with his head and shoulders swathed in bandages , on a rough mattress , as if he had been an ordinary mariner", "context_len": 48}}}, "mangled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "The amidship portion of this space is termed the cockpit , and , though nearly empty , it did not require much imagination on my part to see the forms of mangled seamen dimly outlined in the feeble glimmer of the lantern , young Drake telling me of some of the ghastly sights of the cockpit during action in a highly-worded and realistic style", "context_len": 64}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "Knowing he would be torn , mangled , perhaps killed , if the teeth of the great dog ever fastened upon him , Ben fought for his very life", "context_len": 29}}}, "realistic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "The amidship portion of this space is termed the cockpit , and , though nearly empty , it did not require much imagination on my part to see the forms of mangled seamen dimly outlined in the feeble glimmer of the lantern , young Drake telling me of some of the ghastly sights of the cockpit during action in a highly-worded and realistic style", "context_len": 64}}}, "stack": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 45, "context": "It was certainly Peggy who suggested sitting on the top of the haystack , and making it sway backwards and forwards like a swing , a delightful sensation while it lasted , but which ended suddenly in the collapse of the whole top of the stack , much to Father 's wrath , for it took David a couple of hours to repair the damage , and certainly did not sweeten the old man 's already crusty temper", "context_len": 78}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Oh , I just came over to see what sort of a football eleven you were going to stack up against us this fall , '' answered Tom easily", "context_len": 30}}}, "imperishable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "I could discern the heels of the fore and main masts , and the well of the ship \u2019 s pump , while farther away was a stack of imperishable ship \u2019 s stores , from which a number of rats darted , seemingly unmindful of our presence", "context_len": 48}}}, "respite": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 35, "context": "When we gained the daylight once more I blinked like an owl , breathing in the fresh air with a relish that the stifling atmosphere of between decks had caused ; but short was my respite , for my new friend asked me whether I would be bold enough to go to the foretopmast head", "context_len": 55}}}, "shrouds": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 41, "context": "However , as some of us were on one side of the ship and some on the other , we finally succeeded in getting at the slack of some of the ropes , and then , standing well up in the shrouds , we did our best at lasso-throwing", "context_len": 49}}}, "tenaciously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "I followed , clinging tenaciously to the shrouds with my hands , while my bare feet were tortured by the contact with the sharp ratlines", "context_len": 25}}}, "ratlines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "I followed , clinging tenaciously to the shrouds with my hands , while my bare feet were tortured by the contact with the sharp ratlines", "context_len": 25}}}, "futtock": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "For a brief space his body hung suspended on the inside of the futtock shrouds , then it slowly over-balanced and crashed with a heavy thud across a gun carriage on the deck below", "context_len": 34}}}, "foretop": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "The tops were to be utilized by sharpshooters in the coming fight , for to my delight I found a whole armoury stowed away on the foretop -- muskets , pistols , cutlasses , and two sharp axes , with plenty of powder and ball", "context_len": 45}}}, "topmast": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "And as soon as you have secured possession let me know , and I will send the carpenter and a strong gang aboard to help you to clear away the wreck and get another topmast on end before it falls dark", "context_len": 41}}}, "giddiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Five minutes later I was by his side , and I must confess that on looking down I experienced a feeling of giddiness that required a strong effort on my part to overcome", "context_len": 33}}}, "manikins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Eighty feet below , the deck looked like a long , narrow strip of dazzling white planks , the crew appearing no larger than manikins", "context_len": 25}}}, "lubber": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Hark ye , George Davies ! When yon lubber comes to himself , tell him to make hotfoot for Lyme , and put hundreds of leagues of sea betwixt him and us", "context_len": 33}}}, "shipman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` An old shipman whom I know , one Master Collings , of Gosport , used to say that swimming was a useless art , for when a man fell overboard his agony was only unduly prolonged", "context_len": 37}}}, "gaff": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "George was struck at the gaff and run up to the peak", "context_len": 12}}}, "hove": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "Three days after we left the island the Sea Wolf , for such is the name of the buccaneer \u2019 s ship , hove to in sight of Port Royal , and with the greatest audacity Pedro , or Red Peter , to give him the name he is generally known by , went ashore under a flag of truce , taking me with him as hostage", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Then , as if aware of our formidable character , they turned , two making away to the north-west , two to the south-east , while the fifth , though she showed her heels for a time , backed her main-topsail and hove to", "context_len": 44}}}, "capstan": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` More beef into it , bullies , '' urged the bos \u2019 n to the capstan men , and , as the strain on the cables increased , the rest of the crew , in obedience to an order , doubled along the sloping decks , as well as they were able , towards the bows , the whole vessel trembling with the motion", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Their next task was to repair the splintered foreyard , which they did by fishing it with capstan bars and small spars", "context_len": 22}}}, "pawls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Heave round the capstan , '' came the order , and with a patter of bare feet , the clanking of the pawls , and the merry lilt of the fiddle , the cable came inboard", "context_len": 37}}}, "lilt": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Heave round the capstan , '' came the order , and with a patter of bare feet , the clanking of the pawls , and the merry lilt of the fiddle , the cable came inboard", "context_len": 37}}}, "hearties": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Now , then , my hearties , heave and away ! '' And to an increased pace the anchor came home", "context_len": 22}}}, "medley": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` He also states that the French post was three leagues from the mouth of Yazoo river , close to a village inhabited by a medley of Yazoo , Couroas and Ofogoula Indians , and mentions the tribes in the following order ( I , 163 ) : `` Yazoo Indians , about 100 huts ; further up , Coroas , about 40 huts ; Chactioumas or `` red lobsters '' , about 50 huts , on same river ; Ouf\u00e9-ouglas , about 60 huts ; Tapoussas , not over 25 huts", "context_len": 92}}}, "bowlines": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "A medley of other orders , unintelligible to me , followed ; the sheets were hauled well home , the braces and bowlines made taut , and by the peculiar gliding sensation that followed I knew the Gannet was under way", "context_len": 41}}}, "vie": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Thus commenced the three years \u2019 cruise of my first ship , His Majesty \u2019 s ship Gannet , and I soon accustomed myself to the routine , showing a keen interest in the duties of a midshipman ; and ere long I could vie with my messmates in the most hazardous tasks that fell to their lot", "context_len": 58}}}, "messmates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "Dommett , the boatkeeper , received me without signs of surprise , and on telling him of my misadventure , he merely ejaculated a loud `` Well done '' , which was a favourite expression of his , no matter what caused him to make it , insomuch that on board he was dubbed by his messmates `` Well done Dommett ''", "context_len": 62}}}, "hazardous": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "The officers now debated as to the best course to pursue -- whether to follow the buccaneer , which , undermanned as we were , was hazardous and reckless , or to return with all haste to Port Royal , report our loss , and join with the rest of the fleet in the capture of the insolent pirates", "context_len": 59}}}, "refitted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "This change of mind was symbolized by the custom of the women of breaking to pieces all the household utensils of the past year , and replacing them by new ones ; the men refitted all their property so as to look new , and it was considered extremely disgraceful , even for the most indigent , to eat any of the new maize before the annual busk ( Sketch , pp", "context_len": 72}}}, "tideless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Our task was rendered doubly difficult , first by the oppressive heat , and secondly by the fact that , like the Mediterranean , these waters are practically tideless , so that the difference between the rise and fall can be measured by the span of a man \u2019 s hand", "context_len": 51}}}, "waterline": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "On this account it is impossible for a vessel to be left high and dry , so the operation of cleaning her hull below the waterline is performed by `` careening '' , or allowing her to lie on one bilge , so that the other side is raised above the water", "context_len": 52}}}, "careening": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "On this account it is impossible for a vessel to be left high and dry , so the operation of cleaning her hull below the waterline is performed by `` careening '' , or allowing her to lie on one bilge , so that the other side is raised above the water", "context_len": 52}}}, "bilge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "On this account it is impossible for a vessel to be left high and dry , so the operation of cleaning her hull below the waterline is performed by `` careening '' , or allowing her to lie on one bilge , so that the other side is raised above the water", "context_len": 52}}}, "manual": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "All heavy gear , including the guns , was taken ashore , the manual work being performed by gangs of negro slaves , who toiled and groaned under the lash of their relentless taskmasters", "context_len": 34}}}, "unaccustomed": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "But weighted with his long fur , and unaccustomed to swimming -- for he had never in his life before been in the water , and how he had learned to swim always remained a mystery to the children -- he yet struck out valiantly", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 41, "context": "Something was surely amiss ; but the girl as yet fully realized only one fact -- that tia , always so steadfast and strong and cheerful , had gone hastily from the room in the agitation of one who struggled with unaccustomed tears", "context_len": 43}}}, "niggers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "One is pretty certain that one will meet some worldly folk , who are inclined to think Natives niggers , Converts hypocrites , and Missionaries half-rogues and half-fools ; so that one must not appear as a scrub", "context_len": 38}}}, "blackamoors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "And , mark my words , lad , if ever it comes to pass that these blackamoors are freed , as Master Penn would persuade us to do , then these islands are doomed", "context_len": 34}}}, "combination": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 41, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "outboard": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Just as it swung outboard the sling on the chase parted , and the huge mass of metal fell into a barge alongside , crushing two negroes and tearing through the bottom of the shore-craft", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "At the bugle-call you will throw off coats and boots , put on life-belts -- suppose you know by this time how to put 'em on ? -- and fall in by numbers , facing outboard", "context_len": 36}}}, "gunner": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 58, "context": "Sir Richard , finding himself in this condition after fifteen hours ' hard fighting , and having received about eight hundred shots from great guns , besides various assaults from the enemy , and seeing , moreover , no way by which he might prevent his ship falling into the hands of the Spanish , commanded the master gunner , whom he knew was a most resolute man , to split and sink the ship", "context_len": 75}}}, "landlubber": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Dost want me to teach thee thy trade , landlubber ? Is it not time that ye learned to tie aught but a slippery hitch ? ''", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Bless me for a landlubber if I thought of it at all ! '' replied Felgate", "context_len": 17}}}, "aspersion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "This aspersion on the boatswain \u2019 s workmanship caused a fierce dispute , but this had not lasted long when it was suddenly stopped by another yell of terror", "context_len": 29}}}, "boatswain": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Ay , ay , sir , '' answered I ; and running down the poop ladder I gave the order for the boatswain to pipe the second cutter away while I went below to buckle on my sword and thrust a pair of pistols into my belt", "context_len": 48}}}, "dismantling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In less than three days the work of dismantling the Gannet was complete , and only the hull and the lower masts remained", "context_len": 23}}}, "careened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Floating some five feet above her usual loadline , the ship was towed into the mouth of a muddy creek , and there careened till the whole of the bottom on the larboard side , with the exception of the keel and a few of the lower planks , was clear of the water , disclosing a sloping wall of barnacle-covered timbers", "context_len": 62}}}, "keel": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 40, "context": "Floating some five feet above her usual loadline , the ship was towed into the mouth of a muddy creek , and there careened till the whole of the bottom on the larboard side , with the exception of the keel and a few of the lower planks , was clear of the water , disclosing a sloping wall of barnacle-covered timbers", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Promptly the undismayed crew bundled the passengers into them and lowered away -- an easy task , since the vessel was sinking on a comparatively even keel", "context_len": 27}}}, "desiring": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "They had been condemned to die ; but , owing to a slight difference between the admiral and the governor of Jamaica , their fate was yet undecided , the former wishing to send them to England to grace Execution Dock , the latter desiring to gratify the inhabitants of Port Royal by stringing up the prisoners in front of the castle", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "But when he returned home on flying visits , he found to his dismay that his uncle 's visionary hopes were growing instead of fading ; and from desiring a merely republican England , General Harrison had begun to dream of a theocracy as complete as that of the early Jews , and to look forward to the immediate inauguration of an earthly Reign of the Saints , under the sceptre of Christ Himself , as the Fifth and last of the great monarchies of the world", "context_len": 87}}}, "stringing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "They had been condemned to die ; but , owing to a slight difference between the admiral and the governor of Jamaica , their fate was yet undecided , the former wishing to send them to England to grace Execution Dock , the latter desiring to gratify the inhabitants of Port Royal by stringing up the prisoners in front of the castle", "context_len": 62}}}, "plaza": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "From the Mexican end of town , -- the old `` plaza , '' -- which antedated coal-mines and Americanisms , gleamed the little gold cross of the adobe Church of San Antonio", "context_len": 33}}}, "fronting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "According to Hawkins , the western building , fronting east , contained the m\u00edkos and high-ranked people ; the northern building was the warriors ' ; the southern that of the beloved men , and the eastern that of the young people and their associates", "context_len": 45}}}, "tropical": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 47, "context": "He had climbed to the tops of high mountains and gone down into mines which lay far below the surface of the earth ; had peeped into volcanoes and been shut up among icebergs , at one time had slung his hammock under the trees of a tropical forest , at another had rolled himself in his blankets in the frozen huts of the Esquimaux ; had hunted whales , bears , lions , and tigers ; had passed through all manner of adventures and dangers by land and by sea ; and at last was really coming home , `` tired of his wanderings , to settle down beside his dear old mother and spend the rest of his days with her", "context_len": 123}}}, "seaward": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "All at once a great ball of flame appeared just behind the Jap \u2019 s wings , and his nose dropped seaward", "context_len": 22}}}, "flashes": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 75, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}}}, "arming": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Others , arming themselves with a medley of weapons , hurried to the fortress , from which a gun was fired and then lanterns hoisted as a signal when a vessel was expected", "context_len": 33}}}, "musketeers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "In response to the gun , three companies of musketeers , with drums beating and matches burning , marched from their quarters to the fort , followed ten minutes later by a large body of pikemen , their arms glittering in the light as they passed by the huge wood fires that had been hastily kindled on the battlements", "context_len": 59}}}, "topmasts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "All night we worked like slaves , sending up topmasts , yards , and rigging , shipping stores and ammunition", "context_len": 20}}}, "warship": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "In eleven hours of darkness the Gannet was almost her former self , for , being the only warship on the station ( the rest being , as I have mentioned , away amongst the Antilles ) , the governor had sent orders that no exertion was to be spared in getting her ready for sea", "context_len": 56}}}, "wont": {"freq": 69, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 64, "context": "them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 64, "context": "not to go daily to the market of the jewellers and to clap up acquaintance with the folk , making friends with them and questioning them of buying and selling and giving and taking and dear and cheap , till , one day of the days , he arose in the morning and donning his clothes , went forth , intending , as of wont , for the jewellers ' market ; but , as he went , he heard the crier proclaiming aloud on this wise , `` By commandment of the Lord of Beneficence , the king of the age and monarch of the time and the tide , let all the folk shut their shops and stores and enter their houses , for that the", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 79, "context": "Then there was Pixie , the pony , who would come whinnying up from the further side of a field to poke her soft nose into the children 's pockets for pieces of bread or lumps of sugar ; there were numerous cats who lived in the barns and stables , and Tabbyskins , the stately gray Persian , who usually sat sunning herself on the pigsty wall , keeping a strict eye on naughty Jack , who was wont to harry her if he got the chance", "context_len": 88}}}, "equalled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 98, "context": "Bishop , appealed to these simple savages ; a sentimental ditty , such as `` The Anchor 's weighed '' or `` Tom Bowling , '' would hold them breathless and entranced ; `` Rule , Britannia ! '' or `` Should He upbraid '' set them quivering with excitement ; and they seemed to know by intuition that `` The Sailor 's Hornpipe '' was written to be danced to , and they danced to it accordingly a wild , furious , mad fandango in which the extraordinary nature of the gambols of the performers was only equalled by the ecstasy of their enjoyment", "context_len": 105}}}, "glamour": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "In the offing were five ships that many recognized as belonging to one Lewis , a renegade king \u2019 s officer , who , attracted by the glamour of easily acquired wealth , had seduced his crew from their allegiance and turned buccaneer", "context_len": 43}}}, "seduced": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "The fleet is miserably destroyed ; Brihtric , Edric 's brother , a man like-minded to himself ; accused Wulfnoth , the ealdorman of Sussex , of high treason ; the ealdorman , knowing that he had no chance of justice , seduced the crews of twenty ships , and became a pirate , like unto the Danes themselves", "context_len": 59}}}, "buccaneer": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 83, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "lighters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Nevertheless he could not have completed the task of fitting out so hurriedly if every piece of ordnance had to be brought off to the ship in barges or lighters after she was afloat , so he resorted to the hazardous expedient of careening her still more", "context_len": 47}}}, "resorted": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 41, "context": "By this time even Donald , whose powers of endurance were not yet of the strongest , was content to sit by , an apparent listener ; but much that Harold had to tell having little interest for him , he resorted to that little trick to which some discriminating ears readily lend themselves , of listening to what appealed to him and letting the rest go", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The council-house served , to some extent , the same purposes as the `` great house , '' but was more resorted to in the inclement season than in summer", "context_len": 30}}}, "expedient": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "Nevertheless he could not have completed the task of fitting out so hurriedly if every piece of ordnance had to be brought off to the ship in barges or lighters after she was afloat , so he resorted to the hazardous expedient of careening her still more", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "While feeling the need to draw for herself some line of demarcation between things expedient and things inexpedient , she does not appear to have fallen into the error , so common amongst really earnest and excellent people , of counting that the line which she rightly drew for herself must of necessity be the only right line for everybody else", "context_len": 61}}}, "hempen": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "With a rush and a roar the stout hempen cable ran through the hawsepipe , the vessel snubbed , swung round , and the next moment the cable parted as if made of pack thread", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Right glad would I be to see Falmouth once more other than with a hempen rope round my neck , or with gyves and manacles to prevent my full enjoyment of my native place", "context_len": 34}}}, "thereby": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 79, "context": "`` Upwards it leads to the banqueting hall , and you can comprehend my escape this evening , '' said he ; `` but our path is now downwards , unless you would like to go up and see the drunken beasts of murderers snoring off their debauch upon the floor as they fell ; oh , that it were lawful for a Christian man to cut their throats as they lie ; many innocent lives would be saved thereby , which those brutes will live to destroy", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "To those friends who thought he lost caste thereby he had always the same argument -- that he saw no reason why the cultivation of fields should counteract the habits of refinement and good breeding to which he had been reared ; that in the colonies educated gentlemen set to work to labour with their hands , and are thought none the worse of : so why not in England , where land is good and markets are plentiful , especially when it involved the keeping of a fine old property which had been in the family for so many hundreds of years ?", "context_len": 104}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He did this that thereby nothing might remain of glory or victory to the Spaniards : seeing that in so many hours ' fight , and with so great a navy , they were not able to take her , though they had fifteen hours in which to do so ; and moreover had 15,000 men and fifty-three ships of war against his single vessel of five hundred tons", "context_len": 69}}}, "cables": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` More beef into it , bullies , '' urged the bos \u2019 n to the capstan men , and , as the strain on the cables increased , the rest of the crew , in obedience to an order , doubled along the sloping decks , as well as they were able , towards the bows , the whole vessel trembling with the motion", "context_len": 65}}}, "clanked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The capstan clanked slowly round as the main cable tautened and came in foot by foot ; the negroes , the sweat glistening on their ebony arms and backs , bent to their task , encouraged or goaded by the shouts of their overseers", "context_len": 44}}}, "tautened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The capstan clanked slowly round as the main cable tautened and came in foot by foot ; the negroes , the sweat glistening on their ebony arms and backs , bent to their task , encouraged or goaded by the shouts of their overseers", "context_len": 44}}}, "soundings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The master , wild with rage , called upon the seaman to take soundings , and , this being done , it was found that the Gannet was held by the heel , the forepart being well afloat", "context_len": 38}}}, "forepart": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "The bos \u2019 n \u2019 s voice was heard faintly above the roar of the elements , and looking up I saw that , by the breaking of the ship , the forepart of the Gannet was raised in consequence of the \u2019 midship portion subsiding , and that her bows were nearly level with a flat , rocky ledge but twenty feet away", "context_len": 64}}}, "rakish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "She was a long , rakish , yellow-sided craft , evidently built for speed , and her audacity puzzled us mightily ; but knowing the diabolical cunning of these freebooters , we were determined to take no chance of a surprise", "context_len": 41}}}, "bowline": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Only one of the enemy reached our deck , and he was dragged on board by the clever cast of a running bowline thrown by a seaman , who , seizing his captive in his powerful grasp , demanded and obtained his surrender at the point of a gleaming knife , while his comrades roared with laughter at the hapless Dutchman \u2019 s discomfiture", "context_len": 64}}}, "gunnery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` I believe they do not try to hit him , '' replied the master ; `` or their gunnery is far worse than yesterday , when they hulled the Whitby", "context_len": 31}}}, "hulled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` I believe they do not try to hit him , '' replied the master ; `` or their gunnery is far worse than yesterday , when they hulled the Whitby", "context_len": 31}}}, "dorsal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Following the direction of his finger , we perceived the dorsal fins of two enormous sharks as they cleft their way towards the swimmer ; but , frightened by the splash of the shots , they contented themselves by swimming in large semicircles between us and the fugitive", "context_len": 48}}}, "semicircles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Following the direction of his finger , we perceived the dorsal fins of two enormous sharks as they cleft their way towards the swimmer ; but , frightened by the splash of the shots , they contented themselves by swimming in large semicircles between us and the fugitive", "context_len": 48}}}, "buccaneering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Often in my younger days had old Henry Martin and Master Collings told me tales of a buccaneering Captain Kidd and his bloodthirsty henchman , a renegade Scotsman called Angus Priddys , whose career was ended at Execution Dock ; so I formed a conclusion that these smugglers were men whose illicit dealings were not the worst of their accomplishments", "context_len": 60}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Directly this last body of men embarked the sails were shaken out , and the swift buccaneering craft stood seaward", "context_len": 20}}}, "swivel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Several of the boats were sunk by a well-directed fire from our swivel guns , but eight or nine gained the side of the Prince Royal , and , passing under the comparative shelter afforded by our lofty stern , boarded us on the starboard side , where , owing to the list , our bulwarks were much lower than on the other side", "context_len": 64}}}, "reappear": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "An involuntary cry of horror broke from us ; then , to our surprise , we saw the man reappear , brandishing a sheath-knife , while the shark , in its last throes , floated belly uppermost , a skilful thrust of the knife having practically disembowelled it", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "When a boomerang fell at a distance they would run as fast as they could until near the place , then stand perfectly still for a moment , like a hunting dog , make a dive into the bushes , and reappear with the boomerang in the hand", "context_len": 48}}}, "belly": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 22, "context": "Living on grass , and in the woods , with the milk from the house , he had not that protuberance of belly which swine reared in sties possess , and really merited Charlie \u2019 s encomium of being handsome ; he jumped up on his master and rubbed against his legs , with low grunts , expressive of satisfaction", "context_len": 60}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then , almost before her crew realized the change , the high grasslands of New Guinea were sweeping beneath her belly", "context_len": 21}}}, "disembowelled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "An involuntary cry of horror broke from us ; then , to our surprise , we saw the man reappear , brandishing a sheath-knife , while the shark , in its last throes , floated belly uppermost , a skilful thrust of the knife having practically disembowelled it", "context_len": 48}}}, "ungainly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "He came back in a minute or two , looking rather red about the face , and went back to his seat without approaching me , and at the same time I heard heavy , ungainly steps rushing downstairs , and Mr", "context_len": 42}}}, "raven": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "Perhaps it was because he was one of those rare characters in whose presence we can feel certain of perfect sympathy , or perhaps it grew from a more subtle and silent bond , felt keenly by the child , though never spoken of , for Peggy could remember a time when the Rector 's hair was raven black , and there had been a little Raymond Howell playing about on the smooth lawns of the old garden", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "Each of the lofty ships bore the ensign of its commander ; some carried at the prow the figures of lions , some of bulls , dolphins , dragons , or armed warriors , gaudily painted or even gilded ; while others bore from their mast the ensign of voracious birds -- the eagle , the raven -- which appeared to stretch their wings as the flag expanded in the wind", "context_len": 71}}}, "unconcernedly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "The tension was relieved by the woman , who looked at both the young fellows , and then turned to the surgeon and asked almost unconcernedly , `` Ai n't war a hell of a thing ? ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "dram": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "They do just like some folks at the store , when anybody asks them to take a dram ; they say they don \u2019 t know as it \u2019 s worth while , or as they have any occasion , but they always take it , for all that", "context_len": 49}}}, "draining": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Peggy shook her head , for speech seemed almost impossible at that moment , and she broke away from the keeper 's eager inquiries to kneel down by Rollo 's side , trying vainly to staunch the crimson stream that was draining his life away", "context_len": 45}}}, "episode": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "During this episode the buccaneer had shown us a remarkably clean pair of heels , so that nothing short of an accident to the crowd of canvas she was carrying could ever make us hope to overhaul her", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 11, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "rascally": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "After firing for some time the rascally crew apparently came to the conclusion that they were doing too much damage to their own sails and rigging , the fore-topsail being holed in many places ; so I could look around in comparative security", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Fortunately I was spared the ordeal of attending the trial , but I heard that the gallows or transportation to the West Indies accounted for the whole of the rascally crew , against whom the barbaric crime of wrecking , as well as smuggling , was proved right up to the hilt", "context_len": 52}}}, "relapsing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then , having completed our inspection , we seated ourselves on the framework of the rack , relapsing into a silence that was broken only by the occasional scamper of a swarm of rats across the floor , and the rippling of a stream of water outside the thick stone walls of our prison", "context_len": 54}}}, "subservient": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "At the mention of the `` cat '' the man \u2019 s eyes glittered ominously , then , instantly relapsing into his subservient manner , he jabbered in broken English :", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Though Tom Hadley had generally been subservient to him , he knew very well that if any difficulty should arise between them Tom would be a formidable antagonist", "context_len": 28}}}, "jabbered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "In response to this imperative demand , the native jabbered out directions , telling Bomba the course he must take to reach Snake Island if he did not wish to be swept to destruction by the rapids of the River of Death", "context_len": 42}}}, "vexes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` No ! '' roared Captain Poynings , knitting his brows in that manner peculiar to him when aught vexes him", "context_len": 21}}}, "lieutenants": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The lieutenants were all either killed or dangerously wounded ; the master , though he remained at his post during the engagement , had fallen through loss of blood ; and the purser , who took his part in the fight as bravely as the rest , had had his left arm shattered above the elbow", "context_len": 56}}}, "cay": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "A few days after leaving port yellow fever decimated the crew , and the survivors , unable to handle the ship , ran her aground on a small cay in the Rosario Channel , between the Isla de Pinos and Cuba", "context_len": 41}}}, "pertaining": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "Alexander 's was a simple burial procession , going from the palace where he died to the proper cemetery -- a march of a thousand miles , it is true , but all within his own dominions The greatness of it resulted simply from the magnitude of the scale on which every thing pertaining to the mighty here was performed , for it was nothing but a simple passage from the dwelling to the burial-ground on his own estates , after all", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "The word fihi in the magician 's instructions might indeed be taken in this latter sense , but may just as well be read `` thereto '' or `` pertaining thereto '' as `` therein", "context_len": 35}}}, "trove": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Be it understood that I will deal fairly with all men , dividing the residue into shares according to the regulations pertaining to treasure trove", "context_len": 25}}}, "forestall": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Let no stranger learn and forestall our mission , and I warrant ye the purser shall pay in gold where heretofore ye had but silver", "context_len": 25}}}, "heretofore": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "They were now going to contend , not for single provinces and kingdoms , as heretofore , but for general empire ; and the victory which they were about to achieve would place them on the summit of human glory", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "For the first time , Alden broke the rule which had governed him heretofore : he spoke sharply to the pony and jerked the bridle rein", "context_len": 26}}}, "specie": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "In gratitude , the Spaniard showed his rescuer a plan of where the specie lay , the men agreeing to share the spoil", "context_len": 23}}}, "decimated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "At that epoch the Moskoquis formed a powerful separate republic in the northwest of Mexico ; they succored him with a numerous body of warriors , but were frightfully decimated by the Spaniards , who dismembered Montezuma 's domain , and almost completely depopulated it", "context_len": 45}}}, "galliot": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` \u2019 Bout ship ! '' shouted Drake , bearing down on the long tiller , and the galliot , her sails flapping in the wind like the wings of a wounded bird , came about slowly yet surely , the breeze filled her sails as she lay on the other tack , and once more she slipped into deep water", "context_len": 61}}}, "entrusted": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 14, "context": "Naturally this called forth inquiry , and it soon became known that Percy had entrusted to Seabrooke 's keeping a large sum of money , lest he should himself be tempted to spend any portion of it , as it was to be reserved for a special purpose ; that Seabrooke before going to the dinner on the previous evening had put it , as he supposed , in a secure place , and that this morning the money was gone , while he had discovered slight but unmistakable evidence that his quarters had been ransacked in search of it", "context_len": 100}}}, "hazard": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Already the lust for gold had entered into his soul , and he was ready to hazard everything for the sake of that which had cost the lives of hundreds of men in these seas -- the quest of hidden treasure", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Those ( says he ) who seek to discover hidden things and know the future have invented an art which they call tracing or smiting the sand ; to wit , they take paper or sand or flour and trace thereon at hazard four rows of points , which operation , three times repeated ( i", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "We are ready to follow you at the hazard of our lives , and to march wherever you may lead us", "context_len": 21}}}, "dumbfounded": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Right and left scattered the dumbfounded spectators , and without any attempt at obstruction the fugitives gained the open and unfrequented part of the quay", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Gillis and Dorn , the white rubber hunters , had been dumbfounded at the marvelous accuracy of his shooting", "context_len": 19}}}, "trice": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 71, "context": "He was absent on the third of these little excursions of his , and was surreptitiously amusing his audacious little self by seeing how it seemed to sit in the Oueen 's own stall , when hark ! -- yes , that was going to be The Roseate Hues , and with a bound that came near bringing the royal draperies with him he was out of the stall in a trice and fairly scrambling up the organ stairs", "context_len": 79}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "At each of her guns on the starboard side were men lying prone on the deck , waiting for the signal to trice up the ports , run the guns out , and deliver a crushing broadside", "context_len": 37}}}, "zero": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Now , taking the longitude of Madrid as zero , we find that -- -- ''", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "The rain might descend in torrents , hail , snow and sleet might batter horse and rider like fine birdshot , and the temperature might drop below zero , or throb with heat , still rider and horse who were like one creature must plunge on and ever on , so long as muscle and nerve could stand the terrific strain", "context_len": 61}}}, "twill": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "One flash ends all ! O Clara , when my soul Hath ceased to suffer , can it cease to love ? Methinks , when quitting Earth , \u2019 twill still retain Her image , who was more than Earth to me ! It is a portion of my being , twined With every thought and feeling ; thou wilt weep , My Clara ; thou canst not believe him false To faith and friends , who is so true to thee", "context_len": 82}}}, "flagship": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "A hurried council of war was held on board the flagship , and here Albemarle made the first great mistake of his life ; for it is reported he held the Dutch so cheaply that he ordered Prince Rupert to take twenty vessels of our fleet and make to the westward to find and engage the French , while he relied on his remaining fifty-four ships to meet the formidable array of Dutchmen", "context_len": 73}}}, "westerly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 53, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}}}, "abeam": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "We then revictualled , took in fresh powder , and weighed , steering a westerly course till Negrille Point was well abeam", "context_len": 22}}}, "becalmed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "But , as luck would have it , the weather fell still lighter at sunset on our ninth day out ; and on the following morning at daybreak we found ourselves becalmed within three miles of a British cruiser , which promptly lowered her boats and despatched them to overhaul us ; and by breakfast-time I had the pleasure of finding myself once more under the British flag , our captor proving to be the corvette Cleopatra , by the captain and officers of which I was most kindly received when I had related to them my strange story", "context_len": 99}}}, "jack": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "Ten minutes later Drake and I were spurring hotly towards York , Felgate , by reason of having but one jack boot and wet clothes , being compelled to stay behind , and before long the massive towers of the Minster showed above the skyline", "context_len": 45}}}, "eighteenth": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 14, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "provisioning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Nevertheless , we made preparations for the expedition , provisioning the longboat and the like", "context_len": 15}}}, "league": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 41, "context": "Early on the morrow we weighed anchor , and , under the guidance of Pedro , towed the Gannet , there being no wind , through a gap in the reef , so that her new berth was within half a league of the landingplace -- though there was an inner reef close inshore , on which the sea brake , though not with such violence as on the outer reef", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Sir monk , '' was the reply , `` are you traitor to your king that you thus league yourself with his deadly enemies ? All that is done this night is done by his order", "context_len": 37}}}, "brake": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 60, "context": "'Dear little Coz , here I am , you see , and I assure you I have kept my promise to the letter , and have come home as soon as ever I could ; but home does n't seem a very cheery sort of place when all your relatives are off on a lark , and on your own brake at that , and you must fain content yourself with the companionship of your valet", "context_len": 76}}}, "mattocks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Between us we had but five musketoons and three pistols , the men carrying , on account of the heat , nothing but their mattocks and spades", "context_len": 27}}}, "parallel": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "a quarter of a league towards the shore , and the same distance parallel to it , the breakers preventing us from going nearer , we espied the mouth of the little cove or creek , and ten minutes labour at the oars sufficed to beach the boat in very sheltered water , the trees overhanging the banks and almost meeting those on the opposite side", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 52, "context": "The emigrant trains were disposed to keep to certain paths , where the face of the country compelled a closing in , but in other sections the respective courses were separated by miles , and , as has been shown the parties plodding across the plains , even though their routes were parallel , were often so far apart that for days they saw nothing of one another", "context_len": 68}}}, "peradventure": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "However , since we had been spared thus far , I concluded that we might reasonably hope to be reprieved at least until the next day , and I looked with impatience to be released and conveyed back to my place of confinement until dawn should again summon me forth to witness and , peradventure , be the victim of accumulative horrors", "context_len": 62}}}, "crackle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Dense foliage enclosed us on either side , and , save for the noise of the men \u2019 s footsteps , and the occasional crackle of dry underwood , not a sound either of man or beast broke the stillness of the forest", "context_len": 43}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "When the sun was high above their heads and all the jungle seemed to fry and crackle beneath the heat of it , Bomba and his companions sat down to eat the last of the jaguar meat and some berries and nuts they had gathered by the way", "context_len": 48}}}, "steeper": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The path led gradually upwards , till we emerged into a clearing , the ground rising still steeper to the foot of a low , precipitous cliff", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The incline grew steeper", "context_len": 4}}}, "cockboat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Looking backwards we could see over the tops of the trees to the sea , the Gannet looking like a cockboat in the distance", "context_len": 24}}}, "resuming": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "We are going to have a fine day , Albert asserted , resuming his seat on the steps , and with the authority of one who knows ; and the matter of the weather being thus satisfactorily disposed of , Marie-Celeste made so bold as to introduce another subject ; and as it chanced to meet with Albert 's approval , they chatted merrily together for ever so long", "context_len": 69}}}, "projections": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "On resuming our march we had to scale the cliff , which , though steep , was jagged with projections of rock , which , with strong tendrils of tropical vegetation , afforded plenty of foothold for any ordinary climber to make use of", "context_len": 44}}}, "tendrils": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "On resuming our march we had to scale the cliff , which , though steep , was jagged with projections of rock , which , with strong tendrils of tropical vegetation , afforded plenty of foothold for any ordinary climber to make use of", "context_len": 44}}}, "climber": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "On resuming our march we had to scale the cliff , which , though steep , was jagged with projections of rock , which , with strong tendrils of tropical vegetation , afforded plenty of foothold for any ordinary climber to make use of", "context_len": 44}}}, "pest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "He says children are pests ; and when papa laughed , and asked him if he said that because he remembered what a pest he was when he was a child , he looked mad , and said no ; children were better behaved when he was a boy", "context_len": 49}}}, "buoyancy": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "When my turn came I leapt with all the buoyancy of youth on to the lowermost crag , grasping an overhanging tuft of reeds and grass to gain the next step ; but the reeds were as sharp as a razor , and before I realized it the palm of my hand was cut to the bone , and the blood poured in a stream down my arm and over my doublet", "context_len": 72}}}, "lowermost": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "When my turn came I leapt with all the buoyancy of youth on to the lowermost crag , grasping an overhanging tuft of reeds and grass to gain the next step ; but the reeds were as sharp as a razor , and before I realized it the palm of my hand was cut to the bone , and the blood poured in a stream down my arm and over my doublet", "context_len": 72}}}, "crag": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "When my turn came I leapt with all the buoyancy of youth on to the lowermost crag , grasping an overhanging tuft of reeds and grass to gain the next step ; but the reeds were as sharp as a razor , and before I realized it the palm of my hand was cut to the bone , and the blood poured in a stream down my arm and over my doublet", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "reeds": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 94, "context": "Though I was then happily unaware of the fact , the period of the great annual festival , or Customs , was approaching , and the joy of the populace began to find vent in nocturnal concerts inordinately prolonged , the musical instruments consisting of tom-toms , each beaten by two , three , or four performers -- according to the size of the tom- tom -- with a monotony of cadence that soon became positively maddening , further aggravated by the discordant squealing of a number of flageolet- like instruments made of stout reeds", "context_len": 95}}}, "gashed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` \u2019 Tis better to return with a gashed hand like yours", "context_len": 12}}}, "misadventure": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Dommett , the boatkeeper , received me without signs of surprise , and on telling him of my misadventure , he merely ejaculated a loud `` Well done '' , which was a favourite expression of his , no matter what caused him to make it , insomuch that on board he was dubbed by his messmates `` Well done Dommett ''", "context_len": 62}}}, "insomuch": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 63, "context": "He was a man of great strength , and a thorough seaman , but he often shipped as cook , because he had higher wages ; and a most excellent cook he was : he was also perfectly honest , and , like most very powerful men , of an excellent disposition ; but he would get drunk whenever the opportunity offered , insomuch that they often put him in jail , and locked him up till the vessel was ready for sea", "context_len": 83}}}, "seawater": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "However , he bathed my hand in seawater , although the salt did make it smart mightily , and , tying it with a strip of wet linen , he told me to keep quiet , so as not unduly to excite my head , which was by now throbbing like to burst", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "You put seawater in there and a little can of fuel in here and set the thing going with a match", "context_len": 21}}}, "musket": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 31, "context": "Evidently the Dutchmen thought our losses under fire had been greater than they actually were ; but they soon realized their mistake , and with thrust of pike , swordthrusts , musket and pistol shots , they were swept aside as fast as their heads appeared above the bulwarks", "context_len": 49}}}, "alertly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "We listened alertly , but no other sound was heard", "context_len": 10}}}, "ramming": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "She was not satisfied till she had seen them in his pocket-book , where she herself squeezed them into the smallest possible corner , next thrusting the pocket-book into the very depths of his pockets , and ramming in his handkerchief on top of that , `` to be sure to keep it all safe", "context_len": 55}}}, "stragglers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "Meanwhile the English stragglers were repeatedly cut off ; and did they despatch a small force anywhere , it was sure to fall into an ambush , and be annihilated by the pagans", "context_len": 33}}}, "treacherously": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "He had occupied it one well-remembered season , during which , in that very banqueting hall where we have introduced our readers , Sigeferth and Morcar , the earls of the seven burghs , were treacherously murdered at the banquet after Edric had previously made them heavy with wine", "context_len": 49}}}, "woodcraft": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "He was of New England , having joined us at the Bermudas on our voyage hither , and was skilled in savage warfare and woodcraft , for at one time he had been a member of the trainband of Salem , in New England , which town had oft been in jeopardy from the savages", "context_len": 55}}}, "trainband": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "He was of New England , having joined us at the Bermudas on our voyage hither , and was skilled in savage warfare and woodcraft , for at one time he had been a member of the trainband of Salem , in New England , which town had oft been in jeopardy from the savages", "context_len": 55}}}, "jeopardy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 72, "context": "Why should he be in the jungle , he a white boy , whose rightful heritage of a life with his own kind had been denied him by a cruel fate ? Why did he not have a home like that of Frank Parkhurst , a father who was proud of him , a mother who loved him ? Why had he been fated to have his life placed every day in jeopardy ? He had been cheated of what belonged to him equally with every other boy of the white race", "context_len": 92}}}, "assurances": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "But in spite of this , and all Bessie 's persuasions and assurances that the thing was quite impossible , Willie was not to be convinced that the voice he had twice heard was not that of the old lady who had left the money in the Bible ; and he did not cease regretting that Jennie had not seen her", "context_len": 61}}}, "tholes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Directly darkness set in we lowered a boat , the oars and tholes being muffled to deaden all sound", "context_len": 19}}}, "anointed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "The New Englander had stripped , and had anointed himself from head to foot with a dark , offensive-smelling grease , which , he assured us , would keep him immune from insect bites , and at the same time render him nearly invisible", "context_len": 44}}}, "immune": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "The New Englander had stripped , and had anointed himself from head to foot with a dark , offensive-smelling grease , which , he assured us , would keep him immune from insect bites , and at the same time render him nearly invisible", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "For it was a superstition among the natives that a mad creature was possessed of an evil spirit that made it immune to death", "context_len": 24}}}, "paining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "For my part I could not sleep , my hand paining me greatly , while I troubled deeply for my comrades , particularly my friend Greville Drake ; so by choice I paced the deck the entire night , till with extraordinary suddenness day broke and the sun rose above the horizon", "context_len": 52}}}, "overlooking": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "The offer was accepted , and the tribe was helpful in erecting a spacious fort of about three hundred feet square , on a bluff overlooking the river , and close to their village ( id", "context_len": 36}}}, "rearguard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then he made out a party of men coming down from the hillside , where they had been posted as rearguard , and with them was Pedro", "context_len": 27}}}, "skirted": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Some modern critics believe that de Soto 's army did not cross the mountains into what is now North Carolina and Tennessee , the `` over-hill '' seats of the Cheroki people , but only skirted the southern slope of the Apalachian ridge by passing through Northern Georgia west into Northern Alabama , and then descending Coosa river", "context_len": 59}}}, "undermanned": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The officers now debated as to the best course to pursue -- whether to follow the buccaneer , which , undermanned as we were , was hazardous and reckless , or to return with all haste to Port Royal , report our loss , and join with the rest of the fleet in the capture of the insolent pirates", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "We are beating about for one , but it seems a hard thing to find , we are so undermanned", "context_len": 20}}}, "kedge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "The master would not attempt to beat out through the gap in the reef , as his knowledge of the passage was none too good , neither could we kedge nor tow the Gannet against the wind", "context_len": 37}}}, "despondent": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I feel very despondent , '' said Lady Jane ; `` for although Irene is very much improved , there is a lot of the old nature in her still ; and when you are gone , even the Singletons will be away , for they are going to the seaside for the month of August -- to Herne Bay , I believe", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` A cheer for Boxer Hall ! '' cried Holly Cross , and the despondent players , grieving over their defeat , sent back an answer", "context_len": 26}}}, "mien": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He appeared of noble air and mien , but was evidently but a young man ; he was somewhat above the average height , and looked as though he could wield the sword as well as the harp", "context_len": 38}}}, "hostage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "Three days after we left the island the Sea Wolf , for such is the name of the buccaneer \u2019 s ship , hove to in sight of Port Royal , and with the greatest audacity Pedro , or Red Peter , to give him the name he is generally known by , went ashore under a flag of truce , taking me with him as hostage", "context_len": 67}}}, "enterprising": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "Her friendships were so keen and her affections so warm , that I think she found the world a very pleasant place to be in , and made it better and pleasanter for other people too ( which is , after all , the grand secret of happiness ) , and the energy and enterprising spirits which had caused the tomboy pranks of her childhood were qualities which , turned to good use , proved of the utmost service to her in after-years", "context_len": 83}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` I do not wish to say anything specially personal , '' he remarked , `` but just for once I should like to say , if I never repeat it again , that I think you are a most enterprising , and , let me repeat , most charming young lady", "context_len": 52}}}, "dangle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "Now , sink me ! \u2019 tis a fair exchange : give us the five and take your enterprising \u2019 ( how he sneered when he said this ) \u2019 king \u2019 s men unhurt , or else , for every man of ours who dances at the end of a rope , five of yours shall dangle from our yardarm", "context_len": 61}}}, "yardarm": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "Now , sink me ! \u2019 tis a fair exchange : give us the five and take your enterprising \u2019 ( how he sneered when he said this ) \u2019 king \u2019 s men unhurt , or else , for every man of ours who dances at the end of a rope , five of yours shall dangle from our yardarm", "context_len": 61}}}, "magnanimity": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "His magnanimity , also , in deciding that one single friend of his in a family should save that family , instead of ordaining , as a more short-sighted conqueror would have done , that a single enemy should condemn it , must have awakened a strong feeling of gratitude and regard in the hearts of all who could appreciate fidelity to friends and generosity of spirit", "context_len": 67}}}, "gyves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Right glad would I be to see Falmouth once more other than with a hempen rope round my neck , or with gyves and manacles to prevent my full enjoyment of my native place", "context_len": 34}}}, "manacles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Both men held him , and , in spite of his struggles , the manacles were snapped upon his wrists ; while Jerry , still sitting on the mow , pleaded and sobbed and wrung his hands , the little dog vainly seeking to soothe him by trying to lick his face", "context_len": 52}}}, "carouse": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` The same day the Sea Wolf came into the harbour and landed our people , Lewis and his companions were released , and , after a general carouse on shore , the ship sailed to communicate the news to her consorts", "context_len": 42}}}, "cutthroats": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "Years later I learned that both Captain Lewis and Red Peter returned to England and were received by His Majesty , who , with the same generosity as he showed towards Captain Morgan , Colonel Blood , and other cutthroats of like nature , restored to Lewis his commission ; while Red Peter , under his real name of Peter Tregaskis , became a red-hot Tory squire in his native Cornwall", "context_len": 71}}}, "sojourning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "However , to resume my story , Captain Poynings rejoined the Gannet without delay , and after a year or so of comparatively uneventful sojourning in the Caribbean Sea , we received orders to proceed again to the Mediterranean", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "This town , which was also , as it seems , the sojourning place of Tristan de Luna 's expedition ( 1559 ) , must have been one of the earliest centres of the Maskoki people , though it does not appear among its `` four leading towns ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "bowling": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 63, "context": "Now it was all up-hill , and the wind blowing such a gale that she had need to bend way over , holding firmly to her sailor hat the while , to make any headway whatever ; and now in a trice it was very much down-hill indeed , and the little knees had to be stiffly braced to prevent her ladyship from bowling along at a dangerously rapid pace", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "Hal had thought that Dodds would be far too big and grown up to care about playing with girls ; but when he found out that Dodds actually enjoyed playing cricket with them , and thought a great deal of Drusie 's bowling and Helen 's smart fielding , he began to think that he had made a mistake in supposing that he had grown too old for them", "context_len": 69}}}, "pristine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Her speed was retarded by the presence of a thick vegetable growth on her bottom , her sails had lost their pristine beauty , while her sides , though often repainted , bore signs of the effect of torrid heat and the buffeting of the waves", "context_len": 46}}}, "repainted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Her speed was retarded by the presence of a thick vegetable growth on her bottom , her sails had lost their pristine beauty , while her sides , though often repainted , bore signs of the effect of torrid heat and the buffeting of the waves", "context_len": 46}}}, "torrid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Her speed was retarded by the presence of a thick vegetable growth on her bottom , her sails had lost their pristine beauty , while her sides , though often repainted , bore signs of the effect of torrid heat and the buffeting of the waves", "context_len": 46}}}, "buffeting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Her speed was retarded by the presence of a thick vegetable growth on her bottom , her sails had lost their pristine beauty , while her sides , though often repainted , bore signs of the effect of torrid heat and the buffeting of the waves", "context_len": 46}}}, "assumption": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "He kept whistling a jolly air , with a sidelong glance observed that his recent companions had turned back towards their camp in the clearing , and then , dropping his assumption of the reckless young adventurer , stared seriously ahead and began to figure out the situation in all its details", "context_len": 52}}}, "requiring": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "In ancient battles the soldiers rushed toward each other , and fought hand to hand , in close combat , with swords , or spears , or other weapons requiring great personal strength , so that headlong bravery and muscular force were the qualities which generally carried the day", "context_len": 49}}}, "tactician": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Our captain showed himself to be a tactician as well as a fighter", "context_len": 13}}}, "transforming": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The work of transforming the man-of-war into a seemingly peaceful merchantman was quickly performed , and long before the corsair ( for such there was no doubt she was ) came within range the Gannet was floundering along with yards badly squared , for all the world like a helpless trader , her course having been previously altered as if she were intent on running away", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "All the contents of the house in London , which after her husband 's death had seemed too sad a place to live in , had been brought into the ivy-covered little castle , and under her transforming touch it had soon become as cheery and cosey as possible", "context_len": 49}}}, "floundering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "The work of transforming the man-of-war into a seemingly peaceful merchantman was quickly performed , and long before the corsair ( for such there was no doubt she was ) came within range the Gannet was floundering along with yards badly squared , for all the world like a helpless trader , her course having been previously altered as if she were intent on running away", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "And in the struggle it had lost the grip of its tail upon the bough above and had come floundering to the earth", "context_len": 23}}}, "prone": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "It happened to be a local holiday in Cumberland , and as a result here and there they encountered a solitary specimen of humanity prone upon his back or his face , just as it chanced , by the roadside , or , not quite so badly off as that , reeling along to wherever home might be in that apparently houseless region", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "His friend Heph\u00e6stion was with him , sharing with him every where in all the vicious indulgences to which he had become so prone", "context_len": 24}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "The fellow started back and opened his mouth to yell , but before a sound could issue from it Bomba had clapped one lean , brown hand over his mouth and with the other had thrown him prone on the ground", "context_len": 41}}}, "cutlasses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "No , I do n't want to hunt for any , thank you ! It 's girls ' stuff ! I are n't going to bother with marrying when I grow up ; I mean to be a pirate , and live in a ship with a black flag , and a lot of jolly fellows with pistols and cutlasses , and we 'll overhaul every merchantman we see , and string the sailors up from the yard-arm ! ' and the future buccaneer swung his legs over the rock , and put on a cut-throat expression , strangely at variance with his cherubic cast of countenance", "context_len": 107}}}, "muskets": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 12, "context": "His example animated the men , who immediately began to load their muskets and serve out boarding pikes and broadswords , while the master took steps to lay out a couple of anchors in readiness to warp the ship into deep water directly the floodtide should release her", "context_len": 48}}}, "exposing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 32, "context": "Grimly determined to make it warm for them when they did close with him , the defender sprang on to the roof , and , regardless of the fact that he was exposing himself recklessly , took up his stand by the flagstaff , and , throwing down his catapult , whirled his lasso wildly round his head", "context_len": 58}}}, "sweepings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Thinking us an easy prey , she made no hesitation in showing her true colours , while groups of dark-skinned men , the sweepings of the Barbary ports , clustered on her high foc \u2019 s \u2019 le , yelling and waving their arms in a truly terrifying manner", "context_len": 49}}}, "le": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "Thinking us an easy prey , she made no hesitation in showing her true colours , while groups of dark-skinned men , the sweepings of the Barbary ports , clustered on her high foc \u2019 s \u2019 le , yelling and waving their arms in a truly terrifying manner", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Their temple , described by le Sieur de Tonty ( traveling with la Salle in 1682 ) in French , Hist", "context_len": 21}}}, "terrifying": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Thinking us an easy prey , she made no hesitation in showing her true colours , while groups of dark-skinned men , the sweepings of the Barbary ports , clustered on her high foc \u2019 s \u2019 le , yelling and waving their arms in a truly terrifying manner", "context_len": 49}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 66, "context": "`` How are we to begin ? Shall we do penance like the old monks ? Do you know , Rosamund '' -- here Irene linked her thin , almost steel-like little hand inside Rosamund 's arm -- '' that I am a most voracious reader ? Father was a great collector of books , and when I am tired of frightening the servants , and terrifying Frosty , and annoying mother , I spend days at a time in his library swallowing down the contents of his books", "context_len": 89}}}, "taskmaster": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "The sounds of the oars , the rattling of the chains of the miserable galley slaves , and the sharp crack of the whip of the merciless taskmaster could be distinctly heard , while ever and anon a gun would be fired , merely to impress upon us the fruitlessness of resistance", "context_len": 52}}}, "fruitlessness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "The sounds of the oars , the rattling of the chains of the miserable galley slaves , and the sharp crack of the whip of the merciless taskmaster could be distinctly heard , while ever and anon a gun would be fired , merely to impress upon us the fruitlessness of resistance", "context_len": 52}}}, "tackles": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "All along the main deck there were groups of men standing in almost total darkness , waiting at the gun tackles for the signal to run out the guns", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Sort of object lesson on the way our Padre tackles sin", "context_len": 12}}}, "deathlike": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The feeble glimmer of the fighting-lanterns shone on the glistening arms and bodies of half-naked seamen , who stood in almost deathlike silence listening to the shouts of their unseen foes", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The treetops were still agitated by occasional sharp gusts of wind , but where Bomba and Doto sped along the jungle lay in an almost deathlike hush", "context_len": 27}}}, "truck": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 29, "context": "It was no work at all for those stout , experienced fellows to get the safe clear of the ruins , and , with the aid of a big truck they brought from the freight house , convey it to the new express quarters", "context_len": 44}}}, "rammers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Back ran the guns with the recoil , sponges and rammers did their work , and again the guns roared -- this time in an irregular broadside", "context_len": 27}}}, "casualty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He fell without a sound , and this was the only casualty on the main deck", "context_len": 16}}}, "lateen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Our ordnance had wrought havoc on the corsair , her huge lateen yards lying athwart her decks , while heaps of dead and dying men encumbered her slippery planks", "context_len": 29}}}, "foemen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "We had already suffered considerably , many men having been slain on our fo \u2019 c \u2019 sle and poop , and now , headed by our gallant Captain Poynings , we threw ourselves upon the foemen \u2019 s deck , where we met with a desperate resistance", "context_len": 48}}}, "ignominious": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 93, "context": "Can he speak thus who hath so long espoused The Royal cause , and served that cause so well ? Who , girt with honours , well deserved , hath stood One in a noble Brotherhood of Fame ! Where are the Cavaliers who fought with thee In battle , side by side , who with thee shared The feast , and drained the wine-cup to your King ? Where are they now ? what , gone ? not one remains , T \u2019 assert thy innocence , or shield thee from An ignominious death", "context_len": 95}}}, "desist": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "The captain and the wireless operator were the last to leave , the latter striving in vain to get the damaged transmitter into working order until peremptory orders from his superior obliged him to desist", "context_len": 35}}}, "breastplate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "In a measure he had an advantage , wearing his breastplate ; yet as the scimitar is rarely used save for cutting , the armour did not serve him as readily as it would have done if he had been pitted against a man armed with a pointed sword", "context_len": 49}}}, "swordplay": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Their blades met , and so quick was the swordplay that none could follow it", "context_len": 15}}}, "disengage": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "As the Prussian officer raised his revolver to fire point-blank at Sergeant Fortescue , Malcolm plunged his bayonet into the Hun 's side , while Fortescue reciprocated the service by shooting a German who was about to deal Rifleman Carr a smashing blow with the butt of his rifle before the latter could disengage his blade", "context_len": 56}}}, "bubbles": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "After tea I taught the lads Cross Questions and Crooked Answers , and showed them my splendid bubbles and my chatelaine , which were greatly admired , and my photograph-book , a great treasure to me", "context_len": 36}}}, "implacable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "In his abject misery and dread , Mark Penelly saw , in the stern gaze before him , anger and a vindictive desire for revenge ; he saw therein fierce hate , and an implacable , unchanging condemnation ; he felt that Harry was sustaining him there where he had dragged him to make his sufferings more acute , and that , after holding him up for a while , he would loosen his hold , causing him to sink at once into the deep water by the rocks , and be swept away by the tremendous current", "context_len": 98}}}, "surviving": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "On the Algerine all her crew were accounted for , not one surviving ; while , in spite of our care , the losses amongst the galley slaves were fearful", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "As for the guilt , it lieth with the accursed one , the surviving [ 661 ] brother of the Maugrabin enchanter , who feigneth himself to be Fatimeh the Recluse ; for lo , he hath slain Fatimeh in her cavern and hath donned her dress and disguised himself after her favour and fashion and is come hither , seeking thy destruction , so he may take vengeance on thee for his brother ; and he it is who taught thy wife to seek this of thee", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 78, "context": "She was the older daughter , a strong , healthy girl of seventeen , who never helped her mother about the work , who dressed in such cheap finery as she could obtain by hook or crook , who took music lessons on a rented melodeon paid for out of her mother \u2019 s hard earnings , who felt herself to be a lady unfortunately born out of her sphere , and who was unquestionably ashamed of her surviving parent and her brother and sister", "context_len": 85}}}, "reporting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 61, "context": "Albert gave Marie-Celeste a look which said as plainly as words : That jus ' American little girls could be awful mean , and evidently deciding it would be best to leave that kind of a girl to herself , turned on his heel and walked straight off toward the castle with a consequential air , and as though bent on reporting such unseemly conduct to Her Majesty in person", "context_len": 70}}}, "sextant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Captain Poynings , after deliberating with the master , came to the conclusion that land was not far off , but the weather did not allow of the use of either sextant or quadrant", "context_len": 34}}}, "plugged": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "Dat ain \u2019 t nuffin , he said airily ; de sarpint furgot dat I was on de lookout fur him and as soon as he fired and missed , why , I plugged him ; \u2019 tain \u2019 t wuth speaking \u2019 bout", "context_len": 44}}}, "orb": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "Whenever an eclipse of the moon takes place , it is , of course , when the moon is full , so that the eclipse is always a sudden , and , among an ignorant people , an unexpected waning of the orb in the height of its splendor ; and as such people know not the cause of the phenomenon , they are often extremely terrified", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He located the beautiful orb well to his left instead of in front", "context_len": 13}}}, "shorten": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "We saw the Gannet shorten sail , bowling along on the starboard tack under easy canvas to enable us to keep up with her", "context_len": 24}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "He endeavoured to persuade his men to yield themselves to God , and to the mercy of none else ; that , as they had repulsed so many enemies , they should not shorten the honour of their nation by prolonging their lives by a few hours or days", "context_len": 49}}}, "rove": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Then she seats herself , and , clasping two fair little hands round one knee , just waits , letting eyes rove where they will and thoughts follow", "context_len": 28}}}, "precaution": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 47, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}}}, "pumps": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "Side by side they were thrust on to a little low seat , and their shoes and pumps being quickly removed , were consigned to the keeping of a condescending boots , who , summoned by the touch of an electric bell , carried them away at arm 's length", "context_len": 50}}}, "moderated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "From her build and rig my craft would lay closer to the wind than the Gannet , so I ordered her to be kept on the starboard tack for two hours , then on the larboard tack for another two hours , and so on , hoping by these means to keep within sight of our escort when the gale moderated", "context_len": 61}}}, "gigantic": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 30, "context": "In fact , it is probable that Alexander 's slaughter of the Persian army at Arbela , and subsequent spoliation of Susa , constitute , taken together , the most gigantic case of murder and robbery which was ever committed by man ; so that , in performing these deeds , the great hero attained at last to the glory of having perpetrated the grandest and most imposing of all human crimes", "context_len": 72}}}, "timorous": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "This negro was of a gigantic stature , with powerful limbs , yet of a timorous disposition , so that directly the gale came on he could with difficulty be made to do any work at all , but lay in a heap in the weather scuppers , moaning and muttering in broken English , Spanish , and his native tongue", "context_len": 61}}}, "scuppers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "This negro was of a gigantic stature , with powerful limbs , yet of a timorous disposition , so that directly the gale came on he could with difficulty be made to do any work at all , but lay in a heap in the weather scuppers , moaning and muttering in broken English , Spanish , and his native tongue", "context_len": 61}}}, "speedier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "This we concluded was the Gannet , which we had evidently passed during the night ; but three hours afterwards we could see that it was not our parent ship , but a smaller and speedier craft", "context_len": 37}}}, "exhorted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "So I spoke on the Christian duties of love and forgiveness , and exhorted all present to take joyfully the chastisement of the Lord , even like holy Job ; and that it would all tend to their eternal good , through Him who , when He was reviled , reviled not again", "context_len": 53}}}, "futile": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was nearly noon when I was conducted back to my hut after my futile attempt to cure the king ; and it was not until close upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "The Afrikander , realizing that he was fatiguing himself with futile efforts , adopted semi-defensive tactics , in the hope that the Maori would close", "context_len": 25}}}, "unrecorded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Many a gallant English vessel has met her fate in a glorious but unrecorded effort in similar circumstances , her end unknown at home and her disappearance soon forgotten , save by those bloodthirsty scoundrels who have felt the fangs of an Englishman at bay", "context_len": 45}}}, "disappearance": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 16, "context": "Striding in among the group of boys who were still discussing the very question of the disappearance of the money which had been the main topic of interest ever since the loss was discovered , the bank-note in his hand , he advanced directly to Flagg , who was taking an active part in the conversation -- that is , he had been doing so within the last few moments , since he had returned after a short absence from the school-room , looking , as more than one of the boys observed , `` flushed and rather flurried", "context_len": 99}}}, "bloodthirsty": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": "away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed , with feelings of envy , that many of my fellow-sufferers had already succumbed and become unconscious , if indeed they were not", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` You bloodthirsty old rascal ! '' commented Phil", "context_len": 9}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was difficult , nay , almost impossible , to get a trusty messenger through that multitude of fierce and bloodthirsty foes ; and yet it was of the utmost importance that Sir Colin should have some one to tell him what was passing within the Residency , and show him the best route by which his troops could approach", "context_len": 60}}}, "foeman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "In spite of their number we stuck closely to them , the flagship of Van Tromp , who fought in a manner worthy of our former foeman , his redoubtable sire , being singled out as a prize worth taking", "context_len": 40}}}, "halyards": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was such a beautiful , stanch , great ship , with its large masts and spars and network of interlacing halyards , that its wideness meant more to her just then than even the wideness of the sea ; and she felt so safe and at home on it withal , that all the expected uncanny sensations had need to be postponed to some more favorable occasion", "context_len": 68}}}, "foresail": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "There was a shower of splinters and the broken spar fell , till brought up by the strain on the topsail and t \u2019 gallant sail , and at the same time the halyards of the foresail parted , bringing that sail down to the deck with a run", "context_len": 49}}}, "yawed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "An hour after midnight our mainsail , though treble-reefed , parted with a report that was heard above the storm , the torn canvas streaming out to lee \u2019 ard like so many whips ; and simultaneously our bowsprit carried away close to the gammonings , with the result that the ship yawed , then shot up into the wind", "context_len": 60}}}, "comprising": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 2, "context": "The vessels comprising the convoy were also cautioned when in the danger-zone to avoid `` starting '' ashes from the stokehold , and throwing garbage and refuse overboard , except at specified times , in order to baffle the hostile submarines ' quest", "context_len": 43}}}, "ruffianly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "She was heavily armed and manned , her crew comprising a ruffianly assortment of every nation of south-western Europe , and , judging by the gold ornaments that every man wore , their cruise had been highly successful for these rogues", "context_len": 41}}}, "transferring": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "Marie-Celeste , seated in the library window , and busy in transferring some great luscious strawberries from a plate on the seat beside her to a basket in her lap , is the first to discover a familiar little figure turning in at the gate", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "These two persons were Antipater and Parmenio , the venerable officers who have been already mentioned as having served Philip so faithfully , and as transferring , on the death of the father , their attachment and allegiance at once to the son", "context_len": 43}}}, "slinging": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "He came down feet foremost , however , and they sauntered off along the road to the old water-mill , where the miller 's man was slinging a sack of flour on to a patient donkey who stood , with drooping ears , eyeing the burden which he must carry up far into the mountains , while his mistress , a little black-eyed Welshwoman , poured forth a torrent of gossip in high-pitched tones", "context_len": 74}}}, "foreyard": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Their next task was to repair the splintered foreyard , which they did by fishing it with capstan bars and small spars", "context_len": 22}}}, "scrambles": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "Easter will soon be here , and Archie will come home , and then we shall all have glorious times again ! ' And Peggy 's momentary discontent vanished like snow in sunshine at the enthralling prospect of several new projects which her ingenious friend intended carrying out , and of the picnics , woodland scrambles , and other delights which the holidays would bring in their train", "context_len": 68}}}, "spliced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "He took a running start , and was about to leap full upon the sofa , when , at a signal from Phil , the three chums set the spliced piece of furniture on the grass", "context_len": 36}}}, "viciousness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Cruelty and viciousness were stamped upon every outline of his face , but at the same time there were signs of a courageous nature and resource", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "He saw other indications , also , that the high excitement which the horse felt was not viciousness , but the excess of noble and generous impulses", "context_len": 27}}}, "volubly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "The two pirates conversed volubly in an unknown tongue , then the renegade Englishman turned towards us again", "context_len": 18}}}, "inns": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "But who could long be grave at the outset of so promising an expedition ! The idea of a leisurely driving trip through the lovely Lake Country , stopping here and there , as the spirit moved them , at the comfortable little inns and hotels that abound in the region , had been such a supremely delightful idea , even in mere anticipation , that now that they were actually off enthusiasm knew no bounds , and mirth was literally unconfined", "context_len": 82}}}, "blackamoor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "The recreant blackamoor was almost mad with terror , his skin turned a dusky-greyish hue , and his eyes rolled about in an agony of fright", "context_len": 26}}}, "cutlass": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "So busy were they that I edged towards an arm-rack , and , placing my bound wrists against a sharp cutlass , I succeeded in freeing them from the cords that bound them", "context_len": 33}}}, "lethargy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 45, "context": "Aunt Helen was very long-suffering with respect to hobbies , for she rightly thought that the more a child 's life is filled with interests , the more chance it has of growing up an intelligent and broad-minded individual , and of escaping from that lethargy of boredom which swallows up the lives of many young people who ought to know better how to amuse themselves in God 's beautiful world", "context_len": 71}}}, "personification": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Now that I was free , my lethargy vanished , and I was the personification of active revenge", "context_len": 18}}}, "chainplate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "A hoarse shout announced that my escape was discovered , and a pistol bullet buzzed close to my head , quickly followed by another , that flattened itself against a chainplate", "context_len": 31}}}, "breastwork": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "They then built a breastwork of logs on the beach , and above the tide , reaching to the bow of their craft , to run the boards on", "context_len": 29}}}, "maintop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The only danger was that I might be picked off by musketeers in the maintop or crosstrees , the foremast itself protecting me from any shots from for \u2019 ard", "context_len": 30}}}, "planking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The planking of the top also was stout enough to resist a musket ball , though the thud of shots as they struck the under side of the top at first filled me with misgiving", "context_len": 35}}}, "holed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "After firing for some time the rascally crew apparently came to the conclusion that they were doing too much damage to their own sails and rigging , the fore-topsail being holed in many places ; so I could look around in comparative security", "context_len": 43}}}, "utilized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The tops were to be utilized by sharpshooters in the coming fight , for to my delight I found a whole armoury stowed away on the foretop -- muskets , pistols , cutlasses , and two sharp axes , with plenty of powder and ball", "context_len": 45}}}, "sharpshooters": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "The tops were to be utilized by sharpshooters in the coming fight , for to my delight I found a whole armoury stowed away on the foretop -- muskets , pistols , cutlasses , and two sharp axes , with plenty of powder and ball", "context_len": 45}}}, "trump": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "Still , terrible in defeat , more than half fought their way out of the trap into which they had fallen , and retired upon their camp , closely pursued , until the trump of Edmund recalled the pursuers , anxious lest they should in turn fall into an ambuscade , for reinforcements were awaiting the Danes behind", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Now , you know the fellow is quite able to forge or trump up evidence enough to be mighty unpleasant , so Lord Monck prays you give no colour to anything he may say , by frequenting the company of any suspicious or fanatical people", "context_len": 45}}}, "recreant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Listen to my last words , thou recreant boy ; Sweyn will send for thee early in the morning before the assembled host ; it will be the day of St", "context_len": 32}}}, "fiendish": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 35, "context": "But Huntington got what he deserved at last ( and the smile of grim satisfaction with which Marie-Celeste adorned the statement showed how simply enormous to even her childish mind seemed the crimes of the fiendish Huntington ) , for after he deserted Isabel he fell into the hands of some peasants , who knew what a wretch he was , and who took him and drove a chopper through his neck , and so made an end of him", "context_len": 80}}}, "deadeye": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "When the firing ceased I again looked over the edge of the breastwork , the deadeye lanyards making me practically invisible from the deck", "context_len": 24}}}, "lanyards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "When the firing ceased I again looked over the edge of the breastwork , the deadeye lanyards making me practically invisible from the deck", "context_len": 24}}}, "mainmast": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "The bow of the craft , a few feet aft of the fore-mast , was close timbered , as in ordinary boats ; but from that to the mainmast was a hole large enough to drive in three yoke of oxen abreast", "context_len": 42}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "George was struck , and a white flag fluttered from our mainmast truck", "context_len": 13}}}, "slings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Seizing one of the axes , I attacked the lower rigging vigorously , cutting through shrouds , slings , braces , and halyards , everything that came within reach , thus making my position secure from escalade", "context_len": 37}}}, "escalade": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Seizing one of the axes , I attacked the lower rigging vigorously , cutting through shrouds , slings , braces , and halyards , everything that came within reach , thus making my position secure from escalade", "context_len": 37}}}, "supports": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "Overhead , British shells screeched on their way , as with mathematical precision they fell in the place appointed , to form a `` barrage '' through which neither German supports could advance nor defeated Huns retire without risk of being pulverized by the high-explosive missiles", "context_len": 46}}}, "helmsman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "In spite of the frantic efforts of the helmsman , the pirate ship flew up into the wind , her maintopsail was taken aback , and she was hove to in a helpless state", "context_len": 34}}}, "weatherworn": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Then for the first time I could see the Gannet coming down on the Friend of the Sea , the sun shining on her clouds of weatherworn canvas", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "At four in the afternoon five weatherworn and mud-bespattered travellers arrived at Chatham , where a rowboat took us to Albemarle \u2019 s flagship , which lay at anchor in Gillingham Reach", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "His hair was a deep grizzled red , as also his beard , and his eyes were of the same tinge , his nose somewhat aquiline , and his whole features , weatherworn as they were , were those of one born to command , while they lacked the sheer brutality of expression so conspicuous in some of his subordinates", "context_len": 60}}}, "gauge": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "The action began in a strong wind that , blowing athwart the tide , raised such a steep sea that most of our ships were unable to open their lee \u2019 ard lower-deck ports , a misfortune that more than outbalanced our advantage in having the weather gauge", "context_len": 48}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Eliot was ready for this , and , seeming to gauge the distance the ball would travel , he took it cleanly and easily , shooting past the first man who came at him , dodging the second , and bringing the spectators to their feet by a run that carried him to Clearport \u2019 s thirty yard line ere he was forced out of bounds", "context_len": 66}}}, "blindfolded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "They were dipped into the rain-water barrel by means of a rope and pulley , and they were cast from `` the terrible height into the awful chasm , '' which ordeal consisted merely in being pushed down a space of about three feet , upon some hay , but being blindfolded was supposed to make up for the difference", "context_len": 60}}}, "shortening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Being sure that escape was impossible , the Gannet devoted all her attention to the hull of her foe , at the same time shortening the distance between them", "context_len": 29}}}, "encumbering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "There was Captain Poynings standing unmoved amid the crash and din of the fight , the master standing by the wheel , his head bound with a blood-stained scarf , several men , still in death , encumbering her decks , while amid the throng of excited fighters a continuous procession of wounded was winding its way towards the main hatch", "context_len": 61}}}, "interlocked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "As the crash came , and the two ships were interlocked , the Gannets , headed by their gallant captain , poured over the hammock nettings and gained their enemy \u2019 s deck", "context_len": 33}}}, "contested": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "About 1732 , the ordinary , though contested boundary between them and the Creek confederacy was the ridge that separates the waters of the Tombigbee from those of the Alabama river", "context_len": 31}}}, "spellbound": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "No wonder the child held her breath , spellbound from sheer delight , and no wonder , too , that the spell lost its power to hold her the moment she spied a darling , new little friend of hers standing in the chapel doorway", "context_len": 45}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Accustomed though we were to scenes of bloodshed and violence , this weird outburst , the concentrated expression of mortal agony , held us spellbound", "context_len": 25}}}, "trigger": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 39, "context": "You know dat las ' time we went at um ? Well , suh , I wuz shootin ' at a man right at me , an ' he knock my han ' down des ez I pull de trigger , an ' de ball cotch him right 'twix de hip an ' de knee", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The youth pulled trigger an instant before the other let fly", "context_len": 11}}}, "injuring": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Meanwhile Tige , utterly regardless of the commotion he was causing , or to how great an extent he was injuring his previous high reputation , swam steadily along , dragging the half-drowned pig after him , till he got among the birds , when , taking one in his mouth , he swam to the White Bull ; where Ben , who had watched the whole proceeding , relieved him from the harness , when he swam off and brought in the remainder", "context_len": 84}}}, "hatchway": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "But Captain Bill stood at the rudder-post looking fiercely down the hatchway at him , and when he had eaten what was on the table he dared not ask for more", "context_len": 31}}}, "treading": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 38, "context": "She stood for a little while with the gate in her hand , hesitating whether to go forward or not , then , thinking she could probably pass him quietly without waking him , she walked on , treading on tip-toe", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "In what strange world had he been living ? Had that sullen , desperate wretch been indeed Dick Harrison ? He realized that he was alive ; he could still enjoy the common comforts of food and fire ; he could think ; he could plan ! His feet were once more treading solid earth ; his brain began to spin anew the projects that had delighted him of yore ; his heart began to stir with the hopes of old", "context_len": 81}}}, "frenzied": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "She halted in her dance , and the snakes , uncoiling themselves from about her arms and neck , as though they knew that their part in the frenzied performance was over , slithered off quietly into the long marsh grass and sought their lairs", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "This had caused that frenzied yell", "context_len": 6}}}, "grappling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The boys now pulled up the grappling , holding the canoe stationary with their oars till the surf should come in to drive the ball towards them", "context_len": 27}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "She laid down her work to wipe her eyes , and , observing two of her sons grappling in fraternal war at the house corner , she arose to cuff each one impartially , exclaiming , `` Ea , muchachos ! You fight before my very eyes , eh ? Take that ! and that ! '' Waddling reluctantly back to her sewing , she saw Lola standing in the white-pillared porch of the big adobe house beyond , and a gleam of inspiration crossed the se\u00f1ora 's dark , fat face", "context_len": 92}}}, "appalling": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Quiet , for a minute , '' he whispered warningly to Pope , who , big-eyed and trembling , resembled a man on the threshold of some most appalling discovery", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 96, "context": "Very slowly and gradually the storm worked its way toward the zenith , gathering intensity as it rose , and at length -- probably about ten o'clock -- the first drops of rain , hot and heavy , like gouts of blood , began to fall , quickly increasing to a drenching downpour , accompanied by lightning , green , rose-tinted , violet , sun-bright , that lighted up the town until every object , however minute , was as clearly visible as in broad daylight , while the ceaseless crashing of the thunder was unspeakably appalling", "context_len": 97}}}, "suggestive": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "The arrangement somehow had a sinister , suggestive appearance that made me feel vaguely uncomfortable ; and abandoning the intention , whatever it may have been , that took me to the spot , I returned to my house , and , as soon as Ama made her appearance , asked her what it meant", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The great , ponderous , creaking doors were difficult to close ; and the wind would sweep through them in a manner suggestive of chill and rheumatism", "context_len": 27}}}, "unwounded": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "At least half of the raiders had been incapacitated , and among the wounded had been Nascanora himself , and all the unwounded survivors were at that moment rushing pell-mell through the jungle , frantically eager to put as many miles as possible between themselves and the cabin , where the white man \u2019 s magic had been capped by the calling of the jungle beasts to help", "context_len": 68}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Enough of that , boys ! '' ordered Captain Nicholson , who of all the company officers was the senior one unwounded", "context_len": 23}}}, "trestles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Stretched upon rough wooden trestles , or huddled in rows upon the bare deck , were dozens of human beings , some moaning , others shrieking and cursing in their agony", "context_len": 31}}}, "operate": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "The sway of phonetic laws is all-powerful here , and they operate whenever a slight conflict of syllables disagreeing with the delicate ear of the Cha'hta Indian takes place", "context_len": 29}}}, "smeared": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}}}, "surgery": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "In spite of the pain caused by the rough-and-ready surgery , the father grasped his son \u2019 s hand , encouraging and comforting the boy , as the surgeon probed for the bullet", "context_len": 33}}}, "mallets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Added to the dismal noises came the dull thud of the carpenters \u2019 hammers and mallets as they drove plugs into the shot holes betwixt wind and water , while the creaking of the ship \u2019 s pumps betokened that she was leaking freely", "context_len": 44}}}, "plugs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Added to the dismal noises came the dull thud of the carpenters \u2019 hammers and mallets as they drove plugs into the shot holes betwixt wind and water , while the creaking of the ship \u2019 s pumps betokened that she was leaking freely", "context_len": 44}}}, "leaking": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "Added to the dismal noises came the dull thud of the carpenters \u2019 hammers and mallets as they drove plugs into the shot holes betwixt wind and water , while the creaking of the ship \u2019 s pumps betokened that she was leaking freely", "context_len": 44}}}, "refitting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "I accompanied Drake on shore to pay a visit to the Spanish authorities , asking them to afford us assistance in refitting", "context_len": 22}}}, "deliberating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Captain Poynings , after deliberating with the master , came to the conclusion that land was not far off , but the weather did not allow of the use of either sextant or quadrant", "context_len": 34}}}, "headland": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "At the bottom of the meadows which bounded the Abbey land the river took a sharp turn past a few bold cliffs which rose almost sheer out of the water , and by scrambling along the rocks at the base it was possible to get round this headland and reach the low entrance of the cave , which was raised only a few feet above the level of the river", "context_len": 70}}}, "wrangle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Land ! land ! '' was the cry , and amongst the men for \u2019 ard there was almost a wrangle , some affirming that the headland was the Start , others the Lizard or `` The Bill '' , while a few sanguine men expressed their belief that it was the coast of the Wight", "context_len": 57}}}, "affirming": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` Land ! land ! '' was the cry , and amongst the men for \u2019 ard there was almost a wrangle , some affirming that the headland was the Start , others the Lizard or `` The Bill '' , while a few sanguine men expressed their belief that it was the coast of the Wight", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Mais n \u2019 importe ! the man , the man remains the same ! These locks have proved the keys to a Lady \u2019 s heart e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon", "context_len": 128}}}, "sanguine": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "How the engagement would end we knew not , but our spirits were greatly depressed with our misfortunes , and one and all , having seen that the courage and fortitude of our enemies had been unduly depreciated by our leaders , were far from sanguine as to the prospects of a victory of our hitherto redoubtable fleet", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "She would have felt less sanguine of success if she had heard the conversation which had just taken place between Mrs", "context_len": 21}}}, "gradual": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "And then I told her about mother 's gradual fading and the certainty that she would not live long , and the doctor 's verdict , and the one caution impressed and impressed upon me -- that she was to have no shock of any sort , that everything was to be made smooth and right for her", "context_len": 58}}}, "shoaling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "With great regularity the lead gave a gradual shoaling till twenty-four fathoms were announced", "context_len": 14}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The boat was once more put about and the lead kept going , but still the depth remained the same , or , rather , slightly shoaling", "context_len": 27}}}, "mainsail": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The master of the ship wanted him to `` cut his mainsail and cast about , and to trust to the sailing of his ship '' ; but Sir Richard utterly refused to turn from the enemy , saying that he would rather choose to die than dishonour himself , his country , and her Majesty 's ship , and informed his company that he would pass through the two squadrons in spite of them", "context_len": 75}}}, "phosphorescent": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Rapidly we drifted shorewards , where , in the inky blackness , a line of phosphorescent light denoted the breaking of the boiling water upon an ironbound coast", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "Vain hope ! For in a little while he could see phosphorescent gleams in the water behind him that he knew marked the trails of the caymans", "context_len": 27}}}, "ironbound": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Rapidly we drifted shorewards , where , in the inky blackness , a line of phosphorescent light denoted the breaking of the boiling water upon an ironbound coast", "context_len": 28}}}, "deadening": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "What he said I could not tell , the noise of the elements deadening all other sound , but to his question the captain merely shook his head", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Lem Wacker lay with his foot bandaged up , conscious , and in no intense pain , for the surgeon had given him some deadening medicine", "context_len": 26}}}, "deprecatory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` \u2019 -- -- Mine desires \u2019 '' resumed Master Whitehead , receiving the interruption with a deprecatory cough , `` \u2019 I doe hereby give full directions in soe that the treasures of mine house at Holwick may come to him by right", "context_len": 44}}}, "hawsepipe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "With a rush and a roar the stout hempen cable ran through the hawsepipe , the vessel snubbed , swung round , and the next moment the cable parted as if made of pack thread", "context_len": 35}}}, "midship": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "The bos \u2019 n \u2019 s voice was heard faintly above the roar of the elements , and looking up I saw that , by the breaking of the ship , the forepart of the Gannet was raised in consequence of the \u2019 midship portion subsiding , and that her bows were nearly level with a flat , rocky ledge but twenty feet away", "context_len": 64}}}, "appeals": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 57, "context": "Harold 's principal achievement lay in The Walrus and the Carpenter , and Marie-Celeste 's in the recitation of Jabberwocky from Through the Looking-Glass ; for not only was she able to slip its almost unpronounceable words quite easily from her tongue , but she remembered the explanation of them given by Humpty Dumpty , when Alice appeals to him a little later on in the story , and he modestly informs her that he can explain all the poems that ever were invented , and a good many beside that have n't been invented just yet", "context_len": 97}}}, "inhospitable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Some men eager for a glimpse of the country of their birth , which they had not seen for many a long-drawn month of campaigning in the inhospitable waste of Mesopotamia ; others for the first sight of the Mother Country ; others out of mere curiosity ; while the Maoris peered through the dim light to feast upon the prospects of speedily setting foot on dry land", "context_len": 68}}}, "landlubbers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Would I could get at you , ye cowardly landlubbers ! ''", "context_len": 13}}}, "nought": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 58, "context": "The wedding rejoicings continued all that day , with dancing-women and singing-women , and all the instruments of mirth and minstrelsy were smitten , whilst the queen and the Vizier and his son were exceeding assiduous in keeping up the festivities , so the Lady Bedrulbudour should rejoice and her chagrin be dispelled ; nay , they left nought that day of that which exciteth unto liesse but they did it before her , so she should leave what was in her mind and be cheered", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 68, "context": "replied the other , `` meseemeth thou art a stranger and comest from afar ; but , granting thou art from a far country , hast thou not heard of the Amir Alaeddin , whose repute , methought , filled the earth , and of his palace , a wonder of the world , whereof both far and near have heard ? How is it thou hast heard nought of this nor of the name of Alaeddin , whom Our Lord increase in glory and prosper ? '' Quoth the Maugrabin , `` Marry , it is the utmost of my wish to look upon the palace ; so , an thou wouldst do me a kindness , direct me thither , for that I am a stranger", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Indeed , '' said I , `` but I wish Edric had nought to do with it", "context_len": 18}}}, "bestir": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Now , men , bestir yourselves , '' said the bos \u2019 n , turning towards us", "context_len": 18}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Without showing any tendency to bestir themselves the men looked at each Other enquiringly", "context_len": 14}}}, "overbalancing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "With a hoarse `` Yo ho ! '' the spar was thrust forward , and just as its weight was on the point of overbalancing the weight of the seamen on the inboard part , the extremity touched the edge of the rocks", "context_len": 43}}}, "guideline": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The rope served as a guideline to the rest , and without further mishap the twenty-two survivors of the Gannet made the perilous passage , though after three years \u2019 knocking about on the high seas it was a sorry homecoming", "context_len": 41}}}, "beetling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The ledge itself , though level , was encumbered by numerous massive boulders that had at one time fallen from the beetling cliffs , while to the left ran a path which undoubtedly led to the top of the dizzy heights above us", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` I am , worthy sir , '' replied a short , stout man with heavy , beetling brows , who stood his ground doggedly", "context_len": 25}}}, "reveries": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "But there was no time for mournful reveries on the untimely end of our noble craft and her gallant captain , for already the exploring party had returned with the news that the cliff path had been found , and that a village was not far distant", "context_len": 47}}}, "coldness": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 72, "context": "not to let our left hand , know that which is done by our right hand ; but are we not also commanded so to let our light shine before men that they may see our good works and glorify our Father in heaven ? And can we do so , or truly show our love to him , if we hide the services rendered for his sake behind a mask of coldness and reserve ? My dear aunt , for his sake , for your own , for the sake of the affection and confidence which I wish my children to feel for you , and which I believe you wish to gain , let me satisfy them that it was really you who did this thing", "context_len": 128}}}, "illuminate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The light was not sufficient to illuminate the bottom of the pit , the hole being , I imagined , some thirty feet in depth", "context_len": 25}}}, "oubliette": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Here I was , then , in a kind of natural bottle dungeon or `` oubliette '' , such as I have often seen since , both on the Spanish Main and in our own country", "context_len": 36}}}, "dungeons": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "And fearful places he found them to be ! Boys who were taken to gaol for the first time were put with old and hardened criminals ; the prisons were dirty and ill-smelling ; the dungeons were dark and unhealthy ; and , unless prisoners could afford to pay for comforts , they were obliged to sleep on cold bare floors , even delicate women not being exempted from such cruel treatment", "context_len": 72}}}, "exploration": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "How , then , could I escape ? Climbing was an impossibility , so I staggered to my feet and began a round of exploration , carefully shuffling one foot in front of the other for fear of some hidden pitfall , making towards the sound of water trickling from the roof , a sound that seemed a long way off", "context_len": 61}}}, "pitfall": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "How , then , could I escape ? Climbing was an impossibility , so I staggered to my feet and began a round of exploration , carefully shuffling one foot in front of the other for fear of some hidden pitfall , making towards the sound of water trickling from the roof , a sound that seemed a long way off", "context_len": 61}}}, "ironwork": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "Carefully feeling with both hands , I discovered that I was standing in an angle , and right in the corner my hand came in contact with an object that , on inspection , proved to be a gun ; also , by the smoothness of the barrel I knew that it had recently been in use , there being no rust on the ironwork", "context_len": 65}}}, "illumination": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 24, "context": "The shadows ran swiftly over the ground and whisked out of sight , then came a brief space of gloom , and then the illumination revealed objects with diminishing distinctness , for a hundred yards out on the plain", "context_len": 39}}}, "casks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "In the part opposite where I was standing I saw more weapons , several large casks , and bundles of what looked like woollen and silk goods", "context_len": 27}}}, "replacing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "This change of mind was symbolized by the custom of the women of breaking to pieces all the household utensils of the past year , and replacing them by new ones ; the men refitted all their property so as to look new , and it was considered extremely disgraceful , even for the most indigent , to eat any of the new maize before the annual busk ( Sketch , pp", "context_len": 72}}}, "alighted": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 63, "context": "So they fared on with him in great state , with four of the Viziers for his sword-bearers , whilst all the troops and people of the city , both townsfolk and strangers , walked in procession before him , carrying flambeaux and drums and flutes and instruments of mirth and music , till they brought him to his palace , when he alighted and entering , sat down , as did also the Viziers and Amirs who were in his company , whilst the mamelukes brought sherbets and sweetmeats [ 493 ] and gave all who were with him in the procession to drink , albeit they were a multitude of folk whose number might not be told", "context_len": 119}}}, "layer": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 32, "context": "Some roguish boys , one day when he had been drinking , got him into a store , and putting molasses on his wool , covered it with flour , putting a layer of flour and molasses till his head was as big as a half bushel", "context_len": 47}}}, "crate": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "Where did you Fortress men get the idea that you could desert Sweet Rosy O \u2019 Grady and go gallivanting off with a silly little B-26 ? No wonder you-all had to come home in a Jap crate ! What happened , anyway ?", "context_len": 44}}}, "investigations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "It is also stated that in consequence of their separation the Seminole language had changed greatly from the original Creek ; a statement which is not borne out by recent investigations , and probably refers only to the Seminole towns speaking Hitchiti dialects", "context_len": 43}}}, "storehouse": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 84, "context": "Moreover , he brought him into a place , which he found full of coffers overflowing with royal raiment , such as ravished the wit , gold-inwoven stuffs , Indian and Chinese , and brocades , and he showed him also many other places , all full of that which beggareth description , till at last he brought him into a stable , wherein were horses whose like is not found with the kings of the world ; and therewithin he showed him a storehouse , full of housings and saddles of price , all broidered with pearls and precious stones and so forth", "context_len": 104}}}, "rite": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` He used to laugh a horrible laugh , His fav \u2019 rite cry was \u2019 Priddys \u2019 , His life he held in his own right arm , His soul was Cap \u2019 n Kiddie \u2019 s ! ''", "context_len": 40}}}, "illicit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "Often in my younger days had old Henry Martin and Master Collings told me tales of a buccaneering Captain Kidd and his bloodthirsty henchman , a renegade Scotsman called Angus Priddys , whose career was ended at Execution Dock ; so I formed a conclusion that these smugglers were men whose illicit dealings were not the worst of their accomplishments", "context_len": 60}}}, "smocks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "There were about thirty , all well armed and dressed in usual mariner \u2019 s style , save that two or three wore smocks", "context_len": 24}}}, "beakers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Several carried beakers on their shoulders , while two bore between them a small but heavy chest", "context_len": 17}}}, "carousing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "On one occasion , after he had spent a whole night in drinking and carousing , the guests , when the usual time arrived for separating , proposed that , instead of this , they should begin anew , and commence a second banquet at the end of the first", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "Presently , the princess called for drink and the handmaid immediately filled her the cup ; then she filled for the Maugrabin and the Lady Bedrulbudour proceeded to drink to his life and health , [ 606 ] and he also drank to her life and she fell to carousing [ 607 ] with him", "context_len": 55}}}, "dree": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` Then , '' resumed the speaker , `` if so be as that ye are all content , how comes it that one of ye must needs taake bloodmoney from the gaugers ? And how comes it that dree [ 1 ] of our \u2019 n have been stuck wi \u2019 a Bridport dagger ? `` [ 2 ]", "context_len": 60}}}, "resembling": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 9, "context": "K\u00f3sa is the name of a small forest-bird , resembling a sparrow ; but the name of the town and river could possibly be an ancient form of \u014d sa , \u014ds\u00e1 , 'os\u00e1 poke or pokeweed , a plant with red berries , which grows plentifully and to an enormous height throughout the South", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Like the town Apalachi , the inhabitants of this town spoke a dialect resembling Hitchiti very closely", "context_len": 17}}}, "blotches": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 60, "context": "The rarefaction of the air bothered me a good deal , producing something of the effect of a mirage , and causing the royals of the distant vessel to stand up clear of the horizon as though there were nothing beneath them ; yet , as she rose and fell with the 'scend of the sea , shapeless snow- white blotches appeared and vanished again beneath them occasionally", "context_len": 68}}}, "pimples": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "His complexion was dark , almost of a dirty yellow , while his face was covered with blotches and pimples", "context_len": 20}}}, "reed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 20, "context": "With some show of hesitation the man surrendered the thing , and upon inspection I found it to be a reed of about a foot in length , with a mouthpiece shaped something like that of a whistle , and with four small holes drilled in the length of the tube , whereby an expert performer might produce seven distinct tones ; but the tones were not consecutive , and the instrument was altogether a very poor and inefficient affair", "context_len": 80}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Stidham , gave me the following tale : `` Their ancestors first appeared in the country by coming out of a canebrake or reed thicket ( \u00fatski in Hitchiti ) near the sea coast", "context_len": 34}}}, "ow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` I don \u2019 t know as \u2019 ow ye \u2019 ve been taxed wi \u2019 aught , '' ejaculated the captain , `` but I \u2019 ll do it now", "context_len": 31}}}, "harken": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Not ye ? Ah , now harken ! Know \u2019 st Jim Harker , the court-leet man and king \u2019 s officer at Wareham ? ''", "context_len": 27}}}, "informer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "Master Fallowfield , who , as I afterwards learned from the conversation , was the parish clerk of Worth Matravers church on Sabbaths and holydays , and an arrant smuggler at other times , took the paper and read in a sonorous voice a message from a neighbouring justice to the ill-fated James Harker , telling him that the reward due to the informer Crocker would be paid at any time after Martinmas", "context_len": 73}}}, "gasps": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "He heaved himself out of his wrappings , and with a delighted yelp fell upon Peggy 's curls , worrying them with little gasps of joy , till she had to dive beneath the bedclothes to escape her too sportive pet", "context_len": 41}}}, "executioners": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Yet what a triumph ! One of the executioners , struck by the extraordinary fortitude of the lads , and their evident faith in another life , came and asked that he might also be taught to pray", "context_len": 38}}}, "impassively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The remainder of the rascals looked on impassively , as if thoroughly used to this kind of rough-and-ready justice", "context_len": 19}}}, "pardoned": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` And the Sultan said to him , `` Know , O Alaeddin , that I have pardoned thee , so thou mayst go and look into this affair and make me search for my daughter ; and do not thou present thyself but with her ; nay , an thou bring her not back to me , as my head liveth , I will cut off thine", "context_len": 68}}}, "conditionally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The captain signed for the pistol to be lowered , the bandage was removed , and the culprit , already half-dead with fear , was told that he was pardoned conditionally", "context_len": 31}}}, "lurched": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "Farther along the street stood a square , old-fashioned , story-and-a-half house , with a more modern ell and shed adjoining , and a wretched sagging barn , that lurched on its foundations , and was only kept from toppling farther , and possibly falling , by long , crude timber props , set against its side", "context_len": 57}}}, "hotfoot": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "I never was away from home more than a day in my life till I was married , and it 's been nothing but work ever since , and now to be laid here like a useless log , with everything going hotfoot to destruction ! It 's a good thing you 've come at last , for the children are makin ' sawdust and splinters of every bit of crockery in the house , and that Martha Spriggs has no more management than a settin ' hen", "context_len": 88}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` My advice to you is that you travel hotfoot to Holwick , and leave nothing undone till you lay hands on the treasure", "context_len": 24}}}, "revels": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "A careful mixture of gin and syrup was smeared upon the trees , which were afterwards visited with a lantern , when the unfortunate insects could easily be taken in the midst of their revels , falling sad victims to the sin of intemperance", "context_len": 44}}}, "rollicking": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "The perpetual play upon words , and the almost rollicking fun and nonsense of the whole , remind one of her earlier effort , The Pretender , already given at length ; though the later-written farce is in some respects scarcely equal to the girlish achievement", "context_len": 46}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "But what was true at five o'clock in the afternoon was not true a few hours later , and any one who had seen the same party turn their faces toward home , after a rollicking supper and no end of good cheer at Holly-tree Inn , would have prophesied disaster before they reached it", "context_len": 55}}}, "carnival": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "This was agreed to , and then each lad wrote his own telegram , explaining briefly why he was not in the woods , the carnival forming a good reason for the change", "context_len": 33}}}, "hooting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Again the whistle was repeated -- like the cry of some bird of night -- and one of the smugglers replied with a sound like the hooting of an owl", "context_len": 30}}}, "aperture": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 45, "context": "With levelled bayonets Malcolm , Selwyn , and half a dozen riflemen advanced towards the door in the rear of the pill-box , while M'Turk and M'Kane , each brandishing a bomb , ran close to the wall immediately by the side of the machine-gun aperture", "context_len": 46}}}, "silks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Rosamund herself opened the wardrobe and took out a quantity of beautiful dresses of all sorts and descriptions , mostly white washing silks and muslins and cambrics", "context_len": 27}}}, "wherein": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "Thus the time passed on until the first day of the Customs dawned , when , having received a more than usually substantial meal , I was stripped of the few rags that still covered my nakedness , and , with my hands tightly bound behind me by a thin but strong raw-hide rope , was led forth to the great square wherein the Customs were celebrated , and firmly bound to one of the posts , the erection of which I had witnessed a week earlier", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 63, "context": "Moreover , he brought him into a place , which he found full of coffers overflowing with royal raiment , such as ravished the wit , gold-inwoven stuffs , Indian and Chinese , and brocades , and he showed him also many other places , all full of that which beggareth description , till at last he brought him into a stable , wherein were horses whose like is not found with the kings of the world ; and therewithin he showed him a storehouse , full of housings and saddles of price , all broidered with pearls and precious stones and so forth", "context_len": 104}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 46, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}}}, "axed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` No ; but stop ! P \u2019 raps it \u2019 ll save questions being axed , and straw \u2019 s cheap enow", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "A lot o ' women brought their babies to Jesus and axed Him to fondle of 'em , and take 'em in His arms ; and there wor some men about -- ugh ! I guess as they wor some'ut like father -- and they said to the women , ` Take the babies away as fast as possible ; Jesus is a great , great man , and He ca n't no way be troubled", "context_len": 76}}}, "enow": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` No ; but stop ! P \u2019 raps it \u2019 ll save questions being axed , and straw \u2019 s cheap enow", "context_len": 23}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "What if your king has been insulted in his own banquet hall ? there are hands enow to avenge him without unseemly tumult", "context_len": 23}}}, "spyhole": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Soon the light was sufficient for me to see out of my spyhole", "context_len": 13}}}, "mists": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "'It will not be , dear child , when the mists have rolled away , and in the clear light of the Sun of Righteousness you look across to the other shore", "context_len": 32}}}, "fleecy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "And suppose '' -- as she drew the thin coverings over her -- '' suppose this was a beautiful soft bed , with fleecy blankets and large downy pillows", "context_len": 29}}}, "roofed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Here and there the trench was roofed in , where , from experience , men had learnt it was unhealthy owing to being exposed to machine-gun fire", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "Bartram , who saw it in 1775 , depicts it as the largest , most compact , and best situated Indian town he ever saw ; the habitations were large and neatly built , the walls of the houses consisted of a wooden frame , lathed and plastered inside and outside with a reddish clay , and roofed with cypress bark or shingles", "context_len": 63}}}, "riven": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "So strong was the construction of the masonry that the work of destruction was only partially done , though the keep was riven from base to summit , and several of the smaller towers were thrown bodily out of plumb", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Farther ahead masses of solid rock and earth were falling with a succession of thuds , while , where Messines Ridge had been , was a riven mound of disintegrated Soil , over which a dense cloud of black smoke rolled sullenly in the sultry night air", "context_len": 47}}}, "barrenness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "This much I had heard from report , and now , in spite of my cramped position , and faintness from want of food , I could not help looking with interest on the shattered walls , which still showed the black marks of the powder , though now , after a lapse of twenty years , their barrenness was beginning to be hidden by a kindly garb of ivy", "context_len": 70}}}, "ivy": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 4, "context": "The wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his", "context_len": 128}}}, "sorcery": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 113, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "peasantry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "He is so tired with the threshing just now , poor darling ! and , as he said one night : `` You see , my dear , I have so many troubles of my own at present that the trials of the French peasantry of a hundred years ago seem an affair of quite minor importance", "context_len": 57}}}, "unload": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "This was done , the canvas fell in a heap on the ground , and the men began to unload the straw", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "In due time the camping site was reached , the teamster helped them unload , and then drove back , leaving the four chums alone in quite a lonely stretch of wilderness", "context_len": 32}}}, "overthrowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "With a bound I sprang from the cart , nearly overthrowing the astonished men , who yelled with terror , as if his Satanic Majesty had suddenly appeared", "context_len": 28}}}, "cowherd": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Here I met a cowherd , who looked at me in astonishment , my clothes being in rags and covered with wisps of straw , while my face , blackened with dirt , was surmounted by a crop of ruffled hair that did duty for a hat", "context_len": 47}}}, "wisps": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Here I met a cowherd , who looked at me in astonishment , my clothes being in rags and covered with wisps of straw , while my face , blackened with dirt , was surmounted by a crop of ruffled hair that did duty for a hat", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Wood crackled in the cabin stove , smoke wafted for'ard , wisps eddying into the fugitives ' hiding-place", "context_len": 18}}}, "magistrate": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 43, "context": "I was , however , too ill to be moved ; so the kindly baker , hearing my story , and being informed of my rank , had me put to bed in his own house , where later in the day a magistrate attended to take down my depositions as to the gang of smugglers", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Tut , tut , man , the statute is dead ! Have you a magistrate \u2019 s warrant , Form 226 , giving you authority for this ? Quick , answer me ! I am a King \u2019 s officer , so on your peril speak truly ! ''", "context_len": 50}}}, "depositions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "I was , however , too ill to be moved ; so the kindly baker , hearing my story , and being informed of my rank , had me put to bed in his own house , where later in the day a magistrate attended to take down my depositions as to the gang of smugglers", "context_len": 56}}}, "posthaste": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Madame de Coucy , meanwhile , started for France posthaste , and was the first to carry the news to the court of Charles that Madame Isabel had been captured and dethroned , and then you may be sure all France was up in arms , as they say , in a moment , threatening to avenge La Petite Reine", "context_len": 60}}}, "transportation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 71, "context": "It is very strikingly characteristic of the mingling of poetic sentiment and enthusiasm with calm and calculating business efficiency , which shone conspicuously so often in Alexander 's career , that when he arrived at Sestos , and found that the ships were there , and the army safe , and that there was no enemy to oppose his landing on the Asiatic shore , he left Parmenio to conduct the transportation of the troops across the water , while he himself went away in a single galley on an excursion of sentiment and romantic adventure", "context_len": 96}}}, "barbaric": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "The girl had a sort of barbaric love of brightness and softness ; and one day , as she looked over some fabrics for which Jane , spurred by the approach of the vacation and the fact that Lola was to have a part in the closing exercises of school , had sent to Denver , the girl said suddenly , `` How good my father is to me , tia ! ''", "context_len": 73}}}, "wrecking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Fortunately I was spared the ordeal of attending the trial , but I heard that the gallows or transportation to the West Indies accounted for the whole of the rascally crew , against whom the barbaric crime of wrecking , as well as smuggling , was proved right up to the hilt", "context_len": 52}}}, "prosecuted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "My uncle , Sir George Lee , and Lawyer Whitehead had each prosecuted vigorous enquiries , but all attempts to run the felon to earth had proved fruitless", "context_len": 28}}}, "felon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "My uncle , Sir George Lee , and Lawyer Whitehead had each prosecuted vigorous enquiries , but all attempts to run the felon to earth had proved fruitless", "context_len": 28}}}, "inseparably": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Meanwhile it seemed as if the earth had opened and swallowed both Increase Joyce and the box ( for in my mind the two were inseparably associated ) , though I had a presentiment that I should obtain satisfaction in the end", "context_len": 42}}}, "lengthening": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Twelve months sped swiftly by ere my physical condition became as good as before my malady , and with the lengthening days of January I entertained great hopes of going again to sea", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 59, "context": "`` Or the superlative is expressed by the augmentative adverb m\u00e1hi : very , quite , greatly , largely y\u00edktchi m\u00e1hi , the strongest , which at the same time means : very strong , quite strong ; ` l\u00e1ko m\u00e1hi largest and very large ; m\u00e1himahi tallest and very tall ; the latter also being expressed by a lengthening of the vowel : m\u0101 hi very tall", "context_len": 68}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The shadows were lengthening out there in the garden , and oh if they should have to go home before the story was done !", "context_len": 25}}}, "rupture": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Rumours of a rupture with the States of Holland were in the air , and , taking the aggressive action of the Dutchmen in mercantile matters into consideration , the prospect of a war was hailed with delight", "context_len": 38}}}, "aggressive": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 29, "context": "We have the cruel and lurking Chicasa , the powerful and ingenious but treacherous and corruptible Cha'hta , the magnanimous and hospitable , proud and revengeful Creek , the aggressive Alibamu , the quarrelsome Y\u00e1massi , and the self-willed , independent Seminole , jealous of the enjoyment of his savage freedom in the swamps and everglades of the semi-tropical peninsula", "context_len": 60}}}, "mercantile": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "The city itself , however , though contracted in extent on account of the small dimensions of the island , was very compactly built and strongly fortified , and it contained a vast number of stately and magnificent edifices , which were filled with stores of wealth that had been accumulated by the mercantile enterprise and thrift of many generations", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "Proceeding to England , and posing as a mercantile marine officer , forged documents and an engaging manner procured him an introduction to Whitehall , with the result that he was given a commission in the Royal Naval Reserve and appointed to an armed merchant cruiser", "context_len": 46}}}, "realm": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 7, "context": "Indeed , the little queen , whose realm was the hearts of her nearest and dearest , would have been obliged to abdicate , for a while at least , I fancy , had she not chosen before nightfall of that same day to bury her head in the lap of her very most loyal subject , and with tears and sohs confess to her extreme unreasonableness and avow her determination not soon again to be overtaken by such a sorry state of mind and temper", "context_len": 86}}}, "cordage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "They saturated the sails and the cordage in the same manner , and laid trains of combustible materials through all parts of the vessel , so that when fire should be set in one part it would immediately spread every where , and set the whole mass in flames at once", "context_len": 51}}}, "hemp": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Never did we require cordage and hemp more than we do now", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "and I have just come from a loiter at the Botanical Gardens , which showed us that we need be under no great concern , were hemp and flax exterminated from the vegetable world , and silkworms to leave off being spinsters , as we could dress cheaply and well on plantain fibre , have capital paper and excellent ropes , etc", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But now the loop of hemp hung limp on the saddle-horn , and Gribble , surprised at being nearly thrown , rose in the stirrups to see what was underfoot", "context_len": 30}}}, "joiners": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The blockmakers and joiners have gone away , refusing to work any longer without money ; the sawyers threaten to do the same", "context_len": 23}}}, "workman": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 43, "context": "First of all , he went into a cotton factory , and later to a velveteen factory ; then , having a taste for carpentering , he took to it as a trade , though he was at best but a rough unskilled workman , tramping about the country , and doing odd jobs wherever he could get them", "context_len": 59}}}, "reviling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}}}, "foulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Faith , \u2019 tis hard on them , '' remarked Sir Thomas ; `` but for foulness of tongue they out-vie the daughters of Billingsgate", "context_len": 26}}}, "malcontent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "But alas for the commissioner \u2019 s hopes ! On entering the shop he found that , instead of being diligently employed , the men were listening to a heated discourse from a malcontent from another part of the dockyard", "context_len": 40}}}, "ire": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Why should such a natural and simple request arouse Japazy \u2019 s ire ? Jojasta had told him something", "context_len": 19}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` He was a stalwart knight and keen , And had in many a battle been ; * * * * His eyebrow dark , and eye of fire , Show 'd spirit proud and prompt to ire ; Yet lines of thought upon his cheek Did deep design and council speak", "context_len": 52}}}, "pates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Seizing a stout cudgel from one of the men , he struck out right and left at the astonished party till bruised and cut pates became the order of the day", "context_len": 31}}}, "malcontents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Then , having thoroughly cowed the malcontents by taking more pains in the use of the stick than in any business for the last twelve months ( as he afterwards expressed it ) , he sent for the guard and clapped three of the ringleaders in the stocks", "context_len": 48}}}, "ringleaders": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Then , having thoroughly cowed the malcontents by taking more pains in the use of the stick than in any business for the last twelve months ( as he afterwards expressed it ) , he sent for the guard and clapped three of the ringleaders in the stocks", "context_len": 48}}}, "millstone": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 80, "context": "`` Yes , yes , '' she murmured abstractedly ; `` who could fail to love him ? so valiant and so goodly to look upon , so tender unto his friends , and to me his poor wife , and ever was the inward joy in his bosom breaking forth in praises to God -- and yet '' -- turning wildly on Dick -- '' yet you will let him die ! You are as hard as the nether millstone ! Dick , do not shake your head , you must go ! You must force Prince Rupert to hear you", "context_len": 102}}}, "cannons": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "They had got into their boats and were rowing away from their sinking ship , but they plainly did not know which way to go , because they realized that if Baboo Bajorum began to fire his cannons at them he would blow them to smithereens", "context_len": 46}}}, "regretting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "But in spite of this , and all Bessie 's persuasions and assurances that the thing was quite impossible , Willie was not to be convinced that the voice he had twice heard was not that of the old lady who had left the money in the Bible ; and he did not cease regretting that Jennie had not seen her", "context_len": 61}}}, "ensign": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "Clad in a pair of shorts of a vivid orange hue -- for Te Paheka shared with the rest of the Maoris a love of brilliant colour -- and with a silk red ensign emblazoned with the New Zealand stars round his waist , Te Paheka grinned amicably at the Afrikander", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "declaration": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 6, "context": "It is rather improbable that a declaration of war always preceded the attack , for the advance into the hostile territory was made clandestinely [ 103 ] ; but the resolution of starting upon the warpath was heralded in the towns with great ceremonies", "context_len": 44}}}, "enlightened": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Also month by month fresh indications appeared of the reality of the work going on , -- an inquirer here ; a convert there ; an abusive Muhammadan softened into gentleness ; an ignorant Heathen enlightened ; a bigot persuaded ; and now and again one coming forward , bravely resolute to undergo Baptism , willing to face the almost inevitable persecution following", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "The letterpress is in very able hands , -- a very pretty little fairy-tale for grown-up children , -- that \u2019 s all the rage now , you know , in this enlightened age", "context_len": 34}}}, "straightforward": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` It shows what you are , '' answered Seabrooke , continuing his reproaches , instead of giving the straightforward answer which he considered unnecessary , `` that you have not the decent manliness to demand that which rightfully belonged to you because you were ashamed of your own folly and weakness , but must go and ransack in my quarters to find your money", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 65, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}}}, "readmitted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Sir Thomas Middleton hath given orders that the shipwrights who dwell there are not to be allowed to go home , and those already living there are not to be readmitted to the dockyard", "context_len": 34}}}, "rampant": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "It is rampant when you are not here ; but when you are present it stays quiet mostly , just because I love you", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A Lion rampant over 6 grasshoppers", "context_len": 6}}}, "sultry": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 9, "context": "The air seemed to have grown more close and sultry while the children were within the cave , and , just as they reached the mouth , a low , grumbling sound , which they had heard for some time , but not taken much notice of , broke into a crash of thunder that seemed to make the ground shake beneath them , while at the same instant a brilliant streak of lightning flashed zigzag across the sky , lighting up the gloom behind them to its furthest recess", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "The thermometer in my verandah to-day , where it had been in the shade all the day , was about 107 , that is more than twenty degrees hotter than I have ever seen it in the most sultry day in England", "context_len": 42}}}, "mercifully": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 20, "context": "Peggy shrieked , such a shriek of ghastly terror that it might have been heard a mile away , and mercifully it was heard , for there was an answering call from the wood , followed by a rustle of branches and dead leaves , and the keeper and his son burst through the thick undergrowth , and came scrambling over the fence , almost before the echo of her cry had died away", "context_len": 74}}}, "contagion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Throughout the long sultry summer we were mercifully preserved from the contagion ; then , as autumn came , and still the plague did not appear amongst us , people began to think that with the approach of the cold weather all danger was past", "context_len": 45}}}, "moor": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Finding threats and entreaties useless , the commissioner ordered the Essex to moor in the centre of the harbour", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "All rose to look at them , not without anxiety ; as yet they were far distant , across a wild moor , but as they drew nearer , and their standards could be more clearly discerned , it became gradually evident that it was a reinforcement ; and so it proved , for heralds , galloping forward , announced the men of Dorsetshire", "context_len": 64}}}, "foreshore": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Two days afterwards it was known that three more of her crew had died , and were buried on the foreshore at Gosport ; while , to disinfect the ship , great quantities of brimstone were burnt , but to no purpose", "context_len": 42}}}, "disinfect": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Two days afterwards it was known that three more of her crew had died , and were buried on the foreshore at Gosport ; while , to disinfect the ship , great quantities of brimstone were burnt , but to no purpose", "context_len": 42}}}, "preventive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Many put the cause of the disease , rightly enough , to the filthy habits of the poorer people , and hearing that salt water was a preventive , scores of the inhabitants repaired daily to the seashore , plunging into the bitterly cold waves in the hope of staving off the malady", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Ben would have ploughed two or three furrows around his buildings , which would have been the most effectual preventive ; but , after the vessel was built , he had put his oxen away to pasture", "context_len": 37}}}, "staving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Many put the cause of the disease , rightly enough , to the filthy habits of the poorer people , and hearing that salt water was a preventive , scores of the inhabitants repaired daily to the seashore , plunging into the bitterly cold waves in the hope of staving off the malady", "context_len": 53}}}, "watchmen": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 11, "context": "He passed through all the streets inflaming the curiosity of the watchmen ; the darkness ( for there were very few lamps or lights of any kind , in those days , for public use ) was intense , a drizzling rain was falling , and at length , weary , wet , and dispirited , he returned to the palace , and found that the council , tired of waiting , had at length broken up", "context_len": 77}}}, "supremacy": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "The Creek term \u00e1bi'hka signifies `` pile at the base , heap at the root '' ( \u00e1bi stem , pole ) , and was imparted to this tribe , `` because in the contest for supremacy its warriors heaped up a pile of scalps , covering the base of the war-pole only", "context_len": 53}}}, "rages": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` As the pestilence rages in London town , '' said he , `` it is not meet that a man should risk an inglorious death when he would serve His Majesty better by dying for his country while fighting the Dutch", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "He was fighting for two lives , his own and Casson \u2019 s , and , moreover , one of his fierce rages was upon him ; one of those wild bursts of fury that for the moment gave him the strength of the jaguar , the wile of the fox , the quickness of the snake", "context_len": 57}}}, "shallop": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 12, "context": "This time fortune did not smile on us , for as the shallop was on the Frenchman \u2019 s lee a spurt of flame burst from the man-of-war , immediately followed by a deafening roar , and with it our mizzen mast went by the board with a terrific crash", "context_len": 50}}}, "knocks": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 86, "context": "you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old Noll and padded the hoof to Kinsale , and there I shipped under Prince Rupert", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` There ! there ! '' cried an agonised voice , `` he knocks again ; 't is Elfhelm of Shrewsbury , whom Edric slew ; 't was n't I , 't was Edric , I only shared the spoil ; keep him out , I tell you , keep him out", "context_len": 52}}}, "eastwards": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then , amid a farewell outburst of cheering , the Blimp flew eastwards , to disappear from view behind the lofty Staddon Heights", "context_len": 23}}}, "offshore": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "In these the hundred-odd flying officers were rushed through the spray-filled darkness to a point offshore which the steersmen seemed to find by instinct", "context_len": 24}}}, "slacking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` But say , fellows , '' he went on , in a few minutes , `` do n't you think it 's slacking up some ? ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "undefined": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "The sun had long vanished behind a bank of dark-grey , undefined clouds , while a cold wind howled across our path , moaning through the treetops and raising clouds of choking dust on the sun-dried highway", "context_len": 37}}}, "dispelling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "By this time the moon had risen , dispelling the darkness , though the fog hung around as thick as ever ; but withal there was enough light to see the length of our craft", "context_len": 35}}}, "hulking": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "No fear of that , but he such a big , hulking chap and eats so much that he oughter be made to do something ; I \u2019 m going to put him on tother side of this wagon and make him b \u2019 leve it \u2019 s the most important post of all , and that if he drops asleep , the whole shebang will be wiped out by the redskins", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "There was Charley Tuttle , called Chub for short , a roly-poly , round-faced , laughing chap , who was munching peanuts ; Tim Davis , nicknamed Spotty , even more freckled than Ben , thin-legged , sly-faced , and minus the two front teeth of his upper jaw ; Sam Rollins , a big , hulking , low-browed fellow , who lost no opportunity to bully smaller boys , generally known as Hunk ; Berlin Barker , a cold blond , rather good-looking , but proud and distant in his bearing ; and others who did not impress the new boy at all with their personalities", "context_len": 107}}}, "embellishment": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Marching up to what I conjectured to be the king 's house -- from the fact that it was not only by far the largest dwelling in the enclosure , but was also distinguished by an exclusive embellishment in the form of a row of a dozen poles , each surmounted by a human skull , planted upright in the ground before it -- we halted at a distance of some twenty paces from the entrance , with our backs turned toward the crucifixion tree , the leafless branches of which overshadowed us , and waited", "context_len": 96}}}, "pintle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Oh for a barrel of powder and a slow match lashed to her rudder pintle ! '' exclaimed Drake", "context_len": 20}}}, "spurt": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "This time fortune did not smile on us , for as the shallop was on the Frenchman \u2019 s lee a spurt of flame burst from the man-of-war , immediately followed by a deafening roar , and with it our mizzen mast went by the board with a terrific crash", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "He sprang aside and , drawing his machete from his belt , struck the animal \u2019 s arm a blow that cut deep and caused the blood to spurt into the distorted face of his assailant", "context_len": 36}}}, "wreckage": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 31, "context": "The anxiety of the master on account of the fog had vanished utterly on meeting with the Frenchmen , and with spirited promptness he set the crew to clear away the wreckage and parbuckle the broken mast", "context_len": 37}}}, "parbuckle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The anxiety of the master on account of the fog had vanished utterly on meeting with the Frenchmen , and with spirited promptness he set the crew to clear away the wreckage and parbuckle the broken mast", "context_len": 37}}}, "seeks": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "A chap who is so unpatriotic that he will quit his team because he had a personal grudge against some fellow on it is of no real value to the team anyhow ; and when he seeks to lead others to follow his example he \u2019 s worse than a traitor", "context_len": 51}}}, "exodus": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Meanwhile the news that the Duke of Beaufort \u2019 s squadron was really in the Channel had spread abroad , causing the timid inhabitants of Rye to make preparations for a hurried exodus , while the trainbands were called out by beat of drum , and had assembled in the marketplace", "context_len": 51}}}, "quagmire": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "[ 74 ] `` When the earth was a level plain in the condition of a quagmire , a superior being , in appearance a red man , came down from above , and alighting near the centre of the Choctaw nation , threw up a large mound or hill , called Nanne Wayah , stooping or sloping hill", "context_len": 59}}}, "infirmity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "A grand place , too , to work , you think ; and then you recall with a sigh that the light of that glorious mind has well-nigh gone out , 'neath the weight of physical weariness and infirmity , and then the solitary home begins to look a little like a prison in your eyes , as you realize how glad its inmate would be to exchange it for the Palace of that King whose divine intent for the world he has so marvellously interpreted for us all in the days when soul was still master of hand and brain", "context_len": 101}}}, "baneful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "If he had had but a free hand , I warrant the disgrace of the Dutch in the Medway would never have occurred ; but the baneful influence of the court beauties drove His Majesty almost to poverty , so that when retrenchment had to come it was the fleet that suffered", "context_len": 52}}}, "recalling": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 31, "context": "Be as still as possible , please , he said in a loud whisper , mindful of the old people ; I will be down in a moment , and instantly recalling the party he had seen drive past to Nuneham , there seemed no need to ask who they were or what had happened", "context_len": 55}}}, "equipment": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 93, "context": "`` The King , for all he knew that this was a thing whereto the Vizier might not avail , no , nor the greatest King , [ 373 ] nevertheless exercised his clemency [ 374 ] and granted him the delay he sought ; then , turning to the old woman , he said to her , `` Go to thy son and tell him I give him [ my ] word that my daughter shall be in his name ; [ 375 ] but needs must I take order for her equipment ; [ 376 ] wherefore it behoveth him grant us a delay of three months", "context_len": 109}}}, "mastiff": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 7, "context": "The deep , deep bay of the mastiff sometimes gave warning of the advent of a stranger , or of the step heard from the distance , in the still deep night ; but this was all that Alfgar could learn of the outer world , from which he was banished at so critical a moment", "context_len": 56}}}, "lapdog": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Compared with the Gannet the Prince Royal was as a mastiff by the side of a lapdog , while the smallest of her 100 guns was larger than the heaviest piece of ordnance in my first ship", "context_len": 37}}}, "squadrons": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 70, "context": "The master of the ship wanted him to `` cut his mainsail and cast about , and to trust to the sailing of his ship '' ; but Sir Richard utterly refused to turn from the enemy , saying that he would rather choose to die than dishonour himself , his country , and her Majesty 's ship , and informed his company that he would pass through the two squadrons in spite of them", "context_len": 75}}}, "revile": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "During the whole of the month of May the English fleet remained cruising betwixt Gravelines and Dover , till Albemarle began to revile the Dutch for their cowardice in fearing to leave their harbours , while of de Beaufort we had neither signs nor tidings", "context_len": 45}}}, "union": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 42, "context": "There was long consultation over this in the English camp ; but in spite of Edmund 's own feelings it was decided to receive Edric , since Canute 's fate would seem to be quite decided if England were united by the union of those southern English who had fought under Canute with Edric , and the men of Mercia and Wessex who had won the previous victories", "context_len": 68}}}, "detach": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "When he was nearing his end , he wrote of the disease as a `` providential agent to detach the heart from all earthly affection , prompting much the desire of a Christian soul to be united -- the sooner the better -- with Him who is her only life ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "untoward": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "It was to be held in a hall in Haddonfield , and many were the precautions taken by the committee to prevent any of the hated sophomores from attending , or getting to the place beforehand , lest they might , by some untoward act , `` put it on the blink , '' as Holly Cross used to say", "context_len": 60}}}, "irksome": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Sir George made a stirring speech , the drums beat to quarters , and then came that irksome interval before opening fire that tells so acutely on the nerves of even the most hardened veteran", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "I could chat much longer with you , dear one , but I have other notes to write ; and my pen , or ink , or paper , or something or other , will go wrong to-night , so as to make the act of writing irksome , as well as the note untidy", "context_len": 55}}}, "outbalanced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The action began in a strong wind that , blowing athwart the tide , raised such a steep sea that most of our ships were unable to open their lee \u2019 ard lower-deck ports , a misfortune that more than outbalanced our advantage in having the weather gauge", "context_len": 48}}}, "unflinchingly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 45, "context": "During the time of waiting she had a certain influence over Irene ; not , perhaps , so much as on the first day , when that young lady , charmed , bewildered , and amazed by Rosamund 's firmness , had followed her lead unflinchingly", "context_len": 46}}}, "splintering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "The solution of the mystery was afforded shortly afterwards by three seamen being cut in two apparently by one shot , which finished up its career of death by splintering the base of the mainmast", "context_len": 35}}}, "shambles": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "The butchery of the sentence for treason was carried out to the bitter end , yet of the onlookers there were but a few women who sobbed hysterically or fainted , and but one or two men who pushed their way back , sick with the sight and smell of the shambles", "context_len": 52}}}, "reeving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "With the approach of night both fleets withdrew ; but for us there was little rest , as all hands were employed reeving fresh rigging , splicing spars , and plugging shot holes , while our dead were committed to the deep , and the wounded transhipped to one of the smaller vessels", "context_len": 53}}}, "splicing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Here they fitted the rigging , and altered the ship \u2019 s sails into those of a schooner ; and so well versed were Captain Rhines and his son in all nautical matters , that , by dint of splicing and piecing , they managed to get all the standing rigging , and nearly all the running gear , out of the materials of the wreck", "context_len": 66}}}, "plugging": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "With the approach of night both fleets withdrew ; but for us there was little rest , as all hands were employed reeving fresh rigging , splicing spars , and plugging shot holes , while our dead were committed to the deep , and the wounded transhipped to one of the smaller vessels", "context_len": 53}}}, "descried": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "Once he descried one on top of a distant hill , but it was too far off for a successful shot , and when he started on the trail the animal dashed into a thick forest , and was soon lost", "context_len": 41}}}, "ace": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "Once Drusie , who had wriggled herself through the thick hawthorns at the back of the fort , was within an ace of taking the flag ; but , just as she had climbed up on the roof , the defender , whose face was completely hidden by his helmet , made a grab at her , and she was obliged to fly for her life", "context_len": 66}}}, "reinforced": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "Then through the smoke we perceived that sixteen ships had reinforced the already superior number of the Dutchmen , and , to save ourselves from total destruction , Albemarle hoisted a signal for the English to retreat slowly towards the mouth of the Thames", "context_len": 44}}}, "smarting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Maggie being stronger , pulled the dasher up and down a few times , and did not give up until she was red in the face , and her little hands were smarting with the hard work they were not used to", "context_len": 42}}}, "yawing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Keeping close together , and yawing from time to time in order to deliver a broadside at our pursuers , we held doggedly on our course , till at length a flat calm set in , and both fleets lay inactive at a mile apart , in which situation darkness again overtook us", "context_len": 53}}}, "renewing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "As daylight dawned upon the third day of the fight we continued our retreat , and as a faint southerly wind sprang up the enemy drew near with the intention of renewing the fight , concentrating their efforts on Albemarle \u2019 s ship , which , covering the retreat , presented an undaunted spectacle to our relentless foes", "context_len": 58}}}, "scurvy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Heaven grant \u2019 tis Rupert \u2019 s squadron ! '' ejaculated our captain ; `` though methinks by their noise those scurvy Dutchmen are sure \u2019 tis de Beaufort", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "He did not wish to `` tell tales ; '' but he had not too much confidence in Lewis Flagg -- it would be hard to find the boy in the school who had , especially among the younger ones -- and he could not bear to think that he might have planned some scurvy trick on Seabrooke", "context_len": 58}}}, "unworkable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "With the falling tide the Prince Royal listed heavily to starboard , so that her guns were for the most part unworkable , and her great sides were exposed an easy target for the enemy", "context_len": 35}}}, "pinnaces": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "On the 10th of September , 1591 ( 31st August , old style ) , Lord Thomas Howard , with six of her Majesty 's ships , five victualling ships , a barque and two or three pinnaces , was at anchor near Flores , one of the westerly islands of the Azores , when Captain Middleton brought the news that the Spanish fleet was approaching", "context_len": 66}}}, "ineffective": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "At this range , too , their fire will be ineffective", "context_len": 11}}}, "warp": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "His example animated the men , who immediately began to load their muskets and serve out boarding pikes and broadswords , while the master took steps to lay out a couple of anchors in readiness to warp the ship into deep water directly the floodtide should release her", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Bart felt much gratified over the way all these tangled strands in the warp and woof of his young life had been straightened out , but he experienced a final blessing that filled him with unutterable joy and gratefulness", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Superstition was woven into the very warp and woof of their natures", "context_len": 12}}}, "unapproachable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Splinters flew , ropes , blocks , and spars came tumbling down from aloft , men fell dead or wounded , and shrieks and groans rent the air , while all we could do was to shake our fists in useless rage at our unapproachable foes", "context_len": 46}}}, "boatloads": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Presently we saw boatloads of armed men leaving the Dutch ships , and we realized that an attempt was to be made to carry us by boarding", "context_len": 27}}}, "breastplates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "More boats were sunk alongside by dropping heavy shot into them , those of their crew who wore breastplates perishing miserably in the sea", "context_len": 24}}}, "furnaces": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "Not a boat did we carry that was in a condition to float , otherwise volunteers would not have been lacking who would have risked their lives in an attempt to tow these furnaces clear of us", "context_len": 37}}}, "curtly": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` You are released from arrest , '' declared the Major curtly ; then , as an afterthought , he added : `` It would be advisable that you maintain discretion over the matter", "context_len": 34}}}, "permitting": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 36, "context": "Why , of course , Marie-Celeste ; that is , when a man is a great man to start with , and then does something to make himself greater , the Queen may reward him by permitting him to become a member of the Order , if there happens to be a vacancy ; and there 's nothing much finer can happen to a man than that", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Already they had practical proof that at almost every knot of the twelve-thousand-miles voyage they were open to attack -- Providence and the armed unit of Britain 's fleet permitting", "context_len": 30}}}, "frigate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "This done , we were sent on board a frigate and placed in a dark , stuffy hole below the waterline", "context_len": 21}}}, "booming": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Near the edge of the woods , with a sudden booming whir of wings that made his heart jump , a partridge flew up and went diving away into the deeper forest", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "He remained looking at the spring for a few minutes , and , as he was about to move away he was startled by a deep , booming sound in the woods , off to his left", "context_len": 37}}}, "depreciated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "How the engagement would end we knew not , but our spirits were greatly depressed with our misfortunes , and one and all , having seen that the courage and fortitude of our enemies had been unduly depreciated by our leaders , were far from sanguine as to the prospects of a victory of our hitherto redoubtable fleet", "context_len": 58}}}, "bounteous": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Our reveries were cut short by the appearance of a stolid Dutchman , who brought us a liberal supply of food that , compared with our hard fare of the last month , was a bounteous feast", "context_len": 37}}}, "pallets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "At length , worn out with bodily fatigue , we threw ourselves down on our rough and hard pallets , and slept soundly till we were awakened by the unmistakable sounds that accompany the action of a ship taking in sail", "context_len": 41}}}, "canals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 80, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}}}, "waterways": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Judging by the sounds , they were preparing to take the string of barges in tow , for Malcolm could hear a heavy hawser being dragged along the barge 's waterways and made fast to the towing-bitts a few feet from the bows", "context_len": 43}}}, "taciturn": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was of a somewhat taciturn disposition , though , when properly understood , he was a good-hearted and reliable friend", "context_len": 21}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Now , too , the bell of the red-brick school clamored loudly of mornings ; and dark , taciturn Mexican children , and paler , noisier children from the mining end of town , bubbled out of every door", "context_len": 39}}}, "parole": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "Young and hot-headed , the idea of a possible exchange of prisoners never entered our heads , but on the other hand the excitement of an attempt at escape held out an inducement to refuse our parole", "context_len": 37}}}, "signing": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 23, "context": "Then , taking a bow and quiver of arrows , together with a brace of spears , out of the canoe , and signing to me to do the same , Ama led the way through the dense growth bordering the river bank , until we reached an open grassy space of about twenty acres , sparsely dotted here and there with magnificent trees ; and here Ama signified that we were to camp for the day", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It is on record that on one occasion the news of the signing of an important treaty at Washington affecting the Sioux was known to that tribe before the telegraphic messages could reach the army officers at the reservation", "context_len": 39}}}, "spiral": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "The fuel consisted of dry cane or dry pine slabs split fine ; and , as if it were to give a concrete image of the warming rays of the sun , these split canes were disposed in a spiral line which exhibited several revolutions around the centre", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "At the rear was a covered inclosure and from the rough , stone chimney , built at one end on the outside of the main building , rose a spiral of smoke proof that the cabin had occupants", "context_len": 38}}}, "coating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "At the time of the conquest of Mexico by Cortez , many of the interior towns of that country were whitewashed in the same manner , by means of a shining white clay coating", "context_len": 34}}}, "burgomaster": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "A sign , and we were hurried from the burgomaster \u2019 s presence , and , with a file of pikemen surrounding us , we were taken , not to that loathsome dungeon , but into the open air", "context_len": 39}}}, "wedges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "At length Felgate stood up , and , seizing a hammer that was used apparently to drive the wedges into the boot , he strode across to the door and began to thunder a rain of blows upon it", "context_len": 39}}}, "filtered": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "The hours rolled slowly by , and just as the daylight that filtered through the narrow windows began to fade , our prison door was thrown open and the guards reappeared", "context_len": 31}}}, "phlegmatic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Once more we were taken into the presence of the magistrate , who , phlegmatic as ever , merely raised one eyebrow slightly and tapped the book in front of him with the feathered end of his pen", "context_len": 38}}}, "enquiry": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 97, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "debar": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The gesture was impressive with its silent enquiry , but with the obstinacy of our race we again refused to give any pledge that would debar us from making any attempt at escape", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "It is not in the bond , you know , mother , and anything outside the bond I for one debar", "context_len": 21}}}, "drawbridge": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 48, "context": "They had unfortunately given him a bed in a chamber which looked towards his home : he had chanced to wake , had looked from the window , seen the flames , and had started thither at once , swimming the moat when he could not cross the drawbridge -- suspecting , doubtless , that he was surrounded by treachery", "context_len": 60}}}, "password": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "A challenge and a password were exchanged , and we were handed over to another armed guard , who escorted us to a small room , which , though roughly furnished , was a surprisingly comfortable prison", "context_len": 37}}}, "jailers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "It was now late , but for several hours we tossed uneasily on our straw couches , till , worn out with the excitement and fatigue , we fell asleep , to be awakened only too soon by the entry of our jailers", "context_len": 43}}}, "sabots": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "By signs they informed us that we were to start on a long journey , and providing us with blankets and klompen , or wooden shoes similar to the sabots of the French , they left us to enjoy another meal of porridge , rusk bread , and cheese", "context_len": 49}}}, "rusk": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "By signs they informed us that we were to start on a long journey , and providing us with blankets and klompen , or wooden shoes similar to the sabots of the French , they left us to enjoy another meal of porridge , rusk bread , and cheese", "context_len": 49}}}, "occasioned": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "Stern commander though he was , his men loved him so much that they wanted to give him a month of their pay to assist him in the loss of means occasioned by the fire", "context_len": 35}}}, "files": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "For miles we marched between the files of our escort of pikemen , and we had ample opportunities of studying the nature of the land , which in no small measure well deserves the name of the Low Countries", "context_len": 39}}}, "affords": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "It is wholly legendary , in its first portion even mythical ; it is of a comparatively remote age , exceedingly instructive for ethnography and for the development of religious ideas ; it is full of that sort of na\u00efvet\u00e9 which we like so much to meet in the mental productions of our aborigines , and affords striking instances of the debasing and brutalizing influence of the unrestricted belief in the supernatural and miraculous", "context_len": 74}}}, "windmills": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Though they shut in our range of vision we knew that dry land lay beyond , as we could discern several windmills , roofs of houses , and trees on the other side", "context_len": 33}}}, "wheat": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 38, "context": "What a pleasant ride that was : out of the field where the bars had been let down ; past other fields ready , or nearly ready , for the harvesting ; pale green oats , and golden wheat , the white , sweet-scented buckwheat , and the tall Indian corn ; then through the orchard where a flock of sheep were feeding , past the locust grove , and then into the farmyard ; stopping at last between the open doors of the great barn !", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then Jason planted the dragon 's teeth , just as a man plants wheat", "context_len": 14}}}, "pumping": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "Children stood ankle deep in water , pumping hour after hour , and their work was sometimes prolonged for thirty-six hours continuously ; so that it was no wonder the children died early , that they suffered much from disease , and led cheerless , wretched lives", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "So there was a grand collecting of hats and gloves , and pumping of tyres , and many 'good-byes ' and 'thank you 's , ' and the merry party at last started off on their homeward way , ringing their bells as a parting salute , and declaring they would not soon forget their afternoon at the Abbey", "context_len": 59}}}, "drains": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "There were scores of windmills all busily engaged , not in grinding wheat , but in pumping water from the drains and throwing it over the dykes", "context_len": 27}}}, "dykes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "These dykes , which were cut by the Hollanders to such good purpose during their desperate resistance against the might of Spain , were massive embankments planted with sedge and reeds , and faced in places with straw so as to resist the sea better", "context_len": 45}}}, "embankments": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "These dykes , which were cut by the Hollanders to such good purpose during their desperate resistance against the might of Spain , were massive embankments planted with sedge and reeds , and faced in places with straw so as to resist the sea better", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The most ponderous stones are removed , the strongest fastenings are torn asunder , and embankments the most compact and solid are undermined and washed away", "context_len": 26}}}, "sedge": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "These dykes , which were cut by the Hollanders to such good purpose during their desperate resistance against the might of Spain , were massive embankments planted with sedge and reeds , and faced in places with straw so as to resist the sea better", "context_len": 45}}}, "refrained": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 32, "context": "For a moment I could scarce refrain from throwing myself upon him ; but reflecting that little would be gained thus , and that much might be obtained by strategy , I refrained , and , shrugging my shoulders indifferently , I turned from him and walked to the farther part of the room", "context_len": 54}}}, "alienated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "They therefore remained firm in their original simplicity and integrity , and after some respectful but ineffectual remonstrances , they stood aloof , alienated from their commander in heart , and condemning very strongly , among themselves , his wickedness and folly", "context_len": 42}}}, "undergoing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "There is something so touching in his looking at such a moment to the Saviour , whose Blood , shed for his salvation , was at that moment trickling down in his view ; and there is something so sublime in our Lord \u2019 s conferring Eternal Life , -- such a gift , -- at the time when He was Himself undergoing the terrible sentence of death ! We may envy your dear suffering child , my Laura , when we think how soon , in human expectation , his eyes will behold the King in His beauty", "context_len": 99}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He has a clear sound judgment , and a power of adapting himself to varying circumstances , and of undergoing drudgery", "context_len": 21}}}, "enquired": {"freq": 59, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 59, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` When did you blow in ? '' enquired Travers , as the four made their way along a narrow plank gangway between the lines of huts -- the only means of preventing men sinking above their ankles in mud", "context_len": 40}}}, "footgear": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "His example was quickly followed by Drake , and shortly after I adopted the same ruse , though in reality I had good cause to do so , the unaccustomed nature of my footgear beginning to have an ill effect", "context_len": 40}}}, "alertness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "It was stationed in the center of the army on the field , and on the two sides of it were bodies of cavalry and foot soldiers , more lightly armed than the soldiers of the phalanx , who could accordingly move with more alertness and speed , and carry their action readily wherever it might be called for", "context_len": 59}}}, "dyke": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "Instinct took us towards the sea , from which blew a stinging , salt-laden breeze that caused a sensation of freedom , and when at length we gained the summit of the last rush-grown dyke , we could see the waves lashing the beach in so violent a manner as to make an attempt to escape by boat an absolute impossibility", "context_len": 61}}}, "accoutrements": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Ford was evidently coming down the shoot when his axe handle or some of his accoutrements became entangled in the wire netting ; so that , to clear himself , he had to break through , and , while struggling to do so , he got so severely burned that his recovery was hopeless", "context_len": 55}}}, "waists": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 74, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}}}, "impenetrable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "Jim was of the opinion that they never took enough advantage of the shelter afforded by the thick and almost impenetrable bushes that grew on one side of the fort , and he proposed that while two of them made an attack in the open air , he or Drusie should lie concealed , and if Hal could be drawn out in pursuit they might get a chance of slipping in during his absence", "context_len": 74}}}, "ooze": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 47, "context": "Although he made for the shore at some distance from the old woman , so that he might choose his own time for drawing near her after she had finished with the demon-like dance , he slipped , when he tried for a footing in the slimy ooze of the river bank , and came down with a heavy splash", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Then , gripping the wounded limb , I turned it toward the light of the fire , and saw two marks close together upon the inner part of the arm , just below the elbow , from which , as I gazed , two drops of blood began to ooze slowly", "context_len": 51}}}, "slime": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was a slippery climb , for the wheel was green with slime , and it needed a long step to get from one blade to the other ; but Peggy was utterly fearless , and she had soon pulled herself to the top", "context_len": 44}}}, "sirrahs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "To complete our discomfiture , the sergeant tapped his petronel significantly and exclaimed in perfect English : `` Now , sirrahs , you will perceive that escape is no easy matter", "context_len": 31}}}, "enshrouded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "At times the words Bartow and Laura would escape his lips , but though Bomba listened eagerly for what would follow , nothing came that made more clear for him the mystery in which he was enshrouded", "context_len": 37}}}, "communicative": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "He had grown somewhat communicative of late , talking freely of the country in whose service he was , though very reserved in matters pertaining to the land of his birth", "context_len": 31}}}, "insight": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was exceedingly interesting to the American contingent of the party to gain a little insight into the life of the English navvies ; and they passed the little tap-room , reeking with smoke and smelling of pipes and beer mugs , rather more often than circumstances would warrant , for the sake of looking in on the jolly fellows , and catching a sentence or so of their almost unintelligible dialect", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was a pity that there was not one among them with insight enough to gauge the complacent fellow aright , and at the same time with honesty enough to take him to task for the profitless life he was leading", "context_len": 41}}}, "susceptibilities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Occasionally we had a visit from the governor of the fortress , a Major Van der Wycke , a courteous and honest soldier , who carefully refrained from hurting our susceptibilities with reference to the war , though he told us of the great fire that practically destroyed the best part of London", "context_len": 53}}}, "offhandedly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Thou art a Yorkshireman , perchance ? '' I enquired offhandedly , interrupting him in the midst of a rambling statement", "context_len": 22}}}, "counties": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 45, "context": "A truce to all this , however , for fear you should imagine , and with reason , that even at this late stage I am going to fare so wide of my province of story-teller as to conduct you in guide-book fashion through the counties of Westmoreland and Cumberland", "context_len": 50}}}, "deviation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "The destination toward which he steered , without deviation to right or left , and with great regard for economy of time and space at corners and crossings , was the Little Castle , and he marched up the path from terrace to terrace , and rang the bell with all the complacency of a drum-major", "context_len": 56}}}, "beshrew": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "But , beshrew me ! the name of Holwick reminds me -- -- Dost know Holwick well ? ''", "context_len": 19}}}, "vehemence": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 46, "context": "He had hoped that the consolations of her minister , or the calm of despair , might have brought to her some amount of resignation ; but now this wild trust in the power of Prince Rupert had suddenly inspired the poor lady with a crazy vehemence", "context_len": 47}}}, "whereby": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "He used also to go to the market of the goldsmiths and the market of the jewellers , and there he would sit and look upon the different kinds of jewels and see them bought and sold ; whereby he became aware that the fruits of the trees , wherewith he had filled the purses , [ 318 ] whenas he was in the treasure , were neither glass nor crystal , but jewels , and knew that he had happened upon great wealth , such as kings might nowise compass", "context_len": 91}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "But the blow fell heavily on those who loved him ; and though for nine years he had not seen his children , whereby the sorrow to them was softened , yet the loss to their future could not but be great", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 121, "context": "`` And when , '' [ quoth she ] ; `` I saw this [ seeming ] lack of wit in him , I fell to laughing at him , unknowing his perfidy and his intent ; so I took an old lamp that was in my husband 's pavilion and sent it by the eunuch , who exchanged it with him for a new lamp ; and next day , O my father , at daybreak , we found ourselves in Africa , with the palace and all that was therein ; and I knew not the properties of the lamp which I had exchanged , till my husband Alaeddin came to us and contrived against the Maugrabin a device whereby he delivered us from him", "context_len": 127}}}, "befooling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But either he was befooling himself or me , for I could find nothing", "context_len": 14}}}, "disinterestedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Where did you look ? '' I enquired disinterestedly", "context_len": 10}}}, "delved": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Where did I look ? Now , out on me for a dolt ! I delved every night for more than a fortnight , till the countryside rang with tales of the ghost of Holwick , and none would venture near o \u2019 nights , and hardly by day", "context_len": 50}}}, "adhered": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 57, "context": "( We would say to our readers , that the scythe rifles in those days were not made as at present , by putting sand or emery upon wood , with cement ; but they scratched the wood and made it rough , then smeared it with tallow , and put fine sand on it , which adhered to the tallow and the scratches", "context_len": 64}}}, "identical": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 45, "context": "From this passage it would seem as if the belvedere actually projected from the side of the upper story or soler ( keszr ) , instead of being built on the roof , lantern-wise , or being ( as would appear from earlier passages ) identical with the hall itself , but the whole description is as before remarked", "context_len": 59}}}, "filched": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "He looked fixedly at me for a moment , as if suspecting my thoughts , then from his pocket he produced the identical metal box that he had filched from my father \u2019 s corse", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Here it is ! '' I shouted triumphantly , handing it to Master Whitehead , and straightway I told them of my meeting with Increase Joyce , and how I secured the temporary possession of the filched document", "context_len": 39}}}, "corse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "He looked fixedly at me for a moment , as if suspecting my thoughts , then from his pocket he produced the identical metal box that he had filched from my father \u2019 s corse", "context_len": 35}}}, "jargon": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 47, "context": "Jennie laughed merrily ; but as she was about to answer , the click of the gate was heard , and with shouts of `` He 's coming ! '' from Charlie , `` Poppy , poppy ! '' from the younger boy , and a confused jargon from Tommy , which no one but Jennie could understand , the whole three tumbled down from the window and rushed to the door", "context_len": 72}}}, "major": {"freq": 46, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 46, "word_index": 4, "context": "And now for the major 's motive , for he had one , and he had artfully made use of Richmond to forward his plan : He was desirous of getting possession of the poor shoemaker 's house and land , having in view the purchase of the lot adjoining", "context_len": 50}}}, "eccentricity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was always the same , no matter what the hour of day or night that I might choose to start upon my expeditions ; no surprise was ever displayed at my eccentricity in the choice of times , but I simply could not contrive to elude notice ; and at length it was borne in upon me that if I wished to effect my escape I must adopt tactics of a totally different kind", "context_len": 75}}}, "quill": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I had not quill nor crayon nor paper", "context_len": 8}}}, "crayon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I had not quill nor crayon nor paper", "context_len": 8}}}, "unsatisfactory": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was while matters were in this very unsatisfactory state that I became aware that some event of extreme importance was imminent in the town ; for upon sallying forth from my residence on a certain morning and crossing the great square , in the centre of which stood Banda 's crucifixion tree , I saw that a number of men were engaged in setting up some forty stout , quaintly-carved posts in a circle round about the tree", "context_len": 79}}}, "bethought": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 41, "context": "When [ 413 ] the Vizier heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we", "context_len": 128}}}, "quitted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "Like the Apalachis , the tribe of the heathen Taouachas had quitted the Spanish territory for being harassed by the Alibamu , and fled southwest to the French , who settled them on Mobile river , one league above the Apalachis ( 1710 ; in Margry V , 485-487 )", "context_len": 50}}}, "effecting": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "For the first few days of my captivity my brain was literally seething with schemes for effecting my escape , most of them wildly impossible , I admit ; but some there were that seemed to promise just a ghost of a chance of success -- until I attempted to put them into effect , when the vigilance of my guards -- with the fear of crucifixion , head downward , before their eyes -- invariably baffled me", "context_len": 78}}}, "necessitated": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "This chanced to be the first mishap ; but then could you really call an accident a mishap that simply necessitated a three-days ' stay in the beautiful Wordsworth district ? Our sunshiny little party , at any rate , chose not so to regard it , and scoured the whole lovely region on foot , reading Wordsworth 's poetry in their halts by the roadside , and growing familiar with every foot of the lanes he so dearly loved", "context_len": 80}}}, "abandonment": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "His time was spent , in short , in strange alternations : resolution and energy in forming vast plans one day , and utter abandonment to all the excesses of dissipation and vice the next", "context_len": 35}}}, "twentieth": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "In the middle of the month of June -- I had just celebrated my twentieth birthday in a very despondent style -- Van der Wycke came to us one morning with a beaming face that showed that something very unusual had occurred to upset the stolidity of this typical Dutchman", "context_len": 50}}}, "stolidity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "In the middle of the month of June -- I had just celebrated my twentieth birthday in a very despondent style -- Van der Wycke came to us one morning with a beaming face that showed that something very unusual had occurred to upset the stolidity of this typical Dutchman", "context_len": 50}}}, "heartbreaking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` And in this style he told us the heartbreaking news of the never-to-be-forgotten disgrace at Chatham , of the burning or sinking of the Royal Oak , the James , the London , and several other smaller vessels", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "They have missed her and cried after her and no one could get them asleep until they were fairly worn out and then they sobbed in the most heartbreaking way", "context_len": 30}}}, "subsequently": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 18, "context": "The gentleman at whose house he met me at Nottingham , and who was ashamed of him , subsequently became his servant , and touched his hat to him ; and John has pulled up at my own door in his carriage , with a liveried servant , when I lived near to him in London", "context_len": 56}}}, "garlands": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "The last spark had died out of the paper in the grate and left only black tinder ; the table was left bare , the golden plates and richly embroidered napkins , and the garlands were transformed again into old handkerchiefs , scraps of red and white paper , and discarded artificial flowers all scattered on the floor ; the minstrels in the minstrel gallery had stolen away , and the viols and bassoons were still", "context_len": 76}}}, "supplying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "The chief had noticed that Bomba \u2019 s arrows were all gone , and as a parting proof of friendship insisted on supplying him with a dozen choice ones from his own stock", "context_len": 33}}}, "mortar": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 45, "context": "I sez he ai n't in , but I asks Miss Lilian , and she tells 'em `` Yes , '' and there they all is , pokin ' about amongst the nettles , and grubbin ' up stones , and stickin ' bits of mortar in their pockets , and dodgin ' about with yard measures , for all the world like a set of lunatics", "context_len": 67}}}, "clocks": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "There was a shimmery pink silk frock with lace and ribbons and the daintiest pink kid slippers with diamond buckles and pink silk stockings with lovely clocks", "context_len": 27}}}, "squeezing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "These she insisted on squeezing behind the colonel 's back , and though he would have been more comfortable without them , he allowed her to do it , as she had taken so much trouble to bring them , and smiled and thanked her ; so she was quite sure she had made him perfectly easy", "context_len": 57}}}, "lashing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Instinct took us towards the sea , from which blew a stinging , salt-laden breeze that caused a sensation of freedom , and when at length we gained the summit of the last rush-grown dyke , we could see the waves lashing the beach in so violent a manner as to make an attempt to escape by boat an absolute impossibility", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "And their demoralization was complete when Polulu , with a tremendous roar , came bounding in upon them , his eyes glaring , his tail lashing , his paws striking out like flails", "context_len": 33}}}, "downpour": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 52, "context": "Very slowly and gradually the storm worked its way toward the zenith , gathering intensity as it rose , and at length -- probably about ten o'clock -- the first drops of rain , hot and heavy , like gouts of blood , began to fall , quickly increasing to a drenching downpour , accompanied by lightning , green , rose-tinted , violet , sun-bright , that lighted up the town until every object , however minute , was as clearly visible as in broad daylight , while the ceaseless crashing of the thunder was unspeakably appalling", "context_len": 97}}}, "fended": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "However , he fended not himself neither took heed of this , but ceased not to go round about the city till he came under Alaeddin 's palace , where he fell to crying his loudest , whilst the children called after him , `` Madman ! Madman ! ''", "context_len": 50}}}, "cockle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "On coming alongside we fended off our frail cockle shell , while Felgate climbed softly up her sides and gained the deck", "context_len": 22}}}, "cuddy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Reassured , Peter crept aft , where a slightly raised deck formed the roof of a small cuddy or cabin", "context_len": 20}}}, "housed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Josiah kept no slaves himself but had a shed in which he allowed these captives to be housed", "context_len": 18}}}, "tabernacle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "Alfgar gazed with reverence , as well as love , upon the calm features from which the expression of pain had wholly passed ; the light of the fire , mingling strangely with that of the rising full moon , illumined them in this their first day of nothingness , for the spirit which had lived and dwelt in the tabernacle of clay had fled", "context_len": 65}}}, "windlass": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "They made a bow and stern frame , and set up two ribs on a side where the masts were to come , laid a rough deck at the mast-holes , and forward for the windlass and the heel of the bowsprit to rest on ; the remainder was all open", "context_len": 51}}}, "westwards": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then it was an easy matter to spread the great tanned sail , and having slipped the cable we stood westwards towards England and freedom", "context_len": 25}}}, "triangle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "A hundred miles from Rabaul the ceiling closed again , and Barry turned his Fortress back on the second leg of a big triangle", "context_len": 24}}}, "precedes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The blackness that precedes the dawn is always greatest ; the shore was invisible , and our sole guide as to direction was the wind , which we hoped still blew from the same quarter", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "After Char\u00e0h a new page begins , and the -wah following , which has no connection with what precedes , proves that there is a printer 's lacune , perhaps of a whole line", "context_len": 34}}}, "tacked": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Having tried in vain to edge a way through the press , Peggy and Bobby tacked round by the fields , and scrambling over a garden wall , found a temporary refuge in the churchyard , which stood some height above the level of the road , and where , from the vantage-ground of a convenient tombstone , they found they had an excellent view of the fair below", "context_len": 69}}}, "boundless": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 3, "context": "Oh , the boundless delight of those rolling miles of heather , which looked like a crimson sea spreading onward towards the horizon , and the delicious smell of the sweet-gale as they trod it underfoot ! Great flocks of plovers flew before them , screaming their 'pee-wit , pee-wit ! ' and here and there they roused a snipe or a woodcock , while wild little ponies scampered off like the wind , indignant at having their solitude disturbed", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "His wealth was almost boundless", "context_len": 5}}}, "sandbanks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "At about six in the morning the tide had left us high and dry , and the boundless expanse of sandbanks showed us how hopeless was our task on a dark night", "context_len": 32}}}, "brig": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` For by a most lucky chance I have to-day obtained what I believe to be trustworthy information to the effect that she was sighted four days ago , bound for the Gaboon river -- or perhaps it would be more correct to say that she was sighted steering east , and identified by the master of a brig who knows her perfectly well , and who has since arrived here , and that there is authentic information to the effect that she is this time bound for the Gaboon", "context_len": 90}}}, "disclosures": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "As the 23rd day of April , 1668 , drew near , that being my twenty-first birthday , I found that I was becoming more and more excited over the vague rumours that I had heard from time to time respecting the remarkable disclosures that were to be made under the terms of my father \u2019 s will", "context_len": 58}}}, "furbishing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "My uncle busied himself till all traces of his worry lost themselves in his activity ; my aunt bustled about the house , driving the servants hither and thither , bringing linen from the huge oak chest , furbishing the massive furniture , and causing a huge supply of viands to be prepared for the entertainment of our guests -- for the invited company numbered close on a score , so that our house would be filled to overflowing , and rooms had to be engaged at `` The Bell Inn '' and `` The Blue Posts ''", "context_len": 98}}}, "overflowing": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 15, "context": "Moreover , he brought him into a place , which he found full of coffers overflowing with royal raiment , such as ravished the wit , gold-inwoven stuffs , Indian and Chinese , and brocades , and he showed him also many other places , all full of that which beggareth description , till at last he brought him into a stable , wherein were horses whose like is not found with the kings of the world ; and therewithin he showed him a storehouse , full of housings and saddles of price , all broidered with pearls and precious stones and so forth", "context_len": 104}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "It was a merry , not to say boisterous , party , for Archie was in one of his funniest moods , and told 'tall ' Yankee stories till the children nearly rolled off their seats with laughter , and Lilian went on pouring into her overflowing cup till the tray was swimming with tea", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The jungle abounded with these miniature pools , their bottoms only a muddy ooze at most times , the hollows only full after a rainfall or the overflowing of some stream in the vicinity", "context_len": 34}}}, "greyer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "There were Sir George Lee , greyer and more bent than of yore , Master Hugh Salesbury , Lawyer Whitehead , all from the neighbourhood of Rake ; my old shipmates , Greville Drake and Felgate , the latter accompanied by his young wife , whom he had lately wedded , and several others who had been friends of my father long before I could remember", "context_len": 66}}}, "cough": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 74, "context": "The children had made a nest of grass for him inside a basket , and fed him on worms and scraps of raw meat until he was old enough to fly , when he would follow them everywhere about the farmyard and outbuildings , calling 'Jack , Jack ! ' which , with the mewing of a cat , the gobble of the old turkey-cock , and a close imitation of David 's winter cough , made up the extent of his accomplishments", "context_len": 83}}}, "client": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` True , true ! Sir George , '' replied Master Whitehead ; `` but unfortunately , though Sir Aubrey is not to be held accountable , the fact remains that the complete solution is missing , and , as my late client refused to make a duplicate , the secret is as far off as ever", "context_len": 57}}}, "attains": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` In accordance with the instruction laid down by my late client , Master Owen Wentworth , I have to make the announcement that his son , who this day attains his majority , can now take upon himself his rightful title , Sir Aubrey Wentworth of Holwick , in the county of Yorks", "context_len": 54}}}, "numb": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 60, "context": "It was chilly work getting up in the dark mornings by candle-light , and driving off to school when the sun had scarcely risen ; the four miles of road seemed much longer than they had done in the summertime , and in spite of woollen gloves , the hand which held the reins was apt to be stiff and numb with the cold long before Warford was reached", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 74, "context": "In this case , then , tangled and helpless as he was , Harry Paul could only think for a few moments of the time when he swam into the sea-cave at Pen Point at high tide , and felt the long strands of the bladder wrack curl and twist round his limbs like the tentacles of some sea-monster ; and he realised once more the chilling sense of helpless horror that seemed to numb his faculties", "context_len": 77}}}, "indistinct": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "The first syllable , as pronounced by the Creek Indians , contains a clear , short a , and that the name was written Muscogee and not Mascogee , is not to be wondered at , for the English language , with its surd , indistinct and strongly modified vocalization , will convert the clearest a into a u", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Let 's get back , '' said Fortescue , as the grey-hulled vessels grew more and more indistinct in the starlit night", "context_len": 23}}}, "inconsiderable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The residue , which is no inconsiderable amount , he hid in a secure place , taking good care that it should come to his son on his attaining the age of twenty-one , provided that he showed great promise of filling his position in a right and proper manner", "context_len": 50}}}, "parchments": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "He flung back the heavy lid , and revealed -- neither gold nor jewels , nothing but a pile of musty-looking old parchments and books", "context_len": 25}}}, "accountable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` True , true ! Sir George , '' replied Master Whitehead ; `` but unfortunately , though Sir Aubrey is not to be held accountable , the fact remains that the complete solution is missing , and , as my late client refused to make a duplicate , the secret is as far off as ever", "context_len": 57}}}, "duplicate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` True , true ! Sir George , '' replied Master Whitehead ; `` but unfortunately , though Sir Aubrey is not to be held accountable , the fact remains that the complete solution is missing , and , as my late client refused to make a duplicate , the secret is as far off as ever", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Not altogether to my surprise , I now learned that the Virginia and the Preciosa were one and the same craft , manned by two complete crews -- one American and one Spanish -- and furnished with duplicate sets of papers", "context_len": 41}}}, "correspond": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "They are simply suffixes of pronominal origin , and correspond to our articles the , a , to our relative and demonstrative pronouns , partly also to our adverbs , prepositions and conjunctions", "context_len": 33}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 127, "context": "asked Alaeddin , `` What is the cause that the lattice of yonder kiosk ( kushk ) is not complete ? '' The defective part is soon after referred to , no less than four times , as `` the lattice of the kiosk '' ( sheriyyetu ' l kushk ) , thus showing that , in the writer 's mind , kushk , liwan and shubbak were synonymous terms for the common Arab projecting square-sided window , made of latticework , and I have therefore rendered the three words , when they occur in this sense , by our English `` oriel , '' to whose modern meaning ( a window that juts out , so as to form a small apartment ) , they exactly correspond", "context_len": 128}}}, "wording": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Troth ! '' he ejaculated , `` if a man of law can not frame the wording of a document , how can I , a country gentleman , hope to do it ? ''", "context_len": 36}}}, "feminine": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "This short story of 180 pages is captivating from start to finish , and the masculine reader takes off his hat , and the feminine reader courtesies , to this matron of the tubs , with her homely heroism and true kindness of heart", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "I have written out , large and black , so that I may easily read in dim light , more than 1300 words , to go over regularly every fortnight , masculine separated from feminine nouns", "context_len": 36}}}, "bottommost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Then begin with the bottommost line and read upwards", "context_len": 10}}}, "stipulation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Mistress Felgate gave her permission with the stipulation that her husband must run no needless risks , the compact was sealed by a kiss bestowed by her gallant and unabashed bridegroom , and the conversation was resumed , while mirth and gaiety reigned supreme", "context_len": 44}}}, "bridegroom": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Then the Sultan turned to the Vizier and said to him , `` How now ? Deemest thou Alaeddin worthy to be bridegroom to my daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour ? Hast thou seen and considered this royal building and all these riches which man 's wit can not comprehend ? '' The Vizier , of his envy of Alaeddin , answered him , saying , `` O King of the Age , indeed this palace and its building and all these riches may not be but by means of enchantment , for that no man among men , no , not the mightiest of them in dominion or the greatest in wealth , might avail to upraise and stablish [ the like of ] this building in", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "So she rejoiced , albeit she had been sad and sorry for her [ whilom ] bridegroom the Vizier 's son , -- she rejoiced , [ I say ] , with an exceeding joy , when she saw the jewels and the beauty of the damsels , and was cheered ; whilst her father rejoiced exceedingly in her joy , in that he saw her put off chagrin and dejection", "context_len": 71}}}, "forestalled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "The Spaniards did not relish this , and were going to board the tiny English craft , but again they were forestalled ; for Cochrane with all his men took the Gamo by storm , killed some , and frightened others ; and ere long a marvellous sight was witnessed at Minorca , the great Gamo was brought by the Speedy into the harbour , with over 263 men on board , hale and hearty , whilst Cochrane never had a fifth of that number !", "context_len": 86}}}, "posting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Presently Felgate joined us , and together we slipped out of the house , crossed the street , and entered the establishment that Nicholas Wade ran under the designation of posting stables", "context_len": 32}}}, "mincingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The owner , bald-headed , high-shouldered , and bow-legged like many of his class , came forth and mincingly asked what we required", "context_len": 23}}}, "verity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` But no matter , the brown nag will serve me , for of a verity I feel more at home in a jollyboat than on the back of that beast", "context_len": 31}}}, "punctually": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Punctually , therefore , at six o \u2019 clock she got up , -- like her hero , Fides , conquering Giant Sloth , -- and thus made sure of at least an hour \u2019 s writing before breakfast", "context_len": 39}}}, "cantered": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "Punctually to the minute our steeds were brought round , the farewells were said , and with a loose rein we cantered down the narrow cobble-paved street towards the Landport Gate", "context_len": 31}}}, "timbered": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "The horses \u2019 hoofs echoed under the dark gateway and clattered across the drawbridge , the town of Portsmouth was left behind , and the dense cluster of timbered and red-tiled houses gave place to verdant fields and clumps of tall trees that even now were beginning to burst into leaf", "context_len": 51}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "The quaint timbered house , with its carved gables and red-tiled roof , was built in at one end into a kind of square tower or keep , with tiny turret windows and winding staircase , getting just a little ruinous in places , but held firmly together by masses of ivy , which clung round it like a green mantle", "context_len": 61}}}, "highways": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "It would be useless to try the highways , she knew , so accordingly her search must be in the by-ways , and she made a little tour of investigation round all the back streets between the inn and the Grammar School , but without success , and she was just thinking she must have missed him , and had better return to the inn-yard , when a fortunate chance prompted her to turn up a retired avenue which lay between the two main roads", "context_len": 85}}}, "headline": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "At Petersfield we stopped for our midday meal , and after giving our horses a well-earned rest , we resumed our way northward till the bold headline of Hindhead loomed up in front of us", "context_len": 35}}}, "outgrown": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Lord ha \u2019 mercy on me if \u2019 tis not young Master Wentworth ! '' he exclaimed , showing that , though grown in stature , I had not outgrown the appearance of my boyhood ; and when Drake told him of my newly found title , the old man tottered away to let the frequenters of the inn know the news ; whereat we , unwilling to tarry longer , pushed on towards the towering heights of Hindhead", "context_len": 80}}}, "frequenters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "`` Lord ha \u2019 mercy on me if \u2019 tis not young Master Wentworth ! '' he exclaimed , showing that , though grown in stature , I had not outgrown the appearance of my boyhood ; and when Drake told him of my newly found title , the old man tottered away to let the frequenters of the inn know the news ; whereat we , unwilling to tarry longer , pushed on towards the towering heights of Hindhead", "context_len": 80}}}, "heath": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "But across the heath a clump of trees rising against the pale sky seemed to shelter a group of buildings , where possibly some charitable hand might bestow broken meat on a beggar , or at least a corner in a rick-yard might afford a shelter from the bitter frost that was numbing his limbs", "context_len": 55}}}, "repulsive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "At one end of the beam was a rough chair in which was bound a miserable old woman of repulsive appearance , whose face bore a look of mute despair", "context_len": 30}}}, "immersing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "As we approached , the crowd , too intent to notice our coming , had seized the beam and were swinging it over the pond with the object of immersing the occupant of the ducking stool", "context_len": 36}}}, "dwellers": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Because I know the secrets of the prison house -- knew them before the Danes had murdered the once happy dwellers in this garden of England , which they have made a howling wilderness ; hence I escaped the wrath of the furious parricide , whom the saints destroy , with ease , and laughed in security at their vain efforts to take me ; but we must waste no time ; it yet wants five hours to daybreak ; within those five hours we must reach the opposite shore", "context_len": 91}}}, "interrupters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The mob gave way , still shouting fierce imprecations against the terrified old woman , and making hostile demonstrations against the interrupters of their fiendish sport", "context_len": 26}}}, "conventicle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Who is responsible for this conventicle ? '' he continued , urging his horse towards the ducking stool", "context_len": 19}}}, "warlocks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` By virtue of the act passed in the reign of His Majesty King James the First , of blessed memory , concerning the punishment of sorcerers , witches , warlocks , and the like", "context_len": 35}}}, "tut": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "statute": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Tut , tut , man , the statute is dead ! Have you a magistrate \u2019 s warrant , Form 226 , giving you authority for this ? Quick , answer me ! I am a King \u2019 s officer , so on your peril speak truly ! ''", "context_len": 50}}}, "slimy": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 64, "context": "Even if he had had an open sharp knife in his hand he could hardly have cut himself free , and in the horror of those brief moments he found that his struggles were sending him deeper and deeper , and that unconsciously he had wound himself still farther in the net , till his arms and legs were pinioned in the cold , slimy bonds , which clung to and wrapped round him more and more", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "In an instant he had pounced upon it , and firmly grasping the wriggling creature , he rushed out of the brook , and running over the grass as fast as his little wet , bare feet could carry him , went up to Aunt Patty , and laying the slimy , muddy reptile on her lap , exclaimed , with an air of great triumph and generosity , --", "context_len": 70}}}, "attackers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "But , of course , even a brief absence from the fort left the flag undefended , and there was always a chance that , while one of the attackers was being pursued , some of the others might steal up and succeed in going off with the flag", "context_len": 49}}}, "cowards": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 17, "context": "At the sight of six levelled weapons the crowd drew back ; then , satisfied that the cowards were properly cowed , Felgate jumped from his horse , made his way to the ducking stool , and cut the bonds that held the old crone to the chair", "context_len": 48}}}, "crone": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 44, "context": "At the sight of six levelled weapons the crowd drew back ; then , satisfied that the cowards were properly cowed , Felgate jumped from his horse , made his way to the ducking stool , and cut the bonds that held the old crone to the chair", "context_len": 48}}}, "arching": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 70, "context": "Baulked of their prey , the mob still surged round us , and with a shout of : `` Let \u2019 em have the cat ! '' a great black object was sent flying towards us , and , striking my horse on the crupper , resolved itself into an enormous black cat , that spat and howled , digging its claws into the horse \u2019 s hide , and arching its back like the demon cat that is the reputed companion of every witch", "context_len": 85}}}, "mewed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "A word from the old woman caused the animal to jump towards her , and , climbing on her shoulders , it mewed and purred with a fearsome delight", "context_len": 29}}}, "beldam": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Lifting the beldam to his horse , Felgate placed her pillionwise behind him", "context_len": 13}}}, "highroad": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "We closed in on either side , and , forcing our way through the mob , our pistols still pointed ominously at them , we gained the highroad once more , and trotted unmolested through the village of Liphook", "context_len": 39}}}, "hag": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "Now that the danger was past , Drake and I could not help laughing at our cavalier companion and his fair burden , for the hag had clasped him tightly round the waist with her skinny arms , while the cat , perched on her shoulders , was rubbing its head against the back of Felgate \u2019 s plumed hat , so that it was being continually thrust over his eyes despite his frequent attempts to place it firmly on his head", "context_len": 82}}}, "quavering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` At the top of the Devil \u2019 s Punch Bowl , if it pleases you , sir , '' replied the old woman in a quavering voice , `` for then I shall be safe", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I have never looked so far forward as that , '' she answered in quavering tones ; `` but we need not think of that yet , Dick", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "He returned the scowl of the Indian with a flashing smile that showed all his white teeth , and , beating with his two clenched hands upon his bronzed chest , cried in a loud voice that held no sign of quavering :", "context_len": 43}}}, "uphill": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 87, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "We had been going uphill for some time , and we stopped now before some iron gates ; a woman ran out of a lodge and opened the gates , and then we drove down a short avenue shaded by some fine trees", "context_len": 43}}}, "depressing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Although I had oft journeyed across this bleak hill , never before had I seen it under such depressing circumstances", "context_len": 20}}}, "treetops": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "The sun had long vanished behind a bank of dark-grey , undefined clouds , while a cold wind howled across our path , moaning through the treetops and raising clouds of choking dust on the sun-dried highway", "context_len": 37}}}, "horseshoe": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Just as we reached the summit , where the road makes a vast horseshoe curve round the dark , forbidding cavity known as the Devil \u2019 s Punch Bowl , a heavy rainstorm came on , blotting out the horizon , while a vivid flash of lightning , followed at a short interval by a tremendous clap of thunder , startled our horses , and , be it confessed , ourselves as well", "context_len": 73}}}, "cavity": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "Just as we reached the summit , where the road makes a vast horseshoe curve round the dark , forbidding cavity known as the Devil \u2019 s Punch Bowl , a heavy rainstorm came on , blotting out the horizon , while a vivid flash of lightning , followed at a short interval by a tremendous clap of thunder , startled our horses , and , be it confessed , ourselves as well", "context_len": 73}}}, "rainstorm": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Just as we reached the summit , where the road makes a vast horseshoe curve round the dark , forbidding cavity known as the Devil \u2019 s Punch Bowl , a heavy rainstorm came on , blotting out the horizon , while a vivid flash of lightning , followed at a short interval by a tremendous clap of thunder , startled our horses , and , be it confessed , ourselves as well", "context_len": 73}}}, "blotting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Just as we reached the summit , where the road makes a vast horseshoe curve round the dark , forbidding cavity known as the Devil \u2019 s Punch Bowl , a heavy rainstorm came on , blotting out the horizon , while a vivid flash of lightning , followed at a short interval by a tremendous clap of thunder , startled our horses , and , be it confessed , ourselves as well", "context_len": 73}}}, "smacks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Thunder in April ! And in company with a witch ! This smacks of His Satanic Majesty with a vengeance ! '' muttered Drake , drawing closer to me", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Methinks it smacks somewhat of cowardice to drag a lady into my peril ? ''", "context_len": 16}}}, "heathery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` I have not far to go now -- my home is down there , '' indicating with a skinny finger the rain-blotted heathery pit beneath us", "context_len": 27}}}, "dame": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 37, "context": "Benito was a very young child , scarcely able to walk on the narrow road without the help of some older and wiser hand , and his weak shoulders could not carry any part of the old dame 's load ; but he put his baby hands beneath it , and gave her loving smiles and gentle words , and these brought her help and comfort , so that she went on her way , strengthened for the rest of the journey", "context_len": 82}}}, "mumbled": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 29, "context": "But Ford Fenton got no further , for Dutch Housenlager , putting out his foot , neatly tripped the offending one , and the rest of his sentence was mumbled to the grass", "context_len": 33}}}, "blinking": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "Why do you stand there blinking at me like a fool ? Do not rouse my wrath , Bartow ! The wrath of Sobrinini is a terrible thing , as no one should know better than you", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Donald and Harold were on the eve of laughing outright , but Marie-Celeste , detecting a suspicious blinking in the long curling lashes of the eyelids , kept them still by an imperative gesture", "context_len": 34}}}, "heather": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 10, "context": "Oh , the boundless delight of those rolling miles of heather , which looked like a crimson sea spreading onward towards the horizon , and the delicious smell of the sweet-gale as they trod it underfoot ! Great flocks of plovers flew before them , screaming their 'pee-wit , pee-wit ! ' and here and there they roused a snipe or a woodcock , while wild little ponies scampered off like the wind , indignant at having their solitude disturbed", "context_len": 80}}}, "gorse": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "The walk which Bobby had proposed this afternoon was somewhat of a scramble , for the country rose behind the Abbey into undulating hills , which were fairly steep , though not so high as the Welsh mountains , and were covered for the most part with gorse and rough grass , where the sheep and young bullocks were turned out to graze", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "'No , no ! Up the hill , over the gorse common , and down the yew-tree lane", "context_len": 18}}}, "tarried": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Accordingly , the Lady Bedrulbudour tarried not , but entered the inner pavilion , she and her women ; whereupon Alaeddin arose and locked the door on them and going up to the Maugrabin , put his hand to his sleeve and pulled out the lamp ; after which he drew his sword and cut off the sorcerer 's head", "context_len": 61}}}, "endless": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 25, "context": "Here was a quantity of ground covered with brush , logs , and bushes : to have hauled all this away would have been an endless job , and after that the ground could neither be ploughed nor planted , being entirely matted with green roots , and cold and sour ; besides , the moment the sun was let into it , sprouts would begin to spring up from the stumps , and weeds , blackberry , and raspberry bushes from the ground , and cover it all over", "context_len": 90}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "The baby ceased its endless wailing , and with Jennie to keep things trim after they had once been put in order , the whole household put on its old air of cosy neatness", "context_len": 34}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "It is mortal ; that is , it must die ; but our souls are immortal ; they will go on living for ever and ever , either loving and praising God through all eternity , or sinking down to endless woe and suffering", "context_len": 44}}}, "colonists": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 96, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "whereon": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "A brook coming down from the hills had overflowed the land until a swamp or quagmire had been formed , whereon huge trees rotted in slime , while creeping plants hid the deformity of decay", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` Quoth the Sultan , `` I marvel at thee how thou still deemest evil of Alaeddin ; but methinketh it ariseth from thine envy of him , for that thou wast present when he sought of me a place whereon to build a palace for my daughter and I accorded him , before thee , [ leave to build ] a palace on this ground ; and he who brought me , to my daughter 's dower , jewels such that no kings possess one thereof , shall he lack ableness to build a palace like this ? '' When [ 483 ] the Vizier heard the Sultan 's speech and understood that he loved Alaeddin greatly , his envy of him increased ; withal he availed", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Climbing out with some difficulty , and disturbing in the process hundreds of bat-mice and not a few rats , they found themselves in the midst of some old ruins at the foot of the acclivity whereon the fortress was built , and below them the brook ran rapidly to join the river", "context_len": 53}}}, "manorial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Poverty , through manorial neglect , was only too apparent , and I could not help exclaiming despondently : `` Look , friends ! What a heritage , and hardly a scrap of paper to prove my right to it ! ''", "context_len": 42}}}, "despondently": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Poverty , through manorial neglect , was only too apparent , and I could not help exclaiming despondently : `` Look , friends ! What a heritage , and hardly a scrap of paper to prove my right to it ! ''", "context_len": 42}}}, "servile": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` For , '' quoth Felgate to the servile landlord , `` we have a desire to know more of this old castle , and methinks that good fishing is obtainable in this stream", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Being of a mechanical turn of mind , the two brothers soon reduced the motor to a state of servile tractability , although there was hardly a thoroughfare in Christchurch whose buildings did not bear a more or less permanent record of Juggernaut 's frailties", "context_len": 45}}}, "dace": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Eh , my masters , '' replied he , `` \u2019 tis not to be beaten in all Yorkshire for good sport -- trout , dace , chub , and even the lordly salmon ; and as for t \u2019 old castle -- well , \u2019 tis said that spooks be about", "context_len": 53}}}, "chub": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Eh , my masters , '' replied he , `` \u2019 tis not to be beaten in all Yorkshire for good sport -- trout , dace , chub , and even the lordly salmon ; and as for t \u2019 old castle -- well , \u2019 tis said that spooks be about", "context_len": 53}}}, "spooks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Eh , my masters , '' replied he , `` \u2019 tis not to be beaten in all Yorkshire for good sport -- trout , dace , chub , and even the lordly salmon ; and as for t \u2019 old castle -- well , \u2019 tis said that spooks be about", "context_len": 53}}}, "leastwise": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Leastwise I never care to go yonder missen , for strange noises affright the whole countryside ! ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "wainscoted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It was an oak-panelled , wainscoted room , with a low , smoke-grimed ceiling that was traversed by a massive beam", "context_len": 21}}}, "paved": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "Harold , who had been all through the stables of the Duke of Westminster at Eton Hall , had described something much finer than this -- imposing buildings surrounding a courtyard paved with bevel-edged squares of stone , with not so much as a whisp of hay or straw to be seen anywhere , and in the centre a noble statue of a high-spirited horse , rearing and pulling hard at the bridle , held in the hand of a stalwart groom , who seems fully equal to the occasion", "context_len": 90}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "If he could have seen the handsomely appointed quarters I had been used to -- the paved floors and fine stalls ! Poor lad ! where had he been brought up to think this rough log cabin of some early backwoodsman a suitable place for a Shetland pony ?", "context_len": 49}}}, "ingle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The floor was paved with large stones , while an ingle nook and settle imparted a cheerful aspect to the apartment", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "looked a cheerful place , with its polished-oak dressers and shining brasses , and on a winter 's evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and sent", "context_len": 128}}}, "imparted": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 57, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "mattock": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "But what attracted my attention most was a mattock and a couple of spades , with the rich red clay still sticking to them , lying in a corner of the room", "context_len": 32}}}, "gravedigger": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "In the church to which Joseph Livesey belonged the ringers and singers were hard drinkers , the gravedigger was a drunkard , and the parish clerk was often intoxicated !", "context_len": 30}}}, "cringing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Our host soon appeared , cringing and bowing like the menial that he was", "context_len": 14}}}, "inaudible": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "The landlord took his horse to the stables where ours were kept , and Joyce made to follow , but with some inaudible remark the former succeeded in inducing the villain to enter the house", "context_len": 35}}}, "justices": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Fail us , or play us false , and you \u2019 ll answer to the king \u2019 s justices at York", "context_len": 21}}}, "compliance": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 60, "context": "It is by some lexicologists supposed to have arisen from the circumstance of a man answering another , who begged of him a wine-jar ( hubb ) , with the words , `` Ay , I will give thee a jar and a cover ( kerameh ) also , '' and to have thus become a tropical expression of ready compliance with a petition , as who should say , `` I will give thee what thou askest and more", "context_len": 80}}}, "platters": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 45, "context": "The genie carried him into a treasury full of all manner of gold and silver and precious jewels past count or reckoning , price or estimation ; then he brought him into another place , where he saw all the requisites of the table , platters and spoons and ewers and basins and cups , of gold and silver , and thence to the kitchen , where he found cooks , [ 476 ] with their cooking-gear and utensils , all on like wise of gold and silver", "context_len": 88}}}, "appeasing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "In a few minutes we heard him calling for food and drink , and the clattering of knives and platters showed that he was appeasing his appetite with zest", "context_len": 29}}}, "zest": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "In a few minutes we heard him calling for food and drink , and the clattering of knives and platters showed that he was appeasing his appetite with zest", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "It may be also that , now he was gone for whom and with whom she had written her plays , all zest in that direction was gone with him , and the gift of writing , like a river dammed up in one direction and forced to turn elsewhere , sought naturally a fresh outlet , -- an outlet with which there should be no overpoweringly sad associations", "context_len": 69}}}, "jangle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "In his hand he carried a horn lantern , while the jangle of steel showed that the spades were to be brought to work", "context_len": 24}}}, "confines": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 61, "context": "If ever I attempted such a thing -- and I did , very frequently -- a party of the king 's guards was certain to turn up , in the most exasperatingly casual and unexpected manner , and join me , under the pretence , as they made me understand , that it was extremely dangerous to venture alone beyond the confines of the town , if I pretended that I was engaged in hunting for animals , or plants to be used in my medical practice", "context_len": 87}}}, "spade": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "-- '' You are both mistaken , '' replied their father : `` you , John , have not strength to put the spade far enough into the hard ground ; and you , Jane , would be apt to pull up flowers as well as weeds : and so you must both be satisfied to work in your own little gardens for the present , till the one is stronger , and the other wiser", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Smugglers always had bags of money -- spade guineas , you know , and Spanish doubloons , and all those kinds of things you read about in books", "context_len": 28}}}, "seeker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "For a while no sound save the occasional hoot of an owl and the rapidly dying breeze broke the stillness as we waited for some signs of the renewed efforts of the treasure seeker", "context_len": 34}}}, "swirled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "He paused in a listening attitude , but the only sounds that came to him were those of the wind howling through the leafless branches of the trees , and the swish of the snowflakes as they swirled downward", "context_len": 39}}}, "banished": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 6, "context": "The Yamacraw chief Tomochichi , then banished from one of the Lower Creek towns , spoke in favor of making a treaty with the foreigners , and Yah\u00f3la ` l\u00e1ko , m\u00edko of Kaw\u00edta , allowed Tomochichi and his relatives `` to call the kindred , that love them , out of each of the Creek towns , that they may come together and make one town", "context_len": 67}}}, "mounds": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 59, "context": "The spot was on the northern borders of the ancient kingdom of Sussex -- the land of the Saxon Ella -- a spot marvelously favoured by nature , occupying the summit of a low hill , which commanded a wide prospect on all sides , while itself almost impregnable when fortified , as it was , by ditches and mounds , dug in the usual Danish fashion , for the Danes owed much of their success to their skill in fortification", "context_len": 81}}}, "unrelenting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "I grasped him with a great effort , and we dragged him out of the hole , his jack boot being wrenched from his foot by the unrelenting grip of the tendrils", "context_len": 32}}}, "semicircle": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 72, "context": "Such proceedings as these could not of course long exist without the fame of them reaching the ears of the king , and I had only given some three or four performances when I was summoned to entertain his Majesty and his household , which I did in the great square before the palace , my audience numbering quite two thousand ; Banda and his numerous family being seated in a huge semicircle -- of which I was the centre -- in front of the palace , while the rest of the audience filled the remaining portion of the square", "context_len": 100}}}, "stampede": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "They raised a cheer at our approach , and when we told them of our wants there was a general stampede on the part of the men folk for digging implements", "context_len": 31}}}, "sheriff": {"freq": 51, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 14, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I \u2019 d have brought him around here with me , but the sheriff \u2019 s got handcuffs on him , and it \u2019 s hard to get him out and in the wagon", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Where \u2019 s the beast dodged to , sheriff ? questioned the other man", "context_len": 14}}}, "delve": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Before you delve , or take possession of , any portion of this land , I must have his authority", "context_len": 20}}}, "wroth": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` And where is your authority ? '' I demanded , wroth at being interfered with on my own land", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "When the Marid heard Alaeddin 's words , his face frowned and he was wroth and cried out with a terrible great voice , saying , `` O denier of benefits , doth it not suffice thee that I and all the slaves of the Lamp are at thy service and wouldst thou eke have me bring thee our liege lady , for thy pleasure , and hang her in the dome of thy pavilion , to divert thee and thy wife ? By Allah , ye deserve that I should forthright reduce you both to ashes and scatter you to the winds ! But , inasmuch as ye are ignorant , thou and she , concerning this matter and know not its inward from its outward ,", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "' Wherefore I crave Thy Grace 's clemency and hope that thou wilt pardon me and my son this effrontery neither be wroth with us therefor", "context_len": 26}}}, "script": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Surely it was a pretty pass -- a knight without a scrap of script to prove himself such !", "context_len": 19}}}, "deferential": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "How do you do , dear ? said the lady , extending her hand , which Marie-Celeste , her cheeks aflame with the unexpected abruptness of Albert 's introduction , took in hers , in a pretty deferential sort of way , as though fully conscious of the dignity of her surroundings", "context_len": 52}}}, "stabled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "We walked slowly towards Petergate , where our steeds had been stabled", "context_len": 12}}}, "duration": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 17, "context": "But here the Rector again stepped in , and limited the performance to half an hour 's duration , begging them to choose something appropriate for a Sunday-school audience , and if possible with a moral , and further reminding them that a platform consisting of tables hastily pushed together after the tea was concluded would scarcely give facilities for either footlights , drop-scenes , or side-shifts , a curtain being the utmost he could undertake to manage in that direction", "context_len": 81}}}, "workers": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 94, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}}}, "dams": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "In this the children were sometimes allowed to play , since it was shallow , and they had great fun there , building little dams , sailing the tiny boats which the boys made for them , or dipping the water out in some small pails mamma had bought for them , and carrying it from place to place", "context_len": 59}}}, "lawyers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "On this occasion they had almost completely filled the seats in the hall , staring at the judge , the lawyers , the witnesses and the prisoner , and filling the room with a suppressed hum of comment until called to order", "context_len": 42}}}, "sucked": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 12, "context": "The resolute boy breasted the waves ; but so far from being sucked in , he found it impossible to reach the spot where the ball lay , and the suction began , by reason of the wind , which blew directly in his face , and the sea , that , beyond the influence of the breaker , drove directly to the shore ; and , worn out with effort , he returned exhausted to the boat", "context_len": 78}}}, "descend": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 82, "context": "Mark Penelly had gone over to the other side of the lugger while the conversation was going on , and he did not face the man he looked upon as his rival ; while Harry , unnoticed by the busy fishers as he swam round , went on , touching the sides of the lugger as he lightly swam , but only the next moment to find himself entangled in a quantity of the thin mackerel net , which seemed somehow to descend upon him like a cloud , and before he could realise the fact he was under water , hopelessly fettered by the net , and feeling that if he could not extricate himself directly he should be a dead man", "context_len": 123}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Descend ! why , hang me if it isn \u2019 t a vault !", "context_len": 14}}}, "subterranean": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 12, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "converting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Apparently it had at one time been a subterranean passage between the castle and the village , but walls had been built , converting it into a small chamber of about twenty feet in length and six in breadth", "context_len": 39}}}, "cobbles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Most of this the New Zealanders could not see ; while presently they heard the wretched beast 's hoofs slipping on the cobbles as the barge slowly gathered way", "context_len": 29}}}, "cement": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "( We would say to our readers , that the scythe rifles in those days were not made as at present , by putting sand or emery upon wood , with cement ; but they scratched the wood and made it rough , then smeared it with tallow , and put fine sand on it , which adhered to the tallow and the scratches", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "There were cement mines to be seen , and old white-faced mansions ; and half-way up the boat passed under a lofty iron bridge across which dashed a railway train", "context_len": 30}}}, "rusted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Three eager faces peered anxiously down into what certainly looked like the inside of a small stone coffin , but instead of containing mouldering bones , it held a good-sized chest of oak , bound with iron , rather rusted and crumbling , but still holding quite firmly together", "context_len": 49}}}, "dispatched": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 89, "context": "My deduction of the case is as follows : Eliot has a sister ; this sister is attacked by the vicious dogs of one Fletcher ; Stone rushes to her defense ; he beats off the said dogs and kills one of them ; the before-mentioned Eliot takes his before-mentioned sister home ; he relates to his folks how she was rescued from dire peril and a fearful fate by the before-mentioned Stone ; at once her parents wish to see and thank the said Stone ; Roger is dispatched post haste for the hero of the thrilling and deadly struggle ; said hero is carried off in triumph to the palatial residence of the before-mentioned parents", "context_len": 117}}}, "documentary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Tearing open the covering , I found all the documentary evidence that was required to prove my right to the Manor of Holwick -- the cup of joy was filled to overflowing , and , in spite of my surroundings , I sought relief in a flood of tears", "context_len": 49}}}, "occupies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "The castle still stands , a venerable ruin , but a small yet stately mansion , designed by the great Wren himself , occupies a commanding position within a mile of the shattered remains of our ancestral hall", "context_len": 38}}}, "monarchs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "He did what very few monarchs have done , either before or since ; in securing for himself the pleasures of friendship , and of intimate social communion with a heart kindred to his own , without ruining himself by committing to a favorite powers which he was not qualified to wield", "context_len": 52}}}, "sc": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Sc", "context_len": 1}}}, "edinburgh": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Edinburgh : T", "context_len": 3}}}, "recollections": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 15, "context": "What chords of memory did that face stir in Bomba \u2019 s heart ? What recollections , faint and dim as some far off strains of music , were tugging at his consciousness ? What vague memory told that desolate lad that he was looking at the pictured face of his mother ?", "context_len": 53}}}, "paucity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "A paucity of materials exists as to the early part of the life ; but in later years the difficulty is of a precisely opposite description , arising from a superabundance of details", "context_len": 33}}}, "superabundance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "A paucity of materials exists as to the early part of the life ; but in later years the difficulty is of a precisely opposite description , arising from a superabundance of details", "context_len": 33}}}, "ruthlessly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` O Aescendune , once happy Aescendune ! '' was the thought , the bitter thought , as each hour placed a larger barrier of space between Alfgar and his late home ; all its happy memories came freshly back upon him , and particularly the thought of Ethelgiva , his betrothed , from whom he was so ruthlessly torn , torn as if he left part of himself behind", "context_len": 70}}}, "naturalness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "There is a simplicity and naturalness of style and incident and religion , of the most attractive and healthful kind", "context_len": 20}}}, "subsisted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The food on which they subsisted , on their expeditions , was pounded maize , contained in a small bag , which they carried upon their bodies", "context_len": 27}}}, "froth": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was an awful place in a storm , where the spray , broken up into feathery froth by the battle on the rocks , came flying over the town , and then away landward , like a fine misty rain ; but it was a grand place in a calm", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "When Charlie returned , and saw Rover in such a condition , he came very near bursting into tears ; he knelt down by him , wiped the froth away from his mouth , and rubbed him , calling him good Rover ; but piggy gave no signs of life , except it could be perceived he breathed", "context_len": 58}}}, "conversions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Bateman \u2019 s opinion of the same volume , she observes : What Rowland most objected to was the American affirming that if you take certain means to effect conversions , the result is as sure as harvest following breaking up the ground", "context_len": 43}}}, "obstacles": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 76, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "accidental": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "It is possible it may have been born with the race itself from the accidental throwing of a flat stick ; for from childhood the black fellow shows a natural bent for throwing things , as you can see by watching him use his only other weapons , the spear and club", "context_len": 52}}}, "fragmentary": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The majesty of the churches was indeed ( as a contemporary wrote ) great , but thatched roofs consorted ill with the remains of shattered column and pedestal , and with the fragmentary ruins of the grand amphitheatre , which were yet partly visible , although the stones which had been brought from Bath to build it had been employed largely in church architecture", "context_len": 64}}}, "ruthless": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "The doughty warrior , who had not quailed before the charge of the jaguar , seemed to shrink into himself , and Bomba had a new glimpse into the ruthless character with whom he was soon to deal", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 55, "context": "The painful freeing of his right hand , the fear that in the fall he might have sustained an injury that would cripple him and leave him a prey to the first beast of the jungle that might roam that way , the dread that he might never be able to free himself from those ruthless , imprisoning branches had blinded him to another and more imminent peril that threatened", "context_len": 70}}}, "contributors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "I have also decided not to give the names of Indian Christians , with very few exceptions , -- as of the Head Master of the Native Boys \u2019 School at Batala , whom she counted a personal friend ; also of one or two Ordained Native Clergymen , and one or two contributors of slight material towards this Life", "context_len": 60}}}, "varies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Miss Tucker \u2019 s own spelling of Indian words and names varies extremely ; the word being often given differently when occurring twice in a single page", "context_len": 27}}}, "preface": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 30, "context": "The tale now presented to the indulgence of the public is the second of a series of tales , each complete in itself , which , as stated in the preface to the first of the series , have been told to the senior boys of a large school , in order to secure their interest in historical characters , and to illustrate great epochs in human affairs by the aid of fiction", "context_len": 73}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Good-morning , Albert , answered Marie-Celeste , smiling at the expected , and now , with which , by way of getting the best of a tendency to stutter , Albert was accustomed to preface many of his remarks ; 1 thought I should find you here , she added ; and have you seen the lilacs , Albert ?", "context_len": 60}}}, "penned": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "Only my beloved Mother -- the Laura \u2019 of these pages -- could have penned the words which should adequately tell all that my dear Aunt was to those who knew her best and loved her most fondly", "context_len": 38}}}, "adequately": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Only my beloved Mother -- the Laura \u2019 of these pages -- could have penned the words which should adequately tell all that my dear Aunt was to those who knew her best and loved her most fondly", "context_len": 38}}}, "intrust": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It has , however , been a great satisfaction to me to intrust the preparation of the Life to Miss Giberne ; and I am glad to have this opportunity of expressing my hearty appreciation of the literary skill , the sympathy , and the fidelity to truth with which she has accomplished her task", "context_len": 55}}}, "averse": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Averse as my Aunt ever was to any fuss being made about her , nothing would have reconciled her to the publication of a Biography , save the hope that its story might be used of God to stimulate others to consecrate their lives to the Service of Christ , whether in the Foreign or Home Mission Field", "context_len": 58}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Like as not , mum , replied the old janitor honestly , he 's grown that averse to mingling much with folk , be they big or little", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Having once accepted the belief that Jane was not averse to snatch at any good in her way , even if it belonged to another , the se\u00f1ora found herself still under the sway of this opinion", "context_len": 37}}}, "consecrate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "Averse as my Aunt ever was to any fuss being made about her , nothing would have reconciled her to the publication of a Biography , save the hope that its story might be used of God to stimulate others to consecrate their lives to the Service of Christ , whether in the Foreign or Home Mission Field", "context_len": 58}}}, "profits": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "I am going to have it printed by converted Jews , and the entire profits devoted to the Society for the Conversion of Jews ; so that it will be a little offering from us both to one of the holiest of causes", "context_len": 43}}}, "derived": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 19, "context": "But this term has nothing at all in common with the Algonkin word sh\u00e1wano south , from which are derived the tribal names : Sh\u00e1wano or Shawnee , once on Ohio and Cumberland rivers and their tributaries ; Chowan in Southern Virginia ; Siwoneys in Connecticut ; Sawannoe in New Jersey ( about 1616 ) ; Chaouanons , the southern division of the Illinois or Maskoutens", "context_len": 66}}}, "apportioned": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "Thereupon they fenced in the fields selected for planting by means of rails and pickets , so as to prevent the wild animals from eating the maize-crop , and apportioned some of the land to each family [ 145 ] in the tribe", "context_len": 43}}}, "persecutions": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "PERSECUTIONS 282", "context_len": 2}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "Mingled with many other things , giving an account of their summer among the mountains , and written in Maggie 's own droll , peculiar way , ran the story of Lem and Dolly , of their persecutions , and of the difficulty she and Bessie had had in forgiving their many injuries ; but all that was not new to the mother , who now read for the first time what Maggie had written during the last week", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "PERSECUTIONS", "context_len": 1}}}, "saintly": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 96, "context": "dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of the sunset", "context_len": 128}}}, "constancy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "Smith made a list of subjects for daily prayer , embracing repentance , faith , love , grace to help , gratitude , power to pray , constant direction in all things , a right understanding of the Bible , deliverance from besetting sin , constancy in God 's service , relatives and friends , missionaries , pardon for all ignorance and sin in prayer , etc", "context_len": 67}}}, "faithfulness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 62, "context": "Others suppose that the act was an expression of his implicit faith in the integrity and fidelity of his servant , and that he intended it as testimony , given in a very pointed and decisive , and , at the same time , delicate manner , that he was not suspicious of his friends , or easily led to distrust their faithfulness", "context_len": 63}}}, "consecrating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "They took the cart and the yoke to preserve as sacred relics , consecrating them to Jupiter ; and Gordius tied the yoke to the pole of the cart by a thong of leather , making a knot so close and complicated that nobody could untie it again", "context_len": 48}}}, "signifying": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": ", -- signifying A Lady Of England , -- as the successful author of numberless children \u2019 s books , deserves to be yet more extensively known as the heroic Pioneer of elderly and Honorary volunteers in the broad Mission-fields of our Church", "context_len": 43}}}, "extensively": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "The history of the life of every individual who has , for any reason , attracted extensively the attention of mankind , has been written in a great variety of ways by a multitude of authors , and persons sometimes wonder why we should have so many different accounts of the same thing", "context_len": 53}}}, "newer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "And a beautiful day of it they made ; and early in the afternoon Marie-Celeste made something beautiful besides , quite on her own account -- nothing else than the discovery which gives its name to this chapter , and which happened to be a beautiful discovery , because it was the means of making somebody take new heart and see things in general in a newer and truer light", "context_len": 70}}}, "styles": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "Swan , after whose report this passage is quoted ( Schoolcraft V , 279 ) states that the title of the chief of the confederacy was the great beloved man , while Milfort , who was himself invested with the charge of great warrior of the nation , styles him `` Le Tastan\u00e9gy ou grand chef de guerre , '' adding , however , that in his time he was the highest authority in civil and military affairs ( M\u00e9moire , Note to p", "context_len": 84}}}, "buckling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 90, "context": "Her books , which were much read and appreciated in the youth of the present middle-aged generation , may to some extent have sunk into the background , as the works of successive story-tellers do in the majority of cases retire , each in turn , before newer names and newer styles ; but the splendid example set by Charlotte Tucker , at a time of life when most people are intent upon retiring from work , and taking if they may their ease , -- an example of then buckling on her armour afresh , and of entering upon the toughest toil of all her busy life , will surely never be forgotten", "context_len": 114}}}, "precede": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "Yet surely no records of the past , extant , tell us of such dark threatening clouds as hang over the realm of England at this time ; when the thousandth year since our blessed Lord 's nativity having passed , we seem to be entering on those awful plagues which the Apocalypse tells us must precede the consummation of all things", "context_len": 62}}}, "corruption": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "His mother , a Miss Bruere before marriage , -- probably the name was a corruption of Bruyere , -- was daughter of the then governor of the Bermudas , a gallant old soldier , possessing fourteen children and also a particularly irascible temper", "context_len": 44}}}, "illiberal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Tucker appears to have been a man of gentle temperament and liberal views ; I do not mean Liberal \u2019 in the mere party sense , but liberal as opposed to illiberal", "context_len": 32}}}, "petrify": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "\u2019 Whatever his own opinions may have been , he did not endeavour to force them upon his children ; he did not , in fact , petrify the children \u2019 s little fancies by opposition into a lasting existence", "context_len": 40}}}, "republican": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "But when he returned home on flying visits , he found to his dismay that his uncle 's visionary hopes were growing instead of fading ; and from desiring a merely republican England , General Harrison had begun to dream of a theocracy as complete as that of the early Jews , and to look forward to the immediate inauguration of an earthly Reign of the Saints , under the sceptre of Christ Himself , as the Fifth and last of the great monarchies of the world", "context_len": 87}}}, "revolution": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Although the transports were still a considerable distance from the U-boat danger zone , every revolution of their propellers was bringing them nearer to that part of the South Atlantic where vessels had been known-to have been destroyed by mines", "context_len": 40}}}, "constitution": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 21, "context": "Of course it was considered extremely important that the child destined to be a general should become robust and powerful in constitution from his earliest years , and that he should be inured to hardship and fatigue", "context_len": 37}}}, "ostlers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "George , speaking of this in later years , continues : But habit is strong ; and even now , when I repair to the stables for my horse , I interchange bows with the coachman and the ostlers and all the little idle urchins whom I encounter in the mews", "context_len": 51}}}, "urchins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "George , speaking of this in later years , continues : But habit is strong ; and even now , when I repair to the stables for my horse , I interchange bows with the coachman and the ostlers and all the little idle urchins whom I encounter in the mews", "context_len": 51}}}, "mews": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "George , speaking of this in later years , continues : But habit is strong ; and even now , when I repair to the stables for my horse , I interchange bows with the coachman and the ostlers and all the little idle urchins whom I encounter in the mews", "context_len": 51}}}, "notably": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "of asker , an army ) , but asker is constantly used in post-classical Arabic ( and notably in the Nights ) for `` a single soldier , '' and still more generally the plural ( asakir ) , as here , for `` soldiers", "context_len": 45}}}, "superficial": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The boy worked hard , and rose in his classes quickly ; though at an after period he spoke of his own learning in those days as superficial", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was not with her a superficial reserve , an acted reticence , such as may sometimes be seen in essentially shallow women", "context_len": 23}}}, "relieving": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "What schoolboy of fourteen would not like to visit India \u2019 ? Young Henry seized upon the idea ; and the said aunt , under the impression that she was kindly relieving his father of needless school expenses , actually shipped the lad off as middy in a merchant vessel bound for India , not waiting to write and ask his father \u2019 s permission", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "When the twenty South Africans had left the stokehold the relieving gang was set to work", "context_len": 16}}}, "middy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "What schoolboy of fourteen would not like to visit India \u2019 ? Young Henry seized upon the idea ; and the said aunt , under the impression that she was kindly relieving his father of needless school expenses , actually shipped the lad off as middy in a merchant vessel bound for India , not waiting to write and ask his father \u2019 s permission", "context_len": 65}}}, "complication": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Any one writing from India to England could not look for a reply in less than a year ; and his father was at Bermuda , not even at home , which made a further complication", "context_len": 36}}}, "junk": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "As the work neared completion , the four men erected a camouflage of wreckage above their plane , supporting the junk on a framework of poles", "context_len": 26}}}, "pluckily": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "But there was stuff \u2019 in him , and hardships of all kinds were most pluckily endured", "context_len": 17}}}, "persistency": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "From the first he grappled with the Native languages , showing a vigour and persistency in the study which , many many years later , were visible again in his daughter Charlotte , when grappling with the very same task", "context_len": 40}}}, "untiring": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "I do not remember her ever talking to me on religious subjects ; but her untiring energy and gentle patience made much impression on me", "context_len": 25}}}, "testimonials": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "But he had had heavy work and many troubles , and his health began to fail ; so the following year , after a quarter of a century of unbroken exile , he set off for England , carrying with him Government testimonials , couched in the warmest terms", "context_len": 49}}}, "meritorious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "These testimonials spoke of his long and meritorious services , \u2019 of his peculiar abilities , \u2019 of his talents and acquirements of the highest order , \u2019 of his unwearied diligence , \u2019 of his unimpeached integrity", "context_len": 38}}}, "unwearied": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "They can not be dissociated in the memory of the generations of young people , whose privilege it was to be entertained and gratified by their unwearied attention throughout many a long holiday afternoon and evening , while stuffed by Mrs", "context_len": 41}}}, "diligence": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "It was a daring prospect to imagine that he , a mere boy , would be allowed to succeed to a grown man 's position and salary -- and yet Bart had placed himself in line for it with every prompting of diligence and duty", "context_len": 45}}}, "biographer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "As his biographer says , he had been always so full of life , there had been so much activity of body , so much energy of mind , so much elasticity of spirit , that they had never associated with all this vitality a thought of the stillness of death", "context_len": 51}}}, "preaching": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 38, "context": "I really hope that it may do good for it to be known through Batala that , in a manly game , the Hindus and Muhammadans can not hold a candle to the Christian boys , who go preaching and singing hymns on Sunday ! Piety is all the more attractive from union with manliness", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "No one can deny that some of the said volumes do contain a large amount of direct preaching \u2019 ; not merely of life-lessons , interwoven with the story in such wise that the one could not be read and the other missed , but rather of little sermons so alternating with the story that a child might read the latter and skip the former", "context_len": 65}}}, "decisively": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": "Apparently the tide had turned most decisively , and it was not strange that some of the easily discouraged Oakdalers felt that they were surely beaten", "context_len": 26}}}, "recalls": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "Mention has several times been made of Miss Tucker \u2019 s readiness to give ; and when one recalls the abounding generosity of her father , not to speak of the story of her grandmother on the Boswell side giving away to a beggar the last coin in the house , one can hardly be surprised at the generous tendencies of Charlotte Tucker \u2019 s character", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "While one friend remembers vividly her spirit of ardent devotion , and another recalls especially her work among the poor , a third pictures her sparkling conversation , a fourth her spirited games of play with children", "context_len": 37}}}, "babyhood": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "For -- yes , it is true -- King Roy was only a king in right of his babyhood , being no higher up in this world 's social scale than a carpenter 's son", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "From very babyhood she was earnestly religious , and of a peculiarly serious temperament ; though at the same time energetic and sometimes even lively", "context_len": 25}}}, "exerted": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "Thereafter his influence was steadfastly exerted in the direction of a wise and righteous government of the dark millions of Hindustan ; the land in which he had spent a quarter of a century of his life , and to which afterward not only all his five sons went , but one of his five daughters also , in the advanced years of her life", "context_len": 65}}}, "indomitable": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 19, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}}}, "unwearying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}}}, "convictions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 73, "context": "Tucker was , as may have been already gathered , a man of unusual force of character and of indomitable will ; robust in body and mind ; unwearying in work ; self-reliant , yet never presumptuous ; an absolute gentleman , remarkable for the polished courtesy of his bearing , alike to superiors , equals , and inferiors in social position ; open and straightforward as daylight ; firm in his own convictions , but well able to look on both sides of a question , and liberal towards those who differed from him ; entirely fearless in doing what he held to be right , and entirely free from all thought of self-seeking", "context_len": 115}}}, "observes": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "The snow appears to have soon vanished , as in one letter , written in September , she observes : The mountains are quite high and bold enough for beauty , though to my comfort there is not a soup\u00e7on of snow upon any of them", "context_len": 46}}}, "biography": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "In a letter written long after his death by his daughter Charlotte , she remarked , when speaking of the biography of some well-known man : There is nothing to indicate that he ever said , as our beloved Father said , The publican \u2019 s prayer is the prayer of us all ! \u2019 Probably religious speech never came easily to him", "context_len": 63}}}, "publican": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "In a letter written long after his death by his daughter Charlotte , she remarked , when speaking of the biography of some well-known man : There is nothing to indicate that he ever said , as our beloved Father said , The publican \u2019 s prayer is the prayer of us all ! \u2019 Probably religious speech never came easily to him", "context_len": 63}}}, "abounding": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "One of his main characteristics was an abounding generosity", "context_len": 9}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "Mention has several times been made of Miss Tucker \u2019 s readiness to give ; and when one recalls the abounding generosity of her father , not to speak of the story of her grandmother on the Boswell side giving away to a beggar the last coin in the house , one can hardly be surprised at the generous tendencies of Charlotte Tucker \u2019 s character", "context_len": 66}}}, "evenness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He was an intensely loving and lovable man ; his wonderful sweetness and evenness of temper , never disturbed by heavy work or pressing cares , endearing him to all with whom he came in contact", "context_len": 36}}}, "playfulness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "The union in him of strength with gentleness , of a masterful intellect with a spirit of yielding courtesy , of nobility with playfulness , of generosity with self-restraint , of real religious conviction and experience with frolicsome gaiety , made a combination not more rare than beautiful", "context_len": 48}}}, "frolicsome": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "The union in him of strength with gentleness , of a masterful intellect with a spirit of yielding courtesy , of nobility with playfulness , of generosity with self-restraint , of real religious conviction and experience with frolicsome gaiety , made a combination not more rare than beautiful", "context_len": 48}}}, "enigmas": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "In the year 1835 he published a volume of plays and enigmas , called The Tragedies of Harold and Camoens , dedicated to the Duke of Wellington , for whom he and his family had the deepest esteem and admiration", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "Then every one had a choice of a hymn to be practised for the evening services of the week ; a short verse of the Bible was repeated ; and Sunday enigmas from the Bible were solved", "context_len": 37}}}, "spontaneously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "We should like to know how the childish intellect began to develop ; what first turned her thoughts into the writing line \u2019 ; whether authorship came to her spontaneously or no", "context_len": 32}}}, "alludes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "The passage first alludes to the Seminoles as allies , and then continues : `` They are a mixture of the remains of the Cawittas , Talepoosas , Coosas , Apalachias , Conshacs or Coosades , Oakmulgis , Oconis , Okchoys , Alibamons , Natchez , Weetumkus , Pakanas , Ta\u00ebnsas , Chacsihoomas , Ab\u00e9kas and some other tribes whose names I do not recollect", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "240 , alludes to a tradition purporting that the Chicasa had formerly assisted the Cheroki in driving the Shawanese from the Cumberland river ; the Cheroki desired war , and attacked the Chicasa shortly before 1769 , but were utterly defeated by them at the `` Chicasa Old Fields , '' and retreated by way of Cumberland river and the Cany Fork", "context_len": 62}}}, "bond": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Quoth the Sultan , `` On what wise shall we rid ourselves of this man , seeing I have given him my word and a King 's word is his bond ? '' `` O my lord , '' answered the Vizier , `` my counsel is that thou require of him forty dishes of pure virgin gold , full of jewels , such as she [ 426 ] brought thee the other day , [ 427 ] and forty slave-girls to bear the dishes and forty black slaves", "context_len": 90}}}, "unclouded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Shagbark formed a daring plan as he thus lay in the grass , with his eyes upon the couple , who sometimes faded from sight and then stood out in relief , when the light of the moon was unclouded", "context_len": 40}}}, "griefs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "The solemn pealing of the bell for the mass at the hour of daybreak fell upon Alfgar 's ears , and he turned almost mechanically to the cathedral , yet with vague desire to communicate all his griefs and troubles to a higher power than that of man , and to seek aid from a diviner source", "context_len": 57}}}, "hymn": {"freq": 38, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 38, "word_index": 64, "context": "But why , love , should we fear the act of departing ? How many , many , pass Jordan , as it were , dry-shod ? Remember how peacefully sweet Fanny sank to rest , -- dearest Mother , -- how my Letitia \u2019 s face was lighted up with a smile , -- how our Bible-woman at Batala sang aloud a happy hymn within a few hours of her going ! To me it seems such a simple thing for the -- I had almost said imprisoned soul , to leave its cottage of clay , -- for the bird , as soon as fledged , to spread its wings ! We are winged creatures , and it seems a humiliation to be creeping on earth", "context_len": 128}}}, "extract": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 14, "context": "Milfort 's `` History of the Moskoquis , '' as given above in an extract , is a singular mixture of recent fabrications and distortions of real historic events , with some points traceable to genuine aboriginal folklore", "context_len": 38}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "From these primaries they extract four fresh figures by confronting each point with the corresponding point in the next figure , and counting for each pair a single or double point , according to one of two rules , i", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "237 sqq ; extract in C", "context_len": 6}}}, "combines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "How we used to share each other \u2019 s thoughts from youth , as we shared the same room ! Our honoured Father loved to hear his Laura \u2019 s merry ringing laugh ; when we chatted together he would say to her favourite sister , \u2019 -- meaning herself -- She combines so much", "context_len": 55}}}, "imperfection": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "I doubt that he saw any imperfection in a being so bright , so sweet", "context_len": 15}}}, "underrate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 54, "context": "It is the custom among all nations to give kings the glory of all that is effected by their generals and officers ; and the writers of those days would , of course , in narrating the exploits of the Macedonian army , exaggerate the share which Alexander had in their performances , and underrate those of Parmenio", "context_len": 58}}}, "qualifications": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 78, "context": "Tucker could not quite keep pace with the new-fashioned unconventionalities of young-lady work in London ; and one of the object-sermons , which most impressed me in my College days , was the beautiful self-restraint which these two sisters -- no longer young -- imposed upon themselves , in deference to their aged Mother \u2019 s wishes , in regard to that outside work which inclination , or one might say conviction , as well as opportunity and qualifications , impelled them to participate in", "context_len": 85}}}, "playful": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 53, "context": "Frances , in her queenly apparel , presented him with a bag which contained a Commission to defend England , -- a business which , one is disposed to think , he had already pretty well accomplished ! The Duke received this offering graciously ; and a day or two later the following playful letter arrived from him to Mr", "context_len": 60}}}, "indorsed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "Richmond the trader owed me a note for five hundred dollars , indorsed by Job Stanton , and as he did not pay it , I had to call on Stanton", "context_len": 31}}}, "versifying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "\u2019 It is a valentine written to her sister ; and it shows that at the early age of eleven she had at least begun a little versifying ; usually the line first adopted by incipient authors", "context_len": 37}}}, "incipient": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "\u2019 It is a valentine written to her sister ; and it shows that at the early age of eleven she had at least begun a little versifying ; usually the line first adopted by incipient authors", "context_len": 37}}}, "authors": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 41, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "emblems": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The snow-drops sweet that grace the plain Are emblems , love , of you , With innocence and beauty blest Pure as the morning dew", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "From the roofs were dangling on the inside heterogeneous emblems of peace and trophies of war , as eagles ' feathers , swans ' wings , wooden scalping knives , war clubs , red-painted wands , bunches of hoops on which to dry their scalps , bundles of a war-physic called snake-root ( s\u00ednika in Cheroki ) , baskets , etc", "context_len": 61}}}, "hover": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Sweet rosebud , free from every storm Of life , may peace incline To hover ever round thy bed , My dearest Valentine", "context_len": 23}}}, "undated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Another early effort , undated , but possibly a year or two later , is addressed , To Dolly , the sweet little bud of the morn , \u2019 -- no doubt to the same favourite sister , Dorothea Laura", "context_len": 40}}}, "versification": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "One more apparently early effort remains ; not this time versification , but a birthday letter to Laura , inscribed , To my dear Lady Emma , from her affectionate Tosti", "context_len": 31}}}, "effusions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "When everyone had finished the slips were folded up and placed in a basket , and Lilian , who had been chosen to read the effusions , shut her eyes and drew one out at a venture", "context_len": 37}}}, "unformed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "One is large and unformed ; another is small and cramped ; the third is neat and of a copperplate description", "context_len": 21}}}, "copperplate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "One is large and unformed ; another is small and cramped ; the third is neat and of a copperplate description", "context_len": 21}}}, "crystallized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It may be that her writing was long before it crystallized into any definite shape ; often the case with many-sided people", "context_len": 22}}}, "production": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "Bobby is a fine , tall young fellow , fresh from an agricultural college , where he has learnt so much in the way of scientific farming that he intends to make the fields yield the very highest pitch of production", "context_len": 41}}}, "pedantic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The style is almost as pedantic for the age of sixteen or seventeen as for the age of ten or twelve", "context_len": 21}}}, "chatty": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "On the surface she was free , frank , chatty , quick in response , ready to converse , full of liveliness , fun , and repartee", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Furlong also came out of their shells , and were most agreeable and kind and chatty ; and mother came down to dinner as usual , and sat in the drawing-room as usual , and in the evenings there was music , and I sang my songs and played my pieces and wore my very prettiest dresses , and Albert Fanning looked at me , and looked at me , and Mrs", "context_len": 72}}}, "repartee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "On the surface she was free , frank , chatty , quick in response , ready to converse , full of liveliness , fun , and repartee", "context_len": 27}}}, "exclusively": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "It will also be remembered that several of the larger communities of American Indians are known to the white population exclusively through names borrowed from other languages than their own , as , for instance , the Kalap\u00faya of Oregon , who call themselves Am\u0113 nmei , Kalap\u00faya ( anciently Kalap\u00fayua ) being of Chinook origin , and the Pani , whose name is , according to J", "context_len": 68}}}, "superintend": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 52, "context": "Pigs , poultry , and young animals had to be fed , and the eggs gathered in daily ; the dairy claimed constant attention , for the pans must be scalded and the pails polished bright , and though Joe did the milking and churning , it was Lilian 's business to superintend the making up of the butter , and to weigh each pound with her own hands and print it with the Abbey stamp", "context_len": 76}}}, "famously": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Capital , we are coming on famously , and shall soon be good friends at this rate , '' said the colonel as he stooped and kissed the rosy little mouth which Bessie held up to him", "context_len": 38}}}, "acceptable": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 65, "context": "The captain and master could not , however , see the matter in this light , and besought Sir Richard to have a care of them , declaring that the Spaniards would be ready to treat with them ; and that , as there were a number of gallant men yet living whose wounds were not mortal , they might do their country and prince acceptable service hereafter", "context_len": 68}}}, "recurring": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "In Charlotte \u2019 s earlier years anxiety as to money matters was often experienced ; and recurring Christmastides saw a repeated difficulty in making both ends meet", "context_len": 27}}}, "likenesses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "She had a marked talent for drawing , and could take likenesses of her friends ; good as regarded the salient features , though apt to grow into more of caricatures than the young artist intended", "context_len": 36}}}, "salient": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "She had a marked talent for drawing , and could take likenesses of her friends ; good as regarded the salient features , though apt to grow into more of caricatures than the young artist intended", "context_len": 36}}}, "caricatures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "She had a marked talent for drawing , and could take likenesses of her friends ; good as regarded the salient features , though apt to grow into more of caricatures than the young artist intended", "context_len": 36}}}, "inclusive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "Though her voice was never really very good , she sang much ; and while well able to take a second at sight , she was in after years equally ready to undertake any other part in a glee , inclusive of the bass , which often fell to her share when a man \u2019 s voice happened to be lacking", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Cha'hta distinguishes between the inclusive and exclusive pronouns we , our , but Creek and Hitchiti do not", "context_len": 18}}}, "jungle": {"freq": 363, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 360, "word_index": 81, "context": "How came that picture in the dwelling of Japazy , the half-breed ? Why had a similar picture been in the hut of Sobrinini ? What mysterious link was there between the lovely original of that picture and Sobrinini , the witch woman , Japazy , the master of Jaguar Island , Jojasta , the medicine man of the Moving Mountain , and Casson , his friend and former protector ? Somewhere , some time , those residents of the Amazonian jungle had known the mysterious Bartow , his wife , Laura , and perhaps the little child named Bonny", "context_len": 100}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "Then there were the trogons , motmots and kingfishers glowing with iridescent hues , flocks of scarlet macaws , flamingoes almost equally gorgeous , each standing on one long slender leg and basking in the sun ; herons , plover , toucans and scores of other curious birds that make the Amazon jungle the most wonderful natural aviary on earth", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Still faster he went , Polulu padding beside him and keeping away by his presence not only jungle enemies but Bomba \u2019 s friends , the monkeys and the parrots , who gave him a wide berth when they saw the grim guardian that kept pace with him", "context_len": 48}}}, "springy": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "She had still as of old a peculiarly elastic and springy mode of walking ; and while possessing no pretensions to actual good looks , there was much charm of manner , together with great animation", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The impression left by her dignity \u2019 is strong , singularly so , when considered side by side with a step so springy that some describe it as even jerky", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "He drank in the clear , fresh air with his nostrils , and beneath his feet the springy turf was delightfully soft and yet pleasantly firm", "context_len": 26}}}, "indefatigable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Under the zealous and indefatigable tuition of this young damsel I made astonishingly rapid progress as a student of the language spoken by those around me , and was soon able to converse in it with a very fair amount of freedom", "context_len": 42}}}, "finances": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "At one period , heavy and long-continued work in clearing up the finances \u2019 of the East India Company kept him much apart from the family circle ; and the delight was great when he could leave his big dry books , and be as a boy among the children again", "context_len": 51}}}, "adjectives": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 11, "context": "There is no real substantive verb in the language , and adjectives , when becoming verbified , are turned into attributive verbs , as in Creek : w\u00e1nti strong , hard ; tsaw\u00e1ntus I am strong ; w\u00e1ntus he , it is strong , hard ; w\u00e1ntatik not strong ; w\u00e1ntigus he is very strong ; w\u00e1ntatis he is not strong ; wanta'hl\u00e1tis he is not strong at all", "context_len": 69}}}, "madcap": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "With all her liveliness , however , she was in no sense a madcap , being thoroughly a lady", "context_len": 19}}}, "remembers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "One friend remembers hearing her tell a story of her young days , bearing upon this question of personal appearance", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "While one friend remembers vividly her spirit of ardent devotion , and another recalls especially her work among the poor , a third pictures her sparkling conversation , a fourth her spirited games of play with children", "context_len": 37}}}, "unhesitatingly": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "Yes , she was quite dead , this gentle , faithful , savage girl who , in return for a comparatively slight service , had unhesitatingly abandoned home , kindred , everything , to save me from a cruel and lingering death ; and now the only thing that I could do to show my gratitude was to make sure that no further violence should be offered to her remains", "context_len": 70}}}, "query": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "An artist of repute , who saw her in the later days of her Indian career , has said unhesitatingly , in reply to a query on this subject , -- Plain ! No ! A face with such a look of intellect as Miss Tucker \u2019 s could never be plain", "context_len": 52}}}, "actress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "In addition to her other gifts , Charlotte had something at least of dramatic power , and in her own home-circle she was a spirited actress", "context_len": 26}}}, "camoens": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Camoens is after all set at liberty , none the worse for his imprisonment ; but the distracted Theodora , meeting her other lover and her companion in evil-doing , Ferdinand , attacks him vehemently , with these words --", "context_len": 40}}}, "decides": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "All this is making very free with State belongings , and one of the guides , in charge of a small party of visitors , starts toward him in a decidedly menacing manner ; but Harold intercepts him and explains , and the guide , himself much amused , decides to leave unmolested this gallant little tar of Her Majesty 's", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "A young detective , who , in his private capacity , has solved several mysteries , decides to open an office in another city", "context_len": 24}}}, "theodora": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Theodora in her wrath is helped by another lover , Ferdinand , to carry out her plot , and together they bring a false charge against Ferdinand , who is speedily landed in the dungeons of the Inquisition", "context_len": 38}}}, "repenting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "It was a strange position , and Harry Paul , as he bent down holding Penelly there , recalled all he had heard , and , in spite of his manly feelings , he could not help believing that in a sudden fit of dislike , or under a momentary temptation , Penelly had thrown the nets over him , though evidently repenting the next moment of what he had done", "context_len": 71}}}, "stabs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "( She stabs him", "context_len": 4}}}, "redeemed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "But with that tender conscience , and her simple trust in Him who has redeemed her , I believe all the strength she needs will be granted", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Christ is the All-attractive ; and in the degree that His redeemed ones reflect His Image , it seems to me that they unconsciously attract", "context_len": 25}}}, "stab": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 48, "context": "She had been tired out with the railroad journey , entertaining as it was , then the excitement of meeting them all again , the bump on her forehead when she had come down so hard on Pansy 's head , and the screams that seemed like a stab going from temple to temple tired her inexpressibly", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "The husband , a Muhammadan , would sometimes sit between the two , sharpening a knife , and threatening to stab them", "context_len": 22}}}, "poetic": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "It is very strikingly characteristic of the mingling of poetic sentiment and enthusiasm with calm and calculating business efficiency , which shone conspicuously so often in Alexander 's career , that when he arrived at Sestos , and found that the ships were there , and the army safe , and that there was no enemy to oppose his landing on the Asiatic shore , he left Parmenio to conduct the transportation of the troops across the water , while he himself went away in a single galley on an excursion of sentiment and romantic adventure", "context_len": 96}}}, "thraldom": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The Duke of Wellington himself was present ; prominent still in the minds of men as the Deliverer of Europe , only twenty years earlier , from a tyrant \u2019 s thraldom", "context_len": 32}}}, "abstracted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Laura , still a mere child , in her enthusiasm slipped close up behind , when the Duke was ascending the stairs , and gently abstracted a fallen hair from the shoulder of the hero , which hair she preserved ever after among her choicest treasures ; and Charlotte was no whit behind Laura in this devotion", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The frosty blue eyes of the girl , observing his abstracted manner , led her to infer that Charley , so far from being a help , would prove a source of danger", "context_len": 33}}}, "prettiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The prettiness of her dress on that particular occasion was no doubt accentuated by the fact that in general Charlotte did not attire herself becomingly ; and also by the fact of another young lady being present as a second star of the morning", "context_len": 44}}}, "frances": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Frances , in her queenly apparel , presented him with a bag which contained a Commission to defend England , -- a business which , one is disposed to think , he had already pretty well accomplished ! The Duke received this offering graciously ; and a day or two later the following playful letter arrived from him to Mr", "context_len": 60}}}, "satire": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Just and true as this view of the subject was , there was too much of rebuke and satire in it to have much influence with those to whom it was addressed", "context_len": 32}}}, "markedly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "This side of her appeared more distinctly , and developed more markedly , in India , than in her secluded English home", "context_len": 22}}}, "stratum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Underlying her high spirits was a stratum of deep thought ; and strong principle seems almost from the beginning to have held control over her life", "context_len": 26}}}, "innate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The innate reticence , which veiled so much of herself from others , would naturally in early years extend itself to matters of religion", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "The rigid simplicity which she cultivated was , no doubt , partly a matter of pure economy , that she might have the more to give away , -- partly a matter of her innate generosity ; but partly also it arose from a deep-rooted desire to remove the reproach , which has of late been often levelled at the ease and luxury , real or supposed , of many Missionaries in India or elsewhere", "context_len": 75}}}, "brightens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Though I have often often done wrong since , and shed many many tears , I have never quite lost the light shed on me then , and now it brightens all the future , so that I can scarcely say that I have any care as regards myself -- the Lord will take care of me in advancing age -- in the last sickness -- in what is called death , ( it is only its shadow )", "context_len": 79}}}, "heralded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "It is rather improbable that a declaration of war always preceded the attack , for the advance into the hostile territory was made clandestinely [ 103 ] ; but the resolution of starting upon the warpath was heralded in the towns with great ceremonies", "context_len": 44}}}, "imbibe": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Brought up as Charlotte was in an atmosphere of kindness , of gentleness , of unselfish thought for others , of generosity , of high principle , and of most real religion , albeit not much talked about , she would naturally imbibe the latter almost unconsciously , and as naturally would say little", "context_len": 54}}}, "reverent": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "Now he was standing in admiration before the great oak chair of State beneath the organ gallery , and now nothing loath he mounts the steps that lead to it and runs a finger along the curves of its elaborate carving , and then , with a most reverent air , touches the embroidered cross and garter with which it is decorated", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "Oh , there is a great deal to do , dear child , for some have to be taught the way to the palace , and some have to be brought into audience with the King , ' her voice hushed itself into a reverent whisper", "context_len": 46}}}, "contributor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "A small magazine or serial in manuscript , for family use , was early started among the brothers and sisters , and to this , as might be expected , Charlotte was a frequent contributor", "context_len": 35}}}, "extant": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "No real substantive verb being extant , its want is supplied by \u00f3m\u00e4s , m\u00f3m\u00e4s , t\u00f3y\u00e4s I am so , I am such ; these are conjugated regularly , and when connected with the verbals in -t ( -at , -it , -ut ) of any verb , compose a periphrastic conjugation which displays itself in an almost infinite number of forms", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Yet surely no records of the past , extant , tell us of such dark threatening clouds as hang over the realm of England at this time ; when the thousandth year since our blessed Lord 's nativity having passed , we seem to be entering on those awful plagues which the Apocalypse tells us must precede the consummation of all things", "context_len": 62}}}, "adept": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Charlotte was an adept at playing dance-music for her nephews and nieces ; and at Binfield she also danced a great deal with her brother and the children", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But the Indians , too , were swift and adept in getting through the jungle", "context_len": 15}}}, "anent": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Bi ' l ghewali may be a clerical error for bi ' l ghalibi , `` for the most part , in general , '' in which case we may read , `` Folk in general abound [ in talk ] anent her virtues ; '' or bi ' l ghewali may perhaps be used in the sense ( of which use , however , I know no instance ) of 'in excessive estimation , ' in which latter case the passage might be rendered , `` Folk abound in setting a high value on ( or extolling ) her virtues", "context_len": 101}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "When [ 413 ] the Vizier heard his son 's words , he grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we", "context_len": 128}}}, "unpublished": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "The earliest in date of these unpublished plays , composed for the entertainment of the home-circle , appears to have been The Iron Mask ; achieved in 1839 , when Charlotte was about eighteen years old", "context_len": 36}}}, "fondest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "It was Dedicated , with the fondest esteem and affection , to her beloved Father , Henry St", "context_len": 18}}}, "transparent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The glorious Sun hath reached the farthest west , And clouds transparent tipt with living fire Hang o \u2019 er his glory , bright \u2019 ning to the close", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "In the bomber \u2019 s transparent nose , Chick Enders gazed at the scene , open-mouthed", "context_len": 16}}}, "pensive": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "Her fair hair , untouched by artificial adornment , hung like a shower of gold around her shoulders , while her eyes were of that delicate blue which seemed to reflect the deep summer sky ; but the sweet pensive expression of her face was that which attracted nearly all who knew her , and made her the object of general regard", "context_len": 62}}}, "matins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 76, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}}}, "illumed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 92, "context": "I could , like yon broad setting Sun , For one day tread the path of Liberty , For one day shine a blessing to my Country , Then , like him , set in glory ! Still come they not ? -- then Chateaurouge deceived me ! He said e \u2019 er sunset that they must be here , And I have watched from the first blush of morn , Before the lark his cheerful matins sung , Before the glorious traveller of the skies Had with one ray of gold illumed the east , And still they come not ! -- \u2019 Tis in vain to watch , They will not come to-night ! -- my sinking heart For one day more must sicken in suspense", "context_len": 128}}}, "tokens": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 39, "context": "Charlie was a pattern scholar , a boy after Seabrooke 's own heart , because of his sincere efforts to do right ; and hence he had found favor in his eyes , and he had shown many little tokens of partiality toward the child which had won for him the younger boy 's gratitude and affection", "context_len": 57}}}, "witty": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "\u2019 This witty and amusing little farce shall be given entire in the next chapter , as a fair example of what she was able to accomplish at the age of twenty-one", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "You do not know what bitter feelings I had in my heart , for they really were undeniably some of the most attractive children you could possibly find ; and Florence was so witty , and Janet so delicate and refined and sweet in all her ways ! I could not be angry with them , but I did think it fearfully unfair that so many blessings should be poured on their heads and so few given to me , for I was not even specially clever", "context_len": 87}}}, "farce": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "The perpetual play upon words , and the almost rollicking fun and nonsense of the whole , remind one of her earlier effort , The Pretender , already given at length ; though the later-written farce is in some respects scarcely equal to the girlish achievement", "context_len": 46}}}, "extracts": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 9, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}}}, "dacoit": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "On a January morning , in early dawn , they reached the village in which the dacoit leader , Khansah , was supposed to be concealed ; and after many inquiries they induced an alarmed little native boy to point out silently which hut sheltered Khansah", "context_len": 46}}}, "blunderbuss": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "In the darkness of the entry to one of the huts stood Khansah , holding a loaded blunderbuss", "context_len": 18}}}, "roofless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "He climbed quickly over the low walls of a roofless hut , then turned about , and with his left hand steadying the right hand on the top of the outer wall , he fired his pistol at the dacoit , -- and missed him", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Oftener she worked in her roofless pottery by the ditch , where many a queer jug and vase and bowl , gaudy with ochre and Indian red , came into being and passed early to dust again , for want of firing", "context_len": 42}}}, "rupees": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "His 100 would be enough for all his personal wants , unless he travelled much ; and he might keep a little horse , unless , like -- -- , he preferred spending his extra rupees on something else", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "But when he heard of some more places being gone , he suddenly returned to the treasury , to which his position gave him access , dismissed the fifty Sepoys with a thousand rupees apiece , and then attacked Mr", "context_len": 40}}}, "durance": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "His own relatives responded promptly to this appeal , and Khansah speedily found himself in durance vile", "context_len": 17}}}, "convicted": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "Again Driggs denied everything , and he had covered his tracks so well that it was impossible to find him guilty ; but my father was convicted and sentenced to a long term in prison", "context_len": 35}}}, "preservation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "All who know aught of India know the name of The Panjab \u2019 ; [ 28 ] that province to the far north , a land of five great rivers , where in Mutiny days so much was done for the preservation of our Indian Empire", "context_len": 46}}}, "heathen": {"freq": 74, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 65, "word_index": 52, "context": "But if our Church is to go on growing , as we hope and pray that it may , what would be the advantage of having a tiny chapel , which would not comfortably accommodate ourselves in a fiery climate , and in which there would be no room at all for heathen spectators ? We should be wanting a second ; and how could we procure a second clergyman ? Please thank dear Fred very , very much for his kindness in collecting , and assure him that we wish to make the money go as far as possible", "context_len": 100}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "Those who have seen how things verily are in heathen lands , those who have witnessed the awful and desperate cruelties which there prevail , know what the argument is worth as to the present life", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "As the married pair were about to drive off , rice was brought to be thrown ; but somebody present objected to the custom for India , as originally heathen , and liable to be misunderstood", "context_len": 36}}}, "enigma": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Tucker \u2019 s enigma , is very characteristic of one who through life was always peculiarly ready to give praise to others", "context_len": 22}}}, "sailer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "As for going to Gresford the 3rd of next month , I do not wish to be one of the party at all , at all ! I calculate that Robin will then have been on the waves 76 days ; and though I do not expect him till October , the S -- -- may be a fast sailer , and fast sailers have accomplished the whole voyage in about that time , I believe", "context_len": 76}}}, "melody": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 23, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}}}, "gout": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "I chatted with both the latter amusing gentlemen , and heard from Sir Henry a circumstantial account of his attack of gout , when , he said : I felt as though I could have roared like a bull", "context_len": 39}}}, "quadrille": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Sir Henry thinks that ladies should have a glass of champagne after every dance , quadrille , waltz , or polka ! You would see , said he , if my plan were followed , how many ladies would come", "context_len": 40}}}, "polka": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Sir Henry thinks that ladies should have a glass of champagne after every dance , quadrille , waltz , or polka ! You would see , said he , if my plan were followed , how many ladies would come", "context_len": 40}}}, "applications": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Beutel often receives applications for new village schools , and is so much interested in them that he and our Catechist have one between them", "context_len": 25}}}, "cadetships": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Papa has had applications for cadetships from Lord Jocelyn and H -- -- T -- --", "context_len": 16}}}, "tudor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Tudor is to take luncheon with us , so we have amusement provided for that meal also ; and what a business it will be in the evening ! Such a phalanx of ladies as dear Mother is to head", "context_len": 40}}}, "phalanx": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 15, "context": "If the enemy remained at a distance , and threw javelins or darts at the phalanx , they fell harmless , stopped by the shields which the soldiers wore upon the left arm , and which were held in such a manner as to form a system of scales , which covered and protected the whole mass , and made the men almost invulnerable", "context_len": 64}}}, "diplomatist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Sir Henry Pottinger was a famous diplomatist", "context_len": 7}}}, "acquaintanceship": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "While at Walmer they were a good deal thrown with the Duke of Wellington , and the former acquaintanceship ripened into more of intimacy", "context_len": 24}}}, "undergo": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 17, "context": "96 ) : `` Creeks make the boys swim in the coldest weather ; make them frequently undergo scratching from head to foot , through the skin , with broken glass or gar-fish teeth [ 120 ] , so as to make them all in a gore of blood , and then wash them with cold water ; this is with them the arcanum against all diseases ; but when they design it as a punishment to the boys , they dry-scratch them , i", "context_len": 85}}}, "expostulate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "But why should you expostulate , or why should you be distressed ? Is not Missionary work of all work the highest ? I only fear that I am presumptuous in coming forward ; but it seems as if my dear Lord were calling me to it ; and my heart says , -- Here am I ; send me", "context_len": 60}}}, "unwell": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I hope , however , that dearest Mamma is not unwell , and that the sea-air may do her good and strengthen her", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "' Miss Forster , one of their kindest friends , had been unwell and confined to her room nearly all the springtime , and the children had felt her a loss ; for besides the bond with Archie , she loved young people , and had always a word of help or sympathy for their schemes", "context_len": 56}}}, "rougher": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "She became better skilled in the making of pickles and preserves than ever she could have been in the masculine household at Hunstanton , where the old servants would have broken their hearts if their little mistress had ever set her dainty finger to anything rougher than gathering rose-leaves and lavender to scent the best parlour", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The eye , wandering over every point of the compass , caught no misty mountain range or peak , and the work of the patient oxen was play compared to what it would be when they should have entered the rougher regions farther toward the setting sun", "context_len": 47}}}, "judith": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Judith", "context_len": 1}}}, "charles": {"freq": 87, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 87, "word_index": 0, "context": "CHARLES", "context_len": 1}}}, "theatrical": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "Heigh ho ! how dark it is getting ! Just the sort of place to meet with a ghost of Hamlet , not the sort of hamlet that I \u2019 m looking after , for I have done with theatrical effects , -- I wish that I had done with the effects of cold", "context_len": 54}}}, "steeple": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "The huge cathedral church towered above the masses , rude in architecture , yet still impressive in its proportions , while another church , scarcely smaller in its dimensions , rose from the banks lower down the stream , below the bridge , and the wooden steeple of a third was visible above the roofs of the houses in the western part of the city", "context_len": 65}}}, "fasting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "And -- ey ! ey ! what on earth are those white things upon the grass ? Clothes put out to dry ; what an ass I was not to see that before ! but fasting makes one nervous", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "Nevertheless , you shall say to Sister Harrison all that is in your heart , and she shall act as the Lord shall direct her , and if she elect to go forth into desert places and await the consummation of our hopes afar off , in fasting and prayer , in that fashion also she may serve the Good Old Cause", "context_len": 62}}}, "namesake": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "What if I were to try my fortune at the door ? My poor namesake Prince Charlie must have put wanderers into fashion", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "I have to love her for so many yeasons ; 'cause she is her own self and I ca n't help it , and 'cause I 'm her namesake , and 'cause she 's my dear soldier 's own sister", "context_len": 40}}}, "imbibed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Brief as had been the pony \u2019 s service , he like his companions , had imbibed the fact that his one duty in life was to carry the mail pouches with the highest speed at his command , and that such service was to be performed under the guidance of the man who sat on his back", "context_len": 58}}}, "skeletons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "In a boat were discovered two skeletons ; and amongst other books a Bible , numerous passages in which were underlined , showing that these gallant men in their last hours had the comfort of God 's Word to support them when earthly hopes had passed away", "context_len": 47}}}, "bleached": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Except that he has bleached his hair , he has made no attempt to disguise himself", "context_len": 16}}}, "flogged": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "Mais n \u2019 importe ! the man , the man remains the same ! These locks have proved the keys to a Lady \u2019 s heart e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon", "context_len": 128}}}, "furbelow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "Mais n \u2019 importe ! the man , the man remains the same ! These locks have proved the keys to a Lady \u2019 s heart e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon", "context_len": 128}}}, "prompter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon express for one \u2019 s thoughts before one can express them at all ? Better have wit to cover ignorance , than silence to conceal sense", "context_len": 128}}}, "carrier": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 100, "context": "e \u2019 er now ; and then wit and eloquence ! When I was flogged at school for affirming that a furbelow must be an article , as I knew it to be an article of dress , my Master observed that all my brains lay at the root of my tongue ; and the best position for them too , say I ! Who would keep a prompter to bellow to one from the top of the Monument , and where \u2019 s the use of carrying one \u2019 s brains so high , that one must send a carrier pigeon express for one \u2019 s thoughts before one can express them at all ? Better have wit to cover ignorance , than silence to conceal sense", "context_len": 128}}}, "squint": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "That fellow has such a desperate squint , that I am sure he would make a capital Lear !", "context_len": 19}}}, "entreats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "[ coughs ] at home ? Pray present my compliments to her , and say that a gentleman who has lost his way entreats the favour of shelter for a night under her hospitable roof", "context_len": 35}}}, "highwayman": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Mosely had been taken in by his confident tone , and knowing that he was himself a sham desperado , though a genuine thief and highwayman , had been made to feel uneasy while in Bradley 's company", "context_len": 38}}}, "misgives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "How very cold it is ! I wish that the triangular-nosed fellow would make haste ; and yet my heart misgives me", "context_len": 22}}}, "passport": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "One was a passport signed by one of the biggest men in the country , authorizing Francis Leroy to pass in and out of the Union lines at any time , day or night , and the other -- there were but two -- was some useless information with respect to the movements of the Federal forces between Murfreesborough and Memphis", "context_len": 61}}}, "begs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Sir , my Mistress begs you to walk up", "context_len": 9}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` So I walks over to my granny 's at Marlow last night , and I begs a black pup off her , and here it is", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Your majesty begs the question when you call that coward 'faithful", "context_len": 12}}}, "sophia": {"freq": 39, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 39, "word_index": 0, "context": "SOPHIA", "context_len": 1}}}, "barbara": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 0, "context": "BARBARA", "context_len": 1}}}, "jud": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 0, "context": "JUD", "context_len": 1}}}, "derivations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The word is from the Latin , said her father , who , with most college men , liked to bring his knowledge of derivations to the front now and then , and the dictionary , I think , would tell you that it means God 's thoughtful care for everything created", "context_len": 52}}}, "prophetical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "One would suppose them sometimes prophetical of future events", "context_len": 9}}}, "resemblance": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 71, "context": "It was expected , of course , that faithful old Margaret , who was master in chief of affairs in the Little Castle , would , as usual , in the absence of the family , answer the bell , and the message intended for her was half way over Albert 's lips before he took in the fact that the individual who had opened the door bore about as close resemblance to Margaret as the tower of the Little Castle to its door-mat", "context_len": 84}}}, "denomination": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "] Who can deny that Hanover has a great resemblance to Hand-over , or that Cumberland is as just a denomination for the bloody Duke as if", "context_len": 27}}}, "decapitated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "His spirit seemed unconquered even by the blow which decapitated him , and he drove on", "context_len": 16}}}, "stun": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "They stun me", "context_len": 3}}}, "parrots": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 64, "context": "laid hands if it were but on a hen , if you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old Noll and padded the", "context_len": 128}}}, "pursuits": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 36, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}}}, "recreation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "O , they are my delight , my recreation ! Ornithology , Mythology , Geology , Conchology , fascinate me", "context_len": 20}}}, "fascinate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "O , they are my delight , my recreation ! Ornithology , Mythology , Geology , Conchology , fascinate me", "context_len": 20}}}, "sciences": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "But the characteristics of race and language , which are the most important for ethnology , because they are the most ancient in their origin , are not often alluded to by them , and when the modern sciences of anthropology and ethnology had been established on solid principles many of these southern races had already disappeared or intermingled , and scientific inquiry came too late for their investigation", "context_len": 69}}}, "jacobites": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Jacobites !", "context_len": 2}}}, "apricots": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Stone-fruit , a species of -- of -- apricots", "context_len": 9}}}, "dished": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "I guess we can throw it away now , and eat the real thing , '' and he emptied his plate , and those of his companions , of the dubious mess , and dished out some of the real plum pudding", "context_len": 42}}}, "indisposed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "I feel indisposed -- faint ! [ Exit", "context_len": 8}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The Lady Hilda was at the window of her bower , slightly indisposed ; she had not gone down to the priory , but sat inhaling the rich fragrance of the night as the gentle breeze wafted it from a thousand flowers", "context_len": 42}}}, "ratty": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The cows had no quieting effect on him , and when he saw us he pawed the air and almost wagged his ratty tail off", "context_len": 25}}}, "electricity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "What a flash of electricity has burst on my intellect !", "context_len": 11}}}, "adamant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Brain of adamant ! could not instinct direct you to the feet of your adored Prince ?", "context_len": 17}}}, "recollected": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 26, "context": "In questions of war unanimity was seldom attained in the council of a town , much less in the whole nation ; `` it is not recollected by the oldest man , that more than one-half of the nation went to war at the same time or 'took the war-talk", "context_len": 50}}}, "col": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 0, "context": "COL", "context_len": 1}}}, "non": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "What strange non", "context_len": 3}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "com": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I can not com -- pre -- hend -- I -- -- [ The girls half lead , half push her out", "context_len": 22}}}, "expire": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "My heart beats so , I shall expire", "context_len": 8}}}, "inconvenient": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "The worst of it was that he infected Nancy with his alarms to such an extent that she could scarcely be persuaded to put her nose out at the back-door after dusk ( except on her evening out , when her sweetheart came to fetch her ) , which was distinctly inconvenient if a pail of water were wanted during the evening , or she happened to have left the coal-box standing in the yard", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The spells ain \u2019 t bad , but they \u2019 re mighty inconvenient", "context_len": 13}}}, "caning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "' I may as well make up my mind to take a caning and spare myself the trouble", "context_len": 18}}}, "rites": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 29, "context": "I will make up the loss of the cattle , my brother the prior will have a mass said for poor Guthred , and he shall have the last rites performed at our expense ; it is all we can do for him ; the rest we must leave to the mercy of God", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "At the commencement of the twelfth lunation he performs for four days the same rites as he did at the beginning of the initiation , but on the fifth he leaves the cabin , gathers maize-cobs , burns them to ashes , and with these rubs his whole body", "context_len": 49}}}, "petrifaction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "My Country \u2019 s sake ! what good can it do my Country ? I know your motives , you scientific Monster ! you want to make a petrifaction of me", "context_len": 31}}}, "mire": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "There he sat motionless for a long time , trying to divine by what baleful freak of circumstances he had been brought to this wretched plight ; but , without knowledge of the facts to work upon , he found himself floundering helplessly and blindly in a mire of uncertainty", "context_len": 50}}}, "kneel": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Alas , alas ! and did I not kneel and entreat my dearest husband to heed the words of those good men if he would not mine ? How happy we might have been , even in a hut among the savages ! And you , too , Dick , '' she said tenderly , `` you would have liked well to follow Master Perrient 's leading ; and my dear husband was ready to have you go , seeing all he and Sir Gyles Perrient had set their minds upon for your happiness", "context_len": 95}}}, "jesting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Alden was so amazed that at first he suspected his dusky friend was jesting , but there could be no doubt of his earnestness", "context_len": 24}}}, "storeroom": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Search ! who \u2019 ll search ? The storeroom is the very place", "context_len": 13}}}, "campaigner": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "Going over the top they would unhesitatingly rush a machine-gun emplacement , but crawling away from the enemy , never knowing when a hail of bullets would sweep the ground , was enough to try the nerves of the case-hardened campaigner", "context_len": 41}}}, "riser": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "\u2019 [ 89 ] Always an early riser , Miss Tucker never liked her Ayah to find her still in bed", "context_len": 21}}}, "varieties": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Morning prayers in the chilly little church , an occasional airing in the shabby coach , with its moth-eaten cushions and patched harness , were the only varieties in Audrey 's life", "context_len": 32}}}, "rheumatics": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "But O Sir , how Miss Sophy trembled and turned as white as a lily , and Miss Ratty stamped and sent me to the village -- at that hour , your honour , company in the house -- the ground covered with frost -- I subject to the rheumatics -- and what for , d \u2019 ye think ? to get her twopenceworth of shoe-ribbon , your honour ; and when I brought it , would you believe it ? -- she roared out that it was too narrow and sent me back again", "context_len": 95}}}, "thumps": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then she \u2019 s an out and out Jacobite , and thumps the piano when she sings Charlie is my darling , \u2019 as though she took it for a Whig", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He pounded out the syllables under Bart 's very nose with resounding thumps , bringing down his fist on the impromptu office desk so forcibly that the concussion disturbed the papers on it , and several sheets fell fluttering to the floor", "context_len": 42}}}, "stitching": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "Miss Jones , the village dressmaker , was installed in the Rose Parlour , and appeared to be stitching morning , noon , and night ; the postman 's bag was quite heavy with patterns and catalogues of Indian outfits ; while distant relations and old acquaintances , who had neglected the Abbey for years , kept Nancy in a state of perpetual agitation by turning up suddenly to pay congratulatory calls", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "So much careful stitching by dear fingers", "context_len": 7}}}, "duets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "But they walks alone together , and sings duets together , and he gave her the little poodle , and they says , your honour , d \u2019 ye see", "context_len": 30}}}, "feeds": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "She watches them , feeds them , admires their beauty , and calls them her babies", "context_len": 16}}}, "strawberry": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "They are so surprised when we take off the chains and give them tea and muffins and strawberry jam", "context_len": 19}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "'See , I 've put strawberry jam on your bread-and-butter", "context_len": 10}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Blue Crest had a muffin with strawberry jam on it , and she perched on the end of her plate and pecked away in perfect delight", "context_len": 26}}}, "marjory": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Marjory who has to wash out the grease-spots every day for Miss Sophy", "context_len": 13}}}, "mincing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": ") And there she goes mincing along with her long veil hanging behind , and her little poodle running on before her", "context_len": 22}}}, "wayward": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "All this while poor wayward Ted , whose name you must have guessed almost from the first , was lying wholly oblivious to everything about him , muttering now and then a few delirious , incoherent words , and yet by degrees subsiding into a gentle , regular breathing that the professional ear was quick to detect , and that was full of good omen for the waking in the morning", "context_len": 71}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "] Thirty thousand pounds and a baronetship ! Sir Stephen Stumply ! Ah , if that wayward boy -- the Pretender ! the Pretender ! he \u2019 s in a net , in a net , and I \u2019 ll be hanged if I let him out of it", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "He has grown to love the dear old grandmother with the whole of his wayward heart , and she seems to him the embodiment of all that is calm and loving and benignant", "context_len": 33}}}, "indiscretion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "don \u2019 t speak of him ! folly and indiscretion have been his bane", "context_len": 14}}}, "bane": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas must topis [ 92 ] retain , Must guard against sunstroke , to health such a bane ; And midst frogs and mosquitoes must patient remain , Yes , e \u2019 en when tormented , must smile through their pain ; And , with courage like that of the knights of Charlemagne , By Mission Miss Sahibas snakes should be slain", "context_len": 65}}}, "lugging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "] When it was night , your honour , what sees I through the chink of the kitchen door in the passage but the three young Ladies lugging along a great bundle , and stopping and panting and puffing ? So says I , I \u2019 ll see to the bottom of this , so I pops out suddenly and says , Can I help you , Misses ? \u2019 quite civil like", "context_len": 73}}}, "cram": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Fly , I say , and if it be all as I hope , I \u2019 ll cram you with gold till you choke", "context_len": 24}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "'There was a wild schoolgirl named Hirst , Who of all the bad pupils was worst , Till she started to cram For the Cambridge exam", "context_len": 26}}}, "petulant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "What a sleepless night I have passed , what anxiety , what excitement ! and yet how unlike is he to what I had imagined ! so timid , so petulant ! and that perpetual punning ! It matters not , however , -- his title to our services remains the same ! A strange misgiving is on my soul ; is it the shadow of approaching danger , or only the fear of it ? The Colonel gave me a strange meaning look as he passed me this morning , and said , You are early up , Miss Ratty ; I fear that your rest was broken last night", "context_len": 111}}}, "punning": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "What a sleepless night I have passed , what anxiety , what excitement ! and yet how unlike is he to what I had imagined ! so timid , so petulant ! and that perpetual punning ! It matters not , however , -- his title to our services remains the same ! A strange misgiving is on my soul ; is it the shadow of approaching danger , or only the fear of it ? The Colonel gave me a strange meaning look as he passed me this morning , and said , You are early up , Miss Ratty ; I fear that your rest was broken last night", "context_len": 111}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "As , however , he has managed to fall deeply in love with the punning heroine , all difficulties are solved by their marriage , -- Nellie being equally in love with him", "context_len": 33}}}, "sneaking": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 24, "context": "'The way you rolled him flat was the funniest sight I 've seen for many a year ! Get up , Jones , you sneaking , drivelling cur ! ' -- kicking the prostrate form of that fallen hero", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I have rather a sneaking likeness for Agnes , says she", "context_len": 11}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "In those days I used to come sneaking home at night with a guilty conscience , and the fear of being found out spoilt all the comfort ; but I tell you I felt about right to-day , and couldn \u2019 t help thinking of it when father praised me up so much before you , and was so willing to spare me , though he will have to work very hard while I \u2019 m gone", "context_len": 77}}}, "cor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "COR", "context_len": 1}}}, "sufferer": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 52, "context": "Then Alfgar saw what was the marvellous power of Christianity , and beheld a heroism utterly beyond the fierce excitement which nerved his countrymen for their scenes of carnage and blood ; not one of his pagan friends could have suffered as calmly , as patiently -- it seemed easier for the sufferer to bear than for Alfgar to look on ; once or twice the latter gave audible vent to his emotions , but the look which Bertric turned upon him spoke volumes , and he restrained himself lest he should add to the pain of the victim", "context_len": 99}}}, "survive": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "The farce is ended , the mists of mistake are clearing up , the reign of Folly must fall , let not Anger survive its cause !", "context_len": 27}}}, "blockhead": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "What a labyrinth I am in ! The greatest comfort is that the Colonel is a blockhead , and would not know a prince from a pancake ! [ Exit", "context_len": 30}}}, "pancake": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "What a labyrinth I am in ! The greatest comfort is that the Colonel is a blockhead , and would not know a prince from a pancake ! [ Exit", "context_len": 30}}}, "professions": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "But how insincere those professions had been on the part of Ruspak and how deeply he had resented the affront to his dignity as a medicine man was evident now by the malignity in his eyes and the gloating smile on his lips", "context_len": 43}}}, "scaffold": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 72, "context": "There the sentries , who were stationed at the door , had some ado to keep back the curious throng , and many a sturdy country farmer shouldered his way into the house in the wake of his squire to catch a glimpse of his king , the ill-fated King Charles , who was to rest that night at Farnham on his last journey from the prison at Hurst Castle to the scaffold at Whitehall", "context_len": 75}}}, "exiled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Your Father can not -- he is exiled from his native land", "context_len": 12}}}, "corp": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 0, "context": "CORP", "context_len": 1}}}, "bayonets": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 2, "context": "With levelled bayonets Malcolm , Selwyn , and half a dozen riflemen advanced towards the door in the rear of the pill-box , while M'Turk and M'Kane , each brandishing a bomb , ran close to the wall immediately by the side of the machine-gun aperture", "context_len": 46}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Fix bayonets ! ''", "context_len": 5}}}, "delirious": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "All this while poor wayward Ted , whose name you must have guessed almost from the first , was lying wholly oblivious to everything about him , muttering now and then a few delirious , incoherent words , and yet by degrees subsiding into a gentle , regular breathing that the professional ear was quick to detect , and that was full of good omen for the waking in the morning", "context_len": 71}}}, "humbug": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Well , if this arn \u2019 t droll ! he \u2019 s trying to humbug me", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "He was not a man of brilliant intellectual ability or of rare penetration , but there were times when even he was led to suspect that his companion was a humbug", "context_len": 31}}}, "handcuffs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Stickum , have you handcuffs with you ?", "context_len": 8}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "I \u2019 d have brought him around here with me , but the sheriff \u2019 s got handcuffs on him , and it \u2019 s hard to get him out and in the wagon", "context_len": 34}}}, "effrontery": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "As for me , how can I adventure upon this danger and this effrontery ? Moreover , O my son , on what wise shall I demand thee his daughter of the Sultan and how shall I avail to go in to him ? Nay , if they question me , what shall I answer them ? Most like they will deem me a madwoman", "context_len": 65}}}, "dirks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "It was a lucky chance that we were a day too late for the fair at Culloden ; it \u2019 s no fancy I have for the Highlanders \u2019 dirks", "context_len": 30}}}, "slashing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Four days later I arrived at the mouth of the river , without further adventure , and was fortunate enough to find a fine slashing brigantine flying French colours riding at anchor there", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He staggered like a wing-shot goose under the slashing American fire", "context_len": 11}}}, "parse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I fear that I shall find it as difficult to pass this fellow as I found it at school to parse a sentence from my grammar-book", "context_len": 26}}}, "och": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "] If he should fight ! My poor Mother ; och , if she could see me now , \u2019 twould pit her into high-strikes", "context_len": 25}}}, "steeled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "] If I could but touch his kinder feelings ! I have been accustomed to steal hearts , but I fear that I should find his steeled already", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He had steeled himself for the conflict that seemed almost inevitable and from which he had not expected to emerge alive", "context_len": 21}}}, "matchlock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But the sight of his matchlock makes my blood run cold", "context_len": 11}}}, "contemplated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "She was right in thinking Ben was not a weak boy nor one to be easily frightened ; but had she known that over him hung the dark , chilling shadow of the reformatory , she could not have wondered at the course he had contemplated pursuing , and she might have hesitated about so freely giving him advice", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Then it was discovered that one important accessory to the contemplated tragedy was lacking -- a rope", "context_len": 17}}}, "plotting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Ah ! ha ! my pretty Jacobites , this comes of your plotting", "context_len": 13}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "He said that the Persians had , under the ancestors of Darius , crossed the Hellespont , invaded Greece , laid waste the country , and destroyed cities and towns , and had thus done them incalculable injury ; and that Darius himself had been plotting against his ( Alexander 's ) life , and offering rewards to any one who would kill him", "context_len": 64}}}, "sophy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "SOPHY", "context_len": 1}}}, "unjustifiable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "What an uncomfortable puzzle it all was ! and who was to read the answer to the riddle ? Had Seabrooke lost the money ? Had Percy been guilty of possessing himself of his own property by such unjustifiable means ? Or was one of their number an actual thief ?", "context_len": 51}}}, "custody": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "Peggy and Bobby tried , as Archie expressed it , to 'do a slope , ' but in vain , for , saying she had not seen them for a long time , and should like to talk to them , their unwelcome visitor took the dismayed pair into custody like a female policeman , and whirled them sternly along before her", "context_len": 62}}}, "accuser": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Of what am I charged ? Who is my accuser ? what wretch dares ? [ HORATIA repeats the signs", "context_len": 20}}}, "repeats": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 77, "context": "But more incomprehensible than the existence of so bad a man in such a dark age is the renewed confidence ever accorded him , when , after more than once betraying the armies of his country into the hands of their foes , and fighting openly in the hostile cause , he is again forgiven , nay , received into favour , and sent once more to command the men he has already deceived , until he repeats the experiment , and when it fails is again admitted into confidence", "context_len": 90}}}, "spinach": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Fetch , fetch -- some spinach", "context_len": 6}}}, "ingenuous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Sophy , Sophy , if you are still the ingenuous being I ever believed you to be , tell me in what farce I am thus forced to act a part against my will", "context_len": 34}}}, "accessory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "This epithet ( Puants in French ) may have had an opprobrious meaning in the beginning , but not in later times , when it simply served to distinguish the principal people from the accessory tribes", "context_len": 36}}}, "bewilders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Every sentence that I hear bewilders me yet more", "context_len": 9}}}, "vagabond": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 74, "context": "The [ 422 ] Vizier , who was like to die of envy and chagrin for that which had befallen his son , said in himself , `` How shall one like this marry the Sultan 's daughter and my son lose this honour ? '' So he said to the Sultan , [ 423 ] `` O my lord , it is an easy matter to rid ourselves of [ 424 ] this vagabond , [ 425 ] for that it would not beseem Thy Grace to give thy daughter to a man like this , of whom it is not known what he is", "context_len": 106}}}, "gown": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 29, "context": "Beneath a great spreading pear-tree sat the motherly old lady , the last golden rays of the setting sun falling over her ample figure , in her neat black gown , white apron , and snowy kerchief folded over her bosom , spectacles in hand , and in her lap the pan which held the corn and barley ; while around her were the three little ones dipping their chubby hands into the measure , and scattering the contents among the noisy , scrambling crowd of fowls , themselves full of glee and happiness at this , to them , new pleasure", "context_len": 102}}}, "scamp": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Speak , you scamp ! What has induced you to dress yourself like -- a -- speak ! nor add a falsehood to your other faults and follies", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "I can \u2019 t say he showed any sign of wishing to sneak out , and when I remark that his uncle didn \u2019 t have any trouble in lugging him off , I suppose I exaggerate , but I want to make sure the scamp doesn \u2019 t find an excuse for dodging a square , stand up fight", "context_len": 60}}}, "deceiver": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I am the deceived , not the deceiver", "context_len": 8}}}, "unwittingly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "So , once more , by the matter of a few hours , Konrad von Feldoffer escaped a well-merited death ; while , through ignorance of the terrific preparations made for the blowing up of Messines Ridge , he had unwittingly done the Allies a good turn ; for instead of withdrawing the troops the Hun commander had concentrated a thousand on the mined ridge in order to repel an infantry attack that threatened only in the minds of the German staff", "context_len": 82}}}, "applauding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I own that I too fondly sought the giddy cheer of an applauding audience", "context_len": 14}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "We can not help applauding the extraordinary energy of his genius , though we condemn the selfish and cruel ends to which his life was devoted", "context_len": 26}}}, "pardons": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Oh ! '' said the Captain as fiercely as ever , `` I beg five hundred thousand million pardons , but that is nothing but a bad habit we ca n't get rid of", "context_len": 35}}}, "thenceforward": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "She looked spiteful enough to throw it out of the window , but her husband , laughing at her , doffed his worn straw , coolly put on the elaborate headgear , and became thenceforward a target for the quips of the merry idlers about the door", "context_len": 47}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Miss Tucker had her way , however ; and thenceforward she became Auntie \u2019 to an ever-increasing circle of nephews and nieces in India", "context_len": 24}}}, "sinecure": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "One is disposed to think that the care and responsibility of three little ones , undertaken in the midst of a full and busy family life , and in addition to all the duties of that life , could have been no sinecure , and must have been fraught with many a difficulty", "context_len": 53}}}, "attractiveness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Others were disposed to take a different view as to the peculiar attractiveness of such a name as The White Shroud , and when the volume was published it came out as Glimpses of the Unseen", "context_len": 36}}}, "drolly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Writing home , she described drolly her absence from Batala as -- this strange episode of my life ; -- seven weeks acting Superintendent of the Orphanage , -- three of those weeks sole Missionary at Amritsar , -- and -- oh , bathos ! ten days an ayah -- for I had none other", "context_len": 55}}}, "counterbalance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "We must ask So-and-so ; and how shall we find gentlemen to counterbalance Mrs", "context_len": 14}}}, "healths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "In those days the custom of drinking healths \u2019 had gained sway to an absurd and objectionable extent ; gentlemen being expected to respond to every toast , and not only to sip their wine , but very often to empty their glasses , under pain of giving serious offence", "context_len": 50}}}, "decanter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Tucker always had by his side a decanter of toast and water , from which his glass was filled for the various toasts ; and probably those not in the secret counted him a marvellously hard-headed man", "context_len": 37}}}, "toasts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Tucker always had by his side a decanter of toast and water , from which his glass was filled for the various toasts ; and probably those not in the secret counted him a marvellously hard-headed man", "context_len": 37}}}, "marvellously": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 84, "context": "A grand place , too , to work , you think ; and then you recall with a sigh that the light of that glorious mind has well-nigh gone out , 'neath the weight of physical weariness and infirmity , and then the solitary home begins to look a little like a prison in your eyes , as you realize how glad its inmate would be to exchange it for the Palace of that King whose divine intent for the world he has so marvellously interpreted for us all in the days when soul was still master of hand and brain", "context_len": 101}}}, "vintage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "It 's about a two-hour read , but is well-written and in the vintage Manville Fenn style in which `` how does he get out of this ? '' events follow closely on one another", "context_len": 35}}}, "foibles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "And , despite all her kindness , all her readiness to see the best in everybody , she could not , with her keen sense of humour , have failed to be a good deal amused with the various foibles and absurdities which certain people are wont to display , even in the best society , and when upon their most circumspect behaviour", "context_len": 63}}}, "absurdities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "And , despite all her kindness , all her readiness to see the best in everybody , she could not , with her keen sense of humour , have failed to be a good deal amused with the various foibles and absurdities which certain people are wont to display , even in the best society , and when upon their most circumspect behaviour", "context_len": 63}}}, "circumspect": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "And , despite all her kindness , all her readiness to see the best in everybody , she could not , with her keen sense of humour , have failed to be a good deal amused with the various foibles and absurdities which certain people are wont to display , even in the best society , and when upon their most circumspect behaviour", "context_len": 63}}}, "dulness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "In fact , whatever the dinner-parties may have seemed to herself , her own presence , her bright smile and sparkling conversation , effectually prevented sensations of dulness on the part of others who were there", "context_len": 36}}}, "disapproved": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 43, "context": "Then , looking round to see that he was not being followed , he made his way to a hiding-place he had discovered behind the summer-house , and proceeded to employ himself there after a fashion of which nurse would most strongly have disapproved", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Miss Tucker was much distressed about the Afghan war ; not because of any possible peril or discomfort to herself , but because her judgment disapproved of it as a whole , and also because of the sufferings which she knew it must entail upon the soldiers", "context_len": 47}}}, "preceded": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 32, "context": "That the Creeks arrived in Northern Alabama in or after the time of the French colonization of the Lower Mississippi lands , is another impossibility , and the erection of Fort Toulouse preceded the second French war against the Chicasa by more than twenty years , whereas Milfort represents it as having been a consequence of that war", "context_len": 58}}}, "faithless": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 58, "context": "is coming ; we don \u2019 t know whether she is sticking half-way on the road , waiting for the horse which we offered to send twelve miles , if she required it ! Like the famous little pig , we have eaten all the bread and butter ; and if the grand lady arrives -- without that faithless kahar -- what shall we give her to eat ? I urged Florrie at least to send to the city for meat ; but she fears that in the absence of the cook the guest may arrive", "context_len": 96}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "What would be thought of an English soldier who , on being ordered to some lonely and difficult post , were , instead of going at once , to begin to calculate whether it were worth while , -- whether the cost and trouble of his going would be sufficiently repaid by results ? Yet such is the spirit in which certain soldiers of the Cross -- somewhat faithless soldiers , surely ! -- are disposed to regard this great Marching Order of our Captain and King", "context_len": 87}}}, "discouraged": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 1, "context": "Davenport discouraged the acquaintance as much as possible , never feeling easy even for the life and limbs of her progeny when they were in the society of 'those terrible young Vaughans , ' and revenging herself by scathing remarks upon manners and deportment , which were extremely trying to the feelings of Aunt Helen , who naturally thought her children superior in every way to 'those mealy-faced little Davenports , who look as if they had not the strength or spirits to enjoy themselves , even if they were allowed to try", "context_len": 93}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "I reckon we \u2019 ll have to , answered McGlory , in a discouraged tone", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "At three o'clock in the afternoon , tired , bramble-torn and a little discouraged , he sat down by the roadside to rest and think", "context_len": 25}}}, "observant": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "The main question was rather -- to which especial work was she bidden by her Master ? One can hardly live many years on Earth , with observant eyes , and believe that people are always or generally given exactly that work to do , for which they are by natural powers best adapted", "context_len": 54}}}, "mirrors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "For instance , -- having noticed that girls in an evening party , glancing at a mirror , were apt to be disquieted to find their dresses disorganised , she resolved to have no mirrors at all in her rooms , hoping thereby to secure greater peace of mind among her guests", "context_len": 52}}}, "disparaging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "A young clergyman is stated to have one day , in the presence of the Duke , spoken about foreign Missions in the disparaging terms often affected by a particular class of young men", "context_len": 34}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Most children would have been sharp and disparaging about Mrs", "context_len": 10}}}, "counteracting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "\u2019 Rather curiously , she spoke of herself in old age as having been when young subject to very low spirits \u2019 ; or more strictly , she said that she would have been so subject , but for the counteracting influences of religion \u2019 and work , \u2019 the latter arising from the former", "context_len": 55}}}, "assailed": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 48, "context": "How Bomba met two white rubber hunters , Gillis and Dorn , and won their gratitude by saving their lives when their camp was attacked at night by jaguars ; how he trapped the cooanaradi when it pursued him ; how he drove off the vultures when they assailed his friends the monkeys ; how the latter came to his aid when the cabin was besieged by the headhunters -- these adventures are told in the first book of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 97}}}, "questionings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Tired out finally by these ponderings and questionings , Bomba at last threw himself on the floor and slept", "context_len": 19}}}, "untroubled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "While she could go with a clear and perfectly easy conscience , able to enjoy herself , and untroubled by doubts , she probably did so without harm to herself , so long as her life was not given to pleasures , \u2019 that is to say , so long as she did not unduly love these things , or allow them to occupy a wrong place in her life", "context_len": 70}}}, "intrinsically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "For he that doubteth is condemned if he eat , \u2019 even though the eating may not be actually and intrinsically evil", "context_len": 22}}}, "essence": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Whether or no the things were in their essence wrong , -- and to decide this , each thing would have to be regarded apart , entirely on its own merits , -- they became wrong for Charlotte , so soon as she could no longer accept them with a free and happy mind", "context_len": 54}}}, "overridden": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "They became wrong , at least , unless she felt her doubts to be overridden by the duty of obedience", "context_len": 20}}}, "dictates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Although they did not view the question in precisely the same light , they would not make their conscience the rule for her actions , but would leave her free to be guided by the dictates of her own", "context_len": 39}}}, "ranked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 84, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "demarcation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "While feeling the need to draw for herself some line of demarcation between things expedient and things inexpedient , she does not appear to have fallen into the error , so common amongst really earnest and excellent people , of counting that the line which she rightly drew for herself must of necessity be the only right line for everybody else", "context_len": 61}}}, "repellent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There was nothing morbid or repellent about the development of her sense of duty", "context_len": 14}}}, "snatching": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 103, "context": "`` Beware , woman ! '' shrieked Maggie , and Bessie , thinking it time for her to come to her sister 's aid , joined in the cry , `` Beware , woman ! '' while Franky , always ready to take part in any disturbance , struck at Aunt Patty with his whip , and shouted , `` 'Ware , woman ! '' and Hafed , knowing nothing but that this old lady had alarmed and distressed his young charge , and that it was his duty to protect them , raised his voice in a whoop of defiance , and snatching up the hearth-brush , brandished it in a threatening manner as he danced wildly about her", "context_len": 120}}}, "instanter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "Under this idea , Fanny , like a dear Quixote as she is , formed a grand plan of rushing up to town on Thursday by coach with uncle Charlton , who happened to be coming , and turning you off the music-stool , or snatching the spelling frame from your delicate hand instanter", "context_len": 54}}}, "cogent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "But I opposed this double-quick march for several reasons , which I hope you may think cogent", "context_len": 17}}}, "procedure": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "Whether the powers that rule the entrance to the same came to the conclusion that to a little girl of twelve and a little boy of four the term of general public could not honestly be applied , or whether all rules of procedure and precedence were magnanimously waived in their favor , certain it is that the little card in question bore the incredible inscription : Admit Master Albert Allyn and his little friend , Miss Marie-Celeste Harris , to the East Terrace between the hours of twelve and three on Thursday", "context_len": 93}}}, "unthankful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "You are dear to a sister \u2019 s heart , sweet Laura , and I hope that you are one of the blessings for which I am not unthankful", "context_len": 29}}}, "chess": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 15, "context": "She was teeming with life and energy ; -- whether it were over her favourite chess , when she would wait patiently but eagerly , thinking out each move ; or enjoying the small-talk of society , watching faces and reading characters , to treasure them up for painting in one of her forthcoming volumes ; or teaching a niece the beauties of sound and thought in the Italian of Dante ; or playing at some game of thought with young people ; or reading aloud one of her two favourite dearly-loved and untiringly-studied authors , Shakespeare and Boswell \u2019 s Life of Johnson", "context_len": 104}}}, "verging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Though verging now on her thirtieth year , Charlotte Tucker was still unknown to the public as an Author", "context_len": 19}}}, "comic": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "There ought to have been more verses , but at this point Father had unfortunately got hold of the paper , and persisted in treating the poem in such a comic light that the indignant authoress had never found the heart to finish it , though the fragment was considered very talented by Aunt Helen , and carefully put away in an old work-box , with the first specimens of Peggy 's handwriting and one of Bobby 's little baby-curls", "context_len": 80}}}, "latent": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 65, "context": "on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me , died out , and I began to prepare myself , as best I could , to meet whatever fate might have in store for me with the fortitude befitting a Christian and an Englishman", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Yet as he listened , the doctor had an impression of vital warmth underlying the restraint of the girl 's tone -- an impression of feeling that lay far below the surface , latent and half-suspected", "context_len": 36}}}, "undeveloped": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "They were just like youthful undeveloped ponies , loving , teasing , rebelling , watching each other , and over them they knew was the wise whip hand of the parents", "context_len": 31}}}, "destroys": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Th \u2019 intensity of grief destroys itself", "context_len": 7}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}}}, "torturer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "And so the hours rolled on , which brought the dreaded morn nearer and nearer ; and the victim , comforted by prayer , but without hope in this world , slept , and thought no longer of the torturer 's knife , or felt the cruel anticipations which would rack the waiting mind", "context_len": 54}}}, "beholds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The torturer beholds his Victim stretched Unconscious , pain itself o \u2019 ercome by pain", "context_len": 15}}}, "dooms": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Fate dooms me now to death ; last punishment Which mortal can inflict , -- and yet I feel There \u2019 s mercy in the doom", "context_len": 26}}}, "drain": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Thus to live on Were lingering martyrdom ; it were to die By inches , drain my heart \u2019 s blood drop by drop", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "We shall still have to work hard and live carefully , but now that there will not be the continual drain of the interest on the mortgages going out , I shall be able to put more into the land , and carry out the improvements I have been longing to make for years , and in time the estate will pick up , so that I hope when I go to take my place among the old Crusaders in the transept I shall hand it on to the next generation of Vaughans in better condition than I found it", "context_len": 100}}}, "canst": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "One flash ends all ! O Clara , when my soul Hath ceased to suffer , can it cease to love ? Methinks , when quitting Earth , \u2019 twill still retain Her image , who was more than Earth to me ! It is a portion of my being , twined With every thought and feeling ; thou wilt weep , My Clara ; thou canst not believe him false To faith and friends , who is so true to thee", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Moreover , whoso presenteth himself before the Sultan and craveth favours of him , it behoveth him take in his hand somewhat that sorteth with the royal dignity ; and as I said to thee , how canst thou presume to present thyself before the Sultan and seek of him his daughter , without aught thou mayst proffer him of that which sorteth with his rank ? ''", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "I was never false to thee , nor will I be now ; did I not , for thy sake , look over the death of Elfhelm of Shrewsbury , and put out the eyes of his sons ? canst thou not trust me now ? ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "uncorrupted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Gazing into the uncorrupted depths Of thy pure feelings , thou wilt judge of mine", "context_len": 15}}}, "wert": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` Quoth [ 578 ] the Sultan to him , `` I grant thee , according to thy request , the space of forty days ; but think not to flee from my hand , for that I will fetch thee back , though thou wert above the clouds , not to say upon the face of the earth", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Thou diest a traitor \u2019 s death ; -- but wert thou ours , Then ev \u2019 ry bush around the fatal spot Should hold an armed defender , ev \u2019 ry knoll Conceal an ambushed friend , and at a word A wall of steel should bristle round thy breast ; Then swords should clash with swords , and they who came To shed thy blood lie weltering in their own", "context_len": 72}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Brice had got him long ago , and feared thou wert on a wild-goose chase", "context_len": 15}}}, "espoused": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Can he speak thus who hath so long espoused The Royal cause , and served that cause so well ? Who , girt with honours , well deserved , hath stood One in a noble Brotherhood of Fame ! Where are the Cavaliers who fought with thee In battle , side by side , who with thee shared The feast , and drained the wine-cup to your King ? Where are they now ? what , gone ? not one remains , T \u2019 assert thy innocence , or shield thee from An ignominious death", "context_len": 95}}}, "sparkles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "Friends ! out upon them ! They mock the name ; it were not thus , if thou Hadst drawn thy gallant sword with those who wear No chains but those of Virtue , those who own No earthly Monarch , and uphold no power But that of Liberty ; whose friendship lasts Not only when the red wine sparkles high , And revelry and song profane the night ; If such had been thy comrades and thy friends , Thou hadst not been forsaken thus", "context_len": 86}}}, "revelry": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 63, "context": "Friends ! out upon them ! They mock the name ; it were not thus , if thou Hadst drawn thy gallant sword with those who wear No chains but those of Virtue , those who own No earthly Monarch , and uphold no power But that of Liberty ; whose friendship lasts Not only when the red wine sparkles high , And revelry and song profane the night ; If such had been thy comrades and thy friends , Thou hadst not been forsaken thus", "context_len": 86}}}, "profane": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "Friends ! out upon them ! They mock the name ; it were not thus , if thou Hadst drawn thy gallant sword with those who wear No chains but those of Virtue , those who own No earthly Monarch , and uphold no power But that of Liberty ; whose friendship lasts Not only when the red wine sparkles high , And revelry and song profane the night ; If such had been thy comrades and thy friends , Thou hadst not been forsaken thus", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "He tried in vain to open any intercourse with his ferocious jailor , whose ward was sometimes shared by a comrade , when there was much ungodly revelry below , and snatches of Danish war songs mingled with profane oaths", "context_len": 40}}}, "casts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The gate thou hast defended with thy blood , To-morrow casts thee forth , led out to die ; And the proud towers coldly will look down Upon the closing scene ; for hearts more hard And more impregnable decree thy doom", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Sergeant David Stanlaw , stationed at Spirit River , is puzzled by a local killing , the disappearance of the body , the finding of a code message , and by the mystery of the Listening Forest , which casts a shadow of dread over the little town of Wiggin \u2019 s Creek", "context_len": 53}}}, "ry": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 59, "context": "It ain \u2019 t to be wondered at that he died with debt piled up and a mortgage on the place ; but I don \u2019 t want you to think I \u2019 m complainin \u2019 , an \u2019 if the good Lord lets me keep my health an \u2019 strength , I \u2019 ll pay up ev \u2019 ry dollar somehow", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Ev \u2019 ry day I live I thank the Lord that he \u2019 s been so good to me an \u2019 let me have so many pleasures an \u2019 enjoyments", "context_len": 30}}}, "knoll": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "Among the nations tracing their mythic origin to the earth , or what amounts to the same thing , to caves , deep holes , hills or mountains , are the Pomo of Northern California , who believe that their ancestors , the coyote-men , were created directly from a knoll of red earth , [ 135 ] still visible in their country ; the Nahua , whose seven tribes issued from Chicomoztoc or the `` Seven Caves", "context_len": 78}}}, "ambushed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Thou diest a traitor \u2019 s death ; -- but wert thou ours , Then ev \u2019 ry bush around the fatal spot Should hold an armed defender , ev \u2019 ry knoll Conceal an ambushed friend , and at a word A wall of steel should bristle round thy breast ; Then swords should clash with swords , and they who came To shed thy blood lie weltering in their own", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "More than once by this remarkable strategy he reached a certain place ahead of the Indians and ambushed them when they came up", "context_len": 23}}}, "bristle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Thou diest a traitor \u2019 s death ; -- but wert thou ours , Then ev \u2019 ry bush around the fatal spot Should hold an armed defender , ev \u2019 ry knoll Conceal an ambushed friend , and at a word A wall of steel should bristle round thy breast ; Then swords should clash with swords , and they who came To shed thy blood lie weltering in their own", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Indians use the bare hand and arm -- utensils in common stable use among us whites are the metal curry-combs , bristle body-brushes , corn-brushes , rub rags , sponges , whisks and hoof picks", "context_len": 35}}}, "weltering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 68, "context": "Thou diest a traitor \u2019 s death ; -- but wert thou ours , Then ev \u2019 ry bush around the fatal spot Should hold an armed defender , ev \u2019 ry knoll Conceal an ambushed friend , and at a word A wall of steel should bristle round thy breast ; Then swords should clash with swords , and they who came To shed thy blood lie weltering in their own", "context_len": 72}}}, "mayst": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 59, "context": "Accordingly , when she came up , he saw her and viewed her well , beyond his wish ; and from that time till now , O King of the Age , life hath not been pleasant to him [ 366 ] and he will e'en have me seek her of Thy Grace , [ 367 ] so thou mayst marry her with him , and I can not do away this conceit from his wit , for that the love of her hath gotten possession of his vitals , so that he saith to me , 'Know , O mother mine , that , except I attain my desire , assuredly I am a dead man", "context_len": 117}}}, "thistledown": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The thistledown that floats upon the breeze , The thorny weed which from my path I spurn , The insect which I crush beneath my tread , Are not to me more insignificant , More worthless -- than the Slave whom thou hast named", "context_len": 44}}}, "thorny": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "This he could slit with his machete and secure as much as he wanted of the cooling delicious waters that these plants store up , a fact that , if known , would have saved the lives of many of those who have perished of thirst in the very shadow of the thorny plants", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Nonesuch had raced with Frankie to the very edge of this little rivulet , and then stood still for a moment as if considering what he would do next , when a hand , holding a long , thorny switch , was suddenly put forth from the clump of bushes , and Nonesuch received a stinging blow across his haunches", "context_len": 60}}}, "spurn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The thistledown that floats upon the breeze , The thorny weed which from my path I spurn , The insect which I crush beneath my tread , Are not to me more insignificant , More worthless -- than the Slave whom thou hast named", "context_len": 44}}}, "melts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Thank Heaven ! then my last doubt melts away ; He yet is true , yet faithful to his King ; My sacrifice will not be made for nought", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Ice forms , then melts again", "context_len": 6}}}, "oppressor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "I could , -- yes , I could arm in thy behalf A thousand gallant hands , might I but say , The injured will on the oppressor turn , Unite the love of freedom with revenge , A thousand-fold repay the debt he owes To your brave confidence ; in Ravensby Ye will destroy a foe and win a friend ! \u2019 Could I speak thus -- --", "context_len": 69}}}, "sex": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 38, "context": "Dogood \u2019 s first letter , as well as subsequent ones in which she expressed herself , wittily and clearly , on such varied subjects as the folly of fashionable dress , the character of the so-called weaker sex , the ill effects of liquor , the inferior quality of New England poetry , the need of insurance for widows and old maids , the hypocrisy of certain pretenders to religion , and the uselessness of sending dullards to Harvard simply because their fathers could afford to pay their way", "context_len": 90}}}, "protects": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Thy sex protects thee , Maid , Or thou shouldst learn the meed of treason", "context_len": 15}}}, "treason": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "The fleet is miserably destroyed ; Brihtric , Edric 's brother , a man like-minded to himself ; accused Wulfnoth , the ealdorman of Sussex , of high treason ; the ealdorman , knowing that he had no chance of justice , seduced the crews of twenty ships , and became a pirate , like unto the Danes themselves", "context_len": 59}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Happily it was stupidity not treason that had caused the disastrous loss of time , and the guide was full of sorrow for his error", "context_len": 25}}}, "tomb": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 20, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "pinion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I would not rob Thy pinion of one plume", "context_len": 9}}}, "confesses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Wells confesses , in the preface to this book , that he has a very tender regard for the `` Little People , '' as fairies used to be called in those days , and now he has given us , under the title of `` Witchery Ways , '' some fairy tales of his own which will prove a never-ending delight to every reader", "context_len": 65}}}, "puns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Modern English puns sit oddly upon a background of pre-medi\u00e6val Saxon history", "context_len": 12}}}, "laughable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "fell ill , as she did last year -- her daughter is constantly off and on with fever -- where should we be ? In a laughable dilemma , I should say ; for I don \u2019 t think that Mr", "context_len": 41}}}, "cramp": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then , as Frank carefully shifted his position , to get rid of a cramp in his left leg , his fingers came in contact with his belt filled with cartridges", "context_len": 31}}}, "nellie": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "NELLIE", "context_len": 1}}}, "intolerably": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "No opening was provided for the escape of the smoke or the admission of fresh air , and the building soon became intolerably hot ; but at dance-feasts the natives danced around the fire in the terrible heat and dust , without the least apparent inconvenience", "context_len": 46}}}, "spiders": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 32, "context": "The moment your pranks begin , the moment I hear of any more toads being put into the bread-pan , or wasps and caterpillars descending on poor James 's head , or spiders put down the housemaids ' backs , or leeches being allowed to run about your bedroom , I shall just go off", "context_len": 55}}}, "webs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Why , the very spiders \u2019 webs look as though there were no business doing in them , and not a fly nor even a broom would call at the door ! Heigh-ho !", "context_len": 34}}}, "nell": {"freq": 34, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 0, "context": "NELL", "context_len": 1}}}, "oddity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 72, "context": "Since the memorable time when Captain Cook sailed into Botany Bay in 1769 and saw the naked native Australian poising erect to hurl his peculiar weapon , the boomerang has continued to excite the curiosity and amazement of the civilized world ; and truly the finding of such a scientific weapon in the hands of this so-called lowest order of mankind is an astonishing fact , to be simply accepted as another oddity of this odd , topsy-turvy corner of the world", "context_len": 82}}}, "consulting": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "I have remarked with regret , if you will permit me to say it , an aversion to consulting the Atlas , which -- --", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "And now we have not our old income , this very expensive consulting physician is not required", "context_len": 17}}}, "atlas": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": ", an atlas in fol", "context_len": 5}}}, "bequeathed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "I wish that the cross old gentleman who bequeathed my father Grimhaggard Hall , had lived to repent it , that I do ! I would rather live in the narrowest lane in the City than be cooped up here like a toad in a block", "context_len": 46}}}, "cooped": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "I wish that the cross old gentleman who bequeathed my father Grimhaggard Hall , had lived to repent it , that I do ! I would rather live in the narrowest lane in the City than be cooped up here like a toad in a block", "context_len": 46}}}, "penelope": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Penelope was a distinguished ornament to her sex", "context_len": 8}}}, "navigator": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 5, "context": "You know that the greatest navigator was a Cook ; I dare say that he discovered Chili , Cayenne , and Cura\u00e7oa", "context_len": 22}}}, "sprigs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 59, "context": "These reached as far as they could see on either side , and came so near to the edge of the road , that once , when James had to make way for a heavy hay wagon , and drew in his horses to let it pass , Maggie stretched her hand out of the carriage and pulled some sprigs from one of the bushes", "context_len": 65}}}, "transplanted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "Vaughan , and stopped to give him some good advice as to the general upbringing of his family , even suggesting that Peggy -- for a yearly consideration -- should be transplanted to Pendlefield Rectory , to share the studies and maternal care of the five little Davenports , a proposal which he declined with a haste that was perhaps more emphatic than polite", "context_len": 64}}}, "perpetrate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "To plunder , burn , destroy , and kill , are the lighter and more harmless of the crimes they perpetrate", "context_len": 21}}}, "quiz": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Do you know I fancy her such a prim old quiz ? I should like to know whether she will play at chess with Papa , or teach me the guitar , as you do", "context_len": 35}}}, "guitar": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 68, "context": "One day I came down to breakfast , exclaiming , How beautiful the snow is ! -- when she told me how pleased she was that I could say so , instead of saying , How cold it is ! When I was ill in the year 1872 , she often came to see me , quite disregarding the infection of my throat ; she would play her guitar to me , or , as I grew better , would patiently guide my little fingers to the right places on the strings", "context_len": 92}}}, "exhilarating": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "The total want of all society , except that which the walls of Grimhaggard Hall have the honour constantly to enclose , may perhaps have an effect upon the lady \u2019 s spirits not altogether exhilarating ; but when your brother returns from College , perhaps he may be accompanied by some of his fellow-students", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "On and on the riders travelled , through the exhilarating autumn air , until they stopped for lunch on the borders of a forest which Jack Frost had set ablaze , and which glowed in the sunshine with a dazzling splendour of crimson and bronze and gold", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The excitement of driving to the station in the exhilarating morning air , past houses which , like her uncle 's , seemed the abodes of luxurious ease", "context_len": 28}}}, "applicable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` thy presence '' ) are the titles commonly given to kings in Arabic-speaking countries , although hhedsretuk is strictly applicable only to the Prophet and other high spiritual dignitaries", "context_len": 30}}}, "arises": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "There sometimes arises over this lake a terrible north-east wind , which comes up very suddenly and blows with great violence , causing the waves to rise to a height which would be thought almost impossible by one who had not seen it", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "arises ) from it", "context_len": 4}}}, "freaks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "In all their freaks , in all their wicked doings , they generally kept together , and stood by one another , and he had expected to find her in the hovel when he returned to it that evening", "context_len": 39}}}, "consigned": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Side by side they were thrust on to a little low seat , and their shoes and pumps being quickly removed , were consigned to the keeping of a condescending boots , who , summoned by the touch of an electric bell , carried them away at arm 's length", "context_len": 50}}}, "absurdity": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Because of its happy absurdity the name long clung to Jane ; but despite such small jests every one respected her sterling traits , -- every one , that is , except Se\u00f1ora Vigil , who lived hard by in a mud house like a bird 's nest , and who cherished a grudge against her neighbor", "context_len": 58}}}, "spiteful": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "She looked spiteful enough to throw it out of the window , but her husband , laughing at her , doffed his worn straw , coolly put on the elaborate headgear , and became thenceforward a target for the quips of the merry idlers about the door", "context_len": 47}}}, "clause": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "That would have been a pretty severe lesson to the young man ; but what do you say to the affectionate uncle leaving such a clause as this in his will ? That my father must only have and hold this said Grimhaggard Hall , on condition of poor Mr", "context_len": 50}}}, "revert": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "They were but part of the polishing of the golden staff of her Will , -- to revert to her own allegory of earlier days", "context_len": 25}}}, "argues": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Such a clause argues little charity ; but perhaps it may ultimately prove for the benefit of him whom it was designed to injure", "context_len": 24}}}, "atom": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 79, "context": "He found three thrushes ' nests low down in the elder-bushes , all filled with gaping yellow mouths , the pretty little chaffinch 's up in the ivy-tree , with only two speckly eggs as yet , and Jenny Wren 's household , hidden away in a bank , full of so many children that she surely resembled the old woman who lived in a shoe , and it was a marvel how she could remember which little chirping atom she had fed last", "context_len": 84}}}, "lackey": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Atherton should manage to get in disguised as a lackey", "context_len": 10}}}, "pedlar": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A travelling pedlar is regarded with suspicion ; the butcher-boy must hand the leg of mutton over the gate ; the young apothecary is an object of terror , -- I could not have a tooth pulled out , were I to die for it", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Encouraged by this success , Peggy 's next venture was on a pedlar , who came down the road with a pack on his back and a thick stick in his hand", "context_len": 32}}}, "duller": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Dear me , how it is raining ! The weather seems endeavouring to find out whether it be possible to make Grimhaggard Hall look a little duller than usual", "context_len": 29}}}, "rudders": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "I hope the coach is provided with oar and rudders , for she will certainly have to swim for it !", "context_len": 21}}}, "gig": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 34, "context": "But the strain was too much for Peggy , and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say", "context_len": 128}}}, "disquiet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Scull , being much shaken , is actually admitted within the walls of the old Hall , to the great disquiet of Mr", "context_len": 23}}}, "gigs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Well , if all the gigs and cabs in England are not in coalition against me this day ! And where is your luggage ?", "context_len": 25}}}, "cabs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Well , if all the gigs and cabs in England are not in coalition against me this day ! And where is your luggage ?", "context_len": 25}}}, "coalition": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Well , if all the gigs and cabs in England are not in coalition against me this day ! And where is your luggage ?", "context_len": 25}}}, "bog": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 64, "context": "the peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to laugh at the supernatural , she did not feel quite so brave as she pretended , and found little cold shivers stealing down her back when the wind rose suddenly , and began to wail round the cottage like a hungry creature waiting to be let", "context_len": 128}}}, "dab": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I \u2019 m a regular dab at it", "context_len": 8}}}, "blunders": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "She says I \u2019 m a little absent sometimes ; so if ever I make a few trifling blunders , I hope you \u2019 ll set them down to that score", "context_len": 31}}}, "netting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Ford was evidently coming down the shoot when his axe handle or some of his accoutrements became entangled in the wire netting ; so that , to clear himself , he had to break through , and , while struggling to do so , he got so severely burned that his recovery was hopeless", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Down the field they went , a forward pass netting a good gain and wonderfully saving the wind of the now almost exhausted team", "context_len": 24}}}, "crowfoot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Knitting , netting , -- crow -- crowfoot", "context_len": 8}}}, "pickled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "O I \u2019 m not particular about those sort of things ; but if you want my opinion , why I think pickled tongues are excellent", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "I reckon , neighbor , and he cocked up his eye at the waiter , that I \u2019 ll trouble you for a seltzer lemonade , mixed with a pickled cherry and the cross-section of a ripe orange", "context_len": 38}}}, "inverse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "And the Rule of Three inverse ?", "context_len": 7}}}, "scull": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "SCULL", "context_len": 1}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "I can make a hog \u2019 s trough , and christen it a float , but to make one that will be stiff and light , and scull steady and true , there \u2019 s only one man round here can do it", "context_len": 43}}}, "cowslip": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "After the adventure at the mill-wheel , Aunt Helen , judging wisely that 'Satan finds some mischief still for idle hands to do , ' sent the children into the fields with Lilian to gather cowslips to make cowslip beer", "context_len": 40}}}, "misapprehension": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Subsequent events have shown this to be founded on a misapprehension of the Indian character , which is that of the most outspoken individuality", "context_len": 24}}}, "appertains": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Cramp has had no suspicions , turns out to be the much dreaded nephew , and to him by right Grimhaggard Hall now appertains", "context_len": 24}}}, "increasingly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "It must have been at about this time that Charlotte became increasingly anxious for more of definite outdoor work among the poor", "context_len": 22}}}, "infectious": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` I am obliged to you for making the offer , '' she continued ; `` but it is impossible for me to accept it , for the simple reason that there is just the possibility that Jane may be going to have some infectious disease , in which case I could not hear of any other girl in my establishment running any risk", "context_len": 64}}}, "submitting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "However , while submitting , she probably used from time to time some little pressure to bring about another state of things ; and somewhere about the beginning of 1851 her parents \u2019 reluctant consent \u2019 was , we are told , at length given", "context_len": 45}}}, "taper": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 80, "context": "The door opened , and Edward left the room after a conference with the physician , who sat in a corner of the room compounding drugs at a small table ; a few minutes passed in silence , when he returned and held the door open for the bishop of London , who entered , bearing the viaticum , as the last communion of the sick was then called , and attended by an acolyte , who bore a lighted taper before him and carried a bell", "context_len": 87}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` The very prettiest little paw I ever saw in my life , '' he said then ; `` it 's wonderful how slim it is , and how long , and how white , and what little taper fingers ; it 's wonderful", "context_len": 44}}}, "inflammation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Soon afterwards a sharp attack of fever and inflammation laid him low , and most serious fears for his life were felt", "context_len": 22}}}, "elasticity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "As his biographer says , he had been always so full of life , there had been so much activity of body , so much energy of mind , so much elasticity of spirit , that they had never associated with all this vitality a thought of the stillness of death", "context_len": 51}}}, "vitality": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "As his biographer says , he had been always so full of life , there had been so much activity of body , so much energy of mind , so much elasticity of spirit , that they had never associated with all this vitality a thought of the stillness of death", "context_len": 51}}}, "rally": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Tucker \u2019 s rally from his severe illness ; and Charlotte had the pleasure of being taken to Brussels for the wedding by her brother , St", "context_len": 27}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "No matter what I \u2019 m doin \u2019 , I jest drop an \u2019 don \u2019 t know a thing for a minute or two ; then , after I come out of it , I \u2019 m gen \u2019 rally a little while piecin \u2019 things together", "context_len": 48}}}, "pottles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "DEAREST LAURA , -- We have finished the volume of stories which we were reading -- which by the way resembled the pottles of strawberries sold in the streets , capital at the beginning , but as one gets further on , miserably inferior -- and now Fanny has gone to her dear Will-making , so I keep her pen in company by writing to you", "context_len": 66}}}, "shines": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Now what shall I write to you about , dear -- for we write so often that it is impossible that we should often have much to write about ? The sun shines one day , and does not shine another ; the sea is rough one morning and calm the next", "context_len": 52}}}, "informant": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "-- our friend Christopher the Cook -- who was king at the time that my informant wrote , that is to say , in 1819", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "If Hawkins ' informant used the passive form of h\u00eddsh\u00e4s to see , when speaking of the appearance of the Kas\u00ed ta , it would be more appropriate to say originated , were born than the expression we find in the text : `` found themselves", "context_len": 46}}}, "ecclesiastical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Elfwyn and Herstan talked of the old days of Dunstan ; Alfgar and Hermann of the events of the recent war ; the good bishop and Father Cuthbert on ecclesiastical topics ; the ladies upon some question of dresses and embroidery for the approaching festivity , which seemed to interest them deeply , when an attendant entered , and approaching the king , whispered a message in his ear", "context_len": 69}}}, "epaulettes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Do you remember the name of Thaurepas ( ? ) , the blacky General who weakly surrendered his post to the French ? What do you think the grateful Monsieurs did to him ? Nailed epaulettes on his shoulders and a cocked hat on his head , and then threw him with his wife and children into the sea ! Would one believe such things of men in the 19th century ? I should like to know something of the present state of Hayti , and whether the throne is filled by a son of Henri I", "context_len": 97}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "It bore the inscription `` 1812 , '' and according to the colonel , portrayed a military man life-size , epaulettes , sword , uniform and all -- his maternal grandfather as he had appeared in the battle scene where he had lost a limb", "context_len": 45}}}, "autograph": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "If he were , would you not like to have his autograph ?", "context_len": 13}}}, "luxuriating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "DEAREST LAURA , -- We have been luxuriating in the letters from Paris", "context_len": 13}}}, "lightsome": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "All things look so bright and joyous ! I have twice sung The World is so Bright to-day con amore , and my heart is so lightsome that I could dance", "context_len": 31}}}, "disconnected": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "This is a very disconnected sort of note , a sort of patchwork , for my ears are as much employed as my hand , and I have every now and then a message to darn in , -- then , O my chilblains ! But I am determined to complain of nothing , for I am so overloaded with blessings", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Lilian was away , spending a few days in Shrewsbury with a distant cousin ; Father had gone to Warford , and had taken Bobby with him ; even Rollo had disappeared on some mysterious errand not entirely disconnected with bones , so the young lady was left for once to her own devices", "context_len": 54}}}, "patchwork": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "He ascended the narrow , uncarpeted stairs to his small , uncarpeted room over the kitchen , where a loose board rattled beneath his feet , and the dull light from a single window showed him the old-fashioned , low-posted , corded bedstead with its straw tick , coarse sheets and patchwork quilt pushed back beneath the sloping rafters of the roof", "context_len": 62}}}, "overloaded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "This is a very disconnected sort of note , a sort of patchwork , for my ears are as much employed as my hand , and I have every now and then a message to darn in , -- then , O my chilblains ! But I am determined to complain of nothing , for I am so overloaded with blessings", "context_len": 61}}}, "dahlias": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "This was a form of amusement which appealed far more to Miss Forster 's mind , for her flowers suffered much from drought in summer time , and she was a keen competitor at the local horticultural shows , exhibiting some of the largest carnations and the roundest dahlias in the neighbourhood", "context_len": 52}}}, "circlet": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Ethelred took his place ; upon his head a thin circlet of gold confined his flowing locks already becoming scant , but , as their natural colour was light , not otherwise showing signs of age : he was only in his fortieth year", "context_len": 44}}}, "absentees": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "I hope and trust that we may all have such a happy winter together , when Love \u2019 s shining circlet has all its gems complete except the dear Indian absentees", "context_len": 31}}}, "whist": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "One day it was Little Lord Fauntleroy and Hans Brinker , and then Marie-Celeste had the floor ; and the next it was The Story of a Short Life , when honors were even , as they used to say in whist , because both had so lately read it", "context_len": 50}}}, "neuralgic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "With the coming of winter acute neuralgic pains took possession of him ; and though some little improvement was seen with the advent of spring , it was not permanent", "context_len": 30}}}, "improvement": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 66, "context": "So now in a few words he praised them for their earnest and faithful efforts ; said that he had been treated to a perusal of many of the compositions written during the last term in order that he might himself have an opportunity of judging whether Miss Ashton 's verdict were just , and that he had been both surprised and gratified to observe the improvement made by almost every member of the class", "context_len": 75}}}, "fluctuations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Amid these fluctuations , which included at times very severe suffering , his manly courage and patience were never known to fail", "context_len": 22}}}, "thanking": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Why , Susan , s'pose everybody had n't done everything they could when we thought this one was going to be took , would n't we have thought they was hard-hearted creeturs ? I aint done thanking the Almighty yet for leaving her to us , and I aint the man to refuse nothing to them as is in like trouble , -- not if it was to ride all the way to York with the telegram", "context_len": 78}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Late in the night Henry awoke , and , opening his eyes , saw his aunt kneeling by the side of the bed , and heard her thanking God that he had given her this child 's life , beseeching him , oh , so earnestly , that it might be the means of turning his young heart towards her , that there might be some one in the world to love her", "context_len": 73}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The policeman promised to do this , and then , once more thanking Mr", "context_len": 14}}}, "reapers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "God had blessed our increase , and our garners were full with all manner of store ; women and children had mainly been the reapers , but the Lady Hilda herself had been present amongst them , and so had her daughter , my niece , Ethelgiva , even sometimes labouring with their own hands", "context_len": 55}}}, "calculating": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "It is very strikingly characteristic of the mingling of poetic sentiment and enthusiasm with calm and calculating business efficiency , which shone conspicuously so often in Alexander 's career , that when he arrived at Sestos , and found that the ships were there , and the army safe , and that there was no enemy to oppose his landing on the Asiatic shore , he left Parmenio to conduct the transportation of the troops across the water , while he himself went away in a single galley on an excursion of sentiment and romantic adventure", "context_len": 96}}}, "epistles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "There is a tone of quiet sadness running through the letter , in marked contrast with those joyous epistles to her sister Laura quoted earlier in this chapter", "context_len": 28}}}, "sharer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "It is not hard to realise what the thought must have been to Charlotte of losing this dearly-loved companion , -- her room-mate and the constant sharer of her thoughts and interests from very infancy ; nor is it difficult to believe how bravely she would put aside the recollection of herself , viewing the question from Laura \u2019 s standpoint alone", "context_len": 62}}}, "viewing": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "Presently , the Sultan 's daughter came forth and went round about the city and its thoroughfares and diverted herself by viewing it ; then she repaired to the bath and when she came thither , she lifted her face-veil , as she entered ; whereupon her face shone out , as it were the resplendent sun or a precious pearl , and she was as saith of her one of her describers :", "context_len": 74}}}, "romantically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It must , however , be remembered that Charlotte was romantically enthusiastic on the subject of others \u2019 engagements , and was through life ardently interested in the marriages of her friends", "context_len": 32}}}, "marriages": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "Both parties have a negative voice ; both must be pleased ; and if so -- the banns are published ! This is the compromise between European and Oriental ways of arranging marriages", "context_len": 33}}}, "incentive": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "A peculiar incentive was the possession of war-titles , and the rage for these was as strong among the younger men as that for plunder among the older", "context_len": 28}}}, "publishing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "Haven , having some artistic ability and experience , was the main present support of the family , doing considerable work for a publishing house in the city in the way of illustrations for fashion pages", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Inglis \u2019 objection to publishing The White Shroud , etc", "context_len": 10}}}, "overpoweringly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "But a minute or two later , as we drew nearer the tree , the stench meanwhile becoming almost overpoweringly strong , I detected fastened to the trunk of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may", "context_len": 128}}}, "lambs": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 35, "context": "I ask for no earthly remuneration ; my position in life renders me independent of any exertions of my own ; I pray but for God \u2019 s blessing upon my attempts to instruct His lambs in the things which concern their everlasting welfare ; and deeply gratified should I feel , were my little work to be classed among the numerous valuable publications which you have already given to the world", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "' at bedtime every night , just as I do , and mamma says she thinks of them now , just as little lambs safe-folded by the dear Shepherd they used to pray to every night", "context_len": 36}}}, "publications": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "A newspaper cost sevenpence ; there were no national schools or Sunday schools , no penny publications , no penny postage , no railways , no gas , and no free libraries , and no free education ! Yet so resolute was he in his desire for education that , though he was not even allowed a candle after the elders went to bed , he would sit up till late at night reading by the glow of the embers", "context_len": 80}}}, "connecting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "The tribes who inhabited the watershed of the north shore of the Mexican Gulf must always occupy a prominent place in the study of American Ethnology , as possibly connecting the races of North and South America , and those of the Valley of the Mississippi with those of Anahuac and Mayapan", "context_len": 52}}}, "links": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "The man held up his hands to show the steel bands firmly clasped about his wrists , and joined by a few short links", "context_len": 24}}}, "advertised": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "Already -- going for a moment last night into the court , a woman had confided to her that a little child was being advertised for at all the police stations , and that she wished she could get hold of him , for the reward offered for his recovery was ten pounds", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "At three o'clock prompt , as advertised , Bart ascended the little platform and took up the gavel", "context_len": 18}}}, "proposes": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The present publication proposes to bring before the public , in popular form , some scientific results obtained while studying the language and ethnology of the Creek tribe and its ethnic congeners", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` And the king proposes to enrol you amongst his chosen warriors on that day ; he has marked the skill you have displayed in the mimic contests with spear or sword , your skill as a horseman , and he wishes to see whether in actual battle you will fulfil the promise of the parade ground", "context_len": 57}}}, "circulation": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": ", it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed , with", "context_len": 128}}}, "exchanges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": ", which nom de guerre , in compliance with his wish , and in reliance on his promise to preserve her incognita , she now exchanges for her own", "context_len": 29}}}, "remembrances": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 15, "context": "But alas ! there were no traditions of victory to encourage them ; only gloomy remembrances of defeat ; and , but for the stern call of duty which bade them , as men and Christians , go to the succour of their brethren , the majority would have preferred to remain at home and abide the worst , although they knew full well that submission utterly failed to mitigate the ferocious cruelty of their oppressors , who slew alike the innocent babe and the grey-haired grandsire", "context_len": 87}}}, "twinges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "In any case it is to be hoped that few Authors are , at first starting , so absolutely convinced of their own powers as not to go through certain twinges of bashfulness", "context_len": 33}}}, "definitely": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 28, "context": "When he and Jerry and Pilot departed , with Henry Bailey , who took the boys away until such time as Asher Rand \u2019 s affairs should be definitely settled and a guardian appointed for them , nearly every lad in the village , together with a number of the girls and not a few of the older citizens , accompanied them to the railway station", "context_len": 66}}}, "potentialities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Charlotte was by nature ambitious ; and her literary gift was considerable ; and some of its potentialities appear to have been sacrificed to her ardent desire for usefulness", "context_len": 29}}}, "manifestation": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 40, "context": "As we grew older , we not only more fully understood the exceptional boundlessness of old-fashioned hospitality and kindness which that house and household exemplified thoroughly , but we came to understand somewhat of the heart-source whence issued that truest manifestation , of everyday religion , which evidences itself in an absolutely unselfish consecration , -- consistent , unreserved , and essentially practical , -- for everyday wear , and not only under stimulating environments", "context_len": 75}}}, "didactic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "might have reached a larger circle , might have gained a more extensive influence , if she had less anxiously pressed so very much didactic talk into her tales , -- if too she had more studiously cultivated her own dramatic instincts , and had more closely studied human nature", "context_len": 50}}}, "sermons": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 101, "context": "tell you one thing , Uncle Everett , it was a talk I had with that dear child under an apple-tree , down at Nuneham , that made me feel that some people whom I care a great deal for still had faith in me , and it was she who gave me courage by what she told me to go home as fast as ever I could get there and then , Marie-Celeste , what do you suppose I said to him ? Well , I just , told him that that same dear child had preached me two blessed sermons -- one on the deck of the Majestic and the other exactly where a sermon should be preached , beneath the roof of dear old St", "context_len": 128}}}, "alternating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "No one can deny that some of the said volumes do contain a large amount of direct preaching \u2019 ; not merely of life-lessons , interwoven with the story in such wise that the one could not be read and the other missed , but rather of little sermons so alternating with the story that a child might read the latter and skip the former", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The alternating processes observed here also occur in most other Indian and illiterate languages : tch , dsh alternate with ts , ds , h with k , ; g with the other gutturals , b with p , d with t , \u0101 with e , o with u", "context_len": 50}}}, "studiously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "might have reached a larger circle , might have gained a more extensive influence , if she had less anxiously pressed so very much didactic talk into her tales , -- if too she had more studiously cultivated her own dramatic instincts , and had more closely studied human nature", "context_len": 50}}}, "determinately": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It is found in the simple fact that she determinately stamped down her own personal ambitions , and bent her powers with a most single heart to this task of doing good \u2019 ; that she resolutely yielded herself and her gifts to the Service of her Heavenly Father , desiring only that His Name might be honoured in what she undertook", "context_len": 62}}}, "gauges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "And HE who looks on the heart , HE who gauges our actions not by results but by the motives which prompt them , -- HE , we may well believe , honoured His servant for her faithful work in His Service", "context_len": 42}}}, "recur": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 83, "context": "Also his energy in teaching was reflected by her own burning desire , in old age , to speak on all occasions to the Natives of their deepest needs , and never to miss an opportunity of trying to lead some poor Hindu or Muhammadan to Christ , always with the vivid sense upon her , when she met man or woman , that the call to herself might come before they could meet again , and so a second opportunity might never recur", "context_len": 84}}}, "creations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "For teaching \u2019 purposes her boys may sometimes converse together as boys never do converse ; but none the less those boys are real , and they recur in after years to the memory as only living people or vivid creations ever do recur", "context_len": 44}}}, "allegorical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "One of the most strongly allegorical of her earlier works was The Giant-Killer ; and in that little book she no doubt made free use of her own experiences", "context_len": 29}}}, "adaptation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "This was remarkable in her ; not because of any peculiar result from it in England , but because of its very peculiar adaptation to Indian needs", "context_len": 27}}}, "imagery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "One may almost think of her authorship in England as mainly a long preparation for her Indian toil ; the continuous practice in habits of imagery and allegory , by no means especially suited to our Western minds , gradually fitting her to deal with the Oriental mind , little as she yet dreamt of any such destination for herself", "context_len": 60}}}, "allegory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "One may almost think of her authorship in England as mainly a long preparation for her Indian toil ; the continuous practice in habits of imagery and allegory , by no means especially suited to our Western minds , gradually fitting her to deal with the Oriental mind , little as she yet dreamt of any such destination for herself", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Parable , allegory , and metaphor were the very language in which she thought ; and her thoughts always seemed naturally to clothe themselves in those figures of speech in which the children of the East are wont to express themselves", "context_len": 41}}}, "frictions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The polishing of the darkened gold of her Will she had long known in the small unavoidable frictions of everyday life ; and the plunging of that Will into furnace-heat , and the straightening of its crookedness by means of heavy successive blows , she had begun to know in the death of her dear Father , and would soon know more fully through other sorrows coming after", "context_len": 68}}}, "crookedness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "The polishing of the darkened gold of her Will she had long known in the small unavoidable frictions of everyday life ; and the plunging of that Will into furnace-heat , and the straightening of its crookedness by means of heavy successive blows , she had begun to know in the death of her dear Father , and would soon know more fully through other sorrows coming after", "context_len": 68}}}, "perusing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Bring it in and see what it says , '' proposed Bart , and soon , under the light of the gas in the dining-room , the boys were perusing the strange missive", "context_len": 34}}}, "overload": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I do not like to overload dear Bella with advice", "context_len": 10}}}, "falcon": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 58, "context": "`` Here , then , my boy , on this spot where thou wert once cradled , renounce all these Christian follies and superstitions , and thou shalt go back with me to the camp of King Sweyn , where thou shalt be received as the descendant of warrior kings , and shalt forget that thou , the falcon , wert ever the inmate of the dovecote", "context_len": 67}}}, "morion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "What ! -- the falcon crest and morion , -- the scar on his dark brow -- will not all this throw the poor ignorant thick-lipped hero quite into the shade ? Yes , -- if a sparkling bubble is more glorious than a diamond shut up in a black case", "context_len": 51}}}, "sunshiny": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "In giving the story of Charlotte Tucker , and of the growth of the Church at Batala , with which she was so intimately associated , it is of very real importance to show frankly both sides of the picture , -- the dark side , as well as the bright ; the cloudy as well as the sunshiny", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "This chanced to be the first mishap ; but then could you really call an accident a mishap that simply necessitated a three-days ' stay in the beautiful Wordsworth district ? Our sunshiny little party , at any rate , chose not so to regard it , and scoured the whole lovely region on foot , reading Wordsworth 's poetry in their halts by the roadside , and growing familiar with every foot of the lanes he so dearly loved", "context_len": 80}}}, "nestles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The Mote nestles so curiously in a hollow of the hill , that when you have walked a few hundred yards from it , and naturally turn round to look at the noble mansion which you have left , -- it is actually non inventus", "context_len": 45}}}, "teething": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "What a good thing it has been for your little darling being at so healthy a place during the trying time of teething", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "But she was with Miss Armitage all through that awful time we had with the children teething , and the babies are good", "context_len": 23}}}, "diverting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Your sailor-boy arrived all right ? asked Ted , partly by way of diverting conversation from himself and partly because there was the possibility of meeting him to be provided against", "context_len": 31}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "In grief there is nothing so diverting as action , and now that he had sent his telegram , and hoped to be able to see his mother shortly , it made the bad news a little easier to bear", "context_len": 40}}}, "preaches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Dear Robin preaches to upwards of a hundred blind , and bears the hot weather wonderfully well", "context_len": 17}}}, "wader": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Besides which , the moat might be waded , although it is a doubtful point whether the wader could get on through the weeds and mud", "context_len": 26}}}, "sprightly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "D \u2019 Oyly taking us to see it , when we drove here in two carriages , and you were with the sprightly , and I with the sedate party ? I feel sure that this was the identical old house", "context_len": 41}}}, "magnet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "But , as it is , you serve as a magnet , to help to draw me back to Middlesex without regret", "context_len": 22}}}, "lint": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Duties were meanwhile assigned to all the able-bodied women and boys : some provided buckets and ladders , that , in case the Danes attempted to kindle a flame , they might attempt in vain ; others tore up lint and prepared bandages for the wounded , while others passed into the upper apartments to see that no lights remained which could direct the aim of the foe", "context_len": 68}}}, "hamilton": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 0, "context": "Hamilton : I agree with you that your beloved sister \u2019 s power lies in gifts which can be used to perhaps greater influence here than in an out-station", "context_len": 29}}}, "loiter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "and I have just come from a loiter at the Botanical Gardens , which showed us that we need be under no great concern , were hemp and flax exterminated from the vegetable world , and silkworms to leave off being spinsters , as we could dress cheaply and well on plantain fibre , have capital paper and excellent ropes , etc", "context_len": 62}}}, "flax": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "and I have just come from a loiter at the Botanical Gardens , which showed us that we need be under no great concern , were hemp and flax exterminated from the vegetable world , and silkworms to leave off being spinsters , as we could dress cheaply and well on plantain fibre , have capital paper and excellent ropes , etc", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I \u2019 m sure , if we had sheep , and flax , and pasturage , and I had a loom , and the house full of blankets , and sheets , and nice things , all given to us , I shouldn \u2019 t be half so happy as I am in trying to get them", "context_len": 57}}}, "exterminated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "and I have just come from a loiter at the Botanical Gardens , which showed us that we need be under no great concern , were hemp and flax exterminated from the vegetable world , and silkworms to leave off being spinsters , as we could dress cheaply and well on plantain fibre , have capital paper and excellent ropes , etc", "context_len": 62}}}, "silkworms": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "and I have just come from a loiter at the Botanical Gardens , which showed us that we need be under no great concern , were hemp and flax exterminated from the vegetable world , and silkworms to leave off being spinsters , as we could dress cheaply and well on plantain fibre , have capital paper and excellent ropes , etc", "context_len": 62}}}, "spinsters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "and I have just come from a loiter at the Botanical Gardens , which showed us that we need be under no great concern , were hemp and flax exterminated from the vegetable world , and silkworms to leave off being spinsters , as we could dress cheaply and well on plantain fibre , have capital paper and excellent ropes , etc", "context_len": 62}}}, "plantain": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "and I have just come from a loiter at the Botanical Gardens , which showed us that we need be under no great concern , were hemp and flax exterminated from the vegetable world , and silkworms to leave off being spinsters , as we could dress cheaply and well on plantain fibre , have capital paper and excellent ropes , etc", "context_len": 62}}}, "francs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Charlotte \u2019 s warlike enthusiasm showed itself in the fact that she was willing to pay twenty-five francs apiece for seats at the Champs de Mars , where they might witness the review of 45,000 French troops", "context_len": 37}}}, "surpasses": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "From all this it becomes evident , that the Creek verb surpasses in its large power of polysynthesis the Algonkin , Dakota and Kalapuya verb , and in the richness of its forms approaches closely to the Iroquois verb , which is poorer in tenses , but has an impersonal conjugation and fourteen persons to each tense of the finite verb", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "No power in the world surpasses that of a mother over her children , whether she be English or Hindu or Muhammadan", "context_len": 22}}}, "triumphal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "All in its gala dress as it is now , swarming with people , crowded with soldiers , gay with fluttering flags and triumphal arches , -- it is really a sight in itself", "context_len": 34}}}, "arches": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "They had stories to tell him about the famous hanging gardens , which were artificially constructed in the most magnificent manner , on arches raised high in the air ; and about a vine made of gold , with all sorts of precious stones upon it instead of fruit , which was wrought as an ornament over the throne on which the King of Persia often gave audience ; of the splendid palaces and vast cities of the Persians ; and the banquets , and f\u00eates , and magnificent entertainments and celebrations which they used to have there", "context_len": 98}}}, "illuminating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "and I so enjoyed this exquisite evening in the stately gardens ! A fine military band was performing , the people were happily listening , little children skipping about , the glorious sunset tints illuminating a palace fit for the grand Monarch", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It 's only some illuminating red fire", "context_len": 7}}}, "baba": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "-- I told a fat funny little French baba to-day that I had a niece younger than herself , and asked her if she would not like to see her", "context_len": 30}}}, "outbreak": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 31, "context": "Everything portended it ; the atmosphere was absolutely still , not a twig or even a leaf stirred , all nature seemed to be waiting in breathless suspense for the coming outbreak ; even the insects had ceased to attack us , and had retired to their leafy retreat , and the air was so heavy and close that , naked as I was , I perspired at every pore", "context_len": 70}}}, "carnage": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "Then Alfgar saw what was the marvellous power of Christianity , and beheld a heroism utterly beyond the fierce excitement which nerved his countrymen for their scenes of carnage and blood ; not one of his pagan friends could have suffered as calmly , as patiently -- it seemed easier for the sufferer to bear than for Alfgar to look on ; once or twice the latter gave audible vent to his emotions , but the look which Bertric turned upon him spoke volumes , and he restrained himself lest he should add to the pain of the victim", "context_len": 99}}}, "myriads": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 65, "context": "It was a bright , sunny afternoon , yet not too warm to be pleasant , the air was gay with the hum of bees and butterflies , the blue sky , dappled with fleecy clouds , was reflected in the clear water , mingled with the shadow of the rocks and trees ; swallows skimmed over the surface of the lake , chasing the myriads of insects which hummed in the summer air ; and as the carriage drove along the road which lay between the water and the great overhanging rocks , more than one fish was seen to dart swiftly away from the shady pool where he had been snugly lying till disturbed by the rumble of the wheels", "context_len": 122}}}, "patriotism": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "If Charlotte \u2019 s patriotism had been stirred by the Crimean struggle , this came nearer to her yet ! She had five brothers , all in India , all more or less in daily peril", "context_len": 36}}}, "unsafe": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Lacombe 's Cree or Knisteno Dictionary gives : maskek marsh , swamp , trembling ground unsafe to walk upon ; Maskekowiyiniw the Maskegons or Bogmen , a tribe of Crees , also called Maskekowok , who were formerly Odshibw\u0113 Indians , but left Lake Superior to join the Crees ; their name forms a striking parallel to our southern Maskoki", "context_len": 60}}}, "orphaned": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "A little colored boy , the sole orphaned remainder of a long line of masters of the violin , alone of the army of negroes who had borne the family name , is left to wait upon the old mistress and Miss Patrice at the `` Great House", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "And this was the home to which Chris had come -- to the grandparents who had cared for his orphaned boyhood , and whom he never would have left but for the more certain prospect of well-paid work across the water", "context_len": 41}}}, "ministered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "On Sundays he was in the habit of regularly addressing a collected crowd of Natives ; literally the poor , the maimed , the halt , the blind \u2019 ; and he did not teach them only , but also ministered liberally to their bodily needs", "context_len": 46}}}, "restricting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "In her little sketch Charlotte says of him , -- Careless of his own comfort , restricting his personal expenses to a very narrow compass , he gave to the Missionary cause at the rate of forty pounds monthly , and one year even more \u2019 ; adding that with shrinking from ostentation \u2019 he had never given his name on these occasions", "context_len": 63}}}, "ostentation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "In her little sketch Charlotte says of him , -- Careless of his own comfort , restricting his personal expenses to a very narrow compass , he gave to the Missionary cause at the rate of forty pounds monthly , and one year even more \u2019 ; adding that with shrinking from ostentation \u2019 he had never given his name on these occasions", "context_len": 63}}}, "restricted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "His example , long after , was closely copied by this sister , when she too restricted her personal expenses to a very narrow compass , \u2019 precisely as he had done , and with the same object , that she might have the more to give away", "context_len": 48}}}, "unforeseen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "In June 1857 , like a thunder-clap , not indeed utterly unforeseen but practically unexpected by the majority of Englishmen , came the fearful outbreak ; and for a while it did really almost seem that the British Raj in India was at an end", "context_len": 45}}}, "undeceived": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "But they were soon fatally undeceived , for so perfect had been Edmund 's arrangements , that a storm of arrows burst from all parts of the building and embankment , laying nearly half the assailants dying or wounded on the ground", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Tremblingly she put aside the cloth with which they had veiled it , and was undeceived", "context_len": 16}}}, "intrepid": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Dick 's voice failed suddenly ; what use to torture the unhappy wife of the regicide with the story of his trial and condemnation ? He could not convey to her the intrepid composure , the exulting pride with which Harrison justified the deed for which he was arraigned", "context_len": 49}}}, "landholders": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The lawyer wrote that matters would probably be settled in favor of the present landholders , but that , so long as litigation pended , all titles were so clouded as to make any questions of loans untenable", "context_len": 38}}}, "prodigality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "All who knew him well mourn in him the loss of a true and noble friend , generous even to prodigality , highly talented , a thorough gentleman , and an upright judge", "context_len": 33}}}, "access": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 14, "context": "And that reminds me , Dick , '' she added , with a sudden access of gravity , `` if you had not been clever enough to save my life when the snake bit me , you would most certainly have been one of the victims ; indeed it was with a view to sacrificing you at the Customs that my father accepted you from the Igbo", "context_len": 67}}}, "humanly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Upon its success , humanly speaking , may hang the safety of all our beloved ones in India", "context_len": 18}}}, "verbally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Dearest Mother tries not to think more of India than she can help , and has , I am glad to say , given up reading the papers , so we only give her the good part of the news verbally", "context_len": 41}}}, "excess": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 69, "context": "When he returned to his country , he abode all this time , bewailing that which he had endured of toil and stress , so he might compass the lamp , yet had his travail all been wasted and the morsel had escaped from his hand , after it had reached his mouth ; and he still thought upon all this , bemoaning himself and reviling Alaeddin of the excess of his anger against him ; and whiles he said in himself , `` Since yonder whoreson is dead under the earth , I am content withal and I have hopes of the lamp , that I may yet achieve it , inasmuch as it is still safeguarded", "context_len": 118}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "He became very fond of convivial entertainments and of wine , and often drank to excess", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` But the company is responsible only up to fifty dollars , when they do n't pay excess", "context_len": 18}}}, "attributes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The Almighty , to whom my brother attributes the glory , has hitherto watched over Benares in so marked a manner , that it remained quiet in the midst of disturbances ; and my young niece has bravely ridden through it by her father \u2019 s side , giving confidence to the timid by her fearlessness", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "Hardly knowing what he did , Alfgar obeyed , and when he stood face to face with the stranger , began to lose the uneasy impression that the being who addressed him was otherwise than mortal ; for he saw by the light of the lamp that the gleeman bore all the attributes of a living man", "context_len": 57}}}, "fearlessness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "The Almighty , to whom my brother attributes the glory , has hitherto watched over Benares in so marked a manner , that it remained quiet in the midst of disturbances ; and my young niece has bravely ridden through it by her father \u2019 s side , giving confidence to the timid by her fearlessness", "context_len": 56}}}, "informs": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 71, "context": "Harold 's principal achievement lay in The Walrus and the Carpenter , and Marie-Celeste 's in the recitation of Jabberwocky from Through the Looking-Glass ; for not only was she able to slip its almost unpronounceable words quite easily from her tongue , but she remembered the explanation of them given by Humpty Dumpty , when Alice appeals to him a little later on in the story , and he modestly informs her that he can explain all the poems that ever were invented , and a good many beside that have n't been invented just yet", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "We have a meeting of the syndicate on Wednesday evening , and a letter from the colonel gives your name and informs us that you will be on deck with the bullion from the test run of the mill", "context_len": 39}}}, "fascinations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Probably none who have not experienced it for themselves can quite understand the fascinations of authorship", "context_len": 16}}}, "fullest": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 76, "context": "But in carrying a city by storm , which takes place usually at an unexpected time , and often in the night , the maddened and victorious assaulter suddenly burst into the sacred scenes of domestic peace , and seclusion , and love -- the very worst of men , filled with the worst of passions , stimulated by the resistance they have encountered , and licensed by their victory to give all these passions the fullest and most unrestricted gratification", "context_len": 81}}}, "busiest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "Eighteen years of that Afternoon still lay between the dark days of the Indian Mutiny and her own going out to India , for the Evening of her Life , -- the fourth and last eighteen years , which were to be the fullest and the busiest of all her busy days", "context_len": 52}}}, "rapids": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 38, "context": "Again and again he had viewed the fall , entranced , as the spray-crested torrent dashed over the lip of the cliff into the whirling vortex of water beneath that formed the first of the series of great rapids rushing onward to the river", "context_len": 44}}}, "requisition": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "As the heaviest part of his work at headquarters was over , and as pretty much everything had depended on the reply to General Forrest 's requisition on his superior officer -- who , unfortunately , chanced to be General Bragg -- for arms and ammunition , Harry had no difficulty in securing leave of absence for the day ; and so , when all the arrangements had been made , we set out the next evening for the house where Jack Bledsoe lay", "context_len": 84}}}, "typically": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The late-comer was typically Dutch as far as his clothes and appearance went , but his face was that of Malcolm 's acquaintance on the Muizenburg train", "context_len": 27}}}, "intangible": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "It is an intangible , uplifting something , that sensation that men call self-respect , and when they lose it they seem to lose the capacity for any happiness worth the name , and when they can not be persuaded to make an effort to get it back again , there seems to be little enough that they 're good for", "context_len": 61}}}, "influential": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 37, "context": "Macedon , as will be seen on the following map , was in the northern part of the country occupied by the Greeks , and the most powerful states of the confederacy and all the great and influential cities were south of it", "context_len": 43}}}, "retains": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Tucker retains an honoured place in memories of these and later days as the kindest and most liberal of old aunts , -- so she desired me to designate her , and at once adopted me into her very large circle of favoured nephews and nieces , -- the inexhaustible source of varied goodnesses , especially such as were of the most approved edible nature", "context_len": 65}}}, "designate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "Tucker retains an honoured place in memories of these and later days as the kindest and most liberal of old aunts , -- so she desired me to designate her , and at once adopted me into her very large circle of favoured nephews and nieces , -- the inexhaustible source of varied goodnesses , especially such as were of the most approved edible nature", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The plural forms : tchilok\u00f3ga and tchilokog\u00e1lgi designate in Creek persons speaking another than the Creek language ; tchil\u00f3k\u00e4s I speak an alien language", "context_len": 24}}}, "nephews": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 29, "context": "Stanton understood it no better than she did , perhaps not so well ; but he was very much amused ; and as he thought these young nieces and nephews of his were very wonderful little beings , he told Aunt Patty many of their droll sayings and doings , making himself so agreeable and entertaining , that by the time his sister came in , the old lady had almost forgotten that she had cause to be offended , and was not only quite ready to meet Mrs", "context_len": 89}}}, "nieces": {"freq": 29, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 29, "word_index": 27, "context": "Stanton understood it no better than she did , perhaps not so well ; but he was very much amused ; and as he thought these young nieces and nephews of his were very wonderful little beings , he told Aunt Patty many of their droll sayings and doings , making himself so agreeable and entertaining , that by the time his sister came in , the old lady had almost forgotten that she had cause to be offended , and was not only quite ready to meet Mrs", "context_len": 89}}}, "inexhaustible": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "Tucker retains an honoured place in memories of these and later days as the kindest and most liberal of old aunts , -- so she desired me to designate her , and at once adopted me into her very large circle of favoured nephews and nieces , -- the inexhaustible source of varied goodnesses , especially such as were of the most approved edible nature", "context_len": 65}}}, "edible": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 63, "context": "Tucker retains an honoured place in memories of these and later days as the kindest and most liberal of old aunts , -- so she desired me to designate her , and at once adopted me into her very large circle of favoured nephews and nieces , -- the inexhaustible source of varied goodnesses , especially such as were of the most approved edible nature", "context_len": 65}}}, "dissociated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "They can not be dissociated in the memory of the generations of young people , whose privilege it was to be entertained and gratified by their unwearied attention throughout many a long holiday afternoon and evening , while stuffed by Mrs", "context_len": 41}}}, "boundlessness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "As we grew older , we not only more fully understood the exceptional boundlessness of old-fashioned hospitality and kindness which that house and household exemplified thoroughly , but we came to understand somewhat of the heart-source whence issued that truest manifestation , of everyday religion , which evidences itself in an absolutely unselfish consecration , -- consistent , unreserved , and essentially practical , -- for everyday wear , and not only under stimulating environments", "context_len": 75}}}, "exemplified": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "As we grew older , we not only more fully understood the exceptional boundlessness of old-fashioned hospitality and kindness which that house and household exemplified thoroughly , but we came to understand somewhat of the heart-source whence issued that truest manifestation , of everyday religion , which evidences itself in an absolutely unselfish consecration , -- consistent , unreserved , and essentially practical , -- for everyday wear , and not only under stimulating environments", "context_len": 75}}}, "consecration": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "As we grew older , we not only more fully understood the exceptional boundlessness of old-fashioned hospitality and kindness which that house and household exemplified thoroughly , but we came to understand somewhat of the heart-source whence issued that truest manifestation , of everyday religion , which evidences itself in an absolutely unselfish consecration , -- consistent , unreserved , and essentially practical , -- for everyday wear , and not only under stimulating environments", "context_len": 75}}}, "unreserved": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "All being now ready , the gate in the palisade was thrown open , a conch- shell was blown , and the waiting inhabitants began to pour into the enclosure with all the eagerness and excitement of an audience crowding into the unreserved portions of a theatre , and in a very short time the great square was full , the front ranks pressing close up to a cordon of armed guards that had been drawn round the circle of posts", "context_len": 81}}}, "environments": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 74, "context": "As we grew older , we not only more fully understood the exceptional boundlessness of old-fashioned hospitality and kindness which that house and household exemplified thoroughly , but we came to understand somewhat of the heart-source whence issued that truest manifestation , of everyday religion , which evidences itself in an absolutely unselfish consecration , -- consistent , unreserved , and essentially practical , -- for everyday wear , and not only under stimulating environments", "context_len": 75}}}, "ageing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Such was the life \u2019 s lesson which our association with these two now ageing sisters suggested to us", "context_len": 19}}}, "impelled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 80, "context": "Tucker could not quite keep pace with the new-fashioned unconventionalities of young-lady work in London ; and one of the object-sermons , which most impressed me in my College days , was the beautiful self-restraint which these two sisters -- no longer young -- imposed upon themselves , in deference to their aged Mother \u2019 s wishes , in regard to that outside work which inclination , or one might say conviction , as well as opportunity and qualifications , impelled them to participate in", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It might give the girl pain , indeed , to hear what he felt impelled to tell her ; it is not pleasant to have a broken bone set , yet the end is a good one", "context_len": 37}}}, "juveniles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Still the unbounded hospitality of the open house in Portland Place went on ; and still they were content to devote their time , talents , and energies to successive generations of juveniles and elder guests , without a murmur", "context_len": 40}}}, "abided": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "But she patiently abided her time ; and she did not wait in vain", "context_len": 14}}}, "pretensions": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 35, "context": "But , though Alexander was thus willing to impress his ignorant soldiers with a mysterious veneration for his fictitious divinity , he was not deceived himself on the subject ; he sometimes even made his pretensions to the divine character a subject of joke", "context_len": 44}}}, "slurred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "This at present was a main duty of her life , and she never neglected or slurred it", "context_len": 18}}}, "judgments": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Still , as ever , she was guided and restrained by her Mother \u2019 s wishes , yielding her own desires when the two wills , or the two judgments , happened to lie in opposite directions", "context_len": 37}}}, "knitter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Although not really fond of work , Charlotte was a beautiful knitter", "context_len": 12}}}, "antimacassars": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "She would make most elaborate antimacassars , of delicate lace-like patterns , invented by her own busy brain ; and while working thus she was able to read Shakespeare aloud", "context_len": 30}}}, "patterns": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 118, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "infection": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 59, "context": "One day I came down to breakfast , exclaiming , How beautiful the snow is ! -- when she told me how pleased she was that I could say so , instead of saying , How cold it is ! When I was ill in the year 1872 , she often came to see me , quite disregarding the infection of my throat ; she would play her guitar to me , or , as I grew better , would patiently guide my little fingers to the right places on the strings", "context_len": 92}}}, "famed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was a strange reaction as he hung there -- he , the brave and daring swimmer , famed for his dives off Carn Du , held up by the man he had always denounced as a terrible coward ; whom he had hated from boyhood almost , without cause , and whom really , under the impulse of a horrible temptation , he had on the previous night tried to hamper in his swimming , though not really to drown", "context_len": 81}}}, "untidiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Fanny was of middle height , and thin , with dark eyes ; very neat and orderly in her ways , wherein she was the opposite of Charlotte , who was famed for untidiness in her arrangements", "context_len": 37}}}, "tidiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Charlotte was , however , methodical in plans of action , and in literary work ; and later in life she seems to have struggled hard after habits of greater tidiness , as a matter of principle", "context_len": 37}}}, "scrupulously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "[ Footnote 116 : So that they might hang down and hide his feet and hands , it being a point of Arab etiquette for an inferior scrupulously to avoid showing either of these members in presenting himself ( especially for the first time ) before his superior", "context_len": 48}}}, "debarred": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "In adultery and murder cases the relatives of the gens of the injured party alone had the right of judging and of taking satisfaction ; the m\u00edko and his council were debarred from any interference", "context_len": 35}}}, "conquering": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "He organized armies , crossed the boundary between Europe and Asia , and spent the twelve years of his career in a most triumphant military incursion into the very center and seat of Asiatic power , destroying the Asiatic armies , conquering the most splendid cities , defeating or taking captive the kings , and princes , and generals that opposed his progress", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "We have the most sure word of prophecy that the Day of the Lord is at hand ; therefore the persecuted remnant who do expect the coming of the Fifth and only Monarch , are even now assembled with their swords upon their thighs , to publish their glorious gospel and go forth conquering and to conquer", "context_len": 57}}}, "hermitage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Here she would sit and compose her books ; but she made of it no hermitage", "context_len": 16}}}, "adjunct": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Writing was with her , as we have seen , not the main business of life , but merely an adjunct , -- an additional means of usefulness", "context_len": 28}}}, "uninterrupted": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 67, "context": "The voluminous pages of Father Cuthbert 's diary for the years of bitter woe and misery which followed can not be fully transcribed ; they would fill a volume themselves , and we must content ourselves with a few extracts , which will probably interest our readers , and carry on the thread of the history to the place where our narrative will again flow free and uninterrupted", "context_len": 68}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Yes , he should be uninterrupted , and should proceed at his leisure , and give up the articles which were simply wearing him into an early grave", "context_len": 28}}}, "annoyance": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 52, "context": "A battalion picture-theatre , billiard rooms , voluntary swimming parades , boxing , and a variety of other indoor and outdoor games contributed to the men 's enjoyment ; and , although discipline was well enforced , there was a total absence of irritating petty restrictions that form a constant source of annoyance to the men of the New Armies of the Motherland", "context_len": 63}}}, "undeserving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Often the work itself is of supreme importance ; the interruptions are unnecessary and undeserving of attention", "context_len": 17}}}, "wiriness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Despite her somewhat fragile appearance , and an appetite commonly small , there must have been a marvellous amount of underlying strength , -- of the wiriness \u2019 which often belongs to delicate-looking people", "context_len": 34}}}, "hippopotami": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Chaillu , plunging amongst hippopotami and crocodiles , demolishing big black serpents , or perhaps capturing a baby-gorilla , more troublesome than dear Edgy himself", "context_len": 25}}}, "crocodiles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Chaillu , plunging amongst hippopotami and crocodiles , demolishing big black serpents , or perhaps capturing a baby-gorilla , more troublesome than dear Edgy himself", "context_len": 25}}}, "demolishing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Chaillu , plunging amongst hippopotami and crocodiles , demolishing big black serpents , or perhaps capturing a baby-gorilla , more troublesome than dear Edgy himself", "context_len": 25}}}, "serpents": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Ye have not fought like men , but crept on like serpents , and slain those who , trusting to the faith of Christians , dwelt blindly in our midst", "context_len": 31}}}, "refined": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "We are all just now in a state of indignation about your pork ! Don \u2019 t suppose that it is any fault in the pork ; on the contrary , it is acknowledged to be the most refined pork ever known ; and Mother says that if she shut her eyes , she would not know that she was not eating chicken ! ! We had a beautiful roast of it one day at luncheon ; and Mother cut off a choice bit , to be reserved for our table , cold , while the servants were indulged with the rest of that joint", "context_len": 105}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 40, "context": "You do not know what bitter feelings I had in my heart , for they really were undeniably some of the most attractive children you could possibly find ; and Florence was so witty , and Janet so delicate and refined and sweet in all her ways ! I could not be angry with them , but I did think it fearfully unfair that so many blessings should be poured on their heads and so few given to me , for I was not even specially clever", "context_len": 87}}}, "disclaim": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Miss Charlotte to be disappointed of her reserved bit of pork ! I can hardly keep my countenance , but of course must not disclaim my interest in the question", "context_len": 30}}}, "alligators": {"freq": 26, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 26, "word_index": 45, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}}}, "bridal": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 85, "context": "`` Now Alaeddin had meanwhile gone in to his bride , the Lady Bedrulbudour , to acquaint her with the coming of her father the Sultan ; and when he returned , the Sultan said to him , `` O my son Alaeddin , what is the reason that the lattice [ -work ] of yonder oriel [ 513 ] is not completed ? '' `` O King of the Age , '' replied Alaeddin , `` by reason of the haste made with the bridal , the craftsmen might not avail to [ 514 ] finish it", "context_len": 98}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "As for Alaeddin , he went out that day and saw the rejoicings toward in the city and the palace and fell a-laughing , especially when he heard the folk speak of the honour which had betided the Vizier 's son and the greatness of his good luck , in that he was become the Sultan 's son-in-law , and the exceeding pomp used in his marriage and bridal festivities ; and he said in himself , `` Ye know not , good simple folk that ye are , [ 403 ] what befell him last night , that ye envy him", "context_len": 102}}}, "brevity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "And certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most", "context_len": 128}}}, "transition": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "The first part , We hail thee , has a transition , which we fear that the rules of thorough-bass might not permit ; and the Thistle is hardly equal to either the Shamrock or the Rose , -- of which , you see , I would make a separate song and hymn", "context_len": 53}}}, "unmercifully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I hope that you will excuse my thus venturing to criticise your song and so unmercifully to cut it short", "context_len": 20}}}, "sweetens": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The Lord He is my strength and stay , When sorrow \u2019 s cup o \u2019 erflows the brim ; It sweetens all if we can say , This is from Him ! All comfort , comfort , flows from Him", "context_len": 41}}}, "flows": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "The Lord He is my strength and stay , When sorrow \u2019 s cup o \u2019 erflows the brim ; It sweetens all if we can say , This is from Him ! All comfort , comfort , flows from Him", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Ah , well you know me , my countrymen , for a true Englishman ! -- one in whose veins your blood flows , and who will be only too happy to fight the Danish wolves at your head", "context_len": 40}}}, "glorified": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Even the gulls and shags that floated on the tide seemed to be glorified by the wondrous colour , till Harry , as he sat there with the stout cord of his fishing-line twisted round his hand , felt how majestic and awe-inspiring was the coming of the new-born day , and involuntarily exclaimed :", "context_len": 55}}}, "comfortingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I talked to her as comfortingly as I could , and told her that I thought this sad trial might be sent that she might be like Christiana , walking on a Heavenward path , with all her children with her", "context_len": 41}}}, "tearless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I was glad to draw forth one or two tears , for tearless anguish is the most terrible", "context_len": 18}}}, "disciples": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "She saw the angry disciples trying to send them away , and the face of the dear Saviour of the whole world as , taking one after the other of these lambs in His arms , He said , `` Suffer the little children to come unto Me , and forbid them not , for of such is the kingdom of heaven", "context_len": 62}}}, "inaccessible": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "After the death of Birinus , the zeal of his successors failed to evangelise the southeastern districts of Wessex , until , at length , came Wilfrid , fervent in zeal , and , stationing himself at Selsey , near Chichester , evangelised both Sussex and Wessex , sending out missionaries like-minded with himself , even into the most inaccessible wilds", "context_len": 61}}}, "texts": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "This sort of incapsulation greatly embarrasses interpreters in the rendering of Creek texts in any of the modern European languages , which have a tendency towards analytic and an aversion to synthetic structure of the sentence , and therefore use conjunctions freely", "context_len": 42}}}, "bedridden": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "They are more , I think , for the bedridden", "context_len": 10}}}, "consoling": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "Archie caught a severe chill hunting for microscopical specimens in the pond , and was in bed for some weeks , nursed by his doting aunt , consoling himself for his enforced idleness by planning such improvements at both the Willows and the Abbey as were calculated to make their owners ' hair stand on end", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The one consoling thought was that should he be forced to support himself , it would be only for a few strokes", "context_len": 22}}}, "employments": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Among lesser employments , she is said to have spent hours at a time in looking through her papers and letters -- the collection of a literary lifetime -- and consigning masses of the same to destruction", "context_len": 37}}}, "lightened": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "When [ 379 ] Alaeddin heard his mother 's words and how the Sultan had appointed her for [ 380 ] three months [ thence ] , his heart was lightened and he rejoiced with an exceeding joy and said , `` Since the Sultan hath promised for [ 381 ] three months [ hence ] , true , it [ 382 ] is long , but in any case my joy is great", "context_len": 74}}}, "loquacious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Dear Fanny will probably not start for Brighton till Wednesday week , so she will have the pleasure of welcoming you , and I am sure that you will try not to let her be loquacious", "context_len": 36}}}, "holiest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "I am going to have it printed by converted Jews , and the entire profits devoted to the Society for the Conversion of Jews ; so that it will be a little offering from us both to one of the holiest of causes", "context_len": 43}}}, "deigns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "If we could only feel in our hearts that He really does love us , and that He deigns to care whether we love Him , what a motive it would be for doing everything as in His sight ! We are too apt to think of our Saviour as very far off , and with so many to care for that we are almost beneath His notice", "context_len": 68}}}, "dewdrop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "draws": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 19, "context": "His face had in it that indescribable quality -- a touch of suffering or of sorrow -- that always draws me , and I thought how strange it was that he should sit there ignorant of the fact that a word or two would make me his friend for life", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Well , they are the Danish war ships , and our hour of peril draws near", "context_len": 17}}}, "glitters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Each silver thread that glitters in the hair , Is like a wayside landmark , -- planted there To show Earth \u2019 s pilgrims , as they onward wend , How nearly they approach their journey \u2019 s end ! \u2019", "context_len": 41}}}, "landmark": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Each silver thread that glitters in the hair , Is like a wayside landmark , -- planted there To show Earth \u2019 s pilgrims , as they onward wend , How nearly they approach their journey \u2019 s end ! \u2019", "context_len": 41}}}, "auburn": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "One who knew them both well describes the contrast between aunt and niece at this period , -- Charlotte Tucker , so upright and animated , very thin , fair , with auburn hair , not very abundant , but which curled slightly , naturally , \u2019 -- and Letitia , grave , with beautiful dark eyes and hair , and rather dark complexion", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 121, "context": "How came it that this girl had guessed his secret at once when most people passed him , taking him but for a sturdy beggar ? What made her suspect him of being a fugitive ? Was her offer of shelter but careless good nature , or a heroic endeavour to save a hunted man ? At any rate he had not fallen so low as to draw suspicion on a woman , and a young woman to boot , although she was plainly no nervous , fanciful , fine lady , but a bright , resolute , country girl , with good health and high spirits gleaming from every flash of her bright eyes , and every turn of her auburn head", "context_len": 123}}}, "proverb": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 77, "context": "This , then , is what befell Alaeddin with the Maugrabin ; but Alaeddin , for all this , was not altogether [ 625 ] quit of the accursed enchanter , withal his body had been burned and given to the winds ; for that the accursed one had a brother viler than he [ and yet more skilled ] in magic and geomancy and astrology ; [ nay , they were even ] as saith the proverb , `` A bean and it was cloven in twain ; '' [ 626 ] and each dwelt in one quarter of the world , so they might fill it [ 627 ] with their sorcery and craft and guile", "context_len": 118}}}, "conquers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "A favourite proverb of Letitia \u2019 s was -- Perseverance conquers difficulties \u2019 ; -- and it would have served equally well for A", "context_len": 24}}}, "knell": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "For in my sinking heart there beat An ever-sounding knell ; -- But still I knew the promise sweet , Jesus , Immanuel !", "context_len": 24}}}, "quell": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "In 1876 he had left it , as he thought , for good ; but , as it turned out , it was only for a few weeks ' holiday in England , and then back to quell the rebellion", "context_len": 40}}}, "hothouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "She wrote it when on the Red Sea , which she evidently found very warm , for she described the ship as a hothouse , and said that she and her fellow-passengers would be fine exotics before they arrived", "context_len": 39}}}, "exotics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "She wrote it when on the Red Sea , which she evidently found very warm , for she described the ship as a hothouse , and said that she and her fellow-passengers would be fine exotics before they arrived", "context_len": 39}}}, "harmonium": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "Sure enough , the rogues had bitten holes in that , and pulled out wadding to stuff into my harmonium , doubtless to make a comfortable nest for a family of young mice or rats", "context_len": 35}}}, "timbrel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Sound the loud timbrel o \u2019 er Egypt \u2019 s dark sea , Jehovah hath triumphed , His people are free !", "context_len": 22}}}, "shedding": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "We walked beyond the discolored barn which was shedding its shingles as a pony sheds his hair , and came to a dull old orchard where some quite nice crab apple trees stood knee deep in selfish weeds that were taking the goodness out of the soil", "context_len": 47}}}, "mauve": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "What a bright account you give of your dear busy young party ! Tell dear Otho that I shall be charmed if he makes the discovery of a magenta-coloured caterpillar , or a mauve earwig ; and that as it will be ten times as curious as the Spongmenta Padella , it ought to have a Latin name ten times as long", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 67, "context": "Below the house the meadow sloped down to a river , where a stone bridge led to the village , with its pretty thatched cottages and Norman church , whose square tower stood up like a beacon for the surrounding country ; and away in the distance , tier upon tier , rose the Welsh mountains , fading from green to purple or from purple to misty mauve , till the last were lost in the hazy blue of the sky", "context_len": 81}}}, "earwig": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "What a bright account you give of your dear busy young party ! Tell dear Otho that I shall be charmed if he makes the discovery of a magenta-coloured caterpillar , or a mauve earwig ; and that as it will be ten times as curious as the Spongmenta Padella , it ought to have a Latin name ten times as long", "context_len": 62}}}, "budget": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Hartley , who had seated herself in the nearest chair , awaiting the budget of news that Chris always endeavored to bring out from Nuneham , for the enlivening of the old people", "context_len": 33}}}, "perusal": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 63, "context": "He knew what it was to be without peace ; for having got into debt when he was first in the army , and knowing the distress it caused his family at home , his mind was so troubled that he wrote to his mother : `` Oh , what agony I have endured ! What sleepless nights I have passed since the perusal of that letter ! The review of my past life , especially the retrospect of the last two years , has at last quite startled me , and at the same time disgusted me", "context_len": 98}}}, "acquisition": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "Ben was obliging , and , finding he could give pleasure , he generally complied with the request of the miners and rehearsed such songs as he knew , so that he was considered a decided acquisition by the little company , and his popularity was unbounded", "context_len": 47}}}, "bonny": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "It means a good deal when a man gives all his love to a woman , and I give it all to you ; and when everything is said and done , Westenra , bonny as you are , and lovely , and dainty as you are , you are only a woman and I am only a man", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "Merrily laughed the bonny blue-bell at my proposition that , in addition to the fine of 100 imposed on Mission Miss Sahibas who marry within three years of coming out , it should be part of the contract that they should have all their hair shaved off on the day before the wedding", "context_len": 53}}}, "erysipelas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The illness had been short , -- a severe attack of erysipelas , while Letitia was in her brother \u2019 s house at Moultan", "context_len": 24}}}, "bereavement": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "MY OWN DEAREST LAURA , -- Your dear letters have been very soothing to our Charlotte , and have helped to remind her of the mercies mingled with the bereavement", "context_len": 30}}}, "destine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "I mentioned you , -- for I thought that as her disposition was lively , it would be more easy for her to try to be like you than dear Fanny ; besides she had seen you as a wife and mother , and I did not know whether the Almighty might not destine her to be such", "context_len": 58}}}, "baptized": {"freq": 33, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 73, "context": "peat fire in that lonely cottage , without a sound to break the vast silence , and the knowledge that the nearest human habitation lay fully three miles away ; and Bobby had already asked Peggy if she believed in ghosts , and whether it was true what Joe had told him that the lights you sometimes see at night hovering over a bog are the souls of children who have never been baptized ; and though Peggy had professed to laugh at the supernatural , she did not feel quite so brave as she pretended , and found little cold shivers stealing down her back when the wind rose suddenly , and began to wail round the cottage like a hungry creature waiting to be let in", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "I hope that in another month we may number fifty baptized persons in Batala ; and I have lately been writing out the heading for a Subscription for a Church at our dear Batala", "context_len": 34}}}, "ordain": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "And so Thou didst ordain , O Lord , as Thou didst deem it best , -- That hers should be the earlier dawn , and hers the earlier rest", "context_len": 30}}}, "embossed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "The attire of Edric was similar in shape , but of different colour ; his tunic was of green , edged with brown fur , his mantle of dark cloth , and his belt of embossed leather", "context_len": 37}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "Now his mother was with the Lady Bedrulbudour , and when he came up and did off her veil , she fell to gazing upon the bride 's beauty and grace and looked at the pavilion , the which was all wroughten [ 497 ] of gold and jewels and therein were golden lustres , all embossed with emeralds and rubies ; and she said in herself , `` Methought the Sultan 's palace was magnificent ; but , for this pavilion [ 498 ] alone , I doubt me the greatest of the Chosroes and the kings never owned its match ; nor , methinketh , might all mankind avail to make the like thereof", "context_len": 116}}}, "fourteenth": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Last Sunday I was unable to leave my tent , but I had happy communion with Jesus in my solitude , and derived much pleasure from the fourteenth and fifteenth of St", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "Oh , the Brotherhood of Knights ! That is what an Order is , you know , and this one was founded way back in the fourteenth century , in the time of Edward the Third ; and they say the way it came to be called the Order of the Garter was this : That King Edward was dancing with the Countess of Salisbury , when she had the misfortune to lose her garter ; and then as he stooped to pick it up , and saw every one smiling , he gallantly announced , 'that they should shortly see that garter advanced to so high an honor and renown as to account themselves happy to wear it", "context_len": 119}}}, "sittings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "John in two sittings of about an hour and twenty minutes each", "context_len": 12}}}, "magenta": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "As I gazed with something like awe on its wondrous beauty , suddenly its colour utterly changed ; the magenta became white , with a greenish tinge ; and then -- as suddenly -- the star disappeared ; not as if hidden by a cloud , but as if put out", "context_len": 51}}}, "greenish": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "As I gazed with something like awe on its wondrous beauty , suddenly its colour utterly changed ; the magenta became white , with a greenish tinge ; and then -- as suddenly -- the star disappeared ; not as if hidden by a cloud , but as if put out", "context_len": 51}}}, "tinge": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 20, "context": "His hair was a deep grizzled red , as also his beard , and his eyes were of the same tinge , his nose somewhat aquiline , and his whole features , weatherworn as they were , were those of one born to command , while they lacked the sheer brutality of expression so conspicuous in some of his subordinates", "context_len": 60}}}, "meteor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "The fire-balloon , however , was the success of the evening , for it shot straight up , and floated across the sky like a beautiful meteor , its pink and green sides giving a charming effect , till at length it dwindled away and became a mere speck in the distance , leaving the children more full of admiration than ever for Archie 's talents", "context_len": 66}}}, "occurs": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 22, "context": "91 : h\u00e2stri to fight , make war ; h\u00e2strir warrior , man ; h\u00e2striryi people , tribe ; but Tensagini also occurs in the texts , which would point to an extensive maize culture", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Occurs in W\u00e1ksi hol\u00e1'hta and other Creek titles , perhaps also in the tribal name of the Waxsaws on Santee river , S", "context_len": 23}}}, "purposeless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Hamilton occurs one little sentence well worth quoting , for it is a sentence which might serve as a motto for many a seemingly empty and even purposeless life --", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Writing of these days many years later , when as a Minister of the Crown he was in attendance upon her Majesty at Balmoral , he says : `` I thought my life was aimless , purposeless , and I wanted something else to do ; but events compelled me to what promised to be a dull life and a useless one : the result is that few men have had more interesting work to do ''", "context_len": 77}}}, "untried": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Though marvellously strong and spirited for her time of life , she was now in her fifty-sixth year ; hardly an age when , at the best , a woman is commonly willing to undertake great responsibilities in a new and untried direction", "context_len": 43}}}, "bookseller": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Your sweet Mother will wonder at not receiving the little book which I promised to send her ; but our bookseller , from whom I ordered the copy , has been unable to get it yet", "context_len": 36}}}, "bearish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Of course I looked with some interest at the illustrations which my Publisher sent me ; but I was not a little surprised in the last one to find one whom I considered to be a man represented as a bear ! He was bearish in character certainly , but still -- certainly not a bear in shape", "context_len": 58}}}, "culled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "A few sentences are culled from the many letters which remain , belonging to this period", "context_len": 16}}}, "tasteless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Sometimes I ask myself , -- Now , in my present position , if I had no pleasure in religion , if everything connected with that were cut off , what would be left me ? -- what would life be to me ? O Leila , what a tasteless , what a bitter thing ! We want delights that will not grow old , that will never pall , that will be just as fresh and lovely at eighty as at eighteen", "context_len": 83}}}, "repays": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It calls indeed for the sacrifice of self-will in a hundred little ways ; but it repays those little sacrifices a hundred times over", "context_len": 24}}}, "stanza": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` We 'll get the company poet to write up a special stanza and recite it at the concert , '' declared Fortescue", "context_len": 23}}}, "strengthening": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "The truth was , that Alexander , though he had the sagacity to see that he was placed in circumstances where prodigious moral effect in strengthening his position would be produced by an act of great severity , was swayed by so many generous impulses , which raised him above the ordinary excitements of irritation and revenge , that he had every desire to make the suffering as light , and to limit it by as narrow bounds , as the nature of the case would allow", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "It creaked like an old man uttering his protest against rheumatic joints , and , in spite of the new leg Phil had put on and the strengthening boards , it threatened to collapse", "context_len": 34}}}, "buckler": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "There \u2019 s rest beneath the yew ; I know There \u2019 s deeper Rest in realms above ; The Saviour \u2019 s Arm the valley through Will me uphold with strengthening love ; My hope His Righteousness ; my buckler , faith ; Why should I fear to tread the shades of death ?", "context_len": 55}}}, "ultimate": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "So it was with no great confidence as to the ultimate outcome that he made his way back to the cabin , surrounded by the lowering bucks of the Araos tribe and feeling the suspicious gaze of Lodo boring into his back", "context_len": 42}}}, "sorer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "His heart was sorer now than ever , for it was torture to him to think of the pretty little Pirah , the daughter of Hondura , who had once succored him , in the clutches of the evil Nascanora", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "in the face of the carelessness with which the text treats kushk as synonymous now with keszr or teyyareh and now with liwan or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this", "context_len": 128}}}, "unanswered": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The letter was unanswered for over a year ; but coming at a time when the man of twenty-five was beginning to find that there were better things to be done in life than cliff-climbing in the country , or giving pleasant parties at Oxford , it wrought its purpose , and formed the first step towards the new life", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "He tossed restlessly about , his mind in a tumult of unanswered questions", "context_len": 13}}}, "parable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Parable , allegory , and metaphor were the very language in which she thought ; and her thoughts always seemed naturally to clothe themselves in those figures of speech in which the children of the East are wont to express themselves", "context_len": 41}}}, "entertains": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` I am sure I can not tell , '' I answered ; `` but as we are out , I hope you will let me look at the shops ; I want to tell mother something about the latest fashions ; it often entertains her", "context_len": 46}}}, "enhances": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Probably every hour of suffering in some mysterious way enhances and increases future rapture , -- rapture more intense than we can conceive", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "There was pleasure in watching them ; for though reason at last convinced one that danger there must be under the circumstances , a touch of fear , or rather sense of danger , rather enhances enjoyment", "context_len": 37}}}, "increases": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "Probably every hour of suffering in some mysterious way enhances and increases future rapture , -- rapture more intense than we can conceive", "context_len": 23}}}, "depriving": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "Why had Nascanora made Hondura captive with Casson and the squaw ? Was it because Hondura and his tribe had been on friendly terms with Bomba and the old naturalist ? Or did Nascanora think that the Araos tribe was becoming too powerful , and had he hoped by depriving them of their chief to render his people helpless and throw confusion and panic into their hearts ?", "context_len": 68}}}, "conferring": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 45, "context": "There is something so touching in his looking at such a moment to the Saviour , whose Blood , shed for his salvation , was at that moment trickling down in his view ; and there is something so sublime in our Lord \u2019 s conferring Eternal Life , -- such a gift , -- at the time when He was Himself undergoing the terrible sentence of death ! We may envy your dear suffering child , my Laura , when we think how soon , in human expectation , his eyes will behold the King in His beauty", "context_len": 99}}}, "overstraining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "by fatigue of body , and overstraining of mind and nerves", "context_len": 11}}}, "permits": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Today I noticed Alfgar , the son of Anlaf , at the high mass , and felt a little discomposed at the relaxation of discipline , which , contrary to the canons of the church , permits the unbaptized , as well as persons who ought rightly to be deemed excommunicate , or at least penitents , to be present at the holy mysteries", "context_len": 64}}}, "tenfold": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "After which he asked Miss Minchin to sit down while he explained matters to her fully , and went into such detail as was necessary to make it quite clear to her that Sara 's future was an assured one , and that what had seemed to be lost was to be restored to her tenfold ; also , that she had in Mr", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "inscriptions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Oh , with what a heavy heavy heart must you have put up those parcels , and written those inscriptions ! It will perhaps be a long time before you can realise with calm thankfulness that it is indeed so well with the child that you can rejoice in his safety , his happiness", "context_len": 54}}}, "fetter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "I have often thought that freed spirits probably lead a life of delightful activity ; none of the burdens of the flesh to fetter them down", "context_len": 26}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 67, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}}}, "strengthens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Peter , to which you refer , certainly strengthens the idea ; for the disciples are permitted in so many ways to follow their Master", "context_len": 25}}}, "mourner": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Peggy followed as chief mourner , her arms full of wreaths and flowers , and a piece of black crape , purloined from the scrap-bag , pinned conspicuously upon her hat", "context_len": 31}}}, "ranges": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The suggested choice ranges between Sir Walter Scott , Felicia Hemans , Jean Ingelow , the Author of The Schonberg-Cotta Family , and Miss Sewell , -- a rather curious mixture", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 26, "context": "Malcolm and Dick found bayonet exercise exciting work -- thrusting at suspended sacks stuffed with straw called for strength and strenuous activity -- while at the ranges both lads gained a high percentage of bulls , and in a very short while the `` crossed rifles `` , denoting marksmanship , ornamented the sleeves of their uniforms", "context_len": 57}}}, "moles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "This seems quite a country for moles", "context_len": 7}}}, "gentlemanly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "She knew they were all too gentlemanly to interrupt or trouble her ; but she thought they might grow tired or think it was like babies ' play ; so she told them they might go if they did not like it", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "He was very nice and kind and gentlemanly", "context_len": 8}}}, "disappoints": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "She says that the ending disappoints her ; she would cut off the last page and the four preceding lines , which would completely alter the whole ending", "context_len": 28}}}, "pamphlet": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Franklin , siding with the people , wrote a pamphlet on The Nature and Necessity of a Paper Currency , which he printed himself , and which swayed the Pennsylvania Assembly to pass a bill to issue such paper currency", "context_len": 40}}}, "obviated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "chignon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "A large Honiton lace veil fell over her pure white silk dress ; her lovely hair plaited , instead of made into an ugly chignon , appeared graceful under the white wreath , from which a spray drooped down her neck", "context_len": 41}}}, "bridesmaids": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}}}, "alpacas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I did not think the bridesmaids looking picturesque ; there was too square a look about the purple trimming of their white alpacas", "context_len": 23}}}, "strew": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "'Of course he will ! I wonder you can ask such a question ! Heaven would n't be heaven unless we found Rollo there ! We 'll wrap his body in the Union Jack , and pick all our best flowers to strew round him ; and you might fire off your old pistol over his grave , if Father will let you have any gunpowder -- a parting salute , you know , like they do for officers , ' said Peggy , cheering up a little at the thought of arranging the obsequies", "context_len": 95}}}, "commences": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "688 ) , was so angry at the long delay in building the monastery , that at first he revoked the grant of his predecessors to Hean , but becoming reconciled , gave all his energy to the work , and Cloveshoo x , or Abingdon , became a monastic town , and its history commences as a house of God from Ina , about A", "context_len": 66}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "I have got a box in a good part of the house , so we need not be there more than a few minutes before it commences", "context_len": 27}}}, "prayerful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Clark and Margaret can not come here ; and we must have a serious , prayerful discussion about what is really very important , and too complicated for letters", "context_len": 29}}}, "telegraphed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Harris , concluding that Donald and Chris were quite able to decide what was best in the matter , telegraphed back , No objection , of course , if you think it advisable ; and its welcome message brought more joy to the hearts of Ted and Donald than they could graciously give expression to in the face of Mr", "context_len": 60}}}, "materially": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "This was a singularly fortunate and opportune discovery , for I had already observed that fever and ague were very prevalent among the inhabitants , and I hoped that if by means of a decoction of cinchona bark I could effect a cure , I might be able very materially to improve and strengthen my position in the town", "context_len": 59}}}, "plumpness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "In true Mexican fashion the look of youth had left her betimes , and her swarthy plumpness had early hardened and settled to a look of maturity to which future years could add little", "context_len": 34}}}, "specify": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "\u2019 She did not specify what manner of imprudence hers had been", "context_len": 12}}}, "imprudence": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "The Missionaries scold each other more for imprudence about health than any other thing , and I am the scold of the party , so that as I preach I must practise", "context_len": 32}}}, "symptoms": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 21, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}}}, "toned": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "But dinners are not so reviving as good news , Donald , said Ted excusingly ; and indeed , notwithstanding diminished rations , he felt wonderfully toned up both in mind and body , now that the good friends in the cottage knew just who he was and there was no longer need for any sort of duplicity", "context_len": 58}}}, "mirthfulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "As time went by , the spirit of fun and mirthfulness revived ; and the little ones in her new home could not fail to be a fresh delight to one who so greatly loved children", "context_len": 36}}}, "myrtle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "A few days ago I made a wreath of myrtle", "context_len": 10}}}, "unfading": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "But mother was dying ; there would be no need soon for any of us to trouble about her future , for God Himself was taking it into His own hands , and had prepared for her a mansion , and an unfading habitation", "context_len": 44}}}, "rural": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "The nations exchanged their commodities far more easily and extensively in consequence of these facilities , and the means of comfort and enjoyment were multiplied and increased in thousands and thousands of huts in the great cities of Egypt , and in the rural districts along the banks of the Nile", "context_len": 51}}}, "groves": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "He tried to call up his cooler judgment , but in vain ; pictures of sunny seas and waving palm groves , of gallant fights and sacked towns danced before him , and his broken slumbers only wove the fancies into dreams", "context_len": 42}}}, "bracelet": {"freq": 94, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 94, "word_index": 65, "context": "But as he looked at the individual whom he had mistaken for a deer he felt a second tremor of excitement , for , as he had a glimpse of his face Bart was made aware that the man was none other than the one about whom there was such a mystery -- the man who had sneaked into the schoolhouse the night the diamond bracelet was stolen -- the midnight visitor at the camp , perhaps", "context_len": 77}}}, "coiled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "I of course knew in an instant what it was -- a snake , that very possibly had been attracted to the spot by the notes of my flute , and , startled by the sudden cessation of the music and Ama 's quick , involuntary movement , had instantly coiled itself round her arm and struck at it in its blind and panic-stricken rage", "context_len": 65}}}, "reptile": {"freq": 50, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 49, "word_index": 38, "context": "Acting upon the impulse of the moment , and scarcely knowing what I was about , with a single bound I flung myself upon the terrified girl and , guided more by instinct than reason , seized the reptile immediately behind the head in so vice-like a grip that its jaws at once opened wide , when I tore its hideous coils from the girl 's arm and flung it far from me into the very heart of the blazing fire", "context_len": 81}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Look out , there 's a snake going to get away ! '' He grabbed the lively reptile just in time , and stuffed it into his bag", "context_len": 28}}}, "hiss": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "He thought it an imitation-one , and said something about good imitations , -- when the reptile began to hiss at him", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "It was as smooth as a pond , except where , with a low moan , it heaved up and beat against Carn Du , falling back with an angry hiss as if of disappointment , while all above looked calm and dark and starlit", "context_len": 45}}}, "thriving": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Baby is now thriving nicely , and getting quite fat", "context_len": 10}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "His own kingdom was in a very prosperous and thriving condition , and his ascendency over the other kingdoms and states on the European side had been fully established", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The thrifty village housewives were doing a thriving business in tea and ginger-pop , for a cattle fair is thirsty work , and the inn was filled to overflowing", "context_len": 29}}}, "overland": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "Shagbark had trapped many winters far up among the wild solitudes of the Rockies , and was so familiar with the overland route that none could be better qualified than he to lead a party over the plains", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- The overland mail was particularly long in arriving this time", "context_len": 11}}}, "boswell": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 0, "context": "BOSWELL", "context_len": 1}}}, "gild": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I tried to gild my own little works with Scripture truths ; but now I have pure gold to give to others , -- without admixing with it any alloy of my own", "context_len": 33}}}, "truths": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "I found that poor Kyneswith had impressed all the primary truths of our holy faith deeply upon his mind , although he wanted much building up , and needed instruction in details ; he seemed deeply impressed by the main facts of the life and teaching of our blessed Lord , particularly His message of peace on earth , good will towards men , contrasting so forcibly with the faith of his own people", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "And from that time forward , it may be mentioned , the elder Miss Minchin actually began to stand a little in awe of a sister who , while she looked so foolish , was evidently not quite so foolish as she looked , and might , consequently , break out and speak truths people did not want to hear", "context_len": 60}}}, "admixing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "I tried to gild my own little works with Scripture truths ; but now I have pure gold to give to others , -- without admixing with it any alloy of my own", "context_len": 33}}}, "alloy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "I tried to gild my own little works with Scripture truths ; but now I have pure gold to give to others , -- without admixing with it any alloy of my own", "context_len": 33}}}, "stilly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I dreamed that in the stilly hush of night -- Deep midnight -- I was startled from my sleep By a clear sound as of a trumpet ! Loud It swelled , and louder , thrilling every nerve , Making the heart beat wildly , strangely , till All other senses seemed in hearing lost", "context_len": 55}}}, "billowy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "None could look back , not even to behold If those beloved were nigh ; one thrilling thought Rapt all the multitude , -- Can HE be near ? Then cries of terror rose -- I scarcely heard ; And buildings shook and rocked , and crashing fell , -- I scarcely marked their fall ; the trembling ground Rose like the billowy sea , -- I scarcely felt The motion ; such intensity of hope -- Joy -- expectation -- flooded all my soul ; A tide of living light , o \u2019 erwhelming all The hopes and fears , the cares and woes of earth", "context_len": 107}}}, "shepherds": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Early on Tuesday morning the real business of the day started , for then the shepherds began to bring down their flocks of sheep from the surrounding mountains , the cattle-drovers came with their sleek cows and long-horned young bullocks , and whole herds of rough little Welsh ponies were driven with much noise and shouting from the high moorlands over the border", "context_len": 63}}}, "undefiled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Behold ! Behold ! Like white-winged angels rise the radiant throng That from yon cemetery \u2019 s gloomy verge Have burst , immortal -- glorious -- undefiled ! Bright as the sun their crowns celestial shine , Yet I behold them with undazzled eye", "context_len": 44}}}, "celestial": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "It is true that Alexander 's soldiers , about to leave their homes to go to another quarter of the globe , and into scenes of danger and death from which it was very improbable that many of them would ever return , had no other celestial protection to look up to than the spirits of ancient heroes , who , they imagined , had , somehow or other , found their final home in a sort of heaven among the summits of the mountains , where they reigned , in some sense , over human affairs ; but this , small as it seems to us , was a great deal to them", "context_len": 114}}}, "canopy": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 64, "context": "church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all service time , and if she did not behave properly it might come", "context_len": 128}}}, "aspiring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 70, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Peggy was rather fascinated , but Archie made such very rude inquiries as to whether she were aspiring to attain an equal bulk that she indignantly dragged him away to view the living skeleton , a fearful , hollow-cheeked object , whose bones could almost be counted", "context_len": 47}}}, "fleshly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 78, "context": "Oh that yon glittering canopy of light Would burst asunder , that I might behold Him , whom so long , not seeing , I have loved ! It parted -- lo ! it opened -- as I stood With clasped hands stretched towards Heaven ; my eager gaze Fixed on the widening glory ! Suddenly , As if the burden of the flesh no more Could fetter down the aspiring soul to earth , As if the fleshly nature were consumed -- Lost in the glowing ecstasy of love -- I soared aloft , I mounted through the air , Free as a spirit , rose to meet my Lord , With such a cry of rapture -- that I woke !", "context_len": 123}}}, "measuring": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "O misery ! to wake in darkness , wake From vision of unutterable joy ; Instead of trumpet-sound and song of Heaven , To hear the dull clock measuring out time , When I had seemed to touch Eternity ! In the first pang of disappointed hope , I wept that I could wake from such a dream ; Until Faith gently whispered , Wherefore weep To lose the faint dim shadow of a joy Of which the substance shall one day be thine ? Live in the hope , -- that hope shall brighten life , And sanctify it to its highest end", "context_len": 104}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "With that he jumped , and then , with a measuring tape , he carefully noted the distance he had covered", "context_len": 21}}}, "sanctify": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 98, "context": "O misery ! to wake in darkness , wake From vision of unutterable joy ; Instead of trumpet-sound and song of Heaven , To hear the dull clock measuring out time , When I had seemed to touch Eternity ! In the first pang of disappointed hope , I wept that I could wake from such a dream ; Until Faith gently whispered , Wherefore weep To lose the faint dim shadow of a joy Of which the substance shall one day be thine ? Live in the hope , -- that hope shall brighten life , And sanctify it to its highest end", "context_len": 104}}}, "imply": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Hartley , looking at the white face , with the great dark circles under eyes that during the night had known no wink of sleep , felt sorry in her heart of hearts that she had uttered a single word that would seem to imply reproof", "context_len": 46}}}, "antagonistic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "The view of a castle from within and the view of that same castle from without are totally different ; yet they are not in the least antagonistic", "context_len": 28}}}, "complexities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Probably no character of any human being can be satisfactorily so disposed of ; and there are complexities in the very simplest nature", "context_len": 23}}}, "opposites": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "But the main outlines of some people are more easily perceived , more consistent \u2019 according to popular notions of character-consistency , than the main outlines of some other people ; merely because they happen to embrace fewer opposites", "context_len": 39}}}, "manifestations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "All people did not see her exactly alike , -- partly because of necessity they looked upon her with different eyes , and partly because of necessity she was not the same in her manifestations to all of them", "context_len": 39}}}, "angelic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "'Sure , I 'll let him in , the darlint ! ' said Kathleen O'Riley , running to open the French window and admit the smiling Bobby , who entered with an expression of such angelic innocence that Peggy 's suspicions were instantly aroused", "context_len": 44}}}, "personality": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 58, "context": "He was always warding off death , or doing all that man could do to ward it off , and in many many cases death was afraid of him , and retired from his prey , vanquished by that knowledge , that genius , that sympathy , that love for humanity , which overflowed the little doctor 's personality", "context_len": 59}}}, "unselfishly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "She was very resolute ; very persevering ; very affectionate ; reserved , yet demonstrative ; untidy , yet methodical ; exceedingly anxious for the happiness of all around ; apt often to think people better than they really were ; generous to a fault ; unselfishly ready at all times to put her own wishes aside ; vehement and impulsive , yet never in a hurry or flurry ; unyielding , yet tender ; severe , yet frisky", "context_len": 79}}}, "weaknesses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Of course there were other natural characteristics of a different kind ; weaknesses not wholly mastered ; faults not entirely conquered", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "While she frets over her want of power in speaking Urdu and Panjabi , we are rejoicing , not only in her power of writing for the people , but in her wonderful perception of the national character , her insight into the weaknesses and also into the virtues of our Native friends , Christian and heathen", "context_len": 57}}}, "dictatorial": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "She was not perfect , -- who is ? The strength of determination would occasionally run into obstinacy ; the resolute manner could be a trifle dictatorial ; the very wish to help and please others might be carried out in a way which did not gratify", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "There were voices heard in the distance ; the manly voice of Hughie , who was somewhat dictatorial , and was ordering people about , and telling this person or the other that they were doing things wrong , and was terrifying his sister by his manly ways", "context_len": 48}}}, "vary": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Opinions here vary a good deal among the friends that knew her best ; but those who at different periods of her life lived for any length of time under the same roof , will be able to recall certain instances of an absence of tact , a lack of quick understanding of the feelings of others , which certainly never arose from want of a desire to understand", "context_len": 69}}}, "deficiency": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The deficiency remedied , and the scanty toilet operations performed ( the inhabitants of the dug-out had turned in `` all standing '' , even to their boots ) , the men put on their shrapnel helmets , seized their rifles , and sallied forth into the night", "context_len": 48}}}, "housekeeping": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "She catechized Lilian freely about her housekeeping arrangements , hoped Nancy did her duty , and did not neglect to sweep out corners , told Bobby that his irrepressible curls looked girlish , and his hair ought to be cropped close every week , plied Peggy with embarrassing questions on the subject of fine needlework and stocking-darning , and drank four cups of tea in the meantime , with the air of one conferring a favour thereby", "context_len": 77}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 75, "context": "They were full of their own interests , and , on the few occasions when she happened to meet them , had talked so much of new teachers , new pupils , and class topics of which she knew nothing , that she had felt a little 'out of it , ' and had an uneasy sensation that , if she spoke of the quiet little episodes of her daily round they would vote her housekeeping experiences as decidedly 'slow", "context_len": 80}}}, "efficiently": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "He did not like to leave it behind him and proceed to the eastward ; for , in case of any reverses happening to him , the Tyrians would be very likely to act decidedly against him , and their power on the Mediterranean would enable them to act very efficiently against him on all the coasts of Greece and Asia Minor", "context_len": 62}}}, "unconcern": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` You scoundrel ! '' he ejaculated between his set teeth , and with his eyes actually blazing , `` you stole this , did you ? '' -- flourishing the note before the now terrified Lewis , who , taken thus by surprise , had no time to collect his wits and assume an appearance of unconcern and innocence", "context_len": 60}}}, "reposeful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "She had not the reposeful air , the placid voice and manner , above all , the ready tact , which for good nursing are essential", "context_len": 26}}}, "cowardliness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Self-indulgence , laziness , cowardliness were unknown factors in her existence , and could never have held her back ; but here too there was probably an innate sense of lack of power ; and here too she never through life took to the occupation , as to the manner born", "context_len": 51}}}, "factors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Self-indulgence , laziness , cowardliness were unknown factors in her existence , and could never have held her back ; but here too there was probably an innate sense of lack of power ; and here too she never through life took to the occupation , as to the manner born", "context_len": 51}}}, "noticeable": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 97, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}}}, "protestation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A gentle protestation was of no avail ; for so sure was she of her remedy , that she only supposed her friend to shrink from giving her trouble , and the music went on unchecked", "context_len": 36}}}, "headaches": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 42, "context": "Now , dearest Fred , could there be a clearer opening than at Batala for an earnest Christian man , whether in Orders or not ? I am not thinking of you , for I would not have any one subject to headaches come to this feverish land ; but I am thinking of your brother collegians", "context_len": 57}}}, "disqualifications": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It must not be forgotten , however , that , whatever her natural disqualifications for the part of a nurse might have been , she did in her old age so far overcome them as often to take a share in tending the brown boys \u2019 of the Batala High School when ill , in a manner which won their loving gratitude , although she did not prove successful as a nurse to English invalids", "context_len": 75}}}, "springiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Her very walk was indicative of this ; the elastic springiness of every step", "context_len": 14}}}, "teeming": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "She was teeming with life and energy ; -- whether it were over her favourite chess , when she would wait patiently but eagerly , thinking out each move ; or enjoying the small-talk of society , watching faces and reading characters , to treasure them up for painting in one of her forthcoming volumes ; or teaching a niece the beauties of sound and thought in the Italian of Dante ; or playing at some game of thought with young people ; or reading aloud one of her two favourite dearly-loved and untiringly-studied authors , Shakespeare and Boswell \u2019 s Life of Johnson", "context_len": 104}}}, "incomer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Even in the midst of her literary work , she would at once rise , leave it , and give her whole attention to any subject an incomer might wish to speak to her about", "context_len": 35}}}, "trifled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "He put his question and offer kindly and freely , but in a way which showed his nephew he was not to be trifled with", "context_len": 25}}}, "conversational": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "It possesses an affirmative , negative , interrogative and distributive form , which latter is used as a form for the plural of the subject in the intransitive verbs ; it also has a large number of conversational forms usually derived by contraction , ellipses , etc", "context_len": 47}}}, "claret": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "disappeared , and after a while made her way through the crowd , laden with cakes for us all , finally producing a glass of claret for me from under her cloak , which I was obliged to take then and there", "context_len": 42}}}, "chord": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 70, "context": "Belden told me on the steamer , said Marie-Celeste , with the air of one who settles down for a good talk with a familiar friend , of some musician who heard some one strike two or three chords and then suddenly stop , and after that he ; could not get a wink of sleep till he jumped out of bed and rushed to his piano and struck the chord that belonged at the end of the others", "context_len": 79}}}, "personating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Whilst she , personating a visitor to the sculpture studio , would try to upset our gravity by her amusing remarks on the statues", "context_len": 24}}}, "sculpture": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "The artist who conceived of this prodigious piece of sculpture said that he would so shape the figure that the city should be in one of its hands , and the river should flow out from the other", "context_len": 38}}}, "studio": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "A room in the top story , with a fine north light , was fitted up as a studio for Uncle Fritz , who , though a business man by circumstance , was an artist through and through", "context_len": 38}}}, "statues": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 12, "context": "And such a garden as it proved ! with its fountains and statues and vases , and the orangery on one side , and on the other three sides a beautiful sloping lawn , ascending from the level of the garden to the gray stonewall at the outer edge of the terrace ; and to think that here they were actually walking about in this beautiful garden , instead of merely peering through the fretwork of the iron gate , as some other little children with envious eyes were doing that very moment", "context_len": 93}}}, "rainy": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 46, "context": "She lived for others ; whether in the great work of her life , the use of her pen , the proceeds of which went to fill her charity purse , or in the simple act of leaving her quiet room , on a dull , rainy afternoon , to play a bright country dance or Scotch reel , and set the little ones dancing to vent their superfluous spirits", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "The country inhabited by the Maskoki proper abounds in creek bottoms overflowed in the rainy season , as the country around Opel\u00edka `` swamp-site '' ( from Creek : op\u00edlua , ap\u00edlua swamp , l\u00e4\u00edkita to be stretched out ) , Opil- ` l\u00e1ko `` great swamp , '' west of the above ( Hawkins , p", "context_len": 57}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I do not wonder Lemuel says they do n't mind rainy weather since Pauline makes sunshine to order", "context_len": 18}}}, "crinolines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I remember her in the days of crinolines , standing straight and dignified in her plain dress , without the least attempt at fulness in the skirt", "context_len": 27}}}, "individuality": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "I should think it must have been always so ; her individuality and disregard of the world \u2019 s opinion were so strongly marked", "context_len": 24}}}, "amalgamating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "She never had the art of amalgamating the different parts of her dress ! \u2019 In plain terms , her taste in dress was not good , and she did not take trouble to improve it", "context_len": 36}}}, "knack": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 55, "context": "We 'll just put two or three dishes of this batter together in the pan , add a little more flour , and some salt , and it 'll be ready to bake , '' and , as he talked Fenn soon beat up the batter to the right consistency , for he had a knack of cooking", "context_len": 58}}}, "illustrates": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "One circumstance occurred at the close of this great victory which illustrates the magnanimity of Alexander 's character , and helps to explain the very strong personal attachment which every body within the circle of his influence so obviously felt for him", "context_len": 42}}}, "vexing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Tucker , knowing that Charlotte possessed a new bonnet , and knowing also that there was no fear of vexing Charlotte by the act , lent this new bonnet to the friend , to be worn at the wedding", "context_len": 39}}}, "copying": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "And yet Leonard was just as brave in his own way , was n't he ? It was the prayer that the Prince wrote in his mass-book that I was copying ; it is very beautiful , is n't it ?", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "At last , when he felt that he must not delay another moment , he took leave of her , saying as he went , Well , as usual you have set me thinking , my little friend , but as though he were grateful for the same ; and Marie-Celeste , turning back to finish the copying of the Prince Imperial 's prayer , wondered in her practical little way if anything would come of the thinking , and if so , if she would ever happen to hear what it was ; and yet at the same time not a little sceptical as to any tangible result whatsoever", "context_len": 110}}}, "confronts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Some day , when there is real danger , you \u2019 ll flash out ! \u2019 Perhaps she was thinking of the scene in one of her own little books , when a timid young governess confronts an escaped panther", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Are ye ready for the last act , or are ye timid and do ye shrink back from the terrible danger that confronts ye ? If so , speak , an ' ye shall be allowed to depart in peace", "context_len": 41}}}, "disillusionment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "\u2019 Ever eager to see the best in everybody , she wore rose-coloured spectacles which now and then would lead her into thinking of people much better than they deserved , and disillusionment \u2019 had to be gone through", "context_len": 39}}}, "ensured": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "At least she ensured thus the making of mistakes on the right side , instead of on the wrong", "context_len": 19}}}, "erroneous": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "The marking of the linguistic areas by dots , pointing to the tribal settlements , answers much better the purpose than the coloring of large areas , which conveys the erroneous impression that the population was scattered all over a certain country", "context_len": 42}}}, "readings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Peggy , too , had begged to sit up a little later to listen , and I think she learned almost as much from the readings as from her lessons ; for our English classics are an education in themselves , and those who love them young rarely care to read much trash afterwards", "context_len": 54}}}, "devotional": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "Charlotte kept up her habit of early rising ; and from eight o \u2019 clock till half-past eight each morning she would take her devotional \u2019 walk in the garden , -- hands folded on chest , head up , step firm and dignified", "context_len": 44}}}, "tracts": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 45, "context": "They crossed Cumberland and Tennessee rivers , followed Coosa river in marching along its banks from south to north , [ 146 ] but were too late for the Alibamu , who had previously left the country , partly for Mobile , partly for the tracts held by Cha'hta Indians", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The scientists who now claim that they descend from the mound builders , do so only on the belief that they must have dwelt for uncounted centuries in the fertile tracts where Hernando de Soto found them , and where they have remained up to a recent epoch", "context_len": 48}}}, "enjoyments": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Ev \u2019 ry day I live I thank the Lord that he \u2019 s been so good to me an \u2019 let me have so many pleasures an \u2019 enjoyments", "context_len": 30}}}, "charades": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Besides playing for the children , she would plan games for them , and would superintend charades ; and when they grew older she would read Shakespeare with them , often knitting busily all the while as she read", "context_len": 39}}}, "resides": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "has written a strong note to an English official at no great distance from Batala , -- there not one Englishman resides , -- and I feel little doubt that he will bring the strong arm of the law to protect B -- n", "context_len": 44}}}, "castes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Also , an English ploughboy requires different treatment from a high-caste Indian ; but she was friends \u2019 with boys of all castes there", "context_len": 24}}}, "scruple": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "On making examination , Paris was found to have the finest bull and the king , exercising the despotic power which kings in those days made no scruple of assuming in respect to helpless peasants , took it away", "context_len": 39}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Alden , therefore , did not scruple to linger for a few minutes with his sable friend", "context_len": 17}}}, "inefficiency": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I had seventeen last night , and felt my inefficiency", "context_len": 10}}}, "jotted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "Miss Wauton \u2019 s first impressions of her , jotted down as follows , do not give the impression of a very old lady , dearly as Charlotte Tucker loved to describe herself in those terms : Tall , slight , with lofty brow , sparkling eye , face constantly beaming with love and intelligence ; genius in every look ; figure frail and fairy-like , agile and graceful ; very brisk movements and light tread", "context_len": 76}}}, "plaster": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "I can tell you what to do , said Uncle Isaac ; go and get some of that blue clay by the brook , and mix it up with water that has the chill taken off , and plaster it right on three inches thick , and you \u2019 ll see what it will do ; all you want is to keep the air out", "context_len": 65}}}, "ornamenting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "As we grew older she would help us with our charades and games , planning wonderful card games herself , and ornamenting them with brush and stencil", "context_len": 27}}}, "stencil": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "As we grew older she would help us with our charades and games , planning wonderful card games herself , and ornamenting them with brush and stencil", "context_len": 27}}}, "histories": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "On Sunday afternoons she would take us up to her room , in order that my Mother might rest in peace from the children ; and there we always spent a delightful time , looking over her dressing-case with its treasures , and listening to the histories of each trinket and curiosity , or messing with her paints", "context_len": 58}}}, "messing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "On Sunday afternoons she would take us up to her room , in order that my Mother might rest in peace from the children ; and there we always spent a delightful time , looking over her dressing-case with its treasures , and listening to the histories of each trinket and curiosity , or messing with her paints", "context_len": 58}}}, "mentions": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "As to the name , the Westo Indians may be identified with the Oustacs of Lederer ( who are reported as being at war with the Usherees ) , and with the Hostaqua of Ren\u00e9 de Laudonni\u00e8re , who mentions them as forming a confederacy under a paracusi in the northern parts of the `` Floridian '' territory", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` He also states that the French post was three leagues from the mouth of Yazoo river , close to a village inhabited by a medley of Yazoo , Couroas and Ofogoula Indians , and mentions the tribes in the following order ( I , 163 ) : `` Yazoo Indians , about 100 huts ; further up , Coroas , about 40 huts ; Chactioumas or `` red lobsters '' , about 50 huts , on same river ; Ouf\u00e9-ouglas , about 60 huts ; Tapoussas , not over 25 huts", "context_len": 92}}}, "transaction": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 33, "context": "Bobby had a pretty set of bantams , whose small eggs afforded him much delight and some slight profit , for Aunt Helen bought them from him at threepence a dozen -- a transaction which he always recorded in chalk upon the hen-house door , the pennies being carefully put by towards the purchase of a pair of fantail pigeons , which was at present the summit of his ambition", "context_len": 70}}}, "impressiveness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "fiftieth": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "But to make a new home amid new surroundings , to learn a new language , to enter upon a new line of work , -- these things after the fiftieth birthday have a somewhat alarming sound", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` To carry the comparison still further , '' continued his companion , `` our quota is roughly a fiftieth of the fighting forces of the Empire", "context_len": 27}}}, "dutifully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "For her parents \u2019 sake she had dutifully held back , during the noontide and early afternoon of her history , from much that she would fain have done ; and though the latter part of her afternoon \u2019 had been full and busy , with freedom to do what she willed , yet even this was not enough", "context_len": 59}}}, "willed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "Then , when that which was with them of food was finished , Alaeddin arose and taking one of the platters under his clothes , went in quest of the Jew , so he might sell it to him ; but , as chance willed it , he passed by the shop of a goldsmith , an honest , pious man , who feared God", "context_len": 65}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` decided ( or demanded ) that thou be bountiful to ( or grace ) me with ; '' but icledsa is here used in the colloquial sense of `` willed , vouchsafed", "context_len": 33}}}, "unfettered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "But finding herself thus unfettered , the thought came up , -- Why not devote the Evening of her life to Missionary work ? Why not set an example to others who , like herself , might with advancing years be left free of ties ? Or at least , why not put the matter to the test of actual trial , and prove whether or not elderly women , and not younger ones only , might go forth to work among the Heathen ?", "context_len": 85}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "His fears thrown to the wind , and the exhilarating sensation of unfettered action surging through his veins , Malcolm found himself scrambling over sand-bags and leaping into the pitted No Man 's Land", "context_len": 34}}}, "ties": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "But finding herself thus unfettered , the thought came up , -- Why not devote the Evening of her life to Missionary work ? Why not set an example to others who , like herself , might with advancing years be left free of ties ? Or at least , why not put the matter to the test of actual trial , and prove whether or not elderly women , and not younger ones only , might go forth to work among the Heathen ?", "context_len": 85}}}, "gavotte": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "A woman of fifty , who can lightly dance the gavotte , with springs which a child can not emulate , is not quite an ordinary specimen of advancing years", "context_len": 30}}}, "corresponded": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "vocabulary": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 21, "context": "The object of the lessons was three-fold : to provide reading matter for the little ones who had only a small vocabulary of sight-words ; to acquaint them early with the heroes who have come down to us in song and story ; and to create a desire for literature", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "Francis Le Baron transmitted to the Smithsonian Institution a few ethnologic notices and a vocabulary obtained from the Seminole Indians of Chipko 's ( since deceased ) band , which he had visited in March 1881 in their village near Lake Pierce", "context_len": 42}}}, "yearns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Do not grudge me , dear one , to the work for which my soul yearns", "context_len": 16}}}, "expostulation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "You see by the enclosed that my arrangements are made , and that expostulation would but pain me", "context_len": 18}}}, "mingles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I own with shame that much that is unworthy mingles with my desire to serve the Lord in India ; but the desire itself has , I trust , been put into my mind by Him", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "It mingles with a scientific appreciation of the vastness of the movements and magnitudes by which the effect is produced , and while the one occupies the intellect , the other impresses the soul", "context_len": 34}}}, "unmeasured": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Such an attempt had not been made before ; and the untried always contains unmeasured elements of danger and difficulty", "context_len": 20}}}, "pronunciation": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Doll , '' said Maggie , not at all satisfied with his pronunciation , and speaking in a louder voice , as if Hafed could understand the better for that", "context_len": 31}}}, "griffith": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Griffith", "context_len": 1}}}, "medical": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 90, "context": "But as it happened , the warmth of the wrapping , the influence of the spirit that had been poured liberally down his throat , and the chafing , combined with his naturally strong animal power to revive him from the state of insensibility into which he had fallen , and long before they reached the granite pier of the little harbour his eyes had opened , and he was staring in a peculiarly puzzled way at Mark Penelly , who still knelt beside him in the double character of medical man and nurse", "context_len": 94}}}, "scudding": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I am now quietly scudding over the Atlantic", "context_len": 8}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A scudding cloud mass dimmed it , but not before Barry had glimpsed a long , black shape moving out from shore", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The young Fortress skipper glanced up at the scudding clouds", "context_len": 10}}}, "ventilating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "You who are so kindly anxious about my comfort will be pleased to know that I have a very fair amount of wraps , and am more likely to suffer from heat than cold , seeing that my cabin port-hole is never opened , and that the only way of ventilating it is by leaving the door open , -- a thing not to be thought of at night , as ladies \u2019 and gentlemen \u2019 s cabins are not at all in separate parts of the vessel", "context_len": 88}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "My young master was intensely interested as his uncle talked to him about the width of stalls , the size of windows , the proper kind of concrete flooring which should have wooden stall racks , the ventilating flues and rolling doors", "context_len": 42}}}, "oxygen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The only thing I fear is using up my oxygen at night", "context_len": 12}}}, "satiated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "The vast throng then rolled along upon the top of the wall till they came to stairways and slopes by which they could descend into the city , and , pouring down through all these avenues , they spread over the streets , and satiated the hatred and rage , which had been gathering strength for seven long months , in bursting into houses , and killing and destroying all that came in their way", "context_len": 75}}}, "reminding": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` And she said to him , `` O my son , the Sultan received me with all courtesy , according to his wont , and meseemeth he meant fairly by us , but [ for ] thine accursed enemy the Vizier ; for that , after I had bespoken the Sultan in thy name , even as thou badest me , reminding him that the term for which he had appointed us was past and saying to him , 'If Thy Grace would vouchsafe to give commandment for the marriage of thy daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour with my son Alaeddin , ' -- he turned to the Vizier and spoke to him", "context_len": 113}}}, "sculptured": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "There were twelve of these statues , sculptured with great art , and they were borne along on elevated pedestals , with censers , and incense , and various ceremonies of homage , while vast multitudes of spectators lined the way", "context_len": 41}}}, "alabaster": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` `` Hearkening and obedience , '' replied the Marid and taking him up , brought him intro a bath , never saw King nor Kisra [ 447 ] its like , for it was of alabaster and agate and full of marvellous limnings that ravished the sight , and therein was a saloon all embossed with precious jewels", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` And this '' -- bending tenderly over the soap dish and heaping it with roses -- '' is purest alabaster encrusted with gems", "context_len": 24}}}, "loveliness": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 18, "context": "The Sultan wondered with an exceeding wonderment and was confounded at the beauty of the girls and their loveliness , which overpassed description ; his wit was bewildered , when he saw the golden dishes , full of jewels that dazzled the sight , and he was amazed at this marvel , so that he became as one dumb , unable to speak aught , of the excess of his wonderment ; nay , his wit was the more perplexed , forasmuch as this had all been accomplished in an hour 's time", "context_len": 93}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "And when you die does n't an angel come and take you in his arms and just carry you up and up to the other side of the sky where everything is peace and loveliness , and no one will torment you -- -- ''", "context_len": 45}}}, "amphitheatre": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "The majesty of the churches was indeed ( as a contemporary wrote ) great , but thatched roofs consorted ill with the remains of shattered column and pedestal , and with the fragmentary ruins of the grand amphitheatre , which were yet partly visible , although the stones which had been brought from Bath to build it had been employed largely in church architecture", "context_len": 64}}}, "bergs": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "We , in Batala , seem for years to have been labouring to cut a passage through hard , cold ice , with the chilly bergs of Muhammadanism and Hinduism towering on either hand", "context_len": 34}}}, "smoothest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I could hardly have supposed that any star would have cast such a reflection on the smoothest water as Mars threw on the still ocean", "context_len": 25}}}, "piloting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "One felt that a watchful and skilful captain was cautiously piloting us , avoiding the larger masses of ice , though our vessel passed right over some of the little ones", "context_len": 31}}}, "globes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "I watched the tiny globes of phosphoric light which sometimes gleamed on the water , and the dark objects which I knew to be pieces of floating ice", "context_len": 28}}}, "phosphoric": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I watched the tiny globes of phosphoric light which sometimes gleamed on the water , and the dark objects which I knew to be pieces of floating ice", "context_len": 28}}}, "wondrously": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 79, "context": "He waited with all a fisherman 's patience , and , used as he was to such scenes , he could not help feeling gladdened at the glorious sight that met his gaze , for , one by one , the stars had paled , till only that named after the morning shone out resplendent in the now grey west ; while to eastward all was blushing with bright red and gold and purple and orange , tints so wondrously beautiful and rich that Nature had enough to spare for sea as well as sky", "context_len": 95}}}, "cascade": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 61, "context": "There one moment stood the statuesque figure , white as a cameo cut in the black rock , the next moment there was a gleam of something flashing through the air , and passing into the deep blue wave , which , as if by the contact of the figure , broke into silvery foam , rushing back like a vast cascade towards the Shangles", "context_len": 65}}}, "reverting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "reverting to riflemen on arrival in England , before proceeding across to France", "context_len": 13}}}, "channels": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "In a moment all was bustle ; the watch below tumbled up to lend a hand without waiting to be called ; and in five minutes the noble ship was clothed with canvas from her trucks down , and shearing through the deep blue water with her lee channels buried", "context_len": 50}}}, "dimmed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 127, "context": "of his love and affection for his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour , was wont , every day , when he awoke from his sleep , to open the window and look at her therefrom ; so he arose on the morrow , according to his wont , and opened his chamber-window , so he might see his daughter ; but [ 571 ] when he put out his head and looked for Alaeddin 's palace , he beheld nothing but a place swept [ and level ] , like as it was aforetime , and saw neither palace nor inhabitants ; [ 572 ] whereat amazement clad him and his wit was bewildered and he fell to rubbing his eyes , so haply they were bleared or dimmed", "context_len": 128}}}, "solve": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 17, "context": "They tried to induce Fenn to accompany them , but he was firm in his determination to solve the `` turtle mystery , '' as he called it , and , in the end , Bart , Ned and Frank tramped off through the storm , for it was still snowing , while the stout lad remained behind , watching the turtle , which he had placed on a cleared place on the ground in front of the tent", "context_len": 79}}}, "essaying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Uncle Job still lived in his plain little cottage with his wife and daughter , and still plied his humble task as the village cobbler , essaying sometimes to make shoes when there were none to be repaired", "context_len": 38}}}, "amuses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "amuses the good folk by a few lively remarks as to the doll being weak or tired , etc", "context_len": 19}}}, "teetotaller": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "An expedition to Niagara was achieved with much success ; after which she wrote to one of her aunts in England : My nephews think me amazingly strong , and yet I have become almost a teetotaller", "context_len": 37}}}, "sherry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Except your little bottle of sherry , I have only tasted wine twice since I left you", "context_len": 17}}}, "cascades": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "A rapid stream ran through the woods , dashing down over great boulders , making little cascades and waterfalls as it went , with here and there a deep , clear pool , where the trout lay snugly under the stones ; the rocks under the overhanging trees were carpeted with the softest and greenest of moss , and tall ferns grew right down to the water 's edge , mingling with trailing ivy and creeping moneywort", "context_len": 77}}}, "accessories": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "O the sparkling rush of diamonds , -- the white misty foam breaking on the picturesque rocks beneath , -- the accessories so beautiful , -- the cloud-like veil so transparently lovely !", "context_len": 33}}}, "transparently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "O the sparkling rush of diamonds , -- the white misty foam breaking on the picturesque rocks beneath , -- the accessories so beautiful , -- the cloud-like veil so transparently lovely !", "context_len": 33}}}, "crags": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Earth here is so fair , with bold crags draperied with the richest foliage , that one could imagine her contending for the palm with water ; but water carries the victory at Niagara ; Earth but serves to frame and set off her magnificence", "context_len": 45}}}, "emerges": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then as the river emerges from its veil of spray , -- spray sometimes rising pyramid-like for hundreds of feet , -- it assumes a deeper green , more blue than that of the surrounding foliage , but pure in tint", "context_len": 41}}}, "assumes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Alabama river is formed by the junction of Coosa and Tallapoosa rivers ; pursues a winding course between banks about fifty feet high , and joins Tombigbee river about thirty miles above Mobile bay , when it assumes the name of Mobile river", "context_len": 43}}}, "divides": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "But of course there was only the merest little suggestion of a sympathetic thought to spend on the poor , white lady , with Chris Hartley but just discovered , and after that one instant of transfixed surprise she sped toward him , both hands extended ; and over the gate that divides the first from the second cabin they indulged in the heartiest shaking of hands possible , while hats for the moment were expected to look out for themselves", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Rain falling in a comparative brief area divides so that some of the drops flow westward and find their way into the Columbia and thence to the Pacific", "context_len": 28}}}, "cloudy": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "In the early morning , before the activities of the day have begun , this wharf is a deserted and forbidding place , and on one such early morning in September , with chill air and cloudy skies , and not even a rose tint in the dull east , there was no one to be seen throughout the whole length of the wharf save one slowly moving boy", "context_len": 69}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` But when it rains or is cloudy ? ''", "context_len": 10}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The spot which had roused his suspicion stood out almost as at midday , when the sky is partly cloudy", "context_len": 20}}}, "venetian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I washed and dressed , as much by feeling as by sight , opened my venetian shutters , and walked out into the verandah which commands a fine view of both Falls", "context_len": 32}}}, "verandah": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 14, "context": "The handsome house in its brilliant setting of lawns and trees , the wide verandah with its crimson Mount Washington rockers , luxurious hammocks , and low table covered with freshly-cut magazines , the pleasant-faced man who was her nearest of kin , and his graceful wife in a tea-gown of soft summer silk with rich lace about her throat and wrists , her cousins in their dainty muslins , and Russell in his fresh summer suit", "context_len": 77}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The thermometer in my verandah to-day , where it had been in the shade all the day , was about 107 , that is more than twenty degrees hotter than I have ever seen it in the most sultry day in England", "context_len": 42}}}, "snatches": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "When I was a young girl I often tilted up a volume of Tennyson and read snatches while I compounded a pudding , and found it had a wonderfully inspiring effect , and did not spoil the cooking either , for my `` Tennyson '' puddings generally turned out a great success", "context_len": 52}}}, "waterfall": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 63, "context": "For this current Irene made , and when they got there she suddenly rested on her oars , and looking at Rosamund , said , `` Are you afraid , or are you not ? If the current gets a little stronger we will be drifted to the edge of the lake , and at the edge of the lake there is a waterfall , and over it we will go , and , splash ! splash ! splash ! I took a girl there once ; she was my governess , but I was quite tired of her , and knew the fright she would get in when I took her out in the boat", "context_len": 116}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Now , we can go over the waterfall to-day if you do n't mind", "context_len": 14}}}, "emerald": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "A huge emerald -- credited as part of the treasure of the famous explorer , Cortez , in the 16th Century is stolen from a museum at Roller University", "context_len": 29}}}, "inadequate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 89, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}}}, "conceptions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 69, "context": "Among the twenty millions of people in the United States , there are perhaps two millions , between the ages of fifteen and twenty-five , who wish to become acquainted , in general , with the leading events in the history of the Old World , and of ancient times , but who , coming upon the stage in this land and at this period , have ideas and conceptions so widely different from those of other nations and of other times , that a mere republication of existing accounts is not what they require", "context_len": 95}}}, "gutturals": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "The alternating processes observed here also occur in most other Indian and illiterate languages : tch , dsh alternate with ts , ds , h with k , ; g with the other gutturals , b with p , d with t , \u0101 with e , o with u", "context_len": 50}}}, "consigning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Among lesser employments , she is said to have spent hours at a time in looking through her papers and letters -- the collection of a literary lifetime -- and consigning masses of the same to destruction", "context_len": 37}}}, "truer": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Are we to conclude that -- -- and -- -- are truer workers than dear -- -- spending his strength in breaking stones at K", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 125, "context": "him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought , of God , why did He not own us ? What were General Harrison 's dreams of a pure republic , but vanity ? Who can say if his dreams of heaven were any truer ? ''", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Conscience makes cowards of us all ; '' so the great writer has said ; and truer words never stood out bold and striking from the paper on which they were written", "context_len": 33}}}, "prescience": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Hamilton , the loved Laura of early days , had a truer prescience of how things would be", "context_len": 18}}}, "subordinate": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "In ancient times , however , the great business of the officers , certainly in all the subordinate grades , was to lead on the men , and set them an example by performing themselves deeds in which their own great personal prowess was displayed", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "-- Although succession among all Maskoki tribes was in the female line , the females occupied a subordinate condition among the Creeks , and in their households were subjected , like those of other Indians , to a life of drudgery", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Yet the Author has distinctly felt that fiction must always , in such cases , be subordinate to truth , and that it is only legitimately used as a vehicle of instruction when it fills up the gaps in the outline , without contradicting them in any respect , or interfering with their due order and sequence", "context_len": 57}}}, "flout": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this change of fortune is come about ; 't is unmannerly to ask questions , but she can not look on me as all a stranger , even if she hold no memory of those old", "context_len": 128}}}, "converts": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 22, "word_index": 8, "context": "As for the characters of some of the converts , we only have to look at home , and to see for ourselves what the average civilised and well-taught and highly-trained Englishman is -- how very far in a large majority of cases from being either blameless , or saintly , or entirely faithful to his Baptismal vows", "context_len": 58}}}, "faultless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "So much money spent , and so many lives sacrificed , -- and for what ? Half-a-dozen converts , perhaps , in a dozen years , some of whom prove in the end to be faithless , while others are very far from being faultless saints", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Belden 's faultless attire and distinguished bearing , had rated him at once as one of those high and mighty Londoners , and had expected that he would of course entrench himself on the back seat of the little turnout and , preserving a dignified silence , condescendingly allow himself to be driven about and to be very much bored into the bargain -- all of which , it must be confessed , would have been more in keeping with Mr", "context_len": 81}}}, "outlay": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Harris , presenting the hat to Ted , after making sure that none of the coins were still missing ; and Ted , though wholly bent on practising close economy , felt the circumstances justified the outlay , and did as he was bid", "context_len": 44}}}, "blameless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "As for the characters of some of the converts , we only have to look at home , and to see for ourselves what the average civilised and well-taught and highly-trained Englishman is -- how very far in a large majority of cases from being either blameless , or saintly , or entirely faithful to his Baptismal vows", "context_len": 58}}}, "conversion": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 33, "context": "All his heart was with his English friends ; he felt that in going to the Danish camp he was really going to his death , for although within a few years the conversion of the Northmen took place , yet at this period their hatred of Christianity was simply ferocious , and his father belonged to the old heathen conservatives of his day , as did all his kinsfolk", "context_len": 70}}}, "commensurate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Very often indeed the immediate results , seen to follow upon Missionary work , are not at all commensurate with the amount of money spent", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "But it was not so easy as they had supposed to think up a joke to play on the inventive freshman , that would be commensurate with the trick he had perpetrated on them", "context_len": 34}}}, "heathendom": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "Spirit and mind were as a clear prism , through which the light of Heaven fell , irradiating the atmosphere in which she lived , and which shone out all the more brightly when seen against the dark clouds of heathendom", "context_len": 41}}}, "supercilious": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "Maud 's supercilious manner slipped away from her like magic , and she became once more the pleasant Maud of the dancing-class , and her friends , feeling the change , soon left off trying to be poor little imitations of grown-up people , and began at last to enjoy themselves ; for true enjoyment does not consist in showing off , but in being our simple , natural selves , if people would only believe it", "context_len": 77}}}, "unquestioningly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "All of us who are Baptized in the Name of the Father , and of the Son , and of the Holy Spirit , are bound to His Service who is our Royal Master ; and His orders we have unquestioningly to obey", "context_len": 43}}}, "embodied": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Many old traditions of historic importance must have been embodied in these records ; but the only one given by Milfort , referring to the emigration of the Creeks from their ancient cave-homes along Red river , is so mixed up with incredible matter , that the fixation of the events , as far as then remembered , must have taken place many generations after the arrival of the Creeks in their Alabama homes", "context_len": 74}}}, "cant": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` Oh ! come now , '' said Lem , `` you aint goin ' to make me say I took it by any of that cant", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The hostess who brought Dick 's bowl of onion broth at this moment destroyed his chance of feigning sleep , and he had to resign himself to endure his companion 's conversation which flowed on , garnished with oaths and cant phrases in three or four different languages , without any interruption , till by an unguarded movement Richard exposed his face to the light of the fire , and the stranger stared a moment , and then sprang up exclaiming , `` Body o ' me if it be not Measter Dick himself ! ''", "context_len": 96}}}, "outlays": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "The whole question of our duty as Christians , on this point as on all others , hinges here , -- Are we doing , or are we not doing , that which God wills us to do ? All theories respecting outlays , values , results , sink into utter insignificance beside this question", "context_len": 55}}}, "insignificance": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "The whole question of our duty as Christians , on this point as on all others , hinges here , -- Are we doing , or are we not doing , that which God wills us to do ? All theories respecting outlays , values , results , sink into utter insignificance beside this question", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He spluttered , the rounded sentence on his lips died down to measly insignificance , he stammered , stumbled , and sat down with a red face , his eyes darting rage at poor Bart", "context_len": 35}}}, "sows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "A farmer ploughs his land , then sows his seed , and then waits months for the harvest", "context_len": 18}}}, "scratches": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` Well , '' said Maggie , slowly , as if she were considering , `` maybe her temper corner is not so sharp as it used to be , but her meddling corner is very bad , -- yes , very bad indeed ; and it scratches like everything", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 63, "context": "( We would say to our readers , that the scythe rifles in those days were not made as at present , by putting sand or emery upon wood , with cement ; but they scratched the wood and made it rough , then smeared it with tallow , and put fine sand on it , which adhered to the tallow and the scratches", "context_len": 64}}}, "fuller": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "Miss Forster was not sorry to find so plausible an excuse for keeping her pet nephew at the Willows , and the young man himself had no objections , being fuller than ever of ingenious schemes , only he had transferred the seat of his operations to the Abbey , as offering a wider scope in the way of material , and having the further advantage of a number of appreciative assistants", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Here is the fuller version : Go ye therefore , and teach \u2019 ( Rev", "context_len": 15}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Burton , `` And right soon ( Inshallah ! ) O my daughter , thou shalt have fuller joy with him", "context_len": 21}}}, "triumphs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Davenport a rich relation nor was she herself in the friendless condition necessary for the full requirements of fiction , so she was afraid the round of amusements and social triumphs generally enjoyed by the heroine would not fall to her share", "context_len": 42}}}, "glorify": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Baptize the Nations ; far and nigh The triumphs of the Cross record ; The Name of JESUS glorify Till every kindred call Him Lord", "context_len": 25}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "It is true that we are told not to let our left hand , know that which is done by our right hand ; but are we not also commanded so to let our light shine before men that they may see our good works and glorify our Father in heaven ? And can we do so , or truly show our love to him , if we hide the services rendered for his sake behind a mask of coldness and reserve ? My dear aunt , for his sake , for your own , for the sake of the affection and confidence which I wish my children to feel for you , and which I believe you wish to gain , let me satisfy them that it", "context_len": 128}}}, "differing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "Many transitive verbs have , when their object stands in the plural , a ( distributive ) form differing entirely or partially from the one referring to an object in the singular ; a few others show this change , when their subject passes from the singular to the plural number", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "And do you think that people are to be all massed in heaven , losing their various identities , their differing tastes , their separate natures ? Going from this lower world so full of its adaptations , where colour and form take on a thousand changes , and life and pursuit can be varied almost at will , to a mere dead level of perfect felicity ? To leave earth where no two things are alike , and go to heaven to find no two different ! The Lord 's preparations mean more than that", "context_len": 96}}}, "ayah": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "Writing home , she described drolly her absence from Batala as -- this strange episode of my life ; -- seven weeks acting Superintendent of the Orphanage , -- three of those weeks sole Missionary at Amritsar , -- and -- oh , bathos ! ten days an ayah -- for I had none other", "context_len": 55}}}, "ayahs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The ayahs ought to be glad to help ; for they , at least two or three of them , seem to regard me as a kind of supplementary nurse , and if they want to go to work make over the baby to me", "context_len": 45}}}, "bribing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "I am bribing one to teach me , \u2019 she wrote", "context_len": 11}}}, "supplementary": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "The ayahs ought to be glad to help ; for they , at least two or three of them , seem to regard me as a kind of supplementary nurse , and if they want to go to work make over the baby to me", "context_len": 45}}}, "inserting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "A gentleman went round and collected adjectives haphazard from the passengers , inserting them in the places left blank", "context_len": 19}}}, "personages": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 37, "context": "It was a great glory to him , in a martial point of view , to have such trophies of his victory in his train ; and , of course , the more highly he honored the personages , the more glorious the trophy appeared", "context_len": 45}}}, "behests": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "In his most quiet , proper fashion , he is ever ready to do our behests ; and he never seems to tire of hymn-singing", "context_len": 25}}}, "amateur": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "They saw him pass slowly over the top of the elevation and down the other side , where it was not so easy to trace him , because of the abundance of grass which screened the amateur hunter", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "Fenn was looking for footprints in the soft ground , but he and his chums had made so many on their own account , on their previous visits to the place , and , as they were still visible ( for the ground had not frozen ) , the amateur detective was at a loss", "context_len": 55}}}, "unexceptionable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "was very amiable and good : he had taken a great deal of trouble to amuse the passengers , and his part was perfectly unexceptionable ; but if we all absent ourselves next time I do not think that he will take any offence", "context_len": 44}}}, "embarrassing": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "\u2019 s presence a little embarrassing", "context_len": 6}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 77, "context": "Seizing the first opportunity of slipping away from his schoolmates , he rushed upstairs to the dormitory with the design of throwing the note under Seabrooke 's bed or bureau , where it might be supposed to have fallen ; but seeing the trunk standing there ready packed , the impulse had taken him to put it in that , and without reflecting -- perhaps hardly caring -- that this would place Seabrooke in a still more embarrassing position , he thrust the note within , as Charlie Henderson saw , and fled from the room", "context_len": 96}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "She catechized Lilian freely about her housekeeping arrangements , hoped Nancy did her duty , and did not neglect to sweep out corners , told Bobby that his irrepressible curls looked girlish , and his hair ought to be cropped close every week , plied Peggy with embarrassing questions on the subject of fine needlework and stocking-darning , and drank four cups of tea in the meantime , with the air of one conferring a favour thereby", "context_len": 77}}}, "stimulant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The geographic position of the Creeks in the midst of warlike and aggressive nations was a powerful stimulant for making `` invincibles '' of their male offspring", "context_len": 27}}}, "tamasha": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Many hands make light work ; so , with a good deal of pushing and shouting and tamasha , the carriage was set up again on its wheels", "context_len": 28}}}, "salaam": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "And having said this with a beaming face , he made a little salaam and slipped through the skylight with an agile silentness of movement which showed Becky how easily he had done it before", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Some of them salaam to us , and I make a point of bowing with marked courtesy when they do so", "context_len": 21}}}, "handmaids": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "During late years the ladies of the Church Zenana Society have come in as an additional help to the Societies above-named , -- as true handmaids , \u2019 alike in the Panjab and in other parts of India", "context_len": 38}}}, "compound": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 48, "context": "At the outskirts of the town was a large , newly-erected factory , from which Gotha machines , their wings folded for transit , were being taken away in large motor-lorries , while sandwiched between the building and the outskirts of the town proper was a large barbed-wire compound within which were rows of wooden huts", "context_len": 56}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Objective or compound conjugation", "context_len": 4}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I know , but I do not believe it 's ever well to compound and treat with wrong", "context_len": 19}}}, "creepers": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 2, "context": "The Virginia creepers -- always the first to change -- turned a bright crimson ; here and there a maple flung out a scarlet branch , like a gorgeous banner in the air ; while chestnuts and birch showed a few golden leaves , in beautiful contrast to the vivid green of the foliage which was yet untouched", "context_len": 58}}}, "profoundly": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 28, "context": "Being personally acquainted with the before-mentioned parties , what was more natural than that , on hearing that this heinous crime had been committed , I should become profoundly interested in the case and should resolve to give it my earnest attention with the determination of solving the deep , dark mystery appertaining thereunto ?", "context_len": 55}}}, "elegance": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "Moreover , he noted Alaeddin 's eloquence and the elegance of his speech and was amazed thereat , he and all who were present with him in the Divan , whilst fire was kindled in the Vizier 's heart for envy of Alaeddin , so that he was like to die", "context_len": 51}}}, "mourners": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "Yelf , who has suffered so much from liquor , and is so destitute , bring it on to treat the mourners , and old Jonathan Smullen ( who is going as fast as he can in the same way as Yelf ) drink it , it kind of went against my feelings", "context_len": 53}}}, "seraph": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Tall , fair , elegant , graceful , with a face that Ary Scheffer might have chosen to paint for a seraph , -- her soul seems to correspond to her external appearance", "context_len": 33}}}, "conducive": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "I hope always to keep up the habit , which is so very conducive to health ; but of course I shall not walk so fast when the hot weather comes", "context_len": 31}}}, "superintendence": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "\u2019 This meant that about five Girls \u2019 Schools had been opened for Hindu , Sikh , and Muhammadan scholars , under the superintendence of the Catechist \u2019 s wife , being from time to time visited by the Amritsar Missionary ladies", "context_len": 42}}}, "bamboos": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I have , however , chick blinds to my outer doors ; these are made of thin split bamboos ; and if I let them down , no one can see in", "context_len": 32}}}, "tardy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "They catch the rain , in such cases , with cloths , and afterward wring out the water ; though in this instance , as the historians of that day say , the soldiers did not wait for this tardy method of supply , but the whole detachment held back their heads and opened their mouths , to catch the drops of rain as they fell", "context_len": 66}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "He was tardy in his work as contrasted with his sister , and Cissa died without seeing the work for which he had given the land accomplished", "context_len": 27}}}, "tidies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "I did not open my picture-box for some time after my arrival , but when it was opened it would have pleased you to have seen the pleasure given by its contents , including your lovely tidies", "context_len": 37}}}, "leaven": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "As is natural where the Missionaries are first-rate , there is a great deal of leaven working amongst the heathen", "context_len": 20}}}, "catechism": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "'Whew ! what a catechism ! My name 's Archie , if you want to know , and my godfathers and godmother gave it to me at my baptism", "context_len": 29}}}, "hookah": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "One of the ladies smoked a hookah ; had it been even invisible , we should have been made sensible of its presence by an occasional bubble-bubble sound , and then a perfume -- to our minds by no means odoriferous", "context_len": 41}}}, "odoriferous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "It is possible that the mention of this latter may be an interpolation by some ignorant copyist , who , seeing two only of the three favourite Oriental scents named , took upon himself to complete the odoriferous trinity , so dear to Arab writers , by the addition of ambergris", "context_len": 51}}}, "horridly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Another lady had her teeth horridly blackened by what she had been chewing ; but , generally speaking , the natives \u2019 teeth are very nice and white", "context_len": 28}}}, "communicants": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "Thank God , the sheaves are being gathered in ! What would dear Henry Martyn not have given to have seen what I saw to-day ? So many Natives remained to share the holy Feast , men and women , young and old , -- in our little church there must have been nearly if not quite fifty communicants", "context_len": 59}}}, "affecting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "It is on record that on one occasion the news of the signing of an important treaty at Washington affecting the Sioux was known to that tribe before the telegraphic messages could reach the army officers at the reservation", "context_len": 39}}}, "ripening": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "It is a great privilege to be in India , and specially now , when the blades are ripening , -- though , oh , how few in number , compared with the Muhammadans and heathen !", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "We gather from his statements , that at Odshi-ap\u00f3fa or `` Hickory Ground , '' which is a white town also , the `` priest , or fire-maker of the town '' had the privilege of determining the days of the busk , and that in doing so he was led by the ripening of the maize-crop and by the growth of the cassine-shrub", "context_len": 64}}}, "tailor": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` `` O my son , '' quoth she , `` God deliver me from this ! What , have I lost my wits like thee ? Put away this thought from thy mind and bethink thee who thou art , O my son , -- the son of a tailor , the poorest and least of the tailors in this city , and I also am thy mother and my folk are exceeding poor ; so how wilt thou dare to demand the Sultan 's daughter , whom her father would not vouchsafe to marry with kings ' sons and Sultans , except they were his peers in puissance and rank and noblesse ; nay , were they one degree less than he , he would not", "context_len": 128}}}, "superfluities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "\u2019 I do not want superfluities , \u2019 she wrote ; for mine is a modest income , and I should not like to spend it all on myself", "context_len": 29}}}, "startles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "For example , the number of servants kept , which often startles an Englishman , is unavoidable to some extent , arising from the very low wages given , and the small amount of work which each servant will undertake", "context_len": 40}}}, "tantamount": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "To rough it , \u2019 to brave the climate , to be reckless of hardships , would in the majority of instances be tantamount to suicide", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "The spreading out of the people from the Tallapoosa river to the Chatahutchi and from there to the Savannah must have involved a warfare , struggling , migration and settling down of several centuries , for the advance of the Maskoki proper in this direction was tantamount to the formation of the Maskoki confederacy by subduing or incorporating the tribes standing in their way , and to the still more lengthy process of settling among them", "context_len": 76}}}, "supporter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "A story has been told of an officer , himself a hearty supporter of Missions , who received a very unfavourable impression of one particular Missionary from observing the large amount of comfortable furniture which arrived at the said Missionary \u2019 s bungalow , for the latter \u2019 s use", "context_len": 50}}}, "uncalled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Nevertheless , such judgments will be passed , and it is well if Missionaries can live a life that shall render them uncalled for", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The various small offices in the division had sent in their uncalled for express matter , and this was now grouped under the present roof", "context_len": 25}}}, "approximate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The more closely modern Missionaries can approximate to Early Church Missionaries , the better", "context_len": 14}}}, "harmful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Things harmless may become harmful , if they prove an actual hindrance to success in the work , if they cause an actual lessening of influence", "context_len": 26}}}, "heroically": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Peggy gazed eagerly at an ancient chap-book , which set forth the stories of Dick Turpin and Jack Sheppard , interspersed with rude woodcuts of the gallows and whipping-post ; but she heroically put it aside , as being unsuitable for the day", "context_len": 43}}}, "quotations": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 89, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}}}, "prism": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Spirit and mind were as a clear prism , through which the light of Heaven fell , irradiating the atmosphere in which she lived , and which shone out all the more brightly when seen against the dark clouds of heathendom", "context_len": 41}}}, "irradiating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Spirit and mind were as a clear prism , through which the light of Heaven fell , irradiating the atmosphere in which she lived , and which shone out all the more brightly when seen against the dark clouds of heathendom", "context_len": 41}}}, "authoress": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "And now we were to welcome the well-known and gifted authoress into our house ! This was a privilege ; and earnestly did we look forward to the pleasure of receiving her ; though at the same time we were perhaps conscious of a slight shadow of doubt crossing our minds , as to how far one of Miss Tucker \u2019 s age would be able to accommodate herself to the new surroundings , and bear the trials incident to life and work in a tropical climate", "context_len": 87}}}, "thenceforth": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` A fine handsome child , with flashing black eyes ! '' Thus was Elizabeth Gilbert described at her birth in 1826 ; but at the age of three an attack of scarlet fever deprived her of eyesight ; and thenceforth , for upwards of fifty years , the beautiful things in the world were seen by her no more", "context_len": 60}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "He offered him any sum he might name as a ransom for his mother , wife , and child , and agreed that if he would deliver them up to him on the payment of the ransom , and depart from his dominions , he would thenceforth regard him as an ally and a friend", "context_len": 55}}}, "relationship": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 39, "context": "There was one body called the Kinsmen , who were the relatives of the king , or , at least , so considered , though , as there were fifteen thousand of them , it would seem that the relationship could not have been , in all cases , very near", "context_len": 51}}}, "reverential": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "It represented on her part a very special share in the sympathetic interest extended to all fellow-Missionaries , and on their side a reverential love and esteem , which in many cases could not have been deeper , had the tie been one of natural kinship", "context_len": 46}}}, "tiring": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 4, "context": "I guess I 'm tiring you with talking so much , said Chris ; I 'll tell you all about her some other time ; and Ted , replying , Well , somehow or other , I do seem to get exhausted precious easily , turned over and closed his eyes", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "after nine hours of raft , -- very tiring , for it involved much walking , and it was raining away , -- and twelve of d\u00e2k-gari", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "Those possessing such qualities may be sincere servants of Christ ; but these qualities spoil all likeness ! So , love , here is the result of my cogitations , as I reclined on the sofa to rest myself after rather a tiring little expedition", "context_len": 45}}}, "vernaculars": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "She always wrote her books in English , as there was never any difficulty in getting them translated into the vernaculars", "context_len": 21}}}, "dictionary": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "[ Footnote 638 : Keniz , a word which I can not find in any dictionary , but which appears to be the past participle ( in the secondary form for mecnouz , as ketil , slain , for mertoul , ) of keneza , a lost verb of which only the fourth form acneza , he drank from a cup ( kinz ) , survives , and to mean `` something drunk from a cup", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "The following is a literal rendering of the above passage : `` An upper chamber ( keszr ) and ( or ? ) a kiosk ( kushk , a word explained by a modern Syrian dictionary as meaning ' [ a building ] like a balcony projecting from the level of the rest of the house , ' but by others as an isolated building or pavilion erected on the top of a house , i", "context_len": 76}}}, "intelligibly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "She learnt to express herself intelligibly in both these tongues , though the acquisition of them cost her many an hour of hard labour", "context_len": 24}}}, "intelligently": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "By this incessant industry she acquired a large vocabulary , and was also soon able to read intelligently many vernacular books , which gave her an insight into the religious life of the people", "context_len": 34}}}, "vernacular": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "The Hitchiti , Apalatch\u00fakla and Sawokli tribes , with their branch villages , have also retained their language to this day , notwithstanding their membership in the extensive confederacy , a membership which must have lasted for centuries ; and in fact we can not see how the retention of vernacular speech could hurt the interests of the community even in the slightest way", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Here we present three crackerjack stories of baseball , football , and basketball , written in the vernacular of the boy of to-day , full of action , suspense and thrills , in language every boy will understand , and which we know will be enthusiastically endorsed by all boys", "context_len": 50}}}, "cementing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "During the greater part of 1876 Miss Tucker remained at Amritsar , cementing her friendship with the ladies there , learning the Hindustani and Panjabi languages , studying the ways of the people , and writing little books for translation into the Native tongues", "context_len": 44}}}, "colloquially": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "At her age it was by no means so easy to master a new language as for a younger person ; -- indeed , hard as she toiled , she never did absolutely master any Indian language colloquially , though for a time she became thorough mistress of the Hindustani grammar and construction", "context_len": 53}}}, "annas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "[ 38 ] As rupees and annas may puzzle you , I write in English fashion --", "context_len": 17}}}, "charitably": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Nancy 's hat was a conspicuous object among the smart throng , for Lilian , after one peep at the fair , had charitably kept house herself , and allowed her hand-maiden to take a holiday , and the damsel seemed to have collected so many admirers that the luckless Sally was entirely eclipsed", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "On one special Monday morning in May Peggy got out of bed in that peculiar frame of mind which Father charitably called 'highly strung , ' and Nancy broadly defined as 'having black dog on your back", "context_len": 37}}}, "blemishes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And when she must acknowledge some blemishes , she finds some kind excuse for them", "context_len": 15}}}, "weaves": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Donald Pierce is sent to solve the mystery of his father \u2019 s disappearance , into the unmapped barrens where King Stively weaves his web of wickedness , and rules a territory the size of a small empire with a ruthlessness and cunning that baffles the best of the Mounted Police", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "overworked": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "I feel as if one of my chief works here must be to try and keep up the spirits of my poor , anxious , overworked companions", "context_len": 27}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "At one time the costers had used their donkeys and ponies shamefully , had overworked and underfed them ; but gradually they were made to see how much better it was to treat their animals well", "context_len": 36}}}, "chiselled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "She is so fair , so graceful , so pure-looking , with her chiselled profile and her sweet expression ; I could not make out , however , anything that would do for the lion", "context_len": 35}}}, "populated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Oh , a Sikh village is a curious place ; built of mud , and pretty thickly populated , it reminds me of an ant-hill", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "The by-way that we were now following appeared to be little used , yet it was a wide road and a good one , and probably served as the means of communication between isolated farms , or it may have led to some lonely grist-mill which had been built for the convenience of that thinly populated region", "context_len": 57}}}, "perambulating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It would have amused you to have seen Margaret and me perambulating the village , going through its muddy lanes ; sometimes so narrow that one could have touched the walls on either hand , -- or nearly so", "context_len": 39}}}, "sparrows": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 0, "context": "Sparrows and chickadees , and other birds which make their home with us during the winter , were hopping merrily over the leafless branches , and twittering ceaselessly to one another , as if they were telling of the happy time near at hand , when the warm south winds would blow , and the trees and bushes be covered with their beautiful green summer dress", "context_len": 66}}}, "catacomb": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 72, "context": "But , Laura , you who have an eye for the picturesque , and a soul for the romantic , you should have had a glimpse of us yesterday in the Pandit \u2019 s house , at evening prayer ! The long mud-built room looked strange enough by day ; but at night seen by the gleam of one lamp , it looked -- like the entrance to a cave or a catacomb", "context_len": 73}}}, "priests": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 7, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}}}, "translating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": ", who is translating some of the Bible into Panjabi , wanted -- -- s assistance", "context_len": 16}}}, "detest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` You have told me so before , but I detest compliments , '' was my brusque rejoinder", "context_len": 18}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Every impulse of our hearts led us to detest the cruel deed of treachery about to be consummated , but which we could not prevent", "context_len": 25}}}, "idolatry": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "And as in the field , so at home , you know well , my love and reverence for him came little short of idolatry", "context_len": 25}}}, "imbibing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The Munshi \u2019 s father , the priest , seemed to have had rather a natural fear of his son \u2019 s imbibing what he would consider wrong doctrine", "context_len": 29}}}, "critic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "After a while the priest observed , At first I listened as a critic ; now I listen with interest", "context_len": 20}}}, "tradesman": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "But now Aggie and I are both staying here with gentry of the first class in every way , and you say you 'll be my friend even if I am a tradesman ? ''", "context_len": 35}}}, "idolater": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "This rich man paid the Munshi to come and read the Granth to him , -- I suppose for amusement , as he himself is a Hindu and idolater", "context_len": 29}}}, "refers": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 36, "context": "The notice that each of the seven plants distributed to the Indians belonged , or was the emblem of a certain gens or division of people , is gathered from this passage only , and probably refers to the ingredients of some war-physic , which only a limited number of the gentes may have been entitled to contribute to the annual p\u00faskita", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 1, "context": "This refers more particularly to the Seminoles of the northern parts of what is now Florida ; these Indians showed , like the Creeks , hostile intentions towards the thirteen states during and after the Revolution , and conjointly with the Upper Creeks on Tallapoosa river concluded a treaty of friendship with the Spaniards at Pensacola in May , 1784", "context_len": 60}}}, "utensils": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "This change of mind was symbolized by the custom of the women of breaking to pieces all the household utensils of the past year , and replacing them by new ones ; the men refitted all their property so as to look new , and it was considered extremely disgraceful , even for the most indigent , to eat any of the new maize before the annual busk ( Sketch , pp", "context_len": 72}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 78, "context": "The genie carried him into a treasury full of all manner of gold and silver and precious jewels past count or reckoning , price or estimation ; then he brought him into another place , where he saw all the requisites of the table , platters and spoons and ewers and basins and cups , of gold and silver , and thence to the kitchen , where he found cooks , [ 476 ] with their cooking-gear and utensils , all on like wise of gold and silver", "context_len": 88}}}, "topi": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Of course in England it would look funny to see a lady of my age all in white , with a topi and pugri and white parasol ; but it does not look funny in India", "context_len": 36}}}, "parasol": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "As the last pink parasol and straw hat disappeared through the little gate , Peggy and Bobby emerged from their retirement , somewhat stiff and cramped , and were just about to give vent to a wild war-whoop as some slight relief to their pent-up feelings when they noticed that after all they were not alone", "context_len": 56}}}, "disrespectful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "I believe that we Missionaries are much respected ; we are treated with courtesy ; and one of us may walk alone through crowds of hundreds of natives , and never have a disrespectful word", "context_len": 35}}}, "fatter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Piggy being no longer than the stick but much fatter , stuck in the hole and then there was Lady Annabella suddenly very much alive and crowding Mr", "context_len": 28}}}, "comet": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "He would have me running about the country ; but really I am too old to be a comet like my nephew", "context_len": 22}}}, "pavements": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "This shower proved the last of the rain for that day , and by twelve o'clock the clouds had all rolled away and the pavements were drying rapidly , giving fresh hope to Maggie and Bessie that they would be able to go over to the policeman 's house ; but before that Aunt Patty had returned", "context_len": 57}}}, "goring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "And as for being afraid of a stolid buffalo , that looks as if it never would dream of goring any one , even if its horns were not so set on that it could not do such a thing , there would be small excuse for that", "context_len": 48}}}, "provokes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He is so careful of Mission money , that he almost provokes us by travelling in ways likely to make him ill", "context_len": 22}}}, "betrothed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "`` O Aescendune , once happy Aescendune ! '' was the thought , the bitter thought , as each hour placed a larger barrier of space between Alfgar and his late home ; all its happy memories came freshly back upon him , and particularly the thought of Ethelgiva , his betrothed , from whom he was so ruthlessly torn , torn as if he left part of himself behind", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "But amongst those who stood nearest to the place he must occupy were his betrothed , her mother , Bertha , and young Hermann , who had already got into several quarrels through his fierce espousing of the cause of the accused", "context_len": 42}}}, "unite": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 46, "context": "The sealed packet only contained a formal summons to the general rendezvous of the forces , which was to take place at Dorchester , the episcopal city of the great Midland diocese , and situated in a central position , where Wessex and Mercia could easily unite the flower of their youth", "context_len": 52}}}, "emily": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 0, "context": "Emily and I went armed with books and pictures , to try and sow a little good seed amongst the women , whilst Mr", "context_len": 24}}}, "baptism": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` At all events I think that you should delay no longer , but should seek instruction and baptism , which we will afford you ; and then , unless you really feel life is in danger , you should return to him and try to bear your lot ; it may not be so hard as you think", "context_len": 59}}}, "masculine": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "I had never , never even by my most intimate friends , been spoken of as the `` little thing , '' for I was a tall girl and somewhat large in my ideas , and if anything rather masculine in my mind , and to be spoken of as a little thing , and by Albert Fanning , was about the final straw which broke the camel 's back", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "This short story of 180 pages is captivating from start to finish , and the masculine reader takes off his hat , and the feminine reader courtesies , to this matron of the tubs , with her homely heroism and true kindness of heart", "context_len": 44}}}, "nouns": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "The parts of speech being but imperfectly differentiated , tenses can be expressed in nouns by adding suffixes : m\u00edko chief , mikot\u00e1ti , m\u00edko-\u014d m\u0101 one who was , has been chief ; m\u00edko-ta ` l\u00e1ni a future chief ; adsulagit\u00e1ti the defunct forefathers", "context_len": 45}}}, "palanquin": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Well , I reached the d\u00e2k bungalow ( kind of inn ) early in the morning , took early breakfast , and started in my duli ( kind of palanquin ) at about 6", "context_len": 34}}}, "stoppages": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "They made little of it , but went at the rate of nearly four miles an hour , including brief stoppages", "context_len": 21}}}, "beguile": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Now she was unique in eloquence and sweetness of speech and she proceeded to beguile him and bespeak him with words significant [ 608 ] and sweet , so she might entangle him yet straitlier in the toils of her love", "context_len": 41}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "When with Chris it was forward clear to the bow to lean over the rail and see the magnificent prow cut the water ; or way to the stern , to watch the far-shining train , the screws churned into white foam behind them ; or an hour 'midships , where the ever-varying amusements with which the steerage passengers beguile the weary hours can be looked down upon from the saloon deck of either first or second cabin", "context_len": 78}}}, "mountaineers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "But he had contrived to send detachments around by circuitous and precipitous paths , which even the mountaineers had deemed impracticable , and thus attack his enemies suddenly and unexpectedly from above their own positions", "context_len": 35}}}, "inserted": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` This ( or a similar text ) is evidently the original of Galland 's translation of this episode and it is probable , therefore , that the French translator inserted the mention `` of a certain warm drink '' ( tea ) , out of that mistaken desire for local colouring at all costs which has led so many French authors ( especially those of our own immediate day ) astray", "context_len": 72}}}, "oneself": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "The day passes so fast ; and perhaps at the end one feels -- What has been done ? -- how little ! But as for sitting down to amuse oneself with an English story-book , -- how can that be done by your attached old Missionary Auntie ? \u2019", "context_len": 50}}}, "threepence": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Bobby had a pretty set of bantams , whose small eggs afforded him much delight and some slight profit , for Aunt Helen bought them from him at threepence a dozen -- a transaction which he always recorded in chalk upon the hen-house door , the pennies being carefully put by towards the purchase of a pair of fantail pigeons , which was at present the summit of his ambition", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "When his pocket money of a penny a week was increased to threepence , he felt himself on the high road to wealth , and ere long he was the possessor of a Bible and a grammar , which he set himself to study whenever he could get a spare moment", "context_len": 51}}}, "disrespect": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "my language , my religion , are strange , and yet I neither receive nor fear the slightest disrespect", "context_len": 19}}}, "connects": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I think that two things are wanting to its perfection ; -- first , the soft blue haze which one connects with distant mountains", "context_len": 24}}}, "acquires": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It is a curious thing , dear Laura , that kind of instinct which one acquires in India ! I have often and often thought on the subject", "context_len": 28}}}, "cheetah": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "There would be some fun in meeting with a cheetah or a hyena , -- I should not like a bear unless there were a kud [ 70 ] between us , -- but I shrink from the world and his wife", "context_len": 42}}}, "lurk": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "One takes one \u2019 s early walk in a lonely place , where the cheetah or snake may lurk , without the smallest alarm", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Why might not Bomba lurk about the river bank to the north and descry Japazy \u2019 s coming ? Then he could boldly make himself known and get the interview that he had come so far to seek", "context_len": 38}}}, "outlying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "She knew about Batala , and was interested in the place , no doubt , as in all other outlying parts where Missionary work had been even fitfully attempted", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "He did not know where he was going , but he felt that his only chance was to go with the current till he should be swept near some of the outlying rocks , when they might be drawn into an eddy , and so be able to climb up on to the shell-covered stones , and wait there till they were seen", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Neville intended to carry his bride back with him to India as soon as matters could possibly be arranged , for he held a high position under Government in an outlying province , and could only be spared on short leave", "context_len": 41}}}, "fitfully": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "A raw wind , chill and dank , came from the east , soughing fitfully through the bare limbs of the trees and sending fallen leaves scurrying along the ground", "context_len": 30}}}, "perseveringly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Perhaps Maggie and Fred might not have kept their plots in such good order , or worked so perseveringly at weeding , raking , and watering , had it not been for Harry and Bessie , who never forgot to go each day and look at their plants , and never suffered a weed to do more than show the tip of its head above the soil", "context_len": 67}}}, "rallies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "If however dear Florrie rallies nicely , \u2019 she wrote , I have not the slightest intention of going to cloudland again", "context_len": 22}}}, "cloudland": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "If however dear Florrie rallies nicely , \u2019 she wrote , I have not the slightest intention of going to cloudland again", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Glaisher , and by those who , like him , have made perilous journeys into cloudland", "context_len": 16}}}, "prosaic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "The question of decorations was much discussed , for while Father suggested hanging the walls with sacking , and Lilian voted for garlanding them with wild flowers , both ideas were rejected , the one as too prosaic and the other as not sufficiently durable , and it was not until Peggy conceived the brilliant thought of lining their dwelling with moss that a satisfactory solution was arrived at", "context_len": 69}}}, "avoidance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Arrangements were made for his Baptism in the Church by a Native clergyman ; the matter being kept as quiet as possible , for avoidance of the opposition which was sure to arise", "context_len": 33}}}, "intervene": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Miss Tucker was told only on the morning of the day what was about to happen ; and great was her delight , as well as her fear that some hindrance might intervene", "context_len": 33}}}, "intuitive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I had a kind of intuitive feeling , \u2019 she said , that something might come to prevent the Convert from openly confessing his Lord", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It 's a sort of intuitive --", "context_len": 7}}}, "influencing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "She flung a brick at the head of one Christian Native , who had had a hand in influencing the young Muhammadan ; she raved and beat her breast ; she cursed and tore her hair ; she declared to her son that if he became a Christian she would die", "context_len": 51}}}, "educate": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 22, "context": "Perhaps some years hence a Nihilist crop may rise from tens of thousands of sharp conceited lads whom the Government so carefully educate without God ! They can not possibly all get the prizes in life which they look for ; they won \u2019 t dig , -- so will naturally swell the dangerous classes", "context_len": 55}}}, "forbids": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The mother still holds her unhappy son in bonds , and forbids him even to breathe the air of our compound", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "But our religion forbids me to nourish revenge", "context_len": 8}}}, "departs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "All beauty generally departs with youth , but this woman is really attractive still", "context_len": 14}}}, "gesticulations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He uses sometimes almost frantic gesticulations", "context_len": 6}}}, "pomegranates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "She was so much pleased that she gave him four pomegranates", "context_len": 11}}}, "inefficient": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 78, "context": "With some show of hesitation the man surrendered the thing , and upon inspection I found it to be a reed of about a foot in length , with a mouthpiece shaped something like that of a whistle , and with four small holes drilled in the length of the tube , whereby an expert performer might produce seven distinct tones ; but the tones were not consecutive , and the instrument was altogether a very poor and inefficient affair", "context_len": 80}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` My parish sweetly harmonious , my whole works stereotyping in twenty-one volumes , and my ministry not altogether inefficient at the age of seventy-three", "context_len": 25}}}, "enjoys": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "I do not think that my Margaret at all enjoys being away from us in the schoolhouse , though she keeps bright and brave", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "If you and Bessie could see how much he enjoys the chair , you would feel quite repaid for any pains you took to earn it for him", "context_len": 28}}}, "cricket": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 39, "context": "It was funny to see how the young American citizen was merging into the British public schoolboy , for Archie was losing his Western accent , which only cropped out now and then when he was excited , and cricket and football were beginning to replace Indians and grizzlies in his conversation ; but he was totally unspoilt by his new life , and as jolly and hearty as ever", "context_len": 70}}}, "bolstering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Dashing , energetic games had been the delight of my companions ; and I begin to imagine that cricket , rounders , and bolstering form no bad preparation for Missionary life", "context_len": 31}}}, "cheetahs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Those who might be timid girls in England fearlessly travel at night , quite alone -- save for the company of wild-looking natives , -- through lonely mountain-passes , perhaps through lightning and storm , with the possibility of meeting cheetahs , bears , and snakes", "context_len": 46}}}, "supplemented": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` No , '' answered Tom meekly , and he fell to arranging his books , a task which Sid supplemented by piling the sporting goods indiscriminately in a corner", "context_len": 30}}}, "apostate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": ", -- of whom at that time she thought so well and hopefully as an established Christian , and who in later years was to grieve her most bitterly by himself becoming an apostate", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "This apostate parricide ! never would he live to kiss the hand of such a man ; better die at once , while yet pure from innocent blood", "context_len": 28}}}, "martyred": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Dear little martyred innocent , -- dying because her father gave himself to Christ ! B -- n intends to bring his two elder children ; but of course nothing can be done while baby is dying", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "First and foremost was the king of Southern England , the valiant Ironside , and his attendant and friend Alfgar ; Elfwyn and Father Cuthbert from Aescendune , with the Lady Hilda and Ethelgiva ; Herstan , his wife Bertha , and son Hermann , from Clifton , with his sisters ; and Ethelm , the new bishop of Dorchester , the successor of the martyred Ednoth", "context_len": 67}}}, "merino": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I have here a couple of merino dresses of Maggie 's , and a warm sack , which she has outgrown", "context_len": 22}}}, "squats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The darzi , [ 48 ] who squats in the verandah , is busy on a magnificent dressing-gown , which I have ordered", "context_len": 23}}}, "clips": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Whatever one gives to be made or mended is made or mended in the verandah ; and the darzi , as he cuts out , clips , and sews , talks -- perhaps with the pankah-wala , perhaps a stranger , perhaps the munshi ( tutor ) whose pupil is not quite ready to take her lesson", "context_len": 57}}}, "sews": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Whatever one gives to be made or mended is made or mended in the verandah ; and the darzi , as he cuts out , clips , and sews , talks -- perhaps with the pankah-wala , perhaps a stranger , perhaps the munshi ( tutor ) whose pupil is not quite ready to take her lesson", "context_len": 57}}}, "apathetic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "In almost complete silence the securing-ropes were made fast and the gangways run out by apathetic workmen , while with the utmost dispatch the disembarkation of men and stores began", "context_len": 30}}}, "verbs": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 21, "context": "There is no real substantive verb in the language , and adjectives , when becoming verbified , are turned into attributive verbs , as in Creek : w\u00e1nti strong , hard ; tsaw\u00e1ntus I am strong ; w\u00e1ntus he , it is strong , hard ; w\u00e1ntatik not strong ; w\u00e1ntigus he is very strong ; w\u00e1ntatis he is not strong ; wanta'hl\u00e1tis he is not strong at all", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "The possessive pronoun of the third person singular and plural ( im- , in- , i- ) is prefixed in the same manner to substantives to indicate possession , as it is to verbs to show that an act is performed in the interest or to the detriment of the verbal subject or object", "context_len": 54}}}, "genders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "to come to my rescue , but battled with verbs and genders as well as I could", "context_len": 17}}}, "chaperon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Last night I had to chaperon to our noisy , bustling station after dark a young Missionary , who looks to me quite unfledged", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "If I were perfectly dumb , I should still be useful as a chaperon", "context_len": 14}}}, "unfledged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Last night I had to chaperon to our noisy , bustling station after dark a young Missionary , who looks to me quite unfledged", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "There I met the school-teacher , Miss -- -- , with her young sister , yet more unfledged , bound on the same errand", "context_len": 24}}}, "shocks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "It almost shocks my sense of propriety , young girls travelling at night , -- it is funny even to an old lady , hurrying up and down a bustling platform amongst Natives", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "And as to poor Miss Carter , her nerves have not yet got over the awful shocks that Irene subjected her to", "context_len": 22}}}, "gusto": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He told me with gusto that he had once gone to the house of a Muhammadan friend , who happened to have a little hole in his door , on one of the days of the fast", "context_len": 37}}}, "hypocrite": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "But it was useless to call him `` Methodist '' and `` hypocrite '' ; he had learnt from his mother the value of Bible reading , and possessed sufficient character to care little what his companions said", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "One accused him of `` turning Methodist , '' and departed ; another warned him not to become a hypocrite , and remarked , `` Bad as you were , I never thought you would come to this , old fellow ! '' So for a time he was nearly deserted", "context_len": 51}}}, "exempted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 67, "context": "And fearful places he found them to be ! Boys who were taken to gaol for the first time were put with old and hardened criminals ; the prisons were dirty and ill-smelling ; the dungeons were dark and unhealthy ; and , unless prisoners could afford to pay for comforts , they were obliged to sleep on cold bare floors , even delicate women not being exempted from such cruel treatment", "context_len": 72}}}, "dregs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Of course I had a little fever , but it has left no dregs", "context_len": 14}}}, "alterations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "To be of easy remembrance , there must be something connected with these events which forcibly strikes the imagination and in later times stands out as the principal fact , while minor features of its occurrence disappear or become subject to alterations in the progress of time", "context_len": 47}}}, "evangelist": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Birinus had fixed the centre of his missionary labours at Dorchester , only six miles distant , but the Abbey was the fruit of the heroic zeal of another evangelist , upon whom his mantle fell -- St", "context_len": 38}}}, "pushes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "He said that a shepherd pushes one sheep after another into the fold , and when all are in follows himself ; and that so he would get the people into the Christian fold , and then follow them", "context_len": 39}}}, "unfitness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "You think that your strong point is my unfitness for an out-station", "context_len": 12}}}, "residing": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Fancy the encouragement of seeing B -- n , the one Christian convert residing in Batala , and sharing the Cup with him in our dear Amritsar Church", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 49, "context": "Not content with their morning spent in the Grasmere Church , and beside his grave in the little churchyard without , they even made their way to Wordsworth 's old home -- beautiful Rydal Mount -- hoping , on the strength of a card of introduction to the gentleman residing there , to possibly be allowed to see the house", "context_len": 60}}}, "char": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "CHAR", "context_len": 1}}}, "oppositions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "But for one who is unfaithful , for one who turns his back upon the Light , after seeming to be indeed a Convert , there are many who stand firm , persevering to the end , despite difficulties , discouragements , and bitter oppositions", "context_len": 45}}}, "hindrances": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Difficulties , oppositions , hindrances , prospects of loneliness , imperfect knowledge of Indian languages , increasing age , -- all these were as nothing in the way", "context_len": 28}}}, "isolation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "This isolation from European society is not what I should have chosen for one who can exercise so much influence for good among her own countrymen ; and whose pen can do more for India than perhaps the lives of many others", "context_len": 42}}}, "wrongly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "So the poor old lady sadly thought , taking shame and repentance to herself for all the peevishness and ill-temper of the last two days , seeing where she had acted wrongly and unwisely , and making new resolutions for the future", "context_len": 42}}}, "dissuade": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I do not wish to dissuade you ; indeed , I can not myself see how you can get out of this most mistaken engagement , for the man has behaved well , and I am the first to acknowledge that ; but has it ever occurred to you that you do a man an absolute and terrible injustice when you marry him , loving with all your heart and soul another man ? Do you think it is fair to him ? Do n't you think he ought at least to know this ? ''", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He can \u2019 t dissuade me , but I don \u2019 t want him to try", "context_len": 16}}}, "quadruple": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "had not gone to Batala , but had taken the advice of others , and had remained at Amritsar ! Possibly she might , by devoting herself to writing alone , have accomplished treble or quadruple the number of little books and tracts for India which she did accomplish", "context_len": 49}}}, "fastness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Yet it could have been no light matter , -- this going forth alone , with only one young companion , into a very fastness of Muhammadanism and Heathenism", "context_len": 29}}}, "housekeepers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Why should not other middle-aged ladies go out , as she went out ? -- not necessarily always to attempt full Zenana work ; but to be protectors , housekeepers , nurses , to younger and more active ladies ? Whether it would be right to use any portion of Mission-funds for such a purpose may be doubted ; and in many a case Mission rooms could not be spared ; but there are exceptions as to the latter", "context_len": 79}}}, "angular": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Thin-skinned , anxious , feeble-spirited ladies , easily worried and easily vexed , will not do ; and angular , managing , argumentative ladies would be quite as unsuitable", "context_len": 29}}}, "argumentative": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "There were three with whom he spent many social evenings : a pious young man named Watson , an argumentative one named Osborne , and James Ralph , who fancied himself a poet", "context_len": 33}}}, "unsuitable": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "Peggy gazed eagerly at an ancient chap-book , which set forth the stories of Dick Turpin and Jack Sheppard , interspersed with rude woodcuts of the gallows and whipping-post ; but she heroically put it aside , as being unsuitable for the day", "context_len": 43}}}, "discomforts": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "Those alone may venture who are not only fairly strong in health , vigorous in spirit , fearless as to difficulties , and careless as to discomforts , but who are also gentle , kind-hearted , sympathetic , willing to yield to the judgment of others , ready to please and not to rule", "context_len": 54}}}, "banyan": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Mention has been made of the Mission-tree , -- a large banyan , in front of the Amritsar bungalow , where Miss Tucker had now spent so many months", "context_len": 29}}}, "rooted": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 43, "context": "The tiny fences were pulled up , and scattered far and wide ; lady-slippers , mignonette , verbenas , and all the other simple flowers which had flourished so well , and given such pride and delight to the little gardeners , were rooted up and trampled into the earth ; and , worse than all , the beloved heliotrope and geranium were torn leaf from leaf and sprig from sprig , while their main stems had been twisted and bent , till no hope remained that even these could be revived", "context_len": 92}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}}}, "resided": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 71, "context": "Her three cousins -- Gwendolyn , Russell , and Belle -- had greeted her cordially as soon as the train drew up in a station which , for size and grandeur , surpassed her wildest dreams , and then escorted her between a bewildering panorama of flashing lights , brilliant shop windows , swiftly moving cars , and people in an endless stream to another depot , for her Uncle Robert resided in the suburbs", "context_len": 75}}}, "walled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "The Mobilians seem to be the descendants of the inhabitants of Mauvila , a walled town , at some distance from the seat of the Tuscalusa chief , and dependent on him", "context_len": 32}}}, "gatherings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "For instance , she began speedily to sit upon the floor as Natives do ; and at Indian gatherings or feasts she would not only sit as they sat , but would share their food", "context_len": 35}}}, "bumps": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 68, "context": "The sandals were anything but comfortable , and her feet hurt dreadfully on the stones , while every gorse-bush she passed seemed to be stretching out spiky fingers to scratch her bare legs ; she was tired after her morning 's adventures on the moors , and the eight miles seemed to lengthen out to an interminable vista , in spite of the way being downhill ; sundry bumps and bruises , which she had never noticed at the time , began to ache now , and the bee-stings on her neck smarted , until she hardly knew how to bear the pain", "context_len": 103}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Drunkard bumps into it", "context_len": 4}}}, "dissuaded": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "[ Footnote 138 : Here Burton adds , `` Indeed I had well nigh determined to forfeit all my profit of the Ninth Statue and to bear thee away to Bassorah as my own bride , when my comrade and councillor dissuaded me from so doing , lest I should bring about my death", "context_len": 54}}}, "aloe": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Batala is a picturesque old town , with fine banyan-trees , and many old mango-tree gardens towards the north , enclosed either by walls or by aloe hedges , curiously appropriate for A", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "He has a black heart and a tongue that is as bitter as the aloe plant , said Hondura", "context_len": 19}}}, "hedges": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "But active as he was , he soon found he was no match for the young rascal in front of him , whose feet scarcely seemed to touch the ground , and who threw himself headlong over fences and hedges , as though he had forgotten he had a neck and limbs which might be broken", "context_len": 56}}}, "elementary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "to a little better education , perhaps his little village school may prove not a bad idea , for the scholars would learn what is good from him , though they could only have elementary teaching", "context_len": 36}}}, "quote": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "Tom and Phil forgot the little scene of the afternoon when they got down to studying that night , and as lessons were getting to be pretty `` stiff , '' to quote Sid , it was necessary to put in considerable time over books", "context_len": 45}}}, "curtailing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "It seems , however , worth while to give fuller records of these early days , when all was fresh , and when Miss Tucker \u2019 s interests were keenly awake to her novel surroundings , even though more fulness here means some curtailing later", "context_len": 45}}}, "observable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "It was observable , almost at a glance , that they proceeded from the young and giddy , and that their elders refrained from joining in the cry", "context_len": 28}}}, "terseness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Moreover , the mode of expression gains a terseness and vigour , not always characteristic of the earlier correspondence", "context_len": 19}}}, "matting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "When he came thither , he entered and saw her sleeping on her back on a piece of matting ; so he went up to her and sitting down [ 639 ] on her breast , [ 640 ] drew his dagger and cried out at her ; whereupon she awoke and opening her eyes , saw a man , a Maugrabin , with a drawn dagger , sitting on her breast [ 641 ] and offering to kill her", "context_len": 80}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Her bedroom furniture consisted of a native bedstead , a small table , a wardrobe and two chairs , with a piece of thin matting on the floor , and one or two thin durries", "context_len": 35}}}, "habitable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "However , we did our best with matting , screens , and furniture , to make it look habitable", "context_len": 19}}}, "chinks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "The small , dirty hut , with the hard ground for its floor , its miserable roof , and chinks and crannies which let in the wind and damp , was no place for a sick child on these cool August nights ; and now that Dolly could be moved without putting her to so much pain , it was thought best it should be done", "context_len": 66}}}, "uncurtained": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Clark also , referring to this large and somewhat dreary palace , says of it : The winds blew through many chinks in the uncurtained doors ; and the house was once likened to Eden , because four streams flowed through it", "context_len": 42}}}, "presses": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": ", being stored up in those most convenient presses in the walls , which add exceedingly to one \u2019 s comfort", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "It returned her care by an affection which was quite embarrassing to the worthy girl , for however attractive a pet lamb may be , it becomes distinctly in the way when it insists upon following you into the dining-room with the dinner , or presses its attentions on you when you are engaged in cleaning the grate or scrubbing the floors", "context_len": 62}}}, "vestibule": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Presently they stopped at what seemed to the little girl a great palace with broad white marble steps and tall carved columns lighted by myriads of colored lights and the vestibule was hung with vines", "context_len": 35}}}, "faqir": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "My being elderly and unmarried seemed to be giving an impression that I was a kind of saint or faqir , -- perhaps my being thin and wearing my faithful old green dress added to the impression", "context_len": 37}}}, "yoked": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Let Mission Miss Sahibas from late hours refrain , For they must rise early , and bear a hard strain , Like vigorous cart-horses , drawing a wain , That pull well together , when yoked twain and twain", "context_len": 39}}}, "itinerate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "What an extraordinary and somewhat romantic position I am in , for an elderly lady , who in her youth hardly ever stirred from a London home ! How amazed we should have been when we were girls , if we could have known that I was to find my home in an Oriental palace -- afar from all Europeans -- and itinerate a little in heathen villages ! How good God has been to your loving sister ! \u2019", "context_len": 80}}}, "optional": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Of course , it is quite optional with the servants to attend or not ; and the workmen could easily have drowned my voice , if they had chosen to do so", "context_len": 32}}}, "proprieties": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I am rather careful about the proprieties ; and to be carried in a box is the correct thing", "context_len": 19}}}, "auditors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Why have I come ? Silence amongst my auditors", "context_len": 9}}}, "muhammadans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Muhammadans believe a great deal of the Old Testament ; one can talk to them of Father Adam , and Mother Eve , without shocking them in the least", "context_len": 29}}}, "discourtesy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I have not met with any discourtesy", "context_len": 7}}}, "talc": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I remember how I looked on the picture of such a red duli , painted on talc , and pitied native ladies for having to travel in a box", "context_len": 29}}}, "invalided": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "For Miss Tucker to remain alone at Batala , without a single European companion , could not be thought of ; and so many Missionaries had been invalided during the past unhealthy year , that no one else could possibly be spared", "context_len": 42}}}, "sanctioned": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Instead of punishing him , the king sanctioned the deed , took all their possessions , and sent Sigeferth 's widow to be kept prisoner at Malmesbury", "context_len": 27}}}, "boarder": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 43, "context": "I had never greater difficulty in keeping my composure than I had during that awful meal , but I did eat a cutlet when it was put on my plate , and I did manage to talk to my neighbour , a new boarder who had come up from the country , and did not know her way about anywhere", "context_len": 60}}}, "presidency": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The only picture on the walls was a woodcut from a newspaper , showing the homely features of Abraham Lincoln who had been nominated a short time before by the newly-born Republican party , for the presidency of the United States", "context_len": 41}}}, "cited": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Centurion \u2019 s words , I am a man under authority , \u2019 may be cited as peculiarly applicable to her", "context_len": 22}}}, "ticketing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Several pounds have been spent on prizes , and Florrie and I were for hours yesterday ticketing and preparing them", "context_len": 20}}}, "bari": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "O dear ! O dear ! Why did we trust that sust [ 60 ] kahar , -- or eat up all the bread ? O how shall the bari Bibi ever be fed ? I must go and try to cheer up poor Florrie , who suffers from her head , in addition to being in this fix", "context_len": 59}}}, "rarity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "On account , I suppose , of an Englishwoman with any silver hair being a rarity in India , I seem to be sometimes considered wonderfully old", "context_len": 27}}}, "devi": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "She told me afterwards that she was afraid they were brought as religious offerings , -- flowers are what are used for such offerings , -- and she had heard repeatedly that we are devi", "context_len": 35}}}, "deluded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "\u2019 [ 63 ] What gross , fearful ignorance ! I heard on good authority that in one place in India , not the Panjab , offerings are actually made to a dead European , who was a special object of dread to the Natives , and whom they therefore wish to propitiate as a kind of demon ! Do not the poor , deluded creatures want teachers ? I find the women in general very gentle and courteous , and quite willing to listen when they are spoken to on the subject of religion", "context_len": 95}}}, "sewn": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 61, "context": "'If only we could stay up here a good long time we 'd bring lots of things from home , and paint pictures for the walls , and put them in cork frames , and I really believe , if I tried , I could make up one of those hearthrugs out of little scraps of cloth all pinched up and sewn on , like Nancy made last winter for her sister 's wedding present", "context_len": 75}}}, "engrosses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "-- My note to dear Leila will tell you of the change which now a good deal engrosses my mind", "context_len": 20}}}, "trustingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "They have seen two ladies going fearlessly , trustingly , amongst them , one of them old , and the other so ill that she has fairly broken down in her work -- for Christ \u2019 s sake ! These things may tell more even than preaching", "context_len": 47}}}, "unconnected": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "It was a pure coincidence that the New Zealander and he were on the same boat ; it might be that the latter 's visit to the bridge was utterly unconnected with him", "context_len": 33}}}, "repetitions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "But , oh , how sleepy one gets over the book ! It is so full of repetitions ; the same ideas and stories over and over again", "context_len": 28}}}, "unbelievers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "One is so perpetually reading of the torments of unbelievers , the fires of Gehenna , etc", "context_len": 17}}}, "enlarged": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "Carrisford 's sad face had lighted with a smile , and Ram Dass had been so filled with rapture that he had enlarged upon it and explained to his master how simple it would be to accomplish numbers of other things", "context_len": 41}}}, "religions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Of the sun-worship , which underlies the religions of all the tribes in the Gulf territories , only slight intimations are contained in the Kasi'hta legend , and the important problem , whether the Creeks ever crossed the Mississippi river from west to east in their migrations , seems to be settled by it in the negative , although other legends may be adduced as speaking in its favor", "context_len": 69}}}, "ticking": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 101, "context": "the agitated hives , and letting her glance wander round the little garden , where the tall yellow lilies reared their stately heads over a mass of sweet cottage flowers , pinks and forget-me-nots , poppies and double daisies , sweet-williams -- loved of the bees -- pansies , lupins , and snap-dragons ; over the cottage , where the white roses climbed up the thatch to the very chimneys , and where through the open doorway could be seen the neat kitchen , with its red-brick floor , the settle placed by the fireside , the tall grandfather 's clock ticking away in the corner , and the oak dresser , with its rows of blue willow-pattern plates ; and back again at last to where Mrs", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "Soon they were poring over their books , and all that was heard in the room was the occasional rattle of paper , mingling with the ticking of the clock", "context_len": 30}}}, "placidly": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "\u2019 She found them , to her great relief , quite serene , \u2019 as serene as she was herself in her funny position , \u2019 -- the clock ticking placidly on , undisturbed by the jar", "context_len": 37}}}, "wanders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "One wanders about in the dark , -- perhaps helped by the lightning , -- to find the region of a door that is the chief offender", "context_len": 27}}}, "lotuses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Ah , if I could but give you a sight of the glorious pink water-lilies or lotuses out of our nice tank ! I am not sure , however , whether I would not change them for -- cabbages ; certainly I would for cauliflowers", "context_len": 45}}}, "cauliflowers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Ah , if I could but give you a sight of the glorious pink water-lilies or lotuses out of our nice tank ! I am not sure , however , whether I would not change them for -- cabbages ; certainly I would for cauliflowers", "context_len": 45}}}, "cucumbers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "brought two cucumbers to-day , -- a welcome sight , -- and a Native presented us with some kelas , [ 65 ] -- more welcome still", "context_len": 27}}}, "downhearted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The end of May found her back again in Amritsar , but by no means downhearted", "context_len": 16}}}, "supernumerary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "[ 66 ] So I gave in ! It seems to me very hard to cast off good servants , just because the perpetual changing about makes one rather a supernumerary", "context_len": 31}}}, "overworks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "She overworks , and the season tells on her", "context_len": 9}}}, "opposes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "He is a man of great courage ; he has flung aside the prejudices of his caste ; he vehemently opposes idol-worship , and will readily eat with Christians", "context_len": 29}}}, "torchlight": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Meanwhile the night had fallen so intensely dark that the completion of the task of feeding us unfortunates had to be accomplished by torchlight ; and we had not been very long left to ourselves before the faint flickering of distant lightning and the low muttering and grumbling of thunder warned us to expect a storm of more than ordinary violence", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "I can recommend their Scotch highballs , and as for cocktails , they put up a dry Martini here that goes down like oil , and stirs you up like a torchlight procession", "context_len": 33}}}, "centipede": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "I have never seen either scorpion or centipede in Batala ; but then my long staircase would present a formidable difficulty to such reptiles", "context_len": 24}}}, "crunching": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "But I hate crunching creatures , so called out for some one to kill my centipede", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was a crunching noise of steps -- solitary steps -- on the gravel below", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The commotion increased , the yelping and barking finally dying out , to be succeeded by a low moan , and then there was a silence , and Frank could hear the crunching of bones", "context_len": 35}}}, "condemnation": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 37, "context": "In his abject misery and dread , Mark Penelly saw , in the stern gaze before him , anger and a vindictive desire for revenge ; he saw therein fierce hate , and an implacable , unchanging condemnation ; he felt that Harry was sustaining him there where he had dragged him to make his sufferings more acute , and that , after holding him up for a while , he would loosen his hold , causing him to sink at once into the deep water by the rocks , and be swept away by the tremendous current", "context_len": 98}}}, "fledged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 104, "context": ", love , should we fear the act of departing ? How many , many , pass Jordan , as it were , dry-shod ? Remember how peacefully sweet Fanny sank to rest , -- dearest Mother , -- how my Letitia \u2019 s face was lighted up with a smile , -- how our Bible-woman at Batala sang aloud a happy hymn within a few hours of her going ! To me it seems such a simple thing for the -- I had almost said imprisoned soul , to leave its cottage of clay , -- for the bird , as soon as fledged , to spread its wings ! We are winged creatures , and it seems a humiliation to be creeping on earth so long", "context_len": 128}}}, "imperatively": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "Donald , who had been absent on a tour of the farm with Chris when Harry was at the house the night before , at once surmised who the new-comer was , but gazed in blank amazement , none the less , as Harry , calling him by name , commanded him rather imperatively to stay just where he was for a moment", "context_len": 63}}}, "journeying": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "The southeastern part of the State , through which our friends were journeying , is drained by the North Fork of the Platte and its affluents , including the Laramie and Sweetwater rivers , the Lodge Pole , Rock , Poison Spring , Medicine Bow , Horse and Rawhide creeks", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "His steps now turned toward a trail about which he had learned from the caboclos of the district , a trail that after long journeying would lead him to the river and to Jaguar Island , where Japazy dwelt", "context_len": 39}}}, "hypocrites": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` He says it is the religion of cowards and hypocrites ; that it forms a plea for cowardice when men dare not be men , and is thrown aside fast enough when they have their foes in their power", "context_len": 40}}}, "moire": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Ah ! if we could but keep in the jungle all the time , I need not pack up my Conference Cream , [ 69 ] nor my faithful moire antique", "context_len": 31}}}, "antique": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 105, "context": "For there was something very stirring in the act , and a stranger to the place would hold his breath in dread as he saw Mark Penelly , who was the finest swimmer at the port of Carn Du , climb up the side of the great black rock upon some fine summer evening , then go round along the narrow shelf of shaley stone , till he stood alone there forty feet above the sea , his white figure as he rested against the black rock , every muscle standing out from his well-knit frame , and his arms crossed , looking like some antique statue in its niche", "context_len": 110}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was a complete change when , with his head full of Utopian dreams , `` more of an antique Roman '' than an Englishman , Dick was sent off to serve under General Monck in the army that was to administer as well as to garrison Scotland", "context_len": 48}}}, "hyena": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "There would be some fun in meeting with a cheetah or a hyena , -- I should not like a bear unless there were a kud [ 70 ] between us , -- but I shrink from the world and his wife", "context_len": 42}}}, "pastoral": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Was it not clever ? I hope that the lady profited by the pastoral rebuke , though she can hardly have enjoyed it", "context_len": 23}}}, "trombone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "But I hope that we may not hear our Queen \u2019 s voice by it , if it is to sound like a trombone", "context_len": 24}}}, "matrimony": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` It is because I am thinking he 'll soon be falling into the snares of matrimony that I have come to stay at 17 Graham Square , '' continued Mrs", "context_len": 31}}}, "ruche": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "She was certain that my velvet cap must want a new ruche ; would I send over a whole set of caps for her to improve ? It would amuse her , she said", "context_len": 34}}}, "exaggeration": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` It was a work of no ordinary skill and difficulty to save so many persons in the few moments available for the purpose ; and , when it is mentioned that some of them were very old and crippled , it is no exaggeration to say that it would be impossible to praise too highly Ford 's conduct on this occasion , which has resulted so disastrously to himself", "context_len": 70}}}, "perceptions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Her bright fancies , her quick perceptions , her wise suggestions , are invaluable to all of us in the Mission", "context_len": 21}}}, "frets": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "While she frets over her want of power in speaking Urdu and Panjabi , we are rejoicing , not only in her power of writing for the people , but in her wonderful perception of the national character , her insight into the weaknesses and also into the virtues of our Native friends , Christian and heathen", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "There is my cousin Anice : you know how her mother frets because she is not well educated", "context_len": 18}}}, "virtues": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 19, "context": "But , when he saw that she heartened herself for that which he set forth to her of its virtues , he feared lest she should talk of this to the folk , so he said to her , `` O my mother , beware lest thou bespeak any of the lamp and its uses , for that this is our fortune ; be careful [ 345 ] and exceed not in speech thereof to any one , lest we lose it and lose this our present prosperity , for that it is from it", "context_len": 95}}}, "sustaining": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "In his abject misery and dread , Mark Penelly saw , in the stern gaze before him , anger and a vindictive desire for revenge ; he saw therein fierce hate , and an implacable , unchanging condemnation ; he felt that Harry was sustaining him there where he had dragged him to make his sufferings more acute , and that , after holding him up for a while , he would loosen his hold , causing him to sink at once into the deep water by the rocks , and be swept away by the tremendous current", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` You knew what my duty was , '' I said , with a sustaining arm about her , `` and you brought me here", "context_len": 25}}}, "excitability": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "She has been wonderfully free of fever during the past year ; and the excitability which used to make me anxious has quite passed away", "context_len": 25}}}, "sublunary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "We find it necessary to look after her in such sublunary things ; and many a laugh she has at our anxiety about her appearance", "context_len": 25}}}, "bodice": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "She made quite a picture in her short linsey-woolsey petticoat , with the check apron , her plaid shawl crossed over her cotton bodice , and the frilled white cap framing the kind old face , with its apple cheeks and soft white hair", "context_len": 44}}}, "ruts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "It seemed to her the life , in its bare drudgery , had worn deep seams into her very soul , like country roads in spring-time , whose surface is torn apart in gaping wounds and unsightly ruts by heavy wheels and frost and rain", "context_len": 45}}}, "fairings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "But we did not go just to look at the folk , or to buy fairings either", "context_len": 17}}}, "anniversary": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "She knew very well that Lady Jane would say that she could not go out on the anniversary of her husband 's death ; but she felt at the same time that it was necessary for Irene 's reform that both her mother and governess should accompany her", "context_len": 48}}}, "lowness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Beutel but yesterday of those two village schools , which would -- from the lowness of funds -- have been dropped , but for your last handsome gift", "context_len": 28}}}, "bountiful": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 59, "context": "And suppose I gain admission to the presence , what shall I take by way of offering to the Sultan 's highness ? It [ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm", "context_len": 128}}}, "strays": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "These poor village schools -- since for retrenchment sake they were cast off -- are like waifs and strays", "context_len": 19}}}, "engross": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Instead of talking to the women there , I am certain to have one or two men , descendants of the famous Guru Nanak , who engross the conversation with me almost entirely", "context_len": 33}}}, "talker": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 8, "context": "Now , you 're not much of a talker , and your face gives you the look of a big baby that has got out of its mammy 's yard and do n't know how to git back", "context_len": 38}}}, "reverences": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "He reverences Guru Nanak , [ 71 ] very properly , but thinks that Guru Nanak has given religious tenets such as I am certain that he never did", "context_len": 29}}}, "propounds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "propounds doctrines that are amazing from the lips of a Hindu", "context_len": 11}}}, "chant": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "First Sobrinini \u2019 s voice -- he could not be mistaken in it now -- then other voices joining in a sort of weird chant that chilled the lad \u2019 s blood yet drew him irresistibly toward the point from which the sound was coming", "context_len": 45}}}, "prays": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Oh , did I tell you -- I told somebody -- about my other Brahmin ; the elderly man who prays by the side of our tank ? I have repeatedly spoken to him in my indifferent Panjabi ; and I spoke to my nephew , R", "context_len": 47}}}, "forgetful": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "But Percy was popular , Seabrooke was not ; and even the masters were inclined to believe that the latter must have been careless and forgetful and mislaid the money , while believing he had put it in the place he indicated , and presently -- no one knew exactly how it started or could trace the rumor to its source -- presently it began to be bruited about among the boys that Seabrooke was keeping it for his own use and had never intended to return it to Percy , and was now making him his scape-goat", "context_len": 98}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "With these thoughts in mind and forgetful of the fact that he had had nothing to eat since the afternoon of the day before , Bomba strode into the passage and found his way back to the strange room with the chairs and platform where Sobrinini had sung to him", "context_len": 50}}}, "expounds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "He not only reads the Bible , but expounds", "context_len": 9}}}, "attentive": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 88, "context": "The eyes in the curly heads were busy with the pictures , the tongues in the curly heads were silent , save when now and then one whispered , `` Shall I turn over ? '' or `` Is not that pretty ? '' but the ears in the curly heads were wide open to all that was passing in the bow-window ; while the three young ladies , thinking that the curly heads were heeding nothing but their own affairs , went on chattering as if those attentive ears were miles away", "context_len": 93}}}, "childlike": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He has a sort of childlike readiness to listen , looking full at you when you speak ; and his face quite brightening as if with pleasure when you talk of a Saviour", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "He had shown a childlike pleasure and invention , and the preparations for the carrying out of the plan had filled many a day with interest which would otherwise have dragged wearily", "context_len": 32}}}, "clergymen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "He asked me to-day to have him as a nephew ! How rich I am in these dharm-nephews , -- to say nothing of the real ones ! Now I have five ; one of them being my Afghan , and the others four of the most valuable clergymen in the Panjab Mission", "context_len": 53}}}, "progresses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "I know that you will be curious to hear how the Batala school plan progresses", "context_len": 15}}}, "passively": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "And so this strangely constituted man went forth and spoke to the assembled multitude , who stood passively gazing at the distant conflagration", "context_len": 23}}}, "forsaking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 64, "context": "The moral aim of the tale has been to depict the mental difficulties which our heathen forefathers had severally to encounter ere they could embrace Christianity -- difficulties chiefly arising from the inconsistencies of Christians -- and to set forth the example of one who , having found the `` pearl of great price , '' sold all he had and bought it , forsaking all that could appeal to the imagination of a warlike youth -- '' choosing rather to suffer affliction with the people of God , than to enjoy the pleasures of sin for a season", "context_len": 99}}}, "adults": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "It was the keenest of disappointments to the two youths , who , if possible , were more eager to start on the two-thousand-mile journey than were the adults ; but this disappointment was greatly softened by the knowledge that the delay was only for a few months", "context_len": 48}}}, "thanksgivings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "[ 77 ] He is known to be such a saint , that thanksgivings have been offered again and again for his appointment", "context_len": 23}}}, "chumming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It really seems likely that our comical arrangement will be carried out ; and that I and my nephew will find ourselves chumming together in the midst of a Boys \u2019 School ! !", "context_len": 34}}}, "nucleus": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "A thousand gallant Mercians , the nucleus of an army , each man fit to be a captain , awaited them there , and Edmund felt his spirits revive within him , and his hope for England ; and Alfgar met Hermann with great gladness", "context_len": 45}}}, "unsightly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "Dear little pilgrims ! there were jewels in their way such as they did not dream of ; but it was only earnest seeking such as theirs which could find them ; for they lay hidden beneath many a thorn and bramble and unsightly weed ; and they were to be found only by the help of this very jewel of prayer which shone so brightly that its light guided the little feet to the dark places where the hidden gems lay", "context_len": 82}}}, "bareness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "It must be admitted that the attic was rather delighted in , and that its coldness and bareness quite sank into insignificance when Melchisedec was remembered , and one heard about the sparrows and things one could see if one climbed on the table and stuck one 's head and shoulders out of the skylight", "context_len": 55}}}, "chintz": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "A bedroom chintz would have looked quite out of character , but I have bought a native cloth , with an Oriental pattern , very tapestry-like , old-fashioned conventional flowers and birds on a blue ground", "context_len": 36}}}, "liberality": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "The monastery is well endowed with lands by the liberality of its first founder , as appears in the deeds preserved in our great muniment chest", "context_len": 26}}}, "baptismal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "We hope next Sunday to have a Baptism in our lovely little lake ; and we have been practising baptismal hymns to sing on the joyful occasion", "context_len": 27}}}, "unharmed": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "Up went the smoke , the heather catching fire at once , in went the stick , and out came the bees in an angry swarm ; but something had gone wrong in the calculations , for instead of falling stupefied on to the grass , they flew unharmed through the smoke , and fell upon their tormentors with a buzz of indignation", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "What could it all mean ? After watching and probably signaling they had waited till he rode right into the trap and then had allowed him to ride out again , unharmed and all unsuspicious of his peril", "context_len": 38}}}, "slumbers": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 52, "context": "In some parts of the world there are wild cows , and buffaloes too , which will kill those who attack them , if they can ; but in this happy island , there are no fierce wild beasts , to frighten us from the fields by day , or disturb our slumbers by night", "context_len": 55}}}, "mango": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "We shall require a cemetery too , and have rather set our hearts on a pretty mango tope [ 80 ] at a suitable distance from , but not quite in sight of , the house", "context_len": 36}}}, "tope": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "We shall require a cemetery too , and have rather set our hearts on a pretty mango tope [ 80 ] at a suitable distance from , but not quite in sight of , the house", "context_len": 36}}}, "adult": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "I suspect that I should find some Missionary stations so dull after one like this ! Such as those where year after year passes without an adult baptism being witnessed , -- hardly expected , -- perhaps in some instances hardly hoped for !", "context_len": 44}}}, "concession": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "Jones had genially assured him that on evenings when the weather became colder he would be welcome to sit and study by the open fire in the sitting-room , a concession for which he had been duly grateful", "context_len": 38}}}, "drudges": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "He had described its cheerless look , and the bearing of the child , who seemed as if she was not of the class of those who were treated as drudges and servants", "context_len": 33}}}, "charade": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "I have written an English charade for our young Panjabis to recite ; and the idea has , I think , taken with them", "context_len": 24}}}, "apportioning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "It needed a little management to give a separate part to every one of seventeen boys , apportioning it to the individual \u2019 s capacity", "context_len": 25}}}, "divisions": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 8, "context": "The English hero divided his army into three divisions : The right wing , where he posted around his own person the chosen band whom he had trained during the last few years of retirement ; the left wing , chiefly composed of the men of Wessex ; the centre , the weakest and newest recruits , whom he posted there with as deep a design as led Hannibal to use the same strategy at Cannae", "context_len": 76}}}, "ballot": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "I suspect that there is a sort of wistful longing raised in many a young heart , I wish I were one of those Christian boys ! If there could be a blind ballot of Batala boys , as to whether the whole town should become Christian , I am by no means sure whether the votes would not be in our favour", "context_len": 63}}}, "hermits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "If you could substitute the name Almighty for Hari , and Lord Jesus for Guru , [ 83 ] it might almost seem the composition of hermits in the early centuries , except that celibacy is not enjoined", "context_len": 38}}}, "exhortations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "A springless Cape cart drove them at the maximum pace obtainable by the wiry horse and the vociferous exhortations of the native `` boy ''", "context_len": 25}}}, "deities": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "He gloried in it ; and , as if he wished to add to the solemnity of the scene , he caused an altar to be erected , and offered a sacrifice , by torch-light , to the deities on whose aid his soldiers imagined themselves most dependent for success on the morrow", "context_len": 53}}}, "probation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "There is indeed the ridiculous idea of people having to pass through 840,000 states of existence , -- unless the probation be shortened by meditation , purity , and the repetition of God \u2019 s name , -- but this fearful number of births is regarded as very tiresome indeed", "context_len": 50}}}, "births": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "There is indeed the ridiculous idea of people having to pass through 840,000 states of existence , -- unless the probation be shortened by meditation , purity , and the repetition of God \u2019 s name , -- but this fearful number of births is regarded as very tiresome indeed", "context_len": 50}}}, "communion": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "The door opened , and Edward left the room after a conference with the physician , who sat in a corner of the room compounding drugs at a small table ; a few minutes passed in silence , when he returned and held the door open for the bishop of London , who entered , bearing the viaticum , as the last communion of the sick was then called , and attended by an acolyte , who bore a lighted taper before him and carried a bell", "context_len": 87}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "So , as Albert had expected , this morning proved no exception , and he was unceremoniously left to communion with his own thoughts upon the doorstep ; but it did not prove a long separation", "context_len": 36}}}, "darkens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It shocks me to find how that invention of Satan darkens the moral sense", "context_len": 14}}}, "correspondents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "To one of her correspondents she wrote from Batala on the 6th of July : You know that I am the only Englishwoman within twenty miles", "context_len": 26}}}, "engrossing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "At Amritsar she found herself as usual in the midst of engrossing interests", "context_len": 13}}}, "miseries": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "When at last they stood apart and looked fondly at each other , Bomba was grieved to the heart to see the ravages that the hardships and miseries of the journey had made in the face and frame of the old naturalist", "context_len": 42}}}, "insurmountable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Here this year in Amritsar we have had five women , and last year two , who , in the face of what we might have considered almost insurmountable obstacles , have bravely confessed Christ in baptism", "context_len": 37}}}, "fatality": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "It seems as if by some fatality I must go each year to Dalhousie , \u2019 Charlotte Tucker said in one letter , adding , But I hope to return back in a few days", "context_len": 35}}}, "nonsensical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "I am afraid that -- -- s wife will shrink from breaking pardah , -- that nonsensical pardah , which is a real snare to some baptized bibis", "context_len": 28}}}, "bathos": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Writing home , she described drolly her absence from Batala as -- this strange episode of my life ; -- seven weeks acting Superintendent of the Orphanage , -- three of those weeks sole Missionary at Amritsar , -- and -- oh , bathos ! ten days an ayah -- for I had none other", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was such bathos after the overwrought state of their feelings that they sat down and laughed till the tears ran down their cheeks , treasuring the joke to tell to Archie when he should be well enough to receive visitors , and holding it up as a model to relieve Nancy 's fears", "context_len": 54}}}, "complications": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "'Go to sleep at once , you naughty girl , or I 'll call your aunt , ' said Ellen , putting out the candle to avoid further complications , for she knew she ought not to have been talking within hearing of her charges , and hoped Peggy would forget the matter by morning", "context_len": 55}}}, "sharpening": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "The light shone out in the gathering darkness , and illumined the recesses of the bushes around , and the faces of a large body of men reclining on the bank , or engaged in the task of sharpening their arms while their supper was roasting", "context_len": 46}}}, "schoolrooms": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "We have now only schoolrooms turned into Chapels", "context_len": 8}}}, "quadrupled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Perhaps it will be one or two years before we have collected enough ; and by that time , please God , the flock may have doubled or quadrupled", "context_len": 29}}}, "reverses": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "He did not like to leave it behind him and proceed to the eastward ; for , in case of any reverses happening to him , the Tyrians would be very likely to act decidedly against him , and their power on the Mediterranean would enable them to act very efficiently against him on all the coasts of Greece and Asia Minor", "context_len": 62}}}, "pedal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I had a tin plate made for the pedal part , expressly to keep creatures out ; but they managed to pass it", "context_len": 23}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He isn \u2019 t the only one that puts on the loud pedal when he sleeps", "context_len": 16}}}, "effectual": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "Ben would have ploughed two or three furrows around his buildings , which would have been the most effectual preventive ; but , after the vessel was built , he had put his oxen away to pasture", "context_len": 37}}}, "wadding": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Sure enough , the rogues had bitten holes in that , and pulled out wadding to stuff into my harmonium , doubtless to make a comfortable nest for a family of young mice or rats", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "I suspected the rats , but did not know where they could have got the wadding from , -- when V", "context_len": 21}}}, "fakirs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "-- I do not think that I told you of two Christian fakirs , to whom I was introduced at Amritsar", "context_len": 21}}}, "benefited": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "Further particulars of the careers of Sir Colin Campbell , John Cassell , General Gordon , Sir Henry Havelock , Joseph Livesey , David Livingstone , Robert Moffat , George Moore , Florence Nightingale , Lord Shaftesbury , Agnes Weston , and other men and women whose example has benefited the country , will be found in an attractive series of books issued under the title of The World 's Workers", "context_len": 71}}}, "distracting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Roger had left the automobile likewise , and he came back to them , waiting near at hand until they should recover from the distracting excitement of the moment", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I am afraid poor Father had rather a distracting meal , but he cheerfully ate the smoky toast which the children provided , and did not even grumble when Peggy , by mistake , put six lumps of sugar into his tea", "context_len": 42}}}, "photos": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I s'pose that means sticking a lot of girls ' photos on the wall , some of those crazy banners they embroidered for you , a lot of ribbons , and such truck , '' commented Sid disgustedly", "context_len": 39}}}, "disagreeableness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I suggested the disagreeableness of 840,000 transmigrations ; but she did not seem troubled", "context_len": 14}}}, "quilt": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 92, "context": "In the grate there was a glowing , blazing fire ; on the hob was a little brass kettle hissing and boiling ; spread upon the floor was a thick , warm crimson rug ; before the fire a folding-chair , unfolded , and with cushions on it ; by the chair a small folding-table , unfolded , covered with a white cloth , and upon it spread small covered dishes , a cup , a saucer , a teapot ; on the bed were new warm coverings and a satin-covered down quilt ; at the foot a curious wadded silk robe , a pair of quilted slippers , and some books", "context_len": 112}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then , cautiously laying his quilt aside , he rose , put on his jacket , hat , and shoes , and climbed softly up the steep cabin-stairs to the deck", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "kernel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was something like a nut , for it was rather difficult to get at the kernel", "context_len": 17}}}, "constitutes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It was something for her to remain with her husband ; for , by Muhammadan law , baptism of husband or wife constitutes divorce", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` The wind is certainly chilly , '' she replied , and then , moved by embarrassment , or stirred by the motherly instinct that constitutes more than half the charm of womanhood , she leaned over and tucked the lap-robe about my knees , and then fell back in her place , laughing gleefully , as a child might have laughed", "context_len": 62}}}, "divorce": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "It was something for her to remain with her husband ; for , by Muhammadan law , baptism of husband or wife constitutes divorce", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "To speak against the Prophet is enough to constitute a divorce ! The companions of the man did not let their chance go of half ruining him", "context_len": 27}}}, "miracles": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "A Muhammadan , who had , I suppose , read Christian books , was travelling with some other Muhammadans , and was imprudent enough to say that Muhammad wrought no miracles , and expressed doubts as to his being really a prophet", "context_len": 42}}}, "ruining": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "He did what very few monarchs have done , either before or since ; in securing for himself the pleasures of friendship , and of intimate social communion with a heart kindred to his own , without ruining himself by committing to a favorite powers which he was not qualified to wield", "context_len": 52}}}, "dowry": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "is he not worthy to be the Sultan 's son-in-law and to have his daughter to bride ? '' Now the Vizier marvelled at the greatness of these riches yet more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more , when he saw that the Sultan was content with the bride-gift [ 435 ] and the dowry ; withal he could not gainstand the [ manifest ] truth and say to the Sultan , `` He is not worthy ; '' so he cast about to work upon him by practice , that he might hinder him from giving his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour to Alaeddin , and accordingly said to him , [ 436 ] `` O my lord", "context_len": 128}}}, "scribbling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "At the last moment , smitten by regret because fancied necessity seemed to compel him to leave without bidding the kind widow good-by , he seized a piece of brown paper and the stub of a pencil and sat down to write a few words of farewell Jerry urging him to hasten even while he was scribbling", "context_len": 57}}}, "intricacies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "The towel changed into a bandage under my fingers , and I found that I could not compass the intricacies of the fastenings", "context_len": 23}}}, "purer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "People are on the whole expected to go to Church , -- or , if Dissenters , just as much to go to Chapel , -- and though the going to Church , as a matter of course , does not at all indicate the lack of deeper reasons , of purer motives underlying , it does make the going a very easy matter", "context_len": 64}}}, "heartfelt": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Yes , love , indeed it would be , '' said her mother , thinking as she spoke of Bessie 's heartfelt prayer for the miserable children a few nights since , and sure that it would indeed prove a jewel bright and lovely in the eyes of Him to whom it was offered", "context_len": 55}}}, "tares": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Wheat and tares will grow together until the end ; and bad fish as well as good will be caught in the net", "context_len": 23}}}, "unfaithful": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "But for one who is unfaithful , for one who turns his back upon the Light , after seeming to be indeed a Convert , there are many who stand firm , persevering to the end , despite difficulties , discouragements , and bitter oppositions", "context_len": 45}}}, "discouragements": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "But for one who is unfaithful , for one who turns his back upon the Light , after seeming to be indeed a Convert , there are many who stand firm , persevering to the end , despite difficulties , discouragements , and bitter oppositions", "context_len": 45}}}, "encouragements": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "There were of course disappointments as well as encouragements", "context_len": 9}}}, "goings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Tell Bomba now , he commanded , where have been the comings and goings of Ashati and Neram since they left the cabin of the good squaw Pipina", "context_len": 28}}}, "pressings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "There were goings backward as well as pressings forward", "context_len": 9}}}, "omitting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "Though it is complete in itself , and may be read independently , the chief characters introduced will be recognized as old friends by the readers of `` The Young Explorer , '' the volume just preceding , not omitting Ki Sing , the faithful Chinaman , whose virtues may go far to diminish the prejudice which , justly or unjustly , is now felt toward his countrymen", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "She made little remarks of this sort from time to time , but she did not do what was her invariable habit , and the fact of her omitting to do this caused me some surprise", "context_len": 36}}}, "widespread": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Results of what she did , still more of what she was , were visible enough to others , -- but rather in the shape of a general and widespread influence than in the shape of conversions directly due to her labours", "context_len": 42}}}, "gauged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The worth of any work can never be truly gauged by the amount of success which may appear to follow within a given time ; and to measure the extent or the effects of her loving influence , alike among younger Missionaries and among Indian Christians , especially among the boys in the Baring High School , is utterly impossible", "context_len": 60}}}, "toilsome": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "There , after a long and toilsome winter journey , the funeral cortege arrived , and was joined by his wife Elgitha , his sons Edmund and Edward", "context_len": 28}}}, "memoranda": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "One Native Christian , sending a few slight memoranda , goes so far as to say : I feel sorry to have to add that she signally failed as a Missionary , if by that term is meant the preaching of the Gospel to the heathen of India", "context_len": 48}}}, "hearsay": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "So , in spite of all entreaties for more , he departed , bearing his steaming can with him , and declaring that one good cow was worth all the ghosts and ghostesses in the kingdom , 'for you might do some good to she , but as to they , they was naught but hearsay , after all", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "[ 83 ] Du Pratz , who speaks of them from hearsay only , places them north of the Alibamu , and states that they spoke a language almost the same as the Chicasa ( Hist", "context_len": 36}}}, "slowest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The fact that she herself saw few results says nothing ; for the best results are often slowest in appearing", "context_len": 20}}}, "colloquial": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` decided ( or demanded ) that thou be bountiful to ( or grace ) me with ; '' but icledsa is here used in the colloquial sense of `` willed , vouchsafed", "context_len": 33}}}, "attainable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Grammar and construction she might and did to a considerable extent master , but colloquial fluency was not in her case attainable", "context_len": 22}}}, "fluent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Still , though she never became actually fluent , it is a matter of unquestionable fact that she did both understand and make herself understood , despite occasional verbal mistakes", "context_len": 30}}}, "verbal": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 50, "context": "The possessive pronoun of the third person singular and plural ( im- , in- , i- ) is prefixed in the same manner to substantives to indicate possession , as it is to verbs to show that an act is performed in the interest or to the detriment of the verbal subject or object", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "In spite of the frequent use to which they are put they do not relieve the sentence of its heaviness to any perceptible extent ; for what we call incident clauses and also many co-ordinate principal sentences are uniformly expressed by groups of words , the verb of which stands in the -t or -n verbal , which nearest corresponds to our participle in -ing , or to having ( h", "context_len": 71}}}, "testimonies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "There are testimonies from all sides which abundantly prove this", "context_len": 10}}}, "deprecated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "She always deprecated any attempt to judge of her work by the number of Zenanas on her visiting list ; and indeed it would not be fair to do so , as she did not undertake regular teaching in them", "context_len": 40}}}, "mainstay": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}}}, "enlivened": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "From this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and", "context_len": 128}}}, "mothering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 69, "context": "this time for years to come Miss Tucker was a mainstay of the Boys \u2019 Boarding School , teaching the elder boys the English language and history , taking a motherly interest in all their pursuits , writing for them Batala School songs , inviting them in the evenings to little social entertainments , enlivened by parlour games ; visiting the sick , comforting the home-sick new boy ; mothering the young convert , who had been sent to Batala not less for spiritual shelter than for instruction ; and upholding the hands of workers in the School and Mission generally ; besides carrying on without fail her regular visits to the town and villages , and her literary work for publication , both in England and India", "context_len": 128}}}, "lozenges": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Her Bible Classes were eagerly looked for and well attended , -- it may be , for the sake of lozenges and bits of cake which she distributed at the end , but also for the interest she made everybody feel in the meeting", "context_len": 44}}}, "anecdotes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Whenever her father had a story to tell of anything which had happened to him during his daily duties , Jennie always called it a `` duty story , '' and she was very eager for such anecdotes", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "ANECDOTES ABOUT THE REV", "context_len": 4}}}, "anagrams": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "She amused us with stories , comic songs , historical anecdotes , making anagrams , giving riddles to be solved , and several amusements of the kind", "context_len": 27}}}, "riddles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "She amused us with stories , comic songs , historical anecdotes , making anagrams , giving riddles to be solved , and several amusements of the kind", "context_len": 27}}}, "indoor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Many an evening was spent in Miss Tucker \u2019 s drawing-room , playing various indoor games , of which chess and word-making and word-taking were her favourites", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "A battalion picture-theatre , billiard rooms , voluntary swimming parades , boxing , and a variety of other indoor and outdoor games contributed to the men 's enjoyment ; and , although discipline was well enforced , there was a total absence of irritating petty restrictions that form a constant source of annoyance to the men of the New Armies of the Motherland", "context_len": 63}}}, "artichoke": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "In the latter game she would consider it a great triumph to have made such long words as Jerusalem artichoke", "context_len": 20}}}, "booklets": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "Her tiny Indian booklets , published by the Christian Literature Society at very low prices , are among the most widely selling of the Society \u2019 s productions", "context_len": 28}}}, "productions": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "It is wholly legendary , in its first portion even mythical ; it is of a comparatively remote age , exceedingly instructive for ethnography and for the development of religious ideas ; it is full of that sort of na\u00efvet\u00e9 which we like so much to meet in the mental productions of our aborigines , and affords striking instances of the debasing and brutalizing influence of the unrestricted belief in the supernatural and miraculous", "context_len": 74}}}, "infringing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "Six weeks \u2019 holiday in the year was permitted by the Society under which she worked , and she would seldom take more than a month of this in the hottest weather , that she might be able to get away for a few days at some other time , without infringing on her full ten months and a half of work", "context_len": 62}}}, "wheresoever": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Wheresoever there was need or distress that she heard of , she gave substantial help immediately", "context_len": 16}}}, "scarcity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Again , in 1892 , after we had returned to Kotgur , where there was a great scarcity in the district , and many poor people had hardly one meal a day to eat , and Miss Tucker heard that I gave relief work to some forty or fifty people , she sent me another Rs", "context_len": 56}}}, "multifarious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "Again , in 1889 , when a dear friend of mine , Pastor and Teacher in the United States of North America , with whom I had come out to India in 1869 , had decided to return to India as a Missionary , in order to join and to help me in the multifarious work at Clarkabad , and he found that the money in hand was insufficient to pay for his and his family \u2019 s voyage from Germany , and Miss Tucker heard of it , she immediately sent me 100 , with the direction to forward that sum to him , on condition that he had not left Germany again for America", "context_len": 116}}}, "recipient": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Stirling later learned that their recipient had expressed herself perfectly delighted with the delicate , beautiful gift , but , being a true lady , Bart 's mother said nothing about the matter to those who would have been glad to spread a little gossip unfavorable to the dowdy society queen of Pleasantville", "context_len": 53}}}, "necessaries": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "She therefore kept to a certain sum of money yearly for her own expenses , while giving much away in addition ; she made her clothes last as long as it was possible for them to hold together ; she had hardly any furniture in her rooms ; and she refused all luxuries , including some things which in India are commonly reckoned not luxuries , but absolute necessaries", "context_len": 69}}}, "loans": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The lawyer wrote that matters would probably be settled in favor of the present landholders , but that , so long as litigation pended , all titles were so clouded as to make any questions of loans untenable", "context_len": 38}}}, "applies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Robert Clark speaks of Miss Tucker as an English Christian Faqir , \u2019 -- a curious use of the term , which he applies also to one or two other Missionaries", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Swan ( Schoolcraft V , 259 ) that the Creeks migrated from the northwest to the Seminole country , then back to Okm\u00falgi , Tallapoosa and Coosa rivers , deserves no credit , or applies to small bodies of Indians only", "context_len": 41}}}, "alluding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "After alluding to the manner of her earlier English life , and contrasting it with the manner of her existence at Batala , where two chairs were placed on two sides of a table in a large and almost unfurnished room , \u2019 Mr", "context_len": 44}}}, "inaugurating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "On first going to Batala Charlotte Tucker had had the idea in her mind of inaugurating there a sort of Zenana \u2019 of maiden Missionary ladies , -- a close retreat , from which the foot of Man should be utterly and always excluded", "context_len": 44}}}, "combating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Some judicious combating was needed to break her loose from it ; though when once a gentleman-Missionary had actually arrived , theories went down before the spirit of hospitality", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The composer of the verses had turned the accident into a work of intent on the Padre 's part , representing the latter combating the evil influence of sin", "context_len": 29}}}, "misleading": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "She had not the slightest intention of misleading anybody ; but she was very anxious to put a brave face on the matter ; moreover , she was a Missionary Miss Sahiba , and she might not grumble", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "To call this loose assemblage of towns and tribes a military democracy , in the sense that the majority of the votes decided a question brought before the people in a manner that was binding for the citizens , is entirely wrong and misleading , for Indians regard their actions subject to their own decisions only , or , at the utmost , to those of their individual gens", "context_len": 69}}}, "worthier": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "But nobody did , and so on he fared , thoughtless and selfish , and so wholly absorbed in the present that even alone and at midnight , with his eyes resting full upon his mother 's portrait , he had no thought to give it nor the worthier past that it stood for", "context_len": 54}}}, "etna": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "When she first got up , she used to heat a cup of cocoa with her little etna , for her chhoti hazari", "context_len": 23}}}, "equivalent": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 53, "context": "She treated the servants very courteously , always addressing the Ayah as Bibi ji \u2019 ; and any little thing offered to her at table was accepted with a Thank you , \u2019 or declined with a No , thank you , \u2019 spoken in English , as there is in Hindustani no equivalent for the expression of gratitude", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ Footnote 321 : An idiomatic expression , equivalent to our vulgar English phrase , `` He was struck all of a heap", "context_len": 23}}}, "ascribe": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "There are no doubt many geographic terms , taken from Seminole-Creek , in the south of the peninsula as well as in the north ; it only remains to determine what age we have to ascribe to them", "context_len": 38}}}, "indispensable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "I have the indispensable umbrella in one hand , -- though it be winter , the sun may be blazing , -- my large books in the other", "context_len": 28}}}, "deprivation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "Their bodies wasted and their strength sapped by years of deprivation and torments at the hands of Jojasta could not throw off the fatigue as readily as the healthy jungle lad , whose veins were pulsing with vitality", "context_len": 38}}}, "advocate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` Not even the king , '' she repeated dully ; `` nay , I know not if the king be merciful ; but , '' she cried , suddenly starting up , `` it hath come back to me ; there is one near to the king who may be our advocate -- Prince Rupert ! ''", "context_len": 58}}}, "couplet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "\u2019 He adds : In her verses on the duties and qualifications of ladies for Missionary work in India , the last couplet was , I think , as follows : --", "context_len": 32}}}, "inconveniences": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "I do sometimes feel that there is need of patience ; one has a number of petty inconveniences and annoyances , from which we are guarded in England", "context_len": 28}}}, "annoyances": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "I do sometimes feel that there is need of patience ; one has a number of petty inconveniences and annoyances , from which we are guarded in England", "context_len": 28}}}, "submissive": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Whoever comes out as a Missionary should pray for a brave , patient , cheerful spirit , and a submissive will", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Why had she fled from the island where she had so long held sway ? Why had her formerly submissive slaves turned against her ? Bomba longed to question her , but had to defer that to another time", "context_len": 39}}}, "isolated": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 63, "context": "The following is a literal rendering of the above passage : `` An upper chamber ( keszr ) and ( or ? ) a kiosk ( kushk , a word explained by a modern Syrian dictionary as meaning ' [ a building ] like a balcony projecting from the level of the rest of the house , ' but by others as an isolated building or pavilion erected on the top of a house , i", "context_len": 76}}}, "friendships": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 1, "context": "Her friendships were so keen and her affections so warm , that I think she found the world a very pleasant place to be in , and made it better and pleasanter for other people too ( which is , after all , the grand secret of happiness ) , and the energy and enterprising spirits which had caused the tomboy pranks of her childhood were qualities which , turned to good use , proved of the utmost service to her in after-years", "context_len": 83}}}, "shrivels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Cotton gloves are a comfort in the season when kid shrivels and dries", "context_len": 13}}}, "serviceable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 66, "context": "Of course , it was not very nice for the backs of boots and knickerbockers , and frocks and pinafores were apt to get sadly torn if they caught on a projecting angle ; but what child ever thought of clothes when a twenty-foot slide might be enjoyed ? Certainly not Peggy or Bobby , whose well-worn garments were generally made of the stoutest and most serviceable materials", "context_len": 68}}}, "enumerated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Taensa guide who accompanied Lemoyne d'Iberville , up the Yazoo river in March 1699 , enumerated the villages seen on its low banks in their succession from southwest to northeast , as follows ( Margry IV , 180 ) :", "context_len": 41}}}, "symbolically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": ", containing a list of things needed to make a good and serviceable Missionary , the following are enumerated -- as usual , symbolically expressed : --", "context_len": 27}}}, "sentimentality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Give us tough leather , such as harness and straps are made of ; no romantic sentimentality , but steady , resolute Perseverance", "context_len": 23}}}, "provoke": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 69, "context": "What the fate of the rest of us was to be there was nothing to indicate , but I had no doubt that it would be something quite as dreadful as fire ; and I had fully made up my mind that when my turn came I would endeavour , by insult and invective , to goad my tormentors to such a state of fury and exasperation as should provoke them to finish me off quickly", "context_len": 76}}}, "antecedents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "When maidens of different antecedents , rank , age , temperament , and -- in minor matters -- opinions , are brought together in closest proximity , in a climate which tries the temper , there is at least a possibility of some slight rubs , which without the soothing ointment brought by the Peacemaker may even develop into sores", "context_len": 60}}}, "sores": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "When maidens of different antecedents , rank , age , temperament , and -- in minor matters -- opinions , are brought together in closest proximity , in a climate which tries the temper , there is at least a possibility of some slight rubs , which without the soothing ointment brought by the Peacemaker may even develop into sores", "context_len": 60}}}, "proverbial": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "[ Footnote 258 : A proverbial expression , meaning that , as he did not absolutely kill Alaeddin , though doing what was ( barring a miracle ) certain to cause his death , he could not be said to be his slayer ; a piece of casuistry not peculiar to the East , cf", "context_len": 55}}}, "constitutions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "But the boys were taught to harden their constitutions against the inclemencies of the seasons and the privations in war , and this result they most successfully attained by the so-called initiation , and also by continued bodily exercise before and after that solemn period of their lives", "context_len": 48}}}, "engineering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 4, "context": "One of the greatest engineering feats of the Great War -- in fact , the greatest mining operation in the history of the world 's battles -- had been successfully carried out , a task compared with which the great mine of Beaumont Hamel paled into insignificance", "context_len": 47}}}, "architects": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "An Infant Church has to be built up ; openings are to be made for converts , that they may earn their bread ; churches have to be raised with small funds and no architects , etc", "context_len": 37}}}, "bookworms": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "We are in such want of men ; not mere bookworms , but earnest , devoted , bright , active Christians , who can turn their hands to everything , and help to mould the minds of our rising generation", "context_len": 40}}}, "adapting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Stanley Hall 's suggestions in his monograph , `` How to Teach Reading , '' where he asks for `` true child-editions , made by testing many children with the work piece-meal and cutting and adapting the material till it really and closely fitted the minds and hearts of the children", "context_len": 51}}}, "liver": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Any one who in England suffers from headache , liver , back , and uneven spirits , I would rather entreat to avoid the Panjab", "context_len": 25}}}, "uneven": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 10, "context": "For a hundred yards or more the trail led over uneven rocky ground toward an immense rock , upon rounding which I found myself face to face with , and within half-a-dozen yards of , a splendid full-grown male leopard who was crouching over poor Ama 's motionless body , snarling savagely as he strove with his claws to remove a broken spear , the head of which was buried deep in his neck", "context_len": 74}}}, "palfreys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "She would be one of the choice delicate palfreys , yoked to artillery , who break down and give extra work to the already fully-taxed horses", "context_len": 26}}}, "constitutionally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I am so thankful that I am not constitutionally liable to headache , and that fever does not naturally cling to me", "context_len": 22}}}, "collegians": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "Now , dearest Fred , could there be a clearer opening than at Batala for an earnest Christian man , whether in Orders or not ? I am not thinking of you , for I would not have any one subject to headaches come to this feverish land ; but I am thinking of your brother collegians", "context_len": 57}}}, "salaried": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "I am not writing of one who wishes to become one of the regular salaried Missionaries of our Society ; but of one who has the means to be an Honorary worker", "context_len": 32}}}, "cricketer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "If he had a taste for shooting and fishing , he would find means of gratifying it ; and if he were a good cricketer , it would add to his influence over our boys", "context_len": 35}}}, "architectural": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "`` Later on , when the Sultan visits the enchanted palace for the first time , Alaeddin `` brought him to the high kiosk and he looked at the belvedere ( teyyareh , a square or round erection on the top of a house , either open at the sides or pierced with windows , our architectural term 'lantern ' ) and its casements ( shebabik , pl", "context_len": 68}}}, "fanciful": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 87, "context": "How came it that this girl had guessed his secret at once when most people passed him , taking him but for a sturdy beggar ? What made her suspect him of being a fugitive ? Was her offer of shelter but careless good nature , or a heroic endeavour to save a hunted man ? At any rate he had not fallen so low as to draw suspicion on a woman , and a young woman to boot , although she was plainly no nervous , fanciful , fine lady , but a bright , resolute , country girl , with good health and high spirits gleaming from every flash of her bright eyes , and every turn of her auburn head", "context_len": 123}}}, "sprain": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahiba must know every lane , Climb ladder-like stairs , without fearing a sprain ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must speak very plain , Must rebuke and encourage , must teach and explain ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must grasp well the rein ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must not look for gain , Though doctoring sick folk , like Jenner or Quain", "context_len": 66}}}, "doctoring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahiba must know every lane , Climb ladder-like stairs , without fearing a sprain ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must speak very plain , Must rebuke and encourage , must teach and explain ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must grasp well the rein ; The Mission Miss Sahibas must not look for gain , Though doctoring sick folk , like Jenner or Quain", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Why did n't you let me do the doctoring ? You two dopes are n't able to look after yourselves ! Look at the rug now ! ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "topis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The Mission Miss Sahibas must topis [ 92 ] retain , Must guard against sunstroke , to health such a bane ; And midst frogs and mosquitoes must patient remain , Yes , e \u2019 en when tormented , must smile through their pain ; And , with courage like that of the knights of Charlemagne , By Mission Miss Sahibas snakes should be slain", "context_len": 65}}}, "fondle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "A lot o ' women brought their babies to Jesus and axed Him to fondle of 'em , and take 'em in His arms ; and there wor some men about -- ugh ! I guess as they wor some'ut like father -- and they said to the women , ` Take the babies away as fast as possible ; Jesus is a great , great man , and He ca n't no way be troubled", "context_len": 76}}}, "waned": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "While the larger number of extracts given are , throughout her Indian career , in reference to the work going on round about her , it must not be supposed that her love for relatives and old friends , or her interest in all that concerned them , ever for a moment waned", "context_len": 53}}}, "teem": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The letters teem with loving words and messages ; and every item of news from England is received with delight", "context_len": 20}}}, "mosques": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Ours is not to be a village church , dear , but one in a city of more than 25,000 inhabitants , where there are graceful mosques , a large idol-temple , etc", "context_len": 33}}}, "economical": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "Leonnatus reproved him for this extravagance , and told him that when he became master of the countries where these costly gums were procured , he might be as prodigal of them as he pleased , but that in the mean time it would be proper for him to be more prudent and economical", "context_len": 54}}}, "pice": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "It is a good thing for us , but a better thing for the poor folk , who are thankful to earn their pice", "context_len": 24}}}, "weaver": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Baring intends also to give poor women in the city employment in spinning , and to get a Christian native weaver to make the cotton into towels or napkins", "context_len": 29}}}, "profitable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "Aunt Helen had broadly hinted that an hour 's practice of much-neglected pieces upon the piano in the Rose Parlour would be a profitable means of employing the time , and the fear lest this threat should become an actual command caused Peggy to shun the neighbourhood of the house with elaborate care , and betake herself , with a selection of pets , to the barn", "context_len": 67}}}, "buyers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "There 's some china , too , that will attract many of the buyers , and should go for a fair price , and that Romney portrait ought to be quite a catch", "context_len": 33}}}, "disliking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Baring and myself being the only white folk in Batala , and Natives generally disliking to spend money , except on marriages , funerals , jewels , and sweetmeats", "context_len": 29}}}, "sweetmeats": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 89, "context": "So they fared on with him in great state , with four of the Viziers for his sword-bearers , whilst all the troops and people of the city , both townsfolk and strangers , walked in procession before him , carrying flambeaux and drums and flutes and instruments of mirth and music , till they brought him to his palace , when he alighted and entering , sat down , as did also the Viziers and Amirs who were in his company , whilst the mamelukes brought sherbets and sweetmeats [ 493 ] and gave all who were with him in the procession to drink , albeit they were a multitude of folk whose number might not be told", "context_len": 119}}}, "enumerate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "What follows is an attempt to enumerate and sketch them , the sketch to be of a prevalently topographic nature", "context_len": 20}}}, "inquirers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Of course some Stations are more trying to faith ; some of God \u2019 s servants have to toil for years , and apparently catch nothing ; but about here in various directions one hears of converts and inquirers", "context_len": 39}}}, "weddings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Weddings and Christenings , -- the overflowing pew , -- the corner of it where we used to see the dear bald head of our venerated Father !", "context_len": 28}}}, "venerated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Weddings and Christenings , -- the overflowing pew , -- the corner of it where we used to see the dear bald head of our venerated Father !", "context_len": 28}}}, "guileless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A simple guileless lad , who likes to come , as dear U", "context_len": 13}}}, "rupee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "light , easily carried about , and costing only half a rupee", "context_len": 12}}}, "apropos": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Yesterday , in a Zenana a bright-looking young woman exclaimed , not particularly apropos to anything that I was saying : Jesus Christ is the Son of God", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It had all been managed in some way by Albert 's father , Canon Allyn , apropos of Albert 's having repeated a remark of Marie-Celeste 's , that she should be happy as a queen herself if just once she could be allowed to walk in that garden", "context_len": 49}}}, "shrivel": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The heart does not shrivel up in India , even when one lives in such an out-of-the-way place as Batala", "context_len": 20}}}, "aver": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "To aver that it is Christ \u2019 s Religion -- not our superior strength , wisdom , intelligence -- that holds India for us , is likely to give great offence in high quarters", "context_len": 34}}}, "enthusiasts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 101, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}}}, "pining": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "I am pining for you through all the days and all the nights , through every hour , in the midst of every meal ; not speaking about you , for that is not my way , but just hungering and hungering , and yet you say you will not lock the door", "context_len": 53}}}, "matrimonial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I am engaged in a matrimonial affair", "context_len": 7}}}, "solicits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": ", Mera Bhatija \u2019 s Christian servant , having just been rejected by one woman , solicits , through my Ayah , my good offices to find him a wife", "context_len": 30}}}, "lameness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "He was the joy of their life , and they were so thankful not to have had him killed that they did not seem to mind his lameness", "context_len": 28}}}, "banns": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Both parties have a negative voice ; both must be pleased ; and if so -- the banns are published ! This is the compromise between European and Oriental ways of arranging marriages", "context_len": 33}}}, "improper": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "But that this innocent little child , to whom she had been so unkind , should see for herself that she had acted in an improper way , and one that was only fit for the kitchen or stable , and should tell her so , and show such surprise at hearing her call herself a lady , was very mortifying , and she could not forget it", "context_len": 68}}}, "hamlets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "It was long since he had ventured into a town where he might be questioned and recognized -- the hunted man had only dared ask food or lodging at solitary farms or lonely hamlets ; and as he pushed forward , the gables and twisted chimneys of a mansion house , with garden walls and dove-cote , gave him hopes of help", "context_len": 62}}}, "rustics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "I do not see why rustics should want high education", "context_len": 10}}}, "soup\u00e7on": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "The snow appears to have soon vanished , as in one letter , written in September , she observes : The mountains are quite high and bold enough for beauty , though to my comfort there is not a soup\u00e7on of snow upon any of them", "context_len": 46}}}, "cogitations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Those possessing such qualities may be sincere servants of Christ ; but these qualities spoil all likeness ! So , love , here is the result of my cogitations , as I reclined on the sofa to rest myself after rather a tiring little expedition", "context_len": 45}}}, "simile": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I have been considering to-day that simile of the four different circles round Him Who is the Centre of light , holiness , and beauty", "context_len": 25}}}, "holiness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "At Rome , however , he was prevailed on to go and see the Pope , on the express understanding that he should not be obliged to kiss his holiness 's toe ; and he came away with a very pleasant remembrance of the Holy Father", "context_len": 46}}}, "christ": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Christ is the All-attractive ; and in the degree that His redeemed ones reflect His Image , it seems to me that they unconsciously attract", "context_len": 25}}}, "littleness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "If I be not mistaken in this idea , one sees why anything of littleness or meanness repulses", "context_len": 18}}}, "repulses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "If I be not mistaken in this idea , one sees why anything of littleness or meanness repulses", "context_len": 18}}}, "reclined": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Those possessing such qualities may be sincere servants of Christ ; but these qualities spoil all likeness ! So , love , here is the result of my cogitations , as I reclined on the sofa to rest myself after rather a tiring little expedition", "context_len": 45}}}, "pettiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "But oh , what a solemnising thought it is ! -- The likeness to Him , which we know will be apparent in another world , to begin in this ! The glass of our souls , so spotted and dusty , -- spotted with sin , and dusty with pettiness , -- to be cleansed and polished , so as to receive such an Image ! But you and I , love , have caught a glimpse of that Image in those whom we have been privileged to know ; have we not ? \u2019", "context_len": 96}}}, "cleansed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "But oh , what a solemnising thought it is ! -- The likeness to Him , which we know will be apparent in another world , to begin in this ! The glass of our souls , so spotted and dusty , -- spotted with sin , and dusty with pettiness , -- to be cleansed and polished , so as to receive such an Image ! But you and I , love , have caught a glimpse of that Image in those whom we have been privileged to know ; have we not ? \u2019", "context_len": 96}}}, "pilgrim": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Why should the Children of Light tread the pilgrim way in heaviness ? Light is sown for the righteous , and the crop begins to show itself even here", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` And , '' said Maggie , with just the least little pout , `` I do n't believe she is a dame pilgrim , and I do n't believe she is in the narrow path , not a bit ! ''", "context_len": 42}}}, "heaviness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "In spite of the frequent use to which they are put they do not relieve the sentence of its heaviness to any perceptible extent ; for what we call incident clauses and also many co-ordinate principal sentences are uniformly expressed by groups of words , the verb of which stands in the -t or -n verbal , which nearest corresponds to our participle in -ing , or to having ( h", "context_len": 71}}}, "bigotry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "He is a Mullah \u2019 s ( Muhammadan religious teacher \u2019 s ) son , and has been brought up in a fine school for bigotry", "context_len": 26}}}, "guinea": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "But he made up his mind to put an end , once and for all , to such suggestions from the tempter ; and resolved accordingly that , if he got up late again , he would throw a guinea into the Cam", "context_len": 43}}}, "cretonne": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "At her suggestion , too , the couch and chairs were upholstered with a pretty flower-patterned cretonne , and some lovely white-framed etchings were hung upon the tinted walls", "context_len": 29}}}, "gobble": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "I hope that my Laura will forgive me if I do not gobble up all the groceries myself ! ! Of the chocolate and biscuit I shall probably largely partake ; they are such a comfort on winter mornings", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` It 's a wonder the gobble sirs did n't come after you", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 60, "context": "The children had made a nest of grass for him inside a basket , and fed him on worms and scraps of raw meat until he was old enough to fly , when he would follow them everywhere about the farmyard and outbuildings , calling 'Jack , Jack ! ' which , with the mewing of a cat , the gobble of the old turkey-cock , and a close imitation of David 's winter cough , made up the extent of his accomplishments", "context_len": 83}}}, "thereabouts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Morris -- a little American of twelve or thereabouts -- but there is n't a little girl in all England can hold a candle to her", "context_len": 26}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "'Sir Richard had left a son behind him , a young lad of nine or ten or thereabouts , and he were the heir", "context_len": 24}}}, "insipid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "No fashionable lady , -- no insipid individual , such as you must talk to about weather , etc", "context_len": 19}}}, "abomination": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": ", but a fine , thoughtful young Man , -- who had been given a New Testament , which he is reading every day , and who sat down on the floor , and quietly , gravely , asked me to explain difficulties which he had met with in his reading , such as Daniel \u2019 s abomination of desolation , the two women grinding , etc", "context_len": 67}}}, "lamplight": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "After I came in , Mera Bhatija and I had tea , -- now I am writing to my Laura by lamplight ; and when I lay down my pen , I intend to do a little lessons", "context_len": 38}}}, "vests": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "The four fat young robins stood demurely round a plate of food on the lawn stuffing themselves and looking thankfully up toward the blue sky while their feathery vests swelled out more and more", "context_len": 34}}}, "wickets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": ", tired , but unconquered , held their wickets", "context_len": 9}}}, "periodicals": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "Cheap and good food , tea and coffee both hot and fresh , plenty of light , lots of periodicals and games ; and , for those who wish it , short meetings for prayer and praise", "context_len": 37}}}, "baits": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "Look a \u2019 the rotten old fishin \u2019 tackle I \u2019 ve got in that bo \u2019 t ; if I had money I \u2019 d buy an elegant jointed rod , a triple action reel , a silk line , and any amount of hooks and flies and baits", "context_len": 50}}}, "standpoints": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "It must have been a monotonous life , viewed from ordinary standpoints", "context_len": 12}}}, "inquirer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Also month by month fresh indications appeared of the reality of the work going on , -- an inquirer here ; a convert there ; an abusive Muhammadan softened into gentleness ; an ignorant Heathen enlightened ; a bigot persuaded ; and now and again one coming forward , bravely resolute to undergo Baptism , willing to face the almost inevitable persecution following", "context_len": 63}}}, "abusive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Also month by month fresh indications appeared of the reality of the work going on , -- an inquirer here ; a convert there ; an abusive Muhammadan softened into gentleness ; an ignorant Heathen enlightened ; a bigot persuaded ; and now and again one coming forward , bravely resolute to undergo Baptism , willing to face the almost inevitable persecution following", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 77, "context": "I am afraid this time her stone was bigger , for it bounced with such effect on his shoulder , that he turned round with a flow of language far from elegant , shaking his fist at the tree with so much fury that Peggy was in terror lest he should climb up to find the author of the assault ; but he evidently thought the day too hot for such exercises , and with a final abusive epithet walked away muttering curses on all the children in creation", "context_len": 89}}}, "bigot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Also month by month fresh indications appeared of the reality of the work going on , -- an inquirer here ; a convert there ; an abusive Muhammadan softened into gentleness ; an ignorant Heathen enlightened ; a bigot persuaded ; and now and again one coming forward , bravely resolute to undergo Baptism , willing to face the almost inevitable persecution following", "context_len": 63}}}, "pungent": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "Traces of poison gas lurked in the traverses , pungent fumes from bursting shells wafted over parapet and parados , while the report , passed on from various successive occupants of this section of the line , that a dozen dead Huns had been buried under the floor of the support trench -- the old first-line trench of a Prussian regiment -- seemed to find definite confirmation", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "[ Footnote 289 : Zekiyyeh ( pure ) for dhekiyyeh ( strong , sharp , pungent ) , a common vulgar corruption", "context_len": 22}}}, "similarity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "This weed is said to have received its Maskoki names from a similarity of the top of the green plant with the phallus , which is called in Alibamu and Hitchiti : \u00f3ktchi or \u00e1ktchi", "context_len": 35}}}, "professing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 62, "context": "Then he went forth , wandering in the deserts and knowing not whitherward he should aim , and ceased not going till he came to a river ; whereupon , his hope being cut off for stress of chagrin that possessed him , he thought to cast himself into the stream ; but , for that he was a pious Muslim , professing the unity of God , he feared God in himself and stood on the bank ; of the stream to perform the ablution", "context_len": 86}}}, "penitent": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "But mamma was determined , as it was right she should be , and after rather a hard battle with her little son , she carried him down-stairs , still sobbing , but subdued and penitent , to beg Aunt Patty 's forgiveness", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Now I find that she is so penitent for indulging in even a wrong feeling that she can not rest satisfied without asking pardon , not only of her heavenly Father , but also of me", "context_len": 36}}}, "startle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "There was something so strange and uncanny about it -- for I by this time knew the ground well enough to be fully aware that there ought not to be any moving thing there -- that I stopped playing and sprang to my feet so suddenly that my movement appeared to startle Ama , who uttered a little cry of alarm , or surprise , and made as though she too would spring to her feet", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Startle", "context_len": 1}}}, "conciliating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Of course one \u2019 s manner must be gentle and conciliating , even when meeting the question , Do you think that Muhammad told lies ? with a simple straightforward , Yes", "context_len": 32}}}, "persecuted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "We have the most sure word of prophecy that the Day of the Lord is at hand ; therefore the persecuted remnant who do expect the coming of the Fifth and only Monarch , are even now assembled with their swords upon their thighs , to publish their glorious gospel and go forth conquering and to conquer", "context_len": 57}}}, "subscribing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Baring \u2019 s honour , the boys subscribing to buy the little dainties \u2019 ; and speeches of love and gratitude \u2019 being made", "context_len": 24}}}, "tinier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The babies , \u2019 as she called some of the tinier brown boys , were there also ; one small orphan looking sad and thoughtful \u2019 over the farewell", "context_len": 29}}}, "shakings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then , having with many pointings and shakings of her head made Hafed understand that he was not to go near the fire or windows , or to let the children fall out of the crib , mammy departed", "context_len": 39}}}, "disruption": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Still , in order to show his friendliness toward me , he should not produce disruption on the eleven", "context_len": 19}}}, "resignations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "All our five ladies have crossed the Rubicon ; they have sent in their resignations , with the usual six months \u2019 notice", "context_len": 23}}}, "interregnum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "How the complicated machinery of the Mission will work during the strange interregnum I know not", "context_len": 16}}}, "consummation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "Nevertheless , you shall say to Sister Harrison all that is in your heart , and she shall act as the Lord shall direct her , and if she elect to go forth into desert places and await the consummation of our hopes afar off , in fasting and prayer , in that fashion also she may serve the Good Old Cause", "context_len": 62}}}, "footboy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "When we were young we had the worry of a footboy at our heels , -- it was thought suitable for our position", "context_len": 23}}}, "definition": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "( Do you remember dear Fanny \u2019 s lovely definition of that word ? ) When I was in Edinburgh , dear -- -- was surprised , and I think a little shocked , at my father \u2019 s daughter going in omnibuses", "context_len": 43}}}, "omnibuses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "( Do you remember dear Fanny \u2019 s lovely definition of that word ? ) When I was in Edinburgh , dear -- -- was surprised , and I think a little shocked , at my father \u2019 s daughter going in omnibuses", "context_len": 43}}}, "trammels": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "As if it were any disgrace to my father \u2019 s middle-aged daughter to do what her precious princely Sire had done a hundred times ! O Laura , when one throws aside these trammels of social position , one feels like a horse taken out of harness , and set free in a nice green meadow", "context_len": 57}}}, "miserly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "To be mean and miserly is quite another thing", "context_len": 9}}}, "groundless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Happily these fears were groundless ; but plans were afloat for some temporary arrangement while the Principal remained away", "context_len": 19}}}, "paralytic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Even if I were to become blind or paralytic , I believe that it would be well to stick to Batala", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The nurse came , a wholesome middle aged body who had been for weeks with a helpless paralytic", "context_len": 18}}}, "sowing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "Peggy and Bobby were out the whole day long , following their father about the farm , riding on the slow plough-horses , helping to drive the sheep , or bringing home the cows from the pasture , sowing seeds in their little gardens , and generally revelling in the delicious freedom", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I felt so dissatisfied with myself , -- my work seemed all sowing , and never reaping ! Oh , what a false impression the -- -- gives of me ! And Miss -- -- never published my refutation", "context_len": 39}}}, "representations": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "And the youngsters scattered , to leave the green free , and to feast their imaginations upon the gaudy representations of the various attractions which adorned the sides of the yellow caravans that crept slowly up the dusty road from Warford", "context_len": 41}}}, "ailing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Her one room was hung all round with plants in various stages of drying , and she was always ready to prescribe a remedy for an ailing cow or a sick child , generally at much profit to herself , whatever might be the benefit to the sufferer", "context_len": 48}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "I ain \u2019 t like some folks , thank goodness , that \u2019 s put out and upsot over every little thing that happens ; an \u2019 if I \u2019 d been so , livin \u2019 so many years with an ailing husband , they \u2019 d had me dead an \u2019 buried long before him", "context_len": 56}}}, "overlooks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 72, "context": "The Outram of the book is just the Outram who was the admiration of our girlhood , -- generous , chivalrous , noble ! One feels how much pain that fine spirit would have been saved , had he realised how little it really matters whether good service be appreciated or not by man , if the great Leader accept it , -- if all be done as to Him Who never overlooks or misunderstands ! To our own Master we stand or fall ; let earthly superiors say what they will", "context_len": 92}}}, "misunderstands": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 74, "context": "The Outram of the book is just the Outram who was the admiration of our girlhood , -- generous , chivalrous , noble ! One feels how much pain that fine spirit would have been saved , had he realised how little it really matters whether good service be appreciated or not by man , if the great Leader accept it , -- if all be done as to Him Who never overlooks or misunderstands ! To our own Master we stand or fall ; let earthly superiors say what they will", "context_len": 92}}}, "cumbering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Our work is more like clearing in backwoods , -- there are huge trees and boulders cumbering the ground ; not just weeds overspreading a garden that once was a little cultivated", "context_len": 32}}}, "overspreading": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "One looking closer into his eyes might notice a dull film overspreading them , for Willie Richards was nearly blind , would be quite blind in a few weeks , the doctors said", "context_len": 33}}}, "propositions": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "If Mera Bhatija would bring out a nice wife , it would give much pleasure ; at present plans and propositions only -- I must not say burden me -- but they do not help me", "context_len": 36}}}, "destroyers": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 27, "context": "In double-column line ahead the greyhounds of the sea tore to meet the approaching troopships , then , at a signal from the senior officer , the destroyers `` broke line '' , tearing hither and thither seemingly without order or reason -- zigzagging , pirouetting , and crossing each others ' bows as if participating in an intricate maritime dance", "context_len": 61}}}, "concocting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "While I was taking tea with the Commander , the soldiers were concocting a letter to say that they had collected ten rupees to pay my expenses , and hoped that I would soon come again", "context_len": 36}}}, "uniting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Three centuries ago the tribes of the Maskoki family must have predominated in power over all their neighbors , as they do even now in numbers , and had formed confederacies uniting distant tribes", "context_len": 34}}}, "bearers": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "The bearers lifted the stretcher and continued on their way , while Rifleman Carr , slinging his rifle over his shoulder , hurried towards the German second-line trenches , where , judging by the deep detonations of exploding bombs and the sharp crack of rifle-shots , there was still work to be done", "context_len": 53}}}, "posh": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "demolish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "My attentive kahar , careful that the animal should not hurt me , cried out , Save the horse ! -- as if , instead of its kicking me , the danger was that a mild old lady approaching on foot should demolish the unfortunate animal ! \u2019", "context_len": 48}}}, "incorrectly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Allen Wright derives Alibamu ( also written Allibamous , Alibami , Alb\u00e1mu , incorrectly Alibamon ) from Cha'hta : \u00e1lba thicket and \u00e1yalmu , place cleared ( of trees , thickets ) : \u00e1lba ayam\u00fale I open or clear the thicket", "context_len": 42}}}, "scorpion": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "-- As I was engaged yesterday with a party of our boys , I was interrupted by hearing that my poor dear Ayah had been stung -- bitten , as the people incorrectly say -- by a scorpion", "context_len": 38}}}, "derive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "On the east it is bounded by ranges of barren and rocky hills , and on the west by vast deserts , consisting of moving sands , from which no animal or vegetable life can derive the means of existence", "context_len": 40}}}, "shaping": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "Their faces and hands were scrubbed with wholly unnecessary vigor , and in Albert 's case even ears , and then both children were thrust on to the little low seat again , and drawing a stool in front of them , Babette laid an elaborate manicure set open upon her lap , and gave her whole mind to the shaping and polishing of their nails -- a process in which Albert took great interest , and which was accomplished , it must be confessed , most dexterously and with great expedition", "context_len": 92}}}, "insuperable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 123, "context": "or shubbak , it would seem that what is intended to be described is a lofty hall ( or sorer ) , erected on the roof of the palace , whether round or square we can not tell , but crowned with a dome or cupola and having four-and-twenty deep projecting windows or oriels , the lattice or trellis-work of which latter was formed ( instead of the usual wood ) of emeralds , rubies and other jewels , strung , we may suppose , upon rods of gold or other metal I have , at the risk of wearying my reader , treated this point at some length , as well because it is an important one as to show the almost insuperable difficulties that beset the", "context_len": 128}}}, "unbelieving": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Paul \u2019 s directions as to the duty of believing wives towards unbelieving husbands ; and you can imagine how it complicates the difficulties of Zenana visitors !", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "Dick hurried her into the coach with all speed , and signed to his servant to lead his horse down a retired alley , but the aspect of the gathering crowd was so threatening , and that of his attendant saints so grim , that he began to suspect that his only escape from being stoned by the unbelieving mob , or run through by a Fifth Monarchy corporal , would be to be laid by the heels in a city jail !", "context_len": 83}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "I had asked for help , too , or thought I had , and yet there I was , faithless and unbelieving , not willing to wait his time and way to bring it to me", "context_len": 36}}}, "complicates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Paul \u2019 s directions as to the duty of believing wives towards unbelieving husbands ; and you can imagine how it complicates the difficulties of Zenana visitors !", "context_len": 28}}}, "parody": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "To parody Byron \u2019 s lines --", "context_len": 7}}}, "earache": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "was rather astonished at the wild bhajans , which he declares are all on one note -- but that is a mistake -- but he says that they helped to cure his earache ; a very curious and novel effect , which I never knew before to belong to a bhajan !", "context_len": 52}}}, "undermost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I write playfully , but the real undermost feeling in my heart is that of humble gratitude to Him from Whom all blessings flow , -- the love of true and God-fearing hearts being one of the most precious of those blessings", "context_len": 42}}}, "disreputable": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 81, "context": "The whole family was gathered together on the grass round the wood fire , the smoke of which rose up faintly blue against the russet of the beech-woods behind -- handsome , fierce-eyed men , lying slouching idly on the ground smoking short black pipes ; slatternly women with gay handkerchiefs tied round their black hair , bustling about with something savoury inside tin cans ; ragged brown-skinned little children , gnawing at bones with savage haste ; while a few disreputable dogs waited eagerly for the scraps that were thrown to them from time to time", "context_len": 97}}}, "raiment": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 18, "context": "Moreover , he brought him into a place , which he found full of coffers overflowing with royal raiment , such as ravished the wit , gold-inwoven stuffs , Indian and Chinese , and brocades , and he showed him also many other places , all full of that which beggareth description , till at last he brought him into a stable , wherein were horses whose like is not found with the kings of the world ; and therewithin he showed him a storehouse , full of housings and saddles of price , all broidered with pearls and precious stones and so forth", "context_len": 104}}}, "devilish": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Said I to him , Do you think such a religion is from God ? It is devilish , he frankly assented", "context_len": 22}}}, "pastor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "When he was about twenty-four years old M\u00fcller came over to England , and settled at Teignmouth as pastor of a small church", "context_len": 23}}}, "rejoicings": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 12, "context": "As for Alaeddin , he went out that day and saw the rejoicings toward in the city and the palace and fell a-laughing , especially when he heard the folk speak of the honour which had betided the Vizier 's son and the greatness of his good luck , in that he was become the Sultan 's son-in-law , and the exceeding pomp used in his marriage and bridal festivities ; and he said in himself , `` Ye know not , good simple folk that ye are , [ 403 ] what befell him last night , that ye envy him", "context_len": 102}}}, "sunbeam": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "This seems but a sunbeam to build upon ; yet as I have never known or heard of Muhammadans or Heathen smiling when about to die , -- the death-smile seems exclusively Christian ! -- I can not but hope that the dear little fellow had looked to the Saviour", "context_len": 50}}}, "bishopric": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He is to do the thinking and ordering and arrangement in his little bishopric", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "For some years the city was without bishops , owing to its insecure position during the strife between Wessex and Mercia , but later it appears as the seat of the great Mercian bishopric , retaining its jurisdiction until after the Norman conquest , when the see was transferred to Lincoln", "context_len": 51}}}, "deadness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "For the Europeans he has coldness , deadness , infidelity ! I noticed at Church that but one man stayed to Holy Communion", "context_len": 23}}}, "infidelity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "For the Europeans he has coldness , deadness , infidelity ! I noticed at Church that but one man stayed to Holy Communion", "context_len": 23}}}, "choruses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "His supporters no longer yelled to him to `` hurry up and knock the black out '' , while the Maoris sung their choruses again and again every time Jan failed to drive Te Paheka over the ropes", "context_len": 38}}}, "clark": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Clark", "context_len": 1}}}, "seasonable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I was very glad of the seasonable supply", "context_len": 8}}}, "soups": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "I see that my best plan is quietly to put things in her way that may be strengthening , but not to trouble her by pressing ; and to ensure soups , puddings , etc", "context_len": 35}}}, "nutritious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": ", being all thoroughly nutritious , so that the amount she does take may all do her real good", "context_len": 19}}}, "tahsildar": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The tahsildar [ 98 ] came one day to see us ; and reverently bowing his head before her , he asked her to lay her hand upon it , and pray for him , -- which she did , most earnestly asking that Heavenly light might be poured into his soul", "context_len": 52}}}, "periodical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "One little touch of depression had appeared a few weeks earlier , in a letter written before the visit of the Bishops , wherein Miss Tucker alluded to a slight sketch or account of herself which had been inserted in a Missionary periodical", "context_len": 43}}}, "reaping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I felt so dissatisfied with myself , -- my work seemed all sowing , and never reaping ! Oh , what a false impression the -- -- gives of me ! And Miss -- -- never published my refutation", "context_len": 39}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He worked in his own fields , ploughing , harvesting , and reaping , toiling harder than any of his labourers , and living in as plain a manner as possible", "context_len": 31}}}, "refutation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "I felt so dissatisfied with myself , -- my work seemed all sowing , and never reaping ! Oh , what a false impression the -- -- gives of me ! And Miss -- -- never published my refutation", "context_len": 39}}}, "misapplied": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The English , French and Spaniards frequently called him the Emperor of the Upper and Lower Creeks , a term which is not entirely misapplied when taken in its original sense of `` military commander , '' the imperator of the Romans", "context_len": 42}}}, "cautery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Praise misapplied Is to the generous mind not callous grown A burning cautery", "context_len": 13}}}, "unimportance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "For once in this little fit of down-heartedness , she seems to have somewhat lost her usual balanced view of the comparative unimportance of seemingly successful results", "context_len": 27}}}, "decayed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Even her brave and dauntless spirit had occasionally to pass under a cloud ; more often , as years passed on , and strength decayed", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The tree was old , so old and decayed that the upper side of the trunk had worn away altogether , leaving nothing but a hollow , crumbling shell , as rotten and dry as matchwood", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "No , it ain \u2019 t , said Fred , who had at length found a portion that was less decayed than the rest ; it \u2019 s elm-rind", "context_len": 29}}}, "unfed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Here in the attic -- with the cold night outside -- with the afternoon in the sloppy streets barely passed -- with the memory of the awful unfed look in the beggar child 's eyes not yet faded -- this simple , cheerful thing had happened like a thing of magic", "context_len": 51}}}, "kneeled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "George 's Chapel for a farewell service , and how they kneeled down before the altar , side by side , while the choir sang very sweetly", "context_len": 27}}}, "mug": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 84, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "carven": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` This is a carven flagon , '' said Sara , arranging tendrils of the wreath about the mug", "context_len": 19}}}, "flagon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` This is a carven flagon , '' said Sara , arranging tendrils of the wreath about the mug", "context_len": 19}}}, "encrusted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` And this '' -- bending tenderly over the soap dish and heaping it with roses -- '' is purest alabaster encrusted with gems", "context_len": 24}}}, "bonbon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` If we just had something for bonbon dishes , '' Sara murmured", "context_len": 13}}}, "candlestick": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "Moreover , she had with her eight and-forty slave-girls , whom Alaeddin had presented to her , in each one 's hand a great candle of camphor and ambergris , set in a candlestick of gold , studded with jewels ; and all the men and women in the palace went out with her and fared on before her , till they brought her to her bridegroom 's palace and carrying her up to her pavilion , [ 495 ] attired her in various robes [ 496 ] and displayed her", "context_len": 91}}}, "bated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "But Sara drew back and gazed at it , seeing wonders ; and Becky , after staring in delight , spoke with bated breath", "context_len": 24}}}, "splendors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "They expected that he would be interested in hearing about the splendors , and pomp , and parade of the Persian monarchy", "context_len": 22}}}, "waxen": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "It has a vaulted roof , and a minstrels ' gallery , and a huge chimney filled with blazing oaken logs , and it is brilliant with waxen tapers twinkling on every side", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "So Sara told her , and because her Magic helped her she made her ALMOST see it all : the golden platters -- the vaulted spaces -- the blazing logs -- the twinkling waxen tapers", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "On each sat a large doll -- and such dolls ! They had beautiful faces , waxen hands and feet , and what Bessie called `` live hair , yeal live hair", "context_len": 32}}}, "tapers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "To be sure , the apology for a tree was not dressed with glittering balls , rich bonbons , or rows of tapers ; its branches bore no expensive toys , rare books , or lovely pictures ; but the owner and the little ones for whose delight she dressed it , were quite satisfied , and only pitied those who had no tree at all", "context_len": 66}}}, "unanticipated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "To enter from the chill darkness outside , and find one 's self confronted by a totally unanticipated festal board , draped with red , adorned with white napery , and wreathed with flowers , was to feel that the preparations were brilliant indeed", "context_len": 44}}}, "napery": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "To enter from the chill darkness outside , and find one 's self confronted by a totally unanticipated festal board , draped with red , adorned with white napery , and wreathed with flowers , was to feel that the preparations were brilliant indeed", "context_len": 44}}}, "cleverest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "He was offered the position of town traveller , and soon proved himself to be one of the cleverest business men of the time", "context_len": 24}}}, "bonbons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "To be sure , the apology for a tree was not dressed with glittering balls , rich bonbons , or rows of tapers ; its branches bore no expensive toys , rare books , or lovely pictures ; but the owner and the little ones for whose delight she dressed it , were quite satisfied , and only pitied those who had no tree at all", "context_len": 66}}}, "specious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "The principal motive for Indian wars being the conquest of scalps , slaves , plunder and hunting grounds , the Creeks , conscious of their great power , were not very particular in finding causes for warfare , and did not even advance specious reasons for declaring war", "context_len": 48}}}, "damsels": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 46, "context": "So she rejoiced , albeit she had been sad and sorry for her [ whilom ] bridegroom the Vizier 's son , -- she rejoiced , [ I say ] , with an exceeding joy , when she saw the jewels and the beauty of the damsels , and was cheered ; whilst her father rejoiced exceedingly in her joy , in that he saw her put off chagrin and dejection", "context_len": 71}}}, "bassoons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 73, "context": "The last spark had died out of the paper in the grate and left only black tinder ; the table was left bare , the golden plates and richly embroidered napkins , and the garlands were transformed again into old handkerchiefs , scraps of red and white paper , and discarded artificial flowers all scattered on the floor ; the minstrels in the minstrel gallery had stolen away , and the viols and bassoons were still", "context_len": 76}}}, "minstrel": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 63, "context": "The last spark had died out of the paper in the grate and left only black tinder ; the table was left bare , the golden plates and richly embroidered napkins , and the garlands were transformed again into old handkerchiefs , scraps of red and white paper , and discarded artificial flowers all scattered on the floor ; the minstrels in the minstrel gallery had stolen away , and the viols and bassoons were still", "context_len": 76}}}, "notable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "The news of the murder of Edmund spread far and wide , and awakened deep sorrow and indignation , not only amongst his friends and subjects , but even amongst his former enemies , the Danes , now rapidly yielding to the civilising and softening influences of Christianity , following therein the notable example of their king , Canute , who was everywhere restoring the churches and monasteries he and his had destroyed , and saying , with no faltering voice , albeit , perhaps , with a very inadequate realisation of all the words implied , `` As for me and my house , we will serve the Lord", "context_len": 110}}}, "pertness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was no pertness in Sara 's manner", "context_len": 8}}}, "intemperate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Miss Minchin was infuriated just as she had been before and her anger expressed itself , as before , in an intemperate fashion", "context_len": 23}}}, "tinder": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "The last spark had died out of the paper in the grate and left only black tinder ; the table was left bare , the golden plates and richly embroidered napkins , and the garlands were transformed again into old handkerchiefs , scraps of red and white paper , and discarded artificial flowers all scattered on the floor ; the minstrels in the minstrel gallery had stolen away , and the viols and bassoons were still", "context_len": 76}}}, "skylight": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 25, "context": "The truth was , however , that it was a sound which had called her back -- a real sound -- the click of the skylight as it fell in closing after a lithe white figure which slipped through it and crouched down close by upon the slates of the roof -- just near enough to see what happened in the attic , but not near enough to be seen", "context_len": 70}}}, "downy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "And suppose '' -- as she drew the thin coverings over her -- '' suppose this was a beautiful soft bed , with fleecy blankets and large downy pillows", "context_len": 29}}}, "squeaks": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 60, "context": "The noise grew louder and louder , so that it resembled the squealing of a kitten , and all four began to turn over the moss with eager fingers , till , with a cry , Archie drew out a small round ball of dried grass , about the size of Bobby 's fist , from which issued such crescendo squeaks that there could be no mistake as to the locality of the sound", "context_len": 74}}}, "click": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 13, "context": "Jennie laughed merrily ; but as she was about to answer , the click of the gate was heard , and with shouts of `` He 's coming ! '' from Charlie , `` Poppy , poppy ! '' from the younger boy , and a confused jargon from Tommy , which no one but Jennie could understand , the whole three tumbled down from the window and rushed to the door", "context_len": 72}}}, "bedclothes": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 83, "context": "wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his absence", "context_len": 128}}}, "hob": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "In the grate there was a glowing , blazing fire ; on the hob was a little brass kettle hissing and boiling ; spread upon the floor was a thick , warm crimson rug ; before the fire a folding-chair , unfolded , and with cushions on it ; by the chair a small folding-table , unfolded , covered with a white cloth , and upon it spread small covered dishes , a cup , a saucer , a teapot ; on the bed were new warm coverings and a satin-covered down quilt ; at the foot a curious wadded silk robe , a pair of quilted slippers , and some books", "context_len": 112}}}, "hissing": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "In the grate there was a glowing , blazing fire ; on the hob was a little brass kettle hissing and boiling ; spread upon the floor was a thick , warm crimson rug ; before the fire a folding-chair , unfolded , and with cushions on it ; by the chair a small folding-table , unfolded , covered with a white cloth , and upon it spread small covered dishes , a cup , a saucer , a teapot ; on the bed were new warm coverings and a satin-covered down quilt ; at the foot a curious wadded silk robe , a pair of quilted slippers , and some books", "context_len": 112}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 43, "context": "On Willie 's knee was a Maltese kitten purring away contentedly ; but the moment she caught sight of Flossy , she sprang from her resting-place , and , scampering into a corner , put up her back , and began spitting and hissing in a very impolite manner", "context_len": 49}}}, "wadded": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 99, "context": "In the grate there was a glowing , blazing fire ; on the hob was a little brass kettle hissing and boiling ; spread upon the floor was a thick , warm crimson rug ; before the fire a folding-chair , unfolded , and with cushions on it ; by the chair a small folding-table , unfolded , covered with a white cloth , and upon it spread small covered dishes , a cup , a saucer , a teapot ; on the bed were new warm coverings and a satin-covered down quilt ; at the foot a curious wadded silk robe , a pair of quilted slippers , and some books", "context_len": 112}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "He and Langridge are a pair , and they can stick together , '' and Tom wadded up the invitation and threw it into the waste basket", "context_len": 27}}}, "quilted": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 106, "context": "In the grate there was a glowing , blazing fire ; on the hob was a little brass kettle hissing and boiling ; spread upon the floor was a thick , warm crimson rug ; before the fire a folding-chair , unfolded , and with cushions on it ; by the chair a small folding-table , unfolded , covered with a white cloth , and upon it spread small covered dishes , a cup , a saucer , a teapot ; on the bed were new warm coverings and a satin-covered down quilt ; at the foot a curious wadded silk robe , a pair of quilted slippers , and some books", "context_len": 112}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "He wore a short tunic , girt close around him , and over it a linen breast-plate , strongly quilted", "context_len": 20}}}, "flyleaf": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Something was written on the flyleaf -- just a few words , and they were these :", "context_len": 17}}}, "smudged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Peggy and Bobby as 'the children , ' with crumpled pinafores and smudged faces , were an equal success , for in watching Archie Peggy had forgotten her sudden shyness , and she now threw herself thoroughly into her part , howling most realistically when her nose was scrubbed in the wash-tub , or her hair combed with a fork ; while Bobby stole jam and resisted medicine with lifelike zeal", "context_len": 71}}}, "savory": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "Marie-Celeste was up betimes the next morning -- that is , if betimes means bright and early , and , stopping for a few minutes on her way to indulge in a savory cup of arrowroot , which the stewardess had made ready for her , she passed on up the stairs and out on to the saloon deek , looking as fresh and sweet in her dress of sailor-blue as a fair little morning-glory", "context_len": 75}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` Oh , I guess we can tackle a slice , '' remarked William , and Fenn went proudly to the stove , where the pudding was being kept hot , and soon had it on the table , flanked by two bowls of savory sauce", "context_len": 46}}}, "sandwich": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 66, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "muffin": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Blue Crest had a muffin with strawberry jam on it , and she perched on the end of her plate and pecked away in perfect delight", "context_len": 26}}}, "pinches": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "'We ca n't afford it , Peggy , ' he said briefly ; then , noticing her look of disappointment : 'That 's where the shoe pinches , my dear child", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "No one could guess the strain of talking so incessantly to amuse the babies , of reading to Jack , of having eyes all over to see that he did not torment the little ones , push their playthings out of the way , give them sly pinches or tweak their hair", "context_len": 52}}}, "drowsiness": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "He had not meant that any one should know it , such a modest little knight was he ; but alas ! the weakness of drowsiness had overtaken the valiant little fellow , and in the disorder thereon attendant the shapely little limb had thrust itself forth from the folds of the protecting kilt , and there was the garter plainly visible to the most casual passer-by", "context_len": 67}}}, "luxuriated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was the drowsiness of happy , well-fed childhood , and they sat in the fire glow and luxuriated in it until Sara found herself turning to look at her transformed bed", "context_len": 32}}}, "anyways": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` If it ai n't here in the mornin ' , miss , '' she said , `` it 's been here tonight , anyways , an ' I sha n't never forget it", "context_len": 34}}}, "scullery": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Miss Forster opened her eyes wide enough , as it was , to see a neat telephone , made of two empty cocoa-tins and a piece of waxed string , fixed up between the house and the stables , while a small windmill on the scullery roof turned the coffee-grinder in the kitchen , to the huge amazement and delight of the cook", "context_len": 63}}}, "dispensed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "Chris is leaning over the ship 's side , and Donald , again in sailor rig , is close beside him ; for Ted had dispensed with Donald 's services when he decided to follow up the driving party , and he had at once hurried back to Nuneham to help Chris , who was trying to get everything into shape for the old people before leaving", "context_len": 67}}}, "bounden": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The servants knew that she was allowed to stay because Miss Minchin could not easily find another creature helpless and humble enough to work like a bounden slave for so few shillings a week", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` The case before us , '' said the king , `` is a difficult and trying one , but one which we must discharge in our bounden duty towards our subjects", "context_len": 32}}}, "priggishly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I felt it my duty '' -- priggishly", "context_len": 8}}}, "tatters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "And it 's ridiculous that she should look so grand , and be made so much of , in her rags and tatters ! ''", "context_len": 25}}}, "attics": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 3, "context": "Most of the attics were quite excellent , but this room had a decidedly sloping roof , and rather small windows , and the paper on the walls was ugly , and the accommodation scanty , and what those huge boxes were going to do there was more than I could tell", "context_len": 52}}}, "askance": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "Every time she had come anywhere near them she had gathered her skirts about her as though in fear of actual contamination , and with her pretty head high in the air , as she moved away , would look askance over her shoulder as though not at all sure even then of being at a safe distance", "context_len": 58}}}, "housemaids": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 35, "context": "Oh ! if only every one might have his turn in this world of ours ; but since that is out of the question , let there at least be as much Coney Island for housemaids as is consistent with good morals and faithful discharge of their duties ; at least so thought one dear little mistress , with more heart , perhaps , than discretion , but a heart , all the same , that won every one to her and made life in her household move with infinite smoothness", "context_len": 91}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "Rosamund got up softly and locked the door , not a minute too soon , for she had scarcely done so before the handle was turned and the voice of Irene was heard outside crying through the keyhole , `` What changeling is in this room ? Which of you housemaids has dared to lock herself in ? Come out ! I 've got a big spider ready , and '' -- --", "context_len": 73}}}, "gurgling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "What a noisy , merry , frolicksome stream this was , gurgling and splashing , rushing and tumbling in its rocky bed ; now leaping gracefully in a miniature waterfall over some narrow ledge , now rippling and singing about the roots of the trees and over the pebbles that lay in its course , now flashing in the sunlight , and now hiding in a crevice of the rocks as if it were playing at Bopeep", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Then Harry could see an effort of the reason made over the animal faculties , and for a few moments the drowning man took a few steady strokes , but only to utter a gurgling cry and throw up his hands , beat the water again , and go under", "context_len": 50}}}, "strictness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "The town anciently was known under two other names : Ispok\u00f3gi , or It\u00e1lua ispok\u00f3gi , said to mean `` town of survivors , '' or `` surviving town , remnant of a town '' ; and It\u00e1lua f\u00e1tcha-s\u00edgo , `` incorrect town , town deviating from strictness", "context_len": 48}}}, "muddier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "If it was possible for weather to be worse than it had been the day before , it was worse this day -- wetter , muddier , colder", "context_len": 28}}}, "bookshelf": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The ugly walls were gradually entirely covered with pictures and draperies , ingenious pieces of folding furniture appeared , a bookshelf was hung up and filled with books , new comforts and conveniences appeared one by one , until there seemed nothing left to be desired", "context_len": 46}}}, "conveniences": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The ugly walls were gradually entirely covered with pictures and draperies , ingenious pieces of folding furniture appeared , a bookshelf was hung up and filled with books , new comforts and conveniences appeared one by one , until there seemed nothing left to be desired", "context_len": 46}}}, "shabbiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Sara was sent on errands in all weathers , and scolded and driven hither and thither ; she was scarcely allowed to speak to Ermengarde and Lottie ; Lavinia sneered at the increasing shabbiness of her clothes ; and the other girls stared curiously at her when she appeared in the schoolroom", "context_len": 52}}}, "blissfully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "During the hardest day she could occupy herself blissfully by thinking of what she should see when she opened the attic door , and wondering what new delight had been prepared for her", "context_len": 33}}}, "fattening": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Rows of cottages and gardens , great corn and hayfields , stubble where cattle were browsing , enclosures of fattening pigs whose squealing had a mirthful sound", "context_len": 27}}}, "vagueness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` There is something very disagreeable in seeing that sort of thing in a child of her age , '' said Miss Minchin , with haughty vagueness", "context_len": 27}}}, "unwise": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Do you remember , '' put in the unwise Miss Amelia , `` what she said to you that day in the schoolroom about what you would do if you found out that she was -- ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "plumper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Very naturally , even Becky was beginning to look plumper and less frightened", "context_len": 13}}}, "mattresses": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "I was ready to poke at the mattresses on the new beds , and to admire the chain springs , and to examine the ventilators in the walls of every single room", "context_len": 32}}}, "sara": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Sara did as she was told", "context_len": 6}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Sara looked down and flushed a little , because she thought her pet fancy might not be easy for strangers -- even nice ones -- to understand at first", "context_len": 29}}}, "bachelor": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 58, "context": "And then when it comes to be well known with its family of dear little daughters , I hope that , once in a while , people who have had little children and lost them , and people who have never had them at all , and now and then a maiden lady , or even an old bachelor , will come down there and carry off one or more of the little girls , to bring them up as their own in their own homes , and so room will be made for others", "context_len": 95}}}, "crotchety": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Such a person , however , would be sure to be crotchety and hot-tempered enough to be easily offended", "context_len": 19}}}, "seminary": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "So until almost midnight the entire seminary , realizing somehow that all rules were laid aside , crowded round Ermengarde in the schoolroom and heard read and re-read the letter containing a story which was quite as wonderful as any Sara herself had ever invented , and which had the amazing charm of having happened to Sara herself and the mystic Indian gentleman in the very next house", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Yes ; I live at Miss Minchin 's seminary", "context_len": 10}}}, "envying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Tom and Phil roomed together , and at midnight Tom , who had just fallen into a doze , after envying the sound slumber of his chum , was awakened by the latter", "context_len": 33}}}, "scathingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Perhaps the diamond mines have suddenly appeared again , '' said Lavinia , scathingly", "context_len": 15}}}, "softness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "The girl had a sort of barbaric love of brightness and softness ; and one day , as she looked over some fabrics for which Jane , spurred by the approach of the vacation and the fact that Lola was to have a part in the closing exercises of school , had sent to Denver , the girl said suddenly , `` How good my father is to me , tia ! ''", "context_len": 73}}}, "impolite": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 56, "context": "Hall laughed and offered Franky his stick to ride on , but the little boy would not take it ; and when he found he could not have the sugar-plums , walked away with an offended air , which amused the old gentleman very much , though it distressed his sisters , who thought him very impolite", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Boom ! '' from the impolite pirates", "context_len": 8}}}, "carmichael": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Carmichael to go to her , by actually coming in search of her pupil herself", "context_len": 15}}}, "chirrup": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Do n't chirrup so loud , Donald , '' Janet said", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Once as the party , who were all growing somewhat tired , were rather quiet , they suddenly heard a long , loud chirrup ; and looking round to the side whence the noise came , there , upon a heap of stones , sat a large gray squirrel , with his tail curled gracefully over his back like a plume , and seeming to call attention to himself by his song", "context_len": 72}}}, "incontinently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "All three of them incontinently fled from the room and tumbled into the hall", "context_len": 14}}}, "rosier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "He looked rosier than ever , and brought an atmosphere of freshness and health with him ; but his eyes were disappointed and anxious as they met the invalid 's look of eager question even as they grasped each other 's hands", "context_len": 42}}}, "salaaming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "What was it that brought Ram Dass into the room -- even as his master spoke -- salaaming respectfully , but with a scarcely concealed touch of excitement in his dark , flashing eyes ?", "context_len": 35}}}, "sahib": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "It was my thought that it would please the sahib to see and speak with her", "context_len": 16}}}, "drudge": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "'Do your household duties thoroughly , but do n't let them absorb you entirely , for Father does not want you to be a mere domestic drudge , with no ideas beyond the potato-pan and the pepper-pot", "context_len": 37}}}, "soften": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "It was obvious that he was nothing better than a pirate , but he managed to gloss over the barbarities of the life so well , and to dwell on its picturesque and adventurous side so successfully , that Dick began insensibly to soften in his judgment of the wanderer", "context_len": 50}}}, "misled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "As Caussin de Perceval remarks , it is evident that Shawish ( whether from ignorance or carelessness ) must , in many instances , have utterly misled his French coadjutor ( who had no knowledge of Arabic ) as to the meaning of the original , whilst it is much to be regretted that a writer of exquisite genius and one of the first stylists of the 18th century , such as the author of the Diable Amoureux , ( a masterpiece to be ranked with Manon Lescaut and Le Neveu de Rameau , ) should have stooped to the commission of the flagrant offences against good taste and artistic morality which disfigure well nigh every line of the so-called `` Sequel to the 1001 Nights", "context_len": 126}}}, "clues": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "For some time he had failed to pick up any direct clues of those he was pursuing", "context_len": 17}}}, "lovers": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 49, "context": "I had been feeling almost happy in his society -- I had almost forgotten the Duchess , and even Jim Randolph had been put into the background of my thoughts ; but when Albert proposed that he and I should sit in the `` Lover 's Arbour '' as lovers , I felt a shiver run through me", "context_len": 58}}}, "rigidly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "If they kept rigidly to the codes of civil life they would soon have found themselves very much out in the cold as far as personal comforts were concerned , although on board , in camp , and on active service , it was noticeable that personal property was rightly considered as inviolate", "context_len": 53}}}, "proprietress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "I am Miss Minchin , the proprietress of the Young Ladies ' Seminary next door", "context_len": 15}}}, "solicitor": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "Some of the grit of the old Crusaders was left in his blood , and instead of taking his solicitor 's advice , and selling the place for what it would fetch , he resolved to farm the land himself , and by using every care and economy to free the property , and raise it to its former level in the county", "context_len": 63}}}, "intruded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "And they were stern , for strange thoughts had intruded themselves , seeming to take possession of the young man 's mind , and making him speak and act contrary to his wont", "context_len": 33}}}, "forwardness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I have just discovered that you have been intruded upon through the forwardness of one of my pupils -- a charity pupil", "context_len": 22}}}, "retrieved": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The diamond mines have retrieved themselves", "context_len": 6}}}, "reddened": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Now the warrior 's cup of joy was full , When he drank the blood of his foe , Where the slain lay thick on the gory hill , And torrents of blood from every rill reddened the river below , For Odin 's hall is the Northman 's heaven -- ''", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The native looked at him with curiosity dawning in his reddened eyes", "context_len": 12}}}, "peppery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "This was worse than the eccentric bachelor uncle who might have a peppery temper and be easily offended at the treatment of his niece", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The weather was cold and the night dark , and there were peppery little showers of sleet", "context_len": 17}}}, "patrons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Carrisford chose to tell certain of her patrons how unhappy Sara Crewe had been made , many unpleasant things might happen", "context_len": 21}}}, "hungriest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` I -- TRIED not to be anything else , '' she answered in a low voice -- '' even when I was coldest and hungriest -- I tried not to be", "context_len": 32}}}, "acidly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Now it will not be necessary to try , '' said Miss Minchin , acidly , as Ram Dass salaamed her out of the room", "context_len": 26}}}, "salaamed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Now it will not be necessary to try , '' said Miss Minchin , acidly , as Ram Dass salaamed her out of the room", "context_len": 26}}}, "grovel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "She saw that you were a hard-hearted , worldly woman , and that I was a weak fool , and that we were both of us vulgar and mean enough to grovel on our knees for her money , and behave ill to her because it was taken from her -- though she behaved herself like a little princess even when she was a beggar", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "I am willing to make all the amends I am able for the accidental damage to your property , but I ca n't and wo n't cringe to your injustice , nor grovel at your feet", "context_len": 36}}}, "gurgles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "I shall tell his aunt so the next time I see her , and I do n't care who hears me ! ' she added , catching sight of a grinning face on the veranda , for Archie had stolen out to see the fun , and overflowed in such gurgles of delight at this sally that Lilian trembled for the result", "context_len": 62}}}, "sal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "And she was in danger of making so much noise with her hysterical chokes and gurgles that her sister was obliged to go to her and apply salts and sal volatile to quiet her , instead of pouring forth her indignation at her audacity", "context_len": 44}}}, "mystic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "So until almost midnight the entire seminary , realizing somehow that all rules were laid aside , crowded round Ermengarde in the schoolroom and heard read and re-read the letter containing a story which was quite as wonderful as any Sara herself had ever invented , and which had the amazing charm of having happened to Sara herself and the mystic Indian gentleman in the very next house", "context_len": 68}}}, "blurring": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Glad as she was for Sara 's sake , she went up the last flight of stairs with a lump in her throat and tears blurring her sight", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "His head bent lower , and he blinked his eyelids rapidly to scatter the blurring mist which threatened to blind him", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Nevertheless , when , with a hand on Ben \u2019 s shoulder , Winton told him that he was the man who had saved the day and won the game , he smiled a little , and there was a blurring mist in his eyes", "context_len": 45}}}, "anne": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Anne", "context_len": 1}}}, "passers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "So he told them how , when he sat alone , ill and dull and irritable , Ram Dass had tried to distract him by describing the passers by , and there was one child who passed oftener than any one else ; he had begun to be interested in her -- partly perhaps because he was thinking a great deal of a little girl , and partly because Ram Dass had been able to relate the incident of his visit to the attic in chase of the monkey", "context_len": 89}}}, "profoundness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Ram Dass had been lying flat upon the slates , looking in at the skylight , when the banquet had come to its disastrous conclusion ; he had been sure of the profoundness of Sara 's wearied sleep ; and then , with a dark lantern , he had crept into the room , while his companion remained outside and handed the things to him", "context_len": 65}}}, "boarhound": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "She found beautiful new flowers growing in her room , whimsical little gifts tucked under pillows , and once , as they sat together in the evening , they heard the scratch of a heavy paw on the door , and when Sara went to find out what it was , there stood a great dog -- a splendid Russian boarhound -- with a grand silver and gold collar bearing an inscription", "context_len": 72}}}, "bun": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "`` We walked some , then another man rode us a little way , and the boys went off and I got lost more and more and could n't find a cop , and asked every so many people , and a woman gave me a bun and a cake , and then a man took me across the park and told me to go straight along", "context_len": 67}}}, "hungrier": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "Then she told him the story of the bun shop , and the fourpence she picked up out of the sloppy mud , and the child who was hungrier than herself", "context_len": 31}}}, "irritating": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "A battalion picture-theatre , billiard rooms , voluntary swimming parades , boxing , and a variety of other indoor and outdoor games contributed to the men 's enjoyment ; and , although discipline was well enforced , there was a total absence of irritating petty restrictions that form a constant source of annoyance to the men of the New Armies of the Motherland", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Whistling Jim laughed one of his irritating laughs", "context_len": 8}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "With arms outstretched the Hun staggered towards the Diggers , coughing violently the while under the irritating influence of the smoke-bomb", "context_len": 21}}}, "ravenous": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "It was , indeed , a bold thing for Dolly to ask any thing of one whom she had injured so much ; but she was ravenous with hunger , and having no shame , she had no thought save how she might satisfy it", "context_len": 45}}}, "insides": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Many 's the time she 's told me of it since -- how she sat there in the wet , and felt as if a wolf was a-tearing at her poor young insides", "context_len": 34}}}, "numeral": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "At the same time , my heart , in a way , is still with the little people who count their years by a single numeral ; and so , if you please , I want to take them aside for a moment , and just whisper in their ears that , although A Little Oueen of Hearts may seem a trifle too old for them at first , I have an idea they will not find that fault later on", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The cardinal numeral has a full form ending in -in , and another abbreviated from it used in counting objects , and not extending beyond ten ; an ordinal , with prefix -\u00edsa- , is- , apheret", "context_len": 37}}}, "undignified": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "He was a thoroughly manly little fellow -- nobody questioned that for a moment , not even Ted ; and yet there he sat , his head bowed upon his folded arms , while now and then something very like a sob seemed to shake the well-knit figure and give the boyish head an undignified little bob", "context_len": 57}}}, "mistrustful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "How old was he ? asks Young America , a little mistrustful as to the right sort of stuff ; but what does it matter how old he was , since this is certain , that he was not the boy to cry under any circumstances without abundant reason", "context_len": 49}}}, "geometrical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "He sat up , and resting his head on one hand , reached with the other for the paper-knife , and began cutting queer little geometrical figures on the big silver-cornered blotter that half covered the table", "context_len": 37}}}, "blotter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "He sat up , and resting his head on one hand , reached with the other for the paper-knife , and began cutting queer little geometrical figures on the big silver-cornered blotter that half covered the table", "context_len": 37}}}, "pursed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "But this did not last long ; Harold was simply trying to make up his mind , as the phrase goes , and that soon accomplished , he drew pen , paper and ink toward him and commenced writing a letter , with his head on one side and his lips tightly pursed together", "context_len": 54}}}, "sorrier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "He had never dreamed this would be so ; he had never meant it should be ; but that did not alter the fact that , flattered and made much of ever since he went up to Oxford , he had somehow had little time to think of his mother , and , sorrier than that , little inclination", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` I ca n't help being sorry for Toby , 'cause he has no soul , '' said Bessie ; `` but I 'm a great deal sorrier for those people that do n't think about their souls , and go to Jesus to be saved", "context_len": 46}}}, "contemplate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "The party thus opposed were inactive and silent while Alexander was in their country , on his first visit to southern Greece ; but after his return they began to contemplate more decisive action , and afterward , when they heard of his having undertaken so desperate an enterprise as going northward with his forces , and actually crossing the Danube , they considered him as so completely out of the way that they grew very courageous , and meditated open rebellion", "context_len": 82}}}, "physique": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "To be a Christ Church Senior , with plenty of money and plenty of friends and a head that easily mastered enough learning to make a good showing , left little to be desired , especially when already endowed with a handsome face and a physique that every man envied -- at least , so thought Theodore Harris , and so thought and affirmed the half score of intimate friends who enjoyed many of the good things of this life through his bounty", "context_len": 83}}}, "affirmed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "To be a Christ Church Senior , with plenty of money and plenty of friends and a head that easily mastered enough learning to make a good showing , left little to be desired , especially when already endowed with a handsome face and a physique that every man envied -- at least , so thought Theodore Harris , and so thought and affirmed the half score of intimate friends who enjoyed many of the good things of this life through his bounty", "context_len": 83}}}, "inconvenienced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Why do n't the fellow come back ? he muttered angrily , realizing , as he heard the clock strike half-past twelve , that he had been actually inconvenienced for a whole half hour ; and shortly after the fellow did come back , the dearest little fellow in the world too , by the way , and shut to the big front door and locked it as he had done night after night during the last two years , while Ted was up at Oxford , and he had been living alone with the servants in the pretty little home there at Windsor", "context_len": 104}}}, "unconcealed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "After she had received a word or two of direction from Miss Belmore , the children set off under her guidance , with unconcealed delight on their faces at the prospect of seeing with their own eyes these mysterious apartments , and with a deep-seated hope in each quick-beating heart that in all the full regalia of crown and sceptre and ermine they might somewhere encounter the marvellous Queen", "context_len": 69}}}, "uncivil": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "I went out for a walk and to post a letter , he said , after a moment , and with a perceptible little note of apology in his tone for his uncivil answer of the half hour before", "context_len": 39}}}, "intimidated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Not a bit intimidated , Harold looked him straight in the face", "context_len": 12}}}, "role": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "It might well happen that the father and mother , who were quite willing that their little daughter should have an occasional chat with the postman at home , would prefer not to recognize him in the role of a second-cabin passenger ; and good Chris Hartley felt inclined to call himself all manner of names for thoughtlessly allowing himself to be put in such a position", "context_len": 67}}}, "nonplussed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The role was such a new one for Harold to play that Ted stood utterly nonplussed", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The son looked nonplussed , but finally consented in that case to defray the expense out of his private means -- that is , if it could not be taken out of the young lady 's fortune", "context_len": 37}}}, "american": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "American children are a handful always", "context_len": 6}}}, "goodnight": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "A little later the boys had said goodnight to each other , and not in an altogether unkindly spirit either", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "No one was in his own little room , that he felt rather than saw in a moment ; but some one might be in Edmund 's , and he passed through the door , which he remembered , with a shudder , was shut firmly when Edmund said `` goodnight", "context_len": 51}}}, "twelvemonth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "Ted had not been as angry as Harold had expected , and Harold , sorry for his thrust about money matters , had wound up by being rather conciliatory , and he was happier , on the whole , than he had been any time for a twelvemonth", "context_len": 48}}}, "besetting": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Smith made a list of subjects for daily prayer , embracing repentance , faith , love , grace to help , gratitude , power to pray , constant direction in all things , a right understanding of the Bible , deliverance from besetting sin , constancy in God 's service , relatives and friends , missionaries , pardon for all ignorance and sin in prayer , etc", "context_len": 67}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "When she was much younger , extreme carelessness had been her besetting fault , and , as is the manner of this `` little fox , '' had created much trouble for herself and for others ; but having become convinced that it was her duty to cure herself of this , she had set to work to do it in such earnest that that which had been a burden and a care to her was fast becoming a settled habit , and it was but seldom now that any act or word of heedlessness could be laid to her charge , while her ever obliging disposition and loving heart prompted many a deed of kindness which she never failed to carry out if it were in her", "context_len": 128}}}, "hartley": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "HARTLEY", "context_len": 1}}}, "graciousness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "Besides , Ted had taken quite civil leave of him before going back to Oxford , and had said he fancied would be down again next Sunday , and that he would be on hand , like as not , if Uncle Fritz decided to come over -- all of which , for any one who knew Ted as Harold knew him , was graciousness itself , and made Harold wish he had not waited so long before taking matters into his own hands", "context_len": 84}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "Well , this beats all , said Harold to himself ; what is going to happen next , I wonder ? But he had the graciousness to defer his own greeting to Uncle Everett until he assisted Aunt Lou and Dorothy and Marie-Celeste to dismount , by aid of the brake 's steps , and which much practice , by the way , enabled them to accomplish very skilfully", "context_len": 69}}}, "brace": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "I seated myself , and then seeing that his eyes were still on me , my heart began to beat a little more quickly , and I began to feel uncomfortable and anxious , and then I knew that I must brace myself up to listen to something which would be hard to bear", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then , taking a bow and quiver of arrows , together with a brace of spears , out of the canoe , and signing to me to do the same , Ama led the way through the dense growth bordering the river bank , until we reached an open grassy space of about twenty acres , sparsely dotted here and there with magnificent trees ; and here Ama signified that we were to camp for the day", "context_len": 77}}}, "atoning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The Eton boys in their tall hats ( atoning , as it were , for the extreme briefness of their jackets ) and wide-rolling linen collars were skurrying through the streets as though they had the right of way , as indeed they have in dear old royal Windsor ; and here and there the flowing gown of a colleger spread itself to the April wind and floated out behind , to all appearances as glad as any peacock to show what it could do in that direction", "context_len": 88}}}, "briefness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "The Eton boys in their tall hats ( atoning , as it were , for the extreme briefness of their jackets ) and wide-rolling linen collars were skurrying through the streets as though they had the right of way , as indeed they have in dear old royal Windsor ; and here and there the flowing gown of a colleger spread itself to the April wind and floated out behind , to all appearances as glad as any peacock to show what it could do in that direction", "context_len": 88}}}, "inspiriting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Indeed , who knows of a more inspiriting sight anywhere than Eton College on an April morning ? The quaint old buildings seem to bask in the broad spring sunshine ; the trees that dot the grass-bare turf where the Upper School fronts the street are already casting tiny leaf-shadows , and on the other side , where the garden slopes down to the Thames , many a little branch and bush begins to glow with color", "context_len": 77}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Wherever the danger seemed greatest there was the duke to be found inspiriting his men , restraining them , or putting fresh heart into them", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then , just when it seemed that hope must be abandoned , an inspiriting cry came from Bomba", "context_len": 18}}}, "quadrangle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "He entered , knelt in a mental attitude easier to imagine than describe , but felt some heavenly dew fall upon his bleeding wounds ; he left without waiting to speak to any one at the conclusion of the service , and was crossing the quadrangle to the palace which occupied a portion of the site of modern Christ Church , when a heavy hand was laid upon his shoulder", "context_len": 70}}}, "panoply": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "in the outer quadrangle , with all its panoply of robes of state and globe and sceptre , appears to look a little more chipper than ever and a trifle more conscious of the distinction of being the munificent founder of so glorious an institution", "context_len": 45}}}, "chipper": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "in the outer quadrangle , with all its panoply of robes of state and globe and sceptre , appears to look a little more chipper than ever and a trifle more conscious of the distinction of being the munificent founder of so glorious an institution", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "She was quite chipper with Fred and me now and then , when no grown people were around , and used to tell us stories of things which happened at the old homestead by the hour", "context_len": 36}}}, "penknives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "No wonder the boys love the old place , and even the dingy recitation rooms , whose quaint , high desks and slippery benches are notched with the penknives of many a boy , whose name , as a man , has come to be known through the length and breadth of England", "context_len": 53}}}, "charms": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 52, "context": "May drew to a close with a burst of warm weather , and the Whitsuntide holidays promised to prove all that the heart of the cheap tripper might desire , though beyond a chance cyclist or two that article was as unknown as the dodo in quiet Gorswen , where fortunately the charms of the scenery had not yet been spoilt by picnic parties leaving greasy sandwich papers and ginger-beer bottles in the woods , and demanding noisy entertainment in the village , nor the youth of the neighbourhood corrupted into hanging round the public-house doors to listen to the mirth and songs of the excursionists within , or offer faded bunches of flowers in exchange for halfpence", "context_len": 118}}}, "reams": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "beaches": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "It is a cloudless morning , the white sands of the South-shore beaches shine like silver in the sunlight , and the fresh sea breeze that is stirring holds its own the whole length of Long Island , and blows its purifying way into every street and alley of the vast city that lies at its farther end", "context_len": 58}}}, "purifying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "It is a cloudless morning , the white sands of the South-shore beaches shine like silver in the sunlight , and the fresh sea breeze that is stirring holds its own the whole length of Long Island , and blows its purifying way into every street and alley of the vast city that lies at its farther end", "context_len": 58}}}, "bigness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` asked the princess , `` and where shall we find its egg ? '' And the Moor said to her , `` O my lady , this is a great bird that taketh up camels and elephants in its talons and flieth with them , of its bigness and greatness ; it is mostly to be found in the mountain Caf and the craftsman who builded this palace [ 654 ] is able to bring its egg", "context_len": 78}}}, "transcendent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "A most uninteresting city , this city of Brooklyn , some people affirm ; even those of us who love it best can not claim that it is great in anything but bigness but there are homes there we will match against homes the world over , not for show or for luxury , but for pure and transcendent comfort", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Although a transcendent interest in grown-up people is one of the traits that make it worth while to tell this story of a summer in the life of little Marie-Celeste , yet she was none the less a friend of children of her own age , or over it or under it for that matter , provided they seemed to stand in want of a friend", "context_len": 66}}}, "sameness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "To be sure , the houses that line these streets stand most of them shoulder to shoulder , in barbarous , city-like fashion , and with far too much sameness in their general make-up and plan", "context_len": 36}}}, "claiming": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 78, "context": "Dear old mammy , who nursed every one of us , from Ruthven down to myself , and whom mother gave to Margaret as a treasure past all price when Harry was born , -- poor mammy , who considers herself quite as much one of the family as any Stanton , Duncan , or Bradford among us all , -- to talk of dismissing her ! But nothing less would satisfy Aunt Patty ; and Margaret gently claiming the right to correct her own children and govern her own household as she saw fit , and Henry firmly upholding his wife , Aunt Patty departed that very afternoon in a tremendous passion , and has never entered the house since", "context_len": 121}}}, "preoccupied": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "But there was no room in Dorothy 's mind just then for musings either on nature or Ruskin , and it is to be feared that the dancing blue of the water and the purple shadows on the hills and golden glow of the sunset made little impression on her wholly preoccupied mind", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "What 's the matter ? asks Harold , who is on his knees on the floor , trying to make some very stiff wrapping-paper accommodate itself to the edges and corners of a generous box of luncheon , and is : quite too preoccupied to look up", "context_len": 47}}}, "gossipy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "They are friends and neighbors in the best sense of the word ; too high-minded and preoccupied to be gossipy or prying , they are interested in each other 's affairs with the interest that means a sharing of each other 's joys and sorrows", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "How I wished I could tell him that those were merely two old gossipy daddy loons accompanying their mates to their respective home bays on the lake", "context_len": 27}}}, "hyphen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Indeed , the two names have even become to be written with a hyphen , and seemingly to belong to each other , and to be quite as inseparable as the three syllables of Dorothy or the four of Dorothea", "context_len": 40}}}, "doormat": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "A great white cat peeped out from behind the geraniums which filled the window ; a greyhound lay upon the doormat , and beneath and about the porch hung several bird-cages , containing half a dozen canaries and two mocking-birds , while a donkey and a tame goat looked , the one over , the other between the bars of the fence which divided their little pasture ground from the neat garden", "context_len": 72}}}, "meditates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "People ought n't to leave a canary hanging full in the sunshine on a warm day like this ; and then she meditates awhile on the advantages of living on the side of the street that is shady in the afternoon", "context_len": 41}}}, "thoroughfare": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Being of a mechanical turn of mind , the two brothers soon reduced the motor to a state of servile tractability , although there was hardly a thoroughfare in Christchurch whose buildings did not bear a more or less permanent record of Juggernaut 's frailties", "context_len": 45}}}, "proclivities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "She wishes he would look up , for she has a bow ready for him ; but he does n't , and she must needs defer her social proclivities yet a little while longer", "context_len": 34}}}, "divining": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "Very few of us are heroes when it really comes to the point , and I grieve to say that she looked so glum , and was generally so grumpy and discontented over the arrangement , that unselfish Lilian , divining the cause , instantly proposed to give up her own part to Peggy , and train a little village girl for the second 'child ' instead", "context_len": 67}}}, "mails": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "One rider covered the last 130 miles of the western division , from old Camp Floyd to Salt Lake City , where his partner from the east met and exchanged mails with the comrade going toward the Missouri", "context_len": 38}}}, "inflection": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 2, "context": "The case inflection of the substantive , adjective , of some pronouns and of the nominal forms of the verb is effected by the suffixes : -i for the absolute , -ut for the subjective , -un for the objective case : y\u00e1ti person , y\u00e1tut , y\u00e1tun ; n\u00e1ki what , which , n\u00e1kut , n\u00e1kun", "context_len": 57}}}, "furtively": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "He looked at me hard as I entered , and continued to watch me furtively for some time , and then his face cleared up and I knew that he had recognized me", "context_len": 33}}}, "imparting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Have you told any of the others ? she asked eagerly , when the story 's end had been reached , and hoping in her heart of hearts that she was to have the pleasure of imparting news of such paramount importance to the neighborhood", "context_len": 45}}}, "paramount": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "As Philip had been made commander-in-chief of the Grecian armies which were about to undertake the conquest of Asia , and as , of course , his influence and power in all that related to that vast enterprise would be paramount and supreme ; and as all were ambitious to have a large share in the glory of that expedition , and to participate , as much as possible , in the power and in the renown which seemed to be at Philip 's disposal , all were , of course , very anxious to secure his favor", "context_len": 98}}}, "wherefrom": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 71, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then I hunted about for some soft wood wherefrom to make mouthpieces and the stopped end of the flute ; and it was while I was thus engaged that I made a most important discovery , which was nothing less than that there were several very fine specimens of the cinchona tree growing in the jungle quite close to the town", "context_len": 61}}}, "surpassing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 75, "context": "to learn just what the issue had been , and at a time when he would be able to stop and tell about it ? Would any one else in the world have thought of suggesting that he should give three short little whistles when he reached the Browns ' , in Remsen Street , so that she should know just how near he was ? Surely no one ; and it was just this surpassing interest in every living body , to the utter forgetting of all that concerned herself , that made Marie-Celeste different from other children , that made everybody love her , and that makes it worth while for me to try to tell this story of one summer in her blessed little life", "context_len": 128}}}, "forgetfulness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 58, "context": "Justice , law , liberty were solid facts ; those were the watchwords General Harrison had taught him ; for those he had lived , to those he would be true , whether good or evil fortune awaited him , whether there were , indeed , a heavenly reward for the victor , or but the abyss of forgetfulness at the end of the strife", "context_len": 65}}}, "cogitation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Then she asked after a moment of serious cogitation , Do you suppose , Chris , that any of the children along your route open their fathers ' letters , when they are sure they 're from their cousins ?", "context_len": 40}}}, "gayly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "With the touch of a passing breeze , the maple trees of the yard waved their hands gayly to him , and in the distance beyond the football field Lake Woodrim dimpled and laughed in the golden sunshine", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Though the colonel spoke so gayly , she felt sure there had been something wrong , if there was not now", "context_len": 21}}}, "unread": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 76, "context": "But alas ! for the little cat-naps ; for the lights blinked brightly in the harbor , and the ferry-boats whistled and let off steam in deafening fashion , and the stars came out , and the moon came up , and papa and mamma came home , and chatted gayly besides , with the door wide open into her room , and yet Marie-Celeste never wakened , and Harold 's important letter lay sealed and unread , and as flat as a fluffy head could press it until the light of another morning", "context_len": 94}}}, "somebodies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "In this case there were three somebodies , and you can easily guess who ; for there was that in Harold 's letter that had made Mr", "context_len": 27}}}, "effusive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Harris were in it , and Marie-Celeste was taking effusive and affectionate leave of the maids , who were smiling and crying all in one , after the manner of an Irish parting", "context_len": 33}}}, "jogs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "And now even that is done with , and the carriage rolls off , and the wagon-load of steamer trunks and bags jogs after , and Mary and Bridget and Norah dry their eyes on their respective aprons , and go back to a general cleaning up today , and like as not to Coney Island to-morrow", "context_len": 57}}}, "contrasts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Indeed , she is altogether willing that they should , for if there is ever a time when the contrasts in life will not be overlooked it is when you are on your way to the steamer", "context_len": 37}}}, "infinite": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 89, "context": "Oh ! if only every one might have his turn in this world of ours ; but since that is out of the question , let there at least be as much Coney Island for housemaids as is consistent with good morals and faithful discharge of their duties ; at least so thought one dear little mistress , with more heart , perhaps , than discretion , but a heart , all the same , that won every one to her and made life in her household move with infinite smoothness", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "You 'll need this , said Chris , pushing a clothes-brush and a whisk-broom on to a chair , and you 'll find plenty of water on the stand yonder ; then he came out and closed the door , to the infinite and audible relief of the serving-maid Martha", "context_len": 50}}}, "ifs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Who has said a word until now about any ifs in connection with this trip of ours ? But no sooner are we actually off , scarce ten minutes from home , in fact , than the great , uncomfortable , intimidating creatures come trooping in from every quarter , and the particular one that comes to me is this , If you find you do n't like it when you get there , do n't forget where the blame lies", "context_len": 81}}}, "intimidating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Who has said a word until now about any ifs in connection with this trip of ours ? But no sooner are we actually off , scarce ten minutes from home , in fact , than the great , uncomfortable , intimidating creatures come trooping in from every quarter , and the particular one that comes to me is this , If you find you do n't like it when you get there , do n't forget where the blame lies", "context_len": 81}}}, "trooping": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "Who has said a word until now about any ifs in connection with this trip of ours ? But no sooner are we actually off , scarce ten minutes from home , in fact , than the great , uncomfortable , intimidating creatures come trooping in from every quarter , and the particular one that comes to me is this , If you find you do n't like it when you get there , do n't forget where the blame lies", "context_len": 81}}}, "overgrown": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "He had noticed a little earlier his big brother , Ira , a loutish , overgrown fellow who had gone around with his hands in his pockets sneering at the innocent fun the smaller boys were indulging in , and bragging about his own especial Fourth of July supply of fireworks which were to come from some mysterious source not clearly defined", "context_len": 62}, "VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "The mouth was overgrown with hazel-bushes and brambles and long , trailing twines of ivy , and it seemed to the children as if no foot but theirs had disturbed it for a long time", "context_len": 35}}}, "arrowroot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Marie-Celeste was up betimes the next morning -- that is , if betimes means bright and early , and , stopping for a few minutes on her way to indulge in a savory cup of arrowroot , which the stewardess had made ready for her , she passed on up the stairs and out on to the saloon deek , looking as fresh and sweet in her dress of sailor-blue as a fair little morning-glory", "context_len": 75}}}, "intents": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Out of sight of land had always stood with Marie-Celeste for such an awe-inspiring condition of affairs that she expected to feel all sorts of chilly and creepy feelings when she fairly faced the thought ; and yet here she stood , alone to all intents and purposes , and no land anywhere , and yet not so much as the suggestion of a chill or a creep", "context_len": 68}}}, "network": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "But a fire in a few hours will lick up every stick and leaf , except the large logs and stumps , burn up all the bushes , and the whole network of small roots that cover the ground , so that nothing will start for months , as it destroys all the seeds of the weeds and trees , of which the ground is full ; and if it is dry , and a thorough burn , will so roast the large stumps that very few of them will ever sprout again , -- while , as in Ben \u2019 s case , most of them are spruce , pine , or fir , that never throw up any sprouts from the roots", "context_len": 124}}}, "interlacing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was such a beautiful , stanch , great ship , with its large masts and spars and network of interlacing halyards , that its wideness meant more to her just then than even the wideness of the sea ; and she felt so safe and at home on it withal , that all the expected uncanny sensations had need to be postponed to some more favorable occasion", "context_len": 68}}}, "wideness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "But the steamer did not mind how any of her passengers were feeling -- she must make the best possible record , no matter who was glad or sorry ; and on she steamed , past lonely and beautiful Holyhead , and then through the wide Irish Sea ( that seems indeed a veritable ocean in its wideness ) , until land once more was sighted and the harbor reached , and the anchor dropped off the wonderful docks at Liverpool", "context_len": 81}}}, "uncanny": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 6, "context": "There was something so strange and uncanny about it -- for I by this time knew the ground well enough to be fully aware that there ought not to be any moving thing there -- that I stopped playing and sprang to my feet so suddenly that my movement appeared to startle Ama , who uttered a little cry of alarm , or surprise , and made as though she too would spring to her feet", "context_len": 76}}}, "reefer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "The steamer was pitching a good deal -- pitching horribly , some of the passengers below would have told you , but all the more fun for Marie-Celeste ; and plunging her hand deep in her reefer pocket , she set off at a swinging gait", "context_len": 46}}}, "ladyship": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 61, "context": "Now it was all up-hill , and the wind blowing such a gale that she had need to bend way over , holding firmly to her sailor hat the while , to make any headway whatever ; and now in a trice it was very much down-hill indeed , and the little knees had to be stiffly braced to prevent her ladyship from bowling along at a dangerously rapid pace", "context_len": 70}}}, "grants": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Just as she was nearing the break in the saloon deck that grants an open sky space to the steerage , she discovered some one coming toward her on the deck of the second-class cabin -- some one who looked familiar , notwithstanding the absence of gray coat and brass buttons", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "From their uncle , Cissa , they obtained grants of land , whereon they founded monastic homes", "context_len": 17}}}, "heartiest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "But of course there was only the merest little suggestion of a sympathetic thought to spend on the poor , white lady , with Chris Hartley but just discovered , and after that one instant of transfixed surprise she sped toward him , both hands extended ; and over the gate that divides the first from the second cabin they indulged in the heartiest shaking of hands possible , while hats for the moment were expected to look out for themselves", "context_len": 81}}}, "foresaw": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "Do you mind , Dick , '' she went on , in a calmer tone , as her memory wandered back to happier days , `` do you mind how I foresaw these evil times were at hand , and how I entreated him to flee ? Do you mind , last spring , when that letter came from New England from excellent Master Perrient , how I prayed him to hearken to it ? ''", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "It is a monument , too , that time will not destroy ; its position and character , as Alexander foresaw , by bringing it a continued renovation , secure its perpetuity", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Famine had produced lack of discipline on the part of some of the troops ; and Gordon foresaw well what the end must be , though without a fear for himself", "context_len": 31}}}, "buglers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Meantime , a number of passengers had come on to the decks of both cabins , and a few moments later the little buglers appeared simultaneously on both sides of the saloon , and the call for breakfast rang out on the still sea air", "context_len": 45}}}, "stew": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` And we 'll have roast wild turkey , rabbit stew , partridge potpie and bear steak , also some venison , if Bart has any luck , '' went on Fenn", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I \u2019 ll fetch y \u2019 u a warm supper by and by , for I bought a piece of lamb to stew a-purpose , an \u2019 Sadie is tendin \u2019 it", "context_len": 32}}}, "thoughtlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "It might well happen that the father and mother , who were quite willing that their little daughter should have an occasional chat with the postman at home , would prefer not to recognize him in the role of a second-cabin passenger ; and good Chris Hartley felt inclined to call himself all manner of names for thoughtlessly allowing himself to be put in such a position", "context_len": 67}}}, "doleful": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 34, "context": "The water relieved the smart considerably , and Peggy , happily remembering she had a parcel of biscuits in her pockets , pulled them out and suggested some lunch , for Bobby was looking doleful and injured , and inclined to cast aspersions upon her knowledge of bee-keeping", "context_len": 48}}}, "enforcement": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It is of course nothing but the enforcement of this very rule that renders ocean travel the safe and comfortable thing it is , and that assures you , even in case of accident , that the strictest discipline will be preserved", "context_len": 42}}}, "assures": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "It is of course nothing but the enforcement of this very rule that renders ocean travel the safe and comfortable thing it is , and that assures you , even in case of accident , that the strictest discipline will be preserved", "context_len": 42}}}, "inclines": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Indeed , I have an idea that Captain Revell inclines to apply the same rule to every one aboard of his great steamer , to passengers as well as to officers and crew , and so perhaps regarded the advent of Marie-Celeste in the light of an intrusion", "context_len": 48}}}, "interminable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "The sandals were anything but comfortable , and her feet hurt dreadfully on the stones , while every gorse-bush she passed seemed to be stretching out spiky fingers to scratch her bare legs ; she was tired after her morning 's adventures on the moors , and the eight miles seemed to lengthen out to an interminable vista , in spite of the way being downhill ; sundry bumps and bruises , which she had never noticed at the time , began to ache now , and the bee-stings on her neck smarted , until she hardly knew how to bear the pain", "context_len": 103}}}, "atop": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Albert , who had already become familiar with the appointments of this apartment , stepped at once to the table , near which an elderly lady was sitting , and laying his sailor-hat , nothing loath , atop of a miniature of His Royal Highness the Prince of Wales , announced cavalierly , And -- and now , this is my little American friend , Marie-Celeste", "context_len": 66}}}, "excepting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 8, "context": "With hardly anyone to see him off , excepting a couple of Maori lads who were employed as messengers , Malcolm , accompanied by Te Paheka , set off on the momentous journey that was to end -- where ? Perhaps in France , perhaps on the high seas", "context_len": 49}}}, "woful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "But at this the se\u00f1ora 's face , which had exhibited a kind of woful pleasure in the excitement of the occasion , took on an anxious frown", "context_len": 28}}}, "intruding": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I hope I am not intruding and that you will please to be so kind as to excuse me for troubling you , Mr", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "It was the slithering of a snake through the underbrush , but a snake that , disturbed , was gliding away from the intruding boy", "context_len": 25}}}, "fastidiously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "But I think -- I think ( for whatever her faults Marie-Celeste was fastidiously honest ) the captain himself did not quite like it when I first spoke to him", "context_len": 30}}}, "decisions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "To call this loose assemblage of towns and tribes a military democracy , in the sense that the majority of the votes decided a question brought before the people in a manner that was binding for the citizens , is entirely wrong and misleading , for Indians regard their actions subject to their own decisions only , or , at the utmost , to those of their individual gens", "context_len": 69}}}, "projects": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 63, "context": "In what strange world had he been living ? Had that sullen , desperate wretch been indeed Dick Harrison ? He realized that he was alive ; he could still enjoy the common comforts of food and fire ; he could think ; he could plan ! His feet were once more treading solid earth ; his brain began to spin anew the projects that had delighted him of yore ; his heart began to stir with the hopes of old", "context_len": 81}}}, "resolves": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "It would help rather than hinder that little woman 's numerous projects should she learn to think twice before putting her quick resolves into action", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- He resolves to attack it", "context_len": 6}}}, "presumably": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "With rifles at the slope , the New Zealanders sauntered forward towards their objective , keeping almost at the heels of the barrage , save here and there where a `` pill-box '' , presumably deserted , was found to be chock-a-block with Huns", "context_len": 44}}}, "inference": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "Since the news of the escape of a numerous body of prisoners from D\u00fcren must have been sent far and wide , the inference that the barge had been a hiding-place for some of their number was obvious", "context_len": 38}}}, "appreciating": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 56, "context": "In every room Donald brings his own peculiar methods of investigation to bear , not in the least minding a good deal of mirthful laughter at his expense on the part of Harold and Marie-Celeste ; and Albert , feeling privileged to join in the general merriment , though evidently half the time without in anywise appreciating the situation , only helps on the jollity of things", "context_len": 67}}}, "narration": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 46, "context": "Will you tell it to us , Marie-Celeste ? And Marie-Celeste , nothing loath , and willing at last to substantiate her claims in the ears of doubting Albert , rested a hand comfortably on either arm of her chair , and commenced , preceding her narration with the request , You will correct me , wo n't you , if you find I do not tell it right ? to which Her Majesty smilingly acceded , first asking Miss Belmore to hand her a little jewelled miniature case from among the other treasures on the table", "context_len": 97}}}, "graphically": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Now I 'll tell you about the mud volcano , '' which he did , graphically , so that his chums were eager to go and see it", "context_len": 28}}}, "realization": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was the realization of this kinship of experience and yet of the widely different effect upon soul and character that had impelled him to take his sudden leave of Marie-Celeste , and then , pausing a moment at the smoking-room door , he went on and down to his state-room , for he had much to think over , and a long , long time he sat there , his elbows resting on his knees and his face buried in his hands", "context_len": 83}}}, "faultlessly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Born with a positive genius for spending and being spent , the claims and opportunities of ordinary child friendships seemed hardly to give her enough breathing room ; and so it chanced that she passed very little time with the faultlessly dressed and somewhat overcared-for children of the steamer , who did not seem to need her , and a great deal of time with Chris and Mr", "context_len": 68}}}, "implicit": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 55, "context": "Nothing was farther from Timothy 's thoughts than to make any disturbance whatever -- why should he ? Were they not the best of friends , he and that blessed little Albert ? so he never raised his head from where it rested upon his outstretched paws , only looked up with that gaze of implicit confidence peculiar to the kind eyes of the Laverick setter , and which made Albert lose not a second in spreading his little coat out beneath him , throwing his two arms around Timothy 's neck , and pillowing his head on his beautiful silky coat", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Others suppose that the act was an expression of his implicit faith in the integrity and fidelity of his servant , and that he intended it as testimony , given in a very pointed and decisive , and , at the same time , delicate manner , that he was not suspicious of his friends , or easily led to distrust their faithfulness", "context_len": 63}}}, "inerrancy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "More than once she had shown in her contact with people an inerrancy of intuition ( if my youngest readers will excuse two such big words ) that had greatly gratified her father and mother , who had a theory of their own about the education of children , and gave her rather more rein than some would consider either safe or advisable", "context_len": 63}}}, "paternal": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 22, "context": "At the same time , every movement of the little daughter was carefully watched and every project followed up by a certain paternal relative , and never more so than during those days of steamer life , when so many hours were passed with the new friend and the postman", "context_len": 50}}}, "tucking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then Chris , after carefully tucking her in , in most approved fashion , would be off , with a touch of his hat , and with profound gratitude in his heart for the strength of limb and muscle that made him regard Mr", "context_len": 44}}}, "summarily": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 64, "context": "so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate", "context_len": 128}}}, "bugler": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "A little sick bugler was stowed away in an undesirable second-cabin state-room that had remained unengaged ; and Chris , noticing that a bowl of broth or some sort of nourishing food was carried thither three times a day , but that apart from this no one ever entered or left the state-room , questioned the steward , and as soon as he learned the facts , made his own way in , to the great delight of the lonely little fellow", "context_len": 82}}}, "unengaged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "A little sick bugler was stowed away in an undesirable second-cabin state-room that had remained unengaged ; and Chris , noticing that a bowl of broth or some sort of nourishing food was carried thither three times a day , but that apart from this no one ever entered or left the state-room , questioned the steward , and as soon as he learned the facts , made his own way in , to the great delight of the lonely little fellow", "context_len": 82}}}, "mussed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` Oh ! yes , ma'am , '' said Bessie , `` it suits me very much , but you know sometimes children make a little disorder when they play , and I only meant would you mind if we mussed up your nice house just a very little bit ? ''", "context_len": 52}}}, "whittling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "Would you like me to come and straighten you up a bit ? called Chris , who had really established himself as Donald 's nurse , and sat whittling in his own state-room just across the passage", "context_len": 37}}}, "blanched": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "If the horses had n't succeeded in breaking away from the trap the moment that it went over , I should have been killed surely , for it fell on top of me in some way , and as it was , I could scarcely get from under it ; and the young fellow 's blanched face grew a shade whiter as he realized how narrow had been his escape", "context_len": 70}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "They beheld Alfgar in his night dress , all bloody , holding a dagger in his hand , and with his face blanched to a death-like paleness , uttering cry upon cry", "context_len": 32}}}, "eavesdropper": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was more what we call an asylum in America , explained Chris , who , as a permitted eavesdropper , felt at liberty to join in the conversation on occasion", "context_len": 31}}}, "nines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They keep things scrubbed up to the nines , and everything 's as neat and well ordered as a ship", "context_len": 20}}}, "mitts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "They dress the girls up awful pretty in black dresses with short sleeves , and mitts that come way up over the elbow , like ladies ' gloves at a party , and caps and kerchiefs folded crosswise round their shoulders , like this", "context_len": 44}}}, "kerchiefs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "They dress the girls up awful pretty in black dresses with short sleeves , and mitts that come way up over the elbow , like ladies ' gloves at a party , and caps and kerchiefs folded crosswise round their shoulders , like this", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "It just goes slanting across the benches , and then the girls it happens to strike , no matter how homely they are , really look just beautiful , with their white caps and kerchiefs all lighted up in the sunshine", "context_len": 41}}}, "choir": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 127, "context": "There was the much-longed-for guinea-pig from Father , which had been smuggled up in a hamper , and kept with elaborate care in a remote portion of the barn ( a secret which Bobby found the utmost difficulty in preserving ) ; a nightdress case worked by Lilian 's neat fingers ; a cork frame from Bobby , made under Archie 's supervision ; a round pink-and-white wool mat , which Nancy had crocheted at odd times during the evenings ; and a little mug from Joe , with a Welshwoman in a tall hat on one side , and ' A present from Llandrindod ' on the other , bought on a visit to the famous wells which he had paid in company with the church choir", "context_len": 128}}}, "foundling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Getting your start in a foundling hospital is sort of like being led into the world blindfold", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "' I 've always thought I should like to be a foundling , ' said Peggy", "context_len": 16}}}, "blindfold": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "If I choose I can for this was a very queer proceeding , and he was not going to be led blindfold", "context_len": 22}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Sometimes we have to blindfold them and put chains on them to get them on board , for they always think they are going to be made to walk the plank", "context_len": 31}}}, "retirement": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 72, "context": "Nor was Nancy the only one who indulged in a little innocent vanity , for Joe , too , had been so fastidious in his choice of a red-and-blue spotted necktie and a walking-stick with an ivory handle that the children began to suspect the blacksmith 's rosy-cheeked daughter must be at the bottom of it ; and even David had taken a long-tailed coat and a beaver hat out of the retirement of some mysterious bandboxes and had been quite snappy and particular with Mrs", "context_len": 86}}}, "tantrums": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Lady Jane 's daughter , bless you ! Why , I live there as kitchen-maid , and I tell you the tantrums of that young lady is enough to upset the nerves of the stoutest person", "context_len": 37}}}, "diphtheria": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 30, "context": "Somehow or other I was rather spoiled , you see ; but when I was only five years old Louis and Jack died , both in one week , of diphtheria , and mamma says from that week I have never given her any real trouble", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "How she had got it nobody knew ; but diphtheria Jane had , and of the most malignant type", "context_len": 19}}}, "convalescent": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "I wandered about the camp and through the town with a convalescent 's certificate in my pocket and the desperation of a lover in my heart ; and at the very last , when night was falling , it was Jasper Goodrum , of the Independents , who gave me the news I had been looking for all day", "context_len": 59}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Harris , which caught the driving party at Windemere , asking if he would approve of Donald 's going up to London with a convalescent gentleman who greatly needed his services", "context_len": 31}}}, "sorriest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 66, "context": "The Lady Bedrulbudour raised her head and said to her , `` Blame me not , O my mother ; indeed , it behoved me receive thee with all reverence and worship , since thou honourest me by coming to me ; but I beseech thee hear the cause of this my case and see how this night I have passed hath been for me the sorriest of nights", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Whereupon , `` O my father the Vizier , '' replied the youth , `` [ God ] preserve the Lady Bedrulbudour from leasing ! [ 410 ] Indeed , all she saith is true and these two nights past have been for us the sorriest of nights , instead of being nights of pleasance and delight", "context_len": 57}}}, "loveless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He was going right up to his club in London , to lead the same old loveless , self-centred life , and somehow the glimpse of something very different he had had through Marie-Celeste made it appear more vapid and colorless than ever", "context_len": 43}}}, "vapid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "He was going right up to his club in London , to lead the same old loveless , self-centred life , and somehow the glimpse of something very different he had had through Marie-Celeste made it appear more vapid and colorless than ever", "context_len": 43}}}, "colorless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "He was going right up to his club in London , to lead the same old loveless , self-centred life , and somehow the glimpse of something very different he had had through Marie-Celeste made it appear more vapid and colorless than ever", "context_len": 43}}}, "slaving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Early and late they were kept slaving away , with never a spark of consideration shown them , and nothing but the love they had borne their mistress and an occasional kind word from Harold , proving how he felt in the matter , had carried them through it", "context_len": 49}}}, "afghan": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Just a week to-day , Harold , said Aunt Lou , looking up from a great mass of crocheting , that would soon be a full-grown afghan ; I hope it has n't seemed more like a month to you , dear", "context_len": 42}}}, "thoughtfulness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "Harris had said to her more than once , that there 's nothing but sin itself has so many heavy hearts to answer for as thoughtlessness ; and thoughtfulness , next to love , has lightened and brightened more hearts than anything else in the world and Marie-Celeste knew how thoughtless she had been to press home upon Harold in any way a keener sense of his own great loss", "context_len": 70}}}, "revelling": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "Peggy and Bobby were out the whole day long , following their father about the farm , riding on the slow plough-horses , helping to drive the sheep , or bringing home the cows from the pasture , sowing seeds in their little gardens , and generally revelling in the delicious freedom", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Belle entered Pauline 's room to find her cousin revelling in the exquisite pathos of Whittier 's Snowbound before dressing for dinner", "context_len": 22}}}, "commencing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "You know , she said , commencing in a low tone , so as not to disturb the others , and with the introductory long breath of the conventional story-teller , we have been through the castle three times , so I really know a great deal about it , and it is very fortunate that the Queen happened to be in London , or we should n't have seen some of the rooms at all", "context_len": 76}}}, "introductory": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "You know , she said , commencing in a low tone , so as not to disturb the others , and with the introductory long breath of the conventional story-teller , we have been through the castle three times , so I really know a great deal about it , and it is very fortunate that the Queen happened to be in London , or we should n't have seen some of the rooms at all", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Since this chapter is introductory and intended merely to clear the ground for what follows , I shall close it with an account of the most wonderful ride in the history of the West", "context_len": 34}}}, "patronizing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "I was just going to ask you if you knew , with an extremely patronizing air , which Donald noticed , but was quite too courteous to resent", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "And nothing could , by any possibility , have been more patronizing than the tone in which Albert uttered the words my little friend", "context_len": 24}}}, "mausoleum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "No ; I was going to tell you he is buried in a mausoleum ( very proud of the word ) at Frogmore , just beyond the Long Walk , as they call it , where we drove you , you remember , day before yesterday", "context_len": 46}}}, "matchless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "And older people than Donald have thought the selfsame thing , as they have looked down the same matchless avenue , with its wonderful far-reaching vista of branching elms , and its perfect driveway diminishing to a thread in the distance , with here and there a flock of grazing sheep roaming its ample grass-grown borders , and finding rich and abundant pasture", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "'By this good light , a lass of matchless mettle ! '", "context_len": 12}}}, "elms": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "And older people than Donald have thought the selfsame thing , as they have looked down the same matchless avenue , with its wonderful far-reaching vista of branching elms , and its perfect driveway diminishing to a thread in the distance , with here and there a flock of grazing sheep roaming its ample grass-grown borders , and finding rich and abundant pasture", "context_len": 63}}}, "driveway": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "And older people than Donald have thought the selfsame thing , as they have looked down the same matchless avenue , with its wonderful far-reaching vista of branching elms , and its perfect driveway diminishing to a thread in the distance , with here and there a flock of grazing sheep roaming its ample grass-grown borders , and finding rich and abundant pasture", "context_len": 63}}}, "chancel": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 100, "context": "The children could hear most of what he said , and a very interesting account of the Abbey it proved to be , with a description of the size and extent of the old buildings , and the life led by the ancient monks , which quite delighted Peggy , who revelled in Scott 's novels and historical stories , and which made the past days rise up so clearly before her that she could almost fancy the bell ringing for vespers , and hear the rustle of the gray robes of the friars as they passed silently up the chancel to their stalls in the choir", "context_len": 107}}}, "armor": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 17, "context": "Then there 's a room they call the Guard Room , where they have suits of ancient armor ; and there 's a great oak writing-table in it made from the wood of the old Arctic ship Resolute ; and it tells in an inscription on it how she was abandoned by the English , and how she was found by an American whaling-ship captain three years afterwards , who got her free from the ice", "context_len": 76}}}, "architect": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "and as Donald looked as though he thought he might , and as that was exactly the way he was expected to look , Marie-Celeste complacently continued : Well , first you go up a flight of steps , and you find yourself in a sort of vestibule ; and there 's a splendid portrait of the architect there -- the man who restored the old parts of the castle and added new parts to it and made it all beautiful as it is now ; and from this vestibule you go on and on from one grand room to another", "context_len": 101}}}, "colossal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Then there 's a chair besides , that 's made from an elm-tree that grew where the English beat Napoleon on the field of Waterloo ; and in another part of the room , on a piece of a mast , there 's a great colossal bust of Lord Nelson ; and I 'm ashamed to say I do n't know anything about him , but we ought to , Donald", "context_len": 71}}}, "garter": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 59, "context": "He had not meant that any one should know it , such a modest little knight was he ; but alas ! the weakness of drowsiness had overtaken the valiant little fellow , and in the disorder thereon attendant the shapely little limb had thrust itself forth from the folds of the protecting kilt , and there was the garter plainly visible to the most casual passer-by", "context_len": 67}}}, "booted": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 30, "context": "To every new Knight made the Queen gives a dark blue velvet garter , and what 's more , they are never to appear in public without them , unless booted for riding , and then they are allowed to wear a ribbon of blue silk under their left boot instead", "context_len": 51}}}, "shields": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 50, "context": "As we drew near to the exceedingly narrow gate in the palisade , which had been thrown open to admit us -- and which , I presently saw , was strongly guarded by a number of warriors armed with heavy , broad-bladed spears , murderous-looking swords , and small round shields , or targets , of wood covered with what looked like crocodile hide -- I became sensible of a horrible charnel-house smell ; but it was not until we had passed through the gate , and were inside the palisaded enclosure , that I discovered from whence it emanated", "context_len": 100}}}, "panels": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 94, "context": "'s evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and sent such cold shivers running down their backs that they grew almost too frightened to go to bed", "context_len": 128}}}, "expressively": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Well , Oueen or no , I 'm sure I should n't like to have everything so tearing grand , said Donald , more expressively than elegantly", "context_len": 27}}}, "elegantly": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 6, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}}}, "homelike": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 56, "context": "You can only see her very own rooms from the outside , and you can only imagine what they are like ; but they point out which is the drawing-room and which is her sitting-room , and they do n't call them grand anything , for a comfort , so I suppose they 're lovely and homelike , like other people 's ; but they do look out on a grand garden -- the East Terrace they call it", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "That day his cold lunch tasted sweet indeed , and his little bare room looked strangely attractive and homelike", "context_len": 19}}}, "fountains": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "And such a garden as it proved ! with its fountains and statues and vases , and the orangery on one side , and on the other three sides a beautiful sloping lawn , ascending from the level of the garden to the gray stonewall at the outer edge of the terrace ; and to think that here they were actually walking about in this beautiful garden , instead of merely peering through the fretwork of the iron gate , as some other little children with envious eyes were doing that very moment", "context_len": 93}}}, "regal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 104, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The Sultan was amazed when he saw Alaeddin in this regal array and beheld his grace and goodliness and the mamelukes standing in attendance upon him in all their comeliness and fair favour ; yea , and his wonderment redoubled when Alaeddin 's mother came up attired in rich and costly raiment , as she were a queen , and he saw twelve slave-girls in her service , preceding her , their hands clasped behind their backs , with all worship and observance", "context_len": 83}}}, "tenants": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 63, "context": "But that hero suggesting that it must be after teatime , she cordially agreed with him , and they began to retrace their steps to the entrance , feeling just a little disappointed , for somehow they had imagined a smugglers ' cave would be a jolly , dry sort of a place , with at least a few remains of its former tenants strewn about -- a pistol or two , perhaps , or a coil of rope , or a rusty dagger , just sufficient to give an air of romance to the adventure , even if the missing treasure were not forthcoming", "context_len": 105}}}, "discriminating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "But , in carrying this terrible decision into effect , he acted in a manner so deliberate , discriminating , and cautious , as to diminish very much the irritation and resentment which it would otherwise have caused , and to give it its full moral effect as a measure , not of angry resentment , but of calm and deliberate retribution -- just and proper , according to the ideas of the time", "context_len": 74}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}}}, "valance": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "Poor Rosamund 's terror can be better imagined than described , for the wicked Irene had lifted the valance of the bed , and her bright eyes and a tiny portion of her face could be distinctly seen by any one who happened to glance in that direction", "context_len": 48}}}, "upholstered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "At her suggestion , too , the couch and chairs were upholstered with a pretty flower-patterned cretonne , and some lovely white-framed etchings were hung upon the tinted walls", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It had an upholstered seat and back , and the high arms were made more comfortable by a covering of the same material", "context_len": 23}}}, "etchings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "At her suggestion , too , the couch and chairs were upholstered with a pretty flower-patterned cretonne , and some lovely white-framed etchings were hung upon the tinted walls", "context_len": 29}}}, "captivate": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "Then , by grace of his own idea of fitness , Harold had added to the other furnishings a Dresden china toilet-set , and in this he was perhaps far wiser than he knew , for is there anything so well calculated to captivate at sight the heart of a dainty little maiden as the mysterious round-topped boxes that compose the dainty outfit of the ideal dressing-table ? Then , to crown it all , a pair of ponies and a basket-phaeton had been purchased for the exclusive use of the guests that were to be", "context_len": 96}}}, "stowing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "As I said , then , who would not have taken kindly to the life at Windsor under such conditions , and have lost no time in stowing themselves happily away in the special niche prepared for them ? So Mr", "context_len": 41}}}, "abloom": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "The sun had shone as brilliantly , and the gray round tower , with its grass-grown terraces , had stood out as clearly against the blue of the English sky , but never before -- for Marie-Celeste , that is -- had those terraces been abloom with great masses of lilacs", "context_len": 51}}}, "stutter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Good-morning , Albert , answered Marie-Celeste , smiling at the expected , and now , with which , by way of getting the best of a tendency to stutter , Albert was accustomed to preface many of his remarks ; 1 thought I should find you here , she added ; and have you seen the lilacs , Albert ?", "context_len": 60}}}, "embodiment": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "The proper sense of the Creek name is `` the one who carries , takes the life or breath for them ; '' it is the embodiment of the idea that a great , powerful spirit gives life , or what is synonymous with it , breath to them ( to persons , animals ) , and takes it off from them at will ( is\u00e1kita life , breath ; im-pron", "context_len": 71}}}, "percale": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "He was always the embodiment of dainty freshness , this little four-year-old Albert , and thanks to his mother 's preference , boyish percale dresses still kept the Lilliputian trousers of the period at bay", "context_len": 35}}}, "observations": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "His observations , however , were so exceedingly cursory and so impartially comprehensive , including the path at his feet every whit as carefully as the sky above him , that Marie-Celeste had difficulty in preserving proper decorum", "context_len": 38}}}, "tourists": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Meantime , a party of tourists , with Marshall 's familiar pink guide-hook open in the hands of one of them , had been surveying the chapel at a distance , and now , after a word or two with the children on the doorstep , made their way within", "context_len": 50}}}, "verier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Yes , sighed Albert ; for he knew that his answer meant an end to their chat ; for whenever during these visits of hers a party of tourists were so fortunate as to secure the services of the verier , Mr", "context_len": 42}}}, "cenotaph": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "In their tour of the chapel the travellers from across the water had but reached the wonderful cenotaph of the Princess Charlotte , when a sweet single chord from the great organ broke upon the air , as though the player simply wanted to make sure that the instrument would respond when the time came", "context_len": 55}}}, "tome": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 43, "context": "And now , said Albert , appealing to the Queen , for he felt that quite too much was being taken for granted , will you please tell me what is a tilt-yard ? and what it was dat de knignts would not tome to ? and what was dat little white ting of the Queen 's dat de King carried ? and impatiently as Marie-Celeste brooked the interruption , there was nothing for it but to wait while Her Majesty explained that the tilt-yard was a sort of riding-school for the knights , where they practised for the tournaments , and that the tournaments were occasions when the knights , spear in hand , came together to ride against each other , with a great many people", "context_len": 128}}}, "dey": {"freq": 41, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 31, "context": "When de kitchen-fire am blazin ' , an ' Aunt Dinah stews an ' bakes , All de piccaninnies gather just to smell her pies an ' cakes ; An ' dey cluster round de window like de bees upon a comb , Till Auntie Dinah she get mad , an ' turn an ' drive 'em home ! Shoo , shoo ! piccaninnies , etc", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "The door was open , an ' I jus ' walked in , an ' I dess dey sought I was jus ' a little prince or somethin ' , cause nobody said anythin ' to me till I tame to the room where de Queen 's mother was ; an ' I asked her would n't she tome out in de garden an ' see you ; an ' she said no , she did not feel able to walk very much , but for me to go an ' bring my little friend in", "context_len": 96}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` Marse Cally Shannon , '' said Whistling Jim , the negro , addressing me , `` what you reckon make dem white folks bang aloose at we-all , when we ai n't done a blessed thing ? When it come ter dat , we ai n't ez much ez speaken ter um , an ' here dey come , bangin ' aloose at us", "context_len": 65}}}, "choirmaster": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "For over an hour , with scarce an interruption , Miss Allyn played on and on , and Marie-Celeste never stirred from the choirmaster 's chair , in which she sat absorbed and entranced", "context_len": 34}}}, "aden": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 69, "context": "The king was Charles the Sixth of France , explained the Queen , who was not going to have her little story-teller disconcerted if she could help it ; but Marie-Celeste confessed with perfect honesty , I am afraid I had forgotten that name ; and Albert felt ashamed of himself , and confided in a whisper to Miss Belmore dat he dessed he would n't be so mean aden", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Nes , said Albert complacently ; we must do aden", "context_len": 10}}}, "improvisation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "Bedin aden ; it is n't fair ; bedin aden , Dorothy , please , he urged with all the breath hurrying and excitement had left him ; and Dorothy , at sight of his anxious , entreating face , resolved to begin again , first bringing the interrupted measure to a close with a brief concluding improvisation of her own", "context_len": 61}}}, "brooked": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": ", said Albert , appealing to the Queen , for he felt that quite too much was being taken for granted , will you please tell me what is a tilt-yard ? and what it was dat de knignts would not tome to ? and what was dat little white ting of the Queen 's dat de King carried ? and impatiently as Marie-Celeste brooked the interruption , there was nothing for it but to wait while Her Majesty explained that the tilt-yard was a sort of riding-school for the knights , where they practised for the tournaments , and that the tournaments were occasions when the knights , spear in hand , came together to ride against each other , with a great many people looking on", "context_len": 128}}}, "musician": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "Belden told me on the steamer , said Marie-Celeste , with the air of one who settles down for a good talk with a familiar friend , of some musician who heard some one strike two or three chords and then suddenly stop , and after that he ; could not get a wink of sleep till he jumped out of bed and rushed to his piano and struck the chord that belonged at the end of the others", "context_len": 79}}}, "roseate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "De roseate hoos of early dawn , hummed Albert in a cunning , to-himself sort of way ,", "context_len": 18}}}, "delectation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "and then the same verse through again and still again , as Dorothy was good enough to repeat the brief , sweet strain for his special delectation", "context_len": 27}}}, "effectively": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 22, "context": "Tricked by a crisscross in the second scrimmage , Oakdale permitted Oakes to get round the right end , Spotty Davis being effectively and easily blocked by Stoker , while Crane let Butters through , and the left tackle of the locals flung himself before Hayden , preventing a tackle", "context_len": 50}}}, "chords": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Belden told me on the steamer , said Marie-Celeste , with the air of one who settles down for a good talk with a familiar friend , of some musician who heard some one strike two or three chords and then suddenly stop , and after that he ; could not get a wink of sleep till he jumped out of bed and rushed to his piano and struck the chord that belonged at the end of the others", "context_len": 79}}}, "accidentally": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 71, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "enlarging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "And another queer thing about him -- for there was real pleasure in enlarging on a subject in which her listener took such undisguised interest -- was that he told me one day that he had too much money", "context_len": 39}}}, "aggressively": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 50, "context": "Finally he resolved to take matters into his own hands by suddenly slipping away from Marie-Celeste 's grasp ; and then drawing off a little , and folding both hands behind his back , as though neither of them were to be longer at anybody 's disposal , he said aggressively : And -- and now what are you afraid of , Marie-Celeste ? Do you sink somebody 's goin ' to soot you from de top of one of de towers if you speak out loud ?", "context_len": 88}}}, "barrack": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "out on the door-step , singing the beautiful words loud and clear , so that dear little Leonard would hear ; and then the hand pulling down the curtain at the barrack master 's window , so that the V", "context_len": 40}}}, "aglow": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Yes , she said seriously ; I ca n't find any words for it all -- her face fairly aglow with enthusiasm as she spoke -- everything is so perfectly lovely : the views , and the towers , and the castle itself , and the chapels , and the wonderful Long Walk , so that it seems as though I was just dreaming it all , even to the little room Harold has fitted up so beautifully for me", "context_len": 80}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "She turned to me with her lovely eyes all aglow with joy , and said : --", "context_len": 17}}}, "chapels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "Yes , she said seriously ; I ca n't find any words for it all -- her face fairly aglow with enthusiasm as she spoke -- everything is so perfectly lovely : the views , and the towers , and the castle itself , and the chapels , and the wonderful Long Walk , so that it seems as though I was just dreaming it all , even to the little room Harold has fitted up so beautifully for me", "context_len": 80}}}, "intelligible": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "It must be remembered , that both speak different languages intelligible to each other only in a limited number of words", "context_len": 21}}}, "makings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "No , I 'm afraid not ; but they would if he would let them , I 'm sure , for he had the makings of a splendid fellow in him", "context_len": 31}}}, "tom": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Why does n't he tom to see us now , Dorothy ? Albert questioned", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "Though I was then happily unaware of the fact , the period of the great annual festival , or Customs , was approaching , and the joy of the populace began to find vent in nocturnal concerts inordinately prolonged , the musical instruments consisting of tom-toms , each beaten by two , three , or four performers -- according to the size of the tom- tom -- with a monotony of cadence that soon became positively maddening , further aggravated by the discordant squealing of a number of flageolet- like instruments made of stout reeds", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Ef it war Jeth that asked that tom fool question I shouldn \u2019 t be \u2019 sprised , but I didn \u2019 t look for anything like it from yerself , younker", "context_len": 32}}}, "shortcomings": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "Whatever their faults and shortcomings -- and some of these had been grave enough -- such an idea , such an implication as this had never before presented itself to them -- that there was a thief in their midst , that one of their number had been guilty of flagrant dishonesty , of an absolute theft , and that of a large sum", "context_len": 64}}}, "unceremonious": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "It certainly would seem a very unceremonious proceeding to escort a little party across the great , wide sea , and then follow the fortunes of some of the group , to the utter exclusion of others ; so if you please we will just take a look right away at the snug little English cottage to which Chris Hartley hurried the same April morning that he reluctantly took leave of Marie-Celeste at the steamer", "context_len": 75}}}, "exclusion": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "It certainly would seem a very unceremonious proceeding to escort a little party across the great , wide sea , and then follow the fortunes of some of the group , to the utter exclusion of others ; so if you please we will just take a look right away at the snug little English cottage to which Chris Hartley hurried the same April morning that he reluctantly took leave of Marie-Celeste at the steamer", "context_len": 75}}}, "hawthorn": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "Beyond the hedge lies a pasture meadow , where a flock of sheep are grazing , and encircling the meadow another hedge , less closely clipped , and so making bold to riot here and there in a snowy wealth of hawthorn blossom , A fine Alderney cow , with coat as well cared for as the gray mare 's in the stable , is also enjoying the sweet grass of the meadow , and the shining milk , pans ranged beneath the kitchen window bear witness to the generous service she renders", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 61, "context": "But wherever their thoughts may have been , they instantly both stopped thinking , for first they heard the familiar bugle-call of the steamer ring out on the air in the clearest sort of a whistle ; and then -- could they believe their eyes ? -- there stood Marie-Celeste and Harold right before them on the other side of the hawthorn", "context_len": 62}}}, "encloses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "In front is an even hawthorn hedge , that reaches round to the back as well , and encloses a quaint little kitchen garden", "context_len": 24}}}, "encircling": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "Beyond the hedge lies a pasture meadow , where a flock of sheep are grazing , and encircling the meadow another hedge , less closely clipped , and so making bold to riot here and there in a snowy wealth of hawthorn blossom , A fine Alderney cow , with coat as well cared for as the gray mare 's in the stable , is also enjoying the sweet grass of the meadow , and the shining milk , pans ranged beneath the kitchen window bear witness to the generous service she renders", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Bullets whistling past their ears told them the unpleasant news that the Huns were developing an encircling movement", "context_len": 18}}}, "clipped": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 102, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}}}, "hedgerows": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 17, "context": "And when they reached the cottage , or rather the little lane that runs down between the hedgerows , who did they see at once but Chris himself , busy at work in the garden , and Donald , hoe in hand , close beside him , cutting vigorously at the weeds round some hop-vines , and both working away with such a will and such a farmer-like air that it looked as though both had mistaken their calling", "context_len": 79}}}, "drags": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "There were always more or less college men among them , driving in stylish drags behind spirited horses or in shabby livery turn-outs , according to their station in life , or rather the condition of their pocket-books", "context_len": 38}}}, "mindful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Be as still as possible , please , he said in a loud whisper , mindful of the old people ; I will be down in a moment , and instantly recalling the party he had seen drive past to Nuneham , there seemed no need to ask who they were or what had happened", "context_len": 55}}}, "expeditious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "But expeditious as Chris had been , Mrs", "context_len": 8}}}, "frilled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hartley , in gray wrapper and frilled night-cap , was at the door before him", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "She made quite a picture in her short linsey-woolsey petticoat , with the check apron , her plaid shawl crossed over her cotton bodice , and the frilled white cap framing the kind old face , with its apple cheeks and soft white hair", "context_len": 44}}}, "whiter": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 60, "context": "If the horses had n't succeeded in breaking away from the trap the moment that it went over , I should have been killed surely , for it fell on top of me in some way , and as it was , I could scarcely get from under it ; and the young fellow 's blanched face grew a shade whiter as he realized how narrow had been his escape", "context_len": 70}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "He looked more ill when his eyes were shut than when they were open ; his face was so very , very pale , and his black hair and beard made it look whiter still", "context_len": 35}}}, "tardily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Hartley , who , enveloped in an old-fashioned , large-patterned dressing-gown , had arrived rather tardily upon the scene , and had stood for several seconds glaring down at the two disgraceful specimens", "context_len": 33}}}, "ignoble": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Hartley , showing she was not too old to think ahead in an emergency , and yet drawing a deep sigh with the next breath at the thought of that best spare-room being put to so ignoble a service", "context_len": 39}}}, "inebriety": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "So great was her horror , either by education or intuition , of the state of inebriety , that the moment she surmised that at least two of these midnight visitors were bordering on the same , she could conceive of no means strong enough to express her disapproval", "context_len": 49}}}, "contamination": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "Every time she had come anywhere near them she had gathered her skirts about her as though in fear of actual contamination , and with her pretty head high in the air , as she moved away , would look askance over her shoulder as though not at all sure even then of being at a safe distance", "context_len": 58}}}, "flustered": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "He would glance at Bart , start to speak , lower his eyes , and , turning pale as he seemed to remember , and turning red as he seemed to realize , would fumble at his watch fob , run his fingers through his hair and act flustered generally", "context_len": 50}}}, "andirons": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` The real guest-chambers I may not offer you , '' she sighed , as she poked up the logs that blazed on the rusty andirons ; `` seeing the rats have made such havoc in them , and 't is many years since any one slept there", "context_len": 48}}}, "deplorable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 46, "context": "Now , my friend , he said , I want you to tell me the real name of your friend here , for I am convinced you have not done so , and then I want you to give me a true account of this whole deplorable affair", "context_len": 48}}}, "opiate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The opiate I have given him will account for this heavy sleep", "context_len": 12}}}, "indisposition": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Or , if he did n't take the bracelet , he took the professor 's mud turtle , '' put in Fenn , who had by this time recovered from his indisposition", "context_len": 33}}}, "expedite": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "Martha , who had no notion of waiting on such sorry customers , was careful to place everything within arm 's reach on the table and then to disappear , and the meal was eaten in silence , with no one in the room save the doctor , who kept pacing up and down in a manner that was intended to expedite their departure", "context_len": 64}}}, "scotched": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Yes , rather , drawled the other with cool effrontery ; and knowing he had scotched his man , the doctor turned on his heel , and the two men started off in the direction of the Nuneham station , neither sadder nor wiser , it is to be feared , for the lesson of the night 's experience", "context_len": 59}}}, "foregone": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Arnold , was not there occasion for elaborate preparation ? All this , of course , involved considerable delay , which Chris and the doctor would have gladly foregone ; but it gave Harry Allyn a sorely coveted opportunity for an early talk with Mrs", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "James Randolph , so they need not try it on , but once for all we can not decline the people who are willing to pay us good money , that is a foregone conclusion", "context_len": 35}}}, "entreaty": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "Please wait for me , Albert ; for Albert had scaled the stairs , and in another second would be out of sight ; and for a wonder , Albert waited -- touched , perhaps , by the entreaty in her voice , and perceptibly enjoying the turn of affairs that left him master of the situation", "context_len": 57}}}, "denying": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was no denying that Langridge had influence with the sporting crowd , and it was possible that he might exert it in favor of his new chum and against Phil", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "He hesitated for a moment , not from any intention of refusing to give his uncle his confidence , or denying that he had been in trouble , but from a desire to frame his confession in the best manner possible ; but nothing came to his aid other than the plain , unvarnished truth ; nothing else , he felt , would serve his turn here with that steady , searching eye upon him ; and in a moment he had taken his resolve , and the whole shameful tale was poured into Colonel Rush 's ears", "context_len": 98}}}, "allyn": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Allyn", "context_len": 1}}}, "conducting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 51, "context": "For two or three minutes we twisted hither and thither , blindly , so far as I was concerned , and then another flash came which enabled me to see that my companion was , as I had already suspected , my faithful little friend Ama , and that she was conducting me , by a somewhat circuitous route , toward the gate in the palisade", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Although the new-comers trod stealthily , the stillness of the air and the conducting properties of the calm water carried the sound of their footfalls with disconcerting clearness", "context_len": 28}}}, "plated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The man might have been mistaken for a circus performer , in his brilliant uniform , with plated horn , pistol , scabbard and belt , gay , flower-worked leggings , jingling spurs and fine boots with high heels , such as cowmen and rustlers affect", "context_len": 46}}}, "uncompromising": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Ben could not doubt the honest candor of his companion , but he shrunk from unbosoming himself , dreading to narrate the unpleasant story of the events which had made both Bern Hayden and his father his uncompromising enemies and had forced him to flee like a criminal from his native village in order to escape being sent to the State Reformatory", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Otherwise it is to be doubted if they would have put up half so civilly with the uncompromising treatment they had received at Babette 's hands", "context_len": 26}}}, "enormity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "Rebellion against lawful authority , rank disobedience and deception were to be laid at Percy 's door , not to speak of the pitiable weakness which had suffered him to be led into this wrong , and the enormity of his at least passive acquiescence when Flagg had stolen Seabrooke 's letter ; still worse his own destruction of it , almost involuntary though it was", "context_len": 66}}}, "impressing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "But happily Harry Allyn needed no such impressing", "context_len": 8}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "That such a youthful prince should be selected by so powerful a confederation of nations as their leader in such an enterprise as they were about to engage in , indicates a most extraordinary power on his part of acquiring an ascendency over the minds of men , and of impressing all with a sense of his commanding superiority", "context_len": 59}}}, "graver": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was impossible for any one to regard this adventure in any graver light than he , and yet , strange to say , he was happier than he had been for many a day", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Randolph is true , why things are graver than ever , in fact everything is up", "context_len": 16}}}, "uplifting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It is an intangible , uplifting something , that sensation that men call self-respect , and when they lose it they seem to lose the capacity for any happiness worth the name , and when they can not be persuaded to make an effort to get it back again , there seems to be little enough that they 're good for", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Lawrence went straight on with her story , not pausing for an instant , though that she heard quite as plainly as any one else was to be seen by the flush of color on her cheek , and the uplifting of the already upright head", "context_len": 46}}}, "inexpressible": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "The boy greeting him with a flash of white teeth , made no formality over admitting an entire stranger , but at once introduced him into a little book-closet on the ground floor , where a gentleman was busily engaged in unpacking folios from a great sea chest ; and as he turned to receive the visitor , Dick , to his inexpressible relief , saw a face that had been familiar to him in Scotland", "context_len": 76}}}, "necessities": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "The nomad black fellow makes his primitive humpy , or hut , in a location chosen temporarily , according to his necessities for hunting , fishing , and the like , by cutting a young sapling half through about four feet from the ground , and bending it over to a horizontal position , thus forming a ridge pole , against which boughs and strips of bark are laid", "context_len": 69}}}, "unsparingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Hartley personally , he sought him out , where he was at work in a corner of the meadow , and the result , as he had anticipated , was a very plain talk -- so unsparingly and pointedly plain that Harry winced a good deal in the process , and once or twice came near resenting a mode of procedure that seemed very much akin to knocking a fellow when he 's down", "context_len": 74}}}, "pointedly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "Hartley personally , he sought him out , where he was at work in a corner of the meadow , and the result , as he had anticipated , was a very plain talk -- so unsparingly and pointedly plain that Harry winced a good deal in the process , and once or twice came near resenting a mode of procedure that seemed very much akin to knocking a fellow when he 's down", "context_len": 74}}}, "humiliating": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Morris was welcome to stay , he felt he ought to be able to bear with the rest , no matter how humiliating and , in a measure , unmerited", "context_len": 30}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 89, "context": "Albert , who preferred that everything should be done decently and in order , placed a chair for Marie-Celeste on the other side of the Queen 's little table , and then seated himself on the gilded sofa beside Miss Belmore , in such a comfortable , snuggling-up way that Miss Belmore had to put one arm right round him and give him a sound little kiss by way of punishment , which Albert was courteous enough not to resent , notwithstanding he considered that sort of treatment somewhat humiliating for a boy of four", "context_len": 95}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "It was the most humiliating trick that had been `` pulled off in so many years that the memory of man runneth not to the contrary , '' as Holly Cross put it in one of his favorite quotations", "context_len": 39}}}, "exclaims": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Bother ! she exclaims , her pretty face all of a scowl", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Bother I say too , exclaims Harold ; we ca n't send him home , and with Aunt Lou up in London , there 's no one to leave him with here , and of course we ca n't take him", "context_len": 41}}}, "fora": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Could any one suppose for a moment , that he could hear all the beautiful plans fora Knight-of-the-Garter day discussed in his presence , and never make an effort to have a hand in it ? To be sure , the children had tried to keep the date a close-guarded secret , but Albert had got wind of it , all the same ; and here he was , bright and fresh as the day itself , marching up the path , his little blue sacque folded carefully over one arm , and an inviting luncheon hamper swinging from the other", "context_len": 101}}}, "ungracious": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 36, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}}}, "pansy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 40, "context": "Fortunately , considering the ungracious mood of the two children in the library , his first encounter chanced to be with Donald , who , arrayed in the white and blue of his summer sailor-suit , was bending over the pansy bed , gathering a few beauties into a bunch for Marie-Celeste ; and so absorbed in his task was he that he did not hear Albert 's tread upon the walk", "context_len": 72}}}, "tum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Of course you knowed where I tum from , Donald , Albert replied in his literal fashion ; but where do you s'pose I 'm doin ' ?", "context_len": 28}}}, "buttonholes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "No ; now 's your chance , I guess , said Donald kindly , slipping a great purple and yellow pansy into one of the buttonholes of Albert 's little frilled shirt as he spoke", "context_len": 35}}}, "fin": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Where are de children , anyway ? asked Albert , wonderfully reassured by Donald 's courteous reception ; wo n't you fin ' dem for me , please , Donald , and tell dem I wo n't be a badder , nor ask queshuns , and I 'll jus ' eat my own lunch and --", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "I wuz comin ' atter you anyhow , but Marse Harry holla 'd at me an ' tol ' me fer ter fin ' you an ' say dat de troops gwineter move in de mornin ' an ' our comp'ny starts fust", "context_len": 43}}}, "dem": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 20, "context": "Now tome , please , Marie-Celeste , he pleaded ; let 's hear about de tings before we look at dem and Marie-Celeste , feeling that they were all waiting for her , reluctantly did as she was bid , and dropped into the chair Albert had placed for her", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Marse Cally Shannon , '' said Whistling Jim , the negro , addressing me , `` what you reckon make dem white folks bang aloose at we-all , when we ai n't done a blessed thing ? When it come ter dat , we ai n't ez much ez speaken ter um , an ' here dey come , bangin ' aloose at us", "context_len": 65}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Pshaw ! dat ain \u2019 t nuffin , he said when the wife of Abner Fleming complimented him ; I \u2019 spects to do de same thing a good many more times afore we gits across de plains ; de fac \u2019 is , I \u2019 m on to dem Injins and dey \u2019 ll find it out ; when dey wokes up Jethro Mix dey wokes up de wrong passenger and dere \u2019 s gwine to be trouble in de land", "context_len": 82}}}, "assuming": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 62, "context": "At this the hard hearts relented altogether , and Harold rushed out and gave Albert a toss in the air that was very threatening to the eggs in the luncheon basket ; and as soon as he was on terra firma again Marie-Celeste gave him a good hard hug , and both begged his pardon half a dozen times over for ever assuming for a moment that he was too little , and intimated that they felt very small indeed themselves to think they had been so unfeeling as to plan not to include him in the expedition", "context_len": 98}}}, "intimated": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 73, "context": "At this the hard hearts relented altogether , and Harold rushed out and gave Albert a toss in the air that was very threatening to the eggs in the luncheon basket ; and as soon as he was on terra firma again Marie-Celeste gave him a good hard hug , and both begged his pardon half a dozen times over for ever assuming for a moment that he was too little , and intimated that they felt very small indeed themselves to think they had been so unfeeling as to plan not to include him in the expedition", "context_len": 98}}}, "admirer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "And so matters were beautifully adjusted , and the Knight-of-the-Garter party set out with Harold Harris , student and devoted admirer of the grand old knighthood , filling the important role of interpreter and guide", "context_len": 35}}}, "installed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "George 's , where the banners of the knights are hung and where the knights are duly installed", "context_len": 18}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "Miss Jones , the village dressmaker , was installed in the Rose Parlour , and appeared to be stitching morning , noon , and night ; the postman 's bag was quite heavy with patterns and catalogues of Indian outfits ; while distant relations and old acquaintances , who had neglected the Abbey for years , kept Nancy in a state of perpetual agitation by turning up suddenly to pay congratulatory calls", "context_len": 72}}}, "sidewise": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "On the way Harold held forth , Marie-Celeste and Donald walking one on either side of him , and Albert , determined not to miss a word , trotting along at a sort of sidewise angle just in front , and yet careful to keep well out of the way , too , for fear of the remotest chance of boddering", "context_len": 61}}}, "remotest": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 57, "context": "On the way Harold held forth , Marie-Celeste and Donald walking one on either side of him , and Albert , determined not to miss a word , trotting along at a sort of sidewise angle just in front , and yet careful to keep well out of the way , too , for fear of the remotest chance of boddering", "context_len": 61}}}, "dragon": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 19, "context": "He is supposed to have been a soldier in the Roman Army , and to have killed a monstrous dragon that no one else could overcome , and at last , after being dreadfully tortured for his faith in Christianity , he is also supposed to have died a martyr 's death", "context_len": 52}}}, "popes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Indeed , they knew so little about him way back even in the fifth century , that one of the popes , when he made up a list of the saints , said 'he was one of those whose names are justly reverenced among men , but whose actions are known only to God", "context_len": 54}}}, "reverenced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Indeed , they knew so little about him way back even in the fifth century , that one of the popes , when he made up a list of the saints , said 'he was one of those whose names are justly reverenced among men , but whose actions are known only to God", "context_len": 54}}}, "erudition": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Oh , yes , as though thankful there was some sort of explanation for such uncanny erudition", "context_len": 17}}}, "intimating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "You know what the Crusades were ? Marie-Celeste nodded yes , but intimating , with a significant glance in the direction of Donald and Albert , that probably they did not , Harold took the hint , and began over again", "context_len": 41}}}, "guides": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 33, "context": "By this time the children had reached the Norman Gate , and crossing the quadrangle , Harold led the way into the State apartments , and being well known to most of the guides of the castle , was allowed , with his little party , to pass on unattended , and to make his way straight to the Grand Banqueting Hall", "context_len": 62}}}, "unattended": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "By this time the children had reached the Norman Gate , and crossing the quadrangle , Harold led the way into the State apartments , and being well known to most of the guides of the castle , was allowed , with his little party , to pass on unattended , and to make his way straight to the Grand Banqueting Hall", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "At the break of the first day he went to the square , unattended by others , dressed in white leather moccasins and stockings , with a white dressed deer-skin over his shoulders , and produced there the new fire , by the friction of two dry pieces of wood", "context_len": 50}}}, "curves": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Now he was standing in admiration before the great oak chair of State beneath the organ gallery , and now nothing loath he mounts the steps that lead to it and runs a finger along the curves of its elaborate carving , and then , with a most reverent air , touches the embroidered cross and garter with which it is decorated", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "From the low-lying lands at the river \u2019 s edge the mouth of Rondout Creek curves back into the hills , forming for miles a safe , broad harbor", "context_len": 29}}}, "intercepts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "All this is making very free with State belongings , and one of the guides , in charge of a small party of visitors , starts toward him in a decidedly menacing manner ; but Harold intercepts him and explains , and the guide , himself much amused , decides to leave unmolested this gallant little tar of Her Majesty 's", "context_len": 61}}}, "armorial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "This is done the better to take in the elaborate ceiling , decorated as it is with the armorial bearings of the knights of five centuries , and now , with arm upraised and extended finger , he is entering into some mathematical calculation of his own in connection with the banners that hang just beneath the ceiling", "context_len": 58}}}, "upraised": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 2, "context": "Wentworth 's upraised hands ; she set the boys to swing honey-pots , and the girls to play 'Drop the Handkerchief ' ; she boldly interposed her small person between the fists of two fighting hobbledehoys , and seized a little boy by the boot who had ventured to climb up the stove-pipe ; she welcomed some of the parents who had begun to arrive for the entertainment , and found them comfortable places on the benches , even nursing one of the babies while its mother went to the rescue of an older child , who was being forcibly held down and sat upon by several of its companions ; and by the time the bell was rung , and the audience requested to take their places", "context_len": 128}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "Like a flock of sheep the Huns , with hands still upraised , shuffled on the first of their long trek to captivity -- to some delectable spot in England , where , far from the sound of the guns , there is food in plenty for Hun prisoners of war , German U-boats notwithstanding", "context_len": 55}}}, "vertical": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "And now what does the boy do but suddenly exchange his vertical position for one quite the reverse , and turn all his attention to the carpet ; for did not Harold say it was woven in some special way on purpose ? Yes , sure enough ! here is the Cross of St", "context_len": 54}}}, "gaiter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "George in the centre of each little panel , and here -- crossing to the edge of the room -- the beautiful circle of the gaiter worked into the design of the border", "context_len": 33}}}, "unsuspicious": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "Oh , but it is a wonderful place ! and there are probably other rooms just as wonderful ; so a little closer look at the brass shields and the helmets , and the portraits of the sovereigns ranged along one side , and then , wholly unsuspicious of any disapproval , he walks over to the children and remarks that now he would like to see the other rooms , please", "context_len": 72}}}, "hails": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Albert , who has found much of Harold 's narration quite beyond him , but has never let on for one moment , hails the announcement with great inward rejoicing , and the little quartette make their way to the Guard Chamber , as the place next in interest", "context_len": 49}}}, "quartette": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 34, "context": "Albert , who has found much of Harold 's narration quite beyond him , but has never let on for one moment , hails the announcement with great inward rejoicing , and the little quartette make their way to the Guard Chamber , as the place next in interest", "context_len": 49}}}, "minding": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "In every room Donald brings his own peculiar methods of investigation to bear , not in the least minding a good deal of mirthful laughter at his expense on the part of Harold and Marie-Celeste ; and Albert , feeling privileged to join in the general merriment , though evidently half the time without in anywise appreciating the situation , only helps on the jollity of things", "context_len": 67}}}, "mirthful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "In every room Donald brings his own peculiar methods of investigation to bear , not in the least minding a good deal of mirthful laughter at his expense on the part of Harold and Marie-Celeste ; and Albert , feeling privileged to join in the general merriment , though evidently half the time without in anywise appreciating the situation , only helps on the jollity of things", "context_len": 67}}}, "anywise": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "Alaeddin [ 329 ] waited till his mother had made an end of her speech and said to her , `` O my mother , all that thou thinkest I know ; marry , I know full well that I am the son of poor folk , nor may all this thy talk anywise avail to move me from my purpose ; but I beseech thee , an I be thy very son and thou love me , do me this kindness ; else wilt thou lose me , for death hasteneth upon me , an I attain not my wish of the beloved of my heart", "context_len": 107}}}, "jollity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "In every room Donald brings his own peculiar methods of investigation to bear , not in the least minding a good deal of mirthful laughter at his expense on the part of Harold and Marie-Celeste ; and Albert , feeling privileged to join in the general merriment , though evidently half the time without in anywise appreciating the situation , only helps on the jollity of things", "context_len": 67}}}, "guardsman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then when at noon , by special permission of a very lenient guardsman , the children establish themselves for luncheon on a terrace beneath the shade of the Round Tower , Marie-Celeste and Albert and Harold agree that they had never had such fun -- never !", "context_len": 47}}}, "research": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "We have deemed it appropriate to dwell at length on the history , topography and peculiar customs of the Seminoles on account of their identity with the Creek Indians , the main object of this research", "context_len": 36}}}, "installing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Entering at the door at the south front and crossing to the centre , the children passed directly into the choir , which is really a chapel in itself , and to them of special interest , because the very place where the ceremony of installing ' the knights is performed", "context_len": 51}}}, "initiated": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "It is n't two feet high , but it seemed like a hundred , '' and he pointed to a small ledge of rock , where Sandy Merton and his mates had stood as they lowered from the wagon the lads who were being initiated", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "installation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "It was of course the spot of spots for any one who , like Harold , had been initiated into all the mysteries by being present at an installation , and he did justice to the occasion", "context_len": 37}}}, "lapsed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "He had completely lapsed from his humble estate of the morning , when he felt in duty bound to at least pretend to be an attentive listener , and when they reached the chapel , already such a familiar place to him , he no longer even tried to keep up appearances", "context_len": 52}}}, "verger": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Rosamund , whose eyes were fixed on her , noticed that the verger pointed to the pew in which she herself was sitting , and a minute later the lady came to the door of the pew and said something in a very low voice to Mrs", "context_len": 47}}}, "curl": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 3, "context": "And he did curl up at the bottom of Barty 's big bed of leaves , and almost before he had time to do it Barty had curled up , too , like a squirrel in a nest , and he was fast asleep -- and so was little Man Saturday , who curled up close beside him", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "In this case , then , tangled and helpless as he was , Harry Paul could only think for a few moments of the time when he swam into the sea-cave at Pen Point at high tide , and felt the long strands of the bladder wrack curl and twist round his limbs like the tentacles of some sea-monster ; and he realised once more the chilling sense of helpless horror that seemed to numb his faculties", "context_len": 77}}}, "reasonably": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 41, "context": "She further stated that , upon awaking , she had again gone aloft to take another long and careful look round , but had seen nothing more of our pursuers , and was therefore inclined to believe that we were now reasonably safe , provided , of course , that we did all our travelling at night , kept a sharp look-out , and were careful not to allow ourselves to be fallen in with by other users of the river", "context_len": 81}}}, "setter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "Nothing was farther from Timothy 's thoughts than to make any disturbance whatever -- why should he ? Were they not the best of friends , he and that blessed little Albert ? so he never raised his head from where it rested upon his outstretched paws , only looked up with that gaze of implicit confidence peculiar to the kind eyes of the Laverick setter , and which made Albert lose not a second in spreading his little coat out beneath him , throwing his two arms around Timothy 's neck , and pillowing his head on his beautiful silky coat", "context_len": 102}}}, "pillowing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 94, "context": "Nothing was farther from Timothy 's thoughts than to make any disturbance whatever -- why should he ? Were they not the best of friends , he and that blessed little Albert ? so he never raised his head from where it rested upon his outstretched paws , only looked up with that gaze of implicit confidence peculiar to the kind eyes of the Laverick setter , and which made Albert lose not a second in spreading his little coat out beneath him , throwing his two arms around Timothy 's neck , and pillowing his head on his beautiful silky coat", "context_len": 102}}}, "setters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Now , it is not granted to Laverick setters to purr in pussy 's demonstrative fashion , but they have a subdued little grateful purr of their own , distinctly audible to an ear placed as close as Albert 's chanced to be , and Timothy at once indulged in the same", "context_len": 52}}}, "purr": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The puma tried to purr , and licked the hand that fondled him", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "Resigning himself to the situation , Matt listened while the purr of a motor came to his ears and died away in the direction of New York", "context_len": 27}}}, "indefinitely": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "I could plan nothing , I could hope nothing , but it seemed to me that my journey to Switzerland was indefinitely postponed , and that my future from being settled in every detail , month , week , hour , and all , was as indefinite and vague and shadowy as though I were standing on the brink of the other world", "context_len": 63}}}, "feasting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then along had come the wildcat , intent on a meal , had crept upon the feasting fox , had leaped down from a tree , and , with the quickness of light , had given the death stroke", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "Three days had passed since his departure , and Pauline stood in the doorway feasting her eyes on the lights and shadows which grouped themselves about the distant hills , when Lemuel brushed past her , clad in his Sunday best", "context_len": 41}}}, "garters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "Oh , yes ! There was a time when the wives of the knights were called Ladies of the Society of the Garter , and they used to be allowed to wear violet-colored or white cloth robes 'furred , ' as one old book says , and embroidered with garters", "context_len": 50}}}, "tiles": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "In the living-room of the Home I am going to have a beautiful open fireplace ( for of course there wo n't be any parlor ) -- the most beautiful that can be made -- and right above the tiles and under the ledge of the mantel I am going to have the legend , in gold letters , that will shine even in the twilight , 'For love of Marie-Celeste and then Mr", "context_len": 74}}}, "gamin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "There was a whimsical little look in Donald 's face , which an American street gamin would have interpreted as what are you giving us ? He did not say anything , however ; and just then Harold , who had strolled on by himself , came toward them , his face aglow with merriment", "context_len": 55}}}, "kilt": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "He had not meant that any one should know it , such a modest little knight was he ; but alas ! the weakness of drowsiness had overtaken the valiant little fellow , and in the disorder thereon attendant the shapely little limb had thrust itself forth from the folds of the protecting kilt , and there was the garter plainly visible to the most casual passer-by", "context_len": 67}}}, "imitating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "For when she encased herself in cold silence , and stalked home with lifted head and unseeing eyes , often a little throng of Mexican children would walk behind her , imitating her stately gait and calling mockingly , `` Ea ! ea ! See the madamisela ! See the princess ! She is sister to the king -- that one ! Vah ! vah ! vah ! ''", "context_len": 69}}}, "palfrey": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "Yes , ladies and gentlemen , she said , imitating exactly old Brown 's tone and accent when showing visitors through the chapel , this is a monument erected to the memory of a knight who was killed in battle , together with his noble palfrey", "context_len": 46}}}, "uncontrollable": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "He had felt that this time must come , but now the dread of it passed from him instantly , and he was almost frightened by that feeling of eager fierceness and uncontrollable rage which had possessed him in the hour when he was led to wreak physical violence on Hayden for the destruction of little Jerry \u2019 s fiddle", "context_len": 60}}}, "entreatingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "At this the little limb was suddenly drawn up , that the tell-tale garter might be hid from view ; and then , able to stand it no longer , Albert looked up entreatingly to the children above him , and blushingly explained , Dorothy made it for me , just for a bit of fun , you know ; and then sure to a certainty that he never , never would hear the end of that blue garter , buried his blushes in Timothy 's long silky coat , and rued the hour when Dorothy had so merrily abetted his desire for this particular bit of fun", "context_len": 108}}}, "blushingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "At this the little limb was suddenly drawn up , that the tell-tale garter might be hid from view ; and then , able to stand it no longer , Albert looked up entreatingly to the children above him , and blushingly explained , Dorothy made it for me , just for a bit of fun , you know ; and then sure to a certainty that he never , never would hear the end of that blue garter , buried his blushes in Timothy 's long silky coat , and rued the hour when Dorothy had so merrily abetted his desire for this particular bit of fun", "context_len": 108}}}, "rued": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 91, "context": "At this the little limb was suddenly drawn up , that the tell-tale garter might be hid from view ; and then , able to stand it no longer , Albert looked up entreatingly to the children above him , and blushingly explained , Dorothy made it for me , just for a bit of fun , you know ; and then sure to a certainty that he never , never would hear the end of that blue garter , buried his blushes in Timothy 's long silky coat , and rued the hour when Dorothy had so merrily abetted his desire for this particular bit of fun", "context_len": 108}}}, "abetted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 99, "context": "At this the little limb was suddenly drawn up , that the tell-tale garter might be hid from view ; and then , able to stand it no longer , Albert looked up entreatingly to the children above him , and blushingly explained , Dorothy made it for me , just for a bit of fun , you know ; and then sure to a certainty that he never , never would hear the end of that blue garter , buried his blushes in Timothy 's long silky coat , and rued the hour when Dorothy had so merrily abetted his desire for this particular bit of fun", "context_len": 108}}}, "advisability": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Harris , apparently none the wiser , talked on and on , and the ponies put their heads together , as though actually conferring as to the advisability of stopping to graze a little while by the way", "context_len": 38}}}, "unfitting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "It was well enough while he needed care and nursing , but the boy has always had to rough it , and he 'll have to rough it again ; and I think we 're unfitting him for it", "context_len": 39}}}, "critics": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Some modern critics believe that de Soto 's army did not cross the mountains into what is now North Carolina and Tennessee , the `` over-hill '' seats of the Cheroki people , but only skirted the southern slope of the Apalachian ridge by passing through Northern Georgia west into Northern Alabama , and then descending Coosa river", "context_len": 59}}}, "phaeton": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "They readjusted things in the comfortable little phaeton and tucked the lap-robe about them in trimmer fashion , and then the ponies , feeling a tightening grasp on the lines , and intuitively conscious of a whip poised at an easily descending angle , wisely saw fit to make up for lost time", "context_len": 53}}}, "trimmer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "They readjusted things in the comfortable little phaeton and tucked the lap-robe about them in trimmer fashion , and then the ponies , feeling a tightening grasp on the lines , and intuitively conscious of a whip poised at an easily descending angle , wisely saw fit to make up for lost time", "context_len": 53}}}, "imaginations": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 35, "context": "The sight of his fresh young face and boyish figure recalled to many miners the sons whom they had left behind them , and helped to make more vivid the picture of home which their imaginations often conjured up , and they would have liked to have Ben join their company", "context_len": 51}}}, "mailed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Complete Catalogue of Famous Alger Books , Celebrated Castlemon Books and Renowned Ellis Books mailed on application", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "Young Hermann 's sword broke against a Danish axe ; he rushed in and got within the swing of the weapon ; both wrestled for the deadly steel , they fell , rolled over and over on the grass ; at length Hermann grasped his opponent 's throat like a vice with his mailed hand , and held till the arms of his foe hung nerveless by the side and the face grew black , when , disengaging his right hand , he found his dagger , and drove it to the victim 's heart", "context_len": 95}}}, "thatched": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "That was all there was in it , and yet can you not surmise how even that letter was calculated to work great consternation in the mind of some one in the little thatched cottage -- some one who never saw the letter itself , and who did not so much as know of its existence until it had been read and re-read and thought over and answered , but who when one day he was made acquainted with its contents felt as weak as a kitten for hours afterward ? He happened to be lying on the lounge in the living-room at the time , the same lounge to which he had been carried more dead than alive apparently , just four weeks before", "context_len": 125}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Below the house the meadow sloped down to a river , where a stone bridge led to the village , with its pretty thatched cottages and Norman church , whose square tower stood up like a beacon for the surrounding country ; and away in the distance , tier upon tier , rose the Welsh mountains , fading from green to purple or from purple to misty mauve , till the last were lost in the hazy blue of the sky", "context_len": 81}}}, "lodger": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Seems like , to look at our new lodger , that he 's mendin ' in more ways than one , he had said to his wife as they walked to the parish church on a sunshiny Sunday morning , the second after Ted 's accident", "context_len": 46}}}, "staid": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 15, "context": "They had a travelling uncle who came home to them at last ; but he staid away ten years and did not come home even to see his mother , and I think he ort to do n't you ? But now he is come and has brought so many trunks and boxes with such lots and lots of things and kurositys in them that he is 'most like a Norz ' Ark only better , and his gret coat and cap are made of the bears ' skins he shot and he tells us about the tigers and lions and I do n't like it and Fred and Harry do and Bessie do n't too", "context_len": 116}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Henderson staid , so no explanations were made", "context_len": 8}}}, "bethinking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Marie-Celeste ! exclaimed Ted ; and then , bethinking himself , he asked quite casually , Who is Marie-Celeste , I should like to know ?", "context_len": 26}}}, "adjusting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Morris ; and lowering the shades at the two ivy-grown windows , and adjusting the screen that stood near the bed , Chris left the room", "context_len": 26}}}, "fraud": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "Regret was expressed for the loss of Tasker and Keene , both of whom were highly esteemed by all their shipmates ; and then the conversation diverged to the topic of the audacious Virginia-Preciosa , which , protected by the very ingenious fraud of the double sets of papers and the double crews , was still merrily pursuing her way and bidding defiance to everybody", "context_len": 65}}}, "oblivion": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": "`` `` Far be it '' ( as the Arabs say ) that we should do so cruel a wrong to so well and justly beloved a memory as that of Jacques Cazotte as to attempt to perpetuate the remembrance of a literary crime which one can hardly believe him to have committed in sober earnest ! Rather let us seek to bury in oblivion this his one offence and suffer kind Lethe with its beneficent waters to wash this `` adulterous blot '' from his else unsullied name", "context_len": 89}}}, "transpired": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "Now , this discovery of Ted 's , that Chris was a friend of Marie-Celeste , and the perplexing state of mind that followed , had transpired , you understand , two weeks previous to this particular chapter , and Ted , you remember , is lying on the chintz-covered lounge in the living-room , having gained strength enough in the mean time to walk from his bed to the lounge unaided", "context_len": 72}}}, "shifts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Now and then , as he comes across anything he thinks will interest Ted , he lets the chair drop on to all-fours , shifts his position so as to bring himself into line with the door , and reads the article or paragraph aloud", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "I tell you what , I like to contrive and make shifts , and get along so , better than I do to have everything to do with , or have everything done for me", "context_len": 35}}}, "listens": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` And they tell him everything , what they have read and studied , and the little troubles and differences and perplexities , and he listens and explains and laughs with them when it is funny , and everything is so nice", "context_len": 42}}}, "feigns": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Ted , amused , and grateful as well at the manner in which the old keeper has gradually softened toward him , always listens attentively , and courteously feigns interest , when he finds he can not command the real article", "context_len": 41}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` 'T is just to vex me the old fool feigns himself to be deaf", "context_len": 15}}}, "flits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Hartley , still busy about her morning household duties , occasionally flits in and out of the room , and Ted 's eyes follow her devotedly every moment that she is there", "context_len": 32}}}, "benignant": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "But this was less the cause for the general difference in Jane 's aspect than the fact that her brown hair , parted smoothly on the broad , benignant brow , now had its ends tucked up in a neat knot", "context_len": 41}}}, "empties": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "Hartley 's , Ted has detained her for a moment , to ask some trifling question , and just as she is about to make a reply , Chris , returning from his daily ride into Nuneham for the mail , swings into the room with his breezy , postman-like air , and empties the contents of the little Hartley mail-bag upon the table", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The gasholder is placed in a tank of water , so that there is no waste of gas as the huge iron holder fills or empties", "context_len": 26}}}, "enlivening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Hartley , who had seated herself in the nearest chair , awaiting the budget of news that Chris always endeavored to bring out from Nuneham , for the enlivening of the old people", "context_len": 33}}}, "unobservant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Hartley , and back again to Chris , in hopeless bewilderment , while they , wholly unobservant , continued to converse in what seemed to him most idiotic fashion", "context_len": 29}}}, "idiotic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Hartley , and back again to Chris , in hopeless bewilderment , while they , wholly unobservant , continued to converse in what seemed to him most idiotic fashion", "context_len": 29}}}, "observers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Every load of excavation was carefully taken miles to the rear in the dead of night , in order to baffle the enemy 's aerial observers", "context_len": 26}}}, "petulantly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Well , '' I said somewhat petulantly , `` I can not imagine why you are nervous about her", "context_len": 20}}}, "ascribing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Morris , ascribing Ted 's petulance to the nervousness of slow convalescence , happens to mean a little sailor boy who crossed on the steamer with us , and about whom Mr", "context_len": 32}}}, "petulance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Morris , ascribing Ted 's petulance to the nervousness of slow convalescence , happens to mean a little sailor boy who crossed on the steamer with us , and about whom Mr", "context_len": 32}}}, "blurt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Of course I 'll have to take Donald into my confidence ; but like as not he 'll come suddenly upon me , and blurt out just who I am before I get a chance to give him a point or two", "context_len": 42}}}, "uncrowned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "But alas ! I for one know to a Certainty that in the little castle there was rebellion almost amounting to mutiny , and that one curly , uncrowned head , that need not have had a care in all the world , was tossing uneasily on its pillow", "context_len": 49}}}, "abdicate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Indeed , the little queen , whose realm was the hearts of her nearest and dearest , would have been obliged to abdicate , for a while at least , I fancy , had she not chosen before nightfall of that same day to bury her head in the lap of her very most loyal subject , and with tears and sohs confess to her extreme unreasonableness and avow her determination not soon again to be overtaken by such a sorry state of mind and temper", "context_len": 86}}}, "unreasonableness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "Indeed , the little queen , whose realm was the hearts of her nearest and dearest , would have been obliged to abdicate , for a while at least , I fancy , had she not chosen before nightfall of that same day to bury her head in the lap of her very most loyal subject , and with tears and sohs confess to her extreme unreasonableness and avow her determination not soon again to be overtaken by such a sorry state of mind and temper", "context_len": 86}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It seems that opposite the Nag 's Head Inn the Church of England has built a tiny edifice , and as though to apologize for the apparent unreasonableness of building any church there whatsoever , they have made a most miniature affair of it", "context_len": 44}}}, "wilful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "And all this while the wilful little queen was trying to convince herself that it was ready for Donald 's sake , when the truth was that the long walks with Donald , when Harold -- who was making up some necessary back work at college -- was not at her service , were what she was determined not to give up , and the reading aloud in the evenings , when Donald was such a delightful listener ; and , in fact , the hundred and one little amusing things that Donald was continually doing , and that made the days go by in such happy , merry fashion", "context_len": 110}}}, "capitulated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "But at sunset on the fourth day Marie-Celeste relented , and coming into the house with a white flag of truce at her eyes , threw herself at the feet of her dearest subject , and burying her head , as I have already hinted , in the lap of the same , capitulated body and soul", "context_len": 57}}}, "cheeriest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "They had seen him off at the station -- Harold and she -- and Donald , never allowing himself for a moment to regard this whole affair in any light but the true one , kept a stiff upper lip to the last , and smiled the cheeriest good-by as the guard banged the carriage-door and the train glided out from the depot", "context_len": 63}}}, "depot": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 65, "context": "Her three cousins -- Gwendolyn , Russell , and Belle -- had greeted her cordially as soon as the train drew up in a station which , for size and grandeur , surpassed her wildest dreams , and then escorted her between a bewildering panorama of flashing lights , brilliant shop windows , swiftly moving cars , and people in an endless stream to another depot , for her Uncle Robert resided in the suburbs", "context_len": 75}}}, "reservation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "And so , as you have read , Marie-Celeste did really give in , without so much as a mental reservation , and other hearts than Donald 's were wondrously lightened , and there was joy throughout all the kingdom that the queen had come to her senses", "context_len": 48}}}, "nipped": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 41, "context": "Meantime , Donald 's train made good time to Nuneham ; and there was Chris at the station waiting with open arms to receive him , and , what was more , he took Donald into them in a way that nipped in the bud those queer little misgivings that spring up unbidden when one chances to be leaving old scenes for new", "context_len": 63}}}, "motherliness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "And then when they reached the cottage , there stood dear old Mis , Hartley , looking the picture of motherliness in her snow-white cap and kerchief ; and the welcome that she gave Donald made him feel beyond all doubting that he had but exchanged one dear home for another ; and that meant worlds to a boy who had come to know for the first time what a dear place home might be", "context_len": 75}}}, "intervened": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 3, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}}}, "solicitude": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 8, "context": "Hartley , looking at Ted with the deepest solicitude , so that Ted felt like a fraud , for though tired indeed from the drive , he had quite strength enough to take his seat at the table with the rest but for the presence of that new and undesired guest , Donald", "context_len": 53}}}, "undesired": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Hartley , looking at Ted with the deepest solicitude , so that Ted felt like a fraud , for though tired indeed from the drive , he had quite strength enough to take his seat at the table with the rest but for the presence of that new and undesired guest , Donald", "context_len": 53}}}, "overtaxed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Deeply annoyed that Ted 's strength should have been so apparently overtaxed , Mrs", "context_len": 14}}}, "unbolted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 69, "context": "Her Spanish crew we soon found snugly hidden away in spacious quarters beneath the lazaret ; and , as to the name on her stern , we found that the piece of wood on which it was carved and painted was reversible , having Virginia , New Orleans , carved on one side of it and Preciosa , Havana , on the other , and that it could be unbolted and reversed in a few minutes by lifting a couple of movable planks in the after cabin", "context_len": 87}}}, "enlighten": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "The break that was brought into requisition belonged to Theodore , and was simply appropriated by Harold , for there was no saying by your leave to a fellow who went driving through the country himself without even taking the pains to enlighten you as to his whereabouts", "context_len": 48}}}, "courtyards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "But what seemed lacking in elegance of appointment was made up in the number of horses ; and happening to enter one of the courtyards just as three of the court carriages were about to be driven out of it , the children were intensely interested", "context_len": 46}}}, "stablemen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The mystery of how they were to be driven was solved in a moment , however , when a faultlessly equipped groom threw himself astride of one of the leaders , and the stablemen , standing at the bridles of the four-in-hand , at one and the same moment let go their hold , and sprang quickly out of the way", "context_len": 61}}}, "bridles": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "The mystery of how they were to be driven was solved in a moment , however , when a faultlessly equipped groom threw himself astride of one of the leaders , and the stablemen , standing at the bridles of the four-in-hand , at one and the same moment let go their hold , and sprang quickly out of the way", "context_len": 61}}}, "inspiring": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was very inspiring and exciting to see the three coaches , that were to convey some royal guests to the depot , leave the courtyard one after the other , the horses in each case prancing in wildest fashion and perfectly free , apparently , with the exception of the one mounted leader , to do any outlandish thing that they chose", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was different from advancing with tried and trusted comrades around him and the inspiring dash that accompanied the rush of men confident of victory", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "The sturdy independence he inherited from an ancestry of English tradesfolk and yeomen made him cherish the ideal of an English republic with religious fervour , while , whether leading a prayer meeting or heading a cavalry charge , his inspiring enthusiasm carried away all who were near him", "context_len": 49}}}, "coaches": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Ees , ees we 'd surely stay by her ; but 't is no fitting place here for a young lady ; why , there 's no company -- no coming and going ; and the coaches as used to come to the old Squires 's ; and the quality ; and they fare to have clean forgotten our young lady , dang 'em ! And Squire 's great house turned into an inn ! You think o ' that ! If so be as you goo into Hun'ston , you 'll see the name o ' it , The Royal Oak , and a great oak tree drawed for a sign over the front door", "context_len": 117}}}, "outlandish": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 58, "context": "It was very inspiring and exciting to see the three coaches , that were to convey some royal guests to the depot , leave the courtyard one after the other , the horses in each case prancing in wildest fashion and perfectly free , apparently , with the exception of the one mounted leader , to do any outlandish thing that they chose", "context_len": 63}}}, "consign": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Harris had advised him to put it in his pocket or to consign it to him for safer keeping but to no avail", "context_len": 23}}}, "sesame": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Albert considered the grip of his own right hand the safest place by far for the valuable little square of cardboard , and which was nothing else than the open sesame to the Queen 's own garden , called the East Terrace , and to which the general public only occasionally were admitted", "context_len": 53}}}, "waived": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 48, "context": "Whether the powers that rule the entrance to the same came to the conclusion that to a little girl of twelve and a little boy of four the term of general public could not honestly be applied , or whether all rules of procedure and precedence were magnanimously waived in their favor , certain it is that the little card in question bore the incredible inscription : Admit Master Albert Allyn and his little friend , Miss Marie-Celeste Harris , to the East Terrace between the hours of twelve and three on Thursday", "context_len": 93}}}, "orangery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "And such a garden as it proved ! with its fountains and statues and vases , and the orangery on one side , and on the other three sides a beautiful sloping lawn , ascending from the level of the garden to the gray stonewall at the outer edge of the terrace ; and to think that here they were actually walking about in this beautiful garden , instead of merely peering through the fretwork of the iron gate , as some other little children with envious eyes were doing that very moment", "context_len": 93}}}, "stonewall": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "And such a garden as it proved ! with its fountains and statues and vases , and the orangery on one side , and on the other three sides a beautiful sloping lawn , ascending from the level of the garden to the gray stonewall at the outer edge of the terrace ; and to think that here they were actually walking about in this beautiful garden , instead of merely peering through the fretwork of the iron gate , as some other little children with envious eyes were doing that very moment", "context_len": 93}}}, "fretwork": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 74, "context": "And such a garden as it proved ! with its fountains and statues and vases , and the orangery on one side , and on the other three sides a beautiful sloping lawn , ascending from the level of the garden to the gray stonewall at the outer edge of the terrace ; and to think that here they were actually walking about in this beautiful garden , instead of merely peering through the fretwork of the iron gate , as some other little children with envious eyes were doing that very moment", "context_len": 93}}}, "royalty": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "But with Marie-Celeste it was very different , She stood in worshipful admiration before all the royal belongings , and when permitted to gaze into one or two of the bedrooms where royalty actually put itself to bed , behind beautiful embroidered draperies , her sense of the privilege accorded her fairly made her hold her breath", "context_len": 57}}}, "whimsically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Albert looked at Marie-Celeste a little whimsically , and then said dryly : Well , I don ' know much about what 's happened here , and I s'ould n't sink jus ' an American little girl would know so very much eider", "context_len": 43}}}, "eider": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Albert looked at Marie-Celeste a little whimsically , and then said dryly : Well , I don ' know much about what 's happened here , and I s'ould n't sink jus ' an American little girl would know so very much eider", "context_len": 43}}}, "insinuation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Perhaps not , said Marie-Celeste , half angry at Albert 's insinuation ; but 's'ould n't sink ' or no , I could tell you a good deal if I chose to about one little queen who lived here --", "context_len": 40}}}, "conciliated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Inclined at first to resent the interference , the man stares at Donald a moment , and then , possibly conciliated by the semi-official aspect of his sailor costume , condescends to reply :", "context_len": 34}}}, "consequential": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "Albert gave Marie-Celeste a look which said as plainly as words : That jus ' American little girls could be awful mean , and evidently deciding it would be best to leave that kind of a girl to herself , turned on his heel and walked straight off toward the castle with a consequential air , and as though bent on reporting such unseemly conduct to Her Majesty in person", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Altogether the vehicle had a lordly air , easily matching that of the individual sitting alone on the purple cushions -- a man whose features were not very clear at the distance , although the yellowness of his beard , the glitter of his studded shirt-front , and whole consequential , expansive effect recalled to the doctor 's mind an image of the past , less ornate , indeed , and affluent , but of similar aspect", "context_len": 77}}}, "unworthily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 102, "context": "And what more natural , when you find yourself at the foot of a flight of steps , than to walk up them , no matter if the place does chance to be Windsor Castle ; and then if at the top you find an open door confronting you , what more natural than to walk in , particularly if there happens to be no one to say you nay , and you have half a mind , besides , to seek an audience of the Queen , and report the ungracious conduct of an ungracious little American , who has been unworthily permitted to tread the paths of the royal garden", "context_len": 112}}}, "perceptibly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "Please wait for me , Albert ; for Albert had scaled the stairs , and in another second would be out of sight ; and for a wonder , Albert waited -- touched , perhaps , by the entreaty in her voice , and perceptibly enjoying the turn of affairs that left him master of the situation", "context_len": 57}}}, "detaining": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Did the Queen 's mother come out and ask you to come in ? whispered Marie-Celeste , detaining Albert by main force , while she straightened his necktie and gave his hopelessly frowsy curls a rearranging touch", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Stay , '' she laid a detaining hand upon his arm ; `` where did he live ? ''", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But Miss Clyde laid a detaining hand on her shoulder", "context_len": 10}}}, "unsolicited": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "And this was all the light that was ever thrown on Albert 's unsolicited entree into Windsor Castle", "context_len": 18}}}, "cavalierly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "Albert , who had already become familiar with the appointments of this apartment , stepped at once to the table , near which an elderly lady was sitting , and laying his sailor-hat , nothing loath , atop of a miniature of His Royal Highness the Prince of Wales , announced cavalierly , And -- and now , this is my little American friend , Marie-Celeste", "context_len": 66}}}, "ermine": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 115, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}}}, "lustre": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "When it was full , his mother looked at the dish , but could not gaze fixedly thereon , for the radiance of the jewels and their lustre and the excess of their flashing ; so she shut her eyes and her wit was confounded at them ; yet was she not certified that their value was in very deed so great as her son had said , but bethought her that his speech might be true in that their like was not found with kings", "context_len": 86}}}, "narrating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "But there is one thing , added Marie-Celeste , changing the subject , because unwilling that so important an occasion should be to any extent devoted to any mere narrating of their own childish doings , I would very much like to know , and that is , if Victoria is ever called Madame La Grande Reine ?", "context_len": 58}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "It is the custom among all nations to give kings the glory of all that is effected by their generals and officers ; and the writers of those days would , of course , in narrating the exploits of the Macedonian army , exaggerate the share which Alexander had in their performances , and underrate those of Parmenio", "context_len": 58}}}, "se": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "So I luft ; dem antelopes am wery rewengeful wery rewengeful and I \u2019 se too smart to gib \u2019 em de chance dey wanted to lambast me", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "Dat \u2019 s what I oughter do , but I hain \u2019 t got de courage , Al ; it \u2019 s been on my mind two , free times , and I started in to let you know , but I \u2019 se afeard", "context_len": 45}}}, "annihilate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 66, "context": "Startled by the red-coated Britishers rising up at the word of their leader , they broke and fled ; and the men of the 93rd , who , but a little before , had made up their minds to die where they stood , saw as in a dream their enemies scattered and broken ; and the cloud of horsemen which had threatened to engulf and annihilate them , make no effort to snatch the victory which seemed within their grasp", "context_len": 81}}}, "concealing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 58, "context": "She was not long in discovering the landmarks of which she was in search , and a few minutes later the canoe was threading its tortuous way among a tangled mass of mangrove roots toward the solid bank of the river , landing upon which , we drew our light craft bodily up out of the water , concealing her beneath a broad overhanging mass of foliage which hid her so effectually that I would defy anybody but ourselves to find her", "context_len": 82}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Without waiting for more , the catamaran \u2019 s company threw aside the concealing mats", "context_len": 15}}}, "regalia": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "After she had received a word or two of direction from Miss Belmore , the children set off under her guidance , with unconcealed delight on their faces at the prospect of seeing with their own eyes these mysterious apartments , and with a deep-seated hope in each quick-beating heart that in all the full regalia of crown and sceptre and ermine they might somewhere encounter the marvellous Queen", "context_len": 69}}}, "condescension": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "Meantime , imagine the astonishment of the inmates of the Little Castle to have a finely mounted groom , in the royal livery of the big Castle , ride up to their door , and with that indescribable condescension inherent in even the most ordinary of grooms , hand in a communication , which on being opened imparted the rather astounding information That Her Most Gracious Majesty the Queen , having accidentally made the acquaintance of the little visitors to the East Terrace , had invited them to remain for luncheon at the Castle , and would see that they reached home safely under proper escort later in the afternoon", "context_len": 110}}}, "boudoir": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "First thing I know you fellows will have this a regular boudoir ; and then where will I be ? I expect any day now you 'll be wanting to get rid of this old couch and chair , and get some mission furniture , so that you can have a five o'clock tea here , and invite some girls and chaperons", "context_len": 62}}}, "accede": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "The maid , however , standing stiff and straight , with a towel thrown over her arm and a whisk-broom in hand ready to attack them , looked so very formidable that Marie-Celeste begged Ainslee not to leave them ; and Ainslee , herself appreciating the overbearing self-importance of the maid Babette , was good enough to accede to her request", "context_len": 61}}}, "freshening": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "And then followed such a freshening of toilette as was fairly humiliating in its thoroughness", "context_len": 15}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "It was a dark and gloomy eventide : the sun , which had only made its appearance at intervals during the day , was fast sinking behind a heavy bank of clouds which filled the western horizon ; and the wind , which was freshening to a gale , seemed to bear the storm onward in its track , while it tore the few surviving leaves rudely from the trees , and whirled them in mazy windings", "context_len": 77}}}, "toilette": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "Her toilette would have been complete but for the fact that in her haste her hair had fallen loose from its fastenings and now flowed over her shoulders and down to her waist , black as night and as shiny as silk", "context_len": 42}}}, "thoroughness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "With typical Teutonic thoroughness the Huns had prepared for the possibility of having Messines wrenched from their hands , and , having lost the ground , they were ready to swamp it with high-explosive shells before launching a counter-attack on a large scale", "context_len": 43}}}, "officiating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The trying feature of the proceeding lay in the fact that they were in no way taken into the confidence of the party officiating , or told what move was impending", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Peggy poured out , with a grand air , as if she were officiating at some Court ceremony", "context_len": 18}}}, "condescending": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Side by side they were thrust on to a little low seat , and their shoes and pumps being quickly removed , were consigned to the keeping of a condescending boots , who , summoned by the touch of an electric bell , carried them away at arm 's length", "context_len": 50}}}, "leathers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Marie-Celeste was never more thankful in her life than that every button was on , and that Albert 's little patent leathers were just as good as new ; in fact , that nothing could be urged against those little articles of foot-wear save the grievous offence of dust from the royal garden", "context_len": 53}}}, "vigor": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 9, "context": "Their faces and hands were scrubbed with wholly unnecessary vigor , and in Albert 's case even ears , and then both children were thrust on to the little low seat again , and drawing a stool in front of them , Babette laid an elaborate manicure set open upon her lap , and gave her whole mind to the shaping and polishing of their nails -- a process in which Albert took great interest , and which was accomplished , it must be confessed , most dexterously and with great expedition", "context_len": 92}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "When walking without the unsuspected guides that serve during daylight , one side displays a little more vigor than the other , and causes a deviation from a straight course", "context_len": 30}}}, "dexterously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 87, "context": "Their faces and hands were scrubbed with wholly unnecessary vigor , and in Albert 's case even ears , and then both children were thrust on to the little low seat again , and drawing a stool in front of them , Babette laid an elaborate manicure set open upon her lap , and gave her whole mind to the shaping and polishing of their nails -- a process in which Albert took great interest , and which was accomplished , it must be confessed , most dexterously and with great expedition", "context_len": 92}}}, "reformation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "At last the little toilettes were completed , even to the reformation of Albert 's curls around an ivory curling stick ; and with embroidered dress and well-starched kilt none the worse for the decorous experiences of the morning , they emerged from the little boudoir as spick and span as from the depths of the traditional bandbox", "context_len": 58}}}, "bandbox": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "At last the little toilettes were completed , even to the reformation of Albert 's curls around an ivory curling stick ; and with embroidered dress and well-starched kilt none the worse for the decorous experiences of the morning , they emerged from the little boudoir as spick and span as from the depths of the traditional bandbox", "context_len": 58}}}, "butler": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "A round dozen serving-men with blue coats there was , not to speak of the butler and the steward , and twenty or more in the stables ; and where be 'un all gone -- gone like the leaves ! '' And he spread out his wrinkled hands with a gesture that had a touch of pathos in it", "context_len": 59}}}, "butlers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The butlers -- for there were two of them -- could not have seemed more anxious to please or more obsequious to a veritable little prince and princess ; the luncheon was delicious , and no one could possibly have been more kind and friendly than Miss Bel-more", "context_len": 48}}}, "obsequious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The butlers -- for there were two of them -- could not have seemed more anxious to please or more obsequious to a veritable little prince and princess ; the luncheon was delicious , and no one could possibly have been more kind and friendly than Miss Bel-more", "context_len": 48}}}, "disillusionize": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 71, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}}}, "perennially": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 110, "context": "So it was quite plain that these little uninvited visitors to Windsor Castle were mistaking Her Majesty for Her Majesty 's mother ! She wondered for the moment if she ought not to tell them of their absurd mistake ; and yet no -- she hardly had the right to do that either ; for had not a little conference with Her Majesty resulted in the conclusion that they would not disillusionize their little guests if they could help it ? If possible they should leave the Castle as they entered it -- the Queen of England still the dream-queen of their imagination , regal and stately always , and perennially arrayed in crown , ermine and jewels , and all the royal insignia of her office", "context_len": 127}}}, "familiarly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "And , in the mean time , what a pleasure to talk familiarly with these same bright little visitors , who more likely than not would have retired into speechless embarrassment had anyone ventured the announcement that the great Queen of England was none other than the friendly old lady with whom they were taking all the liberties of commonplace , every-day acquaintance ! And so , happily , no doubt , for their ease of mind , no one felt called upon to make the announcement", "context_len": 87}}}, "mosaic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Did these belong to her ? asked Marie-Celeste , standing in open-eyed wonder before a mosaic table , which had been cleared to make room for a quaint collection of foreign-looking , childish possessions -- a mandolin , a well-worn little missal , a remarkable doll , a necklace or two , numerous little childish trinkets , and thrown over a chair , standing close to the table , a little gown of white silk and exquisite embroidery , yellow and limp with age , but none the less dainty and lovely", "context_len": 92}}}, "mandolin": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "Did these belong to her ? asked Marie-Celeste , standing in open-eyed wonder before a mosaic table , which had been cleared to make room for a quaint collection of foreign-looking , childish possessions -- a mandolin , a well-worn little missal , a remarkable doll , a necklace or two , numerous little childish trinkets , and thrown over a chair , standing close to the table , a little gown of white silk and exquisite embroidery , yellow and limp with age , but none the less dainty and lovely", "context_len": 92}}}, "missal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Did these belong to her ? asked Marie-Celeste , standing in open-eyed wonder before a mosaic table , which had been cleared to make room for a quaint collection of foreign-looking , childish possessions -- a mandolin , a well-worn little missal , a remarkable doll , a necklace or two , numerous little childish trinkets , and thrown over a chair , standing close to the table , a little gown of white silk and exquisite embroidery , yellow and limp with age , but none the less dainty and lovely", "context_len": 92}}}, "trinkets": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "Did these belong to her ? asked Marie-Celeste , standing in open-eyed wonder before a mosaic table , which had been cleared to make room for a quaint collection of foreign-looking , childish possessions -- a mandolin , a well-worn little missal , a remarkable doll , a necklace or two , numerous little childish trinkets , and thrown over a chair , standing close to the table , a little gown of white silk and exquisite embroidery , yellow and limp with age , but none the less dainty and lovely", "context_len": 92}}}, "tings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "But who has kept all dese old tings so long , and how old are dey anyway ? asked more practical Albert , inspecting with curious , critical gaze a little necklace of hammered gold and silver which Miss Belmore had dropped into his lap as one of the few treasures his rather inquisitive touch would not damage", "context_len": 58}}}, "reprobate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "And now , said Albert modestly , considering himself master of ceremonies , please have Marie-Celeste tell what she knows first , for the suspicious little reprobate was keenly anxious to put her boasted knowledge to the test", "context_len": 38}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "They do not reprobate or punish them for any wanton act they may commit ; hence the licentiousness of both sexes up to the time of marriage , and the comparative want of discipline among warriors on their expeditions", "context_len": 39}}}, "ambassadors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Pol , on the Seine , that belonged to one of her father 's ambassadors", "context_len": 15}}}, "journal": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Besides , I wrote them all down in my journal one day on the steamer , and because I was coming here to Windsor to-day , I read them over only last night", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "Man is a sociable being , young Franklin wrote in his journal , and it is , for aught I know , one of the worst of punishments to be excluded from society", "context_len": 33}}}, "tolerated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 63, "context": "Lilian 's secret ambition was to go to Germany to study at the Leipzig Conservatoire , which Herr Frankenburg always described as the very home and abode of the spirit of music , and made her sadly envious with accounts of the wonderful concerts and operas which might be enjoyed in that favoured city , where the very street vendors would not be tolerated if they cried their wares out of tune", "context_len": 72}}}, "impersonated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Nothing could have been prettier than the wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he", "context_len": 128}}}, "acquit": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": "have been prettier than the wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he could reach", "context_len": 128}}}, "uncourtly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 126, "context": "wholly unconscious way in which Marie-Celeste impersonated the grandeur and dignity of the little Isabel , courtesy and all ; so that the Queen said admiringly , My dear , you are a real little queen yourself , and your kingdom must lie in the hearts of all who know you ; and Albert , anxious at once to acquit himself as most loyal of her subjects , shook his head emphatically and remarked , Marie-Celeste is a daisy , and she ought to live in a castle jus ' as fine as anybody ; and then , to prove the wealth of his devotion , he threw his two arms around her waist , which was as high as he could reach , in most uncourtly fashion", "context_len": 128}}}, "demonstration": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 54, "context": "But now there was time for little more than good-bys , and Chris , who had slipped away to harness Jennie , was at the door ; and with farewells as hearty as though they had been friends for a lifetime , Harold and Marie-Celeste climbed into the Saxon wagon , and amid much demonstration on every side were off for the Nuneham station ; but Harold wondered that Donald did not drive into Nuneham with them , and said so", "context_len": 81}}}, "crusades": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Simonette was a poor little slave brought to France from one of the crusades , and I suppose they grew more fond of each other every day , because when they came to England both were so far away from their old home", "context_len": 43}}}, "fixings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "On the way to England Richard came to meet the little Isabel at Calais , in France , and then she was escorted to London in fine style , and after that all her queen 's fixings were taken off and she was brought here to this very Castle , that was to be her home , and everybody called her Madame La Petite Reine", "context_len": 65}}}, "bonne": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Albert would have given a good deal to know what those French words meant , and wished he had not made such a row when his mother had once suggested a French bonne ; but he would not betray his ignorance for anything , and Marie-Celeste was allowed to proceed uninterrupted", "context_len": 51}}}, "canons": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "The canons of the cathedral , the priests of the other churches , the sheriff of the county , the reeve of the borough , the burgesses , all had their places , and the banquet began ; huge joints being carried round to each individual , from which , with his dagger , he cut what he fancied and deposited it on his plate ; then wine , ale , and mead were poured foaming into metal tankards , and lighter delicacies followed", "context_len": 84}}}, "insurrection": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "They had been the staunchest Indian supporters of the new British colony , and had sent 28 men of auxiliary troops to Colonel Barnwell , to defeat the Tuscarora insurrection on the coast of North Carolina ( 1712-13 ) , when they suddenly revolted on April 15th , 1715 , committed the most atrocious deeds against helpless colonists , and showed themselves to be quite the reverse of what their name indicates ( y\u00e1masi , y\u00e1massi , the Creek term for mild , gentle , peaceable [ 44 ] )", "context_len": 90}}}, "tournament": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "But very few came to the tournament , for there were very few who really cared for the King , and it was all a failure , and the Castle seemed a very sad place for La Petite Reine , because the King was going away", "context_len": 46}}}, "ting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "And now , said Albert , appealing to the Queen , for he felt that quite too much was being taken for granted , will you please tell me what is a tilt-yard ? and what it was dat de knignts would not tome to ? and what was dat little white ting of the Queen 's dat de King carried ? and impatiently as Marie-Celeste brooked the interruption , there was nothing for it but to wait while Her Majesty explained that the tilt-yard was a sort of riding-school for the knights , where they practised for the tournaments , and that the tournaments were occasions when the knights , spear in hand , came together to ride against each other , with a great many people", "context_len": 128}}}, "tournaments": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "unseated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 118, "context": "was being taken for granted , will you please tell me what is a tilt-yard ? and what it was dat de knignts would not tome to ? and what was dat little white ting of the Queen 's dat de King carried ? and impatiently as Marie-Celeste brooked the interruption , there was nothing for it but to wait while Her Majesty explained that the tilt-yard was a sort of riding-school for the knights , where they practised for the tournaments , and that the tournaments were occasions when the knights , spear in hand , came together to ride against each other , with a great many people looking on , and when the one who unseated all those who rode against him won the prize", "context_len": 128}}}, "allay": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 37, "context": "And then somebody -- that is , the Queen -- ( who , as you know , was a good deal more of a somebody than Albert gave her credit for ) -- endeavored at once to allay the little fellow 's impatience", "context_len": 43}}}, "deanery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "After that he led her into the deanery -- those are rooms set aside for different uses in connection with the chapel -- and there he gave her a royal box of candies , and sat down and ate some with her , and tried to joke with her , and sipped a little wine , and then another long farewell , and he was gone , never to see the little Queen again", "context_len": 74}}}, "chronicles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The disconnected remarks on the Alibamu Indians which we find in the documents and chronicles represent them as early settlers on Alabama river , at a moderate distance from the confluence of Coosa and Tallapoosa rivers", "context_len": 36}}}, "partings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The partings from Richard and her governess Madame de Coucy , had thrown the child into a fever , and Richard 's uncle , the Duke of York , in whose care she had been left , was at his wit 's ends to know what to do", "context_len": 48}}}, "dethroned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Madame de Coucy , meanwhile , started for France posthaste , and was the first to carry the news to the court of Charles that Madame Isabel had been captured and dethroned , and then you may be sure all France was up in arms , as they say , in a moment , threatening to avenge La Petite Reine", "context_len": 60}}}, "respecter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Well , de Queen 's a daisy too , den , ventured Albert , who , alas ! was no respecter of persons", "context_len": 23}}}, "blushing": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Hush , Albert , said Marie-Celeste , blushing , but very thankful that Miss Belmore and the Queen 's mother seemed more amused than shocked ; and then she added , amid deeper blushes , Oh , will you please tell Her Majesty for me that I never could thank her enough , never ?", "context_len": 55}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 66, "context": "He waited with all a fisherman 's patience , and , used as he was to such scenes , he could not help feeling gladdened at the glorious sight that met his gaze , for , one by one , the stars had paled , till only that named after the morning shone out resplendent in the now grey west ; while to eastward all was blushing with bright red and gold and purple and orange , tints so wondrously beautiful and rich that Nature had enough to spare for sea as well as sky", "context_len": 95}}}, "chopper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "But Huntington got what he deserved at last ( and the smile of grim satisfaction with which Marie-Celeste adorned the statement showed how simply enormous to even her childish mind seemed the crimes of the fiendish Huntington ) , for after he deserted Isabel he fell into the hands of some peasants , who knew what a wretch he was , and who took him and drove a chopper through his neck , and so made an end of him", "context_len": 80}}}, "fingered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "She still loved the weak , bad man , older than her own father , who had fed her on sugar-plums , called her his little sister , fingered her mandolin , and sung with her at morning mass", "context_len": 39}}}, "outrider": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "There one of the court carriages , with a gallant outrider , stood in waiting , and the footman , after receiving directions as to the whereabouts of the Little Castle , sprang to his place , and they were off", "context_len": 41}}}, "footman": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 20, "context": "Yes , there was a coach with lamps on both sides , two horses and a driver , besides a footman who helped them in with a fine air , and drove off as gay as if it was Christmas night , though it were really March", "context_len": 47}}}, "conjecturing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Harold , conjecturing that the children might be sent home in this courtly fashion , was on hand on the steps to receive the favored recipients of royal hospitality", "context_len": 29}}}, "recipients": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Harold , conjecturing that the children might be sent home in this courtly fashion , was on hand on the steps to receive the favored recipients of royal hospitality", "context_len": 29}}}, "accusingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "You are very unkind , Harold , answered Marie-Celeste accusingly", "context_len": 10}}}, "mortifying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 60, "context": "But that this innocent little child , to whom she had been so unkind , should see for herself that she had acted in an improper way , and one that was only fit for the kitchen or stable , and should tell her so , and show such surprise at hearing her call herself a lady , was very mortifying , and she could not forget it", "context_len": 68}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Bradford , it was very mortifying for her ; but what was to be done ? Nothing , just nothing , as far as Aunt Patty was concerned", "context_len": 28}}}, "foggy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a foggy day , and very little light came into the cellar ; but what fitful rays did penetrate the gloom fell upon a very white and sunken little face", "context_len": 32}}}, "liveried": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "The little buttons who keeps guard during the day at the door of the Reform Club and the smartly liveried Irishman who takes his place at night would both tell you that Mr", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "The gentleman at whose house he met me at Nottingham , and who was ashamed of him , subsequently became his servant , and touched his hat to him ; and John has pulled up at my own door in his carriage , with a liveried servant , when I lived near to him in London", "context_len": 56}}}, "modified": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "The first syllable , as pronounced by the Creek Indians , contains a clear , short a , and that the name was written Muscogee and not Mascogee , is not to be wondered at , for the English language , with its surd , indistinct and strongly modified vocalization , will convert the clearest a into a u", "context_len": 59}}}, "bachelors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "They are creatures of habit these self-centred old bachelors , and it takes a great deal to start them out along any new line of action , and doubly so when , like Mr", "context_len": 34}}}, "buoyantly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Belden , they do not know what it is to feel buoyantly well and strong", "context_len": 15}}}, "infallible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "But outward appearances , as often happens , are not by any means the infallible things the world would have us believe , and deep down in Mr", "context_len": 28}}}, "unrest": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "If Jane were not true , none could be ; and the suspicion and unrest rioted back again to the bosom which belief in Jane and the world had softened and calmed", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "His mind in a whirl of unrest and longing , Bomba at last reached the river which he must cross to reach the hut of Pipina", "context_len": 26}}}, "restlessness": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` She 'll be dar ez sho ' ez de worl ' , '' he said , and his earnestness was so vital that it was the means of lifting me across a very bad place in my experience ; yet it did not cure me of the restlessness that had seized me", "context_len": 53}}}, "hansom": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "Then he turned from the window , put on his coat , which was lying in readiness beside him , strolled out from the club , called for a hansom , directing the driver to take him to the station , and never for one minute admitted to himself that he had risen a whole hour earlier in order to do this very thing , or that he was acting on any stronger impulse than that of a passing fancy , born of the midsummer day , and desire for a little variety", "context_len": 93}}}, "entrench": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Belden 's faultless attire and distinguished bearing , had rated him at once as one of those high and mighty Londoners , and had expected that he would of course entrench himself on the back seat of the little turnout and , preserving a dignified silence , condescendingly allow himself to be driven about and to be very much bored into the bargain -- all of which , it must be confessed , would have been more in keeping with Mr", "context_len": 81}}}, "turnout": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Belden 's faultless attire and distinguished bearing , had rated him at once as one of those high and mighty Londoners , and had expected that he would of course entrench himself on the back seat of the little turnout and , preserving a dignified silence , condescendingly allow himself to be driven about and to be very much bored into the bargain -- all of which , it must be confessed , would have been more in keeping with Mr", "context_len": 81}}}, "condescendingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Belden 's faultless attire and distinguished bearing , had rated him at once as one of those high and mighty Londoners , and had expected that he would of course entrench himself on the back seat of the little turnout and , preserving a dignified silence , condescendingly allow himself to be driven about and to be very much bored into the bargain -- all of which , it must be confessed , would have been more in keeping with Mr", "context_len": 81}}}, "tutored": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Oh , yes ; I tutored awhile at home -- just enough to wriggle my way into Cambridge ; and I studied just enough there to get my degree -- no more , I can tell you", "context_len": 37}}}, "imperiously": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Full of healthful energy , it was a relief to her to help the solitary maid in her household work ; that was the least dull part of her new life ; and , in the kitchen , the Queen of Hunstanton could still rule imperiously over the old cowman , and make the dairywoman tremble before her royal displeasure", "context_len": 60}}}, "hedgerow": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Belden looked in the direction indicated , and -- could he believe his eyes ! -- was there not a familiar little figure coming leisurely down the path from the Little Castle , which when it reached the gate in the hedgerow turned in the same direction as they were driving ?", "context_len": 52}}}, "paralyzing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Belden , and the carriage came to a standstill with paralyzing abruptness Now , turn your wheel and let me out", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Harris ? a paralyzing recollection coming over him", "context_len": 8}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "After the first paralyzing shock of the crash the passengers seemed to come to their senses", "context_len": 16}}}, "sexton": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "The right in there of the sexton evidently referred to Braye Chapel , within a few feet of the door by which he had entered ; and glancing in through the open-work carving of the partition enclosing it , he discovered Marie-Celeste seated on a cushion on the floor , her back against the wall , busily writing away on the portfolio on her lap", "context_len": 65}}}, "burnishing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Which way did that little girl go ? he asked of the sexton , who was vigorously burnishing a brass memorial tablet just within the doorway of the chapel", "context_len": 29}}}, "selfishly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "Here his conscience whispered that he might also have been helping his twin sister to enjoy her birthday ; and when he remembered how bravely she had concealed her own disappointment , and how unselfishly she had told him to go and spend his birthday in the manner that pleased him best , he began to see how very selfishly he had behaved", "context_len": 63}}}, "indiscriminate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Several pairs of sparring-men having displayed their prowess and received indiscriminate praise and rebuffs from their respective supporters , the event of the evening was announced", "context_len": 26}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` I must insist that , hereafter , when I , or any other member of the faculty approaches , all indiscriminate knocking or kicking of balls cease until we have passed on", "context_len": 33}}}, "partition": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "The right in there of the sexton evidently referred to Braye Chapel , within a few feet of the door by which he had entered ; and glancing in through the open-work carving of the partition enclosing it , he discovered Marie-Celeste seated on a cushion on the floor , her back against the wall , busily writing away on the portfolio on her lap", "context_len": 65}}}, "vergers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "I can finish the copying any time , and I know about it almost as well as the vergers themselves -- will you let me ? evidently afraid that he would express a preference for a professional guide", "context_len": 38}}}, "interrogative": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Belden to do aught but look and listen , and drop a word of assent now and then , when Marie-Celeste saw fit to impart her information in a somewhat interrogative form ; and in this way they went on from monument to monument , giving of course but a passing glance to many and stopping longest , by tacit agreement , at those which had some special charm or attraction for Marie-Celeste", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The verb forms a considerable number of tenses and incorporates the prefixed object-pronoun , the interrogative and the negative particle ; it has a form for the passive and one for the reflective voice", "context_len": 34}}}, "tangible": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 107, "context": "At last , when he felt that he must not delay another moment , he took leave of her , saying as he went , Well , as usual you have set me thinking , my little friend , but as though he were grateful for the same ; and Marie-Celeste , turning back to finish the copying of the Prince Imperial 's prayer , wondered in her practical little way if anything would come of the thinking , and if so , if she would ever happen to hear what it was ; and yet at the same time not a little sceptical as to any tangible result whatsoever", "context_len": 110}}}, "desirability": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The back seat was reserved for the two grooms , and a bundle of wraps and rugs strapped to the cushion between them showed that they at any rate recognized the desirability of not having too much room at their disposal", "context_len": 41}}}, "indulging": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 2, "context": "Harold was indulging in this meditation as he stood waiting by the break for the rest of the party , for thinking comes very easy when one has nothing to do ; but wise are the folk , big or little , who , like Harold , resolve to banish uncomfortable thoughts from the mind when convinced that thinking is not in the least likely to better them", "context_len": 68}}}, "insupportable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "hopefuls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 78, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "participating": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 89, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "riper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 98, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "unchristian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 109, "context": "Oh , what would he not have given if only his arrival in this world might have been timed in closer proximity to Harold 's and Marie-Celeste 's -- it was such an insupportable thing to be seven long years behind ! But , all the same , his time would come , and his little envious heart secretly cherished the revengeful hope that he , in turn , might have the grim satisfaction of informing other young hopefuls that their extreme youth and diminutive proportions excluded them from participating in this or that pleasure to which his riper age entitled him , all of which unknightly and most unchristian sentiments we trust will be put to rout when he comes to years of discretion", "context_len": 125}}}, "parenthesis": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "But the dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of", "context_len": 128}}}, "supremely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 56, "context": "But who could long be grave at the outset of so promising an expedition ! The idea of a leisurely driving trip through the lovely Lake Country , stopping here and there , as the spirit moved them , at the comfortable little inns and hotels that abound in the region , had been such a supremely delightful idea , even in mere anticipation , that now that they were actually off enthusiasm knew no bounds , and mirth was literally unconfined", "context_len": 82}}}, "unconfined": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 81, "context": "But who could long be grave at the outset of so promising an expedition ! The idea of a leisurely driving trip through the lovely Lake Country , stopping here and there , as the spirit moved them , at the comfortable little inns and hotels that abound in the region , had been such a supremely delightful idea , even in mere anticipation , that now that they were actually off enthusiasm knew no bounds , and mirth was literally unconfined", "context_len": 82}}}, "surreptitious": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "His proposal was that the three of them should pay a surreptitious visit to the school that Friday night , and , from the room of Professor Long , the science teacher , take a number of turtles , snakes and small alligators which the instructor kept for the use of his class in biology", "context_len": 55}}}, "resolutions": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "Father 's reproachful face seemed to rise up out of the darkness , asking 'Where is Bobby ? What have you done with my little boy ? ' Good resolutions made , and alas ! too often forgotten , crowded in now upon her remembrance , and as she listened to the roar of the river , she thought how strange it would seem that they two , so full of life , might in a few hours be floating very still and silent upon that flowing stream , with the world only a memory behind them", "context_len": 97}}}, "colleges": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "It seemed that in one of the colleges somebody lived who Marie-Celeste would have given more to see , next to the Queen ( and , as you know , she had seen her without the asking ) , than any one else in England , and that was the man who calls himself Lewis Carroll , and who has written those incomparable books , Through the Looking-Glass and Alice in Wonderland", "context_len": 72}}}, "berated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 65, "context": "Harris , to whom the idea had first occurred , suggested it , Marie-Celeste had put her two arms round her father 's neck , declaring he was just a darling and yet , when you come to think of it , he was the very same old curmudgeon of a papa , and not one whit altered either , who had been so soundly berated for insisting that it would be better for Donald to have some easy work to do than to idle away the whole summer", "context_len": 89}}}, "larks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was pleasant work wandering among the green meadows picking the sweet-smelling flowers , while the larks sang their loudest overhead , and the little brook babbled by on its path to the river -- more especially pleasant when they remembered that by this time next week school would have begun again , with its attendant woes of Latin grammar and French composition", "context_len": 63}}}, "unpromising": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "So down to Nuneham they went bright and early Saturday morning , and , feeling fine as a lark , or as two larks , to speak more correctly , they preferred doing the walking themselves over the mile and a half out from Nuneham to engaging a most unpromising horse attached to a little carry-all to do it for them", "context_len": 61}}}, "nor'easter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "But working with a will sometimes means nothing more than determination to do one 's duty ; and from what we happen to know , Chris would much have preferred setting cheerily forth on his round in Uncle Sam 's far-away city , and Donald was probably dreaming of the blue boundless sea and the steamer ploughing its way in the teeth of a driving nor'easter", "context_len": 66}}}, "unexplained": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "There was something I suddenly remembered that I had to see to that very minute , stammered Donald , shaking bands with Marie-Celeste and Harold at one and the same moment ; but you may just believe I 'm glad to see you and the warmth of Donald 's welcome fully atoned for the few moments of unexplained delay", "context_len": 59}}}, "unreservedly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "You see , Donald , having been taken so unreservedly into Ted 's confidence , had turned into a thorough diplomat , and had determined to aid and abet his plans in every possible way", "context_len": 35}}}, "diplomat": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "You see , Donald , having been taken so unreservedly into Ted 's confidence , had turned into a thorough diplomat , and had determined to aid and abet his plans in every possible way", "context_len": 35}}}, "abet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "You see , Donald , having been taken so unreservedly into Ted 's confidence , had turned into a thorough diplomat , and had determined to aid and abet his plans in every possible way", "context_len": 35}}}, "larder": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 70, "context": "But the stock , alas ! had accumulated a skim of green mould on the surface , and generally betrayed such symptoms of distress that it was fit for nothing but the pig-trough ; and when Lilian , warned by its awful example , visited her neglected shelves , she found so many forgotten scraps put away into odd corners that she straightway formed the excellent resolution of reviewing the larder daily after breakfast , having an uneasy sensation that it was one of the golden rules which Aunt Helen had particularly impressed upon her , but which , like many others , had slidden into the background of her remembrance", "context_len": 111}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "She chatted away as she tripped from dairy to larder ; it was a rare holiday for the lonely girl to find a companion , and a companion of her own age", "context_len": 32}}}, "flop": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "Cousin Ted ! gasped Marie-Celeste ; and flop went the cup-custard over on one side , and then rolled off of the tray altogether", "context_len": 24}}}, "sarcastically": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` Say , why did n't you put new legs on it , insert new springs , and cover it over while you were about it ? '' he asked sarcastically", "context_len": 31}}}, "hungering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Marie-Celeste , you are a dear child ; and Ted , who was hungering at last for the love of kith and kin , could not keep his eyes from growing a little misty", "context_len": 34}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "custard": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 42, "context": "Thank you , very much , as though Ted had done her the greatest personal favor ; and then , seeming to feel that their talk had come to a natural end , she asked quite casually , Will you have the custard now ? and Ted remarking quite as casually , Yes , thank you , I will , she lifted the tray carefully into his lap", "context_len": 68}}}, "spoonful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Take that last spoonful of custard quickly , please ; it 's good for you", "context_len": 15}}}, "exercising": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "It was Washington 's aim to mature and perfect organizations which would move on prosperously of themselves , without him ; and he was continually withdrawing his hand from action and control in public affairs , taking a higher pleasure in the independent working of the institutions which he had formed and protected , than in exercising , himself , a high personal power", "context_len": 64}}}, "exonerated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "But Donald afterward exonerated Ted from any lack of courage , and was of course delighted when he found that she had pleaded his cause so eloquently as to convince even the old keeper that Ted was fully justified in the course he had thought best to pursue", "context_len": 48}}}, "ups": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Never was fairy tale listened to with more rapt attention than Marie-Celeste 's narration of the ups and downs of Ted 's life as she knew them , and never was heart more gladly grateful than hers when she realized that these good friends were more than willing , for the sake of the end in view , to condone the deception practised upon them", "context_len": 65}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` You ups with an armful of net , and just as young Harry Paul comes round under you , you drops it on top of his head", "context_len": 28}}}, "condone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "Never was fairy tale listened to with more rapt attention than Marie-Celeste 's narration of the ups and downs of Ted 's life as she knew them , and never was heart more gladly grateful than hers when she realized that these good friends were more than willing , for the sake of the end in view , to condone the deception practised upon them", "context_len": 65}}}, "prosy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "Hartley 's last remark , Donald felt sure that the old keeper , of whom he , as well as Ted and Harry Allyn , stood in not a little awe , was not going to take offence at the new turn affairs had taken ; while Harold , to whom it sounded as though they had been having a somewhat prosy sermon , rather congratulated himself that Donald had carried him off to see a neighbor 's kennels down the river", "context_len": 82}}}, "overlong": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Yes , he has , answered Chris , with a look just as knowing , for both were well aware that as soon as their backs were turned Donald would fly to Ted 's rescue from his overlong quarantine down under the apple-tree , and all the significant glances went on right under Harold 's eyes , with never a suspicion on his part", "context_len": 64}}}, "quarantine": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Yes , he has , answered Chris , with a look just as knowing , for both were well aware that as soon as their backs were turned Donald would fly to Ted 's rescue from his overlong quarantine down under the apple-tree , and all the significant glances went on right under Harold 's eyes , with never a suspicion on his part", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Now I think I will have to establish a quarantine , '' declared Dr", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Having made up his mind to see the quarantine establishment at Marseilles , Howard made his way through France , though he was so feared and disliked by the Government that he was warned if he were caught in that country he would be thrown into the Bastille", "context_len": 48}}}, "relying": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "Indeed , Chris and Marie-Celeste , just for the fun of it , indulged in some decidedly pointed remarks , relying ( and in Harold 's case with considerable risk ) upon the literalness of the average boy of sixteen to let their real meaning escape him", "context_len": 47}}}, "literalness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Indeed , Chris and Marie-Celeste , just for the fun of it , indulged in some decidedly pointed remarks , relying ( and in Harold 's case with considerable risk ) upon the literalness of the average boy of sixteen to let their real meaning escape him", "context_len": 47}}}, "revelations": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "How his quick wit saved him ; the terrible dangers to which his indomitable spirit refused to yield , and which he finally surmounted ; how he found Sobrinini at last on her island of snakes and brought her back with him , only to be tantalized with imperfect revelations that made it still necessary to hunt out Japazy on Jaguar Island is told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled , Bomba , the Jungle Boy , at the Giant Cataract ; or , Chief Nascanora and His Captives", "context_len": 92}}}, "litter": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "But Donald was mistaken ; one of a litter of rather young setter puppies , but with the sense of scent well developed , had scaled the sides of the low feed-box , and now lay on its side by the empty plate , feeling somewhat the worse for its foraging expedition", "context_len": 52}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "Finding that a success , Lilian followed it up by 'Pride and Prejudice , ' and the 'Pickwick Papers ' ; for she was glad to discover anything that would wipe away the lines from Father 's forehead , if only for an hour , and distract his thoughts from those terrible deeds and account-books which were wont to litter the dining-room table at nights", "context_len": 65}}}, "puppies": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 13, "context": "But Donald was mistaken ; one of a litter of rather young setter puppies , but with the sense of scent well developed , had scaled the sides of the low feed-box , and now lay on its side by the empty plate , feeling somewhat the worse for its foraging expedition", "context_len": 52}}}, "reviving": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But dinners are not so reviving as good news , Donald , said Ted excusingly ; and indeed , notwithstanding diminished rations , he felt wonderfully toned up both in mind and body , now that the good friends in the cottage knew just who he was and there was no longer need for any sort of duplicity", "context_len": 58}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "As he was being carried out of the ship he swooned , and reviving again desired the company to pray for him", "context_len": 22}}}, "duplicity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "But dinners are not so reviving as good news , Donald , said Ted excusingly ; and indeed , notwithstanding diminished rations , he felt wonderfully toned up both in mind and body , now that the good friends in the cottage knew just who he was and there was no longer need for any sort of duplicity", "context_len": 58}}}, "divulge": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "If you think it easy to be sole possessor of a piece of news that would rejoice the hearts of your nearest and dearest , and yet for extreme precaution 's sake have given your promise on no account to divulge it , why then all that can be said is that you were never in Marie-Celeste 's shoes", "context_len": 59}}}, "legibly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "If it is possible that any little friend of these stories of mine has never happened to have read them , then let me urge you at once to give Aunt Bess or Uncle Jack no rest till both are in your keeping , with your name written very legibly across the fly-leaf of each , so that you can keep them for your very own till you 've no more use for any books whatsoever", "context_len": 76}}}, "spires": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was only two o'clock when they set out , and a walk up the beautiful High Street , past the spires and domes , brick windows and massive gateways of the old churches and colleges that line it , and then a turn at the corner of Aldgate Street , soon brought them to Christ Church", "context_len": 57}}}, "domes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "It was only two o'clock when they set out , and a walk up the beautiful High Street , past the spires and domes , brick windows and massive gateways of the old churches and colleges that line it , and then a turn at the corner of Aldgate Street , soon brought them to Christ Church", "context_len": 57}}}, "gateways": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "It was only two o'clock when they set out , and a walk up the beautiful High Street , past the spires and domes , brick windows and massive gateways of the old churches and colleges that line it , and then a turn at the corner of Aldgate Street , soon brought them to Christ Church", "context_len": 57}}}, "carroll": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Carroll to those of us who know him only through his books --", "context_len": 13}}}, "looms": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Over this entrance looms the beautiful tower containing Great Tom , an old , old bell that tolls a curfew of one hundred and one strokes every night as a signal for the closing of the college", "context_len": 37}}}, "curfew": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Over this entrance looms the beautiful tower containing Great Tom , an old , old bell that tolls a curfew of one hundred and one strokes every night as a signal for the closing of the college", "context_len": 37}}}, "quadrangles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "In many cases the walls of the buildings which surround the quadrangles on the four sides are almost hid beneath a luxurious growth of English ivy , while from April to December the lawns that carpet them are green with the wonderful depth of color peculiar to lawns that have been cultivated for centuries", "context_len": 54}}}, "surround": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "In many cases the walls of the buildings which surround the quadrangles on the four sides are almost hid beneath a luxurious growth of English ivy , while from April to December the lawns that carpet them are green with the wonderful depth of color peculiar to lawns that have been cultivated for centuries", "context_len": 54}}}, "janitor": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 26, "context": "Carroll 's rooms open on the Ton Quad , as it is called , because of the nearness to Great Tom , and they found the janitor , who had been informed of their coming , ready to unlock the door for them", "context_len": 43}}}, "injunction": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "The speeches were over at length , and a last farewell round of the ruins having been made , the society finally took its departure , with the intention of viewing the church , and an injunction for all the members to assemble for tea at the Bluebell Arms at half-past four precisely", "context_len": 53}}}, "wizard": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "But , all the same , their faith in the result was supreme ; for what could a wizard not do who could weave fairy-tales so wonderfully as not to have them seem like fairy-tales at all", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Why , Stirling ! '' he exclaimed , `` are you a wizard ? ''", "context_len": 16}}}, "antipathy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "There was a deadly antipathy between the two tribes , and the head-hunters probably planned to make the Araos their victims as soon as they had made an end of Casson and himself", "context_len": 33}}}, "porti\u00e8re": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Marie-Celeste , to whom this visit meant more than to Harold and Dorothy , even made so bold as to glance through an intervening porti\u00e8re to the bachelor bedroom beyond ; and yet you must know that there was not a vestige of prying curiosity in this investigating mood of hers", "context_len": 51}}}, "investigating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "Marie-Celeste , to whom this visit meant more than to Harold and Dorothy , even made so bold as to glance through an intervening porti\u00e8re to the bachelor bedroom beyond ; and yet you must know that there was not a vestige of prying curiosity in this investigating mood of hers", "context_len": 51}}}, "pronounce": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 92, "context": "The next thing , and sometimes a better thing than knowing your favorite author , is to know how and where he lives ; and it was a matter of supreme delight to Marie-Celeste that henceforth when she should open Lewis Carroll 's books she should be able to picture him working away here in his study , and just as he really looked , too , for by chance or accidents full-length photograph stood on the mantel , which Dorothy , from her visita few years before , was able to pronounce an excellent likeness , and very characteristic", "context_len": 100}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "No plausible analysis of the name Chicasa , which many western tribes , as well as the Chicasa themselves , pronounce Shikasa , Sh\u00edkasha , has yet been suggested", "context_len": 29}}}, "considerately": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The real Pieter Waas happened to be a stranger to the rest of the Afrikanders , and , induced to desert by spy , had considerately transferred his name to his doubtful benefactor", "context_len": 33}}}, "caterpillars": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "The moment your pranks begin , the moment I hear of any more toads being put into the bread-pan , or wasps and caterpillars descending on poor James 's head , or spiders put down the housemaids ' backs , or leeches being allowed to run about your bedroom , I shall just go off", "context_len": 55}}}, "outgrowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Well , the parts I used to like best were the verses ; for Harold , after the manner of the genus who pride themselves on early outgrowing many of the best things of life , relegated the books to the days of his early childhood ; the stories themselves always seemed more meant for girls than for boys", "context_len": 59}}}, "relegated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Well , the parts I used to like best were the verses ; for Harold , after the manner of the genus who pride themselves on early outgrowing many of the best things of life , relegated the books to the days of his early childhood ; the stories themselves always seemed more meant for girls than for boys", "context_len": 59}}}, "bristling": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "The spears were so long that when the men stood in close order , the rear ranks being brought up near to those before them , the points of the spears of eight or ten of the ranks projected in front , forming a bristling wall of points of steel , each one of which was held in its place by the strong arms of an athletic and well-trained soldier", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Barking , biting , and snarling , the combatants had continually to be seized by the tails , forcibly separated , and kicked yelping back to their duties , where they stood with bristling ears , growling at each other through the hurdles , and showing their teeth like a pack of wolves", "context_len": 53}}}, "imputation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "Oh , Miss Dorothy , you know what I mean ; it is n't that I do n't like girls , it 's only that a book like 'Alice ' ought to have just as much interest for boys as girls ; for all Marie-Celeste had in mind was the defence of the imputation that Lewis Carroll 's books were just girls ' books", "context_len": 64}}}, "tirade": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Oh , well , you need n't be quite so hard on me , Harold replied , improving the first opportunity to put in a word , and very much amused at Marie-Celeste 's little tirade", "context_len": 36}}}, "choral": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "At five o'clock promptly the beautiful choral service began , and the sweet music and the earnest spirit of the service seemed to round out to a fitting close that always to be remembered Sunday afternoon in Oxford", "context_len": 38}}}, "delving": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Besides , Harry Allyn , who , as you know , is in dead earnest about his new leaf , is up at Oxford delving away , midsummer though it is , at some back work that was sadly neglected in the spring term , and has actual need to be made up", "context_len": 53}}}, "rake": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "The lumps were so closely wedged that the only way to attack the sloping wall of coal was by means of a long `` fireman 's rake ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "reservations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "No , I 'm not going to take any savings of yours , said Ted , imagining that Donald might so have understood him ; but I want to put the case to you , and have you tell me what to do ; and Donald listened attentively while Ted put his case plainly and without any mental reservations whatever", "context_len": 60}}}, "customer": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "And then Ted asked , as had she : Are you sure it was Belden ? You see , Donald , he continued , I 've an old bachelor uncle whose name is Selden -- my mother 's brother -- and who answers to your description to a dot -- a surly old customer , who would do little enough for me , or any one else , I imagine", "context_len": 70}}}, "valet": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 58, "context": "My dear friend ' ( you know , Donald has to begin that way , because he did n't like to say Marie-Celeste , and so never called me anything ) , 'you will be surprised to find I am in London , and , what is more , that I have come up to London as a valet for a gentleman , and the gentleman , let me tell you , is your cousin , Mr", "context_len": 77}}}, "merge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was astonishing with what celerity Donald had seemed to merge the sailor-boy in the farm-hand , and now in turn the farm-hand in the valet", "context_len": 26}}}, "befitted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 39, "context": "Miss Ashton came over to her with her eyes very suspiciously shining , and stooping down kissed Bessie , saying , `` You blessed child ! '' while Maggie , always readily moved to tears or smiles , as befitted the occasion , put her arms about Bessie 's neck , and grasping her teacher 's skirts with the other hand and laying her head against her , began to cry softly", "context_len": 72}}}, "investing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Ted , however , who , in spite of himself , had still considerable regard for appearances , could not resist the temptation of investing -- though Donald urgently protested against such extravagance -- in a suit of clothes , somewhat less conspicuous than the nautical blue jersey and wide-flapping trousers of Donald 's Sunday best , and better adapted to his new calling", "context_len": 64}}}, "valets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "As you say , sir , said Donald , who was amusing himself and Ted as well by endeavoring to acquit himself as the most respectful of valets", "context_len": 28}}}, "unpretentious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "So forth they fared together , for the little hostelry was by far too unpretentious to boast a city directory ; but the morning was so fine , notwithstanding mid-August weather , that they were tempted to stroll on and on , deferring a little , by tacit consent , the immediate object of their expedition", "context_len": 56}}}, "directory": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 19, "context": "So forth they fared together , for the little hostelry was by far too unpretentious to boast a city directory ; but the morning was so fine , notwithstanding mid-August weather , that they were tempted to stroll on and on , deferring a little , by tacit consent , the immediate object of their expedition", "context_len": 56}}}, "deferring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "So forth they fared together , for the little hostelry was by far too unpretentious to boast a city directory ; but the morning was so fine , notwithstanding mid-August weather , that they were tempted to stroll on and on , deferring a little , by tacit consent , the immediate object of their expedition", "context_len": 56}}}, "misgave": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Whereupon Ted straightway found himself feeling very much dismayed at the announcement , and his heart misgave him , as hearts have a way of doing when the time has come for mere intention to take the more definite form of action", "context_len": 42}}}, "delegated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Bring that boy back ! was his breathless older to the buttons ; but the door being open , he rushed through it himself , deciding that the matter was too important to be delegated to any one less interested than himself", "context_len": 42}}}, "momentarily": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 19, "context": "Why , yes I do , Theodore for although it was years since he had seen him , the momentarily uncovered head had at once established his identity ; but how do you and Donald happen to be in each other 's company ? Marie-Celeste told me Donald was on a farm down in Oxfordshire , and that you -- well , that nobody knew where you were exactly", "context_len": 69}}}, "gravest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "By this deed he established beyond question the fact that he is a boy of fine courage , possessing the instincts which lead him unhesitatingly to face gravest peril in defense of those who are unable to defend themselves", "context_len": 39}}}, "persuading": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 13, "context": "He inquired very earnestly after Alfgar , and I found great difficulty in persuading him to commit the matter to God , which is all that we can do ; for although the river has been dragged , the country searched , no tidings have yet been obtained , and we can only believe that the poor lad has been secretly murdered and buried , or that he has been sent away out of the country", "context_len": 76}}}, "idealist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "It is this home that lends its own special interest to the little lake , for it is the home of that grand old idealist , Ruskin", "context_len": 27}}}, "devastating": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "And the white blood in him , with its inheritance of taste and culture and love of the beautiful , as well as the little knowledge of wider things that Casson , before that devastating gun explosion , had given him , told him that he was in a treasure house", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was a devastating storm that for the time subdued all other forces of the jungle", "context_len": 16}}}, "improvements": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 45, "context": "We shall still have to work hard and live carefully , but now that there will not be the continual drain of the interest on the mortgages going out , I shall be able to put more into the land , and carry out the improvements I have been longing to make for years , and in time the estate will pick up , so that I hope when I go to take my place among the old Crusaders in the transept I shall hand it on to the next generation of Vaughans in better condition than I found it", "context_len": 100}}}, "musings": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "But there was no room in Dorothy 's mind just then for musings either on nature or Ruskin , and it is to be feared that the dancing blue of the water and the purple shadows on the hills and golden glow of the sunset made little impression on her wholly preoccupied mind", "context_len": 53}}}, "theodore": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Theodore 's own uncle ? His name was printed Belden by mistake on the passenger list , and when Mr", "context_len": 20}}}, "selden": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Selden", "context_len": 1}}}, "harold": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Harold be surprised to find his brother in the Little Castle ready to receive him ! Mr", "context_len": 17}}}, "mutually": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "Oh , yes , I understand perfectly ; and you understand too , Miss Dorothy , how I would have told you about it long ago , if I thought I could and everything at last being mutually understood , there was happily no need for further explanations", "context_len": 48}}}, "waterproofs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Everything was lovely and just as it should be , even to the pelting showers that came down with such swift suddenness as to almost soak them through before they could get under cover of waterproofs and umbrellas , and then a moment after left them stranded in brilliant sunshine , fairly steaming within the rubber coats which , with much difficulty , had but just been adjusted", "context_len": 68}}}, "laming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "But just as they were rolling into Grasmere , the off-leader , with the total depravity peculiar to animal nature , struck the only stone visible within a hundred yards on that perfect roadway , laming himself instantly and in most pronounced fashion", "context_len": 43}}}, "halts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "This chanced to be the first mishap ; but then could you really call an accident a mishap that simply necessitated a three-days ' stay in the beautiful Wordsworth district ? Our sunshiny little party , at any rate , chose not so to regard it , and scoured the whole lovely region on foot , reading Wordsworth 's poetry in their halts by the roadside , and growing familiar with every foot of the lanes he so dearly loved", "context_len": 80}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The moment the animal sees it he bounds off in a panic , but does not go far before he halts and looks back", "context_len": 24}}}, "terraced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "The gentleman , however , when they presented themselves at his door , politely bowed them out instead of in , and they were fain to content themselves with the lesser privilege of inspecting the prettily terraced garden", "context_len": 38}}}, "depending": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "When , after the three days ' rest , the off-leader had been coaxed into proper driving condition , they started off once more , but rather late in the afternoon , planning to take things in quite leisurely fashion , out of regard for the same off-leader , and depending upon the wonderful English twilight to bring them into Keswick before ten o'clock", "context_len": 64}}}, "houseless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "It happened to be a local holiday in Cumberland , and as a result here and there they encountered a solitary specimen of humanity prone upon his back or his face , just as it chanced , by the roadside , or , not quite so badly off as that , reeling along to wherever home might be in that apparently houseless region", "context_len": 63}}}, "contingent": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was exceedingly interesting to the American contingent of the party to gain a little insight into the life of the English navvies ; and they passed the little tap-room , reeking with smoke and smelling of pipes and beer mugs , rather more often than circumstances would warrant , for the sake of looking in on the jolly fellows , and catching a sentence or so of their almost unintelligible dialect", "context_len": 72}}}, "navvies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It was exceedingly interesting to the American contingent of the party to gain a little insight into the life of the English navvies ; and they passed the little tap-room , reeking with smoke and smelling of pipes and beer mugs , rather more often than circumstances would warrant , for the sake of looking in on the jolly fellows , and catching a sentence or so of their almost unintelligible dialect", "context_len": 72}}}, "mugs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "It was exceedingly interesting to the American contingent of the party to gain a little insight into the life of the English navvies ; and they passed the little tap-room , reeking with smoke and smelling of pipes and beer mugs , rather more often than circumstances would warrant , for the sake of looking in on the jolly fellows , and catching a sentence or so of their almost unintelligible dialect", "context_len": 72}}}, "proclaims": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "A placard suspended within proclaims the fact that it is the smallest church in all England , and beneath it a contribution-box , of dimensions out of all proportion to the surroundings , invites spare shillings for the maintenance of the lonely little parish", "context_len": 44}}}, "dimensions": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "The huge cathedral church towered above the masses , rude in architecture , yet still impressive in its proportions , while another church , scarcely smaller in its dimensions , rose from the banks lower down the stream , below the bridge , and the wooden steeple of a third was visible above the roofs of the houses in the western part of the city", "context_len": 65}}}, "maintenance": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "A placard suspended within proclaims the fact that it is the smallest church in all England , and beneath it a contribution-box , of dimensions out of all proportion to the surroundings , invites spare shillings for the maintenance of the lonely little parish", "context_len": 44}}}, "roomy": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 9, "context": "The chamber occupied by the king was a long roomy place , containing a single bedstead of carved wood , surmounted by the usual distinctive canopy , from which tapestried hangings depended , and upon which scriptural subjects were woven ; the furniture of the room partook of the usual meagreness of the times", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The children were delighted as their father spoke of the wide , roomy old hall , where they might play on a rainy day , of the spacious , comfortable rooms and long piazza ; as he told how beautiful the lake looked even in this early spring weather , and of the grand old rocks and thick woods which would soon be covered with their green summer dress", "context_len": 69}}}, "churchly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Just as he is concluding the exercise of this truly churchly function , and Marie-Celeste is dropping her very last sixpence into the depths of the appealing hat , the little doorway is suddenly darkened -- -as it has need to be when any one comes through it -- and in the next second Ted is standing in their midst", "context_len": 60}}}, "unalloyed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "I hain \u2019 t been hit at all ! he exclaimed with a new thrill this time of unalloyed bliss ; it must hab been de rheumatics dat shifted to my toe", "context_len": 32}}}, "raptly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Harris were soon taken into Ted 's confidence -- the very next day , in fact , as they were sitting in the garden of the hotel at Keswick -- and listened as raptly to his narration of all that had happened these last few weeks as the little circle outside the cottage door had listened to Marie-Celeste", "context_len": 58}}}, "plausible": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 8, "context": "Miss Forster was not sorry to find so plausible an excuse for keeping her pet nephew at the Willows , and the young man himself had no objections , being fuller than ever of ingenious schemes , only he had transferred the seat of his operations to the Abbey , as offering a wider scope in the way of material , and having the further advantage of a number of appreciative assistants", "context_len": 72}}}, "credulity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Harris to defy credulity was that Ted should have fallen into the hands of the Hartleys , for in what other little cottage in all England could such a transformation have been wrought ? Where else could he have been brought into such close touch with all the old home interests as he had been there , first through Chris and afterward through Donald and Marie-Celeste , and where else could he have come to see so clearly that he had been wilfully trampling upon all that is truest and best in life ? Fritz , said Mrs", "context_len": 98}}}, "transformation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Harris to defy credulity was that Ted should have fallen into the hands of the Hartleys , for in what other little cottage in all England could such a transformation have been wrought ? Where else could he have been brought into such close touch with all the old home interests as he had been there , first through Chris and afterward through Donald and Marie-Celeste , and where else could he have come to see so clearly that he had been wilfully trampling upon all that is truest and best in life ? Fritz , said Mrs", "context_len": 98}}}, "wilfully": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 82, "context": "Harris to defy credulity was that Ted should have fallen into the hands of the Hartleys , for in what other little cottage in all England could such a transformation have been wrought ? Where else could he have been brought into such close touch with all the old home interests as he had been there , first through Chris and afterward through Donald and Marie-Celeste , and where else could he have come to see so clearly that he had been wilfully trampling upon all that is truest and best in life ? Fritz , said Mrs", "context_len": 98}}}, "scheming": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "Harris , only it seems to me that people are often in too much of a hurry to make use of the word , for you ca n't he certain until you are able to look hack upon a thing whether it was surely of God 's ordering or man 's short-sighted scheming", "context_len": 53}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Oh , dear ! '' she concluded , with a sigh of deep depression , `` I wish Lem would get back on the road in a steady job , instead of scheming at this thing and that", "context_len": 38}}}, "crossings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The destination toward which he steered , without deviation to right or left , and with great regard for economy of time and space at corners and crossings , was the Little Castle , and he marched up the path from terrace to terrace , and rang the bell with all the complacency of a drum-major", "context_len": 56}}}, "favoring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Oh , dat 's all right den ! favoring Mr", "context_len": 10}}}, "executive": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Selden stood on one side immensely entertained , for he had previously had no idea that executive ability ever made a showing at quite such an early age", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Inform the honorable captains that they will close the intervals between their ships to five hundred yards , he told his chief executive officer", "context_len": 24}}}, "investigated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "They heard that down at Arlington someone was offering them to the storekeepers at one dollar for two bushels , investigated , detected Dale Wacker peddling the peppers from factory bags , and found that his uncle , Lem , was mixed up in the affair", "context_len": 46}}}, "proprietorship": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Yes , I think I should -- that is , if you do n't mind , Albert ; for Albert 's sense of proprietorship in the Little Castle was very evident", "context_len": 31}}}, "indistinguishable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "While the wounded were being removed , a couple of Prussian Poles , who were employed as ambulance-men , placed Malcolm on a stretcher , and threw a discarded greatcoat over his legs , not realizing that he was an enemy , since the remnants of his khaki uniform were indistinguishable from the field-grey after they had been `` chromed '' by the fumes of bursting shells", "context_len": 67}}}, "trouser": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}}}, "lettering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "This brought the gold lettering partly into view , and enabled Mr", "context_len": 12}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "It was an old-fashioned affair with a simple combination attachment , and so far as Bart could make out had suffered no damage beyond having its coat of lacquer and gilt lettering burned off", "context_len": 34}}}, "confidentially": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "But do you know , very confidentially , dat I felled asleep in de church beside Timothy , so dat de garter showed , and den de children teased me awfully 'bout it , and Marie-Celeste calls me her little knight now almost always", "context_len": 44}}}, "prematurely": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Selden 's delight and amusement , Albert was a constant visitor at the Little Castle , and when Saturday came he put in an appearance at a prematurely early hour , for fear , by any chance , the driving party should reach home before the time appointed ; and as that was exactly what they did do , he congratulated himself very highly for his extraordinary forethought", "context_len": 68}}}, "forethought": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 67, "context": "Selden 's delight and amusement , Albert was a constant visitor at the Little Castle , and when Saturday came he put in an appearance at a prematurely early hour , for fear , by any chance , the driving party should reach home before the time appointed ; and as that was exactly what they did do , he congratulated himself very highly for his extraordinary forethought", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "With characteristic caution and forethought the Huns left nothing undone to safeguard their proceedings ; hence , in the case of barges using the subterranean waterway , a corporal 's guard was placed upon each during the journey through the tunnel", "context_len": 41}}}, "humoring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hardly , I think ; but humoring Albert to the extent of stepping out on to the door-step ; it is a whole hour ahead of time yet", "context_len": 28}}}, "concede": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "But sometimes there is a long way between a decree and its execution , and Bomba , despite the awful peril in which he stood , was by no means ready to concede that he was as good as dead", "context_len": 40}}}, "booked": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Harris found awaiting him was one from Chris , telling him that he and Donald were booked for the Majestic , sailing from Liverpool the first of October", "context_len": 28}}}, "moodiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "All the while , however , he was utterly uncomplaining , seeming to be bent upon making up for all the fretful moodiness of the selfish old bachelor days up in London", "context_len": 32}}}, "defiantly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "He was dressed pretty well and looked prosperous , and there was a mean sneer on his lips as he shamelessly returned the glance of the boy he had wronged , defiantly relying , apparently , on some reserved power he fancied he possessed", "context_len": 44}}}, "sadden": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "The scene with Aunt Patty , and the fear that the weather would not allow Maggie and herself to carry Uncle Ruthven 's gift to blind Willie , on which pleasure , in spite of her father 's warning , she had quite set her mind , were enough to sadden that sensitive little heart", "context_len": 55}}}, "overmastering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "What , Uncle Everett ? Harold 's interest to learn relieving for the moment the overmastering ache at his heart", "context_len": 20}}}, "souvenir": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "Its roses are bound together with a little blue garter , which she was quick to recognize , and she knows very well she has need to thank Uncle Selden for this priceless souvenir of that happy Knight-of-the-Garter party", "context_len": 39}}}, "stares": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Inclined at first to resent the interference , the man stares at Donald a moment , and then , possibly conciliated by the semi-official aspect of his sailor costume , condescends to reply :", "context_len": 34}}}, "condescends": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Inclined at first to resent the interference , the man stares at Donald a moment , and then , possibly conciliated by the semi-official aspect of his sailor costume , condescends to reply :", "context_len": 34}}}, "grandiloquently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then the Queen 's compliments , miss , and a bon voyage ! and grandiloquently delivering himself of this little speech , he presses two packages into her hands and retreats to the tender before she has at all had time to take it in", "context_len": 45}}}, "retreats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Then the Queen 's compliments , miss , and a bon voyage ! and grandiloquently delivering himself of this little speech , he presses two packages into her hands and retreats to the tender before she has at all had time to take it in", "context_len": 45}}}, "craze": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "I have always had a craze to buy doubles of everything , but Gwendolyn was so much older , I could never indulge myself", "context_len": 24}}}, "clippings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The clippings in the old scrap-book were all about the adventures of a Union scout whose name was said to be Captain Frank Leroy", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The newspaper clippings that had been preserved were queerly inconsistent", "context_len": 10}}}, "scout": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 67, "context": "There was a tall and pious-looking man , and two or three civilians who had no particular points about them ; and then there was a burly man , who sat with his hands in his pockets and did nothing but chew tobacco and gaze in the fire , uttering not one word until some of the company fell to discussing Captain Leroy , the famous Union scout", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "We 'll scout round and then take the liberty of crossing the lock bridge", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "That wonderful scout Kit Carson performed the exploit many times , when he had neither the moon nor stars to aid him", "context_len": 22}}}, "hirelings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He was `` one of Lincoln 's hirelings '' and as villanous as he was bold", "context_len": 16}}}, "insistently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "The girl graduate at once jumped to the conclusion that there was a story behind the old scrap-book , else why should it be preserved by her father , who had been a Confederate soldier ? This idea no sooner took shape than she became insistently inquisitive", "context_len": 47}}}, "skirmishes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "As for her father , the very sight of the scrap-book awoke the echoes of a hundred experiences -- long and dangerous rides in the lonely night , battles , sharp skirmishes and bitter sufferings", "context_len": 35}}}, "youngsters": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` You may sing just as much as you wish here , '' he said ; `` singing is the cry of the soul , and I hope you will teach my youngsters to warble half as well as you do -- see , they are running up a flag for you , '' and he pointed to a flagpole on the lawn in front of the house", "context_len": 68}}}, "barest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "You don \u2019 t seem to realize , said Matt , just the barest riffle of temper showing itself , that I hadn \u2019 t any intention of taking such a long ride as this when I left the Flatiron Building", "context_len": 41}}}, "accountants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "In the course of a few weeks General Maury was made commander of the Department of the Gulf , with headquarters at Mobile , where we saw service as clerks and accountants", "context_len": 32}}}, "achievements": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "We had heard that he was in the neighborhood of Chattanooga , giving his men and horses a much-needed rest ; but on the way news came to us that , in spite of his brilliant achievements in the field , he had been deprived of the choicest regiments of his brigade -- men whom he had trained and seasoned to war", "context_len": 62}}}, "regiments": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "They have worn the rock smooth , so that it glistens like glass in the morning sun , for , as if aware of the folly of urging on its regiments of well-mounted cavalry to come dashing in upon the wild white-maned sea-horses , or the more sober lines of heavy infantry in uniforms of green and blue , the sea has for countless ages bombarded Carn Du with stone-shot in the shape of great boulders", "context_len": 76}}}, "mutilation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "After this mutilation of his command , he had been ordered to Murfreesborough to recruit and organize a new brigade", "context_len": 20}}}, "contour": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "By its size and general contour , as given him by the ex-slaves , he conjectured that it must be the island of the big cats", "context_len": 26}}}, "crescent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The valley had more length than breadth , and was shaped something like a half-moon , the road following the contour of the crescent", "context_len": 24}}}, "uncomfortably": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "The day was warm , and Matt , crouched uncomfortably under the coat , was having anything but an enjoyable ride", "context_len": 21}}}, "ez": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 13, "context": "I seed two er th'ee men prowlin ' roun ' in de bushes ez I come 'long", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "You know dat las ' time we went at um ? Well , suh , I wuz shootin ' at a man right at me , an ' he knock my han ' down des ez I pull de trigger , an ' de ball cotch him right 'twix de hip an ' de knee", "context_len": 55}}}, "mo": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Marse Cally , '' he said after a while , `` I b'lieve you done got mo ' settled , sence -- dog ef I do n't b'lieve dat it 's been sence yistiddy ! I dunner wharbouts de change is , but it sho ' is dar", "context_len": 49}}}, "dan": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "It didn \u2019 t go cl \u2019 ar frough , but it \u2019 s jest as bad ; I can \u2019 t lib more dan a few minutes ; go it , Jilk !", "context_len": 34}}}, "ar": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "An ' mo ' dan dat , ef dat ar bung-shell had ' a ' hit somebody , it 'd ' a ' fetched sump'n mo ' dan blood", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "It must have drapped out , he muttered with a wild hope ; dat \u2019 s mighty qu \u2019 ar , he added ; de pain ain \u2019 t dere any more but has gone inter de big toe ob my right fut", "context_len": 43}}}, "musketry": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The air was heavy with suffocating smoke ; fragments of shell were flying with complete impartiality ; shouts , oaths , and curses punctuated the crash of steel and the rattle of musketry , as men in their blind ferocity clutched at each other 's throats and rolled in mortal combat upon the ground", "context_len": 54}}}, "strident": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Meanwhile , the little six-pounder was barking away furiously , and presently we heard a strident voice cut the morning air : `` Go and tell Freeman to put his battery right in on that gun", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Oh , what yelling and cheering there was then ! Even the voice of Bean Perkins , strident as it was , could not be heard above the others", "context_len": 29}}}, "carbines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I could hear loud commands , and shouting , and the rattle of carbines , muskets , and pistols made my ears numb -- but what happened , or when or where , I could no more tell you than the babe at its mother 's breast", "context_len": 47}}}, "inflamed": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 86, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "His mind having thus become inflamed with suspicion , he was ready to believe anything against the missionaries , or to invent something if necessary", "context_len": 25}}}, "bloodshot": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "He seemed to be so weak and wavering in all his movements , his eyes were so bloodshot , and his utterance so thick , that Joe thought he must have been drinking ; but he had not been , -- at any rate , not that morning", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The native writhed and twisted , the eyes bloodshot with terror at the threat", "context_len": 14}}}, "courier": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "The pause after the arrival of the courier was just long enough for the saddle and mail pouches to be transferred , when the relief sped away for the next station , and if all went well , completed his task in schedule time", "context_len": 44}}}, "bulge": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "What you all laughing at ? What \u2019 s de matter wid you , Tony Burke ? If yo \u2019 doan \u2019 shet up straight off , I \u2019 ll frow you down so hard you \u2019 ll make a bulge on toder side de yarth", "context_len": 46}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Jethro \u2019 s eyes seemed to bulge more than ever", "context_len": 10}}}, "harried": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Then Edmund retired into Wessex to raise more troops , and during his absence the Danes took the offensive again , once more besieging London in vain , while they harried all the neighbouring districts until Edmund returned with a large army , drove them into Kent , and gave them such a fearful defeat at Otford that they fled in despair to the Isle of Sheppey , and all men said Edmund would have destroyed them utterly , had not Edric persuaded him to stop the pursuit at Aylesford", "context_len": 90}}}, "scouts": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 0, "context": "Scouts were coming in to make reports at all hours of the night , so that it was practically true , as one of the old campaigners remarked , that a horse could n't whicker in the enemy 's camp `` but what the General 'd hear it sooner or later", "context_len": 51}}}, "campaigners": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Scouts were coming in to make reports at all hours of the night , so that it was practically true , as one of the old campaigners remarked , that a horse could n't whicker in the enemy 's camp `` but what the General 'd hear it sooner or later", "context_len": 51}}}, "whicker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Scouts were coming in to make reports at all hours of the night , so that it was practically true , as one of the old campaigners remarked , that a horse could n't whicker in the enemy 's camp `` but what the General 'd hear it sooner or later", "context_len": 51}}}, "courtesies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "This short story of 180 pages is captivating from start to finish , and the masculine reader takes off his hat , and the feminine reader courtesies , to this matron of the tubs , with her homely heroism and true kindness of heart", "context_len": 44}}}, "forebears": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "But he seemed satisfied when Harry told him that the negro 's forebears for many generations back had belonged to the Herndons", "context_len": 22}}}, "ford": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 67, "context": "`` This horsepath began at the Rock Landing on Ok\u00f3ni river , a British post just below Wilkinson and about four miles below Milledgeville , Georgia , passed Fort Hawkins built upon the Okm\u00falgi old fields , then the site of Macon , on the shore opposite , then Knoxville , then the old Creek agency on Flint river , then crossed Patsil\u0101\u00edka creek , the usual ford on Chatahuchi river lying between Kas\u00ed ta and Apat\u00e1-i Creek", "context_len": 78}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "The first thing they did was to ford the Goomtee , a river about a hundred yards wide , and four or five feet deep", "context_len": 25}}}, "brisker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "This done , we swung into the road that had been taken by the Federals and went forward at a somewhat brisker pace", "context_len": 23}}}, "nigger": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "A nigger will sell his immortal soul for ten dollars , and I 'll git him to leave the door open some night when he 's got a job of jiggering on the peanner and whistling with his mouth at the tavern in the woods", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "When massa go a-drivin ' in de carry-all and pair , Little piccaninnies run behind to shout Hurrah ! and stare ; Den de overseer he come up , and use his big , long lash , And say de road was neber meant to harbour nigger trash ! Shoo , shoo ! piccaninnies , etc", "context_len": 56}}}, "persistent": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 16, "context": "This was in the nature of a surprise to the Union commander , for there were persistent reports that Forrest had passed on the other road , with the evident intention of harrying the Federals at a point where they had no intention of crossing", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "She made no reply , and I think she would have maintained silence the rest of the way but for my persistent chattering", "context_len": 23}}}, "harrying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 82, "context": "`` You forget that when the last invasion of our pagan foes was over , it was agreed in the Witan that a set of beacons should be prepared , in readiness to fire , on the tops of the hills , and that if the Danes appeared again , they should be fired everywhere , in which case Mercia was to hold herself in readiness to come to the aid of Wessex or East Anglia , whichever the foe might be harrying", "context_len": 83}}}, "detaching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "So well assured was he that these reports were trustworthy that he was seriously considering the advisability of detaching a force sufficiently large to capture the Confederate", "context_len": 27}}}, "fordable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was higher than it had been when he crossed it three or four days before , but still fordable ; but as his advance guard began to cross , Freeman 's battery , operated by young Morton , opened on them from the ambuscade in which it had been concealed", "context_len": 51}}}, "operated": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 25, "context": "[ A ] In 1859 , Ben Holladay had sixteen large steamers running between San Francisco and Panama , Oregon , China and Japan , operated 5,000 miles of daily stage coaches , with 500 coaches and express wagons , 500 freight wagons , 5,000 horses and mules with oxen beyond counting", "context_len": 52}}}, "ambuscade": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "On the third or fourth day of the feast he took him to hunt in a wood where he had prepared an ambuscade , and while all the rest were engaged in the chase , the common hangman of Shrewsbury , one Godwin `` port hund , '' or the town 's hound , bribed by Edric to commit the crime , sprang from behind a bush , and foully assassinated the innocent ealdorman", "context_len": 74}}}, "diametrically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But Forrest , looking at the matter from a diametrically opposite point of view , knew that the thing to do was to prevent the capture of the battery , and so he increased the pressure upon the Federal rear to such an extent that his opponent had no time to attend to the Confederate battery", "context_len": 56}}}, "foreseen": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` It has been a hard fight , old friend , '' said the familiar king , `` but we have conquered ; for my part , I would far sooner have stood out against him , battle-axe in hand , than have met this struggle , could I have foreseen it beforehand ; but now I have given him the kiss of peace , peace it must be ; he has no more to dread from me", "context_len": 78}}}, "floundered": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "At first they had all floundered hopelessly about , and it had taken some practice even to slide a few steps ; but long before they had reached the stage of skimming over the frozen surface at the rate of eight or ten miles an hour , which had been their fond ambition , the snow had melted into moist and dirty slush , which was particularly trying , as they had just decided to make a sledge out of an old packing-case , and drag Lilian round the pasture", "context_len": 90}}}, "demoralized": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "They floundered across the river as best they could , and if they had not been American troops they would have been demoralized and rendered useless for fighting purposes ; but , being what they were , they showed their courage on many a hard-fought field as the war went on", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Fresh divisions were hurried up to relieve the wornout and demoralized troops , whose moral had , been badly shaken by the stupendous explosion under Messines Ridge , and the fierce infantry attacks that succeeded it", "context_len": 36}}}, "emphasized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "He had emphasized the fact that it was dangerous work for the Federals to raid Northern Alabama while he was in striking distance , and he had captured army stores and secured horses that were comparatively fresh", "context_len": 37}}}, "flintlock": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The most welcome capture was the arms , for many of his men were armed with flintlock muskets", "context_len": 18}}}, "han": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 69, "context": "`` I know 'd they 'd crowd us out'n house an ' home ef they got a chance ; I could ' a ' took oath to it ! Cuss 'em , an ' contrive 'em , both sides on 'em , all an ' similar ! They 'd as lief make a hoss-stable out'n the house as not , an ' I built it wi ' my two han 's", "context_len": 71}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Dat 's so , suh , but I tuck notice dat you helt han 's wid 'im a mighty long time", "context_len": 22}}}, "dunner": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Marse Cally , '' he said after a while , `` I b'lieve you done got mo ' settled , sence -- dog ef I do n't b'lieve dat it 's been sence yistiddy ! I dunner wharbouts de change is , but it sho ' is dar", "context_len": 49}}}, "pallet": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 8, "context": "Richard Dewey had laid himself down on the pallet , thinking that Bill Mosely had gone about his business , when Tom Hadley , who had been assigned to this duty by his more timid companion , threw himself upon the invalid and overpowered him", "context_len": 45}}}, "remedies": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "And these suspicions were confirmed when , after you had been carried away and imprisoned , my father began to mend , even before the arrival of Mafuta upon the scene , while it seemed extraordinary to me that the witch-doctor should know so well the character of my father 's ailment , that he was able to bring with him precisely the right remedies for administration", "context_len": 67}}}, "contraband": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Medicine , strange to say , had been declared a contraband of war by the Federals , and the surgeon could spare but a driblet of quinine from his small supply ; but he left some , and gave various directions with respect to the possible symptoms that might arise", "context_len": 50}}}, "driblet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Medicine , strange to say , had been declared a contraband of war by the Federals , and the surgeon could spare but a driblet of quinine from his small supply ; but he left some , and gave various directions with respect to the possible symptoms that might arise", "context_len": 50}}}, "sech": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Now it \u2019 s no use to raise sech a fuss , young feller ; you might jest as well take your medicine quiet", "context_len": 24}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` You run away home , and never mind yer fate ; it 'ull come h'all in good time ; and babies have no cause to know sech things", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "I say it \u2019 s just inhuman to treat the poor boy in sech a fashion ! You ain \u2019 t fed him , y \u2019 u say ; y \u2019 u ain \u2019 t even found out if he \u2019 s hongry an \u2019 starvin \u2019", "context_len": 47}}}, "fer": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 20, "context": "I wuz comin ' atter you anyhow , but Marse Harry holla 'd at me an ' tol ' me fer ter fin ' you an ' say dat de troops gwineter move in de mornin ' an ' our comp'ny starts fust", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "I 'm goin ' ter stay up fer hours an ' hours , an ' if yer do n't keep right on tellin ' stories quick , I 'll holler , an ' that 'll make mar mad , an ' then she 'll send par up with a stick ter beat me", "context_len": 53}}}, "upbraid": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "Bishop , appealed to these simple savages ; a sentimental ditty , such as `` The Anchor 's weighed '' or `` Tom Bowling , '' would hold them breathless and entranced ; `` Rule , Britannia ! '' or `` Should He upbraid '' set them quivering with excitement ; and they seemed to know by intuition that `` The Sailor 's Hornpipe '' was written to be danced to , and they danced to it accordingly a wild , furious , mad fandango in which the extraordinary nature of the gambols of the performers was only equalled by the ecstasy of their enjoyment", "context_len": 105}}}, "pencilled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "We delayed our departure on one excuse and another , until finally Bill , who was to be our guide , grew irritable ; and even then we made a further delay while Jack pencilled a note to his colonel , which Harry was to take charge of as long as there was danger of his capture by roving bands of Federals , and then it was to be given to the guide , who thought he could insure its delivery", "context_len": 81}}}, "roving": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 58, "context": "We delayed our departure on one excuse and another , until finally Bill , who was to be our guide , grew irritable ; and even then we made a further delay while Jack pencilled a note to his colonel , which Harry was to take charge of as long as there was danger of his capture by roving bands of Federals , and then it was to be given to the guide , who thought he could insure its delivery", "context_len": 81}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Their roving disposition impelled them to leave this settlement also , and to migrate to the fertile Alachua plains , where they built Cuscowilla on the banks of a lake , and had to defend it against the attacks of the Tomocos , Utinas , Calloosas ( ? ) , Yamases and other remnant tribes of Florida , and the more northern refugees from Carolina , all of whom were helped by the Spaniards", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Lem had such a fancy for a roving life , that this was thought the best thing for him ; but just now even this promised pleasure was lost sight of in his grief at the loss of his kind friend", "context_len": 41}}}, "whinnied": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Alden had taken a minute or two to caress his pony , which whinnied with pleasure at meeting him , but the master was in too great anguish to pay the animal the attention he would have paid in any other circumstances", "context_len": 42}}}, "smarten": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The guide suggested that we smarten up our gait , and we put the horses to a canter", "context_len": 18}}}, "stirrup": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "I thought surely that the man would give out , but he merely caught hold of my stirrup to help him along , and when we came to a cross-road , and halted at his suggestion , he showed as little fatigue as the horses -- this man who seemed too frail to walk a mile", "context_len": 56}}}, "whopped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` I 'll be whopped , '' he exclaimed , `` ef I do n't hear buggy-wheels , an ' they 're comin ' right this way", "context_len": 27}}}, "pickle": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 63, "context": "She was accompanied by a very clean-looking officer on horseback , and he , in turn , was followed by a small escort of cavalry -- I did not take the trouble to count them , for my eyes were all for the lady ; and it was left to Harry Herndon to realize the fact that we were in something of a pickle should the officer take advantage of the position in which he found us", "context_len": 77}}}, "carbine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I had just time to shift my carbine to the front under my overcoat and loosen the flap of my holsters when the lady drove up", "context_len": 26}}}, "cucumber": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "' I have n't seen the colour of a ten-dollar bill in as many years , and you put it in your pocket as cool as a cucumber , and go about looking as glum as a herring", "context_len": 38}}}, "accusations": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "The colour which his unlucky presence among the congregation at Coleman Street on the day of General Harrisons execution , had given to his enemy 's accusations , had made his position perilous in the extreme , for General Monck , and his other secret friends , considered that he had wilfully disregarded their warnings , and were not inclined to exert their influence in his behalf", "context_len": 67}}}, "commandment": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 87, "context": "`` And she said to him , `` O my son , the Sultan received me with all courtesy , according to his wont , and meseemeth he meant fairly by us , but [ for ] thine accursed enemy the Vizier ; for that , after I had bespoken the Sultan in thy name , even as thou badest me , reminding him that the term for which he had appointed us was past and saying to him , 'If Thy Grace would vouchsafe to give commandment for the marriage of thy daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour with my son Alaeddin , ' -- he turned to the Vizier and spoke to him", "context_len": 113}}}, "billeted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Harry served General Forrest temporarily as a courier , while I was billeted with Captain Bill Forrest 's company of Independents , sometimes known as the Forty Thieves , owing to their ability as foragers", "context_len": 35}}}, "foragers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Harry served General Forrest temporarily as a courier , while I was billeted with Captain Bill Forrest 's company of Independents , sometimes known as the Forty Thieves , owing to their ability as foragers", "context_len": 35}}}, "ramble": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I had time to ramble about in the woods , and I took advantage of it to explore the whole countryside in the neighborhood of the camp", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "It was some time before the children thought of returning ; but presently Jim , who never cared to sit still for very long , said that they might as well be going , and added that as the rabbits had been so good they would give them an extra ramble , and take them home by the lane that ran along the top of the hill", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "They spent quite half an hour at this enthralling pastime , till a very persistent cuckoo in a little copse over the hedge recalled them to the principal object of their ramble", "context_len": 32}}}, "pickets": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Thereupon they fenced in the fields selected for planting by means of rails and pickets , so as to prevent the wild animals from eating the maize-crop , and apportioned some of the land to each family [ 145 ] in the tribe", "context_len": 43}}}, "gagged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "There , packed in the stifling holds like sardines , eighteen thousand Jap infantrymen gagged and groaned", "context_len": 17}}}, "detailing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "I confess that I have no pleasure in detailing the schemes of Orton Campbell , who seems to me a very disagreeable character , but it seems necessary", "context_len": 28}}}, "mammy": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` And to think of him , '' she said , wiping the tears of joy from her eyes , -- '' to think of him asking for his old mammy 'most before he had done with his greetings to the gentlefolks ! And him putting his arm about me and giving me a kiss as hearty as he used when he was a boy ; and him been all over the world seein ' all sorts of sights and doin 's", "context_len": 82}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Shoo , shoo ! piccaninnies must n't come near ! Do n't want no little piccaninnies 'way down here ! Ole mammy callin ' you , de melon-beds among -- Shoo ! little piccaninnies , 'way ! go 'long !", "context_len": 40}}}, "jeans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "As everything had been arranged to my hand I was soon going about the camp and the town arrayed in jeans clothes and looking like anything but a soldier", "context_len": 29}}}, "groomed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "I had thought to surprise Whistling Jim , the negro , with my garb , but , as it turned out , the surprise was mine , for that night , when I went to see whether the horses had been properly groomed and fed , I found the door of the stable unlocked", "context_len": 54}}}, "ragamuffins": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": "Both Harry Herndon and myself had tried hard to impress the negro with the necessity of taking unusual precautions to secure the safety of the horses , for they had attracted the attention of the whole camp , which was full of questionable characters , some of whom would have answered to their names if Falstaff had appeared to call the roll of his ragamuffins", "context_len": 65}}}, "blanketed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I swallowed more than half of my irritation when I found that the horses were in their stalls , warmly blanketed , and an abundance of food before them", "context_len": 29}}}, "civilians": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "There was a tall and pious-looking man , and two or three civilians who had no particular points about them ; and then there was a burly man , who sat with his hands in his pockets and did nothing but chew tobacco and gaze in the fire , uttering not one word until some of the company fell to discussing Captain Leroy , the famous Union scout", "context_len": 68}}}, "jiggering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "A nigger will sell his immortal soul for ten dollars , and I 'll git him to leave the door open some night when he 's got a job of jiggering on the peanner and whistling with his mouth at the tavern in the woods", "context_len": 45}}}, "identifying": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` I suppose , Mr Fortescue , '' he said , when I joined him , `` you will not have very much difficulty in identifying the Virginia should we be lucky enough to fall in with her ? ''", "context_len": 40}}}, "sleet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "The rain might descend in torrents , hail , snow and sleet might batter horse and rider like fine birdshot , and the temperature might drop below zero , or throb with heat , still rider and horse who were like one creature must plunge on and ever on , so long as muscle and nerve could stand the terrific strain", "context_len": 61}}}, "suburban": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "I knew that it led to a house that had been built for a suburban home , but now , in the crowded condition of the town , was used as a tavern", "context_len": 33}}}, "surveillance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It had attracted the suspicion of General Forrest and I knew that he had placed it under the surveillance of the Independents", "context_len": 22}}}, "briskness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The two men I followed could have reached their destination in less than twenty minutes if they had gone forward with the briskness that the weather justified ; but there was an argument of some kind between them -- I judged that the stuttering man had no stomach for the part he was to play as a horse-thief", "context_len": 58}}}, "stuttering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "The two men I followed could have reached their destination in less than twenty minutes if they had gone forward with the briskness that the weather justified ; but there was an argument of some kind between them -- I judged that the stuttering man had no stomach for the part he was to play as a horse-thief", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "One of the participants was the stuttering man , whose voice I had heard before the stable-door , and at a moment when I thought that my movements would attract no attention I took advantage of the freedom of a public-house and sauntered aimlessly into the room as if I had no particular business there", "context_len": 55}}}, "civilian": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He was in civilian 's clothes , and I knew by that that he did not care to be recognized", "context_len": 20}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "With a very few exceptions the men , although still in civilian clothes , bore themselves erect , and marched in a way that would have evoked praise from an English drill sergeant", "context_len": 33}}}, "audibly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "Small boys spoke `` pieces , '' which they forgot , being audibly prompted , while the audience experienced untold pangs of sympathy and foreboding", "context_len": 25}}}, "tickles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` What tickles you , my friend ? '' the burly man asked", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "She knew Joel 's tickles , that he loved to give her , long before he sang out , `` Polly Pepper , lying down in the daytime ! Are n't you ashamed ? ''", "context_len": 35}}}, "mither": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Only the sharp eyes av a wife or a mither , ' she says , 'could pick out me husband if he stood be your side , ' she says", "context_len": 30}}}, "ut": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Do n't think ut ! '' replied the Irishman , and with that all joined in the conversation and I heard more of the perilous adventures and hair-breadth escapes of Captain Frank Leroy than you could put in a book", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` If ye 're stayin ' thinkin ' he 's a woman , do n't do ut", "context_len": 17}}}, "delusions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "It seemed that his identity was a mystery , but he was none the less a hero in men 's minds because his very existence had been called in question ; for people will hug delusions to their bosoms in the face of religion itself , as we all know", "context_len": 50}}}, "bosoms": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "It seemed that his identity was a mystery , but he was none the less a hero in men 's minds because his very existence had been called in question ; for people will hug delusions to their bosoms in the face of religion itself , as we all know", "context_len": 50}}}, "participants": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "In earlier days many warriors had several , even four or five of these titles ( tassik\u00e1ya inhotch\u00edfka ) , and when participants of a war party were present in numbers at the taking of a scalp , each of them obtained a war-title according to the report of the fight made by the pak\u0101 dsha on his return home", "context_len": 60}}}, "aimlessly": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "One of the participants was the stuttering man , whose voice I had heard before the stable-door , and at a moment when I thought that my movements would attract no attention I took advantage of the freedom of a public-house and sauntered aimlessly into the room as if I had no particular business there", "context_len": 55}}}, "overrun": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "This is not a guest-room , but we have been so overrun lately that we have had to turn it over to the public", "context_len": 24}}}, "detestable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 64, "context": "we drew nearer the tree , the stench meanwhile becoming almost overpoweringly strong , I detected fastened to the trunk of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In", "context_len": 128}}}, "furbished": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Why , mainly for comfort , '' I answered ; `` and while I am wearing them I am having my uniform , such as it is , furbished up and cleaned a bit", "context_len": 35}}}, "identification": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` She is a full-rigged ship , sir , '' said I ; `` but , as you anticipated , she is still too far off for identification", "context_len": 28}}}, "upholstering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Through long usage the upholstering on the arms of the chair had become worn , and in places the tufts of moss or horse-hair were showing", "context_len": 26}}}, "sorter": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Whistler replied that he could `` sorter '' play", "context_len": 9}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Every time I git sorter billious and downcast an \u2019 dejec \u2019 ed I look \u2019 round till I find somebody that \u2019 s wuss off than I be , an \u2019 then I take holt an \u2019 try to give them a lift , an \u2019 that cheers me up an \u2019 makes me feel thankful an \u2019 content with my lot", "context_len": 63}}}, "plantation": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 6, "context": "Good Parson Perrient had painted Providence plantation as a sort of paradise , where the liberty and toleration dreamt of by a few in England were the law for all ; but was that refuge open to him ? The good parson might be dead ; his daughter wedded to some sturdy settler , who would have no fancy for such a compromising guest as one bearing the hated name of Harrison ! To fly to New England would be but to begin his old life over again , and as Astbury truly said , What had it brought him ? What had he gained ? What had England gained by all they had done and dared ? `` If our cause was , as we thought ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "saunter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Naturally all eyes were turned on the performer , and I took advantage of that fact to rise from the rocking-chair with the roll of paper safe in my pocket , and saunter across the room in the direction of the piano", "context_len": 42}}}, "ramshackle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Leaning against a corner of the ramshackle old instrument , I drank in the melody with a new sense of its wild and melancholy beauty", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Early in the morning of the stranger 's burial , Mexicans from up the ca\u00f1on and down the creek arrived in town in ramshackle wagons , attended by dogs and colts", "context_len": 31}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But though he saw once in a while some ramshackle cabin , it proved invariably to be deserted", "context_len": 18}}}, "simulating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The foolish little song runs along for several stanzas , simulating the sound of dancing feet", "context_len": 16}}}, "invitingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The silly song struck the home note and sent it vibrating through my brain so invitingly that I was almost sorry that Whistling Jim had played it", "context_len": 27}}}, "swaggering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "He turned and went swaggering across the room , and seated himself in the rocking-chair of which I have spoken", "context_len": 20}}}, "superhuman": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 49, "context": "It would seem incredible that such a mind as his could really admit such an absurd superstition as the story of his divine origin , and we must therefore suppose that he gave this direction in order that the place of his interment might confirm the idea of his superhuman nature in the general opinion of mankind", "context_len": 57}}}, "swarthiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "He had had the color of youth in his cheeks , in spite of the swarthiness of his skin , and I had admired the combination -- your light-haired man is for everything that has a touch of the brunette -- but now he had gone white", "context_len": 47}}}, "disgorge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Memory , however , refused to disgorge the details , and I could only gaze helplessly into the fire", "context_len": 19}}}, "proprietary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "He walked across the room to my lad -- I was now beginning to feel a proprietary interest in him -- and seized him roughly by the arm", "context_len": 28}}}, "flamed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "I asked for my hat , and from one word to another we went till she flamed out at me , and said she hated me , and had a great contempt for me ; and then she fell on the floor in a faint", "context_len": 45}}}, "ferociously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Gibbering and mouthing ferociously , froth slavering from its jaws , the huge ape sprang toward Bomba and the cowering slaves", "context_len": 21}}}, "venomous": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Through an unexplainable instinct he knew where some of the women and children were sleeping , and he intended to bound in among them , strike right and left with venomous fierceness , slaying the sleepers with lightning-like quickness , and then dart away in the moonlight", "context_len": 47}}}, "simulate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "But it was only when she spoke again that I was sure -- for not even a twin brother could simulate that round and singularly mellow voice", "context_len": 27}}}, "birdlike": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` She made a birdlike movement with her head and shoulders , looked me up and down , and smiled , but she saw that I was in earnest , and the smile left her face", "context_len": 36}}}, "confidingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` I was not thinking of what she was saying , but of the warm little hand that nestled so confidingly in mine", "context_len": 23}}}, "toggery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "She had entirely recovered her self-possession , but in doing so she forgot the part she was playing , forgot that she was arrayed in the toggery of a man , and was now altogether a woman", "context_len": 37}}}, "humiliate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "I do not remember all that was said , but I tried as hard as I could to conceal from her the fact that I had discovered her sex and her identity ; I had not the least desire to humiliate her by airing my penetration", "context_len": 46}}}, "airing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "I do not remember all that was said , but I tried as hard as I could to conceal from her the fact that I had discovered her sex and her identity ; I had not the least desire to humiliate her by airing my penetration", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "There was no garden , only a low wall on which the milk-cans and most of the family crockery seemed to be taking an airing ; but a patch of potatoes and a scanty half-acre of oats lay beyond the little homestead , roughly railed off to keep out the marauding sheep", "context_len": 52}}}, "penetration": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "I do not remember all that was said , but I tried as hard as I could to conceal from her the fact that I had discovered her sex and her identity ; I had not the least desire to humiliate her by airing my penetration", "context_len": 46}}}, "fractious": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "All the same , she 's laying up misery for herself in her devotion to this fractious child ; I wish I 'd had no hand in it ! ''", "context_len": 30}}}, "shuck": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` What make you so fractious ter-night , Marse Cally ? A little mo ' an ' you 'd ' a ' shuck my head off", "context_len": 26}}}, "flo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` He wuz right knock-kneed , an ' when he walked he walked des like de flo ' wuz burnin ' his foots", "context_len": 23}}}, "foots": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` He wuz right knock-kneed , an ' when he walked he walked des like de flo ' wuz burnin ' his foots", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "If Miles owns this car , he \u2019 s the one that foots this part of the bill", "context_len": 18}}}, "uppity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Seeing that I made no reply , he went on : `` You know what I think , Marse Cally ? Dat uppity li ' l chap is des ez much a man ez you is a 'oman", "context_len": 38}}}, "li": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 29, "context": "In Hawkins ' time the natives of this place had for the larger part left the town and settled up Yuf\u00e1bi creek , and the chief , Hobo-\u00ed ` li m\u00edko , was at variance with the United States and Spanish colonial authorities", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "Chief Hop\u00f3 ` li yah\u00f3la with about 8000 Creeks adhered firmly to the Union cause , and at the head of about 800 of his warriors , aided by auxiliary troops , he defeated the Confederate party in one engagement ; but in a second action he was defeated , and with his followers fled into Kansas", "context_len": 57}}}, "rigged": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 15, "context": "I saw at a glance that the ladies were expecting us , for they were rigged out in their best , which was not very bad , considering that they had been caught between the lines with a wounded man on their hands", "context_len": 43}}}, "drastic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "He had been removed from the cabin on the river at a critical period , and , as a result , he was compelled to go through a long and drastic illness", "context_len": 32}}}, "cadaverous": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 88, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "commodious": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "The house was a very commodious country home and had evidently been built by some prosperous person whose heart and mind turned to the country after he had acquired wealth in the town", "context_len": 33}}}, "threadbare": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "The old piano stood in the corner as glossy as if it had been newly bought , and the carpets on the floor wore a clean look , though some of them were threadbare", "context_len": 34}}}, "obeisance": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "Alaeddin did that which behoveth and befitteth unto kings of obeisance and invocation and said to him , `` O our lord the Sultan , thy Grace 's munificence hath vouchsafed [ 464 ] to accord me the Lady Bedrulbudour thy daughter , albeit I am unworthy of this great favour , for that I am of the lowliest of thy slaves ; wherefore I beseech God that He keep and continue thee", "context_len": 73}}}, "childishness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "At the time , this fact did not add to my happiness , for , what with her womanliness and her childishness , she presented a problem that puzzled and dazzled me , for my mind was wofully lacking in the nimbleness necessary to follow the swift changes of her moods", "context_len": 51}}}, "friendlier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` Jane Ryder was now sure that I did not connect her with the lad -- was sure that I had not pierced her disguise , and she became at once very much friendlier", "context_len": 34}}}, "accounting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "It is a strange fancy , '' she mused ; `` there is no accounting for it", "context_len": 17}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The myths referring to the origin of nations often stand in close connection with myths accounting for the ages of the world or successive creations , with migration legends , and with culture-myths , explaining the origin of certain institutions , manufactures and arts", "context_len": 44}}}, "simpletons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Oh , men are such simpletons ! '' she exclaimed ; `` I do n't know what I should do for amusement if I did n't see the lords of creation once in a great while", "context_len": 37}}}, "lords": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 60, "context": "Eating and drinking were over , and the old gleeman , striking his harp , was beginning a song of harvest home , when in rushed the two young theows who had gone out with Alfgar and Bertric , with the startling intelligence that there was a band of Northmen lurking in the woods , who had seized their young lords , and were , they thought , bent on attacking the place", "context_len": 73}}}, "sabre": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Some one with an oath whacked me over the head with a sabre , my horse stumbled in the darkness , and down I went into chaos", "context_len": 27}}}, "authorizing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "One was a passport signed by one of the biggest men in the country , authorizing Francis Leroy to pass in and out of the Union lines at any time , day or night , and the other -- there were but two -- was some useless information with respect to the movements of the Federal forces between Murfreesborough and Memphis", "context_len": 61}}}, "windpipe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "I enjoyed the man 's discomfiture before it occurred ; I knew what a terrible splutter there would be when the stuff began to melt and run down his windpipe", "context_len": 30}}}, "blundering": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` But , General , '' I protested , `` what I did was through my blundering", "context_len": 17}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Yes , Matt was sure that Dimmock , and his daughter , and Tibbits , the man who had dashed past with McGlory in the red car , were blundering in some way", "context_len": 33}}}, "mouthing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Gibbering and mouthing ferociously , froth slavering from its jaws , the huge ape sprang toward Bomba and the cowering slaves", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "She danced about Bomba in a hideous way , shaking her shriveled fists in the air and mouthing horribly", "context_len": 19}}}, "whisperings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "There were whisperings below , and apparently a hurried consultation , and then a young woman came mincing up the stairs", "context_len": 21}}}, "chafe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "There was a pitcher of water handy , and I sprinkled her face and began to chafe her cold hands", "context_len": 20}}}, "harmed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "Young , vigorous , full of bounding life and hope , his cheering words lingered yet in the ears of the couple , and here he lay on the ground his life driven out by the arrow launched by a demon in wantonness , for Dick Lightfoot had never harmed a hair in the head of one of his kind", "context_len": 60}}}, "malice": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` For our Maggie and Bessie , the dear little workers in the garden of the Lord , who tended the Christian plants of patience , kindness , and forbearance , till their lovely blossoms overran the evil weeds of malice and ill-will , and sowed the seeds of that which brought forth fruit for the glory of God", "context_len": 59}}}, "demented": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "Bart , half decided that he was demented , watched him disappear in the direction of a cheap eating house just beyond the tracks , and started homewards more or less sobered and thoughtful over the peculiar incident", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "So saying , and mumbling to herself as she had before , the demented woman led him out of the strange room with the chairs and platform that had so bewildered the boy and down a long , dark passage", "context_len": 40}}}, "mesmerize": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "If they could mesmerize me , I should like to see them do it", "context_len": 14}}}, "mesmerizers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` Then , seeing that the information failed to interest me , `` What do you think of them -- the mesmerizers ? ''", "context_len": 24}}}, "secession": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "She hates secession , and she hates Secessionists", "context_len": 8}}}, "hags": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` I knew what you 'd say , but I had my reasons for asking the question ; you must go now ; and bear in mind , '' she went on with a sudden display of feeling , `` that the war has made such devil 's hags of the women , and such devil 's imps of the men , that everything is in a tangle", "context_len": 68}}}, "imps": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "`` I knew what you 'd say , but I had my reasons for asking the question ; you must go now ; and bear in mind , '' she went on with a sudden display of feeling , `` that the war has made such devil 's hags of the women , and such devil 's imps of the men , that everything is in a tangle", "context_len": 68}}}, "riderless": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` The grand stand went mad -- 'First money for the riderless pony ! ' they roared , but the judges judged otherwise , and the race had to be run over again", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "But even in that case , they could not fail to notice that the pony was riderless and they would understand why", "context_len": 22}}}, "drummed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Let me see , '' -- the old gentleman drummed on the writing-table , rubbing his white hair with an absorbed hand , -- '' Lef -- Lef ? Yes , I am quite sure , Leffingwell is Pip 's name", "context_len": 42}}}, "frazzle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The old saying , worn to a frazzle with repetition , came to me with new force , and I was sadly alive to the fact that where ignorance is bliss 't is folly to be wise", "context_len": 37}}}, "pervaded": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "scalp": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 18, "word_index": 26, "context": "I tried in vain to shake off the feeling , and at last I pretended to trace it to feverishness resulting from the wound in the scalp ; but I knew this was not so -- I knew that one of the great things of life was behind it all ; I knew that I had come to the hour that young men hope for and older men dread ; I knew that for good or evil my future was wrapped in the mystery and tangle of which Jane Ryder was the centre", "context_len": 93}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Ki Sing is a good fellow , if he is a heathen , and I 'd like to scalp the man that ill-treats him", "context_len": 24}}}, "web": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "Donald Pierce is sent to solve the mystery of his father \u2019 s disappearance , into the unmapped barrens where King Stively weaves his web of wickedness , and rules a territory the size of a small empire with a ruthlessness and cunning that baffles the best of the Mounted Police", "context_len": 51}}}, "unborn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "I knew no more what I intended to do than the babe unborn", "context_len": 13}}}, "lowed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Dey er gwine on a ride now terreckly , Marse Cally , an ' I lowed maybe you 'd want ter go 'long wid um", "context_len": 26}}}, "perturbation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "I had an idea that she would shrink from me or show some perturbation ; but I was never more mistaken in my life", "context_len": 24}}}, "bluntness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` I would n't go with him , '' she declared with some bluntness", "context_len": 14}}}, "rumpus": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "That ain \u2019 t what I \u2019 m driving at , but if a hoss had been wounded or killed , he would have kicked up such a rumpus he \u2019 d stampeded all the others ; they \u2019 d have scattered over the perarie and upset things so promiscuous that we \u2019 d never got \u2019 em agin", "context_len": 59}}}, "raided": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 7, "context": "Christianized Apalachis , who had been frequently raided by Alibamu Indians , fled in 1705 to the French colony at Mobile , where Governor de Bienville gave them lands and grain-seed to settle between the Mobilian and Tohome tribe ; cf", "context_len": 41}}}, "subjecting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "But better for you to be in prison , where you will be safe , than for you to be going around here masquerading as a man and subjecting yourself to the insults of all sorts of men", "context_len": 38}}}, "entrapped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Noble gentleman ! to entrap a woman and then declare she shall not be entrapped ! To gain whatever honor there may be in a woman 's capture by running ahead of his ruffians and capturing her himself ! This is Southern manliness -- this is Southern chivalry ! I am glad I know it for what it really is", "context_len": 61}}}, "dismounted": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 7, "context": "When he arrived , he would have dismounted at the door , but there came up to him one of the Amirs , whom the Sultan had deputed to that office , and said to him , `` O my lord , the commandment is that thou enter , riding on thy charger , so thou mayst alight at the door of the Divan", "context_len": 64}}}, "informality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` He 's right in there , '' said the guard with good-natured informality", "context_len": 14}}}, "scouting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Scouting parties were dispatched to explore the surroundings ; they returned in a month , having discovered a forest , the borders of which were situated on Red river , and contained ample subterranean dwellings", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "A little scouting showed him that he was not on Jaguar Island", "context_len": 12}}}, "tolerate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, "context": "Quiet and self-contained and little given to impulse as he was , Seabrooke , when roused to anger or resentment , was a very lion in his wrath , and there was one thing which he could never tolerate or overlook , and that was any attempt to take an unfair advantage of him", "context_len": 54}}}, "forrest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Forrest to come in here a minute , and do you stay out till I call you", "context_len": 17}}}, "technicality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I 'm letting you off on a technicality , Miss Ryder", "context_len": 11}}}, "dictated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` He went into the next room and dictated to an orderly , and presently brought me a paper signed with his own name , and I have it yet", "context_len": 30}}}, "bluster": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I did n't want her to hear you bluster and threaten ; and , besides , I wanted to tell you what I think of you", "context_len": 26}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Rollins had been astonished , but he knew what was expected of him , and he began to bluster fiercely , taking a step toward Stone , who did not retreat or move", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Look here , stranger , '' said Mosely , with a faint attempt at bluster , `` you 'd better take care what you say to me", "context_len": 28}}}, "discomfit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Nor will silence discomfit them", "context_len": 5}}}, "lorded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "It was necessary to keep the house very still , on account of Aunt Bessie , who was so very nervous that the least sound disturbed her ; and roguish , noisy Frankie was , by Aunt Patty 's earnest request , allowed to go to her house , where , for a few days , he lorded it over that humble servant of his to his heart 's content", "context_len": 70}}}, "disguises": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` Why , I 'd know you through a thousand disguises , as I knew you here that first night", "context_len": 20}}}, "politer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "She was silent for a little space , and then , `` Did it never occur to you that it would be politer to refer to me as Miss Jane Ryder ? '' Now , I had never thought of her as Miss Jane Ryder , and I told her so", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Yes ; but , of course , I do n't mean them : they 're our brothers ; but , of example , do n't you think Hafed is a great deal nicer and politer than Bob ? ''", "context_len": 40}}}, "maltreated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "She was crying and wringing her hands , and , for a moment , I thought she had been maltreated by those whose duty it was to raid the house", "context_len": 30}}}, "wheedle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` First comes you to wheedle her away , and then come your companions to search the house for her", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "But in vain did Astbury implore and wheedle , swear and protest Dick was firm , till at last the rascal began to realize that his prize was slipping from him , and changed his tone and grumbling out --", "context_len": 40}}}, "palsied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "I never knew but one man you could trust with a woman , and he was so palsied that a child could push him over", "context_len": 25}}}, "masquerade": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Do you think I 'd injure a hair of her head ? It is you that have injured her by allowing her to masquerade as a man -- a little thing like that , with nobody to advise her", "context_len": 39}}}, "hickories": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Carroll Shannon , with his straps , and his hickories , and his riding-whips , and I hope he will soon get a woman on whom he can use them all", "context_len": 31}}}, "contrite": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The young woman 's face fell at that , and she seemed to be very contrite", "context_len": 16}}}, "persist": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "Yes , you \u2019 re captain of the team , sneered Bern , and you \u2019 re trying to work for your own advantage ; but let me inform you that if you persist in this course it will be to your decided disadvantage", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` My son , I can do no more for thee ; I see thou wilt persist in thine obstinacy", "context_len": 20}}}, "nettled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "There was just the shadow of a smile hovering around her lips , and it nettled me", "context_len": 17}}}, "puckering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` I 'm sure I do n't know what to make of you , '' she said , puckering her forehead as she stood in the door", "context_len": 27}}}, "coupled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "The party who had brought me to this remote spot were of an entirely different race from those among whom I now found myself , and the fact that we were making our way toward what was obviously the aristocratic part of the town , coupled with the expressive conversation carried on by the leader of my custodians with three or four individuals who had joined us , led me to surmise -- although of course I did not understand a word of what was said -- that I had been brought up the river as a peace- offering or something of that sort , which conclusion was again the reverse of reassuring", "context_len": 113}}}, "peaceable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` And call it 'The Happy Family , ' '' said Fred , mischievously , `` after those celebrated bears and dogs and cats and mice who live together in the most peaceable manner so long as they have no teeth and claws , but who immediately fall to and eat one another up as soon as these are allowed to grow", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "As a sequel to his wonderful story of the pursuit of the Alibamu by the Creeks and the final peaceable settling down of both , Milfort adds some points on the early doings and warrings of the Creeks , which had occurred but a limited number of years before his stay in the tribe , and were recounted to him by one of the m\u00edkos from their memorial beads , like the legendary migration :", "context_len": 75}}}, "prominently": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 40, "context": "The ruffian had hardly hit the wall before the negro was upon him again , making a noise in his throat like some wild animal , his face distorted and the muscles of his arms and body standing out as prominently as if he were covered with huge wens or tumors", "context_len": 51}}}, "wens": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "The ruffian had hardly hit the wall before the negro was upon him again , making a noise in his throat like some wild animal , his face distorted and the muscles of his arms and body standing out as prominently as if he were covered with huge wens or tumors", "context_len": 51}}}, "tumors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "The ruffian had hardly hit the wall before the negro was upon him again , making a noise in his throat like some wild animal , his face distorted and the muscles of his arms and body standing out as prominently as if he were covered with huge wens or tumors", "context_len": 51}}}, "pester": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I 'm gwine ter put 'im whar he ca n't pester nobody else", "context_len": 13}}}, "assaulting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "The besiegers have to invest the city on all sides to cut off all supplies of provisions , and then , in those days , they had to construct engines to make a breach somewhere in the walls , through which an assaulting party could attempt to force their way in", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "Into the gap a strong body of German troops , who , having previously held the wood , knew how to avoid the treacherous swamp , came hurriedly , with the intention of driving a wedge between the assaulting troops", "context_len": 40}}}, "ox": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "The majority of these headlong riders were not seen , for , always hunting the speediest route , they were generally well north or south of the trail of the ox teams", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The old man himself stood by the side of the great ox cart , which was filled with golden sheaves ; and on the top of these Maggie and Bessie sat in state , their hands and round straw hats filled with bright , red poppies", "context_len": 46}}}, "tougher": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I did my best to pull his head off in order to recover my weapon , but his neck was tougher than the joints of white oak , and the two long legs that went to make up the back of the chair came off in my hand , thus giving me a bludgeon very much to my taste", "context_len": 59}}}, "bludgeon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "I did my best to pull his head off in order to recover my weapon , but his neck was tougher than the joints of white oak , and the two long legs that went to make up the back of the chair came off in my hand , thus giving me a bludgeon very much to my taste", "context_len": 59}}}, "unreasoning": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "My anger was of such a blind and unreasoning sort that I had no objections to the horrible proceeding , and if there had been no sudden diversion I should , in all probability , have aided him in carrying out his purpose", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "As for Phil and Langridge , they faced each other , one eager with righteous anger to continue the chastisement , the other mad with the lust of shame and unreasoning", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Their flight was unreasoning", "context_len": 4}}}, "incongruity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The incongruity of the wolf 's swallowing whole the grandmother and child does not destroy the child 's enjoyment of the story , while the happy release of both grandmother and little girl forms a suitable close", "context_len": 37}}}, "jarred": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "With his teeth set , he hugged the ball beneath one arm , the other thrown out stiffly to fend off a dark figure he saw coming at him ; and he left the would-be tackler jarred , dazed and knocked to his knees", "context_len": 44}}}, "asperity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "My laughter must have jarred her , for she said with some asperity , `` You are laughing now , but in a minute you will be laughing on the other side of your mouth ! ''", "context_len": 37}}}, "graduates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I had seen not more than a dozen graduates of the great military academy , but enough to recognize the characteristics that marked them all", "context_len": 25}}}, "bruiser": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` The bruiser has been bruised , '' he laughed", "context_len": 10}}}, "crunched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The negro wrenched the weapon from him , threw it out of reach , seized the hand that had held it and crunched it between his teeth with such savage ferocity that the ruffian howled with pain", "context_len": 37}}}, "thereto": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 73, "context": "However , I could not brook that Thy Grace should honour me [ with thy presence ] in a palace [ 532 ] wherein there was somewhat lacking ; wherefore , so thou mayst know that it was not for lack of ableness that I left it uncomplete , [ 533 ] let Thy Grace go up and see the lattice-work of the kiosk , [ 534 ] an there be aught lacking thereto", "context_len": 74}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "whisk": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "She was driving a good horse , too ; his trot was sufficient to keep our horses in a canter ; and whenever he heard us coming up behind him he would whisk the buggy away as if he scorned company", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "This he did five or six times in succession , to the great amusement and satisfaction of the little ones , who were beginning to hope he would go with them all the way to the house , when with a pert , defiant whisk of his bushy tail , he leaped down the bank , and was lost to sight in the thick trees of the ravine", "context_len": 68}}}, "instrumental": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "In that case , I could not only have escaped , but I could have been instrumental in the capture of her brother and his whole party", "context_len": 27}}}, "enigmatically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` In the nature of things , '' he replied , enigmatically , `` I prefer not to tell you", "context_len": 20}}}, "outriders": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Those were the sisters for whom the coach and four waited , with the footman and outriders , and who would be made welcome at all the grand functions of life , while the Cinderellas were relegated to the chimney corner", "context_len": 41}}}, "poplars": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The lame young man was going slowly on his crutches along the road under the poplars", "context_len": 16}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Tall Lombardy poplars that I found out afterward were the pride of Mr", "context_len": 13}}}, "parcelled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The thought , however , did not satisfy my hunger , and I wondered when and where a halt was to be called and rations parcelled out", "context_len": 27}}}, "vexatious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "It is a vexatious feeling for the young to feel the pangs of hunger , and I was not used to a long fast", "context_len": 24}}}, "batter": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "The rain might descend in torrents , hail , snow and sleet might batter horse and rider like fine birdshot , and the temperature might drop below zero , or throb with heat , still rider and horse who were like one creature must plunge on and ever on , so long as muscle and nerve could stand the terrific strain", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 39, "context": "Then a frying pan was put on the stove , for they had brought along no regular griddle , it was greased , and Frank , who insisted on doing the rest , was allowed to pour out the batter , and do the turning", "context_len": 45}}}, "canteen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Nobody ever thought to bring along anything to drink , unless Mickey Rourke has a canteen in that bundle of his", "context_len": 21}}}, "straddle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Do n't stop for to say good-by , but straddle yure horse an ' be off wit ' ye", "context_len": 19}}}, "womanliness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "At the time , this fact did not add to my happiness , for , what with her womanliness and her childishness , she presented a problem that puzzled and dazzled me , for my mind was wofully lacking in the nimbleness necessary to follow the swift changes of her moods", "context_len": 51}}}, "dazzled": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "The Sultan wondered with an exceeding wonderment and was confounded at the beauty of the girls and their loveliness , which overpassed description ; his wit was bewildered , when he saw the golden dishes , full of jewels that dazzled the sight , and he was amazed at this marvel , so that he became as one dumb , unable to speak aught , of the excess of his wonderment ; nay , his wit was the more perplexed , forasmuch as this had all been accomplished in an hour 's time", "context_len": 93}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Could this be the same girl who had found life intolerable only two hours before ? Now the Aladdin wand of kindly fortune had opened before her dazzled eyes a mine of golden possibilities", "context_len": 34}}}, "nimbleness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "At the time , this fact did not add to my happiness , for , what with her womanliness and her childishness , she presented a problem that puzzled and dazzled me , for my mind was wofully lacking in the nimbleness necessary to follow the swift changes of her moods", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Bomba could do nothing but stand in bewildered silence and watch the old woman as she danced and sang , whirling about the poor stage with a nimbleness that was amazing in one of her age", "context_len": 36}}}, "moods": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 60, "context": "It seemed hard to Peggy to think that the trees would bud and the flowers blossom , and the crops grow , when they were not there to watch it all , for most of us have a kind of feeling that we are the important centre around which Nature turns , instead of only mere spectators of her varying moods , and sometimes it felt so impossible that such an utter upheaval in their lives could really come to pass that she would have to shake herself to believe that it was not all a bad dream ; but as she noticed the quiet preparations that went on , and the added worry on Father 's face , she realized that it was only too true ,", "context_len": 128}}}, "despises": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "A horse is quick to learn what kind of man holds the reins , and woe to him whom the equine despises ! Bucephalus would not have needed any enlightenment had Harper or any one of the regular riders been in the saddle , but he wasn \u2019 t sure about the lusty young fellow who was trying to lord it over him", "context_len": 63}}}, "strap": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 27, "context": "Borden brought home something in a paper bag and Jack begged the bag `` to bust , '' watching his father as he shook out a leather strap cut in thongs and said --", "context_len": 34}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Papa , you may strap me if you want to , but if I had n't fit the boys would have made fun of me and called me sissy , and we went at it like fury", "context_len": 37}}}, "repentance": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 11, "context": "Smith made a list of subjects for daily prayer , embracing repentance , faith , love , grace to help , gratitude , power to pray , constant direction in all things , a right understanding of the Bible , deliverance from besetting sin , constancy in God 's service , relatives and friends , missionaries , pardon for all ignorance and sin in prayer , etc", "context_len": 67}}}, "appurtenances": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Is my youth , with all its appurtenances , worth your acceptance ? '' I asked", "context_len": 17}}}, "manhood": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 88, "context": "Fortunately , the sun 's rays came down hot and bright , and the rock grew warmer , so that by degrees the terribly void look began to leave Mark Penelly 's face , and at last , when Harry held out his hand , saying , `` Do you feel better ? '' Mark Penelly caught it in both of his , clung to it , and , turning half over on his face , laid his forehead against it , and , forgetting his years of manhood , lay there in his weakness , and sobbed and cried like a child", "context_len": 103}}}, "manlike": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Well , it was only too true , and I knew it , but , manlike , I must take some reprisal from the truth", "context_len": 25}}}, "negotiating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He was evidently negotiating for meal or flour ; but it all came to me as in a dream", "context_len": 19}}}, "jig": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "When the taxicab got beyond the place where the eight-miles-an-hour speed limit did not interfere , the driver let the machine out , and the figures in the dial danced a jig", "context_len": 32}}}, "reprimand": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Only one-half of the boys were implicated in that affair ; they had already been punished by the restrictions which had been placed upon them , and were to be further disgraced by the public reprimand which he intended to give them on the dismissal of the school ; and these culprits were probably dreading this or some other severe punishment which would be meted out to them by the report of their misconduct which would be sent home", "context_len": 79}}}, "saturnine": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "At that time it was the general opinion , even among those of his command who were not thrown into daily contact with him , that this truly great man was of a grim and saturnine disposition", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "With a hurried gesture the Major dismissed the prisoner , and , accompanied by the saturnine lieutenant , bolted to a flight of steps leading to a still deeper refuge", "context_len": 30}}}, "bubbled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "The spot chosen was at some distance from the house , up in the woods , where a pure , bright spring bubbled up from the rocks , and then went rippling and singing away in one of those hundred mountain streams", "context_len": 42}}}, "unfailingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "There were times when he fairly bubbled over with boyish humor , and though these moments were rare , he was unfailingly cordial to those that had met his expectations or who had his confidence", "context_len": 35}}}, "shoeless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "I remember that I was shoeless , along with the greater part of my command , though the weather was bitter cold , and my feet were bleeding , and yet when I heard that trumpet voice , ordering us from the wagons to make one more stand , I never thought of my feet", "context_len": 55}}}, "shirker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Nor was there a shirker among the men -- and all because the leader was Forrest", "context_len": 16}}}, "roughshod": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "And we saved that defeated army from annihilation , holding the enemy at bay and driving him back , when , if he had known the true condition of affairs , he would have ridden over us roughshod", "context_len": 38}}}, "eliminated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Her Boston education had not eliminated her curiosity", "context_len": 8}}}, "contingencies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "But for the subtle changes made by peace , the town was the same , and even the old tavern in the woods had survived all the contingencies of war and stood intact , but tenantless", "context_len": 36}}}, "tenantless": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "But for the subtle changes made by peace , the town was the same , and even the old tavern in the woods had survived all the contingencies of war and stood intact , but tenantless", "context_len": 36}}}, "emptiness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Without realizing its coldness and emptiness , her life had been truly void of human warmth before the little , lonely girl stole in to fill it with her piteous , proud presence", "context_len": 33}}}, "elongated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Already the airship had spotted a dark elongated shape beneath the waves", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Feeling about with his hand , he gripped the shoulder of the sleeper , and bracing himself with one foot against the front board , he drew out the elongated form of the offender", "context_len": 34}}}, "laced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "He was dressed in a soft flannel shirt open at the neck , buckskin riding-breeches , leggings , and strong laced boots , the latter provided with spurs", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I certainly do , '' spoke the quarter-back as he laced his canvas jacket", "context_len": 15}}}, "girth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "He was barely half an inch shorter than Malcolm -- they raise tall youths in New Zealand -- of greater girth , and slightly heavier", "context_len": 25}}}, "climatic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was an ideal existence , under perfect climatic conditions", "context_len": 10}}}, "uplands": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "For three weeks no rain had fallen , yet on the breezy uplands the ground was green with verdure", "context_len": 19}}}, "ridges": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "In the strange peacefulness which brooded over her she walked home between the pi\u00f1on-sprinkled hills , where doves were crooning and the far bleating of an upland herd echoed among the barren ridges", "context_len": 33}}}, "loftier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Away to the west could be discerned the lofty ridges of the Southern Alps , some of the loftier peaks still retaining their garb of snow", "context_len": 26}}}, "irregularly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "She drew a long breath , he felt the heart quiver irregularly , the little heart that would need careful watching the next few years , that so far had been worked pretty hard", "context_len": 34}}}, "hilly": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 40, "context": "Precious little of the sun did we see , and it was necessary to keep one 's eyes on the path which wound up and down into dark green hollows where beds of maidenhair fern flourished , or up to hilly spots where rock ferns grew in patches on enormous boulders", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "A number of Naktche families , speaking their paternal language , now live in the hilly parts of the Cheroki Nation , Indian Territory", "context_len": 24}}}, "theodolite": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Tell Kaitiu to take the large theodolite down to No", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Smithers is an ass with the theodolite , and Hedger 's 'trig ' is rotten", "context_len": 15}}}, "substantially": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The story of the origin of this war is substantially this", "context_len": 11}}}, "cupboards": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "She would go round with Nancy and a candle , carefully locking all the doors and securing the shutters , peering fearfully into cupboards and starting at her own shadow on the wall ; and , having finally retreated to her bedroom , would barricade the door with a tin box , and place the poker handy on a chair by her bedside", "context_len": 63}}}, "lockers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "At the gymnasium I made a close inspection of the pilfered lockers , being there to obtain a clew of some sort , a desire which was amply rewarded", "context_len": 29}}}, "ironware": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "At one end was an oil stove , with a meagre supply of crockery and ironware above", "context_len": 17}}}, "ubiquitous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "In one corner were a couple of sporting rifles and some fishing-rods , against which was leaning one of those ubiquitous objects of modern civilization -- a motor tyre", "context_len": 29}}}, "tyre": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "In one corner were a couple of sporting rifles and some fishing-rods , against which was leaning one of those ubiquitous objects of modern civilization -- a motor tyre", "context_len": 29}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "It was mainly on account of that motor tyre that Malcolm was anxious for the arrival of the camp postman", "context_len": 20}}}, "juggernaut": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 13, "context": "But before going on active service Peter had disposed of his share of juggernaut to his young brother , thus , in a manner , helping to mitigate Malcolm 's regret that he was not at least two years older , and thus able to share with his brother the honour , glory , and vicissitudes of fighting the Boche", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The idea of juggernaut ambling along at nearly a modest mile a minute was too funny", "context_len": 16}}}, "sheltering": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "Another party reached the Chicasa country and was granted a home and protection by that tribe ; but the revengeful French colonists declared war upon the hospitable Chicasa for sheltering their mortal enemies , and invaded their lands by way of the Yazoo river in 1736 , but were compelled to retreat after suffering considerable loss", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "In process of time , as fast as the little legs would permit they arrived at Regent 's Park , and Faith , choosing a sheltering tree , placed her little brother in a shady corner , and came close to his side", "context_len": 43}}}, "garage": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Thinking that the car , which seemed to be big and powerful , might be used for towing the taxicab -- in case it was very seriously damaged -- to the nearest garage , Matt jumped for the road", "context_len": 39}}}, "makers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "A car of ancient and composite design -- partly Daimler , partly Darracq , and with a suspicion of half a dozen makers ' parts in the tout ensemble -- the wondrous , once-discarded vehicle had been given to Peter and Malcolm Carr by a cousin of theirs", "context_len": 48}}}, "tractability": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Being of a mechanical turn of mind , the two brothers soon reduced the motor to a state of servile tractability , although there was hardly a thoroughfare in Christchurch whose buildings did not bear a more or less permanent record of Juggernaut 's frailties", "context_len": 45}}}, "frailties": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Being of a mechanical turn of mind , the two brothers soon reduced the motor to a state of servile tractability , although there was hardly a thoroughfare in Christchurch whose buildings did not bear a more or less permanent record of Juggernaut 's frailties", "context_len": 45}}}, "enlisting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Enlisting in the first contingent , he had taken part in the repulse of the first Turkish invasion of Egypt and the heroic yet ill-starred Gallipoli campaign without receiving as much as a scratch , and having hardly spent a day in hospital", "context_len": 43}}}, "mitigate": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 69, "context": "But alas ! there were no traditions of victory to encourage them ; only gloomy remembrances of defeat ; and , but for the stern call of duty which bade them , as men and Christians , go to the succour of their brethren , the majority would have preferred to remain at home and abide the worst , although they knew full well that submission utterly failed to mitigate the ferocious cruelty of their oppressors , who slew alike the innocent babe and the grey-haired grandsire", "context_len": 87}}}, "barracked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Right-o ! '' was the muffled response as Dick `` barracked '' into the hut , still scrubbing his face vigorously with a towel", "context_len": 25}}}, "pannikin": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "A large pannikin of tea awaited the postman , for the jaded animal a bucketful of water", "context_len": 17}}}, "jaded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "A large pannikin of tea awaited the postman , for the jaded animal a bucketful of water", "context_len": 17}}}, "mike": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Mike the postman grinned approvingly as he handed over four newspaper packets and half a dozen letters , while Dick 's consignment showed that that worthy was by no means forgotten", "context_len": 31}}}, "packets": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "The antiquarians did not seem to be discussing anything very learned just at present , for they had drawn out flasks , and packets of sandwiches , and were engaged in picnicking upon the stones in a truly modern fashion , while occasional bursts of laughter were wafted along the air", "context_len": 51}}}, "noxious": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "Into the machine-gun slits bombs were tossed in dozens , until the confined space within the redoubt was filled with noxious smoke from the loud-sounding missiles of destruction", "context_len": 28}}}, "sector": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "I can not say much beyond that we are very busy on our sector of the Front", "context_len": 17}}}, "deleted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It is now a quarter to five , and we parade at half-past for ( words deleted with blue pencil )", "context_len": 21}}}, "carr": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "CARR", "context_len": 1}}}, "gulping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 92, "context": "and she plumped down on the dead leaves by the roadside in such a tempest of tears that Father had to climb out of the gig and pick her up to comfort her ; but as he could not get a word of sense , he popped her in the vacant place by his side and drove on , while she clung to his arm , still shaking with sobs , till they reached the Abbey , where he helped her down , such a miserable little tear-stained picture of woe , gulping out the confession of her escapade , that he had not the heart to scold her , though he had a word of warning to say afterwards upon the danger of such heedless practical jokes", "context_len": 128}}}, "replace": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 11, "context": "It represents the bare cost of a new copper pedestal to replace the one you shot to pieces last night , and it 's a wonder you are not in jail for murder , for had that cannon ball struck a human being -- Enough ! before I take up this outrage with the district attorney in its criminal phase , are you going to settle the damage , or are you not ? ''", "context_len": 75}}}, "surveys": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The preliminary surveys can still go on", "context_len": 7}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "-- Alexander surveys the Persian army", "context_len": 6}}}, "relapse": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "As a scholar he shone ; as a business man he was a failure , owing to the fatal and all too common trait amongst Maoris of the educated class of pleasure in the spending of money , and , oddly enough , to an inherent tendency to relapse , if only temporarily , to an aboriginal existence", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "The prince is better , but only able to rise a few hours each day , and I fear a relapse would be fatal", "context_len": 24}}}, "aboriginal": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 56, "context": "As a scholar he shone ; as a business man he was a failure , owing to the fatal and all too common trait amongst Maoris of the educated class of pleasure in the spending of money , and , oddly enough , to an inherent tendency to relapse , if only temporarily , to an aboriginal existence", "context_len": 58}}}, "dealers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "He had been known to go to the largest dealers in Christ-church and purchase the swiftest car procurable , drive it at breakneck speed until he collided with something , and then sell the remains and retire to his pah until he found an opportunity for another exuberance of pecuniary extravagance", "context_len": 51}}}, "procurable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "He had been known to go to the largest dealers in Christ-church and purchase the swiftest car procurable , drive it at breakneck speed until he collided with something , and then sell the remains and retire to his pah until he found an opportunity for another exuberance of pecuniary extravagance", "context_len": 51}}}, "exuberance": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "He told his tale , how he had conceived suspicions that the Danes intended a winter descent ; how he had risked his life ( in the exuberance of youthful daring ) to ascertain the truth ; how , trusting to his knowledge of Carisbrooke , wherein he had spent many pleasant days in his boyhood , he had ventured amongst the Danes as a gleeman , in imitation of Alfred of old ; how there he had assisted , unsuspected , at a meeting of the council in the great hall , and heard it decided to invade England , and finally how he had escaped", "context_len": 107}}}, "diminution": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "With the heavy drain upon New Zealand 's man power and the sudden and marked diminution in the stream of immigrants , the opportunities to sell land vanished , and with them the prospects of buying another motor-car", "context_len": 38}}}, "extricated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 68, "context": "While things were in this state -- the Thebans having understood that Alexander had been killed at the north , and supposing that , at all events , if this report should not be true , he was , without doubt , still far away , involved in contentions with the barbarian nations , from which it was not to be expected that he could be very speedily extricated -- the whole city was suddenly thrown into consternation by the report that a large Macedonian army was approaching from the north , with Alexander at its head , and that it was , in fact , close upon them", "context_len": 109}}}, "landslide": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "On one occasion Te Paheka had extricated the lad from a dangerous position during a landslide on the Wairakato Ridge ; and now the chance had arrived to repay the courteous native by making him a present of the ancient but still active Juggernaut", "context_len": 44}}}, "malcolm": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Malcolm", "context_len": 1}}}, "ambling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "The turtle was a large one , of a common species , and was ambling along as if it did not at all mind the attention it had attracted", "context_len": 29}}}, "toppling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "Farther along the street stood a square , old-fashioned , story-and-a-half house , with a more modern ell and shed adjoining , and a wretched sagging barn , that lurched on its foundations , and was only kept from toppling farther , and possibly falling , by long , crude timber props , set against its side", "context_len": 57}}}, "bounder": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` You lucky bounder ! '' was Dick Selwyn 's greeting when the chums met at the hut", "context_len": 18}}}, "greenheart": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "303 rifle and the greenheart rod", "context_len": 6}}}, "militarism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Would it be as a wounded , perhaps crippled man , or as a hale and hearty veteran after that still remote day when peace is to be declared , and German militarism crushed once and for all time ?", "context_len": 40}}}, "prolonging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "Te Paheka , torn between the desire to run away with his new gift and to wish his white friend farewell and kia ora in a manner worthy of a dignified and old-standing Maori gentleman , looked like prolonging the leave-taking ceremony indefinitely , until he leave-taking happened to see the tail-end of a Napier racing car disappearing round the corner", "context_len": 61}}}, "pedestrians": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "And the last the lad saw of juggernaut was the car cutting round a sharp corner at a good twenty-five miles an hour , whilst pedestrians scattered right and left to avoid being run down", "context_len": 35}}}, "inquest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Either in the police-court intelligence or in the inquest reports", "context_len": 11}}}, "adage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "And the old adage ' a volunteer is worth two pressed men ' still holds good", "context_len": 16}}}, "enlisted": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "Too late he was traced to Australia , where he enlisted in a Victoria regiment , deserting when the Anzacs were under fire in Gallipoli", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The enlisted members of the B-26 crews were already on board , bunking forward with the petty officers", "context_len": 18}}}, "chucking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` He 's great -- absolutely ! Never even murmured when he had two fellows chucking their hands in on the same day", "context_len": 23}}}, "disintegrated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Farther ahead masses of solid rock and earth were falling with a succession of thuds , while , where Messines Ridge had been , was a riven mound of disintegrated Soil , over which a dense cloud of black smoke rolled sullenly in the sultry night air", "context_len": 47}}}, "recruits": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "The English hero divided his army into three divisions : The right wing , where he posted around his own person the chosen band whom he had trained during the last few years of retirement ; the left wing , chiefly composed of the men of Wessex ; the centre , the weakest and newest recruits , whom he posted there with as deep a design as led Hannibal to use the same strategy at Cannae", "context_len": 76}}}, "grouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It was soon a standing joke that Dowit never could `` do it '' properly , except to grouse", "context_len": 19}}}, "trench": {"freq": 32, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 50, "context": "In places it was eight feet deep , with sand-bags on either side in addition , In others , in marshy spots , where the high-explosive shells had spitefully disturbed the tranquillity of meandering streams and carried the sluggish water to overflow and swamp the surrounding ground , the `` trench '' was above normal ground-level , with a lofty and broad wall of sand-bags to right and left", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "It would be difficult to say who were the most taken aback : the Boches at the sight of a khaki-clad man who might or might not be the foremost of a party of trench raiders , or Malcolm on finding himself confronted by a score of fully-armed Germans", "context_len": 49}}}, "wielder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "If the wielder had omitted to grip the lever , then in four seconds -- -- !", "context_len": 17}}}, "rifleman": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 3, "context": "When the young rifleman described his Scottish journey as the usual round , he was referring to the somewhat curious fact that a large percentage of New Zealanders go to Edinburgh when granted leave after being discharged from hospital", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The lugubrious faces of the Huns had the effect of making the rifleman pull himself together", "context_len": 16}}}, "parabola": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Describing a magnificent parabola , the released bomb hurtled through the air ; but instead of towards the target it was whizzing in the opposite direction -- straight for the group of officers standing a dozen yards from the rear of the trench", "context_len": 43}}}, "subaltern": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "Amidst renewed outbursts of cheering the subaltern retired for repairs and renewals , while the lady judges were fortunately spared the task of bestowing the palm upon the cornet champion of the Thirty-somethingths", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Both struck the waggish subaltern full in the face , and then the nature of the `` main charge '' became apparent", "context_len": 22}}}, "sandbag": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Picking up a conveniently-placed sandbag , he hurled it at the now motionless bomb , missed it , but caught the recumbent form of a portly major squarely between the shoulders", "context_len": 31}}}, "parados": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "Traces of poison gas lurked in the traverses , pungent fumes from bursting shells wafted over parapet and parados , while the report , passed on from various successive occupants of this section of the line , that a dozen dead Huns had been buried under the floor of the support trench -- the old first-line trench of a Prussian regiment -- seemed to find definite confirmation", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Evidently it was the rearmost of an intricate system of field-fortifications , for Malcolm was on the parados side while beyond , merging into smoke and haze , were other ramifications of the maze of trenches , all silent and deserted", "context_len": 41}}}, "percentage": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Malcolm and Dick found bayonet exercise exciting work -- thrusting at suspended sacks stuffed with straw called for strength and strenuous activity -- while at the ranges both lads gained a high percentage of bulls , and in a very short while the `` crossed rifles `` , denoting marksmanship , ornamented the sleeves of their uniforms", "context_len": 57}}}, "bulls": {"freq": 18, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 24, "context": "Each of the lofty ships bore the ensign of its commander ; some carried at the prow the figures of lions , some of bulls , dolphins , dragons , or armed warriors , gaudily painted or even gilded ; while others bore from their mast the ensign of voracious birds -- the eagle , the raven -- which appeared to stretch their wings as the flag expanded in the wind", "context_len": 71}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "denoting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "Malcolm and Dick found bayonet exercise exciting work -- thrusting at suspended sacks stuffed with straw called for strength and strenuous activity -- while at the ranges both lads gained a high percentage of bulls , and in a very short while the `` crossed rifles `` , denoting marksmanship , ornamented the sleeves of their uniforms", "context_len": 57}}}, "marksmanship": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "Malcolm and Dick found bayonet exercise exciting work -- thrusting at suspended sacks stuffed with straw called for strength and strenuous activity -- while at the ranges both lads gained a high percentage of bulls , and in a very short while the `` crossed rifles `` , denoting marksmanship , ornamented the sleeves of their uniforms", "context_len": 57}}}, "riflemen": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "reverting to riflemen on arrival in England , before proceeding across to France", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 1, "context": "The riflemen within hearing expressed their approval of the idea , for the obstacle that was holding up a section of the advance was a row of concrete pill-boxes surrounding the entrance to a deep and extensive cave -- a formidable stronghold known as `` Berlin ''", "context_len": 47}}}, "holders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Nevertheless the rank conferred certain privileges upon the holders , besides giving them valuable experience in the duties of non-commissioned officers", "context_len": 21}}}, "billiard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "A battalion picture-theatre , billiard rooms , voluntary swimming parades , boxing , and a variety of other indoor and outdoor games contributed to the men 's enjoyment ; and , although discipline was well enforced , there was a total absence of irritating petty restrictions that form a constant source of annoyance to the men of the New Armies of the Motherland", "context_len": 63}}}, "voluntary": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "A battalion picture-theatre , billiard rooms , voluntary swimming parades , boxing , and a variety of other indoor and outdoor games contributed to the men 's enjoyment ; and , although discipline was well enforced , there was a total absence of irritating petty restrictions that form a constant source of annoyance to the men of the New Armies of the Motherland", "context_len": 63}}}, "trek": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "Like a flock of sheep the Huns , with hands still upraised , shuffled on the first of their long trek to captivity -- to some delectable spot in England , where , far from the sound of the guns , there is food in plenty for Hun prisoners of war , German U-boats notwithstanding", "context_len": 55}}}, "barracking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` If your theory is correct , how do you account for the fact that those staff officers in the next dug-out came off scot-free until Joliffe thought fit to bring trouble on them and us ? No , stay where you are , Joliffe ! '' he exclaimed , as the bomber 's voice was heard shouting his intention of `` barracking in ''", "context_len": 65}}}, "lateness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Notwithstanding the lateness of the hour , crowds of civilian friends of the departing troops accompanied them -- in motor-cars , horsed vehicles , mounted , and on foot", "context_len": 29}}}, "horsed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Notwithstanding the lateness of the hour , crowds of civilian friends of the departing troops accompanied them -- in motor-cars , horsed vehicles , mounted , and on foot", "context_len": 29}}}, "gamely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Gamely the New Zealanders endured their gruelling ; until the guns lifted and put up a barrage behind them", "context_len": 19}}}, "entrenching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Backs began to ache under the drag of the packs ; entrenching tools began to make their presence aggressively known as they chafed the men 's legs ; rifles were being constantly shifted from shoulder to shoulder or carried at the trail , as the weapons seemingly increased in weight at each step", "context_len": 53}}}, "fettle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The boys are in fine fettle , '' remarked Platoon-sergeant Fortescue to Malcolm", "context_len": 13}}}, "galls": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` As a matter of fact , '' rejoined Malcolm , `` the man has galls on his heels to the size of half-crowns , and one boot is almost full of blood", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Already soldiering had hardened his hands considerably , but stoking , he decided , had proved to be far more strenuous than bayonet exercise , if an aching back , stiff muscles , and galls as big as half-crowns were any criterion", "context_len": 42}}}, "english": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "English born and bred , he had gained a degree at Cambridge , and , failing to turn it to any good account , had been sent to New Zealand by his disappointed father", "context_len": 34}}}, "internal": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 39, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "rearrangements": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Originally a combined passenger and cargo boat , she had been ruthlessly converted into a troop-conveying ship , and the internal rearrangements were not by any means suitable for her new r\u00f4le", "context_len": 32}}}, "vocalist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Let me see : there 's Sergeant Thomson , he 's a bit of a vocalist", "context_len": 16}}}, "duet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "The two Miss Prices from the Post-Office warbled a mild little duet , which gave unbounded satisfaction to their fond mamma , if to nobody else ; the blacksmith 's assistant roared out a sea song in a voice like a tempest ; and a young man on a visit to the miller gave a comic song , which quite took the company by storm", "context_len": 65}}}, "optic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` By the way , what has Lieutenant Nicholson been doing to get his left optic in a sling ? He was n't looking skywards out of one of the ports when Thomson jettisoned his ivories ? ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "skywards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` By the way , what has Lieutenant Nicholson been doing to get his left optic in a sling ? He was n't looking skywards out of one of the ports when Thomson jettisoned his ivories ? ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "jettisoned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` By the way , what has Lieutenant Nicholson been doing to get his left optic in a sling ? He was n't looking skywards out of one of the ports when Thomson jettisoned his ivories ? ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "ivories": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` By the way , what has Lieutenant Nicholson been doing to get his left optic in a sling ? He was n't looking skywards out of one of the ports when Thomson jettisoned his ivories ? ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "tampered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "I do n't insinuate , mind you , that any of the fellows tampered with the ironwork , but all the same the bunk collapsed , and our Padre subsided heavily upon poor little Nicholson", "context_len": 35}}}, "uproariously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": ", laughed uproariously", "context_len": 3}}}, "licence": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` They last six days -- six whole days , in which the people abandon themselves to every kind of licence and cruelty , in which human blood is shed like water", "context_len": 32}}}, "antidote": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Ennui was at all costs to be banished , and the best antidote for that was plenty of hard work", "context_len": 20}}}, "censoring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Even the popular Padre found these entertainments a welcome antidote to the dull business of censoring letters", "context_len": 17}}}, "suffocation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "The men , packed like sardines in a barrel , filled the mess-deck almost to suffocation , their boisterous applause increasing in volume as item succeeded item in quick succession", "context_len": 30}}}, "caustic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Four strapping young fellows , each armed with a highly-polished cornet , appeared and stood facing their critical audience , receiving their caustic comments with a studied indifference", "context_len": 28}}}, "distended": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Do you hear that , Ben ? '' said Jake Bradley , his mouth distended with a grin", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He puffed and blew like a grampus , the tears ran down his distended cheeks , and the perspiration oozed from his forehead , till in disgust he retired from the contest", "context_len": 32}}}, "waggish": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Rifleman Gilway 's eyes were riveted upon the half of a cut , juicy lemon displayed within six inches of his face by a waggish subaltern", "context_len": 26}}}, "acid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The sight of the acid fruit effectually prevented the man getting a single note out of the instrument", "context_len": 18}}}, "grampus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "He puffed and blew like a grampus , the tears ran down his distended cheeks , and the perspiration oozed from his forehead , till in disgust he retired from the contest", "context_len": 32}}}, "oozed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Hungry , and chilly from the damp moisture that oozed down the walls , the poor children sat quietly huddled together watching the cold gray surface of the water , which seemed like some cruel monster ever creeping nearer and nearer to infold them in its treacherous grasp", "context_len": 48}}}, "strategist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The third competitor withdrew without an effort , but the fourth was something of a strategist", "context_len": 16}}}, "tantalizing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "He used his music-card as a screen to shut out the sight of the tantalizing lemon", "context_len": 16}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "The rope was thick and the knife was blunt , and though Hal sawed away with desperate haste the strands parted with tantalizing slowness ; thus , being less able to offer resistance than before , he was hauled rapidly towards the fort", "context_len": 43}}}, "apoplexy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The doctor began to shift uneasily in his chair , anticipating a case of apoplexy", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "It was apoplexy or something like that", "context_len": 7}}}, "velocity": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 46, "context": "The part of the machine upon which the stone was placed would be drawn back by the united strength of many of the soldiers , and then , as it recovered itself when released , the stone would be thrown off into the air with prodigious velocity and force", "context_len": 49}}}, "viscous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "In its wake followed , a compact mass of viscous substance", "context_len": 11}}}, "joker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "of melaoun , accursed ) ; but the word in the text is plainly mulaa'bein ( objective dual of mulaa ' b , a trickster , malicious joker , hence , by analogy , sharper )", "context_len": 36}}}, "primed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "A practical joker had primed Jephson 's cornet with the sticky stuff , plugging it with a wad", "context_len": 18}}}, "outbursts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Amidst renewed outbursts of cheering the subaltern retired for repairs and renewals , while the lady judges were fortunately spared the task of bestowing the palm upon the cornet champion of the Thirty-somethingths", "context_len": 33}}}, "renewals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Amidst renewed outbursts of cheering the subaltern retired for repairs and renewals , while the lady judges were fortunately spared the task of bestowing the palm upon the cornet champion of the Thirty-somethingths", "context_len": 33}}}, "dander": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Many of the references were pointedly personal ; the victims enjoyed them as much as anyone , for it is difficult to raise a New Zealander 's `` dander '' by means of a practical joke", "context_len": 36}}}, "portraying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "And when the reciter commenced a string of verses portraying the catastrophe in the cabin shared by Lieutenant Nicholson and the Padre , the former 's `` Hear , hear ! '' and the latter 's deep bass laugh were heard above the roars of hilarity", "context_len": 46}}}, "composer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Thus urged , the unwilling composer was hauled to the piano and pressed on to the music-stool , where , with many protestations and much bashfulness , she sang the following song :", "context_len": 33}}}, "snore": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Sin turned in , and soon was heard the music of his snore , And then the Padre set to work as none had worked before", "context_len": 27}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "And no doubt I 'll fall asleep -- I always snore a little at first , and when you hear that you may light the burner in the other room and turn it very low and put the window down from the top and skip off to yourself", "context_len": 48}}}, "thirsts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then with a fierce look in his eye , as one who thirsts for blood , He hurled his weight upon the bunk -- there came a sickening thud -- -- ''", "context_len": 32}}}, "impact": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 46, "context": "He was brought to a rude sense of reality when his raft struck violently on a rock that protruded above the boiling foam of a rapid , so violently that it broke apart , the tough withes that bound it snapped by the force of the impact", "context_len": 47}}}, "slithered": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "She halted in her dance , and the snakes , uncoiling themselves from about her arms and neck , as though they knew that their part in the frenzied performance was over , slithered off quietly into the long marsh grass and sought their lairs", "context_len": 45}}}, "bugle": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` I wondered why you stopped dogging or perhaps I should say ponying my footsteps -- -- Now we 'll play the rising tune , '' and going to the hall she took a bugle from a hook and began a lovely tune , first soft and low , then high and piercing", "context_len": 53}}}, "downed": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 17, "context": "He had voluntarily contracted to risk his life by fighting the Boche , but to be `` downed '' without the chance of seeing a shot fired in earnest was hardly playing the game", "context_len": 34}}}, "bunker": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "Just as Malcolm was releasing the top tier , the vessel gave a heavier roll than usual , and a regular cataract of coal shot towards the mouth of the bunker", "context_len": 31}}}, "compartment": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "On board the lugger the fishermen were busily running the net from one compartment of the vessel into the other , still shaking the fish out as they went on , for a sudden squall at the fishing-ground had compelled them to haul in their nets hastily and run for home", "context_len": 51}}}, "stokehold": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 20, "context": "The vessels comprising the convoy were also cautioned when in the danger-zone to avoid `` starting '' ashes from the stokehold , and throwing garbage and refuse overboard , except at specified times , in order to baffle the hostile submarines ' quest", "context_len": 43}}}, "cigarettes": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Worse ! I wonder what sort of dope they put in those cigarettes ? I like a good pipe or a cigar , but I 'm blessed if I can go those coffin nails ! Ah , that air smells good , '' and he breathed in deep of the September air at the window", "context_len": 56}}}, "torpedoed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The troops , however , were quite composed , beyond indulging in friendly bets as to their chances of arriving at Plymouth without being torpedoed", "context_len": 25}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` In the event of this craft being torpedoed , you men will form the crew of this boat", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` You 've been torpedoed already , have you ? '' enquired an Australian , addressing Jack Kennedy", "context_len": 18}}}, "corns": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "While there 's danger they are as well-behaved as kids in a drawing-room ; directly it 's over they let themselves go and start treading on each other 's corns", "context_len": 30}}}, "jamboree": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "A fine old jamboree ! ''", "context_len": 6}}}, "unescorted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Lying helpless on the water was Transport 99 , unescorted and with no other friendly vessel within ten hours ' steaming of her", "context_len": 23}}}, "raider": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "On the other hand there were half a dozen Maxims and nearly two thousand rifles on board , although these would be of little use if the raider kept beyond 200 yards ' range", "context_len": 34}}}, "mobile": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Against an armed and mobile vessel the See Adler would stand but little chance", "context_len": 14}}}, "motors": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "In front of a partly demolished barn , over which was flying a Geneva Cross flag , covered ambulance motors were being filled up with wounded , who , their injuries attended to , were being dispatched to the base hospital", "context_len": 41}}}, "auxiliary": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "They had been the staunchest Indian supporters of the new British colony , and had sent 28 men of auxiliary troops to Colonel Barnwell , to defeat the Tuscarora insurrection on the coast of North Carolina ( 1712-13 ) , when they suddenly revolted on April 15th , 1715 , committed the most atrocious deeds against helpless colonists , and showed themselves to be quite the reverse of what their name indicates ( y\u00e1masi , y\u00e1massi , the Creek term for mild , gentle , peaceable [ 44 ] )", "context_len": 90}}}, "ejecting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The steam had been raised from the auxiliary engines , and the pumps were continuously ejecting water that made its way from the flooded stokehold to the adjoining compartments", "context_len": 29}}}, "artificers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The ship 's artificers were busily engaged in repairing the fractured main steam-pipe", "context_len": 13}}}, "reverberations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Meanwhile there was no line of churned water extending from under the vessel 's quarter as far as the eye could reach , no dull reverberations of the `` screw ''", "context_len": 31}}}, "ennui": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ennui was at all costs to be banished , and the best antidote for that was plenty of hard work", "context_len": 20}}}, "starry": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Two hours later , in the starry night , the new arrival came within hailing distance , and preparations were made to take the Awarua in tow", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "She was very attractive just now , with her face of sunshine and her eyes with their starry look under the long curling lashes", "context_len": 24}}}, "immobility": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "By midnight Transport 99 was moving slowly through the water in the wake of the towing vessel , three hearty cheers from the boys showing their relief at the thought that the tedious period of immobility was at an end", "context_len": 40}}}, "propeller": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Nanu , sweating with the pain of his injured leg , grasped the Kawasaki \u2019 s propeller and leaned his weight on it", "context_len": 23}}}, "cruiser": {"freq": 42, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 42, "word_index": 38, "context": "But , as luck would have it , the weather fell still lighter at sunset on our ninth day out ; and on the following morning at daybreak we found ourselves becalmed within three miles of a British cruiser , which promptly lowered her boats and despatched them to overhaul us ; and by breakfast-time I had the pleasure of finding myself once more under the British flag , our captor proving to be the corvette Cleopatra , by the captain and officers of which I was most kindly received when I had related to them my strange story", "context_len": 99}}}, "media": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "He was a loquacious Welsh-man , always `` in the know '' , and one of the recognized media between the ship 's officers and the rank and file", "context_len": 29}}}, "jap": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Howsomever , they say as a jap cruiser 'as got the hang of the 'Un , an ' you chaps 'ave been done out of a visit to Davy Jones this time", "context_len": 32}}}, "stalwarts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "They were a set of stalwarts , but without exception were up-country farmers and sheep-shearers before they left New Zealand for the still distant Front", "context_len": 25}}}, "reproaching": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The 21-knot cruiser , although steaming under natural draught , was cutting rings round her charge , as if reproaching her for her tardiness", "context_len": 24}}}, "chocks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The davits were swung inboard , the boat resting on `` chocks '' or hinged pieces of wood shaped to fit the lower strakes of the boat", "context_len": 27}}}, "hinged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The davits were swung inboard , the boat resting on `` chocks '' or hinged pieces of wood shaped to fit the lower strakes of the boat", "context_len": 27}}}, "strakes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The davits were swung inboard , the boat resting on `` chocks '' or hinged pieces of wood shaped to fit the lower strakes of the boat", "context_len": 27}}}, "plug": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "The farmer with the plug hat he-hawed at the top of his voice , the miserable owner of the eggs got mad at him , some words ensued , the farmer started after him , the egg owner ran , once outside fired an egg which struck the smooth , shiny tile with a splatter , and the farmer came back into the express office holding his nose , bareheaded , and looking for his rejected straw head-covering", "context_len": 78}}}, "humorist": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "You , '' addressing the would-be humorist , `` will attend to the gripes -- -- ''", "context_len": 17}}}, "gripes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "You , '' addressing the would-be humorist , `` will attend to the gripes -- -- ''", "context_len": 17}}}, "manning": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "To lower away with a practised crew manning the falls would entail a certain amount of risk should the boat surge against the ship 's side ; with a crowd of raw amateurs the danger was magnified threefold", "context_len": 38}}}, "amateurs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "To lower away with a practised crew manning the falls would entail a certain amount of risk should the boat surge against the ship 's side ; with a crowd of raw amateurs the danger was magnified threefold", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "When the mooring-buoy had been thrown overboard , the rope had caught between the bobstay and the stem , with the result that for the last hour the three raw amateurs in salt-water seamanship had been simply keeping their craft straining at the end of the buoy-rope", "context_len": 47}}}, "magnified": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "To lower away with a practised crew manning the falls would entail a certain amount of risk should the boat surge against the ship 's side ; with a crowd of raw amateurs the danger was magnified threefold", "context_len": 38}}}, "hoisting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Urged on by his frantic thoughts , the cowboy began hoisting the window", "context_len": 13}}}, "rasping": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was not the constant clatter of machinery and the rasping of dozens of circular saws in the adjoining factories that kept him awake , but the excitement of the day , culminating in the discovery of his elder brother , whom he had regarded as dead for months past", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "The man was drenched with perspiration and so nearly exhausted with his suffocating imprisonment , that his voice was rasping and hollow", "context_len": 22}}}, "vertically": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Perhaps we 're aground , '' suggested Fortescue , and thrusting his oar vertically into the water he sounded", "context_len": 20}}}, "bowman": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The bowman , making a frantic grab at the upper block of the davit , hung on like grim death until his feet found a hold on the edge of the foremost thwart", "context_len": 33}}}, "davit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The bowman , making a frantic grab at the upper block of the davit , hung on like grim death until his feet found a hold on the edge of the foremost thwart", "context_len": 33}}}, "numbing": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "But across the heath a clump of trees rising against the pale sky seemed to shelter a group of buildings , where possibly some charitable hand might bestow broken meat on a beggar , or at least a corner in a rick-yard might afford a shelter from the bitter frost that was numbing his limbs", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "With an agility so rapid that the spectators had not time to grasp its significance , the Maori regained his feet , dealt a numbing blow upon the biceps of his antagonist , and was off to the opposite corner of the ring", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "With a disconcerting jar that wellnigh dislocated his wrist , and sent a numbing pain through his right arm , the lad realized that he was up against great odds", "context_len": 30}}}, "obliquely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "As it was , in spite of the momentum of his leap , he struck the water obliquely , and with a thud that temporarily winded him", "context_len": 27}}}, "calcium": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Midway between the would-be rescuer and the object of his attentions floated the life-buoy , its position clearly indicated by a cloud of calcium smoke", "context_len": 25}}}, "hampered": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "But the battle continued to rage , and , though he was hampered by his helmet and sorely outnumbered , the valour displayed by the holder of the fort might yet have gained him the day , if Jim , warned by a cry from Hal that the water in the barrel was giving out , had not succeeded in grasping the flagstaff", "context_len": 63}}}, "macready": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Macready was certainly right about the low temperature of the sea", "context_len": 11}}}, "waistcoats": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I have one of those air waistcoats , '' explained the man", "context_len": 13}}}, "emitting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Around these the survivors gathered every day at sunrise , noon , sunset , emitting convulsive cries during thirty to forty days", "context_len": 22}}}, "callousness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He was also at a loss to explain the apparent callousness of the responsible officer of Transport 99 in not promptly lowering a boat and effecting a speedy rescue", "context_len": 29}}}, "escorting": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 70, "context": "She had a confused idea that Lilian was trying to come to her across a row of benches , and was being held back by a teacher ; but otherwise she scarcely knew what was happening , except that she seemed to be the centre for all the eight hundred eyes in the room , till Miss Pope took her by the shoulder and marched her away like a warder escorting a very small convict to gaol", "context_len": 77}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "She had seen the escorting vessels and had taken the risk , but she had reckoned without the far-seeing eyes of the Blimp", "context_len": 23}}}, "circling": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 17, "context": "One or more of the animals would begin a timid , hesitating approach , frequently bounding or circling away for some distance , halting and advancing again , hypnotized by the singular sight whose nature they could not fathom without a closer view", "context_len": 43}}}, "signalman": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Not only was a light showing from the Pomfret Castle ; it was blinking , sending a message in Morse , although the signalman of the cruiser was unable to decipher the code", "context_len": 33}}}, "semaphore": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "A semaphore message was sent to the destroyer next astern to keep a look-out for the drowning man , but he was not seen again", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Semaphore the convoy and report that the men have been picked up", "context_len": 12}}}, "barrister": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "In bow , '' ordered the midshipman , a youth of sixteen or seventeen with the assurance of a successful barrister", "context_len": 21}}}, "quits": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Quits !", "context_len": 2}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Back came the answer : `` Now we are quits ! Congratulations ! ''", "context_len": 14}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Roger is right , he said after a time ; the fellow who knows he \u2019 s right and quits isn \u2019 t worthy to come out on top", "context_len": 29}}}, "convoy": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 31, "word_index": 14, "context": "With the utmost audacity a U-boat had lain submerged across the track of the convoy , trusting to be able to launch her bolt and disappear before even the swift destroyers could take her bearings , and close upon the spot where the tips of her periscopes had appeared when the torpedo had been discharged", "context_len": 55}}}, "convoying": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "The Gosport coaled and left for the Pacific , Until it was definitely established that German raiders no longer infested the route between Wellington and the Horn the presence of a few light cruisers was necessary , otherwise armed merchant-cruisers could effectually perform convoying duties , and release the `` pukka '' warships for other duties in home waters", "context_len": 59}}}, "relaxation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "Today I noticed Alfgar , the son of Anlaf , at the high mass , and felt a little discomposed at the relaxation of discipline , which , contrary to the canons of the church , permits the unbaptized , as well as persons who ought rightly to be deemed excommunicate , or at least penitents , to be present at the holy mysteries", "context_len": 64}}}, "mascot": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "This story tells how the Boys \u2019 Athletic Club was despoiled of its trophies in a strange manner , and how , among other things stolen , was the Golden Eagle mascot", "context_len": 32}}}, "mashed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "His hay ration 's expended , but the cook 's keeping him going on biscuit mashed in 'bubbly '", "context_len": 19}}}, "scoff": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` And if , '' continued the `` bunting-tosser '' , carried away by his brilliant brain-wave , `` Panjie was to fall in with the physical-exercise party , an ' skip round the ship 'arf a dozen times afore breakfast , I 'll allow he 'd scoff his 'ard tack without a murmur", "context_len": 54}}}, "casemate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Apparently indifferent to the grant of limited freedom , it ambled to a recess formed by the side of the casemate and the raised coaming of a closed ammunition-hoist", "context_len": 29}}}, "barricaded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "The Lady Bertha was comforting her two little girls , Ostryth and Alfreda , where they sat , cowering and terrified , in their own little bedchamber , the window so barricaded that no arrow could enter , but yet not sufficiently to keep out the glare of the flames", "context_len": 50}}}, "loaned": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 14, "context": "When we had talked it all over , I took some things Roger had loaned me and left them in the gymnasium , after which I crossed over to the field that I might let Roger know", "context_len": 37}}}, "coign": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Every coign of vantage was packed with interested lower-deck humanity , while from both the fore and after bridges the officers forgathered to watch the performance", "context_len": 26}}}, "forgathered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "In brief , Sling Camp was a piece of New Zealand soil planted in England , where the pick of the manhood of the Southern Dominion forgathered for the final polishing touches of the noble profession of arms", "context_len": 38}}}, "corral": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Panjie 's cage , carried by half a dozen lusty blue-jackets , was deposited in close proximity to the only opening left in the extensive corral", "context_len": 26}}}, "barbers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Not since the eventful day when the Gosport's barbers close-clipped Bingo , the monkey , had such interest been shown in any unofficial incident", "context_len": 24}}}, "unofficial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Not since the eventful day when the Gosport's barbers close-clipped Bingo , the monkey , had such interest been shown in any unofficial incident", "context_len": 24}}}, "ape": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 45, "context": "If , as you say , it was the Spirit of the Jungle that twice saved his life yesterday , the Spirit might be angry if Bomba were afraid and perhaps the next time would not save him from the hairy arm of the great ape or the sharp claws of the jaguar", "context_len": 53}}}, "paraffin": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Cropping , and afterwards washing the animal with grease and paraffin , were the only remedies , and but temporary ; for , shorn of its warm fur , the monkey caught pneumonia and succumbed", "context_len": 35}}}, "pneumonia": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Cropping , and afterwards washing the animal with grease and paraffin , were the only remedies , and but temporary ; for , shorn of its warm fur , the monkey caught pneumonia and succumbed", "context_len": 35}}}, "arena": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Heralded by the chief keeper , a corporal of Red Marines , the kangaroo leapt lightly into the arena in an attitude reminiscent of a light-weight boxer", "context_len": 27}}}, "reminiscent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Heralded by the chief keeper , a corporal of Red Marines , the kangaroo leapt lightly into the arena in an attitude reminiscent of a light-weight boxer", "context_len": 27}}}, "boxer": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}}}, "coaming": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Apparently indifferent to the grant of limited freedom , it ambled to a recess formed by the side of the casemate and the raised coaming of a closed ammunition-hoist", "context_len": 29}}}, "concertina": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Now then , you concertina boys , give us a two-step , '' he called out", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It was some distance behind us , but I could see men sitting tipped back on chairs on the veranda , and someone was playing on a concertina", "context_len": 28}}}, "gender": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Either the kangaroo objected to the marine 's mistake in the matter of gender or else he was disinclined to be forced to perform , for , as the Corporal grasped the animal 's short fore paws , Panjie let rip with one of his powerful hind legs", "context_len": 48}}}, "impaired": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The kangaroo might have been off his feed , but his muscular powers seemed in no way impaired", "context_len": 18}}}, "sliced": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "We had sliced any number of heads off , and after we had carried the rich cargo to our own ship we had blown up the merchant ship without a word of apology", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He winked at Hap Newton , who smiled back despite a sliced cheek", "context_len": 13}}}, "jollies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "At the possibility of a scrap the ship 's company cheered , some yelling encouragement to the kangaroo , others backing the representative of His Majesty 's jollies", "context_len": 28}}}, "stupendous": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 0, "context": "Stupendous though the task was , not a man flinched , although several groused at the lack of consideration on the part of the Staff to send them against a prepared position in a practically-unbroken state ; which showed that the troops were generally ignorant of the measures taken to safeguard them", "context_len": 52}}}, "bluejackets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The latter , betraying no sign of recognition , walked past him ; then , before his companion or any of the bluejackets on deck could prevent him , he cleared the stanchion-rails and leapt headlong into the sea", "context_len": 39}}}, "accountant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Like a dart Panjie dived betwixt the bowed legs of the accountant officer , and , in blind desperation , butted the senior officer of the engineering branch full in the chest", "context_len": 32}}}, "suction": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "The resolute boy breasted the waves ; but so far from being sucked in , he found it impossible to reach the spot where the ball lay , and the suction began , by reason of the wind , which blew directly in his face , and the sea , that , beyond the influence of the breaker , drove directly to the shore ; and , worn out with effort , he returned exhausted to the boat", "context_len": 78}}}, "propellers": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "Although the transports were still a considerable distance from the U-boat danger zone , every revolution of their propellers was bringing them nearer to that part of the South Atlantic where vessels had been known-to have been destroyed by mines", "context_len": 40}}}, "lassos": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "And it was well they did so , for hardly had the lassos been drawn in than they were flung out again with so strong and well-directed an aim that , had Jim not set them the example of flying , one or more of them would have been made prisoners again", "context_len": 52}}}, "verbatim": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He , in common with nearly all the officers and crew , had read the semaphore verbatim", "context_len": 17}}}, "temerity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Sobbing slightly under his breath , and a very little alarmed at the temerity of his own act , little Roy ran down this street", "context_len": 25}}}, "concur": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Do you concur ? ''", "context_len": 5}}}, "adiposity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Here Sergeant Malcolm Carr and Rifleman Macready were received in exchange for Panjie , who , thanks to the store of fodder on board the transport , had been fed into a state of adiposity", "context_len": 35}}}, "tranship": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Meanwhile a reserve transport had been sent across from Simon 's Bay , and orders were given to tranship the troops , stores , and baggage from No", "context_len": 28}}}, "troopships": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "In double-column line ahead the greyhounds of the sea tore to meet the approaching troopships , then , at a signal from the senior officer , the destroyers `` broke line '' , tearing hither and thither seemingly without order or reason -- zigzagging , pirouetting , and crossing each others ' bows as if participating in an intricate maritime dance", "context_len": 61}}}, "transports": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 26, "context": "The train , upon arrival at the place of embarkation , ran straight into the docks , and brought up close to one of the many transports that were berthed there with banked fires ready to sail at any hour of the day or night", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "They were probably Jap landing barges , Barry thought , crammed with armed soldiers from one of the troop transports that had gone down", "context_len": 24}}}, "zone": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "Although the transports were still a considerable distance from the U-boat danger zone , every revolution of their propellers was bringing them nearer to that part of the South Atlantic where vessels had been known-to have been destroyed by mines", "context_len": 40}}}, "undulations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "They lacked the gnarled appearance of those of his antagonist , but their easy , rhythmic undulations contrasted favourably with the jerky , bombastic movements of the Afrikander 's muscles and sinews", "context_len": 32}}}, "signalling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Again the Gosport's flashing-lamp began signalling ; but while the message , whatever it might mean , was still in progress , two tongues of flame leapt from the cruiser 's side , and the simultaneous roar of a double report crashed through the night", "context_len": 45}}}, "detonation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "An instant later a stupendous blaze of light , followed by a detonation the volume of which completely drowned all other sounds , dazzled the eyes and burst upon the ears of the spectators", "context_len": 34}}}, "hatchways": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "From the troop-decks of the transports crowds of men poured through the hatchways", "context_len": 13}}}, "searchlights": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "Matt leaned forward to watch the glow from the searchlights as it played over the massive iron work , penetrated the heavy bars , and lost itself in a dense mass of trees and shrubbery beyond", "context_len": 36}}}, "radius": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "So great was the Huns ' fear of air raids over the industrial towns of the Rhine valley that several of the larger places of detention for prisoners of war had been broken up , and the men sent to numerous small camps in close proximity to towns within the radius of hostile airmen", "context_len": 54}}}, "unit": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Already they had practical proof that at almost every knot of the twelve-thousand-miles voyage they were open to attack -- Providence and the armed unit of Britain 's fleet permitting", "context_len": 30}}}, "telescopes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Kept under observation by means of telescopes and binoculars , the derelict told its own tale", "context_len": 16}}}, "binoculars": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Kept under observation by means of telescopes and binoculars , the derelict told its own tale", "context_len": 16}}}, "shelled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "A gaping hole on one side and a shattered gun-wale on the other , together with traces of fire , showed that the boat had been shelled", "context_len": 27}}}, "perpetrators": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "There were distinct signs that the perpetrators of the outrage had sought to obliterate all traces of their dastardly work : the name of the ship had been scraped off the boat 's bows , her air-tight tanks had been stove in , yet in spite of this precaution the boat still remained awash", "context_len": 54}}}, "obliterate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "There were distinct signs that the perpetrators of the outrage had sought to obliterate all traces of their dastardly work : the name of the ship had been scraped off the boat 's bows , her air-tight tanks had been stove in , yet in spite of this precaution the boat still remained awash", "context_len": 54}}}, "dastardly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "He would have denied it could he have found words in which to do it , had he had time to frame a denial , but he was so entirely off his guard , so confounded by Seabrooke 's sudden accusation and this evidence of the dastardly deed he had performed that he was utterly overwhelmed , and stood speechless , and the picture of detected guilt", "context_len": 67}}}, "leaked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "In spite of all possible care , the secret of the state of affairs at the Abbey soon leaked out in the neighbourhood , causing the utmost consternation and regret , for the Vaughans were universally liked and respected , while for Mr", "context_len": 43}}}, "torpedo": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 51, "context": "With the utmost audacity a U-boat had lain submerged across the track of the convoy , trusting to be able to launch her bolt and disappear before even the swift destroyers could take her bearings , and close upon the spot where the tips of her periscopes had appeared when the torpedo had been discharged", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Her sides were painted in red , white , and blue horizontal stripes , while amidships her name was displayed in letters six feet in height -- in conformity with an arbitrary regulation made by an unscrupulous nation whose U-boats did not hesitate to torpedo at sight , despite the distinguishing marks of neutral craft", "context_len": 55}}}, "projectiles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "5-inch howitzers , supported by the giant 12- and 14-inch long-range guns farther back , threw tons of metal upon the enemy lines , the heavier projectiles hurtling overhead with a roar like that of an express train", "context_len": 38}}}, "coaled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "The Gosport coaled and left for the Pacific , Until it was definitely established that German raiders no longer infested the route between Wellington and the Horn the presence of a few light cruisers was necessary , otherwise armed merchant-cruisers could effectually perform convoying duties , and release the `` pukka '' warships for other duties in home waters", "context_len": 59}}}, "raiders": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Gosport coaled and left for the Pacific , Until it was definitely established that German raiders no longer infested the route between Wellington and the Horn the presence of a few light cruisers was necessary , otherwise armed merchant-cruisers could effectually perform convoying duties , and release the `` pukka '' warships for other duties in home waters", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "At least half of the raiders had been incapacitated , and among the wounded had been Nascanora himself , and all the unwounded survivors were at that moment rushing pell-mell through the jungle , frantically eager to put as many miles as possible between themselves and the cabin , where the white man \u2019 s magic had been capped by the calling of the jungle beasts to help", "context_len": 68}}}, "pukka": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "The Gosport coaled and left for the Pacific , Until it was definitely established that German raiders no longer infested the route between Wellington and the Horn the presence of a few light cruisers was necessary , otherwise armed merchant-cruisers could effectually perform convoying duties , and release the `` pukka '' warships for other duties in home waters", "context_len": 59}}}, "peninsula": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 8, "context": "Near this place , in a bend or peninsula formed by the Tallapoosa river , called Horse Shoe by the whites , the American troops achieved a decisive victory over the Red Stick party of the Creek Indians on March 25th , 1814 , which resulted in the surrender of Weatherford , their leader , and put an end to this bloody campaign", "context_len": 63}}}, "terminates": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "He went first along the southern coast , until at length he came to a place where a mountain chain , called Taurus , comes down to the sea-coast , where it terminates abruptly in cliffs and precipices , leaving only a narrow beach between them and the water below", "context_len": 50}}}, "upland": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "The others agreed without enthusiasm , although loyalty to their chum left no plausible alternative ; so at a steady pace they set out along an upland track that led to the farm", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "After another minute of reflection he turned his face toward the upland , where , in the distance , the white porch and gables of a farmhouse were visible through an opening between two rows of orchard trees", "context_len": 38}}}, "vociferous": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "But their dogs were awake , and saluted us with a vociferous barking , and would not allow us to land until they were driven away by the oars which our theows used with much effect upon their hides", "context_len": 39}}}, "elicited": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Enquiries of the railway officials elicited the information that a train would leave at 7", "context_len": 15}}}, "recriminations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Mutual recriminations were absent , although they knew that it was a serious matter to overstay shore leave", "context_len": 18}}}, "overstay": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Mutual recriminations were absent , although they knew that it was a serious matter to overstay shore leave", "context_len": 18}}}, "cancelled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I want you to get it cancelled ; I 'll give you the order", "context_len": 15}}}, "infantrymen": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "When at length the train crawled out of Muizenberg station the three `` Diggers '' ( as New Zealand infantrymen are commonly dubbed amongst themselves ) had for company a sympathetic fellow-passenger , who on hearing of their plight was quick to suggest a plan", "context_len": 45}}}, "suburb": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "109 is lying nearest in-shore off Woodstock -- that 's a suburb of Cape Town , you know", "context_len": 18}}}, "evinced": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 57, "context": "The death of Parmenio and of his son in this violent manner , when , too , there was so little evidence of their guilt , made a very general and a very unfavorable impression in respect to Alexander ; and not long afterward another case occurred , in some respects still more painful , as it evinced still more strikingly that the mind of Alexander , which had been in his earlier days filled with such noble and lofty sentiments of justice and generosity , was gradually getting to be under the supreme dominion of selfish and ungovernable passions : it was the case of Clitus", "context_len": 107}}}, "slowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The train slowing down as it approached Woodstock station terminated the conversation", "context_len": 12}}}, "chorused": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Oh yes , we know where that is , '' was the chorused reply", "context_len": 15}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "Between each announcement , the second lieutenants softly chorused : Woof , woof ! Woof , woof ! When Barry lifted a large baked potato from the serving dish it was announced that The Big Dog is getting bombed up", "context_len": 40}}}, "estuary": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The sun was sinking with fading splendour behind the lofty downs in the west , and casting his departing beams on the river , the estuary , with the fleet , and the blue hills of Hampshire in the far distance", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The fleet bore the troops of savage soldiery safely -- too safely -- across the waters of the Solent , to the estuary formed by the Medina , where now thousands of visitors seek health and repose , and the towers of Osborne crown the eastern eminences", "context_len": 47}}}, "productive": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Nor was Fortescue 's suggestion to transfer the gear to a lighter craft productive of better results", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 45, "context": "Wealth and plunder prompted the German barbarians , at the beginning of the medi\u00e6val epoch of history , to migrate to the south of Europe ; here , in the Gulf territories , the inducement lay more especially in the quest of a country more productive in grains , edible roots , fish and game", "context_len": 55}}}, "belts": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "This suited his companions , and soon they were cleaning their guns , sorting cartridges and fitting them in their belts , taking care not to make the mistake Frank did , when he was treed by the wildcat ; and looking to their clothing and hunting boots", "context_len": 48}}}, "propulsion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "The three men stripped , secured their clothing on top of their heads by means of their belts , and , two of them taking an oar in case the moored craft was destitute If means of propulsion , they slipped boldly into the water", "context_len": 45}}}, "unbuckled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Holding on to the gunwale with one hand , he unbuckled his bundle and tossed it into the boat ; then , clambering over the stern , proceeded to dress while his companions `` got aboard ''", "context_len": 37}}}, "weighing": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Accordingly , Alaeddin brought out the platter to the old man , who took it and weighing it in his scales , said to him , `` Was it the like of this thou usest to sell to the Jew ? '' `` Ay , '' replied Alaeddin , `` its like and its brother", "context_len": 56}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The new ones have not yet arrived , and in the meantime , as a temporary accommodation , our weighing is done up at the in-freight platform by the official weigh master of the road", "context_len": 35}}}, "chunk": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "Do you know , he carried you right back to the advance dressing-station , and that you were both bowled over by a shell just the other side of Hannebeke stream ? That 's how you got it in the foot , and Te Paheka had a chunk taken off his shoulder", "context_len": 52}}}, "graft": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Jolly hard graft , '' muttered Malcolm breathlessly after a quarter of an hour 's strenuous work", "context_len": 18}}}, "contraption": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "That 's put the kybosh on the whole contraption", "context_len": 9}}}, "overstayed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "The young people talked gaily for some minutes longer , and then Madge , with a look at the clock , said that it was about time Miss Philock would be back to see that Tom had not overstayed", "context_len": 39}}}, "bash": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` If he is a Boche , and I run across him , I 'll bash him , '' said Selwyn vehemently", "context_len": 22}}}, "jackal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Currents , you young jackal ! '' he exclaimed", "context_len": 10}}}, "jackasses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Do you think this is a Bath-bun shop ? We are a crowd of jackasses", "context_len": 16}}}, "shroud": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Jack Frost threw photographs of fairyland upon the windows , and hung the roofs with fringes of crystal pendants , while the snowflakes piled themselves over the fences and made a shroud for the trees , and every day Pauline , with this strange peace in her heart , did her housework to the glory of God", "context_len": 57}}}, "bobstay": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "When the mooring-buoy had been thrown overboard , the rope had caught between the bobstay and the stem , with the result that for the last hour the three raw amateurs in salt-water seamanship had been simply keeping their craft straining at the end of the buoy-rope", "context_len": 47}}}, "mercurial": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Again under cover , their mercurial spirits rose , and when at length the rain ceased , and the train rumbled betwixt the red-earthed , verdant coombes of Devon , bathed in brilliant sunshine , the Anzacs unanimously voted that there were worse places on earth than the Old Country", "context_len": 50}}}, "fancying": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "Nevertheless , he awoke suddenly , fancying that he was dreaming ; for to his ears floated the sound of a violin , on which someone was playing the tune that had so moved him as he was beginning his flight from Oakdale , Home , Sweet Home", "context_len": 48}}}, "tommy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I suppose neither of you thought to bring along any tommy ? ''", "context_len": 13}}}, "commandeered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The vessel was a Union Castle liner commandeered by the Government", "context_len": 11}}}, "excepted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The celebration of the p\u00faskita had a favorable influence upon the minds of the people , for it was a signal of amnesty , absolving the Indian of all crimes , murder excepted , and seemed to bury guilt itself in oblivion", "context_len": 42}}}, "teemed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Still , the omission was soon rectified , and the Home ere long teemed with children", "context_len": 16}}}, "detachments": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 28, "context": "He then went on for a couple of days , when he encamped , in order to send out foraging parties -- that is to say , small detachments , dispatched to explore the surrounding country in search of grain and other food for the horses", "context_len": 46}}}, "believer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Gardner , to whom , with others , he was giving lessons in languages ; and as a result of his intercourse with this man he became again the same simple loving believer that he had been when he learnt to read the Bible at his mother 's knee , or braved the taunts of his school-fellows", "context_len": 57}}}, "surgical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "At first the doctors said he would not get over it , then that if he got over it he would be an idiot ; but finally their surgical skill and careful nursing were rewarded , and he came out well in every respect , except for an awful scar along one side of his head", "context_len": 56}}}, "embellished": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "They are the `` masters of ceremonies , '' and the name h\u00edniha , \u00edniha , which is no longer understood by the present generation , is said to signify `` self-adorner , '' in the sense of `` warrior embellished with body paint", "context_len": 44}}}, "disloyal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "During his stay at Cape Town he had been forced into a squabble with a crowd of disloyal Cape Dutch", "context_len": 20}}}, "contusion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Words led to blows , with the result that three of his opponents were picked up insensible , while Kennedy was taken to the military hospital with a broken wrist and a nasty contusion of the forehead , caused by the nail-shod boot of an eighteen-stone antagonist", "context_len": 47}}}, "kits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` No kits ? '' continued Kennedy", "context_len": 7}}}, "squabbles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "In spite of the utmost endurance on the part of the Imperial officers , petty squabbles were frequent", "context_len": 18}}}, "equanimity": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 28, "context": "But the dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of", "context_len": 128}}}, "championed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "For their part the Maoris accepted the Afrikanders ' remarks with courteous equanimity , but there were others on board who championed them -- with no uncertain voice", "context_len": 28}}}, "codes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "If they kept rigidly to the codes of civil life they would soon have found themselves very much out in the cold as far as personal comforts were concerned , although on board , in camp , and on active service , it was noticeable that personal property was rightly considered as inviolate", "context_len": 53}}}, "inviolate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 52, "context": "If they kept rigidly to the codes of civil life they would soon have found themselves very much out in the cold as far as personal comforts were concerned , although on board , in camp , and on active service , it was noticeable that personal property was rightly considered as inviolate", "context_len": 53}}}, "vanishing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "A few months previously , when Malcolm saw Te Paheka vanishing round a corner as he drove juggernaut at a furious rate , the lad had come to the conclusion that he had seen the last of his Maori friend for many a long day", "context_len": 45}}}, "khaki": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 47, "context": "While the wounded were being removed , a couple of Prussian Poles , who were employed as ambulance-men , placed Malcolm on a stretcher , and threw a discarded greatcoat over his legs , not realizing that he was an enemy , since the remnants of his khaki uniform were indistinguishable from the field-grey after they had been `` chromed '' by the fumes of bursting shells", "context_len": 67}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "A few prisoners in khaki rigs were standing disconsolately at the doors of the huts", "context_len": 15}}}, "legally": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "You must make your own arrangement as to legally representing him , signing his name and the like , and of course you will have to do all the work , for he will be helpless for some time to come", "context_len": 41}}}, "blighter": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` And , '' he added , `` although I 'm not absolutely sure about it , I have an idea that the blighter who let us down on the train from Muizenberg is on board", "context_len": 36}}}, "buck": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 43, "word_index": 25, "context": "Almost before he knew it he was within sight of the boiling spring , and he checked his pace , hoping to come upon the buck licking the salty deposit from the rocks in the little stream that flowed from the place where the mud volcano was", "context_len": 47}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "He had not had much practice in riding at the East , but the practice he had had in California had already made him a good rider , and even if the mustang had taken a fancy to buck he would have found it rather hard to dislodge our young hero", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Evidently that wasn \u2019 t the right way to overcome the conceited young man , so what did Bucephalus do but suddenly buck ? He arched his back , jammed his hoofs together and bounced up and down as if the ground had suddenly become red hot", "context_len": 47}}}, "roped": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "The contests were to take place on the promenade deck , a space having been roped off and seats provided for the officers", "context_len": 23}}}, "rebuffs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Several pairs of sparring-men having displayed their prowess and received indiscriminate praise and rebuffs from their respective supporters , the event of the evening was announced", "context_len": 26}}}, "supporters": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 6, "context": "They had been the staunchest Indian supporters of the new British colony , and had sent 28 men of auxiliary troops to Colonel Barnwell , to defeat the Tuscarora insurrection on the coast of North Carolina ( 1712-13 ) , when they suddenly revolted on April 15th , 1715 , committed the most atrocious deeds against helpless colonists , and showed themselves to be quite the reverse of what their name indicates ( y\u00e1masi , y\u00e1massi , the Creek term for mild , gentle , peaceable [ 44 ] )", "context_len": 90}}}, "compatriots": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "For was it not provocative of racial pride that one of their compatriots should be able to make tunes -- actual tunes ! -- issue from those keys which responded to their own tentative touches merely with thin shrieks or a dull , rumbling note ?", "context_len": 46}}}, "summed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "His heart is as black as Jojasta \u2019 s was , declared Neram , as though that summed up the total of human depravity", "context_len": 24}}}, "challenger": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "A counter-blast of cheering announced the appearance of a challenger -- Sergeant Smithers , of the 2nd Battalion West Othershires", "context_len": 20}}}, "protracted": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "The whole vast field of his conquests became , for many long and weary years after Alexander 's death , the prey to the most ferocious and protracted civil wars", "context_len": 30}}}, "tormenting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 88, "context": "and bright -- saints with halos round their heads , kneeling rapt in prayer , with folded hands , in the midst of green fields and flowers , while the Virgin , clothed in blue and gold , appeared with a whole company of angels from the skies above ; patient martyrs , with wan faces upturned to heaven , while their persecutors flung stones , or heaped on the burning brands ; the blessed passing into the joys of Paradise , with the wicked writhing in the tormenting flames below ; and round all a curious illuminated bordering , where strange faces peered out of twisting foliage , and figures of birds and animals were intertwined with patterns of flowers or the tail of a capital letter", "context_len": 128}}}, "menu": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}}}, "tepid": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 57, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}}}, "outstanding": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}}}, "undermine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "The Sergeant was the best boxer of his regiment , but he had forgotten that a protracted sojourn in the reputed site of the Garden of Eden -- where a boundless expanse of glaring sand , a total absence of verdure , millions of tormenting flies , and a meagre menu consisting chiefly of bully beef and tepid water , are the outstanding characteristics -- is apt to undermine one 's physical condition", "context_len": 73}}}, "olivine": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Te Paheka 's olivine features were wreathed in smiles when Malcolm entered his ward", "context_len": 14}}}, "rhythmic": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "It was a lilting , rhythmic tune , and at the first notes of it the hideous reptiles surrounding Bomba began to sway to the sound and one after another slipped over the edge of the pit and slithered away into the darkness", "context_len": 43}}}, "shorts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Clad in a pair of shorts of a vivid orange hue -- for Te Paheka shared with the rest of the Maoris a love of brilliant colour -- and with a silk red ensign emblazoned with the New Zealand stars round his waist , Te Paheka grinned amicably at the Afrikander", "context_len": 51}}}, "amicably": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "Clad in a pair of shorts of a vivid orange hue -- for Te Paheka shared with the rest of the Maoris a love of brilliant colour -- and with a silk red ensign emblazoned with the New Zealand stars round his waist , Te Paheka grinned amicably at the Afrikander", "context_len": 51}}}, "tattooing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The Maori 's bare chest and back were covered with elaborate tattooing , but , according to modern custom , his face was unmarked", "context_len": 24}}}, "unmarked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The Maori 's bare chest and back were covered with elaborate tattooing , but , according to modern custom , his face was unmarked", "context_len": 24}}}, "handicap": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "He knew the Maori to be a good boxer , as most natives are , but age , if only ten or fifteen years , together with inferior reach and weight , must assuredly handicap Te Paheka considerably", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "The man , despite the handicap of wearing heavy steel plates , ducked agilely , and , reversing his rifle , prodded the New Zealander with the butt of his weapon", "context_len": 31}}}, "incautiously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "The Maoris yelled with delight , but the next instant their hopes were dashed to the ground as Te Paheka , incautiously attempting to follow up his advantage , laid himself open to a terrific blow from the Afrikander 's right", "context_len": 41}}}, "sanded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The girl turned on him quick with a little stamp , of her neat foot on the sanded floor", "context_len": 19}}}, "fateful": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The cool , deliberate voice of the timekeeper calling off the fateful ten seconds silenced all other sounds of approbation or encouragement to the fallen man", "context_len": 26}}}, "biceps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "With an agility so rapid that the spectators had not time to grasp its significance , the Maori regained his feet , dealt a numbing blow upon the biceps of his antagonist , and was off to the opposite corner of the ring", "context_len": 43}}}, "boxers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Before the boxers could engage again `` Time '' sounded", "context_len": 10}}}, "waltzing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` You 're supposed to be sparring , not going in for a waltzing race", "context_len": 15}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "'School is over , hooray ! ' shouted Peggy , banging down her books on the mounting-block , and waltzing into the kitchen , where Aunt Helen and Nancy were busy making jam", "context_len": 33}}}, "cornered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` By Jove , he 's cornered ! '' exclaimed Fortescue , as Te Paheka , stepping back to avoid a left-hander , came in contact with the ropes", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "Your friend here , young Stirling , has got the whip-hand -- I am cornered , and admit it", "context_len": 19}}}, "rasped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I suppose so , '' rasped out the Latin instructor", "context_len": 11}}}, "plexus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "An upper cut caught van Eindhovengen full on the point of his chin , while almost simultaneously the Maori drove home a resounding blow on the Afrikander 's solar plexus", "context_len": 30}}}, "racial": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 6, "context": "For was it not provocative of racial pride that one of their compatriots should be able to make tunes -- actual tunes ! -- issue from those keys which responded to their own tentative touches merely with thin shrieks or a dull , rumbling note ?", "context_len": 46}}}, "ported": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "For four days a course due west was maintained , until the vessels ported helm and Stood north , it being the rule that no two convoys should shape the same course through the North Atlantic", "context_len": 36}}}, "convoys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "For four days a course due west was maintained , until the vessels ported helm and Stood north , it being the rule that no two convoys should shape the same course through the North Atlantic", "context_len": 36}}}, "hooker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "We only had ten minutes before the old hooker sank", "context_len": 10}}}, "disabling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The Maxims began hurling nickel at the rate of 450 shots a minute , with the idea of either disabling the periscope or churning up the water in its vicinity , in order to make it impossible for the U-boat to discharge a torpedo with any degree of accuracy", "context_len": 49}}}, "periscope": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "The Maxims began hurling nickel at the rate of 450 shots a minute , with the idea of either disabling the periscope or churning up the water in its vicinity , in order to make it impossible for the U-boat to discharge a torpedo with any degree of accuracy", "context_len": 49}}}, "churning": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Osborne arrived o ' Monday , with me in the midst of my washing , and never a bit of cake in the house ; and there was Squire Henley and his lady o ' churning day , and I had to leave the butter half made to bring in tea ; and ten to one there 'll be someone more o ' Friday , when I 'm cleaning my kitchens", "context_len": 71}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "Pigs , poultry , and young animals had to be fed , and the eggs gathered in daily ; the dairy claimed constant attention , for the pans must be scalded and the pails polished bright , and though Joe did the milking and churning , it was Lilian 's business to superintend the making up of the butter , and to weigh each pound with her own hands and print it with the Abbey stamp", "context_len": 76}}}, "ricochetting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Simultaneously the helm was starboarded , and the Pomfret Castle steered straight for the patch where the machine-gun bullets were ricochetting from the water", "context_len": 24}}}, "diver": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "The breath of the person who saw it for the first time was held as he looked in vain for the brave diver , or wondered whether the act he had seen was not some mad effort to destroy life", "context_len": 40}}}, "submarine": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "It was not until it was explained to the Afrikanders that the ownerless book was a means by which they might be sent to the bottom of the sea by a hostile submarine that Oom Jan `` rounded '' on the spy", "context_len": 42}}}, "hallucination": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` I am forced to come to the conclusion that you are the victim of an unaccountable hallucination , my lad , '' observed Fortescue to Malcolm , shortly after the diver incident", "context_len": 33}}}, "doggo": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I The fellow , if he is on board , could n't lie doggo all this time", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "No need to investigate lest a living Fritz was lying doggo with the dead", "context_len": 14}}}, "pal": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 4, "context": "Billy Smith and his pal , Nuky , with the aid of Billy \u2019 s father set a trap to catch desperate gangsters which they succeed in doing after many thrilling adventures", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "His voice trailed off into silence and young Dallas said eagerly , `` Let 's have this wonderful pony for a pal too , and let 's have some fancy names", "context_len": 31}}}, "laddie": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Pardon me , laddie , '' said Fortescue firmly , `` but you were n't at all sure about it at the time", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` Kia ora , laddie , '' said Fortescue heartily", "context_len": 10}}}, "portents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "A keen-witted kapitan-leutnant of a U-boat would not fail to take advantage of these it `` signs and portents '' , hence the advisability of giving the steamer 's track a wide berth", "context_len": 33}}}, "cautioned": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 7, "context": "The vessels comprising the convoy were also cautioned when in the danger-zone to avoid `` starting '' ashes from the stokehold , and throwing garbage and refuse overboard , except at specified times , in order to baffle the hostile submarines ' quest", "context_len": 43}}}, "garbage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The vessels comprising the convoy were also cautioned when in the danger-zone to avoid `` starting '' ashes from the stokehold , and throwing garbage and refuse overboard , except at specified times , in order to baffle the hostile submarines ' quest", "context_len": 43}}}, "specified": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The vessels comprising the convoy were also cautioned when in the danger-zone to avoid `` starting '' ashes from the stokehold , and throwing garbage and refuse overboard , except at specified times , in order to baffle the hostile submarines ' quest", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "They voted that plan a good one , and soon afterward William was tramping back through the woods , having promised to be on hand at the time specified", "context_len": 29}}}, "baffle": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "Take him what he seeketh , and that forthright , so he may know that I can avail unto that which he requireth , ay , and more , and that he was deluded by the Vizier ; albeit he thought to baffle me , he and his Vizier", "context_len": 49}}}, "submarines": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "The vessels comprising the convoy were also cautioned when in the danger-zone to avoid `` starting '' ashes from the stokehold , and throwing garbage and refuse overboard , except at specified times , in order to baffle the hostile submarines ' quest", "context_len": 43}}}, "indictment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Why do you mention the fact ? I plead guilty to the indictment , '' rejoined his chum", "context_len": 19}}}, "erred": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "He came to a determination almost at a glance , and seldom erred in his judgment", "context_len": 16}}}, "stokeholds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Boys , '' exclaimed Kennedy , `` there 's a call for volunteers for the stokeholds ! How about it ? ''", "context_len": 23}}}, "intensive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` The convoy has to increase speed -- we 're about to cross the intensive zone -- and the old tub requires a lot of whacking up", "context_len": 27}}}, "whacking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` The convoy has to increase speed -- we 're about to cross the intensive zone -- and the old tub requires a lot of whacking up", "context_len": 27}}}, "earthquake": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 7, "context": "How Bomba was swallowed up in an earthquake and hurled into a subterranean cavern that promised to become a living tomb ; by what a marvelous combination of nerve and good fortune he reached the open air and sunlight ; how he finally found Jojasta after the latter had been fatally hurt by the fall of his temple ; the partial but not sufficient information he received from the dying man regarding his parents ; the obstacles he surmounted and the perils he escaped are told in the preceding volume of this series , entitled : Bomba the Jungle Boy at the Moving Mountain ; or , The Mystery of the Caves of Fire", "context_len": 114}}}, "greasers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The last sentence was addressed to one of the regular hands , who , stripped to the waist like the rest of the Pomfret Castle's firemen and greasers , was responsible for this particular stokehold", "context_len": 35}}}, "boilers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Others , armed with long-handled shovels , fed the capacious furnaces so frequently that the place reverberated to the clanging of the red-hot metal doors at the ends of the multi-tube boilers", "context_len": 32}}}, "shovelling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Like ants the coal-heavers swarmed over the heaped-up cargo , shovelling the coal into barrows and trundling them along the planks on to the quay , whence they disappeared into a large shed about a hundred yards away", "context_len": 38}}}, "trimmers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "At other bunkers a similar operation was performed by their comrades , the `` trimmers '' being specially instructed to remove the coal in a methodical manner , so that there was slight possibility of the remaining contents being thrown out by the roll of the vessel", "context_len": 47}}}, "capacious": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 38, "context": "Brett 's calm voice continued : `` I am a very plain old woman ; I have no youth to boast of , and no looks to boast of ; but I think I have got a somewhat capacious heart , and it is amply willing to take you all in if you wish to come", "context_len": 56}}}, "reverberated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Others , armed with long-handled shovels , fed the capacious furnaces so frequently that the place reverberated to the clanging of the red-hot metal doors at the ends of the multi-tube boilers", "context_len": 32}}}, "avalanches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Since he was wearing a pair of canvas shoes and no socks , he had to display considerable agility in avoiding the miniature avalanches", "context_len": 24}}}, "girder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Back sprang the lad , crouching the while to prevent bringing his head in contact with a low girder", "context_len": 19}}}, "trundled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The children had their hooples , which they trundled merrily before them and Flossy went capering joyously along , sometimes running ahead , for a short distance , and then rushing back to his little mistresses , and if any rough boys made their appearance , keeping very close at their side till all danger was past", "context_len": 57}}}, "grazes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Beyond a few slight grazes , he was uninjured", "context_len": 9}}}, "desisting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Desisting from his labours , he regarded the mass of coal with studied interest", "context_len": 14}}}, "admonition": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Of the kindly advice and admonition given to Percy by his uncle there is no need to speak further ; but it resulted in making Percy feel that he would do anything rather than again run the risk of forfeiting the good opinion which he now valued more than ever", "context_len": 50}}}, "nugget": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 38, "context": "So , as the miners sat round their roaring fire -- for it was getting chilly in the evening -- one and another discussed the interesting question , `` What would I do if I could find a nugget ? '' Various , of course , were the answers", "context_len": 49}}}, "jawing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` What 's all this jawing about ? '' asked the leading hand", "context_len": 13}}}, "complaisantly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` I agree , '' rejoined Fortescue complaisantly", "context_len": 8}}}, "optics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` But cast your optics on this , my festive shoveller", "context_len": 11}}}, "slab": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "At a turn of the road a great slab of Welsh slatestone lay at a sloping angle , shelving down for a distance of about twenty feet , and with its surface so flat and even , and so smooth and polished by the weather , that it made a natural sliding-board , down which it was delightful to toboggan at full speed", "context_len": 63}}}, "contrivance": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "But Fleming showed such a rooted distrust for anything that was intended to 'go off , ' and , indeed , such absolute abhorrence of any further mechanical contrivance in the vicinity of the stables , that Archie had transferred his attentions to the garden , where he was full of a scheme for utilizing the water power of the little stream to irrigate the soil , after the fashion of the Nile in Egypt , in a series of canals between the beds , and had already made the hose-pipe work with capital effect by means of a siphon and an old barrel", "context_len": 104}}}, "astuteness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The contrivance would have been thrown into the furnace , with disastrous results ; but Malcolm 's astuteness had saved the situation", "context_len": 22}}}, "boiler": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was found in one of the boiler suits supplied to the squad ; it was not there when the suits were issued , consequently the article in question must belong to one of you men", "context_len": 36}}}, "blisters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Fortescue was puffing at his pipe , deep in thought ; Selwyn was idly contemplating the unbroken expanse of horizon ; while Malcolm devoted his attention to the examination of half a dozen large blisters on his hands", "context_len": 38}}}, "stoking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Already soldiering had hardened his hands considerably , but stoking , he decided , had proved to be far more strenuous than bayonet exercise , if an aching back , stiff muscles , and galls as big as half-crowns were any criterion", "context_len": 42}}}, "criterion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Already soldiering had hardened his hands considerably , but stoking , he decided , had proved to be far more strenuous than bayonet exercise , if an aching back , stiff muscles , and galls as big as half-crowns were any criterion", "context_len": 42}}}, "occasioning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Along the crowded troop deck the prisoners and their escort made their way , their presence occasioning little interest on the part of the spectators", "context_len": 25}}}, "defaulters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Defaulters were common objects amongst the different Colonial troops who comprised the Pomfret Castle's passengers", "context_len": 15}}}, "comprised": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 55, "context": "It was late in the afternoon when Malcolm and his two chums alighted at Codford station , and , making their way by a roundabout route along the main street of the village , where old-time cottages and hideous wooden shanties stood cheek by jowl , arrived at the vast array of tin huts that comprised the camp", "context_len": 58}}}, "augmented": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Her charming feats on the back of her trained horse , Blanco , win fame and fortune for herself as well , the latter being augmented later by the discovery of gold on certain lands", "context_len": 35}}}, "foolscap": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "In front of them were sheets of foolscap , a book on military law , and a small object wrapped in brown paper", "context_len": 23}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "As he spoke , he pulled a long , ugly , foolscap envelope out of his pocket , and taking a paper from it , opened it , and showed it to me", "context_len": 33}}}, "preliminaries": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "For , though Grasshopper was very fond of jumping and practiced it every time he got a chance , there was something wrong with his method , and he never could get beyond the preliminaries in a contest", "context_len": 38}}}, "comprise": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "[ 13 ] While calling a person of their own people by the name of Ats\u00e1lagi , in the plural Anits\u00e1lagi , they comprise all the Creeks under the name of K\u00fasa , from Coosa river , or more probably from the ancient , far-famed town of the same name : Ag\u00fasa , K\u00fasa , G\u00fasa , a Creek person ; Anig\u00fasa , the Creek people ; G\u00fasa uniwon\u00ed'hsti , the Creek language", "context_len": 73}}}, "unwrapping": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The Major , unwrapping the paper coverings , held up for inspection the notebook that Selwyn had picked up in his bunker", "context_len": 22}}}, "deuce": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Here , what the deuce do you think you 're doing ? '' asked a well-known voice , despite its tone of plaintive asperity", "context_len": 25}}}, "afterthought": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` You are released from arrest , '' declared the Major curtly ; then , as an afterthought , he added : `` It would be advisable that you maintain discretion over the matter", "context_len": 34}}}, "deciphered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` It has been partly deciphered , and is proved to be a means of communication between someone on board the ship and the U-boats", "context_len": 25}}}, "interrogate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "He means to find the owner , and if necessary is going to interrogate every man who went into that stokehold", "context_len": 21}}}, "suspects": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The Diggers waited until the batch of suspects vanished", "context_len": 9}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Do so cautiously , my father , very cautiously , for if Edric suspects you are on his track , he will plot against your life too , and Ethelgiva will have no protector", "context_len": 35}}}, "bamboozling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Taken for granted that he is a spy , what was his object in bamboozling us ? ''", "context_len": 18}}}, "yapping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` The blighter 's yapping in Dutch , '' announced Fortescue , `` and he can speak English perfectly", "context_len": 19}}}, "hallo": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Here , hallo ! '' he shouted , `` who 's let all that net go trailing overboard ? Here 's a mess ! we shall have to run it all through our hands again", "context_len": 35}}}, "denials": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "A torrent of words , plainly indicating indignant denials , wafted through the closed door", "context_len": 15}}}, "pseudo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "At the court of enquiry the pseudo Waas denied all knowledge of the pocket-book , although van Eindhovengen had seen the man with it in his possession without knowing its sinister import", "context_len": 32}}}, "rendering": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 43, "context": "Bargaining in that throng was no easy matter , but their owners would dash in , seize a pony by the mane and tail , and by sheer force drag it away from its companions , the very small size of the animals rendering practicable what would have been impossible with a larger breed", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "The following is a literal rendering of the above passage : `` An upper chamber ( keszr ) and ( or ? ) a kiosk ( kushk , a word explained by a modern Syrian dictionary as meaning ' [ a building ] like a balcony projecting from the level of the rest of the house , ' but by others as an isolated building or pavilion erected on the top of a house , i", "context_len": 76}}}, "blending": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Already the grey outline of the convoying cruiser was blending into invisibility against the gathering mantle of night", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "That all the jarring notes of life Seem blending in a psalm , And all the angles of its strife Slow rounding into calm", "context_len": 24}}}, "invisibility": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Already the grey outline of the convoying cruiser was blending into invisibility against the gathering mantle of night", "context_len": 18}}}, "campaigning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Some men eager for a glimpse of the country of their birth , which they had not seen for many a long-drawn month of campaigning in the inhospitable waste of Mesopotamia ; others for the first sight of the Mother Country ; others out of mere curiosity ; while the Maoris peered through the dim light to feast upon the prospects of speedily setting foot on dry land", "context_len": 68}}}, "optical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was not much to look at , judged from a strictly optical point of view", "context_len": 16}}}, "lighthouse": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Merely a slender lighthouse , rearing itself itself out of the sea , while miles beyond it , and just visible against the pale rosy tints of dawn , was a line of dark-grey cliffs , backed by higher ground that was totally destitute of trees", "context_len": 46}}}, "greyhounds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "In double-column line ahead the greyhounds of the sea tore to meet the approaching troopships , then , at a signal from the senior officer , the destroyers `` broke line '' , tearing hither and thither seemingly without order or reason -- zigzagging , pirouetting , and crossing each others ' bows as if participating in an intricate maritime dance", "context_len": 61}}}, "zigzagging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "In double-column line ahead the greyhounds of the sea tore to meet the approaching troopships , then , at a signal from the senior officer , the destroyers `` broke line '' , tearing hither and thither seemingly without order or reason -- zigzagging , pirouetting , and crossing each others ' bows as if participating in an intricate maritime dance", "context_len": 61}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "It was followed by a trick play , in which Barker drew attention to himself while Eliot went romping and zigzagging through a broken field and crossed the line for the second touchdown", "context_len": 33}}}, "pirouetting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "In double-column line ahead the greyhounds of the sea tore to meet the approaching troopships , then , at a signal from the senior officer , the destroyers `` broke line '' , tearing hither and thither seemingly without order or reason -- zigzagging , pirouetting , and crossing each others ' bows as if participating in an intricate maritime dance", "context_len": 61}}}, "ensigns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The transports dipped ensigns ; the cruiser returned the compliment in a similar manner as she swung round and retraced her course", "context_len": 22}}}, "gratuitously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "I was born an ' bred at Cawsand , '' he added , gratuitously", "context_len": 14}}}, "grouser": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` I 've seen it three times before , '' remarked another -- the inevitable grouser of the company ; `` and , every time it 's been raining cats and dogs", "context_len": 32}}}, "slush": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 62, "context": "At first they had all floundered hopelessly about , and it had taken some practice even to slide a few steps ; but long before they had reached the stage of skimming over the frozen surface at the rate of eight or ten miles an hour , which had been their fond ambition , the snow had melted into moist and dirty slush , which was particularly trying , as they had just decided to make a sledge out of an old packing-case , and drag Lilian round the pasture", "context_len": 90}}}, "licensed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` You 've turned your back on Mesopotamia , boys , '' exclaimed the licensed jester of the company", "context_len": 19}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "But in carrying a city by storm , which takes place usually at an unexpected time , and often in the night , the maddened and victorious assaulter suddenly burst into the sacred scenes of domestic peace , and seclusion , and love -- the very worst of men , filled with the worst of passions , stimulated by the resistance they have encountered , and licensed by their victory to give all these passions the fullest and most unrestricted gratification", "context_len": 81}}}, "jester": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` You 've turned your back on Mesopotamia , boys , '' exclaimed the licensed jester of the company", "context_len": 19}}}, "airship": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Following the direction of the speaker 's outstretched hand Malcolm had his first view of an airship", "context_len": 17}}}, "fuselage": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 14, "context": "Rows of holes like stitching appeared here and there in the Aichi \u2019 s fuselage , but the greenhouse of the Jap plane appeared bulletproof", "context_len": 25}}}, "whirring": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "After whirling through the air for nearly two hundred feet it began to rise , and its flight curved toward the left , taking in a circle of a hundred yards or more in diameter , and fell close to our feet , while throughout its whole course of nearly a thousand feet it kept up a harsh , whirring sound , like the wings of a partridge in full flight , the rotary motion giving it the appearance of a ring or wheel moving through space", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Bomba heard the whirring of wings in retreat", "context_len": 8}}}, "dirigible": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "The drawback , in the matter of dirigible balloons , lay in the fact that the huge bag , necessary to keep them aloft , made them the sport of every wind that blew", "context_len": 34}}}, "balloon": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 18, "context": "Lafitte , Cameron had told Matt , was a member of the A\u00ebro Club , had owned a balloon of his own , and had made many ascensions from the town of Pittsfield , Massachusetts -- which was near Matt \u2019 s old home in the Berkshire Hills ; but , Cameron had also said , Lafitte had given up plain ballooning for dirigibles , and , finally , had turned his back on dirigibles for heavier-than-air machines", "context_len": 78}}}, "diverging": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "Better work could be performed by individual shooting , but the diverging fire from five rifles was a feeble reply to the converging volleys from ten times that number , while the emplacement , constructed to meet an attack from the westward , was ill-designed to ward off an assault from the opposite quarter", "context_len": 54}}}, "outwards": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Feake , Powell , John Rogers , Courteney , Day , and Richard Harrison were the names that rang out above the shouts of the sectaries , who , crying out that the day of the Lord was come , charged outwards with such impetuosity that the soldiers were for a moment forced backwards", "context_len": 54}}}, "periscopes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "With the utmost audacity a U-boat had lain submerged across the track of the convoy , trusting to be able to launch her bolt and disappear before even the swift destroyers could take her bearings , and close upon the spot where the tips of her periscopes had appeared when the torpedo had been discharged", "context_len": 55}}}, "aerial": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 10, "context": "Unseen by the watchers on the troop-ship , a deadly aerial torpedo was worming its way through the water until it reached a depth of sixty feet", "context_len": 27}}}, "spout": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "The children , who wanted a drink , had run around to the well , and were waiting for some one to come and draw water for them , when Frankie climbed upon the spout , and before his sisters could stop him , perched himself astride the well curb", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "The water had a dead , stagnant look , after the eruption , and Fenn was careful not to approach too close , for he could not tell when the spring would spout up again", "context_len": 35}}}, "horizontal": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Even the horizontal and vertical rudders and the twin propellers were visible", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "The nomad black fellow makes his primitive humpy , or hut , in a location chosen temporarily , according to his necessities for hunting , fishing , and the like , by cutting a young sapling half through about four feet from the ground , and bending it over to a horizontal position , thus forming a ridge pole , against which boughs and strips of bark are laid", "context_len": 69}}}, "strafed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then , as if reluctant to sink into obscurity , the strafed U-boat disappeared from mortal ken for all time", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "The task was a hazardous one , but there was never any lack of men willing and eager to run the risk of being strafed for the sake of feeding their comrades in the firing-line", "context_len": 35}}}, "ken": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "It \u2019 s dem dat will flop over like a buckwheat cake de minute dey fires , reflected Jethro , but how de mischief ken I know which way de rapscallions will turn ? Mos \u2019 folks am right-handed , and I guess dat \u2019 s de way this sarpint will flop", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "It was weary , anxious work ; for of course our movements were being regulated by a theory that , for aught we knew to the contrary , might be all wrong ; and as day succeeded day without bringing the expected sail within our ken there were not wanting among us those who denounced the skipper 's plan as foolish , and argued that the proper thing would have been to go direct to the Gaboon , and look there for the Virginia", "context_len": 84}}}, "counterpart": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Not until the last of the convoy passed the western end of the breakwater , and gained the security of Plymouth Sound , did the modern counterpart of the", "context_len": 29}}}, "disembarkation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "In almost complete silence the securing-ropes were made fast and the gangways run out by apathetic workmen , while with the utmost dispatch the disembarkation of men and stores began", "context_len": 30}}}, "entrain": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Again rumour spoke truthfully , for at four o'clock the next afternoon the `` Diggers '' were ordered to entrain for Southampton", "context_len": 22}}}, "entrainment": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The train then ran into the station , and the entrainment of the wounded commenced", "context_len": 15}}}, "forgone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "They wished in vain for the breakfast that the majority had forgone when the Pomfret Castle sighted land", "context_len": 18}}}, "shanties": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "It was late in the afternoon when Malcolm and his two chums alighted at Codford station , and , making their way by a roundabout route along the main street of the village , where old-time cottages and hideous wooden shanties stood cheek by jowl , arrived at the vast array of tin huts that comprised the camp", "context_len": 58}}}, "jowl": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "It was late in the afternoon when Malcolm and his two chums alighted at Codford station , and , making their way by a roundabout route along the main street of the village , where old-time cottages and hideous wooden shanties stood cheek by jowl , arrived at the vast array of tin huts that comprised the camp", "context_len": 58}}}, "drafts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "From watching the men play drafts he concluded that if two persons equal in judgment play for a considerable sum , he that loves money will lose ; his anxiety for the success of the game confounds him", "context_len": 38}}}, "stripe": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "I always knew a hot pilot of his stripe would be a quitter when the real test came", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "About that son of a stripe wearer , Stone", "context_len": 9}}}, "copped": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 7, "context": "The battalion lying on our right barely copped it at all , and the Tommy regiment on our left came off lightly until the Huns had finished with us", "context_len": 29}}}, "platoon": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "Recovering his head-gear , Rifleman Carr joined the group by a discreet and circuitous route , to find Grouser Joliffe and half a dozen men of his platoon engaged upon the task -- up to now unsuccessful -- of clearing out a dug-out", "context_len": 43}}}, "casualties": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Once more the prestige of the German army had suffered a severe blow , while , in prisoners alone , the Huns lost more than the total casualties of the successors to the `` contemptible little army ''", "context_len": 38}}}, "objective": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 39, "context": "The case inflection of the substantive , adjective , of some pronouns and of the nominal forms of the verb is effected by the suffixes : -i for the absolute , -ut for the subjective , -un for the objective case : y\u00e1ti person , y\u00e1tut , y\u00e1tun ; n\u00e1ki what , which , n\u00e1kut , n\u00e1kun", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 45, "context": "The nominal inflection shows but three cases : The first in -i ( or -a , -o , -u ) , which may be called absolute ; [ 132 ] the subjective case in -t , -it ( -at , -ut ) , and the objective in -n , -in ( -an , -un )", "context_len": 55}}}, "subalterns": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Come along , my two subalterns , this is camp life , and you must learn to bivouac like true soldiers", "context_len": 21}}}, "adjust": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "In consequence of the rotary motion of the balloon , which had continued without ceasing since the earth was left , the valve line had become twisted , and he had to leave the car , and to mount into the ring above to adjust it", "context_len": 46}}}, "puttees": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "Went down like a felled ox , and lay there until my puttees -- which I started to unroll but did n't finish -- began smouldering", "context_len": 26}}}, "unroll": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Went down like a felled ox , and lay there until my puttees -- which I started to unroll but did n't finish -- began smouldering", "context_len": 26}}}, "barrage": {"freq": 21, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 21, "word_index": 27, "context": "With a feeling of complete confidence , akin to that of a child for its mother , the New Zealanders literally clung to the skirts of the barrage , at the same time adjuring the distant artillerymen to `` Push it along and let 's get on ! ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "crater": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 47, "context": "Looking like muddy replicas of Lot 's wife , Malcolm and Selwyn at last emerged from the morass , Fortescue was ahead , Corporal Preston too , while M'Turk , with his chum M'Kane hanging on to his back , was just extricating himself from a deep crater", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Across the track was a crater twenty feet in diameter , from which acrid fumes were still slowly emanating from the pulverized earth", "context_len": 23}}}, "ousted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Aye , but we ousted them next morning , '' replied Travers , `` and out of Flers as well", "context_len": 21}}}, "swears": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` And , taking Dick by the arm , the officer continued earnestly , in a low tone , `` You remember that fellow , Patrick Keith , with whom you quarrelled in Edinburgh ; he is here in London in my Lord Lauderdale 's household , and he swears he will be revenged on you", "context_len": 56}}}, "credence": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "In order to determine de Soto 's route in these parts , we have to decide first , whether the days and directions of the compass noted by his chroniclers deserve more credence than the local names transmitted in cases when both form conflicting statements", "context_len": 45}}}, "russet": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "The whole family was gathered together on the grass round the wood fire , the smoke of which rose up faintly blue against the russet of the beech-woods behind -- handsome , fierce-eyed men , lying slouching idly on the ground smoking short black pipes ; slatternly women with gay handkerchiefs tied round their black hair , bustling about with something savoury inside tin cans ; ragged brown-skinned little children , gnawing at bones with savage haste ; while a few disreputable dogs waited eagerly for the scraps that were thrown to them from time to time", "context_len": 97}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The trees were turning to russet and amber and gold , and the swallows had long since flown away", "context_len": 19}}}, "bombing": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "Yet , in spite of the heat , bayonet practice , bombing instruction , and long route marches kept the men lean , active , and in the very pink of condition", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 13, "context": "If that were the case it could mean only one thing : The bombing raid had smashed every installation of value at the air base , including the radio", "context_len": 29}}}, "milestone": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "A smile like a stray beam of sunshine drifted over her troubled young face , at the thought of the second Mrs Harding stopping for one instant in her round of ponderous toil to note the fact that one of her family had reached another milestone in life 's journey", "context_len": 50}}}, "mushroom": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Peggy found the old man seated in his elbow-chair by the fireside , his head enveloped in a huge flat oat-cake , tied on with a red cotton pocket-handkerchief , so that he resembled some new species of mushroom", "context_len": 39}}}, "uniformly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "In spite of the frequent use to which they are put they do not relieve the sentence of its heaviness to any perceptible extent ; for what we call incident clauses and also many co-ordinate principal sentences are uniformly expressed by groups of words , the verb of which stands in the -t or -n verbal , which nearest corresponds to our participle in -ing , or to having ( h", "context_len": 71}}}, "dinky": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Feel as dinky as anything , by gum ! '' he mumbled , without removing the consoling `` fag ''", "context_len": 21}}}, "switch": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 26, "context": "I know that \u2019 s so ; because , sometimes I want Rover to go to the woods , and he won \u2019 t ; I switch him , and he won \u2019 t ; I push him , and he won \u2019 t ; then I put some acorns in my pocket and run ahead , and he \u2019 ll get there as soon as I do", "context_len": 68}, "NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 35, "context": "Ca n't you -- hold on there -- do n't go away -- what 's that -- yes , I hear you but -- do n't go away -- do something -- pull out the switch -- do something -- never mind the electrician -- you do it -- do n't go away -- do n't go away -- Ah -- it 's too late ! ''", "context_len": 67}}}, "porting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Still porting helm , she swung round until she reversed her former direction , then , standing on her course , began to make for Spithead once more", "context_len": 28}}}, "toothbrush": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` One of the brass hats ' has dropped his toothbrush overboard and we 're going back to look for it , '' declared a South Australian", "context_len": 27}}}, "bead": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "And how pretty the young people were in their beautiful summer attire with laces and ribbons and bead chains and white ties with a great bow almost as big as the foot", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "A second glance showed that it was the topknot of a redskin , who dropped down before I could draw bead on him", "context_len": 23}}}, "chortled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` Corker , my boy , you were too sharp on your bead when you chortled , 'Good-bye , Blighty ! ' ''", "context_len": 23}}}, "fours": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Many a time had they seen troops fully equipped proceeding towards the docks ; never since the outbreak of hostilities had they seen soldiers in heavy marching order tramping in column of fours away from the place of embarkation", "context_len": 39}}}, "hoax": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "At the prospect of a gigantic hoax others took up the mendacious parable , and long before the men reached their destination for the night the startling news had spread far and wide", "context_len": 33}}}, "mendacious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "At the prospect of a gigantic hoax others took up the mendacious parable , and long before the men reached their destination for the night the startling news had spread far and wide", "context_len": 33}}}, "fie": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Selwyn had seized upon the opportunity to visit his relations , but when fie again invited Malcolm and Fortescue to accompany him they begged to be excused , and wandered round the town instead", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "fortified": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 9, "context": "In warfare , an army , in investing a fortified place , spends ordinarily a considerable time in burrowing their way along in trenches , half under ground , until they get near enough to plant their cannon where the balls can take effect upon some part of the wall", "context_len": 50}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "The spot was on the northern borders of the ancient kingdom of Sussex -- the land of the Saxon Ella -- a spot marvelously favoured by nature , occupying the summit of a low hill , which commanded a wide prospect on all sides , while itself almost impregnable when fortified , as it was , by ditches and mounds , dug in the usual Danish fashion , for the Danes owed much of their success to their skill in fortification", "context_len": 81}}}, "portcullis": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "In front and in the rear of the advancing German infantry the hail of shells descended like a giant twin portcullis , while the intervening space was thick with shrapnel", "context_len": 30}}}, "lance": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "For every man who levelled lance or drew bow at Agincourt against the French , one hundred and fifty are to-day fighting side by side with their former enemies", "context_len": 29}}}, "slippy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Look slippy and get those rations up", "context_len": 7}}}, "caked": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "Three of them are old acquaintances : Carr , Selwyn , and Macready , all looking lean , dirty , and unkempt , while their uniforms were caked in dried mud and frayed with hard usage", "context_len": 36}}}, "propitious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "For one thing , the weather had been propitious , and the loathsome mud had almost disappeared", "context_len": 17}}}, "untrimmed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The roof was composed of untrimmed tree trunks on which were piled sand-bags sufficiently thick to stop shells of medium calibre", "context_len": 21}}}, "wainscot": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "The wind blowing the ivy about would sound like a hand tapping upon the pane , the cattle trampling in the fields suggested footsteps under the window , and a mouse behind the wainscot would raise her to such a pitch of panic that she would often be obliged to get up and light the candle to reassure herself , and when she at last fell asleep it was generally with her fingers stuffed in her ears , and her head buried under the bedclothes , an uncomfortable proceeding , resulting in such white cheeks and heavy eyes that Father , with some difficulty finding out the cause of the trouble , never left in future without arranging for old David to sleep in the house during his", "context_len": 128}}}, "haversacks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Hanging from nails driven into the rough-and-ready wainscot were the men 's haversacks and other equipment , while ranged alongside the door were their rifles", "context_len": 25}}}, "cylindrical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In the centre of the dug-out stood a cylindrical piece of perforated iron in which a fire was burning dully", "context_len": 20}}}, "dully": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Not even the king , '' she repeated dully ; `` nay , I know not if the king be merciful ; but , '' she cried , suddenly starting up , `` it hath come back to me ; there is one near to the king who may be our advocate -- Prince Rupert ! ''", "context_len": 58}}}, "promiscuously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "The fourth day all classes congregate in the `` great house '' promiscuously ; the game killed on the previous day is given to the public , and the women are cooking the provisions brought in from all sides , over the new fire", "context_len": 44}}}, "hampering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Not only did the advance and support trenches get it hot , but for miles behind the lines hostile shells were dropping promiscuously , on the chance of blowing up one or more of the numerous dumps and otherwise hampering the supply columns", "context_len": 43}}}, "strafe": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "But as night fell the `` strafe '' became desultory , and under cover of darkness the fatigue and foraging parties were able to set to work with a reasonable chance of getting through without being `` done in ''", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "He swung over a line of little Nakajimas , climbed swiftly , and came back to strafe a string of Mitsubishi boats", "context_len": 22}}}, "desultory": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "But as night fell the `` strafe '' became desultory , and under cover of darkness the fatigue and foraging parties were able to set to work with a reasonable chance of getting through without being `` done in ''", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "So , when the guns died down to a desultory shelling , the Huns set to work to clear up the badly-damaged trenches", "context_len": 23}}}, "vive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "A couple of hostile raids had kept them on the qui vive the previous night , while throughout the day they had had but few opportunities for sleep", "context_len": 28}}}, "uncut": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "Messines Ridge was to be carried at the point of the bayonet , and the knowledge that the hostile wire was practically uncut and that the heights bristled with machine-guns was common property", "context_len": 33}}}, "clamber": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The men were used to Grouser Joliffe 's complaints by this time , They knew that when the critical moment arrived Joliffe would be amongst the first to mount the fire-step and clamber over the parapet", "context_len": 36}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "Just before them was a mass of rock , four or five feet high , which seemed to bar the way ; but lifting Bessie as high as he could , Starr set her safely upon the top , then handing her the bananas began to clamber up himself", "context_len": 49}}}, "labyrinths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "With its labyrinths of wire and nests of skilfully-hidden machine-guns Messines Ridge was far more difficult to assault than in the earlier stages of the war , when French won and lost the important position", "context_len": 35}}}, "remedied": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "But these , after all , were not very great troubles , and were soon remedied ; for another bower was built beneath two tall althea bushes near the house , where no evil disposed person would dare to come ; and the boys took care not to leave their boats where they could be meddled with again", "context_len": 58}}}, "shrapnel": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 35, "context": "The deficiency remedied , and the scanty toilet operations performed ( the inhabitants of the dug-out had turned in `` all standing '' , even to their boots ) , the men put on their shrapnel helmets , seized their rifles , and sallied forth into the night", "context_len": 48}}}, "venue": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "All he could do was to keep in touch with the man preceding him until he grew accustomed to the change of venue", "context_len": 23}}}, "reeked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The ground shook under the relentless pressure of the tractor bands , the air reeked with petrol fumes", "context_len": 18}}}, "traverses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Traces of poison gas lurked in the traverses , pungent fumes from bursting shells wafted over parapet and parados , while the report , passed on from various successive occupants of this section of the line , that a dozen dead Huns had been buried under the floor of the support trench -- the old first-line trench of a Prussian regiment -- seemed to find definite confirmation", "context_len": 67}}}, "strafing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "First time was n't much to speak of ; but Plug Street -- ugh ! For five mortal hours I was pinned down , the Huns strafing all the time , and the water rising up through the dirt that covered me up to my chin", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "An ear-splitting roar announced that the strafing of D\u00fcren had commenced", "context_len": 11}}}, "heeded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Now he scarcely heeded the messenger of death and destruction , although showers of dust and calcined wood flew over the parados amongst the ration party", "context_len": 26}}}, "calcined": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Now he scarcely heeded the messenger of death and destruction , although showers of dust and calcined wood flew over the parados amongst the ration party", "context_len": 26}}}, "jumpy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "They could only put it down to the fact that Fritz had a good inkling of the impending stunt and was getting `` jumpy ''", "context_len": 25}}}, "massing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Bart dismissed this matter from his mind and all other disturbing extraneous affairs , massing all his faculties for the time being on getting properly equipped for business", "context_len": 28}}}, "heavies": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 14, "context": "Although the expected barrage had not put in an appearance , the British `` heavies '' were lavishly showering shells upon the German position", "context_len": 24}}}, "trudging": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 21, "context": "Two figures were in sight ; the one that of a man with a pack on his back , who was trudging along the road between the mountain and the lake , going as if he were in a hurry too ; the other that of a boy who was climbing up the steep and rugged path which led to the `` Chief 's Head", "context_len": 65}}}, "blazes": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 12, "context": "We \u2019 ll git our man sure , for the minute he blazes away , I \u2019 ll shoot a little to his right and ye do the same to his left : that \u2019 ll settle him", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Gave my thumb a fierce wrench ! It hurts like blazes ! Why did n't you tell me I was going to fall , and I 'd have stayed in to-night ? '' he asked half humorously", "context_len": 38}}}, "bunched": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Those behind bunched up and halted , while from the front of the single file came a very strong exclamation of pain and anger", "context_len": 24}}}, "zigzag": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 75, "context": "The air seemed to have grown more close and sultry while the children were within the cave , and , just as they reached the mouth , a low , grumbling sound , which they had heard for some time , but not taken much notice of , broke into a crash of thunder that seemed to make the ground shake beneath them , while at the same instant a brilliant streak of lightning flashed zigzag across the sky , lighting up the gloom behind them to its furthest recess", "context_len": 90}}}, "marshy": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "In places it was eight feet deep , with sand-bags on either side in addition , In others , in marshy spots , where the high-explosive shells had spitefully disturbed the tranquillity of meandering streams and carried the sluggish water to overflow and swamp the surrounding ground , the `` trench '' was above normal ground-level , with a lofty and broad wall of sand-bags to right and left", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "The main attack had inclined away to the right , while the thinly-held line between the New Zealand division and the English regiments on the left had been stopped , not by the Huns , but by the impossible condition of the marshy ground", "context_len": 44}}}, "spitefully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "In places it was eight feet deep , with sand-bags on either side in addition , In others , in marshy spots , where the high-explosive shells had spitefully disturbed the tranquillity of meandering streams and carried the sluggish water to overflow and swamp the surrounding ground , the `` trench '' was above normal ground-level , with a lofty and broad wall of sand-bags to right and left", "context_len": 69}}}, "tranquillity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "In places it was eight feet deep , with sand-bags on either side in addition , In others , in marshy spots , where the high-explosive shells had spitefully disturbed the tranquillity of meandering streams and carried the sluggish water to overflow and swamp the surrounding ground , the `` trench '' was above normal ground-level , with a lofty and broad wall of sand-bags to right and left", "context_len": 69}}}, "meandering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "In places it was eight feet deep , with sand-bags on either side in addition , In others , in marshy spots , where the high-explosive shells had spitefully disturbed the tranquillity of meandering streams and carried the sluggish water to overflow and swamp the surrounding ground , the `` trench '' was above normal ground-level , with a lofty and broad wall of sand-bags to right and left", "context_len": 69}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Yes , the river -- the beautiful , meandering , poetical river , '' added Tom", "context_len": 17}}}, "overflow": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "In places it was eight feet deep , with sand-bags on either side in addition , In others , in marshy spots , where the high-explosive shells had spitefully disturbed the tranquillity of meandering streams and carried the sluggish water to overflow and swamp the surrounding ground , the `` trench '' was above normal ground-level , with a lofty and broad wall of sand-bags to right and left", "context_len": 69}}}, "lethal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The lethal weapons would be ominously silent until after dark ; then , on the off-chance of receiving a good bag , they would let loose a hail of bullets", "context_len": 30}}}, "emplacement": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 32, "context": "Better work could be performed by individual shooting , but the diverging fire from five rifles was a feeble reply to the converging volleys from ten times that number , while the emplacement , constructed to meet an attack from the westward , was ill-designed to ward off an assault from the opposite quarter", "context_len": 54}}}, "diagonally": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "Each was bent at the elbow with the hand close to the chest , like a professional runner , but the right hand was empty , while the fingers of the left were closed about the huge bow which he was obliged to hold diagonally before him , to prevent its interference with his running", "context_len": 55}}}, "bowels": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 23, "context": "They went stumbling along over the irregular floor , wondering whether their way was taking them to safety , or only into the bowels of the earth", "context_len": 27}}}, "trucks": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 38, "context": "It made Peggy quite sad to think that the little creatures were mostly bought for the mines , and that , once broken and trained , they would never see daylight again , but spend their lives drawing trucks up and down the low galleries underground , having said good-bye to their native moorlands for ever", "context_len": 56}}}, "camouflaged": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "All through the hours of darkness , for several months past , heavily-laden trucks carefully covered with camouflaged canvas rumbled away from the lines to return empty ere dawn", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Tree tops nearly grazed her belly turret as she swept over a blunt headland , into sight of the camouflaged ships", "context_len": 21}}}, "excavation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "It turned through some slate heaps , lined the side of a deep excavation , and came to an abrupt end where some boards , placed crosswise , barred the sheer descent", "context_len": 32}}}, "sappers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Hallo , chums ! '' called out one of the ration-party as a truck hauled by three sappers rattled along", "context_len": 21}}}, "bombers": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 10, "context": "How , for instance , could four squadrons of medium bombers effect such a complete destruction as the general had described ? Why not use Fortresses and Liberators , such as were even now smashing the U-boat pens at Lorient and Wilhelmshaven ?", "context_len": 43}}}, "tramway": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Ambling along by the side of the tramway came a long line of armoured mastodons", "context_len": 15}}}, "mastodons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Ambling along by the side of the tramway came a long line of armoured mastodons", "context_len": 15}}}, "tractor": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The ground shook under the relentless pressure of the tractor bands , the air reeked with petrol fumes", "context_len": 18}}}, "petrol": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "The ground shook under the relentless pressure of the tractor bands , the air reeked with petrol fumes", "context_len": 18}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Now you come to mention it , sir , '' replied M'Turk , `` it does hum a bit , although it 's not petrol", "context_len": 26}}}, "brutes": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 17, "word_index": 83, "context": "`` Upwards it leads to the banqueting hall , and you can comprehend my escape this evening , '' said he ; `` but our path is now downwards , unless you would like to go up and see the drunken beasts of murderers snoring off their debauch upon the floor as they fell ; oh , that it were lawful for a Christian man to cut their throats as they lie ; many innocent lives would be saved thereby , which those brutes will live to destroy", "context_len": 88}}}, "delays": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "At length , after many delays , the ration-party arrived at the farm -- or , rather , the pile of rubble that was known as La Tuille Farm before a nest of German machine-guns had attracted the notice of an observant battery-commander", "context_len": 43}}}, "rubble": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "At length , after many delays , the ration-party arrived at the farm -- or , rather , the pile of rubble that was known as La Tuille Farm before a nest of German machine-guns had attracted the notice of an observant battery-commander", "context_len": 43}}}, "handiwork": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "He put his hands in his pockets , and whistled carelessly , but it was evident , all the same , that he was pleased to show off his handiwork , even to a little girl", "context_len": 36}}}, "tiers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Piles of bully-beef tins , tiers of barrels and cases , small mountains of loaves covered with tarpaulins , were diminishing rapidly under the heavy calls made upon them by deputations from the men in the trenches", "context_len": 37}}}, "tarpaulins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Piles of bully-beef tins , tiers of barrels and cases , small mountains of loaves covered with tarpaulins , were diminishing rapidly under the heavy calls made upon them by deputations from the men in the trenches", "context_len": 37}}}, "deputations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Let Mester Vaughan go up to Lunnon , and put it fair afore th ' House o ' Commons , like th ' deputations as th ' newspapers tell on , and they 'd listen to un , and see un to his rights", "context_len": 44}}}, "ounce": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "Although within range of hostile guns , the `` dump '' had so far escaped serious damage , To bring the supplies nearer the lines by mechanical transfer would be to court disaster , so every ounce of food had to be carried by squads detailed for that purpose", "context_len": 49}}}, "sh": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` abide in the subsistence of the like of this one '' ( acoumu fi ma '' sh mithl hadha )", "context_len": 21}}}, "gash": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "A gash on the knuckle of his right hand and a few slight scratches on the calves of his legs were the total result of the Anzacs ' efforts until the smoke-bomb came into play", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "A saturated field-bandage held to his wound partly concealed the slight but ugly gash", "context_len": 14}}}, "altering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "When we get back I 'll have a few swings with my rifle and bay'net ; then if the dressing wants altering you 'll bear a hand ? ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "siding": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Franklin , siding with the people , wrote a pamphlet on The Nature and Necessity of a Paper Currency , which he printed himself , and which swayed the Pennsylvania Assembly to pass a bill to issue such paper currency", "context_len": 40}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Its forward section was moving off , having just thrown some cars against others stationary on a siding", "context_len": 18}}}, "flanged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The vehicles each had four flanged wheels", "context_len": 7}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The flanged wheels sank deeply into the soft ground , but by sheer hard work the truck was propelled round the crater to the spot where the lines resumed their-sphere of usefulness", "context_len": 32}}}, "indiscriminately": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "Here and there huge spurts of mud and water leapt towards the rainy sky as German shells burst indiscriminately in the swollen stream ; while everywhere the slowly-flowing water was flecked with little spurts of spray as the machine-gun bullets ricochetted from the surface", "context_len": 44}}}, "propel": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Another time he carved himself two oval slabs of wood , shaped like a painter \u2019 s palette , bored a hole for his thumb , and used them like oars to propel himself along", "context_len": 35}}}, "acrid": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "Breathless from the violent shock , blinded by the shower of dust , deafened by the terrific concussion , and with his sense of smell deadened to everything but the acrid fumes of the burst shell , he was at a loss to know what had happened", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "`` But what ? Is it a noble impulse which will spring to life in rich gratitude when I tell her my story ? Or will a mere hurt , passionate vanity rise to overwhelm us all in its acrid swell ? I shall soon know", "context_len": 46}}}, "emanating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Across the track was a crater twenty feet in diameter , from which acrid fumes were still slowly emanating from the pulverized earth", "context_len": 23}}}, "pulverized": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Across the track was a crater twenty feet in diameter , from which acrid fumes were still slowly emanating from the pulverized earth", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 41, "context": "Overhead , British shells screeched on their way , as with mathematical precision they fell in the place appointed , to form a `` barrage '' through which neither German supports could advance nor defeated Huns retire without risk of being pulverized by the high-explosive missiles", "context_len": 46}}}, "negotiate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "She was notified by the Dauntless Company that two entries , the fourth and fifth east , had entered her property , in which she had never suspected the presence of coal , and that the owners were prepared to negotiate with her suitable terms for the right of working the vein in question", "context_len": 54}}}, "luminous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "On the way back to the other track Malcolm glanced at the luminous face of his wristlet watch", "context_len": 18}}}, "wristlet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "On the way back to the other track Malcolm glanced at the luminous face of his wristlet watch", "context_len": 18}}}, "deadened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Breathless from the violent shock , blinded by the shower of dust , deafened by the terrific concussion , and with his sense of smell deadened to everything but the acrid fumes of the burst shell , he was at a loss to know what had happened", "context_len": 47}}}, "flexed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "But as circulation was resumed , feeling and power came back , and Bomba flexed and unflexed his fingers with a sensation of renewed life", "context_len": 25}}}, "nauseating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "He was beginning to see , despite the darkness and the nauseating , pungent fumes", "context_len": 15}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Malcolm could hear his heart thumping violently under his tunic ; he was fully conscious of an empty , nauseating sensation in the pit of his stomach", "context_len": 27}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "A whiff of nauseating gas drifted across his path", "context_len": 9}}}, "timepiece": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` You ca n't hurt this clock , '' declared Phil , and , as if to prove his words , the fussy little timepiece began ticking away again , as loudly and insistingly as ever", "context_len": 36}}}, "conclusive": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "When Wacker woke up , he would simply say he had sold his rig to two strangers , and , so far as the actual burglary was concerned , would be able to prove a conclusive alibi", "context_len": 37}}}, "prophetic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Sheets of ballads , which Ephraim had bought as a boy , lay side by side with a tattered copy or two of Zadkiel 's prophetic almanac , some advertisements of patent medicines , a recipe for sheep-dip , and a Wesleyan hymn-book", "context_len": 43}}}, "engineers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "This from Selwyn , who was engaged in binding a first-aid dressing round the ribs of the prophetic sergeant of engineers", "context_len": 21}}}, "relaying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Two of them belonged to the ration-party , and the others were Tommies who had been engaged in relaying the uptorn line", "context_len": 22}}}, "exploding": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The two men on the side nearest the exploding shell had been instantly killed , but the others , sheltered to a certain extent by the vehicle , had got off at the expense of a severe shaking", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "The bearers lifted the stretcher and continued on their way , while Rifleman Carr , slinging his rifle over his shoulder , hurried towards the German second-line trenches , where , judging by the deep detonations of exploding bombs and the sharp crack of rifle-shots , there was still work to be done", "context_len": 53}}}, "ramification": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "What further ramification the conversation might have taken is problematical , but it was interrupted just then by the arrival of Ed Kerr , who seemed in much of a hurry", "context_len": 31}}}, "pannikins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "After all , they were able to feed , and in a short space of time pannikins of tea were boiling over the biscuit-tin stoves in the dug-out", "context_len": 28}}}, "rottenest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` This is the rottenest part of the whole business , '' remarked Selwyn", "context_len": 14}}}, "creased": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "He pulled a creased and folded newspaper from his pocket , and , holding it up to the guttering light , pointed a shapely yet begrimed forefinger at a certain paragraph", "context_len": 31}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Seabrooke laid aside half-a-dozen articles , then suddenly started to his feet with an exclamation , holding in his hand a creased and crumpled envelope , which he hastily opened , and took from it -- Percy 's hundred-dollar note !", "context_len": 41}}}, "guttering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "He pulled a creased and folded newspaper from his pocket , and , holding it up to the guttering light , pointed a shapely yet begrimed forefinger at a certain paragraph", "context_len": 31}}}, "begrimed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "He pulled a creased and folded newspaper from his pocket , and , holding it up to the guttering light , pointed a shapely yet begrimed forefinger at a certain paragraph", "context_len": 31}}}, "communications": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "After various attempts , successful and otherwise , to cripple the internal communications of the Dominion , he fled across the border to the United States", "context_len": 26}}}, "posing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Proceeding to England , and posing as a mercantile marine officer , forged documents and an engaging manner procured him an introduction to Whitehall , with the result that he was given a commission in the Royal Naval Reserve and appointed to an armed merchant cruiser", "context_len": 46}}}, "commissioning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Three weeks after commissioning , the merchant-cruiser was torpedoed and sunk in broad daylight by a U-boat", "context_len": 17}}}, "cosmopolitan": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "As a matter of fact , the cosmopolitan rogue was particularly busy in South Africa , drifting thence into German South-West Africa , where he played a conspicuous part in a daring gun-running expedition under the nose of a British cruiser", "context_len": 41}}}, "via": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Then the Carrs were furnished with money and a ticket to enable them to travel via the Hook of Holland to England ; and , with many earnest expressions of gratitude to their benefactors , Peter and Malcolm set out on their roundabout journey back to the firing-line in Flanders", "context_len": 50}}}, "operating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "On the principle that it is advisable to desert a sinking ship in time , von Feldoffer drifted via Johannesburg to Cape Town , where his efforts to get into communication with German mine-layers operating off the Cape met with slight success", "context_len": 42}}}, "instigation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` Belong here ? You work on the coal trestle ! Do n't tell me ! You are covered with coal dust now ! What have you there ? Are you going to play some trick at the instigation of the freshmen ? I demand an answer ! ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "whet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Would his experiences and narrow escape deter him from further enterprises or merely whet his appetite for other surprising adventures ?", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "`` whet proceeded from", "context_len": 4}}}, "gallons": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` There 'd be two gallons of it -- enough to cover the smokehouse", "context_len": 14}}}, "stink": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Fritz will be letting loose a few gallons of stink , I reckon", "context_len": 13}}}, "villas": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The beautiful coasts and inlets were crowded with villas , and invalids then , as now , sought the invigorating breezes , from all parts of the island of Britain , and even from the neighbouring province of Gaul", "context_len": 39}}}, "denizens": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 18, "context": "Bomba built up the fire so that it should serve through the night to keep off the prowling denizens of the forest , and made his refuge secure by rolling a great stone that no animal could dislodge to the entrance of the cave", "context_len": 44}}}, "sectors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` He was here a minute ago , '' declared one , after several fruitless enquiries had been made of the denizens of the two adjoining sectors", "context_len": 27}}}, "insecure": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "After the battle of Arbela , while Alexander marched to Babylon and to Susa , Darius had fled to Ecbatana , and was now there , his family being thus at one of the royal palaces under the command of the conqueror , and he himself independent , but insecure , in the other", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "For some years the city was without bishops , owing to its insecure position during the strife between Wessex and Mercia , but later it appears as the seat of the great Mercian bishopric , retaining its jurisdiction until after the Norman conquest , when the see was transferred to Lincoln", "context_len": 51}}}, "regimental": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then sat on a regimental board , after which I went to the Guards ' camp for Cay ; and we then went , laden with tracts , books and prayers , to the remaining hospitals of the Second Division , where we distributed all we had", "context_len": 47}}}, "irresolutely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The man turned sharply , gave a grunt of Surprise , and before the lad could realize what had happened , he swarmed up the ladder , paused irresolutely for a brief instant on the sandbagged parapet , and leapt into the darkness of No Man 's Land", "context_len": 48}}}, "sandbagged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The man turned sharply , gave a grunt of Surprise , and before the lad could realize what had happened , he swarmed up the ladder , paused irresolutely for a brief instant on the sandbagged parapet , and leapt into the darkness of No Man 's Land", "context_len": 48}}}, "cannoned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Malcolm and Selwyn obeyed promptly , and alighting upon the floor of the trench the latter cannoned into a passing soldier", "context_len": 21}}}, "lieutenancy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "of the old Awarua days and his lieutenancy a thing of the past", "context_len": 13}}}, "veracity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Probably you are right , commented the man , who evidently had faith in the veracity of his nephew , but there has been enough of this ; come with me", "context_len": 31}}}, "groused": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Stupendous though the task was , not a man flinched , although several groused at the lack of consideration on the part of the Staff to send them against a prepared position in a practically-unbroken state ; which showed that the troops were generally ignorant of the measures taken to safeguard them", "context_len": 52}}}, "excusable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 60, "context": "Then he embraced Alaeddin and fell to kissing him and said to him , `` Excuse me , O my son , for that I was going [ 623 ] to bereave thee of thy life , through the wickedness of yonder accursed sorcerer who cast thee into this pit ; and indeed , O my son , I was excusable in that which I did with thee , inasmuch as I saw myself bereft of my daughter and mine only one , who is dearer to me than my kingdom , and thou knowest how fathers ' hearts yearn upon their children , more by token that I have but the Lady Bedrulbudour", "context_len": 114}}}, "tersely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Keep together ! '' whispered Malcolm tersely , as he nervously felt the tip of his quivering bayonet", "context_len": 19}}}, "tornado": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "He doubted whether he could stir up courage at the critical moment to leap over the parapet into the impending tornado of machine-gun bullets and pulverizing , bursting shells", "context_len": 29}}}, "pulverizing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "He doubted whether he could stir up courage at the critical moment to leap over the parapet into the impending tornado of machine-gun bullets and pulverizing , bursting shells", "context_len": 29}}}, "dominating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Even as he took the leap a seven-fold lurid flash burst from the dominating ridge of Messines", "context_len": 17}}}, "dumbfounding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Sand-bags and tons of earth subsided into the trenches so recently vacated by the troops , while a deafening , dumbfounding roar beat upon the lad 's ears", "context_len": 28}}}, "thuds": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "Farther ahead masses of solid rock and earth were falling with a succession of thuds , while , where Messines Ridge had been , was a riven mound of disintegrated Soil , over which a dense cloud of black smoke rolled sullenly in the sultry night air", "context_len": 47}}}, "german": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "German dug-outs , trenches , machine-gun emplacements , and an unknown but vast number of troops went up in the terrific blast", "context_len": 22}}}, "emplacements": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Not only that , Barry Blake put in , but all the forts that can be spared for this job will be coming right in after us to hammer the Jap gun emplacements in the hills", "context_len": 36}}}, "inexplicably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Hostile machine-guns that had almost inexplicably escaped the general carnage were spitting venomously , while in the front German trenches , which were on comparatively level ground to the east of the Messines Ridge , a hot but erratic rifle-fire was directed upon the khaki-clad stormers", "context_len": 46}}}, "venomously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Hostile machine-guns that had almost inexplicably escaped the general carnage were spitting venomously , while in the front German trenches , which were on comparatively level ground to the east of the Messines Ridge , a hot but erratic rifle-fire was directed upon the khaki-clad stormers", "context_len": 46}}}, "barbed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "In the midst of a regular mob of panting , yelling , and shouting men , and preceded by a terrific fusillade of Mills 's bombs , Malcolm found himself struggling through masses of partly-severed barbed wire and up on the hostile parapet", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "This he covered with grease , so as to make it difficult to get a foothold , and planks with barbed hooks were placed ready to catch those who were rash enough to seek their aid", "context_len": 36}}}, "impartiality": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The air was heavy with suffocating smoke ; fragments of shell were flying with complete impartiality ; shouts , oaths , and curses punctuated the crash of steel and the rattle of musketry , as men in their blind ferocity clutched at each other 's throats and rolled in mortal combat upon the ground", "context_len": 54}}}, "punctuated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "The air was heavy with suffocating smoke ; fragments of shell were flying with complete impartiality ; shouts , oaths , and curses punctuated the crash of steel and the rattle of musketry , as men in their blind ferocity clutched at each other 's throats and rolled in mortal combat upon the ground", "context_len": 54}}}, "visor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Presently Rifleman Malcolm Carr found himself confronted by a tall , bearded Prussian , whose head-dress consisted of a steel helmet , with a visor completely covering the upper part of his face as far as his mouth", "context_len": 38}}}, "lunged": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The Prussian lunged with his bayonet", "context_len": 6}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Having their courage restored and being spurred on by Merwin , they lined up and lunged into the scrimmage before the visitors were wholly prepared , and a gain of nine yards through center might have developed into another sensational run had not Eliot himself nailed the man with the pigskin", "context_len": 51}}}, "countered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Barry saw the Aichi \u2019 s play , and countered it", "context_len": 11}}}, "dislocated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 6, "context": "With a disconcerting jar that wellnigh dislocated his wrist , and sent a numbing pain through his right arm , the lad realized that he was up against great odds", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "I do n't mind baseball , but I draw the line at posing as a candidate for a broken neck or a dislocated shoulder", "context_len": 24}}}, "mask": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 70, "context": "not to let our left hand , know that which is done by our right hand ; but are we not also commanded so to let our light shine before men that they may see our good works and glorify our Father in heaven ? And can we do so , or truly show our love to him , if we hide the services rendered for his sake behind a mask of coldness and reserve ? My dear aunt , for his sake , for your own , for the sake of the affection and confidence which I wish my children to feel for you , and which I believe you wish to gain , let me satisfy them that it was really you who did this thing", "context_len": 128}}}, "agilely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The man , despite the handicap of wearing heavy steel plates , ducked agilely , and , reversing his rifle , prodded the New Zealander with the butt of his weapon", "context_len": 31}}}, "reversing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}}}, "inertly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "With a yell of pain the fellow bent his knees and reared his body , only to fall inertly upon the half-suffocated Digger", "context_len": 23}}}, "intercepted": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "The moonlight came into the room at an angle , and it was intercepted by the branches of a tree outside so that its beams were cast only at intervals here and there", "context_len": 33}}}, "bomber": {"freq": 60, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 60, "word_index": 52, "context": "`` If your theory is correct , how do you account for the fact that those staff officers in the next dug-out came off scot-free until Joliffe thought fit to bring trouble on them and us ? No , stay where you are , Joliffe ! '' he exclaimed , as the bomber 's voice was heard shouting his intention of `` barracking in ''", "context_len": 65}}}, "scrapped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Do n't know so much about it , though ; if he had had any left when we scrapped he might have chucked one at my head", "context_len": 28}}}, "belgian": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Belgian made , it had probably been obtained from a looted shop", "context_len": 12}}}, "looted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Belgian made , it had probably been obtained from a looted shop", "context_len": 12}}}, "unsanctioned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Although officially unsanctioned , raiding parties , British , French , and German , frequently carried small revolvers when engaged in paying uninvited and unwelcome visits to the hostile lines", "context_len": 30}}}, "raiding": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Although officially unsanctioned , raiding parties , British , French , and German , frequently carried small revolvers when engaged in paying uninvited and unwelcome visits to the hostile lines", "context_len": 30}}}, "mechanism": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "Failure on the part of the cheap mechanism meant death ; nevertheless , for the sake of his comrades who were exposed to the brisk fire of the machine-gun , he was determined to take the risk", "context_len": 37}}}, "yelp": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "He heaved himself out of his wrappings , and with a delighted yelp fell upon Peggy 's curls , worrying them with little gasps of joy , till she had to dive beneath the bedclothes to escape her too sportive pet", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Even then it growled , and the drunken fools -- drunken they must have been indeed -- threw some heavy missile at it , which caused it to yelp and cease its growling", "context_len": 33}}}, "irresolute": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The irresolute and egotistic policy of these tribes often caused serious difficulties to the government of the English and French colonies , and some of them constantly wavered in their adhesion between the French and the English cause", "context_len": 38}}}, "unfixing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Unfixing the latter , he scratched upon the field-grey paint of the machine-gun the words : `` 99,109 , Carr , No", "context_len": 22}}}, "stationary": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 18, "context": "As each barge passed there was a lively exchange of greetings between their crews and those of the stationary boats ; but , in spite of the fact that the Carrs had picked up several German words during their period of captivity , the hidden listeners were unable to understand the conversation , beyond the knowledge that it referred largely to the air raid of the previous night", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Its forward section was moving off , having just thrown some cars against others stationary on a siding", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The boys now pulled up the grappling , holding the canoe stationary with their oars till the surf should come in to drive the ball towards them", "context_len": 27}}}, "cigarette": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 13, "context": "He began to feel ill long before he had half finished his first cigarette , and by the time he was half-way through the second the most painful qualms seized him , and forgetting the fort and the fight and everything else in his extreme misery he rolled over on the grass , and spent a most unhappy morning", "context_len": 59}}}, "fag": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Feel as dinky as anything , by gum ! '' he mumbled , without removing the consoling `` fag ''", "context_len": 21}, "VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Why , he fags for Dodds just as Hal would like us to fag for him ; only we wo n't", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "There was a moderate breeze , the fag end of a north-wester , and the canoe , which was large , and had excellent oars , sail , and a first-rate steering paddle , went off before it rolling and going over the water at a great rate", "context_len": 48}}}, "detonations": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "The bearers lifted the stretcher and continued on their way , while Rifleman Carr , slinging his rifle over his shoulder , hurried towards the German second-line trenches , where , judging by the deep detonations of exploding bombs and the sharp crack of rifle-shots , there was still work to be done", "context_len": 53}}}, "screeched": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Overhead , British shells screeched on their way , as with mathematical precision they fell in the place appointed , to form a `` barrage '' through which neither German supports could advance nor defeated Huns retire without risk of being pulverized by the high-explosive missiles", "context_len": 46}}}, "fissures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Beyond lay the tortured slopes of Messines Ridge , from the fissures of which escaping smoke trailed upwards in the wan morning light", "context_len": 23}}}, "consolidating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Already the first line of storming troops was engaged in consolidating the captured position , while the supports were assembling and concentrating prior to advancing upon the farthermost of their objectives -- the village of Messines", "context_len": 36}}}, "farthermost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Already the first line of storming troops was engaged in consolidating the captured position , while the supports were assembling and concentrating prior to advancing upon the farthermost of their objectives -- the village of Messines", "context_len": 36}}}, "objectives": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Already the first line of storming troops was engaged in consolidating the captured position , while the supports were assembling and concentrating prior to advancing upon the farthermost of their objectives -- the village of Messines", "context_len": 36}}}, "attitudes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "marshalled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Marshalled in military formation , the `` walking cases '' boarded the carriages , which were similar to the fourth-class compartments of the German State Railways -- hard wooden seats not excepted", "context_len": 32}}}, "droves": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The droves seen in the distance seemed often to cover a fourth of the horizon , and their dark , shaggy backs as they cropped the herbage and hitched continually forward , were like the fretted waves of the sea", "context_len": 40}}}, "tottering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "But in the tottering dug-outs the Prussian die-hards were still offering resistance ; and it was the clearing of their sub-terranean strongholds that was occupying the attention of the victorious New Zealanders", "context_len": 32}}}, "circuitous": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 57, "context": "For two or three minutes we twisted hither and thither , blindly , so far as I was concerned , and then another flash came which enabled me to see that my companion was , as I had already suspected , my faithful little friend Ama , and that she was conducting me , by a somewhat circuitous route , toward the gate in the palisade", "context_len": 66}}}, "unsuccessful": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 47, "context": "If Mosely had judged it of any use to threaten Dewey , he would have done so , hoping to force him to reveal the hiding-place of the gold ; but the undaunted spirit thus far displayed by his victim convinced him that the attempt would be unsuccessful", "context_len": 48}}}, "puttee": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "His wounded arm had given out , and he had the limb supported in a sling made from a puttee", "context_len": 20}}}, "skulking": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The swift-falling darkness , the knowledge that danger was all about them , that insane enemies were skulking perhaps behind a tree , leering at them from branches overhead , crouching in ambush behind a concealing bush or shrub , at any moment to reach out a hairy arm -- all these things combined to fill them with terror", "context_len": 59}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Despite the self-evident cause of the disappearance of the skulking warrior , the youth was ill at ease", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Woe to any skulking savage who might steal back there while Polulu was on guard !", "context_len": 16}}}, "lobbed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Deftly the bomber lobbed another grenade fairly into the yawning cavity", "context_len": 11}}}, "grenade": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Then , with loud yells of triumph , the stormers rushed the position on three sides simultaneously , bombarded the pill-boxes with grenade and smoke-bombs , and soon had the satisfaction of seeing the scanty remnant of a once numerous garrison come forth in fear and terror , accepting their conquerors ' assurances that their lives would be spared", "context_len": 59}}}, "pained": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "She had more than a mile to walk to Sunday-school , and she was anxious to be in time ; but as she walked along , the pained expression called up by Faith 's words had not left her face", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 5, "context": "Fred said perhaps his wounds pained him worse ; but Starr said no , the wounds were doing nicely , and the colonel was not a man to make a fuss about them if they did pain him , for all the time he was suffering so dreadfully that no one thought he could live , he never heard a complaint or a groan from him", "context_len": 66}}}, "lob": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Without exposing any part of his body , which a right-handed man would have had to do owing to the position of the dug-out , M'Turk could lob the bombs fairly into the mouth of the tunnel", "context_len": 37}}}, "density": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "The place was so shrouded in darkness that the fugitives were within a hundred yards of the nearmost house before they discovered the fact ; for , although the sky was clear , a light ground-mist of ever-varying density made observation a matter of difficulty", "context_len": 45}}}, "spectacled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "His head-dress was removed , disclosing the features of a pale , insignificant , and spectacled German", "context_len": 17}}}, "gadget": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` If it had n't been that the gadget was smashed we might have gone on bombing till the end of the war", "context_len": 23}}}, "splayed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The prisoner 's armour was certainly proof against fragments of bombs , even at close range , as the splayed marks upon the steel testified", "context_len": 25}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "His Webb equipment was twisted and cut ; he was plastered in mud and filth from head to foot , while his steel helmet bore the splayed marks of the impact of two pistol-bullets fired at close range", "context_len": 38}}}, "splinter": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 61, "context": "Putting on a new pair of moccasins ( stillipa\u00ed a ) , he leaves the cabin , and during twelve moons abstains from eating the meat of young bucks , of turkey-cocks , fowls , peas and salt , and is also forbidden to pick his ears and to scratch his head with his fingers , but must use a small splinter to perform these operations", "context_len": 66}}}, "acknowledging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "On the first opportunity which presented itself the morning after his arrival at his uncle 's he told her all , extenuating nothing of his own misconduct and weakness in the beginning , and acknowledging that he had almost wilfully suffered himself to be led into disobedience and wrong , and richly deserved all the shame and trouble which had fallen upon him", "context_len": 63}}}, "deflected": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The German 's bullet , fired at a range of ten yards , had struck the upper part of the rim and deflected upwards , completely penetrating the head-dress , while the wearer escaped with a scalp wound , rendering him unconscious for a quarter of an hour", "context_len": 48}}}, "denote": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "These orations were called his Philippics , and from this origin has arisen the practice , which has prevailed ever since that day , of applying the term philippics to denote , in general , any strongly denunciatory harangues", "context_len": 39}}}, "dispersing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 42, "context": "His instructions were that , `` after quelling all disturbances at Allahabad , he should not lose a moment in supporting Sir Henry Lawrence at Lucknow , and Sir Hugh Wheeler at Cawnpore ; and that he should take prompt measures for dispersing and utterly destroying all mutineers and insurgents ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "insulated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "He was too unnerved to descend alone , but there was no more danger , for the live wire was out of the way , and other linemen , with insulated gloves , soon had it in its proper place", "context_len": 40}}}, "bulb": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The other was for internal lighting , but every incandescent bulb had been shattered under the terrific concussion of the great Messines mine", "context_len": 23}}}, "blighters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` From what I know of the blighters , sir , '' remarked M'Turk , `` they did n't show their mugs above ground while we were tumbling over the top", "context_len": 31}}}, "pills": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 22, "context": "Yet , astonishing to say , I was marvellously successful , all things considered , for when at a loss I administered pills compounded of meal dough and strongly flavoured with the first harmless substance that came to hand , and so profound was the belief of these people in my ability that at least half of them were cured by the wonderful power of faith alone", "context_len": 67}}}, "premature": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` You are too premature , Herr Kapitan von Anzaken , '' he continued slowly", "context_len": 15}}}, "explosives": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 49, "context": "? Where is our officer ? How is it that I was next to him , and now Selwyn is nearer the door ; while Joliffe , who is supposed to be on the way to the dressing-station , is here ? And what about the fifty tons of explosives ? ''", "context_len": 52}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "EXPLOSIVES : BREATHS :", "context_len": 4}}}, "unpleasantness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Now , to avoid further unpleasantness , you will throw down your arms and make surrender", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Her light flaxen hair and pale , fair complexion gave her a smooth , shining appearance , and somehow Peggy always thought her manners were smooth and shining too , for she had a way of wriggling out of any little difficulty and unpleasantness , so that the blame rested upon other people , and was always ready to take a mean advantage , or play some of those little underhand tricks which schoolgirls know only too well", "context_len": 78}}}, "inert": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He tried to move , to find a heavy , inert body lying across his legs", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Bomba lifted himself , half-dazed , on his elbow and stared at the great cat , a moment before so terrible , now stretched out prone and inert", "context_len": 28}}}, "hobnailed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It was a pair of hobnailed boots", "context_len": 7}}}, "mirthlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Have I a match ? '' repeated M'Turk mirthlessly", "context_len": 10}}}, "squashed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In one of his pockets Carr found a squashed box containing three or four precious matches", "context_len": 16}}}, "fizzled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "To be sure , the catherine-wheel stuck , and utterly refused to turn , in spite of all pokings and proddings , and the rockets only fizzled off near the ground , instead of shooting up as they ought to have done ; but the squibs and crackers were quite effective , and a train of gunpowder , laid down to represent a fiery serpent , blazed away in fine style", "context_len": 71}}}, "duds": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I 'll tear off a piece , and you can set it alight -- if your matches are n't all duds ! ''", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` I have n't any duds", "context_len": 6}}}, "stertorously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "In the glare of the burning newspaper Malcolm made the astonishing discovery that Grouser Joliffe was lying across his legs , while nearer the room in which the German staff officers had been was Dick Selwyn , leaning against the wainscot and breathing stertorously", "context_len": 44}}}, "concussions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The very ground seemed to emit fire , the earth trembled under the terrific concussions , dense clouds of smoke were rising up from the disintegrated buildings , while the din was indescribably awful", "context_len": 34}}}, "shelling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "He had experienced one shelling from warships , and a worse one from air-borne artillery", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "So , when the guns died down to a desultory shelling , the Huns set to work to clear up the badly-damaged trenches", "context_len": 23}}}, "tinpot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Did n't I draw that little tinpot 's fire , and give you a chance to butt in ? ''", "context_len": 21}}}, "afters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "You wanted looking afters just fancy , nosing around a dug-out and not taking any bombs", "context_len": 16}}}, "twigged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "I kept out of sight while the Captain was taking stock , knowing he 'd send me back if he twigged me", "context_len": 22}}}, "nippy": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` If I had n't been nippy , those Huns would have plugged the lot of you , and more 'n likely they would have got away", "context_len": 27}}}, "coves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "What were those coves doing with the pick and shovel , sir , if they did n't know there was a chance of getting out that way ? ''", "context_len": 29}}}, "displaced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` That passage is closed , at any rate , '' decided Captain Nicholson , glancing in the direction of the mound of debris and the displaced girders", "context_len": 28}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "In a slight depression , littered with coils of severed barbed wire and displaced sand-bags , Captain Nicholson got his men in hand", "context_len": 23}}}, "fizz": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I 'd swop all that fizz for a pannikin of tea , '' declared Joliffe", "context_len": 16}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Bart applied the punk , there was a fizz , a sharp hiss , a writhing worm of quick flame , and then came a fearful report that split the air like the crack of doom", "context_len": 36}}}, "egress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "The first to recover , he waited until M'Kane regained consciousness , and , having placed M'Turk and Selwyn in a reclining position , set off to find an egress and bring assistance", "context_len": 33}}}, "tamper": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "To tamper with them meant certain disaster", "context_len": 7}}}, "retracing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "Then he fared on in the high road till he came to the city and entering , betook himself to the drug-market , where for two diners he bought of [ one of ] the druggists two drachms of rare strong henbane , the son of its minute , [ 595 ] and retracing his steps , returned to the palace", "context_len": 61}}}, "moonshine": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Although I believe that the Hun 's yarn about fifty tons of stuff is all moonshine , '' continued Nicholson , `` I ca n't see how one bomb would raise Cain like this", "context_len": 35}}}, "tunnelling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "It leads towards Messines Ridge , and I guess there 's not much tunnelling left there", "context_len": 16}}}, "clayey": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The ground was clayey and easy to work , but in the absence of shoring material there was a grave risk of the new tunnel caving in", "context_len": 27}}}, "shoring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The ground was clayey and easy to work , but in the absence of shoring material there was a grave risk of the new tunnel caving in", "context_len": 27}}}, "caving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The ground was clayey and easy to work , but in the absence of shoring material there was a grave risk of the new tunnel caving in", "context_len": 27}}}, "dislodging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` No , sir , '' replied Malcolm , who was working at the head of the sap and cautiously dislodging soil , which , in turn , was picked up by M'Turk and passed out so as not to obstruct the portion of the tunnel already dug", "context_len": 48}}}, "obstruct": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` No , sir , '' replied Malcolm , who was working at the head of the sap and cautiously dislodging soil , which , in turn , was picked up by M'Turk and passed out so as not to obstruct the portion of the tunnel already dug", "context_len": 48}}}, "vibrated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 5, "context": "Then , while the air vibrated with the hum and murmur of many excited tongues , shouts and a disturbance in the direction of the palace proclaimed the approach of the king and his household , and presently the entire party , numbering some three hundred , passed in and made their way to a kind of grand- stand , from which an admirable view of all the proceedings was to be obtained", "context_len": 73}}}, "subsidence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "As he did so the subsidence increased , and , amidst a shower of soil , the two riflemen found themselves falling through the air", "context_len": 25}}}, "abyss": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 56, "context": "Justice , law , liberty were solid facts ; those were the watchwords General Harrison had taught him ; for those he had lived , to those he would be true , whether good or evil fortune awaited him , whether there were , indeed , a heavenly reward for the victor , or but the abyss of forgetfulness at the end of the strife", "context_len": 65}}}, "ugh": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "A lot o ' women brought their babies to Jesus and axed Him to fondle of 'em , and take 'em in His arms ; and there wor some men about -- ugh ! I guess as they wor some'ut like father -- and they said to the women , ` Take the babies away as fast as possible ; Jesus is a great , great man , and He ca n't no way be troubled", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ugh ! I shivers when I sees 'em ; I says to myself , that 's me when I 'm old", "context_len": 21}}}, "warren": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 0, "context": "Warren returned with some sewing and without evincing undue curiosity led Marilla to talk of her past , though the child really knew very little about her mother and seemed to have no tender or regretful regard for this Mrs", "context_len": 40}}}, "stinks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Since I 've been out here I 've become a Sort of authority on stinks", "context_len": 15}}}, "fittings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "Malcolm could hear them walking along the barge , testing the locks of the fore and after cuddies , and examining the metal fittings of the winch and the tiller-head", "context_len": 30}}}, "trestle": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Most of the floor space was occupied by long trestle tables , while in one corner was the large stack of blankets and bedding upon which Malcolm and M'Turk had fallen", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Belong here ? You work on the coal trestle ! Do n't tell me ! You are covered with coal dust now ! What have you there ? Are you going to play some trick at the instigation of the freshmen ? I demand an answer ! ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "overthrown": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "It is exceedingly wicked of you , and I tell you plainly I do n't know what I shall do if all my hard work of the whole summer will be overthrown , unless you make me a solemn promise before I leave", "context_len": 43}}}, "earthenware": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "'What on earth has that stupid Nancy given me a hot bottle for on such a warm evening ? ' he exclaimed ; and flinging down the clothes he disclosed to view , not the expected earthenware bottle , but the shrinking and apologetic form of Master Rollo , who , if ever a dog could be said to own a conscience , surely showed he knew he had offended , and repented heartily of his sins", "context_len": 77}}}, "apathetically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Against the doorway were four huge Prussians , leaning apathetically against the timbered supports of the arms-rack", "context_len": 17}}}, "chests": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "He 's made of iron , Pony , and carries pianos and chests of drawers as easily as I would chairs", "context_len": 21}}}, "levelling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Sir Gyles feared much General Harrison might be set on some rash action , and by throwing things into confusion , would leave the way open for the Cavaliers to join with the vile levelling party to root out all good in the land", "context_len": 44}}}, "unblinkingly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The Boche 's eyes stared unblinkingly at the muzzle of the weapon , while his companions showed no signs of shaking off their apathy", "context_len": 24}}}, "apathy": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 26, "context": "She went above them in class -- yes ! but also she went above the Americans ! The little Mexicans , aware of a certain mental apathy , had not enviously regarded the exploits of the `` smart '' Americans", "context_len": 40}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "But Casson had become a mere child again and could not remember , did not want to talk , was wrapped in apathy , as much of a companion as a stone image", "context_len": 33}}}, "painlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Others were eating when death overtook them suddenly and painlessly", "context_len": 10}}}, "concrete": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 28, "context": "A machine-gun would be silenced for a few seconds and then resume its fire ; for each weapon , in addition to the protection afforded by the massive concrete walls , was equipped with a steel shield through which a narrow sighting-aperture afforded the only vulnerable spot", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 22, "context": "The fuel consisted of dry cane or dry pine slabs split fine ; and , as if it were to give a concrete image of the warming rays of the sun , these split canes were disposed in a spiral line which exhibited several revolutions around the centre", "context_len": 48}}}, "orifice": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "As he spoke , the orifice was darkened , and a gruff voice from above exclaimed , to the accompaniment of a string of highly uncomplimentary ejaculations :", "context_len": 28}}}, "affably": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Evenin ' , strangers , '' he remarked , as affably as a rather gruff voice and manner would permit", "context_len": 21}}}, "helpers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 31, "context": "M\u00fcller cast about in his mind as to why this should be so , and he discovered that whilst he had asked God for money to open the Home and for helpers , he had forgotten to pray that the children might be sent ; and to this he attributed such a strange occurrence", "context_len": 54}}}, "steeply": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "These , lashed together , were lowered into the hole and allowed to rest upon the steeply sloping sides", "context_len": 19}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Some twenty feet from the river the ground sloped very steeply , and such was the rate at which Jumbo was going that , when he reached this part , he could not stop himself , but tumbled head over heels , and rolling down the bank disappeared with a big , loud splash into the water", "context_len": 57}}}, "aborigine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` What have you been doing ? Cleaning out a chimney ? You 're as black as an aborigine", "context_len": 19}}}, "larrikin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Do I not , the larrikin ! '' replied Kennedy", "context_len": 11}}}, "withdrawing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "And when , at night , impressed with the sense of solemnity which night always imparts to strange and novel scenes , they looked up to the bright round moon , pleased with the expression of cheerfulness and companionship which beams always in her light , to find her suddenly waning , changing her form , withdrawing her bright beams , and looking down upon them with a lurid and murky light , it was not surprising that they felt an emotion of terror", "context_len": 84}}}, "mined": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 63, "context": "So , once more , by the matter of a few hours , Konrad von Feldoffer escaped a well-merited death ; while , through ignorance of the terrific preparations made for the blowing up of Messines Ridge , he had unwittingly done the Allies a good turn ; for instead of withdrawing the troops the Hun commander had concentrated a thousand on the mined ridge in order to repel an infantry attack that threatened only in the minds of the German staff", "context_len": 82}}}, "airmen": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 4, "context": "Fearing attacks from British airmen , the German High Command had given orders that , as far as possible , troops were to be moved toward the Front in hospital trains , while , to bring up additional machine-guns with the least danger and delay , the motor-ambulances , still displaying the symbol that all unkultured nations respect , were employed to their utmost capacity", "context_len": 65}}}, "funk": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "They absolutely funk it", "context_len": 4}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` The Boches are still on guard , '' he reported , `` an ' the dogs ; but ai n't they in a funk", "context_len": 25}}}, "derrick": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Placed on a stretcher , that was raised by means of a rope running through a block at the end of a hastily constructed derrick , the injured men were taken up the funnel-like shaft , while the others ascended by means of ladders , Captain Nicholson being the last to quit the dug-out that might have proved to be his grave", "context_len": 62}}}, "battalions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "After receiving medical attention , Selwyn and Joliffe were sent to the base hospital , while Captain Nicholson and Riflemen Carr , M'Turk , and M'Kane set out to rejoin their battalions at Messines village", "context_len": 35}}}, "entrenched": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "de Soto , when reaching the `` Alibamo province , '' had to fight the natives entrenched within a palisaded fort ( fuerte de Alibamo , Garc", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 56, "context": "The following day they continued their march to the southeast , sometimes hiding in woods , for the country was mainly occupied by Ethelred 's troops ; sometimes pursued by larger bodies of horsemen , but always successful in distancing them , until , at the approach of eventide , they came in sight of the entrenched camp of the northern host", "context_len": 62}}}, "reorganized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The Persians were driven back , the Greeks effected their landing , reorganized and formed on the shore , and the Persians , finding that all was lost , fled in all directions", "context_len": 33}}}, "materialized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Since the threatened counter-attack had not yet materialized , most of the New Zealanders were resting in the dug-outs obligingly constructed by Fritz , who little thought that he would have to abandon his painstaking work except upon the conclusion of a victorious German peace", "context_len": 45}}}, "painstaking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Since the threatened counter-attack had not yet materialized , most of the New Zealanders were resting in the dug-outs obligingly constructed by Fritz , who little thought that he would have to abandon his painstaking work except upon the conclusion of a victorious German peace", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Jethro resumed his painstaking progress and kept it up until within two hundred yards of the group , no one of which raised a head", "context_len": 25}}}, "participated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "During his comparatively brief service in the trenches he had been called upon to repel isolated raids , both by day and night ; he had taken part in several successful excursions over the top to harry the German trenches ; he had participated in one of the greatest actions on the Western Front ; but , for the first time , he was helping to man a captured position against a massive hostile counter-attack", "context_len": 75}}}, "swooping": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Overhead , far above the bursting shells , aeroplanes were swooping hither and thither", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The bomber sank through the fluffy cloud mass like a swooping eagle", "context_len": 12}}}, "fliers": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 34, "context": "As a matter of fact they were both , and high in the air fierce combats were in progress as the Hun airmen sought in vain to drive off the almost too daring British fliers", "context_len": 35}}}, "serried": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Not a shot was fired from the Anzac trenches -- although dozens of Maxims , Lewis guns , and rifles were ready to receive Fritz in the strictly conventional way -- until the foremost of the serried grey-clad masses drew within seven hundred yards", "context_len": 44}}}, "outpouring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Then , like the outpouring of a dozen concentrated thunderstorms , British guns that hitherto had been silent set up a barrage -- so heavy that the German fire , furious though it was , seemed negligible in comparison", "context_len": 39}}}, "thunderstorms": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Then , like the outpouring of a dozen concentrated thunderstorms , British guns that hitherto had been silent set up a barrage -- so heavy that the German fire , furious though it was , seemed negligible in comparison", "context_len": 39}}}, "negligible": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Then , like the outpouring of a dozen concentrated thunderstorms , British guns that hitherto had been silent set up a barrage -- so heavy that the German fire , furious though it was , seemed negligible in comparison", "context_len": 39}}}, "desisted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "The dense masses desisted , recoiled , and attempted to flee through the barrage , while death and wounds took heavy toll", "context_len": 22}}}, "surrendering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "With hands upraised and cries of `` Kamerad '' on their lips the surrendering men passed between the advancing troops , who saluted them with ironical advice to `` Cut it out , and not so much of your Kamerad stunt ! ''", "context_len": 43}}}, "adjuring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "With a feeling of complete confidence , akin to that of a child for its mother , the New Zealanders literally clung to the skirts of the barrage , at the same time adjuring the distant artillerymen to `` Push it along and let 's get on ! ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "artillerymen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "With a feeling of complete confidence , akin to that of a child for its mother , the New Zealanders literally clung to the skirts of the barrage , at the same time adjuring the distant artillerymen to `` Push it along and let 's get on ! ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "flanking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Times without number he had been impressed , and had impressed others , with the need of keeping in touch with the flanking companies", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "There were also a couple of trunks , while , flanking the pile , was the old sofa and the arm chair", "context_len": 22}}}, "coils": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 61, "context": "Acting upon the impulse of the moment , and scarcely knowing what I was about , with a single bound I flung myself upon the terrified girl and , guided more by instinct than reason , seized the reptile immediately behind the head in so vice-like a grip that its jaws at once opened wide , when I tore its hideous coils from the girl 's arm and flung it far from me into the very heart of the blazing fire", "context_len": 81}}}, "consolidate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The heat of battle over , they set to work to consolidate and hold the position they had carried at such a cost", "context_len": 23}}}, "scribbled": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "You know it was the Military Bazaar at the Assembly Rooms on Saturday , and I suppose most of the girls were there , and had no time to do their home-work , so they just scribbled down Bertha 's figures before the bell rang", "context_len": 45}}}, "unfixed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Malcolm took the folded paper and thrust it in his pocket , unfixed his bayonet and returned it to the scabbard , slung his rifle , and started off at a run in the direction of the invisible Messines village", "context_len": 40}}}, "scabbard": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 22, "context": "The man might have been mistaken for a circus performer , in his brilliant uniform , with plated horn , pistol , scabbard and belt , gay , flower-worked leggings , jingling spurs and fine boots with high heels , such as cowmen and rustlers affect", "context_len": 46}}}, "ethics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "According to the ethics of the Great War a dispatch-bearer must walk while under shell-fire , but when exposed to rifle-fire he may run without loss of dignity or prestige", "context_len": 30}}}, "imploringly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` But you will , Zekle , and I 'll never forget your goodness , '' said Penelly imploringly ; and then hastily locking the door to make sure that his visitor did not go , he went out of the room straight to a desk in his father 's office , which he opened with a key of his own , and returned directly with four five-pound notes and five sovereigns", "context_len": 72}}}, "thudding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "Then the thudding foot-falls drew nearer , while the unmistakable sound of a coil of rope being thrown upon the deck of one of the other barges was heard", "context_len": 29}}}, "terrain": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "And yet , only a few miles away , was the war-tortured belt of terrain , a mass of ruined buildings , even now being rebuilt , where Briton and Gaul were slowly yet surely wresting French soil from the Hun", "context_len": 41}}}, "strand": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "She wore a dress of thin white , and around her slender neck was a curious old strand of turquoise beads which had been found carefully hidden away in the Mexican trunk", "context_len": 32}}}, "entanglement": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "His foot tripped on a strand of wire , the lowermost and only intact part of an entanglement", "context_len": 18}}}, "instep": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Gradually he made the discovery that beyond a grazed instep , for one of the barbs had penetrated his boot , he was unwounded", "context_len": 24}}}, "ascertaining": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "In consequence of this his appearance at court , the daughter of Priam , whose name was Cassandra , became acquainted with him , and , inquiring into his story , succeeded in ascertaining that he was her brother , the long-lost child , that had been supposed to be put to death", "context_len": 53}}}, "whiff": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "It was such a lovely summer morning , one of those brilliant , glorious days when the world looks as if it had been newly created , and came to us with a whiff of Paradise about it", "context_len": 38}}}, "fatalism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "A kind of blind fatalism gripped him", "context_len": 7}}}, "discernible": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Fifty yards ahead was a zigzag trench , its direction only discernible by interrupted sections of sand-bags and badly-shattered wire", "context_len": 20}}}, "rearmost": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Evidently it was the rearmost of an intricate system of field-fortifications , for Malcolm was on the parados side while beyond , merging into smoke and haze , were other ramifications of the maze of trenches , all silent and deserted", "context_len": 41}}}, "merging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "It was funny to see how the young American citizen was merging into the British public schoolboy , for Archie was losing his Western accent , which only cropped out now and then when he was excited , and cricket and football were beginning to replace Indians and grizzlies in his conversation ; but he was totally unspoilt by his new life , and as jolly and hearty as ever", "context_len": 70}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In a word , he seemed intent in merging , in every way , his original character and habits of action in the effeminacy , luxury , and vice of the Eastern world , which he had at first so looked down upon and despised", "context_len": 45}}}, "ramifications": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Evidently it was the rearmost of an intricate system of field-fortifications , for Malcolm was on the parados side while beyond , merging into smoke and haze , were other ramifications of the maze of trenches , all silent and deserted", "context_len": 41}}}, "blundered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Bending to avoid the flying fragments that were descending like rain , Malcolm , regardless of his sprained foot , bolted round the traverse , and before he was fully aware of the fact he had blundered right into a party of Huns", "context_len": 43}}}, "unsling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The New Zealander 's first impulse was to unsling his rifle", "context_len": 11}}}, "profiting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "By use of his magazine he might drive the Huns into the next bay , and , profiting by the diversion , effect a smart retirement", "context_len": 26}}}, "overruled": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Me eatee aftelward , '' said Ki Sing when he perceived that his presence at the table was the subject of controversy , but he was overruled by Richard Dewey , who possessed a large share of independence , and would not allow himself to be controlled or influenced by the prejudices of others", "context_len": 55}}}, "confiscated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "This amiable proposal was overruled by the others , and , after the prisoner had been searched and his belongings confiscated , Malcolm was marched along the trench , preceded and followed by men with loaded rifles", "context_len": 37}}}, "corpulent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "One , a major , was short and corpulent", "context_len": 9}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Up came the two watchmen , breathing stentorously , for they were middle-aged and corpulent", "context_len": 15}}}, "convex": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Bald-headed , of florid complexion , and with abnormally-puffed eyelids , magnified still more by a pair of heavy convex glasses , the Saxon had Landsturmer written all over him", "context_len": 30}}}, "hawkish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "His hawkish features , upturned moustache , and colourless skin gave him a truly Machiavellian aspect", "context_len": 16}}}, "ruff": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "He wanted only a pointed beard and a ruff to complete the living representation of a sixteenth-century portrait of one of the ruffianly Margraves of the Palatinate", "context_len": 27}}}, "bamboozle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "If I try to bamboozle them there will be trouble", "context_len": 10}}}, "attributing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Foiled in that direction , and attributing his discomfiture to the fact that the military map was quite inadequate to present needs , the Lieutenant wrote in his notebook again", "context_len": 30}}}, "inquisitor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` How you arrive at our lines ? '' continued the inquisitor", "context_len": 12}}}, "irrelevant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Malcolm thought fit to reply in a totally irrelevant string of Maori phrases , concluding with `` Haeremai te kai '' ( come to dinner ) and a decisive shake of his head", "context_len": 33}}}, "te": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Malcolm thought fit to reply in a totally irrelevant string of Maori phrases , concluding with `` Haeremai te kai '' ( come to dinner ) and a decisive shake of his head", "context_len": 33}}}, "mercenaries": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` It is evident , '' he remarked in German to his companion , `` that the prisoner is one of the Englander 's mercenaries -- from Portugal , perhaps , or even from one of those outlandish and unheard-of nations that have presumptuously declared war against us", "context_len": 48}}}, "presumptuously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "`` It is evident , '' he remarked in German to his companion , `` that the prisoner is one of the Englander 's mercenaries -- from Portugal , perhaps , or even from one of those outlandish and unheard-of nations that have presumptuously declared war against us", "context_len": 48}}}, "sergeants": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "What would happen to you and me if those Englanders repeated the success they had over the 46th Westphalians ? By some means the enemy found out that von Tondhoven had executed the two sergeants who were caught just beyond our entanglements -- and what was the result ? Not a single officer of the 46th Regiment was given quarter", "context_len": 60}}}, "entanglements": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "What would happen to you and me if those Englanders repeated the success they had over the 46th Westphalians ? By some means the enemy found out that von Tondhoven had executed the two sergeants who were caught just beyond our entanglements -- and what was the result ? Not a single officer of the 46th Regiment was given quarter", "context_len": 60}}}, "khakis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "At any moment that infernal barrage might start , and then the khakis would be swarming on top of us", "context_len": 20}}}, "snapshots": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "To Malcolm 's mortification the Major held up a packet of documents taken from the prisoner -- his pay-book , a few letters and post cards from far-off New Zealand , and a few snapshots of incidents on board the transport Awarua", "context_len": 42}}}, "feigned": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Jane 's voice had rather a feigned lightness as she replied , `` You have everything you want , do n't you ? '' No one but herself knew that for some time she had been paying Mr", "context_len": 38}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 55, "context": "`` [ 662 ] Therewith he disappeared , and as for Alaeddin , when he heard this , his wit fled from his head and his joints trembled at the cry wherewith the Marid cried out at him ; but he took heart and leaving his closet , went in straightway to his wife and feigned to her that his head irked him , of his knowledge that Fatimeh was renowned for the secret of healing [ 663 ] all aches and pains", "context_len": 83}}}, "dictionaries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "A reference to two different Anglo-German dictionaries followed , accompanied by many guttural ejaculations from the baffled Teutons", "context_len": 18}}}, "purport": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "At length , after a more than usually intense paroxysm of pain had passed , Gouroo , Banda 's favourite wife , who was present , and whose virulent animosity I had been unfortunate enough to arouse , bent over the patient and whispered something in his ear , the purport of which I could not catch", "context_len": 57}}}, "concreted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "With the exception of a few sentries , stationed in concreted , sand-bagged shelters , the trench was deserted", "context_len": 19}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The New Zealanders settled Berlin redoubt most effectively , and in far less time than had been taken in reducing Van Meulen , Otto , and other concreted strongholds", "context_len": 29}}}, "parapets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The army within , sheltered behind the parapets of the walls , and standing there in a position above that of their assailants , have such great advantages in the contest that a long time often elapses before they can be compelled to surrender", "context_len": 44}}}, "showering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Moreover , he gave commandment unto his mamelukes , and they went out to the door of the palace and fell to showering gold upon the folk", "context_len": 27}}}, "infinitesimal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "'What 's the use ? It is surprising with what an infinitesimal fraction of knowledge one can get through this old world", "context_len": 22}}}, "moistening": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Ben sat by Sally , who had now fallen asleep , listened to the rain upon the roof , moistening the parched earth , and relieving him of all anxiety in respect to the fire kindling again during the night", "context_len": 40}}}, "billets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Often while in billets or standing by in a dug-out his chum would ask him to oblige with a whistling solo ; but now he was forced to confess that the result was not exactly melody", "context_len": 36}}}, "solo": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "A violin solo followed from the village tailor , who was much applauded , most of the hearers being persuaded that not even the band in the Warford Public Gardens could surpass 'Bill Evans and his fiddle", "context_len": 37}}}, "enquiringly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Without showing any tendency to bestir themselves the men looked at each Other enquiringly", "context_len": 14}}}, "noticeably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The carrying out of orders promptly -- generally the German soldiers ' chief concern -- was noticeably absent", "context_len": 18}}}, "counselled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "On the other hand , prudence counselled him to stay where he was", "context_len": 13}}}, "yellowish": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 1, "context": "A yellowish brown old fellow was leading them , and Cassowary said to Dallas , `` Another introduction -- Sir Veteran Vere de Vere , and Lady Annabella Vere de Vere and all the little Vere de Vere piggie-wiggies -- Yellow Boy , Saffron , Quince , Crocus , Jaundice , Topaz , Sulphur , London Fog , Sandy , Amberine , Tawny and their cousins and second cousins too numerous to mention", "context_len": 73}}}, "dugout": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "At the entrance to the dugout into which he had been taken to be questioned , a dozen men were vigorously plying pick and shovel , the while urged to still greater efforts by a gigantic sergeant", "context_len": 37}}}, "normally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Perhaps first out of a desire to do good , Franklin did his best to cheer her up , and it pleased him no end to see the color gradually come back to her cheeks as her normally high spirits returned", "context_len": 41}}}, "voicing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I 'll win through yet ! '' exclaimed the lad , voicing his thoughts aloud", "context_len": 16}}}, "stretchers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The ground was crowded with bundles of straw and stretchers , each occupied by a helpless human being", "context_len": 18}}}, "materialize": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "When the bombardment cleared , the threatened infantry assault did not materialize", "context_len": 12}}}, "greatcoat": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "While the wounded were being removed , a couple of Prussian Poles , who were employed as ambulance-men , placed Malcolm on a stretcher , and threw a discarded greatcoat over his legs , not realizing that he was an enemy , since the remnants of his khaki uniform were indistinguishable from the field-grey after they had been `` chromed '' by the fumes of bursting shells", "context_len": 67}}}, "remnants": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 20, "word_index": 23, "context": "These names were written down in 1559 , and do not show much affinity with Timucua ; but since they are the only remnants of the Calusa language , I present the full list : `` Tampa , Tomo , Tuchi , Sogo , No ( which signifies 'beloved village ' ) , Sinapa , Sinaesta , Metamapo , Sacaspada , Calaobe , Estame , Yagua , Guaya , Guevu , Muspa , Casitoa , Tatesta , Coyovea , Jutun , Tequemapo , Comachica , Quiseyove and two others in the vicinity", "context_len": 92}}}, "chromed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "While the wounded were being removed , a couple of Prussian Poles , who were employed as ambulance-men , placed Malcolm on a stretcher , and threw a discarded greatcoat over his legs , not realizing that he was an enemy , since the remnants of his khaki uniform were indistinguishable from the field-grey after they had been `` chromed '' by the fumes of bursting shells", "context_len": 67}}}, "bemoaned": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Now that they were wounded they bemoaned the fact that their injuries were not greater , and envied those of their comrades who were permanently disabled and unfit for further service in the field", "context_len": 34}}}, "disgorged": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "See here , babies , '' and he disgorged a paper of peppermints that at once soothed broken hearts", "context_len": 19}}}, "symbol": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "The hayoy\u00e1lgi , coming from the four corners of the world to light the sacred fire , the symbol of the sun , are the winds fanning it to a higher flame , and the purpose of the story is to make an oracular power of the sacred flame , by which the Holder of Breath , or Great Spirit , could be placed in communication with his Indian wards , and enabled to take care of them", "context_len": 78}}}, "postcards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "One morning an orderly went round the ward distributing postcards to enable the patients to write to their relations and friends", "context_len": 21}}}, "postcard": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "The nurse , puzzled that the patient could write and yet be unable to read , called a doctor 's attention to the fact , and Malcolm 's postcard was kept back for examination", "context_len": 34}}}, "garbed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Escorted by two Landsturmers , and garbed in very motley attire , the New Zealander was marched through the streets to the railway station , and after a six-hour journey the train stopped at a small station that , from the name on the Fahrkartenausgabe , was called D\u00fcren", "context_len": 49}}}, "industrial": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "So great was the Huns ' fear of air raids over the industrial towns of the Rhine valley that several of the larger places of detention for prisoners of war had been broken up , and the men sent to numerous small camps in close proximity to towns within the radius of hostile airmen", "context_len": 54}}}, "workshops": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The workshops were disgorging their occupants -- old men , women , and children , whose emaciated features contrasted vividly with those of the prosperous munition-workers in Great Britain", "context_len": 29}}}, "disgorging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The workshops were disgorging their occupants -- old men , women , and children , whose emaciated features contrasted vividly with those of the prosperous munition-workers in Great Britain", "context_len": 29}}}, "machines": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 77, "context": "Lafitte , Cameron had told Matt , was a member of the A\u00ebro Club , had owned a balloon of his own , and had made many ascensions from the town of Pittsfield , Massachusetts -- which was near Matt \u2019 s old home in the Berkshire Hills ; but , Cameron had also said , Lafitte had given up plain ballooning for dirigibles , and , finally , had turned his back on dirigibles for heavier-than-air machines", "context_len": 78}}}, "transit": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "It is a serious undertaking to get a great body of men and horses across a broad and rapid river , when the people of the country have done all in their power to remove or destroy all possible means of transit , and when hostile bands are on the opposite bank , to embarrass and impede the operations by every mode in their power", "context_len": 65}}}, "sandwiched": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "At the outskirts of the town was a large , newly-erected factory , from which Gotha machines , their wings folded for transit , were being taken away in large motor-lorries , while sandwiched between the building and the outskirts of the town proper was a large barbed-wire compound within which were rows of wooden huts", "context_len": 56}}}, "rectangular": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Between the outer and inner rectangular fences , a distance of fifty feet , more guards kept vigilant watch ; while at frequent intervals tall look-out boxes had been erected to enable the sentries to keep the whole of the camp under observation", "context_len": 43}}}, "perfunctory": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Here he was registered and given a number , and afterwards subjected to perfunctory examination by a doctor , who , finding that the prisoner exhibited no trace of infection or contagious disease , passed him as a fit inmate of the camp", "context_len": 43}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "One of the men even flashed an electric torch over the side , but it was a purely perfunctory action", "context_len": 20}}}, "initiation": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 23, "context": "At the commencement of the twelfth lunation he performs for four days the same rites as he did at the beginning of the initiation , but on the fifth he leaves the cabin , gathers maize-cobs , burns them to ashes , and with these rubs his whole body", "context_len": 49}}}, "rigs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "Here are three hook-and-line rigs , so it shouldn \u2019 t take us long to catch a meal", "context_len": 18}}}, "unrepresented": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "In vain Rifleman Carr looked for a New Zealand uniform : these were mostly Tommies and Jocks , a sprinkling of Canadians , and two West Indians ; Anzacs seemed to be unrepresented in the motley throng of captives", "context_len": 39}}}, "barmy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Blow me if ' e ai n't barmy ! ''", "context_len": 11}}}, "doss": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "After roll-call I 'll arrange for you ( indicating Malcolm ) to doss in No", "context_len": 15}}}, "reunited": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It was long after midnight when the reunited brothers ended their conversation", "context_len": 12}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 69, "context": "As it was , during her husband 's lifetime she thought of no one but him , and since his death her best thoughts were devoted to his memory : to keep flowers always on his grave ; to see that his portrait was dusted day after day , and that flowers were put under it ; to kneel there and utter prayers that he and she might be reunited in a better world , absorbed her strongest thoughts", "context_len": 79}}}, "aircraft": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 27, "context": "Malcolm was fairly fortunate in being in the same party as his brother , their work being to construct new roads in the vicinity of the large aircraft factory", "context_len": 29}}}, "gunshot": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "So , either by gunshot or spear wounds , died another of that glorious band of martyrs who have , century after century , fearlessly laid down their lives to advance the Kingdom of God", "context_len": 35}}}, "cools": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "That sort of thing cools a fellow down a bit ; but when we get a fair chance -- -- ! ''", "context_len": 22}}}, "deteriorate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "She absolutely cried when she saw me in it , and said that if I was determined to deteriorate in that way , she would give up the boarding-house and go to the cottage in the country without a moment 's hesitation", "context_len": 42}}}, "whirr": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "One night the men were for the most part asleep on their straw mattresses , dog-tired with their labours , when the hitherto constant whirr of machinery stopped", "context_len": 28}}}, "blasts": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 13, "context": "Bridges , too , caused delays , for , in spite of vigorous blasts of the tug 's fog-horn , the persons in charge displayed no great activity in manning the winches by which the obstructions were swung", "context_len": 38}}}, "facetiously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` The dawgs ' , Sargint ? '' asked a man facetiously", "context_len": 12}}}, "siren": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The siren had ceased its two-pitched wail", "context_len": 7}}}, "reverberating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The hollow , reverberating sound of five reports fell upon the listeners ' ears", "context_len": 14}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "When at last the rain ceased and the last reverberating peal of thunder died away in the distance , Bomba rose with a sigh and for a few moments left Tatuc alone on the broken flooring of the hut", "context_len": 39}}}, "howitzer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": ", as calmly as if giving the range of a howitzer", "context_len": 11}}}, "biplanes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Flying in perfect V-shaped formation , and at a low altitude that made the airmen more certain of hitting their objectives , were eleven biplanes standing out sharply against the star-lit sky", "context_len": 32}}}, "antis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Away on the left a battery of antis", "context_len": 8}}}, "enclosures": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "Rows of cottages and gardens , great corn and hayfields , stubble where cattle were browsing , enclosures of fattening pigs whose squealing had a mirthful sound", "context_len": 27}}}, "skulls": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 30, "context": "It is a very fierce looking ship , with guns sticking out through holes , and there are black flags as well as black sails , and white bones and skulls are painted on them", "context_len": 35}}}, "outstrip": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Panic-stricken , the grey-bearded and bald-headed guards deserted their posts and bolted precipitately , as if by running they could outstrip a squadron of biplanes moving at a hundred miles an hour", "context_len": 32}}}, "peppering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The Huns , as Peter Carr had predicted , had ceased fire , and were tearing away to take up a fresh position whence they could serve their guns without fear of the earth-returning shrapnel peppering their gunners", "context_len": 38}}}, "emit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The very ground seemed to emit fire , the earth trembled under the terrific concussions , dense clouds of smoke were rising up from the disintegrated buildings , while the din was indescribably awful", "context_len": 34}}}, "indescribably": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "The cry of vengeance , indescribably weird and savage , was taken up by the Indians and filled the jungle with a long wailing shriek that chilled the blood of Bomba as he thought of what might have happened to him had it not been for Grico \u2019 s timely appearance", "context_len": 51}}}, "unclimbable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Almost before they were aware of it they had passed what had been lines of unclimbable fence", "context_len": 17}}}, "pilots": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Make sure of your target , even if you have to sit on it , '' was one of the maxims of the daring pilots belonging to a breed that produces the best airmen in the world , bar none", "context_len": 41}}}, "produces": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Make sure of your target , even if you have to sit on it , '' was one of the maxims of the daring pilots belonging to a breed that produces the best airmen in the world , bar none", "context_len": 41}}}, "detours": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "He had to swerve and occasionally make rather long detours to avoid natural obstacles , but he lost no time", "context_len": 20}}}, "waterway": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "With characteristic caution and forethought the Huns left nothing undone to safeguard their proceedings ; hence , in the case of barges using the subterranean waterway , a corporal 's guard was placed upon each during the journey through the tunnel", "context_len": 41}}}, "looming": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Looming ominously through the misty , drizzling dawn , the pill-box might have accounted for scores of gallant New Zealanders , for it was crammed with Huns , and well provided with machine-guns", "context_len": 33}}}, "uprights": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "To the left was a string of barges ; beyond , looming faintly through the air , the outlines of a house and the uprights of a swing bridge", "context_len": 29}}}, "ignores": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Friend Hans is evidently entertaining the bargees and ignores Kaiser Bill 's lighting restrictions", "context_len": 14}}}, "coaly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "When the journey was resumed the coaly flotilla would be proceeding nearer the German-Dutch frontier -- perhaps to Holland itself , as almost every ton of coal imported into that country , since the tightening of the blockade , came from the Westphalian pits and was exchanged for badly-wanted foodstuffs", "context_len": 50}}}, "frontier": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 14, "context": "When the journey was resumed the coaly flotilla would be proceeding nearer the German-Dutch frontier -- perhaps to Holland itself , as almost every ton of coal imported into that country , since the tightening of the blockade , came from the Westphalian pits and was exchanged for badly-wanted foodstuffs", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Eight of them have crossed the frontier up to the present , and I should n't be surprised if others do the same in the course of the next few days", "context_len": 31}}}, "imported": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "When the journey was resumed the coaly flotilla would be proceeding nearer the German-Dutch frontier -- perhaps to Holland itself , as almost every ton of coal imported into that country , since the tightening of the blockade , came from the Westphalian pits and was exchanged for badly-wanted foodstuffs", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "You see , these are special imported Italian bees , Breeders", "context_len": 11}}}, "blockade": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "When the journey was resumed the coaly flotilla would be proceeding nearer the German-Dutch frontier -- perhaps to Holland itself , as almost every ton of coal imported into that country , since the tightening of the blockade , came from the Westphalian pits and was exchanged for badly-wanted foodstuffs", "context_len": 50}}}, "pits": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "When the journey was resumed the coaly flotilla would be proceeding nearer the German-Dutch frontier -- perhaps to Holland itself , as almost every ton of coal imported into that country , since the tightening of the blockade , came from the Westphalian pits and was exchanged for badly-wanted foodstuffs", "context_len": 50}}}, "foodstuffs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "When the journey was resumed the coaly flotilla would be proceeding nearer the German-Dutch frontier -- perhaps to Holland itself , as almost every ton of coal imported into that country , since the tightening of the blockade , came from the Westphalian pits and was exchanged for badly-wanted foodstuffs", "context_len": 50}}}, "padlock": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 66, "context": "The thoughts of his father 's plight , a haunting dread that Colonel Harrington might make him some trouble , and the uncertainty of continued work in the express service , all combined to depress his mind with anxiety and suspense , and he tried to dismiss the themes by whistling a quiet , soothing tune as he started to get the hammer to put the padlock in place", "context_len": 69}}}, "clears": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "Ere he died , being satisfied beyond doubt that there was no hope for him , he made a confession which fully exonerates your father and clears his name of the stigma upon it", "context_len": 34}}}, "forepeak": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Making his way for'ard , Peter discovered that there was a forepeak , but the cover was securely padlocked", "context_len": 19}}}, "padlocked": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "He took Bart through the beautiful grounds of his sumptuous home , and to a windowless padlocked room in the loft of the stable", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "They are really grinning from ear to ear with pleasure because they see you come without being chained and padlocked", "context_len": 20}}}, "props": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "Farther along the street stood a square , old-fashioned , story-and-a-half house , with a more modern ell and shed adjoining , and a wretched sagging barn , that lurched on its foundations , and was only kept from toppling farther , and possibly falling , by long , crude timber props , set against its side", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "For there she sat , with her inboard props turning slowly in the sun , and her name painted clear on the fuselage", "context_len": 23}}}, "slantwise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The barrel staves , set slantwise between the sloping bank of coal and the after bulkhead , served as a roof , while , to camouflage their place of concealment , coal was piled on the boards until the new level was about the same as the original one", "context_len": 49}}}, "camouflage": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The barrel staves , set slantwise between the sloping bank of coal and the after bulkhead , served as a roof , while , to camouflage their place of concealment , coal was piled on the boards until the new level was about the same as the original one", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 31, "context": "Over the low-lying banks the water had flowed , until for nearly a hundred yards in width there was water everywhere , hiding the tenacious mud , and acting as a camouflage to thousands of deep craters", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Even the darkness might not be camouflage enough to fool the Nip patrols", "context_len": 13}}}, "landward": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "It was an awful place in a storm , where the spray , broken up into feathery froth by the battle on the rocks , came flying over the town , and then away landward , like a fine misty rain ; but it was a grand place in a calm", "context_len": 51}}}, "header": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "`` Swim for it ! '' he exclaimed ; but , as the Carrs ran to the side of the barge , with the intention of taking a header into the canal , a voice was heard calling :", "context_len": 39}}}, "metaphorically": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "It is , however , abundantly evident from the general context that this is the author 's intention in the passage in question , Alaeddin 's head being metaphorically in the hands of ( or pledged to ) the headsman , inasmuch as he had engaged to return and suffer decapitation in case he should not succeed in recovering the princess within forty days", "context_len": 64}}}, "cocks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Animals were lowing , bellowing , bleating , whinnying , or squeaking in every note of the octave ; cocks crew , ducks quacked , dogs barked , and men talked and shouted in Welsh and English till a chance comer might have imagined himself in pandemonium", "context_len": 47}}}, "whitewash": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Thank goodness they did n't whitewash the coal ! '' he remarked", "context_len": 13}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "I , said John , mean to paint my steers \u2019 yoke , my gunning float , sled , and the boat father made me , if we can get enough ; and I \u2019 ll paint my bedroom , then put some into whitewash and paint the walls", "context_len": 49}}}, "thefts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` To stop thefts , '' was the reply", "context_len": 9}}}, "whitewashed": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` I wondered what the idea was when I saw whitewashed stacks of coal in various railway sidings in England , so I enquired", "context_len": 24}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "The little low whitewashed cottage was a humble enough place , but it looked cheerful this Sunday afternoon , with the sunlight streaming in through the tiny window , and a few early white roses shedding their sweet perfume in the small garden in front", "context_len": 45}}}, "sidings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` I wondered what the idea was when I saw whitewashed stacks of coal in various railway sidings in England , so I enquired", "context_len": 24}}}, "whitened": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "A thief could n't disturb the heap without leaving a tell-tale black gap in the whitened level of the stack", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Summer dust whitened the road , and rose to her tread in alkaline clouds", "context_len": 14}}}, "rationed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` We 're rationed on a four-days basis , are n't we ? '' The meal consisted of a Plasmon biscuit , a small bar of chocolate , and a slice of potato bread", "context_len": 34}}}, "radiation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` It 's early morning yet , and the coal has lost its heat by radiation", "context_len": 16}}}, "voluble": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}}}, "buxom": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 56, "context": "Supposedly the string of barges was `` manned '' by women , the diminishing group halting at each barge to prolong the conversation before the crews boarded their respective boats , until , by the clatter almost overhead , the fugitives knew that the last barge had received its complement -- two , perhaps three , buxom and stolid German women", "context_len": 61}}}, "sluiced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Pails clattered , water sluiced along the diminutive after deck", "context_len": 10}}}, "fussily": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then a steam-propelled craft came up , fussily and noisily", "context_len": 10}}}, "fenders": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "`` Oh , you just wait ! '' cried Tom , shaking his fist at his chum , who sank down into the depths of the old easy chair , and held up his feet as fenders to keep the indignant one at a distance", "context_len": 45}}}, "cumbersome": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "2 had started ; another and another , until the cumbersome flotilla was in motion", "context_len": 15}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Instead of remaining in the vehicle and firing over the tailboard , where only his head would have been exposed , Jethro stood in the open that is , directly in front of the cumbersome structure", "context_len": 36}}}, "pitilessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Overhead the hot sun poured pitilessly down upon the absorbent coal", "context_len": 11}}}, "absorbent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Their wet clothes were spread out against the wall of their retreat , so that the heat of the sun 's rays , penetrating the absorbent coal , would dry them sufficiently for the men before nightfall", "context_len": 37}}}, "torturing": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The one that had been bitten by the piranha was already badly swollen , and every step was accompanied by a torturing pang", "context_len": 23}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 54, "context": "Death was easy enough to face among the high enthusiasms of the battlefield ; but here , in the city , where the busy world was eating and drinking and making money among these sordid surroundings , what radiance of a celestial city could flash from opened gates to support a victim through a torturing death ? Could faith win a victory even here ?", "context_len": 65}}}, "tediously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "At irregular intervals the barges had to be passed through locks , and since the locks admitted only two boats at a time , and the hawser had to be cast off before the gate opened and secured again when the lower level was reached , progress was tediously slow", "context_len": 50}}}, "winches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Bridges , too , caused delays , for , in spite of vigorous blasts of the tug 's fog-horn , the persons in charge displayed no great activity in manning the winches by which the obstructions were swung", "context_len": 38}}}, "obstructions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Bridges , too , caused delays , for , in spite of vigorous blasts of the tug 's fog-horn , the persons in charge displayed no great activity in manning the winches by which the obstructions were swung", "context_len": 38}}}, "industrialism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The hum of industrialism was plainly audible to the two fugitives", "context_len": 11}}}, "quays": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The barges were constantly bumping into craft either tied up to the quays or proceeding in the opposite direction", "context_len": 19}}}, "filtering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Some of the lumps began to shift , while particles of coal dust , filtering through the interstices , floated in the already-stifling air , causing intense irritation to the fugitives ' eyes and throats", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The smelts going in to eat the bait , he gradually drew it up , and , when almost at the surface , gave a quick jerk ; but the water was so long filtering through the handkerchief that they all swam out", "context_len": 43}}}, "interstices": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "The dry leaves and grass which I had arranged as kindling material instantly caught fire , and in a few minutes the flames were darting fiercely upward through the interstices , and wreathing themselves about the corpse", "context_len": 37}}}, "grunting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The sound of the grunting and groaning of the tug 's noisy engine was magnified tenfold , echoing and re-echoing along the domed expanse , while clouds of sulphurous smoke permeated everything", "context_len": 32}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Coming from the north was a grunting drove of creatures", "context_len": 10}}}, "domed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The sound of the grunting and groaning of the tug 's noisy engine was magnified tenfold , echoing and re-echoing along the domed expanse , while clouds of sulphurous smoke permeated everything", "context_len": 32}}}, "sulphurous": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "The sound of the grunting and groaning of the tug 's noisy engine was magnified tenfold , echoing and re-echoing along the domed expanse , while clouds of sulphurous smoke permeated everything", "context_len": 32}}}, "permeated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "The sound of the grunting and groaning of the tug 's noisy engine was magnified tenfold , echoing and re-echoing along the domed expanse , while clouds of sulphurous smoke permeated everything", "context_len": 32}}}, "populous": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 17, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "maintaining": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 53, "context": "The Danes , clad in black armour , with their ponderous battle-axes , and fierce visages , upon which no gentle ray of mercy had yet shone ; the English , their minds set upon avenging the outraged national honour , the desolated homes , the slaughtered families : the Danes bent on maintaining their cruel superiority ; the English bent on reversing it or dying : the Danes hitherto victorious on nearly every field ; the English turning upon their oppressors as men to whom the only thing which could make life tolerable was victory", "context_len": 96}}}, "lava": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 30, "context": "People were rushing about in fright , men , women and children , fleeing they knew not where , their only thought to get away from the fiery rivers of lava that were now pouring down rapidly toward the doomed town", "context_len": 41}}}, "dilly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "If I do n't get a drink of water I 'll go dilly ! ''", "context_len": 15}}}, "consecutive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 67, "context": "With some show of hesitation the man surrendered the thing , and upon inspection I found it to be a reed of about a foot in length , with a mouthpiece shaped something like that of a whistle , and with four small holes drilled in the length of the tube , whereby an expert performer might produce seven distinct tones ; but the tones were not consecutive , and the instrument was altogether a very poor and inefficient affair", "context_len": 80}}}, "predatory": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "He could only suggest that the women manning the boat took care to avoid Populous districts , so that they could go ashore without exposing the cargo to the predatory activities of the war-tried inhabitants", "context_len": 35}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The stories they had both heard of predatory bands of Danes who had wandered far from their main body , and had sought gratification for their lust for plunder and blood in remote spots where the inhabitants dwelt in fancied security , came to their minds , and also the inquiries which had been made in the Danish camp concerning their home and the circumstances of St", "context_len": 67}}}, "persistence": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 72, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "smokers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Judging by the persistence with which the two smokers stuck to their seats by the open window , Malcolm felt certain that they had a special interest in the barge and its contents", "context_len": 33}}}, "pellet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Abner Fleming felt the zip of a bullet which grazed his forehead , and one of his neighbors on the other side of the circle was scraped by a pellet of lead which abraded an arm", "context_len": 36}}}, "thudded": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Immediately after came the `` pluff '' of an air-gun being discharged , and a pellet thudded against a post on the tow-path", "context_len": 23}}}, "overhang": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Silently the fugitives lowered themselves into the water , and , swimming cautiously , gained the slight protection afforded by the bluff overhang of the boat 's quarter and long , projecting rudder", "context_len": 33}}}, "testing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "Stanley Hall 's suggestions in his monograph , `` How to Teach Reading , '' where he asks for `` true child-editions , made by testing many children with the work piece-meal and cutting and adapting the material till it really and closely fitted the minds and hearts of the children", "context_len": 51}}}, "cuddies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Malcolm could hear them walking along the barge , testing the locks of the fore and after cuddies , and examining the metal fittings of the winch and the tiller-head", "context_len": 30}}}, "winch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Malcolm could hear them walking along the barge , testing the locks of the fore and after cuddies , and examining the metal fittings of the winch and the tiller-head", "context_len": 30}}}, "wetting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "To cross the canal without wetting the food and blankets he swam back with the spoils held over his head", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Well , '' said the colonel , `` I suppose I shall have to forgive her too , since she saved you from a wetting , and took a bad cold in your service", "context_len": 35}}}, "quench": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 62, "context": "They crawl along the ground to get at the canteens of their dead companions , in hopes to find , remaining in them , some drops to drink ; and if there is a little brook meandering through the battle-field , its bed gets filled and choked up with the bodies of those who crawled there , in their agony , to quench their horrible thirst , and die", "context_len": 69}}}, "stowaways": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Making use of their observation-holes the stowaways saw that the craft had tied up alongside another barge which was fast to a long quay", "context_len": 24}}}, "barrows": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "Nearly fifty people were lounging about , regarding the new arrival with curious interest , while on the adjoining barge stood about a dozen men in grimy overalls , with planks , barrows , and spades , in readiness to commence work", "context_len": 42}}}, "trundling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Like ants the coal-heavers swarmed over the heaped-up cargo , shovelling the coal into barrows and trundling them along the planks on to the quay , whence they disappeared into a large shed about a hundred yards away", "context_len": 38}}}, "superimposed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "With a mighty heave of his shoulders Peter sent the barrel staves and their superimposed covering of coal flying", "context_len": 19}}}, "waterside": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "With lowered heads they charged straight for the nearmost of the waterside idlers", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Everywhere along the canal wharves were barges , often double- and triple-tiered , but alongside the waterside edges of the gardens were several small pleasure-craft", "context_len": 25}}}, "idlers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "She looked spiteful enough to throw it out of the window , but her husband , laughing at her , doffed his worn straw , coolly put on the elaborate headgear , and became thenceforward a target for the quips of the merry idlers about the door", "context_len": 47}}}, "cobbled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Along the cobbled street the two men ran , Passers-by stood stock-still in amazement", "context_len": 14}}}, "neutral": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "Her sides were painted in red , white , and blue horizontal stripes , while amidships her name was displayed in letters six feet in height -- in conformity with an arbitrary regulation made by an unscrupulous nation whose U-boats did not hesitate to torpedo at sight , despite the distinguishing marks of neutral craft", "context_len": 55}}}, "astounded": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The astounded Alden turned to follow him with his eyes , when he descried a tall bony horse approaching , on whose back was a massive man with shaggy whiskers , and a pipe in his mouth", "context_len": 37}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 42, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}}}, "refugees": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 62, "context": "Their roving disposition impelled them to leave this settlement also , and to migrate to the fertile Alachua plains , where they built Cuscowilla on the banks of a lake , and had to defend it against the attacks of the Tomocos , Utinas , Calloosas ( ? ) , Yamases and other remnant tribes of Florida , and the more northern refugees from Carolina , all of whom were helped by the Spaniards", "context_len": 74}}}, "diddled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "You remember I told you about him just before we said good-bye to D\u00fcren : how he diddled Fortescue , Selwyn , and me at the Cape and was collared on board the Pomfret Castle , and afterwards managed to join the N", "context_len": 43}}}, "collared": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "You remember I told you about him just before we said good-bye to D\u00fcren : how he diddled Fortescue , Selwyn , and me at the Cape and was collared on board the Pomfret Castle , and afterwards managed to join the N", "context_len": 43}}}, "mufti": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "He saw Malcolm ascend the bridge , but , the latter being in mufti and having his back turned towards him , von Feldoffer did not recognize the New Zealander", "context_len": 30}}}, "conformity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "Her sides were painted in red , white , and blue horizontal stripes , while amidships her name was displayed in letters six feet in height -- in conformity with an arbitrary regulation made by an unscrupulous nation whose U-boats did not hesitate to torpedo at sight , despite the distinguishing marks of neutral craft", "context_len": 55}}}, "arbitrary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Her sides were painted in red , white , and blue horizontal stripes , while amidships her name was displayed in letters six feet in height -- in conformity with an arbitrary regulation made by an unscrupulous nation whose U-boats did not hesitate to torpedo at sight , despite the distinguishing marks of neutral craft", "context_len": 55}}}, "repatriated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "There were about a dozen repatriated Britons from Ruhleben , a score of Dutch merchants , the two New Zealanders , and the spy , Konrad von Feldoffer", "context_len": 28}}}, "dissension": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The German authorities knew full well the beneficial effect to their armies once they could provoke unhealthy rivalry and bitter dissension between the American and British troops , but they forgot the force of the trite quotation of an American admiral : `` Blood is thicker than water ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "trite": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "The German authorities knew full well the beneficial effect to their armies once they could provoke unhealthy rivalry and bitter dissension between the American and British troops , but they forgot the force of the trite quotation of an American admiral : `` Blood is thicker than water ''", "context_len": 49}}}, "porters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The latter was watching the people on the jetty when he saw von Feldoffer , preceded by a couple of porters , elbowing his way through the crowd of onlookers to board the vessel", "context_len": 34}}}, "elbowing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The latter was watching the people on the jetty when he saw von Feldoffer , preceded by a couple of porters , elbowing his way through the crowd of onlookers to board the vessel", "context_len": 34}}}, "territorial": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "We possess no vocabulary of it , and even the tribal name belongs to Chicasa : t\u00fannig post , pillar , support , probably post of territorial demarcation of their lands on the Yazoo river", "context_len": 35}}}, "undeterred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Yet undeterred , the captain of the Koning der Zee had sailed regularly since the memorable 4th of August , 1914 , risking U-boats and floating mines to uphold the flag of Holland on waters that were hers by equal right with other nations of the world", "context_len": 47}}}, "porpoises": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Like porpoises , the deadly cylinders swung towards the ship under the strain on the span", "context_len": 16}}}, "cylinders": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "The explosion in the cylinders was unfailing , the mixture of air and gasoline was perfect , and the coils hummed their beautiful rhythm to the well-timed spark", "context_len": 28}}}, "plating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Calculated to blow a hole in the bottom of the most strongly constructed war-ship afloat , the mines simply pulverized the thin steel plating of the luckless Dutch vessel", "context_len": 29}}}, "transmitter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The captain and the wireless operator were the last to leave , the latter striving in vain to get the damaged transmitter into working order until peremptory orders from his superior obliged him to desist", "context_len": 35}}}, "tolerant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The man eyed the erratically-clad New Zealander with tolerant amusement", "context_len": 10}}}, "bluejacket": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "The intentionally-misleading use of the word 'mister ' led the bluejacket to believe that Malcolm was a junior officer of one of the overseas contingent", "context_len": 25}}}, "overseas": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "That the latter should be collecting these things for mercenary purposes with the design , when he should have enough , of shipping them overseas and selling them at fabulous prices , and then following them to live for the rest of his life in wealth and luxury amid the civilization whose value he knew , did not enter Bomba \u2019 s mind", "context_len": 63}}}, "unimpeachable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` There would be a most unholy rumpus if I ran the fellow in and he turned out to be a neutral of unimpeachable character", "context_len": 25}}}, "notified": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 2, "context": "She was notified by the Dauntless Company that two entries , the fourth and fifth east , had entered her property , in which she had never suspected the presence of coal , and that the owners were prepared to negotiate with her suitable terms for the right of working the vein in question", "context_len": 54}}}, "awarded": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 42, "context": "Ashton ; then continued , `` we all bind ourselves , do we not , to do as Bessie wishes and to keep this little transaction a secret among ourselves , making no mention to Lena Neville that the prize was not awarded to her in the first instance ? ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "awards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Other awards had been made to Sergeant Fortescue and Rifleman Selwyn", "context_len": 11}}}, "rebuilt": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "It was pitiful to see the blackened ruins of churches and palace , which had not been rebuilt since the Danish raid of 1010 , but the commoner dwellings were rising with rapidity from their ashes , or had already risen , for the shelter of the earthworks and other fortifications was not to be despised , and prevented the place from being utterly abandoned", "context_len": 65}}}, "wresting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "And yet , only a few miles away , was the war-tortured belt of terrain , a mass of ruined buildings , even now being rebuilt , where Briton and Gaul were slowly yet surely wresting French soil from the Hun", "context_len": 41}}}, "jay": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Yes and light in the head too , '' said a contemptuous voice , and glancing up we saw a blue jay sitting solitary and looking rather ugly on a bough just near us", "context_len": 35}}}, "moves": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` All I know is that the division moves up to the front on the 3rd of next month , so it looks as if we 're going to shake Fritz by the scruff of his neck", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "But , by a series of clever moves on the part of the young sleuth , the crime is solved and the diamond found in a most unusual hiding place", "context_len": 30}}}, "scruff": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "She was up the yew-tree in two leaps and a bound , and by crawling along an overhanging bough , clinging to a branch , and making a long arm , she managed to seize puss by the scruff of her neck , and release her from her dangerous position", "context_len": 50}}}, "shuffled": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 106, "context": "that he should suppose her to be capable of such an evasion , which to her sense of uprightness would have been as bad as speaking a falsehood by word of mouth ; `` no , of course I did not , that would have been telling her , would it not ? One can do a falsehood as well as tell it , '' and although she had intended no reflection upon her brother , no thrust at him , Percy was ashamed as he remembered how often , during the last few months , he had done this very thing ; how he had shuffled and evaded , and thought it no great harm as long as he did not put his falsehood into actual words", "context_len": 128}}}, "estaminet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "We were billeted at an estaminet that had copped it pretty thick", "context_len": 12}}}, "outbuildings": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 58, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}}}, "ablutions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "He had only just started his ablutions when Madame 's face appeared at the only window left in the inn", "context_len": 20}}}, "na": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "[ Footnote 55 : Badki tecouli [ na ]", "context_len": 9}}}, "poo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Soap na poo", "context_len": 3}}}, "dud": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It was a thundering good job when he slung that dud bomb at Featherstone Camp , or I might n't be here now", "context_len": 23}}}, "linking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Shelter was provided by the simple expedient of linking up suitable shell-craters , with which the soft ground was liberally besprinkled", "context_len": 21}}}, "besprinkled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Shelter was provided by the simple expedient of linking up suitable shell-craters , with which the soft ground was liberally besprinkled", "context_len": 21}}}, "spasmodic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "On the second day the men take their war-physic , a decoction of the button-snake root , in such quantities as would produce strong spasmodic effects", "context_len": 26}}}, "gruelling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Gamely the New Zealanders endured their gruelling ; until the guns lifted and put up a barrage behind them", "context_len": 19}}}, "jumpiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Up to the present their attention was thrown away ; neither Henderson nor Stewart showed the faintest indication of `` jumpiness ''", "context_len": 22}}}, "disjointed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Her voice died away in disjointed mutterings , and from neither her nor Casson could he get anything clearer that evening nor in the days that followed", "context_len": 27}}}, "cloaked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Beg pardon , sir ! '' he exclaimed , for standing in the doorway , cloaked and wearing his shrapnel-helmet , was the Brigadier", "context_len": 25}}}, "brigadiers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "It had been an anxious time , especially to the commanding officers , and , in order to satisfy themselves that the boys were still in a position to carry out the attack , the brigadiers made personal tours along the firing-line", "context_len": 42}}}, "tours": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "It had been an anxious time , especially to the commanding officers , and , in order to satisfy themselves that the boys were still in a position to carry out the attack , the brigadiers made personal tours along the firing-line", "context_len": 42}}}, "dial": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Well , if here is n't another beggar for crumbs , '' chattered an impudent little voice near me , and looking round I saw a chipmunk sitting on a sun dial and washing his face and paws for supper", "context_len": 41}}}, "craters": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "Over the low-lying banks the water had flowed , until for nearly a hundred yards in width there was water everywhere , hiding the tenacious mud , and acting as a camouflage to thousands of deep craters", "context_len": 37}}}, "loathe": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` That horror ! That dreadful stick-in-the-mud ! Oh , she is detestable ! I can not tell you how I loathe her", "context_len": 23}}}, "inkling": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "Not a word of this conversation had reached Stone \u2019 s ears , yet , sitting on the blanket and looking toward Winton and Hayden , Ben somehow obtained a slight inkling of the truth", "context_len": 35}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "When supper was over , to satisfy Jimmy , Ben was compelled to tell about the football game , and this he did with such modesty that the listeners , who had not witnessed the contest , were given no inkling as to how conspicuously he had figured in it", "context_len": 50}}}, "reoccupied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The Huns intended attacking at about the same time as the New Zealanders , and the hitherto deserted block-houses had been reoccupied during the night by swarms of German infantry", "context_len": 30}}}, "howitzers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "5-inch howitzers , supported by the giant 12- and 14-inch long-range guns farther back , threw tons of metal upon the enemy lines , the heavier projectiles hurtling overhead with a roar like that of an express train", "context_len": 38}}}, "inaccurately": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "So intense was the fire that the German artillery now replied but feebly and inaccurately , while , a sure sign that Fritz was `` done '' red and green rockets sent their distress signals from the enemy position for aid that was not forthcoming", "context_len": 45}}}, "adjustment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Here and there men gave a final adjustment to their steel helmets or fumbled with their equipment , but for the most part the New Zealanders stood motionless , with firm , set faces , awaiting the command to unleash", "context_len": 40}}}, "unleash": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Here and there men gave a final adjustment to their steel helmets or fumbled with their equipment , but for the most part the New Zealanders stood motionless , with firm , set faces , awaiting the command to unleash", "context_len": 40}}}, "outposts": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Almost before he was aware of it , Malcolm found himself confronted by a practically intact concrete block-house , which was so near the New Zealand outposts that it had escaped damage during the bombardment", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now , first of all , we must set our outposts around to give us due warning of their approach ; and then every man must arm himself as best he can , and let me see what figure you can all make", "context_len": 43}}}, "helmeted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Furtively a German poked his steel helmeted head through the doorway", "context_len": 11}}}, "morass": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "Looking like muddy replicas of Lot 's wife , Malcolm and Selwyn at last emerged from the morass , Fortescue was ahead , Corporal Preston too , while M'Turk , with his chum M'Kane hanging on to his back , was just extricating himself from a deep crater", "context_len": 48}}}, "spurts": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "Jethro showed his natural timidity , and kept as close as he could to the veteran , while Alden indulged in spurts of his own which more than once brought results", "context_len": 31}}}, "flecked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Here and there huge spurts of mud and water leapt towards the rainy sky as German shells burst indiscriminately in the swollen stream ; while everywhere the slowly-flowing water was flecked with little spurts of spray as the machine-gun bullets ricochetted from the surface", "context_len": 44}}}, "ricochetted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "Here and there huge spurts of mud and water leapt towards the rainy sky as German shells burst indiscriminately in the swollen stream ; while everywhere the slowly-flowing water was flecked with little spurts of spray as the machine-gun bullets ricochetted from the surface", "context_len": 44}}}, "pestilent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "When a man was hit when crossing that forbidding morass it generally meant death to him -- death by suffocation in the pestilent mud of Flanders", "context_len": 26}}}, "replicas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Looking like muddy replicas of Lot 's wife , Malcolm and Selwyn at last emerged from the morass , Fortescue was ahead , Corporal Preston too , while M'Turk , with his chum M'Kane hanging on to his back , was just extricating himself from a deep crater", "context_len": 48}}}, "extricating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Looking like muddy replicas of Lot 's wife , Malcolm and Selwyn at last emerged from the morass , Fortescue was ahead , Corporal Preston too , while M'Turk , with his chum M'Kane hanging on to his back , was just extricating himself from a deep crater", "context_len": 48}}}, "muck": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "During the summer it was a treacherous place to visit , for there were deep muck holes scattered through it , and more than one cow , and several horses , had broken out of the pastures , and wandered into the wet place , only to sink down to their deaths", "context_len": 52}}}, "essayed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "He would attempt to carry out the task the bomber had essayed when a chance bullet struck him down : to wipe out the viper 's nest and to silence the deadly machine-gun that was loosing a fresh bolt of ammunition upon the floundering men making their way across the swollen stream", "context_len": 52}}}, "viper": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "He would attempt to carry out the task the bomber had essayed when a chance bullet struck him down : to wipe out the viper 's nest and to silence the deadly machine-gun that was loosing a fresh bolt of ammunition upon the floundering men making their way across the swollen stream", "context_len": 52}}}, "redoubt": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "The Guards and Highlanders strove in friendly emulation who should be first in the Russian redoubt ; but Sir Colin , well ahead of his own men was first in the battery shouting : --", "context_len": 35}}}, "hellish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Bravely a number of New Zealanders rushed forward with bomb and bayonet , only to drop in the mud under the hellish machine-gun fire", "context_len": 24}}}, "apertures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "For nearly twenty minutes the men maintained a hot fire , concentrating their aim upon the narrow apertures through which the machine-guns were delivering their death-dealing bullets", "context_len": 27}}}, "vulnerable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "A machine-gun would be silenced for a few seconds and then resume its fire ; for each weapon , in addition to the protection afforded by the massive concrete walls , was equipped with a steel shield through which a narrow sighting-aperture afforded the only vulnerable spot", "context_len": 47}}}, "disheartening": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "His death , far from disheartening the men , added fuel to their burning ardour", "context_len": 15}}}, "blockhouse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The entrance to the blockhouse was forbidding enough", "context_len": 8}}}, "grenades": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "We ducked into a slit trench and started tossing hand grenades , but the Japs chucked them right back at us before they could explode", "context_len": 25}}}, "clawing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "What he did see was a puma , larger than the one he had slain outside , clawing at the inner door between the two rooms and at times hurling its huge body against the door", "context_len": 36}}}, "gouging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "In hot blood civilized men went back to primeval instincts and fought like wild beasts , clawing , tearing and gouging when it was too close work for the bayonet", "context_len": 30}}}, "ripped": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 26, "context": "For a time it seemed that Boxer would score , as , after getting possession of the ball by means of a forward pass , they ripped off twenty yards , and followed that up by gathering in ten more by a smashing play through center", "context_len": 46}}}, "plastered": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 47, "context": "Bartram , who saw it in 1775 , depicts it as the largest , most compact , and best situated Indian town he ever saw ; the habitations were large and neatly built , the walls of the houses consisted of a wooden frame , lathed and plastered inside and outside with a reddish clay , and roofed with cypress bark or shingles", "context_len": 63}}}, "unfix": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Hallo , Henderson ! '' he exclaimed , catching sight of the man , who was vainly struggling to unfix the remains of his bayonet", "context_len": 26}}}, "greenhorn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "A greater contrast to the mild-spoken , 'Varsity-educated greenhorn , who , a few years previously , was down on his luck in New Zealand , could hardly be imagined", "context_len": 30}}}, "commissioned": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "of the remnants of C Company , while not a single commissioned officer was left standing", "context_len": 16}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 3, "context": "My friend has commissioned me to see this matter through , for if his worst surmises are fulfilled Miss Mullins , and you also , Miss Wickham , and of course your mother , may find yourselves in an uncomfortable position", "context_len": 41}}}, "augment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Scores of prisoners , making their way in the opposite direction , were visible and comforting signs that the day was still going well ; while wounded New Zealanders , painfully making for the dressing-stations , were able to augment the news by the announcement that the Diggers were up and over the Abraham Heights", "context_len": 55}}}, "impediment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "On the slope , the mud , though still ankle-deep , was a hardly-noticeable impediment", "context_len": 15}}}, "stalking": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then one day , when he had come upon the trail of a tapir and was stalking it , that sixth sense of his told him that he , too , was being followed", "context_len": 34}}}, "garrisons": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "The Egyptian garrisons of the Soudan towns were sore beset by the legions which were gathering beneath the banners of the Mahdi , who , flushed with victory , was threatening an eruption into Lower Egypt itself", "context_len": 37}}}, "consolidation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Before the men could be sent forward a pioneer battalion , composed mostly of Maoris , whose skill at rapid digging-in had won the admiration of the High Command , came surging up to assist in the consolidation of the captured position", "context_len": 42}}}, "grousing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The man , ragged and battle-worn , was grousing no longer", "context_len": 11}}}, "fags": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Cigars , fags , and drinks no end", "context_len": 8}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Why , he fags for Dodds just as Hal would like us to fag for him ; only we wo n't", "context_len": 21}}}, "mineral": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "He handed Malcolm a bottle of soda-water , and bestowed a similar gift upon Selwyn and Sergeant Fortescue , for two canvas bags , meant to carry a stock of bombs , were crammed with filled bottles of mineral water from the captured dug-out", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The name for salt , h\u00e1pi , a mineral which had a sacrificial importance , is found also in Yuchi in the form t\u00e1pi , but Creek has \u00f3k-tchanua , Hitchiti : ok-tch\u00e1hane", "context_len": 33}}}, "spearing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Like spearing eels in the Waikato , it was", "context_len": 10}}}, "eels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Like spearing eels in the Waikato , it was", "context_len": 10}}}, "contrivances": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was almost sunset when Peggy , having seen the last of Archie 's contrivances , and bidden good-bye to kind Miss Forster , passed by the Rectory hedge , and hearing the brisk sound of the mowing-machine , pushed open the little gate and went in , knowing she was always sure of a welcome", "context_len": 56}}}, "undetected": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "So intricate was the going that , although several bodies of troops had passed well ahead , there were pill-boxes and other fortified posts left undetected in their rear", "context_len": 29}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "As for me , my father knows that there is only one person -- myself -- who would dare to do what I have done for you to-night ; and even were I to succeed in returning to my own quarters undetected -- which is exceedingly doubtful -- his anger at your loss will be so great that he would assuredly condemn me to take your place at the stake", "context_len": 70}}}, "resurrect": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Fritz , lying perdu , while the crowd of Anzacs poured onward , would resurrect his tic-tocs and direct a withering machine-gun fire into the backs of the luckless men", "context_len": 30}}}, "reciprocated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "As the Prussian officer raised his revolver to fire point-blank at Sergeant Fortescue , Malcolm plunged his bayonet into the Hun 's side , while Fortescue reciprocated the service by shooting a German who was about to deal Rifleman Carr a smashing blow with the butt of his rifle before the latter could disengage his blade", "context_len": 56}}}, "dressings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "While Rifleman Carr was busy with first-aid dressings , Sergeant Fortescue was pondering over the situation", "context_len": 16}}}, "jeopardized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Yet if the rule were broken , and every unwounded man took upon himself to succour his disabled chum , the advance would be jeopardized", "context_len": 25}}}, "plastic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "From his waist downwards he was literally caked with plastic mud", "context_len": 11}}}, "wedge": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "Into the gap a strong body of German troops , who , having previously held the wood , knew how to avoid the treacherous swamp , came hurriedly , with the intention of driving a wedge between the assaulting troops", "context_len": 40}}}, "converging": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "We were raising her very fast now that both ships were steering upon converging lines ; I could already see nearly to the foot of her topsails ; and I settled myself comfortably , determined to remain where I was until I could absolutely identify her , although even at this time I had scarcely a shadow of a doubt that it was the long-sought Virginia , or rather the Preciosa , that I held in the field of my telescope", "context_len": 81}}}, "volleys": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Better work could be performed by individual shooting , but the diverging fire from five rifles was a feeble reply to the converging volleys from ten times that number , while the emplacement , constructed to meet an attack from the westward , was ill-designed to ward off an assault from the opposite quarter", "context_len": 54}}}, "conserving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "For full five minutes the defenders lay low , replying cautiously to the hostile fire , yet conserving their energies for the time when the Huns would attempt to rush the scantily-held post", "context_len": 33}}}, "parried": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The second promptly lunged with his bayonet , and , although Malcolm parried , the blade transfixed his left arm just above the elbow", "context_len": 24}}}, "blues": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "The `` blues '' might be ill-fitting and sloppy , but it was a point of honour amongst the `` boys '' that their head-gear should be smart", "context_len": 28}}}, "stiffish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` It 's a stiffish pull", "context_len": 6}}}, "gip": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` It gives me gip at times ; suppose I 'm a bit out of training , too , '' replied Malcolm", "context_len": 22}}}, "portals": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "had ) the four-and-twenty portals '' ( mejouz , apparently a Syrian variant of mejaz , lit", "context_len": 17}}}, "asphalted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Following an asphalted path between well-kept lawns they arrived at the corrugated-iron building in which Sergeant Fortescue was to be found", "context_len": 21}}}, "prologue": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Prologue -- '' Under which King ? '' I", "context_len": 9}}}, "bonniest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "This induced me to consider him more attentively , and thus to study well one of the bonniest baby faces it has ever been my lot to behold", "context_len": 28}}}, "dimple": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "He was a rounded and curved creature -- not an angle anywhere about him ; his chin was a dimple , his lips rose-buds , his eyes sapphires ; his little head was a mass of tangled golden curls ; sunshine seemed to kiss him all over -- hair , eyes , lips , even to the small pink toes -- for he had pulled off his shoes and stockings , which were held tightly in two fat hands", "context_len": 79}}}, "sapphires": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "He was a rounded and curved creature -- not an angle anywhere about him ; his chin was a dimple , his lips rose-buds , his eyes sapphires ; his little head was a mass of tangled golden curls ; sunshine seemed to kiss him all over -- hair , eyes , lips , even to the small pink toes -- for he had pulled off his shoes and stockings , which were held tightly in two fat hands", "context_len": 79}}}, "meaningless": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "He was full of heart-sunshine too , for his gay voice babbled continually , saying words , to our deaf ears meaningless , but which , doubtless , the angels understood very well", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "At intervals he would sink into a stupor that would endure for hours , to be broken again by wild tossings and meaningless phrases", "context_len": 24}}}, "boo": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Only see how she runs when she gets on the top of one of these long seas ; and how they come up under the stern , and roll over , and go boo", "context_len": 34}}}, "moneymaking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "John Henry Warden was a carpenter by trade ; he was a well-to-do workman , employed constantly in a profitable and moneymaking business", "context_len": 23}}}, "widower": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "This brother owned a farm on which there lived with him a man named Adams , a widower , and his little daughter , Dorothy", "context_len": 25}}}, "defrauded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Yes , he was outwardly a model character : he worked early and late ; he saved money ; he was never in debt ; he defrauded no man ; his evenings were spent either in attending lectures of working men like himself or studying the subjects he loved at home ; he never drank ; he never swore ; he was looked up to , and brought forward as an example to follow for many a poor drunken wretch", "context_len": 80}}}, "uprightly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "It was to this man -- this hard , hard man -- who lived so uprightly in the eyes of his fellow men , but so far from his God , that the same God of love and pity and infinite compassion would yet reveal Himself", "context_len": 46}}}, "whirlwind": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 50, "context": "Peggy 's head was aching , her eyes were red and swollen with crying , and dark despair was settling down upon her , when the door suddenly opened , and Lilian burst in and caught her in a regular bear 's hug , while Nora followed like a perfect whirlwind only a yard behind", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Against such whirlwind playing as this the Fairview line crumpled and went to pieces", "context_len": 14}}}, "rebelling": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Roy , seated on the floor , was rebelling at his shoes and stockings being put on , and Faith had to use all her powers of imagination in describing the outside world , to induce him to submit to the process", "context_len": 42}}}, "fireguard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "She placed the fireguard carefully before the fire , kissed her little brother , and with a beating heart slipped out", "context_len": 21}}}, "debates": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "He organised meetings , got up debates , and did , in short , those thousand and one things which an energetic , clever man can do to put fire and life into such proceedings", "context_len": 35}}}, "weekdays": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Meanwhile , little Roy pursued his way down the long street which led from his home to another , which on weekdays was full of shops and gay with light and many-coloured windows", "context_len": 33}}}, "temperate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Religious , and yet allowing all harmless and innocent amusements both for Sundays and weekdays ; temperate , but permitting the use of beer and wine in moderation", "context_len": 28}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 74, "context": "The former was content to lie there gazing into his enemy 's eyes , for his strength was returning with every breath ; that breathing was less laboured , and , in place of his heart throbbing and jumping , sending hot gushes of blood , as it were , choking to his throat , it began to settle steadily down to its ordinary labours in the breast of a strongly-built , healthy , temperate man", "context_len": 76}}}, "pouting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Yes ; the golden-brown eyelashes , the tangled mass of bright hair , the full pouting lips , the rounded limbs , made up a picture which might well cause any father 's heart to beat with love and pride ; and doubtless there was much of both in Warden 's soul just then", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` A little bit jealous , perhaps , '' said Aunt Susan , giving a quick glance at Lucy 's pouting face , then turning away with a sigh", "context_len": 29}}}, "earnings": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 55, "context": "She was the older daughter , a strong , healthy girl of seventeen , who never helped her mother about the work , who dressed in such cheap finery as she could obtain by hook or crook , who took music lessons on a rented melodeon paid for out of her mother \u2019 s hard earnings , who felt herself to be a lady unfortunately born out of her sphere , and who was unquestionably ashamed of her surviving parent and her brother and sister", "context_len": 85}}}, "retrench": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "He began to consider by what means he could add to his earnings , by what means he could retrench his present expenses", "context_len": 23}}}, "disagreeably": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Warden laughed again disagreeably , and , shutting the door on Davis , returned to his work ; but the little incident and the burning , angry words of the despairing man shook him unpleasantly , and his temper , never one of the best , was in such a ruffled condition , that it only wanted the faintest provocation to kindle it into a blaze", "context_len": 66}}}, "provocation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "Warden laughed again disagreeably , and , shutting the door on Davis , returned to his work ; but the little incident and the burning , angry words of the despairing man shook him unpleasantly , and his temper , never one of the best , was in such a ruffled condition , that it only wanted the faintest provocation to kindle it into a blaze", "context_len": 66}}}, "incalculable": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "He said that the Persians had , under the ancestors of Darius , crossed the Hellespont , invaded Greece , laid waste the country , and destroyed cities and towns , and had thus done them incalculable injury ; and that Darius himself had been plotting against his ( Alexander 's ) life , and offering rewards to any one who would kill him", "context_len": 64}}}, "prospectus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He was drawing up a prospectus for the new society , and his head was bent low over the paper", "context_len": 20}}}, "fader": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Ob course not ; I found dat out de fust day , when I axed him how old he was , what war de name ob his fader and mother , wheder he was married or engaged and who he war gwine to wote fur as President , and some more sich trifles", "context_len": 53}}}, "uncomprehending": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Meanwhile the little child , stunned by the blows , terrified by the rough , hard words , totally uncomprehending what he had done wrong , for Faith had many times given him old papers to tear , lay for a moment or two trembling on the floor", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "A confusion of Spanish and English hurtled on Jane 's uncomprehending ear ; some one climbing the step cried , `` Ave Maria ! '' as his eyes fell on the couch", "context_len": 32}}}, "madden": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` There ! do n't touch them ; 't is jest enough to madden a man , but you shall suffer", "context_len": 21}}}, "forgiving": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "But oh ! how changed was everything now ! God , who was spoken of as very loving , very forgiving , very kind at Sunday-school , was dreadfully angry with her", "context_len": 32}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 49, "context": "Mingled with many other things , giving an account of their summer among the mountains , and written in Maggie 's own droll , peculiar way , ran the story of Lem and Dolly , of their persecutions , and of the difficulty she and Bessie had had in forgiving their many injuries ; but all that was not new to the mother , who now read for the first time what Maggie had written during the last week", "context_len": 79}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "But the more she loved and cherished her own plant , the more bitterly did she grieve over the destruction of the gardens of the two little girls who had been so kind and forgiving to her", "context_len": 37}}}, "inter": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "I think as I would n't go out , Honey ; the little 'un will be brought back now they has put it inter the hands of the perleece", "context_len": 29}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "Jest because , mebbe , you got inter some foolish boy scrape an \u2019 got this Hayden boy down on you , be y \u2019 u goin \u2019 to let him keep y \u2019 u from gittin \u2019 an eddercation , to make a man of y \u2019 u , an \u2019 take you through the world ?", "context_len": 58}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "But did you do nothing since ? Why you should ha ' done heaps and heaps -- you should ha ' gone to the perlice , and put the young 'un inter the ` Hue and Cry ; ' you should ha ' done all that last night , Faith", "context_len": 50}}}, "gin": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "A careful mixture of gin and syrup was smeared upon the trees , which were afterwards visited with a lantern , when the unfortunate insects could easily be taken in the midst of their revels , falling sad victims to the sin of intemperance", "context_len": 44}}}, "canny": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` It do n't seem no way canny like , to hear him going on like that , '' said one of the group", "context_len": 24}}}, "detectives": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` I ha ' had rewards put up , and the detectives are on the watch", "context_len": 16}}}, "advertisements": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Sheets of ballads , which Ephraim had bought as a boy , lay side by side with a tattered copy or two of Zadkiel 's prophetic almanac , some advertisements of patent medicines , a recipe for sheep-dip , and a Wesleyan hymn-book", "context_len": 43}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "They speak of their aristocratic neighbours , and are mostly advertisements themselves", "context_len": 12}}}, "rewards": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 56, "context": "He said that the Persians had , under the ancestors of Darius , crossed the Hellespont , invaded Greece , laid waste the country , and destroyed cities and towns , and had thus done them incalculable injury ; and that Darius himself had been plotting against his ( Alexander 's ) life , and offering rewards to any one who would kill him", "context_len": 64}}}, "duster": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "Then , if that was so , would not the very best thing Faith could do be to go to Him first herself , and tell Him that Roy was lost -- that he had gone quite , quite far away , and ask Him to help her to find him ? She placed the Bible on the table , got a duster , and , tenderly removing its dust , opened it", "context_len": 73}}}, "dearth": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "With the exception of a great loneliness , and a great dearth of the holy love of fatherhood , her life had been sheltered from all the rough winds which blow upon the class a little below her own", "context_len": 39}}}, "fatherhood": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "With the exception of a great loneliness , and a great dearth of the holy love of fatherhood , her life had been sheltered from all the rough winds which blow upon the class a little below her own", "context_len": 39}}}, "tho": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "And you , too , confess , when you weary have been , At the end of your journey , wherever you roam ; That , tho ' houses , and parks , and fine rivers you 've seen , There 's no place so happy , so sweet as your home", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` I 'd jutht like you to have thith one , 'cauthe you 're tho good and I love you , '' said Katy , and she put up her lips , for a kiss , to first one little cousin and then the other", "context_len": 45}}}, "pouted": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 42, "context": "She was in the red frock that she usually wore ; her wild hair curled in elf-locks all over her head ; her eyes , bright as stars , shone in the middle of her little elfin face ; her charming lips pouted just for a moment", "context_len": 47}}}, "pewter": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 77, "context": "He was took sudden , you understand -- in some sort of fit like ; and young master -- that 's Passon Perrient as they calls him , our young missis ' father -- and missis , they was away at Ipswich , and come back all of a scuffle and finds him dead ; and by all I hear , not the value of a penny-piece in the house in money -- plenty of silver and pewter you understand , but no money whatsumever", "context_len": 85}}}, "pawned": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Another woman would have pawned the little things , but Hannah did not care to make money by this child who had come in the place of her own", "context_len": 29}}}, "pawnbroker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "She put the dainty blue frock , the white pinafore , the little shoes and socks , into a box which was well hidden away under the bed ; then while Roy still slept she slipped out , and purchased at a pawnbroker 's for a shilling , a set of little garments such as her own child , were he alive , would wear", "context_len": 65}}}, "livelong": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "If only it were in the night she had to leave him , little Roy would sleep , and there would be no danger ; but he was a wakeful , lively child , and seldom closed his eyes for the livelong day", "context_len": 43}}}, "dirtier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "This woman too occupied a cellar , but it was a far worse one than Hannah 's , smaller , dirtier , and crowded with children , from ten years of age to a baby of six months", "context_len": 38}}}, "soothes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Here 's the stuff , it looks innercent , do n't it ? like a drop o ' water ; but fur all that , -- it 's wonderful how it soothes off a fretful baby", "context_len": 37}}}, "critter": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Thank yer , neighbour , '' replied Hannah , `` and now ef yer 'll be so good-natured as to give me the name o ' the bottle , why I 'll run to the chemist 's and get a little and run wid it to the poor worn-out critter this werry night", "context_len": 54}}}, "accost": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "As she walked quickly down the street which led directly from the court , she was met by two girls , one of whom she knew , and paused for a moment to accost", "context_len": 34}}}, "spec": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Oh , yes , '' answered Meg brightly ; `` I 'm on my h'own spec ' now , I and this yere gal ; we 're purwiding fur one another", "context_len": 32}}}, "pence": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` No , '' replied John ; `` it is true I carry all my wealth in my little wallet , and have only a few pence in my pocket ; but I have faith in God I shall yet succeed", "context_len": 41}}}, "lodgers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Now Hannah was rather fond of Meg , and would gladly have nearly paid the rent of her cellar by admitting these two little lodgers , but the presence of Roy of course made this impossible", "context_len": 36}}}, "loitering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` I ca n't no how , '' she said ; `` I ha ' a job on hand as 'ull take h'up all my spare room , and I ca n't ha ' no gals a loitering around", "context_len": 39}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 44, "context": "She and Lilian had been very anxious to get home early that afternoon , for they had meant to sow seeds in the garden ; and Father was always angry if they kept Bobby waiting , for he did not like him to be loitering about the inn-yard listening to the talk of the stable-boys", "context_len": 55}}}, "respectability": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 48, "context": "That youngest girl , had she seen her before ? Her face puzzled , nay more , it annoyed her ; she was an anxious , thin , dark-eyed child ; her dress was as ragged as Meg 's , but somehow she looked far above Meg in respectability", "context_len": 49}}}, "peart": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` But I need n't go fur to axe , '' she continued , `` fur though you ai n't much to boast on now , Meg , yet you look more peart than when she wor allers a wallopping of yer", "context_len": 42}}}, "gutter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Sunday came round again , finding Faith no longer in her neat and comfortable home , but a gutter child , dressed as badly , and in quite as great rags , as the worst-looking child around her", "context_len": 38}}}, "pawn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Meg was her companion and staunch friend , but it seemed no hardship in Meg 's eyes to counsel Faith to pawn her neat and good clothes , and to receive in exchange garments in which her father would scarcely recognise her", "context_len": 42}}}, "ebb": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 54, "context": "At this moment a rushing sound was heard , and a vessel with all sail set , and bearing the white foam before her bows with the rapidity of her motion , shot into the harbor , and was run high upon the soft sand of the beach , the tide being at half ebb", "context_len": 55}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Since the tide was high , and already beginning to ebb , there was no need to tie the catamaran", "context_len": 20}}}, "untaught": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "What right had He not to take her at her word , and allow her to be good in her own way ? It had never yet entered into her poor , untaught mind that in keeping little Roy she was keeping what was not her own", "context_len": 47}}}, "offshoot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Meg was a wild , untaught , uncared-for Arab child , a true offshoot of the lowest of the people", "context_len": 20}}}, "lowest": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 57, "context": "Since the memorable time when Captain Cook sailed into Botany Bay in 1769 and saw the naked native Australian poising erect to hurl his peculiar weapon , the boomerang has continued to excite the curiosity and amazement of the civilized world ; and truly the finding of such a scientific weapon in the hands of this so-called lowest order of mankind is an astonishing fact , to be simply accepted as another oddity of this odd , topsy-turvy corner of the world", "context_len": 82}}}, "ungoverned": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "She looked at her for a moment , and then said , `` Then thank God every day of your life , Bessie , that he has saved you from the misery of growing up with a self-willed , ungoverned temper", "context_len": 41}}}, "unlocking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "But now they saw Fanny Porter unlocking the door , and Maggie and Bessie ran eagerly forward to ask the use of the little house", "context_len": 25}}}, "fretfulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "His fretfulness ceased , his blue eyes closed , and he slept ; but though sleep was always supposed to be so very good for children , Hannah could never discover that little Roy awoke refreshed or the better for his sleep", "context_len": 42}}}, "obtuse": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "On Sunday , however , Hannah only gave him one small dose , for even to her obtuse mind the thought had occurred that it might not be doing him so much good as she had hoped", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` You always have a good appetite of your own , '' observed Mosely , not without sarcasm , which , however , Tom Hadley was too obtuse to comprehend", "context_len": 30}}}, "missis": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "But alas ! the doctor was not at home , he was out at church , and so was the missis ; he would be back in about an hour ; would the woman call again in an hour ? Hannah 's heart sank within her ; the night had turned very chilly , and little Roy , sleeping heavily in her arms , seemed to grow colder and colder ; dare she keep him in the winter streets for a whole hour ?", "context_len": 84}}}, "thundercloud": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Her violent anger , her passion of tears , had left behind them a kind of calm -- nay more , a very deep calm ; it was as though a thundercloud had rolled across a very blue sky , leaving it when past bluer and brighter than before", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Even in a crisis , people get more or less accustomed to the thundercloud overhead , and the feeling of insecurity below", "context_len": 22}}}, "bluer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Her violent anger , her passion of tears , had left behind them a kind of calm -- nay more , a very deep calm ; it was as though a thundercloud had rolled across a very blue sky , leaving it when past bluer and brighter than before", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The morning was brighter and the sea and the sky even bluer than they had been the day before", "context_len": 19}}}, "bended": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "`` My baby ! '' said nurse , who seemed to think the baby her own special property , -- '' my baby ! Do you think I 'd risk her neck in a gimcrack like that ? There is n't one of them I 'd trust a hand 's breadth with ye , not if ye was to go down on your bended knees", "context_len": 65}}}, "shivers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Ugh ! I shivers when I sees 'em ; I says to myself , that 's me when I 'm old", "context_len": 21}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 113, "context": "'s evening , when the shutters were closed and the settle drawn close to the fire , it seemed the cosiest spot in the world ; and Peggy and Bobby would often escape from the sober atmosphere of the dining-room to pull their little stools into the ingle nook , and listen to Nancy 's wonderful tales of ghosts and goblins , which seemed twice as thrilling when the wind was howling like a banshee in the chimney , and rattling the doors till they could fancy that spirit fingers were tapping on the panels , and only waiting a chance to catch them in the dark passages , and sent such cold shivers running down their backs that they grew almost too frightened to go to bed", "context_len": 128}}}, "thoroughfares": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 46, "context": "When they were finished , the Maugrabin paid him their price , even that which he sought , and taking the lamps , carried them to the khan , where he laid them in a basket and fell to going round about in the markets and thoroughfares of the city and crying out , `` Ho ! who will barter an old lamp for a new lamp ? '' When the folk heard him crying this , they laughed at him and said , `` Certes , this man is mad , since he goeth about , bartering new lamps for old", "context_len": 102}}}, "swerved": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "The cab swerved and creaked , and the horses ' hoofs beat an alarming tattoo on the cobblestones", "context_len": 18}}}, "bonnie": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "For long before the daisies came into blossom on her grave in the country cemetery to which Warden had her carried , the roses had come back to the bonnie cheeks of sweet baby Roy , and the health and brightness to his eyes", "context_len": 44}}}, "prayerfully": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "It is enough to say of his future life , that he acted as only a man could act who carried that belief to its logical conclusion , and who very humbly and very prayerfully followed in the steps of the Master whom he loved", "context_len": 45}}}, "steadier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "She still saw her baby boy , and as she grew steadier and more respectable day by day , and week by week , Warden gradually gave her more to do in his house , until finally she almost lived there", "context_len": 41}}}, "brighton": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "BRIGHTON : 129 , NORTH STREET", "context_len": 6}}}, "dismounting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Troopers were dismounting and leading their horses away to their stables , or were lounging at the doors of the houses where they were quartered , and a crowd of curious country folk and villagers gathered to stare at them , and even to put questions to the more affable-looking of the steel-coated soldiers", "context_len": 54}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Dismounting , he walked briskly up the slope until near the top", "context_len": 12}}}, "flagged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The press was greatest round the entrance of a house of the better class that stood back from the street with all the dignity that a flagged forecourt and a couple of high brick gate-pillars could lend it", "context_len": 38}}}, "forecourt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "The press was greatest round the entrance of a house of the better class that stood back from the street with all the dignity that a flagged forecourt and a couple of high brick gate-pillars could lend it", "context_len": 38}}}, "gentry": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` No , dame , no , he be a-going to his supper , folks say , and they wo n't let none into his parlour but gentry , save these here lobsters as go where they please , and hold themselves as good as gentlefolk , rot 'em ! ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "gentlefolk": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` No , dame , no , he be a-going to his supper , folks say , and they wo n't let none into his parlour but gentry , save these here lobsters as go where they please , and hold themselves as good as gentlefolk , rot 'em ! ''", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Job 's Chaldeans were gentlefolk in comparison with our Danes", "context_len": 10}}}, "rot": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` No , dame , no , he be a-going to his supper , folks say , and they wo n't let none into his parlour but gentry , save these here lobsters as go where they please , and hold themselves as good as gentlefolk , rot 'em ! ''", "context_len": 51}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Here and there they came upon the relics of strife , in the shape of bodies of men and horses left to rot , and in one spot , where a ford had been defended , the rival nations had left their fallen representatives by hundreds", "context_len": 46}}}, "squires": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "There were tombs in memory of them in a side transept of the church -- stalwart Crusaders , lying with legs crossed and meek hands folded in prayer ; stout Elizabethan squires and their dames , with ruffs round their necks , and rows of prim little kneeling children beneath them ; full-faced Jacobean worthies in curled wigs , with sculptured cherubs weeping over extinguished torches ; and there was a high old pew with a carved canopy over it , and an escutcheon bearing a coat of arms with a dragon on it , which , when Peggy was very little , she had always associated with the dragon in the Book of Revelation , and had an uneasy feeling that its eye was upon her all", "context_len": 128}}}, "recessed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "The group of officers and visitors who were gathered round the hearth , spoke to each other in low tones as they glanced with looks of curiosity , and even covert amusement , at two gentlemen who stood in the recessed window , in earnest talk", "context_len": 46}}}, "fitter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "A bright , eager boy , whose brown hands one would think fitter to hold a top than to caress the hilt of his new sword ; a boy young enough to be proud of his position , proud of his soldier 's dress ; to whom life was a very interesting but a very simple matter", "context_len": 57}}}, "peevishness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "But the dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of", "context_len": 128}}}, "hazel": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "His buff coat and crimson sash were like those worn by the boy , and the velvet cap he carried in his hand left uncovered curls as brown ; but instead of the childish calm of the boy 's hazel eyes , the older man 's glance now flashed with the fire of an eagle , now glowed with the exalted enthusiasm of a poet", "context_len": 65}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "A greater contrast to the melancholy scene could not be imagined than her young face glowing with life and health , the ruddy coils of chestnut hair , and the bright hazel eyes that roved far and wide over the empty landscape , as she caught the swinging gates , and began to tie them in place with a piece of cord", "context_len": 62}}}, "skewered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` By my faith , a rare bit of steel ! And how many king 's men hast thou skewered with it ? ''", "context_len": 24}}}, "foils": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "In the center was piled a collection of baseball bats , tennis racquets , boxing gloves , foils , catching gloves , a football , some running trousers , a couple of sweaters , and a nondescript collection of books", "context_len": 40}}}, "deadliest": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Or a cooanaradi , deadliest of all venomous snakes , might launch itself from the underbrush and inject its poison into his veins", "context_len": 23}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "That word of evil omen , the word that stood for the fiercest and deadliest snake in the Amazonian jungle ! What significance did it have in a conversation that presumably concerned itself with Bomba ?", "context_len": 36}}}, "paladin": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "But the face , overshadowed by the heavy curls of a fashionable periwig , was worn and roughened by exposure and hardship , and the weary gloom that darkened the noble forehead and drooped the haughty lips marked the years of disappointment that had changed the fiery paladin of 1642 into the sad and cynical Rupert of the Restoration", "context_len": 59}}}, "dost": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 30, "context": "Then the slave of the lamp came to him and taking him up , set him down in his house and said to him , `` O my lord , dost thou need aught ? '' `` Yes , '' answered Alaeddin ; `` I will have thee bring me eight-and-forty mamelukes , [ 454 ] four-and-twenty to walk before me and four-and-twenty to walk behind me , with their horses and clothes and arms , and let all that is upon them and their horses be of stuffs costly and precious exceedingly , such as are not found in kings ' treasuries", "context_len": 103}, "VERB": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 68, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Bertric of Aescendune , thou mayst save thy life on one condition ; dost thou wish to live ? ''", "context_len": 21}}}, "privy": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "When the damsel saw him , she opened him the privy door and he went in to the Lady Bedrulbudour [ 596 ] and said to her , `` Harkye , I will have thee dress and tire thyself and put away melancholy from thee ; and when the accursed Maugrabin cometh to thee , do thou receive him with 'Welcome and fair welcome ' and go to meet him with a smiling face and bid him come sup with thee and profess to him that thou hast forgotten thy beloved Alaeddin and thy father and that thou lovest him with an exceeding love", "context_len": 104}}}, "councillor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "[ Footnote 138 : Here Burton adds , `` Indeed I had well nigh determined to forfeit all my profit of the Ninth Statue and to bear thee away to Bassorah as my own bride , when my comrade and councillor dissuaded me from so doing , lest I should bring about my death", "context_len": 54}}}, "withershins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` If his Majesty took Major Harrison 's counsel , our cause were won ; but the stars will go withershins ere that come to pass", "context_len": 26}}}, "dolorous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Herbert , to see his Majesty in such pleasant spirits , for methinks his condition could scarce be more dolorous", "context_len": 20}}}, "parliament": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 82, "context": "But I make no doubt that the sight of these your loyal neighbours whom you have called in , and the very blessings of the poor folk in the street , are somewhat of a balm to his heart , also I can not deny that those gentlemen '' -- looking over at the officers -- '' have used us very civilly during the day 's ride ; methinks his Majesty finds himself more at ease with them than with those crop-eared parliament men and their preachers", "context_len": 87}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "And when he said further that what was done , was done in the name of the parliament , which was the only lawful authority , for that the generality of the people in England , Scotland , and Ireland had owned it by obeying it , and foreign States by sending embassies to it , they were cut to the heart and desired to silence him", "context_len": 67}}}, "herbert": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Herbert", "context_len": 1}}}, "privily": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 86, "context": "`` Quoth he , `` Speak ; what is the news ? '' And she said to him , `` The Sultan hath gone from his promise to thee in the matter of his daughter , the Lady Bedrulbudour , for that this very night the Vizier 's son goeth in to her ; and indeed methought at the time , [ 385 ] O my son , the Vizier would change the Sultan 's mind , even as I told thee that he bespoke him privily before me", "context_len": 89}}}, "assassin": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "The blood of Englishmen hath been poured out like water at the word of this man , it crieth out against him unto God ; the Cause needeth not the aid of any secret assassin ; he shall render his account in public unto the high court of Parliament", "context_len": 49}}}, "reckoning": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 21, "context": "The genie carried him into a treasury full of all manner of gold and silver and precious jewels past count or reckoning , price or estimation ; then he brought him into another place , where he saw all the requisites of the table , platters and spoons and ewers and basins and cups , of gold and silver , and thence to the kitchen , where he found cooks , [ 476 ] with their cooking-gear and utensils , all on like wise of gold and silver", "context_len": 88}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "each pair of double points in line with each other , or ( 2 ) reckoning a double point for each pair of like points ( single or double ) , corresponding one with another on the same line ' and a single point for each , unlike pair", "context_len": 49}}}, "hazarded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` But can they -- dare they -- touch the king ? He is not as other men , '' hazarded the boy", "context_len": 23}}}, "partaker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Thou hast set thine hand to the plough , boy , and art a partaker in our great work", "context_len": 19}}}, "glimmering": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "`` 'Is there any hope ? ' To which an answer peal 'd from that high land , But in a tongue no man could understand ; And on the glimmering limit far withdrawn God made Himself an awful rose of Dawn", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Daylight was just glimmering through the trees", "context_len": 7}}}, "dukes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Everything was new ; the better part of the people had never even seen a king , and now they had the daily sight of a live king , and a couple of royal dukes besides , walking about the streets and feeding ducks in the parks like ordinary human beings", "context_len": 51}}}, "covenants": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "To the winds with high-flown theories of government and religion , with ideals , and standards , and rules , and covenants ! Let us all be comfortable , and hang any one who might trouble our holiday !", "context_len": 39}}}, "agitators": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "This popular fear of agitators who might disturb the rule of the Merry Monarch chimed in very well with the feelings of the old cavaliers , who felt that heavy amends were due to them for the sorrows and hardships of the last twenty years , and no doom could be too awful for the murderers who had laid sacrilegious hands upon the sacred person of the king", "context_len": 68}}}, "sacrilegious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "This popular fear of agitators who might disturb the rule of the Merry Monarch chimed in very well with the feelings of the old cavaliers , who felt that heavy amends were due to them for the sorrows and hardships of the last twenty years , and no doom could be too awful for the murderers who had laid sacrilegious hands upon the sacred person of the king", "context_len": 68}}}, "regicide": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` How ! you must be beside yourself to come to me -- me ! -- Rupert ! on such an errand ! Because , forsooth , I exchanged civilities with one I held an honourable enemy , you dare to expect my interest on behalf of a regicide ! I vow , sir , every man who even witnessed that most abominable and unnatural murder should swing , did it depend on me", "context_len": 74}}}, "coronals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "Yet , full as London was of hopes and fears , of mad gaiety and black despair , the October day was as sweet and still as any day of any autumn ; the late roses blossomed as of old in the gardens of the Strand , and vine-leaves wreathed the citizens ' with their wonted coronals of ruby and gold", "context_len": 61}}}, "mercer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "An upper chamber above a mercer 's shop in Watling Street was decked with all the pride of city housewifery ; the pewter dishes on the sideboard shone like silver , and the marigolds and lavender in a great beaupot on the window-sill filled all the pleasant chamber with autumn fragrance", "context_len": 51}}}, "housewifery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "An upper chamber above a mercer 's shop in Watling Street was decked with all the pride of city housewifery ; the pewter dishes on the sideboard shone like silver , and the marigolds and lavender in a great beaupot on the window-sill filled all the pleasant chamber with autumn fragrance", "context_len": 51}}}, "sideboard": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "An upper chamber above a mercer 's shop in Watling Street was decked with all the pride of city housewifery ; the pewter dishes on the sideboard shone like silver , and the marigolds and lavender in a great beaupot on the window-sill filled all the pleasant chamber with autumn fragrance", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "On the sideboard facets of cut glass sparkled and gleamed with many diamond colors", "context_len": 14}}}, "marigolds": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 58, "context": "' Oh , yes , you darling birdie ! do you want to say you know all about it ? Bessie , let 's think the canary is a kind of keepsake from Dolly , 'cause you know it seems as if it came by her , and mamma says it is of no use to take the marigolds to town , for they will be quite dead in a few weeks", "context_len": 72}}}, "cobweb": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The room was that of wealthy people , and the rich silk gown and cobweb lawn of a lady who lay huddled up in the corner of a great settle were such as city matrons loved to wear", "context_len": 38}}}, "comely": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 31, "context": "Fanning had been away for over a fortnight , and I must say we greatly enjoyed her absence , and I in particular enjoyed it ; but when I saw her comely , good-humoured , beaming face now , it seemed to me that my heart went out to her", "context_len": 50}}}, "disordered": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 59, "context": "Father Cuthbert and Edmund , amongst others , turned round to see what caused the lull , and started from their seats as they beheld at the end of the room Alfgar , his face pale as one risen from the dead , his black locks hanging dishevelled around his neck , his garments torn , his whole person disordered", "context_len": 60}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The disordered beds were raked smoothly over ; the little fence carefully cleaned from the mud which covered it , and set up again ; and all the withered , bruised flowers and leaves carried away", "context_len": 36}}}, "battlefield": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 12, "context": "Death was easy enough to face among the high enthusiasms of the battlefield ; but here , in the city , where the busy world was eating and drinking and making money among these sordid surroundings , what radiance of a celestial city could flash from opened gates to support a victim through a torturing death ? Could faith win a victory even here ?", "context_len": 65}}}, "railed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 18, "context": "He was borne up in a very ecstasy as it seemed to me , and when the judges railed on him for his share in the death of the king , he told them his conscience was clear , for in what he did , there was more from God than men are aware of", "context_len": 55}}}, "generality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "And when he said further that what was done , was done in the name of the parliament , which was the only lawful authority , for that the generality of the people in England , Scotland , and Ireland had owned it by obeying it , and foreign States by sending embassies to it , they were cut to the heart and desired to silence him", "context_len": 67}}}, "embassies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 52, "context": "And when he said further that what was done , was done in the name of the parliament , which was the only lawful authority , for that the generality of the people in England , Scotland , and Ireland had owned it by obeying it , and foreign States by sending embassies to it , they were cut to the heart and desired to silence him", "context_len": 67}}}, "arraigned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "Dick 's voice failed suddenly ; what use to torture the unhappy wife of the regicide with the story of his trial and condemnation ? He could not convey to her the intrepid composure , the exulting pride with which Harrison justified the deed for which he was arraigned", "context_len": 49}}}, "powerlessness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 73, "context": "Harrison asked no question , she did not even answer his words ; for a moment she doubted if she had heard him ; but then she spoke : spoke with a calmness that startled him till he realized that she dreamt even yet that her husband might escape , and was too completely absorbed in devising schemes for his deliverance to have time to realize her own misery or measure her own powerlessness", "context_len": 74}}}, "wickedly": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "Consider ! I am very sure there are some we can move to help us ! Count over your friends ; there must be some one with a heart of flesh left in all England ! General Monck loved you well once , though he wrote so wickedly counselling Oliver Cromwell to be very severe unto my beloved one when they threw him into prison at Portland", "context_len": 67}}}, "blasphemer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "Move but General Monck to have him cast once again into prison , and I will pray for him till my dying day ! They say that blasphemer , Harry Marten , will but be imprisoned ; why should my saint have a harder fate ? Oh , let him but live , and though I never set eyes on him more , I shall be a happy woman ! ''", "context_len": 71}}}, "whomsoever": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Whomsoever they let escape , they will never loose their hold on him", "context_len": 13}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Now I noticed a curiously subdued but distinct commotion among the spectators of the front rank , each of whom seemed anxious to surrender his apparently advantageous position to whomsoever might be willing to accept it", "context_len": 36}}}, "distraught": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "I can send in a private message from you to your friend , but Lynn is so distraught with loyalty that it might fare ill with Mr", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 100, "context": "When she raised the veil from her face and Alaeddin saw her , he said , `` Verily , her fashion glorifieth the Great Creator and extolled be the perfection of Him who made her and graced her with this beauty and goodliness ! '' And his back was cloven in sunder , [ 321 ] when he saw her ; his thought was confounded and his understanding [ 322 ] dazed and the love of her gat hold upon his whole heart ; so he turned back and returning home , went in to his mother , like one distraught", "context_len": 101}}}, "hearken": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 71, "context": "Do you mind , Dick , '' she went on , in a calmer tone , as her memory wandered back to happier days , `` do you mind how I foresaw these evil times were at hand , and how I entreated him to flee ? Do you mind , last spring , when that letter came from New England from excellent Master Perrient , how I prayed him to hearken to it ? ''", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Yes , father , hearken , '' said Edward", "context_len": 10}}}, "haled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 94, "context": "When you went to Scotland first , you and he were of one mind , one heart , but when you came hither again , your head was stuffed full of worldly wisdom and time-serving devices , talking of a Lord Protector instead of the glory of God , and hand and glove with that cruel Cromwell who did throw my saint into prison ! Your heart was turned from those that reared you , and given to their enemies ! And now you can stand by unmoved and see him you once loved haled to prison and death ! ''", "context_len": 101}}}, "rogers": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 0, "context": "Rogers had occasional flashes of what can only be termed inspired common sense ; and if he were judiciously approached , it was even possible that such an incalculable person might use his influence to restrain the old soldiers of his congregation from rushing on immediate destruction", "context_len": 47}}}, "abstractedly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Yes , yes , '' she murmured abstractedly ; `` who could fail to love him ? so valiant and so goodly to look upon , so tender unto his friends , and to me his poor wife , and ever was the inward joy in his bosom breaking forth in praises to God -- and yet '' -- turning wildly on Dick -- '' yet you will let him die ! You are as hard as the nether millstone ! Dick , do not shake your head , you must go ! You must force Prince Rupert to hear you", "context_len": 102}}}, "importunity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "It may be we should read bi [ hatheti 'll ] terikeh , `` by [ this ] means ; '' but the rendering in the text seems the more probable one , the Sultan meaning that he would thus get rid of Alaeddin 's importunity by practice , without open breach of faith or violence", "context_len": 56}}}, "tradesfolk": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The sturdy independence he inherited from an ancestry of English tradesfolk and yeomen made him cherish the ideal of an English republic with religious fervour , while , whether leading a prayer meeting or heading a cavalry charge , his inspiring enthusiasm carried away all who were near him", "context_len": 49}}}, "yeomen": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "The male population was divided into a class of head-men or chiefs , recruited by popular election , the selection being made among the most valorous men and the best orators in their councils ; and in two classes of `` yeomen '' : the `` warriors '' and the `` fighting men , '' these being inferior to the warriors", "context_len": 61}}}, "theocracy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "But when he returned home on flying visits , he found to his dismay that his uncle 's visionary hopes were growing instead of fading ; and from desiring a merely republican England , General Harrison had begun to dream of a theocracy as complete as that of the early Jews , and to look forward to the immediate inauguration of an earthly Reign of the Saints , under the sceptre of Christ Himself , as the Fifth and last of the great monarchies of the world", "context_len": 87}}}, "inauguration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "But when he returned home on flying visits , he found to his dismay that his uncle 's visionary hopes were growing instead of fading ; and from desiring a merely republican England , General Harrison had begun to dream of a theocracy as complete as that of the early Jews , and to look forward to the immediate inauguration of an earthly Reign of the Saints , under the sceptre of Christ Himself , as the Fifth and last of the great monarchies of the world", "context_len": 87}}}, "monarchies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 83, "context": "But when he returned home on flying visits , he found to his dismay that his uncle 's visionary hopes were growing instead of fading ; and from desiring a merely republican England , General Harrison had begun to dream of a theocracy as complete as that of the early Jews , and to look forward to the immediate inauguration of an earthly Reign of the Saints , under the sceptre of Christ Himself , as the Fifth and last of the great monarchies of the world", "context_len": 87}}}, "consolations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "He had hoped that the consolations of her minister , or the calm of despair , might have brought to her some amount of resignation ; but now this wild trust in the power of Prince Rupert had suddenly inspired the poor lady with a crazy vehemence", "context_len": 47}}}, "enthusiasms": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 114, "context": "Happily both her father and Aunt Helen shared his views , and , knowing Peggy 's generous , affectionate nature , were able to lead her more by love than severity ( for with human hearts it is often like the fable of the sun and the wind : they will respond to a kindly touch , while harshness will only make them sullen and obstinate ) , and they further held the opinion that it is better for a child to have many interests and much energy , even though these qualities prove a little troublesome , than to grow up clipped to the prim pattern of those who may have outlived their enthusiasms", "context_len": 115}}}, "conflicting": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` No , '' said Rosamund , who was absolutely torn in the midst of many conflicting emotions : her anxiety for her friend , her knowledge of what had happened the night before , her ever-increasing dislike to Lucy -- and , in fact , the whole false position in which she found herself -- all distressed her beyond measure", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 43, "context": "In order to determine de Soto 's route in these parts , we have to decide first , whether the days and directions of the compass noted by his chroniclers deserve more credence than the local names transmitted in cases when both form conflicting statements", "context_len": 45}}}, "courtiers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "Then , instead of attempting to restrain him , and worrying and checking him by useless efforts to hold him in , he gave him the rein freely , and animated and encouraged him with his voice , so that the horse flew across the plains at the top of his speed , the king and the courtiers looking on , at first with fear and trembling , but soon afterward with feelings of the greatest admiration and pleasure", "context_len": 79}}}, "suitors": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "He had imagined that a royal ante-chamber must be thronged with courtiers and suitors , and his shy pride was relieved to find the way was at least not barred by gilded grooms-in-waiting , or fashionable loungers", "context_len": 37}}}, "loungers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "He had imagined that a royal ante-chamber must be thronged with courtiers and suitors , and his shy pride was relieved to find the way was at least not barred by gilded grooms-in-waiting , or fashionable loungers", "context_len": 37}}}, "heralds": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Zounds ! Captain Harrison , '' cried the gentleman , merrily , `` are you the first swallow that heralds a summer ? I swear you are the first visitor that has crossed the threshold since we landed yesterday , and I thought you were anchored in Edinburgh", "context_len": 49}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "All rose to look at them , not without anxiety ; as yet they were far distant , across a wild moor , but as they drew nearer , and their standards could be more clearly discerned , it became gradually evident that it was a reinforcement ; and so it proved , for heralds , galloping forward , announced the men of Dorsetshire", "context_len": 64}}}, "roundhead": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` Say you so ? I ' faith , I was near forgetting that your party is down in the world , you have so little the cut of a square-toed roundhead ! I am heartily sorry you are in trouble", "context_len": 41}}}, "kinsman": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` True , true ; and 'mon cousin ' is a very pretty fellow , and a right loving kinsman to boot when he does not forget ! But to-day he is away a-hunting , and the Duke of York is making sheep 's eyes at the fox 's clever daughter Nan , so here we sit solitary", "context_len": 58}}}, "jars": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 37, "context": "A beautiful portrait of the Queen of Bohemia , that could come from no meaner brush than that of Vandyke , hung on the wall , while beneath it a table was heaped with dusty bottles and jars , retorts , and powder-flasks", "context_len": 43}}}, "retorts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "A beautiful portrait of the Queen of Bohemia , that could come from no meaner brush than that of Vandyke , hung on the wall , while beneath it a table was heaped with dusty bottles and jars , retorts , and powder-flasks", "context_len": 43}}}, "chiseled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "At a table in the midst of this picturesque confusion , sat a man of middle age , whose thoughtful eyes and finely chiseled features still showed the beauty inherited from his mother , the luckless Queen of Hearts", "context_len": 39}}}, "overshadowed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": "For the space of nearly a minute there now prevailed an intense silence while King Banda sat glowering at our party , and especially at me , in a manner that caused cold chills to run down my back , as I reflected that this was the man who was responsible for the gruesome fruit borne by the tree , the branches of which overshadowed us , and that if he should by any chance take the fancy into his head to further decorate that tree by nailing a white man to it , there was nobody but myself within some hundreds of miles who would dream of saying him nay ; and I somehow had a conviction that my disapproval of such a course would not very", "context_len": 128}}}, "periwig": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "But the face , overshadowed by the heavy curls of a fashionable periwig , was worn and roughened by exposure and hardship , and the weary gloom that darkened the noble forehead and drooped the haughty lips marked the years of disappointment that had changed the fiery paladin of 1642 into the sad and cynical Rupert of the Restoration", "context_len": 59}}}, "imperious": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 49, "context": "The sound of a voice calling impatiently to Hal to hurry up with that ball , and not to be all night about it , was what first drew their attention to his whereabouts ; and feeling rather astonished that any one should venture to address him in that imperious way , they crept up to the edge of the wood , and became silent spectators of what was going on", "context_len": 71}}}, "flicked": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "But he had forgotten the impatient movement with which he had flicked his pen to the other side of the room , and as he paused to search for it Dick caught the opportunity , and stepped over to the table", "context_len": 41}}}, "civilities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` How ! you must be beside yourself to come to me -- me ! -- Rupert ! on such an errand ! Because , forsooth , I exchanged civilities with one I held an honourable enemy , you dare to expect my interest on behalf of a regicide ! I vow , sir , every man who even witnessed that most abominable and unnatural murder should swing , did it depend on me", "context_len": 74}}}, "juggle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Go to those of your own party , who have had the wit to secure their own necks ; maybe they may also have the skill to juggle your kinsman out of jail", "context_len": 33}}}, "duello": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "`` Now , sir , I warrant you know by experience that a broadsword is a good enough thing to have in your fist on the field of battle ; but , the war over , 't is neither fit for a lady 's chamber nor for a courtier 's duello ; 't is but a commodity of rusty iron to fling in the lumber-room", "context_len": 65}}}, "commodity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "Each girl will please pack a small bag , if she possesses such a useful commodity , and we must walk as fast as ever we can to the station , for my poor dear husband has no end of things for me to attend to to-day , and the moment we get to Dartford we shall have to bustle about , I can tell you", "context_len": 66}}}, "follower": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Good faith , '' he cried ; `` 't is worth an hour lost to learn that Tumble-down Dick had one follower , and , I warrant , a faithful one ! Aller Teufel ! thou art as good a lad as I have seen in this most virtuous and loyal city", "context_len": 53}}}, "carrion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Harkye , sir , I am not one of those that love to deck out a city with carrion", "context_len": 20}}}, "gibbet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "This healing and blessed Parliament , I hear , holds a gibbet a prettier sight than a stricken field ; that is not my mind , and if I can move any of these valiant pantaloons to let General Harrison 's body be delivered to his friends , I will do it", "context_len": 52}}}, "godfather": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Forswear Square Toes ; repent thee of Republicanism , and I 'll stand godfather to thy new life ! So go and get thee wisdom ! '' And the young fellow turned , laughing , back to his work , while Richard sadly retraced his steps to Mrs", "context_len": 48}}}, "squalid": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "buckles": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "joviality": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 79, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "gazer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 108, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "trample": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 110, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "butchery": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 122, "context": "But the sight inspired little horror or pity in the throng : orange girls called their wares , squalid beggars beset beplumed gentlemen , burly ruffians shouldered back prim citizens in their broadcloth and silver buckles ; the press , the smell , the noise of shouts and oaths and scraps of songs were much the same as had hailed the Second Charles 's entry into London six months before ; but the faces were changed , their coarse joviality was gone , and they were inflamed with the frenzy of the bull-fighter , the loathsome curiosity that will not miss one horrid detail , even if the gazer must trample down his own mother to get a better view of the butchery", "context_len": 123}}}, "execration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The shouts swelled into a deep roar of execration , as the sledge on which the prisoner lay bound neared the place of execution , and Richard Harrison , struggling to keep his place as close to the victim as he might , thought with grim bitterness of the day when this same mob , silent and cowed , had seen General Harrison ride back from the scaffold at Whitehall", "context_len": 70}}}, "dastards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` The dastards dared not lift a finger then , though it was for their liberty we struck the blow", "context_len": 20}}}, "deliverers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Ben had now opportunity to see who his deliverers were , and to thank them , which he did in no measured terms", "context_len": 23}}}, "asserting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "He gave Adams a letter certifying to his having a claim of over three thousand dollars against the property , which he told Adams to show to his rich brother when he died , asserting that , although Colonel Harrington had shamefully neglected him , he would never dishonorably repudiate a claim of that kind", "context_len": 55}}}, "wrongfully": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "He had been exasperated to a great degree by Flagg 's endeavor to drug him on the night of the expedition to Rice 's , and that with good reason ; and now his suspicions , nay , more than suspicions aroused that he was trying to make it appear that he , Seabrooke , had wrongfully kept Percy 's money and then pretended that the latter had taken it from him by stealth , enraged him beyond bounds", "context_len": 79}}}, "hysterically": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "The butchery of the sentence for treason was carried out to the bitter end , yet of the onlookers there were but a few women who sobbed hysterically or fainted , and but one or two men who pushed their way back , sick with the sight and smell of the shambles", "context_len": 52}}}, "rabble": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "His bold curious eyes let nothing pass unnoticed , yet when the torture of the half-dead victim was ended even his lips were somewhat white , though he shouted and waved his hat with the loyal rabble who cheered and cheered again at the headsman 's final speech : `` So perish all King Charles 's enemies", "context_len": 57}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "At Pensford the rabble made a bull savage , and then tried to make it attack his congregation ; at Whitechapel they drove cows among the listeners and threw stones , one of which hit Wesley between the eyes ; but after he had wiped away the blood he went on with his address , telling the people that `` God hath not given us the spirit of fear ''", "context_len": 70}}}, "sprig": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "The other was a heliotrope which had been given to Bessie by her papa , and which also had been little more than a sprig when she first had it ; but it was now a flourishing little plant , not running riotous in the way Maggie 's geranium did , but fresh and fair , with a cluster or two of delicate , perfumed blossoms , and the promise of more", "context_len": 72}}}, "pleasuring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Lord , how time flies , and how one forgets business when there is any pleasuring toward ; my lord will be in a fine fume ; '' and Mr", "context_len": 30}}}, "iniquity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The artillery of the Lord is called out , the iniquity of this Babylon is full , the saints are even now assembled , and expect the call to arms", "context_len": 30}}}, "widowhood": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Truly your good aunt doth forget her widowhood in the expectation of the day that is presently to break", "context_len": 19}}}, "councils": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "The male population was divided into a class of head-men or chiefs , recruited by popular election , the selection being made among the most valorous men and the best orators in their councils ; and in two classes of `` yeomen '' : the `` warriors '' and the `` fighting men , '' these being inferior to the warriors", "context_len": 61}}}, "coached": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 66, "context": "`` For the sake of him who hath even now rendered up himself as a martyr for the Lord 's cause , I may not be angry with any word of yours , '' he answered sadly ; `` but I do entreat of you to take heed ! Would you lay down your arms and live in peace among your cattle and your corn , coached and complimented into effeminacy and foolishness ? Oh , for shame ! Rub your eyes and look about you ! What was the fate of the men of Sodom when they thought Lot was one that mocked when he warned them ! ''", "context_len": 110}}}, "complimented": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 68, "context": "`` For the sake of him who hath even now rendered up himself as a martyr for the Lord 's cause , I may not be angry with any word of yours , '' he answered sadly ; `` but I do entreat of you to take heed ! Would you lay down your arms and live in peace among your cattle and your corn , coached and complimented into effeminacy and foolishness ? Oh , for shame ! Rub your eyes and look about you ! What was the fate of the men of Sodom when they thought Lot was one that mocked when he warned them ! ''", "context_len": 110}}}, "effeminacy": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 70, "context": "`` For the sake of him who hath even now rendered up himself as a martyr for the Lord 's cause , I may not be angry with any word of yours , '' he answered sadly ; `` but I do entreat of you to take heed ! Would you lay down your arms and live in peace among your cattle and your corn , coached and complimented into effeminacy and foolishness ? Oh , for shame ! Rub your eyes and look about you ! What was the fate of the men of Sodom when they thought Lot was one that mocked when he warned them ! ''", "context_len": 110}}}, "endangering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "I think not the Kingdom of God is to be brought to us by the sword ; nor will I be a party to endangering any shred of liberty yet left to the people of England by breaking the peace whether by word or deed", "context_len": 45}}}, "shred": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "I think not the Kingdom of God is to be brought to us by the sword ; nor will I be a party to endangering any shred of liberty yet left to the people of England by breaking the peace whether by word or deed", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "Here , Your Honor , you may see the torn place in this lad \u2019 s coat-sleeve , and you may also perceive beyond question that the shred of fabric discovered by Piper clinging to the nail in Bernard Hayden \u2019 s locker corresponds with the material of this garment", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "thighs": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "The broad-brimmed hat flaring up in front , the fluttering handkerchief about the neck , the fringed collar , the close fitting coat , cartridge belt at the waist , gloved hands , revolver at the hip , rifle across his thighs , fringed trouser legs , tucked just below the knees into the boots , spurs , keen , alert eyes , body leaning slightly forward but as firm in the saddle , as if the rider were a part of the pony ; these with mail pouches locked and secured in place , were the noticeable points of the man who dashed up at headlong speed his horse setting his legs and coming to a stop within a single bound", "context_len": 122}}}, "abides": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Harrison doth lay aside her sorrow , and abides with the saints in Colman Street , to add her praises and prayers unto theirs", "context_len": 24}}}, "fruition": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Ay ; she even now expects till the fruition of our hopes be granted , and General Harrison doth arise from death to lead us on to victory", "context_len": 29}}}, "err": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "Brett wo n't allow the girls even to come over from Dartford , which father says is a pity ; but still , one must err on the safe side", "context_len": 30}}}, "uncongenial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "He strode along behind the flying form of the little minister , inwardly furious at the saintly and exasperating person who forced him to seek out the company that was precisely the most painful and uncongenial to him , when his one sole idea was to hide himself in solitude like some wounded animal and there wrestle down the grief and horror that possessed him", "context_len": 65}}}, "wrestle": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 99, "context": "Peggy , hard at work behind the scenes , put her eye to a hole in the curtain , and surveyed the prospect below , where children big and little were engaged in jumping over the forms , chasing each other round the stove , and generally acting more like monkeys at the Zoo than civilized human beings , while the Rector strove in vain to collect them at the empty end of the room to play games , and the curate , a shy young man fresh from Oxford , looked as if he would have preferred to wrestle with a consignment of heathens from the Cannibal Islands", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` After these arrangements , some other Indians came from the west , met them , and had a great wrestle with the three towns ; they made ballsticks and played with them , with bows and arrows , and with the \u00e1tassa , the war club", "context_len": 47}}}, "numbed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "Yet the grief and horror was still only in the background of his mind , his brain felt numbed , though an instinctive dread warned him that they lurked there ready at the first opportunity to spring out on him with overwhelming force", "context_len": 43}}}, "blasphemous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "He knew that the extraordinary person in whose company he walked was completely deaf to the usual reasons that govern men 's conduct ; but , mixed up with his insane and even blasphemous beliefs , Mr", "context_len": 37}}}, "judiciously": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 57, "context": "Haven , an ex-editor , had written up a good `` puff '' for a local paper , inserted gratis an exciting comment and anticipation in reference to the impending sale , and Darry and Bob had printed fifteen hundred dodgers on their home press , very neat and presentable in appearance , and these had been judiciously distributed for miles around , and posted up in stores and depots", "context_len": 70}}}, "ruder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Rogers was a gentleman by birth and a scholar by training , and was therefore accessible to arguments that did not affect the ruder members of his sect", "context_len": 28}}}, "sect": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "On a rickety platform at the end of the of the room a preacher in a Geneva gown was holding forth in the most violent language of the sect , and all around the grim listeners hung on his words with immovable attention , leaning on their pikes or holding their drawn swords across their knees", "context_len": 56}}}, "hackney": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The minister readily assented , and they turned into Watling Street , where , as Dick had foretold , a hackney coach stood ready packed before the mercer 's shop that had belonged to Mrs", "context_len": 35}}}, "foretold": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 23, "context": "When the holy bishop departed this life , I was one of the few who stood round his bed , and as he foretold of the evil to come , he bade us all bear our portion manfully , for the time , he said , would be short in which to endure , and the eternal crown secure", "context_len": 59}}}, "cob": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Harrison 's father , and a groom was leading a stout cob up and down beside it", "context_len": 17}}}, "watcher": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "Alden \u2019 s belief was that the monstrous animal would keep on with his ponderous gait in the grotesque attempt to overtake the fleet footed horse which was safe beyond his reach ; but to the consternation of the watcher the brute halted at the very spot where the fugitive had landed when he dropped from the saddle", "context_len": 58}}}, "forsworn": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "By this time , the night was come ; so he looked at his palace and his cares and sorrows were dispelled from him and he trusted in God , after he had forsworn hope , that he should see his bride once again", "context_len": 44}}}, "protectorate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Tut , tut , Harrison ; what though we have forsworn our protectorate sins and got a batch of new ones to suit the new times , we are not all born to be play-book heroes like you", "context_len": 39}}}, "revenged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "`` And , taking Dick by the arm , the officer continued earnestly , in a low tone , `` You remember that fellow , Patrick Keith , with whom you quarrelled in Edinburgh ; he is here in London in my Lord Lauderdale 's household , and he swears he will be revenged on you", "context_len": 56}}}, "forge": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Now , you know the fellow is quite able to forge or trump up evidence enough to be mighty unpleasant , so Lord Monck prays you give no colour to anything he may say , by frequenting the company of any suspicious or fanatical people", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Past the forge , whence the cheery chink , chink of the blacksmith 's hammer came mingled with the refrain of a stirring melody from a good bass voice ; under the spreading yew-trees of the churchyard , and out through the lych-gate to the old mill , where the great wheel was turning slowly round , its dripping blades gleaming bright in the sunshine ; up the steep path through the little hazel-wood , scrambling over the ladder-like stile into the narrow lane that ran ever uphill towards the mountains , which loomed before them , rugged in outline , and shaded in a mist of purple blue", "context_len": 109}}}, "frequenting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Now , you know the fellow is quite able to forge or trump up evidence enough to be mighty unpleasant , so Lord Monck prays you give no colour to anything he may say , by frequenting the company of any suspicious or fanatical people", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Around the chapel was a broad walk , shaded with stately oaks , and the path was the frequenting place of the college lads", "context_len": 24}}}, "caned": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` I caned Keith in the High Street of Edinburgh for that he kicked a woman who by chance stood in his way , '' answered Dick , hotly ; `` and if I meet him in Fleet Street , I will cane him once more there", "context_len": 47}}}, "furtherance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` That will doubtless be much to the advantage of Keith 's manners , '' laughed the other , `` but scarce to the furtherance of your safety ! Now , I ride to Harrow to-night -- why will you not bear me company and lie at my house , and so travel into the country for a while", "context_len": 59}}}, "kinswoman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "`` Shame , shame , brethren ; let no man dare to call the kinsman of a martyr a spy ! This is Richard Harrison , and it is but decent he have leave to come and go and speak with his kinswoman in liberty", "context_len": 45}}}, "godly": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 40, "context": "She assured me many times that she quite understood ; that she had gone through precisely the same phases with regard to Albert Fanning the first but that it had all come right , and that her passion for the godly man had been very strong by-and-by", "context_len": 47}}}, "fanatics": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "The shouting was so violent that the congregation effectually deafened themselves to the knocking that began to make itself heard at the door of the room , and it was not till the knocking changed to the clang of crowbars , and the door gave way before the assailants , that the excited fanatics realized that their enemies were upon them", "context_len": 61}}}, "whoop": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 98, "context": "`` Beware , woman ! '' shrieked Maggie , and Bessie , thinking it time for her to come to her sister 's aid , joined in the cry , `` Beware , woman ! '' while Franky , always ready to take part in any disturbance , struck at Aunt Patty with his whip , and shouted , `` 'Ware , woman ! '' and Hafed , knowing nothing but that this old lady had alarmed and distressed his young charge , and that it was his duty to protect them , raised his voice in a whoop of defiance , and snatching up the hearth-brush , brandished it in a threatening manner as he danced wildly about her", "context_len": 120}}}, "ribald": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "The shouts and excitement in the meeting-house had warned the passers-by that something was in the wind , and a good many loiterers were hanging about the doors , who welcomed them with cries of `` Whoop , Roundhead ! whoop , crop ears ! '' and ribald parodies on the war-like psalms , whose sound could be clearly heard through the open windows of the room they had just left", "context_len": 71}}}, "parodies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "The shouts and excitement in the meeting-house had warned the passers-by that something was in the wind , and a good many loiterers were hanging about the doors , who welcomed them with cries of `` Whoop , Roundhead ! whoop , crop ears ! '' and ribald parodies on the war-like psalms , whose sound could be clearly heard through the open windows of the room they had just left", "context_len": 71}}}, "psalms": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 52, "context": "The shouts and excitement in the meeting-house had warned the passers-by that something was in the wind , and a good many loiterers were hanging about the doors , who welcomed them with cries of `` Whoop , Roundhead ! whoop , crop ears ! '' and ribald parodies on the war-like psalms , whose sound could be clearly heard through the open windows of the room they had just left", "context_len": 71}}}, "crowbars": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "The shouting was so violent that the congregation effectually deafened themselves to the knocking that began to make itself heard at the door of the room , and it was not till the knocking changed to the clang of crowbars , and the door gave way before the assailants , that the excited fanatics realized that their enemies were upon them", "context_len": 61}}}, "doorways": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 21, "context": "People knew the time of their passing , and looked out for them daily ; kindly women would come to cottage doorways to nod and smile ; the inn-keeper at the Halfway Arms set his clock by their arrival ; they had struck up quite a friendship with the postman , two milkmen , and the driver of a fish-cart , while the old man who broke stones by the roadside always nodded and gave them 'Good-morning , ' and fired a facetious remark or two after them , which the children imagined he must relieve the monotony of his work by composing during the intervals of their coming and going", "context_len": 111}}}, "sectaries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Feake , Powell , John Rogers , Courteney , Day , and Richard Harrison were the names that rang out above the shouts of the sectaries , who , crying out that the day of the Lord was come , charged outwards with such impetuosity that the soldiers were for a moment forced backwards", "context_len": 54}}}, "corroborate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Tom Hadley , who had not the prolific imagination of his comrade , listened in open-mouthed wonder to the fanciful tale , but did not offer to corroborate it in his usual manner", "context_len": 33}}}, "formulate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Rogers was too breathless to formulate , Dick hurried him down the dark road toward the corner where he knew his horse was waiting", "context_len": 24}}}, "potters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Harrison 's county home , but lurking among the potters ' huts on the Staffordshire moors , and only venturing to visit his friends under cover of night", "context_len": 28}}}, "circulated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "Even in the first year she printed 500 copies a month of her letters ( `` little bluebacks '' the sailors called them , on account of the colour of their cover ) ; but before many years had passed as many as 21,000 a month were printed and circulated", "context_len": 50}}}, "idealism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "While their opinions diverged completely , their feelings were in harmony , or rather the glowing faith and single-hearted idealism of the elder man had illuminated the being of the younger", "context_len": 31}}}, "unworldly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Richard 's youthful wisdom had often grown impatient of his uncle 's wild fancies , or smiled with affectionate mockery on his Utopian dreams ; but unconsciously the young man had always measured his own thoughts and actions by the unworldly standard of General Harrison 's ideal", "context_len": 47}}}, "yawned": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "Where Richard had seen that noble figure treading the path before him , a sudden gulf yawned , the leader had vanished , the path was lost , and the blank fog was around him", "context_len": 35}}}, "administrating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Whether fighting or administrating or farming , his simple and practical nature had found its natural outlet in work", "context_len": 19}}}, "speculations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Speculations on religion or forms of government had little attraction for him ; there was always some work to be done , and that he found more congenial than meditation", "context_len": 30}}}, "plantations": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "His relations painted to him in glowing colours the future that still opened to him in the New England plantations where their friend Parson Perrient was sure to offer him a warm welcome , and to satisfy their wishes , he made his way eastward , hoping to find a ship bound for Holland at King 's Lynn , and so to take passage for the New World from Rotterdam", "context_len": 70}}}, "creak": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "If there were more girls like you in the world I should n't be surprised if there were a lot of good men too ; and the world could be oiled on all its hinges , so to speak , so that it would n't creak and jump and fret one at every turn as it seems to have an unpleasant habit of doing at the present moment", "context_len": 68}}}, "coppers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Hullo , good mother , food and drink as quick as may be , most especially drink , and none of your small beer for me , '' he shouted , jingling a few coppers in his hand with the air of an alderman ordering turtle and venison", "context_len": 49}}}, "alderman": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "`` Hullo , good mother , food and drink as quick as may be , most especially drink , and none of your small beer for me , '' he shouted , jingling a few coppers in his hand with the air of an alderman ordering turtle and venison", "context_len": 49}}}, "venison": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` Hullo , good mother , food and drink as quick as may be , most especially drink , and none of your small beer for me , '' he shouted , jingling a few coppers in his hand with the air of an alderman ordering turtle and venison", "context_len": 49}}}, "tankard": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "Why , I could talk all night if I was to start in telling you of all the brave sport we had ! One time , I mind , we landed , there was a town , Santa Ysabel they called it , as it might be here '' -- arranging a tankard at the corner of the table -- '' with a good high road leading up to it from the sea , as it might be my tobacco pipe '' -- laying it down with care ; `` and if you 'll believe me , sir , we took and run races , as it might be along my tobacco pipe , and as soon as them Spaniards was 'ware of our coming , they took", "context_len": 128}}}, "potations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Richard answered civilly , and pulling his hat over his eyes leaned back as one disposed to sleep ; but the new-comer seemed to have no fancy for solitary potations", "context_len": 30}}}, "hallooed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Take a pull at my ale , friend , '' he hallooed , pushing the steaming mixture under Dick 's nose", "context_len": 22}}}, "stingo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "`` It 's rare stingo , 'schrecklich gut ' as the Dutchmen say , though it be a slut that brewed it", "context_len": 22}}}, "gut": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` It 's rare stingo , 'schrecklich gut ' as the Dutchmen say , though it be a slut that brewed it", "context_len": 22}}}, "slut": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` It 's rare stingo , 'schrecklich gut ' as the Dutchmen say , though it be a slut that brewed it", "context_len": 22}}}, "brewed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` It 's rare stingo , 'schrecklich gut ' as the Dutchmen say , though it be a slut that brewed it", "context_len": 22}}}, "gizzards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Folks in this country want something to warm their gizzards ! ''", "context_len": 12}}}, "maudlin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Ay , dear , dear , '' meditated Astbury , in a maudlin tone of regret", "context_len": 17}}}, "intentionally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Whether intentionally or not , the man had slipped back into his Staffordshire accent and dropped the strongest of his oaths , and Dick could not prevent a feeling of bitter amusement at seeing that this drunken ne'er-do-well , whom his uncle had persuaded to enlist in the hopes of drilling him into a decent life , had yielded to the influence of General Harrison 's character just as he himself had done", "context_len": 73}}}, "maundered": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Which of them had been the wiser -- which was the better off ? The fellow maundered on , taking a drink at his replenished tankard now and then", "context_len": 29}}}, "ballads": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 2, "context": "Sheets of ballads , which Ephraim had bought as a boy , lay side by side with a tattered copy or two of Zadkiel 's prophetic almanac , some advertisements of patent medicines , a recipe for sheep-dip , and a Wesleyan hymn-book", "context_len": 43}}}, "vagabonds": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "Richard hesitated to ask in what fashion Astbury had made his voyage , seeing that the usual way to dispose of thieves and vagabonds was to ship them off to the American plantations ; but Astbury loved the sound of his own voice , and stretching out his legs towards the fire , took up his tale in the fashion of the professional story-teller", "context_len": 64}}}, "expletives": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "His history ran somewhat as follows , though it sounds bald enough without the expletives with which he garnished it , growing somewhat less shy of his Major 's nephew as he went on", "context_len": 34}}}, "bellyful": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "`` I went across seas first time along o ' Lord General Cromwell to Ireland , and he gave us our bellyful of fighting , and no mistake ; but it ai n't fighting that I complain of , having been always held a valiant man of my inches ; '' and he puffed out his broad chest and looked a very crusader", "context_len": 63}}}, "crusader": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` I went across seas first time along o ' Lord General Cromwell to Ireland , and he gave us our bellyful of fighting , and no mistake ; but it ai n't fighting that I complain of , having been always held a valiant man of my inches ; '' and he puffed out his broad chest and looked a very crusader", "context_len": 63}}}, "complains": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "And we can not have fewer servants , we have only four , very much less than most people would have for an establishment of this kind , and Emma already complains of pains in her legs , and says she is worn out going up and down stairs", "context_len": 49}}}, "wench": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "If so be you laid hands if it were but on a hen , if you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old", "context_len": 128}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "'Good sooth ! I know not be she wench or swain ; Her face proclaims her one , her deeds the other ! '", "context_len": 24}}}, "commons": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 89, "context": "you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old Noll and padded the hoof to Kinsale , and there I shipped under Prince Rupert", "context_len": 128}}}, "hoof": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 117, "context": "you 'scaped the gallows your back paid for your chicken , and as for kissing an Irish wench , they 'd have hanged a colonel for doing of it ! And they great woods ! Now I 've seen woods as is worth the seeing , chock full of monkeys and grapes and parrots and such like , but they Irish woods ! Caramba ! I 'd sooner be hanged than set eyes on them again ! So as I was saying , 't was hard knocks and short commons and long sermons , and agues to boot ; so when we come to Cork , I just turned my back on old Noll and padded the hoof to Kinsale , and there I shipped under Prince Rupert", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Then we can hoof it to the next town , drop in at a telegraph office -- --", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "His heart sank , for hardly half a dozen steps were taken when \u2019 Ceph limped again : the halt grew more pronounced , and suddenly he hobbled one or two steps on three legs , holding the remaining hoof clear of the ground as he did so", "context_len": 48}}}, "judicially": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Ay , there was a good deal to be said for Prince Rupert , '' answered Astbury , judicially -- '' a good deal", "context_len": 25}}}, "provost": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "We overhauled a many good ships , and many a pretty bout of fighting we had ; and when we went ashore , well , there was n't any of old Noll 's provost marshals after us", "context_len": 37}}}, "marshals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "We overhauled a many good ships , and many a pretty bout of fighting we had ; and when we went ashore , well , there was n't any of old Noll 's provost marshals after us", "context_len": 37}}}, "pineapples": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "sloop": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "And if we got tired of swinging in a hammock , and eating of fruit and smoking tobacco , why there was a many jolly fellows ready to whip into a little sloop we had handy , and off to -- to -- to spoil the papishers", "context_len": 47}}}, "gloss": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "It was obvious that he was nothing better than a pirate , but he managed to gloss over the barbarities of the life so well , and to dwell on its picturesque and adventurous side so successfully , that Dick began insensibly to soften in his judgment of the wanderer", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "He 'll find it sort of soothing , and it 'll put such a gloss on him you 'll be able to see your face in his coat", "context_len": 28}}}, "barbarities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "It was obvious that he was nothing better than a pirate , but he managed to gloss over the barbarities of the life so well , and to dwell on its picturesque and adventurous side so successfully , that Dick began insensibly to soften in his judgment of the wanderer", "context_len": 50}}}, "insensibly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "It was obvious that he was nothing better than a pirate , but he managed to gloss over the barbarities of the life so well , and to dwell on its picturesque and adventurous side so successfully , that Dick began insensibly to soften in his judgment of the wanderer", "context_len": 50}}}, "snuggest": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "I have a nice little pot buried in a safe place on a certain key ; I 've got a map here '' -- and he thumped his broad chest -- '' here , sewed in the lining of my coat , and the place marked with a cross ; and I tell you , sir , there 's enough gold in that pot to fit out the snuggest little pinnace any man need want to see", "context_len": 77}}}, "pinnace": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 70, "context": "I have a nice little pot buried in a safe place on a certain key ; I 've got a map here '' -- and he thumped his broad chest -- '' here , sewed in the lining of my coat , and the place marked with a cross ; and I tell you , sir , there 's enough gold in that pot to fit out the snuggest little pinnace any man need want to see", "context_len": 77}}}, "commonwealth": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 72, "context": "His unexpected appearance , when they imagined him entangled among the mountains and wilds of unknown regions in the north ; his sudden investiture of Thebes ; the assault ; the calm deliberations in respect to the destiny of the city , and the slow , cautious , discriminating , but inexorable energy with which the decision was carried into effect , all coming in such rapid succession , impressed the Grecian commonwealth with the conviction that the personage they had to deal with was no boy in character , whatever might be his years", "context_len": 95}}}, "apes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "They had proceeded only a short distance when a horrible chattering overhead caused them to look up , and in the branches of a tree they saw two big apes gibbering and grinning at them , with the same awful look in their eyes that had marked the first one they had encountered", "context_len": 53}}}, "commonwealths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Why should he not sail in the wake of Drake and Raleigh , and make himself a name ? Ay , and found new commonwealths in the land of sunset ?", "context_len": 31}}}, "pah": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Pah ! that revolted him -- that was little better than common thievery", "context_len": 13}}}, "pulse": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 21, "context": "She had seven years of the richness of the old world , learning languages , listening to music that stirred every pulse of her soul , haunting art galleries with loving companionship that somehow saw the best and most beautiful in everything if it was not always high art", "context_len": 49}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Somehow she made Barry \u2019 s pulse beat faster than any girl had done before", "context_len": 15}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "There was a numbness in it that gave place to pain as the blood began to pulse strongly through it , but Bomba knew that in a short time it would be as well as ever", "context_len": 36}}}, "distinctness": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "His sober reason began to awaken , and then , almost with the distinctness of a living voice , the words flashed back on him : `` It is to secure the just liberties of the people of God that thou art pledged to live or die for it", "context_len": 49}}}, "watchwords": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Justice , law , liberty were solid facts ; those were the watchwords General Harrison had taught him ; for those he had lived , to those he would be true , whether good or evil fortune awaited him , whether there were , indeed , a heavenly reward for the victor , or but the abyss of forgetfulness at the end of the strife", "context_len": 65}}}, "moorlands": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "Early on Tuesday morning the real business of the day started , for then the shepherds began to bring down their flocks of sheep from the surrounding mountains , the cattle-drovers came with their sleek cows and long-horned young bullocks , and whole herds of rough little Welsh ponies were driven with much noise and shouting from the high moorlands over the border", "context_len": 63}}}, "rooks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The rooks croaked drearily to each other as they winged their way inland , and the gulls circled wailing over the heath before taking their flight to roost on some lonely sand-bank , and no other sound broke the monotonous plunge of the cold waves", "context_len": 45}}}, "croaked": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 2, "context": "The rooks croaked drearily to each other as they winged their way inland , and the gulls circled wailing over the heath before taking their flight to roost on some lonely sand-bank , and no other sound broke the monotonous plunge of the cold waves", "context_len": 45}}}, "gulls": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 41, "context": "He had no difficulty in getting half-a-dozen damaged mackerel down in the harbour -- fish that had been torn by the nets ; but he was only just in time , for in the soft grey light he could see the gulls already busy floating down on their ghostly-looking wings in the gloom , uttering a mournful , peevish wail , and carrying off fragments of fish for their morning meal", "context_len": 71}}}, "circled": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 45, "context": "Then she offered her a piece of cake and a lovely pear , and afterward took her down to see the flower garden that was fairly rioting in beauty , and a flock of snowy white chickens , as well as some fine pigeons that circled around like swallows", "context_len": 49}}}, "balustraded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "He hurried on as fast as his weary limbs could carry him , with a terror of the icy darkness that was closing in like the shadow of death descending upon him , and almost at a run he reached his goal , and stood on the balustraded stone arch that crossed the ice-encumbered moat of the old house", "context_len": 59}}}, "ramped": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The gates before him creaked on their rusty hinges , the gryffons that had ramped so proudly on the gate-posts , had fallen from their high estate , and lay grovelling among the dead flags that fringed the moat", "context_len": 39}}}, "grovelling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The gates before him creaked on their rusty hinges , the gryffons that had ramped so proudly on the gate-posts , had fallen from their high estate , and lay grovelling among the dead flags that fringed the moat", "context_len": 39}}}, "casements": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 63, "context": "`` Later on , when the Sultan visits the enchanted palace for the first time , Alaeddin `` brought him to the high kiosk and he looked at the belvedere ( teyyareh , a square or round erection on the top of a house , either open at the sides or pierced with windows , our architectural term 'lantern ' ) and its casements ( shebabik , pl", "context_len": 68}}}, "pattens": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 0, "context": "Pattens wo n't trouble you any more , his man has gone , but there is Merriman 's man to come on , and there is Allthorp 's man , and there are all the others , and then , above and beyond all , there 's the landlord , Mr", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "PATTENS", "context_len": 1}}}, "carol": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The click of pattens on the paving-stones , and a carol hummed in the clear tones of a girl 's voice , as her tall lithe figure came round the corner of the apparently deserted house", "context_len": 36}}}, "roved": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "A greater contrast to the melancholy scene could not be imagined than her young face glowing with life and health , the ruddy coils of chestnut hair , and the bright hazel eyes that roved far and wide over the empty landscape , as she caught the swinging gates , and began to tie them in place with a piece of cord", "context_len": 62}}}, "cowsheds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The girl glanced over towards the cowsheds , and then , with an impatient shake of her head , and a murmur of `` Lazy old John , '' she carefully guided her bewildered guest into a great kitchen , and deposited him in the corner of a settle by the fire", "context_len": 52}}}, "infused": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "`` Madam , '' he said , when at length he laid down the spoon and straightened himself , `` I do truly hold you have saved my life this night ; and , indeed , not only have you delivered this poor body from danger , but the new spirit your kindness hath infused into me will go far to carry me to my journey 's end", "context_len": 68}}}, "acknowledgement": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "For all , I do tender my thankful acknowledgement", "context_len": 9}}}, "ails": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Indeed , kind mistress , little ails me but weariness", "context_len": 11}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Hi , hi ! what ails the fellows ? '' as all the squirrels sprang from him and whisked up the walnut tree", "context_len": 24}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` This ought to be good for whatever ails you , but I do n't like it", "context_len": 17}}}, "qualmish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "`` Ay , '' he answered , `` and 't is not very like I should there meet with a second good Samaritan to succour me ; but I trust I shall go forward bravely now ; 't is but the warm room hath made me somewhat qualmish", "context_len": 48}}}, "dairy": {"freq": 23, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 23, "word_index": 57, "context": "Fanny , though kind and good-natured , was not a very wise young woman ; and when she had rushed into the house in such an excited manner , she said that she had put the cup on the shelf of the little window , that Lem had come over the rocks at the back of the dairy , put his hand in at the window , snatched out the cup , and run up the mountain with it", "context_len": 79}}}, "cradles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "'T was no concern of yours ? Sure this new king is not a Herod that he should make war on men for what they did as babes in their cradles ! You were but a child in those days ! ''", "context_len": 42}}}, "befit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Because , madam , dangerous words befit a dangerous man , '' he answered more gently", "context_len": 17}}}, "doer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "He was more of a dreamer than a doer , and , instead of trying to accomplish things , he spent far too much time in meditating on what he might accomplish", "context_len": 32}}}, "spiritless": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` And as he spoke , the sudden life that had flashed into his eyes , seemed to flicker and go out like a candle , the soldier was changed back into a dull and spiritless wayfarer", "context_len": 37}}}, "masterfulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The charming masterfulness of her manner , the toss of her proud little head , might have quickened duller pulses than those of Richard Harrison", "context_len": 25}}}, "jailor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "He tried in vain to open any intercourse with his ferocious jailor , whose ward was sometimes shared by a comrade , when there was much ungodly revelry below , and snatches of Danish war songs mingled with profane oaths", "context_len": 40}}}, "ladle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Other paragraphs in the report read thus : `` Received anonymously five large cheeses ; received a box of dessert knives and forks , a cruet , a silver soup ladle and a silver cup ; from Clifton , twelve tons house coals ; from Bedminster , a monster loaf , 200 lbs", "context_len": 53}}}, "dumplings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` I knew no man alive could resist the charm of my dumplings ! Now , take patience but a little , and you shall see how well I order things for my visitor ! '' and she ran gaily out of the room", "context_len": 44}}}, "particularize": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "I need not particularize concerning their malice , as by God 's help I have escaped it for the time", "context_len": 20}}}, "unaccountably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` When I was on my way to London in May two years ago , '' he answered flushing unaccountably", "context_len": 20}}}, "lecturing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` That was when my father was lecturing at Ipswich , '' she answered , `` and I was with him , and we were there still when the tidings came of the fit that carried off my grandfather suddenly ; so you saw him later than I , '' she concluded wistfully", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "He had on an old overcoat , and a basket of tools was thrown over his shoulder with which to earn his food in case temperance lecturing failed", "context_len": 28}}}, "heal": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "`` The Maugrabin could scarce believe his ears of this speech , [ 667 ] for that this was what he sought ; so he went up to Alaeddin , as he would lay his hand on his head , after the fashion of Fatimeh the recluse , and heal him of his pain", "context_len": 54}}}, "lukewarm": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Ah , and is that also why you were too lukewarm a Laodicean to go out in Venner 's rising last month ? ''", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Most of them were sleeping , some with their bodies half-submerged beneath the lukewarm , shallow ooze", "context_len": 17}}}, "plotter": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "Nevertheless , mine enemies found it an easy thing to bring witnesses to swear I was seen in Venner 's company , and pressed hard on my hiding-place ; so seeing I was not wealthy enough or easy enough to bribe their witnesses to refrain from lies , I e'en fled , and have the hue and cry after me for a dangerous plotter", "context_len": 64}}}, "amended": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "But your plan may be so far amended that you will do best to stay here in hiding till the day before the ship may sail", "context_len": 26}}}, "seagulls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` No one comes here save the crows and seagulls , or maybe a ghost", "context_len": 15}}}, "shun": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 44, "context": "Aunt Helen had broadly hinted that an hour 's practice of much-neglected pieces upon the piano in the Rose Parlour would be a profitable means of employing the time , and the fear lest this threat should become an actual command caused Peggy to shun the neighbourhood of the house with elaborate care , and betake herself , with a selection of pets , to the barn", "context_len": 67}}}, "cowman": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "Full of healthful energy , it was a relief to her to help the solitary maid in her household work ; that was the least dull part of her new life ; and , in the kitchen , the Queen of Hunstanton could still rule imperiously over the old cowman , and make the dairywoman tremble before her royal displeasure", "context_len": 60}}}, "clack": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "This house does but serve me for withdrawing-room , when I am weary of old Molly 's clack and out of patience with her husband", "context_len": 25}}}, "basked": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "A mighty noise above stairs of moving furniture and the patter of light footsteps came to Harrison as he basked by the great fire ; and it was not till the evening was growing late that Audrey reappeared , and , dropping a curtsey with a charming air of demureness , prayed leave to marshal his worship to his bedchamber", "context_len": 60}}}, "demureness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "A mighty noise above stairs of moving furniture and the patter of light footsteps came to Harrison as he basked by the great fire ; and it was not till the evening was growing late that Audrey reappeared , and , dropping a curtsey with a charming air of demureness , prayed leave to marshal his worship to his bedchamber", "context_len": 60}}}, "bedchamber": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 59, "context": "A mighty noise above stairs of moving furniture and the patter of light footsteps came to Harrison as he basked by the great fire ; and it was not till the evening was growing late that Audrey reappeared , and , dropping a curtsey with a charming air of demureness , prayed leave to marshal his worship to his bedchamber", "context_len": 60}}}, "bedsteads": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Donkeys , dogs , rats , everything indeed in the way of flesh , had been consumed ; even boot leather , the straps of native bedsteads , and mimosa gum did not come amiss to the sorely-tried garrison", "context_len": 39}}}, "mountebank": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "They are too lusty for such mountebank scrambles ! And now , sir , your fire burns bright , and I will wish you good night , and God keep you in safety", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` And is there no one but this little mountebank to look after you ? '' asked the old lady", "context_len": 20}}}, "pestilential": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It might be only the pestilential den men then called a jail ; it might be the slave-ship , and the chain-gang in Barbadoes ; it might be the gibbet , with the hand of the executioner scrabbling in his entrails", "context_len": 41}}}, "scrabbling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "It might be only the pestilential den men then called a jail ; it might be the slave-ship , and the chain-gang in Barbadoes ; it might be the gibbet , with the hand of the executioner scrabbling in his entrails", "context_len": 41}}}, "entrails": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 71, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}}}, "coherent": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "He remembered with a surprise that was full of relief that he had talked to her for long that evening , and his words had been coherent -- that he had actually jested ! He was not mad ! That horrible execution was true ; it was no insane dream ; but other things were real too", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "Sobrinini had said nothing coherent since her rescue , but sat crouched on the bottom of the boat mumbling to herself , her long , disheveled hair shielding her face", "context_len": 30}}}, "exorcised": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "A girl 's calm voice , a girl 's clear eyes seemed to have exorcised the demon that had pursued him", "context_len": 21}}}, "jested": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "He remembered with a surprise that was full of relief that he had talked to her for long that evening , and his words had been coherent -- that he had actually jested ! He was not mad ! That horrible execution was true ; it was no insane dream ; but other things were real too", "context_len": 57}}}, "carolled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The sounds of a ballad carolled below , awoke him next morning to the knowledge that his hostess was already at the house , and about her morning tasks", "context_len": 29}}}, "grandchild": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "like an enchanted damosel of a fairy tale ? 'T is a strange plight for such a tenderly-nurtured maid , for old Sir Gyles guarded her as the very apple of his eye ! And what state did not he keep , and Hunstanton Hall ! And with what a retinue did he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would", "context_len": 128}}}, "nymph": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": "of his eye ! And what state did not he keep , and Hunstanton Hall ! And with what a retinue did he ride to visit us at Highgate ! Yet here is his grandchild without man or maid to serve her , working with her hands like -- was I about to say a farm wench ? Fie , fie , like a nymph of Arcadia , rather ! I can not but call to mind the romances my master whipped me so soundly for wasting my lesson-hours over in Newcastle Grammar School ! I wonder would she flout me , did she guess how like one of those enchanted princesses I deem her ? But , in sad earnest , I must needs ask how this", "context_len": 128}}}, "clink": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A clink of chains down the cart-track drew his attention , and in a minute more an old man hobbled into the yard carrying a couple of milk pails on a yoke", "context_len": 32}}}, "bides": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Missis , her told us her 'd got a visitor , '' continued the milkman , resting his pails on the top of a low wall , and straightening his shoulders ; `` her bides down at the cottage along o ' we now -- 't is too lonesome for a young maid here o ' nights", "context_len": 58}}}, "christening": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "'There 's naught like oil and brickdust for putting a polish on metals , ' observed Nancy , complacently scouring away at Bobby 's christening mug", "context_len": 26}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Primrose 's efforts at gentility , declaring that the philosophy of the light-hearted Vicar exactly suited his own circumstances , and christening Lilian and Peggy 'Olivia ' and 'Sophia ' on the spot", "context_len": 33}}}, "gentlefolks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "`` And to think of him , '' she said , wiping the tears of joy from her eyes , -- '' to think of him asking for his old mammy 'most before he had done with his greetings to the gentlefolks ! And him putting his arm about me and giving me a kiss as hearty as he used when he was a boy ; and him been all over the world seein ' all sorts of sights and doin 's", "context_len": 82}}}, "windy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "And when all come to be settled , why then Passon Perrient he was on the windy side of the hedge , and he just sold the horses and cows and the old house and went across seas , and our young missis , she come to her aunt , old Madam Isham , and Molly , that 's my wife , and I , we come along on her ; but 't was a change -- that it was", "context_len": 80}}}, "dang": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "`` Ees , ees we 'd surely stay by her ; but 't is no fitting place here for a young lady ; why , there 's no company -- no coming and going ; and the coaches as used to come to the old Squires 's ; and the quality ; and they fare to have clean forgotten our young lady , dang 'em ! And Squire 's great house turned into an inn ! You think o ' that ! If so be as you goo into Hun'ston , you 'll see the name o ' it , The Royal Oak , and a great oak tree drawed for a sign over the front door", "context_len": 117}}}, "goo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 87, "context": "`` Ees , ees we 'd surely stay by her ; but 't is no fitting place here for a young lady ; why , there 's no company -- no coming and going ; and the coaches as used to come to the old Squires 's ; and the quality ; and they fare to have clean forgotten our young lady , dang 'em ! And Squire 's great house turned into an inn ! You think o ' that ! If so be as you goo into Hun'ston , you 'll see the name o ' it , The Royal Oak , and a great oak tree drawed for a sign over the front door", "context_len": 117}}}, "dairywoman": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "Full of healthful energy , it was a relief to her to help the solitary maid in her household work ; that was the least dull part of her new life ; and , in the kitchen , the Queen of Hunstanton could still rule imperiously over the old cowman , and make the dairywoman tremble before her royal displeasure", "context_len": 60}}}, "parvenus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Madam Isham considered most of her country neighbours to be either parvenus or white-washed rebels , while she was too proud to show her poverty to the few gentlefolk she considered worthy of her acquaintance", "context_len": 35}}}, "maundering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Old , sad , and sour , Audrey found the old lady 's maundering lamentations over the good times of King James a sad contrast to her grandfather 's discussions of public matters , or her father 's learned conversation", "context_len": 40}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Penelly bit his lips and said nothing , while Zekle went maundering on about his duty , and how unwilling he was to take the money , till , seeing an awkward look in his victim 's eyes , he concluded that he had better go , and went out , turning at the door to tell Penelly that he might be quite comfortable now , and wishing him good-night", "context_len": 70}}}, "discussions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "Old , sad , and sour , Audrey found the old lady 's maundering lamentations over the good times of King James a sad contrast to her grandfather 's discussions of public matters , or her father 's learned conversation", "context_len": 40}}}, "frontiersmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 82, "context": "dull external life had no real effect on Audrey 's spirits ; she bore her great-aunt 's peevishness and the monotony of her days with cheerful equanimity , for this all was but a parenthesis ; soon she would join the beloved father whom she tended and petted and scolded and revered , and they would begin a new life in a wonderful country , where she should see live savages with painted faces and feather head-dresses , and valiant soldiers and frontiersmen , whose adventures were as romantic as those of Robin Hood , and saintly ministers who had fled from persecution , like the people in Fox 's Book of Martyrs ; her brilliant fancy painted the Western land with all the hues of the sunset", "context_len": 128}}}, "teachings": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 49, "context": "But Aunt Patty was learning to give up her own will , slowly and with difficulty it might be , with many a struggle , many a failure , as had been shown this morning ; but still , thanks to the whispers of the better spirit by whose teachings she had lately been led , she was taking to heart the lesson so hard to learn because so late begun", "context_len": 71}}}, "formalism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` The narrow formalism of Madam Isham 's creed drove Audrey more and more to dwell on the lessons she had loved , but hardly comprehended , and in her solitude she rediscovered for herself the reasonings which had led Sir Gyles Perrient to stand with Eliot and Pym against the encroachments of the Crown", "context_len": 55}}}, "rediscovered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` The narrow formalism of Madam Isham 's creed drove Audrey more and more to dwell on the lessons she had loved , but hardly comprehended , and in her solitude she rediscovered for herself the reasonings which had led Sir Gyles Perrient to stand with Eliot and Pym against the encroachments of the Crown", "context_len": 55}}}, "fittingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` I trust the little maid behaved herself fittingly , '' laughed Audrey", "context_len": 13}}}, "canoes": {"freq": 24, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 21, "context": "As we sat down to partake , of it together , Ama informed me that one of her father 's largest canoes , manned by forty paddlers , and commanded by a chief whom she had recognised , had passed slowly down the river about an hour after I had composed myself to rest , the chief in charge intently scrutinising both banks , as they went , evidently in search of some indication of our presence , and had finally passed out of sight to the southward ; after which Ama had descended and taken a few hours ' rest", "context_len": 101}}}, "cordon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}}}, "headman": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "I soon found that I was the cause of all the commotion , for no sooner did I step out of the canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have", "context_len": 128}}}, "genuineness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 82, "context": "canoe than , although my travelling companions formed themselves into a cordon round me , and the headman or chief who had me in charge strove by virtue of his authority to prevent such a happening , there occurred a wild rush on the part of the crowd to get at least a sight of me , while those who could get near enough to me insisted upon touching my skin , apparently with the object of satisfying themselves as to the genuineness of its colour ; and from their eagerness and their exclamations of astonishment I came to the conclusion that although they might have heard of , they had never actually seen a white man before , a conclusion which I afterwards found to be correct", "context_len": 128}}}, "palisade": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 11, "context": "As we drew near to the exceedingly narrow gate in the palisade , which had been thrown open to admit us -- and which , I presently saw , was strongly guarded by a number of warriors armed with heavy , broad-bladed spears , murderous-looking swords , and small round shields , or targets , of wood covered with what looked like crocodile hide -- I became sensible of a horrible charnel-house smell ; but it was not until we had passed through the gate , and were inside the palisaded enclosure , that I discovered from whence it emanated", "context_len": 100}}}, "slaver": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 44, "context": "I had already made my plans during the short passage of the boat between the two vessels ; consequently the moment that we were all aboard young Copplestone , who had come with me , led a party of men forward to drive the slaver 's crew below , while I , with a couple of sturdy seamen to back me up , ascended to the poop", "context_len": 67}}}, "mulatto": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "To start with , their colour was much lighter , being a clear brown of varying degrees of depth , from that of the mulatto to a tint not many shades deeper than that of the average Spaniard", "context_len": 38}}}, "ringlets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "It was invariably curly , that of the men growing in close , tiny ringlets clustering thickly all over the head , while that of the women , because it was worn longer , I suppose , took the form of long graceful curls", "context_len": 44}}}, "belle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "They were certainly an exceptionally fine race of people , the men being lithe , clean-limbed , muscular fellows , every one of them apparently in the pink of condition , while the faces and figures of the women , especially the younger ones , would have excited the envy of many an English belle", "context_len": 55}}}, "define": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 9, "context": "But there was a something , very difficult to define , in the expression of these people that I did not at all like , a hardness about the mouth , and a cruel glint in the eyes -- especially of the men -- which looked at me in a manner that suggested all sorts of unpleasant possibilities , and excited within me a distinct longing to be almost anywhere rather than where I was", "context_len": 75}}}, "glint": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "But there was a something , very difficult to define , in the expression of these people that I did not at all like , a hardness about the mouth , and a cruel glint in the eyes -- especially of the men -- which looked at me in a manner that suggested all sorts of unpleasant possibilities , and excited within me a distinct longing to be almost anywhere rather than where I was", "context_len": 75}}}, "targets": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 53, "context": "As we drew near to the exceedingly narrow gate in the palisade , which had been thrown open to admit us -- and which , I presently saw , was strongly guarded by a number of warriors armed with heavy , broad-bladed spears , murderous-looking swords , and small round shields , or targets , of wood covered with what looked like crocodile hide -- I became sensible of a horrible charnel-house smell ; but it was not until we had passed through the gate , and were inside the palisaded enclosure , that I discovered from whence it emanated", "context_len": 100}}}, "crocodile": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "As we drew near to the exceedingly narrow gate in the palisade , which had been thrown open to admit us -- and which , I presently saw , was strongly guarded by a number of warriors armed with heavy , broad-bladed spears , murderous-looking swords , and small round shields , or targets , of wood covered with what looked like crocodile hide -- I became sensible of a horrible charnel-house smell ; but it was not until we had passed through the gate , and were inside the palisaded enclosure , that I discovered from whence it emanated", "context_len": 100}}}, "palisaded": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 90, "context": "As we drew near to the exceedingly narrow gate in the palisade , which had been thrown open to admit us -- and which , I presently saw , was strongly guarded by a number of warriors armed with heavy , broad-bladed spears , murderous-looking swords , and small round shields , or targets , of wood covered with what looked like crocodile hide -- I became sensible of a horrible charnel-house smell ; but it was not until we had passed through the gate , and were inside the palisaded enclosure , that I discovered from whence it emanated", "context_len": 100}}}, "decomposition": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "But a minute or two later , as we drew nearer the tree , the stench meanwhile becoming almost overpoweringly strong , I detected fastened to the trunk of the tree , in a manner that was not at first apparent , nine human corpses , some of them so far advanced in decomposition that even the birds would not approach them ! Then I understood that I saw before me that detestable thing of which I had often heard as the most prominent object in the typical African native town or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may", "context_len": 128}}}, "crucifixion": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 98, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}}}, "lianas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 69, "context": "or village , the `` crucifixion tree , '' upon which the petty despot who rules over that particular community is wont summarily to put to a cruel and lingering death such of his subjects as may be unfortunate enough to offend him ! In some cases , I believe , the monarch is content to cause his victims to be securely lashed to the crucifixion tree by stout lianas , there to perish slowly of hunger and thirst ; but King Banda , the potentate whose will was law in this particular town , had carried his cruelty to its utmost limit by adopting the time-honoured method of nailing his victims to the tree with spike nails driven through the hands and feet into the tough timber", "context_len": 128}}}, "taboo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "The enormous crowd who had followed us up from the beach were not permitted to enter the palisaded enclosure , which was strictly taboo to the common herd ; our party therefore now consisted solely of those who had brought me up the river , four individuals who had joined us outside the gate -- and whom I took to be officials of some sort -- and my unworthy self ; and , for my own part , I would very willingly have waived the distinction of forming one of the party", "context_len": 92}}}, "conjectured": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 5, "context": "Marching up to what I conjectured to be the king 's house -- from the fact that it was not only by far the largest dwelling in the enclosure , but was also distinguished by an exclusive embellishment in the form of a row of a dozen poles , each surmounted by a human skull , planted upright in the ground before it -- we halted at a distance of some twenty paces from the entrance , with our backs turned toward the crucifixion tree , the leafless branches of which overshadowed us , and waited", "context_len": 96}}}, "leopard": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 56, "context": "Neither Peggy nor Bobby had ever seen a wild-beast show before , for those visits to the Zoo , which are the joy of little Londoners , do not fall to the share of country children , and Archie had quite a lively time keeping them out of harm 's way , for Peggy declared the leopard looked so exactly like Tabbyskins at home that she must positively try to stroke it , and was under the rope and up to the bars before Archie could seize her by her skirts and drag her back , while Bobby 's curiosity on the subject of jackals and hyenas nearly led to the ejection of the whole party from the tent", "context_len": 119}}}, "carvings": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "In my collection I have them varying from almost straight to a shape like that of the letter V , nearly straight , curved , plain , ornamented , some with strange carvings , and all varying according to different sections of the country and individual tribes , each having its own make or style , showing respectively rough crudeness or considerable finish , and being especially characteristic in the ends or points -- all of which a boomerang connoisseur will distinguish at once , and locate as to tribe and section", "context_len": 92}}}, "decrepitude": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "The contrast between these two men was most remarkable , for whereas Mafuta appeared to be the living embodiment of extreme age , King Banda could scarcely have been forty ; and while Mafuta was an image of decrepitude , Banda , despite his excessive corpulence , appeared to be -- what in fact he was -- a man of immense physical strength", "context_len": 63}}}, "corpulence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "The contrast between these two men was most remarkable , for whereas Mafuta appeared to be the living embodiment of extreme age , King Banda could scarcely have been forty ; and while Mafuta was an image of decrepitude , Banda , despite his excessive corpulence , appeared to be -- what in fact he was -- a man of immense physical strength", "context_len": 63}}}, "dissimilarity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Yet , notwithstanding this marked dissimilarity in their appearance , there was one point of strong resemblance between them : the expression of their faces , and particularly of their eyes , was ineffably cruel", "context_len": 35}}}, "ineffably": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "Peace ! sweet , sweet peace ! oh how joyful it was to be once more in the deep woods of Aescendune , to hear the sweet song of the birds , and to fear no evil ! Sweet , ineffably sweet were those days to Alfgar and Ethelgiva !", "context_len": 50}}}, "prostrating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "At length , when the prolonged silence was beginning distinctly to get upon our nerves , the king spoke to the headman of our party , addressing to him a few curt words in a decidedly ungracious tone of voice ; whereupon the headman , taking the precaution first to conciliate his Majesty by prostrating himself and rubbing his nose in the dust in token of abject submission , rose to his feet and proceeded to spin a long yarn , of which I was evidently the subject , since he repeatedly pointed to me", "context_len": 95}}}, "punctures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "He must have included in his narrative the incident of the snake-bite , for at one point he seized my right hand and , turning the palm upward , pointed out the spot where the two tiny punctures of the poison fangs were still faintly visible", "context_len": 46}}}, "corroborative": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 63, "context": "It appeared as if this part of his story was received with grave suspicion by both Banda and Mafuta , for I was led forward in order that each in turn might examine the marks ; and after this had been done , several of the savages who had been present at the time were invited to give what I took to be corroborative testimony", "context_len": 65}}}, "reiterating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}}}, "altercation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}}}, "hubbub": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "But Banda promptly intervened , reiterating his order to his guards ; whereupon there ensued between the two great men a most unseemly altercation , the hubbub of which had the effect of bringing the entire royal household to the door of the palace , when , catching sight of me , they unceremoniously swarmed out and crowded round me with every expression of the most unbounded astonishment , particularly on the part of the women , who apparently could not persuade themselves that the colour of my skin and hair were real , for they not only took my skin between their fingers , but gently pinched it", "context_len": 109}}}, "gabbling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "I thought that the women-folk seemed rather well disposed toward me , I therefore did the best I could to strengthen this feeling by smiling at them and speaking to them in a gentle tone of voice , with the result that before another five minutes had passed we were all gabbling and laughing together like so many children , although neither side understood a word of what was said by the other", "context_len": 73}}}, "yam": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "But before I could compose myself to rest two other women entered , one of whom bore , upon a thick biscuit-like cake the size of an ordinary dinner-plate , two roast ribs of goat and a generous portion of boiled yam , while the other carried a calabash full of what I took to be some kind of native beer", "context_len": 61}}}, "calabash": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "But before I could compose myself to rest two other women entered , one of whom bore , upon a thick biscuit-like cake the size of an ordinary dinner-plate , two roast ribs of goat and a generous portion of boiled yam , while the other carried a calabash full of what I took to be some kind of native beer", "context_len": 61}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Although the origin from the earth is certainly the most natural that could suggest itself to primitive man , there are a number of nations claiming provenience from the sky ( the Tukabatchi were let down from the sky in a gourd or calabash ) : from the sun ( Yuchi ) , from the moon , from the sea , from the ashes of fire ( Sh\u00e1wano ) , from eggs ( Quichhua ) or certain plants", "context_len": 78}}}, "inordinately": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Though I was then happily unaware of the fact , the period of the great annual festival , or Customs , was approaching , and the joy of the populace began to find vent in nocturnal concerts inordinately prolonged , the musical instruments consisting of tom-toms , each beaten by two , three , or four performers -- according to the size of the tom- tom -- with a monotony of cadence that soon became positively maddening , further aggravated by the discordant squealing of a number of flageolet- like instruments made of stout reeds", "context_len": 95}}}, "cadence": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 71, "context": "Though I was then happily unaware of the fact , the period of the great annual festival , or Customs , was approaching , and the joy of the populace began to find vent in nocturnal concerts inordinately prolonged , the musical instruments consisting of tom-toms , each beaten by two , three , or four performers -- according to the size of the tom- tom -- with a monotony of cadence that soon became positively maddening , further aggravated by the discordant squealing of a number of flageolet- like instruments made of stout reeds", "context_len": 95}}}, "flute": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}}}, "tootling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 103, "context": "Now , although I have not hitherto had occasion to mention the fact , I was passionately fond of music , and rather fancied myself as a performer upon the flute ; one night , therefore , when one of these hideous concerts was in full blast , and when , consequently , it was useless to attempt to sleep , I sallied forth , accompanied as usual by my guards , and made my way round to the great square in front of the king 's house , where , squatted round a huge fire , some twenty of these enthusiasts were tootling and thumping with a vigour that I could not help regarding as utterly misplaced", "context_len": 118}}}, "flageolet": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "I had resolved to make myself either a flute or a flageolet , whichever might prove easiest , and I accordingly selected with great care half-a-dozen of the most suitable reeds that I could find , and , borrowing his spear from one of my guards , cut them , taking care that they should be of ample length for my purpose", "context_len": 62}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "mouthpiece": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "With some show of hesitation the man surrendered the thing , and upon inspection I found it to be a reed of about a foot in length , with a mouthpiece shaped something like that of a whistle , and with four small holes drilled in the length of the tube , whereby an expert performer might produce seven distinct tones ; but the tones were not consecutive , and the instrument was altogether a very poor and inefficient affair", "context_len": 80}}}, "gesticulation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "It furnished me with an idea , however , and on the following day , by dint of much suggestive gesticulation , I contrived to intimate to my guard my desire to obtain a reed similar to those from which the native instruments were made", "context_len": 45}}}, "mouthpieces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Then I hunted about for some soft wood wherefrom to make mouthpieces and the stopped end of the flute ; and it was while I was thus engaged that I made a most important discovery , which was nothing less than that there were several very fine specimens of the cinchona tree growing in the jungle quite close to the town", "context_len": 61}}}, "cinchona": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "Then I hunted about for some soft wood wherefrom to make mouthpieces and the stopped end of the flute ; and it was while I was thus engaged that I made a most important discovery , which was nothing less than that there were several very fine specimens of the cinchona tree growing in the jungle quite close to the town", "context_len": 61}}}, "conveniently": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "They found an unexpected help , however , for a recent storm had blown down a large oak , which now stretched itself very conveniently over the stream like a bridge , and by the aid of its branches it was quite easy to hop across and climb up the bank at the other side", "context_len": 55}}}, "compounded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Yet , astonishing to say , I was marvellously successful , all things considered , for when at a loss I administered pills compounded of meal dough and strongly flavoured with the first harmless substance that came to hand , and so profound was the belief of these people in my ability that at least half of them were cured by the wonderful power of faith alone", "context_len": 67}}}, "detrimental": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "All this , however , was exceedingly detrimental to the reputation of Mafuta , the chief witch-doctor of the community , who found his power and influence rapidly waning , and he soon discovered means to make me understand that I must cease to trespass upon what he deemed his own exclusive sphere of operations , on pain of making him my mortal enemy", "context_len": 64}}}, "prescribe": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "This of course was bad , for I was in no position to make any man my enemy , much less an individual of such power and influence as Mafuta ; nevertheless I continued to prescribe for all who came to me , trusting that if ever it should come to a struggle between Mafuta and myself , the gratitude of my patients would suffice to turn the scale in my favour", "context_len": 72}}}, "tootle": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Meanwhile I devoted my spare moments to the construction of a flute , and , after two or three partial failures , succeeded in producing an instrument of very sweet tone and a sufficient range of notes to enable me to tootle the air of several of the most popular songs of the day , as well as a fairly full repertoire of jigs , hornpipes , and other dance music", "context_len": 71}}}, "repertoire": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "Meanwhile I devoted my spare moments to the construction of a flute , and , after two or three partial failures , succeeded in producing an instrument of very sweet tone and a sufficient range of notes to enable me to tootle the air of several of the most popular songs of the day , as well as a fairly full repertoire of jigs , hornpipes , and other dance music", "context_len": 71}}}, "jigs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "Meanwhile I devoted my spare moments to the construction of a flute , and , after two or three partial failures , succeeded in producing an instrument of very sweet tone and a sufficient range of notes to enable me to tootle the air of several of the most popular songs of the day , as well as a fairly full repertoire of jigs , hornpipes , and other dance music", "context_len": 71}}}, "hornpipes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 65, "context": "Meanwhile I devoted my spare moments to the construction of a flute , and , after two or three partial failures , succeeded in producing an instrument of very sweet tone and a sufficient range of notes to enable me to tootle the air of several of the most popular songs of the day , as well as a fairly full repertoire of jigs , hornpipes , and other dance music", "context_len": 71}}}, "fandango": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 84, "context": "Bishop , appealed to these simple savages ; a sentimental ditty , such as `` The Anchor 's weighed '' or `` Tom Bowling , '' would hold them breathless and entranced ; `` Rule , Britannia ! '' or `` Should He upbraid '' set them quivering with excitement ; and they seemed to know by intuition that `` The Sailor 's Hornpipe '' was written to be danced to , and they danced to it accordingly a wild , furious , mad fandango in which the extraordinary nature of the gambols of the performers was only equalled by the ecstasy of their enjoyment", "context_len": 105}}}, "gambols": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 92, "context": "Bishop , appealed to these simple savages ; a sentimental ditty , such as `` The Anchor 's weighed '' or `` Tom Bowling , '' would hold them breathless and entranced ; `` Rule , Britannia ! '' or `` Should He upbraid '' set them quivering with excitement ; and they seemed to know by intuition that `` The Sailor 's Hornpipe '' was written to be danced to , and they danced to it accordingly a wild , furious , mad fandango in which the extraordinary nature of the gambols of the performers was only equalled by the ecstasy of their enjoyment", "context_len": 105}}}, "manifested": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "It was now that I first began to grow aware of the fact that there was a certain member of the king 's household who seemed to be taking rather more interest in me than any one else had thus far manifested", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 8, "context": "And certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most", "context_len": 128}}}, "tarred": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "At that instant the thing upon which my gaze was fixed , and which looked like half a fathom of stiff tarred lanyard , darted with lightning swiftness at the girl and coiled itself about her shapely bare arm , while a piercing scream rang out from her pallid lips", "context_len": 50}}}, "lanyard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "At that instant the thing upon which my gaze was fixed , and which looked like half a fathom of stiff tarred lanyard , darted with lightning swiftness at the girl and coiled itself about her shapely bare arm , while a piercing scream rang out from her pallid lips", "context_len": 50}}}, "cessation": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "I of course knew in an instant what it was -- a snake , that very possibly had been attracted to the spot by the notes of my flute , and , startled by the sudden cessation of the music and Ama 's quick , involuntary movement , had instantly coiled itself round her arm and struck at it in its blind and panic-stricken rage", "context_len": 65}}}, "ablaze": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 67, "context": "Without wasting a moment , I applied my lips to the double wound , intending to suck the poison from it , even as I had done in my own case ; but another startling scream from the girl caused me to look up , and , following the direction of her terrified glance , I looked behind me and beheld the king himself , his eyes ablaze with demoniac fury , in the very act of raising a spear that he had snatched from the hand of one of his guards , to drive it through my body", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "On and on the riders travelled , through the exhilarating autumn air , until they stopped for lunch on the borders of a forest which Jack Frost had set ablaze , and which glowed in the sunshine with a dazzling splendour of crimson and bronze and gold", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Down in the village the cottage gardens were ablaze with flowers , and even old Ephraim had forgotten his rheumatism , and crawled out to bask in the hot sunshine on the low wall , and called out a friendly 'Good-marnin ' ! ' as the children went by", "context_len": 49}}}, "demoniac": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 69, "context": "Without wasting a moment , I applied my lips to the double wound , intending to suck the poison from it , even as I had done in my own case ; but another startling scream from the girl caused me to look up , and , following the direction of her terrified glance , I looked behind me and beheld the king himself , his eyes ablaze with demoniac fury , in the very act of raising a spear that he had snatched from the hand of one of his guards , to drive it through my body", "context_len": 99}}}, "contaminated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "But no sooner did he lower the threatening spear than I once more glued my lips to the wound , sucking hard at it with the object of extracting the poison before it had contaminated the blood ; and in this effort I was happily successful , for although there was a slight swelling of the limb , and some pain for an hour or two , that was all that happened ; and before morning my patient had quite recovered from all the effects of her alarming adventure", "context_len": 89}}}, "adjacent": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "I was allowed to go freely where I pleased , it is true , even to the extent of making long hunting or exploring excursions into the adjacent country , but -- whether or not by the king 's orders I could never satisfactorily ascertain -- I soon found that I could never manage to steal off anywhere alone", "context_len": 59}}}, "exasperatingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "If ever I attempted such a thing -- and I did , very frequently -- a party of the king 's guards was certain to turn up , in the most exasperatingly casual and unexpected manner , and join me , under the pretence , as they made me understand , that it was extremely dangerous to venture alone beyond the confines of the town , if I pretended that I was engaged in hunting for animals , or plants to be used in my medical practice", "context_len": 87}}}, "curtailed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "I therefore very gradually curtailed my excursions , and when I undertook them was careful that there should be nothing in the nature of secrecy connected with my movements", "context_len": 29}}}, "handicapped": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 47, "context": "She was frequently in and out , as often as three or four times a day , and very seldom indeed less than twice ; moreover , she seemed exceedingly anxious to become my instructress in her own language , and as I had already felt heavily handicapped on several occasions by my inability to converse freely with those around me I made no demur , although I must confess that I at length began to view with vague disquietude the extreme freedom of intercourse thus instituted by the young woman", "context_len": 91}}}, "demur": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "She was frequently in and out , as often as three or four times a day , and very seldom indeed less than twice ; moreover , she seemed exceedingly anxious to become my instructress in her own language , and as I had already felt heavily handicapped on several occasions by my inability to converse freely with those around me I made no demur , although I must confess that I at length began to view with vague disquietude the extreme freedom of intercourse thus instituted by the young woman", "context_len": 91}}}, "disquietude": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 79, "context": "She was frequently in and out , as often as three or four times a day , and very seldom indeed less than twice ; moreover , she seemed exceedingly anxious to become my instructress in her own language , and as I had already felt heavily handicapped on several occasions by my inability to converse freely with those around me I made no demur , although I must confess that I at length began to view with vague disquietude the extreme freedom of intercourse thus instituted by the young woman", "context_len": 91}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Nevertheless , Spotty \u2019 s words added to Stone \u2019 s disquietude of mind , for he also believed that the loss of Hayden from the team to say nothing of Barker could not be compensated for", "context_len": 37}}}, "foreshadowed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Yet I scarcely knew precisely what it was that I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether", "context_len": 128}}}, "relax": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 88, "context": "I feared , but I certainly had a feeling that the situation was not altogether devoid of peril , one of the most obvious of which was foreshadowed in the question which I frequently asked myself , What would the king think of the intimacy of his daughter with one of totally different race and views of life , should the matter chance to come to his knowledge ? Therefore I kept a very close watch upon myself , and was careful never to allow my manner to relax in the slightest degree from the strictest formality , although to preserve consistently this attitude of extreme reserve was sometimes exceedingly difficult with a companion of so amiable and altogether winsome a manner and disposition as that of Ama", "context_len": 128}}}, "beneficent": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 75, "context": "`` `` Far be it '' ( as the Arabs say ) that we should do so cruel a wrong to so well and justly beloved a memory as that of Jacques Cazotte as to attempt to perpetuate the remembrance of a literary crime which one can hardly believe him to have committed in sober earnest ! Rather let us seek to bury in oblivion this his one offence and suffer kind Lethe with its beneficent waters to wash this `` adulterous blot '' from his else unsullied name", "context_len": 89}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "And suppose I gain admission to the presence , what shall I take by way of offering to the Sultan 's highness ? It [ 333 ] is true , O my son , that the Sultan is clement and rejecteth none that cometh to him for protection or craveth a boon of him , for that he is bountiful and beneficent unto all , great and small ; [ 334 ] but he bestoweth his favours upon those who are deserving thereof or who have done some feat of arms before him or have wrought for the service or defence of the realm ; and thou , O my son , tell me , what hast thou done for [ 335 ] the Sultan or the realm", "context_len": 128}}}, "amateurish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "I must cure the king , or it would be so much the worse for me ! And how was I to cure him ? My knowledge of disease was of the slightest and most amateurish kind , and , for aught that I could tell to the contrary , might not even be sufficient to enable me to diagnose the case correctly , much less to treat it successfully ! However , there was no use in meeting trouble half-way ; the only possible course was to obey the summons forthwith , and do my best , leaving the result in the hands of Providence", "context_len": 106}}}, "diagnose": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "I must cure the king , or it would be so much the worse for me ! And how was I to cure him ? My knowledge of disease was of the slightest and most amateurish kind , and , for aught that I could tell to the contrary , might not even be sufficient to enable me to diagnose the case correctly , much less to treat it successfully ! However , there was no use in meeting trouble half-way ; the only possible course was to obey the summons forthwith , and do my best , leaving the result in the hands of Providence", "context_len": 106}}}, "eater": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Of very little , for I am but a moderate eater , '' answered the king", "context_len": 17}}}, "plantains": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "There was , first , a broiled fish , fresh from the river , with boiled yams ; then a few roast plantains -- not more than a dozen , I think ; then the roast rib of a cow ; a few handfuls of boiled rice ; and -- yes , I think that was all , except a bowl of jaro ' -- the latter being a kind of native beer", "context_len": 73}}}, "rib": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "There was , first , a broiled fish , fresh from the river , with boiled yams ; then a few roast plantains -- not more than a dozen , I think ; then the roast rib of a cow ; a few handfuls of boiled rice ; and -- yes , I think that was all , except a bowl of jaro ' -- the latter being a kind of native beer", "context_len": 73}}}, "vomiting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I therefore came to the conclusion that if vomiting could be freely induced almost immediate relief ought to follow ; and I accordingly prescribed the only emetic which I could think of at the moment , namely , copious draughts of warm water , followed by tickling the back of the throat with a feather", "context_len": 55}}}, "emetic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "I therefore came to the conclusion that if vomiting could be freely induced almost immediate relief ought to follow ; and I accordingly prescribed the only emetic which I could think of at the moment , namely , copious draughts of warm water , followed by tickling the back of the throat with a feather", "context_len": 55}}}, "enumeration": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 86, "context": "These means were so far successful that the patient acknowledged a certain measure of relief ; but after a time the burning pains recurred and seemed to become more acute than ever , to my profound dismay ; for , goaded almost to madness by the intensity of his suffering , the king was rapidly growing as dangerous as a wounded buffalo , and , between the paroxysms of his anguish , began to threaten all and sundry with certain pains and penalties , the mere enumeration of which made my flesh creep and plunged me into a cold perspiration", "context_len": 100}}}, "virulent": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "They spent quite a long time in the shooting-gallery , and won a cocoa-nut , which Bobby proudly carried round the fair , and they had their photographs taken in a group , but some air-bubbles having unfortunately got on to the plate , their countenances came out speckled as though they were suffering from a virulent attack of small-pox ; but Peggy kept it , all the same , as a memento of the occasion", "context_len": 76}}}, "pretender": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "And as for the white man , let the presumptuous pretender be closely confined in his own hut until I can decide upon the nature of his punishment", "context_len": 28}}}, "besought": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 17, "context": "The captain and master could not , however , see the matter in this light , and besought Sir Richard to have a care of them , declaring that the Spaniards would be ready to treat with them ; and that , as there were a number of gallant men yet living whose wounds were not mortal , they might do their country and prince acceptable service hereafter", "context_len": 68}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "grieved and was exceeding chagrined , for that he had thought to greaten his son and advance him by making him the King 's son-in-law ; so he bethought himself and was perplexed anent the matter and what was to do therein ; [ 414 ] and indeed it irked him sore that the marriage should be dissolved , for that he had long besought [ 415 ] the Ten [ 416 ] that he might compass the like of that affair ; [ 417 ] so he said to his son , `` Have patience , O my son , so we may see [ how it will be ] to-night , and we will set over you guards to guard you ; but do not thou", "context_len": 128}}}, "requital": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 66, "context": "upon sunset that I got any further news , when one of the guards who had me in charge informed me , as he brought in my supper , that Mafuta had completely cured the king within an hour of the moment when he was first summoned to his Majesty 's bedside ; that Banda had already risen from his couch ; and that , in requital for his service , Mafuta had claimed -- and been granted -- the right to dispose of me as he pleased upon the occasion of the forthcoming festival of the Customs ! Which meant , of course , that I was to die by some exquisite refinement of torture , the nature of which would probably be too dreadful for description", "context_len": 128}}}, "conspired": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "For I very shrewdly suspected that Gouroo and Mafuta were equally interested in my downfall -- might , indeed , have conspired in some mysterious manner to bring it about -- and would probably take care that it should be as complete and disastrous as savage vindictiveness could make it", "context_len": 50}}}, "vindictiveness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "For I very shrewdly suspected that Gouroo and Mafuta were equally interested in my downfall -- might , indeed , have conspired in some mysterious manner to bring it about -- and would probably take care that it should be as complete and disastrous as savage vindictiveness could make it", "context_len": 50}}}, "paradox": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The days now dragged themselves away upon leaden feet , yet -- apparent paradox -- with frightful rapidity ; for I now no longer had a household to attend to my wants ; my meals were brought to me with unfailing regularity by my guards , but they had apparently been forbidden to communicate with me , for not a word could I get out of them , good , bad , or indifferent", "context_len": 74}}}, "pandemonium": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "virulence": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "incarceration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "The excited buzz and bustle of preparation pervaded the town all day , and every day , while night again became a pandemonium of barbarous sounds -- for the tom-tom and flageolet concerts had been resumed with tenfold virulence since my incarceration -- and on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she", "context_len": 128}}}, "verified": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 79, "context": "on one occasion a terrific uproar announced the arrival of the unhappy prisoners who had been captured , in order that the festival might lose nothing of its importance or impressiveness through lack of a sufficient tale of victims ; but I could not detect any indications of an attempt on the part of any one to communicate with me ; and at length the latent hope that Ama 's boast of her influence with her father might be verified , and that she might succeed in inducing the king to spare me , died out , and I began to prepare myself , as best I could , to meet whatever fate might have in store for me with the fortitude befitting a Christian and an Englishman", "context_len": 128}}}, "supinely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "But do not suppose that all this while I was supinely and tamely acquiescing in the fate that awaited me", "context_len": 20}}}, "acquiescing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "But do not suppose that all this while I was supinely and tamely acquiescing in the fate that awaited me", "context_len": 20}}}, "nakedness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "Thus the time passed on until the first day of the Customs dawned , when , having received a more than usually substantial meal , I was stripped of the few rags that still covered my nakedness , and , with my hands tightly bound behind me by a thin but strong raw-hide rope , was led forth to the great square wherein the Customs were celebrated , and firmly bound to one of the posts , the erection of which I had witnessed a week earlier", "context_len": 87}}}, "invective": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "What the fate of the rest of us was to be there was nothing to indicate , but I had no doubt that it would be something quite as dreadful as fire ; and I had fully made up my mind that when my turn came I would endeavour , by insult and invective , to goad my tormentors to such a state of fury and exasperation as should provoke them to finish me off quickly", "context_len": 76}}}, "goad": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "What the fate of the rest of us was to be there was nothing to indicate , but I had no doubt that it would be something quite as dreadful as fire ; and I had fully made up my mind that when my turn came I would endeavour , by insult and invective , to goad my tormentors to such a state of fury and exasperation as should provoke them to finish me off quickly", "context_len": 76}}}, "portended": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 1, "context": "Everything portended it ; the atmosphere was absolutely still , not a twig or even a leaf stirred , all nature seemed to be waiting in breathless suspense for the coming outbreak ; even the insects had ceased to attack us , and had retired to their leafy retreat , and the air was so heavy and close that , naked as I was , I perspired at every pore", "context_len": 70}}}, "myrmidons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "No sooner were the royal party seated than a commotion on the opposite side of the square portended another arrival ; and in a few minutes , through a narrow lane that had been formed in the dense mass of people , Mafuta and his myrmidons , to the number of nearly a hundred , came leaping and bounding into the open space beneath the crucifixion tree", "context_len": 67}}}, "necklaces": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 54, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}}}, "girdles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 67, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}}}, "revolting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 81, "context": "Daubed all over their naked bodies with black , white , and red paint , with their hair gathered into a knot on the crown of the head , and decorated with long feathers , strings of big beads , and long strips of scarlet cloth -- obtained from goodness knows where -- with necklaces of birds ' and animals ' claws about their necks , and girdles of animals ' entrails round their waists , they presented as hideous and revolting a picture as can possibly be imagined as they went careering madly round the circle , each man waving a long spear over his head", "context_len": 107}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "304-311 ) are dwelt upon by several other writers , and were of a revolting character", "context_len": 16}}}, "inimical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 72, "context": "But , singularly enough , no one seemed desirous to avail himself of his neighbour 's generosity ; and the reason soon became apparent ; for presently , in the midst of their wild bounding round the inner edge of the tightly packed mass of spectators , they came to a sudden halt , and Mafuta , advancing alone , proceeded to `` smell out '' those who were supposed to be inimical to the king 's or his own authority , or against whom either of them had a secret grudge", "context_len": 92}}}, "smite": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 74, "context": "With his body bent , his head thrust forward , and his nostrils working , he slowly passed along the inner face of the crowd , his shifty eyes darting hither and thither , until his gaze happened to fall upon one of the individuals for whom he was looking , when he would come to a halt , appear to be following a scent , and finally stretch forth his spear and lightly smite some man or woman on the head with it", "context_len": 84}}}, "onlooker": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 116, "context": "certainly there was a very commendable celerity manifested by those who had the direction of affairs ; there was no disposition to keep the holiday-makers waiting ; the unhappy victims were led up , one after the other , before King Banda , and their supposed crimes very briefly recited to him , whereupon his Majesty , with equal brevity , pronounced their sentence -- in all cases that of death -- which was at once carried out , the only difference consisting in the mode of execution ; some of the unfortunate wretches being secured to the crucifixion tree in one way , some in another ; but it was very difficult for a mere onlooker to decide which of the plans adopted inflicted the most suffering", "context_len": 128}}}, "ensue": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Moreover , it was he who taught thee to seek of me a Roc 's egg , so my destruction should ensue thereof ; and if thou misdoubt of my word , come and see whom I have slain", "context_len": 39}}}, "batches": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Then , in batches of ten at a time , forty more victims were triced up to the boughs of this accursed tree by raw-hide ropes fastened to one wrist or one ankle , in such positions that their bodies showed clearly against the bright background of sky ; and , while thus suspended , whosoever would was at liberty to shoot at them with bows and arrows , the great object being , apparently , to pierce the body with as many arrows as possible without inflicting a mortal injury", "context_len": 91}}}, "bunglers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "But there were a few bunglers among the crowd , for some of the shots went so far astray that instead of hitting the mark at which they were supposed to be aimed , they hit and slew some half-a-dozen of the crucified ones", "context_len": 44}}}, "crucified": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "But there were a few bunglers among the crowd , for some of the shots went so far astray that instead of hitting the mark at which they were supposed to be aimed , they hit and slew some half-a-dozen of the crucified ones", "context_len": 44}}}, "uninitiated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "To the uninitiated this may seem a not particularly inhumane form of inflicting the death punishment ; but I , who saw the whole remaining part of the day spent in doing some fifty poor wretches to death in that fashion , can tell a very different story ; there is no need to enter into details , but I may say that those who were weakest , and who succumbed most quickly , were to be most envied", "context_len": 79}}}, "inhumane": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "To the uninitiated this may seem a not particularly inhumane form of inflicting the death punishment ; but I , who saw the whole remaining part of the day spent in doing some fifty poor wretches to death in that fashion , can tell a very different story ; there is no need to enter into details , but I may say that those who were weakest , and who succumbed most quickly , were to be most envied", "context_len": 79}}}, "filmy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}}}, "overcast": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 56, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 8, "context": "Even as he lifted his eyes toward the overcast sky , the pure white moon burst through a widening rift and poured its light like a benediction upon the silent world", "context_len": 31}}}, "louring": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}}}, "veiling": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 84, "context": "The day had opened with a cloudless sky and brilliant sunshine ; but , as the hours dragged themselves slowly away , clouds , light and filmy at first but gradually growing more dense and threatening , began to gather , until toward evening the sun became blotted out and the whole vault of heaven grew overcast and louring , as though nature , horrified and disgusted at the orgy of human cruelty being enacted here on this little spot of earth , were veiling her face to shut out the shameful sight", "context_len": 93}}}, "disperse": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "By the time that the proceedings of the day were over and the enormous crowd began to disperse , it became evident that a more than usually violent tropical thunderstorm was brewing , although it might be some hours yet before it would burst over the blood-stained town", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "We won \u2019 t make so many hits , but we \u2019 ll make the Japs disperse , so their flak won \u2019 t be so concentrated", "context_len": 27}}}, "brewing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "By the time that the proceedings of the day were over and the enormous crowd began to disperse , it became evident that a more than usually violent tropical thunderstorm was brewing , although it might be some hours yet before it would burst over the blood-stained town", "context_len": 48}}}, "blackest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "There were some things one in particular he would conveniently forget to mention ; but he would not fail to paint in blackest colors the character of the lad he despised", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Who are you calling a coward ? Who are you pushing ? snarled the low-browed chap , scowling his blackest , and assuming his fiercest aspect , his huge hands clenched", "context_len": 31}}}, "unimaginable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "But although I had passed through the day unscathed , so far as actual bodily injury was concerned , it had nevertheless been a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything", "context_len": 128}}}, "extremities": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": "a day of suffering for me , growing ever more acute as the hours dragged wearily away , for , apart from the feelings of horror with which I had witnessed the display of so much unimaginable cruelty and torture , the bonds which confined me to my post had been drawn so tightly as greatly to impede the circulation of blood through my extremities , until by the time that the great square was empty of its crowd of bloodthirsty revellers , the anguish had become so great that I was almost in a fainting condition and could give but scant attention to anything beyond the pangs that racked every nerve of my tortured body ; in fact I observed , with feelings of envy , that", "context_len": 128}}}, "reprieved": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "However , since we had been spared thus far , I concluded that we might reasonably hope to be reprieved at least until the next day , and I looked with impatience to be released and conveyed back to my place of confinement until dawn should again summon me forth to witness and , peradventure , be the victim of accumulative horrors", "context_len": 62}}}, "accumulative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 60, "context": "However , since we had been spared thus far , I concluded that we might reasonably hope to be reprieved at least until the next day , and I looked with impatience to be released and conveyed back to my place of confinement until dawn should again summon me forth to witness and , peradventure , be the victim of accumulative horrors", "context_len": 62}}}, "sacrificial": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 67, "context": "But I soon discovered that even this small measure of mercy was to be denied me ; food and drink were indeed to be served out to us -- in order , as I surmised , that we might meet the ordeal in store for us with unabated strength -- but the night was to be passed , as the day had been , secured to the sacrificial post , exposed , naked and helpless , to the elements and to the myriad insect plagues that attacked us unceasingly", "context_len": 89}}}, "plagues": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 84, "context": "But I soon discovered that even this small measure of mercy was to be denied me ; food and drink were indeed to be served out to us -- in order , as I surmised , that we might meet the ordeal in store for us with unabated strength -- but the night was to be passed , as the day had been , secured to the sacrificial post , exposed , naked and helpless , to the elements and to the myriad insect plagues that attacked us unceasingly", "context_len": 89}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "Yet surely no records of the past , extant , tell us of such dark threatening clouds as hang over the realm of England at this time ; when the thousandth year since our blessed Lord 's nativity having passed , we seem to be entering on those awful plagues which the Apocalypse tells us must precede the consummation of all things", "context_len": 62}}}, "copiously": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "I noted that while those who had not succumbed to their sufferings were fed as they stood still bound to their posts , those who had become unconscious were temporarily released , and revived by being copiously soused with water , and , further , were allowed to eat and drink while seated on the ground , before they were lashed up afresh ; and I took the hint , feigning insensibility for the sake of the few minutes of temporary relief that I hoped thus to win", "context_len": 88}}}, "soused": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "I noted that while those who had not succumbed to their sufferings were fed as they stood still bound to their posts , those who had become unconscious were temporarily released , and revived by being copiously soused with water , and , further , were allowed to eat and drink while seated on the ground , before they were lashed up afresh ; and I took the hint , feigning insensibility for the sake of the few minutes of temporary relief that I hoped thus to win", "context_len": 88}}}, "sapped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "I gathered from their remarks that they were seriously concerned at my condition , for it seemed that I was reserved for some very especial refinement of torture , the satisfactory application of which demanded that I must come to it in the possession of my full strength , which they feared had been seriously sapped by the suffering which I had already endured , and they freely expressed their concern lest , under existing circumstances , I should not furnish quite so much sport as was being expected of me", "context_len": 91}}}, "circulate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "But , oh , what exquisite torment was mine when , my bonds being released , the blood once more began to circulate through my benumbed members ! I could have screamed aloud with the excruciating agony , had not my pride prevented me ; and it was a full hour before I had sufficiently recovered the use of my hands to enable me to convey food and drink to my lips", "context_len": 72}}}, "inexpressibly": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 74, "context": "The food and drink provided for me were of an especially nourishing character , and when at length I had partaken of as much as I could force down my throat I was again lashed to my stake , but this time so carefully that , while for me to loose my bonds was an impossibility , the circulation of blood was in nowise impeded ; and for even this small mercy I was inexpressibly grateful", "context_len": 76}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "She had been tired out with the railroad journey , entertaining as it was , then the excitement of meeting them all again , the bump on her forehead when she had come down so hard on Pansy 's head , and the screams that seemed like a stab going from temple to temple tired her inexpressibly", "context_len": 57}}}, "pore": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 69, "context": "Everything portended it ; the atmosphere was absolutely still , not a twig or even a leaf stirred , all nature seemed to be waiting in breathless suspense for the coming outbreak ; even the insects had ceased to attack us , and had retired to their leafy retreat , and the air was so heavy and close that , naked as I was , I perspired at every pore", "context_len": 70}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Only Peggy sometimes stole up the stone staircase , and taking one of the strange books from its hiding-place , would pore over the quaint pictures which bordered the pages", "context_len": 30}}}, "mortify": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "But even to them relief was promised , for with the impending downpour of rain their wounds would quickly mortify , and then their sufferings would soon be at an end", "context_len": 31}}}, "zenith": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}}}, "drenching": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 51, "context": "Very slowly and gradually the storm worked its way toward the zenith , gathering intensity as it rose , and at length -- probably about ten o'clock -- the first drops of rain , hot and heavy , like gouts of blood , began to fall , quickly increasing to a drenching downpour , accompanied by lightning , green , rose-tinted , violet , sun-bright , that lighted up the town until every object , however minute , was as clearly visible as in broad daylight , while the ceaseless crashing of the thunder was unspeakably appalling", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 67, "context": "As for Peggy , a young gipsy tramp would have looked more respectable , for the brown holland dress , which had started out stiff and clean yesterday morning , was smeared with whinberry juice , black smudges from the kettle , and green stains from the mossy stones in the stream , and clung around her bare legs in damp , clammy folds , while the drenching rain had reduced the poppies in her hat to a scarlet pulp , which dripped down in crimson tears upon her cheek", "context_len": 90}}}, "ceaseless": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 89, "context": "Very slowly and gradually the storm worked its way toward the zenith , gathering intensity as it rose , and at length -- probably about ten o'clock -- the first drops of rain , hot and heavy , like gouts of blood , began to fall , quickly increasing to a drenching downpour , accompanied by lightning , green , rose-tinted , violet , sun-bright , that lighted up the town until every object , however minute , was as clearly visible as in broad daylight , while the ceaseless crashing of the thunder was unspeakably appalling", "context_len": 97}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "So that was the father of Phyllis and Marjorie Norton , that vulgar man with the loud voice and the coarse red face , and those pretty frocks and handsome carriages were all paid for out of the ruin of poor people 's homes ; for the lower parts of Warford had a bad character , and the clergy waged ceaseless war against the terrible curse of drink", "context_len": 68}}}, "rivulets": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 108, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}}}, "brooks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 114, "context": "In the very height of the storm , when thunder , lightning , and rain together were raging in a perfect pandemonium , a stream of steel-blue lightning darted straight from the zenith , struck the crucifixion tree , and shattered it into a thousand fragments , leaving a great hole in the ground where it had stood ! The storm continued to rage in full fury for about an hour , and then the flashes of lightning , with their accompanying peals of thunder , gradually became less frequent , although the rain continued to beat down upon the parched earth in a perfect deluge which formed rivulets , ay , and even brooks of quite respectable size , flowing in every direction", "context_len": 124}}}, "enjoin": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "Suddenly my guide halted , and pressed herself and me close up against the wall of a building of some kind , at the same time feeling for my face in the darkness , and laying her finger on my lips to enjoin perfect silence", "context_len": 45}}}, "circumspectly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 35, "context": "Still hurrying me forward , Ama led the way through the grove and along its edge , until we eventually reached a narrow bush path , through which it was necessary to wend our way circumspectly , for it was now as black as a wolf 's mouth , save when an occasional flicker of lightning from the now fast-receding storm momentarily lit up our surroundings", "context_len": 66}}}, "sheaf": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "Upon an overturned sheaf , sat mammy , her baby on her knee , the little one crowing and laughing , and shaking her dimpled hands , each of which grasped half a dozen ears of wheat , a new and wonderful plaything to baby 's eyes , as they bobbed their heads up and down with the motion", "context_len": 59}}}, "implicate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "That must not be , my dear girl ; you have already done nobly in freeing me , and in providing me with the means of flight , and I must now do the best I can for myself ; I can not consent to implicate you by permitting you to accompany me", "context_len": 53}}}, "adieu": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Therefore let me now bid you adieu , with my warmest and most grateful thanks , not only for what you have done for me to-night , but also for the friendship which you have shown me from the moment when I first came to know you", "context_len": 47}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "And so the last sad words of adieu were spoken as bravely as might be , and the little troop , about fifty in number , departed from the hall", "context_len": 30}}}, "tortures": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 85, "context": "It was a quiet spot , with long gardens leading to old-fashioned houses on the one side , and the tall palings of a cricket-field on the other -- just the spot where nobody would be likely to come along and make a disturbance , and so evidently Jones minor seemed to think , for he held Bobby pinned against the wall with one hand , while with the other he amused himself by tweaking his ears , pulling his hair , and any other tortures which his ingenious mind could suggest at the moment", "context_len": 95}}}, "tributary": {"freq": 22, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "In little more than ten minutes from the moment of starting we swept out of the tributary stream into the main river , the current of which was also flowing pretty rapidly , though not , of course , so swiftly as that of the lesser stream ; and now , as we pushed off into mid-channel , we found time to exchange a few remarks", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 32, "context": "Tanner 's map ( 1827 ) marks an Indian town Luchepoga on west bank of Tallapoosa river , about ten miles above Tukab\u00e1tchi Talah\u00e1ssi ; also Luchanpogan creek , as a western tributary of Chatahuchi river , in 33 8 Lat", "context_len": 41}}}, "hinting": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "But when Gouroo whispered to my father , hinting at your incapacity , and suggesting that Mafuta should be sent for , my suspicions began to take definite shape , and , although I was not able to verify those suspicions , I finally made up my mind that the whole occurrence was the outcome of a plot between Gouroo and Mafuta -- your only enemies -- to ruin you", "context_len": 70}}}, "incapacity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "But when Gouroo whispered to my father , hinting at your incapacity , and suggesting that Mafuta should be sent for , my suspicions began to take definite shape , and , although I was not able to verify those suspicions , I finally made up my mind that the whole occurrence was the outcome of a plot between Gouroo and Mafuta -- your only enemies -- to ruin you", "context_len": 70}}}, "ailment": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 85, "context": "Now , by the decree of destiny , Fatimeh came down on the morrow to the city and the enchanter , going forth the Khan in the morning , saw the folk crowding together ; so he went up , to see what was toward , and found Fatimeh standing , whilst every one who had a pain or an ache came to her , seeking her blessing and soliciting her prayers , and whenas she stroked him , he was made whole of his ailment", "context_len": 86}}}, "administration": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 66, "context": "And these suspicions were confirmed when , after you had been carried away and imprisoned , my father began to mend , even before the arrival of Mafuta upon the scene , while it seemed extraordinary to me that the witch-doctor should know so well the character of my father 's ailment , that he was able to bring with him precisely the right remedies for administration", "context_len": 67}}}, "intercede": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "I tried to intercede for you , not once but many times ; but my father had suffered horribly , and had been terribly frightened", "context_len": 25}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` O Bertric , Bertric ! '' he cried , `` intercede for me , pray for me", "context_len": 18}}}, "interceding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "He was still dreadfully angry with me for interceding in your behalf , but I persisted ; and at length he told me that if I did not wish to witness the Customs I might remain at home , and of course I did so , although I knew that you were not to suffer until to-morrow", "context_len": 57}}}, "stratagem": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 1, "context": "By stratagem they drew them from their island , with all the canoes in their possession , to a place where others lay in ambush for them , engaged them in battle , took away their canoes to pass over to the island , and destroyed there all the property of the tribe", "context_len": 53}}}, "paddlers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "As we sat down to partake , of it together , Ama informed me that one of her father 's largest canoes , manned by forty paddlers , and commanded by a chief whom she had recognised , had passed slowly down the river about an hour after I had composed myself to rest , the chief in charge intently scrutinising both banks , as they went , evidently in search of some indication of our presence , and had finally passed out of sight to the southward ; after which Ama had descended and taken a few hours ' rest", "context_len": 101}}}, "execrated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "The girl was silent so long that I was obliged to repeat my question before I could get an answer , and when at length she replied , I feared I could detect tears in her voice , and could have execrated myself for a stony-hearted wretch", "context_len": 47}}}, "unspeakable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 29, "context": "It was clear to me that this unsophisticated young savage had been dominated in her actions by one idea alone , that of saving me from a death of unspeakable horror ; she knew that , in so doing , she was cutting herself off for ever from her own people , to whom it would be impossible for her to return ; and , in her absolute simplicity , she had evidently thought that it would be the easiest thing in the world for her to throw in her lot with mine", "context_len": 93}}}, "undeceive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "How was I to undeceive her ; how make her understand the absolute impossibility of such a thing ? I greatly feared that to convince her of this would be wholly beyond my power", "context_len": 34}}}, "insoluble": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The problem seemed absolutely insoluble ; and at length I came to the conclusion that the only thing to be done was to leave the issue to destiny", "context_len": 28}}}, "mangrove": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "There was nothing of an alarming character in sight , and therefore , as soon as I had descended to the ground , we both set out for the spot where we had hidden our canoe , launched her , and made our way out through the labyrinth of mangrove roots to the margin of the river , where we lay perdu until the darkness had completely fallen , when we boldly pushed off into the strength of the current , and steadily pursued our way", "context_len": 86}}}, "wherewithal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 98, "context": "She further mentioned that , as she felt sure her father would have despatched a party in pursuit of us , which , she expected , would by this time be , not far behind us , it would be very desirable to keep a watch for them , since it was important that we should know as much as possible of their movements ; and she accordingly suggested that I should climb a particularly lofty tree which she indicated , and keep a look-out for them , while she went off into the forest to seek the wherewithal to furnish a breakfast", "context_len": 103}}}, "shin": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "As Ama , taking her bow and arrows , tripped lightly away toward the forest , I proceeded to shin up the tree , and presently , after some labour , found myself among its topmost branches , which towered high above those of all the other trees in the neighbourhood , and -- it being by this time broad daylight -- obtained a magnificent view of the surrounding country , extending for , as I estimated , some forty miles toward the south and east , while toward the north and west the view was shut off by high hills , through which the river wound its way", "context_len": 109}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The young skipper made his way forward to where Crayle sat binding a handkerchief around his grazed shin", "context_len": 18}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He \u2019 s a corking half-back , observed a fellow who wore shin guards", "context_len": 14}}}, "topmost": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "As Ama , taking her bow and arrows , tripped lightly away toward the forest , I proceeded to shin up the tree , and presently , after some labour , found myself among its topmost branches , which towered high above those of all the other trees in the neighbourhood , and -- it being by this time broad daylight -- obtained a magnificent view of the surrounding country , extending for , as I estimated , some forty miles toward the south and east , while toward the north and west the view was shut off by high hills , through which the river wound its way", "context_len": 109}}}, "unobstructed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "To my surprise I found that our camping-place was much nearer the river than I had supposed , and I was thus able to obtain a clear and unobstructed view of its surface for many miles north and south , except a width of a few yards on its eastern side , which was shut off by the mangroves and low scrub which grew along its margin", "context_len": 67}}}, "mangroves": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 58, "context": "To my surprise I found that our camping-place was much nearer the river than I had supposed , and I was thus able to obtain a clear and unobstructed view of its surface for many miles north and south , except a width of a few yards on its eastern side , which was shut off by the mangroves and low scrub which grew along its margin", "context_len": 67}}}, "mow": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "She did not call to me , or announce her return in any way , but set to work to mow a circle of about ten feet in diameter in the long grass ; and then , having produced fire by rubbing two pieces of wood together , she proceeded very carefully to burn off the short grass left inside the circle , setting fire to it , allowing it to burn for a few seconds , and then beating it out again with a branch , in order that the fire might not spread and burn us out , to say nothing of betraying our presence by the smoke that it would raise", "context_len": 114}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 28, "context": "Both men held him , and , in spite of his struggles , the manacles were snapped upon his wrists ; while Jerry , still sitting on the mow , pleaded and sobbed and wrung his hands , the little dog vainly seeking to soothe him by trying to lick his face", "context_len": 52}}}, "immeasurably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "It must have been about four o'clock in the afternoon , judging from the position of the sun , when I awoke to find Ama crouching over the fire , busily preparing another meal which -- even as I rose and stretched myself luxuriously , feeling immeasurably refreshed and invigorated by my long sleep -- she pronounced ready", "context_len": 58}}}, "invigorated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "It must have been about four o'clock in the afternoon , judging from the position of the sun , when I awoke to find Ama crouching over the fire , busily preparing another meal which -- even as I rose and stretched myself luxuriously , feeling immeasurably refreshed and invigorated by my long sleep -- she pronounced ready", "context_len": 58}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "' I feel refreshed and invigorated", "context_len": 6}}}, "awaking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "She further stated that , upon awaking , she had again gone aloft to take another long and careful look round , but had seen nothing more of our pursuers , and was therefore inclined to believe that we were now reasonably safe , provided , of course , that we did all our travelling at night , kept a sharp look-out , and were careful not to allow ourselves to be fallen in with by other users of the river", "context_len": 81}}}, "users": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 77, "context": "She further stated that , upon awaking , she had again gone aloft to take another long and careful look round , but had seen nothing more of our pursuers , and was therefore inclined to believe that we were now reasonably safe , provided , of course , that we did all our travelling at night , kept a sharp look-out , and were careful not to allow ourselves to be fallen in with by other users of the river", "context_len": 81}}}, "tragically": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "And then what was to become of poor Ama , my gentle and loving companion ? Alas , destiny was soon to answer that question , and most tragically , too , had we but known it", "context_len": 37}}}, "foresight": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The sagacity and foresight which these statesmen evinced in these suggestions were abundantly confirmed in the end", "context_len": 17}}}, "meridian": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 74, "context": "Here Captain Duquesne secured an excellent departure by means of three carefully taken bearings of the cape , observed at intervals of two hours , by means of which he was able to establish our position on the chart with the utmost accuracy ; and , this done , we held on a westerly course , the skipper 's intention being not to haul up to the northward until he had arrived at the meridian of 20 west longitude , lest he should fall in with any of the cruisers of the Slave Squadron", "context_len": 94}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The line of demarcation separating the two `` nations , '' and following the meridian , is not , however , of a binding character , for individuals of both peoples settle east or west of it , wherever they please ( G", "context_len": 43}}}, "wakefulness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "I had been sound asleep for some hours , for the sun was well past the meridian , when I was startled into sudden and complete wakefulness , and sprang up with the sensation that I had heard Ama screaming and calling upon me for help", "context_len": 46}}}, "divest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Yet I could not divest my mind of the conviction that my abrupt awakening had been caused by a cry for help from Ama having reached my ears ; and , seizing my weapons , I set out in search of her", "context_len": 42}}}, "glade": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 2, "context": "A grassy glade under a tall beech seemed intended by Nature for a summer-house , for there was a large flat stone in the middle , which served for a table , and a circle of little stones round , just high enough for seats , so that you might imagine Queen Mab and her fairies dined here on moonlight nights , with the squirrels for guests and the bats and owls for waiters", "context_len": 74}}}, "streamlet": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 54, "context": "The `` form '' in the grass where she had lain was plain enough to the sight , as also were her tracks in the direction of the forest , and these I followed for some distance without much difficulty , coming out at length into an open glade , through which a tiny streamlet made its way", "context_len": 58}}}, "outcrop": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "And , leading away from this , there was a broad , blood-stained trail , as though a body had been dragged along through the grass and over some rocky ground , further on , toward another and much bigger outcrop of rock", "context_len": 43}}}, "granite": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "On the one side there was the open sea , on the other the piled-up granite , which rose up like hand-built buttresses , composed of vast squared masses rising tier upon tier", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 56, "context": "But as it happened , the warmth of the wrapping , the influence of the spirit that had been poured liberally down his throat , and the chafing , combined with his naturally strong animal power to revive him from the state of insensibility into which he had fallen , and long before they reached the granite pier of the little harbour his eyes had opened , and he was staring in a peculiarly puzzled way at Mark Penelly , who still knelt beside him in the double character of medical man and nurse", "context_len": 94}}}, "male": {"freq": 20, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "For a hundred yards or more the trail led over uneven rocky ground toward an immense rock , upon rounding which I found myself face to face with , and within half-a-dozen yards of , a splendid full-grown male leopard who was crouching over poor Ama 's motionless body , snarling savagely as he strove with his claws to remove a broken spear , the head of which was buried deep in his neck", "context_len": 74}, "NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 81, "context": "The genie immediately appeared to him and said , `` Seek what thou wilt , O my lord ; '' whereupon quoth Alaeddin , `` I seek the Sultan 's daughter in marriage and he requireth of me forty dishes of pure gold , each ten pounds in weight and full of the jewels which be in the garden of the treasure , the forty dishes to be borne by forty slave girls and each slave-girl to be accompanied by a male slave ; wherefore I will have thee bring me this , all of it", "context_len": 96}}}, "motionlessly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Never in my life had I felt more deadly cool and self-possessed than I did then as I aimed steadily at the animal 's right eye ; I felt that I could not miss ; nor did I ; for while we thus stood motionlessly staring at each other , I released the string , and the next instant the great lithe beast sprang convulsively into the air , with the butt of my arrow protruding from his eye and the point buried deep in his brain", "context_len": 87}}}, "hyaenas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 72, "context": "My first impulse was to carry the body down to where the soil was softer , and there dig a grave for it ; but while I was considering this plan , it occurred to me that , with no more efficient tool than a spear to serve as a shovel , it would be practically impossible for me to bury the body deep enough to protect it from the jackals and hyaenas ; and I therefore determined that , instead of burying it , I would burn it", "context_len": 89}}}, "pyre": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 14, "context": "I was doing a most imprudent thing to linger by the side of the pyre , for the smoke , in the first place , and the light of the flames when it fell dark , could scarcely have failed to attract to the spot any savages who might have been in the neighbourhood , when my plight would probably have been as bad as ever ; but at that moment my sorrow at the loss of my companion overcame every other feeling , and , for the moment at least , I was quite indifferent as to what befell me", "context_len": 101}}}, "impalpable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "There was an abundance of fallen boughs and twigs in the adjacent jungle to enable me to build a funeral pyre ; and I should have the melancholy satisfaction of actually watching the reduction of the body to impalpable ashes", "context_len": 40}}}, "combustible": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 69, "context": "From this land Ben had hauled his spars , and logs for boards , leaving the tops of the trees and all the brush ; in addition to this there was left quite a growth of other trees , that were not fit for timber ; these he and Joe had cut early in the spring , so that the soil was completely covered with a dense mass of combustible matter , as dry as tinder", "context_len": 76}}}, "incineration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "It took me the remainder of the day to collect as much dry and combustible material as I considered would be needful to accomplish the complete incineration of the body , and to build the pyre ; but it was done at last ; and then , once more raising the corpse in my arms , I gently placed it on the top", "context_len": 63}}}, "ignited": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "Father , however , getting to hear of the project , forbade it so emphatically that the disgusted aeronaut was obliged to give way , and consoled himself by constructing a fire-balloon out of gay strips of pink and green tissue-paper , which , ignited by methylated spirits , was to be set off with great effect on Peggy 's birthday", "context_len": 61}}}, "wreathing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 41, "context": "Aunt Patty 's home was a pretty , snug cottage on the side of a hill ; its front covered with a beautiful trumpet creeper , which went climbing up to the very top of the many-cornered old chimney , and wreathing itself over the little porch and the bow window of the sitting-room , until the house looked like a quiet green nest", "context_len": 64}}}, "pelt": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 80, "context": "Meanwhile , since I was almost naked , and was hoping soon to find myself once more among civilised people , it occurred to me that the skin of the leopard which had wrought this dire tragedy might be of use to me as material out of which to fashion some sort of a garment ; and , therefore , while the flames of the pyre were still blazing brilliantly I utilised their light to enable me to strip the pelt off the great carcase", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The old man continued to splutter and snarl and flourish his cane , upon which Tuttle and Cooper made a pretense of skurrying around in great haste for rocks to pelt him with , and he beat a hasty retreat toward his wretched hovel", "context_len": 44}}}, "desecrated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "When the fire had entirely died down , and I had satisfied myself that there was nothing left of poor Ama to be desecrated by fang of beast or beak of bird , I sorrowfully retired from the fatal spot , carrying the leopard 's skin with me , and making my way with some difficulty to the place where the canoe lay concealed , sprang in and shoved off", "context_len": 70}}}, "fang": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "When the fire had entirely died down , and I had satisfied myself that there was nothing left of poor Ama to be desecrated by fang of beast or beak of bird , I sorrowfully retired from the fatal spot , carrying the leopard 's skin with me , and making my way with some difficulty to the place where the canoe lay concealed , sprang in and shoved off", "context_len": 70}}}, "brigantine": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "But where on earth have you sprung from ? From that fine brigantine that has just come in , I imagine , since I see that the boat which brought you is returning to her ; but I mean before that", "context_len": 41}}}, "choosers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "It did not need a second look at her to tell me that she was a slaver ; but beggars must not be choosers", "context_len": 24}}}, "shipment": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "It appeared that he was ashore at the moment making arrangements for the shipment of a cargo of slaves on the next day ; but the chief mate was aboard , and upon representing myself to him as a shipwrecked Englishman who had been carried away captive into the interior , and had just effected my escape , he gave me permission to remain , saying that he had no doubt Captain Duquesne would receive me if I were willing to work my passage to Martinique", "context_len": 86}}}, "domiciled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 78, "context": "Some hours later the captain returned , and upon my repeating to him the yarn which I had spun to the mate he not only very readily consented to my working my passage , but also offered me two excellent suits of clothes , two shirts , two pairs of stockings , a pair of shoes , and a worsted cap in exchange for my leopard-skin , which offer I gladly accepted ; and that night found me domiciled in the forecastle of L'Esperance as one of her crew", "context_len": 89}}}, "inaugurated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Naturally enough , having heard that I had been a captive among the savages , they insisted upon my relating to them my adventures ; and this inaugurated an evening of yarn-spinning in the forecastle , the incidents related having reference for the most part to the slave-trade", "context_len": 48}}}, "appropriately": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 12, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}}}, "grog": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}}}, "circulating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Thanks to his last robbery , he and his companion were tolerably well supplied with gold-dust , which was a common circulating medium in California at that time", "context_len": 28}}}, "slavers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 75, "context": "There was one grizzled old scoundrel , in particular , nicknamed -- appropriately enough , no doubt -- '' Red Hand , '' who was full of reminiscence and anecdote ; and by-and-by , when the grog had been circulating for some time , he made mention of the names Virginia and Preciosa , at which I pricked up my ears ; for I remembered at once that those were the names of the two slavers that our own and the American Government were so anxious to lay by the heels , and which had hitherto baffled all our efforts and laughed at our most carefully laid plans", "context_len": 108}}}, "suppression": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "And , in like manner , if an American cruiser happened to fall in with her , she showed Spanish colours , mustered her Spanish crew on deck , and produced her Spanish papers for inspection if she were boarded , there being no treaty between America and Spain for the suppression of slavery", "context_len": 54}}}, "notoriously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Peter Grimm , notoriously the stingiest man in Pleasantville , who raised the sourest apples in the town and spent most of his time watching the boys and picking up what fruit rolled outside of the fence , bided his time with watchful ferret eyes until a promising-looking package came along", "context_len": 51}}}, "ninth": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "But , as luck would have it , the weather fell still lighter at sunset on our ninth day out ; and on the following morning at daybreak we found ourselves becalmed within three miles of a British cruiser , which promptly lowered her boats and despatched them to overhaul us ; and by breakfast-time I had the pleasure of finding myself once more under the British flag , our captor proving to be the corvette Cleopatra , by the captain and officers of which I was most kindly received when I had related to them my strange story", "context_len": 99}}}, "corvette": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 75, "context": "But , as luck would have it , the weather fell still lighter at sunset on our ninth day out ; and on the following morning at daybreak we found ourselves becalmed within three miles of a British cruiser , which promptly lowered her boats and despatched them to overhaul us ; and by breakfast-time I had the pleasure of finding myself once more under the British flag , our captor proving to be the corvette Cleopatra , by the captain and officers of which I was most kindly received when I had related to them my strange story", "context_len": 99}}}, "foundered": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "But just then , the thoughts of every one in the house except mother ( and the news was carefully kept from her ) , were full of a great and terrible catastrophe , and even I forgot all about the Duchess , for one of our largest Orient liners had foundered on some sunken rocks not far from Port Adelaide , off the coast of South Australia , and there had been a terrific shipwreck , and almost every one on board was drowned", "context_len": 85}}}, "dunnage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Meanwhile , since you seem to have returned to us flying light , without any ` dunnage , ' I would recommend you to get hold of the ship 's tailor and see what he can do for you in the matter of knocking you up a uniform", "context_len": 48}}}, "therefrom": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 83, "context": "it in detail , of how , after parting from the Eros , we had carried on in the hope of overtaking the Virginia ; of how we had been caught in and overwhelmed by the hurricane ; of how I came to go adrift , alone , in the longboat ; of how I had been run down by La Mouette , and of my treatment on board her ; of my adventures in King Banda 's town , and my escape therefrom with the aid of poor Ama ; of the death of the latter -- at which all hands expressed their sincere regret ; and , finally , of how I had reached L'Esperance , and the extraordinary story I had heard while aboard her", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 36, "context": "The Sultan , of his love and affection for his daughter the Lady Bedrulbudour , was wont , every day , when he awoke from his sleep , to open the window and look at her therefrom ; so he arose on the morrow , according to his wont , and opened his chamber-window , so he might see his daughter ; but [ 571 ] when he put out his head and looked for Alaeddin 's palace , he beheld nothing but a place swept [ and level ] , like as it was aforetime , and saw neither palace nor inhabitants ; [ 572 ] whereat amazement clad him and his wit was bewildered and he fell to rubbing his eyes , so haply they were", "context_len": 128}}}, "elucidation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "It is not to be supposed that I was allowed to spin my yarn without interruption ; on the contrary , I was bombarded with a continuous fire of questions for the elucidation of points that I had failed to make quite clear ; and when I had finished the captain was pleased to express himself as perfectly satisfied with all that I had done , and that the loss of the Dolphin was due to causes entirely beyond my control", "context_len": 81}}}, "garrulity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Ah ! '' ejaculated the skipper , with a deep sigh of satisfaction ; `` thanks to your friend Red Hand 's garrulity in his cups , Mr Fortescue , we shall now know how to deal with that precious craft", "context_len": 42}}}, "animatedly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "Bart could see by their gestures that they were animatedly discussing the situation , and they seemed to be closely looking over the ground with a view to locating a site for a new express shed", "context_len": 36}}}, "authentic": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 53, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "shoals": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 47, "context": "At first sight nothing seems more frail than a herring or mackerel net , one of those slight pieces of mesh-work that , in a continuation of lengths perhaps half-a-mile long , is let down into the sea to float with the tide , ready for the shoals of fish that dart against it as it forms a filmy wall across their way", "context_len": 63}}}, "supposititious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The skipper therefore carefully laid down upon his chart the supposititious course of the Virginia from the Gaboon to Cape Palmas , and thence onward to the Caribbean Sea ; and then shaped a course to enable us to fall in with her on the latter , at a spot about one hundred miles to the westward of Palmas", "context_len": 59}}}, "spanker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Having reached this spot , we shortened sail to our three topsails , spanker , and jib , and slowly worked to windward along that course , tacking every two hours until we had worked up to within sight of the cape , and then bearing up and running off to leeward for a distance of one hundred miles again , keeping a hand aloft on the main-royal yard as look-out from dawn to dark", "context_len": 75}}}, "jib": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": "Having reached this spot , we shortened sail to our three topsails , spanker , and jib , and slowly worked to windward along that course , tacking every two hours until we had worked up to within sight of the cape , and then bearing up and running off to leeward for a distance of one hundred miles again , keeping a hand aloft on the main-royal yard as look-out from dawn to dark", "context_len": 75}}}, "insinuations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "But Captain Perry , having carefully thought the whole thing out , stuck to his guns , refusing to budge an inch from his original arrangement , in response to the hints and insinuations of those who disagreed with him", "context_len": 40}}}, "soundness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "Though John Cassell died comparatively young -- he was only forty-eight when his death took place in 1865 -- he had done a grand life 's work ; and the soundness of his judgment is shown by the fact that works which he planned retain their hold upon the people to this day", "context_len": 53}}}, "longish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` Well , sir , '' answered I , `` it is a longish stretch , I admit", "context_len": 18}}}, "rarefaction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The rarefaction of the air bothered me a good deal , producing something of the effect of a mirage , and causing the royals of the distant vessel to stand up clear of the horizon as though there were nothing beneath them ; yet , as she rose and fell with the 'scend of the sea , shapeless snow- white blotches appeared and vanished again beneath them occasionally", "context_len": 68}}}, "mirage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The rarefaction of the air bothered me a good deal , producing something of the effect of a mirage , and causing the royals of the distant vessel to stand up clear of the horizon as though there were nothing beneath them ; yet , as she rose and fell with the 'scend of the sea , shapeless snow- white blotches appeared and vanished again beneath them occasionally", "context_len": 68}}}, "topgallant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And swinging myself on to the topgallant backstay , I slid rapidly down to the deck", "context_len": 16}}}, "backstay": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "And swinging myself on to the topgallant backstay , I slid rapidly down to the deck", "context_len": 16}}}, "shearing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "In a moment all was bustle ; the watch below tumbled up to lend a hand without waiting to be called ; and in five minutes the noble ship was clothed with canvas from her trucks down , and shearing through the deep blue water with her lee channels buried", "context_len": 50}}}, "instructing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "She was by this time dead ahead of us ; and the skipper thereupon gave orders to bear away four points and set the larboard studdingsails ; at the same time instructing the look-out to give us instant warning of any change in the stranger 's course or amount of sail set", "context_len": 52}}}, "soaring": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "In a few minutes more we had lifted the heads of her courses above the horizon , still edging away and keeping her about four points on our port bow ; and presently , as we watched her , we saw the Stars and Stripes go soaring up to her gaff-end", "context_len": 51}}}, "conning": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "Not to be outdone in politeness , we hoisted our colours also ; and for the next quarter of an hour the two craft continued to close , the chase stolidly maintaining her course , while we , under the skipper 's skilful conning , continued to edge very gradually away , as the other vessel sped to leeward , checking our weather braces by a few inches at a time until our yards were all but square", "context_len": 78}}}, "forefoot": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Still , at sea there is always the possibility of an accident , therefore as soon as we were near enough the captain gave orders to clear away the bow gun and pitch a shot across the fellow 's forefoot , as a hint that we wanted to have a talk to him", "context_len": 53}}}, "veer": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` All hands out of the boat , and veer her away astern ! '' ordered I as we swept alongside ; and the next moment I and my party were over the rail and on deck", "context_len": 37}}}, "fooling": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` I acknowledge that you did the trick very handsomely when I boarded you on a former occasion ; but there is going to be no fooling this time I assure you", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Let a fellow alone , ca n't you ? Quit fooling ! This is no freshman 's room ! ''", "context_len": 21}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "FOOLING THE ENEMY", "context_len": 3}}}, "goldarned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Well , I 'll be goldarned ! '' exclaimed the man , suddenly recognising me", "context_len": 16}}}, "jigger": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` If it ai n't the young Britisher that -- jigger my buttons if I did n't think I 'd seen yer before , stranger", "context_len": 25}}}, "registry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 53, "context": "So saying , I descended the poop ladder and , entering the cabin , made my way to the skipper 's state-room , and , opening a desk which I found there , soon discovered the genuine set of papers declaring the ship 's name to be the Preciosa , her port of registry Havana , and her ownership Spanish", "context_len": 60}}}, "reversible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "Her Spanish crew we soon found snugly hidden away in spacious quarters beneath the lazaret ; and , as to the name on her stern , we found that the piece of wood on which it was carved and painted was reversible , having Virginia , New Orleans , carved on one side of it and Preciosa , Havana , on the other , and that it could be unbolted and reversed in a few minutes by lifting a couple of movable planks in the after cabin", "context_len": 87}}}, "movable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 81, "context": "Her Spanish crew we soon found snugly hidden away in spacious quarters beneath the lazaret ; and , as to the name on her stern , we found that the piece of wood on which it was carved and painted was reversible , having Virginia , New Orleans , carved on one side of it and Preciosa , Havana , on the other , and that it could be unbolted and reversed in a few minutes by lifting a couple of movable planks in the after cabin", "context_len": 87}}}, "males": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 16, "context": "She had a full cargo , consisting of seven hundred and thirty negroes , all young males , on board ; and as she was a remarkably fast and well-built ship she was a prize worth having , to say nothing of the credit that we should win by putting a stop to her vagaries", "context_len": 55}}}, "adjudicated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "In due course she was adjudicated upon and condemned by the Mixed Commission ; but I did not remain at Sierra Leone for that to take place ; for upon our arrival we found that a packet had come in from England a few days previously bringing letters for me , acquainting me with the sad news of my father 's death and urging me to proceed home immediately to supervise the winding up of his affairs , and to assume the management of the very important property that he had left behind him", "context_len": 94}}}, "supervise": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 70, "context": "In due course she was adjudicated upon and condemned by the Mixed Commission ; but I did not remain at Sierra Leone for that to take place ; for upon our arrival we found that a packet had come in from England a few days previously bringing letters for me , acquainting me with the sad news of my father 's death and urging me to proceed home immediately to supervise the winding up of his affairs , and to assume the management of the very important property that he had left behind him", "context_len": 94}}}, "philadelphia": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "PHILADELPHIA : D", "context_len": 3}}}, "watershed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The tribes who inhabited the watershed of the north shore of the Mexican Gulf must always occupy a prominent place in the study of American Ethnology , as possibly connecting the races of North and South America , and those of the Valley of the Mississippi with those of Anahuac and Mayapan", "context_len": 52}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The Cheroki , or more correctly , Ts\u00e1lagi nation is essentially a hill people ; their numerous settlements were divided into two great sections by the watershed ridge of the Alleghany mountains , in their language Un\u00e9ga kat\u00fasi ( `` white , whitish mountains '' ) , of which even now a portion is called `` Smoky Mountains", "context_len": 58}}}, "ethnography": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 23, "context": "It is wholly legendary , in its first portion even mythical ; it is of a comparatively remote age , exceedingly instructive for ethnography and for the development of religious ideas ; it is full of that sort of na\u00efvet\u00e9 which we like so much to meet in the mental productions of our aborigines , and affords striking instances of the debasing and brutalizing influence of the unrestricted belief in the supernatural and miraculous", "context_len": 74}}}, "ethnology": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "The present publication proposes to bring before the public , in popular form , some scientific results obtained while studying the language and ethnology of the Creek tribe and its ethnic congeners", "context_len": 32}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Language is not only the most general and important help to ethnology , but outside of race , it is also the most ancient of all ; ethnologists are well aware of this fact , but do not generally apply it to their studies , because they find it too tedious to acquire the language of unlettered tribes by staying long enough among them", "context_len": 64}}}, "ethnic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "At a public council held at Savannah , in May 1733 , a Lower Creek chief from Kaw\u00edta expressed the hope that the Y\u00e1massi may be in time reunited to his people ; a fact which fully proves the ethnic affinity of the two national bodies", "context_len": 46}}}, "congeners": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The present publication proposes to bring before the public , in popular form , some scientific results obtained while studying the language and ethnology of the Creek tribe and its ethnic congeners", "context_len": 32}}}, "furthering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The method of furthering ethnographic study by all the means which the study of language can afford , has been too little appreciated up to the present time , but has been constantly kept in view in this publication", "context_len": 39}}}, "ethnographic": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 12, "context": "Among the tribes of the Maskoki family , we notice the following ethnographic practices : the use of the red and white colors as symbols of war and peace , an extensive system of totemic gentes , the use of the Ilex cassine for the manufacture of the black drink , the erection of artificial mounds , the belief in a deity called `` Master of Life , '' and original sun-worship", "context_len": 72}}}, "ethnologists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Language is not only the most general and important help to ethnology , but outside of race , it is also the most ancient of all ; ethnologists are well aware of this fact , but do not generally apply it to their studies , because they find it too tedious to acquire the language of unlettered tribes by staying long enough among them", "context_len": 64}}}, "unlettered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 56, "context": "Language is not only the most general and important help to ethnology , but outside of race , it is also the most ancient of all ; ethnologists are well aware of this fact , but do not generally apply it to their studies , because they find it too tedious to acquire the language of unlettered tribes by staying long enough among them", "context_len": 64}}}, "linguistic": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` The Hellenic , especially Doric tradition of an immigration from Thrace and Macedonia through Epirus and Thessalia into Greece is confirmed by linguistic and historic facts , but the Roman legend concerning the descent of the founders of the `` Eternal City '' from Troy was acknowledged to be a pious fraud by the ancients themselves", "context_len": 57}}}, "dialects": {"freq": 53, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 52, "word_index": 7, "context": "There are terms which occur in all dialects in the same or nearly the same form , as h\u00e1si sun , \u00edtchu , \u00edssi deer , \u00f3fi , \u00edfa dog , the terms for chief , black , yellow , bird , snake , buffalo , turtle , fox ( also in Cheroki : tsu'hl\u00e1 ) , the numerals and the personal pronouns ; they must , therefore , have been once the common property of the still undivided , primordial tribe", "context_len": 82}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "What the Maskoki dialects presently spoken , as far as published , have in common , may be stated in a general way in the following outlines :", "context_len": 28}}}, "phonetically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The majority of them are laid down in phonetically defective missionary alphabets , about which we are prompted to repeat what the citizens of the young colony of Mexico wrote to the government of Spain , in Cortez 's time : `` Send to us pious and Christian men , as preachers , bishops and missionaries , but do not send us scholars , who , with their pettifogging distinctions and love of contention , create nothing but disorder and strife", "context_len": 81}}}, "alphabets": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The majority of them are laid down in phonetically defective missionary alphabets , about which we are prompted to repeat what the citizens of the young colony of Mexico wrote to the government of Spain , in Cortez 's time : `` Send to us pious and Christian men , as preachers , bishops and missionaries , but do not send us scholars , who , with their pettifogging distinctions and love of contention , create nothing but disorder and strife", "context_len": 81}}}, "bishops": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 53, "context": "The majority of them are laid down in phonetically defective missionary alphabets , about which we are prompted to repeat what the citizens of the young colony of Mexico wrote to the government of Spain , in Cortez 's time : `` Send to us pious and Christian men , as preachers , bishops and missionaries , but do not send us scholars , who , with their pettifogging distinctions and love of contention , create nothing but disorder and strife", "context_len": 81}}}, "pettifogging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 68, "context": "The majority of them are laid down in phonetically defective missionary alphabets , about which we are prompted to repeat what the citizens of the young colony of Mexico wrote to the government of Spain , in Cortez 's time : `` Send to us pious and Christian men , as preachers , bishops and missionaries , but do not send us scholars , who , with their pettifogging distinctions and love of contention , create nothing but disorder and strife", "context_len": 81}}}, "contention": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 73, "context": "The majority of them are laid down in phonetically defective missionary alphabets , about which we are prompted to repeat what the citizens of the young colony of Mexico wrote to the government of Spain , in Cortez 's time : `` Send to us pious and Christian men , as preachers , bishops and missionaries , but do not send us scholars , who , with their pettifogging distinctions and love of contention , create nothing but disorder and strife", "context_len": 81}}}, "churchmen": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` [ 1 ] In the same manner , some Creek scholars and churchmen agreed five times in succession , before 1853 , upon standard alphabets to be followed in transcribing Creek , but , as Judge G", "context_len": 38}}}, "transcribing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The Spanish orthography , in which these names are laid down , is unfitted for transcribing Indian languages , perhaps as much so as the English orthography ; nevertheless , we recognize the frequently-occurring terminal -esta , -sta , which sounds quite like Timucua", "context_len": 44}}}, "statements": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 4, "context": "The document contained two statements , one written in 1846 , mentioning that Sir John Franklin and all were well ; and a second , written in 1848 , to say that they had been obliged to abandon the Erebus and Terror , that Sir John Franklin had died in June , 1847 , and that they had already lost nine officers and fifteen men", "context_len": 65}}}, "migration": {"freq": 30, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 30, "word_index": 27, "context": "The spreading out of the people from the Tallapoosa river to the Chatahutchi and from there to the Savannah must have involved a warfare , struggling , migration and settling down of several centuries , for the advance of the Maskoki proper in this direction was tantamount to the formation of the Maskoki confederacy by subduing or incorporating the tribes standing in their way , and to the still more lengthy process of settling among them", "context_len": 76}}}, "aborigines": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 53, "context": "It is wholly legendary , in its first portion even mythical ; it is of a comparatively remote age , exceedingly instructive for ethnography and for the development of religious ideas ; it is full of that sort of na\u00efvet\u00e9 which we like so much to meet in the mental productions of our aborigines , and affords striking instances of the debasing and brutalizing influence of the unrestricted belief in the supernatural and miraculous", "context_len": 74}}}, "debasing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 61, "context": "It is wholly legendary , in its first portion even mythical ; it is of a comparatively remote age , exceedingly instructive for ethnography and for the development of religious ideas ; it is full of that sort of na\u00efvet\u00e9 which we like so much to meet in the mental productions of our aborigines , and affords striking instances of the debasing and brutalizing influence of the unrestricted belief in the supernatural and miraculous", "context_len": 74}}}, "brutalizing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 63, "context": "It is wholly legendary , in its first portion even mythical ; it is of a comparatively remote age , exceedingly instructive for ethnography and for the development of religious ideas ; it is full of that sort of na\u00efvet\u00e9 which we like so much to meet in the mental productions of our aborigines , and affords striking instances of the debasing and brutalizing influence of the unrestricted belief in the supernatural and miraculous", "context_len": 74}}}, "unrestricted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 79, "context": "But in carrying a city by storm , which takes place usually at an unexpected time , and often in the night , the maddened and victorious assaulter suddenly burst into the sacred scenes of domestic peace , and seclusion , and love -- the very worst of men , filled with the worst of passions , stimulated by the resistance they have encountered , and licensed by their victory to give all these passions the fullest and most unrestricted gratification", "context_len": 81}}}, "underlies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Of the sun-worship , which underlies the religions of all the tribes in the Gulf territories , only slight intimations are contained in the Kasi'hta legend , and the important problem , whether the Creeks ever crossed the Mississippi river from west to east in their migrations , seems to be settled by it in the negative , although other legends may be adduced as speaking in its favor", "context_len": 69}}}, "territories": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 15, "context": "Of the sun-worship , which underlies the religions of all the tribes in the Gulf territories , only slight intimations are contained in the Kasi'hta legend , and the important problem , whether the Creeks ever crossed the Mississippi river from west to east in their migrations , seems to be settled by it in the negative , although other legends may be adduced as speaking in its favor", "context_len": 69}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Among the various nationalities of the Gulf territories the Maskoki family of tribes occupied a central and commanding position", "context_len": 19}}}, "intimations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Of the sun-worship , which underlies the religions of all the tribes in the Gulf territories , only slight intimations are contained in the Kasi'hta legend , and the important problem , whether the Creeks ever crossed the Mississippi river from west to east in their migrations , seems to be settled by it in the negative , although other legends may be adduced as speaking in its favor", "context_len": 69}}}, "migrations": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 46, "context": "Of the sun-worship , which underlies the religions of all the tribes in the Gulf territories , only slight intimations are contained in the Kasi'hta legend , and the important problem , whether the Creeks ever crossed the Mississippi river from west to east in their migrations , seems to be settled by it in the negative , although other legends may be adduced as speaking in its favor", "context_len": 69}}}, "adduced": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Of which country ? If the whole country is meant , which was at the dawn of history held by Maskoki tribes , the name of the Yazoo river may be adduced as an argument for the truth of this tradition , for yasu , yashu is the Yuchi term for leaf and any leaf-bearing tree , even pines ( from y\u00e1 , wood , tree ) , and Kaw\u00edta has been mentioned above", "context_len": 75}}}, "deficient": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "The American Indian as a rule is deficient in muscular development , but this one showed several moderate ridges that doubled and shifted in response to the rhythmic swinging of his arms", "context_len": 32}}}, "conjugational": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Buckner 's Creek Grammar , with its numerous defects , I have extracted but a few conjugational forms of the verb isita to take , but have availed myself of some linguistic manuscripts of Mrs", "context_len": 35}}}, "manuscripts": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 22, "context": "It seemed to contain papers of some kind , and so many bidders had bought old deeds , contracts , plans , manuscripts and the like , utterly valueless to them , that the lot hung at twenty-five cents for several minutes", "context_len": 42}}}, "translator": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` This ( or a similar text ) is evidently the original of Galland 's translation of this episode and it is probable , therefore , that the French translator inserted the mention `` of a certain warm drink '' ( tea ) , out of that mistaken desire for local colouring at all costs which has led so many French authors ( especially those of our own immediate day ) astray", "context_len": 72}}}, "emigration": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 25, "context": "Many old traditions of historic importance must have been embodied in these records ; but the only one given by Milfort , referring to the emigration of the Creeks from their ancient cave-homes along Red river , is so mixed up with incredible matter , that the fixation of the events , as far as then remembered , must have taken place many generations after the arrival of the Creeks in their Alabama homes", "context_len": 74}}}, "revisions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "My heartfelt thanks are also due to other Indians , who have materially helped me in my repeated revisions of the subject matter embodied in these volumes , and in other investigations", "context_len": 32}}}, "delegates": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "The Spaniards established in Northern Florida paid honors to the `` emperor of the Cowetas , '' therewith hoping to influence all the Lower and Upper Creeks , and in 1710 received Kaw\u00edta delegates with distinction at St", "context_len": 38}}}, "libraries": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "copies of the Thousand and One Nights known to exist in the public libraries of Europe Might yet cast some light upon the origin of the interpolated tales ; but , in view of the strong presumption afforded by internal evidence that they are of modern composition and form no part of the authentic text , it can hardly be expected , where the result and the value of that result are alike so doubtful , that any competent person will be found to undertake so heavy a task , except as incidental to some more general enquiry", "context_len": 98}}}, "posterity": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "This also shows the process , how historic legends and traditions are forming among uncultured nations , which are possessed of imperfect means only for the transmission of ideas to posterity", "context_len": 31}}}, "exhaustively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "They have described their picturesque attire , their curious , sometimes awkward , habits and customs , their dwellings and plantations , their government in times of peace and war , as exhaustively as they could do , or thought fit to do", "context_len": 43}}}, "confederacies": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "In 1564 , R\u00e9n\u00e9 de Laudonni\u00e8re heard of five head chiefs ( paracusi ) of confederacies in the Timucua country , and from Pareja we can infer that seven or more dialects were spoken in its circumference", "context_len": 37}}}, "kingdoms": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 12, "context": "They were now going to contend , not for single provinces and kingdoms , as heretofore , but for general empire ; and the victory which they were about to achieve would place them on the summit of human glory", "context_len": 40}}}, "empires": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "They distinguished tribes from confederacies , and called the latter kingdoms and empires , governed by princes , kings and emperors", "context_len": 21}}}, "anthropology": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "But the characteristics of race and language , which are the most important for ethnology , because they are the most ancient in their origin , are not often alluded to by them , and when the modern sciences of anthropology and ethnology had been established on solid principles many of these southern races had already disappeared or intermingled , and scientific inquiry came too late for their investigation", "context_len": 69}}}, "intermingled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 58, "context": "But the characteristics of race and language , which are the most important for ethnology , because they are the most ancient in their origin , are not often alluded to by them , and when the modern sciences of anthropology and ethnology had been established on solid principles many of these southern races had already disappeared or intermingled , and scientific inquiry came too late for their investigation", "context_len": 69}}}, "antiquities": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 17, "context": "They still form a compact body of Indians for themselves , and their history , customs and antiquities can be studied at the present time almost as well as they could at the beginning of the nineteenth century", "context_len": 38}}}, "exhaustive": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "A full elucidation of the history and antiquities of the subject of our inquiries , the Creeks , is possible only after having obtained an exhaustive knowledge of the tribes and nations living around them", "context_len": 35}}}, "rediscovering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "The more populous among them have preserved their language and remember many of their ancestors ' customs and habits , so that active exploration in the field can still be helpful to us in many respects in tracing and rediscovering their ancient condition", "context_len": 43}}}, "predominated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "But the 'Ladies ' Column ' should have been a little more explicit as to the quantity of flavouring , for , when it came to a matter of eating , the herbs so entirely predominated that the mutton was utterly lost , and , as she had unfortunately cut up the stalks as well as the leaves , the mixture bore a horrible resemblance to chopped hay", "context_len": 68}}}, "scientists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The scientists who now claim that they descend from the mound builders , do so only on the belief that they must have dwelt for uncounted centuries in the fertile tracts where Hernando de Soto found them , and where they have remained up to a recent epoch", "context_len": 48}}}, "builders": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The scientists who now claim that they descend from the mound builders , do so only on the belief that they must have dwelt for uncounted centuries in the fertile tracts where Hernando de Soto found them , and where they have remained up to a recent epoch", "context_len": 48}}}, "uncounted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The scientists who now claim that they descend from the mound builders , do so only on the belief that they must have dwelt for uncounted centuries in the fertile tracts where Hernando de Soto found them , and where they have remained up to a recent epoch", "context_len": 48}}}, "epoch": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 52, "context": "An approach to its reconstruction could be attempted by carefully comparing the lexicon and grammatic forms of the dialects presently spoken , and an individual acquainted with them all , or at least with their four representatives , might also compose a comparative grammar of these dialects as spoken at the present epoch of their development , which would reveal many points concerning the ancient or historic shape of the language once common to all these tribes", "context_len": 77}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "At that epoch the Moskoquis formed a powerful separate republic in the northwest of Mexico ; they succored him with a numerous body of warriors , but were frightfully decimated by the Spaniards , who dismembered Montezuma 's domain , and almost completely depopulated it", "context_len": 45}}}, "topographic": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "In the territory once occupied by their tribes no topographic name appears to point to an earlier and alien population ; and as to their exterior , the peculiar olive admixture to their cinnamon complexion is a characteristic which they have in common with all other southern tribes", "context_len": 48}}}, "alien": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 44, "context": "They had all duly performed the religious duties of the day , and had been greatly edified by the homily of Father Cuthbert at mass ; and now innocent mirth was to close the hallowed day -- mirth which they well believed was not alien to the birthday of Him who once sanctified the marriage festivities at Cana by His first miracle", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The plural forms : tchilok\u00f3ga and tchilokog\u00e1lgi designate in Creek persons speaking another than the Creek language ; tchil\u00f3k\u00e4s I speak an alien language", "context_len": 24}}}, "admixture": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 48, "context": "Language is not an absolute indicator of race , but it is more so in America than elsewhere , for the large number of linguistic families in the western hemisphere proves that the populations speaking their dialects have suffered less than in the eastern by encroachment , foreign admixture , forcible alteration or entire destruction", "context_len": 55}}}, "cinnamon": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "In the territory once occupied by their tribes no topographic name appears to point to an earlier and alien population ; and as to their exterior , the peculiar olive admixture to their cinnamon complexion is a characteristic which they have in common with all other southern tribes", "context_len": 48}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Prognathism is not frequent among them , and the complexion of both is a rather dark cinnamon , with the southern olive tinge", "context_len": 23}}}, "ethnologic": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 91, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Clay MacCauley in 1880 visited the Seminoles settled in the southern parts of the peninsula , to take their census and institute ethnologic researches", "context_len": 24}}}, "sequence": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 56, "context": "Yet the Author has distinctly felt that fiction must always , in such cases , be subordinate to truth , and that it is only legitimately used as a vehicle of instruction when it fills up the gaps in the outline , without contradicting them in any respect , or interfering with their due order and sequence", "context_len": 57}}}, "traceable": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 24, "context": "As he spoke he ran down over the boulders , and plunged off a rock into the clear sea , his white figure being traceable against the olive brown sea-wrack waving far below , as he swam for some distance below the surface , and then rose , shook the water from his eyes , and struck out for the lugger lying becalmed in the offing", "context_len": 66}}}, "subdivisions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "The cause of the warring , or the pretense for it , against `` some other Indians from the west '' is curiously similar to the rivalry in athletic sports , which took place between the western Iroquois and their subdivisions , and finally led to the destruction of the Erie or K\u00e1'hkwa Indians ( Cusick , Johnson )", "context_len": 59}}}, "tribal": {"freq": 37, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 35, "word_index": 21, "context": "But this term has nothing at all in common with the Algonkin word sh\u00e1wano south , from which are derived the tribal names : Sh\u00e1wano or Shawnee , once on Ohio and Cumberland rivers and their tributaries ; Chowan in Southern Virginia ; Siwoneys in Connecticut ; Sawannoe in New Jersey ( about 1616 ) ; Chaouanons , the southern division of the Illinois or Maskoutens", "context_len": 66}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The relation of husband to wife and family being the foundation of all tribal , social and political life , should certainly be treated as fully as it deserves , but in this context only incident notes can be given on this subject", "context_len": 43}}}, "topography": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 39, "context": "A correct and detailed knowledge of the Indian trails leading through their country , and called by them warpaths , horse trails , and by the white traders `` trading roads , '' forms an important part of Indian topography and history", "context_len": 42}}}, "contacts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "These contacts were chiefly of a hostile character , for the normal state of barbaric tribes is to live in almost permanent mutual conflicts", "context_len": 24}}}, "conflicts": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 60, "context": "74 ) some English traders found means to draw the eastern party and the district of Coosa ( together called Oypat-oocooloo , `` small nation '' ) into a civil war with the western divisions , called Oocooloo-Falaya ( `` long tribe '' ) , Oocooloo-Hanal\u00e9 ( `` six tribes '' ) , and Chickasawhays , which , after many conflicts and the destruction of East Congeeto , ended with the peace of 1763", "context_len": 74}}}, "prevalently": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "What follows is an attempt to enumerate and sketch them , the sketch to be of a prevalently topographic nature", "context_len": 20}}}, "anthropological": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The genealogy of the Maskoki tribes can not be established on anthropological , that is racial , characteristics ; these Indians formerly incorporated so many alien elements into their towns , and have become so largely mixed with half-castes in the nineteenth century , that a division on racial grounds has become almost impossible", "context_len": 54}}}, "classifying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "We are not thoroughly acquainted with the racial or anthropological peculiarities of the nations surrounding the Maskoki proper on all sides , but in their languages we possess an excellent help for classifying them", "context_len": 34}}}, "hemisphere": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Language is not an absolute indicator of race , but it is more so in America than elsewhere , for the large number of linguistic families in the western hemisphere proves that the populations speaking their dialects have suffered less than in the eastern by encroachment , foreign admixture , forcible alteration or entire destruction", "context_len": 55}}}, "populations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "encroachment": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "Language is not an absolute indicator of race , but it is more so in America than elsewhere , for the large number of linguistic families in the western hemisphere proves that the populations speaking their dialects have suffered less than in the eastern by encroachment , foreign admixture , forcible alteration or entire destruction", "context_len": 55}}}, "southeast": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Northwest of that ridge lay the Cheroki villages of the Overhill settlement , \u00d3tari , \u00d3tali ( `` up , above '' ) , along the Great and Little Tennessee rivers and their tributaries , while southeast of it , in the mountains of North Carolina and on the head waters of the Georgia rivers , extended the towns of the Lower Cheroki , or Erati ( in Cheroki \u00e9lati , below , nether )", "context_len": 76}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "When I visited Natchez city in January 1882 , I was informed that the White Apple village , called Apilua ( Vpelois ) and mentioned by Le Page du Pratz , is supposed to have existed twelve to fifteen miles southeast of the city", "context_len": 44}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "But there is at least one fact which goes to show that the settling of the Creeks proceeded from west to east and southeast", "context_len": 24}}}, "tributaries": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` Northwest of that ridge lay the Cheroki villages of the Overhill settlement , \u00d3tari , \u00d3tali ( `` up , above '' ) , along the Great and Little Tennessee rivers and their tributaries , while southeast of it , in the mountains of North Carolina and on the head waters of the Georgia rivers , extended the towns of the Lower Cheroki , or Erati ( in Cheroki \u00e9lati , below , nether )", "context_len": 76}}}, "southernmost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Their southernmost villages known to us were Hirrihigua , near Tampa Bay , and Tucururu , near Cape Ca\u00f1averal , on the Atlantic Coast", "context_len": 24}}}, "memoir": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "They are called Calos on de Bry 's map ( 1591 ) , otherwise Colusa , Callos , Carlos , and formed a confederacy of many villages , the names of which are given in the memoir of Hernando d'Escalante Fontanedo ( M\u00e9moire sur la Floride , in Ternaux-Compans ' Collection XX , p", "context_len": 54}}}, "chroniclers": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 26, "context": "It is well said that no man is utterly bad , and perhaps he possessed domestic virtues which were thought unworthy of the attention of the chroniclers ; but as they picture him -- now prompting Ethelred to deeds of treachery against the Danes , now joining those Danes themselves , and surpassing them in cruelty -- now seeking pretended reconciliation , only to betray his foe more surely , and in all this aided and supported by the weak , unprincipled king -- as thus pictured there is scarcely a blacker character in history", "context_len": 95}}}, "vocalic": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 31, "context": "Although Maskoki speech , taken as a whole , belongs to the agglutinative type of languages , some forms of it , especially the predicative inflection of the verb and the vocalic changes in the radicals , strongly remind us of the inflective languages", "context_len": 44}}}, "affinity": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 13, "context": "These names were written down in 1559 , and do not show much affinity with Timucua ; but since they are the only remnants of the Calusa language , I present the full list : `` Tampa , Tomo , Tuchi , Sogo , No ( which signifies 'beloved village ' ) , Sinapa , Sinaesta , Metamapo , Sacaspada , Calaobe , Estame , Yagua , Guaya , Guevu , Muspa , Casitoa , Tatesta , Coyovea , Jutun , Tequemapo , Comachica , Quiseyove and two others in the vicinity", "context_len": 92}}}, "instigated": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Meanwhile the English continued the pursuit until a small remnant of Danes repassed the river ; only a small remnant of the party which , as it will be easily guessed , instigated by Edric , had sallied forth to besiege the place where Edmund had found refuge , who had so recently provoked the bitter hostility of Sweyn", "context_len": 59}}}, "incursions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 49, "context": "they gradually became converted to Christianity , revolted in 1687 against their Spanish oppressors , and early in the eighteenth century ( 1706 ) were so reduced in number that they yielded easily to the attacks of the Y\u00e1massi Indians , who , instigated by English colonists , made incursions upon their villages from the North", "context_len": 56}}}, "appellation": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 28, "context": "The five head chiefs , Saturiwa , Holata Utina , Potanu , Onethcaqua and Hostaqua are only tribal names ( in the second , Utina is the tribal appellation ) , and the dialects , as far as known , were those of Timagoa , Potanu , Itafi , the Fresh-Water district , Tucururu , Santa Lucia de Acuera , and Mocama ( `` on the coast '' )", "context_len": 69}}}, "coincided": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The last but one probably coincided with that of A\u00efs", "context_len": 10}}}, "worshiped": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "They worshiped the sun in the shape of a stuffed deer raised upon the end of a high beam planted in the ground ; this gave , probably , origin to their name A\u00efs , for B", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "This was not , however , so impious as it would at first view seem , for the gods whom the ancients worshiped were , in fact , only deifications of old heroes and kings who had lived in early times , and had acquired a reputation for supernatural powers by the fame of their exploits , exaggerated in descending by tradition in superstitious times", "context_len": 65}}}, "romans": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 19, "word_index": 0, "context": "Romans states that they showed the `` hole in the ground , '' from which they came , between their nation and the Chicasa , and told the colonists that their neighbors were surprised at seeing a people rise at once out of the earth ( p", "context_len": 47}}}, "interprets": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Romans interprets A\u00efsahatcha by Deer river ( itchi , itche deer , in Creek and Seminole )", "context_len": 17}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "We can not get her to make herself the least pleasant ; whatever we do she interprets in the wrong manner , and how we are to keep the peace I do n't know", "context_len": 34}}}, "cf": {"freq": 124, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 120, "word_index": 67, "context": "In 1559 another Spanish leader , Tristan de Luna , disembarked in or near Mobile bay , then went north in quest of gold and treasure , reached Nanipacna , or `` pueblo Santa Cruz de Nanipacna , '' and from there arrived , after experiencing many privations and trials , among the Co\u00e7as , who were then engaged in warfare with the Napochies ( nanpissa ? cf", "context_len": 68}, "VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "Mu l\u00e1salgi ; said to mean `` people of Mukl\u00e1sa town '' ; cf", "context_len": 14}}}, "recorded": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 27, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}}}, "confederacy": {"freq": 44, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 44, "word_index": 50, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "sur": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "They are called Calos on de Bry 's map ( 1591 ) , otherwise Colusa , Callos , Carlos , and formed a confederacy of many villages , the names of which are given in the memoir of Hernando d'Escalante Fontanedo ( M\u00e9moire sur la Floride , in Ternaux-Compans ' Collection XX , p", "context_len": 54}}}, "cacique": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": ", de la Vega ) and the Fidalgo of Elvas : that the cacique of Chica\u00e7a came with the caciques of Alimamu and of Nicalasa , [ 53 ] whereupon a fight took place", "context_len": 34}}}, "thirteenth": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Fontanedo states that he was prisoner in these parts from his thirteenth to his thirtieth year ; that he knew four languages , but was not familiar with those of A\u00efs and Teaga , not having been there", "context_len": 39}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Chipper Cooper had just finished telling for the thirteenth time that morning how Stone had defended Amy Eliot and knocked the stuffing out of Fletcher \u2019 s dogs , his every statement having been confirmed by Chub Tuttle , who was making a sort of after-breakfast lunch on peanuts", "context_len": 49}}}, "resembles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Some may be explained by the Creek language , but only one of them , Tampa ( it\u00edmpi close to it , near it ) is Creek to a certainty ; Tuchi resembles t\u00fatchi kidneys ; Sogo , s\u00e1-uka rattle , gourd-rattle , and No is the radix of a-no-k\u00edtcha lover , anuk\u00eddsh\u0101s I love , which agrees with the interpretation given by Fontanedo", "context_len": 64}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Casiste resembles Kas\u00ed'hta , but can not have been Kas\u00ed ta on Chatahuchi river , for de Soto reached Tuskalusa or `` Black Warrior , '' which I take to be a town on the river of that name , within two days from Casiste , traveling west", "context_len": 48}}}, "radix": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 47, "context": "Some may be explained by the Creek language , but only one of them , Tampa ( it\u00edmpi close to it , near it ) is Creek to a certainty ; Tuchi resembles t\u00fatchi kidneys ; Sogo , s\u00e1-uka rattle , gourd-rattle , and No is the radix of a-no-k\u00edtcha lover , anuk\u00eddsh\u0101s I love , which agrees with the interpretation given by Fontanedo", "context_len": 64}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "and Sauk : sibe , in Odshibw\u0113 : sibi ) and hence the radix meth- is probably identical with mas- in maskek", "context_len": 22}}}, "interpretation": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 60, "context": "Some may be explained by the Creek language , but only one of them , Tampa ( it\u00edmpi close to it , near it ) is Creek to a certainty ; Tuchi resembles t\u00fatchi kidneys ; Sogo , s\u00e1-uka rattle , gourd-rattle , and No is the radix of a-no-k\u00edtcha lover , anuk\u00eddsh\u0101s I love , which agrees with the interpretation given by Fontanedo", "context_len": 64}}}, "augmentative": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "`` Or the superlative is expressed by the augmentative adverb m\u00e1hi : very , quite , greatly , largely y\u00edktchi m\u00e1hi , the strongest , which at the same time means : very strong , quite strong ; ` l\u00e1ko m\u00e1hi largest and very large ; m\u00e1himahi tallest and very tall ; the latter also being expressed by a lengthening of the vowel : m\u0101 hi very tall", "context_len": 68}}}, "unfitted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The Spanish orthography , in which these names are laid down , is unfitted for transcribing Indian languages , perhaps as much so as the English orthography ; nevertheless , we recognize the frequently-occurring terminal -esta , -sta , which sounds quite like Timucua", "context_len": 44}}}, "terminal": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "The Spanish orthography , in which these names are laid down , is unfitted for transcribing Indian languages , perhaps as much so as the English orthography ; nevertheless , we recognize the frequently-occurring terminal -esta , -sta , which sounds quite like Timucua", "context_len": 44}, "UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 25, "context": "Natchez is the old-fashioned plural adopted from French orthography ; we might just as well write Iroquoiz , Islinoiz or Adayez , instead of the terminal -s now designating the plural in French", "context_len": 33}}}, "geographic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "If oki water formed a component part of it , it would stand first , as in the Hitchiti geographic terms Ok\u0115l\u00e1kni `` yellow water , '' Okifen\u00f3ke `` wavering , shaking waters , '' Okm\u00falgi `` bubbling water , '' Okitch\u00f3bi `` river , '' lit", "context_len": 47}}}, "rapacious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The Calusa bore the reputation of being a savage and rapacious people , and B", "context_len": 15}}}, "denounces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "292 ) denounces them as having been pirates", "context_len": 8}}}, "southeastern": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 15, "context": "After the death of Birinus , the zeal of his successors failed to evangelise the southeastern districts of Wessex , until , at length , came Wilfrid , fervent in zeal , and , stationing himself at Selsey , near Chichester , evangelised both Sussex and Wessex , sending out missionaries like-minded with himself , even into the most inaccessible wilds", "context_len": 61}}}, "vocabularies": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` This term appears in all the vocabularies : kaw\u00edta , man , male ; kohwita , ko-ita , plural kohino ' h , man ; kota , man , contracted from kow'ta , kowita ; also in compounds : kow\u0115t-ten-ch\u014d\u014d , chief ; koh\u00edtta makinnung , chief of a people", "context_len": 51}}}, "contiguous": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 30, "context": "d'Iberville reports , that at the time of his visit ( March 1699 ) the villages of the Naktche made up one town only , and formed a complex of contiguous villages called Th\u00e9lo\u00ebl or Th\u00e9co\u00ebl [ 27 ] ( Margry IV , 179 )", "context_len": 45}}}, "transcribe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The passage of Adair being the only notice on the extent of the Kataba language found in the early authors , excepting Lawson , I transcribe it here in full ( History , pp", "context_len": 34}}}, "alliances": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Events of this kind are migrations , long warfare or decisive battles , which resulted either in defeat or victory , alliances with cognate or friendly tribes , times of abundance , of famines and epidemics", "context_len": 36}}}, "inhabit": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Six different nations were said to inhabit the banks of the river , probably all of Cha'hta lineage ; among them are mentioned the Pascogoulas , Chozettas , Bilocchi , Moctoby , all insignificant in numbers", "context_len": 36}}}, "musculature": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` In bodily size they are smaller than the Creeks , but lithe and of wiry musculature , the muscles often protruding from the body", "context_len": 25}}}, "polygamous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Their descent is in the male line , and they were once polygamous", "context_len": 13}}}, "efflux": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The monthly efflux of the Sun , whom they considered as of the female sex , fell to the earth , as they say , and from this the Yuchi people took its origin", "context_len": 34}}}, "pagans": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 67, "context": "Early in the morning , before the high mass , whereat the neighbourhood is generally present , I received a missive from the sheriff , bidding me , in the name of the King , to exhort my people to remain at home tonight , since danger is afoot , and there is likely , he says , to be a rising on the part of the pagans who dwell amongst us", "context_len": 72}}}, "popularly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The name Kas\u00ed'hta , Kasi ta , is popularly explained as `` coming from the sun '' ( h\u0103 si ) and being identical with has\u00ed'hta", "context_len": 26}}}, "warble": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Popularly expressed , their language sounds 'like the warble of the prairie-chickens", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` You may sing just as much as you wish here , '' he said ; `` singing is the cry of the soul , and I hope you will teach my youngsters to warble half as well as you do -- see , they are running up a flag for you , '' and he pointed to a flagpole on the lawn in front of the house", "context_len": 68}}}, "conjurers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 29, "context": "Traditions on early migrations , which have originated in the people to which they refer and bear the imprint of genuineness , not that of a late fabrication by conjurers or mixed-bloods , usually contain indications of importance which are confirmed by arch\u00e6ologic and linguistic researches", "context_len": 46}}}, "symbolic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Then another singular mixture is prepared , of which the ingredients must have been of symbolic significance : Old maize cobs and pine burs are placed in a pot and burned to ashes", "context_len": 33}}}, "ritual": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "Although the ritual of the busk differed in every Creek tribe , many analogies can be traced with well-known customs among the Aztec and Maya nations , whose `` unlucky five days '' at the year 's close equally terminated with rejoicings , as the precursors of a new life", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "`` Society , '' thereby meaning well-bred and cultured men and women , has as much right to lay down rules to dress and conduct as any `` secret '' society has to insist upon ritual and ceremony", "context_len": 38}}}, "phonetics": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "From what we know of it , it shows no radical affinity with any known American tongue , and its phonetics have often been noticed for their strangeness", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "PHONETICS", "context_len": 1}}}, "ultima": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The accent often rests on the ultima ( Powell 's mscr", "context_len": 11}}}, "ascribes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "vocabulary ) , and Ware ascribes to them , though wrongly , the Hottentot cluck", "context_len": 15}}}, "numerals": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 58, "context": "There are terms which occur in all dialects in the same or nearly the same form , as h\u00e1si sun , \u00edtchu , \u00edssi deer , \u00f3fi , \u00edfa dog , the terms for chief , black , yellow , bird , snake , buffalo , turtle , fox ( also in Cheroki : tsu'hl\u00e1 ) , the numerals and the personal pronouns ; they must , therefore , have been once the common property of the still undivided , primordial tribe", "context_len": 82}}}, "decimal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The numerals follow the decimal , not the quinary system as they do in the Maskoki languages", "context_len": 17}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The faculty for forming compound words is considerable , and the numerals show the decimal form of computation", "context_len": 18}}}, "quinary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "The numerals follow the decimal , not the quinary system as they do in the Maskoki languages", "context_len": 17}}}, "dual": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "Intransitive verbs show special forms , according to the number of the subject ( singular , dual , plural )", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "of melaoun , accursed ) ; but the word in the text is plainly mulaa'bein ( objective dual of mulaa ' b , a trickster , malicious joker , hence , by analogy , sharper )", "context_len": 36}}}, "intransitive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 27, "context": "It possesses an affirmative , negative , interrogative and distributive form , which latter is used as a form for the plural of the subject in the intransitive verbs ; it also has a large number of conversational forms usually derived by contraction , ellipses , etc", "context_len": 47}}}, "distinguishes": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "When he distinguishes himself , he obtains a seat in the central cabin of the public square", "context_len": 17}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Cha'hta distinguishes between the inclusive and exclusive pronouns we , our , but Creek and Hitchiti do not", "context_len": 18}}}, "habitat": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` The idea that their forefathers issued from caves was so deeply engrafted in the minds of these Indians , that some of them took any conspicuous cave or any country rich in caves to be the primordial habitat of their race", "context_len": 42}}}, "testifies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "de Soto reached these parts with his army , the `` queen '' ( se\u00f1ora , ca\u00e7ica ) of the country met him at the town Cofeta\u00e7que on a barge , a circumstance which testifies to the existence of a considerable water-course there", "context_len": 43}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": ", in confirming this statement , testifies to the existence of the sound R in their language , which occurs neither in Naktche nor in the Maskoki dialects or Shetimasha ( II , 220-221 )", "context_len": 35}}}, "consonants": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "Cofeta\u00e7que , written also Cofitachiqui ( Biedma ) , Cofachiqui ( Garcilaso de la Vega ) , Cutifachiqui ( consonants inverted , Elvas ) was seven days ' march from Chalaque ( Cheroki ) `` province , '' and distant from the sea about thirty leagues , as stated by the natives of the place", "context_len": 55}}}, "inverted": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": "Cofeta\u00e7que , written also Cofitachiqui ( Biedma ) , Cofachiqui ( Garcilaso de la Vega ) , Cutifachiqui ( consonants inverted , Elvas ) was seven days ' march from Chalaque ( Cheroki ) `` province , '' and distant from the sea about thirty leagues , as stated by the natives of the place", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "investigators": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "29 ) believes , with other investigators , that Cofeta\u00e7que stood at Silver Bluff , on the left bank of the Savannah river , about twenty-five miles by water below Augusta", "context_len": 31}}}, "plural": {"freq": 31, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 24, "word_index": 19, "context": "[ 13 ] While calling a person of their own people by the name of Ats\u00e1lagi , in the plural Anits\u00e1lagi , they comprise all the Creeks under the name of K\u00fasa , from Coosa river , or more probably from the ancient , far-famed town of the same name : Ag\u00fasa , K\u00fasa , G\u00fasa , a Creek person ; Anig\u00fasa , the Creek people ; G\u00fasa uniwon\u00ed'hsti , the Creek language", "context_len": 73}, "NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 21, "context": "It possesses an affirmative , negative , interrogative and distributive form , which latter is used as a form for the plural of the subject in the intransitive verbs ; it also has a large number of conversational forms usually derived by contraction , ellipses , etc", "context_len": 47}}}, "compounds": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "`` This term appears in all the vocabularies : kaw\u00edta , man , male ; kohwita , ko-ita , plural kohino ' h , man ; kota , man , contracted from kow'ta , kowita ; also in compounds : kow\u0115t-ten-ch\u014d\u014d , chief ; koh\u00edtta makinnung , chief of a people", "context_len": 51}}}, "collective": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 55, "context": "The second part of the name , -chiqui , is a term foreign to Yuchi , but found in all the dialects of Maskoki in the function of house , dwelling , ( tch\u00faku , tch\u00f3ko , and in the eastern or Apalachian dialects , tch\u00edki ) and has to be rendered here in the collective sense of houses , town", "context_len": 61}}}, "signification": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 48, "context": "Then ( says our author ) , the geomant proceeds to examine the sixteen figures thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it", "context_len": 128}}}, "localities": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 61, "context": "This table however includes , with a few exceptions , only places situated near Apalachicola river ( east and west of it ) , in Alabama , Georgia and Florida ; the list was probably made at a time when Florida was still under Spanish domination , which accounts for the fact that the county names are not added to the localities", "context_len": 62}}}, "epochs": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 64, "context": "The tale now presented to the indulgence of the public is the second of a series of tales , each complete in itself , which , as stated in the preface to the first of the series , have been told to the senior boys of a large school , in order to secure their interest in historical characters , and to illustrate great epochs in human affairs by the aid of fiction", "context_len": 73}}}, "irrefutable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "This raises the query , did the natives of this `` capital '' speak Creek or Yuchi ? Who will attempt to give an irrefutable answer to this query ?", "context_len": 30}}}, "hawkins": {"freq": 27, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 27, "word_index": 0, "context": "Hawkins , who attempted to introduce some unity among the towns for the purpose of facilitating the transaction of business of the nation , and their intercourse with the United States Government , proposed various measures , as the classing of the towns into nine districts ; these were adopted at Tukabatchi by the chiefs of the nation , on November 27th , 1799", "context_len": 64}}}, "falsely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The man had spoken under the fear of death , if he spoke falsely", "context_len": 14}}}, "savanna": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "Bartram traveled through the Seminole country , about 1773 , he was informed that Cuscowilla , a town on a lake of the same name and a sort of Seminole capital , had been built by Indians from Ok\u00f3ni old town , settled upon the Alachua plains : `` They abdicated the ancient Alachua town on the borders of the savanna , about fifty miles west from the river San Juan , and built here , calling the new town Cuscowilla", "context_len": 81}}}, "busk": {"freq": 25, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 25, "word_index": 67, "context": "This change of mind was symbolized by the custom of the women of breaking to pieces all the household utensils of the past year , and replacing them by new ones ; the men refitted all their property so as to look new , and it was considered extremely disgraceful , even for the most indigent , to eat any of the new maize before the annual busk ( Sketch , pp", "context_len": 72}}}, "rev": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 0, "context": "Rev", "context_len": 1}}}, "depicts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "Bartram , who saw it in 1775 , depicts it as the largest , most compact , and best situated Indian town he ever saw ; the habitations were large and neatly built , the walls of the houses consisted of a wooden frame , lathed and plastered inside and outside with a reddish clay , and roofed with cypress bark or shingles", "context_len": 63}}}, "intermarried": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "This was greatly disliked by his townspeople , and he was prevailed upon to move across Chatahuchi river , opposite to where Yuchi town was in Hawkins ' time ; he settled there with his three brothers , two of whom had intermarried with Yuchis", "context_len": 45}}}, "eulogizes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Hawkins eulogizes the people by stating that they are more civil , orderly and industrious than their neighbors ( the Lower Creeks ) , the men more attached to their wives , and these more chaste", "context_len": 36}}}, "chaste": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Hawkins eulogizes the people by stating that they are more civil , orderly and industrious than their neighbors ( the Lower Creeks ) , the men more attached to their wives , and these more chaste", "context_len": 36}}}, "estimates": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "He estimates the number of their warriors ( `` gun-men '' ) , including those of the three branch villages , at about two hundred and fifty", "context_len": 27}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Hawkins ' time are mere estimates", "context_len": 6}}}, "lathed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Bartram , who saw it in 1775 , depicts it as the largest , most compact , and best situated Indian town he ever saw ; the habitations were large and neatly built , the walls of the houses consisted of a wooden frame , lathed and plastered inside and outside with a reddish clay , and roofed with cypress bark or shingles", "context_len": 63}}}, "cypress": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "Bartram , who saw it in 1775 , depicts it as the largest , most compact , and best situated Indian town he ever saw ; the habitations were large and neatly built , the walls of the houses consisted of a wooden frame , lathed and plastered inside and outside with a reddish clay , and roofed with cypress bark or shingles", "context_len": 63}}}, "immigration": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` The Hellenic , especially Doric tradition of an immigration from Thrace and Macedonia through Epirus and Thessalia into Greece is confirmed by linguistic and historic facts , but the Roman legend concerning the descent of the founders of the `` Eternal City '' from Troy was acknowledged to be a pious fraud by the ancients themselves", "context_len": 57}}}, "les": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "[ Footnote 555 : Here Galland says , `` Il entra dans le lien le plus fameux et le plus frequente par les personnel de grande distinction , ou l'on s'assembloit pour boire d'une certaine boisson chance qui luy etoit connue des son premier voyage", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ 24 ] The French called them `` les petits Ta\u00ebnsas '' in contradistinction to the `` great ( or northern ) Ta\u00ebnsas , '' on Mississippi and Taensa rivers", "context_len": 30}}}, "vineyards": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "10 ) states that lands were obtained from `` les Savanogu\u00e9s , sauvages qui habitent cette partie ( de Tougoulou ) , '' for the plantation of vineyards , about 1775", "context_len": 31}}}, "anciently": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 5, "context": "A part of the Cheroki anciently dwelt in caves ; and concerning the caverns from which the Creeks claim to have issued , James Adair gives the following interesting disclosure : `` It is worthy of notice , that the Muskohgeh cave , out of which one of their politicians persuaded them their ancestors formerly ascended to their present terrestrial abode , lies in the Nanne Hamgeh old town , inhabited by the Mississippi-Nachee Indians , [ 150 ] which is one of the most western parts of their old-inhabited country", "context_len": 91}}}, "locate": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 12, "word_index": 49, "context": "From a thorough comparative study of the Yuchi language , the Maskoki dialects and the local nomenclature of the country , we can alone expect any reliable information upon the extent and the area of territory anciently held by the Yuchi ; but at present it is safest to locate their `` priscan home '' upon both sides of Lower Savannah river", "context_len": 62}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 86, "context": "In my collection I have them varying from almost straight to a shape like that of the letter V , nearly straight , curved , plain , ornamented , some with strange carvings , and all varying according to different sections of the country and individual tribes , each having its own make or style , showing respectively rough crudeness or considerable finish , and being especially characteristic in the ends or points -- all of which a boomerang connoisseur will distinguish at once , and locate as to tribe and section", "context_len": 92}}}, "prehistoric": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Intercourse between the Creek and the Cheroki Indians must have taken place in prehistoric times , as evidenced by local names , and more so by Cheroki terms adopted into the Creek language", "context_len": 33}}}, "evidenced": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "Intercourse between the Creek and the Cheroki Indians must have taken place in prehistoric times , as evidenced by local names , and more so by Cheroki terms adopted into the Creek language", "context_len": 33}}}, "emigrated": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 11, "context": "The Cheroki language was spoken in many dialects before the people emigrated to the lands allotted to them in the northeastern part of the Indian Territory , and even now a difference may be observed between the Western Cheroki and the Eastern or Mountain Cheroki , which is the language of the people that remained in the hills of Western North Carolina and Eastern Tennessee", "context_len": 65}}}, "dialectic": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "The degree of dialectic difference points approximately to the date of the separation of the respective communities , and untold centuries must have elapsed since the two main branches of the family were torn asunder , for Cha'hta differs about as much from Creek as the literary German does from Icelandic", "context_len": 51}}}, "centering": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 74, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "narcotic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "[ Footnote 16 : The name Keowe is taken from a narcotic plant used for catching fish , which grew in the vicinity of that village", "context_len": 26}}}, "fastnesses": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 50, "context": "It happened , however , that a short time afterward there was another opportunity to contend for the same bull , and Paris , disguising himself as a prince , appeared in the lists , conquered every competitor , and bore away the bull again to his home in the fastnesses of the mountain", "context_len": 54}}}, "sallies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 84, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}}}, "wm": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Wm", "context_len": 1}}}, "looser": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The women wore the hair long , even reaching to the knees , but plucked it out from all the other parts of the body , especially the looser part of the sex ( Memoirs , pp", "context_len": 37}}}, "sudatory": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` It is variously called by travelers : hot-house , sudatory , assembly-room , winter council-house , mountain-house , [ 110 ] or , from its circular shape , rotunda", "context_len": 30}}}, "structures": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 16, "context": "She looked in amazement at the elegantly furnished apartments of Madame Louise , and the wonderful structures of feathers and lace and ribbon , which the voluble saleswoman assured them were cheap at thirty dollars , and was lost in a rapturous delight , as , with the calmness of experienced shoppers , her cousins went from one department to another in White 's and Hovey 's , laying in a supply of airy nothings of which she did not even know the use ; always being treated by them with the same delicate consideration : there was nothing forced upon her , only , as they were getting things , she might as well be fitted too", "context_len": 118}}}, "bottoms": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 10, "context": "The country inhabited by the Maskoki proper abounds in creek bottoms overflowed in the rainy season , as the country around Opel\u00edka `` swamp-site '' ( from Creek : op\u00edlua , ap\u00edlua swamp , l\u00e4\u00edkita to be stretched out ) , Opil- ` l\u00e1ko `` great swamp , '' west of the above ( Hawkins , p", "context_len": 57}}}, "pottery": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Oftener she worked in her roofless pottery by the ditch , where many a queer jug and vase and bowl , gaudy with ochre and Indian red , came into being and passed early to dust again , for want of firing", "context_len": 42}}}, "recruited": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "The male population was divided into a class of head-men or chiefs , recruited by popular election , the selection being made among the most valorous men and the best orators in their councils ; and in two classes of `` yeomen '' : the `` warriors '' and the `` fighting men , '' these being inferior to the warriors", "context_len": 61}}}, "valorous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The male population was divided into a class of head-men or chiefs , recruited by popular election , the selection being made among the most valorous men and the best orators in their councils ; and in two classes of `` yeomen '' : the `` warriors '' and the `` fighting men , '' these being inferior to the warriors", "context_len": 61}}}, "orators": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "The male population was divided into a class of head-men or chiefs , recruited by popular election , the selection being made among the most valorous men and the best orators in their councils ; and in two classes of `` yeomen '' : the `` warriors '' and the `` fighting men , '' these being inferior to the warriors", "context_len": 61}}}, "killer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "But Charlie , who was better versed in the business , took out the hook with his killer -- a stick made to fit the hook , and with which he knocked the fish on the head as he pulled them in", "context_len": 42}}}, "parallels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Distinction in reward of exploits was conferred through the honorary titles of Outacity , `` man killer , '' Kolona , `` raven '' and `` Beloved , '' names to which parallels will be found among the Creeks", "context_len": 39}}}, "clans": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "Seven clans or gentes exist among the Cheroki , and many of them observe to the present day the regulations imposed by the gentile organization", "context_len": 25}}}, "gentes": {"freq": 36, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 35, "context": "Among the tribes of the Maskoki family , we notice the following ethnographic practices : the use of the red and white colors as symbols of war and peace , an extensive system of totemic gentes , the use of the Ilex cassine for the manufacture of the black drink , the erection of artificial mounds , the belief in a deity called `` Master of Life , '' and original sun-worship", "context_len": 72}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Besides the fact that gentes Nos", "context_len": 6}}}, "gentile": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Seven clans or gentes exist among the Cheroki , and many of them observe to the present day the regulations imposed by the gentile organization", "context_len": 25}}}, "gens": {"freq": 80, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 72, "word_index": 21, "context": "The notice that each of the seven plants distributed to the Indians belonged , or was the emblem of a certain gens or division of people , is gathered from this passage only , and probably refers to the ingredients of some war-physic , which only a limited number of the gentes may have been entitled to contribute to the annual p\u00faskita", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 33, "context": "The only points which could be gathered were , that individuals belonging to the panther and the wildcat gentes could not intermarry , nor could the Tchuk\u00f3talgi with the individuals of the toad gens or Sop\u00e1ktalgi", "context_len": 36}}}, "phratry": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 10, "context": "Their name is identical with Imukl\u00e1sha or the `` opposite phratry '' in the Cha'hta nation , from which Mukl\u00e1sha , a Creek town , also received its name", "context_len": 29}}}, "totems": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "Both have two phratries only , each of which were ( originally ) subdivided , in an equal manner , into four gentes ; but the thorough-going difference in the totems of the 8-12 gentes points to a very ancient separation of the two national bodies", "context_len": 46}}}, "delegate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Richard Wolf , delegate of the people to the United States government , as follows :", "context_len": 16}}}, "clan": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "Many towns appeared rather compactly built , although they were composed of irregular clusters of four to eight houses standing together ; each of these clusters contained a gens ( `` clan or family of relations , '' C", "context_len": 39}}}, "loki": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "They can marry into all gentes , except into the long hair clan , because this contains their `` aunts '' ( \u00e4 ` loki )", "context_len": 26}}}, "phratries": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 3, "context": "Both have two phratries only , each of which were ( originally ) subdivided , in an equal manner , into four gentes ; but the thorough-going difference in the totems of the 8-12 gentes points to a very ancient separation of the two national bodies", "context_len": 46}}}, "prefix": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 31, "context": "The cardinal numeral has a full form ending in -in , and another abbreviated from it used in counting objects , and not extending beyond ten ; an ordinal , with prefix -\u00edsa- , is- , apheret", "context_len": 37}, "NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "Here the hu- is the predicative prefix : he is , she is , they are , and appears often as ho- , hui- , ku-", "context_len": 26}}}, "animate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "Thus Humask\u00f3gi means `` they are Masko '' , the suffix -gi , -ki being the plural ending of the animate order of substantives in Sh\u00e1wano", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The distinction between animate and inanimate gender is not made in this language family ; much less that between the male and the female sex", "context_len": 25}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "He made an address to them , in which he recounted all their past exploits , praised the courage and perseverance which they had shown thus far , and endeavored to animate them with a desire to proceed", "context_len": 38}}}, "totemic": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Among the tribes of the Maskoki family , we notice the following ethnographic practices : the use of the red and white colors as symbols of war and peace , an extensive system of totemic gentes , the use of the Ilex cassine for the manufacture of the black drink , the erection of artificial mounds , the belief in a deity called `` Master of Life , '' and original sun-worship", "context_len": 72}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "It is possible that among the above totemic gentes some are in fact phratries and not gentes ; and the two fires ( or t\u00fatka ) of the Creeks are not real phratries , but formal divisions only", "context_len": 38}}}, "differs": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 30, "context": "Caleb Swan , who , in his above description of the cabins in the square , copied the original seen at Odshi-ap\u00f3fa or Little Talassie , where he stopped , differs in several particulars , especially in the allotment of the cabins to the authorities , from Hawkins , who resided in Kaw\u00edta", "context_len": 53}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "P\u00e9nicaut , who accompanied d'Iberville in this expedition , gives an account of the Yazoo villages , which differs in some respects from the above : Going up the river of the Yazoux for four leagues , there are found on the right the villages inhabited by six savage nations , called `` les Yasoux , les Offogoulas , les Tonicas , les Coroas , les Ouitoupas et les Oussip\u00e9s", "context_len": 70}}}, "narrated": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 77, "context": "How Bomba saved the camp of Gillis and Dorn , rubber hunters , from a night attack by jaguars -- how he trapped the deadly cooanaradi , the most dreaded serpent of the South American wilds , when it pursued him ; his adventures with alligators and anacondas ; the besieging of his cabin by the headhunters ; how his friends of the forest came to his aid when he was fearfully beset ; all this is narrated in the first volumes of this series , entitled : Bomba , the Jungle Boy ; or , The Old Naturalist \u2019 s Secret", "context_len": 102}}}, "substantiated": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "The tradition that the Cheroki , or rather a portion of them , were found living in caves , is substantiated by the appellation `` Cave-dwellers , '' given to them by the Northern Indians", "context_len": 35}}}, "ind": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Ind", "context_len": 1}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 36, "context": "You see I was jest about to enter the stable door that mornin \u2019 w \u2019 en that young feller appeared a-comin \u2019 down the path , and as \u2019 e appeared I disappeared be \u2019 ind the corner o \u2019 the barn", "context_len": 43}}}, "arkansas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "ARKANSAS", "context_len": 1}}}, "gros": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The men are described as stout and thick-set ( gros et trapus ) , the women as pretty and light-complexioned", "context_len": 20}}}, "affluent": {"freq": 17, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 36, "context": "d'Iberville , in 1702 , gives their number at 2000 families , probably including the Alibamu , stating that both tribes have their first settlements 35 to 40 leagues to the northeast , on an eastern affluent of Mobile river , joining it five leagues above the fort", "context_len": 48}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 71, "context": "Altogether the vehicle had a lordly air , easily matching that of the individual sitting alone on the purple cushions -- a man whose features were not very clear at the distance , although the yellowness of his beard , the glitter of his studded shirt-front , and whole consequential , expansive effect recalled to the doctor 's mind an image of the past , less ornate , indeed , and affluent , but of similar aspect", "context_len": 77}}}, "abbreviated": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 17, "context": "The numeral has two forms for the cardinal number : one used attributively , and another , abbreviated from it , used exclusively for counting ; there are , outside of this , forms for the ordinal , for the distributive , and for the adverbial numeral", "context_len": 47}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "t\u0101sk\u00e1-is , t\u0101 sk\u00e4s I jump ( tul\u00fap-kalis in Hitchiti ) ; or be considered an abbreviated form of t\u00e1skialgi warriors ; cf", "context_len": 23}}}, "bends": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The Tensas river , in French Bayou Tensa , which joins the Washita river at Trinity City , after forming a prodigious number of bends , and flowing past a multitude of artificial mounds , still keeps up the memory of this extinct tribe", "context_len": 44}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` Does n't it look like a slender young living thing swaying there in the wind ? See how it bends its pretty head and waves its green arms toward us", "context_len": 31}}}, "grouped": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 23, "context": "Three days had passed since his departure , and Pauline stood in the doorway feasting her eyes on the lights and shadows which grouped themselves about the distant hills , when Lemuel brushed past her , clad in his Sunday best", "context_len": 41}}}, "culture": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 60, "context": "The little man squinted at the tips of the trees waving their skeleton branches in the crisp air , then brought a calm gaze to the excited old face : `` Not exactly ; but I do say when you make a boy like Ben turn from the path he has marked out for himself , all the education that culture would crowd on him is just so much to break down the boy", "context_len": 74}}}, "sojourned": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "When d'Iberville sojourned among them , lightning struck their temple and destroyed it , upon which the mothers sacrificed their infants , to appease the wrath of the incensed deity , by throwing them into the burning edifice ( Margry IV , 414 , etc", "context_len": 45}}}, "infants": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 28, "context": "Her gruesome voice as she recounted what the goblins did to naughty boys made several small sinners in the back benches shake in their shoes -- many innocent infants felt they should never dare to go to bed in the dark again ; and the wild shriek with which she ended her announcement that 'the goblins are about ' quite brought down the house , and the children shouted and yelled and cheered as the Rector strove to get order once more , and wind up the proceedings with the National Anthem", "context_len": 92}}}, "appease": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "When d'Iberville sojourned among them , lightning struck their temple and destroyed it , upon which the mothers sacrificed their infants , to appease the wrath of the incensed deity , by throwing them into the burning edifice ( Margry IV , 414 , etc", "context_len": 45}}}, "incensed": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "When d'Iberville sojourned among them , lightning struck their temple and destroyed it , upon which the mothers sacrificed their infants , to appease the wrath of the incensed deity , by throwing them into the burning edifice ( Margry IV , 414 , etc", "context_len": 45}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "He had been at first incensed at the indignity which had been put upon his sister by Philip 's treatment of her ; but Philip now tried to appease his anger , also , by friendly negotiations and messages", "context_len": 39}}}, "deity": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 61, "context": "Among the tribes of the Maskoki family , we notice the following ethnographic practices : the use of the red and white colors as symbols of war and peace , an extensive system of totemic gentes , the use of the Ilex cassine for the manufacture of the black drink , the erection of artificial mounds , the belief in a deity called `` Master of Life , '' and original sun-worship", "context_len": 72}}}, "topographically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The Taensa grammar speaks of a northern and of a southern , more polished dialect , but does not locate them topographically", "context_len": 22}}}, "contradistinction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "[ 24 ] The French called them `` les petits Ta\u00ebnsas '' in contradistinction to the `` great ( or northern ) Ta\u00ebnsas , '' on Mississippi and Taensa rivers", "context_len": 30}}}, "corrupt": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The Taensa were then speaking their native language and , besides this , a corrupt Chicasa dialect , called the Mobilian language by the French", "context_len": 25}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "`` Aunt Patty , you have laid up your treasure where moth and rust do not corrupt ; but surely it is bearing interest on earth", "context_len": 26}}}, "warring": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 51, "context": "It is not unlikely that these Taensa were identical with the `` petits Ta\u00ebnsas '' seen by Lemoyne d'Iberville at the Huma town in March 1700 , and described by him as migratory , but living most of the time at three days ' distance west of Huma , and then warring against the Bayogoulas", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The cause of the warring , or the pretense for it , against `` some other Indians from the west '' is curiously similar to the rivalry in athletic sports , which took place between the western Iroquois and their subdivisions , and finally led to the destruction of the Erie or K\u00e1'hkwa Indians ( Cusick , Johnson )", "context_len": 59}}}, "unsuccessfully": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "[ Footnote 24 : At that time they were warring unsuccessfully against the Huma ( 1713 ) ; P\u00e9nicaut ( in Margry V , 508", "context_len": 25}}}, "sq": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 33, "context": "Follows the account of the Chicasa migration , as told by their old men to the United States agent stationed among them , and printed in Schoolcraft , Indians , I , 309 sq :", "context_len": 35}}}, "migratory": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "It is not unlikely that these Taensa were identical with the `` petits Ta\u00ebnsas '' seen by Lemoyne d'Iberville at the Huma town in March 1700 , and described by him as migratory , but living most of the time at three days ' distance west of Huma , and then warring against the Bayogoulas", "context_len": 55}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Migratory dispositions seem to have inhered to the Tonica or Tunica tribe in a higher degree than to their southern neighbors , for in the short lapse of two centuries we see them stationed at more than three places", "context_len": 39}}}, "sustenance": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "The Gulf States ' Indians were no longer in the condition of pure hunting tribes ; they had settled in stationary villages , and derived the main part of their sustenance from agriculture and fishing", "context_len": 35}}}, "denis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Denis", "context_len": 1}}}, "defines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "P\u00e9nicaut defines the name differently , for he states ( Margry V , 387 ) `` we found ( northwest of Lake Pontchartrain ) a river , Tandgepao , which in the Indian signifies ' bled blanc", "context_len": 37}}}, "incorporated": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 63, "context": "The Greek historians , on the other hand , make no mention of this visit to Jerusalem ; and some persons think that it was never made , but that the story arose and was propagated from generation to generation among the Jews , through the influence of their desire to magnify the importance and influence of their worship , and that Josephus incorporated the account into his history without sufficiently verifying the facts", "context_len": 74}}}, "disrupted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "The Tangibao tribe was `` destroyed by the Oumas , '' as stated in a passage of Margry ( IV , 168 ) ; by which is meant , that they were scattered and their tribal connection disrupted", "context_len": 38}}}, "wends": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "This stream wends its way first south , then west , in a semi-circular course , around the present city of Natchez , Mississippi , and runs at an average distance of three to four miles from it", "context_len": 38}}}, "designating": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "The substantive noun does not inflect for number except in a few terms designating persons which form a plural in -agi , -aki : m\u00edko chief , m\u00edkagi chiefs , to be distinguished from m\u00edkalgi class from which chiefs are chosen ; h\u00fananwa man , h\u00f3kti woman ; hunant\u00e1gi , h\u00f3ktagi", "context_len": 51}}}, "elided": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "The Cheroki Indians call a Nache , Natche or N\u00e1ktche person A no tse , A nno tse , the people or tribe An\u00edno tse , shortened into Ani ` htse , which proves that a guttural has been elided from the present form of the name", "context_len": 47}}}, "adverb": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "`` Or the superlative is expressed by the augmentative adverb m\u00e1hi : very , quite , greatly , largely y\u00edktchi m\u00e1hi , the strongest , which at the same time means : very strong , quite strong ; ` l\u00e1ko m\u00e1hi largest and very large ; m\u00e1himahi tallest and very tall ; the latter also being expressed by a lengthening of the vowel : m\u0101 hi very tall", "context_len": 68}}}, "celebrity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "centralized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The Naktche tribe owes its celebrity and almost romantic fame to several causes : their towns were populous , the government more centralized than that of the surrounding native populations ; the French settled in their vicinity , and hence their authors have left to posterity more information concerning their confederacy than concerning other tribes ; their stubborn resistance to French encroachments gave them a high reputation for bravery ; their religious customs , centering in a highly developed sun-worship , made of them an object of curious interest and far-going ethnologic speculation for the white colonists , whose views on the Naktche we must receive with the utmost caution", "context_len": 110}}}, "annalist": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The annalist P\u00e9nicaut , who visited these parts in 1704 , states that the nine villages were situated in a delightful country , swarming with buffaloes , drained by rivulets and partly wooded", "context_len": 33}}}, "infanticide": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He alludes to their human sacrifices , the frequency of infanticide , and gives descriptions of their temple , perpetual fire , their `` marche des cadavres '' and articles of dress", "context_len": 32}}}, "topographical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "I presume that they are given in the topographical order as they followed each other on St", "context_len": 17}}}, "etymologies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The etymologies added to the names contain the opinions of the Creek delegates visiting Washington every year , and they seldom differed among each other on any name", "context_len": 28}}}, "suffixed": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "But in other respects , case and many other relations are pointed out by an extensive series of suffixed or enclitic syllables , mostly monosyllabic , which Byington calls article-pronouns , and writes as separate words", "context_len": 36}}}, "homonymous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "The frequent occurrence of homonymous terms forms a peculiar difficulty in the study of the dialects", "context_len": 16}}}, "frenchified": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Although these names are considerably frenchified in their orthography , the meaning of some admits of no doubt", "context_len": 18}}}, "admits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Although these names are considerably frenchified in their orthography , the meaning of some admits of no doubt", "context_len": 18}}}, "sites": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "The frequent removals of towns to new sites , lying at short distances only , may be easily explained by the unhealthiness of the old site , produced by the constant accumulation of refuse and filth around the towns , which never had anything like sewers or efficient regulations of sanitary police", "context_len": 52}}}, "archives": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 70, "context": "Wilfrid at Aescendune , it seems in some sort my duty , following the example of many worthy brethren , to write some account of the origin and history of the priory over which it has pleased God to make me overseer , and to note , as occasion serves from time to time , such passing events as seem worthy of remembrance ; which record , deposited in the archives of the house , may preserve our memory when our bodies are but dust , and other brethren fill our places in the choir", "context_len": 95}}}, "dispersion": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "The settlement of the French on the heights of Natchez , the growing animosity of the natives against the intruders , the three successive wars , the massacre of the colonists in November , 1729 , and the final dispersion of the tribe in February 1730 , are well-known historic facts and need not be repeated in this volume", "context_len": 59}}}, "disorganized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The disorganized warriors retreated with their families to different parts of the country", "context_len": 13}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The Thebans , scattered and disorganized as they were , had sent envoys in this way", "context_len": 16}}}, "confluence": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` The subterranean dwellings , mentioned and visited by Milfort as being the legendary home of the `` Moskoquis , '' are not mentioned here ; and in French colonial times the `` Forks of Red river '' designated the confluence of Washita and Red rivers", "context_len": 46}}}, "harbored": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "Hawkins ' time ( 1799 ) , on a creek of the same name , joining Coosa river sixty miles above its confluence with Tallapoosa river , and harbored from fifty to one hundred warriors ( Hawkins , p", "context_len": 39}}}, "peculiarity": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "The Grigras were called so on account of a peculiarity in their pronunciation ; it probably referred to what the French call grasseyer , and the Canadian French parler gras", "context_len": 30}}}, "implies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "In conversation the Grigras often used this word grigra , which also implies the use of the articulation R", "context_len": 19}}}, "articulation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "In conversation the Grigras often used this word grigra , which also implies the use of the articulation R", "context_len": 19}}}, "polysynthetic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Naktche is a vocalic language , rich in verbal forms , and , to judge from a few specimens , polysynthetic to a considerable degree in its affixes", "context_len": 28}}}, "affixes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "Naktche is a vocalic language , rich in verbal forms , and , to judge from a few specimens , polysynthetic to a considerable degree in its affixes", "context_len": 28}}}, "dispositions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Migratory dispositions seem to have inhered to the Tonica or Tunica tribe in a higher degree than to their southern neighbors , for in the short lapse of two centuries we see them stationed at more than three places", "context_len": 39}}}, "inhered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Migratory dispositions seem to have inhered to the Tonica or Tunica tribe in a higher degree than to their southern neighbors , for in the short lapse of two centuries we see them stationed at more than three places", "context_len": 39}}}, "navigable": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "The king and his favourite chieftains took up their abode at Carisbrooke , situate about eight miles up the stream , but above the spot where it ceases to be navigable", "context_len": 31}}}, "massacred": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "In 1706 , when expecting to become involved in a conflict with the Chicasa and Alibamu Indians , the Tonica tribe , or a part of it , fled southward to the towns of the Huma , and massacred a number of these near the site where New Orleans was built afterwards ( French , Hist", "context_len": 56}}}, "influx": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "The Moskoquis discovered their tracks and followed them to the Ohio river , north shore , thence to the influx of Wabash river , then crossed Ohio river into what is now Kentucky , continued their march in a southern direction , and finally arrived in the Yazoo country , where they stayed for several years", "context_len": 56}}}, "locates": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "44 , locates them a few miles below that spot , opposite Pointe Coup\u00e9e and ten miles below the Pascagoulas , on Mississippi river", "context_len": 24}}}, "confirming": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Philip wished to make this wedding not merely the means of confirming his reconciliation with his former wife and son , and establishing friendly relations with the King of Epirus : he also prized it as an occasion for paying marked and honorable attention to the princes and great generals of the other states of Greece", "context_len": 56}}}, "ed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Every time I git sorter billious and downcast an \u2019 dejec \u2019 ed I look \u2019 round till I find somebody that \u2019 s wuss off than I be , an \u2019 then I take holt an \u2019 try to give them a lift , an \u2019 that cheers me up an \u2019 makes me feel thankful an \u2019 content with my lot", "context_len": 63}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "[ Footnote 89 : Sic ( berec ed dunya ) ; but dunya ( the world ) is perhaps meant to be taken here by synecdoche m the sense of `` sky", "context_len": 32}}}, "nineteenth": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 41, "context": "The genealogy of the Maskoki tribes can not be established on anthropological , that is racial , characteristics ; these Indians formerly incorporated so many alien elements into their towns , and have become so largely mixed with half-castes in the nineteenth century , that a division on racial grounds has become almost impossible", "context_len": 54}}}, "affinities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 53, "context": "The Indians of the upper and middle part of the peninsula of California claim descent from the Yuma population north of them ; the Tinn\u00e9-Apache of New Mexico and the Gila river , Arizona , also point to an ancient home in the far north , and both traditions are confirmed by the affinities of their dialects", "context_len": 57}}}, "philological": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Latham , in his `` Opuscula ; Essays , chiefly philological , '' etc", "context_len": 14}}}, "septs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "Every tribe of a people or nation has its own migration myth or legend , which in some points coincides , in others conflicts with those of the neighboring septs", "context_len": 30}}}, "fallacious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "When Milfort passed through the Red river country about 1780 , the Caddo , whom he describes as fallacious in trading , were at war with the Cha'hta ( M\u00e9moire , p", "context_len": 32}}}, "famine": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "In 1705 some Colapissa from the Talcatcha river , four leagues from Lake Pontchartrain , settled upon the northern bank of this lake at Castembayouque ( now Bayou Castin , at Mandeville ) , and were joined , six months after , by a party of `` Nassitoches , '' whom famine had driven from their homes on Red river", "context_len": 60}}}, "parentage": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 44, "context": "If he shared to some extent the fears that had taken such strong possession of Ashati and Neram in regard to Sobrinini and her island of snakes , his eagerness to learn from the lips of the old witch woman those facts about his parentage that had been so long denied him drove all other feelings from his mind", "context_len": 59}}}, "myths": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 3, "context": "Many of these myths are etymological , as that of the Greeks , stating that they originated from stones thrown by Deucalion behind himself ( stone , and people ) ; that of Adam , being created from earth ; adam , in Hebrew , signifies person and mankind , adom , adum , fem", "context_len": 55}}}, "monogamists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "These Indians were strict monogamists", "context_len": 5}}}, "chieftaincy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The chieftaincy was a life-long office among them", "context_len": 8}}}, "foreheads": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Ye have one more chance , candidates , '' went on the president , as he touched the foreheads of the four with something cold and clammy -- a hand , from the feel of it , but it was only a rubber glove , filled with cracked ice", "context_len": 50}}}, "derivation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 35, "context": "of keramat ) , properly a favour or mark of grace , a supernatural gift bestowed by God upon His pious servants , by virtue whereof they perform miracles , which latter are also by derivation called keramat", "context_len": 38}}}, "suffixes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 24, "context": "The case inflection of the substantive , adjective , of some pronouns and of the nominal forms of the verb is effected by the suffixes : -i for the absolute , -ut for the subjective , -un for the objective case : y\u00e1ti person , y\u00e1tut , y\u00e1tun ; n\u00e1ki what , which , n\u00e1kut , n\u00e1kun", "context_len": 57}}}, "prefixes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 33, "context": "There are two classes of personal pronouns , the relative and the absolute ( the former referring to something said previously ) , but the personal inflection of the verb is effected by prefixes , the predicative suffix 'h being added to the end of each form in the affirmative conjugation", "context_len": 51}}}, "pronouns": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 62, "context": "There are terms which occur in all dialects in the same or nearly the same form , as h\u00e1si sun , \u00edtchu , \u00edssi deer , \u00f3fi , \u00edfa dog , the terms for chief , black , yellow , bird , snake , buffalo , turtle , fox ( also in Cheroki : tsu'hl\u00e1 ) , the numerals and the personal pronouns ; they must , therefore , have been once the common property of the still undivided , primordial tribe", "context_len": 82}}}, "faculty": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` I must insist that , hereafter , when I , or any other member of the faculty approaches , all indiscriminate knocking or kicking of balls cease until we have passed on", "context_len": 33}}}, "computation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The faculty for forming compound words is considerable , and the numerals show the decimal form of computation", "context_len": 18}}}, "indicates": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 71, "context": "They had been the staunchest Indian supporters of the new British colony , and had sent 28 men of auxiliary troops to Colonel Barnwell , to defeat the Tuscarora insurrection on the coast of North Carolina ( 1712-13 ) , when they suddenly revolted on April 15th , 1715 , committed the most atrocious deeds against helpless colonists , and showed themselves to be quite the reverse of what their name indicates ( y\u00e1masi , y\u00e1massi , the Creek term for mild , gentle , peaceable [ 44 ] )", "context_len": 90}}}, "th": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 17, "context": "Oh ! such a bad cat ! ! ! ! ! ! Jennie she can not say th , and afterwards I laughed about it , but Bessie said I ought not , because she can not say r and that was 'most the same", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "-- The dialects possess the sound f and the palatalized l ( ` l ) , but lack th , v and r , while nasalization of the vocalic element is more peculiar to the western than to the eastern divisions", "context_len": 41}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The Sh\u00e1wano word for creek , brook , branch of river is metht\u00e9kui ; Sh\u00e1wano often has th where the northern dialects have s ( th\u00edpi river , in Potawat", "context_len": 30}}}, "extenuation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "In extenuation of this charge , Milfort asserts that they `` do not eat men , but roast them only , on account of the cruelties first enacted against their ancestors by the Spaniards '' ( p", "context_len": 37}}}, "asserts": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 7, "context": "In extenuation of this charge , Milfort asserts that they `` do not eat men , but roast them only , on account of the cruelties first enacted against their ancestors by the Spaniards '' ( p", "context_len": 37}}}, "comprises": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The enumeration of the southern linguistic stocks winds up with the At\u00e1kapa ; but it comprises only the families the existence of which is proved by vocabularies", "context_len": 27}}}, "areas": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "The areas of the southern languages being usually small , they could easily escape discovery , insomuch as the attention of the explorers and colonists was directed more toward ethnography than toward aboriginal linguistics", "context_len": 34}}}, "linguistics": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "The areas of the southern languages being usually small , they could easily escape discovery , insomuch as the attention of the explorers and colonists was directed more toward ethnography than toward aboriginal linguistics", "context_len": 34}}}, "mountainous": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 38, "context": "The left arm hung to his hip while the other lay on the top of the rock , and was extended full length , the hand closed around the butt of a revolver which was leveled at the mountainous brute , lumbering heavily forward with his head swinging from side to side", "context_len": 52}}}, "explorer": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 7, "context": "Billy Smith , son of an eminent explorer , learns to pilot a seaplane aboard a Naval Air Station , and immediately fares forth upon a series of the most exciting cruises and mysterious quests by air , by land and by sea that have ever fallen to the lot of man", "context_len": 52}}}, "compression": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "What roils me , added Alden to himself , with a flash of the eye and a compression of the lips , is that he must have taken my politeness last night for fear of him", "context_len": 36}}}, "analogy": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 4, "context": "We see the same analogy reigning at the present day , when the sons of the wealthy , who are born to fortune , substitute pride , and arrogance , and vicious self-indulgence and waste for the modesty , and prudence , and virtue of their sires , by means of which the fortune was acquired", "context_len": 56}}}, "corroborated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "This Piper did with evident great reluctance and disappointment ; and , Theodore Welcome being present , he was called to the stand , where he corroborated the statement of the last witness and also identified the coin as the one he had received from Davis", "context_len": 46}}}, "inactivity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Owing to the inactivity of the local historians , our ethnographic information on the North and South Carolina Indians is extremely meagre and unsatisfactory", "context_len": 24}}}, "decennium": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Some of them , as the Timucua and Y\u00e1massi settlements , are taken from dates somewhat earlier , while the location of the At\u00e1kapa tribe is known to us only from the first decennium of the nineteenth century", "context_len": 38}}}, "conveys": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "The marking of the linguistic areas by dots , pointing to the tribal settlements , answers much better the purpose than the coloring of large areas , which conveys the erroneous impression that the population was scattered all over a certain country", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "[ Footnote 153 : I prefer this old English form of the Arabic word Meghrebiy ( a native of El Meghreb or North-Western Africa ) to `` Moor , '' as the latter conveys a false impression to the modern reader , who would naturally suppose him to be a native of Morocco , whereas the enchanter came , as will presently appear , from biladu ' l gherbi ' l jewwaniy , otherwise Ifrikiyeh , i", "context_len": 77}}}, "agriculture": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Important benefits were thus conferred on the whole neighbourhood ; agriculture flourished , learning increased , a sanctuary for the oppressed was provided , and last , though not least in Ina 's eyes , a bulwark against Mercia was provided for the neighbourhood ; while the poor and the afflicted found their happiness in every way promoted by the neighbourhood of the monastery", "context_len": 64}}}, "completeness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "The location of the Chicasa , Cheroki , Seminole and Caddo ( Pani ) tribes were not indicated with that completeness which the subject requires", "context_len": 25}}}, "lineage": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 91, "context": "He knew not then that the example before him would nerve him in moments of severest trial , then fast approaching , that the one accusation urged against the Christians , which he had felt most keenly , that of cowardice , was answered in the weak yet valiant boy , who found strength in the name of Christ to endure all for His sake ; neither did his fierce countrymen know that they were preparing a disappointment for the pagan Anlaf , and for all those of his house and lineage", "context_len": 92}}}, "intermissions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "With short intermissions they kept up warfare with all the circumjacent Indian communities , and also among each other", "context_len": 19}}}, "circumjacent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "With short intermissions they kept up warfare with all the circumjacent Indian communities , and also among each other", "context_len": 19}}}, "corruptible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "We have the cruel and lurking Chicasa , the powerful and ingenious but treacherous and corruptible Cha'hta , the magnanimous and hospitable , proud and revengeful Creek , the aggressive Alibamu , the quarrelsome Y\u00e1massi , and the self-willed , independent Seminole , jealous of the enjoyment of his savage freedom in the swamps and everglades of the semi-tropical peninsula", "context_len": 60}}}, "magnanimous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "We have the cruel and lurking Chicasa , the powerful and ingenious but treacherous and corruptible Cha'hta , the magnanimous and hospitable , proud and revengeful Creek , the aggressive Alibamu , the quarrelsome Y\u00e1massi , and the self-willed , independent Seminole , jealous of the enjoyment of his savage freedom in the swamps and everglades of the semi-tropical peninsula", "context_len": 60}}}, "everglades": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 55, "context": "We have the cruel and lurking Chicasa , the powerful and ingenious but treacherous and corruptible Cha'hta , the magnanimous and hospitable , proud and revengeful Creek , the aggressive Alibamu , the quarrelsome Y\u00e1massi , and the self-willed , independent Seminole , jealous of the enjoyment of his savage freedom in the swamps and everglades of the semi-tropical peninsula", "context_len": 60}}}, "egotistic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The irresolute and egotistic policy of these tribes often caused serious difficulties to the government of the English and French colonies , and some of them constantly wavered in their adhesion between the French and the English cause", "context_len": 38}}}, "adhesion": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 30, "context": "The irresolute and egotistic policy of these tribes often caused serious difficulties to the government of the English and French colonies , and some of them constantly wavered in their adhesion between the French and the English cause", "context_len": 38}}}, "folklore": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 34, "context": "The quaint sayings of the villagers are often as full of humour as those Scotch books over which people rave so much , and many of their stories are such interesting survivals of ancient folklore that I have often longed to collect them in writing", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` folk-lore '' and `` folklore '' )", "context_len": 8}}}, "anthropologically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The two differ anthropologically in their exterior , the people of the western or Cha'hta branch being thick-set and heavy , that of the eastern or Creek connection more lithe and tall", "context_len": 32}}}, "prognathism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Prognathism is not frequent among them , and the complexion of both is a rather dark cinnamon , with the southern olive tinge", "context_len": 23}}}, "endows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The general intelligence of this gifted race renders it susceptible for civilization , endows it with eloquence , but does not always restrain it from the outbursts of the wildest passion", "context_len": 31}}}, "practices": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Among the tribes of the Maskoki family , we notice the following ethnographic practices : the use of the red and white colors as symbols of war and peace , an extensive system of totemic gentes , the use of the Ilex cassine for the manufacture of the black drink , the erection of artificial mounds , the belief in a deity called `` Master of Life , '' and original sun-worship", "context_len": 72}}}, "anthropophagy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "No instances of anthropophagy are recorded , but the custom of scalping seems to have been indigenous among them", "context_len": 19}}}, "scalping": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "No instances of anthropophagy are recorded , but the custom of scalping seems to have been indigenous among them", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "From the roofs were dangling on the inside heterogeneous emblems of peace and trophies of war , as eagles ' feathers , swans ' wings , wooden scalping knives , war clubs , red-painted wands , bunches of hoops on which to dry their scalps , bundles of a war-physic called snake-root ( s\u00ednika in Cheroki ) , baskets , etc", "context_len": 61}}}, "indigenous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "No instances of anthropophagy are recorded , but the custom of scalping seems to have been indigenous among them", "context_len": 19}}}, "scalped": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Their warriors cut off and scalped some of the Moskoqui scouts , who had come to ascertain their whereabouts", "context_len": 19}}}, "scalps": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 87, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Whenever any of their warriors get scalps , they give them to the boys to go and throw them into the sink where the dog was", "context_len": 26}}}, "deformation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The artificial flattening of the foreheads of male infants seems to have prevailed in the western branch only , but some kind of skull deformation could be observed throughout the Gulf territories", "context_len": 32}}}, "adhering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The re-interment of dead bodies , after cleaning their bones from the adhering muscles several months after death , is recorded more especially for the western branch , but was probably observed among all tribes in various modifications", "context_len": 38}}}, "modifications": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "The re-interment of dead bodies , after cleaning their bones from the adhering muscles several months after death , is recorded more especially for the western branch , but was probably observed among all tribes in various modifications", "context_len": 38}}}, "genealogy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The genealogy of the Maskoki tribes can not be established on anthropological , that is racial , characteristics ; these Indians formerly incorporated so many alien elements into their towns , and have become so largely mixed with half-castes in the nineteenth century , that a division on racial grounds has become almost impossible", "context_len": 54}}}, "subdivision": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Hence , the only characteristic by which a subdivision of the family can be attempted , is that of language", "context_len": 20}}}, "synopsis": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Following their ancient topographic location from east to west , we obtain the following synopsis :", "context_len": 16}}}, "segmentation": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 20, "context": "This fact seems to point either to a long historic development of the tribe , through which so large a segmentation was brought about , or to internal dissensions , which could produce the same result", "context_len": 36}}}, "repelled": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "The numerous incorporations of foreign elements have not been able to alter the purity of their language ; the number of intrusive words is very small , and the grammar has repelled every foreign intrusion", "context_len": 35}}}, "approximately": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "The degree of dialectic difference points approximately to the date of the separation of the respective communities , and untold centuries must have elapsed since the two main branches of the family were torn asunder , for Cha'hta differs about as much from Creek as the literary German does from Icelandic", "context_len": 51}}}, "diverge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Although the dialects of Maskoki do not now diverge from each other more than did the Semitic dialects two thousand years ago , the time when they all had a common language , or , in other words , the time preceding the separation into four divisions must lie further back than eight or ten thousand years", "context_len": 57}}}, "reconstruct": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "We can not expect to reconstruct the parent Maskoki language spoken at that time but very imperfectly , since the oldest text known to exist in any of the dialects dates from A", "context_len": 33}}}, "reconstruction": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "An approach to its reconstruction could be attempted by carefully comparing the lexicon and grammatic forms of the dialects presently spoken , and an individual acquainted with them all , or at least with their four representatives , might also compose a comparative grammar of these dialects as spoken at the present epoch of their development , which would reveal many points concerning the ancient or historic shape of the language once common to all these tribes", "context_len": 77}}}, "lexicon": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "An approach to its reconstruction could be attempted by carefully comparing the lexicon and grammatic forms of the dialects presently spoken , and an individual acquainted with them all , or at least with their four representatives , might also compose a comparative grammar of these dialects as spoken at the present epoch of their development , which would reveal many points concerning the ancient or historic shape of the language once common to all these tribes", "context_len": 77}}}, "palatalized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "-- The dialects possess the sound f and the palatalized l ( ` l ) , but lack th , v and r , while nasalization of the vocalic element is more peculiar to the western than to the eastern divisions", "context_len": 41}}}, "nasalization": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "-- The dialects possess the sound f and the palatalized l ( ` l ) , but lack th , v and r , while nasalization of the vocalic element is more peculiar to the western than to the eastern divisions", "context_len": 41}}}, "alveolar": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Creek possesses all sounds of the general Maskoki alphabet ; but here and in Hitchiti the gutturals g , k , are often pronounced with the tongue resting upon the fore or alveolar part of the palate", "context_len": 37}}}, "palate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Creek possesses all sounds of the general Maskoki alphabet ; but here and in Hitchiti the gutturals g , k , are often pronounced with the tongue resting upon the fore or alveolar part of the palate", "context_len": 37}}}, "phonetic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "The sway of phonetic laws is all-powerful here , and they operate whenever a slight conflict of syllables disagreeing with the delicate ear of the Cha'hta Indian takes place", "context_len": 29}}}, "aspirated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "Not aspirated", "context_len": 2}}}, "spirants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Spirants", "context_len": 1}}}, "nasals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Nasals", "context_len": 1}}}, "trills": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "He did not go to the front door , but , well acquainted with the disposition of the household , paused under a rear window , picked up a handful of gravel , threw it against the upper panes , and gave three low but distinct whistling trills", "context_len": 48}}}, "palatals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Palatals tch , ts dsh , ds y \u0144 ` l", "context_len": 11}}}, "ts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Palatals tch , ts dsh , ds y \u0144 ` l", "context_len": 11}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The alternating processes observed here also occur in most other Indian and illiterate languages : tch , dsh alternate with ts , ds , h with k , ; g with the other gutturals , b with p , d with t , \u0101 with e , o with u", "context_len": 50}}}, "labials": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Labials p b f w m", "context_len": 6}}}, "nasalized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Vowels : -- i , e , \u0101 , a , o , u ; with their long and nasalized sounds", "context_len": 21}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "is\u00e1gig\u014d ? ( suffix -g\u014d often nasalized into -g\u014dn , -k\u014dn , -kun )", "context_len": 14}}}, "vowel": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 62, "context": "`` Or the superlative is expressed by the augmentative adverb m\u00e1hi : very , quite , greatly , largely y\u00edktchi m\u00e1hi , the strongest , which at the same time means : very strong , quite strong ; ` l\u00e1ko m\u00e1hi largest and very large ; m\u00e1himahi tallest and very tall ; the latter also being expressed by a lengthening of the vowel : m\u0101 hi very tall", "context_len": 68}}}, "consonant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The syllable is quite simple in its structure ; it consists either of a vowel only , or begins with one consonant ( in the eastern division with one or two ) , and ends in a vowel", "context_len": 38}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "[ Footnote 141 : Or we may read `` went out , glad and rejoicing , with ( bi ) the young lady ; '' but the reading in the test is more consonant with the general style of the Nights", "context_len": 41}}}, "deviations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Deviations from this rule must be explained by phonetic alteration , elision , etc", "context_len": 14}}}, "elision": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Deviations from this rule must be explained by phonetic alteration , elision , etc", "context_len": 14}}}, "verbified": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "There is no real substantive verb in the language , and adjectives , when becoming verbified , are turned into attributive verbs , as in Creek : w\u00e1nti strong , hard ; tsaw\u00e1ntus I am strong ; w\u00e1ntus he , it is strong , hard ; w\u00e1ntatik not strong ; w\u00e1ntigus he is very strong ; w\u00e1ntatis he is not strong ; wanta'hl\u00e1tis he is not strong at all", "context_len": 69}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ Footnote 46 : This adjective is found verbified in isiman\u014dl\u0103idshit `` he has caused himself to be a runaway", "context_len": 20}}}, "attributively": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "The numeral has two forms for the cardinal number : one used attributively , and another , abbreviated from it , used exclusively for counting ; there are , outside of this , forms for the ordinal , for the distributive , and for the adverbial numeral", "context_len": 47}}}, "participles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The fact that all adjectives can be verbified , could be better expressed as follows : The adjectives used attributively are participles of attributive verbs and inflected for number like these , their so-called plural being the plural form of a verb", "context_len": 42}}}, "attributive": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 20, "context": "There is no real substantive verb in the language , and adjectives , when becoming verbified , are turned into attributive verbs , as in Creek : w\u00e1nti strong , hard ; tsaw\u00e1ntus I am strong ; w\u00e1ntus he , it is strong , hard ; w\u00e1ntatik not strong ; w\u00e1ntigus he is very strong ; w\u00e1ntatis he is not strong ; wanta'hl\u00e1tis he is not strong at all", "context_len": 69}}}, "inflected": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 26, "context": "The fact that all adjectives can be verbified , could be better expressed as follows : The adjectives used attributively are participles of attributive verbs and inflected for number like these , their so-called plural being the plural form of a verb", "context_len": 42}}}, "noun": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 53, "context": "Burton reads yamin and supposes it to be a copyist 's error for yasmin , but this is a mistake ; the word in the text is clearly yas , though the final s , being somewhat carelessly written in the Arabic MS , might easily be mistaken for mn with an undotted noun", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "A dual exists neither in the noun nor in the pronoun , but in most of the intransitive verbs", "context_len": 19}}}, "finite": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "From all this it becomes evident , that the Creek verb surpasses in its large power of polysynthesis the Algonkin , Dakota and Kalapuya verb , and in the richness of its forms approaches closely to the Iroquois verb , which is poorer in tenses , but has an impersonal conjugation and fourteen persons to each tense of the finite verb", "context_len": 61}}}, "prefixed": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": ", prefixed , which is the possessive pronoun , are nominal forms , and represent nomina agentis and nomina actionis", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "The possessive pronoun of the third person singular and plural ( im- , in- , i- ) is prefixed in the same manner to substantives to indicate possession , as it is to verbs to show that an act is performed in the interest or to the detriment of the verbal subject or object", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The verb forms a considerable number of tenses and incorporates the prefixed object-pronoun , the interrogative and the negative particle ; it has a form for the passive and one for the reflective voice", "context_len": 34}}}, "possessive": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 1, "context": "The possessive pronoun of the third person singular and plural ( im- , in- , i- ) is prefixed in the same manner to substantives to indicate possession , as it is to verbs to show that an act is performed in the interest or to the detriment of the verbal subject or object", "context_len": 54}}}, "pronoun": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 2, "context": "The possessive pronoun of the third person singular and plural ( im- , in- , i- ) is prefixed in the same manner to substantives to indicate possession , as it is to verbs to show that an act is performed in the interest or to the detriment of the verbal subject or object", "context_len": 54}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The pronoun im- , in- , i- in all these names ( ihinih\u00e1lgi , intast\u0115na \u00e1lgi , etc", "context_len": 18}}}, "substantives": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "( 2 ) when two nouns , especially substantives , stand in the relation of possession , the possessor stands in the absolute case before the object possessed , the pronoun im- , in- , i- being prefixed to the latter", "context_len": 41}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "The possessive pronoun of the third person singular and plural ( im- , in- , i- ) is prefixed in the same manner to substantives to indicate possession , as it is to verbs to show that an act is performed in the interest or to the detriment of the verbal subject or object", "context_len": 54}}}, "diminutives": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "The three cases of the noun are not accurately distinguished from each other in their functions ; substantives form diminutives in -odshi , -osi , -usi , etc", "context_len": 28}}}, "detriment": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "One Saturday afternoon Peggy had had a long scramble through copse and bracken , over fences and along stubble fields , and had filled her big basket almost full , somewhat to the detriment of her costume and fingers , and she was now working her way home along the edge of the pasture , picking as she went", "context_len": 59}}}, "tenses": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 44, "context": "From all this it becomes evident , that the Creek verb surpasses in its large power of polysynthesis the Algonkin , Dakota and Kalapuya verb , and in the richness of its forms approaches closely to the Iroquois verb , which is poorer in tenses , but has an impersonal conjugation and fourteen persons to each tense of the finite verb", "context_len": 61}}}, "incorporates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "The verb forms a considerable number of tenses and incorporates the prefixed object-pronoun , the interrogative and the negative particle ; it has a form for the passive and one for the reflective voice", "context_len": 34}}}, "reduplication": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "They are formed by a partial reduplication of the radix , when the basis is monosyllabic , or often of the last syllable of the basis , when the word is polysyllabic", "context_len": 32}}}, "distributive": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 15, "context": "Many transitive verbs have , when their object stands in the plural , a ( distributive ) form differing entirely or partially from the one referring to an object in the singular ; a few others show this change , when their subject passes from the singular to the plural number", "context_len": 51}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "The numeral has two forms for the cardinal number : one used attributively , and another , abbreviated from it , used exclusively for counting ; there are , outside of this , forms for the ordinal , for the distributive , and for the adverbial numeral", "context_len": 47}}}, "frequentative": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "The majority of the adjectives and of the attributive verbs derived from them form derivatives , which in some instances may be called distributive , in others frequentative and iterative forms", "context_len": 31}}}, "iterative": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "The majority of the adjectives and of the attributive verbs derived from them form derivatives , which in some instances may be called distributive , in others frequentative and iterative forms", "context_len": 31}}}, "derivatives": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "The majority of the adjectives and of the attributive verbs derived from them form derivatives , which in some instances may be called distributive , in others frequentative and iterative forms", "context_len": 31}}}, "suffixation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Derivatives are formed by prefixation and suffixation , many of the derivational being identical with inflectional affixes in these dialects", "context_len": 20}}}, "derivational": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "Derivatives are formed by prefixation and suffixation , many of the derivational being identical with inflectional affixes in these dialects", "context_len": 20}}}, "inflectional": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Derivatives are formed by prefixation and suffixation , many of the derivational being identical with inflectional affixes in these dialects", "context_len": 20}}}, "agglutinative": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Although Maskoki speech , taken as a whole , belongs to the agglutinative type of languages , some forms of it , especially the predicative inflection of the verb and the vocalic changes in the radicals , strongly remind us of the inflective languages", "context_len": 44}}}, "predicative": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 36, "context": "There are two classes of personal pronouns , the relative and the absolute ( the former referring to something said previously ) , but the personal inflection of the verb is effected by prefixes , the predicative suffix 'h being added to the end of each form in the affirmative conjugation", "context_len": 51}}}, "radicals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Although Maskoki speech , taken as a whole , belongs to the agglutinative type of languages , some forms of it , especially the predicative inflection of the verb and the vocalic changes in the radicals , strongly remind us of the inflective languages", "context_len": 44}}}, "inflective": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 42, "context": "Although Maskoki speech , taken as a whole , belongs to the agglutinative type of languages , some forms of it , especially the predicative inflection of the verb and the vocalic changes in the radicals , strongly remind us of the inflective languages", "context_len": 44}}}, "syncope": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Words , phrases and sentences are sometimes composed by syncope , a process which is more characteristic of the agglutinative than of the inflective type , and is by no means confined to the languages of America", "context_len": 37}}}, "coincide": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Whether the forks of the Red river were supposed to coincide with the `` mouth of the earth '' in the legend can be decided only when we shall have a better knowledge of Creek folklore", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Your tastes and mine coincide to a nicety", "context_len": 8}}}, "lexical": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The table may be helpful for giving a general idea of the lexical differences existing between the dialects explored :", "context_len": 20}}}, "alb": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Alb", "context_len": 1}}}, "enables": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` Yet not one to be encouraged , save when it enables one to perform necessary deeds of daring for some worthy object , such as holy Scripture praises in the heroes of old", "context_len": 34}}}, "discrepancies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "This is such a favourable opportunity that I think we had better seize the occasion while we are on the spot to make fresh observations in case of any discrepancies in our descriptions", "context_len": 33}}}, "primordial": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 80, "context": "There are terms which occur in all dialects in the same or nearly the same form , as h\u00e1si sun , \u00edtchu , \u00edssi deer , \u00f3fi , \u00edfa dog , the terms for chief , black , yellow , bird , snake , buffalo , turtle , fox ( also in Cheroki : tsu'hl\u00e1 ) , the numerals and the personal pronouns ; they must , therefore , have been once the common property of the still undivided , primordial tribe", "context_len": 82}}}, "reducible": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The terms for maize disagree but apparently , and seem to be reducible to one radix , atch or ash ; the terms for dog agree in all dialects -- hence , the Maskoki tribes planted maize and kept dogs before , probably many centuries before they separated ; and the term \u00edfa went over from them to the Timucua", "context_len": 60}}}, "phallus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "This weed is said to have received its Maskoki names from a similarity of the top of the green plant with the phallus , which is called in Alibamu and Hitchiti : \u00f3ktchi or \u00e1ktchi", "context_len": 35}}}, "designates": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "The suffix -algi , though sometimes used as a plural suffix , designates collectivity : u-ika\u00edwa spring of water , u-ikaiw\u00e1lki place with water-springs , and u-ikain\u00e1lki people living at the springs ; al\u00edktcha conjurer , al\u00edktchalgi conjurers as one body , taken in a body", "context_len": 46}}}, "surd": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "The first syllable , as pronounced by the Creek Indians , contains a clear , short a , and that the name was written Muscogee and not Mascogee , is not to be wondered at , for the English language , with its surd , indistinct and strongly modified vocalization , will convert the clearest a into a u", "context_len": 59}}}, "vocalization": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "The first syllable , as pronounced by the Creek Indians , contains a clear , short a , and that the name was written Muscogee and not Mascogee , is not to be wondered at , for the English language , with its surd , indistinct and strongly modified vocalization , will convert the clearest a into a u", "context_len": 59}}}, "incorporation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 13, "context": "Whether the name Maskoki was given to the Creeks before or after the incorporation of the towns speaking other languages than theirs , we are unable to tell , but the name figures in some of the oldest documents on this people", "context_len": 42}}}, "swampland": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "8 , states that the Delawares call the Creeks Masquachki : `` swampland", "context_len": 13}}}, "component": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "If oki water formed a component part of it , it would stand first , as in the Hitchiti geographic terms Ok\u0115l\u00e1kni `` yellow water , '' Okifen\u00f3ke `` wavering , shaking waters , '' Okm\u00falgi `` bubbling water , '' Okitch\u00f3bi `` river , '' lit", "context_len": 47}}}, "suffix": {"freq": 19, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 35, "context": "Byington explains it by helping people , allies , in the Cha'hta ap\u0103l\u0103tchi \u00f3kla , but the original form of the name is Apala tchi \u00f3kli , not ap\u00e1latchi ; - tchi is a Hitchiti suffix of adjectives , and ap\u00e1lui in that dialect means `` on the other side of", "context_len": 51}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 51, "context": "my am- , an- , a- tcha- , inverted : atch- thy tchi- , tchi- , inverted : itch- his , her , its im- , in- , i- im- , in- , i- our p\u00fa'hni , pu- pun- , pu- , po- your tch\u00ed tchi , tchi- tchi- , with suffix their im- , in- , i- i- etc", "context_len": 60}}}, "watkins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Watkins ' Cree Dictionary , with its English , unscientific orthography , has musk\u0101g , musk\u0101k swamp , marsh ; Musk\u0101goo Swampy Indian , Maskegon ; Musk\u0101goowew he is a Swampy Indian", "context_len": 32}}}, "unscientific": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Watkins ' Cree Dictionary , with its English , unscientific orthography , has musk\u0101g , musk\u0101k swamp , marsh ; Musk\u0101goo Swampy Indian , Maskegon ; Musk\u0101goowew he is a Swampy Indian", "context_len": 32}}}, "abounds": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The country inhabited by the Maskoki proper abounds in creek bottoms overflowed in the rainy season , as the country around Opel\u00edka `` swamp-site '' ( from Creek : op\u00edlua , ap\u00edlua swamp , l\u00e4\u00edkita to be stretched out ) , Opil- ` l\u00e1ko `` great swamp , '' west of the above ( Hawkins , p", "context_len": 57}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Bomba knew at once what it was the saw-toothed piranha , the voracious fish that abounds in all the tributaries of the Amazon", "context_len": 23}}}, "uninhabitable": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 9, "context": "This chain forms a broad belt of lofty and uninhabitable land , and extends from the Black Sea to the Adriatic", "context_len": 21}}}, "bellicose": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Trumbull , taken from an Algonkin dialect , and means lungy , not bellicose , inferior , while their own name is Tsar\u00edksi ts\u00e1riks `` men of men", "context_len": 28}}}, "supra": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "supra", "context_len": 1}}}, "transmitted": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 22, "context": "In that country Ren\u00e9 de Laudonni\u00e8re in 1564 established a fortification in Port Royal Bay , called Charlefort , and the terms transmitted by him , being all of Creek origin , leave no doubt about the affinity of the natives , yatiqui interpreter , tola laurel , Olataraca , viz", "context_len": 51}}}, "tola": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "In that country Ren\u00e9 de Laudonni\u00e8re in 1564 established a fortification in Port Royal Bay , called Charlefort , and the terms transmitted by him , being all of Creek origin , leave no doubt about the affinity of the natives , yatiqui interpreter , tola laurel , Olataraca , viz", "context_len": 51}}}, "pr": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "pr", "context_len": 1}}}, "fairbanks": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Fairbanks , History of St", "context_len": 5}}}, "manageable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "125 , mentions the following dates from Spanish annals : `` The Yemasees , always peaceful and manageable , had a principal town , Macarisqui , near St", "context_len": 28}}}, "augustine": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 0, "context": "Augustine in 1742", "context_len": 3}}}, "surveyor": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "For , years before , when Jane 's `` forty '' was measured off by the surveyor , it had been developed that the Vigil homestead was out of bounds , and that a small strip of its back yard belonged in the Combs tract", "context_len": 45}}}, "perpetuates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "A few miles north of Savannah city there is a town and railroad crossing , Yemassee , which perpetuates their tribal name", "context_len": 22}}}, "staunchest": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "They had been the staunchest Indian supporters of the new British colony , and had sent 28 men of auxiliary troops to Colonel Barnwell , to defeat the Tuscarora insurrection on the coast of North Carolina ( 1712-13 ) , when they suddenly revolted on April 15th , 1715 , committed the most atrocious deeds against helpless colonists , and showed themselves to be quite the reverse of what their name indicates ( y\u00e1masi , y\u00e1massi , the Creek term for mild , gentle , peaceable [ 44 ] )", "context_len": 90}}}, "confederates": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 23, "context": "In 1713 , after the Alibamu had made an inroad into the Carolinas with a host of Kataba and Abika Indians , their confederates , the head-chief of the first-named tribe besought the French commander at Mobile bay to erect a fort in his own country", "context_len": 46}}}, "massacres": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Wholesale massacres of colonists occurred around Pocotaligo , on Port Royal Island and at Stono , and the number of victims was estimated at four hundred", "context_len": 26}}}, "provenience": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "Although the origin from the earth is certainly the most natural that could suggest itself to primitive man , there are a number of nations claiming provenience from the sky ( the Tukabatchi were let down from the sky in a gourd or calabash ) : from the sun ( Yuchi ) , from the moon , from the sea , from the ashes of fire ( Sh\u00e1wano ) , from eggs ( Quichhua ) or certain plants", "context_len": 78}}}, "tumuli": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "137 , speaks of the `` sepulchres or tumuli of the Yamasees who were here slain in the last decisive battle , the Creeks having driven them to this point , between the doubling of the river ( St", "context_len": 39}}}, "cemeteries": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 41, "context": "She is an inveterate relic-hunter ; is enraptured with Bunker Hill and the Old South ; delights in Cornhill , and wherever she can find a crooked old street that reminds her of Washington ; and pokes about all the old cemeteries , until I feel as eerie as Coleridge 's ancient mariner", "context_len": 53}}}, "disappears": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "To clear his young cousin of suspicion , the older boy undertakes to solve the mystery which deepens when one man disappears and another is found murdered on the golf course", "context_len": 31}}}, "ethnographically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "All the above dates permit us to conclude that , ethnographically , the Y\u00e1massi were for the main part of Creek origin , but that some foreign admixture , either Kataba or Yuchi , had taken place , which will account for the presence of their local names of foreign origin", "context_len": 51}}}, "disaffected": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Yamacraw Indians , who had followed him to the Savannah river , consisted mainly of disaffected Lower Creek and of some Y\u00e1massi Indians", "context_len": 24}}}, "seminole": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "SEMINOLE", "context_len": 1}}}, "bleating": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 98, "context": "There was the flower garden , delicious with all kinds of roses now in full bloom ; the very swing where papa used to swing when he was a boy , the stream and pond where he used to sail his boats and set up his water-mills ; and beyond all , the large farm-yard with its many outbuildings , looking almost like a village by itself ; while from one of the great barns whose wide doors stood open came the cackling of poultry and cooing of pigeons , the lowing of cows and oxen , and bleating of calves , all the pleasant noises of a large and thrifty farm", "context_len": 112}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "David had found it bleating beside its dead mother one bitter morning early in March , and had carried the poor orphan into the kitchen , where Nancy had reared it on a feeding-bottle like a baby", "context_len": 37}}}, "indians": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 0, "context": "Indians use the bare hand and arm -- utensils in common stable use among us whites are the metal curry-combs , bristle body-brushes , corn-brushes , rub rags , sponges , whisks and hoof picks", "context_len": 35}}}, "transient": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "The rest of them , like Yagua and others , seem to be of Caribbean origin , and a transient or stationary population of Caribs is mentioned by Hervas , Catalogo de las lenguas I , p", "context_len": 37}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Florence had enjoyed but a transient view of the lady 's features , but she already had a tolerably correct idea of her character", "context_len": 24}}}, "separatists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The Seminoles of modern times are a people compounded of the following elements : separatists from the Lower Creek and Hitchiti towns ; remnants of tribes partly civilized by the Spaniards ; Y\u00e1massi Indians and some negroes", "context_len": 37}}}, "productiveness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "26 , they had emigrated from Ok\u00f3ni , S\u00e1wokli , Yuf\u00e1la , Tam\u00e1 ` la , Apalatch\u00fakla and Hitchiti ( all of which are Lower Creek towns ) , being invited to Florida by the plenty of game , the mildness of the climate and the productiveness of the soil", "context_len": 50}}}, "immigrations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Some of the larger immigrations from the Creek towns into those parts occurred : in 1750 , after the end of the Revolutionary war , in 1808 and after the revolt of the Upper Creeks in 1814", "context_len": 37}}}, "abdicated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "Bartram traveled through the Seminole country , about 1773 , he was informed that Cuscowilla , a town on a lake of the same name and a sort of Seminole capital , had been built by Indians from Ok\u00f3ni old town , settled upon the Alachua plains : `` They abdicated the ancient Alachua town on the borders of the savanna , about fifty miles west from the river San Juan , and built here , calling the new town Cuscowilla", "context_len": 81}}}, "seceded": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The Seminoles were always regarded as a sort of outcasts by the Creek tribes from which they had seceded , and no doubt there were reasons for this", "context_len": 28}}}, "conjointly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "This refers more particularly to the Seminoles of the northern parts of what is now Florida ; these Indians showed , like the Creeks , hostile intentions towards the thirteen states during and after the Revolution , and conjointly with the Upper Creeks on Tallapoosa river concluded a treaty of friendship with the Spaniards at Pensacola in May , 1784", "context_len": 60}}}, "cession": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 3, "context": "It stipulated a cession of the lands between the Savannah and Altamaha rivers , and of some islands on the Atlantic coast , to the British ; it further stipulated promises to enter into a commercial treaty at a later date , to place themselves under the general government of Great Britain , to live in peace with the colonies , to capture runaway slaves and deliver them at Charleston , Savannah or Palachukla garrison for a consideration", "context_len": 78}}}, "annuities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 41, "context": "After the cession of Florida , and its incorporation into the American Union ( 1819 ) , the Seminoles gave up all their territory by the treaty of Fort Moultrie , September 18th , 1823 , receiving in exchange goods and annuities", "context_len": 42}}}, "dissented": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The larger portion were removed , but the more stubborn part dissented , and thus gave origin to one of the gravest conflicts which ever occurred between Indians and whites", "context_len": 30}}}, "entailing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "Primarily Lola 's air of hauteur provoked resentment ; but hauteur in poor attire would have been only amusing , while in red cashmere it was felt to be a serious matter , entailing upon every one the sense of a personal affront", "context_len": 43}}}, "census": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "278 ) , and in 1791 all the Creek `` gun-men '' were estimated to number between 5000 and 6000 ; the same number is given for these in the census of 1832 ( Schoolcraft V , 262 sqq", "context_len": 39}}}, "domination": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "This table however includes , with a few exceptions , only places situated near Apalachicola river ( east and west of it ) , in Alabama , Georgia and Florida ; the list was probably made at a time when Florida was still under Spanish domination , which accounts for the fact that the county names are not added to the localities", "context_len": 62}}}, "appellations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 4, "context": "Any of the nine appellations contained in the item above , and any name composed with one of them , is a war-title ; all others , as Old Red Shoe , are simply names or war-names", "context_len": 37}}}, "parentheses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The remarks included in parentheses were added by myself", "context_len": 9}}}, "boundary": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 25, "context": "`` Would you like a heaven so small , so human , that mortal words could line it out , and mortal wishes be its boundary ? The things we look for are prepared by One whose thoughts are as far above our thoughts as the broad starlit heaven is above this little gaslit earth", "context_len": 55}, "NOUN": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 6, "context": "He organized armies , crossed the boundary between Europe and Asia , and spent the twelve years of his career in a most triumphant military incursion into the very center and seat of Asiatic power , destroying the Asiatic armies , conquering the most splendid cities , defeating or taking captive the kings , and princes , and generals that opposed his progress", "context_len": 63}}}, "comprehends": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Chehaws -- On the Flint river ( comprehends the villages planted there from Chiaha , on Chatahuchi river )", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The appellation of imm\u00edkagi comprehends all the men of that gens from which the m\u00edko in the town ceremonies , not the m\u00edko as a political office-holder , is selected", "context_len": 30}}}, "appreciable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The dialect of the vocabulary does not differ from Creek in any appreciable degree", "context_len": 14}}}, "intermix": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "On marriage customs and the annual busk of these Indians he makes the following remarks : `` They do not marry or intermix with the whites , and are very jealous of the virtue of their women , punishing with death any squaw that accepts the attentions of a white man", "context_len": 51}}}, "punishing": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 82, "context": "The strong sentiment of love and devotion with which he inspired all around him at the commencement of his career , was now gone , and his generals and officers were continually planning schemes to depose him from the power which he seemed no longer to have the energy to wield ; or , at least , Alexander was continually suspecting that such plans were formed , and he was kept in a continual state of uneasiness and anxiety in discovering and punishing them", "context_len": 84}}}, "squaw": {"freq": 28, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 28, "word_index": 10, "context": "Why had Nascanora made Hondura captive with Casson and the squaw ? Was it because Hondura and his tribe had been on friendly terms with Bomba and the old naturalist ? Or did Nascanora think that the Araos tribe was becoming too powerful , and had he hoped by depriving them of their chief to render his people helpless and throw confusion and panic into their hearts ?", "context_len": 68}}}, "accepts": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "On marriage customs and the annual busk of these Indians he makes the following remarks : `` They do not marry or intermix with the whites , and are very jealous of the virtue of their women , punishing with death any squaw that accepts the attentions of a white man", "context_len": 51}}}, "plurality": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 43, "context": "In order to show the inflection of a verb ( or rather a part of it ) , going parallel to the one chosen as the Creek paradigm , we select \u00edsiki to take , to carry ; \u00e1wiki being used when a plurality of objects is concerned ; Creek : \u00edsita , tch\u00e1wita", "context_len": 54}}}, "misdemeanor": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Tom Tiger , a fine-looking Indian , is said to have broken this rule by marrying two wives , for which misdemeanor he was banished from the tribe", "context_len": 28}}}, "conformably": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "This procured him absolution , conformably to their laws", "context_len": 9}}}, "auriferous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The auriferous ledges of the Cheroki country were said to be within `` the extreme confines of the Apalachi province '' ( Fontanedo , 1559 ) , and the Apalachi found by Narvaez was fifteen days ' march north of Aute , [ 49 ] a roadstead or harbor on the Gulf of Mexico , though the Indians had stated to him that it lay at a distance of nine days ' travel only", "context_len": 74}}}, "ledges": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 27, "context": "These sand-birds feed on the shore till they are chock brimful , and the tide comes and drives them off ; then they fly on to these ledges ; but they are as afraid of getting wet as a cat ; and when the tide comes up around the rock , they huddle together to keep out of the water , till they are all in a bunch , and the rock looks blue with them ; it \u2019 s the greatest chance for a shot ; but , continued he , after a pause , perhaps Mrs", "context_len": 98}}}, "roadstead": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "The auriferous ledges of the Cheroki country were said to be within `` the extreme confines of the Apalachi province '' ( Fontanedo , 1559 ) , and the Apalachi found by Narvaez was fifteen days ' march north of Aute , [ 49 ] a roadstead or harbor on the Gulf of Mexico , though the Indians had stated to him that it lay at a distance of nine days ' travel only", "context_len": 74}}}, "ascribed": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 46, "context": "595 ) that in 1701 a difficulty arose between the Apalachicolys and the Apalachis on account of depredations committed ; that the Spanish call those Indians Apalachicolys , the French Conchaques , and that they counted about 2000 families -- an equal number of men being ascribed to the Apalachis , who were under Spanish rule", "context_len": 56}}}, "westernmost": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "Later in the sixteenth century the boundary between the Timucua and the Apalachi lands is stated to have been on or near the Vacissa river ; Ibitachuco or Black Lake being the eastern Apalachi boundary , the westernmost town of the Timucua being Asile ( Ausile , Oxilla )", "context_len": 49}}}, "aggressiveness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Although the governor of Florida had but few troops to oppose , he marched against them and daunted their aggressiveness ( sobervia ) by forcing them to a disastrous retreat and following them into their own country ( Barcia , Ensayo , p", "context_len": 43}}}, "caciques": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "In 1688 a number of Apalachi chiefs ( caciques ) addressed a letter of complaint to Charles the Second , king of Spain ( 1700 ) , concerning the exactions to which their former governors had subjected them , and other topics relating to their actual condition", "context_len": 47}}}, "archbishopric": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": ") , and other documents written in Apalachi are preserved in the archives of Havana , the seat of the archbishopric , to which Apalachi and all the other settlements comprised within the diocese of St", "context_len": 36}}}, "diocese": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "The sealed packet only contained a formal summons to the general rendezvous of the forces , which was to take place at Dorchester , the episcopal city of the great Midland diocese , and situated in a central position , where Wessex and Mercia could easily unite the flower of their youth", "context_len": 52}}}, "festivals": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 17, "context": "The square occupied the central part of the town , and was reserved for the celebration of festivals , especially the annual busk or fast ( p\u00faskita ) , for the meetings of chiefs , headmen and `` beloved men , '' and for the performance of daily dances", "context_len": 49}}}, "soreheads": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It then raised the `` red pole '' as a sign of war , and became conspicuous as a sort of political centre for these Southern `` soreheads", "context_len": 28}}}, "mythic": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 5, "context": "Among the nations tracing their mythic origin to the earth , or what amounts to the same thing , to caves , deep holes , hills or mountains , are the Pomo of Northern California , who believe that their ancestors , the coyote-men , were created directly from a knoll of red earth , [ 135 ] still visible in their country ; the Nahua , whose seven tribes issued from Chicomoztoc or the `` Seven Caves", "context_len": 78}}}, "informants": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Some Hitchiti Indians trace their mythic origin to a fall from the sky , but my informants , Chicote and G", "context_len": 21}}}, "canebrake": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Stidham , gave me the following tale : `` Their ancestors first appeared in the country by coming out of a canebrake or reed thicket ( \u00fatski in Hitchiti ) near the sea coast", "context_len": 34}}}, "sunned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "They sunned and dried their children during four days , then set out , arrived at a lake and stopped there", "context_len": 21}}}, "permanency": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Some thought it was the sea , but it was a lake ; they set out again , traveled up a stream and settled there for a permanency", "context_len": 28}}}, "kicks": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "At every second line of this song the singer kicks at a log , feigning to start up the deer by the noise from its recesses in the woods", "context_len": 29}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` You ends must get down the field like lightning on kicks , and we 're going to do a good deal of kicking this year", "context_len": 26}}}, "analogous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "of the Maskoki language-family is analogous , though by no means identical with the Creek dialect in its grammatic outlines", "context_len": 20}}}, "sexes": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "They do not reprobate or punish them for any wanton act they may commit ; hence the licentiousness of both sexes up to the time of marriage , and the comparative want of discipline among warriors on their expeditions", "context_len": 39}}}, "sonant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The phonetic system is the same as in Creek , except that the sonant mutes , b , g , are more distinctly heard ( d is quite rare )", "context_len": 30}}}, "mutes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The phonetic system is the same as in Creek , except that the sonant mutes , b , g , are more distinctly heard ( d is quite rare )", "context_len": 30}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "That is a little ahead of anything I ever saw before ! was the exclamation of the puzzled Alden ; we have plenty of mutes in the east but I never met any on the plains , and I don \u2019 t believe he is one", "context_len": 46}}}, "processes": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The processes of alternation are the same in both dialects", "context_len": 10}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The alternating processes observed here also occur in most other Indian and illiterate languages : tch , dsh alternate with ts , ds , h with k , ; g with the other gutturals , b with p , d with t , \u0101 with e , o with u", "context_len": 50}}}, "alternation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The processes of alternation are the same in both dialects", "context_len": 10}}}, "vowels": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Many vowels of substantives are short in Creek , which appear long in Hitchiti : \u0103 pi tree : H", "context_len": 20}}}, "morphology": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "MORPHOLOGY", "context_len": 1}}}, "subjective": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 34, "context": "The case inflection of the substantive , adjective , of some pronouns and of the nominal forms of the verb is effected by the suffixes : -i for the absolute , -ut for the subjective , -un for the objective case : y\u00e1ti person , y\u00e1tut , y\u00e1tun ; n\u00e1ki what , which , n\u00e1kut , n\u00e1kun", "context_len": 57}}}, "verbals": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "No real substantive verb being extant , its want is supplied by \u00f3m\u00e4s , m\u00f3m\u00e4s , t\u00f3y\u00e4s I am so , I am such ; these are conjugated regularly , and when connected with the verbals in -t ( -at , -it , -ut ) of any verb , compose a periphrastic conjugation which displays itself in an almost infinite number of forms", "context_len": 63}}}, "inflect": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "The substantive noun does not inflect for number except in a few terms designating persons which form a plural in -agi , -aki : m\u00edko chief , m\u00edkagi chiefs , to be distinguished from m\u00edkalgi class from which chiefs are chosen ; h\u00fananwa man , h\u00f3kti woman ; hunant\u00e1gi , h\u00f3ktagi", "context_len": 51}}}, "corresponds": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 43, "context": "Here , Your Honor , you may see the torn place in this lad \u2019 s coat-sleeve , and you may also perceive beyond question that the shred of fabric discovered by Piper clinging to the nail in Bernard Hayden \u2019 s locker corresponds with the material of this garment", "context_len": 50}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 59, "context": "In spite of the frequent use to which they are put they do not relieve the sentence of its heaviness to any perceptible extent ; for what we call incident clauses and also many co-ordinate principal sentences are uniformly expressed by groups of words , the verb of which stands in the -t or -n verbal , which nearest corresponds to our participle in -ing , or to having ( h", "context_len": 71}}}, "verbifying": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hitchiti has a greater power of verbifying substantives than Creek : m\u00edki chief , mik\u00f3lis I am chief ; tch\u00f3yi pine-tree , tch\u00f3yus it is a pine tree", "context_len": 28}}}, "gradation": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "Minuitive gradation is effected by inversion of the sense in the sentence and the use of the comparative ; they say : `` silver is costlier than iron , '' instead of saying : `` iron is less costly than silver", "context_len": 41}}}, "syntactically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The gradation of the adjective is expressed either by the attributive verb , to which isi- , is- is prefixed , or in some other ways syntactically :", "context_len": 28}}}, "cardinal": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 41, "context": "Then ( says our author ) , the geomant proceeds to examine the sixteen figures thus obtained ( each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "Folded four times is expressed by the cardinal : po ` l\u00f3tki s\u00edtaki ; folded eight times : po ` l\u00f3tki tusnap\u00e1kin", "context_len": 22}}}, "ordinal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "The numeral has two forms for the cardinal number : one used attributively , and another , abbreviated from it , used exclusively for counting ; there are , outside of this , forms for the ordinal , for the distributive , and for the adverbial numeral", "context_len": 47}}}, "adverbial": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "The numeral has two forms for the cardinal number : one used attributively , and another , abbreviated from it , used exclusively for counting ; there are , outside of this , forms for the ordinal , for the distributive , and for the adverbial numeral", "context_len": 47}}}, "po": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "paradigm": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "In order to show the inflection of a verb ( or rather a part of it ) , going parallel to the one chosen as the Creek paradigm , we select \u00edsiki to take , to carry ; \u00e1wiki being used when a plurality of objects is concerned ; Creek : \u00edsita , tch\u00e1wita", "context_len": 54}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "In this case the pronoun im- , in- , i- is prefixed ; paradigm given above", "context_len": 16}}}, "pl": {"freq": 48, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 48, "word_index": 67, "context": "`` Later on , when the Sultan visits the enchanted palace for the first time , Alaeddin `` brought him to the high kiosk and he looked at the belvedere ( teyyareh , a square or round erection on the top of a house , either open at the sides or pierced with windows , our architectural term 'lantern ' ) and its casements ( shebabik , pl", "context_len": 68}}}, "lis": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 6, "context": "The future tenses : is\u00e1 ` lis I shall take , 2 s", "context_len": 13}}}, "reciprocal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "From this verb \u00edsiki , \u00e1wiki the language does not form any passive , reciprocal , reflective and causative voice , but employs verbs from other radices instead", "context_len": 28}}}, "causative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "From this verb \u00edsiki , \u00e1wiki the language does not form any passive , reciprocal , reflective and causative voice , but employs verbs from other radices instead", "context_len": 28}}}, "employs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "From this verb \u00edsiki , \u00e1wiki the language does not form any passive , reciprocal , reflective and causative voice , but employs verbs from other radices instead", "context_len": 28}}}, "radices": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "From this verb \u00edsiki , \u00e1wiki the language does not form any passive , reciprocal , reflective and causative voice , but employs verbs from other radices instead", "context_len": 28}}}, "conjugation": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 52, "context": "No real substantive verb being extant , its want is supplied by \u00f3m\u00e4s , m\u00f3m\u00e4s , t\u00f3y\u00e4s I am so , I am such ; these are conjugated regularly , and when connected with the verbals in -t ( -at , -it , -ut ) of any verb , compose a periphrastic conjugation which displays itself in an almost infinite number of forms", "context_len": 63}}}, "declarative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The same verb to strike gives origin to the following genera verbi , each appearing under two different forms , and all being quoted in the present tense of the declarative mode , affirmative voice :", "context_len": 36}}}, "skunk": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 7, "context": "`` Get up , you little clumsy skunk ! You 'll half kill her ! ''", "context_len": 16}}}, "racoon": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 6, "context": "s\u00e1wut \u00e1hi \u00edgapun untch\u00f3 olis the racoon sits on the top of the tree", "context_len": 14}}}, "derives": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Allen Wright derives Alibamu ( also written Allibamous , Alibami , Alb\u00e1mu , incorrectly Alibamon ) from Cha'hta : \u00e1lba thicket and \u00e1yalmu , place cleared ( of trees , thickets ) : \u00e1lba ayam\u00fale I open or clear the thicket", "context_len": 42}}}, "generic": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 4, "context": "It is therefore a generic term and not a specific tribal name ; hence it was applied to several tribes simultaneously , and they were reported to have seven towns , Tangibao among them , which were distant eight days ' travel by land E", "context_len": 45}}}, "possessors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "Things felt very flat when the excitement was all over and the last of the guests had left the Abbey , and the children found themselves wishing that life could be a perpetual round of bouquets and favours and wedding-cake , not forgetting presents to the bridesmaids , for Lilian and Peggy were the proud possessors of charming gold lockets set with turquoises , with portraits of Aunt Helen inside them , 'the gifts of the bridegroom , ' as the local paper described them , while a silver watch and chain consoled Bobby for not taking a more prominent part in the ceremony , and made him declare that his new uncle was ' a brick , and no mistake", "context_len": 121}}}, "migrated": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` [ 52 ] The earliest oral tradition of the Alibamu of a migration is , that they migrated from the Cahawba and Alabama rivers to the junction of the Tuscaloosa ( ? ) and Coosa rivers , where they sojourned for two years", "context_len": 44}}}, "auxiliaries": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 21, "context": "Then , too , he felt sure that by this time the people of Hondura \u2019 s tribe , with what auxiliaries they could have mustered , had set out to rescue their chief", "context_len": 34}}}, "watchfulness": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 61, "context": "An alliance concluded by the Alibamu with the Mobilians did not last long , for in 1708 they arrived with a host of Cheroki , Abika and Kataba Indians , in the vicinity of the French fort on Mobile Bay , where Naniabas , Tohomes and Mobilians had settled , but were foiled in their attack upon the Mobilians through the watchfulness of the tribe and of the French colonists", "context_len": 70}}}, "aggressors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "Being reinforced by other Indians from the Upper Creek towns , `` their uncles , '' they repulsed the aggressors and destroyed their villages , as well as those of the Spaniards", "context_len": 32}}}, "inroad": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "In 1713 , after the Alibamu had made an inroad into the Carolinas with a host of Kataba and Abika Indians , their confederates , the head-chief of the first-named tribe besought the French commander at Mobile bay to erect a fort in his own country", "context_len": 46}}}, "agriculturists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The majority , however , settled in Polk county , in the southeastern corner of Texas , became agriculturists , and about 1862 numbered over two hundred persons", "context_len": 28}}}, "indolent": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 19, "context": "The southern Cha'hta , settled on fertile ground west of Mobile and southwest of Pascogoula , are dirty , indolent and cowardly , miserably dressed and inveterate beggars", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "It can not be said he was bright , but he was strong , fond of every member of the family , indolent , and a good servant when forced to work", "context_len": 32}}}, "adultery": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "In adultery and murder cases the relatives of the gens of the injured party alone had the right of judging and of taking satisfaction ; the m\u00edko and his council were debarred from any interference", "context_len": 35}}}, "dignitary": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "In Milfort 's time this dignitary had become a civil and military officer , [ 98 ] and nowadays his functions are those of a civil functionary only", "context_len": 28}}}, "headwaters": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 36, "context": "The Cha'hta trace their mythic origin from the `` Stooping , Leaning or Winding Hill , '' N\u00e1ni W\u00e1ya , a mound of fifty feet altitude , situated in Winston county , Mississippi , on the headwaters of Pearl river", "context_len": 40}}}, "lucid": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "Bartram viewed the `` terraces , on which formerly stood their town-house or rotunda and square or areopagus , '' and gives a lucid description of them", "context_len": 27}}}, "lat": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Lat", "context_len": 1}}}, "nitrous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "63 : `` They live in the centre of an uneven and large nitrous savannah ; have in it one town , long one mile and a half , very narrow and irregular ; this they divide into seven ( towns ) , by the names of Melattaw 'hat and feather , ' Chatelaw 'copper town , ' Chukafalaya 'long town , ' Tuckahaw ' a certain weed , ' Ashuck hooma 'red grass", "context_len": 74}}}, "savannah": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "63 : `` They live in the centre of an uneven and large nitrous savannah ; have in it one town , long one mile and a half , very narrow and irregular ; this they divide into seven ( towns ) , by the names of Melattaw 'hat and feather , ' Chatelaw 'copper town , ' Chukafalaya 'long town , ' Tuckahaw ' a certain weed , ' Ashuck hooma 'red grass", "context_len": 74}}}, "broils": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "Subsequently they must have removed several hundred miles south of Tuscalusa river , perhaps on account of intertribal broils with the Alibamu ; for in the year 1708 we find them settled on Mobile Bay , where the French had allowed them , the Naniaba and Tohome , to erect lodges around their fort", "context_len": 54}}}, "cognate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "They were constantly engaged in quarrels and broils with all their Indian neighbors : sometimes with the cognate Cha'hta and with the Creeks , at other times with the Cheroki , Illinois , Kickapu , Sh\u00e1wano , Tonica , Mobilians , Osage and Arkansas ( Kapaha ) Indians", "context_len": 48}}}, "purporting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "240 , alludes to a tradition purporting that the Chicasa had formerly assisted the Cheroki in driving the Shawanese from the Cumberland river ; the Cheroki desired war , and attacked the Chicasa shortly before 1769 , but were utterly defeated by them at the `` Chicasa Old Fields , '' and retreated by way of Cumberland river and the Cany Fork", "context_len": 62}}}, "establishing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 22, "context": "Philip wished to make this wedding not merely the means of confirming his reconciliation with his former wife and son , and establishing friendly relations with the King of Epirus : he also prized it as an occasion for paying marked and honorable attention to the princes and great generals of the other states of Greece", "context_len": 56}}}, "emigrate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "Lilian hunted out an old piece of carpet and a tablecloth , and the place looked so comfortable that the children , fired by Archie 's accounts of the log-cabins in the Rocky Mountains , longed to put in a cooking-stove and emigrate there altogether", "context_len": 45}}}, "treaties": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 27, "context": "Art thou willing to accept such penance as the Church , in consideration of thy perjuries , thy murders , which man may not avenge , since treaties protect thee -- but which God will surely remember , if thou repent not -- to accept such penance , I say , as the Church shall impose ? ''", "context_len": 58}}}, "connections": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 33, "context": "The oldest immigration to Chatahuchi river is that of the Kas\u00ed ta and Kaw\u00edta tribes , both of whom , as our legend shows , found the K\u00fasa and the Apalatch\u00fakla with their connections , in situ , probably the \u00c1bi ka also", "context_len": 43}}}, "subdivided": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Both have two phratries only , each of which were ( originally ) subdivided , in an equal manner , into four gentes ; but the thorough-going difference in the totems of the 8-12 gentes points to a very ancient separation of the two national bodies", "context_len": 46}}}, "commercial": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 35, "context": "It stipulated a cession of the lands between the Savannah and Altamaha rivers , and of some islands on the Atlantic coast , to the British ; it further stipulated promises to enter into a commercial treaty at a later date , to place themselves under the general government of Great Britain , to live in peace with the colonies , to capture runaway slaves and deliver them at Charleston , Savannah or Palachukla garrison for a consideration", "context_len": 78}}}, "inhabiting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "Everybody knows , that when the Spaniards conquered Mexico , they experienced but little difficulty in subduing the peaceable nation inhabiting those southwestern countries by means of their firearms , which proved to be far superior to the bows and arrows of their opponents , and against which courage availed almost nothing", "context_len": 52}}}, "compares": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Jefferys ( I , 165 ) , compares it to the `` lingua franca in the Levant ; they call it the vulgar tongue", "context_len": 24}}}, "lingua": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Jefferys ( I , 165 ) , compares it to the `` lingua franca in the Levant ; they call it the vulgar tongue", "context_len": 24}}}, "par": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 45, "context": "I 'm goin ' ter stay up fer hours an ' hours , an ' if yer do n't keep right on tellin ' stories quick , I 'll holler , an ' that 'll make mar mad , an ' then she 'll send par up with a stick ter beat me", "context_len": 53}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "[ Footnote 6 : `` La suite des Mille et une Nuits , Contes Arabes trafluits par Dom Chavis et M", "context_len": 21}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 97, "context": "of the Cabinet des Fees ( printed in 1793 , though antedated 1788 and 1789 ) do not form the first edition of Chavis and Cazotte 's so-called Sequel , which was in 1793 added , by way of supplement , to the Cabinet des Fees , having been first published in 1788 ( two years after the completion-in thirty-seven volumes-of that great storehouse of supernatural fiction ) under the title of `` Les Veillees Persanes '' or `` Les Veillees du Sultan Schahriar avec la Sultane Scheherazade , histoires incroyables , amusantes et morales , traduites par M", "context_len": 99}}}, "sic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "219 ) : `` La langue Tchicacha est parl\u00e9e aussi par les Chatkas ( sic ! ) et ( corrompue ) par les Taensas ; cette langue corrompue est appel\u00e9e Mobilienne par les Fran\u00e7ais , '' etc", "context_len": 37}}}, "alleges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": ", and in Margry V , 442 , where P\u00e9nicaut alleges to have studied the languages of the Louisiana savages pretty thoroughly for five years , `` surtout le Mobilien , qui est le principal et qu'on entend par toutes les nations", "context_len": 42}}}, "surtout": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": ", and in Margry V , 442 , where P\u00e9nicaut alleges to have studied the languages of the Louisiana savages pretty thoroughly for five years , `` surtout le Mobilien , qui est le principal et qu'on entend par toutes les nations", "context_len": 42}}}, "intermarriage": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Copeland states that the descent is in the female line , that no intermarriage takes place among individuals of the same gens , and that property as well as the office of chief is hereditary in the gens", "context_len": 38}}}, "headmen": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 32, "context": "Sp\u00e1ne or Spanish gens ; m\u00edngos or chiefs could be chosen from this gens only , and were hereditary in the female line ; sh\u0103-\u00e9 or racoon gens ; second chiefs or headmen were selected from it ; kuishto or tiger gens ; ko-intch\u016bsh or catamount gens ; n\u00e1ni or fish gens ; \u00edssi or deer gens ; hal\u00f3ba or ? gens ; fosh\u00e9 or bird gens ; hunshkon\u00e9 or skunk gens , the least respected of them all", "context_len": 79}}}, "ratified": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` The treaty of land-cession , commerce and alliance was signed May 21st , and ratified by the trustees of the colony of Georgia , October 18th , 1733", "context_len": 29}}}, "donation": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "`` To-day , '' writes M\u00fcller on 7th September , `` our income altogether was about 300 -- a plain proof that we do not wait on the Lord in vain ; for every donation we receive is a direct answer to prayer , because we never ask a single human being for anything", "context_len": 54}}}, "swampy": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "The countries along Yazoo river are low and swampy grounds , subject to inundations , especially the narrow strip of land extending between that river and the Mississippi", "context_len": 28}}}, "inundations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "Such land is called alluvial ; it is always level , and , as it consists of successive deposits from the turbid waters of the river , made in the successive inundations , it forms always a very rich soil , deep and inexhaustible , and is , of course , extremely fertile", "context_len": 53}}}, "warred": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Compare also the Napochies , who , at the time of Tristan de Luna 's visit , warred with the Co\u00e7a ( or Kusa , on Coosa river ? ) : `` Co\u00e7as tenian guerra con los Napochies '' ; Barcia , Ensayo , p", "context_len": 45}}}, "guerra": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "Compare also the Napochies , who , at the time of Tristan de Luna 's visit , warred with the Co\u00e7a ( or Kusa , on Coosa river ? ) : `` Co\u00e7as tenian guerra con los Napochies '' ; Barcia , Ensayo , p", "context_len": 45}}}, "con": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Compare also the Napochies , who , at the time of Tristan de Luna 's visit , warred with the Co\u00e7a ( or Kusa , on Coosa river ? ) : `` Co\u00e7as tenian guerra con los Napochies '' ; Barcia , Ensayo , p", "context_len": 45}}}, "confederated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Another Yazoo tribe , mentioned at a later period as confederated with the Chicasa are the Tch\u00fala , Chola or `` Foxes", "context_len": 22}}}, "yazoo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Yazoo is not a Cha'hta word , although the Cha'hta had a `` clan '' of that name : Y\u0101 sho \u00f3kla , Y\u00e1shukla , as I am informed by Gov", "context_len": 31}}}, "products": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "The Professor could , therefore , go on with the writing of his great work on Greek anthology ; even if the money for this unique treatise came in slowly , there would be enough to keep the little family from the products of the school", "context_len": 46}}}, "establishes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Milfort establishes a thorough distinction between the northern and the southern Cha'hta as to their pursuits of life and moral character", "context_len": 21}}}, "inveterate": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 3, "context": "She is an inveterate relic-hunter ; is enraptured with Bunker Hill and the Old South ; delights in Cornhill , and wherever she can find a crooked old street that reminds her of Washington ; and pokes about all the old cemeteries , until I feel as eerie as Coleridge 's ancient mariner", "context_len": 53}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "'What an inveterate bookworm you are , Paul , ' and Belle looked at the pile of volumes Pauline had brought from the library to study in the long morning hours which the force of a lifelong habit gave her , before the rest of the family were astir", "context_len": 49}}}, "mortuary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The mortuary customs , part of which were exceedingly barbaric , are spoken of with many details by Milfort ( p", "context_len": 21}}}, "agricultural": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 27, "context": "Gorswen , having taken its annual holiday at Easter , made no account of Whit-week , and went on with its work as usual , for the agricultural labourer does not claim so much in the way of pleasure as his brethren of the loom or the forge , and is content with an occasional fair or village feast to break the monotony of his daily life", "context_len": 67}}}, "burials": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "More information on Cha'hta burials will be found in H", "context_len": 10}}}, "yarrow": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Yarrow , Indian mortuary customs ; in First Report of U", "context_len": 11}}}, "disorders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "On the last day all neighbors assembled , the poles were pulled up , and the lamentation ended with drinking , carousing and great disorders", "context_len": 25}}}, "emigrating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The Cha'hta emigrating to these western parts were looked at by their countrymen at home in the same light as the Seminoles were by the Creeks", "context_len": 26}}}, "locally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Starting from the humpy , we crossed a little stretch of scrubby country , and struck into the sun-fretted gum-tree forest , locally known as the bush", "context_len": 27}}}, "intermarry": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "The only points which could be gathered were , that individuals belonging to the panther and the wildcat gentes could not intermarry , nor could the Tchuk\u00f3talgi with the individuals of the toad gens or Sop\u00e1ktalgi", "context_len": 36}}}, "accusers": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "Wright then continues : `` Woman 's brothers are considered natural guardians of the children , even during father 's lifetime ; counsel was taken for criminals from their phratry , the opposite phratry , or rather the principal men of this , acting as accusers", "context_len": 46}}}, "disuse": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 12, "context": "The ancient distinction between red and white towns began to fall into disuse with the approach of the white colonists , which entailed the spread of agricultural pursuits among these Indians ; nevertheless frequent reference is made to it by the modern Creeks", "context_len": 43}}}, "cannibal": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 25, "context": "The legends of the Cha'hta speak of a giant race , peaceable and agricultural ( nah\u00fallo ) [ 71 ] , and also of a cannibal race , both of which they met east of the Mississippi river", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 28, "context": "This time the group starts out on a cruise simply for pleasure , but their adventuresome spirits lead them into the thick of things on a South Sea cannibal island", "context_len": 30}}}, "epidemic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 91, "context": "the winter , for many of the picturesque cottages were dark , unwholesome places inside , and lay low on the damp fields by the river ; but the invalids crept out now into the sunlight , and the mild breezes blew roses into wan cheeks and brightness into dull eyes , bringing back health , that most priceless of gifts , to the village -- to all , indeed , but the Rector , who had been sick-nurse , doctor 's assistant , family friend and chaplain combined during the epidemic , and now that the strain was over broke down so utterly that the physician insisted upon a complete rest and change of air , ordering him off immediately to the high meadows of the Alps", "context_len": 128}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "A kind of hydrophobia would sometimes be communicated by a snake-bitten monkey to its mates , and by them to any human being that came within their reach while the epidemic was raging", "context_len": 33}}}, "priestly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "It was a sort of temple , and the Unkala , a priestly order , had the custody or care of it", "context_len": 22}}}, "chanting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The I ksa A numpule or `` clan-speakers '' prepared the bones of great warriors for burial , and the Unkala went at the head of the mourners to that temple , chanting hymns in an unknown tongue", "context_len": 38}}}, "preternatural": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "The most circumstantial account of this preternatural occurrence is laid down in the following narrative", "context_len": 15}}}, "immortality": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 64, "context": "But they judged wrong , for he had wrestled with his sorrow , like Jacob with the angel of old , and came forth from the struggle with hair indeed as white as snow , but a face so full of the glory of his conquest that those who looked felt as if he , too , had died , and they saw his immortality", "context_len": 65}}}, "indurated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The earth then indurated , the hills were formed by the agitation of the waters and winds on the soft mud", "context_len": 21}}}, "ch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "[ Footnote 75 : Compare the poetic vision , parallel to this , contained in Ezekiel , ch", "context_len": 18}}}, "coeval": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Here the creation of the Cha'hta is made coeval with the creation of the earth , and some features of the story give evidence of modern and rationalistic tendencies of the relator", "context_len": 32}}}, "rationalistic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Here the creation of the Cha'hta is made coeval with the creation of the earth , and some features of the story give evidence of modern and rationalistic tendencies of the relator", "context_len": 32}}}, "relator": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "Here the creation of the Cha'hta is made coeval with the creation of the earth , and some features of the story give evidence of modern and rationalistic tendencies of the relator", "context_len": 32}}}, "legislation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Other Cha'hta traditions state that the people came from the west , and stopped at Nani Waya , only to obtain their laws and phratries from the Creator -- a story made to resemble the legislation on Mount Sinai", "context_len": 39}}}, "myth": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Every tribe of a people or nation has its own migration myth or legend , which in some points coincides , in others conflicts with those of the neighboring septs", "context_len": 30}}}, "antiquarian": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 38, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Valuable ? Not from a money point of view , I am afraid ; but no doubt they would prove very interesting to some antiquarian who could read them", "context_len": 29}}}, "superposed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Five different worlds are there supposed to have existed , superposed to each other , and some of the orifices through which the `` old people '' crawled up are visible at the present time", "context_len": 35}}}, "orifices": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Five different worlds are there supposed to have existed , superposed to each other , and some of the orifices through which the `` old people '' crawled up are visible at the present time", "context_len": 35}}}, "pagan": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 36, "context": "Then Alfgar saw what was the marvellous power of Christianity , and beheld a heroism utterly beyond the fierce excitement which nerved his countrymen for their scenes of carnage and blood ; not one of his pagan friends could have suffered as calmly , as patiently -- it seemed easier for the sufferer to bear than for Alfgar to look on ; once or twice the latter gave audible vent to his emotions , but the look which Bertric turned upon him spoke volumes , and he restrained himself lest he should add to the pain of the victim", "context_len": 99}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "`` You forget that when the last invasion of our pagan foes was over , it was agreed in the Witan that a set of beacons should be prepared , in readiness to fire , on the tops of the hills , and that if the Danes appeared again , they should be fired everywhere , in which case Mercia was to hold herself in readiness to come to the aid of Wessex or East Anglia , whichever the foe might be harrying", "context_len": 83}}}, "adventitious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Besides the Naniaba [ 77 ] and Mobilian Indians , the French had settled in their vicinity a pagan Cha'hta tribe from the northwest and an adventitious band of Apalaches , who had fled the Spanish domination in Florida", "context_len": 39}}}, "styled": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 20, "context": "MacGillivray , head chief of the Creeks in the latter part of the eighteenth century , or as he was styled , `` Emperor of the Creek Nation , '' lived at Task\u00edgi , where he owned a house and property along Coosa river , half a league from Fort Toulouse ; Milfort , M\u00e9moire , p", "context_len": 57}}}, "environs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Later on they crossed the Mississippi to its western side , and are mentioned as wanderers on Bayou Crocodile and its environs ( 1806 ) , which they frequent even now , and on the Lake of Avoyelles", "context_len": 38}}}, "lagoons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "This term refers to their position upon the in- and out-flow of Lake Pontchartrain and other coast lagoons , combined with their watchfulness for hostile parties passing these places", "context_len": 29}}}, "specific": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "It is therefore a generic term and not a specific tribal name ; hence it was applied to several tribes simultaneously , and they were reported to have seven towns , Tangibao among them , which were distant eight days ' travel by land E", "context_len": 45}}}, "epidemics": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Events of this kind are migrations , long warfare or decisive battles , which resulted either in defeat or victory , alliances with cognate or friendly tribes , times of abundance , of famines and epidemics", "context_len": 36}}}, "opossum": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "A fire was burning in the centre of the temples , and near the door were figures of animals , the `` chouco\u00fcacha '' or opossum being one of them", "context_len": 30}}}, "tambours": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "[ Footnote 79 : Margry IV , 175 : `` des tambours chychycouchy , qui sont des calebasses", "context_len": 18}}}, "delta": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The full form of the tribal name is Bayuk-\u00f3kla or river-tribe , creek- or bayou-people ; the Cha'hta word for a smaller river , or river forming part of a delta is b\u00e1yuk , contr", "context_len": 35}}}, "colonization": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "That the Creeks arrived in Northern Alabama in or after the time of the French colonization of the Lower Mississippi lands , is another impossibility , and the erection of Fort Toulouse preceded the second French war against the Chicasa by more than twenty years , whereas Milfort represents it as having been a consequence of that war", "context_len": 58}}}, "annalists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "[ 80 ] Their hostilities with the Tangipahoa are referred to by the French annalists , and ended in the destroying of the Tangipahoa town by the Huma ; Margry IV , 168", "context_len": 33}}}, "vendetta": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "On Escambia river there are Cha'hta at the present time , who keep up the custom of family vendetta or blood revenge , and that river is also mentioned as a constant battle-field between the Creeks and Cha'hta tribes by W", "context_len": 41}}}, "cessions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "The council lasted from August 11th to 21st , and terminated in a treaty , by which the towns renewed their `` fealty '' to the king of Great Britain , and confirmed their cessions of territory , while Oglethorpe engaged that the British should not encroach upon their reserved lands , and that their traders should deal fairly and honestly with the Indians", "context_len": 64}}}, "fords": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "A list of the way-stations and fords on the post-road between Lower Tallapoosa river and the Bay of Mobile is appended to Hawkins ' Sketch , p", "context_len": 27}}}, "appended": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 20, "context": "A list of the way-stations and fords on the post-road between Lower Tallapoosa river and the Bay of Mobile is appended to Hawkins ' Sketch , p", "context_len": 27}}}, "acceptations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The origin of these different acceptations can only be accounted for by the generic meaning of the appellation Conshac", "context_len": 19}}}, "combinations": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 85, "context": "each of which has its name and its mansion , corresponding to one of the twelve signs of the zodiac or the four cardinal points , as well as its signification , good or bad , and indicates also , in a special way , a certain part of the elemental world ) and to note each figure according to its presage of weal or ill ; and so , with the aid of an astrological table giving the explanations of the various signs and combinations , according to the nature of the figure , its aspect , influence and temperament ( astrologically considered ) and the natural object it indicates , a judgment is formed upon the question for a solution of which the operation was undertaken", "context_len": 128}}}, "bt": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Words can not begin with two consonants ; the Creek st is replaced by sht , and combinations like tl , bt , nt do not occur ( Byington 's Grammar , p", "context_len": 33}}}, "enclitic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "But in other respects , case and many other relations are pointed out by an extensive series of suffixed or enclitic syllables , mostly monosyllabic , which Byington calls article-pronouns , and writes as separate words", "context_len": 36}}}, "monosyllabic": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "But in other respects , case and many other relations are pointed out by an extensive series of suffixed or enclitic syllables , mostly monosyllabic , which Byington calls article-pronouns , and writes as separate words", "context_len": 36}}}, "pronominal": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "They are simply suffixes of pronominal origin , and correspond to our articles the , a , to our relative and demonstrative pronouns , partly also to our adverbs , prepositions and conjunctions", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "A transitive verb connected with its indirect pronominal object conjugates in the same manner , unless there is in it the idea for the benefit of , or for the detriment of , or from , away from somebody or something connected with it", "context_len": 44}}}, "adverbs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "They are simply suffixes of pronominal origin , and correspond to our articles the , a , to our relative and demonstrative pronouns , partly also to our adverbs , prepositions and conjunctions", "context_len": 33}}}, "prepositions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "`` in '' ( fi ) ; but fi is evidently used here in mistake for bi , the two prepositions being practically interchangeable in modern Arabic of the style of our present text", "context_len": 34}}}, "conjunctions": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 40, "context": "This sort of incapsulation greatly embarrasses interpreters in the rendering of Creek texts in any of the modern European languages , which have a tendency towards analytic and an aversion to synthetic structure of the sentence , and therefore use conjunctions freely", "context_len": 42}}}, "modal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "They form combinations among themselves , and supply verbal inflection with its modal suffixes or exponents", "context_len": 16}}}, "exponents": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "They form combinations among themselves , and supply verbal inflection with its modal suffixes or exponents", "context_len": 16}}}, "modifies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Verbal inflection is rich in tenses and other forms , and largely modifies the radix to express changes in voice , mode and tense", "context_len": 24}}}, "disagreeing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The sway of phonetic laws is all-powerful here , and they operate whenever a slight conflict of syllables disagreeing with the delicate ear of the Cha'hta Indian takes place", "context_len": 29}}}, "fills": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "Yet the Author has distinctly felt that fiction must always , in such cases , be subordinate to truth , and that it is only legitimately used as a vehicle of instruction when it fills up the gaps in the outline , without contradicting them in any respect , or interfering with their due order and sequence", "context_len": 57}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "The gasholder is placed in a tank of water , so that there is no waste of gas as the huge iron holder fills or empties", "context_len": 26}}}, "folio": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Bureau of Ethnology , fills five folio volumes , contains about 17,000 items ( words , phrases and sentences ) , and was completed about 1833", "context_len": 26}}}, "accentuation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 38, "context": "The missionary alphabet used by him , which is also the alphabet of Cha'hta literature , is very imperfect , as it fails to express all sounds of the language by signs for each , and entirely neglects accentuation", "context_len": 39}}}, "intonations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The pronunciation of Cha'hta is so delicate and pliant that only a superior scientific alphabet can approximately express its peculiar sounds and intonations", "context_len": 23}}}, "adam": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 0, "context": "Adam", "context_len": 1}}}, "affiliation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The Creek Indians or Maskoki proper occupy , in historic times , a central position among the other tribes of their affiliation , and through their influence and physical power , which they attained by forming a comparatively strong and permanent national union , have become the most noteworthy of all the Southern tribes of the United States territories", "context_len": 59}}}, "noteworthy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "The Creek Indians or Maskoki proper occupy , in historic times , a central position among the other tribes of their affiliation , and through their influence and physical power , which they attained by forming a comparatively strong and permanent national union , have become the most noteworthy of all the Southern tribes of the United States territories", "context_len": 59}}}, "dominant": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 47, "context": "As a confirmation of this , it is asserted that the Creeks annihilated the Y\u00e1massi Indians completely , so that they disappeared entirely among their number ; that the Tukabatchi , Task\u00edgi and other tribes of foreign descent abandoned their paternal language to adopt that of the dominant Creeks", "context_len": 49}}}, "attenuate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "But there are facts which tend to attenuate or disprove this tradition", "context_len": 12}}}, "disprove": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "But there are facts which tend to attenuate or disprove this tradition", "context_len": 12}}}, "membership": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "The Leather Apron Club , the town \u2019 s wealthier citizens nicknamed the Junto , because of the humble working class background of its membership", "context_len": 25}}}, "retention": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 48, "context": "The Hitchiti , Apalatch\u00fakla and Sawokli tribes , with their branch villages , have also retained their language to this day , notwithstanding their membership in the extensive confederacy , a membership which must have lasted for centuries ; and in fact we can not see how the retention of vernacular speech could hurt the interests of the community even in the slightest way", "context_len": 64}}}, "militates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The existence of Cheroki local names amid the Creek settlements strongly militates in favor of this ; we have Etowa , Ok\u00f3ni , Chiaha , Tam\u00e1 ` li , \u00c1tasi , Task\u00edgi , Amakalli", "context_len": 34}}}, "immigrated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "In the minds of many of our readers it will ever remain doubtful that the Creek tribes immigrated into the territories of the Eastern Gulf States by crossing the Lower Mississippi river", "context_len": 32}}}, "inundation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "The towns and villages of the Creeks were in the eighteenth century built along the banks of rivers and their smaller tributaries , often in places subject to inundation during large freshets , which occurred once in about fifteen years", "context_len": 40}}}, "freshets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The towns and villages of the Creeks were in the eighteenth century built along the banks of rivers and their smaller tributaries , often in places subject to inundation during large freshets , which occurred once in about fifteen years", "context_len": 40}}}, "compactly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "The city itself , however , though contracted in extent on account of the small dimensions of the island , was very compactly built and strongly fortified , and it contained a vast number of stately and magnificent edifices , which were filled with stores of wealth that had been accumulated by the mercantile enterprise and thrift of many generations", "context_len": 60}}}, "kilns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The huts and cabins of the Lower Creeks resembled , from a distance , clusters of newly-burned brick kilns , from the high color of the clay", "context_len": 27}}}, "thoroughgoing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "Another thoroughgoing distinction in the settlements of the Creek nation was that of the red or war towns and the white or peace towns", "context_len": 24}}}, "conservative": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 16, "context": "Your stock , just on this three months \u2019 showing , is worth , at a conservative estimate , five hundred dollars a share -- and you paid only five dollars a share for it ! You \u2019 re worth fifty thousand now , but you \u2019 ll be worth ten times that if the deal I have on with certain New York parties goes through", "context_len": 66}}}, "allege": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The Aht , on the western coast of Vancouver Island , allege that animals were first produced at Cape Flattery , Washington Territory , and from the union of some of these with a star , which fell from heaven , came the first men , and from them sprang all the race of Nitin-aht , Klayok-aht and M\u00e1kah or Klass-aht Indians", "context_len": 62}}}, "promiscuous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "That ain \u2019 t what I \u2019 m driving at , but if a hoss had been wounded or killed , he would have kicked up such a rumpus he \u2019 d stampeded all the others ; they \u2019 d have scattered over the perarie and upset things so promiscuous that we \u2019 d never got \u2019 em agin", "context_len": 59}}}, "proprietors": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "These they could not obtain in their nearest neighborhood without warring with their proprietors , and therefore often repaired to distant countries to seek new homes ( Bartram , Travels , p", "context_len": 32}}}, "removals": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The frequent removals of towns to new sites , lying at short distances only , may be easily explained by the unhealthiness of the old site , produced by the constant accumulation of refuse and filth around the towns , which never had anything like sewers or efficient regulations of sanitary police", "context_len": 52}}}, "unhealthiness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The frequent removals of towns to new sites , lying at short distances only , may be easily explained by the unhealthiness of the old site , produced by the constant accumulation of refuse and filth around the towns , which never had anything like sewers or efficient regulations of sanitary police", "context_len": 52}}}, "sewers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "The frequent removals of towns to new sites , lying at short distances only , may be easily explained by the unhealthiness of the old site , produced by the constant accumulation of refuse and filth around the towns , which never had anything like sewers or efficient regulations of sanitary police", "context_len": 52}}}, "sanitary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 50, "context": "The frequent removals of towns to new sites , lying at short distances only , may be easily explained by the unhealthiness of the old site , produced by the constant accumulation of refuse and filth around the towns , which never had anything like sewers or efficient regulations of sanitary police", "context_len": 52}}}, "opprobrious": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "omits": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "He mentions the towns only , and omits all the villages which have branched off from the towns", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "[ Footnote 559 : He had apparently learned ( though the Arabic author omits , with characteristic carelessness , to tell us so ) that Alaeddin was absent a", "context_len": 29}}}, "epithets": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}}}, "stinking": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 90, "context": "Although I do not know the Creek term which corresponded to it in the eighteenth century , it is not improbable that such a designation was in vogue ; for we find many similar opprobrious epithets among other Indians , as Cuitlateca or `` excrementers '' in Mexico ; Puants or Metsmetskop among the Naktche [ 89 ] ; Inkalik , `` sons of louse-eggs '' among the Eskimo ; K\u0101 katilsh or `` arm-pit-stinkers '' among the Klamaths of Southwestern Oregon ; M\u00f3ki or M\u00faki , `` cadaverous , stinking , '' an epithet originally given to one of the Sh\u00ednumo or M\u00f3ki towns for lack of bravery , and belonging to the Sh\u00ednumo language : m\u00faki dead", "context_len": 119}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` Why , that Patsy 's a reg'lar bum ! He 's called me names -- he plays hookey too , and he tried to trip me up and I give him a left-hander , and he called me a stinking pup and ever so many nasty names and then we went at it", "context_len": 54}}}, "skunks": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 20, "context": ", being appointed from it ; therefore it can be conjectured that from the Chicasa tribe a term like `` skunks , '' `` stinkards , '' may have been transferred and applied to the less esteemed gentes of other nations", "context_len": 41}}}, "alphabetic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "In this alphabetic list of ancient Creek towns and villages I have included all the names of inhabited places which I have found recorded before the emigration of the people to the Indian Territory", "context_len": 34}}}, "palatal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "The local names are written according to my scientific system of phonetics , the only change introduced being that of the palatal tch for ch", "context_len": 25}}}, "originated": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 6, "context": ") A legend , which evidently originated from the name already existing , relates that wayfarers passing there had left a large provision-basket ( s\u00e1ka ) at this locality , which was upset and left rotting , so that finally it became flattened out : from pat\u00e4\u00eddsh\u00e4s I spread out something ; pat\u00e1yi , partic", "context_len": 55}}}, "regulating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "She kept a severe eye on the small children , patted them firmly on the back when they choked , and refused to allow them to grab at the cake , regulating their tea according to her own notions of what was good for them , and turning stolen lumps of sugar out of their pockets with the cleverness of a detective", "context_len": 62}}}, "identifies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "[ Footnote 90 : The map appended to the French edition of Bartram identifies them with the K\u00fasa : `` Abikas ou Coussas", "context_len": 23}}}, "maturity": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "The name signifies `` sweet potato-mother '' ( \u00e1hi , \u00edki ) , from the circumstance that when planting sweet potatoes ( \u00e1hi ) , the fruit sown remains in the ground until the new crop comes to maturity", "context_len": 39}}}, "pursues": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Alabama river is formed by the junction of Coosa and Tallapoosa rivers ; pursues a winding course between banks about fifty feet high , and joins Tombigbee river about thirty miles above Mobile bay , when it assumes the name of Mobile river", "context_len": 43}}}, "landscapes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Boats travel from the junction to Mobile bay in about nine days , through a fertile country , with high , cleared fields and romantic landscapes ( Hawkins )", "context_len": 29}}}, "secrete": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The Creek term an\u00e1ti means a brushy , swampy place , where persons can secrete themselves", "context_len": 16}}}, "decay": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 25, "context": "This fort had once been a summer-house , but it had long since been disused , and would , no doubt , have fallen into decay , had not the children hit upon the idea of making it the scene of their pitched battles , and had so propped it up and strengthened it that it was impossible to take except by surprise", "context_len": 63}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "It is marvellous how well they are preserved , but the oak and the stone combined must have kept out the air , and parchment does not decay like paper", "context_len": 30}}}, "rotunda": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` It is variously called by travelers : hot-house , sudatory , assembly-room , winter council-house , mountain-house , [ 110 ] or , from its circular shape , rotunda", "context_len": 30}}}, "areopagus": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "Bartram viewed the `` terraces , on which formerly stood their town-house or rotunda and square or areopagus , '' and gives a lucid description of them", "context_len": 27}}}, "deputies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "So they entered all , for that it was the appointed time of the morning-meal and they had set one table for the Sultan and the Lady Bedrulbudour and Alaeddin and another for the Vizier and the officers of state and grandees of the realm and captains and chamberlains and deputies", "context_len": 51}}}, "etymology": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "Stidham heard of the following etymology of the name : In cleaning the ground for the town square and making it even , the ground and sweeping finally formed a ridge on the outside of the chunk-yard or play-ground ; from this ridge the town was called ap\u00e1la tch'-ukla", "context_len": 49}}}, "otters": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "A town of the same name , `` where otters live , '' existed among the Cheroki", "context_len": 17}}}, "devastated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The name means : `` Hurricane Town , '' for h\u00fatali in Creek is wind , huy\u00e1na passing ; it therefore marks a locality once devastated by a passing hurricane", "context_len": 30}}}, "exempt": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "Ikanatch\u00e1ka , or Holy Ground , a town on the southern side of Alabama river , built on holy ground , and therefore said to be exempt from any possible inroads of the white people", "context_len": 35}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "An undefinable air of suspicion hung over the four chums , though Fenn , from the fact that he had not entered the school , was , more or less , exempt", "context_len": 32}}}, "insurgent": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Weatherford , the leader of the insurgent Creeks , and their prophet Hilis'-h\u00e1ko resided there ; the forces gathered at this place by them were defeated December 23d , 1813", "context_len": 30}}}, "concave": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The term im\u00fakfa is Hitchiti , for ( 1 ) shell ; ( 2 ) metallic ornament of concave shape ; Hawkins interprets the name by `` gorget made of a conch", "context_len": 32}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It is held with the flat side down and the concave edge forward , and is thrown from over the shoulder", "context_len": 21}}}, "conch": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "The term im\u00fakfa is Hitchiti , for ( 1 ) shell ; ( 2 ) metallic ornament of concave shape ; Hawkins interprets the name by `` gorget made of a conch", "context_len": 32}}}, "licentiousness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "The natives were friendly to the whites and fond of visiting them ; the old chiefs were orderly men , desirous and active in restraining the young `` braves '' from the licentiousness which they had contracted through their intercourse with the scum of the white colonists", "context_len": 47}}}, "strictures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Hawkins makes some strictures at their incompetency for farming ; `` they do not know the season for planting , or , if they do , they never avail themselves of what they know , as they always plant one month too late '' ( p", "context_len": 46}}}, "incompetency": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Hawkins makes some strictures at their incompetency for farming ; `` they do not know the season for planting , or , if they do , they never avail themselves of what they know , as they always plant one month too late '' ( p", "context_len": 46}}}, "conical": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Their rifles were muzzle-loaders , with percussion caps , but they used the conical bullet , and Alden had learned long before to shoot from the saddle with his horse on a run", "context_len": 33}}}, "fishery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The fishery in the western channel of the river , below the falls , belonged to Kaw\u00edta , that in the eastern channel to Kas\u00ed'hta", "context_len": 25}}}, "limestone": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 30, "context": "When that shaft got down fifty feet , ore indications had petered out complete ; and when it got down a hundred feet , there wasn \u2019 t even a limestone stringer -- nothing but country rock , with no more yellow metal than you \u2019 d find in the sand at Far Rockaway", "context_len": 54}}}, "poisoning": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "`` The great and old beloved town of refuge , Koosah , which stands high on the eastern side of a bold river , about two hundred and fifty yards broad , that runs by the late dangerous Alebahma fort , down to the black poisoning Mobille , and so into the gulph of Mexico : '' Adair , History , p", "context_len": 62}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "Not one of them but would have been shocked at the thought that she was poisoning the minds of the dear little children whom they all loved towards the aged relative whom they were bound to reverence and respect", "context_len": 39}}}, "abbreviation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "It was , perhaps , from this place that the Alabama river was , in earlier times , called Coosa or Coussa river , but since Hawkins and others make no mention of this town , I surmise that it was identical with Koass\u00e1ti , the name being an abbreviation from the latter", "context_len": 53}}}, "manufacturing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Called so because reeds were obtained there for manufacturing arrow shafts", "context_len": 11}}}, "posterior": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "It was called so after the Treaty of New York , concluded between the United States Government and the Creek confederacy , at a date posterior to the settlement of this town , August 7th , 1790", "context_len": 37}}}, "petit": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "27 : `` le petit Talessy ou village des Noyers", "context_len": 10}}}, "migrate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Their roving disposition impelled them to leave this settlement also , and to migrate to the fertile Alachua plains , where they built Cuscowilla on the banks of a lake , and had to defend it against the attacks of the Tomocos , Utinas , Calloosas ( ? ) , Yamases and other remnant tribes of Florida , and the more northern refugees from Carolina , all of whom were helped by the Spaniards", "context_len": 74}}}, "adjoins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The town adjoins that of Chiaha ; Bartram calls it Hoositchi", "context_len": 11}}}, "associates": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 52, "context": "When the army of relief forced its way into Lucknow , Kavanagh was always near the commander-in-chief ; and , when at length they drew near to the besieged , he was one of the first at the Residency , and as he approached a loud cheer burst forth from his old associates", "context_len": 53}}}, "cymbal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "49 , renders the name by `` cymbal creek", "context_len": 9}}}, "contraction": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 42, "context": "It possesses an affirmative , negative , interrogative and distributive form , which latter is used as a form for the plural of the subject in the intransitive verbs ; it also has a large number of conversational forms usually derived by contraction , ellipses , etc", "context_len": 47}}}, "conic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "On this bluff are also five conic mounds , the largest thirty yards in diameter at the base", "context_len": 18}}}, "linguistically": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "But since the town formerly spoke another language , it is , in view of the frequency of Cheroki names in the Creek country , appropriate to regard Task\u00edgi as linguistically identical with `` Toskegee , '' a Cheroki town on Great Tennessee river , southern shore , mentioned by several authors , and appearing on Lieutenant H", "context_len": 58}}}, "tadpole": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Tokog\u00e1lgi , or `` tadpole place , '' a small Yuchi settlement on Kitchofuni creek , a northern affluent of Flint river , Georgia , which joins it about 31 40 Lat", "context_len": 32}}}, "deviating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 45, "context": "The town anciently was known under two other names : Ispok\u00f3gi , or It\u00e1lua ispok\u00f3gi , said to mean `` town of survivors , '' or `` surviving town , remnant of a town '' ; and It\u00e1lua f\u00e1tcha-s\u00edgo , `` incorrect town , town deviating from strictness", "context_len": 48}}}, "spunk": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "`` Now I tell you there is n't much fun in living to be old , and I seem to have lost my spunk", "context_len": 24}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` Well , you have spunk ! You know , if you annoy me in any way , I should think nothing of putting either Fuzz or Buzz into your bed", "context_len": 31}}}, "cribs": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "This was a great treat , for playing in the cribs and beds was not allowed without special permission , and Franky , being provided with a pair of reins , and a chair turned upside down for a horse , took his post as driver , in great glee ; while the three little girls were packed in as passengers , Maggie holding the baby", "context_len": 66}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Granby and Jennie , the three little boys snugly tucked in their cribs up-stairs , the baby lying quiet in its cradle , and Mrs", "context_len": 25}}}, "participle": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 24, "context": "[ Footnote 638 : Keniz , a word which I can not find in any dictionary , but which appears to be the past participle ( in the secondary form for mecnouz , as ketil , slain , for mertoul , ) of keneza , a lost verb of which only the fourth form acneza , he drank from a cup ( kinz ) , survives , and to mean `` something drunk from a cup", "context_len": 76}}}, "cartographers": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Also written Wolkukay by cartographers ; Wacacoys , in Census List of 1832 ; Wiccakaw by Bartram ( 1775 )", "context_len": 20}}}, "quotes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "Hawkins does not mention this old settlement , but Bartram , who traveled from 1773 to 1778 , quotes Whittumke among the Upper Creek towns speaking the `` Stincard tongue , '' which in this instance was the Koass\u00e1ti dialect", "context_len": 40}}}, "rumbles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "from u-\u00edwa `` water , '' and t\u00famk\u012bs `` it rumbles , makes noise", "context_len": 14}}}, "trails": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 11, "context": "The early explorers of North American countries all followed these Indian trails : Narvaez , Hernando de Soto , Tristan de Luna , Juan del Pardo , Lederer and Lawson , because they were led along these tracks by their Indian guides", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Yet it was by no means certain , for these tribes shifted their locations frequently as they followed the game trails or searched for better fishing places", "context_len": 27}}}, "warpaths": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "A correct and detailed knowledge of the Indian trails leading through their country , and called by them warpaths , horse trails , and by the white traders `` trading roads , '' forms an important part of Indian topography and history", "context_len": 42}}}, "del": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The early explorers of North American countries all followed these Indian trails : Narvaez , Hernando de Soto , Tristan de Luna , Juan del Pardo , Lederer and Lawson , because they were led along these tracks by their Indian guides", "context_len": 42}}}, "maternal": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 42, "context": "Alfred , her eldest son -- for Edmund was the offspring of an early amour of the king -- was on the other side of the bed , a well-made youth , combining in his features the haughty bearing of his Norman maternal ancestors with the English traits of his father ; but now his expression was one of distress and anxiety , which was yet more deeply shared by his younger brother , Edward , who even at this period manifested that strong sense of religious obligation and that early devotion which in later years caused him to be numbered amongst canonised saints", "context_len": 104}}}, "ceremonially": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The term fire evidently refers to council fires , which had to be kindled ceremonially by the friction of two pieces of wood", "context_len": 23}}}, "constituting": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 29, "context": "The term fire was also applied by Sh\u00e1wanos and other Northern Indians to the States formed by the early colonists , and is still used of the States now constituting the American Union : the thirteen fires , the seventeen fires , etc", "context_len": 43}}}, "stipulations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "A convention was arranged with the chiefs of the Lower Creeks at Savannah in 1735 , during which the legend of the Kasi ta migration was delivered , but it does not appear whether any new treaty stipulations were mooted or not at that meeting", "context_len": 45}}}, "murders": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Art thou willing to accept such penance as the Church , in consideration of thy perjuries , thy murders , which man may not avenge , since treaties protect thee -- but which God will surely remember , if thou repent not -- to accept such penance , I say , as the Church shall impose ? ''", "context_len": 58}}}, "cougar": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "K\u00e1tsalgi panther gens ; k\u00e1tsa panther , cougar", "context_len": 8}}}, "otter": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "alligator": {"freq": 35, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 34, "word_index": 4, "context": "He knew that the alligator was coming at a faster pace than his own , but he hoped that the start he had gained might yet enable him to reach the shore before those terrible jaws should close on him", "context_len": 40}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "wildcat": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 38, "context": "This suited his companions , and soon they were cleaning their guns , sorting cartridges and fitting them in their belts , taking care not to make the mistake Frank did , when he was treed by the wildcat ; and looking to their clothing and hunting boots", "context_len": 48}}}, "governing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The tribe , as far as constituting a politic body governing itself , is called in Creek it\u00e1lua , which could also be rendered by : community or civil district", "context_len": 30}}}, "elects": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The town elects him for life from a certain gens", "context_len": 10}}}, "counsellors": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 39, "context": "This will appear still better when we speak of the warrior class ; and it may be appropriate to remember that no man felt himself bound by decrees of a popular assembly , by edicts of chiefs and their counsellors , or by treaties concluded by these with alien tribes or governments", "context_len": 52}}}, "appoints": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The council appoints the Great Warrior , approves or rejects the nominations for a m\u00edko 's assistant , and gives advice in law , war or peace questions", "context_len": 28}}}, "rejects": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "The council appoints the Great Warrior , approves or rejects the nominations for a m\u00edko 's assistant , and gives advice in law , war or peace questions", "context_len": 28}}}, "nominations": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The council appoints the Great Warrior , approves or rejects the nominations for a m\u00edko 's assistant , and gives advice in law , war or peace questions", "context_len": 28}}}, "advisers": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 11, "context": "He called a council of his ablest and best friends and advisers , consisting of the principal officers of his army , and laid the facts before them", "context_len": 28}}}, "presided": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "There was a small general shop in Pengarth , where groceries and boots , apples and red-herrings , seemed all mixed up with yards of print and ribbon , penny ink-bottles , milk-cans , sun-bonnets , gingerbread , pear-drops , and bull's-eyes , presided over by a funny old woman in a black wig and horn spectacles , who seemed to find much enjoyment in gossiping with her customers ; for the children had to wait quite a long time , while the chief points of last Sunday 's sermon in chapel and the new dress of the minister 's wife were freely discussed", "context_len": 104}}}, "constraining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Since Indian character expresses itself in the most pronounced , self-willed independence , the power of the authorities was more of a persuasive than of a constraining or commanding nature", "context_len": 30}}}, "decrees": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "This will appear still better when we speak of the warrior class ; and it may be appropriate to remember that no man felt himself bound by decrees of a popular assembly , by edicts of chiefs and their counsellors , or by treaties concluded by these with alien tribes or governments", "context_len": 52}}}, "edicts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "This will appear still better when we speak of the warrior class ; and it may be appropriate to remember that no man felt himself bound by decrees of a popular assembly , by edicts of chiefs and their counsellors , or by treaties concluded by these with alien tribes or governments", "context_len": 52}}}, "governments": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "It had not been the aim of Alexander 's life to establish firm and well-settled governments in the countries that he conquered , to encourage order , and peace , and industry among men , and to introduce system and regularity in human affairs , so as to leave the world in a better condition than he found it", "context_len": 59}}}, "rulings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The law exercised by the gens was more powerful than all these temporary rulings , and , in fact , was the real motive power in the Indian community", "context_len": 29}}}, "offspring": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "It is also stated that a woman had the privilege of killing her offspring during the first lunation after the birth , but when she did so after that term she was put to death herself", "context_len": 36}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "Alfred , her eldest son -- for Edmund was the offspring of an early amour of the king -- was on the other side of the bed , a well-made youth , combining in his features the haughty bearing of his Norman maternal ancestors with the English traits of his father ; but now his expression was one of distress and anxiety , which was yet more deeply shared by his younger brother , Edward , who even at this period manifested that strong sense of religious obligation and that early devotion which in later years caused him to be numbered amongst canonised saints", "context_len": 104}}}, "surest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "It was a matter of impulse , and Harry Paul 's experience should have taught him that keeping perfectly cool , and urging the boat along to where he had last seen the body , was the surest way of rendering help", "context_len": 42}}}, "fostering": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "The surest means of ascending the ladder of honor was the capture of scalps from the enemy , and the policy of the red or bloody towns was that of fostering the warlike spirit by frequent raids and expeditions", "context_len": 39}}}, "menials": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "In some towns young men were treated as menials before they had performed some daring deeds on the battle-field or acquired a war title", "context_len": 24}}}, "abridged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "This initiation into manhood usually lasted from four to eight months , but in certain rare instances could be abridged to twelve days", "context_len": 23}}}, "honorific": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "Women and boys never had but one name , and whenever a warrior had , by successive campaigns , five or six honorific titles conferred upon him , he became generally known by one or two of these only", "context_len": 39}}}, "physic": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 38, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}, "NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 16, "context": ", while the western building served for keeping the apparatus for cooking black drink , war physic , and to store lumber", "context_len": 22}}}, "functionary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "In Milfort 's time this dignitary had become a civil and military officer , [ 98 ] and nowadays his functions are those of a civil functionary only", "context_len": 28}}}, "generalissimo": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "In cases when the towns had resolved upon a general war , a leader for all the town-tust\u0115n\u00faggis was appointed in the person of a `` generalissimo , '' called also pak\u0101 dsha , tust\u0115n\u00faggi , or tust\u0115n\u00faggi ` l\u00e1ko", "context_len": 40}}}, "distinctive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The chamber occupied by the king was a long roomy place , containing a single bedstead of carved wood , surmounted by the usual distinctive canopy , from which tapestried hangings depended , and upon which scriptural subjects were woven ; the furniture of the room partook of the usual meagreness of the times", "context_len": 54}}}, "totem": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 7, "context": "To the five war-titles below , the totem of the gens was often added , so that , for instance , one of the yah\u00f3lalgi , who offered the black drink , could be called \u00edtcho yah\u00f3la h\u00e1dsho , or y", "context_len": 41}}}, "hallooing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "\u00edtcho yah\u00f3la tassik\u00e1ya deer hallooing warrior", "context_len": 6}}}, "charmer": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 18, "context": "The knowledge thus acquired , it was believed , imparted to the person a full conjuring power and charmer 's influence over the antagonist , and enabled him to conquer the hostile warriors at a distance ( hup\u00e1-i ) and before reaching them , or to make them come near enough for easy capture", "context_len": 54}}}, "brawling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Ispah\u00eddshi , name of a headman , and usually spelt Spiechee : `` whooping , brawling '' while taking off the scalp", "context_len": 22}}}, "erroneously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "They are often , but erroneously , rendered `` thy majesty '' ; a title which does not exist in the East and which is , as is well known to students of history , of comparatively recent use in Europe", "context_len": 41}}}, "clandestinely": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "It is rather improbable that a declaration of war always preceded the attack , for the advance into the hostile territory was made clandestinely [ 103 ] ; but the resolution of starting upon the warpath was heralded in the towns with great ceremonies", "context_len": 44}}}, "prepuce": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "W\u00e1ksi , Cha'hta term referring to the drawing up of the prepuce", "context_len": 12}}}, "obscene": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The name conveyed the idea of a low , unmanly behavior , but had no obscene meaning", "context_len": 17}}}, "injurious": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Other nations regard epithets like these ( , verpi ) as highly injurious , and load their enemies with them , as the Tchiglit-Inuit do the Tinn\u00e9 Indians of the interior : taordshioit , ortcho-todsho-eitut", "context_len": 35}}}, "conjuror": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 58, "context": "The flying lady fluttered across the tent so naturally that you quite forgot you could see the wire that supported her airy form ; the Japanese acrobats climbed ropes , hung head downwards from poles , and suspended themselves in a combination of marvellous attitudes , finally tying their agile bodies together in a knot ; a Hindoo conjuror performed marvels in the way of canaries from under pocket-handkerchiefs , umbrellas out of top-hats , even producing yards and yards of coloured ribbons out of his own open mouth , much to Bobby 's mystification ; while the horses pounded round the ring with quite commendable spirit , and a lady rider in gauze and spangles jumped through hoops on to their backs with reckless daring", "context_len": 126}}}, "purify": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "To begin with , a fast of either four or eight days and the eating of certain bitter weeds was prescribed , to purify the system and to prepare the youth for a ready comprehension of the objects of tuition", "context_len": 40}}}, "conjuring": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The knowledge thus acquired , it was believed , imparted to the person a full conjuring power and charmer 's influence over the antagonist , and enabled him to conquer the hostile warriors at a distance ( hup\u00e1-i ) and before reaching them , or to make them come near enough for easy capture", "context_len": 54}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Charlie was a nervous child and rather imaginative , and was always conjuring up possibilities of disaster in his own mind", "context_len": 21}}}, "foregoing": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "A war party always proceeded in Indian file , each man stepping into the footprints of the foregoing , to prevent the enemy from knowing their number", "context_len": 27}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "[ Footnote 436 : The whole of the foregoing passage is so confused that I think it well to add here ( l ) a literal translation , as I read it : `` So the Vizier , yea , indeed , he marvelled at the greatness of that wealth more than the Sultan , but envy was killing him and waxed on him more and more when he saw the Sultan that he was satisfied with ( or accepted of ) the bride-gift and the dowry ; however , it was not possible to him that he should gainsay the truth and should say to the Sultan , 'He is not worthy ; ' only , he practised with a device upon the Sultan so he should", "context_len": 128}}}, "vestiges": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "The island , the mountain , and the river remain , preserving their original names to the present day , except that the river is now called the Mender , but , although various vestiges of ancient ruins are found scattered about the plain , no spot can be identified as the site of the city", "context_len": 56}}}, "taunted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "As he quickened his steps , he suddenly realized that he must pass the wretched little tumble-down home of Tige Fletcher , a dirty , crabbed , old recluse , who hated boys because he had been taunted and tormented by them , and who kept two fierce dogs , which were regarded as vicious and dangerous", "context_len": 57}}}, "nude": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Milfort relates that his men fought nude , because they had noticed that the fragments of clothing entering the body with the point of the missile rendered the wound much more dangerous than the missile itself", "context_len": 36}}}, "mercilessly": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "When making prisoners the Creeks habitually spared only the lives of children , killing mercilessly the adult males and females", "context_len": 20}}}, "females": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 14, "context": "-- Although succession among all Maskoki tribes was in the female line , the females occupied a subordinate condition among the Creeks , and in their households were subjected , like those of other Indians , to a life of drudgery", "context_len": 41}}}, "contiguity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 15, "context": "The encampments for the night ( h\u00e1pu ) were round-shaped , every man lying in contiguity to another in a circle , and leaving only a small issue , which was guarded by the commander", "context_len": 35}}}, "piazzas": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "A Choctaw makes his camp , in traveling , in form of a sugar loaf ; a Chicasa makes it in form of our arbours ; a Creek like to our sheds or piazzas , to a timber-house", "context_len": 38}}}, "disparate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The Creek confederacy , or `` league of the Muscogulgee '' was a purely political organization connecting the various and disparate elements , which composed it , for common action against external aggression", "context_len": 33}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Swan , who only traveled through the country to leave it again , makes the following interesting statement concerning the political and social status of the disparate tribes composing the Creek confederacy ( 1791 ; in Schoolcraft V , 259", "context_len": 40}}}, "aggression": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "The Creek confederacy , or `` league of the Muscogulgee '' was a purely political organization connecting the various and disparate elements , which composed it , for common action against external aggression", "context_len": 33}}}, "democracy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "To call this loose assemblage of towns and tribes a military democracy , in the sense that the majority of the votes decided a question brought before the people in a manner that was binding for the citizens , is entirely wrong and misleading , for Indians regard their actions subject to their own decisions only , or , at the utmost , to those of their individual gens", "context_len": 69}}}, "unanimity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "In questions of war unanimity was seldom attained in the council of a town , much less in the whole nation ; `` it is not recollected by the oldest man , that more than one-half of the nation went to war at the same time or 'took the war-talk", "context_len": 50}}}, "counsels": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "But I will do violence to myself , and will write all things accurately , in order it may serve to show that there were those amongst us who were not consenting parties , who entered not into the counsels of those men of blood , whom may God `` reward after their deeds , and according to the wickedness of their own inventions", "context_len": 64}}}, "persists": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "If the Great Warrior persists and 'goes out , ' he is followed by all who are for war", "context_len": 19}}}, "conventions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Time and place of these conventions were fixed by a chief , and the space of time between warning and that of assembly was called `` broken days", "context_len": 28}}}, "chef": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 55, "context": "Swan , after whose report this passage is quoted ( Schoolcraft V , 279 ) states that the title of the chief of the confederacy was the great beloved man , while Milfort , who was himself invested with the charge of great warrior of the nation , styles him `` Le Tastan\u00e9gy ou grand chef de guerre , '' adding , however , that in his time he was the highest authority in civil and military affairs ( M\u00e9moire , Note to p", "context_len": 84}}}, "unity": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 64, "context": "Then he went forth , wandering in the deserts and knowing not whitherward he should aim , and ceased not going till he came to a river ; whereupon , his hope being cut off for stress of chagrin that possessed him , he thought to cast himself into the stream ; but , for that he was a pious Muslim , professing the unity of God , he feared God in himself and stood on the bank ; of the stream to perform the ablution", "context_len": 86}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "It seemed as if one furious Fourth of July powder blast had disrupted the very foundations of all the family hopes and happiness , leaving a blackened wreck where there had been unity , comfort and peace", "context_len": 37}}}, "facilitating": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Hawkins , who attempted to introduce some unity among the towns for the purpose of facilitating the transaction of business of the nation , and their intercourse with the United States Government , proposed various measures , as the classing of the towns into nine districts ; these were adopted at Tukabatchi by the chiefs of the nation , on November 27th , 1799", "context_len": 64}}}, "classing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 39, "context": "Hawkins , who attempted to introduce some unity among the towns for the purpose of facilitating the transaction of business of the nation , and their intercourse with the United States Government , proposed various measures , as the classing of the towns into nine districts ; these were adopted at Tukabatchi by the chiefs of the nation , on November 27th , 1799", "context_len": 64}}}, "looseness": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": ", is another proof for the looseness of the `` powerful Creek confederacy", "context_len": 13}}}, "status": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "Swan , who only traveled through the country to leave it again , makes the following interesting statement concerning the political and social status of the disparate tribes composing the Creek confederacy ( 1791 ; in Schoolcraft V , 259", "context_len": 40}}}, "appurtenance": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "It contained the only public buildings of the town , the great house and the council-house , and , as an appurtenance , the play-ground", "context_len": 25}}}, "focus": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 4, "context": "The square was the focus of the public and social life of the town ; its present Creek name , intch\u00faka ` l\u00e1ko , is taken from the `` great house '' as its principal portion", "context_len": 36}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "It has got a shade , and you can focus the rays so they will not show outside", "context_len": 18}}}, "granaries": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "Of other buildings destined for public use I have found no mention , except of granaries or corn-cribs , which were under the supervision of the m\u00edko", "context_len": 27}}}, "partitions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "platforms": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 8, "context": "He prepared a number of ships , with platforms raised upon them in such a manner that , on getting near the walls , they could be let down , and form a sort of bridge , over which the men could pass to the broken fragments of the wall , and thence ascend through the breach above", "context_len": 58}}}, "allotment": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 38, "context": "Caleb Swan , who , in his above description of the cabins in the square , copied the original seen at Odshi-ap\u00f3fa or Little Talassie , where he stopped , differs in several particulars , especially in the allotment of the cabins to the authorities , from Hawkins , who resided in Kaw\u00edta", "context_len": 53}}}, "heterogeneous": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 8, "context": "From the roofs were dangling on the inside heterogeneous emblems of peace and trophies of war , as eagles ' feathers , swans ' wings , wooden scalping knives , war clubs , red-painted wands , bunches of hoops on which to dry their scalps , bundles of a war-physic called snake-root ( s\u00ednika in Cheroki ) , baskets , etc", "context_len": 61}}}, "bridging": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Other privileged towns possessed a covered square , by which term is meant a bridging over of the entrance spaces left between the four buildings by means of canes laid on poles", "context_len": 32}}}, "ministrants": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "In the centre of the area of the `` great house '' a perpetual fire was burning , fed by four logs , and kept up by public ministrants especially appointed for the purpose", "context_len": 34}}}, "redressed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "`` They met there every day , drank the \u00e1ssi or black drink , continued deliberations on public and domestic affairs , attended to complaints and redressed them ; then conversed about news while smoking , or amused themselves at playing `` roll the bullet '' in a sort of ten-pin alley", "context_len": 52}}}, "locations": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "Yet it was by no means certain , for these tribes shifted their locations frequently as they followed the game trails or searched for better fishing places", "context_len": 27}}}, "cone": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Its appearance is generally described as that of a huge cone placed on an octagonal frame about twelve feet high , and covered with tufts of bark", "context_len": 27}}}, "octagonal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Its appearance is generally described as that of a huge cone placed on an octagonal frame about twelve feet high , and covered with tufts of bark", "context_len": 27}}}, "elevation": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 23, "context": "It is true that the walls rose directly from the surface of the water , and of course a considerable part of their elevation was required to bring them up to the level of the surface of the land ; and then , in addition to this , they had to be carried up the whole ordinary height of a city wall to afford the usual protection to the edifices and dwellings within", "context_len": 73}}}, "warming": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 26, "context": "The fuel consisted of dry cane or dry pine slabs split fine ; and , as if it were to give a concrete image of the warming rays of the sun , these split canes were disposed in a spiral line which exhibited several revolutions around the centre", "context_len": 48}}}, "revolutions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "The fuel consisted of dry cane or dry pine slabs split fine ; and , as if it were to give a concrete image of the warming rays of the sun , these split canes were disposed in a spiral line which exhibited several revolutions around the centre", "context_len": 48}}}, "rotundas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "[ Footnote 112 : Bartram states that the Creek rotundas were of the same architecture as those of the Cheroki , but of much larger dimensions : Travels , p", "context_len": 30}}}, "inclement": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "The council-house served , to some extent , the same purposes as the `` great house , '' but was more resorted to in the inclement season than in summer", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Whenever he had thought his work too hard , or his hours too long , or the weather too inclement for further labor , he would stop in his tracks and turn his head around to his driver , and stand gazing in mute appeal , until he was urged forward", "context_len": 51}}}, "punishments": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 34, "context": "The children all shrieked with laughter at the neat way in which their parents had trapped them , and I saw that there was good feeling in this family even in the matter of punishments", "context_len": 35}}}, "leadership": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 8, "word_index": 8, "context": "The team \u2019 s subsequent adventures under the leadership of Captain Charlie Minor as he brings them back to the State College Gymnasium where the two last quarters of the Championship game are played next evening , climaxes twenty-four pulsating hours of adventure and basketball in the FIGHTING FIVE", "context_len": 49}}}, "segment": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The play-ground occupied the northwestern angle of the public square , and formed an oblong segment of it , of rather irregular shape", "context_len": 23}}}, "maypoles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 62, "context": "There was also a sort of ball play in use among the Creeks and many other Indian tribes , by which a ball ( p\u00faku ) was aimed at an object suspended on the top of a high pole , or , as it is played now , at the top of two twin poles ( puk-\u00e1bi ) , called sometimes `` maypoles", "context_len": 63}}}, "sprinkle": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "`` Why , you are a coward , then , '' said the man with his hands in his pockets , and to show his disgust he began to sprinkle the boulders about with tobacco-juice", "context_len": 35}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "It grew darker and darker and there was a sprinkle of rain", "context_len": 12}}}, "ministrant": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The fire in the centre is made by friction , very early in the day , by a ministrant especially appointed for the purpose , called the fire-maker", "context_len": 28}}}, "converge": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "At the spot where the logs converge , the new fire is kindled and the logs are consumed during the first four days of the p\u00faskita", "context_len": 26}}}, "hearths": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "On the fourth day the women rise early to obtain a spark of the new fire ; they bring it to their own hearths , which were previously cleaned and sprinkled with sand , and then kindle their fires on them", "context_len": 41}}}, "aforementioned": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "The fifth day is devoted to the bringing in of four other logs , which are disposed and kindled as aforementioned , and then the men drink \u00e1ssi", "context_len": 28}}}, "ingredients": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 39, "context": "The notice that each of the seven plants distributed to the Indians belonged , or was the emblem of a certain gens or division of people , is gathered from this passage only , and probably refers to the ingredients of some war-physic , which only a limited number of the gentes may have been entitled to contribute to the annual p\u00faskita", "context_len": 62}}}, "cobs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "Then another singular mixture is prepared , of which the ingredients must have been of symbolic significance : Old maize cobs and pine burs are placed in a pot and burned to ashes", "context_len": 33}}}, "puberty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Four girls below the age of puberty bring ashes from home , put them in the pot , and stir up all together , after which the men mix white clay with water in two pans", "context_len": 36}}}, "utters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Having gone half way , he utters the death-whoop , and repeats it four times before he reaches the water 's edge", "context_len": 22}}}, "amnesty": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "The celebration of the p\u00faskita had a favorable influence upon the minds of the people , for it was a signal of amnesty , absolving the Indian of all crimes , murder excepted , and seemed to bury guilt itself in oblivion", "context_len": 42}}}, "absolving": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The celebration of the p\u00faskita had a favorable influence upon the minds of the people , for it was a signal of amnesty , absolving the Indian of all crimes , murder excepted , and seemed to bury guilt itself in oblivion", "context_len": 42}}}, "symbolized": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "This change of mind was symbolized by the custom of the women of breaking to pieces all the household utensils of the past year , and replacing them by new ones ; the men refitted all their property so as to look new , and it was considered extremely disgraceful , even for the most indigent , to eat any of the new maize before the annual busk ( Sketch , pp", "context_len": 72}}}, "incomplete": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "An incomplete and unsatisfactory , though curious list of the elements then ( 1771 ) composing the Maskoki confederacy and of its western allies is contained in B", "context_len": 28}}}, "amplifies": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Swan witnessed at Odshi-ap\u00f3fa , near the confluence of the Coosa and Tallapoosa rivers ; it explains and amplifies many of the incidents related by Hawkins", "context_len": 26}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Burton amplifies , `` She wondered at the marvellous sight and the glamour of the scene", "context_len": 17}}}, "formalities": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 32, "context": "Maybe the judge or some will one question you whether you are willing to take me for a father , since you are old enough to choose , and there are several formalities , but the thing is often done , and you will be mine , mine , '' pressing her to his heart in rapture", "context_len": 57}}}, "ceremonial": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Then four other men entered the square in the same manner , carrying branches of the new cassine , some of which the priest threw into the fire , the rest being immediately parched and cooked for ceremonial use", "context_len": 39}}}, "unlawful": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 33, "context": "Colonel Rush would not say so to the children , seeing that no such idea had occurred to them , but it was his own opinion that Hannah had in some way obtained unlawful possession of Percy 's letter to Lena , had mastered its contents , and then taken steps for his relief which she believed could not be discovered", "context_len": 61}}}, "congregate": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The fourth day all classes congregate in the `` great house '' promiscuously ; the game killed on the previous day is given to the public , and the women are cooking the provisions brought in from all sides , over the new fire", "context_len": 44}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "I 've read of 'salt licks , ' where deer congregate , but I never figured out that our boiling spring might be one", "context_len": 24}}}, "convivial": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 26, "context": "The rigorous fasting observed also fitted the people to prepare for a new moral life , and made them more receptive for the supernatural ; the convivial scene which closed the busk typified the idea that all men , whether low or high , are born brethren", "context_len": 47}}}, "solstitial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "It appears from all that the busk is not a solstitial celebration , but a rejoicing over the first fruits of the year", "context_len": 23}}}, "mankind": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 109, "context": "Now his mother was with the Lady Bedrulbudour , and when he came up and did off her veil , she fell to gazing upon the bride 's beauty and grace and looked at the pavilion , the which was all wroughten [ 497 ] of gold and jewels and therein were golden lustres , all embossed with emeralds and rubies ; and she said in herself , `` Methought the Sultan 's palace was magnificent ; but , for this pavilion [ 498 ] alone , I doubt me the greatest of the Chosroes and the kings never owned its match ; nor , methinketh , might all mankind avail to make the like thereof", "context_len": 116}, "NOUN": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 60, "context": "Since the memorable time when Captain Cook sailed into Botany Bay in 1769 and saw the naked native Australian poising erect to hurl his peculiar weapon , the boomerang has continued to excite the curiosity and amazement of the civilized world ; and truly the finding of such a scientific weapon in the hands of this so-called lowest order of mankind is an astonishing fact , to be simply accepted as another oddity of this odd , topsy-turvy corner of the world", "context_len": 82}}}, "rigorous": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "The rigorous fasting observed also fitted the people to prepare for a new moral life , and made them more receptive for the supernatural ; the convivial scene which closed the busk typified the idea that all men , whether low or high , are born brethren", "context_len": 47}}}, "receptive": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 37, "context": "Squire Bidwell , who happened to be at once the local justice of the peace and a good friend of Joe Gaston , found it hard to believe that the boy who had been an apt and receptive pupil in his Sunday school had proved to be a common thief", "context_len": 50}}}, "typified": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "This was in the centre of the place , where the two main streets crossed , and , on inquiring , the lads learned that it was planned to hold a sort of procession , with the Old Year , typified by Father Time , going out , while the New Year came in", "context_len": 54}}}, "purification": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 40, "context": "`` 'Since they learned the virtues of these herbs , their women , at certain times , have a separate fire , and remain apart from the men five , six , and seven days , for the sake of purification", "context_len": 41}}}, "analogies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Although the ritual of the busk differed in every Creek tribe , many analogies can be traced with well-known customs among the Aztec and Maya nations , whose `` unlucky five days '' at the year 's close equally terminated with rejoicings , as the precursors of a new life", "context_len": 50}}}, "precursors": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 45, "context": "Although the ritual of the busk differed in every Creek tribe , many analogies can be traced with well-known customs among the Aztec and Maya nations , whose `` unlucky five days '' at the year 's close equally terminated with rejoicings , as the precursors of a new life", "context_len": 50}}}, "restrict": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "Abundant material for the study of ethnography is on hand for the earlier and later periods of the Creek nation ; but here we have to restrict ourselves to some points which are especially adapted to the illustration of the migration legends", "context_len": 42}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "There are some that speak of the chiefs only and not of the people , or fill the tales with mythic heroes and impossible events , while the more sober and intelligent restrict the miraculous element to narrow limits , though never excluding it entirely", "context_len": 45}}}, "context": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 10, "context": "It is , however , abundantly evident from the general context that this is the author 's intention in the passage in question , Alaeddin 's head being metaphorically in the hands of ( or pledged to ) the headsman , inasmuch as he had engaged to return and suffer decapitation in case he should not succeed in recovering the princess within forty days", "context_len": 64}}}, "households": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 26, "context": "-- Although succession among all Maskoki tribes was in the female line , the females occupied a subordinate condition among the Creeks , and in their households were subjected , like those of other Indians , to a life of drudgery", "context_len": 41}}}, "divorces": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Divorces were of frequent occurrence", "context_len": 5}}}, "lunation": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "At the commencement of the twelfth lunation he performs for four days the same rites as he did at the beginning of the initiation , but on the fifth he leaves the cabin , gathers maize-cobs , burns them to ashes , and with these rubs his whole body", "context_len": 49}}}, "illegitimate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "[ 119 ] This may have been the practice in a few Creek tribes , but it is doubtful that such was the general law in all , except in regard to illegitimate offspring", "context_len": 34}}}, "succinct": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "96 ( 1775 ) , in the following succinct form :", "context_len": 11}}}, "braining": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}}}, "brooms": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 70, "context": "`` The women are employed , besides the cultivation of the earth , in dressing the victuals , preparing , scraping , braining , rubbing and smoaking the Roe-skins , making macksens of them , spinning buffaloe wool , making salt , preparing cassine drink , drying the cham\u00e6rops and passiflora , making cold flour for traveling , gathering nuts and making their milk ; likewise in making baskets , brooms , pots , bowls and other earthen and wooden vessels", "context_len": 81}}}, "gore": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 46, "context": "96 ) : `` Creeks make the boys swim in the coldest weather ; make them frequently undergo scratching from head to foot , through the skin , with broken glass or gar-fish teeth [ 120 ] , so as to make them all in a gore of blood , and then wash them with cold water ; this is with them the arcanum against all diseases ; but when they design it as a punishment to the boys , they dry-scratch them , i", "context_len": 85}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Then , with lowered head , the animal tried to gore the prostrate lad", "context_len": 14}}}, "manipulation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "[ Footnote 120 : Also practiced once a year upon the Shetimasha warriors , on their knee-joints , by men expressly appointed to this manipulation", "context_len": 25}}}, "initiating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "The ceremony of initiating youth into manhood , says B", "context_len": 10}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "As the four started off , with Fenn in the lead , they chuckled to think of the blank looks of the initiating team , when the members should return for the candidates , to find them missing", "context_len": 38}}}, "gathers": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 34, "context": "At the commencement of the twelfth lunation he performs for four days the same rites as he did at the beginning of the initiation , but on the fifth he leaves the cabin , gathers maize-cobs , burns them to ashes , and with these rubs his whole body", "context_len": 49}}}, "intoxicates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "A youth of the proper age gathers two handfuls of the sow\u00e1tchko plant , which intoxicates and maddens , and eats this very bitter root for a whole day , after which he steeps the leaves in water and drinks from this", "context_len": 42}}}, "maddens": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "A youth of the proper age gathers two handfuls of the sow\u00e1tchko plant , which intoxicates and maddens , and eats this very bitter root for a whole day , after which he steeps the leaves in water and drinks from this", "context_len": 42}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` When we can not get blood we can drink the red wine , The Sea King sang in his might ; For it maddens the brain , it gives strength to the arm , And kindles the soul in the fight", "context_len": 42}}}, "steeps": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "A youth of the proper age gathers two handfuls of the sow\u00e1tchko plant , which intoxicates and maddens , and eats this very bitter root for a whole day , after which he steeps the leaves in water and drinks from this", "context_len": 42}}}, "spoonfuls": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "After sunset he eats two or three spoonfuls of boiled grits", "context_len": 11}}}, "grits": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "Boiled grits -- the only food allowed to him during the first four moons -- may be cooked for him by a little girl , but on a fire kindled especially for his own use", "context_len": 35}}}, "abstains": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Putting on a new pair of moccasins ( stillipa\u00ed a ) , he leaves the cabin , and during twelve moons abstains from eating the meat of young bucks , of turkey-cocks , fowls , peas and salt , and is also forbidden to pick his ears and to scratch his head with his fingers , but must use a small splinter to perform these operations", "context_len": 66}}}, "performs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "At the commencement of the twelfth lunation he performs for four days the same rites as he did at the beginning of the initiation , but on the fifth he leaves the cabin , gathers maize-cobs , burns them to ashes , and with these rubs his whole body", "context_len": 49}}}, "elicits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "At the end of the moon he elicits transpiration by sleeping under blankets , then goes into cold water , an act which ends the ceremony", "context_len": 26}}}, "transpiration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "At the end of the moon he elicits transpiration by sleeping under blankets , then goes into cold water , an act which ends the ceremony", "context_len": 26}}}, "physicking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "The herb medicines are administered to him by the \u00edsti pak\u0101 dsha ` l\u00e1ko or `` great leader , '' who , when speaking of him , says : pusidshedsh\u0113 yi sanatchumitch\u00e4 tch\u00e4-is , [ 123 ] `` I am passing him through the physicking process repeatedly , '' or : n\u00e1ki om\u00e1lga imaki ` l\u00e4 dsh\u00e4yi s\u00e1 ` lit \u00f3m\u00e4s , tch\u00ed , `` I am teaching him all the matters proper for him to think of", "context_len": 78}}}, "exposures": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "enchanting": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 52, "context": "While the physical exposures and privations were thought to render him strong in body and fearless in battle , the dreams coming upon him , in consequence of the exhaustion by hunger and maddening by all sorts of physic , were supposed to furnish him visions , which would reveal to him enchanting views for future life , material riches and the ways to acquire them , the principles of bravery and persistence , the modes of charming enemies and game at a distance , of obtaining scalps , and prospects of general happiness and of a respected position in his tribe", "context_len": 102}}}, "perpetuate": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 105, "context": "The Sultan accordingly went up to the pavilion [ 535 ] and entering the kiosk , [ 536 ] viewed it right and left and saw no manner defect in its lattices , but found them all perfect ; whereat he was astounded and embracing Alaeddin , fell a-kissing him and saying , `` O my son , what is this extraordinary thing ? In one night thou dost a work wherefrom the jewellers would fail in months ! By Allah , methinketh thou hast not thy fellow [ 537 ] in the world ! '' Quoth Alaeddin , `` God prolong thy life and perpetuate thy continuance ! Thy slave is not worthy of this praise", "context_len": 117}}}, "pictorial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "-- To perpetuate the memory of historical facts , as epidemics , tribal wars , migrations , the Creeks possessed the pictorial or ideographic writing , the material generally used for it being tanned skins", "context_len": 35}}}, "ideographic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "-- To perpetuate the memory of historical facts , as epidemics , tribal wars , migrations , the Creeks possessed the pictorial or ideographic writing , the material generally used for it being tanned skins", "context_len": 35}}}, "transmitting": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "47-49 ) mentions another mode of transmitting facts to posterity , which shows a certain analogy with the wampum-belts of the Iroquois and Algonkin tribes", "context_len": 25}}}, "rosary": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "It consisted of strings of small beads , in shape of a narrow ribbon ( banderole ) or rosary ( chapelet )", "context_len": 22}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Then she gave him her staff and taught him how he should walk and how he should do , whenas he went down into the city ; moreover , she put her rosary on his neck and finally giving him the mirror , said to him , `` Look now ; thou differest not from me in aught", "context_len": 58}}}, "chronology": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 34, "context": "The events of each year were kept strictly distinct from the events of any subsequent year by a certain arrangement of the grains , and thus the strings proved reliable documents as to the chronology of tribal events", "context_len": 38}}}, "chefs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The oldest of the m\u00edkalgi ( les chefs des vieillards ) often recounted to Milfort , who had risen to the dignity of `` chief warrior '' in the nation , episodes of early Creek history , suggested to them by these `` national archives", "context_len": 45}}}, "fixation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "Many old traditions of historic importance must have been embodied in these records ; but the only one given by Milfort , referring to the emigration of the Creeks from their ancient cave-homes along Red river , is so mixed up with incredible matter , that the fixation of the events , as far as then remembered , must have taken place many generations after the arrival of the Creeks in their Alabama homes", "context_len": 74}}}, "pueblo": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "Then he traversed the Tallise `` province , '' peopled with many towns , and entered the great pueblo of Tallise on September 18th , to stay there twenty days", "context_len": 30}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "In 1559 another Spanish leader , Tristan de Luna , disembarked in or near Mobile bay , then went north in quest of gold and treasure , reached Nanipacna , or `` pueblo Santa Cruz de Nanipacna , '' and from there arrived , after experiencing many privations and trials , among the Co\u00e7as , who were then engaged in warfare with the Napochies ( nanpissa ? cf", "context_len": 68}}}, "maine": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Many other towns were visible on the opposite side of the `` maine river , '' on which Tallisi [ 125 ] stood", "context_len": 23}}}, "computed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "De Soto arrived in Mavila on October 18th , and the whole distance from Co\u00e7a to Tuscalusa is computed by the Knight of Elvas at sixty leagues , the direction of the route being from north to south", "context_len": 38}}}, "var": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "The villages of Chiaha ( Chisca , Ychiaha , China , var", "context_len": 12}}}, "tali": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "chronicler": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 44, "context": "'T is easy to stand in the deadly gap and keep the foe from a beleaguered citadel : men praise the deed , and there is a feeling of conscious pride which sustains one , but the truly great deeds are those which no chronicler records", "context_len": 46}}}, "emperor": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "The Spaniards established in Northern Florida paid honors to the `` emperor of the Cowetas , '' therewith hoping to influence all the Lower and Upper Creeks , and in 1710 received Kaw\u00edta delegates with distinction at St", "context_len": 38}}}, "therewith": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "The Spaniards established in Northern Florida paid honors to the `` emperor of the Cowetas , '' therewith hoping to influence all the Lower and Upper Creeks , and in 1710 received Kaw\u00edta delegates with distinction at St", "context_len": 38}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 54, "context": "Thou sayst , O my mother , that I have no gift to present to the Sultan , according to the wont of the folk , whilst in fact I have by me a gift and an offering , the like whereof methinketh no king ever possessed , no , nor aught to match therewith ; for [ 338 ] thou must know , O my mother , that the fruits , which I brought in the purses [ 339 ] from the treasure and which I deemed glass or crystal , are very jewels , methinketh all the kings of the world may not compass the least of them , and I , of my companying with the jewellers , know that they are precious stones", "context_len": 127}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "It was a great disappointment to him to do this -- yet he was able to write , `` It is my duty to acknowledge an overruling and directing Providence in all the very minutest things , by being in whatever state I am therewith content", "context_len": 46}}}, "cede": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "Wikatch\u00e1mpa , the Ok\u00f3ni m\u00edko , proclaimed that his tribe would peaceably cede to the British all lands not needed by themselves", "context_len": 22}}}, "trustees": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` The treaty of land-cession , commerce and alliance was signed May 21st , and ratified by the trustees of the colony of Georgia , October 18th , 1733", "context_len": 29}}}, "pledges": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "The treaty was confirmed by pledges on the side of the Creeks , which consisted in a bundle of buckskins for each town , whereas the English made presents of arms , garments , etc", "context_len": 35}}}, "buckskins": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 36, "context": "Shagbark crumpled up some dry fragments from a plug of tobacco , in the palm of his hand , punched them into the bowl of his pipe , switched a match along the side of his buckskins , applied the tiny flame , and rode to the head of the company without another word", "context_len": 54}}}, "fickleness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Bradley and Dewey , knowing the fickleness of a mining-community , were a little apprehensive that their original suspicions might again be aroused , and that some among them might be led to think they had make a mistake , after all , and hung the wrong men", "context_len": 48}}}, "steadiness": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 50, "context": "Sure-footed as a goat , steady of head and nerve , reckless of danger , yet with sense enough to remember the Colonel 's charge not to look below him , he reached her side , freed her clothes from the clinging bushes ; then , with a care and steadiness which Mr", "context_len": 53}}}, "convention": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "A convention was arranged with the chiefs of the Lower Creeks at Savannah in 1735 , during which the legend of the Kasi ta migration was delivered , but it does not appear whether any new treaty stipulations were mooted or not at that meeting", "context_len": 45}}}, "preclude": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "They also requested that brass weights and sealed measures should be deposited with the m\u00edkos of each town , to preclude the traders settled among them from cheating", "context_len": 28}}}, "abstracts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The contents of such papers as relate more especially to South Carolina are hinted at in numerous abstracts of them given in a catalogue in Collections of South Carolina Historical Society , Vols", "context_len": 33}}}, "catalogue": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "The contents of such papers as relate more especially to South Carolina are hinted at in numerous abstracts of them given in a catalogue in Collections of South Carolina Historical Society , Vols", "context_len": 33}}}, "irruption": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "They probably could not now have averted destruction , but they might , perhaps , have prevented its coming upon them in so terrible a shape as the irruption of thirty thousand frantic and infuriated soldiers through the breaches in their walls to take their city by storm", "context_len": 48}}}, "dispossessed": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 64, "context": "518 , in describing the mounds of the country , states `` that the region lying between Savanna river and Oakmulge , east and west , and from the sea coast ( of the Atlantic ) to the Cherokee or Apalachean mountains ( filled with these mounds ) was possessed by the Cherokees since the arrival of the Europeans ; but they were afterwards dispossessed by the Muscogulges , and all that country was probably , many ages preceding the Cherokee invasion , inhabited by one nation or confederacy ( unknown to the Cherokees , Creeks )", "context_len": 97}}}, "enfeebled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The enfeebled Creeks then left the country and went to the Coosa river", "context_len": 13}}}, "hostility": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 56, "context": "Meanwhile the English continued the pursuit until a small remnant of Danes repassed the river ; only a small remnant of the party which , as it will be easily guessed , instigated by Edric , had sallied forth to besiege the place where Edmund had found refuge , who had so recently provoked the bitter hostility of Sweyn", "context_len": 59}}}, "embodying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 21, "context": "Her arms were folded across her butternut waist , and under the man 's hat a grim resolution seemed to be embodying itself", "context_len": 23}}}, "civilizing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Surrounded as they were by white settlements on all sides , this revolt , known also as the Red Stick War , was the last consequential sign of reaction of the aboriginal Creek mind against civilizing influences", "context_len": 37}}}, "statistic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "The statistic dates of the Creek population given before B", "context_len": 10}}}, "tillable": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Commissioner of Indian Affairs for 1881 gives a Creek population of 15,000 , settled upon 3,215,495 acres of land ; one half of these are tillable , but only 80,000 acres were cultivated during that year by these Indians", "context_len": 39}}}, "vocalized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "of Maskoki is a harmonious , clearly vocalized form of speech , averse to nasalization", "context_len": 15}}}, "syntax": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 1, "context": "The syntax is remarkably simple and uniform ; the multiplicity of grammatic forms precludes the formation of many syntactic rules , just as in Sanscrit", "context_len": 25}}}, "endings": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 23, "context": "An archaic form , called the female language , exists outside of the common Creek , and mainly differs from it in the endings of the verbs", "context_len": 27}}}, "alternate": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The alternating processes observed here also occur in most other Indian and illiterate languages : tch , dsh alternate with ts , ds , h with k , ; g with the other gutturals , b with p , d with t , \u0101 with e , o with u", "context_len": 50}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He eats the herbage which grows among the rocks and hills that alternate with the great sandy plains in all these countries", "context_len": 22}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Thus in alternate hope and despair he sank at last to sleep -- nature had its way -- even as the criminal has slept on the rack", "context_len": 27}}}, "rhetoric": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "The accent shifts for rhetoric and syntactic causes , and many unaccented syllables are pronounced long", "context_len": 16}}}, "syntactic": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Gradation of adjectives and of attributive verbs formed from these can be effected in different ways , which are more perfect and expressive here than in those Indian languages which can express gradation only by syntactic means", "context_len": 37}}}, "unaccented": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The accent shifts for rhetoric and syntactic causes , and many unaccented syllables are pronounced long", "context_len": 16}}}, "modulation": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "In the pronunciation of the natives there is a sort of singing modulation , which likes to lengthen the last syllables of a sentence", "context_len": 24}}}, "vocative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The absolute case , when used as a vocative , often lengthens or strongly accentuates the last syllable", "context_len": 18}}}, "lengthens": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 27, "context": "'Therefore , they thought it good you hear a play , And frame your mind to mirth and merriment , Which bars a thousand harms , and lengthens life", "context_len": 29}}}, "accentuates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The absolute case , when used as a vocative , often lengthens or strongly accentuates the last syllable", "context_len": 18}}}, "indirect": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 16, "context": "In history , William Bradford , a worthy man in his own way , has two indirect claims to fame", "context_len": 20}}}, "locative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "The suffix -n indicates the direct and indirect object , and also sometimes the locative case", "context_len": 16}}}, "provisional": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 9, "context": "Coach Lighton named those who were to constitute a provisional 'varsity eleven , and , to his delight , Tom 's name was among the first named", "context_len": 27}}}, "vocables": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I call it absolute , because the natives , when giving vocables of the language not forming part of a sentence , mention them in that case in Creek , in Hitchiti , in Koass\u00e1ti , etc", "context_len": 37}}}, "inflections": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": ", from the regular or standard forms ; and in some of its inflections also a reverential besides the common form", "context_len": 21}}}, "collectivity": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "The suffix -algi , though sometimes used as a plural suffix , designates collectivity : u-ika\u00edwa spring of water , u-ikaiw\u00e1lki place with water-springs , and u-ikain\u00e1lki people living at the springs ; al\u00edktcha conjurer , al\u00edktchalgi conjurers as one body , taken in a body", "context_len": 46}}}, "differentiated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The parts of speech being but imperfectly differentiated , tenses can be expressed in nouns by adding suffixes : m\u00edko chief , mikot\u00e1ti , m\u00edko-\u014d m\u0101 one who was , has been chief ; m\u00edko-ta ` l\u00e1ni a future chief ; adsulagit\u00e1ti the defunct forefathers", "context_len": 45}}}, "defunct": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "The parts of speech being but imperfectly differentiated , tenses can be expressed in nouns by adding suffixes : m\u00edko chief , mikot\u00e1ti , m\u00edko-\u014d m\u0101 one who was , has been chief ; m\u00edko-ta ` l\u00e1ni a future chief ; adsulagit\u00e1ti the defunct forefathers", "context_len": 45}}}, "appending": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "Adjectives are made negative by appending the privative particle -go , -gu , -ko , -ku : \u00edtskisusi having a mother , itskisusiko motherless ; h\u00ed ` li good , h\u00ed ` ligo not good , bad", "context_len": 37}}}, "polysyllabic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "They are formed by a partial reduplication of the radix , when the basis is monosyllabic , or often of the last syllable of the basis , when the word is polysyllabic", "context_len": 32}}}, "privative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Adjectives are made negative by appending the privative particle -go , -gu , -ko , -ku : \u00edtskisusi having a mother , itskisusiko motherless ; h\u00ed ` li good , h\u00ed ` ligo not good , bad", "context_len": 37}}}, "prefixing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "A comparative is formed by prefixing isim- , isin- , isi- , apheretically sim- , sin- , si- to the adjective or the attributive verb , the two objects compared standing usually before the adjective or verb", "context_len": 37}}}, "superlative": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 1, "context": "A superlative is formed by placing i ` li- , apheretically ` li- , before the comparative : m\u00e1hi tall , is\u00edmmahi taller than , i'lis\u00edmmahi , ` lis\u00edmmahi , ` lis\u00edmahi tallest of , lit", "context_len": 36}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Or the superlative is expressed by the augmentative adverb m\u00e1hi : very , quite , greatly , largely y\u00edktchi m\u00e1hi , the strongest , which at the same time means : very strong , quite strong ; ` l\u00e1ko m\u00e1hi largest and very large ; m\u00e1himahi tallest and very tall ; the latter also being expressed by a lengthening of the vowel : m\u0101 hi very tall", "context_len": 68}}}, "inversion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Minuitive gradation is effected by inversion of the sense in the sentence and the use of the comparative ; they say : `` silver is costlier than iron , '' instead of saying : `` iron is less costly than silver", "context_len": 41}}}, "costlier": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Minuitive gradation is effected by inversion of the sense in the sentence and the use of the comparative ; they say : `` silver is costlier than iron , '' instead of saying : `` iron is less costly than silver", "context_len": 41}}}, "complements": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "What we call prepositions are generally nominal forms in Creek , inflected like nouns and placed after their complements as postpositions , governing the absolute case :", "context_len": 27}}}, "substituting": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "sa- , s- ; a distributive substituting -\u00e1kin to -in of the cardinal , and an adverbial form in -a", "context_len": 20}, "VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Evans , '' he hailed , as he recognized the regular flagman on duty for whom Wacker had been substituting for three days past", "context_len": 24}}}, "ya": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "h\u00eda , ya , \u00ed-a this ( close by ) ; subj", "context_len": 12}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` O company '' ( ya jema't ) , a polite formula of address , equivalent to our `` Gentlemen", "context_len": 20}}}, "inflects": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The Creek verb is of the polysynthetic type , and inflects by means of prefixes , infixes and ( chiefly by ) suffixes", "context_len": 23}}}, "infixes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The Creek verb is of the polysynthetic type , and inflects by means of prefixes , infixes and ( chiefly by ) suffixes", "context_len": 23}}}, "ellipses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "It possesses an affirmative , negative , interrogative and distributive form , which latter is used as a form for the plural of the subject in the intransitive verbs ; it also has a large number of conversational forms usually derived by contraction , ellipses , etc", "context_len": 47}}}, "affix": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 45, "context": "Zein-ud-din ( Adornment of the Faith ) [ he ] of the Images , Zein ( adornment ) not being a name used by the Arabic-speaking races , unless with some such addition as ud-Din ( `` of the Faith '' ) , and the affix ul Asnam ( `` [ He ] of the Images '' ) being a sobriquet arising from the circumstances of the hero 's after-life , unless its addition , as recommended by the astrologers , is meant as an indication of the latter 's fore-knowledge of what was to befall him thereafter", "context_len": 98}}}, "aorist": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "`` believed not what time ( ayyumetn ) the day broke ; '' but ayyumeta ( of which ayyumeta is a vulgar corruption ) supposes the future and should be used with the aorist", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "aorist passive of IV ) , i", "context_len": 7}}}, "transitive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "Many transitive verbs have , when their object stands in the plural , a ( distributive ) form differing entirely or partially from the one referring to an object in the singular ; a few others show this change , when their subject passes from the singular to the plural number", "context_len": 51}}}, "combining": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "Alfred , her eldest son -- for Edmund was the offspring of an early amour of the king -- was on the other side of the bed , a well-made youth , combining in his features the haughty bearing of his Norman maternal ancestors with the English traits of his father ; but now his expression was one of distress and anxiety , which was yet more deeply shared by his younger brother , Edward , who even at this period manifested that strong sense of religious obligation and that early devotion which in later years caused him to be numbered amongst canonised saints", "context_len": 104}}}, "conjugated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "No real substantive verb being extant , its want is supplied by \u00f3m\u00e4s , m\u00f3m\u00e4s , t\u00f3y\u00e4s I am so , I am such ; these are conjugated regularly , and when connected with the verbals in -t ( -at , -it , -ut ) of any verb , compose a periphrastic conjugation which displays itself in an almost infinite number of forms", "context_len": 63}}}, "periphrastic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 51, "context": "No real substantive verb being extant , its want is supplied by \u00f3m\u00e4s , m\u00f3m\u00e4s , t\u00f3y\u00e4s I am so , I am such ; these are conjugated regularly , and when connected with the verbals in -t ( -at , -it , -ut ) of any verb , compose a periphrastic conjugation which displays itself in an almost infinite number of forms", "context_len": 63}}}, "impersonal": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "From all this it becomes evident , that the Creek verb surpasses in its large power of polysynthesis the Algonkin , Dakota and Kalapuya verb , and in the richness of its forms approaches closely to the Iroquois verb , which is poorer in tenses , but has an impersonal conjugation and fourteen persons to each tense of the finite verb", "context_len": 61}}}, "surpass": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 31, "context": "A violin solo followed from the village tailor , who was much applauded , most of the hearers being persuaded that not even the band in the Warford Public Gardens could surpass 'Bill Evans and his fiddle", "context_len": 37}}}, "subjunctive": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Conditional or subjunctive mode", "context_len": 4}}}, "exhortative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "\u00edsipas ! take ! ( reverential or exhortative )", "context_len": 9}}}, "derivative": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "This form had better be called a derivative form than a voice , as will appear from the following instances :", "context_len": 21}}}, "ki": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "k\u00ed ` l\u00e4s I know , ki ` l\u00eddsh\u00e4s I inform , apprize , i-uki ` lku\u00eddsh\u00e4s I explain myself", "context_len": 20}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "hui": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "hu\u00ed ` l\u00e4s I stand , hui ` l\u00eddsh\u00e4s I set up , place , make stand", "context_len": 17}}}, "conjugates": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "A transitive verb connected with its indirect pronominal object conjugates in the same manner , unless there is in it the idea for the benefit of , or for the detriment of , or from , away from somebody or something connected with it", "context_len": 44}}}, "clauses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "In spite of the frequent use to which they are put they do not relieve the sentence of its heaviness to any perceptible extent ; for what we call incident clauses and also many co-ordinate principal sentences are uniformly expressed by groups of words , the verb of which stands in the -t or -n verbal , which nearest corresponds to our participle in -ing , or to having ( h", "context_len": 71}}}, "embarrasses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "This sort of incapsulation greatly embarrasses interpreters in the rendering of Creek texts in any of the modern European languages , which have a tendency towards analytic and an aversion to synthetic structure of the sentence , and therefore use conjunctions freely", "context_len": 42}}}, "interpreters": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "This sort of incapsulation greatly embarrasses interpreters in the rendering of Creek texts in any of the modern European languages , which have a tendency towards analytic and an aversion to synthetic structure of the sentence , and therefore use conjunctions freely", "context_len": 42}}}, "analytic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "This sort of incapsulation greatly embarrasses interpreters in the rendering of Creek texts in any of the modern European languages , which have a tendency towards analytic and an aversion to synthetic structure of the sentence , and therefore use conjunctions freely", "context_len": 42}}}, "synthetic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "This sort of incapsulation greatly embarrasses interpreters in the rendering of Creek texts in any of the modern European languages , which have a tendency towards analytic and an aversion to synthetic structure of the sentence , and therefore use conjunctions freely", "context_len": 42}}}, "conjunction": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 1, "context": "A conjunction corresponding in every respect to our and exists in none of the Maskoki dialects", "context_len": 16}}}, "multiplicity": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 33, "context": "The wonder always is that it does not break with even a few pounds of fish therein , but it rarely does , for co-operation is power , and it is in the multiplicity of crossing threads that the strength consists", "context_len": 41}}}, "fortuitous": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Some of them are undoubtedly borrowed , while others may rest on a fortuitous resemblance", "context_len": 15}}}, "cr": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 0, "context": "Cr", "context_len": 1}}}, "undying": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "But there are other incidents which pass from father to son through many generations , and the remembrance of them , though altered in many particulars and variously recounted , seems to be undying", "context_len": 34}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "In that last sentence , perhaps , lies the secret of the man 's far-reaching and undying influence", "context_len": 18}}}, "famines": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 33, "context": "Events of this kind are migrations , long warfare or decisive battles , which resulted either in defeat or victory , alliances with cognate or friendly tribes , times of abundance , of famines and epidemics", "context_len": 36}}}, "uncultured": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "This also shows the process , how historic legends and traditions are forming among uncultured nations , which are possessed of imperfect means only for the transmission of ideas to posterity", "context_len": 31}}}, "transmission": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "This also shows the process , how historic legends and traditions are forming among uncultured nations , which are possessed of imperfect means only for the transmission of ideas to posterity", "context_len": 31}}}, "traditionary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Whenever this traditionary lore is written down by a civilized people , then the gathering of these tales , half mythic and half historic , forms a commencement of historiography , and by later generations is regarded as valued material for clearing up the dawn of history", "context_len": 47}}}, "lore": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "Up to this period we have availed ourselves of extracts from the Diary of Father Cuthbert ; but the events of the following four years , as recorded in that record , although full of interest for the antiquarian or the lover of monastic lore , would possess scant interest for the general reader , and have also little connection with the course of our tale ; therefore we will convey the information they contain , which properly pertains to our subject , in few words , and those our own , returning occasionally to the Diary", "context_len": 97}}}, "historiography": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "Whenever this traditionary lore is written down by a civilized people , then the gathering of these tales , half mythic and half historic , forms a commencement of historiography , and by later generations is regarded as valued material for clearing up the dawn of history", "context_len": 47}}}, "excluding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "There are some that speak of the chiefs only and not of the people , or fill the tales with mythic heroes and impossible events , while the more sober and intelligent restrict the miraculous element to narrow limits , though never excluding it entirely", "context_len": 45}}}, "prohibits": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Many of the North American tribes , especially on the Pacific coast , have no knowledge of early events in their tribe , because a severe law prohibits them from calling their dead relatives by their names", "context_len": 37}}}, "suffices": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "This superstition alone suffices to destroy the historic sense in the population , but does not seem to have operated among the Aztecs , Mayas and Quichhuas to any noticeable degree", "context_len": 31}}}, "bulrushes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "These latter we call autochthonic ; the Kalapuya of Will\u00e1met Valley , Oregon , and the Washo around Carson , Nevada , who claim to have originated from bulrushes in the vicinity , belong to these", "context_len": 36}}}, "astronomical": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "Their migration legends are intermingled with myths and mythic ideas ; nevertheless , they prove that the migrations took place in comparatively recent times , and that these accounts are not pure astronomical or other fictions", "context_len": 36}}}, "fictions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 35, "context": "Their migration legends are intermingled with myths and mythic ideas ; nevertheless , they prove that the migrations took place in comparatively recent times , and that these accounts are not pure astronomical or other fictions", "context_len": 36}}}, "synonymous": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 68, "context": "asked Alaeddin , `` What is the cause that the lattice of yonder kiosk ( kushk ) is not complete ? '' The defective part is soon after referred to , no less than four times , as `` the lattice of the kiosk '' ( sheriyyetu ' l kushk ) , thus showing that , in the writer 's mind , kushk , liwan and shubbak were synonymous terms for the common Arab projecting square-sided window , made of latticework , and I have therefore rendered the three words , when they occur in this sense , by our English `` oriel , '' to whose modern meaning ( a window that juts out , so as to form a small apartment ) , they exactly correspond", "context_len": 128}}}, "cult": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 42, "context": "The Master of Life , also called Suta-l\u00e4\u00edkati , `` resident in the sky , '' is not a pure abstraction , but has to be brought into connection with the sun-worship of all Americans , which again became associated with the cult of the fire-flame", "context_len": 46}}}, "monotheistic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "In the eyes of the missionaries and Christian settlers , the paramount importance and abstract character of the Master of Breath made him appear as the centre of an almost monotheistic religion ; but on closer investigation it will be found that the Creeks believed in many genii and mythic animals besides , two of which were the isti-p\u00e1pa and the snake , which furnished the snake horn as a war-talisman", "context_len": 71}}}, "genii": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 47, "context": "In the eyes of the missionaries and Christian settlers , the paramount importance and abstract character of the Master of Breath made him appear as the centre of an almost monotheistic religion ; but on closer investigation it will be found that the Creeks believed in many genii and mythic animals besides , two of which were the isti-p\u00e1pa and the snake , which furnished the snake horn as a war-talisman", "context_len": 71}}}, "ogres": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 39, "context": "It would be singular indeed , if the Creeks were the only Indians of America who believed solely in the Great Spirit and not also in a number of lesser conceptions of imagination , as dwarfs , giants , ogres , fairies , hobgoblins and earth-spirits", "context_len": 46}}}, "hobgoblins": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 43, "context": "It would be singular indeed , if the Creeks were the only Indians of America who believed solely in the Great Spirit and not also in a number of lesser conceptions of imagination , as dwarfs , giants , ogres , fairies , hobgoblins and earth-spirits", "context_len": 46}}}, "etymological": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "Many of these myths are etymological , as that of the Greeks , stating that they originated from stones thrown by Deucalion behind himself ( stone , and people ) ; that of Adam , being created from earth ; adam , in Hebrew , signifies person and mankind , adom , adum , fem", "context_len": 55}}}, "originators": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 78, "context": "The spectacle of the body , and the statement of the king , produced a scene of great and universal excitement in the assembly , and this excitement was raised to the highest pitch by the announcement which Alexander now made , that he had reason to believe that Philotas and his father Parmenio , officers who had enjoyed his highest favor , and in whom he had placed the most unbounded confidence , were the authors and originators of the whole design", "context_len": 83}}}, "amounts": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 13, "context": "Among the nations tracing their mythic origin to the earth , or what amounts to the same thing , to caves , deep holes , hills or mountains , are the Pomo of Northern California , who believe that their ancestors , the coyote-men , were created directly from a knoll of red earth , [ 135 ] still visible in their country ; the Nahua , whose seven tribes issued from Chicomoztoc or the `` Seven Caves", "context_len": 78}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "The interest has been accumulating till it amounts to nearer one hundred and seventy thousand dollars", "context_len": 16}}}, "imprint": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 36, "context": "Yet there was a wondrous beauty still lingering over them ; they seemed etherialised -- as if an angel 's smile had last stirred their lines , when the spirit went forth , and left its imprint of wonder , joy , and awe thereon ; and Alfgar instinctively turned from them to the blue depths of heaven above , where a few stars were visible , although dimmed by the moonlight ; and he seemed to trace his beloved Bertric 's passage to the realms of bliss", "context_len": 88}}}, "fabrication": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "Traditions on early migrations , which have originated in the people to which they refer and bear the imprint of genuineness , not that of a late fabrication by conjurers or mixed-bloods , usually contain indications of importance which are confirmed by arch\u00e6ologic and linguistic researches", "context_len": 46}}}, "founders": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` The Hellenic , especially Doric tradition of an immigration from Thrace and Macedonia through Epirus and Thessalia into Greece is confirmed by linguistic and historic facts , but the Roman legend concerning the descent of the founders of the `` Eternal City '' from Troy was acknowledged to be a pious fraud by the ancients themselves", "context_len": 57}}}, "ancients": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 21, "context": "This was not , however , so impious as it would at first view seem , for the gods whom the ancients worshiped were , in fact , only deifications of old heroes and kings who had lived in early times , and had acquired a reputation for supernatural powers by the fame of their exploits , exaggerated in descending by tradition in superstitious times", "context_len": 65}}}, "longitudinal": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "In many instances , though by no means in all , the migrations are seen to follow the direction of the longitudinal axis of the continent", "context_len": 26}}}, "axis": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "In many instances , though by no means in all , the migrations are seen to follow the direction of the longitudinal axis of the continent", "context_len": 26}}}, "agencies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "[ 138 ] This would be a thorough misapprehension of the various agencies which are at work in producing folk-lore", "context_len": 20}}}, "coincides": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "Every tribe of a people or nation has its own migration myth or legend , which in some points coincides , in others conflicts with those of the neighboring septs", "context_len": 30}}}, "reproduction": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 7, "context": "You can read for yourself from the reproduction of the last page of his diary , written on the 14th December , 1884 , his own estimate of the length of time he could hold out ; and , though he managed to keep back the enemy for another month , yet on the 26th January , 1885 , whilst yet Sir Charles Wilson and the British troops were fighting their way up the river Nile to his relief , Khartoum fell", "context_len": 82}}}, "premise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "To the reproduction and critical examination of the different Creek migration legends transmitted to us we premise a short chapter on the mythic and legendary tales referring to the migrations of the other Maskoki nations", "context_len": 35}}}, "rationalism": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "The narrative of the interpreter , who seems to have been somewhat imbued with the spirit of rationalism , continues as follows :", "context_len": 23}}}, "encampment": {"freq": 16, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 16, "word_index": 60, "context": "Vaughan did not take quite such a serious view of the episode , he thought the gipsies were certainly undesirable additions to the countryside , and sent a hint to the local police , with the result that , when Peggy and Bobby screwed up their courage , and dared each other to go and have another peep at the encampment , they found the tent struck and the wanderers flown , with nothing to mark their passing visit but a few dirty pieces of paper and the ashes of the wood fire , though loud complaints from neighbouring farmers as to missing ducks and geese , and traces of snares found by the keepers in the preserves , showed that they had not gone away empty-handed", "context_len": 127}}}, "perpendicularly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 6, "context": "Besides its enormous walls , rising perpendicularly out of the water , it was defended by ships well armed and manned", "context_len": 21}}}, "citadel": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 13, "context": "The tale contains a similar motive as that of the foundation of the citadel at Thebes by Kadmus , who was ordered by an oracle to follow a wandering heifer until it would settle in the grass , and then to found a city on the spot", "context_len": 47}}}, "oracle": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 24, "context": "The tale contains a similar motive as that of the foundation of the citadel at Thebes by Kadmus , who was ordered by an oracle to follow a wandering heifer until it would settle in the grass , and then to found a city on the spot", "context_len": 47}}}, "heifer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 29, "context": "The tale contains a similar motive as that of the foundation of the citadel at Thebes by Kadmus , who was ordered by an oracle to follow a wandering heifer until it would settle in the grass , and then to found a city on the spot", "context_len": 47}}}, "watercourse": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` In traveling from the West to the Promised Land in the East , they have no recollection of crossing any large watercourse except the Mississippi river ; they had to fight their way through enemies on all sides , but can not now remember the names of them", "context_len": 49}}}, "headings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "The migration legends now current among the Alibamu and the Hitchiti are but short in form and have been referred to under the respective headings", "context_len": 25}}}, "eastwardly": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 5, "context": "After this they spread out eastwardly to Otch\u00edsi-h\u00e1tchi or Okmulgi river , to Okoni river , to Og\u012btchi or How-ge-chuh river , to Ch\u00edska t\u00e1lofa h\u00e1tchi or Savannah river , called sometimes Sawan\u00f3gi", "context_len": 33}}}, "straggled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Some of the Chicasa straggled off and settled near Augusta , from whence they returned and settled near Kasi ta , and thence rejoined their own people", "context_len": 27}}}, "subduing": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 55, "context": "The spreading out of the people from the Tallapoosa river to the Chatahutchi and from there to the Savannah must have involved a warfare , struggling , migration and settling down of several centuries , for the advance of the Maskoki proper in this direction was tantamount to the formation of the Maskoki confederacy by subduing or incorporating the tribes standing in their way , and to the still more lengthy process of settling among them", "context_len": 76}}}, "succored": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "At that epoch the Moskoquis formed a powerful separate republic in the northwest of Mexico ; they succored him with a numerous body of warriors , but were frightfully decimated by the Spaniards , who dismembered Montezuma 's domain , and almost completely depopulated it", "context_len": 45}}}, "dismembered": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "At that epoch the Moskoquis formed a powerful separate republic in the northwest of Mexico ; they succored him with a numerous body of warriors , but were frightfully decimated by the Spaniards , who dismembered Montezuma 's domain , and almost completely depopulated it", "context_len": 45}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "The Roman power fell at last , and when the English pirates , our own ancestors , like the Danes of our story , attacked the dismembered provinces of the empire , its wealth and position on the coast made it an early object of attack -- happy those who fled early", "context_len": 52}}}, "depopulated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 24, "context": "Alexander marched his troops rapidly off the ground , leaving , as the direct result of the battle , a wide extent of country depopulated and desolate , with this vast mass of putrefaction and pestilence reigning in awful silence and solitude in the midst of it", "context_len": 47}}}, "aquatic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Innumerable flocks of aquatic and other birds congregated around the salt ponds of the prairie and on the waters of Red River", "context_len": 22}}}, "calumets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "While the young people of both sexes were occupied at the agricultural work , the old ones were smoking their calumets", "context_len": 21}}}, "expatriate": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "But soon their destinies took a downward turn , and forced them to expatriate themselves for a second time", "context_len": 19}}}, "hostiles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "A number of their men were killed by the Albamo or Alibamu , and the young men sent after them were unable to meet the hostiles and to chastise them", "context_len": 30}}}, "vanguard": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "On their march the vanguard was formed by the young braves , the rear-guard by the old men , and the non-combatants were placed in the centre", "context_len": 27}}}, "belligerent": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "The two belligerent tribes now crossed Mississippi river , and the Alibamu , having an advance of eight days over their pursuers , fled before them into the interior parts to the east", "context_len": 33}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Alden read this belligerent message and smiled", "context_len": 7}}}, "embittered": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "This deed so embittered the injured tribe , that their m\u00edkos resolved to dispossess the enemy of their territory for the third time", "context_len": 23}}}, "dispossess": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "They thought it might , perhaps , be somewhat difficult to dispossess such a visitor after he should once get installed in their castles and palaces", "context_len": 26}}}, "integral": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "The Alibamu accepted the offer , settled on the land , built a town on it , called Coussehat\u00e9 , and since then form an integral part of the Moskoqui people , which now assumed the name of Creeks", "context_len": 39}}}, "assemblies": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "He and Fred had found two Mitsubishi bombers with engines apparently unhurt and wings not too badly damaged , though the tail assemblies , fuselages and undercarriages were in sad shape", "context_len": 31}}}, "rivulet": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Nonesuch had raced with Frankie to the very edge of this little rivulet , and then stood still for a moment as if considering what he would do next , when a hand , holding a long , thorny switch , was suddenly put forth from the clump of bushes , and Nonesuch received a stinging blow across his haunches", "context_len": 60}}}, "petite": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Shortly after this event , the small tribe of the Yuchi ( la petite nation des Udgis ) , partly dismembered by the British , also fled to the Creek towns and were given a territory on Chatahutchi river", "context_len": 39}}}, "disastrously": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 67, "context": "`` It was a work of no ordinary skill and difficulty to save so many persons in the few moments available for the purpose ; and , when it is mentioned that some of them were very old and crippled , it is no exaggeration to say that it would be impossible to praise too highly Ford 's conduct on this occasion , which has resulted so disastrously to himself", "context_len": 70}}}, "warded": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 60, "context": "`` When [ 409 ] the Sultan heard his daughter 's story , he was sore concerned and his eyes brimmed with tears ; then , sheathing the sword and coming up to her , he kissed her and said to her , `` O my daughter , why didst thou not tell me yesterday , so I might have warded off from thee the torment and affright which have befallen thee this night ? But no matter ; arise and put away from thee this thought , and to-night I will set over thee those who shall guard thee , so there shall not again befall thee that which befell yesternight", "context_len": 112}}}, "acceding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "A second attack of theirs was warded off by the tribe , by acceding to the peace arrangements proposed by the French", "context_len": 22}}}, "evacuated": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "After the loss of the Canadian provinces , Fort Toulouse was evacuated by the French", "context_len": 15}}}, "localizing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "Unfortunately the statement concerning the immigration of the Cheroki is without any details , and therefore is of no avail in localizing the Cheroki towns or colonies within the Creek territory ( p", "context_len": 33}}}, "bayou": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "They crossed the territories held by the Upper Cha'hta , passed through Mobile , the confluence of Iberville bayou with Mississippi river , St", "context_len": 24}}}, "cavities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "The noise of a few gun-shots brought out of these spacious cavities a large number of bisons , wild oxen and wild horses , which ran , frightened as they were by the unusual explosions , head over heels , over precipices of more than eighty feet of perpendicular height into the slimy waters of Red river", "context_len": 57}}}, "bisons": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "The noise of a few gun-shots brought out of these spacious cavities a large number of bisons , wild oxen and wild horses , which ran , frightened as they were by the unusual explosions , head over heels , over precipices of more than eighty feet of perpendicular height into the slimy waters of Red river", "context_len": 57}}}, "grottoes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "The party concluded to pass the inclement season in these grottoes , which they had reached about Christmas time", "context_len": 19}}}, "oracular": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 67, "context": "Beyond the fact that a lady in white was supposed to haunt both the ruins and the oak-wood , weeping and wringing her hands in orthodox ghostly fashion , the children had not been able to learn much of the story , for there were so many and divergent accounts of it , all told with uncertainty as to names and dates , and in that very oracular -- not to say muddled -- style sometimes indulged in by rustic historians", "context_len": 81}}}, "chieftaincies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 26, "context": "[ 148 ] The number four is conspicuous here as well as in the legend related by Tchikilli ; we have four hayoy\u00e1lgi , four principal chieftaincies , four years of warfare , etc", "context_len": 34}}}, "chronological": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The facts subsequently related are given without such chronological dates as we find with the previous ones , but the narrator evidently tried to condense into the space of a few years what it took generations to accomplish", "context_len": 38}}}, "condense": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The facts subsequently related are given without such chronological dates as we find with the previous ones , but the narrator evidently tried to condense into the space of a few years what it took generations to accomplish", "context_len": 38}}}, "incorporating": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "The spreading out of the people from the Tallapoosa river to the Chatahutchi and from there to the Savannah must have involved a warfare , struggling , migration and settling down of several centuries , for the advance of the Maskoki proper in this direction was tantamount to the formation of the Maskoki confederacy by subduing or incorporating the tribes standing in their way , and to the still more lengthy process of settling among them", "context_len": 76}}}, "fabrications": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "Milfort 's `` History of the Moskoquis , '' as given above in an extract , is a singular mixture of recent fabrications and distortions of real historic events , with some points traceable to genuine aboriginal folklore", "context_len": 38}}}, "distortions": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "Milfort 's `` History of the Moskoquis , '' as given above in an extract , is a singular mixture of recent fabrications and distortions of real historic events , with some points traceable to genuine aboriginal folklore", "context_len": 38}}}, "invader": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 23, "context": "The knots and boles of the trunks framed themselves into grimacing faces that seemed to be chuckling over the fate of the young invader who had come unbidden into their realm", "context_len": 31}}}, "abodes": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "The excitement of driving to the station in the exhilarating morning air , past houses which , like her uncle 's , seemed the abodes of luxurious ease", "context_len": 28}}}, "caverns": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "A part of the Cheroki anciently dwelt in caves ; and concerning the caverns from which the Creeks claim to have issued , James Adair gives the following interesting disclosure : `` It is worthy of notice , that the Muskohgeh cave , out of which one of their politicians persuaded them their ancestors formerly ascended to their present terrestrial abode , lies in the Nanne Hamgeh old town , inhabited by the Mississippi-Nachee Indians , [ 150 ] which is one of the most western parts of their old-inhabited country", "context_len": 91}}}, "disclosure": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 29, "context": "A part of the Cheroki anciently dwelt in caves ; and concerning the caverns from which the Creeks claim to have issued , James Adair gives the following interesting disclosure : `` It is worthy of notice , that the Muskohgeh cave , out of which one of their politicians persuaded them their ancestors formerly ascended to their present terrestrial abode , lies in the Nanne Hamgeh old town , inhabited by the Mississippi-Nachee Indians , [ 150 ] which is one of the most western parts of their old-inhabited country", "context_len": 91}}}, "terrestrial": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 59, "context": "A part of the Cheroki anciently dwelt in caves ; and concerning the caverns from which the Creeks claim to have issued , James Adair gives the following interesting disclosure : `` It is worthy of notice , that the Muskohgeh cave , out of which one of their politicians persuaded them their ancestors formerly ascended to their present terrestrial abode , lies in the Nanne Hamgeh old town , inhabited by the Mississippi-Nachee Indians , [ 150 ] which is one of the most western parts of their old-inhabited country", "context_len": 91}}}, "engrafted": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` The idea that their forefathers issued from caves was so deeply engrafted in the minds of these Indians , that some of them took any conspicuous cave or any country rich in caves to be the primordial habitat of their race", "context_len": 42}}}, "addicted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "As they were addicted to heliolatry , it may be suggested that their conjurers advised them to travel , for luck , to the east only , because the east was the rising place of the sun , their protector and benefactor", "context_len": 42}}}, "heliolatry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "As they were addicted to heliolatry , it may be suggested that their conjurers advised them to travel , for luck , to the east only , because the east was the rising place of the sun , their protector and benefactor", "context_len": 42}}}, "deduced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 21, "context": "It may be observed here , that from the moving of the heavenly bodies from east to west the Pani Indians deduced the superstition that they should never move directly east in their travels", "context_len": 34}}}, "acknowledgments": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "It contained the Indians ' grateful acknowledgments for the honors and civilities paid to Tomochichi , etc", "context_len": 17}}}, "pamphlets": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 62, "context": "The members seemed to take copious notes in pocket-books , and asked occasional questions , one literary-looking lady , in spectacles and a large black hat with nodding feathers , being particularly insistent , and volunteering so much information that she threatened to usurp the place of the lecturer , and had to be gently suppressed , while an old gentleman distributed pamphlets broadcast , declaring he had had them specially printed for the occasion", "context_len": 75}}}, "commissary": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "von Reck was the commissary of those German-Protestant emigrants whom religious persecution had expelled from Salzburg , in Styria , their native city", "context_len": 23}}}, "thunders": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "It thunders to this day ; and men are very much afraid of it", "context_len": 14}, "NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "The water thunders in the ears , and a feeling of helplessness and awe sometimes comes over the best of swimmers", "context_len": 21}}}, "tomahawk": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 11, "context": "The Indians gave chase , and one of them threw a tomahawk , and struck it into the side of his neck ; he kept on the horse just long enough to reach home , and fell on the door-step ; and for all the horse run , the Indians were at the door almost as soon as he", "context_len": 59}}}, "digged": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "They digged a pit and stretched over it a net made of hickory-bark", "context_len": 13}}}, "pinewood": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "The lion rushed at it , and fell in the pit , over which they threw the net , and killed him with blazing pinewood", "context_len": 25}}}, "cranes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "`` 'They left this place , and came to a creek , called Wattoolahawka hutche , Whooping-creek , so called from the whooping of cranes , a great many being there", "context_len": 31}}}, "italicized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Words surrounded by are italicized", "context_len": 5}}}, "spellings": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "Obvious printer 's errors have been repaired , other inconsistent spellings have been kept , including inconsistent use of diacritical mark ( e", "context_len": 23}}}, "diacritical": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 78, "context": "`` The Sultan `` goes round in the kiosk '' and seeing `` the casement ( shubbak ) , which Alaeddin had purposely left defective , without completion , '' said to the Vizier , `` Knowest thou the reason ( or cause ) of the lack of completion of this casement and its lattices ? '' ( shearihi , or quaere , `` [ this ] lattice , '' the copyist having probably omitted by mistake the diacritical points over the final ha )", "context_len": 85}}}, "douglas": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "DOUGLAS", "context_len": 1}}}, "maltese": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "She had brought such a pretty one from the store one day , a real maltese with black whiskers , but Bridget said she could n't have a cat forever round under her feet and made her take it back", "context_len": 40}}}, "banana": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 15, "context": "`` It was pretty hard work to take up that first one of giving the banana to Dolly ; and , Maggie , do you know I did such a very naughty thing as 'most to wish He did not give me the chance I had asked for : but , after that , all the rest were very easy to take up , and I did not find it at all hard to forgive Dolly every thing she had done", "context_len": 81}}}, "brogans": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "I have a little foot though , only it do n't look so in these big brogans", "context_len": 17}}}, "weeny": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "I do think if the glass slipper was n't too teeny weeny I could wear it", "context_len": 16}}}, "toted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 14, "context": "I am worth a great deal of money , but why I had been toted from down South to New York , and from New York to a Canadian stock farm and from the stock farm away up here to this out-of-the-way place in the woods was a problem to me", "context_len": 51}}}, "godmothers": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "I saw so little of my own mother and she was always tired , fairy godmothers are different", "context_len": 18}}}, "grinder": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "I saw you dancing with an organ grinder", "context_len": 8}}}, "shimmery": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "There was a shimmery pink silk frock with lace and ribbons and the daintiest pink kid slippers with diamond buckles and pink silk stockings with lovely clocks", "context_len": 27}}}, "daintiest": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 35, "context": "Well , she was all right then , and he carried her off ; but first she walked round and made her manners to every man there as polite as you please , looking the daintiest little lady that ever walked on two feet ; and when I put her into the carriage , did n't she thank me for letting her into the station , and being kind to her , as if it was a favor I 'd been doing , and not my duty ; and as if a man could help it that once looked at her", "context_len": 101}}}, "chandelier": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "And a great circular room , down stairs lighted by a magnificent chandelier whose prisms seemed in constant motion and rayed off every imaginable color with a faint musical sound", "context_len": 30}}}, "prisms": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 14, "context": "And a great circular room , down stairs lighted by a magnificent chandelier whose prisms seemed in constant motion and rayed off every imaginable color with a faint musical sound", "context_len": 30}}}, "informally": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "Some were dancing about informally , some talking and laughing", "context_len": 10}}}, "velvets": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "There were the most elegantly attired boys in silks and velvets made in all pretty fashions", "context_len": 16}}}, "petticoats": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 19, "context": "Dad put an axe into my hand , and taught me to chop kindling before I was out of petticoats , and when we went up the Rockies shooting grizzlies we built log-cabins , and I can tell you there was no carpenter to fetch there nearer than a hundred miles , so I guess I ought to know how to handle a saw and fix up a bit of lumber", "context_len": 71}}}, "encore": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 10, "context": "'Say them `` Little Orphaned Annie '' instead of an encore , ' whispered Lilian to Peggy , as the stamping still went on , and nobody seemed inclined to go", "context_len": 31}}}, "dancer": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "And you 're such a lovely dancer", "context_len": 7}}}, "candied": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "There were mounds of fruit and flowers , nuts from all over the world , piles of cake , candied fruit , ices made in all kinds of shape", "context_len": 29}}}, "ices": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 57, "context": "The snowy table with its silver dishes and graceful centre-piece of hot-house blooms , the crystal sparkling in the rosy glow cast by silken-shaded , massively carved lamps , the perfect , noiseless serving , and the bright conversation which flowed freely , little hindered by the different courses of soup and fish , and game and ices -- conversation about things that were happening in the world which seemed to be growing larger every minute , apt allusions by Mr Davis , lively sallies by Belle , and quotations by Russell from authors who seemed to be household friends , so highly were they held in reverence", "context_len": 108}}}, "tomorrows": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "You are not thinking of all the tomorrows", "context_len": 8}}}, "averagely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Jack Borden , the junior partner in a fairly successful law firm , his wife an averagely nice , sensible body , Miss Florence , her husband 's sister , a bright girl of three and twenty , whose lover was in South America on a five years ' contract , with one year yet to serve", "context_len": 57}}}, "nursemaid": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 9, "context": "After the twins were born they tried a grown nursemaid who bored them by sitting around when she was upstairs and making many excuses to get down to the kitchen , where she disputed with Bridget who declared one or the other of them must go , and they simply could not give up Bridget", "context_len": 55}}}, "grandnephew": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 44, "context": "Aunt Hetty Vanderveer who was queer and going on to eighty , who could n't live with a relative for they always wanted to borrow her money , got tangled up in a house on which she had a mortgage , and called her grandnephew , Mr", "context_len": 47}}}, "charities": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "One day a friend came in for a subscription to some of her charities and heard the appeal", "context_len": 18}}}, "trouncing": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 25, "context": "Only if it was n't for Jack ! He pinched her sometimes , and once he kicked her but his mother gave him a good trouncing", "context_len": 26}}}, "spools": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 2, "context": "`` Three spools of silk , two twist , black cotton number 60 , white cotton , 60 , 70 and 80", "context_len": 22}}}, "capsized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Go it sonny , '' cried a man laughing , but she gained on him and took him by the arm with a jerk that nearly capsized them both", "context_len": 30}}}, "aisles": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 8, "context": "Young and old , they crowded the narrow aisles , even up to the altar space , where a row of tapers burned in the solemn gloom", "context_len": 27}}}, "walker": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` What is all this row ? '' asked the floor walker", "context_len": 12}}}, "insolently": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` You 'd better shut up , Dewey , '' said Mosely , insolently ; `` you 're a dead duck , and you 're only gettin ' this foolish heathen into trouble", "context_len": 33}}}, "playthings": {"freq": 11, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 48, "context": "`` I 'd have to be as fat as Auntie Belle to do that , '' she laughed , `` and now papa I want Marilla to tell you about a queer Home she was put in where they would n't let the little girls have dolls nor playthings , and they made dollies out of clothes pins and had to hide them", "context_len": 63}}}, "idealized": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "`` We 've indulged him too much , and we have idealized childhood too much ; we 've laughed at his smart tricks and his saucy replies , and tried high moral suasion , but we must turn over a new leaf", "context_len": 42}}}, "suasion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` We 've indulged him too much , and we have idealized childhood too much ; we 've laughed at his smart tricks and his saucy replies , and tried high moral suasion , but we must turn over a new leaf", "context_len": 42}}}, "mortifies": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 1, "context": "That mortifies me awfully", "context_len": 4}}}, "thongs": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Borden brought home something in a paper bag and Jack begged the bag `` to bust , '' watching his father as he shook out a leather strap cut in thongs and said --", "context_len": 34}}}, "expedients": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 19, "context": "`` Ca n't you give me a note to her ? '' asked Tom , who was fertile in expedients where young ladies were concerned", "context_len": 25}}}, "biff": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "He soon learned what a `` punch below the belt '' meant , and a `` biff in the eye '' and several other fighting terms", "context_len": 26}}}, "willows": {"freq": 12, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 11, "word_index": 36, "context": "But now she took the babies out to the big park where she could sit and watch the merry children at play and the beds of flowers coming out , and there were the funny pussy willows and the long tails of yellow forsythia and some squirrels running around , and birds calling to each other", "context_len": 56}, "VERB_PresT": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 17, "context": "He was still wandering and speculating , when he caught the gleam of water through the sparse willows that lined the trail", "context_len": 22}}}, "sundays": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Sundays were pretty good ; Mr", "context_len": 6}}}, "unanswerable": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "'Why should you go ? ' Pauline said , in her cheery , unanswerable way , when they spoke of leaving : 'it does us good to have you , and it does you good to be here , ' and Muriel and her mother were content", "context_len": 47}}}, "aromatic": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 36, "context": "At the foot of the throne was the coffin , made , it is said , of solid gold , and containing , besides the body , a large quantity of the most costly spices and aromatic perfumes , which filled the air with their odor", "context_len": 46}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Bobby could see her quite plainly in the fire light , and could catch the curious aromatic smell which rose up from the smouldering wood", "context_len": 25}}}, "ammonia": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 17, "context": "They rubbed him and put him in some dry clothes and gave him a dose of aromatic ammonia to steady his nerves , and then some supper", "context_len": 27}}}, "crib": {"freq": 14, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 14, "word_index": 49, "context": "Cassowary , who was almost as much at home under the water as on it , was plunging about like a seal , and just as I arrived had brought Dallas to the surface , and holding him by the collar with one hand and hanging on to the crib with the other she gravely watched Champ and Big Chief giving first aid to the drowning by putting my poor little master on his face and pressing the water out of his lungs", "context_len": 83}}}, "naively": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 17, "context": "I tried to be good for I did n't like to be punished , '' she said naively", "context_len": 18}}}, "summering": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` What a streak of good fortune ! Now we really do own the house free and clear , I thought our summering would be quite moderate but it was n't", "context_len": 31}}}, "hammocks": {"freq": 15, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 15, "word_index": 23, "context": "The handsome house in its brilliant setting of lawns and trees , the wide verandah with its crimson Mount Washington rockers , luxurious hammocks , and low table covered with freshly-cut magazines , the pleasant-faced man who was her nearest of kin , and his graceful wife in a tea-gown of soft summer silk with rich lace about her throat and wrists , her cousins in their dainty muslins , and Russell in his fresh summer suit", "context_len": 77}}}, "rambled": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Then they ran off home and he rambled on and on until he came to a street up a few steps", "context_len": 21}}}, "kegs": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "That such a cave existed the children knew well , for Joe had once been inside , and had told them wonderful stories of smugglers in bygone days , and of kegs of brandy , and bales of silk and lace , and boxes of tea and tobacco , which were still supposed to be hidden in its depths , only waiting for some enterprising spirit to discover their hiding-place and bring them to light again", "context_len": 76}}}, "crossroads": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` I turn off at the crossroads , ye kin ride that fur", "context_len": 13}}}, "affronted": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "They both laughed and Jack felt rather affronted", "context_len": 8}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "He was still keeping up the pursuit and importuning the affronted official as both were lost to view behind a track of freights", "context_len": 23}}}, "clouding": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Then it was clouding up and night was coming on", "context_len": 10}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "There seemed a sudden dreariness in the world like a bright day clouding over", "context_len": 14}}}, "bub": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "`` What 's the matter bub ? '' asked a kindly voice", "context_len": 12}}}, "sobbingly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "`` Oh , I ca n't tell all the places , '' sobbingly", "context_len": 13}}}, "entrances": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 32, "context": "`` Come on , boys ! '' cried Bart , when he came in sight of the tents , and saw Ned and the others sweeping away the snow from the front entrances", "context_len": 33}}}, "dinnertime": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 4, "context": "And when it came dinnertime with no Jack , and the dreadful things that one could conjure up -- being run over , being kidnapped -- for he was such a pretty little fellow ! Mr", "context_len": 36}}}, "conjure": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 31, "context": "At this wonder took her ; [ 384 ] he went to an oilman 's shop there open and buying oil of him , said to him , `` [ I conjure thee ] by thy life , O uncle , tell me what is toward to-day in this city , that the folk are making this decoration and the markets [ are shut ] and the houses all adorned and the troops drawn up in state ? '' Quoth he , `` O woman , methinketh thou art a stranger and art not of this city", "context_len": 97}}}, "kidnapped": {"freq": 9, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 9, "word_index": 37, "context": "Bomba accompanied them for some distance , and then took leave of them , promising to pick up the trail at once and co\u00f6perate with them in every way possible in the rescue of their chief and kidnapped families , as well as of Casson and Pipina", "context_len": 47}}}, "hysteric": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 24, "context": "And now they all hugged and kissed him ; and what with the fatigue , the fright and all , Jack really had an hysteric", "context_len": 25}}}, "redound": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 67, "context": "She could hardly believe it , hardly believe that in spite of her enforced absence from school , in spite of her inability to hand in her compositions for so many weeks , she had been the one to receive this much coveted opportunity , and that she was not only free to bestow it upon her own little country-woman , but that her own credit would redound to that of Miss Ashton", "context_len": 73}}}, "cop": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 31, "context": "`` We walked some , then another man rode us a little way , and the boys went off and I got lost more and more and could n't find a cop , and asked every so many people , and a woman gave me a bun and a cake , and then a man took me across the park and told me to go straight along", "context_len": 67}}}, "scoldings": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 43, "context": "Aunt Helen fondly imagined herself to be a great disciplinarian , but her own lively youth was still such a recent remembrance that her eyes were wont to twinkle and the corners of her mouth to twitch in the middle of her severest scoldings , and the children always had a feeling that so long as they did not do anything rude or wrong , or run into any very imminent danger , their escapades were secretly condoned by their aunt , who admired pluck and spirit , however much she might feel it incumbent upon her to lecture them", "context_len": 100}}}, "jounces": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": null, "context": null, "context_len": 0}}}, "mama": {"freq": 10, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 10, "word_index": 3, "context": "`` Oh , mama , I never will do it again , never ! '' Jack hung round his mother 's neck and cried and she cried with him , thinking of her tumult of agony last night", "context_len": 38}}}, "spasms": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` Well '' -- Jack had spasms of direct truth tellings now and then , like most children", "context_len": 18}}}, "dusted": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 44, "context": "As it was , during her husband 's lifetime she thought of no one but him , and since his death her best thoughts were devoted to his memory : to keep flowers always on his grave ; to see that his portrait was dusted day after day , and that flowers were put under it ; to kneel there and utter prayers that he and she might be reunited in a better world , absorbed her strongest thoughts", "context_len": 79}}}, "auto": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 17, "context": "`` Stevie lived quite a distance below and had brought no end of playthings , had an auto wagon that two could sit in , though only one could be chauffeur", "context_len": 31}}}, "kindergarten": {"freq": 8, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 103, "context": "she merely said , `` I am glad you have come to your senses , '' and then she got up and began to prepare for lunch ; so that Irene , feeling like a beaten child , and yet with a sense of happiness which she had never experienced before in the whole course of her life , went off to her own room , smoothed out her tangled hair , tidied her dress , and came down to lunch also , looking quite like a little ordinary Christian child -- the sort of child who might have been first to a kindergarten and then at a good school -- not the wild , obnoxious , terrible little creature whom every servant and every governess alike dreaded", "context_len": 128}, "VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "He was n't very good , but after awhile he went to kindergarten", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 11, "context": "Miss Pope , will you kindly take Margaret Vaughan into the kindergarten classroom , where she will wait until I come to her ? Each form may now leave the room in turn", "context_len": 33}}}, "injunctions": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 28, "context": "Lilian , in a print dress , apron , and sun-bonnet , made a charming little village mother , and trotted off with her basket , leaving many injunctions for 'John ' to follow", "context_len": 34}}}, "richards": {"freq": 108, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 107, "word_index": 0, "context": "Richards pulled down the covering of the cradle , turned over the pillow , looked under the table , peeped into the sugar-bowl , pepper-pot , and stove , and at last pretended to be much astonished to discover the baby upon its mother 's lap , after which the hunt was carried on in search of a place big enough to kiss", "context_len": 63}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "RICHARDS", "context_len": 1}}}, "responsive": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 21, "context": "It was bid up pretty high , and the crowd urged him to disclose his treasure , but Grimm was not responsive to any mutual human sentiment and sat down with the package in his lap", "context_len": 36}}}, "nightgown": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Now I 'll find a nice nightgown , and we 'll see what the doctor says", "context_len": 16}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "After the babies came in from their outing they were washed , undressed , and a nice warm wrapper put over their nightgown , and then fed", "context_len": 27}}}, "blueish": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "They were a sort of blueish gray , but now very heavy and dull", "context_len": 14}}}, "alcove": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 51, "context": "Lewis had searched for and taken the money with the object of playing an annoying trick upon Seabrooke and Percy , but proposing , after giving both `` a good fright , '' to put it back where he had found it , or in some other place in Seabrooke 's alcove where he might be supposed to have mislaid it", "context_len": 61}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "In its alcove the white bed was partly hidden by porti\u00e8res", "context_len": 11}}}, "prostration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "And a case of heat prostration", "context_len": 6}}}, "underfeeding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Overwork and underfeeding I think , and the heat", "context_len": 9}}}, "comatose": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "`` It is a kind of comatose state", "context_len": 8}, "UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Then he ran back to Casson , who was in a half comatose condition , pulled him to his feet and , half dragging , half lifting him , staggered through the doorway and laid his burden under the trees", "context_len": 40}}}, "deformed": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 49, "context": "He noted their glorious appearance in the water , their bodies of a bright green , mixed with a silver colour , and their tails of a shining golden yellow , and wondered at the vulgar error of the painters , who always represent this fish monstrously crooked and deformed", "context_len": 50}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Before us lay the fated village , one mass of deformed and blackened ruins , from which the dark smoke ceaselessly arose , and made the air painful to breathe", "context_len": 30}}}, "wafts": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "The sun was going down and some stray wafts of wind wandered along , which made the heat rather more endurable", "context_len": 21}}}, "bruise": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "`` Jane , '' she said as she walked into the room , `` did you notice any bruise on the child 's head while you were bathing her", "context_len": 29}}}, "trolleys": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 27, "context": "`` Why there are some beautiful long streets and stores and churches and a park and rows of houses built together like this , and schools and trolleys -- ''", "context_len": 30}}}, "discoloration": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 19, "context": "They looked over Marilla 's head and found one spot above the ear that had a small bit of discoloration , but it was not in a dangerous place", "context_len": 29}}}, "tweak": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 49, "context": "No one could guess the strain of talking so incessantly to amuse the babies , of reading to Jack , of having eyes all over to see that he did not torment the little ones , push their playthings out of the way , give them sly pinches or tweak their hair", "context_len": 52}}}, "lulled": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 13, "context": "Finally the mournful sweep of the wind and the fitful beating of rain lulled his senses , and he slept slept to dream of Hayden leering triumphantly upon him", "context_len": 29}}}, "screech": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 24, "context": "She just worries your wife 's life out ; and every time she goes lumberin ' over the floor , Mary is ready to screech with the jar", "context_len": 28}, "NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 5, "context": "The warrior emitted a rasping screech , flung up his arms and dived head foremost , so close to the Conestoga that he slid like a sleigh over the ice beyond the hind wheels and lay motionless on his face", "context_len": 40}}}, "nursemaids": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "Such children are not strong enough for nursemaids", "context_len": 8}}}, "suffused": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 5, "word_index": 30, "context": "I do not believe I am a real missionary , and I have a theory -- it may not be a very noble one , '' and a soft color suffused her fine face , `` that people who bring children into the world ought to be made to feel the responsibility of them and not shift them on society at large , trusting Providence to take care of them", "context_len": 70}}}, "forgives": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresT": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 2, "context": "'She always forgives us , however naughty we are ; and she told me once -- the night she was crying over the old letters in her writing-desk -- that if you quarrelled with anyone it was better to make it up at once , and not let it go on for ever", "context_len": 53}}}, "conduce": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 5, "context": "It does n't near always conduce to morality", "context_len": 8}}}, "tennis": {"freq": 13, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 13, "word_index": 26, "context": "Tea , good as it was , came to an end at last , and the children went out into the grounds , some to play tennis , some croquet , and some to wander away , two and two , each talking , as girls will , of their hopes and fears and ambitions", "context_len": 55}}}, "croquet": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 29, "context": "Tea , good as it was , came to an end at last , and the children went out into the grounds , some to play tennis , some croquet , and some to wander away , two and two , each talking , as girls will , of their hopes and fears and ambitions", "context_len": 55}}}, "warbles": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Such long sweet warbles , such a merry staccato with little pauses , as if he asked -- '' Now , what do you think of that ? '' and the child laughed with a sense of glee", "context_len": 38}}}, "staccato": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "Such long sweet warbles , such a merry staccato with little pauses , as if he asked -- '' Now , what do you think of that ? '' and the child laughed with a sense of glee", "context_len": 38}}}, "wholesomely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Why did you think you could never walk again ? '' Jane laughed wholesomely", "context_len": 15}}}, "poached": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 11, "context": "'Pheasants , I should think , and rabbits that they have poached , ' whispered Bobby", "context_len": 16}}}, "humorously": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 37, "context": "`` Gave my thumb a fierce wrench ! It hurts like blazes ! Why did n't you tell me I was going to fall , and I 'd have stayed in to-night ? '' he asked half humorously", "context_len": 38}}}, "persuasively": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "Then he seated himself on the couch at her feet and smiled so persuasively that she really could n't resist", "context_len": 20}}}, "overdrawn": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Surely the child must be a genius , she made the things so real and not overdrawn", "context_len": 17}}}, "irradiated": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "Then she drew a long breath and a lovely smile irradiated her eyes", "context_len": 13}, "UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 30, "context": "Walker , Ben \u2019 s seatmate , who had once felt it a sore affliction to be placed beside him , now hovered near , seeking to enjoy a little irradiated glory", "context_len": 32}}}, "joyousness": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 3, "context": "Then the unalterable joyousness of his glance reassured her , and she rushed forward crying , `` Oh , it 's you ! It 's you ! ''", "context_len": 28}}}, "unawakened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 7, "context": "What possibilities were lying in her soul unawakened", "context_len": 8}}}, "upbraiding": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "`` Oh , you did not scold her -- ? '' in a half upbraiding tone", "context_len": 16}}}, "flit": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 20, "context": "Winton , watching from his position at the side of the field , permitted a crinkle of a smile to flit across his face , even though he realized that the splendid and surprising defense had been accomplished , almost unaided , by two players", "context_len": 45}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 22, "context": "`` Lo ! in that house of misery A lady with a lamp I see Pass through the glimmering gloom , And flit from room to room", "context_len": 27}}}, "foundry": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "His father is going to put him in a foundry", "context_len": 10}}}, "coarsened": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 9, "context": "Think of hands fitted for the nicest surgery being coarsened by contact with rough iron and hard tools", "context_len": 18}}}, "palely": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 15, "context": "The little face was framed in with rings of shining hair , the lips were palely pink and parted with a half smile , the skin still showed blue veins", "context_len": 30}}}, "pudgy": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 10, "context": "The babies were much thinner and their faces not so pudgy , but Marilla thought they still resembled the Campbell soup little girl and laughed in spite of her own hurts", "context_len": 31}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 18, "context": "The name called back his wolf adventure , then he roared with laughter , for the freckled-faced fat pudgy little boy known as Sojer was doing a goose-step toward the flower beds", "context_len": 32}}}, "bannister": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 28, "context": "And there was Jane to wait upon them , and she seemed so pleased to have Marilla down stairs though the little girl had held tight to the bannister , lest she should lose her balance and fall", "context_len": 38}}}, "sedgy": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 12, "context": "The pictures were of woods and lakes , or a bit of sedgy river", "context_len": 14}}}, "asparagus": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 23, "context": "I will work the ship for another week or so , and there will be an extra nice dinner to-night , and spring asparagus , real English grown , and your mother shall have the greater portion of it", "context_len": 39}}}, "lanced": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_Past": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "They have each cut two teeth and they had to be lanced", "context_len": 12}}}, "laundress": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 13, "context": "All their meals were taken over in the next cottage , and the laundress came twice a week for the washings", "context_len": 21}}}, "vaudeville": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 25, "context": "But Miss Armitage insisted on spending it a year when the girl threw up her friend and joined a concert troupe , slipping presently into vaudeville where she was a success", "context_len": 31}}}, "profuse": {"freq": 3, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 46, "context": "When Alexander was a young man in Macedon , before his father 's death , he was one day present at the offering of sacrifices , and one of his teachers and guardians , named Leonnatus , who was standing by , thought he was rather profuse in his consumption of frankincense and myrrh", "context_len": 54}}}, "congestion": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "It was near congestion of the brain I believe", "context_len": 9}}}, "providential": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 15, "context": "When he was nearing his end , he wrote of the disease as a `` providential agent to detach the heart from all earthly affection , prompting much the desire of a Christian soul to be united -- the sooner the better -- with Him who is her only life ''", "context_len": 51}}}, "borden": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 0, "context": "Borden ! '' Marilla sat up", "context_len": 6}}}, "jane": {"freq": 7, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 7, "word_index": 0, "context": "Jane was generally up the last in the house , and I had not the slightest doubt I should still find her in her dinner dress , and ready for conversation", "context_len": 31}}}, "clime": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 14, "context": "Then an uncle at Los Angeles besought her to come out to that genial clime and spend her remaining days with him , for now he was quite alone", "context_len": 29}}}, "intrinsic": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 6, "context": "Such of the furniture as had intrinsic value was to be stored with a friend , the rest sold", "context_len": 19}}}, "businesses": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "Then Main Street was devoted to really fine stores , Brandon to offices and men 's businesses , the Postoffice being there", "context_len": 22}}}, "reprehensibly": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 11, "context": "How odd it seemed ! She thought sometimes that she felt reprehensibly young , as if she was having girlhood over again in her heart , but it was a richer , wiser and more fervent girlhood , with the added experiences of the woman", "context_len": 45}}}, "troupe": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 20, "context": "But Miss Armitage insisted on spending it a year when the girl threw up her friend and joined a concert troupe , slipping presently into vaudeville where she was a success", "context_len": 31}}}, "proffer": {"freq": 5, "POS": {"NOUN": {"freq": 3, "word_index": 15, "context": "The city was bringing up one of its fine avenues and a syndicate made a proffer for the land", "context_len": 19}, "VERB": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 57, "context": "Moreover , whoso presenteth himself before the Sultan and craveth favours of him , it behoveth him take in his hand somewhat that sorteth with the royal dignity ; and as I said to thee , how canst thou presume to present thyself before the Sultan and seek of him his daughter , without aught thou mayst proffer him of that which sorteth with his rank ? ''", "context_len": 68}}}, "frugally": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"UNK": {"freq": 2, "word_index": 12, "context": "Propped against the house-wall , Diego Vigil sat munching a corn-cake and frugally dispersing crumbs to the magpies which hovered about him in short , blue-glancing flights", "context_len": 27}}}, "discount": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 13, "context": "`` As if a few years made any difference ! Why you could discount seven years at least", "context_len": 18}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 3, "context": "Net ( no discount to S", "context_len": 6}}}, "chimes": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 8, "context": "And now it was Sunday morning and the chimes were pealing -- '' Oh , come all ye faithful", "context_len": 19}}}, "pealing": {"freq": 2, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 10, "context": "And now it was Sunday morning and the chimes were pealing -- '' Oh , come all ye faithful", "context_len": 19}, "NOUN": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "The solemn pealing of the bell for the mass at the hour of daybreak fell upon Alfgar 's ears , and he turned almost mechanically to the cathedral , yet with vague desire to communicate all his griefs and troubles to a higher power than that of man , and to seek aid from a diviner source", "context_len": 57}}}, "shirking": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 16, "context": "You would n't like me to be always looking for what was nice and pleasant and shirking other things , would you ? ''", "context_len": 24}}}, "cedars": {"freq": 4, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 4, "word_index": 32, "context": "The slope was steadily upward for a fourth of a mile , when it reached a nearly level plateau , and wound in and out among rocks , stunted pines , gnarled cedars , and ravines , interspersed with valleys and comparatively smooth stretches , with now and then a mountain torrent across which the travelers made their way with difficulty", "context_len": 61}}}, "caroling": {"freq": 1, "POS": {"VERB_PresC": {"freq": 1, "word_index": 2, "context": "Birds were caroling all sorts of joyous tunes and the tree twigs were gaily dancing", "context_len": 15}}}, "tunes": {"freq": 6, "POS": {"NOUN_P": {"freq": 6, "word_index": 38, 